window.BIO004_CARD_BANK = {"courseLabel":"BIO 004 Human Anatomy","version":3,"modules":[{"id":"m1","n":1,"title":"Module 1. Foundations, Cells, Tissues and Skin","exam":"Exam 1","extra":false,"topics":[{"id":"m1-terminology","title":"Language of anatomy: position, directions, planes, regions","summary":"anatomical position, directional terms, body planes and sections, regional terms (anterior and posterior), levels of organization","mw":"2026-08-17","tr":"2026-08-18","cards":[{"id":"L00001","dok":1,"q":"Which regional term names the heel of the foot?","a":"Calcaneal.","options":["Tarsal","Calcaneal","Plantar","Sural"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Calcaneal names the heel, and the word comes from the calcaneus, the heel bone that forms the back of the foot. It is listed with the posterior regional terms because the heel is seen best from behind. Linking a regional term to the bone underneath it is an easy way to remember many of these names.","notes":["Tarsal names the ankle, the region over the tarsal bones where the leg meets the foot. It would be correct for a question about the ankle.","Correct. Calcaneal is the heel, named for the calcaneus.","Plantar names the sole of the foot, the surface you stand on, not the heel at the back. Pick plantar if the question asked for the bottom of the foot.","Sural names the calf, the fleshy posterior part of the leg above the heel. Choose it when the question asks about the back of the leg."],"lt":"t-anatomical-terminology","lid":"c1","comp":5,"competencyId":"w1-regional-terms"},{"id":"L00002","dok":1,"q":"Which regional term refers to the armpit?","a":"Axillary.","options":["Axillary","Acromial","Antecubital","Brachial"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Axillary names the armpit, the hollow beneath the shoulder where the arm joins the trunk. Clinicians use the term often, for example when they take an axillary temperature or examine the axillary lymph nodes. It sits right next to several other upper limb regions, which is why it gets mixed up with them.","notes":["Correct. Axillary is the armpit.","Acromial names the point of the shoulder, the bony tip formed by the acromion of the scapula. It is superior to the armpit, and it would be the answer for a question about the top of the shoulder.","Antecubital names the front of the elbow, the crease where blood is often drawn. It would be correct for a question about the anterior elbow, not the armpit.","Brachial names the arm, the segment between the shoulder and the elbow. Pick brachial if the question asked about the upper arm."],"lt":"t-anatomical-terminology","lid":"c2","comp":5,"competencyId":"w1-regional-terms"},{"id":"L00003","dok":1,"q":"In anatomical position, which way do the palms face?","a":"Forward.","options":["Toward the thighs","Forward","Toward each other","Backward"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"In anatomical position the body stands erect with the feet flat, the head and eyes facing forward, and the arms at the sides with the palms facing forward. Turning the palms forward keeps the two forearm bones side by side instead of crossed, and it points the thumbs away from the body. Every directional term is defined from this one posture.","notes":["Palms resting against the thighs is how most people stand when relaxed, which is why this choice feels familiar. Anatomical position deliberately rotates the palms forward instead.","Correct. The palms face forward, with the thumbs pointing away from the body.","Palms facing each other happens when the arms are held out in front of the body, as in clapping. In anatomical position the arms hang at the sides, so the palms cannot face each other.","Palms facing backward is what happens when the forearm rotates so the radius crosses over the ulna, a position called pronation. Many people rest this way, but it is not anatomical position."],"lt":"t-anatomical-terminology","lid":"c3","comp":2,"competencyId":"w1-anatomical-position"},{"id":"L00004","dok":1,"q":"Which directional term is the opposite of proximal?","a":"Distal.","options":["Lateral","Deep","Inferior","Distal"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Proximal and distal are a paired set used on the limbs. Proximal means closer to the point where the limb attaches to the trunk, and distal means farther from that attachment, so the fingers are distal to the elbow. Directional terms come in opposite pairs, and learning them as pairs makes each one easier to remember.","notes":["Lateral means away from the midline, and its opposite is medial. Pick lateral if the question asked for the opposite of medial.","Deep means away from the body surface, and its opposite is superficial. It would be the answer for the opposite of superficial.","Inferior means below, or away from the head, and its opposite is superior. It is tempting because the distal end of a hanging arm is also lower, but inferior describes the whole body, not distance along a limb.","Correct. Distal is the opposite of proximal."],"lt":"t-anatomical-terminology","lid":"c4","comp":4,"competencyId":"w1-directional-terms"},{"id":"L00005","dok":1,"q":"Which plane divides the body into superior and inferior parts?","a":"The transverse plane.","options":["Frontal plane","Transverse plane","Midsagittal plane","Sagittal plane"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The transverse plane, also called the horizontal plane, runs parallel to the ground and divides the body into superior and inferior portions. The cut surface it produces is called a cross section. A CT image is the most familiar example of a transverse section.","notes":["The frontal plane, also called the coronal plane, is vertical and divides the body into anterior and posterior portions. Choose it when the question asks which plane separates front from back.","Correct. The transverse plane separates superior from inferior.","The midsagittal plane is the sagittal plane that lies exactly on the midline, so it divides the body into equal left and right halves. Pick it for a question about equal right and left halves.","A sagittal plane is vertical and divides the body into left and right parts. It would be the answer for a cut separating right from left."],"lt":"t-anatomical-terminology","lid":"c5","comp":3,"competencyId":"w1-planes-sections"},{"id":"L00006","dok":1,"q":"Which term describes a body lying face down?","a":"Prone.","options":["Lateral recumbent","Supine","Prone","Erect"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Prone means lying face down, on the front of the body. Its partner term, supine, means lying face up on the back. Both describe a body lying down, and directional terms still refer to anatomical position no matter which of these positions the patient is in.","notes":["Lateral recumbent means lying on one side. It would be the answer for a patient lying on the left or right side, not face down.","Supine is the opposite position, lying face up on the back. Pick supine for a patient lying on the back for an examination.","Correct. Prone is lying face down.","Erect means standing upright, which is how the body is placed in anatomical position. It is not a lying position at all, so it cannot describe someone face down."],"lt":"t-anatomical-terminology","lid":"c6","comp":2,"competencyId":"w1-anatomical-position"},{"id":"L00007","dok":1,"q":"Which regional term names the front of the elbow?","a":"Antecubital.","options":["Carpal","Antecubital","Brachial","Olecranal"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Antecubital names the front of the elbow, the shallow crease on the anterior surface where the arm meets the forearm. The prefix ante means before or in front, and cubital refers to the elbow. This is the usual site for drawing blood because superficial veins lie close to the skin there.","notes":["Carpal names the wrist, over the carpal bones. Choose it for a question about the wrist, which is distal to the elbow.","Correct. Antecubital is the anterior elbow.","Brachial names the arm between the shoulder and the elbow. It would be correct for a question about the upper arm.","Olecranal names the back of the elbow, over the olecranon of the ulna, the bony point you lean on. Pick it when the question asks about the posterior elbow."],"lt":"t-anatomical-terminology","lid":"c7","comp":5,"competencyId":"w1-regional-terms"},{"id":"L00008","dok":1,"q":"Which regional term refers to the thigh?","a":"Femoral.","options":["Crural","Patellar","Femoral","Coxal"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Femoral names the thigh, the region between the hip and the knee, and it takes its name from the femur, the thigh bone. The term appears on both the anterior and posterior views because the thigh is visible from both sides. Several vessels and nerves in the thigh share the name, such as the femoral artery.","notes":["Crural names the leg, the shin area between the knee and the ankle. In anatomy the word leg means only this lower segment, so pick crural for a question about the shin.","Patellar names the front of the knee, over the kneecap. Choose it for a question about the anterior knee.","Correct. Femoral is the thigh.","Coxal names the hip, just superior to the thigh. It would be correct for a question about the hip region."],"lt":"t-anatomical-terminology","lid":"c8","comp":5,"competencyId":"w1-regional-terms"},{"id":"L00009","dok":1,"q":"Which is the smallest of the six levels of structural organization?","a":"The chemical level.","options":["Chemical level","Tissue level","Cellular level","Organ level"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The chemical level, made of atoms and molecules such as oxygen, water, and proteins, is the smallest and simplest level of structural organization. Each level above it is built from the one below: chemicals form cells, cells form tissues, and tissues form organs. A problem at a low level, such as a faulty protein, can show up at every level above it.","notes":["Correct. The chemical level is the smallest.","The tissue level is a group of similar cells working together, such as stratified squamous epithelium. It is two steps above the chemical level, and it would answer a question about groups of similar cells.","The cellular level is the smallest living level, but cells are built from molecules, so they sit one step above the chemical level. Pick cellular if the question asked for the smallest living unit.","The organ level combines two or more tissue types into a structure with a defined job, such as the stomach. It is three steps above the chemical level."],"lt":"t-anatomical-terminology","lid":"c9","comp":1,"competencyId":"w1-levels-organization"},{"id":"L00010","dok":1,"q":"Which directional term means toward the body surface?","a":"Superficial.","options":["Deep","Distal","Superficial","Medial"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Superficial means toward or at the body surface, so the skin is superficial to the muscles beneath it. Its opposite is deep, meaning farther from the surface. This pair is the only one that describes depth, which is why it is used so often for layers.","notes":["Deep is the opposite of superficial and means away from the body surface, as the bones are deep to the muscles. Pick it for a question asking which term means away from the surface.","Distal means farther from the point where a limb attaches to the trunk, so it describes position along a limb rather than depth. It would be right for a question about the fingers relative to the wrist.","Correct. Superficial means toward the surface.","Medial means toward the midline of the body. It answers questions about side-to-side position, such as the nose being medial to the cheekbones."],"lt":"t-anatomical-terminology","lid":"c10","comp":4,"competencyId":"w1-directional-terms"},{"id":"L00011","dok":1,"q":"Which regional term names the navel?","a":"Umbilical.","options":["Inguinal","Umbilical","Abdominal","Sternal"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Umbilical names the navel, or belly button, at the center of the anterior abdomen. It is a small region inside the larger abdominal region. The same word is used for the central box of the nine abdominopelvic regions, which surrounds the navel.","notes":["Inguinal names the groin, where the thigh meets the trunk. Choose it for a question about the groin, which lies inferior and lateral to the navel.","Correct. Umbilical is the navel.","Abdominal names the whole area below the chest and above the pelvis. The navel lies inside it, so abdominal is too broad when the question asks for the navel itself.","Sternal names the area over the breastbone, in the middle of the chest. It would be correct for a question about the breastbone region."],"lt":"t-anatomical-terminology","lid":"c11","comp":5,"competencyId":"w1-regional-terms"},{"id":"L00012","dok":1,"q":"Which regional term refers to the back of the knee?","a":"Popliteal.","options":["Crural","Sural","Popliteal","Patellar"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Popliteal names the back of the knee, the hollow you can feel behind the knee joint when it bends. It is a posterior regional term and the partner of the patellar region on the front of the knee. Large vessels pass through this hollow, which is why a pulse can be felt there.","notes":["Crural names the leg, the shin area between the knee and the ankle. Choose it for a question about the shin.","Sural names the calf, the posterior leg just below the popliteal region. It would be the answer for a question about the calf.","Correct. Popliteal is the back of the knee.","Patellar names the front of the knee over the kneecap. It is the anterior partner of popliteal, so pick it if the question asked about the front of the knee."],"lt":"t-anatomical-terminology","lid":"c12","comp":5,"competencyId":"w1-regional-terms"},{"id":"L00013","dok":2,"q":"A structure described as intermediate sits in what position?","a":"Between a medial and a lateral structure.","options":["Between a medial and a lateral structure","On the opposite side of the body from another structure","Between a superior and an inferior structure","Closer to the body surface than another structure"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Intermediate describes a structure lying between a more medial structure and a more lateral one. For example, the collarbone is intermediate between the breastbone, which is medial, and the shoulder, which is lateral. It is the in-between term for the medial and lateral axis only.","notes":["Correct. Intermediate means between a medial and a lateral structure.","A structure on the opposite side of the body is contralateral. This choice would fit a question asking for the meaning of contralateral.","A structure between a superior and an inferior one is simply described as inferior to one and superior to the other. Intermediate is reserved for the medial and lateral axis, so this choice mixes up the two axes.","A structure closer to the body surface is superficial. Pick that wording if the question asked what superficial means."],"lt":"t-anatomical-terminology","lid":"c13","comp":4,"competencyId":"w1-directional-terms"},{"id":"L00014","dok":2,"q":"Comparing the wrist with the elbow along the upper limb, which directional term applies to the wrist?","a":"Distal.","options":["Proximal","Distal","Superficial","Medial"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Along a limb, distal means farther from the point where the limb attaches to the trunk. The wrist is farther from the shoulder than the elbow is, so the wrist is distal to the elbow. The pair works in reverse too, so the elbow is proximal to the wrist.","notes":["Proximal means closer to the limb's attachment to the trunk. It describes the elbow relative to the wrist, so it is the mirror-image answer to this question.","Correct. The wrist is distal to the elbow.","Superficial describes nearness to the body surface, not position along a limb. It would be right for a question comparing the skin with a muscle beneath it.","Medial means toward the midline of the body, which describes side-to-side position rather than position along a limb. It would fit a question such as how the little finger relates to the thumb."],"lt":"t-anatomical-terminology","lid":"c14","comp":4,"competencyId":"w1-directional-terms"},{"id":"L00015","dok":2,"q":"What distinguishes a midsagittal plane from a parasagittal plane?","a":"The midsagittal plane lies exactly on the midline and produces equal halves.","options":["It runs horizontally rather than vertically","It lies exactly on the midline, producing equal halves","It divides anterior from posterior parts","It is set at an angle between horizontal and vertical"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Both planes are sagittal, meaning vertical cuts that separate left from right. The midsagittal, or median, plane lies exactly on the midline and produces equal left and right halves. A parasagittal plane runs parallel to it but off the midline, so it produces unequal left and right parts.","notes":["A horizontal plane is the transverse plane, which divides superior from inferior. Both midsagittal and parasagittal planes are vertical, so orientation does not tell them apart.","Correct. The midsagittal plane sits on the midline and gives equal halves.","Dividing anterior from posterior is the job of the frontal, or coronal, plane. It would be the answer for a question about separating front from back.","A plane set at an angle is an oblique plane. Pick that description for an oblique section, not for either sagittal plane."],"lt":"t-anatomical-terminology","lid":"c15","comp":3,"competencyId":"w1-planes-sections"},{"id":"L00016","dok":2,"q":"The chest as a whole and the breastbone area within it have separate regional terms. Which pair matches them correctly?","a":"Thoracic for the chest, sternal for the breastbone.","options":["Sternal for the chest, thoracic for the breastbone","Thoracic for the chest, mammary for the breastbone","Thoracic for the chest, sternal for the breastbone","Mammary for the chest, thoracic for the breastbone"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Thoracic is the broad term for the whole chest area, and sternal is the narrower term for the area over the breastbone, the sternum. Many regional terms nest this way, with a small named region sitting inside a larger one. The umbilical region inside the abdominal region is another example.","notes":["This reverses the two terms. Sternal is the narrow term for the breastbone area and thoracic is the broad term for the chest, so the pairing is backward.","Thoracic is right for the chest, but mammary names the breast region, not the breastbone. The breastbone region is sternal.","Correct. Thoracic is the chest and sternal is the breastbone.","Mammary names the breast region, not the chest as a whole, and thoracic is not the breastbone term. Mammary would fit a question about the breasts."],"lt":"t-anatomical-terminology","lid":"c16","comp":5,"competencyId":"w1-regional-terms"},{"id":"L00017","dok":2,"q":"An anatomist needs to describe a structure as toward the back of the body. Which term and synonym are correct?","a":"Posterior, also called dorsal.","options":["Inferior, also called caudal","Anterior, also called ventral","Posterior, also called dorsal","Superior, also called cranial"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Posterior means toward the back of the body, and in humans its synonym is dorsal. Its opposite is anterior, also called ventral. Because humans stand upright, the back of the body and the dorsal surface are the same surface, so the two words can be used interchangeably.","notes":["Inferior, or caudal, means toward the feet or tail end. It describes the head-to-foot axis, not front versus back.","Anterior, or ventral, means toward the front of the body. It is the opposite of what the question asks, so pick it for a structure toward the front.","Correct. Posterior, or dorsal, means toward the back.","Superior, or cranial, means toward the head. It would be correct for a structure located above another, not one toward the back."],"lt":"t-anatomical-terminology","lid":"c17","comp":4,"competencyId":"w1-directional-terms"},{"id":"L00018","dok":2,"q":"Why does anatomical position serve as the reference for every directional term?","a":"Directional terms are defined from it, so a description holds in any posture.","options":["Directional terms are defined from it, so descriptions hold in any posture","It is the position most patients are in during an examination","It is the position the body naturally takes when fully relaxed","It is the only position in which the left and right sides match"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Every directional term is defined as if the body were in anatomical position: erect, facing forward, arms at the sides, palms forward. Because everyone uses that same reference, a phrase like anterior distal forearm points to the same spot whether the patient is standing, sitting, or lying down. Right and left are also always the patient's own right and left.","notes":["Correct. The fixed reference keeps descriptions the same in any posture.","Most patients are examined sitting or lying supine, not standing in anatomical position. The reference works precisely because descriptions do not depend on the position the patient is actually in.","A relaxed standing person usually has the palms facing the thighs, which differs from anatomical position. The palms are turned forward on purpose, so the forearm bones are not crossed.","The left and right sides of the body are roughly mirror images in many postures, not only this one. Symmetry is not what makes anatomical position the reference."],"lt":"t-anatomical-terminology","lid":"c18","comp":2,"competencyId":"w1-anatomical-position"},{"id":"L00019","dok":2,"q":"Which pair of directional terms is used only on the limbs and never on the trunk?","a":"Proximal and distal.","options":["Proximal and distal","Superficial and deep","Superior and inferior","Medial and lateral"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Proximal and distal describe position along a limb, measured from the point where the limb attaches to the trunk. The trunk has no such attachment point, so the course uses these terms on the limbs only. On the trunk the same idea of nearer and farther along the body is expressed with superior and inferior.","notes":["Correct. Proximal and distal are limb terms only.","Superficial and deep describe distance from the body surface and apply everywhere. They would be the answer for a question about layers, such as skin over muscle.","Superior and inferior describe above versus below and work anywhere on the body, including the trunk and head. Pick this pair for a question about vertical position on the trunk.","Medial and lateral describe position relative to the midline. They apply to the trunk as well as the limbs, as when the nose is described as medial to the cheekbones."],"lt":"t-anatomical-terminology","lid":"c19","comp":4,"competencyId":"w1-directional-terms"},{"id":"L00020","dok":2,"q":"Which regional term names the lateral side of the leg, between the knee and the ankle?","a":"Fibular.","options":["Tarsal","Crural","Sural","Fibular"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Fibular, also called peroneal, names the lateral side of the leg, where the fibula runs between the knee and the ankle. The fibula is the thin lateral bone of the leg, so the region borrows its name. It appears on the posterior regional term list along with sural and calcaneal.","notes":["Tarsal names the ankle, over the tarsal bones. It would be correct for a question about the ankle, which is distal to the leg.","Crural names the leg as the shin area between the knee and the ankle. It is the general anterior leg term, so it is less specific than the lateral leg term the question wants.","Sural names the calf, the posterior part of the leg. Pick it if the question asked for the back of the leg rather than its lateral side.","Correct. Fibular is the lateral side of the leg."],"lt":"t-anatomical-terminology","lid":"c20","comp":5,"competencyId":"w1-regional-terms"},{"id":"L00021","dok":2,"q":"The cheek and the chin each have a regional term. Which pairing is correct?","a":"Buccal for the cheek, mental for the chin.","options":["Mental for the cheek, buccal for the chin","Buccal for the cheek, mental for the chin","Otic for the cheek, mental for the chin","Buccal for the cheek, oral for the chin"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Buccal names the cheek and mental names the chin. Mental comes from the Latin word for chin, not from the word for mind, which is a common source of confusion. Both are anterior regions of the face.","notes":["This reverses the two terms. The cheek is buccal and the chin is mental, so the pairing is backward.","Correct. Buccal is the cheek and mental is the chin.","Otic names the ear region on the side of the head, not the cheek. Pick otic for a question about the ear.","Buccal is right for the cheek, but oral names the mouth region, not the chin. Oral would fit a question about the mouth."],"lt":"t-anatomical-terminology","lid":"c21","comp":5,"competencyId":"w1-regional-terms"},{"id":"L00022","dok":2,"q":"Which regional term belongs to the appendicular region rather than the axial region?","a":"Carpal.","options":["Lumbar","Sternal","Cervical","Carpal"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The appendicular region is the limbs, and the axial region is the head, neck, and trunk, the central axis of the body. Carpal names the wrist, which is part of the upper limb, so it is appendicular. The other three terms all name parts of the neck or trunk.","notes":["Lumbar names the lower back, part of the trunk, so it is axial. Pick it for a question about the lower back.","Sternal names the breastbone area of the chest, part of the trunk. It is axial, so it does not belong to a limb.","Cervical names the neck, which is part of the axial region. It would be correct for a question asking which axial term names the neck.","Correct. Carpal is the wrist, part of a limb."],"lt":"t-anatomical-terminology","lid":"c22","comp":5,"competencyId":"w1-regional-terms"},{"id":"L00023","dok":2,"q":"Cranial and caudal form a paired set. What relationship does the pair describe?","a":"Toward the head versus toward the tail end of the body.","options":["Toward the midline versus away from the midline","Toward the front versus toward the back of the body","Toward the head versus toward the tail end of the body","Nearer versus farther from the body surface"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Cranial, also written cephalad, means toward the head, and caudal means toward the tail end, the inferior end of the trunk. The pair describes the same head-to-tail axis as superior and inferior. On the human trunk the two pairs are often used interchangeably.","notes":["Toward versus away from the midline is described by medial and lateral. This would be correct for that pair.","Front versus back is described by anterior and posterior, also called ventral and dorsal. Pick this relationship for that pair.","Correct. Cranial is toward the head and caudal is toward the tail end.","Nearer versus farther from the surface is described by superficial and deep. This relationship belongs to that pair."],"lt":"t-anatomical-terminology","lid":"c23","comp":4,"competencyId":"w1-directional-terms"},{"id":"L00024","dok":2,"q":"Which plane would separate the body into front and back portions?","a":"The frontal (coronal) plane.","options":["The transverse plane","The oblique plane","The midsagittal plane","The frontal (coronal) plane"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The frontal plane, also called the coronal plane, is a vertical plane that runs from side to side, dividing the body into anterior and posterior portions. Picture a cut running from one ear to the other straight down through the body. MRI images are often read in this plane.","notes":["The transverse plane is horizontal and divides the body into superior and inferior portions. Pick it for a cross section, such as a CT slice.","An oblique plane cuts at an angle other than 90 degrees. Its portions are angled, so it is not the plane that cleanly separates front from back.","The midsagittal plane is vertical but runs front to back on the midline, so it divides the body into equal left and right halves. It would be correct for separating right from left.","Correct. The frontal plane separates anterior from posterior."],"lt":"t-anatomical-terminology","lid":"c24","comp":3,"competencyId":"w1-planes-sections"},{"id":"L00025","dok":2,"q":"The tissue level of organization is built directly from which level?","a":"The cellular level.","options":["The cellular level","The organ system level","The chemical level","The organ level"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"A tissue is a group of similar cells that work together to perform a specific function, so the tissue level is built directly from the cellular level. Stratified squamous epithelium, for example, is many layers of similar cells. Each level of organization is assembled from the level just below it.","notes":["Correct. Tissues are built from cells.","The organ system level is two steps above tissues and is made of organs working toward one broad function. It is built from organs, not cells.","The chemical level, atoms and molecules, builds cells rather than tissues directly. It is two steps below the tissue level, and it would be the answer for what cells are made of.","The organ level is above the tissue level, because an organ is made of two or more tissue types. Pick it for a question about what tissues combine to form."],"lt":"t-anatomical-terminology","lid":"c25","comp":1,"competencyId":"w1-levels-organization"},{"id":"L00026","dok":2,"q":"Which regional term names the sole of the foot, as distinct from the heel?","a":"Plantar.","options":["Plantar","Calcaneal","Pedal","Tarsal"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Plantar names the sole of the foot, the surface that contacts the ground. It is the foot's counterpart to the palmar surface of the hand. Calcaneal, pedal, and plantar all involve the foot, so the precise term depends on which part is meant.","notes":["Correct. Plantar is the sole of the foot.","Calcaneal names the heel only, the back of the foot over the calcaneus. Pick it when the question asks about the heel.","Pedal names the foot as a whole. It is the broad term, so it would fit a question about the entire foot rather than the sole.","Tarsal names the ankle region, over the tarsal bones. It would be correct for a question about the ankle."],"lt":"t-anatomical-terminology","lid":"c26","comp":5,"competencyId":"w1-regional-terms"},{"id":"L00027","dok":2,"q":"A surgeon makes a single vertical incision exactly on the midline of the abdomen. Which plane does the incision follow?","a":"The midsagittal (median) plane.","options":["The midsagittal plane","The transverse plane","A parasagittal plane","The frontal plane"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"A vertical cut placed exactly on the midline follows the midsagittal, or median, plane. The midline is the imaginary vertical line from the top of the head through the nose and down to the pelvis, so a cut along it would divide the body into equal left and right halves. A surgical incision down the middle of the abdomen lies in this plane.","notes":["Correct. A vertical midline cut follows the midsagittal plane.","The transverse plane is horizontal and divides superior from inferior. It would fit a horizontal incision running across the abdomen.","A parasagittal plane is also vertical and separates left from right, but it lies off the midline. Pick it if the incision had been placed to one side of the midline.","The frontal plane runs from side to side and divides the body into anterior and posterior portions. It would describe a cut separating front from back, not a vertical line down the front of the abdomen."],"lt":"t-anatomical-terminology","lid":"c27","comp":3,"competencyId":"w1-planes-sections"},{"id":"L00028","dok":2,"q":"A chart records a wound on the posterior surface of the elbow. Which regional term names that exact site?","a":"Olecranal.","options":["Antecubital","Antebrachial","Olecranal","Brachial"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Olecranal names the posterior surface of the elbow, over the olecranon, the bony point of the ulna that you lean on. It is the posterior partner of the antecubital region on the front of the elbow. Charting the exact surface tells the next clinician precisely where to look.","notes":["Antecubital names the anterior surface of the elbow, the crease where blood is drawn. It is the opposite surface, so pick it for a wound on the front of the elbow.","Antebrachial names the forearm, between the elbow and the wrist. Choose it for a wound on the forearm.","Correct. Olecranal is the back of the elbow.","Brachial names the arm between the shoulder and the elbow. It would be correct for a wound on the upper arm."],"lt":"t-anatomical-terminology","lid":"c28","comp":5,"competencyId":"w1-regional-terms"},{"id":"L00029","dok":2,"q":"A CT scanner produces a cross-sectional image of the trunk that divides it into upper and lower parts. Which section does the image represent?","a":"A transverse section.","options":["A transverse section","A frontal section","An oblique section","A midsagittal section"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"A standard CT image is a transverse section, a horizontal slice that divides the body into superior and inferior parts. The radiologist views each slice as a cross section of the trunk. Knowing the plane tells you which view you are looking at and which structures can appear together.","notes":["Correct. A CT slice is a transverse section.","A frontal section divides the body into anterior and posterior parts. It would match an image that shows the body as if viewed from the front, a view MRI often provides.","An oblique section is cut at an angle other than 90 degrees. It is not the standard horizontal slice described here.","A midsagittal section is a vertical cut on the midline that divides the body into equal left and right halves. It would match an image showing the body from the side, split down the middle."],"lt":"t-anatomical-terminology","lid":"c29","comp":3,"competencyId":"w1-planes-sections"},{"id":"L00030","dok":2,"q":"A patient has one injury on the right hand and another on the right knee. Which directional term describes how the two sites relate to each other?","a":"They are ipsilateral.","options":["They are intermediate","They are contralateral","They are bilateral","They are ipsilateral"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Ipsilateral means on the same side of the body. Both injuries are on the patient's right side, so they are ipsilateral to each other even though one is on the upper limb and the other on the lower limb. Its opposite, contralateral, would describe a right hand and a left knee.","notes":["Intermediate describes a structure lying between a medial and a lateral structure. It does not describe how two separate sites on the same side relate.","Contralateral means on opposite sides of the body. Pick it if one injury were on the right and the other on the left.","Bilateral means present on both the left and right sides, such as both knees being injured. Here both injuries are on the right, so bilateral does not fit.","Correct. Both sites are on the right, so they are ipsilateral."],"lt":"t-anatomical-terminology","lid":"c30","comp":4,"competencyId":"w1-directional-terms"},{"id":"L00031","dok":3,"q":"A structure in the forearm is described as both proximal and lateral. In anatomical position, where does it sit?","a":"Near the elbow end of the forearm, on the thumb side.","options":["Near the wrist end of the forearm, on the little-finger side","Near the wrist end of the forearm, on the thumb side","Near the elbow end of the forearm, on the thumb side","Near the elbow end of the forearm, on the little-finger side"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Proximal places the structure closer to where the limb attaches to the trunk, so toward the elbow end of the forearm. Lateral places it away from the midline, and with the palms facing forward in anatomical position, the thumb side of the forearm is the lateral side. Putting the two terms together gives the elbow end on the thumb side.","notes":["This location is distal and medial, the opposite of both given terms. It would be the answer for a structure described as distal and medial.","The thumb side is right for lateral, but the wrist end is distal. This would fit a structure described as distal and lateral.","Correct. Proximal is the elbow end and lateral is the thumb side.","The elbow end is right for proximal, but the little-finger side is medial in anatomical position. This would fit a structure described as proximal and medial."],"lt":"t-anatomical-terminology","lid":"c31","comp":4,"competencyId":"w1-directional-terms"},{"id":"L00032","dok":2,"q":"A lab specimen is cut with a vertical cut that produces right and left parts of unequal size. Which plane was used?","a":"A parasagittal plane.","options":["A parasagittal plane","A frontal plane","A transverse plane","A midsagittal plane"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"A parasagittal plane is a vertical plane parallel to the midline but off to one side. Like every sagittal plane it separates left from right, but because it misses the midline, the two parts are unequal. The word para means beside, so a parasagittal plane runs beside the midsagittal plane.","notes":["Correct. A vertical cut with unequal right and left parts is parasagittal.","A frontal plane is vertical, but it separates front from back rather than right from left. It would fit a cut that produced anterior and posterior parts.","A transverse plane is horizontal and produces superior and inferior parts. It would be the answer for a cut separating top from bottom.","A midsagittal plane lies exactly on the midline, so its right and left halves are equal. Pick it if the cut had produced two equal halves."],"lt":"t-anatomical-terminology","lid":"c32","comp":3,"competencyId":"w1-planes-sections"},{"id":"L00033","dok":2,"q":"A clinician notes a bruise on the anterior surface of the knee. Which regional term records that location?","a":"Patellar.","options":["Patellar","Crural","Popliteal","Femoral"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Patellar names the anterior surface of the knee, where the patella, or kneecap, sits. It is on the anterior regional term list and is the front partner of the popliteal region behind the knee. Naming the surface precisely keeps the note from being read as the back of the knee.","notes":["Correct. Patellar is the front of the knee.","Crural names the leg, the shin area below the knee. It would be correct for a bruise on the shin.","Popliteal names the posterior surface of the knee, the hollow behind it. It is the opposite surface, so pick it for a bruise behind the knee.","Femoral names the thigh, above the knee. Choose it for a bruise between the hip and the knee."],"lt":"t-anatomical-terminology","lid":"c33","comp":5,"competencyId":"w1-regional-terms"},{"id":"L00034","dok":2,"q":"A note describes pain in the area where the thigh meets the trunk on the front of the body. Which regional term names that area?","a":"Inguinal.","options":["Pubic","Inguinal","Coxal","Femoral"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Inguinal names the groin, the crease on the front of the body where the thigh meets the trunk. It lies on either side of the pubic region. Groin pain is a common complaint, so this term shows up often in clinical notes.","notes":["Pubic names the area near the genital region, in the midline between the two inguinal regions. Pick it for pain centered over the pubic bones.","Correct. Inguinal is the groin.","Coxal names the hip, on the side of the body over the hip joint. It would be correct for pain on the lateral hip rather than the front crease.","Femoral names the thigh itself, distal to the groin. Choose it for pain in the thigh rather than at the junction with the trunk."],"lt":"t-anatomical-terminology","lid":"c34","comp":5,"competencyId":"w1-regional-terms"},{"id":"L00035","dok":2,"q":"A finding is recorded over the back of the head. Which regional term names that location?","a":"Occipital","options":["Occipital","Frontal","Otic","Cervical"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Occipital names the back of the head, over the occipital bone. It is listed among the posterior regional terms, just above the cervical region of the neck. Cephalic is the general term for the whole head, and occipital is the specific term for its back surface.","notes":["Correct. Occipital is the back of the head.","Frontal names the forehead, the front of the head. It is the opposite surface, so pick it for a finding on the forehead.","Otic names the ear region, on the side of the head. Pick it for a finding on or around the ear.","Cervical names the neck, inferior to the head. Choose it for a finding on the neck."],"lt":"t-anatomical-terminology","lid":"c35","comp":5,"competencyId":"w1-regional-terms"},{"id":"L00036","dok":2,"q":"A muscle lies beneath the skin, farther from the body surface. Compared with the skin, which directional term applies to the muscle?","a":"Deep.","options":["Proximal","Lateral","Superficial","Deep"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Deep means farther from the body surface. The muscle lies under the skin, so the muscle is deep to the skin, and the skin is superficial to the muscle. Superficial and deep are the pair used whenever layers are stacked on top of one another.","notes":["Proximal describes position along a limb, closer to the trunk. It would be correct for a question such as how the elbow relates to the wrist.","Lateral means away from the midline. It describes side-to-side position, not depth beneath the surface.","Superficial means closer to the body surface. It describes the skin relative to the muscle, so it is the mirror-image answer to this question.","Correct. The muscle is deep to the skin."],"lt":"t-anatomical-terminology","lid":"c36","comp":4,"competencyId":"w1-directional-terms"},{"id":"L00037","dok":2,"q":"A patient is positioned lying on the back, face up, for an examination. Which term names this position?","a":"Supine.","options":["Prone","Supine","Lateral recumbent","Anatomical position"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Supine means lying face up, on the back. It is the most common position for examining the abdomen. Even with the patient supine, directional terms are still given as if the body were in anatomical position.","notes":["Prone is the opposite, lying face down on the front. Pick it for a patient lying on the stomach, as for an examination of the back.","Correct. Lying face up is supine.","Lateral recumbent means lying on one side. It would be the answer for a patient turned onto the left or right side.","Anatomical position is standing erect with the palms forward. It is the reference posture for directional terms, not a lying position."],"lt":"t-anatomical-terminology","lid":"c37","comp":2,"competencyId":"w1-anatomical-position"},{"id":"L00038","dok":2,"q":"A structure is described over the sacrum, in the area between the hips. Which regional term applies?","a":"Sacral","options":["Gluteal","Sacral","Lumbar","Coxal"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Sacral names the area between the hips, over the sacrum at the base of the spine. It is a posterior regional term in the axial region. The lumbar region sits just above it, and the two gluteal regions lie on either side of it.","notes":["Gluteal names the buttock, lateral to the sacral region. It would be correct for a structure over the buttock.","Correct. Sacral is the area between the hips over the sacrum.","Lumbar names the lower back, or loin area, superior to the sacral region. Pick it for a structure in the small of the back above the sacrum.","Coxal names the hip, on the side of the body over the hip joint. It would be correct for a structure on the lateral hip, not the midline area between the hips."],"lt":"t-anatomical-terminology","lid":"c38","comp":5,"competencyId":"w1-regional-terms"},{"id":"L00039","dok":2,"q":"A regional term names the thumb specifically, separate from the term for the fingers in general. Which term names the thumb?","a":"Pollex.","options":["Digital","Pollex","Carpal","Manus"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Pollex is the regional term for the thumb. The fingers in general are digital, or phalangeal, but the thumb has its own name because of its special position and movement. The big toe has a matching term, hallux.","notes":["Digital names the fingers or toes in general. It would answer a question about the fingers as a group, not the thumb specifically.","Correct. Pollex is the thumb.","Carpal names the wrist, proximal to the hand. It would be correct for a question about the wrist.","Manus names the hand as a whole. Pick it for a question about the entire hand."],"lt":"t-anatomical-terminology","lid":"c39","comp":5,"competencyId":"w1-regional-terms"},{"id":"L00040","dok":2,"q":"Moving down the upper limb from the trunk, which order places these regions from proximal to distal: carpal, brachial, acromial, antebrachial?","a":"Acromial, brachial, antebrachial, carpal.","options":["Acromial, antebrachial, brachial, carpal","Brachial, acromial, carpal, antebrachial","Carpal, antebrachial, brachial, acromial","Acromial, brachial, antebrachial, carpal"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Starting at the trunk, the acromial region is the point of the shoulder, then the brachial region is the arm, the antebrachial region is the forearm, and the carpal region is the wrist. The prefix ante in antebrachial means before, so the forearm comes after the brachial region along the limb. Ordering regions this way also reinforces what proximal and distal mean.","notes":["This order puts the forearm before the arm. The brachial region, the arm, is proximal to the antebrachial region, the forearm.","This order puts the arm above the shoulder and the wrist above the forearm, which mixes up both pairs. The shoulder is the most proximal of these regions.","This is the correct sequence written in reverse, running from distal to proximal. It would be the answer if the question asked for distal-to-proximal order.","Correct. Shoulder, arm, forearm, wrist."],"lt":"t-anatomical-terminology","lid":"c40","comp":5,"competencyId":"w1-regional-terms"},{"id":"L00041","dok":3,"q":"A note reads: lesion on the anterior, distal, lateral forearm. In anatomical position, where is the lesion?","a":"On the front of the forearm, near the wrist, on the thumb side.","options":["On the front of the forearm, near the wrist, on the thumb side","On the back of the forearm, near the wrist, on the thumb side","On the front of the forearm, near the elbow, on the little-finger side","On the back of the forearm, near the elbow, on the little-finger side"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Anterior places the lesion on the front surface, distal places it toward the wrist end, and lateral places it on the thumb side, because the thumb points away from the midline when the palms face forward. Reading each term separately and then combining them is the reliable way to decode a description like this.","notes":["Correct. Front, near the wrist, thumb side.","Near the wrist and the thumb side are right, but the back of the forearm is posterior. This would fit a posterior, distal, lateral lesion.","The front surface is right, but near the elbow is proximal and the little-finger side is medial. This would fit an anterior, proximal, medial lesion.","This location reverses all three terms: posterior, proximal, and medial. It would fit a lesion charted as posterior, proximal, medial forearm."],"lt":"t-anatomical-terminology","lid":"c41","comp":4,"competencyId":"w1-directional-terms"},{"id":"L00042","dok":3,"q":"A frontal section is cut through the head just in front of the ears. Which group of regions lies entirely on the anterior portion?","a":"Frontal, orbital, nasal, and oral.","options":["Occipital and otic","Occipital, nasal, and mental","Frontal, orbital, nasal, and oral","Orbital, otic, and occipital"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"A frontal section divides the head into anterior and posterior portions. With the cut just in front of the ears, the forehead (frontal), eyes (orbital), nose (nasal), and mouth (oral) all fall on the anterior portion. The ears (otic) and the back of the head (occipital) fall on the posterior portion.","notes":["The otic region, the ear, and the occipital region, the back of the head, both lie behind a cut made in front of the ears. This group would be the answer for the posterior portion.","The nasal and mental regions are anterior, but the occipital region is the back of the head. This group also mixes regions from both portions.","Correct. These face regions all lie in front of the cut.","The orbital region is anterior, but the otic and occipital regions are behind the cut. This group mixes regions from both portions."],"lt":"t-anatomical-terminology","lid":"c42","comp":3,"competencyId":"w1-planes-sections"},{"id":"L00043","dok":2,"q":"Tracing down the back of the body from the head, which order places these posterior regions from superior to inferior: lumbar, occipital, sacral, cervical?","a":"Occipital, cervical, lumbar, sacral.","options":["Sacral, lumbar, cervical, occipital","Occipital, lumbar, cervical, sacral","Occipital, cervical, lumbar, sacral","Cervical, occipital, sacral, lumbar"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"From the top down, the occipital region is the back of the head, the cervical region is the neck, the lumbar region is the lower back, and the sacral region is the area between the hips over the sacrum. These regions follow the vertebral column from the skull to the base of the spine. Superior and inferior are the right terms for ordering along the trunk.","notes":["This is the correct sequence in reverse, running from inferior to superior. It would be the answer if the question asked for inferior-to-superior order.","This order places the lower back above the neck. The cervical region, the neck, is superior to the lumbar region.","Correct. Head, neck, lower back, then sacrum.","This order places the neck above the head and the sacrum above the lower back. The occipital region is the most superior, and the sacral region is the most inferior of these."],"lt":"t-anatomical-terminology","lid":"c43","comp":5,"competencyId":"w1-regional-terms"},{"id":"L00044","dok":2,"q":"A region is part of the axial region, on the anterior surface of the head, and surrounds the eye. Which regional term fits?","a":"Orbital.","options":["Occipital","Orbital","Frontal","Otic"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Orbital, also called ocular, names the eye region on the anterior face. It takes its name from the orbit, the bony socket that holds the eye. Like all head regions, it belongs to the axial region.","notes":["Occipital names the back of the head, the posterior surface. It would be correct for a region on the back of the skull.","Correct. Orbital is the eye region.","Frontal names the forehead, just above the eyes. It is anterior and on the head, but it does not name the eye region itself.","Otic names the ear region, on the side of the head. Pick it for a question about the ear."],"lt":"t-anatomical-terminology","lid":"c44","comp":5,"competencyId":"w1-regional-terms"},{"id":"L00045","dok":2,"q":"Moving down the lower limb from the trunk, which order places these regions from proximal to distal: tarsal, coxal, crural, femoral?","a":"Coxal, femoral, crural, tarsal.","options":["Tarsal, crural, femoral, coxal","Coxal, crural, femoral, tarsal","Femoral, coxal, tarsal, crural","Coxal, femoral, crural, tarsal"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Starting at the trunk, the coxal region is the hip, the femoral region is the thigh, the crural region is the leg or shin, and the tarsal region is the ankle. In anatomy the thigh and the leg are separate segments, with the knee between them. This sequence matches the bones in order: hip bone, femur, tibia and fibula, then tarsals.","notes":["This is the correct sequence in reverse, running from distal to proximal. It would answer a question asking for distal-to-proximal order.","This order places the leg above the thigh. The femoral region, the thigh, is proximal to the crural region, the leg.","This order puts the thigh above the hip and the ankle above the leg. The hip is the most proximal region of the lower limb.","Correct. Hip, thigh, leg, ankle."],"lt":"t-anatomical-terminology","lid":"c45","comp":5,"competencyId":"w1-regional-terms"},{"id":"L00046","dok":1,"q":"Which level of structural organization is the smallest living unit of the body?","a":"The cellular level.","options":["Organismal level","Chemical level","Tissue level","Cellular level"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The cellular level is the smallest level that is alive. A cell, such as a keratinocyte or a neuron, carries out the basic life processes on its own. The chemical level is smaller, but atoms and molecules by themselves are not alive.","notes":["The organismal level is the whole living person, all organ systems together. It is the largest level, not the smallest.","The chemical level is smaller than the cellular level, but atoms and molecules are not living. Pick it if the question asked for the smallest level overall.","The tissue level is alive, but it is made of many similar cells working together, so it is not the smallest living unit. It would answer a question about groups of similar cells.","Correct. The cell is the smallest living unit."],"lt":"t-anatomical-terminology","lid":"c1-x2","comp":1,"competencyId":"w1-levels-organization"},{"id":"L00047","dok":2,"q":"A clinician facing a patient charts a mole on the left cheek. Whose left does the chart mean?","a":"The patient's own left.","options":["The patient's own left","The left side of a printed body diagram as you look at it","The clinician's left while facing the patient","Either side, as long as the chart says which view was used"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Anatomical right and left are always the patient's own right and left, never the observer's. When you face a patient, their left side is on your right, so charting from your own view would place the mole on the wrong cheek. Using the patient's sides is part of describing the body from anatomical position.","notes":["Correct. Right and left always belong to the patient.","A diagram drawn facing you shows the patient's left on your right, so reading the diagram's left as you see it reverses the sides. Diagrams are labeled from the patient's point of view.","Facing the patient, the clinician's left is the patient's right. Charting from the observer's view is the classic error the anatomical convention is designed to prevent.","Clinical notes do not switch conventions from chart to chart. Every clinician reads right and left as the patient's own, so a note never needs to state a viewpoint."],"lt":"t-anatomical-terminology","lid":"c2-x2","comp":2,"competencyId":"w1-anatomical-position"},{"id":"L00048","dok":1,"q":"Which description matches anatomical position?","a":"Standing erect, facing forward, arms at the sides, palms forward.","options":["Lying face up, arms at the sides, palms facing up","Standing erect, facing forward, arms raised to the sides, palms down","Standing erect, facing forward, arms at the sides, palms against the thighs","Standing erect, facing forward, arms at the sides, palms forward"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Anatomical position is the body standing erect with the feet flat and slightly apart, the head and eyes facing forward, the arms at the sides, and the palms facing forward. Each detail matters, especially the palms, because turning them forward sets which side of the forearm is lateral. It is the single reference posture behind every directional term.","notes":["Lying face up is the supine position. It describes how a patient may be lying, but anatomical position is upright.","Raising the arms to the sides changes the position of the upper limbs. In anatomical position the arms hang down at the sides.","This is a relaxed standing posture with the palms resting on the thighs. It is close, but anatomical position turns the palms forward.","Correct. Erect, facing forward, arms at the sides, palms forward."],"lt":"t-anatomical-terminology","lid":"c3-x2","comp":2,"competencyId":"w1-anatomical-position"},{"id":"L00049","dok":1,"q":"What is the midline of the body?","a":"An imaginary vertical line from the top of the head through the nose and down the spine to the pelvis.","options":["A vertical line down the side of the body that separates front from back","A horizontal line through the navel that separates upper from lower","A vertical line down the center of the body, head to pelvis","The line where each limb joins the trunk"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The midline is the imaginary vertical line running from the top of the head, through the nose, down the spine, to the pelvis. It divides the body into equal left and right halves. It is the reference for medial and lateral, so medial means toward this line and lateral means away from it.","notes":["A line separating front from back would lie in a frontal plane. The midline runs down the center, not down the side.","A horizontal line through the navel is the level used to draw the four abdominal quadrants. It separates upper from lower, which the midline does not do.","Correct. The midline runs vertically down the center of the body.","The point where a limb joins the trunk is the reference for proximal and distal. It is not the midline, which is the reference for medial and lateral."],"lt":"t-anatomical-terminology","lid":"c4-x2","comp":4,"competencyId":"w1-directional-terms"},{"id":"L00050","dok":1,"q":"The frontal plane has a second name. What is it?","a":"The coronal plane.","options":["Median plane","Coronal plane","Horizontal plane","Oblique plane"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The frontal plane is also called the coronal plane. It is vertical and divides the body into anterior and posterior portions. Each of the main planes has an alternate name, and both names are used in lab and imaging reports.","notes":["Median plane is the other name for the midsagittal plane, the vertical plane on the midline that divides left from right. Pick it if the question asked for the second name of the midsagittal plane.","Correct. The frontal plane is also called the coronal plane.","Horizontal plane is the other name for the transverse plane, which divides superior from inferior. It would be correct for the second name of the transverse plane.","An oblique plane is cut at an angle other than 90 degrees. It is a separate plane, not another name for the frontal plane."],"lt":"t-anatomical-terminology","lid":"c5-x2","comp":3,"competencyId":"w1-planes-sections"},{"id":"L00051","dok":1,"q":"A plane is an imaginary flat surface passing through the body. What is the cut surface that a plane produces called?","a":"A section.","options":["A section","A quadrant","A cavity","A region"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"A plane is the imaginary flat surface, and the section is the cut surface it produces. That is why a CT image is called a transverse section: the transverse plane is where the cut runs, and the section is the surface you see. The two words are related but not interchangeable.","notes":["Correct. The cut surface is a section.","A quadrant is one of the four areas of the abdomen drawn by two planes through the navel. It is an area on the surface, not the cut surface of a plane.","A cavity is a space inside the body that houses organs, such as the thoracic cavity. It exists whether or not the body is cut.","A region is a named area of the body surface, such as the femoral or carpal region. It is not produced by cutting along a plane."],"lt":"t-anatomical-terminology","lid":"c6-x2","comp":3,"competencyId":"w1-planes-sections"},{"id":"L00052","dok":1,"q":"What is the directional opposite of superior?","a":"Inferior.","options":["Distal","Lateral","Posterior","Inferior"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Superior means toward the head, or above, and its paired opposite is inferior, meaning away from the head, or below. The pair describes the vertical axis of the body and can be used anywhere, including the trunk. Learning the pairs together prevents mixing terms from different axes.","notes":["Distal means farther from the limb's attachment to the trunk, and its opposite is proximal. It would be correct for the opposite of proximal.","Lateral means away from the midline, and its opposite is medial. Choose it for the opposite of medial.","Posterior means toward the back, and its opposite is anterior. Pick posterior if the question asked for the opposite of anterior.","Correct. Inferior is the opposite of superior."],"lt":"t-anatomical-terminology","lid":"c7-x2","comp":4,"competencyId":"w1-directional-terms"},{"id":"L00053","dok":1,"q":"Which directional term means toward the midline of the body?","a":"Medial.","options":["Proximal","Lateral","Medial","Superficial"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Medial means toward the midline, the imaginary vertical line down the center of the body. The nose is medial to the cheekbones, and the little finger is on the medial side of the hand in anatomical position. Its opposite is lateral.","notes":["Proximal means closer to where a limb attaches to the trunk. It describes position along a limb, not distance from the midline.","Lateral is the opposite of medial and means away from the midline. Pick it for a question asking which term means away from the center line.","Correct. Medial means toward the midline.","Superficial means toward the body surface. It describes depth, not side-to-side position."],"lt":"t-anatomical-terminology","lid":"c8-x2","comp":4,"competencyId":"w1-directional-terms"},{"id":"L00054","dok":1,"q":"Proximal means closer to which reference point?","a":"The point where the limb attaches to the trunk.","options":["The head end of the body","The limb's attachment to the trunk","The midline of the whole body","The skin surface over the limb"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Proximal means closer to the point where a limb attaches to the trunk, and distal means farther from it. For the upper limb that point is the shoulder, so the elbow is proximal to the wrist. Because the reference is the limb's attachment, the course uses this pair on the limbs only.","notes":["Closeness to the head is described by superior, or cranial. It would be the reference point for those terms.","Correct. Proximal is measured from the limb's attachment to the trunk.","Closeness to the midline is described by medial. Pick the midline if the question asked about medial.","Closeness to the body surface is described by superficial. It would be the reference point if the question asked what superficial means."],"lt":"t-anatomical-terminology","lid":"c9-x2","comp":4,"competencyId":"w1-directional-terms"},{"id":"L00055","dok":1,"q":"Which regional term names the point of the shoulder?","a":"Acromial.","options":["Brachial","Axillary","Acromial","Scapular"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Acromial names the point of the shoulder, the bony tip you can feel at the top and side of the shoulder. It takes its name from the acromion, a process of the scapula. It appears on the posterior regional term list.","notes":["Brachial names the arm, between the shoulder and the elbow. Choose it for a question about the upper arm.","Axillary names the armpit, the hollow beneath the shoulder. Pick it for a question about the armpit.","Correct. Acromial is the point of the shoulder.","Scapular names the shoulder blade on the upper back. It would be correct for a question about the flat bone area on the posterior thorax."],"lt":"t-anatomical-terminology","lid":"c10-x2","comp":5,"competencyId":"w1-regional-terms"},{"id":"L00056","dok":1,"q":"Which regional term names the area below the chest and above the pelvis?","a":"Abdominal.","options":["Umbilical","Thoracic","Abdominal","Pelvic"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Abdominal names the anterior trunk below the chest and above the pelvis. It is a broad region that contains smaller named regions, such as the umbilical region around the navel. The same area is divided into quadrants or nine regions when a location needs to be more exact.","notes":["Umbilical names only the navel, a small region inside the abdominal region. It is too narrow for the whole area described.","Thoracic names the chest area, superior to the abdomen. It would be correct for a question about the chest.","Correct. Abdominal is between the chest and the pelvis.","Pelvic names the lower abdomen or hip area, inferior to the abdominal region. Pick it for the area just above the pubic region."],"lt":"t-anatomical-terminology","lid":"c12-x2","comp":5,"competencyId":"w1-regional-terms"},{"id":"L00057","dok":1,"q":"Which regional term names the big toe?","a":"Hallux.","options":["Pedal","Hallux","Digital","Pollex"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Hallux is the regional term for the big toe, often called the great toe. It is the foot's counterpart to the pollex, the thumb. The other toes are covered by the general term digital, or phalangeal.","notes":["Pedal names the foot as a whole. Choose it for a question about the entire foot.","Correct. Hallux is the big toe.","Digital names the fingers or toes in general. It would fit a question about the toes as a group, not the big toe specifically.","Pollex names the thumb, the matching digit on the hand. Pick it for a question about the thumb."],"lt":"t-anatomical-terminology","lid":"c13-x2","comp":5,"competencyId":"w1-regional-terms"},{"id":"L00058","dok":1,"q":"How many levels of structural organization does this course list, from chemical to organismal?","a":"Six.","options":["Six","Five","Four","Seven"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The course lists six levels, from least to most complex: chemical, cellular, tissue, organ, organ system, and organismal. Each level is built from the one before it. Writing them in order once is the quickest way to check the count.","notes":["Correct. There are six levels.","Five usually comes from forgetting the organ system level between organ and organismal. The list runs chemical, cellular, tissue, organ, organ system, organismal.","Four would leave out two of the levels, usually the chemical and organismal ends of the list. The full sequence starts at atoms and molecules and ends with the whole person.","Seven comes from adding a level the course does not use, such as splitting atoms and molecules into separate levels. This course groups them together as the chemical level."],"lt":"t-anatomical-terminology","lid":"c14-x2","comp":1,"competencyId":"w1-levels-organization"},{"id":"L00059","dok":1,"q":"What is the regional term for the buttock?","a":"Gluteal.","options":["Coxal","Gluteal","Sacral","Lumbar"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Gluteal names the buttock, on the posterior side of the body below the lower back. The large gluteal muscles give the region its shape. It sits lateral to the sacral region and inferior to the lumbar region.","notes":["Coxal names the hip, on the side of the body over the hip joint. Choose it for a question about the hip rather than the buttock.","Correct. Gluteal is the buttock.","Sacral names the area between the hips, over the sacrum, medial to the two gluteal regions. Pick it for a question about the base of the spine.","Lumbar names the lower back, superior to the buttock. It would be correct for the small of the back."],"lt":"t-anatomical-terminology","lid":"c15-x2","comp":5,"competencyId":"w1-regional-terms"},{"id":"L00060","dok":1,"q":"A wound is on the front of the body. Which directional term describes its location?","a":"Anterior.","options":["Lateral","Posterior","Anterior","Superior"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Anterior, also called ventral, means toward the front of the body. In anatomical position the face, chest, and palms are on the anterior side. Its opposite is posterior, or dorsal, toward the back.","notes":["Lateral means away from the midline, toward the side. It would fit a wound on the side of the body.","Posterior, or dorsal, means toward the back of the body. It is the opposite of what the question describes, so pick it for a wound on the back.","Correct. Anterior means toward the front.","Superior means toward the head, or above. It describes vertical position, not front versus back."],"lt":"t-anatomical-terminology","lid":"c16-x2","comp":4,"competencyId":"w1-directional-terms"},{"id":"L00061","dok":1,"q":"Which directional term means near the center of a structure?","a":"Central.","options":["Medial","Central","Peripheral","Proximal"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Central means near the center of a structure, and its opposite, peripheral, means away from that center. The pair is used for the central and outer parts of an organ, and it is the idea behind the names central and peripheral nervous system. Unlike medial, which always refers to the body's midline, central refers to the center of whatever structure is being described.","notes":["Medial means toward the midline of the whole body. It is close in meaning, but it always uses the body's midline as the reference, not the center of a particular structure.","Correct. Central means near the center of a structure.","Peripheral is the opposite of central and means away from the center of a structure. Pick it for a question asking which term means toward the outer edge.","Proximal means closer to where a limb attaches to the trunk. It describes position along a limb rather than nearness to a center."],"lt":"t-anatomical-terminology","lid":"c17-x2","comp":4,"competencyId":"w1-directional-terms"},{"id":"L00062","dok":2,"q":"The trachea runs down the front of the neck, in front of the vertebral column. Which term completes the sentence: the trachea is ___ to the spine?","a":"Ventral.","options":["Lateral","Ventral","Inferior","Dorsal"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Ventral, another word for anterior, means toward the front of the body. The trachea lies in front of the vertebral column, so the trachea is ventral to the spine. Turned around, the same relationship reads as the spine being dorsal to the trachea.","notes":["Lateral means away from the midline. The trachea and the spine both sit on the midline, so lateral does not describe how they relate.","Correct. The trachea is ventral, or anterior, to the spine.","Inferior means below, toward the feet. The trachea and the spine run side by side down the neck, so the relationship is front to back, not above and below.","Dorsal means toward the back. It describes the spine relative to the trachea, so it is the mirror-image answer."],"lt":"t-anatomical-terminology","lid":"c18-x2","comp":4,"competencyId":"w1-directional-terms"},{"id":"L00063","dok":1,"q":"A section is cut through the body at an angle other than 90 degrees, so it is neither purely horizontal nor purely vertical. Which plane produced it?","a":"An oblique plane.","options":["Parasagittal plane","Transverse plane","Frontal plane","Oblique plane"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"An oblique plane passes through the body at an angle other than 90 degrees, somewhere between horizontal and vertical. The portions it produces are angled rather than cleanly superior and inferior, or anterior and posterior. It is the one plane defined by its angle rather than by the parts it separates.","notes":["A parasagittal plane is vertical and off the midline, giving unequal left and right parts. It would fit a vertical cut placed to one side.","The transverse plane is horizontal and divides the body into superior and inferior portions. Pick it for a flat cross section, such as a CT slice.","The frontal plane is vertical and divides the body into anterior and posterior portions. It would be correct for a vertical cut separating front from back.","Correct. An angled cut is an oblique section."],"lt":"t-anatomical-terminology","lid":"c19-x2","comp":3,"competencyId":"w1-planes-sections"},{"id":"L00064","dok":2,"q":"A lesion on the left side of the brain causes weakness on the right side of the body. Which term describes the relationship between the lesion and the weakness?","a":"Contralateral.","options":["Ipsilateral","Bilateral","Intermediate","Contralateral"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Contralateral means on the opposite side of the body. A lesion on the left side of the brain with weakness on the right side of the body is a contralateral relationship. The term matters in neurology because a lesion on one side of the brain often shows its effect on the opposite side of the body.","notes":["Ipsilateral means on the same side of the body. It would fit a left-sided lesion causing left-sided weakness.","Bilateral means on both sides of the body. Pick it if the weakness affected the left and right sides together.","Intermediate describes a structure lying between a medial and a lateral structure. It does not describe a left-to-right relationship between two findings.","Correct. Opposite sides are contralateral."],"lt":"t-anatomical-terminology","lid":"c20-x2","comp":4,"competencyId":"w1-directional-terms"},{"id":"L00065","dok":1,"q":"Which regional term names the breast region?","a":"Mammary.","options":["Mammary","Axillary","Sternal","Thoracic"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Mammary names the breast region on the anterior chest. The word is related to the mammary glands, which lie in the breast. It is one of several named regions inside the larger thoracic region.","notes":["Correct. Mammary is the breast region.","Axillary names the armpit, lateral to the breast region. Choose it for a question about the armpit.","Sternal names the area over the breastbone in the middle of the chest, medial to the mammary regions. Pick it for the breastbone area.","Thoracic names the chest as a whole. It contains the mammary regions, so it is broader than the question asks for."],"lt":"t-anatomical-terminology","lid":"c21-x2","comp":5,"competencyId":"w1-regional-terms"},{"id":"L00066","dok":2,"q":"Which regional term names the spinal column area, and which division of the body does it belong to?","a":"Vertebral, an axial region.","options":["Sural, an axial region","Brachial, an axial region","Vertebral, an appendicular region","Vertebral, an axial region"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Vertebral names the area over the spinal column on the back. The axial region is the head, neck, and trunk, and the spine runs down the center of the trunk, so vertebral is an axial term. The appendicular region is reserved for the limbs.","notes":["Sural names the calf, which is part of the lower limb and therefore appendicular. Pick sural for a question about the calf.","Brachial names the arm, between the shoulder and the elbow, and the arm is part of a limb. It is appendicular, so both halves of this choice are wrong.","Vertebral is the right term, but the spine is part of the trunk, which is axial. This choice confuses the two divisions.","Correct. Vertebral is the spinal column area, part of the axial region."],"lt":"t-anatomical-terminology","lid":"c22-x2","comp":5,"competencyId":"w1-regional-terms"},{"id":"L00067","dok":2,"q":"Antecubital and olecranal both name parts of the elbow. Which surfaces do they describe?","a":"Antecubital is the front, olecranal is the back.","options":["Antecubital is the front, olecranal is the back","Both describe the back of the elbow","Both describe the front of the elbow","Antecubital is the back, olecranal is the front"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Antecubital names the front of the elbow, the crease where blood is often drawn. Olecranal names the back of the elbow, over the olecranon of the ulna. Together they are a front-and-back pair for one joint, much like patellar and popliteal at the knee.","notes":["Correct. Antecubital is anterior and olecranal is posterior.","Only olecranal is on the back. Antecubital names the front of the elbow.","Only antecubital is on the front. Olecranal names the bony point at the back of the elbow.","This reverses the two terms. The prefix ante means in front, which is a quick way to remember that antecubital is the front."],"lt":"t-anatomical-terminology","lid":"c24-x2","comp":5,"competencyId":"w1-regional-terms"},{"id":"L00068","dok":2,"q":"Organs are built from smaller units. Which level of organization sits one step below the organ level?","a":"The tissue level.","options":["The tissue level","The organismal level","The cellular level","The chemical level"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"An organ is a structure made of two or more tissue types with a defined job, so the tissue level sits directly below it. The stomach, for example, combines epithelial, connective, muscle, and nervous tissue. Each level is assembled from the level just beneath it.","notes":["Correct. Organs are built from tissues.","The organismal level is the whole person, at the top of the hierarchy. It sits above the organ system level, not below the organ level.","The cellular level is two steps below the organ level, because cells first group into tissues. It would be one step below the tissue level.","The chemical level is the bottom of the hierarchy, three steps below organs. Pick it for the smallest level overall."],"lt":"t-anatomical-terminology","lid":"c25-x2","comp":1,"competencyId":"w1-levels-organization"},{"id":"L00069","dok":3,"q":"A chart reads: wound on the posterior, distal forearm. Where on the body is the wound?","a":"On the back of the forearm, near the wrist.","options":["On the front of the forearm, near the wrist","On the back of the forearm, near the elbow","On the front of the forearm, near the elbow","On the back of the forearm, near the wrist"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Posterior means the back surface, and distal means farther from the limb's attachment to the trunk, which on the forearm is the wrist end. Combining the two places the wound on the back of the forearm near the wrist. Reading one term at a time keeps the location from getting scrambled.","notes":["Near the wrist matches distal, but the front of the forearm is anterior. This would fit an anterior, distal wound.","The back of the forearm matches posterior, but near the elbow is proximal. This would fit a posterior, proximal wound.","This reverses both terms: the front is anterior and near the elbow is proximal. It would fit a wound charted as anterior, proximal forearm.","Correct. Back of the forearm, near the wrist."],"lt":"t-anatomical-terminology","lid":"c26-x2","comp":4,"competencyId":"w1-directional-terms"},{"id":"L00070","dok":2,"q":"A frontal section through the knee divides it into anterior and posterior portions. The patellar region falls on the anterior portion. Which region falls on the posterior portion?","a":"The popliteal region.","options":["Femoral","Popliteal","Sural","Crural"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"A frontal section separates front from back. At the knee, the front is the patellar region over the kneecap, and the back is the popliteal region, the hollow behind the knee. Thinking of regional terms in front-and-back pairs makes questions about sections much easier.","notes":["Femoral names the thigh, above the knee. It would be cut by a frontal section through the thigh, not the knee.","Correct. The popliteal region is the back of the knee.","Sural names the calf, which is below the knee on the back of the leg. It is posterior, but it is not part of the knee, so it is not where this cut lands.","Crural names the leg, the shin area below the knee. It is on the front of the leg, below the level of this cut."],"lt":"t-anatomical-terminology","lid":"c27-x2","comp":5,"competencyId":"w1-regional-terms"},{"id":"L00071","dok":2,"q":"A patient points to pain in the calf, just below the hollow behind the knee. Which pair of regional terms best describes the site?","a":"The sural region, just below the popliteal region.","options":["Sural region, just below the popliteal region","Plantar region, just below the calcaneal region","Crural region, just below the patellar region","Femoral region, just above the popliteal region"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Sural names the calf, the fleshy back of the leg, and popliteal names the hollow behind the knee. The pain sits on the back of the leg directly below the knee, so it is in the sural region just below the popliteal region. Using two neighboring regional terms pins down the location precisely.","notes":["Correct. The calf is sural, below the popliteal hollow.","Plantar is the sole of the foot and calcaneal is the heel, both far below the calf. This pair would fit pain on the bottom of the foot.","Crural and patellar are both on the front of the leg and knee. This pair would describe pain on the shin just below the kneecap.","Femoral is the thigh, which is above the knee, not below it. This pair would fit pain on the back of the thigh."],"lt":"t-anatomical-terminology","lid":"c28-x2","comp":5,"competencyId":"w1-regional-terms"},{"id":"L00072","dok":2,"q":"A clinician records that a structure on the patient's right is contralateral to a finding. On which side of the patient is the finding?","a":"On the patient's left side.","options":["On both sides of the patient","On the patient's left side","On the patient's midline","On the patient's right side"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Contralateral means on the opposite side of the body. If a structure on the right is contralateral to the finding, the finding must be on the left. Both sides are named from the patient's own point of view.","notes":["Findings on both sides would be described as bilateral. Contralateral points to one specific opposite side.","Correct. Opposite the right side is the left side.","A finding on the midline is neither right nor left, so it cannot be on the opposite side from anything. Contralateral requires two separate sides.","The patient's right side would make the two ipsilateral, on the same side. Pick it if the note had said ipsilateral."],"lt":"t-anatomical-terminology","lid":"c29-x2","comp":4,"competencyId":"w1-directional-terms"},{"id":"L00073","dok":3,"q":"A patient lies prone on the exam table, and a wound on the back of the thigh faces the ceiling. Which directional term correctly describes the surface of the thigh with the wound?","a":"Posterior.","options":["Anterior","Posterior","Inferior","Superior"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Directional terms are always given as if the body were in anatomical position, no matter how the patient is lying. In anatomical position the back of the thigh faces backward, so the wound is on the posterior surface. The fact that it faces the ceiling while the patient is prone does not change its name.","notes":["Anterior is the front of the thigh. It would be correct if the wound were on the front of the thigh, which faces the table when the patient is prone.","Correct. The back of the thigh is the posterior surface in any position.","Inferior means toward the feet. It describes vertical position along the body, not which surface of the thigh is involved.","Superior is tempting because the wound faces up while the patient lies prone. In anatomical terms, though, superior means toward the head, not toward the ceiling."],"lt":"t-anatomical-terminology","lid":"c30-x2","comp":2,"competencyId":"w1-anatomical-position"},{"id":"L00074","dok":2,"q":"Which of these is a sign rather than a symptom?","a":"A fever measured with a thermometer.","options":["Fatigue the patient reports","Pain the patient rates as severe","A fever measured with a thermometer","Nausea the patient describes"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"A sign is objective, something a clinician can observe or measure, such as a temperature reading, a visible rash, or a fast pulse. A symptom is subjective, the patient's own experience that an observer cannot measure directly. A fever recorded with a thermometer is measured, so it is a sign.","notes":["Fatigue is the patient's own sense of tiredness. It is reported rather than measured, so it is a symptom.","Pain is experienced and described by the patient, so it is the classic example of a symptom. Even when the patient rates it on a scale, the rating is still the patient's own report, not a measurement.","Correct. A measured fever is an objective sign.","Nausea is something the patient feels and reports, and no one else can measure it directly. That makes it a symptom, not a sign."],"lt":"t-anatomical-terminology","lid":"d3-t-anatomical-terminology-1"},{"id":"L00075","dok":2,"q":"A surgical note should say a structure is superior to the heart rather than above the heart. Why is superior the safer word?","a":"Above changes with how the body is lying, while superior always means toward the head in anatomical position.","options":["Above shifts with body position, but superior always means toward the head","Above and superior mean opposite things in anatomy","Above is the formal anatomical term, and superior is informal","Superior is used only on the trunk, while above is used only on the limbs"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"In everyday speech, above depends on how the body is oriented, so for a patient lying supine on the table it becomes unclear. Superior is defined from anatomical position and always means toward the head, whatever position the patient is in. That fixed meaning is why anatomical terms are used in charts and surgical notes.","notes":["Correct. Superior keeps one meaning in every position.","Above and superior point the same way when a person is standing upright. The problem with above is that its meaning shifts with position, not that it means the opposite.","This reverses the two words. Superior is the formal anatomical term, and above is the everyday word.","Superior and inferior can be used anywhere on the body, trunk included. The limb-only pair is proximal and distal, which is a different rule."],"lt":"t-anatomical-terminology","lid":"d3-t-anatomical-terminology-2","comp":2,"competencyId":"w1-anatomical-position"},{"id":"L00077","dok":2,"q":"Two students argue about whether the thumb is medial or lateral. Why is the thumb the lateral digit of the hand?","a":"With the palms facing forward in anatomical position, the thumb points away from the midline.","options":["The thumb is lateral because it is the shortest digit","Medial and lateral cannot be used to describe the hand","The thumb is lateral only when the palm faces backward","With the palms facing forward, the thumb points away from the midline"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Medial and lateral are judged with the body in anatomical position, where the palms face forward. In that position the thumb points away from the midline, so it is the lateral digit and the little finger is the medial digit. If the hand is allowed to rotate, the thumb can appear to move toward the midline, which is exactly why the fixed reference posture is used.","notes":["Length has nothing to do with medial and lateral, which describe position relative to the midline. The thumb is lateral because of where it sits, not because of its size.","Medial and lateral apply to every part of the body, including the hand. The thumb side of the hand is lateral and the little-finger side is medial.","When the palm faces backward the forearm bones cross, and the thumb points toward the body. That is the position that makes students think the thumb is medial, so it is the source of the argument, not the answer.","Correct. Palms forward places the thumb on the lateral side."],"lt":"t-anatomical-terminology","lid":"d3-t-anatomical-terminology-4","comp":4,"competencyId":"w1-directional-terms"},{"id":"L00078","dok":3,"q":"A radiologist wants one image that shows both lungs side by side along their full height, from their tops down to the diaphragm. Which plane should the image be cut in?","a":"A frontal (coronal) plane.","options":["A transverse plane","A frontal (coronal) plane","A midsagittal plane","A parasagittal plane"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"A frontal plane runs vertically from side to side, so a single frontal section can show the right and left lungs together along their full height, from the top of each lung down to the base resting on the diaphragm. A transverse slice also shows both lungs, but only at one horizontal level at a time. The view the radiologist needs decides the plane.","notes":["A transverse plane is horizontal, so each slice shows both lungs at only one level, like a CT image. Pick it if the goal were a cross section of the chest at a single height.","Correct. A frontal section shows both lungs from top to diaphragm.","A midsagittal plane runs down the midline and passes between the lungs, through the heart and mediastinum, so it shows neither lung well. It would suit a side view of the heart, trachea, and spine.","A parasagittal plane is vertical and can show one lung from top to bottom, but because it lies to one side it cannot show both lungs together. Pick it for a side view of a single lung."],"lt":"t-anatomical-terminology","lid":"d3-t-anatomical-terminology-5","comp":3,"competencyId":"w1-planes-sections"},{"id":"L00079","dok":2,"q":"Irritable bowel syndrome disrupts normal bowel function but has no clear structural abnormality and no single identifiable cause. How does the course classify it?","a":"As a disorder.","options":["A disease","An infectious condition","A disorder","An acute condition"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"In the course's terms, a disorder is a disruption of normal function that often lacks a single clear cause. A disease has identifiable physical or biochemical changes, a predictable course, and an identifiable cause. Irritable bowel syndrome has no clear structural abnormality, so it fits the definition of a disorder, and the notes use it as an example of a functional bowel disorder.","notes":["A disease has an identifiable cause and clear physical or biochemical changes, such as tuberculosis caused by a specific bacterium. Pick disease for a condition like that, with a known cause and predictable course.","An infectious condition is caused by a microorganism, as influenza and tuberculosis are. Nothing in the description points to an infection, and the lack of a single identifiable cause argues against one.","Correct. Irritable bowel syndrome is a disorder.","An acute condition has a sudden onset and a short duration, like appendicitis. Irritable bowel syndrome tends to be long lasting, so acute does not fit."],"lt":"t-anatomical-terminology","lid":"d3-t-anatomical-terminology-6"},{"id":"L00080","dok":2,"q":"Complete the lab sentence using a directional term: the nose is ___ to the cheekbones.","a":"Medial.","options":["Proximal","Deep","Lateral","Medial"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The nose sits on the midline of the face, and the cheekbones lie on either side of it. Medial means toward the midline, so the nose is medial to the cheekbones. Reversing the sentence, the cheekbones are lateral to the nose.","notes":["Proximal is reserved for the limbs, meaning closer to where a limb attaches to the trunk. It is not used to compare structures on the face.","Deep means farther from the body surface. The nose and cheekbones are compared side to side here, not by depth.","Lateral means away from the midline. It describes the cheekbones relative to the nose, so it is the mirror-image answer.","Correct. The nose is medial to the cheekbones."],"lt":"t-anatomical-terminology","lid":"d3-t-anatomical-terminology-7","comp":4,"competencyId":"w1-directional-terms"},{"id":"L00244","dok":1,"q":"How many basic tissue types build every organ in the body?","a":"Four.","options":["Six","Three","Four","Five"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Every organ in the body is built from four basic tissue types: epithelial, connective, muscle, and nervous tissue. Each has a general job: epithelium covers and lines, connective tissue supports and binds, muscle moves, and nervous tissue senses and signals. Organs differ in how much of each tissue they contain and how the tissues are arranged.","notes":["Six overcounts even more, usually by splitting cartilage, bone, and blood away from connective tissue. All of those belong to the connective tissue family.","Three is the number of embryonic germ layers (ectoderm, mesoderm, and endoderm), which give rise to the tissues. Students pick it when they mix up germ layers with tissue types.","Correct. Epithelial, connective, muscle, and nervous tissue.","Five overcounts. Students sometimes count blood or bone as separate types, but both are kinds of connective tissue."],"lt":"t-tissues","lid":"c1","comp":1,"competencyId":"w1-levels-organization"},{"id":"L00296","dok":1,"q":"What is a tissue?","a":"A group of similar cells that share a structure and a job.","options":["Two or more organs working together on one job","The fluid that surrounds the body's organs","A group of similar cells sharing a structure and a job","A single cell that carries out many different jobs"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"A tissue is a group of similar cells that share a structure and work together on a specific job. Tissues are one level of organization above cells and one level below organs, which are built from two or more tissue types. Epithelial, connective, muscle, and nervous tissue are the four basic kinds.","notes":["Two or more organs working together form an organ system, such as the digestive system. That is two levels above tissue.","The fluid around cells and organs, such as interstitial fluid, is not made of cells, so it is not a tissue. Blood is different because it contains cells in a fluid matrix, which is why it counts as a connective tissue.","Correct. A tissue is a group of similar cells with a shared structure and job.","A single cell is the smallest living unit, one level below tissue. Many similar cells working together are needed to form a tissue."],"lt":"t-tissues","lid":"c1-x2","comp":1,"competencyId":"w1-levels-organization"},{"id":"N-a17d-1-1","dok":2,"q":"Which order lists these levels from least to most complex: tissue, organ system, cellular, organ?","a":"Cellular, tissue, organ, organ system.","options":["Tissue, cellular, organ, organ system.","Cellular, organ, tissue, organ system.","Organ system, organ, tissue, cellular.","Cellular, tissue, organ, organ system."],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Each level is built from the one before it. Cells group into tissues, two or more tissue types form an organ, and organs working together form an organ system. The chemical level sits below all of these and the organismal level above them.","notes":["Tissue cannot come before cellular, because a tissue is a group of similar cells. The cell is the smallest living unit, so it comes first.","Organ cannot come before tissue, because an organ is made of two or more tissue types. The tissue has to exist before the organ can be built.","This runs from most to least complex, the reverse of what was asked. It would be right if the question asked for the order from the organ system down to the cell.","Correct. Cells build tissues, tissues build organs, and organs work together as an organ system."],"comp":1,"competencyId":"w1-levels-organization"},{"id":"N-a17d-1-2","dok":3,"q":"A student looks at a slide of stratified squamous epithelium from the skin. She then steps back to the whole skin, which also contains connective tissue, muscle, and nerves. Which two levels of organization did she move between?","a":"From the tissue level to the organ level.","options":["From the cellular level to the tissue level.","From the tissue level to the organ level.","From the organ level to the organ system level.","From the chemical level to the cellular level."],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Stratified squamous epithelium is a group of similar cells doing one job, so it is a tissue. The skin is built from two or more tissue types with a defined job, which makes it an organ. Moving from one epithelium to the whole skin is a step from the tissue level to the organ level.","notes":["The slide already shows a sheet of many similar cells, which is a tissue, not single cells. This pair would fit if she moved from one keratinocyte to the whole epithelium.","Correct. The epithelium is one tissue, and the skin made of several tissue types is an organ.","The whole skin is the organ, not the organ system. The organ system level would be the skin together with hair, nails, and glands as the integumentary system.","Neither view is that small. The chemical level is atoms and molecules such as water or a protein, and the cellular level is a single cell such as a keratinocyte."],"comp":1,"competencyId":"w1-levels-organization"},{"id":"N-a17d-1-3","dok":3,"q":"A disease damages keratinocytes, the cells of the epidermis. Each level of organization is built from the one before it. Which levels are affected next, moving upward?","a":"The epithelial tissue, then the skin, then the integumentary system.","options":["The epithelial tissue, then the skin, then the integumentary system.","The atoms and molecules inside the keratinocytes, and nothing above them.","Only the keratinocytes, because damage stays at the level where it starts.","The whole person directly, skipping the tissue and organ levels between."],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Because each level is built from the one below it, a failure at a lower level carries upward. Keratinocytes build stratified squamous epithelium, that tissue is part of the skin as an organ, and the skin belongs to the integumentary system. The damage moves up through each level in order.","notes":["Correct. Damaged cells weaken the tissue they build, then the organ, then the organ system.","This moves downward instead of upward. The chemical level sits below the cell, so it is what the cell is made of, not what is built from the cell.","A failure at a lower level always has consequences above it, because the higher levels are made from the lower ones. Damage cannot stay sealed at the cell level.","Levels are not skipped. The person is affected only through the tissue, organ, and organ system that the damaged cells help build."],"comp":1,"competencyId":"w1-levels-organization"},{"id":"N-a17d-1-4","dok":3,"q":"The stomach wall contains epithelium, connective tissue, smooth muscle, and nervous tissue. Which level is the stomach, and which level does it form together with the liver, pancreas, and the rest of the alimentary canal?","a":"It is an organ, and together they form an organ system.","options":["It is a tissue, and together they form an organ.","It is an organ system, and together they form the organism.","It is an organ, and together they form the organism.","It is an organ, and together they form an organ system."],"correctIndex":3,"why":"An organ is a structure built from two or more tissue types with a defined job, and the stomach has all four tissue types. Organs that work together toward one broad function form an organ system, so the stomach, liver, pancreas, and alimentary canal together form the digestive system.","notes":["A tissue is one group of similar cells, but the stomach holds four tissue types. It would be right for the smooth muscle layer alone.","The stomach by itself is one organ, not a whole system. The digestive system is the organ system it belongs to, and the organism is all the systems together.","The stomach is an organ, but the digestive organs together form one organ system. The organism is every organ system together, the whole person.","Correct. Four tissue types make the stomach an organ, and the digestive organs together form an organ system."],"comp":1,"competencyId":"w1-levels-organization"},{"id":"N-a17d-2-1","dok":1,"q":"In anatomical position, where are the arms?","a":"At the sides of the body.","options":["Raised above the head.","Crossed over the chest.","At the sides of the body.","Held straight out to the sides."],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Anatomical position is the body erect, feet flat and slightly apart, head and eyes facing forward, arms at the sides, and palms facing forward. Every directional term is read from this one posture.","notes":["Raised arms are not part of the standard posture. Arms overhead would change where the limbs sit, which is why one fixed posture is used.","Crossed arms would turn the palms inward and hide the front of the trunk. In anatomical position the arms hang at the sides.","Correct. The arms hang at the sides with the palms facing forward.","Arms held straight out are a different pose. In anatomical position they rest at the sides with the palms forward."],"comp":2,"competencyId":"w1-anatomical-position"},{"id":"N-a17d-2-2","dok":3,"q":"A patient stands with arms at the sides and the palms turned toward the thighs, so the thumbs point forward. Which term describes the thumb relative to the little finger, and why does this pose not change the answer?","a":"Lateral, because directional terms are always read from anatomical position.","options":["Lateral, because directional terms are always read from anatomical position.","Anterior, because the thumb points forward in the patient's current pose.","Lateral, because directional terms are read from the examiner's point of view.","Medial, because the thumb is closer to the midline whenever the palm turns inward."],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Directional terms always assume anatomical position, with the palms facing forward. In that position the thumb sits on the side away from the midline, so it is lateral to the little finger. How the patient happens to be holding the hands does not change the description.","notes":["Correct. With the palms forward in anatomical position, the thumb is farther from the midline.","This describes the hand in the patient's current pose, not in anatomical position. Directional terms never change with how a person is actually standing, and in anatomical position the thumb is lateral.","Lateral is right, but the reason is wrong. Terms are given from the patient's perspective in anatomical position, never from the examiner's side.","Turning the palm changes the pose, not the term. In anatomical position the palms face forward and the thumb is lateral."],"comp":2,"competencyId":"w1-anatomical-position"},{"id":"N-a17d-2-3","dok":3,"q":"A patient lies prone. A clinician standing at the patient's left side charts a mole on the side of the back nearest to her. Which side is charted, and what rule decides it?","a":"The patient's left, because left and right always mean the patient's own sides.","options":["The patient's left, because left and right always mean the patient's own sides.","The patient's right, because lying prone reverses the patient's left and right.","The clinician's left, because charts are written from the examiner's view.","No side, because left and right are not used while a patient lies prone."],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Prone means lying face down, on the front. The side of the back nearest a clinician standing at the patient's left is the patient's left side. Right and left are always the patient's own right and left, so the chart reads left no matter where the clinician stands or how the patient lies.","notes":["Correct. The mole is on the patient's own left side, and charts always use the patient's left and right.","Lying prone turns the body over but does not swap its sides. The patient's left arm is still the left arm when face down.","Charts never use the examiner's left and right, because those change every time the examiner moves. They always use the patient's own sides.","Left and right are used in every position. Because they refer to the patient's own body, they work the same whether the patient is supine, prone, or standing."],"comp":2,"competencyId":"w1-anatomical-position"},{"id":"N-a17d-3-1","dok":3,"q":"On a lab model, the thigh is cut so the cut face shows the femur as a single round bone ringed by muscle. Which plane made the cut, and which two parts does it produce?","a":"Transverse, making superior and inferior parts.","options":["Frontal, making anterior and posterior parts.","Transverse, making superior and inferior parts.","Midsagittal, making equal right and left halves.","Parasagittal, making unequal right and left parts."],"correctIndex":1,"why":"A long bone looks like a single round profile only when it is cut straight across its length. That is a horizontal cut, the transverse plane, which divides the body or a limb into superior and inferior parts and produces a cross section.","notes":["A frontal cut runs vertically down the thigh, so the femur would show as a long strip, not a ring. The frontal plane is the one that makes front and back portions.","Correct. Cutting straight across the thigh gives a round cross section and separates upper from lower.","The midsagittal plane runs on the body's midline, which does not pass through a thigh. It would be right for a cut down the center of the head or trunk.","A parasagittal cut runs vertically, so the femur would appear long, not round. It is the plane for a vertical cut off the midline."],"comp":3,"competencyId":"w1-planes-sections"},{"id":"N-a17d-5-1","dok":3,"q":"A cut runs along the anterior upper limb from the antecubital region down to the carpal region. Which region lies between them, and which division of the body does it belong to?","a":"The antebrachial region, in the appendicular division.","options":["The brachial region, in the appendicular division.","The antebrachial region, in the axial division.","The olecranal region, in the axial division.","The antebrachial region, in the appendicular division."],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The antecubital region is the front of the elbow and the carpal region is the wrist. The forearm between them is the antebrachial region. It is part of a limb, and the limbs make up the appendicular region, while the head, neck, and trunk are axial.","notes":["The brachial region is the upper arm, above the elbow, not between the elbow and the wrist. It is appendicular, but it is on the wrong side of the elbow.","Antebrachial is the right region, but the forearm is part of a limb. Limbs are appendicular; axial is the head, neck, and trunk.","The olecranal region is the back of the elbow, not the forearm, and it is part of a limb, so it is appendicular, not axial.","Correct. The forearm lies between elbow and wrist, and as part of a limb it is appendicular."],"comp":5,"competencyId":"w1-regional-terms"},{"id":"N-a17d-5-2","dok":3,"q":"On a lab model, one pin sits on the front of the lower limb just below the patellar region, over the shin. A second pin sits on the back of the lower limb at the same level. Which terms name the two pins?","a":"Crural for the front pin and sural for the back pin.","options":["Crural for the front pin and sural for the back pin.","Femoral for the front pin and popliteal for the back pin.","Sural for the front pin and crural for the back pin.","Crural for the front pin and fibular for the back pin."],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Below the knee, the front of the leg over the shin is the crural region. The back of the leg at the same level is the calf, the sural region. Both lie on the leg between the knee and the ankle, one anterior and one posterior.","notes":["Correct. The shin is crural and the calf behind it is sural.","Both pins sit below the knee. Femoral is the thigh above the knee, and popliteal is the hollow at the back of the knee itself, not the calf below it.","These are reversed. Crural is the shin on the front, and sural is the calf on the back.","Crural is right for the front, but fibular names the lateral side of the leg, not the back. The back of the leg at that level is the calf, the sural region."],"comp":5,"competencyId":"w1-regional-terms"},{"id":"N-a17d-5-3","dok":3,"q":"A chart lists three injuries: buccal, olecranal, and lumbar. How many are in the appendicular region, and which one?","a":"One, the olecranal injury at the back of the elbow.","options":["One, the buccal injury on the cheek.","Two, the olecranal and the lumbar injuries.","One, the olecranal injury at the back of the elbow.","None, because all three are on the trunk or head."],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Buccal is the cheek, olecranal is the back of the elbow, and lumbar is the lower back. The head, neck, and trunk are axial, and the limbs are appendicular. Only the elbow is on a limb, so only the olecranal injury is appendicular.","notes":["The cheek is on the head, which is axial. The buccal injury is axial, and the elbow is the one on a limb.","The lumbar region is the lower back, part of the trunk, so it is axial. Only the olecranal injury is on a limb.","Correct. The elbow is on the upper limb, while the cheek and lower back are axial.","The olecranal region is the back of the elbow, which is on the upper limb. That makes it appendicular, so the count cannot be zero."],"comp":5,"competencyId":"w1-regional-terms"}]},{"id":"m1-cavities","title":"Body cavities, membranes and abdominal quadrants","summary":"dorsal and ventral cavities, serous membranes, abdominopelvic quadrants and nine regions","mw":"2026-08-17","tr":"2026-08-18","cards":[{"id":"L00076","dok":3,"q":"A tumor on a kidney must be reached with as little disturbance to other organs as possible. Why might a surgeon approach the kidney from the back rather than through the front of the abdomen?","a":"The kidney is retroperitoneal, so a posterior approach reaches it without opening the peritoneal cavity.","options":["It is intraperitoneal, so it hangs toward the back on a mesentery","It sits in the thoracic cavity, above the diaphragm","It lies in the pelvic cavity, behind the urinary bladder","It is retroperitoneal, so a back approach avoids the peritoneal cavity"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The kidneys are retroperitoneal, meaning they lie behind the peritoneum, between it and the posterior abdominal wall. Going in through the front would mean opening the peritoneal cavity and moving the organs inside it out of the way. Approaching from the back reaches the kidney more directly, which is why knowing whether an organ is retroperitoneal shapes the surgical plan.","notes":["Intraperitoneal organs are wrapped in visceral peritoneum inside the peritoneal cavity, like the stomach. The kidney is not one of them; it is the K in SAD PUCKER.","The thoracic cavity is above the diaphragm and holds the lungs and heart. The kidneys lie below the diaphragm, against the posterior abdominal wall.","The pelvic cavity holds the urinary bladder, rectum, and internal reproductive organs. The kidneys are higher up, in the abdominal portion against the back wall.","Correct. The kidney lies behind the peritoneum, so a back approach avoids the peritoneal cavity."],"lt":"t-anatomical-terminology","lid":"d3-t-anatomical-terminology-3","comp":9,"competencyId":"w1-peritoneum-relationships"},{"id":"L00081","dok":3,"q":"Why is the abdominopelvic area divided into four quadrants or nine regions rather than described simply as the belly?","a":"Naming a specific zone ties a symptom to the organs that lie there, which narrows the likely cause.","options":["The zones mark where the abdominal muscles attach","Each zone is lined by a different serous membrane","Each zone contains exactly one organ","It ties a symptom to the organs beneath that zone"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Each quadrant or region has known organs beneath it, so placing pain in a specific zone points toward specific organs. Right lower quadrant pain, for example, raises suspicion of the appendix. Quadrants are the quick clinical shorthand, and the nine regions are the more precise version used when a location has to be described exactly.","notes":["The quadrants and regions are drawn by imaginary planes through the navel and other landmarks, not by muscle attachments. They describe where organs lie beneath the surface.","The whole abdominopelvic cavity is lined by one serous membrane, the peritoneum. The zones are drawn on the surface by planes, not defined by membranes.","Most zones contain several organs or parts of organs, and large organs such as the liver span more than one zone. The value of the grid is narrowing the possibilities, not naming a single organ.","Correct. Each zone links a symptom to the organs beneath it."],"lt":"t-anatomical-terminology","lid":"d3-t-anatomical-terminology-8","comp":10,"competencyId":"w1-abdominopelvic-map"},{"id":"L00082","dok":1,"q":"Which cavity encloses the brain?","a":"The cranial cavity.","options":["Vertebral cavity","Cranial cavity","Thoracic cavity","Pleural cavity"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The cranial cavity, formed by the bones of the skull, encloses the brain. It is one of the two parts of the dorsal, or posterior, body cavity. It connects with the vertebral cavity below, and both are lined by the meninges.","notes":["The vertebral cavity, also called the spinal cavity, encloses the spinal cord. It is the other half of the dorsal cavity, so pick it for a question about the spinal cord.","Correct. The cranial cavity holds the brain.","The thoracic cavity holds the lungs and the heart, above the diaphragm. It is part of the ventral cavity.","A pleural cavity is the thin space around each lung, between the two layers of pleura. It would be the answer for a question about the space surrounding a lung."],"lt":"t-body-cavities","lid":"c1","comp":6,"competencyId":"w1-body-cavities"},{"id":"L00083","dok":1,"q":"Which structure separates the thoracic cavity from the abdominopelvic cavity?","a":"The diaphragm.","options":["The mediastinum","The pericardium","The peritoneum","The diaphragm"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The diaphragm is the dome-shaped muscular partition that forms the floor of the thoracic cavity and the roof of the abdominopelvic cavity. It is the most useful landmark in the ventral cavity, because it tells you which membranes and fluids you are dealing with. Above it are the pleurae and pericardium, and below it is the peritoneum.","notes":["The mediastinum is the central compartment of the thoracic cavity, between the two lungs. It divides the thorax side to side, not the thorax from the abdomen.","The pericardium is the sac around the heart. It sits on top of the diaphragm but does not separate the two cavities.","The peritoneum is the serous membrane lining the abdominopelvic cavity. Pick it for a question about the membrane of the abdomen.","Correct. The diaphragm separates the thoracic and abdominopelvic cavities."],"lt":"t-body-cavities","lid":"c2","comp":6,"competencyId":"w1-body-cavities"},{"id":"L00084","dok":1,"q":"Which serous membrane surrounds each lung?","a":"The pleura.","options":["Pleura","Pericardium","Peritoneum","Meninges"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The pleura is the serous membrane of the lungs. Its visceral layer covers each lung, its parietal layer lines the chest wall, and the pleural cavity between them holds pleural fluid. That fluid lets the lungs slide against the chest wall during breathing.","notes":["Correct. The pleura surrounds each lung.","The pericardium is the serous membrane around the heart. Pick it for a question about the membrane that wraps the heart.","The peritoneum is the serous membrane of the abdominopelvic cavity. It would be the answer for the membrane around the abdominal organs.","The meninges cover the brain and spinal cord in the dorsal cavity. They are not one of the three serous membranes of the ventral cavity."],"lt":"t-body-cavities","lid":"c3","comp":7,"competencyId":"w1-serous-membranes"},{"id":"L00085","dok":1,"q":"Which layer of a serous membrane lines the cavity wall?","a":"The parietal layer.","options":["The submucosa","The fibrous layer","The visceral layer","The parietal layer"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Every serous membrane is a double layer folded back on itself. The parietal layer lines the inner body wall, and the visceral layer covers the surface of the organ. The two words keep the same meaning in every cavity: parietal goes with the wall and visceral goes with the organ.","notes":["The submucosa is a connective tissue layer in the wall of the digestive tract. It is not part of a serous membrane.","A fibrous layer belongs only to the pericardium, as the tough outer fibrous pericardium. It is not one of the two layers that every serous membrane has.","The visceral layer covers the surface of the organ, not the wall. Pick it for the layer lying directly on the heart, a lung, or an abdominal organ.","Correct. The parietal layer lines the wall."],"lt":"t-body-cavities","lid":"c4","comp":7,"competencyId":"w1-serous-membranes"},{"id":"L00086","dok":1,"q":"In SAD PUCKER, the memory aid for retroperitoneal structures, what does the letter K stand for?","a":"The kidneys.","options":["The ureters","The pancreas","The colon","The kidneys"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"SAD PUCKER lists the structures that lie behind the peritoneum, and K stands for the kidneys. The kidneys sit against the posterior abdominal wall with peritoneum covering only their front surface. Their ureters, the U, run down from them in the same retroperitoneal space.","notes":["The ureters are the U in SAD PUCKER. They would be the answer for the meaning of U.","The pancreas, except its tail, is the P in SAD PUCKER. Choose it for the meaning of P.","The colon is the C in SAD PUCKER, specifically the ascending and descending colon. Pick it for the meaning of C.","Correct. K is the kidneys."],"lt":"t-body-cavities","lid":"c5","comp":9,"competencyId":"w1-peritoneum-relationships"},{"id":"L00087","dok":1,"q":"Which abdominopelvic quadrant contains the appendix?","a":"The right lower quadrant.","options":["The right lower quadrant","The left upper quadrant","The left lower quadrant","The right upper quadrant"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The appendix hangs from the cecum, the start of the large intestine, in the right lower quadrant. That is why appendicitis classically causes right lower quadrant pain. The quadrants are drawn by a median plane and a transverse plane through the navel.","notes":["Correct. The appendix is in the right lower quadrant.","The left upper quadrant holds the stomach and spleen. Choose it for a question about those organs.","The left lower quadrant holds the descending and sigmoid colon. It would be the answer for those parts of the large intestine.","The right upper quadrant holds the liver and gallbladder. Pick it for a question about those organs."],"lt":"t-body-cavities","lid":"c6","comp":10,"competencyId":"w1-abdominopelvic-map"},{"id":"L00088","dok":1,"q":"Which cavity lies within the mediastinum and surrounds the heart?","a":"The pericardial cavity.","options":["The peritoneal cavity","The cranial cavity","The pericardial cavity","The pleural cavity"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The pericardial cavity is the thin space between the parietal and visceral layers of the serous pericardium, around the heart. It holds pericardial fluid, which reduces friction as the heart beats. The heart and its pericardium sit within the mediastinum, between the two lungs.","notes":["The peritoneal cavity is the space between the layers of peritoneum in the abdomen. Choose it for a question about the abdominal organs.","The cranial cavity holds the brain and is part of the dorsal cavity. It would be the answer for the brain's cavity.","Correct. The pericardial cavity surrounds the heart.","The pleural cavities surround the lungs and lie on either side of the mediastinum, not inside it. Pick a pleural cavity for the space around a lung."],"lt":"t-body-cavities","lid":"c7","comp":6,"competencyId":"w1-body-cavities"},{"id":"L00089","dok":1,"q":"Which of the nine abdominopelvic regions occupies the upper center of the grid?","a":"The epigastric region.","options":["Hypogastric region","Right hypochondriac region","Umbilical region","Epigastric region"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The epigastric region is the upper center box of the nine-region grid, just below the sternum, and its key structure is the stomach. The prefix epi means above, and gastric refers to the stomach. The regions below it in the center column are the umbilical and the hypogastric.","notes":["The hypogastric region is the lower center box and holds the urinary bladder. It would be the answer for the bottom of the center column.","The right hypochondriac region is the upper right box, holding the liver and gallbladder. Choose it for the upper right of the grid.","The umbilical region is the center box, around the navel, and holds much of the small intestine. Pick it for the middle of the grid.","Correct. The epigastric region is the upper center."],"lt":"t-body-cavities","lid":"c8","comp":10,"competencyId":"w1-abdominopelvic-map"},{"id":"L00090","dok":1,"q":"Which of these is the largest body cavity?","a":"The abdominopelvic cavity.","options":["The abdominopelvic cavity","The pericardial cavity","The cranial cavity","The thoracic cavity"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The abdominopelvic cavity, below the diaphragm, is the largest single body cavity. It holds the digestive organs, the urinary bladder, and the internal reproductive organs. It has no wall dividing its abdominal and pelvic parts, which is why it is treated as one cavity.","notes":["Correct. The abdominopelvic cavity is the largest.","The pericardial cavity is the thin space around the heart. It is one of the smallest cavities, a narrow space holding a film of fluid.","The cranial cavity holds only the brain. It is much smaller than either cavity of the ventral body cavity.","The thoracic cavity holds the lungs and heart above the diaphragm. It is large, but smaller than the abdominopelvic cavity below it."],"lt":"t-body-cavities","lid":"c9","comp":6,"competencyId":"w1-body-cavities"},{"id":"L00091","dok":1,"q":"What is the epithelium of a serous membrane called?","a":"Mesothelium.","options":["Epicardium","Mucosa","Endothelium","Mesothelium"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The epithelium of a serous membrane is called mesothelium. It is a thin simple squamous epithelium resting on a layer of loose connective tissue, and it secretes the serous fluid that lets organs glide against the body wall. The pleura, pericardium, and peritoneum are all built this way.","notes":["The epicardium is one specific serous layer, the visceral pericardium on the surface of the heart. It is a named membrane, not the general term for the epithelium of every serous membrane.","A mucosa is a mucous membrane, which lines passages that open to the outside, such as the digestive and respiratory tracts. It is the opposite kind of membrane from a serous membrane.","Endothelium is the simple squamous epithelium that lines the inside of blood vessels and the heart. Pick it for a question about the lining of vessels.","Correct. Serous membranes are covered by mesothelium."],"lt":"t-body-cavities","lid":"c10","comp":7,"competencyId":"w1-serous-membranes"},{"id":"L00092","dok":1,"q":"Which serous membrane belongs to the abdominopelvic cavity?","a":"The peritoneum.","options":["The peritoneum","The pleura","The pericardium","The meninges"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The peritoneum is the serous membrane of the abdominopelvic cavity. Its parietal layer lines the abdominal wall, its visceral layer covers the abdominal organs, and the peritoneal cavity between them holds peritoneal fluid. It is the largest of the three serous membranes.","notes":["Correct. The peritoneum lines the abdominopelvic cavity.","The pleura is the serous membrane of the lungs, above the diaphragm. Pick it for the membrane around a lung.","The pericardium is the serous membrane around the heart. It would be correct for the membrane that wraps the heart.","The meninges cover the brain and spinal cord in the dorsal cavity. They are not one of the serous membranes of the ventral cavity."],"lt":"t-body-cavities","lid":"c11","comp":7,"competencyId":"w1-serous-membranes"},{"id":"L00093","dok":2,"q":"The cranial and vertebral cavities together make up which major body cavity?","a":"The dorsal (posterior) cavity.","options":["The ventral (anterior) cavity","The thoracic cavity","The dorsal (posterior) cavity","The abdominopelvic cavity"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The dorsal, or posterior, cavity has two subdivisions: the cranial cavity holding the brain and the vertebral cavity holding the spinal cord. The two are continuous through the large opening at the base of the skull, and both are lined by the meninges. The other major cavity, the ventral cavity, holds the organs of the chest and abdomen.","notes":["The ventral, or anterior, cavity is the other major cavity, made of the thoracic and abdominopelvic cavities. Pick it for a question about the cavities holding the chest and abdominal organs.","The thoracic cavity is one subdivision of the ventral cavity, holding the lungs and heart. It is not a major cavity that contains the cranial and vertebral cavities.","Correct. The cranial and vertebral cavities form the dorsal cavity.","The abdominopelvic cavity is the other subdivision of the ventral cavity, below the diaphragm. It does not include the cranial or vertebral cavities."],"lt":"t-body-cavities","lid":"c12","comp":6,"competencyId":"w1-body-cavities"},{"id":"L00094","dok":2,"q":"Which layer of a serous membrane covers the outer surface of an organ?","a":"The visceral layer.","options":["The visceral layer","The fibrous pericardium","The parietal layer","The mucosa"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The visceral layer of a serous membrane lies directly on the surface of the organ. The word visceral refers to the viscera, the internal organs. The parietal layer is its partner, lining the body wall, and the serous fluid between them lets the organ move without friction.","notes":["Correct. The visceral layer covers the organ.","The fibrous pericardium is the tough outer sac around the heart, made of dense connective tissue. It surrounds the serous pericardium but is not the layer touching the heart.","The parietal layer lines the inner body wall, not the organ. Pick it for the layer against the chest wall or abdominal wall.","The mucosa is the lining of a tube that opens to the outside, such as the stomach or trachea. It lines the inside of a hollow organ, not its outer surface."],"lt":"t-body-cavities","lid":"c13","comp":7,"competencyId":"w1-serous-membranes"},{"id":"L00095","dok":2,"q":"Which two cavities are continuous, with no wall dividing them?","a":"The abdominal and pelvic cavities.","options":["The pleural and pericardial cavities","The abdominal and pelvic cavities","The thoracic and abdominal cavities","The cranial and thoracic cavities"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The abdominal and pelvic cavities are two named parts of one continuous space, the abdominopelvic cavity. No membrane or muscle separates them; the pelvic part is simply the lower portion cradled by the bony pelvis. That is why fluid or infection in the abdomen can settle into the pelvis.","notes":["The pleural and pericardial cavities are separate serous spaces, one around each lung and one around the heart. Keeping them separate means a problem in one does not automatically spread to the others.","Correct. The abdominal and pelvic cavities form one continuous space.","The thoracic and abdominal cavities are separated by the diaphragm. Pick this pair for a question about which cavities the diaphragm divides.","The cranial cavity belongs to the dorsal cavity and the thoracic cavity to the ventral cavity, so they are entirely separate. The cranial cavity is continuous with the vertebral cavity instead."],"lt":"t-body-cavities","lid":"c14","comp":6,"competencyId":"w1-body-cavities"},{"id":"L00097","dok":2,"q":"In the course's description of the mediastinum, which structure forms its posterior boundary?","a":"The vertebral column.","options":["The sternum","The diaphragm","The vertebral column","The lungs"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The mediastinum is the central compartment of the thoracic cavity, between the two lungs. Its boundaries are the thoracic inlet superiorly, the diaphragm inferiorly, the sternum anteriorly, the vertebral column posteriorly, and the lungs laterally. The vertebral column forms the back wall of this compartment.","notes":["The sternum forms the anterior boundary of the mediastinum. Pick it for the front wall.","The diaphragm forms the inferior boundary of the mediastinum. It would be the answer for the floor of the compartment.","Correct. The vertebral column is the posterior boundary.","The lungs form the lateral boundaries, one on each side. Choose them for a question about the side walls of the mediastinum."],"lt":"t-body-cavities","lid":"c16","comp":8,"competencyId":"w1-mediastinum"},{"id":"L00099","dok":2,"q":"Which plane separates the superior mediastinum from the inferior mediastinum?","a":"The transverse thoracic plane.","options":["The transverse thoracic plane","The transumbilical plane","The median plane","The midclavicular plane"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The transverse thoracic plane is a horizontal plane passing from the sternal angle, the ridge where the manubrium meets the body of the sternum, back to the disc between the fourth and fifth thoracic vertebrae. Everything above it is the superior mediastinum and everything below it is the inferior mediastinum. Because the plane is horizontal, it divides the compartment into upper and lower parts.","notes":["Correct. The transverse thoracic plane divides superior from inferior mediastinum.","The transumbilical plane is the horizontal plane through the navel used to draw the abdominal quadrants. It is far below the thorax, in the abdomen.","The median plane is vertical and runs down the midline, dividing right from left. The mediastinum itself lies along this plane, so it does not separate upper from lower parts.","A midclavicular plane is a vertical line through the middle of each clavicle, used to draw the nine abdominopelvic regions. Being vertical, it cannot divide the mediastinum into upper and lower parts."],"lt":"t-body-cavities","lid":"c18","comp":8,"competencyId":"w1-mediastinum"},{"id":"L00100","dok":2,"q":"In the course's version of SAD PUCKER, the letter D stands for which segments of the duodenum?","a":"The second and third segments.","options":["The first and fourth segments","The second and third segments","The first and second segments","The third and fourth segments"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The duodenum, the first part of the small intestine, curves around the head of the pancreas. In the course notes, D in SAD PUCKER stands for the second and third segments of the duodenum, which lie behind the peritoneum. The first segment, right after the stomach, is wrapped in peritoneum and stays more mobile.","notes":["The first segment is intraperitoneal, so this pair cannot be correct. Students often pick it by mixing up which end of the duodenum is the exception.","Correct. D stands for the second and third segments.","The first segment, just past the stomach, is the part of the duodenum that is not retroperitoneal. Any pair that includes it cannot be what D stands for.","The third segment is retroperitoneal, but this pair leaves out the second segment, which is also retroperitoneal and part of D. The course's version of the memory aid names the second and third segments."],"lt":"t-body-cavities","lid":"c19","comp":9,"competencyId":"w1-peritoneum-relationships"},{"id":"L00101","dok":2,"q":"Which abdominopelvic region lies directly below the umbilical region, in the lower center of the grid?","a":"The hypogastric (pubic) region.","options":["The left lumbar region","The right iliac region","The hypogastric (pubic) region","The epigastric region"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The hypogastric region, also called the pubic region, is the lower center box of the nine-region grid, directly below the umbilical region. The prefix hypo means below, so it is the region below the stomach area. Its key structure is the urinary bladder.","notes":["The left lumbar region is the middle left box, beside the umbilical region, and holds the descending colon. It is on the side of the grid, not below the center.","The right iliac region is the lower right box, holding the cecum and appendix. It is lateral, not in the center column.","Correct. The hypogastric region is directly below the umbilical region.","The epigastric region is the upper center box, above the umbilical region. Pick it for the region holding the stomach."],"lt":"t-body-cavities","lid":"c20","comp":10,"competencyId":"w1-abdominopelvic-map"},{"id":"L00102","dok":2,"q":"Which membranes line both the cranial cavity and the vertebral cavity?","a":"The meninges.","options":["The pleura","The meninges","The peritoneum","The pericardium"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The cranial and vertebral cavities form the dorsal cavity, one continuous space from the brain down the spinal cord. The meninges line this whole space, wrapping the brain and spinal cord together. Inflammation of these membranes is meningitis.","notes":["The pleura is the serous membrane around the lungs, in the thoracic cavity. Pick it for a question about the lungs.","Correct. The meninges line the dorsal cavity.","The peritoneum is the serous membrane of the abdominopelvic cavity. It would be correct for the membrane around the abdominal organs.","The pericardium is the membrane around the heart. Choose it for a question about the heart's sac."],"lt":"t-body-cavities","lid":"c21","comp":6,"competencyId":"w1-body-cavities"},{"id":"L00104","dok":2,"q":"In the four-quadrant scheme, which plane divides the abdomen into right and left?","a":"The median plane.","options":["A transverse plane through the umbilicus","A frontal plane","The median plane","A parasagittal plane"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Two planes draw the quadrants: the median, or midsagittal, plane and a transverse plane through the umbilicus. The median plane is vertical on the midline, so it splits the abdomen into right and left halves. The transverse plane through the navel then splits each half into upper and lower quadrants.","notes":["The transverse plane through the umbilicus is the other quadrant plane, but it divides upper from lower. Pick it for the plane that separates the upper quadrants from the lower ones.","A frontal plane separates front from back. It is not used to draw the quadrants, which are marked on the front of the abdomen.","Correct. The median plane divides right from left.","A parasagittal plane is vertical but lies off the midline, so it would split the abdomen into unequal parts. The quadrants use the midline itself."],"lt":"t-body-cavities","lid":"c23","comp":10,"competencyId":"w1-abdominopelvic-map"},{"id":"L00105","dok":2,"q":"On a transverse section of the abdomen, the kidneys lie behind the parietal peritoneum, pressed against the posterior abdominal wall. How are they classified?","a":"Retroperitoneal.","options":["Pelvic","Intraperitoneal","Mediastinal","Retroperitoneal"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The retroperitoneum is the area behind the peritoneum but in front of the spine, and organs lying there are retroperitoneal. The kidneys sit in exactly this position, with peritoneum covering only their front surfaces. They are the K in SAD PUCKER.","notes":["Pelvic organs, such as the urinary bladder and rectum, sit in the lower part of the abdominopelvic cavity. The kidneys are much higher, against the posterior abdominal wall.","Intraperitoneal organs are fully wrapped in visceral peritoneum and suspended within the peritoneal cavity, like the stomach. The kidneys are not wrapped this way.","Mediastinal describes structures in the central compartment of the thoracic cavity, such as the heart. The kidneys are below the diaphragm.","Correct. The kidneys are retroperitoneal."],"lt":"t-body-cavities","lid":"c24","comp":9,"competencyId":"w1-peritoneum-relationships"},{"id":"L00106","dok":2,"q":"A needle is advanced to drain fluid that has collected around a lung. Which cavity does the needle enter?","a":"A pleural cavity.","options":["A pleural cavity","The peritoneal cavity","The pericardial cavity","The mediastinum"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The space around each lung, between the visceral pleura on the lung and the parietal pleura on the chest wall, is a pleural cavity. Normally it holds only a thin film of pleural fluid. When extra fluid builds up there, a needle placed through the chest wall can drain it.","notes":["Correct. Fluid around a lung is in a pleural cavity.","The peritoneal cavity is in the abdomen, below the diaphragm. It would be correct for fluid around the abdominal organs.","The pericardial cavity surrounds the heart. Pick it for fluid collected around the heart.","The mediastinum is the central compartment between the two pleural cavities, holding the heart, trachea, and esophagus. It is beside the lungs, not the space around them."],"lt":"t-body-cavities","lid":"c25","comp":6,"competencyId":"w1-body-cavities"},{"id":"L00107","dok":2,"q":"A mass is found in the inferior mediastinum, between the body of the sternum and the pericardium. Which subdivision contains it?","a":"The anterior mediastinum.","options":["The posterior mediastinum","The anterior mediastinum","The middle mediastinum","The superior mediastinum"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The inferior mediastinum is divided by the pericardium into three parts. The anterior mediastinum is the narrow space in front of the pericardium, behind the body of the sternum. The middle mediastinum is the pericardium with the heart inside it, and the posterior mediastinum lies behind the pericardium.","notes":["The posterior mediastinum lies behind the pericardium, in front of the vertebral column. Choose it for a mass behind the heart.","Correct. The space between the sternum and pericardium is the anterior mediastinum.","The middle mediastinum is the pericardium and the heart within it. It would be the answer for a mass inside the pericardial sac.","The superior mediastinum lies above the transverse thoracic plane, not in the inferior mediastinum. Pick it for a mass above the level of the sternal angle."],"lt":"t-body-cavities","lid":"c26","comp":8,"competencyId":"w1-mediastinum"},{"id":"L00108","dok":2,"q":"A clinician places pain in the upper right region of the nine-region grid, over the gallbladder. Which region is this?","a":"The right hypochondriac region.","options":["The right hypochondriac region","The right iliac region","The epigastric region","The right lumbar region"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The right hypochondriac region is the upper right box of the grid, under the lower ribs on the right side. Hypochondriac means below the cartilage, referring to the costal cartilages of the ribs. Its key structures are the liver and the gallbladder.","notes":["Correct. The upper right region is the right hypochondriac.","The right iliac region is the lower right box, holding the cecum and appendix. Choose it for pain typical of appendicitis.","The epigastric region is the upper center box, holding the stomach. Pick it for pain just below the sternum.","The right lumbar region is the middle right box, holding the ascending colon. It sits below the right hypochondriac region."],"lt":"t-body-cavities","lid":"c27","comp":10,"competencyId":"w1-abdominopelvic-map"},{"id":"L00109","dok":1,"q":"SAD PUCKER lists the pancreas as retroperitoneal with one exception. Which part of the pancreas is the exception?","a":"The tail.","options":["The neck","The tail","The head","The body"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Most of the pancreas lies behind the peritoneum, across the posterior abdominal wall. The course notes list the pancreas in SAD PUCKER as retroperitoneal except the tail. The tail extends toward the spleen, and that end is the part not counted as retroperitoneal.","notes":["The neck is the short section joining the head to the body, and it is retroperitoneal. It is included in the P of SAD PUCKER.","Correct. The tail is the exception.","The head of the pancreas is retroperitoneal, nestled in the curve of the duodenum. It is part of what P stands for in SAD PUCKER.","The body of the pancreas crosses the posterior abdominal wall behind the stomach and is retroperitoneal. It is included in the P of SAD PUCKER."],"lt":"t-body-cavities","lid":"c28","comp":9,"competencyId":"w1-peritoneum-relationships"},{"id":"L00110","dok":1,"q":"Which cavity lies superior to the diaphragm and is enclosed by the rib cage?","a":"The thoracic cavity.","options":["The abdominopelvic cavity","The cranial cavity","The thoracic cavity","The vertebral cavity"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The thoracic cavity is the upper part of the ventral cavity, enclosed by the ribs and sternum and floored by the diaphragm. It holds the lungs and the heart. The mediastinum is its central compartment, between the two pleural cavities.","notes":["The abdominopelvic cavity is below the diaphragm. Pick it for a structure in the abdomen or pelvis.","The cranial cavity is inside the skull and holds the brain. It is part of the dorsal cavity.","Correct. The thoracic cavity is above the diaphragm, inside the rib cage.","The vertebral cavity runs inside the vertebral column and holds the spinal cord. It is part of the dorsal cavity, not the space within the rib cage."],"lt":"t-body-cavities","lid":"c29","comp":6,"competencyId":"w1-body-cavities"},{"id":"L00111","dok":3,"q":"A surgeon notes that an organ is almost completely wrapped in visceral peritoneum and moves fairly freely within the peritoneal cavity. Which organ fits?","a":"The stomach.","options":["An adrenal gland","The abdominal aorta","The stomach","The right kidney"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"An organ wrapped in visceral peritoneum and suspended within the peritoneal cavity is intraperitoneal. The stomach is intraperitoneal, held in place by folds of peritoneum but still able to move as it fills and empties. The other three choices all lie behind the peritoneum and are part of SAD PUCKER.","notes":["The adrenal glands sit on top of the kidneys behind the peritoneum. They are the S, for suprarenal, in SAD PUCKER.","The abdominal aorta runs down the posterior abdominal wall in front of the spine, behind the peritoneum. It is part of the A in SAD PUCKER.","Correct. The stomach is intraperitoneal and fairly mobile.","The kidneys are retroperitoneal, fixed against the posterior abdominal wall with peritoneum covering only their front. They are the K in SAD PUCKER."],"lt":"t-body-cavities","lid":"c30","comp":9,"competencyId":"w1-peritoneum-relationships"},{"id":"L00112","dok":2,"q":"A patient has pain over both the spleen and the stomach. Which quadrant contains both organs?","a":"The left upper quadrant.","options":["The right lower quadrant","The right upper quadrant","The left lower quadrant","The left upper quadrant"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The left upper quadrant holds the stomach and the spleen, along with the left lobe of the liver, the left kidney, and the body and tail of the pancreas. The spleen tucks under the left ribs behind the stomach. Pain over both organs therefore points to the left upper quadrant.","notes":["The right lower quadrant holds the cecum and appendix. It would be correct for classic appendicitis pain.","The right upper quadrant holds the liver and gallbladder. Pick it for pain over the gallbladder.","The left lower quadrant holds the descending and sigmoid colon. Choose it for pain over the lower left colon.","Correct. The stomach and spleen are in the left upper quadrant."],"lt":"t-body-cavities","lid":"c31","comp":10,"competencyId":"w1-abdominopelvic-map"},{"id":"L00113","dok":2,"q":"A needle is placed into the vertebral cavity to sample fluid. Which membranes line the cavity it enters?","a":"The meninges.","options":["The meninges","The peritoneum","The pericardium","The pleura"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The vertebral cavity is part of the dorsal cavity and holds the spinal cord. Like the cranial cavity, it is lined by the meninges, the membranes that wrap the brain and spinal cord. A needle sampling fluid from this space must pass into the meninges.","notes":["Correct. The vertebral cavity is lined by the meninges.","The peritoneum lines the abdominopelvic cavity. It would be correct for a needle placed into the abdomen.","The pericardium surrounds the heart. Choose it for a needle placed into the sac around the heart.","The pleura lines the thoracic cavity around the lungs. Pick it for a needle placed around a lung."],"lt":"t-body-cavities","lid":"c32","comp":6,"competencyId":"w1-body-cavities"},{"id":"L00114","dok":1,"q":"The right hypochondriac region contains the gallbladder and which other organ?","a":"The liver.","options":["The liver","The cecum","The sigmoid colon","The spleen"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The right hypochondriac region is the upper right box of the grid, under the right lower ribs. Its key structures are the liver and the gallbladder, which sits on the underside of the liver. The large right lobe of the liver fills much of this region.","notes":["Correct. The liver shares the right hypochondriac region with the gallbladder.","The cecum is in the right iliac region, the lower right box. It would be correct for the region holding the appendix.","The sigmoid colon is in the left iliac region, the lower left box. Choose it for the lower left of the grid.","The spleen is in the left hypochondriac region, the mirror-image box on the left. Pick it for the upper left region."],"lt":"t-body-cavities","lid":"c33","comp":10,"competencyId":"w1-abdominopelvic-map"},{"id":"L00115","dok":2,"q":"A radiologist reports a finding inside the pericardial sac, next to the heart. Which mediastinal subdivision is this?","a":"The middle mediastinum.","options":["The superior mediastinum","The posterior mediastinum","The middle mediastinum","The anterior mediastinum"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The middle mediastinum is the pericardium and everything inside it, including the heart and the roots of the great vessels. It is the largest part of the inferior mediastinum. The anterior and posterior subdivisions lie in front of and behind it.","notes":["The superior mediastinum lies above the transverse thoracic plane, above the heart. Pick it for a finding near the aortic arch.","The posterior mediastinum lies behind the pericardium and holds the esophagus and descending aorta. It is outside the sac, behind the heart.","Correct. The pericardium and heart form the middle mediastinum.","The anterior mediastinum is the narrow space in front of the pericardium, behind the sternum. It is outside the sac, not inside it."],"lt":"t-body-cavities","lid":"c34","comp":8,"competencyId":"w1-mediastinum"},{"id":"L00116","dok":3,"q":"A ruptured appendix causes inflammation across the whole abdomen rather than a small local infection. Which feature of the peritoneal cavity explains this?","a":"It is a closed space, so spilled contents have nowhere to drain and spread through the cavity.","options":["It opens to the outside through the umbilicus, letting bacteria in","It is closed, so spilled contents cannot drain and spread throughout","It is continuous with the pleural cavities above the diaphragm","It is filled with air, which carries bacteria upward"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"A serous cavity is a closed space. When the appendix ruptures, bowel contents spill into the peritoneal cavity and have nowhere to drain, so they spread through the cavity and inflame the peritoneum across the abdomen. The result is peritonitis, a widespread problem rather than a local one.","notes":["The umbilicus is a scar on the abdominal wall and does not open into the peritoneal cavity. The problem is that the cavity has no outlet, not that it has an opening.","Correct. The closed peritoneal cavity lets the spill spread.","The diaphragm separates the peritoneal cavity from the pleural cavities, so they are not continuous. Peritonitis stays below the diaphragm.","The peritoneal cavity normally holds a thin film of serous fluid, not air. The spread happens because contents move through the fluid-filled closed space."],"lt":"t-body-cavities","lid":"c35","comp":7,"competencyId":"w1-serous-membranes"},{"id":"L00118","dok":2,"q":"An organ sits in the lower center of the nine-region grid, just behind the pubic bones, and can be described in either lower quadrant. Which organ is it?","a":"The urinary bladder.","options":["The urinary bladder","The ascending colon","The sigmoid colon","The cecum"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The urinary bladder is the key structure of the hypogastric region, the lower center box of the grid. Because it sits on the midline, the quadrant line runs right through it, so it can be described in either the right or the left lower quadrant. Midline organs are the reason the nine regions are sometimes more precise than quadrants.","notes":["Correct. The urinary bladder is in the hypogastric region on the midline.","The ascending colon runs up the right side in the right lumbar region. It is lateral, not in the lower center.","The sigmoid colon sits in the left iliac region, the lower left box. It belongs to the left lower quadrant only.","The cecum sits in the right iliac region, the lower right box, so it belongs to the right lower quadrant only. Pick it for the start of the large intestine."],"lt":"t-body-cavities","lid":"c37","comp":10,"competencyId":"w1-abdominopelvic-map"},{"id":"L00119","dok":2,"q":"Starting at the brain and moving down to the spinal cord, which description of the dorsal cavity spaces and their lining is correct?","a":"The cranial cavity, then the continuous vertebral cavity, both lined by the meninges.","options":["The cranial cavity, then the continuous vertebral cavity, both lined by the meninges","The thoracic cavity, then the vertebral cavity, both lined by pleura","The cranial cavity, then the pleural cavity, both lined by the meninges","The vertebral cavity, then the abdominopelvic cavity, both lined by peritoneum"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The brain sits in the cranial cavity, which opens directly into the vertebral cavity holding the spinal cord. Together they form the dorsal cavity, and the meninges line the whole length. The serous membranes, such as pleura and peritoneum, belong only to the ventral cavity.","notes":["Correct. Cranial cavity to vertebral cavity, lined by the meninges.","The thoracic cavity is part of the ventral cavity and holds the lungs and heart, not the brain. Pleura lines the thoracic cavity, not the vertebral cavity.","The pleural cavity surrounds a lung in the thorax and is lined by pleura. It is not connected to the cranial cavity.","The abdominopelvic cavity is a ventral cavity lined by peritoneum. It is not a continuation of the vertebral cavity."],"lt":"t-body-cavities","lid":"c38","comp":6,"competencyId":"w1-body-cavities"},{"id":"L00120","dok":2,"q":"Which structure passes through both the superior mediastinum and the posterior mediastinum?","a":"The esophagus.","options":["The ascending aorta","The pulmonary trunk","The esophagus","The thymus"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The esophagus enters the thorax in the superior mediastinum and continues down behind the heart through the posterior mediastinum before passing through the diaphragm to reach the stomach. Because it runs the full length of the thorax, it crosses from one subdivision into the next. Long tubes like the esophagus and trachea do not stay within a single subdivision.","notes":["The ascending aorta rises from the heart inside the pericardium, so it is in the middle mediastinum. The aorta reaches the posterior mediastinum only after the arch, as the descending aorta.","The pulmonary trunk leaves the right ventricle inside the pericardium, in the middle mediastinum. It does not pass behind the heart.","Correct. The esophagus runs through the superior and posterior mediastinum.","The thymus lies in the superior mediastinum and extends into the anterior mediastinum, in front of the heart. It does not reach the posterior mediastinum."],"lt":"t-body-cavities","lid":"c39","comp":8,"competencyId":"w1-mediastinum"},{"id":"L00121","dok":2,"q":"An organ lies in the left hypochondriac region, is intraperitoneal, and sits in the left upper quadrant. Which organ fits all three descriptions?","a":"The spleen.","options":["The urinary bladder","The spleen","The descending colon","The left kidney"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The spleen tucks under the left lower ribs, which places it in the left hypochondriac region and the left upper quadrant. It is wrapped in visceral peritoneum, so it is intraperitoneal. It is the key structure of the left hypochondriac region in the course notes.","notes":["The urinary bladder sits in the hypogastric region on the midline, low in the pelvis. It fits none of the three descriptions.","Correct. The spleen is left hypochondriac, intraperitoneal, and in the left upper quadrant.","The descending colon runs down the left side through the left lumbar region and is retroperitoneal, part of the C in SAD PUCKER. It fails two of the three descriptions.","The left kidney sits in the left upper quadrant, but it is retroperitoneal, the K in SAD PUCKER. It fails the intraperitoneal part of the description."],"lt":"t-body-cavities","lid":"c40","comp":10,"competencyId":"w1-abdominopelvic-map"},{"id":"L00122","dok":1,"q":"Which two cavities make up the dorsal (posterior) body cavity?","a":"The cranial and vertebral cavities.","options":["The cranial and vertebral cavities","The thoracic and abdominopelvic cavities","The pleural and pericardial cavities","The abdominal and pelvic cavities"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The dorsal cavity has two subdivisions: the cranial cavity, which holds the brain, and the vertebral cavity, which holds the spinal cord. They are continuous with each other and lined by the meninges. Every other cavity named here belongs to the ventral cavity.","notes":["Correct. The cranial and vertebral cavities form the dorsal cavity.","The thoracic and abdominopelvic cavities are the two subdivisions of the ventral cavity. Pick this pair for a question about the ventral cavity.","The pleural and pericardial cavities are serous spaces inside the thoracic cavity, around the lungs and the heart. They belong to the ventral cavity.","The abdominal and pelvic cavities are the two parts of the abdominopelvic cavity, below the diaphragm. They belong to the ventral cavity."],"lt":"t-body-cavities","lid":"c1-x2","comp":6,"competencyId":"w1-body-cavities"},{"id":"L00123","dok":1,"q":"Which body cavity holds the spinal cord?","a":"The vertebral cavity.","options":["Abdominopelvic cavity","Vertebral cavity","Cranial cavity","Thoracic cavity"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The vertebral cavity, also called the spinal cavity, runs inside the vertebral column and holds the spinal cord. It is the lower half of the dorsal cavity and is continuous with the cranial cavity above. The bony vertebrae surrounding it protect the cord.","notes":["The abdominopelvic cavity holds the digestive organs, bladder, and internal reproductive organs. It is a ventral cavity below the diaphragm.","Correct. The vertebral cavity holds the spinal cord.","The cranial cavity holds the brain, inside the skull. It is the upper half of the dorsal cavity, so pick it for the brain.","The thoracic cavity holds the lungs and heart. It lies in front of the vertebral column, not inside it."],"lt":"t-body-cavities","lid":"c2-x2","comp":6,"competencyId":"w1-body-cavities"},{"id":"L00124","dok":1,"q":"Which two cavities make up the ventral (anterior) body cavity?","a":"The thoracic and abdominopelvic cavities.","options":["The thoracic and abdominopelvic cavities","The abdominal and pelvic cavities","The pleural and pericardial cavities","The cranial and vertebral cavities"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The ventral cavity has two main subdivisions, the thoracic cavity above the diaphragm and the abdominopelvic cavity below it. The thoracic cavity holds the lungs and heart, and the abdominopelvic cavity holds the abdominal and pelvic organs. The diaphragm is the partition between them.","notes":["Correct. The ventral cavity is the thoracic plus the abdominopelvic cavity.","The abdominal and pelvic cavities are the two parts of the abdominopelvic cavity alone. This pair leaves out the thoracic cavity.","The pleural and pericardial cavities are smaller serous spaces inside the thoracic cavity. They are part of only one of the two subdivisions.","The cranial and vertebral cavities make up the dorsal cavity, holding the brain and spinal cord. Pick this pair for the dorsal cavity."],"lt":"t-body-cavities","lid":"c3-x2","comp":6,"competencyId":"w1-body-cavities"},{"id":"L00125","dok":1,"q":"The thoracic cavity contains which major organs?","a":"The lungs and the heart.","options":["The stomach and liver","The brain and spinal cord","The lungs and the heart","The urinary bladder and rectum"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The thoracic cavity is the part of the ventral cavity above the diaphragm, enclosed by the rib cage. It holds the two lungs, each in its own pleural cavity, and the heart in the mediastinum between them. The rib cage protects these organs and moves with breathing.","notes":["The stomach and liver are in the abdominal part of the abdominopelvic cavity, below the diaphragm. They would be correct for the upper abdomen.","The brain and spinal cord are in the dorsal cavity, in the cranial and vertebral cavities. Pick this pair for the dorsal cavity.","Correct. The thoracic cavity holds the lungs and heart.","The urinary bladder and rectum are in the pelvic part of the abdominopelvic cavity. Choose them for the pelvic cavity."],"lt":"t-body-cavities","lid":"c4-x2","comp":6,"competencyId":"w1-body-cavities"},{"id":"L00126","dok":1,"q":"Where is pleural fluid found?","a":"Between the visceral pleura and the parietal pleura.","options":["Within the mediastinum, beside the heart","Between the visceral and parietal pleura","Inside the air sacs of the lung","Between the parietal pleura and the ribs"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Pleural fluid sits in the pleural cavity, the thin space between the visceral pleura on the lung surface and the parietal pleura on the chest wall. The fluid reduces friction so the lung can slide against the wall during breathing. Every serous membrane follows this same pattern, with fluid between its two layers.","notes":["The mediastinum is the central compartment between the two pleural cavities. It holds the heart and other structures, not pleural fluid.","Correct. Pleural fluid lies between the two pleural layers.","The air sacs, or alveoli, are inside the lung tissue and hold air for gas exchange. Pleural fluid is outside the lung, around it.","The parietal pleura lines the inside of the chest wall, so there is no fluid-filled cavity between it and the ribs. The serous space is on the other side of the parietal layer."],"lt":"t-body-cavities","lid":"c5-x2","comp":7,"competencyId":"w1-serous-membranes"},{"id":"L00127","dok":1,"q":"What does the visceral peritoneum cover?","a":"The outer surfaces of the abdominal organs.","options":["The surface of the heart","The surface of each lung","The outer surfaces of the abdominal organs","The inner surface of the abdominal wall"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The visceral peritoneum is the layer of the peritoneum that lies directly on the abdominal organs, forming their outermost covering, called the serosa. Its partner, the parietal peritoneum, lines the abdominal wall. The peritoneal cavity between them holds a thin film of peritoneal fluid.","notes":["The surface of the heart is covered by the visceral pericardium, also called the epicardium. Choose it for the visceral layer of the pericardium.","The surface of each lung is covered by the visceral pleura. It would be correct for the visceral layer of the pleura.","Correct. The visceral peritoneum covers the abdominal organs.","The inner surface of the abdominal wall is lined by the parietal peritoneum. Pick it for the question of what the parietal layer lines."],"lt":"t-body-cavities","lid":"c6-x2","comp":7,"competencyId":"w1-serous-membranes"},{"id":"L00128","dok":1,"q":"What is the central compartment of the thoracic cavity, between the two lungs, called?","a":"The mediastinum.","options":["The diaphragm","The pericardium","The mediastinum","The peritoneum"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The mediastinum is the central compartment of the thoracic cavity, between the two lungs. It holds the heart, trachea, esophagus, major blood vessels, and thymus. It is bounded by the sternum in front, the vertebral column behind, and the diaphragm below.","notes":["The diaphragm is the muscular floor of the thoracic cavity and the inferior boundary of the mediastinum. It is a partition, not the compartment itself.","The pericardium is the sac around the heart. It sits inside the mediastinum, but it is only one of the structures there.","Correct. The mediastinum is the central compartment of the thorax.","The peritoneum is the serous membrane of the abdominopelvic cavity. It is a membrane below the diaphragm, not a compartment of the thorax."],"lt":"t-body-cavities","lid":"c7-x2","comp":8,"competencyId":"w1-mediastinum"},{"id":"L00129","dok":1,"q":"Inflammation of the serous membrane that lines the abdominopelvic cavity is called what?","a":"Peritonitis.","options":["Peritonitis","Pleurisy","Meningitis","Pericarditis"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The peritoneum lines the abdominopelvic cavity, and inflammation of it is called peritonitis. Common causes include infection, trauma, and a ruptured appendix, and typical findings are abdominal pain, fever, and a rigid abdomen. Each named membrane has its own matching inflammatory condition.","notes":["Correct. Inflammation of the peritoneum is peritonitis.","Pleurisy is inflammation of the pleura around the lungs, causing sharp chest pain with breathing. Pick it for the lung membrane.","Meningitis is inflammation of the meninges around the brain and spinal cord. Choose it for fever, headache, and a stiff neck.","Pericarditis is inflammation of the pericardium around the heart. It would be correct for chest pain with a friction rub over the heart."],"lt":"t-body-cavities","lid":"c8-x2","comp":7,"competencyId":"w1-serous-membranes"},{"id":"L00130","dok":3,"q":"A patient has chest pain, and the clinician hears a scratchy rubbing sound timed with each heartbeat. Which membrane is most likely inflamed?","a":"The pericardium.","options":["The peritoneum","The pericardium","The pleura","The meninges"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Normally serous fluid lets the two layers of the serous pericardium glide past each other with every heartbeat. When the pericardium is inflamed, the layers rub instead of glide, producing a friction rub that keeps time with the heart. This is pericarditis, and the timing of the sound with the heartbeat points to the pericardium.","notes":["The peritoneum lines the abdomen, below the diaphragm. Its inflammation, peritonitis, causes abdominal pain and rigidity rather than a sound over the heart.","Correct. A rub timed with the heartbeat points to pericarditis.","An inflamed pleura can also cause sharp chest pain and a rubbing sound, but those follow breathing, not the heartbeat. Pick the pleura for pain and a rub that come with each breath, as in pleurisy.","The meninges surround the brain and spinal cord. Meningitis causes fever, headache, and a stiff neck, not chest sounds."],"lt":"t-body-cavities","lid":"c9-x2","comp":7,"competencyId":"w1-serous-membranes"},{"id":"L00131","dok":1,"q":"In SAD PUCKER, what does the letter S stand for?","a":"The suprarenal (adrenal) glands.","options":["The stomach","The sigmoid colon","The spleen","The suprarenal glands"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"S stands for the suprarenal glands, another name for the adrenal glands. Supra means above, and renal refers to the kidney, so the name says these glands sit on top of the kidneys. Like the kidneys, they lie behind the peritoneum against the posterior abdominal wall.","notes":["The stomach is intraperitoneal and fairly mobile, held by folds of peritoneum. It does not belong in a list of retroperitoneal structures.","The sigmoid colon is intraperitoneal, held to the posterior wall by the mesocolon. The colon in SAD PUCKER means only the ascending and descending colon.","The spleen starts with S, but it is intraperitoneal, wrapped in visceral peritoneum in the left upper quadrant. It is not part of SAD PUCKER.","Correct. S is the suprarenal glands."],"lt":"t-body-cavities","lid":"c10-x2","comp":9,"competencyId":"w1-peritoneum-relationships"},{"id":"L00132","dok":1,"q":"How many regions does the abdominopelvic grid divide the abdomen into?","a":"Nine.","options":["Four","Nine","Six","Three"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Four planes, two vertical and two horizontal, divide the abdominopelvic area into nine regions arranged in a three-by-three grid. The center column holds the epigastric, umbilical, and hypogastric regions, with hypochondriac, lumbar, and iliac regions on each side. The nine regions are the more precise anatomical version of the four quadrants.","notes":["Four is the number of quadrants, drawn with only two planes. Pick four for the quadrant scheme.","Correct. The grid has nine regions.","Six comes from counting only two rows of three, which leaves out one horizontal band. The grid has three rows, upper, middle, and lower.","Three is the number of regions in a single row or column of the grid. The full grid has three rows of three."],"lt":"t-body-cavities","lid":"c11-x2","comp":10,"competencyId":"w1-abdominopelvic-map"},{"id":"L00133","dok":1,"q":"Which of the nine regions sits in the center of the grid around the navel, with small intestine as its key structure?","a":"The umbilical region.","options":["Right lumbar region","Epigastric region","Umbilical region","Hypogastric region"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The umbilical region is the center box of the nine-region grid, surrounding the navel. Loops of small intestine fill much of this area, so the small intestine is its key structure. It shares its name with the umbilical regional term for the navel.","notes":["The right lumbar region is the middle right box, beside the umbilical region, and holds the ascending colon. It is lateral, not central.","The epigastric region is the upper center box, above the umbilical region, and holds the stomach. Pick it for the area just below the sternum.","Correct. The umbilical region is the center box.","The hypogastric region is the lower center box, below the umbilical region, and holds the urinary bladder. It would be correct for the lowest center region."],"lt":"t-body-cavities","lid":"c12-x2","comp":10,"competencyId":"w1-abdominopelvic-map"},{"id":"L00134","dok":2,"q":"How are the nine abdominopelvic regions drawn?","a":"With four planes, two vertical and two horizontal.","options":["With two planes, one vertical and one horizontal","With four planes, two vertical and two horizontal","With three horizontal planes","With four vertical planes"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Two vertical planes and two horizontal planes cross the abdomen like a tic-tac-toe board, creating three columns and three rows. That gives nine regions in all. The four quadrants, by contrast, need only two planes.","notes":["One vertical and one horizontal plane create only four areas. This describes the four quadrants, drawn by the median plane and a transverse plane through the navel.","Correct. Two vertical and two horizontal planes make the nine-region grid.","Horizontal planes alone would cut the abdomen into stacked bands with no right and left columns. The grid needs vertical planes as well.","Vertical planes alone would cut the abdomen into side-by-side strips with no upper and lower rows. The grid needs horizontal planes as well."],"lt":"t-body-cavities","lid":"c13-x2","comp":10,"competencyId":"w1-abdominopelvic-map"},{"id":"L00135","dok":2,"q":"In the course's description of the mediastinum, what forms its superior boundary?","a":"The thoracic inlet.","options":["The thoracic inlet","The diaphragm","The lungs","The sternum"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The thoracic inlet is the opening at the top of the rib cage, where the trachea, esophagus, and large vessels pass between the neck and the thorax. It forms the superior boundary of the mediastinum. The diaphragm forms the opposite, inferior boundary.","notes":["Correct. The thoracic inlet is the superior boundary.","The diaphragm forms the inferior boundary, the floor of the mediastinum. Pick it for the lower boundary.","The lungs form the lateral boundaries, one on each side. Choose them for the side walls.","The sternum forms the anterior boundary of the mediastinum. It would be correct for the front wall."],"lt":"t-body-cavities","lid":"c14-x2","comp":8,"competencyId":"w1-mediastinum"},{"id":"L00136","dok":1,"q":"Which of these structures is found inside the mediastinum?","a":"The thymus.","options":["The right kidney","The thymus","The spleen","The left lung"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The mediastinum holds the heart, trachea, esophagus, major blood vessels, and thymus. The thymus sits in the upper front part of the mediastinum, behind the sternum. The lungs are not inside the mediastinum; they form its side walls.","notes":["The right kidney is in the abdomen, behind the peritoneum. Choose it for a retroperitoneal organ, not a mediastinal one.","Correct. The thymus is in the mediastinum.","The spleen is below the diaphragm, in the left upper quadrant of the abdomen. It would be correct for an organ in the left hypochondriac region.","The left lung lies in its own pleural cavity, beside the mediastinum. The lungs form the lateral boundaries of the mediastinum rather than its contents."],"lt":"t-body-cavities","lid":"c15-x2","comp":8,"competencyId":"w1-mediastinum"},{"id":"L00137","dok":2,"q":"Which of these is a retroperitoneal structure?","a":"The ureters.","options":["The stomach","The transverse colon","The spleen","The ureters"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The ureters run from the kidneys down the posterior abdominal wall to the bladder, behind the peritoneum. They are the U in SAD PUCKER. The other three choices are intraperitoneal, wrapped in visceral peritoneum and suspended within the peritoneal cavity.","notes":["The stomach is intraperitoneal and fairly mobile, held by folds of peritoneum. Pick it for an example of an intraperitoneal organ.","The transverse colon is intraperitoneal, held by the mesocolon. Only the ascending and descending parts of the colon are retroperitoneal.","The spleen is intraperitoneal, wrapped in visceral peritoneum under the left ribs. It is a common trap because it sits near the left kidney, which is retroperitoneal.","Correct. The ureters are retroperitoneal."],"lt":"t-body-cavities","lid":"c16-x2","comp":9,"competencyId":"w1-peritoneum-relationships"},{"id":"L00138","dok":2,"q":"A needle drains fluid from the cavity surrounding a lung. What is this procedure called?","a":"Thoracentesis.","options":["Lumbar puncture","Pericardiocentesis","Paracentesis","Thoracentesis"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Thoracentesis drains fluid from a pleural cavity, the space around a lung. The name combines thorax, the chest, with centesis, a puncture to remove fluid. Each named procedure matches the cavity it enters.","notes":["A lumbar puncture samples fluid from around the spinal cord in the vertebral cavity. Choose it for sampling the fluid of the dorsal cavity.","Pericardiocentesis removes fluid from the pericardial cavity around the heart. It would be correct for fluid squeezing the heart.","Paracentesis removes fluid from the peritoneal cavity in the abdomen. Pick it for draining fluid around the abdominal organs.","Correct. Draining the pleural cavity is thoracentesis."],"lt":"t-body-cavities","lid":"c17-x2","comp":6,"competencyId":"w1-body-cavities"},{"id":"L00139","dok":1,"q":"Which quadrant contains the gallbladder and most of the liver?","a":"The right upper quadrant.","options":["The right upper quadrant","The right lower quadrant","The left lower quadrant","The left upper quadrant"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The liver sits under the right side of the diaphragm, and the gallbladder lies on its underside, so both are in the right upper quadrant. Only the smaller left lobe of the liver reaches into the left upper quadrant. Right upper quadrant pain often points to the gallbladder.","notes":["Correct. The gallbladder and most of the liver are in the right upper quadrant.","The right lower quadrant holds the cecum and appendix. It would be correct for appendicitis pain.","The left lower quadrant holds the descending and sigmoid colon. Choose it for the lower left part of the large intestine.","The left upper quadrant holds the stomach and spleen, along with only the left lobe of the liver. Pick it for the stomach and spleen."],"lt":"t-body-cavities","lid":"c18-x2","comp":10,"competencyId":"w1-abdominopelvic-map"},{"id":"L00140","dok":2,"q":"Which of these parts of the intestine is mostly retroperitoneal?","a":"The duodenum.","options":["The jejunum","The transverse colon","The ileum","The duodenum"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The duodenum, the first and shortest part of the small intestine, is mostly retroperitoneal, curving behind the peritoneum around the head of the pancreas. It is the D in SAD PUCKER. The rest of the small intestine hangs freely on the mesentery.","notes":["The jejunum, the middle part of the small intestine, is intraperitoneal and suspended by the mesentery. That is why it moves freely in the abdomen.","The transverse colon is intraperitoneal, held by the mesocolon. The parts of the colon that are retroperitoneal are the ascending and descending colon.","The ileum, the last part of the small intestine, is intraperitoneal and suspended by the mesentery. Pick the jejunum or ileum for examples of mobile, intraperitoneal intestine.","Correct. Most of the duodenum is retroperitoneal."],"lt":"t-body-cavities","lid":"c19-x2","comp":9,"competencyId":"w1-peritoneum-relationships"},{"id":"L00141","dok":2,"q":"In the nine-region grid, which region has the descending colon as its key structure?","a":"The left lumbar region.","options":["Umbilical region","Left iliac region","Left lumbar region","Left hypochondriac region"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The left lumbar region is the middle box on the left side of the grid, and its key structure is the descending colon running down the left flank. Lumbar refers to the loin, the side of the body between the ribs and the hip. Its mirror image on the right, the right lumbar region, holds the ascending colon.","notes":["The umbilical region is the center box, holding small intestine. It is medial to the left lumbar region.","The left iliac region is the lower left box, and its key structure is the sigmoid colon. Choose it for the S-shaped colon just before the rectum.","Correct. The descending colon is the key structure of the left lumbar region.","The left hypochondriac region is the upper left box, under the left ribs, and its key structure is the spleen. Pick it for the spleen."],"lt":"t-body-cavities","lid":"c20-x2","comp":10,"competencyId":"w1-abdominopelvic-map"},{"id":"L00142","dok":2,"q":"A patient has fever, headache, and a stiff neck from an infection of the membranes around the brain and spinal cord. What is the condition called?","a":"Meningitis.","options":["Pericarditis","Peritonitis","Meningitis","Pleurisy"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The meninges line the dorsal cavity, wrapping the brain and spinal cord. Infection and inflammation of these membranes is meningitis, with fever, headache, and a stiff neck as its classic signs and symptoms. Because the skull is rigid, swelling inside it raises pressure on the brain.","notes":["Pericarditis is inflammation of the pericardium around the heart, with chest pain and a friction rub. Choose it for chest pain with a rub timed to the heartbeat.","Peritonitis is inflammation of the peritoneum in the abdomen, causing abdominal pain, fever, and rigidity. Pick it for an abdominal infection such as a ruptured appendix.","Correct. Inflamed meninges cause meningitis.","Pleurisy is inflammation of the pleura around the lungs, causing sharp chest pain and a dry cough. It would be correct for pain that worsens with each breath."],"lt":"t-body-cavities","lid":"c21-x2","comp":6,"competencyId":"w1-body-cavities"},{"id":"L00143","dok":2,"q":"Which structure lies in the superior mediastinum?","a":"The arch of the aorta.","options":["The descending thoracic aorta","The heart","The inferior vena cava","The arch of the aorta"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The superior mediastinum is the part of the mediastinum above the transverse thoracic plane, at the level of the sternal angle. The arch of the aorta curves through this space and gives off its large branches to the head and upper limbs. The trachea and esophagus also pass through it.","notes":["The descending thoracic aorta runs down behind the heart in the posterior mediastinum. Pick it for a vessel in the posterior mediastinum.","The heart sits inside the pericardium in the middle mediastinum, below the transverse thoracic plane. It would be correct for the middle mediastinum.","The inferior vena cava passes up through the diaphragm and enters the right atrium almost immediately. It lies low in the thorax, near the heart, not in the superior mediastinum.","Correct. The aortic arch is in the superior mediastinum."],"lt":"t-body-cavities","lid":"c22-x2","comp":8,"competencyId":"w1-mediastinum"},{"id":"L00145","dok":2,"q":"In the course's description of the mediastinum, what forms its lateral boundaries?","a":"The lungs.","options":["The vertebral column","The ribs","The lungs","The sternum"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The mediastinum is the central compartment of the thorax, between the two lungs, so the lungs form its lateral boundaries. Each lung, wrapped in its pleura, presses against the side of the mediastinum. The sternum and vertebral column close it off in front and behind.","notes":["The vertebral column forms the posterior boundary. Choose it for the back wall of the mediastinum.","The ribs form the side walls of the whole thoracic cavity, but the lungs lie between the ribs and the mediastinum. The ribs are therefore lateral to the lungs, not to the mediastinum directly.","Correct. The lungs form the lateral boundaries.","The sternum forms the anterior boundary. Pick it for the front wall of the mediastinum."],"lt":"t-body-cavities","lid":"c24-x2","comp":8,"competencyId":"w1-mediastinum"},{"id":"L00146","dok":2,"q":"Which organ would NOT be found in the pelvic cavity?","a":"The liver.","options":["The uterus","The rectum","The liver","The urinary bladder"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The pelvic cavity is the lower part of the abdominopelvic cavity, cradled by the bony pelvis. It holds the urinary bladder, the rectum, and the internal reproductive organs such as the uterus. The liver lies high in the abdominal part, just under the diaphragm.","notes":["The uterus sits in the pelvic cavity between the urinary bladder and the rectum. It is one of the internal reproductive organs held in the pelvis.","The rectum is the last part of the large intestine and lies in the pelvic cavity, in front of the sacrum. It is the R in SAD PUCKER.","Correct. The liver is in the upper abdomen, not the pelvis.","The urinary bladder sits low in the pelvic cavity, just behind the pubic bones. It is the key structure of the hypogastric region."],"lt":"t-body-cavities","lid":"c25-x2","comp":6,"competencyId":"w1-body-cavities"},{"id":"L00147","dok":3,"q":"An older adult has pain in the left lower quadrant. Which condition does this location classically suggest?","a":"Diverticulitis of the sigmoid colon.","options":["Sigmoid diverticulitis","Appendicitis","Splenic rupture","Gallbladder inflammation"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The left lower quadrant holds the descending and sigmoid colon. Small pouches can form in the wall of the sigmoid colon, and when they become inflamed the condition is called diverticulitis, a classic cause of left lower quadrant pain in older adults. Knowing which organs lie in each quadrant narrows the likely cause of pain.","notes":["Correct. Left lower quadrant pain classically suggests sigmoid diverticulitis.","Appendicitis causes pain in the right lower quadrant, where the appendix hangs from the cecum. Pick it for the mirror-image location on the right.","The spleen is in the left upper quadrant, under the left ribs. A ruptured spleen causes pain high on the left, not low.","Gallbladder disease causes pain in the right upper quadrant, under the liver. It would be correct for upper right pain."],"lt":"t-body-cavities","lid":"c26-x2","comp":10,"competencyId":"w1-abdominopelvic-map"},{"id":"L00148","dok":3,"q":"A patient with a leaking abdominal aorta reports flank and back pain rather than spreading abdominal pain. Where is the blood most likely collecting?","a":"In the retroperitoneum.","options":["In the retroperitoneum","In the pericardial cavity","In a pleural cavity","In the peritoneal cavity"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The abdominal aorta is retroperitoneal, lying behind the peritoneum against the posterior wall, and it is part of the A in SAD PUCKER. Blood leaking from it collects in the retroperitoneum, near the back and flanks, instead of spreading through the peritoneal cavity. This is why knowing an organ's peritoneal position predicts how a bleed will present.","notes":["Correct. A retroperitoneal vessel bleeds into the retroperitoneum.","The pericardial cavity surrounds the heart. Blood there would compress the heart rather than cause flank pain.","The pleural cavities are above the diaphragm, around the lungs. Blood there would come from a chest injury and affect breathing.","Blood in the peritoneal cavity spreads freely around the abdominal organs and causes widespread abdominal pain. That would fit a bleed from an intraperitoneal organ such as the spleen."],"lt":"t-body-cavities","lid":"c27-x2","comp":9,"competencyId":"w1-peritoneum-relationships"},{"id":"L00149","dok":3,"q":"A clinician needs to sample the fluid around the spinal cord. Which cavity holds that fluid, and which procedure reaches it?","a":"The vertebral cavity, reached by a lumbar puncture.","options":["The pleural cavity, reached by thoracentesis","The cranial cavity, reached by a lumbar puncture","The peritoneal cavity, reached by paracentesis","The vertebral cavity, reached by a lumbar puncture"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The spinal cord and the fluid around it lie in the vertebral cavity, part of the dorsal cavity. A lumbar puncture places a needle between vertebrae in the lower back to draw that fluid. The procedure name and the cavity have to match for the answer to be correct.","notes":["Thoracentesis drains fluid from a pleural cavity around a lung. Pick this pair for fluid collected around a lung.","A lumbar puncture is the right procedure, but the cranial cavity holds the brain, inside the skull. The needle enters the vertebral cavity in the lower back, not the skull.","Paracentesis drains fluid from the peritoneal cavity in the abdomen. Choose this pair for fluid around the abdominal organs.","Correct. The vertebral cavity is sampled by a lumbar puncture."],"lt":"t-body-cavities","lid":"c28-x2","comp":6,"competencyId":"w1-body-cavities"},{"id":"L00150","dok":2,"q":"A mass is found in the posterior mediastinum, behind the heart. Which vessel runs through that subdivision?","a":"The descending thoracic aorta.","options":["The pulmonary trunk","The descending thoracic aorta","The superior vena cava","The ascending aorta"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"After arching over the heart, the aorta turns downward as the descending thoracic aorta and runs through the posterior mediastinum, in front of the vertebral column. The esophagus travels beside it in this same space. A mass behind the heart can therefore press on the descending aorta or the esophagus.","notes":["The pulmonary trunk leaves the right ventricle inside the pericardium, in the middle mediastinum. Pick it for a vessel at the base of the heart.","Correct. The descending thoracic aorta runs through the posterior mediastinum.","The superior vena cava descends on the right side of the superior mediastinum to enter the right atrium. It lies in front of the posterior mediastinum.","The ascending aorta rises from the left ventricle inside the pericardium, in the middle mediastinum. It is in front of the posterior mediastinum, not within it."],"lt":"t-body-cavities","lid":"c29-x2","comp":8,"competencyId":"w1-mediastinum"},{"id":"L00151","dok":3,"q":"During surgery, one abdominal organ is easy to lift and move, while another stays fixed against the back wall. Which difference between the two organs explains this?","a":"The mobile organ is intraperitoneal and the fixed organ is retroperitoneal","options":["The mobile organ is covered by parietal peritoneum and the fixed organ by visceral peritoneum","The mobile organ is in the pelvic cavity and the fixed organ is in the abdominal cavity","The mobile organ is retroperitoneal and the fixed organ is intraperitoneal","The mobile organ is intraperitoneal and the fixed organ is retroperitoneal"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Intraperitoneal organs are wrapped in visceral peritoneum and suspended within the peritoneal cavity by folds of peritoneum, so they can be lifted and moved. Retroperitoneal organs lie behind the peritoneum, pressed against the posterior abdominal wall, so they stay in place. This difference also shapes how a surgeon reaches each organ.","notes":["Parietal peritoneum lines the body wall and passes over the front of fixed organs such as the kidneys, while visceral peritoneum wraps the mobile organs. This choice has the two layers backward.","The abdominal and pelvic cavities are one continuous space, and both hold mobile and fixed organs. Which cavity an organ sits in does not decide how freely it moves.","This reverses the two categories. Retroperitoneal organs are the fixed ones, held against the posterior wall, while intraperitoneal organs are the mobile ones.","Correct. Intraperitoneal organs move, and retroperitoneal organs stay fixed."],"lt":"t-body-cavities","lid":"c30-x2","comp":9,"competencyId":"w1-peritoneum-relationships"},{"id":"L00152","dok":2,"q":"The pleura secretes a thin serous fluid into the space between its two layers. Why does this fluid matter as the lungs expand and recoil with every breath?","a":"It lets the two membrane layers slide over each other with little friction.","options":["It fills the alveoli and keeps them from collapsing","It carries oxygen from the chest wall into the lung","It is mucus that traps dust before it reaches the lung","It lets the two layers glide with little friction"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"A serous membrane has two layers with a thin film of fluid between them. As the lungs expand and recoil, the visceral pleura on the lung must glide against the parietal pleura on the chest wall thousands of times a day. The serous fluid reduces friction so that movement happens smoothly and without damage.","notes":["The material that helps keep the alveoli open is surfactant, made inside the lung by alveolar cells. Pleural fluid lies outside the lung, between the pleural layers.","Oxygen reaches the blood across the walls of the alveoli inside the lung, not through the chest wall. Pleural fluid has no role in gas exchange.","Mucus is produced by mucous membranes lining the airways, where it traps dust and microbes. Serous fluid is thin and watery and lies outside the lung, not in the airways.","Correct. Serous fluid reduces friction between the layers."],"lt":"t-body-cavities","lid":"d3-t-body-cavities-1","comp":7,"competencyId":"w1-serous-membranes"},{"id":"L00153","dok":2,"q":"The brain and spinal cord sit within the dorsal cavity, surrounded by bone. Why does a bony enclosure suit these organs?","a":"Nervous tissue is soft and easily damaged, so rigid bone shields it from impact.","options":["Bone produces the fluid that bathes the brain and spinal cord","Bony walls allow the dorsal cavity to be lined by serous membranes","The brain and spinal cord change size during activity, and bone allows that","Nervous tissue is soft and easily damaged, so rigid bone shields it from impact"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The brain and spinal cord are soft and easily injured, so the skull and vertebral column form a hard case around them. The trade-off is that the case cannot expand, which is why swelling or infection inside the skull, as in meningitis, raises pressure on the brain. The ventral cavity organs, which change size, are surrounded by more flexible walls instead.","notes":["The fluid around the brain and spinal cord is produced inside the brain, not by the surrounding bone. Bone protects the fluid and the nervous tissue but does not make the fluid.","The dorsal cavity is lined by the meninges, which are not serous membranes. Serous membranes belong to the ventral cavity.","Rigid bone does the opposite of allowing a change in size, and the brain and spinal cord do not swell and shrink with normal activity. Flexible walls are found around organs that do change size, such as the stomach and lungs.","Correct. Rigid bone protects delicate nervous tissue."],"lt":"t-body-cavities","lid":"d3-t-body-cavities-2","comp":6,"competencyId":"w1-body-cavities"},{"id":"L00154","dok":3,"q":"A stab wound lets air into the right pleural cavity, and the right lung collapses. Why does the left lung keep working?","a":"Each lung has its own separate pleural cavity, so air in the right one does not reach the left.","options":["The left lung has no pleura around it","Each lung sits in its own separate pleural cavity","The diaphragm separates the right lung from the left lung","The pericardial cavity absorbs the air before it reaches the left side"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Each lung sits in its own pleural cavity, and the two cavities are separated by the mediastinum. When air enters one pleural cavity, the seal that holds that lung expanded is lost and the lung collapses, but the other pleural cavity stays sealed. Keeping each lung in its own closed space limits the damage to one side.","notes":["Both lungs are wrapped in pleura, with a visceral layer on the lung and a parietal layer on the chest wall. The left lung is protected by being in a separate cavity, not by lacking pleura.","Correct. Separate pleural cavities keep the injury to one side.","The diaphragm lies below the lungs and separates the thoracic cavity from the abdominopelvic cavity. The structure between the two lungs is the mediastinum.","The pericardial cavity is a closed space around the heart and is not connected to either pleural cavity. It does not take up air from the chest."],"lt":"t-body-cavities","lid":"d3-t-body-cavities-3","comp":6,"competencyId":"w1-body-cavities"},{"id":"L00156","dok":1,"q":"In SAD PUCKER, what does the letter A stand for?","a":"The aorta and inferior vena cava.","options":["The ascending colon","The appendix","The aorta and inferior vena cava","The adrenal (suprarenal) glands"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"A stands for the aorta and the inferior vena cava, the two great vessels that run down the posterior abdominal wall in front of the spine. Both lie behind the peritoneum. This explains why a leak from the abdominal aorta tends to collect in the retroperitoneum.","notes":["The ascending colon is retroperitoneal, but it belongs to the C in SAD PUCKER along with the descending colon. Choose it for the meaning of C.","The appendix hangs from the cecum on its own small fold of peritoneum and is intraperitoneal. It is not part of SAD PUCKER.","Correct. A is the aorta and inferior vena cava.","The adrenal glands are retroperitoneal, but in SAD PUCKER they are the S, listed under their other name, the suprarenal glands. Pick them for the meaning of S."],"lt":"t-body-cavities","lid":"d3-t-body-cavities-5","comp":9,"competencyId":"w1-peritoneum-relationships"},{"id":"L00157","dok":3,"q":"Inflammation of the pleura, called pleurisy, makes each breath sharply painful. Based on the membrane's normal job, why does breathing hurt?","a":"The inflamed layers no longer glide smoothly and rub against each other as the lung moves.","options":["The inflamed pleura presses on the heart with every beat","Inflammation stops the lung from moving at all","Its rough layers rub instead of gliding as the lung moves","The inflamed pleura fills the airways with fluid"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Normally pleural fluid lets the visceral and parietal pleura slide past each other without friction. When the pleura is inflamed, the surfaces roughen and the two layers rub instead of glide, so every breath drags them against each other. That is why pleurisy causes sharp chest pain that comes and goes with breathing.","notes":["Pain timed with each heartbeat comes from an inflamed pericardium, in pericarditis. Pleurisy follows the rhythm of breathing instead.","The lung still moves with each breath in pleurisy, which is exactly why it hurts. If the lung stopped moving, the layers would not rub.","Correct. Rough, inflamed layers rub with each breath.","The pleura lies outside the lung, so its inflammation does not fill the airways. Fluid in the airways and air sacs would point to a problem inside the lung, such as pneumonia."],"lt":"t-body-cavities","lid":"d3-t-body-cavities-6","comp":7,"competencyId":"w1-serous-membranes"},{"id":"L00158","dok":2,"q":"The thoracic cavity is enclosed by the bony rib cage, while the abdominal cavity wall is mostly muscle. How does each wall suit the organs inside it?","a":"Bone shields the heart and lungs, while a muscular wall can stretch as the digestive organs fill.","options":["Bone is needed because the thoracic organs are retroperitoneal, while the abdominal organs are not","Bone shields the heart and lungs, while a muscular wall can stretch as the digestive organs fill","Bone keeps the lungs from changing size, while muscle keeps the stomach from changing size","Muscle is enough in the abdomen because the abdominal organs have no serous membrane"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The heart and lungs are vital organs that work constantly, and the rib cage protects them while still moving with each breath. The digestive organs change size as they fill and empty, so a muscular abdominal wall that can stretch suits them. Each wall matches what the organs inside it need.","notes":["Retroperitoneal describes abdominal organs behind the peritoneum, such as the kidneys. It does not apply to the heart and lungs in the thorax.","Correct. Bone protects the thoracic organs, and muscle lets the abdomen expand.","The lungs must change size with every breath, and the stomach expands after a meal. Neither wall is there to prevent a change in size.","The abdominal organs do have a serous membrane, the peritoneum. The muscular wall suits them because they need room to expand."],"lt":"t-body-cavities","lid":"d3-t-body-cavities-7","comp":6,"competencyId":"w1-body-cavities"},{"id":"L00160","dok":1,"q":"Which of the nine abdominopelvic regions contains the cecum and appendix?","a":"The right iliac region.","options":["The epigastric region","The right lumbar region","The hypogastric region","The right iliac region"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The right iliac region is the lower right box of the nine-region grid, and its key structures are the cecum and appendix. It is named for the iliac bone of the hip, which lies beneath it. This is where appendicitis pain classically settles.","notes":["The epigastric region is the upper center box, holding the stomach. Choose it for the area just below the sternum.","The right lumbar region is the middle right box, holding the ascending colon. It sits above the right iliac region.","The hypogastric region is the lower center box, holding the urinary bladder. Pick it for the region on the midline just above the pubic bones.","Correct. The cecum and appendix are in the right iliac region."],"lt":"t-body-cavities","lid":"w1g-bc-1","comp":10,"competencyId":"w1-abdominopelvic-map"},{"id":"L00161","dok":1,"q":"The sigmoid colon lies mainly in which of the nine abdominopelvic regions?","a":"The left iliac region.","options":["The left lumbar region","The umbilical region","The left hypochondriac region","The left iliac region"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The left iliac region is the lower left box of the grid, and its key structure is the sigmoid colon, the S-shaped last part of the colon before the rectum. It is the mirror image of the right iliac region, which holds the cecum and appendix. The sigmoid colon is a common site of diverticulitis.","notes":["The left lumbar region is the middle left box, holding the descending colon. Pick it for the part of the colon just above the sigmoid colon.","The umbilical region is the center box around the navel, holding small intestine. It would be correct for loops of small intestine.","The left hypochondriac region is the upper left box, holding the spleen. Choose it for the area under the left ribs.","Correct. The sigmoid colon is in the left iliac region."],"lt":"t-body-cavities","lid":"w1g-bc-2","comp":10,"competencyId":"w1-abdominopelvic-map"},{"id":"L02294","dok":2,"q":"The gallbladder sits on the posterior surface of the liver. In which abdominopelvic quadrant would pain from an inflamed gallbladder be expected?","a":"The right upper quadrant.","options":["The right upper quadrant","The left lower quadrant","The left upper quadrant","The right lower quadrant"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The four quadrants are made by one vertical and one horizontal line crossing at the umbilicus. The liver lies mostly in the right upper quadrant under the diaphragm, and the gallbladder tucks against its posterior surface, so gallbladder pain from cholecystitis is felt in the right upper quadrant. Remember that right and left are the patient's right and left.","notes":["Correct. The liver and gallbladder are the key organs of the right upper quadrant.","The left lower quadrant holds the descending and sigmoid colon. It would fit a question about pain from the lower left part of the large intestine.","The left upper quadrant holds the stomach and spleen. It would be the answer for pain from the spleen, not the gallbladder.","The right lower quadrant holds the cecum and appendix. Pick it for appendicitis pain, a common mix-up because both conditions cause right-sided pain."],"lt":"t-accessory-digestive-organs","lid":"fr15","comp":10,"competencyId":"w1-abdominopelvic-map"},{"id":"L02306","dok":1,"q":"In which of the nine abdominopelvic regions does the appendix lie?","a":"The right iliac region.","options":["The right iliac region","The umbilical region","The right lumbar region","The hypogastric region"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The nine regions divide the abdomen more precisely than the four quadrants. The right iliac region (also called the right inguinal region) is the lower right region, and it holds the cecum and the appendix. Clinicians often press over the appendix at a spot called McBurney point, which lies in this region, when checking for appendicitis.","notes":["Correct. The cecum and appendix lie in the right iliac region.","The umbilical region is the central region around the navel and holds mostly small intestine. Pick it for the region at the center of the nine-region grid.","The right lumbar region sits at waist level on the right and holds the ascending colon. It would be the answer for the ascending colon, which lies just above the cecum.","The hypogastric region is the lower middle region, just above the pubic bones, and it holds the urinary bladder. Pick it for the bladder, not the appendix."],"lt":"t-accessory-digestive-organs","lid":"c11","comp":10,"competencyId":"w1-abdominopelvic-map"},{"id":"L02312","dok":1,"q":"In which abdominopelvic quadrant is the appendix located?","a":"The right lower quadrant.","options":["The left upper quadrant","The right upper quadrant","The right lower quadrant","The left lower quadrant"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The appendix hangs from the cecum, the first part of the large intestine, which sits in the right lower quadrant. This is why appendicitis typically causes pain and tenderness in the lower right abdomen. Right and left always refer to the patient's right and left, not the viewer's.","notes":["The left upper quadrant holds the stomach and spleen. It would be the answer for the spleen, not the appendix.","The right upper quadrant holds the liver and gallbladder. Pick it for gallbladder pain from cholecystitis.","Correct. The appendix and cecum are in the right lower quadrant.","The left lower quadrant holds the descending and sigmoid colon. Students sometimes pick it by mixing up the patient's right with their own right when facing a diagram."],"lt":"t-accessory-digestive-organs","lid":"c17","comp":10,"competencyId":"w1-abdominopelvic-map"},{"id":"N-a17d-6-1","dok":3,"q":"On a lateral-view model, a probe in the thoracic cavity is pushed straight down through the diaphragm. Which cavity does it enter, and which major cavity is that part of?","a":"The abdominopelvic cavity, part of the ventral cavity.","options":["The vertebral cavity, part of the dorsal cavity.","The abdominopelvic cavity, part of the ventral cavity.","The pelvic cavity, part of the dorsal cavity.","The pericardial cavity, part of the ventral cavity."],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The diaphragm is the muscular partition between the thoracic cavity above and the abdominopelvic cavity below. Passing down through it puts the probe in the abdominopelvic cavity. Both the thoracic and abdominopelvic cavities are subdivisions of the ventral (anterior) cavity.","notes":["The vertebral cavity runs through the vertebrae and holds the spinal cord. It is reached by going posterior into the spine, not down through the diaphragm.","Correct. Below the diaphragm is the abdominopelvic cavity, and it belongs to the ventral cavity.","The pelvic cavity is the lower part of the abdominopelvic cavity, and it is ventral, not dorsal. The probe would reach the abdominal part first.","The pericardial cavity surrounds the heart above the diaphragm, inside the mediastinum. Passing down through the diaphragm leaves the thoracic cavity instead of entering the pericardium."],"comp":6,"competencyId":"w1-body-cavities"},{"id":"N-a17d-7-1","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares the parietal pleura with the visceral pericardium?","a":"The parietal pleura lines the thoracic wall, and the visceral pericardium covers the heart.","options":["The parietal pleura covers the lung, and the visceral pericardium lines the heart's sac.","Both line the cavity wall, because both are layers of a serous membrane.","Both cover an organ surface, because both sit inside the thoracic cavity.","The parietal pleura lines the thoracic wall, and the visceral pericardium covers the heart."],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Parietal goes with the wall and visceral goes with the organ, in every serous membrane. The parietal pleura lines the thoracic wall, and the visceral pericardium, also called the epicardium, covers the outer surface of the heart.","notes":["These are reversed. The visceral pleura covers the lung, and the parietal pericardium is the layer of the sac around the heart.","Only parietal layers line the wall. The visceral pericardium is the layer on the heart's own surface.","Only visceral layers cover an organ. Sitting in the thoracic cavity does not change the parietal pleura's job of lining the wall.","Correct. Parietal lines the wall and visceral covers the organ, whichever membrane it is."],"comp":7,"competencyId":"w1-serous-membranes"},{"id":"N-a17d-8-1","dok":3,"q":"A CT shows a mass above the level of the sternal angle, between the sternum and the vertebral column, wrapped around the arch of the aorta. Which compartment is it in, and which other structure in that compartment could it press on?","a":"The superior mediastinum, which also holds the trachea.","options":["The superior mediastinum, which also holds the trachea.","The middle mediastinum, which also holds the azygos vein.","The anterior mediastinum, which also holds the heart.","The posterior mediastinum, which also holds the pulmonary trunk."],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The transverse thoracic plane, from the sternal angle to the disc between T4 and T5, separates the superior mediastinum above from the inferior mediastinum below. A mass above that plane around the aortic arch is in the superior mediastinum, which also holds the trachea, esophagus, brachiocephalic veins, and thymus.","notes":["Correct. Above the sternal angle is the superior mediastinum, where the trachea runs beside the aortic arch.","The middle mediastinum is below the sternal angle and holds the heart within the pericardium. The azygos vein is in the posterior mediastinum.","The anterior mediastinum is below the sternal angle, between the sternum and the pericardium. The heart sits in the middle mediastinum, not the anterior one.","The posterior mediastinum is below the sternal angle, behind the pericardium. The pulmonary trunk is in the middle mediastinum with the heart."],"comp":8,"competencyId":"w1-mediastinum"},{"id":"N-a17d-8-2","dok":3,"q":"On a model, the pericardial sac is lifted out with everything inside it. Which compartment has been emptied, and which large vessel leaving the heart went with it?","a":"The middle mediastinum, taking the pulmonary trunk with it.","options":["The posterior mediastinum, taking the thoracic aorta with it.","The superior mediastinum, taking the arch of the aorta with it.","The middle mediastinum, taking the pulmonary trunk with it.","The anterior mediastinum, taking the thymus with it."],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The middle mediastinum is the pericardium and the structures within it. Lifting out the sac removes the heart along with the start of its great vessels, the ascending aorta and the pulmonary trunk, which are listed in the middle compartment.","notes":["The posterior mediastinum lies behind the pericardium, between it and the vertebral column. The thoracic aorta stays there when the sac is removed.","The superior mediastinum is above the sternal angle, not inside the pericardium. The arch of the aorta stays there.","Correct. The pericardium and its contents are the middle mediastinum, which includes the pulmonary trunk.","The anterior mediastinum lies in front of the pericardium, behind the sternum. The thymus remnant stays there when the sac is removed."],"comp":8,"competencyId":"w1-mediastinum"},{"id":"N-a17d-8-3","dok":3,"q":"A stab wound passes straight back through the body of the sternum, below the sternal angle, and stops just after it enters the pericardium. Which compartments did it pass through, in order?","a":"The anterior mediastinum, then the middle mediastinum.","options":["The anterior mediastinum, then the middle mediastinum.","The superior mediastinum, then the posterior mediastinum.","The middle mediastinum, then the posterior mediastinum.","The anterior mediastinum, then the posterior mediastinum."],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Below the sternal angle the inferior mediastinum is divided front to back. The anterior mediastinum lies between the body of the sternum and the pericardium, and the middle mediastinum is the pericardium and what it holds. A wound going straight back from the sternum crosses the anterior compartment first and then enters the middle one.","notes":["Correct. Behind the sternum is the anterior compartment, and the pericardium marks the middle compartment.","The wound is below the sternal angle, so it never enters the superior mediastinum. The posterior mediastinum lies behind the pericardium, beyond where the wound stopped.","The wound starts at the sternum, so it must cross the anterior mediastinum first. It stopped on entering the pericardium, before the posterior compartment.","The anterior compartment is right, but the wound stopped in the pericardium. The posterior mediastinum is behind the pericardium and was not reached."],"comp":8,"competencyId":"w1-mediastinum"},{"id":"N-a17d-10-1","dok":3,"q":"On a lab model, both midclavicular planes plus the subcostal and transtubercular planes are drawn. A pin sits in the box directly below the right lumbar region. Which region is this, and which organ in it is the classic source of right lower quadrant pain?","a":"The right iliac region, which holds the appendix.","options":["The right iliac region, which holds the appendix.","The hypogastric region, which holds the urinary bladder.","The right hypochondriac region, which holds the gallbladder.","The left iliac region, which holds the sigmoid colon."],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The four planes draw a three by three grid. The right column runs right hypochondriac, right lumbar, and right iliac from top to bottom, so the box below the right lumbar region is the right iliac region. It holds the cecum and appendix, and the right lower quadrant is the classic site of appendicitis pain.","notes":["Correct. Below the right lumbar region is the right iliac region, home of the cecum and appendix.","The hypogastric region is the lower center box, below the umbilical region, not below the right lumbar region. It holds the urinary bladder.","The right hypochondriac region is above the right lumbar region, at the top of the right column. It holds the liver and gallbladder.","The left iliac region is on the opposite side, below the left lumbar region. It holds the sigmoid colon."],"comp":10,"competencyId":"w1-abdominopelvic-map"}]},{"id":"m1-cell","title":"Anatomy of the cell","summary":"plasma membrane, organelles, nucleus, cytoskeleton, cell junctions","mw":"2026-08-19","tr":"2026-08-20","cards":[{"id":"L00162","dok":1,"q":"Which structure forms the outer boundary that encloses a cell and separates it from its surroundings?","a":"The plasma membrane.","options":["The glycocalyx","The cytoskeleton","The plasma membrane","The nuclear envelope"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The plasma membrane is a flexible, semipermeable boundary made mostly of lipids and proteins. It wraps the whole cell, separates the cell's contents from the fluid outside, and controls what passes in and out. Every other structure in the cell sits inside it, which is why it counts as one of the three basic regions of the cell.","notes":["The glycocalyx is the sugar-rich coat of glycoproteins and glycolipids on the outer surface of the membrane. It sits on the boundary rather than forming it, and it is the right answer for questions about cell recognition.","The cytoskeleton is the internal framework of protein fibers (microfilaments, intermediate filaments, and microtubules) that supports the cell from inside. It would be the answer for a question about what gives the cell its internal shape and support.","Correct. The plasma membrane is the outer boundary that encloses the whole cell.","The nuclear envelope is a double membrane, but it surrounds only the nucleus, not the whole cell. Choose it if a question asks what separates the nucleus from the cytoplasm."],"lt":"t-cell-anatomy","lid":"c1","comp":11,"competencyId":"w1-generalized-cell"},{"id":"L00164","dok":1,"q":"Which region of the cell includes the cytosol plus the organelles and lies between the plasma membrane and the nucleus?","a":"The cytoplasm.","options":["The nucleolus","The nucleoplasm","The chromatin","The cytoplasm"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The cytoplasm is everything inside the plasma membrane except the nucleus: the organelles plus the cytosol, the watery fluid that suspends them. It is the middle of the three basic regions of the cell, lying between the plasma membrane on the outside and the nucleus on the inside. Many of the cell's everyday reactions take place here.","notes":["The nucleolus is a dense region inside the nucleus where ribosomal subunits are assembled. It is a single structure within the nucleus, not a whole region of the cell.","The nucleoplasm is the thick fluid inside the nucleus that supports the chromatin and nucleolus. It is the answer if a question asks about the fluid within the nuclear envelope rather than the region outside it.","Chromatin is the loose network of DNA and histone proteins inside the nucleus. Pick it for a question about the form DNA takes when the cell is not dividing.","Correct. The cytoplasm is the cytosol plus the organelles, between the membrane and the nucleus."],"lt":"t-cell-anatomy","lid":"c3","comp":11,"competencyId":"w1-generalized-cell"},{"id":"L00165","dok":1,"q":"Which membrane component is a double layer that forms the basic continuous fabric of the plasma membrane?","a":"The phospholipid bilayer.","options":["Integral proteins","Cholesterol","The glycocalyx","The phospholipid bilayer"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The phospholipid bilayer is two layers of phospholipids arranged with their water-loving heads facing the fluids inside and outside the cell and their water-fearing tails tucked toward the middle. This continuous double sheet is the basic fabric of the plasma membrane. Proteins, cholesterol, and carbohydrate chains are all set into it or attached to it.","notes":["Integral proteins are embedded in the bilayer, and many span it completely to form channels and transporters. They float within the fabric, so choose them for a question about proteins that cross the membrane.","Cholesterol molecules are wedged between the phospholipids, where they stabilize the membrane and moderate its fluidity. They are scattered within the fabric rather than forming it, so cholesterol is the answer for a question about membrane stability.","The glycocalyx is the carbohydrate-rich coat on the outer surface of the membrane. It sits on top of the bilayer and is the answer for questions about cell recognition, not the membrane's basic structure.","Correct. The phospholipid bilayer is the continuous double layer that makes up the membrane."],"lt":"t-cell-anatomy","lid":"c4","comp":12,"competencyId":"w1-plasma-membrane"},{"id":"L00166","dok":1,"q":"Which organelle is described as a stack of flattened membrane sacs?","a":"The Golgi apparatus.","options":["Mitochondria","Lysosomes","Rough ER","The Golgi apparatus"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The Golgi apparatus is a series of flattened membrane sacs, called cisternae, stacked like a pile of plates, with small vesicles budding from its edges. It receives proteins and lipids from the endoplasmic reticulum, modifies and sorts them, and packages them for delivery. The stacked, flattened shape is the feature that identifies it.","notes":["Mitochondria are bean-shaped organelles with a double membrane whose inner layer folds into cristae. Pick them when the description mentions a double membrane or ATP production.","Lysosomes are single round membrane sacs filled with digestive enzymes. Choose them for a question about breaking down worn-out parts or debris, not a stack of flat sacs.","Rough ER is a network of membrane sacs and tubes studded with ribosomes and continuous with the nuclear envelope. It is the answer if the description mentions ribosomes on the surface.","Correct. A stack of flattened sacs is the Golgi apparatus."],"lt":"t-cell-anatomy","lid":"c5","comp":14,"competencyId":"w1-organelles"},{"id":"L00167","dok":1,"q":"Which organelle has a double membrane?","a":"Mitochondria.","options":["Lysosomes","Vesicles","Smooth ER","Mitochondria"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"A mitochondrion is enclosed by two membranes. The outer membrane is smooth, and the inner membrane folds into shelves called cristae, which add surface area for making ATP. Lysosomes, the ER, and vesicles are each surrounded by only a single membrane, so the double membrane is a quick way to pick out a mitochondrion.","notes":["Lysosomes are surrounded by a single membrane that holds in their digestive enzymes. They would be the answer for a question about the cell's digestion and recycling of worn parts.","Vesicles are small sacs bounded by a single membrane that carry materials between organelles or to the cell surface. They are the answer for questions about transport within the cell.","Smooth ER is a network of single-membrane tubes without ribosomes. Choose it for a question about making lipids, detoxifying drugs, or storing calcium.","Correct. Mitochondria have a smooth outer membrane and a folded inner membrane."],"lt":"t-cell-anatomy","lid":"c6","comp":14,"competencyId":"w1-organelles"},{"id":"L00168","dok":1,"q":"Which non-membranous structure is made of a large and a small subunit, each built of ribosomal RNA and protein?","a":"Ribosomes.","options":["Ribosomes","Peroxisomes","Microtubules","Centrioles"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Ribosomes are small particles with no membrane around them. Each one has a large and a small subunit made of ribosomal RNA and protein, and the two subunits work together to read messenger RNA and build proteins. Ribosomes float free in the cytosol or attach to the rough ER, which is what gives rough ER its bumpy look.","notes":["Correct. Ribosomes are built of two subunits of ribosomal RNA and protein.","Peroxisomes are membrane-bound sacs of enzymes, so they do not fit a question about a non-membranous structure. They are the answer for breaking down fatty acids and hydrogen peroxide or detoxifying alcohol.","Microtubules are hollow tubes made of the protein tubulin, part of the cytoskeleton. Pick them for a question about the tracks vesicles travel on or the core of cilia and flagella.","Centrioles are short cylinders made of microtubules in a 9 plus 0 arrangement, found in pairs within the centrosome. They are the answer for a question about organizing the spindle during cell division."],"lt":"t-cell-anatomy","lid":"c7","comp":14,"competencyId":"w1-organelles"},{"id":"L00169","dok":1,"q":"Which structure is the dense region inside the nucleus where ribosomal subunits are assembled?","a":"The nucleolus.","options":["The nucleolus","The nucleoplasm","Chromatin","The nuclear envelope"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The nucleolus is a dense, darkly staining region inside the nucleus with no membrane of its own. It is where ribosomal RNA is made and combined with proteins to assemble ribosomal subunits, which then leave through the nuclear pores. A cell that makes a lot of protein often has a large, obvious nucleolus.","notes":["Correct. The nucleolus is where ribosomal subunits are built.","The nucleoplasm is the thick fluid inside the nucleus that the chromatin and nucleolus sit in. It is the answer for a question about the fluid of the nucleus, not a dense body.","Chromatin is the network of DNA wound on histone proteins spread through the nucleus. It is the answer for a question about the form DNA takes when the cell is not dividing.","The nuclear envelope is the double membrane that surrounds the whole nucleus. Choose it for a question about what separates the nucleus from the cytoplasm."],"lt":"t-cell-anatomy","lid":"c8","comp":13,"competencyId":"w1-nucleus"},{"id":"L00170","dok":1,"q":"What is the loose, threadlike form of DNA wound on proteins, present when the cell is not dividing, called?","a":"Chromatin.","options":["Nucleolus","Chromatin","Chromosomes","Nucleoplasm"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Chromatin is DNA wrapped around histone proteins and spread loosely through the nucleus as fine threads. This loose form lets the cell read its genes during normal activity. When the cell gets ready to divide, the chromatin coils tightly into compact chromosomes that are visible under the microscope.","notes":["The nucleolus is a dense region of the nucleus where ribosomal subunits are assembled. Pick it for a question about ribosome production, not about the form of DNA.","Correct. Chromatin is the loose form of DNA in a cell that is not dividing.","Chromosomes are the same DNA coiled into tight rod-shaped bodies, visible during cell division. Choose them when the question describes a condensed form seen in a dividing cell.","Nucleoplasm is the fluid of the nucleus, not DNA itself. It would be the answer for a question about the material that suspends the chromatin and nucleolus."],"lt":"t-cell-anatomy","lid":"c9","comp":13,"competencyId":"w1-nucleus"},{"id":"L00171","dok":1,"q":"Which surface specialization of the plasma membrane is a single long extension, the only human example being the sperm tail?","a":"The flagellum.","options":["The flagellum","Microvilli","Cilia","The glycocalyx"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"A flagellum is a single long, whip-like projection that moves the whole cell through fluid. Its core is built of microtubules in a 9 plus 2 arrangement, the same pattern found in cilia. In the human body the only cell with a flagellum is the sperm, whose tail drives it forward.","notes":["Correct. The sperm tail is a flagellum, the only one in the human body.","Microvilli are tiny finger-like folds of the plasma membrane that increase surface area for absorption, as in the small intestine. They do not move the cell, so they are the answer for absorption questions.","Cilia are short, numerous projections that beat to move substances such as mucus across the cell surface, as in the respiratory tract. They move material past the cell rather than propelling the cell itself.","The glycocalyx is a fuzzy coat of glycoproteins and glycolipids on the cell surface, not a projection. It is the answer for questions about cell recognition."],"lt":"t-cell-anatomy","lid":"c10","comp":12,"competencyId":"w1-plasma-membrane"},{"id":"L00172","dok":1,"q":"Which cytoskeletal fibers are the thinnest and are made of actin?","a":"Microfilaments.","options":["Microtubules","Intermediate filaments","Microfilaments","Centrioles"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Microfilaments are the thinnest fibers of the cytoskeleton and are made of the protein actin. They help set the cell's shape and allow movement, for example at the cell surface and in muscle contraction. Of the three cytoskeletal fibers, microfilaments are the thinnest, intermediate filaments are in the middle, and microtubules are the thickest.","notes":["Microtubules are the thickest cytoskeletal fibers, hollow tubes made of tubulin. They are the answer for questions about vesicle tracks or the core of cilia, flagella, and centrioles.","Intermediate filaments are rope-like fibers of middle thickness made of various fibrous proteins. Choose them for a question about resisting tension and giving the cell mechanical strength.","Correct. Microfilaments are thin actin fibers.","Centrioles are short cylinders built from microtubules, not a type of cytoskeletal fiber. They are the answer for a question about organizing the spindle during cell division."],"lt":"t-cell-anatomy","lid":"c11","comp":14,"competencyId":"w1-organelles"},{"id":"L00173","dok":1,"q":"Which structure is the double membrane that encloses the nucleus?","a":"The nuclear envelope.","options":["The plasma membrane","The nuclear envelope","The glycocalyx","The inner mitochondrial membrane"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The nuclear envelope is a double membrane that wraps the nucleus and separates it from the cytoplasm, keeping a controlled environment around the DNA. It is pierced by nuclear pores that let RNA and proteins pass in and out. Its outer membrane is continuous with the rough ER, which links the nucleus to the endomembrane system.","notes":["The plasma membrane is a single bilayer around the whole cell, not around the nucleus. It is the answer for a question about the cell's outer boundary.","Correct. The nuclear envelope is the double membrane around the nucleus.","The glycocalyx is a carbohydrate coat on the outside of the plasma membrane, not a membrane around the nucleus. Choose it for a question about cell recognition.","The inner mitochondrial membrane is one of the two membranes of a mitochondrion, folded into cristae for ATP production. It belongs to the mitochondrion, so pick it for a question about where cristae are found."],"lt":"t-cell-anatomy","lid":"c12","comp":13,"competencyId":"w1-nucleus"},{"id":"L00174","dok":2,"q":"A membrane protein is firmly embedded in the bilayer and spans it completely. How is this protein classified?","a":"As an integral protein.","options":["As a glycolipid","As an integral protein","As a peripheral protein","As cholesterol"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Integral proteins are set firmly into the phospholipid bilayer, and most pass all the way through it from one side to the other. Because they cross the membrane, they can form channels, carriers, and receptors that connect the inside of the cell with the outside. Proteins that only rest on one surface are called peripheral proteins instead.","notes":["A glycolipid is a lipid with a sugar chain attached, part of the cell's surface coat. It is not a protein at all, so it fits a question about cell recognition molecules instead.","Correct. A protein embedded in and spanning the bilayer is an integral protein.","Peripheral proteins sit loosely on the inner or outer surface of the membrane and are not embedded in it. They would be the answer if the protein were only attached to one face.","Cholesterol is a lipid wedged among the phospholipids that stabilizes the membrane. It is not a protein, so choose it for a question about membrane fluidity and stability."],"lt":"t-cell-anatomy","lid":"c13","comp":12,"competencyId":"w1-plasma-membrane"},{"id":"L00175","dok":2,"q":"A protein sits loosely attached to only the inner face of the membrane and is not embedded in the bilayer. Which type of membrane protein is it?","a":"A peripheral protein.","options":["A channel protein","An integral protein","A transmembrane protein","A peripheral protein"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Peripheral proteins are attached loosely to the inner or outer face of the membrane, often bound to an integral protein, rather than being buried in the bilayer. Because they do not cross the membrane, they cannot form channels. Many act as enzymes or help connect the cytoskeleton to the membrane.","notes":["A channel protein is a type of integral protein that spans the membrane and forms a pore for ions or water. It must cross the bilayer, so it fits a question about passage through the membrane.","Integral proteins are embedded in the bilayer, and most span it completely. They would be the answer if the protein were firmly set into the membrane rather than resting on one face.","A transmembrane protein is an integral protein that crosses the entire bilayer. That is the opposite of a protein resting on one surface, so pick it only when the protein passes from one side to the other.","Correct. A protein resting loosely on one face is a peripheral protein."],"lt":"t-cell-anatomy","lid":"c14","comp":12,"competencyId":"w1-plasma-membrane"},{"id":"L00176","dok":2,"q":"A cell is densely packed with rough ER. Which structure would be most abundant studding that organelle's surface?","a":"Ribosomes.","options":["Ribosomes","Centrioles","Microvilli","Lysosomes"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Rough ER gets its name from the ribosomes attached to the outside of its membranes, which give it a bumpy look. These bound ribosomes build proteins that will be secreted or placed into membranes, and the new proteins enter the ER as they are made. A cell full of rough ER is therefore covered in ribosomes.","notes":["Correct. Ribosomes are what make rough ER rough.","Centrioles are paired cylinders inside the centrosome near the nucleus. They organize the spindle during cell division and are not attached to the ER.","Microvilli are finger-like folds of the plasma membrane on the cell's outer surface. They increase surface area for absorption and have nothing to do with the ER.","Lysosomes are separate sacs of digestive enzymes floating in the cytoplasm. They are the answer for a question about breaking down worn-out parts, not about what covers rough ER."],"lt":"t-cell-anatomy","lid":"c15","comp":14,"competencyId":"w1-organelles"},{"id":"L00177","dok":2,"q":"Which membranous organelle has a tubular membrane network with no ribosomes attached?","a":"Smooth ER.","options":["Rough ER","The Golgi apparatus","Smooth ER","Peroxisomes"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Smooth ER is a network of membrane tubes with no ribosomes attached, which gives it a smooth look. It makes lipids, helps detoxify drugs, and stores calcium ions. The absence of ribosomes is what separates it from rough ER, which is studded with them and makes proteins.","notes":["Rough ER is the same kind of membrane network but with ribosomes on its surface. It is the answer when the question describes a ribosome-studded network that makes proteins.","The Golgi apparatus is a stack of flattened sacs rather than a tubular network. Choose it for a question about modifying, sorting, and packaging proteins.","Correct. Smooth ER is the ER without ribosomes.","Peroxisomes are small round membrane sacs of enzymes, not a connected network. They are the answer for breaking down fatty acids and hydrogen peroxide."],"lt":"t-cell-anatomy","lid":"c16","comp":14,"competencyId":"w1-organelles"},{"id":"L00178","dok":2,"q":"A slide shows a cell surface covered with many short hair-like extensions. Which surface specialization is this?","a":"Cilia.","options":["Cilia","Glycocalyx","Microvilli","A flagellum"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Cilia are short, hair-like projections, and a single cell can carry hundreds of them. Each has a core of microtubules in a 9 plus 2 arrangement, which lets cilia beat in coordinated waves. In the airways they sweep a layer of mucus and trapped particles up toward the throat.","notes":["Correct. Many short hair-like extensions are cilia.","The glycocalyx is a thin carbohydrate coat on the cell surface, not a projection. Pick it for a question about cell recognition.","Microvilli are much smaller finger-like folds of the membrane that do not beat. They add surface area for absorption and appear as a fuzzy brush border on intestinal cells.","A flagellum is a single long projection that moves the whole cell, as in the sperm tail. It is the answer when the cell has one long whip rather than many short hairs."],"lt":"t-cell-anatomy","lid":"c17","comp":12,"competencyId":"w1-plasma-membrane"},{"id":"L00179","dok":2,"q":"Which organelle's membrane is continuous with the outer membrane of the nuclear envelope?","a":"Rough ER.","options":["The centrosome","Rough ER","Mitochondria","Lysosomes"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The outer membrane of the nuclear envelope is continuous with the membranes of the rough ER, so the two form one connected membrane system. This connection places the nucleus at the start of the endomembrane system. Messenger RNA leaving through the nuclear pores can reach ribosomes on the nearby rough ER.","notes":["The centrosome sits near the nucleus but has no membrane at all, so it cannot share membrane with the nuclear envelope. It is the answer for a question about organizing microtubules.","Correct. The rough ER is continuous with the outer nuclear membrane.","Mitochondria have their own separate double membrane and are not connected to the nuclear envelope. They are the answer for questions about ATP production.","Lysosomes are separate membrane sacs that are not joined to the nuclear envelope. They are the answer for a question about digesting worn-out parts."],"lt":"t-cell-anatomy","lid":"c18","comp":14,"competencyId":"w1-organelles"},{"id":"L00180","dok":2,"q":"Which non-membranous structure organizes the microtubules of the cell and lies near the nucleus?","a":"The centrosome.","options":["The nucleolus","Smooth ER","The Golgi apparatus","The centrosome"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The centrosome is a region of cytoplasm near the nucleus that serves as the cell's microtubule-organizing center. It holds a pair of centrioles, and microtubules grow outward from it. During cell division it helps build the mitotic spindle that separates the chromosomes.","notes":["The nucleolus is inside the nucleus, not beside it, and it assembles ribosomal subunits. It would be the answer for a question about ribosome production.","Smooth ER is a membranous network, so it does not fit a question about a non-membranous structure. Choose it for making lipids, detoxifying drugs, or storing calcium.","The Golgi apparatus is a membranous stack of flattened sacs that modifies and packages proteins. It is not the organizing center for microtubules.","Correct. The centrosome is the microtubule-organizing center."],"lt":"t-cell-anatomy","lid":"c19","comp":14,"competencyId":"w1-organelles"},{"id":"L00181","dok":2,"q":"Where is cholesterol positioned in the plasma membrane?","a":"Wedged among the phospholipids of the bilayer.","options":["Lining the channels of the nuclear pores","Studding the outer surface of the rough ER","Forming the fuzzy coat on the outer surface","Wedged among the phospholipids of the bilayer"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Cholesterol molecules are tucked in between the phospholipids of the bilayer. From that position they stabilize the membrane and keep its fluidity steady as temperature changes, so the membrane does not become too stiff or too loose. They are part of the membrane fabric rather than a coat or a separate layer.","notes":["Nuclear pores are lined by proteins and pierce the nuclear envelope. That location fits a question about how RNA leaves the nucleus, not where cholesterol sits.","The surface of rough ER is studded with ribosomes. That location is correct for a question about where secretory proteins are built.","The fuzzy external coat is the glycocalyx, made of glycoproteins and glycolipids. That location would be correct for a question about the molecules used in cell recognition.","Correct. Cholesterol sits between the phospholipids."],"lt":"t-cell-anatomy","lid":"c20","comp":12,"competencyId":"w1-plasma-membrane"},{"id":"L00182","dok":2,"q":"Which paired structures sit within the centrosome and organize the spindle during cell division?","a":"Centrioles.","options":["Ribosomes","Nuclear pores","Microfilaments","Centrioles"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Centrioles are short cylinders of microtubules arranged in a 9 plus 0 pattern, found as a pair inside the centrosome. They do not move the cell; instead they help organize the mitotic spindle that pulls chromosomes apart during division. The 9 plus 0 pattern goes with organizing, while the 9 plus 2 pattern of cilia and flagella goes with movement.","notes":["Ribosomes are particles of RNA and protein that build proteins, either free in the cytosol or on rough ER. Pick them for a protein synthesis question.","Nuclear pores are protein-lined channels through the nuclear envelope. They control traffic into and out of the nucleus and play no role in building the spindle.","Microfilaments are thin actin fibers of the cytoskeleton that support cell shape and movement. They are not paired structures in the centrosome.","Correct. The paired centrioles in the centrosome organize the spindle."],"lt":"t-cell-anatomy","lid":"c21","comp":14,"competencyId":"w1-organelles"},{"id":"L00183","dok":2,"q":"The glycocalyx is built of which kinds of molecules?","a":"Glycoproteins and glycolipids.","options":["RNA and protein","Glycoproteins and glycolipids","Actin and tubulin","Phospholipids only"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The glycocalyx is the fuzzy coat on the outside of the plasma membrane, made of the sugar chains of glycoproteins and glycolipids. Its pattern of sugars differs between cell types, so it works like an identification tag. Cells use it to recognize one another, and the immune system uses it to tell self from non-self.","notes":["RNA and protein are the building materials of ribosomes. Choose this pair for a question about what makes up the large and small ribosomal subunits.","Correct. The glycocalyx is made of glycoproteins and glycolipids.","Actin and tubulin are the proteins that build the cytoskeleton: actin forms microfilaments and tubulin forms microtubules. They are the answer for a question about the cell's internal framework.","Phospholipids form the bilayer itself, the basic fabric of the membrane. They are the right answer for what the membrane is built from, not for the sugar coat on top of it."],"lt":"t-cell-anatomy","lid":"c22","comp":12,"competencyId":"w1-plasma-membrane"},{"id":"L00184","dok":2,"q":"Microtubules of the cytoskeleton are best described structurally as which of the following?","a":"The thickest fibers, hollow tubes.","options":["Rope-like solid fibers","The thickest fibers, hollow tubes","The thinnest fibers, made of actin","Paired rod-shaped structures"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Microtubules are the thickest fibers of the cytoskeleton and are hollow tubes built from the protein tubulin. They act as tracks that vesicles and organelles travel along, and they form the core of centrioles, cilia, and flagella. Their hollow, tube-like shape sets them apart from the solid microfilaments and intermediate filaments.","notes":["Rope-like solid fibers of middle thickness are intermediate filaments. That description fits a question about resisting tension and giving the cell strength.","Correct. Microtubules are the thickest cytoskeletal fibers and are hollow.","The thinnest fibers made of actin are microfilaments. That description is correct for a question about cell shape and movement at the cell surface.","Paired rod-shaped structures in the centrosome are centrioles. They are built from microtubules but are organelles, not a description of the fibers themselves."],"lt":"t-cell-anatomy","lid":"c23","comp":14,"competencyId":"w1-organelles"},{"id":"L00185","dok":2,"q":"Which structures are the protein-lined channels through the nuclear envelope that regulate traffic in both directions?","a":"Nuclear pores.","options":["Nuclear pores","Microvilli","Vesicles","Gap junctions"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Nuclear pores are openings through both layers of the nuclear envelope, each lined by a ring of proteins. They control traffic in both directions: messenger RNA and ribosomal subunits leave the nucleus, and proteins needed inside the nucleus enter. This lets the DNA stay protected inside while its instructions still reach the cytoplasm.","notes":["Correct. Nuclear pores are the channels through the nuclear envelope.","Microvilli are finger-like folds of the plasma membrane that increase surface area for absorption. They are on the outside of the cell, not in the nuclear envelope.","Vesicles are small membrane sacs that carry materials between organelles or to the cell surface. They move through the cytoplasm rather than forming channels in the nuclear envelope.","Gap junctions are protein channels that connect the cytoplasm of two neighboring cells. They are the answer for questions about cell-to-cell communication, not traffic through the nuclear envelope."],"lt":"t-cell-anatomy","lid":"c24","comp":13,"competencyId":"w1-nucleus"},{"id":"L00186","dok":2,"q":"Among the membranous organelles, which one is a small membrane sac that moves substances within the cell?","a":"A vesicle.","options":["A vesicle","A microtubule","A centriole","A ribosome"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"A vesicle is a small sac enclosed by a single membrane that carries materials from one place to another inside the cell. Transport vesicles move products from the ER to the Golgi apparatus, and secretory vesicles carry finished products to the plasma membrane. Because they are wrapped in membrane, vesicles can fuse with other membranes to deliver their contents.","notes":["Correct. Vesicles are the small membrane sacs that move materials.","A microtubule is a hollow protein tube of the cytoskeleton with no membrane. Vesicles travel along microtubules, so pick it for a question about the tracks rather than the carrier.","A centriole is a non-membranous cylinder of microtubules in the centrosome. It is the answer for a question about organizing the spindle during cell division.","A ribosome has no membrane; it is a particle of RNA and protein that builds proteins. It is the answer for a question about protein synthesis."],"lt":"t-cell-anatomy","lid":"c25","comp":14,"competencyId":"w1-organelles"},{"id":"L00187","dok":2,"q":"Which cytoskeletal fibers are rope-like and resist pulling forces to give the cell strength?","a":"Intermediate filaments.","options":["Microtubules","Reticular fibers","Microfilaments","Intermediate filaments"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Intermediate filaments are rope-like fibers made of various tough fibrous proteins, with a thickness between microfilaments and microtubules. They resist pulling forces, which keeps cells from tearing apart under tension. In the skin they anchor into desmosomes and help hold the cells of the epidermis together.","notes":["Microtubules are the thickest cytoskeletal fibers, hollow tubes of tubulin. Choose them for a question about vesicle transport tracks or the core of cilia and flagella.","Reticular fibers are fine branching fibers in the matrix of connective tissue, outside the cells. They support soft organs such as lymph nodes and the spleen and are not part of the cytoskeleton.","Microfilaments are the thinnest cytoskeletal fibers, made of actin. They are the answer for questions about cell shape and movement.","Correct. Intermediate filaments are the rope-like fibers that resist tension."],"lt":"t-cell-anatomy","lid":"c26","comp":14,"competencyId":"w1-organelles"},{"id":"L00188","dok":2,"q":"An electron micrograph shows an organelle that is a membrane network studded with ribosomes. Which organelle is it?","a":"Rough ER.","options":["Smooth ER","Mitochondria","Rough ER","The Golgi apparatus"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The ribosomes stuck to its membrane surface identify rough ER. These bound ribosomes make proteins that will be secreted from the cell or built into membranes, and the new proteins pass into the ER as they are made. From there the proteins are sent on to the Golgi apparatus in transport vesicles.","notes":["Smooth ER has the same kind of membrane network but no ribosomes on its surface. It is the answer when the network is described as smooth and involved in lipid production or detoxification.","Mitochondria are separate organelles with a double membrane and inner folds called cristae. They are the answer when the description mentions ATP production or cristae.","Correct. A ribosome-studded membrane network is rough ER.","The Golgi apparatus is a stack of flattened sacs without ribosomes. Pick it when the description is of stacked sacs that package and ship products."],"lt":"t-cell-anatomy","lid":"c27","comp":14,"competencyId":"w1-organelles"},{"id":"L00190","dok":2,"q":"A cell contains a small membrane sac filled with digestive enzymes that breaks down worn-out parts. Which organelle is described?","a":"A lysosome.","options":["A ribosome","A peroxisome","A lysosome","A mitochondrion"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"A lysosome is a single-membrane sac filled with digestive enzymes. It breaks down large molecules, worn-out organelles, and engulfed bacteria or debris, a cleanup job that includes recycling the cell's own parts. When a lysosomal enzyme is missing, as in Tay-Sachs disease, undigested material builds up inside the cell.","notes":["A ribosome has no membrane and does not digest anything; it builds proteins. Choose it for a question about protein synthesis.","A peroxisome is also a small membrane sac of enzymes, but its enzymes break down fatty acids and hydrogen peroxide and detoxify alcohol. Pick it for a question about detoxification in liver or kidney cells.","Correct. A membrane sac of digestive enzymes is a lysosome.","A mitochondrion is a double-membraned organelle that produces ATP. It is the answer for an energy production question, not for digesting worn-out parts."],"lt":"t-cell-anatomy","lid":"c29","comp":14,"competencyId":"w1-organelles"},{"id":"L00191","dok":2,"q":"A structure inside the nucleus appears tightly coiled and is visible only while the cell is dividing. What is it?","a":"A chromosome.","options":["A nuclear pore","The nucleolus","A chromosome","Chromatin"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Before a cell divides, its loose chromatin coils tightly into compact, rod-shaped chromosomes. Coiling makes the DNA visible under the light microscope and lets it be divided cleanly between the two new cells. When the cell is not dividing, the same DNA relaxes back into chromatin.","notes":["A nuclear pore is a protein-lined opening in the nuclear envelope. It controls traffic in and out of the nucleus and does not coil or appear only during division.","The nucleolus is a dense region of the nucleus where ribosomal subunits are assembled. It is present in a non-dividing cell and is not a coiled form of DNA.","Correct. Tightly coiled DNA visible during division is a chromosome.","Chromatin is the same DNA in its loose, threadlike form, seen when the cell is not dividing. It is the answer for a question about DNA during normal cell activity."],"lt":"t-cell-anatomy","lid":"c30","comp":13,"competencyId":"w1-nucleus"},{"id":"L00192","dok":2,"q":"A slide shows finger-like extensions covering the apical surface of an absorptive cell. Which surface specialization is shown?","a":"Microvilli.","options":["Glycocalyx","Cilia","A flagellum","Microvilli"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Microvilli are tiny finger-like folds of the plasma membrane packed side by side on the free (apical) surface of absorptive cells. They multiply the surface area available for taking in nutrients, and under the light microscope they look like a fuzzy brush border. They are supported by microfilaments and do not beat the way cilia do.","notes":["The glycocalyx is a carbohydrate coat on the membrane surface, not a set of projections. Pick it for a question about cell identity and recognition.","Cilia are longer hair-like projections that beat to sweep mucus across a surface, as in the trachea. They would be the answer for a lining that moves material rather than absorbs it.","A flagellum is a single long tail that propels a whole cell, found only on sperm in humans. It does not cover an absorptive surface.","Correct. Finger-like extensions on an absorptive surface are microvilli."],"lt":"t-cell-anatomy","lid":"c31","comp":12,"competencyId":"w1-plasma-membrane"},{"id":"L00194","dok":2,"q":"A cell has lost its nucleus and organelles entirely, freeing interior space. Which cell type shows this structural adaptation?","a":"The red blood cell.","options":["The white blood cell","The red blood cell","The skeletal muscle cell","The sperm cell"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"A mature red blood cell pushes out its nucleus and loses its organelles as it develops, so it is described as anucleated. Losing these parts frees the space inside the cell for hemoglobin, the protein that carries oxygen. The trade-off is that the cell cannot repair or divide itself, so it lasts only a few months.","notes":["White blood cells keep their nucleus and organelles and can change shape to squeeze between other cells. Choose one for a question about a cell that migrates to a site of infection.","Correct. The mature red blood cell has no nucleus and no organelles.","A skeletal muscle cell keeps its organelles, has many nuclei, and is packed with mitochondria for energy. It is the answer for a question about a long, multinucleated cell built for contraction.","A sperm cell keeps a nucleus, since it must deliver its DNA to the egg, and it moves with a flagellum. Pick it for a question about the only human cell with a flagellum."],"lt":"t-cell-anatomy","lid":"c33","comp":11,"competencyId":"w1-generalized-cell"},{"id":"L00195","dok":1,"q":"A composite diagram is drawn to show every organelle and the features shared by most body cells, though no real cell looks exactly like it. What is this model called?","a":"The generalized cell.","options":["The generalized cell","The cytoskeleton","The glycocalyx","The endomembrane system"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The generalized cell is a teaching model that combines the features shared by most body cells into one drawing: a plasma membrane, cytoplasm with its organelles, and a nucleus. No real cell looks exactly like it, because each cell type has more of some organelles and fewer of others to match its job. The model gives a common starting point before you compare specialized cells.","notes":["Correct. The composite teaching model is the generalized cell.","The cytoskeleton is the internal framework of protein fibers inside a cell. It is the answer for a question about cell shape, support, and internal transport.","The glycocalyx is the sugar-rich coat on the outside of the plasma membrane. It is the answer for a question about cell recognition, not a model of the whole cell.","The endomembrane system is the connected set of membranous organelles that make, modify, and ship proteins and lipids, such as the ER, Golgi apparatus, and vesicles. It is one part of a cell, not a model of a whole cell."],"lt":"t-cell-anatomy","lid":"c34","comp":11,"competencyId":"w1-generalized-cell"},{"id":"L00196","dok":2,"q":"A cell type changes its shape freely so it can squeeze between other cells to reach a site of infection. Which cell shows this structural adaptation?","a":"The white blood cell.","options":["The red blood cell","The skeletal muscle cell","The white blood cell","The nerve cell"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"White blood cells must leave the bloodstream and travel through tissues to reach an injury or infection. They can change shape freely, which lets them squeeze through gaps between the cells of capillary walls and crawl through tissue. Most body cells stay in one place, so this ability to migrate is a special adaptation.","notes":["Red blood cells are flexible enough to bend through narrow capillaries, but they stay inside blood vessels and do not migrate into tissues. Their key adaptation is losing the nucleus to make room for hemoglobin.","A skeletal muscle cell is long, cylindrical, and anchored within a muscle, so it does not travel between other cells. It is the answer for a question about a cell packed with mitochondria for contraction.","Correct. White blood cells change shape to migrate through tissue.","A nerve cell (neuron) stays in place for life and sends signals along long processes. It would be the answer for a question about a cell built to carry electrical signals, not one that migrates."],"lt":"t-cell-anatomy","lid":"c35","comp":11,"competencyId":"w1-generalized-cell"},{"id":"L00197","dok":2,"q":"On the secretory pathway, which structure buds from the rough ER and carries a newly made protein to the Golgi apparatus?","a":"A transport vesicle.","options":["A nuclear pore","A transport vesicle","A lysosome","A secretory vesicle"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"A transport vesicle buds off the rough ER carrying newly made protein and travels to the Golgi apparatus, where it fuses and delivers its cargo. The Golgi then modifies and sorts the product and packs it into a secretory vesicle for the last leg to the plasma membrane. The transport vesicle works before the Golgi, and the secretory vesicle works after it.","notes":["A nuclear pore is a channel in the nuclear envelope that lets messenger RNA leave the nucleus. It comes before the ER on the route and does not carry protein away from it.","Correct. The transport vesicle carries protein from the rough ER to the Golgi.","A lysosome is a sac of digestive enzymes that breaks down worn parts. It is the answer for a question about digestion inside the cell, not for carrying a product between stops.","A secretory vesicle buds from the Golgi apparatus and carries the finished product to the plasma membrane for release. It is the answer for the step after the Golgi, not the step leaving the ER."],"lt":"t-cell-anatomy","lid":"c36","comp":14,"competencyId":"w1-organelles"},{"id":"L00198","dok":2,"q":"On the secretory route, which cytoskeletal structures serve as the tracks the vesicles follow between stops?","a":"Microtubules.","options":["Nuclear pores","Microfilaments","Microtubules","Intermediate filaments"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Microtubules are hollow tubes of tubulin that run through the cytoplasm like a network of rails. Vesicles and organelles are moved along them from one part of the cell to another, including from the ER to the Golgi apparatus and from the Golgi to the plasma membrane. This transport role is one of the main jobs of the cytoskeleton.","notes":["Nuclear pores are openings in the nuclear envelope for RNA and proteins. They are gateways in and out of the nucleus, not tracks through the cytoplasm.","Microfilaments are thin actin fibers that support the cell surface and allow cell movement and changes in shape. They would be the answer for a question about the cytoskeleton's role in contraction or surface support.","Correct. Vesicles travel along microtubules.","Intermediate filaments are rope-like fibers that resist tension and give cells mechanical strength. Pick them for a question about holding cells together under stress."],"lt":"t-cell-anatomy","lid":"c37","comp":14,"competencyId":"w1-organelles"},{"id":"L00199","dok":2,"q":"A cell takes in outside material by enclosing it inside a newly formed vesicle. Which process is this, the reverse of the outward secretory path?","a":"Endocytosis.","options":["Exocytosis","Endocytosis","Budding from the ER","Packaging at the Golgi"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"In endocytosis, the plasma membrane folds inward around material outside the cell and pinches off to form a new vesicle inside the cell. It runs in the opposite direction from exocytosis, where a vesicle from inside fuses with the plasma membrane and releases its contents outward. Phagocytosis, where a cell engulfs bacteria or debris, is one type of endocytosis.","notes":["Exocytosis moves material out of the cell when a vesicle fuses with the plasma membrane. It is the answer for the last step of the secretory pathway, the direction opposite to this question.","Correct. Taking material in by forming a vesicle is endocytosis.","Transport vesicles bud from the ER early in the outward route, carrying new protein to the Golgi. That step happens inside the cell and moves products outward, not inward.","The Golgi apparatus packages finished products into secretory vesicles partway along the outward route. That step still moves material toward the outside, so it is not the reverse process."],"lt":"t-cell-anatomy","lid":"c38","comp":14,"competencyId":"w1-organelles"},{"id":"L00200","dok":2,"q":"A cell is long and cylindrical, has many nuclei, and is densely packed with mitochondria. Which cell fits this structural description?","a":"The skeletal muscle cell.","options":["The skeletal muscle cell","The nerve cell","The red blood cell","The white blood cell"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"A skeletal muscle cell, also called a muscle fiber, is long and cylindrical and contains many nuclei. It is packed with mitochondria because contraction uses a large, steady supply of ATP. Cells that do the most work tend to have the most mitochondria, so organelle numbers give a clue to a cell's job.","notes":["Correct. A long, multinucleated cell packed with mitochondria is a skeletal muscle cell.","A nerve cell has one nucleus in its cell body and long branching processes rather than a cylindrical shape. Pick it for a question about a cell that carries electrical signals.","A mature red blood cell has no nucleus and no mitochondria, which leaves room for hemoglobin. It would be the answer for a question about an anucleated cell.","A white blood cell has a single nucleus and changes shape to squeeze between cells. Choose it for a question about a cell that migrates to a site of infection."],"lt":"t-cell-anatomy","lid":"c39","comp":14,"competencyId":"w1-organelles"},{"id":"L00201","dok":3,"q":"Trace the correct order a secreted protein follows through the endomembrane system after it is made.","a":"Rough ER, transport vesicle, Golgi apparatus, secretory vesicle, exocytosis.","options":["Rough ER, transport vesicle, Golgi apparatus, secretory vesicle, exocytosis","Rough ER, Golgi apparatus, transport vesicle, exocytosis, secretory vesicle","Transport vesicle, rough ER, Golgi apparatus, exocytosis, secretory vesicle","Golgi apparatus, rough ER, transport vesicle, secretory vesicle, exocytosis"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"A protein made for export is built on ribosomes of the rough ER, and then a transport vesicle carries it to the Golgi apparatus. The Golgi modifies, sorts, and packages it into a secretory vesicle, which travels to the plasma membrane and releases it by exocytosis. Each stop has one job in turn: make, carry, finish, carry, release.","notes":["Correct. Rough ER, transport vesicle, Golgi apparatus, secretory vesicle, then exocytosis.","This order skips the transport vesicle between the ER and the Golgi and places exocytosis before the secretory vesicle. Release at the membrane can only happen after a secretory vesicle has carried the product there.","This order starts with a transport vesicle, but a vesicle has nothing to carry until the rough ER makes the protein. It also places exocytosis before the secretory vesicle that delivers the product.","This order starts at the Golgi apparatus, but the Golgi cannot work on a protein until the rough ER has made it. Students choose it when they remember the Golgi as the shipping center and forget that manufacturing comes first."],"lt":"t-cell-anatomy","lid":"c40","comp":14,"competencyId":"w1-organelles"},{"id":"L00202","dok":3,"q":"Order these structures from where messenger RNA is made to where a finished protein leaves the cell: nuclear pore, rough ER, plasma membrane, nucleus.","a":"Nucleus, nuclear pore, rough ER, plasma membrane.","options":["Rough ER, nuclear pore, nucleus, plasma membrane","Nucleus, rough ER, nuclear pore, plasma membrane","Nucleus, nuclear pore, rough ER, plasma membrane","Nuclear pore, nucleus, rough ER, plasma membrane"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Messenger RNA is copied from DNA inside the nucleus. It leaves through a nuclear pore and reaches ribosomes on the rough ER, where the protein is built, and the finished protein is eventually released at the plasma membrane. The route runs from the center of the cell outward, which is a useful check for any sequence question like this.","notes":["This order starts at the rough ER and moves inward to the nucleus, which runs the route backward. The message travels outward from the nucleus, not inward toward it.","This order puts the rough ER before the nuclear pore, but the messenger RNA must pass through a pore before it can reach the ER. Students pick it when they forget that the nucleus is closed off except at its pores.","Correct. Nucleus, nuclear pore, rough ER, plasma membrane.","This order begins at the nuclear pore, but messenger RNA is made inside the nucleus first and only then exits through a pore."],"lt":"t-cell-anatomy","lid":"c41","comp":14,"competencyId":"w1-organelles"},{"id":"L00203","dok":2,"q":"Match each cell to its structural adaptation, in this order: a cell packed with mitochondria, a cell with no nucleus, and a cell that changes shape freely.","a":"Skeletal muscle cell, red blood cell, white blood cell.","options":["White blood cell, skeletal muscle cell, red blood cell","Skeletal muscle cell, white blood cell, red blood cell","Skeletal muscle cell, red blood cell, white blood cell","Red blood cell, white blood cell, skeletal muscle cell"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Each cell's structure matches its job. A skeletal muscle cell is packed with mitochondria because contraction needs a large supply of ATP. A red blood cell loses its nucleus to make room for hemoglobin, and a white blood cell changes shape freely so it can squeeze between cells to reach an infection.","notes":["This set gives the mitochondria-packed description to the white blood cell and the missing nucleus to the muscle cell. A skeletal muscle cell has many nuclei, the opposite of having none.","This set gets the muscle cell right but swaps the other two. The red blood cell is the one with no nucleus, while the white blood cell is the one that changes shape to migrate.","Correct. Muscle cell, red blood cell, white blood cell.","This set gives the mitochondria-packed description to the red blood cell, which actually has no mitochondria at all. It also gives the missing nucleus to the white blood cell, which keeps its nucleus."],"lt":"t-cell-anatomy","lid":"c42","comp":11,"competencyId":"w1-generalized-cell"},{"id":"L00205","dok":2,"q":"Sort these structures into membranous versus non-membranous: Golgi apparatus, ribosomes, lysosomes, centrioles.","a":"Membranous: Golgi apparatus and lysosomes. Non-membranous: ribosomes and centrioles.","options":["Membranous: Golgi apparatus and lysosomes. Non-membranous: ribosomes and centrioles","Membranous: Golgi apparatus and centrioles. Non-membranous: ribosomes and lysosomes","Membranous: ribosomes and centrioles. Non-membranous: Golgi apparatus and lysosomes","Membranous: ribosomes and lysosomes. Non-membranous: Golgi apparatus and centrioles"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Membranous organelles are wrapped in their own membrane. The Golgi apparatus is a stack of membrane sacs and a lysosome is a single membrane sac of enzymes, so both are membranous. Ribosomes are particles of RNA and protein and centrioles are cylinders of microtubules, so neither has a membrane.","notes":["Correct. The Golgi and lysosomes have membranes; ribosomes and centrioles do not.","This sort calls centrioles membranous, but centrioles are made of microtubules with no membrane. Lysosomes, which it calls non-membranous, depend on a membrane to hold their enzymes.","This sort reverses both groups. Students choose it when they mix up which structures are bounded by membrane and which are protein particles or fibers.","This sort calls ribosomes membranous, but a ribosome is just RNA and protein with no membrane around it. It also calls the Golgi apparatus non-membranous, when the Golgi is built entirely of membrane sacs."],"lt":"t-cell-anatomy","lid":"c44","comp":14,"competencyId":"w1-organelles"},{"id":"L00206","dok":2,"q":"Starting from the outside of a cell and moving inward, order these structures: glycocalyx, phospholipid bilayer, cytoplasm, nucleus.","a":"Glycocalyx, phospholipid bilayer, cytoplasm, nucleus.","options":["Glycocalyx, cytoplasm, phospholipid bilayer, nucleus","Glycocalyx, phospholipid bilayer, cytoplasm, nucleus","Cytoplasm, glycocalyx, phospholipid bilayer, nucleus","Phospholipid bilayer, glycocalyx, cytoplasm, nucleus"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The glycocalyx is the sugar-rich coat on the outer surface of the cell, so it comes first. Next is the phospholipid bilayer of the plasma membrane, the boundary itself, then the cytoplasm, and finally the nucleus near the center. This order follows the three basic regions of the cell from the outside in.","notes":["This order places the cytoplasm outside the phospholipid bilayer. The cytoplasm lies inside the plasma membrane, so the membrane must come before it.","Correct. Glycocalyx, bilayer, cytoplasm, nucleus.","This order starts with the cytoplasm, which is inside the cell. Anything moving inward must cross the glycocalyx and the bilayer before reaching the cytoplasm.","This order puts the bilayer outside the glycocalyx. The glycocalyx is attached to the outer face of the membrane, so it is the outermost layer."],"lt":"t-cell-anatomy","lid":"c45","comp":11,"competencyId":"w1-generalized-cell"},{"id":"L00207","dok":1,"q":"What are the three basic regions of a generalized cell?","a":"The plasma membrane, the cytoplasm, and the nucleus.","options":["The nucleus, the nucleolus, and the chromatin","The ER, the Golgi apparatus, and the vesicles","The cytosol, the ribosomes, and the organelles","The plasma membrane, the cytoplasm, and the nucleus"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Every generalized cell has three basic regions. The plasma membrane is the outer boundary, the cytoplasm fills the space between the membrane and the nucleus, and the nucleus is the control center that holds the DNA. Every other structure, from ribosomes to the Golgi apparatus, belongs to one of these three regions.","notes":["The nucleus, nucleolus, and chromatin all belong to one region, the nucleus. This group would answer a question about structures found inside the nucleus.","The ER, Golgi apparatus, and vesicles are members of the endomembrane system, which lies in the cytoplasm. They would answer a question about the organelles that make and ship proteins.","The cytosol, ribosomes, and organelles are all parts of the cytoplasm, which is only one of the three regions. Pick this list for a question about what the cytoplasm contains.","Correct. Plasma membrane, cytoplasm, and nucleus are the three regions."],"lt":"t-cell-anatomy","lid":"c1-x2","comp":11,"competencyId":"w1-generalized-cell"},{"id":"L00212","dok":1,"q":"Which organelle is usually the largest structure in the cell and serves as its command center?","a":"The nucleus.","options":["The lysosome","The mitochondrion","The Golgi apparatus","The nucleus"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The nucleus is usually the largest organelle in the cell and acts as its command center. It holds the DNA, controls which genes are turned on, and is where DNA is copied into RNA. By controlling which genes are used, the nucleus directs growth, division, and the cell's specialized work.","notes":["A lysosome is a small sac of digestive enzymes that breaks down worn-out parts and debris. Pick it for a question about digestion and recycling inside the cell.","The mitochondrion produces most of the cell's ATP through cellular respiration. It is the answer for an energy question, and it is much smaller than the nucleus.","The Golgi apparatus modifies, sorts, and packages proteins and lipids. It is the answer for a question about the cell's shipping center, not its control center.","Correct. The nucleus is the largest organelle and the control center."],"lt":"t-cell-anatomy","lid":"c6-x2","comp":13,"competencyId":"w1-nucleus"},{"id":"L00214","dok":1,"q":"Which organelle produces most of the cell's ATP?","a":"The mitochondria.","options":["The peroxisomes","The lysosomes","The mitochondria","The ribosomes"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Mitochondria produce most of the cell's ATP, the molecule cells use for energy, through cellular respiration. Their inner membrane folds into cristae, which multiply the surface area available for this work. Cells with high energy demands, such as muscle cells, contain large numbers of mitochondria.","notes":["Peroxisomes break down fatty acids and hydrogen peroxide and detoxify alcohol, especially in liver and kidney cells. Choose them for a detoxification question.","Lysosomes break down worn-out parts and debris with digestive enzymes. They are the answer for a digestion or recycling question.","Correct. Mitochondria are the cell's ATP producers.","Ribosomes build proteins by reading messenger RNA. They use energy but do not produce the cell's ATP."],"lt":"t-cell-anatomy","lid":"c9-x2","comp":14,"competencyId":"w1-organelles"},{"id":"L00215","dok":1,"q":"Which organelle modifies, sorts, and packages proteins and lipids, tagging them for delivery to the right place in or out of the cell?","a":"The Golgi apparatus.","options":["The lysosome","The centrosome","The Golgi apparatus","The rough endoplasmic reticulum"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The Golgi apparatus receives proteins and lipids from the ER in transport vesicles. It modifies them, sorts them, and tags them for delivery, then sends them off in vesicles to the plasma membrane, to lysosomes, or out of the cell. That receive, finish, and dispatch role is why it is often called the cell's shipping center.","notes":["A lysosome is a sac of digestive enzymes that breaks down worn parts and debris. It is one of the places the Golgi ships products to, not the organelle that does the packaging.","The centrosome is a non-membranous region near the nucleus that organizes microtubules. Pick it for a question about building the spindle during cell division.","Correct. The Golgi apparatus modifies, sorts, and packages products.","The rough ER makes membrane and secretory proteins on its ribosomes and then sends them to the Golgi. It is the answer for where these proteins are made, which is the step before sorting and packaging."],"lt":"t-cell-anatomy","lid":"c10-x2","comp":14,"competencyId":"w1-organelles"},{"id":"L00217","dok":1,"q":"Which structures are the site of protein synthesis, where messenger RNA is read to build a protein?","a":"Ribosomes.","options":["Lysosomes","Centrioles","Ribosomes","Peroxisomes"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Ribosomes are where proteins are built. A ribosome reads the code in messenger RNA and links amino acids together in the matching order to form a protein. Free ribosomes make proteins used inside the cell, while ribosomes bound to the rough ER make proteins for secretion or for insertion into membranes.","notes":["Lysosomes break proteins and other large molecules down rather than building them. They are the answer for a question about digestion inside the cell.","Centrioles are paired microtubule cylinders that organize the spindle during cell division. They are not involved in reading messenger RNA.","Correct. Ribosomes are the site of protein synthesis.","Peroxisomes are enzyme sacs that break down fatty acids and hydrogen peroxide and detoxify alcohol. Pick them for a detoxification question."],"lt":"t-cell-anatomy","lid":"c12-x2","comp":14,"competencyId":"w1-organelles"},{"id":"L00218","dok":1,"q":"What is the cytosol?","a":"The gel-like fluid that suspends the organelles.","options":["The gel-like fluid that suspends the organelles","The fuzzy external coat of the cell","The double membrane around the nucleus","The internal protein framework of the cell"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The cytosol is the fluid part of the cytoplasm, made of water with dissolved ions, enzymes, and small organic molecules. The organelles are suspended in it. Many of the cell's chemical reactions, glycolysis among them, take place in the cytosol, so it is an active part of the cell rather than filler.","notes":["Correct. The cytosol is the fluid that the organelles sit in.","The fuzzy external coat is the glycocalyx, made of glycoproteins and glycolipids. That description fits a question about cell recognition.","The double membrane around the nucleus is the nuclear envelope. That description fits a question about what separates the nucleus from the cytoplasm.","The internal protein framework is the cytoskeleton, made of microfilaments, intermediate filaments, and microtubules. That description fits a question about cell shape and support."],"lt":"t-cell-anatomy","lid":"c13-x2","comp":11,"competencyId":"w1-generalized-cell"},{"id":"L00222","dok":2,"q":"A cell needs to recognize and adhere to neighboring cells. Which membrane component carries this identity role?","a":"The glycocalyx.","options":["The glycocalyx","The phospholipid bilayer","Peripheral proteins","Cholesterol"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The glycocalyx is the fuzzy coat of glycoproteins and glycolipids on the outside of the plasma membrane. The particular pattern of its sugar chains works like an identity badge, so cells use it to recognize and stick to the right neighbors. The immune system also reads these markers to tell the body's own cells from foreign ones.","notes":["Correct. The glycocalyx acts as the cell's identity tag.","The phospholipid bilayer is the basic fabric of the membrane and acts as its barrier. Pick it for a question about what forms the membrane, not what labels the cell's identity.","Peripheral proteins rest loosely on one face of the membrane and often act as enzymes or attach the cytoskeleton. They would be the answer for a protein that is not embedded in the bilayer.","Cholesterol sits among the phospholipids and keeps the membrane stable and properly fluid. Choose it for a question about membrane stability across temperature changes."],"lt":"t-cell-anatomy","lid":"c17-x2","comp":12,"competencyId":"w1-plasma-membrane"},{"id":"L00223","dok":2,"q":"A protein destined for secretion is being made and enters the endomembrane system. Which organelle is the starting point of that path?","a":"The rough endoplasmic reticulum.","options":["A secretory vesicle","The rough endoplasmic reticulum","The lysosome","The Golgi apparatus"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Proteins meant for secretion or for membranes are built on ribosomes attached to the rough ER, and they enter the ER as they are made. That makes the rough ER the first stop of the endomembrane route. From there the protein moves to the Golgi apparatus in a transport vesicle and then out to the cell surface in a secretory vesicle.","notes":["A secretory vesicle carries the finished product from the Golgi to the plasma membrane. It is the answer for the final leg of the route.","Correct. The rough ER is where the secretory path begins.","A lysosome is one of the destinations the Golgi can ship enzymes to, near the end of the route. It is the answer for a question about digesting worn parts.","The Golgi apparatus is the middle stop, where proteins arriving from the ER are modified and packaged. It is the answer for a question about sorting and shipping, not where the path begins."],"lt":"t-cell-anatomy","lid":"c18-x2","comp":14,"competencyId":"w1-organelles"},{"id":"L00225","dok":2,"q":"A vesicle fuses with the plasma membrane and releases its product outside the cell. What is this exit process called?","a":"Exocytosis.","options":["Exocytosis","Autophagy","Endocytosis","Phagocytosis"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"In exocytosis, a secretory vesicle travels to the plasma membrane, fuses with it, and opens to the outside, releasing its contents. This is the last step of the secretory pathway, and it is how gland cells release products such as digestive enzymes. The vesicle's membrane becomes part of the plasma membrane as it fuses.","notes":["Correct. Release by a vesicle fusing with the membrane is exocytosis.","Autophagy is the cell recycling its own worn-out components inside lysosomes. It happens within the cell and does not release anything outside.","Endocytosis brings material into the cell by folding the membrane inward to form a new vesicle. It runs in the opposite direction to this question.","Phagocytosis is a type of endocytosis in which a cell engulfs bacteria or debris, as macrophages do. It brings material in, so it would be the answer for a question about a cell eating particles."],"lt":"t-cell-anatomy","lid":"c20-x2","comp":14,"competencyId":"w1-organelles"},{"id":"L00226","dok":2,"q":"Messenger RNA must move out of the nucleus into the cytoplasm. Through which structure does it pass?","a":"The nuclear pores.","options":["The nucleolus","The nuclear pores","Gap junctions","Transport vesicles"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Messenger RNA is made in the nucleus but used by ribosomes in the cytoplasm, so it has to cross the nuclear envelope. It leaves through nuclear pores, the protein-lined channels that pass through both layers of the envelope. The pores control this traffic while keeping the DNA itself inside the nucleus.","notes":["The nucleolus is a dense region inside the nucleus where ribosomal subunits are assembled. It stays inside the nucleus and is not a passage through the envelope.","Correct. Messenger RNA exits through the nuclear pores.","Gap junctions are channels that connect the cytoplasm of two neighboring cells. They are the answer for passing ions between cells, not for moving RNA out of the nucleus.","Transport vesicles carry newly made proteins from the rough ER to the Golgi apparatus. They work later in the pathway, after the protein has already been made."],"lt":"t-cell-anatomy","lid":"c21-x2","comp":13,"competencyId":"w1-nucleus"},{"id":"L00227","dok":2,"q":"A red blood cell loses its nucleus and organelles as it matures. How does this structural change match its job?","a":"It frees interior space for oxygen-carrying hemoglobin.","options":["It allows the cell to contract forcefully","It frees interior space for oxygen-carrying hemoglobin","It increases the cell's surface area for absorption","It lets the cell squeeze out between other cells"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"A red blood cell's job is to carry oxygen, and the protein that does this, hemoglobin, needs room. By losing its nucleus and organelles as it matures, the cell frees its interior to be filled with hemoglobin. This is a clear case of a cell's structure being shaped by its function.","notes":["Forceful contraction is the job of muscle cells, which keep their nuclei and pack in mitochondria for energy. Pick this for a question about a skeletal muscle cell.","Correct. Losing the nucleus makes room for hemoglobin.","Increasing surface area for absorption is the job of microvilli on cells such as those lining the small intestine. It would be the answer for an absorptive cell, not a red blood cell.","Squeezing out between other cells is the white blood cell's adaptation, achieved by changing shape freely. That description fits a cell that migrates into tissue, not one that drops its nucleus."],"lt":"t-cell-anatomy","lid":"c22-x2","comp":11,"competencyId":"w1-generalized-cell"},{"id":"L00228","dok":2,"q":"A cell type is described as packed with mitochondria. Which job does that adaptation best support?","a":"Contraction of a muscle cell, which demands a heavy supply of ATP.","options":["Patrolling tissue for infection","Contraction of a muscle cell","Storing fat inside the cell","Oxygen transport in the blood"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Mitochondria produce ATP, so a cell packed with them must have a large and steady energy demand. Muscle contraction is one of the most energy-hungry jobs in the body, which is why skeletal muscle cells contain great numbers of mitochondria. The number of mitochondria gives a quick clue to how hard a cell works.","notes":["Patrolling tissue for infection is the job of white blood cells, whose key adaptation is changing shape to migrate. Pick this for a question about a cell that squeezes between other cells.","Correct. Contraction needs a large ATP supply, so muscle cells are packed with mitochondria.","Storing fat is the job of adipocytes, which are mostly filled by a single large lipid droplet. That job fits a question about energy storage in adipose tissue, not energy use.","Oxygen transport is the red blood cell's job, and mature red blood cells have no mitochondria at all. Choose this for a question about an anucleated cell full of hemoglobin."],"lt":"t-cell-anatomy","lid":"c23-x2","comp":14,"competencyId":"w1-organelles"},{"id":"L00231","dok":2,"q":"A genetic defect leaves a cell's lysosomes unable to break down their contents, and material builds up inside the cell. Which type of disorder results?","a":"A lysosomal storage disease.","options":["A lysosomal storage disease","Primary ciliary dyskinesia","A ribosomal protein disorder","A mitochondrial disorder"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Lysosomes are the cell's digestive organelles. If an enzyme inside them is missing or does not work, the material that enzyme should break down piles up inside the cell until the cell fails. Disorders built this way are called lysosomal storage diseases, and Tay-Sachs disease, where lipids build up in nerve cells, is the classic example.","notes":["Correct. Failed lysosomal digestion causes a lysosomal storage disease.","Primary ciliary dyskinesia comes from cilia that do not beat properly, so the airways cannot clear mucus. It is the answer for a case of repeated respiratory infections.","A defect in ribosomes would affect the cell's ability to build proteins. It is the answer for a case about failed protein synthesis, not failed digestion of cell material.","A mitochondrial disorder comes from mitochondria that cannot make enough ATP, so high-energy tissues like muscle and brain are hit first. Pick it when the case describes energy failure rather than buildup of undigested material."],"lt":"t-cell-anatomy","lid":"c26-x2","comp":14,"competencyId":"w1-organelles"},{"id":"L00232","dok":2,"q":"A patient's airway cilia do not form correctly, so the airways cannot clear mucus and infections keep returning. Which condition does this describe?","a":"Primary ciliary dyskinesia.","options":["Primary ciliary dyskinesia","A glycocalyx adhesion defect","A lysosomal storage disease","A mitochondrial disorder"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Cilia in the airways beat in waves to move a sheet of mucus, with its trapped dust and microbes, up toward the throat. In primary ciliary dyskinesia the cilia are built incorrectly and cannot beat properly, so mucus sits in the airways. The result is repeated respiratory infections, which points straight back to the cilia.","notes":["Correct. Defective cilia that cannot clear mucus describe primary ciliary dyskinesia.","A defect in the glycocalyx would affect how cells recognize and stick to each other. It is not linked to mucus clearance, which depends on the beating of cilia.","A lysosomal storage disease comes from a missing lysosomal enzyme that lets undigested material build up inside cells. It is the answer for a case like Tay-Sachs disease.","A mitochondrial disorder comes from poor ATP production and hits high-energy tissues like muscle and brain first. It would fit a case of muscle weakness, not trapped mucus."],"lt":"t-cell-anatomy","lid":"c27-x2","comp":12,"competencyId":"w1-plasma-membrane"},{"id":"L00235","dok":2,"q":"Which organelle outside the nucleus contains its own DNA and ribosomes, so it can make some of its own proteins?","a":"The mitochondrion.","options":["The peroxisome","The lysosome","The Golgi apparatus","The mitochondrion"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Mitochondria contain their own small loop of DNA and their own ribosomes, so they can make some of the proteins they need. Most of the cell's DNA is in the nucleus, but this separate mitochondrial DNA is passed on in its own pattern. Because of that, some mitochondrial disorders are inherited differently from disorders of nuclear genes.","notes":["A peroxisome is an enzyme sac that breaks down fatty acids and hydrogen peroxide. It does not hold its own DNA, so pick it for a detoxification question instead.","A lysosome is a sac of digestive enzymes with no DNA of its own; its enzymes are made by ribosomes on the rough ER. It is the answer for a question about breaking down worn parts.","The Golgi apparatus modifies and packages proteins made elsewhere and has no DNA. Choose it for a question about sorting and shipping cell products.","Correct. Mitochondria have their own DNA and ribosomes."],"lt":"t-cell-anatomy","lid":"c30-x2","comp":14,"competencyId":"w1-organelles"},{"id":"L00236","dok":3,"q":"A pancreatic cell makes and exports large amounts of digestive enzyme protein. Which organelle would you expect to be especially abundant in it?","a":"Rough ER.","options":["Rough ER","Smooth ER","Peroxisomes","Lysosomes"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Proteins made for export are built by ribosomes on the rough ER, so the amount of rough ER sets how much protein a cell can produce. A pancreatic cell that ships out large amounts of digestive enzyme needs an extensive rough ER to keep up. The Golgi apparatus and secretory vesicles are also busy in such a cell, since they finish and release the product.","notes":["Correct. Heavy protein export calls for abundant rough ER.","Smooth ER makes lipids, detoxifies drugs, and stores calcium, and it has no ribosomes. It would be abundant in a cell that makes lipids or detoxifies drugs, such as a liver cell.","Peroxisomes break down fatty acids and hydrogen peroxide and detoxify alcohol. They are plentiful in liver and kidney cells, not in a cell specialized for exporting protein.","Lysosomes digest worn-out parts and engulfed material. They would be abundant in a cell such as a macrophage that breaks down a lot of debris."],"lt":"t-cell-anatomy","lid":"d3-t-cell-anatomy-1","comp":14,"competencyId":"w1-organelles"},{"id":"L00237","dok":3,"q":"A patient has a disorder in which mitochondria cannot make enough ATP. Which tissues are most likely to show problems first?","a":"Muscle and brain.","options":["Adipose and areolar tissue","Muscle and brain","Epidermis and hair","Cartilage and tendon"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Mitochondria supply most of the cell's ATP, so when they fail, the tissues that use the most energy are affected first. Skeletal and cardiac muscle, the brain, and the kidney have very high energy demands and are packed with mitochondria. That is why mitochondrial disorders often show up as muscle weakness and nervous system problems.","notes":["Adipose and areolar connective tissue have scattered cells and modest energy needs compared with muscle and brain. They would be the answer for questions about energy storage and padding.","Correct. High-energy tissues such as muscle and brain fail first.","The surface layers of the epidermis and the hair shaft are made of dead, keratin-filled cells that use no ATP. They would not be the first tissues to show an energy shortage.","Cartilage and tendon have low metabolic activity and a poor blood supply, so they use relatively little energy. They are the answer for questions about tissues that heal slowly, not about high energy demand."],"lt":"t-cell-anatomy","lid":"d3-t-cell-anatomy-2","comp":14,"competencyId":"w1-organelles"},{"id":"L00238","dok":2,"q":"The phospholipids of the plasma membrane face their water-fearing tails inward. Why does this arrangement make the membrane semipermeable?","a":"Its oily core lets small lipid-soluble molecules through but blocks charged ions that lack a transport protein.","options":["Its water-loving core lets ions through freely but blocks oxygen","Its oily core passes lipid-soluble molecules but blocks most ions","Its cholesterol forms open pores that let every molecule cross","Its glycocalyx filters molecules by size before they reach the bilayer"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The middle of the bilayer is made of the hydrophobic (water-fearing) tails, which form an oily layer. Small lipid-soluble molecules and gases such as O2 and CO2 slip through this layer, but charged ions and large water-soluble molecules cannot cross without help from transport proteins. Letting some substances through while holding back others is exactly what semipermeable means.","notes":["This reverses the real arrangement, since the core is water-fearing, not water-loving. Oxygen actually crosses easily, while ions are the ones held back.","Correct. The oily core lets lipid-soluble molecules pass and holds back ions.","Cholesterol does not form pores; it sits among the phospholipids to stabilize the membrane and moderate its fluidity. Pores and channels are formed by integral proteins.","The glycocalyx is a sugar coat used for cell recognition, not a size filter. Students pick this when they confuse the outer coat with the barrier formed by the bilayer itself."],"lt":"t-cell-anatomy","lid":"d3-t-cell-anatomy-3","comp":12,"competencyId":"w1-plasma-membrane"},{"id":"L00239","dok":2,"q":"The nucleus is wrapped in a double membrane studded with pores rather than being sealed shut. Why are these pores necessary?","a":"They let messenger RNA leave and needed proteins enter while the DNA stays inside.","options":["They let the DNA move out so ribosomes can read it","They let ribosomes from the cytoplasm move in to make RNA","They let RNA leave and proteins enter while DNA stays inside","They connect the nucleus directly to neighboring cells' nuclei"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The DNA must stay protected inside the nucleus, but its instructions have to reach the ribosomes in the cytoplasm. Messenger RNA and ribosomal subunits leave through the nuclear pores, and proteins the nucleus needs come in the same way. The pores control this two-way traffic, so a sealed envelope would cut the nucleus off from the rest of the cell.","notes":["The DNA stays inside the nucleus at all times; only RNA copies of its instructions leave. Students choose this when they forget that messenger RNA is the go-between.","Ribosomal subunits are assembled in the nucleolus and travel outward through the pores, not inward. Students pick this when they reverse the direction of ribosome traffic.","Correct. The pores allow two-way traffic while keeping the DNA inside.","Nuclear pores open only into the cell's own cytoplasm. Direct connections between neighboring cells are made by gap junctions in the plasma membrane, which is the answer for cell-to-cell communication."],"lt":"t-cell-anatomy","lid":"d3-t-cell-anatomy-4","comp":13,"competencyId":"w1-nucleus"},{"id":"L00240","dok":2,"q":"Lysosomes are membrane-bound sacs full of digestive enzymes. Why is it important that these enzymes are kept inside a membrane rather than loose in the cytoplasm?","a":"The membrane keeps the enzymes from digesting the cell's own healthy parts.","options":["It keeps the enzymes from digesting the cell's healthy parts","It turns the enzymes into proteins that can be secreted","It lets the enzymes pass through nuclear pores to reach DNA","It allows the lysosome to produce ATP for digestion"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Lysosomal enzymes are powerful enough to break down proteins, lipids, and whole worn-out organelles. Sealed inside a membrane, they work only on material delivered to the lysosome, such as engulfed bacteria or old cell parts. If a lysosome ruptures, its enzymes can damage the cell's own structures, which shows why the membrane matters.","notes":["Correct. The membrane confines the enzymes to targeted material.","The membrane does not change what the enzymes are. Proteins meant for secretion leave in secretory vesicles from the Golgi apparatus, a different route from the lysosome.","Lysosomal enzymes do not travel into the nucleus, and the DNA is not their target. Nuclear pores carry RNA and proteins between the nucleus and the cytoplasm.","ATP is produced by mitochondria, not lysosomes. A lysosome's membrane holds enzymes in; it does not generate energy."],"lt":"t-cell-anatomy","lid":"d3-t-cell-anatomy-5","comp":14,"competencyId":"w1-organelles"},{"id":"L00241","dok":2,"q":"Microvilli are tiny folds that greatly increase the surface of cells lining the small intestine. Why does that extra surface matter there?","a":"More surface area gives more room to absorb nutrients from digested food.","options":["More surface area gives more room to absorb nutrients","Microvilli secrete mucus that protects the intestinal wall","Microvilli form a waterproof layer that limits water loss","Microvilli beat in waves to push food along the intestine"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Nutrients are absorbed across the plasma membrane of the cells lining the small intestine, so the amount of membrane limits how much can be taken in. Folding that membrane into thousands of tiny microvilli greatly increases the surface area without making the cells larger. Under the microscope these folds appear as the brush border on the apical surface.","notes":["Correct. Extra surface area means more absorption.","Mucus in the intestine is secreted by goblet cells, single-celled glands scattered among the absorptive cells. They would be the answer for a question about protecting the lining with mucus.","A waterproof barrier would block absorption, the opposite of what the intestinal lining needs. Waterproofing is a job of the keratinized epidermis of the skin.","Microvilli do not beat; that is what cilia do. Food is pushed along the intestine by smooth muscle in the wall, not by projections on the lining cells."],"lt":"t-cell-anatomy","lid":"d3-t-cell-anatomy-6","comp":12,"competencyId":"w1-plasma-membrane"},{"id":"L00242","dok":3,"q":"A cell's Golgi apparatus stops working, but its rough ER still makes protein normally. Which problem would most directly follow?","a":"Proteins would not be modified, sorted, and packaged for delivery.","options":["Ribosomes would stop building new proteins","Proteins would not be sorted and packaged for delivery","Messenger RNA would be trapped in the nucleus","The cell could not make enough ATP"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The Golgi apparatus is the stop where proteins from the ER are modified, sorted, tagged, and packed into vesicles for their destinations. If it fails, proteins can still be made on the rough ER, but they cannot be finished and sent to the plasma membrane, to lysosomes, or out of the cell. The problem shows up at the shipping step, not the manufacturing step.","notes":["Ribosomes on the rough ER work before the Golgi and do not depend on it to build proteins. That outcome would follow from a ribosome problem.","Correct. Without the Golgi, proteins are not finished and shipped.","Messenger RNA leaves the nucleus through nuclear pores, a step that happens long before the Golgi. That outcome fits a problem with the nuclear pores.","ATP is produced by mitochondria, which work independently of the Golgi. That outcome fits a mitochondrial disorder."],"lt":"t-cell-anatomy","lid":"d3-t-cell-anatomy-7","comp":14,"competencyId":"w1-organelles"},{"id":"L00243","dok":2,"q":"A red blood cell loses its nucleus as it matures, while a neuron keeps its nucleus for life. Why does this difference make sense given each cell's job?","a":"The red blood cell needs room for hemoglobin and lives only months, while the neuron must maintain itself for a lifetime.","options":["Neurons divide constantly and need a nucleus; red blood cells never divide","Red blood cells trade the nucleus for extra mitochondria to make energy","Red blood cells need room for hemoglobin; neurons must maintain themselves for life","Neurons store hemoglobin in the nucleus; red blood cells store it in cytoplasm"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"A red blood cell's job is to carry oxygen, so it gives up its nucleus and organelles to fill its interior with hemoglobin. It does not divide or repair itself and is replaced after a few months. A neuron usually lasts a lifetime without being replaced, so it must keep its nucleus to direct the steady production of proteins that maintain it.","notes":["Most neurons do not divide at all after development, so the nucleus is not kept for division. Students choose this when they assume every cell with a nucleus must be dividing.","Mature red blood cells have no mitochondria; they lose them along with the nucleus. Students pick this when they assume an active cell must swap its nucleus for energy organelles.","Correct. Each cell's nucleus decision matches its job and lifespan.","Neurons do not store hemoglobin, and in red blood cells hemoglobin fills the cytoplasm because there is no nucleus. This option mixes up which cell carries oxygen."],"lt":"t-cell-anatomy","lid":"d3-t-cell-anatomy-8","comp":11,"competencyId":"w1-generalized-cell"},{"id":"N-m1-cell-1","dok":1,"q":"Oxygen and carbon dioxide cross the plasma membrane freely, while proteins and charged ions need a transport mechanism. Which term describes a membrane like this?","a":"Semipermeable.","options":["Hydrophobic","Semipermeable","Permeable","Impermeable"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"A semipermeable membrane lets some substances cross and holds back others, based on their size, charge, and solubility. Small lipid-soluble molecules and gases such as O2 and CO2 diffuse through freely, while larger or charged substances rely on transport proteins. The plasma membrane is the main example, and this selectivity lets the cell control what enters and leaves.","notes":["Hydrophobic means water-fearing and describes the phospholipid tails in the core of the bilayer. It explains part of why the membrane is selective, but it is not the term for the membrane's overall permeability.","Correct. Letting some substances through and not others is semipermeable.","A permeable barrier lets substances pass freely without restriction. It describes something like water moving through open water channels (aquaporins), not a membrane that sorts what crosses.","An impermeable barrier blocks movement unless a specific mechanism carries the substance across. It describes how the membrane treats proteins or ions with no transporter, but not the membrane as a whole, which lets gases through."],"comp":12,"competencyId":"w1-plasma-membrane"},{"id":"N-m1-cell-2","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares the cytoplasm and the cytosol?","a":"The cytoplasm is the organelles plus the cytosol; the cytosol is only the fluid part.","options":["The cytoplasm is fluid inside the nucleus; the cytosol is fluid outside it","The cytoplasm is organelles plus fluid; the cytosol is only the fluid","The cytosol is organelles plus fluid; the cytoplasm is only the fluid","The cytoplasm includes the nucleus; the cytosol excludes the nucleus"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The cytoplasm is everything inside the plasma membrane except the nucleus, which means the organelles together with the fluid they float in. The cytosol is just that fluid: water with dissolved ions, enzymes, and small molecules. So the cytosol is one part of the cytoplasm, not another name for it.","notes":["The fluid inside the nucleus is the nucleoplasm, a separate term. This option would be correct for a question comparing the nucleoplasm with the cytosol.","Correct. The cytosol is the fluid part of the cytoplasm.","This reverses the two terms. Students mix them up because both start with cyto-, but the cytosol is the fluid alone, and the cytoplasm is the larger region that contains it.","Neither term includes the nucleus. The cytoplasm stops at the nuclear envelope, and the material inside the nucleus has its own name."],"comp":11,"competencyId":"w1-generalized-cell"},{"id":"N-m1-cell-3","dok":1,"q":"Which model describes the plasma membrane as a fluid sea of phospholipids with proteins floating in it and drifting sideways?","a":"The fluid mosaic model.","options":["The endomembrane system","The generalized cell model","The sliding filament model","The fluid mosaic model"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The fluid mosaic model describes the membrane as a flexible sheet of phospholipids in which proteins and other molecules float like tiles in a mosaic. The phospholipids and proteins can drift sideways within the layer, so the membrane is not a rigid wall. This movement gives the membrane flexibility and lets its parts interact.","notes":["The endomembrane system is the network of membranous organelles, such as the ER, Golgi apparatus, and vesicles, that make and ship proteins and lipids. It is a group of organelles, not a model of the plasma membrane.","The generalized cell model is a composite drawing of a whole cell that shows the features shared by most body cells. It describes the entire cell, not the structure of the membrane.","The sliding filament model explains how thick and thin filaments slide past each other when a muscle fiber contracts. It belongs to muscle tissue, which comes up in Module 3.","Correct. The fluid mosaic model describes the membrane this way."],"comp":12,"competencyId":"w1-plasma-membrane"},{"id":"N-m1-cell-4","dok":1,"q":"The flattened membrane sacs that make up the Golgi apparatus are called what?","a":"Cisternae.","options":["Cisternae","Microvilli","Lacunae","Cristae"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The Golgi apparatus is built from a stack of flattened membrane sacs called cisternae. Products arriving from the ER are modified within the cisternae and then leave in vesicles that bud from the edges of the stack. The stacked cisternae are the feature to look for when identifying the Golgi.","notes":["Correct. The Golgi's flattened sacs are cisternae.","Microvilli are finger-like folds of the plasma membrane on the outer surface of absorptive cells. They are the answer for a question about increasing surface area for absorption.","Lacunae are small spaces in the matrix of cartilage and bone where chondrocytes and osteocytes sit. They belong to connective tissue, not to an organelle.","Cristae are the folds of the inner membrane of a mitochondrion, which increase surface area for making ATP. The names look alike, so pick cristae only for a question about mitochondria."],"comp":14,"competencyId":"w1-organelles"},{"id":"N-m1-cell-5","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares free ribosomes and ribosomes bound to the rough ER?","a":"Free ribosomes make proteins used inside the cell; bound ribosomes make proteins for secretion or membranes.","options":["Free ones make proteins for export or membranes; bound ones make proteins for inside the cell","Free ones work inside the nucleus; bound ones work in the cytoplasm","Free ones make lipids; bound ones make proteins","Free ones make proteins for inside the cell; bound ones make proteins for export or membranes"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"All ribosomes build proteins, but where they sit decides where the protein goes. Free ribosomes in the cytosol make proteins that stay in the cell and work in the cytosol. Ribosomes bound to the rough ER make proteins that will be secreted or placed into membranes, because those proteins must travel through the ER and Golgi apparatus first.","notes":["This swaps the two jobs. Proteins headed out of the cell must enter the endomembrane route, which starts at the rough ER, so they are made by bound ribosomes.","Ribosomal subunits are assembled in the nucleolus, but finished ribosomes work in the cytoplasm. Free ribosomes float in the cytosol, not inside the nucleus.","Ribosomes make only proteins. Lipids are made by the smooth ER, which has no ribosomes at all.","Correct. Location decides the protein's destination."],"comp":14,"competencyId":"w1-organelles"},{"id":"N-m1-cell-6","dok":1,"q":"What is the viscous fluid inside the nucleus that supports the chromatin and nucleolus called?","a":"Nucleoplasm.","options":["Nucleolus","Cytosol","Nucleoplasm","Chromatin"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The nucleoplasm is the thick fluid enclosed by the nuclear envelope. The chromatin and the nucleolus sit within it, much as organelles sit in the cytosol outside the nucleus. Keeping the two fluids separate is one of the jobs of the nuclear envelope.","notes":["The nucleolus is a dense region suspended in the nucleoplasm where ribosomal subunits are assembled. It is a structure within the fluid, not the fluid itself.","The cytosol is the fluid of the cytoplasm, outside the nucleus, where the organelles are suspended. Pick it for a question about the fluid between the plasma membrane and the nuclear envelope.","Correct. The fluid of the nucleus is the nucleoplasm.","Chromatin is the loose network of DNA and histone proteins in the nucleus. It is supported by the nucleoplasm and is the answer for a question about the form DNA takes between divisions."],"comp":13,"competencyId":"w1-nucleus"},{"id":"N-m1-cell-7","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares cilia and flagella?","a":"Cilia move substances across the cell surface; a flagellum moves the whole cell.","options":["Cilia absorb nutrients; flagella secrete mucus","Cilia have a 9 plus 0 core; flagella have a 9 plus 2 core","Cilia move the whole cell; a flagellum moves substances across the surface","Cilia move substances across the surface; a flagellum moves the whole cell"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Cilia and flagella share the same internal design, a core of microtubules in a 9 plus 2 arrangement that lets them move. Cilia are short and numerous and sweep substances such as mucus across the surface of a cell that stays in place, as in the respiratory tract. A flagellum is long and single and propels the cell itself, and in humans the only example is the sperm tail.","notes":["Absorbing nutrients is the job of microvilli, and mucus is secreted by goblet cells. Neither description fits cilia or flagella, which are projections built for movement.","Both cilia and flagella have the 9 plus 2 arrangement. The 9 plus 0 arrangement belongs to centrioles, which organize rather than move.","This reverses their jobs. Cells with cilia, like those lining the trachea, stay in place while their cilia move mucus past them.","Correct. Cilia move material past the cell, and a flagellum moves the cell."],"comp":12,"competencyId":"w1-plasma-membrane"},{"id":"N-m1-cell-8","dok":1,"q":"Which cytoskeletal component is made of tubulin and forms the core of centrioles, cilia, and flagella?","a":"Microtubules.","options":["Intermediate filaments","Microfilaments","Microtubules","Collagen fibers"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Microtubules are hollow tubes built from the protein tubulin, the thickest of the three cytoskeletal fibers. Besides acting as tracks for vesicles, they are the building blocks of centrioles (9 plus 0 arrangement) and of cilia and flagella (9 plus 2 arrangement). That makes microtubules important for both cell division and cell movement.","notes":["Intermediate filaments are rope-like fibers made of various tough proteins that resist tension. Pick them for a question about mechanical strength.","Microfilaments are thin fibers made of actin that support the cell's shape and allow movement at its surface. They are the answer for a question about actin.","Correct. Microtubules are made of tubulin and build centrioles, cilia, and flagella.","Collagen fibers are strong fibers in the matrix of connective tissue, outside the cells. They are not part of the cytoskeleton and are the answer for a question about the most abundant connective tissue fiber."],"comp":14,"competencyId":"w1-organelles"},{"id":"N-m1-cell-9","dok":2,"q":"Which of these organelles is part of the endomembrane system?","a":"The lysosome.","options":["The nucleolus","The mitochondrion","The centrosome","The lysosome"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The endomembrane system is the connected group of membranous organelles that make, modify, and move proteins and lipids. It includes the nuclear envelope, rough and smooth ER, the Golgi apparatus, lysosomes, and vesicles, all linked directly or by vesicles. Lysosomes belong because their enzymes are made on the rough ER and packaged by the Golgi apparatus.","notes":["The nucleolus is a dense region inside the nucleus with no membrane of its own. It is the answer for a question about assembling ribosomal subunits.","The mitochondrion has its own double membrane, DNA, and ribosomes and is not connected to the ER and Golgi by vesicles. It is the answer for a question about ATP production.","The centrosome has no membrane, so it cannot be part of a membrane system. It is the answer for a question about organizing microtubules.","Correct. Lysosomes are part of the endomembrane system."],"comp":14,"competencyId":"w1-organelles"},{"id":"N-m1-cell-10","dok":1,"q":"Which membrane component provides stability and moderates the membrane's fluidity as temperature changes?","a":"Cholesterol.","options":["Integral proteins","Glycoproteins","Cholesterol","Peripheral proteins"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Cholesterol molecules are wedged between the phospholipids of the bilayer. They keep the membrane from becoming too stiff when cold or too loose when warm, which stabilizes it. This is why cholesterol is listed as a membrane component even though the bilayer itself is the main fabric.","notes":["Integral proteins span the bilayer and form channels, carriers, and receptors. Choose them for a question about how substances or signals pass through the membrane.","Glycoproteins carry the sugar chains of the glycocalyx and are used for cell recognition and immune responses. Pick them for a question about how cells identify one another.","Correct. Cholesterol stabilizes the membrane and moderates its fluidity.","Peripheral proteins rest on one face of the membrane and act as enzymes or link the membrane to the cytoskeleton. They are the answer for a protein that is not embedded in the bilayer."],"comp":12,"competencyId":"w1-plasma-membrane"},{"id":"N-m1-cell-11","dok":1,"q":"Which of these is a standard example of a multinucleated cell?","a":"A skeletal muscle fiber.","options":["A skeletal muscle fiber","A smooth muscle cell","A neuron","A mature red blood cell"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"A multinucleated cell has more than one nucleus, and skeletal muscle fibers are the standard example, with many nuclei along each long fiber. Liver cells (hepatocytes) can also have more than one nucleus. Most body cells have a single nucleus, and mature red blood cells have none.","notes":["Correct. Skeletal muscle fibers have many nuclei.","Smooth muscle cells are spindle-shaped with a single central nucleus. They would be the answer for a question about non-striated muscle in the walls of hollow organs.","A neuron has a single nucleus in its cell body. Pick it for a question about cells that carry electrical signals.","A mature red blood cell is anucleated, meaning it has no nucleus at all. It is the answer for a question about a cell that gives up its nucleus to make room for hemoglobin."],"comp":11,"competencyId":"w1-generalized-cell"},{"id":"N-m1-cell-12","dok":2,"q":"Which structure has microtubules in a 9 plus 0 arrangement and organizes rather than moves?","a":"Centrioles.","options":["Cilia","Centrioles","Flagella","Microvilli"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Centrioles are short cylinders of microtubules in a 9 plus 0 arrangement, meaning nine sets around the edge and none in the center. This pattern goes with organizing, and centrioles help build the spindle during cell division. Cilia and flagella use a 9 plus 2 arrangement, with two extra microtubules in the center, and that pattern goes with movement.","notes":["Cilia have the 9 plus 2 arrangement and beat to move mucus across the cell surface. They would be the answer for a question about the moving projections of the airway lining.","Correct. Centrioles have the 9 plus 0 arrangement and organize the spindle.","Flagella also have the 9 plus 2 arrangement and propel the whole cell, as in the sperm tail. Pick them for a question about the single long projection that moves a cell.","Microvilli are supported by actin microfilaments, not microtubules, and they do not move. They are the answer for a question about increasing surface area for absorption."],"comp":14,"competencyId":"w1-organelles"},{"id":"N-m1-cell-13","dok":2,"q":"After the Golgi apparatus finishes a protein for export, which structure carries it to the plasma membrane?","a":"A secretory vesicle.","options":["A lysosome","A transport vesicle","A secretory vesicle","A nuclear pore"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The Golgi apparatus packs finished products into secretory vesicles, which bud from its edge and travel to the plasma membrane along microtubules. There the vesicle fuses with the membrane and releases its contents by exocytosis. This is the last leg of the secretory pathway, after a transport vesicle has already carried the protein from the ER to the Golgi.","notes":["A lysosome is a sac of digestive enzymes that the Golgi supplies, but it does not carry products to the cell surface. It is the answer for a question about digestion inside the cell.","A transport vesicle carries the protein from the rough ER to the Golgi apparatus, the leg before this one. Choose it for the step that leaves the ER.","Correct. Secretory vesicles carry finished products from the Golgi to the membrane.","A nuclear pore is a channel through the nuclear envelope for messenger RNA and proteins. It is at the very start of the route, not at the step after the Golgi."],"comp":14,"competencyId":"w1-organelles"},{"id":"N-m1-cell-14","dok":1,"q":"Which organelle breaks down hydrogen peroxide and helps detoxify alcohol, and is plentiful in liver and kidney cells?","a":"The peroxisome.","options":["The ribosome","The lysosome","The peroxisome","The Golgi apparatus"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Peroxisomes are small membrane sacs of enzymes that break down fatty acids, amino acids, and hydrogen peroxide, a harmful by-product of metabolism. They also help detoxify alcohol, which is why liver and kidney cells contain many of them. They look similar to lysosomes, so their contents and job are what tell them apart.","notes":["A ribosome builds proteins and has no membrane or detoxifying enzymes. Choose it for a protein synthesis question.","A lysosome is also an enzyme-filled sac, but its enzymes digest worn-out organelles, debris, and engulfed bacteria. Pick it for a question about the cell's digestion and recycling.","Correct. Peroxisomes break down hydrogen peroxide and help detoxify alcohol.","The Golgi apparatus modifies, sorts, and packages proteins and lipids. It is the answer for a question about the cell's shipping center."],"comp":14,"competencyId":"w1-organelles"},{"id":"N-m1-cell-15","dok":1,"q":"A mature red blood cell has no nucleus. Which term describes a cell like this?","a":"Anucleated.","options":["Uninucleated","Anucleated","Binucleated","Multinucleated"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Anucleated means without a nucleus. Mature red blood cells are the standard example: they lose their nucleus as they develop, which makes room for hemoglobin to carry oxygen. Because they have no nucleus, they cannot divide or make new proteins, and they are replaced after a few months.","notes":["Uninucleated cells have a single nucleus, which describes most body cells, including cardiac and smooth muscle cells. It is the opposite of what a mature red blood cell shows.","Correct. A cell with no nucleus is anucleated.","Binucleated cells have exactly two nuclei, which some liver cells show. The term still describes a cell that has nuclei, so it cannot fit a cell with none.","Multinucleated cells have more than one nucleus, as in skeletal muscle fibers and some liver cells. Pick this term for a long muscle fiber with many nuclei."],"comp":11,"competencyId":"w1-generalized-cell"},{"id":"N-m1-cell-16","dok":3,"q":"A liver cell must detoxify drugs and make lipids. Which organelle would you expect to be especially well developed in it?","a":"Smooth ER.","options":["Lysosomes","Smooth ER","The Golgi apparatus","Rough ER"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Smooth ER is the organelle that makes lipids, detoxifies drugs, and stores calcium, and it has no ribosomes. A liver cell that clears drugs from the blood and makes lipids therefore carries a large amount of smooth ER. Matching the amount of each organelle to the cell's job is the same idea that explains why protein-exporting cells are full of rough ER.","notes":["Lysosomes digest worn-out parts and engulfed debris. They would be well developed in a phagocytic cell such as a macrophage, not in a cell built for detoxifying drugs.","Correct. Drug detoxification and lipid production call for abundant smooth ER.","The Golgi apparatus modifies and packages proteins and lipids made elsewhere. It is the answer for a question about sorting and shipping products.","Rough ER makes proteins for secretion and membranes on its ribosomes. It would be the answer for a cell that exports large amounts of protein, such as a pancreatic enzyme-secreting cell."],"comp":14,"competencyId":"w1-organelles"},{"id":"N-m1-cell-17","dok":2,"q":"Rough ER sends its newly made proteins onward in transport vesicles. Which organelle receives them next?","a":"The Golgi apparatus.","options":["A lysosome","The nucleus","The Golgi apparatus","A mitochondrion"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Transport vesicles carry newly made proteins from the rough ER to the Golgi apparatus, the next stop on the endomembrane route. The Golgi modifies the proteins, sorts them, and tags them for their final destinations. From there, secretory vesicles carry products to the plasma membrane, and other vesicles carry enzymes to lysosomes.","notes":["A lysosome can be a final destination for enzymes, but they reach it only after passing through the Golgi apparatus. It is the answer for a question about where digestive enzymes end up.","The nucleus is where messenger RNA is made, before the protein is built at the rough ER. Proteins leaving the ER do not travel backward to the nucleus.","Correct. The Golgi apparatus is the next stop after the rough ER.","Mitochondria make ATP and have their own ribosomes for some of their proteins. They are not a stop on the endomembrane route."],"comp":14,"competencyId":"w1-organelles"},{"id":"N-m1-cell-18","dok":1,"q":"What are the folds of the inner mitochondrial membrane called?","a":"Cristae.","options":["Cristae","Microvilli","Cisternae","Rugae"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The inner membrane of a mitochondrion folds back and forth into shelves called cristae. These folds multiply the surface area on which ATP is produced, so a mitochondrion can make more energy without getting larger. The same idea of folding to gain surface area shows up in microvilli on absorptive cells.","notes":["Correct. The inner mitochondrial folds are cristae.","Microvilli are finger-like folds of the plasma membrane on the outer surface of absorptive cells. They are the answer for a question about absorption, not ATP production.","Cisternae are the flattened membrane sacs stacked to form the Golgi apparatus. The two words sound alike, so match cisternae with the Golgi and cristae with the mitochondrion.","Rugae are large folds in the wall of organs such as the stomach that let the organ expand. They are a whole-organ feature, not part of an organelle."],"comp":14,"competencyId":"w1-organelles"},{"id":"N-m1-cell-19","dok":2,"q":"Lysosomes take part in both phagocytosis and autophagy. Which statement correctly compares the two?","a":"Phagocytosis engulfs outside pathogens or debris; autophagy recycles the cell's own parts.","options":["Phagocytosis builds proteins; autophagy builds lipids","Phagocytosis takes in outside debris; autophagy recycles the cell's own parts","Phagocytosis recycles the cell's own parts; autophagy takes in outside debris","Phagocytosis releases products; autophagy brings fluid into the cell"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"In phagocytosis, a cell engulfs bacteria or debris from outside, and lysosomes then digest the captured material. In autophagy, the cell wraps up its own worn-out organelles and sends them to lysosomes to be broken down and recycled. Both rely on lysosomal enzymes, but one handles material from outside and the other handles the cell's own parts.","notes":["Building proteins is the job of ribosomes, and building lipids is the job of smooth ER. Phagocytosis and autophagy are both about breaking things down.","Correct. Phagocytosis handles outside material and autophagy handles the cell's own parts.","This swaps the two processes. The prefix phago- refers to eating something taken in, while auto- means self, which points to the cell's own components.","Releasing products from the cell is exocytosis, the last step of the secretory pathway. Neither phagocytosis nor autophagy sends material out of the cell."],"comp":14,"competencyId":"w1-organelles"},{"id":"N-m1-cell-20","dok":1,"q":"Which cell junction uses cadherins linked to intermediate filaments to bind neighboring cells firmly, and is abundant in the outer epidermis and cardiac muscle?","a":"The desmosome.","options":["The gap junction","The hemidesmosome","The desmosome","The tight junction"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"A desmosome is a spot of strong adhesion between two neighboring cells. Cadherin proteins hold the two cells together, and on the inside they connect to intermediate filaments, which resist pulling. That is why desmosomes are abundant in tissues under constant tension, such as the epidermis and cardiac muscle.","notes":["A gap junction is a protein channel connecting neighboring cells so ions and small molecules can pass. It is the answer for a question about cell-to-cell communication.","A hemidesmosome anchors a cell to the basement membrane below it rather than to another cell, using integrins. Pick it for a question about attaching the epidermis to the basement membrane.","Correct. Desmosomes bind cell to cell under tension.","A tight junction seals neighboring cells together so substances cannot leak between them, using claudins and occludins. It is the answer for a question about preventing leakage, as in the stomach and bladder lining."],"comp":17,"competencyId":"w1-cell-junctions"},{"id":"N-m1-cell-21","dok":1,"q":"Which cell junction is built from connexin proteins that form channels between neighboring cells?","a":"The gap junction.","options":["The gap junction","The tight junction","The desmosome","The adherens junction"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Gap junctions are clusters of channels made from connexin proteins that link the cytoplasm of two neighboring cells. Small ions and molecules pass directly through them, which lets cells share nutrients and lets electrical impulses spread from cell to cell. They are found in places such as cardiac muscle, the GI tract, the uterus, and some neurons.","notes":["Correct. Gap junctions are connexin channels.","Tight junctions use claudins and occludins to seal neighboring cells so nothing leaks between them. They block passage rather than create channels.","Desmosomes use cadherins attached to intermediate filaments to bind cells firmly under tension. They anchor cells together and do not let molecules pass between them.","Adherens junctions use cadherins attached to microfilaments to form adhesion belts that hold cells together. They give mechanical stability, not a passage between cells."],"comp":17,"competencyId":"w1-cell-junctions"},{"id":"N-m1-cell-22","dok":3,"q":"A patient has a disorder in which the hemidesmosomes of the skin fail. Which separation is most likely?","a":"The epidermis lifts away from the basement membrane.","options":["Cardiac muscle cells stop passing impulses to each other","Fluid leaks between the cells of the bladder lining","The epidermis lifts away from the basement membrane","Neighboring epidermal cells pull apart from each other"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Hemidesmosomes rivet the basal cells of the epidermis to the basement membrane beneath them. When they fail, the whole epidermis can lift off the basement membrane, producing a deep blister. Loss of desmosomes causes a different blister, because there the cells separate from one another instead.","notes":["Impulses pass between cardiac muscle cells through gap junctions in the intercalated discs. Failure of gap junctions, not hemidesmosomes, would disrupt that communication.","Leakage between lining cells follows failure of tight junctions, which seal epithelia such as the bladder and stomach lining. It does not describe a hemidesmosome problem.","Correct. Without hemidesmosomes, the epidermis separates from the basement membrane.","Cells pulling apart from each other is what happens when desmosomes fail, since desmosomes bind cell to cell. Pick this outcome for a disorder of desmosomal adhesion."],"comp":17,"competencyId":"w1-cell-junctions"},{"id":"L00262","dok":2,"q":"Which junctions seal neighboring epithelial cells so substances cannot leak between them?","a":"Tight junctions.","options":["Desmosomes","Tight junctions","Basement membranes","Gap junctions"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Tight junctions are made of transmembrane proteins (claudins and occludins) that fuse the membranes of neighboring cells together, sealing the space between them. This stops substances from leaking between cells, so anything crossing the epithelium has to pass through the cells themselves. They are prominent in the linings of the stomach, intestines, and urinary bladder, and in the blood-brain barrier.","notes":["Desmosomes bind neighboring cells strongly so they do not pull apart under tension. They hold cells together but do not seal the gaps between them.","Correct. Tight junctions seal the space between cells.","Basement membranes anchor the whole epithelium to the connective tissue below. They are a sheet under the tissue, not a junction between neighboring cells.","Gap junctions are channels that let ions and small molecules pass between neighboring cells. They connect cells for communication rather than sealing the space between them."],"lt":"t-tissues","lid":"c19","comp":17,"competencyId":"w1-cell-junctions"},{"id":"L00272","dok":2,"q":"Which junctions are channels that let neighboring cells pass ions and small molecules directly to each other?","a":"Gap junctions.","options":["Gap junctions","Tight junctions","Desmosomes","Microvilli"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Gap junctions are clusters of protein channels that connect the cytoplasm of neighboring cells. Ions and small molecules pass directly through them, which lets cells share nutrients and lets electrical signals spread from cell to cell. In cardiac muscle they help the heart contract in a coordinated way, and they are found in many other tissues too.","notes":["Correct. Gap junctions are channels between cells.","Tight junctions seal neighboring cells so nothing leaks between them. They block passage rather than create a channel.","Desmosomes anchor neighboring cells strongly against mechanical stress. They hold cells together but do not let molecules pass between them.","Microvilli are finger-like folds of the plasma membrane that increase surface area for absorption. They are not junctions between cells."],"lt":"t-tissues","lid":"c29","comp":17,"competencyId":"w1-cell-junctions"},{"id":"L00331","dok":1,"q":"Which cell junction forms an adhesion belt of cadherins tied to actin microfilaments, just deep to the tight junctions?","a":"The adherens junction.","options":["The gap junction","The hemidesmosome","The adherens junction","The tight junction"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"An adherens junction forms a continuous belt around the cell, just below the tight junctions. Transmembrane cadherin proteins link neighboring cells, and inside each cell they attach to actin microfilaments. This belt gives mechanical stability and helps epithelial surfaces resist being pulled apart, as in the cells lining the intestine.","notes":["A gap junction is a channel of connexin proteins that lets ions and small molecules pass between cells. It is the answer for cell-to-cell communication.","A hemidesmosome anchors the base of a cell to the basement membrane using integrins. It is the answer for attaching cells to the sheet beneath them.","Correct. The adherens junction is the cadherin and actin adhesion belt.","A tight junction seals neighboring cells with claudins and occludins so nothing leaks between them. It sits just above the adherens junction and is the answer for preventing leakage."],"lt":"t-tissues","lid":"w1g-ti-1","comp":17,"competencyId":"w1-cell-junctions"},{"id":"L00332","dok":1,"q":"Which junction anchors the basal surface of an epithelial cell to the underlying basement membrane?","a":"The hemidesmosome.","options":["The hemidesmosome","The desmosome","The gap junction","The adherens junction"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"A hemidesmosome looks like half of a desmosome and anchors the basal surface of an epithelial cell to the basement membrane below it. Its main proteins are integrins. It keeps the epidermis from separating from the basement membrane under mechanical stress.","notes":["Correct. Hemidesmosomes anchor cells to the basement membrane.","A desmosome binds two neighboring cells to each other, side to side. It is the answer for cell-to-cell adhesion, not cell-to-basement membrane attachment.","A gap junction is a channel between neighboring cells for ions and small molecules. It does not anchor cells to anything.","An adherens junction forms an adhesion belt between neighboring cells near their apical ends. It holds cells to each other, not to the basement membrane."],"lt":"t-tissues","lid":"w1g-ti-2","comp":17,"competencyId":"w1-cell-junctions"},{"id":"L00333","dok":2,"q":"A desmosome and a hemidesmosome both anchor cells. What does each one bind?","a":"A desmosome binds two adjacent cells; a hemidesmosome binds a cell to the basement membrane.","options":["A desmosome binds two adjacent cells; a hemidesmosome binds a cell to the basement membrane","Both bind two adjacent cells","A desmosome binds a cell to the basement membrane; a hemidesmosome binds two cells","Both bind a cell to the basement membrane"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"A desmosome is a spot weld between two neighboring cells, while a hemidesmosome attaches the base of one cell to the basement membrane below it. The name helps: hemi means half, so the half-desmosome has only one cell taking part and binds to a sheet instead. That is why hemidesmosomes are found only on the basal surface, while desmosomes are found between cells throughout an epithelium.","notes":["Correct. Desmosomes join cells, and hemidesmosomes join a cell to the basement membrane.","Only the desmosome binds two neighboring cells. If both did, nothing would anchor the epithelium to the basement membrane, and the whole layer could lift off.","This reverses the two. Desmosomes join cell to cell, and the half-named hemidesmosome joins a cell to the basement membrane.","Only the hemidesmosome binds to the basement membrane. If both did, nothing would hold neighboring cells together under tension."],"lt":"t-tissues","lid":"w1g-ti-3","comp":17,"competencyId":"w1-cell-junctions"},{"id":"N-a17d-11-1","dok":3,"q":"A cell diagram shows a dark nucleus surrounded by a granular region packed with small organelles, all enclosed by a thin outer boundary. A student labels the granular region cytosol. What is the better label, and why?","a":"Cytoplasm, because the region includes the organelles as well as the cytosol.","options":["Nucleoplasm, because the region surrounds the nucleus and supports it.","Cytosol, because the organelles are not counted as part of any region.","Cytoplasm, because the region includes the organelles as well as the cytosol.","Plasma membrane, because the region lies just inside the outer boundary."],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The cytoplasm is everything between the plasma membrane and the nucleus, the cytosol plus the organelles. Cytosol is only the fluid part. A region drawn with organelles in it is the cytoplasm, so cytosol is too narrow a label.","notes":["Nucleoplasm is the fluid inside the nucleus, not around it. It supports the chromatin and the nucleolus.","Organelles are part of the cytoplasm. Cytosol would be right only for the fluid between the organelles.","Correct. Cytoplasm is the cytosol plus the organelles, which is what the region shows.","The plasma membrane is the thin outer boundary itself. The granular region inside it is the cytoplasm."],"comp":11,"competencyId":"w1-generalized-cell"},{"id":"N-a17d-11-2","dok":3,"q":"A mature red blood cell and a skeletal muscle fiber are compared. Which of the three cell regions is missing from one of them, and how many does each cell have?","a":"The nucleus: the red blood cell has none, and the muscle fiber has many.","options":["The nucleus: the red blood cell has none, and the muscle fiber has many.","The cytoplasm: the muscle fiber has cytoplasm, and the red blood cell has none.","The nucleus: the red blood cell has one, and the muscle fiber has none.","The nucleus: the red blood cell has many, and the muscle fiber has none."],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Every cell has a plasma membrane and cytoplasm, but the number of nuclei varies. A mature red blood cell is anucleated, because losing the nucleus makes room for hemoglobin. A skeletal muscle fiber is multinucleated, with many nuclei. So the nucleus is the region present in one cell and missing from the other.","notes":["Correct. The red blood cell is anucleated, and the skeletal muscle fiber is multinucleated.","Both cells have cytoplasm. The red blood cell's cytoplasm is packed with hemoglobin, so it is the nucleus, not the cytoplasm, that the red blood cell lacks.","Neither count is right. A mature red blood cell has no nucleus, and a skeletal muscle fiber has many, so it is the standard multinucleated example.","This is reversed. The red blood cell is the anucleated cell, and the skeletal muscle fiber is the multinucleated one."],"comp":11,"competencyId":"w1-generalized-cell"},{"id":"N-a17d-11-3","dok":3,"q":"A liver cell on a slide has two nuclei. A student decides it must be abnormal because the generalized cell has one nucleus. What is wrong with this reasoning?","a":"Liver cells can have more than one nucleus, and the generalized cell is only a model.","options":["Liver cells normally have no nucleus, so any nucleus at all is a sign of damage.","Liver cells can have more than one nucleus, and the generalized cell is only a model.","The generalized cell has two nuclei, so every normal body cell has two.","Only red blood cells can have extra nuclei, so this cell must be a red blood cell."],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The generalized cell is a composite model that shows features shared by most cells, and no real cell looks exactly like it. Hepatocytes in the liver are a standard example of a multinucleated cell, along with skeletal muscle fibers. Two nuclei in a liver cell is normal.","notes":["Hepatocytes do have nuclei, sometimes more than one. The cell with no nucleus is the mature red blood cell.","Correct. Hepatocytes are a standard multinucleated example, and the generalized cell is a composite.","The generalized cell shows one nucleus. The number of nuclei varies by cell type, so it is not a rule for every cell.","Mature red blood cells have no nucleus at all. The standard multinucleated examples are hepatocytes and skeletal muscle fibers."],"comp":11,"competencyId":"w1-generalized-cell"},{"id":"N-a17d-12-1","dok":3,"q":"An electron micrograph shows a protein on the inner face of the membrane. It is not embedded in the bilayer and is attached only to the end of a protein that spans the bilayer. Which two protein types are shown?","a":"A peripheral protein bound to an integral protein.","options":["An integral protein bound to a peripheral protein.","A peripheral protein bound to an integral protein.","Two integral proteins joined across the bilayer.","Two peripheral proteins joined on the inner face."],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Integral proteins are firmly embedded in the bilayer, and most span it completely. Peripheral proteins are not embedded. They attach loosely to one face of the membrane or to an integral protein. The loose protein is peripheral and the spanning one it holds is integral.","notes":["This reverses the two. The protein that spans the bilayer is the integral one, and the loosely attached one is peripheral.","Correct. The loose protein is peripheral, and the one spanning the bilayer is integral.","Only one protein spans the bilayer. The other is not embedded, which makes it peripheral, not integral.","The protein that spans the bilayer is embedded, so it is integral. Only the loosely attached one is peripheral."],"comp":12,"competencyId":"w1-plasma-membrane"},{"id":"N-a17d-12-2","dok":3,"q":"A slide of the small intestine shows a dense border of short finger-like extensions on the free surface. A slide of the trachea shows longer hair-like extensions. Which extensions are these, and which of them has a 9 plus 2 microtubule core?","a":"Microvilli on the intestine and cilia on the trachea; only the cilia have the 9 plus 2 core.","options":["Cilia on the intestine and microvilli on the trachea; only the microvilli have the 9 plus 2 core.","Microvilli on the intestine and cilia on the trachea; both have the 9 plus 2 core.","Microvilli on the intestine and flagella on the trachea; only the flagella have the 9 plus 2 core.","Microvilli on the intestine and cilia on the trachea; only the cilia have the 9 plus 2 core."],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Microvilli are finger-like extensions that increase surface area for absorption, which fits the intestinal lining. Cilia are short hair-like extensions that sweep mucus across the airway surface. Cilia and flagella are built on a 9 plus 2 microtubule core, and microvilli are not.","notes":["The surfaces are swapped. The intestine's finger-like border is microvilli, and the trachea's hair-like extensions are cilia, which are the ones with the 9 plus 2 core.","The labels are right, but microvilli do not have the 9 plus 2 microtubule core. That core belongs to cilia and flagella, the extensions that move.","The trachea's many hair-like extensions are cilia. A flagellum is a single long extension, and the only human example is the sperm tail.","Correct. Absorptive intestine carries microvilli, the airway carries cilia, and only cilia have the 9 plus 2 core."],"comp":12,"competencyId":"w1-plasma-membrane"},{"id":"N-a17d-12-3","dok":3,"q":"A membrane protein forms a channel that lets ions pass from outside the cell to the inside. Which protein type must it be, and why could the other type not form this channel?","a":"Integral, because only it spans the bilayer; a peripheral protein sits on one face.","options":["Peripheral, because only it touches the fluid; an integral protein is buried inside.","Integral, because it is loosely attached; a peripheral protein is fixed in place.","Integral, because only it spans the bilayer; a peripheral protein sits on one face.","Peripheral, because it spans the bilayer; an integral protein sits on one face."],"correctIndex":2,"why":"A channel has to reach from one side of the membrane to the other. Integral proteins are firmly embedded, most span the bilayer completely, and they form the channels, carriers, and receptors. Peripheral proteins attach to only one face, so they cannot open a path through the membrane.","notes":["Peripheral proteins sit on one face, so they cannot open a path all the way through. Integral proteins span the bilayer and are exposed on both sides.","Integral is right, but integral proteins are firmly embedded, not loosely attached. Loose attachment describes peripheral proteins.","Correct. A channel must cross the whole bilayer, and only integral proteins do.","This swaps the two types. Integral proteins span the bilayer, and peripheral proteins sit on one face."],"comp":12,"competencyId":"w1-plasma-membrane"},{"id":"N-a17d-13-1","dok":3,"q":"A micrograph of a cell that is not dividing shows no rod-shaped chromosomes in the nucleus. A student concludes the cell has lost its DNA. What is the error?","a":"The DNA is present as loose chromatin and coils into chromosomes only during division.","options":["The DNA has moved out through the nuclear pores and will return during division.","The DNA is present as loose chromatin and coils into chromosomes only during division.","The DNA is stored in the nucleolus, which hides it until the cell divides.","The DNA is dissolved in the nucleoplasm and is never visible in any form."],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Chromatin is the loose, threadlike form of DNA wound on proteins, present when the cell is not dividing. When the cell divides, chromatin coils tightly into rod-shaped chromosomes that can be seen. A nondividing cell with no visible chromosomes still has all its DNA, in the chromatin form.","notes":["The DNA stays inside the nucleus. Nuclear pores let messenger RNA and ribosomal subunits out and proteins in, not the cell's DNA.","Correct. Between divisions the DNA stays as chromatin, which is why no chromosomes are visible.","The nucleolus is where ribosomal subunits are assembled, not where the DNA is stored. The DNA is spread through the nucleus as chromatin.","The nucleoplasm is the fluid that supports the chromatin and nucleolus. The DNA is visible as chromatin and condenses into chromosomes during division."],"comp":13,"competencyId":"w1-nucleus"},{"id":"N-a17d-13-2","dok":3,"q":"A ribosomal subunit has to reach the cytoplasm, where it will help build proteins. Where in the nucleus is it assembled, and which structure does it leave through?","a":"It is assembled in the nucleolus and leaves through a nuclear pore.","options":["It is assembled in the chromatin and leaves through the nuclear envelope membrane.","It is assembled in the nucleoplasm and leaves through the rough ER.","It is assembled in the nucleolus and leaves through the plasma membrane.","It is assembled in the nucleolus and leaves through a nuclear pore."],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The nucleolus is the dense region inside the nucleus where ribosomal subunits are assembled. The nuclear envelope is a double membrane, so material crosses it through the nuclear pores, the protein-lined channels that let ribosomal subunits and messenger RNA move out to the cytoplasm.","notes":["Chromatin is the DNA itself, not the site where subunits are built. Material crosses the envelope through its pores, not directly through the membrane.","The nucleoplasm is the supporting fluid, and the nucleolus inside it is the assembly site. The rough ER lies outside the nucleus, so it is not an exit from it.","The nucleolus is right, but the plasma membrane is the outer boundary of the whole cell. Passing through it would put the subunit outside the cell, not in the cytoplasm.","Correct. Subunits are built in the nucleolus and exit through the nuclear pores."],"comp":13,"competencyId":"w1-nucleus"},{"id":"N-a17d-13-3","dok":3,"q":"An electron micrograph shows ribosome-studded membranes running directly off the outer surface of the nuclear envelope. Which organelle is this, and what does it show about the envelope?","a":"The rough ER, showing the envelope's outer membrane is continuous with it.","options":["The Golgi apparatus, showing the envelope is a single membrane joined to it.","The rough ER, showing the envelope's outer membrane is continuous with it.","The smooth ER, showing the envelope's inner membrane is continuous with it.","The rough ER, showing the envelope is a single membrane with no pores."],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The rough ER is a membrane network studded with ribosomes. The nuclear envelope is a double membrane, and its outer membrane is continuous with the rough ER. Ribosome-covered membranes running straight off the envelope show that connection.","notes":["The Golgi is a stack of flattened sacs with no ribosomes, and the envelope is a double membrane, not a single one.","Correct. Ribosomes mark the rough ER, and it joins the outer membrane of the double nuclear envelope.","Smooth ER has no ribosomes, so it would not look studded. It is the outer membrane of the envelope that joins the ER, not the inner one.","Rough ER is right, but the envelope is a double membrane with nuclear pores running through it."],"comp":13,"competencyId":"w1-nucleus"},{"id":"N-a17d-17-1","dok":3,"q":"A toxin breaks down the claudins and occludins between cells of the intestinal lining. Which junction fails, and what can now happen?","a":"Tight junctions fail, so substances can leak between the cells.","options":["Gap junctions fail, so the cells can no longer pass ions to each other.","Desmosomes fail, so the cells pull apart under tension.","Hemidesmosomes fail, so the lining lifts off the basement membrane.","Tight junctions fail, so substances can leak between the cells."],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Claudins and occludins are the proteins of tight junctions. Tight junctions fuse the membranes of neighboring cells and seal the gap between them, which keeps organ contents from leaking into the surrounding tissue. When they fail, material can pass between the cells.","notes":["Gap junctions are built from connexins, not claudins and occludins. They are the channels that let ions and small molecules pass between cells.","Desmosomes are built from cadherins linked to intermediate filaments. They resist pulling forces, and their loss causes blistering in the epidermis.","Hemidesmosomes use integrins to anchor cells to the basement membrane. The toxin targets the proteins of tight junctions instead.","Correct. Claudins and occludins build tight junctions, which seal the space between cells."],"comp":17,"competencyId":"w1-cell-junctions"},{"id":"N-a17d-17-2","dok":3,"q":"On a slide of cardiac muscle, an intercalated disc joins two cells. One junction there holds the cells together under pulling force, and another lets ions pass from cell to cell. Which pair is it?","a":"Desmosomes hold the cells together, and gap junctions pass the ions.","options":["Tight junctions hold the cells together, and desmosomes pass the ions.","Gap junctions hold the cells together, and desmosomes pass the ions.","Desmosomes hold the cells together, and gap junctions pass the ions.","Hemidesmosomes hold the cells together, and tight junctions pass the ions."],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Desmosomes rivet cells together against pulling forces, and cardiac muscle cells are one of their main locations. Gap junctions are protein channels that pass ions and small molecules between neighboring cells, and they are found in cardiac tissue. The intercalated disc uses both.","notes":["Tight junctions seal gaps between epithelial cells, and desmosomes do not form channels. The ion channels at the disc are gap junctions.","These are reversed. Gap junctions are the channels for ions, and desmosomes are the ones that hold the cells together.","Correct. Desmosomes resist tension and gap junctions let ions pass between the cardiac cells.","Hemidesmosomes anchor a cell to a basement membrane, not to another cell, and tight junctions seal rather than pass ions."],"comp":17,"competencyId":"w1-cell-junctions"}]},{"id":"m1-epithelial","title":"Epithelial tissue","summary":"classification, named epithelia, locations, glands, basement membrane","mw":"2026-08-19","tr":"2026-08-20","cards":[{"id":"L00246","dok":1,"q":"Which tissue type covers body surfaces, lines cavities and vessels, and forms glands?","a":"Epithelial tissue.","options":["Muscle tissue","Connective tissue","Epithelial tissue","Nervous tissue"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Epithelial tissue covers the body's outer surface, lines internal cavities, tubes, and vessels, and forms glands. Its cells are packed tightly with very little material between them, and one surface always faces open space. Its main jobs are acting as a selective barrier, secreting products, and protecting against wear.","notes":["Muscle tissue contracts to produce movement and generate force. It is the answer for a tissue that moves bones, pumps blood, or pushes material through hollow organs.","Connective tissue binds, supports, and protects other tissues and is found beneath epithelium, never on a body surface. It is the answer for the most widespread and diverse tissue.","Correct. Epithelium covers, lines, and forms glands.","Nervous tissue detects changes and generates nerve impulses. It is the answer for the tissue of the brain, spinal cord, and nerves."],"lt":"t-tissues","lid":"c3","comp":16,"competencyId":"w1-epithelial-id"},{"id":"L00257","dok":1,"q":"Which structure anchors an epithelium to the connective tissue below it?","a":"The basement membrane.","options":["A gap junction","A desmosome","The glycocalyx","The basement membrane"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The basement membrane is a thin sheet under the basal surface of every epithelium. It is made of a basal lamina (collagen, laminin, and glycoproteins) and a reticular lamina (collagen and glycoproteins), and it anchors and supports the epithelium on the connective tissue below. It also restricts large molecules and helps with wound healing.","notes":["A gap junction is a channel between neighboring cells that lets ions and small molecules pass. It is the answer for a question about cell-to-cell communication.","A desmosome binds two neighboring cells to each other with cadherins and intermediate filaments. It holds cells together side to side, not the epithelium to the tissue below.","The glycocalyx is the sugar coat on the outside of individual cells, used for recognition. It is on the cell surface, not a sheet that anchors the whole epithelium.","Correct. The basement membrane anchors epithelium to connective tissue."],"lt":"t-tissues","lid":"c14","comp":16,"competencyId":"w1-epithelial-id"},{"id":"L00260","dok":2,"q":"Epithelia are classified on two features. One is the shape of the surface cells. What is the other?","a":"The number of cell layers.","options":["The number of cell layers","The germ layer of origin","The fiber type in the matrix","The presence of a duct"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Epithelium is named using two features: the number of cell layers and the shape of the cells at the free surface. One layer is simple and more than one is stratified, and the surface cells are squamous (flat), cuboidal (cube-shaped), or columnar (tall). Putting the two together gives names such as simple squamous or stratified columnar.","notes":["Correct. Layers plus surface cell shape name an epithelium.","The germ layer of origin describes where a tissue comes from in the embryo. It is not part of an epithelium's name.","Fiber type is used to describe connective tissue, such as dense regular tissue with parallel collagen. Epithelium has very little matrix and no fiber-based naming.","Whether a duct is present separates exocrine glands, which have ducts, from endocrine glands, which do not. It classifies glands, not covering and lining epithelium."],"lt":"t-tissues","lid":"c17","comp":16,"competencyId":"w1-epithelial-id"},{"id":"L00261","dok":2,"q":"An epithelium classified as simple has how many cell layers?","a":"One.","options":["One","Many","Three","Two"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"A simple epithelium has a single layer of cells, with every cell resting on the basement membrane. A single layer keeps the path across the tissue short, so simple epithelia are built for diffusion, filtration, absorption, and secretion. Epithelia with more than one layer are called stratified and are built for protection.","notes":["Correct. Simple means one layer.","Many layers describes a stratified epithelium, such as the stratified squamous epithelium of the skin. Choose it for a question about an epithelium built to resist wear.","Three layers would also make an epithelium stratified. No category of epithelium is defined by exactly three layers, so students pick this only when guessing a number.","Two layers is common in stratified cuboidal epithelium, found in the ducts of sweat and salivary glands. Any epithelium with two or more layers is stratified, not simple."],"lt":"t-tissues","lid":"c18","comp":16,"competencyId":"w1-epithelial-id"},{"id":"L00263","dok":1,"q":"Squamous cells, used to name an epithelium, have which shape?","a":"Flat and thin.","options":["Flat and thin","Tall and column-like","Cube-shaped","Spindle-shaped"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Squamous cells are flat and thin, like scales or floor tiles, so their nuclei often look flattened too. Because they are so thin, a layer of squamous cells gives substances a very short path to cross, which suits diffusion and filtration. In a stratified epithelium, the name squamous refers to the flat cells at the free surface.","notes":["Correct. Squamous cells are flat and thin.","Tall, column-like cells are columnar, with nuclei usually near the base. They are the answer for the lining of the stomach and intestines.","Cube-shaped cells are cuboidal, about as tall as they are wide, with round central nuclei. They are the answer for kidney tubules and small glands.","Spindle-shaped cells, thick in the middle and tapered at the ends, describe smooth muscle cells. That shape belongs to muscle tissue, not to epithelium."],"lt":"t-tissues","lid":"c20","comp":16,"competencyId":"w1-epithelial-id"},{"id":"L00264","dok":2,"q":"A gland releases its product through a duct onto a surface. Which gland is an example of this type?","a":"A sweat gland.","options":["The thyroid gland","The pituitary gland","A sweat gland","The adrenal gland"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Glands that release their product into a duct that carries it onto a surface or into a lumen are exocrine glands. Sweat glands are a clear example: sweat travels up a duct and out onto the skin surface. Salivary and sebaceous glands are other exocrine examples, while endocrine glands have no ducts and release hormones into the blood.","notes":["The thyroid gland is an endocrine gland with no duct; it releases hormones directly into the bloodstream. Pick it for a question about a ductless gland.","The pituitary gland is an endocrine gland that releases hormones into the blood without a duct. It is the answer for a question about endocrine glands.","Correct. A sweat gland is an exocrine gland with a duct.","The adrenal gland is an endocrine gland that secretes hormones into the blood. It would be the answer for a question about ductless glands, not glands with ducts."],"lt":"t-tissues","lid":"c21","comp":16,"competencyId":"w1-epithelial-id"},{"id":"L00269","dok":2,"q":"Which epithelial classification term describes one cell layer that looks layered and often lines the airways?","a":"Pseudostratified.","options":["Pseudostratified","Stratified","Transitional","Simple squamous"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Pseudostratified epithelium looks layered because its nuclei sit at different heights, but every cell actually touches the basement membrane, so it is truly a single layer. Pseudo means false, so the name says it only seems stratified. It usually carries cilia and goblet cells and lines the trachea and bronchi.","notes":["Correct. Pseudostratified is one layer that looks layered.","A stratified epithelium truly has two or more layers, and only the deepest cells touch the basement membrane. Choose it for a tissue built for protection, such as the epidermis.","Transitional epithelium has several true layers whose surface cells change from dome-shaped to flat as the organ stretches. It is the answer for the urinary bladder and ureters.","Simple squamous epithelium is one layer of flat cells and does not look layered. It is the answer for the air sacs of the lungs and the lining of blood vessels."],"lt":"t-tissues","lid":"c26","comp":16,"competencyId":"w1-epithelial-id"},{"id":"L00273","dok":2,"q":"Which general structural feature is true of epithelial tissue?","a":"It is avascular but innervated, with a free apical surface.","options":["It is richly vascular with abundant matrix between cells","It has cells spaced widely apart in a fluid matrix","It has little capacity to regenerate after injury","It is avascular but innervated, with a free apical surface"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Epithelial tissue has no blood vessels of its own (it is avascular) and depends on diffusion from the connective tissue beneath it, but it does have nerve endings (it is innervated). Its cells are tightly packed with very little matrix, and each epithelium has one free apical surface facing open space. It also regenerates quickly because its cells divide often.","notes":["Being richly vascular with abundant matrix describes most connective tissue, not epithelium. Epithelium has no blood vessels and very little matrix.","Cells widely spaced in a fluid matrix describes blood, a connective tissue. Epithelial cells are packed tightly side by side.","Epithelium actually regenerates well, which is why skin wounds and the gut lining heal quickly. Slow healing is typical of cartilage, which is avascular.","Correct. Epithelium is avascular, innervated, and has a free surface."],"lt":"t-tissues","lid":"c30","comp":16,"competencyId":"w1-epithelial-id"},{"id":"L00274","dok":2,"q":"A slide shows a tissue with many cell layers and flat cells at its free surface. Which epithelial type is it?","a":"Stratified squamous epithelium.","options":["Simple squamous epithelium","Pseudostratified columnar epithelium","Stratified squamous epithelium","Simple columnar epithelium"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Name an epithelium by counting the layers first and then looking at the cells at the free surface. Many layers make it stratified, and flat surface cells make it squamous, so this is stratified squamous epithelium. It protects against abrasion and is found in the skin (keratinized) and in the mouth, esophagus, and vagina (non-keratinized).","notes":["Simple squamous epithelium has flat cells but only one layer. It is the answer for the air sacs of the lungs and the lining of blood vessels.","Pseudostratified epithelium only looks layered; all of its cells touch the basement membrane, and its surface cells are columnar. It is the answer for the lining of the trachea.","Correct. Many layers with flat surface cells is stratified squamous.","Simple columnar epithelium is one layer of tall cells. It is the answer for the lining of the stomach and intestines."],"lt":"t-tissues","lid":"c31","comp":16,"competencyId":"w1-epithelial-id"},{"id":"L00276","dok":2,"q":"A slide shows a single layer of cube-shaped cells lining a kidney tubule. Which epithelial type is it?","a":"Simple cuboidal epithelium.","options":["Simple squamous epithelium","Stratified cuboidal epithelium","Simple columnar epithelium","Simple cuboidal epithelium"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"One layer of cells makes the epithelium simple, and cells about as tall as they are wide, with round central nuclei, make it cuboidal. Simple cuboidal epithelium handles secretion and absorption and lines kidney tubules, small glands, and the surface of the ovary. In the kidney tubules it reabsorbs substances from the fluid passing through.","notes":["Simple squamous epithelium has flat, thin cells, not cube-shaped ones. It is the answer for the air sacs of the lungs and capillary walls.","Stratified cuboidal epithelium has two or more layers of cube-shaped cells and lines the ducts of sweat, salivary, and mammary glands. It would need more than one layer.","Simple columnar epithelium has tall cells with nuclei near the base. It is the answer for the lining of the stomach and intestines.","Correct. One layer of cube-shaped cells is simple cuboidal."],"lt":"t-tissues","lid":"c33","comp":16,"competencyId":"w1-epithelial-id"},{"id":"L00281","dok":2,"q":"A tissue lines the urinary tract and stretches and recoils as the bladder fills and empties. Which epithelial type is it?","a":"Transitional epithelium.","options":["Stratified columnar epithelium","Transitional epithelium","Simple squamous epithelium","Pseudostratified columnar epithelium"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Transitional epithelium lines the urinary bladder, the ureters, and part of the urethra. It has several layers, and its surface cells change from dome-shaped to flat as the organ fills and stretches, then return to dome-shaped as it empties. This lets the lining expand without tearing while still keeping urine in.","notes":["Stratified columnar epithelium has several layers with tall surface cells and is found in the male urethra and some large gland ducts. It does not stretch the way the bladder lining must.","Correct. The stretchy urinary lining is transitional epithelium.","Simple squamous epithelium is a single layer of flat cells built for diffusion and filtration. It is the answer for the air sacs of the lungs and blood vessel linings.","Pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium is one layer that looks layered and lines the trachea and bronchi. It is the answer for an airway lining that moves mucus."],"lt":"t-tissues","lid":"c38","comp":16,"competencyId":"w1-epithelial-id"},{"id":"L00287","dok":2,"q":"A slide shows a single layer of tall cells lining the intestine, suited to absorption. Which epithelial type is it?","a":"Simple columnar epithelium.","options":["Simple columnar epithelium","Stratified columnar epithelium","Simple cuboidal epithelium","Simple squamous epithelium"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"One layer of tall, column-shaped cells with nuclei near the base identifies simple columnar epithelium. Its tall cells have room for the machinery of absorption and secretion, and they often carry microvilli and goblet cells. It lines the digestive tract from the stomach to the rectum, and the gallbladder.","notes":["Correct. One layer of tall cells is simple columnar.","Stratified columnar epithelium has several layers and is found in places like the male urethra. It is too thick to suit absorption.","Simple cuboidal epithelium has cube-shaped cells with round central nuclei. It is the answer for kidney tubules and small glands.","Simple squamous epithelium is one layer of flat, thin cells. It is the answer for the air sacs of the lungs and the lining of blood vessels."],"lt":"t-tissues","lid":"c44","comp":16,"competencyId":"w1-epithelial-id"},{"id":"L00289","dok":2,"q":"A slide shows a single layer of very flat, thin cells forming the lining of a blood vessel. Which epithelial type is it?","a":"Simple squamous epithelium.","options":["Simple cuboidal epithelium","Transitional epithelium","Stratified squamous epithelium","Simple squamous epithelium"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"A single layer of very flat, thin cells is simple squamous epithelium. It gives substances the shortest possible path to cross, so it is used wherever diffusion or filtration must be fast. It lines blood vessels, the air sacs (alveoli) of the lungs, and the filtering capillaries of the kidney, and it forms the serous membranes.","notes":["Simple cuboidal epithelium has cube-shaped cells. It is the answer for kidney tubules and small glands.","Transitional epithelium has several layers that stretch and flatten as an organ fills. It is the answer for the urinary bladder and ureters.","Stratified squamous epithelium has many layers with flat surface cells and is built to resist abrasion. It is the answer for the skin and the lining of the mouth and esophagus.","Correct. One layer of flat cells is simple squamous."],"lt":"t-tissues","lid":"c46","comp":16,"competencyId":"w1-epithelial-id"},{"id":"L00292","dok":2,"q":"Sort these epithelia by their number of true cell layers: simple squamous, pseudostratified ciliated columnar, stratified squamous, transitional.","a":"One layer: simple squamous and pseudostratified. More than one: stratified squamous and transitional.","options":["One layer: simple squamous and pseudostratified. More than one: stratified squamous and transitional","One layer: simple squamous and transitional. More than one: pseudostratified and stratified squamous","One layer: pseudostratified and transitional. More than one: simple squamous and stratified squamous","One layer: simple squamous only. More than one: pseudostratified, stratified squamous, and transitional"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Simple squamous epithelium is one layer of flat cells. Pseudostratified epithelium also has only one true layer, because every cell touches the basement membrane even though the nuclei sit at different heights. Stratified squamous and transitional epithelia both have several true layers, with only the deepest cells touching the basement membrane.","notes":["Correct. Simple squamous and pseudostratified are one layer; the other two are layered.","Transitional epithelium has several true layers, which is part of what lets the bladder lining stretch. Pseudostratified is the one that only looks layered.","Simple squamous is one layer by definition, and transitional has several layers. This sort reverses those two.","This sort treats pseudostratified epithelium as layered because it looks layered. The prefix pseudo means false: all of its cells reach the basement membrane, so it is one layer."],"lt":"t-tissues","lid":"c49","comp":16,"competencyId":"w1-epithelial-id"},{"id":"L00307","dok":2,"q":"A tissue must protect a surface against constant wear. Which epithelial arrangement best suits this job?","a":"Stratified epithelium.","options":["Simple epithelium","Pseudostratified epithelium","Stratified epithelium","Transitional epithelium"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Stratified epithelium has several layers of cells, so the surface cells can be worn away and replaced from below without exposing the tissue underneath. That makes it the right design for surfaces that face constant friction, such as the skin, mouth, and esophagus. Simple epithelium, with only one layer, would be damaged quickly by the same wear.","notes":["Simple epithelium has a single layer built for diffusion, absorption, and secretion. It is the answer for thin exchange surfaces like the lung air sacs, not for resisting wear.","Pseudostratified epithelium is one true layer that looks layered and usually carries cilia and goblet cells. It is the answer for the airway lining that moves mucus.","Correct. Several replaceable layers suit a surface that faces wear.","Transitional epithelium is layered and stretches as the bladder fills. It is the answer for a lining that must change shape, not one that faces abrasion."],"lt":"t-tissues","lid":"c16-x2","comp":16,"competencyId":"w1-epithelial-id"},{"id":"L00309","dok":2,"q":"A gland releases its product into the blood rather than onto a surface. How is this gland classified?","a":"An endocrine gland.","options":["An endocrine gland","A sebaceous gland","An exocrine gland","A merocrine gland"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Endocrine glands have no ducts. They release their products, hormones, into the bloodstream, which carries them throughout the body. The thyroid, adrenal, and pituitary glands are examples, while glands that use ducts to deliver products to a surface are exocrine.","notes":["Correct. A ductless gland that releases into the blood is endocrine.","A sebaceous (oil) gland is an exocrine gland that releases its oily product into a hair follicle. It is the classic example of holocrine secretion, not a gland that empties into the blood.","An exocrine gland releases its product into a duct that carries it onto a surface or into a lumen, as sweat and salivary glands do. It is the answer for a gland with a duct.","Merocrine describes how many exocrine gland cells release their product, by exocytosis, leaving the cell unharmed. It is the answer for salivary glands and the pancreas, not for release into the blood."],"lt":"t-tissues","lid":"c18-x2","comp":16,"competencyId":"w1-epithelial-id"},{"id":"L00318","dok":3,"q":"A small cut that stays within the epidermis heals quickly and hurts noticeably. Which two structural features of epithelium explain this?","a":"Many dividing cells and a nerve supply.","options":["Gap junctions and a rich blood supply","Many dividing cells and a nerve supply","An abundant matrix and a nerve supply","A rich blood supply and many dividing cells"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Epithelium regenerates quickly because its cells divide often to replace those lost at the surface. It is also innervated, with nerve endings among its cells, so even a shallow cut is felt. It has no blood vessels of its own, which is why a cut that stays in the epidermis may not bleed.","notes":["Gap junctions let cells share ions and small molecules but do not explain fast healing or pain. Epithelium also lacks blood vessels, so this option has both parts wrong.","Correct. Frequent cell division and nerve endings explain fast healing and pain.","Epithelium has very little matrix; its cells are packed tightly together. A nerve supply is right, but abundant matrix describes connective tissue.","Epithelium is avascular, so it has no blood supply of its own. Dividing cells are right, but blood vessels belong to the connective tissue beneath it."],"lt":"t-tissues","lid":"c27-x2","comp":16,"competencyId":"w1-epithelial-id"},{"id":"L00320","dok":2,"q":"Most cancers are carcinomas, which arise from one tissue type. Which tissue is it, and why is it a common origin?","a":"Epithelium, because its cells divide often.","options":["Muscle tissue, because it contracts constantly","Epithelium, because its cells divide often","Connective tissue, because it is the most widespread","Nervous tissue, because it processes signals"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Carcinomas are cancers that arise from epithelium, and they make up the majority of cancers. Epithelial cells divide often to replace cells lost from surfaces, and each division is a chance for a mistake in copying DNA. Epithelium also lines the surfaces most exposed to damaging agents, such as the skin, lungs, and gut.","notes":["Contraction does not damage DNA, and most muscle cells rarely divide in adults. Muscle cancers are rare and are a type of sarcoma.","Correct. Carcinomas come from epithelium, which divides often.","Cancers of connective tissue are called sarcomas, and they are much less common than carcinomas. Being widespread does not make a tissue the most frequent source of cancer.","Neurons in the adult rarely divide, so they offer few chances for copying errors. Processing signals does not make a tissue more likely to become cancerous."],"lt":"t-tissues","lid":"c29-x2","comp":16,"competencyId":"w1-epithelial-id"},{"id":"L00322","dok":2,"q":"Simple squamous epithelium lines the air sacs of the lungs and the inner walls of capillaries. Why is this flat, single-layer design used in both places?","a":"Both sites move material across the wall, and one thin layer shortens the path.","options":["Both face heavy abrasion, which flat cells resist best","Both secrete large amounts of mucus, which flat cells produce","Both move material across the wall, and one thin layer shortens the path","Both need a thick barrier to block material from crossing"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The air sacs of the lungs and the walls of capillaries both exist to let materials cross quickly: oxygen and carbon dioxide in the lungs, and nutrients, gases, and wastes in capillaries. A single layer of flat cells gives the shortest possible path, so diffusion is fast. The trade-off is fragility, which is why the alveoli are so easily damaged.","notes":["Resisting abrasion is the job of stratified squamous epithelium, which sheds its top layers. A single flat layer is easily damaged by friction.","Mucus is made by goblet cells and mucous glands, usually in columnar or pseudostratified epithelium. Flat squamous cells are not built for heavy secretion.","Correct. A single flat layer gives the shortest path for diffusion.","A thick barrier would slow the crossing of materials, the opposite of what lungs and capillaries need. Thick, protective layers are found where blocking is the goal, as in the skin."],"lt":"t-tissues","lid":"d3-t-tissues-1","comp":16,"competencyId":"w1-epithelial-id"},{"id":"L00323","dok":2,"q":"The epidermis is stratified squamous epithelium, while the lining of the stomach is simple columnar. Why does each location get a different number of cell layers?","a":"Skin faces abrasion and needs replaceable layers, while the stomach lining needs a single layer for secretion.","options":["The stomach faces more abrasion, so it needs more layers than skin","Skin faces abrasion and needs replaceable layers; the stomach needs one layer for secretion","Skin absorbs nutrients and needs one layer; the stomach needs many layers","Both have many layers, but the stomach's are hidden under mucus"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Many layers protect against surface wear because the top cells can be lost and replaced from below, which fits the skin. A single layer of tall cells keeps the path for secretion and absorption short and leaves room in each cell for that work, which fits the stomach lining. The trade is protection versus exchange, so the two sites are built differently on purpose.","notes":["The stomach lining is protected by mucus and is built for secretion, not for heavy friction. The skin actually faces far more abrasion than the stomach lining.","Correct. Protection calls for layers, and secretion calls for a single layer.","This reverses the two sites. The skin does not absorb nutrients, and a thick layered lining in the stomach would get in the way of its secretion.","The stomach lining really is a single layer of simple columnar epithelium; mucus covers it but does not hide extra layers. Students choose this when they cannot reconcile a thin lining with a harsh environment."],"lt":"t-tissues","lid":"d3-t-tissues-2","comp":16,"competencyId":"w1-epithelial-id"},{"id":"L00326","dok":2,"q":"Pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium lines much of the airway. Why is its ciliated, mucus-trapping design suited to that location?","a":"It traps inhaled debris in mucus and sweeps it away from the lungs.","options":["It traps debris in mucus and sweeps it away from the lungs","It absorbs oxygen directly into the blood across the cilia","It forms a thick, waterproof seal that blocks airflow","It secretes digestive enzymes onto food passing through"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium lines the trachea and bronchi. Its goblet cells secrete mucus that traps inhaled dust and microbes, and its cilia beat to sweep that mucus up toward the throat, away from the lungs. This keeps debris from reaching the delicate air sacs, where gas exchange happens across thin simple squamous epithelium.","notes":["Correct. Mucus traps debris and cilia sweep it out.","Gas exchange happens deeper in the lungs, across the simple squamous epithelium of the air sacs. Cilia move mucus; they do not absorb oxygen.","A waterproof seal that blocked airflow would stop breathing. Waterproofing is the job of the keratinized epidermis of the skin.","Digestive enzymes are secreted into the digestive tract by glands such as the pancreas, not into the airways. The airway lining secretes mucus, not enzymes."],"lt":"t-tissues","lid":"d3-t-tissues-5","comp":16,"competencyId":"w1-epithelial-id"},{"id":"L00327","dok":2,"q":"Why does an endocrine gland not need a duct, while an exocrine gland does?","a":"Endocrine products enter the blood, while exocrine products need a duct to reach a surface.","options":["Endocrine cells burst to release their product, so no duct is needed","Endocrine glands are too large for a duct to fit inside them","Endocrine products enter the blood; exocrine products need a duct to reach a surface","Exocrine glands make hormones that can travel only through ducts"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Endocrine glands release hormones into the surrounding tissue fluid, where they enter the bloodstream and travel throughout the body, so no tube is needed. Exocrine products such as sweat, saliva, and digestive enzymes must reach one specific surface or cavity, and the duct is the path that carries them there. The pancreas does both, sending enzymes through a duct and hormones into the blood.","notes":["A cell that bursts to release its product describes holocrine secretion, a type of exocrine secretion seen in sebaceous glands. It is not why endocrine glands lack ducts.","Gland size has nothing to do with having a duct. Tiny sweat glands have ducts, while the larger adrenal glands do not.","Correct. The blood carries endocrine products, while ducts carry exocrine products.","Hormones are the products of endocrine glands, and they travel in the blood, not in ducts. Exocrine products include sweat, saliva, and digestive enzymes."],"lt":"t-tissues","lid":"d3-t-tissues-6","comp":16,"competencyId":"w1-epithelial-id"},{"id":"L00328","dok":2,"q":"Transitional epithelium lines the urinary bladder. Why is this tissue type, rather than simple squamous, the right choice there?","a":"It changes shape as the bladder fills and empties, stretching without tearing.","options":["It is keratinized to resist abrasion from urine","It is one thin layer that lets urine diffuse back into the blood","It changes shape as the bladder fills, stretching without tearing","It carries cilia that sweep urine toward the urethra"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The bladder repeatedly fills and empties, so its lining must change its surface area without tearing. Transitional epithelium has several layers whose surface cells are dome-shaped when the bladder is empty and flatten out as it fills. Simple squamous epithelium is a single fragile layer that would tear when stretched and would let material cross too easily.","notes":["Keratinized epithelium is found in the skin, where it resists abrasion and drying. The bladder lining is not keratinized; it needs to stretch rather than toughen.","Letting urine diffuse back into the blood would undo the work of the kidneys, so the bladder lining must act as a barrier. A single thin layer is the design for exchange surfaces such as the lung air sacs.","Correct. Transitional epithelium stretches as the bladder fills.","The bladder lining does not have cilia, and urine is moved by the muscular wall, not by beating projections. Ciliated epithelium is the answer for the airways."],"lt":"t-tissues","lid":"d3-t-tissues-7","comp":16,"competencyId":"w1-epithelial-id"},{"id":"N-m1-epithelial-1","dok":1,"q":"Which germ layer forms the epithelial lining of the digestive and respiratory tracts?","a":"Endoderm.","options":["Mesoderm","Endoderm","Mesenchyme","Ectoderm"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The endoderm is the innermost of the three germ layers. It forms the epithelial lining of the digestive tract and the respiratory tract, which fits its position on the inside of the early embryo. The muscle and connective tissue around those linings come from the mesoderm.","notes":["The mesoderm is the middle layer and forms muscle, bone, cartilage, blood, and most connective tissue. It forms the walls around the gut and airways, but not their inner epithelial lining.","Correct. The endoderm forms the lining of the gut and airways.","Mesenchyme is an embryonic connective tissue that develops into all types of connective tissue. It is a tissue, not one of the three germ layers.","The ectoderm is the outer layer and forms the epidermis of the skin and all nervous tissue. Pick it for the outer covering of the body or the brain."],"comp":15,"competencyId":"w1-germ-layers"},{"id":"N-m1-epithelial-2","dok":2,"q":"A slide of the esophagus lining shows several cell layers, with cuboidal cells in the deep layers and flat cells at the free surface. How is this epithelium named?","a":"Stratified squamous epithelium.","options":["Stratified squamous epithelium","Simple squamous epithelium","Simple cuboidal epithelium","Stratified cuboidal epithelium"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Stratified epithelia are named for the shape of the cells at the free (apical) surface, not the deep cells. In stratified squamous epithelium the deepest cells are cuboidal or columnar, but they flatten as they are pushed toward the surface, so the name is squamous. Name the layers first, then the shape of the surface cells.","notes":["Correct. Several layers with flat surface cells is stratified squamous.","Simple squamous has only one layer of flat cells. This tissue has several layers, so it must be stratified.","Simple cuboidal is a single layer of cube-shaped cells, as in kidney tubules. This tissue has several layers and flat surface cells, so neither part of the name fits.","Stratified cuboidal would need cube-shaped cells at the surface. Students pick it when they name the tissue from its deep cells rather than its surface cells."],"comp":16,"competencyId":"w1-epithelial-id"},{"id":"N-m1-epithelial-3","dok":2,"q":"Which location is lined by non-keratinized stratified squamous epithelium?","a":"The esophagus.","options":["The epidermis of the skin","The trachea","The esophagus","The kidney tubules"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Non-keratinized stratified squamous epithelium has many layers for protection, but its surface cells stay alive and moist rather than filling with keratin. It lines surfaces that face friction but must stay wet, such as the mouth, esophagus, and vagina. The skin uses the keratinized form, which is dry and tough.","notes":["The epidermis is keratinized stratified squamous epithelium, with dead keratin-filled cells at the surface that make it dry and water-resistant. Pick it for the keratinized version.","The trachea is lined by pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium that moves mucus. It is the answer for an airway lining.","Correct. The esophagus is lined by non-keratinized stratified squamous epithelium.","Kidney tubules are lined by simple cuboidal epithelium, built for secretion and absorption. It is a single layer, not a stratified one."],"comp":16,"competencyId":"w1-epithelial-id"},{"id":"N-m1-epithelial-4","dok":1,"q":"In which mode of exocrine secretion does the entire cell rupture to release its product, as in sebaceous glands?","a":"Holocrine.","options":["Apocrine","Endocrine","Merocrine","Holocrine"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"In holocrine secretion, the product builds up inside the cell until the whole cell ruptures and becomes part of the secretion. Sebaceous (oil) glands work this way, and new cells must keep dividing to replace those lost. A simple memory aid is that holocrine sounds like whole, and the whole cell is secreted.","notes":["Apocrine secretion pinches off the apical part of the cell with the product, and the cell then repairs itself. It is the answer for mammary glands.","Endocrine describes ductless glands that release hormones into the blood. It is a gland category, not a way an exocrine cell releases its product.","Merocrine secretion releases the product by exocytosis and leaves the cell unharmed. It is the most common type and is the answer for salivary glands and the pancreas.","Correct. In holocrine secretion the whole cell is released."],"comp":16,"competencyId":"w1-epithelial-id"},{"id":"N-m1-epithelial-5","dok":1,"q":"Which mode of exocrine secretion releases the product by exocytosis and leaves the cell unharmed?","a":"Merocrine.","options":["Endocrine","Holocrine","Apocrine","Merocrine"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"In merocrine secretion, the product is packed into secretory vesicles that fuse with the plasma membrane and release their contents by exocytosis. The cell loses nothing but the product, so it can keep secreting. It is the most common type of exocrine secretion and is used by salivary glands and the pancreas.","notes":["Endocrine refers to ductless glands that release hormones into the blood. It does not describe how an exocrine cell releases its product.","Holocrine secretion releases the product by rupturing the entire cell, so the cell is destroyed. It is the answer for sebaceous glands.","Apocrine secretion releases the product by pinching off the top of the cell, which then repairs itself. It is the answer for mammary glands.","Correct. Merocrine secretion uses exocytosis and spares the cell."],"comp":16,"competencyId":"w1-epithelial-id"},{"id":"N-m1-epithelial-6","dok":1,"q":"Which cell is a unicellular gland that secretes mucus?","a":"The goblet cell.","options":["The goblet cell","The mast cell","The adipocyte","The plasma cell"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"A goblet cell is a single-celled exocrine gland shaped like a wine goblet, with a swollen top full of mucus. Goblet cells are scattered among the cells of simple columnar and pseudostratified epithelium in the intestines and airways. The mucus they release lubricates and protects the lining and traps particles.","notes":["Correct. Goblet cells are single-celled mucus glands.","A mast cell sits in connective tissue along blood vessels and secretes histamine during inflammation. It is the answer for a question about allergic and inflammatory responses.","An adipocyte is a connective tissue cell that stores fat. It is the answer for adipose tissue, not mucus secretion.","A plasma cell develops from a B lymphocyte and secretes antibodies. It is the answer for antibody production in connective tissue."],"comp":16,"competencyId":"w1-epithelial-id"},{"id":"N-m1-epithelial-7","dok":2,"q":"Which gland is the classic example of a mixed gland, with both exocrine and endocrine parts?","a":"The pancreas.","options":["The pancreas","The pituitary gland","The thyroid gland","A sweat gland"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The pancreas is a mixed gland. Its exocrine part secretes digestive enzymes into a duct that empties into the small intestine, and its endocrine part releases insulin and glucagon directly into the blood. One organ therefore uses both delivery routes.","notes":["Correct. The pancreas is both exocrine and endocrine.","The pituitary gland is purely endocrine, releasing hormones into the blood. Pick it for a question about endocrine glands at the base of the brain.","The thyroid gland is purely endocrine, releasing its hormones into the blood with no duct. It is the answer for a ductless gland in the neck.","A sweat gland is purely exocrine, sending sweat through a duct onto the skin surface. It is the answer for a gland with a duct."],"comp":16,"competencyId":"w1-epithelial-id"},{"id":"N-m1-epithelial-8","dok":1,"q":"Which epithelium, made of two or more layers of cube-shaped cells, lines the ducts of sweat glands?","a":"Stratified cuboidal epithelium.","options":["Transitional epithelium","Stratified cuboidal epithelium","Simple cuboidal epithelium","Stratified squamous epithelium"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Stratified cuboidal epithelium has two or more layers of cube-shaped cells. It is fairly uncommon and is found mainly in the ducts of sweat, mammary, and salivary glands, where it gives some protection and limited secretion. The extra layer makes the duct wall sturdier than a single layer would be.","notes":["Transitional epithelium has several layers that stretch as the organ fills and lines the urinary bladder. It is not found in gland ducts.","Correct. Sweat gland ducts are lined by stratified cuboidal epithelium.","Simple cuboidal epithelium has cube-shaped cells but only one layer. It is the answer for kidney tubules and small glands.","Stratified squamous epithelium has flat surface cells and many layers. It is the answer for the skin and the esophagus."],"comp":16,"competencyId":"w1-epithelial-id"},{"id":"N-m1-epithelial-9","dok":2,"q":"A multicellular exocrine gland has a branching duct and rounded secretory portions. What is its structural name?","a":"Compound acinar.","options":["Simple acinar","Simple tubular","Compound tubular","Compound acinar"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Gland names combine two features. The duct is simple if it does not branch and compound if it branches, and the secretory portion is tubular if it is tube-shaped and acinar if it is rounded. A branching duct with rounded secretory portions is therefore a compound acinar gland.","notes":["Simple acinar gets the rounded secretory portion right but describes an unbranched duct. The branching duct makes this gland compound.","Simple tubular would mean an unbranched duct with a tube-shaped secretory part. Both features are the opposite of this gland.","Compound tubular gets the branching duct right but describes a tube-shaped secretory portion. Rounded secretory portions make it acinar.","Correct. Branching duct plus rounded secretory portions is compound acinar."],"comp":16,"competencyId":"w1-epithelial-id"},{"id":"N-m1-epithelial-10","dok":1,"q":"In which mode of secretion does the apical part of the cell pinch off with the product, after which the cell repairs itself, as in mammary glands?","a":"Apocrine.","options":["Holocrine","Endocrine","Apocrine","Merocrine"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"In apocrine secretion, the product collects at the apical (top) end of the cell, and that part of the cell pinches off to release it. The rest of the cell survives, repairs itself, and secretes again. Mammary glands are the standard example.","notes":["Holocrine secretion destroys the whole cell to release its product. It is the answer for sebaceous glands.","Endocrine describes ductless glands that release hormones into the blood. It is not a way that an exocrine cell releases its product.","Correct. Apocrine secretion pinches off the top of the cell.","Merocrine secretion releases the product by exocytosis without losing any of the cell. It is the answer for salivary glands and the pancreas."],"comp":16,"competencyId":"w1-epithelial-id"},{"id":"L00380","dok":2,"q":"The epidermis is keratinized stratified squamous epithelium. What does keratinized tell you about its surface cells?","a":"They are dead cells filled with keratin.","options":["They are cube-shaped cells that secrete sweat","They are dead cells filled with keratin","They are living cells with cilia on top","They are cells that stretch and change shape"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Keratinized means the surface cells have filled with the tough protein keratin and died, forming a dry, durable, water-resistant layer. This is the form of stratified squamous epithelium found in the epidermis. The nonkeratinized form, with living moist surface cells, lines wet surfaces such as the mouth, esophagus, and vagina.","notes":["Cube-shaped cells describe cuboidal epithelium, which lines the ducts of sweat glands. This would be the answer for the tissue forming sweat gland ducts, not the skin surface.","Correct. Keratinized surface cells are dead and packed with keratin.","Living ciliated cells are found in pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium lining the airways. Pick this description if the question asked about the tissue that sweeps mucus in the trachea.","Cells that stretch and change shape describe transitional epithelium of the urinary bladder. Choose it if the question asked which epithelium lets the bladder expand."],"lt":"t-integumentary","lid":"c2-x2","comp":16,"competencyId":"w1-epithelial-id"},{"id":"L00416","dok":1,"q":"Sebaceous glands release sebum by which mode of secretion, in which the whole cell fills with product and ruptures?","a":"Holocrine secretion.","options":["Merocrine secretion","Holocrine secretion","Apocrine secretion","Endocrine secretion"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"In holocrine secretion, product builds up in the cytoplasm until the entire cell ruptures, so the dead cell becomes part of the secretion. Sebaceous glands work this way, and new cells from the base of the gland replace those that are lost. The prefix holo means whole, which matches the whole cell being released.","notes":["Merocrine secretion releases product by exocytosis and leaves the cell unharmed; it is the most common type. Pick it if the question asked how salivary glands or eccrine sweat glands secrete.","Correct. Holocrine secretion releases whole ruptured cells, as sebaceous glands do.","Apocrine secretion releases product by pinching off the apical (top) part of the cell, which then repairs itself. The course notes give mammary glands as the example, so choose it for a question about milk release.","Endocrine is not a mode of exocrine secretion; it describes ductless glands that release hormones into the blood. It would be the answer for glands such as the thyroid."],"lt":"t-integumentary","lid":"w1g-ig-1","comp":25,"competencyId":"w1-skin-accessory"},{"id":"L00417","dok":2,"q":"An eccrine sweat gland and a sebaceous gland release their products in different ways. Which pairing is correct?","a":"The eccrine gland is merocrine, and the sebaceous gland is holocrine.","options":["Both glands are merocrine","The eccrine gland is merocrine, and the sebaceous gland is holocrine","Both glands are holocrine","The eccrine gland is holocrine, and the sebaceous gland is merocrine"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Eccrine sweat glands release watery sweat by exocytosis, leaving the secretory cells intact, which is merocrine secretion. Sebaceous glands fill whole cells with sebum and then rupture them, which is holocrine secretion. The two glands sit side by side in the dermis but use opposite strategies.","notes":["Eccrine glands are merocrine, but sebaceous glands are not, because their cells rupture. This would be right for two glands like a salivary gland and an eccrine sweat gland.","Correct. Eccrine glands are merocrine and sebaceous glands are holocrine.","Sebaceous glands are holocrine, but eccrine glands keep their cells intact. Students choose this when they assume all skin glands secrete the same way.","This pairing reverses the two modes. Sweat glands do not destroy their cells to make sweat, while sebaceous cells do burst to release sebum."],"lt":"t-integumentary","lid":"w1g-ig-2","comp":25,"competencyId":"w1-skin-accessory"},{"id":"N-m1-epithelial-500","dok":1,"q":"Which surface of an epithelial cell faces the lumen or the free surface of the tissue?","a":"The apical surface.","options":["The basal surface","The basement membrane","The apical surface","The lateral surface"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Every epithelial cell has three surfaces. The apical surface faces the open space, either a lumen (the hollow inside of a tube or organ) or the outside of the body, and it is where you find cilia, microvilli, or keratin when a tissue has them. Because epithelium is named by the shape of its apical cells, finding this surface is the first step in identifying a slide.","notes":["The basal surface is the opposite side of the cell, resting on the basement membrane. Pick it if the question asked which surface is anchored to the connective tissue below.","The basement membrane is not part of the cell at all. It is the thin layer beneath the basal surface that anchors the epithelium to the connective tissue below.","Correct. The apical surface faces the lumen or free surface.","The lateral surfaces are the sides of the cell that face its neighbors, where most cell junctions sit. Pick them if the question asked which surface connects one epithelial cell to the next."],"comp":16,"competencyId":"w1-epithelial-id"},{"id":"N-m1-epithelial-501","dok":1,"q":"Which surface of an epithelial cell faces the neighboring cells and holds most of the cell junctions?","a":"The lateral surface.","options":["The lateral surface","The basal surface","The free surface","The apical surface"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The lateral surfaces are the sides of an epithelial cell, where it presses against the cells beside it. Junctions such as tight junctions and desmosomes are concentrated here, which is how epithelial cells stay tightly packed and form a continuous sheet with very little material between them.","notes":["Correct. The lateral surfaces face neighboring cells and carry the junctions that hold the sheet together.","The basal surface rests on the basement membrane. Pick it if the question asked which surface anchors the cell to the tissue below, the site of hemidesmosomes.","The free surface is another name for the exposed apical side of the tissue, which faces a lumen or the outside rather than neighboring cells.","The apical surface faces the lumen or the outside, not other cells. Pick it if the question asked where cilia or microvilli are found."],"comp":16,"competencyId":"w1-epithelial-id"},{"id":"N-m1-epithelial-502","dok":1,"q":"What do microvilli on the apical surface of an epithelial cell do?","a":"They increase the surface area for absorption.","options":["They increase the surface area for absorption","They waterproof the surface with keratin","They sweep mucus along the surface","They anchor the cell to the basement membrane"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Microvilli are tiny, finger-like folds of the plasma membrane on the apical surface. They do not move; instead, they greatly increase the amount of membrane in contact with the lumen, so more material can be absorbed. That is why they are common on simple columnar cells lining the small intestine and on simple cuboidal cells of kidney tubules.","notes":["Correct. Microvilli add surface area so the cell can absorb more.","Keratin is a tough protein that fills the surface cells of the epidermis. It belongs to keratinized stratified squamous epithelium, not to microvilli.","Sweeping mucus is the job of cilia, which are longer, motile projections. Pick this if the question were about the projections on the cells lining the trachea.","Anchoring to the basement membrane is done on the basal surface by hemidesmosomes. It is the opposite end of the cell from microvilli."],"comp":16,"competencyId":"w1-epithelial-id"},{"id":"N-m1-epithelial-503","dok":1,"q":"What do cilia on the apical surface of an epithelium do?","a":"They move mucus and other substances along the surface.","options":["They store fat for energy","They absorb nutrients from the lumen","They move mucus along the surface","They secrete hormones into the blood"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Cilia are hair-like projections on the apical surface that beat together in waves. Their motion pushes a layer of mucus, or other material, across the surface in one direction. In the airways this carries trapped dust and microbes up and away from the lungs.","notes":["Fat storage is the job of adipocytes in adipose connective tissue. It has nothing to do with projections on an epithelial surface.","Absorption is helped by microvilli, which increase surface area but do not beat. Pick this if the question asked about the brush border of intestinal cells.","Correct. Cilia beat in waves to move mucus and particles along the surface.","Hormones are released into the blood by endocrine glands, which have no ducts. Pick this if the question asked what an endocrine gland does."],"comp":16,"competencyId":"w1-epithelial-id"},{"id":"N-m1-epithelial-504","dok":1,"q":"Goblet cells are scattered among the cells of which two epithelia?","a":"Simple columnar and pseudostratified ciliated columnar.","options":["Simple columnar and pseudostratified ciliated columnar","Transitional epithelium and stratified cuboidal epithelium","Keratinized and non-keratinized stratified squamous","Simple squamous and simple cuboidal"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Goblet cells are single-celled glands that secrete mucus, and on a slide they look like pale, cup-shaped cells tucked between taller neighbors. They appear in simple columnar epithelium of the digestive tract and in pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium of the airways. Both linings need a protective, moist coat of mucus over their surface.","notes":["Correct. Goblet cells sit in the columnar linings of the gut and the airways.","Transitional epithelium lines the bladder and stratified cuboidal lines sweat gland ducts. Neither one carries goblet cells.","Stratified squamous epithelium protects the skin, mouth, and esophagus by layering cells. Its surface is protected by layers or keratin, not by goblet cells.","Simple squamous and simple cuboidal cells are too short to house goblet cells. These tissues are built for diffusion and for secretion or absorption in places such as the alveoli and kidney tubules."],"comp":16,"competencyId":"w1-epithelial-id"},{"id":"N-m1-epithelial-505","dok":1,"q":"What are the main functions of simple squamous epithelium?","a":"Diffusion and filtration.","options":["Diffusion and filtration","Moving mucus with cilia","Protection against abrasion","Stretching as an organ fills"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Simple squamous epithelium is a single layer of very thin, flat cells, so substances only have to cross one thin cell to get through. That makes it ideal for diffusion, as in the alveoli of the lungs, and for filtration, as in the glomeruli of the kidneys. It also lines blood vessels and forms the surface of serous membranes.","notes":["Correct. A single thin layer is built for diffusion and filtration.","Moving mucus with cilia is the job of pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium in the trachea and bronchi.","Protection against abrasion needs many layers, which is the job of stratified squamous epithelium in the skin, mouth, and esophagus. A single thin layer would wear through.","Stretching as an organ fills is the job of transitional epithelium in the urinary bladder and ureters."],"comp":16,"competencyId":"w1-epithelial-id"},{"id":"N-m1-epithelial-506","dok":1,"q":"Which epithelium, uncommon in the body, is found in parts of the male urethra and in the ducts of some glands?","a":"Stratified columnar epithelium.","options":["Simple squamous epithelium","Keratinized stratified squamous epithelium","Simple cuboidal epithelium","Stratified columnar epithelium"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Stratified columnar epithelium has several layers, and the cells at the free surface are tall and column-shaped. It is rare in the body, found mainly in parts of the male urethra and in the ducts of some glands, where it gives both protection and some secretion.","notes":["Simple squamous epithelium is a single flat layer used for diffusion. Pick it if the question asked about the alveoli or the lining of blood vessels.","Keratinized stratified squamous epithelium is the epidermis of the skin, the most exposed surface in the body. Pick it if the question asked about a dry, outer surface.","Simple cuboidal epithelium is a single layer of cube-shaped cells. Pick it if the question asked about kidney tubules or the surface of the ovary.","Correct. Stratified columnar is the rare type found in the male urethra and some gland ducts."],"comp":16,"competencyId":"w1-epithelial-id"},{"id":"N-m1-epithelial-507","dok":2,"q":"A slide of the small intestine lining shows a single layer of tall cells with a fuzzy, brush-like edge on their apical surface. What forms this brush border?","a":"Microvilli.","options":["Cilia","Microvilli","Goblet cells","Keratin"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The brush border is the fuzzy band you see along the apical surface of absorptive cells, and it is made of thousands of microvilli. In the small intestine, the job is absorbing nutrients, so the extra membrane surface area that microvilli provide fits that location. Cilia can also look fuzzy, but they are longer and are found in the airways, not on intestinal absorptive cells.","notes":["Cilia are longer, motile projections that move mucus. Pick them if the slide came from the trachea, where the lining is pseudostratified ciliated columnar.","Correct. Microvilli form the brush border and add surface area for absorption.","Goblet cells are present in the intestinal lining, but they are whole cells that look like pale cups, not a fuzzy edge. Pick them if the question asked which cells secrete mucus.","Keratin is the tough protein in the dead surface layers of the epidermis. Pick it if the slide showed a thick, layered skin surface."],"comp":16,"competencyId":"w1-epithelial-id"},{"id":"N-m1-epithelial-508","dok":2,"q":"A slide shows many cell layers. The deep cells are cuboidal, but the top layers are flat, dead, and lack nuclei, forming a compact pink band. Which tissue is it?","a":"Keratinized stratified squamous epithelium.","options":["Keratinized stratified squamous epithelium","Stratified cuboidal epithelium","Non-keratinized stratified squamous epithelium","Transitional epithelium"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Many layers with flat surface cells make this stratified squamous epithelium. The deciding clue is the surface: cells that have filled with keratin, lost their nuclei, and died form a compact layer at the top, which marks it as keratinized. This is the epidermis of the skin.","notes":["Correct. Dead, flat, anucleate surface cells filled with keratin mark the keratinized type found in the epidermis.","Stratified cuboidal epithelium usually has just two layers of cube-shaped cells around a small lumen. Pick it for the duct of a sweat gland.","Non-keratinized stratified squamous epithelium also has flat surface cells, but they are alive and still have nuclei. Pick it for a moist lining such as the esophagus, mouth, or vagina.","Transitional epithelium has rounded, dome-shaped surface cells in a relaxed organ. Pick it for a slide of the urinary bladder or ureter."],"comp":16,"competencyId":"w1-epithelial-id"},{"id":"N-m1-epithelial-509","dok":2,"q":"A slide of the trachea shows nuclei at several different heights, cilia on the apical surface, and goblet cells, yet every cell touches the basement membrane. Which tissue is it?","a":"Pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium.","options":["Pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium","Transitional epithelium","Stratified columnar epithelium","Simple columnar epithelium"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Pseudo means false. The nuclei sit at different heights, so the tissue looks layered, but every cell rests on the basement membrane, which makes it truly a single layer. Add cilia, goblet cells, and a location in the trachea, and the tissue is pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium.","notes":["Correct. Staggered nuclei with every cell on the basement membrane, plus cilia and goblet cells, mark pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium.","Transitional epithelium has true layers with dome-shaped surface cells and no cilia. Pick it for the urinary bladder.","Stratified columnar epithelium has true layers, with only the deepest cells on the basement membrane. Pick it for the rare lining of parts of the male urethra.","Simple columnar epithelium is one layer of tall cells whose nuclei line up in a single row near the base. Pick it for the stomach or intestine."],"comp":16,"competencyId":"w1-epithelial-id"},{"id":"N-m1-epithelial-510","dok":2,"q":"The serosa is the outer covering of many organs inside the ventral body cavity. Which epithelium forms its surface?","a":"Simple squamous epithelium.","options":["Transitional epithelium","Simple squamous epithelium","Simple columnar epithelium","Stratified squamous epithelium"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The serosa is the visceral layer of a serous membrane, and its surface is a single layer of flat cells. A thin, smooth, moist sheet lets organs slide against the body wall with little friction, which suits the serous membranes. This is why the serosa is listed among the locations of simple squamous epithelium, along with the alveoli and the lining of blood vessels.","notes":["Transitional epithelium lines the inside of the bladder and ureters so they can stretch. It is not found on the outer surface of organs.","Correct. A single sheet of flat cells forms the smooth surface of the serosa.","Simple columnar epithelium lines the inside of the digestive tract from the stomach to the rectum. It forms the inner lining of the gut, not its outer covering.","Stratified squamous epithelium is thick and built to resist abrasion. Pick it for the skin, mouth, or esophagus, not a slippery organ covering."],"comp":16,"competencyId":"w1-epithelial-id"},{"id":"N-m1-epithelial-511","dok":2,"q":"A slide shows a single layer of cube-shaped cells with round, central nuclei. Besides the kidney tubules, where else would you expect to find this tissue?","a":"On the surface of the ovary.","options":["Lining the trachea","On the surface of the ovary","Lining the urinary bladder","Lining the alveoli of the lungs"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"A single layer of cube-shaped cells with round central nuclei is simple cuboidal epithelium. It handles secretion and absorption and is found in kidney tubules, in small glands, and on the surface of the ovary.","notes":["The trachea is lined by pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium, with tall cells, cilia, and goblet cells. Pick it for a slide with staggered nuclei and cilia.","Correct. Simple cuboidal epithelium covers the surface of the ovary as well as lining kidney tubules.","The urinary bladder is lined by transitional epithelium, which has several layers that stretch. Pick it for a layered tissue with dome-shaped surface cells.","The alveoli are lined by simple squamous epithelium, which is flat and thin for gas exchange. Pick it for a slide of very thin, flat cells."],"comp":16,"competencyId":"w1-epithelial-id"},{"id":"N-m1-epithelial-512","dok":3,"q":"In a heavy smoker, part of the ciliated lining of the bronchi can be replaced by stratified squamous epithelium. What does the airway lose with this change?","a":"The cilia that sweep mucus and trapped debris away from the lungs.","options":["The cilia that sweep mucus out of the airway","Its ability to stretch as the airway fills","Its blood supply, since epithelium becomes vascular","Its basement membrane anchoring the epithelium"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The normal bronchial lining is pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium, where goblet cells make mucus and cilia beat to carry trapped particles upward and out. Stratified squamous epithelium is tougher against irritation but has no cilia or goblet cells. So the airway gains protection at that spot but loses its cleaning action, and mucus and debris collect instead of being swept away.","notes":["Correct. Stratified squamous epithelium has no cilia, so the airway loses its mucus-clearing action at that spot.","Stretching as an organ fills is the special feature of transitional epithelium in the urinary bladder. The bronchial lining was never built to stretch like that.","All epithelium is avascular, both before and after this change. It always relies on the connective tissue underneath for nutrients, so blood supply is not what changes.","Every epithelium, simple or stratified, rests on a basement membrane. The new tissue still has one, so this is not what is lost."],"comp":16,"competencyId":"w1-epithelial-id"},{"id":"N-m1-epithelial-513","dok":3,"q":"A slide from a full, stretched urinary bladder shows a few layers of fairly flat surface cells, and a student labels it stratified squamous. Which tissue is it really, and why do the surface cells look flat?","a":"Transitional epithelium, because its surface cells flatten when the bladder is stretched.","options":["Transitional, because its surface cells flatten when stretched","Stratified cuboidal, because its cells shrink as urine collects","Keratinized stratified squamous, because the surface is dead","Simple squamous, because one thin layer allows diffusion"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Transitional epithelium lines the urinary bladder and ureters. Its surface cells are dome-shaped when the bladder is empty, but as the bladder fills and stretches they flatten out, and the layers thin. That is exactly why a full-bladder slide can be mistaken for stratified squamous, so the location is the clue that settles it.","notes":["Correct. Transitional surface cells change from dome-shaped to flat as the bladder stretches.","Stratified cuboidal epithelium lines sweat gland ducts and does not change shape with filling. The idea that its cells shrink is a made-up explanation.","The bladder lining is not keratinized, and its surface cells are alive. Keratinized stratified squamous is the epidermis of the skin.","Simple squamous epithelium is a single layer, but this slide shows a few layers. Pick it for a slide of the alveoli or a blood vessel lining."],"comp":16,"competencyId":"w1-epithelial-id"},{"id":"N-m1-epithelial-514","dok":2,"q":"The epidermis is keratinized stratified squamous epithelium, but the lining of the esophagus is non-keratinized. What best explains this difference?","a":"Skin faces dry air and needs a tough, water-resistant surface, while the esophagus stays moist.","options":["The esophagus needs cilia on its surface to push food downward","Skin is avascular, while the esophageal lining has its own blood vessels","The esophagus lacks a basement membrane, so it cannot hold keratin","Skin faces dry air, while the esophagus surface stays moist"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Both tissues are many layers thick with flat surface cells, because both face rubbing and wear. Skin is exposed to dry air, so its surface cells fill with keratin and die, forming a tough layer that resists water loss. The esophagus is kept moist by mucus and saliva, so its surface cells stay alive and the lining stays soft and flexible as food passes.","notes":["The esophagus has no cilia; food is moved by muscle contractions in its wall. Cilia belong to the airway lining, such as the trachea.","All epithelium is avascular, including both the epidermis and the esophageal lining. Each one gets its nutrients by diffusion from the connective tissue below.","Every epithelium, including the esophageal lining, rests on a basement membrane. This is not a real difference between the two tissues.","Correct. A dry outer surface gets keratin, while a moist inner lining stays non-keratinized."],"comp":16,"competencyId":"w1-epithelial-id"}]},{"id":"m1-connective","title":"Connective tissue","summary":"CT proper, cartilage, bone, blood as CT, fibers and cells, locations","mw":"2026-08-24","tr":"2026-08-25","cards":[{"id":"L00247","dok":1,"q":"Which tissue type is the most diverse and widespread, binding and supporting other tissues?","a":"Connective tissue.","options":["Epithelial tissue","Muscle tissue","Nervous tissue","Connective tissue"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Connective tissue is the most diverse and widespread tissue type, ranging from fluid blood to hard bone. It binds and supports other tissues, protects and insulates organs, stores energy as fat, and transports materials in the blood. What these different forms share is a plan of scattered cells within a large extracellular matrix.","notes":["Epithelial tissue covers surfaces, lines cavities, and forms glands, with tightly packed cells and little matrix. It is the answer for a tissue that forms coverings and linings.","Muscle tissue contracts to produce movement and force. It is the answer for a tissue specialized for movement, not for binding and support.","Nervous tissue detects changes and carries nerve impulses. It is the answer for the tissue of the brain, spinal cord, and nerves.","Correct. Connective tissue is the most diverse and widespread type."],"lt":"t-tissues","lid":"c4","comp":18,"competencyId":"w1-connective-id"},{"id":"L00248","dok":1,"q":"Which three ingredients make up every connective tissue?","a":"Cells, fibers, and ground substance.","options":["Neurons, fibers, and plasma","Cells, junctions, and ducts","Cells, fibers, and ground substance","Cells, fibers, and a basement membrane"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Every connective tissue is built from the same three ingredients: cells, protein fibers, and ground substance. The fibers and ground substance together form the extracellular matrix, which makes up most of the tissue's volume. Changing the proportions of these ingredients is what makes blood liquid, cartilage firm, and bone hard.","notes":["Neurons are the signaling cells of nervous tissue, and plasma is the fluid matrix of blood only. This list mixes nervous tissue with one specific connective tissue.","Cell junctions and ducts are features of epithelium and glands. Epithelial cells are joined tightly by junctions, while connective tissue cells are scattered in matrix.","Correct. Cells, fibers, and ground substance.","The basement membrane belongs to epithelium; it anchors the epithelium to the connective tissue below. Cells and fibers are correct, but the third ingredient of connective tissue is ground substance."],"lt":"t-tissues","lid":"c5","comp":18,"competencyId":"w1-connective-id"},{"id":"L00249","dok":1,"q":"Which connective tissue fiber type is strong, ropelike, and the most abundant?","a":"Collagen fibers.","options":["Muscle fibers","Collagen fibers","Elastic fibers","Reticular fibers"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Collagen fibers are thick, strong, ropelike fibers that resist pulling forces. Collagen is the most abundant protein in the body, about 25 percent of all protein, and it is found in most connective tissues, including bone, tendon, and cartilage. When collagen fibers run in parallel bundles, as in a tendon, they give great strength in one direction.","notes":["Muscle fibers are muscle cells, not fibers in a connective tissue matrix. Students pick this because the word fiber is shared, but muscle fibers are living cells that contract.","Correct. Collagen fibers are the strong, abundant, ropelike fibers.","Elastic fibers are made of elastin and can stretch to about 150 percent of their relaxed length and then recoil. They are the answer for skin, lungs, and artery walls that must spring back.","Reticular fibers are fine, branching collagen fibers coated with glycoprotein that form delicate networks. They are the answer for the supporting framework of soft organs such as lymph nodes and the spleen."],"lt":"t-tissues","lid":"c6","comp":18,"competencyId":"w1-connective-id"},{"id":"L00250","dok":1,"q":"Which cell builds the fibers and ground substance of connective tissue proper?","a":"The fibroblast.","options":["The macrophage","The chondrocyte","The fibroblast","The adipocyte"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Fibroblasts are the most common cells of connective tissue proper. They migrate through the tissue and secrete both the protein fibers and the ground substance that make up the matrix. In the blast and cyte naming pattern, fibroblasts are the active, matrix-building form, while fibrocytes are the mature cells that maintain it.","notes":["Macrophages are phagocytic cells, derived from monocytes, that engulf bacteria and debris. They defend connective tissue rather than build its matrix.","Chondrocytes are mature cartilage cells sitting in lacunae, where they maintain the cartilage matrix. They are the answer for cartilage, which is a supporting connective tissue rather than connective tissue proper.","Correct. Fibroblasts secrete the fibers and ground substance.","Adipocytes are connective tissue cells that store fat as triglycerides. They are the answer for adipose tissue, not for the cell that builds fibers."],"lt":"t-tissues","lid":"c7","comp":18,"competencyId":"w1-connective-id"},{"id":"L00251","dok":1,"q":"Which tissue is the most common type of cartilage, with fine collagen fibers and a glassy-looking matrix?","a":"Hyaline cartilage.","options":["Elastic cartilage","Fibrocartilage","Dense regular connective tissue","Hyaline cartilage"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Hyaline cartilage is the most common cartilage in the body. Its collagen fibers are so fine they are not visible with ordinary staining, which gives the matrix a smooth, glassy look, and its chondrocytes sit in small spaces called lacunae. It covers the ends of long bones and forms the costal cartilages, the nose, and the rings of the trachea.","notes":["Elastic cartilage looks similar but has many elastic fibers that let it bend and spring back. It is the answer for the external ear and the epiglottis.","Fibrocartilage has thick, visible collagen bundles that resist compression and absorb shock. It is the answer for intervertebral discs, the pubic symphysis, and the knee menisci.","Dense regular connective tissue has densely packed parallel collagen fibers with fibroblasts between them, not chondrocytes in lacunae. It is the answer for tendons and ligaments.","Correct. Hyaline cartilage is the most common, glassy cartilage."],"lt":"t-tissues","lid":"c8","comp":26,"competencyId":"w2-cartilage-types"},{"id":"L00255","dok":1,"q":"Compact bone is built around repeating structural units called what?","a":"Osteons.","options":["Lacunae","Trabeculae","Lamellae","Osteons"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Compact bone is built from repeating cylindrical units called osteons. Each osteon is a set of concentric rings of bone matrix, called lamellae, around a central canal that carries blood vessels and nerves. Osteocytes sit in small spaces called lacunae between the rings.","notes":["Lacunae are the small spaces where osteocytes sit within an osteon. They are parts of the unit, not the repeating unit itself.","Trabeculae are the thin bony struts of spongy bone, arranged as an open lattice with marrow in the spaces. Pick them for a question about the inner bone tissue at the ends of long bones.","Lamellae are the concentric rings of matrix that make up each osteon. They are the layers of the unit, so choose them for a question about the rings around the central canal.","Correct. Osteons are the repeating units of compact bone."],"lt":"t-tissues","lid":"c12","comp":30,"competencyId":"w2-compact-spongy"},{"id":"L00256","dok":1,"q":"Which connective tissue consists of cells and cell fragments suspended in a fluid matrix called plasma?","a":"Blood.","options":["Blood","Adipose tissue","Areolar tissue","Reticular tissue"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Blood is a fluid connective tissue. Its cells and cell fragments (red blood cells, white blood cells, and platelets) are suspended in a fluid matrix called plasma. It counts as connective tissue because it follows the same plan of cells within an extracellular matrix, and it connects the body by transporting materials.","notes":["Correct. Blood is cells and fragments in plasma.","Adipose tissue is made mostly of fat-storing adipocytes with very little matrix. It is the answer for energy storage, insulation, and cushioning.","Areolar tissue is loose connective tissue with a gel-like ground substance and a mix of collagen, elastic, and reticular fibers. It is the answer for the soft packing beneath epithelium and around organs.","Reticular tissue is a network of fine reticular fibers that forms the framework of soft organs such as lymph nodes and the spleen. Its matrix is not fluid."],"lt":"t-tissues","lid":"c13","comp":18,"competencyId":"w1-connective-id"},{"id":"L00259","dok":2,"q":"Muscle, bone, cartilage, blood, and most connective tissue all develop from which germ layer?","a":"Mesoderm.","options":["Endoderm","Mesoderm","Ectoderm","Ectoderm and endoderm together"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The mesoderm is the middle germ layer. It forms muscle, bone, cartilage, blood, and most connective tissue, which together make up much of the body's bulk. Embryonic mesenchyme, the tissue that develops into all types of connective tissue, comes mainly from this layer.","notes":["The endoderm forms the epithelial lining of the digestive and respiratory tracts. Pick it for a question about the inner lining of the gut or airways.","Correct. The mesoderm forms the supporting and moving tissues.","The ectoderm forms the epidermis and all nervous tissue. It would be the answer for the brain, spinal cord, and outer skin.","These two layers form coverings, linings, and nervous tissue, not the supporting and moving tissues listed here. Students choose this when they forget the middle layer."],"lt":"t-tissues","lid":"c16","comp":15,"competencyId":"w1-germ-layers"},{"id":"L00265","dok":2,"q":"The extracellular matrix of a connective tissue is made of which two components together?","a":"Fibers and ground substance.","options":["Cells and fibers","Cells and ground substance","Fibers and ground substance","Fibers and a basement membrane"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The extracellular matrix is the nonliving material between connective tissue cells, made of protein fibers plus ground substance. The fibers give strength and stretch, and the ground substance fills the spaces and holds tissue fluid. In most connective tissues the matrix takes up far more of the volume than the cells do.","notes":["Cells are a separate ingredient of connective tissue; they live in the matrix but are not part of it. Fibers are half right, but ground substance completes the matrix.","Ground substance is half right, but the cells are not part of the matrix. The second matrix component is the protein fibers.","Correct. The matrix is fibers plus ground substance.","The basement membrane is the anchoring sheet beneath an epithelium. Fibers are half right, but the other matrix component is ground substance."],"lt":"t-tissues","lid":"c22","comp":18,"competencyId":"w1-connective-id"},{"id":"L00266","dok":2,"q":"Dense regular connective tissue is built of parallel collagen and forms which structures?","a":"Tendons and ligaments.","options":["The walls of large arteries","Tendons and ligaments","Lymph nodes and the spleen","The dermis and organ capsules"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Dense regular connective tissue is packed with collagen fibers that all run parallel, with rows of fibroblasts squeezed between them. This arrangement makes it extremely strong when pulled along the line of the fibers, which is exactly what tendons and ligaments need. Tendons connect muscle to bone, and ligaments connect bone to bone.","notes":["The walls of large arteries contain elastic connective tissue, rich in elastic fibers that let the wall stretch and recoil with each heartbeat.","Correct. Parallel collagen forms tendons and ligaments.","Lymph nodes and the spleen are supported by reticular connective tissue, a fine branching network of reticular fibers.","The dermis and organ capsules are made of dense irregular connective tissue, whose collagen runs in many directions to resist forces from all sides."],"lt":"t-tissues","lid":"c23","comp":18,"competencyId":"w1-connective-id"},{"id":"L00267","dok":2,"q":"Which connective tissue fiber type forms fine branching networks that support soft organs such as lymph nodes and the spleen?","a":"Reticular fibers.","options":["Muscle fibers","Elastic fibers","Collagen fibers","Reticular fibers"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Reticular fibers are thin collagen fibers coated with glycoprotein that branch to form a fine, delicate mesh. This mesh forms the internal framework, or stroma, of soft organs such as lymph nodes, the spleen, and bone marrow, where it holds cells in place. Reticular fibers also help form the basement membrane.","notes":["Muscle fibers are muscle cells that contract, not fibers in a connective tissue matrix. Students pick this because the word fiber is shared.","Elastic fibers stretch and recoil because they contain elastin. They are the answer for skin, lungs, and artery walls.","Collagen fibers are thick, strong, ropelike fibers that resist pulling. They are the answer for tendons, ligaments, and most connective tissues, not a fine branching mesh.","Correct. Reticular fibers form the supporting mesh of soft organs."],"lt":"t-tissues","lid":"c24","comp":18,"competencyId":"w1-connective-id"},{"id":"L00271","dok":2,"q":"What is the material that fills the space between connective tissue cells and fibers and holds tissue fluid?","a":"Ground substance.","options":["The basement membrane","Plasma","Ground substance","Collagen"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Ground substance is the material that fills the space between connective tissue cells and fibers. It can be fluid, gel-like, or calcified, and it is made of water and organic molecules such as chondroitin sulfate. It holds tissue fluid, supports and binds cells, and lets nutrients and wastes move between the blood and the cells.","notes":["The basement membrane is the anchoring sheet beneath an epithelium. It lies between epithelium and connective tissue rather than filling spaces within connective tissue.","Plasma is the fluid matrix of blood specifically. It would be the answer for a question about what blood cells are suspended in.","Correct. Ground substance fills the space between cells and fibers.","Collagen is a protein fiber in the matrix, not the material between the fibers. It is the answer for the strong, ropelike component of connective tissue."],"lt":"t-tissues","lid":"c28","comp":18,"competencyId":"w1-connective-id"},{"id":"L00275","dok":2,"q":"A slide shows cells in lacunae surrounded by a firm matrix, and the tissue has no blood vessels. Which tissue is it?","a":"Cartilage.","options":["Compact bone","Cartilage","Dense regular connective tissue","Areolar tissue"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Cells sitting in small spaces called lacunae, surrounded by a firm matrix with no blood vessels running through it, identify cartilage. Its cells are chondrocytes, and because cartilage is avascular, nutrients must diffuse through the matrix to reach them. This lack of blood vessels is why cartilage heals so slowly.","notes":["Compact bone also has cells (osteocytes) in lacunae, but its matrix is calcified and arranged in osteons around central canals that carry blood vessels. Pick it when the slide shows concentric rings around canals.","Correct. Cells in lacunae with no blood vessels is cartilage.","Dense regular connective tissue has parallel collagen fibers with rows of fibroblasts, not cells in lacunae. It is the answer for a tendon or ligament slide.","Areolar tissue is loose, with a mix of fibers and scattered cells in a soft, gel-like matrix. It is the answer for the packing tissue under epithelium, and it has blood vessels."],"lt":"t-tissues","lid":"c32","comp":18,"competencyId":"w1-connective-id"},{"id":"L00277","dok":2,"q":"A connective tissue shows densely packed collagen fibers all running parallel in one direction. Which subtype is it?","a":"Dense regular connective tissue.","options":["Dense irregular connective tissue","Dense regular connective tissue","Reticular connective tissue","Areolar connective tissue"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Collagen fibers packed tightly and all running in the same direction identify dense regular connective tissue. Rows of flattened fibroblast nuclei lie between the fiber bundles. This arrangement resists pulling along one line, which is why it forms tendons and ligaments.","notes":["Dense irregular connective tissue also has densely packed collagen, but its bundles run in many directions. It is the answer for the dermis and organ capsules.","Correct. Parallel collagen is dense regular connective tissue.","Reticular connective tissue is a fine branching network of reticular fibers. It is the answer for the framework of lymph nodes and the spleen.","Areolar connective tissue is loose, with scattered fibers of several types and lots of open space. It is the answer for the soft packing beneath epithelium."],"lt":"t-tissues","lid":"c34","comp":18,"competencyId":"w1-connective-id"},{"id":"L00279","dok":2,"q":"A cartilage slide from an intervertebral disc shows a tough matrix dominated by thick collagen bundles. Which cartilage type is it?","a":"Fibrocartilage.","options":["Fibrocartilage","Hyaline cartilage","Dense regular connective tissue","Elastic cartilage"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"A cartilage matrix dominated by thick bundles of collagen identifies fibrocartilage. Those collagen bundles let it resist compression and absorb shock, which is why it is found in intervertebral discs, the pubic symphysis, and the menisci of the knee. Its chondrocytes often sit in rows between the collagen bundles.","notes":["Correct. Thick collagen bundles in cartilage mean fibrocartilage.","Hyaline cartilage has fine collagen fibers that are not visible with ordinary staining, giving a smooth, glassy matrix. It is the answer for the ends of long bones, the costal cartilages, and the trachea.","Dense regular connective tissue also has thick parallel collagen, but its cells are fibroblasts between fibers, not chondrocytes in lacunae. It is the answer for tendons and ligaments.","Elastic cartilage is packed with elastic fibers that let it bend and spring back. It is the answer for the external ear and the epiglottis."],"lt":"t-tissues","lid":"c36","comp":26,"competencyId":"w2-cartilage-types"},{"id":"L00280","dok":2,"q":"A slide shows an open lattice of thin bony struts with marrow in the spaces. Which tissue is it?","a":"Spongy bone.","options":["Hyaline cartilage","Spongy bone","Compact bone","Dense regular connective tissue"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Spongy bone is an open lattice of thin bony struts called trabeculae, with marrow filling the spaces between them. The lattice makes bone lighter while still supporting stress along the lines of the struts. It fills the ends of long bones and the inside of flat bones, beneath a shell of compact bone.","notes":["Hyaline cartilage has chondrocytes in lacunae within a smooth, glassy matrix and no marrow. It is the answer for the ends of long bones at the joint surface.","Correct. An open lattice with marrow is spongy bone.","Compact bone is the dense outer layer of bone, built from tightly packed osteons with no large open spaces. Pick it for a slide showing concentric rings around central canals.","Dense regular connective tissue is parallel collagen fibers with fibroblasts, forming tendons and ligaments. It is not mineralized and has no marrow spaces."],"lt":"t-tissues","lid":"c37","comp":30,"competencyId":"w2-compact-spongy"},{"id":"L00282","dok":2,"q":"A connective tissue slide shows large, empty-looking cells that store fat. Which subtype is it?","a":"Adipose tissue.","options":["Reticular tissue","Adipose tissue","Dense irregular tissue","Areolar tissue"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Adipose tissue is made of adipocytes, cells almost completely filled by a large fat droplet that pushes the nucleus to the edge. On a slide the fat is dissolved during preparation, so the cells look empty, like a net of rings. Adipose tissue stores energy, insulates the body, and cushions organs such as the kidneys and heart.","notes":["Reticular tissue is a fine branching mesh of reticular fibers. It is the answer for the framework of lymph nodes and the spleen.","Correct. Fat-filled, empty-looking cells are adipose tissue.","Dense irregular tissue is thick collagen bundles running in many directions with few cells. It is the answer for the dermis and organ capsules.","Areolar tissue is loose connective tissue with scattered fibers and a variety of cells in a soft matrix. It is the answer for the packing tissue beneath epithelium."],"lt":"t-tissues","lid":"c39","comp":18,"competencyId":"w1-connective-id"},{"id":"L00283","dok":2,"q":"A slide from the dermis of the skin shows thick collagen bundles running in all directions. Which subtype is it?","a":"Dense irregular connective tissue.","options":["Dense irregular connective tissue","Elastic connective tissue","Dense regular connective tissue","Areolar connective tissue"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Dense irregular connective tissue is packed with thick collagen bundles running in many directions. Because the fibers point every way, the tissue resists pulling from any direction, which suits the dermis of the skin and the capsules around organs and joints. Fibroblasts are scattered among the bundles.","notes":["Correct. Collagen in all directions is dense irregular connective tissue.","Elastic connective tissue is dominated by elastic fibers that stretch and recoil. It is the answer for the walls of large arteries.","Dense regular connective tissue has collagen bundles that all run parallel, giving strength in one direction. It is the answer for tendons and ligaments.","Areolar connective tissue is loose, with thin scattered fibers and a lot of open ground substance. It is the answer for the soft packing beneath epithelium."],"lt":"t-tissues","lid":"c40","comp":18,"competencyId":"w1-connective-id"},{"id":"L00284","dok":2,"q":"A cartilage slide from the external ear shows a matrix packed with elastic fibers. Which tissue is it?","a":"Elastic cartilage.","options":["Hyaline cartilage","Elastic cartilage","Fibrocartilage","Elastic connective tissue"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Elastic cartilage has chondrocytes in lacunae like other cartilage, but its matrix is packed with dark-staining elastic fibers. These fibers let it bend and spring back into shape, which is why it forms the external ear (pinna) and the epiglottis. Hyaline cartilage would be too stiff for these structures.","notes":["Hyaline cartilage has a smooth, glassy matrix with fine collagen fibers that do not show with ordinary staining. It is the answer for the ends of long bones, the nose, and the trachea.","Correct. Cartilage with elastic fibers in the ear is elastic cartilage.","Fibrocartilage is dominated by thick collagen bundles that resist compression. It is the answer for intervertebral discs and the knee menisci.","Elastic connective tissue is also rich in elastic fibers, but it has fibroblasts rather than chondrocytes in lacunae. It is the answer for the walls of large arteries, not the ear."],"lt":"t-tissues","lid":"c41","comp":26,"competencyId":"w2-cartilage-types"},{"id":"L00288","dok":2,"q":"A slide shows a soft, loosely arranged connective tissue with scattered fibers that wraps and cushions organs and holds tissue fluid. Which subtype is it?","a":"Areolar connective tissue.","options":["Fibrocartilage","Dense regular connective tissue","Areolar connective tissue","Adipose connective tissue"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Areolar connective tissue is loose connective tissue with a mix of collagen, elastic, and reticular fibers scattered through a soft, gel-like ground substance. That open structure holds tissue fluid and gives support with some stretch. It lies under epithelia and wraps around organs and blood vessels, cushioning them and holding them in place.","notes":["Fibrocartilage is a tough cartilage with chondrocytes in lacunae among thick collagen bundles. It is the answer for intervertebral discs and the knee menisci.","Dense regular connective tissue has tightly packed parallel collagen fibers. It is the answer for tendons and ligaments.","Correct. Soft, loose packing tissue is areolar connective tissue.","Adipose tissue is dominated by large fat-filled adipocytes and stores energy and insulates. It is the answer for a slide of empty-looking cells packed together."],"lt":"t-tissues","lid":"c45","comp":18,"competencyId":"w1-connective-id"},{"id":"L00295","dok":2,"q":"A tissue has cells in lacunae, a firm matrix with fine collagen, no blood vessels, and covers the ends of bones at a joint. Which tissue is it?","a":"Hyaline cartilage.","options":["Fibrocartilage","Hyaline cartilage","Dense regular connective tissue","Compact bone"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Chondrocytes in lacunae and an avascular matrix mark the tissue as cartilage. A firm matrix with fine collagen fibers points to hyaline cartilage, and its location covering the ends of bones, as articular cartilage, confirms it. Hyaline cartilage gives a smooth surface for joint movement.","notes":["Fibrocartilage is also avascular with cells in lacunae, but its matrix is dominated by thick collagen bundles. It is the answer for intervertebral discs and the knee menisci, not the ends of long bones.","Correct. All four features point to hyaline cartilage.","Dense regular connective tissue has fibroblasts between parallel collagen fibers rather than cells in lacunae. It is the answer for tendons and ligaments.","Compact bone also has cells in lacunae, but it is well supplied with blood vessels running through the central canals of its osteons. An avascular tissue cannot be bone."],"lt":"t-tissues","lid":"c52","comp":26,"competencyId":"w2-cartilage-types"},{"id":"L00317","dok":3,"q":"A torn knee meniscus barely repairs itself. Which structural feature of cartilage explains this slow healing?","a":"Cartilage is avascular, with no blood vessels.","options":["Cartilage has a rich blood supply","Cartilage is avascular, with no blood vessels","Cartilage contains only elastic fibers","Cartilage is made of many cell layers"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Cartilage, including the fibrocartilage of the knee meniscus, has no blood vessels of its own. Repair depends on blood bringing oxygen, nutrients, and repair cells, so in cartilage these must slowly diffuse in through the matrix instead. That is why a torn meniscus heals poorly, while a skin wound closes in days.","notes":["Cartilage has no blood supply; a rich blood supply describes bone and most other connective tissue. A rich blood supply would speed healing, not slow it.","Correct. Without blood vessels, cartilage heals slowly.","Only elastic cartilage is dominated by elastic fibers, and the meniscus is fibrocartilage with thick collagen bundles. Fiber type is not what slows healing; the lack of blood vessels is.","Many cell layers describe a stratified epithelium, not cartilage. Cartilage is made of scattered chondrocytes in a firm matrix."],"lt":"t-tissues","lid":"c26-x2","comp":18,"competencyId":"w1-connective-id"},{"id":"L00319","dok":3,"q":"A tendon tears when it is twisted sideways rather than pulled along its length. Which structural property of dense regular connective tissue explains this?","a":"Its collagen runs parallel, so it is strong in only one direction.","options":["It is avascular like cartilage","Its collagen runs in all directions","It contains mostly elastic fibers","Its collagen runs parallel in one direction"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"In dense regular connective tissue, the collagen bundles all run parallel along the length of the tendon. That makes the tendon very strong when pulled in that one direction but much weaker against forces from other angles, such as twisting. Dense irregular tissue, with fibers in every direction, trades some of that one-way strength for resistance from all sides.","notes":["Tendons do have a poor blood supply, which slows their healing, but that does not explain why twisting tears them. Their weakness to twisting comes from the parallel fiber direction.","Collagen running in all directions describes dense irregular connective tissue, such as the dermis. That arrangement would resist twisting better, not worse.","Mostly elastic fibers describes elastic connective tissue in artery walls. Tendons are dominated by collagen, not elastic fibers.","Correct. Parallel collagen gives strength in one direction only."],"lt":"t-tissues","lid":"c28-x2","comp":18,"competencyId":"w1-connective-id"},{"id":"L00324","dok":3,"q":"A large artery wall must stretch with each heartbeat and then spring back. Which connective tissue fiber should dominate there, and why?","a":"Elastic fibers, because they stretch and then recoil to their original shape.","options":["Reticular fibers, because they form a soft supporting mesh","Collagen fibers, because they resist stretch with great strength","Keratin fibers, because they make a tissue tough and waterproof","Elastic fibers, because they stretch and then recoil to shape"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Elastic fibers are made of elastin and can stretch to about 150 percent of their resting length and then return to their original shape. A large artery wall is stretched by every surge of blood from the heart, and elastic fibers let it expand and then recoil. This is why the walls of large arteries contain elastic connective tissue.","notes":["Reticular fibers form a fine supporting mesh in soft organs such as lymph nodes and the spleen. They hold cells in place but do not provide stretch and recoil.","Collagen fibers resist stretching and give tensile strength, but they do not spring back after being stretched. They are the answer for tendons and ligaments.","Keratin is a protein inside epidermal cells, not a connective tissue fiber. It is the answer for what makes the skin's surface tough and water-resistant.","Correct. Elastic fibers let the artery wall stretch and recoil."],"lt":"t-tissues","lid":"d3-t-tissues-3","comp":18,"competencyId":"w1-connective-id"},{"id":"L00329","dok":2,"q":"Adipose tissue stores fat, cushions organs, and insulates the body. Why is its cell structure, a large lipid droplet pushing the nucleus to the edge, well matched to that job?","a":"One large droplet stores the most fat in each cell, which suits energy storage.","options":["The droplet conducts heat away from the body to cool it","The droplet holds water that cushions the organs","The droplet is empty space that keeps the cell light","One large droplet stores the most fat per cell"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"An adipocyte is almost completely filled by one large lipid droplet, with its nucleus and cytoplasm squeezed into a thin rim at the edge. Packing the cell this way stores the most fat in the least space, which is ideal for a tissue whose main jobs are energy storage, insulation, and cushioning. The fat also conducts heat poorly, so it helps hold in body heat.","notes":["Fat conducts heat poorly, which is why adipose tissue insulates and helps hold heat in. This option reverses that property.","The droplet holds lipid (triglycerides), not water. Adipose does cushion organs, but it does so with fat, which also stores energy.","The droplet looks empty on slides only because the fat dissolves during slide preparation. In the living tissue it is full of lipid.","Correct. One large droplet maximizes fat storage per cell."],"lt":"t-tissues","lid":"d3-t-tissues-8","comp":18,"competencyId":"w1-connective-id"},{"id":"L00330","dok":2,"q":"Connective tissue has cells scattered within a large amount of nonliving material. Why does this abundant matrix suit connective tissue's roles?","a":"The matrix, not the cells, gives the support, cushioning, and connection these tissues provide.","options":["Connective tissue has almost no material between its cells","The matrix gives the support, cushioning, and connection","The matrix lets the cells absorb nutrients from a free surface","The matrix keeps the tissue from supporting anything"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"In connective tissue, most of the work is done by the matrix the cells secrete rather than by the cells themselves. The fibers and ground substance give bone its hardness, cartilage its firm cushioning, and tendons their strength. That is the opposite of epithelium, where tightly packed cells with little matrix form the working surface.","notes":["Having almost no material between cells describes epithelium, where cells are packed tightly. Connective tissue is defined by its large amount of matrix.","Correct. The matrix does most of connective tissue's work.","A free surface that faces open space is a feature of epithelium. Connective tissue lies beneath epithelium and never forms a body surface.","The matrix is what does the supporting, so it cannot prevent support. Students choose this when they think of nonliving material as filler with no function."],"lt":"t-tissues","lid":"d3x-t-tissues-1","comp":18,"competencyId":"w1-connective-id"},{"id":"N-m1-connective-1","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares blast cells (such as fibroblasts) with cyte cells (such as fibrocytes)?","a":"Blasts divide and secrete matrix; cytes are mature and maintain it.","options":["Blasts occur only in bone; cytes occur only in cartilage","Blasts divide and secrete matrix; cytes are mature and maintain it","Blasts are mature and maintain matrix; cytes divide and secrete it","Blasts break down matrix; cytes build new matrix"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"In connective tissue, cell names ending in -blast mark immature, active cells that divide and secrete new extracellular matrix. Names ending in -cyte mark the mature form, which maintains and repairs the matrix already in place. The same pattern applies to fibroblasts and fibrocytes, osteoblasts and osteocytes, and chondroblasts and chondrocytes.","notes":["Both forms occur in several connective tissues. Fibroblasts and fibrocytes are in connective tissue proper, osteoblasts and osteocytes in bone, and chondroblasts and chondrocytes in cartilage.","Correct. Blasts build matrix, and cytes maintain it.","This reverses the two. The -blast form is the builder, and the -cyte form is the mature maintainer.","Breaking down matrix is not the job of either form. In bone, that job belongs to a separate cell type, the osteoclast, which comes up in Module 2."],"comp":18,"competencyId":"w1-connective-id"},{"id":"N-m1-connective-2","dok":1,"q":"Which connective tissue cell, found along blood vessels, secretes histamine that widens blood vessels during inflammation?","a":"The mast cell.","options":["The adipocyte","The mast cell","The fibroblast","The plasma cell"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Mast cells sit along the small blood vessels that supply connective tissue. When tissue is injured or exposed to an allergen, they release histamine, which widens nearby blood vessels and helps start inflammation. They are one of the cells to identify on a slide of loose areolar connective tissue.","notes":["Adipocytes store fat as triglycerides. They are the answer for adipose tissue, not inflammation.","Correct. Mast cells release histamine.","Fibroblasts are the most common connective tissue cells and secrete fibers and ground substance. They build matrix rather than releasing histamine.","Plasma cells develop from B lymphocytes and secrete antibodies. They are the answer for antibody production in the connective tissue of the gut and airways."],"comp":18,"competencyId":"w1-connective-id"},{"id":"N-m1-connective-3","dok":1,"q":"Which connective tissue cell develops from a monocyte and engulfs bacteria and debris by phagocytosis?","a":"The macrophage.","options":["The macrophage","The mast cell","The plasma cell","The fibroblast"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Macrophages are large phagocytic cells that develop from monocytes, a type of white blood cell. They engulf and digest bacteria, dead cells, and debris. Fixed macrophages stay in one tissue, such as the liver, lungs, or spleen, while wandering macrophages move to sites of infection or inflammation.","notes":["Correct. Macrophages come from monocytes and engulf debris.","Mast cells release histamine during inflammation rather than engulfing debris. They are the answer for the cell that widens blood vessels.","Plasma cells come from B lymphocytes, not monocytes, and they secrete antibodies. They fight infection with antibodies rather than by engulfing it.","Fibroblasts build connective tissue fibers and ground substance. They are the answer for the cell that makes matrix."],"comp":18,"competencyId":"w1-connective-id"},{"id":"N-m1-connective-4","dok":1,"q":"Which connective tissue cell develops from a B lymphocyte and secretes antibodies?","a":"The plasma cell.","options":["The fibroblast","The mast cell","The macrophage","The plasma cell"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Plasma cells develop from B lymphocytes and secrete antibodies, proteins that tag specific invaders for destruction. They are common in the connective tissue beneath the linings of the digestive and respiratory tracts and in lymphatic tissue, where invaders are most likely to enter. Their name refers to the cell type, not to blood plasma.","notes":["Fibroblasts secrete fibers and ground substance, not antibodies. They are the answer for the cell that builds the matrix.","Mast cells release histamine to widen blood vessels during inflammation. They are the answer for the cell involved in allergic responses.","Macrophages come from monocytes and engulf bacteria and debris. They fight infection by phagocytosis, not by secreting antibodies.","Correct. Plasma cells secrete antibodies."],"comp":18,"competencyId":"w1-connective-id"},{"id":"N-m1-connective-5","dok":1,"q":"What is the fluid extracellular matrix of blood called?","a":"Plasma.","options":["Plasma","Ground substance","Lymph","Synovial fluid"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Blood is a connective tissue whose matrix is a fluid called plasma. The red blood cells, white blood cells, and platelets are suspended in it, and it carries dissolved nutrients, gases, hormones, and wastes. A fluid matrix is what lets blood flow through the vessels.","notes":["Correct. The matrix of blood is plasma.","Ground substance is the general term for the material between cells and fibers in connective tissue, often a gel. Blood's fluid matrix has its own name, plasma.","Lymph is the fluid carried in lymphatic vessels after it drains from the tissues. It is not the matrix of blood.","Synovial fluid is secreted by the synovial membrane and lubricates joint cavities. It is not part of blood."],"comp":18,"competencyId":"w1-connective-id"},{"id":"N-m1-connective-6","dok":1,"q":"Which embryonic connective tissue, with widely scattered fibroblasts in a jelly-like matrix, supports the umbilical cord?","a":"Mucous connective tissue.","options":["Areolar connective tissue","Hyaline cartilage","Embryonic mesenchyme","Mucous connective tissue"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Mucous connective tissue has widely scattered fibroblasts in a jelly-like matrix, and it is found in the umbilical cord, where it supports the cord's blood vessels. It is one of the two embryonic connective tissues. The other, mesenchyme, is the tissue that gives rise to all connective tissues.","notes":["Areolar connective tissue is adult loose connective tissue found beneath epithelium and around organs. It is not the jelly-like tissue of the umbilical cord.","Hyaline cartilage has chondrocytes in lacunae within a firm, glassy matrix. It is the answer for the ends of long bones, not the umbilical cord.","Embryonic mesenchyme has irregular cells in a semifluid matrix and is found in the embryo under the skin and along developing bones. It differentiates into all types of connective tissue.","Correct. Mucous connective tissue supports the umbilical cord."],"comp":18,"competencyId":"w1-connective-id"},{"id":"N-m1-connective-7","dok":2,"q":"Most connective tissue is highly vascular. Which two connective tissues are the main exceptions, with little or no blood supply?","a":"Cartilage and tendon.","options":["Dense irregular and reticular tissue","Bone and blood","Cartilage and tendon","Areolar and adipose tissue"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Most connective tissues have a rich blood supply, but cartilage has no blood vessels and tendons have very few. Without a good blood supply, oxygen, nutrients, and repair cells reach these tissues slowly. That is why injuries such as a torn meniscus or a ruptured calcaneal (Achilles) tendon heal slowly.","notes":["Dense irregular and reticular connective tissue both have a good blood supply. The dermis, made of dense irregular tissue, bleeds readily when cut.","Bone is well supplied with blood vessels that run through the central canals of its osteons, and blood is itself the fluid inside vessels. Neither lacks a blood supply.","Correct. Cartilage and tendon are the exceptions.","Areolar and adipose tissue are both well supplied with blood vessels, and blood vessels are one of the structures to find on an adipose slide."],"comp":18,"competencyId":"w1-connective-id"},{"id":"N-m1-connective-8","dok":2,"q":"Which type of cartilage forms the costal cartilages that connect the ribs to the sternum?","a":"Hyaline cartilage.","options":["Elastic cartilage","Dense regular connective tissue","Hyaline cartilage","Fibrocartilage"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The costal cartilages are made of hyaline cartilage, the most common type, with fine collagen fibers in a glassy matrix. Hyaline cartilage gives firm but flexible support, which lets the rib cage move slightly during breathing. Hyaline cartilage also covers the ends of long bones and forms the nose, trachea, and larynx.","notes":["Elastic cartilage is packed with elastic fibers and is very bendable. It is the answer for the external ear and the epiglottis.","Dense regular connective tissue forms tendons and ligaments, which are made of parallel collagen and fibroblasts. It does not have chondrocytes in lacunae and is not cartilage.","Correct. Costal cartilage is hyaline cartilage.","Fibrocartilage has thick collagen bundles that resist compression. It is the answer for intervertebral discs, the pubic symphysis, and the knee menisci."],"comp":26,"competencyId":"w2-cartilage-types"},{"id":"N-m1-connective-9","dok":1,"q":"Which embryonic tissue differentiates into all types of connective tissue?","a":"Embryonic mesenchyme.","options":["Mucous connective tissue","Embryonic mesenchyme","Ectoderm","Endoderm"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Embryonic mesenchyme is a connective tissue of the embryo with irregular cells in a semifluid matrix and delicate reticular fibers. It is found under the developing skin and along developing bones, and it gives rise to every type of connective tissue, from blood to bone. Most mesenchyme comes from the mesoderm.","notes":["Mucous connective tissue is the jelly-like embryonic tissue of the umbilical cord. It supports the cord but does not give rise to the other connective tissues.","Correct. Mesenchyme gives rise to all connective tissues.","The ectoderm is the outer germ layer and forms the epidermis and nervous tissue. It is not the source of connective tissue in general.","The endoderm is the inner germ layer and forms the epithelial lining of the digestive and respiratory tracts. It does not form connective tissue."],"comp":18,"competencyId":"w1-connective-id"},{"id":"N-m1-connective-500","dok":1,"q":"Elastic fibers in connective tissue are built mainly from which protein?","a":"Elastin.","options":["Keratin","Collagen","Elastin","Actin"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Elastic fibers are made mostly of the protein elastin, supported by a glycoprotein called fibrillin. Elastin lets the fiber stretch to about 150 percent of its relaxed length and then spring back. That is why elastic fibers are plentiful in the skin, lungs, and blood vessel walls, which all stretch and recoil.","notes":["Keratin is the tough protein that fills the surface cells of the epidermis, hair, and nails. It belongs to epithelial cells, not to connective tissue fibers.","Collagen is the protein of collagen fibers and, coated with glycoproteins, of reticular fibers. It resists pulling but does not stretch and recoil, so pick it for the strong fibers of tendons.","Correct. Elastin is the stretchy protein of elastic fibers.","Actin is a protein of the cytoskeleton and of the thin filaments in muscle cells. It is found inside cells, not in the fibers of the extracellular matrix."],"comp":18,"competencyId":"w1-connective-id"},{"id":"N-m1-connective-501","dok":1,"q":"Which fibers are found in loose areolar connective tissue?","a":"All three types, collagen, elastic, and reticular, loosely arranged.","options":["Only reticular fibers, forming a fine mesh","Collagen, elastic, and reticular, loosely mixed","Only elastic fibers, packed in wavy sheets","Only collagen fibers, packed in parallel bundles"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Areolar connective tissue is the general packing tissue of the body. It has a loose arrangement of all three fiber types with cells scattered among them and plenty of ground substance, which gives it strength, elasticity, and support. It sits under epithelia and around organs and blood vessels.","notes":["A mesh of reticular fibers alone is the signature of reticular connective tissue. Pick it for the framework of lymph nodes and the spleen.","Correct. Areolar tissue has all three fiber types in a loose arrangement.","A tissue dominated by elastic fibers is elastic connective tissue. Pick it for the wall of a large artery such as the aorta.","Parallel, tightly packed collagen bundles describe dense regular connective tissue. Pick it for tendons and ligaments."],"comp":18,"competencyId":"w1-connective-id"},{"id":"N-m1-connective-502","dok":1,"q":"Besides the subcutaneous tissue under the skin, where is adipose tissue commonly found?","a":"Around the kidneys and the heart.","options":["In the tendons of the limbs","In the walls of the trachea","Around the kidneys and the heart","At the ends of long bones"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Adipose tissue is connective tissue made of fat-storing cells called adipocytes. It stores energy, insulates the body, and cushions organs. Besides the subcutaneous layer, it collects around organs such as the kidneys and the heart, where it pads and protects them.","notes":["Tendons are dense regular connective tissue with parallel collagen bundles. Pick dense regular connective tissue if the question asked about tendons.","The trachea is held open by rings of hyaline cartilage and lined by pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium. Adipose is not what builds its wall.","Correct. Adipose tissue pads organs such as the kidneys and the heart.","The ends of long bones are covered by hyaline cartilage, which gives a smooth joint surface. Pick hyaline cartilage for that location."],"comp":18,"competencyId":"w1-connective-id"},{"id":"N-m1-connective-503","dok":1,"q":"On a cartilage slide, what is the perichondrium?","a":"The layer of dense connective tissue that surrounds the cartilage.","options":["The small space in the matrix that holds a chondrocyte","A dense connective tissue layer around the cartilage","The gel-like ground substance between the cartilage cells","The membrane that covers the outer surface of bone"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Peri means around and chondr means cartilage, so the perichondrium is the layer around cartilage. It is a band of dense connective tissue at the edge of hyaline and elastic cartilage, and because it has blood vessels, the avascular cartilage inside relies on it for nutrients. Fibrocartilage is the cartilage type listed in lab as having no perichondrium.","notes":["The small space that holds a chondrocyte is a lacuna. Pick lacuna if the question asked where cartilage cells sit.","Correct. The perichondrium is the dense connective tissue layer around cartilage.","The gel-like material between cells is part of the extracellular matrix, the ground substance. It fills the cartilage rather than wrapping its outside.","The membrane covering bone is the periosteum. The name is similar, but oste means bone, so pick it for a bone slide."],"comp":26,"competencyId":"w2-cartilage-types"},{"id":"N-m1-connective-504","dok":1,"q":"Besides the external ear, which structure is made of elastic cartilage?","a":"The epiglottis.","options":["The epiglottis","The costal cartilages","The pubic symphysis","The intervertebral discs"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Elastic cartilage looks like hyaline cartilage but has many elastic fibers in its matrix. That lets it bend and then return to its shape. It is found in the external ear (the pinna) and in the epiglottis, the flap that tips over the airway when you swallow.","notes":["Correct. The epiglottis is elastic cartilage, like the external ear.","The costal cartilages that join the ribs to the sternum are hyaline cartilage. Pick hyaline for them.","The pubic symphysis is a pad of fibrocartilage, built to resist compression. Pick fibrocartilage for this joint.","The intervertebral discs are made with fibrocartilage, which absorbs shock between vertebrae. Pick fibrocartilage for a disc."],"comp":26,"competencyId":"w2-cartilage-types"},{"id":"N-m1-connective-505","dok":1,"q":"The pubic symphysis, where the two hip bones meet at the front, contains which type of cartilage?","a":"Fibrocartilage.","options":["Dense regular connective tissue","Elastic cartilage","Fibrocartilage","Hyaline cartilage"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Fibrocartilage has thick bundles of collagen fibers running through its matrix, which makes it the toughest cartilage. It resists compression and absorbs shock, so it is found where bones are pushed together under load, such as the pubic symphysis, the intervertebral discs, and the menisci of the knee.","notes":["Dense regular connective tissue is not cartilage at all; it has fibroblasts instead of chondrocytes in lacunae. Pick it for tendons and ligaments.","Elastic cartilage bends and springs back. Pick it for the external ear or the epiglottis.","Correct. The pubic symphysis contains a pad of fibrocartilage.","Hyaline cartilage has a smooth, glassy matrix with fine collagen fibers. Pick it for the ends of long bones, the costal cartilages, or the trachea."],"comp":26,"competencyId":"w2-cartilage-types"},{"id":"N-m1-connective-506","dok":1,"q":"The C-shaped rings that hold the trachea open are made of which type of cartilage?","a":"Hyaline cartilage.","options":["Elastic cartilage","Fibrocartilage","Dense irregular connective tissue","Hyaline cartilage"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Hyaline cartilage is the most common cartilage. It is firm but flexible, with fine collagen fibers that do not show with ordinary staining, which gives the matrix its glassy look. That combination keeps the trachea open while still letting it bend, and the same tissue is found in the larynx, nose, costal cartilages, and the ends of long bones.","notes":["Elastic cartilage is more flexible and springs back after bending. Pick it for the epiglottis or the external ear.","Fibrocartilage resists heavy compression. Pick it for the intervertebral discs, the pubic symphysis, or the knee menisci.","Dense irregular connective tissue has collagen running in many directions but is not cartilage. Pick it for the dermis of the skin.","Correct. The tracheal rings are hyaline cartilage."],"comp":26,"competencyId":"w2-cartilage-types"},{"id":"N-m1-connective-507","dok":1,"q":"Where is connective tissue found in relation to epithelium?","a":"Below the epithelium, never on a free body surface.","options":["Between the epithelial cells, holding them together","Only inside bones, separate from any epithelium","Below the epithelium, never on a free body surface","On the free surface, above the epithelium"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Epithelium covers surfaces and lines cavities, while connective tissue supports and connects other tissues from beneath. Connective tissue is found below epithelial tissue and never forms a free surface. Because epithelium has no blood vessels, it depends on the blood supply of the connective tissue underneath for its nutrients.","notes":["Epithelial cells are held to each other by cell junctions such as tight junctions and desmosomes, not by connective tissue between them.","Connective tissue is found throughout the body, not only in bone. It includes areolar tissue, adipose, cartilage, blood, and more.","Correct. Connective tissue lies beneath epithelium and does not form free surfaces.","Free surfaces are covered by epithelium, not connective tissue. This choice reverses the two tissues."],"comp":18,"competencyId":"w1-connective-id"},{"id":"N-m1-connective-508","dok":2,"q":"A slide from a lymph node shows a fine, dark-staining network of branching fibers forming a mesh, with many cells sitting in its spaces. Which tissue is it?","a":"Reticular connective tissue.","options":["Reticular connective tissue","Areolar connective tissue","Dense irregular connective tissue","Adipose connective tissue"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Reticular connective tissue is built around a fine, branching mesh of reticular fibers, which take up dark stains. This mesh forms the stroma, the internal framework, of soft organs such as lymph nodes and the spleen, and blood cells sit in its open spaces. The location in a lymph node plus the mesh pattern is the giveaway.","notes":["Correct. A dark-staining branching mesh in a lymph node is reticular connective tissue.","Areolar connective tissue has a loose mix of thick collagen and thin elastic fibers with scattered cells. Pick it for tissue found just under an epithelium or around vessels.","Dense irregular connective tissue has thick collagen bundles running in many directions. Pick it for the dermis of the skin.","Adipose tissue is made of large, empty-looking fat cells with little matrix. Pick it for the subcutaneous tissue or the fat around the kidneys."],"comp":18,"competencyId":"w1-connective-id"},{"id":"N-m1-connective-509","dok":2,"q":"A slide from the wall of the aorta shows a connective tissue dominated by wavy, dark-staining elastin fibers, with no lacunae. Which tissue is it?","a":"Elastic connective tissue.","options":["Dense regular connective tissue","Reticular connective tissue","Elastic connective tissue","Elastic cartilage"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Elastic connective tissue is a dense connective tissue in which elastin fibers dominate. It is found where a wall must stretch and recoil, such as the large arteries that expand with every heartbeat. The absence of lacunae tells you it is not cartilage, even though elastic cartilage also has many elastic fibers.","notes":["Dense regular connective tissue is dominated by parallel collagen bundles with fibroblasts between them. Pick it for a tendon or ligament.","Reticular connective tissue has a fine mesh of reticular fibers holding cells. Pick it for a lymph node or the spleen.","Correct. Dominant elastin fibers without lacunae, from an artery wall, mark elastic connective tissue.","Elastic cartilage has elastic fibers too, but its cells sit in lacunae. Pick it for a slide of the external ear or the epiglottis."],"comp":18,"competencyId":"w1-connective-id"},{"id":"N-m1-connective-510","dok":2,"q":"On a loose areolar slide you see thick, pink, ribbon-like fibers and thin, dark, branching threads. What are the thick pink fibers?","a":"Collagen fibers.","options":["Reticular fibers","Elastic fibers","Smooth muscle fibers","Collagen fibers"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Collagen fibers are the thickest and strongest connective tissue fibers, and on a typical areolar slide they appear as broad, pink, wavy ribbons. The thin, dark, branching threads are elastic fibers. Learning to tell these two apart is one of the lab structures for the areolar slide.","notes":["Reticular fibers are very fine and form a mesh. They are the defining fiber of reticular connective tissue in lymph nodes and the spleen.","Elastic fibers are the thin, dark, branching threads on the same slide. Pick them if the question asked about the thin dark fibers.","Smooth muscle fibers are whole spindle-shaped cells with a central nucleus, not extracellular fibers. Pick them for a slide of the wall of the intestine.","Correct. The thick pink ribbons are collagen fibers."],"comp":18,"competencyId":"w1-connective-id"},{"id":"N-m1-connective-511","dok":2,"q":"On an areolar slide, a few large cells sit along small blood vessels, and each is packed with dark-staining granules. Which cells are they?","a":"Mast cells.","options":["Adipocytes","Fibroblasts","Chondrocytes","Mast cells"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Mast cells sit along the blood vessels that supply connective tissue. Their granules hold histamine, which widens blood vessels during inflammation, and the packed granules are why the cells stain so darkly. Mast cells are one of the structures you identify on the areolar slide.","notes":["Adipocytes are fat cells that look empty because a single large fat droplet fills them. Pick them for an adipose slide.","Fibroblasts are the common cells that make fibers and ground substance. They look like slender cells with oval nuclei, without dark granules.","Chondrocytes are cartilage cells sitting in lacunae within a firm matrix. Pick them for a cartilage slide.","Correct. Large, granule-packed cells along blood vessels are mast cells."],"comp":18,"competencyId":"w1-connective-id"},{"id":"N-m1-connective-512","dok":2,"q":"On a dense regular connective tissue slide, which cells have the flattened nuclei squeezed in rows between the parallel collagen bundles?","a":"Fibroblasts.","options":["Mast cells","Fibroblasts","Osteocytes","Macrophages"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Fibroblasts make the collagen fibers and ground substance of connective tissue proper. In dense regular connective tissue the collagen is packed in parallel bundles, so the fibroblasts get pressed into thin rows between them, and you see their nuclei as flattened dark streaks running in the same direction as the fibers.","notes":["Mast cells are larger cells packed with dark granules, found along blood vessels. Pick them on an areolar slide.","Correct. The flattened nuclei in rows between collagen bundles belong to fibroblasts.","Osteocytes are mature bone cells sitting in lacunae within a hard, calcified matrix. Pick them on a compact bone slide.","Macrophages are phagocytic cells that develop from monocytes and engulf debris. They are not the cells that line up between tendon fibers."],"comp":18,"competencyId":"w1-connective-id"},{"id":"N-m1-connective-513","dok":2,"q":"A slide shows concentric rings of hard matrix around a central canal, with small cells in lacunae linked by tiny channels. Which tissue is it?","a":"Compact bone.","options":["Dense regular connective tissue","Compact bone","Hyaline cartilage","Spongy bone"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The concentric rings are lamellae arranged around a central (Haversian) canal, and together they form an osteon, the repeating unit of compact bone. The cells in lacunae are osteocytes, and the tiny channels connecting them are canaliculi. This ring pattern is what sets compact bone apart from every other connective tissue on a slide.","notes":["Dense regular connective tissue has parallel collagen bundles and fibroblasts, with no lacunae or rings. Pick it for a tendon.","Correct. Concentric lamellae around a central canal form the osteons of compact bone.","Hyaline cartilage also has cells in lacunae, but its matrix is glassy and smooth with no rings or canals. Pick it for the trachea or the ends of long bones.","Spongy bone is an open lattice of thin bony struts with marrow between them and no osteons. Pick it for the interior of a bone end."],"comp":30,"competencyId":"w2-compact-spongy"},{"id":"N-m1-connective-514","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares bone with cartilage?","a":"Bone has a hard, calcified matrix and a blood supply, while cartilage has a firm, flexible matrix and no blood vessels.","options":["Bone is calcified and vascular, while cartilage is flexible and avascular","Bone is avascular, while cartilage has a rich supply of blood vessels","Bone has no cells, while cartilage has chondrocytes in lacunae","Both have cells in lacunae, and both are flexible tissues"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Bone and cartilage are both supporting connective tissues with cells in lacunae, but their matrix and blood supply differ. Bone matrix is calcified, which makes it hard and inflexible, and bone is well supplied with blood vessels. Cartilage matrix is firm but pliable, and cartilage has no blood vessels, so it heals slowly.","notes":["Correct. Bone is hard and vascular, while cartilage is flexible and avascular.","This reverses the two tissues. Cartilage is the avascular one, which is why a torn meniscus heals slowly, while bone has a good blood supply.","Bone does have cells. Osteocytes live in lacunae in bone just as chondrocytes live in lacunae in cartilage.","The first half is true, since both have cells in lacunae, but bone is hard and inflexible because its matrix is calcified. Only cartilage is flexible."],"comp":18,"competencyId":"w1-connective-id"},{"id":"N-m1-connective-515","dok":2,"q":"Slide one shows tightly packed cells with almost no material between them and a free surface. Slide two shows scattered cells in a large amount of matrix. Which statement identifies them?","a":"Slide one is epithelium and slide two is connective tissue.","options":["Slide one is connective tissue and slide two is epithelium","Both slides are epithelium at different magnifications","Slide one is epithelium and slide two is connective tissue","Both slides are connective tissue from different organs"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The amount of matrix is the quickest way to separate the two tissue types. Epithelium has tightly packed cells with very little extracellular matrix and a free apical surface. Connective tissue has relatively few cells spread through a large extracellular matrix of fibers and ground substance, and it never forms a free surface.","notes":["This reverses the two. A large matrix with scattered cells is the mark of connective tissue, not epithelium.","Changing the magnification does not create a large matrix between cells. Slide two has a feature epithelium lacks.","Correct. Packed cells with a free surface is epithelium, and scattered cells in matrix is connective tissue.","Connective tissue does not form a free surface with tightly packed cells. Slide one has the features of epithelium."],"comp":18,"competencyId":"w1-connective-id"},{"id":"N-m1-connective-516","dok":2,"q":"In osteoarthritis, the smooth tissue covering the ends of the bones in a joint wears away. Which tissue is being lost?","a":"Hyaline cartilage.","options":["Elastic cartilage","Dense regular connective tissue","Fibrocartilage","Hyaline cartilage"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The ends of long bones in a joint are covered by hyaline cartilage, which gives a smooth, slick surface for joint movement. In osteoarthritis this articular cartilage gradually wears down. Because cartilage has no blood vessels, it repairs itself poorly, so the damage tends to build over time.","notes":["Elastic cartilage is found in the external ear and epiglottis, not in weight-bearing joints.","Dense regular connective tissue forms tendons and ligaments, which attach structures at a joint but do not cover the bone ends.","Fibrocartilage forms shock-absorbing pads such as the menisci and intervertebral discs. Pick it if the question asked about a torn meniscus rather than the bone-end surface.","Correct. The smooth covering of bone ends at a joint is hyaline cartilage."],"comp":26,"competencyId":"w2-cartilage-types"},{"id":"N-m1-connective-517","dok":2,"q":"Blood is a liquid, yet it is classified as a connective tissue. What best supports that classification?","a":"It has cells suspended in an extracellular matrix, plasma, and it develops from mesenchyme like other connective tissues.","options":["It covers body surfaces and lines the inside of the blood vessels","Its cells sit in a matrix, plasma, and it arises from mesenchyme","Its cells are joined by intercalated discs and contract together","It is built from neurons and the glial cells that support them"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Every connective tissue is cells plus an extracellular matrix. In blood, the cells and cell fragments are suspended in plasma, which is a fluid matrix, so blood fits that plan even though it flows. Blood also develops from embryonic mesenchyme, the tissue that gives rise to all connective tissues, and it serves as the body's major transport system.","notes":["Covering surfaces and lining vessels is the job of epithelium. The lining of blood vessels is simple squamous epithelium, not blood.","Correct. Cells in a fluid matrix, with an origin in mesenchyme, place blood among the connective tissues.","Intercalated discs and contraction describe cardiac muscle tissue. Pick this for the heart wall, not for blood.","Neurons and glial cells make up nervous tissue. Pick this for the brain, spinal cord, or nerves."],"comp":18,"competencyId":"w1-connective-id"},{"id":"N-m1-connective-518","dok":3,"q":"After an ankle sprain, the area under the skin becomes puffy with excess fluid (edema). Which connective tissue's loose, fluid-holding matrix soaks up most of this fluid?","a":"Areolar connective tissue.","options":["Dense regular connective tissue","Compact bone","Hyaline cartilage","Areolar connective tissue"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Areolar connective tissue has loosely arranged fibers and a large amount of watery ground substance, so it acts as a reservoir for tissue fluid. When an area is injured or inflamed, extra fluid leaks into this loose tissue, and it swells like a sponge. That swelling is called edema.","notes":["Dense regular connective tissue is packed tightly with parallel collagen and has little room for fluid. Pick it for the tendons and ligaments that may be strained in a sprain.","Compact bone has a hard, calcified matrix that cannot soak up fluid. Pick it for the dense outer layer of a bone.","Hyaline cartilage has a firm matrix and no blood vessels. It covers joint surfaces and does not swell like a sponge under the skin.","Correct. Loose areolar tissue holds tissue fluid and swells in edema."],"comp":18,"competencyId":"w1-connective-id"},{"id":"N-m1-connective-519","dok":2,"q":"You can fold the external ear forward and it springs right back into shape. Which feature of its tissue explains this?","a":"Its cartilage matrix is packed with elastic fibers.","options":["Its matrix is calcified like compact bone","Its cartilage has a rich blood supply","Its cartilage is filled with thick collagen bundles","Its cartilage matrix is packed with elastic fibers"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The external ear is made of elastic cartilage, which has the same basic plan as hyaline cartilage but with many elastic fibers in its matrix. Elastic fibers stretch and then return to their original shape, so the ear can bend without breaking and then recoil. The same tissue in the epiglottis lets it flip down over the airway and back up again.","notes":["A calcified matrix is hard and inflexible, the opposite of what the ear does. Pick it for bone.","All cartilage is avascular, including the ear. A blood supply would not explain the springiness anyway.","Thick collagen bundles describe fibrocartilage, which is tough and resists compression rather than springing back. Pick it for an intervertebral disc.","Correct. Elastic fibers in the matrix let the ear bend and recoil."],"comp":26,"competencyId":"w2-cartilage-types"},{"id":"N-a17d-26-1","dok":3,"q":"A slide shows chondrocytes in lacunae and a tough matrix crowded with thick collagen bundles. No perichondrium wraps the edge of the tissue. Which structure could this sample have come from?","a":"A knee meniscus.","options":["A knee meniscus.","Articular cartilage.","The epiglottis.","A tracheal ring."],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Work it in two steps. Thick bundles of collagen mark fibrocartilage, and fibrocartilage is the type with no perichondrium on your lab list. Fibrocartilage is found in the intervertebral discs, the knee menisci, and the pubic symphysis, so the meniscus is the only structure here that fits.","notes":["Correct. Thick collagen bundles with no perichondrium mark fibrocartilage, and the knee menisci are made of it.","Articular cartilage, which caps the bone ends at a joint, is hyaline cartilage, with fine collagen and a glassy matrix, so it would not show thick collagen bundles.","The epiglottis is elastic cartilage. Its matrix is packed with elastic fibers, and it has a perichondrium around it.","The tracheal rings are hyaline cartilage, with fine collagen you cannot see on ordinary staining and a perichondrium around the edge."],"comp":26,"competencyId":"w2-cartilage-types"},{"id":"N-a17d-26-2","dok":3,"q":"An unlabeled slide shows chondrocytes in lacunae, a perichondrium at the edge, and a matrix packed with elastic fibers. A student labels it as tissue from the trachea. What is wrong with the label?","a":"It is elastic cartilage, so it came from the ear or epiglottis.","options":["It is hyaline cartilage, so the trachea label is correct.","It is elastic cartilage, so it came from the ear or epiglottis.","It is fibrocartilage, so it came from the pubic symphysis.","It is hyaline cartilage, so it came from the nose instead."],"correctIndex":1,"why":"First name the tissue from what you see: a matrix full of elastic fibers is elastic cartilage. Then match the tissue to its locations. Elastic cartilage is found in the external ear and the epiglottis, while the trachea is built from hyaline cartilage, so the label points to the wrong tissue.","notes":["Hyaline cartilage has fine collagen and a glassy matrix, not a matrix packed with elastic fibers. The trachea is hyaline, but this slide is not.","Correct. Many elastic fibers mean elastic cartilage, which is found in the external ear and the epiglottis.","Fibrocartilage shows thick collagen bundles and has no perichondrium. This slide has both a perichondrium and elastic fibers.","The nose is hyaline cartilage, but the elastic fibers on this slide rule hyaline out. The mix-up is knowing the nose is cartilage without checking the fiber type."],"comp":26,"competencyId":"w2-cartilage-types"}]},{"id":"m1-muscle-nervous-tissue","title":"Muscle and nervous tissue (histology)","summary":"skeletal, cardiac, smooth muscle tissue under the microscope; neurons and neuroglia as tissue; membranes","mw":"2026-08-31","tr":"2026-09-01","cards":[{"id":"L00245","dok":1,"q":"Which germ layer is the outer layer of the early embryo?","a":"Ectoderm.","options":["The epidermis","Endoderm","Mesoderm","Ectoderm"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The early embryo forms three germ layers, and the ectoderm is the outermost. It gives rise to the epidermis of the skin and to all nervous tissue, including the brain and spinal cord, which develop from the ectodermal neural tube. The mesoderm lies in the middle and the endoderm lies innermost.","notes":["The epidermis is a tissue that develops from the ectoderm, not a germ layer itself. Students pick it because the names sound alike, but it is the product of the outer layer, not the layer.","The endoderm is the innermost germ layer. It forms the epithelial lining of the digestive and respiratory tracts.","The mesoderm is the middle germ layer. It forms muscle, bone, cartilage, blood, and most connective tissue.","Correct. The ectoderm is the outer germ layer."],"lt":"t-tissues","lid":"c2","comp":15,"competencyId":"w1-germ-layers"},{"id":"L00252","dok":1,"q":"Which tissue has long, cylindrical, striated cells with many nuclei?","a":"Skeletal muscle.","options":["Smooth muscle","Skeletal muscle","Cardiac muscle","Dense regular connective tissue"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Skeletal muscle cells are long, cylindrical, and contain many nuclei, and they show striations (stripes) under the microscope. Each cell forms when many embryonic cells fuse together, which is why it has so many nuclei. Skeletal muscle is attached to bones and produces voluntary movement.","notes":["Smooth muscle cells are spindle-shaped with a single central nucleus and no striations. They are the answer for the walls of hollow organs such as the intestines and bladder.","Correct. Long, cylindrical, multinucleated cells are skeletal muscle.","Cardiac muscle cells are striated but branched, usually with one nucleus, and joined by intercalated discs. They are the answer for the muscle of the heart wall.","Dense regular connective tissue also shows long parallel structures, but they are collagen fibers with flattened fibroblast nuclei squeezed between them. Pick it for a tendon or ligament slide, not a muscle."],"lt":"t-tissues","lid":"c9","comp":19,"competencyId":"w1-muscle-tissue-id"},{"id":"L00253","dok":1,"q":"Which two cell types make up nervous tissue?","a":"Neurons and neuroglia.","options":["Neuroglia and chondrocytes","Neurons and adipocytes","Neurons and fibroblasts","Neurons and neuroglia"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Nervous tissue has two kinds of cells. Neurons are the signaling cells that carry electrical impulses, with a cell body and long processes. Neuroglia, or glial cells, are smaller supporting cells that protect, nourish, and insulate the neurons, and they far outnumber them.","notes":["Chondrocytes are cartilage cells sitting in lacunae. Neuroglia are half right, but the signaling cells of nervous tissue are neurons, not chondrocytes.","Adipocytes are fat-storing cells of adipose connective tissue. Neurons are half right, but adipocytes do not belong in nervous tissue.","Fibroblasts are the cells that build connective tissue fibers and matrix. Neurons are half right, but fibroblasts belong to connective tissue, not nervous tissue.","Correct. Nervous tissue is neurons plus neuroglia."],"lt":"t-tissues","lid":"c10","comp":20,"competencyId":"w1-nervous-tissue-id"},{"id":"L00254","dok":1,"q":"Which body membrane lines joint cavities and is made of connective tissue only, with no epithelium?","a":"The synovial membrane.","options":["The serous membrane","The cutaneous membrane","The synovial membrane","The mucous membrane"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The synovial membrane lines the cavities of synovial joints and is the only body membrane made entirely of connective tissue, with no epithelium. It secretes synovial fluid, which lubricates the joint. The other three membranes, mucous, serous, and cutaneous, each pair an epithelium with an underlying layer of connective tissue.","notes":["A serous membrane is simple squamous epithelium over a thin layer of connective tissue and lines closed ventral cavities, such as the pleura, pericardium, and peritoneum. It is the answer for membranes around the lungs, heart, and abdominal organs.","The cutaneous membrane is the skin, a keratinized stratified squamous epithelium over dense connective tissue. It is the answer for the body's dry outer membrane.","Correct. The synovial membrane is connective tissue only.","A mucous membrane has an epithelium over loose connective tissue and lines cavities open to the outside, such as the digestive and respiratory tracts. Pick it for a moist lining that secretes mucus."],"lt":"t-tissues","lid":"c11","comp":21,"competencyId":"w1-body-membranes"},{"id":"L00258","dok":2,"q":"Nervous tissue and the epidermis of the skin both arise from which germ layer?","a":"Ectoderm.","options":["Mesoderm","Both mesoderm and endoderm","Ectoderm","Endoderm"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The ectoderm is the outermost of the three germ layers. It gives rise to the epidermis of the skin and to all nervous tissue, because the brain and spinal cord develop from a tube of ectoderm called the neural tube. This shared origin links the outer skin and the nervous system in development.","notes":["The mesoderm forms muscle, bone, cartilage, blood, and most connective tissue. It would be the answer for the connective tissue of the dermis, but not for the epidermis or nervous tissue.","Neither the mesoderm nor the endoderm forms nervous tissue or the epidermis. Students choose this when they are unsure and hedge across layers.","Correct. Both come from the ectoderm.","The endoderm forms the epithelial lining of the digestive and respiratory tracts. Pick it for a question about the inner lining of the gut or airways."],"lt":"t-tissues","lid":"c15","comp":15,"competencyId":"w1-germ-layers"},{"id":"L00268","dok":2,"q":"Cardiac muscle cells are joined end to end by which structures?","a":"Intercalated discs.","options":["Synapses","Tight junctions","Hemidesmosomes","Intercalated discs"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Cardiac muscle cells are joined end to end by intercalated discs, which show up as dark lines across the fibers under the microscope. Each disc contains desmosomes, which hold the cells together as the heart contracts, and gap junctions, which let the contraction signal pass directly from cell to cell. Together they let the heart muscle contract as a coordinated unit.","notes":["Synapses are where a neuron passes its signal to another neuron, a muscle, or a gland. They connect nerve cells to their targets, not cardiac muscle cells to each other.","Tight junctions seal neighboring epithelial cells so substances cannot leak between them, as in the stomach and bladder lining. They are not the end-to-end connections of cardiac muscle.","Hemidesmosomes anchor epithelial cells to the basement membrane beneath them. They attach cells to a sheet, not cardiac cells to each other.","Correct. Intercalated discs join cardiac muscle cells."],"lt":"t-tissues","lid":"c25","comp":60,"competencyId":"cv-cardiac-muscle"},{"id":"L00270","dok":2,"q":"Which body membrane is made of a keratinized epithelium over dense connective tissue and is a dry membrane?","a":"The cutaneous membrane.","options":["The cutaneous membrane","The mucous membrane","The serous membrane","The synovial membrane"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The cutaneous membrane is the skin. Its surface layer is the epidermis, a keratinized stratified squamous epithelium, and beneath it is the dermis, made of dense connective tissue. It is the only dry membrane in the body, because keratin makes its surface tough and water-resistant.","notes":["Correct. The cutaneous membrane is the skin.","A mucous membrane is kept moist by mucus and lines cavities that open to the outside, such as the mouth, airways, and gut. It is the answer for a wet lining, not a dry one.","A serous membrane is simple squamous epithelium over thin connective tissue that lines closed body cavities and secretes a watery fluid. It is the answer for the pleura, pericardium, and peritoneum.","The synovial membrane lines joint cavities and has no epithelium at all. It is the answer for the membrane made of connective tissue only."],"lt":"t-tissues","lid":"c27","comp":21,"competencyId":"w1-body-membranes"},{"id":"L00278","dok":2,"q":"A muscle slide shows branching striated cells with one nucleus each, joined end to end by dark crossbands. Which muscle type is it?","a":"Cardiac muscle.","options":["Skeletal muscle","Smooth muscle","Cardiac muscle","Dense regular connective tissue"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Branching, striated cells with a single central nucleus, joined end to end by dark lines called intercalated discs, identify cardiac muscle. The discs are where neighboring cells connect, and they contain desmosomes and gap junctions. Cardiac muscle is found only in the heart wall and contracts involuntarily.","notes":["Skeletal muscle is striated too, but its cells are long, unbranched cylinders with many nuclei at the edge, and it has no intercalated discs.","Smooth muscle has spindle-shaped cells with one central nucleus and no striations. It is the answer for the walls of hollow organs such as the intestines and bladder.","Correct. Branching cells with intercalated discs are cardiac muscle.","Dense regular connective tissue shows parallel collagen fibers with flattened fibroblast nuclei. It has no branching cells or crossbands and is the answer for a tendon slide."],"lt":"t-tissues","lid":"c35","comp":19,"competencyId":"w1-muscle-tissue-id"},{"id":"L00285","dok":2,"q":"A muscle slide from the wall of a hollow organ shows spindle-shaped cells, each with a single nucleus and no striations. Which muscle type is it?","a":"Smooth muscle.","options":["Skeletal muscle","Smooth muscle","Cardiac muscle","Dense regular connective tissue"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Spindle-shaped cells, thick in the middle and tapered at both ends, each with one central nucleus and no striations, identify smooth muscle. It forms the walls of hollow organs such as the intestines, bladder, and blood vessels, where it contracts involuntarily to move contents along. The lack of striations is what gives it the name smooth.","notes":["Skeletal muscle has long cylindrical cells with many nuclei and visible striations. It is the answer for muscle attached to bones under voluntary control.","Correct. Spindle-shaped, non-striated cells are smooth muscle.","Cardiac muscle has branching striated cells joined by intercalated discs. It is the answer for the heart wall.","Dense regular connective tissue has parallel collagen fibers with flattened fibroblast nuclei, which can look wavy. It is the answer for a tendon slide, not the wall of a hollow organ."],"lt":"t-tissues","lid":"c42","comp":19,"competencyId":"w1-muscle-tissue-id"},{"id":"L00286","dok":2,"q":"A body membrane lines the airways, is kept moist, and is an epithelium over loose connective tissue. Which membrane is it?","a":"A mucous membrane.","options":["A serous membrane","A synovial membrane","A mucous membrane","A cutaneous membrane"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"A mucous membrane lines body cavities and passages that open to the outside, such as the airways and the digestive, urinary, and reproductive tracts. It is made of an epithelium resting on loose connective tissue called the lamina propria, and it is kept moist by mucus. That moist surface traps particles and protects the lining.","notes":["A serous membrane lines closed ventral cavities and covers the organs inside them, producing a thin watery fluid. It is the answer for the pleura, pericardium, and peritoneum.","A synovial membrane lines joint cavities and has no epithelium. It is the answer for the membrane made only of connective tissue.","Correct. A moist lining of an open passage is a mucous membrane.","The cutaneous membrane is the skin, a keratinized epithelium over dense connective tissue that stays dry. It is the answer for the body's outer covering."],"lt":"t-tissues","lid":"c43","comp":21,"competencyId":"w1-body-membranes"},{"id":"L00290","dok":2,"q":"Which chain correctly links a germ layer to a tissue it forms and then to a location of that tissue?","a":"Ectoderm forms nervous tissue, found in the brain.","options":["Ectoderm forms nervous tissue, found in the brain","Mesoderm forms the epidermis, found in the skin","Endoderm forms nervous tissue, found in the brain","Ectoderm forms bone, found in the skeleton"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The ectoderm, the outer germ layer, forms all nervous tissue, and the brain is made of nervous tissue, so this chain links correctly at every step. Checking each link separately is how to catch the errors in the other chains. The ectoderm also forms the epidermis, the mesoderm forms bone and most connective tissue, and the endoderm forms the linings of the gut and airways.","notes":["Correct. Ectoderm forms nervous tissue, which builds the brain.","The epidermis comes from the ectoderm, not the mesoderm. The mesoderm would be correct for the dermis or for bone.","The endoderm forms the epithelial lining of the digestive and respiratory tracts, not nervous tissue. The second and third links are right, but the chain starts from the wrong layer.","Bone develops from the mesoderm, not the ectoderm. The chain would be correct if it started with the mesoderm."],"lt":"t-tissues","lid":"c47","comp":15,"competencyId":"w1-germ-layers"},{"id":"L00293","dok":2,"q":"Match each membrane to its composition: cutaneous, serous, and synovial. Which set of pairings is correct?","a":"Cutaneous is keratinized epithelium over dense connective tissue; serous is simple squamous over thin connective tissue; synovial is connective tissue only","options":["Cutaneous is keratinized epithelium over dense connective tissue; serous is simple squamous over thin connective tissue; synovial is connective tissue only","Cutaneous is simple squamous over thin connective tissue; serous is connective tissue only; synovial is keratinized epithelium","Cutaneous is connective tissue only; serous is keratinized epithelium; synovial is simple squamous over thin connective tissue","Cutaneous is simple squamous epithelium; serous is connective tissue only; synovial is keratinized epithelium over dense connective tissue"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The cutaneous membrane, the skin, is a keratinized stratified squamous epithelium over dense connective tissue. A serous membrane is simple squamous epithelium over a thin layer of connective tissue and lines closed body cavities. The synovial membrane lines joint cavities and has no epithelium at all, only connective tissue.","notes":["Correct. Each membrane is matched to its real composition.","This set gives the skin a simple squamous epithelium, which describes a serous membrane. It also puts keratinized epithelium in a joint, when the synovial membrane has no epithelium.","This set calls the skin connective tissue only, but the skin has an epidermis, a keratinized epithelium. Connective tissue only describes the synovial membrane.","This set gives the skin a simple squamous epithelium, which belongs to a serous membrane, and gives the synovial membrane the keratinized epithelium over dense connective tissue that belongs to the skin. It also calls the serous membrane connective tissue only, which describes the synovial membrane."],"lt":"t-tissues","lid":"c50","comp":21,"competencyId":"w1-body-membranes"},{"id":"L00294","dok":2,"q":"Match each muscle type to its cell description: skeletal, cardiac, and smooth. Which set is correct?","a":"Skeletal has long cylindrical multinucleate cells, cardiac has branching cells with intercalated discs, smooth has spindle-shaped cells with one nucleus.","options":["Skeletal is spindle-shaped with one nucleus; cardiac is long and cylindrical; smooth is branched","Skeletal is long, cylindrical, and multinucleate; cardiac is branched with intercalated discs; smooth is spindle-shaped","Skeletal is branched with intercalated discs; cardiac is spindle-shaped; smooth is long and cylindrical","Skeletal is long and cylindrical; cardiac is spindle-shaped; smooth is branched with intercalated discs"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Skeletal muscle cells are long, cylindrical, and multinucleated. Cardiac muscle cells are branched and joined by intercalated discs, and smooth muscle cells are spindle-shaped with a single central nucleus. Shape, number of nuclei, and the presence of intercalated discs are the quickest features for telling the three apart on a slide.","notes":["This set gives skeletal muscle the spindle shape of smooth muscle and gives smooth muscle the branching of cardiac muscle. Each description is real but attached to the wrong tissue.","Correct. Each muscle type is matched to its cell shape.","This set gives skeletal muscle the intercalated discs that only cardiac muscle has. It also gives smooth muscle the long cylindrical shape of skeletal muscle.","This set gets skeletal muscle right but swaps the other two. Intercalated discs belong to cardiac muscle, and the spindle shape belongs to smooth muscle."],"lt":"t-tissues","lid":"c51","comp":19,"competencyId":"w1-muscle-tissue-id"},{"id":"L00304","dok":1,"q":"Which description fits skeletal muscle tissue?","a":"Striated and voluntary.","options":["Non-striated and involuntary","Striated and involuntary","Non-striated and voluntary","Striated and voluntary"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Skeletal muscle is striated, meaning its cells show light and dark bands under the microscope, and it is voluntary, meaning it is under conscious control. It is attached to bones (and occasionally to skin) and produces body movement and heat. Of the three muscle types, it is the only one that is both striated and voluntary.","notes":["Non-striated and involuntary describes smooth muscle in the walls of hollow organs such as the intestines and bladder. It would be the answer for a question about smooth muscle.","Striated and involuntary describes cardiac muscle, which has bands like skeletal muscle but contracts without conscious control. Pick it for the muscle of the heart wall.","No muscle tissue in the body is both non-striated and voluntary. Students choose this when they remember that skeletal muscle is voluntary but mix up which muscle lacks striations.","Correct. Skeletal muscle is striated and voluntary."],"lt":"t-tissues","lid":"c13-x2","comp":77,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-tissue-types"},{"id":"L00313","dok":2,"q":"Cardiac muscle is striated, involuntary, and made of branching cells joined by intercalated discs. Which of these features is unique to cardiac muscle among the three muscle types?","a":"Intercalated discs joining branching cells.","options":["Involuntary control","Intercalated discs joining cells","A spindle-shaped cell body","Striations across the cells"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Intercalated discs joining branching cells are found only in cardiac muscle. Its striations are shared with skeletal muscle, and its involuntary control is shared with smooth muscle, so neither feature identifies cardiac muscle alone. Looking for the one feature no other tissue has is the fastest way to identify it on a slide.","notes":["Involuntary control is shared with smooth muscle, so it does not single out cardiac muscle. It would separate cardiac from skeletal muscle only.","Correct. Only cardiac muscle has intercalated discs.","A spindle-shaped cell body is the feature of smooth muscle, not cardiac muscle. Pick it for a question about what is unique to smooth muscle.","Striations are also present in skeletal muscle, so they cannot identify cardiac muscle by themselves. They would help separate cardiac from smooth muscle, but not from skeletal."],"lt":"t-tissues","lid":"c22-x2","comp":77,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-tissue-types"},{"id":"N-m1-muscle-nervous-tissue-1","dok":1,"q":"Which body membrane lines closed ventral body cavities and covers the organs within them, such as the pleura around the lungs?","a":"A serous membrane.","options":["A mucous membrane","A cutaneous membrane","A serous membrane","A synovial membrane"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"A serous membrane is a thin layer of simple squamous epithelium over a small amount of connective tissue. Its parietal layer lines the cavity wall and its visceral layer covers the organs, and it secretes a watery fluid that lets organs slide with little friction. The pleura, pericardium, and peritoneum are the three serous membranes.","notes":["A mucous membrane lines passages that open to the outside, such as the airways and digestive tract. It is the answer for a moist lining that secretes mucus, not a closed cavity.","The cutaneous membrane is the skin, the body's dry outer covering. It does not line any internal cavity.","Correct. Serous membranes line closed ventral cavities.","The synovial membrane lines joint cavities and has no epithelium. It is the answer for a membrane inside a joint, not around the lungs."],"comp":21,"competencyId":"w1-body-membranes"},{"id":"N-m1-muscle-nervous-tissue-2","dok":2,"q":"Which two cell junctions are found in the intercalated discs of cardiac muscle?","a":"Desmosomes and gap junctions.","options":["Tight junctions and desmosomes","Tight junctions and hemidesmosomes","Hemidesmosomes and gap junctions","Desmosomes and gap junctions"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Intercalated discs, where cardiac muscle cells meet end to end, contain desmosomes and gap junctions. The desmosomes keep the cells from pulling apart as the heart contracts, and the gap junctions let the contraction signal pass directly from one cell to the next. Together they let the heart wall work as one coordinated unit.","notes":["Desmosomes are right, but tight junctions seal epithelial linings against leakage. The signaling junction in the discs is the gap junction.","Tight junctions seal epithelial cells, and hemidesmosomes anchor epithelial cells to the basement membrane. Neither is part of the intercalated discs.","Gap junctions are right, but hemidesmosomes attach a cell to a basement membrane, not to a neighboring muscle cell. The anchoring junction in the discs is the desmosome.","Correct. Desmosomes hold the cells together and gap junctions pass the signal."],"comp":60,"competencyId":"cv-cardiac-muscle"},{"id":"N-m1-muscle-nervous-tissue-3","dok":1,"q":"Which muscle tissue is striated but usually has only one nucleus per cell?","a":"Cardiac muscle.","options":["Cardiac muscle","Smooth muscle","Skeletal muscle","Dense regular connective tissue"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Cardiac muscle cells are striated like skeletal muscle cells, but each usually has a single, centrally placed nucleus. Skeletal muscle cells are striated with many nuclei, and smooth muscle cells have one nucleus but no striations. Checking striations and nuclei together separates all three muscle types.","notes":["Correct. Cardiac muscle is striated with one nucleus per cell.","Smooth muscle cells have a single nucleus but no striations. It is the answer for a non-striated tissue in the walls of hollow organs.","Skeletal muscle is striated, but each long cell has many nuclei. It is the answer for a striated, multinucleated tissue.","Dense regular connective tissue has wavy parallel collagen fibers that can look banded, but it is not muscle and its fibroblasts are not striated cells. It is the answer for a tendon slide."],"comp":19,"competencyId":"w1-muscle-tissue-id"},{"id":"N-m1-muscle-nervous-tissue-4","dok":2,"q":"A nervous tissue slide shows a large cell body with a prominent nucleus and several long processes branching from it, surrounded by many small nuclei. What is the large cell?","a":"A multipolar neuron.","options":["A fibroblast","A neuroglial cell","A multipolar neuron","A smooth muscle cell"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"A large cell body with a prominent nucleus and several long processes extending from it is a multipolar neuron, the most common type of neuron. Its processes are many dendrites and a single axon. The many small nuclei scattered around it belong to neuroglia, the smaller supporting cells that far outnumber neurons.","notes":["A fibroblast is a connective tissue cell with an elongated nucleus that builds fibers. It does not have a large cell body with long branching processes.","Neuroglia are the small cells whose nuclei surround the neuron on this slide. They support and protect neurons and are much smaller than a neuron's cell body.","Correct. A large cell body with many processes is a multipolar neuron.","A smooth muscle cell is spindle-shaped with a single central nucleus and no branching processes. It is the answer for a slide of a hollow organ wall."],"comp":20,"competencyId":"w1-nervous-tissue-id"},{"id":"L03476","dok":1,"q":"Which statement correctly describes how cardiac muscle is controlled?","a":"It is involuntary.","options":["It is voluntary, contracting only when we decide to move","It is involuntary, contracting without conscious control","It is involuntary only while the vagus nerve stimulates it","It is voluntary at rest and involuntary during exercise"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Cardiac muscle is involuntary, which means we cannot make it contract or stop by choosing to. The heart sets its own beat through its conduction system, and autonomic nerves only speed it up or slow it down. Skeletal muscle is the only muscle tissue under voluntary control.","notes":["This describes skeletal muscle, the voluntary muscle attached to bones that contracts when you decide to move. Choose voluntary if the question asked about a muscle such as the deltoid, not the heart.","Correct. Cardiac muscle contracts on its own, without conscious control.","This confuses starting the beat with adjusting it. The vagus nerve carries parasympathetic fibers that slow the heart rate, but the heart beats involuntarily whether or not the vagus is active, as a transplanted heart with its nerves cut shows.","This is a made-up mix. Cardiac muscle never switches to voluntary control; exercise changes the heart rate through autonomic nerves, but the muscle stays involuntary the whole time."],"lt":"t-cardiac-muscle","lid":"W3-120","comp":77,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-tissue-types"},{"id":"L03477","dok":1,"q":"Cardiac muscle is striated. What produces these stripes under the microscope?","a":"Ordered sarcomeres in each cell.","options":["Many nuclei along each cell","Intercalated discs between cells","Branching of the cells","Ordered sarcomeres in each cell"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Striations are the alternating light and dark bands seen across a muscle cell. They come from sarcomeres, the repeating contractile units, lined up in order along the cell. Cardiac muscle is striated for the same reason skeletal muscle is, because both are built of ordered sarcomeres, while smooth muscle lacks this arrangement and looks unstriped.","notes":["Many nuclei per cell is a feature of skeletal muscle fibers, not cardiac muscle, which usually has one central nucleus. The number of nuclei also has nothing to do with the stripes.","Intercalated discs are the dark junction lines where neighboring cardiomyocytes meet end to end. They mark cell boundaries, and they are the answer if the question asked which feature identifies cardiac muscle and sets it apart from skeletal muscle.","Branching describes the shape of cardiomyocytes, which split and connect to several neighbors. It would be the answer for how cardiac cells form a network, but it does not create the stripes.","Correct. Sarcomeres lined up in order produce the striations, in both cardiac and skeletal muscle."],"lt":"t-cardiac-muscle","lid":"W3-121","comp":19,"competencyId":"w1-muscle-tissue-id"},{"id":"L03484","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares cardiac muscle with skeletal muscle?","a":"Both are striated, but cardiac muscle is involuntary with branched cells.","options":["Both are voluntary, but only skeletal muscle has visible striations","Both are involuntary, but cardiac muscle cells contain many nuclei","Both are striated, but cardiac muscle is involuntary with branched cells","Neither is striated, but both have cells joined by intercalated discs"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Cardiac and skeletal muscle are both striated because both are built of ordered sarcomeres. They differ in control and cell shape. Skeletal muscle is voluntary, with long, cylindrical, unbranched cells that each hold many nuclei, while cardiac muscle is involuntary, with short, branched cells that usually have one central nucleus and are joined by intercalated discs.","notes":["Only skeletal muscle is voluntary, and cardiac muscle is striated too. This statement would fit only if the heart were under conscious control, which it is not.","Skeletal muscle is voluntary, and the cell with many nuclei is the skeletal muscle fiber, not the cardiomyocyte. This choice swaps the nucleus feature between the two tissues.","Correct. Both are striated; cardiac muscle is involuntary and its cells are short and branched.","Both tissues are clearly striated, and intercalated discs are found only in cardiac muscle. Pick intercalated discs for a question about the feature that identifies cardiac muscle on a slide."],"lt":"t-cardiac-muscle","lid":"W3-145","comp":77,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-tissue-types"},{"id":"N-m1-muscle-nervous-tissue-500","dok":1,"q":"Where are the nuclei located in a skeletal muscle fiber?","a":"Many nuclei along the edge of the fiber, just under the cell membrane.","options":["No nuclei, since the fibers are dead cells","One nucleus in the center of the cell","Many nuclei along the edge, under the membrane","Two nuclei, one at each tapered end"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"A skeletal muscle fiber is one very long cell formed when many embryonic cells fuse, so it ends up with many nuclei. These nuclei are pushed to the periphery, just under the sarcolemma (the fiber's plasma membrane), because the inside of the cell is packed with contractile myofibrils. Multiple peripheral nuclei are one of the lab structures for identifying skeletal muscle.","notes":["Skeletal muscle fibers are living cells. Dead cells without nuclei describe the keratinized surface of the epidermis, not muscle.","One central nucleus fits cardiac and smooth muscle cells. Pick it if the question asked about either of those tissues.","Correct. Skeletal muscle fibers have many nuclei at the periphery.","No muscle type has one nucleus at each end. This is a made-up pattern, perhaps from picturing the tapered ends of smooth muscle cells."],"comp":19,"competencyId":"w1-muscle-tissue-id"},{"id":"N-m1-muscle-nervous-tissue-501","dok":1,"q":"How many nuclei does a smooth muscle cell have, and where are they?","a":"A single nucleus in the center of the cell.","options":["Many nuclei along the edge","No nucleus at all","Several nuclei at the cell junctions","A single nucleus in the center"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Smooth muscle cells are spindle-shaped, wide in the middle and tapering at both ends, and each one has a single nucleus in its thick central region. On a slide the nuclei often look like elongated ovals lying in the middle of each cell. A single central nucleus with no striations is how you recognize smooth muscle.","notes":["Many nuclei along the edge describe skeletal muscle fibers. Pick this for a slide of long, striated, cylindrical fibers.","Smooth muscle cells are living cells and each has a nucleus. Cells without nuclei, such as mature red blood cells, belong to other tissues.","Nuclei are not located at cell junctions in any muscle tissue. This mixes up nuclei with the intercalated discs that join cardiac cells.","Correct. Each smooth muscle cell has one central nucleus."],"comp":19,"competencyId":"w1-muscle-tissue-id"},{"id":"N-m1-muscle-nervous-tissue-502","dok":1,"q":"What is the plasma membrane of a skeletal muscle fiber called?","a":"The sarcolemma.","options":["The perichondrium","The sarcolemma","The neurolemma","The basement membrane"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Sarco means flesh, and lemma means husk or covering, so the sarcolemma is the plasma membrane of a muscle fiber. On a skeletal muscle slide it is the thin outer boundary of each fiber, with the peripheral nuclei lying just beneath it. It is one of the structures listed for identification on the skeletal muscle slide.","notes":["The perichondrium is the dense connective tissue layer that surrounds cartilage. Pick it for a hyaline or elastic cartilage slide.","Correct. The sarcolemma is the plasma membrane of a muscle fiber.","The neurolemma is the outer layer of a Schwann cell wrapped around a peripheral axon. Pick it for a question about nerve fibers, not muscle.","The basement membrane is the layer that anchors an epithelium to the connective tissue below. It is not the membrane of a muscle cell."],"comp":74,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-fiber-parts"},{"id":"N-m1-muscle-nervous-tissue-503","dok":1,"q":"Where is smooth muscle tissue found?","a":"In the walls of hollow organs such as the intestines and bladder.","options":["In the walls of the intestines and bladder","Attached to the bones of the skeleton","In the walls of the heart","Covering the ends of long bones"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Smooth muscle forms the walls of hollow organs, such as the intestines and the urinary bladder. Its slow, involuntary contractions squeeze and propel substances along, which is why food moves through the gut without any conscious effort.","notes":["Correct. Smooth muscle lines the walls of hollow organs such as the intestines and bladder.","Muscle attached to bones is skeletal muscle, which is striated and voluntary. Pick it for the biceps or quadriceps.","The walls of the heart are cardiac muscle, which is striated and has intercalated discs. Pick it for the myocardium.","The ends of long bones are covered by hyaline cartilage, which is connective tissue, not muscle."],"comp":77,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-tissue-types"},{"id":"N-m1-muscle-nervous-tissue-504","dok":1,"q":"Skeletal muscle is usually attached to bones. To which other structure is it occasionally attached?","a":"The skin.","options":["The stomach wall","The heart wall","The skin","The lining of blood vessels"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Most skeletal muscles pull on bones, but some attach to the skin. The muscles of facial expression are the classic example: they pull on the skin of the face to make expressions such as a smile or a frown. These muscles are still skeletal muscle, striated and under voluntary control.","notes":["The stomach wall contains smooth muscle, which is involuntary. Pick it if the question asked where smooth muscle is found.","The heart wall is made of cardiac muscle, not skeletal muscle attached to it. Pick it if the question asked where cardiac muscle is found.","Correct. Some skeletal muscles, such as those of facial expression, attach to the skin.","The lining of blood vessels is simple squamous epithelium. It is not an attachment point for skeletal muscle."],"comp":77,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-tissue-types"},{"id":"N-m1-muscle-nervous-tissue-505","dok":2,"q":"A cross section of muscle shows large, rounded-to-angular fiber profiles, each with several nuclei at its edge and none in its center. Which tissue is it?","a":"Skeletal muscle.","options":["Skeletal muscle","Dense regular connective tissue","Smooth muscle","Cardiac muscle"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"In a cross section you see each muscle fiber cut across like a slice of a cylinder. Skeletal muscle fibers are large and have many nuclei pushed to the periphery, so each profile shows nuclei around its edge and none in the middle. Cardiac and smooth muscle cells would show a single nucleus near the center when the cut passes through it.","notes":["Correct. Large fibers with several peripheral nuclei mark skeletal muscle.","Dense regular connective tissue has collagen bundles and fibroblasts rather than muscle fibers. Pick it for a tendon.","Smooth muscle cells are small and have one central nucleus. Pick it for a slide of the wall of the intestine.","Cardiac muscle cells have a single central nucleus, so a cross section would show nuclei in the middle of the profiles. Pick it for a slide of the heart wall."],"comp":19,"competencyId":"w1-muscle-tissue-id"},{"id":"N-m1-muscle-nervous-tissue-506","dok":2,"q":"You need a sample of smooth muscle tissue to make a slide. Which location would give you one?","a":"The wall of the small intestine.","options":["The wall of the small intestine","The wall of the left ventricle","The biceps brachii","The tongue"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Smooth muscle lives in the walls of hollow organs, and the small intestine has layers of it that squeeze food along. Each of the other locations contains striated muscle instead. Matching each muscle tissue to its typical location is part of identifying it in lab.","notes":["Correct. The wall of the small intestine contains smooth muscle.","The wall of the left ventricle is cardiac muscle. It would give you a slide of branching, striated cells joined by intercalated discs.","The biceps brachii is a skeletal muscle that moves the forearm. It would give you a slide of long, striated, multinucleated fibers.","The tongue is made of skeletal muscle, which lets you move it voluntarily when you speak and swallow."],"comp":77,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-tissue-types"},{"id":"N-m1-muscle-nervous-tissue-507","dok":2,"q":"Which feature do cardiac and smooth muscle share that skeletal muscle does not?","a":"Involuntary control.","options":["Involuntary control","Attachment to bones","Many peripheral nuclei","Striations"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Cardiac muscle pumps blood and smooth muscle moves material through hollow organs, and neither one is under conscious control. Skeletal muscle is the only voluntary muscle tissue. Grouping features this way, which are shared and which are unique, makes the three tissues easier to tell apart.","notes":["Correct. Cardiac and smooth muscle are both involuntary.","Attachment to bones is a feature of skeletal muscle alone. Cardiac muscle forms the heart wall and smooth muscle lines hollow organs.","Many peripheral nuclei are found only in skeletal muscle. Pick this if the question asked what is unique to skeletal muscle.","Striations are shared by skeletal and cardiac muscle. Smooth muscle is the one without them, so this feature does not fit."],"comp":77,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-tissue-types"},{"id":"N-m1-muscle-nervous-tissue-508","dok":2,"q":"Which feature do skeletal and cardiac muscle share that smooth muscle does not?","a":"Striations.","options":["Spindle-shaped cells","Intercalated discs","Striations","Voluntary control"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Skeletal and cardiac muscle both show striations, the alternating light and dark bands across the cells, because their contractile proteins are lined up in regular repeating units. Smooth muscle has the same kinds of proteins but not in that orderly arrangement, so it looks smooth. That is how it got its name.","notes":["Spindle-shaped cells are the mark of smooth muscle. Pick this if the question asked how smooth muscle cells are shaped.","Intercalated discs are found only in cardiac muscle. Pick this if the question asked what is unique to cardiac muscle.","Correct. Skeletal and cardiac muscle are both striated, while smooth muscle is not.","Voluntary control belongs only to skeletal muscle. Cardiac muscle is involuntary."],"comp":19,"competencyId":"w1-muscle-tissue-id"},{"id":"N-m1-muscle-nervous-tissue-509","dok":2,"q":"Two cell junctions are found in the intercalated discs of cardiac muscle. Which one holds neighboring cells together so they do not pull apart as the heart contracts?","a":"Desmosomes.","options":["Tight junctions","Hemidesmosomes","Gap junctions","Desmosomes"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Intercalated discs contain desmosomes and gap junctions, and each has a different job. Desmosomes strongly bind one cell to the next and keep them from separating under tension, which matters in a muscle that contracts with every heartbeat. Gap junctions are channels that let the impulse pass from cell to cell so the heart contracts as a unit.","notes":["Tight junctions seal the space between cells so contents cannot leak, as in the lining of the stomach and bladder. They are not part of intercalated discs.","Hemidesmosomes anchor a cell to a basement membrane, not to a neighboring cell. Pick them for the attachment of the epidermis to its basement membrane.","Gap junctions are also in the intercalated discs, but they are channels that let impulses spread between cells. Pick them if the question asked how the signal passes from one cardiac cell to the next.","Correct. Desmosomes hold cardiac cells together under the tension of contraction."],"comp":60,"competencyId":"cv-cardiac-muscle"},{"id":"N-m1-muscle-nervous-tissue-510","dok":3,"q":"After a heart attack, the dead area of heart muscle is replaced by scar tissue rather than new muscle. What best explains this?","a":"Cardiac muscle cells have very limited ability to divide, so connective tissue fills the gap.","options":["Cardiac muscle lacks nuclei, so it cannot make new proteins","Cardiac muscle cells divide very little, so scar tissue fills the gap","Cardiac muscle has no blood supply, so nothing can reach the area","Cardiac muscle is voluntary, so it heals only with exercise"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Mature cardiac muscle cells have very limited ability to divide, so the heart cannot replace large numbers of lost cells. Instead, fibroblasts lay down collagen, and the area becomes a scar of dense connective tissue. Scar tissue holds the wall together, but it cannot contract, so the heart pumps less strongly.","notes":["Cardiac muscle cells do have nuclei, usually one central nucleus per cell. This choice confuses them with cells that lack nuclei, such as mature red blood cells.","Correct. Cardiac cells divide very little, so dense connective tissue forms a scar in their place.","Cardiac muscle has a rich blood supply through the coronary arteries. A heart attack happens when that supply is blocked, but normal heart muscle is not avascular.","Cardiac muscle is involuntary, not voluntary. Exercise does not regrow cardiac muscle that has already died."],"comp":60,"competencyId":"cv-cardiac-muscle"},{"id":"N-m1-muscle-nervous-tissue-511","dok":2,"q":"A slide labeled tongue shows striated, multinucleated fibers running in several directions. Which tissue is it, and how is it controlled?","a":"Skeletal muscle, under voluntary control.","options":["Skeletal muscle, under voluntary control","Smooth muscle, under involuntary control","Cardiac muscle, under involuntary control","Smooth muscle, under voluntary control"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Striations together with many nuclei per fiber mark skeletal muscle. The tongue is skeletal muscle, which is why you can move it at will to speak, chew, and swallow. Its fibers run in several directions, which lets the tongue change shape as well as position.","notes":["Correct. The tongue is skeletal muscle and moves voluntarily.","Smooth muscle has no striations and only one nucleus per cell. Pick it for the wall of the stomach or intestine.","Cardiac muscle is striated but has one central nucleus and intercalated discs, and it is found only in the heart wall.","Smooth muscle is involuntary, and it lacks the striations seen on this slide. Neither half of this choice fits the tongue."],"comp":77,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-tissue-types"},{"id":"N-m1-muscle-nervous-tissue-512","dok":3,"q":"An unknown slide shows striated fibers that branch, each with one central nucleus. A student calls it skeletal muscle because of the striations. What is the error?","a":"Striations are shared by skeletal and cardiac muscle; branching and a single central nucleus point to cardiac muscle.","options":["Branching cells are the mark of skeletal muscle, so the student is right","A central nucleus is found only in nervous tissue, so it must be a neuron","Striations appear only in smooth muscle, so it must be smooth","Cardiac muscle is also striated, and branching cells mark cardiac"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Striations narrow the choice to skeletal or cardiac muscle but cannot settle it, because both are striated. The deciding features are branching cells and one central nucleus, which belong to cardiac muscle. Skeletal fibers are unbranched and have many nuclei at their edges.","notes":["Skeletal muscle fibers are long and unbranched. Branching is a feature of cardiac muscle, so the student is not right.","Many cells, including cardiac and smooth muscle cells, have a central nucleus. A striated fiber is muscle, not a neuron.","Smooth muscle is the one muscle tissue without striations. This choice reverses the rule.","Correct. Both striated types have stripes, and the branching cells with central nuclei point to cardiac muscle."],"comp":19,"competencyId":"w1-muscle-tissue-id"},{"id":"N-m1-muscle-nervous-tissue-513","dok":1,"q":"Which part of a neuron receives incoming signals from other cells?","a":"The dendrites.","options":["The axon","The axon hillock","The synaptic bulb","The dendrites"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Dendrites are the short, branched, tree-like processes that extend from the cell body. Their job is to receive incoming signals and carry them toward the cell body. A multipolar neuron has many dendrites but only one axon, which is how you tell them apart on a slide.","notes":["The axon carries the impulse away from the cell body toward other cells. Pick it if the question asked which process sends the signal out.","The axon hillock is the cone-shaped region where the cell body joins the axon. Pick it if the question asked where the axon begins.","The synaptic bulb is the swollen tip at the end of an axon terminal, where the signal is passed to the next cell. It sends signals rather than receiving them.","Correct. Dendrites receive incoming signals."],"comp":20,"competencyId":"w1-nervous-tissue-id"},{"id":"N-m1-muscle-nervous-tissue-514","dok":1,"q":"Which part of a neuron carries the nerve impulse away from the cell body toward other cells?","a":"The axon.","options":["The cell body","The nucleolus","The axon","The dendrite"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The axon is the single long process of a neuron. It carries the nerve impulse away from the cell body, and at its far end it branches into axon terminals that contact the next cell. A neuron can have many dendrites, but it has only one axon.","notes":["The cell body holds the nucleus and is the neuron's metabolic center. It is where the axon starts, not the process that carries the impulse away.","The nucleolus is a small dark spot inside the nucleus, visible in the cell body on a neuron slide. It is not a process and does not carry impulses.","Correct. The axon carries the impulse away from the cell body.","Dendrites carry signals toward the cell body, the opposite direction. Pick them if the question asked which processes receive incoming signals."],"comp":20,"competencyId":"w1-nervous-tissue-id"},{"id":"N-m1-muscle-nervous-tissue-515","dok":1,"q":"Which part of a neuron contains the nucleus and serves as the cell's metabolic center?","a":"The cell body, or soma.","options":["The dendrites","The cell body","The synaptic bulb","The axon"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The cell body, also called the soma, holds the nucleus and most of the cytoplasm, so it is where the neuron makes its proteins and keeps itself alive. On a multipolar neuron slide it is the large central part of the cell, often with a clearly visible nucleus and a dark nucleolus inside.","notes":["Dendrites are branched processes that receive signals. They extend from the cell body but do not hold the nucleus.","Correct. The cell body holds the nucleus and is the neuron's metabolic center.","The synaptic bulb is the swollen tip of an axon terminal that passes the signal to the next cell. It is far from the nucleus.","The axon is the single long process that carries impulses away. It contains no nucleus."],"comp":20,"competencyId":"w1-nervous-tissue-id"},{"id":"N-m1-muscle-nervous-tissue-516","dok":1,"q":"On a neuron, what is the synaptic bulb?","a":"The swollen tip at the end of an axon terminal.","options":["The swollen tip at the end of an axon terminal","A short, branched process that receives signals","The cone-shaped region where the axon begins","A gap in the myelin sheath along the axon"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"At its far end, the axon splits into fine branches called axon terminals, and each one ends in a small swelling, the synaptic bulb (also called the synaptic end bulb). The bulb holds the machinery that passes the signal to the next cell. It is one of the three nerve tissue structures listed for the Module 1 lab.","notes":["Correct. The synaptic bulb is the swollen tip of an axon terminal.","A short, branched process that receives signals is a dendrite. Pick it if the question asked what brings signals toward the cell body.","The cone-shaped region where the axon begins is the axon hillock, at the junction with the cell body. Pick it if the question asked where the axon starts.","A gap in the myelin sheath is a node of Ranvier. Pick it for a question about a myelinated axon in a nerve."],"comp":20,"competencyId":"w1-nervous-tissue-id"},{"id":"N-m1-muscle-nervous-tissue-517","dok":1,"q":"Where in the body is nervous tissue found?","a":"The brain, spinal cord, and nerves.","options":["The walls of the intestines and bladder","The tendons and ligaments","The heart wall and blood vessels","The brain, spinal cord, and nerves"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Nervous tissue is made of neurons and neuroglia, and its job is to detect changes and carry electrical signals. It is found in the brain and spinal cord, which make up the central nervous system, and in the nerves that branch out to the rest of the body.","notes":["The walls of the intestines and bladder are mainly smooth muscle, lined by epithelium. Pick smooth muscle for that location.","Tendons and ligaments are dense regular connective tissue. Pick them for a slide of parallel collagen bundles.","The heart wall is cardiac muscle, and blood vessel walls are mostly smooth muscle and connective tissue. Nerves reach these organs, but the walls themselves are not nervous tissue.","Correct. Nervous tissue makes up the brain, spinal cord, and nerves."],"comp":20,"competencyId":"w1-nervous-tissue-id"},{"id":"N-m1-muscle-nervous-tissue-518","dok":2,"q":"On a nervous tissue slide, one large neuron with long processes is surrounded by many small, dark nuclei that have no visible processes. What are the small nuclei?","a":"Neuroglia (glial cells).","options":["Synaptic bulbs","Dendrites","Neuroglia","Smooth muscle cells"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Neuroglia are the supporting cells of nervous tissue. They are much smaller and far more numerous than neurons, so on a slide they appear as many small, dark nuclei scattered around the large neuron cell bodies. Telling neurons from neuroglia under the microscope is a listed lab skill.","notes":["Synaptic bulbs are swellings at the tips of axon terminals. They are parts of a neuron, not separate cells with nuclei.","Dendrites are processes of the neuron itself and do not have their own nuclei. Pick them if the question asked about the branches coming off the cell body.","Correct. The many small nuclei around the neuron belong to neuroglia.","Smooth muscle cells are spindle-shaped muscle cells in the walls of hollow organs. They are not found scattered among neurons in nervous tissue."],"comp":20,"competencyId":"w1-nervous-tissue-id"},{"id":"N-m1-muscle-nervous-tissue-519","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares neurons with neuroglia?","a":"Neuroglia are smaller and far more numerous than neurons, and unlike neurons they can still divide.","options":["Neuroglia carry impulses, while neurons only support and protect","Neurons and neuroglia are the same size and both carry impulses","Neuroglia are smaller and more numerous, and only neuroglia can divide","Neurons are smaller and more numerous, and only neurons can divide"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Neurons are the excitable cells that receive and transmit nerve impulses, and mature neurons cannot divide. Neuroglia are the support cells that nourish, support, and protect neurons. They are smaller and far more numerous, and they keep the ability to divide.","notes":["This swaps the two roles. Neurons carry impulses, while neuroglia support and protect them.","Neurons are generally much larger than neuroglia, and carrying impulses is the job of neurons, not glia.","Correct. Neuroglia are smaller, more numerous, and still able to divide.","This reverses the two cells. Neurons are the larger cells, and mature neurons cannot divide."],"comp":20,"competencyId":"w1-nervous-tissue-id"},{"id":"N-m1-muscle-nervous-tissue-520","dok":3,"q":"A glioma is a brain tumor that arises from glial cells. Why do tumors like this arise from glia rather than from mature neurons?","a":"Glial cells can still divide, while mature neurons cannot.","options":["Glial cells can still divide, while mature neurons cannot","Glial cells are larger than neurons, so they have more room to grow","Neurons are protected by intercalated discs that stop growth","Neurons are found only in nerves, not inside the brain"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"A tumor is a mass of cells dividing out of control, so it can only arise from cells that are still able to divide. Mature neurons have lost that ability, but neuroglia keep it. That is why tumors of brain tissue come from glial cells rather than from mature neurons.","notes":["Correct. Tumors come from dividing cells, and glia can divide while mature neurons cannot.","Glial cells are smaller than neurons, not larger. Size is not what lets a cell form a tumor anyway.","Intercalated discs are junctions between cardiac muscle cells, not structures in nervous tissue.","Neurons are found throughout the brain and spinal cord as well as in ganglia. Their cell bodies are plentiful inside the brain."],"comp":20,"competencyId":"w1-nervous-tissue-id"},{"id":"N-m1-muscle-nervous-tissue-521","dok":1,"q":"Which body membrane lines the cavities that open to the outside of the body, such as the digestive, respiratory, urinary, and reproductive tracts?","a":"The mucous membrane.","options":["The mucous membrane","The cutaneous membrane","The synovial membrane","The serous membrane"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Mucous membranes, also called mucosae, line the body cavities and tubes that open to the exterior. They are an epithelium resting on a layer of loose connective tissue, and their surface is kept moist, often by mucus. The lining of the mouth, stomach, trachea, and bladder are all mucous membranes.","notes":["Correct. Mucous membranes line the tracts that open to the exterior.","The cutaneous membrane is the skin, a dry membrane covering the outside of the body rather than lining an internal tract.","The synovial membrane lines the cavities of freely movable joints. Pick it for the knee or shoulder joint.","Serous membranes line closed cavities with no opening to the outside, such as the pleural, pericardial, and peritoneal cavities."],"comp":21,"competencyId":"w1-body-membranes"},{"id":"N-m1-muscle-nervous-tissue-522","dok":1,"q":"What does serous fluid do in the space between the parietal and visceral layers of a serous membrane?","a":"It reduces friction so organs can move against the body wall.","options":["It carries oxygen to the organs","It reduces friction as organs move","It traps dust and microbes","It waterproofs the body surface"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"A serous membrane is a double layer, with the parietal layer lining the body wall and the visceral layer covering the organ. The thin film of serous fluid between them acts as a lubricant, so the heart, lungs, and abdominal organs can slide against the wall as they beat, expand, and move without rubbing damage.","notes":["Carrying oxygen to organs is the job of red blood cells in the blood, not of serous fluid.","Correct. Serous fluid lubricates the layers so organs move with little friction.","Trapping dust and microbes is the job of mucus on mucous membranes, such as the lining of the airways.","Waterproofing the body surface is done by the keratinized cutaneous membrane, the skin."],"comp":21,"competencyId":"w1-body-membranes"},{"id":"N-m1-muscle-nervous-tissue-523","dok":1,"q":"In a mucous membrane, what is the lamina propria?","a":"The layer of loose connective tissue beneath the epithelium.","options":["The basement membrane between the epithelial cells","The loose connective tissue layer under the epithelium","The film of mucus that goblet cells spread over the epithelium","The single layer of flat cells on its surface"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"A mucous membrane has two parts: an epithelium on the surface and a layer of loose (areolar) connective tissue beneath it. That connective tissue layer is the lamina propria. It supports the epithelium and carries the blood vessels that the avascular epithelium depends on, and it appears on epithelial slides as one of the structures to identify when present.","notes":["The basement membrane sits below the epithelium, not between its cells, and it is a thin anchoring layer rather than a full layer of connective tissue.","Correct. The lamina propria is the loose connective tissue under the epithelium of a mucous membrane.","Mucus is a secretion on top of the epithelium, made by goblet cells. It is not a tissue layer.","A single layer of flat surface cells is simple squamous epithelium, which forms the surface of serous membranes rather than the lamina propria."],"comp":21,"competencyId":"w1-body-membranes"},{"id":"N-m1-muscle-nervous-tissue-524","dok":1,"q":"What does the synovial membrane secrete?","a":"Synovial fluid, which lubricates the joint.","options":["Sebum, which oils the skin and hair","Synovial fluid, which lubricates the joint","Serous fluid, which lubricates organs in a cavity","Mucus, which traps particles in the airway"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The synovial membrane lines the cavity of a freely movable joint, such as the knee or shoulder. It is made of connective tissue only, with no epithelium, and it secretes synovial fluid. This fluid lubricates the joint and nourishes the avascular cartilage covering the bone ends.","notes":["Sebum is secreted by sebaceous glands in the skin by the holocrine mode. Pick it for a question about oil on the skin and hair.","Correct. The synovial membrane secretes synovial fluid into the joint cavity.","Serous fluid is secreted by serous membranes such as the pleura and peritoneum. Pick it for the fluid around the lungs or abdominal organs.","Mucus is secreted by goblet cells and glands of mucous membranes. Pick it for the lining of the airway or gut."],"comp":21,"competencyId":"w1-body-membranes"},{"id":"N-m1-muscle-nervous-tissue-525","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares serous and mucous membranes?","a":"Serous membranes line closed cavities, while mucous membranes line cavities and tracts that open to the outside.","options":["Serous membranes line closed cavities, and mucous membranes line open tracts","Mucous membranes line closed cavities, and serous membranes line open tracts","Both line joint cavities and secrete synovial fluid","Both are dry membranes covered by keratinized epithelium"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The deciding feature is whether the space opens to the outside. Serous membranes, such as the pleura, pericardium, and peritoneum, line closed cavities and cover the organs inside them. Mucous membranes line the tracts that do open to the exterior, such as the digestive and respiratory tracts. Both are moist membranes.","notes":["Correct. Serous membranes line closed cavities and mucous membranes line tracts open to the outside.","This reverses the two. The pleural and peritoneal cavities are closed and lined by serous membranes, while the gut and airways are open tracts lined by mucous membranes.","Joint cavities are lined by the synovial membrane, which secretes synovial fluid. Neither serous nor mucous membranes do this.","A dry membrane with keratinized epithelium is the cutaneous membrane, the skin. Serous and mucous membranes are both moist."],"comp":21,"competencyId":"w1-body-membranes"},{"id":"N-m1-muscle-nervous-tissue-526","dok":3,"q":"A patient has an infection of the inner lining of the urinary bladder. Which type of body membrane is infected?","a":"A mucous membrane.","options":["A synovial membrane","The cutaneous membrane","A serous membrane","A mucous membrane"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The urinary tract opens to the outside of the body through the urethra, so the inner lining of the bladder is a mucous membrane. Its epithelium is transitional epithelium, resting on a lamina propria of connective tissue. Infections that enter from the outside travel up along this mucous lining.","notes":["The synovial membrane lines joint cavities. Pick it for an infected knee or shoulder joint.","The cutaneous membrane is the skin on the outside of the body. Pick it for a skin infection.","Serous membranes line closed cavities such as the peritoneal cavity. Pick a serous membrane if the question asked about the covering on the outside of an organ in that cavity, not the inner lining of a tract.","Correct. The bladder lining is a mucous membrane because the urinary tract opens to the exterior."],"comp":21,"competencyId":"w1-body-membranes"},{"id":"N-m1-muscle-nervous-tissue-527","dok":2,"q":"Rheumatoid arthritis attacks the membrane that lines the cavity of freely movable joints. Which membrane is it, and what is it made of?","a":"The synovial membrane, made of connective tissue with no epithelium.","options":["The serous membrane, made of simple squamous epithelium","The cutaneous membrane, made of keratinized epithelium","The mucous membrane, made of epithelium over loose connective tissue","The synovial membrane, made of connective tissue only"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Freely movable joints are lined by the synovial membrane, the one body membrane made of connective tissue alone, with no epithelial layer. It secretes synovial fluid into the joint cavity. In rheumatoid arthritis, the immune system attacks this membrane and the joint cartilage, starting with the small joints.","notes":["Serous membranes line closed body cavities such as the pleural and peritoneal cavities. Pick a serous membrane for pleurisy or peritonitis.","The cutaneous membrane is the skin. It covers the body surface and is not found inside joints.","Mucous membranes line tracts open to the exterior, such as the gut and airways. They do not line joints.","Correct. The synovial membrane lines joint cavities and is made of connective tissue only."],"comp":21,"competencyId":"w1-body-membranes"},{"id":"N-a17d-15-1","dok":1,"q":"Which germ layer is the middle layer of the early embryo?","a":"Mesoderm.","options":["Ectoderm.","Endoderm.","Mesoderm.","Epidermis."],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The early embryo forms three primary germ layers. Ectoderm is the outer layer, mesoderm the middle layer, and endoderm the inner layer. Every tissue in the body traces back to one of them.","notes":["Ectoderm is the outer germ layer. It forms the epidermis and all nervous tissue.","Endoderm is the inner germ layer. It forms the epithelial lining of the digestive and respiratory tracts.","Correct. Mesoderm is the middle germ layer, the source of muscle, bone, cartilage, and blood.","The epidermis is the outer layer of the skin, not a germ layer. It develops from the ectoderm."],"comp":15,"competencyId":"w1-germ-layers"},{"id":"N-a17d-15-2","dok":1,"q":"Melanocytes, the pigment cells of the epidermis, develop from which germ layer?","a":"Ectoderm.","options":["Mesoderm.","Endoderm.","Dermis.","Ectoderm."],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Melanocytes develop from the ectoderm of the embryo. The ectoderm is the outer germ layer, the same layer that forms the epidermis and all nervous tissue.","notes":["Mesoderm forms muscle, bone, cartilage, blood, and most connective tissue, not the pigment cells of the epidermis.","Endoderm forms the epithelial lining of the digestive and respiratory tracts and their glands, not skin pigment cells.","The dermis is the deep layer of the skin, not a germ layer. Melanocytes sit in the epidermis, above it.","Correct. Melanocytes develop from the ectoderm, the outer germ layer."],"comp":15,"competencyId":"w1-germ-layers"},{"id":"N-a17d-15-3","dok":3,"q":"A biopsy of the stomach shows simple columnar epithelium lining the inside, with a layer of smooth muscle deeper in the wall. Which germ layer formed each tissue?","a":"Endoderm formed the lining, and mesoderm formed the smooth muscle.","options":["Ectoderm formed the lining, and mesoderm formed the smooth muscle.","Mesoderm formed the lining, and endoderm formed the smooth muscle.","Endoderm formed both the lining and the smooth muscle.","Endoderm formed the lining, and mesoderm formed the smooth muscle."],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Endoderm, the inner germ layer, forms the epithelial lining of the digestive and respiratory tracts. Mesoderm, the middle layer, forms muscle, including the smooth muscle of the stomach wall. One organ wall can hold tissues from different germ layers.","notes":["Ectoderm forms the epidermis and nervous tissue, not the lining of the digestive tract. The stomach lining comes from endoderm.","These are swapped. The digestive lining comes from endoderm, and muscle comes from mesoderm.","Endoderm forms only the epithelial lining. The smooth muscle in the wall comes from mesoderm, the germ layer that forms muscle.","Correct. The digestive lining comes from endoderm, and muscle comes from mesoderm."],"comp":15,"competencyId":"w1-germ-layers"},{"id":"N-a17d-15-4","dok":3,"q":"A section of thick skin shows the epidermis, the dense irregular connective tissue of the dermis, and blood inside dermal vessels. Which statement about their germ layers is correct?","a":"The epidermis came from ectoderm, and the dermis and blood came from mesoderm.","options":["All three came from ectoderm, because they are all part of the skin.","The epidermis came from ectoderm, and the dermis and blood came from mesoderm.","The epidermis came from endoderm, and the dermis and blood came from mesoderm.","The epidermis and dermis came from ectoderm, and only the blood came from mesoderm."],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Ectoderm forms the epidermis. The dermis is connective tissue, and mesoderm forms blood and most connective tissue. So one organ, the skin, is built from two germ layers stacked on each other.","notes":["Being in the same organ does not mean the same germ layer. The dermis is connective tissue and the blood is a fluid connective tissue, and both come from mesoderm.","Correct. The epidermis is ectodermal, while the connective tissue of the dermis and the blood are mesodermal.","Endoderm forms the lining of the digestive and respiratory tracts, not the epidermis. The epidermis comes from ectoderm.","The dermis is dense irregular connective tissue, and most connective tissue comes from mesoderm, not ectoderm. Only the epidermis is ectodermal here."],"comp":15,"competencyId":"w1-germ-layers"},{"id":"N-a17d-15-5","dok":3,"q":"The trachea has an epithelial lining, rings of hyaline cartilage in its wall, and nervous tissue in the nerves that reach it. Which set matches each tissue to its germ layer?","a":"Lining from endoderm, cartilage from mesoderm, nervous tissue from ectoderm.","options":["Lining from ectoderm, cartilage from mesoderm, nervous tissue from endoderm.","Lining from mesoderm, cartilage from endoderm, nervous tissue from ectoderm.","Lining from endoderm, cartilage from ectoderm, nervous tissue from mesoderm.","Lining from endoderm, cartilage from mesoderm, nervous tissue from ectoderm."],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Endoderm forms the epithelial lining of the respiratory tract. Mesoderm forms cartilage along with bone, muscle, and blood. Ectoderm forms all nervous tissue. The trachea shows all three germ layers in one organ.","notes":["The lining and nervous tissue are swapped. The respiratory lining comes from endoderm, and nervous tissue comes from ectoderm.","Cartilage comes from mesoderm, not endoderm, and the airway lining comes from endoderm. Only the nerves are matched correctly here.","The lining is right, but cartilage comes from mesoderm and nervous tissue from ectoderm. Those two are swapped.","Correct. Airway lining is endodermal, cartilage is mesodermal, and nervous tissue is ectodermal."],"comp":15,"competencyId":"w1-germ-layers"},{"id":"N-a17d-19-1","dok":3,"q":"A sample from the wall of the small intestine shows muscle cells with no striations. Which cell shape and nucleus pattern should the slide show?","a":"Spindle-shaped cells, each with a single central nucleus.","options":["Spindle-shaped cells, each with a single central nucleus.","Long cylindrical fibers with many nuclei at the edges.","Branching cells, each with one central nucleus and dark crossbands.","Spindle-shaped cells, each with many nuclei at the edges."],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Muscle in the wall of a hollow organ such as the intestine, with no striations, is smooth muscle. Smooth muscle cells are spindle-shaped, nonstriated, and have one nucleus each. The location and the missing striations both point to smooth muscle, which tells you the shape and nuclei to look for.","notes":["Correct. Nonstriated muscle in a hollow organ wall is smooth muscle, with spindle cells and one nucleus each.","Long cylindrical fibers with many peripheral nuclei are skeletal muscle, which is striated and attached to bone.","Branching cells joined by dark crossbands are cardiac muscle, which is striated and found only in the heart wall.","The spindle shape is right, but smooth muscle cells are uninucleated. Many nuclei at the edges is the skeletal muscle pattern."],"comp":19,"competencyId":"w1-muscle-tissue-id"},{"id":"N-a17d-19-2","dok":3,"q":"A slide shows long cylindrical striated fibers, each with many nuclei pressed against the edges. Where was this sample most likely taken, and is it under voluntary control?","a":"From a muscle attached to bone, and it is voluntary.","options":["From the heart wall, and it is involuntary.","From a muscle attached to bone, and it is voluntary.","From the wall of the bladder, and it is involuntary.","From a muscle attached to bone, and it is involuntary."],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Long, cylindrical, striated fibers with many peripheral nuclei are skeletal muscle. Skeletal muscle is attached to bones and occasionally skin, and it carries out voluntary movement.","notes":["Heart muscle is striated, but its cells branch and have one central nucleus. Many edge nuclei rule out cardiac muscle.","Correct. These features identify skeletal muscle, which attaches to bone and is voluntary.","The bladder wall has smooth muscle, which has no striations and one nucleus per cell.","The location is right, but skeletal muscle is voluntary. Cardiac and smooth muscle are the involuntary types."],"comp":19,"competencyId":"w1-muscle-tissue-id"},{"id":"N-a17d-20-1","dok":3,"q":"On a multipolar neuron slide, a student finds the one spot where a single long process leaves the cell body. Which part of the neuron is that spot, and which process does it lead into?","a":"The axon hillock, leading into the axon.","options":["The synaptic bulb, leading into a dendrite.","The axon hillock, leading into the axon.","The axon hillock, leading into a dendrite.","The nucleolus, leading into the axon."],"correctIndex":1,"why":"A multipolar neuron has many dendrites and only one axon. The axon leaves the cell body at a region called the axon hillock. Finding the hillock tells you which process is the axon, the one that carries the impulse away from the cell body.","notes":["The synaptic bulb is at the far end of the axon, not where a process leaves the cell body. Dendrites are the branching processes that receive signals.","Correct. The axon hillock is where the single axon leaves the cell body.","The hillock is right, but it leads into the axon. Dendrites are the many branching processes that receive signals and carry them toward the cell body.","The nucleolus is the dark spot inside the nucleus, in the middle of the cell body. It does not form part of any process."],"comp":20,"competencyId":"w1-nervous-tissue-id"},{"id":"N-a17d-20-2","dok":3,"q":"A student views a large neuron cell body with a nucleus that contains one dark dot, surrounded by many small, separate nuclei. She labels the dark dot a glial cell inside the neuron. What is the dark dot, and what are the glia?","a":"The dot is the nucleolus; the glia are the small nuclei around the neuron.","options":["The dot is the axon hillock; the glia are the branching dendrites.","The dot is a glial cell; the small nuclei around it are other neurons.","The dot is the nucleolus; the glia are the small nuclei around the neuron.","The dot is the synaptic bulb; the glia are the nucleoli of the neuron."],"correctIndex":2,"why":"A neuron's large nucleus holds a dark nucleolus, both structures to identify on the multipolar neuron slide. Neuroglia are the supporting cells of nervous tissue, and on a slide they appear as many small nuclei scattered around the neurons. Glia sit beside neurons, not inside them.","notes":["The axon hillock is at the base of the axon, outside the nucleus. Dendrites are parts of the neuron, not glial cells.","Glia do not sit inside a neuron's nucleus. The small, numerous nuclei around the large cell are the neuroglia.","Correct. The dark dot is the nucleolus, and the many small nuclei around the neuron are neuroglia.","The synaptic bulb is at the end of the axon, far from the nucleus. Nucleoli are part of nuclei, not separate glial cells."],"comp":20,"competencyId":"w1-nervous-tissue-id"},{"id":"N-a17d-21-1","dok":3,"q":"During abdominal surgery, a surgeon examines the membrane covering the outer surface of the stomach and then the membrane lining the inside of the stomach. Which type of body membrane is each?","a":"Serous on the outer surface, and mucous on the inner lining.","options":["Mucous on the outer surface, and serous on the inner lining.","Serous on both surfaces, since both sit inside the abdominopelvic cavity.","Serous on the outer surface, and mucous on the inner lining.","Synovial on the outer surface, and mucous on the inner lining."],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The outer surface of the stomach faces the closed peritoneal cavity, so it is covered by the visceral peritoneum, a serous membrane. The inside of the stomach is part of the digestive tract, which opens to the outside of the body, so it is lined by a mucous membrane, an epithelium over loose connective tissue that is kept moist. One organ can carry a different membrane on each surface.","notes":["These are reversed. Mucous membranes line tracts that open to the outside, such as the inside of the stomach, and serous membranes cover organs in closed cavities.","The outer surface is serous, but the inside of the stomach is part of the digestive tract, which opens to the outside. Tracts that open to the outside are lined by mucous membrane.","Correct. The visceral peritoneum on the outside is serous, and the digestive lining inside is mucous.","The inner lining is right, but synovial membrane lines joint cavities, not organs. The outer surface of the stomach is covered by serous membrane, the visceral peritoneum."],"comp":21,"competencyId":"w1-body-membranes"},{"id":"N-a17d-21-2","dok":3,"q":"One patient burns the skin of the forearm, and another burns the lining of the mouth. Which membrane is each, and what difference in makeup separates them?","a":"Cutaneous, a dry keratinized epithelium, and mucous, a moist epithelium on loose connective tissue.","options":["Mucous, a dry keratinized epithelium, and cutaneous, a moist epithelium on loose connective tissue.","Cutaneous, a dry keratinized epithelium, and serous, a simple squamous epithelium in a closed cavity.","Cutaneous, a dry keratinized epithelium, and mucous, a moist epithelium on loose connective tissue.","Serous, a moist epithelium on loose connective tissue, and synovial, connective tissue only."],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The skin is the cutaneous membrane, a keratinized epithelium over dense connective tissue, and it is dry. The mouth opens to the outside, so it is lined by a mucous membrane, an epithelium over loose connective tissue that is kept moist.","notes":["These are reversed. The dry keratinized membrane is the skin, and the moist one lining the mouth is the mucous membrane.","The skin is right, but the mouth opens to the outside, so it has a mucous membrane. Serous membranes line closed ventral cavities.","Correct. Skin is the dry cutaneous membrane, and the mouth has a moist mucous membrane.","The forearm skin is the cutaneous membrane, not a serous membrane. The synovial membrane lines joint cavities, not the mouth."],"comp":21,"competencyId":"w1-body-membranes"}]},{"id":"m1-integumentary","title":"Integumentary system","summary":"epidermis layers, thick vs thin skin, dermis, hypodermis, hair, glands, nails","mw":"2026-09-02","tr":"2026-09-03","cards":[{"id":"L00334","dok":1,"q":"Which two layers make up the skin itself?","a":"The epidermis and the dermis.","options":["The epidermis and the hypodermis","The papillary layer and the reticular layer","The epidermis and the dermis","The dermis and the hypodermis"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The skin proper has two layers: the epidermis, a thin superficial layer of epithelium, and the dermis, a thicker connective tissue layer beneath it. The hypodermis (subcutaneous layer) lies deep to the dermis and anchors the skin to underlying structures, but it is not counted as part of the skin. Keeping this boundary in mind helps when you later sort injection depths and burn depths.","notes":["The epidermis is part of the skin, but the hypodermis is the fatty layer beneath the skin and is not part of it. Pick this pairing only if the question asked for the outermost and innermost layers a needle crosses before reaching muscle.","The papillary and reticular layers are the two layers of the dermis only. They would be the answer if the question asked for the two layers of the dermis, not of the whole skin.","Correct. The epidermis on top and the dermis beneath it are the two layers of the skin proper.","The dermis is part of the skin, but the hypodermis lies below the skin. This pairing would fit a question asking which two layers sit deep to the epidermis."],"lt":"t-integumentary","lid":"c1","comp":22,"competencyId":"w1-skin-layers"},{"id":"L00335","dok":1,"q":"Which layer of the skin is the superficial layer?","a":"The epidermis.","options":["The reticular layer","The hypodermis","The dermis","The epidermis"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The epidermis is the superficial (surface) layer of the skin. It is made of keratinized stratified squamous epithelium and has no blood vessels of its own. The dermis lies directly beneath it, and the hypodermis lies deeper still, below the skin proper.","notes":["The reticular layer is the deep part of the dermis, so it is one of the deepest parts of the skin. Choose it if the question asked which dermal layer lies closest to the hypodermis.","The hypodermis is the fatty subcutaneous layer below the skin, not a layer of the skin itself. It would be the answer for the layer that anchors the skin and stores fat.","The dermis is the deeper of the two skin layers, made of connective tissue that holds vessels, nerves, and glands. Pick it if the question asked for the deep layer of the skin.","Correct. The epidermis forms the outer surface of the skin."],"lt":"t-integumentary","lid":"c2","comp":22,"competencyId":"w1-skin-layers"},{"id":"L00336","dok":1,"q":"Which tissue makes up the epidermis?","a":"Keratinized stratified squamous epithelium.","options":["Areolar connective tissue","Adipose tissue","Keratinized stratified squamous epithelium","Dense irregular connective tissue"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The epidermis is keratinized stratified squamous epithelium. Stratified means many layers of cells, squamous means the surface cells are flat, and keratinized means those surface cells are dead and packed with the tough protein keratin. This build lets the epidermis take constant wear while the deeper cells keep dividing to replace what is lost.","notes":["Areolar connective tissue is the loose tissue of the papillary layer of the dermis. Pick it if the question asked what tissue forms the superficial dermis.","Adipose tissue is fat, and it makes up most of the hypodermis beneath the skin. Choose it if the question asked about the subcutaneous layer.","Correct. The epidermis is a layered epithelium whose flat surface cells are filled with keratin.","Dense irregular connective tissue makes up the reticular layer, the deep bulk of the dermis. It would be the answer for the tissue that gives the skin its strength."],"lt":"t-integumentary","lid":"c3","comp":22,"competencyId":"w1-skin-layers"},{"id":"L00337","dok":1,"q":"Which layer lies beneath the skin and is made mostly of adipose tissue?","a":"The hypodermis.","options":["The hypodermis","The reticular layer","The deep fascia","The dermis"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The hypodermis, also called the subcutaneous layer, lies deep to the dermis and is made of adipose and areolar connective tissue. It stores fat and anchors the skin to the fascia around muscles and bones. Although it sits right under the skin, it is not part of the skin itself.","notes":["Correct. The hypodermis is the fatty subcutaneous layer deep to the dermis.","The reticular layer is the deep part of the dermis, made of dense irregular connective tissue with thick collagen bundles. It would be right for the dermal layer that gives skin its strength, not the fatty layer below the skin.","The deep fascia is a sheet of dense connective tissue wrapping muscles, deep to the hypodermis. Choose it if the question asked what the hypodermis anchors the skin to.","The dermis is the deep layer of the skin proper, made mostly of dense irregular connective tissue rather than fat. Pick it if the question asked which skin layer holds the vessels, nerves, and glands."],"lt":"t-integumentary","lid":"c4","comp":22,"competencyId":"w1-skin-layers"},{"id":"L00338","dok":1,"q":"Which stratum of the epidermis is the deepest?","a":"The stratum basale.","options":["The stratum corneum","The stratum granulosum","The stratum basale","The stratum spinosum"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The stratum basale is the deepest stratum, a single row of cells resting on the basement membrane right above the dermis. These cells divide to produce new keratinocytes, which are pushed upward through the other strata. The course mnemonic Baby Screams Grandpa Loves Coffee lists the strata from deep to superficial: basale, spinosum, granulosum, lucidum, corneum.","notes":["The stratum corneum is the most superficial stratum, made of flat dead keratinized cells. Pick it if the question asked which stratum forms the surface.","The stratum granulosum sits in the middle of the epidermis, above the spinosum. It would be the answer if the question asked which stratum lies just deep to the lucidum in thick skin.","Correct. The stratum basale is the deepest stratum and the site of cell division.","The stratum spinosum lies directly on top of the basale, so students often mistake it for the deepest layer. Choose it if the question asked which stratum is just superficial to the basale."],"lt":"t-integumentary","lid":"c5","comp":23,"competencyId":"w1-epidermal-strata"},{"id":"L00339","dok":1,"q":"Which stratum forms the surface of the epidermis?","a":"The stratum corneum.","options":["The stratum lucidum","The stratum granulosum","The stratum basale","The stratum corneum"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The stratum corneum is the outermost stratum, made of many layers of flat, dead cells filled with keratin. These cells form a tough, water-resistant barrier and are constantly shed and replaced from below. In thick skin the corneum is much thicker, which is the main reason thick skin is thick.","notes":["The stratum lucidum is a thin, clear layer just deep to the corneum, found only in thick skin. It would be the answer if the question asked which layer sits right under the surface layer on the palms and soles.","The stratum granulosum lies in the middle of the epidermis, where cells begin to die and fill with keratin. Choose it if the question asked which stratum lies just under the corneum in thin skin.","The stratum basale is the deepest stratum, where new cells are made. Pick it if the question asked where keratinocytes are born.","Correct. The stratum corneum is the dead, keratinized surface layer."],"lt":"t-integumentary","lid":"c6","comp":23,"competencyId":"w1-epidermal-strata"},{"id":"L00340","dok":1,"q":"Which epidermal cell produces keratin, the tough protective protein of the epidermis?","a":"The keratinocyte.","options":["The intraepidermal macrophage (Langerhans cell)","The keratinocyte","The melanocyte","The tactile (Merkel) cell"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Keratinocytes make up about 90 percent of epidermal cells and produce keratin, a tough fibrous protein that protects the skin from abrasion, heat, microbes, and chemicals. They also make lamellar granules, which release a water-repellent sealant. As keratinocytes move toward the surface they fill with keratin and die, forming the stratum corneum.","notes":["Intraepidermal macrophages (Langerhans cells) come from red bone marrow and take part in immune responses against microbes in the skin. Choose them if the question asked about immune defense in the epidermis.","Correct. Keratinocytes are the most numerous epidermal cells and they make keratin.","Melanocytes produce melanin, the pigment that colors skin and absorbs UV light. Pick them if the question asked about pigment.","Tactile (Merkel) cells sit in the deepest layer of the epidermis and contact a sensory nerve ending for touch. They would be the answer for the touch-related epidermal cell."],"lt":"t-integumentary","lid":"c7","comp":23,"competencyId":"w1-epidermal-strata"},{"id":"L00341","dok":1,"q":"Which epidermal cell produces melanin?","a":"The melanocyte.","options":["The tactile (Merkel) cell","The intraepidermal macrophage (Langerhans cell)","The keratinocyte","The melanocyte"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Melanocytes make melanin, a yellow-red or brown-black pigment that contributes to skin color and absorbs damaging UV light. They have long slender projections that pass melanin granules to nearby keratinocytes. Inside the keratinocytes, the melanin gathers over the nucleus and shields the DNA.","notes":["Tactile (Merkel) cells contact a sensory nerve ending to detect touch. Choose them if the question asked which epidermal cell takes part in sensation.","Intraepidermal macrophages (Langerhans cells) are immune cells that help the body recognize invading microbes. They would be the answer for an immune question, not a pigment question.","Keratinocytes receive melanin from melanocytes but do not make it; they make keratin. Pick them if the question asked which cell produces keratin or stores the transferred pigment.","Correct. Melanocytes produce melanin and transfer it to keratinocytes."],"lt":"t-integumentary","lid":"c8","comp":23,"competencyId":"w1-epidermal-strata"},{"id":"L00342","dok":1,"q":"Which layer of the dermis is the superficial layer?","a":"The papillary layer.","options":["The papillary layer","The hypodermis","The reticular layer","The stratum basale"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The dermis has two layers. The papillary layer is the thin superficial layer, about one fifth of the dermis, and it pushes up into the epidermis as dermal papillae. The reticular layer is the thicker deep layer that makes up about four fifths of the dermis.","notes":["Correct. The papillary layer is the superficial part of the dermis, just under the epidermis.","The hypodermis lies beneath the dermis and is not part of the skin. Choose it if the question asked for the fatty layer under the skin.","The reticular layer is the deep, thick layer of the dermis made of dense irregular connective tissue. Pick it if the question asked for the deep dermal layer.","The stratum basale is the deepest stratum of the epidermis, sitting just above the papillary layer. It would be the answer for the deepest epidermal layer, not a dermal layer."],"lt":"t-integumentary","lid":"c9","comp":24,"competencyId":"w1-dermis-layers"},{"id":"L00343","dok":1,"q":"What is the onion-shaped, expanded base of a hair follicle called?","a":"The hair bulb.","options":["The hair root","The hair bulb","The papilla of the hair","The hair shaft"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The hair bulb is the onion-shaped expansion at the base of each follicle. It holds the hair matrix, a layer of dividing cells that grows the hair, and it surrounds the papilla of the hair, a small knob of connective tissue with blood vessels that nourish the matrix. Damage to the bulb stops hair growth from that follicle.","notes":["The hair root is the part of the hair buried in the dermis inside the follicle. It would be the answer for the part of the hair below the skin surface, not the expanded base.","Correct. The hair bulb is the swollen base of the follicle where growth happens.","The papilla of the hair is the knob of areolar connective tissue and blood vessels that sits inside the bulb. Choose it if the question asked what supplies blood to the growing hair.","The hair shaft is the part of the hair that projects above the skin surface. Pick it if the question asked which part of the hair you can see."],"lt":"t-integumentary","lid":"c10","comp":25,"competencyId":"w1-skin-accessory"},{"id":"L00344","dok":1,"q":"Which part of a nail contains the dividing cells that produce new nail?","a":"The nail matrix.","options":["The lunula","The nail matrix","The hyponychium","The nail body"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The nail matrix is epithelium at the proximal end of the nail, near the nail root, and it contains dividing cells that produce new nail cells. As new cells form, the nail is pushed forward over the fingertip. Damage to the matrix can permanently change or stop nail growth.","notes":["The lunula is the whitish crescent at the proximal end of the nail body, pale because the epithelium there is thickened. Students confuse it with the matrix because it lies close to it, but the lunula is part of the visible nail.","Correct. The nail matrix is the growth region where new nail cells are made.","The hyponychium is thickened stratum corneum beneath the free edge that secures the nail to the fingertip. It would be the answer for the structure under the free edge.","The nail body is the visible plate of hard keratin. Pick it if the question asked which part of the nail you can see and paint."],"lt":"t-integumentary","lid":"c11","comp":25,"competencyId":"w1-skin-accessory"},{"id":"L00345","dok":1,"q":"Which gland secretes sebum, an oily secretion?","a":"The sebaceous gland.","options":["The eccrine sweat gland","The apocrine sweat gland","The sebaceous gland","The ceruminous gland"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Sebaceous glands are the oil glands of the skin. Their secretory portion sits in the dermis, and most of their ducts open into a hair follicle, so the sebum coats the hair and skin. Sebum is a mixture of triglycerides, cholesterol, proteins, and salts that helps keep hair and skin from drying out.","notes":["Eccrine sweat glands release watery sweat directly onto the skin surface to help cool the body. Pick them if the question asked about the most numerous sweat glands.","Apocrine sweat glands release a thicker sweat with lipids and proteins into hair follicles of the axillae and groin. Choose them if the question asked about sweat glands that become active at puberty.","Correct. Sebaceous glands secrete oily sebum, mostly into hair follicles.","Ceruminous glands secrete cerumen (earwax) into the external auditory canal. They would be the answer for a question about the ear canal."],"lt":"t-integumentary","lid":"c12","comp":25,"competencyId":"w1-skin-accessory"},{"id":"L00346","dok":1,"q":"Which organ is the largest in the body by weight?","a":"The skin.","options":["The brain","The skin","The lungs","The liver"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The skin is the largest organ in the body by weight. In an adult it covers roughly 22 square feet and weighs about 10 to 11 pounds, about 7 percent of total body weight. It counts as an organ because it combines all four tissue types working together.","notes":["The brain is a large organ protected by the skull, but it weighs far less than the skin. Students sometimes pick it because it is the most complex organ, which is a different idea from the largest.","Correct. The skin outweighs every other organ.","The lungs fill much of the thoracic cavity, but they are light because they are mostly air spaces. Choose them if the question asked which organs fill most of the thoracic cavity.","The liver is the largest internal organ and the heaviest gland, but it weighs much less than the skin. Pick it if the question asked for the largest organ inside the abdominal cavity."],"lt":"t-integumentary","lid":"c13","comp":22,"competencyId":"w1-skin-layers"},{"id":"L00347","dok":1,"q":"Which layer of the skin holds the blood vessels, nerves, and glands?","a":"The dermis.","options":["The stratum granulosum","The epidermis","The stratum basale","The dermis"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The dermis is the connective tissue layer of the skin, and it contains blood vessels, nerves, glands, and hair follicles. Because the epidermis has no blood vessels of its own, it depends on diffusion from the dermis for nutrients. That is why the dermis is described as essential to the survival of the epidermis.","notes":["The stratum granulosum is a middle epidermal stratum where keratinocytes start to die. Choose it if the question asked which stratum lies just deep to the corneum in thin skin.","The epidermis is avascular, meaning it has no blood vessels, and its glands only reach into it from below. Pick it if the question asked which skin layer is made of epithelium.","The stratum basale is the deepest epidermal stratum, the row of dividing cells on the basement membrane. It would be the answer for the layer that produces new keratinocytes, but like the rest of the epidermis it has no vessels.","Correct. The dermis contains the skin's vessels, nerves, and glands."],"lt":"t-integumentary","lid":"c14","comp":22,"competencyId":"w1-skin-layers"},{"id":"L00348","dok":2,"q":"A keratinocyte is born in the deepest stratum and is pushed toward the surface. Which stratum does it reach last?","a":"The stratum corneum.","options":["The stratum granulosum","The stratum spinosum","The stratum lucidum","The stratum corneum"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Keratinocytes are made in the stratum basale and are pushed upward as new cells form beneath them. As they rise they fill with keratin and die, and they end up in the stratum corneum at the surface, where they are eventually shed. This is true in both thick and thin skin, because the corneum is always the outermost stratum.","notes":["The stratum granulosum is a middle stratum where cells begin to die. It would be the answer for the last stratum reached before the corneum in thin skin.","The stratum spinosum is the second stratum, directly above the basale, so the cell passes through it early. Pick it if the question asked which stratum the cell enters first after leaving the basale.","The stratum lucidum lies just deep to the corneum, and only in thick skin. Choose it if the question asked for the next-to-last stratum a cell passes through on the palm or sole.","Correct. The stratum corneum is the surface layer and the final stop before the cell is shed."],"lt":"t-integumentary","lid":"c15","comp":23,"competencyId":"w1-epidermal-strata"},{"id":"L00349","dok":1,"q":"Thick skin of the palms and soles has five epidermal strata. Which stratum is essentially missing in thin skin?","a":"The stratum lucidum.","options":["The stratum corneum","The stratum spinosum","The stratum lucidum","The stratum basale"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Thin skin covers most of the body and has four strata: basale, spinosum, granulosum, and corneum. Thick skin of the palms, soles, and palmar surfaces of the digits adds a fifth, the stratum lucidum, a thin clear band between the granulosum and the corneum. These are the areas of greatest friction, which is why they carry the extra layer.","notes":["The stratum corneum is present in all skin as the dead surface layer, although it is thicker in thick skin. It would be the answer if the question asked which stratum makes the biggest contribution to thick skin's thickness.","The stratum spinosum is present in all skin, directly above the basale. Choose it if the question asked which stratum lies just superficial to the basale.","Correct. The stratum lucidum is found in thick skin and is essentially absent in thin skin.","The stratum basale is present in all skin because it holds the dividing cells that renew the epidermis. Pick it if the question asked which stratum is deepest."],"lt":"t-integumentary","lid":"c16","comp":23,"competencyId":"w1-epidermal-strata"},{"id":"L00350","dok":1,"q":"Which epidermal cell contacts a sensory nerve ending to detect touch?","a":"The tactile (Merkel) cell.","options":["The tactile (Merkel) cell","The keratinocyte","The intraepidermal macrophage (Langerhans cell)","The melanocyte"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Tactile cells, also called Merkel cells, are the least numerous epidermal cells and sit in the deepest layer of the epidermis. Each one contacts the flattened ending of a sensory neuron, called a tactile disc or Merkel disc. Together they detect light touch.","notes":["Correct. Tactile (Merkel) cells pair with a sensory nerve ending to sense touch.","Keratinocytes make keratin and form most of the epidermis, but they are not touch receptors. Pick them if the question asked which cell makes up about 90 percent of the epidermis.","Intraepidermal macrophages (Langerhans cells) are immune cells that help recognize microbes entering the skin. Choose them if the question asked about immune defense in the epidermis.","Melanocytes make melanin, the pigment that shields DNA from UV light. They would be the answer for a question about skin color."],"lt":"t-integumentary","lid":"c17","comp":23,"competencyId":"w1-epidermal-strata"},{"id":"L00351","dok":1,"q":"Which dermal layer forms the dermal papillae, the finger-like projections that push up into the epidermis?","a":"The papillary layer.","options":["The papillary layer","The hypodermis","The reticular layer","The stratum granulosum"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The papillary layer is the superficial dermis, and its upper surface rises into small finger-shaped projections called dermal papillae. The papillae increase the contact area between dermis and epidermis, which helps anchor and feed the epidermis. On the palms, soles, and fingertips they are arranged in rows that raise the epidermal ridges we see as fingerprints.","notes":["Correct. The papillary layer forms the dermal papillae.","The hypodermis lies beneath the skin and has no papillae. Choose it if the question asked which layer stores fat beneath the skin.","The reticular layer is the deep dermis with thick collagen bundles, and it does not form papillae. Pick it if the question asked which dermal layer holds the hair follicles and sweat glands.","The stratum granulosum is a middle layer of the epidermis, not the dermis. It would be the answer for the stratum where keratinocytes begin to die."],"lt":"t-integumentary","lid":"c18","comp":24,"competencyId":"w1-dermis-layers"},{"id":"L00352","dok":1,"q":"Which dermal layer is made of dense irregular connective tissue and holds the hair follicles, sweat glands, and sebaceous glands?","a":"The reticular layer.","options":["The hypodermis","The stratum basale","The reticular layer","The papillary layer"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The reticular layer is the deep four fifths of the dermis. It is dense irregular connective tissue, meaning thick collagen bundles running in many directions, which gives the skin its strength and resistance to stretching. It also holds the larger blood vessels, nerves, hair follicles, sweat glands, and sebaceous glands.","notes":["The hypodermis is the subcutaneous layer of adipose and areolar tissue below the skin. Choose it if the question asked where lamellated corpuscles and fat storage are found.","The stratum basale is epithelium, the deepest layer of the epidermis, not connective tissue. It would be the answer for where new keratinocytes are made.","Correct. The reticular layer is dense irregular connective tissue that houses the follicles and glands.","The papillary layer is the thin superficial dermis made of areolar connective tissue. Pick it if the question asked which layer contains the capillary loops and Meissner corpuscles."],"lt":"t-integumentary","lid":"c19","comp":24,"competencyId":"w1-dermis-layers"},{"id":"L00353","dok":1,"q":"Which small muscle attaches to a hair follicle and pulls the hair upright, producing goose bumps?","a":"The arrector pili.","options":["The arrector pili","The hair bulb","The hair root plexus","The dermal root sheath"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The arrector pili is a small band of smooth muscle that runs from the dermis to the side of a hair follicle. When it contracts, it pulls the hair shaft perpendicular to the skin surface and dimples the skin around it, producing goose bumps. Because it is smooth muscle, the contraction is involuntary.","notes":["Correct. The arrector pili is the smooth muscle that stands the hair up.","The hair bulb is the onion-shaped base of the follicle where the hair grows. It would be the answer for the region that contains the hair matrix.","The hair root plexus is a network of sensory nerve endings wrapped around each follicle that detects light touch when the hair moves. Pick it if the question asked how a hair senses being brushed.","The dermal root sheath is the dermis that surrounds the follicle like a sleeve. Choose it if the question asked which connective tissue wraps the follicle."],"lt":"t-integumentary","lid":"c20","comp":25,"competencyId":"w1-skin-accessory"},{"id":"L00354","dok":1,"q":"Which sweat gland is the most numerous and opens directly onto the skin surface?","a":"The eccrine sweat gland.","options":["The sebaceous gland","The apocrine sweat gland","The ceruminous gland","The eccrine sweat gland"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Eccrine sweat glands are found throughout most of the body, especially on the forehead, palms, and soles. Their coiled secretory part sits mostly in the deep dermis, and their duct runs up through the epidermis to open on the skin surface. They release watery sweat that cools the body as it evaporates.","notes":["Sebaceous glands are oil glands, not sweat glands, and most open into hair follicles. They would be the answer for a question about sebum.","Apocrine sweat glands are found in the axillae, groin, and areolae, and their ducts open into hair follicles rather than onto the surface. Pick them if the question asked about sweat glands that empty into follicles.","Ceruminous glands are modified sweat glands of the external ear canal that make earwax. Choose them if the question asked which glands open into the external auditory canal.","Correct. Eccrine glands are the most numerous sweat glands and open onto the skin surface."],"lt":"t-integumentary","lid":"c21","comp":25,"competencyId":"w1-skin-accessory"},{"id":"L00355","dok":1,"q":"The cuticle of a nail is also known by what other name?","a":"The eponychium.","options":["The hyponychium","The lunula","The nail matrix","The eponychium"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The cuticle is the eponychium, a narrow band of epidermis that extends from the nail wall and adheres to the proximal margin of the nail. It is made of stratum corneum. The prefix epi means upon, which helps separate it from the hyponychium (hypo means below), the thickened layer beneath the free edge.","notes":["The hyponychium is the thickened stratum corneum beneath the free edge that secures the nail to the fingertip. The look-alike name is the trap; choose it if the question asked about the tissue under the free edge.","The lunula is the whitish crescent at the proximal end of the nail body. Pick it if the question asked about the little moon shape on the nail.","The nail matrix is the epithelium proximal to the nail root where new nail cells are made. It would be the answer for the growth region of the nail.","Correct. The eponychium is the cuticle."],"lt":"t-integumentary","lid":"c22","comp":25,"competencyId":"w1-skin-accessory"},{"id":"L00356","dok":1,"q":"Which gland secretes cerumen (earwax) into the external ear canal?","a":"The ceruminous gland.","options":["The ceruminous gland","The mammary gland","The sebaceous gland","The apocrine sweat gland"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Ceruminous glands are modified sweat glands found only in the external auditory canal. They secrete cerumen, a waxy material, onto the surface of the canal or into the ducts of nearby sebaceous glands. Cerumen helps trap dust and debris before it reaches the eardrum.","notes":["Correct. Ceruminous glands produce cerumen in the external ear canal.","Mammary glands are also modified sweat glands, but they lie in the breasts and produce milk. Pick them if the question asked which modified sweat gland produces milk.","Sebaceous glands make oily sebum, and in the ear canal their ducts can receive cerumen, but they do not make it. Choose them if the question asked which glands secrete oil onto the hair.","Apocrine sweat glands release a thick sweat into hair follicles of the axillae and groin. They would be the answer for sweat glands that become active at puberty."],"lt":"t-integumentary","lid":"c23","comp":25,"competencyId":"w1-skin-accessory"},{"id":"L00357","dok":2,"q":"Ceruminous glands and mammary glands are both described as modified versions of which gland type?","a":"Sweat glands.","options":["Salivary glands","Sweat glands","Sebaceous glands","Tarsal glands"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Ceruminous glands of the ear canal and mammary glands of the breast are both modified sweat glands. Each keeps the basic plan of a sweat gland, a secretory portion with a duct, but makes a specialized product: cerumen in the ear and milk in the breast. Recognizing them as sweat gland relatives helps you group the skin glands into oil glands and sweat glands.","notes":["Salivary glands are digestive glands around the mouth, not skin glands. They would be the answer for a question about glands that release saliva into the oral cavity.","Correct. Both ceruminous and mammary glands are modified sweat glands.","Sebaceous glands are the oil glands of the skin, a separate family from sweat glands. Pick them if the question asked which glands secrete sebum into hair follicles.","Tarsal glands are oil glands in the eyelids and are themselves a form of sebaceous gland. Choose them if the question asked which sebaceous glands lie along the eyelid margins."],"lt":"t-integumentary","lid":"c24","comp":25,"competencyId":"w1-skin-accessory"},{"id":"L00358","dok":1,"q":"Which accessory structure of the skin is a plate of hard, dead keratinized cells covering the dorsal surface of the tip of a finger or toe?","a":"The nail.","options":["A hair","A sebaceous gland","A hair follicle","A nail"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Nails are plates of tightly packed, hard, dead keratinized epidermal cells covering the dorsal surfaces of the distal parts of the digits. The nail body is comparable to the stratum corneum, except its cells hold a harder keratin and are not shed. Nails protect the fingertips and give counter pressure that helps us grasp and handle small objects.","notes":["A hair is also made of dead keratinized cells, but it forms a thin strand rather than a flat plate. Pick it if the question asked which keratinized structure grows from a follicle.","A sebaceous gland is an oil gland that usually empties into a hair follicle. Choose it if the question asked which accessory structure secretes sebum.","A hair follicle is the sheath of epidermal and dermal tissue that surrounds the hair root. It would be the answer for the structure that holds a hair in the skin.","Correct. A nail is a flat plate of hard keratin on the dorsal fingertip or toe tip."],"lt":"t-integumentary","lid":"c25","comp":25,"competencyId":"w1-skin-accessory"},{"id":"L00359","dok":2,"q":"Hair follicles, nails, and skin glands all develop from which layer of the skin, even though most of them reach down into the dermis?","a":"The epidermis.","options":["The papillary layer","The hypodermis","The epidermis","The dermis"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The accessory structures of the skin form as downgrowths of the epidermis. A hair follicle, for example, has an external root sheath that is a downward continuation of the epidermis, and the hair matrix arises from the stratum basale. Because follicles and glands are lined with epidermal cells living deep in the dermis, they can help regrow the epidermis after a partial-thickness burn.","notes":["The papillary layer is the superficial dermis with capillary loops and touch receptors. Choose it if the question asked which layer forms the dermal papillae.","The hypodermis is the fatty layer beneath the skin, and some gland coils reach into it, but the structures do not form there. It would be the answer for where lamellated corpuscles and fat storage are found.","Correct. Hair, nails, and skin glands are epidermal in origin.","The dermis surrounds and supports follicles and glands, but it does not produce them. Pick it if the question asked which layer houses most of the follicles and glands."],"lt":"t-integumentary","lid":"c26","comp":25,"competencyId":"w1-skin-accessory"},{"id":"L00360","dok":1,"q":"Besides the skin itself, which structures belong to the integumentary system?","a":"Hair, nails, skin glands, and sensory receptors.","options":["The deep fascia and the periosteum","Skeletal muscles and their tendons","Superficial lymph nodes and lymphatic vessels","Hair, nails, skin glands, and sensory receptors"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The integumentary system is the skin (the cutaneous membrane) plus its associated structures: hair, oil glands, sweat glands, sensory receptors, and nails. These structures work with the skin to protect the body, regulate temperature, and sense the environment. Nearby tissues such as muscle, fascia, and lymph nodes belong to other systems.","notes":["The deep fascia wraps muscles and the periosteum covers bones, so both lie deep to the skin. Choose them if the question asked what the hypodermis anchors the skin to.","Skeletal muscles and tendons belong to the muscular system and lie deep to the hypodermis. Pick them if the question asked which system moves the skeleton.","Superficial lymph nodes and lymphatic vessels lie beneath the skin, but they belong to the lymphatic system. They would be the answer for a question about structures that filter lymph.","Correct. Hair, nails, glands, and sensory receptors are the associated structures of the integumentary system."],"lt":"t-integumentary","lid":"c27","comp":25,"competencyId":"w1-skin-accessory"},{"id":"L00361","dok":1,"q":"What is the part of a hair that lies within the follicle, beneath the skin surface, called?","a":"The hair root.","options":["The hair follicle","The hair bulb","The hair shaft","The hair root"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"A hair has two parts. The hair root is the portion that penetrates into the dermis, and sometimes the subcutaneous layer, inside the follicle. The hair shaft is the portion that projects above the skin surface, and both are made of dead keratinized cells.","notes":["The hair follicle is the sheath of tissue that surrounds the root, not the hair itself. Choose it if the question asked which structure encloses the hair root.","The hair bulb is the onion-shaped base of the follicle that contains the dividing matrix. It would be the answer for the growth region at the bottom of the follicle.","The hair shaft is the part of the hair above the skin surface. Pick it if the question asked which part of the hair you can see or cut.","Correct. The hair root is the buried part of the hair within the follicle."],"lt":"t-integumentary","lid":"c28","comp":25,"competencyId":"w1-skin-accessory"},{"id":"L00362","dok":1,"q":"What is the whitish crescent at the proximal end of the nail body called?","a":"The lunula.","options":["The hyponychium","The eponychium","The lunula","The free edge"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The lunula, meaning little moon, is the crescent-shaped area at the proximal end of the nail body. It looks whitish because the epithelium beneath it is thickened, so the pink of the vascular tissue below does not show through. The rest of the nail body looks pink because blood vessels lie close beneath it.","notes":["The hyponychium is thickened stratum corneum under the free edge that secures the nail to the fingertip. It would be the answer for the tissue under the tip of the nail.","The eponychium is the cuticle, the band of epidermis along the proximal nail wall. Pick it if the question asked for the other name of the cuticle.","Correct. The lunula is the pale crescent at the base of the nail body.","The free edge is the part of the nail extending past the fingertip, and it is white because no capillaries lie under it. Choose it if the question asked which part of the nail you trim."],"lt":"t-integumentary","lid":"c29","comp":25,"competencyId":"w1-skin-accessory"},{"id":"L00363","dok":2,"q":"On the fingertips, dermal papillae push the epidermis up into epidermal ridges that form fingerprints. Besides forming fingerprints, what do these ridges do?","a":"They increase grip and touch sensitivity.","options":["They anchor hairs that sense light touch","They secrete sweat that cools the hand","They increase grip and touch sensitivity","They store fat that cushions the fingertip"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Epidermal ridges increase the surface area where dermis meets epidermis, which raises the number of touch corpuscles and makes the fingertips more sensitive. The ridges also increase friction, which improves grip. They form only where dermal papillae are well developed and lined up in rows, as on the palms, soles, and fingertips.","notes":["The fingertips are thick skin and have no hair follicles. Hair root plexuses would be the answer for how hairy skin senses light touch, but they do not exist on the palms or fingertips.","Sweat is made by eccrine sweat glands, which open along the ridges but are separate structures. Pick sweat glands if the question asked what keeps the palms moist or cools the body.","Correct. The ridges improve grip by adding friction and improve touch by adding receptors.","Fat storage is the job of the hypodermis, the adipose layer beneath the skin. It would be the answer if the question asked which layer cushions and insulates."],"lt":"t-integumentary","lid":"c30","comp":24,"competencyId":"w1-dermis-layers"},{"id":"L00364","dok":2,"q":"Looking at a slide of the epidermis, you see a single row of cube-shaped, actively dividing cells resting on the basement membrane. Which stratum is this?","a":"The stratum basale.","options":["The stratum spinosum","The stratum corneum","The stratum granulosum","The stratum basale"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The stratum basale is a single row of cells sitting on the basement membrane, the thin layer that separates the epidermis from the dermis. These cells divide to make new keratinocytes, and the layer also holds melanocytes and tactile cells. On a slide, look for the one-cell-thick row right at the border with the dermis.","notes":["The stratum spinosum is several layers thick and sits just above the basale, with cells that look spiny on slides. Choose it if the slide showed multiple rows of spiny cells above the basal row.","The stratum corneum is many layers of flat, dead cells at the surface. It would be the answer if the slide showed the pale, flaky top of the epidermis.","The stratum granulosum shows several rows of flattening cells filled with dark granules, in the middle of the epidermis. Pick it if the slide showed cells with dark granules just beneath the clear or dead layers.","Correct. A single dividing row on the basement membrane is the stratum basale."],"lt":"t-integumentary","lid":"c31","comp":23,"competencyId":"w1-epidermal-strata"},{"id":"L00365","dok":3,"q":"A skin section from the sole of the foot shows a thin, clear band just deep to the surface layer. Which stratum is this, and what does it tell you about the sample?","a":"The stratum lucidum, so the sample is thick skin.","options":["The stratum lucidum, so the sample is thick skin","The stratum basale, so the sample is thin skin","The stratum granulosum, so the sample is thin skin","The stratum spinosum, so the sample is thick skin"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The stratum lucidum is a thin, clear band between the granulosum and the corneum, and it is present only in thick skin. Thick skin covers the palms, soles, and palmar surfaces of the digits, the areas of greatest friction. Finding a lucidum on a slide is the quickest way to label the sample as thick skin.","notes":["Correct. A clear band under the corneum is the stratum lucidum, which marks thick skin.","The stratum basale is the single row of dividing cells at the bottom of the epidermis, not just under the surface. It would be the answer for the deepest stratum in any skin.","The stratum granulosum lies deep to the lucidum and shows dark granules rather than a clear band, and it is present in both skin types. Pick it if the band were granular and sat directly under the corneum in thin skin.","The stratum spinosum is the spiny layer above the basale, present in both skin types and not clear. Choose it if the question asked which stratum sits directly superficial to the basale."],"lt":"t-integumentary","lid":"c32","comp":23,"competencyId":"w1-epidermal-strata"},{"id":"L00366","dok":2,"q":"An intradermal injection, such as a tuberculosis skin test, places a small amount of fluid in which layer?","a":"The dermis.","options":["The dermis","The skeletal muscle beneath the hypodermis","The stratum corneum","The hypodermis"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Intradermal means within the dermis, so the needle tip sits just under the epidermis but above the hypodermis. Fluid placed there raises a small bump on the skin surface. The word itself tells you the layer: intra means within.","notes":["Correct. An intradermal injection places fluid within the dermis.","Skeletal muscle lies deep to the hypodermis and is the target of an intramuscular injection. Choose it if the question asked where an intramuscular vaccine is delivered.","The stratum corneum is the dead surface layer of the epidermis, too thin and too superficial to hold an injection. Pick it if the question asked which layer a topical cream first contacts.","The hypodermis is the fatty subcutaneous layer, the target of a subcutaneous injection such as insulin. It would be the answer if the question named a subcutaneous route."],"lt":"t-integumentary","lid":"c33","comp":22,"competencyId":"w1-skin-layers"},{"id":"L00367","dok":3,"q":"A partial-thickness burn destroys the epidermis over an area but leaves the deeper dermis with its hair follicles and sweat glands intact. Where will new epidermal cells come from?","a":"From epidermal cells lining the surviving hair follicles and sweat glands.","options":["From fibroblasts in the reticular layer of the dermis","From adipocytes in the fatty hypodermis below","From dead keratinized cells of the stratum corneum","From epidermal cells lining surviving follicles and glands"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Hair follicles and sweat glands are lined with epidermal cells that extend deep into the dermis. When a burn destroys the surface epidermis but spares the deeper dermis, these surviving epidermal cells divide and spread out to rebuild the epidermis. That is why a partial-thickness burn can heal without a graft, while a full-thickness burn that destroys the follicles and glands usually cannot.","notes":["Fibroblasts are connective tissue cells that make collagen and help form scar tissue, but they cannot make epidermis. Pick them if the question asked which cells build the fibers of the dermis.","Adipocytes are fat cells of the hypodermis and do not form epithelium. They would be the answer if the question asked which cells store fat beneath the skin.","Cells of the stratum corneum are dead and cannot divide, and in this burn they are gone anyway. Students pick them because they are epidermal, but only living, dividing epidermal cells can rebuild the layer.","Correct. The epidermal linings of surviving follicles and glands regrow the surface epidermis."],"lt":"t-integumentary","lid":"c34","comp":25,"competencyId":"w1-skin-accessory"},{"id":"L00368","dok":2,"q":"A section of axillary skin shows sweat glands whose ducts open into hair follicles rather than onto the skin surface. Which gland type is this?","a":"Apocrine sweat glands.","options":["Ceruminous glands","Eccrine sweat glands","Apocrine sweat glands","Sebaceous glands"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Apocrine sweat glands are found in the skin of the axillae, groin, areolae, and bearded regions of the face, and their ducts open into hair follicles. Their sweat contains the same components as eccrine sweat plus lipids and proteins. Eccrine glands, by contrast, open directly onto the skin surface.","notes":["Ceruminous glands are modified sweat glands found only in the external ear canal. They would be the answer if the section came from the ear canal.","Eccrine sweat glands are the most numerous sweat glands and open directly onto the skin surface. Pick them if the ducts opened through pores on the skin.","Correct. Apocrine sweat glands empty into hair follicles in the axillae and groin.","Sebaceous glands also open into hair follicles, but they are oil glands that secrete sebum, not sweat. Choose them if the glands were described as producing oil."],"lt":"t-integumentary","lid":"c35","comp":25,"competencyId":"w1-skin-accessory"},{"id":"L00369","dok":3,"q":"A skin specimen lacks a stratum lucidum, has a thin stratum corneum, and contains many hair follicles. From which site was it most likely taken?","a":"The posterior forearm.","options":["The posterior forearm","The sole of the foot","The palm of the hand","The pad of a fingertip"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Skin with no stratum lucidum, a thin corneum, and hair follicles is thin skin, which covers every part of the body except the palms, soles, and palmar surfaces of the digits. The posterior forearm is ordinary hairy skin, so it fits. Thick skin has a lucidum and a thick corneum but no hair follicles.","notes":["Correct. The posterior forearm is hairy thin skin without a lucidum.","The sole of the foot is thick skin, with the thickest corneum in the body and no hair. It would be the answer for a specimen with a very thick corneum and a clear lucidum.","The palm is thick skin with a stratum lucidum and no hair follicles. Pick it if the specimen had five strata and no hair.","The fingertip pad is thick skin with epidermal ridges and no hair follicles. Choose it if the specimen showed fingerprint ridges and a lucidum."],"lt":"t-integumentary","lid":"c36","comp":23,"competencyId":"w1-epidermal-strata"},{"id":"L00370","dok":2,"q":"A specimen shows a plate of hard keratin over the dorsal tip of a finger, with dividing cells hidden under the skin fold at its proximal end. Which pair names the structure and its growth region?","a":"The nail and the nail matrix.","options":["The nail and the hyponychium","The nail and the lunula","The nail and the nail matrix","The hair and the hair bulb"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"A plate of hard keratin over the dorsal fingertip is a nail. Its growth region is the nail matrix, epithelium proximal to the nail root, tucked under the skin fold, where dividing cells produce new nail. The new cells push the nail body forward toward the free edge.","notes":["The hyponychium is thickened stratum corneum under the free edge at the distal end of the nail. Choose it if the question asked what secures the nail to the fingertip.","The lunula is the pale crescent at the proximal end of the visible nail body, not the hidden region of dividing cells. It would be the answer if the question asked about the whitish half-moon you can see.","Correct. The structure is the nail and its growth region is the nail matrix.","A hair is a strand, not a plate, and its growth region is the hair bulb at the base of a follicle. Pick this pair if the specimen showed a strand growing from a follicle."],"lt":"t-integumentary","lid":"c37","comp":25,"competencyId":"w1-skin-accessory"},{"id":"L00371","dok":2,"q":"A needle is pushed from the skin surface straight down to a muscle. In what order does it pass through the layers?","a":"Epidermis, dermis, hypodermis, then muscle.","options":["Dermis, epidermis, hypodermis, then muscle","Epidermis, hypodermis, dermis, then muscle","Hypodermis, epidermis, dermis, then muscle","Epidermis, dermis, hypodermis, then muscle"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"From the surface inward, the needle crosses the epidermis, then the dermis, then the hypodermis, and finally reaches the muscle beneath the deep fascia. The epidermis and dermis make up the skin, and the hypodermis is the fatty layer that anchors the skin to the muscle. This same order explains injection routes: intradermal stops in the dermis, subcutaneous in the hypodermis, and intramuscular in the muscle.","notes":["This order puts the dermis on top, but the epidermis is always the surface layer. Students make this mix-up when they forget that the epidermis lies on top of the dermis, not beneath it.","This order places the hypodermis between the two skin layers, but the epidermis and dermis are directly attached to each other. Students choose it when they confuse the hypodermis with a layer inside the skin.","This order starts with the hypodermis, which is the deepest of the three layers. It would only make sense if the needle were traveling from the muscle outward, and even then the epidermis would come last.","Correct. Epidermis, dermis, hypodermis, then muscle is the surface-to-deep order."],"lt":"t-integumentary","lid":"c38","comp":22,"competencyId":"w1-skin-layers"},{"id":"L00372","dok":2,"q":"In thick skin, in what order does a keratinocyte pass through the strata, from where it is born to the surface?","a":"Basale, spinosum, granulosum, lucidum, corneum.","options":["Basale, granulosum, spinosum, lucidum, corneum","Corneum, lucidum, granulosum, spinosum, basale","Basale, spinosum, granulosum, lucidum, corneum","Spinosum, basale, granulosum, corneum, lucidum"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"A keratinocyte is born in the stratum basale, then moves through the spinosum, the granulosum, the lucidum, and finally the corneum, where it is shed. The mnemonic Baby Screams Grandpa Loves Coffee gives this deep-to-superficial order. In thin skin the sequence is the same except that the lucidum is missing.","notes":["This order swaps the spinosum and granulosum. The spinosum sits directly on the basale, and the granulosum comes after it, so this is a common swap of the two middle layers.","This is the correct list run backward, from surface to deep. It would be the answer if the question asked for the strata from superficial to deep.","Correct. Basale, spinosum, granulosum, lucidum, corneum is the deep-to-surface path.","This order starts in the spinosum and ends in the lucidum, but cells are born in the basale and the corneum is always the surface. Students choose it when they lose track of which layers sit at the two ends."],"lt":"t-integumentary","lid":"c39","comp":23,"competencyId":"w1-epidermal-strata"},{"id":"L00373","dok":2,"q":"Moving from above the skin surface down to the base of a follicle, which order names the hair regions correctly?","a":"Hair shaft, hair root, hair bulb.","options":["Hair bulb, hair root, hair shaft","Hair root, hair shaft, hair bulb","Hair shaft, hair root, hair bulb","Hair shaft, hair bulb, hair root"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The hair shaft projects above the skin, the hair root is the part buried in the dermis inside the follicle, and the hair bulb is the onion-shaped base at the bottom of the follicle. The bulb holds the dividing matrix that grows the hair. So moving downward you meet shaft, then root, then bulb.","notes":["This is the correct order run backward, from the base up to the surface. It would be the answer if the question asked for the regions from deep to superficial.","This order puts the root above the shaft, but the shaft is the only part above the skin. Students choose it when they mix up which term means the visible part.","Correct. Shaft, root, bulb is the order from the surface down.","This order puts the bulb above the root, but the bulb is the very bottom of the follicle. It confuses the growing base with the buried middle part of the hair."],"lt":"t-integumentary","lid":"c40","comp":25,"competencyId":"w1-skin-accessory"},{"id":"L00374","dok":2,"q":"A section through the thick skin of the palm runs from the outermost epidermal stratum down to the dermal layer that holds the sweat glands. Which pair names the first stratum and the last dermal layer reached?","a":"Stratum corneum first, then the reticular layer.","options":["Stratum lucidum first, then the papillary layer","Stratum corneum first, then the reticular layer","Stratum basale first, then the papillary layer","Stratum corneum first, then the papillary layer"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The outermost stratum in all skin is the stratum corneum, even in thick skin where the lucidum lies just beneath it. The sweat glands sit in the reticular layer, the deep dense irregular part of the dermis. The papillary layer above it holds capillary loops and touch receptors, not the gland coils.","notes":["The stratum lucidum lies just beneath the corneum in thick skin, so it is reached second. Pick the lucidum if the question asked which stratum appears directly under the surface layer of the palm.","Correct. The section starts in the stratum corneum and ends in the reticular layer.","The stratum basale is the deepest stratum, so it cannot be the first one reached from the surface. This pair would describe the last epidermal layer and the first dermal layer a section passes through.","The corneum is correct as the first layer, but the papillary layer holds capillary loops and Meissner corpuscles, not sweat glands. Choose the papillary layer if the question asked where the dermal papillae are."],"lt":"t-integumentary","lid":"c41","comp":24,"competencyId":"w1-dermis-layers"},{"id":"L00375","dok":2,"q":"Which order lists the superficial skin layer, the superficial dermal layer, and the deep dermal layer, from superficial to deep?","a":"Epidermis, papillary layer, reticular layer.","options":["Dermis, papillary layer, reticular layer","Epidermis, reticular layer, hypodermis","Epidermis, papillary layer, reticular layer","Epidermis, reticular layer, papillary layer"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The epidermis is the surface layer of the skin. Beneath it, the dermis has a thin superficial papillary layer of areolar connective tissue and a thick deep reticular layer of dense irregular connective tissue. The papilla in papillary refers to the dermal papillae that push up into the epidermis, a reminder that this layer touches the epidermis.","notes":["This order starts with the dermis, but the dermis is the deep skin layer and the papillary and reticular layers are parts of it. Students choose it when they forget that the epidermis sits above everything else.","This order skips the papillary layer and adds the hypodermis, which is below the skin. It would fit a question asking for the epidermis, the deep dermis, and the layer under the skin.","Correct. Epidermis, papillary layer, reticular layer runs from superficial to deep.","This order reverses the two dermal layers. The papillary layer forms the papillae that touch the epidermis, so it must come before the reticular layer."],"lt":"t-integumentary","lid":"c42","comp":24,"competencyId":"w1-dermis-layers"},{"id":"L00376","dok":3,"q":"Two skin samples are compared. The first has five epidermal strata and no hair follicles. The second has four strata and many hair follicles. Which statement is correct?","a":"The first sample is thick skin, and the second lacks the stratum lucidum.","options":["The second sample is thick skin, and it lacks the stratum spinosum","The first sample is thin skin, and the second lacks the stratum corneum","The first sample is thick skin, and the second lacks the stratum lucidum","The second sample is thick skin, and it lacks the stratum basale"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Thick skin has all five strata and no hair follicles, so the first sample is thick skin. Thin skin has four strata because it essentially lacks the stratum lucidum, and it carries hair follicles, so the second sample is thin skin. The basale, spinosum, granulosum, and corneum are present in both kinds of skin.","notes":["The second sample is thin skin, and the spinosum is present in both skin types. Pick the spinosum only if the question asked which stratum lies directly above the basale.","Five strata means thick skin, not thin, and every skin type has a stratum corneum at the surface. This option would only fit if thin skin were defined by a missing surface layer, which it is not.","Correct. Five strata with no hair is thick skin, and the four-strata sample lacks the lucidum.","The second sample has hair, which marks thin skin, and no skin lacks the stratum basale because it is the dividing layer. Students choose this when they link more hair with thicker skin."],"lt":"t-integumentary","lid":"c43","comp":23,"competencyId":"w1-epidermal-strata"},{"id":"L00377","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares the blood supply of the epidermis and the dermis?","a":"The epidermis has no blood vessels, and the dermis holds the vessels, nerves, and glands.","options":["Neither layer has vessels, so both are fed from the hypodermis","The dermis has no blood vessels, and the epidermis holds the vessels and nerves","Both layers have their own capillary beds of equal size","The epidermis has no blood vessels, and the dermis holds the vessels, nerves, and glands"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The epidermis is avascular, meaning it has no blood vessels, so its living cells are fed by diffusion from capillaries in the dermis. The dermis is a vascular connective tissue that holds blood vessels, nerves, glands, and hair follicles. This is why the dermis is essential to the survival of the epidermis.","notes":["The dermis has its own extensive vessel network, including capillary loops in the dermal papillae. The hypodermis does carry large vessels, but it is not the direct supply for the epidermis.","This statement reverses the two layers. The dermis is the vascular layer, and the epidermis is the one without vessels.","Only the dermis has capillary beds; the epidermis has none. Students choose this when they assume every living layer must have its own blood supply.","Correct. The epidermis is avascular and depends on the vascular dermis."],"lt":"t-integumentary","lid":"c44","comp":22,"competencyId":"w1-skin-layers"},{"id":"L00378","dok":1,"q":"Which list names the four skin glands compared in this course?","a":"Sebaceous, eccrine sweat, apocrine sweat, and ceruminous glands.","options":["Sebaceous, eccrine sweat, apocrine sweat, and ceruminous glands","Eccrine sweat, apocrine sweat, ceruminous, and salivary glands","Sebaceous, apocrine sweat, ceruminous, and gastric glands","Sebaceous, eccrine sweat, apocrine sweat, and lacrimal glands"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The four skin glands are the sebaceous (oil) glands, the eccrine sweat glands, the apocrine sweat glands, and the ceruminous glands of the ear canal. They differ in where they are found, where their ducts open, what they secrete, and when they become active. All four develop from the epidermis and sit in the dermis or the upper subcutaneous layer.","notes":["Correct. Sebaceous, eccrine, apocrine, and ceruminous are the four skin glands.","Salivary glands are digestive glands that release saliva into the mouth, not skin glands. They would be the answer for a question about the parotid, submandibular, and sublingual glands.","Gastric glands lie in the stomach lining and secrete gastric juice, so they belong to the digestive system. Choose them if the question asked which glands are found in the stomach mucosa.","Lacrimal glands make tears and sit in the orbit above the eye, so they are not skin glands. Pick them if the question asked which gland produces tears."],"lt":"t-integumentary","lid":"c45","comp":25,"competencyId":"w1-skin-accessory"},{"id":"L00379","dok":1,"q":"Which statement about the hypodermis is correct?","a":"It lies deep to the dermis and is not part of the skin.","options":["It lies deep to the dermis, outside the skin","It is another name for the reticular layer","It is the deepest layer of the skin proper","It lies between the epidermis and the dermis"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The hypodermis, or subcutaneous layer, lies beneath the dermis and is not part of the skin. It is made of areolar and adipose tissue that stores fat and anchors the skin to the fascia around muscles and bones. The skin proper ends at the bottom of the reticular layer of the dermis.","notes":["Correct. The hypodermis is beneath the dermis and outside the skin proper.","The reticular layer is the deep layer of the dermis, made of dense irregular connective tissue. Pick it if the question asked for the deep part of the dermis, not the fatty layer beneath the skin.","The deepest layer of the skin proper is the dermis, specifically its reticular layer. Students choose this when they count the hypodermis as part of the skin.","Nothing lies between the epidermis and dermis except the basement membrane. This option confuses the hypodermis with the boundary between the two skin layers."],"lt":"t-integumentary","lid":"c1-x2","comp":22,"competencyId":"w1-skin-layers"},{"id":"L00381","dok":1,"q":"Which pressure receptor sits deep in the skin, in the hypodermis with the large blood vessels?","a":"The lamellated (Pacinian) corpuscle.","options":["The Meissner corpuscle","The tactile (Merkel) disc","The hair root plexus","The lamellated (Pacinian) corpuscle"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Lamellated corpuscles are large, onion-like nerve endings sensitive to pressure, and they sit deep, in the hypodermis with the large blood vessels. Because they are deep, they respond to firm pressure and vibration rather than light touch. The light touch receptors sit closer to the surface, in the dermal papillae and the epidermis.","notes":["Meissner corpuscles are touch receptors in the dermal papillae of the papillary layer. Pick them if the question asked which receptor sits in the dermal papillae for fine touch.","Tactile (Merkel) discs are nerve endings in contact with tactile cells in the deepest layer of the epidermis. They would be the answer for the touch receptor found in the epidermis.","The hair root plexus is a network of nerve endings around each hair follicle in the dermis. Choose it if the question asked how a hair senses being moved.","Correct. The lamellated corpuscle is the deep pressure receptor of the hypodermis."],"lt":"t-integumentary","lid":"c3-x2","comp":24,"competencyId":"w1-dermis-layers"},{"id":"L00382","dok":1,"q":"Which stratum lies directly superficial to the stratum basale?","a":"The stratum spinosum.","options":["The stratum corneum","The stratum granulosum","The stratum spinosum","The stratum lucidum"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The stratum spinosum is the second stratum from the bottom, resting directly on the stratum basale. It is several cells thick, and its cells look spiny on slides. Using the mnemonic Baby Screams Grandpa Loves Coffee, the S for spinosum comes right after the B for basale.","notes":["The stratum corneum is the surface stratum, the farthest from the basale. Choose it if the question asked which stratum forms the outer surface.","The stratum granulosum is the third stratum, above the spinosum. Pick it if the question asked which stratum lies directly superficial to the spinosum.","Correct. The stratum spinosum sits directly on the basale.","The stratum lucidum is the fourth stratum, present only in thick skin. It would be the answer for the stratum directly deep to the corneum on the palm or sole."],"lt":"t-integumentary","lid":"c4-x2","comp":23,"competencyId":"w1-epidermal-strata"},{"id":"L00383","dok":2,"q":"In thin skin, which stratum lies directly beneath the stratum corneum?","a":"The stratum granulosum.","options":["The stratum granulosum","The stratum basale","The stratum lucidum","The stratum spinosum"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Thin skin has four strata: basale, spinosum, granulosum, and corneum. Because the stratum lucidum is essentially missing in thin skin, the granulosum lies directly beneath the corneum. In thick skin the lucidum would slip in between those two layers.","notes":["Correct. Without a lucidum, the granulosum sits right under the corneum.","The stratum basale is the deepest stratum, far from the corneum. Choose it if the question asked which stratum rests on the basement membrane.","The stratum lucidum lies directly beneath the corneum only in thick skin, and thin skin essentially lacks it. Pick it if the question asked the same thing about the palm or sole.","The stratum spinosum is the second layer from the bottom, beneath the granulosum. It would be the answer for the stratum directly above the basale."],"lt":"t-integumentary","lid":"c5-x2","comp":23,"competencyId":"w1-epidermal-strata"},{"id":"L00384","dok":1,"q":"Where in the body would you find skin that has a stratum lucidum?","a":"The palms and soles.","options":["The scalp","The upper back","The eyelids","The palms and soles"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The stratum lucidum is present only in thick skin, which covers the palms, the soles, and the palmar surfaces of the digits. These are the areas of greatest friction and pressure. Everywhere else, including hairy areas such as the scalp, is thin skin without a lucidum.","notes":["The scalp is thin skin with many hair follicles, and thick skin never has hair. Pick the scalp if the question asked where terminal hairs are densest.","The upper back is thin skin, even though its dermis is quite thick. Students pick it because they confuse a thick dermis with thick skin, which is defined by the epidermis.","The eyelids are among the thinnest skin of the body and have no lucidum. Choose the eyelids if the question asked where tarsal glands, a form of sebaceous gland, are found.","Correct. The palms and soles are thick skin with a stratum lucidum."],"lt":"t-integumentary","lid":"c6-x2","comp":23,"competencyId":"w1-epidermal-strata"},{"id":"L00385","dok":1,"q":"Besides keratin, keratinocytes produce lamellar granules. What do these granules release?","a":"A water-repellent sealant.","options":["Watery sweat","A water-repellent sealant","Oily sebum","Melanin pigment"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Lamellar granules are made by keratinocytes and release a water-repellent sealant between the cells of the upper epidermis. The sealant decreases water entry and loss and helps block foreign material. Together with keratin, it makes the epidermis a protective barrier.","notes":["Watery sweat is made by eccrine sweat glands and released through pores. Choose it if the question asked what eccrine glands release to cool the body.","Correct. Lamellar granules release a water-repellent sealant.","Sebum is made by sebaceous glands in the dermis and released into hair follicles. It would be the answer if the question asked what oil glands secrete.","Melanin is made by melanocytes and passed to keratinocytes, not made by keratinocytes. Pick melanin if the question asked what melanocytes transfer to keratinocytes."],"lt":"t-integumentary","lid":"c7-x2","comp":23,"competencyId":"w1-epidermal-strata"},{"id":"L00386","dok":2,"q":"Skin color differs between people mainly because of which difference?","a":"The amount of melanin their melanocytes produce and transfer.","options":["The number of melanocytes in the epidermis","The number of blood vessels in the dermis","The amount of melanin the melanocytes make","The thickness of the stratum corneum"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The number of melanocytes is about the same in all people. Differences in skin color come from how much pigment those melanocytes produce and transfer to keratinocytes. Exposure to UV light raises melanin production, which is what a tan is.","notes":["The number of melanocytes is about the same in everyone, so it does not explain color differences. Students choose this when they assume darker skin must have more pigment cells. Melanocyte number would be the answer for vitiligo, where patches of skin lose their melanocytes.","Dermal blood vessels add a pink to red tone through hemoglobin, and their content changes with flushing or low oxygen. They would be the answer for color changes like erythema or cyanosis, not for differences between people.","Correct. Color depends on how much melanin the melanocytes make and pass on.","Corneum thickness changes with friction, which is why the palms and soles are thick skin, but it is not what sets a person's skin color. Pick it if the question asked why thick skin is thick."],"lt":"t-integumentary","lid":"c8-x2","comp":23,"competencyId":"w1-epidermal-strata"},{"id":"L00387","dok":1,"q":"Which tissue makes up the papillary layer of the dermis?","a":"Areolar connective tissue.","options":["Dense regular connective tissue","Dense irregular connective tissue","Adipose tissue","Areolar connective tissue"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The papillary layer is the thin, superficial dermis, made of areolar connective tissue with thin collagen and fine elastic fibers. This loose tissue leaves room for capillary loops, Meissner corpuscles, and free nerve endings in the dermal papillae. The deeper reticular layer is the tougher dense irregular connective tissue.","notes":["Dense regular connective tissue has parallel collagen fibers and forms tendons and ligaments, not the dermis. Choose it if the question asked what tissue connects muscle to bone.","Dense irregular connective tissue makes up the reticular layer, the deep four fifths of the dermis. Pick it if the question asked about the deep dermal layer.","Adipose tissue makes up most of the hypodermis beneath the skin. It would be the answer for the subcutaneous layer.","Correct. The papillary layer is areolar connective tissue."],"lt":"t-integumentary","lid":"c9-x2","comp":24,"competencyId":"w1-dermis-layers"},{"id":"L00388","dok":2,"q":"Which dermal layer gives the skin most of its strength and its resistance to stretching?","a":"The reticular layer.","options":["The hypodermis","The reticular layer","The stratum spinosum","The papillary layer"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The reticular layer makes up about four fifths of the dermis and is built of thick bundles of collagen in a net-like arrangement. That collagen gives the skin its strength, while its elastic fibers allow stretch and recoil. When this layer is overstretched and torn, as in pregnancy, the result is visible stretch marks.","notes":["The hypodermis is the fatty layer beneath the skin, made for fat storage and anchoring. Choose it if the question asked which layer insulates and cushions.","Correct. The reticular layer's thick collagen bundles provide strength.","The stratum spinosum is an epidermal stratum, not part of the dermis. It would be the answer for the stratum directly above the basale.","The papillary layer is the thin superficial dermis with fine fibers, built to anchor and feed the epidermis rather than to resist pulling. Pick it if the question asked which layer forms the dermal papillae."],"lt":"t-integumentary","lid":"c10-x2","comp":24,"competencyId":"w1-dermis-layers"},{"id":"L00389","dok":1,"q":"Which type of tissue makes up the arrector pili?","a":"Smooth muscle.","options":["Dense regular connective tissue","Smooth muscle","Cardiac muscle","Skeletal muscle"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The arrector pili is a small band of smooth muscle that connects the dermis to a hair follicle. Smooth muscle contracts involuntarily, which is why goose bumps appear on their own when you are cold or frightened. When it contracts, it pulls the hair shaft perpendicular to the skin surface.","notes":["Dense regular connective tissue forms tendons and ligaments, which cannot contract. Choose it if the question asked what attaches a skeletal muscle to bone.","Correct. The arrector pili is smooth muscle, so it contracts involuntarily.","Cardiac muscle is found only in the heart wall. It would be the answer for the muscle tissue with intercalated discs.","Skeletal muscle is voluntary, striated muscle attached to bones, such as the muscles of facial expression. Pick it if the question asked which muscle tissue moves the skin of the face."],"lt":"t-integumentary","lid":"c11-x2","comp":25,"competencyId":"w1-skin-accessory"},{"id":"L00390","dok":1,"q":"Where does the duct of most sebaceous glands empty?","a":"Into a hair follicle.","options":["Directly onto the skin surface through a pore","Into the blood vessels of the dermis","Into the external ear canal","Into a hair follicle"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Most sebaceous glands are connected to a hair follicle, so the sebum coats the hair and then spreads onto the skin. This is why sebaceous glands are absent from the palms and soles, which have no hair follicles. A few, such as those on the lips, open directly onto the surface.","notes":["Opening directly onto the surface through a pore describes eccrine sweat glands. Pick it if the question asked where the ducts of the most numerous sweat glands open.","Releasing a product into the blood describes endocrine glands, which have no ducts. Sebaceous glands are exocrine, so choose this only for a question about hormone-secreting glands.","The external ear canal receives cerumen from ceruminous glands. It would be the answer if the question asked where ceruminous glands release their product.","Correct. Most sebaceous ducts open into a hair follicle."],"lt":"t-integumentary","lid":"c13-x2","comp":25,"competencyId":"w1-skin-accessory"},{"id":"L00391","dok":2,"q":"Where is the coiled secretory portion of most eccrine sweat glands, and where does the duct open?","a":"In the deep dermis, with the duct opening onto the skin surface.","options":["In the subcutaneous layer, with the duct opening into the ear canal","In the deep dermis, with the duct opening into a hair follicle","In the stratum basale, with the duct opening onto the skin surface","In the deep dermis, with the duct opening onto the skin surface"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The coiled secretory part of an eccrine sweat gland lies mostly in the deep dermis, sometimes reaching the upper subcutaneous layer. Its duct winds up through the dermis and epidermis to open as a pore on the skin surface. That direct route lets sweat spread over the skin and cool the body as it evaporates.","notes":["A secretory portion in the subcutaneous layer with output into the ear canal describes ceruminous glands. Choose it if the question asked where earwax is made.","Opening into a hair follicle describes apocrine sweat glands and sebaceous glands. Pick this if the question asked about the sweat glands of the axillae and groin.","The stratum basale is an epidermal layer and cannot hold a gland's secretory coil. Students choose this because the duct does pass through the epidermis, but the gland itself sits in the dermis.","Correct. Eccrine glands coil in the deep dermis and open onto the surface."],"lt":"t-integumentary","lid":"c14-x2","comp":25,"competencyId":"w1-skin-accessory"},{"id":"L00392","dok":1,"q":"A hair has three concentric layers. Which layer forms the major part of the hair shaft?","a":"The cortex.","options":["The cuticle","The cortex","The medulla","The hair matrix"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Both the hair root and shaft have three layers. The medulla is the inner core of 2 to 3 rows of cells, the cortex is the thick middle layer of elongated cells that forms most of the shaft, and the cuticle is a single outer layer of thin, flat, heavily keratinized cells. Melanin passes into the cortex and medulla, so the cortex carries much of the hair's color.","notes":["The hair cuticle is the single outermost layer of flat keratinized cells, not to be confused with the nail cuticle. It would be the answer for the outermost layer of the hair.","Correct. The cortex makes up most of the hair shaft.","The medulla is the inner core of the hair, only 2 to 3 rows of cells. Pick it if the question asked for the innermost layer.","The hair matrix is the layer of dividing cells in the hair bulb that grows the hair, not a layer of the shaft. Choose it if the question asked where hair cells are made."],"lt":"t-integumentary","lid":"c15-x2","comp":25,"competencyId":"w1-skin-accessory"},{"id":"L00393","dok":2,"q":"In thin skin, which order lists the strata a keratinocyte passes through, from where it is born to the surface?","a":"Basale, spinosum, granulosum, corneum.","options":["Basale, granulosum, spinosum, corneum","Basale, spinosum, granulosum, lucidum, corneum","Corneum, granulosum, spinosum, basale","Basale, spinosum, granulosum, corneum"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Thin skin has four strata. A keratinocyte is born in the stratum basale, then moves through the spinosum and granulosum, and ends in the corneum at the surface. Thin skin essentially lacks the stratum lucidum, so that step is skipped.","notes":["This order swaps the spinosum and granulosum. The spinosum sits directly on the basale, so this is a mix-up of the two middle layers.","This five-strata order is correct for thick skin, which has a stratum lucidum. Pick it if the question asked about the palm or sole.","This is the thin skin order run backward, from surface to deep. It would be the answer if the question asked for the strata from superficial to deep.","Correct. Basale, spinosum, granulosum, corneum is the thin skin path."],"lt":"t-integumentary","lid":"c16-x2","comp":23,"competencyId":"w1-epidermal-strata"},{"id":"L00394","dok":2,"q":"The epidermal ridges of the palms and soles, the basis of fingerprints, are raised by which structures?","a":"Dermal papillae of the papillary layer.","options":["Adipose lobules of the hypodermis","Dead cells of the stratum corneum","Collagen bundles of the reticular layer","Dermal papillae of the papillary layer"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Dermal papillae are finger-shaped projections of the papillary layer that push up into the underside of the epidermis. In thick skin they are well developed and lined up in parallel rows, so the epidermis above them forms ridges. Those ridges are the fingerprints, and their pattern is set by the dermis beneath.","notes":["The adipose lobules of the hypodermis cushion and insulate, well below the skin. They would be the answer for the layer that stores fat.","The stratum corneum follows the ridges at the surface, but it does not create them. Choose it if the question asked which layer forms the visible surface of a fingerprint.","The reticular layer's collagen bundles give the skin strength but lie too deep to shape the epidermis. Pick them if the question asked which dermal structure resists stretching.","Correct. Rows of dermal papillae push the epidermis up into ridges."],"lt":"t-integumentary","lid":"c17-x2","comp":24,"competencyId":"w1-dermis-layers"},{"id":"L00395","dok":2,"q":"A nurse must place an injection in the hypodermis. Which injection route reaches that layer?","a":"A subcutaneous injection.","options":["An intradermal injection","An intravenous injection","An intramuscular injection","A subcutaneous injection"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Subcutaneous means beneath the skin, and the hypodermis is the subcutaneous layer, so a subcutaneous injection places medication in the fatty tissue under the dermis. Insulin is a common example. The route names follow the layers from shallow to deep: intradermal, subcutaneous, then intramuscular.","notes":["An intradermal injection stops in the dermis, above the hypodermis. Pick it if the question asked how a tuberculosis skin test is given.","An intravenous injection goes directly into a vein, into the blood rather than a tissue layer. Choose it if the question asked how medication is delivered straight into the bloodstream.","An intramuscular injection passes through the hypodermis into skeletal muscle. It would be the answer for a vaccine given into the deltoid.","Correct. A subcutaneous injection targets the hypodermis."],"lt":"t-integumentary","lid":"c18-x2","comp":22,"competencyId":"w1-skin-layers"},{"id":"L00396","dok":1,"q":"In which stratum of the epidermis are tactile (Merkel) cells located?","a":"The stratum basale.","options":["The stratum granulosum","The stratum spinosum","The stratum corneum","The stratum basale"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Tactile (Merkel) cells are the least numerous epidermal cells and sit in the deepest layer of the epidermis, the stratum basale. That position puts them right above the dermis, where each one can contact the flattened ending of a sensory neuron, the tactile disc. Together they detect light touch.","notes":["The stratum granulosum is a middle layer where keratinocytes begin to die. It would be the answer for the stratum just beneath the corneum in thin skin.","The stratum spinosum is the layer just above the basale, several cells thick. Pick it if the question asked which stratum lies directly superficial to the basale.","The stratum corneum is dead surface cells, which cannot serve as living sensory cells. Choose it if the question asked which stratum is constantly shed.","Correct. Tactile cells sit in the stratum basale, the deepest stratum."],"lt":"t-integumentary","lid":"c19-x2","comp":23,"competencyId":"w1-epidermal-strata"},{"id":"L00397","dok":2,"q":"Capillary loops, Meissner corpuscles, and free nerve endings are found together in which structures?","a":"The dermal papillae.","options":["The dermal papillae","The hypodermis","The hair bulbs","The epidermal strata"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The dermal papillae of the papillary layer hold three things you identify in lab: capillary loops that feed the epidermis by diffusion, Meissner corpuscles for touch and pressure, and free nerve endings for temperature, light touch, and itching. Their position right beneath the epidermis is what makes them suited to these jobs. Being close to the surface lets the receptors sense fine touch and lets the capillaries supply the avascular epidermis.","notes":["Correct. The dermal papillae hold capillary loops, Meissner corpuscles, and free nerve endings.","The hypodermis holds lamellated corpuscles and large blood vessels, not capillary loops and Meissner corpuscles. Choose it if the question asked where deep pressure receptors lie.","The hair bulb holds the hair matrix and surrounds the papilla of the hair, a separate knob of vessels that feeds the growing hair. Pick it if the question asked where hair cells divide.","The epidermis is avascular and holds no capillary loops or Meissner corpuscles, though it does contain tactile discs. It would be the answer if the question asked where tactile (Merkel) cells sit."],"lt":"t-integumentary","lid":"c20-x2","comp":24,"competencyId":"w1-dermis-layers"},{"id":"L00399","dok":2,"q":"Which feature is present in thin skin but absent from thick skin?","a":"Hair follicles.","options":["Hair follicles","Eccrine sweat glands","A stratum lucidum","Epidermal ridges"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Thin skin covers most of the body and carries hair follicles, arrector pili muscles, and sebaceous glands. Thick skin, on the palms, soles, and palmar surfaces of the digits, has none of these. Thick skin instead adds a stratum lucidum, epidermal ridges, and a greater number of sweat glands.","notes":["Correct. Hair follicles are found in thin skin and absent from thick skin.","Eccrine sweat glands are present in both skin types, and they are more numerous in thick skin. Choose them if the question asked which glands are most concentrated on the palms.","The stratum lucidum is the opposite case: present in thick skin and essentially lacking in thin skin. Pick it if the question asked which feature thick skin has that thin skin lacks.","Epidermal ridges form in thick skin, where dermal papillae are well developed and in rows. They would be the answer for a feature found on the fingertips but not the forearm."],"lt":"t-integumentary","lid":"c22-x2","comp":23,"competencyId":"w1-epidermal-strata"},{"id":"L00400","dok":1,"q":"At what stage of life do apocrine sweat glands become active?","a":"At puberty.","options":["In early childhood","In older adulthood","Soon after birth","At puberty"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Apocrine sweat glands, found in the axillae, groin, and areolae, begin secreting at puberty. Their sweat contains lipids and proteins in addition to the usual components, and it empties into hair follicles. Sebaceous glands also become much more active at puberty, which is part of why body odor and acne often start in the teen years.","notes":["Early childhood is a quiet period for these glands; sebaceous glands, for example, stay relatively inactive through childhood. Students choose it when they guess that glands switch on gradually as a child grows.","Older adulthood is not when any skin gland turns on; gland activity generally declines with age. This choice reflects a guess rather than a real onset time.","Soon after birth is when eccrine sweat glands and ceruminous glands begin to work. Pick it if the question asked about the onset of eccrine sweating.","Correct. Apocrine sweat glands become active at puberty."],"lt":"t-integumentary","lid":"c24-x2","comp":25,"competencyId":"w1-skin-accessory"},{"id":"L00401","dok":2,"q":"A shallow paper cut stays entirely within the epidermis. What would you expect to happen?","a":"It does not bleed, because the epidermis has no blood vessels.","options":["It does not bleed, because the epidermis has no blood vessels","It bleeds slowly, because the stratum lucidum holds small veins","It bleeds, because arteries run through the stratum basale","It bleeds heavily, because capillary loops lie in the stratum corneum"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The epidermis is avascular, meaning it has no blood vessels of its own. A cut that stays within it does not reach any vessels, so it does not bleed. Bleeding begins only when a cut reaches the dermis, where the capillary loops of the dermal papillae and deeper vessels are found.","notes":["Correct. The epidermis is avascular, so a cut confined to it does not bleed.","The stratum lucidum is a clear layer of dead cells found only in thick skin, with no veins. It would be the answer if the question asked which stratum appears only on the palms and soles.","The stratum basale is a row of dividing epithelial cells with no arteries. Students choose this because the basale is the deepest stratum, closest to the dermis, but the vessels stop at the dermis.","Capillary loops are in the dermal papillae of the papillary dermis, not in the stratum corneum. Pick capillary loops if the question asked which vessels supply the epidermis by diffusion."],"lt":"t-integumentary","lid":"c25-x2","comp":22,"competencyId":"w1-skin-layers"},{"id":"L00402","dok":2,"q":"A burn blisters and reaches into the dermis but does not destroy its full thickness. How is this burn classified?","a":"A partial-thickness (second-degree) burn.","options":["A partial-thickness (second-degree) burn","A fourth-degree burn","A full-thickness (third-degree) burn","A superficial (first-degree) burn"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Burn classes follow the skin layers. A partial-thickness burn reaches into the dermis and blisters because the junction between dermis and epidermis fails. It can still heal on its own because surviving hair follicles and sweat glands, lined with epidermal cells, lie deep in the dermis.","notes":["Correct. A blistering burn into, but not through, the dermis is partial thickness.","A fourth-degree burn extends beyond the skin into deeper tissues such as muscle or bone. Choose it only if the burn reached structures below the hypodermis.","A full-thickness burn destroys the entire dermis and its follicles and glands, so it usually needs a graft. It would be the answer if the burn went all the way through the dermis.","A superficial burn involves the epidermis only, like a mild sunburn, and heals because the stratum basale is intact. Pick it if the burn were red and painful without blisters."],"lt":"t-integumentary","lid":"c26-x2","comp":22,"competencyId":"w1-skin-layers"},{"id":"L00403","dok":3,"q":"A full-thickness burn destroys the epidermis and dermis, and the center of the burn is often painless. Why?","a":"The nerve endings in the dermis have been destroyed.","options":["The skin regrows too quickly for pain to register","The hypodermis blocks pain signals from the dermis","The epidermis never had any sensory endings","The nerve endings in the dermis have been destroyed"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Most of the skin's sensory receptors, including free nerve endings that detect pain and temperature, lie in the dermis. A full-thickness burn destroys the dermis, so those receptors are destroyed too, and the center of the burn may feel numb. The less damaged edges, where nerve endings survive, are usually very painful.","notes":["A full-thickness burn cannot regrow on its own because the stratum basale and the follicles and glands are gone. Students choose this when they mix up how fast skin heals with whether it can sense pain.","The hypodermis does not block pain signals; it holds fat, large vessels, and lamellated corpuscles. It would be the answer if the question asked where deep pressure receptors are found.","The epidermis does contain sensory structures, such as tactile discs, and nerve endings reach it from the dermis. This choice reflects the true fact that the epidermis has no blood vessels, which students confuse with having no nerves.","Correct. The burn destroys the dermal nerve endings that would sense pain."],"lt":"t-integumentary","lid":"c27-x2","comp":24,"competencyId":"w1-dermis-layers"},{"id":"L00404","dok":2,"q":"A superficial burn damages the epidermis but spares the stratum basale, and the skin repairs itself without a graft. Why does sparing this layer allow healing?","a":"It holds the dividing cells that regenerate the epidermis.","options":["It holds the dividing cells that rebuild the epidermis","It stores the skin's water supply","It forms the dead protective surface","It carries the skin's blood supply"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The stratum basale holds stem cells that divide to produce new keratinocytes. When it survives an injury, these cells can repopulate all the layers above it. If the basale is destroyed over a large area along with the follicles and glands, there is no source of new epidermis, and a graft is needed.","notes":["Correct. The basale's dividing cells rebuild the epidermis.","Water loss is limited by the sealant from lamellar granules in the upper epidermis, and the basale does not store water. Students choose this when they confuse barrier functions with regeneration.","The dead protective surface is the stratum corneum, at the opposite end of the epidermis. Pick the corneum if the question asked which stratum is shed and replaced.","The skin's blood supply lies in the dermis, and the basale, like the rest of the epidermis, is avascular. Choose the dermis if the question asked which layer feeds the epidermis."],"lt":"t-integumentary","lid":"c28-x2","comp":23,"competencyId":"w1-epidermal-strata"},{"id":"L00405","dok":2,"q":"Which order lists injection routes from the shallowest to the deepest tissue they reach?","a":"Intradermal, subcutaneous, intramuscular.","options":["Intradermal, subcutaneous, intramuscular","Intramuscular, subcutaneous, intradermal","Subcutaneous, intradermal, intramuscular","Intradermal, intramuscular, subcutaneous"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Each route is named for the layer it reaches. Intradermal places fluid within the dermis, subcutaneous places it in the hypodermis beneath the skin, and intramuscular places it in skeletal muscle deep to the hypodermis. The order of the routes matches the order of the layers: dermis, hypodermis, muscle.","notes":["Correct. Intradermal, subcutaneous, intramuscular runs from shallow to deep.","This is the correct order run backward, from deepest to shallowest. It would be the answer if the question asked for the routes from deep to shallow.","This order puts subcutaneous first, but the hypodermis lies beneath the dermis, so intradermal is shallower. Students choose it when they read sub as meaning just under the epidermis.","This order places intramuscular above subcutaneous, but muscle lies deep to the hypodermis. It would only be right if muscle sat between the dermis and the fatty layer, which it does not."],"lt":"t-integumentary","lid":"c29-x2","comp":22,"competencyId":"w1-skin-layers"},{"id":"L00406","dok":3,"q":"A full-thickness burn destroys the stratum basale, the hair follicles, and the sweat glands across a wide area. Why does this injury usually need a skin graft?","a":"No dividing epidermal cells remain in the wound to regenerate the epidermis.","options":["The graft is needed only to restore normal skin color","No living epidermal cells remain to regrow the epidermis","The dermis cannot survive without the epidermis above it","The hypodermis must be replaced before any healing can begin"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"New epidermis can come only from living epidermal cells, either in the stratum basale or in the linings of hair follicles and sweat glands. A full-thickness burn destroys all of these, so the wound has no source of new epidermis. A graft, a patch of healthy skin moved from a donor site, supplies that missing epidermis and protects against fluid loss and infection.","notes":["Skin grafts protect against fluid loss and infection, reduce scarring, and preserve function, so color is a minor benefit. This choice reflects a misconception that grafting is mainly cosmetic.","Correct. With the basale, follicles, and glands gone, nothing remains to regrow the epidermis.","In this burn the dermis itself has been destroyed, so its survival is not the issue. Students choose this because the dermis does feed the epidermis, but here the problem is the lost source of new cells.","The hypodermis is not part of the skin and does not have to be rebuilt first. Choose the hypodermis if the question asked which layer stores fat beneath the skin."],"lt":"t-integumentary","lid":"c30-x2","comp":23,"competencyId":"w1-epidermal-strata"},{"id":"L00407","dok":3,"q":"The epidermis has no blood vessels, yet its deepest cells are alive while its surface cells are dead. What explains the difference?","a":"Deep cells are close enough to the dermis to get nutrients by diffusion, while surface cells are too far away.","options":["Deep cells are fed directly by vessels of the hypodermis","Deep cells sit near dermal capillaries and get nutrients by diffusion","Surface cells had their own blood vessels that later closed off","Surface cells are killed each day by direct sunlight exposure"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Because the epidermis is avascular, every living epidermal cell depends on nutrients diffusing up from capillaries in the dermis. Cells in the deepest strata are close to that supply, but as they are pushed toward the surface they get farther away, fill with keratin, and die. The dead, keratin-filled surface layer then serves as the skin's tough barrier.","notes":["The hypodermis lies beneath the dermis and does not feed the epidermis directly. The dermis would be the answer for the layer whose capillaries nourish the epidermis.","Correct. Distance from the dermal capillaries decides which epidermal cells stay alive.","No part of the epidermis has blood vessels at any stage. Students choose this when they forget that the whole epidermis is avascular.","Sunlight can damage skin cells, but the surface cells die as part of normal maturation even in skin that never sees the sun. This choice reflects a misconception that the dead layer is sun damage."],"lt":"t-integumentary","lid":"d3-t-integumentary-1","comp":22,"competencyId":"w1-skin-layers"},{"id":"L00408","dok":3,"q":"A burn that destroys the full dermis heals with scar tissue that has no hair and no sweat glands, while a shallow scrape heals with both. Why does depth matter?","a":"Hair follicles and sweat glands sit in the dermis, so destroying the dermis removes them.","options":["Hair follicles and sweat glands sit in the dermis, so destroying the dermis removes them","Hair follicles and sweat glands sit in the epidermis, so any scrape removes them","Depth does not matter, and the difference comes from the heat of the burn alone","Scar tissue grows from the hypodermis, which cannot make glands"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Hair follicles and sweat glands begin as downgrowths of the epidermis but sit deep in the dermis. A shallow scrape leaves them intact, so they remain and also help rebuild the surface. A burn that destroys the whole dermis destroys these structures too, and the scar that fills the wound cannot rebuild them.","notes":["Correct. Destroying the dermis destroys the follicles and glands within it.","Follicles and glands extend deep into the dermis, which is why a shallow scrape leaves them behind. Students choose this because these structures develop from the epidermis, but they live in the dermis.","Depth is the deciding factor, since it determines whether the follicles and glands survive. This choice reflects the misconception that heat alone, not the layer reached, sets the outcome.","Scar tissue forms from connective tissue in the wound, and the hypodermis is not the source. Choose the hypodermis if the question asked which layer anchors the skin to the fascia."],"lt":"t-integumentary","lid":"d3-t-integumentary-2","comp":25,"competencyId":"w1-skin-accessory"},{"id":"L00409","dok":2,"q":"Inside a keratinocyte, melanin granules gather over the side of the nucleus that faces the skin surface. What does this placement accomplish?","a":"It shields the DNA in the nucleus from ultraviolet light.","options":["It shields the nuclear DNA from UV light","It toughens the cell against abrasion","It waterproofs the cell against water loss","It alerts immune cells to invading microbes"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Melanocytes pass melanin granules to keratinocytes, where the granules cluster into a protective veil over the surface-facing side of the nucleus. Melanin absorbs ultraviolet light, so the veil blocks UV rays before they can damage the DNA. Melanocytes themselves remain vulnerable to UV damage, since they protect other cells rather than themselves.","notes":["Correct. The melanin veil absorbs UV light before it reaches the DNA.","Toughness against abrasion comes from keratin, the fibrous protein that fills keratinocytes. It would be the answer for the main protective role of keratin.","Waterproofing comes from the sealant released by lamellar granules, which keratinocytes also make. Pick waterproofing if the question asked what lamellar granules do.","Alerting the immune system is the job of intraepidermal macrophages (Langerhans cells). Choose this if the question asked which epidermal cell responds to invading microbes."],"lt":"t-integumentary","lid":"d3-t-integumentary-3","comp":23,"competencyId":"w1-epidermal-strata"},{"id":"L00410","dok":2,"q":"When the body overheats, which pair of skin responses helps lower body temperature?","a":"Sweat production increases and dermal blood vessels dilate.","options":["Sweat production increases and dermal blood vessels constrict","Arrector pili contract and dermal blood vessels constrict","Sweat production increases and dermal blood vessels dilate","Sweat production decreases and dermal blood vessels constrict"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"When the body is too warm, dermal blood vessels dilate (widen) and bring warm blood close to the surface, where heat is lost to the environment. At the same time, sweat production increases, and evaporation of sweat cools the surface. Both responses aim to shed heat.","notes":["More sweat helps cool the body, but constricted vessels keep warm blood away from the surface and hold heat in. Students choose this when they remember only half of the heat-loss response.","Arrector pili contraction and vessel constriction go with being cold, producing goose bumps and keeping blood in the core. This would fit a question about responses to a chilly room.","Correct. More sweat and wider dermal vessels both release heat.","Less sweat and narrower vessels conserve heat, which is the skin's response to cold. Pick this pair if the question asked how the skin responds when the body is too cold."],"lt":"t-integumentary","lid":"d3-t-integumentary-4","comp":25,"competencyId":"w1-skin-accessory"},{"id":"L00411","dok":2,"q":"Thick skin on the palms and soles has a stratum lucidum and a much thicker stratum corneum. Why is this build found there?","a":"Palms and soles face constant friction and pressure, and extra keratinized layers resist wear.","options":["They face constant friction, and extra keratin layers resist wear","They absorb water, and extra layers draw water inward","They have the most melanocytes, and extra layers store the pigment","They hold more hair follicles, and extra layers anchor the hairs"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The palms, soles, and fingertips are the areas of greatest friction and pressure. The biggest contributor to epidermal thickness is an increased number of layers in the stratum corneum, which builds up in response to mechanical stress. These extra dead, keratin-filled layers protect the living tissue beneath.","notes":["Correct. Extra keratinized layers protect surfaces that take constant friction and pressure.","Keratin and the lamellar granule sealant limit water movement through the skin rather than drawing it in. This choice reflects a misconception that skin layers soak up water.","Melanocytes are most numerous in darker areas such as the nipples, areolae, and face, not the palms and soles. Choose the areolae if the question asked where melanocytes are especially numerous.","Thick skin has no hair follicles at all. Students choose this when they assume thicker skin must hold more structures; hair follicles would be the answer for a feature of thin skin."],"lt":"t-integumentary","lid":"d3-t-integumentary-5","comp":23,"competencyId":"w1-epidermal-strata"},{"id":"L00412","dok":3,"q":"A patient's nail beds and lips look bluish (cyanosis) because their blood is low in oxygen. Why does low oxygen change the visible color?","a":"Hemoglobin low in oxygen looks darker and bluer, and it shows through where dermal vessels are close to the surface.","options":["Melanocytes make a blue pigment when oxygen drops","Oxygen-poor hemoglobin looks bluish and shows through thin tissue","Carotene in the hypodermis turns blue when oxygen drops","The stratum corneum thickens and scatters blue light when oxygen drops"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Skin color includes a pink to red tone from hemoglobin in the dermal capillaries, and that tone depends on how much oxygen the blood carries. Blood low in oxygen looks darker and bluish, and the color shows through where tissue is thin and vessels lie near the surface. That is why cyanosis is most apparent in the nail beds, lips and other mucous membranes.","notes":["Melanocytes make melanin, a yellow-red to brown-black pigment, and they do not respond to blood oxygen. Pick melanocytes if the question asked what darkens skin after sun exposure.","Correct. Oxygen-poor hemoglobin looks bluish and shows through thin tissue.","Carotene is a yellow-orange pigment that does not change color with oxygen. It would be the answer if the question asked which pigment gives skin a yellowish tone from diet.","The stratum corneum thickens with friction, not with low oxygen. This choice reflects a guess that the color comes from the surface rather than from blood below it."],"lt":"t-integumentary","lid":"d3-t-integumentary-6","comp":24,"competencyId":"w1-dermis-layers"},{"id":"L00413","dok":3,"q":"When a dehydrated patient's skin is pinched, it stays tented instead of springing back. Which explanation fits?","a":"The dermis has lost tissue fluid, so it is less plump and recoils poorly.","options":["The hypodermis has gained extra fat that holds the fold","The dermis has lost tissue fluid, so it is less plump and recoils poorly","The melanocytes have stopped making pigment","The stratum corneum has dried out and locks the skin in place"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The dermis is a connective tissue with collagen and elastic fibers that let skin stretch and recoil. When the body is well hydrated, the dermis is full of tissue fluid and pinched skin springs back quickly. In dehydration, the dermis loses fluid, so a pinched fold returns slowly or stays tented.","notes":["Dehydration does not add fat, and the hypodermis is not what makes the skin spring back. Choose the hypodermis if the question asked which layer stores fat.","Correct. Loss of dermal fluid makes the skin slow to recoil.","Pigment production has nothing to do with skin recoil. Melanocytes would be the answer if the question asked why skin tans after UV exposure.","The stratum corneum is a thin layer of dead cells and cannot hold a fold of skin in place. Students choose it because dry skin and dehydration sound alike, but the recoil comes from the dermis."],"lt":"t-integumentary","lid":"d3-t-integumentary-7","comp":24,"competencyId":"w1-dermis-layers"},{"id":"L00414","dok":2,"q":"Why is most of the nail body pink while its free edge looks white?","a":"Capillaries lie beneath the nail body, but there are none beneath the free edge.","options":["The free edge is made of a different, softer keratin","Melanocytes beneath the free edge bleach the nail","Capillaries lie under the nail body but not the free edge","The nail body contains a pink pigment that the free edge lacks"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The nail body is clear, hard keratin, and it looks pink because the vascular tissue beneath it shows through. The free edge extends past the fingertip, so no capillaries lie under it and it looks white. The lunula looks whitish for a related reason: the epithelium beneath it is thickened, so the vessels do not show.","notes":["The nail body and free edge are the same hard keratin; only what lies beneath them differs. Pick a difference in keratin if the question asked how the nail body differs from the stratum corneum, since the nail holds a harder keratin and is not shed.","Melanocytes make pigment rather than bleach it, and they do not explain the white free edge. Choose melanocytes if the question asked which cells make the pigment for skin and hair color.","Correct. Blood vessels under the nail body make it pink, and the free edge has none beneath it.","The nail has no pink pigment of its own; the color comes from blood beneath it. Students choose this when they assume color must come from pigment rather than blood showing through."],"lt":"t-integumentary","lid":"d3-t-integumentary-8","comp":25,"competencyId":"w1-skin-accessory"},{"id":"L00415","dok":3,"q":"On a cold day, goose bumps appear on a person's forearm but never on the palm of the hand. Why?","a":"The palm is thick skin, which has no hair follicles or arrector pili.","options":["The palm is thick skin, which has no hair follicles or arrector pili","The palm's arrector pili are skeletal muscle and need a conscious command","The palm has extra sweat glands that keep it warm","The palm has a stratum lucidum that blocks muscle contraction"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Goose bumps form when arrector pili, small smooth muscles attached to hair follicles, contract and pull the hairs upright. Thick skin of the palms and soles has no hair follicles and therefore no arrector pili. With nothing to contract, goose bumps cannot form there, while hairy thin skin like the forearm shows them readily.","notes":["Correct. Without follicles and arrector pili, thick skin cannot form goose bumps.","Arrector pili are smooth muscle that contracts involuntarily, and the palm has none at all. Skeletal muscle would be the answer for muscle you move on purpose, such as the muscles that close the hand.","Sweat glands help cool the body, not warm it, and they are not involved in goose bumps. Eccrine glands would be the answer for which glands are especially numerous in palm skin.","The stratum lucidum is a clear epidermal layer and has no effect on muscle. Pick it if the question asked which stratum appears only in thick skin."],"lt":"t-integumentary","lid":"d3x-t-integumentary-1","comp":25,"competencyId":"w1-skin-accessory"},{"id":"L00418","dok":1,"q":"Which cell in the dermis engulfs bacteria and viruses that get past the defenses of the epidermis?","a":"The macrophage.","options":["The adipocyte","The macrophage","The intraepidermal macrophage (Langerhans cell)","The fibroblast"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The dermis contains a few macrophages, phagocytes that engulf bacteria, viruses, and debris that get past the epidermis. They are a second line of skin defense behind the intraepidermal macrophages of the epidermis. The other cells of the dermis are fibroblasts, which make fibers, and a few adipocytes near the subcutaneous layer.","notes":["Adipocytes are fat cells found near the boundary with the subcutaneous layer. They would be the answer for the cells that store fat.","Correct. Dermal macrophages engulf microbes that reach the dermis.","Intraepidermal macrophages (Langerhans cells) also defend the skin, but they live in the epidermis, not the dermis, and mainly alert the immune system to invaders. Choose them if the question asked which immune cell sits in the epidermis.","Fibroblasts are the main cells of the dermis and make its collagen and elastic fibers. Pick them if the question asked which cells build the dermal fibers."],"lt":"t-integumentary","lid":"w1g-ig-3","comp":24,"competencyId":"w1-dermis-layers"},{"id":"N-a17d-22-1","dok":3,"q":"A skin slide shows keratinized stratified squamous epithelium on top, dense irregular connective tissue below it, and a deep zone of large fat-filled cells. A pin sits in the fat zone. What is pinned, and is it part of the skin?","a":"The hypodermis, which is not part of the skin.","options":["The dermis, which is part of the skin.","The hypodermis, which is the deepest layer of the skin.","The hypodermis, which is not part of the skin.","The epidermis, which is part of the skin."],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The epidermis is keratinized stratified squamous epithelium, and the dermis below it is dense irregular connective tissue. Under them lies the hypodermis, the subcutaneous layer of areolar and adipose tissue. The skin itself is only the epidermis and the dermis, so the hypodermis is not part of it.","notes":["The dermis is the dense irregular connective tissue layer above the fat zone. It is part of the skin, but it is not where the pin sits.","Hypodermis is the right layer, but the skin has only two layers, the epidermis and the dermis. The hypodermis lies beneath the skin.","Correct. The fat zone is the hypodermis, which lies beneath the skin and is not part of it.","The epidermis is the keratinized stratified squamous epithelium on top, the shallowest layer, not the deep fat zone."],"comp":22,"competencyId":"w1-skin-layers"},{"id":"N-a17d-22-2","dok":3,"q":"A splinter passes through the epidermis and stops in the dense irregular connective tissue beneath it. Which layer has it reached, and would you expect it to bleed?","a":"The dermis, and yes, because the dermis holds blood vessels.","options":["The dermis, and yes, because the dermis holds blood vessels.","The dermis, and no, because the dermis has no blood vessels.","The hypodermis, and yes, because it holds large blood vessels.","The epidermis, and no, because the epidermis is avascular."],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The dense irregular connective tissue just beneath the epidermis is the dermis. The epidermis is avascular, but the dermis holds the blood vessels, nerves, and glands. A splinter that reaches the dermis can draw blood.","notes":["Correct. Dense irregular connective tissue under the epidermis is the dermis, which has blood vessels.","The dermis is right, but it is vascular. The layer with no blood vessels is the epidermis above it.","The hypodermis is deeper, made of areolar and adipose tissue, not dense irregular connective tissue. The splinter stopped above it.","The splinter has already passed through the epidermis. The epidermis is avascular, which is why a cut that stays in it does not bleed."],"comp":22,"competencyId":"w1-skin-layers"}]}]},{"id":"m2","n":2,"title":"Module 2. The Skeletal System","exam":"Exam 2","extra":false,"topics":[{"id":"m2-bone-tissue","title":"Bone tissue, the long bone and bone microanatomy","summary":"long bone parts, compact and spongy bone, osteon, bone cells, ossification, cartilage","mw":"2026-09-14","tr":"2026-09-10","cards":[{"id":"L00419","dok":1,"q":"What are the cells of cartilage called?","a":"Chondrocytes.","options":["Chondrocytes","Osteoblasts","Fibroblasts","Osteocytes"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Chondrocytes are the cells of cartilage. Each one sits in its own small cavity in the matrix called a lacuna, and together they maintain the firm, gel-like cartilage matrix around them. The prefix chondro- means cartilage, which is a quick way to separate them from the bone cells, whose names start with osteo-.","notes":["Correct. Chondrocytes are the cartilage cells, each housed in a lacuna.","Osteoblasts are the bone-building cells that secrete new bone matrix. Choose them if the question asked which cell lays down new bone.","Fibroblasts are the cells of connective tissue proper that make fibers such as collagen. They would fit a question about the main cell of dense or areolar connective tissue, not cartilage.","Osteocytes are mature bone cells sitting in lacunae within hard bone matrix. They would be the answer if the question asked which cell maintains bone matrix day to day."],"lt":"t-bone-histology","lid":"c1","comp":26,"competencyId":"w2-cartilage-types"},{"id":"L00420","dok":1,"q":"What is the connective tissue sheath wrapped around most cartilage called?","a":"The perichondrium.","options":["The perichondrium","The endosteum","The epimysium","The periosteum"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The perichondrium is a sheath of dense connective tissue that wraps most cartilage. The name breaks down as peri- (around) plus chondr- (cartilage). It is also the source of new cartilage during appositional growth, when cartilage is added at its outer surface.","notes":["Correct. The perichondrium is the sheath around most cartilage.","The endosteum is the thin membrane lining the internal surfaces of bone, such as the medullary cavity. Pick it if the question asked what lines the inside of a bone.","The epimysium is the connective tissue layer wrapping an entire skeletal muscle. It would be right for a question about muscle coverings, which follow the same peri- and epi- naming pattern.","The periosteum is the connective tissue membrane covering the outer surface of a bone. It would be the answer if the question were about the covering of a bone rather than of cartilage."],"lt":"t-bone-histology","lid":"c2","comp":26,"competencyId":"w2-cartilage-types"},{"id":"L00421","dok":1,"q":"Which type of cartilage is the most common in the body?","a":"Hyaline cartilage.","options":["Elastic cartilage","Fibrocartilage","Hyaline cartilage","Calcified cartilage"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Hyaline cartilage is the most common type. It has fine collagen fibers that give it a glassy, smooth look, and it is found at the ends of long bones, in the nose, in the airways, and in the costal cartilages that join the ribs to the sternum. It also forms the model that most bones replace as they develop.","notes":["Elastic cartilage has many elastic fibers, which make it flexible and springy. It would be the answer for a question about the cartilage of the external ear or the epiglottis.","Fibrocartilage has thick bundles of collagen, making it tough and shock-absorbing. Choose it for a question about the intervertebral discs, the knee menisci, or the pubic symphysis.","Correct. Hyaline cartilage is the most common cartilage in the body.","Calcified cartilage is not one of the three cartilage types. It describes cartilage whose matrix has hardened, as in the zone of calcification of the growth plate, so it fits a growth plate question instead."],"lt":"t-bone-histology","lid":"c3","comp":26,"competencyId":"w2-cartilage-types"},{"id":"L00422","dok":1,"q":"Which bone shape class describes the carpals and tarsals?","a":"Short bones.","options":["Long bones","Short bones","Flat bones","Irregular bones"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Short bones are roughly cube-shaped, about as long as they are wide. The carpals of the wrist and the tarsals of the ankle are the classic examples. Their blocky shape suits joints that need many small gliding movements rather than long levers.","notes":["Long bones are longer than they are wide, with a shaft and two ends, like the femur and humerus. They would be the answer if the question named the bones of the arm, thigh, or the metacarpals and phalanges.","Correct. The carpals and tarsals are short, roughly cube-shaped bones.","Flat bones are thin and often curved, like the sternum, the ribs, and most skull bones. Pick this class for a question about bones that protect organs as broad plates.","Irregular bones have complex shapes that fit no other group, like the vertebrae and hip bones. They would fit a question about a vertebra, not a wrist or ankle bone."],"lt":"t-bone-histology","lid":"c4","comp":28,"competencyId":"w2-bone-shapes"},{"id":"L00423","dok":1,"q":"What is the shaft of a long bone called?","a":"The diaphysis.","options":["The medullary cavity","The epiphysis","The diaphysis","The metaphysis"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The diaphysis is the shaft of a long bone, a tube of compact bone wrapped around a hollow medullary cavity. It forms most of the bone's length and carries weight along the long axis. The expanded ends on either side are the epiphyses.","notes":["The medullary cavity is the hollow space inside the diaphysis that holds marrow. It is inside the shaft rather than the shaft itself, so pick it for a question about where yellow marrow is stored.","The epiphysis is each expanded end of a long bone, made of spongy bone under a thin compact shell. It would be the answer if the question asked about the ends of the bone.","Correct. The diaphysis is the shaft of a long bone.","The metaphysis is the region between the shaft and an end, where the growth plate or its remnant sits. Choose it for a question about where the diaphysis and epiphysis meet."],"lt":"t-bone-histology","lid":"c5","comp":29,"competencyId":"w2-long-bone-gross"},{"id":"L00424","dok":1,"q":"What is the structural unit of compact bone called?","a":"The osteon.","options":["The osteon","The canaliculus","The trabecula","The lamella"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The osteon, also called the Haversian system, is the structural unit of compact bone. Each osteon is a cylinder of concentric rings of matrix (lamellae) around a central canal that carries blood vessels and nerves. Osteons run along the length of the bone, which makes compact bone strong along its long axis.","notes":["Correct. The osteon is the structural unit of compact bone.","A canaliculus is a tiny channel linking lacunae so osteocytes can exchange materials. Pick it for a question about how osteocytes communicate and receive nutrients.","A trabecula is a single strut in the open lattice of spongy bone. It would be the answer if the question asked for the structural unit of spongy bone instead of compact bone.","A lamella is one ring of matrix within an osteon. It is a part of the osteon rather than the whole unit, so choose it if the question asked about the rings around the central canal."],"lt":"t-bone-histology","lid":"c6","comp":30,"competencyId":"w2-compact-spongy"},{"id":"L00425","dok":1,"q":"Which bone cell builds and secretes new bone matrix?","a":"The osteoblast.","options":["The osteocyte","The osteoblast","The osteoclast","The osteogenic cell"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The osteoblast is the bone-building cell that secretes new bone matrix. Once an osteoblast becomes surrounded by the matrix it has made, it settles into a lacuna and becomes an osteocyte. A simple memory aid is that osteoblasts build bone.","notes":["The osteocyte is a mature bone cell in a lacuna that maintains the matrix day to day. Choose it if the question asked about the cell that keeps existing bone healthy rather than building new bone.","Correct. Osteoblasts build and secrete new bone matrix.","The osteoclast is the large, multinucleated cell that breaks down and resorbs bone matrix. It would be the answer if the question asked which cell removes bone.","The osteogenic cell is the stem cell of bone tissue that divides to produce new osteoblasts. Pick it for a question about which cell gives rise to the bone builders."],"lt":"t-bone-histology","lid":"c7","comp":31,"competencyId":"w2-bone-cells"},{"id":"L00426","dok":1,"q":"Which bone cell breaks down and resorbs bone matrix?","a":"The osteoclast.","options":["The osteoblast","The osteoclast","The osteocyte","The osteogenic cell"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The osteoclast breaks down and resorbs bone matrix. It is a large cell with many nuclei, and unlike the other three bone cells, it comes from a blood-cell line. Its work is balanced by osteoblasts, which build new matrix, so bone is constantly being reshaped.","notes":["The osteoblast builds and secretes new bone matrix, which is the opposite job. It would be the answer if the question asked which cell lays down bone.","Correct. Osteoclasts break down and resorb bone.","The osteocyte is a mature bone cell that maintains the matrix from inside a lacuna. Pick it for a question about the most common cell in mature bone.","The osteogenic cell is the bone stem cell that divides to make osteoblasts. Choose it if the question asked about the cell that starts the bone-building lineage."],"lt":"t-bone-histology","lid":"c8","comp":31,"competencyId":"w2-bone-cells"},{"id":"L00427","dok":1,"q":"Which type of cartilage is found in the external ear and the epiglottis?","a":"Elastic cartilage.","options":["Elastic cartilage","Hyaline cartilage","Fibrocartilage","Articular cartilage"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Elastic cartilage contains many elastic fibers in its matrix, which makes it flexible and springy. That is why the external ear bends and springs back, and why the epiglottis can flip down over the larynx and return to position. Both structures need to bend repeatedly without losing their shape.","notes":["Correct. Elastic cartilage forms the external ear and the epiglottis.","Hyaline cartilage has fine collagen fibers and a glassy look. It would be the answer for the nose, the airways, the costal cartilages, or the ends of long bones.","Fibrocartilage has thick collagen bundles and resists compression. Choose it for the intervertebral discs, the knee menisci, or the pubic symphysis.","Articular cartilage is the hyaline cartilage that caps the ends of bones at a joint. It is a location name rather than a separate type, so pick it only for a question about joint surfaces."],"lt":"t-bone-histology","lid":"c9","comp":26,"competencyId":"w2-cartilage-types"},{"id":"L00428","dok":1,"q":"What is the hollow core of the diaphysis that holds marrow called?","a":"The medullary cavity.","options":["The metaphysis","The medullary cavity","The lacuna","The central canal"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The medullary cavity is the hollow space running down the middle of the diaphysis. In an adult it stores yellow marrow, which is mostly fat. Making the shaft a hollow tube keeps the bone strong while saving weight.","notes":["The metaphysis is the region between the diaphysis and an epiphysis, holding the growth plate or its remnant. Pick it if the question asked where the shaft widens into the end.","Correct. The medullary cavity is the marrow-filled core of the diaphysis.","A lacuna is a tiny cavity in the matrix that houses one osteocyte or chondrocyte. Choose it for a question about where a single bone or cartilage cell sits.","The central canal is the small channel at the core of a single osteon, carrying blood vessels and nerves. It would be the answer if the question were about the microscopic canal inside an osteon, not the large cavity of the whole shaft."],"lt":"t-bone-histology","lid":"c10","comp":29,"competencyId":"w2-long-bone-gross"},{"id":"L00429","dok":1,"q":"What is the open lattice of bony struts that forms spongy bone called?","a":"Trabeculae.","options":["Lamellae","Trabeculae","Osteons","Canaliculi"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Trabeculae are the thin bony struts that form the open lattice of spongy bone. They line up along the lines of stress the bone experiences, so spongy bone stays strong while being much lighter than compact bone. The spaces between trabeculae hold red marrow.","notes":["Lamellae are the concentric rings of matrix in compact bone. They would be the answer for a question about the rings around a central canal.","Correct. Trabeculae are the bony struts of spongy bone.","Osteons are the cylindrical structural units of compact bone. Pick them if the question asked what compact bone is built from, since spongy bone has no osteons.","Canaliculi are tiny channels that connect lacunae so osteocytes can exchange materials. Choose them for a question about how osteocytes receive nutrients."],"lt":"t-bone-histology","lid":"c11","comp":30,"competencyId":"w2-compact-spongy"},{"id":"L00430","dok":2,"q":"Cartilage has no blood vessels of its own. What is the direct result of this?","a":"Cartilage heals slowly.","options":["It cannot bend or stretch","It heals slowly after injury","It contains no living cells","It contains no collagen fibers"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Cartilage is avascular, meaning it has no blood vessels of its own. Nutrients must diffuse slowly through the matrix to reach the chondrocytes, and repair cells and supplies cannot be delivered quickly. As a result, damaged cartilage, such as a torn meniscus, heals slowly and often incompletely.","notes":["Cartilage is actually firm but flexible, and elastic cartilage is especially springy. This choice confuses lacking blood vessels with lacking flexibility, which are unrelated properties.","Correct. Without blood vessels, cartilage repairs itself slowly.","Cartilage does contain living cells, the chondrocytes, sitting in lacunae. This choice mixes up having no blood vessels with having no cells.","All three cartilage types contain collagen, from the fine fibers of hyaline cartilage to the thick bundles of fibrocartilage. This choice reflects a misconception that avascular means fiber-free."],"lt":"t-bone-histology","lid":"c12","comp":26,"competencyId":"w2-cartilage-types"},{"id":"L00431","dok":2,"q":"Which type of cartilage forms the intervertebral discs and the knee menisci?","a":"Fibrocartilage.","options":["Articular cartilage","Fibrocartilage","Hyaline cartilage","Elastic cartilage"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Fibrocartilage contains thick bundles of collagen fibers, which make it tough and good at absorbing shock. That suits the intervertebral discs and the knee menisci, which take heavy compressive loads with every step. The pubic symphysis is another fibrocartilage joint.","notes":["Articular cartilage is the hyaline cartilage covering bone ends inside a joint. Pick it for a question about the smooth surface where two bones glide, not for discs or menisci.","Correct. Fibrocartilage forms the discs and the menisci.","Hyaline cartilage is the smooth, glassy, most common type. It would be the answer for the ends of long bones, the airways, the nose, or the costal cartilages.","Elastic cartilage is springy because of its many elastic fibers. Choose it for the external ear or the epiglottis."],"lt":"t-bone-histology","lid":"c13","comp":26,"competencyId":"w2-cartilage-types"},{"id":"L00432","dok":2,"q":"A small bone forms within a tendon, as the patella does. Which shape class does it belong to?","a":"Sesamoid bone.","options":["Short bone","Sesamoid bone","Flat bone","Irregular bone"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Sesamoid bones are small bones that develop inside a tendon. The patella, or kneecap, is the classic example, sitting in the tendon of the quadriceps muscle as it crosses the knee. The name comes from their resemblance to sesame seeds.","notes":["Short bones are roughly cube-shaped, like the carpals and tarsals. Choose this class for wrist and ankle bones.","Correct. A bone that forms within a tendon is a sesamoid bone.","Flat bones are thin, often curved plates such as the sternum, ribs, and most skull bones. Pick this class for a broad protective bone.","Irregular bones have complex shapes that fit no other group, like vertebrae and hip bones. They would be the answer for a bone defined by its odd shape rather than by forming in a tendon."],"lt":"t-bone-histology","lid":"c14","comp":28,"competencyId":"w2-bone-shapes"},{"id":"L00433","dok":2,"q":"Which membrane covers the outer surface of a bone, and which lines its internal surfaces?","a":"The periosteum covers the outside and the endosteum lines the inside.","options":["Periosteum outside, endosteum inside","Endosteum outside, periosteum inside","Periosteum outside, perichondrium inside","Perichondrium outside, endosteum inside"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The periosteum is the connective tissue membrane on the outer surface of a bone, and perforating (Sharpey) fibers anchor it firmly into the bone beneath. The endosteum is a thin membrane lining the internal surfaces, including the medullary cavity. The prefixes help: peri- means around, and endo- means within.","notes":["Correct. The periosteum is outside and the endosteum lines the inside.","This reverses the two membranes. Students choose it when they mix up the prefixes, but endo- means within, so the endosteum is always the inner lining.","The perichondrium is never inside a bone, since it is the sheath around cartilage. Choose it for a cartilage question, and keep the endosteum as the inner lining of bone.","The perichondrium wraps cartilage, not bone. It would be correct only if the question asked about the sheath around a piece of cartilage."],"lt":"t-bone-histology","lid":"c15","comp":29,"competencyId":"w2-long-bone-gross"},{"id":"L00434","dok":2,"q":"Which structure caps the epiphysis where one bone meets another at a joint?","a":"Articular cartilage.","options":["The epiphyseal line","The endosteum","Articular cartilage","The periosteum"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Articular cartilage is a layer of hyaline cartilage that caps the epiphysis where it meets another bone. Its smooth, slick surface reduces friction and cushions the bone ends as the joint moves. Because it has no blood supply, damage to it heals poorly.","notes":["The epiphyseal line is the bony remnant of the growth plate, found deep inside the bone at the metaphysis. Pick it for a question about what remains after growth in length ends.","The endosteum lines the internal surfaces of bone, such as the medullary cavity. Choose it if the question asked what lines the inside of a bone.","Correct. Articular cartilage caps the epiphysis at the joint.","The periosteum covers the outer bone surface everywhere except at the joint surfaces. It would be the answer for a question about the membrane covering the shaft."],"lt":"t-bone-histology","lid":"c16","comp":29,"competencyId":"w2-long-bone-gross"},{"id":"L00435","dok":2,"q":"Which canal of compact bone runs crosswise and links the central canals of neighboring osteons?","a":"The perforating canal, also called the Volkmann canal.","options":["A canaliculus","The central canal","The perforating canal","The medullary cavity"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Perforating canals, also called Volkmann canals, run crosswise through compact bone, roughly at right angles to the osteons. They connect the central canals of neighboring osteons with each other and with the periosteum and medullary cavity, so blood vessels and nerves can reach every osteon.","notes":["A canaliculus is a hair-thin channel connecting lacunae within an osteon. Choose it for a question about how individual osteocytes exchange materials.","The central canal runs lengthwise down the core of each osteon. It would be the answer if the question asked about the canal in the middle of one osteon.","Correct. Perforating canals run crosswise and link the central canals.","The medullary cavity is the large hollow core of the diaphysis that holds marrow. Pick it for a question about where marrow is stored in the shaft."],"lt":"t-bone-histology","lid":"c17","comp":30,"competencyId":"w2-compact-spongy"},{"id":"L00436","dok":2,"q":"Which tiny channels connect the lacunae and let osteocytes exchange materials?","a":"Canaliculi.","options":["Lamellae","Central canals","Trabeculae","Canaliculi"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Canaliculi are tiny channels running through the hard matrix between lacunae. They link osteocytes to each other and to the central canal, so nutrients and wastes can pass from cell to cell. Without them, osteocytes buried in mineralized matrix could not survive.","notes":["Lamellae are the rings of matrix around the central canal. They are the solid layers that canaliculi pass through, so choose them for a question about the concentric rings of an osteon.","Central canals are the larger channels at the core of each osteon, carrying blood vessels and nerves. They would be the answer if the question asked where the blood supply of an osteon runs.","Trabeculae are the bony struts of spongy bone. Pick them for a question about the lattice that fills the epiphyses.","Correct. Canaliculi connect lacunae so osteocytes can exchange materials."],"lt":"t-bone-histology","lid":"c18","comp":30,"competencyId":"w2-compact-spongy"},{"id":"L00437","dok":2,"q":"Which bone cell comes from a blood-cell line, unlike the other three bone cells?","a":"The osteoclast.","options":["The osteoclast","The osteocyte","The osteoblast","The osteogenic cell"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Three of the four bone cells form one lineage: the osteogenic cell divides to make osteoblasts, and osteoblasts become osteocytes. The osteoclast is the outsider. It comes from a blood-cell line, is large with many nuclei, and is the only bone cell that takes bone away.","notes":["Correct. The osteoclast arises from a blood-cell line.","The osteocyte is a mature osteoblast that has become trapped in matrix, so it is part of the bone lineage. Choose it for a question about the cell maintaining matrix inside a lacuna.","The osteoblast comes from the osteogenic cell, so it belongs to the bone lineage. It would be the answer if the question asked which cell secretes new matrix.","The osteogenic cell is the stem cell at the start of the bone lineage. Pick it if the question asked which cell divides to produce osteoblasts."],"lt":"t-bone-histology","lid":"c19","comp":31,"competencyId":"w2-bone-cells"},{"id":"L00438","dok":2,"q":"Which route of ossification builds the flat bones of the skull?","a":"Intramembranous ossification.","options":["Appositional growth","Endochondral ossification","Interstitial growth","Intramembranous ossification"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"In intramembranous ossification, bone forms directly within a sheet of connective tissue membrane, with no cartilage model first. This is how the flat bones of the skull form. The name tells the story: intra- (within) a membrane.","notes":["Appositional growth is how a bone grows wider by adding bone beneath the periosteum. It describes growth of an existing bone, so choose it for a question about a bone thickening.","Endochondral ossification builds bone by replacing a hyaline cartilage model. It would be the answer for most bones of the body, such as the long bones of the limbs.","Interstitial growth is growth from within, as at the epiphyseal plate when a long bone lengthens. Pick it for a question about how a long bone grows longer.","Correct. The flat skull bones form by intramembranous ossification."],"lt":"t-bone-histology","lid":"c20","comp":32,"competencyId":"w2-ossification-growth"},{"id":"L00439","dok":2,"q":"Which route of ossification forms most bones of the body by replacing a cartilage model?","a":"Endochondral ossification.","options":["Intramembranous ossification","Endochondral ossification","Interstitial growth","Appositional growth"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Endochondral ossification forms most bones of the body. A model of hyaline cartilage is laid down first, and bone gradually replaces it. The name means within (endo-) cartilage (chondr-), which describes where the bone forms.","notes":["Intramembranous ossification forms bone directly within a connective tissue membrane, with no cartilage model. It would be the answer for the flat bones of the skull.","Correct. Endochondral ossification replaces a cartilage model and forms most bones.","Interstitial growth is growth from within, which lengthens a long bone at the epiphyseal plate. Pick it for a question about growth in length.","Appositional growth adds bone at the outer surface so a bone grows wider. Choose it for a question about thickening, not about how a bone first forms."],"lt":"t-bone-histology","lid":"c21","comp":32,"competencyId":"w2-ossification-growth"},{"id":"L00440","dok":2,"q":"At which structure does a long bone grow longer?","a":"At the epiphyseal plate.","options":["At the articular cartilage","At the periosteum","At the epiphyseal plate","At the medullary cavity"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The epiphyseal plate, or growth plate, is a disc of hyaline cartilage in the metaphysis between the shaft and the end. Cartilage there keeps growing and is replaced by bone on the shaft side, which pushes the epiphysis farther away and lengthens the bone. When growth ends, the plate closes and leaves the epiphyseal line.","notes":["Articular cartilage caps the bone end at the joint and does not drive lengthening. Pick it for a question about the smooth joint surface.","The periosteum is where a bone grows wider, through appositional growth. It would be the answer if the question asked where a bone increases in width.","Correct. Long bones lengthen at the epiphyseal plate.","The medullary cavity is the hollow marrow space in the shaft. It enlarges as the bone grows, but it is not where lengthening happens. Choose it for a question about where yellow marrow is stored."],"lt":"t-bone-histology","lid":"c22","comp":32,"competencyId":"w2-ossification-growth"},{"id":"L00441","dok":2,"q":"Appositional growth changes a long bone in which way?","a":"The bone grows wider.","options":["It loses its marrow","It grows wider","It grows longer","It becomes shorter"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Appositional growth adds new bone at the outer surface, beneath the periosteum, so the bone grows wider. Apposition means placing something next to something else, which describes laying new layers on the surface. At the same time, bone is removed from the inner surface so the medullary cavity widens too.","notes":["Appositional growth does not remove marrow. This choice confuses adding bone on the outside with the inner remodeling that actually enlarges the marrow cavity.","Correct. Appositional growth makes a bone wider.","Growth in length happens through interstitial growth at the epiphyseal plate. This would be the answer if the question asked about interstitial growth.","No normal growth process makes a bone shorter. This choice reflects a mix-up between growth and bone loss."],"lt":"t-bone-histology","lid":"c23","comp":32,"competencyId":"w2-ossification-growth"},{"id":"L00442","dok":2,"q":"Which type of cartilage growth adds new cartilage at the outer surface, from the perichondrium?","a":"Appositional growth.","options":["Interstitial growth","Endochondral ossification","Appositional growth","Intramembranous ossification"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Cartilage grows in two directions. In appositional growth, cells from the perichondrium add new cartilage at the outer surface. In interstitial growth, chondrocytes divide inside the matrix and the cartilage expands from within.","notes":["Interstitial growth expands cartilage from within, as chondrocytes divide inside the matrix. It would be the answer if the question described growth from the inside.","Endochondral ossification is the process of bone replacing a cartilage model. Choose it for a question about how most bones form, not how cartilage grows.","Correct. Appositional growth adds cartilage at the surface from the perichondrium.","Intramembranous ossification forms bone directly within a membrane. Pick it for a question about the flat bones of the skull."],"lt":"t-bone-histology","lid":"c24","comp":27,"competencyId":"w2-cartilage-growth"},{"id":"L00443","dok":1,"q":"What is the remnant of the growth plate after growth in length ends called?","a":"The epiphyseal line.","options":["The articular cartilage","The epiphyseal line","The epiphyseal plate","The diaphysis"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"When a long bone stops growing in length, the cartilage of the growth plate is fully replaced by bone. What is left is a thin bony line, the epiphyseal line, in the metaphysis. Seeing it on an X-ray tells a clinician the skeleton is mature.","notes":["Articular cartilage is the hyaline cartilage on the joint surface of the epiphysis. Choose it for a question about what covers the bone end at a joint.","Correct. The epiphyseal line is the remnant of the closed growth plate.","The epiphyseal plate is the active cartilage growth plate of a still-growing bone. It would be the answer if the question asked where a child's bone lengthens.","The diaphysis is the shaft of the long bone. Pick it for a question about the long middle portion of the bone."],"lt":"t-bone-histology","lid":"c25","comp":29,"competencyId":"w2-long-bone-gross"},{"id":"L00444","dok":2,"q":"Which structural difference distinguishes spongy bone from compact bone?","a":"Spongy bone has no osteons.","options":["Spongy bone has more osteons","Spongy bone has no bone cells","Spongy bone has no osteons","Spongy bone has no matrix"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Compact bone is built from osteons, each with a central canal. Spongy bone has no osteons; instead it is an open lattice of trabeculae with red marrow in the spaces. On a slide, the quickest way to tell them apart is to look for central canals.","notes":["This reverses the real difference. Osteons are the units of compact bone, so a tissue full of them would be compact bone, not spongy bone.","Spongy bone does contain bone cells, including osteocytes in lacunae within the trabeculae. Students pick this when they confuse lacking osteons with lacking cells.","Correct. Spongy bone is built from trabeculae and has no osteons.","Spongy bone has true bone matrix in every trabecula. This choice mistakes the open spaces between struts for an absence of matrix."],"lt":"t-bone-histology","lid":"c26","comp":30,"competencyId":"w2-compact-spongy"},{"id":"L00445","dok":2,"q":"Which structure houses a single osteocyte within compact bone?","a":"A lacuna.","options":["A lamella","A lacuna","A canaliculus","A central canal"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"A lacuna is a small cavity in the matrix, and in bone each one holds a single osteocyte. Lacunae sit between the lamellae of an osteon and are linked to each other by canaliculi. Cartilage uses the same arrangement, with a chondrocyte in each lacuna.","notes":["A lamella is one ring of bony matrix around the central canal. Choose it for a question about the concentric layers of an osteon.","Correct. Each osteocyte sits in its own lacuna.","A canaliculus is a hair-thin channel running between lacunae. It would be the answer for a question about how osteocytes exchange materials.","The central canal is the channel at the core of an osteon carrying blood vessels and nerves. Pick it if the question asked what runs down the center of the osteon."],"lt":"t-bone-histology","lid":"c27","comp":30,"competencyId":"w2-compact-spongy"},{"id":"L00446","dok":2,"q":"A bone is described as longer than it is wide, with a shaft and two ends. Which shape class and example fit this description?","a":"A long bone, such as the humerus.","options":["An irregular bone, such as a vertebra","A short bone, such as a carpal","A flat bone, such as the sternum","A long bone, such as the humerus"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Long bones are defined by being longer than they are wide, with a shaft (the diaphysis) and two expanded ends (the epiphyses). The humerus fits that description exactly. Size does not decide the class, since even the small phalanges of the fingers are long bones.","notes":["A vertebra is an irregular bone with a complex shape. This pairing fits a question about bones that fit no other shape group.","A carpal is a short bone, roughly cube-shaped. This pairing fits a question about blocky wrist or ankle bones.","The sternum is a flat bone, thin and plate-like, with no shaft. This pairing would be right for a question about a broad, thin protective bone.","Correct. The humerus is a long bone with a shaft and two ends."],"lt":"t-bone-histology","lid":"c28","comp":28,"competencyId":"w2-bone-shapes"},{"id":"L00447","dok":2,"q":"A compact bone slide shows concentric rings of matrix surrounding a single canal that carries blood vessels. What is the canal at the core?","a":"The central canal.","options":["A perforating canal","The medullary cavity","The central canal","A canaliculus"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The rings are lamellae, and together with the canal they form an osteon. The canal at the core of each osteon is the central canal, also called the Haversian canal, and it carries blood vessels and nerves along the length of the bone. Finding central canals on a slide is how you confirm you are looking at compact bone.","notes":["A perforating canal runs crosswise between osteons rather than sitting at the center of the rings. It would be the answer for a canal that links neighboring central canals.","The medullary cavity is the large marrow space of the whole shaft, not a canal inside one osteon. Pick it for a question about where yellow marrow is stored.","Correct. The central canal sits at the core of the osteon.","A canaliculus is a hair-thin channel between lacunae, far too small to carry blood vessels. Choose it for a question about how osteocytes connect to one another."],"lt":"t-bone-histology","lid":"c29","comp":30,"competencyId":"w2-compact-spongy"},{"id":"L00448","dok":2,"q":"A child's X-ray of a long bone shows a dark band of cartilage between the shaft and the expanded end. Which structure is this band?","a":"The epiphyseal plate.","options":["The epiphyseal plate","The medullary cavity","The articular cartilage","The epiphyseal line"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The epiphyseal plate is a disc of hyaline cartilage in the metaphysis, between the diaphysis and the epiphysis. Cartilage is not mineralized, so on an X-ray it looks like a gap or dark band in the bone. Its presence means the bone is still growing in length.","notes":["Correct. The cartilage band in a child's bone is the epiphyseal plate.","The medullary cavity is the marrow space inside the shaft, running lengthwise, not a band across the bone near its end. Choose it for a question about where marrow is stored.","Articular cartilage sits on the outer joint surface of the epiphysis, not between the shaft and the end. Pick it for a question about the surface where two bones meet.","The epiphyseal line is the bony remnant left after the plate closes, so it shows up in adults, not as a cartilage band in a child. It would be the answer for an adult X-ray."],"lt":"t-bone-histology","lid":"c30","comp":32,"competencyId":"w2-ossification-growth"},{"id":"L00449","dok":2,"q":"A section through the epiphysis of a long bone shows an open lattice of bony struts with marrow in the spaces and no osteons. Which tissue is this?","a":"Spongy bone.","options":["Spongy bone","Hyaline cartilage","Fibrocartilage","Compact bone"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The epiphyses of long bones are filled with spongy bone under a thin shell of compact bone. Spongy bone is an open lattice of trabeculae, the bony struts, with red marrow filling the spaces between them. It has no osteons, and that missing central canal is the clue that separates it from compact bone.","notes":["Correct. A lattice of trabeculae with marrow and no osteons is spongy bone.","Hyaline cartilage is a smooth, glassy tissue with chondrocytes in lacunae and no bony struts. Choose it for a section of articular cartilage or the growth plate.","Fibrocartilage has thick parallel bundles of collagen and no marrow spaces. Pick it for a section of an intervertebral disc or a knee meniscus.","Compact bone is dense and built from osteons with central canals. It would be the answer if the section showed tightly packed rings around canals, as in the wall of the shaft."],"lt":"t-bone-histology","lid":"c31","comp":30,"competencyId":"w2-compact-spongy"},{"id":"L00450","dok":2,"q":"A long bone is increasing in width, with new bone being added at its outer surface beneath the periosteum. Which type of growth is this?","a":"Appositional growth.","options":["Interstitial growth","Appositional growth","Endochondral ossification","Intramembranous ossification"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Appositional growth adds new bone in layers at the outer surface, beneath the periosteum, so the bone gets wider. It continues throughout life, which is why bones can thicken in response to use. Growth in length is a separate process that happens at the epiphyseal plate.","notes":["Interstitial growth is growth from within, which lengthens a long bone at the epiphyseal plate. It would be the answer if the bone were getting longer rather than wider.","Correct. Adding bone beneath the periosteum to widen the bone is appositional growth.","Endochondral ossification is how most bones first form by replacing a cartilage model. Pick it for a question about how a long bone develops before birth.","Intramembranous ossification is how flat skull bones first form within a membrane. Choose it for a question about the origin of the skull bones, not the widening of a long bone."],"lt":"t-bone-histology","lid":"c32","comp":32,"competencyId":"w2-ossification-growth"},{"id":"L00451","dok":2,"q":"A flat bone of the skull is forming directly within a sheet of connective tissue membrane, with no cartilage model. Which route of ossification is this?","a":"Intramembranous ossification.","options":["Intramembranous ossification","Interstitial growth","Endochondral ossification","Appositional growth"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Intramembranous ossification forms bone directly inside a connective tissue membrane, skipping the cartilage stage. The flat bones of the skull form this way. The absence of a cartilage model is the clue that rules out endochondral ossification.","notes":["Correct. Bone forming directly within a membrane is intramembranous ossification.","Interstitial growth is growth from within an existing tissue, such as a long bone lengthening at its growth plate. Choose it for a question about growth in length.","Endochondral ossification needs a hyaline cartilage model that bone then replaces. It would be the answer for a long bone of the limb forming from cartilage.","Appositional growth adds layers at the surface to make a bone wider. Pick it for a question about a bone thickening beneath the periosteum."],"lt":"t-bone-histology","lid":"c33","comp":32,"competencyId":"w2-ossification-growth"},{"id":"L00452","dok":2,"q":"Which list gives the five zones of the growth plate in order, from the epiphysis toward the diaphysis?","a":"Resting cartilage, proliferation, hypertrophy, calcification, ossification.","options":["Proliferation, resting cartilage, hypertrophy, calcification, ossification","Ossification, calcification, hypertrophy, proliferation, resting cartilage","Resting cartilage, hypertrophy, proliferation, ossification, calcification","Resting cartilage, proliferation, hypertrophy, calcification, ossification"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The growth plate reads like a timeline from the epiphysis down. Resting cartilage anchors the plate to the epiphysis, cells in the zone of proliferation divide and stack into columns, cells in the zone of hypertrophy enlarge, the matrix hardens in the zone of calcification, and bone replaces the cartilage in the zone of ossification next to the diaphysis.","notes":["This puts proliferation ahead of resting cartilage. The resting zone must come first because it is the layer that anchors the plate to the epiphysis.","This is the correct sequence read backward, from the diaphysis toward the epiphysis. It would be right only if the question asked for the order starting at the shaft.","This swaps two pairs. Cells must divide before they can enlarge, and the matrix must calcify before bone can replace it, so the true order is proliferation then hypertrophy, and calcification then ossification.","Correct. Resting, proliferation, hypertrophy, calcification, ossification is the order."],"lt":"t-bone-histology","lid":"c34","comp":32,"competencyId":"w2-ossification-growth"},{"id":"L00453","dok":2,"q":"Trace the path of nutrients from a blood vessel in the central canal of an osteon to an osteocyte buried in the matrix. Which order is correct?","a":"Central canal, then canaliculi, then the lacuna holding the osteocyte.","options":["Perforating canal, then a lamella, then a lacuna","Central canal, then a lacuna, then canaliculi","Central canal, then canaliculi, then a lacuna","A lacuna, then canaliculi, then the central canal"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Blood vessels run in the central canal at the core of the osteon. From there, nutrients pass outward through canaliculi, the tiny channels that cut through the hard lamellae, until they reach the lacuna where the osteocyte sits. Wastes travel the same route in reverse.","notes":["Nutrients cannot pass through a solid lamella, which is a ring of hard matrix. A perforating canal would start the route only if the vessel came in crosswise from another osteon, and canaliculi would still be needed.","This skips the connecting channels. A lacuna is the destination, and nutrients can only reach it through canaliculi, so the lacuna must come last.","Correct. Nutrients move from the central canal through canaliculi to the lacuna.","This is the route in reverse, from the cell to the blood. It would be correct if the question asked how wastes leave an osteocyte."],"lt":"t-bone-histology","lid":"c35","comp":30,"competencyId":"w2-compact-spongy"},{"id":"L00454","dok":2,"q":"Which list orders the regions of a long bone along a straight line from the joint surface at one end into the shaft?","a":"Articular cartilage, epiphysis, metaphysis, diaphysis.","options":["Epiphysis, articular cartilage, diaphysis, metaphysis","Articular cartilage, metaphysis, epiphysis, diaphysis","Articular cartilage, epiphysis, metaphysis, diaphysis","Diaphysis, metaphysis, epiphysis, articular cartilage"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"At the joint, articular cartilage caps the end of the bone. Beneath it is the epiphysis, the expanded end filled with spongy bone. Next comes the metaphysis, which holds the growth plate or epiphyseal line, and then the diaphysis, the shaft with the medullary cavity in its center.","notes":["Articular cartilage is on the outer joint surface, so nothing in the bone lies outside it. This order also places the shaft before the metaphysis, which reverses their real relationship.","This swaps the epiphysis and metaphysis. The metaphysis is the transition zone between the end and the shaft, so it cannot sit directly under the joint surface.","Correct. Articular cartilage, epiphysis, metaphysis, then diaphysis.","This is the right sequence read in reverse, from the shaft out to the joint. It would be correct if the question started in the diaphysis."],"lt":"t-bone-histology","lid":"c36","comp":29,"competencyId":"w2-long-bone-gross"},{"id":"L00455","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly pairs the route that builds the flat bones of the skull with the type of growth that lengthens a long bone?","a":"Intramembranous ossification builds the flat skull bones, and interstitial growth lengthens a long bone.","options":["Intramembranous builds them, and appositional growth lengthens a bone","Endochondral builds them, and interstitial growth lengthens a bone","Endochondral builds them, and appositional growth lengthens a bone","Intramembranous builds them, and interstitial growth lengthens a bone"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The flat bones of the skull form by intramembranous ossification, directly within a connective tissue membrane. Long bones lengthen by interstitial growth, growth from within at the epiphyseal plate. Appositional growth works at the surface and makes bones wider, not longer.","notes":["The first half is right, but appositional growth makes a bone wider. This choice would be right only if the question asked how a long bone grows in width.","The growth half is right, but the flat skull bones do not form from a cartilage model. Endochondral ossification would be right for the femur or humerus.","Both halves are wrong here. Endochondral ossification forms most other bones from a cartilage model, and appositional growth widens bones, so this pairing would fit a question about limb bones growing in width.","Correct. Intramembranous for the flat skull bones, interstitial growth for length."],"lt":"t-bone-histology","lid":"c37","comp":32,"competencyId":"w2-ossification-growth"},{"id":"L00456","dok":2,"q":"Which set correctly names three parts of an osteon: the canal at its center, the rings of matrix around that canal, and the small cavities between those rings?","a":"Central canal, lamellae, lacunae.","options":["Central canal, lamellae, lacunae","Medullary cavity, lamellae, canaliculi","Perforating canal, lamellae, canaliculi","Central canal, trabeculae, lacunae"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Each osteon has a central canal at its core carrying blood vessels and nerves. Concentric rings of bony matrix, the lamellae, surround the canal. Between the lamellae are small cavities, the lacunae, and each lacuna holds one osteocyte.","notes":["Correct. Central canal, lamellae, and lacunae are the three named parts.","The medullary cavity is the marrow space of the whole shaft, not the center of a single osteon. Canaliculi connect lacunae but are not the cavities that hold osteocytes.","A perforating canal runs crosswise between osteons rather than at the center of one, and canaliculi are channels, not cavities. This set would partly fit a question about the connecting passages of compact bone.","Trabeculae are the struts of spongy bone, which has no osteons at all. They would belong in a set describing spongy bone instead."],"lt":"t-bone-histology","lid":"c38","comp":30,"competencyId":"w2-compact-spongy"},{"id":"L00457","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly matches each cartilage type to a structure it forms: the airways, the external ear, and an intervertebral disc?","a":"Hyaline cartilage forms the airways, elastic cartilage forms the external ear, and fibrocartilage forms an intervertebral disc.","options":["Fibrocartilage for the airways, hyaline for the ear, elastic for the disc","Elastic for the airways, hyaline for the ear, fibrocartilage for the disc","Hyaline for the airways, fibrocartilage for the ear, elastic for the disc","Hyaline for the airways, elastic for the ear, fibrocartilage for the disc"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Each cartilage type matches the job of its location. Hyaline cartilage, smooth and firm, holds the airways open. Elastic cartilage, packed with elastic fibers, lets the external ear bend and spring back. Fibrocartilage, with thick collagen bundles, absorbs the compression in an intervertebral disc.","notes":["This scrambles all three. Fibrocartilage is tough and compression-resistant, which suits a disc, not the airways, and the flexible ear needs elastic cartilage.","This swaps hyaline and elastic cartilage. The airway rings are hyaline, and the springiness of the ear comes from elastic fibers.","The airway match is right, but the ear and the disc are swapped. A disc needs the tough collagen bundles of fibrocartilage, while the bendable ear needs elastic cartilage.","Correct. Hyaline for the airways, elastic for the ear, fibrocartilage for the disc."],"lt":"t-bone-histology","lid":"c39","comp":26,"competencyId":"w2-cartilage-types"},{"id":"L00458","dok":2,"q":"Which list orders the bone cells along a single developmental line, from the stem cell to the cell that maintains mature matrix?","a":"Osteogenic cell, osteoblast, osteocyte.","options":["Osteogenic cell, osteoclast, osteocyte","Osteocyte, osteoblast, osteogenic cell","Osteoblast, osteogenic cell, osteocyte","Osteogenic cell, osteoblast, osteocyte"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The osteogenic cell is the stem cell of bone tissue, and it divides to produce osteoblasts. An osteoblast builds matrix, and once it is surrounded by that matrix it becomes an osteocyte, which maintains the bone day to day. The osteoclast is not part of this line, since it comes from a blood-cell line.","notes":["This places the osteoclast in the bone lineage. Osteoclasts come from a blood-cell line and break bone down, so they do not turn into osteocytes.","This is the correct lineage read backward. Mature osteocytes do not turn back into stem cells, so the line runs only from the osteogenic cell forward.","This puts the osteoblast before its own stem cell. The osteogenic cell has to come first because it is the cell that divides to produce osteoblasts.","Correct. Osteogenic cell, then osteoblast, then osteocyte."],"lt":"t-bone-histology","lid":"c40","comp":31,"competencyId":"w2-bone-cells"},{"id":"L00460","dok":1,"q":"In cartilage, what is the small cavity in the matrix that holds each chondrocyte called?","a":"A lacuna.","options":["A central canal","A canaliculus","A lacuna","A trabecula"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Each chondrocyte sits in a small cavity in the cartilage matrix called a lacuna, which means little lake. Bone uses the same word for the cavity that holds an osteocyte. In cartilage, nutrients reach the chondrocyte by diffusing through the matrix, since cartilage has no blood vessels.","notes":["A central canal is the channel at the core of an osteon carrying blood vessels. Cartilage has no central canals, so this choice fits only a compact bone question.","A canaliculus is a tiny channel in bone that links lacunae to each other. It would be the answer for a question about how osteocytes connect, a feature cartilage does not have.","Correct. A chondrocyte sits in a lacuna.","A trabecula is a bony strut of spongy bone. Pick it for a question about the lattice inside the epiphysis."],"lt":"t-bone-histology","lid":"c2-x2","comp":26,"competencyId":"w2-cartilage-types"},{"id":"L00461","dok":1,"q":"Which bone shape class includes the femur and the humerus?","a":"Long bones.","options":["Irregular bones","Short bones","Long bones","Flat bones"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Long bones are longer than they are wide, with a shaft and two ends. The femur of the thigh and the humerus of the arm are the textbook examples. Their shape makes them strong levers for movement.","notes":["Irregular bones have complex shapes that fit no other class, like the vertebrae and hip bones. Choose this class for a vertebra.","Short bones are roughly cube-shaped, like the carpals and tarsals. They would be the answer for a question about wrist or ankle bones.","Correct. The femur and humerus are long bones.","Flat bones are thin and often curved, like the sternum, ribs, and most skull bones. Pick this class for broad protective bones."],"lt":"t-bone-histology","lid":"c4-x2","comp":28,"competencyId":"w2-bone-shapes"},{"id":"L00462","dok":1,"q":"Which bone is the classic example of a sesamoid bone?","a":"The patella.","options":["The sternum","The calcaneus","The scapula","The patella"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"A sesamoid bone is a small bone that forms within a tendon. The patella, or kneecap, develops inside the tendon of the quadriceps muscle where it crosses the front of the knee. That position lets it protect the tendon and improve the muscle's leverage.","notes":["The sternum is a flat bone in the midline of the chest. Choose it for a question about flat bones that protect the heart.","The calcaneus is the heel bone, one of the tarsals, so it is a short bone. Pick it for a question about ankle bones or the bone the calcaneal tendon attaches to.","The scapula is a flat bone, a broad triangular plate on the back of the thorax. It would be the answer for a question about the shoulder blade or a flat bone of the pectoral girdle.","Correct. The patella is the classic sesamoid bone."],"lt":"t-bone-histology","lid":"c5-x2","comp":28,"competencyId":"w2-bone-shapes"},{"id":"L00463","dok":1,"q":"The central canal at the core of an osteon is also known by which eponym?","a":"The Haversian canal.","options":["The nutrient canal","The Haversian canal","The Volkmann canal","The Sharpey canal"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The central canal is also called the Haversian canal, and the whole osteon is called the Haversian system. It runs lengthwise at the core of each osteon and carries blood vessels and nerves. Eponyms like this show up on lab labels, so it helps to pair each one with its plain name.","notes":["The nutrient foramen is the opening in the shaft where the nutrient artery enters the bone. Choose it for a question about where the main artery gets into a long bone.","Correct. The central canal is the Haversian canal.","The Volkmann canal is the perforating canal, which runs crosswise and links central canals. It would be the answer if the question asked for the other name of the perforating canal.","Sharpey is the name attached to the perforating fibers, the collagen bundles that anchor the periosteum to bone. There is no Sharpey canal, so this choice mixes up a fiber with a canal."],"lt":"t-bone-histology","lid":"c9-x2","comp":30,"competencyId":"w2-compact-spongy"},{"id":"L00464","dok":1,"q":"What are the concentric rings of bony matrix around the central canal called?","a":"Lamellae.","options":["Lamellae","Trabeculae","Lacunae","Canaliculi"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Lamellae are the thin rings of bony matrix layered around the central canal of an osteon, like the rings of a tree trunk. These concentric lamellae give the osteon its strength. Lacunae holding osteocytes sit between them.","notes":["Correct. The concentric rings around the central canal are lamellae.","Trabeculae are the struts of spongy bone. They would be the answer for a question about the lattice that fills the ends of long bones.","Lacunae are the small cavities between the lamellae that each hold an osteocyte. Choose them for a question about where bone cells sit.","Canaliculi are tiny channels connecting lacunae. Pick them for a question about how osteocytes exchange nutrients and wastes."],"lt":"t-bone-histology","lid":"c10-x2","comp":30,"competencyId":"w2-compact-spongy"},{"id":"L00465","dok":2,"q":"The trabeculae of spongy bone are not arranged randomly. Along what are they aligned?","a":"Along lines of stress.","options":["Along the periosteum","Along the central canals","Along the epiphyseal line","Along lines of stress"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Trabeculae line up along the lines of stress the bone experiences, so each strut carries load in the direction force actually travels. This lets spongy bone stay strong while remaining light. Bone remodels over time, so the pattern shifts if the loads on the bone change.","notes":["The periosteum is the outer covering of the bone, lying on compact bone rather than among the trabeculae. Choose it for a question about the membrane on the outer surface.","Spongy bone has no osteons and so no central canals to line up with. This choice mixes up the structure of spongy bone with that of compact bone.","The epiphyseal line is the remnant of the growth plate, a thin band across the bone. Trabeculae pass through that region, but they are oriented by load, not by the line. Pick it for a question about what remains after growth in length ends.","Correct. Trabeculae are aligned along lines of stress."],"lt":"t-bone-histology","lid":"c11-x2","comp":30,"competencyId":"w2-compact-spongy"},{"id":"L00466","dok":2,"q":"An osteoblast becomes surrounded by the matrix it has secreted. Which cell does it become?","a":"An osteocyte.","options":["An osteogenic cell","A chondrocyte","An osteoclast","An osteocyte"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Osteoblasts build bone by secreting matrix around themselves. Once an osteoblast is fully surrounded, it sits in a lacuna and becomes an osteocyte, a mature bone cell that maintains the matrix day to day. This is the last step of the osteogenic cell to osteoblast to osteocyte lineage.","notes":["The osteogenic cell is the stem cell that comes before the osteoblast, so an osteoblast cannot turn back into one. Pick it for a question about the cell that produces osteoblasts.","A chondrocyte is a cartilage cell in a lacuna of cartilage matrix. Choose it for a question about cartilage, since bone cells do not become chondrocytes.","The osteoclast comes from a blood-cell line, not from osteoblasts, and it breaks bone down. It would be the answer for a question about the cell that resorbs bone.","Correct. An osteoblast surrounded by its own matrix becomes an osteocyte."],"lt":"t-bone-histology","lid":"c16-x2","comp":31,"competencyId":"w2-bone-cells"},{"id":"L00469","dok":2,"q":"In interstitial growth of cartilage, where does the new cartilage come from?","a":"Chondrocytes dividing inside the matrix.","options":["Osteoblasts beneath the periosteum","Cells of the perichondrium at the surface","Chondrocytes dividing inside the matrix","Osteoclasts in the medullary cavity"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Cartilage grows in two ways. In interstitial growth, chondrocytes divide inside the matrix and the new cells secrete more matrix, so the cartilage expands from within. Interstitial means within the spaces, which describes growth from the inside out.","notes":["Osteoblasts beneath the periosteum add bone, not cartilage, when a bone grows wider. Choose this for a question about appositional growth of bone.","Cells of the perichondrium add cartilage at the outer surface, which is appositional growth. This would be the answer if the question asked about appositional growth.","Correct. Interstitial growth comes from chondrocytes dividing within the matrix.","Osteoclasts break bone down rather than building anything. Pick them for a question about which cell resorbs bone matrix."],"lt":"t-bone-histology","lid":"c19-x2","comp":27,"competencyId":"w2-cartilage-growth"},{"id":"L00470","dok":2,"q":"A bone cell maintains the bone matrix day to day from within a lacuna. Which cell is this?","a":"The osteocyte.","options":["The osteocyte","The osteogenic cell","The osteoblast","The osteoclast"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Osteocytes are mature bone cells, former osteoblasts that became trapped in the matrix they made. Each sits in its own lacuna and maintains the surrounding matrix, staying connected to its neighbors through canaliculi. They are the most common cells in mature bone.","notes":["Correct. The osteocyte maintains the matrix from inside a lacuna.","The osteogenic cell is the stem cell that divides to produce osteoblasts. Choose it for a question about where new bone-building cells come from.","The osteoblast builds new matrix on bone surfaces and is not yet enclosed in a lacuna. It would be the answer for a question about the cell that secretes new bone.","The osteoclast is a large multinucleated cell that resorbs bone. Pick it for a question about the cell that breaks bone down."],"lt":"t-bone-histology","lid":"c20-x2","comp":31,"competencyId":"w2-bone-cells"},{"id":"L00471","dok":2,"q":"Articular cartilage, which caps the ends of bones at a joint, is made of which type of cartilage?","a":"Hyaline cartilage.","options":["Dense regular connective tissue","Fibrocartilage","Elastic cartilage","Hyaline cartilage"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Articular cartilage is hyaline cartilage. Its fine collagen fibers give it a smooth, glassy surface, so the bone ends glide over each other with little friction. Like all cartilage it lacks blood vessels, which is why worn joint surfaces heal poorly.","notes":["Dense regular connective tissue has parallel collagen fibers and forms tendons and ligaments. Choose it for a question about the tissue connecting muscle to bone.","Fibrocartilage is tough and shock-absorbing, and it forms the menisci inside the knee. Students sometimes choose it for that reason, but the cartilage covering the bone ends themselves is hyaline. Pick fibrocartilage for the menisci or intervertebral discs.","Elastic cartilage is springy and flexible. It would be the answer for the external ear or the epiglottis.","Correct. Articular cartilage is hyaline cartilage."],"lt":"t-bone-histology","lid":"c21-x2","comp":26,"competencyId":"w2-cartilage-types"},{"id":"L00472","dok":2,"q":"Which type of marrow fills the spaces between the trabeculae of spongy bone in the epiphyses?","a":"Red marrow.","options":["Yellow marrow","Hyaline cartilage","Compact bone","Red marrow"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Red marrow fills the spaces of spongy bone in the epiphyses and is the site of blood cell formation. Yellow marrow, which is mostly stored fat, fills the medullary cavity of the adult shaft. Remembering where each sits helps you place spongy bone at the ends and the marrow cavity in the shaft.","notes":["Yellow marrow is fat stored in the medullary cavity of the adult diaphysis. It would be the answer for a question about what fills the hollow core of the shaft.","Hyaline cartilage covers the joint surface and forms the growth plate, but it does not fill the spaces between trabeculae. Pick it for a question about the articular surface.","Compact bone forms the dense outer shell around the spongy bone, not the material inside its spaces. Choose it for a question about the wall of the shaft.","Correct. Red marrow fills the spaces of spongy bone."],"lt":"t-bone-histology","lid":"c22-x2","comp":29,"competencyId":"w2-long-bone-gross"},{"id":"L00474","dok":2,"q":"Which description fits the osteoclast?","a":"A large cell with many nuclei that resorbs bone.","options":["A cell on the bone surface that secretes matrix","A large cell with many nuclei that resorbs bone","A small cell in a lacuna that maintains matrix","A stem cell that divides to make bone builders"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The osteoclast is a large, multinucleated cell that breaks down and resorbs bone matrix. It comes from a blood-cell line, unlike the other three bone cells, and it works alongside osteoblasts as bone is constantly reshaped.","notes":["This describes the osteoblast, which builds new matrix on bone surfaces. Pick it for a question about the bone-forming cell.","Correct. The osteoclast is large, multinucleated, and resorbs bone.","This describes the osteocyte, a mature bone cell sitting in a lacuna. It would be the answer for a question about the cell that keeps existing matrix healthy.","This describes the osteogenic cell, the stem cell of bone tissue. Choose it for a question about where osteoblasts come from."],"lt":"t-bone-histology","lid":"c24-x2","comp":31,"competencyId":"w2-bone-cells"},{"id":"L00475","dok":2,"q":"During endochondral ossification, bone replaces a model made of which tissue?","a":"Hyaline cartilage.","options":["Fibrocartilage","A fibrous connective tissue membrane","Hyaline cartilage","Elastic cartilage"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"In endochondral ossification, a model of the future bone is first laid down in hyaline cartilage. Bone then gradually replaces that cartilage, and the last cartilage left in a growing long bone is at the epiphyseal plate and the articular surface. Most bones of the body form this way.","notes":["Fibrocartilage forms the intervertebral discs and menisci and is not the model for developing bones. Pick it for a question about shock-absorbing pads.","A fibrous connective tissue membrane is the starting tissue for intramembranous ossification. Choose it for a question about how the flat bones of the skull form.","Correct. Endochondral ossification replaces a hyaline cartilage model.","Elastic cartilage stays as cartilage in the external ear and epiglottis and is not replaced by bone. It would be the answer for a question about those flexible structures."],"lt":"t-bone-histology","lid":"c25-x2","comp":32,"competencyId":"w2-ossification-growth"},{"id":"L00476","dok":3,"q":"The growth plate shows up as a gap on a child's X-ray. Why does it look like a gap?","a":"It is made of cartilage, which is not mineralized like bone.","options":["It is the medullary cavity filled with marrow","It is cartilage, which is not mineralized like bone","It is a fracture line that has not yet healed","It is a space left where osteoclasts removed bone"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"X-rays show mineralized bone clearly, while unmineralized tissue such as cartilage lets the beam pass and looks dark. The epiphyseal plate is a disc of hyaline cartilage, so it appears as a gap between the shaft and the end of the bone. Once growth ends and bone replaces the plate, the gap is gone and a thin epiphyseal line remains.","notes":["The medullary cavity runs lengthwise inside the shaft, not across the bone near its end. Pick it for a question about where yellow marrow is stored.","Correct. The plate is cartilage, so it does not show like mineralized bone.","A fracture can also appear as a dark line, which is why students confuse the two. A growth plate sits in a predictable spot at the metaphysis, so this choice would only fit an irregular break line after an injury.","Osteoclasts resorb bone in small areas during remodeling, but they do not create a band across the bone. This choice mixes up normal growth cartilage with bone loss."],"lt":"t-bone-histology","lid":"c26-x2","comp":32,"competencyId":"w2-ossification-growth"},{"id":"L00477","dok":2,"q":"An adult X-ray shows an epiphyseal line at the end of a long bone. What does this finding tell the clinician?","a":"The growth plate has closed and the skeleton is mature.","options":["The growth plate has closed","The bone is being resorbed by osteoclasts","The bone is still growing in length","The bone has a recent fracture across its end"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The epiphyseal line is the bony remnant of the growth plate. It forms when the cartilage of the plate has been fully replaced by bone at the end of growth in length. Seeing a line instead of a cartilage gap tells the clinician the skeleton is mature.","notes":["Correct. An epiphyseal line means the plate has closed.","Osteoclasts remodel bone throughout life, but that process does not produce the epiphyseal line. Pick resorption for a question about loss of bone density, not a normal growth landmark.","An actively growing bone would show the epiphyseal plate as a cartilage gap. This choice would be right if the X-ray showed a gap rather than a line.","A fracture line is irregular and follows the injury, while the epiphyseal line sits in a fixed position at the metaphysis. This choice confuses a normal landmark with an injury."],"lt":"t-bone-histology","lid":"c27-x2","comp":29,"competencyId":"w2-long-bone-gross"},{"id":"L00478","dok":3,"q":"A long bone tends to break under a twisting force rather than a force along its length. Which structural feature explains this?","a":"Its osteons are aligned with the long axis","options":["Its osteons are aligned with the long axis","It is made entirely of spongy bone","Its osteons lie across the long axis","It has no perforating canals"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The osteons of compact bone run parallel to the long axis of the bone, along the lines of stress it normally carries. That makes a long bone very strong against forces along its length, such as body weight. A twisting force acts off that axis, against the grain of the osteons, so it is the force most likely to cause a break.","notes":["Correct. Osteons run with the long axis, so twisting forces off that axis are the dangerous ones.","The shaft of a long bone is compact bone, with spongy bone mainly in the epiphyses. This choice would describe the inside of the bone ends, not the shaft.","This reverses the real arrangement. Osteons run lengthwise along the shaft, in line with the body weight the bone normally carries, not across it.","Long bones do have perforating canals, which carry vessels crosswise between osteons. This choice treats a blood vessel channel as a strength feature, which it is not."],"lt":"t-bone-histology","lid":"c28-x2","comp":30,"competencyId":"w2-compact-spongy"},{"id":"L00479","dok":2,"q":"The five zones of the growth plate run from the epiphysis to the diaphysis. Which zone lies closest to the epiphysis?","a":"The zone of resting cartilage.","options":["The zone of hypertrophy","The zone of resting cartilage","The zone of calcification","The zone of ossification"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The zone of resting cartilage is the first zone, sitting right against the epiphysis. Its job is to anchor the growth plate to the epiphysis. Moving toward the shaft, the cells divide, enlarge, calcify, and are finally replaced by bone in the zone of ossification beside the diaphysis.","notes":["The zone of hypertrophy is the third zone, where chondrocytes enlarge. Pick it for a question about where the cartilage cells swell.","Correct. The zone of resting cartilage lies closest to the epiphysis and anchors the plate.","The zone of calcification is the fourth zone, where the cartilage matrix hardens. Choose it for a question about where the matrix calcifies just before bone forms.","The zone of ossification is the last zone, next to the diaphysis, where bone replaces the cartilage. It would be the answer if the question asked which zone is closest to the shaft."],"lt":"t-bone-histology","lid":"c29-x2","comp":32,"competencyId":"w2-ossification-growth"},{"id":"L00480","dok":3,"q":"A child breaks a bone, and the fracture runs through the growth plate. Why do clinicians describe pediatric fractures by how they cross the plate?","a":"A fracture through the plate can slow or stop the bone from growing.","options":["The plate is the strongest region of a child's bone","Breaks through the plate always heal faster than others","The plate is where the nutrient artery enters the bone","A break through the plate can slow or stop growth in length"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The epiphyseal plate is where a long bone grows in length. If a fracture damages the plate, that growth can slow or stop, which may leave the limb shorter or crooked. Describing the fracture by how it crosses the plate tells the care team how much risk there is to future growth.","notes":["The plate is cartilage, which is weaker than the bone around it, so it is actually a common site of injury in children. This choice reverses the real situation.","There is no rule that growth plate fractures heal faster, and cartilage generally heals slowly. This choice ignores the real concern, which is future growth.","The nutrient artery enters through the nutrient foramen in the shaft, not at the growth plate. Pick the nutrient foramen for a question about how the main artery reaches the marrow.","Correct. Damage to the plate can disturb growth in length."],"lt":"t-bone-histology","lid":"c30-x2","comp":32,"competencyId":"w2-ossification-growth"},{"id":"L00481","dok":3,"q":"Compact bone is built from osteons, each with a central canal. Why does each osteon need a canal at its core?","a":"It carries blood vessels to supply osteocytes buried in hard matrix.","options":["It stores yellow marrow for each individual osteon","It brings blood vessels to the buried osteocytes","It holds the single osteocyte that maintains the osteon","It anchors the periosteum firmly to the bone surface"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Mineralized bone matrix is too hard and dense for nutrients to diffuse through it. The central canal brings blood vessels (and nerves) right into the middle of each osteon, and canaliculi carry nutrients from there out to the osteocytes in their lacunae. Without that core blood supply, the cells deep in compact bone would die.","notes":["Yellow marrow is stored in the medullary cavity of the shaft, not inside individual osteons. Pick the medullary cavity for a question about where fat is stored in a long bone.","Correct. The central canal brings a blood supply to the osteocytes.","Each osteon contains many osteocytes, each sitting in its own lacuna between the lamellae. Pick the lacuna for a question about where a single bone cell lives.","Perforating (Sharpey) fibers, bundles of collagen, are what anchor the periosteum to the bone. Choose them for a question about how the outer membrane stays attached."],"lt":"t-bone-histology","lid":"d3-t-bone-histology-1","comp":30,"competencyId":"w2-compact-spongy"},{"id":"L00482","dok":3,"q":"Spongy bone fills the ends of long bones, while compact bone forms the walls of the shaft. Why does spongy bone suit the ends?","a":"Its trabeculae line up along stress lines, giving strength with less weight.","options":["It is denser than compact bone, so it adds the most strength","The ends carry no load, so a weaker tissue is enough","Its trabeculae give strength with less weight","It contains the growth plate, so it lets the bone lengthen"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The ends of long bones receive forces from several directions at the joint. Spongy bone handles this with trabeculae, struts aligned along the lines of stress, so it is strong where force travels while staying light. Its spaces also hold red marrow, the site of blood cell formation.","notes":["Spongy bone is lighter and less dense than compact bone, not denser. This choice reverses the two tissues.","The ends of long bones carry large loads at every joint. This choice mistakes the lighter design of spongy bone for weakness.","Correct. Trabeculae aligned with stress give strength at low weight.","The growth plate is a separate cartilage disc in the metaphysis, and it is the cartilage, not the spongy bone, that allows lengthening. Pick the epiphyseal plate for a question about where length is added."],"lt":"t-bone-histology","lid":"d3-t-bone-histology-2","comp":30,"competencyId":"w2-compact-spongy"},{"id":"L00483","dok":3,"q":"Bone is constantly remodeled, with osteoblasts building and osteoclasts breaking down. Why is ongoing breakdown, not just building, part of healthy bone?","a":"Removing old bone lets bone repair small damage, reshape to fit its loads, and release stored calcium.","options":["It lets bone repair, reshape, and release calcium","Osteoclasts build new bone, so more breakdown adds bone mass","Osteoclasts turn into osteoblasts once they finish resorbing","Breakdown happens only in disease and not in healthy bone"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Remodeling pairs osteoclasts, which remove old or damaged matrix, with osteoblasts, which lay down new matrix in its place. This lets bone repair tiny cracks, reshape itself to the loads it carries, and release calcium it stores when the body needs it. Building alone could only add bone, never correct or reshape it.","notes":["Correct. Breakdown is half of a repair and reshaping cycle.","This swaps the cell roles. Osteoclasts resorb bone, and osteoblasts are the builders, so this choice would be right only if the names were reversed.","Osteoclasts come from a blood-cell line and do not become osteoblasts. Osteoblasts come from osteogenic cells, so this choice mixes up the two lineages.","Osteoclasts are active in healthy bone every day. This choice confuses normal remodeling with disease, where breakdown outpaces building."],"lt":"t-bone-histology","lid":"d3-t-bone-histology-3","comp":31,"competencyId":"w2-bone-cells"},{"id":"L00484","dok":3,"q":"Osteocytes sit in lacunae and connect through thin channels called canaliculi. Why are the canaliculi essential to osteocyte survival?","a":"They let nutrients and wastes pass between the cells and the blood vessel, since hard matrix blocks diffusion.","options":["They carry blood vessels deep into the center of the osteon","They let nutrients and wastes move between cells and blood vessels","They store calcium crystals away from the osteocytes","They hold the osteocytes in place between the lamellae"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Bone matrix is mineralized and too dense for nutrients to diffuse through. Canaliculi are tiny channels that run from lacuna to lacuna and back to the central canal, where the blood vessels are. Osteocytes extend thin processes through these channels and pass nutrients and wastes cell to cell.","notes":["Blood vessels run in the central canal and in perforating canals, which are much larger than canaliculi. Pick the central canal for a question about where the osteon's vessels run.","Correct. Canaliculi are the supply lines between the osteocytes and the blood.","Calcium is stored in the mineralized matrix itself, not in the canaliculi. This choice mixes up a channel with the mineral store of bone.","Lacunae are the cavities that hold each osteocyte in place. Choose the lacuna for a question about where an osteocyte sits."],"lt":"t-bone-histology","lid":"d3-t-bone-histology-4","comp":30,"competencyId":"w2-compact-spongy"},{"id":"L00485","dok":3,"q":"The organic part of bone matrix is mostly collagen, and the inorganic part is mostly calcium mineral. Why does bone need both?","a":"Collagen gives flexibility and resists tension, while mineral gives hardness and resists compression.","options":["Both resist only compression, so either one alone would work","Collagen anchors the periosteum, while mineral lines the marrow cavity","Collagen resists tension and bending, while mineral resists compression","Mineral provides flexibility, while collagen provides hardness"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Collagen fibers give bone some flexibility and resistance to pulling and bending forces, while calcium mineral makes it hard and resistant to crushing. Bone with too little mineral bends too easily, and bone with too little collagen becomes brittle and snaps. Together they make a tissue that is both hard and tough.","notes":["The two components resist different forces. If either one alone were enough, bone would not become brittle when collagen is lacking or soft when mineral is lacking.","Perforating fibers made of collagen do anchor the periosteum, but that is not why collagen is in the matrix itself, and mineral does not line the marrow cavity. The endosteum is what lines the cavity.","Correct. Collagen handles tension, and mineral handles compression.","This reverses the two components. Mineral is the hard part of the matrix, and collagen supplies the flexibility."],"lt":"t-bone-histology","lid":"d3-t-bone-histology-5"},{"id":"L00486","dok":3,"q":"In a child the epiphyseal plate is cartilage, and in an adult it becomes a bony line. Why must the plate be cartilage while the bone is growing in length?","a":"Cartilage can grow from within and then be replaced by bone, which lengthens the bone.","options":["Cartilage carries the blood supply to the growing bone end","Bone can expand from within its matrix as easily as cartilage","Cartilage can grow from within and then be replaced by bone","Cartilage is stronger than bone at bearing body weight"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Cartilage can grow interstitially, meaning its cells divide and expand the tissue from within. At the growth plate, new cartilage keeps forming on the epiphysis side while bone replaces older cartilage on the shaft side, so the bone lengthens. Hard bone matrix cannot expand from within, which is why the plate has to stay cartilage until growth ends.","notes":["Cartilage has no blood vessels of its own, so it cannot be the blood supply for anything. This choice ignores one of cartilage's defining features.","Bone can only add matrix at its surfaces, not swell from within, because its matrix is mineralized. That limitation is exactly why lengthening needs a cartilage plate.","Correct. Cartilage grows from within and is then replaced by bone.","Cartilage is actually weaker than bone, which is why growth plates are a common fracture site in children. This choice reverses their strength."],"lt":"t-bone-histology","lid":"d3-t-bone-histology-6","comp":32,"competencyId":"w2-ossification-growth"},{"id":"L00487","dok":3,"q":"A weight lifter's bones become denser over years of training. Why does repeated mechanical stress increase bone density?","a":"Bone remodels in response to load, with osteoblasts adding matrix where stress is greatest.","options":["Loading reopens the epiphyseal plates so adult bones grow","Loading turns the cartilage of the growth plate into new bone","Osteoblasts add matrix where stress is high","Loading makes osteoclasts switch to building new matrix"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Bone is living tissue that is constantly remodeled. When a bone carries more load, osteoblasts add matrix where stress is greatest and the trabeculae realign along the new stress lines, so the bone becomes denser and stronger. When load is removed, as in long bed rest, the balance shifts the other way and bone is lost.","notes":["Closed epiphyseal plates do not reopen, and adult bones do not grow longer with training. Density increases, not length.","In an adult the growth plate has already closed into the epiphyseal line, so there is no plate cartilage left to convert. This choice confuses growth in childhood with adaptation in adults.","Correct. Bone adapts to load through remodeling.","Osteoclasts always resorb bone; they do not switch to building. The building is done by osteoblasts, so this choice swaps the cell roles."],"lt":"t-bone-histology","lid":"d3-t-bone-histology-7","comp":31,"competencyId":"w2-bone-cells"},{"id":"L00488","dok":3,"q":"After a fracture, a soft cartilage callus forms first and is later replaced by bone. Why does the body build cartilage before bone?","a":"Cartilage can form without a blood supply and bridges the gap until vessels regrow and bone replaces it.","options":["Osteoclasts build the cartilage callus, which only they can make","The cartilage callus lengthens the bone to make up for the break","Cartilage can form without a blood supply and bridges the gap first","Cartilage is the permanent repair tissue and stays in place"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"A fracture tears the blood vessels at the break, leaving the area with a poor blood supply at first. Cartilage does not need blood vessels of its own, so it can form a callus that bridges the bone ends early. As new vessels grow in, the cartilage is replaced by bone, much as bone replaces cartilage during endochondral ossification.","notes":["Osteoclasts resorb bone and play no part in making cartilage. The callus cartilage is made by chondrocytes that develop at the fracture site.","The callus joins the broken ends; it does not add length. Growth in length happens only at an open epiphyseal plate.","Correct. Avascular cartilage can bridge the break before blood supply returns.","The cartilage callus is temporary and is replaced by a bony callus that is later remodeled. This choice stops the repair story too early."],"lt":"t-bone-histology","lid":"d3-t-bone-histology-8","comp":26,"competencyId":"w2-cartilage-types"},{"id":"L00489","dok":3,"q":"The flat bones of the skull ossify directly within a fibrous membrane, while long bones form from a cartilage model. How does each route suit each bone?","a":"Thin protective plates can form directly as sheets, while long bones need a cartilage model that allows lengthwise growth.","options":["The route a bone takes has no link to its shape or growth","Long bones form in membranes so they can widen; skull bones need cartilage","Skull bones need a cartilage model so that they can grow in length","Skull plates form directly as sheets; long bones need cartilage to lengthen"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The flat skull bones are thin protective plates, and intramembranous ossification builds them directly as sheets inside a membrane, without a cartilage stage. Long bones form by endochondral ossification from a hyaline cartilage model, and the cartilage that remains at the epiphyseal plates lets them keep growing in length through childhood.","notes":["The two routes do match the bones they build: flat sheets from membranes, and growing levers from cartilage models with growth plates. This choice misses that link.","This swaps the two routes. Long bones are the ones with a cartilage model, and the skull's flat bones form in membranes.","Flat skull bones form without a cartilage model, and they do not lengthen at growth plates the way long bones do. This choice applies the long bone pattern to the skull.","Correct. Each route matches the shape and growth needs of the bone."],"lt":"t-bone-histology","lid":"d3-t-bone-histology-9","comp":32,"competencyId":"w2-ossification-growth"},{"id":"L00490","dok":1,"q":"Which lamellae run around the entire outer and inner circumference of the shaft, deep to the periosteum and around the medullary cavity?","a":"Circumferential lamellae.","options":["Circumferential lamellae","Trabeculae","Interstitial lamellae","Concentric lamellae"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Circumferential lamellae are layers of bone that wrap the entire outer surface of the shaft, just under the periosteum, and the entire inner surface around the medullary cavity. They belong to the whole bone rather than to any single osteon. The name comes from circumference, the distance around a circle.","notes":["Correct. Circumferential lamellae wrap the whole shaft inside and out.","Trabeculae are the struts of spongy bone, not layers of compact bone. Choose them for a question about the lattice inside the epiphysis.","Interstitial lamellae are leftover fragments of old osteons filling the gaps between intact ones. Pick them for a question about the partial rings between osteons.","Concentric lamellae are the rings around the central canal of one osteon. They would be the answer for a question about the layers of a single osteon."],"lt":"t-bone-histology","lid":"w2g-bh-1","comp":30,"competencyId":"w2-compact-spongy"},{"id":"L00491","dok":1,"q":"What are the incomplete lamellae wedged between intact osteons, left over from old osteons during remodeling, called?","a":"Interstitial lamellae.","options":["Interstitial lamellae","Concentric lamellae","Trabeculae","Circumferential lamellae"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Interstitial lamellae are fragments of older osteons that were partly broken down during remodeling. They fill the irregular gaps between the intact osteons. Interstitial means in between, which matches where they sit.","notes":["Correct. The leftover fragments between osteons are interstitial lamellae.","Concentric lamellae are the complete rings around a central canal. They would be the answer for a question about the rings of a whole osteon.","Trabeculae are the struts of spongy bone. Choose them for a question about the lattice in the ends of long bones.","Circumferential lamellae wrap the outer and inner surfaces of the shaft. Pick them for a question about the layers just beneath the periosteum."],"lt":"t-bone-histology","lid":"w2g-bh-2","comp":30,"competencyId":"w2-compact-spongy"},{"id":"L00492","dok":2,"q":"A compact bone slide shows complete rings around a central canal, partial fragments between those units, and layers wrapping the bone's outer edge. Which list names these lamellae in that order?","a":"Concentric, interstitial, then circumferential.","options":["Interstitial, concentric, then circumferential","Circumferential, interstitial, then concentric","Concentric, interstitial, then circumferential","Concentric, circumferential, then interstitial"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Complete rings around a central canal are concentric lamellae, the rings of an osteon. The partial fragments between osteons are interstitial lamellae, left from older osteons. Layers wrapping the whole outer or inner surface of the shaft are circumferential lamellae.","notes":["This swaps the first two. The complete rings of an osteon are concentric lamellae, and the fragments between osteons are interstitial lamellae.","This reverses the first and last terms. Circumferential lamellae wrap the edge of the bone, so they match the third description, not the first.","Correct. Concentric, interstitial, then circumferential.","This swaps the last two. Fragments between osteons are interstitial, and the layers around the bone's edge are circumferential."],"lt":"t-bone-histology","lid":"w2g-bh-3","comp":30,"competencyId":"w2-compact-spongy"},{"id":"N-m2-bone-tissue-401","dok":1,"q":"Which bone shape class includes the sternum, the ribs, and most skull bones?","a":"Flat bones.","options":["Flat bones","Long bones","Irregular bones","Short bones"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Flat bones are thin and often curved. The sternum, the ribs, and most bones of the skull are flat bones. Their broad, plate-like shape makes them good at protecting organs, such as the brain in the cranium and the heart and lungs in the thoracic cage.","notes":["Correct. The sternum, ribs, and most skull bones are flat bones.","Long bones are longer than they are wide, with a shaft and two ends, like the femur and humerus. They would be the answer for a question about the bones of the limbs.","Irregular bones have complex shapes that fit no other class, like the vertebrae and hip bones. Choose this class for a question about a vertebra.","Short bones are roughly cube-shaped, like the carpals and tarsals. Pick this class for a question about wrist and ankle bones."],"comp":28,"competencyId":"w2-bone-shapes"},{"id":"N-m2-bone-tissue-402","dok":1,"q":"Which bone shape class includes the vertebrae and the hip bones?","a":"Irregular bones.","options":["Sesamoid bones","Flat bones","Short bones","Irregular bones"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Irregular bones have complex shapes that do not fit the long, short, flat, or sesamoid groups. The vertebrae, with their bodies, arches, and processes, and the hip bones are the standard examples. Their shapes reflect the many muscles, joints, and openings they have to accommodate.","notes":["Sesamoid bones are small bones formed within a tendon, like the patella. Pick this class for a question about the kneecap.","Flat bones are thin, often curved plates such as the sternum, ribs, and most skull bones. They would be the answer for a question about the bones that roof the cranium.","Short bones are roughly cube-shaped, like the carpals and tarsals. Choose this class for a question about wrist or ankle bones.","Correct. The vertebrae and hip bones are irregular bones."],"comp":28,"competencyId":"w2-bone-shapes"},{"id":"N-m2-bone-tissue-403","dok":2,"q":"What anchors the periosteum firmly into the underlying bone?","a":"Perforating (Sharpey) fibers.","options":["Perforating (Sharpey) fibers","Circumferential lamellae","Trabeculae","Canaliculi"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Perforating fibers, also called Sharpey fibers, are bundles of collagen that run from the periosteum into the underlying bone matrix. They pin the periosteum tightly to the bone surface. This firm attachment matters because tendons and ligaments pull on bone through the periosteum.","notes":["Correct. Perforating (Sharpey) fibers anchor the periosteum.","Circumferential lamellae are layers of bone just under the periosteum, wrapping the shaft. They lie beneath the periosteum, but they are bone layers, not the fibers that tie it down. Choose them for a question about the outer layers of compact bone.","Trabeculae are the struts of spongy bone, deep inside the bone ends. Pick them for a question about the lattice that holds red marrow.","Canaliculi are tiny channels connecting lacunae inside the bone, used for exchange of nutrients and wastes. They would be the answer for a question about how osteocytes communicate."],"comp":29,"competencyId":"w2-long-bone-gross"},{"id":"N-m2-bone-tissue-404","dok":1,"q":"What is the opening in the shaft of a long bone where the nutrient artery enters called?","a":"The nutrient foramen.","options":["The central canal","The nutrient foramen","The medullary cavity","The perforating canal"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The nutrient foramen is a small opening in the diaphysis where the nutrient artery passes through the compact bone into the medullary cavity. From there, branches supply the marrow and the inner part of the bone. You can often see this opening as a small hole on the shaft of a dry bone in lab.","notes":["The central canal runs down the core of a single osteon and is microscopic. It would be the answer for a question about the canal at the center of the osteon.","Correct. The nutrient artery enters through the nutrient foramen.","The medullary cavity is the hollow core of the shaft that the nutrient artery supplies after it enters. Choose it for a question about where marrow is stored.","Perforating canals are microscopic channels running crosswise between osteons. Pick them for a question about how central canals are linked to each other."],"comp":29,"competencyId":"w2-long-bone-gross"},{"id":"N-m2-bone-tissue-405","dok":1,"q":"In an adult long bone, what fills the medullary cavity of the shaft?","a":"Yellow marrow.","options":["Spongy bone","Hyaline cartilage","Yellow marrow","Red marrow"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"In an adult, the medullary cavity of the diaphysis is filled with yellow marrow, which is mostly stored fat. Red marrow, the site of blood cell formation, is found in the spongy bone of the epiphyses. Keeping the two marrow types in their places helps you map a long bone from end to end.","notes":["Spongy bone fills the epiphyses under a thin compact shell, not the hollow core of the shaft. Pick it for a question about the tissue inside the bone ends.","Hyaline cartilage covers the joint surfaces and forms the growth plate. Choose it for a question about articular cartilage or the epiphyseal plate.","Correct. Yellow marrow fills the adult medullary cavity.","Red marrow fills the spaces of spongy bone in the epiphyses and makes blood cells. It would be the answer for a question about the marrow in the ends of the bone."],"comp":29,"competencyId":"w2-long-bone-gross"},{"id":"N-m2-bone-tissue-406","dok":1,"q":"Which region of a long bone lies between the diaphysis and the epiphysis and holds the growth plate or its remnant?","a":"The metaphysis.","options":["The articular cartilage","The periosteum","The medullary cavity","The metaphysis"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The metaphysis is the region where the shaft widens into the expanded end of the bone. In a growing bone it holds the epiphyseal plate, and in an adult it holds the epiphyseal line. The prefix meta- means between, which matches its position between the diaphysis and the epiphysis.","notes":["Articular cartilage caps the epiphysis at the joint surface, at the far end of the bone. Choose it for a question about where two bones meet.","The periosteum is the membrane covering the outer surface of the bone. Pick it for a question about the outer covering.","The medullary cavity is the hollow core of the diaphysis that holds marrow. It would be the answer for a question about the space inside the shaft.","Correct. The metaphysis lies between the shaft and the end."],"comp":29,"competencyId":"w2-long-bone-gross"},{"id":"N-m2-bone-tissue-407","dok":2,"q":"In which zone of the growth plate do chondrocytes divide and stack into columns?","a":"The zone of proliferation.","options":["The zone of proliferation","The zone of resting cartilage","The zone of ossification","The zone of hypertrophy"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The zone of proliferation is the second zone of the growth plate. Here chondrocytes divide rapidly and line up in columns, like stacks of coins. This cell division is what pushes the epiphysis away from the shaft and drives growth in length.","notes":["Correct. Chondrocytes divide and stack into columns in the zone of proliferation.","The zone of resting cartilage is the first zone, anchoring the plate to the epiphysis, and its cells are not dividing rapidly. It would be the answer for a question about the zone closest to the epiphysis.","The zone of ossification is the last zone, where bone replaces the cartilage next to the diaphysis. Choose it for a question about where new bone is laid down.","The zone of hypertrophy comes next, where the stacked chondrocytes enlarge. Pick it for a question about where cartilage cells swell."],"comp":32,"competencyId":"w2-ossification-growth"},{"id":"N-m2-bone-tissue-408","dok":2,"q":"In which zone of the growth plate do the chondrocytes enlarge?","a":"The zone of hypertrophy.","options":["The zone of calcification","The zone of hypertrophy","The zone of resting cartilage","The zone of proliferation"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The zone of hypertrophy is the third zone of the growth plate. Chondrocytes that were produced in the zone of proliferation grow larger here. Hypertrophy means enlargement, so the name tells you what happens in this zone.","notes":["The zone of calcification follows hypertrophy, and there the cartilage matrix hardens. Pick it for a question about where the matrix calcifies.","Correct. Chondrocytes enlarge in the zone of hypertrophy.","The zone of resting cartilage anchors the plate to the epiphysis. Choose it for a question about the first zone of the plate.","The zone of proliferation is where chondrocytes divide and stack into columns, just before they enlarge. It would be the answer for a question about cell division in the plate."],"comp":32,"competencyId":"w2-ossification-growth"},{"id":"N-m2-bone-tissue-409","dok":1,"q":"Which bone cell is the stem cell of bone tissue, dividing to produce new osteoblasts?","a":"The osteogenic cell.","options":["The osteogenic cell","The osteoclast","The osteoblast","The osteocyte"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The osteogenic cell is the stem cell of bone tissue. It divides to produce new osteoblasts, which build bone and later become osteocytes. This makes it the starting point of the osteogenic cell to osteoblast to osteocyte lineage.","notes":["Correct. The osteogenic cell is the stem cell that produces osteoblasts.","The osteoclast comes from a blood-cell line and breaks down bone, so it is outside this lineage. Pick it for a question about bone resorption.","The osteoblast is the cell the stem cell produces, the one that builds new matrix. Choose it for a question about bone formation.","The osteocyte is a mature bone cell in a lacuna, at the end of the lineage rather than the start. It would be the answer for a question about the cell that maintains matrix."],"comp":31,"competencyId":"w2-bone-cells"},{"id":"N-m2-bone-tissue-410","dok":1,"q":"The costal cartilages that join the ribs to the sternum are made of which type of cartilage?","a":"Hyaline cartilage.","options":["Dense irregular connective tissue","Elastic cartilage","Hyaline cartilage","Fibrocartilage"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The costal cartilages are bars of hyaline cartilage connecting the ribs to the sternum. Hyaline cartilage is firm but slightly flexible, which lets the chest wall move a little during breathing. It is the same type found at the ends of long bones, in the nose, and in the airways.","notes":["Dense irregular connective tissue forms the dermis and the capsules around organs, not cartilage bars. Choose it for a question about tissue with collagen running in many directions.","Elastic cartilage is springy and found in the external ear and epiglottis. It would be the answer for a question about cartilage that bends and springs back.","Correct. The costal cartilages are hyaline cartilage.","Fibrocartilage is tough and shock-absorbing, found in the intervertebral discs, knee menisci, and pubic symphysis. Pick it for a question about cartilage that resists compression."],"comp":26,"competencyId":"w2-cartilage-types"},{"id":"N-a17d-27-1","dok":1,"q":"What are the two ways cartilage grows?","a":"Appositional and interstitial growth.","options":["Intramembranous and endochondral growth.","Proliferation and hypertrophy.","Appositional and interstitial growth.","Compact and spongy growth."],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Cartilage has two modes of growth. Appositional growth adds new cartilage at the outer surface, starting from the perichondrium. Interstitial growth expands the cartilage from within, as chondrocytes divide inside the matrix.","notes":["These are the two routes of ossification, the ways bone forms. They do not describe how cartilage itself grows.","These are two zones of the growth plate, where chondrocytes divide and then enlarge. They are steps in bone lengthening, not the two modes of cartilage growth.","Correct. Cartilage grows by adding at its outer surface (appositional) and by expanding from within (interstitial).","Compact and spongy are the two kinds of bone tissue. They are not growth modes, and cartilage is neither."],"comp":27,"competencyId":"w2-cartilage-growth"},{"id":"N-a17d-27-2","dok":1,"q":"Appositional growth of cartilage begins in which structure?","a":"The perichondrium.","options":["The perichondrium.","The lacunae.","The periosteum.","The epiphyseal plate."],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The perichondrium is the connective tissue sheath wrapped around most cartilage. Appositional growth starts there, laying new cartilage onto the outer surface, so the cartilage gets thicker at its edge.","notes":["Correct. Appositional growth starts from the perichondrium and adds new cartilage at the outer surface.","Lacunae are the small cavities in the matrix that hold the chondrocytes. Growth from cells inside the matrix is interstitial growth, not appositional.","The periosteum covers the outer surface of a bone, and a bone grows wider beneath it. The cartilage version of that covering is the perichondrium.","The epiphyseal plate is the disc of hyaline cartilage where a long bone grows longer. It is not the starting point of appositional cartilage growth."],"comp":27,"competencyId":"w2-cartilage-growth"},{"id":"N-a17d-27-3","dok":1,"q":"Which phrase describes interstitial growth of cartilage?","a":"The cartilage expands from within.","options":["New cartilage is added at the outer surface.","The cartilage expands from within.","Bone replaces a hyaline cartilage model.","Bone forms within a connective tissue membrane."],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Interstitial means within the tissue. Chondrocytes sitting in their lacunae divide inside the matrix, so the cartilage enlarges from the inside. This is the opposite of appositional growth, which adds new cartilage at the outer surface.","notes":["This describes appositional growth, which starts from the perichondrium. Interstitial growth happens inside the matrix.","Correct. In interstitial growth, chondrocytes divide inside the matrix and the cartilage expands from within.","This describes endochondral ossification, the way most bones form. It is about bone forming, not cartilage growing.","This describes intramembranous ossification, which builds the flat bones of the skull. It does not involve cartilage growth."],"comp":27,"competencyId":"w2-cartilage-growth"},{"id":"N-a17d-27-5","dok":2,"q":"One cartilage gets thicker as new cartilage is laid down at its edge from the surrounding sheath. A second cartilage enlarges as chondrocytes divide inside its matrix. Which pair names the two growth modes, in that order?","a":"Appositional, then interstitial.","options":["Appositional, then interstitial.","Interstitial, then appositional.","Intramembranous, then endochondral.","Appositional in both, at different rates."],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Sort each case by where the growth begins. The first starts at the perichondrium and adds to the outer surface, which is appositional growth. The second starts with chondrocytes dividing inside the matrix, which is interstitial growth.","notes":["Correct. Growth from the sheath at the edge is appositional, and growth from dividing chondrocytes inside is interstitial.","This reverses the two. Interstitial growth comes from dividing chondrocytes inside the matrix, and appositional growth adds at the surface.","These are the two routes of bone formation. Neither describes cartilage enlarging from its edge or from within.","Only the first case adds at the surface. Chondrocytes dividing inside the matrix is a different mode, interstitial growth."],"comp":27,"competencyId":"w2-cartilage-growth"},{"id":"N-a17d-27-6","dok":3,"q":"Fibrocartilage has no perichondrium. Using that fact, which mode of growth would a knee meniscus be unable to use?","a":"Appositional growth, which starts from the perichondrium.","options":["Interstitial growth, which starts from the perichondrium.","Interstitial growth, since a meniscus has no chondrocytes.","Neither mode, since fibrocartilage cannot grow at all.","Appositional growth, which starts from the perichondrium."],"correctIndex":3,"why":"First recall that the knee menisci are fibrocartilage, which has no perichondrium. Then recall where each growth mode begins. Appositional growth starts from the perichondrium, so a meniscus cannot use it, but it still has chondrocytes in its matrix, so interstitial growth remains possible.","notes":["Interstitial growth starts with chondrocytes dividing inside the matrix, not from the perichondrium. A meniscus still has chondrocytes.","Fibrocartilage does have chondrocytes in lacunae. What it lacks is a perichondrium, which affects appositional growth.","Losing the perichondrium removes only appositional growth. The chondrocytes inside the matrix can still divide.","Correct. Appositional growth starts from the perichondrium, so cartilage without one cannot grow that way."],"comp":27,"competencyId":"w2-cartilage-growth"},{"id":"N-a17d-27-7","dok":3,"q":"A long bone grows longer by interstitial growth at the epiphyseal plate and wider by appositional growth beneath the periosteum. Why are the same two names used for cartilage growth?","a":"Interstitial works from within, and appositional adds at the covered surface.","options":["Interstitial always makes tissue wider, and appositional makes it longer.","Both names mean growth that starts from the perichondrium.","Interstitial needs a covering sheath, and appositional needs cells inside.","Interstitial works from within, and appositional adds at the covered surface."],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The names describe where growth happens, not which tissue is growing. In cartilage, interstitial growth comes from chondrocytes dividing inside the matrix, and appositional growth adds new cartilage from the perichondrium at the surface. The long bone follows the same pattern, lengthening inside the cartilage plate and widening at its surface beneath the periosteum.","notes":["This reverses the bone example. In a long bone, interstitial growth lengthens it and appositional growth widens it.","Only appositional cartilage growth starts from the perichondrium. Interstitial growth starts with chondrocytes inside the matrix.","This swaps the two. Appositional growth works from the covering sheath, and interstitial growth relies on cells dividing inside.","Correct. In both tissues, interstitial growth works from within and appositional growth adds at the outer surface under a covering."],"comp":27,"competencyId":"w2-cartilage-growth"},{"id":"N-a17d-27-8","dok":3,"q":"In a damaged region of hyaline cartilage, the chondrocytes deep in the matrix have died, but the perichondrium around the cartilage is intact. Which growth can still happen there?","a":"Appositional growth, from the perichondrium.","options":["Interstitial growth, from within the matrix.","Interstitial growth, from the perichondrium.","Appositional growth, from the perichondrium.","Neither, since cartilage grows only from lacunae."],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Link each growth mode to the structure it needs. Interstitial growth needs living chondrocytes dividing inside the matrix, and those are gone. Appositional growth starts from the perichondrium and adds cartilage at the surface, so it can still occur.","notes":["Interstitial growth needs chondrocytes dividing inside the matrix. With those cells dead, this mode is lost.","Interstitial growth does not start at the perichondrium. Growth from the perichondrium is appositional.","Correct. The perichondrium is intact, so new cartilage can still be added at the outer surface.","Lacunae only house chondrocytes. Cartilage can also grow from the perichondrium, which is still intact here."],"comp":27,"competencyId":"w2-cartilage-growth"},{"id":"N-a17d-28-1","dok":2,"q":"Which list gives the correct shape class for a vertebra, a rib, and a carpal, in that order?","a":"Irregular, flat, and short.","options":["Irregular, flat, and short.","Short, long, and irregular.","Irregular, long, and sesamoid.","Flat, flat, and short."],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Match each bone to its shape class. Vertebrae have complex shapes that fit no other group, so they are irregular. Ribs are thin and curved, which makes them flat bones even though they are long and narrow. Carpals are roughly cube-shaped, which makes them short bones.","notes":["Correct. Vertebrae are irregular, ribs are flat, and carpals are short.","Vertebrae are irregular, not short, and carpals are short, not irregular. Ribs look long, but they are thin and curved, so they are flat.","The rib is the trap here. It is thin and curved, so it is flat rather than long, and carpals are short bones, not bones formed in a tendon.","The rib and carpal are right, but a vertebra is irregular. Flat bones are thin and often curved, like the sternum and most skull bones."],"comp":28,"competencyId":"w2-bone-shapes"},{"id":"N-a17d-28-2","dok":3,"q":"In lab, a student holds a roughly cube-shaped bone that sits with seven others to form the wrist. The student labels it a long bone because it is in a limb. What is the correct class, and why was the label wrong?","a":"Short, because shape class depends on shape, not location.","options":["Long, because every bone in a limb is a long bone.","Short, because shape class depends on shape, not location.","Sesamoid, because small wrist bones form within tendons.","Irregular, because the wrist bones fit no other group."],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Bones are classified by their shape, not by the region they sit in. A roughly cube-shaped bone is a short bone, and the carpals of the wrist are the standard example. Being in a limb does not make a bone long; a long bone needs a shaft with two ends.","notes":["Limbs hold long bones like the femur and humerus, but also short bones like the carpals and tarsals. Location does not set the class.","Correct. A cube-shaped carpal is a short bone, since classes are set by shape.","Sesamoid bones form within a tendon, like the patella. The carpals are short bones, not sesamoid bones.","Irregular bones, like vertebrae and hip bones, have complex shapes that fit no group. A cube-shaped carpal fits the short class."],"comp":28,"competencyId":"w2-bone-shapes"},{"id":"N-a17d-28-3","dok":3,"q":"The femur and the patella both sit at the knee, yet they belong to different shape classes. What explains the difference?","a":"The femur has a shaft with two ends; the patella forms in a tendon.","options":["The femur has a shaft with two ends; the patella forms in a tendon.","The femur is thin and curved; the patella is roughly cube-shaped.","The femur fits no other group; the patella is a long bone with a shaft.","The femur forms in a tendon; the patella has a shaft with two ends."],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Classify each bone on its own features. The femur is longer than it is wide, with a shaft and two ends, so it is a long bone. The patella is a small bone formed within the quadriceps tendon, so it is a sesamoid bone. Sitting near each other does not put them in the same class.","notes":["Correct. The femur is a long bone with a shaft and two ends, and the patella is a sesamoid bone formed within a tendon.","Thin and curved describes flat bones, and cube-shaped describes short bones. Neither fits the femur or the patella.","Fitting no other group describes irregular bones. The femur is a long bone and the patella is a sesamoid bone.","This swaps the two. The patella is the bone that forms within a tendon, and the femur is the one with a shaft."],"comp":28,"competencyId":"w2-bone-shapes"},{"id":"N-a17d-28-4","dok":3,"q":"A lab tray holds a sternum, a vertebra, and a hip bone. A student says all three are flat bones because they have broad surfaces. Which correction is right?","a":"Only the sternum is flat; the vertebra and hip bone are irregular.","options":["All three are irregular, because none has a shaft.","The sternum and hip bone are flat; the vertebra is short.","Only the sternum is flat; the vertebra and hip bone are irregular.","Only the hip bone is flat; the sternum and vertebra are irregular."],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Check each bone against the class definitions. The sternum is thin and flat, so it is a flat bone along with the ribs and most skull bones. Vertebrae and hip bones have complex shapes that fit no other group, so both are irregular, even though the hip bone has a broad surface.","notes":["Having no shaft rules out a long bone, not a flat one. The sternum is thin and flat, so it is a flat bone.","The hip bone looks broad, but its complex shape makes it irregular. Vertebrae are irregular too, not short like the carpals.","Correct. The sternum is a flat bone, while vertebrae and hip bones are irregular.","This reverses the sternum and hip bone. The sternum is the flat bone here, and the hip bone is irregular."],"comp":28,"competencyId":"w2-bone-shapes"},{"id":"N-a17d-29-1","dok":3,"q":"An adult long bone is cut lengthwise in lab. One expanded end holds a lattice of bony struts filled with red tissue, and the hollow of the shaft holds yellow, fatty tissue. What are you looking at?","a":"Epiphysis spongy bone with red marrow; medullary cavity with yellow marrow.","options":["Epiphysis compact bone with yellow marrow; medullary cavity with red marrow.","Epiphysis spongy bone with red marrow; medullary cavity with yellow marrow.","Diaphysis spongy bone with red marrow; metaphysis with yellow marrow.","Epiphyseal plate with red marrow; periosteum with yellow marrow."],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Name the region first, then the marrow. The expanded end is the epiphysis, and its lattice of trabeculae is spongy bone, whose spaces hold red marrow. The hollow of the shaft is the medullary cavity of the diaphysis, which in an adult stores fat as yellow marrow.","notes":["This swaps the marrows and the bone type. A lattice of struts is spongy bone, which holds red marrow, while the adult shaft holds yellow marrow.","Correct. The epiphysis holds spongy bone with red marrow, and the adult shaft's medullary cavity holds yellow marrow.","The diaphysis is the shaft, not the expanded end, and the metaphysis is the region holding the growth plate or its remnant. The hollow of the shaft is the medullary cavity.","The epiphyseal plate is hyaline cartilage where a bone lengthens, and the periosteum is the outer membrane. Neither holds marrow."],"comp":29,"competencyId":"w2-long-bone-gross"},{"id":"N-a17d-29-2","dok":3,"q":"A surgeon drills into the middle of an adult femur's shaft from the outside to reach the marrow. Which layers does the drill pass, in order, and what does it reach?","a":"Periosteum, compact bone, endosteum, then yellow marrow.","options":["Periosteum, compact bone, endosteum, then yellow marrow.","Endosteum, compact bone, periosteum, then yellow marrow.","Periosteum, spongy bone, endosteum, then red marrow.","Articular cartilage, compact bone, endosteum, then red marrow."],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Picture the shaft from the outside in. The periosteum covers the outer surface, the wall of the diaphysis is a tube of compact bone, and the endosteum lines the internal surface. Inside is the medullary cavity, which in an adult shaft holds yellow marrow.","notes":["Correct. The drill crosses the periosteum, the compact bone wall, and the endosteum into the yellow marrow of the medullary cavity.","This swaps the two membranes. The periosteum is on the outer surface and the endosteum lines the inside.","The shaft wall is compact bone, not spongy bone. Spongy bone with red marrow fills the epiphyses at the ends.","Articular cartilage caps the epiphysis at a joint, not the shaft. The adult shaft holds yellow marrow, not red."],"comp":29,"competencyId":"w2-long-bone-gross"},{"id":"N-a17d-29-3","dok":3,"q":"A fall peels the periosteum away at two spots on an adult long bone: the middle of the shaft and the side of one expanded end. Which bone tissue lies just under the periosteum at each spot?","a":"Shaft: compact bone; end: a thin compact shell over spongy bone.","options":["Shaft: spongy bone; end: a thin spongy shell over compact bone.","Shaft: compact bone; end: a thin compact shell over spongy bone.","Shaft: compact bone; end: spongy bone with no compact shell.","Shaft: articular cartilage; end: compact bone over yellow marrow."],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Name each region, then its tissue. The middle of the shaft is the diaphysis, a tube of compact bone around the medullary cavity, so compact bone lies right under the periosteum there. The expanded end is the epiphysis, which is spongy bone under a thin compact shell, so the periosteum sits on that shell with spongy bone just beneath it.","notes":["This flips the two tissues. The shaft wall is compact bone, and spongy bone fills the inside of each end under a compact shell.","Correct. The diaphysis is a tube of compact bone, and each epiphysis is spongy bone under a thin compact shell.","The shaft is right, but the epiphysis is not bare spongy bone. A thin shell of compact bone covers the spongy bone inside it.","Articular cartilage caps the epiphysis where it meets another bone, not the shaft. The spongy bone of the ends holds red marrow, while yellow marrow fills the adult shaft."],"comp":29,"competencyId":"w2-long-bone-gross"},{"id":"N-a17d-31-1","dok":1,"q":"Which bone cell is a large cell with many nuclei?","a":"The osteoclast.","options":["The osteocyte.","The osteoclast.","The osteoblast.","The osteogenic cell."],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The osteoclast is a large, multinucleated cell that comes from a blood-cell line. That origin sets it apart from the other three bone cells, which form one lineage, and it is the only one that breaks down bone matrix.","notes":["The osteocyte is a mature osteoblast sitting alone in a lacuna, maintaining the matrix. It is not a large multinucleated cell.","Correct. The osteoclast is a large multinucleated cell that resorbs bone.","The osteoblast builds and secretes new bone matrix and later becomes an osteocyte. It is not the multinucleated cell.","The osteogenic cell is the stem cell of bone that divides to make osteoblasts. It is not the large multinucleated cell."],"comp":31,"competencyId":"w2-bone-cells"},{"id":"N-a17d-31-2","dok":3,"q":"On a bone slide, a single cell sits in a lacuna between lamellae, linked to neighboring cells by tiny channels. A student calls it an osteoblast. What is the cell, and what does its position tell you?","a":"An osteocyte, a former osteoblast now walled in by matrix.","options":["An osteoblast, still building matrix at the bone surface.","An osteoclast, a blood-line cell that resorbs the lamellae.","An osteogenic cell that will soon become an osteoblast.","An osteocyte, a former osteoblast now walled in by matrix."],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Start with the location. A cell sitting in a lacuna between lamellae, linked by canaliculi, is an osteocyte. Then trace its history: an osteoblast secretes matrix, and once it is surrounded by that matrix it becomes an osteocyte, which maintains the bone day to day.","notes":["Osteoblasts build new matrix, but once a cell is enclosed in a lacuna it has become an osteocyte. That is the student's mistake.","Osteoclasts are large multinucleated cells that break down bone. A single cell resting in a lacuna and linked by canaliculi is an osteocyte.","Osteogenic cells are the stem cells that divide to make osteoblasts. A cell enclosed in a lacuna is further along the lineage, an osteocyte.","Correct. A cell in a lacuna between lamellae is an osteocyte, an osteoblast that became surrounded by its own matrix."],"comp":31,"competencyId":"w2-bone-cells"},{"id":"N-a17d-32-1","dok":1,"q":"What are the two routes of ossification?","a":"Intramembranous and endochondral.","options":["Appositional and interstitial.","Proliferation and calcification.","Intramembranous and endochondral.","Compact and spongy."],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Ossification is the formation of bone, and it follows one of two routes. Intramembranous ossification forms bone directly within a connective tissue membrane and builds the flat bones of the skull. Endochondral ossification replaces a hyaline cartilage model and builds most bones of the body.","notes":["These are the two ways cartilage and bone grow, at the surface or from within. They are not the routes by which bone first forms.","These are two zones of the growth plate. They describe steps in lengthening a bone, not the two routes of ossification.","Correct. Bone forms either directly in a membrane (intramembranous) or by replacing a cartilage model (endochondral).","These are the two kinds of bone tissue. They describe what bone looks like, not how it forms."],"comp":32,"competencyId":"w2-ossification-growth"},{"id":"N-a17d-32-2","dok":1,"q":"Which zone of the growth plate lies closest to the diaphysis?","a":"The zone of ossification.","options":["The zone of resting cartilage.","The zone of proliferation.","The zone of ossification.","The zone of hypertrophy."],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The five zones run from the epiphysis to the diaphysis: resting cartilage, proliferation, hypertrophy, calcification, and ossification. The zone of ossification is at the diaphysis end, where cartilage is replaced by bone.","notes":["Resting cartilage is the first zone, at the epiphysis end, where it anchors the plate. It is the farthest zone from the diaphysis.","Proliferation is the second zone, where chondrocytes divide and stack into columns. It sits near the epiphysis end.","Correct. The zone of ossification is the last zone, where cartilage is replaced by bone next to the diaphysis.","Hypertrophy is the middle zone, where chondrocytes enlarge. Two more zones lie between it and the diaphysis."],"comp":32,"competencyId":"w2-ossification-growth"},{"id":"N-a17d-32-3","dok":1,"q":"What does the zone of resting cartilage do in the growth plate?","a":"It anchors the growth plate to the epiphysis.","options":["Its chondrocytes divide and stack into columns.","It anchors the growth plate to the epiphysis.","Its chondrocytes enlarge.","Its cartilage is replaced by bone."],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The zone of resting cartilage sits at the top of the growth plate, next to the epiphysis. Its job is to anchor the plate to the epiphysis, while the zones below it carry out division, enlargement, calcification, and replacement by bone.","notes":["This happens in the zone of proliferation, the second zone. Resting cartilage anchors the plate instead.","Correct. The zone of resting cartilage is the first zone and anchors the plate to the epiphysis.","Enlarging chondrocytes mark the zone of hypertrophy, the third zone. They are not the job of the resting zone.","This happens in the zone of ossification, at the diaphysis end. The resting zone is at the opposite end."],"comp":32,"competencyId":"w2-ossification-growth"},{"id":"N-a17d-32-4","dok":1,"q":"The epiphyseal plate, where a long bone grows longer, is a disc of which tissue?","a":"Hyaline cartilage.","options":["Hyaline cartilage.","Fibrocartilage.","Compact bone.","Elastic cartilage."],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The epiphyseal plate is a disc of hyaline cartilage between the epiphysis and the diaphysis, and it is where a long bone lengthens. When growth ends, the plate closes and leaves the bony epiphyseal line.","notes":["Correct. The epiphyseal plate is a disc of hyaline cartilage.","Fibrocartilage, with thick collagen bundles, forms the intervertebral discs, menisci, and pubic symphysis. The growth plate is hyaline.","Compact bone forms the shaft wall. The plate only becomes bone, as the epiphyseal line, after growth ends.","Elastic cartilage is found in the external ear and the epiglottis. The growth plate is hyaline cartilage."],"comp":32,"competencyId":"w2-ossification-growth"}]},{"id":"m2-skull","title":"The skull","summary":"cranial and facial bones, sutures, foramina, markings, fontanelles, hyoid","mw":"2026-09-16","tr":"2026-09-15","cards":[{"id":"L00493","dok":1,"q":"How many bones make up the skull, not counting the hyoid bone?","a":"22 bones.","options":["8 bones","14 bones","22 bones","26 bones"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The skull has 22 bones in two groups. Eight cranial bones form the braincase that encloses the brain, and fourteen facial bones frame the face and hold the teeth. The hyoid bone in the neck is associated with the skull but is counted separately.","notes":["Eight is the number of cranial bones alone. It would be the answer if the question asked how many bones enclose the brain.","Fourteen is the number of facial bones alone. Pick it for a question about how many bones form the facial skeleton.","Correct. The skull has 22 bones, eight cranial and fourteen facial.","Twenty-six is the number of bones usually counted in the adult vertebral column, the 24 vertebrae plus the sacrum and coccyx. Choose it for a question about the spine, not the skull."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-skull","lid":"c1","comp":33,"competencyId":"w2-skull-bones"},{"id":"L00494","dok":1,"q":"Which group of skull bones encloses and protects the brain?","a":"The cranial bones.","options":["The cranial bones","The auditory ossicles","The cervical vertebrae","The facial bones"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The eight cranial bones form the cranium, the braincase that encloses and protects the brain. They are the frontal, two parietal, two temporal, occipital, sphenoid, and ethmoid bones. The fourteen facial bones form the front of the skull instead.","notes":["Correct. The cranial bones enclose the brain.","The auditory ossicles are the tiny bones of the middle ear, housed inside the temporal bone. Pick them for a question about the bones that transmit sound.","The cervical vertebrae form the neck and surround the spinal cord, not the brain. Choose them for a question about the bones below the skull.","The facial bones frame the face, form the jaws, and hold the teeth. They would be the answer for a question about the bones of the face and upper and lower jaws."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-skull","lid":"c2","comp":33,"competencyId":"w2-skull-bones"},{"id":"L00495","dok":1,"q":"How many cranial bones are there?","a":"Eight.","options":["Eight","Twenty-two","Six","Fourteen"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"There are eight cranial bones. Four are single (frontal, occipital, sphenoid, and ethmoid) and two are paired (parietal and temporal), which gives 4 plus 4 for a total of eight. Together they form the braincase.","notes":["Correct. There are eight cranial bones.","Twenty-two is the total for the whole skull, cranial plus facial. Pick it for a question about how many bones form the skull.","Six is the number of different cranial bone names. Students arrive at six by forgetting that the parietal and temporal bones each come as a pair.","Fourteen is the number of facial bones. It would be the answer for a question about the facial skeleton."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-skull","lid":"c3","comp":33,"competencyId":"w2-skull-bones"},{"id":"L00496","dok":1,"q":"Which cranial bone forms the forehead?","a":"The frontal bone.","options":["The frontal bone","The parietal bone","The occipital bone","The temporal bone"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The frontal bone forms the forehead with its broad squamous part. It also forms the roof of the orbits and the floor of the anterior cranial fossa, and it contains the frontal sinus above the eyes. It is a single bone that meets the two parietal bones at the coronal suture.","notes":["Correct. The frontal bone forms the forehead.","The parietal bones form most of the superior and lateral walls of the cranium, behind the forehead. They would be the answer for a question about the top and sides of the skull.","The occipital bone forms the back of the skull and much of the cranial base. Pick it for a question about the back of the head or the foramen magnum.","The temporal bones form the lower sides of the cranium and house the ear. Choose them for a question about the region around the ear."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-skull","lid":"c4","comp":33,"competencyId":"w2-skull-bones"},{"id":"L00497","dok":1,"q":"Which cranial bone contains the foramen magnum?","a":"The occipital bone.","options":["The parietal bone","The temporal bone","The occipital bone","The sphenoid bone"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The occipital bone forms the back of the skull and much of the cranial base. Its foramen magnum, the large central opening, is where the brainstem continues into the spinal cord. The occipital condyles beside it meet the atlas, the first cervical vertebra.","notes":["The parietal bones form the top and sides of the cranium and have no major foramen for the spinal cord. Choose them for a question about the sagittal suture.","The temporal bones contain the carotid canal and the ear openings. Pick them for a question about the external acoustic meatus or the mastoid process.","Correct. The foramen magnum is in the occipital bone.","The sphenoid bone sits in the center of the cranial base and contains the sella turcica, the optic canal, and the foramen ovale. It would be the answer for a question about any of those."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-skull","lid":"c5","comp":37,"competencyId":"w2-skull-foramina"},{"id":"L00498","dok":1,"q":"Which cranial bone houses the structures of the ear?","a":"The temporal bone.","options":["The parietal bone","The sphenoid bone","The ethmoid bone","The temporal bone"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The temporal bones form the lower sides of the cranium. Each one has the external acoustic meatus, the opening of the ear canal, and a dense petrous part that houses the middle and inner ear. The mastoid process behind the ear is also part of the temporal bone.","notes":["The parietal bones form the top and upper sides of the cranium, above the temporal bones. They would be the answer for a question about the skull roof.","The sphenoid bone is the central wedge of the cranial base. Choose it for a question about the sella turcica or the bone that touches every other cranial bone.","The ethmoid bone lies deep between the orbits and forms part of the nasal cavity. Pick it for a question about the cribriform plate or the nasal septum.","Correct. The temporal bone houses the ear."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-skull","lid":"c6","comp":33,"competencyId":"w2-skull-bones"},{"id":"L00499","dok":1,"q":"Which facial bone is the only freely movable bone of the skull?","a":"The mandible.","options":["The zygomatic bone","The maxilla","The vomer","The mandible"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The mandible, or lower jaw, is the only skull bone that moves freely. Its condylar process meets the mandibular fossa of the temporal bone at the temporomandibular joint, a synovial joint that allows chewing and speaking. All the other skull bones are locked together by sutures.","notes":["The zygomatic bone is the cheekbone, fixed in place by sutures. Choose it for a question about the bone forming the prominence of the cheek.","The maxilla forms the upper jaw and is fixed to the other skull bones by sutures. It would be the answer for a question about the bone that holds the upper teeth.","The vomer is a single facial bone that forms the inferior part of the nasal septum. Pick it for a question about the bony nasal partition.","Correct. The mandible is the only freely movable skull bone."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-skull","lid":"c7","comp":33,"competencyId":"w2-skull-bones"},{"id":"L00500","dok":1,"q":"Which facial bones are the cheekbones?","a":"The zygomatic bones.","options":["The zygomatic bones","The palatine bones","The nasal bones","The lacrimal bones"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The zygomatic bones are the cheekbones, forming the prominence of each cheek and part of the lateral wall and floor of the orbit. Each one meets the zygomatic process of the temporal bone to complete the zygomatic arch.","notes":["Correct. The zygomatic bones are the cheekbones.","The palatine bones form the posterior part of the hard palate. Choose them for a question about the back of the roof of the mouth.","The nasal bones form the bridge of the nose. They would be the answer for a question about the bones between the eyes at the top of the nose.","The lacrimal bones are tiny bones in the medial wall of the orbit, grooved for the tear duct. Pick them for a question about where tears drain."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-skull","lid":"c8","comp":33,"competencyId":"w2-skull-bones"},{"id":"L00501","dok":1,"q":"Which suture joins the two parietal bones along the midline of the skull roof?","a":"The sagittal suture.","options":["The lambdoid suture","The sagittal suture","The coronal suture","The squamous suture"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The sagittal suture runs along the midline of the skull roof, front to back, joining the right and left parietal bones. Its name matches the sagittal plane, which also runs front to back and divides the body into right and left.","notes":["The lambdoid suture joins the parietal bones to the occipital bone at the back of the skull. Pick it for a question about the suture shaped like an upside-down V at the back of the head.","Correct. The sagittal suture joins the two parietal bones in the midline.","The coronal suture runs across the top of the skull, side to side, joining the frontal bone to the parietal bones. It would be the answer for the suture behind the forehead.","The squamous suture joins a parietal bone to a temporal bone on each side. Choose it for a question about the suture on the lower side of the skull."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-skull","lid":"c9","comp":35,"competencyId":"w2-sutures-fontanelles"},{"id":"L00502","dok":1,"q":"What is the largest fontanelle of the infant skull?","a":"The anterior fontanelle.","options":["The anterior fontanelle","The anterolateral fontanelle","The posterolateral fontanelle","The posterior fontanelle"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The anterior fontanelle is the largest of the soft spots on an infant's skull. It sits where the frontal bone meets the two parietal bones and is the last to close, at about 18 to 24 months. Its size and location make it the one clinicians check on a newborn.","notes":["Correct. The anterior fontanelle is the largest.","The anterolateral fontanelles, also called the sphenoidal fontanelles, are small paired spots on the sides of the skull. Choose them for a question about the fontanelles near the sphenoid bone.","The posterolateral fontanelles, also called the mastoid fontanelles, are small paired spots behind the ears. Pick them for a question about the fontanelles near the mastoid region.","The posterior fontanelle, also called the occipital fontanelle, is smaller and sits where the parietal bones meet the occipital bone. It closes early, at about 2 months, so pick it for a question about the first fontanelle to close."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-skull","lid":"c10","comp":35,"competencyId":"w2-sutures-fontanelles"},{"id":"L00503","dok":1,"q":"Which paranasal sinuses are the largest?","a":"The maxillary sinuses.","options":["The sphenoidal sinuses","The frontal sinuses","The maxillary sinuses","The ethmoidal sinuses"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The maxillary sinuses are the largest paranasal sinuses. They fill much of the body of each maxilla in the cheek region, just above the roots of the upper teeth. Like all the paranasal sinuses, they lighten the skull and drain into the nasal cavity.","notes":["The sphenoidal sinus lies within the body of the sphenoid bone, deep in the skull. Choose it for a question about the sinus just below the sella turcica.","The frontal sinuses sit in the frontal bone above the orbits. They would be the answer for a question about the sinuses behind the forehead.","Correct. The maxillary sinuses are the largest.","The ethmoidal sinuses are many small air cells in the ethmoid bone between the orbits. Pick them for a question about the sinuses made of small air cells."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-skull","lid":"c11","comp":36,"competencyId":"w2-skull-cavities"},{"id":"L00504","dok":1,"q":"Which bone is U-shaped, sits in the anterior neck below the mandible, and does not articulate with any other bone?","a":"The hyoid bone.","options":["The mandible","The hyoid bone","The atlas","The vomer"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The hyoid bone is a U-shaped bone in the front of the neck, just below the mandible. It is the only bone that does not form a joint with any other bone; instead, ligaments suspend it from the styloid processes of the temporal bones. It anchors the tongue and several muscles of the neck and larynx.","notes":["The mandible is U-shaped too, which is why students confuse them, but it articulates with the temporal bones at the jaw joints. Pick it for a question about the lower jaw.","Correct. The hyoid bone touches no other bone.","The atlas is the first cervical vertebra, a ring-shaped bone that articulates with the occipital condyles above and the axis below. Choose it for a question about the vertebra that holds up the skull.","The vomer is a thin bone forming the lower part of the nasal septum, and it articulates with several bones. It would be the answer for a question about the bony nasal partition."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-skull","lid":"c12","comp":33,"competencyId":"w2-skull-bones"},{"id":"L00505","dok":1,"q":"Which marking of the sphenoid bone is the saddle-shaped seat that cradles the pituitary gland?","a":"The sella turcica.","options":["The glabella","The cribriform plate","The crista galli","The sella turcica"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The sella turcica, which means Turkish saddle, is a saddle-shaped depression on the body of the sphenoid bone. Its deep seat holds the pituitary gland in the middle of the cranial base. The sphenoidal sinus lies just below it within the body of the sphenoid.","notes":["The glabella is the smooth area of the frontal bone between the eyebrow ridges. Pick it for a question about the spot between the eyebrows.","The cribriform plate is the perforated plate of the ethmoid bone that olfactory nerve filaments pass through. Choose it for a question about the sense of smell.","The crista galli is an upright ridge on the ethmoid bone where the falx cerebri attaches. It would be the answer for a question about the attachment of that fold of the brain covering.","Correct. The sella turcica cradles the pituitary gland."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-skull","lid":"c13","comp":34,"competencyId":"w2-skull-markings"},{"id":"L00506","dok":1,"q":"Which marking of the temporal bone is the bump you can feel behind the ear?","a":"The mastoid process.","options":["The zygomatic process","The coronoid process","The mastoid process","The styloid process"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The mastoid process is the rounded bump of the temporal bone just behind the ear. It is an attachment point for neck muscles, and inside it is filled with small air cells. You can feel it easily by pressing behind your earlobe.","notes":["The zygomatic process of the temporal bone runs forward to meet the cheekbone and form the zygomatic arch. Pick it for a question about the bar of bone in front of the ear.","The coronoid process belongs to the mandible, not the temporal bone, and is the anterior process where the temporalis muscle attaches. Choose it for a question about the jaw.","Correct. The mastoid process is the bump behind the ear.","The styloid process is a slender spike pointing down below the ear, too deep to feel easily. It would be the answer for a question about the anchor for tongue and neck muscles and the ligaments that hold the hyoid."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-skull","lid":"c14","comp":34,"competencyId":"w2-skull-markings"},{"id":"L00507","dok":2,"q":"Which one cranial bone articulates with every other cranial bone?","a":"The sphenoid bone.","options":["The occipital bone","The sphenoid bone","The ethmoid bone","The frontal bone"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The sphenoid bone is the central wedge of the cranial base, shaped somewhat like a bat with its wings spread. Because it sits in the middle of the skull floor, it touches every other cranial bone. It contains the sella turcica, the optic canals, and the sphenoidal sinus.","notes":["The occipital bone forms the back of the skull and much of the cranial base, but it does not touch the frontal or ethmoid bones. Pick it for a question about the foramen magnum.","Correct. The sphenoid touches every other cranial bone.","The ethmoid bone lies deep between the orbits and forms part of the nasal cavity. It would be the answer for a question about the deepest cranial bone or the cribriform plate.","The frontal bone forms the forehead and orbital roofs, but it does not reach the occipital bone. Choose it for a question about the forehead."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-skull","lid":"c15","comp":33,"competencyId":"w2-skull-bones"},{"id":"L00508","dok":2,"q":"Which cranial bone is the deepest, lying between the orbits and forming part of the nasal cavity?","a":"The ethmoid bone.","options":["The sphenoid bone","The temporal bone","The frontal bone","The ethmoid bone"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The ethmoid bone is the deepest cranial bone, tucked between the orbits behind the nose. It forms the roof and part of the walls of the nasal cavity, the upper part of the nasal septum through its perpendicular plate, and it holds the ethmoidal air cells. Its cribriform plate lets the olfactory nerve filaments through.","notes":["The sphenoid bone lies just behind the ethmoid as the central wedge of the cranial base. It would be the answer for a question about the bone that touches every other cranial bone.","The temporal bones form the lower sides of the cranium and house the ear. Pick them for a question about the bones around the ear canal.","The frontal bone forms the forehead and roof of the orbits, in front of and above the ethmoid. Choose it for a question about the forehead or the frontal sinus.","Correct. The ethmoid is the deepest cranial bone, between the orbits."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-skull","lid":"c16","comp":33,"competencyId":"w2-skull-bones"},{"id":"L00509","dok":2,"q":"Which cranial bones come in pairs rather than as single bones?","a":"The parietal and temporal bones.","options":["The parietal and temporal bones","The occipital and sphenoid bones","The sphenoid and ethmoid bones","The frontal and occipital bones"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Of the eight cranial bones, only the parietal and temporal bones are paired, one on each side of the head. The frontal, occipital, sphenoid, and ethmoid bones are single bones that cross the midline. Two pairs plus four single bones gives the total of eight.","notes":["Correct. The parietal and temporal bones are the paired cranial bones.","The occipital and sphenoid bones are both single bones of the cranial base. Choose this pair for a question about bones that form much of the skull floor.","The sphenoid and ethmoid bones are single bones that sit in the midline of the cranial base. Pick this pair for a question about the two deep, single cranial bones near the orbits.","The frontal and occipital bones are single midline bones at the front and back of the skull. This pair would be the answer if the question asked for two single cranial bones at opposite ends of the skull."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-skull","lid":"c17","comp":33,"competencyId":"w2-skull-bones"},{"id":"L00510","dok":2,"q":"Which marking of the occipital bone articulates with the atlas, the first cervical vertebra?","a":"The occipital condyles.","options":["The external occipital protuberance","The occipital condyles","The mastoid process","The condylar process"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The occipital condyles are the paired knobs beside the foramen magnum. They rest on the superior articular facets of the atlas to form the atlanto-occipital joint. This is the joint that lets you nod your head yes.","notes":["The external occipital protuberance is the midline bump on the back of the head and does not form a joint. Pick it for a question about the bump you can feel at the back of the skull.","Correct. The occipital condyles articulate with the atlas.","The mastoid process is a bump on the temporal bone behind the ear, used for muscle attachment, and it does not form a joint. Pick it for a question about the bump you can feel behind the ear.","The condylar process belongs to the mandible, and its head meets the temporal bone at the jaw joint. Students confuse it with the occipital condyles because of the similar name. Choose it for a question about the temporomandibular joint."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-skull","lid":"c18","comp":34,"competencyId":"w2-skull-markings"},{"id":"L00511","dok":2,"q":"Which marking of the temporal bone forms the socket of the temporomandibular joint?","a":"The mandibular fossa.","options":["The external acoustic meatus","The mastoid process","The styloid process","The mandibular fossa"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The mandibular fossa is a shallow socket on the underside of the temporal bone, just in front of the ear canal. The condylar process of the mandible fits into it, forming the temporomandibular joint (TMJ), the jaw joint.","notes":["The external acoustic meatus is the opening of the ear canal, just behind the mandibular fossa. Pick it for a question about where sound enters the skull.","The mastoid process is the bump behind the ear where neck muscles attach. It would be the answer for a question about the bump you can feel behind the earlobe.","The styloid process is a slender spike below the ear that anchors tongue and neck muscles. Choose it for a question about the bony point the hyoid is slung from.","Correct. The mandibular fossa is the socket of the jaw joint."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-skull","lid":"c19","comp":34,"competencyId":"w2-skull-markings"},{"id":"L00512","dok":2,"q":"Which marking of the maxilla forms the anterior part of the hard palate?","a":"The palatine process.","options":["The frontal process","The alveolar process","The palatine process","The zygomatic process"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The palatine process is a horizontal shelf on each maxilla. The right and left palatine processes meet in the midline to form the front part of the hard palate, the bony roof of the mouth. The palatine bones complete the hard palate behind them.","notes":["The frontal process projects upward beside the nose to meet the frontal bone. Pick it for a question about the part of the maxilla that reaches toward the forehead.","The alveolar process is the ridge of the maxilla that holds the upper teeth. It would be the answer for a question about the tooth sockets.","Correct. The palatine processes of the maxillae form the anterior hard palate.","The zygomatic process of the maxilla projects laterally to meet the cheekbone. Choose it for a question about where the maxilla joins the zygomatic bone."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-skull","lid":"c20","comp":36,"competencyId":"w2-skull-cavities"},{"id":"L00513","dok":2,"q":"Which two facial bones are single rather than paired?","a":"The vomer and the mandible.","options":["The vomer and the mandible","The zygomatic bones and the mandible","The maxillae and the palatine bones","The nasal bones and the vomer"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Of the fourteen facial bones, twelve come in six pairs and only two are single: the vomer and the mandible. Both cross the midline. The vomer forms the lower part of the nasal septum, and the mandible is the lower jaw.","notes":["Correct. The vomer and the mandible are the single facial bones.","The mandible is single, but the zygomatic bones are paired cheekbones. Choose the zygomatic bones for a question about the cheeks.","The maxillae and palatine bones are both paired, together forming the hard palate. This choice would fit a question about the bones of the roof of the mouth.","The vomer is single, but the nasal bones are paired, meeting at the bridge of the nose. Pick the nasal bones for a question about the bridge of the nose."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-skull","lid":"c21","comp":33,"competencyId":"w2-skull-bones"},{"id":"L00514","dok":2,"q":"Which suture joins the frontal bone to the two parietal bones?","a":"The coronal suture.","options":["The squamous suture","The sagittal suture","The coronal suture","The lambdoid suture"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The coronal suture runs across the top of the skull from side to side, just behind the forehead. It joins the frontal bone in front to the two parietal bones behind. It runs in the same direction as the coronal (frontal) plane, which is how it got its name.","notes":["The squamous suture joins a parietal bone to a temporal bone on each side. Choose it for a question about the suture on the lower side of the cranium.","The sagittal suture joins the two parietal bones to each other along the midline. It would be the answer for the suture running front to back on top of the skull.","Correct. The coronal suture joins the frontal bone to the parietal bones.","The lambdoid suture joins the parietal bones to the occipital bone at the back of the skull. Pick it for a question about the back of the head."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-skull","lid":"c22","comp":35,"competencyId":"w2-sutures-fontanelles"},{"id":"L00515","dok":2,"q":"The pterion is the junction of which four bones?","a":"The frontal, parietal, temporal, and sphenoid bones.","options":["Frontal, parietal, temporal, and sphenoid","Frontal, parietal, occipital, and sphenoid","Parietal, temporal, occipital, and ethmoid","Frontal, temporal, occipital, and sphenoid"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The pterion is an H-shaped junction on the side of the skull, in the temple region, where the frontal, parietal, temporal, and sphenoid (greater wing) bones meet. It is the thinnest part of the skull. The middle meningeal artery runs just beneath it, so a blow here can tear the artery.","notes":["Correct. The frontal, parietal, temporal, and sphenoid bones meet at the pterion.","The occipital bone is at the back of the skull and is too far back to reach the temple. Swap it for the temporal bone and the set is correct.","The occipital bone is at the back and the ethmoid bone is deep inside the skull, so neither reaches the temple. This set leaves out the frontal and sphenoid bones that actually form the pterion.","This set drops the parietal bone and adds the occipital bone, which sits at the back of the skull. The parietal bone is needed to form the upper part of the H."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-skull","lid":"c23","comp":35,"competencyId":"w2-sutures-fontanelles"},{"id":"L00516","dok":2,"q":"Which marking of the ethmoid bone is the perforated plate that the olfactory nerve filaments pass through?","a":"The cribriform plate.","options":["The cribriform plate","The crista galli","The perpendicular plate","The lateral masses"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The cribriform plate is a horizontal plate of the ethmoid bone, full of small holes, that forms the roof of the nasal cavity. Filaments of the olfactory nerve, cranial nerve I, pass up through these holes from the nasal lining to the brain. Cribriform means sieve-like.","notes":["Correct. The cribriform plate is perforated for the olfactory nerve filaments.","The crista galli is the upright ridge on top of the cribriform plate where the falx cerebri attaches. It would be the answer for a question about that attachment point.","The perpendicular plate is the vertical plate forming the upper part of the nasal septum. Pick it for a question about the bony nasal partition.","The lateral masses are the side portions of the ethmoid that contain the ethmoidal air cells. Choose them for a question about the ethmoidal sinuses."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-skull","lid":"c24","comp":34,"competencyId":"w2-skull-markings"},{"id":"L00517","dok":2,"q":"Which structure passes through the foramen magnum?","a":"The spinal cord, where it continues from the medulla oblongata.","options":["The internal jugular vein","The spinal cord","The optic nerve","The internal carotid artery"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The foramen magnum is the large opening in the occipital bone where the medulla oblongata of the brainstem continues into the spinal cord. The vertebral arteries and the spinal accessory nerve also pass through it. It is the one large opening between the brain and the spinal cord.","notes":["The internal jugular vein leaves the skull through the jugular foramen, between the temporal and occipital bones. Choose it for a question about the jugular foramen.","Correct. The spinal cord passes through the foramen magnum.","The optic nerve passes through the optic canal of the sphenoid bone. It would be the answer for a question about the opening from the orbit to the brain.","The internal carotid artery enters the skull through the carotid canal of the temporal bone. Pick it for a question about the carotid canal."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-skull","lid":"c25","comp":37,"competencyId":"w2-skull-foramina"},{"id":"L00518","dok":2,"q":"Which opening transmits the optic nerve?","a":"The optic canal.","options":["The foramen ovale","The optic canal","The jugular foramen","The superior orbital fissure"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The optic canal is an opening in the sphenoid bone at the back of the orbit. The optic nerve, cranial nerve II, passes through it from the eye to the brain. It sits just medial to the superior orbital fissure.","notes":["The foramen ovale, in the sphenoid bone, passes a branch of the trigeminal nerve to the lower jaw. Pick it for a question about that nerve branch.","Correct. The optic nerve passes through the optic canal.","The jugular foramen, between the temporal and occipital bones, passes the internal jugular vein. Choose it for a question about where the main vein leaves the skull.","The superior orbital fissure, also in the sphenoid bone, passes the nerves that move the eye. It would be the answer for a question about the opening for the eye-moving nerves."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-skull","lid":"c26","comp":37,"competencyId":"w2-skull-foramina"},{"id":"L00519","dok":2,"q":"Which opening transmits the internal carotid artery into the skull?","a":"The carotid canal.","options":["The carotid canal","The jugular foramen","The stylomastoid foramen","The foramen magnum"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The carotid canal is a passage through the temporal bone that carries the internal carotid artery into the cranial cavity. The internal carotid is one of the main arteries supplying the brain. Its name matches the vessel it carries.","notes":["Correct. The internal carotid artery enters through the carotid canal.","The jugular foramen carries the internal jugular vein out of the skull. It would be the answer for a question about the main vein draining the brain.","The stylomastoid foramen is the exit of the facial nerve between the styloid and mastoid processes. Choose it for a question about where that nerve leaves the skull.","The foramen magnum carries the spinal cord and the vertebral arteries, not the internal carotid. Pick it for a question about the vertebral arteries entering the skull."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-skull","lid":"c27","comp":37,"competencyId":"w2-skull-foramina"},{"id":"L00520","dok":2,"q":"Which bone forms part of the medial wall of the orbit and is grooved for the tear duct?","a":"The lacrimal bone.","options":["The nasal bone","The zygomatic bone","The palatine bone","The lacrimal bone"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The lacrimal bones are the smallest facial bones, one in the medial wall of each orbit. Each has a groove that holds the passage carrying tears from the eye toward the nasal cavity. Lacrimal means related to tears.","notes":["The nasal bones form the bridge of the nose, in front of the orbit rather than inside its wall. They would be the answer for a question about the bridge of the nose.","The zygomatic bone forms the lateral wall and part of the floor of the orbit, on the opposite side from the tear duct. Pick it for a question about the cheekbone.","The palatine bone contributes only a small piece to the back of the orbit floor and mostly forms the posterior hard palate. Choose it for a question about the back of the roof of the mouth.","Correct. The lacrimal bone is grooved for the tear duct."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-skull","lid":"c28","comp":36,"competencyId":"w2-skull-cavities"},{"id":"L00521","dok":1,"q":"What is the domed roof of the cranium, the skullcap, called?","a":"The calvaria.","options":["The cranial base","The calvaria","The glabella","The cranial fossa"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The calvaria is the domed skullcap, the roof of the cranium. It is formed mainly by the frontal, parietal, and occipital bones. The floor beneath the brain is a different region called the cranial base.","notes":["The cranial base is the floor of the cranium, beneath the brain. It would be the answer for a question about the part of the skull divided into anterior, middle, and posterior fossae.","Correct. The calvaria is the skullcap.","The glabella is the small smooth area of the frontal bone between the eyebrows. Pick it for a question about that single landmark.","A cranial fossa is one of the three depressions in the cranial base that hold parts of the brain. Choose it for a question about the floor of the cranium."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-skull","lid":"c29","comp":33,"competencyId":"w2-skull-bones"},{"id":"L00522","dok":1,"q":"Which part of the mandible is the vertical portion that rises on each side?","a":"The ramus.","options":["The body","The angle","The alveolar process","The ramus"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The ramus is the vertical part of the mandible on each side, rising from the angle of the jaw up toward the skull. At its top it splits into the condylar process behind and the coronoid process in front. The mandibular foramen opens on its inner surface.","notes":["The body is the horizontal, U-shaped part of the mandible that holds the lower teeth. It would be the answer for a question about the part of the jaw at the chin.","The angle is the corner where the body turns upward into the ramus. Pick it for a question about the corner of the jaw below the ear.","The alveolar process is the ridge that holds the lower teeth. Choose it for a question about the tooth sockets.","Correct. The ramus is the vertical portion."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-skull","lid":"c30","comp":34,"competencyId":"w2-skull-markings"},{"id":"L00523","dok":2,"q":"Which process of the mandible is the anterior process where the temporalis chewing muscle attaches?","a":"The coronoid process.","options":["The condylar process","The alveolar process","The coronoid process","The styloid process"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The coronoid process is the thin, pointed anterior process at the top of the mandibular ramus. The temporalis, a chewing muscle that fans out over the side of the skull, attaches to it. The mandibular notch separates it from the condylar process behind.","notes":["The condylar process is the posterior process whose head meets the temporal bone at the jaw joint. It would be the answer for a question about the part of the mandible that forms the TMJ.","The alveolar process is the ridge that holds the lower teeth. Pick it for a question about the tooth sockets.","Correct. The temporalis attaches to the coronoid process.","The styloid process belongs to the temporal bone, not the mandible. Choose it for a question about the slender spike below the ear."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-skull","lid":"c31","comp":34,"competencyId":"w2-skull-markings"},{"id":"L00524","dok":2,"q":"Which opening, located between the styloid and mastoid processes, is the exit of the facial nerve from the skull?","a":"The stylomastoid foramen.","options":["The carotid canal","The internal acoustic meatus","The jugular foramen","The stylomastoid foramen"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The stylomastoid foramen lies on the underside of the temporal bone, between the styloid process and the mastoid process. Its name simply joins the two landmarks it sits between. The facial nerve leaves the skull here on its way to the face.","notes":["The carotid canal carries the internal carotid artery into the skull. Pick it for a question about that artery.","The internal acoustic meatus is where the facial nerve enters the temporal bone from the cranial cavity. It would be the answer if the question asked for the entry point rather than the exit.","The jugular foramen carries the internal jugular vein out of the skull. Choose it for a question about the vein draining the brain.","Correct. The facial nerve exits at the stylomastoid foramen."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-skull","lid":"c32","comp":37,"competencyId":"w2-skull-foramina"},{"id":"L00525","dok":2,"q":"A student finds an immovable fibrous joint at the back of the skull joining the parietal bones to the occipital bone. Which suture is this?","a":"The lambdoid suture.","options":["The sagittal suture","The squamous suture","The coronal suture","The lambdoid suture"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The lambdoid suture is at the back of the skull, where the two parietal bones meet the occipital bone. Its shape resembles the Greek letter lambda, an upside-down V, which is how it got its name. Like all sutures, it is an immovable fibrous joint.","notes":["The sagittal suture joins the two parietal bones along the midline on top of the skull. Pick it for the suture running front to back.","The squamous suture joins a parietal bone to a temporal bone on the side of the skull. Choose it for the suture above the ear.","The coronal suture is at the front, joining the frontal bone to the parietal bones. It would be the answer for the suture behind the forehead.","Correct. The lambdoid suture joins the parietal and occipital bones."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-skull","lid":"c33","comp":35,"competencyId":"w2-sutures-fontanelles"},{"id":"L00526","dok":2,"q":"An anatomist examines the deep saddle-shaped seat on the body of the sphenoid bone. Which gland normally rests in this seat?","a":"The pituitary gland.","options":["The lacrimal gland","The thyroid gland","The pineal gland","The pituitary gland"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The seat is the sella turcica, the Turkish saddle of the sphenoid bone. It cradles the pituitary gland, which hangs from the base of the brain by a stalk. Its position in the middle of the cranial base protects this small gland.","notes":["The lacrimal gland sits in the upper lateral part of the orbit and produces tears. Choose it for a question about where tears are made.","The thyroid gland is in the anterior neck, wrapped around the trachea, far from the skull. It would be the answer for a question about the gland below the larynx.","The pineal gland is in the brain itself, at the back of the diencephalon, and does not sit in a bony seat. Students sometimes confuse it with the pituitary because both are small glands near the brain. Pick it for a question about the epithalamus.","Correct. The pituitary gland sits in the sella turcica."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-skull","lid":"c34","comp":34,"competencyId":"w2-skull-markings"},{"id":"L00527","dok":2,"q":"On an infant's skull, a clinician feels a fibrous membrane gap where the frontal bone meets the two parietal bones. Which fontanelle is this?","a":"The anterior fontanelle.","options":["The posterolateral fontanelle","The posterior fontanelle","The anterior fontanelle","The anterolateral fontanelle"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The anterior fontanelle is at the junction of the frontal bone and the two parietal bones, on the top of the head toward the front. It is the largest fontanelle and closes at about 18 to 24 months. In the adult, the same spot is where the coronal and sagittal sutures meet.","notes":["The posterolateral (mastoid) fontanelles are behind the ears on each side. Pick them for a question about a soft spot near the mastoid region.","The posterior fontanelle is where the parietal bones meet the occipital bone, at the back of the head. It would be the answer if the gap were at the back of the skull.","Correct. The frontal and parietal bones meet at the anterior fontanelle.","The anterolateral (sphenoidal) fontanelles are on the sides of the skull near the sphenoid bone. Choose them for a question about a soft spot at the side of the head."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-skull","lid":"c35","comp":35,"competencyId":"w2-sutures-fontanelles"},{"id":"L00528","dok":2,"q":"A dentist needs to numb the nerve that supplies the lower teeth. Which mandibular opening is the target for the injection?","a":"The mandibular foramen.","options":["The mental foramen","The supraorbital foramen","The infraorbital foramen","The mandibular foramen"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The mandibular foramen opens on the inner surface of the mandibular ramus. The nerve to the lower teeth enters the mandible here, so dentists inject anesthetic near this opening to numb the lower teeth on that side. This is the standard dental block.","notes":["The mental foramen is on the outer surface of the mandibular body, below the premolars, and passes the nerve to the chin and lower lip. It would be the answer for a question about numbness of the chin.","The supraorbital foramen is in the frontal bone above the orbit. Choose it for a question about the nerve and vessels to the forehead.","The infraorbital foramen is on the maxilla, below the orbit, and passes the infraorbital nerve to the face. Pick it for a question about the upper face and cheek.","Correct. The mandibular foramen is the target for the lower dental block."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-skull","lid":"c36","comp":37,"competencyId":"w2-skull-foramina"},{"id":"L00529","dok":2,"q":"A view of the skull base shows an opening between the temporal and occipital bones that carries a large vein. Which opening is this?","a":"The jugular foramen.","options":["The foramen ovale","The hypoglossal canal","The carotid canal","The jugular foramen"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The jugular foramen lies between the temporal and occipital bones in the skull base. The internal jugular vein, which drains blood from the brain, leaves the skull through it. It sits just behind the carotid canal, so the main artery in and the main vein out are close neighbors.","notes":["The foramen ovale is in the sphenoid bone and passes a branch of the trigeminal nerve to the lower jaw. Pick it for a question about that nerve branch.","The hypoglossal canal is in the occipital bone and passes the hypoglossal nerve, cranial nerve XII. Choose it for a question about that nerve.","The carotid canal is in the temporal bone and carries the internal carotid artery into the skull. It would be the answer if the opening carried the major artery rather than the vein.","Correct. The jugular foramen carries the internal jugular vein."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-skull","lid":"c37","comp":37,"competencyId":"w2-skull-foramina"},{"id":"L00530","dok":2,"q":"A clinician studies the thin H-shaped junction on the side of the skull where four bones meet. Which region is this?","a":"The pterion.","options":["The external occipital protuberance","The glabella","The mastoid process","The pterion"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The pterion is the H-shaped junction in the temple region where the frontal, parietal, temporal, and sphenoid bones meet. It is the thinnest part of the skull. Because the middle meningeal artery runs just beneath it, a blow here can cause dangerous bleeding inside the skull.","notes":["The external occipital protuberance is the midline bump at the back of the head, on the occipital bone alone. Choose it for a question about the back of the skull.","The glabella is the smooth area of the frontal bone between the eyebrows, a single-bone landmark. It would be the answer for a question about the spot between the eyebrows.","The mastoid process is a thick bump of the temporal bone behind the ear, not a junction of bones. Pick it for a question about the bump behind the ear.","Correct. The pterion is the four-bone junction on the side of the skull."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-skull","lid":"c38","comp":35,"competencyId":"w2-sutures-fontanelles"},{"id":"L00531","dok":2,"q":"A probe runs down the bony nasal septum from its upper part to its lower part. Which bones does it cross, in order?","a":"The perpendicular plate of the ethmoid bone, then the vomer.","options":["The nasal bone, then the vomer","The vomer, then the perpendicular plate","The perpendicular plate, then the vomer","The cribriform plate, then the vomer"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The nasal septum is the partition between the right and left nasal cavities. Its upper bony part is the perpendicular plate of the ethmoid bone, and its lower bony part is the vomer. Septal cartilage completes the front of the septum.","notes":["The nasal bones form the bridge of the nose on the outside, not the septum. Choose them for a question about the bony bridge.","This is the correct pair in reverse. The vomer is the lower part of the septum, so it would come first only if the probe ran from bottom to top.","Correct. The perpendicular plate is above, and the vomer is below.","The cribriform plate is horizontal and forms the roof of the nasal cavity, not part of the septum. Pick it for a question about where the olfactory nerve filaments pass."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-skull","lid":"c39","comp":36,"competencyId":"w2-skull-cavities"},{"id":"L00532","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly names the cranial bone that forms the roof of the orbit, the cranial bone whose greater wings help form the orbit and lateral skull wall, and the cheekbone that helps form the orbit?","a":"Frontal roofs it, sphenoid has the wings, zygomatic is the cheekbone","options":["Occipital roofs it, sphenoid has the wings, zygomatic is the cheekbone","Parietal roofs it, ethmoid has the wings, maxilla is the cheekbone","Frontal roofs it, temporal has the wings, nasal is the cheekbone","Frontal roofs it, sphenoid has the wings, zygomatic is the cheekbone"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The orbit is built from seven bones. The frontal bone forms its roof through its orbital plates. The sphenoid bone contributes through its greater wings, which also form part of the lateral skull wall. The zygomatic bone, the cheekbone, forms the lateral wall and part of the floor.","notes":["The occipital bone is at the back of the skull and does not reach the orbit. The rest of this set is right, so swap in the frontal bone for the roof.","The parietal bones form the top and sides of the cranium, far from the orbit. The greater wings belong to the sphenoid, not the ethmoid, and the maxilla is the upper jaw, not the cheekbone.","The frontal bone is right, but the temporal bone has no greater wings, and the nasal bones form the bridge of the nose. Greater wings belong only to the sphenoid.","Correct. Frontal, sphenoid, and zygomatic."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-skull","lid":"c40","comp":36,"competencyId":"w2-skull-cavities"},{"id":"L00533","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly describes the bones of the hard palate from front to back?","a":"The palatine processes of the maxillae in front, then the palatine bones behind.","options":["The palatine bones in front, then the maxillae behind","The maxillae in front, then the palatine bones behind","The maxillae in front, then the vomer behind","The vomer in front, then the maxillae behind"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The hard palate is the bony roof of the mouth and the floor of the nasal cavity. The palatine processes of the two maxillae form its larger front part, and the horizontal plates of the two palatine bones form the back part. Despite their name, the palatine bones form only the posterior palate.","notes":["This reverses the two bones. Students choose it because the palatine bones are named for the palate, but they form only the back portion.","Correct. Maxillae in front, palatine bones behind.","The maxillae are right for the front, but the vomer stands upright in the septum and does not form the back of the palate. The palatine bones complete the palate.","The vomer is a vertical bone in the nasal septum, sitting above the palate rather than forming it. Pick it for a question about the lower nasal septum."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-skull","lid":"c41","comp":36,"competencyId":"w2-skull-cavities"},{"id":"L00534","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly matches each opening to what it transmits: the optic canal, the foramen magnum, and the carotid canal?","a":"The optic canal transmits the optic nerve, the foramen magnum the spinal cord, and the carotid canal the internal carotid artery.","options":["Optic canal: spinal cord; foramen magnum: carotid artery; carotid canal: optic nerve","Optic canal: optic nerve; foramen magnum: spinal cord; carotid canal: internal carotid","Optic canal: carotid artery; foramen magnum: optic nerve; carotid canal: spinal cord","Optic canal: optic nerve; foramen magnum: carotid artery; carotid canal: spinal cord"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Most of these openings are named for what passes through them. The optic canal in the sphenoid bone carries the optic nerve, the foramen magnum in the occipital bone carries the spinal cord, and the carotid canal in the temporal bone carries the internal carotid artery.","notes":["This scrambles all three. The spinal cord is far too large for the optic canal and passes only through the foramen magnum.","Correct. Optic nerve, spinal cord, and internal carotid artery.","This shifts every match by one. The names give the answer away: the optic canal carries the optic nerve, and the carotid canal carries the carotid artery.","The optic match is right, but the other two are swapped. The spinal cord passes through the foramen magnum, and the internal carotid passes through the carotid canal."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-skull","lid":"c42","comp":37,"competencyId":"w2-skull-foramina"},{"id":"L00535","dok":2,"q":"Trace the facial nerve through the temporal bone from the cranial cavity to the outside of the skull base. Where does it enter and where does it exit?","a":"It enters at the internal acoustic meatus and exits at the stylomastoid foramen.","options":["Enters at the stylomastoid foramen, exits at the internal acoustic meatus","Enters at the external acoustic meatus, exits at the carotid canal","Enters at the jugular foramen, exits at the stylomastoid foramen","Enters at the internal acoustic meatus, exits at the stylomastoid foramen"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The facial nerve leaves the brainstem and enters the temporal bone through the internal acoustic meatus, along with the nerve for hearing. It travels through the bone and exits on the underside of the skull at the stylomastoid foramen, between the styloid and mastoid processes, before spreading across the face.","notes":["This is the right pair in reverse. The internal acoustic meatus opens toward the brain, so it must be the entry point for a nerve coming from the brainstem.","The external acoustic meatus is the outer ear canal, which carries sound inward and no nerve from the brain. The carotid canal carries the internal carotid artery.","The exit is right, but the jugular foramen carries the internal jugular vein out of the skull. The facial nerve enters through the internal acoustic meatus instead.","Correct. In at the internal acoustic meatus, out at the stylomastoid foramen."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-skull","lid":"c43","comp":37,"competencyId":"w2-skull-foramina"},{"id":"L00536","dok":2,"q":"Which set correctly names three features of the sphenoid bone: the saddle on its body, the opening that passes the optic nerve, and the opening that passes the nerves that move the eye?","a":"The sella turcica, the optic canal, and the superior orbital fissure.","options":["Glabella, optic canal, and superior orbital fissure","Crista galli, optic canal, and foramen ovale","Sella turcica, optic canal, and superior orbital fissure","Sella turcica, foramen magnum, and superior orbital fissure"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The sphenoid bone holds several key features. The sella turcica on its body cradles the pituitary gland. The optic canal passes the optic nerve from the eye, and the superior orbital fissure, just lateral to it, passes the nerves that move the eye.","notes":["The glabella is a landmark of the frontal bone between the eyebrows, not a saddle. The rest of this set is right, so swap in the sella turcica.","The crista galli belongs to the ethmoid bone, and the foramen ovale passes a branch of the trigeminal nerve to the lower jaw, not the eye-moving nerves.","Correct. Sella turcica, optic canal, and superior orbital fissure.","The foramen magnum is in the occipital bone and carries the spinal cord, not the optic nerve. Swap in the optic canal and the set is correct."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-skull","lid":"c44","comp":34,"competencyId":"w2-skull-markings"},{"id":"L00537","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly matches each paranasal sinus to the bone it occupies: the frontal sinus, the sphenoidal sinus, and the maxillary sinus?","a":"Frontal sinus in the frontal bone, sphenoidal sinus in the sphenoid, maxillary sinus in the maxilla.","options":["Frontal: ethmoid; sphenoidal: sphenoid; maxillary: frontal bone","Frontal: frontal bone; sphenoidal: sphenoid; maxillary: maxilla","Frontal: maxilla; sphenoidal: ethmoid; maxillary: frontal bone","Frontal: frontal bone; sphenoidal: maxilla; maxillary: ethmoid"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Each paranasal sinus is named for the bone that contains it. The frontal sinus is in the frontal bone above the orbits, the sphenoidal sinus is in the body of the sphenoid, and the maxillary sinus, the largest, is in the maxilla in the cheek region. The ethmoidal sinuses, the fourth group, are air cells in the ethmoid bone.","notes":["The sphenoidal match is right, but the frontal sinus is not in the ethmoid. The ethmoid holds the ethmoidal air cells instead.","Correct. Each sinus sits in the bone it is named for.","This scrambles all three. Since the sinuses are named for their bones, the frontal sinus belongs in the frontal bone and the maxillary sinus in the maxilla.","The frontal match is right, but the other two are scrambled. The sphenoidal sinus sits in the sphenoid, and the maxillary sinus sits in the maxilla."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-skull","lid":"c45","comp":36,"competencyId":"w2-skull-cavities"},{"id":"L00538","dok":2,"q":"A clinician examines the air-filled cavity within the body of the sphenoid bone, just below the sella turcica. Which paranasal sinus is this?","a":"The sphenoidal sinus.","options":["The ethmoidal sinuses","The frontal sinus","The maxillary sinus","The sphenoidal sinus"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The sphenoidal sinus is the air space within the body of the sphenoid bone, deep in the center of the skull and just below the sella turcica that holds the pituitary. Like the other paranasal sinuses, it lightens the skull and drains into the nasal cavity.","notes":["The ethmoidal sinuses are many small air cells in the lateral masses of the ethmoid bone, between the orbits. Pick them for a question about the sinuses between the eyes.","The frontal sinus is in the frontal bone above the orbits. It would be the answer for a sinus behind the forehead.","The maxillary sinus is in the maxilla, in the cheek region above the upper teeth. Choose it for a question about the largest sinus.","Correct. The cavity in the body of the sphenoid is the sphenoidal sinus."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-skull","lid":"c46","comp":36,"competencyId":"w2-skull-cavities"},{"id":"L00539","dok":2,"q":"Which marking of the frontal bone is the smooth area between the eyebrow ridges?","a":"The glabella.","options":["The supraorbital margin","The glabella","The orbital plate","The squamous part"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The glabella is the smooth, slightly raised area of the frontal bone between the eyebrow ridges, just above the bridge of the nose. It is a common surface landmark. The name comes from a Latin word meaning smooth and hairless.","notes":["The supraorbital margin is the ridge above each orbit, under the eyebrow. It would be the answer for a question about the bony rim at the top of the eye socket.","Correct. The glabella is the smooth area between the eyebrows.","The orbital plates roof the orbits and floor the anterior cranial fossa, so they lie inside the skull rather than on its surface. Choose them for a question about the roof of the eye socket.","The squamous part is the broad, flat plate that forms most of the forehead. Pick it for a question about the large forehead region of the frontal bone."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-skull","lid":"c47","comp":34,"competencyId":"w2-skull-markings"},{"id":"L00540","dok":2,"q":"Which opening of the mandible passes the nerve and vessels to the chin and lower lip?","a":"The mental foramen.","options":["The mandibular notch","The mental foramen","The mandibular foramen","The infraorbital foramen"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The mental foramen is a small opening on the outer surface of the mandibular body, below the premolar teeth. The nerve and vessels to the chin and lower lip exit here. Mental in this case comes from the Latin for chin, not from the mind.","notes":["The mandibular notch is the gap between the condylar and coronoid processes at the top of the ramus, not an opening for a nerve to the chin. Pick it for a question about the dip between those two processes.","Correct. The mental foramen serves the chin and lower lip.","The mandibular foramen is on the inner surface of the ramus, where the nerve to the lower teeth enters the bone. It would be the answer for a question about the target of a lower dental block.","The infraorbital foramen is on the maxilla below the orbit and passes a nerve to the upper face. Choose it for a question about the cheek and upper lip region."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-skull","lid":"c48","comp":37,"competencyId":"w2-skull-foramina"},{"id":"L00541","dok":1,"q":"Which facial bones form the bridge of the nose?","a":"The nasal bones.","options":["The lacrimal bones","The nasal bones","The vomer","The inferior nasal conchae"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The two nasal bones meet in the midline to form the bony bridge of the nose, between the orbits. Below them, the lower part of the nose is shaped by cartilage rather than bone, which is why the tip of the nose is flexible.","notes":["The lacrimal bones are in the medial wall of each orbit and hold the tear duct. They would be the answer for a question about the bone grooved for tears.","Correct. The nasal bones form the bridge of the nose.","The vomer forms the lower part of the nasal septum, inside the nasal cavity. Pick it for a question about the bony partition between the nostrils.","The inferior nasal conchae are scroll-shaped bones on the lateral walls of the nasal cavity. Choose them for a question about the bones that project into the nasal airway."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-skull","lid":"c49","comp":33,"competencyId":"w2-skull-bones"},{"id":"L00542","dok":2,"q":"An anatomist finds an upright bony ridge on the ethmoid bone projecting up into the cranial cavity, where a fold of the brain covering attaches. Which marking is this?","a":"The crista galli.","options":["The crista galli","The cribriform plate","The sella turcica","The perpendicular plate"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The crista galli, meaning rooster's comb, is an upright ridge on top of the ethmoid bone. It projects up into the anterior cranial fossa, and the falx cerebri, a fold of the dura mater between the cerebral hemispheres, attaches to it.","notes":["Correct. The crista galli is the ridge where the falx cerebri attaches.","The cribriform plate is a horizontal, perforated plate on either side of the crista galli. Pick it for a question about where the olfactory nerve filaments pass.","The sella turcica is on the sphenoid bone, not the ethmoid, and holds the pituitary gland. Choose it for a question about the saddle-shaped seat in the cranial base.","The perpendicular plate also runs vertically, but it projects downward into the nasal cavity to form the upper nasal septum. It would be the answer for a question about the septum."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-skull","lid":"c50","comp":34,"competencyId":"w2-skull-markings"},{"id":"L00543","dok":2,"q":"The orbit, the bony eye socket, is built from how many bones?","a":"Seven bones.","options":["Four bones","Nine bones","Seven bones","Five bones"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The orbit is built from seven bones: the frontal, sphenoid, zygomatic, maxilla, lacrimal, ethmoid, and palatine. Three are cranial bones (frontal, sphenoid, ethmoid), and four are facial bones. Building it from many bones is why the orbit has both a sturdy rim and some thin walls.","notes":["Four is the number of bones that meet at the pterion, a different landmark on the side of the skull. Students mix up the two counts when studying skull junctions together.","Nine overcounts by adding bones that do not reach the orbit, such as the nasal bone or the temporal bone. Neither of these forms part of the eye socket.","Correct. Seven bones form the orbit.","Five leaves out some of the smaller contributors, such as the lacrimal and palatine bones. Students who count only the bones forming the rim tend to land on a lower number."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-skull","lid":"c51","comp":36,"competencyId":"w2-skull-cavities"},{"id":"L00544","dok":2,"q":"Which list correctly names the suture between the two parietal bones, the suture between the frontal and parietal bones, and the suture between a parietal and a temporal bone, in that order?","a":"The sagittal, coronal, and squamous sutures.","options":["Sagittal, coronal, and squamous","Lambdoid, coronal, and squamous","Coronal, sagittal, and lambdoid","Sagittal, squamous, and coronal"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The sagittal suture joins the two parietal bones along the midline. The coronal suture joins the frontal bone to the parietal bones across the top of the skull. The squamous suture joins each parietal bone to the temporal bone below it on the side of the skull.","notes":["Correct. Sagittal, coronal, then squamous.","The lambdoid suture joins the parietal bones to the occipital bone, not the two parietals to each other. The midline suture between the parietals is the sagittal suture.","This swaps the first two and ends with the lambdoid suture, which joins the parietal bones to the occipital bone at the back. The lambdoid would fit a question about the back of the skull.","This has the right three sutures, but the last two are swapped. The frontal and parietal bones meet at the coronal suture, and the parietal and temporal bones meet at the squamous suture."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-skull","lid":"c52","comp":35,"competencyId":"w2-sutures-fontanelles"},{"id":"L00546","dok":1,"q":"Which bone contains the supraorbital foramen?","a":"The frontal bone.","options":["The frontal bone","The zygomatic bone","The maxilla","The sphenoid bone"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The supraorbital foramen is an opening in the supraorbital margin of the frontal bone, the ridge above each orbit. It passes the supraorbital nerve and vessels to the forehead. Supra- means above, so the name places it above the orbit.","notes":["Correct. The supraorbital foramen is in the frontal bone.","The zygomatic bone forms the cheek and the lateral rim of the orbit, not the upper rim. Choose it for a question about the cheekbone.","The maxilla has the infraorbital foramen, below the orbit. Students confuse the two because the names differ only by the prefix, so pick the maxilla for a question about the infraorbital foramen.","The sphenoid bone forms the back of the orbit and contains the optic canal and superior orbital fissure. Pick it for a question about openings deep in the orbit."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-skull","lid":"c3-x2","comp":37,"competencyId":"w2-skull-foramina"},{"id":"L00548","dok":1,"q":"Which bones form the upper jaw and hold the upper teeth?","a":"The maxillae.","options":["The palatine bones","The maxillae","The zygomatic bones","The mandible"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The two maxillae form the upper jaw. Their alveolar processes hold the upper teeth, their palatine processes form the front of the hard palate, and each contains a maxillary sinus. The maxillae meet almost every other facial bone, so they are often called the keystone of the face.","notes":["The palatine bones form the back of the hard palate but hold no teeth. They would be the answer for a question about the posterior roof of the mouth.","Correct. The maxillae form the upper jaw.","The zygomatic bones are the cheekbones, lateral to the maxillae. Choose them for a question about the prominence of the cheek.","The mandible is the lower jaw and holds the lower teeth. Pick it for a question about the only movable skull bone."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-skull","lid":"c6-x2","comp":33,"competencyId":"w2-skull-bones"},{"id":"L00550","dok":1,"q":"Which single facial bone forms the inferior part of the nasal septum?","a":"The vomer.","options":["A nasal bone","The ethmoid bone","The mandible","The vomer"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The vomer is a thin, single facial bone that forms the lower part of the nasal septum. The perpendicular plate of the ethmoid forms the upper part, and septal cartilage completes the front. The name means plowshare, which describes its shape.","notes":["The nasal bones are paired and form the bridge of the nose, not the septum. Pick them for a question about the bony bridge.","The ethmoid bone forms the upper part of the septum through its perpendicular plate, but it is a cranial bone, not a facial one. Choose it for a question about the superior nasal septum.","The mandible is the other single facial bone, but it is the lower jaw, not part of the nose. It would be the answer for a question about the movable jaw.","Correct. The vomer forms the inferior nasal septum."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-skull","lid":"c8-x2","comp":36,"competencyId":"w2-skull-cavities"},{"id":"L00551","dok":1,"q":"The coronal suture joins which bones?","a":"The frontal bone and the two parietal bones.","options":["A parietal bone and a temporal bone","The two parietal bones to each other","The parietal bones and the occipital bone","The frontal bone and the two parietal bones"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The coronal suture runs across the top of the skull from side to side, just behind the forehead. It joins the frontal bone in front to the two parietal bones behind. In an infant, the anterior fontanelle sits where it crosses the sagittal suture.","notes":["This describes the squamous suture on the side of the skull. Choose it if the question asked about the squamous suture.","This describes the sagittal suture, which runs along the midline of the skull roof. It would be the answer if the question named the sagittal suture.","This describes the lambdoid suture at the back of the skull. Pick it if the question asked about the lambdoid suture.","Correct. The coronal suture joins the frontal bone to the parietal bones."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-skull","lid":"c10-x2","comp":35,"competencyId":"w2-sutures-fontanelles"},{"id":"L00557","dok":2,"q":"The mandibular fossa, the socket of the jaw joint, is a marking of which bone?","a":"The temporal bone.","options":["The occipital bone","The sphenoid bone","The temporal bone","The mandible"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The mandibular fossa is a socket on the underside of the temporal bone, just in front of the ear canal. It is named for the bone that fits into it, the mandible, not for the bone it is on. The condylar process of the mandible sits in it to form the temporomandibular joint.","notes":["The occipital bone forms the back of the skull and articulates with the atlas, not the mandible. Choose it for a question about the occipital condyles.","The sphenoid bone is just in front of the temporal bone in the cranial base, and its pterygoid processes anchor chewing muscles, but it does not form the jaw socket. Pick it for a question about the sella turcica.","Correct. The mandibular fossa is on the temporal bone.","The mandible supplies the condylar process that fits into the fossa, but the fossa itself is on the temporal bone. Students pick the mandible because the fossa is named after it."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-skull","lid":"c17-x2","comp":34,"competencyId":"w2-skull-markings"},{"id":"L00559","dok":2,"q":"The optic canal, which passes the optic nerve, is located in which bone?","a":"The sphenoid bone.","options":["The sphenoid bone","The frontal bone","The maxilla","The ethmoid bone"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The optic canal passes through the sphenoid bone at the back of the orbit, at the base of its lesser wing. The optic nerve, cranial nerve II, travels through it from the eye to the brain. The superior orbital fissure, also in the sphenoid, lies just beside it.","notes":["Correct. The optic canal is in the sphenoid bone.","The frontal bone forms the roof of the orbit and contains the supraorbital foramen. Pick it for a question about the opening above the eye.","The maxilla forms the floor of the orbit and contains the infraorbital foramen. Choose it for a question about the opening below the eye.","The ethmoid bone forms part of the medial wall of the orbit and contains the cribriform plate for the olfactory nerve. It would be the answer for a question about the nerve of smell."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-skull","lid":"c19-x2","comp":37,"competencyId":"w2-skull-foramina"},{"id":"L00560","dok":2,"q":"The carotid canal of the temporal bone transmits which vessel?","a":"The internal carotid artery.","options":["The vertebral artery","The internal jugular vein","The external carotid artery","The internal carotid artery"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The carotid canal carries the internal carotid artery through the temporal bone into the cranial cavity, where it helps supply the brain. Its partner on the drainage side, the internal jugular vein, leaves through the nearby jugular foramen.","notes":["The vertebral arteries enter the skull through the foramen magnum. Pick them for a question about the arteries that pass alongside the spinal cord.","The internal jugular vein leaves the skull through the jugular foramen, just behind the carotid canal. It would be the answer for a question about the jugular foramen.","The external carotid artery stays outside the skull and supplies the face and scalp. Choose it for a question about blood supply to the face, since only the internal carotid enters the cranium.","Correct. The internal carotid artery passes through the carotid canal."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-skull","lid":"c20-x2","comp":37,"competencyId":"w2-skull-foramina"},{"id":"L00561","dok":2,"q":"Where on the head can the mastoid process be felt?","a":"Just behind the ear.","options":["At the point of the chin","Just behind the ear","Just above the orbit","At the back of the head in the midline"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The mastoid process is a rounded bump of the temporal bone just behind and below the ear. It anchors neck muscles and is filled with small air cells. You can feel it easily by pressing behind your earlobe.","notes":["The point of the chin is formed by the mandible, near the mental foramen. Pick it for a question about the front of the lower jaw.","Correct. The mastoid process is just behind the ear.","The ridge above the orbit is the supraorbital margin of the frontal bone. It would be the answer for a question about the bony rim under the eyebrow.","The midline bump at the back of the head is the external occipital protuberance of the occipital bone. Choose it for a question about that landmark."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-skull","lid":"c21-x2","comp":34,"competencyId":"w2-skull-markings"},{"id":"L00562","dok":2,"q":"The occipital condyles articulate with which bone of the vertebral column?","a":"The atlas.","options":["The seventh cervical vertebra","The atlas","The axis","The first thoracic vertebra"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The occipital condyles rest on the superior articular facets of the atlas, the first cervical vertebra (C1). Together they form the atlanto-occipital joint, which lets you nod yes. The atlas, in turn, pivots on the axis below it.","notes":["The seventh cervical vertebra has the prominent spinous process at the base of the neck. Pick it for a question about the vertebra prominens.","Correct. The occipital condyles articulate with the atlas.","The axis is C2, which sits below the atlas and has the dens that the atlas pivots around. It would be the answer for a question about the vertebra that allows shaking the head no.","The first thoracic vertebra is the first vertebra to articulate with a rib. Choose it for a question about where the thoracic region begins."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-skull","lid":"c22-x2","comp":34,"competencyId":"w2-skull-markings"},{"id":"L00564","dok":2,"q":"Which part of the temporal bone is the dense wedge that houses the middle and inner ear?","a":"The petrous part.","options":["The zygomatic process","The petrous part","The styloid process","The squamous part"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The petrous part is the dense, wedge-shaped portion of the temporal bone that projects into the cranial base. It houses the middle and inner ear. Petrous means rock-like, which describes how hard this bone is.","notes":["The zygomatic process projects forward to join the cheekbone and form the zygomatic arch. Pick it for a question about the arch in front of the ear.","Correct. The petrous part houses the middle and inner ear.","The styloid process is a slender spike below the ear that anchors tongue and neck muscles. Choose it for a question about where the hyoid ligaments attach.","The squamous part is the thin, flat portion of the temporal bone forming the lower side wall of the skull. It would be the answer for a question about the flat plate above the ear."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-skull","lid":"c24-x2","comp":34,"competencyId":"w2-skull-markings"},{"id":"L00565","dok":2,"q":"Which of these bones forms part of the wall of the orbit?","a":"The lacrimal bone","options":["The parietal bone","The nasal bone","The lacrimal bone","The vomer"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The orbit is built from seven bones: the frontal, sphenoid, zygomatic, maxilla, lacrimal, ethmoid, and palatine. The lacrimal bone forms part of the medial wall and is grooved for the tear duct. None of the other three bones listed forms any of the orbit's walls.","notes":["The parietal bones form the top and sides of the cranium, well behind and above the orbit. They would be the answer for a question about the skull roof.","The nasal bones form the bridge of the nose between the orbits but are not part of the orbital walls. Students choose them because they sit so close to the eyes. Choose them for a question about the bridge of the nose.","Correct. The lacrimal bone forms part of the medial wall of the orbit.","The vomer forms the lower part of the nasal septum in the midline of the nasal cavity. Pick it for a question about the bony nasal partition."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-skull","lid":"c25-x2","comp":36,"competencyId":"w2-skull-cavities"},{"id":"L00566","dok":2,"q":"A blow to the side of the head fractures the skull at the pterion. Which artery running just beneath this site is at risk of tearing?","a":"The middle meningeal artery.","options":["The vertebral artery","The facial artery","The internal carotid artery","The middle meningeal artery"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The pterion is the thinnest part of the skull, where the frontal, parietal, temporal, and sphenoid bones meet. The middle meningeal artery runs just beneath it on the inner surface, supplying the meninges. A fracture here can tear the artery and cause bleeding inside the skull, which is a medical emergency.","notes":["The vertebral arteries enter through the foramen magnum at the back of the skull base. Pick them for a question about the foramen magnum.","The facial artery runs outside the skull across the face, so a pterion fracture would not tear it. Choose it for a question about blood supply to the face.","The internal carotid artery enters through the carotid canal deep in the skull base, well away from the side wall. It would be the answer for a question about the carotid canal.","Correct. The middle meningeal artery runs beneath the pterion."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-skull","lid":"c26-x2","comp":35,"competencyId":"w2-sutures-fontanelles"},{"id":"L00567","dok":2,"q":"An infection at the root of an upper tooth spreads into a nearby air-filled cavity. Which sinus is most likely involved?","a":"The maxillary sinus.","options":["The sphenoidal sinus","The frontal sinus","The ethmoidal sinuses","The maxillary sinus"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The maxillary sinus fills the body of the maxilla, and its floor sits just above the roots of the upper teeth. Because the two are so close, an infection at a tooth root can spread into the sinus. The maxillary sinuses are also the largest of the paranasal sinuses.","notes":["The sphenoidal sinus is deep in the body of the sphenoid bone, behind the nasal cavity. Pick it for a question about the sinus just below the pituitary gland.","The frontal sinus is in the frontal bone above the orbits, far from the teeth. It would be the answer for sinus pain in the forehead.","The ethmoidal sinuses are air cells between the orbits, above the level of the teeth. Choose them for a question about sinus pain between the eyes.","Correct. The maxillary sinus sits just above the upper tooth roots."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-skull","lid":"c27-x2","comp":36,"competencyId":"w2-skull-cavities"},{"id":"L00568","dok":2,"q":"Rising pressure inside the rigid skull forces brain tissue toward the one large opening in the cranial base. Through which opening can the tissue be forced?","a":"The foramen magnum.","options":["The superior orbital fissure","The foramen magnum","The optic canal","The carotid canal"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The skull is a rigid box, so swelling or bleeding inside it raises pressure with nowhere to go. The foramen magnum is the only large opening between the cranial cavity and the vertebral canal, so brain tissue can be pushed down through it and compress the brainstem. That is why rising intracranial pressure is an emergency.","notes":["The superior orbital fissure is a slit leading to the orbit that passes the nerves that move the eye. Choose it for a question about those nerves.","Correct. The foramen magnum is the large opening that brain tissue can be forced through.","The optic canal is a small opening for the optic nerve and leads into the orbit, not toward the spinal cord. It would be the answer for a question about the path of the optic nerve.","The carotid canal is a narrow passage for the internal carotid artery. Pick it for a question about how that artery enters the skull."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-skull","lid":"c28-x2","comp":37,"competencyId":"w2-skull-foramina"},{"id":"L00569","dok":2,"q":"A nurse checks an infant for dehydration by feeling a soft spot on the top of the skull. Which fontanelle is used for this check?","a":"The anterior fontanelle.","options":["The anterolateral fontanelle","The posterior fontanelle","The anterior fontanelle","The posterolateral fontanelle"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The anterior fontanelle is the largest fontanelle and stays open the longest, until about 18 to 24 months, so it is easy to feel on top of the head. A sunken anterior fontanelle can signal dehydration, and a bulging one can signal raised pressure inside the skull.","notes":["The anterolateral fontanelles are small, on the sides of the skull, and close by about 3 months. Pick them for a question about the sphenoidal fontanelles.","The posterior fontanelle is small and closes at about 2 months, so it is not useful for checks later in infancy. It would be the answer for a question about the first fontanelle to close.","Correct. The anterior fontanelle is the one checked for dehydration.","The posterolateral fontanelles are behind the ears and are not on top of the head. Choose them for a question about the mastoid fontanelles."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-skull","lid":"c29-x2","comp":35,"competencyId":"w2-sutures-fontanelles"},{"id":"L00570","dok":3,"q":"A patient loses the sense of smell after a head injury. Damage to which skull structure would best explain this?","a":"The cribriform plate of the ethmoid bone.","options":["The mental foramen of the mandible","The mastoid process of the temporal bone","The cribriform plate of the ethmoid","The sella turcica of the sphenoid"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The olfactory nerve filaments pass from the nasal lining up through the many small holes of the cribriform plate to reach the brain. The plate is thin and perforated, so a blow to the head can fracture it and tear these filaments. That cuts the pathway for smell.","notes":["The mental foramen passes the nerve to the chin and lower lip. Choose it for a question about numbness of the chin.","The mastoid process is a muscle attachment behind the ear filled with air cells. Pick it for a question about the bump behind the ear or an infection spreading from the middle ear.","Correct. Damage to the cribriform plate can tear the olfactory nerve filaments.","The sella turcica holds the pituitary gland. Damage there would be the answer for a question about injury to the pituitary, not the sense of smell."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-skull","lid":"c30-x2","comp":34,"competencyId":"w2-skull-markings"},{"id":"L00571","dok":3,"q":"An infant's skull bones are joined by soft fibrous fontanelles rather than fused sutures. Why does this matter at birth and in early life?","a":"It lets the skull compress during birth and then expand as the brain grows.","options":["It makes the skull heavier so the head is more stable","It lets the skull compress at birth and expand as the brain grows","It lets the skull bones move apart freely so the jaw can open","It keeps the brain from growing until the bones have fused"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Fontanelles are fibrous membrane gaps between the bones of an infant's skull. At birth they let the skull bones shift and overlap so the head can pass through the birth canal. Afterward they leave room for the skull to enlarge as the brain grows rapidly, and they close gradually over the first two years.","notes":["Membrane gaps make the skull lighter and more flexible, not heavier. This choice reverses the effect of having unfused bones.","Correct. Fontanelles allow compression at birth and growth afterward.","Jaw opening happens at the temporomandibular joint, not at the fontanelles. This choice mixes up the jaw joint with the soft spots of the cranium.","The brain grows fastest in infancy, while the fontanelles are open. This choice reverses the relationship, since the open fontanelles are what make room for that growth."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-skull","lid":"d3-t-axial-skeleton-skull-1","comp":35,"competencyId":"w2-sutures-fontanelles"},{"id":"L00572","dok":3,"q":"Adult cranial bones meet at immovable, interlocking sutures. Why is an immovable joint suited to the bones around the brain?","a":"Locked joints form a stable protective case around the brain.","options":["Immovable joints let the cranial bones keep growing in length","Locked joints make the skull bones much lighter","Immovable joints let the cranium expand with each heartbeat","Locked joints form a stable protective case around the brain"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"A suture is an immovable fibrous joint, and the wavy, interlocking edges of the cranial bones lock them together. This turns the cranium into a single rigid case that protects the brain from forces coming from any direction. Movement is needed only at the jaw, which has its own synovial joint.","notes":["Skull bones do not grow in length at sutures the way long bones grow at epiphyseal plates. This choice applies the long bone growth pattern to the skull.","The type of joint does not change bone weight. The skull is lightened by its air-filled sinuses, so pick the paranasal sinuses for a question about reducing skull weight.","The adult cranium does not expand, which is exactly why rising pressure inside it is dangerous. This choice assumes the skull flexes when it does not.","Correct. Rigid sutures make a stable protective case."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-skull","lid":"d3-t-axial-skeleton-skull-2","comp":35,"competencyId":"w2-sutures-fontanelles"},{"id":"L00573","dok":2,"q":"Several skull bones, such as the maxillae and the frontal bone, contain air-filled paranasal sinuses. What is one advantage of these hollow spaces?","a":"They lighten the skull while it keeps its outer strength.","options":["They store blood that supplies the brain","They hold cerebrospinal fluid around the brain","They house the structures of the inner ear","They make the skull lighter"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The paranasal sinuses are air-filled spaces within the frontal, ethmoid, sphenoid, and maxillary bones. They make the skull lighter without weakening its outer surfaces, and they add resonance to the voice. They drain into the nasal cavity, which is why a head cold can spread into them as sinusitis.","notes":["The sinuses hold air, not blood. Blood reaches the brain through the internal carotid and vertebral arteries, so pick those for a question about brain blood supply.","Cerebrospinal fluid fills the ventricles and the space around the brain, inside the meninges. Choose the subarachnoid space for a question about where CSF surrounds the brain.","The inner ear is housed in the dense petrous part of the temporal bone, not in an air sinus. Pick the petrous part for a question about where the hearing organs sit.","Correct. The sinuses lighten the skull."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-skull","lid":"d3-t-axial-skeleton-skull-3","comp":36,"competencyId":"w2-skull-cavities"},{"id":"L00574","dok":3,"q":"The foramen magnum is a large opening in the occipital bone at the base of the skull. Why must an opening this large exist there?","a":"The brainstem continues into the spinal cord there, so the cord must pass through the skull base.","options":["The esophagus passes through it on its way to the stomach","The brainstem continues into the spinal cord through it","It makes room for the atlas to rotate on the skull","The internal jugular veins leave the skull through it"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The medulla oblongata, the lowest part of the brainstem, continues into the spinal cord at the foramen magnum. The cord must pass from the cranial cavity into the vertebral canal, so the skull needs a large opening directly above the spine. The vertebral arteries and spinal accessory nerve also pass through it.","notes":["The esophagus runs in the neck in front of the vertebral column and never enters the skull. Pick the pharynx and esophagus for a question about the path of food.","Correct. The spinal cord passes through the foramen magnum.","The atlas rotates on the axis below it, not on the skull, and it meets the skull at the occipital condyles beside the foramen. Pick the occipital condyles for a question about the skull's joint with the spine.","The internal jugular veins leave through the jugular foramina, between the temporal and occipital bones. Choose the jugular foramen for a question about venous drainage of the brain."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-skull","lid":"d3-t-axial-skeleton-skull-4","comp":37,"competencyId":"w2-skull-foramina"},{"id":"L00575","dok":3,"q":"The mandible is the only freely movable skull bone, meeting the temporal bone at the temporomandibular joint. Why does the jaw need a movable joint when the rest of the skull does not?","a":"Chewing and speaking require the lower jaw to move, unlike the bones that protect the brain.","options":["All skull bones move equally, so the mandible is not different","Chewing and speech need a moving jaw","The jaw must move to pump cerebrospinal fluid around the brain","The jaw moves to give the brain extra room as it grows"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The temporomandibular joint is a synovial joint between the condylar process of the mandible and the mandibular fossa of the temporal bone. It lets the lower jaw open, close, and slide for chewing and speaking. The cranial bones have a different job, protecting the brain, so they are locked together by immovable sutures.","notes":["In the adult, every other skull bone is fixed by sutures, so the mandible really is different. This choice ignores the difference between sutures and a synovial joint.","Correct. The jaw moves for chewing and speech, while the cranium stays rigid for protection.","Cerebrospinal fluid is produced and circulated within the ventricles and meninges, independent of jaw movement. Pick the choroid plexus for a question about where CSF is made.","Room for brain growth comes from the fontanelles and sutures of the infant cranium, not the jaw. Choose the fontanelles for a question about how the skull grows with the brain."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-skull","lid":"d3-t-axial-skeleton-skull-5","comp":33,"competencyId":"w2-skull-bones"},{"id":"L00576","dok":3,"q":"The cribriform plate of the ethmoid bone is thin and full of small holes. Why does a hard blow to the head put this plate at special risk?","a":"It fractures easily, which can tear the olfactory nerve filaments or let cerebrospinal fluid leak into the nose.","options":["It fractures easily, tearing smell nerves or leaking CSF into the nose","It is the thickest skull bone and transfers the force to the brain","It is made of cartilage, so it cannot heal after a blow","Its holes carry the internal carotid arteries, which can tear"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The cribriform plate is a thin horizontal plate that separates the nasal cavity below from the brain above. Its many holes for the olfactory nerve filaments make it weak, so a blow can fracture it. A fracture can tear the filaments and cause loss of smell, and it can open a path for cerebrospinal fluid to leak into the nose.","notes":["Correct. Its thin, perforated structure makes it easy to fracture.","The cribriform plate is one of the thinnest parts of the skull, not the thickest. Pick the petrous part of the temporal bone for a question about the densest region of the skull.","The cribriform plate is bone, not cartilage. Cartilage does form the front of the nasal septum, so pick septal cartilage for a question about the flexible part of the nose.","The holes carry the olfactory nerve filaments, not arteries. The internal carotid enters through the carotid canal, so choose that for a question about the artery."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-skull","lid":"d3-t-axial-skeleton-skull-6","comp":34,"competencyId":"w2-skull-markings"},{"id":"L00577","dok":3,"q":"The orbit is built from parts of several bones. Why can a blunt blow to the eye cause a blowout fracture of the orbital floor instead of breaking the rim?","a":"The floor is a thin wall, so the sudden pressure breaks it before the sturdy rim.","options":["The floor is formed by the frontal bone, which is very thin","The orbit is made of cartilage, which tears more easily than bone","The floor is the thickest part of the orbit, so it absorbs the force","The floor is a thin wall, so pressure breaks it before the sturdy rim"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The orbital rim is thick and strong, but the floor of the orbit, formed mainly by the maxilla above the maxillary sinus, is a thin plate of bone. A blunt object such as a ball can press the eye back into the socket and spike the pressure inside it. The pressure breaks the weakest wall, the floor, while the rim stays intact.","notes":["The frontal bone forms the roof of the orbit, not the floor. The floor is formed mainly by the maxilla, so pick the frontal bone only for a question about the orbital roof.","The orbit is made entirely of bone, with no cartilage in its walls. This choice confuses the orbit with the flexible cartilage of the external nose.","This reverses the real structure. The rim is the thick, sturdy part, and the floor is thin, which is why it is the part that gives way.","Correct. The thin orbital floor breaks before the strong rim."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-skull","lid":"d3-t-axial-skeleton-skull-7","comp":36,"competencyId":"w2-skull-cavities"},{"id":"L00578","dok":3,"q":"The inner ear sits deep within the petrous part of the temporal bone. Why is this location an advantage?","a":"The delicate hearing and balance organs are protected by very dense bone.","options":["The delicate hearing and balance organs gain protection from dense bone","The petrous part is hollow, so it holds air for the inner ear","The petrous part is the thinnest bone, so sound reaches the ear easily","The inner ear makes new bone, so it must sit inside a bone"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The petrous part of the temporal bone is the densest, hardest region of the skull, and its name means rock-like. The inner ear holds the delicate organs of hearing and balance, and burying them inside this dense bone protects them from injury. The middle ear is housed in the same region.","notes":["Correct. Dense petrous bone protects the inner ear.","The petrous part is dense, not hollow. The air spaces of the temporal bone are the mastoid air cells behind the ear, so pick those for a question about air within the temporal bone.","The thinnest part of the skull is the pterion, on the side of the cranium. This choice mixes up the densest region with the thinnest.","The inner ear does not produce bone. This choice confuses the organ with the bone cells that build bone, the osteoblasts."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-skull","lid":"d3-t-axial-skeleton-skull-8","comp":34,"competencyId":"w2-skull-markings"},{"id":"N-m2-skull-401","dok":2,"q":"Where on an infant's skull is the posterior fontanelle located?","a":"Where the parietal bones meet the occipital bone.","options":["Behind the ear near the mastoid region","On the side of the skull near the sphenoid bone","Where the frontal bone meets the parietal bones","Where the parietal bones meet the occipital bone"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The posterior fontanelle, also called the occipital fontanelle, is at the back of the infant's head, where the two parietal bones meet the occipital bone. It is small and closes early, at about 2 months. In the adult, the lambdoid suture runs through this area.","notes":["This describes the posterolateral, or mastoid, fontanelles. Choose it for a question about the soft spots behind the ears.","This describes the anterolateral, or sphenoidal, fontanelles. Pick it for a question about the soft spots on the sides of the skull.","This is the location of the anterior fontanelle, the largest one. It would be the answer for a question about the fontanelle checked for dehydration.","Correct. The posterior fontanelle is at the parietal and occipital junction."],"comp":35,"competencyId":"w2-sutures-fontanelles"},{"id":"N-m2-skull-402","dok":2,"q":"The hypoglossal canal, which passes cranial nerve XII, is found in which bone?","a":"The occipital bone.","options":["The mandible","The sphenoid bone","The temporal bone","The occipital bone"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The hypoglossal canal is a small canal in the occipital bone, just above each occipital condyle beside the foramen magnum. It passes the hypoglossal nerve, cranial nerve XII, which heads toward the tongue. Hypo- means below and glossal means tongue.","notes":["The mandible contains the mandibular and mental foramina. Choose it for a question about the nerve to the lower teeth or the chin.","The sphenoid bone contains the optic canal, the superior orbital fissure, and the foramen ovale. Pick it for a question about openings passing nerves to the eye or jaw.","The temporal bone contains the carotid canal, the internal acoustic meatus, and the stylomastoid foramen. It would be the answer for a question about any of those openings.","Correct. The hypoglossal canal is in the occipital bone."],"comp":37,"competencyId":"w2-skull-foramina"},{"id":"N-m2-skull-403","dok":2,"q":"The foramen ovale passes a branch of the trigeminal nerve to the lower jaw. In which bone is it found?","a":"The sphenoid bone.","options":["The sphenoid bone","The occipital bone","The temporal bone","The ethmoid bone"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The foramen ovale is an oval opening in the greater wing of the sphenoid bone, in the floor of the middle cranial fossa. It passes the branch of the trigeminal nerve that serves the lower jaw. Ovale simply means oval, describing its shape.","notes":["Correct. The foramen ovale is in the sphenoid bone.","The occipital bone contains the foramen magnum and the hypoglossal canals. Pick it for a question about the opening for the spinal cord.","The temporal bone sits just behind the foramen ovale and has the carotid canal and the acoustic meatuses. It would be the answer for a question about the opening for the internal carotid artery.","The ethmoid bone contains the cribriform foramina for the olfactory nerve. Choose it for a question about the nerve of smell."],"comp":37,"competencyId":"w2-skull-foramina"},{"id":"N-m2-skull-404","dok":2,"q":"Which marking of the temporal bone is the slender spike below the ear that anchors tongue and neck muscles and the ligaments that hold the hyoid?","a":"The styloid process.","options":["The zygomatic process","The mastoid process","The coronoid process","The styloid process"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The styloid process is a thin, pointed spike projecting down from the underside of the temporal bone, below the ear. Muscles of the tongue and neck attach to it, and ligaments run from it to suspend the hyoid bone. Stylo- means pen or pillar, which matches its shape.","notes":["The zygomatic process projects forward from the temporal bone to meet the cheekbone. Pick it for a question about the zygomatic arch.","The mastoid process is the rounded bump behind the ear, an attachment for neck muscles. It would be the answer for a question about the bump you can feel behind the earlobe.","The coronoid process belongs to the mandible and anchors the temporalis muscle. Choose it for a question about the jaw.","Correct. The styloid process is the slender spike that anchors the hyoid ligaments."],"comp":34,"competencyId":"w2-skull-markings"},{"id":"N-m2-skull-405","dok":2,"q":"The zygomatic arch is formed where the zygomatic bone meets which marking?","a":"The zygomatic process of the temporal bone.","options":["The greater wing of the sphenoid bone","The zygomatic process of the temporal bone","The coronoid process of the mandible","The frontal process of the maxilla"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The zygomatic arch is the bony bar along the side of the face, between the cheek and the ear. It forms where the zygomatic process of the temporal bone reaches forward to join the zygomatic bone, the cheekbone. You can feel it as a ridge in front of the ear.","notes":["The greater wing of the sphenoid forms part of the lateral skull wall and orbit, deep to the arch. Choose it for a question about the bones at the pterion.","Correct. The zygomatic process of the temporal bone completes the arch.","The coronoid process of the mandible sits beneath the zygomatic arch and anchors the temporalis, but it does not form the arch. Pick it for a question about the attachment of the temporalis muscle.","The frontal process of the maxilla rises beside the nose to meet the frontal bone. It would be the answer for a question about the part of the maxilla near the bridge of the nose."],"comp":34,"competencyId":"w2-skull-markings"},{"id":"N-m2-skull-406","dok":2,"q":"Which nasal conchae are separate facial bones rather than parts of the ethmoid bone?","a":"The inferior nasal conchae.","options":["The lateral masses","The superior nasal conchae","The middle nasal conchae","The inferior nasal conchae"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The nasal cavity has three pairs of scroll-shaped conchae on its lateral walls. The superior and middle conchae are projections of the ethmoid bone, but the inferior nasal conchae are separate facial bones. That is why the inferior nasal conchae are counted among the fourteen facial bones.","notes":["The lateral masses are the side portions of the ethmoid that contain the ethmoidal air cells, and the superior and middle conchae hang from them. Choose them for a question about where the ethmoidal sinuses are.","The superior conchae are part of the ethmoid bone, not separate bones. They would be the answer for a question about the highest scrolls of the nasal wall.","The middle conchae are also part of the ethmoid bone. Pick them for a question about the ethmoid's scroll-shaped projections below the superior conchae.","Correct. The inferior nasal conchae are separate facial bones."],"comp":33,"competencyId":"w2-skull-bones"},{"id":"N-m2-skull-407","dok":2,"q":"The infraorbital foramen, which passes a nerve to the face below the eye, is found in which bone?","a":"The maxilla.","options":["The zygomatic bone","The frontal bone","The maxilla","The sphenoid bone"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The infraorbital foramen is an opening in the maxilla just below the orbit. The infraorbital nerve passes through it to supply the skin of the face below the eye. Infra- means below, so the name places it under the orbit.","notes":["The zygomatic bone forms the cheekbone, lateral to the infraorbital foramen. Pick it for a question about the prominence of the cheek.","The frontal bone contains the supraorbital foramen, above the orbit. It would be the answer if the question asked about the opening above the eye.","Correct. The infraorbital foramen is in the maxilla.","The sphenoid bone forms the back of the orbit and contains the optic canal. Choose it for a question about openings deep in the orbit."],"comp":37,"competencyId":"w2-skull-foramina"},{"id":"N-m2-skull-408","dok":2,"q":"The atlanto-occipital joint, where the occipital condyles meet the atlas, mainly allows which head movement?","a":"Nodding the head yes.","options":["Opening and closing the jaw","Tilting the skull bones apart","Nodding the head yes","Shaking the head no"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The atlanto-occipital joint forms where the occipital condyles rest on the superior articular facets of the atlas. Its shape allows the head to rock forward and back, which is the nodding yes motion. Rotation of the head, the no motion, happens at the joint between the atlas and the axis instead.","notes":["Opening and closing the jaw happens at the temporomandibular joint between the mandible and the temporal bone. Pick it for a question about chewing.","Adult skull bones do not move apart, since they are locked by sutures. This choice mixes up a joint between the skull and spine with the joints between skull bones.","Correct. The atlanto-occipital joint lets you nod yes.","Shaking the head no happens at the atlantoaxial joint, where the atlas pivots around the dens of the axis. It would be the answer for a question about the joint between C1 and C2."],"comp":40,"competencyId":"w2-regional-vertebrae"},{"id":"N-m2-skull-409","dok":2,"q":"Which projections of the sphenoid bone point downward and anchor the chewing muscles?","a":"The pterygoid processes.","options":["The greater wings","The styloid processes","The lesser wings","The pterygoid processes"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The pterygoid processes project downward from the underside of the sphenoid bone, behind the maxillae. They serve as attachment points for chewing muscles. Pterygoid means wing-shaped, which can be confusing because the sphenoid also has separate greater and lesser wings.","notes":["The greater wings are broad lateral parts of the sphenoid that form part of the cranial floor, the orbit, and the lateral skull wall. They would be the answer for a question about the sphenoid at the pterion.","The styloid processes belong to the temporal bones and anchor tongue and neck muscles. Choose them for a question about where the hyoid is suspended.","The lesser wings are smaller anterior parts of the sphenoid that form part of the anterior cranial fossa. Pick them for a question about the floor beneath the frontal lobes.","Correct. The pterygoid processes anchor the chewing muscles."],"comp":34,"competencyId":"w2-skull-markings"},{"id":"N-m2-skull-410","dok":1,"q":"What is the midline bump felt at the back of the head called?","a":"The external occipital protuberance.","options":["The mastoid process","The external occipital protuberance","The parietal eminence","The occipital condyle"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The external occipital protuberance is a midline bump on the outer surface of the occipital bone. You can feel it at the back of the head, at about the level where the head meets the neck. It is an attachment point for a ligament and muscles of the back of the neck.","notes":["The mastoid process is a paired bump behind each ear, on the temporal bone, not in the midline. It would be the answer for a question about the bump behind the earlobe.","Correct. The midline bump at the back of the head is the external occipital protuberance.","The parietal eminence is the rounded high point on each parietal bone, at the widest part of the skull. Choose it for a question about the sides of the skull roof.","The occipital condyles are on the underside of the skull beside the foramen magnum and cannot be felt from the outside. Pick them for a question about the joint with the atlas."],"comp":34,"competencyId":"w2-skull-markings"},{"id":"N-m2-skull-411","dok":2,"q":"Which process of the mandible has a head that fits into the mandibular fossa of the temporal bone?","a":"The condylar process.","options":["The coronoid process","The alveolar process","The condylar process","The mastoid process"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The condylar process is the posterior process at the top of the mandibular ramus. Its rounded head fits into the mandibular fossa of the temporal bone, forming the temporomandibular joint. The mandibular notch separates it from the coronoid process in front.","notes":["The coronoid process is the anterior process of the ramus, where the temporalis muscle attaches. It would be the answer for a question about that muscle attachment.","The alveolar process is the ridge that holds the lower teeth. Pick it for a question about the tooth sockets.","Correct. The head of the condylar process sits in the mandibular fossa.","The mastoid process belongs to the temporal bone, behind the ear, and is not part of the mandible. Choose it for a question about the bump behind the ear."],"comp":34,"competencyId":"w2-skull-markings"},{"id":"N-m2-skull-412","dok":1,"q":"Which opening of the temporal bone is the entrance to the ear canal on the outside of the skull?","a":"The external acoustic meatus.","options":["The external acoustic meatus","The carotid canal","The stylomastoid foramen","The internal acoustic meatus"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The external acoustic meatus is the opening of the ear canal on the outer side of the temporal bone. Sound travels through it toward the eardrum and middle ear. Meatus means passage, and external tells you it opens on the outside of the skull.","notes":["Correct. The external acoustic meatus is the opening of the ear canal.","The carotid canal carries the internal carotid artery into the skull. Choose it for a question about the main artery to the brain.","The stylomastoid foramen is on the underside of the skull, between the styloid and mastoid processes, and is where the facial nerve exits. Pick it for a question about that exit.","The internal acoustic meatus opens on the inner side of the temporal bone, facing the brain, and carries the nerves for hearing and facial movement. It would be the answer for a question about where the facial nerve enters the temporal bone."],"comp":37,"competencyId":"w2-skull-foramina"},{"id":"N-m2-skull-413","dok":1,"q":"The floor of the cranium, the cranial base, is shaped into how many cranial fossae?","a":"Three.","options":["Five","Two","Three","Four"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The cranial base is shaped into three cranial fossae, which step down from front to back like a set of shelves: anterior, middle, and posterior. Each fossa cradles a different part of the brain. The frontal bone and the lesser wings of the sphenoid help form the anterior fossa, and the occipital bone forms much of the posterior fossa.","notes":["Five is the number of zones in the growth plate, not the number of cranial fossae. Students sometimes carry a memorized number over from another list.","Two leaves out one level. Students who think only of front and back miss the middle cranial fossa, which holds the sella turcica and the foramen ovale.","Correct. There are three cranial fossae: anterior, middle, and posterior.","Four is the number of bones that meet at the pterion, and it is also the number of paranasal sinus groups. It would fit a question about either of those, not the cranial fossae."],"comp":33,"competencyId":"w2-skull-bones"},{"id":"N-a17d-33-1","dok":3,"q":"On a lateral skull, a student traces the bony bar of the zygomatic arch from the cheek back toward the ear. Which two bones does the arch join, and which group does each belong to?","a":"The zygomatic bone, a facial bone, and the temporal bone, a cranial bone.","options":["The zygomatic bone and the maxilla, both facial bones.","The temporal bone and the sphenoid, both cranial bones.","The zygomatic bone, a facial bone, and the temporal bone, a cranial bone.","The zygomatic bone, a cranial bone, and the temporal bone, a facial bone."],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The arch is formed where the zygomatic process of the temporal bone meets the zygomatic bone. The zygomatic bone is the cheekbone, one of the fourteen facial bones. The temporal bone houses the ear and is one of the eight cranial bones, so the arch bridges the two groups.","notes":["The maxilla meets the cheekbone through its own zygomatic process, but the arch running back toward the ear reaches the temporal bone.","The sphenoid forms the central wedge of the cranial base and part of the lateral wall. The arch runs to the cheekbone, a facial bone.","Correct. The zygomatic arch joins the zygomatic bone of the face to the temporal bone of the cranium.","The bones are right but the groups are swapped. The cheekbone is facial, and the temporal bone is cranial."],"comp":33,"competencyId":"w2-skull-bones"},{"id":"N-a17d-33-2","dok":3,"q":"A student sorts the skull and places the ethmoid with the facial bones because it forms part of the nasal cavity. Which correction is right?","a":"It is a cranial bone, one of the eight in the braincase.","options":["It is a facial bone, since it forms the lower nasal septum.","It is counted separately, like the hyoid bone.","It is a facial bone, since its conchae are facial bones.","It is a cranial bone, one of the eight in the braincase."],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Group membership follows the bone, not the space it borders. The ethmoid is the deepest cranial bone, lying between the orbits, and it counts among the eight cranial bones. Its superior and middle conchae and perpendicular plate help build the nasal cavity, but that does not make it facial.","notes":["The lower part of the nasal septum is the vomer, a facial bone. The ethmoid forms the upper part with its perpendicular plate.","The hyoid is the bone counted separately from the skull. The ethmoid is one of the eight cranial bones.","Only the inferior nasal conchae are separate facial bones. The superior and middle conchae are parts of the ethmoid.","Correct. The ethmoid is one of the eight cranial bones, even though it helps form the nasal cavity."],"comp":33,"competencyId":"w2-skull-bones"},{"id":"N-a17d-35-1","dok":1,"q":"Which suture joins a parietal bone to a temporal bone on the side of the skull?","a":"The squamous suture.","options":["The coronal suture.","The lambdoid suture.","The sagittal suture.","The squamous suture."],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The squamous suture runs along the lower side of the skull, where each parietal bone meets the temporal bone below it. There is one on each side of the head.","notes":["The coronal suture joins the frontal bone to the two parietal bones, across the top of the skull behind the forehead.","The lambdoid suture joins the parietal bones to the occipital bone at the back of the skull.","The sagittal suture joins the two parietal bones along the midline of the skull roof.","Correct. The squamous suture joins a parietal bone and a temporal bone, one on each side."],"comp":35,"competencyId":"w2-sutures-fontanelles"},{"id":"N-a17d-35-2","dok":3,"q":"On a fetal skull, a student finds a soft membrane gap at the spot where the coronal and sagittal sutures will later meet. Which fontanelle is it, and about when does it close?","a":"The anterior fontanelle, by about 18 to 24 months.","options":["The anterior fontanelle, by about 18 to 24 months.","The posterior fontanelle, by about 2 months.","The anterolateral fontanelle, by about 3 months.","The anterior fontanelle, by about 2 months."],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Work from the sutures to the bones. The coronal suture joins the frontal bone to the parietals, and the sagittal suture joins the two parietals, so they meet where the frontal and parietal bones come together. That is the anterior fontanelle, the largest one, which closes at about 18 to 24 months.","notes":["Correct. The coronal and sagittal sutures meet where the frontal and parietal bones meet, the site of the anterior fontanelle.","The posterior fontanelle lies where the parietal bones meet the occipital bone, at the back, where the sagittal suture meets the lambdoid suture.","The anterolateral, or sphenoidal, fontanelles are on the side of the skull, one on each side, not at the top.","The site is right, but about 2 months is when the posterior fontanelle closes. The anterior one stays open until about 18 to 24 months."],"comp":35,"competencyId":"w2-sutures-fontanelles"},{"id":"N-a17d-36-1","dok":1,"q":"The ethmoidal sinuses, the ethmoid air cells, lie within which bone?","a":"The ethmoid bone.","options":["The sphenoid bone.","The frontal bone.","The ethmoid bone.","The maxilla."],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Each paranasal sinus is named for the bone it occupies. The ethmoidal sinuses are the ethmoid air cells, held in the lateral masses of the ethmoid between the orbits.","notes":["The sphenoid holds the sphenoidal sinus, within its body. The ethmoid air cells are in the ethmoid.","The frontal bone holds the frontal sinus, above the orbits. The ethmoidal sinuses lie between the orbits.","Correct. The ethmoidal sinuses are the air cells of the ethmoid, between the orbits.","The maxillae hold the maxillary sinuses, the largest sinuses, in the cheek region."],"comp":36,"competencyId":"w2-skull-cavities"},{"id":"N-a17d-36-2","dok":1,"q":"The nasal septum is built from the vomer, the perpendicular plate of the ethmoid, and which other structure?","a":"Septal cartilage.","options":["Septal cartilage.","The inferior nasal conchae.","The palatine bones.","The cribriform plate."],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The nasal septum divides the nasal cavity into two sides. Its bony part is the perpendicular plate of the ethmoid above and the vomer below, and septal cartilage completes it.","notes":["Correct. The nasal septum is the vomer plus the perpendicular plate of the ethmoid plus septal cartilage.","The inferior nasal conchae are scroll-shaped bones on the lateral wall of the nasal cavity, not part of the midline septum.","The palatine bones form the posterior hard palate and part of the orbit. They do not form the septum.","The cribriform plate is the perforated horizontal plate of the ethmoid that the olfactory filaments pass through. The septum is built from the vertical perpendicular plate instead."],"comp":36,"competencyId":"w2-skull-cavities"},{"id":"N-a17d-36-3","dok":3,"q":"A patient fractures the back part of the bony roof of the mouth. Which bone is broken, and which group of skull bones does it belong to?","a":"A palatine bone, a facial bone.","options":["A palatine bone, a facial bone.","A maxilla, a facial bone.","The sphenoid, a cranial bone.","The vomer, a facial bone."],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The hard palate is the bony roof of the mouth, with the maxillae in front and the palatine bones behind. A break at the back part therefore involves a palatine bone. The palatine bones are paired facial bones.","notes":["Correct. The palatine bones form the posterior hard palate and are facial bones.","The maxillae form the front of the hard palate through their palatine processes. The back part is the palatine bones.","The sphenoid is the central wedge of the cranial base and does not form the roof of the mouth.","The vomer forms the lower part of the nasal septum, not the hard palate."],"comp":36,"competencyId":"w2-skull-cavities"},{"id":"N-a17d-36-4","dok":3,"q":"Moving backward along the medial wall of the orbit, a probe slides from a small bone grooved for the tear duct onto a deeper bone between the orbits that holds air cells. Which bones are these, and which sinus does the second one hold?","a":"The lacrimal bone, then the ethmoid with its ethmoidal sinuses.","options":["The lacrimal bone, then the sphenoid with its sphenoidal sinus.","The nasal bone, then the ethmoid with its ethmoidal sinuses.","The lacrimal bone, then the maxilla with its maxillary sinus.","The lacrimal bone, then the ethmoid with its ethmoidal sinuses."],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Identify each bone, then its sinus. The small bone of the medial orbital wall grooved for the tear duct is the lacrimal bone. The deeper bone between the orbits is the ethmoid, the deepest cranial bone, and its air cells are the ethmoidal sinuses.","notes":["The sphenoid helps form the orbit, but its sinus is in its body in the cranial base. The bone between the orbits with air cells is the ethmoid.","The nasal bones form the bridge of the nose. The orbital bone grooved for the tear duct is the lacrimal bone.","The maxillary sinus lies in the cheek region, below the orbit. The bone between the orbits with air cells is the ethmoid.","Correct. The lacrimal bone is grooved for the tear duct, and the ethmoid between the orbits holds the ethmoidal sinuses."],"comp":36,"competencyId":"w2-skull-cavities"},{"id":"N-a17d-37-1","dok":1,"q":"Which openings in the ethmoid bone pass the olfactory nerve filaments, cranial nerve I?","a":"The cribriform foramina.","options":["The optic canal.","The foramen ovale.","The cribriform foramina.","The superior orbital fissure."],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The cribriform plate is the perforated horizontal plate of the ethmoid, and its many small openings are the cribriform foramina. The filaments of the olfactory nerve, cranial nerve I, pass through them.","notes":["The optic canal is in the sphenoid and passes the optic nerve, cranial nerve II.","The foramen ovale is in the sphenoid and passes a branch of the trigeminal nerve to the lower jaw.","Correct. The olfactory filaments pass through the cribriform foramina of the ethmoid.","The superior orbital fissure is in the sphenoid and passes nerves that supply the eye, including those that move it."],"comp":37,"competencyId":"w2-skull-foramina"},{"id":"N-a17d-37-2","dok":3,"q":"Inside the skull, a student finds two openings in the same bone: a canal carrying cranial nerve II and, beside it, a slit carrying the nerves that move the eye. Which bone holds them, and what are the openings?","a":"The sphenoid, with the optic canal and superior orbital fissure.","options":["The ethmoid, with the cribriform foramina and optic canal.","The sphenoid, with the foramen ovale and jugular foramen.","The temporal, with the internal acoustic meatus and carotid canal.","The sphenoid, with the optic canal and superior orbital fissure."],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Name each opening from what it carries, then find the bone. Cranial nerve II is the optic nerve, which runs through the optic canal. The nerves that move the eye pass through the superior orbital fissure, and both openings are in the sphenoid.","notes":["The cribriform foramina in the ethmoid carry the olfactory filaments, cranial nerve I. The optic canal is in the sphenoid.","The foramen ovale is in the sphenoid but carries a trigeminal branch, and the jugular foramen lies between the temporal and occipital bones.","These temporal bone openings carry the hearing and facial nerves and the internal carotid artery, not the nerves of the eye.","Correct. The optic canal and the superior orbital fissure are both in the sphenoid."],"comp":37,"competencyId":"w2-skull-foramina"},{"id":"N-a17d-37-3","dok":3,"q":"A skull fracture runs through the temporal bone. Which two openings of that bone could be damaged, and what passes through each?","a":"Internal acoustic meatus (hearing, facial nerves); carotid canal (internal carotid).","options":["Internal acoustic meatus (hearing, facial nerves); carotid canal (internal carotid).","Foramen ovale (trigeminal branch); optic canal (optic nerve).","Internal acoustic meatus (internal carotid); carotid canal (hearing nerves).","Foramen magnum (spinal cord); hypoglossal canal (nerve XII)."],"correctIndex":0,"why":"First list the openings that pierce the temporal bone, then recall their contents. The internal acoustic meatus carries the nerves for hearing and for facial movement, and the carotid canal carries the internal carotid artery. A fracture through the temporal bone puts both at risk.","notes":["Correct. The temporal bone holds the internal acoustic meatus and the carotid canal.","Both of these openings are in the sphenoid, not the temporal bone.","The openings are right but the contents are swapped. The carotid canal carries the internal carotid artery.","Both of these are in the occipital bone. The foramen magnum passes the spinal cord, and the hypoglossal canal passes cranial nerve XII."],"comp":37,"competencyId":"w2-skull-foramina"}]},{"id":"m2-spine-thorax","title":"Vertebral column and thoracic cage","summary":"vertebral regions, curvatures, vertebra parts, atlas and axis, sacrum, coccyx, sternum, ribs","mw":"2026-09-21","tr":"2026-09-17","cards":[{"id":"L00579","dok":1,"q":"How many vertebrae are in the cervical region of the vertebral column?","a":"Seven, C1 to C7.","options":["Twelve","Twenty-four","Five","Seven"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The cervical region is the neck, and it has seven vertebrae, numbered C1 through C7 from the skull downward. These are the smallest vertebrae in the column because they carry only the weight of the head. A quick way to keep the counts straight is to think of the day pattern: breakfast at 7, lunch at 12, dinner at 5, for cervical, thoracic, and lumbar.","notes":["Twelve is the number of thoracic vertebrae, one for each pair of ribs. Pick twelve if the question asks about the chest region of the spine.","Twenty-four is the total number of separate, unfused vertebrae in the cervical, thoracic, and lumbar regions together. It would be right for a question about all the movable vertebrae, not just the neck.","Five is the number of lumbar vertebrae in the lower back, L1 to L5. Choose five if the question asks about the lumbar region or the number of fused sacral vertebrae.","Correct. The neck holds seven cervical vertebrae, C1 through C7."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-spine","lid":"c1","comp":38,"competencyId":"w2-spine-regions"},{"id":"L00580","dok":1,"q":"How many vertebrae are in the thoracic region of the vertebral column?","a":"Twelve, T1 to T12.","options":["Four","Seven","Five","Twelve"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The thoracic region is the chest portion of the spine, and it has twelve vertebrae, T1 through T12. The count matches the twelve pairs of ribs, because each thoracic vertebra articulates with a pair of ribs. Linking the vertebrae to the ribs makes the number easy to recall.","notes":["Four is the usual number of small vertebrae that fuse to form the coccyx, the tailbone. Pick four if the question were about the coccyx.","Seven is the number of cervical vertebrae in the neck. It would be the answer if the question asked about the cervical region.","Five is the number of lumbar vertebrae and also the number of vertebrae that fuse to form the sacrum. Choose five for either of those questions.","Correct. There are twelve thoracic vertebrae, matching the twelve pairs of ribs."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-spine","lid":"c2","comp":38,"competencyId":"w2-spine-regions"},{"id":"L00581","dok":1,"q":"How many vertebrae are in the lumbar region of the vertebral column?","a":"Five, L1 to L5.","options":["Seven","Five","Three","Twelve"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The lumbar region is the lower back, and it has five vertebrae, L1 through L5. These are the largest separate vertebrae because they carry the weight of everything above them. Below L5 the column continues as the sacrum.","notes":["Seven is the cervical count, the vertebrae of the neck. It would be right for a question about the cervical region.","Correct. The lower back has five lumbar vertebrae, L1 through L5.","Three does not match any region of the vertebral column. Students sometimes pick it by mixing up the vertebra count with the three bones of the sternum or the three phalanges in a finger.","Twelve is the thoracic count, one vertebra for each pair of ribs. Choose twelve if the question asked about the chest region of the spine."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-spine","lid":"c3","comp":38,"competencyId":"w2-spine-regions"},{"id":"L00582","dok":1,"q":"How many separate, unfused vertebrae are in the adult vertebral column, not counting the sacrum and coccyx?","a":"Twenty-four.","options":["Twenty-six","Thirty-three","Twelve","Twenty-four"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The separate vertebrae are the ones in the three movable regions: seven cervical, twelve thoracic, and five lumbar, which adds up to twenty-four. The sacrum and coccyx are each made of several vertebrae fused into a single bone, so they are counted separately. Adding those two fused bones gives the adult total of twenty-six bones.","notes":["Twenty-six is the total number of bones in the adult column when the sacrum and coccyx are each counted as one bone. Pick it if the question asks how many bones make up the whole adult column.","Thirty-three is the number of vertebrae present early in development, before the sacral and coccygeal vertebrae fuse. Choose it for a question about the developing column.","Twelve is only the thoracic count. Students pick it when they count one region instead of adding all three movable regions together.","Correct. Seven cervical plus twelve thoracic plus five lumbar gives twenty-four separate vertebrae."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-spine","lid":"c4","comp":38,"competencyId":"w2-spine-regions"},{"id":"L00583","dok":1,"q":"Which bone is formed by five fused vertebrae and is wedged between the two hip bones?","a":"The sacrum.","options":["The axis","The atlas","The coccyx","The sacrum"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The sacrum is a single triangular bone made of five vertebrae that fuse in the adult. It sits wedged between the two hip bones, forming the back wall of the pelvis. Its job is to pass the weight of the upper body into the pelvic girdle and down to the legs.","notes":["The axis is C2, the second cervical vertebra, which carries the dens. It would be right for a question about the vertebra the head rotates on.","The atlas is C1, the first cervical vertebra, a ring that holds up the skull. Pick it for a question about the vertebra that supports the head.","The coccyx is the tailbone, a small bone of usually four fused vertebrae just below the sacrum. Choose it if the question describes the tailbone or the lowest part of the column.","Correct. The sacrum is five fused vertebrae wedged between the hip bones."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-spine","lid":"c5","comp":40,"competencyId":"w2-regional-vertebrae"},{"id":"L00584","dok":1,"q":"What is the anatomical name for the tailbone?","a":"The coccyx.","options":["The coccyx","The vertebra prominens","The sacral promontory","The sacrum"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The coccyx is the tailbone, a small bone formed by several tiny vertebrae (usually four) fused together at the very bottom of the vertebral column. It sits just below the sacrum. It serves as an attachment point for pelvic ligaments and muscles.","notes":["Correct. The coccyx is the fused tailbone at the bottom of the column.","The vertebra prominens is C7, the vertebra with the long spinous process you can feel at the base of the neck. It belongs at the top of the column, not the bottom.","The sacral promontory is the anterior ridge of the first sacral vertebra. Choose it if the question asks about a landmark on the front of the sacrum.","The sacrum is the larger triangular bone just above the coccyx, wedged between the hip bones. Pick it if the question asks about five fused vertebrae or the back wall of the pelvis."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-spine","lid":"c6","comp":40,"competencyId":"w2-regional-vertebrae"},{"id":"L00585","dok":1,"q":"What is the thick, weight-bearing anterior portion of a vertebra called?","a":"The body.","options":["The spinous process","The body","The pedicle","The vertebral arch"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The body, also called the centrum, is the thick, drum-shaped part at the front of a vertebra. Stacked bodies carry the weight of the trunk, and intervertebral discs sit between them as cushions. Everything else on a vertebra, the arch and its processes, projects from the back of the body.","notes":["The spinous process is the single projection pointing backward from the arch, the bump you feel along your back. It is posterior, not the weight-bearing front.","Correct. The body is the thick anterior portion that bears weight.","The pedicles are the two short bars that connect the arch to the body. Pick them for a question about what joins the arch to the body.","The vertebral arch is the bony ring behind the body that surrounds the spinal cord. Choose it if the question asks what encloses the vertebral foramen from behind."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-spine","lid":"c7","comp":39,"competencyId":"w2-typical-vertebra"},{"id":"L00586","dok":1,"q":"Which single projection points backward from the vertebral arch?","a":"The spinous process.","options":["The transverse process","The pedicle","The spinous process","The articular process"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The spinous process is the one projection that extends straight back from the vertebral arch in the midline. The row of spinous processes forms the bumps you can feel down the middle of your back. Muscles and ligaments attach to them.","notes":["The transverse processes are a pair that point laterally, to the left and right, from the arch. Choose them if the question asks about the side projections.","The pedicles are short bars connecting the arch to the body, not projections that stick out backward. Choose them for a question about what links the arch to the body.","Correct. The spinous process is the single backward-pointing projection.","The articular processes are the superior and inferior pairs that join one vertebra to the next. Pick them for a question about how neighboring vertebrae connect."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-spine","lid":"c8","comp":39,"competencyId":"w2-typical-vertebra"},{"id":"L00587","dok":1,"q":"What is the opening enclosed by the body and arch of a single vertebra called?","a":"The vertebral foramen.","options":["The vertebral canal","The vertebral foramen","The intervertebral foramen","The transverse foramen"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The vertebral foramen is the large hole in each vertebra, bounded by the body in front and the arch behind. The spinal cord passes through it. The word foramen simply means a hole for something to pass through.","notes":["The vertebral canal is the long channel made when many vertebral foramina line up. Choose it if the question describes the whole stacked channel rather than one opening.","Correct. The vertebral foramen is the hole in one vertebra that the spinal cord passes through.","The intervertebral foramen is a gap between two stacked vertebrae, not a hole in a single bone. Pick it if the question asks where spinal nerves exit the column.","The transverse foramen is a small hole in each transverse process of a cervical vertebra. It would be the answer for a question about where the vertebral arteries pass."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-spine","lid":"c9","comp":39,"competencyId":"w2-typical-vertebra"},{"id":"L00588","dok":1,"q":"Which vertebra is the atlas?","a":"C1.","options":["C7","C2","C1","T1"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The atlas is C1, the first cervical vertebra, sitting directly under the skull. It is named after the figure from Greek myth who held up the world, because it holds up the head. It is a ring with no body and no spinous process.","notes":["C7 is the vertebra prominens, with the long spinous process at the base of the neck. Pick it for a question about that palpable landmark.","C2 is the axis, the vertebra with the upward peg called the dens. Choose C2 if the question asks about the axis.","Correct. The atlas is C1, the first cervical vertebra.","T1 is the first thoracic vertebra, the first one to carry a pair of ribs. It would be right for a question about where the thoracic region begins."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-spine","lid":"c10","comp":40,"competencyId":"w2-regional-vertebrae"},{"id":"L00589","dok":1,"q":"Which vertebra is the axis?","a":"C2.","options":["C5","C2","C7","C1"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The axis is C2, the second cervical vertebra. Its defining feature is the dens, a peg that projects upward into the ring of the atlas. The atlas and skull turn around this peg when you shake your head no, which is why C2 is called the axis.","notes":["C5 is an ordinary, typical cervical vertebra with no special name. Students pick it when they guess a middle number rather than recalling that the named vertebrae are C1, C2, and C7.","Correct. The axis is C2, the vertebra that bears the dens.","C7 is the vertebra prominens, with its long spinous process felt at the base of the neck. Pick it for a question about that landmark.","C1 is the atlas, the ring with no body that holds up the skull. Choose C1 if the question asks about the atlas."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-spine","lid":"c11","comp":40,"competencyId":"w2-regional-vertebrae"},{"id":"L00590","dok":1,"q":"What is the upward bony peg on the axis called?","a":"The dens.","options":["The spinous process","The dens","The sacral promontory","The transverse process"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The dens, also called the odontoid process, is a tooth-like peg that rises from the body of the axis (C2). It fits into the front of the atlas ring and is held there by a ligament. The atlas pivots around the dens when the head turns side to side.","notes":["The spinous process points backward from the vertebral arch, not upward. Choose it if the question asks about the midline projection you feel along the back.","Correct. The dens is the upward peg of the axis.","The sacral promontory is a ridge on the front of the first sacral vertebra, far from the neck. It would be right for a question about the anterior edge of the sacrum.","The transverse processes point out to the sides of a vertebra. Pick them for a question about the lateral projections."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-spine","lid":"c12","comp":40,"competencyId":"w2-regional-vertebrae"},{"id":"L00591","dok":1,"q":"Which type of cartilage forms an intervertebral disc?","a":"Fibrocartilage.","options":["Elastic cartilage","Articular cartilage","Hyaline cartilage","Fibrocartilage"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Intervertebral discs are made of fibrocartilage, the toughest cartilage, packed with thick collagen fibers. Fibrocartilage resists being squeezed and pulled, which suits a pad that cushions the weight of the trunk between vertebral bodies. The same tissue forms the pubic symphysis and the knee menisci.","notes":["Elastic cartilage is the flexible cartilage of the external ear and epiglottis. Pick it for a question about a structure that bends and springs back into shape.","Articular cartilage is hyaline cartilage covering the ends of bones in synovial joints. It would be right for a question about the smooth cap on a joint surface.","Hyaline cartilage is the smooth, glassy cartilage of costal cartilages, the airways, and joint surfaces. Choose it if the question asks what connects the ribs to the sternum.","Correct. The discs are fibrocartilage, built to withstand compression."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-spine","lid":"c13","comp":39,"competencyId":"w2-typical-vertebra"},{"id":"L00592","dok":1,"q":"What is the tough outer ring of an intervertebral disc called?","a":"The anulus fibrosus.","options":["The vertebral arch","The lamina","The nucleus pulposus","The anulus fibrosus"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The anulus fibrosus is the tough ring of fibrocartilage that forms the outer part of each disc. Anulus means ring and fibrosus means fibrous. It holds the soft center in place and resists tearing when the disc is loaded.","notes":["The vertebral arch is the bony ring behind the vertebral body. Students confuse it with the disc ring because both are described as rings, but the arch is bone that surrounds the spinal cord.","A lamina is one of the two flat bony plates that complete the vertebral arch behind. It is part of the vertebra, not the disc, so pick it only for a question about the arch.","The nucleus pulposus is the soft, gel-like core inside the ring. Choose it if the question asks about the center of the disc.","Correct. The anulus fibrosus is the fibrous outer ring of the disc."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-spine","lid":"c14","comp":39,"competencyId":"w2-typical-vertebra"},{"id":"L00593","dok":1,"q":"What is the soft, gel-like core of an intervertebral disc called?","a":"The nucleus pulposus.","options":["The dens","The nucleus pulposus","The anulus fibrosus","The centrum"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The nucleus pulposus is the soft, gel-like center of each intervertebral disc. Pulposus means pulpy. When the spine is loaded, this core spreads the pressure out evenly, and if the outer ring tears, it is the part that bulges out in a herniated disc.","notes":["The dens is the upward peg of the axis (C2). It would be right for a question about the pivot for turning the head.","Correct. The nucleus pulposus is the gel-like core of the disc.","The anulus fibrosus is the tough outer ring that surrounds the core. Pick it if the question asks about the fibrous ring of the disc.","The centrum is another name for the vertebral body, the bone the disc sits between. Choose it for a question about the weight-bearing part of a vertebra."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-spine","lid":"c15","comp":39,"competencyId":"w2-typical-vertebra"},{"id":"L00594","dok":1,"q":"Which part of the sternum is the superior portion that articulates with the clavicles?","a":"The manubrium.","options":["The manubrium","The body","The jugular notch","The xiphoid process"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The sternum has three fused parts, and the manubrium is the top one. It articulates with the clavicles at the sternoclavicular joints and with the first pair of ribs. Its shape is often compared to the handle of a sword, with the body as the blade.","notes":["Correct. The manubrium is the superior part and joins the clavicles.","The body is the long middle part of the sternum, where most of the true ribs attach. Choose it if the question asks about the largest, central section.","The jugular notch is a dip at the top of the manubrium, a landmark rather than a separate part. Choose it if the question asks about the notch you can feel at the base of the neck.","The xiphoid process is the small inferior tip of the sternum. Pick it for a question about the bottom of the breastbone."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-spine","lid":"c16","comp":41,"competencyId":"w2-thoracic-cage"},{"id":"L00595","dok":2,"q":"Which two spinal curvatures are primary curvatures, present from birth?","a":"The thoracic and sacral curvatures.","options":["The cervical and thoracic curvatures","The lumbar and sacral curvatures","The thoracic and sacral curvatures","The cervical and lumbar curvatures"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"A newborn's spine is curved in one long C shape, like its position in the uterus. The thoracic and sacral curvatures keep that original direction, so they are the primary curvatures. The cervical and lumbar curvatures form later as the baby lifts its head and then stands, so they are secondary.","notes":["This pair mixes a secondary curve (cervical) with a primary curve (thoracic). It would only fit if both curves had the same origin, which they do not.","This pair mixes the secondary lumbar curve with the primary sacral curve. Students pick it because both are low in the back, but location does not decide primary or secondary.","Correct. The thoracic and sacral curvatures are present at birth.","The cervical and lumbar curvatures are the two secondary curves that develop after birth. Choose this pair if the question asks which curves develop with infant milestones."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-spine","lid":"c17","comp":38,"competencyId":"w2-spine-regions"},{"id":"L00596","dok":2,"q":"Which spinal curvature develops as an infant begins to hold up its head?","a":"The cervical curvature.","options":["The cervical curvature","The sacral curvature","The thoracic curvature","The lumbar curvature"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The cervical curvature is a secondary curve that forms in the neck when an infant starts lifting and holding up its head. Holding the head up against gravity bends the neck region the opposite way from the fetal C shape. The other secondary curve, the lumbar, comes later with standing and walking.","notes":["Correct. The cervical curvature forms as the infant holds up its head.","The sacral curvature is a primary curve present from birth. It would be right for a question about the original curve in the pelvic region.","The thoracic curvature is a primary curve, present at birth. Choose it if the question asks about a curve the baby is born with in the chest region.","The lumbar curvature is also secondary, but it develops later, when a child begins to stand and walk. Pick it for a question about that milestone."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-spine","lid":"c18","comp":38,"competencyId":"w2-spine-regions"},{"id":"L00597","dok":2,"q":"Which spinal curvature is secondary and develops as a child stands and walks?","a":"The lumbar curvature.","options":["The sacral curvature","The lumbar curvature","The thoracic curvature","The cervical curvature"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The lumbar curvature forms in the lower back once a child begins standing and walking. Standing upright bends the lumbar spine opposite to the fetal C shape, which makes it a secondary curvature. It balances the trunk over the hips and legs.","notes":["The sacral curvature is primary and present at birth. Pick it for a question about the curve in the pelvic region that the baby already has.","Correct. The lumbar curvature develops with standing and walking.","The thoracic curvature is primary and present at birth. Choose it if the question asks about the original curve in the chest region.","The cervical curvature is also secondary, but it develops earlier, when the infant holds up its head. Choose it if the question names that earlier milestone."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-spine","lid":"c19","comp":38,"competencyId":"w2-spine-regions"},{"id":"L00598","dok":2,"q":"Which two parts of a vertebra connect the vertebral arch to the body?","a":"The pedicles.","options":["The transverse processes","The pedicles","The articular processes","The laminae"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The pedicles are two short, thick bars that project backward from the vertebral body and form the sides of the arch. Pedicle means little foot, since they are the feet the arch stands on. Notches above and below each pedicle help form the intervertebral foramina where spinal nerves exit.","notes":["The transverse processes project to the sides from where the pedicles and laminae meet. Pick them for a question about the lateral projections.","Correct. The pedicles link the arch to the body.","The articular processes are the superior and inferior pairs that join one vertebra to the next. Choose them for a question about connections between neighboring vertebrae.","The laminae are the two flat plates that complete the arch behind, meeting in the midline at the spinous process. Choose them if the question asks what closes the back of the arch."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-spine","lid":"c20","comp":39,"competencyId":"w2-typical-vertebra"},{"id":"L00599","dok":2,"q":"Which two flat plates complete the vertebral arch behind?","a":"The laminae.","options":["The pedicles","The bodies","The laminae","The spinous processes"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The laminae are two flat plates of bone that extend from the pedicles toward the midline, where they meet to close the back of the vertebral arch. The spinous process projects from the spot where they join. A laminectomy, removal of the laminae, is a surgery that opens the arch to relieve pressure on the spinal cord.","notes":["The pedicles are the short bars that connect the arch to the body at the front of the arch. Choose them if the question asks what links the arch to the body.","The body is the thick weight-bearing part in front of the arch. Choose it for a question about the anterior part of a vertebra.","Correct. The laminae are the flat plates that close the arch behind.","The spinous process is a single projection, not a pair of plates, and it sticks out from where the laminae meet. Pick it for a question about the backward-pointing projection."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-spine","lid":"c21","comp":39,"competencyId":"w2-typical-vertebra"},{"id":"L00600","dok":2,"q":"Through which openings do the spinal nerves exit the vertebral column?","a":"The intervertebral foramina.","options":["The intervertebral foramina","The transverse foramina","The sacral hiatus","The vertebral foramina"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The intervertebral foramina are openings on each side between two stacked vertebrae, formed by notches above and below the pedicles. A spinal nerve leaves the vertebral canal through each one. That is why a bulging disc or bone spur near this opening can press on a nerve and send pain down a limb.","notes":["Correct. Spinal nerves exit through the intervertebral foramina between vertebrae.","The transverse foramina are small holes in the transverse processes of cervical vertebrae. Pick them for a question about where the vertebral arteries pass.","The sacral hiatus is one opening at the lower end of the sacral canal on the back of the sacrum. It would be right for a question about where the sacral canal ends.","The vertebral foramina are the large central holes that stack to form the vertebral canal, which holds the spinal cord itself. Choose them if the question asks where the cord runs."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-spine","lid":"c22","comp":39,"competencyId":"w2-typical-vertebra"},{"id":"L00601","dok":2,"q":"Which vertebral region has transverse foramina in its transverse processes?","a":"The cervical region.","options":["The thoracic region","The cervical region","The lumbar region","The sacral region"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Only cervical vertebrae have transverse foramina, small holes through each transverse process. The vertebral arteries travel up through these holes on their way to the brain. Because no other region has them, a transverse foramen is enough by itself to identify a cervical vertebra.","notes":["Thoracic vertebrae are identified by costal facets where the ribs articulate, not transverse foramina. Choose the thoracic region if the question names costal facets.","Correct. Transverse foramina are found only in cervical vertebrae.","Lumbar vertebrae have neither transverse foramina nor costal facets. Pick the lumbar region for a question about massive, kidney-shaped bodies built for weight bearing.","The sacrum is a fused bone with sacral foramina that pass the sacral nerves. Students confuse these with transverse foramina, but sacral foramina are a different feature on a different bone."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-spine","lid":"c23","comp":40,"competencyId":"w2-regional-vertebrae"},{"id":"L00602","dok":2,"q":"Which vertebral region has costal facets where the ribs articulate?","a":"The thoracic region.","options":["The coccygeal region","The cervical region","The thoracic region","The lumbar region"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Costal facets are small smooth joint surfaces on thoracic vertebrae, on the bodies and on the transverse processes, where the ribs meet the spine. Costal means rib. Since ribs attach only in the chest, costal facets identify a thoracic vertebra.","notes":["The coccyx is the small fused tailbone and does not articulate with ribs. It would be right for a question about the lowest fused segment of the column.","Cervical vertebrae have transverse foramina for the vertebral arteries but no rib joints. Choose the cervical region if the question names transverse foramina or a bifid spinous process.","Correct. Costal facets for the ribs are found on thoracic vertebrae.","Lumbar vertebrae have no costal facets and no transverse foramina. Pick the lumbar region for a question about the largest, kidney-shaped bodies."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-spine","lid":"c24","comp":40,"competencyId":"w2-regional-vertebrae"},{"id":"L00603","dok":2,"q":"Which vertebrae are the largest, with thick, kidney-shaped bodies built for weight bearing?","a":"The lumbar vertebrae.","options":["The lumbar vertebrae","The coccygeal vertebrae","The cervical vertebrae","The thoracic vertebrae"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The lumbar vertebrae in the lower back are the largest separate vertebrae, with massive, kidney-shaped bodies. They support the weight of the head, arms, and trunk above them, so they need the biggest weight-bearing surfaces. Body size increases steadily from the neck down to the lumbar region.","notes":["Correct. Lumbar vertebrae are the largest, with kidney-shaped bodies.","The coccygeal vertebrae are tiny and fused into the tailbone. They would fit a question about the smallest, lowest segments of the column.","Cervical vertebrae are the smallest, with small oval bodies, because they carry only the head. Choose them for a question about the smallest vertebrae or transverse foramina.","Thoracic vertebrae have medium, heart-shaped bodies and costal facets for the ribs. Pick them for a question that names a heart-shaped body."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-spine","lid":"c25","comp":40,"competencyId":"w2-regional-vertebrae"},{"id":"L00604","dok":2,"q":"The atlas is unlike a typical vertebra. Which two features does the atlas lack?","a":"A body and a spinous process.","options":["Transverse processes and transverse foramina","Anterior and posterior arches","A body and a spinous process","A vertebral foramen and articular facets"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The atlas (C1) is a ring of bone made of an anterior arch and a posterior arch, with no body and no true spinous process. The missing body leaves room for the dens of the axis to sit in the front of the ring. Like other cervical vertebrae, the atlas still has transverse processes with transverse foramina.","notes":["The atlas does have transverse processes, and each has a transverse foramen like every cervical vertebra. Thoracic and lumbar vertebrae are the ones that lack transverse foramina, though they still have transverse processes.","The anterior and posterior arches are exactly what the atlas is made of. They replace the body and form its ring shape, so the atlas clearly has them.","Correct. The atlas has no body and no spinous process.","The atlas has a large vertebral foramen for the spinal cord and articular facets for the skull and the axis. Students pick this when they assume an unusual vertebra lacks everything."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-spine","lid":"c26","comp":40,"competencyId":"w2-regional-vertebrae"},{"id":"L00605","dok":2,"q":"Which vertebra is the vertebra prominens, with a long spinous process you can feel at the base of the neck?","a":"C7.","options":["C5","C2","C1","C7"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"C7 is the last cervical vertebra, and its spinous process is long and not forked, so it sticks out at the base of the neck. Prominens means prominent. Because it is easy to feel, clinicians use it as a starting point for counting vertebrae.","notes":["C5 is a typical cervical vertebra with a short, often forked spinous process that is hard to feel. Students pick it when they guess a middle neck vertebra instead of the lowest one.","C2 is the axis, which carries the dens. Pick C2 for a question about the peg the head turns on.","C1 is the atlas, which has no spinous process at all. Choose C1 for a question about the ring that holds up the skull.","Correct. C7 is the vertebra prominens."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-spine","lid":"c27","comp":40,"competencyId":"w2-regional-vertebrae"},{"id":"L00606","dok":2,"q":"Which ridge marks where the manubrium meets the body of the sternum?","a":"The sternal angle.","options":["The xiphoid process","The sternal angle","The costal cartilage","The jugular notch"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The sternal angle is a slight horizontal ridge where the manubrium joins the body of the sternum. It lies at the level of the second rib, so it is a reliable landmark you can feel through the skin. Clinicians find it first and then count ribs downward from there.","notes":["The xiphoid process is the small tip at the bottom of the sternum. Pick it for a question about the inferior end of the breastbone.","Correct. The sternal angle is the ridge at the manubrium and body junction.","Costal cartilage is the hyaline cartilage that connects a rib toward the sternum. It would be right for a question about what links the ribs to the sternum, not a sternal ridge.","The jugular notch is the central dip at the top of the manubrium, at the base of the neck. Choose it if the question asks about the notch above the sternum."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-spine","lid":"c28","comp":41,"competencyId":"w2-thoracic-cage"},{"id":"L00607","dok":2,"q":"Which rib pairs are the true ribs?","a":"Pairs 1 to 7.","options":["Pairs 8 to 10","Pairs 1 to 7","Pairs 11 and 12","Pairs 8 to 12"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The true ribs are pairs 1 through 7, and each one attaches directly to the sternum by its own costal cartilage. They are called true because they have a direct line to the sternum. The ribs below them are false ribs, which do not reach the sternum on their own.","notes":["Pairs 8 to 10 are the false ribs that join the costal cartilage of the rib above. They would be the answer for a question about ribs that reach the sternum only indirectly.","Correct. Pairs 1 to 7 attach directly to the sternum.","Pairs 11 and 12 are the floating ribs, with no anterior attachment at all. Pick them for a question about the ribs that end freely in the abdominal wall.","Pairs 8 to 12 are the false ribs, which do not reach the sternum directly. Choose them if the question asks about the false ribs."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-spine","lid":"c29","comp":41,"competencyId":"w2-thoracic-cage"},{"id":"L00608","dok":2,"q":"Which rib pairs are the floating ribs, with no anterior attachment at all?","a":"Pairs 11 and 12.","options":["Pairs 8 to 10","Pairs 1 and 2","Pairs 11 and 12","Pairs 1 to 7"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Pairs 11 and 12 are the floating ribs. They attach to the vertebrae in back but end freely in the muscles of the abdominal wall, with no cartilage connection to the sternum. They are counted as a subset of the false ribs, pairs 8 to 12.","notes":["Pairs 8 to 10 are false ribs that do have an anterior connection, through the costal cartilage of the rib above. Pick them for a question about indirect attachment.","Pairs 1 and 2 are true ribs at the top of the cage. Students pick them by confusing floating with the shortest or highest ribs, but the floating ribs are the lowest.","Correct. Pairs 11 and 12 have no anterior attachment.","Pairs 1 to 7 are the true ribs, which attach directly to the sternum. Choose them if the question asks about direct sternal attachment."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-spine","lid":"c30","comp":41,"competencyId":"w2-thoracic-cage"},{"id":"L00609","dok":1,"q":"What is the small inferior tip of the sternum called?","a":"The xiphoid process.","options":["The jugular notch","The sternal angle","The manubrium","The xiphoid process"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The xiphoid process is the small, pointed tip at the bottom of the sternum. It starts out as cartilage and slowly turns to bone with age. Several abdominal muscles attach to it, and its name comes from the Greek word for sword.","notes":["The jugular notch is the dip at the top of the manubrium, at the base of the neck. It would be right for a question about the notch at the superior edge of the sternum.","The sternal angle is the ridge where the manubrium meets the body, level with the second rib. Pick it for a question about the landmark used to count ribs.","The manubrium is the superior part of the sternum, the part that meets the clavicles. Choose it if the question asks about the top of the breastbone.","Correct. The xiphoid process is the inferior tip of the sternum."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-spine","lid":"c31","comp":41,"competencyId":"w2-thoracic-cage"},{"id":"L00610","dok":1,"q":"Which part of a rib is the posterior end that articulates with the vertebral bodies?","a":"The head.","options":["The neck","The costal cartilage","The head","The tubercle"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The head is the posterior end of a rib, and it fits against the bodies of the thoracic vertebrae at the costal facets. Just beyond the head is a short neck, and then the tubercle, which meets the transverse process. Together these two joints anchor each rib to the spine.","notes":["The neck is the short narrow section between the head and the tubercle. It does not form a joint itself, so pick it only for a question about the segment that connects those two parts.","Costal cartilage is the hyaline cartilage at the anterior end of a rib that connects it toward the sternum. Choose it for a question about the front end of the rib.","Correct. The head is the posterior end that meets the vertebral bodies.","The tubercle is the knob just past the neck of the rib, and it articulates with the transverse process of a vertebra, not the body. Choose it if the question asks about the rib's joint with the transverse process."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-spine","lid":"c32","comp":41,"competencyId":"w2-thoracic-cage"},{"id":"L00611","dok":2,"q":"The tubercle of a rib articulates with which part of a thoracic vertebra?","a":"A transverse process.","options":["The spinous process","A transverse process","The vertebral body","A pedicle"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Each rib makes two joints with the spine. The head of the rib meets the vertebral bodies, and the tubercle, a knob a little farther along the rib, meets the transverse process. The transverse processes of thoracic vertebrae carry costal facets for this purpose.","notes":["The spinous process points backward in the midline and does not form a joint with any rib. Students pick it because it is the most familiar bump of a vertebra, but ribs attach to the sides.","Correct. The rib tubercle articulates with a transverse process.","The vertebral body receives the head of the rib, not the tubercle. Choose the body if the question asks where the rib head articulates.","The pedicles connect the arch to the body and do not carry a facet for the rib. Pick a pedicle for a question about what links the arch to the body."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-spine","lid":"c33","comp":41,"competencyId":"w2-thoracic-cage"},{"id":"L00612","dok":2,"q":"In lab, a student picks up a loose vertebra with a small oval body, a short forked spinous process, and a hole in each transverse process. Which region is it from?","a":"The cervical region.","options":["The cervical region","The lumbar region","The sacral region","The thoracic region"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Each clue points to the neck. Cervical vertebrae are the smallest, with small oval bodies, and their spinous processes are short and often bifid, meaning forked. Most decisive of all, only cervical vertebrae have transverse foramina, the holes in the transverse processes that pass the vertebral arteries.","notes":["Correct. A forked spinous process and transverse foramina identify a cervical vertebra.","Lumbar vertebrae have thick, kidney-shaped bodies and short, blunt spinous processes, with no transverse foramina. Pick the lumbar region for the largest, heaviest vertebra.","The sacrum is a single triangular bone of fused vertebrae, so a loose single vertebra cannot come from it. Choose the sacral region for a question about the fused bone between the hip bones.","Thoracic vertebrae have heart-shaped bodies, long downward-angled spinous processes, and costal facets for ribs. Choose the thoracic region if the vertebra shows rib facets."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-spine","lid":"c34","comp":40,"competencyId":"w2-regional-vertebrae"},{"id":"L00613","dok":2,"q":"A vertebra has a heart-shaped body, a long spinous process angled sharply downward, and smooth facets on its body for ribs. Which region is it from?","a":"The thoracic region.","options":["The lumbar region","The thoracic region","The cervical region","The coccygeal region"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"These features describe a thoracic vertebra. The bodies are heart-shaped, the spinous processes are long and point steeply downward, overlapping like shingles, and the costal facets on the body and transverse processes are where the ribs articulate. The rib facets alone are enough to place it in the thoracic region.","notes":["Lumbar vertebrae have massive, kidney-shaped bodies and short, blunt spinous processes, with no rib facets. Pick the lumbar region for the largest vertebra in the column.","Correct. Costal facets and a heart-shaped body identify a thoracic vertebra.","Cervical vertebrae have small oval bodies, short forked spinous processes, and transverse foramina. Choose the cervical region if the vertebra has holes in its transverse processes.","The coccyx is a small fused tailbone with no rib facets and no long spinous processes. It fits a question about the lowest fused segment of the column."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-spine","lid":"c35","comp":40,"competencyId":"w2-regional-vertebrae"},{"id":"L00614","dok":2,"q":"A vertebra has a thick, kidney-shaped body and a short, blunt spinous process, with no rib facets and no transverse foramina. Which region is it from?","a":"The lumbar region.","options":["The sacral region","The thoracic region","The cervical region","The lumbar region"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"A lumbar vertebra is identified as much by what it lacks as by what it has. It has the largest, kidney-shaped body for bearing weight and a short, blunt spinous process, but no costal facets (so it is not thoracic) and no transverse foramina (so it is not cervical). That combination leaves only the lumbar region.","notes":["The sacrum is a single fused bone, not a separate vertebra with its own spinous process. It would be right for a question about the fused bone between the hip bones.","Thoracic vertebrae have costal facets for the ribs and heart-shaped bodies. Pick the thoracic region if rib facets are present.","Cervical vertebrae always have transverse foramina, so a vertebra without them cannot be cervical. Choose the cervical region when you see those holes in the transverse processes.","Correct. A massive kidney-shaped body without rib facets or transverse foramina is lumbar."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-spine","lid":"c36","comp":40,"competencyId":"w2-regional-vertebrae"},{"id":"L00615","dok":2,"q":"A clinician runs a finger down the back of a patient's neck and feels one spinous process sticking out more than the others at the base of the neck. Which vertebra is it?","a":"The vertebra prominens, C7.","options":["The vertebra prominens, C7","The axis, C2","The atlas, C1","The fifth cervical vertebra, C5"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"C7, the vertebra prominens, has a long spinous process that is not forked, so it projects clearly at the base of the neck. Most other cervical spinous processes are short and lie deep under the neck muscles. Clinicians use C7 as a starting landmark for counting vertebrae down the back.","notes":["Correct. The prominent spinous process at the base of the neck belongs to C7.","The axis, C2, does have a spinous process, but it sits high in the neck just below the skull. Pick C2 for a question about the vertebra bearing the dens.","The atlas, C1, has no spinous process at all, so it cannot be felt as a projecting bump. Choose it for a question about the ring that holds up the skull.","C5 is a typical cervical vertebra in the middle of the neck with a short, often forked spinous process that is hard to feel. Students pick it when they guess a mid-neck vertebra instead of the lowest one."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-spine","lid":"c37","comp":40,"competencyId":"w2-regional-vertebrae"},{"id":"L00616","dok":2,"q":"Before listening to a patient's heart, a nurse feels for a bony ridge on the sternum level with the second rib, then counts ribs down from it. Which landmark is the nurse using?","a":"The sternal angle.","options":["The xiphoid process","The costal margin","The sternal angle","The jugular notch"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The sternal angle is the ridge where the manubrium meets the body of the sternum, and it sits at the level of the second rib. Because the first rib is hidden behind the clavicle, the sternal angle is the most reliable starting point for counting ribs and the spaces between them.","notes":["The xiphoid process is the small tip at the bottom of the sternum, far below the second rib. Pick it for a question about the inferior end of the sternum.","The costal margin is the lower edge of the rib cage, formed by the joined costal cartilages of the lower ribs. It would fit a question about the lower border of the thoracic cage, not a ridge level with rib 2.","Correct. The sternal angle marks the second rib and starts the rib count.","The jugular notch is the dip at the top of the manubrium, above the level of the first rib. Choose it if the question asks about the notch at the base of the neck."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-spine","lid":"c38","comp":41,"competencyId":"w2-thoracic-cage"},{"id":"L00617","dok":2,"q":"On the lateral side of the sacrum, a student finds an ear-shaped surface that joins the hip bone. What is this surface called?","a":"The auricular surface.","options":["The median sacral crest","The auricular surface","The sacral hiatus","The sacral promontory"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The auricular surface is the ear-shaped joint surface on each side of the sacrum. Auricular means ear-like. It meets a matching surface on the ilium of the hip bone to form the sacroiliac joint, which carries body weight from the spine into the pelvis.","notes":["The median sacral crest is the ridge of fused spinous processes along the posterior midline. Pick it for a question about the bumpy ridge down the back of the sacrum.","Correct. The auricular surface joins the hip bone at the sacroiliac joint.","The sacral hiatus is the opening at the lower end of the sacral canal. It would be right for a question about where the sacral canal ends.","The sacral promontory is the anterior ridge of the first sacral vertebra, at the top front of the sacrum. Choose it for a question about the front edge of the sacrum."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-spine","lid":"c39","comp":40,"competencyId":"w2-regional-vertebrae"},{"id":"L00618","dok":2,"q":"On the posterior surface of the sacrum, a ridge runs down the midline. It formed from fused spinous processes. Which marking is it?","a":"The median sacral crest.","options":["The sacral promontory","The auricular surface","The median sacral crest","The sacral canal"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The median sacral crest is a bumpy ridge running down the middle of the back of the sacrum. Each bump is what remains of the spinous process of one of the five sacral vertebrae, now fused together. Median means in the midline, and crest means a ridge.","notes":["The sacral promontory is a ridge on the front of the first sacral vertebra, not the back. Choose it for a question about the anterior edge of the sacrum.","The auricular surface is the ear-shaped joint surface on the side of the sacrum. Pick it for a question about where the sacrum meets the hip bone.","Correct. The median sacral crest is the ridge of fused spinous processes.","The sacral canal is the channel running through the sacrum, the continuation of the vertebral canal. It is a passage inside the bone, not a ridge on its surface."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-spine","lid":"c40","comp":40,"competencyId":"w2-regional-vertebrae"},{"id":"L00619","dok":2,"q":"Which list gives the five regions of the vertebral column in order from superior to inferior?","a":"Cervical, thoracic, lumbar, sacrum, coccyx.","options":["Thoracic, cervical, lumbar, sacrum, coccyx","Cervical, lumbar, thoracic, sacrum, coccyx","Cervical, thoracic, lumbar, coccyx, sacrum","Cervical, thoracic, lumbar, sacrum, coccyx"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Moving down the body, the column begins in the neck (cervical), continues through the chest where the ribs attach (thoracic), then the lower back (lumbar). Below the lumbar region come the two fused bones, the sacrum between the hip bones and finally the coccyx, the tailbone. Matching each region to a body area makes the order easy to rebuild.","notes":["This order starts with the thoracic region, but the neck vertebrae sit above the chest. The cervical region is always first, directly under the skull.","This order swaps the thoracic and lumbar regions. The thoracic region is in the chest, above the lower back, so it must come before the lumbar region.","This order puts the coccyx above the sacrum. The coccyx is the tailbone at the very bottom, so it always comes last.","Correct. Cervical, thoracic, lumbar, sacrum, and coccyx is the superior to inferior order."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-spine","lid":"c41","comp":38,"competencyId":"w2-spine-regions"},{"id":"L00620","dok":2,"q":"Which list gives the four spinal curvatures from superior to inferior with the correct type for each?","a":"Cervical (secondary), thoracic (primary), lumbar (secondary), sacral (primary).","options":["Cervical (secondary), lumbar (secondary), thoracic (primary), sacral (primary)","Cervical (primary), thoracic (secondary), lumbar (primary), sacral (secondary)","Cervical (secondary), thoracic (primary), lumbar (secondary), sacral (primary)","Thoracic (primary), cervical (secondary), lumbar (secondary), sacral (primary)"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The curves alternate down the spine. The cervical curve is secondary (formed when the infant holds up its head), the thoracic is primary (present at birth), the lumbar is secondary (formed with standing and walking), and the sacral is primary. The two primary curves are the ones that keep the original fetal C shape.","notes":["This list has the types right but places the lumbar curve above the thoracic. The lower back lies below the chest, so the thoracic curve comes second.","This list has the order right but reverses every type. The thoracic and sacral curves are the primary ones present at birth, not the cervical and lumbar.","Correct. The curves run cervical, thoracic, lumbar, sacral, alternating secondary and primary.","This list has the types right but puts the thoracic curve above the cervical. The neck is above the chest, so the cervical curve comes first."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-spine","lid":"c42","comp":38,"competencyId":"w2-spine-regions"},{"id":"L00621","dok":2,"q":"A probe passes from the front of a vertebra straight back to the tip of its spinous process, staying on one side of the vertebral foramen. Which list gives the parts it crosses in order?","a":"The body, the pedicle, the lamina, then the spinous process.","options":["The spinous process, the lamina, the pedicle, then the body","The body, the pedicle, the lamina, then the spinous process","The body, the lamina, the pedicle, then the spinous process","The pedicle, the body, the spinous process, then the lamina"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The body is in front. The pedicles project backward from the body to form the sides of the arch, and the laminae continue from the pedicles toward the midline to close the arch behind. The spinous process projects backward from the point where the two laminae meet, so it is the last part reached.","notes":["This is the correct sequence run backward, from the spinous process to the body. It would be right only if the probe started at the back and moved forward.","Correct. Body, pedicle, lamina, and spinous process is the front to back path.","This order swaps the pedicle and lamina. The pedicle attaches directly to the body, so it must come before the lamina.","This order starts at the pedicle and ends at the lamina. The body is the front of the vertebra, and the spinous process is at the very back, so neither can be in the middle."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-spine","lid":"c43","comp":39,"competencyId":"w2-typical-vertebra"},{"id":"L00622","dok":2,"q":"Which set correctly names the joint used to nod yes, the peg the atlas pivots around to shake no, and the vertebra that carries that peg?","a":"The atlanto-occipital joint, the dens, and the axis.","options":["The atlanto-occipital joint, the dens, and the atlas","The atlanto-occipital joint, the spinous process, and the axis","The atlanto-axial joint, the dens, and the axis","The atlanto-occipital joint, the dens, and the axis"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Nodding yes happens at the atlanto-occipital joint, where the atlas meets the occipital condyles of the skull. Shaking the head no happens when the atlas, carrying the skull, rotates around the dens. The dens is the upward peg of the axis, C2, not of the atlas.","notes":["This set gives the dens to the atlas. The atlas is a ring with no body or peg, and it is the axis that carries the dens.","The spinous process points backward from the arch and is not a pivot. Students pick it when they forget the special name of the upward peg on the axis.","The atlanto-axial joint is where the atlas pivots on the dens, the joint for shaking no. It would be the answer for the no motion, but the question asks for the joint used to nod yes.","Correct. The yes nod is at the atlanto-occipital joint, and the atlas turns on the dens of the axis."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-spine","lid":"c44","comp":40,"competencyId":"w2-regional-vertebrae"},{"id":"L00623","dok":3,"q":"A patient has pain shooting down one leg from a herniated lumbar disc. Which description correctly traces how the disc presses on a spinal nerve?","a":"The nucleus pulposus pushes through the anulus fibrosus and presses a nerve at an intervertebral foramen.","options":["The nucleus pulposus pushes through the anulus fibrosus and presses a nerve at an intervertebral foramen","The nucleus pulposus pushes through the anulus fibrosus and presses a nerve at a transverse foramen","The anulus fibrosus pushes through the nucleus pulposus and presses a nerve at an intervertebral foramen","The nucleus pulposus pushes through a lamina and presses a nerve at an intervertebral foramen"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"In a herniated disc, the tough outer ring (anulus fibrosus) tears and the soft gel core (nucleus pulposus) bulges out through the tear. The bulge often presses on a spinal nerve where it leaves the column through an intervertebral foramen. Because that nerve serves the leg, the pain travels down the limb.","notes":["Correct. The core pushes through the torn ring and presses a nerve at an intervertebral foramen.","Transverse foramina are holes in cervical transverse processes that carry the vertebral arteries, not exits for spinal nerves, and lumbar vertebrae do not have them. Choose transverse foramen only for a question about the vertebral arteries.","This reverses the two parts of the disc. The anulus is the outer ring and stays in place, while the soft core is what escapes through it.","A lamina is a bony plate of the vertebral arch, not part of the disc, so the core does not push through it. Pick the lamina for a question about what closes the arch behind."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-spine","lid":"c45","comp":39,"competencyId":"w2-typical-vertebra"},{"id":"L00624","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly describes the bones that form the walls of the thoracic cage?","a":"The sternum in front, the ribs on the sides, and the thoracic vertebrae behind.","options":["The clavicles in front, the ribs on the sides, and the thoracic vertebrae behind","The ribs in front, the sternum on the sides, and the thoracic vertebrae behind","The sternum in front, the ribs on the sides, and the thoracic vertebrae behind","The sternum in front, the thoracic vertebrae on the sides, and the ribs behind"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The thoracic cage is a bony basket that shields the heart and lungs. The sternum forms the front wall in the midline, the twelve pairs of ribs curve around to form the sides, and the twelve thoracic vertebrae form the back wall where the ribs attach. All three are part of the axial skeleton.","notes":["The clavicles belong to the pectoral girdle, which attaches the arm to the trunk. They sit on top of the cage but are not part of its walls, so choose them only for a question about the shoulder girdle.","This swaps the sternum and the ribs. The sternum is a single midline bone in front, so it cannot form the sides.","Correct. Sternum in front, ribs at the sides, and thoracic vertebrae behind.","This swaps the vertebrae and the ribs. The vertebral column runs down the back midline, and the ribs are what wrap around the sides."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-spine","lid":"c46","comp":41,"competencyId":"w2-thoracic-cage"},{"id":"L00625","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly classifies the ribs by how they attach to the sternum?","a":"True ribs (1 to 7) attach directly, false ribs (8 to 12) do not, and floating ribs (11 and 12) are false ribs.","options":["True ribs (1 to 10) attach directly, false ribs (11 and 12) do not, and floating ribs are true ribs","True ribs (1 to 7) attach directly, false ribs (8 to 10) do not, and floating ribs are a third group","True ribs (8 to 12) attach directly, false ribs (1 to 7) do not, and floating ribs are true ribs","True ribs (1 to 7) attach directly, false ribs (8 to 12) do not, and floating ribs are false ribs"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"True ribs, pairs 1 to 7, each reach the sternum by their own costal cartilage. False ribs, pairs 8 to 12, do not reach it directly: pairs 8 to 10 join the cartilage of the rib above, and pairs 11 and 12 have no front attachment at all. Those last two are the floating ribs, which are counted as part of the false ribs.","notes":["Pairs 8 to 10 do not reach the sternum on their own, because each joins the costal cartilage of the rib above. Students choose this when they count any rib with cartilage toward the front as a true rib.","This leaves pairs 11 and 12 out of the false ribs. The false ribs are 8 to 12, so the floating ribs are a subset of them rather than a separate group.","This flips the numbering. The upper ribs, 1 to 7, are the ones with a direct attachment, and the lower ribs are false.","Correct. True ribs are 1 to 7, false ribs are 8 to 12, and the floating ribs belong to the false group."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-spine","lid":"c47","comp":41,"competencyId":"w2-thoracic-cage"},{"id":"L00626","dok":2,"q":"Which set correctly names the channel through the movable vertebrae, its continuation through the sacrum, and the opening at the lower end of that sacral channel?","a":"The vertebral canal, the sacral canal, and the sacral hiatus.","options":["The vertebral canal, the sacral canal, and the sacral promontory","The sacral canal, the vertebral canal, and the sacral hiatus","The vertebral canal, the sacral hiatus, and the sacral foramina","The vertebral canal, the sacral canal, and the sacral hiatus"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The stacked vertebral foramina of the cervical, thoracic, and lumbar vertebrae form the vertebral canal. Inside the sacrum the same channel continues as the sacral canal. It ends at the sacral hiatus, an opening on the lower back of the sacrum where the laminae of the last sacral segment failed to meet in the midline.","notes":["The sacral promontory is a ridge on the front of the first sacral vertebra, not an opening. Choose it for a question about the anterior edge of the sacrum.","This swaps the first two names. The vertebral canal runs through the movable vertebrae above, and the sacral canal is its lower continuation.","The sacral foramina are the paired openings that let sacral nerves out of the sacrum, not the opening at the end of the canal. Pick them for a question about where the sacral nerves exit.","Correct. Vertebral canal, sacral canal, then sacral hiatus."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-spine","lid":"c48","comp":40,"competencyId":"w2-regional-vertebrae"},{"id":"L00627","dok":2,"q":"Which structure is the anterior ridge of the first sacral vertebra?","a":"The sacral promontory.","options":["The auricular surface","The median sacral crest","The sacral hiatus","The sacral promontory"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The sacral promontory is the forward-bulging upper edge of the body of the first sacral vertebra. A promontory is a point of land that juts out, and this ridge juts into the pelvis. It marks part of the boundary between the upper (false) pelvis and the lower (true) pelvis.","notes":["The auricular surface is the ear-shaped joint surface on the side of the sacrum. It would be right for a question about where the sacrum meets the hip bone.","The median sacral crest is the ridge of fused spinous processes on the posterior surface. Pick it for a question about the midline ridge on the back of the sacrum.","The sacral hiatus is the opening at the inferior end of the sacral canal on the back of the sacrum. Choose it for a question about where the sacral canal ends.","Correct. The sacral promontory is the anterior ridge of S1."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-spine","lid":"c49","comp":40,"competencyId":"w2-regional-vertebrae"},{"id":"L00628","dok":2,"q":"Which joint, between the atlas and the occipital condyles of the skull, lets you nod your head yes?","a":"The atlanto-occipital joint.","options":["The temporomandibular joint","The atlanto-occipital joint","The atlanto-axial joint","The sacroiliac joint"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The atlanto-occipital joint is where the superior surfaces of the atlas cradle the occipital condyles, the two rounded knobs on the base of the skull. The name combines its two parts, atlas and occipital bone. Rocking the skull forward and back on the atlas produces the yes nod.","notes":["The temporomandibular joint is the jaw joint between the mandible and the temporal bone. It would be right for a question about opening and closing the mouth.","Correct. The atlanto-occipital joint produces the yes nod.","The atlanto-axial joint is between the atlas and the axis, where the atlas pivots on the dens. Choose it if the question asks about shaking the head no.","The sacroiliac joint is where the sacrum meets the hip bone in the pelvis. Pick it for a question about the auricular surface of the sacrum."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-spine","lid":"c50","comp":40,"competencyId":"w2-regional-vertebrae"},{"id":"L00629","dok":1,"q":"How many bones make up the adult vertebral column?","a":"26 bones.","options":["12 bones","24 bones","26 bones","33 bones"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The adult column has 24 separate vertebrae (7 cervical, 12 thoracic, 5 lumbar) plus two fused bones, the sacrum and the coccyx. Each fused bone counts as one, giving 26 bones in total. Early in development there are 33 vertebrae, but the sacral and coccygeal ones fuse.","notes":["12 is the number of thoracic vertebrae or of rib pairs. Students pick it by recalling one region's count instead of the whole column.","24 is only the separate vertebrae of the three movable regions. It would be right if the question excluded the sacrum and coccyx.","Correct. 24 separate vertebrae plus the sacrum and coccyx make 26 bones.","33 is the number of vertebrae early in development, before the sacral and coccygeal vertebrae fuse. Choose it for a question about the developing column."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-spine","lid":"c1-x2","comp":38,"competencyId":"w2-spine-regions"},{"id":"L00630","dok":1,"q":"Which region of the vertebral column contains seven vertebrae?","a":"The cervical region.","options":["The thoracic region","The cervical region","The sacrum","The lumbar region"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The cervical region, in the neck, contains seven vertebrae, C1 through C7. This includes the specialized atlas (C1), axis (C2), and vertebra prominens (C7). Remembering 7 for the neck, 12 for the chest, and 5 for the lower back covers all three movable regions.","notes":["The thoracic region has twelve vertebrae, one for each pair of ribs. Choose it if the question asks about twelve vertebrae.","Correct. The cervical region has seven vertebrae.","The sacrum is a single bone made of five fused vertebrae. It would be right for a question about five vertebrae fused into one bone.","The lumbar region has five vertebrae in the lower back. Pick it for a question about five separate, weight-bearing vertebrae."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-spine","lid":"c2-x2","comp":38,"competencyId":"w2-spine-regions"},{"id":"L00631","dok":1,"q":"Which region of the vertebral column contains twelve vertebrae?","a":"The thoracic region.","options":["The cervical region","The coccyx","The lumbar region","The thoracic region"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The thoracic region contains twelve vertebrae, T1 through T12, in the chest. There are also twelve pairs of ribs, and each thoracic vertebra articulates with a pair. Linking the number of thoracic vertebrae to the number of rib pairs makes both easy to remember.","notes":["The cervical region has seven vertebrae in the neck. Choose it for a question about seven vertebrae.","The coccyx is a small fused tailbone, usually of four tiny vertebrae. It would be right for a question about the lowest fused bone of the column.","The lumbar region has five vertebrae in the lower back. Pick it for a question about five separate vertebrae.","Correct. The thoracic region has twelve vertebrae."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-spine","lid":"c3-x2","comp":38,"competencyId":"w2-spine-regions"},{"id":"L00632","dok":1,"q":"What makes a spinal curvature a primary curvature?","a":"It is present at birth.","options":["It is present at birth","It develops as a child stands and walks","It is an exaggerated, abnormal curve","It develops as an infant holds up its head"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Primary curvatures are the ones a baby is born with, left over from the curled C shape of the fetus. In the adult these are the thoracic and sacral curvatures. Curvatures that appear later with infant milestones are called secondary.","notes":["Correct. A primary curvature is present at birth.","Developing with standing and walking describes the lumbar curvature, which is secondary. Pick this for a question about how the lower back curve forms.","An exaggerated curve is a disorder such as kyphosis or lordosis, not a normal primary curve. Choose this for a question about abnormal curvatures.","Developing as the infant holds up its head describes the cervical curvature, which is secondary. Choose this for a question about how the neck curve forms."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-spine","lid":"c4-x2","comp":38,"competencyId":"w2-spine-regions"},{"id":"L00633","dok":1,"q":"What is another name for the body of a vertebra?","a":"The centrum.","options":["The centrum","The lamina","The pedicle","The dens"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The body of a vertebra is also called the centrum, because it forms the central, weight-bearing core of each vertebra. The bodies stack into a column separated by intervertebral discs. Both names appear in lab and on exams, so it helps to know they mean the same part.","notes":["Correct. The centrum is another name for the vertebral body.","A lamina is one of the two flat plates that close the vertebral arch behind. Choose it for a question about the back of the arch.","A pedicle is one of the two short bars connecting the arch to the body. Pick it for a question about what links the arch to the body.","The dens is the upward peg of the axis, sometimes called the odontoid process. Students mix up these alternate names, but the dens is a peg on C2, not the body of every vertebra."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-spine","lid":"c5-x2","comp":39,"competencyId":"w2-typical-vertebra"},{"id":"L00634","dok":1,"q":"When the vertebral foramina of many vertebrae line up, what channel do they form?","a":"The vertebral canal.","options":["The vertebral canal","The carpal tunnel","The central canal","The sacral canal"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Each vertebra has a vertebral foramen, and when the vertebrae are stacked those holes line up into one long bony tunnel called the vertebral canal. The spinal cord runs inside it, protected on all sides by bone. A single opening is a foramen, and the whole stacked channel is the canal.","notes":["Correct. Stacked vertebral foramina form the vertebral canal.","The carpal tunnel is a passage at the wrist under the flexor retinaculum. It would be right for a question about where flexor tendons and the median nerve enter the hand.","The central canal is the tiny fluid-filled channel inside the spinal cord itself, not a bony tunnel. Pick it for a question about the canal in the middle of the cord's gray matter.","The sacral canal is the lower continuation of the vertebral canal inside the fused sacrum. Choose it if the question asks about the channel through the sacrum specifically."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-spine","lid":"c6-x2","comp":39,"competencyId":"w2-typical-vertebra"},{"id":"L00635","dok":1,"q":"What is the first cervical vertebra called?","a":"The atlas.","options":["The dens","The vertebra prominens","The axis","The atlas"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The first cervical vertebra, C1, is the atlas. It sits directly under the skull and holds up the head, like the figure from myth who held up the world. It is a ring with no body and no spinous process.","notes":["The dens is a peg on the axis, not a whole vertebra. It would be right for a question about the pivot the atlas turns around.","The vertebra prominens is C7, the last cervical vertebra, with the long spinous process at the base of the neck. Pick it for a question about C7.","The axis is C2, the second cervical vertebra, which carries the dens. Choose it if the question asks about the second cervical vertebra.","Correct. C1 is the atlas."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-spine","lid":"c8-x2","comp":40,"competencyId":"w2-regional-vertebrae"},{"id":"L00636","dok":1,"q":"What is the second cervical vertebra called?","a":"The axis.","options":["The atlas","The vertebra prominens","The axis","The sacrum"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The second cervical vertebra, C2, is the axis. It is named for its dens, an upward peg that acts as the axis of rotation when the atlas and skull turn side to side. That rotation is the motion of shaking your head no.","notes":["The atlas is C1, the ring directly under the skull. Choose it if the question asks about the first cervical vertebra.","The vertebra prominens is C7, with the long spinous process felt at the base of the neck. Pick it for a question about the seventh cervical vertebra.","Correct. C2 is the axis.","The sacrum is the fused triangular bone between the hip bones, not a cervical vertebra. It would be right for a question about five fused vertebrae."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-spine","lid":"c9-x2","comp":40,"competencyId":"w2-regional-vertebrae"},{"id":"L00637","dok":1,"q":"The sternum is made of how many fused parts?","a":"Three","options":["Five","Four","Three","Two"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The sternum, or breastbone, is a flat bone made of three parts fused together. From top to bottom they are the manubrium, the body, and the xiphoid process. The sternal angle marks the junction between the first two parts.","notes":["Five is the number of fused vertebrae in the sacrum, not the number of sternal parts. Students pick it when they mix up the sternum with the sacrum, another bone built from fused parts.","Four is too many. Students may count a landmark such as the jugular notch or sternal angle as a separate part, but those are features, not parts.","Correct. The manubrium, body, and xiphoid process make three parts.","Two would leave out one of the parts. Students choose it when they think of the manubrium and body but forget the small xiphoid process at the bottom."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-spine","lid":"c11-x2","comp":41,"competencyId":"w2-thoracic-cage"},{"id":"L00638","dok":1,"q":"How many pairs of ribs does the thoracic cage have?","a":"Twelve pairs.","options":["Ten pairs","Twelve pairs","Seven pairs","Five pairs"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"There are twelve pairs of ribs, twenty-four ribs in all, matching the twelve thoracic vertebrae they attach to in back. They are grouped by how they reach the sternum in front: seven pairs of true ribs and five pairs of false ribs, two of which are floating ribs.","notes":["Ten counts the ribs that have any anterior cartilage connection, pairs 1 to 10. Students pick it by forgetting the two floating pairs below.","Correct. There are twelve pairs of ribs.","Seven is the number of true rib pairs, the ones that attach directly to the sternum. Choose it for a question about the true ribs only.","Five is the number of false rib pairs, pairs 8 to 12. It would be right for a question about how many pairs are false ribs."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-spine","lid":"c12-x2","comp":41,"competencyId":"w2-thoracic-cage"},{"id":"L00639","dok":1,"q":"Which rib pairs are classified as false ribs?","a":"Pairs 8 to 12.","options":["Pairs 1 to 7","Pairs 11 and 12","Pairs 1 to 10","Pairs 8 to 12"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"False ribs are pairs 8 through 12, the ribs that do not reach the sternum by their own costal cartilage. Pairs 8 to 10 connect indirectly by joining the cartilage of the rib above, and pairs 11 and 12 have no front attachment at all. So every rib below the seventh pair is a false rib.","notes":["Pairs 1 to 7 are the true ribs, which each attach directly to the sternum. Choose them for a question about direct sternal attachment.","Pairs 11 and 12 are the floating ribs, which are only part of the false rib group. Pick them for a question about ribs with no anterior attachment at all.","Pairs 1 to 10 mixes the seven true ribs with three false ribs. Students choose it when they count every rib with a cartilage link to the front as the same group.","Correct. Pairs 8 to 12 are the false ribs."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-spine","lid":"c13-x2","comp":41,"competencyId":"w2-thoracic-cage"},{"id":"L00640","dok":2,"q":"How do rib pairs 8, 9, and 10 connect to the front of the thoracic cage?","a":"Each joins the costal cartilage of the rib above.","options":["Each joins the costal cartilage of the rib above","Each attaches to the xiphoid process by a ligament","Each attaches directly to the sternum by its own cartilage","Each ends freely in the abdominal wall"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Ribs 8 to 10 are false ribs that reach the sternum only indirectly. The costal cartilage of each one merges with the cartilage of the rib above it, and that shared cartilage eventually reaches the sternum through rib 7. Together these joined cartilages form the lower edge of the rib cage, the costal margin.","notes":["Correct. Ribs 8 to 10 join the costal cartilage of the rib above.","The xiphoid process is the small tip of the sternum and serves as a muscle attachment, not the anchor for ribs 8 to 10. Students pick it because those ribs sit near the bottom of the sternum.","Attaching directly by its own cartilage describes the true ribs, pairs 1 to 7. Choose this for a question about the true ribs.","Ending freely in the abdominal wall describes the floating ribs, pairs 11 and 12. Pick this for a question about the floating ribs."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-spine","lid":"c14-x2","comp":41,"competencyId":"w2-thoracic-cage"},{"id":"L00641","dok":1,"q":"What is the central dip at the top of the manubrium, easily felt at the base of the neck, called?","a":"The jugular notch.","options":["The xiphoid process","The costal notch","The jugular notch","The sternal angle"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The jugular notch is the shallow dip in the middle of the superior border of the manubrium, between the two clavicles. You can feel it at the base of the front of the neck. It is also called the suprasternal notch and is a common surface landmark.","notes":["The xiphoid process is the small tip at the bottom of the sternum. Pick it for a question about the inferior end of the breastbone.","Costal notches are the small notches along the sides of the sternum where the costal cartilages of the ribs attach. Choose them for a question about where ribs meet the sternum.","Correct. The jugular notch is the dip at the top of the manubrium.","The sternal angle is the ridge where the manubrium meets the body of the sternum, lower down at the level of the second rib. Choose it for a question about the landmark used to count ribs."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-spine","lid":"c15-x2","comp":41,"competencyId":"w2-thoracic-cage"},{"id":"L00642","dok":2,"q":"A vertebra has a short spinous process that splits into two tips (a bifid spinous process). Which region does it most likely come from?","a":"The cervical region.","options":["The lumbar region","The sacral region","The cervical region","The thoracic region"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"A bifid, or forked, spinous process is typical of cervical vertebrae, especially C2 through C6. Thoracic and lumbar spinous processes end in a single tip. A forked spinous process and transverse foramina are the two quick features that identify a vertebra from the neck.","notes":["Lumbar spinous processes are short, blunt, and point straight back as a single tip. Pick the lumbar region for a vertebra with a massive kidney-shaped body.","The sacrum is a fused bone whose spinous processes merge into the median sacral crest. It would be right for a question about that ridge on the back of the sacrum.","Correct. A forked spinous process points to the cervical region.","Thoracic spinous processes are long, single, and angled sharply downward. Choose the thoracic region for a vertebra with a long, downward-pointing spinous process or costal facets."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-spine","lid":"c16-x2","comp":40,"competencyId":"w2-regional-vertebrae"},{"id":"L00643","dok":2,"q":"A vertebra has a heart-shaped body. Which region does it come from?","a":"The thoracic region.","options":["The thoracic region","The lumbar region","The coccygeal region","The cervical region"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Thoracic vertebrae have medium-sized bodies shaped like a heart when viewed from above. Body shape changes along the column: small and oval in the neck, heart-shaped in the chest, and large and kidney-shaped in the lower back. Thoracic vertebrae also carry costal facets for the ribs, which confirms the region.","notes":["Correct. A heart-shaped body identifies a thoracic vertebra.","Lumbar vertebrae have thick, kidney-shaped bodies, the largest in the column. Pick the lumbar region for a massive weight-bearing body.","The coccygeal vertebrae are tiny fused remnants without a distinct heart-shaped body. They would fit a question about the tailbone.","Cervical vertebrae have small, oval bodies, the smallest in the column. Choose the cervical region for a small oval body or transverse foramina."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-spine","lid":"c17-x2","comp":40,"competencyId":"w2-regional-vertebrae"},{"id":"L00644","dok":2,"q":"A vertebra has a short, blunt spinous process that points straight back instead of angling downward. Which region does it come from?","a":"The lumbar region.","options":["The thoracic region","The sacral region","The lumbar region","The cervical region"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Lumbar vertebrae have short, thick, blunt spinous processes that project straight backward, often compared to a hatchet blade. This contrasts with the long, downward-angled thoracic spinous processes and the short, forked cervical ones. The straight-back direction leaves gaps between lumbar spinous processes, which is why a needle can be placed there for a lumbar puncture.","notes":["Thoracic spinous processes are long and angled sharply downward, overlapping the vertebra below. Pick the thoracic region for that shingled pattern.","The sacrum has no separate spinous processes; they are fused into the median sacral crest. It would fit a question about that fused ridge.","Correct. A short, blunt spinous process pointing straight back identifies a lumbar vertebra.","Cervical spinous processes are short but usually forked at the tip, not blunt and broad. Choose the cervical region for a bifid spinous process."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-spine","lid":"c18-x2","comp":40,"competencyId":"w2-regional-vertebrae"},{"id":"L00645","dok":2,"q":"The atlanto-occipital joint allows the head to nod yes. Which two structures meet at this joint?","a":"The atlas and the occipital condyles.","options":["The axis and the occipital condyles","The atlas and the occipital condyles","The axis and the atlas","The atlas and the dens"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The name of the joint tells you its parts: atlanto refers to the atlas (C1) and occipital refers to the occipital bone of the skull. The rounded occipital condyles on the base of the skull rest in the superior facets of the atlas. Rocking the condyles forward and back on the atlas produces the yes nod.","notes":["The axis is C2 and does not touch the skull. Students pick it by mixing up the two named cervical vertebrae, but the atlas is the one directly under the skull.","Correct. The atlas meets the occipital condyles.","The axis and atlas together form the atlanto-axial joint, not the atlanto-occipital joint. Pick this pair for a question about the rotation joint between C1 and C2.","The atlas and the dens meet at the atlanto-axial joint, which turns the head side to side. Choose this pair for a question about shaking the head no."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-spine","lid":"c19-x2","comp":40,"competencyId":"w2-regional-vertebrae"},{"id":"L00646","dok":1,"q":"The sternal angle sits level with which rib?","a":"The second rib.","options":["The second rib","The fourth rib","The first rib","The seventh rib"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The costal cartilage of the second rib attaches at the sternal angle, the ridge where the manubrium meets the body of the sternum. Because the sternal angle is easy to feel and the first rib is hard to reach under the clavicle, clinicians start counting ribs at the second rib beside the angle.","notes":["Correct. The sternal angle is level with the second rib.","The fourth rib attaches lower on the body of the sternum. Students pick a middle rib when they picture the angle near the middle of the sternum, but it sits near the top.","The first rib attaches to the manubrium, above the sternal angle, and is mostly hidden behind the clavicle. Choose it for a question about the rib that articulates with the manubrium below the clavicle.","The seventh rib is the last true rib, attaching near the bottom of the sternal body by the xiphoid process. Pick it for a question about the lowest true rib."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-spine","lid":"c20-x2","comp":41,"competencyId":"w2-thoracic-cage"},{"id":"L00647","dok":2,"q":"In cervical vertebrae, which structures travel through the transverse foramina?","a":"The vertebral arteries.","options":["The spinal cord","The spinal nerves","The sacral nerves","The vertebral arteries"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The transverse foramina are small holes in the transverse processes of cervical vertebrae. Stacked one above another, they form a protected bony channel for the vertebral arteries as they climb the neck to supply the brain. This is why transverse foramina are found only in the cervical region.","notes":["The spinal cord runs through the large central vertebral foramen of each vertebra. Pick the spinal cord for a question about the vertebral foramen or vertebral canal.","Spinal nerves leave the column through the intervertebral foramina between vertebrae, not through the transverse processes. Choose spinal nerves for a question about the intervertebral foramina.","The sacral nerves exit through the sacral foramina of the sacrum, far below the neck. It would be right for a question about the openings in the sacrum.","Correct. The vertebral arteries pass through the transverse foramina."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-spine","lid":"c21-x2","comp":40,"competencyId":"w2-regional-vertebrae"},{"id":"L00648","dok":1,"q":"Which two bones meet at the sacroiliac joint?","a":"The sacrum and the ilium of the hip bone.","options":["The sacrum and the fifth lumbar vertebra","The ilium and the femur","The sacrum and the ilium of the hip bone","The sacrum and the coccyx"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The sacroiliac joint is named for its two bones, the sacrum and the ilium, the upper wing of the hip bone. The auricular surface on each side of the sacrum meets a matching auricular surface on the ilium. This joint transfers the weight of the upper body from the spine into the pelvis.","notes":["The sacrum meets L5 at the lumbosacral joint at the top of the sacrum. Pick this pair for a question about where the lumbar spine joins the sacrum.","The femur meets the hip bone at the hip joint, in the acetabulum, not at the ilium's joint with the spine. This pair fits a question about the hip joint.","Correct. The sacroiliac joint joins the sacrum and the ilium.","The sacrum and coccyx meet at the sacrococcygeal joint at the bottom of the sacrum. Choose this pair for a question about where the tailbone attaches."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-spine","lid":"c22-x2","comp":40,"competencyId":"w2-regional-vertebrae"},{"id":"L00649","dok":1,"q":"The seventh cervical vertebra is called the vertebra prominens. What feature earns it that name?","a":"Its long spinous process felt at the base of the neck.","options":["Its long spinous process felt at the base of the neck","Its large, kidney-shaped weight-bearing body","Its ring shape with no body","Its upward peg that the atlas turns around"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Prominens means prominent. C7 has a long spinous process that is not forked, and it sticks out under the skin at the base of the back of the neck. Because it is easy to find, it serves as a landmark for counting vertebrae.","notes":["Correct. C7 is named for its prominent, palpable spinous process.","A large, kidney-shaped body belongs to lumbar vertebrae, which carry the most weight. Choose this feature for a question about the lumbar region.","A ring with no body describes the atlas, C1. It would be right for a question about the vertebra that holds up the skull.","The upward peg is the dens of the axis, C2. Pick this feature for a question about the axis."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-spine","lid":"c23-x2","comp":40,"competencyId":"w2-regional-vertebrae"},{"id":"L00650","dok":1,"q":"What is the fibrocartilage pad between two adjacent vertebral bodies called?","a":"The intervertebral disc.","options":["The intervertebral disc","The articular process","The vertebral arch","The costal cartilage"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"An intervertebral disc is a pad of fibrocartilage that sits between the bodies of two neighboring vertebrae. Each disc has a tough outer ring (anulus fibrosus) and a soft gel core (nucleus pulposus). The discs absorb shock and allow small movements between vertebrae.","notes":["Correct. The intervertebral disc is the fibrocartilage pad between vertebral bodies.","The articular processes are bony projections that join one vertebra to the next at small joints behind the bodies. Choose them for a question about the bony connections between vertebral arches.","The vertebral arch is the bony ring behind the body that surrounds the spinal cord. Pick it for a question about what encloses the vertebral foramen.","Costal cartilage is hyaline cartilage that connects the ribs toward the sternum. It would be right for a question about the cartilage at the front of the rib cage."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-spine","lid":"c24-x2","comp":39,"competencyId":"w2-typical-vertebra"},{"id":"L00651","dok":1,"q":"Which paired processes of a vertebra join it to the vertebrae directly above and below?","a":"The superior and inferior articular processes.","options":["The transverse processes","The superior and inferior articular processes","The spinous processes","The pedicles"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Each vertebra has a pair of superior articular processes that point up and a pair of inferior articular processes that point down. The inferior processes of one vertebra meet the superior processes of the vertebra below at small joints. These joints guide and limit how the spine bends and twists.","notes":["The transverse processes point out to the sides and serve as muscle attachments, and in the thorax they meet the ribs. Choose them for a question about the lateral projections.","Correct. The articular processes link one vertebra to the next.","Each vertebra has a single spinous process pointing back, and it does not form a joint with the next vertebra. Students pick it because spinous processes line up down the back, but they only anchor muscles and ligaments.","The pedicles connect the arch to the body of the same vertebra, not to neighboring vertebrae. Pick them for a question about what joins the arch to the body."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-spine","lid":"c25-x2","comp":39,"competencyId":"w2-typical-vertebra"},{"id":"L00652","dok":2,"q":"A patient has pain that travels down one leg after the soft core of a lumbar disc pushes through its torn outer ring. What is this condition called?","a":"A herniated disc.","options":["Scoliosis","A herniated disc","Kyphosis","A fractured dens"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"A herniated disc happens when the nucleus pulposus bulges through a tear in the anulus fibrosus. The bulge can press on a spinal nerve as it exits through an intervertebral foramen. Because that nerve carries signals from the leg, the pain is felt along the limb rather than only in the back.","notes":["Scoliosis is an abnormal sideways curve of the spine. Choose it if the question describes a spine that curves to one side.","Correct. A disc core pushing through a torn ring is a herniated disc.","Kyphosis is an exaggerated thoracic curve that rounds the upper back. Pick it for a question about a hunched upper back.","A fractured dens is a break of the peg on the axis, high in the neck. It would be right for a question about a serious injury near the brainstem."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-spine","lid":"c26-x2","comp":39,"competencyId":"w2-typical-vertebra"},{"id":"L00653","dok":2,"q":"During a school screening, a student bends forward and the nurse sees that the spine curves to one side. Which abnormal curvature is this?","a":"Scoliosis.","options":["Kyphosis","Lordosis","A herniated disc","Scoliosis"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Scoliosis is an abnormal sideways curve of the spine. The normal curvatures all bend forward or backward, so any curve to the left or right is abnormal. The other named abnormal curvatures, kyphosis and lordosis, are exaggerations of the normal front-to-back curves.","notes":["Kyphosis is an exaggerated thoracic curve that rounds the upper back forward, a front-to-back problem. Choose it for a hunched upper back.","Lordosis is an exaggerated lumbar curve that makes the lower back sway inward. Pick it for a question about an excessive curve in the lower back.","A herniated disc is a bulging disc core pressing on a nerve, not a curvature. It would be right for a question about pain traveling down a limb.","Correct. A sideways curve is scoliosis."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-spine","lid":"c27-x2","comp":38,"competencyId":"w2-spine-regions"},{"id":"L00654","dok":2,"q":"During CPR, a rescuer locates the small bony tip at the lower end of the sternum so that the hands press on the sternum just above it rather than on the tip. Which structure is the rescuer finding?","a":"The xiphoid process.","options":["The jugular notch","The manubrium","The xiphoid process","The sternal angle"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The xiphoid process is the small inferior tip of the sternum. Rescuers use it as a landmark so they press on the lower half of the sternum body above it. Pressing directly on the xiphoid could break it and push it toward the liver, so finding it keeps the hands in the right spot.","notes":["The jugular notch is the dip at the top of the manubrium at the base of the neck. Pick it for a question about the superior border of the sternum.","The manubrium is the superior part of the sternum near the clavicles, far from where compressions are given. Choose it for a question about the top of the sternum.","Correct. The xiphoid process is the inferior tip that guides hand placement.","The sternal angle is the ridge between the manubrium and body, level with the second rib. It would be right for a question about where to start counting ribs."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-spine","lid":"c28-x2","comp":41,"competencyId":"w2-thoracic-cage"},{"id":"L00655","dok":2,"q":"A diver strikes the top of the head on the pool floor and fractures a bony peg that is held against the atlas by a ligament. Which structure is fractured?","a":"The dens of the axis.","options":["The anterior arch of the atlas","The transverse process of C1","The spinous process of C7","The dens of the axis"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The dens is the upward peg of the axis (C2). It sits inside the front of the atlas ring, held there by a strong ligament, so the atlas can pivot around it. A fracture of the dens is very serious because the fragment lies right next to the brainstem and upper spinal cord.","notes":["The anterior arch of the atlas is the front part of the C1 ring that the dens presses against. It is part of the ring, not a peg, so choose it for a question about the front of the atlas.","The transverse process of C1 projects to the side and contains a transverse foramen for the vertebral artery. It would fit a question about the lateral parts of the atlas.","The spinous process of C7 is the long projection felt at the base of the neck. Pick it for a question about the vertebra prominens.","Correct. The peg held against the atlas is the dens of the axis."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-spine","lid":"c29-x2","comp":40,"competencyId":"w2-regional-vertebrae"},{"id":"L00656","dok":2,"q":"A broken vertebra is treated as an emergency mainly because of a delicate structure that runs through the column. Which structure is at risk?","a":"The spinal cord.","options":["The esophagus","The spinal cord","The red bone marrow","The nucleus pulposus"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The stacked vertebral foramina form the vertebral canal, which carries the spinal cord. A fractured vertebra can shift or splinter into the canal and injure the cord, which can cause paralysis below the level of injury. That is why a vertebral fracture is dangerous for what runs inside the column, not only for the bone.","notes":["The esophagus runs in front of the vertebral column, not through it. It would be right for a question about the tube carrying food to the stomach.","Correct. The spinal cord runs through the vertebral canal and can be injured by a fracture.","Red bone marrow does fill the spongy bone of the vertebral bodies, but damage to it is not what makes a spinal fracture an emergency. Students pick it by reading inside the bone literally rather than thinking about the canal.","The nucleus pulposus is the gel core of an intervertebral disc. Choose it for a question about a herniated disc, not a fractured vertebra."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-spine","lid":"c30-x2","comp":39,"competencyId":"w2-typical-vertebra"},{"id":"L00657","dok":3,"q":"The adult vertebral column has four curves instead of being a straight rod. What do these curves do for the spine?","a":"They act like a spring, absorbing shock and helping balance the body's weight.","options":["They hold the vertebrae rigidly in line so the column cannot bend","They create the openings where spinal nerves leave the column","They act like a spring, absorbing shock and helping balance the body's weight","They give every vertebra a place for a rib to attach"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"A column with alternating curves can flex and rebound under a load, much like a spring, so the jolts of walking and running are cushioned instead of traveling straight up to the skull. The curves also keep the weight of the head and trunk balanced over the hips. This gives the spine strength, flexibility, and balance at the same time.","notes":["The curves do the opposite of making the spine rigid; they add flexibility. Students who picture the spine as a support pole pick this, but a rigid column would pass every jolt to the head.","The openings for spinal nerves are the intervertebral foramina, formed by notches between neighboring vertebrae, not by the curves. Choose this idea for a question about where spinal nerves exit.","Correct. The curves work like a spring for shock absorption and balance.","Ribs attach only to the twelve thoracic vertebrae, and that has nothing to do with the curves. Pick rib attachment for a question about costal facets on thoracic vertebrae."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-spine","lid":"d3-t-axial-skeleton-spine-1","comp":38,"competencyId":"w2-spine-regions"},{"id":"L00658","dok":3,"q":"Lumbar vertebrae have much larger bodies than cervical vertebrae. What best explains this size difference?","a":"Lower vertebrae carry more of the body's weight above them.","options":["Lower vertebrae carry more of the body's weight above them","Lumbar vertebrae must anchor the twelve pairs of ribs","Lumbar vertebrae need room for transverse foramina","Lumbar vertebrae surround a much thicker spinal cord"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Weight adds up as you move down the spine. Cervical vertebrae carry only the head, but lumbar vertebrae carry the head, arms, and entire trunk. Larger, thicker bodies spread that heavier load, so vertebral body size increases steadily from the neck to the lower back.","notes":["Correct. Lumbar bodies are larger because they bear more weight.","Ribs attach to the thoracic vertebrae, not the lumbar ones, and lumbar vertebrae have no costal facets. Choose rib attachment for a question about thoracic features.","Transverse foramina are found only in cervical vertebrae, and lumbar vertebrae lack them. Pick transverse foramina for a question about identifying a cervical vertebra.","The spinal cord does not get thicker in the lower back; in adults it ends near the first or second lumbar vertebra. Students assume big vertebrae must hold a big cord, but the size is about weight."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-spine","lid":"d3-t-axial-skeleton-spine-2","comp":40,"competencyId":"w2-regional-vertebrae"},{"id":"L00659","dok":3,"q":"Each intervertebral disc has a soft gel core inside a tough outer ring. How does this two-part design suit the disc's job?","a":"The gel core spreads pressure while the tough ring holds the core in place.","options":["The gel core spreads pressure while the tough ring holds the core in place","The two layers enclose a joint cavity filled with synovial fluid","The tough core bears the weight while the gel ring lets the vertebrae slide","The ring anchors the spinal nerves as they leave the column"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"When the spine is loaded, the gel-like nucleus pulposus squashes slightly and spreads the force evenly across the vertebral bodies. The fibrous anulus fibrosus surrounds the core, keeps it contained, and resists the tension the spreading core creates. The pairing gives both cushioning and containment.","notes":["Correct. The core spreads pressure and the ring contains it.","An intervertebral disc forms a cartilaginous joint with no synovial cavity. Choose a synovial cavity for a question about freely movable joints such as the shoulder or knee.","This reverses the layers. The core is the soft gel and the ring is the tough fibrous part, not the other way around.","Spinal nerves exit through the intervertebral foramina beside the disc, but the disc does not anchor them. Students pick this because a bulging disc can press a nerve, which is a problem, not the disc's purpose."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-spine","lid":"d3-t-axial-skeleton-spine-3","comp":39,"competencyId":"w2-typical-vertebra"},{"id":"L00660","dok":2,"q":"The atlas is a ring of bone with no body, sitting directly under the skull. Which job does the atlas perform?","a":"It holds up the skull and forms the joint for nodding yes with the occipital condyles.","options":["It carries the dens that the head rotates around","It carries costal facets for the first pair of ribs","It forms the long spinous process felt at the base of the neck","It holds up the skull and forms the joint for nodding yes with the occipital condyles"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The atlas supports the head. Its superior facets cradle the occipital condyles of the skull, forming the atlanto-occipital joint that rocks the head forward and back to nod yes. With no body in the way, the front of its ring holds the dens of the axis, and the large opening leaves room for the spinal cord.","notes":["Carrying the dens is the job of the axis, C2. The atlas pivots around the dens but does not carry it, so choose this for a question about the axis.","The first pair of ribs articulates with T1, the first thoracic vertebra. Pick this for a question about where the thoracic region and the ribs begin.","The long palpable spinous process belongs to C7, the vertebra prominens. The atlas has no spinous process at all.","Correct. The atlas holds up the skull and forms the yes-nodding joint."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-spine","lid":"d3-t-axial-skeleton-spine-4","comp":40,"competencyId":"w2-regional-vertebrae"},{"id":"L00661","dok":2,"q":"The axis has an upward peg, the dens, that fits into the ring of the atlas. Which head movement does this arrangement make possible?","a":"Turning the head side to side, as in shaking no.","options":["Turning the head side to side, as in shaking no","Nodding the head yes","Tilting the ear toward the shoulder","Opening and closing the jaw"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The dens acts as a pivot. The atlas, carrying the skull on top of it, rotates around the dens at the atlanto-axial joint, which turns the head left and right. That is the motion of shaking your head no, and it is why C2 is called the axis.","notes":["Correct. The atlas pivots on the dens to turn the head side to side.","Nodding yes happens at the atlanto-occipital joint between the atlas and the skull. Choose it for a question about the joint above the atlas.","Tilting the ear toward the shoulder is side bending shared across many cervical vertebrae, not rotation on a single peg. Students pick it because it is also a sideways movement of the head.","Opening and closing the jaw happens at the temporomandibular joint between the mandible and temporal bone. It would be right for a question about chewing or talking."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-spine","lid":"d3-t-axial-skeleton-spine-5","comp":40,"competencyId":"w2-regional-vertebrae"},{"id":"L00662","dok":2,"q":"Thoracic vertebrae have costal facets that cervical and lumbar vertebrae lack. Why are these facets found only in the thoracic region?","a":"Ribs articulate with the spine only in the thoracic region.","options":["They pass the vertebral arteries up to the brain","Ribs articulate with the spine only in the thoracic region","They hold the intervertebral discs in place","They anchor the clavicles to the spine"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Costal means rib, and costal facets are the smooth joint surfaces where ribs meet the spine. The head of each rib meets facets on the vertebral bodies, and the tubercle meets a facet on the transverse process. Since ribs attach only to the twelve thoracic vertebrae, only they need these facets.","notes":["Passing the vertebral arteries is the job of the transverse foramina, found only in cervical vertebrae. Choose this for a question about a feature unique to the neck.","Correct. Costal facets exist where the ribs articulate, which is only in the thoracic region.","Intervertebral discs sit between the vertebral bodies and are held by their attachment to the bodies, not by facets. Students pick this because the facets and discs are both near the body.","The clavicles articulate with the manubrium of the sternum and the acromion of the scapula, never with the spine. Pick the clavicle for a question about the pectoral girdle."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-spine","lid":"d3-t-axial-skeleton-spine-6","comp":40,"competencyId":"w2-regional-vertebrae"},{"id":"L00663","dok":3,"q":"The sacrum forms from five vertebrae that fuse into one solid bone. Why is a fused bone an advantage at this location?","a":"It forms a stable base that passes body weight into the pelvis and legs.","options":["It lets the lower back twist more freely","It protects the spinal cord, which ends inside the sacrum","It forms a stable base that passes body weight into the pelvis and legs","It gives the floating ribs a place to attach"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The sacrum sits between the hip bones and transfers the weight of the entire upper body into the pelvic girdle at the sacroiliac joints. Movement here would make the base unstable, so five separate vertebrae fusing into one rigid bone gives the strength and stability this load transfer needs. Mobility is provided higher up in the column instead.","notes":["Fusion reduces movement rather than adding it. Choose free movement for a question about the separate vertebrae of the cervical or lumbar regions.","In adults the spinal cord ends near the first or second lumbar vertebra, so it does not reach the sacrum. The sacral canal carries nerve roots instead, and students pick this by assuming the cord runs the full length of the column.","Correct. Fusion creates a strong, stable base for weight transfer.","The floating ribs attach to the thoracic vertebrae T11 and T12 in back and to nothing in front. Pick them for a question about ribs with no anterior attachment."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-spine","lid":"d3-t-axial-skeleton-spine-7","comp":40,"competencyId":"w2-regional-vertebrae"},{"id":"L00664","dok":3,"q":"Why is it important that the vertebral foramina of stacked vertebrae line up with one another?","a":"Together they form a continuous bony tunnel that protects the spinal cord.","options":["Together they form a continuous bony tunnel that protects the spinal cord","Together they hold the intervertebral discs between the bodies","Together they form the openings where spinal nerves leave the column","Together they carry the vertebral arteries up to the brain"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Each vertebra contributes one ring around the spinal cord. When the rings line up, they form the vertebral canal, a continuous tunnel that shields the cord along its whole length, much as the skull guards the brain. If the openings were out of line, parts of the cord would be exposed or pinched.","notes":["Correct. The aligned foramina form the vertebral canal that protects the cord.","The discs sit between the vertebral bodies in front of the foramina and are not held by them. Students pick this because the discs also line up down the column.","Spinal nerves leave through the intervertebral foramina, the side gaps between vertebrae, not through the central foramina. Choose this for a question about intervertebral foramina.","The vertebral arteries climb through the transverse foramina of cervical vertebrae. Pick this for a question about transverse foramina."],"lt":"t-axial-skeleton-spine","lid":"d3-t-axial-skeleton-spine-8","comp":39,"competencyId":"w2-typical-vertebra"},{"id":"N-m2-spine-thorax-1","dok":2,"q":"An older patient has a rounded, hunched upper back from an exaggerated thoracic curve. Which abnormal curvature is this?","a":"Kyphosis.","options":["Scoliosis","Kyphosis","Lordosis","A herniated disc"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Kyphosis is an exaggerated thoracic curvature that rounds the upper back forward into a hunch. The thoracic curve is normal, but in kyphosis it is bent much more than usual. It is especially common in older adults whose vertebral bodies have weakened and compressed.","notes":["Scoliosis is a sideways curve of the spine to the left or right. Pick it for a question about a spine that bends to one side.","Correct. An exaggerated thoracic curve is kyphosis.","Lordosis is an exaggerated lumbar curve that makes the lower back sway inward. Choose it for a question about the lower back.","A herniated disc is a disc core pushing through its outer ring and pressing a nerve, not a curvature. It would be right for a question about pain shooting down a limb."],"comp":38,"competencyId":"w2-spine-regions"},{"id":"N-m2-spine-thorax-2","dok":2,"q":"A patient's lower back sways inward much more than normal because of an exaggerated lumbar curve. Which abnormal curvature is this?","a":"Lordosis.","options":["Kyphosis","A primary curvature","Scoliosis","Lordosis"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Lordosis is an exaggerated lumbar curvature, which deepens the normal inward curve of the lower back into a swayback. It can show up temporarily in late pregnancy, when the weight in front pulls the lower spine forward. The lumbar curve is normal; lordosis means it is curved too far.","notes":["Kyphosis is an exaggerated thoracic curve, a hunch in the upper back. Choose it for a question about the upper back.","A primary curvature is a normal curve present at birth, the thoracic or sacral curve, not an abnormality. The lumbar curve is actually secondary, so this does not fit either way.","Scoliosis is a sideways curve, not a front-to-back exaggeration. Pick it for a question about a spine that curves to one side.","Correct. An exaggerated lumbar curve is lordosis."],"comp":38,"competencyId":"w2-spine-regions"},{"id":"N-m2-spine-thorax-3","dok":2,"q":"Which joint lets you shake your head no?","a":"The atlanto-axial joint.","options":["The temporomandibular joint","The atlanto-axial joint","The sternoclavicular joint","The atlanto-occipital joint"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The atlanto-axial joint lies between the atlas (C1) and the axis (C2). The atlas, carrying the skull, pivots around the dens of the axis, turning the head left and right. Its name combines the two vertebrae that meet there.","notes":["The temporomandibular joint is the jaw joint between the mandible and the temporal bone. Pick it for a question about chewing or opening the mouth.","Correct. The atlanto-axial joint turns the head side to side.","The sternoclavicular joint joins the clavicle to the manubrium of the sternum. It would be right for a question about the joint linking the upper limb to the axial skeleton.","The atlanto-occipital joint is between the atlas and the skull, and it rocks the head forward and back. Choose it for a question about nodding yes."],"comp":40,"competencyId":"w2-regional-vertebrae"},{"id":"N-a17d-38-1","dok":3,"q":"An infant can now hold up its head but has not yet stood or walked. Which curvatures does its spine have, and which one is still missing?","a":"Thoracic, sacral, and cervical; the lumbar curve is still missing.","options":["Only thoracic and sacral; cervical and lumbar are missing.","Cervical and lumbar; thoracic and sacral are missing.","Thoracic, sacral, and lumbar; the cervical curve is missing.","Thoracic, sacral, and cervical; the lumbar curve is still missing."],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Sort the curves by when they appear. The thoracic and sacral curvatures are primary and present from birth. The cervical curvature develops as the infant holds up its head, and the lumbar curvature develops only once the child stands and walks, so it has not formed yet.","notes":["This would be true at birth. Holding up the head is the milestone that produces the cervical curvature.","The thoracic and sacral curves are primary, present from birth, so they cannot be missing.","This reverses the two secondary curves. The cervical curve comes with head control, and the lumbar curve comes with walking.","Correct. The thoracic and sacral curves are present from birth, the cervical forms with head control, and the lumbar waits for walking."],"comp":38,"competencyId":"w2-spine-regions"},{"id":"N-a17d-38-2","dok":3,"q":"In lab, one loose bone is five fused vertebrae wedged between the hip bones, and another is four fused vertebrae. Which regions are they, and how do they explain 26 adult bones instead of 33 vertebrae?","a":"Sacrum and coccyx; 9 vertebrae fuse into 2 bones.","options":["Lumbar region and coccyx; 9 vertebrae fuse into 2 bones.","Sacrum and coccyx; 9 vertebrae fuse into 2 bones.","Sacrum and coccyx; 7 vertebrae fuse into 1 bone.","Sacrum and lumbar region; 5 lumbar vertebrae fuse."],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Identify the bones first. Five fused vertebrae between the hip bones form the sacrum, and four fused vertebrae form the coccyx. Those nine vertebrae become just two bones, so 33 vertebrae become 24 separate vertebrae plus the sacrum and coccyx, which is 26.","notes":["The lumbar region has 5 separate vertebrae that do not fuse. The fused bone between the hip bones is the sacrum.","Correct. Five sacral and four coccygeal vertebrae fuse into 2 bones, so 33 becomes 26.","The bones are right but the count is off. Five sacral plus four coccygeal is 9 vertebrae, forming 2 bones.","The lumbar vertebrae stay separate in the adult. The second fused bone is the coccyx, the tailbone."],"comp":38,"competencyId":"w2-spine-regions"},{"id":"N-a17d-40-1","dok":3,"q":"In lab, a student is handed a vertebra with a hole in each transverse process and a bony peg rising from its body. Which vertebra is it, and which joint does the peg form?","a":"The axis (C2); its dens forms the atlanto-axial joint.","options":["The atlas (C1); its dens forms the atlanto-occipital joint.","The axis (C2); its dens forms the atlanto-occipital joint.","The vertebra prominens (C7); its peg forms the atlanto-axial joint.","The axis (C2); its dens forms the atlanto-axial joint."],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Use one feature at a time. A hole in each transverse process is a transverse foramen, which places the bone in the cervical region. The upward peg is the dens, which only the axis has, and the atlas pivots around it at the atlanto-axial joint to turn the head side to side.","notes":["The atlas is a ring with no body, so it has no peg. The atlanto-occipital joint is where the atlas meets the occipital condyles for the yes nod.","The bone is right, but the dens pivots in the atlas at the atlanto-axial joint. The atlanto-occipital joint is between the atlas and the skull.","C7 is cervical, but it is known for a long spinous process at the base of the neck. Only the axis carries the dens.","Correct. Transverse foramina make it cervical, and the upward peg, the dens, makes it the axis."],"comp":40,"competencyId":"w2-regional-vertebrae"},{"id":"N-a17d-41-1","dok":3,"q":"Starting at the rib that sits level with the sternal angle, a clinician counts down five more ribs. Which rib is reached, and how does it attach in front?","a":"Rib 7, a true rib with its own costal cartilage to the sternum.","options":["Rib 7, a false rib joined to the costal cartilage of the rib above.","Rib 8, a false rib joined to the costal cartilage of the rib above.","Rib 6, a floating rib with no anterior attachment at all.","Rib 7, a true rib with its own costal cartilage to the sternum."],"correctIndex":3,"why":"First locate the start. The sternal angle sits level with the second rib, so counting five more ribs down reaches rib 7. Ribs 1 to 7 are true ribs, each attached directly to the sternum by its own costal cartilage.","notes":["The count is right, but rib 7 is the last true rib. Only ribs 8 to 10 join the costal cartilage of the rib above.","The sternal angle is level with rib 2, not rib 3, so five more ribs ends at rib 7.","Only ribs 11 and 12 are floating ribs. Rib 6 is a true rib, and the count reaches rib 7 anyway.","Correct. The sternal angle is level with rib 2, five more ribs down is rib 7, and ribs 1 to 7 are true ribs."],"comp":41,"competencyId":"w2-thoracic-cage"},{"id":"N-a17d-41-2","dok":3,"q":"A patient fractures rib 11. A student worries the broken rib will pull on the sternum. Why is that not a concern?","a":"Rib 11 is a floating rib with no anterior attachment.","options":["Rib 11 is a true rib whose own cartilage absorbs the pull.","Rib 11 is a floating rib with no anterior attachment.","Rib 11 joins the costal cartilage of rib 10 instead.","Rib 11 attaches to the xiphoid process, which is cartilage."],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Place the rib in its group, then recall that group's attachment. Ribs 11 and 12 are the floating ribs, a subset of the false ribs, and they have no anterior attachment at all. With no link to the sternum, a break in rib 11 cannot pull on it.","notes":["True ribs are pairs 1 to 7. Rib 11 is a floating rib with no costal link to the sternum.","Correct. Ribs 11 and 12 are floating ribs and do not attach to anything in front.","Only ribs 8 to 10 join the cartilage of the rib above. Rib 11 has no anterior attachment.","The xiphoid is the inferior tip of the sternum and a muscle attachment. Rib 11 has no anterior attachment at all, to the xiphoid or anywhere else."],"comp":41,"competencyId":"w2-thoracic-cage"},{"id":"N-a17d-41-3","dok":3,"q":"A clinician feels the central dip at the top of the breastbone, then slides a finger down to the first ridge. Which part of the sternum was crossed, and which rib lies level with the ridge?","a":"The manubrium; rib 2 lies level with the ridge.","options":["The manubrium; rib 2 lies level with the ridge.","The body; rib 2 lies level with the ridge.","The manubrium; rib 1 lies level with the ridge.","The xiphoid process; rib 7 lies level with the ridge."],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Start with the landmarks. The central dip is the jugular notch on top of the manubrium, and the first ridge below it is the sternal angle, where the manubrium meets the body. The sternal angle sits level with the second rib, the starting point for counting ribs.","notes":["Correct. The dip is the jugular notch at the top of the manubrium, and the ridge is the sternal angle, level with rib 2.","The body is the long middle part below the sternal angle. The part between the jugular notch and the ridge is the manubrium.","The manubrium does articulate with the first ribs, but the sternal angle sits level with the second rib.","The xiphoid process is the small inferior tip of the sternum, at the opposite end from the jugular notch."],"comp":41,"competencyId":"w2-thoracic-cage"}]},{"id":"m2-upper-extremity","title":"Pectoral girdle and upper limb bones","summary":"clavicle, scapula, humerus, radius, ulna, carpals, metacarpals, phalanges and markings","mw":"2026-09-23","tr":"2026-09-22","cards":[{"id":"L00665","dok":1,"q":"Which two bones make up the pectoral girdle?","a":"The clavicle and the scapula.","options":["The clavicle and the sternum","The humerus and the scapula","The radius and the ulna","The clavicle and the scapula"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The pectoral girdle, also called the shoulder girdle, is the clavicle in front and the scapula in back. Together they attach the upper limb to the trunk. The clavicle connects to the sternum, and the scapula holds the socket for the humerus.","notes":["The sternum belongs to the axial skeleton, not the girdle, although the clavicle articulates with it. Choose this pair for a question about the sternoclavicular joint.","The humerus is the bone of the arm, which hangs from the girdle rather than forming part of it. This pair would describe the bones that meet at the shoulder joint.","The radius and ulna are the two bones of the forearm. Pick them for a question about the bones between the elbow and the wrist.","Correct. The clavicle and scapula form the pectoral girdle."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-upper","lid":"c1","comp":42,"competencyId":"w2-pectoral-girdle"},{"id":"L00666","dok":1,"q":"What is the common name for the clavicle?","a":"The collarbone.","options":["The breastbone","The funny bone","The collarbone","The shoulder blade"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The clavicle is the collarbone, an S-shaped bone that runs horizontally across the top of the thorax from the sternum to the shoulder. It lies just under the skin, so you can feel its whole length. It acts as a strut that holds the shoulder out from the body.","notes":["The breastbone is the sternum, the flat midline bone at the front of the chest. Pick it for a question about the bone the clavicle attaches to medially.","The funny bone is not a bone at all but the spot behind the medial epicondyle of the humerus where the ulnar nerve runs. It would fit a question about the jolt felt in the little finger.","Correct. The clavicle is the collarbone.","The shoulder blade is the scapula, the flat triangular bone on the upper back. Choose it for a question about the posterior bone of the pectoral girdle."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-upper","lid":"c2","comp":42,"competencyId":"w2-pectoral-girdle"},{"id":"L00667","dok":1,"q":"Which bone is the single bone of the arm, from shoulder to elbow?","a":"The humerus.","options":["The ulna","The humerus","The radius","The scapula"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"In anatomy, the arm (brachium) is only the part between the shoulder and the elbow, and it has a single bone, the humerus. The forearm, between the elbow and the wrist, has two bones. Keeping arm and forearm separate is important for naming muscles and bones correctly.","notes":["The ulna is the medial bone of the forearm, on the little-finger side. Pick it for a question about the bone that forms the point of the elbow.","Correct. The humerus is the one bone of the arm.","The radius is the lateral bone of the forearm, between the elbow and the wrist. Choose it for a question about the thumb side of the forearm.","The scapula is the shoulder blade, part of the pectoral girdle rather than the arm. It would be right for a question about the bone holding the shoulder socket."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-upper","lid":"c3","comp":43,"competencyId":"w2-arm-forearm"},{"id":"L00668","dok":1,"q":"How many carpal bones form the wrist?","a":"Eight.","options":["Fourteen","Eight","Seven","Five"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The wrist, or carpus, is made of eight small carpal bones arranged in two rows of four. The proximal row meets the radius, and the distal row meets the metacarpals of the palm. Many small bones gliding against each other let the wrist move smoothly in several directions.","notes":["Fourteen is the number of phalanges in one hand. Pick it for a question about the finger bones.","Correct. There are eight carpal bones.","Seven is the number of tarsal bones in the ankle and foot. Students mix up the carpals and tarsals because both are short bones of the wrist and ankle.","Five is the number of metacarpals, the bones of the palm. Choose five for a question about the palm."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-upper","lid":"c4","comp":44,"competencyId":"w2-hand-bones"},{"id":"L00669","dok":1,"q":"What is the shallow socket on the scapula that receives the head of the humerus?","a":"The glenoid cavity.","options":["The coracoid process","The glenoid cavity","The olecranon fossa","The acromion"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The glenoid cavity is a shallow, oval socket on the lateral angle of the scapula. The rounded head of the humerus fits into it to form the glenohumeral joint, the shoulder joint. Because the socket is so shallow, the shoulder moves freely but dislocates more easily than deeper joints.","notes":["The coracoid process is a hook-like projection on the front of the scapula used for muscle attachment. It would be right for a question about that anterior hook.","Correct. The glenoid cavity is the shoulder socket for the humeral head.","The olecranon fossa is a deep pit on the back of the distal humerus that receives the olecranon of the ulna. Choose it for a question about the elbow.","The acromion is the flat process at the tip of the scapular spine, the point of the shoulder. Pick it for a question about where the clavicle meets the scapula."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-upper","lid":"c5","comp":42,"competencyId":"w2-pectoral-girdle"},{"id":"L00670","dok":1,"q":"Which bony marking is the ridge running across the posterior surface of the scapula?","a":"The spine of the scapula.","options":["The coracoid process","The spine of the scapula","The medial border","The acromion"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The spine of the scapula is a prominent ridge that crosses the back of the shoulder blade at an angle. It divides the posterior surface into a smaller hollow above (supraspinous fossa) and a larger hollow below (infraspinous fossa). It ends laterally as the flattened acromion.","notes":["The coracoid process is a hook-like projection on the front of the scapula. Pick it for a question about the anterior hook used as a muscle attachment.","Correct. The spine is the ridge across the posterior scapula.","The medial border is the edge of the scapula closest to the vertebral column. It would be right for a question about the vertebral edge of the triangle.","The acromion is the flattened lateral end of the spine, forming the point of the shoulder. Choose it for a question about the tip of the spine."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-upper","lid":"c6","comp":42,"competencyId":"w2-pectoral-girdle"},{"id":"L00671","dok":1,"q":"What is the rounded proximal end of the humerus called?","a":"The head.","options":["The trochlea","The capitulum","The deltoid tuberosity","The head"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The head of the humerus is the smooth, rounded ball at its proximal end. It faces medially into the glenoid cavity of the scapula, forming the ball of the ball-and-socket shoulder joint. In bone-marking vocabulary, a head is a bony expansion carried on a neck.","notes":["The trochlea is the spool-shaped surface at the distal end that meets the ulna. Pick it for a question about the hinge surface of the elbow.","The capitulum is a small rounded knob at the distal end of the humerus that meets the radius. Choose it for a question about the elbow end of the humerus.","The deltoid tuberosity is a roughened area on the shaft where the deltoid muscle attaches. It would be right for a question about a muscle attachment on the middle of the bone.","Correct. The head is the rounded proximal end."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-upper","lid":"c7","comp":43,"competencyId":"w2-arm-forearm"},{"id":"L00672","dok":1,"q":"How many phalanges are in one human hand?","a":"Fourteen.","options":["Ten","Fourteen","Fifteen","Eight"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The thumb has two phalanges (proximal and distal), and each of the other four fingers has three (proximal, middle, and distal). That gives 2 plus 12, or fourteen phalanges per hand. The thumb is the one digit missing a middle phalanx.","notes":["Ten would be right only if every digit had two phalanges. Students reach it by giving all the fingers the thumb's count.","Correct. Two in the thumb and three in each finger make fourteen.","Fifteen would be right only if the thumb had three phalanges like the other fingers. Students pick it by forgetting that the thumb lacks a middle phalanx.","Eight is the number of carpal bones in the wrist. Choose it for a question about the wrist."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-upper","lid":"c8","comp":44,"competencyId":"w2-hand-bones"},{"id":"L00673","dok":1,"q":"What is the deep posterior pit on the distal humerus that receives the ulna when the elbow straightens?","a":"The olecranon fossa.","options":["The olecranon fossa","The coronoid fossa","The intertubercular sulcus","The supraspinous fossa"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The olecranon fossa is a deep hollow on the back of the distal humerus. When the elbow straightens, the olecranon, the hook-shaped tip of the ulna, swings into this pit. The bone-on-bone fit stops the elbow from extending too far.","notes":["Correct. The olecranon fossa receives the olecranon when the elbow extends.","The coronoid fossa is a smaller pit on the front of the distal humerus that receives the coronoid process of the ulna when the elbow bends. Choose it for a question about elbow flexion.","The intertubercular sulcus is a groove near the top of the humerus between the two tubercles. It would be right for a question about where a biceps tendon runs.","The supraspinous fossa is a hollow on the scapula above its spine. Pick it for a question about the posterior shoulder blade."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-upper","lid":"c9","comp":43,"competencyId":"w2-arm-forearm"},{"id":"L00674","dok":1,"q":"Which bones form the palm of the hand?","a":"The metacarpals.","options":["The metacarpals","The phalanges","The tarsals","The carpals"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The five metacarpals are the long bones inside the palm, one leading to each digit. Meta means beyond, so the metacarpals are the bones beyond the carpus (wrist). Their rounded distal heads form the knuckles when you make a fist.","notes":["Correct. The metacarpals form the palm.","The phalanges are the bones of the fingers and thumb, distal to the palm. Pick them for a question about the digits.","The tarsals are the bones of the ankle and back of the foot. Students mix them up with carpals, but tarsals belong to the lower limb.","The carpals are the eight small bones of the wrist, proximal to the palm. Choose them for a question about the wrist."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-upper","lid":"c10","comp":44,"competencyId":"w2-hand-bones"},{"id":"L00675","dok":1,"q":"What is the anatomical name for the thumb?","a":"The pollex.","options":["The hallux","The pollex","The carpus","The pisiform"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The pollex is the anatomical name for the thumb, digit 1 of the hand. It has only two phalanges, a proximal and a distal. Its metacarpal meets the trapezium at a saddle joint, which lets the thumb swing across the palm to touch the fingertips.","notes":["The hallux is the great toe, digit 1 of the foot. Choose it for a question about the big toe.","Correct. The pollex is the thumb.","The carpus is the wrist as a whole, the region made by the eight carpal bones. It would be right for a question about the wrist.","The pisiform is a small pea-shaped carpal bone on the little-finger side of the wrist. Pick it for a question about the proximal row of carpals."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-upper","lid":"c11","comp":44,"competencyId":"w2-hand-bones"},{"id":"L00676","dok":1,"q":"Which carpal bones make up the proximal row of the wrist?","a":"Scaphoid, lunate, triquetrum, and pisiform.","options":["Scaphoid, lunate, triquetrum, and pisiform","Trapezium, trapezoid, capitate, and hamate","Trapezium, lunate, pisiform, and hamate","Scaphoid, capitate, hamate, and lunate"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The proximal row, the row closer to the forearm, is the scaphoid, lunate, triquetrum, and pisiform, listed from the thumb side to the little-finger side. The mnemonic So Long To Pinky covers this row. The scaphoid and lunate are the two that meet the radius.","notes":["Correct. Scaphoid, lunate, triquetrum, and pisiform form the proximal row.","Trapezium, trapezoid, capitate, and hamate are the distal row, the row next to the metacarpals. Choose this set for a question about the distal row.","This set also mixes rows, pairing the trapezium and hamate from the distal row with the lunate and pisiform from the proximal row. It does not match either row.","This set mixes two proximal bones (scaphoid and lunate) with two distal bones (capitate and hamate). Students make this mistake when they remember the names but not which row each belongs to."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-upper","lid":"c12","comp":44,"competencyId":"w2-hand-bones"},{"id":"L00677","dok":1,"q":"What is the roughened area on the humeral shaft where the deltoid muscle attaches?","a":"The deltoid tuberosity.","options":["The deltoid tuberosity","The greater tubercle","The radial tuberosity","The lateral epicondyle"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The deltoid tuberosity is a rough, raised area on the lateral side of the middle of the humeral shaft. The deltoid, the muscle that caps the shoulder, inserts there. In bone-marking vocabulary, a tuberosity is a large, rough projection where a muscle attaches.","notes":["Correct. The deltoid tuberosity is on the humeral shaft.","The greater tubercle is a bump on the lateral side of the proximal humerus, near the head. Choose it for a question about the lateral bump near the shoulder.","The radial tuberosity is on the radius just below its neck, not on the humerus. It would be right for a question about a muscle attachment on the proximal radius.","The lateral epicondyle is a projection at the distal end of the humerus, above the capitulum. Pick it for a question about the outer elbow."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-upper","lid":"c13","comp":43,"competencyId":"w2-arm-forearm"},{"id":"L00678","dok":2,"q":"The clavicle is the only bony link in which connection?","a":"The upper limb to the axial skeleton.","options":["The radius to the ulna","The first rib to the sternum","The scapula to the humerus","The upper limb to the axial skeleton"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The clavicle is the sole bony strut connecting the upper limb to the axial skeleton. Its medial end joins the manubrium of the sternum, and its lateral end joins the acromion of the scapula. The scapula itself is held to the trunk only by muscles, which is why the shoulder is so mobile.","notes":["The radius and ulna connect to each other at the radioulnar joints and by the interosseous membrane. Pick this pair for a question about the forearm.","The first rib connects to the manubrium by its own costal cartilage, not through the clavicle. This would fit a question about how the first rib attaches to the sternum.","The scapula and humerus connect directly at the glenohumeral (shoulder) joint without the clavicle. Choose this pair for a question about the shoulder joint.","Correct. The clavicle is the only bony link between the upper limb and the axial skeleton."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-upper","lid":"c14","comp":42,"competencyId":"w2-pectoral-girdle"},{"id":"L00679","dok":2,"q":"The sternal end of the clavicle articulates with which structure?","a":"The manubrium of the sternum.","options":["The body of the sternum","The manubrium of the sternum","The coracoid process of the scapula","The acromion of the scapula"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The sternal end is the medial end of the clavicle. It meets the manubrium, the top part of the sternum, at the sternoclavicular joint. This is the only joint that connects the upper limb to the axial skeleton.","notes":["The body of the sternum is the long middle part, below the manubrium, where ribs 2 to 7 attach. Students pick it because it is also part of the sternum, but the clavicle meets only the manubrium.","Correct. The sternal end meets the manubrium at the sternoclavicular joint.","The coracoid process is a hook on the front of the scapula that anchors ligaments and muscles but does not form a joint with the clavicle. Pick it for a question about the anterior hook of the scapula.","The acromion meets the acromial end, the lateral end of the clavicle, at the acromioclavicular joint. Choose it for a question about the lateral end."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-upper","lid":"c15","comp":42,"competencyId":"w2-pectoral-girdle"},{"id":"L00680","dok":2,"q":"Which marking on the distal humerus articulates with the radius?","a":"The capitulum.","options":["The olecranon fossa","The medial epicondyle","The trochlea","The capitulum"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The capitulum is a small rounded knob on the lateral side of the distal humerus. Capitulum means little head, and the disc-shaped head of the radius rests against it. In anatomical position the radius is lateral, so it pairs with the lateral articular surface.","notes":["The olecranon fossa is a pit on the back of the humerus that receives the olecranon of the ulna. It would be right for a question about elbow extension.","The medial epicondyle is a projection above the trochlea for muscle attachment, not a joint surface. Pick it for a question about the funny bone or the medial elbow bump.","The trochlea is the spool-shaped surface on the medial side that articulates with the ulna. Choose it for a question about the ulna's partner at the elbow.","Correct. The capitulum articulates with the radius."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-upper","lid":"c16","comp":43,"competencyId":"w2-arm-forearm"},{"id":"L00681","dok":2,"q":"Which marking on the distal humerus articulates with the ulna?","a":"The trochlea.","options":["The capitulum","The greater tubercle","The lateral epicondyle","The trochlea"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The trochlea is a spool-shaped surface on the medial side of the distal humerus. Trochlea means pulley. The trochlear notch of the ulna wraps around it to form the hinge of the elbow, which bends and straightens in one plane.","notes":["The capitulum is the rounded lateral knob that articulates with the radius. Choose it for a question about the radius at the elbow.","The greater tubercle is at the proximal end of the humerus near the shoulder. It would be right for a question about a lateral muscle attachment near the head.","The lateral epicondyle is a projection above the capitulum for muscle attachment, not an articular surface. Pick it for a question about the outer elbow bump.","Correct. The trochlea articulates with the ulna."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-upper","lid":"c17","comp":43,"competencyId":"w2-arm-forearm"},{"id":"L00682","dok":2,"q":"In anatomical position, on which side of the forearm does the radius lie?","a":"The lateral side, the thumb side.","options":["The medial side, the little-finger side","The anterior side, in front of the ulna","The lateral side, the thumb side","The posterior side, behind the ulna"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"In anatomical position, with the palms facing forward, the radius lies on the lateral side of the forearm, in line with the thumb. The ulna lies on the medial side, in line with the little finger. The two bones sit side by side, so they are described as lateral and medial rather than front and back.","notes":["The medial, little-finger side is where the ulna lies. Choose this side for a question about the ulna.","The radius is not anterior to the ulna in anatomical position; the two bones are parallel and side by side. Front and back positions would only describe the pronated forearm.","Correct. The radius is lateral, on the thumb side.","In anatomical position the radius and ulna lie side by side, not one behind the other. Students pick a front or back position when they picture the forearm turned palm down, where the radius crosses over the ulna."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-upper","lid":"c18","comp":43,"competencyId":"w2-arm-forearm"},{"id":"L00683","dok":2,"q":"The trochlear notch of the ulna grips which marking of the humerus?","a":"The trochlea.","options":["The medial epicondyle","The olecranon fossa","The capitulum","The trochlea"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The trochlear notch is a deep C-shaped curve on the proximal ulna, between the olecranon and the coronoid process. It wraps around the spool-shaped trochlea of the humerus like a wrench around a bolt. This tight fit forms the hinge of the elbow.","notes":["The medial epicondyle is a projection for muscle attachment and does not form a joint surface. Pick it for a question about the medial elbow bump.","The olecranon fossa receives the olecranon when the elbow straightens, but it is a pit for the tip of the ulna, not the surface the notch wraps around. Choose it for a question about elbow extension.","The capitulum meets the head of the radius, not the ulna. Choose it for a question about the radius at the elbow.","Correct. The trochlear notch grips the trochlea."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-upper","lid":"c19","comp":43,"competencyId":"w2-arm-forearm"},{"id":"L00684","dok":2,"q":"Which scapular process forms the point of the shoulder at the lateral tip of the spine?","a":"The acromion.","options":["The acromion","The superior angle","The coracoid process","The glenoid cavity"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The acromion is the flattened lateral end of the scapular spine. It forms the bony point at the top of the shoulder that you can feel, and it articulates with the lateral end of the clavicle at the acromioclavicular joint. Acromion means the highest point of the shoulder.","notes":["Correct. The acromion is the point of the shoulder.","The superior angle is the upper medial corner of the scapular triangle, near the neck. It would be right for a question about the corners of the scapula.","The coracoid process is a hook that projects forward from the front of the scapula, below the clavicle. Choose it for a question about the anterior hook used for muscle attachment.","The glenoid cavity is the shallow socket for the head of the humerus, not a process. Pick it for a question about the shoulder joint socket."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-upper","lid":"c20","comp":42,"competencyId":"w2-pectoral-girdle"},{"id":"L00685","dok":2,"q":"The head of the radius articulates with which two structures at the elbow?","a":"The capitulum of the humerus and the radial notch of the ulna.","options":["The olecranon fossa and the coronoid process","The trochlea of the humerus and the trochlear notch of the ulna","The capitulum of the humerus and the ulnar notch of the radius","The capitulum of the humerus and the radial notch of the ulna"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The disc-shaped head of the radius touches two bones. Its top surface rests against the capitulum of the humerus, and its rim spins against the radial notch on the side of the ulna. The joint with the radial notch is the proximal radioulnar joint, which lets the radius rotate when the palm turns.","notes":["The olecranon fossa and coronoid process are humerus and ulna features that take part in extension and flexion, not radial head joints. Pick them for a question about the ulna's movement at the elbow.","The trochlea and trochlear notch are the surfaces where the humerus and ulna meet, forming the elbow hinge. Choose this pair for a question about the ulna's joint with the humerus.","The ulnar notch is on the radius itself, at the wrist, and it receives the head of the ulna. Students pick it because the names mirror each other, but the radial head meets the radial notch.","Correct. The radial head meets the capitulum and the radial notch of the ulna."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-upper","lid":"c21","comp":43,"competencyId":"w2-arm-forearm"},{"id":"L00686","dok":2,"q":"Which carpal bone articulates with the first metacarpal at the base of the thumb?","a":"The trapezium.","options":["The trapezoid","The scaphoid","The trapezium","The capitate"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The trapezium is the distal-row carpal on the thumb side. It meets the base of the first metacarpal at the carpometacarpal joint of the thumb, a saddle joint that lets the thumb swing across the palm. A helpful memory link is that the trapeziUM sits under the thUMb.","notes":["The trapezoid sits next to the trapezium but articulates with the second metacarpal, the index finger's. Pick it for a question about the base of the index finger.","The scaphoid is in the proximal row on the thumb side and meets the radius, not a metacarpal. Choose it for a question about the most commonly fractured carpal.","Correct. The trapezium meets the first metacarpal.","The capitate is the largest carpal, in the center of the distal row, and it meets the third metacarpal. It would be right for a question about the largest carpal bone."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-upper","lid":"c22","comp":44,"competencyId":"w2-hand-bones"},{"id":"L00687","dok":2,"q":"Which forearm bone has a styloid process on the medial side of the wrist?","a":"The ulna.","options":["The humerus","The ulna","The radius","The scaphoid"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Both forearm bones end in a pointed styloid process at the wrist. The ulna is the medial bone, so its styloid process is on the medial, little-finger side, where you can feel a bump. The radius has its styloid process on the lateral, thumb side.","notes":["The humerus is the bone of the arm and ends at the elbow, not the wrist. Pick it for a question about the epicondyles or capitulum.","Correct. The ulna has the medial styloid process.","The radius also has a styloid process, but on the lateral, thumb side of the wrist. Choose the radius for a question about the lateral styloid process.","The scaphoid is a carpal bone of the wrist, not a forearm bone, and it has no styloid process. It would be right for a question about the most frequently fractured carpal."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-upper","lid":"c23","comp":43,"competencyId":"w2-arm-forearm"},{"id":"L00688","dok":2,"q":"Which list names the phalanges of the index finger in order from the palm to the fingertip?","a":"Proximal, middle, distal.","options":["Proximal, distal, middle","Proximal, middle, distal","Distal, middle, proximal","Proximal and distal only"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Each finger other than the thumb has three phalanges named by their position. The proximal phalanx is closest to the palm, the middle phalanx is next, and the distal phalanx forms the fingertip. Proximal means nearer to the trunk and distal means farther from it.","notes":["This order places the distal phalanx in the middle. Distal means farthest from the trunk, so the distal phalanx must be at the tip.","Correct. Proximal, middle, then distal from palm to fingertip.","This is the correct list run in reverse, from fingertip to palm. It would be right if the question asked for the order starting at the fingertip.","Only proximal and distal describes the thumb, which lacks a middle phalanx. Choose this for a question about the phalanges of the thumb."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-upper","lid":"c24","comp":44,"competencyId":"w2-hand-bones"},{"id":"L00689","dok":2,"q":"The intertubercular sulcus of the humerus lies between which two markings?","a":"The greater tubercle and the lesser tubercle.","options":["The greater tubercle and the lesser tubercle","The head and the anatomical neck","The medial and lateral epicondyles","The capitulum and the trochlea"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The intertubercular sulcus is a groove on the front of the proximal humerus. Its name tells you where it is: inter means between and tubercular refers to the tubercles, so it runs between the greater and lesser tubercles. A tendon of the biceps brachii slides in this groove, which is also called the bicipital groove.","notes":["Correct. The sulcus runs between the greater and lesser tubercles.","The anatomical neck is a narrow groove just below the head, not a groove between two bumps. It would be right for a question about where the head meets the rest of the proximal humerus.","The epicondyles are projections at the distal end of the humerus, with no groove named for lying between them. Pick them for a question about muscle attachments at the elbow.","The capitulum and trochlea are the two articular surfaces at the distal end of the humerus. Choose them for a question about where the radius and ulna meet the humerus."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-upper","lid":"c25","comp":43,"competencyId":"w2-arm-forearm"},{"id":"L00690","dok":2,"q":"Which hollow of the scapula lies on its anterior surface, facing the ribs?","a":"The subscapular fossa.","options":["The subscapular fossa","The supraspinous fossa","The glenoid cavity","The infraspinous fossa"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The subscapular fossa is the broad, shallow hollow covering most of the anterior surface of the scapula, the side that faces the ribs. Sub means under, and this surface lies underneath the scapula against the thoracic wall. The subscapularis muscle fills it.","notes":["Correct. The subscapular fossa is on the anterior surface.","The supraspinous fossa is on the posterior surface, above the scapular spine. Choose it for a question about the hollow above the spine.","The glenoid cavity is the shallow socket at the lateral angle that holds the head of the humerus. It would be right for a question about the shoulder joint.","The infraspinous fossa is on the posterior surface, below the scapular spine. Pick it for a question about the larger hollow below the spine."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-upper","lid":"c26","comp":42,"competencyId":"w2-pectoral-girdle"},{"id":"L00691","dok":2,"q":"The ulnar notch of the radius receives which structure to form the distal radioulnar joint?","a":"The head of the ulna.","options":["The styloid process of the ulna","The head of the radius","The head of the ulna","The olecranon"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The ulnar notch is a small concave surface on the medial side of the distal radius. It is named for the bone that fits into it: the head of the ulna, which is at the ulna's distal (wrist) end. This distal radioulnar joint lets the radius swing around the ulna when the palm turns over.","notes":["The ulnar styloid process is a pointed projection beside the ulnar head, not the part that sits in the notch. Pick it for a question about the medial bump at the wrist.","The head of the radius is at the elbow end and fits into the radial notch of the ulna, forming the proximal radioulnar joint. Choose it for a question about the elbow end.","Correct. The head of the ulna fits into the ulnar notch at the wrist.","The olecranon is at the proximal end of the ulna, forming the point of the elbow. It would be right for a question about the elbow."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-upper","lid":"c27","comp":43,"competencyId":"w2-arm-forearm"},{"id":"L00692","dok":2,"q":"The metacarpals are numbered 1 to 5. Where does the numbering begin?","a":"At the thumb side.","options":["At the little-finger side","At the thumb side","At the index finger","At the middle finger"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Metacarpals are numbered from lateral to medial in anatomical position, starting with the thumb as number 1 and ending with the little finger as number 5. The digits follow the same numbering, so the thumb is digit 1. This matches the lab list, which numbers metacarpals lateral to medial.","notes":["The little finger is metacarpal 5, the last in the series, not the first. Students start there when they forget that numbering runs from lateral to medial in anatomical position.","Correct. Numbering starts at the thumb, which is metacarpal 1.","The index finger is metacarpal 2, the one that meets the trapezoid. Students pick it because they think of the thumb as separate from the fingers.","The middle finger is metacarpal 3, in the center of the hand. Choose it for a question about which metacarpal meets the capitate."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-upper","lid":"c28","comp":44,"competencyId":"w2-hand-bones"},{"id":"L00693","dok":2,"q":"Which marking is the narrow groove just below the head of the humerus?","a":"The anatomical neck.","options":["The anatomical neck","The intertubercular sulcus","The deltoid tuberosity","The surgical neck"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The anatomical neck is a slight groove right at the edge of the humeral head, marking where the smooth joint surface ends. It sits above the greater and lesser tubercles. The surgical neck, by contrast, is a narrowing below the tubercles where the shaft begins.","notes":["Correct. The anatomical neck is the groove just below the head.","The intertubercular sulcus is a groove between the two tubercles, running down the shaft rather than around the head. It would be right for a question about where a biceps tendon runs.","The deltoid tuberosity is a rough area on the middle of the shaft. Pick it for a question about the deltoid muscle attachment.","The surgical neck is lower, the narrowing below the tubercles, and it is a common fracture site. Choose it for a question about where the humerus most often breaks near the shoulder."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-upper","lid":"c29","comp":43,"competencyId":"w2-arm-forearm"},{"id":"L00694","dok":2,"q":"In lab, a student points to a rounded knob on the lateral side of the distal humerus where a forearm bone meets it. Which marking is the student pointing to?","a":"The capitulum.","options":["The greater tubercle","The lateral epicondyle","The trochlea","The capitulum"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The capitulum is the rounded knob on the lateral half of the distal humerus. The head of the radius meets it at the elbow. Fixing the humerus in anatomical position first makes the pairing easy: the capitulum and radius are both lateral, and the trochlea and ulna are both medial.","notes":["The greater tubercle is at the proximal end of the humerus near the shoulder. It would be right for a question about a lateral bump near the head.","The lateral epicondyle is a roughened projection just above the capitulum, used for muscle attachment rather than as a joint surface. Pick it for a question about the outer elbow bump.","The trochlea is the spool-shaped surface on the medial side that meets the ulna. Choose it for a medial articular surface.","Correct. The lateral rounded knob that meets the radius is the capitulum."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-upper","lid":"c30","comp":43,"competencyId":"w2-arm-forearm"},{"id":"L00695","dok":2,"q":"Starting at the elbow and moving toward the wrist along the thumb side of the forearm, which bone are you following?","a":"The radius.","options":["The first metacarpal","The ulna","The humerus","The radius"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The radius is the lateral forearm bone, which means it lies on the thumb side in anatomical position. It is narrow at the elbow, where its disc-shaped head sits, and wide at the wrist, where it forms most of the joint with the carpals. Tracing the thumb side from elbow to wrist follows the radius the whole way.","notes":["The first metacarpal is in the hand, beyond the wrist, in line with the thumb. It would be right for a path from the wrist to the base of the thumb.","The ulna runs along the medial, little-finger side of the forearm. Choose it for a path along the little-finger side.","The humerus is the arm bone, proximal to the elbow, so it does not run between the elbow and the wrist. Pick it for a path from the shoulder to the elbow.","Correct. The thumb side of the forearm is the radius."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-upper","lid":"c31","comp":43,"competencyId":"w2-arm-forearm"},{"id":"L00696","dok":2,"q":"A student feels the bony point at the very tip of the elbow when the arm is straight. Which marking is this?","a":"The olecranon of the ulna.","options":["The radial tuberosity","The olecranon of the ulna","The coronoid process of the ulna","The medial epicondyle of the humerus"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The olecranon is the large, hook-shaped proximal end of the ulna, and it forms the point of the elbow you lean on. When the elbow straightens, the olecranon fits into the olecranon fossa on the back of the humerus. It lies just under the skin, so it is easy to feel.","notes":["The radial tuberosity is a roughened bump on the front of the proximal radius, buried under muscle. It would be right for a question about a muscle attachment just below the radial neck.","Correct. The olecranon forms the point of the elbow.","The coronoid process is on the front of the proximal ulna, so it cannot be felt at the back tip of the elbow. Choose it for a question about the anterior lip that fits into the coronoid fossa during flexion.","The medial epicondyle is a bump on the inner side of the elbow, off to the side of the point. Pick it for a question about the funny bone or the medial elbow bump."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-upper","lid":"c32","comp":43,"competencyId":"w2-arm-forearm"},{"id":"L00697","dok":2,"q":"Which hollow lies above the spine on the posterior surface of the scapula?","a":"The supraspinous fossa.","options":["The infraspinous fossa","The glenoid cavity","The supraspinous fossa","The subscapular fossa"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The spine of the scapula divides its back surface into two hollows. Supra means above, so the supraspinous fossa is the smaller hollow above the spine. The supraspinatus muscle, one of the rotator cuff muscles, fills it.","notes":["The infraspinous fossa is the larger hollow below the spine. Choose it for a question using infra, meaning below.","The glenoid cavity is the socket for the head of the humerus, not a hollow on the back of the blade. It would be right for a question about the shoulder joint.","Correct. The supraspinous fossa is above the spine.","The subscapular fossa is on the anterior surface facing the ribs. Pick it for a question about the front of the scapula."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-upper","lid":"c33","comp":42,"competencyId":"w2-pectoral-girdle"},{"id":"L00698","dok":2,"q":"A hook-like process projects forward from the upper scapula, below the clavicle, and serves as a muscle attachment. Which marking is it?","a":"The coracoid process.","options":["The spine of the scapula","The coracoid process","The lateral border","The acromion"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The coracoid process is a curved, finger-like hook that juts forward from the top of the scapula, just below the lateral clavicle. Its name means shaped like a crow's beak. Several muscles and ligaments of the shoulder and chest attach to it.","notes":["The spine of the scapula is a ridge across the posterior surface, not a hook that points forward. Pick it for a question about the ridge dividing the back of the blade.","Correct. The coracoid process is the forward-pointing hook.","The lateral border is the edge of the scapula nearest the armpit, also called the axillary border. It would be right for a question about the edges of the scapular triangle.","The acromion is the flat process at the lateral tip of the spine, forming the point of the shoulder. Choose it for a question about where the scapula meets the lateral clavicle."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-upper","lid":"c34","comp":42,"competencyId":"w2-pectoral-girdle"},{"id":"L00699","dok":2,"q":"An X-ray shows a fracture across the humerus at the narrowing just below the greater and lesser tubercles. Which marking is the fracture site?","a":"The surgical neck.","options":["The anatomical neck","The intertubercular sulcus","The deltoid tuberosity","The surgical neck"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The surgical neck is the narrowing of the humerus just below the tubercles, where the wider proximal end tapers into the shaft. It is named surgical because it fractures so often, especially in falls. The anatomical neck is higher, directly at the edge of the head.","notes":["The anatomical neck is the groove right below the head, above the tubercles. Choose it for a question about the groove marking the edge of the head.","The intertubercular sulcus is the groove running between the tubercles, not a narrowing below them. It would be right for a question about the biceps tendon groove.","The deltoid tuberosity is a rough area farther down the shaft, on the lateral side. Pick it for a question about the deltoid attachment.","Correct. The narrowing below the tubercles is the surgical neck."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-upper","lid":"c35","comp":43,"competencyId":"w2-arm-forearm"},{"id":"L00700","dok":2,"q":"After a fall on an outstretched hand, an X-ray shows a broken carpal bone in the proximal row on the thumb side. Which bone is it?","a":"The scaphoid.","options":["The pisiform","The trapezium","The lunate","The scaphoid"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The scaphoid is the proximal-row carpal on the thumb side, and it is the carpal fractured most often. In a fall on an outstretched hand, force passes from the palm through the scaphoid to the radius, so it takes the brunt. Its name means boat-shaped.","notes":["The pisiform is in the proximal row on the little-finger side, the opposite side from the thumb. It would be right for a question about the small pea-shaped carpal.","The trapezium is on the thumb side but in the distal row, where it meets the first metacarpal. Pick it for a question about the base of the thumb.","The lunate is also in the proximal row but sits in the middle, next to the scaphoid. Choose it for a question about the carpal most often dislocated.","Correct. The scaphoid is the proximal-row carpal on the thumb side and the most often fractured."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-upper","lid":"c36","comp":44,"competencyId":"w2-hand-bones"},{"id":"L00701","dok":2,"q":"Which forearm marking is the disc-shaped proximal end that articulates with the capitulum?","a":"The head of the radius.","options":["The olecranon of the ulna","The coronoid process of the ulna","The radial tuberosity","The head of the radius"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The head of the radius is a flat, disc-shaped end at the top of the radius. Its upper surface rests against the capitulum of the humerus, and its rim turns inside the radial notch of the ulna. That disc shape lets the radius spin in place when the forearm turns the palm up or down.","notes":["The olecranon is the hook-shaped proximal end of the ulna at the back of the elbow, which fits the olecranon fossa. Choose it for a question about the point of the elbow.","The coronoid process is the front lip of the proximal ulna that helps grip the trochlea. It would be right for a question about the anterior ulna at the elbow.","The radial tuberosity is a rough bump just below the radial neck where a muscle attaches, not a joint surface. Pick it for a question about a muscle attachment on the radius.","Correct. The disc-shaped head of the radius meets the capitulum."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-upper","lid":"c37","comp":43,"competencyId":"w2-arm-forearm"},{"id":"L00702","dok":2,"q":"Which marking is the medial projection of the distal humerus, just above the trochlea?","a":"The medial epicondyle.","options":["The medial epicondyle","The lesser tubercle","The lateral epicondyle","The coronoid process"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The medial epicondyle is a large bump on the inner side of the distal humerus, above the spool-shaped trochlea. Epicondyle means above a condyle, a raised area beside a joint surface. It serves as a muscle attachment, and the ulnar nerve passes just behind it.","notes":["Correct. The medial epicondyle sits above the trochlea.","The lesser tubercle is at the proximal end of the humerus near the head, not near the elbow. Pick it for a question about the anterior bump near the shoulder.","The lateral epicondyle is the projection on the outer side, above the capitulum. Choose it for a question about the lateral bump of the distal humerus.","The coronoid process belongs to the ulna, not the humerus. It would be right for a question about the front lip of the proximal ulna."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-upper","lid":"c38","comp":43,"competencyId":"w2-arm-forearm"},{"id":"L00703","dok":2,"q":"On the proximal ulna, a lip projects forward and fits into a small pit on the humerus when the elbow bends. Which marking is it?","a":"The coronoid process.","options":["The coronoid process","The styloid process","The radial notch","The olecranon"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The coronoid process is the anterior lip of the proximal ulna, forming the lower edge of the trochlear notch. When the elbow bends fully, it fits into the coronoid fossa on the front of the humerus. The olecranon does the matching job at the back when the elbow straightens.","notes":["Correct. The coronoid process fits the coronoid fossa during flexion.","The ulnar styloid process is at the distal end, at the wrist, not the elbow. Pick it for a question about the medial bump of the wrist.","The radial notch is a smooth surface on the lateral side of the proximal ulna that holds the head of the radius. It would be right for a question about the proximal radioulnar joint.","The olecranon is the posterior hook of the proximal ulna, which fits the olecranon fossa when the elbow straightens. Choose it for a question about elbow extension."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-upper","lid":"c39","comp":43,"competencyId":"w2-arm-forearm"},{"id":"L00704","dok":2,"q":"Following the medial side of the forearm from elbow to wrist, which bone are you tracking, and which marking forms its elbow end?","a":"The ulna, with the olecranon at its elbow end.","options":["The ulna, with the olecranon at its elbow end","The radius, with its head at the elbow end","The ulna, with its styloid process at its elbow end","The humerus, with the lateral epicondyle at its elbow end"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The ulna is the medial forearm bone, on the little-finger side. Its proximal end is large and hook-shaped, with the olecranon forming the point of the elbow. The ulna is wide at the elbow and narrow at the wrist, the opposite of the radius.","notes":["Correct. The medial forearm bone is the ulna, and the olecranon forms its elbow end.","The radius and its disc-shaped head are on the lateral, thumb side. Choose this pairing for a path along the thumb side of the forearm.","The ulna is the right bone, but its styloid process is at the wrist end, not the elbow end. Students mix up the two ends when they remember the markings but not where each sits.","The humerus is the arm bone, above the elbow, and the lateral epicondyle is on its outer side. Pick it for a question about the outer bump of the distal humerus."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-upper","lid":"c40","comp":43,"competencyId":"w2-arm-forearm"},{"id":"L00705","dok":2,"q":"Which joint is formed where the acromion of the scapula meets the lateral end of the clavicle?","a":"The acromioclavicular joint.","options":["The glenohumeral joint","The sternoclavicular joint","The acromioclavicular joint","The sacroiliac joint"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Joints are often named for the two parts that meet, and the acromioclavicular joint joins the acromion with the clavicle. It sits at the top of the shoulder, where you can feel a small bump. A hard fall onto the point of the shoulder can injure this joint, often called a separated shoulder.","notes":["The glenohumeral joint is the shoulder joint proper, where the humeral head sits in the glenoid cavity. Pick it for a question about the ball-and-socket joint of the shoulder.","The sternoclavicular joint is at the medial end of the clavicle, where it meets the manubrium. Choose it for a question about the only joint linking the upper limb to the axial skeleton.","Correct. The acromion and clavicle meet at the acromioclavicular joint.","The sacroiliac joint joins the sacrum and the ilium in the pelvis. It would be right for a question about the auricular surface of the sacrum."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-upper","lid":"c41","comp":42,"competencyId":"w2-pectoral-girdle"},{"id":"L00706","dok":2,"q":"Which marking is the smaller anterior bump near the head of the humerus, serving as a muscle attachment?","a":"The lesser tubercle.","options":["The greater tubercle","The medial epicondyle","The deltoid tuberosity","The lesser tubercle"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The proximal humerus has two tubercles, small rounded bumps for muscle attachment. The lesser tubercle is the smaller one, facing forward (anteriorly), and the greater tubercle is the larger one on the lateral side. The intertubercular sulcus runs between them.","notes":["The greater tubercle is the larger bump on the lateral side of the proximal humerus. Choose it for a question about the lateral bump near the head.","The medial epicondyle is at the distal end of the humerus near the elbow. It would be right for a question about the inner elbow bump.","The deltoid tuberosity is on the middle of the shaft, well below the head. Pick it for a question about where the deltoid attaches.","Correct. The lesser tubercle is the anterior bump near the head."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-upper","lid":"c42","comp":43,"competencyId":"w2-arm-forearm"},{"id":"L00707","dok":2,"q":"Which carpal bone in the distal row lies between the trapezium and the capitate and meets the second metacarpal?","a":"The trapezoid.","options":["The hamate","The scaphoid","The trapezoid","The lunate"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The distal row runs trapezium, trapezoid, capitate, and hamate from the thumb side. The trapezoid is the small second bone in that row, wedged between the trapezium and the capitate. It meets the base of the second metacarpal, the one for the index finger.","notes":["The hamate is at the little-finger end of the distal row and has a hook. Choose it for a question about the medial-most distal carpal.","The scaphoid is in the proximal row, next to the radius. Pick it for a question about the carpal most often fractured.","Correct. The trapezoid lies between the trapezium and capitate.","The lunate is also in the proximal row, in the middle, and it is the carpal most often dislocated. It would be right for a question about that injury."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-upper","lid":"c43","comp":44,"competencyId":"w2-hand-bones"},{"id":"L00708","dok":1,"q":"Which three bones meet at the elbow?","a":"The humerus, radius, and ulna.","options":["The femur, tibia, and patella","The radius, ulna, and carpals","The humerus, radius, and ulna","The humerus, scapula, and clavicle"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The elbow is where the arm meets the forearm. The distal humerus meets the ulna at the trochlea and the radius at the capitulum, and the radius and ulna also meet each other at the proximal radioulnar joint. All three bones share one joint capsule.","notes":["The femur, tibia, and patella form the knee, the lower limb joint that matches the elbow's position. Students pick it because the knee and elbow are both hinge joints in the middle of a limb.","The radius, ulna, and carpals meet in the wrist region. Pick this set for a question about the wrist.","Correct. The humerus, radius, and ulna meet at the elbow.","The humerus, scapula, and clavicle meet in the shoulder region. Choose this set for a question about the bones around the shoulder."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-upper","lid":"c44","comp":43,"competencyId":"w2-arm-forearm"},{"id":"L00709","dok":2,"q":"Which list traces the bones of the upper limb in order from the shoulder joint to a fingertip, not counting the girdle?","a":"Humerus, radius and ulna, carpal, metacarpal, phalanges.","options":["Humerus, carpal, radius and ulna, metacarpal, phalanges","Humerus, radius and ulna, metacarpal, carpal, phalanges","Clavicle, humerus, radius, metacarpal, phalanges","Humerus, radius and ulna, carpal, metacarpal, phalanges"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The limb runs arm, forearm, wrist, palm, then fingers. That means the humerus first, then the radius and ulna, then a carpal bone of the wrist, then a metacarpal in the palm, and finally the phalanges of a finger. Meta means beyond, so the metacarpals come right after the carpals.","notes":["This list places the carpals before the forearm bones. The wrist lies between the forearm and the palm, so the carpals must come after the radius and ulna.","This list swaps the carpal and metacarpal. The carpals form the wrist and the metacarpals form the palm beyond them.","The clavicle belongs to the pectoral girdle, which the question excludes, and this list also skips the carpals. Choose a clavicle-first list only for a path that starts at the sternum.","Correct. Humerus, radius and ulna, carpal, metacarpal, then phalanges."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-upper","lid":"c45","comp":44,"competencyId":"w2-hand-bones"},{"id":"L00710","dok":2,"q":"Which list names the distal row of carpal bones in order from the thumb side to the little-finger side?","a":"Trapezium, trapezoid, capitate, hamate.","options":["Scaphoid, lunate, triquetrum, pisiform","Trapezoid, trapezium, capitate, hamate","Hamate, capitate, trapezoid, trapezium","Trapezium, trapezoid, capitate, hamate"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The distal row starts at the thumb with the trapezium, which meets the first metacarpal, then the trapezoid, then the large capitate, and ends with the hooked hamate on the little-finger side. The mnemonic Here Comes The Thumb gives the row in reverse, starting from the little-finger side: hamate, capitate, trapezoid, trapezium.","notes":["Scaphoid, lunate, triquetrum, and pisiform make up the proximal row, not the distal row. Choose this list for the proximal row.","This order swaps the trapezoid and trapezium. The trapezium is the one next to the thumb, so it comes first from the thumb side.","This is the correct row listed in the opposite direction, from the little-finger side to the thumb. It would be right if the question asked for that direction.","Correct. Trapezium, trapezoid, capitate, and hamate run from the thumb side."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-upper","lid":"c46","comp":44,"competencyId":"w2-hand-bones"},{"id":"L00711","dok":2,"q":"Which set correctly names the girdle bone that forms the shoulder joint, the bone of the arm, and the socket where they meet?","a":"The scapula, the humerus, and the glenoid cavity.","options":["The clavicle, the humerus, and the acromion","The scapula, the ulna, and the olecranon fossa","The clavicle, the radius, and the trochlear notch","The scapula, the humerus, and the glenoid cavity"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The shoulder joint, or glenohumeral joint, is where the head of the humerus sits in the glenoid cavity of the scapula. The scapula is the girdle bone that carries the socket, and the humerus is the single bone of the arm. The clavicle braces the shoulder but does not form part of this joint.","notes":["The clavicle meets the acromion at the acromioclavicular joint, but it does not hold the humeral head. Choose this set for a question about the joint at the top of the shoulder.","The ulna is a forearm bone, and the olecranon fossa is a pit on the humerus at the elbow. Pick this combination for a question about elbow extension.","The clavicle and radius never articulate, and the trochlear notch is on the ulna at the elbow. This set mixes bones from different regions, so it fits no single joint.","Correct. The scapula and humerus meet at the glenoid cavity."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-upper","lid":"c47","comp":42,"competencyId":"w2-pectoral-girdle"},{"id":"L00712","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly matches each forearm bone to the markings at its wrist end?","a":"The radius has a lateral styloid process and an ulnar notch; the ulna has a medial styloid process.","options":["Radius: lateral styloid process and ulnar notch. Ulna: medial styloid process","Radius: lateral styloid process and ulnar notch. Ulna: trochlear notch","Radius: lateral styloid process and radial notch. Ulna: medial styloid process","Radius: medial styloid process and radial notch. Ulna: lateral styloid process"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"At the wrist, each forearm bone ends in a styloid process on its own side: lateral for the radius and medial for the ulna. The radius also has an ulnar notch on its medial side, which receives the head of the ulna at the distal radioulnar joint. Notches are named for the bone that fits into them.","notes":["Correct. The radius carries the lateral styloid process and the ulnar notch, and the ulna carries the medial styloid process.","The trochlear notch is at the elbow end of the ulna, where it grips the trochlea of the humerus. Pick it for a question about the elbow hinge.","The radial notch is a feature of the proximal ulna that holds the head of the radius at the elbow. Choose it for a question about the proximal radioulnar joint.","This reverses the sides of the styloid processes and puts the radial notch on the radius. The radial notch is on the ulna at the elbow, and the radius is the lateral bone."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-upper","lid":"c48","comp":43,"competencyId":"w2-arm-forearm"},{"id":"L00713","dok":2,"q":"Which path correctly traces the bony connections from the manubrium of the sternum out to the shoulder socket?","a":"Manubrium to the sternal end of the clavicle, acromial end of the clavicle to the acromion, then the scapula's glenoid cavity.","options":["Manubrium to the acromial end of the clavicle, sternal end to the acromion, then the glenoid cavity","Manubrium to the sternal end of the clavicle, acromial end to the acromion, then the glenoid cavity","Manubrium to the sternal end of the clavicle, acromial end to the coracoid process, then the glenoid cavity","Manubrium to the scapula, scapula to the clavicle, then the clavicle's glenoid cavity"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The clavicle is the bridge. Its medial (sternal) end meets the manubrium at the sternoclavicular joint, and its lateral (acromial) end meets the acromion of the scapula at the acromioclavicular joint. The scapula then carries the glenoid cavity, the socket for the head of the humerus.","notes":["This swaps the two ends of the clavicle. The sternal end is medial and meets the sternum, while the acromial end is lateral and meets the acromion.","Correct. Manubrium, clavicle, acromion, then the glenoid cavity of the scapula.","The coracoid process is a hook on the front of the scapula. Ligaments tie it to the clavicle, but the clavicle forms its joint with the acromion, so choose the coracoid for a question about the anterior hook.","The scapula does not touch the manubrium; it reaches the trunk only through the clavicle and muscles. The glenoid cavity is also a scapular feature, not part of the clavicle."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-upper","lid":"c49","comp":42,"competencyId":"w2-pectoral-girdle"},{"id":"L00714","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares how the radius and ulna meet the humerus at the elbow?","a":"The ulna grips the trochlea, and the radius meets the capitulum.","options":["Both bones grip the trochlea, and neither meets the capitulum","The ulna grips the trochlea, and the radius meets the capitulum","The radius grips the trochlea, and the ulna meets the capitulum","The ulna grips the capitulum, and the radius meets the olecranon fossa"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"At the elbow the medial ulna wraps its trochlear notch around the spool-shaped trochlea, forming the hinge. The lateral radius rests its disc-shaped head against the rounded capitulum. In anatomical position the medial bone pairs with the medial surface and the lateral bone with the lateral surface.","notes":["Only the ulna grips the trochlea; the radius has its own partner, the capitulum. Students pick this when they remember the hinge but forget the radius also touches the humerus.","Correct. The ulna grips the trochlea, and the radius meets the capitulum.","This swaps the partners. The radius is lateral and meets the lateral capitulum, while the ulna is medial and grips the medial trochlea.","The ulna does not touch the capitulum, and the olecranon fossa receives the ulna's olecranon, not the radius. This mixes up both bones' partners."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-upper","lid":"c50","comp":43,"competencyId":"w2-arm-forearm"},{"id":"L00715","dok":1,"q":"In anatomical terms, which region of the upper limb spans from the elbow to the wrist?","a":"The forearm.","options":["The forearm","The pectoral girdle","The hand","The arm"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Anatomy divides the upper limb into four parts: the pectoral girdle, the arm (shoulder to elbow), the forearm (elbow to wrist), and the hand. The forearm, or antebrachium, contains the radius and ulna. In everyday speech arm can mean the whole limb, but in anatomy it means only the part with the humerus.","notes":["Correct. The forearm spans the elbow to the wrist.","The pectoral girdle is the clavicle and scapula, which attach the limb to the trunk. It would be right for a question about the shoulder girdle.","The hand spans the wrist to the fingertips and contains the carpals, metacarpals, and phalanges. Pick it for a question about the region beyond the wrist.","The arm, or brachium, spans the shoulder to the elbow and contains only the humerus. Choose it for a question about the region above the elbow."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-upper","lid":"c3-x2","comp":43,"competencyId":"w2-arm-forearm"},{"id":"L00716","dok":2,"q":"What is the fibrous sheet that spans the shafts of the radius and ulna, binding them together?","a":"The interosseous membrane.","options":["The periosteum","The interosseous membrane","The flexor retinaculum","The articular cartilage"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The interosseous membrane is a sheet of dense connective tissue stretched between the shafts of the radius and ulna. Interosseous means between bones. It holds the two forearm bones together as a fibrous joint, a syndesmosis, and gives forearm muscles a broad surface for attachment.","notes":["The periosteum is the membrane covering the outer surface of each bone. It wraps a single bone rather than connecting two, so choose it for a question about the bone's outer covering.","Correct. The interosseous membrane joins the radius and ulna along their shafts.","The flexor retinaculum is a fibrous band across the front of the wrist that roofs the carpal tunnel. Choose it for a question about the carpal tunnel.","Articular cartilage is the smooth hyaline cartilage on joint surfaces at the ends of bones, not a sheet between shafts. Pick it for a question about the lining of a synovial joint."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-upper","lid":"c4-x2","comp":43,"competencyId":"w2-arm-forearm"},{"id":"L00717","dok":1,"q":"Which forearm bone lies on the medial, little-finger side?","a":"The ulna.","options":["The radius","The humerus","The fibula","The ulna"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"In anatomical position, the ulna lies on the medial side of the forearm, in line with the little finger. The radius lies on the lateral, thumb side. The ulna is the bone that forms the point of the elbow with its olecranon.","notes":["The radius is the lateral forearm bone on the thumb side. Choose it for a question about the thumb side.","The humerus is the bone of the arm, above the elbow, not a forearm bone. Pick it for a question about the region from the shoulder to the elbow.","The fibula is the lateral bone of the leg, in the lower limb. Students mix it up with the ulna because both are the thinner partner in a two-bone limb segment.","Correct. The ulna is on the medial, little-finger side."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-upper","lid":"c5-x2","comp":43,"competencyId":"w2-arm-forearm"},{"id":"L00718","dok":1,"q":"Which border of the scapula runs closest and parallel to the vertebral column?","a":"The medial (vertebral) border.","options":["The superior border","The medial (vertebral) border","The inferior angle","The lateral (axillary) border"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The scapula is a triangle with three borders. The medial border faces the spine, which is why it is also called the vertebral border. The lateral border faces the armpit (axilla) and is called the axillary border, and the short superior border runs along the top.","notes":["The superior border is the short top edge of the scapula, near the suprascapular notch. Pick it for a question about the upper edge of the triangle.","Correct. The medial border is the vertebral border closest to the spine.","The inferior angle is the lowest point of the triangle, where the medial and lateral borders meet, not a border itself. It would be right for a question about the bottom tip of the scapula.","The lateral border faces the armpit and runs down from the glenoid cavity. Choose it for a question about the axillary border."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-upper","lid":"c7-x2","comp":42,"competencyId":"w2-pectoral-girdle"},{"id":"L00719","dok":1,"q":"How are the eight carpal bones arranged in the wrist?","a":"In two rows of four.","options":["In one row of eight","In four rows of two","In a proximal row of five and a distal row of three","In two rows of four"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The carpals form two rows of four. The proximal row (scaphoid, lunate, triquetrum, pisiform) meets the forearm, and the distal row (trapezium, trapezoid, capitate, hamate) meets the metacarpals. The mnemonic So Long To Pinky, Here Comes The Thumb lists the proximal row and then the distal row.","notes":["One row of eight would leave no separation between the bones meeting the forearm and those meeting the palm. The carpals are always described in two rows.","Four rows of two does not match the wrist; there are only a proximal and a distal row. Students pick it when they remember the numbers two and four but swap them.","A split of five and three mixes up the rows. Each row has exactly four bones, so a five and three split comes from misplacing one bone.","Correct. The carpals sit in two rows of four."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-upper","lid":"c8-x2","comp":44,"competencyId":"w2-hand-bones"},{"id":"L00720","dok":1,"q":"How many metacarpal bones form the palm of one hand?","a":"Five.","options":["Eight","Fourteen","Three","Five"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"There are five metacarpals, one leading to each digit, numbered 1 to 5 starting at the thumb. They lie inside the palm between the carpals and the phalanges. Their rounded heads form the knuckles of a closed fist.","notes":["Eight is the number of carpal bones in the wrist. Pick it for a question about the wrist.","Fourteen is the number of phalanges in one hand. It would be right for a question about the finger bones.","Three is the number of phalanges in each finger other than the thumb. Choose it for a question about the bones in one finger.","Correct. Five metacarpals form the palm."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-upper","lid":"c9-x2","comp":44,"competencyId":"w2-hand-bones"},{"id":"L00721","dok":2,"q":"Which carpal bone, in the middle of the proximal row, is the one most frequently dislocated?","a":"The lunate.","options":["The lunate","The capitate","The hamate","The scaphoid"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The lunate is a moon-shaped carpal in the center of the proximal row, between the scaphoid and the triquetrum. It is the carpal most frequently dislocated, usually pushed toward the palm, where it can crowd the carpal tunnel. Lunate means moon-shaped.","notes":["Correct. The lunate is the carpal most frequently dislocated.","The capitate is the largest carpal, in the middle of the distal row. Pick it for a question about the largest carpal bone.","The hamate is at the little-finger end of the distal row and has a hook-shaped process. It would be right for a question about the hooked carpal.","The scaphoid is the proximal-row carpal on the thumb side, and it is the one most often fractured rather than dislocated. Choose it for a question about the most common carpal fracture."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-upper","lid":"c10-x2","comp":44,"competencyId":"w2-hand-bones"},{"id":"L00722","dok":1,"q":"Which marking is the larger lateral bump near the head of the humerus, a muscle attachment?","a":"The greater tubercle.","options":["The lateral epicondyle","The lesser tubercle","The greater tubercle","The capitulum"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The greater tubercle is the larger of the two bumps at the proximal humerus, sitting on the lateral side just beyond the head. Several rotator cuff muscles attach to it. It forms the rounded outer contour of the shoulder below the acromion.","notes":["The lateral epicondyle is on the lateral side but at the distal end, near the elbow. Pick it for a question about the outer bump of the elbow.","The lesser tubercle is the smaller bump on the anterior side near the head. Choose it for a question about the anterior tubercle.","Correct. The greater tubercle is the larger lateral bump near the head.","The capitulum is the rounded joint surface at the distal end that meets the radius. It would be right for a question about the elbow joint surfaces."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-upper","lid":"c12-x2","comp":43,"competencyId":"w2-arm-forearm"},{"id":"L00723","dok":1,"q":"Which roughened bump on the radius, just below its neck, serves as a muscle attachment?","a":"The radial tuberosity.","options":["The radial tuberosity","The styloid process","The ulnar notch","The deltoid tuberosity"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The radial tuberosity is a rough, oval bump on the medial front of the proximal radius, just below the neck. The tendon of the biceps brachii attaches there. As with all tuberosities, its rough surface marks where a strong tendon pulls on the bone.","notes":["Correct. The radial tuberosity lies just below the neck of the radius.","The radial styloid process is at the distal end of the radius, on the lateral side of the wrist. Pick it for a question about the lateral wrist bump.","The ulnar notch is a joint surface at the distal radius that receives the head of the ulna. It would be right for a question about the distal radioulnar joint.","The deltoid tuberosity is on the shaft of the humerus, not the radius. Choose it for a question about the deltoid's attachment."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-upper","lid":"c13-x2","comp":43,"competencyId":"w2-arm-forearm"},{"id":"L00724","dok":1,"q":"Which carpal bone is the largest?","a":"The capitate.","options":["The capitate","The scaphoid","The pisiform","The lunate"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The capitate is the largest carpal bone. It sits in the center of the distal row, between the trapezoid and the hamate, and its rounded head fits into a hollow formed by the scaphoid and lunate. It lines up with the third metacarpal, the long bone of the middle finger.","notes":["Correct. The capitate is the largest carpal.","The scaphoid is the carpal most often fractured, not the largest. Pick it for a question about a fall on an outstretched hand.","The pisiform is the smallest carpal, a pea-shaped bone on the little-finger side of the proximal row. Choose it for a question about the smallest carpal.","The lunate is the carpal most frequently dislocated. It would be right for a question about that injury."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-upper","lid":"c14-x2","comp":44,"competencyId":"w2-hand-bones"},{"id":"L00725","dok":1,"q":"Which small bump on the scapula sits just above the glenoid cavity?","a":"The supraglenoid tubercle.","options":["The infraglenoid tubercle","The supraglenoid tubercle","The coracoid process","The superior angle"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The supraglenoid tubercle is a small bump at the top edge of the glenoid cavity. Supra means above, so its name tells you its position relative to the socket. The tendon of the long head of the biceps brachii attaches there.","notes":["The infraglenoid tubercle is the bump just below the glenoid cavity. Choose it for a question using infra, meaning below.","Correct. The supraglenoid tubercle is just above the glenoid cavity.","The coracoid process is a larger hook that projects forward from the scapula, medial to the glenoid cavity. Pick it for a question about the anterior hook below the clavicle.","The superior angle is the upper medial corner of the scapula, far from the glenoid cavity. It would be right for a question about the corners of the scapular triangle."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-upper","lid":"c15-x2","comp":42,"competencyId":"w2-pectoral-girdle"},{"id":"L00726","dok":2,"q":"The acromial end of the clavicle articulates with which structure?","a":"The acromion of the scapula.","options":["The coracoid process of the scapula","The manubrium of the sternum","The acromion of the scapula","The head of the humerus"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The acromial end is the flattened lateral end of the clavicle. As its name suggests, it meets the acromion of the scapula, forming the acromioclavicular joint at the top of the shoulder. The medial end does the opposite job, meeting the manubrium.","notes":["The coracoid process lies just below the lateral clavicle and is tied to it by strong ligaments, but it does not form the joint. Pick it for a question about the anterior hook of the scapula.","The manubrium meets the sternal, or medial, end of the clavicle. Choose it for a question about the sternal end.","Correct. The acromial end meets the acromion.","The head of the humerus sits in the glenoid cavity of the scapula and never touches the clavicle. It would be right for a question about the shoulder joint."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-upper","lid":"c16-x2","comp":42,"competencyId":"w2-pectoral-girdle"},{"id":"L00727","dok":2,"q":"Which small pit on the anterior distal humerus, just above the capitulum, receives the head of the radius when the elbow bends fully?","a":"The radial fossa.","options":["The coronoid fossa","The radial groove","The radial fossa","The olecranon fossa"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The radial fossa is a shallow pit on the front of the humerus above the capitulum. When the elbow is fully bent, the rim of the radial head moves up into it. It is the lateral partner of the coronoid fossa, which sits above the trochlea and receives the ulna.","notes":["The coronoid fossa is the pit above the trochlea, on the medial side of the front of the humerus, and it receives the coronoid process of the ulna. Choose it for the ulna during elbow flexion.","The radial groove is a shallow groove on the posterior shaft of the humerus where the radial nerve runs. It would be right for a question about that nerve's path, not a pit at the elbow.","Correct. The radial fossa receives the radial head during full flexion.","The olecranon fossa is the deep pit on the back of the humerus that receives the olecranon when the elbow straightens. Pick it for a question about elbow extension."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-upper","lid":"c17-x2","comp":43,"competencyId":"w2-arm-forearm"},{"id":"L00728","dok":1,"q":"Which projection on the lateral side of the distal humerus sits just above the capitulum?","a":"The lateral epicondyle.","options":["The medial epicondyle","The greater tubercle","The lateral epicondyle","The deltoid tuberosity"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The lateral epicondyle is a projection on the outer side of the distal humerus, just above the capitulum. It is smaller than the medial epicondyle and serves as an attachment for many forearm muscles. Each epicondyle sits above its matching articular surface: lateral above the capitulum, medial above the trochlea.","notes":["The medial epicondyle is on the inner side, above the trochlea. Choose it for a question about the medial elbow bump.","The greater tubercle is also lateral, but it is at the proximal end of the humerus near the shoulder. Pick it for a question about the lateral bump near the head.","Correct. The lateral epicondyle lies above the capitulum.","The deltoid tuberosity is on the lateral middle of the shaft, well above the elbow. It would be right for a question about the deltoid attachment."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-upper","lid":"c18-x2","comp":43,"competencyId":"w2-arm-forearm"},{"id":"L00729","dok":2,"q":"When the elbow bends fully, the coronoid process of the ulna fits into which pit on the humerus?","a":"The coronoid fossa.","options":["The olecranon fossa","The glenoid cavity","The coronoid fossa","The radial fossa"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The coronoid fossa is a small pit on the anterior distal humerus, just above the trochlea. When the elbow flexes, the coronoid process on the front of the ulna slides up into it. Each process of the proximal ulna has a matching fossa on the humerus: coronoid in front for flexion and olecranon behind for extension.","notes":["The olecranon fossa is on the back of the humerus and receives the olecranon when the elbow straightens. Choose it for a question about elbow extension.","The glenoid cavity is the shoulder socket on the scapula, not a pit at the elbow. It would be right for a question about where the humeral head sits.","Correct. The coronoid process fits the coronoid fossa during flexion.","The radial fossa is the lateral pit above the capitulum that receives the head of the radius during flexion. Pick it for a question about the radius."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-upper","lid":"c19-x2","comp":43,"competencyId":"w2-arm-forearm"},{"id":"L00730","dok":2,"q":"Which marking of the ulna wraps around the trochlea of the humerus to form the elbow hinge?","a":"The trochlear notch.","options":["The trochlear notch","The olecranon fossa","The radial notch","The ulnar notch"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The trochlear notch is a deep, C-shaped curve at the proximal end of the ulna, framed by the olecranon behind and the coronoid process in front. It hugs the spool-shaped trochlea, which lets the elbow bend and straighten in one plane. The notch is named for the structure that fits into it.","notes":["Correct. The trochlear notch grips the trochlea.","The olecranon fossa is a pit on the humerus, not a marking of the ulna. Pick it for a question about where the olecranon rests when the elbow straightens.","The radial notch is a smaller surface on the lateral side of the proximal ulna that holds the head of the radius. Choose it for a question about the proximal radioulnar joint.","The ulnar notch is on the distal radius at the wrist, where it receives the head of the ulna. It would be right for a question about the distal radioulnar joint."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-upper","lid":"c20-x2","comp":43,"competencyId":"w2-arm-forearm"},{"id":"L00731","dok":1,"q":"Which marking of the radius projects on the lateral, thumb side of the wrist?","a":"The styloid process of the radius.","options":["The head of the radius","The radial tuberosity","The ulnar notch","The styloid process of the radius"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The radial styloid process is a pointed projection at the distal end of the radius, on its lateral side. You can feel it on the thumb side of the wrist. The ulna has its own styloid process on the medial side, so each forearm bone has a styloid process on its own side.","notes":["The head of the radius is at the elbow end, where it meets the capitulum. It would be right for a question about the proximal radius.","The radial tuberosity is at the proximal end of the radius, near the elbow. Pick it for a question about a muscle attachment just below the radial neck.","The ulnar notch is also at the distal radius, but on the medial side, where it receives the head of the ulna. Choose it for a question about the distal radioulnar joint.","Correct. The radial styloid process is on the lateral side of the wrist."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-upper","lid":"c21-x2","comp":43,"competencyId":"w2-arm-forearm"},{"id":"L00732","dok":2,"q":"Which humeral marking is an oblique groove on the posterior shaft, along which the radial nerve runs?","a":"The radial groove.","options":["The anatomical neck","The radial groove","The olecranon fossa","The intertubercular sulcus"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The radial groove is a shallow groove that spirals obliquely across the back of the humeral shaft. The radial nerve runs in it, close to the bone. Because the nerve lies right against the bone here, a fracture of the shaft can injure it.","notes":["The anatomical neck is a groove just below the head, at the top of the bone, not along the shaft. Pick it for a question about the edge of the humeral head.","Correct. The radial nerve runs in the radial groove.","The olecranon fossa is a deep pit at the distal end of the posterior humerus. It would be right for a question about where the olecranon rests when the elbow straightens.","The intertubercular sulcus is a groove on the front of the proximal humerus, between the tubercles, holding a biceps tendon. Choose it for a question about that anterior groove."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-upper","lid":"c22-x2","comp":43,"competencyId":"w2-arm-forearm"},{"id":"L00733","dok":1,"q":"Which carpal bones make up the distal row of the wrist?","a":"Trapezium, trapezoid, capitate, and hamate.","options":["Trapezium, trapezoid, capitate, and hamate","Scaphoid, trapezoid, hamate, and triquetrum","Scaphoid, lunate, triquetrum, and pisiform","Trapezium, lunate, capitate, and pisiform"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The distal row is the row next to the metacarpals: trapezium, trapezoid, capitate, and hamate, from the thumb side. In the mnemonic So Long To Pinky, Here Comes The Thumb, the second half (Here Comes The Thumb) gives the distal row from the little-finger side. These bones meet the bases of the five metacarpals.","notes":["Correct. The trapezium, trapezoid, capitate, and hamate form the distal row.","This set also mixes rows, pairing the scaphoid and triquetrum from the proximal row with two distal bones. It does not match either row.","Scaphoid, lunate, triquetrum, and pisiform make up the proximal row, next to the forearm. Choose this set for the proximal row.","This set mixes rows, adding the lunate and pisiform from the proximal row. Students make this mistake when they recall the names without their positions."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-upper","lid":"c23-x2","comp":44,"competencyId":"w2-hand-bones"},{"id":"L00734","dok":2,"q":"How many phalanges does the thumb have, and how many does each of the other fingers have?","a":"The thumb has two, and each finger has three.","options":["The thumb has three, and each finger has two","The thumb and each finger have two","The thumb has two, and each finger has three","The thumb and each finger have three"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The thumb, or pollex, has only two phalanges, a proximal and a distal, because it lacks a middle phalanx. Each of the other four fingers has three: proximal, middle, and distal. That is why the hand has fourteen phalanges in total.","notes":["This reverses the counts. The thumb is the short digit with fewer phalanges, not more.","Giving every finger two would mean no finger has a middle phalanx. This count fits only the thumb.","Correct. Two in the thumb and three in each finger.","Giving the thumb three would mean it has a middle phalanx, which it does not. This would add up to fifteen phalanges per hand instead of fourteen."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-upper","lid":"c24-x2","comp":44,"competencyId":"w2-hand-bones"},{"id":"L00735","dok":1,"q":"Which hollow lies below the spine on the posterior surface of the scapula?","a":"The infraspinous fossa.","options":["The supraspinous fossa","The subscapular fossa","The glenoid cavity","The infraspinous fossa"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The spine of the scapula divides the back of the bone into two hollows. Infra means below, so the infraspinous fossa is the larger hollow beneath the spine. The infraspinatus muscle, one of the rotator cuff muscles, fills it.","notes":["The supraspinous fossa is the smaller hollow above the spine. Choose it for a question using supra, meaning above.","The subscapular fossa is on the anterior surface, facing the ribs. Pick it for a question about the front of the scapula.","The glenoid cavity is the shoulder socket at the lateral angle, not a hollow on the back of the blade. It would be right for a question about where the humeral head sits.","Correct. The infraspinous fossa lies below the spine."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-upper","lid":"c25-x2","comp":42,"competencyId":"w2-pectoral-girdle"},{"id":"L00736","dok":2,"q":"A cyclist falls onto an outstretched arm and breaks the slender S-shaped bone running across the top of the chest, most often in its middle third. Which bone is broken?","a":"The clavicle.","options":["The clavicle","The first rib","The humerus","The scapula"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The clavicle is a slender, S-shaped strut that runs from the sternum to the shoulder just under the skin. In a fall on the shoulder or an outstretched arm, force travels up the limb and into the clavicle, which often breaks in its middle third. It is one of the most commonly broken bones in the body.","notes":["Correct. The S-shaped bone across the top of the chest is the clavicle.","The first rib is a short, flat rib tucked behind the clavicle, not an S-shaped bone across the top of the chest. Pick it for a question about the rib that articulates with the manubrium.","The humerus is the long bone of the arm, which runs vertically, not across the chest. It would be right for a question about a fracture at the surgical neck or shaft.","The scapula is a flat triangular bone on the upper back, padded by thick muscle, so it rarely breaks in a simple fall. Choose it for a question about the shoulder blade."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-upper","lid":"c26-x2","comp":42,"competencyId":"w2-pectoral-girdle"},{"id":"L00737","dok":2,"q":"An older adult breaks the humerus near the shoulder, and the doctor checks for nerve injury because the axillary nerve runs right beside the break. Which part of the humerus is fractured?","a":"The surgical neck.","options":["The capitulum","The anatomical neck","The surgical neck","The deltoid tuberosity"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The surgical neck is the narrowing below the greater and lesser tubercles, and it is a common fracture site in falls. The axillary nerve wraps around the back of the humerus at this level, so a break here can stretch or injure it. That is why a surgical neck fracture is checked for nerve damage.","notes":["The capitulum is at the distal end of the humerus, at the elbow, not near the shoulder. It would be right for a question about where the radius meets the humerus.","The anatomical neck is the groove just below the head, above the tubercles, and it breaks much less often. Choose it for a question about the edge of the humeral head.","Correct. The axillary nerve runs beside the surgical neck.","The deltoid tuberosity is a muscle attachment on the middle of the shaft, below the level where the axillary nerve wraps the bone. Pick it for a question about where the deltoid inserts."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-upper","lid":"c27-x2","comp":43,"competencyId":"w2-arm-forearm"},{"id":"L00740","dok":3,"q":"A scaphoid fracture that is missed on the first X-ray may never heal properly. Which feature of the scaphoid explains this?","a":"Its blood supply is poor.","options":["It is a sesamoid bone inside a tendon","It sits in the distal row, away from the radius","It is the largest carpal, so it heals slowly","Its blood supply is poor"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Bone heals by bringing in blood, cells, and nutrients to the break. The scaphoid has a poor blood supply, and in many people the vessels enter only at one end, so a break can cut off part of the bone. Without treatment, that part may not heal and can even die, which is why scaphoid injuries are taken seriously.","notes":["The sesamoid carpal is the pisiform, a pea-shaped bone on the little-finger side. Pick it for a question about the smallest carpal.","The scaphoid is in the proximal row and meets the radius directly. Students pick this when they mix it up with the trapezium, the thumb-side bone of the distal row.","The largest carpal is the capitate, not the scaphoid, and size does not explain poor healing. Choose the capitate for a question about the largest carpal.","Correct. The scaphoid's poor blood supply limits healing."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-upper","lid":"c30-x2","comp":44,"competencyId":"w2-hand-bones"},{"id":"L00741","dok":3,"q":"The pectoral girdle attaches to the axial skeleton only at the small sternoclavicular joint, and the scapula is held mainly by muscles. What does this loose attachment give the upper limb?","a":"A wide range of motion at the shoulder.","options":["A wide range of motion at the shoulder","A weight-bearing base like the pelvic girdle","The most stable joint in the body","A scapula fixed tightly against the ribs"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"With only one small joint linking it to the trunk, the pectoral girdle can glide and tilt freely over the back of the rib cage. The scapula moves along with the arm, adding to the reach of the shoulder joint. The trade-off is less stability, which suits a limb built for reaching and handling objects rather than bearing weight.","notes":["Correct. The loose attachment gives the shoulder great mobility.","The pelvic girdle is the weight-bearing one, firmly joined to the sacrum at the sacroiliac joints. Choose a weight-bearing base for a question about the pelvic girdle.","The shoulder region is mobile, not highly stable; a loose girdle and a shallow socket make it prone to dislocation. Students pick this by assuming that important joints must be the most stable.","The scapula is not fixed; it slides over the ribs as the arm moves. A tightly fixed scapula would describe the opposite of how the pectoral girdle works."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-upper","lid":"d3-t-appendicular-skeleton-upper-1","comp":42,"competencyId":"w2-pectoral-girdle"},{"id":"L00742","dok":3,"q":"Why is the clavicle so prone to fracture in a fall onto an outstretched arm?","a":"It is a slender strut that carries force from the arm to the trunk.","options":["It is a slender strut that carries force from the arm to the trunk","It is made mostly of cartilage, which snaps easily","It bears the full weight of the trunk when standing","It is buried deep under thick muscle that pins it in place"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The clavicle holds the shoulder out from the body and is the only bony link between the arm and the axial skeleton. When a person lands on an outstretched hand, the force travels up the arm and through the clavicle toward the sternum. Because the bone is slender and lies just under the skin, it often snaps, usually in its middle third.","notes":["Correct. As the strut between arm and trunk, the clavicle takes the force of the fall.","The clavicle is bone, not cartilage. Students may confuse it with the costal cartilages nearby, which are hyaline cartilage connecting the ribs to the sternum.","The weight of the trunk is carried by the vertebral column and passed to the pelvis, not by the clavicle. Choose weight bearing for a question about the lumbar vertebrae or sacrum.","The clavicle lies just under the skin, which is why you can feel its whole length. Thick muscle padding describes bones like the scapula, which rarely break in a simple fall."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-upper","lid":"d3-t-appendicular-skeleton-upper-2","comp":42,"competencyId":"w2-pectoral-girdle"},{"id":"L00744","dok":3,"q":"The forearm has two parallel bones, the radius and ulna, instead of one. What movement does this two-bone design make possible?","a":"Turning the palm up and down as the radius rotates around the ulna.","options":["Tilting the wrist toward the thumb or little finger","Bending and straightening the elbow","Bringing the thumb across the palm to the fingertips","Turning the palm up and down as the radius rotates around the ulna"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Turning the palm down (pronation) and up (supination) happens when the radius rotates around the ulna at the proximal and distal radioulnar joints. When the palm turns down, the radius crosses over the ulna. A single forearm bone could not cross over itself, so this rotation depends on having two bones.","notes":["Tilting the wrist side to side happens at the wrist joint and between the carpal bones. It would be right for a question about how the carpals allow wrist motion.","Bending and straightening the elbow happens at the hinge between the humerus and ulna, which would work with one forearm bone. Choose this for a question about the humeroulnar joint.","Bringing the thumb to the fingertips is opposition, which depends on the saddle joint at the base of the thumb. Pick it for a question about the trapezium and first metacarpal.","Correct. Two bones allow the radius to rotate around the ulna."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-upper","lid":"d3-t-appendicular-skeleton-upper-4","comp":43,"competencyId":"w2-arm-forearm"},{"id":"L00746","dok":3,"q":"The wrist contains eight small carpal bones instead of one solid block. How does this design help the hand?","a":"Many small bones gliding on each other let the wrist bend and tilt smoothly while staying stable.","options":["The carpals produce the rotation that turns the palm up and down","Many small bones gliding on each other let the wrist bend and tilt smoothly while staying stable","Each carpal anchors one digit, so the hand needs eight","Many small bones lock together so the wrist cannot bend"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Each carpal glides a little against its neighbors, and those small movements add up across many joints. The result is a wrist that can bend forward and back and tilt side to side smoothly. Ligaments binding the bones keep the wrist stable under load at the same time.","notes":["Turning the palm up and down is done by the radius rotating around the ulna, not by the carpals. Choose that for a question about pronation and supination.","Correct. Shared gliding among many bones gives smooth, stable wrist motion.","There are five digits, each served by one metacarpal, not one carpal per digit. Pick five for a question about the metacarpals.","The carpals do the opposite of locking; their many small joints allow movement. Students pick this by assuming more bones means more rigidity."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-upper","lid":"d3-t-appendicular-skeleton-upper-6","comp":44,"competencyId":"w2-hand-bones"},{"id":"N-m2-upper-extremity-1","dok":2,"q":"Which joint is the only joint that links the upper limb to the axial skeleton?","a":"The sternoclavicular joint.","options":["The costovertebral joint","The sternoclavicular joint","The acromioclavicular joint","The glenohumeral joint"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The sternoclavicular joint is where the sternal end of the clavicle meets the manubrium of the sternum. The sternum is part of the axial skeleton, and the clavicle is part of the appendicular skeleton, so this is the single bony connection between the upper limb and the trunk. Everything else holding the shoulder in place is muscle.","notes":["Costovertebral joints connect the ribs to the thoracic vertebrae, both parts of the axial skeleton. They would be right for a question about where the rib heads meet the spine.","Correct. The sternoclavicular joint is the only joint between the upper limb and the axial skeleton.","The acromioclavicular joint joins the clavicle to the acromion, two bones that are both part of the pectoral girdle. Choose it for a question about the joint at the top of the shoulder.","The glenohumeral joint joins the humerus to the scapula, both appendicular bones. Pick it for a question about the shoulder joint proper."],"comp":42,"competencyId":"w2-pectoral-girdle"},{"id":"N-m2-upper-extremity-2","dok":1,"q":"Which bump on the scapula sits just below the glenoid cavity?","a":"The infraglenoid tubercle.","options":["The supraglenoid tubercle","The acromion","The inferior angle","The infraglenoid tubercle"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The infraglenoid tubercle is a roughened bump at the lower edge of the glenoid cavity, on the lateral border of the scapula. Infra means below, so the name places it under the socket. The long head of the triceps brachii attaches there.","notes":["The supraglenoid tubercle is the smaller bump just above the glenoid cavity. Choose it for a question using supra, meaning above.","The acromion is the flat process at the tip of the scapular spine, above and behind the glenoid cavity. It would be right for a question about the point of the shoulder.","The inferior angle is the lowest point of the whole scapula, far below the glenoid cavity. Pick it for a question about the bottom tip of the scapular triangle.","Correct. The infraglenoid tubercle lies just below the glenoid cavity."],"comp":42,"competencyId":"w2-pectoral-girdle"},{"id":"N-m2-upper-extremity-3","dok":1,"q":"In bone-marking vocabulary, what is a tuberosity?","a":"A large, rough projection.","options":["A shallow basin","A groove","A hole for vessels or nerves","A large, rough projection"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Every bone marking is a projection, a depression, or an opening. A tuberosity is a projection: a large, rough area where a strong tendon attaches, such as the deltoid tuberosity of the humerus or the radial tuberosity. A tubercle is the smaller, rounded version.","notes":["A shallow basin is a fossa, a type of depression, such as the olecranon fossa. Choose it for a question about a fossa.","A groove is a sulcus, a type of depression, such as the intertubercular sulcus. It would be right for a question about a sulcus.","A hole for vessels or nerves is a foramen, a type of opening, such as a nutrient foramen. Pick it for a question about a foramen.","Correct. A tuberosity is a large, rough projection."],"comp":45,"competencyId":"w2-bone-markings-vocab"},{"id":"N-m2-upper-extremity-4","dok":1,"q":"The olecranon fossa is an example of which kind of bone marking?","a":"A fossa, a shallow basin (a depression).","options":["A tubercle, a small rounded bump","A foramen, a hole for vessels or nerves","An epicondyle, a raised area above a condyle","A fossa, a shallow basin"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"A fossa is a depression, a basin-like hollow in a bone surface. The olecranon fossa on the back of the distal humerus is a deep one that holds the olecranon of the ulna when the elbow straightens. Other examples include the supraspinous and infraspinous fossae of the scapula.","notes":["A tubercle is a small rounded bump, a projection, such as the greater tubercle of the humerus. Choose it for a question about a small bump near the humeral head.","A foramen is an opening that passes completely through bone, such as a nutrient foramen. Pick it for a question about a hole for vessels or nerves.","An epicondyle is a projection above a condyle, such as the medial epicondyle of the humerus. It would be right for a question about the bumps on either side of the distal humerus.","Correct. The olecranon fossa is a fossa, a depression."],"comp":45,"competencyId":"w2-bone-markings-vocab"},{"id":"N-a17d-42-1","dok":3,"q":"On an articulated skeleton, a student follows the S-shaped clavicle out to the point of the shoulder. Which end of the clavicle is there, which scapular marking does it meet, and which joint is formed?","a":"The acromial end, at the acromion, forming the acromioclavicular joint.","options":["The sternal end, at the acromion, forming the sternoclavicular joint.","The acromial end, at the acromion, forming the acromioclavicular joint.","The acromial end, at the coracoid process, forming the glenohumeral joint.","The acromial end, at the glenoid cavity, forming the shoulder joint."],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Work outward from the midline. The medial sternal end meets the manubrium, and the lateral acromial end reaches the shoulder. The point of the shoulder is the acromion, the flat process at the tip of the scapular spine, and the joint between them is the acromioclavicular joint.","notes":["The sternal end is the medial end, which meets the manubrium at the sternoclavicular joint, not the shoulder.","Correct. The lateral, acromial end of the clavicle meets the acromion at the acromioclavicular joint.","The coracoid is the hook-like anterior process, and the glenohumeral joint is the shoulder joint between the humerus and glenoid cavity.","The glenoid cavity receives the head of the humerus, not the clavicle. The clavicle meets the acromion."],"comp":42,"competencyId":"w2-pectoral-girdle"},{"id":"N-a17d-43-1","dok":3,"q":"An X-ray shows a break through the projection just above the distal humeral surface that meets the ulna. Which marking is broken, and which nerve runs just behind it?","a":"The medial epicondyle, with the ulnar nerve behind it.","options":["The lateral epicondyle, with the ulnar nerve behind it.","The medial epicondyle, with the radial nerve behind it.","The lateral epicondyle, with the radial nerve behind it.","The medial epicondyle, with the ulnar nerve behind it."],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Find the surface first. The distal humeral surface that meets the ulna is the trochlea, the medial spool. The projection just above the trochlea is the medial epicondyle, and the ulnar nerve passes just behind it, the spot called the funny bone.","notes":["The lateral epicondyle sits above the capitulum, which meets the radius. The surface meeting the ulna is medial.","The marking is right, but the radial nerve runs in the radial groove on the posterior shaft. The ulnar nerve passes the medial epicondyle.","Both parts are off. The surface that meets the ulna is the medial trochlea, and the nerve behind the medial epicondyle is the ulnar nerve.","Correct. The trochlea meets the ulna, the medial epicondyle sits above it, and the ulnar nerve passes behind it."],"comp":43,"competencyId":"w2-arm-forearm"},{"id":"N-a17d-43-2","dok":3,"q":"In lab, a student holds a loose forearm bone. Its proximal end is a disc-shaped head, and its distal end has a styloid process on one side and a notch on the other. Which bone is it, and which humeral marking does its head meet?","a":"The radius; its head meets the capitulum.","options":["The radius; its head meets the capitulum.","The ulna; its head meets the trochlea.","The radius; its head meets the trochlea.","The ulna; its head meets the capitulum."],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Name the bone from its ends. A disc-shaped head at the proximal end and an ulnar notch at the distal end belong to the radius, the lateral forearm bone. At the elbow, the radial head articulates with the capitulum, the lateral knob of the distal humerus.","notes":["Correct. A disc-shaped proximal head belongs to the radius, which meets the capitulum.","The ulna's elbow end is the olecranon and trochlear notch, which grip the trochlea. Its head is at the wrist end, not the elbow.","The bone is right, but the trochlea is the medial spool that meets the ulna. The radius meets the lateral capitulum.","The capitulum meets the radius, not the ulna. A disc-shaped proximal head is the radial head."],"comp":43,"competencyId":"w2-arm-forearm"},{"id":"N-a17d-44-1","dok":3,"q":"On an articulated wrist, a student puts a finger on the carpal most often dislocated, then moves one bone toward the little finger within the same row. Which bone is the student touching now?","a":"The triquetrum.","options":["The triquetrum.","The scaphoid.","The pisiform.","The capitate."],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Two steps get you there. The carpal most often dislocated is the lunate, the second bone of the proximal row. The proximal row runs scaphoid, lunate, triquetrum, pisiform from the thumb side (So Long To Pinky), so one bone toward the little finger is the triquetrum.","notes":["Correct. The lunate is most often dislocated, and the next bone toward the little finger in the proximal row is the triquetrum.","The scaphoid is next to the lunate, but on the thumb side. It is the carpal most often fractured.","The pisiform is the last bone of the proximal row on the little-finger side, two bones from the lunate, not one.","The capitate is the largest carpal and sits in the distal row. The student stayed in the proximal row."],"comp":44,"competencyId":"w2-hand-bones"},{"id":"N-a17d-45-1","dok":1,"q":"In bone-marking vocabulary, what is a sulcus?","a":"A groove.","options":["A shallow basin.","A small bump.","A bony hole.","A groove."],"correctIndex":3,"why":"A sulcus is a groove in a bone, one of the depression markings. The intertubercular sulcus of the humerus, the groove between the tubercles that holds a biceps tendon, is the standard example.","notes":["A shallow basin is a fossa, such as the olecranon fossa. It is a depression, but not a groove.","A small rounded bump is a tubercle, a projection. A sulcus dips into the bone instead.","A hole for vessels or nerves is a foramen, an opening. A sulcus is a groove that does not pass through the bone.","Correct. A sulcus is a groove, a type of depression."],"comp":45,"competencyId":"w2-bone-markings-vocab"},{"id":"N-a17d-45-2","dok":1,"q":"Which bone-marking term names a raised area above a condyle?","a":"Epicondyle.","options":["Tubercle.","Facet.","Head.","Epicondyle."],"correctIndex":3,"why":"An epicondyle is a projection that sits just above a condyle. The medial epicondyle of the humerus is the standard example, and the femur has medial and lateral epicondyles above its condyles.","notes":["A tubercle is a small rounded bump, like the greater tubercle of the humerus. It is not defined by sitting above a condyle.","A facet is a smooth, nearly flat articular surface, like the articular facets of vertebrae. It is not a raised area.","A head is a bony expansion carried on a neck, like the head of the humerus. It is defined by the neck below it, not by sitting above a condyle.","Correct. An epicondyle is a raised area above a condyle."],"comp":45,"competencyId":"w2-bone-markings-vocab"},{"id":"N-a17d-45-3","dok":2,"q":"The deltoid tuberosity, the mandibular fossa, and the mental foramen are three bone markings. Which list classifies them in that order?","a":"Projection, depression, opening.","options":["Projection, depression, opening.","Projection, opening, depression.","Depression, depression, opening.","Projection, depression, depression."],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Classify each by its term. A tuberosity is a large rough projection. A fossa is a shallow basin, which makes it a depression. A foramen is a hole for vessels or nerves, which makes it an opening.","notes":["Correct. A tuberosity is a projection, a fossa is a depression, and a foramen is an opening.","This swaps the last two. A fossa is a shallow basin, a depression, and a foramen is a hole, an opening.","A tuberosity is not a depression. It is a large rough projection where a muscle attaches.","A foramen is not a depression. It passes through the bone, which makes it an opening."],"comp":45,"competencyId":"w2-bone-markings-vocab"},{"id":"N-a17d-45-4","dok":2,"q":"Which of these bone markings is a depression?","a":"The intertubercular sulcus.","options":["The greater tubercle.","The mastoid process.","The supraorbital foramen.","The intertubercular sulcus."],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Depressions dip into the bone without passing through it, and the two depression terms are fossa and sulcus. The intertubercular sulcus is a groove between the tubercles of the humerus, so it is a depression.","notes":["A tubercle is a small rounded bump, so the greater tubercle is a projection.","A process is any bony prominence, so the mastoid process is a projection.","A foramen is a hole for vessels or nerves, so it is an opening rather than a depression.","Correct. A sulcus is a groove, which makes it a depression."],"comp":45,"competencyId":"w2-bone-markings-vocab"},{"id":"N-a17d-45-5","dok":2,"q":"Where one vertebra meets the next, the joining surfaces are smooth and nearly flat. Which kind of marking is this?","a":"A facet, an articular surface.","options":["A fossa, a depression.","A facet, an articular surface.","A tubercle, a projection.","A sulcus, a depression."],"correctIndex":1,"why":"A facet is a smooth, nearly flat articular surface where one bone meets another. The articular facets of the vertebrae, on their articular processes, are the standard example.","notes":["A fossa is a shallow basin, such as the olecranon fossa. The vertebral joint surfaces are nearly flat, not basins.","Correct. A smooth, nearly flat surface is a facet, an articular surface.","A tubercle is a small rounded bump. A flat joining surface is a facet.","A sulcus is a groove, such as the intertubercular sulcus. A flat joint surface is not a groove."],"comp":45,"competencyId":"w2-bone-markings-vocab"},{"id":"N-a17d-45-6","dok":3,"q":"A student reads lateral epicondyle of the femur on the lab list. Before finding it, what can the student predict about it from the term alone?","a":"It projects just above the lateral condyle, the knob meeting the tibia.","options":["It is a groove beside the lateral condyle that holds a tendon.","It is a hole in the lateral condyle that passes a nerve.","It projects just above the lateral condyle, the knob meeting the tibia.","It is a basin below the lateral condyle that receives the patella."],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Use the term to predict the structure. An epicondyle is a projection, a raised area above a condyle. On the femur, the medial and lateral condyles are the rounded distal knobs that articulate with the tibia, so the lateral epicondyle should project just above the lateral condyle.","notes":["A groove is a sulcus, a depression. An epicondyle is a projection.","A hole for vessels or nerves is a foramen, an opening. An epicondyle is a raised area.","Correct. An epicondyle is a raised area above a condyle, and the femoral condyles meet the tibia.","A basin is a fossa, a depression, and an epicondyle sits above a condyle, not below it. The patella glides on the patellar surface of the femur."],"comp":45,"competencyId":"w2-bone-markings-vocab"},{"id":"N-a17d-45-7","dok":3,"q":"The term head is used for the proximal ends of both the humerus and the femur. What earns both the name, and what lies just below each head?","a":"Each is an expansion carried on a neck, so a neck lies below.","options":["Each is a large rough projection, so a tuberosity lies below.","Each is an expansion carried on a neck, so a neck lies below.","Each is a smooth flat facet, so a sulcus lies below.","Each is a shallow basin, so a foramen lies below."],"correctIndex":1,"why":"A head is defined as a bony expansion carried on a neck. The head of the humerus sits on the anatomical neck, and the head of the femur sits on the femoral neck, so the term predicts a narrower neck just below each head.","notes":["A large rough projection is a tuberosity. A head is smooth and rounded, carried on a neck.","Correct. A head is a bony expansion carried on a neck, and both bones have a neck below the head.","A facet is nearly flat, and both heads are rounded. What lies below each head is a neck.","A shallow basin is a fossa, a depression. A head is a projection, not a basin."],"comp":45,"competencyId":"w2-bone-markings-vocab"},{"id":"N-a17d-45-8","dok":3,"q":"On a humerus, a student points to the groove between the tubercles and to the hole in the shaft where the artery enters. The student labels both as depressions. Which correction is right?","a":"The groove is a depression, but the hole is a foramen, an opening.","options":["The groove is a depression, but the hole is a foramen, an opening.","Both are openings, since a groove is a long foramen.","The hole is a fossa and the groove is a facet.","The groove is a tubercle, a projection, and the hole is a fossa."],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Name each marking, then classify it. The groove between the tubercles is the intertubercular sulcus, and a sulcus is a depression. The hole where the nutrient artery enters is the nutrient foramen, and a foramen passes through the bone, so it is an opening.","notes":["Correct. The intertubercular sulcus is a depression, but the nutrient foramen is an opening.","A groove does not pass through the bone. The intertubercular sulcus is a depression.","A fossa is a shallow basin and a facet is a smooth flat joint surface. The hole is a foramen and the groove is a sulcus.","The tubercles are the bumps on either side of the groove, not the groove itself, and a hole is a foramen."],"comp":45,"competencyId":"w2-bone-markings-vocab"}]},{"id":"m2-lower-extremity","title":"Pelvic girdle and lower limb bones","summary":"hip bone, pelvis, femur, patella, tibia, fibula, tarsals, metatarsals, phalanges and markings","mw":"2026-09-28","tr":"2026-09-24","cards":[{"id":"L00749","dok":1,"q":"Which three bones fuse to form each hip bone?","a":"The ilium, the ischium, and the pubis.","options":["The ilium, the ischium, and the pubis","The ilium, the sacrum, and the pubis","The ischium, the pubis, and the coccyx","The ilium, the ischium, and the femur"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Each hip bone, also called the os coxae or coxal bone, starts out as three separate bones: the ilium on top, the ischium at the back and bottom, and the pubis in front. These three fuse together at the acetabulum, the socket that holds the head of the femur. The sacrum and coccyx join the two hip bones to complete the pelvis, but they are not part of the hip bone itself.","notes":["Correct. The ilium, ischium, and pubis fuse at the acetabulum to form one hip bone.","The ilium and pubis are correct, but the sacrum is an axial bone formed from fused vertebrae. It joins the ilium at the sacroiliac joint, so it is a neighbor of the hip bone rather than one of its parts. The sacrum would fit a question about which bones complete the bony pelvis.","The ischium and pubis are correct, but the coccyx is the tailbone at the bottom of the vertebral column. Choose the coccyx if the question asked for the small axial bone that, along with the sacrum, completes the bony pelvis.","The ilium and ischium are correct, but the femur is the thigh bone, a separate bone whose head sits in the acetabulum. Students pick this when they blur the hip bone with the hip joint, which includes the femur."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-lower","lid":"c1","comp":46,"competencyId":"w2-pelvic-girdle"},{"id":"L00750","dok":1,"q":"What is the common name for the femur?","a":"The thigh bone.","options":["The kneecap","The shin bone","The thigh bone","The heel bone"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The femur is the thigh bone, the single bone that spans the region from the hip to the knee. It is the longest and strongest bone in the body because it carries the body's weight from the pelvis down to the leg. Its head fits into the acetabulum at the hip, and its condyles meet the tibia at the knee.","notes":["The kneecap is the patella, a sesamoid bone that sits in front of the knee within the quadriceps tendon. It is the answer if the question asked what the patella is commonly called.","The shin bone is the tibia, the medial bone of the leg between the knee and the ankle. It would be the answer if the question asked for the common name of the tibia.","Correct. The femur is the thigh bone, the only bone between the hip and the knee.","The heel bone is the calcaneus, the largest tarsal bone at the back of the foot. Pick it if the question asked for the common name of the calcaneus."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-lower","lid":"c2","comp":47,"competencyId":"w2-thigh-knee"},{"id":"L00751","dok":1,"q":"Which bone is the kneecap?","a":"The patella.","options":["The patella","The navicular","The calcaneus","The talus"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The kneecap is the patella, a sesamoid bone, meaning a bone that forms inside a tendon. It develops within the quadriceps tendon and sits in front of the knee, where it glides over the patellar surface of the femur. Below it, the patellar ligament continues down to the tibial tuberosity.","notes":["Correct. The patella is the kneecap, a sesamoid bone in the quadriceps tendon.","The navicular is a boat-shaped tarsal on the medial side of the foot, between the talus and the cuneiforms. It would fit a question about the tarsal just in front of the talus.","The calcaneus is the heel bone, the largest tarsal. Choose it if the question asked which bone forms the heel.","The talus is the tarsal bone at the ankle that articulates with the tibia and fibula and receives the body's weight from the leg. It would be the answer for the ankle bone that sits on top of the calcaneus."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-lower","lid":"c3","comp":47,"competencyId":"w2-thigh-knee"},{"id":"L00752","dok":1,"q":"How many tarsal bones form the ankle and the back of the foot?","a":"Seven.","options":["Five","Eight","Seven","Fourteen"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"There are seven tarsal bones in each foot: the talus, the calcaneus, the navicular, the cuboid, and three cuneiforms (medial, intermediate, and lateral). Together they form the ankle and the posterior half of the foot. The talus carries the body's weight from the leg, and the calcaneus forms the heel.","notes":["Five is the number of metatarsals, the bones of the sole between the tarsals and the toes. It would be right if the question asked how many metatarsals are in one foot.","Eight is the number of carpal bones in the wrist. Students who mirror the hand onto the foot pick it, but the foot has one fewer tarsal than the hand has carpals.","Correct. Each foot has seven tarsals.","Fourteen is the number of phalanges in one foot, the toe bones. Pick it if the question asked how many phalanges are in each foot."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-lower","lid":"c4","comp":49,"competencyId":"w2-foot-bones"},{"id":"L00753","dok":1,"q":"What is the deep cup of the hip bone that receives the head of the femur called?","a":"The acetabulum.","options":["The greater sciatic notch","The obturator foramen","The acetabulum","The iliac fossa"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The acetabulum is the deep, cup-shaped socket on the lateral side of the hip bone. It is the spot where the ilium, ischium, and pubis fuse, and it receives the head of the femur to form the hip joint. Because the socket is deep, the hip is much more stable than the shoulder.","notes":["The greater sciatic notch is a deep notch on the posterior border of the ilium that the sciatic nerve passes through. It would be the answer if the question asked where the largest nerve in the body leaves the pelvis.","The obturator foramen is the large opening below the acetabulum, framed by the ischium and the pubis. It is a hole through the bone, not a socket, and it would be the answer for the largest opening in the hip bone.","Correct. The acetabulum is the socket that receives the femoral head.","The iliac fossa is the smooth, concave inner surface of the ilium, facing the abdomen. It is shallow and does not articulate with any bone. Pick it if the question asked about the inner surface of the ilium."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-lower","lid":"c5","comp":46,"competencyId":"w2-pelvic-girdle"},{"id":"L00754","dok":1,"q":"Which two bones form the leg, the region from the knee to the ankle?","a":"The tibia and the fibula.","options":["The tibia and the fibula","The femur and the tibia","The talus and the calcaneus","The femur and the patella"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"In anatomy, the leg is only the region between the knee and the ankle, and it contains two bones. The tibia is the thick, medial, weight-bearing shin bone, and the fibula is the slender lateral bone that does not bear body weight. The thigh, above the knee, is a separate region with the femur as its only long bone.","notes":["Correct. The tibia and fibula are the two bones of the leg.","The femur and tibia meet at the knee joint, but only the tibia is in the leg. This pair would fit a question about which bones form the tibiofemoral joint.","The talus and calcaneus are tarsal bones of the foot, below the ankle. Pick them if the question asked for the two largest tarsals or the bones of the heel and ankle.","The femur and patella belong to the thigh and knee, above the leg. They would be the answer if the question asked which bones are found in the thigh region from hip to knee."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-lower","lid":"c6","comp":48,"competencyId":"w2-leg-bones"},{"id":"L00755","dok":1,"q":"Which tarsal bone is the heel bone and the largest tarsal?","a":"The calcaneus.","options":["The talus","The calcaneus","The cuboid","The navicular"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The calcaneus is the heel bone and the largest of the seven tarsals. It sits under the talus and projects backward, where it takes the impact of each heel strike and anchors the calcaneal (Achilles) tendon. The talus above it is the bone that receives weight directly from the tibia.","notes":["The talus is the tarsal that sits on top of the calcaneus and articulates with the tibia and fibula. It would be the answer for the tarsal that receives the body's weight from the leg.","Correct. The calcaneus is the heel bone and the largest tarsal.","The cuboid is a cube-shaped tarsal on the lateral side of the foot, in front of the calcaneus. Choose it if the question asked which tarsal lies on the lateral side, in line with the fourth and fifth metatarsals.","The navicular is a boat-shaped tarsal on the medial side, between the talus and the three cuneiforms. It would fit a question about the tarsal directly in front of the talus."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-lower","lid":"c7","comp":49,"competencyId":"w2-foot-bones"},{"id":"L00756","dok":1,"q":"What is the large lateral projection at the proximal end of the femur called?","a":"The greater trochanter.","options":["The fovea capitis","The linea aspera","The greater trochanter","The lesser trochanter"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The greater trochanter is the large bump on the lateral side of the proximal femur, just beyond the neck. It is a major attachment site for hip muscles, and you can feel it on the side of your hip. Its smaller partner, the lesser trochanter, sits on the medial side a little lower down.","notes":["The fovea capitis is the small pit on the femoral head that anchors a ligament to the acetabulum. Choose it if the question asked about the pit on the head of the femur.","The linea aspera is the rough ridge running down the posterior shaft of the femur, an attachment line for thigh muscles. It would be the answer if the question asked about the back of the femoral shaft.","Correct. The greater trochanter is the large lateral projection near the top of the femur.","The lesser trochanter is the smaller projection on the medial side of the proximal femur, below the neck. Pick it if the question asked for the small medial projection."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-lower","lid":"c8","comp":47,"competencyId":"w2-thigh-knee"},{"id":"L00757","dok":1,"q":"How many phalanges are in one human foot?","a":"Fourteen.","options":["Seven","Fourteen","Ten","Five"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Each foot has fourteen phalanges. The great toe, the hallux, has only two (a proximal and a distal phalanx), and each of the other four toes has three (proximal, middle, and distal). That gives 2 plus 4 times 3, which is fourteen, the same count as in the hand.","notes":["Seven is the number of tarsal bones in each foot. Pick it if the question asked how many bones form the ankle and back of the foot.","Correct. The foot has fourteen phalanges: two in the hallux and three in each other toe.","Ten would be the count if every toe had only two phalanges, like the great toe. Students who generalize the hallux to all the toes land on this number.","Five is the number of metatarsals, one behind each toe. It would be right if the question asked how many metatarsals are in one foot."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-lower","lid":"c9","comp":49,"competencyId":"w2-foot-bones"},{"id":"L00758","dok":1,"q":"What is the curved upper ridge of the ilium, the rim you rest your hands on, called?","a":"The iliac crest.","options":["The pubic crest","The iliac crest","The ischial tuberosity","The iliac fossa"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The iliac crest is the curved superior border of the ilium. When you put your hands on your hips, they rest on this ridge. It ends in front at the anterior superior iliac spine and in back at the posterior superior iliac spine, and clinicians use it as a landmark they can feel through the skin.","notes":["The pubic crest is the ridge along the upper border of the pubis, at the front of the pelvis near the midline. It would be the answer if the question asked about the ridge on the pubis.","Correct. The iliac crest is the curved upper ridge of the ilium.","The ischial tuberosity is the roughened knob of the ischium that you sit on. It would fit a question about the part of the hip bone that bears weight when sitting.","The iliac fossa is the smooth, concave inner surface of the ilium, not its upper rim. Pick it if the question asked for the shallow depression on the inside of the ilium."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-lower","lid":"c10","comp":46,"competencyId":"w2-pelvic-girdle"},{"id":"L00759","dok":1,"q":"What is the anatomical name for the great toe?","a":"The hallux.","options":["The pollex","The digitus minimus","The hallux","The calcaneus"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The great toe is called the hallux. It is digit I of the foot, and it has only two phalanges, a proximal and a distal, while toes 2 to 5 have three. Metatarsal I sits directly behind it.","notes":["The pollex is the thumb, the matching digit I of the hand. Students mix the two up because both are the first digit with only two phalanges. Pick the pollex if the question asked about the hand.","The digitus minimus is the little digit, the fifth toe or the fifth finger. It would be the answer if the question asked for the anatomical name of the smallest toe.","Correct. The hallux is the great toe.","The calcaneus is the heel bone, a tarsal, not a toe at all. Choose it if the question asked which bone forms the heel."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-lower","lid":"c11","comp":49,"competencyId":"w2-foot-bones"},{"id":"L00760","dok":1,"q":"What is the rough ridge running down the posterior shaft of the femur called?","a":"The linea aspera.","options":["The fovea capitis","The tibial tuberosity","The intercondylar fossa","The linea aspera"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The linea aspera, which means rough line, is a raised ridge along the back of the femoral shaft. It has a medial and a lateral lip and serves as an attachment line for many thigh muscles, including the adductors. Near the distal end its lips split into the medial and lateral supracondylar lines.","notes":["The fovea capitis is the small pit on the head of the femur. It would be the answer for the depression where a ligament anchors the femoral head to the acetabulum.","The tibial tuberosity is a bump on the anterior tibia, below the knee, where the patellar ligament attaches. It is on a different bone and the opposite surface.","The intercondylar fossa is also on the posterior femur, but it is the notch between the two condyles at the distal end, not a ridge down the shaft. Pick it if the question asked about the notch between the femoral condyles.","Correct. The linea aspera is the rough ridge down the posterior femoral shaft."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-lower","lid":"c12","comp":47,"competencyId":"w2-thigh-knee"},{"id":"L00761","dok":1,"q":"What is the large opening in the hip bone framed by the ischium and the pubis called?","a":"The obturator foramen.","options":["The obturator foramen","The greater sciatic notch","The acetabulum","The pubic symphysis"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The obturator foramen is the largest opening in the skeleton, a big hole in the lower hip bone surrounded by the ischium and the pubis. It sits just below and in front of the acetabulum. In life it is mostly closed over by a membrane, with a small gap for vessels and a nerve to reach the thigh.","notes":["Correct. The obturator foramen is the large opening framed by the ischium and pubis.","The greater sciatic notch is a deep notch on the posterior border of the ilium, not a full ring framed by the ischium and pubis. Pick it if the question asked where the sciatic nerve leaves the pelvis.","The acetabulum is the socket where all three hip bones meet, and it holds the head of the femur. It is a cup, not an opening, so it would be the answer for the hip socket.","The pubic symphysis is the cartilage joint where the two pubic bones meet at the front midline. It would fit a question about where the left and right hip bones join anteriorly."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-lower","lid":"c13","comp":46,"competencyId":"w2-pelvic-girdle"},{"id":"L00763","dok":2,"q":"The auricular surface of the ilium joins which bone at the sacroiliac joint?","a":"The sacrum.","options":["The coccyx","The fifth lumbar vertebra","The pubis","The sacrum"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The auricular surface is the ear-shaped area on the posterior, medial side of the ilium. It meets a matching auricular surface on the side of the sacrum, forming the sacroiliac joint. This joint is where the weight of the upper body passes from the vertebral column into the pelvic girdle and on to the legs.","notes":["The coccyx is the tailbone at the bottom of the sacrum. It does not touch the ilium, so it would only be the answer if the question asked which bone articulates with the inferior tip of the sacrum.","The fifth lumbar vertebra sits on top of the sacrum at the lumbosacral joint. Pick it if the question asked which vertebra rests directly on the sacrum.","The pubis is part of the same hip bone, at the front. It meets the opposite pubis at the pubic symphysis, so it would be the answer for that anterior joint.","Correct. The auricular surface of the ilium articulates with the sacrum at the sacroiliac joint."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-lower","lid":"c15","comp":46,"competencyId":"w2-pelvic-girdle"},{"id":"L00764","dok":2,"q":"Which leg bone is the weight-bearing bone, and on which side of the leg does it lie?","a":"The tibia, on the medial side.","options":["The tibia, on the medial side","The tibia, on the lateral side","The fibula, on the lateral side","The fibula, on the medial side"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The tibia is the thick shin bone on the medial side of the leg, and it carries the body's weight from the femur down to the talus. The fibula lies lateral to it and is slender because it does not bear body weight. Remembering that the tibia is medial also tells you that the medial malleolus at the ankle belongs to the tibia.","notes":["Correct. The tibia is the weight-bearing bone, and it lies on the medial side of the leg.","The tibia is the weight-bearing bone, but it lies medially, not laterally. Students who picture the leg from behind or flip their own leg in their head often swap the sides.","The fibula is on the lateral side, but it does not bear body weight. It would be the answer if the question asked which leg bone is slender and lateral.","The fibula is neither weight-bearing nor medial. This choice reverses both facts, so it reflects mixing up which leg bone is which."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-lower","lid":"c16","comp":48,"competencyId":"w2-leg-bones"},{"id":"L00765","dok":2,"q":"The medial malleolus is the bump on the inner side of the ankle. Which bone bears it?","a":"The tibia.","options":["The calcaneus","The fibula","The talus","The tibia"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The medial malleolus is the distal end of the tibia, which projects downward on the medial side of the ankle. Because the tibia is the medial bone of the leg, its malleolus is the medial one. Together with the lateral malleolus of the fibula, it forms the sides of the socket that holds the talus at the ankle joint.","notes":["The calcaneus is the heel bone, below and behind the ankle joint. It would be the answer if the question asked which bone forms the heel.","The fibula bears the lateral malleolus, the bump on the outer side of the ankle. Pick it if the question asked about the outer ankle bump.","The talus sits between the two malleoli and fits into the socket they form. It has no malleolus of its own, so it would be the answer for the tarsal bone that articulates with both leg bones.","Correct. The medial malleolus is part of the tibia."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-lower","lid":"c17","comp":48,"competencyId":"w2-leg-bones"},{"id":"L00766","dok":2,"q":"Which bone bears the lateral malleolus at the ankle?","a":"The fibula.","options":["The talus","The tibia","The femur","The fibula"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The lateral malleolus is the distal end of the fibula, the bump on the outer side of the ankle. The fibula is the lateral bone of the leg, so its malleolus is the lateral one. It sits a little lower than the medial malleolus and is the common site of an ankle fracture after a rolled ankle.","notes":["The talus sits between the two malleoli and carries no malleolus itself. Pick it if the question asked which tarsal fits between the tibia and fibula.","The tibia bears the medial malleolus on the inner side of the ankle. It would be the answer if the question asked about the medial bump.","The femur is the thigh bone and ends at the knee, far above the ankle. Its distal markings are the condyles and epicondyles, so it would fit a question about the knee end of the thigh.","Correct. The lateral malleolus belongs to the fibula."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-lower","lid":"c18","comp":48,"competencyId":"w2-leg-bones"},{"id":"L00767","dok":2,"q":"Which tarsal bone articulates with the tibia and fibula and receives the body's weight from the leg?","a":"The talus.","options":["The calcaneus","The navicular","The talus","The cuboid"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The talus is the highest tarsal bone. It fits between the medial and lateral malleoli and articulates with the tibia above, forming the ankle (talocrural) joint. Weight passes from the tibia to the talus, and from there it spreads back to the calcaneus and forward through the other tarsals to the metatarsals.","notes":["The calcaneus is the heel bone below the talus. It receives weight from the talus, not directly from the leg, so it would be the answer for the largest tarsal or the heel bone.","The navicular lies in front of the talus on the medial side of the foot. Pick it if the question asked which tarsal sits between the talus and the cuneiforms.","Correct. The talus articulates with the tibia and fibula and takes the body's weight from the leg.","The cuboid lies on the lateral side of the foot, in front of the calcaneus. It would be the answer for the lateral tarsal that meets the fourth and fifth metatarsals."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-lower","lid":"c19","comp":49,"competencyId":"w2-foot-bones"},{"id":"L00768","dok":2,"q":"The tibial tuberosity on the front of the tibia is the attachment site for which structure?","a":"The patellar ligament.","options":["The calcaneal tendon","The quadriceps tendon","The patellar ligament","The anterior cruciate ligament"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The tibial tuberosity is the roughened bump on the anterior tibia just below the knee. The patellar ligament runs from the patella down to this tuberosity. Because the patellar ligament is really the continuation of the quadriceps tendon, this bump is where the pull of the quadriceps finally reaches the tibia to extend the knee.","notes":["The calcaneal tendon, or Achilles tendon, attaches to the calcaneus at the heel. Choose it if the question asked which tendon anchors to the back of the heel bone.","The quadriceps tendon attaches to the patella, above the knee. It continues below the patella as the patellar ligament, so pick the quadriceps tendon if the question asked what attaches to the base of the patella.","Correct. The patellar ligament anchors on the tibial tuberosity.","The anterior cruciate ligament lies inside the knee joint and attaches to the top of the tibia between the condyles, not to the front of the shaft. It would be the answer for the ligament that stops the tibia sliding forward."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-lower","lid":"c20","comp":48,"competencyId":"w2-leg-bones"},{"id":"L00769","dok":2,"q":"Which markings of the femur articulate with the tibia at the knee?","a":"The medial and lateral condyles.","options":["The greater and lesser trochanters","The medial and lateral epicondyles","The head and the neck","The medial and lateral condyles"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The medial and lateral condyles are the large, rounded knobs at the distal femur. Their smooth surfaces rest on the medial and lateral condyles of the tibia, forming the two tibiofemoral joints of the knee. The epicondyles sit just above them and serve as attachment points rather than joint surfaces.","notes":["The trochanters are muscle attachment sites at the proximal femur, near the hip. They would be the answer if the question asked for the large projections near the top of the femur.","The epicondyles are the projections just above the condyles where ligaments and muscles attach. They do not touch the tibia, so pick them if the question asked what sits just superior to the condyles.","The head and neck are at the proximal end of the femur. The head articulates with the acetabulum at the hip, so this pair fits a question about the hip joint, not the knee.","Correct. The femoral condyles articulate with the tibia."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-lower","lid":"c21","comp":47,"competencyId":"w2-thigh-knee"},{"id":"L00770","dok":2,"q":"The pubic symphysis is the joint where which structures meet at the front of the pelvis?","a":"The two pubic bones.","options":["The ischium and the ilium","The pubis and the sacrum","The two ilia","The two pubic bones"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The pubic symphysis is the midline joint at the front of the pelvis where the left and right pubic bones meet. A disc of fibrocartilage joins them, making it a cartilaginous joint that is slightly movable. It closes the pelvic ring in front, while the sacroiliac joints close it in back.","notes":["The ischium and ilium are parts of the same hip bone that fuse at the acetabulum, not at a joint at the front. Pick them only if the question asked which bones fuse together with the pubis to form the hip bone.","The pubis and sacrum are at opposite ends of the pelvis and do not articulate. The sacrum joins the ilium, so the sacroiliac joint is the right answer for where the sacrum meets the hip bone.","The two ilia never meet each other. Each ilium joins the sacrum at a sacroiliac joint in the back, so the ilia would only fit a question about the sacroiliac joints.","Correct. The two pubic bones meet at the pubic symphysis."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-lower","lid":"c22","comp":46,"competencyId":"w2-pelvic-girdle"},{"id":"L00771","dok":2,"q":"How many phalanges does each toe other than the great toe have?","a":"Three.","options":["Two","One","Four","Three"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Toes 2 through 5 each have three phalanges: a proximal, a middle, and a distal phalanx. The great toe, the hallux, is the exception, with only a proximal and a distal phalanx. This is the same pattern as the fingers and the thumb.","notes":["Two is the number of phalanges in the great toe, the hallux. Pick it if the question asked about the great toe specifically.","One phalanx is too few for any toe. This answer usually comes from thinking of each toe as a single bone, when even the hallux has two.","Four phalanges do not occur in any normal human toe. Students choosing four may be adding the metatarsal to the toe's three phalanges.","Correct. Each of the four lateral toes has three phalanges."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-lower","lid":"c23","comp":49,"competencyId":"w2-foot-bones"},{"id":"L00772","dok":2,"q":"Which bones, together with the sacrum and coccyx, form the bony pelvis?","a":"The two hip bones.","options":["The two hip bones","The two ilia only","The lumbar vertebrae","The two femurs"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The bony pelvis is a ring made of the two hip bones in front and on the sides, closed in back by the sacrum and coccyx. Each hip bone includes the fused ilium, ischium, and pubis, so it takes the whole hip bone, not just the ilium, to complete the ring. The femurs attach to the pelvis but are not part of it.","notes":["Correct. The two hip bones plus the sacrum and coccyx form the bony pelvis.","The ilia are only the upper parts of the hip bones. Leaving out the ischium and pubis would leave the front and bottom of the pelvic ring open. This choice would fit a question about which part of each hip bone joins the sacrum.","The lumbar vertebrae sit above the sacrum in the lower back. They carry weight down to the pelvis but are not part of it. They would fit a question about the vertebral region just above the sacrum.","The femurs are the thigh bones. They articulate with the pelvis at the acetabula but belong to the free lower limb. Pick them if the question asked which bones form the hip joints with the pelvis."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-lower","lid":"c24","comp":46,"competencyId":"w2-pelvic-girdle"},{"id":"L00773","dok":2,"q":"The strong, roughened knob of the ischium that you sit on is which marking?","a":"The ischial tuberosity.","options":["The iliac crest","The ischial spine","The ischial tuberosity","The pubic crest"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The ischial tuberosity is the thick, rough knob at the bottom of the ischium. When you sit upright, your weight rests on the left and right ischial tuberosities, which is why they are often called the sitting bones. Their rough surface also anchors the hamstring muscles.","notes":["The iliac crest is the curved upper rim of the ilium where you rest your hands. It would fit a question about the top border of the hip bone.","The ischial spine is a pointed projection on the ischium just above the tuberosity. It points into the pelvis rather than down toward a seat, so pick it if the question asked for the pointed projection above the sitting knob.","Correct. The ischial tuberosity is the knob you sit on.","The pubic crest is the ridge on the upper border of the pubis, at the front of the pelvis. It would be the answer for the ridge near the front midline."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-lower","lid":"c25","comp":46,"competencyId":"w2-pelvic-girdle"},{"id":"L00774","dok":2,"q":"The fovea capitis is a small pit found on which part of the femur?","a":"The head.","options":["The neck","The lateral condyle","The head","The greater trochanter"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The fovea capitis is a small pit near the center of the femoral head. A ligament runs from this pit to the acetabulum, helping anchor the head in the socket, and a small artery travels with it. Its name means pit of the head, which is a quick way to remember where it is.","notes":["The neck is the narrow region just below the head. It has no pit, and it would be the answer if the question asked for the common site of a hip fracture.","The lateral condyle is at the distal femur, at the knee. It would be the answer if the question asked which knob of the femur rests on the lateral tibial condyle.","Correct. The fovea capitis sits on the femoral head.","The greater trochanter is the large lateral bump at the proximal femur where hip muscles attach. Pick it if the question asked about the large lateral projection."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-lower","lid":"c26","comp":47,"competencyId":"w2-thigh-knee"},{"id":"L00775","dok":2,"q":"What is the broad superior border of the patella called?","a":"The base.","options":["The apex","The base","The crest","The fovea"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The patella is shaped like a rounded triangle pointing down. Its broad top edge is called the base, and its pointed bottom tip is called the apex. The quadriceps tendon attaches along the base, and the patellar ligament leaves from the apex.","notes":["The apex is the pointed inferior tip of the patella, the opposite end from the base. Pick it if the question asked about the lower point where the patellar ligament begins.","Correct. The broad superior border of the patella is its base.","A crest is a ridge, as in the iliac crest or pubic crest, and the patella has no marking by that name. This choice comes from borrowing a name used for ridges on other bones.","A fovea is a small pit, as in the fovea capitis of the femoral head. The patella has no fovea, so this choice reflects mixing up markings from different bones."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-lower","lid":"c27","comp":47,"competencyId":"w2-thigh-knee"},{"id":"L00776","dok":2,"q":"How many metatarsals form the sole of the foot, and how are they numbered?","a":"Five, numbered I to V starting at the great toe.","options":["Five, numbered I to V starting at the great toe","Fourteen, numbered so each matches a phalanx","Seven, numbered I to VII starting at the great toe","Five, numbered I to V starting at the little toe"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Each foot has five metatarsals, one behind each toe. They are numbered I to V from medial to lateral, so metatarsal I sits behind the great toe (the hallux) and metatarsal V sits behind the little toe. The same medial-to-lateral numbering is used for the toes themselves.","notes":["Correct. There are five metatarsals, numbered I to V from the great toe side.","Fourteen is the number of phalanges in one foot. That count would be right if the question asked about the toe bones, not the metatarsals.","Seven is the number of tarsal bones, not metatarsals. Pick seven if the question asked how many tarsals form the ankle and back of the foot.","The count is right, but the numbering runs from the medial side, so metatarsal I is behind the great toe, not the little toe. Students reverse it when they forget that digit I is the hallux."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-lower","lid":"c28","comp":49,"competencyId":"w2-foot-bones"},{"id":"L00777","dok":2,"q":"Which three arches spread body weight across the foot?","a":"The medial and lateral longitudinal arches and the transverse arch.","options":["The anterior and posterior longitudinal arches and the transverse arch","The medial and lateral transverse arches and the longitudinal arch","The medial and lateral longitudinal arches and the transverse arch","The medial, intermediate, and lateral longitudinal arches"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The foot has two longitudinal arches running front to back, a higher medial one and a lower lateral one, plus one transverse arch running side to side across the foot. Together they spread weight between the heel and the balls of the foot and spring back with each step. When the medial longitudinal arch drops, the result is flat feet.","notes":["Longitudinal arches run front to back, so they are named for the side of the foot they are on, medial and lateral, not anterior and posterior. This choice mixes up direction terms.","This flips the two arch types. There is only one transverse arch, running across the foot, and two longitudinal arches, one medial and one lateral.","Correct. The foot has medial and lateral longitudinal arches and a transverse arch.","There is no intermediate longitudinal arch. This choice borrows the medial, intermediate, and lateral naming used for the three cuneiform bones."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-lower","lid":"c29","comp":49,"competencyId":"w2-foot-bones"},{"id":"L00778","dok":2,"q":"A clinician feels for the prominent bony point at the front end of the iliac crest to locate an injection site. Which marking is it?","a":"The anterior superior iliac spine.","options":["The ischial spine","The anterior superior iliac spine","The anterior inferior iliac spine","The posterior superior iliac spine"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The anterior superior iliac spine, often shortened to ASIS, is the point where the iliac crest ends at the front. It sits just under the skin, so clinicians can feel it to find injection sites and check whether the pelvis is level. The anterior inferior iliac spine lies a little below it and is much harder to feel.","notes":["The ischial spine is a pointed projection on the ischium inside the pelvis, not on the iliac crest. It would be the answer for the projection just above the ischial tuberosity.","Correct. The anterior superior iliac spine is the palpable point at the front of the iliac crest.","The anterior inferior iliac spine is on the front of the ilium, but it sits below the ASIS and is not the end of the crest. Choose it if the question asked for the projection just below the anterior superior iliac spine.","The posterior superior iliac spine is at the back end of the iliac crest, near the sacroiliac joint. Pick it if the question asked for the point at the back of the crest, often seen as a dimple in the lower back."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-lower","lid":"c30","comp":46,"competencyId":"w2-pelvic-girdle"},{"id":"L00779","dok":2,"q":"Moving distally from the knee along the medial side of the leg, which bone do you follow down to the ankle?","a":"The tibia.","options":["The femur","The talus","The fibula","The tibia"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The tibia is the medial bone of the leg, so tracing the inner side of the leg from knee to ankle follows the tibia the whole way. It begins with its medial and lateral condyles at the knee and ends in the medial malleolus, the inner ankle bump. Its sharp anterior border is the shin you can feel under the skin.","notes":["The femur is the thigh bone and ends at the knee. It would be the answer if the path ran from the hip to the knee instead.","The talus is a tarsal bone at the ankle where the path ends, not the bone you follow down the leg. Choose it if the question asked which bone the tibia rests on at the ankle.","The fibula runs along the lateral side of the leg, from its head just below the knee to the lateral malleolus. Pick it if the question traced the outer side of the leg.","Correct. The tibia runs along the medial side of the leg from knee to ankle."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-lower","lid":"c31","comp":48,"competencyId":"w2-leg-bones"},{"id":"L00780","dok":2,"q":"Which marking is the rounded ball at the proximal end of the femur that seats into the hip socket?","a":"The head of the femur.","options":["The neck of the femur","The lateral condyle","The head of the femur","The greater trochanter"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The head of the femur is the smooth, rounded ball at the top of the bone. It fits into the acetabulum of the hip bone to form the hip joint, a ball-and-socket joint. The narrower neck connects the head to the shaft, and the small pit on the head is the fovea capitis.","notes":["The neck is the narrow region just below the head that angles toward the shaft. It would be the answer if the question asked about the common hip fracture site.","The lateral condyle is a rounded knob at the distal femur that rests on the tibia at the knee. It would be the answer for a knee joint surface, not the hip.","Correct. The head of the femur is the ball that seats into the acetabulum.","The greater trochanter is a large lateral bump near the top of the femur where hip muscles attach. It does not enter the socket, so pick it if the question asked for the big lateral projection."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-lower","lid":"c32","comp":47,"competencyId":"w2-thigh-knee"},{"id":"L00782","dok":2,"q":"Which marking is the deep notch on the posterior border of the ilium?","a":"The greater sciatic notch.","options":["The auricular surface","The lesser sciatic notch","The greater sciatic notch","The obturator foramen"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The greater sciatic notch is a large, deep notch on the posterior border of the ilium, above the ischial spine. The sciatic nerve, the largest nerve in the body, passes through it from the pelvis into the gluteal region. A smaller notch, the lesser sciatic notch, lies below the ischial spine on the ischium.","notes":["The auricular surface is the ear-shaped joint surface on the medial ilium that meets the sacrum. It would be the answer for the part of the ilium that forms the sacroiliac joint.","The lesser sciatic notch is the smaller notch below the ischial spine, on the ischium rather than the ilium. Pick it if the question asked for the notch just under the ischial spine.","Correct. The greater sciatic notch is the deep notch on the posterior border of the ilium.","The obturator foramen is a full opening in the lower hip bone framed by the ischium and pubis, not a notch on the posterior border. It would be the answer for the largest hole in the hip bone."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-lower","lid":"c34","comp":46,"competencyId":"w2-pelvic-girdle"},{"id":"L00784","dok":2,"q":"Which tarsal bone sits directly under the talus and forms the back of the foot?","a":"The calcaneus.","options":["The cuboid","The medial cuneiform","The calcaneus","The navicular"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The calcaneus lies directly beneath the talus and projects backward to form the heel. It is the largest tarsal and takes the impact of heel strike. The talus passes weight down to it, and from there the load spreads forward through the other tarsals to the metatarsals.","notes":["The cuboid lies in front of the calcaneus on the lateral side of the foot. It would be the answer for the lateral tarsal that meets metatarsals IV and V.","The medial cuneiform is one of three wedge-shaped tarsals in the front row, in line with the great toe. Choose it if the question asked which tarsal articulates with metatarsal I.","Correct. The calcaneus sits under the talus and forms the heel.","The navicular lies in front of the talus on the medial side of the foot, not underneath it. Pick it if the question asked for the tarsal between the talus and the cuneiforms."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-lower","lid":"c36","comp":49,"competencyId":"w2-foot-bones"},{"id":"L00785","dok":2,"q":"Which marking is the pointed projection of the ischium located just above the ischial tuberosity?","a":"The ischial spine.","options":["The pubic crest","The iliac crest","The ischial spine","The ischial tuberosity"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The ischial spine is a pointed projection on the posterior ischium that sits above the ischial tuberosity and points toward the inside of the pelvis. It separates the greater sciatic notch above it from the lesser sciatic notch below it. It also serves as an attachment point for a ligament that runs to the sacrum.","notes":["The pubic crest is the ridge on the upper border of the pubis at the front of the pelvis. It would be the answer for the ridge near the pubic symphysis.","The iliac crest is the curved upper rim of the ilium where you rest your hands. It would fit a question about the top border of the hip bone.","Correct. The ischial spine is the pointed projection just above the tuberosity.","The ischial tuberosity is the rough knob below the spine that you sit on. Pick it if the question asked which part of the ischium bears weight when sitting."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-lower","lid":"c37","comp":46,"competencyId":"w2-pelvic-girdle"},{"id":"L00786","dok":2,"q":"Which femoral marking is the notch between the distal condyles on the posterior surface?","a":"The intercondylar fossa.","options":["The linea aspera","The intercondylar fossa","The greater trochanter","The fovea capitis"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The intercondylar fossa is the deep notch between the medial and lateral condyles on the back of the distal femur. The cruciate ligaments of the knee attach inside this notch. On the front of the distal femur, the condyles are joined instead by the smooth patellar surface where the kneecap glides.","notes":["The linea aspera is also on the posterior femur, but it is a ridge running down the shaft, above the condyles. Pick it if the question asked about the rough line on the back of the shaft.","Correct. The intercondylar fossa is the notch between the femoral condyles on the posterior side.","The greater trochanter is a large lateral projection at the proximal femur. It would fit a question about the big bump on the side of the hip.","The fovea capitis is a small pit on the femoral head at the hip end. It would be the answer for the pit where a ligament anchors the head to the acetabulum."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-lower","lid":"c38","comp":47,"competencyId":"w2-thigh-knee"},{"id":"L00787","dok":2,"q":"The sharp anterior border felt directly under the skin as the shin belongs to which bone?","a":"The tibia.","options":["The tibia","The femur","The patella","The fibula"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The tibia has a sharp anterior border, also called the anterior crest, that runs down the front of the leg just under the skin. That ridge is what you feel as your shin. Because there is so little soft tissue over it, a blow to the shin hurts and a fracture there is easy to feel.","notes":["Correct. The anterior border of the tibia forms the shin.","The femur is the thigh bone, above the knee. Its shaft is wrapped in thick muscle and cannot be felt as a sharp edge, so it would fit a question about the bone of the thigh.","The patella is the kneecap at the front of the knee, not along the shin. Choose it if the question asked which bone you feel at the front of the knee.","The fibula is on the lateral side of the leg and is covered by muscle along most of its length. Only its head and lateral malleolus are easy to feel, so pick it if the question asked about the outer ankle bump."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-lower","lid":"c39","comp":48,"competencyId":"w2-leg-bones"},{"id":"L00788","dok":2,"q":"Following the lateral side of the leg from knee to ankle, which proximal marking and which bone are you tracking?","a":"The head of the fibula and the fibula.","options":["The head of the fibula and the fibula","The lateral condyle and the tibia","The greater trochanter and the femur","The medial malleolus and the tibia"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The fibula is the slender lateral bone of the leg. Its proximal end is a knob called the head, which you can feel on the outer side of the leg just below the knee, and its distal end is the lateral malleolus. Following the outer leg from knee to ankle traces this bone from head to malleolus.","notes":["Correct. The lateral leg is the fibula, which starts proximally at its head.","The tibia does have a lateral condyle, but the tibia is the medial bone of the leg, so following it would trace the inner side. Pick this pair if the question asked which tibial marking the fibular head sits under.","The greater trochanter and femur are at the hip and thigh, above the knee. This pair would fit a path along the lateral thigh, not the leg.","The medial malleolus is the distal end of the tibia on the inner ankle, the opposite side and opposite end from this path. It would be the answer for the inner ankle bump."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-lower","lid":"c40","comp":48,"competencyId":"w2-leg-bones"},{"id":"L00789","dok":2,"q":"Which region is the narrower ring of the pelvis below the pelvic brim?","a":"The true pelvis.","options":["The iliac fossa","The false pelvis","The pelvic brim","The true pelvis"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The pelvic brim is a rim of bone that divides the pelvis into two regions. Below it is the true pelvis, a narrower ring that surrounds the pelvic cavity and forms the birth canal. Above it is the false pelvis, the wide flare of the ilia that supports the lower abdominal organs.","notes":["The iliac fossa is the concave inner surface of the ilium, which forms the wall of the false pelvis. It would fit a question about the smooth inner face of the ilium.","The false pelvis is the wide flare above the pelvic brim, formed mainly by the ilia. Pick it if the question asked for the region above the brim.","The pelvic brim is the dividing line itself, not a region. It would be the answer if the question asked what separates the true and false pelvis.","Correct. The true pelvis is the narrower ring below the pelvic brim."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-lower","lid":"c41","comp":46,"competencyId":"w2-pelvic-girdle"},{"id":"L00790","dok":2,"q":"Which femoral marking is the smaller projection on the medial side near the proximal end?","a":"The lesser trochanter.","options":["The medial epicondyle","The greater trochanter","The lesser trochanter","The medial condyle"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The lesser trochanter is a small, rounded projection on the medial and slightly posterior side of the proximal femur, just below the neck. It is a muscle attachment site. The much larger greater trochanter sits on the lateral side at about the same level.","notes":["The medial epicondyle is a medial projection at the distal end, just above the medial condyle. It would fit a question about the attachment bump above the knee joint surface.","The greater trochanter is the large projection on the lateral side of the proximal femur. Pick it if the question asked for the big lateral bump.","Correct. The lesser trochanter is the small medial projection at the proximal femur.","The medial condyle is on the medial side, but at the distal end of the femur, where it rests on the tibia. It would be the answer for a knee joint surface."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-lower","lid":"c42","comp":47,"competencyId":"w2-thigh-knee"},{"id":"L00791","dok":2,"q":"Which marking is the pointed inferior tip of the kneecap?","a":"The apex of the patella.","options":["The base of the patella","The apex of the patella","The tibial tuberosity","The lateral condyle"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The patella is roughly triangular, with its point facing down. That pointed inferior tip is the apex, and the broad superior border is the base. The patellar ligament leaves the apex and runs down to the tibial tuberosity.","notes":["The base is the broad superior border of the patella, the opposite end from the apex. Pick it if the question asked about the top edge where the quadriceps tendon attaches.","Correct. The apex is the pointed inferior tip of the patella.","The tibial tuberosity is a bump on the anterior tibia below the knee where the patellar ligament ends. Choose it if the question asked where the patellar ligament attaches.","The lateral condyle is a knob of the femur or tibia that forms part of the knee joint surface, not a part of the patella. It would be the answer for a joint surface at the knee."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-lower","lid":"c43","comp":47,"competencyId":"w2-thigh-knee"},{"id":"L00792","dok":2,"q":"Tracing the bones of the free lower limb from the hip, which bone comes first, and which two follow it in the leg?","a":"The femur first, then the tibia and the fibula.","options":["The tibia first, then the femur and the fibula","The patella first, then the femur and the tibia","The femur first, then the tibia and the fibula","The fibula first, then the tibia and the femur"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The free lower limb begins with the thigh, which has a single bone, the femur, running from the hip to the knee. Below the knee is the leg, which has two parallel bones, the medial tibia and the lateral fibula. The pattern of one bone and then two matches the upper limb, with the humerus followed by the radius and ulna.","notes":["The tibia is in the leg, below the knee, so it cannot come before the femur. This order puts a leg bone in the thigh.","The patella sits in front of the knee, between the femur and tibia, not at the hip. Pick the patella if the question asked which bone lies in front of the knee joint.","Correct. The femur comes first, then the tibia and fibula in the leg.","The fibula is a leg bone that does not even reach the knee joint, so it cannot be first. This order reflects starting at the wrong end of the limb."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-lower","lid":"c44","comp":47,"competencyId":"w2-thigh-knee"},{"id":"L00793","dok":2,"q":"Trace the bones of the free lower limb in order from the hip joint to the tip of a toe, not counting the hip bone.","a":"Femur, then tibia and fibula, then tarsals, then a metatarsal, then phalanges.","options":["Femur, tibia and fibula, metatarsal, tarsals, phalanges","Femur, tibia and fibula, tarsals, metatarsal, phalanges","Hip bone, femur, tibia, metatarsal, phalanges","Femur, tarsals, tibia and fibula, metatarsal, phalanges"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Starting at the hip, the femur spans the thigh to the knee. The tibia and fibula span the leg to the ankle, where the tarsals form the ankle and back of the foot. A metatarsal then runs through the sole to the base of a toe, and the phalanges form the toe itself.","notes":["This swaps the tarsals and the metatarsal. The tarsals form the ankle and back of the foot, and the metatarsals lie in front of them, so tarsals come first.","Correct. Femur, tibia and fibula, tarsals, metatarsal, phalanges.","This sequence starts with the hip bone, which the question excludes because it belongs to the pelvic girdle, and it also skips the fibula and the tarsals. It would only fit a question that asked to include the girdle.","This puts the tarsals in the thigh region, before the leg bones. The tarsals form the ankle, so they come after the tibia and fibula, not before."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-lower","lid":"c45","comp":49,"competencyId":"w2-foot-bones"},{"id":"L00794","dok":2,"q":"Which set correctly names a pelvic girdle bone, the thigh bone, and the socket where they articulate?","a":"The hip bone, the femur, and the acetabulum.","options":["The hip bone, the femur, and the obturator foramen","The hip bone, the tibia, and the acetabulum","The sacrum, the femur, and the acetabulum","The hip bone, the femur, and the acetabulum"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The pelvic girdle is made of the two hip bones, and each one meets the thigh bone, the femur, at the hip joint. The socket of that joint is the acetabulum, a deep cup on the lateral hip bone that receives the head of the femur. This ball-and-socket arrangement links the lower limb to the axial skeleton.","notes":["The obturator foramen is a large opening in the hip bone below the acetabulum, not a socket. Nothing articulates in it, so it would only be the answer for the opening framed by the ischium and pubis.","The tibia is a leg bone that articulates with the femur at the knee, not with the hip bone. This set would fit only if the question asked about the knee.","The sacrum is an axial bone, not part of the pelvic girdle, and it never meets the femur. It joins the hip bone at the sacroiliac joint, so pick the sacrum for that joint.","Correct. The hip bone and femur articulate at the acetabulum."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-lower","lid":"c46","comp":46,"competencyId":"w2-pelvic-girdle"},{"id":"L00795","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly matches each leg bone to the malleolus at its ankle end?","a":"The tibia carries the medial malleolus and the fibula carries the lateral malleolus.","options":["The fibula carries both malleoli and the tibia carries neither","The tibia carries the lateral malleolus and the fibula carries the medial malleolus","The tibia carries both malleoli and the fibula carries neither","The tibia carries the medial malleolus and the fibula carries the lateral malleolus"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Each leg bone ends in one malleolus, and the name follows the side the bone is on. The tibia is the medial leg bone, so its distal end is the medial malleolus, the inner ankle bump. The fibula is lateral, so its distal end is the lateral malleolus, the outer ankle bump.","notes":["The fibula carries only the lateral malleolus. Giving it both bumps comes from thinking of the fibula as the ankle bone and the tibia as the knee bone.","This reverses the pairing. The tibia is the medial bone, so it cannot carry the lateral malleolus. Students who mix up which leg bone is medial fall into this swap.","The tibia carries only the medial malleolus. The outer ankle bump belongs to the fibula, so this choice ignores the fibula's distal end.","Correct. Tibia with the medial malleolus, fibula with the lateral malleolus."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-lower","lid":"c47","comp":48,"competencyId":"w2-leg-bones"},{"id":"L00796","dok":2,"q":"Name the three regions of the hip bone in this order: the superior fan-shaped part, the posteroinferior part, and the anteromedial part.","a":"Ilium, then ischium, then pubis.","options":["Ilium, ischium, pubis","Ischium, ilium, pubis","Pubis, ischium, ilium","Ilium, pubis, ischium"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The ilium is the broad, fan-shaped upper part of the hip bone, topped by the iliac crest. The ischium forms the posterior and lower part, including the ischial tuberosity you sit on. The pubis forms the front, medial part and meets the other pubis at the pubic symphysis.","notes":["Correct. Ilium is superior, ischium is posteroinferior, and pubis is anteromedial.","The ischium is posteroinferior, not the fan-shaped top. The fan-shaped superior part is the ilium, which holds the iliac crest.","This reverses the order. The pubis is the anteromedial part, not the superior fan, so it belongs last in this sequence.","The ilium is right, but the pubis is anteromedial and the ischium is posteroinferior. This choice swaps the last two."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-lower","lid":"c48","comp":46,"competencyId":"w2-pelvic-girdle"},{"id":"L00797","dok":2,"q":"Comparing the two ends of the leg bones, which statement correctly describes where the knee condyles and the malleoli are found?","a":"The tibia bears the condyles and the medial malleolus, and the fibula bears the head and the lateral malleolus.","options":["The fibula bears the condyles and the medial malleolus, and the tibia bears the head and the lateral malleolus","The tibia bears the condyles and the lateral malleolus, and the fibula bears the head and the medial malleolus","The tibia bears the condyles and the medial malleolus, and the fibula bears the head and the lateral malleolus","The tibia bears the head and the medial malleolus, and the fibula bears the condyles and the lateral malleolus"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Only the tibia forms part of the knee joint, so only the tibia has condyles that meet the femur. Its distal end is the medial malleolus. The fibula is a slender lateral bone that stays out of the knee joint, with a simple head at its proximal end and the lateral malleolus at its distal end.","notes":["This swaps the bones completely. The fibula never touches the femur, so it has no knee condyles, and it is lateral, so it carries the lateral malleolus.","The proximal ends are right, but the malleoli are reversed. The tibia is medial, so it ends in the medial malleolus.","Correct. The tibia has the condyles and medial malleolus, and the fibula has the head and lateral malleolus.","The malleoli are right, but the proximal ends are swapped. The tibia is the bone with condyles at the knee, and the fibula ends proximally in its head."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-lower","lid":"c49","comp":48,"competencyId":"w2-leg-bones"},{"id":"L00798","dok":2,"q":"Trace the path of body weight from the sacrum to the femur, naming the bones and joints in order.","a":"Sacrum to ilium at the sacroiliac joint, then the acetabulum to the femoral head at the hip joint.","options":["Sacrum to ilium at the sacroiliac joint, then acetabulum to femoral head at the hip joint","Sacrum to pubis at the pubic symphysis, then acetabulum to femoral head at the hip joint","Sacrum to ischium at the acetabulum, then ilium to femoral neck at the hip joint","Sacrum to ilium at the sacroiliac joint, then obturator foramen to femoral head at the hip joint"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Weight from the vertebral column reaches the sacrum and passes sideways into each ilium through the sacroiliac joint, where the auricular surfaces of the two bones meet. The hip bone then carries the load down to the acetabulum. The acetabulum receives the head of the femur at the hip joint, passing the weight into the thigh.","notes":["Correct. Sacrum to ilium at the sacroiliac joint, then acetabulum to femoral head at the hip.","The sacrum does not touch the pubis. The pubic symphysis joins the two pubic bones at the front, so this path would only fit a question about the front of the pelvic ring.","The sacrum meets the ilium, not the ischium, and the femur articulates by its head, not its neck. This choice mixes up both the joint partners and the femoral marking.","The first joint is right, but the obturator foramen is an opening, not a socket, and the femur does not articulate there. The femoral head sits in the acetabulum."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-lower","lid":"c50","comp":46,"competencyId":"w2-pelvic-girdle"},{"id":"L00799","dok":1,"q":"How many bones fuse to form each hip bone?","a":"Three.","options":["Four","Three","Two","Five"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Each hip bone forms from three bones, the ilium, the ischium, and the pubis, which are separate in a child and fuse at the acetabulum by early adulthood. That is why the adult hip bone has three named regions even though it is one bone. Two hip bones, along with the sacrum and coccyx, make up the bony pelvis.","notes":["Four would come from adding the sacrum to the hip bone. The sacrum joins the hip bone at the sacroiliac joint but is a separate axial bone.","Correct. The ilium, ischium, and pubis fuse to form each hip bone.","Two leaves out one of the three regions. Students sometimes forget the ischium or count only the large ilium and the pubis at the front.","Five is the number of vertebrae that fuse to form the sacrum. Pick it if the question asked how many vertebrae fuse into the sacrum."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-lower","lid":"c1-x2","comp":46,"competencyId":"w2-pelvic-girdle"},{"id":"L00801","dok":1,"q":"Which part of the hip bone is the large, fan-shaped superior portion?","a":"The ilium.","options":["The ilium","The acetabulum","The pubis","The ischium"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The ilium is the largest part of the hip bone, a broad, flared blade that forms its upper portion. Its upper rim is the iliac crest, its smooth inner surface is the iliac fossa, and its auricular surface joins the sacrum. The ilium also forms the upper part of the acetabulum.","notes":["Correct. The ilium is the large fan-shaped upper part of the hip bone.","The acetabulum is the deep socket on the side of the hip bone where all three parts meet. It is a cup, not a fan-shaped blade, so it would fit a question about the hip socket.","The pubis is the anteromedial part of the hip bone that meets its partner at the pubic symphysis. It would be the answer for the front, medial region.","The ischium is the posteroinferior part of the hip bone, including the ischial tuberosity you sit on. Pick it if the question asked for the lower back part."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-lower","lid":"c3-x2","comp":46,"competencyId":"w2-pelvic-girdle"},{"id":"L00804","dok":1,"q":"What is the deep cup where the ilium, ischium, and pubis meet and fuse?","a":"The acetabulum.","options":["The iliac fossa","The obturator foramen","The pubic symphysis","The acetabulum"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The acetabulum is the deep socket on the lateral surface of the hip bone, and each of the three parts, the ilium, ischium, and pubis, contributes a piece of it. Their fusion lines meet in the cup. The acetabulum receives the head of the femur to form the hip joint.","notes":["The iliac fossa is the smooth inner surface of the ilium alone. Pick it if the question asked for the concave inner face of the ilium.","The obturator foramen is the large opening framed by the ischium and pubis only, below the acetabulum. It is a hole, not a cup, so it would be the answer for that opening.","The pubic symphysis is where the left and right pubic bones meet at the front midline. It joins two separate hip bones rather than the three parts of one, so it fits a question about the front joint of the pelvis.","Correct. The three hip bone regions meet in the acetabulum."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-lower","lid":"c6-x2","comp":46,"competencyId":"w2-pelvic-girdle"},{"id":"L00805","dok":1,"q":"Which bone is the longest and strongest bone in the body?","a":"The femur.","options":["The fibula","The humerus","The tibia","The femur"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The femur, the thigh bone, is the longest and strongest bone in the body. It carries the full weight of the upper body from the hip to the knee and anchors the large muscles of the thigh and hip. Its length also makes up a big share of a person's standing height.","notes":["The fibula is long but slender and does not bear body weight. It would fit a question about the thin lateral bone of the leg.","The humerus is the longest bone of the upper limb, but it does not carry body weight and is shorter than the femur. Pick it if the question asked about the arm.","The tibia is the second longest bone and the main weight-bearing bone of the leg, but it is shorter than the femur. It would be the answer for the strongest bone below the knee.","Correct. The femur is the longest and strongest bone in the body."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-lower","lid":"c7-x2","comp":47,"competencyId":"w2-thigh-knee"},{"id":"L00806","dok":1,"q":"In the classification of bones by shape, the kneecap is which type?","a":"A sesamoid bone.","options":["A sesamoid bone","An irregular bone","A flat bone","A long bone"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The patella is a sesamoid bone, meaning a bone that forms within a tendon. It develops inside the quadriceps tendon at the front of the knee. Sesamoid bones protect the tendon and improve its leverage as it slides over the joint.","notes":["Correct. The patella is a sesamoid bone that forms in the quadriceps tendon.","An irregular bone has a complex shape that fits no other category, such as a vertebra. Choose it if the question asked how a vertebra is classified.","A flat bone is thin and plate-like, such as the sternum or the bones of the skull roof. It would be the answer for a bone like the parietal.","A long bone is longer than it is wide, with a shaft and two ends, like the femur or tibia. Pick it if the question asked how the femur is classified."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-lower","lid":"c8-x2","comp":28,"competencyId":"w2-bone-shapes"},{"id":"L00812","dok":1,"q":"What is the small pit on the head of the femur called?","a":"The fovea capitis.","options":["The linea aspera","The fovea capitis","The greater trochanter","The intercondylar fossa"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The fovea capitis is a small pit near the center of the femoral head. It anchors a ligament that runs to the acetabulum, helping hold the head in its socket, and a small artery to the head travels in that ligament. The name means pit of the head.","notes":["The linea aspera is the rough ridge down the back of the femoral shaft, a muscle attachment line. Pick it if the question asked about the ridge on the posterior shaft.","Correct. The fovea capitis is the pit on the femoral head.","The greater trochanter is a large lateral projection, the opposite of a pit. Choose it if the question asked for the big bump on the side of the proximal femur.","The intercondylar fossa is a notch, but it lies between the condyles at the distal femur, not on the head. It would be the answer for the notch at the back of the knee end of the femur."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-lower","lid":"c14-x2","comp":47,"competencyId":"w2-thigh-knee"},{"id":"L00816","dok":2,"q":"Which bony bump of the ankle belongs to the tibia?","a":"The medial malleolus.","options":["The tibial tuberosity","The head of the fibula","The lateral malleolus","The medial malleolus"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The distal end of the tibia projects downward on the inner side of the ankle as the medial malleolus. The tibia is the medial leg bone, so its ankle bump is the medial one. The two malleoli together form the sides of the socket that holds the talus.","notes":["The tibial tuberosity is on the tibia, but at the front of the bone just below the knee, where the patellar ligament attaches. It would be the answer for the bump below the kneecap.","The head of the fibula is the knob at the top of the fibula, below the lateral knee. It belongs to the other leg bone and the other end of the leg.","The lateral malleolus is the outer ankle bump, and it belongs to the fibula. Pick it if the question asked which ankle bump belongs to the fibula.","Correct. The medial malleolus is the tibia's ankle bump."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-lower","lid":"c18-x2","comp":48,"competencyId":"w2-leg-bones"},{"id":"L00817","dok":2,"q":"A surgeon removes a length of one leg bone to use as a bone graft because that bone does not carry body weight. Which bone is it?","a":"The fibula.","options":["The fibula","The patella","The tibia","The femur"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The fibula is the slender lateral bone of the leg, and it does not carry the body's weight. Its main jobs are anchoring muscles and forming the lateral side of the ankle. Because the tibia carries the load, a segment of the fibula's shaft can be removed for a graft while the person can still stand and walk.","notes":["Correct. The fibula does not bear body weight, so part of it can be harvested as a graft.","The patella is a small sesamoid bone in the quadriceps tendon, not a long bone with a shaft to harvest. It would fit a question about the bone in front of the knee.","The tibia is the weight-bearing bone of the leg, so removing part of it would leave the leg unable to support the body. It would be the answer if the question asked which leg bone carries the weight.","The femur carries the entire weight of the body through the thigh. Pick it if the question asked for the longest and strongest bone."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-lower","lid":"c19-x2","comp":48,"competencyId":"w2-leg-bones"},{"id":"L00821","dok":2,"q":"Which surface of the hip bone joins the sacrum at the sacroiliac joint?","a":"The auricular surface.","options":["The acetabulum","The iliac fossa","The auricular surface","The pubic symphysis"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The auricular surface is an ear-shaped area on the medial, posterior part of the ilium. It fits against a matching auricular surface on the side of the sacrum to form the sacroiliac joint. Auricular means ear-shaped, which describes its outline.","notes":["The acetabulum is the socket on the lateral hip bone that receives the femoral head. Pick it if the question asked which surface forms the hip joint.","The iliac fossa is the smooth, concave inner surface of the ilium, in front of the auricular surface. It faces the abdomen and does not articulate with any bone.","Correct. The auricular surface of the ilium meets the sacrum.","The pubic symphysis is the joint where the two pubic bones meet at the front of the pelvis. It would be the answer if the question asked where the hip bones join each other anteriorly."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-lower","lid":"c23-x2","comp":46,"competencyId":"w2-pelvic-girdle"},{"id":"L00822","dok":1,"q":"Besides the talus and the calcaneus, which bones make up the other five tarsals?","a":"The navicular, the cuboid, and the three cuneiforms.","options":["The navicular, the cuboid, and three metatarsals","The scaphoid, the cuboid, and the three cuneiforms","The navicular, the cuboid, and the three cuneiforms","The navicular, the hamate, and the three cuneiforms"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The seven tarsals are the talus, the calcaneus, the navicular, the cuboid, and the medial, intermediate, and lateral cuneiforms. The navicular sits in front of the talus on the medial side, the cuboid sits in front of the calcaneus on the lateral side, and the three cuneiforms lie in front of the navicular.","notes":["The navicular and cuboid are right, but the metatarsals are the five bones of the sole in front of the tarsals, not tarsals themselves. Pick metatarsals if the question asked about the bones between the tarsals and the toes.","The scaphoid is a carpal bone of the wrist, not a tarsal. Students mix it up with the navicular because both are boat-shaped, and in older books the scaphoid was even called the navicular of the hand.","Correct. The navicular, cuboid, and three cuneiforms complete the seven tarsals.","The hamate is a carpal bone of the wrist with a hook-like process. This choice borrows a wrist bone into the foot, which is a common mix-up between carpals and tarsals."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-lower","lid":"c24-x2","comp":49,"competencyId":"w2-foot-bones"},{"id":"L00823","dok":2,"q":"A pelvis is wide and shallow, with a broad pubic angle and a large outlet. Which sex does it most likely belong to, and why?","a":"Female, because a wider outlet is adapted for childbirth.","options":["Female, because a wider outlet is adapted for childbirth","Male, because a wider pelvis supports a heavier frame","Female, because a shallow pelvis protects the organs better","Male, because a broad pubic angle anchors larger muscles"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The typical female pelvis is wider and shallower, with a broader pubic angle and a larger outlet. These features enlarge the opening that a baby passes through during birth. The typical male pelvis is narrower and deeper, with a narrower pubic angle and heavier markings.","notes":["Correct. The wide, shallow shape with a large outlet is a female adaptation for childbirth.","A male pelvis is narrower and deeper, not wider. A heavier build shows up as thicker bone and more prominent markings rather than a wider ring.","The sex is right, but the reason is wrong. A shallower pelvis does not protect organs better; its shape is adapted to widen the birth canal.","A broad pubic angle is a female trait, not a male one. The male pubic angle is narrower, so this choice reverses the comparison."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-lower","lid":"c25-x2","comp":46,"competencyId":"w2-pelvic-girdle"},{"id":"L00824","dok":2,"q":"An older adult with thinned bone falls and fractures the narrow, angled part of the femur just below the head. Which region broke?","a":"The femoral neck.","options":["The lateral condyle","The greater trochanter","The linea aspera","The femoral neck"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The femoral neck is the narrow, angled region that connects the head of the femur to the shaft. It is the usual site of a broken hip, especially in older adults whose bone has thinned from osteoporosis. Because the blood supply to the femoral head runs close to the neck, a fracture here can threaten the head itself.","notes":["The lateral condyle is at the distal femur, at the knee. It would be the answer for an injury to the knee end of the bone.","The greater trochanter is the large lateral bump below the neck. It can be bruised or broken in a fall, but it is a projection, not the narrow region just below the head.","The linea aspera is a ridge along the back of the femoral shaft. Pick it if the question asked about the muscle attachment line on the posterior shaft.","Correct. The femoral neck is the narrow region below the head and the common hip fracture site."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-lower","lid":"c26-x2","comp":47,"competencyId":"w2-thigh-knee"},{"id":"L00825","dok":2,"q":"A person rolls the ankle so the sole turns inward and fractures the bony bump on the outer side of the ankle. Which marking broke?","a":"The lateral malleolus of the fibula.","options":["The lateral malleolus of the fibula","The lateral condyle of the tibia","The head of the fibula","The medial malleolus of the tibia"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The bump on the outer side of the ankle is the lateral malleolus, the distal end of the fibula. It is the most common ankle fracture site, often from rolling the ankle. Because the fibula does not bear body weight, this fracture is usually less serious than a break of the tibia.","notes":["Correct. The outer ankle bump is the lateral malleolus of the fibula.","The lateral condyle of the tibia is at the knee, where the tibia meets the femur. It would fit a question about a fracture of the tibial joint surface at the knee.","The head of the fibula is on the same bone, but at the proximal end, just below the knee. Pick it if the question described an injury on the outer side of the leg near the knee.","The medial malleolus is the inner ankle bump at the distal tibia. It would be the answer if the injury were on the inner side of the ankle."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-lower","lid":"c27-x2","comp":48,"competencyId":"w2-leg-bones"},{"id":"L00826","dok":2,"q":"A patient's footprint shows the whole sole touching the floor, with no gap along the inner edge of the foot. Which structure has dropped?","a":"The medial longitudinal arch.","options":["The transverse arch","The medial longitudinal arch","The lateral longitudinal arch","The calcaneal tendon"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"A normal footprint has a curved gap along the inner edge because the medial longitudinal arch lifts that part of the sole off the ground. When this arch drops, the inner sole flattens onto the floor, a condition called flat feet. The arches normally spread weight across the foot and spring back with each step.","notes":["The transverse arch runs side to side across the foot, under the tarsals and bases of the metatarsals. It would fit a question about the arch that curves across the width of the foot.","Correct. A dropped medial longitudinal arch produces flat feet.","The lateral longitudinal arch runs along the outer edge of the foot and is naturally low, so the outer sole already touches the ground. It would be the answer only if the question described a change along the outer edge.","The calcaneal tendon attaches to the back of the heel and does not form an arch of the sole. Pick it if the question asked which tendon anchors to the calcaneus."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-lower","lid":"c28-x2","comp":49,"competencyId":"w2-foot-bones"},{"id":"L00827","dok":2,"q":"The rounded head of the femur sits in a deep cup formed by three fused bones. Which joint is described?","a":"The hip joint, at the acetabulum.","options":["The knee joint, at the condyles","The hip joint, at the acetabulum","The sacroiliac joint, at the auricular surface","The ankle joint, at the malleoli"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The acetabulum is the deep cup where the ilium, ischium, and pubis fuse, and it receives the head of the femur. Together they form the hip joint, a ball-and-socket joint. The depth of the socket makes the hip very stable for carrying body weight.","notes":["The knee joint is where the femoral condyles rest on the tibial condyles. These are rounded knobs on fairly flat surfaces, not a ball in a deep cup.","Correct. The femoral head in the acetabulum forms the hip joint.","The sacroiliac joint is where the auricular surface of the ilium meets the sacrum. Pick it if the question asked where the hip bone joins the vertebral column.","The ankle joint is where the talus fits between the tibia and fibula, with the malleoli forming its sides. It does not involve the femur."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-lower","lid":"c29-x2","comp":46,"competencyId":"w2-pelvic-girdle"},{"id":"L00828","dok":1,"q":"Following the lower limb from the hip to the knee, which single bone spans that region?","a":"The femur.","options":["The femur","The talus","The tibia","The fibula"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The region from the hip to the knee is the thigh, and it contains a single long bone, the femur. Below the knee, the leg has two bones, the tibia and fibula. The patella sits at the knee but is not part of the thigh's skeleton.","notes":["Correct. The femur is the only bone of the thigh.","The talus is a tarsal bone at the ankle. It would fit a question about the bone that receives weight from the tibia.","The tibia spans the leg, from the knee to the ankle. It would be the answer if the question asked about the medial bone below the knee.","The fibula spans the lateral leg, from just below the knee to the ankle. Pick it if the question asked about the slender lateral leg bone."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-lower","lid":"c30-x2","comp":47,"competencyId":"w2-thigh-knee"},{"id":"L00830","dok":2,"q":"The femur is the longest and strongest bone in the body. Which fact about its position best explains why it must be so strong?","a":"It carries the body's weight from the hip to the knee during standing and walking.","options":["It shields the pelvic organs from blows to the side of the hip","It carries the body's weight from the hip to the knee during standing and walking","It anchors the small muscles of the foot that push off during walking","It spans from the hip all the way to the ankle"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The femur is the only bone in the thigh, so all the weight of the head, trunk, and upper limbs passes through it on the way from the pelvis to the knee. Walking, running, and jumping multiply those forces. Large thigh and hip muscles also pull on it, so its thick, strong shaft is built to resist heavy loads.","notes":["The femur does not shield the pelvic organs. The bony pelvis, made of the hip bones, sacrum, and coccyx, surrounds and protects them.","Correct. The femur carries body weight through the thigh, so it must be strong.","The muscles of the foot attach to the bones of the leg and foot, not the femur. The femur anchors large hip and thigh muscles instead.","The femur ends at the knee. The tibia and fibula span the distance from the knee to the ankle, so this choice stretches the thigh bone too far."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-lower","lid":"d3-t-appendicular-skeleton-lower-2","comp":47,"competencyId":"w2-thigh-knee"},{"id":"L00831","dok":3,"q":"The patella sits within the tendon in front of the knee. How does this bone, embedded in the tendon, improve knee function?","a":"It increases the leverage of the quadriceps tendon for extending the knee.","options":["It forms the main weight-bearing surface between the femur and tibia","It anchors the cruciate ligaments inside the knee joint","It increases the leverage of the quadriceps tendon for extending the knee","It locks the knee in flexion so the leg cannot straighten"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The patella is a sesamoid bone that forms inside the quadriceps tendon. It holds the tendon farther away from the knee joint as it crosses the front of the knee, which gives the quadriceps better leverage when it straightens the leg. It also protects the front of the joint and keeps the tendon in position as it glides over the femur.","notes":["The weight-bearing surfaces of the knee are the femoral and tibial condyles, cushioned by the menisci. The patella glides on the front of the femur and does not carry body weight between the bones.","The cruciate ligaments attach to the femur and tibia inside the joint, not to the patella. Pick the intercondylar fossa of the femur if the question asked where the cruciates anchor on the femur.","Correct. The patella improves the leverage of the quadriceps for knee extension.","The patella helps the quadriceps straighten the knee, not stop it. This choice reverses the bone's role."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-lower","lid":"d3-t-appendicular-skeleton-lower-3","comp":47,"competencyId":"w2-thigh-knee"},{"id":"L00832","dok":3,"q":"The leg has two bones, but the tibia is much thicker than the fibula. Which explanation fits their different builds?","a":"The tibia carries body weight from knee to ankle, while the fibula mainly anchors muscles and steadies the ankle.","options":["The tibia carries the weight, while the fibula anchors muscles and steadies the ankle","The fibula carries weight to the heel, while the tibia mainly anchors muscles","The fibula forms part of the knee joint, so it must stay thin to let the knee bend","The two bones share body weight equally, so the size difference is cosmetic"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The tibia meets the femur at the knee and the talus at the ankle, so the body's weight passes straight down through it. The fibula sits beside it and does not join the knee joint, so it carries little load. Its jobs are to anchor leg muscles and form the lateral wall of the ankle with the lateral malleolus.","notes":["Correct. The thick tibia carries the weight, and the slender fibula anchors muscles and steadies the ankle.","This reverses the roles. The tibia is the weight-bearing bone, and weight passes from it to the talus, not from the fibula to the heel.","The fibula does not take part in the knee joint at all. Only the femur, tibia, and patella form the knee, so this explanation rests on a false premise.","The bones do not share weight equally. Bone thickness generally matches load, and the thick tibia carries nearly all of it."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-lower","lid":"d3-t-appendicular-skeleton-lower-4","comp":48,"competencyId":"w2-leg-bones"},{"id":"L00833","dok":3,"q":"The bones of the foot form arches instead of lying flat. Why is an arched foot better than a flat platform?","a":"The arches spread weight across the sole and spring back to absorb shock with each step.","options":["They hold the foot rigid so it cannot flex during walking","They lift the heel off the ground so only the toes bear weight","They shorten the foot so it fits the ankle joint more tightly","They spread weight across the sole and give with each step"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The medial and lateral longitudinal arches and the transverse arch turn the foot into a curved, springy structure. Weight from the talus is spread back to the heel and forward to the heads of the metatarsals instead of resting on one spot. With each step the arches give slightly and then recoil, absorbing shock.","notes":["The arches do the opposite. They let the foot give and recoil under load rather than staying rigid.","The heel, the calcaneus, is one of the main weight-bearing points of a standing foot. The arches spread weight between the heel and the balls of the foot rather than lifting the heel.","The arches do not change how the talus fits the ankle joint. This choice links two separate features of the foot that are not related.","Correct. The arches distribute weight and act as shock absorbers."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-lower","lid":"d3-t-appendicular-skeleton-lower-5","comp":49,"competencyId":"w2-foot-bones"},{"id":"L00835","dok":2,"q":"Which feature of a typical female pelvis is an adaptation for childbirth?","a":"A broader pubic angle and a larger outlet.","options":["A narrower pubic angle and a smaller outlet","A taller, narrower true pelvis","A deeper pelvis with heavier markings","A broader pubic angle and a larger outlet"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"A typical female pelvis is wider and shallower than a male pelvis, with a broader pubic angle and a larger outlet. These features enlarge the bony passage the baby moves through during birth. A typical male pelvis is narrower and deeper, with a narrower pubic angle and heavier markings.","notes":["A narrower pubic angle and smaller outlet describe the typical male pelvis. This would be the answer if the question asked for a male feature.","A taller, narrower true pelvis would shrink the birth canal. The female true pelvis is wider and shallower, so this choice reverses the adaptation.","A deeper pelvis with heavier markings is typical of a male pelvis, reflecting a heavier build. Pick it if the question asked how a male pelvis differs.","Correct. A broader pubic angle and larger outlet widen the birth canal."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-lower","lid":"d3-t-appendicular-skeleton-lower-7","comp":46,"competencyId":"w2-pelvic-girdle"},{"id":"L00836","dok":3,"q":"The calcaneus is large and projects backward behind the ankle. Why does its size and shape suit its job?","a":"It takes the impact of heel strike and gives the calcaneal tendon leverage to raise the heel.","options":["It articulates with all five metatarsals, so it must be wide","It absorbs heel strike and gives the calcaneal tendon leverage","It receives the body's weight directly from the tibia","It forms the ankle joint between the two malleoli"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The calcaneus is the heel bone, the largest tarsal. When you walk, the heel hits the ground first, so this bone must withstand that impact. Its backward projection also anchors the calcaneal (Achilles) tendon far behind the ankle, giving the calf muscles leverage to lift the heel and push off.","notes":["The calcaneus articulates in front with the cuboid, not with the metatarsals. The metatarsals meet the cuboid and the three cuneiforms.","Correct. The calcaneus absorbs heel strike and gives the calcaneal tendon leverage.","The talus, not the calcaneus, receives weight directly from the tibia. Pick the talus if the question asked which tarsal sits under the leg bones.","The talus sits between the malleoli and forms the ankle joint. The calcaneus lies below the talus and is not part of that joint."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-lower","lid":"d3-t-appendicular-skeleton-lower-8","comp":49,"competencyId":"w2-foot-bones"},{"id":"N-m2-lower-extremity-1","dok":1,"q":"Which small bump sits at the top of the medial epicondyle of the femur?","a":"The adductor tubercle.","options":["The gluteal tuberosity","The adductor tubercle","The medial condyle","The lesser trochanter"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The adductor tubercle is a small, raised bump on the upper edge of the medial epicondyle, near the distal end of the femur. It marks where the medial supracondylar line ends and serves as an attachment point for part of the adductor magnus muscle, which is how it got its name. On a lab femur, find the medial epicondyle first and then look just above it.","notes":["The gluteal tuberosity is a rough area on the posterior, proximal femur below the greater trochanter. It would fit a question about the upper back of the femoral shaft.","Correct. The adductor tubercle sits on top of the medial epicondyle.","The medial condyle is the large rounded joint surface below the medial epicondyle that rests on the tibia. It would be the answer for the knob that forms part of the knee joint.","The lesser trochanter is also on the medial side, but at the proximal end of the femur, just below the neck. Pick it if the question asked for the small medial projection near the hip."],"comp":47,"competencyId":"w2-thigh-knee"},{"id":"N-m2-lower-extremity-2","dok":2,"q":"Which ridge runs between the greater and lesser trochanters on the anterior surface of the femur?","a":"The intertrochanteric line.","options":["The intertrochanteric line","The gluteal tuberosity","The linea aspera","The intertrochanteric crest"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Two markings join the greater and lesser trochanters. On the front of the femur, a low ridge called the intertrochanteric line connects them, and on the back, a more prominent ridge called the intertrochanteric crest does the same. A useful way to remember it is that the line is on the front and the crest, which stands out more, is on the back.","notes":["Correct. The intertrochanteric line joins the trochanters on the anterior surface.","The gluteal tuberosity is a rough area on the posterior femur below the greater trochanter. It would be the answer for the attachment area on the upper back of the shaft.","The linea aspera runs down the posterior shaft of the femur, not between the trochanters. It would be the answer for the long rough ridge on the back of the shaft.","The intertrochanteric crest also joins the two trochanters, but it runs across the posterior surface. Pick it if the question asked about the back of the femur."],"comp":47,"competencyId":"w2-thigh-knee"},{"id":"N-m2-lower-extremity-3","dok":2,"q":"Which roughened area on the posterior femur, just below the greater trochanter, continues down into the lateral lip of the linea aspera?","a":"The gluteal tuberosity.","options":["The gluteal tuberosity","The lesser trochanter","The intertrochanteric crest","The adductor tubercle"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The gluteal tuberosity is a rough strip on the posterior surface of the proximal femur, below the greater trochanter. It runs downward and blends into the lateral lip of the linea aspera. Its name comes from the gluteus maximus, which attaches to it.","notes":["Correct. The gluteal tuberosity lies below the greater trochanter and leads into the linea aspera.","The lesser trochanter is a rounded projection on the medial side of the proximal femur. It is a single knob, not a rough strip that continues down the shaft.","The intertrochanteric crest is a ridge running between the greater and lesser trochanters on the back of the femur, above the shaft. It would be the answer for the ridge joining the two trochanters posteriorly.","The adductor tubercle is a small bump at the distal femur, on top of the medial epicondyle. It would be the answer for the bump just above the knee on the medial side."],"comp":47,"competencyId":"w2-thigh-knee"},{"id":"N-m2-lower-extremity-4","dok":1,"q":"Which smooth surface on the anterior distal femur does the patella glide over?","a":"The patellar surface.","options":["The tibial tuberosity","The intercondylar fossa","The patellar surface","The medial epicondyle"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The patellar surface is a smooth, shallow groove on the front of the distal femur, between the condyles. The back of the patella slides up and down in it as the knee bends and straightens, forming the patellofemoral joint. It is one of the three articulations inside the knee joint cavity.","notes":["The tibial tuberosity is on the anterior tibia, below the knee. It anchors the patellar ligament rather than providing a surface for the patella to glide on.","The intercondylar fossa is the notch between the condyles on the posterior side of the distal femur. Pick it if the question asked about the back of the knee end of the femur.","Correct. The patella glides on the patellar surface of the femur.","The medial epicondyle is a projection on the medial side of the distal femur, above the medial condyle. It is a ligament attachment site, not a gliding surface."],"comp":47,"competencyId":"w2-thigh-knee"},{"id":"N-m2-lower-extremity-5","dok":2,"q":"Which ridge projects upward between the medial and lateral condyles on the top of the tibia?","a":"The intercondylar eminence.","options":["The anterior border","The intercondylar fossa","The tibial tuberosity","The intercondylar eminence"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The intercondylar eminence is a raised ridge on the superior surface of the tibia, between its two flat condyles. It fits up into the intercondylar fossa of the femur, and the cruciate ligaments attach in front of and behind it. The names help: an eminence sticks up, and a fossa dips in.","notes":["The anterior border is the sharp ridge down the front of the tibial shaft that you feel as the shin. It would be the answer for the shin, not the top of the tibia.","The intercondylar fossa is the notch between the condyles of the femur, not the tibia. It is a depression rather than a ridge, and it sits just above the eminence when the knee is assembled.","The tibial tuberosity is on the front of the tibia below the knee, where the patellar ligament attaches. It would be the answer for the anterior bump below the kneecap.","Correct. The intercondylar eminence rises between the tibial condyles."],"comp":48,"competencyId":"w2-leg-bones"},{"id":"N-m2-lower-extremity-6","dok":2,"q":"The fibular notch, where the distal fibula fits against the tibia, is found on which part of the tibia?","a":"The lateral side of the distal tibia.","options":["The superior surface between the condyles","The anterior surface of the proximal tibia","The medial side of the distal tibia","The lateral side of the distal tibia"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The fibular notch is a shallow groove on the lateral side of the distal tibia. The lower end of the fibula sits in it, and the two bones are held together there by ligaments at the distal tibiofibular joint, a syndesmosis. Because the fibula is the lateral leg bone, its notch on the tibia faces laterally.","notes":["The superior surface between the condyles holds the intercondylar eminence. It would be the answer if the question asked where the ridge between the tibial condyles is found.","The anterior surface of the proximal tibia holds the tibial tuberosity, where the patellar ligament attaches. Pick that location if the question asked about the tuberosity.","The medial side of the distal tibia is where the medial malleolus projects. The fibula lies lateral to the tibia, so it cannot meet the tibia on the medial side.","Correct. The fibular notch is on the lateral side of the distal tibia."],"comp":48,"competencyId":"w2-leg-bones"},{"id":"N-m2-lower-extremity-7","dok":1,"q":"Which notch lies just below the ischial spine?","a":"The lesser sciatic notch.","options":["The obturator foramen","The ischial tuberosity","The lesser sciatic notch","The greater sciatic notch"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The ischial spine divides two notches on the posterior border of the hip bone. Above it is the large greater sciatic notch, and below it is the smaller lesser sciatic notch, which lies between the spine and the ischial tuberosity. Using the spine as the landmark keeps the two notches straight.","notes":["The obturator foramen is a large opening framed by the ischium and pubis, in front of the ischium. It is a full hole, not a notch on the posterior border.","The ischial tuberosity is the knob you sit on, just below the lesser sciatic notch. It is a projection, not a notch.","Correct. The lesser sciatic notch is just below the ischial spine.","The greater sciatic notch lies above the ischial spine, on the ilium. Pick it if the question asked where the sciatic nerve leaves the pelvis."],"comp":46,"competencyId":"w2-pelvic-girdle"},{"id":"N-m2-lower-extremity-8","dok":1,"q":"Which projection lies on the anterior border of the ilium, just below the anterior superior iliac spine?","a":"The anterior inferior iliac spine.","options":["The posterior superior iliac spine","The posterior inferior iliac spine","The anterior inferior iliac spine","The ischial spine"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The ilium has four spines, named for front or back and upper or lower. On the anterior border, the anterior superior iliac spine marks the front end of the iliac crest, and the anterior inferior iliac spine sits a little below it. The posterior border has a matching pair at the back.","notes":["The posterior superior iliac spine is at the back end of the iliac crest. Pick it if the question asked for the upper spine on the posterior border.","The posterior inferior iliac spine is just below the posterior superior spine, at the back of the ilium. It would be the answer for the lower spine on the posterior border.","Correct. The anterior inferior iliac spine is just below the anterior superior iliac spine.","The ischial spine is on the ischium, between the greater and lesser sciatic notches. It is not on the ilium at all."],"comp":46,"competencyId":"w2-pelvic-girdle"},{"id":"N-m2-lower-extremity-9","dok":1,"q":"Which tarsal bone lies directly in front of the talus on the medial side of the foot?","a":"The navicular.","options":["The medial cuneiform","The cuboid","The calcaneus","The navicular"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The navicular is a boat-shaped tarsal on the medial side of the foot. It sits between the head of the talus behind it and the three cuneiforms in front of it. On the lateral side, the cuboid holds the matching position in front of the calcaneus.","notes":["The medial cuneiform is one row farther forward, in front of the navicular and behind metatarsal I. It would be the answer for the tarsal that meets the base of the great toe's metatarsal.","The cuboid is on the lateral side of the foot, in front of the calcaneus. Pick it if the question asked for the lateral tarsal that meets metatarsals IV and V.","The calcaneus is the heel bone, below and behind the talus, not in front of it. It would be the answer for the largest tarsal.","Correct. The navicular sits in front of the talus on the medial side."],"comp":49,"competencyId":"w2-foot-bones"},{"id":"N-m2-lower-extremity-10","dok":2,"q":"The three cuneiforms articulate with which metatarsals?","a":"Metatarsals I, II, and III.","options":["Metatarsals IV and V only","Metatarsals III, IV, and V","Metatarsals II, III, and IV","Metatarsals I, II, and III"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The medial, intermediate, and lateral cuneiforms line up with the first three metatarsals: the medial cuneiform with metatarsal I, the intermediate with metatarsal II, and the lateral with metatarsal III. The cuboid, on the lateral side, articulates with metatarsals IV and V. Remembering that the metatarsals are numbered from the great toe makes the pattern easy to place.","notes":["Metatarsals IV and V articulate with the cuboid. Pick this if the question asked which metatarsals meet the cuboid.","Metatarsals IV and V meet the cuboid, not the cuneiforms. This choice shifts the cuneiforms toward the lateral side of the foot.","This leaves out metatarsal I, which meets the medial cuneiform, and includes metatarsal IV, which meets the cuboid. It is off by one position.","Correct. The cuneiforms meet metatarsals I, II, and III."],"comp":49,"competencyId":"w2-foot-bones"},{"id":"N-m2-lower-extremity-11","dok":1,"q":"Which phalanx does the great toe lack?","a":"The middle phalanx.","options":["The distal phalanx","The middle phalanx","It lacks none of them","The proximal phalanx"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The hallux, or great toe, has only two phalanges, a proximal and a distal. Toes 2 to 5 each have three: proximal, middle, and distal. That is why each foot has fourteen phalanges instead of fifteen.","notes":["The great toe does have a distal phalanx, the one at its tip that carries the toenail. The missing one is the middle phalanx.","Correct. The hallux has no middle phalanx.","If the great toe had all three phalanges, each foot would have fifteen. Students who assume every toe matches the others choose this.","The great toe does have a proximal phalanx, the one that joins metatarsal I. It is the middle phalanx that is missing."],"comp":49,"competencyId":"w2-foot-bones"},{"id":"N-m2-lower-extremity-12","dok":1,"q":"Which small projection sits at the lateral end of the pubic crest?","a":"The pubic tubercle.","options":["The pubic tubercle","The pubic symphysis","The ischial spine","The anterior inferior iliac spine"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The pubic crest is the ridge along the upper border of the body of the pubis. At its lateral end it rises into a small bump called the pubic tubercle. The tubercle is a useful landmark at the front of the pelvis, low and to each side of the midline.","notes":["Correct. The pubic tubercle is at the lateral end of the pubic crest.","The pubic symphysis is the joint at the medial end of the pubis where the two pubic bones meet, not a projection at the lateral end of the crest.","The ischial spine is a pointed projection on the posterior ischium, inside the pelvis. It would be the answer for the projection between the greater and lesser sciatic notches.","The anterior inferior iliac spine is on the front of the ilium, above the acetabulum. It would be the answer for the projection just below the ASIS."],"comp":46,"competencyId":"w2-pelvic-girdle"},{"id":"N-m2-lower-extremity-13","dok":2,"q":"Which bars of bone extend from the body of the pubis?","a":"The superior and inferior pubic rami.","options":["The superior and inferior pubic rami","The greater and lesser trochanters","The ischial spine and ischial tuberosity","The anterior superior and inferior iliac spines"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Each pubis has a body near the midline and two bars of bone, called rami, that extend from it. The superior pubic ramus runs toward the acetabulum, and the inferior pubic ramus runs down and back to join the ischium. Together with the ischium, the rami frame the obturator foramen.","notes":["Correct. The superior and inferior pubic rami extend from the body of the pubis.","The trochanters are projections of the proximal femur, not the hip bone. They would be the answer for the muscle attachment sites near the femoral neck.","The ischial spine and tuberosity are projections of the ischium at the back and bottom of the hip bone. They would be the answer for markings of the ischium.","The anterior iliac spines are projections on the front border of the ilium, not bars extending from the pubis. Pick them if the question asked about the front of the ilium."],"comp":46,"competencyId":"w2-pelvic-girdle"},{"id":"N-a17d-46-1","dok":3,"q":"Sitting on a hard bench, a person feels pressure on two bony knobs. Which part of the hip bone carries these knobs, and which pointed projection lies just above each one?","a":"The ischium; the ischial spine lies above each tuberosity.","options":["The ilium; the anterior superior iliac spine lies above.","The pubis; the pubic tubercle lies above each knob.","The ischium; the iliac crest lies just above each tuberosity.","The ischium; the ischial spine lies above each tuberosity."],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The knobs you sit on are the ischial tuberosities, the strong, roughened knobs of the ischium, the posteroinferior part of the hip bone. Just above each tuberosity is the ischial spine, a pointed projection, with the lesser sciatic notch below the spine.","notes":["The ilium is the large fan-shaped superior part. The ASIS is at the front of the iliac crest, far from where you sit.","The pubis is the anteromedial part of the hip bone, at the front of the pelvis. The knobs you sit on are on the ischium.","The part is right, but the iliac crest is the upper ridge of the ilium. The ischial spine is the projection just above the tuberosity.","Correct. You sit on the ischial tuberosities, and the ischial spine sits just above each one."],"comp":46,"competencyId":"w2-pelvic-girdle"},{"id":"N-a17d-46-2","dok":3,"q":"A clinician presses the prominent point at the front of the iliac crest, then follows the crest backward to its far end. Which two landmarks are touched, and which bone are they on?","a":"The anterior then posterior superior iliac spines, on the ilium.","options":["The anterior then posterior superior iliac spines, on the ischium.","The anterior superior iliac spine, then the ischial spine.","The anterior inferior iliac spine, then the greater sciatic notch.","The anterior then posterior superior iliac spines, on the ilium."],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Follow the crest. The iliac crest is the curved upper ridge of the ilium, the rim you rest your hands on. Its prominent front point is the anterior superior iliac spine, and its back end is the posterior superior iliac spine, so both landmarks are on the ilium.","notes":["The landmarks are right, but the iliac crest and its spines belong to the ilium. The ischium is the posteroinferior part you sit on.","The ischial spine is on the ischium, above the ischial tuberosity. Following the crest backward keeps you on the ilium.","The AIIS lies below the ASIS, not on the crest, and the greater sciatic notch is on the posterior border, below the crest's end.","Correct. The ASIS is at the front of the iliac crest and the PSIS is at the back, both on the ilium."],"comp":46,"competencyId":"w2-pelvic-girdle"},{"id":"N-a17d-46-3","dok":3,"q":"A deep buttock wound injures the sciatic nerve, the largest nerve in the body, where it passes through a notch in the hip bone. Which notch is it, and where on the hip bone does it lie?","a":"The greater sciatic notch, on the posterior border of the ilium.","options":["The lesser sciatic notch, just below the ischial spine.","The greater sciatic notch, on the posterior border of the ilium.","The obturator foramen, framed by the ischium and the pubis.","The greater sciatic notch, on the posterior border of the pubis."],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Start with the nerve. The sciatic nerve, the largest nerve in the body, passes through the greater sciatic notch. That notch is the deep notch on the posterior border of the ilium, the large fan-shaped superior part of the hip bone.","notes":["The lesser sciatic notch lies on the ischium, just below the ischial spine. The sciatic nerve passes through the greater sciatic notch on the ilium instead.","Correct. The sciatic nerve passes through the greater sciatic notch, the deep notch on the posterior border of the ilium.","The obturator foramen is the large opening framed by the ischium and the pubis, at the front of the hip bone. It is an opening, not the notch the sciatic nerve passes through.","The notch is right, but it lies on the ilium. The pubis is the anteromedial part of the hip bone, at the front of the pelvis."],"comp":46,"competencyId":"w2-pelvic-girdle"},{"id":"N-a17d-47-1","dok":3,"q":"On a loose femur, a student sees a rough ridge running down the shaft and a notch between the distal knobs on the same side. Which surface is the student looking at, and what are the ridge and notch?","a":"Posterior; the linea aspera and the intercondylar fossa.","options":["Anterior; the linea aspera and the intercondylar fossa.","Posterior; the linea aspera and the intercondylar fossa.","Posterior; the intertrochanteric line and the patellar surface.","Anterior; the gluteal tuberosity and the fovea capitis."],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Name the markings, then use them to orient the bone. The rough ridge down the shaft is the linea aspera, and the notch between the distal condyles is the intercondylar fossa. Both are on the posterior surface, so the student is looking at the back of the femur.","notes":["The markings are right, but both sit on the posterior surface. The anterior distal femur has the smooth patellar surface instead.","Correct. The linea aspera runs down the posterior shaft, and the intercondylar fossa is on the posterior surface between the condyles.","The intertrochanteric line is on the anterior surface near the top, and the patellar surface is the smooth anterior surface the patella glides over.","The gluteal tuberosity is a roughened area near the top of the posterior femur, and the fovea capitis is the pit on the femoral head."],"comp":47,"competencyId":"w2-thigh-knee"},{"id":"N-a17d-47-2","dok":3,"q":"On a knee model, a student sets the patella in place with its pointed tip facing up. Which part is at the top, and how should the patella be turned for anatomical position?","a":"The apex; turn it so the apex points down.","options":["The base; turn it so the base points down.","The apex; it is already in anatomical position.","The base; it is already in anatomical position.","The apex; turn it so the apex points down."],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Name the parts first. The base is the broad superior border of the patella, and the apex is its pointed inferior tip. If the pointed tip is facing up, the bone is upside down, so it must be turned until the apex points down and the base sits on top.","notes":["The pointed tip is the apex, not the base. The base is the broad border and belongs on top.","The tip is the apex, but the apex is the inferior part. With it facing up, the patella is upside down.","The pointed tip is the apex, and pointing up is the wrong way. The broad base belongs at the top.","Correct. The apex is the pointed inferior tip, so in anatomical position it points down."],"comp":47,"competencyId":"w2-thigh-knee"},{"id":"N-a17d-48-1","dok":1,"q":"Which marking on the front of the tibia anchors the patellar ligament?","a":"The tibial tuberosity.","options":["The medial malleolus.","The intercondylar eminence.","The tibial tuberosity.","The fibular notch."],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The tibial tuberosity is a roughened projection on the front of the proximal tibia, below the condyles. The patellar ligament runs from the patella down to it.","notes":["The medial malleolus is the medial bump of the ankle at the distal end of the tibia, far from the knee.","The intercondylar eminence is the ridge projecting up between the tibial condyles, on top of the tibia rather than on its front.","Correct. The tibial tuberosity on the front of the tibia anchors the patellar ligament.","The fibular notch is on the distal tibia, where the fibula fits against it. It is not a ligament anchor at the knee."],"comp":48,"competencyId":"w2-leg-bones"},{"id":"N-a17d-48-2","dok":1,"q":"What do the medial and lateral condyles of the tibia articulate with?","a":"The femur.","options":["The talus.","The femur.","The fibula.","The patella."],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The medial and lateral condyles are the broad proximal knobs of the tibia. They articulate with the medial and lateral condyles of the femur, forming the medial and lateral tibiofemoral joints of the knee.","notes":["The talus articulates with the distal tibia and fibula at the ankle, not with the tibial condyles at the knee.","Correct. The tibial condyles articulate with the condyles of the femur at the knee.","The fibula meets the tibia at the side of the leg, but the condyles on top of the tibia meet the femur.","The patella glides on the patellar surface of the femur. It does not articulate with the tibial condyles."],"comp":48,"competencyId":"w2-leg-bones"},{"id":"N-a17d-48-3","dok":1,"q":"Which bone is the slender lateral bone of the leg?","a":"The fibula.","options":["The tibia.","The fibula.","The femur.","The ulna."],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The leg has two bones. The fibula is the slender, lateral one and does not bear body weight. The tibia is the thicker, medial shin bone that carries the weight.","notes":["The tibia is the thick, medial, weight-bearing shin bone, not the slender lateral one.","Correct. The fibula is the slender bone on the lateral side of the leg.","The femur is the thigh bone, from hip to knee. It is not a bone of the leg.","The ulna is the medial bone of the forearm. The slender lateral bone of the leg is the fibula."],"comp":48,"competencyId":"w2-leg-bones"},{"id":"N-a17d-48-4","dok":3,"q":"A runner rolls an ankle and fractures the bony bump on the outer side of the ankle. Why is this usually less serious for weight bearing than a fracture of the other leg bone?","a":"The fibula is broken, and the tibia carries the body weight.","options":["The tibia is broken, but its malleolus carries no weight.","The fibula is broken, and it carries most of the body weight.","The fibula is broken, and the tibia carries the body weight.","The tibia is broken, and it shares weight equally with the fibula."],"correctIndex":2,"why":"First name the bone. The bump on the outer side of the ankle is the lateral malleolus of the fibula. Then link it to weight bearing: the tibia carries the body weight and the fibula does not, which is why a tibial fracture is the more serious of the two.","notes":["The outer ankle bump is the lateral malleolus of the fibula. The tibia forms the medial malleolus, and it is the weight-bearing bone.","The bone is right, but the fibula does not bear body weight. If it did, the break would be more serious, not less.","Correct. The lateral malleolus belongs to the fibula, which does not bear weight, while the tibia does.","The bump on the outer ankle belongs to the fibula, and the two bones do not share the weight. The tibia carries it."],"comp":48,"competencyId":"w2-leg-bones"},{"id":"N-a17d-48-5","dok":3,"q":"A student must identify a loose leg bone. It is thick, has two condyles on top, a rough bump on the front below them, and one bump projecting from the side of its lower end. Which bone is it, and where does that lower bump lie?","a":"The tibia; the medial malleolus lies on the medial side.","options":["The fibula; the lateral malleolus lies on the lateral side.","The tibia; the medial malleolus lies on the medial side.","The tibia; the lateral malleolus lies on the lateral side.","The femur; the medial condyle lies on the medial side."],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Identify the bone from its features, then place the bump. A thick leg bone with two condyles on top and a tibial tuberosity on the front is the tibia, the weight-bearing bone. The bump on the side of its lower end is the medial malleolus, which faces the medial side of the ankle.","notes":["The fibula is slender, with only a head at its proximal end. Condyles and a tuberosity on a thick bone mark the tibia.","Correct. A thick bone with condyles and a tibial tuberosity is the tibia, and its lower bump is the medial malleolus.","The bone is right, but the lateral malleolus belongs to the fibula. The tibia forms the medial malleolus.","The femur is a thigh bone with its condyles at the distal end and a head at the top, not a leg bone."],"comp":48,"competencyId":"w2-leg-bones"},{"id":"N-a17d-49-1","dok":3,"q":"A person lands on the heels after a fall and fractures the largest tarsal. Which bone broke, and which tarsal sits on top of it, carrying weight down from the tibia?","a":"The calcaneus; the talus sits on top of it.","options":["The talus; the calcaneus sits on top of it.","The calcaneus; the talus sits on top of it.","The calcaneus; the navicular sits on top of it.","The cuboid; the talus sits on top of it."],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Identify the broken bone first. The largest tarsal is the calcaneus, the heel bone. The tarsal that articulates with the tibia and fibula and receives the body's weight is the talus, which sits on top of the calcaneus and passes the weight down to the heel.","notes":["This swaps the two. The talus receives the weight from the tibia and sits above the calcaneus, the largest tarsal.","Correct. The calcaneus is the largest tarsal, the heel bone, and the talus sits above it.","The navicular is one of the other tarsals, in front of the talus on the medial side. It does not receive weight from the tibia.","The cuboid is one of the smaller tarsals. The largest tarsal, the heel bone, is the calcaneus."],"comp":49,"competencyId":"w2-foot-bones"}]},{"id":"m2-joints","title":"Joints","summary":"structural and functional classes of joints, synovial joint parts and types, named joints and ligaments","mw":"2026-09-28","tr":"2026-09-24","cards":[{"id":"L00743","dok":3,"q":"The head of the humerus sits in the shallow glenoid cavity. Why does this give the shoulder a huge range of motion but also make it prone to dislocation?","a":"A shallow socket lets the head move freely in many directions but holds it less securely.","options":["A shallow socket holds the head tightly, so the joint rarely dislocates","A shallow socket lets the head move freely in many directions but holds it less securely","The shoulder is a pivot joint, so it rotates around a single axis","The shoulder is a hinge joint, so it moves freely in one plane only"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The glenohumeral joint is a ball-and-socket joint with a very shallow socket. Little bone surrounds the humeral head, so it can swing in nearly every direction. That same lack of bony containment means muscles and ligaments do most of the holding, and the head can slip out under force.","notes":["A deep socket that holds the head tightly describes the hip joint, which is stable and rarely dislocates. Choose this description for a question about the acetabulum.","Correct. A shallow socket trades security for range of motion.","A pivot joint that rotates around one axis describes the atlanto-axial joint or the proximal radioulnar joint. It would be right for a question about turning the head or the palm.","A hinge joint that moves in one plane describes the elbow between the humerus and ulna. Pick it for a question about bending and straightening the elbow."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-upper","lid":"d3-t-appendicular-skeleton-upper-3","comp":55,"competencyId":"w2-shoulder-knee"},{"id":"L00745","dok":3,"q":"The joint between the humerus and ulna is a hinge that moves in one plane. Why does a hinge suit this joint better than a ball and socket?","a":"The elbow needs strong, stable bending and straightening in one plane.","options":["A hinge has no joint cavity, which makes it stronger","A hinge lets the elbow turn the palm up and down","A hinge lets the elbow move in every direction like the shoulder","The elbow needs strong, stable bending and straightening in one plane"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The elbow's main job is to shorten and lengthen the reach of the arm by flexing and extending, often while lifting or pushing. A hinge, with the trochlear notch wrapped around the trochlea, does this strongly and stays stable under load. Motion in many directions would make the joint wobbly and weak for that task.","notes":["The elbow is a synovial joint with a joint cavity, like all hinge joints. Joints without a cavity are fibrous or cartilaginous, such as sutures or the pubic symphysis.","Turning the palm happens at the radioulnar joints, where the radius rotates around the ulna, not at the hinge. Choose that for a question about pronation and supination.","Moving in every direction describes a ball-and-socket joint like the shoulder, the opposite of a hinge. Students pick this when they confuse the definitions of the two joint types.","Correct. A hinge gives stable flexion and extension in one plane."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-upper","lid":"d3-t-appendicular-skeleton-upper-5","comp":54,"competencyId":"w2-synovial-types"},{"id":"L00747","dok":2,"q":"The joint between the trapezium and the first metacarpal is a saddle joint. What does this joint shape let the thumb do?","a":"Swing across the palm to touch the fingertips (opposition).","options":["Move only like a hinge in one plane","Swing across the palm to touch the fingertips (opposition)","Bend at a middle phalanx like the other fingers","Rotate the radius to turn the palm over"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"In a saddle joint, both surfaces are curved like a horse saddle, one fitting crosswise into the other. This allows movement in two planes, so the thumb can move toward and away from the palm and across it. Combining those motions lets the thumb meet the fingertips, which makes a precision grip possible.","notes":["Moving like a hinge in one plane describes the joints between the phalanges, not the base of the thumb. Choose a hinge for a question about the interphalangeal joints.","Correct. The saddle joint lets the thumb oppose the fingers.","The thumb has no middle phalanx; it has only a proximal and a distal phalanx. Students pick this when they assume the thumb is built like the other fingers.","Turning the palm over is done by the radioulnar joints of the forearm, far from the thumb's base. Pick that for a question about pronation and supination."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-upper","lid":"d3-t-appendicular-skeleton-upper-7","comp":54,"competencyId":"w2-synovial-types"},{"id":"L00748","dok":3,"q":"Each finger has several short phalanges joined by hinge joints. Why is a finger built this way rather than as one rigid bone?","a":"Several hinges let the finger curl around objects of different shapes.","options":["The segments let the fingers spread apart from each other","Several hinges let the finger curl around objects of different shapes","Several hinges let each finger rotate in every direction","The segments let the finger bend backward as far as forward"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The joints between phalanges are hinges that each bend in one plane. With two or three hinges in a row, a finger can curl at several points and wrap closely around a ball, a pen, or a handle. A single rigid digit could only push or poke, not grasp.","notes":["Spreading the fingers apart happens at the knuckle joints between the metacarpals and proximal phalanges, not at the hinges between phalanges. Pick that for a question about the metacarpophalangeal joints.","Correct. Multiple hinges let the finger curl around objects.","Hinge joints move in one plane only, so they cannot rotate a finger in every direction. Students pick this by confusing hinge joints with ball-and-socket joints.","The finger hinges bend mainly toward the palm, and ligaments limit how far they bend backward. Choose a joint with equal motion both ways only for a question about a freely movable joint like the shoulder."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-upper","lid":"d3-t-appendicular-skeleton-upper-8","comp":54,"competencyId":"w2-synovial-types"},{"id":"L00829","dok":3,"q":"The head of the femur sits deep in the acetabulum, unlike the shallow socket of the shoulder. Why does the hip trade some mobility for this deep socket?","a":"The hip bears the body's weight, so it needs the stability of a deep socket.","options":["A deep socket lets the femur rotate farther than a shallow one would","The hip bears the body's weight, so it needs the stability of a deep socket","The hip needs more mobility than the shoulder, so its socket is deeper","A deep socket removes the need for strong ligaments around the hip"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Both the hip and the shoulder are ball-and-socket joints, but they are built for different jobs. The hip carries the body's weight, so its deep acetabulum, deepened further by a fibrocartilage labrum, wraps much of the femoral head and resists dislocation. The cost is less range of motion than the shoulder, whose shallow glenoid cavity favors mobility over stability.","notes":["A deeper socket covers more of the ball, which limits how far the femur can swing. Students who link a bigger socket with more motion pick this, but depth adds stability, not range.","Correct. The weight-bearing hip uses a deep socket for stability at the cost of some mobility.","This reverses the comparison. The shoulder is the more mobile joint, and its socket is the shallow one. A deeper socket limits movement rather than adding it.","The hip is reinforced by strong ligaments as well as its deep socket. The two work together, so this choice wrongly treats socket depth as a replacement for ligaments."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-lower","lid":"d3-t-appendicular-skeleton-lower-1","comp":54,"competencyId":"w2-synovial-types"},{"id":"L00834","dok":3,"q":"The ankle joint lets the foot move up and down but resists side-to-side motion. Why does this restricted motion suit the ankle?","a":"Hinge motion suits walking, and limiting side-to-side motion keeps a weight-bearing joint stable","options":["A ball-and-socket ankle would bear weight better because it moves in every direction","Hinge motion suits walking, and limiting side-to-side motion keeps a weight-bearing joint stable","Side-to-side motion is blocked so that the knee can do all of the turning","The ankle is a plane joint, so its surfaces glide only up and down"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The ankle (talocrural) joint is a hinge joint, with the talus held between the tibia and the two malleoli. A hinge allows dorsiflexion and plantar flexion, the up-and-down motion needed for walking, while the malleoli block most sideways motion. That limit keeps a joint that carries the whole body's weight stable.","notes":["More directions of motion mean less stability. A ball-and-socket ankle would be easier to twist and dislocate under the body's weight.","Correct. A hinge suits walking and keeps a weight-bearing joint stable.","The knee is a modified hinge joint that mainly flexes and extends, and it does not turn the foot. Turning the sole inward and outward happens at the joints between the tarsals instead.","The ankle is a hinge joint, not a plane joint. Plane joints, such as the intertarsal joints, glide side to side and back and forth with flat surfaces."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-lower","lid":"d3-t-appendicular-skeleton-lower-6","comp":54,"competencyId":"w2-synovial-types"},{"id":"L00837","dok":1,"q":"What is a joint, or articulation?","a":"The place where two or more bones meet.","options":["The shaft of a long bone","The lining of a body cavity","The place where two or more bones meet","A cord of tissue that joins muscle to bone"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"A joint, also called an articulation, is any place where two or more bones meet. Some joints are fused or bound so tightly that they do not move, like the sutures of the skull, while others, like the knee, move freely. Joints are classified both by their structure and by how much movement they allow.","notes":["The shaft of a long bone is the diaphysis, the long middle part between the two ends. Pick it if the question asked for the name of the bone shaft.","The lining of a body cavity is a serous membrane, such as the pleura or peritoneum. It would fit a question about membranes, not bones.","Correct. A joint is where two or more bones meet.","A cord of tissue joining muscle to bone is a tendon. It crosses joints but is not the joint itself, so it would be the answer if the question asked what attaches muscle to bone."],"lt":"t-articulations","lid":"c1","comp":50,"competencyId":"w2-joint-classification"},{"id":"L00838","dok":1,"q":"Which two criteria define the structural classification of joints?","a":"Whether a synovial cavity is present and what type of connective tissue holds the bones together.","options":["The blood supply and the nerve supply of the joint","Whether a synovial cavity is present and what tissue binds the bones","The degree of movement and the number of axes","The shape of the bone surfaces and the strength of the ligaments"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The structural classification looks at how a joint is built. It asks whether there is a synovial cavity between the bones and what kind of connective tissue holds them together, dense fibrous tissue, cartilage, or an articular capsule. That gives the three structural classes: fibrous, cartilaginous, and synovial.","notes":["Joints do have blood and nerve supplies, but neither is used to classify them. This choice lists real joint features that play no role in classification.","Correct. Structural classification uses the presence of a synovial cavity and the type of connecting tissue.","Degree of movement is the basis of the functional classification, into synarthroses, amphiarthroses, and diarthroses. Number of axes is used to sort synovial joints. Pick this if the question asked about classifying by function.","The shape of the bone surfaces is used to name the six types of synovial joint, such as hinge or ball-and-socket. It would fit a question about sorting synovial joints, not the structural classes."],"lt":"t-articulations","lid":"c2","comp":50,"competencyId":"w2-joint-classification"},{"id":"L00839","dok":1,"q":"Which functional class of joint is immovable?","a":"A synarthrosis.","options":["A synarthrosis","An amphiarthrosis","A diarthrosis","A symphysis"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The functional classification sorts joints by how much they move. A synarthrosis is immovable, as in the sutures of the adult skull and a tooth in its socket. An amphiarthrosis is slightly movable, and a diarthrosis is freely movable.","notes":["Correct. A synarthrosis is an immovable joint.","An amphiarthrosis is slightly movable, like the pubic symphysis or the distal tibiofibular joint. It would be the answer for joints that give a little but are not free.","A diarthrosis is a freely movable joint, and every synovial joint is a diarthrosis. Pick it if the question asked which functional class moves freely, like the knee or shoulder.","A symphysis is a structural type of cartilaginous joint joined by a fibrocartilage disc, and it is slightly movable. It fits a question about the pubic symphysis or intervertebral joints, not a functional class."],"lt":"t-articulations","lid":"c3","comp":50,"competencyId":"w2-joint-classification"},{"id":"L00840","dok":1,"q":"Which structural class of joint has a synovial cavity?","a":"The synovial joint.","options":["The synovial joint","The cartilaginous joint","The fibrous joint","The symphysis"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Only synovial joints have a synovial cavity, a fluid-filled space between the articulating bones enclosed by an articular capsule. This space lets the bone ends move freely over each other, so all synovial joints are diarthroses. Fibrous and cartilaginous joints have no cavity; their bones are bound directly by tissue.","notes":["Correct. The synovial joint is the only structural class with a synovial cavity.","A cartilaginous joint has no cavity. Its bones are joined by hyaline cartilage or fibrocartilage, as at the pubic symphysis. It would be the answer for the class joined by cartilage.","A fibrous joint has no cavity. Its bones are held closely by dense connective tissue, as in the sutures of the skull. Pick it if the question asked which class is joined by dense collagen fibers.","A symphysis is one type of cartilaginous joint, joined by a fibrocartilage disc with no cavity. It would be the answer for joints like the pubic symphysis."],"lt":"t-articulations","lid":"c4","comp":50,"competencyId":"w2-joint-classification"},{"id":"L00841","dok":1,"q":"Which type of fibrous joint holds a tooth in its bony socket?","a":"A gomphosis.","options":["A suture","A syndesmosis","A synchondrosis","A gomphosis"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"A gomphosis is a peg-in-socket fibrous joint. The cone-shaped root of a tooth sits in its socket in the jaw and is held there by the periodontal ligament. It is immovable in a healthy mouth, so it is a synarthrosis.","notes":["A suture is a fibrous joint with interlocking edges found only between skull bones. Pick it if the question asked about the joints between the cranial bones.","A syndesmosis is a fibrous joint where the bones are farther apart and united by a ligament, like the distal tibiofibular joint. It would be the answer for a slightly movable fibrous joint held by a ligament.","A synchondrosis is a cartilaginous joint joined by hyaline cartilage, like the epiphyseal growth plate. It would fit a question about bones joined by hyaline cartilage.","Correct. A tooth is held in its socket by a gomphosis."],"lt":"t-articulations","lid":"c5","comp":51,"competencyId":"w2-fibrous-cartilaginous"},{"id":"L00842","dok":1,"q":"What kind of cartilage covers the articulating bone surfaces inside a synovial joint?","a":"Hyaline cartilage.","options":["Fibrocartilage","Elastic cartilage","Hyaline cartilage","Dense regular connective tissue"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Articular cartilage is hyaline cartilage, a smooth, glassy cartilage that covers the ends of the bones inside a synovial joint. Its slippery surface reduces friction and absorbs shock. It has no blood supply, so it relies on synovial fluid for nutrients.","notes":["Fibrocartilage is the tough cartilage of the menisci, the labrum, and the pubic symphysis. Pick it if the question asked what the menisci or intervertebral discs are made of.","Elastic cartilage contains many elastic fibers and is found in the external ear and the epiglottis. It would be the answer for cartilage that bends and springs back.","Correct. Articular cartilage is hyaline cartilage.","Dense regular connective tissue is the parallel-fibered tissue of tendons and ligaments, not a cartilage. It would fit a question about what ligaments are made of."],"lt":"t-articulations","lid":"c6","comp":52,"competencyId":"w2-synovial-structure"},{"id":"L00843","dok":1,"q":"Which structure secretes synovial fluid?","a":"The synovial membrane.","options":["The articular cartilage","The fibrous membrane","The periosteum","The synovial membrane"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The synovial membrane is the inner layer of the articular capsule, lining the synovial cavity. It secretes synovial fluid, a slippery fluid made of hyaluronic acid and interstitial fluid. The fluid lubricates the joint, nourishes the articular cartilage, and carries phagocytic cells that clear debris.","notes":["The articular cartilage covers the bone ends and is bathed by synovial fluid, but it does not make the fluid. It would be the answer for the smooth covering on the bone surfaces.","The fibrous membrane is the outer layer of the capsule, made of dense irregular connective tissue. It gives the capsule strength and attaches to the periosteum but does not secrete fluid.","The periosteum is the membrane covering the outer surface of bone outside the joint. Pick it if the question asked what the fibrous membrane of the capsule attaches to.","Correct. The synovial membrane secretes synovial fluid."],"lt":"t-articulations","lid":"c7","comp":52,"competencyId":"w2-synovial-structure"},{"id":"L00844","dok":1,"q":"What is the anatomical name for the shoulder joint?","a":"The glenohumeral joint.","options":["The acromioclavicular joint","The radioulnar joint","The glenohumeral joint","The sternoclavicular joint"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The shoulder joint is the glenohumeral joint, named for the two surfaces that meet there: the glenoid cavity of the scapula and the head of the humerus. It is a ball-and-socket joint and the most freely movable joint in the body. Its shallow socket and loose capsule make it easy to dislocate.","notes":["The acromioclavicular joint is where the acromion of the scapula meets the lateral end of the clavicle, at the top of the shoulder. Pick it if the question asked about the joint between the scapula and clavicle.","The radioulnar joints connect the radius and ulna in the forearm and allow pronation and supination. They would be the answer for a pivot joint of the forearm.","Correct. The glenohumeral joint is the shoulder joint.","The sternoclavicular joint is where the medial end of the clavicle meets the manubrium of the sternum. It is the only bony joint between the upper limb and the axial skeleton."],"lt":"t-articulations","lid":"c8","comp":55,"competencyId":"w2-shoulder-knee"},{"id":"L00845","dok":1,"q":"Which movement decreases the angle between articulating bones?","a":"Flexion.","options":["Abduction","Circumduction","Flexion","Extension"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Flexion decreases the angle between two bones, usually in the sagittal plane. Bending the elbow so the forearm comes toward the arm is a clear example. Extension is the reverse and increases the angle again.","notes":["Abduction moves a body part away from the midline, as when you raise the arm out to the side. It would be the answer for movement away from the midline.","Circumduction moves the distal end of a part in a circle, as when you draw a circle in the air with your arm. It would fit a question about circular movement.","Correct. Flexion decreases the angle between bones.","Extension increases the angle between bones, as when you straighten the elbow. Pick it if the question asked which movement opens the angle."],"lt":"t-articulations","lid":"c9","comp":53,"competencyId":"w2-joint-movements"},{"id":"L00846","dok":1,"q":"What are the articular discs of a synovial joint, such as those in the knee, also called?","a":"Menisci.","options":["Labra","Bursae","Menisci","Retinacula"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Articular discs are crescent-shaped pads of fibrocartilage that sit between the bone surfaces in some synovial joints. They are also called menisci, the most familiar being the medial and lateral menisci of the knee. They improve the fit of the bones, absorb shock, distribute weight, and help spread synovial fluid.","notes":["A labrum is a fibrocartilage rim around the socket of a ball-and-socket joint, as at the shoulder and hip. It lines the edge of a socket rather than sitting between the bones.","Bursae are small fluid-filled sacs that reduce friction between tissues near a joint. They are sacs, not cartilage pads, so pick them if the question asked what cushions a tendon rubbing over bone.","Correct. Articular discs are also called menisci.","Retinacula are bands of fascia that hold structures in place, such as the patellar retinacula beside the kneecap. They would be the answer for the bands that strengthen the front of the knee."],"lt":"t-articulations","lid":"c10","comp":52,"competencyId":"w2-synovial-structure"},{"id":"L00847","dok":1,"q":"Which knee structure is the continuation of the quadriceps tendon from the patella to the tibial tuberosity?","a":"The patellar ligament.","options":["The patellar ligament","The tibial collateral ligament","The oblique popliteal ligament","The anterior cruciate ligament"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The quadriceps tendon attaches to the base of the patella and continues below it as the patellar ligament, which ends on the tibial tuberosity. It strengthens the front of the knee joint. Because it connects the patella to the tibia, bone to bone, it is called a ligament even though it carries the quadriceps' pull.","notes":["Correct. The patellar ligament runs from the patella to the tibial tuberosity.","The tibial collateral ligament runs along the medial side of the knee, outside the capsule. It would be the answer for the medial ligament torn in the unhappy triad.","The oblique popliteal ligament is a broad ligament that strengthens the back of the knee. Pick it if the question asked which ligament reinforces the posterior surface.","The anterior cruciate ligament lies inside the knee joint and stops the tibia from sliding forward. It would be the answer for the ligament most often torn in serious knee injuries."],"lt":"t-articulations","lid":"c11","comp":55,"competencyId":"w2-shoulder-knee"},{"id":"L00848","dok":1,"q":"Which four muscles make up the rotator cuff of the shoulder?","a":"Supraspinatus, infraspinatus, teres minor, and subscapularis.","options":["Deltoid, biceps brachii, triceps brachii, and pectoralis major","Supraspinatus, teres major, coracobrachialis, and subscapularis","Supraspinatus, infraspinatus, teres minor, and subscapularis","Trapezius, rhomboid major, levator scapulae, and serratus anterior"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The rotator cuff is made of four muscles that start on the scapula and wrap the head of the humerus: the supraspinatus, infraspinatus, teres minor, and subscapularis, remembered as SITS. Their tendons hold the humeral head in the shallow glenoid cavity. Because the shoulder's ligaments add little strength, these muscles are its main source of stability.","notes":["These are large muscles that move the shoulder and elbow, but none of them wraps the humeral head as a cuff. Pick this group if the question asked for the major muscles of the shoulder and arm.","Two of these are right, but the teres major and coracobrachialis are not cuff muscles. Students mix up teres major with teres minor because of the shared name.","Correct. SITS: supraspinatus, infraspinatus, teres minor, subscapularis.","These muscles move and steady the scapula against the thorax rather than holding the humeral head in its socket. They would fit a question about muscles that move the scapula."],"lt":"t-articulations","lid":"c12","comp":55,"competencyId":"w2-shoulder-knee"},{"id":"L00849","dok":2,"q":"A joint is described as freely movable. Which functional class does it belong to?","a":"A diarthrosis.","options":["An amphiarthrosis","A synarthrosis","A synchondrosis","A diarthrosis"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"A diarthrosis is a freely movable joint. All synovial joints, such as the shoulder, elbow, hip, and knee, are diarthroses because their fluid-filled cavity lets the bone ends move over each other. The other functional classes are the immovable synarthrosis and the slightly movable amphiarthrosis.","notes":["An amphiarthrosis is slightly movable, like the pubic symphysis. It would be the answer for a joint that gives a little but is not free.","A synarthrosis is immovable, like an adult skull suture. Pick it if the question described a joint that does not move.","A synchondrosis is a structural type of cartilaginous joint joined by hyaline cartilage, such as the epiphyseal plate. It is immovable, so it fits a question about growth plates.","Correct. A freely movable joint is a diarthrosis."],"lt":"t-articulations","lid":"c13","comp":50,"competencyId":"w2-joint-classification"},{"id":"L00850","dok":2,"q":"The pubic bones are joined by a broad disc of fibrocartilage. Which structural type of joint is the pubic symphysis?","a":"A symphysis.","options":["A synchondrosis","A syndesmosis","A symphysis","A suture"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"A symphysis is a cartilaginous joint in which the bones are joined by a broad, flat disc of fibrocartilage. It is slightly movable, an amphiarthrosis. Examples include the pubic symphysis, the manubriosternal joint, and the joints between vertebral bodies, and all symphyses lie in the midline.","notes":["A synchondrosis is also cartilaginous, but its bones are joined by hyaline cartilage and it is immovable, like the epiphyseal growth plate. Pick it if the question described hyaline cartilage.","A syndesmosis is a fibrous joint where the bones are united by a ligament, like the distal tibiofibular joint. It would be the answer for a joint held by a ligament.","Correct. A fibrocartilage disc joining two bones makes a symphysis.","A suture is a fibrous joint with interlocking edges found only between skull bones. It would fit a question about the cranial joints."],"lt":"t-articulations","lid":"c14","comp":51,"competencyId":"w2-fibrous-cartilaginous"},{"id":"L00851","dok":2,"q":"The shafts of the radius and ulna are bound by a broad sheet of dense connective tissue that allows slight movement. Which type of fibrous joint is this?","a":"A syndesmosis.","options":["A syndesmosis","A gomphosis","A synostosis","A suture"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"A syndesmosis is a fibrous joint in which bones are joined by a ligament or a broad sheet of dense connective tissue, called an interosseous membrane, so slight movement is possible. That makes it an amphiarthrosis. The interosseous membranes between the radius and ulna and between the tibia and fibula are both syndesmoses.","notes":["Correct. A broad sheet of dense connective tissue between two bones, the interosseous membrane, forms a syndesmosis.","A gomphosis is the peg-in-socket fibrous joint of a tooth in its bony socket. It would be the answer for how teeth are held in the jaw.","A synostosis is a joint where two bones have fused completely into one, as when a suture ossifies. It is immovable and has no connective tissue sheet left between the bones.","A suture is a fibrous joint found only between skull bones, with thin connective tissue and interlocking edges. Pick it if the question described joints of the cranium."],"lt":"t-articulations","lid":"c15","comp":51,"competencyId":"w2-fibrous-cartilaginous"},{"id":"L00852","dok":2,"q":"The epiphyseal growth plate joins bone to bone with hyaline cartilage and permits no movement. Which joint type is it?","a":"A synchondrosis.","options":["A synchondrosis","A syndesmosis","A symphysis","A gomphosis"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"A synchondrosis is a cartilaginous joint in which the connecting material is hyaline cartilage. It is immovable, a synarthrosis. The epiphyseal growth plate is the classic example, and when bone replaces its cartilage after growth ends, the joint becomes a synostosis.","notes":["Correct. The growth plate is a synchondrosis, joined by hyaline cartilage.","A syndesmosis is a fibrous joint united by a ligament, like the distal tibiofibular joint. It would be the answer for a joint held by a ligament rather than cartilage.","A symphysis is cartilaginous too, but its bones are joined by a fibrocartilage disc and it is slightly movable, like the pubic symphysis. Pick it if the question described fibrocartilage.","A gomphosis is the fibrous joint of a tooth in its socket, held by the periodontal ligament. It would fit a question about teeth."],"lt":"t-articulations","lid":"c16","comp":51,"competencyId":"w2-fibrous-cartilaginous"},{"id":"L00853","dok":2,"q":"A synovial joint has flat or slightly curved surfaces that glide across one another. Which type is it?","a":"A plane joint.","options":["A hinge joint","A saddle joint","A plane joint","A pivot joint"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"A plane joint has flat or slightly curved articulating surfaces that slide back and forth and side to side over each other, a movement called gliding. Examples are the intercarpal joints in the wrist and the intertarsal joints in the foot. The angle between the bones does not change during gliding.","notes":["A hinge joint has a convex surface fitting into a concave one and allows only flexion and extension, like the elbow. Pick it if the question described back-and-forth bending in one plane.","A saddle joint has surfaces that are each both concave and convex, like the carpometacarpal joint of the thumb. It would be the answer for that saddle-shaped fit.","Correct. Flat, gliding surfaces define a plane joint.","A pivot joint has a rounded surface turning within a ring of bone and ligament, like the atlantoaxial joint. It would be the answer for a joint that only rotates."],"lt":"t-articulations","lid":"c17","comp":54,"competencyId":"w2-synovial-types"},{"id":"L00854","dok":2,"q":"Which type of synovial joint moves around three axes, allowing triaxial movement?","a":"A ball-and-socket joint.","options":["A condyloid joint","A pivot joint","A ball-and-socket joint","A hinge joint"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"A ball-and-socket joint has a rounded ball fitting into a cup, so the bone can move around three axes: flexion and extension, abduction and adduction, and rotation. The shoulder and hip are the two examples. This makes them the most freely movable synovial joints.","notes":["A condyloid joint is biaxial, with an oval projection in an oval depression, like the radiocarpal joint at the wrist. It allows flexion, extension, abduction, and adduction but not true rotation.","A pivot joint is uniaxial too, allowing only rotation, like the atlantoaxial joint. It would be the answer for a joint that turns around a single axis.","Correct. The ball-and-socket joint is triaxial.","A hinge joint is uniaxial, moving only in flexion and extension, like the elbow. Pick it if the question asked for a joint with one axis of bending."],"lt":"t-articulations","lid":"c18","comp":54,"competencyId":"w2-synovial-types"},{"id":"L00855","dok":2,"q":"The carpometacarpal joint of the thumb has surfaces that are each both concave and convex. Which synovial joint type is it?","a":"A saddle joint.","options":["A plane joint","A pivot joint","A saddle joint","A condyloid joint"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"In a saddle joint, each articulating surface curves up in one direction and down in the other, like a rider sitting in a saddle. The two surfaces fit together and allow biaxial movement. The carpometacarpal joint at the base of the thumb is the main example, and it allows the thumb to move across the palm in opposition.","notes":["A plane joint has flat or slightly curved surfaces that glide, like the intercarpal joints next door in the wrist. It would be the answer for gliding surfaces.","A pivot joint has a rounded surface turning in a ring, like the atlantoaxial joint, and allows only rotation. It would fit a question about turning the head side to side.","Correct. The thumb's carpometacarpal joint is a saddle joint.","A condyloid joint is also biaxial, but it has an oval projection fitting into an oval depression, like the radiocarpal joint. Pick it if the question described an oval knob in an oval socket."],"lt":"t-articulations","lid":"c19","comp":54,"competencyId":"w2-synovial-types"},{"id":"L00856","dok":2,"q":"Which movement is the direct opposite of abduction?","a":"Adduction.","options":["Circumduction","Adduction","Rotation","Extension"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Abduction moves a body part away from the midline, as when you raise your arm out to the side. Adduction brings it back toward the midline, so the two are opposites. A handy memory aid is that adduction adds the limb back to the body.","notes":["Circumduction moves the distal end of a part in a circle, combining flexion, extension, abduction, and adduction. It is a combined movement, not the opposite of any one of them.","Correct. Adduction, movement toward the midline, is the opposite of abduction.","Rotation turns a bone around its own long axis, medially or laterally. It does not move the limb toward or away from the midline.","Extension is the opposite of flexion, not abduction. It increases the angle between bones, as when you straighten the elbow."],"lt":"t-articulations","lid":"c20","comp":53,"competencyId":"w2-joint-movements"},{"id":"L00857","dok":2,"q":"The fibular collateral ligament of the knee lies outside the articular capsule. Which category of ligament is it?","a":"An extracapsular ligament.","options":["A retinaculum","An intracapsular ligament","An articular disc","An extracapsular ligament"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Ligaments that lie outside the articular capsule are called extracapsular ligaments. The fibular (lateral) and tibial (medial) collateral ligaments of the knee are examples. They reinforce the sides of the joint and resist sideways bending.","notes":["A retinaculum is a band of fascia that holds structures in place, like the patellar retinacula on either side of the kneecap. It would fit a question about the bands that strengthen the front of the knee.","Intracapsular ligaments lie inside the capsule, like the cruciate ligaments of the knee, though synovial folds keep them out of the synovial cavity. Pick it if the question described a cruciate ligament.","An articular disc, or meniscus, is a fibrocartilage pad between the bones, not a ligament. It would be the answer for the C-shaped pads of the knee.","Correct. A ligament outside the capsule is extracapsular."],"lt":"t-articulations","lid":"c21","comp":52,"competencyId":"w2-synovial-structure"},{"id":"L00858","dok":2,"q":"Which connective tissue forms the fibrous membrane, the outer layer of an articular capsule?","a":"Dense irregular connective tissue.","options":["Dense regular connective tissue","Hyaline cartilage","Areolar connective tissue","Dense irregular connective tissue"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The fibrous membrane is made of dense irregular connective tissue, in which collagen fibers run in many directions. That arrangement lets the capsule resist pulling from many angles while staying flexible. It attaches to the periosteum of the bones, and its thickened bundles form many of the joint's ligaments.","notes":["Dense regular connective tissue has parallel collagen fibers and forms tendons and most ligaments, which are pulled mainly in one direction. Pick it if the question asked what a tendon is made of.","Hyaline cartilage forms the articular cartilage on the bone ends, not the capsule. It would fit a question about the smooth covering of the bone surfaces.","Areolar connective tissue, with some elastic fibers, forms the synovial membrane, the inner layer of the capsule. It would be the answer for the inner layer.","Correct. The fibrous membrane is dense irregular connective tissue."],"lt":"t-articulations","lid":"c22","comp":52,"competencyId":"w2-synovial-structure"},{"id":"L00859","dok":2,"q":"A tube-shaped bursa wraps a tendon where it passes through a tight space at the wrist. What is this structure called?","a":"A tendon sheath.","options":["An articular fat pad","A tendon sheath","An articular disc","A labrum"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"A tendon sheath is a tube-shaped bursa that wraps around a tendon where it runs through a tight space, such as at the wrist and ankle. The fluid inside lets the tendon slide with little friction as the muscle contracts. Ordinary bursae are flat sacs, while a tendon sheath is rolled into a tube.","notes":["An articular fat pad is a cushion of adipose tissue in some synovial joints. It would be the answer for fatty padding inside a joint, not a tube around a tendon.","Correct. A tube-shaped bursa around a tendon is a tendon sheath.","An articular disc, or meniscus, is a fibrocartilage pad between two bone surfaces inside a joint. It would be the answer for the C-shaped pads of the knee.","A labrum is a fibrocartilage rim that deepens the socket of the shoulder or hip. Pick it if the question asked what deepens a ball-and-socket joint."],"lt":"t-articulations","lid":"c23","comp":52,"competencyId":"w2-synovial-structure"},{"id":"L00860","dok":2,"q":"A synovial joint fits a convex surface into a concave one and allows only flexion and extension. Which type is it?","a":"A hinge joint.","options":["A ball-and-socket joint","A hinge joint","A plane joint","A condyloid joint"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"A hinge joint works like a door hinge, with a convex bone surface fitting into a concave one. It is uniaxial, so it allows only flexion and extension. The elbow, the ankle, and the interphalangeal joints of the fingers and toes are examples.","notes":["A ball-and-socket joint is triaxial, allowing movement in all directions, like the shoulder and hip. It would be the answer for the most freely movable joint type.","Correct. A convex-concave joint limited to flexion and extension is a hinge joint.","A plane joint has flat surfaces that glide over each other, like the intercarpal joints. It would be the answer for gliding motion.","A condyloid joint has an oval projection in an oval depression and is biaxial, allowing abduction and adduction as well. Pick it if the question described the radiocarpal or knuckle joints."],"lt":"t-articulations","lid":"c24","comp":54,"competencyId":"w2-synovial-types"},{"id":"L00861","dok":2,"q":"Which knee structure is the C-shaped fibrocartilage disc on the medial side of the joint?","a":"The medial meniscus.","options":["The medial meniscus","The transverse ligament","The lateral meniscus","The tibial collateral ligament"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The knee has two menisci, fibrocartilage pads that sit on top of the tibial condyles. The medial meniscus is C-shaped, while the lateral meniscus is more circular, almost O-shaped. The medial meniscus is one of the three structures torn in the unhappy triad.","notes":["Correct. The medial meniscus is the C-shaped disc on the medial side.","The transverse ligament is the band that joins the two menisci to each other at the front. It would fit a question about what connects the medial and lateral menisci.","The lateral meniscus is on the lateral side and is more circular, closer to an O shape. Pick it if the question asked for the O-shaped disc.","The tibial collateral ligament is on the medial side too, but it is a ligament outside the capsule, not a cartilage disc. It would be the answer for the band reinforcing the medial knee."],"lt":"t-articulations","lid":"c25","comp":55,"competencyId":"w2-shoulder-knee"},{"id":"L00862","dok":2,"q":"A pivot joint, such as the joint between the radius and ulna near the elbow, moves around how many axes?","a":"One axis","options":["Two axes","One axis","No axis","Three axes"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"A pivot joint is uniaxial. A rounded surface turns within a ring formed by bone and ligament, so the only possible movement is rotation around one long axis. The proximal radioulnar joint, which lets the radius turn for pronation and supination, and the atlantoaxial joint are examples.","notes":["Two axes describe biaxial joints such as condyloid and saddle joints. Pick two if the question asked about the wrist or the thumb's carpometacarpal joint.","Correct. A pivot joint rotates around a single axis.","A joint with no axis of movement would not turn or bend at all, like an adult skull suture. A pivot joint clearly turns around an axis, so this choice confuses it with a fixed joint.","Three axes describe a ball-and-socket joint, like the shoulder or hip. It would be the answer for the most freely movable joint type."],"lt":"t-articulations","lid":"c26","comp":54,"competencyId":"w2-synovial-types"},{"id":"L00863","dok":2,"q":"A joint has no synovial cavity, its bones are held by dense connective tissue rich in collagen, and it permits little or no movement. Which structural class is it?","a":"A fibrous joint.","options":["A fibrous joint","A synovial joint","A diarthrosis","A cartilaginous joint"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Fibrous joints have no cavity, and dense connective tissue rich in collagen fibers holds the bones closely together. That tight binding allows little or no movement. Sutures, syndesmoses (including the interosseous membranes), and gomphoses are the fibrous joints.","notes":["Correct. No cavity and dense collagen-rich connective tissue define a fibrous joint.","A synovial joint has a fluid-filled cavity and moves freely, the opposite of this description. Pick it if the question described a joint with a capsule and articular cartilage.","A diarthrosis is a functional class meaning freely movable, not a structural class. It would only fit a joint that moves freely, which this one does not.","A cartilaginous joint also lacks a cavity, but its bones are joined by cartilage, not dense fibrous tissue. It would be the answer for joints like the pubic symphysis."],"lt":"t-articulations","lid":"c27","comp":50,"competencyId":"w2-joint-classification"},{"id":"L00864","dok":2,"q":"A joint has a synovial cavity, hyaline articular cartilage on the bone ends, and an articular capsule, and it moves freely. Which structural and functional description fits it?","a":"A synovial joint that is a diarthrosis.","options":["A cartilaginous joint that is a synarthrosis","A synovial joint that is a diarthrosis","A fibrous joint that is a synarthrosis","A cartilaginous joint that is an amphiarthrosis"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"A synovial cavity, articular cartilage, and an articular capsule are the defining parts of a synovial joint. Because the bone ends are separated by fluid and can move over each other, every synovial joint is a diarthrosis, meaning freely movable. Joints are named both ways, so it helps to pair the two classifications.","notes":["This describes a joint like the epiphyseal growth plate, joined by hyaline cartilage and immovable. It would be the answer for a synchondrosis.","Correct. It is a synovial joint, and synovial joints are diarthroses.","This describes a joint like an adult skull suture, bound by dense connective tissue and immovable. Pick it for the sutures.","This describes a joint like the pubic symphysis, joined by fibrocartilage and slightly movable. It has no cavity or capsule, so it does not match."],"lt":"t-articulations","lid":"c28","comp":50,"competencyId":"w2-joint-classification"},{"id":"L00865","dok":2,"q":"An oval projection on one bone fits into an oval depression on another, and the joint moves around two axes. Which synovial joint type is described?","a":"A condyloid joint.","options":["A saddle joint","A hinge joint","A condyloid joint","A ball-and-socket joint"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"A condyloid joint has an oval projection fitting into an oval depression. It is biaxial, allowing flexion and extension plus abduction and adduction, but no true rotation. The radiocarpal joint at the wrist and the metacarpophalangeal joints at the knuckles are examples.","notes":["A saddle joint is also biaxial, but each of its surfaces is both concave and convex, like the thumb's carpometacarpal joint. Pick it if the question described saddle-shaped surfaces.","A hinge joint has a convex surface in a concave one and is uniaxial, like the elbow. It would be the answer for a joint limited to flexion and extension.","Correct. An oval projection in an oval depression is a condyloid joint.","A ball-and-socket joint has a round ball in a cup and is triaxial, like the shoulder. It would be the answer for movement around three axes."],"lt":"t-articulations","lid":"c29","comp":54,"competencyId":"w2-synovial-types"},{"id":"L00866","dok":2,"q":"A joint between two skull bones has interlocking edges. It is immovable in an adult but slightly movable in an infant. Which joint type is it, and how is it classified at each age?","a":"A suture, a synarthrosis in adults and an amphiarthrosis in infants.","options":["A symphysis, a synarthrosis in adults and an amphiarthrosis in infants","A suture, a synarthrosis in adults and an amphiarthrosis in infants","A gomphosis, a synarthrosis at all ages","A syndesmosis, an amphiarthrosis at all ages"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"A suture is a fibrous joint found only between skull bones, joined by thin dense connective tissue with interlocking edges. In adults it is immovable, a synarthrosis. In infants and young children the sutures are slightly movable, an amphiarthrosis, which lets the skull change shape slightly at birth and grow with the brain.","notes":["The age pattern is right, but a symphysis is a cartilaginous joint with a fibrocartilage disc, like the pubic symphysis. Skull bones are joined by sutures instead.","Correct. A suture is a synarthrosis in adults and an amphiarthrosis in infants.","A gomphosis is the joint of a tooth in its socket. It is immovable, but it is not found between two skull bones and does not have interlocking edges.","A syndesmosis is a fibrous joint held by a ligament, like the distal tibiofibular joint. It is not found between skull bones."],"lt":"t-articulations","lid":"c30","comp":51,"competencyId":"w2-fibrous-cartilaginous"},{"id":"L00867","dok":2,"q":"An athlete takes a lateral blow to a planted knee and tears the tibial collateral ligament, the medial meniscus, and the anterior cruciate ligament together. What is this injury pattern called?","a":"The unhappy triad.","options":["A dislocated knee","Bursitis","A rotator cuff tear","The unhappy triad"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The unhappy triad is a knee injury from a blow to the lateral side of a planted leg. The force bends the knee inward and tears three structures at once: the tibial collateral ligament, the medial meniscus, and the anterior cruciate ligament. It is common in contact sports such as football.","notes":["A dislocated knee is a displacement of the tibia relative to the femur, most often forward, and it can damage the popliteal artery. It would be the answer if the bones were knocked out of alignment.","Bursitis is inflammation of a bursa from overuse, infection, or rheumatoid arthritis. It would fit a question about a swollen, painful fluid sac near a joint.","A rotator cuff tear is an injury to the tendons of the SITS muscles at the shoulder. Pick it if the question described a shoulder injury.","Correct. These three torn structures make up the unhappy triad."],"lt":"t-articulations","lid":"c31","comp":55,"competencyId":"w2-shoulder-knee"},{"id":"L00868","dok":2,"q":"A joint has a rounded surface that turns within a ring formed by bone and ligament, and it permits only rotation. Which type is it, and how many axes does it use?","a":"A pivot joint, uniaxial.","options":["A hinge joint, uniaxial","A pivot joint, uniaxial","A condyloid joint, biaxial","A saddle joint, biaxial"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"In a pivot joint, a rounded or pointed surface turns inside a ring made partly of bone and partly of ligament. The only possible motion is rotation around one axis, so it is uniaxial. The atlantoaxial joint, which turns the head side to side, and the radioulnar joint are examples.","notes":["A hinge joint is uniaxial too, but its one movement is flexion and extension, not rotation. Pick it if the question described the elbow or an interphalangeal joint.","Correct. A ring around a rounded surface that allows only rotation is a uniaxial pivot joint.","A condyloid joint has an oval projection in an oval depression and moves around two axes. It would be the answer for the wrist or the knuckles.","A saddle joint has surfaces that are each concave and convex and moves around two axes. It would fit a question about the thumb's carpometacarpal joint."],"lt":"t-articulations","lid":"c32","comp":54,"competencyId":"w2-synovial-types"},{"id":"L00869","dok":2,"q":"A surgeon notes a thin, loose articular capsule and a shallow socket holding a large bone head, making the joint highly mobile but unstable. Which joint is being described?","a":"The glenohumeral (shoulder) joint.","options":["The coxal (hip) joint","The glenohumeral (shoulder) joint","The tibiofemoral (knee) joint","The humeroulnar (elbow) joint"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The glenohumeral joint pairs the large head of the humerus with the small, shallow glenoid cavity of the scapula, inside a thin, loose capsule. That design gives the shoulder the greatest range of motion of any joint, but the head is held in place mostly by the rotator cuff muscles rather than by bone or ligaments. The result is a joint that dislocates easily.","notes":["The hip is a ball-and-socket joint too, but its socket, the acetabulum, is deep and its capsule and ligaments are strong. Pick the hip if the question described a deep socket built for stability.","Correct. A large head in a shallow socket with a loose capsule describes the shoulder.","The knee is a modified hinge joint whose bones meet at fairly flat condyles, held by strong ligaments and menisci. It has no ball and socket, so pick it if the question described cruciate ligaments and menisci.","The elbow is a hinge joint with closely fitted bone surfaces that limit it to flexion and extension. It would be the answer for a stable, uniaxial joint of the upper limb."],"lt":"t-articulations","lid":"c33","comp":55,"competencyId":"w2-shoulder-knee"},{"id":"L00870","dok":2,"q":"A ligament crosses within the knee joint and stops the tibia from sliding forward relative to the femur. Which ligament is described?","a":"The anterior cruciate ligament.","options":["The anterior cruciate ligament","The tibial collateral ligament","The fibular collateral ligament","The posterior cruciate ligament"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The anterior cruciate ligament, or ACL, is an intracapsular ligament that crosses inside the knee from the front of the tibia to the femur. Its job is to stop the tibia from sliding forward under the femur. It is torn in roughly 70 percent of serious knee injuries.","notes":["Correct. The ACL crosses within the knee and stops forward sliding of the tibia.","The tibial collateral ligament lies on the medial side of the knee, outside the capsule, and resists sideways bending. It would be the answer for the medial ligament torn in the unhappy triad.","The fibular collateral ligament lies on the lateral side of the knee, outside the capsule. It would be the answer for the extracapsular ligament on the lateral side.","The posterior cruciate ligament also crosses within the knee, but it stops the tibia from sliding backward. Pick it if the question asked which ligament matters most on stairs and inclines."],"lt":"t-articulations","lid":"c34","comp":55,"competencyId":"w2-shoulder-knee"},{"id":"L00871","dok":2,"q":"A fibrous joint has more space between the bones than a suture, and the bones are united by a ligament, so it is slightly movable. Which fibrous joint type fits?","a":"A syndesmosis.","options":["A synchondrosis","A suture","A gomphosis","A syndesmosis"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"A syndesmosis is a fibrous joint with more distance between the bones and more dense connective tissue than a suture, arranged as a ligament. This looser binding allows slight movement, so it is an amphiarthrosis. The distal tibiofibular joint, held by the anterior tibiofibular ligament, is the standard example.","notes":["A synchondrosis is a cartilaginous joint joined by hyaline cartilage, like a growth plate, not a fibrous joint held by a ligament.","A suture has very little space between the bones, which are joined by a thin layer of connective tissue with interlocking edges. It is the answer for the immovable joints between skull bones.","A gomphosis is the peg-in-socket joint of a tooth, held by the periodontal ligament. It is immovable and is the answer only for teeth in their sockets.","Correct. A slightly movable fibrous joint united by a ligament is a syndesmosis."],"lt":"t-articulations","lid":"c35","comp":51,"competencyId":"w2-fibrous-cartilaginous"},{"id":"L00872","dok":2,"q":"A clinician examines a joint that contains three articulations in one joint cavity: a medial and a lateral articulation between the femur and tibia, plus one between the patella and the femur. Which joint is this?","a":"The knee (tibiofemoral) joint.","options":["The knee (tibiofemoral) joint","The shoulder joint","The hip joint","The ankle joint"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The knee is the largest and most complex joint in the body. Inside one joint cavity it has three articulations: the medial and lateral tibiofemoral joints between the femoral and tibial condyles, and the patellofemoral joint between the patella and the patellar surface of the femur. It works as a modified hinge joint.","notes":["Correct. The knee holds three articulations in one cavity.","The shoulder is the glenohumeral joint, a single articulation between the humeral head and the glenoid cavity. It would be the answer for a ball-and-socket joint of the upper limb.","The hip involves the femur, but only one articulation, the femoral head in the acetabulum. The tibia and patella are not part of it.","The ankle is where the talus meets the tibia and fibula. The femur and patella do not reach it, so pick the ankle if the question described the talus between the malleoli."],"lt":"t-articulations","lid":"c36","comp":55,"competencyId":"w2-shoulder-knee"},{"id":"L00873","dok":2,"q":"A rim of fibrocartilage deepens the socket of a ball-and-socket joint and is found at the shoulder and hip. What is this structure?","a":"A labrum.","options":["A bursa","A labrum","An articular fat pad","A meniscus"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"A labrum is a ring of fibrocartilage attached around the edge of a socket, which makes the socket deeper and slightly larger. The glenoid labrum surrounds the glenoid cavity of the shoulder, and the acetabular labrum surrounds the acetabulum of the hip. A deeper socket holds the ball more securely.","notes":["A bursa is a small fluid-filled sac that reduces friction between tissues near a joint. It would be the answer for the subacromial sac at the shoulder.","Correct. A fibrocartilage rim that deepens a socket is a labrum.","An articular fat pad is a cushion of adipose tissue inside some joints. It is fat, not fibrocartilage, so it would fit a question about fatty cushioning.","A meniscus is also fibrocartilage, but it is a crescent-shaped pad lying between two bone surfaces, as in the knee, not a rim around a socket. Pick it for the C-shaped and O-shaped pads of the knee."],"lt":"t-articulations","lid":"c37","comp":52,"competencyId":"w2-synovial-structure"},{"id":"L00874","dok":2,"q":"An autoimmune disease attacks the cartilage and synovial membrane of the joints, usually starting in small joints and affecting both sides of the body. Which joint disorder is described?","a":"Rheumatoid arthritis.","options":["Rheumatoid arthritis","Osteoarthritis","Bursitis","Gouty arthritis"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Rheumatoid arthritis is an autoimmune disease, meaning the immune system attacks the body's own tissue. It inflames the synovial membrane and destroys the articular cartilage, usually in the small joints first and symmetrically on both sides. That pattern separates it from the wear-and-tear damage of osteoarthritis.","notes":["Correct. An autoimmune attack on cartilage and synovial membrane, on both sides, is rheumatoid arthritis.","Osteoarthritis is a progressive loss of joint cartilage from aging and wear, mainly in large weight-bearing joints like the knees and hips. Pick it if the question described wear rather than an immune attack.","Bursitis is inflammation of a bursa, a fluid sac near a joint, usually from overuse or infection. It would fit a question about a single inflamed sac rather than the joint lining.","Gouty arthritis comes from sodium urate crystals deposited in a joint, often at the base of the big toe. It would be the answer for crystal deposits causing sudden joint pain."],"lt":"t-articulations","lid":"c38"},{"id":"L00875","dok":3,"q":"A joint lets a limb move away from the midline, back toward it, forward and back, and trace a circle with its distal end, but it does not rotate. How many axes of motion are needed for these movements?","a":"Two axes.","options":["One axis","Two axes","Three axes","Four axes"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Abduction and adduction happen around one axis, and flexion and extension happen around a second axis. Circumduction is not a separate axis; it is a blend of those four movements in sequence that makes the distal end trace a circle. Since the joint does not rotate, two axes are enough, which is the pattern of biaxial joints like the condyloid radiocarpal joint.","notes":["One axis allows only one pair of movements, such as flexion and extension at a hinge. It cannot produce both side-to-side and forward-and-back motion.","Correct. These movements need two axes, as in a biaxial joint.","A third axis would be needed for rotation around the bone's long axis, which this joint does not do. Pick three if the joint also rotated, as at the shoulder.","No synovial joint moves around four axes. Students land here by counting circumduction as its own axis, when it is really a combination of the other movements."],"lt":"t-articulations","lid":"c39","comp":54,"competencyId":"w2-synovial-types"},{"id":"L00876","dok":2,"q":"Put the structural classes of joints in this order: no cavity with dense fibrous tissue, then no cavity with cartilage, then a synovial cavity present.","a":"Fibrous joint, then cartilaginous joint, then synovial joint.","options":["Synovial joint, cartilaginous joint, fibrous joint","Cartilaginous joint, fibrous joint, synovial joint","Fibrous joint, cartilaginous joint, synovial joint","Fibrous joint, synovial joint, cartilaginous joint"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Fibrous joints have no cavity and are held by dense connective tissue rich in collagen. Cartilaginous joints also lack a cavity but are joined by hyaline cartilage or fibrocartilage. Synovial joints are the only class with a synovial cavity, enclosed by an articular capsule.","notes":["This is the exact reverse order. The synovial joint is the one with a cavity, so it belongs last, not first.","This swaps the first two. Cartilaginous joints are joined by cartilage, so they match the second description, and fibrous joints match the first.","Correct. Fibrous, then cartilaginous, then synovial.","This puts the synovial joint second, but the second description has no cavity. Only the cartilaginous joint fits no cavity with cartilage."],"lt":"t-articulations","lid":"c40","comp":50,"competencyId":"w2-joint-classification"},{"id":"L00877","dok":2,"q":"Which description correctly gives the structural class, the functional class, and the synovial joint type of the elbow?","a":"A synovial joint, a diarthrosis, and a hinge joint.","options":["A cartilaginous joint, an amphiarthrosis, and a symphysis","A fibrous joint, a synarthrosis, and a suture","A synovial joint, a diarthrosis, and a hinge joint","A synovial joint, an amphiarthrosis, and a ball-and-socket joint"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The elbow has a synovial cavity and capsule, so structurally it is a synovial joint, and like all synovial joints it is a diarthrosis, freely movable. Its convex and concave surfaces allow only flexion and extension, which makes it a hinge joint. Naming a joint all three ways is exactly what lab practicals ask for.","notes":["This describes the pubic symphysis, a slightly movable cartilaginous joint. The elbow has a cavity and moves freely, so none of these labels fit.","This describes an adult skull suture, which is immovable. Pick it for the joints between cranial bones.","Correct. The elbow is a synovial joint, a diarthrosis, and a hinge joint.","The structural class is right, but synovial joints are diarthroses, not amphiarthroses, and the elbow moves in only one plane. The ball-and-socket type would fit the shoulder."],"lt":"t-articulations","lid":"c41","comp":50,"competencyId":"w2-joint-classification"},{"id":"L00878","dok":2,"q":"Order these three synovial joint types from fewest axes of movement to most: hinge, ball-and-socket, and condyloid.","a":"Hinge, then condyloid, then ball-and-socket.","options":["Condyloid, hinge, ball-and-socket","Hinge, ball-and-socket, condyloid","Hinge, condyloid, ball-and-socket","Ball-and-socket, condyloid, hinge"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"A hinge joint is uniaxial, moving only in flexion and extension. A condyloid joint is biaxial, adding abduction and adduction. A ball-and-socket joint is triaxial, adding rotation as well, so the order from fewest to most axes is hinge, condyloid, ball-and-socket.","notes":["This puts the biaxial condyloid joint ahead of the uniaxial hinge joint. The hinge has the fewest axes, so it comes first.","This places the triaxial ball-and-socket joint before the biaxial condyloid joint. Three axes is the most, so ball-and-socket belongs last.","Correct. One axis, then two, then three.","This is the order from most axes to fewest, the reverse of what was asked."],"lt":"t-articulations","lid":"c42","comp":54,"competencyId":"w2-synovial-types"},{"id":"L00879","dok":2,"q":"The fibrous membrane of a synovial joint's articular capsule attaches to which covering of the bones?","a":"The periosteum.","options":["The articular cartilage","The synovial membrane","The endosteum","The periosteum"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The fibrous membrane is the strong outer layer of the articular capsule, made of dense irregular connective tissue. It attaches to the periosteum, the connective tissue membrane covering the outer surface of each bone, so the capsule is continuous with the bones it unites. Thickened bundles of this membrane form the joint's ligaments.","notes":["The articular cartilage covers the bone ends inside the joint cavity. The capsule attaches around it, not onto it. Pick it if the question asked what covers the articulating surfaces.","The synovial membrane is the inner layer of the capsule itself, lying against the fibrous membrane, not a bone covering. It would be the answer for the layer that secretes synovial fluid.","The endosteum lines the inner marrow cavity of a bone, not its outer surface. It would fit a question about the membrane inside the medullary cavity.","Correct. The fibrous membrane attaches to the periosteum."],"lt":"t-articulations","lid":"c43","comp":52,"competencyId":"w2-synovial-structure"},{"id":"L00880","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares extracapsular and intracapsular ligaments of the knee, with an example of each?","a":"An extracapsular ligament lies outside the capsule, like the fibular collateral ligament, and an intracapsular ligament lies inside it, like the anterior cruciate ligament.","options":["Extracapsular lies inside the capsule, like the fibular collateral, and intracapsular lies outside, like the anterior cruciate","Extracapsular lies outside the capsule, like the fibular collateral, and intracapsular lies inside, like the anterior cruciate","Extracapsular lies outside the capsule, like the anterior cruciate, and intracapsular lies inside, like the fibular collateral","Both lie inside the capsule and differ only in whether they cross each other"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Extra means outside and intra means inside, and the names refer to the articular capsule. The collateral ligaments run along the outer sides of the knee, outside the capsule, so they are extracapsular. The cruciate ligaments cross inside the capsule, though synovial folds keep them out of the synovial cavity, so they are intracapsular.","notes":["The examples are right, but the definitions are reversed. Extra means outside, so extracapsular ligaments lie outside the capsule.","Correct. Collaterals are extracapsular, and cruciates are intracapsular.","The definitions are right, but the examples are swapped. The cruciate ligaments cross inside the knee, and the collateral ligaments run along its sides.","Only the cruciate ligaments lie inside the capsule. The collateral ligaments lie outside it, so the two groups differ in position, not just in whether they cross."],"lt":"t-articulations","lid":"c44","comp":55,"competencyId":"w2-shoulder-knee"},{"id":"L00881","dok":2,"q":"The distal tibiofibular joint is united by a ligament. How is it classified structurally and functionally, and how does that compare with the knee?","a":"It is a fibrous syndesmosis and an amphiarthrosis, while the knee is a synovial joint and a diarthrosis.","options":["A synovial joint and a diarthrosis, the same as the knee","A fibrous syndesmosis and an amphiarthrosis, while the knee is a synovial diarthrosis","A cartilaginous symphysis and an amphiarthrosis, while the knee is a synovial diarthrosis","A fibrous suture and a synarthrosis, while the knee is a synovial diarthrosis"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"At the distal tibiofibular joint the tibia and fibula are bound by the anterior tibiofibular ligament, with no cavity, which makes it a fibrous joint of the syndesmosis type. It allows only slight movement, so it is an amphiarthrosis. The knee, by contrast, has a synovial cavity and capsule and moves freely, so it is a synovial joint and a diarthrosis.","notes":["The distal tibiofibular joint has no synovial cavity and moves only slightly, so it is not synovial like the knee. This choice treats every joint in the leg as freely movable.","Correct. The distal tibiofibular joint is a fibrous syndesmosis and an amphiarthrosis, unlike the synovial knee.","The functional class is right, but the bones are joined by a ligament, not a fibrocartilage disc. A symphysis would describe the pubic symphysis.","Sutures are found only between skull bones and are immovable in adults. The distal tibiofibular joint is held by a ligament and gives slightly."],"lt":"t-articulations","lid":"c45","comp":51,"competencyId":"w2-fibrous-cartilaginous"},{"id":"L00883","dok":1,"q":"What single criterion does the functional classification of joints use?","a":"The degree of movement the joint permits.","options":["The type of connective tissue present","The degree of movement the joint permits","Whether a synovial cavity is present","The shape of the articulating surfaces"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The functional classification sorts joints only by how much they move. Immovable joints are synarthroses, slightly movable joints are amphiarthroses, and freely movable joints are diarthroses. The structural classification, by contrast, looks at the cavity and the connecting tissue.","notes":["The type of connective tissue is one of the two structural criteria, which separate fibrous, cartilaginous, and synovial joints. Pick it if the question asked about structural classification.","Correct. Functional classification uses the degree of movement.","Whether a synovial cavity is present is the other structural criterion. It would be part of the answer for how joints are classified by structure.","The shape of the articulating surfaces is used to sort synovial joints into six types, such as hinge and pivot. It would fit a question about naming synovial joint types."],"lt":"t-articulations","lid":"c3-x2","comp":50,"competencyId":"w2-joint-classification"},{"id":"L00886","dok":1,"q":"Which type of fibrous joint is found only between the bones of the skull?","a":"A suture.","options":["A suture","A symphysis","A gomphosis","A syndesmosis"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Sutures are fibrous joints found only between skull bones. Thin layers of dense connective tissue join the bones, and their interlocking edges add strength and help absorb shock. Sutures are immovable in adults and slightly movable in infants.","notes":["Correct. Sutures occur only between skull bones.","A symphysis is a cartilaginous joint joined by fibrocartilage, like the pubic symphysis. It is not a fibrous joint at all.","A gomphosis is a fibrous joint found in the skull region, but it holds a tooth in its socket rather than joining two skull bones. Pick it if the question asked about teeth.","A syndesmosis is a fibrous joint held by a ligament, like the distal tibiofibular joint in the leg. It would be the answer for a slightly movable fibrous joint outside the skull."],"lt":"t-articulations","lid":"c6-x2","comp":51,"competencyId":"w2-fibrous-cartilaginous"},{"id":"L00888","dok":1,"q":"Which two types of cartilaginous joint are distinguished by the kind of cartilage that joins the bones?","a":"Synchondrosis and symphysis.","options":["Plane joint and hinge joint","Suture and gomphosis","Syndesmosis and symphysis","Synchondrosis and symphysis"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Cartilaginous joints have no cavity and come in two types. A synchondrosis is joined by hyaline cartilage and is immovable, like the epiphyseal growth plate. A symphysis is joined by a broad disc of fibrocartilage and is slightly movable, like the pubic symphysis.","notes":["Plane and hinge joints are two of the six types of synovial joint, sorted by surface shape. They would fit a question about synovial joints.","Sutures and gomphoses are both fibrous joints, held by dense connective tissue rather than cartilage. Pick this pair if the question asked for two immovable fibrous joints.","The symphysis is right, but a syndesmosis is a fibrous joint united by a ligament. Students mix them up because the names both start with syn.","Correct. A synchondrosis uses hyaline cartilage and a symphysis uses fibrocartilage."],"lt":"t-articulations","lid":"c8-x2","comp":51,"competencyId":"w2-fibrous-cartilaginous"},{"id":"L00890","dok":1,"q":"What is the inner layer of the articular capsule called?","a":"The synovial membrane.","options":["The synovial membrane","The articular cartilage","The periosteum","The fibrous membrane"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The articular capsule has two layers. The outer fibrous membrane is tough dense irregular connective tissue, and the inner synovial membrane is areolar connective tissue with elastic fibers that lines the cavity. The synovial membrane secretes synovial fluid.","notes":["Correct. The synovial membrane is the inner layer of the capsule.","The articular cartilage covers the bone ends inside the joint. It lines the bone surfaces, not the capsule.","The periosteum covers the outer surface of bone, outside the joint. It is where the fibrous membrane attaches, not a layer of the capsule.","The fibrous membrane is the outer layer of the capsule, which gives it strength and attaches to the periosteum. Pick it if the question asked about the outer layer."],"lt":"t-articulations","lid":"c10-x2","comp":52,"competencyId":"w2-synovial-structure"},{"id":"L00891","dok":1,"q":"What is the main lubricating component of synovial fluid?","a":"Hyaluronic acid.","options":["Fibrin","Calcium salts","Collagen","Hyaluronic acid"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Synovial fluid is a clear, pale-yellow, viscous fluid made of hyaluronic acid and interstitial fluid. The hyaluronic acid gives the fluid its slippery, slightly thick texture, so it forms a thin film that reduces friction between the cartilage surfaces. The fluid also nourishes the cartilage and thins as the joint moves.","notes":["Fibrin is the protein that forms the mesh of a blood clot. It would be the answer for what holds a clot together, not for joint lubrication.","Calcium salts harden the matrix of bone. Calcium deposits in a joint would increase friction, not reduce it.","Collagen is the strong protein fiber of ligaments, tendons, and the fibrous membrane. It gives tissues strength but does not lubricate.","Correct. Hyaluronic acid makes synovial fluid slippery."],"lt":"t-articulations","lid":"c11-x2","comp":52,"competencyId":"w2-synovial-structure"},{"id":"L00897","dok":2,"q":"Which synovial joint type allows rotation, as at the joint between the atlas and the axis?","a":"A pivot joint.","options":["A hinge joint","A saddle joint","A pivot joint","A plane joint"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The atlantoaxial joint is a pivot joint. The dens of the axis projects upward into a ring formed by the atlas and a ligament, and the atlas turns around it when you shake your head no. Pivot joints are uniaxial and allow only rotation.","notes":["A hinge joint is uniaxial too, but it allows flexion and extension, not rotation. Pick it if the question asked about the elbow or the interphalangeal joints.","A saddle joint has surfaces that are each concave and convex and is biaxial, like the thumb's carpometacarpal joint. It would be the answer for the base of the thumb.","Correct. The atlantoaxial joint is a pivot joint.","A plane joint has flat surfaces that glide, like the intercarpal joints. It would be the answer for gliding, not turning."],"lt":"t-articulations","lid":"c17-x2","comp":54,"competencyId":"w2-synovial-types"},{"id":"L00900","dok":2,"q":"What is the chief source of stability for the shoulder joint?","a":"The rotator cuff muscles.","options":["The rotator cuff muscles","The deltoid muscle","The glenohumeral ligaments","The coracohumeral ligament"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The shoulder's ligaments add little strength, and its bony socket is shallow. Most of its stability comes from the rotator cuff, the supraspinatus, infraspinatus, teres minor, and subscapularis, whose tendons wrap the joint and hold the humeral head in the glenoid cavity. This is why rotator cuff injuries so often leave the shoulder weak and unstable.","notes":["Correct. The rotator cuff muscles are the shoulder's main stabilizers.","The deltoid is the large muscle that caps the shoulder and is the main abductor of the arm. It moves the shoulder powerfully but does not wrap the joint like the cuff tendons do.","The glenohumeral ligaments are three thickenings of the anterior capsule that help only near the limits of motion. Pick them if the question asked which ligaments reinforce the front of the capsule.","The coracohumeral ligament runs from the coracoid process to the greater tubercle and strengthens the top of the capsule. It would be the answer for the ligament reinforcing the superior capsule."],"lt":"t-articulations","lid":"c20-x2","comp":55,"competencyId":"w2-shoulder-knee"},{"id":"L00902","dok":2,"q":"Which type of joint best describes the knee (tibiofemoral) joint?","a":"A modified hinge joint.","options":["A saddle joint","A pivot joint","A ball-and-socket joint","A modified hinge joint"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The knee is classed as a modified hinge joint. Its main movements are flexion and extension, like a hinge, but the rounded femoral condyles also allow a little rotation when the knee is bent. It is the largest and most complex joint in the body and the one most often injured.","notes":["A saddle joint has surfaces that are each concave and convex, like the thumb's carpometacarpal joint. It would be the answer for the base of the thumb.","A pivot joint allows only rotation around one axis, like the atlantoaxial joint. It would be the answer for a joint whose main job is turning.","A ball-and-socket joint has a round head in a cup and moves in all directions, like the hip just above the knee. Pick it if the question asked about the hip.","Correct. The knee is a modified hinge joint."],"lt":"t-articulations","lid":"c22-x2","comp":55,"competencyId":"w2-shoulder-knee"},{"id":"L00903","dok":1,"q":"Which form of arthritis is a progressive, degenerative loss of joint cartilage from aging and wear?","a":"Osteoarthritis.","options":["Osteoarthritis","Rheumatoid arthritis","Gouty arthritis","Bursitis"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Osteoarthritis is the gradual wearing away of articular cartilage that comes with age and use. It mainly affects the large weight-bearing joints such as the knees and hips. Unlike rheumatoid arthritis, it is not caused by the immune system.","notes":["Correct. Osteoarthritis is degenerative wear of joint cartilage.","Rheumatoid arthritis is an autoimmune disease that inflames the synovial membrane and destroys cartilage, usually starting in small joints on both sides. Pick it if the question described an immune attack.","Gouty arthritis is caused by sodium urate crystals deposited in a joint, often at the base of the big toe. It would be the answer for crystal deposits.","Bursitis is inflammation of a bursa, a fluid sac near the joint, not loss of the cartilage itself. It would fit a question about an inflamed sac from overuse."],"lt":"t-articulations","lid":"c23-x2"},{"id":"L00904","dok":2,"q":"In gouty arthritis, what deposits in the joints and erodes the cartilage?","a":"Sodium urate crystals.","options":["Sodium urate crystals","Cholesterol plaques","Fibrin clots","Calcium phosphate crystals"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Gouty arthritis, or gout, happens when sodium urate crystals build up in a joint. The crystals irritate the tissue, causing inflammation and pain, and over time they erode the articular cartilage. A classic site is the joint at the base of the big toe.","notes":["Correct. Sodium urate crystals deposit in the joints in gout.","Cholesterol plaques build up in artery walls in atherosclerosis. They would be the answer for a vessel disorder, not a joint disorder.","Fibrin clots form when blood clots, such as after an injury that bleeds into a joint. They are not crystals and do not cause gout.","Calcium phosphate is the mineral that hardens normal bone matrix. It is not the crystal of gout, so pick it if the question asked what makes bone hard."],"lt":"t-articulations","lid":"c24-x2"},{"id":"L00905","dok":2,"q":"An athlete stretches and tears a ligament, the band of tissue that stabilizes a joint. What is this injury called?","a":"A sprain.","options":["A sprain","Bursitis","A strain","A dislocation"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"A sprain is a stretched or torn ligament. Because ligaments hold bones together and keep the joint stable, a sprained joint is painful, swollen, and often wobbly. Ankle and knee sprains are among the most common joint injuries.","notes":["Correct. A stretched or torn ligament is a sprain.","Bursitis is inflammation of a bursa, a fluid-filled sac, usually from overuse. It would be the answer for a swollen sac rather than a torn band.","A strain is a stretched or torn muscle or tendon, not a ligament. Students confuse the two because the words sound alike, so pick strain if the injury were to a muscle.","A dislocation is the displacement of bones out of their normal alignment at a joint. Ligaments may be damaged too, but the defining feature is bones out of place."],"lt":"t-articulations","lid":"c25-x2"},{"id":"L00909","dok":2,"q":"A synchondrosis at a growth plate is gradually replaced by bone, fusing two parts into one. What is the resulting joint called?","a":"A synostosis.","options":["A symphysis","A synostosis","A suture","A syndesmosis"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"A synostosis is a joint where two separate bones, or parts of a bone, have fused completely into one by bone. When growth ends, the hyaline cartilage of the epiphyseal plate is replaced by bone, and the synchondrosis becomes a synostosis. Skull sutures that ossify in adulthood also become synostoses.","notes":["A symphysis is a slightly movable cartilaginous joint joined by fibrocartilage, like the pubic symphysis. It stays cartilage and does not fuse.","Correct. When bone replaces the cartilage, the joint becomes a synostosis.","A suture is a fibrous joint between skull bones. A suture can later ossify into a synostosis, but a growth plate never becomes a suture.","A syndesmosis is a fibrous joint held by a ligament, like the distal tibiofibular joint. It would be the answer for a slightly movable fibrous joint."],"lt":"t-articulations","lid":"c29-x2","comp":51,"competencyId":"w2-fibrous-cartilaginous"},{"id":"L00910","dok":2,"q":"A clinician sees a knee that swelled up immediately after an injury. What does immediate swelling most likely indicate?","a":"Blood from torn vessels.","options":["Blood from torn vessels","Worn articular cartilage","Excess synovial fluid","Deposited urate crystals"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Swelling right after a knee injury means blood is leaking into the joint from torn vessels, often from a torn ligament such as the ACL. Swelling that appears later, over hours, is usually excess synovial fluid produced as the synovial membrane reacts. The timing of the swelling helps a clinician judge what was damaged.","notes":["Correct. Immediate swelling is blood from torn vessels.","Worn articular cartilage is the slow damage of osteoarthritis over years. It would not swell a knee within minutes of an injury.","Excess synovial fluid causes swelling too, but it builds up gradually and is the delayed type. Pick it if the swelling appeared hours after the injury.","Urate crystals cause gouty arthritis, which flares without an injury, often at the big toe. They would be the answer for sudden joint pain with no trauma."],"lt":"t-articulations","lid":"c30-x2"},{"id":"L00911","dok":3,"q":"The shoulder is the most mobile joint in the body but dislocates more easily than the hip. Which feature of its structure explains this trade-off?","a":"A shallow socket and loose capsule allow wide motion but hold the humeral head less securely.","options":["Its ligaments are the strongest in the body, which loosens the joint","A shallow socket and loose capsule hold the humeral head loosely","A deep socket grips the humeral head tightly, which strains the capsule","The humeral head is smaller than its socket, so it slips out easily"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The glenoid cavity is shallow compared with the large humeral head, and the capsule is thin and loose. That design lets the arm move freely in every direction, but bone and ligaments do little to hold the head in place, so the rotator cuff muscles must do most of the work. The hip makes the opposite trade, with a deep socket that gives stability at the cost of range.","notes":["The shoulder's ligaments add little strength, which is why the joint relies on muscles. Strong ligaments would tighten a joint, not loosen it.","Correct. A shallow socket and loose capsule give mobility at the cost of stability.","The shoulder's socket is shallow, not deep. A deep socket is a feature of the hip and makes a joint more stable, not less.","The humeral head is actually large relative to the small glenoid cavity. Instability comes from the shallow socket, not from a small head."],"lt":"t-articulations","lid":"d3-t-articulations-1","comp":55,"competencyId":"w2-shoulder-knee"},{"id":"L00912","dok":3,"q":"Synovial joints have a fluid-filled cavity and move freely, while sutures of the adult skull do not move. Why does a joint cavity go along with movement?","a":"The cavity separates the bone ends so they can move over each other, while sutured bones are bound directly by fibrous tissue.","options":["Movement depends only on the muscles that cross a joint, not on its structure","The cavity holds a glue-like fluid that locks the bones together","Sutures contain more synovial fluid than synovial joints do","The cavity frees the bone ends to move, while fibrous tissue binds sutured bones"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"In a synovial joint, the bone ends are not attached to each other directly. They are separated by a fluid-filled cavity and united only by the surrounding capsule, so they can slide and turn. In a suture, dense connective tissue binds the bone edges tightly with no space between them, which prevents movement.","notes":["Muscles produce movement, but the joint's structure sets how much movement is possible. A muscle cannot move bones that are bound together at a suture.","Synovial fluid is a lubricant, not a glue. It reduces friction so the bones move more easily.","Sutures have no synovial cavity or synovial fluid at all. They are fibrous joints.","Correct. A cavity frees the bone ends to move, while fibrous tissue binds sutured bones directly."],"lt":"t-articulations","lid":"d3-t-articulations-2","comp":50,"competencyId":"w2-joint-classification"},{"id":"L00913","dok":3,"q":"Articular cartilage has no blood supply. Why does this cartilage depend on joint movement to stay healthy?","a":"Movement circulates synovial fluid, which brings the cartilage nutrients it cannot get from blood.","options":["Movement wears the cartilage thin so nutrients can pass in from the bone","The cartilage has its own capillaries, so movement is not needed","Movement warms the cartilage so it can make its own nutrients","Movement circulates synovial fluid, which carries nutrients into the cartilage"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Articular cartilage is avascular, meaning it has no blood vessels. Its oxygen and nutrients come from synovial fluid, which also carries away its wastes. Movement and the pressing and releasing of the cartilage help move fluid in and out of it, so regular joint use supports cartilage health.","notes":["Wearing cartilage thin is the damage seen in osteoarthritis, not a way it stays healthy. Thinning harms the joint rather than feeding it.","Articular cartilage has no blood vessels. That is exactly why it relies on synovial fluid for nourishment.","Movement does help synovial fluid thin out, but cartilage cells cannot make nutrients from warmth. They must receive them from the fluid.","Correct. Synovial fluid, moved by joint activity, nourishes the avascular cartilage."],"lt":"t-articulations","lid":"d3-t-articulations-3","comp":52,"competencyId":"w2-synovial-structure"},{"id":"L00914","dok":3,"q":"Ligaments connect bone to bone and are built mostly of collagen fibers. Why does this composition suit a ligament's job?","a":"Collagen resists stretching, so the ligament holds the bones together and limits excess motion.","options":["Collagen is soft and cushioning, so the ligament absorbs shock","Collagen stretches easily, so the ligament never limits motion","Collagen carries nerve signals that tell the joint when to stop","Collagen resists stretching, so the ligament limits excess motion"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Ligaments are dense connective tissue packed with collagen fibers, which are very strong when pulled. That strength lets a ligament hold two bones together, steer their movement, and stop the joint from moving past a safe range. Taut ligaments are one of the main factors that limit a joint's range of motion.","notes":["Cushioning in a joint comes from articular cartilage and the fibrocartilage menisci. Ligaments resist pulling rather than absorbing compression.","Collagen does not stretch easily. Its strength against pulling is what allows ligaments to limit motion and prevent dislocation.","Nerve signals are carried by nerves, not by collagen. The joint does have nerves for pain and position, but collagen fibers do not conduct signals.","Correct. Strong collagen fibers let ligaments hold bones together and limit motion."],"lt":"t-articulations","lid":"d3-t-articulations-4","comp":52,"competencyId":"w2-synovial-structure"},{"id":"L00915","dok":3,"q":"A hinge joint such as the elbow moves in only one plane, while a ball-and-socket joint moves in several. What explains the difference?","a":"The shape of the joint surfaces.","options":["The surface shapes: a hinge bends in one plane, while a ball in a cup turns in many","Range of motion depends only on muscles, never on the shape of the joint","A ball-and-socket joint is limited to one plane, while a hinge moves in many","Both shapes allow the same motion, and ligaments alone make the difference"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The six synovial joint types are defined by the shape of their surfaces, and that shape sets the axes of movement. In a hinge, a convex surface fits a concave one so the bone can only swing back and forth in flexion and extension. A ball in a cup can turn in any direction, giving flexion, extension, abduction, adduction, and rotation.","notes":["Correct. Surface shape sets the axes of motion.","Muscles produce movement, but bone shape is one of the main factors that limits range of motion. A muscle cannot rotate a hinge joint that has no surface to rotate on.","This reverses the two joints. The hinge is the uniaxial one, and the ball-and-socket is triaxial.","Ligament tension does affect range of motion, but the surface shapes of a hinge and a ball-and-socket are what give them different numbers of axes."],"lt":"t-articulations","lid":"d3-t-articulations-5","comp":54,"competencyId":"w2-synovial-types"},{"id":"L00916","dok":3,"q":"Bursae are small fluid sacs placed where tendons and other tissues rub over bone near a joint. Why does inflammation of a bursa cause pain with movement?","a":"The bursa normally cushions friction, so when it is swollen and irritated, movement rubs and presses on the inflamed sac.","options":["The bursa normally locks the joint, and swelling releases it too far","A swollen bursa drains all synovial fluid out of the joint cavity","Pain in a joint always comes from bone, never from a fluid sac","The bursa normally cushions friction, so movement presses on the inflamed sac"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"A bursa is a fluid-filled sac that sits between moving tissues, such as a tendon and a bone, to reduce friction. In bursitis, the sac becomes inflamed from overuse, infection, or rheumatoid arthritis. Every movement that slides the tissues over the swollen sac presses on it, causing pain, swelling, and limited movement.","notes":["Bursae do not lock joints. Their job is to let tissues slide past each other with less friction.","Swelling of a bursa adds fluid to the sac itself. It does not drain fluid out of the joint cavity.","Bones are not the only source of joint pain. Inflamed soft tissues such as a bursa, tendon, or synovial membrane are common sources too.","Correct. Movement presses on the inflamed bursa that normally cushions friction."],"lt":"t-articulations","lid":"d3-t-articulations-6","comp":52,"competencyId":"w2-synovial-structure"},{"id":"L00917","dok":3,"q":"The joints between vertebral bodies are cartilaginous joints that allow slight movement and absorb shock, unlike rigid sutures. Why does the fibrocartilage pad between vertebrae suit the spine?","a":"The pad compresses to cushion loads and allows small movements, giving the spine flexibility and shock absorption.","options":["The pad is a fluid-filled cavity, so the vertebrae move freely like a synovial joint","The pad binds the vertebrae rigidly, like a suture, so the spine cannot bend","The pad compresses to cushion loads and allows small movements","The pad is made of bone, so it passes shock straight through to the next vertebra"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The joints between vertebral bodies are symphyses, cartilaginous joints joined by a broad pad of fibrocartilage. The pad gives slightly under load, cushioning the vertebrae when you walk or jump, and allows a small amount of movement at each level. Added up along the column, those small movements let the spine bend and twist.","notes":["The intervertebral symphyses have no synovial cavity. Freely movable synovial joints do exist in the vertebral column, but they are the small joints between the vertebral arches, not the pads between the bodies.","A suture is an immovable fibrous joint of the skull. The intervertebral joints are slightly movable, so they do not bind the vertebrae rigidly.","Correct. The fibrocartilage pad cushions loads and allows slight movement.","The pad is fibrocartilage, not bone. Bone would be rigid and would not absorb shock."],"lt":"t-articulations","lid":"d3-t-articulations-7","comp":51,"competencyId":"w2-fibrous-cartilaginous"},{"id":"L00918","dok":3,"q":"When a joint is held still in a cast for weeks, it often becomes stiff. Which explanation fits this loss of range of motion?","a":"Unused connective tissue around the joint tightens, and the cartilage loses the nourishment that movement brings.","options":["Stiffness after a cast comes only from weak muscles, not from changes in the joint","Immobilization floods the capsule with extra synovial fluid","Unused connective tissue tightens, and the cartilage gets less nourishment","The cast causes the bones to fuse into a synostosis"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"A joint needs movement to stay healthy. When it is held still, the capsule and ligaments shorten and tighten, and the articular cartilage gets less fresh synovial fluid, since movement helps move that fluid in and out of the cartilage. After the cast comes off, gentle movement gradually restores range of motion.","notes":["Muscles do weaken in a cast, but that is not the only cause. The capsule, ligaments, and cartilage also change, which is why the joint itself feels stiff.","Swelling with extra synovial fluid follows injury or inflammation, not simple rest. Immobilization reduces the circulation of fluid rather than flooding the joint.","Correct. Tightened connective tissue and poorer cartilage nourishment reduce range of motion.","A synostosis forms when bones fuse, such as when a growth plate ossifies. A few weeks in a cast does not fuse a healthy synovial joint."],"lt":"t-articulations","lid":"d3-t-articulations-8","comp":52,"competencyId":"w2-synovial-structure"},{"id":"L00919","dok":3,"q":"The knee is reinforced by ligaments inside and around it and by cartilage pads. Why does the knee need so much reinforcement?","a":"It carries large loads while moving, and its bone surfaces fit loosely, so ligaments and menisci supply the stability.","options":["Its bones interlock tightly, so the soft tissue is there only for cushioning","It carries heavy loads, and its bones fit loosely, so soft tissues add stability","The reinforcement stops the knee from bending under load","The knee bears little weight, so its supports mainly guide movement"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The rounded femoral condyles sit on the fairly flat tops of the tibial condyles, so the bones alone do not hold the knee together. The menisci improve that fit, and the cruciate and collateral ligaments keep the tibia from sliding or bending sideways. These supports are needed because the knee carries much of the body's weight while bending and straightening.","notes":["The knee's bones do not interlock tightly. The poor bony fit is the reason ligaments and menisci are so important.","Correct. Heavy loads plus a loose bony fit make the knee depend on ligaments and menisci.","The supports let the knee bend safely rather than stopping it. They limit only abnormal motions such as forward or backward sliding of the tibia.","The knee carries a large share of the body's weight. Its supports are needed for stability, not just to guide motion."],"lt":"t-articulations","lid":"d3x-t-articulations-1","comp":55,"competencyId":"w2-shoulder-knee"},{"id":"L00920","dok":1,"q":"Which movement brings a limb toward the midline of the body?","a":"Adduction.","options":["Abduction","Adduction","Circumduction","Rotation"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Adduction moves a body part toward the midline, as when you lower an outstretched arm back to your side. Its opposite, abduction, moves the part away from the midline. Both happen in the frontal plane.","notes":["Abduction moves a limb away from the midline, as when you raise your arm out to the side. Pick it for movement away from the body.","Correct. Adduction brings a limb toward the midline.","Circumduction moves the distal end of a limb in a circle. It combines several movements rather than simply moving toward the midline.","Rotation turns a bone around its own long axis, as when you turn your arm so the palm faces in. It does not change the limb's distance from the midline."],"lt":"t-articulations","lid":"w2g-ar-1","comp":53,"competencyId":"w2-joint-movements"},{"id":"L00921","dok":1,"q":"In which movement does the distal end of a limb trace a circle while the proximal end stays fixed?","a":"Circumduction.","options":["Rotation","Adduction","Elevation","Circumduction"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Circumduction is the movement of the distal end of a body part in a circle, while the proximal end stays in place. It is a combination of flexion, extension, abduction, and adduction done in sequence. Drawing a circle in the air with a straight arm is circumduction at the shoulder.","notes":["Rotation turns a bone around its own long axis without moving its distal end in a circle. Pick it if the question described turning the arm in place.","Adduction moves a limb toward the midline in a straight path. It is one part of circumduction but not the whole circle.","Elevation lifts a body part upward, as in shrugging the shoulders. It would be the answer for a superior movement.","Correct. Tracing a circle with the distal end is circumduction."],"lt":"t-articulations","lid":"w2g-ar-2","comp":53,"competencyId":"w2-joint-movements"},{"id":"L00922","dok":1,"q":"Rotating the forearm so the palm faces posteriorly is called what?","a":"Pronation.","options":["Supination","Eversion","Pronation","Opposition"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Pronation turns the forearm so the palm faces posteriorly, or downward when the forearm is held out in front. During pronation the radius crosses over the ulna. Supination is the reverse and returns the palm to face anteriorly, as in anatomical position.","notes":["Supination turns the palm to face anteriorly, the position of the hand in anatomical position. Pick it for turning the palm forward or up.","Eversion turns the sole of the foot outward. It is a foot movement, not a forearm movement.","Correct. Turning the palm to face posteriorly is pronation.","Opposition moves the thumb across the palm to touch the fingertips. It would be the answer for a thumb movement."],"lt":"t-articulations","lid":"w2g-ar-3","comp":53,"competencyId":"w2-joint-movements"},{"id":"L00923","dok":1,"q":"Rotating the forearm so the palm faces anteriorly, as in anatomical position, is called what?","a":"Supination.","options":["Retraction","Pronation","Supination","Inversion"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Supination turns the forearm so the palm faces anteriorly, as it does in anatomical position. In supination the radius and ulna lie parallel. A memory aid is that you hold a bowl of soup with a supinated hand.","notes":["Retraction moves a body part back, as when you pull the shoulders back. It would be the answer for squaring the shoulders.","Pronation turns the palm to face posteriorly, crossing the radius over the ulna. Pick it for turning the palm back or down.","Correct. Turning the palm to face anteriorly is supination.","Inversion turns the sole of the foot inward. It is a foot movement, not a forearm movement."],"lt":"t-articulations","lid":"w2g-ar-4","comp":53,"competencyId":"w2-joint-movements"},{"id":"L00924","dok":1,"q":"Lifting the foot so the toes point upward, decreasing the angle at the ankle, is which movement?","a":"Dorsiflexion.","options":["Dorsiflexion","Plantar flexion","Eversion","Elevation"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Dorsiflexion bends the foot upward at the ankle, bringing the top of the foot (the dorsum) toward the shin. It happens when you walk on your heels or lift your toes off the ground. The opposite movement, pointing the toes down, is plantar flexion.","notes":["Correct. Lifting the toes toward the shin is dorsiflexion.","Plantar flexion points the toes downward, as when you stand on tiptoe. Pick it for the movement toward the sole.","Eversion turns the sole of the foot outward. It tilts the foot sideways rather than lifting the toes.","Elevation lifts a body part such as the shoulders or the mandible superiorly. The ankle movements have their own names, dorsiflexion and plantar flexion."],"lt":"t-articulations","lid":"w2g-ar-5","comp":53,"competencyId":"w2-joint-movements"},{"id":"L00925","dok":1,"q":"Pointing the toes downward, as when standing on tiptoe, is which movement?","a":"Plantar flexion.","options":["Dorsiflexion","Inversion","Plantar flexion","Protraction"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Plantar flexion bends the foot at the ankle toward the sole (the plantar surface), pointing the toes down. It happens when you stand on tiptoe or push off during walking. Its opposite is dorsiflexion, which lifts the toes toward the shin.","notes":["Dorsiflexion lifts the toes upward toward the shin. Pick it for walking on the heels.","Inversion turns the sole of the foot inward. It tilts the foot sideways rather than pointing the toes down.","Correct. Pointing the toes down is plantar flexion.","Protraction moves a body part forward, as when you jut the jaw forward. It would be the answer for forward movement of the mandible or scapula."],"lt":"t-articulations","lid":"w2g-ar-6","comp":53,"competencyId":"w2-joint-movements"},{"id":"L00926","dok":1,"q":"Turning the sole of the foot to face medially, or inward, is which movement?","a":"Inversion.","options":["Dorsiflexion","Eversion","Inversion","Plantar flexion"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Inversion turns the sole of the foot to face medially, toward the other foot. Most ankle sprains happen when the foot is forced too far into inversion. The opposite movement, turning the sole outward, is eversion.","notes":["Dorsiflexion lifts the toes toward the shin. It bends the foot up rather than turning the sole inward.","Eversion turns the sole to face laterally, away from the midline. Pick it for the outward turn.","Correct. Turning the sole inward is inversion.","Plantar flexion points the toes downward, as on tiptoe. It bends the foot down rather than turning the sole."],"lt":"t-articulations","lid":"w2g-ar-7","comp":53,"competencyId":"w2-joint-movements"},{"id":"L00927","dok":1,"q":"Turning the sole of the foot to face laterally, or outward, is which movement?","a":"Eversion.","options":["Plantar flexion","Rotation","Inversion","Eversion"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Eversion turns the sole of the foot to face laterally, away from the midline. It is the opposite of inversion, which turns the sole inward. These side-to-side movements happen mainly at the joints between the tarsal bones rather than at the ankle hinge.","notes":["Plantar flexion points the toes down at the ankle. It bends the foot down rather than turning the sole.","Rotation turns a bone around its own long axis, such as the humerus or femur. The foot's sideways turning has its own names, inversion and eversion.","Inversion turns the sole to face medially, toward the other foot. Pick it for the inward turn.","Correct. Turning the sole outward is eversion."],"lt":"t-articulations","lid":"w2g-ar-8","comp":53,"competencyId":"w2-joint-movements"},{"id":"L00928","dok":1,"q":"Lifting a body part superiorly, such as shrugging the shoulders, is which movement?","a":"Elevation.","options":["Protraction","Circumduction","Elevation","Depression"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Elevation moves a body part upward, as when you shrug the shoulders or close the mouth by raising the mandible. Depression is the opposite, lowering the part again. These terms are used mainly for the scapula and the mandible.","notes":["Protraction moves a body part forward in the horizontal plane, as when you jut the jaw out. It would be the answer for forward movement.","Circumduction moves the distal end of a limb in a circle. It would fit a question about drawing circles with the arm.","Correct. Shrugging the shoulders upward is elevation.","Depression lowers a body part, as when you drop the shoulders or open the mouth. Pick it for the downward movement."],"lt":"t-articulations","lid":"w2g-ar-9","comp":53,"competencyId":"w2-joint-movements"},{"id":"L00929","dok":1,"q":"Moving a body part anteriorly in the horizontal plane, such as jutting the jaw forward, is which movement?","a":"Protraction.","options":["Protraction","Elevation","Rotation","Retraction"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Protraction moves a body part forward in the horizontal plane. Jutting the mandible forward and reaching the arms forward, which slides the scapulae around the chest wall, are both protraction. Retraction is the opposite, pulling the part back.","notes":["Correct. Moving the jaw forward is protraction.","Elevation lifts a body part upward, such as shrugging the shoulders. It would be the answer for a superior movement.","Rotation turns a bone around its own long axis. It does not slide a part forward.","Retraction pulls a body part backward, as when you squeeze the shoulder blades together. Pick it for the backward movement."],"lt":"t-articulations","lid":"w2g-ar-10","comp":53,"competencyId":"w2-joint-movements"},{"id":"L00930","dok":1,"q":"Pulling a body part posteriorly in the horizontal plane, such as squaring the shoulders back, is which movement?","a":"Retraction.","options":["Protraction","Depression","Retraction","Supination"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Retraction moves a body part backward in the horizontal plane. Squeezing the shoulder blades together and pulling the jaw back after jutting it forward are examples. It is the opposite of protraction.","notes":["Protraction moves a body part forward, as when you reach forward or jut the jaw. Pick it for the forward movement.","Depression lowers a body part, such as dropping the shoulders or opening the mouth. It would be the answer for a downward movement.","Correct. Pulling the shoulders back is retraction.","Supination turns the forearm so the palm faces anteriorly. It is a forearm rotation, not a backward slide."],"lt":"t-articulations","lid":"w2g-ar-11","comp":53,"competencyId":"w2-joint-movements"},{"id":"L00931","dok":1,"q":"Moving the thumb across the palm to touch the tips of the other fingers is which movement?","a":"Opposition.","options":["Circumduction","Opposition","Supination","Abduction"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Opposition moves the thumb across the palm so its tip meets the tips of the other fingers. It happens at the carpometacarpal joint of the thumb, a saddle joint, and it is what lets us grip and handle small objects.","notes":["Circumduction moves the distal end of a part in a circle. The thumb can circumduct, but touching the fingertips is a specific movement with its own name.","Correct. Bringing the thumb across to touch the fingertips is opposition.","Supination turns the forearm so the palm faces anteriorly. It moves the whole hand, not the thumb alone.","Abduction moves a part away from the midline. Moving the thumb away from the palm is abduction of the thumb, not opposition."],"lt":"t-articulations","lid":"w2g-ar-12","comp":53,"competencyId":"w2-joint-movements"},{"id":"L00932","dok":2,"q":"A patient rolls the ankle so the sole turns inward, and then a therapist turns it outward. Name the two movements in order.","a":"Inversion, then eversion.","options":["Eversion, then inversion","Dorsiflexion, then plantar flexion","Pronation, then supination","Inversion, then eversion"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Turning the sole inward, toward the other foot, is inversion. Turning it outward is eversion. Rolled ankles usually happen in inversion, which is why the lateral side of the ankle, including the lateral malleolus and its ligaments, is so often injured.","notes":["This reverses the order. Inward comes first in the scenario, and inward is inversion.","Dorsiflexion and plantar flexion bend the foot up and down at the ankle. They would fit lifting the toes and then pointing them.","Pronation and supination are forearm movements in this course, turning the palm back and forward. They would be the answer for turning the palm down and then up.","Correct. Inward is inversion, then outward is eversion."],"lt":"t-articulations","lid":"w2g-ar-13","comp":53,"competencyId":"w2-joint-movements"},{"id":"L00933","dok":2,"q":"Starting in anatomical position, you rotate the forearm so the palm faces backward, then return it. Which sequence of movements is this?","a":"Pronation, then supination.","options":["Pronation, then supination","Supination, then pronation","Protraction, then retraction","Inversion, then eversion"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"In anatomical position the palm faces anteriorly, which is the supinated position. Turning the palm to face posteriorly is pronation, and returning it to face forward is supination. The radius crosses over the ulna in pronation and lies parallel to it in supination.","notes":["Correct. Pronation turns the palm back, and supination returns it.","This reverses the order. Turning away from anatomical position comes first, and that first movement is pronation.","Protraction and retraction move a part forward and back, like the jaw or the scapula. They would fit reaching forward and squaring the shoulders.","Inversion and eversion turn the sole of the foot in and out. They would be the answer for foot movements."],"lt":"t-articulations","lid":"w2g-ar-14","comp":53,"competencyId":"w2-joint-movements"},{"id":"L00934","dok":2,"q":"Reaching the arms forward, then squaring the shoulders back, moves the scapulae through which pair of movements?","a":"Protraction, then retraction.","options":["Rotation, then circumduction","Protraction, then retraction","Retraction, then protraction","Elevation, then depression"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Reaching forward slides the scapulae forward around the chest wall, which is protraction. Squaring the shoulders back pulls the scapulae toward the spine, which is retraction. Both happen in the horizontal plane.","notes":["Rotation turns a bone around its long axis, and circumduction moves a distal end in a circle. Neither describes sliding the scapulae forward and back.","Correct. Forward is protraction, and back is retraction.","This reverses the order. The forward reach comes first, and forward movement is protraction.","Elevation and depression lift and lower the scapulae, as in shrugging and dropping the shoulders. Pick them for up-and-down motion."],"lt":"t-articulations","lid":"w2g-ar-15","comp":53,"competencyId":"w2-joint-movements"},{"id":"L00935","dok":1,"q":"The cruciate ligaments lie inside the knee's articular capsule. Ligaments in this position are classified as what?","a":"Intracapsular ligaments.","options":["Extracapsular ligaments","Collateral ligaments","Articular discs","Intracapsular ligaments"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Intracapsular ligaments lie inside the articular capsule. The anterior and posterior cruciate ligaments of the knee are the main examples, and synovial folds keep them out of the synovial cavity itself. They cross each other in an X, which is what cruciate means.","notes":["Extracapsular ligaments lie outside the capsule, like the collateral ligaments of the knee. Pick it if the question described a ligament on the outer side of the joint.","Collateral ligaments are the extracapsular ligaments on the medial and lateral sides of the knee. They are the cruciates' counterparts outside the capsule.","Articular discs, or menisci, are fibrocartilage pads between the bones, not ligaments. They would be the answer for the C-shaped and O-shaped pads of the knee.","Correct. Ligaments inside the capsule are intracapsular."],"lt":"t-articulations","lid":"w2g-ar-16","comp":52,"competencyId":"w2-synovial-structure"},{"id":"N-m2-joints-1","dok":2,"q":"Which knee ligament crosses within the joint and stops the tibia from sliding backward, which matters most on stairs and inclines?","a":"The posterior cruciate ligament.","options":["The tibial collateral ligament","The oblique popliteal ligament","The anterior cruciate ligament","The posterior cruciate ligament"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The posterior cruciate ligament, or PCL, is an intracapsular ligament that crosses inside the knee. It stops the tibia from sliding backward under the femur. That job matters most when walking down stairs or inclines, when the femur tends to slide forward on the tibia.","notes":["The tibial collateral ligament runs along the medial side of the knee, outside the capsule. It would be the answer for the medial ligament torn in the unhappy triad.","The oblique popliteal ligament strengthens the back of the knee joint from outside. It does not cross within the joint, so it would fit a question about the posterior surface of the capsule.","The anterior cruciate ligament also crosses inside the knee, but it stops the tibia from sliding forward. Pick it if the question asked which ligament is torn most often in serious knee injuries.","Correct. The posterior cruciate ligament stops backward sliding of the tibia."],"comp":55,"competencyId":"w2-shoulder-knee"},{"id":"N-m2-joints-2","dok":1,"q":"Which knee structure is the more circular, almost O-shaped fibrocartilage disc on the lateral side of the joint?","a":"The lateral meniscus.","options":["The medial meniscus","The lateral meniscus","The fibular collateral ligament","The transverse ligament"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The knee has two menisci resting on the tibial condyles. The lateral meniscus is more circular, close to an O shape, while the medial meniscus is C-shaped. Both are fibrocartilage pads that improve the fit of the femur on the tibia and absorb shock.","notes":["The medial meniscus is the C-shaped disc on the medial side. Pick it if the question asked for the C-shaped meniscus or the one torn in the unhappy triad.","Correct. The lateral meniscus is the more circular disc on the lateral side.","The fibular collateral ligament is on the lateral side too, but it is a ligament outside the capsule, not a cartilage disc. It would be the answer for the lateral extracapsular ligament.","The transverse ligament is the band that joins the two menisci at the front. It would be the answer for what connects the medial and lateral menisci."],"comp":55,"competencyId":"w2-shoulder-knee"},{"id":"N-m2-joints-3","dok":1,"q":"Which band joins the medial and lateral menisci of the knee to each other?","a":"The transverse ligament.","options":["The transverse ligament","The patellar ligament","The arcuate popliteal ligament","The posterior cruciate ligament"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The transverse ligament is a short band that runs across the front of the knee joint and connects the medial meniscus to the lateral meniscus. It ties the front edges of the two menisci together. It is easy to confuse with the transverse humeral ligament at the shoulder, which holds a tendon in place instead.","notes":["Correct. The transverse ligament joins the two menisci.","The patellar ligament runs from the patella to the tibial tuberosity on the front of the knee. Pick it if the question asked about the continuation of the quadriceps tendon.","The arcuate popliteal ligament strengthens the lower lateral part of the back of the knee. It would be the answer for posterior reinforcement on the lateral side.","The posterior cruciate ligament crosses inside the knee and stops the tibia from sliding backward. It connects the tibia to the femur, not the menisci to each other."],"comp":55,"competencyId":"w2-shoulder-knee"},{"id":"N-m2-joints-4","dok":2,"q":"An athlete twists a knee, and it is not swollen right away but becomes puffy several hours later. What does this delayed swelling most likely indicate?","a":"Excess synovial fluid.","options":["Fat from the articular fat pads","Sodium urate crystals","Excess synovial fluid","Blood from torn vessels"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The timing of knee swelling gives a clue to its cause. Swelling that appears immediately is blood from torn vessels. Swelling that builds up over hours is excess synovial fluid, produced as the irritated synovial membrane reacts to the injury.","notes":["Articular fat pads are cushions of adipose tissue inside some joints. They do not leak fat into the joint to cause swelling after an injury.","Sodium urate crystals cause gouty arthritis, which flares without an injury, often at the base of the big toe. They would not explain swelling after a twist.","Correct. Delayed swelling is excess synovial fluid.","Blood from torn vessels causes swelling right away, within minutes of the injury. Pick it if the knee swelled immediately."]},{"id":"N-m2-joints-5","dok":2,"q":"When the elbow is fully flexed, the forearm muscles press against the muscles of the arm and stop further bending. Which factor limiting range of motion is at work?","a":"Contact of soft parts.","options":["Contact of soft parts","Hormones","Bone shape","Ligament tension"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Several factors set the limits of joint movement: the shape and fit of the bones, the tension of the ligaments, contact between soft body parts, and hormones. When the bulk of the forearm meets the bulk of the arm, the soft tissue itself blocks further flexion. That is contact of soft parts, which is why very muscular people often cannot flex the elbow as far.","notes":["Correct. Soft tissue pressing against soft tissue is contact of soft parts.","Hormones can loosen joints, as when relaxin loosens the pubic symphysis late in pregnancy. They would be the answer for a change in joint looseness over time, not a mechanical block.","Bone shape limits motion when the bones themselves meet or fit tightly, as when the olecranon of the ulna locks into its fossa during full extension. It would be the answer for that bony stop.","Ligament tension limits motion when ligaments are pulled taut and stop the bones from moving farther. Pick it if a stretched ligament were the limit."]},{"id":"N-m2-joints-6","dok":2,"q":"Which shoulder ligament holds the tendon of the long head of the biceps brachii in place between the greater and lesser tubercles?","a":"The transverse humeral ligament.","options":["The transverse humeral ligament","The glenoid labrum","The glenohumeral ligaments","The coracohumeral ligament"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The transverse humeral ligament is a narrow band that bridges the groove between the greater and lesser tubercles of the humerus. The tendon of the long head of the biceps brachii runs in that groove, and the ligament holds it in place. It is one of the supporting structures of the shoulder joint.","notes":["Correct. The transverse humeral ligament holds the long head of the biceps tendon in its groove.","The glenoid labrum is a rim of fibrocartilage around the glenoid cavity that deepens the socket. It would be the answer for what enlarges the shoulder socket.","The glenohumeral ligaments are three thickenings of the anterior capsule that steady the joint near the limits of motion. They would be the answer for reinforcement of the front of the capsule.","The coracohumeral ligament runs from the coracoid process to the greater tubercle and strengthens the top of the capsule. Pick it if the question asked about the superior capsule."],"comp":55,"competencyId":"w2-shoulder-knee"},{"id":"N-m2-joints-7","dok":2,"q":"Which shoulder ligament runs from the coracoid process to the greater tubercle of the humerus and strengthens the superior part of the capsule?","a":"The coracohumeral ligament.","options":["The coracohumeral ligament","The transverse humeral ligament","The tibial collateral ligament","The glenohumeral ligaments"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The coracohumeral ligament is named for the two bones it connects, the coracoid process of the scapula and the humerus. It attaches to the greater tubercle and reinforces the top of the shoulder's articular capsule. Even so, the shoulder's ligaments add little strength overall, and most stability comes from the rotator cuff.","notes":["Correct. The coracohumeral ligament runs from the coracoid process to the greater tubercle.","The transverse humeral ligament bridges the greater and lesser tubercles to hold the biceps tendon. Pick it if the question asked what keeps the long head of the biceps in its groove.","The tibial collateral ligament is a knee ligament on the medial side of the joint. It would be the answer for a question about the knee, not the shoulder.","The glenohumeral ligaments are three thickenings on the front of the capsule, not a band from the coracoid process. They would be the answer for anterior reinforcement."],"comp":55,"competencyId":"w2-shoulder-knee"},{"id":"N-m2-joints-8","dok":2,"q":"The acromioclavicular joint, where the acromion of the scapula meets the clavicle, has flat surfaces that glide slightly. Which type of synovial joint is it?","a":"A plane joint.","options":["A plane joint","A ball-and-socket joint","A hinge joint","A pivot joint"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The acromioclavicular joint joins the acromion of the scapula to the lateral end of the clavicle at the top of the shoulder. Its flat articular surfaces slide on each other, which makes it a plane joint. Small gliding movements here let the scapula shift as the arm moves.","notes":["Correct. The acromioclavicular joint is a plane joint.","A ball-and-socket joint is the glenohumeral joint just below the acromioclavicular joint. Pick it if the question asked about the shoulder joint itself.","A hinge joint has a convex surface in a concave one and allows flexion and extension, like the elbow. It would be the answer for a joint that bends in one plane.","A pivot joint allows only rotation in a ring of bone and ligament, like the atlantoaxial joint. It would be the answer for a joint that turns."],"comp":54,"competencyId":"w2-synovial-types"},{"id":"N-m2-joints-9","dok":1,"q":"The talocrural joint, where the talus meets the tibia and fibula, is which type of synovial joint?","a":"A hinge joint.","options":["A pivot joint","A ball-and-socket joint","A hinge joint","A plane joint"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The talocrural joint is the ankle joint. The talus fits between the tibia and the two malleoli, and the joint moves in one plane, allowing dorsiflexion and plantar flexion. Movement in one plane makes it a hinge joint.","notes":["A pivot joint allows only rotation, like the atlantoaxial joint. It would be the answer for a joint that turns around one axis.","A ball-and-socket joint moves in every direction, like the hip. The ankle is built to block sideways motion, so it is not a ball-and-socket joint.","Correct. The talocrural joint is a hinge joint.","A plane joint has flat surfaces that glide, like the intertarsal joints just below the ankle. Pick it for the joints between the tarsal bones."],"comp":54,"competencyId":"w2-synovial-types"},{"id":"N-m2-joints-10","dok":2,"q":"The manubriosternal joint is joined by a disc of fibrocartilage and lies in the midline. Which type of cartilaginous joint is it?","a":"A symphysis.","options":["A plane joint","A symphysis","A synchondrosis","A suture"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"A symphysis is a cartilaginous joint joined by a broad, flat disc of fibrocartilage, and it is slightly movable. All symphyses lie in the midline of the body. The manubriosternal joint, between the manubrium and the body of the sternum, is one example, along with the pubic symphysis and the joints between vertebral bodies.","notes":["A plane joint is a synovial joint with gliding surfaces and a joint cavity. The manubriosternal joint has no cavity, so it is not synovial.","Correct. The manubriosternal joint is a symphysis.","A synchondrosis is joined by hyaline cartilage and is immovable. Pick it for the joint between the first rib and the manubrium or for a growth plate.","A suture is a fibrous joint found only between skull bones. It is not a cartilaginous joint."],"comp":51,"competencyId":"w2-fibrous-cartilaginous"},{"id":"N-m2-joints-11","dok":2,"q":"The first rib is joined to the manubrium by hyaline cartilage, and the joint is immovable. Which type of joint is it?","a":"A synchondrosis.","options":["A syndesmosis","A symphysis","A gomphosis","A synchondrosis"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"A synchondrosis is a cartilaginous joint in which hyaline cartilage joins the bones, and it is immovable. Besides the epiphyseal growth plate, the joint between the first rib and the manubrium is a classic example. Its rigidity helps anchor the top of the rib cage.","notes":["A syndesmosis is a fibrous joint held by a ligament, like the distal tibiofibular joint. It would be the answer for bones united by a ligament, not by cartilage.","A symphysis is joined by fibrocartilage and is slightly movable, like the manubriosternal joint just below. Pick it if the question described a fibrocartilage disc.","A gomphosis is the peg-in-socket fibrous joint of a tooth. It would be the answer for teeth in their sockets.","Correct. The first rib joins the manubrium at a synchondrosis."],"comp":51,"competencyId":"w2-fibrous-cartilaginous"},{"id":"N-m2-joints-12","dok":1,"q":"What is the specific name for extension that continues past the anatomical position, as when you tip your head back to look at the ceiling?","a":"Hyperextension.","options":["Hyperextension","Circumduction","Lateral flexion","Rotation"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Extension increases the angle between bones and returns a part to anatomical position. When the movement keeps going beyond anatomical position, it is called hyperextension. Tipping the head back to look straight up and swinging the arm back behind the body are examples.","notes":["Correct. Extension beyond anatomical position is hyperextension.","Circumduction moves the distal end of a part in a circle. It would be the answer for rolling the head in a circle, not tipping it straight back.","Lateral flexion bends the head or trunk sideways in the frontal plane. Pick it if the question described tilting an ear toward the shoulder.","Rotation turns a bone around its own long axis, as when you shake the head no. It would be the answer for turning the head side to side."],"comp":53,"competencyId":"w2-joint-movements"},{"id":"N-m2-joints-13","dok":1,"q":"Bending the trunk sideways in the frontal plane, as when you reach down to touch the side of your knee, is which movement?","a":"Lateral flexion.","options":["Hyperextension","Abduction","Lateral flexion","Rotation"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Lateral flexion is bending the trunk or the head sideways, in the frontal plane. It occurs at the joints of the vertebral column, such as when you tilt your trunk to one side or bring an ear toward a shoulder. Ordinary flexion, by contrast, bends forward in the sagittal plane.","notes":["Hyperextension is extension past anatomical position, as in bending backward. It would be the answer for leaning back, not sideways.","Abduction moves a limb away from the midline, as when you raise an arm out to the side. The trunk's sideways bending has its own name, lateral flexion.","Correct. Bending the trunk sideways is lateral flexion.","Rotation turns a part around its long axis, as when you twist the trunk to look behind you. It would be the answer for twisting, not bending."],"comp":53,"competencyId":"w2-joint-movements"},{"id":"N-m2-joints-14","dok":1,"q":"Which movement is a simple back-and-forth or side-to-side sliding of one bone surface on another, with no change in the angle between the bones?","a":"Gliding.","options":["Rotation","Gliding","Circumduction","Flexion"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Gliding is the simplest movement at a synovial joint. Flat or slightly curved surfaces slide over each other back and forth or side to side, and the angle between the bones does not change. It happens at plane joints, such as the intercarpal and intertarsal joints.","notes":["Rotation turns a bone around its own long axis. It would be the answer for turning the head or the humerus in place.","Correct. Sliding without changing the angle is gliding.","Circumduction moves the distal end of a part in a circle, combining several angular movements. It changes angles throughout, so it is not gliding.","Flexion decreases the angle between bones, so it is an angular movement, not a slide. Pick it for bending the elbow or knee."],"comp":53,"competencyId":"w2-joint-movements"},{"id":"N-m2-joints-15","dok":2,"q":"Why does warming up before exercise make the joints move more easily?","a":"Synovial fluid is gel-like at rest and becomes thinner with movement.","options":["Movement drains synovial fluid out of the joint so the bones touch","Warming up shortens the ligaments so the joint is tighter","Synovial fluid is gel-like at rest and becomes thinner with movement","Warming up adds new articular cartilage to the bone ends"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Synovial fluid, made of hyaluronic acid and interstitial fluid, is thick and gel-like when a joint has been at rest. As the joint moves, the fluid thins and spreads as a slippery film between the cartilage surfaces. That is why a few minutes of easy movement before exercise makes the joints feel looser.","notes":["Movement spreads synovial fluid over the joint surfaces rather than draining it. Bone ends touching without fluid would increase friction.","Ligaments do not shorten during a warm-up, and a tighter joint would move less freely, not more.","Correct. Synovial fluid thins with movement, which eases the joints.","Articular cartilage does not grow during a warm-up. Its thickness changes only slowly over time, so this cannot explain a quick change in how the joint feels."],"comp":52,"competencyId":"w2-synovial-structure"},{"id":"N-m2-joints-16","dok":2,"q":"A person described as double-jointed can bend some joints much farther than usual. What explains this, and what is the trade-off?","a":"Greater ligament flexibility, which makes the joints less stable.","options":["Shallower sockets in every joint, which make them stiffer","An extra joint in each finger, which makes the fingers weaker","Greater ligament flexibility, which makes the joints less stable","Extra synovial fluid, which makes the cartilage wear faster"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Double-jointed people do not have extra joints. Their ligaments are more flexible than usual, so the bones can move farther before the ligaments become taut. Because ligament tension is one of the main things holding a joint together, these joints are less stable and dislocate more easily.","notes":["A shallow socket would allow more motion, not stiffness, and double-jointedness is about ligaments, not socket shape. This choice mixes two separate ideas.","Double-jointed people have the normal number of joints and phalanges. The name is misleading, which is why students often take it literally.","Correct. Looser ligaments allow more motion but less stability.","Extra synovial fluid is a sign of joint irritation or injury, not the cause of unusual flexibility. It would be the answer for delayed swelling after a knee injury."],"comp":52,"competencyId":"w2-synovial-structure"},{"id":"N-m2-joints-17","dok":3,"q":"A car crash dislocates a person's knee, pushing the tibia forward on the femur. Which vessel behind the knee is most at risk of damage?","a":"The popliteal artery.","options":["The femoral artery","The dorsalis pedis artery","The popliteal artery","The great saphenous vein"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"A dislocated knee is a displacement of the tibia relative to the femur, most often anteriorly. The popliteal artery runs through the popliteal fossa directly behind the knee joint, so it can be stretched or torn when the bones shift. Damage to it can cut off blood flow to the leg and foot, which makes a knee dislocation an emergency.","notes":["The femoral artery runs through the thigh and becomes the popliteal artery only as it passes behind the knee. Pick it if the injury were in the thigh.","The dorsalis pedis artery runs on the top of the foot, far from the knee. It is a pulse point for checking blood flow to the foot, not the vessel injured at the knee.","Correct. The popliteal artery lies directly behind the knee and is at risk in a dislocation.","The great saphenous vein is a superficial vein running up the medial side of the leg and thigh. It is not tucked behind the knee joint like the popliteal artery."],"comp":127,"competencyId":"w5-lab-lower-limb-vessels-nerves"},{"id":"N-m2-joints-18","dok":1,"q":"A bone turning around its own long axis, as when you shake your head no, is which movement?","a":"Rotation.","options":["Circumduction","Rotation","Lateral flexion","Abduction"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Rotation is the turning of a bone around its own long axis. Shaking the head no rotates the atlas around the dens of the axis. In the limbs, rotation toward the midline is medial rotation and rotation away from it is lateral rotation.","notes":["Circumduction moves the distal end of a part in a circle, so the part sweeps out a cone. In rotation the part stays in place and only turns.","Correct. Turning around the long axis is rotation.","Lateral flexion tilts the head or trunk sideways, bringing an ear toward a shoulder. It would be the answer for tilting, not turning.","Abduction moves a limb away from the midline. It would be the answer for raising the arm out to the side."],"comp":53,"competencyId":"w2-joint-movements"},{"id":"N-m2-joints-19","dok":1,"q":"Lowering the mandible to open the mouth, or dropping the shoulders after a shrug, is which movement?","a":"Depression.","options":["Plantar flexion","Depression","Retraction","Elevation"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Depression moves a body part inferiorly, as when you lower the mandible to open the mouth or drop the shoulders. Elevation is the opposite movement, lifting the part upward. These terms are used mainly for the mandible and the scapula.","notes":["Plantar flexion points the toes down at the ankle. It applies to the foot, not the mandible or shoulders.","Correct. Lowering the mandible or the shoulders is depression.","Retraction pulls a part backward in the horizontal plane, as when you square the shoulders. It would be the answer for a backward movement.","Elevation lifts a body part upward, as in shrugging the shoulders or closing the mouth. Pick it for the upward movement."],"comp":53,"competencyId":"w2-joint-movements"},{"id":"N-a17d-50-1","dok":3,"q":"A student argues that any joint without a synovial cavity must be immovable. Which joint disproves this, and how is it classified?","a":"The distal tibiofibular joint, fibrous but slightly movable.","options":["An adult skull suture, which is fibrous and immovable.","The elbow joint, which is synovial and freely movable.","The distal tibiofibular joint, fibrous but slightly movable.","The epiphyseal plate, which is cartilaginous and immovable."],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Structure and function are separate classifications, so check one joint against both. The distal tibiofibular joint is a syndesmosis: it has no synovial cavity and is held by a ligament, so it is fibrous. It still allows slight movement, which makes it an amphiarthrosis and disproves the claim.","notes":["An adult suture has no cavity and is immovable, so it fits the student's claim rather than disproving it.","The elbow has a synovial cavity, so it says nothing about joints without one.","Correct. A syndesmosis has no cavity, yet it is slightly movable, an amphiarthrosis.","The epiphyseal plate is a synchondrosis with no cavity, and it is immovable, so it supports the claim."],"comp":50,"competencyId":"w2-joint-classification"},{"id":"N-a17d-50-2","dok":3,"q":"A lab model shows two bone ends capped with smooth cartilage, enclosed by a capsule around a fluid-filled space. Before you learn the joint's name, what can you say about its functional class?","a":"It is a diarthrosis, because all synovial joints move freely.","options":["It is a diarthrosis, because all synovial joints move freely.","It is an amphiarthrosis, since cartilage allows only slight give.","It is a synarthrosis, since the capsule binds the bones tightly.","It cannot be classed until its synovial type is known."],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Use the structure to reach the function. A fluid-filled cavity inside an articular capsule, with articular cartilage on the bone ends, makes this a synovial joint. All synovial joints are diarthroses, freely movable, so the functional class is known before the joint is named.","notes":["Correct. A capsule around a fluid-filled cavity makes it synovial, and every synovial joint is a diarthrosis.","Slightly movable joints are syndesmoses and symphyses, which have no cavity. Articular cartilage in a synovial joint does not bind the bones.","Immovable joints are sutures and gomphoses, with no cavity. The capsule here encloses a fluid-filled cavity that allows movement.","The synovial type sets the axes of movement, not the functional class. Every synovial type is a diarthrosis."],"comp":50,"competencyId":"w2-joint-classification"},{"id":"N-a17d-51-1","dok":1,"q":"In a gomphosis, which ligament holds the tooth in its bony socket?","a":"The periodontal ligament.","options":["The anterior tibiofibular ligament.","The periodontal ligament.","The interosseous membrane.","The transverse ligament."],"correctIndex":1,"why":"A gomphosis is a fibrous joint in which a cone-shaped peg, the tooth, is held in a bony socket by the periodontal ligament. It is immovable, a synarthrosis.","notes":["The anterior tibiofibular ligament unites the distal tibia and fibula in a syndesmosis, not a tooth in its socket.","Correct. The periodontal ligament holds a tooth in its socket in a gomphosis.","The interosseous membrane is a syndesmosis, a sheet of dense connective tissue binding the shafts of the radius and ulna, or the tibia and fibula. It does not hold a tooth.","The transverse ligament joins the two menisci of the knee to each other."],"comp":51,"competencyId":"w2-fibrous-cartilaginous"},{"id":"N-a17d-51-2","dok":3,"q":"The growth plate and the joint between the first rib and the manubrium are both joined by hyaline cartilage. A student calls both symphyses because they are cartilaginous. What is the correct type, and what decides it?","a":"Both are synchondroses, because hyaline cartilage joins the bones.","options":["Both are symphyses, because they are slightly movable.","The plate is a synchondrosis; the rib joint is a symphysis.","Both are synchondroses, because hyaline cartilage joins the bones.","Both are syndesmoses, because a band of tissue joins them."],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The two cartilaginous joint types are told apart by the cartilage that joins the bones. A synchondrosis is joined by hyaline cartilage and is immovable, as at the growth plate and the first rib with the manubrium. A symphysis is joined by a broad, flat disc of fibrocartilage and is slightly movable.","notes":["Symphyses are joined by fibrocartilage and are slightly movable. These joints are hyaline and immovable.","Both joints are joined by hyaline cartilage, so both are synchondroses. A symphysis needs a fibrocartilage disc.","Correct. Hyaline cartilage joining the bones defines a synchondrosis.","Syndesmoses are fibrous joints united by a ligament. These joints are held by cartilage, so they are cartilaginous."],"comp":51,"competencyId":"w2-fibrous-cartilaginous"},{"id":"N-a17d-51-3","dok":3,"q":"The skull of an 8-year-old shows a suture running down the middle of the frontal bone. What is this suture, and what does its presence tell you?","a":"A metopic suture, a frontal suture kept past about age six.","options":["A sagittal suture, joining the two parietal bones.","A coronal suture, joining the frontal and parietal bones.","A metopic suture, a frontal suture kept past about age six.","A synostosis, a suture that has already fused into bone."],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Identify the joint, then interpret its age. A suture in the frontal bone is a frontal suture, and when it is still present past about age six it is called a metopic suture. Like all sutures, it is a fibrous joint found only between bones of the skull.","notes":["The sagittal suture runs along the midline between the two parietal bones, behind the frontal bone, not through it.","The coronal suture runs across the top of the skull between the frontal and parietal bones, not down the middle of the frontal bone.","Correct. The metopic suture is a frontal suture that persists past about age six.","A synostosis is a joint where two bones have fused completely. A visible suture line means the frontal halves have not fused."],"comp":51,"competencyId":"w2-fibrous-cartilaginous"},{"id":"N-a17d-53-1","dok":3,"q":"From anatomical position, a person raises the arm out to the side until it is overhead, then lowers it back to the side. Which two movements occur, and at which type of joint?","a":"Abduction then adduction, at a ball-and-socket joint.","options":["Abduction then adduction, at a ball-and-socket joint.","Adduction then abduction, at a ball-and-socket joint.","Flexion then extension, at a hinge joint.","Elevation then depression, at a plane joint."],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Name the movements by their direction relative to the midline. Raising the arm out to the side moves it away from the midline, which is abduction, and lowering it back is adduction. The shoulder, where this happens, is a ball-and-socket joint.","notes":["Correct. Moving away from the midline is abduction, moving back is adduction, and the shoulder is a ball-and-socket joint.","The joint is right, but the order is reversed. Moving away from the midline comes first, and that is abduction.","Flexion and extension change the angle in the sagittal plane, as when the arm moves forward and back. The shoulder is not a hinge joint.","Elevation and depression lift and lower a part, such as shrugging the shoulders. Raising the arm sideways is abduction."],"comp":53,"competencyId":"w2-joint-movements"},{"id":"N-a17d-53-2","dok":3,"q":"From anatomical position, a person turns the forearm so the palm faces posteriorly. Which movement is this, and what happens to the forearm bones?","a":"Pronation, with the radius crossing over the ulna.","options":["Supination, with the radius crossing over the ulna.","Pronation, with the ulna crossing over the radius.","Pronation, with the radius crossing over the ulna.","Medial rotation, with neither forearm bone moving."],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Start in anatomical position with the palm facing anteriorly, which is supination. Turning the palm to face posteriorly is pronation. In pronation the radius crosses over the ulna, using the proximal and distal radioulnar joints.","notes":["Supination turns the palm to face anteriorly, the anatomical position. In supination the radius and ulna lie side by side.","The movement is right, but the radius is the bone that crosses over in pronation.","Correct. Turning the palm to face posteriorly is pronation, and the radius crosses over the ulna.","Medial rotation turns a bone around its long axis toward the midline. Turning the palm is pronation, which moves the radius."],"comp":53,"competencyId":"w2-joint-movements"},{"id":"N-a17d-53-3","dok":3,"q":"A person walks on the heels with the toes lifted, then rises onto tiptoe. Which two ankle movements occur, in order, and what type of joint is the ankle?","a":"Dorsiflexion then plantar flexion, at a hinge joint.","options":["Plantar flexion then dorsiflexion, at a hinge joint.","Inversion then eversion, at a plane joint.","Dorsiflexion then plantar flexion, at a pivot joint.","Dorsiflexion then plantar flexion, at a hinge joint."],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Walking on the heels bends the foot up at the ankle, which is dorsiflexion. Rising onto tiptoe points the toes down, which is plantar flexion. The ankle is a hinge joint, which moves in one axis of flexion and extension.","notes":["The joint is right, but the order is reversed. Lifting the toes is dorsiflexion and comes first here.","Inversion and eversion turn the sole inward and outward. They are not the up and down movements of heel and toe walking.","The movements are right, but pivot joints allow only rotation, as at the atlantoaxial joint. The ankle is a hinge joint.","Correct. Lifting the toes is dorsiflexion, pointing them down is plantar flexion, and the ankle is a hinge joint."],"comp":53,"competencyId":"w2-joint-movements"},{"id":"N-a17d-54-1","dok":1,"q":"Which type of synovial joint is the shoulder joint?","a":"A ball-and-socket joint.","options":["A hinge joint.","A saddle joint.","A ball-and-socket joint.","A condyloid joint."],"correctIndex":2,"why":"In a ball-and-socket joint, a ball fits a cup and the joint moves around three axes. The shoulder and the hip are the two examples, and the shoulder is the most freely movable joint in the body.","notes":["Hinge joints move in one axis, flexion and extension, as at the elbow, ankle, and interphalangeal joints.","A saddle joint has surfaces that are each concave and convex. The carpometacarpal joint of the thumb is the example.","Correct. The shoulder is a ball-and-socket joint.","A condyloid joint fits an oval projection into an oval depression, as at the radiocarpal and metacarpophalangeal joints."],"comp":54,"competencyId":"w2-synovial-types"},{"id":"N-a17d-54-2","dok":1,"q":"Which type of synovial joint are the intercarpal joints between the wrist bones?","a":"Plane joints.","options":["Pivot joints.","Hinge joints.","Plane joints.","Saddle joints."],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Plane joints have flat or slightly curved surfaces that glide across each other. The intercarpal and intertarsal joints are the standard examples.","notes":["Pivot joints fit a rounded surface into a ring of bone and ligament for rotation, as at the atlantoaxial and radioulnar joints.","Hinge joints allow only flexion and extension, as at the elbow and ankle. The small carpal surfaces glide instead.","Correct. The intercarpal joints are plane joints with flat surfaces that glide.","The only saddle joint example is the carpometacarpal joint of the thumb, not the joints between the carpals."],"comp":54,"competencyId":"w2-synovial-types"},{"id":"N-a17d-54-3","dok":1,"q":"Which type of synovial joint is the radiocarpal joint at the wrist?","a":"A condyloid joint.","options":["A condyloid joint.","A plane joint.","A pivot joint.","A ball-and-socket joint."],"correctIndex":0,"why":"A condyloid joint fits an oval projection into an oval depression and moves around two axes. The radiocarpal joint at the wrist and the metacarpophalangeal joints are the examples.","notes":["Correct. The radiocarpal joint is a condyloid joint.","Plane joints have flat surfaces that glide, as between the carpals themselves. The radiocarpal joint is condyloid.","Pivot joints allow only rotation, as at the atlantoaxial and radioulnar joints.","Ball-and-socket joints are triaxial, as at the shoulder and hip. The wrist joint is condyloid and biaxial."],"comp":54,"competencyId":"w2-synovial-types"}]}]},{"id":"m3","n":3,"title":"Module 3. Heart, Vessels, Muscle and Blood","exam":"Exam 3","extra":false,"topics":[{"id":"m3-heart-structure","title":"Heart: pericardium, wall, chambers and surface features","summary":"location, pericardium, heart wall layers, chambers, septa, auricles, sulci, internal features (pectinate muscles, trabeculae carneae, fossa ovalis)","mw":"2026-10-05","tr":"2026-10-01","cards":[{"id":"L00096","dok":1,"q":"The visceral layer of the serous pericardium has a second name. What is it?","a":"The epicardium.","options":["The parietal pericardium","The fibrous pericardium","The endocardium","The epicardium"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The visceral layer of the serous pericardium is the same membrane as the epicardium, the outermost layer of the heart wall. It is called visceral because it lies on the organ, and epicardium because it sits on top of the heart muscle. Recognizing both names prevents counting it twice when listing the layers around the heart.","notes":["The parietal pericardium is the serous layer that lines the inside of the fibrous pericardium. It is the wall-side layer, not the layer on the heart.","The fibrous pericardium is the tough, outermost sac that anchors the heart in the mediastinum. It is separate from the serous layers and does not touch the heart surface.","The endocardium is the innermost layer of the heart wall, lining the chambers and valves. Pick it for a question about the lining inside the heart.","Correct. The visceral pericardium is the epicardium."],"lt":"t-body-cavities","lid":"c15","comp":56,"competencyId":"cv-surfaces"},{"id":"L00117","dok":2,"q":"Moving from the outside inward toward the heart muscle, which order lists the layers and space around the heart correctly?","a":"Fibrous pericardium, parietal serous pericardium, pericardial cavity, visceral serous pericardium (epicardium).","options":["Epicardium, pericardial cavity, parietal serous pericardium, fibrous pericardium","Parietal serous pericardium, fibrous pericardium, pericardial cavity, epicardium","Pericardial cavity, fibrous pericardium, epicardium, parietal serous pericardium","Fibrous pericardium, parietal serous pericardium, pericardial cavity, epicardium"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The outermost layer is the tough fibrous pericardium, which anchors the heart and prevents overfilling. Lining its inner surface is the parietal layer of the serous pericardium, then comes the pericardial cavity with its film of fluid, and finally the visceral layer, the epicardium, lies on the heart itself. The parietal-then-visceral pattern is the same in every serous membrane.","notes":["This is the correct sequence written in reverse, from the heart outward. It would be the answer if the question asked for the order from the inside out.","This order puts the serous parietal layer outside the fibrous sac. The fibrous pericardium is the outermost layer, with the parietal serous layer lining its inner surface.","This order starts with the cavity, which is a space between two serous layers and cannot be the outermost structure. It also separates the fibrous pericardium from the parietal layer fused to it.","Correct. Fibrous, parietal, cavity, visceral."],"lt":"t-body-cavities","lid":"c36","comp":56,"competencyId":"cv-surfaces"},{"id":"L00159","dok":3,"q":"Fluid building up in the pericardial cavity, called cardiac tamponade, can stop the heart from pumping well. Using the structure of the pericardium, why does this happen?","a":"The tough fibrous pericardium cannot stretch quickly, so the fluid presses on the heart and keeps its chambers from filling.","options":["The fluid drains into the pleural cavities and collapses the lungs","The fluid seeps into the heart muscle and makes it beat too fast","The stiff fibrous sac lets the fluid squeeze the heart and limit filling","The fluid passes into the chambers and thins the blood"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The fibrous pericardium is a tough, inelastic sac of dense connective tissue that normally anchors the heart and keeps it from overfilling. When fluid collects in the pericardial cavity inside it, the sac cannot stretch to make room, so the fluid squeezes the heart from outside. The chambers cannot fill fully, and the heart pumps less blood with each beat.","notes":["The pericardial cavity is a closed space, separate from the pleural cavities. The fluid stays trapped around the heart, which is what makes it dangerous.","The pericardial cavity is outside the heart wall, so the fluid does not enter the heart muscle. The problem is pressure on the heart from outside.","Correct. The inelastic sac turns extra fluid into pressure on the heart.","The pericardial cavity does not connect with the inside of the heart chambers. The fluid stays in the sac and compresses the heart."],"lt":"t-body-cavities","lid":"d3-t-body-cavities-8","comp":56,"competencyId":"cv-surfaces"},{"id":"L01496","dok":1,"q":"In which division of the thoracic cavity does the heart sit?","a":"The mediastinum.","options":["The retroperitoneal space","The left pleural cavity","The peritoneal cavity","The mediastinum"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The heart sits in the mediastinum, the central compartment of the thoracic cavity between the two lungs. The mediastinum runs from the sternum in front to the vertebral column behind and also holds the great vessels, trachea, and esophagus. The heart rests on the diaphragm within it and tilts to the left.","notes":["The retroperitoneal space lies behind the peritoneum on the posterior abdominal wall, where the kidneys sit. Pick it for the kidneys or pancreas, not the heart.","Each pleural cavity is the space around one lung, on either side of the mediastinum. The heart tilts toward the left, but it lies between the pleural cavities, so pick the pleural cavity for a question about the lungs.","The peritoneal cavity is inside the abdominopelvic cavity, below the diaphragm, and holds organs like the stomach and intestines. Pick it for an abdominal organ, not the heart.","Correct. The heart sits in the mediastinum, between the two lungs."],"lt":"t-heart","lid":"c1","comp":8,"competencyId":"w1-mediastinum"},{"id":"L01497","dok":1,"q":"What is the blunt, rounded inferior tip of the heart called?","a":"The apex.","options":["The base","The auricle","The coronary sulcus","The apex"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The apex is the rounded inferior tip of the heart, pointing down, forward, and to the left. It is formed by the tip of the left ventricle. The heart's orientation can feel upside down at first, because the apex points downward while the base is the broad surface at the top.","notes":["The base is the broad superior surface of the heart, where the great vessels enter and leave. Pick the base if the question asked about the surface opposite the tip.","An auricle is a small wrinkled pouch on each atrium. Pick it if the question asked about the ear-shaped flap that slightly increases atrial capacity.","The coronary sulcus is the groove that circles the heart between the atria and the ventricles, carrying coronary vessels. Pick it for a question about that surface groove.","Correct. The apex is the pointed inferior tip, formed by the left ventricle."],"lt":"t-heart","lid":"c2","comp":57,"competencyId":"cv-chambers"},{"id":"L01498","dok":1,"q":"What is the broad superior surface of the heart, where the great vessels attach, called?","a":"The base.","options":["The sternocostal surface","The diaphragmatic surface","The apex","The base"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The base is the broad superior surface of the heart, formed mostly by the left atrium, where the great vessels enter and leave. The name can be confusing because the base sits at the top, opposite the apex at the bottom. Thinking of the heart as a cone resting on its tip helps, since the wide end of a cone is its base.","notes":["The sternocostal surface is the anterior surface, mostly right ventricle, lying behind the sternum and ribs. Pick it if the question asked which surface faces the front of the chest.","The diaphragmatic surface is the inferior surface, mostly left ventricle, resting on the diaphragm. Pick it if the question asked which surface sits on the diaphragm.","The apex is the rounded inferior tip of the heart, formed by the left ventricle. Pick it if the question asked about the tip that points down and to the left.","Correct. The base is the broad superior surface where the great vessels attach."],"lt":"t-heart","lid":"c3","comp":57,"competencyId":"cv-chambers"},{"id":"L01499","dok":1,"q":"What is the double-walled sac that encloses the heart called?","a":"The pericardium.","options":["The peritoneum","The pericardium","The pleura","The endocardium"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The pericardium is the double-walled sac around the heart. Its outer part is the tough fibrous pericardium, and inside that is the serous pericardium, a thin membrane with one layer lining the sac and one layer on the heart's surface. The prefix peri means around and cardi means heart, so the name describes exactly what it does.","notes":["The peritoneum is the serous membrane that lines the abdominopelvic cavity and covers its organs. Pick it for a question about the abdomen.","Correct. The pericardium is the double-walled sac that encloses the heart.","The pleura is the double-layered serous membrane around each lung. Pick the pleura if the question asked about the membrane covering the lungs.","The endocardium is the smooth inner lining of the heart chambers, not a sac outside the heart. Pick it if the question asked what lines the chambers."],"lt":"t-heart","lid":"c4","comp":56,"competencyId":"cv-surfaces"},{"id":"L01500","dok":1,"q":"Which layer of the heart wall is the thick, muscular middle layer that contracts?","a":"The myocardium.","options":["The endocardium","The myocardium","The epicardium","The fibrous pericardium"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The myocardium is the thick middle layer of the heart wall, made of cardiac muscle arranged in spiralling bundles. It makes up about 95 percent of the wall's thickness and is the layer that actually contracts to pump blood. The prefix myo means muscle, which is a quick way to remember its job.","notes":["The endocardium is the thin, smooth inner lining of the chambers and valves. Pick it if the question asked what lines the inside of the heart.","Correct. The myocardium is the thick cardiac muscle layer that contracts.","The epicardium is the thin outer layer of the heart wall, a serous membrane with fat and coronary vessels in it. Pick it if the question asked about the outermost wall layer.","The fibrous pericardium is the tough outer sac around the heart, not part of the heart wall. Pick it if the question asked what anchors the heart in the mediastinum."],"lt":"t-heart","lid":"c5","comp":56,"competencyId":"cv-surfaces"},{"id":"L01501","dok":1,"q":"Which layer forms the smooth inner lining of the heart chambers?","a":"The endocardium.","options":["The myocardium","The parietal pericardium","The endocardium","The epicardium"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The endocardium is the innermost layer of the heart wall, a smooth endothelium that lines the chambers. Its smoothness lets blood flow past without clotting or catching. The prefix endo means within, which matches its position on the inside of the heart.","notes":["The myocardium is the thick muscular middle layer that contracts. Pick it if the question asked about the layer that pumps blood.","The parietal layer of the serous pericardium lines the inside of the fibrous sac, facing the pericardial cavity. Pick it for a question about the lining of the sac, not the lining of the chambers.","Correct. The endocardium is the smooth endothelial lining of the chambers.","The epicardium is the outer layer of the heart wall, on the heart's surface. Pick it if the question asked about the outermost layer of the wall."],"lt":"t-heart","lid":"c6","comp":56,"competencyId":"cv-surfaces"},{"id":"L01502","dok":1,"q":"How many chambers does the human heart have?","a":"Four.","options":["Three","Four","Five","Two"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The human heart has four chambers, two atria above and two ventricles below. The atria are thin-walled receiving chambers, and the ventricles are thick-walled pumping chambers. Having a separate right and left pair lets the heart run the pulmonary and systemic circuits side by side without mixing the blood.","notes":["A three-chambered heart, with two atria and one shared ventricle, is found in amphibians such as frogs. The human heart has a separate ventricle on each side.","Correct. The heart has four chambers: right atrium, right ventricle, left atrium, and left ventricle.","There is no fifth chamber. Students sometimes count an auricle as a chamber, but an auricle is only a pouch on an atrium.","Two is the number of atria, or the number of ventricles, but not the total. Pick two if the question asked how many ventricles the heart has."],"lt":"t-heart","lid":"c7","comp":57,"competencyId":"cv-chambers"},{"id":"L01503","dok":1,"q":"Which heart chamber has the thickest wall?","a":"The left ventricle.","options":["The left ventricle","The left atrium","The right atrium","The right ventricle"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The left ventricle has the thickest wall of the four chambers because it pumps blood out through the aorta to the entire body. Pushing blood through the whole systemic circuit takes much more force than any other chamber needs. In a cross section of the ventricles, the left ventricle looks like a thick ring and the right like a thinner crescent beside it.","notes":["Correct. The left ventricle has the thickest wall because it pumps to the entire body.","The left atrium is a thin-walled receiving chamber that only moves blood a short distance into the left ventricle. Pick it if the question asked which chamber receives the pulmonary veins.","The right atrium is a thin-walled receiving chamber for blood from the body. Pick it if the question asked which chamber receives the venae cavae.","The right ventricle is thicker than the atria but much thinner than the left ventricle, since it pumps only to the nearby lungs. Pick it if the question asked which chamber pumps to the lungs."],"lt":"t-heart","lid":"c8","comp":57,"competencyId":"cv-chambers"},{"id":"L01504","dok":1,"q":"What is the thick wall between the two ventricles called?","a":"The interventricular septum.","options":["The fibrous pericardium","The interventricular septum","The coronary sulcus","The interatrial septum"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The interventricular septum is the thick muscular wall that divides the right ventricle from the left ventricle. Inter means between, and septum means a dividing wall, so the name reads as the wall between the ventricles. It keeps oxygen-poor blood on the right separate from oxygen-rich blood on the left.","notes":["The fibrous pericardium is the tough outer sac around the whole heart. Pick it if the question asked what anchors the heart in the mediastinum.","Correct. The interventricular septum divides the two ventricles.","The coronary sulcus is a groove on the outer surface of the heart between the atria and ventricles, carrying coronary vessels. It is a surface groove, not an internal wall.","The interatrial septum is the thinner wall between the two atria, where the fossa ovalis is found. Pick it if the question asked about the wall between the atria."],"lt":"t-heart","lid":"c9","comp":57,"competencyId":"cv-chambers"},{"id":"L01505","dok":1,"q":"What is the small wrinkled pouch on each atrium called?","a":"An auricle.","options":["A cusp","A papillary muscle","An auricle","The fossa ovalis"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"An auricle is a small, wrinkled, ear-shaped pouch that projects from each atrium and slightly increases its capacity. The name comes from the Latin for little ear. On a heart model, the auricles are easy to spot as flaps on the front of each atrium, next to the base of the great arteries.","notes":["A cusp is one flap of a heart valve. Pick it if the question asked about the leaflets that close a valve opening.","A papillary muscle is a cone-shaped muscle projection inside a ventricle that anchors the chordae tendineae. Pick it if the question asked what anchors the valve cords.","Correct. An auricle is the wrinkled pouch on each atrium.","The fossa ovalis is an oval depression in the interatrial septum, the remnant of a fetal opening. It is a dip inside the heart, not a pouch on the outside."],"lt":"t-heart","lid":"c10","comp":56,"competencyId":"cv-surfaces"},{"id":"L01510","dok":1,"q":"The epicardium, the outer layer of the heart wall, is the same membrane as which layer of the pericardium?","a":"The visceral layer of the serous pericardium.","options":["The fibrous pericardium","The parietal layer of the serous pericardium","The visceral layer of the serous pericardium","The pericardial cavity"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The serous pericardium has two layers. The visceral layer lies directly on the heart's surface, and that same membrane is called the epicardium when you describe it as part of the heart wall. Visceral means on an organ and parietal means on a wall, which is a pattern that repeats for the pleura and peritoneum.","notes":["The fibrous pericardium is the tough outer sac of dense connective tissue. Pick it if the question asked what anchors the heart and prevents overfilling.","The parietal layer lines the inside of the fibrous pericardium, facing the heart across the pericardial cavity. Pick it if the question asked which serous layer lines the sac.","Correct. The epicardium and the visceral layer of the serous pericardium are the same membrane.","The pericardial cavity is the fluid-filled space between the visceral and parietal layers. It is a space, not a membrane, so pick it for a question about where pericardial fluid sits."],"lt":"t-heart","lid":"c15","comp":56,"competencyId":"cv-surfaces"},{"id":"L01526","dok":1,"q":"What is the space between the parietal and visceral layers of the serous pericardium called?","a":"The pericardial cavity.","options":["The mediastinum","The pericardial cavity","The pleural cavity","The medullary cavity"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The serous pericardium is folded into two layers, a parietal layer lining the sac and a visceral layer on the heart. The thin space between them is the pericardial cavity, and it holds a small film of serous fluid. That fluid lets the two layers slide over each other as the heart beats.","notes":["The mediastinum is the central region of the thoracic cavity that holds the heart. It contains the whole pericardium, so pick it for a question about where the heart sits.","Correct. The pericardial cavity is the space between the two serous layers.","The pleural cavity is the space between the two layers of pleura around a lung. Pick it if the question asked about the space around the lungs.","The medullary cavity is the hollow center of a long bone's shaft, filled with marrow. Pick it for a question about bone anatomy."],"lt":"t-heart","lid":"c31","comp":56,"competencyId":"cv-surfaces"},{"id":"L01529","dok":1,"q":"Which part of the pericardium is the tough outer layer of dense connective tissue that anchors the heart in the mediastinum?","a":"The fibrous pericardium.","options":["The fibrous pericardium","The endocardium","The parietal layer of the serous pericardium","The epicardium"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The fibrous pericardium is the tough, inelastic outer layer of the pericardial sac. It is made of dense connective tissue, which anchors the heart to surrounding structures in the mediastinum. Because it does not stretch, it also keeps the heart from overfilling with blood.","notes":["Correct. The fibrous pericardium is the tough outer layer that anchors the heart.","The endocardium is the smooth lining of the heart chambers. Pick it if the question asked what lines the inside of the heart.","The parietal layer of the serous pericardium is a thin membrane lining the inside of the fibrous sac. Pick it if the question asked which serous layer faces the pericardial cavity from the outside.","The epicardium is the thin serous membrane on the heart's surface, the outer layer of the heart wall. Pick it if the question asked about the outermost layer of the heart wall rather than the sac."],"lt":"t-heart","lid":"c34","comp":56,"competencyId":"cv-surfaces"},{"id":"L01531","dok":1,"q":"Which chamber forms most of the base of the heart?","a":"The left atrium.","options":["The right atrium","The left ventricle","The right ventricle","The left atrium"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The base of the heart is its broad superior and posterior surface, and it is formed mostly by the left atrium. This is where the four pulmonary veins enter, so the base is crowded with great vessels. The opposite end, the apex, is formed by the left ventricle.","notes":["The right atrium forms the right border of the heart and receives the venae cavae. Pick it if the question asked which chamber receives blood from the body.","The left ventricle forms the apex and most of the inferior, diaphragmatic surface. Pick it if the question asked which chamber forms the tip of the heart.","The right ventricle forms most of the anterior, or sternocostal, surface of the heart. Pick it if the question asked which chamber lies right behind the sternum.","Correct. The left atrium forms most of the base of the heart."],"lt":"t-heart","lid":"c36","comp":57,"competencyId":"cv-chambers"},{"id":"L01533","dok":1,"q":"Which layer of the heart wall is a thin serous membrane on the heart's outer surface, with fat and the coronary vessels running in it?","a":"The epicardium.","options":["The myocardium","The endocardium","The fibrous pericardium","The epicardium"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The epicardium is the outermost layer of the heart wall. It is a thin serous membrane, the same membrane as the visceral layer of the serous pericardium. Fat and the coronary arteries and veins run within it, which is why the surface of a real heart looks yellowish along its grooves.","notes":["The myocardium is the thick middle layer of cardiac muscle that contracts. Pick it if the question asked about the layer that pumps blood.","The endocardium is the smooth inner lining of the chambers and valves. Pick it if the question asked what lines the inside of the heart.","The fibrous pericardium is the tough outer sac around the heart, not part of the heart wall. Pick it for the layer that anchors the heart in the mediastinum.","Correct. The epicardium is the thin serous outer layer where fat and coronary vessels run."],"lt":"t-heart","lid":"c38","comp":56,"competencyId":"cv-surfaces"},{"id":"L01534","dok":2,"q":"Which chamber forms most of the anterior surface of the heart, lying just behind the sternum and ribs?","a":"The right ventricle.","options":["The left ventricle","The right atrium","The left atrium","The right ventricle"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The heart is rotated in the chest so that its right side faces forward. As a result, the right ventricle forms most of the anterior, or sternocostal, surface, lying right behind the sternum and ribs. The left ventricle sits more to the left and underneath, forming the apex and resting on the diaphragm.","notes":["The left ventricle forms the apex and most of the inferior surface on the diaphragm. Pick it if the question asked which chamber forms the tip of the heart.","The right atrium forms the right border of the heart. Pick it if the question asked which chamber receives the venae cavae.","The left atrium forms most of the base, on the posterior side of the heart. Pick it if the question asked which chamber faces back toward the vertebral column.","Correct. The right ventricle forms most of the anterior surface of the heart."],"lt":"t-heart","lid":"c39","comp":57,"competencyId":"cv-chambers"},{"id":"L01536","dok":1,"q":"After a chest injury, blood leaks into the space between the visceral and parietal layers of the serous pericardium and begins to compress the heart. Where is the blood collecting?","a":"In the pericardial cavity.","options":["In the pericardial cavity","In the mediastinum outside the pericardium","In a heart chamber","In the pleural cavity"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The space between the visceral and parietal serous layers is the pericardial cavity, which normally holds only a thin film of fluid. Because the fibrous pericardium around it does not stretch, blood collecting there has nowhere to go and squeezes the heart. This compression is called cardiac tamponade, and it limits how much the heart can fill.","notes":["Correct. Blood between the serous layers collects in the pericardial cavity.","The mediastinum is the region that contains the pericardium, but blood outside the pericardial sac would not be trapped between the serous layers. Pick it for bleeding into the central chest outside the heart's sac.","A heart chamber holds circulating blood inside the heart. Blood in a chamber is normal and does not press on the heart from outside.","The pleural cavity is the space around a lung. Blood there, called a hemothorax, compresses the lung rather than the heart."],"lt":"t-heart","lid":"c41","comp":56,"competencyId":"cv-surfaces"},{"id":"L01538","dok":1,"q":"What are the parallel muscular ridges on the inner walls of the atria and auricles called?","a":"The pectinate muscles.","options":["The pectinate muscles","The trabeculae carneae","The chordae tendineae","The papillary muscles"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The pectinate muscles are parallel ridges of muscle on the inner walls of the atria and auricles. Their name comes from the Latin for comb, because the ridges line up like the teeth of a comb. In the right atrium, a ridge called the crista terminalis separates the rough pectinate wall from the smooth posterior wall.","notes":["Correct. The pectinate muscles are the comb-like ridges inside the atria and auricles.","The trabeculae carneae are irregular muscular ridges on the inner walls of the ventricles, not the atria. Pick them if the question asked about ridges inside the ventricles.","The chordae tendineae are fibrous cords, not muscle ridges, that tie the atrioventricular valve cusps to the papillary muscles. Pick them for the cords that hold the valves.","The papillary muscles are cone-shaped projections in the ventricles that anchor the chordae tendineae. Pick them if the question asked what anchors the valve cords."],"lt":"t-heart","lid":"c43","comp":56,"competencyId":"cv-surfaces"},{"id":"L01542","dok":2,"q":"Which list gives the three layers of the heart wall in order from outermost to innermost?","a":"Epicardium, then myocardium, then endocardium.","options":["Epicardium, then myocardium, then endocardium","Endocardium, then myocardium, then epicardium","Epicardium, then endocardium, then myocardium","Myocardium, then epicardium, then endocardium"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The heart wall is built from outside in as epicardium, myocardium, and endocardium. The prefixes give the order away: epi means upon, myo means muscle, and endo means within. So the thin membrane on the surface is outside, the thick muscle is in the middle, and the smooth lining is inside.","notes":["Correct. From outside in, the layers are the epicardium, myocardium, and endocardium.","This list is the right layers in reverse, from innermost to outermost. Pick it if the question asked for the order from inside out.","This list places the endocardium in the middle, but the endocardium is the inner lining that touches the blood. The thick myocardium sits between the two thin layers.","This list puts the muscle layer on the outside. The myocardium is covered on its outer surface by the epicardium, so it cannot be outermost."],"lt":"t-heart","lid":"c47","comp":56,"competencyId":"cv-surfaces"},{"id":"L01548","dok":1,"q":"The heart sits in the mediastinum, tilted to the left. Which structure does it rest on?","a":"The diaphragm.","options":["The sternum","The diaphragm","The first rib","The liver"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The heart sits in the mediastinum between the lungs and rests on the diaphragm, the dome-shaped muscle that separates the thoracic and abdominal cavities. Its inferior surface, formed mostly by the left ventricle, is even called the diaphragmatic surface. The heart tilts so that about two thirds of its mass lies left of the midline.","notes":["The sternum lies in front of the heart, with the right ventricle just behind it. Pick the sternum if the question asked which bone lies anterior to the heart.","Correct. The heart rests on the diaphragm.","The first rib is high in the thorax near the base of the neck, well above the heart. Pick it for a question about the top of the thoracic cage.","The liver lies below the diaphragm in the abdominal cavity, under the heart but separated from it by the diaphragm. Pick it for the large organ in the upper right abdomen."],"lt":"t-heart","lid":"c1-x2","comp":8,"competencyId":"w1-mediastinum"},{"id":"L01549","dok":2,"q":"The term myocardial infarction names the heart wall layer that dies in a heart attack. Which layer is it?","a":"The myocardium.","options":["The endocardium","The myocardium","The epicardium","The fibrous pericardium"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Myocardial means relating to the myocardium, the thick middle layer of cardiac muscle that contracts. An infarction is an area of tissue that dies when its blood supply is cut off. In a heart attack a blocked coronary artery starves part of the myocardium of oxygen, so the pumping muscle itself is damaged.","notes":["The endocardium is the thin inner lining of the chambers. Pick it for inflammation of the heart lining, called endocarditis.","Correct. A myocardial infarction is death of part of the myocardium.","The epicardium is the thin outer serous layer where the coronary vessels run. The blockage may sit in a vessel on this surface, but the tissue that dies is the muscle it supplies.","The fibrous pericardium is the tough sac around the heart, not part of the heart wall. Pick it if the question asked what anchors the heart in the mediastinum."],"lt":"t-heart","lid":"c3-x2","comp":56,"competencyId":"cv-surfaces"},{"id":"L01550","dok":1,"q":"Which layer of the heart wall lines the chambers and also covers the surfaces of the heart valves?","a":"The endocardium.","options":["The epicardium","The myocardium","The visceral pericardium","The endocardium"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The endocardium is a smooth endothelium that lines every chamber and folds over the valve cusps, so the valves are covered by the same lining. It is continuous with the inner lining of the great vessels. A smooth, unbroken lining lets blood flow without catching or clotting.","notes":["The epicardium is the outer layer of the heart wall, on the heart's surface. Pick it if the question asked about the layer where the coronary vessels run.","The myocardium is the muscular middle layer that contracts. Valve cusps contain no cardiac muscle, so pick the myocardium for the pumping layer.","The visceral pericardium is the same membrane as the epicardium, on the outside of the heart. Pick it if the question asked which serous layer covers the heart's surface.","Correct. The endocardium lines the chambers and covers the valves."],"lt":"t-heart","lid":"c4-x2","comp":56,"competencyId":"cv-surfaces"},{"id":"L01557","dok":1,"q":"What is the smooth-walled posterior part of the right atrium, where the venae cavae empty, called?","a":"The sinus venarum.","options":["The aortic vestibule","The conus arteriosus","The sinus venarum","The coronary sinus"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The sinus venarum is the smooth-walled posterior part of the right atrium, where the superior and inferior venae cavae empty. Its name means the sinus of the veins. A ridge called the crista terminalis separates this smooth part from the rough, pectinate-ridged anterior wall of the atrium.","notes":["The aortic vestibule is the smooth outflow tract of the left ventricle, leading to the aortic valve. Pick it for the left ventricle's exit path.","The conus arteriosus is the smooth outflow tract of the right ventricle, leading up to the pulmonary valve. Pick it if the question asked about the smooth exit path of the right ventricle.","Correct. The sinus venarum is the smooth posterior part of the right atrium that receives the venae cavae.","The coronary sinus is a vein on the posterior heart that drains the cardiac veins into the right atrium. It is a vessel, not a region of the atrial wall."],"lt":"t-heart","lid":"c16-x2","comp":56,"competencyId":"cv-surfaces"},{"id":"L01560","dok":1,"q":"What are the irregular muscular ridges on the inner walls of the ventricles called?","a":"The trabeculae carneae.","options":["The pectinate muscles","The papillary muscles","The trabeculae carneae","The chordae tendineae"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The trabeculae carneae are the irregular, criss-crossing muscular ridges on the inner walls of both ventricles. The name means little beams of flesh, which describes their rough, beam-like look. They are different from the papillary muscles, which are larger cone-shaped projections that anchor the valve cords.","notes":["The pectinate muscles are parallel ridges found in the atria and auricles, not the ventricles. Pick them if the question asked about the ridges inside the atria.","The papillary muscles are cone-shaped projections in the ventricles that anchor the chordae tendineae. They are in the same chamber, but pick them for the structures that hold the valve cords.","Correct. The trabeculae carneae are the irregular ridges on the inner ventricle walls.","The chordae tendineae are fibrous cords, not muscle ridges, that tie the atrioventricular valve cusps to the papillary muscles. Pick them for the cords that hold the valves."],"lt":"t-heart","lid":"c19-x2","comp":56,"competencyId":"cv-surfaces"},{"id":"L01563","dok":1,"q":"Which wall separates the two atria and contains the fossa ovalis?","a":"The interatrial septum.","options":["The interventricular septum","The fibrous pericardium","The crista terminalis","The interatrial septum"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The interatrial septum is the thin wall between the right and left atria. On its right atrial side is the fossa ovalis, an oval depression that marks where the fetal foramen ovale once let blood pass between the atria. The interatrial septum is much thinner than the muscular interventricular septum below it.","notes":["The interventricular septum is the thick wall between the two ventricles. Pick it if the question asked about the wall separating the right and left ventricles.","The fibrous pericardium is the tough outer sac around the heart. Pick it for the layer that anchors the heart in the mediastinum.","The crista terminalis is a ridge inside the right atrium that separates its smooth posterior wall from its rough anterior wall. It is a ridge within one atrium, not the wall between the two.","Correct. The interatrial septum divides the atria and contains the fossa ovalis."],"lt":"t-heart","lid":"c22-x2","comp":57,"competencyId":"cv-chambers"},{"id":"L01565","dok":1,"q":"Which layer of the serous pericardium lines the inner surface of the fibrous pericardium?","a":"The parietal layer.","options":["The parietal layer","The epicardium","The endocardium","The visceral layer"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The serous pericardium has two layers. The parietal layer lines the inside of the fibrous pericardium, and the visceral layer covers the heart's surface. Parietal refers to a wall and visceral refers to an organ, and the same naming pattern is used for the pleura around the lungs and the peritoneum in the abdomen.","notes":["Correct. The parietal layer lines the inner surface of the fibrous pericardium.","The epicardium is another name for the visceral layer, on the surface of the heart. Pick it for the outermost layer of the heart wall.","The endocardium is the inner lining of the heart chambers, not part of the pericardium. Pick it if the question asked what lines the inside of the heart.","The visceral layer lies directly on the heart's surface, not on the fibrous sac. Pick it if the question asked which serous layer covers the heart."],"lt":"t-heart","lid":"c24-x2","comp":56,"competencyId":"cv-surfaces"},{"id":"L01570","dok":1,"q":"Fluid builds up in the pericardial cavity and squeezes the heart from outside, so it cannot fill properly. What is this condition called?","a":"Cardiac tamponade.","options":["Cardiac tamponade","Pleural effusion","Myocardial infarction","Mitral valve prolapse"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The pericardial cavity normally holds only a thin film of fluid. Because the fibrous pericardium around it does not stretch, extra fluid or blood in that space presses inward on the heart. This compression is cardiac tamponade, and it limits how much the chambers can fill between beats.","notes":["Correct. Fluid in the pericardial cavity squeezing the heart is cardiac tamponade.","A pleural effusion is fluid collecting in the pleural cavity around a lung. It compresses the lung rather than the heart, so pick it for fluid around a lung.","A myocardial infarction is death of heart muscle from a blocked coronary artery. Pick it for a heart attack, not for fluid around the heart.","Mitral valve prolapse is when the mitral valve cusps bulge back into the left atrium as the ventricle contracts. Pick it for a valve problem, not outside compression of the heart."],"lt":"t-heart","lid":"c29-x2","comp":56,"competencyId":"cv-surfaces"},{"id":"L01573","dok":2,"q":"The wall of the left ventricle is much thicker than the wall of the right ventricle. Why does this difference make sense?","a":"The left ventricle must push blood through the entire body, which takes far more pressure than pumping to the nearby lungs.","options":["The left ventricle holds a much larger volume of blood than the right","It must push blood through the whole body at higher pressure","The left ventricle pumps blood to the lungs, which are farther from the heart","The left ventricle carries oxygen-rich blood, which is heavier to move"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The right ventricle only has to send blood a short distance through the low-pressure pulmonary circuit. The left ventricle must drive blood through the whole systemic circuit, from the head to the toes, which requires much higher pressure. Producing that pressure takes more muscle, so the left ventricle has the thickest wall even though both ventricles pump about the same volume with each beat.","notes":["Both ventricles pump about the same volume of blood with each beat, since whatever goes through the lungs must come back to the body. The difference is pressure, not volume.","Correct. The left ventricle needs more muscle to generate the high pressure of the systemic circuit.","This reverses the two ventricles. The right ventricle pumps to the lungs, which are close to the heart, and the left ventricle pumps to the body.","Oxygen-rich blood is not meaningfully heavier than oxygen-poor blood. This is a misconception, since the wall thickness comes from the pressure each circuit requires."],"lt":"t-heart","lid":"d3-t-heart-2","comp":57,"competencyId":"cv-chambers"},{"id":"L01578","dok":2,"q":"A thin film of serous fluid fills the pericardial cavity. What does this fluid do during every heartbeat?","a":"It reduces friction so the serous layers glide over each other as the heart moves.","options":["It stores extra blood for the next beat","It supplies oxygen to the outer layers of the heart wall","It reduces friction as the serous layers slide","It keeps the heart from overfilling with blood"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The heart moves with every beat, roughly a hundred thousand times a day, and its surface would rub against the sac around it. The serous fluid in the pericardial cavity lets the visceral and parietal layers slide smoothly over each other with very little friction. The same idea applies to the pleural fluid around the lungs and the peritoneal fluid in the abdomen.","notes":["Blood for the next beat is held in the chambers, mainly the atria, not in the pericardial cavity. Blood in the pericardial cavity is a problem that compresses the heart.","Oxygen reaches the heart wall through the coronary arteries, which run in the epicardium. Pick this for a question about the coronary circulation.","Correct. The serous fluid reduces friction as the heart beats.","Keeping the heart from overfilling is a job of the fibrous pericardium, the tough inelastic outer sac. Pick this for the fibrous pericardium, not the serous fluid."],"lt":"t-heart","lid":"d3-t-heart-7","comp":56,"competencyId":"cv-surfaces"},{"id":"L01579","dok":2,"q":"In the adult heart, the septum completely separates the right side from the left side. Why is this full separation important?","a":"It keeps oxygen-poor and oxygen-rich blood from mixing, so the body receives fully oxygenated blood.","options":["It anchors the four heart valves in place","It keeps oxygen-poor and oxygen-rich blood from mixing","It mixes the two bloods so oxygen levels even out","It lets the right and left sides beat at different times"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The right side of the heart handles oxygen-poor blood on its way to the lungs, and the left side handles oxygen-rich blood on its way to the body. A complete septum keeps these two streams apart. When there is a hole in the septum, called a septal defect, the bloods can mix and the blood sent to the body carries less oxygen.","notes":["The valves are anchored by the fibrous skeleton, rings of dense connective tissue around the four valve openings. Pick this for a question about the fibrous skeleton.","Correct. Full separation keeps the two bloods apart so the body gets fully oxygenated blood.","Mixing the two bloods would lower the oxygen content of blood sent to the body, which is the problem a septal defect causes. The septum exists to prevent mixing, not allow it.","The two sides of the heart contract together, not at different times. The septum is a dividing wall, not a timing device."],"lt":"t-heart","lid":"d3-t-heart-8","comp":57,"competencyId":"cv-chambers"},{"id":"L03329","dok":1,"q":"Which description best defines the heart?","a":"A hollow, muscular organ that pumps blood through the blood vessels.","options":["A hollow muscular organ that pumps blood","A spongy organ where blood exchanges gases","A gland that releases hormones into blood","A solid organ that filters old blood cells"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The heart is a hollow, muscular organ about the size of a closed fist. Its four chambers fill with blood, and its muscular wall (the myocardium) contracts to push that blood through the vessels. It sits in the mediastinum between the lungs and drives blood through two circuits, one to the lungs and one to the body.","notes":["Correct. The heart is a hollow muscular pump for the blood.","A spongy organ where blood exchanges gases describes a lung. Pick the lungs for the organs where blood picks up O2 and releases CO2.","A gland that releases hormones into the blood is an endocrine gland, such as the thyroid. Pick that for a question about hormone-secreting organs.","A solid organ that filters old blood cells is the spleen, a lymphatic organ in the upper left abdomen. Pick the spleen for a question about removing worn out red blood cells."],"lt":"t-heart-gross-anatomy","lid":"W3-001","comp":57,"competencyId":"cv-chambers"},{"id":"L03330","dok":1,"q":"Which direction does the apex of the heart point?","a":"Down, forward, and to the left.","options":["Up, back, and to the right","Straight up toward the neck","Down, forward, and to the left","Down, back, and to the right"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The apex is the blunt, rounded tip of the heart, formed by the left ventricle. Because the heart sits tilted in the mediastinum, the apex points down, forward, and to the left, and about two thirds of the heart's mass lies to the left of the midline. This tilt is why the heartbeat is felt best on the left side of the chest.","notes":["Up, back, and to the right is roughly the opposite of the apex. The base of the heart faces up and back, so students who mix up apex and base may pick this.","The great vessels at the base point upward toward the neck, not the apex. Pick upward for the direction the aortic arch and venae cavae lead from the base.","Correct. The apex points inferiorly, anteriorly, and to the left.","The apex points down, but toward the front and the left. Choosing the right side ignores the leftward tilt of the heart."],"lt":"t-heart-gross-anatomy","lid":"W3-003","comp":57,"competencyId":"cv-chambers"},{"id":"L03331","dok":1,"q":"What is the base of the heart?","a":"The broad superior surface where the great vessels enter and leave.","options":["The pointed inferior tip of the left ventricle","The broad superior surface where great vessels attach","The anterior surface lying under the sternum","The inferior surface resting on the diaphragm"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Although the word base sounds like it should be on the bottom, the base of the heart is its broad superior surface, formed mostly by the left atrium. This is where the great vessels, such as the aorta, pulmonary trunk, and venae cavae, enter and leave the heart. The apex is the opposite end, the pointed tip at the bottom.","notes":["The pointed inferior tip of the left ventricle is the apex. Pick apex for the part of the heart that points down and to the left.","Correct. The base is the broad top surface where the great vessels attach.","The anterior surface under the sternum is the sternocostal surface, formed mostly by the right ventricle. Pick it for the surface facing the front of the chest.","The inferior surface resting on the diaphragm is the diaphragmatic surface, formed mostly by the left ventricle. Pick it for the surface touching the diaphragm."],"lt":"t-heart-gross-anatomy","lid":"W3-004","comp":57,"competencyId":"cv-chambers"},{"id":"L03336","dok":1,"q":"What double-walled sac encloses the heart?","a":"The pericardium.","options":["The pleura","The mediastinum","The pericardium","The peritoneum"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The pericardium is the double-walled sac around the heart. Its outer fibrous layer anchors the heart and prevents overfilling, and its inner serous layer forms a fluid-filled space that lets the heart beat without friction. Peri means around and cardium means heart.","notes":["The pleura is the serous membrane around each lung. Pick it for the sac that surrounds a lung in the thoracic cavity.","The mediastinum is the central region of the thoracic cavity that holds the heart, not a sac or membrane. Pick it for the space between the lungs where the heart sits.","Correct. The pericardium is the sac around the heart.","The peritoneum is the serous membrane lining the abdominopelvic cavity and covering its organs. Pick it for a membrane around the stomach or intestines."],"lt":"t-heart-gross-anatomy","lid":"W3-009","comp":56,"competencyId":"cv-surfaces"},{"id":"L03337","dok":1,"q":"What is the tough, inelastic outer layer of the pericardium called?","a":"The fibrous pericardium.","options":["The endocardium","The fibrous pericardium","The visceral layer of the serous pericardium","The parietal layer of the serous pericardium"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The fibrous pericardium is the outermost layer of the pericardial sac, made of dense connective tissue. It is tough and does not stretch much, which lets it anchor the heart in the mediastinum and keep the heart from overfilling. The thinner serous pericardium lies inside it.","notes":["The endocardium is the smooth inner lining of the heart chambers and valves. Pick it for the innermost layer of the heart wall, not part of the sac.","Correct. The fibrous pericardium is the tough outer layer.","The visceral layer of the serous pericardium lies on the heart surface and is also called the epicardium. Pick it for the membrane stuck to the heart itself.","The parietal layer of the serous pericardium is a thin serous membrane that lines the inside of the fibrous pericardium. Pick it for the layer directly beneath the fibrous sac."],"lt":"t-heart-gross-anatomy","lid":"W3-010","comp":56,"competencyId":"cv-surfaces"},{"id":"L03338","dok":1,"q":"What does the fibrous pericardium do?","a":"It anchors the heart in the mediastinum and prevents overfilling.","options":["It lines the chambers with a smooth surface","It covers the heart with a fat-filled surface","It contracts to pump blood out","It anchors the heart and prevents overfilling"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The fibrous pericardium is made of dense, inelastic connective tissue. Because it is tough and attached to surrounding structures, it holds the heart in place in the mediastinum. Because it does not stretch much, it also keeps the heart from overfilling with blood.","notes":["Lining the chambers with a smooth surface is the job of the endocardium. Pick it for the innermost layer that blood flows across.","The surface layer with fat and coronary vessels is the epicardium, the visceral layer of the serous pericardium. Pick it for the outer layer of the heart wall.","Contracting to pump blood is the job of the myocardium, the cardiac muscle layer. Pick it for the layer that generates force.","Correct. The fibrous pericardium anchors the heart and limits overfilling."],"lt":"t-heart-gross-anatomy","lid":"W3-011","comp":56,"competencyId":"cv-surfaces"},{"id":"L03339","dok":1,"q":"What are the two layers of the serous pericardium?","a":"The parietal layer and the visceral layer.","options":["The myocardium and endocardium","The fibrous and serous layers","The epicardium and endocardium","The parietal and visceral layers"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The serous pericardium is a thin double membrane. Its parietal layer lines the inside of the fibrous pericardium, and its visceral layer lies directly on the heart surface, where it is also called the epicardium. The pericardial cavity, a thin space filled with serous fluid, lies between these two layers.","notes":["The myocardium and endocardium are the middle and inner layers of the heart wall. Pick them for the muscle layer and lining of the heart itself.","Fibrous and serous are the two main parts of the whole pericardium, not the two layers of the serous part. Pick this pair if the question asks for the two parts of the pericardial sac.","The epicardium is the visceral layer, but the endocardium lines the chambers and is not part of the pericardium. This choice mixes one correct layer with an unrelated one.","Correct. The serous pericardium has a parietal layer and a visceral layer."],"lt":"t-heart-gross-anatomy","lid":"W3-012","comp":56,"competencyId":"cv-surfaces"},{"id":"L03340","dok":1,"q":"Which layer of the serous pericardium lies directly on the heart surface?","a":"The visceral layer.","options":["The visceral layer","The endocardial layer","The fibrous layer","The parietal layer"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"In any serous membrane, the visceral layer covers the organ itself and the parietal layer lines the cavity wall. The visceral layer of the serous pericardium clings to the heart surface and is the same membrane as the epicardium, the outer layer of the heart wall. Viscus means organ, which is a useful way to remember it.","notes":["Correct. The visceral layer lies on the heart and is also called the epicardium.","The endocardium lines the inside of the heart chambers, not the outer surface. Pick it for the innermost layer of the heart wall.","The fibrous pericardium is the tough outer sac and is not part of the serous pericardium. Pick it for the layer that anchors the heart.","The parietal layer lines the inside of the fibrous pericardium, away from the heart surface. Pick it for the serous layer on the sac side of the pericardial cavity."],"lt":"t-heart-gross-anatomy","lid":"W3-013","comp":56,"competencyId":"cv-surfaces"},{"id":"L03341","dok":1,"q":"Which layer of the serous pericardium lines the inside of the fibrous pericardium?","a":"The parietal layer.","options":["The epicardium","The visceral layer","The myocardium","The parietal layer"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The parietal layer of the serous pericardium is fused to the inner surface of the fibrous pericardium. Parietal means wall, so this layer lines the wall of the sac. It faces the visceral layer across the pericardial cavity.","notes":["The epicardium is another name for the visceral layer, which sits on the heart surface. Pick it for the outer layer of the heart wall.","The visceral layer lies on the heart surface, on the opposite side of the pericardial cavity. Pick it for the layer that clings to the heart.","The myocardium is the cardiac muscle layer of the heart wall, deep to the epicardium. Pick it for the layer that contracts.","Correct. The parietal layer lines the fibrous pericardium."],"lt":"t-heart-gross-anatomy","lid":"W3-014","comp":56,"competencyId":"cv-surfaces"},{"id":"L03342","dok":1,"q":"Where is the pericardial cavity?","a":"Between the parietal and visceral layers of the serous pericardium.","options":["Between the parietal and visceral serous layers","Between the two pleural layers of each lung","Between the myocardium and the endocardium","Inside the four chambers of the heart"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The pericardial cavity is a thin space between the parietal layer, which lines the fibrous sac, and the visceral layer, which covers the heart. It holds a film of serous fluid that lets the two layers slide over each other as the heart beats. It is a potential space, so normally it holds only a small amount of fluid.","notes":["Correct. The pericardial cavity lies between the parietal and visceral layers.","The space between the two pleural layers is the pleural cavity around a lung. Pick it for the fluid-filled space surrounding each lung.","The myocardium and endocardium are layers of the heart wall that are attached to each other with no cavity between. Pick them for the middle and inner layers of the wall.","The chambers are the hollow interior of the heart, where blood flows. They are separated from the pericardial cavity by the entire heart wall."],"lt":"t-heart-gross-anatomy","lid":"W3-015","comp":56,"competencyId":"cv-surfaces"},{"id":"L03343","dok":1,"q":"What fluid reduces friction as the heart beats?","a":"Serous fluid in the pericardial cavity.","options":["Cerebrospinal fluid","Synovial fluid","Lymph","Serous fluid"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The serous layers of the pericardium secrete a thin film of serous fluid into the pericardial cavity. This fluid lets the visceral layer on the heart slide smoothly against the parietal layer each time the heart contracts and relaxes. The same kind of fluid reduces friction around the lungs and abdominal organs.","notes":["Cerebrospinal fluid surrounds and cushions the brain and spinal cord. Pick it for the fluid in the subarachnoid space and ventricles.","Synovial fluid lubricates freely movable joints such as the knee and shoulder. Pick it for the fluid inside a joint cavity.","Lymph is the fluid carried in lymphatic vessels back to the veins. Pick it for fluid collected from the tissues.","Correct. Serous fluid in the pericardial cavity reduces friction."],"lt":"t-heart-gross-anatomy","lid":"W3-016","comp":56,"competencyId":"cv-surfaces"},{"id":"L03344","dok":2,"q":"Which list gives the three layers of the heart wall in order from outside to inside?","a":"Epicardium, myocardium, endocardium.","options":["Epicardium, endocardium, myocardium","Epicardium, myocardium, endocardium","Myocardium, epicardium, endocardium","Endocardium, myocardium, epicardium"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The epicardium (epi means upon) is the thin outer layer on the heart surface. The myocardium (myo means muscle) is the thick middle layer of cardiac muscle that contracts. The endocardium (endo means within) is the smooth inner lining of the chambers and valves.","notes":["The endocardium is the innermost layer and cannot sit between the other two. This order puts the thick muscle layer on the inside where the lining should be.","Correct. Epicardium, then myocardium, then endocardium.","The myocardium is the middle layer, not the outer one. Students who think of the muscle as the main part of the heart may put it first.","This list runs from inside to outside, the reverse of what was asked. It would be correct if the question asked for the order from the chambers outward."],"lt":"t-heart-gross-anatomy","lid":"W3-017","comp":56,"competencyId":"cv-surfaces"},{"id":"L03345","dok":1,"q":"What is the outer layer of the heart wall?","a":"The epicardium.","options":["The fibrous pericardium","The endocardium","The epicardium","The myocardium"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The epicardium is the thin serous membrane on the outer surface of the heart wall. It is the same membrane as the visceral layer of the serous pericardium, and fat and the coronary vessels run within it. The fibrous pericardium lies outside it but belongs to the sac, not the heart wall.","notes":["The fibrous pericardium is the outer layer of the sac around the heart, not part of the heart wall. Pick it for the tough layer anchoring the heart in the mediastinum.","The endocardium is the inner lining of the chambers and valves. Pick it for the innermost layer.","Correct. The epicardium is the outer layer of the heart wall.","The myocardium is the thick middle muscle layer. Pick it for the layer that contracts."],"lt":"t-heart-gross-anatomy","lid":"W3-018","comp":56,"competencyId":"cv-surfaces"},{"id":"L03346","dok":1,"q":"What is the thick middle layer of the heart wall?","a":"The myocardium.","options":["The epicardium","The myocardium","The endocardium","The pericardium"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The myocardium is the thick middle layer of the heart wall, built of cardiac muscle in spiraling bundles. It makes up about ninety five percent of the wall thickness and is the layer that contracts to pump blood. Its thickness varies by chamber, and it is thickest in the left ventricle.","notes":["The epicardium is the thin outer layer on the heart surface. Pick it for the layer carrying the coronary vessels and fat.","Correct. The myocardium is the thick middle muscle layer.","The endocardium is the thin inner lining of the chambers. Pick it for the smooth surface that blood touches.","The pericardium is the sac around the heart, outside the heart wall. Pick it for the double-walled membrane that encloses the heart."],"lt":"t-heart-gross-anatomy","lid":"W3-019","comp":56,"competencyId":"cv-surfaces"},{"id":"L03347","dok":1,"q":"What is the inner layer of the heart wall?","a":"The endocardium.","options":["The endocardium","The visceral pericardium","The myocardium","The epicardium"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The endocardium is a smooth endothelium (a thin sheet of flat cells) that lines the inside of all four chambers and covers the valves. It is continuous with the inner lining of the great vessels. Its smoothness lets blood flow across it without catching.","notes":["Correct. The endocardium is the innermost layer of the heart wall.","The visceral pericardium is another name for the epicardium, the outer layer. Students sometimes pick it because visceral sounds like it means inner.","The myocardium is the thick middle layer of cardiac muscle. Pick it for the contracting layer.","The epicardium is the outer layer on the heart surface. Pick it for the outermost layer of the wall."],"lt":"t-heart-gross-anatomy","lid":"W3-020","comp":56,"competencyId":"cv-surfaces"},{"id":"L03348","dok":1,"q":"Which layer of the heart wall is made of cardiac muscle?","a":"The myocardium.","options":["The fibrous pericardium","The myocardium","The endocardium","The epicardium"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The myocardium is the layer of cardiac muscle in the heart wall. Its cells are short, branched, striated, and joined end to end by intercalated discs, which let the whole heart contract as a coordinated unit. The other layers are thin membranes or connective tissue.","notes":["The fibrous pericardium is dense connective tissue forming the outer sac. Pick it for the tough layer that anchors the heart.","Correct. The myocardium is the cardiac muscle layer.","The endocardium is a thin endothelium lining the chambers and valves. Pick it for the smooth inner surface, not the muscle.","The epicardium is a thin serous membrane on the heart surface. Pick it for the outer layer where the coronary vessels run."],"lt":"t-heart-gross-anatomy","lid":"W3-021","comp":56,"competencyId":"cv-surfaces"},{"id":"L03349","dok":1,"q":"Which layer of the heart wall lines the chambers?","a":"The endocardium.","options":["The epicardium","The parietal pericardium","The endocardium","The myocardium"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The endocardium lines the inner surface of all four chambers. It is a smooth endothelium resting on a thin layer of connective tissue, and it gives blood a slick surface to flow across. It also covers the valves and continues into the lining of the great vessels.","notes":["The epicardium covers the outer surface of the heart. Pick it for the outermost layer of the wall.","The parietal pericardium lines the fibrous sac outside the heart. Pick it for the serous layer facing the pericardial cavity from the sac side.","Correct. The endocardium lines the chambers.","The myocardium lies just outside the endocardium and forms the muscle of the chamber walls. Pick it for the layer that contracts."],"lt":"t-heart-gross-anatomy","lid":"W3-022","comp":56,"competencyId":"cv-surfaces"},{"id":"L03350","dok":1,"q":"Which layer of the heart wall covers the surfaces of the valves?","a":"The endocardium.","options":["The myocardium","The fibrous skeleton","The endocardium","The epicardium"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The valve cusps are folds of tissue covered on both surfaces by endocardium, the same smooth lining that covers the chambers. This smooth covering lets blood pass over the valves without catching. Because the valves are covered by endocardium, infection of the valves is a form of endocarditis.","notes":["The myocardium is muscle and does not extend into the valve cusps. Valves open and close passively, so pick the myocardium only for the chamber walls that contract.","The fibrous skeleton is the set of dense connective tissue rings around the valve openings. It anchors the valves but does not cover their surfaces, so pick it for what holds the valves in place.","Correct. The endocardium covers the valves as well as lining the chambers.","The epicardium covers the outer surface of the heart, far from the valves inside. Pick it for the outer layer of the wall."],"lt":"t-heart-gross-anatomy","lid":"W3-023","comp":56,"competencyId":"cv-surfaces"},{"id":"L03351","dok":1,"q":"The epicardium is the visceral layer of which membrane?","a":"The serous pericardium.","options":["The serous pericardium","The peritoneum","The visceral pleura","The fibrous pericardium"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The epicardium and the visceral layer of the serous pericardium are the same membrane under two names. You call it the epicardium when describing the heart wall and the visceral serous pericardium when describing the sac. It faces the parietal layer across the pericardial cavity.","notes":["Correct. The epicardium is the visceral layer of the serous pericardium.","The peritoneum is the serous membrane of the abdominopelvic cavity. Its visceral layer covers abdominal organs such as the stomach, not the heart.","The visceral pleura covers the surface of a lung, not the heart. Pick it for the membrane stuck to the lung surface.","The fibrous pericardium is a single tough layer of dense connective tissue with no visceral layer. Pick it for the outer sac that anchors the heart."],"lt":"t-heart-gross-anatomy","lid":"W3-024","comp":56,"competencyId":"cv-surfaces"},{"id":"L03352","dok":1,"q":"Which two chambers are the receiving chambers of the heart?","a":"The right atrium and the left atrium.","options":["The right atrium and right ventricle","The left atrium and left ventricle","The right and left atria","The right and left ventricles"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The two atria are the receiving chambers. The right atrium receives oxygen-poor blood returning from the body, and the left atrium receives oxygen-rich blood returning from the lungs. Their walls are thin because they only push blood a short distance into the ventricles below.","notes":["These two chambers make up the right side of the heart, one receiving and one pumping. Pick this pair for the side that drives the pulmonary circuit.","These two chambers make up the left side of the heart, one receiving and one pumping. Pick this pair for the side that drives the systemic circuit.","Correct. The two atria receive blood returning to the heart.","The ventricles are the pumping chambers that send blood out of the heart. Pick them for the chambers that push blood into the pulmonary trunk and aorta."],"lt":"t-heart-gross-anatomy","lid":"W3-026","comp":57,"competencyId":"cv-chambers"},{"id":"L03353","dok":1,"q":"Which two chambers are the pumping chambers of the heart?","a":"The right ventricle and the left ventricle.","options":["The right and left atria","The right and left ventricles","The right atrium and right ventricle","The left atrium and left ventricle"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The two ventricles are the pumping chambers that send blood out of the heart. The right ventricle pumps into the pulmonary trunk toward the lungs, and the left ventricle pumps into the aorta toward the body. Their walls are much thicker than the atrial walls because they must push blood through an entire circuit.","notes":["The atria are the receiving chambers. Pick them for the chambers that collect blood returning from the body and lungs.","Correct. The ventricles pump blood out of the heart.","This pair is the whole right side of the heart. Pick it for the side that drives the pulmonary circuit.","This pair is the whole left side of the heart. Pick it for the side that drives the systemic circuit."],"lt":"t-heart-gross-anatomy","lid":"W3-027","comp":57,"competencyId":"cv-chambers"},{"id":"L03358","dok":1,"q":"Which chamber of the heart has the thickest wall?","a":"The left ventricle.","options":["The left ventricle","The left atrium","The right atrium","The right ventricle"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The left ventricle has the thickest myocardium of the four chambers because it pumps blood through the systemic circuit to the entire body. The right ventricle only pumps to the nearby lungs, so its wall is thinner. The atria have the thinnest walls because they push blood only into the ventricles below.","notes":["Correct. The left ventricle has the thickest wall.","The left atrium is a thin-walled receiving chamber. Pick it for the chamber receiving blood from the pulmonary veins.","The right atrium is a thin-walled receiving chamber. Pick it for the chamber where the venae cavae and coronary sinus empty.","The right ventricle has a thicker wall than either atrium but a much thinner wall than the left ventricle. Pick it for the chamber that pumps to the lungs."],"lt":"t-heart-gross-anatomy","lid":"W3-032","comp":57,"competencyId":"cv-chambers"},{"id":"L03364","dok":1,"q":"Which wall separates the two atria?","a":"The interatrial septum.","options":["The coronary sulcus","The interatrial septum","The interventricular septum","The fibrous pericardium"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The interatrial septum is the thin wall between the right and left atria. Inter means between, so the name says it sits between the atria. It holds the fossa ovalis, the remnant of the fetal opening that once let blood pass from the right atrium to the left.","notes":["The coronary sulcus is a groove on the outside of the heart that marks the boundary between the atria and the ventricles. Pick it for the external groove where the coronary sinus runs.","Correct. The interatrial septum separates the two atria.","The interventricular septum is the thick wall between the two ventricles. Pick it for the wall separating the pumping chambers.","The fibrous pericardium is the outer sac around the heart, not a wall inside it. Pick it for the tough layer anchoring the heart in the mediastinum."],"lt":"t-heart-gross-anatomy","lid":"W3-038","comp":57,"competencyId":"cv-chambers"},{"id":"L03365","dok":1,"q":"Which wall separates the two ventricles?","a":"The interventricular septum.","options":["The coronary sulcus","The interatrial septum","The moderator band","The interventricular septum"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The interventricular septum is the thick muscular wall between the right and left ventricles. It keeps oxygen-poor blood on the right side from mixing with oxygen-rich blood on the left. On the surface of the heart, its position is marked by the anterior and posterior interventricular grooves.","notes":["The coronary sulcus is an external groove marking the boundary between the atria and the ventricles. Pick it for the groove that encircles the heart.","The interatrial septum is the thin wall between the two atria. Pick it for the wall that holds the fossa ovalis.","The moderator band is a muscular band that crosses the right ventricle from the septum to the anterior papillary muscle. It attaches to the septum but does not separate the chambers.","Correct. The interventricular septum separates the ventricles."],"lt":"t-heart-gross-anatomy","lid":"W3-039","comp":57,"competencyId":"cv-chambers"},{"id":"L03366","dok":1,"q":"What is the fossa ovalis?","a":"An oval depression in the interatrial septum.","options":["An oval depression in the interatrial septum","A small wrinkled pouch on each atrium","A smooth outflow tract leading to the pulmonary valve","A muscular band crossing the right ventricle"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The fossa ovalis is a shallow oval depression in the interatrial septum, seen from inside the right atrium. Fossa means a shallow pit. It marks the site of the foramen ovale, an opening that let blood pass between the atria before birth and then sealed shut.","notes":["Correct. The fossa ovalis is an oval depression in the interatrial septum.","A small wrinkled pouch on each atrium is an auricle. Pick it for the ear-shaped flap that slightly increases atrial capacity.","A smooth outflow tract leading to the pulmonary valve is the conus arteriosus of the right ventricle. Pick it for the smooth funnel below the pulmonary trunk.","A muscular band crossing the right ventricle is the moderator band. Pick it for the structure carrying conduction fibers to the anterior papillary muscle."],"lt":"t-heart-gross-anatomy","lid":"W3-040","comp":56,"competencyId":"cv-surfaces"},{"id":"L03368","dok":1,"q":"What is the small, wrinkled, ear-shaped pouch that projects from each atrium?","a":"An auricle.","options":["A fossa ovalis","A papillary muscle","A coronary sinus","An auricle"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Each atrium has an auricle, a small wrinkled pouch named for its resemblance to an ear (auricle means little ear). The auricles are visible on the anterior surface of the heart beside the great vessels. Their inner walls are lined with pectinate muscles, and they slightly increase how much blood each atrium can hold.","notes":["The fossa ovalis is an oval depression in the interatrial septum, seen inside the right atrium. Pick it for the remnant of the foramen ovale.","A papillary muscle is a cone-shaped projection of the ventricle wall that anchors the chordae tendineae. Pick it for the muscle that holds the AV valves shut.","The coronary sinus is a large vein on the posterior heart that drains into the right atrium. Pick it for the vessel collecting the cardiac veins.","Correct. The auricle is the ear-shaped pouch on each atrium."],"lt":"t-heart-gross-anatomy","lid":"W3-042","comp":56,"competencyId":"cv-surfaces"},{"id":"L03369","dok":1,"q":"What do the auricles do?","a":"They slightly increase the capacity of the atria.","options":["Anchor the chordae tendineae","Separate the atria from the ventricles","Drain blood from the heart wall","Slightly increase atrial capacity"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The auricles are small pouches that extend from the atria. They add a little extra room to each atrium, so the atria can hold slightly more blood before it passes into the ventricles. Their inner walls have the same parallel pectinate muscles found in the atria.","notes":["Anchoring the chordae tendineae is the job of the papillary muscles in the ventricles. Pick papillary muscles for that role.","Separating the atria from the ventricles is the job of the fibrous skeleton and the AV valves. Pick the fibrous skeleton for the connective tissue that insulates atria from ventricles.","Draining blood from the heart wall is the job of the cardiac veins and coronary sinus. Pick the coronary sinus for the vessel emptying that blood into the right atrium.","Correct. The auricles slightly increase the capacity of the atria."],"lt":"t-heart-gross-anatomy","lid":"W3-043","comp":56,"competencyId":"cv-surfaces"},{"id":"L03370","dok":1,"q":"Which structures are parallel muscular ridges on the inner walls of the atria and auricles?","a":"Pectinate muscles.","options":["Papillary muscles","Chordae tendineae","Pectinate muscles","Trabeculae carneae"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Pectinate muscles are parallel ridges of muscle on the inner walls of the atria and auricles. Pectinate means comb-like, because the ridges line up like the teeth of a comb. In the right atrium, a ridge called the crista terminalis separates the rough pectinate wall from the smooth posterior wall.","notes":["Papillary muscles are cone-shaped projections in the ventricles that anchor the chordae tendineae. Pick them for the muscles holding the AV valves shut.","Chordae tendineae are fibrous cords, not muscle ridges, linking AV valve cusps to papillary muscles. Pick them for the heart strings attached to the valves.","Correct. Pectinate muscles are the comb-like ridges in the atria and auricles.","Trabeculae carneae are irregular muscular ridges on the inner walls of the ventricles, not the atria. Pick them for the rough ridges inside a ventricle."],"lt":"t-heart-gross-anatomy","lid":"W3-044","comp":56,"competencyId":"cv-surfaces"},{"id":"L03371","dok":1,"q":"Which structures are irregular muscular ridges on the inner walls of the ventricles?","a":"Trabeculae carneae.","options":["Pectinate muscles","Chordae tendineae","Crista terminalis","Trabeculae carneae"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Trabeculae carneae are irregular, crisscrossing ridges of muscle on the inner walls of both ventricles. The name means little beams of flesh. They give the ventricle walls a rough inner texture, unlike the parallel comb-like pectinate muscles of the atria.","notes":["Pectinate muscles are parallel ridges in the atria and auricles. Pick them for the comb-like ridges of the receiving chambers.","Chordae tendineae are fibrous cords, not muscle ridges, that tie AV valve cusps to papillary muscles. Pick them for the heart strings.","The crista terminalis is a single ridge in the right atrium separating its smooth and rough walls. Pick it for that one atrial landmark.","Correct. Trabeculae carneae are the irregular ridges inside the ventricles."],"lt":"t-heart-gross-anatomy","lid":"W3-045","comp":56,"competencyId":"cv-surfaces"},{"id":"L03372","dok":1,"q":"Which structure is a muscular band that crosses the right ventricle from the interventricular septum to the base of the anterior papillary muscle?","a":"The moderator band.","options":["The moderator band","The chordae tendineae","The crista terminalis","The pectinate muscles"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The moderator band, also called the septomarginal trabecula, is a muscular band found in the right ventricle. It runs from the interventricular septum across the chamber to the base of the anterior papillary muscle. It carries part of the conduction system, which helps the papillary muscle contract on time.","notes":["Correct. The moderator band crosses the right ventricle to the anterior papillary muscle.","Chordae tendineae are thin fibrous cords running from papillary muscles to the AV valve cusps. Pick them for the cords attached to the valves, not a muscle band from the septum.","The crista terminalis is a ridge in the right atrium, not the ventricle. Pick it for the ridge separating the smooth and rough parts of the right atrial wall.","Pectinate muscles are parallel ridges in the atria and auricles. Pick them for the comb-like ridges of the atrial walls."],"lt":"t-heart-gross-anatomy","lid":"W3-046","comp":56,"competencyId":"cv-surfaces"},{"id":"L03374","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares the atria and the ventricles?","a":"Atria receive blood returning to the heart, and ventricles pump blood out of the heart.","options":["Atria and ventricles both pump blood out of the heart","Atria pump blood out, and ventricles receive returning blood","Atria have thicker walls because they hold more blood","Atria receive returning blood, and ventricles pump it out"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The atria are the receiving chambers: blood returning from the body or lungs enters them first. The ventricles are the pumping chambers that push blood out into the pulmonary trunk and aorta. This difference in job explains why the ventricles have much thicker walls than the atria.","notes":["Only the ventricles push blood out of the heart into the great arteries. The atria push blood only a short distance into the ventricles.","This reverses the two roles. Blood enters the atria from veins and leaves the ventricles through arteries, so pick this only if you have the chambers flipped.","Atria have thinner walls, not thicker, because they do little pumping. Pick the ventricles, especially the left, for the thickest walls.","Correct. Atria receive blood, and ventricles pump it out of the heart."],"lt":"t-heart-gross-anatomy","lid":"W3-048","comp":57,"competencyId":"cv-chambers"},{"id":"L03376","dok":2,"q":"Why is the wall of the left ventricle thicker than the wall of the right ventricle?","a":"It pumps blood through the systemic circuit to the whole body.","options":["It receives more blood than the right ventricle","It pumps blood through the systemic circuit to the whole body","It must push blood up into the left atrium","It holds the coronary sinus within its wall"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The left ventricle pumps blood into the aorta and through the systemic circuit, which reaches every tissue of the body. Pushing blood through such a long, branching circuit takes much more force than pushing it to the nearby lungs. Thicker myocardium gives the left ventricle that extra force.","notes":["Both ventricles pump about the same volume of blood with each beat. The difference is how hard each must push, not how much blood each receives.","Correct. The long systemic circuit requires a thicker, stronger wall.","Blood moves from the left atrium down into the left ventricle, not the other way. Pushing blood back into an atrium would be backflow, which the mitral valve prevents.","The coronary sinus lies in the groove on the posterior surface of the heart and empties into the right atrium. It does not explain wall thickness."],"lt":"t-heart-gross-anatomy","lid":"W3-050","comp":57,"competencyId":"cv-chambers"},{"id":"L03377","dok":2,"q":"Why can the right ventricle have a thinner wall than the left ventricle?","a":"It only pumps blood to the nearby lungs.","options":["It receives oxygen-rich blood that is easier to move","It pumps less blood with each beat","Its valves do most of the pumping work","It only pumps blood to the nearby lungs"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The right ventricle drives the pulmonary circuit, a short loop to the lungs right beside the heart. Moving blood through this short circuit takes less force than pushing it through the whole body. So the right ventricle needs a thinner wall, and in cross section it wraps around the thicker left ventricle in a crescent shape.","notes":["The right ventricle carries oxygen-poor blood, not oxygen-rich. Oxygen content also does not change how hard blood is to move.","Both ventricles eject about the same volume with each beat. Choosing this confuses volume with the force needed to move it.","Valves only open and close to keep flow one-way. They do no pumping, so the myocardium does all the work.","Correct. The short pulmonary circuit needs less force."],"lt":"t-heart-gross-anatomy","lid":"W3-051","comp":57,"competencyId":"cv-chambers"},{"id":"L03378","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares the apex and the base of the heart?","a":"The apex is the inferior tip, and the base is the superior surface where the great vessels attach.","options":["The apex and the base are both formed by the right ventricle","The apex holds the great vessels, and the base rests on the diaphragm","The apex is the inferior tip, and the base is the superior surface","The apex is the superior surface, and the base is the inferior tip"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The apex is the pointed inferior tip of the heart, formed by the left ventricle and pointing down, forward, and to the left. The base is the broad superior surface, formed mostly by the left atrium, where the great vessels enter and leave. The names are opposite to what many students expect, since the base sits on top.","notes":["The apex is formed by the left ventricle, and the base mostly by the left atrium. The right ventricle forms most of the anterior surface instead.","The great vessels attach at the base, not the apex. The surface resting on the diaphragm is the inferior surface, formed mostly by the left ventricle.","Correct. The apex is the inferior tip and the base is the superior surface.","This reverses the two. Students often assume the base must be at the bottom, but in the heart it is the top surface."],"lt":"t-heart-gross-anatomy","lid":"W3-052","comp":57,"competencyId":"cv-chambers"},{"id":"L03382","dok":2,"q":"What is the main job of the fluid-filled pericardial cavity?","a":"It lets the heart beat with very little friction.","options":["It stores extra blood for the ventricles","It supplies oxygen to the heart wall","It lets the heart beat with little friction","It anchors the heart to the diaphragm"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The pericardial cavity holds a thin film of serous fluid between the parietal and visceral layers of the serous pericardium. As the heart contracts and relaxes, the two layers slide past each other on this fluid with very little friction. Without it, the beating heart would rub against its sac.","notes":["The pericardial cavity holds only a thin film of fluid, never blood. Blood is stored in the chambers, and fluid or blood filling this space is a dangerous condition called cardiac tamponade.","Oxygen reaches the heart wall through the coronary arteries. Pick them for the heart's own blood supply.","Correct. The serous fluid lets the layers glide as the heart beats.","Anchoring the heart is the job of the fibrous pericardium, which attaches to the diaphragm and surrounding structures. Pick it for holding the heart in place."],"lt":"t-heart-gross-anatomy","lid":"W3-056","comp":56,"competencyId":"cv-surfaces"},{"id":"L03383","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares the fibrous and serous pericardium?","a":"The fibrous pericardium anchors and protects; the serous pericardium forms friction-reducing membranes.","options":["Both are muscular layers that help the heart contract","The fibrous layer reduces friction, and the serous layer anchors the heart","The fibrous layer lines the chambers, and the serous layer forms the valves","The fibrous layer anchors the heart, and the serous layer reduces friction"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The fibrous pericardium is a tough sac of dense connective tissue that anchors the heart in the mediastinum and prevents overfilling. The serous pericardium is a thin double membrane inside it, whose parietal and visceral layers enclose the fluid-filled pericardial cavity. That fluid lets the heart beat with little friction.","notes":["Neither layer of the pericardium is muscle. The cardiac muscle is the myocardium, the middle layer of the heart wall.","This swaps the two roles. The tough dense connective tissue is the anchor, and the thin slippery membranes reduce friction.","Lining the chambers and covering the valves is the job of the endocardium. The pericardium is entirely outside the heart wall.","Correct. Fibrous anchors and protects, and serous reduces friction."],"lt":"t-heart-gross-anatomy","lid":"W3-057","comp":56,"competencyId":"cv-surfaces"},{"id":"L03384","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares the epicardium and the endocardium?","a":"The epicardium covers the outside of the heart; the endocardium lines the chambers and covers the valves.","options":["The epicardium covers the outside, and the endocardium lines the chambers","The epicardium lines the chambers, and the endocardium covers the outside","Both are made of cardiac muscle that contracts","Both are layers of the fibrous pericardium"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Epi means upon and endo means within. The epicardium is the thin serous membrane on the outer surface of the heart, the same as the visceral layer of the serous pericardium. The endocardium is the smooth endothelium lining the chambers and covering the valves.","notes":["Correct. Epicardium outside, endocardium inside.","This swaps the two. Use the prefixes: epi is on the outside, endo is on the inside.","Only the myocardium is cardiac muscle. The epicardium is a serous membrane and the endocardium is an endothelium.","The fibrous pericardium is a single tough layer of the sac. The epicardium is the visceral layer of the serous pericardium, and the endocardium is not part of the pericardium at all."],"lt":"t-heart-gross-anatomy","lid":"W3-058","comp":56,"competencyId":"cv-surfaces"},{"id":"L03385","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares the myocardium and the endocardium?","a":"The myocardium contracts to pump blood; the endocardium provides a smooth inner lining.","options":["The myocardium forms a smooth lining, and the endocardium contracts","The myocardium covers the valves, and the endocardium anchors them","Both reduce friction between the heart and its sac","The myocardium contracts, and the endocardium forms a smooth lining"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The myocardium is the thick middle layer of cardiac muscle, and it does the contracting that pumps blood. The endocardium is a thin, smooth endothelium covering the inner surface of the myocardium, lining the chambers and covering the valves. One layer generates force and the other gives blood a smooth surface to flow across.","notes":["This swaps the two roles. The thick muscle layer contracts, and the thin endothelium lines it.","The endocardium covers the valves, and the fibrous skeleton anchors them. The myocardium does neither, since valves have no muscle.","Reducing friction between the heart and its sac is the job of the serous pericardium and its fluid, outside the heart wall.","Correct. Myocardium contracts, and endocardium lines."],"lt":"t-heart-gross-anatomy","lid":"W3-059","comp":56,"competencyId":"cv-surfaces"},{"id":"L03386","dok":2,"q":"Why does it matter that the endocardium is smooth?","a":"It gives blood a smooth surface to flow across in the chambers and over the valves.","options":["It anchors the heart in the mediastinum","It gives blood a smooth surface to flow across","It lets the heart slide inside its sac","It lets the heart wall contract with force"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The endocardium is a smooth endothelium that lines every chamber, covers the valves, and continues into the lining of the great vessels. Because it is smooth, blood flows across it without catching or clotting. When the endocardium is damaged, for example by infection, the roughened surface disturbs flow and can harm the valves.","notes":["Anchoring the heart is the job of the fibrous pericardium. Pick it for the tough sac holding the heart in place.","Correct. A smooth lining lets blood flow freely through the chambers and over the valves.","Sliding within the sac depends on the serous pericardium and its fluid, on the outside of the heart. Pick the serous pericardium for that job.","Contraction is the job of the myocardium. Pick it for the layer that generates force."],"lt":"t-heart-gross-anatomy","lid":"W3-060","comp":56,"competencyId":"cv-surfaces"},{"id":"L03387","dok":2,"q":"A patient's heart pumps weakly because its wall cannot contract with normal force. Which layer of the heart wall is most likely damaged?","a":"The myocardium.","options":["The myocardium","The endocardium","The fibrous pericardium","The epicardium"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The myocardium is the cardiac muscle layer, making up about ninety five percent of the wall thickness. It is the only layer that contracts, so damage to it directly weakens pumping. A heart attack, for example, kills a patch of myocardium when its coronary blood supply is blocked.","notes":["Correct. The myocardium is the contracting layer.","The endocardium is a thin lining of the chambers and valves. Damage there can affect the valves, but it does not supply contractile force.","The fibrous pericardium is the outer sac, not part of the wall. Pick it for the layer that anchors the heart and limits overfilling.","The epicardium is a thin serous membrane on the heart surface with no contractile tissue. Pick it for the layer where the coronary vessels run."],"lt":"t-heart-gross-anatomy","lid":"W3-061","comp":56,"competencyId":"cv-surfaces"},{"id":"L03388","dok":1,"q":"Which layer of the heart wall is continuous with the inner lining of the great vessels?","a":"The endocardium.","options":["The myocardium","The parietal pericardium","The endocardium","The epicardium"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The endocardium is an endothelium, a sheet of flat cells, and it continues without a break into the endothelium lining the great vessels. This gives blood one unbroken smooth surface from the vessels, through the chambers, and back out. The innermost layer of a blood vessel wall, the tunica intima, uses this same kind of lining.","notes":["The myocardium is cardiac muscle that stops at the bases of the great vessels. Vessel walls contain smooth muscle in their tunica media instead.","The parietal pericardium lines the fibrous sac, outside the heart. Pick it for the serous layer facing the pericardial cavity from the sac side.","Correct. The endocardium continues into the lining of the great vessels.","The epicardium covers the outer surface of the heart. It does reflect onto the roots of the great vessels as part of the serous pericardium, but it does not line their inside."],"lt":"t-heart-gross-anatomy","lid":"W3-062","comp":56,"competencyId":"cv-surfaces"},{"id":"L03389","dok":2,"q":"Why are the walls of the atria thinner than the walls of the ventricles?","a":"The atria receive blood and only push it a short distance into the ventricles.","options":["The atria pump blood out to the entire body","The atria are made of smooth muscle instead of cardiac muscle","The atria carry only oxygen-poor blood","The atria only push blood a short distance into the ventricles"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The atria are receiving chambers. Most blood flows from the atria into the ventricles on its own, and atrial contraction only tops off the ventricles a short distance below. Because so little force is needed, the atria have thin walls, while the ventricles need thick walls to push blood out through the circuits.","notes":["Pumping blood to the body is the job of the left ventricle, the thickest chamber. Pick it for the systemic pump.","The atria are made of cardiac muscle, the same tissue as the ventricles, just in a thinner layer. Smooth muscle is found in vessel walls and hollow organs like the stomach.","Only the right atrium carries oxygen-poor blood. The left atrium receives oxygen-rich blood from the lungs, and oxygen content does not set wall thickness anyway.","Correct. A short push into the ventricles needs only a thin wall."],"lt":"t-heart-gross-anatomy","lid":"W3-063","comp":57,"competencyId":"cv-chambers"},{"id":"L03390","dok":2,"q":"Why are the walls of the ventricles thicker than the walls of the atria?","a":"The ventricles must generate enough force to pump blood out of the heart through an entire circuit.","options":["They hold the valves that pump the blood","They must push blood out through a whole circuit","They receive blood from the great veins","They must stretch to store blood between beats"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The ventricles are the pumping chambers. The right ventricle pushes blood through the pulmonary circuit and the left ventricle pushes it through the systemic circuit, so each must generate far more force than an atrium. A thicker myocardium provides that force, and the left ventricle, with the longest circuit, is thickest of all.","notes":["Valves do no pumping. They are passive flaps that open and close as pressure changes, so the muscle wall does all the work.","Correct. Pumping blood through a whole circuit requires thick muscle.","Receiving blood from the great veins is the job of the atria. Pick the atria for the chambers that collect venous return.","Storing blood between beats needs a stretchy, thin wall, which fits the atria better. Thick walls exist for force, not storage."],"lt":"t-heart-gross-anatomy","lid":"W3-064","comp":57,"competencyId":"cv-chambers"},{"id":"L03391","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares pectinate muscles and trabeculae carneae?","a":"Pectinate muscles are parallel ridges in the atria; trabeculae carneae are irregular ridges in the ventricles.","options":["Both are fibrous cords that hold the AV valves shut","Pectinate muscles line the atria, and trabeculae carneae line the ventricles","Pectinate muscles are in the ventricles, and trabeculae carneae are in the atria","Both are smooth areas found only in the outflow tracts"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Pectinate muscles are parallel, comb-like ridges on the inner walls of the atria and auricles. Trabeculae carneae are irregular, crisscrossing ridges on the inner walls of the ventricles. Both are ridges of myocardium, and their location tells you which chamber you are looking at in a dissected heart.","notes":["The fibrous cords holding the AV valves are the chordae tendineae. Pick them for the heart strings attached to papillary muscles.","Correct. Pectinate muscles are atrial ridges, and trabeculae carneae are ventricular ridges.","This swaps their locations. Pectinate (comb-like) ridges belong to the atria, and trabeculae carneae belong to the ventricles.","The smooth outflow tracts are the conus arteriosus and aortic vestibule, which lack ridges. Pick those for the smooth passages leading to the semilunar valves."],"lt":"t-heart-gross-anatomy","lid":"W3-065","comp":56,"competencyId":"cv-surfaces"},{"id":"L03392","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares the interatrial and interventricular septa?","a":"The interatrial septum separates the atria; the interventricular septum separates the ventricles.","options":["The interatrial septum separates the atria, and the interventricular septum separates the ventricles","The interatrial septum separates the ventricles, and the interventricular septum separates the atria","Both septa are thin walls containing the fossa ovalis","Both septa separate the atria from the ventricles"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"A septum is a dividing wall. The interatrial septum is the thin wall between the right and left atria, and it holds the fossa ovalis. The interventricular septum is the thick muscular wall between the right and left ventricles. Neither septum separates an atrium from a ventricle; that boundary is formed by the fibrous skeleton and the AV valves.","notes":["Correct. Each septum is named for the chambers it separates.","This swaps the names. Read the root of each word: atrial for the atria and ventricular for the ventricles.","Only the interatrial septum is thin and holds the fossa ovalis. The interventricular septum is thick and muscular.","The atria are separated from the ventricles by the fibrous skeleton and the AV valves, not by either septum. Pick the AV valves for the openings between atria and ventricles."],"lt":"t-heart-gross-anatomy","lid":"W3-066","comp":57,"competencyId":"cv-chambers"},{"id":"L03393","dok":2,"q":"Why is the fossa ovalis found in the interatrial septum?","a":"It marks where the fetal foramen ovale once connected the two atria.","options":["It marks where the fetal foramen ovale joined the atria","It is where the superior vena cava enters","It marks where the ductus arteriosus once attached","It is the opening where the coronary sinus drains"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Before birth, the foramen ovale was an opening in the interatrial septum that let blood pass from the right atrium to the left atrium, bypassing the lungs. After birth, a flap seals it shut, leaving a shallow oval depression called the fossa ovalis. Its location simply marks where that fetal opening used to be.","notes":["Correct. The fossa ovalis is the sealed site of the foramen ovale.","The superior vena cava enters the top of the right atrium, in the smooth posterior part called the sinus venarum. Pick it for where venous return from the upper body enters.","The ductus arteriosus connected the pulmonary trunk to the aorta and became the ligamentum arteriosum outside the heart. Pick it for the cord between those two vessels.","The coronary sinus opens into the right atrium near the tricuspid valve, not as a depression in the septum. Pick it for the vessel draining the heart wall."],"lt":"t-heart-gross-anatomy","lid":"W3-067","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L03407","dok":3,"q":"Fluid builds up in the pericardial cavity and presses on the heart from outside. Which heart function is most directly limited?","a":"Filling of the chambers.","options":["Oxygen pickup in the lungs","Closing of the valves","Spread of the electrical signal","Filling of the chambers"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The fibrous pericardium does not stretch much, so extra fluid in the pericardial cavity has nowhere to go but inward. That pressure squeezes the heart from outside and keeps the chambers from expanding to fill with blood. With less filling, less blood is pumped out with each beat.","notes":["Oxygen pickup happens in the alveoli of the lungs, outside the pericardium. Pick the lungs for a problem with gas exchange.","Valves close because of pressure differences inside the heart, which outside fluid does not directly change. Pick valve closure for a problem with the cusps, chordae, or papillary muscles.","The electrical signal travels through the conduction system inside the heart wall. Pick the conduction system for a problem with the heartbeat's rhythm.","Correct. External pressure keeps the chambers from expanding and filling."],"lt":"t-heart-gross-anatomy","lid":"W3-081","comp":56,"competencyId":"cv-surfaces"},{"id":"L03408","dok":1,"q":"Fluid fills the pericardial cavity until it squeezes the heart and limits how much it can fill. What is this condition called?","a":"Cardiac tamponade.","options":["Myocardial infarction","Mitral valve prolapse","Cardiac tamponade","Endocarditis"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Cardiac tamponade is the buildup of fluid in the pericardial cavity that compresses the heart. Because the fibrous pericardium cannot stretch, the fluid presses inward and prevents the chambers from filling. The same fluid film that normally lets the heart glide can, in excess, stop it from working.","notes":["A myocardial infarction is death of heart muscle when a coronary artery is blocked. Pick it for a heart attack, not for fluid around the heart.","Mitral valve prolapse is when the mitral cusps bulge back into the left atrium. Pick it for a valve problem involving the chordae tendineae.","Correct. Cardiac tamponade is fluid compressing the heart from the pericardial cavity.","Endocarditis is inflammation, often infection, of the endocardium lining the chambers and valves. Pick it for a problem on the inside of the heart."],"lt":"t-heart-gross-anatomy","lid":"W3-082","comp":56,"competencyId":"cv-surfaces"},{"id":"L03409","dok":2,"q":"A knife enters the pericardial sac from the front of the chest. Which layer does it pierce first?","a":"The fibrous pericardium.","options":["The myocardium","The visceral layer of the serous pericardium","The parietal layer of the serous pericardium","The fibrous pericardium"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"From outside in, the layers around and of the heart are the fibrous pericardium, the parietal layer of the serous pericardium, the pericardial cavity, the visceral layer (epicardium), the myocardium, and the endocardium. A blade entering the sac would pierce the tough fibrous pericardium first. Bleeding into the pericardial cavity from such a wound can cause cardiac tamponade.","notes":["The myocardium is the heart muscle itself, deep to the whole pericardium and the epicardium. Pick it for a wound that has reached the heart wall.","The visceral layer lies on the heart surface across the pericardial cavity. Pick it for the layer reached right before the heart muscle.","The parietal layer lines the inside of the fibrous pericardium, so it is the second layer reached. Pick it for the serous layer just deep to the fibrous sac.","Correct. The fibrous pericardium is the outermost layer of the sac."],"lt":"t-heart-gross-anatomy","lid":"W3-083","comp":56,"competencyId":"cv-surfaces"},{"id":"L03410","dok":2,"q":"A heart attack destroys a patch of myocardium. Which ability of the heart is most directly reduced?","a":"The force of contraction.","options":["The gliding of the heart within its sac","The smoothness of the chamber lining","The anchoring of the heart in the mediastinum","The force of contraction"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The myocardium is the cardiac muscle layer that contracts to pump blood. When a coronary artery is blocked, the myocardium beyond it is starved of oxygen and dies, which is a myocardial infarction. Dead muscle cannot contract, so the heart pumps with less force.","notes":["Gliding within the sac depends on the serous pericardium and its fluid. Pick it for a problem in the pericardial cavity.","The smoothness of the chamber lining depends on the endocardium. Pick it for damage to the inner lining or valves.","Anchoring depends on the fibrous pericardium. Pick it for the tough outer sac.","Correct. The myocardium generates the force of contraction."],"lt":"t-heart-gross-anatomy","lid":"W3-084","comp":56,"competencyId":"cv-surfaces"},{"id":"L03411","dok":2,"q":"An infection damages the thin lining that covers the surfaces of the heart valves. Which layer of the heart wall is infected?","a":"The endocardium.","options":["The epicardium","The endocardium","The fibrous pericardium","The myocardium"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The valve cusps are covered by endocardium, the smooth endothelium that also lines the chambers. Infection of this layer is called endocarditis, and because the valves are covered by it, valve damage is a common result. A roughened valve surface may then fail to close cleanly.","notes":["The epicardium covers the outside of the heart, far from the valves. Pick it for the surface layer where the coronary vessels run.","Correct. The endocardium covers the valves, so infection there is endocarditis.","The fibrous pericardium is part of the sac around the heart, not the wall. Pick it for the tough outer layer.","The myocardium is muscle and does not cover the valves. Pick it for a problem with contraction."],"lt":"t-heart-gross-anatomy","lid":"W3-085","comp":56,"competencyId":"cv-surfaces"},{"id":"L03421","dok":3,"q":"In a cross section through both ventricles, one has a thick wall and round cavity while the other is thin-walled and crescent-shaped. Which is the thick one, and why?","a":"The left ventricle, because it pumps blood through the systemic circuit to the whole body.","options":["The left ventricle, because it pumps blood to the whole body","The right ventricle, because it receives blood from the whole body","The right ventricle, because it pumps blood to the lungs","The left ventricle, because it receives blood from the lungs"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The left ventricle drives the systemic circuit, sending blood to every tissue, which takes far more force than pushing blood to the nearby lungs. Its thick myocardium provides that force and gives it a round cavity in cross section. The thinner right ventricle wraps around it in a crescent shape.","notes":["Correct. The left ventricle is thick because it powers the systemic circuit.","The right ventricle receives blood from the right atrium, not directly from the body, and receiving blood does not require a thick wall.","The right ventricle does pump to the lungs, but that short circuit needs less force, so it is the thin, crescent-shaped one.","The chamber is right, but the reason is wrong. Receiving blood takes little force; wall thickness reflects pumping, not receiving."],"lt":"t-heart-gross-anatomy","lid":"W3-095","comp":57,"competencyId":"cv-chambers"},{"id":"L03422","dok":2,"q":"A baby is born with a hole in the interventricular septum. Which chambers can abnormally exchange blood?","a":"The right and left ventricles.","options":["The right atrium and right ventricle","The right and left ventricles","The right and left atria","The left atrium and left ventricle"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The interventricular septum is the thick wall between the right and left ventricles. A hole in it, called a ventricular septal defect, lets blood pass between the two ventricles. This mixes blood that should stay on separate sides of the heart.","notes":["These chambers are normally connected through the tricuspid valve, so they are not separated by a septum. Pick them for a problem with the tricuspid valve.","Correct. The interventricular septum separates the two ventricles.","The atria are separated by the interatrial septum. Pick this pair for a hole in that wall, such as a patent foramen ovale.","These chambers are normally connected through the mitral valve. Pick them for a problem with the mitral valve."],"lt":"t-heart-gross-anatomy","lid":"W3-096","comp":57,"competencyId":"cv-chambers"},{"id":"L03423","dok":2,"q":"The foramen ovale fails to close after birth, leaving a hole in the interatrial septum. Which chambers can abnormally exchange blood?","a":"The right and left atria.","options":["The right and left atria","The right and left ventricles","The left atrium and left ventricle","The right ventricle and pulmonary trunk"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The interatrial septum separates the right and left atria. When the foramen ovale stays open after birth, a condition called a patent foramen ovale, blood can still pass between the two atria. Patent means open, and the condition is named for the fetal structure that failed to seal.","notes":["Correct. A hole in the interatrial septum connects the two atria.","The ventricles are separated by the interventricular septum. Pick this pair for a ventricular septal defect.","The left atrium and left ventricle are normally joined through the mitral valve. A septal hole does not connect them.","The right ventricle and pulmonary trunk are normally joined through the pulmonary valve. Pick them for a pulmonary valve problem."],"lt":"t-heart-gross-anatomy","lid":"W3-097","comp":57,"competencyId":"cv-chambers"},{"id":"L03424","dok":2,"q":"In a dissected heart, a student sees a muscular band stretching from the interventricular septum across the chamber to the base of a papillary muscle. Which chamber is she looking into?","a":"The right ventricle.","options":["The right atrium","The left ventricle","The left atrium","The right ventricle"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The band described is the moderator band, or septomarginal trabecula, and it is a landmark of the right ventricle. It runs from the septum to the anterior papillary muscle and carries part of the conduction system. Finding it is a reliable way to identify the right ventricle on a lab practical.","notes":["The right atrium has pectinate muscles and the crista terminalis, not papillary muscles. Pick it when you see the fossa ovalis and the openings of the venae cavae.","The left ventricle has papillary muscles and trabeculae carneae but no moderator band. Pick it when you see the thickest wall and the mitral valve chordae.","The left atrium has no papillary muscles. Pick it when you see the openings of the four pulmonary veins.","Correct. The moderator band is found in the right ventricle."],"lt":"t-heart-gross-anatomy","lid":"W3-098","comp":56,"competencyId":"cv-surfaces"},{"id":"L03426","dok":2,"q":"Where is the heart located?","a":"In the mediastinum, between the lungs, resting on the diaphragm.","options":["In the left pleural cavity, surrounded by the left lung","In the abdominopelvic cavity, just below the diaphragm","In the mediastinum, between the lungs and resting on the diaphragm","In the dorsal body cavity, beside the vertebral column"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The heart sits in the mediastinum, the central region of the thoracic cavity between the two lungs. It rests on the diaphragm and is tilted so that about two thirds of its mass lies left of the midline. It is enclosed in its own pericardial sac, separate from the pleural cavities around the lungs.","notes":["The left pleural cavity surrounds the left lung only. The heart leans left, but it sits in the mediastinum inside its own pericardium.","The abdominopelvic cavity lies below the diaphragm and holds organs such as the stomach and liver. The heart rests on top of the diaphragm, not under it.","Correct. The heart lies in the mediastinum between the lungs, on top of the diaphragm.","The dorsal body cavity holds the brain and spinal cord. Pick it for the cranial and vertebral cavities, not the heart."],"lt":"t-heart-gross-anatomy","lid":"W3-100","comp":8,"competencyId":"w1-mediastinum"},{"id":"L03474","dok":1,"q":"What type of tissue forms the myocardium, the thick middle layer of the heart wall?","a":"Cardiac muscle tissue.","options":["Cardiac muscle tissue","Simple squamous epithelium","Dense irregular connective tissue","Smooth muscle tissue"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The myocardium is the thick middle layer of the heart wall, making up about ninety five percent of its thickness, and it is built of cardiac muscle tissue arranged in spiraling bundles. This is the layer that actually contracts and pumps blood. Cardiac muscle is found only in the heart, so the myocardium is the one place you will see it.","notes":["Correct. The myocardium is cardiac muscle, the only muscle tissue that forms the heart wall.","Simple squamous epithelium is a single layer of flat cells. It forms the endothelium of the endocardium lining the chambers and the surface of the epicardium, so it fits a question about the inner or outer heart wall layers, not the myocardium.","Dense irregular connective tissue forms tough coverings such as the fibrous pericardium and the dermis. Choose it for the outer layer of the pericardial sac, which anchors the heart, not for the contracting middle layer of the wall.","Smooth muscle is the involuntary, nonstriated muscle in the walls of hollow organs and blood vessels, such as the tunica media of an artery. It would be the answer if the question asked what muscle tissue narrows a blood vessel, not what forms the heart wall."],"lt":"t-cardiac-muscle","lid":"W3-118","comp":56,"competencyId":"cv-surfaces"},{"id":"L03979","dok":1,"q":"What is the name of the smooth outflow tract of the left ventricle that leads blood to the aortic valve?","a":"The aortic vestibule.","options":["Aortic vestibule","Conus arteriosus","Fossa ovalis","Sinus venarum"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Most of the inner ventricular wall is covered in trabeculae carneae, which are irregular muscular ridges. Just below each semilunar valve the wall becomes smooth to form an outflow tract. In the left ventricle this smooth outflow tract is the aortic vestibule, and it leads blood up to the aortic valve.","notes":["Correct. The aortic vestibule is the smooth outflow tract of the left ventricle.","The conus arteriosus is the matching smooth outflow tract of the right ventricle, leading to the pulmonary valve. It would be the answer if the question asked about the right ventricle.","The fossa ovalis is the oval depression in the interatrial septum left by the fetal foramen ovale. It would be the answer if the question asked about the remnant of a fetal shunt.","The sinus venarum is the smooth posterior part of the right atrium where the venae cavae empty. It would be the answer if the question asked where the venae cavae open into the heart."],"lt":"t-pathway-integrated-tracing","lid":"W3-787","comp":56,"competencyId":"cv-surfaces"},{"id":"L03980","dok":1,"q":"What is the name of the smooth outflow tract of the right ventricle that leads blood to the pulmonary valve?","a":"The conus arteriosus.","options":["Crista terminalis","Moderator band","Conus arteriosus","Aortic vestibule"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The conus arteriosus is the smooth, funnel-shaped upper part of the right ventricle. It leads blood from the ridged main chamber up to the pulmonary valve and into the pulmonary trunk. Its smooth wall contrasts with the trabeculae carneae lining the rest of the ventricle.","notes":["The crista terminalis is a ridge in the right atrium that separates its smooth posterior wall from its rough, pectinate anterior wall. It would be the answer if the question were about the inside of the right atrium.","The moderator band is a muscular band in the right ventricle that carries part of the conduction system to the anterior papillary muscle. It would be the answer if the question asked which right ventricle structure carries conduction fibers.","Correct. The conus arteriosus leads from the right ventricle to the pulmonary valve.","The aortic vestibule is the smooth outflow tract of the left ventricle, leading to the aortic valve. It would be the answer if the question asked about the left ventricle."],"lt":"t-pathway-integrated-tracing","lid":"W3-788","comp":56,"competencyId":"cv-surfaces"},{"id":"L03996","dok":1,"q":"Which heart chamber has the thickest wall and pumps blood into the aorta?","a":"Left ventricle","options":["Left atrium","Left ventricle","Right ventricle","Right atrium"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The left ventricle pumps blood into the aorta and out through the entire systemic circuit, from the head to the toes. Pushing blood that far takes far more force than sending it to the nearby lungs, so the left ventricle has the thickest myocardium (the heart muscle layer) of the four chambers. Its tip also forms the apex of the heart.","notes":["The left atrium is a thin-walled receiving chamber that collects oxygen-rich blood from the pulmonary veins. It would be the answer if the question asked which chamber forms most of the base of the heart.","Correct. The left ventricle has the thickest wall and pumps into the aorta.","The right ventricle pumps blood only to the lungs through the pulmonary trunk, a short, low-pressure route, so its wall is thinner. Pick it if the question asked which chamber pumps into the pulmonary trunk.","The right atrium is a thin-walled receiving chamber that collects blood from the venae cavae and coronary sinus. Choose it if the question asked which chamber receives blood returning from the body."],"lt":"t-imagefree-structure-recognition","lid":"W3-804","comp":57,"competencyId":"cv-chambers"},{"id":"L03997","dok":1,"q":"Which layer of the heart wall is the same membrane as the visceral layer of the serous pericardium?","a":"Epicardium","options":["Epicardium","Fibrous pericardium","Endocardium","Myocardium"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The epicardium is the outermost layer of the heart wall, a thin serous membrane that covers the heart surface and carries fat and the coronary vessels. It is the visceral layer of the serous pericardium under a second name. You call it the epicardium when describing the heart wall and the visceral pericardium when describing the sac around the heart.","notes":["Correct. The epicardium and the visceral serous pericardium are the same membrane.","The fibrous pericardium is the tough outer layer of the sac around the heart, not part of the heart wall. Choose it if the question asked which layer anchors the heart in the mediastinum and prevents overfilling.","The endocardium is the innermost layer, a smooth endothelium lining the chambers and covering the valves. It would be the answer if the question asked which layer lines the inside of the heart.","The myocardium is the thick middle layer made of cardiac muscle, the part of the wall that contracts. Pick it if the question asked which layer makes up most of the wall thickness."],"lt":"t-imagefree-structure-recognition","lid":"W3-805","comp":56,"competencyId":"cv-surfaces"},{"id":"L03998","dok":1,"q":"Which layer of the heart wall is made of cardiac muscle and does the work of contraction?","a":"Myocardium","options":["Myocardium","Parietal pericardium","Endocardium","Epicardium"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The myocardium is the thick middle layer of the heart wall, built of cardiac muscle tissue arranged in spiraling bundles. It makes up about 95 percent of the wall thickness and is the only layer that contracts. The layers on either side of it are thin membranes that cover or line it.","notes":["Correct. The myocardium is the cardiac muscle layer that contracts.","The parietal pericardium is the serous layer lining the inside of the fibrous pericardium, so it belongs to the sac rather than the heart wall. Choose it if the question asked which layer forms the outer boundary of the pericardial cavity.","The endocardium is the thin endothelial lining of the chambers and valves. It would be the answer if the question asked which layer lines the chambers and covers the valves.","The epicardium is the thin serous membrane on the outer surface of the heart, where fat and the coronary vessels run. Pick it if the question asked for the outermost layer of the heart wall."],"lt":"t-imagefree-structure-recognition","lid":"W3-806","comp":56,"competencyId":"cv-surfaces"},{"id":"L03999","dok":1,"q":"Which layer of the heart wall is a smooth endothelium that lines the chambers and covers the valves?","a":"Endocardium","options":["Epicardium","Myocardium","Fibrous pericardium","Endocardium"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The endocardium is the innermost layer of the heart wall. It is a smooth sheet of endothelium (a single layer of flat cells) that lines every chamber and covers the valve cusps, so blood flows over a slick surface. It is continuous with the tunica intima, the lining of the great vessels.","notes":["The epicardium covers the outside of the heart rather than lining the inside. Pick it if the question asked which layer is the same as the visceral serous pericardium.","The myocardium is the thick cardiac muscle layer in the middle of the wall. It would be the answer if the question asked which layer contracts to pump blood.","The fibrous pericardium is the tough outer sac of dense connective tissue around the whole heart. Choose it if the question asked what anchors the heart and keeps it from overfilling.","Correct. The endocardium lines the chambers and covers the valves."],"lt":"t-imagefree-structure-recognition","lid":"W3-807","comp":56,"competencyId":"cv-surfaces"},{"id":"L04000","dok":1,"q":"Which layer around the heart is tough, inelastic dense connective tissue that anchors the heart in the mediastinum?","a":"Fibrous pericardium","options":["Endocardium","Parietal serous pericardium","Visceral serous pericardium","Fibrous pericardium"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The fibrous pericardium is the outermost layer of the sac around the heart. It is made of dense connective tissue, so it is tough and does not stretch much. That lets it anchor the heart to the surrounding structures of the mediastinum and keep the heart from overfilling with blood.","notes":["The endocardium is the inner lining of the heart chambers and valves, not part of the pericardial sac. Choose it if the question asked which layer the blood touches inside the heart.","The parietal serous pericardium is a thin serous membrane lining the inner surface of the fibrous pericardium. Pick it if the question asked which layer forms the outer wall of the pericardial cavity.","The visceral serous pericardium lies directly on the heart surface and is also called the epicardium. It would be the answer if the question asked which pericardial layer is also part of the heart wall.","Correct. The fibrous pericardium is the tough anchoring layer of the sac."],"lt":"t-imagefree-structure-recognition","lid":"W3-808","comp":56,"competencyId":"cv-surfaces"},{"id":"L04001","dok":1,"q":"What is the name of the space between the parietal and visceral layers of the serous pericardium, which holds a thin film of serous fluid?","a":"Pericardial cavity","options":["Peritoneal cavity","Pericardial cavity","Pleural cavity","Mediastinum"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The pericardial cavity is the thin space between the two layers of the serous pericardium. It holds a small amount of serous fluid that reduces friction so the heart can glide as it beats. If too much fluid collects there, it presses on the heart and limits how much it can fill, a condition called cardiac tamponade.","notes":["The peritoneal cavity is the space between the parietal and visceral peritoneum in the abdomen. Choose it if the question asked about the serous space around the abdominal organs.","Correct. The pericardial cavity holds the serous fluid between the two serous layers.","The pleural cavity is the fluid-filled space between the parietal and visceral pleurae around each lung. Pick it if the question asked about the space that lets the lungs glide against the chest wall.","The mediastinum is the central compartment of the thoracic cavity that holds the heart, great vessels, trachea, and esophagus. It would be the answer if the question asked where the whole heart sits, not the fluid space right around it."],"lt":"t-imagefree-structure-recognition","lid":"W3-809","comp":56,"competencyId":"cv-surfaces"},{"id":"L04002","dok":1,"q":"Which wall separates the right atrium from the left atrium?","a":"Interatrial septum","options":["Interatrial septum","Fibrous skeleton","Crista terminalis","Interventricular septum"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The interatrial septum is the thin wall that divides the right atrium from the left atrium. It keeps oxygen-poor blood on the right side apart from oxygen-rich blood on the left. On its right atrial side you can find the fossa ovalis, an oval depression left over from the fetal foramen ovale.","notes":["Correct. The interatrial septum divides the two atria.","The fibrous skeleton is the set of dense connective tissue rings around the four valve openings, which separates the atria from the ventricles rather than the left side from the right. It would be the answer if the question asked what anchors the valves and electrically insulates the atria from the ventricles.","The crista terminalis is a ridge inside the right atrium that separates its smooth posterior wall from its rough, pectinate anterior wall. Choose it if the question asked about the ridge between the two parts of the right atrial wall.","The interventricular septum is the thick wall between the two ventricles. Pick it if the question asked which wall separates the right and left ventricles."],"lt":"t-imagefree-structure-recognition","lid":"W3-810","comp":57,"competencyId":"cv-chambers"},{"id":"L04003","dok":1,"q":"Which wall separates the right ventricle from the left ventricle?","a":"Interventricular septum","options":["Interatrial septum","Moderator band","Interventricular septum","Coronary sulcus"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The interventricular septum is the thick muscular wall between the right and left ventricles. It keeps blood headed to the lungs separate from blood headed to the body. The AV bundle and the bundle branches of the conduction system run along this septum on their way toward the apex.","notes":["The interatrial septum is the thinner wall between the two atria, where the fossa ovalis sits. Pick it if the question asked which wall separates the right and left atria.","The moderator band is a muscular band inside the right ventricle that carries part of the conduction system to the anterior papillary muscle. It would be the answer if the question asked about the band that crosses the right ventricle.","Correct. The interventricular septum divides the two ventricles.","The coronary sulcus is the groove on the outside of the heart that circles between the atria and the ventricles and holds coronary vessels. Choose it if the question asked which external groove marks the boundary between the upper and lower chambers."],"lt":"t-imagefree-structure-recognition","lid":"W3-811","comp":57,"competencyId":"cv-chambers"},{"id":"L04004","dok":1,"q":"In the adult heart, which feature is the shallow oval depression on the right atrial side of the interatrial septum?","a":"Fossa ovalis","options":["Fossa ovalis","Foramen ovale","Auricle","Sinus venarum"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The fossa ovalis is a shallow oval depression in the interatrial septum, seen from inside the right atrium. It marks the spot where the foramen ovale, an opening between the atria, let fetal blood skip the lungs. After birth that opening seals, and the depression is what remains.","notes":["Correct. The fossa ovalis is the oval depression in the interatrial septum.","The foramen ovale is the fetal opening in the interatrial septum that lets blood pass from the right atrium to the left atrium. Pick it if the question asked about the fetal shunt itself rather than the depression it leaves behind.","An auricle is a small wrinkled pouch on the outside of each atrium that slightly increases its capacity. It would be the answer if the question asked about the ear-shaped flap on the surface of an atrium.","The sinus venarum is the smooth-walled posterior part of the right atrium, where the venae cavae empty. Choose it if the question asked which part of the right atrial wall is smooth and receives the venae cavae."],"lt":"t-imagefree-structure-recognition","lid":"W3-812","comp":56,"competencyId":"cv-surfaces"},{"id":"L04005","dok":1,"q":"Which parallel muscular ridges line the inner walls of the atria and the auricles?","a":"Pectinate muscles","options":["Chordae tendineae","Papillary muscles","Pectinate muscles","Trabeculae carneae"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Pectinate muscles are parallel ridges of muscle on the inner walls of the atria and auricles. The name comes from the Latin word for comb, because the ridges line up like the teeth of a comb. In the right atrium they cover the rough anterior wall, which the crista terminalis separates from the smooth posterior wall.","notes":["Chordae tendineae are fibrous cords, not muscle, that tie the cusps of the AV valves to the papillary muscles. Choose them if the question asked what keeps the AV valve cusps from flipping back into the atria.","Papillary muscles are cone-shaped projections of the ventricle wall that anchor the chordae tendineae. They would be the answer if the question asked what holds the cords of the AV valves.","Correct. Pectinate muscles are the comb-like ridges of the atria and auricles.","Trabeculae carneae are irregular muscular ridges on the inner walls of the ventricles, not the atria. Pick them if the question asked about the ridges inside the ventricles."],"lt":"t-imagefree-structure-recognition","lid":"W3-813","comp":56,"competencyId":"cv-surfaces"},{"id":"L04006","dok":1,"q":"Which irregular muscular ridges line the inner walls of the ventricles?","a":"Trabeculae carneae","options":["Crista terminalis","Pectinate muscles","Chordae tendineae","Trabeculae carneae"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Trabeculae carneae are the irregular ridges and bundles of muscle on the inner walls of both ventricles. The name means little beams of flesh. They give the ventricular lining a rough, crisscrossed look, unlike the orderly parallel pectinate muscles of the atria, which helps you tell the two kinds of chamber apart on a model.","notes":["The crista terminalis is a single ridge in the right atrium marking where the smooth and rough parts of the wall meet. It would be the answer if the question asked about the ridge separating the sinus venarum from the pectinate wall.","Pectinate muscles are the parallel, comb-like ridges in the atria and auricles. Pick them if the question asked about the ridges on the atrial walls.","Chordae tendineae are thin fibrous cords attached to the AV valve cusps, not ridges of muscle on the wall. Choose them if the question asked what connects the valve cusps to the papillary muscles.","Correct. Trabeculae carneae are the irregular muscular ridges of the ventricles."],"lt":"t-imagefree-structure-recognition","lid":"W3-814","comp":56,"competencyId":"cv-surfaces"},{"id":"L04090","dok":3,"q":"Fluid builds up in the pericardial cavity and squeezes the heart from outside. Which heart function is most directly limited?","a":"How fully the ventricles can fill.","options":["How much oxygen the alveoli take up","How fully the ventricles can fill","How well the semilunar valves close","How fast the SA node fires"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The pericardial cavity sits between the parietal and visceral layers of the serous pericardium, and it normally holds only a thin film of fluid that lets the heart glide as it beats. The fibrous pericardium around it is tough and does not stretch, so extra fluid has nowhere to go and presses inward on the heart. The chambers cannot expand fully, and less blood enters the ventricles before each beat. This condition is called cardiac tamponade.","notes":["Oxygen uptake happens across the alveolar walls in the lungs, which are wrapped by the pleurae, not the pericardium. This would fit a question about pneumonia or emphysema, where the exchange surface in the lungs is damaged.","Correct. Fluid pressing in from outside keeps the ventricles from expanding, so they fill with less blood.","The semilunar valves are the pulmonary and aortic valves at the exits of the ventricles, and their pocket-shaped cusps close on their own. Choose them for a question about backflow from the aorta or pulmonary trunk into a ventricle.","The SA node is the pacemaker in the wall of the right atrium, and it keeps generating the beat on its own. Pick it if the question described a failing pacemaker or a rhythm that is too slow, not pressure on the outside of the heart."],"lt":"t-clinical-application-and-misconceptions","lid":"W3-901","comp":56,"competencyId":"cv-surfaces"},{"id":"L04131","dok":2,"q":"Why does long-term high blood pressure in the systemic arteries put extra strain on the left ventricle?","a":"It must push against higher pressure in the aorta.","options":["It must push against higher pressure in the aorta","It loses its blood supply from the coronary sinus","It receives less blood from the left atrium","It must push against higher pressure in the pulmonary trunk"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The left ventricle pumps blood through the aortic valve into the aorta and out through the systemic circuit. In systemic hypertension, the pressure in those arteries stays high, so the ventricle has to squeeze harder with every beat to push blood out against it. Over time this extra workload strains the left ventricle and can thicken its wall.","notes":["Correct. The left ventricle pumps into the aorta, so higher systemic pressure means harder work with every beat.","The coronary sinus is a vein that drains the heart wall into the right atrium; it does not supply the ventricle. The heart wall is fed by the coronary arteries, so this choice mixes up drainage with supply.","Systemic hypertension raises the pressure the ventricle pumps against; it does not reduce the blood arriving from the left atrium. This choice confuses a problem with filling with a problem with pumping out.","The pulmonary trunk receives blood from the right ventricle, not the left. High pressure in the lung circuit would strain the right ventricle, so pick this for a question about the right side of the heart."],"lt":"t-clinical-application-and-misconceptions","lid":"W3-945","comp":57,"competencyId":"cv-chambers"},{"id":"L04156","dok":2,"q":"Why can inflammation of the endocardium damage the heart valves?","a":"It lines the chambers and covers the valves.","options":["It makes up most of the wall's thickness","It anchors the heart in the mediastinum","It lines the chambers and covers the valves","It is the outer layer of the heart wall"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The endocardium is the innermost layer of the heart wall, a smooth endothelium that lines all four chambers. It also covers the surfaces of the valves and is continuous with the lining of the great vessels. Because the valves are covered by this same layer, inflammation or infection of the endocardium (endocarditis) can spread to the valve cusps and damage them.","notes":["Most of the wall's thickness, about ninety five percent, is the myocardium, the layer of cardiac muscle. It would be the answer for a question about which layer contracts.","Anchoring the heart in the mediastinum is the job of the fibrous pericardium, the tough outer layer of the pericardial sac. Pick it for a question about what holds the heart in place and prevents overfilling.","Correct. The endocardium covers the valves, so its inflammation reaches them directly.","The outer layer of the heart wall is the epicardium, also called the visceral layer of the serous pericardium. Choose it for a question about the layer where the coronary vessels run."],"lt":"t-clinical-application-and-misconceptions","lid":"W3-972","comp":56,"competencyId":"cv-surfaces"},{"id":"L04157","dok":2,"q":"Why is the fossa ovalis considered a landmark of fetal circulation?","a":"It marks where the foramen ovale was.","options":["It marks where the umbilical vein was","It marks where the foramen ovale was","It marks where the ductus arteriosus was","It marks where the ductus venosus was"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The fossa ovalis is a shallow oval depression in the interatrial septum, the wall between the right and left atria. Before birth, an opening in that septum, the foramen ovale, let blood pass from the right atrium straight to the left atrium and bypass the lungs. After birth the opening seals, and the fossa ovalis is the mark it leaves behind.","notes":["The umbilical vein carried blood from the placenta toward the liver, outside the heart. Its remnant is the ligamentum teres, the round ligament of the liver, so pick it for a question about that ligament.","Correct. The fossa ovalis is the sealed remnant of the foramen ovale.","The ductus arteriosus was a vessel between the pulmonary trunk and the aorta, outside the heart. Its remnant is the ligamentum arteriosum, so pick that cord for a question about the ductus arteriosus.","The ductus venosus was the shunt through the fetal liver, and its remnant is the ligamentum venosum on the inferior surface of the liver. Choose it for a question about the liver bypass."],"lt":"t-clinical-application-and-misconceptions","lid":"W3-973","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L04167","dok":2,"q":"Why does the left ventricle have the thickest wall of the four chambers?","a":"It pumps blood to the entire body.","options":["It holds the SA node in its wall","It pumps blood only to the nearby lungs","It receives blood from the venae cavae","It pumps blood to the entire body"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The left ventricle pumps oxygen-rich blood into the aorta and out through the systemic circuit, which reaches every tissue from head to toe. Pushing blood through that long, extensive circuit takes much more force than any other chamber needs. Its thick myocardium supplies that force, so it has the thickest wall of the four chambers.","notes":["The SA node is in the wall of the right atrium, near the opening of the superior vena cava. It would be the answer for a question about where the pacemaker sits, not about wall thickness.","Pumping only to the nearby lungs describes the right ventricle, which has a thinner wall for exactly that reason. Pick it for a question about the right ventricle.","The venae cavae empty into the right atrium, a thin-walled receiving chamber. Choose the right atrium for a question about where blood from the body returns.","Correct. Driving blood through the entire body takes the most force, so the left ventricle has the most muscle."],"lt":"t-clinical-application-and-misconceptions","lid":"W3-985","comp":57,"competencyId":"cv-chambers"},{"id":"L04168","dok":2,"q":"Why is the wall of the right ventricle thinner than the wall of the left ventricle?","a":"It pumps blood only to the nearby lungs.","options":["It pumps blood to the whole body","It receives blood from the pulmonary veins","It only receives blood and does not pump","It pumps blood only to the nearby lungs"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The right ventricle pumps blood into the pulmonary trunk and through the pulmonary circuit, a short loop to the lungs right beside the heart. Moving blood through that short circuit takes much less force than pushing it through the entire body. The right ventricle therefore needs less muscle, and its wall is thinner than the left ventricle's.","notes":["Pumping to the whole body is the job of the left ventricle, which is why its wall is the thickest. Pick it for a question about the left ventricle.","The pulmonary veins return oxygen-rich blood from the lungs to the left atrium, not the right ventricle. Choose the left atrium for a question about where the pulmonary veins empty.","The right ventricle does pump, sending blood into the pulmonary trunk. Receiving blood with little pumping describes the atria, the thin-walled chambers that collect returning blood.","Correct. The short pulmonary circuit needs less force, so the right ventricle has a thinner wall."],"lt":"t-clinical-application-and-misconceptions","lid":"W3-986","comp":57,"competencyId":"cv-chambers"},{"id":"L04182","dok":1,"q":"Which layer of the heart wall is the thick layer of cardiac muscle that does the contracting?","a":"The myocardium.","options":["Epicardium","Fibrous pericardium","Endocardium","Myocardium"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The myocardium is the middle layer of the heart wall, made of cardiac muscle tissue arranged in spiraling bundles. It makes up about 95 percent of the wall's thickness, and its contraction is what pumps blood out of the chambers. It is thickest in the left ventricle, which pumps blood to the whole body.","notes":["The epicardium is the thin serous membrane on the outer surface of the heart, where fat and the coronary vessels run. Pick it if the question asked which layer is the same membrane as the visceral serous pericardium.","The fibrous pericardium is the tough outer sac of dense connective tissue that anchors the heart in the mediastinum. It contains no muscle; choose it for the layer that keeps the heart from overfilling.","The endocardium is the smooth endothelium lining the chambers and covering the valves. It would be the answer for the layer that is continuous with the lining of the great vessels.","Correct. The myocardium is the cardiac muscle layer and forms the bulk of the wall."],"lt":"t-cv-surfaces","lid":"cv-surfaces-r2","comp":56,"competencyId":"cv-surfaces"},{"id":"L04183","dok":1,"q":"What is the pericardium?","a":"The double-walled sac that encloses the heart.","options":["The serous membrane that surrounds each lung","The rings of dense connective tissue around the valves","The smooth lining of the heart chambers and valves","The double-walled sac that encloses the heart"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The pericardium is the double-walled sac that encloses the heart in the mediastinum. Its tough outer fibrous layer anchors the heart and keeps it from overfilling, and its inner serous layer folds back on itself to form a parietal layer and a visceral layer. The thin film of serous fluid between those two serous layers lets the heart beat without friction.","notes":["That describes the pleura, the serous membrane of the lungs. It would be the answer for the membrane with a parietal layer on the thoracic wall and a visceral layer on the lung surface.","That describes the fibrous skeleton of the heart, which anchors the valves and electrically insulates the atria from the ventricles. Choose it if the question asked what holds the valve openings in shape.","That describes the endocardium, the innermost layer of the heart wall. Pick it if the question asked which layer lines the chambers and covers the valves.","Correct. The pericardium is the sac around the heart, with a fibrous outer layer and a serous inner layer."],"lt":"t-cv-surfaces","lid":"cv-surfaces-r3","comp":56,"competencyId":"cv-surfaces"},{"id":"L04184","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares the fibrous pericardium and the serous pericardium?","a":"The fibrous pericardium is the tough outer layer that anchors the heart; the serous pericardium is the thin double membrane inside it, with parietal and visceral layers.","options":["The serous layer is tough and anchors the heart; the fibrous layer is a thin double membrane inside it","The fibrous layer lines the heart chambers; the serous layer lines the thoracic wall","The fibrous layer is tough and anchors the heart; the serous layer is a thin double membrane inside it","The fibrous layer is cardiac muscle that contracts; the serous layer is a thin membrane outside it"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The pericardium has two parts. The fibrous pericardium is the outer layer of dense, inelastic connective tissue that anchors the heart in the mediastinum and keeps it from overfilling. The serous pericardium sits inside it as a double membrane: its parietal layer lines the fibrous pericardium, and its visceral layer (the epicardium) covers the heart surface, with serous fluid between them.","notes":["This swaps the two layers. The word fibrous is the clue: fibrous tissue is the tough, anchoring layer, while serous membranes are thin and secrete fluid. Students pick this when they mix up which name goes with which texture.","The lining of the chambers is the endocardium, and the membrane lining the thoracic wall is the parietal pleura. Neither is part of the pericardium; choose statements like these for questions about the inner heart wall or the lungs.","Correct. The fibrous layer is the tough anchor, and the serous layer is the thin double membrane that holds the lubricating fluid.","The muscle layer that contracts is the myocardium, the middle layer of the heart wall. The fibrous pericardium contains no muscle, so this option would fit only a question about what the myocardium does."],"lt":"t-cv-surfaces","lid":"cv-surfaces-r4","comp":56,"competencyId":"cv-surfaces"},{"id":"L04185","dok":3,"q":"Fluid builds up in the pericardial cavity and squeezes the heart, a condition called cardiac tamponade. Why does this keep the heart from filling properly?","a":"The fluid presses on the heart from outside, so the chambers cannot expand to fill.","options":["The fluid presses on the heart from outside, so the chambers cannot expand","The fluid leaks through the wall into the chambers and dilutes the blood","The fluid stops the SA node from sending its signal to the atria","The fluid blocks the openings of the coronary arteries at the aorta"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The pericardial cavity is the space between the parietal and visceral layers of the serous pericardium, and it normally holds only a thin film of fluid. Because the fibrous pericardium around it is tough and does not stretch, extra fluid has nowhere to go and presses inward on the heart. The chambers cannot expand fully between beats, so less blood fills them, even though the heart muscle itself is healthy.","notes":["Correct. The trapped fluid compresses the heart from outside and limits how much each chamber can fill.","The pericardial cavity lies outside the heart wall, and fluid there does not pass through the myocardium into the chambers. Students choose this when they picture the pericardial cavity as connected to the inside of the heart.","The SA node is the pacemaker in the wall of the right atrium, and fluid outside the heart does not switch it off. Pick an answer about the SA node for a question on what sets the heart rate.","The coronary arteries open from the base of the aorta, not into the pericardial cavity. A blocked coronary artery describes a myocardial infarction (heart attack), which starves the muscle of oxygen rather than squeezing the heart."],"lt":"t-cv-surfaces","lid":"cv-surfaces-r5","comp":56,"competencyId":"cv-surfaces"},{"id":"L04186","dok":1,"q":"Which list names the four chambers of the heart?","a":"The right and left atria and the right and left ventricles.","options":["Right and left atria, right and left ventricles","Right and left ventricles, conus arteriosus and aortic vestibule","Right and left atria, right and left auricles","Right and left atria, interatrial and interventricular septa"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The heart has four chambers. The two atria are the upper receiving chambers, taking in blood returning from the body and the lungs, and the two ventricles are the lower pumping chambers that send blood out into the pulmonary trunk and the aorta. The right side handles oxygen-poor blood and the left side handles oxygen-rich blood.","notes":["Correct. Two atria receive blood and two ventricles pump it out.","The conus arteriosus and the aortic vestibule are the smooth outflow tracts of the right and left ventricles, leading to the pulmonary and aortic valves. They are parts of the ventricles, so they would be the answer only for a question about where blood leaves each ventricle.","The auricles are small wrinkled pouches on the outside of each atrium that slightly increase its capacity. They are parts of the atria, not separate chambers; pick them if the question asked for the ear-shaped flaps on the atria.","The interatrial and interventricular septa are walls that divide the chambers, not chambers themselves. Choose them if the question asked what separates the right side of the heart from the left."],"lt":"t-cv-chambers","lid":"cv-chambers-r1","comp":57,"competencyId":"cv-chambers"},{"id":"L04189","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares the walls of the atria and the ventricles?","a":"The atria are thin-walled receiving chambers; the ventricles are thick-walled pumping chambers.","options":["The atria have thick walls because they push blood out through the great arteries","The atria are thick-walled pumping chambers; the ventricles are thin-walled receiving chambers","The atria and ventricles have walls of equal thickness because they move the same blood","The atria are thin-walled receiving chambers; the ventricles are thick-walled pumping chambers"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The atria receive blood returning to the heart and only need to push it a short distance down into the ventricles, so their walls are thin. The ventricles must pump blood out of the heart into the pulmonary trunk and aorta, so they have thick muscular walls. Wall thickness tracks the work each chamber does, which is also why the left ventricle is thicker than the right.","notes":["The great arteries, the pulmonary trunk and the aorta, leave from the ventricles, not the atria. The atria send blood only into the ventricles below them, so this mistakes which chambers connect to the arteries.","This reverses the two roles. The atria receive blood from veins and the ventricles pump it into arteries, so the ventricles are the thick ones. Students choose this when they forget that the atria sit on top as receiving chambers.","The chambers do move the same blood, but not with the same effort. Wall thickness depends on how hard a chamber must push, so the pumping ventricles are much thicker than the receiving atria.","Correct. Receiving chambers are thin, and pumping chambers are thick."],"lt":"t-cv-chambers","lid":"cv-chambers-r4","comp":57,"competencyId":"cv-chambers"},{"id":"L04190","dok":3,"q":"A newborn has a hole in the interventricular septum. Which two kinds of blood can now mix that the septum normally keeps apart?","a":"Oxygen-poor blood in the right ventricle and oxygen-rich blood in the left ventricle.","options":["Oxygen-poor blood of the pulmonary trunk and oxygen-rich blood of the aorta","Oxygen-poor blood of the right atrium and oxygen-rich blood of the left atrium","Oxygen-rich blood of the left ventricle and blood already in the aorta","Oxygen-poor blood of the right ventricle and oxygen-rich blood of the left ventricle"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The interventricular septum is the thick wall between the two ventricles. The right ventricle holds oxygen-poor blood headed for the lungs and the left ventricle holds oxygen-rich blood headed for the body, so the septum keeps the pulmonary and systemic circuits separate at the pumping chambers. A hole in it lets the two kinds of blood mix.","notes":["The pulmonary trunk and the aorta are linked before birth by the ductus arteriosus, a vessel outside the heart. Pick this for a question about a patent ductus arteriosus, the fetal shunt that failed to close.","The atria are separated by the interatrial septum, not the interventricular septum. A hole there, such as a foramen ovale that never closed, would be the answer if the defect were between the atria.","The left ventricle and the aorta are separated by the aortic valve, not by a septum. Choose this pairing for a question about what happens when the aortic valve fails to close.","Correct. The septum divides the two ventricles, so a hole there mixes right ventricle blood with left ventricle blood."],"lt":"t-cv-chambers","lid":"cv-chambers-r5","comp":57,"competencyId":"cv-chambers"}]},{"id":"m3-heart-valves-flow","title":"Heart valves and blood flow through the heart","summary":"AV and semilunar valves, chordae tendineae, papillary muscles, pulmonary and systemic circuits, step-by-step blood pathway","mw":"2026-10-05","tr":"2026-10-01","cards":[{"id":"L01511","dok":1,"q":"Which chamber receives blood from the body through the superior and inferior venae cavae?","a":"The right atrium.","options":["The right atrium","The left ventricle","The right ventricle","The left atrium"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The superior and inferior venae cavae return oxygen-poor blood from the body, and both empty into the right atrium. This makes the right atrium the first stop in the heart for blood coming back from the systemic circuit. From there, blood passes the tricuspid valve into the right ventricle.","notes":["Correct. Both venae cavae empty into the right atrium.","The left ventricle receives blood from the left atrium and pumps it into the aorta. Pick it if the question asked which chamber pumps blood to the body.","The right ventricle receives blood from the right atrium through the tricuspid valve, not directly from the venae cavae. Pick it if the question asked which chamber pumps blood to the lungs.","The left atrium receives oxygen-rich blood from the lungs through the four pulmonary veins. Pick it if the question asked where the pulmonary veins empty."],"lt":"t-heart","lid":"c16","comp":57,"competencyId":"cv-chambers"},{"id":"L01512","dok":1,"q":"Blood leaving the right ventricle enters which vessel?","a":"The pulmonary trunk.","options":["The pulmonary veins","The aorta","The pulmonary trunk","The superior vena cava"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"When the right ventricle contracts, blood passes through the pulmonary valve into the pulmonary trunk. The pulmonary trunk then splits into the right and left pulmonary arteries, which carry the oxygen-poor blood to the lungs. This is the start of the pulmonary circuit.","notes":["The pulmonary veins carry blood from the lungs back to the left atrium, so they bring blood into the heart. Pick them for the return trip from the lungs.","The aorta receives blood from the left ventricle and carries it to the body. Pick it if the question asked where blood goes when it leaves the left ventricle.","Correct. The right ventricle empties into the pulmonary trunk, which leads to the lungs.","The superior vena cava returns blood from the upper body to the right atrium. Pick it if the question asked which vessel drains the head and arms."],"lt":"t-heart","lid":"c17","comp":57,"competencyId":"cv-chambers"},{"id":"L01514","dok":1,"q":"Which valve lies between the right atrium and the right ventricle?","a":"The tricuspid valve.","options":["The aortic valve","The mitral valve","The tricuspid valve","The pulmonary valve"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The tricuspid valve is the right atrioventricular valve, sitting between the right atrium and the right ventricle. It has three cusps, which gives it its name. When the right ventricle contracts, the tricuspid valve closes so blood does not flow back into the right atrium.","notes":["The aortic valve is the semilunar valve at the exit of the left ventricle, leading into the aorta. Pick it for the left ventricle's outflow.","The mitral valve is the left atrioventricular valve, between the left atrium and left ventricle. Pick it if the question asked about the left side.","Correct. The tricuspid valve sits between the right atrium and right ventricle.","The pulmonary valve is the semilunar valve at the exit of the right ventricle, leading into the pulmonary trunk. Pick it if the question asked about the right ventricle's outflow."],"lt":"t-heart","lid":"c19","comp":58,"competencyId":"cv-valves"},{"id":"L01515","dok":1,"q":"Which valve lies between the left atrium and the left ventricle?","a":"The mitral valve.","options":["The pulmonary valve","The tricuspid valve","The mitral valve","The aortic valve"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The mitral valve is the left atrioventricular valve, between the left atrium and the left ventricle. It is also called the bicuspid valve because it has two cusps, and it is the only heart valve with two. It closes when the left ventricle contracts so blood does not flow back into the left atrium.","notes":["The pulmonary valve is the semilunar valve between the right ventricle and the pulmonary trunk. Pick it for the right ventricle's outflow.","The tricuspid valve is the right atrioventricular valve, between the right atrium and right ventricle. Pick it if the question asked about the right side.","Correct. The mitral valve sits between the left atrium and left ventricle.","The aortic valve is the semilunar valve between the left ventricle and the aorta. Pick it if the question asked which valve blood crosses as it leaves the left ventricle."],"lt":"t-heart","lid":"c20","comp":58,"competencyId":"cv-valves"},{"id":"L01516","dok":1,"q":"Which valve guards the exit from the right ventricle into the pulmonary trunk?","a":"The pulmonary valve.","options":["The pulmonary valve","The tricuspid valve","The mitral valve","The aortic valve"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The pulmonary valve is a semilunar valve with three pocket-shaped cusps, sitting at the outflow of the right ventricle where the pulmonary trunk begins. It opens when the right ventricle contracts and closes afterward so blood in the pulmonary trunk cannot fall back into the ventricle. Each ventricle has an inflow valve and an outflow valve, and the pulmonary valve is the right ventricle's outflow valve.","notes":["Correct. The pulmonary valve guards the right ventricle's exit into the pulmonary trunk.","The tricuspid valve is the inflow valve of the right ventricle, between the right atrium and right ventricle. Pick it if the question asked which valve blood crosses to enter the right ventricle.","The mitral valve is the inflow valve of the left ventricle, between the left atrium and left ventricle. Pick it for the left side's atrioventricular valve.","The aortic valve guards the exit of the left ventricle into the aorta. Pick it if the question asked about the left ventricle's outflow."],"lt":"t-heart","lid":"c21","comp":58,"competencyId":"cv-valves"},{"id":"L01517","dok":1,"q":"Which valve guards the exit from the left ventricle into the aorta?","a":"The aortic valve.","options":["The pulmonary valve","The tricuspid valve","The aortic valve","The mitral valve"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The aortic valve is the semilunar valve at the outflow of the left ventricle, where the aorta begins. When the left ventricle contracts, the aortic valve opens and blood surges into the aorta. When the ventricle relaxes, the valve closes so blood in the aorta cannot flow back into the ventricle.","notes":["The pulmonary valve guards the right ventricle's exit into the pulmonary trunk. Pick it if the question asked about the right ventricle's outflow.","The tricuspid valve is the right atrioventricular valve, between the right atrium and right ventricle. Pick it for the right side's inflow valve.","Correct. The aortic valve guards the left ventricle's exit into the aorta.","The mitral valve is the inflow valve of the left ventricle, between the left atrium and left ventricle. Pick it if the question asked which valve blood crosses to enter the left ventricle."],"lt":"t-heart","lid":"c22","comp":58,"competencyId":"cv-valves"},{"id":"L01518","dok":1,"q":"How many cusps does the tricuspid valve have?","a":"Three.","options":["Six","Three","Two","Four"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The tricuspid valve has three cusps, and its name says so: tri means three and cusp means a flap. A cusp is one of the leaflets that meet to close a valve opening. The tricuspid valve is the right atrioventricular valve, and its three cusps are tethered by chordae tendineae to papillary muscles in the right ventricle.","notes":["Six is a misconception, perhaps from adding the cusps of two valves together. A single valve has two or three cusps.","Correct. The tricuspid valve has three cusps, as its name says.","Two cusps belong to the mitral valve, which is why it is also called the bicuspid valve. Pick two if the question asked about the left atrioventricular valve.","No heart valve normally has four cusps. Students who choose four may be counting the four valves of the heart rather than the flaps of one valve."],"lt":"t-heart","lid":"c23","comp":58,"competencyId":"cv-valves"},{"id":"L01519","dok":2,"q":"Which two valves are the atrioventricular valves?","a":"The tricuspid and mitral valves.","options":["The pulmonary and aortic valves","The tricuspid and mitral valves","The tricuspid and pulmonary valves","The mitral and aortic valves"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Atrioventricular valves sit between an atrium and a ventricle, which is what the name means. The tricuspid valve is on the right side and the mitral valve is on the left side. Both have chordae tendineae and papillary muscles that hold them shut when the ventricles contract.","notes":["The pulmonary and aortic valves are the semilunar valves, at the exits of the ventricles into the great arteries. Pick this pair if the question asked which valves are semilunar.","Correct. The tricuspid and mitral valves sit between the atria and ventricles.","This pair joins the right ventricle's inflow and outflow valves. It would fit a question about the two valves of the right ventricle, but only the tricuspid is atrioventricular.","This pair joins the left ventricle's inflow and outflow valves. It would fit a question about the two valves of the left ventricle, but only the mitral is atrioventricular."],"lt":"t-heart","lid":"c24","comp":58,"competencyId":"cv-valves"},{"id":"L01520","dok":1,"q":"Which structures are the strong fibrous cords that tie the cusps of an atrioventricular valve to the ventricle wall?","a":"The chordae tendineae.","options":["The papillary muscles","The trabeculae carneae","The chordae tendineae","The pectinate muscles"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The chordae tendineae are thin, strong cords of fibrous tissue that run from the edges of the atrioventricular valve cusps down to the papillary muscles. They work like the strings of a parachute, keeping the cusps from flipping back into the atrium when the ventricle contracts. Their name means tendinous cords, which describes their tough, string-like look.","notes":["The papillary muscles are the cone-shaped muscle projections that the chordae tendineae attach to. They anchor the cords rather than being the cords, so pick them if the question asked what the chordae attach to.","The trabeculae carneae are irregular muscular ridges on the inner walls of the ventricles. Pick them if the question asked about the ridged texture of the ventricle walls.","Correct. The chordae tendineae are the fibrous cords that tie the cusps to the ventricle wall.","The pectinate muscles are parallel muscular ridges on the walls of the atria and auricles. Pick them for a question about the ridges inside the atria."],"lt":"t-heart","lid":"c25","comp":59,"competencyId":"cv-valve-support"},{"id":"L01521","dok":1,"q":"What are the cone-shaped muscle projections of the ventricle wall that anchor the chordae tendineae?","a":"The papillary muscles.","options":["The trabeculae carneae","The papillary muscles","The pectinate muscles","The auricles"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The papillary muscles are cone-shaped projections that rise from the inner wall of each ventricle. The chordae tendineae run from their tips to the cusps of the atrioventricular valves. When the ventricle contracts, the papillary muscles tighten the cords so the valve stays shut against the pressure.","notes":["The trabeculae carneae are the general irregular ridges covering the inner ventricle walls. They are in the same chamber, but they do not anchor the valve cords, so pick them for the ridged texture of the ventricle wall.","Correct. The papillary muscles anchor the chordae tendineae.","The pectinate muscles are parallel ridges on the walls of the atria and auricles, not in the ventricles. Pick them for a question about the comb-like ridges inside the atria.","The auricles are wrinkled pouches on the outside of each atrium. Pick them if the question asked about the ear-shaped flaps on the atria."],"lt":"t-heart","lid":"c26","comp":59,"competencyId":"cv-valve-support"},{"id":"L01530","dok":2,"q":"When the left ventricle contracts, which valve closes to keep blood from flowing back into the left atrium?","a":"The mitral valve.","options":["The tricuspid valve","The pulmonary valve","The aortic valve","The mitral valve"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Each valve prevents backflow into the chamber behind it. The mitral valve sits between the left atrium and left ventricle, so when the left ventricle squeezes, the mitral valve snaps shut and blood cannot return to the left atrium. Blood is then forced out through the open aortic valve instead.","notes":["The tricuspid valve prevents backflow into the right atrium when the right ventricle contracts. Pick it for the right side.","The pulmonary valve guards the exit of the right ventricle. Pick it for backflow from the pulmonary trunk into the right ventricle.","The aortic valve opens, not closes, when the left ventricle contracts, letting blood into the aorta. It closes afterward to prevent backflow into the left ventricle, so pick it for backflow from the aorta.","Correct. The mitral valve closes to prevent backflow into the left atrium."],"lt":"t-heart","lid":"c35","comp":58,"competencyId":"cv-valves"},{"id":"L01532","dok":2,"q":"Which two valves have pocket-shaped cusps and no chordae tendineae?","a":"The pulmonary and aortic valves.","options":["The mitral and aortic valves","The tricuspid and mitral valves","The tricuspid and pulmonary valves","The pulmonary and aortic valves"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The pulmonary and aortic valves are the semilunar valves, named for their half-moon shaped cusps. Each cusp is a small pocket that fills with blood when flow tries to reverse, and the filled pockets press together to seal the opening. Because they close themselves this way, they need no chordae tendineae or papillary muscles.","notes":["This pair mixes an atrioventricular valve, the mitral, with a semilunar valve, the aortic. It would fit a question about the two valves of the left ventricle.","The tricuspid and mitral valves are the atrioventricular valves, which do have chordae tendineae and papillary muscles. Pick this pair if the question asked which valves are held shut by cords.","This pair mixes an atrioventricular valve, the tricuspid, with a semilunar valve, the pulmonary. It would fit a question about the two valves of the right ventricle.","Correct. The semilunar valves have pocket-shaped cusps and no chordae tendineae."],"lt":"t-heart","lid":"c37","comp":58,"competencyId":"cv-valves"},{"id":"L01539","dok":1,"q":"The mitral valve is also known by which other name?","a":"The bicuspid valve.","options":["The tricuspid valve","The pulmonary semilunar valve","The aortic semilunar valve","The bicuspid valve"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The mitral valve is the left atrioventricular valve, and it has two cusps, so it is also called the bicuspid valve. The name mitral comes from its resemblance to a bishop's mitre, a two-pointed hat. It is the only heart valve with two cusps, which is a handy way to tell it apart from the other three.","notes":["The tricuspid valve is the right atrioventricular valve, with three cusps. Pick it for the valve between the right atrium and right ventricle.","The pulmonary semilunar valve guards the exit of the right ventricle into the pulmonary trunk. Pick it for the right ventricle's outflow.","The aortic semilunar valve guards the exit of the left ventricle into the aorta. It is on the same side of the heart as the mitral valve but at the outflow, so pick it for the left ventricle's exit.","Correct. The mitral valve is also called the bicuspid valve because it has two cusps."],"lt":"t-heart","lid":"c44","comp":58,"competencyId":"cv-valves"},{"id":"L01541","dok":2,"q":"When the right ventricle contracts, which valve closes to keep blood from flowing back into the right atrium?","a":"The tricuspid valve.","options":["The mitral valve","The tricuspid valve","The aortic valve","The pulmonary valve"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The tricuspid valve sits between the right atrium and right ventricle. When the right ventricle contracts, rising pressure pushes the three cusps together, and the chordae tendineae keep them from flipping back. This seals the opening so blood leaves through the pulmonary valve instead of returning to the right atrium.","notes":["The mitral valve prevents backflow into the left atrium when the left ventricle contracts. Pick it for the left side.","Correct. The tricuspid valve closes to stop backflow into the right atrium.","The aortic valve guards the exit of the left ventricle. Pick it for backflow from the aorta into the left ventricle.","The pulmonary valve opens when the right ventricle contracts, letting blood into the pulmonary trunk. It closes afterward to prevent backflow into the right ventricle, so pick it for backflow from the pulmonary trunk."],"lt":"t-heart","lid":"c46","comp":58,"competencyId":"cv-valves"},{"id":"L01543","dok":2,"q":"Starting in the right atrium, which three structures does blood pass through next, in order?","a":"The tricuspid valve, the right ventricle, then the pulmonary valve.","options":["The tricuspid valve, the right ventricle, then the pulmonary valve","The tricuspid valve, the right ventricle, then the aortic valve","The pulmonary valve, the right ventricle, then the tricuspid valve","The mitral valve, the right ventricle, then the pulmonary valve"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"From the right atrium, blood drops through the tricuspid valve into the right ventricle. The right ventricle then contracts and pushes the blood up through the pulmonary valve into the pulmonary trunk. Each side of the heart follows the same pattern: atrium, atrioventricular valve, ventricle, semilunar valve.","notes":["Correct. Blood passes the tricuspid valve, the right ventricle, and then the pulmonary valve.","The first two steps are right, but the aortic valve is on the left side, at the exit of the left ventricle. The right ventricle's exit valve is the pulmonary valve.","This order runs backward, putting the exit valve first and the entry valve last. Blood enters the right ventricle through the tricuspid valve and leaves through the pulmonary valve.","The mitral valve is the left atrioventricular valve, between the left atrium and left ventricle. The valve between the right atrium and right ventricle is the tricuspid."],"lt":"t-heart","lid":"c48","comp":61,"competencyId":"cv-blood-pathway"},{"id":"L01544","dok":2,"q":"After blood leaves the lungs, which three heart structures does it pass through, in order, before reaching the aortic valve?","a":"The left atrium, the mitral valve, then the left ventricle.","options":["The left ventricle, the mitral valve, then the left atrium","The left atrium, the mitral valve, then the left ventricle","The right atrium, the tricuspid valve, then the right ventricle","The left atrium, the tricuspid valve, then the left ventricle"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Blood returning from the lungs through the pulmonary veins enters the left atrium. It then passes through the mitral valve into the left ventricle, which contracts and pushes it through the aortic valve into the aorta. This is the left-side version of the atrium, valve, ventricle pattern.","notes":["This order runs backward. Blood fills the atrium first and then drops into the ventricle, so the left atrium must come before the left ventricle.","Correct. After the lungs, blood passes the left atrium, the mitral valve, and the left ventricle.","This is the sequence for blood returning from the body, on the right side of the heart. Pick it if the question started with blood arriving through the venae cavae.","The chambers are right, but the tricuspid valve is on the right side of the heart. The valve between the left atrium and left ventricle is the mitral valve."],"lt":"t-heart","lid":"c49","comp":61,"competencyId":"cv-blood-pathway"},{"id":"L01545","dok":2,"q":"Which sequence correctly traces blood from the right ventricle to the left atrium?","a":"Pulmonary valve, pulmonary trunk, lungs, pulmonary veins, left atrium.","options":["Aortic valve, aorta, lungs, pulmonary veins, left atrium","Pulmonary valve, pulmonary trunk, lungs, venae cavae, left atrium","Pulmonary valve, pulmonary trunk, lungs, pulmonary veins, left atrium","Tricuspid valve, pulmonary trunk, lungs, pulmonary veins, left atrium"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"This route is the pulmonary circuit. Blood leaves the right ventricle through the pulmonary valve into the pulmonary trunk, travels in the pulmonary arteries to the lungs, picks up oxygen, and returns through the pulmonary veins to the left atrium. Everything in this loop carries the word pulmonary, which makes it easier to trace.","notes":["The aortic valve and aorta carry blood out of the left ventricle to the body, not to the lungs. Pick this start for blood leaving the left ventricle on the systemic circuit.","The venae cavae return blood from the body to the right atrium, not from the lungs to the left atrium. Blood from the lungs returns through the pulmonary veins.","Correct. This is the pulmonary circuit from the right ventricle to the left atrium.","The tricuspid valve is the entrance to the right ventricle, not its exit. Blood leaves the right ventricle through the pulmonary valve."],"lt":"t-heart","lid":"c50","comp":61,"competencyId":"cv-blood-pathway"},{"id":"L01546","dok":2,"q":"Which set correctly names the left ventricle's inflow valve, its outflow valve, and the great vessel it pumps into?","a":"Mitral valve in, aortic valve out, into the aorta.","options":["Mitral valve in, aortic valve out, into the aorta","Mitral valve in, pulmonary valve out, into the pulmonary trunk","Tricuspid valve in, pulmonary valve out, into the pulmonary trunk","Aortic valve in, mitral valve out, into the aorta"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Blood enters the left ventricle from the left atrium through the mitral valve. When the left ventricle contracts, the mitral valve closes and blood leaves through the aortic valve into the aorta. Pairing each ventricle with its inflow valve, outflow valve, and great vessel is a fast way to check any blood flow question.","notes":["Correct. The left ventricle fills through the mitral valve and empties through the aortic valve into the aorta.","The inflow valve is right, but the pulmonary valve and pulmonary trunk belong to the right ventricle. The left ventricle's outflow is the aortic valve into the aorta.","This is the full set for the right ventricle. Pick it if the question asked about the right ventricle's inflow valve, outflow valve, and great vessel.","This set swaps the roles of the two valves. The mitral valve lets blood in from the left atrium, and the aortic valve lets blood out, so the order here runs backward."],"lt":"t-heart","lid":"c51","comp":58,"competencyId":"cv-valves"},{"id":"L01547","dok":2,"q":"Which set correctly names the right ventricle's inflow valve, its outflow valve, and the great vessel it pumps into?","a":"Tricuspid valve in, pulmonary valve out, into the pulmonary trunk.","options":["Tricuspid valve in, pulmonary valve out, into the pulmonary trunk","Mitral valve in, aortic valve out, into the aorta","Pulmonary valve in, tricuspid valve out, into the pulmonary trunk","Tricuspid valve in, aortic valve out, into the aorta"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Blood enters the right ventricle from the right atrium through the tricuspid valve. When the right ventricle contracts, it pushes blood through the pulmonary valve into the pulmonary trunk, which carries it toward the lungs. Everything on the outflow side of the right ventricle is named pulmonary, which helps keep the sides apart.","notes":["Correct. The right ventricle fills through the tricuspid valve and empties through the pulmonary valve into the pulmonary trunk.","This is the full set for the left ventricle. Pick it if the question asked about the left ventricle's inflow valve, outflow valve, and great vessel.","This set swaps the two valves. The tricuspid valve lets blood in from the right atrium, and the pulmonary valve lets blood out, so the order here runs backward.","The inflow valve is right, but the aortic valve and aorta belong to the left ventricle. The right ventricle sends blood to the lungs, not the body."],"lt":"t-heart","lid":"c52","comp":58,"competencyId":"cv-valves"},{"id":"L01556","dok":2,"q":"Which heart valves have chordae tendineae attached to their cusps?","a":"Only the tricuspid and mitral valves.","options":["All four heart valves","Only the pulmonary and aortic valves","Only the mitral valve","Only the tricuspid and mitral valves"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Chordae tendineae belong only to the atrioventricular valves, the tricuspid and mitral. These valves face the full force of ventricular contraction and need cords and papillary muscles to keep their cusps from flipping into the atria. The semilunar valves have pocket-shaped cusps that fill with blood and close themselves, so they have no chordae.","notes":["Only two of the four valves have chordae. The semilunar valves seal by filling their pocket-shaped cusps, so they need no cords.","The pulmonary and aortic valves are the semilunar valves, the two that lack chordae tendineae. Pick this pair if the question asked which valves close without cords.","The mitral valve does have chordae, but so does the tricuspid valve on the right side. Both atrioventricular valves are anchored the same way.","Correct. Only the atrioventricular valves, tricuspid and mitral, have chordae tendineae."],"lt":"t-heart","lid":"c15-x2","comp":59,"competencyId":"cv-valve-support"},{"id":"L01558","dok":1,"q":"Which chamber pumps blood to the lungs through the pulmonary trunk?","a":"The right ventricle.","options":["The right ventricle","The left atrium","The right atrium","The left ventricle"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The right ventricle receives oxygen-poor blood from the right atrium and pumps it through the pulmonary valve into the pulmonary trunk. The trunk divides into the pulmonary arteries that carry the blood to the lungs. Because the lungs are close by and the pulmonary circuit is low-pressure, the right ventricle's wall is thinner than the left's.","notes":["Correct. The right ventricle pumps blood into the pulmonary trunk toward the lungs.","The left atrium receives blood returning from the lungs through the pulmonary veins. Pick it for the chamber at the end of the pulmonary circuit.","The right atrium passes blood down into the right ventricle through the tricuspid valve. It is one step earlier, so pick it for the chamber that receives the venae cavae.","The left ventricle pumps blood into the aorta and out to the body. Pick it if the question asked which chamber drives the systemic circuit."],"lt":"t-heart","lid":"c17-x2","comp":57,"competencyId":"cv-chambers"},{"id":"L01559","dok":2,"q":"Which valve prevents blood in the aorta from flowing back into the left ventricle?","a":"The aortic valve.","options":["The mitral valve","The pulmonary valve","The aortic valve","The tricuspid valve"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The aortic valve is the semilunar valve between the left ventricle and the aorta. After the left ventricle contracts and relaxes, blood in the aorta tries to fall back, and that backflow fills the valve's pocket-shaped cusps and snaps them shut. Each valve guards against backflow into the chamber just behind it.","notes":["The mitral valve prevents backflow from the left ventricle into the left atrium. Pick it if the question asked which valve protects the left atrium.","The pulmonary valve prevents backflow from the pulmonary trunk into the right ventricle. Pick it for the right ventricle's outflow.","Correct. The aortic valve stops backflow from the aorta into the left ventricle.","The tricuspid valve prevents backflow from the right ventricle into the right atrium. Pick it for the right atrioventricular opening."],"lt":"t-heart","lid":"c18-x2","comp":58,"competencyId":"cv-valves"},{"id":"L01561","dok":1,"q":"Which circuit does the right side of the heart drive?","a":"The pulmonary circuit.","options":["The systemic circuit","The pulmonary circuit","The coronary circulation","The hepatic portal system"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The right side of the heart receives oxygen-poor blood from the body and pumps it to the lungs and back, which is the pulmonary circuit. The left side pumps oxygen-rich blood out to the body and back, which is the systemic circuit. The two sides work side by side, so every drop of blood passes through both circuits in turn.","notes":["The systemic circuit is driven by the left side of the heart, carrying blood to the body through the aorta. Pick it if the question asked about the left side.","Correct. The right side of the heart drives the pulmonary circuit to the lungs.","The coronary circulation is the heart wall's own blood supply, a branch of the systemic circuit fed by the aorta. Pick it for a question about the vessels that supply the myocardium.","The hepatic portal system carries blood from the digestive organs to the liver before it returns to the heart. Pick it for a question about blood flow through the liver."],"lt":"t-heart","lid":"c20-x2","comp":69,"competencyId":"bvn-circulatory-routes"},{"id":"L01567","dok":2,"q":"A drop of blood is being pumped out of the right ventricle. Which structure does it pass through next?","a":"The pulmonary valve.","options":["The tricuspid valve","The pulmonary valve","The aortic valve","The mitral valve"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"When the right ventricle contracts, blood leaves through the pulmonary valve, the semilunar valve at the ventricle's exit, and enters the pulmonary trunk. From there it travels to the lungs. The pulmonary valve then closes so the blood cannot fall back into the right ventricle.","notes":["The tricuspid valve is where the blood entered the right ventricle, one step earlier. It closes during contraction, so pick it for blood moving from the right atrium into the right ventricle.","Correct. Blood leaving the right ventricle passes through the pulmonary valve next.","The aortic valve is the exit of the left ventricle. The drop will reach it much later, after the lungs and the left side of the heart.","The mitral valve is between the left atrium and left ventricle. The drop reaches it after returning from the lungs, so pick it for the step after the left atrium."],"lt":"t-heart","lid":"c26-x2","comp":61,"competencyId":"cv-blood-pathway"},{"id":"L01568","dok":2,"q":"A drop of blood has just returned from the lungs through the pulmonary veins. Which chamber does it enter?","a":"The left atrium.","options":["The right atrium","The right ventricle","The left atrium","The left ventricle"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The four pulmonary veins carry oxygen-rich blood from the lungs into the left atrium. This is the end of the pulmonary circuit and the start of the left side of the heart. From the left atrium, the blood passes the mitral valve into the left ventricle.","notes":["The right atrium receives oxygen-poor blood from the body through the venae cavae and the coronary sinus. Pick it for blood returning from the systemic circuit.","The right ventricle receives blood from the right atrium and pumps it toward the lungs. Pick it for the chamber that sends blood out in the pulmonary trunk.","Correct. The pulmonary veins empty into the left atrium.","The left ventricle is one step later, receiving blood from the left atrium through the mitral valve. Pick it for the chamber that pumps blood into the aorta."],"lt":"t-heart","lid":"c27-x2","comp":61,"competencyId":"cv-blood-pathway"},{"id":"L01571","dok":2,"q":"A drop of blood in the right atrium moves down into the right ventricle. Which valve does it cross?","a":"The tricuspid valve.","options":["The mitral valve","The aortic valve","The tricuspid valve","The pulmonary valve"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Between the right atrium and right ventricle sits the tricuspid valve, the right atrioventricular valve. Blood passes through it as it drops from the atrium into the ventricle. When the ventricle then contracts, the tricuspid valve closes so the blood goes forward into the pulmonary trunk instead of back.","notes":["The mitral valve is the atrioventricular valve on the left side, between the left atrium and left ventricle. Pick it for the same step on the left side of the heart.","The aortic valve is the exit of the left ventricle into the aorta. Pick it for blood leaving the left ventricle.","Correct. Blood moving from the right atrium to the right ventricle crosses the tricuspid valve.","The pulmonary valve is the exit of the right ventricle, one step later. Pick it for blood leaving the right ventricle."],"lt":"t-heart","lid":"c30-x2","comp":61,"competencyId":"cv-blood-pathway"},{"id":"L01572","dok":2,"q":"The atrioventricular valves are tied to papillary muscles by chordae tendineae. Why are these cords needed when the ventricles contract forcefully?","a":"They keep the valve cusps from flipping back into the atria when ventricular pressure rises.","options":["They carry the electrical signal from the atria to the ventricles","They stop the cusps from flipping back into the atria","They anchor the semilunar valves to the walls of the great arteries","They pull the valve cusps open so blood can enter the ventricles"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"When a ventricle contracts, the pressure inside it rises sharply and pushes the atrioventricular cusps upward toward the atrium. The chordae tendineae, held taut by the contracting papillary muscles, act like the strings of a parachute and stop the cusps from turning inside out. If a cord or papillary muscle fails, the valve leaks and blood flows back into the atrium.","notes":["The signal from the atria to the ventricles travels through the conduction system, by way of the AV node and AV bundle. Pick the AV bundle for that pathway, since the chordae are passive fibrous cords.","Correct. The chordae keep the cusps from flipping into the atria under high ventricular pressure.","The semilunar valves have no chordae tendineae. Their pocket-shaped cusps close themselves when blood fills them, so this choice confuses the two kinds of valve.","The atrioventricular valves open on their own when pressure in the atrium is higher than in the ventricle. The cords hold the cusps back rather than pulling them open."],"lt":"t-heart","lid":"d3-t-heart-1","comp":59,"competencyId":"cv-valve-support"},{"id":"L01575","dok":2,"q":"Heart valves have no muscle of their own and are not moved by nerves. How do they still keep blood moving in only one direction?","a":"Each valve is pushed open when pressure behind it is higher and pushed shut when the pressure reverses.","options":["Muscle within each cusp contracts to push blood forward","The valves stay open, and the order of chamber contraction sets the direction","Nerves open and close each valve in time with the heartbeat","Pressure differences push each valve open and then shut"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Valves are passive one-way gates moved by blood pressure. When pressure behind a valve is higher than pressure in front of it, blood pushes the cusps open and flows forward. When pressure reverses, the backflow catches the cusps and presses them shut, so blood cannot go the wrong way.","notes":["Valve cusps are made of connective tissue covered by endocardium, with no muscle inside. The papillary muscles are in the ventricle wall and only hold the cords taut.","If the valves stayed open, blood would slosh back and forth with every contraction. Closing valves are what keep the flow one way, which is why a leaky valve causes backflow.","Nerves can change heart rate, but they do not open or close individual valves. Students who choose this are picturing valves as actively controlled doors.","Correct. Pressure differences open and close the valves, allowing only forward flow."],"lt":"t-heart","lid":"d3-t-heart-4","comp":58,"competencyId":"cv-valves"},{"id":"L03332","dok":1,"q":"Which circuit is driven by the right side of the heart?","a":"The pulmonary circuit.","options":["The hepatic portal system","The pulmonary circuit","The coronary circuit","The systemic circuit"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The right side of the heart receives oxygen-poor blood from the body and pumps it through the pulmonary trunk to the lungs. That loop, from the heart to the lungs and back, is the pulmonary circuit. The left side drives the other loop, the systemic circuit to the body.","notes":["The hepatic portal system carries blood from the digestive organs to the liver. Pick it for a question about blood draining the gut.","Correct. The right side pumps blood to the lungs in the pulmonary circuit.","The coronary circulation is the heart's own blood supply, which branches from the base of the aorta. Pick it for the vessels feeding the heart wall.","The systemic circuit is driven by the left side of the heart, which pumps blood into the aorta. Pick it for the left ventricle's job."],"lt":"t-heart-gross-anatomy","lid":"W3-005","comp":69,"competencyId":"bvn-circulatory-routes"},{"id":"L03333","dok":1,"q":"Which circuit is driven by the left side of the heart?","a":"The systemic circuit.","options":["The hepatic portal system","The systemic circuit","The pulmonary circuit","The coronary circulation"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The left side of the heart receives oxygen-rich blood from the lungs and pumps it out through the aorta to every tissue of the body. That loop, from the heart to the body and back, is the systemic circuit. Because this circuit is so long, the left ventricle has the thickest wall of the four chambers.","notes":["The hepatic portal system carries blood from the digestive organs to the liver before it returns to the heart. Pick it for blood draining the gut.","Correct. The left side pumps blood into the aorta for the systemic circuit.","The pulmonary circuit is driven by the right side, which pumps blood to the lungs. Pick it for the right ventricle's job.","The coronary circulation is the heart wall's own supply, running from the coronary arteries to the coronary sinus. Pick it for the vessels that feed the myocardium."],"lt":"t-heart-gross-anatomy","lid":"W3-006","comp":69,"competencyId":"bvn-circulatory-routes"},{"id":"L03334","dok":1,"q":"Where does the pulmonary circuit carry blood?","a":"To the lungs and back to the heart.","options":["To the body tissues and back","To the heart wall and back","To the liver and back","To the lungs and back"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The pulmonary circuit carries oxygen-poor blood from the right ventricle through the pulmonary trunk and pulmonary arteries to the lungs. There the blood picks up O2 and releases CO2, then returns through the pulmonary veins to the left atrium. Pulmo means lung, which is a quick way to remember this circuit.","notes":["Carrying blood to the body tissues and back is the systemic circuit. Pick it for the loop that starts at the left ventricle and aorta.","Carrying blood to the heart wall and back is the coronary circulation. Pick it for the coronary arteries and cardiac veins.","Carrying blood from the gut to the liver is the hepatic portal system. Pick it for a question about nutrient-rich blood reaching the liver.","Correct. The pulmonary circuit is the loop to the lungs and back."],"lt":"t-heart-gross-anatomy","lid":"W3-007","comp":69,"competencyId":"bvn-circulatory-routes"},{"id":"L03335","dok":1,"q":"Where does the systemic circuit carry blood?","a":"To the tissues of the body and back to the heart.","options":["To the body tissues and back","To the lungs and back","To the atria and back","To the lymph nodes and back"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The systemic circuit begins when the left ventricle pumps oxygen-rich blood into the aorta. Branches of the aorta deliver that blood to tissues throughout the body, and oxygen-poor blood returns through the venae cavae to the right atrium. The word systemic refers to the whole body system.","notes":["Correct. The systemic circuit carries blood to the body tissues and back.","Carrying blood to the lungs and back is the pulmonary circuit. Pick it for the loop driven by the right ventricle.","The atria are chambers inside the heart that receive blood from both circuits. They are not a destination of either circuit.","Lymph nodes filter lymph, not blood. Pick them for a question about where lymph is cleaned before it returns to the veins."],"lt":"t-heart-gross-anatomy","lid":"W3-008","comp":69,"competencyId":"bvn-circulatory-routes"},{"id":"L03354","dok":1,"q":"Which chamber receives oxygen-poor blood returning from the body?","a":"The right atrium.","options":["The left ventricle","The right ventricle","The left atrium","The right atrium"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Oxygen-poor blood from the body returns through the superior and inferior venae cavae, and from the heart wall through the coronary sinus, into the right atrium. This is the first step of the pathway of blood through the heart. From the right atrium, blood passes through the tricuspid valve into the right ventricle.","notes":["The left ventricle receives blood from the left atrium and pumps it to the body. Pick it for the chamber that sends blood into the aorta.","The right ventricle receives blood from the right atrium, not directly from the body. Pick it for the chamber that pumps blood to the lungs.","The left atrium receives oxygen-rich blood from the lungs through the pulmonary veins. Pick it for blood returning from the pulmonary circuit.","Correct. The right atrium receives systemic venous return."],"lt":"t-heart-gross-anatomy","lid":"W3-028","comp":57,"competencyId":"cv-chambers"},{"id":"L03355","dok":1,"q":"Which chamber receives blood returning from the lungs?","a":"The left atrium.","options":["The right ventricle","The right atrium","The left ventricle","The left atrium"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"After picking up O2 in the lungs, blood returns through the four pulmonary veins into the left atrium. This oxygen-rich blood then passes through the mitral valve into the left ventricle. The left atrium is the receiving chamber at the end of the pulmonary circuit.","notes":["The right ventricle pumps blood to the lungs, not from them. Pick it for the chamber that sends blood into the pulmonary trunk.","The right atrium receives oxygen-poor blood from the body through the venae cavae. Pick it for blood returning from the systemic circuit.","The left ventricle receives blood from the left atrium, one step later. Pick it for the chamber that pumps blood into the aorta.","Correct. The left atrium receives oxygen-rich blood from the pulmonary veins."],"lt":"t-heart-gross-anatomy","lid":"W3-029","comp":57,"competencyId":"cv-chambers"},{"id":"L03356","dok":1,"q":"Which chamber pumps blood to the lungs?","a":"The right ventricle.","options":["The left ventricle","The right atrium","The right ventricle","The left atrium"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The right ventricle contracts and pushes oxygen-poor blood through the pulmonary valve into the pulmonary trunk, which branches into the pulmonary arteries to the lungs. This starts the pulmonary circuit. The right ventricle receives that blood from the right atrium.","notes":["The left ventricle pumps blood into the aorta for the body. Pick it for the systemic circuit.","The right atrium receives blood from the body and passes it to the right ventricle. It pushes blood only a short distance, so pick it for the chamber receiving venous return.","Correct. The right ventricle pumps blood to the lungs.","The left atrium receives blood returning from the lungs. Pick it for the end of the pulmonary circuit, not its start."],"lt":"t-heart-gross-anatomy","lid":"W3-030","comp":57,"competencyId":"cv-chambers"},{"id":"L03357","dok":1,"q":"Which chamber pumps blood to the body?","a":"The left ventricle.","options":["The right ventricle","The right atrium","The left atrium","The left ventricle"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The left ventricle contracts with force and pushes oxygen-rich blood through the aortic valve into the aorta, which carries it to the whole body. This starts the systemic circuit. Because this circuit is long, the left ventricle has the thickest wall of the four chambers.","notes":["The right ventricle pumps blood to the lungs through the pulmonary trunk. Pick it for the pulmonary circuit.","The right atrium receives oxygen-poor blood from the body. Pick it for the chamber where the venae cavae empty.","The left atrium receives oxygen-rich blood from the lungs and passes it to the left ventricle. Pick it for the chamber receiving pulmonary venous return.","Correct. The left ventricle pumps blood to the body through the aorta."],"lt":"t-heart-gross-anatomy","lid":"W3-031","comp":57,"competencyId":"cv-chambers"},{"id":"L03375","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares the right and left sides of the heart?","a":"The right side drives the pulmonary circuit, and the left side drives the systemic circuit.","options":["The right side drives the pulmonary circuit, and the left drives the systemic","Both sides pump blood into the aorta at the same time","The right side carries oxygen-rich blood, and the left carries oxygen-poor blood","The right side drives the systemic circuit, and the left drives the pulmonary"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The right side of the heart receives oxygen-poor blood from the body and pumps it to the lungs, so it drives the pulmonary circuit. The left side receives oxygen-rich blood from the lungs and pumps it to the body, so it drives the systemic circuit. The heart is really two pumps side by side.","notes":["Correct. Right side to lungs, left side to body.","Only the left ventricle pumps into the aorta. The right ventricle pumps into the pulmonary trunk, so the two sides send blood to different circuits.","This reverses the oxygen content. The right side carries oxygen-poor blood from the body, and the left side carries oxygen-rich blood from the lungs.","This reverses the circuits. The right ventricle feeds the pulmonary trunk, and the left ventricle feeds the aorta."],"lt":"t-heart-gross-anatomy","lid":"W3-049","comp":69,"competencyId":"bvn-circulatory-routes"},{"id":"L03379","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares the pulmonary and systemic circuits?","a":"The pulmonary circuit runs heart to lungs to heart; the systemic circuit runs heart to body to heart.","options":["Pulmonary runs heart to lungs to heart, and systemic runs heart to body to heart","Pulmonary runs heart to body to heart, and systemic runs heart to lungs to heart","Pulmonary carries oxygen-rich blood away from the heart, and systemic carries oxygen-poor blood","Both circuits begin in the left ventricle and end in the right atrium"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The pulmonary circuit carries oxygen-poor blood from the right ventricle to the lungs and returns oxygen-rich blood to the left atrium. The systemic circuit carries oxygen-rich blood from the left ventricle to the body and returns oxygen-poor blood to the right atrium. Blood passes through both circuits in a figure eight, with the heart at the crossing point.","notes":["Correct. Pulmonary is heart to lungs to heart, and systemic is heart to body to heart.","This swaps the destinations. Pulmo means lung, so the pulmonary circuit is the loop to the lungs.","This reverses the oxygen content. Blood leaving in the pulmonary trunk is oxygen-poor, and blood leaving in the aorta is oxygen-rich.","Only the systemic circuit begins in the left ventricle and ends in the right atrium. The pulmonary circuit begins in the right ventricle and ends in the left atrium."],"lt":"t-heart-gross-anatomy","lid":"W3-053","comp":69,"competencyId":"bvn-circulatory-routes"},{"id":"L03380","dok":2,"q":"Blood returning from the lungs must reach the pump for the systemic circuit. Which chambers does it pass through, in order?","a":"The left atrium, then the left ventricle.","options":["The left ventricle, then the left atrium","The left atrium, then the left ventricle","The right ventricle, then the left atrium","The right atrium, then the right ventricle"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The pulmonary veins deliver oxygen-rich blood to the left atrium. From there it passes through the mitral valve into the left ventricle, which pumps it into the aorta for the systemic circuit. The left side of the heart is the link between pulmonary venous return and systemic pumping.","notes":["These are the right chambers in the wrong order. Blood always enters an atrium first and then flows down into the ventricle below it.","Correct. Left atrium first, then left ventricle.","The right ventricle and left atrium are not directly connected. Blood leaving the right ventricle must travel through the lungs before reaching the left atrium.","The right atrium and right ventricle handle blood returning from the body and pump it to the lungs. Pick this pair for the link between systemic return and pulmonary pumping."],"lt":"t-heart-gross-anatomy","lid":"W3-054","comp":61,"competencyId":"cv-blood-pathway"},{"id":"L03381","dok":2,"q":"Blood returning from the body must reach the pump for the pulmonary circuit. Which chambers does it pass through, in order?","a":"The right atrium, then the right ventricle.","options":["The right ventricle, then the right atrium","The left atrium, then the left ventricle","The right atrium, then the left atrium","The right atrium, then the right ventricle"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The venae cavae and coronary sinus deliver oxygen-poor blood to the right atrium. It passes through the tricuspid valve into the right ventricle, which pumps it into the pulmonary trunk toward the lungs. The right side of the heart links systemic venous return to pulmonary pumping.","notes":["These are the right chambers in the wrong order. Blood always fills an atrium first and then flows down into its ventricle.","The left atrium and left ventricle handle blood returning from the lungs and pump it to the body. Pick this pair for the link between pulmonary return and systemic pumping.","In the adult heart there is no opening between the atria. Blood crossed from the right atrium to the left only before birth, through the foramen ovale.","Correct. Right atrium first, then right ventricle."],"lt":"t-heart-gross-anatomy","lid":"W3-055","comp":61,"competencyId":"cv-blood-pathway"},{"id":"L03398","dok":2,"q":"Why does the aorta arise from the left ventricle rather than the right?","a":"The left ventricle holds oxygen-rich blood that is bound for the body.","options":["The left ventricle receives blood straight from the venae cavae","The left ventricle is the chamber nearest the lungs","The left ventricle holds oxygen-poor blood bound for the lungs","The left ventricle holds oxygen-rich blood bound for the body"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Blood reaching the left ventricle has just returned from the lungs through the left atrium, so it is rich in O2. The aorta carries blood to the tissues of the body, which need that oxygen. Connecting the aorta to the left ventricle sends freshly oxygenated blood into the systemic circuit.","notes":["The venae cavae empty into the right atrium, not the left ventricle. Pick the right atrium for that venous return.","Both ventricles sit beside the lungs, so nearness does not explain the connection. What matters is that the left ventricle holds blood that has already been to the lungs.","Oxygen-poor blood bound for the lungs is held in the right ventricle. Pick it for the chamber connected to the pulmonary trunk.","Correct. The left ventricle sends oxygen-rich blood to the body through the aorta."],"lt":"t-heart-gross-anatomy","lid":"W3-072","comp":62,"competencyId":"bvn-great-vessels"},{"id":"L03399","dok":2,"q":"Why does the pulmonary trunk arise from the right ventricle?","a":"The right ventricle holds oxygen-poor blood that must go to the lungs.","options":["The right ventricle receives blood from the pulmonary veins","The right ventricle has the thickest wall of the four chambers","The right ventricle holds oxygen-poor blood that must go to the lungs","The right ventricle holds oxygen-rich blood bound for the body"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The right ventricle receives oxygen-poor blood that has just returned from the body. That blood needs to pick up O2, so the right ventricle pumps it into the pulmonary trunk and on to the lungs. Linking the right ventricle to the pulmonary trunk sends used blood straight to be refreshed.","notes":["The pulmonary veins empty into the left atrium. Pick the left atrium for the chamber receiving blood from the lungs.","The left ventricle, not the right, has the thickest wall. Pick the left ventricle for the strongest pump.","Correct. Oxygen-poor blood in the right ventricle goes to the lungs through the pulmonary trunk.","Oxygen-rich blood bound for the body is held in the left ventricle. Pick it for the chamber connected to the aorta."],"lt":"t-heart-gross-anatomy","lid":"W3-073","comp":62,"competencyId":"bvn-great-vessels"},{"id":"L03400","dok":2,"q":"Which sequence correctly traces blood from the right atrium to the left ventricle?","a":"Right atrium, right ventricle, lungs, left atrium, left ventricle.","options":["Right atrium, right ventricle, left atrium, left ventricle","Right atrium, right ventricle, lungs, left atrium, left ventricle","Right atrium, left atrium, lungs, right ventricle, left ventricle","Right atrium, lungs, right ventricle, left atrium, left ventricle"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Blood entering the right atrium passes to the right ventricle, which pumps it through the pulmonary trunk to the lungs. It returns through the pulmonary veins to the left atrium and then flows into the left ventricle. The right and left sides are not directly connected in the adult heart, so the lungs must sit between them in the sequence.","notes":["This skips the lungs. In the adult heart, blood cannot go straight from the right ventricle to the left atrium; it must pass through the pulmonary circuit first.","Correct. Blood must pass through the lungs between the right and left sides.","Blood does not cross from the right atrium to the left atrium after birth. That shortcut existed only in the fetus through the foramen ovale.","The right atrium sends blood to the right ventricle first, and only the ventricle pumps it to the lungs. An atrium cannot send blood out of the heart."],"lt":"t-heart-gross-anatomy","lid":"W3-074","comp":61,"competencyId":"cv-blood-pathway"},{"id":"L03401","dok":2,"q":"A student writes: the left atrium pumps blood to the body. Which statement corrects this?","a":"The left atrium sends blood to the left ventricle, and the left ventricle pumps it to the body.","options":["The left atrium sends blood to the left ventricle, which pumps it to the body","The right atrium pumps blood to the body through the aorta","The left atrium sends blood to the right ventricle, which pumps it to the body","The left atrium sends blood to the lungs, which return it to the body"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Atria are receiving chambers that pass blood down to the ventricle on the same side. The left atrium sends oxygen-rich blood through the mitral valve into the left ventricle. Only the left ventricle pumps blood into the aorta and out to the body.","notes":["Correct. The left atrium fills the left ventricle, which pumps to the body.","The right atrium receives blood from the body and does not pump into the aorta. Only the left ventricle connects to the aorta.","The left atrium and right ventricle are not connected. Each atrium empties into the ventricle on its own side.","The left atrium receives blood from the lungs; it does not send blood to them. The right ventricle is the chamber that sends blood to the lungs."],"lt":"t-heart-gross-anatomy","lid":"W3-075","comp":57,"competencyId":"cv-chambers"},{"id":"L03402","dok":2,"q":"A student writes: the right atrium pumps blood to the lungs. Which statement corrects this?","a":"The right atrium sends blood to the right ventricle, and the right ventricle pumps it to the lungs.","options":["The left atrium pumps blood to the lungs through the pulmonary veins","The right atrium sends blood to the left atrium, which pumps it to the lungs","The right atrium sends blood to the left ventricle, which pumps it to the lungs","The right atrium sends blood to the right ventricle, which pumps it to the lungs"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The right atrium receives oxygen-poor blood from the body and passes it through the tricuspid valve to the right ventricle. The right ventricle then pumps that blood through the pulmonary valve into the pulmonary trunk and on to the lungs. Atria pass blood down; ventricles pump it out.","notes":["The pulmonary veins carry blood toward the left atrium, not away from it. The left atrium receives blood from the lungs instead of sending it there.","The atria are not connected in the adult heart. Blood crossed between the atria only before birth, through the foramen ovale.","The right atrium does not connect to the left ventricle, and the left ventricle pumps to the body. Each atrium empties into the ventricle on its own side.","Correct. The right atrium fills the right ventricle, which pumps to the lungs."],"lt":"t-heart-gross-anatomy","lid":"W3-076","comp":57,"competencyId":"cv-chambers"},{"id":"L03406","dok":2,"q":"Why is the heart described as a double pump?","a":"The right side pumps blood to the lungs, and the left side pumps blood to the body.","options":["It beats twice for every breath","It pumps blood and lymph through separate chambers","The atria and the ventricles each pump blood to the body","The right side pumps to the lungs, and the left side pumps to the body"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The heart is two pumps working side by side. The right side receives blood from the body and pumps it to the lungs in the pulmonary circuit, and the left side receives blood from the lungs and pumps it to the body in the systemic circuit. Both sides contract at the same time with each heartbeat.","notes":["Heart rate and breathing rate are not linked in a fixed two to one ratio. The word double refers to the two circuits, not to timing.","The heart pumps only blood. Lymph moves through lymphatic vessels without any heart chamber.","Only the left ventricle pumps blood to the body. The atria push blood only into the ventricles below them.","Correct. The two sides pump to two separate circuits."],"lt":"t-heart-gross-anatomy","lid":"W3-080","comp":69,"competencyId":"bvn-circulatory-routes"},{"id":"L03414","dok":2,"q":"If the pulmonary veins are blocked, which chamber receives less blood?","a":"The left atrium.","options":["The right ventricle","The left atrium","The left ventricle","The right atrium"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The four pulmonary veins carry oxygen-rich blood from the lungs into the left atrium. If they are blocked, the left atrium is the first chamber to receive less blood. The left ventricle would then fill less too, but only as a downstream effect.","notes":["The right ventricle receives blood from the right atrium and pumps toward the lungs. A pulmonary vein blockage lies downstream of it, so it does not receive less blood first.","Correct. The pulmonary veins drain directly into the left atrium.","The left ventricle would eventually fill less, but it receives blood from the left atrium, not directly from the pulmonary veins. Pick it for a blocked mitral valve.","The right atrium receives the venae cavae and coronary sinus, not the pulmonary veins. Pick it for a blockage of systemic venous return."],"lt":"t-heart-gross-anatomy","lid":"W3-088","comp":57,"competencyId":"cv-chambers"},{"id":"L03415","dok":2,"q":"If the pulmonary trunk is blocked, which chamber struggles to empty?","a":"The right ventricle.","options":["The right ventricle","The left ventricle","The left atrium","The right atrium"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The right ventricle pumps blood through the pulmonary valve into the pulmonary trunk. A blockage in the trunk means the right ventricle is pushing against resistance and cannot empty normally. The chamber that pumps directly into a blocked vessel is the one that struggles first.","notes":["Correct. The right ventricle pumps directly into the pulmonary trunk.","The left ventricle pumps into the aorta. Pick it for a blockage at the start of the aorta.","The left atrium receives blood from the lungs, downstream of the blockage. It would receive less blood, but it does not pump into the pulmonary trunk.","The right atrium empties into the right ventricle, not into the pulmonary trunk. It would be affected only later, as blood backs up."],"lt":"t-heart-gross-anatomy","lid":"W3-089","comp":57,"competencyId":"cv-chambers"},{"id":"L03416","dok":2,"q":"If the aorta is narrowed near its origin, which chamber has to work against increased resistance?","a":"The left ventricle.","options":["The left atrium","The right atrium","The left ventricle","The right ventricle"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The left ventricle pumps blood through the aortic valve directly into the aorta. A narrowing near the start of the aorta forces the left ventricle to push harder to move the same blood. Over time, this extra work can thicken the left ventricular wall.","notes":["The left atrium sends blood to the left ventricle, not into the aorta. It is one step upstream of the chamber that faces the resistance.","The right atrium receives blood from the body. Pick it for a blockage in the venae cavae.","Correct. The left ventricle pumps directly into the aorta.","The right ventricle pumps into the pulmonary trunk. Pick it for a narrowing at the start of the pulmonary circuit."],"lt":"t-heart-gross-anatomy","lid":"W3-090","comp":57,"competencyId":"cv-chambers"},{"id":"L03417","dok":2,"q":"Why would failure of the left ventricle reduce blood flow to the body's tissues?","a":"The left ventricle pumps blood into the aorta and the systemic circuit.","options":["It pumps blood into the aorta","It receives blood from the venae cavae","It pumps blood into the pulmonary trunk","It drains blood from the heart wall"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The left ventricle is the pump for the systemic circuit. It pushes oxygen-rich blood into the aorta, whose branches deliver it to every tissue. If the left ventricle fails, less blood enters the aorta, so the body's tissues receive less blood.","notes":["Correct. The left ventricle supplies the aorta and the whole systemic circuit.","The venae cavae empty into the right atrium. Pick the right atrium for the chamber receiving blood from the body.","The pulmonary trunk is fed by the right ventricle. Pick the right ventricle for the pump to the lungs.","Draining the heart wall is the job of the cardiac veins and the coronary sinus. Pick the coronary sinus for that drainage."],"lt":"t-heart-gross-anatomy","lid":"W3-091","comp":57,"competencyId":"cv-chambers"},{"id":"L03418","dok":2,"q":"Why would failure of the right ventricle reduce blood flow to the lungs?","a":"The right ventricle pumps blood into the pulmonary trunk and lungs.","options":["It pumps blood into the aorta","It holds the mitral valve","It receives blood from the pulmonary veins","It pumps blood into the pulmonary trunk"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The right ventricle is the pump for the pulmonary circuit. It pushes oxygen-poor blood through the pulmonary valve into the pulmonary trunk, which branches to the lungs. If it fails, less blood reaches the lungs to pick up oxygen.","notes":["The aorta is fed by the left ventricle. Pick the left ventricle for the pump to the body.","The mitral valve sits between the left atrium and left ventricle. The right ventricle receives blood through the tricuspid valve instead.","The pulmonary veins empty into the left atrium. Pick the left atrium for blood returning from the lungs.","Correct. The right ventricle supplies the pulmonary trunk and the lungs."],"lt":"t-heart-gross-anatomy","lid":"W3-092","comp":57,"competencyId":"cv-chambers"},{"id":"L03419","dok":2,"q":"A student says oxygen-rich blood enters the right atrium. Which statement corrects the mistake?","a":"The right atrium receives oxygen-poor blood from the body","options":["The right atrium receives oxygen-poor blood from the lungs","The right atrium receives no blood directly from vessels","The right atrium receives oxygen-rich blood from the lungs","The right atrium receives oxygen-poor blood from the body"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The right atrium is the receiving chamber for systemic venous return. The venae cavae and coronary sinus bring it blood that has already delivered its oxygen to the body's tissues, so that blood is oxygen-poor. Oxygen-rich blood from the lungs enters the left atrium instead.","notes":["The oxygen level is right, but blood from the lungs is oxygen-rich and enters the left atrium. This choice mixes up where the right atrium's blood comes from.","The right atrium receives blood directly from three large veins, the superior vena cava, inferior vena cava, and coronary sinus, plus a few small anterior cardiac veins.","Oxygen-rich blood from the lungs enters the left atrium through the pulmonary veins. This choice describes the left atrium.","Correct. The right atrium receives oxygen-poor blood from the body."],"lt":"t-heart-gross-anatomy","lid":"W3-093","comp":57,"competencyId":"cv-chambers"},{"id":"L03420","dok":2,"q":"A student says oxygen-poor blood enters the left atrium. Which statement corrects the mistake?","a":"The left atrium receives oxygen-rich blood from the pulmonary veins.","options":["The left atrium receives blood from the right ventricle","The left atrium receives oxygen-rich blood from the venae cavae","The left atrium receives oxygen-rich blood from the pulmonary veins","The left atrium receives oxygen-poor blood from the pulmonary veins"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Blood reaching the left atrium has just passed through the lungs, where it picked up O2 and released CO2. It arrives through the four pulmonary veins and is oxygen-rich. The left atrium then passes it to the left ventricle for delivery to the body.","notes":["The right ventricle pumps blood to the lungs, not into the left atrium. The lungs sit between these two chambers in the pathway.","The venae cavae carry oxygen-poor blood and empty into the right atrium. Pick them for the right atrium, not the left.","Correct. The pulmonary veins bring oxygen-rich blood to the left atrium.","The vessels are right, but the pulmonary veins carry blood that has just picked up oxygen in the lungs. This choice keeps the student's original mistake."],"lt":"t-heart-gross-anatomy","lid":"W3-094","comp":57,"competencyId":"cv-chambers"},{"id":"L03427","dok":1,"q":"How many valves are found in the heart?","a":"Four.","options":["Six","Three","Two","Four"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The heart has four valves: two atrioventricular (AV) valves between the atria and ventricles, and two semilunar valves where the ventricles meet their great arteries. The tricuspid and mitral valves are the AV valves, and the pulmonary and aortic valves are the semilunar valves. Together they keep blood moving in one direction.","notes":["Six is not a valve count for the heart. Students may reach it by mixing up valves with cusps or with the vessels that attach at the base.","Three may come from the tricuspid valve's three cusps. Cusps are the flaps of a single valve, not separate valves.","Two would count only one type, such as the two AV valves. Students sometimes forget that the ventricles have exit valves as well as entry valves.","Correct. The heart has four valves."],"lt":"t-heart-valves","lid":"W3-101","comp":58,"competencyId":"cv-valves"},{"id":"L03428","dok":1,"q":"Which list names the four heart valves?","a":"Tricuspid, mitral, pulmonary, and aortic.","options":["Tricuspid, bicuspid, mitral, and aortic","Tricuspid, mitral, pulmonary, and aortic","Tricuspid, mitral, pulmonary, and coronary","Tricuspid, mitral, aortic, and sinoatrial"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The four valves are the tricuspid valve (right atrium to right ventricle), the mitral or bicuspid valve (left atrium to left ventricle), the pulmonary valve (right ventricle to pulmonary trunk), and the aortic valve (left ventricle to aorta). The first two are AV valves and the last two are semilunar valves. Learning them in the order blood meets them helps: tricuspid, pulmonary, mitral, aortic.","notes":["Bicuspid and mitral are two names for the same valve, so this list counts one valve twice and leaves out the pulmonary valve.","Correct. Tricuspid, mitral, pulmonary, and aortic are the four heart valves.","There is no coronary valve. The coronary arteries leave the aorta just above the aortic valve, which may cause this mix-up.","The sinoatrial (SA) node is the pacemaker of the conduction system, not a valve. Pick it for where each heartbeat starts."],"lt":"t-heart-valves","lid":"W3-102","comp":58,"competencyId":"cv-valves"},{"id":"L03431","dok":1,"q":"Which valve lies between the right ventricle and the pulmonary trunk?","a":"The pulmonary valve.","options":["The mitral valve","The pulmonary valve","The tricuspid valve","The aortic valve"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The pulmonary valve is a semilunar valve with three pocket-shaped cusps. It opens when the right ventricle contracts, letting blood into the pulmonary trunk, and closes as the ventricle relaxes to stop blood falling back. It sits at the top of the conus arteriosus, the smooth outflow tract of the right ventricle.","notes":["The mitral valve sits between the left atrium and left ventricle. Pick it for the left AV valve.","Correct. The pulmonary valve guards the exit from the right ventricle.","The tricuspid valve is the entrance to the right ventricle, from the right atrium. Pick it for the valve one step earlier.","The aortic valve guards the exit from the left ventricle into the aorta. Pick it for the left side's semilunar valve."],"lt":"t-heart-valves","lid":"W3-105","comp":58,"competencyId":"cv-valves"},{"id":"L03432","dok":1,"q":"Which valve lies between the left ventricle and the aorta?","a":"The aortic valve.","options":["The tricuspid valve","The mitral valve","The pulmonary valve","The aortic valve"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The aortic valve is a semilunar valve with three pocket-shaped cusps. It opens when the left ventricle contracts, sending oxygen-rich blood into the aorta, and closes to keep that blood from falling back into the ventricle. The coronary arteries branch from the aorta just above it.","notes":["The tricuspid valve sits between the right atrium and right ventricle. Pick it for the right AV valve.","The mitral valve is the entrance to the left ventricle, from the left atrium. Pick it for the valve one step earlier.","The pulmonary valve guards the exit of the right ventricle into the pulmonary trunk. Pick it for the right side's semilunar valve.","Correct. The aortic valve is between the left ventricle and aorta."],"lt":"t-heart-valves","lid":"W3-106","comp":58,"competencyId":"cv-valves"},{"id":"L03433","dok":1,"q":"Which two valves are the atrioventricular (AV) valves?","a":"The tricuspid and mitral valves.","options":["The mitral and aortic valves","The tricuspid and pulmonary valves","The pulmonary and aortic valves","The tricuspid and mitral valves"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Atrioventricular valves sit between an atrium and a ventricle. The tricuspid valve is the right AV valve and the mitral valve is the left AV valve. Both are anchored by chordae tendineae and papillary muscles, which hold them shut when the ventricles contract.","notes":["This pairs the left AV valve with the left semilunar valve. Pick it for the two valves on the left side of the heart.","This pairs the right AV valve with the right semilunar valve. Pick it for the two valves on the right side of the heart.","The pulmonary and aortic valves are the semilunar valves at the exits of the ventricles. Pick this pair for the valves leading into the great arteries.","Correct. The tricuspid and mitral valves are the AV valves."],"lt":"t-heart-valves","lid":"W3-107","comp":58,"competencyId":"cv-valves"},{"id":"L03434","dok":1,"q":"Which two valves are the semilunar valves?","a":"The pulmonary and aortic valves.","options":["The tricuspid and mitral valves","The pulmonary and aortic valves","The tricuspid and aortic valves","The mitral and pulmonary valves"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Semilunar means half-moon, describing the shape of the three pocket-like cusps in each of these valves. The pulmonary valve guards the exit of the right ventricle and the aortic valve guards the exit of the left ventricle. They have no chordae tendineae, because their cusps close by filling with blood.","notes":["The tricuspid and mitral valves are the AV valves between atria and ventricles. Pick this pair for the valves anchored by chordae tendineae.","Correct. The pulmonary and aortic valves are the semilunar valves.","This mixes one AV valve with one semilunar valve. Pick it only for a question pairing the first and last valves blood passes through.","This mixes the left AV valve with the right semilunar valve. It does not match any single valve type."],"lt":"t-heart-valves","lid":"W3-108","comp":58,"competencyId":"cv-valves"},{"id":"L03435","dok":1,"q":"How many cusps does the mitral valve have?","a":"Two.","options":["Two","Three","One","Four"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The mitral valve has two cusps, which is why it is also called the bicuspid valve. The other three heart valves each have three cusps: the tricuspid valve and both semilunar valves. The mitral valve is the only valve with two.","notes":["Correct. The mitral valve has two cusps.","Three cusps belong to the tricuspid valve and to the pulmonary and aortic valves. Pick three for any heart valve except the mitral.","No heart valve has a single cusp. One flap could not seal an opening the way two or three meeting flaps do.","No heart valve has four cusps. This number may come from the four valves or four chambers."],"lt":"t-heart-valves","lid":"W3-110","comp":58,"competencyId":"cv-valves"},{"id":"L03436","dok":1,"q":"Which valve is also called the bicuspid valve?","a":"The mitral valve.","options":["The aortic valve","The tricuspid valve","The mitral valve","The pulmonary valve"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The mitral valve, the left AV valve, is also called the bicuspid valve because it has two cusps. Its other name comes from its resemblance to a bishop's miter, a tall two-pointed hat. Your lab list calls it the left atrioventricular (bicuspid or mitral) valve.","notes":["The aortic valve is a semilunar valve with three cusps. Pick it for the exit of the left ventricle.","The tricuspid valve is the right AV valve with three cusps. Pick it for the valve whose name says three.","Correct. The mitral valve is the bicuspid valve.","The pulmonary valve is a semilunar valve with three cusps. Pick it for the exit of the right ventricle."],"lt":"t-heart-valves","lid":"W3-111","comp":58,"competencyId":"cv-valves"},{"id":"L03437","dok":1,"q":"Which valve prevents backflow into the right atrium?","a":"The tricuspid valve.","options":["The aortic valve","The pulmonary valve","The mitral valve","The tricuspid valve"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"A valve prevents backflow into the chamber blood has just left. Blood leaves the right atrium through the tricuspid valve, so when the right ventricle contracts, the tricuspid valve closes and keeps blood from being pushed back into the right atrium.","notes":["The aortic valve stops backflow into the left ventricle from the aorta. Pick it for the exit of the left ventricle.","The pulmonary valve stops backflow into the right ventricle from the pulmonary trunk. Pick it for the chamber one step later.","The mitral valve stops backflow into the left atrium. Pick it for the left side's AV valve.","Correct. The tricuspid valve stops backflow into the right atrium."],"lt":"t-heart-valves","lid":"W3-112","comp":58,"competencyId":"cv-valves"},{"id":"L03438","dok":1,"q":"Which valve prevents backflow into the left atrium?","a":"The mitral valve.","options":["The mitral valve","The tricuspid valve","The aortic valve","The pulmonary valve"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Blood leaves the left atrium through the mitral valve into the left ventricle. When the left ventricle contracts, the mitral valve closes so that blood is pushed out through the aorta rather than back into the left atrium. The chordae tendineae and papillary muscles keep the mitral cusps from flipping backward.","notes":["Correct. The mitral valve stops backflow into the left atrium.","The tricuspid valve stops backflow into the right atrium. Pick it for the right side's AV valve.","The aortic valve stops backflow into the left ventricle. Pick it for the exit of the left ventricle.","The pulmonary valve stops backflow into the right ventricle. Pick it for the exit of the right ventricle."],"lt":"t-heart-valves","lid":"W3-113","comp":58,"competencyId":"cv-valves"},{"id":"L03439","dok":1,"q":"Which valve prevents backflow into the right ventricle?","a":"The pulmonary valve.","options":["The aortic valve","The tricuspid valve","The pulmonary valve","The mitral valve"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Blood leaves the right ventricle through the pulmonary valve into the pulmonary trunk. When the right ventricle relaxes, blood in the trunk tends to fall back, filling the pocket-shaped cusps and snapping the pulmonary valve shut. This keeps blood from returning to the right ventricle.","notes":["The aortic valve prevents backflow into the left ventricle. Pick it for the left side's semilunar valve.","The tricuspid valve is the entrance to the right ventricle and prevents backflow into the right atrium. Pick it for the chamber one step earlier.","Correct. The pulmonary valve stops backflow into the right ventricle.","The mitral valve prevents backflow into the left atrium. Pick it for the left AV valve."],"lt":"t-heart-valves","lid":"W3-114","comp":58,"competencyId":"cv-valves"},{"id":"L03440","dok":1,"q":"Which valve prevents backflow into the left ventricle?","a":"The aortic valve.","options":["The aortic valve","The mitral valve","The pulmonary valve","The tricuspid valve"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Blood leaves the left ventricle through the aortic valve into the aorta. When the left ventricle relaxes, blood in the aorta fills the three pocket-shaped cusps and closes the valve, keeping blood from falling back into the ventricle. The mitral valve, the entrance to the left ventricle, guards the left atrium instead.","notes":["Correct. The aortic valve stops backflow into the left ventricle.","The mitral valve is the entrance to the left ventricle and prevents backflow into the left atrium. Students pick it because it touches the left ventricle too.","The pulmonary valve prevents backflow into the right ventricle. Pick it for the right side's semilunar valve.","The tricuspid valve prevents backflow into the right atrium. Pick it for the right AV valve."],"lt":"t-heart-valves","lid":"W3-115","comp":58,"competencyId":"cv-valves"},{"id":"L03441","dok":1,"q":"What are the chordae tendineae?","a":"Fibrous cords that tie the cusps of the AV valves to the papillary muscles.","options":["Muscular ridges on the inner walls of the ventricles","Pocket-shaped cusps of the semilunar valves","Fibrous cords tying AV valve cusps to papillary muscles","Connective tissue rings around the valve openings"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The chordae tendineae, sometimes called heart strings, are strong fibrous cords. They run from the edges of the tricuspid and mitral valve cusps down to the papillary muscles in the ventricle wall. When the ventricle contracts, they keep the cusps from being pushed back into the atria.","notes":["Muscular ridges on the ventricle walls are the trabeculae carneae. Pick them for the rough inner texture of the ventricles.","Pocket-shaped cusps belong to the pulmonary and aortic valves, which have no chordae. Pick them for the semilunar valves.","Correct. Chordae tendineae tie AV valve cusps to papillary muscles.","The connective tissue rings around the valves form the fibrous skeleton. Pick it for what anchors the valves and insulates the atria from the ventricles."],"lt":"t-heart-valves","lid":"W3-116","comp":59,"competencyId":"cv-valve-support"},{"id":"L03442","dok":1,"q":"What do the papillary muscles anchor?","a":"The chordae tendineae.","options":["The coronary arteries","The pectinate muscles","The chordae tendineae","The semilunar valve cusps"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Papillary muscles are cone-shaped projections of the ventricle wall. The chordae tendineae attach to their tips and run up to the cusps of the AV valves. When the ventricle contracts, the papillary muscles contract too, keeping the chordae taut so the cusps stay shut.","notes":["The coronary arteries run in the grooves on the outer surface of the heart. Pick them for the vessels supplying the myocardium.","The pectinate muscles are ridges in the atrial walls, not attached to the papillary muscles. Pick them for the comb-like ridges of the atria and auricles.","Correct. The papillary muscles anchor the chordae tendineae.","The semilunar valve cusps have no anchoring cords or muscles; they close by filling with blood. Pick them for the pulmonary and aortic valves."],"lt":"t-heart-valves","lid":"W3-117","comp":59,"competencyId":"cv-valve-support"},{"id":"L03443","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares the AV valves with the semilunar valves?","a":"AV valves lie between atria and ventricles; semilunar valves lie between ventricles and their arteries.","options":["AV valves lie between atria and ventricles, and semilunar valves lie between ventricles and arteries","AV valves lie between ventricles and arteries, and semilunar valves lie between atria and ventricles","Both types lie between the two atria","Both types are anchored by chordae tendineae and papillary muscles"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The AV valves (tricuspid and mitral) are the entrances to the ventricles, between each atrium and its ventricle. The semilunar valves (pulmonary and aortic) are the exits, between each ventricle and the great artery it pumps into. Only the AV valves have chordae tendineae and papillary muscles.","notes":["Correct. AV valves are ventricle entrances, and semilunar valves are ventricle exits.","This swaps the two types. Use the name atrioventricular as a clue: it joins an atrium to a ventricle.","No valve lies between the two atria; they are separated by the interatrial septum. Pick the septum for the wall between the atria.","Only the AV valves have chordae and papillary muscles. Semilunar cusps fill with blood and close on their own."],"lt":"t-heart-valves","lid":"W3-136","comp":58,"competencyId":"cv-valves"},{"id":"L03444","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares the tricuspid and mitral valves?","a":"The tricuspid has three cusps on the right side; the mitral has two cusps on the left side.","options":["The tricuspid is on the left, and the mitral is on the right","Both are semilunar valves at the exits of the ventricles","The tricuspid has three cusps on the right, and the mitral has two cusps on the left","The tricuspid has two cusps on the left, and the mitral has three cusps on the right"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Both valves are atrioventricular valves anchored by chordae tendineae and papillary muscles. The tricuspid valve sits on the right, between the right atrium and right ventricle, and has three cusps. The mitral valve sits on the left, between the left atrium and left ventricle, and has two cusps.","notes":["This swaps the sides. Blood meets the tricuspid valve first, on the right, and the mitral (bicuspid) valve later, on the left.","Both are AV valves, not semilunar valves. The semilunar valves are the pulmonary and aortic valves at the ventricle exits.","Correct. Tricuspid is right with three cusps, and mitral is left with two.","This swaps both the sides and the cusp counts. Remember that tri means three, and the tricuspid is on the right."],"lt":"t-heart-valves","lid":"W3-137","comp":58,"competencyId":"cv-valves"},{"id":"L03445","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares the pulmonary and aortic valves?","a":"Both are semilunar valves; the pulmonary guards the exit of the right ventricle and the aortic guards the exit of the left.","options":["The pulmonary guards the left ventricle exit, and the aortic the right","Both are semilunar; pulmonary guards the right ventricle exit, aortic the left","The pulmonary has two cusps, and the aortic has three","Both are AV valves held by chordae tendineae"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The pulmonary and aortic valves are both semilunar valves with three pocket-shaped cusps. The pulmonary valve sits where the right ventricle opens into the pulmonary trunk, and the aortic valve sits where the left ventricle opens into the aorta. Neither has chordae tendineae.","notes":["This swaps the sides. The pulmonary trunk leaves the right ventricle and the aorta leaves the left.","Correct. Both are semilunar valves, one at each ventricle exit.","Both semilunar valves have three cusps. The only two-cusped valve is the mitral valve.","Neither is an AV valve, and neither has chordae tendineae. Pick this description for the tricuspid and mitral valves."],"lt":"t-heart-valves","lid":"W3-138","comp":58,"competencyId":"cv-valves"},{"id":"L03447","dok":2,"q":"Why don't the semilunar valves need chordae tendineae?","a":"Their pocket-shaped cusps fill with backflowing blood and close themselves.","options":["Their cusps are made of muscle that contracts shut","They are anchored by the papillary muscles instead","They only open once per minute","Their pocket-shaped cusps fill with blood and close themselves"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Each semilunar valve has three pocket-shaped cusps, like small cups facing up into the artery. When the ventricle relaxes and blood starts to fall back, it fills these pockets, which press together and seal the opening. The design closes itself, so no cords or muscles are needed.","notes":["Valve cusps contain no muscle; they are folds of endocardium over connective tissue. Pick the myocardium for the muscle that contracts.","Papillary muscles are found only with the AV valves. Pick them for the muscles anchoring the tricuspid and mitral chordae.","The semilunar valves open with every heartbeat, many times a minute. This choice misreads how often they work.","Correct. Blood filling the pocket-shaped cusps closes the valve."],"lt":"t-heart-valves","lid":"W3-140","comp":58,"competencyId":"cv-valves"},{"id":"L03448","dok":1,"q":"Papillary muscles are projections of which structure?","a":"The ventricle wall.","options":["The valve cusps","The interatrial septum","The atrial wall","The ventricle wall"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Papillary muscles are cone-shaped projections of the myocardium of the ventricle wall. They rise from the floor and walls of each ventricle, and the chordae tendineae run from their tips to the AV valve cusps. Because they are part of the ventricle wall, they contract when the ventricle does.","notes":["The valve cusps contain no muscle. They are the flaps that the chordae attach to, at the other end from the papillary muscles.","The interatrial septum separates the atria and holds the fossa ovalis. Pick it for the wall between the receiving chambers.","The atrial wall has pectinate muscles, not papillary muscles. Pick the atrial wall for the comb-like ridges.","Correct. Papillary muscles project from the ventricle wall."],"lt":"t-heart-valves","lid":"W3-141","comp":59,"competencyId":"cv-valve-support"},{"id":"L03449","dok":2,"q":"What job do all four heart valves share?","a":"They keep blood flowing in one direction by preventing backflow.","options":["They keep blood flowing in one direction","They pump blood out of the ventricles","They start each heartbeat","They mix blood between the two sides"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Each valve opens to let blood pass forward and closes to stop it from flowing back into the chamber it just left. With four valves in a row, blood can only move one way: atrium to ventricle to artery on each side. This one-way flow is what makes the heart an effective pump.","notes":["Correct. All four valves enforce one-way flow.","Valves do no pumping. The myocardium of the ventricles generates the force, and valves only open and close.","Each heartbeat starts at the sinoatrial (SA) node in the right atrium wall. Pick the SA node for the heart's pacemaker.","The two sides of the adult heart are kept separate, and no valve connects them. Mixing between sides happens only through a defect, such as a septal hole."],"lt":"t-heart-valves","lid":"W3-150","comp":58,"competencyId":"cv-valves"},{"id":"L03450","dok":1,"q":"A valve that does not close cleanly lets blood slip backward. What does a clinician hear through a stethoscope because of the turbulent flow?","a":"A heart murmur.","options":["A crackle in the lungs","A heart murmur","A gurgling bowel sound","A wheeze"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"When a valve leaks or is narrowed, blood no longer flows smoothly through it. The turbulence makes an extra sound, heard through a stethoscope as a murmur. One-way flow through four well-sealed valves is what keeps the normal heart sounds clean.","notes":["Crackles are heard over the lungs when the alveoli contain fluid. Pick them for a problem in the respiratory zone, not the heart.","Correct. Turbulent flow through a faulty valve is heard as a murmur.","Bowel sounds come from the movement of gas and contents through the intestines. Pick them for a question about the digestive tract.","A wheeze is a whistling sound from air forced through narrowed airways, as in asthma. Pick it for a problem in the bronchial tree."],"lt":"t-heart-valves","lid":"W3-153","comp":58,"competencyId":"cv-valves"},{"id":"L03451","dok":2,"q":"If the tricuspid valve fails to close, blood leaks backward into which chamber when the right ventricle contracts?","a":"The right atrium.","options":["The right ventricle","The pulmonary trunk","The right atrium","The left atrium"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The tricuspid valve sits between the right atrium and right ventricle and normally closes when the right ventricle contracts. If it fails, some of the blood being pushed by the right ventricle leaks back into the right atrium. This backward leak is called regurgitation.","notes":["The right ventricle is the chamber doing the pushing, so blood leaks out of it, not into it. Pick it for a failing pulmonary valve.","The pulmonary trunk is where blood should go forward. A tricuspid leak sends blood backward, away from the trunk.","Correct. A leaky tricuspid valve lets blood flow back into the right atrium.","The left atrium sits behind the mitral valve on the other side of the heart. Pick it for a failing mitral valve."],"lt":"t-heart-valves","lid":"W3-161","comp":58,"competencyId":"cv-valves"},{"id":"L03452","dok":2,"q":"If the mitral valve fails to close, blood leaks backward into which chamber when the left ventricle contracts?","a":"The left atrium.","options":["The left ventricle","The left atrium","The aorta","The right atrium"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The mitral valve sits between the left atrium and left ventricle. When the left ventricle contracts, the mitral valve should close so all the blood goes out through the aorta. If it fails, some blood is pushed back into the left atrium, which is called mitral regurgitation.","notes":["The left ventricle is the chamber doing the pushing, so blood leaks out of it, not into it. Pick it for a failing aortic valve.","Correct. A leaky mitral valve sends blood back into the left atrium.","The aorta is where blood should go forward. A mitral leak sends blood backward, away from the aorta.","The right atrium sits behind the tricuspid valve. Pick it for a failing tricuspid valve."],"lt":"t-heart-valves","lid":"W3-162","comp":58,"competencyId":"cv-valves"},{"id":"L03453","dok":2,"q":"If the pulmonary valve fails to close, blood leaks backward into which chamber as the right ventricle relaxes?","a":"The right ventricle.","options":["The right ventricle","The right atrium","The left atrium","The left ventricle"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The pulmonary valve guards the exit from the right ventricle into the pulmonary trunk. When the ventricle relaxes, the valve should snap shut so blood stays in the trunk. If it fails, blood falls back from the pulmonary trunk into the right ventricle.","notes":["Correct. A leaky pulmonary valve lets blood fall back into the right ventricle.","The right atrium is protected by the tricuspid valve, not the pulmonary valve. Pick it for a failing tricuspid valve.","The left atrium is protected by the mitral valve. Pick it for a failing mitral valve.","The left ventricle is protected by the aortic valve. Pick it for a failing aortic valve."],"lt":"t-heart-valves","lid":"W3-163","comp":58,"competencyId":"cv-valves"},{"id":"L03454","dok":2,"q":"If the aortic valve fails to close, blood leaks backward into which chamber as the left ventricle relaxes?","a":"The left ventricle.","options":["The right atrium","The right ventricle","The left atrium","The left ventricle"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The aortic valve guards the exit from the left ventricle into the aorta. As the ventricle relaxes, the valve should close so blood stays in the aorta. If it fails, blood falls back from the aorta into the left ventricle, so the ventricle must pump some of the same blood again.","notes":["The right atrium is protected by the tricuspid valve. Pick it for a failing tricuspid valve.","The right ventricle is protected by the pulmonary valve. Pick it for a failing pulmonary valve.","The left atrium is protected by the mitral valve. Pick it for a failing mitral valve.","Correct. A leaky aortic valve lets blood fall back into the left ventricle."],"lt":"t-heart-valves","lid":"W3-164","comp":58,"competencyId":"cv-valves"},{"id":"L03455","dok":2,"q":"A patient has ruptured chordae tendineae. Which valves are most directly affected?","a":"The AV valves.","options":["All four valves equally","The semilunar valves","The AV valves","The pulmonary valve only"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Chordae tendineae attach only to the cusps of the AV valves, the tricuspid and mitral. When they rupture, the cusps lose their anchor and can be pushed back into the atrium when the ventricle contracts. The semilunar valves have no chordae and are not directly affected.","notes":["Only two valves have chordae. The pulmonary and aortic valves would be unaffected.","The semilunar valves have no chordae tendineae, so ruptured chordae do not touch them. Pick them for problems with pocket-shaped cusps.","Correct. Chordae tendineae belong only to the AV valves.","The pulmonary valve is a semilunar valve with no chordae. Pick it for a problem at the exit of the right ventricle."],"lt":"t-heart-valves","lid":"W3-165","comp":59,"competencyId":"cv-valve-support"},{"id":"L03456","dok":3,"q":"A heart attack kills a papillary muscle in the left ventricle. Which valve is most at risk of failing?","a":"The mitral valve.","options":["The mitral valve","The aortic valve","The tricuspid valve","The pulmonary valve"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The papillary muscles of the left ventricle anchor the chordae tendineae of the mitral valve. If one dies, its chordae go slack and the mitral cusps can be pushed back into the left atrium. The aortic valve also opens from the left ventricle, but it has no papillary muscles.","notes":["Correct. Left ventricular papillary muscles hold the mitral valve.","The aortic valve sits at the exit of the left ventricle, but semilunar valves have no papillary muscles or chordae. Pick it for a problem with the left ventricle's outflow.","The tricuspid valve is anchored by papillary muscles in the right ventricle. Pick it for a right ventricular papillary muscle injury.","The pulmonary valve is a semilunar valve with no papillary muscles. Pick it for the exit of the right ventricle."],"lt":"t-heart-valves","lid":"W3-166","comp":59,"competencyId":"cv-valve-support"},{"id":"L03457","dok":2,"q":"Which sequence traces the anchoring chain that holds an AV valve shut, from the ventricle wall to the valve?","a":"Papillary muscle, chordae tendineae, valve cusp.","options":["Papillary muscle, chordae tendineae, valve cusp","Chordae tendineae, papillary muscle, valve cusp","Trabeculae carneae, pectinate muscle, valve cusp","Papillary muscle, valve cusp, chordae tendineae"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The papillary muscles rise from the ventricle wall. Chordae tendineae run from their tips up to the edges of the AV valve cusps. When the ventricle contracts, this chain stays taut and keeps the cusps from being pushed back into the atrium, which is why damage anywhere along it can make the valve fail.","notes":["Correct. Papillary muscle, then chordae tendineae, then the valve cusp.","This puts the chordae next to the wall. In fact the papillary muscle projects from the wall, and the chordae connect it to the cusp.","Trabeculae carneae are ridges on the ventricle walls and pectinate muscles are in the atria. Neither is part of the chain holding a valve shut.","This puts the cusp in the middle. The chordae are what link the papillary muscle to the cusp, so they must sit between them."],"lt":"t-heart-valves","lid":"W3-168","comp":59,"competencyId":"cv-valve-support"},{"id":"L03458","dok":2,"q":"Blood leaks from the aorta back into the left ventricle each time the ventricle relaxes. Which valve is failing?","a":"The aortic valve.","options":["The mitral valve","The tricuspid valve","The aortic valve","The pulmonary valve"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The aortic valve is the only valve between the aorta and the left ventricle. Its job is to close as the ventricle relaxes, so blood stays in the aorta. A leak in that exact direction points to aortic valve failure, called aortic regurgitation.","notes":["The mitral valve separates the left atrium from the left ventricle. Pick it for blood leaking back into the left atrium.","The tricuspid valve separates the right atrium from the right ventricle. Pick it for blood leaking back into the right atrium.","Correct. The aortic valve separates the aorta from the left ventricle.","The pulmonary valve separates the pulmonary trunk from the right ventricle. Pick it for a leak on the right side."],"lt":"t-heart-valves","lid":"W3-172","comp":58,"competencyId":"cv-valves"},{"id":"L03459","dok":2,"q":"Blood leaks from the pulmonary trunk back into the right ventricle each time the ventricle relaxes. Which valve is failing?","a":"The pulmonary valve.","options":["The pulmonary valve","The aortic valve","The tricuspid valve","The mitral valve"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The pulmonary valve sits between the right ventricle and the pulmonary trunk. It should close as the right ventricle relaxes so blood stays in the trunk. When it is incompetent (unable to close fully), blood falls back into the right ventricle.","notes":["Correct. The pulmonary valve separates the pulmonary trunk from the right ventricle.","The aortic valve separates the aorta from the left ventricle. Pick it for a leak on the left side.","The tricuspid valve separates the right atrium from the right ventricle. Pick it for a leak back into the right atrium.","The mitral valve separates the left atrium from the left ventricle. Pick it for a leak back into the left atrium."],"lt":"t-heart-valves","lid":"W3-173","comp":58,"competencyId":"cv-valves"},{"id":"L03460","dok":2,"q":"In mitral valve prolapse, the mitral cusps bulge back into the left atrium when the ventricle contracts. Which structures normally prevent this?","a":"The chordae tendineae.","options":["The trabeculae carneae","The pectinate muscles","The chordae tendineae","The moderator band"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The chordae tendineae tie the mitral cusps to the papillary muscles of the left ventricle. When the ventricle contracts, they hold the cusps in place so they meet and seal instead of bulging into the atrium. If the chordae are stretched or weak, the cusps prolapse, meaning they slip backward.","notes":["The trabeculae carneae are muscular ridges on the ventricle walls and do not attach to the cusps. Pick them for the rough inner surface of the ventricles.","The pectinate muscles are ridges in the atrial walls. Pick them for the comb-like ridges of the atria and auricles.","Correct. The chordae tendineae hold the mitral cusps in place.","The moderator band is in the right ventricle and carries conduction fibers. It has no connection to the mitral valve on the left side."],"lt":"t-heart-valves","lid":"W3-174","comp":59,"competencyId":"cv-valve-support"},{"id":"L03461","dok":2,"q":"What do the papillary muscles do as the ventricles contract?","a":"They contract and keep the chordae tendineae taut, holding the AV cusps shut.","options":["They relax so the AV cusps can swing into the atria","They contract and keep the chordae taut so the AV cusps stay shut","They push the semilunar cusps open","They squeeze blood forward out of the atria"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The papillary muscles are part of the ventricle wall, so they contract along with it. As they contract, they pull on the chordae tendineae and keep them taut. This holds the tricuspid and mitral cusps together against the pressure of the contracting ventricle, so the cusps do not flip into the atria.","notes":["If the papillary muscles relaxed, the cusps would be free to flip into the atria, which is exactly the failure they prevent. This choice describes what happens when a papillary muscle is damaged.","Correct. Contracting papillary muscles keep the chordae taut.","The semilunar valves open because blood pressure in the ventricle rises above the pressure in the artery. They have no connection to the papillary muscles.","Atrial contraction comes from the thin atrial walls themselves, just before the ventricles contract. Papillary muscles sit inside the ventricles."],"lt":"t-heart-valves","lid":"W3-175","comp":59,"competencyId":"cv-valve-support"},{"id":"L03462","dok":2,"q":"In pulmonary valve stenosis, the pulmonary valve is narrowed. Which chamber must work harder first?","a":"The right ventricle.","options":["The right ventricle","The right atrium","The left ventricle","The left atrium"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Stenosis means narrowing. The pulmonary valve is the exit of the right ventricle, so a narrowed pulmonary valve forces the right ventricle to push harder to move blood into the pulmonary trunk. The chamber directly behind a narrowed exit valve is the first to feel the strain.","notes":["Correct. The right ventricle pumps through the pulmonary valve.","The right atrium empties through the tricuspid valve. Pick it for tricuspid stenosis.","The left ventricle pumps through the aortic valve. Pick it for aortic stenosis.","The left atrium empties through the mitral valve. Pick it for mitral stenosis."],"lt":"t-heart-valves","lid":"W3-176","comp":58,"competencyId":"cv-valves"},{"id":"L03463","dok":2,"q":"In aortic stenosis, the aortic valve is narrowed. Which chamber must work harder first?","a":"The left ventricle.","options":["The left atrium","The left ventricle","The right ventricle","The right atrium"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The aortic valve is the exit of the left ventricle. When it is narrowed, the left ventricle must push harder to force blood into the aorta. Over time, this extra work can thicken the left ventricular wall.","notes":["The left atrium empties through the mitral valve, one step upstream. Pick it for mitral stenosis.","Correct. The left ventricle pumps through the aortic valve.","The right ventricle pumps through the pulmonary valve. Pick it for pulmonary stenosis.","The right atrium empties through the tricuspid valve. Pick it for tricuspid stenosis."],"lt":"t-heart-valves","lid":"W3-177","comp":58,"competencyId":"cv-valves"},{"id":"L03464","dok":2,"q":"Why does blood normally not flow from the ventricles back into the atria?","a":"The AV valves close when the ventricles contract.","options":["The AV valves close when the ventricles contract","The semilunar valves close when the ventricles contract","The interventricular septum blocks the path","The atria contract at the same moment as the ventricles"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The AV valves, tricuspid and mitral, sit between each atrium and its ventricle. When the ventricles contract, rising pressure pushes the cusps together and the chordae tendineae hold them shut. With the AV valves sealed, blood can only leave the ventricles through the semilunar valves into the arteries.","notes":["Correct. Closed AV valves block the way back into the atria.","The semilunar valves open, not close, when the ventricles contract, so blood can leave into the arteries. They close when the ventricles relax, protecting the ventricles from backflow.","The interventricular septum separates the two ventricles from each other, not the ventricles from the atria. Pick it for a ventricular septal defect.","The atria contract first and then relax while the ventricles contract. Their timing alone would not stop backflow."],"lt":"t-heart-valves","lid":"W3-178","comp":58,"competencyId":"cv-valves"},{"id":"L03465","dok":2,"q":"Which narrowed valve would most directly restrict blood leaving the left ventricle into the aorta?","a":"The aortic valve (aortic stenosis).","options":["The mitral valve","The aortic valve","The pulmonary valve","The tricuspid valve"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Blood leaving the left ventricle must pass through the aortic valve to enter the aorta. If that valve is narrowed, a condition called aortic stenosis, less blood gets through with each beat. The other valves sit elsewhere in the pathway and would affect different steps.","notes":["A narrowed mitral valve restricts blood entering the left ventricle from the left atrium, one step earlier. Pick it for reduced filling of the left ventricle.","Correct. The aortic valve is the gateway from the left ventricle into the aorta.","A narrowed pulmonary valve restricts blood leaving the right ventricle. Pick it for reduced flow into the pulmonary trunk.","A narrowed tricuspid valve restricts blood entering the right ventricle. Pick it for reduced filling on the right side."],"lt":"t-heart-valves","lid":"W3-179","comp":58,"competencyId":"cv-valves"},{"id":"L03466","dok":2,"q":"Which narrowed valve would most directly restrict blood leaving the right ventricle into the pulmonary trunk?","a":"The pulmonary valve (pulmonary stenosis).","options":["The mitral valve","The pulmonary valve","The tricuspid valve","The aortic valve"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Blood leaving the right ventricle must pass through the pulmonary valve to enter the pulmonary trunk. If that valve is narrowed, called pulmonary stenosis, less blood reaches the lungs with each beat. The right ventricle has to push harder to overcome it.","notes":["A narrowed mitral valve restricts blood entering the left ventricle. Pick it for reduced left ventricular filling.","Correct. The pulmonary valve is the gateway from the right ventricle into the pulmonary trunk.","A narrowed tricuspid valve restricts blood entering the right ventricle, one step earlier. Pick it for reduced right ventricular filling.","A narrowed aortic valve restricts blood leaving the left ventricle. Pick it for reduced flow into the aorta."],"lt":"t-heart-valves","lid":"W3-180","comp":58,"competencyId":"cv-valves"},{"id":"L03467","dok":2,"q":"Which statement explains why the AV valves need chordae tendineae and papillary muscles but the semilunar valves do not?","a":"Contracting ventricles push up on the AV cusps and would flip them into the atria, while semilunar cusps close by filling with blood.","options":["Semilunar valves are made of muscle and close themselves by contracting","Semilunar valves face stronger pressure and need thicker cusps instead","AV cusps would be pushed into the atria, while semilunar cusps close by filling","AV valves open only once per minute and need extra support"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"When a ventricle contracts, high pressure pushes up against the AV cusps from below. Without the chordae tendineae, held taut by the papillary muscles, the cusps would turn inside out into the atria. Semilunar cusps are shaped like pockets that fill with falling blood and press together, so they seal without any anchors.","notes":["No valve contains muscle. All valves open and close passively as blood pressure changes on either side.","Cusp thickness is not the explanation. The key difference is the shape of the cusps and the direction of the pressure they face.","Correct. AV cusps need anchors against ventricular pressure, while semilunar cusps seal by filling.","The AV valves open with every heartbeat, many times a minute. Their support is needed against pressure, not because of how often they open."],"lt":"t-heart-valves","lid":"W3-181","comp":59,"competencyId":"cv-valve-support"},{"id":"L03468","dok":2,"q":"Blood that passes through the pulmonary valve enters which circuit?","a":"The pulmonary circuit.","options":["The pulmonary circuit","The hepatic portal system","The coronary circulation","The systemic circuit"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The pulmonary valve opens into the pulmonary trunk, which divides into the pulmonary arteries to the lungs. Blood that passes through it has entered the pulmonary circuit, where it will pick up O2 before returning to the left atrium. A problem with this valve therefore affects flow to the lungs first.","notes":["Correct. The pulmonary valve opens into the pulmonary circuit.","The hepatic portal system carries blood from the digestive organs to the liver. Pick it for blood draining the gut.","The coronary circulation begins with the coronary arteries just above the aortic valve. Pick it for the heart's own blood supply.","Blood enters the systemic circuit through the aortic valve. Pick it for the valve at the exit of the left ventricle."],"lt":"t-heart-valves","lid":"W3-183","comp":69,"competencyId":"bvn-circulatory-routes"},{"id":"L03469","dok":2,"q":"In mitral stenosis, the mitral valve is narrowed. Which chamber would not fill normally?","a":"The left ventricle.","options":["The left atrium","The left ventricle","The right ventricle","The right atrium"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The mitral valve is the entrance to the left ventricle, the doorway blood uses to move down from the left atrium. When it is narrowed, less blood can get through, so the left ventricle does not fill normally. Blood instead backs up in the left atrium behind the narrowed valve.","notes":["The left atrium sits behind the narrowed valve, so it overfills rather than underfills. Pick it for the chamber where blood backs up.","Correct. A narrowed mitral valve limits filling of the left ventricle.","The right ventricle fills through the tricuspid valve. Pick it for tricuspid stenosis.","The right atrium fills from the venae cavae and coronary sinus, with no valve in the way. Pick it for blocked systemic venous return."],"lt":"t-heart-valves","lid":"W3-184","comp":58,"competencyId":"cv-valves"},{"id":"L03470","dok":3,"q":"If the tricuspid valve could not close at all, blood pushed backward by the right ventricle would enter the right atrium and back up toward which vessels?","a":"The venae cavae.","options":["The pulmonary veins","The aorta","The coronary arteries","The venae cavae"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"With no working tricuspid valve, each right ventricular contraction sends blood back into the right atrium. The right atrium is fed by the superior and inferior venae cavae, so blood backs up into those veins and the veins of the body. Tracing backward from a failed valve tells you where the congestion builds.","notes":["The pulmonary veins feed the left atrium. Pick them for backup caused by a failed mitral valve.","The aorta carries blood away from the left ventricle and is not upstream of the right atrium. Pick it for a problem with the aortic valve.","The coronary arteries leave the aorta to supply the heart wall. They are not part of the backward path from the right atrium.","Correct. Blood backs up from the right atrium into the venae cavae."],"lt":"t-heart-valves","lid":"W3-189","comp":58,"competencyId":"cv-valves"},{"id":"L03471","dok":3,"q":"If the mitral valve could not close at all, blood pushed backward by the left ventricle would enter the left atrium and back up toward which organs?","a":"The lungs, through the pulmonary veins.","options":["The body tissues, through the venae cavae","The heart wall, through the coronary sinus","The lungs, through the pulmonary veins","The body tissues, through the aorta"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"With no working mitral valve, each left ventricular contraction sends blood back into the left atrium. The left atrium is fed by the pulmonary veins from the lungs, so blood backs up into those veins and the lungs. This is why severe mitral valve failure can cause fluid to build up in the lungs.","notes":["The venae cavae feed the right atrium. Pick them for backup caused by a failed tricuspid valve.","The coronary sinus drains into the right atrium, not the left. Pick it for the vessel draining the heart wall.","Correct. Blood backs up from the left atrium into the pulmonary veins and lungs.","The aorta carries blood forward from the left ventricle, not backward from the left atrium. Pick it for normal flow to the body."],"lt":"t-heart-valves","lid":"W3-190","comp":58,"competencyId":"cv-valves"},{"id":"L03472","dok":2,"q":"What opens and closes the heart valves?","a":"Differences in blood pressure on either side of each valve.","options":["Nerve signals sent directly to each valve","Pressure differences in the blood on either side","Contraction of muscle inside each cusp","The papillary muscles pulling the cusps open"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Heart valves are passive flaps of endocardium over connective tissue, with no muscle of their own. When blood pressure behind a valve is higher than in front of it, the valve is pushed open; when pressure in front becomes higher, blood pushes the cusps shut. The chambers create these pressure changes as they contract and relax.","notes":["No nerves open or close the valves. Nerves to the heart act on the SA node and the myocardium, which change heart rate and force.","Correct. Pressure differences push the valves open and shut.","Valve cusps contain no muscle. Pick the myocardium for heart tissue that contracts.","Papillary muscles hold the AV cusps shut against ventricular pressure; they do not pull them open. The valves open when atrial pressure rises above ventricular pressure."],"lt":"t-heart-valves","lid":"W3-191","comp":58,"competencyId":"cv-valves"},{"id":"L03473","dok":2,"q":"Blood passing through which valve leaves the heart and enters the systemic arteries?","a":"The aortic valve.","options":["The mitral valve","The pulmonary valve","The tricuspid valve","The aortic valve"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The aortic valve opens from the left ventricle into the aorta, the first artery of the systemic circuit. Blood passing through it is on its way to the body's tissues. The pulmonary valve is the matching exit on the right side, but it leads into the pulmonary circuit instead.","notes":["The mitral valve lets blood move from the left atrium into the left ventricle, so blood passing through it is still inside the heart.","The pulmonary valve also lets blood leave the heart, but into the pulmonary trunk toward the lungs. Pick it for entry into the pulmonary circuit.","The tricuspid valve lets blood move from the right atrium into the right ventricle, still inside the heart. Pick it for the entrance to the right ventricle.","Correct. The aortic valve opens into the aorta and the systemic arteries."],"lt":"t-heart-valves","lid":"W3-195","comp":58,"competencyId":"cv-valves"},{"id":"L03488","dok":1,"q":"Where does oxygen-poor blood returning from the body first enter the heart?","a":"Right atrium.","options":["Right atrium","Left ventricle","Left atrium","Right ventricle"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Oxygen-poor blood returns from the body through the superior and inferior venae cavae, and both empty into the right atrium. The coronary sinus also delivers blood from the heart wall to this chamber. The right atrium then passes the blood through the tricuspid valve to the right ventricle.","notes":["Correct. Both venae cavae empty into the right atrium.","The left ventricle pumps oxygen-rich blood into the aorta. It would be the answer for the chamber that sends blood out to the body.","The left atrium receives oxygen-rich blood from the lungs through the four pulmonary veins. Choose it for where blood first enters the heart after the lungs.","The right ventricle is the next chamber, receiving blood from the right atrium through the tricuspid valve. It would be the answer for which chamber pumps blood to the lungs."],"lt":"t-heart-blood-flow","lid":"W3-126","comp":57,"competencyId":"cv-chambers"},{"id":"L03489","dok":1,"q":"Blood leaves the right atrium through which valve?","a":"Tricuspid valve.","options":["Aortic valve","Mitral valve","Tricuspid valve","Pulmonary valve"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The tricuspid valve is the right atrioventricular valve, with three cusps, sitting between the right atrium and the right ventricle. Blood passes through it from the atrium down into the ventricle, and the valve closes when the ventricle contracts so blood cannot flow back into the atrium.","notes":["The aortic valve is the semilunar valve between the left ventricle and the aorta. It is the answer for the last valve blood crosses before leaving the heart for the body.","The mitral (bicuspid) valve is the left atrioventricular valve, between the left atrium and the left ventricle. It would be the answer for blood leaving the left atrium.","Correct. The tricuspid valve connects the right atrium to the right ventricle.","The pulmonary valve is the semilunar valve between the right ventricle and the pulmonary trunk. Choose it for blood leaving the right ventricle, one step later in the pathway."],"lt":"t-heart-blood-flow","lid":"W3-127","comp":58,"competencyId":"cv-valves"},{"id":"L03490","dok":1,"q":"Which chamber pumps blood through the pulmonary valve?","a":"Right ventricle.","options":["Left atrium","Right atrium","Right ventricle","Left ventricle"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The pulmonary valve guards the exit of the right ventricle, where it opens into the pulmonary trunk. When the right ventricle contracts, it pushes oxygen-poor blood through this semilunar valve and on toward the lungs. This is the start of the pulmonary circuit.","notes":["The left atrium passes oxygen-rich blood through the mitral valve to the left ventricle. Pick it for the chamber that receives the pulmonary veins.","The right atrium sends blood to the right ventricle through the tricuspid valve, not the pulmonary valve. Choose it for the chamber that receives blood from the venae cavae.","Correct. The right ventricle pumps blood through the pulmonary valve into the pulmonary trunk.","The left ventricle pumps blood through the aortic valve into the aorta. It is the left-side mirror of this answer and would be right for a question about the valve leading to the body."],"lt":"t-heart-blood-flow","lid":"W3-128","comp":58,"competencyId":"cv-valves"},{"id":"L03491","dok":1,"q":"After passing through the pulmonary valve, blood enters which vessel?","a":"Pulmonary trunk.","options":["Pulmonary trunk","Superior vena cava","Pulmonary veins","Aorta"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The pulmonary valve sits at the exit of the right ventricle, and on the far side of it is the pulmonary trunk. This large vessel carries oxygen-poor blood upward and then splits into the right and left pulmonary arteries, which take the blood to the lungs.","notes":["Correct. The pulmonary trunk lies just beyond the pulmonary valve.","The superior vena cava returns oxygen-poor blood from the head, neck, and upper limbs into the right atrium. Choose it for a vessel that brings blood into the heart, not one that carries blood out.","The pulmonary veins carry oxygen-rich blood from the lungs back to the left atrium. They are the answer for the vessels that return blood to the heart after the lungs, not the vessel that leaves the right ventricle.","The aorta receives blood from the left ventricle through the aortic valve. It would be the answer for the vessel just past the aortic valve, the left-side mirror of this question."],"lt":"t-heart-blood-flow","lid":"W3-129","comp":58,"competencyId":"cv-valves"},{"id":"L03492","dok":1,"q":"Along the pathway of blood through the heart and lungs, where does blood pick up oxygen?","a":"Lungs.","options":["Right ventricle","Left atrium","Lungs","Pulmonary trunk"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Blood picks up oxygen in the lungs, where the capillaries surrounding the alveoli (tiny air sacs) exchange gases with inhaled air. Blood arrives there oxygen-poor through the pulmonary arteries, picks up O2 and releases CO2, and returns to the left atrium through the pulmonary veins.","notes":["The right ventricle holds oxygen-poor blood and pumps it toward the lungs. Choose it for the chamber that sends blood into the pulmonary circuit, not where oxygen is added.","The left atrium receives blood that is already oxygen-rich, delivered by the pulmonary veins. It would be the answer for the first chamber to receive oxygenated blood.","Correct. Gas exchange in the lung capillaries oxygenates the blood.","The pulmonary trunk carries oxygen-poor blood from the right ventricle toward the lungs. Its thick wall does not allow gas exchange; pick it for the vessel just past the pulmonary valve."],"lt":"t-heart-blood-flow","lid":"W3-130","comp":69,"competencyId":"bvn-circulatory-routes"},{"id":"L03493","dok":1,"q":"Which chamber of the heart receives oxygen-rich blood returning from the lungs?","a":"Left atrium.","options":["Right ventricle","Right atrium","Left atrium","Left ventricle"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Oxygen-rich blood leaves the lungs in the four pulmonary veins, and these empty into the left atrium. The left atrium then passes the blood through the mitral valve into the left ventricle, which pumps it out to the body.","notes":["The right ventricle holds oxygen-poor blood and pumps it to the lungs. Pick it for the chamber that starts the pulmonary circuit.","The right atrium receives oxygen-poor blood from the body through the venae cavae and from the heart wall through the coronary sinus. Choose it for where returning systemic blood enters the heart.","Correct. The pulmonary veins empty into the left atrium.","The left ventricle does hold oxygen-rich blood, but it receives it from the left atrium, not directly from the lungs. It is the answer for the chamber that pumps blood into the aorta."],"lt":"t-heart-blood-flow","lid":"W3-131","comp":57,"competencyId":"cv-chambers"},{"id":"L03494","dok":1,"q":"Blood enters the left ventricle through which valve?","a":"Mitral valve.","options":["Pulmonary valve","Mitral valve","Aortic valve","Tricuspid valve"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The mitral valve, also called the bicuspid valve because it has two cusps, is the left atrioventricular valve. It lies between the left atrium and the left ventricle, so blood passes through it to fill the left ventricle. When the ventricle contracts, the mitral valve closes to keep blood from flowing back into the left atrium.","notes":["The pulmonary valve lets blood leave the right ventricle for the pulmonary trunk. Pick it for the exit valve on the right side of the heart.","Correct. The mitral valve opens from the left atrium into the left ventricle.","The aortic valve is how blood leaves the left ventricle, not how it enters. Choose it for the valve between the left ventricle and the aorta.","The tricuspid valve is the right atrioventricular valve, letting blood into the right ventricle. It is the mirror image of the mitral valve and would be right for the same question about the right ventricle."],"lt":"t-heart-blood-flow","lid":"W3-132","comp":58,"competencyId":"cv-valves"},{"id":"L03495","dok":1,"q":"Which valve must blood pass through just before entering the aorta?","a":"Aortic valve.","options":["Aortic valve","Tricuspid valve","Mitral valve","Pulmonary valve"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The aortic valve is the semilunar valve between the left ventricle and the aorta. When the left ventricle contracts, the valve's three pocket-shaped cusps open and blood flows into the aorta. When the ventricle relaxes, the cusps fill with blood and close, keeping blood from falling back into the ventricle.","notes":["Correct. The aortic valve opens from the left ventricle into the aorta.","The tricuspid valve lies between the right atrium and the right ventricle. Choose it for the first valve blood crosses after entering the heart from the venae cavae.","The mitral valve lies between the left atrium and the left ventricle, one step earlier in the pathway. It is the answer for the valve blood crosses to enter the left ventricle.","The pulmonary valve sits between the right ventricle and the pulmonary trunk. It is the right-side counterpart and would be correct for the valve just before the pulmonary trunk."],"lt":"t-heart-blood-flow","lid":"W3-133","comp":58,"competencyId":"cv-valves"},{"id":"L03496","dok":1,"q":"Through which vessel does blood leave the heart to begin the systemic circuit?","a":"Aorta.","options":["Pulmonary trunk","Aorta","Superior vena cava","Pulmonary veins"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The systemic circuit is the loop that carries blood from the left side of the heart out to the body and back. It begins when the left ventricle pumps oxygen-rich blood through the aortic valve into the aorta, the largest artery, whose branches reach every region of the body.","notes":["The pulmonary trunk is where blood leaves the heart to begin the pulmonary circuit to the lungs. Choose it for the outflow vessel of the right ventricle.","Correct. The aorta carries blood out of the left ventricle to the body.","The superior vena cava brings blood back into the right atrium at the end of the systemic circuit. It would be the answer for where blood from the upper body returns, not where it leaves.","The pulmonary veins bring oxygen-rich blood into the left atrium at the end of the pulmonary circuit. They carry blood toward the heart, not out to the body."],"lt":"t-heart-blood-flow","lid":"W3-134","comp":62,"competencyId":"bvn-great-vessels"},{"id":"L03497","dok":2,"q":"Blood returning from the body enters the heart. Which sequence lists the first three structures it meets, in order?","a":"Right atrium, tricuspid valve, right ventricle.","options":["Right atrium, tricuspid valve, right ventricle","Right atrium, mitral valve, right ventricle","Right ventricle, tricuspid valve, right atrium","Left atrium, mitral valve, left ventricle"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Blood from the body arrives in the venae cavae, which empty into the right atrium. From there it passes through the tricuspid valve, the right atrioventricular valve, and fills the right ventricle. The right ventricle then pumps it toward the lungs.","notes":["Correct. Right atrium, then tricuspid valve, then right ventricle.","The mitral valve is on the left side, between the left atrium and left ventricle. This sequence puts a left-side valve on the right side, a common mix-up between the two atrioventricular valves.","This lists the right structures in reverse order. Blood always moves from atrium to ventricle, so the atrium comes first; this order would mean blood flowing backward.","This is the correct sequence for oxygen-rich blood returning from the lungs through the pulmonary veins. It would be right if the question started on the left side of the heart."],"lt":"t-heart-blood-flow","lid":"W3-147","comp":61,"competencyId":"cv-blood-pathway"},{"id":"L03498","dok":2,"q":"Blood has left the pulmonary trunk through the pulmonary arteries. Which sequence does it follow next?","a":"Lungs, pulmonary veins, left atrium.","options":["Lungs, pulmonary veins, right atrium","Aorta, lungs, left atrium","Lungs, pulmonary veins, left atrium","Lungs, venae cavae, right atrium"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The pulmonary arteries carry oxygen-poor blood into the lungs, where it picks up oxygen in the capillaries around the alveoli. The now oxygen-rich blood drains into the pulmonary veins, and the four pulmonary veins empty into the left atrium. This completes the pulmonary circuit.","notes":["The pulmonary veins do drain the lungs, but they empty into the left atrium. The right atrium receives the venae cavae and the coronary sinus, so this sequence ends in the wrong chamber.","The aorta carries blood from the left ventricle to the body and is not part of the pulmonary circuit. It comes later, after the left atrium and left ventricle.","Correct. Lungs, then pulmonary veins, then left atrium.","The venae cavae return oxygen-poor blood from the body to the right atrium at the end of the systemic circuit. This sequence would fit blood coming back from the body, not from the lungs."],"lt":"t-heart-blood-flow","lid":"W3-148","comp":61,"competencyId":"cv-blood-pathway"},{"id":"L03499","dok":2,"q":"Starting in the left atrium, which sequence does blood follow next?","a":"Mitral valve, left ventricle, aortic valve.","options":["Mitral valve, left ventricle, aortic valve","Aortic valve, left ventricle, mitral valve","Mitral valve, left ventricle, pulmonary valve","Tricuspid valve, left ventricle, aortic valve"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"From the left atrium, blood passes through the mitral valve, the left atrioventricular valve, into the left ventricle. The left ventricle, the thickest-walled chamber, then contracts and pushes the blood through the aortic valve into the aorta. Each valve opens only in the forward direction, so the order is fixed.","notes":["Correct. Mitral valve, then left ventricle, then aortic valve.","These are the right valves listed backward. Blood enters the left ventricle through the mitral valve and leaves through the aortic valve, never the reverse.","The pulmonary valve is the exit of the right ventricle, not the left. This sequence would need to be on the right side, leading to the pulmonary trunk, for that valve to appear.","The tricuspid valve is the right atrioventricular valve, between the right atrium and right ventricle. It would start the sequence if the question began in the right atrium."],"lt":"t-heart-blood-flow","lid":"W3-149","comp":61,"competencyId":"cv-blood-pathway"},{"id":"L03501","dok":1,"q":"The pulmonary valve sits between which two structures?","a":"Right ventricle and pulmonary trunk.","options":["Right atrium and right ventricle","Pulmonary trunk and pulmonary arteries","Left ventricle and aorta","Right ventricle and pulmonary trunk"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The pulmonary valve is a semilunar valve with three pocket-shaped cusps at the exit of the right ventricle. Blood pushed out of the right ventricle passes through it into the pulmonary trunk, and when the ventricle relaxes the cusps fill and close so blood cannot fall back into the ventricle.","notes":["The tricuspid valve sits between the right atrium and right ventricle. Choose this pair for the right atrioventricular valve.","The pulmonary trunk simply divides into the right and left pulmonary arteries, with no valve at the split. This pair would be the answer for where the pulmonary trunk branches, not where a valve sits.","The aortic valve sits between the left ventricle and the aorta. It is the left-side twin of the pulmonary valve and would be right for the same question about the aortic valve.","Correct. The pulmonary valve guards the opening from the right ventricle into the pulmonary trunk."],"lt":"t-heart-blood-flow","lid":"W3-155","comp":58,"competencyId":"cv-valves"},{"id":"L03502","dok":2,"q":"Which structure lies immediately before the mitral valve in the pathway of blood?","a":"Left atrium.","options":["Left ventricle","Right atrium","Left atrium","Pulmonary trunk"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The mitral valve is the left atrioventricular valve, so it connects the left atrium above to the left ventricle below. Blood collects in the left atrium from the pulmonary veins and then passes through the mitral valve into the left ventricle, which makes the left atrium the structure just before it.","notes":["The left ventricle comes immediately after the mitral valve, not before it. It would be the answer for the chamber that the mitral valve opens into.","The right atrium sits immediately before the tricuspid valve, the right atrioventricular valve. Choose it if the question asked about the tricuspid valve instead.","Correct. The left atrium empties through the mitral valve.","The pulmonary trunk is the vessel just past the pulmonary valve, carrying blood to the lungs. It is several steps earlier in the pathway and has no direct connection to the mitral valve."],"lt":"t-heart-blood-flow","lid":"W3-156","comp":61,"competencyId":"cv-blood-pathway"},{"id":"L03503","dok":1,"q":"The aortic valve sits between which two structures?","a":"Left ventricle and aorta.","options":["Right ventricle and pulmonary trunk","Left atrium and left ventricle","Left ventricle and aorta","Aorta and brachiocephalic trunk"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The aortic valve is a semilunar valve with three cusps that guards the exit of the left ventricle. Blood forced out of the left ventricle passes through it into the ascending aorta. When the ventricle relaxes, the cusps fill and close so blood cannot leak back into the left ventricle.","notes":["The pulmonary valve sits between the right ventricle and the pulmonary trunk. It is the right-side twin of the aortic valve.","The mitral valve sits between the left atrium and the left ventricle. Choose this pair for the left atrioventricular valve, one step earlier in the pathway.","Correct. The aortic valve opens from the left ventricle into the aorta.","The brachiocephalic trunk is the first branch off the aortic arch, and there is no valve where it leaves the aorta. This pair would describe where the aortic arch first branches, not where a heart valve sits."],"lt":"t-heart-blood-flow","lid":"W3-157","comp":58,"competencyId":"cv-valves"},{"id":"L03504","dok":2,"q":"When the right ventricle contracts, which valve opens to let blood leave it?","a":"Pulmonary valve.","options":["Mitral valve","Aortic valve","Tricuspid valve","Pulmonary valve"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"When the right ventricle contracts, the pressure inside it pushes the pulmonary valve open and blood flows into the pulmonary trunk. At the same moment the tricuspid valve is forced shut, so blood cannot return to the right atrium. Each ventricle has an inlet valve that closes and an outlet valve that opens when it contracts.","notes":["The mitral valve is the inlet of the left ventricle and closes when that ventricle contracts. It would be right for the valve that stops backflow into the left atrium.","The aortic valve opens when the left ventricle contracts, letting blood into the aorta. Choose it for the outlet valve of the left ventricle.","The tricuspid valve is the inlet of the right ventricle, and it closes, not opens, when the ventricle contracts. It would be the answer for the valve that stops blood from flowing back into the right atrium.","Correct. The pulmonary valve is the outlet of the right ventricle."],"lt":"t-heart-blood-flow","lid":"W3-159","comp":58,"competencyId":"cv-valves"},{"id":"L03505","dok":1,"q":"Which chamber contracts to push blood into the aorta?","a":"Left ventricle.","options":["Left ventricle","Left atrium","Right ventricle","Right atrium"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The left ventricle is the last chamber blood passes through before leaving for the body. When it contracts, it forces oxygen-rich blood through the aortic valve into the aorta. Because it has to push blood through the entire systemic circuit, it has the thickest wall of the four chambers.","notes":["Correct. The left ventricle pumps blood through the aortic valve into the aorta.","The left atrium contracts to top off the left ventricle through the mitral valve. It sits one step earlier and never connects directly to the aorta.","The right ventricle pushes blood into the pulmonary trunk, not the aorta. It would be the answer for the chamber that sends blood to the lungs.","The right atrium pushes blood into the right ventricle through the tricuspid valve. Choose it for the chamber that receives the venae cavae."],"lt":"t-heart-blood-flow","lid":"W3-160","comp":57,"competencyId":"cv-chambers"},{"id":"L03509","dok":2,"q":"Why is the left ventricle the last chamber blood passes through before entering the systemic circuit?","a":"It pumps blood directly into the aorta through the aortic valve.","options":["It pumps blood straight into the aorta through the aortic valve","It empties into the right atrium through the coronary sinus","It receives blood directly from both venae cavae","It sends blood to the lungs through the pulmonary trunk"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The systemic circuit begins at the aorta, and the left ventricle is the only chamber that opens into it. Blood passes from the left atrium through the mitral valve into the left ventricle, and when the ventricle contracts it pushes the blood through the aortic valve into the aorta. No other chamber lies between the left ventricle and the body.","notes":["Correct. The left ventricle opens directly into the aorta, the start of the systemic circuit.","The coronary sinus collects blood from the heart wall and empties into the right atrium. Chambers do not drain into it; it would be the answer for how venous blood from the myocardium returns.","The venae cavae empty into the right atrium, the first chamber of the pathway, not the last. This statement describes the right atrium.","The pulmonary trunk leaves the right ventricle and carries blood to the lungs. This describes the right ventricle, the last chamber before the pulmonary circuit."],"lt":"t-heart-blood-flow","lid":"W3-188","comp":57,"competencyId":"cv-chambers"},{"id":"L03510","dok":2,"q":"Trace blood from the venae cavae to the pulmonary trunk. Which sequence is correct?","a":"Right atrium, tricuspid valve, right ventricle, pulmonary valve.","options":["Right ventricle, tricuspid valve, right atrium, pulmonary valve","Right atrium, pulmonary valve, right ventricle, tricuspid valve","Right atrium, tricuspid valve, right ventricle, pulmonary valve","Right atrium, mitral valve, right ventricle, aortic valve"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The venae cavae empty into the right atrium. Blood passes through the tricuspid valve into the right ventricle, and when the right ventricle contracts it pushes the blood through the pulmonary valve into the pulmonary trunk. Each side of the heart follows the same pattern: atrium, atrioventricular valve, ventricle, semilunar valve.","notes":["This puts the right ventricle before the right atrium, which would mean blood flowing backward. The atrium always receives blood first and then fills the ventricle.","This swaps the two right-side valves. The tricuspid valve lies between the right atrium and right ventricle and the pulmonary valve guards the exit of the right ventricle, so the tricuspid comes first.","Correct. Right atrium, tricuspid valve, right ventricle, pulmonary valve, then the pulmonary trunk.","This uses the left-side valves on the right side. The mitral and aortic valves belong to the left heart; this pattern would fit a trace from the left atrium to the aorta."],"lt":"t-heart-blood-flow","lid":"W3-193","comp":61,"competencyId":"cv-blood-pathway"},{"id":"L03511","dok":2,"q":"Trace blood from the pulmonary veins to the aorta. Which sequence is correct?","a":"Left atrium, mitral valve, left ventricle, aortic valve.","options":["Left atrium, aortic valve, left ventricle, mitral valve","Left ventricle, mitral valve, left atrium, aortic valve","Left atrium, tricuspid valve, left ventricle, pulmonary valve","Left atrium, mitral valve, left ventricle, aortic valve"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The pulmonary veins empty into the left atrium. Blood passes through the mitral valve into the left ventricle, and when the left ventricle contracts it forces the blood through the aortic valve into the aorta. This mirrors the right side, with the mitral valve as the atrioventricular valve and the aortic valve as the semilunar valve.","notes":["This swaps the two left-side valves. The mitral valve is the inlet of the left ventricle and the aortic valve is its outlet, so the mitral valve comes first.","This places the left ventricle before the left atrium, which would mean blood flowing backward. The pulmonary veins enter the left atrium, so the atrium has to come first.","The tricuspid and pulmonary valves are on the right side of the heart. This sequence would fit a trace through the right heart from the venae cavae to the pulmonary trunk.","Correct. Left atrium, mitral valve, left ventricle, aortic valve, then the aorta."],"lt":"t-heart-blood-flow","lid":"W3-194","comp":61,"competencyId":"cv-blood-pathway"},{"id":"L03512","dok":2,"q":"Blood returning from the body passes through the pulmonary circuit before the left ventricle sends it back out. Why does this order matter?","a":"Blood must pick up oxygen in the lungs before it is delivered to the body's tissues.","options":["Blood must pick up oxygen in the lungs before reaching the tissues","Blood must lose its pressure in the lungs before entering the aorta","The lungs add nutrients absorbed from the digestive tract","The lungs remove worn-out red blood cells from circulation"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Blood returning from the body is oxygen-poor because the tissues have used its O2. The right side of the heart sends this blood to the lungs, where it picks up oxygen and gives off CO2, and only then does it return to the left side to be pumped out through the aorta. Running the lung loop first means the body always receives freshly oxygenated blood.","notes":["Correct. The pulmonary circuit oxygenates blood before the systemic circuit delivers it.","Blood does lose pressure as it passes through the lung capillaries, but that is not the reason for the order. The left ventricle has to rebuild the pressure to drive blood through the body; the purpose of the lung loop is oxygenation.","Nutrients from digestion enter the blood in the intestines and travel to the liver through the hepatic portal system. The lungs exchange gases, not nutrients.","Removing worn-out red blood cells is a job of the spleen and liver, not the lungs. Choose the spleen for a question about where old red blood cells are broken down."],"lt":"t-heart-blood-flow","lid":"W3-196","comp":69,"competencyId":"bvn-circulatory-routes"},{"id":"L03895","dok":2,"q":"In the normal pathway of blood through the heart, oxygen-poor blood leaving the superior vena cava enters which chamber next?","a":"The right atrium.","options":["Left ventricle","Right atrium","Left atrium","Right ventricle"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The superior vena cava returns oxygen-poor blood from the head, neck, and upper limbs and empties straight into the right atrium. The right atrium is the receiving chamber for blood coming back from the body, so it is always the first chamber blood enters after either vena cava. From there the blood moves on through the tricuspid valve into the right ventricle.","notes":["The left ventricle receives blood from the left atrium through the mitral valve and pumps it into the aorta. It would be the answer if the question started at the mitral valve.","Correct. The superior vena cava empties directly into the right atrium.","The left atrium receives oxygen-rich blood from the four pulmonary veins, not blood from the venae cavae. Pick it if the question started at the pulmonary veins.","The right ventricle is one step later. Blood reaches it only after leaving the right atrium and crossing the tricuspid valve. It would be the answer if the question started at the tricuspid valve."],"lt":"t-pathway-heart-blood-flow","lid":"W3-701","comp":61,"competencyId":"cv-blood-pathway"},{"id":"L03896","dok":2,"q":"Blood returning from the legs travels up the inferior vena cava. Which chamber of the heart does it enter next?","a":"The right atrium.","options":["Left ventricle","Left atrium","Right atrium","Right ventricle"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The inferior vena cava carries oxygen-poor blood from the trunk and lower limbs and opens into the right atrium, just as the superior vena cava does from above. Both venae cavae deliver blood from the body to the right atrium, which starts the trip toward the lungs. Next the blood passes through the tricuspid valve into the right ventricle.","notes":["The left ventricle pumps oxygen-rich blood into the aorta and never receives blood straight from the venae cavae. It would be right if the question started at the mitral valve.","The left atrium receives oxygen-rich blood from the lungs through the pulmonary veins. It would be the answer if the blood were returning from the lungs instead of the legs.","Correct. The inferior vena cava empties into the right atrium.","The right ventricle is the chamber after the right atrium, reached by crossing the tricuspid valve. Pick it if the question started at the tricuspid valve."],"lt":"t-pathway-heart-blood-flow","lid":"W3-702","comp":61,"competencyId":"cv-blood-pathway"},{"id":"L03897","dok":2,"q":"In the normal pathway of blood through the heart, blood leaving the right atrium passes through which valve next?","a":"The tricuspid valve.","options":["Mitral valve","Pulmonary valve","Tricuspid valve","Aortic valve"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The tricuspid valve is the atrioventricular valve between the right atrium and the right ventricle. An atrioventricular valve sits between an atrium and the ventricle below it. Blood in the right atrium flows down through the tricuspid valve into the right ventricle, and the valve closes to keep it from flowing back.","notes":["The mitral valve is the atrioventricular valve on the left side, between the left atrium and the left ventricle. It would be the answer if the blood were leaving the left atrium.","The pulmonary valve sits between the right ventricle and the pulmonary trunk, one chamber later. Pick it if the question asked which valve blood crosses as it leaves the right ventricle.","Correct. The tricuspid valve guards the opening from the right atrium into the right ventricle.","The aortic valve sits between the left ventricle and the aorta, on the opposite side of the heart. It would be right if the question asked which valve blood crosses as it leaves the left ventricle."],"lt":"t-pathway-heart-blood-flow","lid":"W3-703","comp":61,"competencyId":"cv-blood-pathway"},{"id":"L03898","dok":2,"q":"In the normal pathway of blood through the heart, blood that has just crossed the tricuspid valve enters which chamber?","a":"The right ventricle.","options":["Left ventricle","Right atrium","Left atrium","Right ventricle"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The tricuspid valve lies between the right atrium and the right ventricle, so blood that crosses it lands in the right ventricle. The right ventricle then contracts and pumps the blood upward through the pulmonary valve toward the lungs. Its chordae tendineae and papillary muscles hold the tricuspid cusps shut during that contraction.","notes":["The left ventricle sits below the mitral valve, not the tricuspid valve. Pick it if the blood had just crossed the mitral valve.","The right atrium is the chamber blood came from before the tricuspid valve, not the one it enters after. It would be the answer if the question started at the venae cavae.","The left atrium receives blood from the pulmonary veins and sits above the mitral valve. It would be the answer if the question started at the pulmonary veins.","Correct. Blood that crosses the tricuspid valve enters the right ventricle."],"lt":"t-pathway-heart-blood-flow","lid":"W3-704","comp":61,"competencyId":"cv-blood-pathway"},{"id":"L03899","dok":2,"q":"In the normal pathway of blood through the heart, the right ventricle pumps blood out through which valve?","a":"The pulmonary valve.","options":["Aortic valve","Pulmonary valve","Tricuspid valve","Mitral valve"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The pulmonary valve is a semilunar valve between the right ventricle and the pulmonary trunk. Semilunar valves have pocket-shaped cusps and guard the exits from the ventricles into the great arteries. When the right ventricle contracts, the pulmonary valve opens and blood moves into the pulmonary trunk on its way to the lungs.","notes":["The aortic valve is the exit valve of the left ventricle, leading into the aorta. It would be the answer if the question asked how the left ventricle sends blood to the body.","Correct. The pulmonary valve is the exit from the right ventricle into the pulmonary trunk.","The tricuspid valve is the entrance to the right ventricle from the right atrium, so it closes when the ventricle contracts. Pick it if the question asked which valve blood crosses to get into the right ventricle.","The mitral valve is the entrance to the left ventricle from the left atrium. It would be right if the question asked which valve blood crosses on its way out of the left atrium."],"lt":"t-pathway-heart-blood-flow","lid":"W3-705","comp":61,"competencyId":"cv-blood-pathway"},{"id":"L03900","dok":2,"q":"In the normal pathway of blood through the heart, blood that has just passed the pulmonary valve enters which vessel?","a":"The pulmonary trunk.","options":["Aorta","Pulmonary veins","Right and left pulmonary arteries","Pulmonary trunk"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The pulmonary valve opens directly into the pulmonary trunk, the single large artery that leaves the right ventricle. A short distance above the heart, the pulmonary trunk divides into the right and left pulmonary arteries, which carry the oxygen-poor blood to the two lungs.","notes":["The aorta leaves the left ventricle through the aortic valve and carries oxygen-rich blood to the body. Pick it if the blood had just crossed the aortic valve.","The pulmonary veins carry oxygen-rich blood from the lungs back to the left atrium, so they are at the far end of the pulmonary circuit. They would be the answer if the question asked which vessels leave the lungs.","The right and left pulmonary arteries come one step later, where the pulmonary trunk branches. They would be the answer if the question started at the pulmonary trunk.","Correct. The pulmonary valve opens into the pulmonary trunk."],"lt":"t-pathway-heart-blood-flow","lid":"W3-706","comp":61,"competencyId":"cv-blood-pathway"},{"id":"L03901","dok":1,"q":"In the adult pathway of blood, the pulmonary trunk divides into which vessels?","a":"The right and left pulmonary arteries.","options":["Four pulmonary veins","Right and left pulmonary arteries","Right and left brachiocephalic veins","Right and left coronary arteries"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The pulmonary trunk is a short, wide artery that leaves the right ventricle and splits into the right and left pulmonary arteries. Each pulmonary artery enters a lung at its hilum and carries oxygen-poor blood to the lung capillaries. These are arteries because they carry blood away from the heart, even though the blood in them is low in oxygen.","notes":["The four pulmonary veins return oxygen-rich blood from the lungs to the left atrium. Pick them if the question asked which vessels blood takes after the lungs.","Correct. The pulmonary trunk divides into the right and left pulmonary arteries.","The brachiocephalic veins drain the head and upper limbs and join to form the superior vena cava. They would fit a question about venous return from the head and arms, not the pulmonary circuit.","The right and left coronary arteries branch from the base of the aorta, not the pulmonary trunk, and supply the heart wall itself. They would be the answer if the question asked what branches from the base of the aorta."],"lt":"t-pathway-heart-blood-flow","lid":"W3-707","comp":62,"competencyId":"bvn-great-vessels"},{"id":"L03902","dok":2,"q":"In the pulmonary circuit, blood that has picked up oxygen in the lung capillaries leaves the lungs through which vessels?","a":"The pulmonary veins.","options":["Pulmonary veins","Pulmonary arteries","Pulmonary trunk","Venae cavae"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"After gas exchange in the lung capillaries, oxygen-rich blood collects into the pulmonary veins. There are four of them, two from each lung, and they carry the blood back to the left atrium. They are veins because they carry blood toward the heart, even though their blood is rich in oxygen.","notes":["Correct. Oxygen-rich blood leaves the lungs in the pulmonary veins.","The pulmonary arteries carry oxygen-poor blood into the lungs, the step just before the capillaries. They would be the answer if the question asked which vessels deliver blood to the lungs.","The pulmonary trunk is the large artery leaving the right ventricle, two steps before the lungs. It would be the answer if the question started at the pulmonary valve.","The venae cavae return oxygen-poor blood from the body to the right atrium. Pick them if the question asked how blood from the systemic circuit returns to the heart."],"lt":"t-pathway-heart-blood-flow","lid":"W3-708","comp":62,"competencyId":"bvn-great-vessels"},{"id":"L03903","dok":2,"q":"In the normal pathway of blood through the heart, oxygen-rich blood in the pulmonary veins empties into which chamber?","a":"The left atrium.","options":["Right atrium","Left atrium","Right ventricle","Left ventricle"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The four pulmonary veins open into the left atrium, which receives the oxygen-rich blood returning from the lungs. The left atrium makes up most of the base of the heart, on its posterior side. From there blood passes through the mitral valve into the left ventricle.","notes":["The right atrium receives oxygen-poor blood from the venae cavae and the coronary sinus. It would be the answer if the question started at either vena cava.","Correct. The pulmonary veins empty into the left atrium.","The right ventricle receives blood from the right atrium and pumps it to the lungs. It would be the answer if the question started at the tricuspid valve.","The left ventricle comes one step later, after the mitral valve. Pick it if the question started at the mitral valve."],"lt":"t-pathway-heart-blood-flow","lid":"W3-709","comp":61,"competencyId":"cv-blood-pathway"},{"id":"L03904","dok":2,"q":"In the normal pathway of blood through the heart, blood leaving the left atrium passes through which valve next?","a":"The mitral valve.","options":["Aortic valve","Tricuspid valve","Pulmonary valve","Mitral valve"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The mitral valve, also called the bicuspid valve because it has two cusps, is the atrioventricular valve between the left atrium and the left ventricle. Blood flows down through it into the left ventricle. Chordae tendineae and papillary muscles hold its cusps shut when the left ventricle contracts.","notes":["The aortic valve is the exit from the left ventricle into the aorta, one chamber later. It would be the answer if the question asked which valve blood crosses as it leaves the left ventricle.","The tricuspid valve is the atrioventricular valve on the right side, between the right atrium and the right ventricle. Pick it if the blood were leaving the right atrium.","The pulmonary valve is the exit from the right ventricle into the pulmonary trunk. It would be right if the question asked how blood leaves the right ventricle.","Correct. The mitral valve separates the left atrium from the left ventricle."],"lt":"t-pathway-heart-blood-flow","lid":"W3-710","comp":61,"competencyId":"cv-blood-pathway"},{"id":"L03905","dok":2,"q":"In the normal pathway of blood through the heart, blood that has just crossed the mitral valve enters which chamber?","a":"The left ventricle.","options":["Left ventricle","Right atrium","Left atrium","Right ventricle"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The mitral valve lies between the left atrium and the left ventricle, so blood that crosses it enters the left ventricle. The left ventricle has the thickest wall of the four chambers because it pumps blood out through the aortic valve to the entire body.","notes":["Correct. Blood that crosses the mitral valve enters the left ventricle.","The right atrium receives blood from the venae cavae and never receives blood through the mitral valve. It would be the answer if the question started at the superior or inferior vena cava.","The left atrium is where the blood was before it crossed the mitral valve. It would be the answer if the question started at the pulmonary veins.","The right ventricle sits below the tricuspid valve, on the right side of the heart. Pick it if the blood had just crossed the tricuspid valve."],"lt":"t-pathway-heart-blood-flow","lid":"W3-711","comp":61,"competencyId":"cv-blood-pathway"},{"id":"L03906","dok":2,"q":"In the normal pathway of blood through the heart, the left ventricle pumps blood out through which valve?","a":"The aortic valve.","options":["Mitral valve","Pulmonary valve","Tricuspid valve","Aortic valve"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The aortic valve is the semilunar valve between the left ventricle and the aorta. When the left ventricle contracts, the aortic valve opens and oxygen-rich blood flows into the aorta for delivery to the body. When the ventricle relaxes, the cusps fill and close so blood cannot slip back into the ventricle.","notes":["The mitral valve is the entrance to the left ventricle from the left atrium, and it closes when the ventricle contracts. It would be the answer if the question asked which valve blood crosses to get into the left ventricle.","The pulmonary valve is the exit from the right ventricle into the pulmonary trunk. Pick it if the question asked how the right ventricle sends blood to the lungs.","The tricuspid valve is the entrance to the right ventricle from the right atrium. It would fit a question about blood leaving the right atrium.","Correct. The aortic valve is the exit from the left ventricle into the aorta."],"lt":"t-pathway-heart-blood-flow","lid":"W3-712","comp":61,"competencyId":"cv-blood-pathway"},{"id":"L03907","dok":2,"q":"In the normal pathway of blood through the heart, blood that has just passed the aortic valve enters which vessel?","a":"The aorta.","options":["Pulmonary veins","Inferior vena cava","Pulmonary trunk","Aorta"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The aortic valve opens directly into the aorta, the largest artery in the body. The aorta carries oxygen-rich blood from the left ventricle out to the systemic circuit. Passing the aortic valve is the last step of blood's trip through the heart before it heads to the body.","notes":["The pulmonary veins bring oxygen-rich blood from the lungs into the left atrium, well before the aortic valve. Pick them if the question asked which vessels leave the lungs.","The inferior vena cava returns oxygen-poor blood from the trunk and legs to the right atrium. It would be the answer if the question asked which vessel brings blood from the lower body back to the heart.","The pulmonary trunk receives blood through the pulmonary valve from the right ventricle. It would be the answer if the blood had just passed the pulmonary valve.","Correct. The aortic valve opens into the aorta."],"lt":"t-pathway-heart-blood-flow","lid":"W3-713","comp":61,"competencyId":"cv-blood-pathway"},{"id":"L03908","dok":2,"q":"Which sequence correctly traces blood from the superior vena cava to the pulmonary trunk?","a":"Superior vena cava, right atrium, tricuspid valve, right ventricle, pulmonary valve, pulmonary trunk.","options":["Superior vena cava, right ventricle, tricuspid valve, right atrium, pulmonary valve, pulmonary trunk","Superior vena cava, right atrium, tricuspid valve, right ventricle, pulmonary valve, pulmonary trunk","Superior vena cava, right atrium, tricuspid valve, right ventricle, aortic valve, pulmonary trunk","Superior vena cava, right atrium, mitral valve, right ventricle, pulmonary valve, pulmonary trunk"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The superior vena cava empties into the right atrium. Blood then crosses the tricuspid valve into the right ventricle, which pumps it through the pulmonary valve into the pulmonary trunk. Every structure on this route belongs to the right side of the heart, which drives the pulmonary circuit.","notes":["This sequence reverses the right atrium and right ventricle. The atrium is the receiving chamber, so blood from a vena cava must enter the right atrium first. The ventricle is the pumping chamber that comes after the tricuspid valve.","Correct. This is the full right-side route from the superior vena cava to the pulmonary trunk.","This sequence uses the aortic valve as the exit from the right ventricle. The aortic valve guards the exit from the left ventricle into the aorta, so it would belong in a trace that ends at the aorta.","This sequence puts the mitral valve between the right atrium and right ventricle. The mitral valve is on the left side, between the left atrium and left ventricle, so it would belong in a trace from the pulmonary veins to the aorta."],"lt":"t-pathway-full-tracing","lid":"W3-714","comp":61,"competencyId":"cv-blood-pathway"},{"id":"L03909","dok":2,"q":"Blood travels from the inferior vena cava to the lungs. Which two heart valves does it cross, in order?","a":"The tricuspid valve, then the pulmonary valve.","options":["Pulmonary valve, then tricuspid valve","Tricuspid valve, then pulmonary valve","Mitral valve, then aortic valve","Tricuspid valve, then aortic valve"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Blood from the inferior vena cava enters the right atrium and crosses the tricuspid valve into the right ventricle. The right ventricle then pumps it through the pulmonary valve into the pulmonary trunk, which branches to the lungs. On each side of the heart, blood crosses the atrioventricular valve first and the semilunar valve second.","notes":["These are the right valves in the wrong order. Blood must enter the right ventricle through the tricuspid valve before the ventricle can pump it out through the pulmonary valve.","Correct. The tricuspid valve comes first, then the pulmonary valve.","The mitral and aortic valves are the two left-side valves. Blood crosses them, in that order, on the way from the lungs to the body, so they would be the answer for a trace from the pulmonary veins to the aorta.","The tricuspid valve is right, but the aortic valve is the exit from the left ventricle, not the right. It would fit only if the question were about blood leaving the left ventricle for the body."],"lt":"t-pathway-full-tracing","lid":"W3-715","comp":61,"competencyId":"cv-blood-pathway"},{"id":"L03910","dok":2,"q":"Which sequence correctly traces oxygen-rich blood from the lungs to the aorta?","a":"Pulmonary veins, left atrium, mitral valve, left ventricle, aortic valve, aorta.","options":["Pulmonary veins, left atrium, tricuspid valve, left ventricle, aortic valve, aorta","Pulmonary veins, left ventricle, mitral valve, left atrium, aortic valve, aorta","Pulmonary arteries, left atrium, mitral valve, left ventricle, aortic valve, aorta","Pulmonary veins, left atrium, mitral valve, left ventricle, aortic valve, aorta"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Oxygen-rich blood leaves the lungs in the pulmonary veins, which empty into the left atrium. It crosses the mitral valve into the left ventricle, and the left ventricle pumps it through the aortic valve into the aorta. This is the left side of the heart, which drives the systemic circuit.","notes":["This sequence uses the tricuspid valve on the left side. The tricuspid valve sits between the right atrium and right ventricle, so it belongs in a trace from the venae cavae to the lungs.","This sequence swaps the left atrium and left ventricle. The pulmonary veins empty into the left atrium, the receiving chamber, and the left ventricle comes after the mitral valve.","This sequence starts with the pulmonary arteries. Those carry oxygen-poor blood into the lungs from the pulmonary trunk, so they belong in a trace from the right ventricle to the lungs, not out of the lungs.","Correct. This is the full left-side route from the lungs to the aorta."],"lt":"t-pathway-full-tracing","lid":"W3-716","comp":61,"competencyId":"cv-blood-pathway"},{"id":"L03911","dok":2,"q":"Which chamber pumps blood into the pulmonary circuit, and which chamber receives it when it returns?","a":"It leaves from the right ventricle and returns to the left atrium.","options":["Leaves from the left ventricle, returns to the right atrium","Leaves from the right ventricle, returns to the left atrium","Leaves from the right atrium, returns to the left ventricle","Leaves from the left atrium, returns to the right ventricle"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The pulmonary circuit is the loop to the lungs and back. The right ventricle pumps oxygen-poor blood through the pulmonary valve into the pulmonary trunk and pulmonary arteries. After gas exchange in the lungs, the pulmonary veins return oxygen-rich blood to the left atrium, which ends the circuit.","notes":["This describes the systemic circuit, which leaves the left ventricle through the aorta and returns through the venae cavae to the right atrium. It would be the answer if the question asked about the systemic circuit.","Correct. The right ventricle sends blood to the lungs and the left atrium receives it back.","Atria are receiving chambers, so the right atrium does not pump blood into a circuit, and the left ventricle does not receive blood from the lungs. This pairing mixes up which chambers receive and which pump.","This reverses the true flow. The left atrium receives blood from the lungs rather than sending it out, and the right ventricle never receives blood straight from the lungs."],"lt":"t-pathway-full-tracing","lid":"W3-717","comp":69,"competencyId":"bvn-circulatory-routes"},{"id":"L03912","dok":2,"q":"In the systemic circuit, which vessel carries blood out of the heart, and which vessels bring it back?","a":"The aorta carries it out, and the venae cavae bring it back.","options":["Pulmonary trunk carries it out, venae cavae bring it back","Aorta carries it out, pulmonary veins bring it back","Aorta carries it out, venae cavae bring it back","Pulmonary trunk carries it out, pulmonary veins bring it back"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The systemic circuit is the loop from the left side of the heart out to the body and back to the right side. The left ventricle pumps oxygen-rich blood into the aorta, which branches into arteries, arterioles, and capillaries throughout the body. The blood then returns through venules and veins to the superior and inferior venae cavae, which empty into the right atrium.","notes":["The venae cavae are the right returning vessels, but the pulmonary trunk carries blood to the lungs, not the body. It is the outgoing vessel of the pulmonary circuit.","The aorta is the right outgoing vessel, but the pulmonary veins return blood from the lungs, not from the body. They belong to the pulmonary circuit.","Correct. The aorta begins the systemic circuit and the venae cavae end it.","The pulmonary trunk and pulmonary veins are the outgoing and returning vessels of the pulmonary circuit. They would be the answer if the question asked about the loop to the lungs."],"lt":"t-pathway-full-tracing","lid":"W3-718","comp":69,"competencyId":"bvn-circulatory-routes"},{"id":"L03928","dok":2,"q":"Tracing blood backward through the heart, which chamber does blood occupy just before it crosses the tricuspid valve?","a":"The right atrium.","options":["Right atrium","Left ventricle","Right ventricle","Left atrium"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The tricuspid valve separates the right atrium above from the right ventricle below. Blood in the right atrium flows down through the tricuspid valve into the right ventricle, so the right atrium is the chamber just before it. Reading the pathway backward like this checks that you know what each valve separates.","notes":["Correct. Blood is in the right atrium just before it crosses the tricuspid valve.","The left ventricle sits below the mitral valve and above the aortic valve. It would be the answer if the question asked which chamber comes just before the aortic valve.","The right ventricle is where blood arrives after crossing the tricuspid valve, not where it comes from. It would be the answer if the question asked which chamber comes just before the pulmonary valve.","The left atrium sits above the mitral valve, not the tricuspid valve. It would be the answer if the question asked which chamber comes just before the mitral valve."],"lt":"t-pathway-next-step-recall","lid":"W3-734","comp":61,"competencyId":"cv-blood-pathway"},{"id":"L03929","dok":2,"q":"Tracing blood backward through the heart, blood that has just passed the pulmonary valve was pumped out by which chamber?","a":"The right ventricle.","options":["Right ventricle","Left ventricle","Left atrium","Right atrium"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The pulmonary valve sits between the right ventricle and the pulmonary trunk. When the right ventricle contracts, the pulmonary valve opens and blood moves into the pulmonary trunk. So any blood that has just passed the pulmonary valve came from the right ventricle.","notes":["Correct. The right ventricle pumps blood out through the pulmonary valve.","The left ventricle pumps blood through the aortic valve into the aorta, not through the pulmonary valve. It would be the answer if the blood had just passed the aortic valve.","The left atrium empties through the mitral valve into the left ventricle. It would be the answer if the blood had just crossed the mitral valve.","The right atrium empties through the tricuspid valve, not the pulmonary valve. It would be the answer if the blood had just crossed the tricuspid valve."],"lt":"t-pathway-next-step-recall","lid":"W3-735","comp":61,"competencyId":"cv-blood-pathway"},{"id":"L03930","dok":2,"q":"Tracing blood backward through the heart, blood that has just crossed the mitral valve came from which chamber?","a":"The left atrium.","options":["Right ventricle","Right atrium","Left ventricle","Left atrium"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The mitral valve is the atrioventricular valve between the left atrium and the left ventricle. Blood from the pulmonary veins collects in the left atrium and then flows down through the mitral valve into the left ventricle. So blood that has just crossed the mitral valve came from the left atrium.","notes":["The right ventricle pumps blood through the pulmonary valve, not the mitral valve. It would be the answer if the blood had just passed the pulmonary valve.","The right atrium empties through the tricuspid valve on the right side. It would be the answer if the blood had just crossed the tricuspid valve.","The left ventricle is where blood ends up after crossing the mitral valve, not where it comes from. It would be the answer if the blood had just passed the aortic valve.","Correct. Blood crosses the mitral valve on its way from the left atrium to the left ventricle."],"lt":"t-pathway-next-step-recall","lid":"W3-736","comp":61,"competencyId":"cv-blood-pathway"},{"id":"L03931","dok":2,"q":"Tracing blood backward through the heart, blood that has just passed the aortic valve was pumped out by which chamber?","a":"The left ventricle.","options":["Left ventricle","Right ventricle","Left atrium","Right atrium"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The aortic valve sits between the left ventricle and the aorta. The left ventricle, the chamber with the thickest wall, contracts and forces blood through the aortic valve into the aorta for delivery to the body. Blood that has just passed the aortic valve therefore came from the left ventricle.","notes":["Correct. The left ventricle pumps blood out through the aortic valve.","The right ventricle pumps blood through the pulmonary valve into the pulmonary trunk. It would be the answer if the blood had just passed the pulmonary valve.","The left atrium empties through the mitral valve into the left ventricle, one step earlier. It would be the answer if the blood had just crossed the mitral valve.","The right atrium empties through the tricuspid valve into the right ventricle. It would be the answer if the blood had just crossed the tricuspid valve."],"lt":"t-pathway-next-step-recall","lid":"W3-737","comp":61,"competencyId":"cv-blood-pathway"},{"id":"L03935","dok":2,"q":"A clot from a calf vein has traveled up to the right atrium. Which valve will it cross next?","a":"The tricuspid valve.","options":["Tricuspid valve","Aortic valve","Mitral valve","Pulmonary valve"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"A clot in a vein follows normal venous return. From the right atrium, blood flows down through the tricuspid valve into the right ventricle, so the tricuspid valve is the next valve the clot meets. In a normal adult heart there is no opening in the interatrial septum, so the clot cannot cross to the left side.","notes":["Correct. From the right atrium the clot crosses the tricuspid valve into the right ventricle.","The aortic valve is the exit from the left ventricle into the aorta. It would be the answer if the clot had somehow reached the left ventricle, which a venous clot cannot do without crossing the lungs.","The mitral valve is on the left side, between the left atrium and left ventricle. A clot could only reach it by first passing through the lungs, so it would be the answer for blood leaving the left atrium.","The pulmonary valve is the exit from the right ventricle, one chamber later. The clot will cross it after it has passed the tricuspid valve and entered the right ventricle."],"lt":"t-pathway-applied-sequencing","lid":"W3-742","comp":61,"competencyId":"cv-blood-pathway"},{"id":"L03936","dok":2,"q":"A clot is being pumped out of the right ventricle. Which valve does it cross as it leaves?","a":"The pulmonary valve.","options":["Mitral valve","Pulmonary valve","Aortic valve","Tricuspid valve"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The right ventricle pumps blood out through the pulmonary valve into the pulmonary trunk. A clot riding in that blood crosses the pulmonary valve and heads for the lungs. This is why a clot from the venous side lodges in a pulmonary artery rather than going out to the body.","notes":["The mitral valve lets blood from the left atrium into the left ventricle. It would be the answer for a clot moving from the left atrium into the left ventricle.","Correct. Blood and anything in it leave the right ventricle through the pulmonary valve.","The aortic valve is the exit from the left ventricle. It would be the answer for a clot leaving the left ventricle, which would travel out to the body instead of the lungs.","The tricuspid valve is the entrance to the right ventricle, and it closes when the ventricle contracts. The clot already crossed it on the way in from the right atrium."],"lt":"t-pathway-applied-sequencing","lid":"W3-743","comp":61,"competencyId":"cv-blood-pathway"},{"id":"L03937","dok":2,"q":"A clot has just passed through the pulmonary valve. Which vessel does it enter?","a":"The pulmonary trunk.","options":["Pulmonary veins","Aorta","Pulmonary trunk","Superior vena cava"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The pulmonary valve opens into the pulmonary trunk, so a clot that passes the valve enters the pulmonary trunk. The trunk then splits into the right and left pulmonary arteries, which carry the clot toward the lungs. As those arteries branch and narrow, the clot lodges and blocks flow, which is a pulmonary embolism.","notes":["The pulmonary veins carry blood out of the lungs to the left atrium, beyond the lungs. A clot would only reach them after somehow passing through the lung capillaries.","The aorta receives blood from the left ventricle through the aortic valve. It would be the answer if the clot had just passed the aortic valve.","Correct. The pulmonary valve opens into the pulmonary trunk.","The superior vena cava brings blood from the upper body into the right atrium, much earlier in the route. It would be the answer if the question asked where a clot from an arm vein enters the heart."],"lt":"t-pathway-applied-sequencing","lid":"W3-744","comp":61,"competencyId":"cv-blood-pathway"},{"id":"L03938","dok":2,"q":"An echocardiogram shows blood leaking backward from the right ventricle into the right atrium each time the ventricle contracts. Which valve is failing to close?","a":"The tricuspid valve.","options":["Mitral valve","Aortic valve","Pulmonary valve","Tricuspid valve"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Regurgitation means blood leaking backward through a valve that does not close fully. The tricuspid valve sits between the right atrium and right ventricle and normally closes when the right ventricle contracts. If blood leaks from the right ventricle back into the right atrium, the tricuspid valve is the one failing.","notes":["The mitral valve separates the left atrium and left ventricle. It would be the answer if blood leaked from the left ventricle into the left atrium.","The aortic valve separates the left ventricle and aorta. It would be the answer if blood leaked from the aorta back into the left ventricle.","The pulmonary valve sits between the right ventricle and pulmonary trunk. If it failed, blood would leak from the pulmonary trunk back into the right ventricle, not into the right atrium.","Correct. The tricuspid valve separates the right atrium from the right ventricle."],"lt":"t-pathway-applied-sequencing","lid":"W3-745","comp":58,"competencyId":"cv-valves"},{"id":"L03939","dok":2,"q":"An echocardiogram shows blood leaking backward from the left ventricle into the left atrium each time the ventricle contracts. Which valve is failing to close?","a":"The mitral valve.","options":["Tricuspid valve","Mitral valve","Pulmonary valve","Aortic valve"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The mitral valve is the atrioventricular valve between the left atrium and the left ventricle. Its chordae tendineae and papillary muscles hold it shut when the left ventricle contracts. If blood leaks from the left ventricle back into the left atrium, the mitral valve is not closing properly.","notes":["The tricuspid valve is the matching valve on the right side. It would be the answer if blood leaked from the right ventricle into the right atrium.","Correct. The mitral valve separates the left ventricle from the left atrium.","The pulmonary valve sits between the right ventricle and pulmonary trunk. It would be the answer if blood leaked from the pulmonary trunk back into the right ventricle.","The aortic valve sits between the left ventricle and the aorta. A leaking aortic valve lets blood fall back from the aorta into the left ventricle, not from the ventricle into the atrium."],"lt":"t-pathway-applied-sequencing","lid":"W3-746","comp":58,"competencyId":"cv-valves"},{"id":"L03940","dok":2,"q":"An echocardiogram shows blood falling back from the aorta into the left ventricle each time the ventricle relaxes. Which valve is failing to close?","a":"The aortic valve.","options":["Pulmonary valve","Tricuspid valve","Aortic valve","Mitral valve"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The aortic valve is the semilunar valve between the left ventricle and the aorta. When the ventricle relaxes, its pocket-shaped cusps fill with blood and snap shut so blood cannot return to the ventricle. Blood falling from the aorta back into the left ventricle means the aortic valve is not sealing.","notes":["The pulmonary valve is the semilunar valve on the right side. It would be the answer if blood fell back from the pulmonary trunk into the right ventricle.","The tricuspid valve separates the right atrium and right ventricle. It would be the answer if blood leaked from the right ventricle back into the right atrium.","Correct. The aortic valve separates the aorta from the left ventricle.","The mitral valve separates the left atrium and left ventricle. It would be the answer if blood leaked from the left ventricle back into the left atrium."],"lt":"t-pathway-applied-sequencing","lid":"W3-747","comp":58,"competencyId":"cv-valves"},{"id":"L03941","dok":2,"q":"An echocardiogram shows blood falling back from the pulmonary trunk into the right ventricle each time the ventricle relaxes. Which valve is failing to close?","a":"The pulmonary valve.","options":["Tricuspid valve","Pulmonary valve","Mitral valve","Aortic valve"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The pulmonary valve is the semilunar valve between the right ventricle and the pulmonary trunk. When the right ventricle relaxes, the valve should close so blood in the pulmonary trunk cannot return. Backflow from the pulmonary trunk into the right ventricle points to a pulmonary valve that is not sealing.","notes":["The tricuspid valve is the entrance to the right ventricle from the right atrium. It would be the answer if blood leaked from the right ventricle back into the right atrium.","Correct. The pulmonary valve separates the pulmonary trunk from the right ventricle.","The mitral valve separates the left atrium and left ventricle. It would be the answer if blood leaked from the left ventricle into the left atrium.","The aortic valve is the matching semilunar valve on the left side. It would be the answer if blood fell back from the aorta into the left ventricle."],"lt":"t-pathway-applied-sequencing","lid":"W3-748","comp":58,"competencyId":"cv-valves"},{"id":"L03942","dok":2,"q":"A patient's left ventricle must work much harder than normal to push blood into the aorta because the valve between them is stiff and narrowed (stenotic). Which valve is affected?","a":"The aortic valve.","options":["Tricuspid valve","Pulmonary valve","Aortic valve","Mitral valve"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"A stenotic valve is stiff and narrowed, so it does not open fully and blood has trouble getting through. The valve that blood must pass to go from the left ventricle into the aorta is the aortic valve. When it is narrowed, the left ventricle has to push harder to move the same amount of blood.","notes":["The tricuspid valve is the entrance to the right ventricle from the right atrium. It would be the answer if blood had trouble moving from the right atrium into the right ventricle.","The pulmonary valve is the exit from the right ventricle. It would be the answer if the right ventricle were struggling to push blood into the pulmonary trunk.","Correct. The aortic valve is the passage from the left ventricle into the aorta.","The mitral valve is the entrance to the left ventricle from the left atrium. A stenotic mitral valve would slow blood moving from the left atrium into the left ventricle instead."],"lt":"t-pathway-applied-sequencing","lid":"W3-749","comp":58,"competencyId":"cv-valves"},{"id":"L03943","dok":2,"q":"A patient's right ventricle must work much harder than normal to push blood into the pulmonary trunk because the valve between them is stiff and narrowed (stenotic). Which valve is affected?","a":"The pulmonary valve.","options":["Mitral valve","Pulmonary valve","Tricuspid valve","Aortic valve"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"A stenotic valve does not open fully, which narrows the passage for blood. Blood leaving the right ventricle must pass the pulmonary valve to enter the pulmonary trunk. If that valve is narrowed, the right ventricle has to push harder to send blood to the lungs.","notes":["The mitral valve is the entrance to the left ventricle from the left atrium. It would be the answer if blood had trouble moving from the left atrium into the left ventricle.","Correct. The pulmonary valve is the passage from the right ventricle into the pulmonary trunk.","The tricuspid valve is the entrance to the right ventricle, not its exit. A narrowed tricuspid valve would slow blood moving from the right atrium into the right ventricle.","The aortic valve is the exit from the left ventricle into the aorta. It would be the answer if the left ventricle were struggling to push blood into the aorta."],"lt":"t-pathway-applied-sequencing","lid":"W3-750","comp":58,"competencyId":"cv-valves"},{"id":"L03957","dok":2,"q":"Blood travels from the left atrium out to the systemic arteries. Which two heart valves does it cross, in order?","a":"The mitral valve, then the aortic valve.","options":["Aortic valve, then mitral valve","Tricuspid valve, then pulmonary valve","Mitral valve, then pulmonary valve","Mitral valve, then aortic valve"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"From the left atrium, blood crosses the mitral valve into the left ventricle. The left ventricle then pumps it through the aortic valve into the aorta, which branches into the systemic arteries. On each side of the heart, the atrioventricular valve comes first and the semilunar valve second.","notes":["These are the right two valves in the wrong order. Blood has to enter the left ventricle through the mitral valve before the ventricle can pump it out through the aortic valve.","The tricuspid and pulmonary valves are the right-side valves. They would be the answer for blood traveling from the right atrium to the lungs.","The mitral valve is right, but the pulmonary valve is the exit from the right ventricle, not the left. It would fit only if the question were about blood leaving the right ventricle for the lungs.","Correct. The mitral valve comes first, then the aortic valve."],"lt":"t-pathway-integrated-tracing","lid":"W3-764","comp":61,"competencyId":"cv-blood-pathway"},{"id":"L03958","dok":2,"q":"A student traces blood from the right atrium to the pulmonary arteries and writes: right atrium, tricuspid valve, right ventricle, aortic valve, pulmonary trunk, pulmonary arteries. Which correction fixes the trace?","a":"Replace the aortic valve with the pulmonary valve.","options":["Replace the pulmonary trunk with the aorta","Replace the aortic valve with the pulmonary valve","Replace the tricuspid valve with the mitral valve","Swap the right atrium and the right ventricle"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The right ventricle sends blood to the lungs through the pulmonary valve, which opens into the pulmonary trunk. The aortic valve belongs to the left ventricle and opens into the aorta. Swapping in the pulmonary valve makes every structure in the trace belong to the right side of the heart, which is what the pulmonary circuit needs.","notes":["The pulmonary trunk is correct, since it is the vessel that branches into the pulmonary arteries. The aorta would fit only if the trace were heading to the body instead of the lungs.","Correct. The right ventricle empties through the pulmonary valve, not the aortic valve.","The tricuspid valve is already correct, since it separates the right atrium from the right ventricle. The mitral valve would be correct only in a trace through the left side of the heart.","The order of the chambers is already correct. Blood enters the right atrium first and the right ventricle second, so swapping them would add an error."],"lt":"t-pathway-integrated-tracing","lid":"W3-765","comp":61,"competencyId":"cv-blood-pathway"},{"id":"L03965","dok":3,"q":"A red blood cell leaves a vein in the foot and travels to the left ventricle. In what order does it pass through the other three chambers?","a":"Right atrium, right ventricle, left atrium.","options":["Right ventricle, right atrium, left atrium","Right atrium, left atrium","Left atrium, right atrium, right ventricle","Right atrium, right ventricle, left atrium"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Blood from the foot returns through the leg veins and the inferior vena cava to the right atrium. It crosses the tricuspid valve into the right ventricle, which pumps it through the pulmonary circuit to the lungs. The pulmonary veins return it to the left atrium, and the mitral valve lets it into the left ventricle.","notes":["This reverses the right atrium and right ventricle. The atrium is the receiving chamber for blood from the venae cavae, so it comes before the ventricle.","This route skips the right ventricle and the lungs by going straight from the right atrium to the left atrium. That shortcut exists only in the fetus, through the foramen ovale, so it would be the answer for fetal circulation, not an adult.","Venous blood from the body never enters the left atrium first. The left atrium receives blood only from the lungs, so it must come after the right heart and the pulmonary circuit.","Correct. The cell passes the right atrium, right ventricle, lungs, and left atrium before reaching the left ventricle."],"lt":"t-pathway-integrated-tracing","lid":"W3-772","comp":61,"competencyId":"cv-blood-pathway"},{"id":"L03983","dok":2,"q":"A student traces oxygen-rich blood from the lungs to the body and writes: pulmonary veins, left atrium, tricuspid valve, left ventricle, aortic valve, aorta. Which correction fixes the trace?","a":"Replace the tricuspid valve with the mitral valve.","options":["Replace the aortic valve with the pulmonary valve","Swap the left atrium and the left ventricle","Replace the pulmonary veins with the pulmonary arteries","Replace the tricuspid valve with the mitral valve"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"On the left side of the heart, blood crosses the mitral valve to move from the left atrium into the left ventricle. The tricuspid valve is the right-side atrioventricular valve, between the right atrium and right ventricle. Every other step in the student's trace is already correct.","notes":["The aortic valve is already correct as the exit from the left ventricle into the aorta. The pulmonary valve would fit only in a trace from the right ventricle to the lungs.","The chamber order is already correct. The pulmonary veins empty into the left atrium first, and the left ventricle comes after the atrioventricular valve, so swapping them would add an error.","The pulmonary veins are already correct, since they carry oxygen-rich blood from the lungs to the left atrium. The pulmonary arteries carry blood into the lungs, so they would belong in a trace from the right ventricle.","Correct. The mitral valve, not the tricuspid, separates the left atrium from the left ventricle."],"lt":"t-pathway-integrated-tracing","lid":"W3-791","comp":61,"competencyId":"cv-blood-pathway"},{"id":"L03984","dok":2,"q":"A student traces oxygen-poor blood from the body to the lungs and writes: venae cavae, right atrium, tricuspid valve, right ventricle, pulmonary valve, pulmonary veins. Which correction fixes the trace?","a":"Replace the pulmonary veins with the pulmonary trunk.","options":["Replace the tricuspid valve with the mitral valve","Replace the pulmonary valve with the aortic valve","Replace the right atrium with the left atrium","Replace the pulmonary veins with the pulmonary trunk"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The pulmonary valve opens into the pulmonary trunk, which branches into the pulmonary arteries that carry oxygen-poor blood to the lungs. The pulmonary veins run the other way, carrying oxygen-rich blood out of the lungs to the left atrium. Every other step in the student's trace is already correct.","notes":["The tricuspid valve is already correct, since it separates the right atrium from the right ventricle. The mitral valve belongs in a trace through the left side of the heart.","The pulmonary valve is already correct as the exit from the right ventricle. The aortic valve is the exit from the left ventricle and would belong in a trace that ends at the aorta.","The right atrium is already correct, because the venae cavae empty into it. The left atrium receives blood from the pulmonary veins, so it would belong in a trace from the lungs to the body.","Correct. Blood passing the pulmonary valve enters the pulmonary trunk, not the pulmonary veins."],"lt":"t-pathway-integrated-tracing","lid":"W3-792","comp":61,"competencyId":"cv-blood-pathway"},{"id":"L03993","dok":1,"q":"Which heart chamber receives blood from the superior vena cava, the inferior vena cava, and the coronary sinus?","a":"Right atrium","options":["Left ventricle","Left atrium","Right ventricle","Right atrium"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The right atrium is the receiving chamber for oxygen-poor blood coming back to the heart. The superior vena cava brings blood from the head, neck, and upper limbs, the inferior vena cava brings blood from the trunk and lower limbs, and the coronary sinus brings blood that has already served the heart wall. All three empty into the right atrium, which then passes the blood through the tricuspid valve to the right ventricle.","notes":["The left ventricle receives blood from the left atrium and pumps it into the aorta. Choose it if the question asked which chamber sends blood out to the whole body.","The left atrium receives oxygen-rich blood from the lungs through the four pulmonary veins, not blood returning from the body. Pick it if the question asked which chamber the pulmonary veins drain into.","The right ventricle receives blood from the right atrium through the tricuspid valve and pumps it into the pulmonary trunk. It would be the answer if the question asked which chamber sends blood to the lungs.","Correct. The venae cavae and the coronary sinus all empty into the right atrium."],"lt":"t-imagefree-structure-recognition","lid":"W3-801","comp":57,"competencyId":"cv-chambers"},{"id":"L03994","dok":1,"q":"Which heart chamber receives oxygen-rich blood from the four pulmonary veins?","a":"Left atrium","options":["Right ventricle","Left ventricle","Left atrium","Right atrium"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The pulmonary veins carry blood that has just picked up oxygen in the lungs back to the heart, and all four of them open into the left atrium. From there the blood passes through the mitral valve into the left ventricle, which pumps it out to the body. The left atrium is where the pulmonary circuit ends and the blood begins its trip through the left side of the heart.","notes":["The right ventricle receives oxygen-poor blood from the right atrium and sends it toward the lungs. Choose it if the question asked which chamber pumps into the pulmonary trunk.","The left ventricle is the next stop after the left atrium, receiving blood through the mitral valve. It would be the answer if the question asked which chamber pumps blood into the aorta.","Correct. All four pulmonary veins empty into the left atrium.","The right atrium receives oxygen-poor blood from the body through the superior and inferior venae cavae and from the heart wall through the coronary sinus. Pick it if the question listed those vessels instead of the pulmonary veins."],"lt":"t-imagefree-structure-recognition","lid":"W3-802","comp":57,"competencyId":"cv-chambers"},{"id":"L03995","dok":1,"q":"Which heart chamber pumps blood into the pulmonary trunk?","a":"Right ventricle","options":["Left ventricle","Left atrium","Right atrium","Right ventricle"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The right ventricle receives oxygen-poor blood from the right atrium and, when it contracts, pushes that blood through the pulmonary valve into the pulmonary trunk. The pulmonary trunk then splits into the right and left pulmonary arteries, which carry the blood to the lungs. This makes the right ventricle the pump for the pulmonary circuit, the loop between the heart and the lungs.","notes":["The left ventricle pumps blood through the aortic valve into the aorta, starting the systemic circuit. Pick it if the question asked which chamber pumps into the aorta.","The left atrium receives oxygen-rich blood from the pulmonary veins and passes it to the left ventricle. Choose it if the question asked where blood returning from the lungs first enters the heart.","The right atrium is a receiving chamber that passes blood down through the tricuspid valve into the right ventricle. It would be the answer if the question asked which chamber receives the venae cavae.","Correct. The right ventricle sends blood through the pulmonary valve into the pulmonary trunk."],"lt":"t-imagefree-structure-recognition","lid":"W3-803","comp":57,"competencyId":"cv-chambers"},{"id":"L04011","dok":1,"q":"Which fibrous cords tie the cusps of the atrioventricular valves to the papillary muscles?","a":"Chordae tendineae","options":["Moderator band","Trabeculae carneae","Chordae tendineae","Pectinate muscles"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The chordae tendineae are strong fibrous cords, often called the heart strings, that connect the cusps of the tricuspid and mitral valves to the papillary muscles. When the ventricle contracts, the cords hold the cusps in place so the valve cannot flip back into the atrium. Only the atrioventricular valves have chordae tendineae, because the semilunar valves close on their own as their cusps fill with blood.","notes":["The moderator band is a muscular band in the right ventricle that carries part of the conduction system to the anterior papillary muscle. Choose it if the question asked which band crosses the right ventricle carrying conduction fibers.","Trabeculae carneae are irregular ridges of muscle on the ventricle walls, not cords attached to the valves. Pick them if the question asked about the rough muscular ridges lining the ventricles.","Correct. The chordae tendineae tie the AV valve cusps to the papillary muscles.","Pectinate muscles are parallel muscular ridges on the walls of the atria and auricles. They would be the answer if the question asked about the comb-like ridges inside the atria."],"lt":"t-imagefree-structure-recognition","lid":"W3-819","comp":59,"competencyId":"cv-valve-support"},{"id":"L04012","dok":1,"q":"Which cone-shaped muscular projections of the ventricle wall anchor the chordae tendineae?","a":"Papillary muscles","options":["Pectinate muscles","Papillary muscles","Trabeculae carneae","Crista terminalis"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Papillary muscles are cone-shaped projections of the ventricle wall, and the chordae tendineae attach to their tips. When the ventricle contracts, the papillary muscles contract with it and pull on the chordae, holding the AV valve cusps shut against the pressure inside the ventricle. If a papillary muscle is damaged, for example in a heart attack, the valve can fail and leak.","notes":["Pectinate muscles are parallel ridges on the walls of the atria and auricles, and they have no connection to the valves. Pick them if the question asked about the comb-like ridges in the atria.","Correct. The papillary muscles anchor the chordae tendineae in the ventricles.","Trabeculae carneae are the irregular ridges that roughen the rest of the ventricle wall, but they do not anchor the valve cords. They would be the answer if the question asked about the crisscrossed muscular ridges lining the ventricles.","The crista terminalis is a ridge in the right atrium that separates the smooth posterior wall from the pectinate anterior wall. Choose it if the question asked about the landmark ridge inside the right atrium."],"lt":"t-imagefree-structure-recognition","lid":"W3-820","comp":59,"competencyId":"cv-valve-support"},{"id":"L04085","dok":2,"q":"Which pair of heart valves has no chordae tendineae or papillary muscles, because their pocket-shaped cusps fill with blood and close on their own?","a":"The pulmonary and aortic valves","options":["Tricuspid and mitral valves","Mitral and aortic valves","Pulmonary and aortic valves","Tricuspid and pulmonary valves"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The pulmonary and aortic valves are the semilunar valves, guarding the exits of the ventricles. Their cusps are shaped like small pockets, so when the ventricles relax, blood falling back fills the pockets and presses them together to seal the opening. Because they close themselves this way, they need no chordae tendineae or papillary muscles, which belong only to the atrioventricular valves.","notes":["The tricuspid and mitral valves are the atrioventricular valves, and they are the ones that do have chordae tendineae and papillary muscles. Pick this pair if the question asked which valves are held shut by cords during ventricular contraction.","This pair mixes one valve of each type, since the mitral valve is an AV valve with chordae and the aortic valve is semilunar. Students choose it when they group the two left-sided valves together instead of grouping by valve type.","Correct. The semilunar valves, pulmonary and aortic, have no chordae tendineae.","This pair mixes one valve of each type, since the tricuspid valve is an AV valve with chordae and the pulmonary valve is semilunar. Students choose it when they group the two right-sided valves together instead of grouping by valve type."],"lt":"t-imagefree-mixed-recognition","lid":"W3-896","comp":58,"competencyId":"cv-valves"},{"id":"L04088","dok":3,"q":"After a heart attack, a papillary muscle in the left ventricle is damaged, and its chordae tendineae can no longer hold the valve cusps shut. When the left ventricle contracts, into which chamber will some blood leak backward?","a":"The left atrium","options":["Left atrium","Left ventricle","Right ventricle","Right atrium"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The papillary muscles of the left ventricle anchor the chordae tendineae of the mitral valve, the valve between the left atrium and the left ventricle. When the ventricle contracts, these structures normally hold the mitral cusps closed so blood can only go out into the aorta. If a papillary muscle fails, the cusps can be pushed back and some blood leaks backward into the left atrium.","notes":["Correct. A failing mitral valve lets blood leak back into the left atrium.","The left ventricle is the chamber doing the contracting, so it is where the leaking blood comes from, not where it goes. Students choose it when they lose track of which side of the mitral valve is upstream.","The right ventricle is separated from the left ventricle by the interventricular septum, which a papillary muscle problem does not open. Choose it only if the question described a hole in the interventricular septum.","The right atrium is on the other side of the heart and is separated from the left ventricle by septa, not by the mitral valve. Pick it if the damaged papillary muscle had been in the right ventricle, which would let blood leak back through the tricuspid valve."],"lt":"t-imagefree-mixed-recognition","lid":"W3-899","comp":59,"competencyId":"cv-valve-support"},{"id":"L04092","dok":1,"q":"A heart murmur is caused by blood leaking backward through a valve that does not close cleanly. What kind of valve problem is this?","a":"Valve regurgitation.","options":["Heart block","Valve regurgitation","Cardiac tamponade","Valve stenosis"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Each heart valve should seal completely when it closes so blood moves in only one direction. A valve that fails to seal lets blood slip backward, and this is called regurgitation (the valve is also described as incompetent or insufficient). The backward jet of blood is turbulent, and that turbulence is the swishing sound heard through a stethoscope as a murmur.","notes":["Heart block is a conduction problem in which the signal is delayed or stopped at the AV node, so the atria and ventricles beat out of step. It is an electrical disorder and does not involve a leaking valve.","Correct. Regurgitation means a valve leaks and lets blood flow backward, and the turbulence is heard as a murmur.","Cardiac tamponade is fluid in the pericardial cavity compressing the heart from outside. It would be the answer if the question described the heart being squeezed so that it cannot fill.","Stenosis is a valve whose opening has become narrowed and stiff, so it does not open fully and blood is squeezed forward through a smaller hole. Pick stenosis when a question describes blocked forward flow out of a chamber, not backflow."],"lt":"t-clinical-application-and-misconceptions","lid":"W3-903","comp":58,"competencyId":"cv-valves"},{"id":"L04093","dok":3,"q":"A heart attack damages a papillary muscle in the left ventricle, and the mitral valve begins to fail. What best explains the failure?","a":"The chordae lose their anchor, so the cusps swing into the atrium.","options":["The coronary sinus is blocked, so blood backs up into the valve","The chordae lose their anchor, so the cusps swing into the atrium","The SA node loses its blood supply, so the valve gets no signal to close","The pericardium loses its anchor, so the heart shifts out of position"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The mitral valve is an atrioventricular valve, and its cusps are tied to the ventricle wall by the chordae tendineae, which are anchored on the papillary muscles. When the ventricle contracts, the papillary muscles hold those cords tight so the cusps cannot be forced back into the left atrium. If a papillary muscle dies, the cords lose their anchor, the cusps can prolapse (bulge backward) into the atrium, and blood leaks backward.","notes":["The coronary sinus is the large vein on the posterior heart that drains the cardiac veins into the right atrium. It would be the answer for a question about where blood from the heart wall returns, not about how a valve is held shut.","Correct. Without a working papillary muscle, the chordae cannot hold the mitral cusps shut against a contracting ventricle.","The SA node is the pacemaker in the right atrium, and valves do not close because of electrical signals; they move passively with the pressure of the blood on each side. Pick the SA node for a question about what sets the heart rate.","The pericardium is the sac around the whole heart and anchors it in the mediastinum, but it does not hold the valve cusps. Choose it for a question about what keeps the heart in place or prevents overfilling."],"lt":"t-clinical-application-and-misconceptions","lid":"W3-904","comp":59,"competencyId":"cv-valve-support"},{"id":"L04132","dok":2,"q":"In aortic stenosis, the aortic valve opening is narrowed and stiff. Why does this strain the left ventricle?","a":"It must push blood through a smaller exit.","options":["Its inflow from the left atrium is blocked","Its pacemaker signal is slowed","Blood leaks back into it from the aorta","It must push blood through a smaller exit"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The aortic valve is the semilunar valve between the left ventricle and the aorta, the exit through which the left ventricle sends blood to the body. Stenosis means the valve opening has become narrowed and stiff, so it does not open fully. The left ventricle has to squeeze much harder to push the same amount of blood through the smaller opening, which strains its wall.","notes":["Blood enters the left ventricle from the left atrium through the mitral valve, not the aortic valve. A narrowed mitral valve would be the answer for blocked inflow.","The SA node is in the right atrial wall and sets the heart rate on its own. Valves do not control it, so this choice mixes up the valves with the conduction system.","Backflow from the aorta into the ventricle describes aortic regurgitation, a leaky valve that fails to close. Pick it when the valve does not seal, not when it does not open fully.","Correct. The left ventricle has to push harder to get blood through the narrowed aortic valve."],"lt":"t-clinical-application-and-misconceptions","lid":"W3-946","comp":58,"competencyId":"cv-valves"},{"id":"L04133","dok":2,"q":"In pulmonary stenosis, the pulmonary valve is narrowed. Which chamber is strained, and why?","a":"The right ventricle, because its exit to the lungs is narrowed.","options":["The right ventricle, because its exit to the lungs is narrowed","The left ventricle, because its exit into the aorta is narrowed","The right atrium, because its exit into the right ventricle is narrowed","The left atrium, because its exit into the left ventricle is narrowed"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The pulmonary valve is the semilunar valve between the right ventricle and the pulmonary trunk. When it is narrowed (stenosis), the right ventricle has to squeeze harder to push blood through the smaller opening toward the lungs. The extra workload falls on the chamber just upstream of the narrowed valve, which is the right ventricle.","notes":["Correct. The right ventricle pumps through the pulmonary valve, so it bears the extra workload.","The left ventricle's exit is the aortic valve. This describes aortic stenosis, so pick it if the narrowed valve led into the aorta.","The right atrium empties through the tricuspid valve, not the pulmonary valve. A narrowed tricuspid valve would be the answer for strain on the right atrium.","The left atrium empties through the mitral valve, on the opposite side of the heart from the pulmonary valve. It would be strained by a narrowed mitral valve."],"lt":"t-clinical-application-and-misconceptions","lid":"W3-947","comp":58,"competencyId":"cv-valves"},{"id":"L04165","dok":2,"q":"Why do the AV valves have chordae tendineae and papillary muscles while the semilunar valves do not?","a":"Contracting ventricles could push the AV cusps backward.","options":["The semilunar valves are held shut by the pericardium","Contracting ventricles could push the AV cusps backward","The AV valves open toward the arteries and need strength","The semilunar valves are too small to need any support"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The AV valves (tricuspid and mitral) sit between each atrium and ventricle. When a ventricle contracts, the blood pushes up against their cusps, and without support the cusps could be forced back into the atrium. The chordae tendineae tie the cusps to the papillary muscles, which hold the cords tight so the cusps stay shut. The semilunar valves have pocket-shaped cusps that fill with blood from above and close themselves, so they need no cords.","notes":["The fibrous pericardium is the tough outer sac around the whole heart. It anchors the heart and prevents overfilling, but it does not touch the valves, so pick it for a question about the pericardial sac.","Correct. The cords and papillary muscles keep the AV cusps from being forced back into the atria.","The AV valves open from the atria into the ventricles, not toward the arteries. The valves that open into arteries are the semilunar valves, the pulmonary and aortic valves.","Size is not the reason. The semilunar valves close on their own because their pocket-shaped cusps fill with blood flowing back toward them, a shape that needs no anchors."],"lt":"t-clinical-application-and-misconceptions","lid":"W3-983","comp":59,"competencyId":"cv-valve-support"},{"id":"L04166","dok":2,"q":"Why is it important that each heart valve lets blood flow in only one direction?","a":"Backflow would reduce the blood moving forward.","options":["One-way valves insulate the atria from the ventricles","One-way valves keep the two sides' blood from mixing","One-way valves set the heart rate","Backflow would reduce the blood moving forward"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Each chamber squeezes blood toward the next part of the pathway, and the valves make sure it goes forward. If a valve leaks, part of every squeeze sends blood back where it came from instead of onward. Less blood moves forward to the lungs or body with each beat, and the heart has to work harder to make up the difference. A leaky valve is described as regurgitation.","notes":["Electrical insulation between the atria and ventricles comes from the fibrous skeleton, the dense connective tissue rings around the valves. Pick the fibrous skeleton for a question about why the AV bundle is the only electrical route.","Oxygen-rich and oxygen-poor blood are kept apart by the interatrial and interventricular septa, the walls between the left and right sides. Choose the septa for a question about keeping the two circuits separate.","The heart rate is set by the SA node, the pacemaker in the right atrial wall. Valves open and close passively with blood pressure and play no part in timing.","Correct. One-way flow makes sure each squeeze sends blood onward instead of backward."],"lt":"t-clinical-application-and-misconceptions","lid":"W3-984","comp":58,"competencyId":"cv-valves"},{"id":"L04191","dok":1,"q":"Which pair names the two atrioventricular (AV) valves and where each one sits?","a":"The tricuspid valve (right atrium to right ventricle) and the mitral valve (left atrium to left ventricle).","options":["Tricuspid (right atrium to right ventricle) and mitral (left atrium to left ventricle)","Tricuspid (right atrium to right ventricle) and pulmonary (right ventricle to pulmonary trunk)","Pulmonary (right ventricle to pulmonary trunk) and aortic (left ventricle to aorta)","Mitral (right atrium to right ventricle) and tricuspid (left atrium to left ventricle)"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The atrioventricular valves sit between each atrium and the ventricle below it, which is where their name comes from. The tricuspid valve, with three cusps, guards the right side, and the mitral (bicuspid) valve, with two cusps, guards the left side. Both are held in place by chordae tendineae and papillary muscles, which the semilunar valves lack.","notes":["Correct. The tricuspid is on the right side and the mitral is on the left.","The tricuspid is an AV valve, but the pulmonary valve is a semilunar valve. This pair would fit a question asking for the two valves on the right side of the heart.","These are the two semilunar valves, which guard the exits from the ventricles into the great arteries. Pick this pair if the question asked for the semilunar valves.","The two AV valves are named here but placed on the wrong sides. The mitral valve is on the left, between the left atrium and left ventricle, and the tricuspid is on the right."],"lt":"t-cv-valves","lid":"cv-valves-r1","comp":58,"competencyId":"cv-valves"},{"id":"L04192","dok":1,"q":"Which pair names the two semilunar valves and where each one sits?","a":"The pulmonary valve (right ventricle to pulmonary trunk) and the aortic valve (left ventricle to aorta).","options":["Mitral (left atrium to left ventricle) and aortic (left ventricle to aorta)","Tricuspid (right atrium to right ventricle) and mitral (left atrium to left ventricle)","Aortic (right ventricle to pulmonary trunk) and pulmonary (left ventricle to aorta)","Pulmonary (right ventricle to pulmonary trunk) and aortic (left ventricle to aorta)"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The semilunar valves guard the exits from the two ventricles into the great arteries. The pulmonary valve sits between the right ventricle and the pulmonary trunk, and the aortic valve sits between the left ventricle and the aorta. Each has three pocket-shaped cusps that fill with blood and close on their own, so they need no chordae tendineae.","notes":["The mitral is an AV valve and the aortic is a semilunar valve. This pair would be correct for a question asking which two valves are on the left side of the heart.","These are the two atrioventricular valves, between each atrium and ventricle. Pick this pair if the question asked for the AV valves.","The names are right but the locations are swapped. Each semilunar valve is named for the artery it opens into: the pulmonary valve opens into the pulmonary trunk and the aortic valve opens into the aorta.","Correct. Each semilunar valve sits at the exit of a ventricle and is named for the artery it opens into."],"lt":"t-cv-valves","lid":"cv-valves-r2","comp":58,"competencyId":"cv-valves"},{"id":"L04193","dok":1,"q":"How many cusps do the tricuspid valve and the mitral valve each have?","a":"The tricuspid has three cusps; the mitral has two.","options":["Tricuspid two, mitral three","Both have three cusps","Both have two cusps","Tricuspid three, mitral two"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The tricuspid valve, between the right atrium and right ventricle, has three cusps, as its name says (tri- means three). The mitral valve, between the left atrium and left ventricle, has two cusps, which is why it is also called the bicuspid valve. The two semilunar valves, pulmonary and aortic, each have three cusps.","notes":["This swaps the counts. The tricuspid name gives away its three cusps, and the mitral valve's other name, bicuspid, gives away its two.","Three cusps is right for the tricuspid valve and for both semilunar valves, but the mitral valve has only two. Students choose this when they lump the mitral in with the three-cusped valves.","Two cusps is right only for the mitral (bicuspid) valve. Students pick this when they forget that tri- in tricuspid means three.","Correct. Three cusps on the right AV valve and two on the left."],"lt":"t-cv-valves","lid":"cv-valves-r3","comp":58,"competencyId":"cv-valves"},{"id":"L04194","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly describes the backflow each type of heart valve prevents?","a":"The AV valves prevent backflow into the atria; the semilunar valves prevent backflow into the ventricles.","options":["AV valves prevent backflow into the venae cavae; semilunar valves prevent backflow into the atria","AV valves prevent backflow into the atria; semilunar valves prevent backflow into the ventricles","AV valves prevent backflow into the ventricles; semilunar valves prevent backflow into the atria","Both AV and semilunar valves prevent backflow from the ventricles into the atria"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Each valve stops blood from flowing back into the chamber it just left. The AV valves sit between the atria and ventricles, so when the ventricles contract they close and keep blood from being pushed back up into the atria. The semilunar valves sit at the ventricle exits, so when the ventricles relax they close and keep blood in the pulmonary trunk and aorta from falling back into the ventricles.","notes":["The AV valves sit between the atria and the ventricles, not at the openings of the venae cavae, and the semilunar valves are nowhere near the atria. This option mixes up where each valve sits along the pathway.","Correct. Each valve protects the chamber just upstream of it.","This reverses the two jobs. A valve keeps blood from returning to the chamber upstream of it, and the atria are upstream of the AV valves while the ventricles are upstream of the semilunar valves.","Only the AV valves stand between the atria and the ventricles. The semilunar valves guard the ventricle exits, so they keep blood from falling back out of the great arteries into the ventricles."],"lt":"t-cv-valves","lid":"cv-valves-r4","comp":58,"competencyId":"cv-valves"},{"id":"L04195","dok":2,"q":"When the left ventricle contracts, blood slips backward through the mitral valve into the left atrium, heard as a murmur. Which structural failure could cause this?","a":"A torn chordae tendineae cord (or a damaged papillary muscle) that no longer holds the cusp shut.","options":["Damaged pectinate muscles","A torn chordae tendineae cord","A narrowed coronary sinus","A thinned interatrial septum"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The chordae tendineae are fibrous cords that tie the cusps of an AV valve to the papillary muscles of the ventricle wall. When the ventricle contracts, they hold the cusps shut so the cusps cannot flip back into the atrium. If a cord tears or a papillary muscle is damaged, part of the cusp is no longer held, so the valve leaks and blood slips back into the left atrium.","notes":["Pectinate muscles are parallel muscular ridges on the inner walls of the atria and auricles, and they have no connection to the valve cusps. Pick them if the question asked which ridges line the inside of the atria.","Correct. Without the cord holding it, the cusp is pushed back into the atrium and the valve leaks.","The coronary sinus is the large vein on the back of the heart that drains the cardiac veins into the right atrium. A problem there affects drainage of the heart wall, not the closing of the mitral valve; choose it for a question about where cardiac veins empty.","The interatrial septum is the wall between the two atria. Weakness there would affect blood moving between the atria, not blood leaking back through the mitral valve from the ventricle."],"lt":"t-cv-valves","lid":"cv-valves-r5","comp":59,"competencyId":"cv-valve-support"},{"id":"L04196","dok":1,"q":"From which vessels does the right atrium receive blood?","a":"The superior and inferior venae cavae, and the coronary sinus from the heart wall.","options":["The four pulmonary veins returning from the lungs","The pulmonary trunk and the pulmonary arteries","The aorta and the coronary arteries at its base","The superior and inferior venae cavae and the coronary sinus"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The right atrium is the receiving chamber for oxygen-poor blood. The superior vena cava brings blood from the head, neck, and upper limbs, the inferior vena cava brings blood from the trunk and lower limbs, and the coronary sinus brings blood that has already supplied the heart wall. All of it then passes through the tricuspid valve into the right ventricle.","notes":["The four pulmonary veins return oxygen-rich blood from the lungs to the left atrium. Pick them if the question asked what empties into the left atrium.","The pulmonary trunk and its pulmonary arteries carry blood out of the right ventricle toward the lungs. They would be the answer if the question asked where blood goes after it leaves the right ventricle.","The aorta and coronary arteries carry blood away from the heart. The aorta leaves the left ventricle and the coronary arteries branch from its base; choose them for a question on where the heart wall gets its oxygen-rich supply.","Correct. Blood from the body returns through the two venae cavae, and blood from the heart wall returns through the coronary sinus."],"lt":"t-cv-blood-pathway","lid":"cv-blood-pathway-r1","comp":57,"competencyId":"cv-chambers"},{"id":"L04197","dok":1,"q":"Which circuit does each side of the heart drive?","a":"The right side drives the pulmonary circuit; the left side drives the systemic circuit.","options":["The right side drives the pulmonary circuit; the left side drives the systemic circuit","The right side drives the systemic circuit; the left side drives the pulmonary circuit","The atria drive the pulmonary circuit; the ventricles drive the systemic circuit","The right side drives both circuits; the left side only receives blood"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The heart works as two pumps side by side. The right side receives oxygen-poor blood from the body and pumps it through the pulmonary trunk to the lungs, which is the pulmonary circuit. The left side receives oxygen-rich blood from the lungs and pumps it through the aorta to the rest of the body, which is the systemic circuit.","notes":["Correct. Right side to the lungs, left side to the body.","This swaps the two sides. The right ventricle opens into the pulmonary trunk, which goes to the lungs, and the left ventricle opens into the aorta, which goes to the body.","Both circuits are driven by ventricles, one on each side. The atria are receiving chambers that only pass blood down to the ventricles, so this mixes up the top-and-bottom split of the heart with the right-and-left split.","Each side of the heart both receives and pumps. The left ventricle is the strongest pump in the heart, sending blood out through the aorta, so it does far more than receive."],"lt":"t-cv-blood-pathway","lid":"cv-blood-pathway-r2","comp":69,"competencyId":"bvn-circulatory-routes"},{"id":"L04199","dok":2,"q":"Blood moves from the right atrium to the pulmonary trunk. Which two valves does it cross, in order?","a":"The tricuspid valve, then the pulmonary valve.","options":["Tricuspid valve, then aortic valve","Pulmonary valve, then tricuspid valve","Tricuspid valve, then pulmonary valve","Mitral valve, then aortic valve"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Blood leaving the right atrium first passes through the tricuspid valve, the AV valve on the right side, into the right ventricle. When the right ventricle contracts, the blood passes through the pulmonary valve, a semilunar valve, into the pulmonary trunk. Both valves are on the right side because this blood has not yet been to the lungs.","notes":["The tricuspid valve is right, but the aortic valve leads from the left ventricle into the aorta. Students choose this when they forget that the right ventricle empties into the pulmonary trunk.","These are the right valves but in reverse order. Blood must cross the AV valve into the ventricle before it can leave the ventricle through the semilunar valve.","Correct. The AV valve comes first, then the semilunar valve.","These are the matching valves on the left side of the heart, crossed by blood going from the left atrium to the aorta. Pick this pair if the question started at the left atrium."],"lt":"t-cv-blood-pathway","lid":"cv-blood-pathway-r4","comp":61,"competencyId":"cv-blood-pathway"},{"id":"L04200","dok":2,"q":"Trace a drop of blood from the right atrium until it enters the aorta. Which sequence is correct?","a":"Tricuspid valve, right ventricle, pulmonary valve, lungs, left atrium, mitral valve, left ventricle, aortic valve.","options":["Tricuspid valve, right ventricle, pulmonary valve, lungs, left ventricle, mitral valve, left atrium, aortic valve","Tricuspid valve, right ventricle, aortic valve, lungs, left atrium, mitral valve, left ventricle, pulmonary valve","Tricuspid valve, right ventricle, pulmonary valve, lungs, left atrium, mitral valve, left ventricle, aortic valve","Mitral valve, right ventricle, aortic valve, lungs, left atrium, tricuspid valve, left ventricle, pulmonary valve"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Blood leaving the right atrium crosses the tricuspid valve into the right ventricle, then the pulmonary valve into the pulmonary trunk and on to the lungs. It returns oxygen-rich through the pulmonary veins to the left atrium, crosses the mitral valve into the left ventricle, and leaves through the aortic valve into the aorta. Each side follows the same pattern: atrium, AV valve, ventricle, semilunar valve.","notes":["This sends blood from the lungs into the left ventricle first. Returning blood always enters an atrium first, so the pulmonary veins empty into the left atrium, which then fills the left ventricle through the mitral valve.","This swaps the two semilunar valves. The right ventricle opens through the pulmonary valve into the pulmonary trunk, and the left ventricle opens through the aortic valve into the aorta.","Correct. Each side runs atrium, AV valve, ventricle, semilunar valve, with the lungs in between.","This swaps the valves between the two sides. The tricuspid and pulmonary valves belong to the right side, and the mitral and aortic valves belong to the left, so the right ventricle cannot open through an aortic valve."],"lt":"t-cv-blood-pathway","lid":"cv-blood-pathway-r5","comp":61,"competencyId":"cv-blood-pathway"},{"id":"L04212","dok":1,"q":"Which structure is a cone-shaped muscular projection of the ventricle wall that anchors the chordae tendineae?","a":"A papillary muscle.","options":["Trabeculae carneae","Moderator band","Papillary muscle","Pectinate muscles"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The papillary muscles are cone-shaped projections of the ventricle wall that rise into each ventricle. The chordae tendineae run from their tips to the cusps of the AV valve above, so together they hold the valve shut against the pressure of a contracting ventricle. If a papillary muscle is damaged, the valve it anchors can fail and leak.","notes":["The trabeculae carneae are irregular muscular ridges covering the inner walls of the ventricles, but they do not anchor the chordae. Pick them for a question about the rough, ridged surface inside the ventricles.","The moderator band is a muscular band in the right ventricle that carries part of the conduction system to the anterior papillary muscle. Choose it if the question asked which band crosses the right ventricle carrying conduction fibers.","Correct. The papillary muscles are the anchors at the lower end of the chordae tendineae.","The pectinate muscles are parallel ridges on the walls of the atria and auricles, not the ventricles. They would be the answer for the ridges inside the atria."],"lt":"t-cv-valve-support","lid":"cv-valve-support-r2","comp":59,"competencyId":"cv-valve-support"},{"id":"L04213","dok":2,"q":"How do the chordae tendineae and papillary muscles keep an AV valve from failing?","a":"They hold the valve cusps shut against the pressure of a contracting ventricle.","options":["They carry the electrical signal from the atria to the valve cusps","They pull the semilunar valve cusps closed after each heartbeat","They pull the AV valve cusps open so the ventricles can fill","They hold the cusps shut against the pressure of a contracting ventricle"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"When a ventricle contracts, the rising pressure pushes the AV valve cusps up toward the atrium. The papillary muscles anchor the chordae tendineae, which act like tethers that keep the cusps from being pushed inside out into the atrium. The valve stays sealed, so blood leaves through the semilunar valve instead of flowing back into the atrium.","notes":["The chordae tendineae are fibrous cords, not conducting tissue. The signal reaches the ventricles through the AV bundle, so choose a conduction structure if the question asked how the signal crosses into the ventricles.","Only the AV valves have chordae tendineae and papillary muscles. The semilunar valves have pocket-shaped cusps that fill with blood and close themselves; students pick this when they assume every valve has the same anchoring structures.","AV valves open because blood pushes them open as the ventricles fill, not because cords pull on them. Students choose this when they picture the chordae as ropes that open the valve rather than tethers that keep it from flipping back.","Correct. The cords and their muscle anchors keep the cusps from flipping back into the atrium."],"lt":"t-cv-valve-support","lid":"cv-valve-support-r3","comp":59,"competencyId":"cv-valve-support"}]},{"id":"m3-coronary-great-vessels","title":"Great vessels and coronary circulation","summary":"aorta, pulmonary trunk, venae cavae, pulmonary veins, coronary arteries and branches, cardiac veins, coronary sinus","mw":"2026-10-05","tr":"2026-10-01","cards":[{"id":"L01508","dok":1,"q":"Which great vessel carries blood from the left ventricle out to the body?","a":"The aorta.","options":["The pulmonary trunk","The superior vena cava","The aorta","The coronary sinus"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The aorta is the largest artery in the body. It leaves the left ventricle, passing through the aortic valve, and carries oxygen-rich blood to every part of the body through its branches. This makes it the start of the systemic circuit.","notes":["The pulmonary trunk leaves the right ventricle and carries oxygen-poor blood to the lungs. Pick it if the question asked which vessel leaves the right ventricle.","The superior vena cava returns oxygen-poor blood from the head, neck, and upper limbs to the right atrium. It brings blood into the heart, so pick it for a question about venous return from the upper body.","Correct. The aorta carries blood from the left ventricle to the body.","The coronary sinus is a vein on the back of the heart that drains the heart wall into the right atrium. Pick it for a question about where the cardiac veins empty."],"lt":"t-heart","lid":"c13","comp":62,"competencyId":"bvn-great-vessels"},{"id":"L01509","dok":1,"q":"How many pulmonary veins return blood to the left atrium?","a":"Four","options":["Four","Two","Six","One"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Four pulmonary veins return oxygen-rich blood from the lungs to the left atrium, typically two from each lung. On the posterior surface of a heart model they appear as four openings entering the left atrium. No other chamber receives the pulmonary veins, so counting four openings on the back of the heart is a quick way to spot the left atrium.","notes":["Correct. Four pulmonary veins, two from each lung, enter the left atrium.","Two is the number of pulmonary veins from each lung, not the total. Students who choose two have counted one side only.","Six is an overcount. Each lung typically sends only two pulmonary veins to the left atrium, so students who choose six are guessing rather than counting the openings on the left atrium.","One vessel describes the pulmonary trunk, which leaves the right ventricle as a single vessel before splitting. Pick one for the number of pulmonary trunks."],"lt":"t-heart","lid":"c14","comp":62,"competencyId":"bvn-great-vessels"},{"id":"L01513","dok":1,"q":"After the aorta rises out of the left ventricle, which part of it curves over the top of the heart before turning downward?","a":"The aortic arch.","options":["The descending thoracic aorta","The ascending aorta","The aortic arch","The abdominal aorta"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The aorta has named parts along its path. It begins as the ascending aorta rising from the left ventricle, then curves over the top of the heart as the aortic arch, and then runs down behind the heart as the descending thoracic aorta. After passing through the diaphragm it becomes the abdominal aorta.","notes":["The descending thoracic aorta is the segment after the arch, running down along the vertebral column behind the heart. Pick it for the part of the aorta in the posterior thorax.","The ascending aorta is the short first segment that rises straight up from the left ventricle, before the curve. The coronary arteries branch from its base, so pick it for a question about where the coronary arteries begin.","Correct. The aortic arch is the curved segment over the top of the heart.","The abdominal aorta is the segment below the diaphragm, in the abdomen. Pick it for a question about the aorta's branches to the abdominal organs."],"lt":"t-heart","lid":"c18","comp":62,"competencyId":"bvn-great-vessels"},{"id":"L01524","dok":1,"q":"The coronary sinus empties the blood it collects into which chamber?","a":"The right atrium.","options":["The right ventricle","The left atrium","The left ventricle","The right atrium"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The coronary sinus is a large vein in the groove on the posterior heart that collects blood from the cardiac veins. It empties into the right atrium, the same chamber that receives the venae cavae. That makes sense, because blood that has fed the heart wall is oxygen-poor and needs to go to the lungs like the rest of the body's venous blood.","notes":["The right ventricle receives blood from the right atrium through the tricuspid valve. Pick it if the question asked which chamber pumps blood into the pulmonary trunk.","The left atrium receives oxygen-rich blood from the lungs through the pulmonary veins. Pick it if the question asked where the pulmonary veins empty.","The left ventricle receives blood from the left atrium and pumps it into the aorta. Pick it if the question asked which chamber feeds the coronary arteries through the aorta.","Correct. The coronary sinus empties into the right atrium."],"lt":"t-heart","lid":"c29","comp":63,"competencyId":"cv-coronary"},{"id":"L01525","dok":1,"q":"From where do the right and left coronary arteries branch?","a":"From the base of the aorta.","options":["From the coronary sinus","From the base of the aorta","From the aortic arch","From the pulmonary trunk"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The two coronary arteries are the first branches of the aorta. They leave the base of the ascending aorta, just above the aortic valve, and run in the grooves on the heart's surface to supply the heart wall. Because they branch off right at the start, the heart wall is the first tissue to receive freshly oxygenated blood from the left ventricle.","notes":["The coronary sinus is a vein that collects blood from the heart wall and empties into the right atrium. It drains the heart rather than supplying it, so pick it for venous drainage.","Correct. The coronary arteries branch from the base of the aorta, just above the aortic valve.","The aortic arch is the curved segment above the heart, whose branches supply the head, neck, and upper limbs. Pick it for the brachiocephalic trunk, left common carotid, and left subclavian arteries, not the coronary arteries.","The pulmonary trunk carries oxygen-poor blood from the right ventricle to the lungs. It gives off the pulmonary arteries, so pick it if the question asked where the pulmonary arteries begin."],"lt":"t-heart","lid":"c30","comp":63,"competencyId":"cv-coronary"},{"id":"L01527","dok":2,"q":"A central venous catheter is placed in a vein near the collarbone and threaded toward the heart. Which great vessel does it pass through to enter the right atrium from above?","a":"The superior vena cava.","options":["The pulmonary trunk","The superior vena cava","The aorta","The inferior vena cava"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Veins of the head, neck, and upper limbs all drain into the superior vena cava, which empties into the top of the right atrium. A catheter threaded from a vein near the collarbone follows that same venous path. It travels with the flow of blood toward the heart and enters the right atrium from above.","notes":["The pulmonary trunk carries blood from the right ventricle to the lungs. A catheter would reach it only after passing through the right atrium and right ventricle, not on the way in.","Correct. A line from the upper body follows the superior vena cava into the right atrium.","The aorta is an artery carrying blood away from the left ventricle. A venous catheter travels with venous blood toward the heart, so it would not reach the aorta.","The inferior vena cava returns blood from the trunk and lower limbs, entering the right atrium from below. Pick it for a catheter threaded up from a vein in the groin."],"lt":"t-heart","lid":"c32","comp":62,"competencyId":"bvn-great-vessels"},{"id":"L01528","dok":2,"q":"A blood clot breaks loose from a deep vein in the leg. Which great vessel carries it into the right atrium?","a":"The inferior vena cava.","options":["The pulmonary veins","The superior vena cava","The coronary sinus","The inferior vena cava"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Veins of the lower limbs drain upward into the inferior vena cava, which returns blood from the trunk and legs to the right atrium. A clot that breaks loose in a leg vein rides that venous flow into the right atrium. From there it passes through the right ventricle and can lodge in the lungs, which is why leg clots are dangerous.","notes":["The pulmonary veins return blood from the lungs to the left atrium. A clot from the leg reaches the right side of the heart first, so it would not travel in the pulmonary veins.","The superior vena cava drains the head, neck, and upper limbs. Pick it if the clot had started in a vein of the arm.","The coronary sinus collects blood from the heart wall's own veins and empties into the right atrium. Pick it for venous blood coming from the myocardium, not from the legs.","Correct. The inferior vena cava carries blood from the legs into the right atrium."],"lt":"t-heart","lid":"c33","comp":62,"competencyId":"bvn-great-vessels"},{"id":"L01537","dok":1,"q":"Which cardiac vein runs alongside the anterior interventricular artery, also called the LAD, on the front of the heart?","a":"The great cardiac vein.","options":["The great cardiac vein","The middle cardiac vein","The anterior cardiac veins","The small cardiac vein"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The cardiac veins travel in the same grooves as the coronary arteries. The great cardiac vein runs up the anterior interventricular sulcus beside the anterior interventricular artery, the LAD. It then curves around to the back of the heart and drains into the coronary sinus.","notes":["Correct. The great cardiac vein runs beside the anterior interventricular artery.","The middle cardiac vein runs with the posterior interventricular artery on the back of the heart. Pick it if the question asked which vein travels with the posterior interventricular artery.","The anterior cardiac veins drain the right ventricle directly into the right atrium, skipping the coronary sinus. Pick them if the question asked which veins bypass the coronary sinus.","The small cardiac vein runs with the right marginal artery along the right border of the heart. Pick it if the question asked which vein travels with the right marginal artery."],"lt":"t-heart","lid":"c42","comp":63,"competencyId":"cv-coronary"},{"id":"L01540","dok":1,"q":"The pulmonary trunk divides into which vessels?","a":"The right and left pulmonary arteries.","options":["The right and left pulmonary arteries","The right and left pulmonary veins","The right and left coronary arteries","The right and left brachiocephalic arteries"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The pulmonary trunk leaves the right ventricle as one short, wide vessel and then splits into the right and left pulmonary arteries, one to each lung. They are called arteries because they carry blood away from the heart, even though that blood is oxygen-poor. In the lungs the blood picks up oxygen and returns in the pulmonary veins.","notes":["Correct. The pulmonary trunk divides into the right and left pulmonary arteries.","The pulmonary veins carry blood from the lungs back to the left atrium. They bring blood toward the heart, so pick them for the return trip from the lungs.","The right and left coronary arteries branch from the base of the aorta to supply the heart wall. Pick them for the heart's own blood supply.","There is only one brachiocephalic artery, on the right, and it branches from the aortic arch, not the pulmonary trunk. Students may be thinking of the right and left brachiocephalic veins, which join to form the superior vena cava."],"lt":"t-heart","lid":"c45","comp":62,"competencyId":"bvn-great-vessels"},{"id":"L01554","dok":1,"q":"The aorta, the largest artery in the body, receives blood directly from which chamber?","a":"The left ventricle.","options":["The left atrium","The right atrium","The left ventricle","The right ventricle"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The aorta begins at the aortic valve, at the outflow of the left ventricle. When the left ventricle contracts, it pushes oxygen-rich blood through the aortic valve into the aorta and out to the body. That is why the left ventricle has the thickest wall of the four chambers.","notes":["The left atrium passes blood into the left ventricle through the mitral valve. It is one step before the aorta, so pick it for the chamber that receives the pulmonary veins.","The right atrium receives blood from the body through the venae cavae. Pick it if the question asked where oxygen-poor blood first enters the heart.","Correct. The aorta receives blood from the left ventricle.","The right ventricle pumps blood into the pulmonary trunk, not the aorta. Pick it if the question asked which chamber sends blood to the lungs."],"lt":"t-heart","lid":"c13-x2","comp":62,"competencyId":"bvn-great-vessels"},{"id":"L01555","dok":2,"q":"The pulmonary veins carry oxygen-rich blood, yet they are still classified as veins. Why?","a":"They carry blood toward the heart.","options":["They have thicker walls than arteries","They carry blood toward the heart","They carry oxygen-poor blood","They carry blood away from the heart"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Arteries and veins are named for the direction of flow, not for how much oxygen the blood carries. Any vessel that carries blood away from the heart is an artery, and any vessel that carries blood toward the heart is a vein. The pulmonary veins bring blood from the lungs back to the left atrium, so they are veins even though that blood is rich in oxygen.","notes":["Veins generally have thinner walls than arteries of similar size, because venous blood is under lower pressure. Pick thicker walls for a question about what distinguishes arteries.","Correct. Veins are defined by carrying blood toward the heart.","The pulmonary veins carry oxygen-rich blood, which is the point of the question. Students who pick this are using the rule of thumb that veins carry oxygen-poor blood, which fails in the pulmonary circuit.","Carrying blood away from the heart is what defines an artery. Pick this for the pulmonary arteries, which carry oxygen-poor blood away from the heart to the lungs."],"lt":"t-heart","lid":"c14-x2","comp":67,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-types"},{"id":"L01562","dok":2,"q":"Blood supplying the heart wall flows from the aorta to the coronary arteries, myocardial capillaries, and cardiac veins. Which vessel does it enter next, before reaching the right atrium?","a":"The coronary sinus.","options":["The superior vena cava","The coronary sinus","The inferior vena cava","The pulmonary veins"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The coronary circulation runs from the aorta to the coronary arteries, through the capillaries of the myocardium, into the cardiac veins, and then into the coronary sinus. The coronary sinus is a large vein in the groove on the back of the heart that empties into the right atrium. A few small anterior cardiac veins skip the sinus and drain straight into the right atrium.","notes":["The superior vena cava returns blood from the head, neck, and upper limbs to the right atrium. It does not collect blood from the heart wall, so pick it for upper body drainage.","Correct. The cardiac veins drain into the coronary sinus, which empties into the right atrium.","The inferior vena cava returns blood from the trunk and lower limbs to the right atrium. Pick it for drainage from below the diaphragm, not from the heart wall.","The pulmonary veins return oxygen-rich blood from the lungs to the left atrium. Pick them for the end of the pulmonary circuit."],"lt":"t-heart","lid":"c21-x2","comp":63,"competencyId":"cv-coronary"},{"id":"L01564","dok":1,"q":"Which coronary artery gives rise to the anterior interventricular artery and the circumflex artery?","a":"The left coronary artery.","options":["The posterior interventricular artery","The right marginal artery","The right coronary artery","The left coronary artery"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The left coronary artery leaves the base of the aorta and quickly splits into two main branches. The anterior interventricular artery, often called the LAD, runs down the front of the heart between the ventricles, and the circumflex artery curves around the left side in the coronary sulcus. Together they supply the left atrium, most of the left ventricle, the anterior septum, and the apex.","notes":["The posterior interventricular artery runs down the back of the heart between the ventricles and usually comes from the right coronary artery. It is a branch itself, not the source of the circumflex.","The right marginal artery is a branch of the right coronary artery running along the right border of the heart. It does not give rise to other main coronary branches.","The right coronary artery gives off the right marginal artery and, in most people, the posterior interventricular artery. Pick it if the question asked which artery gives rise to those branches.","Correct. The left coronary artery branches into the anterior interventricular and circumflex arteries."],"lt":"t-heart","lid":"c23-x2","comp":63,"competencyId":"cv-coronary"},{"id":"L01569","dok":1,"q":"A clot blocks a coronary artery, and the patch of heart muscle beyond the blockage begins to die. What is this event called?","a":"A myocardial infarction.","options":["A myocardial infarction","Cardiac tamponade","A heart murmur","An aneurysm"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The coronary arteries are the heart's own supply line, and they are fairly narrow. When one is blocked, the myocardium it feeds is starved of oxygen and its muscle begins to die. Death of heart muscle from a blocked coronary artery is a myocardial infarction, commonly called a heart attack.","notes":["Correct. Death of heart muscle from a blocked coronary artery is a myocardial infarction.","Cardiac tamponade is fluid building up in the pericardial cavity and squeezing the heart from outside. Pick it for a heart compressed by fluid, not one starved by a blocked artery.","A heart murmur is the sound of turbulent blood flow, often from a valve that does not close cleanly. Pick it for a question about a leaky or narrowed valve.","An aneurysm is a weakened, bulging section of an artery wall. Pick it for a question about a ballooning vessel, not a blocked one."],"lt":"t-heart","lid":"c28-x2","comp":63,"competencyId":"cv-coronary"},{"id":"L01576","dok":3,"q":"The heart's chambers are always full of blood, yet the heart needs its own coronary arteries. Why can't the heart wall simply take what it needs from the blood in its chambers?","a":"The wall is too thick for oxygen to diffuse in from the chambers, so vessels must carry blood into the muscle.","options":["The wall is too thick for oxygen to diffuse in from the chambers","Heart muscle uses so little oxygen that a small separate supply is enough","The endocardium is a solid barrier that blocks all contact with the blood","The blood in the chambers has already lost all of its oxygen"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Oxygen can diffuse only a very short distance through tissue. The myocardium is far too thick for oxygen in the chamber blood to reach its deeper layers, so only the innermost lining can be fed that way. The coronary arteries solve this by branching into capillaries throughout the muscle, which is why a blocked coronary artery starves heart tissue even when the chambers are full.","notes":["Correct. The thick myocardium needs vessels that bring blood into its depth.","This is backward. Heart muscle has a very high oxygen demand, which is why its cells are packed with mitochondria and why it is damaged so quickly when its supply is blocked.","The endocardium is a thin, smooth endothelium, and the innermost layer of the wall can in fact receive some oxygen from chamber blood. The barrier is the thickness of the myocardium, not the lining.","Blood in the left atrium and left ventricle is freshly oxygenated from the lungs. The problem is reaching the deep muscle, not a lack of oxygen in the chamber blood."],"lt":"t-heart","lid":"d3-t-heart-5","comp":63,"competencyId":"cv-coronary"},{"id":"L03359","dok":1,"q":"Which vessels return blood from the body to the right atrium?","a":"The superior and inferior venae cavae.","options":["The right and left pulmonary arteries","The superior and inferior venae cavae","The right and left brachiocephalic veins","The four pulmonary veins"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The superior vena cava returns oxygen-poor blood from the head, neck, and upper limbs, and the inferior vena cava returns it from the trunk and lower limbs. Both empty directly into the right atrium. Together with the coronary sinus, they bring all of the systemic venous return back to the heart.","notes":["The pulmonary arteries carry blood away from the heart to the lungs. Pick them for the branches of the pulmonary trunk.","Correct. The superior and inferior venae cavae empty into the right atrium.","The brachiocephalic veins join to form the superior vena cava, so they drain into it rather than into the heart. Pick them for the veins that merge to make the SVC.","The pulmonary veins return oxygen-rich blood from the lungs to the left atrium. Pick them for blood coming back from the pulmonary circuit."],"lt":"t-heart-gross-anatomy","lid":"W3-033","comp":62,"competencyId":"bvn-great-vessels"},{"id":"L03360","dok":1,"q":"Which vessel collects blood from the cardiac veins and empties it into the right atrium?","a":"The coronary sinus.","options":["The inferior vena cava","The middle cardiac vein","The coronary sinus","The great cardiac vein"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The coronary sinus is a large vein on the posterior surface of the heart, lying in the groove between the atria and ventricles. It collects blood from the cardiac veins that drain the heart wall and empties into the right atrium. The flow of the heart's own supply is aorta, coronary arteries, myocardial capillaries, cardiac veins, coronary sinus, right atrium.","notes":["The inferior vena cava returns blood from the trunk and lower limbs to the right atrium. Pick it for systemic venous return from below the diaphragm.","The middle cardiac vein runs with the posterior interventricular artery and empties into the coronary sinus. Pick it for the vein in the posterior interventricular groove.","Correct. The coronary sinus collects the cardiac veins and drains into the right atrium.","The great cardiac vein runs with the anterior interventricular artery and drains into the coronary sinus, not straight into the atrium. Pick it for the vein alongside the LAD."],"lt":"t-heart-gross-anatomy","lid":"W3-034","comp":63,"competencyId":"cv-coronary"},{"id":"L03361","dok":1,"q":"Which vessels return blood from the lungs to the left atrium?","a":"The four pulmonary veins.","options":["The four pulmonary veins","The pulmonary trunk","The two pulmonary arteries","The superior and inferior venae cavae"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Four pulmonary veins, two from each lung, carry oxygen-rich blood back to the left atrium. They are one of the few veins in the body that carry oxygen-rich blood. Remember that veins are defined by carrying blood toward the heart, not by how much oxygen the blood holds.","notes":["Correct. Four pulmonary veins return blood from the lungs to the left atrium.","The pulmonary trunk leaves the right ventricle and carries blood toward the lungs. Pick it for the large vessel at the start of the pulmonary circuit.","The pulmonary arteries carry oxygen-poor blood away from the heart to the lungs. Pick them for the vessels leaving the pulmonary trunk.","The venae cavae return oxygen-poor blood from the body to the right atrium. Pick them for systemic venous return."],"lt":"t-heart-gross-anatomy","lid":"W3-035","comp":62,"competencyId":"bvn-great-vessels"},{"id":"L03362","dok":1,"q":"Which vessel carries blood from the right ventricle toward the lungs?","a":"The pulmonary trunk.","options":["The superior vena cava","The pulmonary veins","The aorta","The pulmonary trunk"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The pulmonary trunk leaves the right ventricle, passing through the pulmonary valve, and soon divides into the right and left pulmonary arteries that enter the lungs. It carries oxygen-poor blood, even though it is an artery. Arteries are defined by carrying blood away from the heart.","notes":["The superior vena cava returns blood from the head, neck, and arms to the right atrium. Pick it for venous return from the upper body.","The pulmonary veins carry blood back from the lungs into the left atrium. Pick them for blood returning to the heart from the lungs.","The aorta leaves the left ventricle and carries oxygen-rich blood to the body. Pick it for the start of the systemic circuit.","Correct. The pulmonary trunk carries blood from the right ventricle to the lungs."],"lt":"t-heart-gross-anatomy","lid":"W3-036","comp":62,"competencyId":"bvn-great-vessels"},{"id":"L03363","dok":1,"q":"Which vessel carries blood from the left ventricle to the body?","a":"The aorta.","options":["The inferior vena cava","The pulmonary veins","The pulmonary trunk","The aorta"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The aorta, the largest artery in the body, leaves the left ventricle through the aortic valve. It arches over the heart and then descends through the thorax and abdomen, giving off branches that carry oxygen-rich blood to every part of the body. Its first branches, the coronary arteries, supply the heart wall itself.","notes":["The inferior vena cava carries blood toward the heart from the trunk and lower limbs. Pick it for venous return entering the right atrium.","The pulmonary veins bring blood from the lungs to the left atrium. Pick them for the vessels that fill the left side of the heart.","The pulmonary trunk leaves the right ventricle and carries blood to the lungs. Pick it for the start of the pulmonary circuit.","Correct. The aorta carries blood from the left ventricle to the body."],"lt":"t-heart-gross-anatomy","lid":"W3-037","comp":62,"competencyId":"bvn-great-vessels"},{"id":"L03394","dok":1,"q":"The superior vena cava, inferior vena cava, and coronary sinus all empty into which chamber?","a":"The right atrium.","options":["The right ventricle","The left ventricle","The left atrium","The right atrium"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"All three vessels carry oxygen-poor blood back to the heart. The venae cavae drain the body, and the coronary sinus drains the heart wall itself. They all empty into the right atrium, the receiving chamber for systemic venous return.","notes":["The right ventricle receives blood from the right atrium, not directly from any vein. Pick it for the chamber feeding the pulmonary trunk.","The left ventricle receives blood from the left atrium and pumps into the aorta. Pick it for the systemic pump.","The left atrium receives oxygen-rich blood from the four pulmonary veins. Pick it for blood returning from the lungs.","Correct. All three empty into the right atrium."],"lt":"t-heart-gross-anatomy","lid":"W3-068","comp":57,"competencyId":"cv-chambers"},{"id":"L03395","dok":1,"q":"Which chamber receives oxygen-rich blood through four vessels?","a":"The left atrium","options":["The left ventricle","The right ventricle","The left atrium","The right atrium"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Four pulmonary veins, two from each lung, deliver oxygen-rich blood to the left atrium. The left atrium then passes this blood through the mitral valve into the left ventricle. The number four is a good clue on a lab practical when you are identifying the left atrium from behind.","notes":["The left ventricle receives blood from the left atrium through the mitral valve, not directly from veins. Pick it for the chamber feeding the aorta.","The right ventricle receives oxygen-poor blood from the right atrium. Pick it for the chamber feeding the pulmonary trunk.","Correct. The four pulmonary veins drain into the left atrium.","The right atrium receives oxygen-poor blood from three large veins, the two venae cavae and the coronary sinus, plus a few small anterior cardiac veins. Pick it for systemic venous return."],"lt":"t-heart-gross-anatomy","lid":"W3-069","comp":57,"competencyId":"cv-chambers"},{"id":"L03396","dok":1,"q":"Which chamber sends blood into the pulmonary trunk?","a":"The right ventricle.","options":["The left ventricle","The right ventricle","The right atrium","The left atrium"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The right ventricle contracts and pushes oxygen-poor blood through the pulmonary valve into the pulmonary trunk. The trunk then splits into the right and left pulmonary arteries headed for the lungs. The smooth outflow path leading up to the pulmonary valve is the conus arteriosus.","notes":["The left ventricle pumps into the aorta. Pick it for the start of the systemic circuit.","Correct. The right ventricle pumps into the pulmonary trunk.","The right atrium sends blood down into the right ventricle, not into an artery. Pick it for the chamber receiving the venae cavae.","The left atrium receives blood from the pulmonary veins. Pick it for the end of the pulmonary circuit."],"lt":"t-heart-gross-anatomy","lid":"W3-070","comp":57,"competencyId":"cv-chambers"},{"id":"L03397","dok":1,"q":"Which chamber sends blood into the aorta?","a":"The left ventricle.","options":["The left atrium","The left ventricle","The right atrium","The right ventricle"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The left ventricle contracts with force and pushes oxygen-rich blood through the aortic valve into the aorta. The smooth outflow path leading to the aortic valve is the aortic vestibule. From the aorta, blood reaches every tissue of the body.","notes":["The left atrium sends blood down through the mitral valve into the left ventricle. Pick it for the chamber receiving the pulmonary veins.","Correct. The left ventricle pumps into the aorta.","The right atrium receives oxygen-poor blood from the body. Pick it for the chamber where the venae cavae empty.","The right ventricle pumps into the pulmonary trunk. Pick it for the start of the pulmonary circuit."],"lt":"t-heart-gross-anatomy","lid":"W3-071","comp":57,"competencyId":"cv-chambers"},{"id":"L03403","dok":2,"q":"A student writes: pulmonary veins carry blood away from the heart. Which statement corrects this?","a":"Pulmonary veins carry blood toward the heart, into the left atrium.","options":["Pulmonary veins carry blood toward the heart, into the right atrium","Pulmonary veins carry blood toward the heart, into the left atrium","Pulmonary veins carry blood away from the heart, out of the left ventricle","Pulmonary veins carry blood away from the heart, out of the right ventricle"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"By definition, veins carry blood toward the heart and arteries carry it away. The pulmonary veins return oxygen-rich blood from the lungs into the left atrium. The vessel carrying blood away from the heart to the lungs is the pulmonary trunk and its pulmonary arteries.","notes":["The direction is right, but the right atrium receives the venae cavae and coronary sinus, not the pulmonary veins. Pick the right atrium for systemic venous return.","Correct. Pulmonary veins carry blood from the lungs into the left atrium.","Blood leaving the left ventricle travels in the aorta. Pick the aorta for the vessel carrying blood away to the body.","Blood leaving the right ventricle travels in the pulmonary trunk, an artery. This choice repeats the student's error of calling an outgoing vessel a vein."],"lt":"t-heart-gross-anatomy","lid":"W3-077","comp":62,"competencyId":"bvn-great-vessels"},{"id":"L03404","dok":2,"q":"A student writes: the pulmonary trunk leaves the left ventricle. Which statement corrects this?","a":"The pulmonary trunk leaves the right ventricle.","options":["The pulmonary trunk leaves the right ventricle","The pulmonary trunk enters the left atrium","The pulmonary trunk leaves the right atrium","The pulmonary trunk leaves the left atrium"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The pulmonary trunk carries oxygen-poor blood to the lungs, so it must leave the chamber that holds oxygen-poor blood ready to be pumped. That chamber is the right ventricle. The left ventricle is connected to the aorta instead.","notes":["Correct. The pulmonary trunk leaves the right ventricle.","The vessels entering the left atrium are the pulmonary veins. The pulmonary trunk carries blood away from the heart, not into it.","The right atrium is a receiving chamber that sends blood to the right ventricle. No artery leaves an atrium.","The left atrium receives the pulmonary veins and has no outgoing artery. Pick it for where blood from the lungs arrives."],"lt":"t-heart-gross-anatomy","lid":"W3-078","comp":62,"competencyId":"bvn-great-vessels"},{"id":"L03405","dok":2,"q":"A student writes: the aorta leaves the right ventricle. Which statement corrects this?","a":"The aorta leaves the left ventricle.","options":["The aorta leaves the right atrium","The aorta enters the right atrium","The aorta leaves the left atrium","The aorta leaves the left ventricle"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The aorta carries oxygen-rich blood to the body, so it leaves the chamber that holds oxygen-rich blood ready to be pumped, the left ventricle. Blood passes through the aortic valve to enter it. The right ventricle is connected to the pulmonary trunk instead.","notes":["The right atrium receives oxygen-poor blood from the body and sends it to the right ventricle. No artery leaves it.","The vessels entering the right atrium are the venae cavae and the coronary sinus. The aorta carries blood away from the heart, not into it.","The left atrium sends blood down into the left ventricle and has no outgoing artery. Pick it for the chamber receiving the pulmonary veins.","Correct. The aorta leaves the left ventricle."],"lt":"t-heart-gross-anatomy","lid":"W3-079","comp":62,"competencyId":"bvn-great-vessels"},{"id":"L03412","dok":2,"q":"A clot blocks blood returning from the lower body to the heart. Which vessel is blocked, and which chamber receives less blood?","a":"The inferior vena cava and the right atrium.","options":["The pulmonary veins and the left atrium","The superior vena cava and the right atrium","The inferior vena cava and the right atrium","The inferior vena cava and the left atrium"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The inferior vena cava carries oxygen-poor blood from the trunk and lower limbs up to the heart. It empties into the right atrium, so a clot in it reduces the blood reaching that chamber. Both venae cavae drain into the right atrium; only the location of the clot tells you which one.","notes":["The pulmonary veins carry blood from the lungs to the left atrium. Pick them for a blockage in the return from the lungs.","The superior vena cava drains the head, neck, and upper limbs. Pick it for a clot blocking return from the upper body.","Correct. Blood from the lower body returns through the inferior vena cava to the right atrium.","The vessel is right, but the inferior vena cava empties into the right atrium, not the left. Pick the left atrium only for vessels coming from the lungs."],"lt":"t-heart-gross-anatomy","lid":"W3-086","comp":62,"competencyId":"bvn-great-vessels"},{"id":"L03413","dok":2,"q":"A clot blocks blood returning from the head, neck, and arms. Which vessel is most likely involved?","a":"The superior vena cava.","options":["The superior vena cava","The inferior vena cava","The pulmonary veins","The coronary sinus"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The superior vena cava collects oxygen-poor blood from the head, neck, and upper limbs and empties it into the top of the right atrium. It forms where the right and left brachiocephalic veins join. A clot there backs up blood in the upper body.","notes":["Correct. The superior vena cava drains the head, neck, and arms.","The inferior vena cava drains the trunk and lower limbs. Pick it for blocked return from below the diaphragm.","The pulmonary veins return blood from the lungs to the left atrium. Pick them for blocked return from the lungs.","The coronary sinus drains the heart wall into the right atrium. Pick it for venous return from the myocardium."],"lt":"t-heart-gross-anatomy","lid":"W3-087","comp":62,"competencyId":"bvn-great-vessels"},{"id":"L03506","dok":2,"q":"A student says the pulmonary veins carry oxygen-poor blood to the heart. Which correction is accurate?","a":"The pulmonary veins carry oxygen-rich blood from the lungs to the left atrium.","options":["They carry oxygen-rich blood from the lungs to the left atrium","They carry oxygen-poor blood from the lungs to the right atrium","They carry oxygen-rich blood from the left ventricle to the lungs","They carry oxygen-poor blood from the right ventricle to the lungs"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The pulmonary veins drain the lungs, where blood has just picked up oxygen, so they carry oxygen-rich blood. They empty into the left atrium. They are still veins because a vein is defined by direction, carrying blood toward the heart, not by how much oxygen the blood holds.","notes":["Correct. Pulmonary veins bring freshly oxygenated blood from the lungs to the left atrium.","This keeps the student's error and adds another one. Blood leaving the lungs has just been oxygenated, and the right atrium receives the venae cavae and coronary sinus, not the pulmonary veins.","Blood moving from the heart to the lungs travels in arteries, not veins. This describes no real vessel; the left ventricle actually sends its blood to the body through the aorta.","This describes the pulmonary trunk and pulmonary arteries, which carry oxygen-poor blood from the right ventricle to the lungs. It is the right statement for those vessels, which count as arteries because they carry blood away from the heart."],"lt":"t-heart-blood-flow","lid":"W3-185","comp":62,"competencyId":"bvn-great-vessels"},{"id":"L03507","dok":2,"q":"A student says arteries always carry oxygen-rich blood. Which vessels prove this statement wrong?","a":"The pulmonary arteries.","options":["Coronary arteries","Pulmonary arteries","Aorta","Pulmonary veins"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"An artery is any vessel that carries blood away from the heart, whatever its oxygen content. The pulmonary arteries, branches of the pulmonary trunk, carry oxygen-poor blood away from the right ventricle to the lungs. They are true arteries carrying oxygen-poor blood, so the student's rule does not hold.","notes":["The coronary arteries branch from the base of the aorta and carry oxygen-rich blood to the myocardium. They follow the usual pattern and would be the answer for the blood supply of the heart wall.","Correct. The pulmonary arteries are arteries that carry oxygen-poor blood.","The aorta carries oxygen-rich blood away from the left ventricle, so it fits the student's rule rather than breaking it. It would be the answer for the largest artery or the start of the systemic circuit.","The pulmonary veins are the exception to a different rule. They carry oxygen-rich blood toward the heart, so they disprove the claim that veins always carry oxygen-poor blood."],"lt":"t-heart-blood-flow","lid":"W3-186","comp":67,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-types"},{"id":"L03508","dok":2,"q":"A student says veins always carry oxygen-poor blood. Which vessels prove this statement wrong?","a":"The pulmonary veins.","options":["Venae cavae","Pulmonary arteries","Pulmonary veins","Cardiac veins"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"A vein is any vessel that carries blood toward the heart. The pulmonary veins carry blood from the lungs, where it has just picked up oxygen, back to the left atrium, so they are veins that carry oxygen-rich blood. Direction, not oxygen content, decides whether a vessel is an artery or a vein.","notes":["The superior and inferior venae cavae carry oxygen-poor blood from the body to the right atrium. They follow the student's rule and would be the answer for where systemic blood returns to the heart.","The pulmonary arteries carry oxygen-poor blood away from the heart. They break the opposite rule, that arteries always carry oxygen-rich blood, but they are not veins.","Correct. The pulmonary veins are veins that carry oxygen-rich blood.","The cardiac veins drain oxygen-poor blood from the myocardium into the coronary sinus. They fit the usual pattern and would be the answer for the venous drainage of the heart wall."],"lt":"t-heart-blood-flow","lid":"W3-187","comp":67,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-types"},{"id":"L03513","dok":1,"q":"What are the great vessels of the heart?","a":"The large arteries and veins attached to the base of the heart.","options":["The coronary arteries running in the grooves on the heart surface","The small vessels branching through the lung tissue","The largest arteries and veins of the lower limbs","The large arteries and veins attached to the base of the heart"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The great vessels are the large arteries and veins that enter and leave the heart at its base, the broad superior surface. They are the superior and inferior venae cavae, the pulmonary trunk, the pulmonary veins, and the aorta. Together they carry all the blood moving into and out of the heart's chambers.","notes":["The coronary arteries run in the grooves (sulci) on the heart's surface and supply the heart wall itself. They are small and would be the answer for coronary circulation, not the great vessels.","Vessels branching through the lung tissue are branches of the pulmonary arteries and veins plus the small bronchial vessels. They would be the answer for the blood supply within the lungs, not the vessels attached to the heart.","The large vessels of the lower limbs, such as the femoral artery and great saphenous vein, are far from the heart. They are part of the systemic circuit but are not attached to the heart.","Correct. The great vessels are the large vessels attached at the base of the heart."],"lt":"t-heart-vessels-coronary","lid":"W3-201","comp":62,"competencyId":"bvn-great-vessels"},{"id":"L03514","dok":1,"q":"The superior vena cava returns blood from which regions?","a":"Head, neck, and upper limbs.","options":["The lungs","Head, neck, and upper limbs","Lower limbs, abdomen, and pelvis","The heart wall"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The superior vena cava is the large vein that returns oxygen-poor blood from the regions above the heart, the head, neck, and upper limbs, into the right atrium. Its partner, the inferior vena cava, returns blood from the regions below. Both empty into the right atrium.","notes":["Blood from the lungs returns through the four pulmonary veins to the left atrium. This would be the answer for the pulmonary veins.","Correct. The superior vena cava drains the head, neck, and upper limbs.","The lower limbs, abdomen, and pelvis drain into the inferior vena cava. Choose this for the other vena cava, which enters the right atrium from below.","Blood from the heart wall drains through the cardiac veins into the coronary sinus, which empties into the right atrium. Choose the heart wall for the coronary sinus."],"lt":"t-heart-vessels-coronary","lid":"W3-202","comp":62,"competencyId":"bvn-great-vessels"},{"id":"L03515","dok":1,"q":"The inferior vena cava returns blood from which regions?","a":"Lower limbs, abdomen, and pelvis.","options":["Head, neck, and upper limbs","The lungs","The heart wall","Lower limbs, abdomen, and pelvis"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The inferior vena cava is the large vein that returns oxygen-poor blood from the regions below the diaphragm, including the lower limbs, abdomen, and pelvis. It runs up along the posterior abdominal wall, passes through the diaphragm, and empties into the right atrium.","notes":["The head, neck, and upper limbs drain into the superior vena cava. Choose this for the vena cava that enters the right atrium from above.","Blood from the lungs returns through the pulmonary veins to the left atrium. It never enters a vena cava.","The heart wall drains through the cardiac veins into the coronary sinus. Pick the heart wall for a question about the coronary sinus.","Correct. The inferior vena cava drains the regions below the diaphragm."],"lt":"t-heart-vessels-coronary","lid":"W3-203","comp":62,"competencyId":"bvn-great-vessels"},{"id":"L03516","dok":1,"q":"The superior vena cava empties into which chamber?","a":"Right atrium.","options":["Right ventricle","Left ventricle","Left atrium","Right atrium"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The superior vena cava carries oxygen-poor blood from the head, neck, and upper limbs and opens into the upper part of the right atrium. The right atrium is the receiving chamber for all blood returning from the systemic circuit, so both venae cavae end there.","notes":["The right ventricle receives blood from the right atrium through the tricuspid valve. No vein empties directly into a ventricle; ventricles pump blood out into arteries.","The left ventricle receives blood from the left atrium and pumps it into the aorta. It would be the answer for the chamber that starts the systemic circuit.","The left atrium receives oxygen-rich blood from the pulmonary veins, not the venae cavae. Choose it for where blood returning from the lungs enters.","Correct. The superior vena cava opens into the right atrium."],"lt":"t-heart-vessels-coronary","lid":"W3-204","comp":62,"competencyId":"bvn-great-vessels"},{"id":"L03517","dok":1,"q":"The superior vena cava, the inferior vena cava, and the coronary sinus all empty into which chamber?","a":"Right atrium.","options":["Right ventricle","Right atrium","Left atrium","Left ventricle"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"All three of these veins carry oxygen-poor blood back to the heart, and all three empty into the right atrium. The superior vena cava drains the upper body, the inferior vena cava drains the lower body, and the coronary sinus drains the heart wall. The right atrium is the collecting chamber for the systemic circuit.","notes":["The right ventricle receives blood from the right atrium, not directly from any vein. It is the answer for the chamber that pumps blood into the pulmonary trunk.","Correct. All three return oxygen-poor blood to the right atrium.","The left atrium receives only the four pulmonary veins, which bring oxygen-rich blood from the lungs. Choose it for where pulmonary blood returns.","The left ventricle receives blood from the left atrium and sends it into the aorta. No vein drains directly into it."],"lt":"t-heart-vessels-coronary","lid":"W3-205","comp":57,"competencyId":"cv-chambers"},{"id":"L03518","dok":1,"q":"Which great vessel carries blood away from the right ventricle?","a":"Pulmonary trunk.","options":["Superior vena cava","Pulmonary trunk","Aorta","Pulmonary veins"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The pulmonary trunk is the large artery that leaves the right ventricle, beyond the pulmonary valve. It carries oxygen-poor blood upward and then splits into the right and left pulmonary arteries, which lead to the lungs. It is the first vessel of the pulmonary circuit.","notes":["The superior vena cava brings blood into the right atrium from the upper body. It carries blood toward the heart, so it cannot carry blood away from a ventricle.","Correct. The pulmonary trunk leaves the right ventricle.","The aorta carries blood away from the left ventricle, not the right. It is the left-side counterpart and would be the answer for the vessel leaving the left ventricle.","The pulmonary veins bring oxygen-rich blood from the lungs into the left atrium. They are the answer for the vessels returning blood at the end of the pulmonary circuit."],"lt":"t-heart-vessels-coronary","lid":"W3-206","comp":62,"competencyId":"bvn-great-vessels"},{"id":"L03520","dok":1,"q":"How many pulmonary veins usually empty into the left atrium, and what kind of blood do they carry?","a":"Four, carrying oxygen-rich blood.","options":["Four, carrying oxygen-poor blood","Four, carrying oxygen-rich blood","Two, carrying oxygen-poor blood","Two, carrying oxygen-rich blood"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Usually four pulmonary veins, two from each lung, empty into the left atrium. They carry oxygen-rich blood, because the blood has just picked up oxygen in the lungs. These veins complete the pulmonary circuit and deliver blood to the left side of the heart for pumping to the body.","notes":["The count is right, but the blood is not. Blood in the pulmonary veins has just left the lungs and is oxygen-rich; oxygen-poor blood enters the lungs through the pulmonary arteries.","Correct. Four pulmonary veins bring oxygen-rich blood to the left atrium.","Both parts are off. Each lung usually sends two pulmonary veins, four in all, and blood leaving the lungs is oxygen-rich. Oxygen-poor blood travels to the lungs in the pulmonary arteries.","The oxygen content is right, but the count is not. Each lung usually sends two pulmonary veins, so the left atrium receives four; students often guess two by picturing one vein per lung."],"lt":"t-heart-vessels-coronary","lid":"W3-208","comp":62,"competencyId":"bvn-great-vessels"},{"id":"L03521","dok":1,"q":"What is the largest artery in the body?","a":"Aorta.","options":["Aorta","Femoral artery","Common carotid artery","Pulmonary trunk"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The aorta is the largest artery in the body. It leaves the left ventricle, arches over the heart, and descends through the thorax and abdomen, giving off branches that supply every region of the body. It is an elastic artery, so it stretches with each heartbeat and recoils between beats to keep blood moving.","notes":["Correct. The aorta is the largest artery in the body.","The femoral artery is the main artery of the thigh, a muscular artery well downstream of the aorta. It would be the answer for the large artery of the anterior thigh.","The common carotid arteries carry blood up the neck to the head. Choose them for the main arteries of the neck, not the largest artery.","The pulmonary trunk is a large elastic artery leaving the right ventricle, but it is short and smaller than the aorta. It would be the answer for the artery that carries blood toward the lungs."],"lt":"t-heart-vessels-coronary","lid":"W3-210","comp":62,"competencyId":"bvn-great-vessels"},{"id":"L03522","dok":1,"q":"Which statement correctly describes the blood the aorta carries?","a":"Oxygen-rich blood from the left ventricle to the body.","options":["Oxygen-poor blood from the right ventricle to the lungs","Oxygen-poor blood from the body to the right atrium","Oxygen-rich blood from the left ventricle to the body","Oxygen-rich blood from the lungs to the left atrium"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The aorta receives oxygen-rich blood from the left ventricle through the aortic valve. Its branches then carry that blood to every region of the body, which makes the aorta the main trunk of the systemic circuit.","notes":["This describes the pulmonary trunk and pulmonary arteries, which carry oxygen-poor blood to the lungs. Choose this statement for the vessel leaving the right ventricle.","This describes the superior and inferior venae cavae, which return oxygen-poor blood from the body. It fits the veins that enter the right atrium.","Correct. The aorta carries oxygen-rich blood from the left ventricle to the body.","This describes the pulmonary veins, which bring oxygen-rich blood back from the lungs. It would be correct for the vessels entering the left atrium."],"lt":"t-heart-vessels-coronary","lid":"W3-211","comp":62,"competencyId":"bvn-great-vessels"},{"id":"L03523","dok":1,"q":"What is coronary circulation?","a":"The blood supply and drainage of the heart wall itself.","options":["The flow of blood between the heart and the lungs","The flow of blood from the heart to the head and neck","The blood supply and drainage of the heart wall itself","The flow of blood through the four chambers of the heart"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Coronary circulation is the heart's own blood supply. The blood passing through the chambers does not nourish the thick myocardium, so the heart wall gets its oxygen from the coronary arteries, which leave the base of the aorta. Cardiac veins then drain the wall into the coronary sinus, which empties into the right atrium.","notes":["Blood flowing between the heart and the lungs is the pulmonary circuit. Choose it for the loop that oxygenates blood.","Blood flowing to the head and neck travels in branches of the aortic arch, such as the common carotid arteries. That is part of the systemic circuit, not the heart's own supply.","Correct. Coronary circulation supplies and drains the heart wall.","The path through the four chambers is the pathway of blood through the heart. This blood passes through the heart but does not supply its wall, which is why the coronary vessels are needed."],"lt":"t-heart-vessels-coronary","lid":"W3-212","comp":63,"competencyId":"cv-coronary"},{"id":"L03524","dok":1,"q":"Where do the coronary arteries originate?","a":"At the base of the aorta.","options":["From the aortic arch","At the base of the aorta","From the pulmonary trunk","From the coronary sinus"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The right and left coronary arteries are the first branches of the aorta. They arise from its base, just above the aortic valve, and then run in the grooves on the heart's surface to supply the myocardium. Branching this early means the heart wall receives oxygen-rich blood straight from the left ventricle.","notes":["The aortic arch gives off the brachiocephalic trunk, left common carotid artery, and left subclavian artery. Choose the arch for the arteries to the head and upper limbs; the coronary arteries arise earlier, at the base of the ascending aorta.","Correct. The coronary arteries leave the base of the aorta, just above the aortic valve.","The pulmonary trunk carries oxygen-poor blood to the lungs, so it would be a poor source for the heart wall. No coronary artery branches from it.","The coronary sinus is a vein that collects blood from the cardiac veins and empties into the right atrium. It is the drain of the coronary circulation, not its source."],"lt":"t-heart-vessels-coronary","lid":"W3-213","comp":63,"competencyId":"cv-coronary"},{"id":"L03525","dok":1,"q":"What tissue do the coronary arteries supply?","a":"The myocardium of the heart wall.","options":["The organs of the abdomen","The tissues of the head and neck","The alveolar walls of the lungs","The myocardium of the heart wall"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The coronary arteries supply the heart wall, above all the myocardium, the thick layer of cardiac muscle that does the pumping. The myocardium works nonstop and needs a steady supply of oxygen, but it cannot draw enough from the blood passing through the chambers, so it relies on its own arteries.","notes":["The abdominal organs are supplied by branches of the abdominal aorta, such as the celiac trunk and mesenteric arteries. That would be the answer for the digestive tract's blood supply.","The head and neck are supplied by the common carotid and vertebral arteries, branches of the aortic arch and subclavian arteries. Choose them for the arteries of the neck.","The lungs receive blood from the pulmonary arteries, which bring oxygen-poor blood for gas exchange, and from small bronchial arteries. The coronary arteries do not reach the lungs.","Correct. The coronary arteries feed the myocardium."],"lt":"t-heart-vessels-coronary","lid":"W3-214","comp":63,"competencyId":"cv-coronary"},{"id":"L03526","dok":1,"q":"Which vessel collects blood from most of the cardiac veins?","a":"Coronary sinus.","options":["Great cardiac vein","Coronary sinus","Superior vena cava","Inferior vena cava"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The coronary sinus is a large vein on the posterior surface of the heart, lying in the groove between the atria and ventricles. The great, middle, and small cardiac veins all drain into it, and it empties into the right atrium. A few small anterior cardiac veins bypass it and drain straight into the right atrium, which is why it collects most, not all, of the heart's venous blood.","notes":["The great cardiac vein is one of the veins that drains into the coronary sinus. It runs with the anterior interventricular artery (LAD) and would be the answer for the vein in the anterior interventricular groove.","Correct. The coronary sinus collects the cardiac veins.","The superior vena cava drains the head, neck, and upper limbs into the right atrium. It does not receive the cardiac veins.","The inferior vena cava drains the lower limbs, abdomen, and pelvis into the right atrium. The cardiac veins do not join it."],"lt":"t-heart-vessels-coronary","lid":"W3-215","comp":63,"competencyId":"cv-coronary"},{"id":"L03527","dok":1,"q":"The coronary sinus empties into which chamber?","a":"Right atrium.","options":["Right atrium","Right ventricle","Left ventricle","Left atrium"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The coronary sinus carries oxygen-poor blood drained from the heart wall, so it returns that blood to the same chamber that receives the venae cavae, the right atrium. From there the blood joins the rest of the systemic return and heads to the lungs.","notes":["Correct. The coronary sinus opens into the right atrium.","The right ventricle receives blood from the right atrium through the tricuspid valve, not directly from the coronary sinus. It is the answer for the chamber that pumps blood to the lungs.","The left ventricle receives blood from the left atrium and pumps it into the aorta. No vein empties into it.","The left atrium receives oxygen-rich blood from the pulmonary veins. Oxygen-poor blood from the heart wall does not go there; choose it for where pulmonary blood returns."],"lt":"t-heart-vessels-coronary","lid":"W3-216","comp":63,"competencyId":"cv-coronary"},{"id":"L03528","dok":1,"q":"What are the two main coronary arteries?","a":"Right coronary artery and left coronary artery.","options":["Anterior and posterior interventricular arteries","Right and left marginal arteries","Right and left coronary arteries","Right and left pulmonary arteries"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The two main coronary arteries are the right coronary artery (RCA) and the left coronary artery (LCA). Both leave the base of the aorta and run in the grooves on the heart's surface. Each then gives off smaller named branches that spread over the myocardium.","notes":["The anterior and posterior interventricular arteries are branches, not the main coronary arteries. The anterior one (LAD) comes from the left coronary artery and the posterior one (PDA) usually comes from the right.","The right and left marginal arteries are smaller branches running along the heart's borders. The right marginal comes from the right coronary artery and the left marginal from the left coronary artery.","Correct. The RCA and LCA are the two main coronary arteries.","The right and left pulmonary arteries are branches of the pulmonary trunk that carry oxygen-poor blood to the lungs. Choose them for the pulmonary circuit, not the heart's own supply."],"lt":"t-heart-vessels-coronary","lid":"W3-217","comp":63,"competencyId":"cv-coronary"},{"id":"L03529","dok":1,"q":"Which two arteries are the main branches of the right coronary artery?","a":"Right marginal artery and posterior interventricular artery.","options":["Anterior interventricular and circumflex arteries","Right marginal and circumflex arteries","Right marginal and posterior interventricular arteries","Anterior and posterior interventricular arteries"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The right coronary artery runs in the groove between the right atrium and right ventricle. It gives off the right marginal artery along the right border of the heart and, in most people, continues around to the back of the heart as the posterior interventricular artery, also called the posterior descending artery (PDA).","notes":["The anterior interventricular artery (LAD) and the circumflex artery are the main branches of the left coronary artery. Choose this pair for the left side.","The right marginal artery is a right coronary branch, but the circumflex artery belongs to the left coronary artery. This pair mixes one branch from each side.","Correct. The right marginal and posterior interventricular arteries branch from the RCA.","These two arteries run in the anterior and posterior interventricular grooves, but they come from different main arteries. The anterior one comes from the left coronary artery, so only the posterior one is a right coronary branch."],"lt":"t-heart-vessels-coronary","lid":"W3-218","comp":63,"competencyId":"cv-coronary"},{"id":"L03530","dok":1,"q":"What is another name for the posterior interventricular artery?","a":"Posterior descending artery, or PDA.","options":["Posterior descending artery","Right marginal artery","Circumflex artery","Left anterior descending artery"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The posterior interventricular artery is also called the posterior descending artery, or PDA, because it descends along the posterior interventricular groove toward the apex. In most people it is a branch of the right coronary artery, and it supplies the posterior part of the interventricular septum.","notes":["Correct. The posterior interventricular artery is the posterior descending artery (PDA).","The right marginal artery is the other main branch of the right coronary artery, running along the heart's right border. It is a separate vessel, not another name for the PDA.","The circumflex artery is a branch of the left coronary artery that wraps around the left side of the heart in the groove between the left atrium and ventricle. It is a separate vessel with its own course.","The left anterior descending artery (LAD) is the other name for the anterior interventricular artery, the left coronary branch on the front of the heart. It is the PDA's counterpart on the anterior surface."],"lt":"t-heart-vessels-coronary","lid":"W3-219","comp":63,"competencyId":"cv-coronary"},{"id":"L03531","dok":1,"q":"Which arteries are main branches of the left coronary artery?","a":"Anterior interventricular artery (LAD) and circumflex artery.","options":["Anterior interventricular and circumflex arteries","Posterior interventricular and circumflex arteries","Anterior interventricular and right marginal arteries","Right marginal and posterior interventricular arteries"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The left coronary artery is short. Soon after leaving the aorta it divides into the anterior interventricular artery, better known as the LAD, which runs down the front of the heart between the ventricles, and the circumflex artery, which curves around the left side in the groove between the left atrium and ventricle. These branches supply most of the left side of the heart.","notes":["Correct. The LAD and the circumflex artery branch from the left coronary artery.","The circumflex is a left coronary branch, but the posterior interventricular artery usually comes from the right coronary artery. This pair mixes one branch from each side.","The anterior interventricular artery belongs to the left coronary artery, but the right marginal artery belongs to the right. This pair mixes the two sides.","The right marginal and posterior interventricular arteries are the main branches of the right coronary artery. Choose this pair for the right side."],"lt":"t-heart-vessels-coronary","lid":"W3-220","comp":63,"competencyId":"cv-coronary"},{"id":"L03532","dok":1,"q":"The LAD, or left anterior descending artery, is also called which artery?","a":"Anterior interventricular artery.","options":["Circumflex artery","Right marginal artery","Posterior interventricular artery","Anterior interventricular artery"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"LAD stands for left anterior descending artery, the clinical name for the anterior interventricular artery. It is a branch of the left coronary artery that descends toward the apex along the anterior interventricular groove, the groove on the front of the heart that marks the septum between the ventricles. The great cardiac vein runs beside it.","notes":["The circumflex artery is the other main branch of the left coronary artery, curving around the left side of the heart. It does not descend along the front of the septum.","The right marginal artery is a branch of the right coronary artery running along the right border of the heart. It would be the answer for the artery that the small cardiac vein accompanies.","The posterior interventricular artery is the PDA, the posterior descending artery, which usually branches from the right coronary artery. It runs on the back of the heart and is the LAD's posterior counterpart.","Correct. The LAD is the anterior interventricular artery."],"lt":"t-heart-vessels-coronary","lid":"W3-221","comp":63,"competencyId":"cv-coronary"},{"id":"L03533","dok":1,"q":"Which coronary artery usually supplies the SA and AV nodes?","a":"Right coronary artery.","options":["Right coronary artery","Left coronary artery","Left marginal artery","Anterior interventricular artery"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"In most people the right coronary artery supplies both the SA node, the pacemaker in the wall of the right atrium, and the AV node, in the floor of the right atrium. This fits its location, since the RCA runs along the right side of the heart. It is also why a blockage of the RCA can cause rhythm problems as well as muscle damage.","notes":["Correct. The RCA usually supplies both nodes.","The left coronary artery supplies the left atrium, left ventricle, anterior septum, and apex. In a minority of people one of its branches supplies the SA node, but the usual source of both nodes is the right coronary artery.","The left marginal artery runs along the left border of the heart and supplies the lateral wall of the left ventricle. It is far from the right atrium, where both nodes sit.","The anterior interventricular artery (LAD) runs down the front of the septum and supplies the anterior septum, much of the left ventricle, and the apex. Choose it for a question about the artery to the front of the left ventricle."],"lt":"t-heart-vessels-coronary","lid":"W3-222","comp":63,"competencyId":"cv-coronary"},{"id":"L03534","dok":1,"q":"In the usual pattern, which artery supplies the apex of the heart?","a":"Left coronary artery.","options":["Left subclavian artery","Pulmonary trunk","Left coronary artery","Right coronary artery"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"In the usual pattern, the left coronary artery supplies the left atrium, the left ventricle, the anterior part of the interventricular septum, and the apex. Its anterior interventricular branch, the LAD, runs down the front of the heart toward the apex. The apex is the tip of the left ventricle, so it makes sense that the left coronary artery serves it.","notes":["The left subclavian artery is the third branch of the aortic arch and supplies the left upper limb. Choose it for the artery heading toward the left arm.","The pulmonary trunk carries oxygen-poor blood from the right ventricle to the lungs. It does not supply the heart wall, and it would be the answer for the vessel leaving the right ventricle.","Correct. The left coronary artery, through the LAD, supplies the apex.","The right coronary artery supplies the right atrium, most of the right ventricle, the posterior septum, and usually the SA and AV nodes. Choose it for a question about the conduction nodes or the right ventricle."],"lt":"t-heart-vessels-coronary","lid":"W3-223","comp":63,"competencyId":"cv-coronary"},{"id":"L03535","dok":1,"q":"Which cardiac vein runs alongside the LAD (anterior interventricular artery)?","a":"Great cardiac vein.","options":["Middle cardiac vein","Small cardiac vein","Anterior cardiac veins","Great cardiac vein"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The great cardiac vein runs in the anterior interventricular groove beside the LAD, draining the front of the heart. It then curves around to the posterior surface and empties into the coronary sinus. Cardiac veins travel in the same grooves as the coronary arteries, so learning the arteries gives you the veins.","notes":["The middle cardiac vein runs with the posterior interventricular artery (PDA) on the back of the heart. Choose it for the vein in the posterior interventricular groove.","The small cardiac vein runs with the right marginal artery along the right border of the heart. It would be the answer for the vein beside that right coronary branch.","The anterior cardiac veins drain the front of the right ventricle straight into the right atrium, bypassing the coronary sinus. Pick them for the veins that skip the coronary sinus, not for the vein paired with the LAD.","Correct. The great cardiac vein accompanies the LAD."],"lt":"t-heart-vessels-coronary","lid":"W3-224","comp":63,"competencyId":"cv-coronary"},{"id":"L03536","dok":1,"q":"Which cardiac vein runs alongside the posterior interventricular artery (PDA)?","a":"Middle cardiac vein.","options":["Middle cardiac vein","Great cardiac vein","Coronary sinus","Small cardiac vein"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The middle cardiac vein runs in the posterior interventricular groove beside the PDA, draining the back of both ventricles. It empties into the coronary sinus near where the sinus opens into the right atrium. Its name fits its position, in the middle of the posterior surface between the two ventricles.","notes":["Correct. The middle cardiac vein accompanies the PDA.","The great cardiac vein runs with the anterior interventricular artery (LAD) on the front of the heart. It is the anterior counterpart of the middle cardiac vein.","The coronary sinus is the large collecting vein in the posterior groove between the atria and ventricles. It receives the middle cardiac vein but does not run beside the PDA itself.","The small cardiac vein runs with the right marginal artery along the right border of the heart. Choose it for the vein beside that branch."],"lt":"t-heart-vessels-coronary","lid":"W3-225","comp":63,"competencyId":"cv-coronary"},{"id":"L03537","dok":1,"q":"Which cardiac vein runs alongside the right marginal artery?","a":"Small cardiac vein.","options":["Great cardiac vein","Small cardiac vein","Middle cardiac vein","Anterior cardiac veins"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The small cardiac vein runs along the right border of the heart with the right marginal artery, draining that part of the right side of the heart. It ends by joining the coronary sinus. Like the other named cardiac veins, it shares a groove with a coronary artery branch.","notes":["The great cardiac vein runs with the anterior interventricular artery (LAD) on the front of the heart. It would be the answer for the vein paired with the LAD.","Correct. The small cardiac vein accompanies the right marginal artery.","The middle cardiac vein runs with the posterior interventricular artery (PDA) in the posterior interventricular groove. Choose it for the vein on the back of the heart between the ventricles.","The anterior cardiac veins drain the front of the right ventricle directly into the right atrium, skipping the coronary sinus. They are not the vein paired with the right marginal artery."],"lt":"t-heart-vessels-coronary","lid":"W3-226","comp":63,"competencyId":"cv-coronary"},{"id":"L03538","dok":1,"q":"What is a myocardial infarction?","a":"Death of heart muscle from a blocked blood supply.","options":["Inflammation of the serous pericardium","Death of heart muscle from a blocked coronary artery","A leaky valve that lets blood flow backward","Fluid squeezing the heart within the pericardial cavity"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Myocardial infarction is the medical name for a heart attack. Myo means muscle, cardial means heart, and an infarction is an area of tissue that dies because its blood supply is cut off. When a coronary artery is blocked, the myocardium beyond the blockage is starved of oxygen and its muscle begins to die.","notes":["Inflammation of the pericardium is pericarditis. Choose that for a problem with the sac around the heart, not death of the heart muscle.","Correct. A myocardial infarction is death of heart muscle after its coronary supply is blocked.","A valve that does not close cleanly lets blood leak backward, which can be heard as a murmur. That is a valve problem and would be the answer for what produces a heart murmur.","Fluid building up in the pericardial cavity and squeezing the heart is cardiac tamponade. It limits how much the heart can fill, but it is not caused by a blocked coronary artery."],"lt":"t-heart-vessels-coronary","lid":"W3-227","comp":63,"competencyId":"cv-coronary"},{"id":"L03539","dok":2,"q":"Which sequence correctly traces blood through the coronary circulation?","a":"Aorta, coronary arteries, myocardial capillaries, cardiac veins, coronary sinus, right atrium.","options":["Aorta, coronary arteries, myocardial capillaries, cardiac veins, coronary sinus, left atrium","Pulmonary trunk, coronary arteries, myocardial capillaries, cardiac veins, coronary sinus, right atrium","Aorta, cardiac veins, myocardial capillaries, coronary arteries, coronary sinus, right atrium","Aorta, coronary arteries, myocardial capillaries, cardiac veins, coronary sinus, right atrium"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Coronary blood starts in the aorta, where the coronary arteries branch off its base. The arteries feed the capillaries of the myocardium, where oxygen and nutrients pass to the muscle. The blood then drains into the cardiac veins, which empty into the coronary sinus, and the coronary sinus returns it to the right atrium.","notes":["Every step is right except the last. The coronary sinus empties into the right atrium; the left atrium receives only the pulmonary veins from the lungs.","The pulmonary trunk carries oxygen-poor blood to the lungs and gives off no coronary branches. The coronary arteries arise from the base of the aorta, so the trace must start there.","This puts the veins before the arteries. Blood always flows from arteries into capillaries and then into veins, so the cardiac veins come after the capillaries.","Correct. Aorta, coronary arteries, capillaries, cardiac veins, coronary sinus, right atrium."],"lt":"t-heart-vessels-coronary","lid":"W3-241","comp":63,"competencyId":"cv-coronary"},{"id":"L03540","dok":2,"q":"Why does the heart need its own coronary circulation?","a":"The thick myocardium cannot get enough oxygen from the blood inside its chambers.","options":["The coronary vessels carry oxygen-poor blood to the lungs","All four heart chambers hold only oxygen-poor blood","The deep myocardium cannot get enough oxygen from chamber blood","The coronary vessels pump blood to assist the ventricles"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The myocardium is thick and works without rest, so it needs a large, steady supply of oxygen and nutrients. The blood rushing through the chambers can reach only the innermost cells of the wall, so the rest of the muscle depends on the coronary arteries. These arteries bring oxygen-rich blood into the wall itself and spread it through capillaries in the muscle.","notes":["Carrying oxygen-poor blood to the lungs is the job of the pulmonary trunk and pulmonary arteries. Choose that for the pulmonary circuit, not for the heart's own supply.","Only the right chambers hold oxygen-poor blood; the left atrium and left ventricle are full of oxygen-rich blood. The real issue is reaching the deep muscle of the wall, not the oxygen content of the chamber blood.","Correct. The deep myocardium depends on the coronary arteries for its oxygen.","Vessels do not pump blood; only the heart's chambers do. This choice confuses the coronary arteries, which supply the wall, with the ventricles, which do the pumping."],"lt":"t-heart-vessels-coronary","lid":"W3-242","comp":63,"competencyId":"cv-coronary"},{"id":"L03541","dok":2,"q":"Why can a blocked coronary artery cause a heart attack?","a":"The myocardium beyond the blockage loses its oxygen supply and begins to die.","options":["The blocked artery stops blood from leaving the left ventricle","The myocardium beyond the blockage loses oxygen and begins to die","The blockage also closes the coronary sinus so the wall cannot drain","The blockage reverses blood flow through the aortic valve"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Each coronary artery is the supply line for its own patch of myocardium. If it is blocked, the muscle downstream receives no oxygen-rich blood, and before long those cells begin to die. That area of dead muscle is a myocardial infarction, or heart attack, and it weakens the heart's ability to pump.","notes":["The coronary arteries supply the heart wall; they are not the path blood takes out of the left ventricle. Blood leaves the left ventricle through the aortic valve into the aorta, which stays open in a heart attack.","Correct. Muscle beyond the blockage is starved of oxygen and dies.","The coronary sinus is the vein that drains the heart wall, and an arterial blockage does not close it. A problem with the coronary sinus would affect drainage, not the oxygen supply.","The aortic valve keeps blood from falling back from the aorta into the left ventricle, and a coronary blockage does not reverse it. A leaking valve would produce a murmur, not death of heart muscle."],"lt":"t-heart-vessels-coronary","lid":"W3-243","comp":63,"competencyId":"cv-coronary"},{"id":"L03542","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares the pulmonary veins with the venae cavae?","a":"Pulmonary veins bring oxygen-rich blood to the left atrium; venae cavae bring oxygen-poor blood to the right atrium.","options":["Pulmonary veins bring oxygen-poor blood to the right atrium; venae cavae bring oxygen-rich blood to the left atrium","Pulmonary veins bring oxygen-rich blood to the left atrium; venae cavae bring oxygen-poor blood to the right atrium","Both bring oxygen-poor blood into the right atrium of the heart","Pulmonary veins carry blood away from the heart; venae cavae carry blood toward it"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Both sets of vessels are veins, because both carry blood toward the heart, but they serve different circuits. The pulmonary veins end the pulmonary circuit, bringing oxygen-rich blood from the lungs into the left atrium. The superior and inferior venae cavae end the systemic circuit, bringing oxygen-poor blood from the body into the right atrium.","notes":["This swaps every detail between the two. Blood from the lungs is oxygen-rich and enters the left side, while blood from the body is oxygen-poor and enters the right side.","Correct. The pulmonary veins return oxygen-rich blood to the left atrium and the venae cavae return oxygen-poor blood to the right atrium.","Only the venae cavae carry oxygen-poor blood to the right atrium. The pulmonary veins carry oxygen-rich blood from the lungs to the left atrium, which is the main thing that sets them apart.","All veins carry blood toward the heart, including the pulmonary veins. Carrying blood away from the heart would make a vessel an artery, as with the pulmonary trunk."],"lt":"t-heart-vessels-coronary","lid":"W3-244","comp":62,"competencyId":"bvn-great-vessels"},{"id":"L03543","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares the pulmonary trunk with the aorta?","a":"The pulmonary trunk leaves the right ventricle; the aorta leaves the left ventricle.","options":["Both leave the left ventricle, one heading to the lungs and one to the body","The pulmonary trunk leaves the right ventricle; the aorta leaves the left ventricle","The pulmonary trunk leaves the left ventricle; the aorta leaves the right ventricle","The pulmonary trunk carries oxygen-rich blood; the aorta carries oxygen-poor blood"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The pulmonary trunk and the aorta are the two great arteries leaving the heart, and each comes from a different ventricle. The pulmonary trunk carries oxygen-poor blood from the right ventricle toward the lungs, and the aorta carries oxygen-rich blood from the left ventricle to the body. Having one outflow vessel per ventricle is what keeps the two circuits separate.","notes":["Each ventricle has its own outflow vessel, and the left ventricle opens only into the aorta. The pulmonary trunk comes from the right ventricle, which is why the two sides of the heart can serve two separate circuits.","Correct. The right ventricle opens into the pulmonary trunk and the left ventricle opens into the aorta.","This reverses the sides. The right ventricle drives the pulmonary circuit to the lungs, and the left ventricle drives the systemic circuit to the body.","This reverses the oxygen content. The pulmonary trunk carries oxygen-poor blood to be oxygenated in the lungs, and the aorta carries oxygen-rich blood out to the body."],"lt":"t-heart-vessels-coronary","lid":"W3-245","comp":62,"competencyId":"bvn-great-vessels"},{"id":"L03544","dok":2,"q":"Why are the pulmonary arteries unusual among arteries?","a":"They carry oxygen-poor blood away from the heart.","options":["They carry blood toward the heart instead of away from it","They branch directly from the left ventricle","They have no tunica media in their walls","They carry oxygen-poor blood away from the heart"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Most arteries carry oxygen-rich blood, but the pulmonary arteries carry oxygen-poor blood from the right ventricle to the lungs. They are still arteries because an artery is defined by direction: it carries blood away from the heart. In the lungs this blood picks up oxygen before returning in the pulmonary veins.","notes":["Carrying blood toward the heart would make them veins. The pulmonary arteries carry blood away from the heart, like every artery; what is unusual is the oxygen content of that blood.","The pulmonary arteries branch from the pulmonary trunk, which leaves the right ventricle. The left ventricle's only outflow vessel is the aorta.","The pulmonary arteries have all three tunics, including a tunica media of smooth muscle and elastic fibers. A wall made of only the tunica intima belongs to a capillary.","Correct. They are arteries that carry oxygen-poor blood."],"lt":"t-heart-vessels-coronary","lid":"W3-246","comp":67,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-types"},{"id":"L03545","dok":2,"q":"Why are the pulmonary veins unusual among veins?","a":"They carry oxygen-rich blood toward the heart.","options":["They carry oxygen-rich blood toward the heart","They empty into the right atrium","They have thicker walls than the aorta","They carry blood away from the heart"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Most veins carry oxygen-poor blood back from the tissues, but the pulmonary veins carry oxygen-rich blood from the lungs to the left atrium. They are still veins because they carry blood toward the heart. Their blood has just been oxygenated in the lung capillaries.","notes":["Correct. They are veins that carry oxygen-rich blood.","The pulmonary veins empty into the left atrium. The right atrium receives the venae cavae and the coronary sinus, which carry oxygen-poor blood.","Veins, including the pulmonary veins, have thinner walls than arteries, and the aorta has one of the thickest walls of any vessel. This choice reverses the usual artery and vein wall pattern.","No vein carries blood away from the heart; that would make it an artery. The pulmonary veins carry blood toward the heart, like all veins."],"lt":"t-heart-vessels-coronary","lid":"W3-247","comp":67,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-types"},{"id":"L03546","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares the regions supplied by the right and left coronary arteries?","a":"The right supplies most of the right ventricle and usually the nodes; the left supplies most of the left ventricle and the apex.","options":["The right supplies the anterior septum; the left supplies the posterior septum","The right supplies the left ventricle and the apex; the left supplies the right ventricle and the two nodes","Both supply only the atria, since the ventricles are fed by the blood in their chambers","The right feeds most of the right ventricle and usually the nodes; the left, most of the left ventricle and apex"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"In the usual pattern, the right coronary artery supplies the right atrium, most of the right ventricle, part of the left ventricle, the posterior septum, and usually the SA and AV nodes. The left coronary artery supplies the left atrium, most of the left ventricle, the anterior septum, and the apex. Each artery mainly feeds the side of the heart it travels along.","notes":["This reverses the septum. The LAD, a left coronary branch, supplies the anterior septum, and the PDA, usually a right coronary branch, supplies the posterior septum.","This swaps the two sides. The left coronary artery is the one that reaches the apex and most of the left ventricle, while the right coronary artery usually feeds the nodes in the right atrium.","Both coronary arteries supply the ventricles as well as the atria, and the thick ventricular walls need them most. The blood passing through the chambers does not nourish the deep myocardium, which is why coronary arteries exist.","Correct. Each coronary artery mainly supplies its own side of the heart."],"lt":"t-heart-vessels-coronary","lid":"W3-248","comp":63,"competencyId":"cv-coronary"},{"id":"L03547","dok":2,"q":"Why is the LAD (anterior interventricular artery) considered so clinically important?","a":"It supplies the anterior septum, much of the left ventricle, and the apex.","options":["It is the only artery that supplies the right atrium","It carries blood out of the right ventricle to the lungs","It feeds the anterior septum, much of the left ventricle, and the apex","It drains blood from the heart wall into the coronary sinus"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The LAD runs down the front of the heart in the anterior interventricular groove and supplies the anterior part of the interventricular septum, much of the front wall of the left ventricle, and the apex. The left ventricle pumps blood to the entire body, so losing the LAD's territory takes out a large share of the heart's pumping muscle.","notes":["The right atrium is supplied mainly by the right coronary artery, which runs along the right side of the heart. Choose the RCA for the right atrium and the nodes within it.","Carrying blood from the right ventricle to the lungs is the job of the pulmonary trunk. The LAD is a small artery on the heart surface, not a great vessel.","Correct. The LAD feeds a large part of the left ventricle, the anterior septum, and the apex.","Draining the heart wall into the coronary sinus is the job of the cardiac veins. The great cardiac vein runs beside the LAD, so it would be the answer for the vein that drains the LAD's territory."],"lt":"t-heart-vessels-coronary","lid":"W3-249","comp":63,"competencyId":"cv-coronary"},{"id":"L03548","dok":1,"q":"Most cardiac veins drain into the coronary sinus. Which veins bypass it and drain straight into the right atrium?","a":"Anterior cardiac veins.","options":["Small cardiac vein","Great cardiac vein","Anterior cardiac veins","Middle cardiac vein"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The anterior cardiac veins are small veins on the front of the right ventricle. Instead of joining the coronary sinus, they open directly into the right atrium. All the other named cardiac veins empty into the coronary sinus first, and the coronary sinus then empties into the right atrium.","notes":["The small cardiac vein runs with the right marginal artery and empties into the coronary sinus. It would be the answer for the vein along the heart's right border.","The great cardiac vein runs with the LAD on the front of the heart and then curves around to empty into the coronary sinus. It is the answer for the vein paired with the anterior interventricular artery.","Correct. The anterior cardiac veins skip the coronary sinus and open into the right atrium.","The middle cardiac vein runs with the PDA on the back of the heart and empties into the coronary sinus. Choose it for the vein in the posterior interventricular groove."],"lt":"t-heart-vessels-coronary","lid":"W3-250","comp":63,"competencyId":"cv-coronary"},{"id":"L03549","dok":2,"q":"The coronary arteries are small. Why is a problem in them so serious?","a":"They supply the myocardium that pumps blood for the entire body.","options":["They supply the myocardium that pumps blood for the entire body","They are the main vessels returning blood to the right atrium","They form the valves that keep blood flowing forward","They carry all of the blood leaving the left ventricle"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The coronary arteries carry only a small share of the blood the heart pumps, but that blood feeds the myocardium, the muscle that drives both circulations. If they fail, the heart muscle weakens or dies, and every organ loses its blood supply as the pump fails. Their importance comes from what they feed, not from their size.","notes":["Correct. They feed the muscle that powers the whole circulation.","Returning blood to the right atrium is the job of the venae cavae and the coronary sinus. The coronary arteries deliver blood to the heart wall rather than draining it.","The valves are flaps of endocardium over a connective tissue core, not arteries. Choose the valves for a question about one-way flow through the heart.","Nearly all the blood leaving the left ventricle enters the aorta and travels to the body. Only a small fraction enters the coronary arteries at the aortic base."],"lt":"t-heart-vessels-coronary","lid":"W3-251","comp":63,"competencyId":"cv-coronary"},{"id":"L03550","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly pairs the cardiac veins with the arteries they run beside?","a":"Great with the LAD, middle with the PDA, small with the right marginal artery.","options":["Great with the LAD, middle with the PDA, small with the right marginal artery","Great with the PDA, middle with the LAD, small with the right marginal artery","Great with the LAD, middle with the right marginal artery, small with the PDA","Great with the right marginal artery, middle with the PDA, small with the LAD"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Each named cardiac vein travels in the same groove as a coronary artery. The great cardiac vein runs with the anterior interventricular artery (LAD) on the front, the middle cardiac vein runs with the posterior interventricular artery (PDA) on the back, and the small cardiac vein runs with the right marginal artery along the right border. All three end in the coronary sinus.","notes":["Correct. Great with the LAD, middle with the PDA, small with the right marginal artery.","This swaps the great and middle cardiac veins. The great cardiac vein is on the front of the heart with the LAD, and the middle cardiac vein is on the back with the PDA.","The great cardiac vein is paired correctly here, but the middle and small cardiac veins are swapped. The middle vein runs with the PDA and the small vein runs with the right marginal artery.","This swaps the great and small cardiac veins. The small cardiac vein runs along the right border with the right marginal artery, and the great cardiac vein runs with the LAD."],"lt":"t-heart-vessels-coronary","lid":"W3-252","comp":63,"competencyId":"cv-coronary"},{"id":"L03551","dok":3,"q":"A patient with a blocked right coronary artery develops an abnormal heart rhythm. Which structures, usually supplied by the RCA, best explain this?","a":"The SA and AV nodes.","options":["SA and AV nodes","Pulmonary and aortic valves","Anterior septum and apex","Left atrium and left auricle"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"In most people the right coronary artery supplies the SA node, the pacemaker that starts each beat, and the AV node, which passes the signal on to the ventricles. If the RCA is blocked, these nodal cells are starved of oxygen and may not fire or conduct normally. That is why an RCA blockage can cause a rhythm problem in addition to damaged muscle.","notes":["Correct. The RCA usually feeds both nodes, so its blockage can disturb the rhythm.","The semilunar valve cusps are thin flaps of endocardium and connective tissue that do not set the rhythm. They would be the answer for a question about preventing backflow from the great arteries.","The anterior septum and apex are supplied by the LAD, a branch of the left coronary artery. Damage there would follow an LAD blockage and mainly weaken the left ventricle.","The left atrium and its auricle are supplied by the left coronary artery, mainly through the circumflex branch. They hold no node and would point to a left coronary problem."],"lt":"t-heart-vessels-coronary","lid":"W3-271","comp":63,"competencyId":"cv-coronary"},{"id":"L03552","dok":2,"q":"A patient has a blockage in the LAD. Which region of the heart is most at risk?","a":"The anterior septum, the front of the left ventricle, and the apex.","options":["Anterior septum, front of the left ventricle, and apex","Right border of the right ventricle","Posterior septum and the back of both ventricles","Right atrium, SA node, and AV node"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The LAD runs down the front of the heart in the anterior interventricular groove. It supplies the anterior part of the interventricular septum, much of the front wall of the left ventricle, and the apex. A blockage here starves those areas of oxygen, so they are the regions where heart muscle would die.","notes":["Correct. These are the regions the LAD supplies.","The right border of the heart is supplied by the right marginal artery, a branch of the right coronary artery. It would be at risk if that branch were blocked.","The posterior septum and the back of the ventricles are supplied by the posterior interventricular artery (PDA), usually a branch of the right coronary artery. That region fits a blockage of the PDA.","The right atrium and both nodes are usually supplied by the right coronary artery. Choose them for an RCA blockage, especially one that causes a rhythm problem."],"lt":"t-heart-vessels-coronary","lid":"W3-272","comp":63,"competencyId":"cv-coronary"},{"id":"L03553","dok":3,"q":"Coronary artery disease narrows the coronary arteries. Why can this lead to pumping failure?","a":"The myocardium receives too little oxygen and contracts more weakly.","options":["The narrowed arteries let blood leak backward through the valves","The narrowed arteries block blood from entering the right atrium","The pericardium thickens and stops the heart from beating","The myocardium receives too little oxygen and contracts more weakly"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Coronary artery disease is a narrowing of the coronary arteries, usually by fatty plaques in the vessel wall (atherosclerosis). Less blood reaches the myocardium, so the muscle cells run short of oxygen and cannot contract with full force. If the supply drops far enough, muscle dies, and the weakened wall cannot pump enough blood for the body.","notes":["Backward leak through a valve happens when a valve fails to close, producing a murmur. Coronary disease affects the supply to the muscle, not the valve cusps directly.","Blood enters the right atrium through the venae cavae and coronary sinus, and narrowed coronary arteries do not block those veins. Choose the venae cavae for a question about blood returning from the body.","Excess fluid in the pericardial cavity can restrict filling, as in cardiac tamponade. That is a problem of the sac around the heart, not of the arteries feeding the muscle.","Correct. Starved muscle contracts weakly, so the heart pumps less."],"lt":"t-heart-vessels-coronary","lid":"W3-273","comp":63,"competencyId":"cv-coronary"},{"id":"L03554","dok":2,"q":"If the coronary sinus were blocked, which flow would be impaired?","a":"Venous drainage from the heart wall into the right atrium.","options":["Blood flow from the right ventricle into the pulmonary trunk","Blood flow from the lungs into the left atrium","Arterial supply from the aorta into the myocardium","Venous drainage from the heart wall into the right atrium"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The coronary sinus is the large vein that collects blood from the great, middle, and small cardiac veins and empties it into the right atrium. A blockage there would back up the venous drainage of most of the heart wall. Only the small anterior cardiac veins, which open directly into the right atrium, would still drain normally.","notes":["Flow from the right ventricle into the pulmonary trunk passes through the pulmonary valve. It is part of the pulmonary circuit and does not involve the coronary sinus.","Flow from the lungs into the left atrium travels in the pulmonary veins. That would be the answer for a blockage of the pulmonary veins.","The arterial supply to the myocardium comes from the coronary arteries at the base of the aorta. A blocked coronary artery, not the coronary sinus, would cut that supply.","Correct. The coronary sinus drains most of the heart wall into the right atrium."],"lt":"t-heart-vessels-coronary","lid":"W3-274","comp":63,"competencyId":"cv-coronary"},{"id":"L03555","dok":2,"q":"A blockage of the left coronary artery would cause the greatest loss of pumping muscle in which chamber?","a":"Left ventricle.","options":["Left atrium","Left ventricle","Right ventricle","Right atrium"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The left coronary artery supplies the left atrium, most of the left ventricle, the anterior septum, and the apex. The left ventricle has the thickest wall of the four chambers and pumps blood to the whole body, so a left coronary blockage destroys the most pumping muscle there. This is why left coronary blockages tend to be the most dangerous.","notes":["The left atrium is also supplied by the left coronary artery, but its wall is thin and it does little of the pumping. The thick-walled left ventricle has far more muscle at stake.","Correct. The left ventricle has the most muscle in the left coronary territory.","Most of the right ventricle is supplied by the right coronary artery. Choose it for a question about an RCA blockage.","The right atrium is supplied mainly by the right coronary artery. It would be threatened by an RCA blockage, not a left coronary one."],"lt":"t-heart-vessels-coronary","lid":"W3-275","comp":63,"competencyId":"cv-coronary"},{"id":"L03556","dok":2,"q":"The right coronary artery supplies most of the wall of which ventricle, making it the ventricle most threatened by an RCA blockage?","a":"Right ventricle.","options":["Right ventricle","Left ventricle","Both ventricles equally","Neither ventricle, only the atria"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The right coronary artery runs in the groove between the right atrium and right ventricle and supplies the right atrium, most of the right ventricle, part of the left ventricle, and the posterior septum. Because it feeds most of the right ventricular wall, the right ventricle is the ventricle most threatened when the RCA is blocked, and pumping to the lungs suffers.","notes":["Correct. Most of the right ventricle depends on the RCA.","Most of the left ventricle is supplied by the left coronary artery, and the RCA feeds only part of it. The left ventricle would be the answer for a left coronary blockage.","The two ventricles are not supplied equally by either artery. The right coronary artery feeds most of the right ventricle, while the left coronary artery feeds most of the left ventricle.","The RCA does supply the right atrium, but it also supplies most of the right ventricle. The ventricular muscle is where an RCA blockage does the most pumping damage."],"lt":"t-heart-vessels-coronary","lid":"W3-276","comp":63,"competencyId":"cv-coronary"},{"id":"L03557","dok":3,"q":"Depending on where it occurs, a myocardial infarction can cause problems beyond weaker pumping. Which statement best explains why?","a":"The dying area may include conduction tissue or papillary muscles as well as contractile muscle.","options":["An infarction blocks the venae cavae so blood cannot return","An infarction destroys the fibrous pericardium that anchors the heart","An infarction spreads to the lungs through the pulmonary veins","The dying area may include conduction tissue or papillary muscles"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"An infarction kills whatever myocardium the blocked artery supplied. Depending on the location, that patch may include conduction tissue such as the SA node, AV node, or bundle branches, which disturbs the rhythm, or a papillary muscle, which can let an atrioventricular valve fail. So the result can be a rhythm problem or a leaking valve as well as weaker pumping.","notes":["The venae cavae are large veins outside the heart wall, and an infarction does not block them. A blocked vena cava would stop blood returning from the body, a different problem from dying heart muscle.","The fibrous pericardium is a dense connective tissue sac outside the heart wall, with its own blood supply. An infarction damages heart muscle, not the pericardial sac.","An infarction is a local area of dead heart muscle and does not spread through the pulmonary veins, which carry blood from the lungs toward the heart. This choice invents a route the damage cannot take.","Correct. Conduction tissue and papillary muscles are myocardium too, so they can die in an infarction."],"lt":"t-heart-vessels-coronary","lid":"W3-277","comp":63,"competencyId":"cv-coronary"},{"id":"L03558","dok":3,"q":"Why can a coronary artery blockage lead to papillary muscle damage and a leaking valve?","a":"Papillary muscles are part of the ventricular myocardium and depend on the coronary arteries.","options":["Papillary muscles are ventricular myocardium fed by the coronary arteries","Papillary muscles are conduction tissue that stops firing first","Papillary muscles sit inside the aorta beside the coronary openings","Papillary muscles are dense connective tissue fed by blood in the atria"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Papillary muscles are cone-shaped projections of the ventricular wall, so they are myocardium supplied by the coronary arteries like the rest of the wall. They anchor the chordae tendineae that hold the atrioventricular valves shut. If a blockage kills a papillary muscle, the chordae lose their anchor and the tricuspid or mitral valve can fail, letting blood leak back into the atrium.","notes":["Correct. Papillary muscles are myocardium and lose their supply when a coronary artery is blocked.","Papillary muscles are contractile myocardium, not conduction tissue, although the moderator band carries part of the conduction system to the anterior papillary muscle of the right ventricle. Choose the conduction system for a question about rhythm, not valve support.","Papillary muscles project from the inner walls of the ventricles, not from the aorta. The aortic valve is a semilunar valve and has no papillary muscles or chordae tendineae.","Papillary muscles are cardiac muscle, not dense connective tissue; the chordae tendineae are the fibrous cords. Blood in the chambers also does not nourish the deep wall, which is why the coronary arteries are needed."],"lt":"t-heart-vessels-coronary","lid":"W3-278","comp":63,"competencyId":"cv-coronary"},{"id":"L03559","dok":3,"q":"Why is damage to the apex especially serious in a left coronary blockage?","a":"The apex is formed by the left ventricle, the chamber that pumps blood to the body.","options":["The apex is where the pulmonary veins enter the heart","The apex holds the aortic valve, which would stop closing","The apex contains the SA node that starts every heartbeat","It is formed by the left ventricle, the chamber that pumps blood to the body"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The apex is the blunt inferior tip of the heart, and it is formed by the left ventricle. Its muscle is supplied by the left coronary artery through the LAD. If that muscle dies, the left ventricle, which pumps blood into the aorta and out to the whole body, loses part of its pumping wall.","notes":["The pulmonary veins enter the left atrium at the base of the heart, the opposite end from the apex. Choose the base for where the great vessels attach.","The aortic valve sits at the base of the heart, at the exit of the left ventricle into the aorta. It is at the opposite end from the apex.","The SA node sits in the wall of the right atrium near the opening of the superior vena cava, far from the apex. It would be the answer for where each heartbeat starts.","Correct. The apex is left ventricle muscle, and the left ventricle pumps blood to the whole body."],"lt":"t-heart-vessels-coronary","lid":"W3-298","comp":63,"competencyId":"cv-coronary"},{"id":"L03560","dok":2,"q":"Which statement best explains why coronary circulation and the conduction system are linked clinically?","a":"Coronary arteries feed the conduction tissue, so a blockage can disturb the rhythm as well as weaken contraction.","options":["Coronary arteries feed the conduction tissue, so a blockage can disturb the rhythm","The conduction system pumps blood through the coronary arteries","Coronary veins carry the electrical signal from the SA node to the ventricles","The conduction system is made of blood vessels that branch from the aorta"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The conduction system is made of specialized cardiac muscle cells, and like all myocardium it depends on the coronary arteries for oxygen. The right coronary artery usually feeds the SA and AV nodes, and coronary branches that enter the septum supply the AV bundle and bundle branches. A coronary blockage can therefore starve conduction tissue and cause a rhythm problem in addition to killing contractile muscle.","notes":["Correct. Conduction tissue depends on the coronary supply, so a blockage can affect rhythm.","The conduction system carries an electrical signal; it does not pump blood. The contraction of the myocardium is what moves blood, including into the coronary arteries from the aorta.","Veins carry blood, not electrical signals. The signal travels from the SA node to the ventricles through the AV node, AV bundle, bundle branches, and Purkinje fibers.","The conduction system is made of specialized cardiac muscle cells, not blood vessels. The vessels branching from the base of the aorta are the coronary arteries."],"lt":"t-heart-vessels-coronary","lid":"W3-300","comp":63,"competencyId":"cv-coronary"},{"id":"L03914","dok":2,"q":"Which sequence correctly traces coronary circulation from the aorta back to the right atrium?","a":"Aorta, coronary arteries, myocardial capillaries, cardiac veins, coronary sinus, right atrium.","options":["Aorta, coronary arteries, myocardial capillaries, coronary sinus, cardiac veins, right atrium","Aorta, coronary arteries, myocardial capillaries, cardiac veins, coronary sinus, right atrium","Aorta, cardiac veins, myocardial capillaries, coronary arteries, coronary sinus, right atrium","Aorta, coronary arteries, myocardial capillaries, cardiac veins, coronary sinus, left atrium"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The coronary arteries branch from the base of the aorta and carry oxygen-rich blood to the capillaries of the myocardium, the heart muscle. Cardiac veins collect the blood from those capillaries and empty into the coronary sinus, a large vein on the posterior heart. The coronary sinus then opens into the right atrium.","notes":["This sequence reverses the coronary sinus and the cardiac veins. The cardiac veins are the smaller tributaries that feed into the coronary sinus, and the coronary sinus is the last vessel before the heart chamber.","Correct. This is the coronary route from the aorta to the right atrium.","This sequence puts the cardiac veins before the capillaries. Veins carry blood away from capillaries, so the coronary arteries must come first, feeding the capillaries, and the cardiac veins come after.","This sequence ends in the left atrium, which receives blood only from the pulmonary veins. The coronary sinus returns oxygen-poor blood, so it empties into the right atrium with the rest of the body's venous return."],"lt":"t-pathway-full-tracing","lid":"W3-720","comp":63,"competencyId":"cv-coronary"},{"id":"L03951","dok":2,"q":"In coronary circulation, blood that has passed through the capillaries of the myocardium enters which vessels next?","a":"The cardiac veins.","options":["Coronary sinus","Cardiac veins","Pulmonary veins","Coronary arteries"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The heart wall has its own circulation. Coronary arteries feed the capillaries of the myocardium, and after exchange in those capillaries the blood collects into the cardiac veins. The cardiac veins then drain into the coronary sinus, which empties into the right atrium.","notes":["The coronary sinus is one step later. It collects blood from the cardiac veins and empties into the right atrium, so it would be the answer if the question started at the cardiac veins.","Correct. Myocardial capillaries drain into the cardiac veins.","The pulmonary veins drain the lungs into the left atrium and play no part in draining the heart wall. They would be the answer if the question asked which vessels leave the lung capillaries.","The coronary arteries come before the capillaries, carrying oxygen-rich blood from the aorta to the heart muscle. They would be the answer if the question asked what supplies the myocardial capillaries."],"lt":"t-pathway-integrated-tracing","lid":"W3-758","comp":63,"competencyId":"cv-coronary"},{"id":"L03952","dok":1,"q":"Which cardiac vein runs alongside the anterior interventricular artery, the LAD, and drains into the coronary sinus?","a":"The great cardiac vein.","options":["Middle cardiac vein","Anterior cardiac veins","Small cardiac vein","Great cardiac vein"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Each named cardiac vein travels in the same groove as a coronary artery. The great cardiac vein runs in the anterior interventricular sulcus with the anterior interventricular artery, also called the left anterior descending artery or LAD. It carries blood from that region around to the coronary sinus, which empties into the right atrium.","notes":["The middle cardiac vein runs with the posterior interventricular artery on the back of the heart. It would be the answer if the question named the posterior interventricular artery, the PDA.","The anterior cardiac veins drain the right ventricle straight into the right atrium, bypassing the coronary sinus. They would be the answer if the question asked which veins skip the coronary sinus.","The small cardiac vein runs with the right marginal artery along the lower right border of the heart. It would be the answer if the question named the right marginal artery.","Correct. The great cardiac vein travels with the LAD."],"lt":"t-pathway-integrated-tracing","lid":"W3-759","comp":63,"competencyId":"cv-coronary"},{"id":"L03953","dok":1,"q":"Which cardiac vein runs alongside the posterior interventricular artery, the PDA, and drains into the coronary sinus?","a":"The middle cardiac vein.","options":["Middle cardiac vein","Anterior cardiac veins","Small cardiac vein","Great cardiac vein"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The middle cardiac vein lies in the posterior interventricular sulcus, the groove on the back of the heart between the two ventricles. The posterior interventricular artery, also called the posterior descending artery or PDA, runs in the same groove. The middle cardiac vein carries blood from that region into the coronary sinus and on to the right atrium.","notes":["Correct. The middle cardiac vein travels with the PDA.","The anterior cardiac veins drain the right ventricle directly into the right atrium without joining the coronary sinus. They would be the answer if the question asked which veins bypass the coronary sinus.","The small cardiac vein runs with the right marginal artery along the lower right border. It would be the answer if the question named the right marginal artery.","The great cardiac vein runs with the anterior interventricular artery on the front of the heart. It would be the answer if the question named the LAD."],"lt":"t-pathway-integrated-tracing","lid":"W3-760","comp":63,"competencyId":"cv-coronary"},{"id":"L03954","dok":1,"q":"Which cardiac vein runs alongside the right marginal artery and drains into the coronary sinus?","a":"The small cardiac vein.","options":["Middle cardiac vein","Great cardiac vein","Small cardiac vein","Anterior cardiac veins"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The small cardiac vein runs along the lower right border of the heart with the right marginal artery, a branch of the right coronary artery. It carries blood from that area into the coronary sinus, which empties into the right atrium. Learning the arteries makes the veins easier, because each named vein shares a groove with an artery.","notes":["The middle cardiac vein runs with the posterior interventricular artery on the back of the heart. It would be the answer if the question named the PDA.","The great cardiac vein runs with the anterior interventricular artery on the front of the heart. It would be the answer if the question named the LAD.","Correct. The small cardiac vein travels with the right marginal artery.","The anterior cardiac veins drain the right ventricle straight into the right atrium, so they never join the coronary sinus. They would be the answer if the question asked which veins skip the coronary sinus."],"lt":"t-pathway-integrated-tracing","lid":"W3-761","comp":63,"competencyId":"cv-coronary"},{"id":"L03981","dok":1,"q":"Oxygen-poor blood from the head, neck, and upper limbs returns to the right atrium through which vessel?","a":"The superior vena cava.","options":["Superior vena cava","Coronary sinus","Pulmonary veins","Inferior vena cava"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The superior vena cava collects venous blood from the head, neck, and upper limbs and empties into the top of the right atrium. Together with the inferior vena cava from below, it returns the systemic circuit's oxygen-poor blood to the heart.","notes":["Correct. The superior vena cava drains the head, neck, and upper limbs.","The coronary sinus collects blood from the heart wall itself through the cardiac veins. It would be the answer if the question asked how blood from the myocardium returns to the right atrium.","The pulmonary veins carry oxygen-rich blood from the lungs to the left atrium, not oxygen-poor blood from the body. They would be the answer if the question asked how blood returns from the lungs.","The inferior vena cava drains the trunk and lower limbs and enters the right atrium from below. It would be the answer if the blood were coming from the legs or abdomen."],"lt":"t-pathway-integrated-tracing","lid":"W3-789","comp":62,"competencyId":"bvn-great-vessels"},{"id":"L03982","dok":1,"q":"Oxygen-poor blood from the trunk and lower limbs returns to the right atrium through which vessel?","a":"The inferior vena cava.","options":["Coronary sinus","Hepatic portal vein","Inferior vena cava","Superior vena cava"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The inferior vena cava is the large vein that collects blood from the trunk and lower limbs. It runs up through the abdomen beside the aorta and empties into the lower part of the right atrium. Along with the superior vena cava, it ends the systemic circuit.","notes":["The coronary sinus drains the heart wall through the cardiac veins. It would be the answer if the question asked where the cardiac veins empty.","The hepatic portal vein carries blood from the digestive organs to the liver, not to the heart. It would be the answer if the question asked how blood from the intestines reaches the liver.","Correct. The inferior vena cava drains the trunk and lower limbs.","The superior vena cava drains the head, neck, and upper limbs, entering the right atrium from above. It would be the answer if the blood were coming from the arms or head."],"lt":"t-pathway-integrated-tracing","lid":"W3-790","comp":62,"competencyId":"bvn-great-vessels"},{"id":"L03989","dok":1,"q":"The right and left coronary arteries, which supply the heart wall, branch from which part of the circulation?","a":"The base of the aorta.","options":["Pulmonary trunk","Coronary sinus","Aortic arch","Base of the aorta"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The two coronary arteries leave the base of the aorta, just beyond the aortic valve, so they receive oxygen-rich blood as soon as it leaves the left ventricle. They then run in the grooves on the heart's surface and branch over the myocardium.","notes":["The pulmonary trunk carries oxygen-poor blood from the right ventricle to the lungs, so it could not supply the heart wall with oxygen. It would be the answer if the question asked which vessel branches into the pulmonary arteries.","The coronary sinus is a vein that collects blood from the cardiac veins and empties it into the right atrium. It would be the answer if the question asked where the heart wall's venous blood drains.","The aortic arch is farther along the aorta and gives off the brachiocephalic trunk, left common carotid, and left subclavian arteries. It would be the answer if the question asked where the arteries to the head and upper limbs arise.","Correct. The coronary arteries arise from the base of the aorta."],"lt":"t-pathway-integrated-tracing","lid":"W3-797","comp":63,"competencyId":"cv-coronary"},{"id":"L03990","dok":1,"q":"The great, middle, and small cardiac veins all empty into which structure before their blood reaches the right atrium?","a":"The coronary sinus.","options":["Inferior vena cava","Coronary sinus","Superior vena cava","Pulmonary veins"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The coronary sinus is a large vein on the posterior surface of the heart. The great, middle, and small cardiac veins drain into it, and it empties into the right atrium. Only the small anterior cardiac veins skip it and drain the right ventricle straight into the right atrium.","notes":["The inferior vena cava drains the trunk and lower limbs into the right atrium. It would be the answer if the question asked how blood from the legs returns to the heart.","Correct. The named cardiac veins drain into the coronary sinus.","The superior vena cava drains the head, neck, and upper limbs into the right atrium. It would be the answer if the question asked how blood from the arms returns to the heart.","The pulmonary veins carry oxygen-rich blood from the lungs to the left atrium. They would be the answer if the question asked how blood returns from the lungs."],"lt":"t-pathway-integrated-tracing","lid":"W3-798","comp":63,"competencyId":"cv-coronary"},{"id":"L04013","dok":1,"q":"Which great vessel returns oxygen-poor blood from the head, neck, and upper limbs to the right atrium?","a":"Superior vena cava","options":["Coronary sinus","Pulmonary trunk","Superior vena cava","Inferior vena cava"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The superior vena cava is the large vein that collects oxygen-poor blood from the upper body, including the head, neck, and upper limbs. It enters the top of the right atrium. Its partner, the inferior vena cava, brings blood from below and enters the bottom of the same chamber.","notes":["The coronary sinus is the large vein on the back of the heart that collects blood from the heart wall itself. Choose it if the question asked which vessel returns blood from the cardiac veins to the right atrium.","The pulmonary trunk is an artery that carries blood away from the right ventricle toward the lungs. It would be the answer if the question asked which vessel leaves the right ventricle.","Correct. The superior vena cava drains the head, neck, and upper limbs into the right atrium.","The inferior vena cava returns oxygen-poor blood from the trunk and lower limbs to the right atrium. Pick it if the question asked which vessel drains the lower body."],"lt":"t-imagefree-structure-recognition","lid":"W3-821","comp":62,"competencyId":"bvn-great-vessels"},{"id":"L04014","dok":1,"q":"Which great vessel returns oxygen-poor blood from the trunk and lower limbs to the right atrium?","a":"Inferior vena cava","options":["Superior vena cava","Aorta","Coronary sinus","Inferior vena cava"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The inferior vena cava is the largest vein in the body. It runs up the back of the abdomen beside the aorta, passes through the diaphragm, and empties into the lower part of the right atrium. It carries oxygen-poor blood from the abdomen, pelvis, and lower limbs.","notes":["The superior vena cava returns blood from the head, neck, and upper limbs to the top of the right atrium. Pick it if the question asked which vessel drains the upper body.","The aorta is the main artery leaving the left ventricle, carrying oxygen-rich blood away from the heart. Choose it if the question asked which vessel sends blood out to the body, not back to the heart.","The coronary sinus collects blood from the heart wall through the cardiac veins and empties into the right atrium. It would be the answer if the question asked how blood from the myocardium returns to the heart chambers.","Correct. The inferior vena cava drains the trunk and lower limbs."],"lt":"t-imagefree-structure-recognition","lid":"W3-822","comp":62,"competencyId":"bvn-great-vessels"},{"id":"L04015","dok":1,"q":"Which vessel is the largest artery in the body, carrying oxygen-rich blood away from the left ventricle?","a":"Aorta","options":["Aorta","Pulmonary trunk","Brachiocephalic trunk","Common carotid artery"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The aorta leaves the left ventricle through the aortic valve, arches over the heart, and runs down through the thorax and abdomen. Every systemic artery in the body branches off it directly or indirectly. It is an elastic artery, so it stretches with each heartbeat and recoils between beats to keep blood moving.","notes":["Correct. The aorta is the largest artery in the body.","The pulmonary trunk is also a large elastic artery, but it leaves the right ventricle and carries oxygen-poor blood toward the lungs. Pick it if the question asked which artery begins the pulmonary circuit.","The brachiocephalic trunk is the first branch of the aortic arch, supplying the right side of the head and neck and the right upper limb. It would be the answer if the question asked for the first vessel to branch off the aortic arch.","The common carotid arteries are branches that carry blood up the neck to the head. Choose one if the question asked which artery supplies the head and neck."],"lt":"t-imagefree-structure-recognition","lid":"W3-823","comp":62,"competencyId":"bvn-great-vessels"},{"id":"L04016","dok":1,"q":"Which great vessel carries blood out of the right ventricle?","a":"Pulmonary trunk","options":["Pulmonary trunk","Superior vena cava","Aorta","Pulmonary veins"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The pulmonary trunk leaves the right ventricle through the pulmonary valve and splits into the right and left pulmonary arteries, one to each lung. It is an artery because it carries blood away from the heart, even though that blood is oxygen-poor. This is a reminder that arteries and veins are named by direction of flow, not by how much oxygen they carry.","notes":["Correct. The pulmonary trunk carries blood out of the right ventricle toward the lungs.","The superior vena cava brings oxygen-poor blood from the upper body into the right atrium. Choose it if the question asked which vessel returns blood from the head and upper limbs.","The aorta carries oxygen-rich blood out of the left ventricle to the body. Pick it if the question asked which vessel leaves the left ventricle.","The pulmonary veins carry oxygen-rich blood from the lungs into the left atrium, so they bring blood toward the heart. They would be the answer if the question asked which vessels return blood from the lungs."],"lt":"t-imagefree-structure-recognition","lid":"W3-824","comp":62,"competencyId":"bvn-great-vessels"},{"id":"L04017","dok":1,"q":"Which vessels return oxygen-rich blood from the lungs to the left atrium?","a":"Pulmonary veins","options":["Cardiac veins","Venae cavae","Pulmonary veins","Pulmonary arteries"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The four pulmonary veins, usually two from each lung, carry blood that has just picked up oxygen in the lungs back to the left atrium. They are veins because they carry blood toward the heart, even though their blood is oxygen-rich. Together with the pulmonary arteries they form the pulmonary circuit.","notes":["The cardiac veins drain blood from the heart wall and mostly empty into the coronary sinus. Choose them if the question asked which veins drain the myocardium.","The venae cavae return oxygen-poor blood from the body to the right atrium. They would be the answer if the question asked which vessels empty into the right atrium.","Correct. The pulmonary veins return oxygen-rich blood to the left atrium.","The pulmonary arteries branch from the pulmonary trunk and carry oxygen-poor blood away from the heart to the lungs. Pick them if the question asked which vessels take blood into the lungs."],"lt":"t-imagefree-structure-recognition","lid":"W3-825","comp":62,"competencyId":"bvn-great-vessels"},{"id":"L04018","dok":1,"q":"Which large vein on the posterior surface of the heart collects the cardiac veins and empties into the right atrium?","a":"Coronary sinus","options":["Coronary sinus","Anterior cardiac veins","Great cardiac vein","Inferior vena cava"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The coronary sinus is a wide vein lying in the groove between the atria and ventricles on the back of the heart. The great, middle, and small cardiac veins drain into it, and it empties into the right atrium. The coronary flow runs aorta, coronary arteries, myocardial capillaries, cardiac veins, coronary sinus, right atrium.","notes":["Correct. The coronary sinus collects the cardiac veins and empties into the right atrium.","The anterior cardiac veins drain the right ventricle directly into the right atrium, bypassing the coronary sinus. Choose them if the question asked which cardiac veins skip the coronary sinus.","The great cardiac vein runs with the anterior interventricular artery (LAD) and drains into the coronary sinus rather than straight into a chamber. Pick it if the question asked which cardiac vein travels beside the LAD.","The inferior vena cava returns blood from the trunk and lower limbs to the right atrium, not blood from the heart wall. It would be the answer if the question asked which vessel drains the lower body."],"lt":"t-imagefree-structure-recognition","lid":"W3-826","comp":63,"competencyId":"cv-coronary"},{"id":"L04091","dok":1,"q":"A coronary artery is blocked, and the patch of heart muscle it supplies dies. What is this event called?","a":"Myocardial infarction.","options":["Stroke","Cardiac tamponade","Myocardial infarction","Aneurysm"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The coronary arteries branch from the base of the aorta and are the heart wall's own blood supply. When one is blocked, the myocardium beyond the blockage is starved of oxygen and its muscle cells begin to die. Death of tissue from lost blood supply is called an infarction, so death of heart muscle is a myocardial infarction, the medical term for a heart attack.","notes":["A stroke is loss of blood supply to part of the brain from a blocked or burst artery. It would be the answer if the blocked vessel supplied brain tissue rather than the heart wall.","Cardiac tamponade is fluid building up in the pericardial cavity and squeezing the heart from outside. Pick it when fluid around the heart limits how much the heart can fill, not when an artery is blocked.","Correct. A myocardial infarction is the death of heart muscle after its coronary blood supply is cut off.","An aneurysm is a balloon-like bulge in a weakened artery wall, most often in the aorta or the arteries of the brain. Choose it when a wall is stretching outward and might rupture, not when a lumen is blocked."],"lt":"t-clinical-application-and-misconceptions","lid":"W3-902","comp":63,"competencyId":"cv-coronary"},{"id":"L04104","dok":1,"q":"Plaque gradually narrows the arteries that supply the heart wall. Which disorder fits?","a":"Coronary artery disease.","options":["Cardiac tamponade","Coronary artery disease","Heart block","Peripheral artery disease"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The right and left coronary arteries and their branches leave the base of the aorta and supply the myocardium. Coronary artery disease is narrowing of these vessels, usually by atherosclerotic plaque. A narrowed coronary artery can starve the heart muscle of oxygen, and if it becomes fully blocked, the result is a myocardial infarction.","notes":["Cardiac tamponade is fluid filling the pericardial cavity and squeezing the heart from outside. It would be the answer for a problem with heart filling, not narrowed vessels.","Correct. Coronary artery disease is narrowing of the coronary arteries that feed the heart wall.","Heart block is a conduction disorder at the AV node that makes the atria and ventricles beat out of step. It is an electrical problem, so choose it for a rhythm question rather than narrowed arteries.","Peripheral artery disease is the same plaque narrowing in the arteries of the limbs, usually the legs. Pick it when a question describes leg pain on walking."],"lt":"t-clinical-application-and-misconceptions","lid":"W3-918","comp":70,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-disorders"},{"id":"L04125","dok":2,"q":"A student says that veins always carry oxygen-poor blood. Which fact shows this statement is wrong?","a":"The pulmonary veins carry oxygen-rich blood to the left atrium.","options":["The pulmonary arteries carry oxygen-rich blood to the lungs","The pulmonary veins carry oxygen-rich blood to the left atrium","The coronary sinus carries oxygen-rich blood to the left atrium","The venae cavae carry oxygen-rich blood to the right atrium"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Vessels are named by the direction they carry blood, not by how much oxygen it holds: arteries carry blood away from the heart and veins carry it toward the heart. The four pulmonary veins return blood from the lungs, where it has just picked up oxygen, to the left atrium. They are veins carrying oxygen-rich blood, which disproves the rule.","notes":["The pulmonary arteries carry oxygen-poor blood from the right ventricle to the lungs, not oxygen-rich blood. They are the exception to the matching rule that arteries carry oxygen-rich blood.","Correct. The pulmonary veins are veins that carry oxygen-rich blood.","The coronary sinus carries oxygen-poor blood from the heart wall and empties into the right atrium, not the left. Choose it for a question about how the myocardium drains.","The superior and inferior venae cavae carry oxygen-poor blood from the body to the right atrium. They fit the student's rule, so pick them for a question about where systemic blood returns to the heart."],"lt":"t-clinical-application-and-misconceptions","lid":"W3-939","comp":67,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-types"},{"id":"L04126","dok":2,"q":"A student says that arteries always carry oxygen-rich blood. Which vessels show this statement is wrong?","a":"The pulmonary arteries.","options":["The pulmonary veins","The renal arteries","The pulmonary arteries","The coronary arteries"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"An artery is any vessel that carries blood away from the heart, whatever its oxygen content. The right ventricle pumps oxygen-poor blood returned from the body into the pulmonary trunk, which splits into the right and left pulmonary arteries headed to the lungs. They are arteries carrying oxygen-poor blood, so they disprove the student's rule.","notes":["The pulmonary veins are the exception on the other side, veins carrying oxygen-rich blood from the lungs to the left atrium. They would be the answer if the student had claimed that veins always carry oxygen-poor blood.","The renal arteries branch from the abdominal aorta and carry oxygen-rich blood to the kidneys. They follow the usual pattern, so choose them for a question about the kidney's blood supply.","Correct. The pulmonary arteries carry oxygen-poor blood from the right ventricle to the lungs.","The coronary arteries branch from the base of the aorta and carry oxygen-rich blood to the myocardium. They follow the usual pattern, so pick them for a question about the heart wall's own blood supply."],"lt":"t-clinical-application-and-misconceptions","lid":"W3-940","comp":67,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-types"},{"id":"L04142","dok":3,"q":"A blockage in the right coronary artery causes a rhythm problem as well as damaged muscle. What anatomy explains the rhythm problem?","a":"It usually supplies the SA and AV nodes.","options":["It usually supplies the left atrium","It usually supplies the apex of the heart","It usually supplies the SA and AV nodes","It usually supplies the anterior septum"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The SA node and AV node are part of the conduction system, the specialized cells that start and relay each heartbeat. In most people both nodes get their blood from the right coronary artery. When the RCA is blocked, the nodes can be starved of oxygen along with the muscle, so the beat may slow, become irregular, or be blocked at the AV node.","notes":["The left atrium is supplied by the left coronary artery, mainly through its circumflex branch. Choose it for a question about the regions the left coronary artery feeds.","The apex is supplied by the left coronary artery, mainly through the anterior interventricular artery (the LAD). Pick the apex for a question about what a LAD blockage damages.","Correct. The RCA supplies the SA and AV nodes in most people.","The anterior part of the interventricular septum is supplied by the LAD, a branch of the left coronary artery. The RCA's posterior interventricular branch supplies the posterior septum instead."],"lt":"t-clinical-application-and-misconceptions","lid":"W3-957","comp":63,"competencyId":"cv-coronary"},{"id":"L04143","dok":2,"q":"Why can a blockage of the anterior interventricular artery (the LAD) be especially dangerous?","a":"It supplies much of the left ventricle and apex.","options":["It supplies the right atrium and right ventricle","It supplies much of the left ventricle and apex","It drains the heart wall into the coronary sinus","It is the only vessel supplying the SA node"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The anterior interventricular artery, also called the left anterior descending artery (LAD), is a branch of the left coronary artery that runs in the groove between the ventricles on the front of the heart. It supplies the anterior wall of the left ventricle, the anterior part of the interventricular septum, and the apex. The left ventricle pumps blood to the whole body, so losing a large area of its muscle seriously weakens the heart's pumping.","notes":["The right atrium and most of the right ventricle are supplied by the right coronary artery. Pick it for a question about what an RCA blockage damages.","Correct. The LAD feeds a large part of the left ventricle, the main pumping chamber.","Draining the heart wall is the job of the cardiac veins and the coronary sinus. The great cardiac vein runs beside the LAD, but the LAD itself is an artery that delivers blood rather than draining it.","The SA node is usually supplied by the right coronary artery, not the LAD. Choose the RCA for a question about why a blockage causes a rhythm problem."],"lt":"t-clinical-application-and-misconceptions","lid":"W3-958","comp":63,"competencyId":"cv-coronary"},{"id":"L04201","dok":1,"q":"Where do the coronary arteries arise?","a":"From the base of the aorta.","options":["The pulmonary trunk","The coronary sinus","The base of the aorta","The aortic arch"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The right and left coronary arteries branch off the base of the aorta, just above the aortic valve, so they are the first branches the aorta gives off. From there they run in the grooves on the heart's surface and branch over the myocardium. This gives the heart wall first access to the oxygen-rich blood leaving the left ventricle.","notes":["The pulmonary trunk carries oxygen-poor blood from the right ventricle to the lungs and has no coronary branches. Choose it for a question about which vessel splits into the right and left pulmonary arteries.","The coronary sinus is a vein on the back of the heart that collects blood from the cardiac veins and drains into the right atrium. It would be the answer for where coronary blood ends up, not where the arteries begin.","Correct. The coronary arteries are the first branches of the aorta, just above the aortic valve.","The aortic arch gives off the brachiocephalic trunk, the left common carotid artery, and the left subclavian artery, which supply the head, neck, and upper limbs. Pick it if the question asked where those three branches arise."],"lt":"t-cv-coronary","lid":"cv-coronary-r1","comp":63,"competencyId":"cv-coronary"},{"id":"L04202","dok":1,"q":"Which two arteries come directly off the aorta to supply the heart wall?","a":"The right coronary artery and the left coronary artery.","options":["The circumflex and marginal arteries","The anterior and posterior interventricular arteries","The right and left pulmonary arteries","The right and left coronary arteries"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The two coronary arteries, right and left, leave the base of the aorta and supply the myocardium. The right coronary artery serves the right atrium, most of the right ventricle, and usually the SA and AV nodes, while the left coronary artery serves the left atrium, the left ventricle, and the anterior septum. Every other named coronary artery is a branch of one of these two.","notes":["The circumflex and left marginal arteries are branches of the left coronary artery, and the right marginal artery comes off the right. Choose them for a question about branches that run along the sides of the heart.","These are branches, not the two main coronary arteries. The anterior interventricular artery is a branch of the left coronary artery and the posterior interventricular artery usually comes off the right; pick them if the question asked which arteries run in the interventricular grooves.","The right and left pulmonary arteries branch from the pulmonary trunk and carry oxygen-poor blood to the lungs. They would be the answer if the question asked how blood reaches each lung.","Correct. The right and left coronary arteries are the two main vessels, and the rest are their branches."],"lt":"t-cv-coronary","lid":"cv-coronary-r2","comp":63,"competencyId":"cv-coronary"},{"id":"L04203","dok":1,"q":"What does the coronary sinus do?","a":"Collects blood from the cardiac veins and empties it into the right atrium","options":["Drains the right ventricle wall directly into the right atrium","Carries oxygen-rich blood from the aorta into the myocardium","Collects blood from the cardiac veins and empties it into the right atrium","Collects blood from the cardiac veins and empties it into the left atrium"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The coronary sinus is a large vein in the groove on the posterior surface of the heart. The great, middle, and small cardiac veins drain into it, and it empties that oxygen-poor blood into the right atrium. Along with the two venae cavae, it is one of the three large veins that empty into the right atrium; only the small anterior cardiac veins enter the right atrium on their own.","notes":["That describes the anterior cardiac veins, which bypass the coronary sinus and empty straight into the right atrium. Pick it if the question asked which cardiac veins skip the coronary sinus.","That is the job of the coronary arteries, which leave the base of the aorta and branch over the myocardium. Choose this for a question about how the heart wall gets its oxygen supply.","Correct. The coronary sinus is the collecting vein of the heart wall, and it opens into the right atrium.","The coronary sinus does collect the cardiac veins, but it carries oxygen-poor blood, which returns to the right side of the heart. Students pick this when they confuse it with the pulmonary veins, which are the vessels that empty into the left atrium."],"lt":"t-cv-coronary","lid":"cv-coronary-r3","comp":63,"competencyId":"cv-coronary"},{"id":"L04204","dok":2,"q":"Which sequence traces blood through the coronary circulation, from the aorta back into the heart?","a":"Aorta, coronary arteries, capillaries of the myocardium, cardiac veins, coronary sinus, right atrium.","options":["Aorta, coronary arteries, myocardial capillaries, cardiac veins, coronary sinus, right atrium","Pulmonary trunk, coronary arteries, myocardial capillaries, cardiac veins, coronary sinus, right atrium","Aorta, coronary arteries, myocardial capillaries, cardiac veins, coronary sinus, left atrium","Aorta, cardiac veins, myocardial capillaries, coronary arteries, coronary sinus, right atrium"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The heart wall is supplied like any other organ: arteries, then capillaries, then veins. Oxygen-rich blood leaves the base of the aorta in the coronary arteries, delivers oxygen in the capillaries of the myocardium, and drains into the cardiac veins. The cardiac veins empty into the coronary sinus, which returns the oxygen-poor blood to the right atrium.","notes":["Correct. Arteries, capillaries, veins, then the coronary sinus into the right atrium.","The pulmonary trunk carries oxygen-poor blood to the lungs and gives off no coronary arteries. The coronary arteries branch from the aorta, which carries the oxygen-rich blood the heart muscle needs.","Everything is in order until the last step. Oxygen-poor blood from the heart wall returns to the right atrium, just like blood from the rest of the body; the left atrium receives only the pulmonary veins.","This puts the veins before the arteries. Blood always passes from arteries to capillaries to veins, so the cardiac veins come after the capillaries, not before them."],"lt":"t-cv-coronary","lid":"cv-coronary-r4","comp":63,"competencyId":"cv-coronary"},{"id":"L04205","dok":1,"q":"A clot blocks a branch of the left coronary artery. The patch of myocardium beyond the block is starved of oxygen and begins to die. What is this event called?","a":"A myocardial infarction (heart attack).","options":["Cardiac tamponade","Heart block","Myocardial infarction","Aneurysm"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"A myocardial infarction, commonly called a heart attack, is death of heart muscle caused by a blocked coronary artery. The coronary arteries are the heart wall's own supply line, and they are narrow, so a block cuts off oxygen to every cell downstream of it. Infarction means tissue death from loss of blood supply, and myocardial names the heart muscle layer that is affected.","notes":["Cardiac tamponade is fluid building up in the pericardial cavity and squeezing the heart from outside. Pick it if the scenario described fluid around the heart limiting how much it can fill.","Heart block is a conduction disorder in which the electrical signal is delayed or stopped at the AV node. It would be the answer if the scenario described the atria and ventricles beating out of step.","Correct. A blocked coronary artery leads to death of the heart muscle it supplies.","An aneurysm is a balloon-like bulge in a weakened artery wall that can rupture. Choose it for a scenario about a bulging aorta or brain artery, not a blocked coronary artery."],"lt":"t-cv-coronary","lid":"cv-coronary-r5","comp":63,"competencyId":"cv-coronary"},{"id":"L04218","dok":1,"q":"Which vessel is the largest artery in the body, carrying oxygen-rich blood out of the left ventricle?","a":"The aorta.","options":["Inferior vena cava","Aorta","Common carotid artery","Pulmonary trunk"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The aorta is the largest artery in the body. It leaves the left ventricle through the aortic valve, arches over the heart, and descends through the thorax and abdomen, giving off branches that carry oxygen-rich blood to every part of the body. Its first branches are the coronary arteries, which supply the heart wall itself.","notes":["The inferior vena cava is a vein, not an artery, and it returns oxygen-poor blood from the trunk and lower limbs to the right atrium. Choose it for a question about the large vein draining the lower body.","Correct. The aorta carries oxygen-rich blood from the left ventricle to the whole body.","The common carotid arteries carry blood up the neck to the head and are much smaller than the aorta. They would be the answer for a question about the main arteries supplying the head and neck.","The pulmonary trunk is also a large artery leaving the heart, but it leaves the right ventricle and carries oxygen-poor blood to the lungs. Pick it if the question asked which vessel the right ventricle pumps into."],"lt":"t-bvn-great-vessels","lid":"bvn-great-vessels-r1","comp":62,"competencyId":"bvn-great-vessels"},{"id":"L04219","dok":1,"q":"What does the pulmonary trunk carry, and where does it take it?","a":"Oxygen-poor blood from the right ventricle toward the lungs, through the right and left pulmonary arteries.","options":["Oxygen-poor blood from the body to the right atrium","Oxygen-poor blood from the right ventricle toward the lungs","Oxygen-rich blood from the lungs to the left atrium","Oxygen-rich blood from the left ventricle to the body"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The pulmonary trunk leaves the right ventricle through the pulmonary valve and carries oxygen-poor blood toward the lungs. It soon splits into the right and left pulmonary arteries, one to each lung, where the blood picks up oxygen and gives off carbon dioxide. Because it carries blood away from the heart, it counts as an arterial vessel even though its blood is low in oxygen.","notes":["That describes the superior and inferior venae cavae, which return oxygen-poor blood to the right atrium. They would be the answer for the vessels that empty into the right atrium.","Correct. The pulmonary trunk takes oxygen-poor blood from the right ventricle toward the lungs.","That describes the pulmonary veins, which return oxygen-rich blood from the lungs to the left atrium. Pick it if the question asked what the left atrium receives.","That describes the aorta, which leaves the left ventricle and supplies the whole body. Choose it for the vessel the left ventricle pumps into."],"lt":"t-bvn-great-vessels","lid":"bvn-great-vessels-r2","comp":62,"competencyId":"bvn-great-vessels"},{"id":"L04220","dok":1,"q":"How many pulmonary veins return blood to the heart, and which chamber do they enter?","a":"Four; they return oxygen-rich blood from the lungs to the left atrium.","options":["Four, entering the right atrium","Two, entering the left ventricle","Four, entering the left atrium","Two, entering the left atrium"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"There are four pulmonary veins, two from each lung, and all four empty into the left atrium. They carry oxygen-rich blood that has just passed through the lungs. From the left atrium, that blood crosses the mitral valve into the left ventricle to be pumped out to the body.","notes":["Four is right, but the right atrium receives oxygen-poor blood from the venae cavae and coronary sinus. The pulmonary veins carry oxygen-rich blood, so they return to the left side of the heart.","Returning blood always enters an atrium before a ventricle, so no vein empties straight into the left ventricle. Students pick this when they skip the left atrium in the pathway.","Correct. Four pulmonary veins, two from each lung, all entering the left atrium.","The left atrium is right, but there are two veins from each lung, four in all. Students choose two when they count one vein per lung."],"lt":"t-bvn-great-vessels","lid":"bvn-great-vessels-r3","comp":62,"competencyId":"bvn-great-vessels"},{"id":"L04221","dok":2,"q":"The pulmonary arteries carry oxygen-poor blood and the pulmonary veins carry oxygen-rich blood. Why are they still called arteries and veins?","a":"Vessels are named by the direction of flow relative to the heart, not by oxygen content.","options":["Vessels are named by which ventricle pumps blood into them","Vessels are named by wall thickness, and these have unusually thin walls","Vessels are named by the direction they carry blood relative to the heart","Vessels are named by the oxygen content of the blood they carry"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Arteries are defined as vessels that carry blood away from the heart, and veins as vessels that carry blood toward it. The pulmonary arteries carry blood away from the right ventricle to the lungs, so they are arteries even though that blood is low in oxygen. The pulmonary veins carry blood back to the left atrium, so they are veins even though that blood has just picked up oxygen in the lungs.","notes":["Both ventricles pump into arteries, the right into the pulmonary trunk and the left into the aorta, and no ventricle pumps into a vein. Students choose this when they try to link each vessel name to one side of the heart.","Arteries do usually have thicker walls than veins, but wall thickness is a feature of each vessel type, not the basis for its name. Pick wall thickness for a question about telling an artery from a vein on a slide.","Correct. Direction of flow, away from or toward the heart, is what defines an artery or a vein.","This is the rule students often learn first, that arteries carry oxygen-rich blood and veins carry oxygen-poor blood. It works for the systemic circuit, but it is not how vessels are named, which is why the pulmonary vessels seem to break it."],"lt":"t-bvn-great-vessels","lid":"bvn-great-vessels-r4","comp":67,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-types"},{"id":"L04222","dok":2,"q":"A drop of blood leaves the right ventricle and travels to the left atrium. Which sequence of vessels does it pass through?","a":"Pulmonary trunk, pulmonary arteries, the lung capillaries, pulmonary veins, then the left atrium.","options":["Pulmonary veins, lung capillaries, pulmonary arteries, pulmonary trunk","Pulmonary trunk, pulmonary veins, lung capillaries, pulmonary arteries","Aorta, pulmonary arteries, lung capillaries, pulmonary veins","Pulmonary trunk, pulmonary arteries, lung capillaries, pulmonary veins"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The right ventricle pumps blood through the pulmonary valve into the pulmonary trunk, which splits into the right and left pulmonary arteries. Those arteries branch down to the capillaries around the alveoli, where the blood picks up oxygen. The blood then collects into the pulmonary veins, which carry it back to the left atrium, completing the pulmonary circuit.","notes":["These are the right vessels traced in reverse, from the left atrium back to the right ventricle. Blood leaves the heart in arteries and returns in veins, so the pulmonary trunk comes first.","This puts the veins before the capillaries. Blood moves from arteries to capillaries to veins, so the pulmonary arteries come before the lung capillaries and the pulmonary veins come after them.","The aorta leaves the left ventricle and carries blood to the body, not the lungs. Pick the aorta as the starting vessel only if the question traced blood from the left ventricle.","Correct. Trunk, arteries, capillaries, then veins back to the left atrium."],"lt":"t-bvn-great-vessels","lid":"bvn-great-vessels-r5","comp":69,"competencyId":"bvn-circulatory-routes"}]},{"id":"m3-conduction","title":"Cardiac muscle and the conduction system","summary":"cardiac muscle cells, intercalated discs, SA node, AV node, AV bundle, bundle branches, Purkinje fibers, cardiac nerve supply","mw":"2026-10-05","tr":"2026-10-01","cards":[{"id":"L00941","dok":1,"q":"Which two cell junctions are found in the intercalated discs that join cardiac muscle cells?","a":"Desmosomes and gap junctions.","options":["Desmosomes and hemidesmosomes","Tight junctions and gap junctions","Hemidesmosomes and tight junctions","Desmosomes and gap junctions"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Intercalated discs are the dark bands where neighboring cardiac muscle cells meet end to end. Desmosomes hold the cells together so they do not pull apart when the heart contracts, and gap junctions are small channels that let the electrical signal pass directly from one cell to the next. Working together, these two junctions let the whole heart wall contract as one coordinated unit.","notes":["Desmosomes are correct, but hemidesmosomes anchor a cell to its basement membrane rather than to a neighboring cell. Pick hemidesmosomes for a question about what keeps the epidermis attached to the basement membrane.","Gap junctions are present in intercalated discs, but tight junctions are not. Tight junctions seal neighboring epithelial cells together so material cannot leak between them, so pick them for a question about the leak-proof lining of the intestine or bladder.","Neither of these junctions joins cardiac cells end to end. Hemidesmosomes anchor a cell to the basement membrane beneath it, and tight junctions seal epithelial cells together, so this pair fits questions about epithelium.","Correct. Desmosomes provide adhesion and gap junctions provide electrical coupling in the intercalated discs."],"lt":"t-muscle-types","lid":"c5","comp":60,"competencyId":"cv-cardiac-muscle"},{"id":"L00942","dok":1,"q":"Which structural feature is unique to cardiac muscle and joins its cells end to end?","a":"Intercalated discs.","options":["Intercalated discs","Terminal cisterns","Striations","Fascicles"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Intercalated discs are the junctions where one cardiac muscle cell meets the next, and they show up on a slide as dark lines crossing the fibers. They contain desmosomes that hold the cells together and gap junctions that pass the electrical signal from cell to cell. Because no other muscle tissue has them, intercalated discs are the quickest way to identify cardiac muscle.","notes":["Correct. Intercalated discs join cardiac cells end to end and are found only in cardiac muscle.","Terminal cisterns are the enlarged end sacs of the sarcoplasmic reticulum inside a skeletal muscle fiber, sitting on either side of a T tubule. Pick them for a question about the parts of a triad.","Striations are the light and dark bands made by lined-up contractile proteins, and both skeletal and cardiac muscle have them, so they are not unique to cardiac muscle. Pick striations for a question about what skeletal and cardiac muscle share.","Fascicles are bundles of skeletal muscle fibers wrapped in perimysium, and they are a feature of a whole skeletal muscle, not a junction between cells. Pick them for a question about what the perimysium surrounds."],"lt":"t-muscle-types","lid":"c6","comp":60,"competencyId":"cv-cardiac-muscle"},{"id":"L01266","dok":2,"q":"Cardiac muscle cells are joined by intercalated discs that contain gap junctions. Why are the gap junctions essential for the heart to work as a pump?","a":"They let the electrical signal pass directly from cell to cell, so the heart muscle contracts as a coordinated unit.","options":["They isolate each cell so it beats on its own rhythm","They pass the signal so the cells contract together","They store blood between cells for later ejection","They hold the cells together so they do not pull apart"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Intercalated discs join cardiac muscle cells end to end. They contain two kinds of junctions: desmosomes, which hold the cells together against the force of contraction, and gap junctions, which let the electrical signal pass directly from one cell to the next. This coupling lets the whole heart muscle contract together as a single pump.","notes":["Isolated cells would beat out of step and the heart could not pump. This is the opposite of what gap junctions do.","Correct. Gap junctions electrically couple the cardiac cells.","Blood is held in the heart's chambers, not between muscle cells. Intercalated discs are cell junctions, not storage spaces.","This is the job of the desmosomes in the intercalated discs. Pick desmosomes for the mechanical adhesion between cardiac cells."],"lt":"t-muscle-structure","lid":"d3-t-muscle-structure-6","comp":60,"competencyId":"cv-cardiac-muscle"},{"id":"L01506","dok":1,"q":"What type of muscle tissue forms the myocardium?","a":"Cardiac muscle.","options":["Smooth muscle","Cardiac muscle","Dense regular connective tissue","Skeletal muscle"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The myocardium is built of cardiac muscle, an involuntary, striated muscle tissue found nowhere else in the body. Its cells are short and branched and joined end to end by intercalated discs. That connected structure lets the whole heart contract as one coordinated unit.","notes":["Smooth muscle is involuntary but lacks striations, and it lines hollow organs such as the stomach and blood vessels. Pick it for the muscle in the wall of an artery or the gut.","Correct. Cardiac muscle forms the myocardium.","Dense regular connective tissue forms tendons and ligaments, with parallel collagen fibers. It is not muscle, so pick it for a question about tendons.","Skeletal muscle is striated but voluntary and attaches to bones. Pick it for a muscle you can move on command, such as the biceps."],"lt":"t-heart","lid":"c11","comp":60,"competencyId":"cv-cardiac-muscle"},{"id":"L01507","dok":1,"q":"What are the specialized junctions where neighboring cardiomyocytes meet end to end called?","a":"Intercalated discs.","options":["T tubules","Sarcomeres","Chordae tendineae","Intercalated discs"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Intercalated discs are the specialized junctions where cardiac muscle cells join end to end. Under the microscope they look like dark lines crossing the fibers, and they are one of the easiest ways to identify cardiac muscle. They hold the cells together and let the contraction signal pass from one cell to the next.","notes":["T tubules are inward folds of the muscle cell membrane that carry the signal deep into the cell. They are inside a single cell, not junctions between cells.","A sarcomere is the repeating contractile unit inside a muscle cell, and rows of sarcomeres create the striations. Pick it for a question about the basic unit of contraction.","Chordae tendineae are fibrous cords that tie valve cusps to papillary muscles. Pick them for a question about what holds the atrioventricular valves in place.","Correct. Intercalated discs join cardiomyocytes end to end."],"lt":"t-heart","lid":"c12","comp":60,"competencyId":"cv-cardiac-muscle"},{"id":"L01522","dok":2,"q":"Within the intercalated discs, which junctions let the contraction signal pass directly from one cardiomyocyte to the next?","a":"Gap junctions.","options":["Gap junctions","Tight junctions","Desmosomes","Sarcomeres"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Gap junctions are tiny protein channels that connect the cytoplasm of neighboring cells. In the intercalated discs they let the electrical signal for contraction pass straight from one cardiomyocyte to the next. This is why the heart muscle contracts as one coordinated unit rather than cell by cell.","notes":["Correct. Gap junctions are channels that pass the contraction signal from cell to cell.","Tight junctions seal neighboring cells together to block leakage between them, as in the lining of the intestine. They are not part of the intercalated disc, so pick them for a question about epithelial seals.","Desmosomes are the other junction in the intercalated discs, but they anchor cells together rather than pass signals. Pick desmosomes if the question asked what keeps the cells from pulling apart.","A sarcomere is the contractile unit inside a muscle cell, made of thick and thin filaments. It is not a junction between cells, so pick it for a question about the unit of contraction."],"lt":"t-heart","lid":"c27","comp":60,"competencyId":"cv-cardiac-muscle"},{"id":"L01523","dok":2,"q":"Within the intercalated discs, which junctions anchor cardiomyocytes so they do not pull apart during contraction?","a":"Desmosomes.","options":["Desmosomes","Gap junctions","Tight junctions","Mitochondria"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Desmosomes are anchoring junctions that rivet neighboring cells together with protein fibers. Cardiac muscle pulls hard with every heartbeat, so the desmosomes in the intercalated discs keep the cells from tearing apart under that strain. Desmosomes are also common in other tissues that are stretched, such as the epidermis.","notes":["Correct. Desmosomes anchor cardiomyocytes so they do not pull apart.","Gap junctions are the channels in the intercalated discs that pass the contraction signal between cells. Pick them if the question asked how the signal spreads from cell to cell.","Tight junctions seal cells together to block leakage, as in the lining of the gut. They are not part of the intercalated disc, so pick them for a question about leak-proof epithelial seals.","Mitochondria are organelles that make ATP, and cardiac muscle is packed with them. They power contraction but do not hold cells together, so pick them for a question about the cell's energy supply."],"lt":"t-heart","lid":"c28","comp":60,"competencyId":"cv-cardiac-muscle"},{"id":"L01535","dok":2,"q":"Under the microscope, a tissue shows short, branching, striated cells, each with one central nucleus, joined end to end by dark crossing lines. Which tissue is it?","a":"Cardiac muscle.","options":["Dense regular connective tissue","Cardiac muscle","Smooth muscle","Skeletal muscle"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Cardiac muscle cells are short and branched, with striations and usually one central nucleus. The dark lines crossing the fibers are intercalated discs, the junctions where cells meet end to end, and they are found only in cardiac muscle. Branching plus intercalated discs is the quickest way to identify this tissue on a slide.","notes":["Dense regular connective tissue has wavy parallel collagen fibers with flattened fibroblast nuclei between them. Pick it for a slide of a tendon or ligament.","Correct. Branching striated cells with one nucleus and intercalated discs are cardiac muscle.","Smooth muscle cells are spindle-shaped with one central nucleus but have no striations and no intercalated discs. Pick smooth muscle for a slide of unstriped, tapered cells.","Skeletal muscle fibers are striated but long, unbranched, and multinucleated, with nuclei pushed to the edges. Pick skeletal muscle for a slide of long cylinders with many peripheral nuclei."],"lt":"t-heart","lid":"c40","comp":60,"competencyId":"cv-cardiac-muscle"},{"id":"L01551","dok":2,"q":"Cardiac muscle shares one feature with skeletal muscle and a different feature with smooth muscle. Which statement is correct?","a":"It is striated like skeletal muscle and involuntary like smooth muscle.","options":["It is multinucleated like skeletal muscle and involuntary like smooth muscle","It is striated like skeletal muscle and involuntary like smooth muscle","It is voluntary like skeletal muscle and unstriated like smooth muscle","It is striated like smooth muscle and voluntary like skeletal muscle"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Cardiac muscle is striated, like skeletal muscle, because both are built from orderly rows of sarcomeres. It is also involuntary, like smooth muscle, so you cannot consciously control your heartbeat. That combination of striated and involuntary is unique to cardiac muscle.","notes":["The second half is right, but cardiac muscle cells usually have one central nucleus. Long multinucleated fibers are a feature of skeletal muscle only.","Correct. Cardiac muscle is striated like skeletal muscle and involuntary like smooth muscle.","Both halves are wrong. Cardiac muscle is not under conscious control and it is striated. This statement describes neither cardiac muscle nor any other single tissue.","Smooth muscle is the one muscle tissue without striations, and cardiac muscle is involuntary. Both halves of this statement are mixed up."],"lt":"t-heart","lid":"c9-x2","comp":77,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-tissue-types"},{"id":"L01552","dok":1,"q":"An intercalated disc contains which pair of cell junctions?","a":"Desmosomes and gap junctions.","options":["Tight junctions and desmosomes","Desmosomes and gap junctions","Tight junctions and gap junctions","Synapses and gap junctions"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Each intercalated disc does two jobs, and each job has its own junction. Desmosomes anchor the cells together so they do not tear apart when the heart contracts, and gap junctions are channels that pass the contraction signal straight from cell to cell. Together they let the heart work as one strong, coordinated unit.","notes":["Desmosomes are correct, but tight junctions belong to epithelial sheets, not cardiac muscle. This pair would fit a question about junctions in an epithelium.","Correct. Intercalated discs contain desmosomes for anchoring and gap junctions for signaling.","Gap junctions are correct, but tight junctions are not found in intercalated discs. Tight junctions seal epithelial cells to stop leakage, so pick them for the lining of the gut.","A synapse is the junction where a neuron passes a signal to another cell, such as at the neuromuscular junction of skeletal muscle. Cardiomyocytes pass the signal to each other through gap junctions instead."],"lt":"t-heart","lid":"c10-x2","comp":60,"competencyId":"cv-cardiac-muscle"},{"id":"L01553","dok":2,"q":"Suppose the gap junctions between cardiomyocytes stopped working. What would be most directly lost?","a":"The direct spread of the contraction signal from cell to cell, so the heart would not contract as one unit.","options":["The supply of ATP to the contracting cells","The direct spread of the contraction signal from cell to cell","The anchoring of the chordae tendineae to the ventricle wall","The anchoring that keeps cells from pulling apart"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Gap junctions are channels in the intercalated discs that let the electrical signal pass straight from one cardiomyocyte to the next. Without them, the signal could not sweep across the heart muscle, and the cells would no longer contract together as a coordinated unit. That coordinated squeeze is what makes the heart an effective pump.","notes":["ATP comes from the mitochondria, which fill about a quarter of each cardiomyocyte. Pick this if the question asked what the mitochondria supply.","Correct. Gap junctions pass the contraction signal from cell to cell, so losing them breaks the coordinated contraction.","The chordae tendineae attach to the papillary muscles, which are part of the ventricle wall. Gap junctions do not hold them, so pick this for a question about papillary muscle damage.","Anchoring cells together is the job of the desmosomes. Pick this if the question asked what would fail if the desmosomes broke down."],"lt":"t-heart","lid":"c11-x2","comp":60,"competencyId":"cv-cardiac-muscle"},{"id":"L01566","dok":2,"q":"Mitochondria fill about a quarter of the volume of each cardiomyocyte. Why does cardiac muscle need so many?","a":"To make the energy for constant, lifelong contraction.","options":["To pass the contraction signal between cells","To hold neighboring cells together","To store oxygen for times when blood flow stops","To make the energy for constant, lifelong contraction"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Mitochondria are the organelles that make ATP, the energy molecule that powers muscle contraction. The heart contracts about once a second for an entire lifetime and cannot pause to rest, so its cells need a large, steady supply of ATP. Packing each cardiomyocyte with mitochondria meets that demand, which also makes the heart heavily dependent on a constant oxygen supply.","notes":["Passing the contraction signal between cells is the job of the gap junctions in the intercalated discs. Pick this for a question about gap junctions.","Holding neighboring cells together is the job of the desmosomes in the intercalated discs. Pick this for a question about desmosomes.","Mitochondria do not store oxygen. Oxygen is held in muscle by myoglobin, a protein, and the heart has only a small reserve, which is why it is damaged quickly when blood flow stops.","Correct. Abundant mitochondria supply ATP for the heart's nonstop work."],"lt":"t-heart","lid":"c25-x2","comp":60,"competencyId":"cv-cardiac-muscle"},{"id":"L01577","dok":2,"q":"The signal for each heartbeat starts at the SA node, spreads across the atria, and then pauses briefly at the AV node before reaching the ventricles. Why is this short delay useful?","a":"It lets the atria finish emptying into the ventricles before the ventricles contract.","options":["It lets the right and left ventricles contract at different times","It lets the atria finish filling the ventricles first","It lets the ventricles contract first so the atria can fill","It gives the SA node time to make the next signal"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"For the heart to pump well, the ventricles should be full before they squeeze. The brief hold at the AV node gives the atria time to contract and push their last blood into the ventricles. Then the signal travels on through the AV bundle, bundle branches, and Purkinje fibers, and the ventricles contract with a full load.","notes":["The right and left ventricles contract together, since the signal reaches both through the bundle branches at nearly the same time. The delay separates the atria from the ventricles, not the two ventricles from each other.","Correct. The AV node delay lets the atria finish filling the ventricles.","This reverses the order. The atria contract first and the ventricles follow, so the delay lets ventricular filling finish before ventricular contraction.","The SA node sets its own rhythm and does not wait on the AV node. The pause affects how the signal passes to the ventricles, not when the next beat starts."],"lt":"t-heart","lid":"d3-t-heart-6","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L01580","dok":1,"q":"Which structure of the cardiac conduction system is the natural pacemaker of the heart?","a":"The sinoatrial (SA) node.","options":["The atrioventricular node","The sinoatrial node","The Purkinje fibers","The atrioventricular bundle"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The sinoatrial (SA) node is a small cluster of specialized cardiac muscle cells in the wall of the right atrium. It fires on its own faster than any other part of the conduction system, about 60 to 100 times a minute, so its signal sets the pace for the whole heart. That is why it is called the natural pacemaker, and it is the first stop in the conduction pathway.","notes":["The AV node sits in the floor of the right atrium and briefly delays the signal before passing it on to the ventricles. It is the answer for where the signal pauses, and it can act as a slower backup pacemaker if the SA node fails, but it is not the normal pacemaker.","Correct. The SA node fires first and fastest, so it sets the heart's rhythm.","The Purkinje fibers are the last part of the pathway, spreading the signal through the walls of both ventricles. Pick them if the question asked which structure hands the signal to the ventricular muscle cells.","The AV bundle, also called the bundle of His, carries the signal into the interventricular septum. Choose it if the question asked for the only electrical link between the atria and the ventricles."],"lt":"t-cardiac-conduction","lid":"c1","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L01581","dok":1,"q":"In which chamber wall is the sinoatrial node located?","a":"The wall of the right atrium.","options":["The wall of the right ventricle","The wall of the left ventricle","The wall of the right atrium","The wall of the left atrium"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The SA node sits in the wall of the right atrium, close to where the superior vena cava empties into that chamber. This placement fits the order of the heartbeat. The signal starts at the top of the heart in an atrium, spreads across both atria so they contract first, and only then travels down to the ventricles.","notes":["The right ventricle pumps blood into the pulmonary trunk and receives the signal late, through the right bundle branch and the Purkinje fibers. Pick it if the question asked which chamber sends blood to the lungs.","The left ventricle has the thickest wall in the heart and receives the signal near the end of the pathway, through the Purkinje fibers. It would fit a question about which chamber pumps blood into the aorta.","Correct. The SA node lies in the right atrial wall near the opening of the superior vena cava.","The left atrium receives oxygenated blood from the pulmonary veins and holds no named node of the conduction system. Students pick it because they remember the SA node is in an atrium, but it is the right atrium, not the left."],"lt":"t-cardiac-conduction","lid":"c2","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L01582","dok":1,"q":"The sinoatrial node lies near the opening of which large vein?","a":"The superior vena cava.","options":["The inferior vena cava","The superior vena cava","The coronary sinus","A pulmonary vein"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The SA node lies in the upper part of the right atrial wall, near the opening of the superior vena cava, the large vein that returns blood from the head, neck and upper limbs. This puts the pacemaker high on the heart, so the signal starts at the top and travels downward toward the apex.","notes":["The inferior vena cava also opens into the right atrium, but lower down, bringing blood from the trunk and lower limbs. It would be the answer if the question asked which vein returns blood from below the diaphragm.","Correct. The SA node sits in the right atrial wall near where the superior vena cava enters.","The coronary sinus collects blood from the heart's own cardiac veins and opens low on the posterior wall of the right atrium. It would be the answer if the question asked which vessel returns blood from the heart wall itself.","The pulmonary veins return oxygenated blood from the lungs to the left atrium, not the right atrium where the SA node sits. Pick them if the question asked which vessels empty into the left atrium."],"lt":"t-cardiac-conduction","lid":"c3","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L01583","dok":1,"q":"Where is the atrioventricular node located?","a":"In the floor of the right atrium, near the interatrial septum.","options":["In the floor of the right atrium","In the superior interventricular septum","In the wall of the left ventricle","Throughout both ventricle walls"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The AV node lies in the floor of the right atrium, close to the interatrial septum, the wall between the two atria. This spot sits right at the junction of the atria and the ventricles, which suits its job. It collects the signal after it crosses the atria, holds it briefly, and then passes it down into the AV bundle.","notes":["Correct. The AV node sits in the right atrial floor next to the interatrial septum.","The superior part of the interventricular septum holds the AV bundle, also called the bundle of His, the next structure after the AV node. Pick this location if the question asked where the AV bundle lies.","The left ventricle wall contains Purkinje fibers but no node. Students sometimes place the AV node in a ventricle because of the word ventricular in its name, but the node sits on the atrial side.","This describes the Purkinje fibers, the final part of the pathway. It would be correct if the question asked where the Purkinje fibers are found."],"lt":"t-cardiac-conduction","lid":"c4","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L01584","dok":1,"q":"What is the other name for the atrioventricular bundle?","a":"The bundle of His.","options":["The cardiac plexus","The bundle of His","The Purkinje fibers","The bundle branches"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The atrioventricular (AV) bundle is also called the bundle of His, after the anatomist who described it. Both names refer to the same short strand of conducting cells that runs from the AV node into the superior interventricular septum. Textbooks, lab lists and ECG reports use the two names interchangeably, so you need to recognize both.","notes":["The cardiac plexus is a network of autonomic nerve fibers near the base of the heart, not part of the conduction system itself. Pick it if the question asked how sympathetic and parasympathetic nerves reach the nodes.","Correct. The AV bundle and the bundle of His are the same structure.","The Purkinje fibers are a separate, later part of the pathway, spread through the walls of both ventricles. They would be the answer if the question asked which fibers deliver the signal to the ventricular muscle.","The right and left bundle branches are what the AV bundle splits into, and they run down either side of the interventricular septum. Choose them if the question asked which structures come right after the bundle of His."],"lt":"t-cardiac-conduction","lid":"c5","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L01585","dok":1,"q":"In which part of the heart does the atrioventricular bundle lie?","a":"The superior part of the interventricular septum.","options":["The upper interventricular septum","The floor of the right atrium","The wall of the right atrium","The apex of the heart"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The AV bundle runs from the AV node into the superior part of the interventricular septum, the muscular wall between the two ventricles. To get there it crosses the fibrous skeleton, a framework of dense connective tissue that electrically insulates the atria from the ventricles. That makes the AV bundle the only electrical bridge between the upper and lower chambers, and just below this point it splits into the right and left bundle branches.","notes":["Correct. The AV bundle lies in the upper part of the interventricular septum.","The floor of the right atrium near the interatrial septum is where the AV node sits, one step before the AV bundle. Pick it if the question asked for the location of the AV node.","The wall of the right atrium near the superior vena cava holds the SA node. Pick it if the question asked where the pacemaker is.","The apex is the pointed bottom of the heart, where the bundle branches deliver the signal to the Purkinje fibers. It would fit a question about where ventricular contraction begins."],"lt":"t-cardiac-conduction","lid":"c6","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L01586","dok":1,"q":"How many bundle branches carry the signal down the interventricular septum?","a":"Two, a right and a left bundle branch.","options":["One","Two","Three","Four"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The AV bundle divides into two bundle branches, a right bundle branch and a left bundle branch. They run down the right and left sides of the interventricular septum toward the apex, so each ventricle gets its own branch of the signal. At the apex they pass the signal on to the Purkinje fibers.","notes":["One is the number of AV bundles. Students who confuse the single bundle of His with the branches it splits into tend to pick this number.","Correct. There is a right bundle branch and a left bundle branch, one for each ventricle.","Three may come from counting the AV bundle together with its two branches. The bundle itself divides into just two branches, one for each ventricle.","Four matches the number of heart chambers, which is a common mix-up. Only the two ventricles receive bundle branches, so there are two, not four."],"lt":"t-cardiac-conduction","lid":"c7","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L01587","dok":1,"q":"Where are the Purkinje fibers located?","a":"Throughout the walls of both ventricles.","options":["In the floor of the right atrium","Throughout the walls of both ventricles","In the superior interventricular septum","In the wall of the right atrium"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The Purkinje fibers branch through the walls of both ventricles, spreading out from the apex into the ventricular muscle. They are the last part of the conduction pathway and hand the signal directly to the contractile cells, the ordinary working heart muscle cells. Because they fan out from the bottom of the heart, the ventricles contract from the apex upward.","notes":["The floor of the right atrium, near the interatrial septum, holds the AV node. Pick this location if the question asked where the signal pauses before entering the ventricles.","Correct. The Purkinje fibers spread through the walls of both ventricles.","The superior interventricular septum holds the AV bundle, which comes before the bundle branches and the Purkinje fibers. Choose it if the question asked where the bundle of His lies.","The right atrial wall holds the SA node near the superior vena cava. It would be correct if the question asked where the pacemaker sits."],"lt":"t-cardiac-conduction","lid":"c8","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L01588","dok":1,"q":"Which region of the brain contains the cardiac centers that adjust heart rate?","a":"The medulla oblongata.","options":["The medulla oblongata","The midbrain","The cerebellum","The thalamus"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The cardiac centers are regions of the medulla oblongata, the lowest part of the brainstem, just above the spinal cord. From there, autonomic nerves travel to the heart and reach the SA and AV nodes through the cardiac plexus, where they speed up or slow down the rate the SA node sets. The medulla also holds centers for breathing, which is one reason damage to it is so dangerous.","notes":["Correct. The cardiac centers sit in the medulla oblongata of the brainstem.","The midbrain is the upper part of the brainstem, above the pons, and holds visual and auditory reflex centers. Students pick it because it is part of the brainstem, but the cardiac centers are lower down, in the medulla.","The cerebellum sits behind the brainstem and coordinates balance and smooth movement. Pick it if the question asked which brain region fine-tunes skeletal muscle movement.","The thalamus is part of the diencephalon and relays sensory information to the cerebral cortex. It would be the answer for the brain's main sensory relay station."],"lt":"t-cardiac-conduction","lid":"c9","comp":65,"competencyId":"cv-cardiac-nerves"},{"id":"L01589","dok":1,"q":"What is the cardiac plexus?","a":"A network of autonomic nerve fibers near the base of the heart.","options":["A cluster of pacemaker cells in the right atrial wall","A strand of conducting cells in the interventricular septum","A fine network of conducting fibers in the ventricle walls","A network of autonomic nerve fibers near the base of the heart"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The cardiac plexus is a network of autonomic nerve fibers near the base of the heart, close to the great vessels. Sympathetic and parasympathetic fibers pass through it to reach the SA and AV nodes, where they adjust the heart rate. It is part of the heart's nerve supply, not part of the conduction system itself.","notes":["This describes the SA node, the heart's natural pacemaker. Pick it if the question asked what the SA node is.","This describes the AV bundle, or bundle of His. Choose it if the question asked for the only electrical link between the atria and the ventricles. The plexus is made of nerve fibers, not conducting muscle cells.","This describes the Purkinje fibers, the last part of the conduction pathway. It would be correct if the question asked what delivers the signal to the ventricular muscle.","Correct. The cardiac plexus is the nerve network that carries autonomic fibers to the nodes."],"lt":"t-cardiac-conduction","lid":"c10","comp":65,"competencyId":"cv-cardiac-nerves"},{"id":"L01590","dok":1,"q":"Which nerve carries the parasympathetic fibers that slow the heart rate?","a":"The vagus nerve.","options":["The vagus nerve","The phrenic nerve","The trigeminal nerve","The facial nerve"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Parasympathetic fibers reach the heart in the vagus nerve, cranial nerve X, which leaves the medulla and travels down through the neck into the thorax. Its fibers join the cardiac plexus and end at the SA and AV nodes, where they slow the heart rate. The name vagus means wandering, which fits a cranial nerve that reaches all the way into the thorax and abdomen.","notes":["Correct. The vagus nerve carries the parasympathetic fibers that slow the heart.","The phrenic nerve comes from the cervical plexus and runs down through the thorax alongside the pericardium to reach the diaphragm. Students pick it because it passes right beside the heart, but it does not adjust heart rate. Pick it if the question asked which nerve travels to the diaphragm.","The trigeminal nerve, cranial nerve V, is the main sensory nerve of the face and has three divisions. Choose it if the question asked which cranial nerve carries touch and pain from the face.","The facial nerve, cranial nerve VII, exits the skull near the ear and spreads across the face. It would be the answer for a cranial nerve that branches over the face, not one that reaches the heart."],"lt":"t-cardiac-conduction","lid":"c11","comp":65,"competencyId":"cv-cardiac-nerves"},{"id":"L01591","dok":2,"q":"Which conduction structure fires fastest, about 60 to 100 times a minute, and therefore sets the heart rate?","a":"The sinoatrial node.","options":["The bundle branches","The Purkinje fibers","The atrioventricular node","The sinoatrial node"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Several parts of the conduction system can fire on their own, but the SA node fires fastest, about 60 to 100 times a minute. Because its signal arrives before the slower sites have a chance to fire, it sets the pace for the entire heart. A normal rhythm started by the SA node is called a sinus rhythm.","notes":["The bundle branches carry the signal down both sides of the interventricular septum and do not set the rate. Pick them if the question asked which structures run toward the apex from the AV bundle.","The Purkinje fibers can fire on their own only very slowly, so they never set the normal pace. They would be correct if the question asked which structures spread the signal through the ventricle walls.","The AV node can fire on its own, but more slowly than the SA node, so normally it just relays the signal after a short delay. It would be the answer if the question asked which structure takes over as a backup pacemaker if the SA node fails.","Correct. The SA node is the fastest site, so it sets the heart rate."],"lt":"t-cardiac-conduction","lid":"c12","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L01592","dok":2,"q":"Which conduction structure is the only electrical link between the atria and the ventricles?","a":"The atrioventricular bundle.","options":["The Purkinje fibers","The bundle branches","The sinoatrial node","The atrioventricular bundle"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The atria and ventricles are separated by the fibrous skeleton, a framework of dense connective tissue that does not conduct electrical signals. The AV bundle, or bundle of His, is the one strand of conducting tissue that passes through it, so every normal heartbeat must travel this route to reach the ventricles. This is why damage at the AV node or AV bundle can disconnect the atrial beat from the ventricular beat.","notes":["The Purkinje fibers spread the signal within the ventricle walls and do not cross between the atria and the ventricles. Choose them if the question asked which structures reach the contractile cells of the ventricles.","The bundle branches are already below the fibrous skeleton, carrying the signal down the septum between the ventricles. They would be correct if the question asked what the AV bundle divides into.","The SA node is the pacemaker in the right atrial wall and starts the signal, but it sits entirely on the atrial side. Pick it if the question asked which structure fires first.","Correct. The AV bundle is the only conducting path through the fibrous skeleton."],"lt":"t-cardiac-conduction","lid":"c13","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L01593","dok":2,"q":"In the cardiac conduction pathway, which structure comes just before the bundle branches?","a":"The atrioventricular bundle.","options":["The atrioventricular node","The atrioventricular bundle","The Purkinje fibers","The sinoatrial node"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The pathway runs SA node, across the atria, AV node, AV bundle, bundle branches, then Purkinje fibers. The AV bundle sits directly before the bundle branches because it is the single trunk that divides into the right and left branches at the top of the interventricular septum.","notes":["The AV node comes two steps before the bundle branches, with the AV bundle between them. It would be the answer if the question asked which structure lies just before the AV bundle.","Correct. The AV bundle divides into the bundle branches.","The Purkinje fibers come just after the bundle branches, not before them. Choose them if the question asked which structure the bundle branches hand the signal to.","The SA node is the first structure in the pathway, several steps before the bundle branches. Pick it if the question asked which structure starts each heartbeat."],"lt":"t-cardiac-conduction","lid":"c14","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L01594","dok":2,"q":"Which conduction structure delivers the signal directly to the contractile cells of the ventricle walls?","a":"The Purkinje fibers.","options":["The atrioventricular node","The Purkinje fibers","The bundle branches","The atrioventricular bundle"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The Purkinje fibers branch through the walls of both ventricles and connect with the contractile cells, the ordinary cardiac muscle cells that do the pumping. They are the final link in the conduction pathway. Once the Purkinje fibers deliver the signal, the ventricular muscle contracts.","notes":["The AV node is in the floor of the right atrium and delays the signal before it enters the ventricles. It would be correct if the question asked where the signal pauses.","Correct. The Purkinje fibers are the last step, handing the signal to the working ventricular muscle.","The bundle branches carry the signal down the interventricular septum toward the apex, one step before the Purkinje fibers. Pick them if the question asked which structures run down either side of the septum.","The AV bundle carries the signal from the AV node into the top of the interventricular septum. Choose it if the question asked for the only electrical bridge between the atria and the ventricles."],"lt":"t-cardiac-conduction","lid":"c15","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L01595","dok":2,"q":"At which structure of the conduction system does the signal pause briefly before entering the ventricles?","a":"At the atrioventricular node.","options":["The bundle branches","The Purkinje fibers","The sinoatrial node","The atrioventricular node"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"After the signal spreads across both atria, it reaches the AV node in the floor of the right atrium and slows down there for a fraction of a second. This brief delay gives the atria time to finish squeezing their blood into the ventricles before the ventricles are signaled to contract. After the pause, the signal continues into the AV bundle.","notes":["The bundle branches conduct quickly down the septum and do not hold the signal. They would be the answer if the question asked which structures carry the signal toward the apex.","The Purkinje fibers conduct the signal rapidly through the ventricle walls so the ventricles contract together. Choose them if the question asked which structures deliver the signal to the ventricular muscle.","The SA node starts the signal rather than holding it. Pick it if the question asked which structure fires first and sets the pace.","Correct. The AV node holds the signal briefly so the atria can finish emptying."],"lt":"t-cardiac-conduction","lid":"c16","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L01596","dok":2,"q":"Two named structures of the conduction system sit within the right atrium. Which pair is it?","a":"The sinoatrial node and the atrioventricular node.","options":["The bundle branches and the Purkinje fibers","The AV node and the Purkinje fibers","The SA node and the AV node","The AV bundle and the bundle branches"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Both nodes sit in the right atrium. The SA node is in the upper right atrial wall near the opening of the superior vena cava, and the AV node is in the floor of the right atrium near the interatrial septum. Everything after the AV node, from the AV bundle to the Purkinje fibers, lies in the interventricular septum or the ventricle walls.","notes":["These are the two structures farthest down the pathway, running toward the apex and through the ventricle walls. They would fit a question about which structures carry the signal within the ventricles.","Only half of this pair is right. The AV node is in the right atrium, but the Purkinje fibers are spread through both ventricle walls, so students pick it when they remember one node but not the other.","Correct. Both the SA node and the AV node are in the right atrium.","These two lie in the interventricular septum, not the right atrium. Pick this pair if the question asked which conduction structures are found within the septum."],"lt":"t-cardiac-conduction","lid":"c17","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L01597","dok":2,"q":"Which two conduction structures both lie within the interventricular septum?","a":"The atrioventricular bundle and the bundle branches.","options":["The AV bundle and the bundle branches","The SA node and the bundle branches","The SA node and the AV node","The AV node and the Purkinje fibers"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The AV bundle enters the superior part of the interventricular septum and splits into the right and left bundle branches, which run down either side of the septum toward the apex. So both of these structures sit within the septum. The two nodes are above, in the right atrium, and the Purkinje fibers spread out into the ventricle walls.","notes":["Correct. The AV bundle and its two branches are the septal part of the pathway.","Only the bundle branches are in the septum. The SA node is high in the right atrial wall near the superior vena cava, at the opposite end of the pathway.","Both nodes are in the right atrium, not the septum between the ventricles. Pick this pair if the question asked which conduction structures lie in the right atrium.","Neither belongs here. The AV node sits in the floor of the right atrium and the Purkinje fibers spread through the ventricle walls; students pick this pair when they mix up the AV node with the AV bundle, whose names are nearly identical."],"lt":"t-cardiac-conduction","lid":"c18","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L01598","dok":2,"q":"What conduction event does the P wave of an ECG show?","a":"The signal spreading across the atria.","options":["The signal spreading across the atria","The signal spreading across the ventricles","The atria electrically recovering","The ventricles electrically recovering"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The P wave is the first small bump of each heartbeat on an ECG, and it lines up with the signal spreading across both atria right after the SA node fires. The atria contract just after the P wave. The larger QRS complex comes next, showing the signal spreading through the ventricles.","notes":["Correct. The P wave shows the signal crossing the atria.","The signal spreading across the ventricles produces the QRS complex, the tall spike after the P wave. Pick it if the question asked what the QRS complex shows.","The atria do recover electrically, but this happens at the same time as the much larger QRS complex, so it is hidden and has no wave of its own. Students choose it by assuming every chamber event must have its own visible wave.","Ventricular recovery shows up as the T wave, the last deflection of the heartbeat. It would be correct if the question asked what the T wave represents."],"lt":"t-cardiac-conduction","lid":"c19","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L01599","dok":2,"q":"On an ECG, which part of the tracing corresponds to the signal spreading across the ventricles?","a":"The QRS complex.","options":["The flat line after the P wave","The P wave","The T wave","The QRS complex"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The QRS complex is the tall, sharp spike of each heartbeat, and it lines up with the signal spreading through the ventricles by way of the bundle branches and Purkinje fibers. It is larger than the P wave because the ventricles have far more muscle than the atria. The ventricles contract just after the QRS complex.","notes":["The flat line between the P wave and the QRS complex mostly reflects the brief pause at the AV node, when the signal is held before it enters the ventricles. Choose it if the question asked where the AV node delay appears on an ECG.","The P wave is the small first bump, showing the signal spreading across the atria. Pick it if the question asked about the atria.","The T wave comes after the QRS complex and shows the ventricles electrically recovering before they relax. It would be correct if the question asked about ventricular recovery.","Correct. The QRS complex shows the signal sweeping through the ventricles."],"lt":"t-cardiac-conduction","lid":"c20","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L01600","dok":2,"q":"Which nerves reach the heart through the cardiac plexus and speed up the heart rate?","a":"The sympathetic nerves.","options":["The parasympathetic nerves","The somatic motor nerves","The phrenic nerves","The sympathetic nerves"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Sympathetic nerves belong to the part of the autonomic nervous system that prepares the body for activity. Their fibers reach the heart through the cardiac plexus and end on the SA and AV nodes, where they raise the heart rate. They speed up the beat that the SA node already makes; they do not create it.","notes":["Parasympathetic fibers also pass through the cardiac plexus, but they slow the heart rate. Pick them if the question asked which autonomic fibers, carried in the vagus nerve, slow the heart.","Somatic motor nerves control skeletal muscle, the voluntary muscle you move on purpose, and they do not supply the heart. They would be the answer if the question asked which nerves stimulate voluntary muscle.","The phrenic nerves run down through the thorax past the heart to reach the diaphragm, and they do not adjust heart rate. Choose them if the question asked which nerves from the cervical plexus travel to the diaphragm.","Correct. Sympathetic fibers speed the rate set by the SA node."],"lt":"t-cardiac-conduction","lid":"c21","comp":65,"competencyId":"cv-cardiac-nerves"},{"id":"L01601","dok":1,"q":"Which term is the umbrella name for any deviation from the normal heart rhythm?","a":"Arrhythmia.","options":["Ectopic pacemaker","Tachycardia","Arrhythmia","Heart block"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Arrhythmia means any deviation from the normal heart rhythm, so it is the umbrella term that covers all the specific conduction disorders. A rhythm that is too fast, too slow, irregular, or started from the wrong place all count as arrhythmias. The other terms each name one particular kind.","notes":["An ectopic pacemaker is one specific arrhythmia, in which a site outside the SA node takes over the rhythm. Choose it if the question described the beat starting in the wrong place.","Tachycardia is one specific arrhythmia, an abnormally fast resting heart rate. It would be correct if the question described a heart beating too fast at rest.","Correct. Arrhythmia is the general term that includes all the others.","Heart block is one specific arrhythmia, in which the signal is delayed or stopped at the AV node. Pick it if the question described the atria and ventricles beating out of step."],"lt":"t-cardiac-conduction","lid":"c22","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L01602","dok":2,"q":"Where in the heart does heart block delay or stop the signal?","a":"At the atrioventricular node.","options":["The atrioventricular node","The cardiac plexus","The Purkinje fibers","The sinoatrial node"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"In heart block, the signal from the atria is delayed or stopped at the AV node, so it reaches the ventricles late or not at all. Because the AV node and AV bundle form the only route through the insulating fibrous skeleton, a block here can leave the atria and ventricles beating out of step. An artificial pacemaker can carry the signal past the block.","notes":["Correct. Heart block happens at the AV node, between the atria and the ventricles.","The cardiac plexus is a network of autonomic nerves near the base of the heart, not part of the conduction pathway, so the heartbeat signal does not travel through it. Pick it if the question asked how autonomic nerves reach the nodes.","The Purkinje fibers deliver the signal within the ventricle walls. They would be the answer if the question asked which structure hands the signal to the ventricular muscle.","The SA node starts the signal. A failing SA node causes a slow or missing pacemaker signal, which would fit a question about what an artificial pacemaker replaces when the natural pacemaker fails."],"lt":"t-cardiac-conduction","lid":"c23","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L01603","dok":2,"q":"If the atrioventricular bundle were destroyed, which two parts of the heart would lose their only electrical connection?","a":"The atria and the ventricles.","options":["The SA node and the AV node","The right ventricle and the left ventricle","The right atrium and the left atrium","The atria and the ventricles"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The fibrous skeleton is a layer of dense connective tissue between the atria and the ventricles that does not carry electrical signals. The AV bundle is the one strand of conducting tissue that crosses it, so it is the only electrical bridge from the upper chambers to the lower chambers. If it were destroyed, the atria could still beat from the SA node, but their signal could not reach the ventricles.","notes":["The SA node and AV node are connected through the atrial muscle, upstream of the AV bundle, so damage to the bundle would not cut them apart. This pair would fit a question about which two structures sit in the right atrium.","Both ventricles receive the signal below the AV bundle, through the right and left bundle branches, so the bundle does not connect them to each other. Students choose this pair because the bundle splits toward the two ventricles, but it links the upper chambers to the lower ones.","The two atria are joined by atrial muscle, and the SA node signal spreads across both of them without using the AV bundle. Pick this pair if the question asked which chambers the signal reaches right after the SA node fires.","Correct. The AV bundle is the only conducting bridge from the atria to the ventricles."],"lt":"t-cardiac-conduction","lid":"c24","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L01604","dok":2,"q":"A conduction structure sits in the right atrial wall near the opening of the superior vena cava and fires first. Which structure is it?","a":"The sinoatrial node.","options":["The sinoatrial node","The bundle branches","The atrioventricular node","The atrioventricular bundle"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Two clues point to the SA node: its location in the right atrial wall near the opening of the superior vena cava, and the fact that it fires first. Because it fires faster than any other part of the conduction system, it sets the pace for the whole heart, which is why it is called the natural pacemaker.","notes":["Correct. Its location and its role as the first to fire both identify the SA node.","The bundle branches run down both sides of the interventricular septum toward the apex. Choose them if the description mentioned a right and a left portion in the septum.","The AV node is also in the right atrium, but in the floor near the interatrial septum, and it receives the signal after it crosses the atria. Pick it if the description mentioned the floor of the right atrium and a brief delay.","The AV bundle lies in the superior interventricular septum, below the atria. It would be correct for a description of the only electrical bridge between the atria and the ventricles."],"lt":"t-cardiac-conduction","lid":"c25","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L01605","dok":2,"q":"A conduction structure lies in the floor of the right atrium near the interatrial septum, where it briefly delays the signal. Which structure is it?","a":"The atrioventricular node.","options":["The atrioventricular bundle","The Purkinje fibers","The atrioventricular node","The sinoatrial node"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The floor of the right atrium near the interatrial septum is the home of the AV node, and the brief delay is its signature job. Holding the signal for a moment lets the atria finish emptying into the ventricles before the ventricles contract. After the delay, the AV node passes the signal into the AV bundle.","notes":["The AV bundle comes right after the AV node and sits in the superior interventricular septum. Choose it for a description of the only electrical bridge from the atria to the ventricles.","The Purkinje fibers spread through the ventricle walls and conduct quickly rather than holding the signal. They would match a description of a fine network handing the signal to the ventricular muscle.","Correct. The location in the right atrial floor and the delay both identify the AV node.","The SA node is also in the right atrium, but high in the wall near the superior vena cava, and it starts the signal rather than delaying it. Pick it for a description of the structure that fires first."],"lt":"t-cardiac-conduction","lid":"c26","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L01606","dok":2,"q":"A conduction structure divides into a right portion and a left portion that run down opposite sides of the interventricular septum. Which step of the pathway is this?","a":"The AV bundle dividing into the bundle branches.","options":["The AV bundle dividing into the bundle branches","The AV node passing the signal into the AV bundle","The bundle branches passing the signal to the Purkinje fibers","The SA node passing the signal across the atria"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The AV bundle enters the top of the interventricular septum and then splits into the right and left bundle branches. These run down opposite sides of the septum toward the apex, one branch for each ventricle. A single structure becoming two portions that travel down the septum fits only this step.","notes":["Correct. The AV bundle splits into the right and left bundle branches.","The AV node passes the signal into a single AV bundle; nothing splits into right and left parts at this step. Pick it if the question asked what follows the AV node.","The bundle branches do hand the signal to the Purkinje fibers near the apex, but that step fans out through the ventricle walls rather than splitting into two portions in the septum. Choose it if the question asked what happens at the end of the bundle branches.","The SA node's signal spreads outward across both atria, not down the septum. This would fit a question about the first step after the pacemaker fires."],"lt":"t-cardiac-conduction","lid":"c27","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L01607","dok":2,"q":"A conduction structure forms a fine network spread throughout both ventricle walls and hands the signal to the working muscle cells. Which structure is it?","a":"The Purkinje fibers.","options":["The Purkinje fibers","The bundle branches","The atrioventricular node","The atrioventricular bundle"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"A fine network spread through both ventricle walls is the Purkinje fibers. They are the last part of the conduction pathway, passing the signal to the contractile cells so the ventricles contract. Because they spread upward from the apex, contraction starts at the bottom of the ventricles and moves toward the top.","notes":["Correct. The Purkinje fibers are the network that reaches the ventricular muscle.","The bundle branches are two tracts that run down the interventricular septum, not a network through the walls. Pick them for a description of a right and a left portion in the septum.","The AV node is a compact cluster in the floor of the right atrium that delays the signal. It would fit a description of where the signal pauses.","The AV bundle is a short single strand in the superior interventricular septum. Choose it for a description of the only electrical link between the atria and the ventricles."],"lt":"t-cardiac-conduction","lid":"c28","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L01608","dok":2,"q":"On a normal ECG, which part of the tracing lines up with the ventricles electrically recovering?","a":"The T wave.","options":["The QRS complex","The flat line after the P wave","The P wave","The T wave"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The T wave is the last deflection of each heartbeat, following the QRS complex. It lines up with the ventricles electrically recovering so they are ready for the next signal, and the ventricles relax after it. So the order within one beat is P wave, QRS complex, then T wave.","notes":["The QRS complex shows the signal spreading across the ventricles, just before they contract. It would be correct if the question asked about ventricular spread rather than recovery.","The flat line between the P wave and the QRS complex mostly reflects the pause at the AV node. Choose it if the question asked where the AV node delay appears on an ECG.","The P wave is the first small bump, showing the signal spreading across the atria. Pick it if the question asked which deflection comes just before the atria contract.","Correct. The T wave shows the ventricles recovering."],"lt":"t-cardiac-conduction","lid":"c29","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L01609","dok":2,"q":"On an ECG, which mechanical event follows the P wave?","a":"The atria contract.","options":["The ventricles relax","The atria contract","The ventricles contract","The SA node fires"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The P wave shows the signal spreading across both atria, and the atrial muscle contracts right after it. Each deflection works the same way: the electrical event comes first and the mechanical event follows. So the P wave is followed by atrial contraction, the QRS complex by ventricular contraction, and the T wave by ventricular relaxation.","notes":["The ventricles relax after the T wave, which shows them electrically recovering. It would be correct if the question asked what follows the T wave.","Correct. The atria contract right after the P wave.","The ventricles contract after the QRS complex, which shows the signal spreading across the ventricles. Pick it if the question asked what follows the QRS complex.","The SA node fires just before the P wave, starting the signal that the P wave then shows spreading across the atria. Students pick it because the SA node starts the beat, but its firing comes before the P wave, not after it."],"lt":"t-cardiac-conduction","lid":"c30","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L01610","dok":2,"q":"A conduction disorder is described as a site outside the sinoatrial node taking over the heart's rhythm. Which disorder is it?","a":"An ectopic pacemaker.","options":["Heart block","Bradycardia","An ectopic pacemaker","Atrial fibrillation"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Ectopic means out of place, so an ectopic pacemaker is a site outside the SA node that starts firing and takes over the rhythm. When this happens, the normal pacing order of the heart is lost because the beat no longer starts at the top of the right atrium. The SA node is supposed to be the only pacemaker in a normal heartbeat.","notes":["Heart block is a delay or stop of the signal at the AV node, so the problem is a blocked route rather than a new starting point. It would be the answer if the question described the atria and ventricles beating out of step.","Bradycardia is an abnormally slow resting heart rate, which can happen even when the SA node is still in charge. Pick it if the question described a heart rate that is too slow.","Correct. An ectopic pacemaker is a site outside the SA node that takes over.","Atrial fibrillation is rapid, disorganized signaling across the atria so they quiver, not one organized site taking over. Choose it if the question described the atria quivering instead of contracting."],"lt":"t-cardiac-conduction","lid":"c31","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L01611","dok":2,"q":"A conduction disorder is described as rapid, disorganized signals in the atria, so the atria quiver instead of contracting. Which disorder is it?","a":"Atrial fibrillation.","options":["Atrial fibrillation","Heart block","Ventricular fibrillation","Tachycardia"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Fibrillation means quivering, and in atrial fibrillation rapid, disorganized signals in the atria make them quiver instead of giving one coordinated squeeze. The ventricles still pump, so the condition is common and treatable rather than immediately fatal. The location in the name tells you which chambers are affected.","notes":["Correct. Rapid, disorganized atrial signals make the atria quiver.","Heart block is a delay or stop at the AV node, not chaotic signaling in the atria. Choose it if the question described the signal failing to pass from the atria to the ventricles.","Ventricular fibrillation is the same kind of chaotic signaling, but in the ventricles, so they cannot pump at all. Pick it if the question described the ventricles quivering, a life-threatening emergency.","Tachycardia is an abnormally fast resting heart rate in which the beats are still organized. It would be correct if the question described a heart beating too fast at rest."],"lt":"t-cardiac-conduction","lid":"c32","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L01612","dok":2,"q":"A conduction disorder is described as rapid, disorganized signals in the ventricles, so the ventricles cannot pump. Which disorder is it?","a":"Ventricular fibrillation.","options":["Ventricular fibrillation","Atrial fibrillation","Bradycardia","Ectopic pacemaker"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"In ventricular fibrillation, chaotic signals make the ventricles quiver instead of contracting, so they move no blood out to the lungs or body. Because the ventricles are the main pumping chambers, this is a life-threatening emergency. It is treated with a defibrillator, which delivers a shock that stops all the chaotic signals at once.","notes":["Correct. Disorganized ventricular signals stop the ventricles from pumping.","Atrial fibrillation is the same chaotic quivering, but in the atria, so the ventricles keep pumping. Pick it if the question described the atria quivering in a common, treatable condition.","Bradycardia is an abnormally slow but organized heart rate, so the ventricles still pump. It would be the answer if the question described a resting heart rate that is too slow.","An ectopic pacemaker is a single site outside the SA node taking over the rhythm, not chaotic signaling throughout the ventricles. Choose it if the question described the beat starting from the wrong place."],"lt":"t-cardiac-conduction","lid":"c33","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L01613","dok":1,"q":"A conduction disorder is described as an abnormally slow resting heart rate. Which disorder is it?","a":"Bradycardia.","options":["Bradycardia","Atrial fibrillation","Heart block","Tachycardia"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Bradycardia is an abnormally slow resting heart rate. The prefix brady means slow and cardia refers to the heart. The concern is that a heart beating too slowly may not deliver enough blood flow to the body.","notes":["Correct. Brady means slow, so bradycardia is a slow resting heart rate.","Atrial fibrillation is rapid, disorganized signaling in the atria that makes them quiver. It would be the answer if the question described the atria quivering instead of contracting.","Heart block is a delay or stop of the signal at the AV node. It can slow the ventricles, but the disorder is defined by the blocked route, so choose it if the question described the atria and ventricles beating out of step.","Tachycardia is the opposite, an abnormally fast resting heart rate (tachy means fast). Pick it if the question described a heart beating too fast at rest."],"lt":"t-cardiac-conduction","lid":"c34","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L01614","dok":2,"q":"Which sequence lists the conduction structures in the order the signal travels, starting at the pacemaker?","a":"SA node, AV node, AV bundle, bundle branches, Purkinje fibers.","options":["SA node, AV node, bundle branches, AV bundle, Purkinje fibers","SA node, AV node, AV bundle, bundle branches, Purkinje fibers","SA node, AV bundle, AV node, bundle branches, Purkinje fibers","AV node, SA node, bundle branches, AV bundle, Purkinje fibers"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The signal starts at the SA node in the right atrial wall, crosses both atria, and pauses at the AV node in the right atrial floor. It then passes through the AV bundle into the interventricular septum, splits into the right and left bundle branches, and finally spreads through the ventricle walls in the Purkinje fibers. The order runs from the top of the heart down to the apex and then out through the ventricles.","notes":["This sequence puts the bundle branches before the AV bundle. The branches are what the bundle divides into, so the AV bundle has to come first.","Correct. This is the full pathway from the SA node to the Purkinje fibers.","This sequence swaps the AV node and the AV bundle. The node must come first because it receives the signal from the atria and delays it before the bundle carries it into the septum.","This sequence starts at the AV node, but the SA node is the pacemaker and always fires first. It also places the bundle branches before the AV bundle that they branch from."],"lt":"t-cardiac-conduction","lid":"c35","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L01615","dok":2,"q":"Moving from the right atrium toward the apex of the heart, in what order do you meet these structures: bundle branches, SA node, AV bundle, AV node?","a":"SA node, AV node, AV bundle, bundle branches.","options":["SA node, AV node, AV bundle, bundle branches","SA node, AV bundle, AV node, bundle branches","AV bundle, SA node, AV node, bundle branches","AV node, SA node, AV bundle, bundle branches"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The SA node is highest, in the upper right atrial wall near the superior vena cava. The AV node is lower, in the floor of the right atrium near the interatrial septum. Below that, the AV bundle sits in the superior interventricular septum, and the bundle branches run down the septum toward the apex, so location and signal order match.","notes":["Correct. This order runs from the top of the right atrium down the septum toward the apex.","This order places the AV bundle above the AV node. The AV node is still in the right atrium, while the AV bundle has already crossed into the interventricular septum.","This order starts with the AV bundle, which actually lies below both nodes in the septum. Students who confuse the AV bundle with the AV node tend to place it too high.","This order puts the AV node above the SA node. The SA node sits higher, near the opening of the superior vena cava, while the AV node is down in the atrial floor."],"lt":"t-cardiac-conduction","lid":"c36","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L01617","dok":2,"q":"Which choice correctly matches the three normal ECG deflections to their conduction events, in the order they occur during one heartbeat?","a":"P wave for atrial spread, QRS complex for ventricular spread, T wave for ventricular recovery.","options":["QRS complex for atrial spread, P wave for ventricular spread, T wave for ventricular recovery","P wave for ventricular spread, QRS complex for atrial spread, T wave for atrial recovery","P wave for atrial spread, QRS complex for ventricular spread, T wave for ventricular recovery","T wave for atrial spread, P wave for ventricular spread, QRS complex for ventricular recovery"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Each heartbeat on an ECG shows three deflections in order. The small P wave shows the signal spreading across the atria, the tall QRS complex shows it spreading across the ventricles, and the T wave shows the ventricles electrically recovering. The order of the waves follows the order of the conduction pathway, from the atria down to the ventricles.","notes":["This match swaps the P wave and the QRS complex. The P wave comes first because the atria receive the signal first, and the larger QRS complex reflects the bigger muscle mass of the ventricles.","This match reverses the atria and ventricles for the first two waves and assigns the T wave to the atria. Atrial recovery is hidden inside the QRS complex, so the T wave belongs to the ventricles.","Correct. P is atrial spread, QRS is ventricular spread, and T is ventricular recovery.","This match shifts every label by one position. The heartbeat starts with the P wave for the atria, not the T wave, which is always the last deflection."],"lt":"t-cardiac-conduction","lid":"c38","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L01618","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly places the AV node in the conduction pathway?","a":"After the signal crosses the atria and before the AV bundle.","options":["After the signal crosses the atria and before the AV bundle","After the SA node fires and before the signal crosses the atria","After the AV bundle and before the bundle branches","After the bundle branches and before the Purkinje fibers"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The SA node fires first and the signal spreads across both atria. It then reaches the AV node, which holds it briefly, and passes it on to the AV bundle in the interventricular septum. The AV node is therefore the link between the atrial part of the pathway and the septal part.","notes":["Correct. The AV node receives the signal after it crosses the atria and passes it to the AV bundle.","The signal goes straight from the SA node across the atrial muscle, so the AV node cannot come before that spread. Students pick this when they think the two nodes connect directly, but the atria are between them.","Nothing sits between the AV bundle and the bundle branches, because the bundle simply divides into them. This placement would be right for a question about where the AV bundle splits.","The bundle branches pass the signal straight to the Purkinje fibers near the apex, with no node between them. This placement mixes up the AV node, which is in the right atrium, with the lower parts of the pathway."],"lt":"t-cardiac-conduction","lid":"c39","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L01619","dok":2,"q":"Which choice gives the order of the conduction structures within the interventricular septum and then the structure the signal reaches right after leaving the septum?","a":"AV bundle then bundle branches in the septum, then the Purkinje fibers in the ventricle walls.","options":["Bundle branches then AV bundle in the septum, then the Purkinje fibers in the ventricle walls","AV bundle then Purkinje fibers in the septum, then the bundle branches in the ventricle walls","AV bundle then bundle branches in the septum, then the Purkinje fibers in the ventricle walls","AV node then AV bundle in the septum, then the bundle branches in the ventricle walls"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Inside the interventricular septum, the AV bundle comes first and then divides into the right and left bundle branches, which run down toward the apex. From there the signal passes to the Purkinje fibers, which spread through the walls of both ventricles. This is the lower half of the conduction pathway, after the AV node.","notes":["This order reverses the septal structures. The AV bundle is the single trunk that splits into the branches, so it must come before them.","This order swaps the bundle branches and the Purkinje fibers. The Purkinje fibers are the last structures, spread through the ventricle walls, while the bundle branches stay in the septum.","Correct. The AV bundle and then the bundle branches lie in the septum, and the Purkinje fibers come next.","The AV node is not in the interventricular septum; it sits in the floor of the right atrium. The bundle branches also run in the septum, not out in the ventricle walls."],"lt":"t-cardiac-conduction","lid":"c40","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L01622","dok":1,"q":"What is the heart's built-in ability to generate its own rhythm called?","a":"Autorhythmicity.","options":["Autorhythmicity","Excitability","Conductivity","Contractility"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Autorhythmicity is the heart's ability to generate its own rhythm without a signal from the nervous system. The word breaks down as auto (self) plus rhythm, so it means self-rhythm. This property belongs to the conduction system, especially the SA node, which is why a transplanted heart with its nerves cut still beats.","notes":["Correct. Autorhythmicity means the heart sets its own rhythm.","Excitability is the ability of a cell to respond to a stimulus. It would be the answer if the question asked about a cell's ability to react when a signal arrives, rather than to start the rhythm on its own.","Conductivity is the ability to pass a signal along from cell to cell. Choose it if the question asked about spreading the signal rather than generating it.","Contractility is the ability of muscle to shorten and produce force. Pick it if the question asked what lets the heart muscle squeeze once it receives a signal."],"lt":"t-cardiac-conduction","lid":"c3-x2","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L01623","dok":1,"q":"A textbook refers to the bundle of His. Which conduction structure does this name identify?","a":"The atrioventricular bundle.","options":["The atrioventricular node","The Purkinje fibers","The bundle branches","The atrioventricular bundle"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The bundle of His is another name for the atrioventricular (AV) bundle. It is the short strand of conducting cells that carries the signal from the AV node into the superior interventricular septum. It is the only electrical link between the atria and the ventricles, and just below it the pathway splits into the right and left bundle branches.","notes":["The AV node is the structure just before the bundle of His, in the floor of the right atrium. Students mix them up because both names start with atrioventricular, but the node delays the signal while the bundle carries it into the septum.","The Purkinje fibers are the last part of the pathway, spread through the ventricle walls. Pick them if the question asked which fibers deliver the signal to the ventricular muscle.","The bundle branches are the right and left divisions that the bundle of His splits into. They would be correct if the question asked what comes right after the bundle of His.","Correct. The bundle of His and the AV bundle are the same structure."],"lt":"t-cardiac-conduction","lid":"c4-x2","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L01627","dok":1,"q":"What does an electrocardiogram (ECG) record?","a":"The heart's electrical activity, recorded from the body surface.","options":["The oxygen level in the blood","The heart's electrical activity","The volume pumped with each beat","The pressure inside the chambers"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"An electrocardiogram, also called an ECG or EKG, is a tracing of the heart's electrical activity recorded through electrodes on the skin. Each deflection of a heartbeat lines up with an event in the conduction system, such as the signal spreading across the atria or the ventricles. A clinician reads it to judge whether the heart's rhythm and timing are normal.","notes":["Blood oxygen is measured with a pulse oximeter on a fingertip or with a blood sample. It would be the answer if the question asked what a pulse oximeter checks.","Correct. An ECG records electrical activity through electrodes on the skin.","The volume pumped per beat is called the stroke volume, and it is estimated with imaging or other tests rather than read off an ECG. Students confuse the two because both describe the heartbeat, but the ECG shows only electrical events.","Chamber pressure is measured with a catheter placed inside the heart, not with skin electrodes. Pick it if the question asked what a cardiac catheter measures."],"lt":"t-cardiac-conduction","lid":"c9-x2","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L01628","dok":1,"q":"Which ECG deflection lines up with the signal spreading across the atria?","a":"The P wave.","options":["The flat line after the P wave","The T wave","The P wave","The QRS complex"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The P wave is the first small bump of each heartbeat. It lines up with the signal spreading across both atria right after the SA node fires, and the atria contract just after it. It is small because the atria have relatively little muscle compared with the ventricles.","notes":["The flat line after the P wave mostly reflects the brief pause at the AV node, after the atria have already received the signal. Choose it if the question asked where the AV node delay appears.","The T wave is the last deflection and shows the ventricles electrically recovering. It would be correct if the question asked which deflection comes before the ventricles relax.","Correct. The P wave shows the signal crossing the atria.","The QRS complex is the tall spike that shows the signal spreading across the ventricles. Pick it if the question asked about the ventricles rather than the atria."],"lt":"t-cardiac-conduction","lid":"c10-x2","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L01629","dok":2,"q":"On an ECG, the QRS complex is much taller than the P wave. Why?","a":"The ventricles contain much more muscle than the atria.","options":["The ventricles contain much more muscle than the atria","The AV node strengthens the signal before passing it on","The SA node fires a second, stronger signal for the ventricles","The Purkinje fibers add an extra beat of their own"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The QRS complex shows the signal spreading across the ventricles, and the P wave shows it spreading across the atria. The ventricles have far thicker, more muscular walls than the atria because they pump blood out of the heart, so far more cells carry the signal at once. More muscle produces a larger electrical signal at the skin, which is why the QRS complex is taller.","notes":["Correct. The larger muscle mass of the ventricles produces the larger deflection.","The AV node does not strengthen the signal; it delays it briefly so the atria can finish emptying. Students pick this when they confuse the node's timing role with boosting the signal.","The SA node fires once per heartbeat, and that single signal travels through the whole pathway. This choice comes from imagining separate signals for the atria and ventricles, which the conduction system does not use.","The Purkinje fibers spread the same signal through the ventricle walls rather than creating an extra beat. Pick them if the question asked which structures hand the signal to the ventricular muscle."],"lt":"t-cardiac-conduction","lid":"c11-x2","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L01630","dok":2,"q":"On an ECG, which conduction event does the T wave mark?","a":"The ventricles electrically recovering.","options":["The ventricles electrically recovering","The signal spreading across the atria","The atria contracting","The signal pausing at the AV node"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The T wave is the last deflection of each heartbeat, after the QRS complex. It marks the ventricles electrically recovering, resetting so they can respond to the next signal. The ventricles relax right after the T wave.","notes":["Correct. The T wave marks ventricular recovery.","The signal spreading across the atria is shown by the P wave, the first small bump. It would be correct if the question asked what the P wave shows.","The atria contract after the P wave, at the start of the heartbeat, not at the end. Pick this event if the question asked what follows the P wave.","The pause at the AV node appears as the flat line between the P wave and the QRS complex, not as a wave. Choose it if the question asked where the AV node delay shows on an ECG."],"lt":"t-cardiac-conduction","lid":"c12-x2","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L01631","dok":1,"q":"What is an arrhythmia?","a":"Any deviation from the normal heart rhythm.","options":["Any deviation from the normal heart rhythm","A blocked coronary artery","A leaking heart valve","Fluid collecting in the pericardial cavity"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"An arrhythmia is any deviation from the normal heart rhythm. It is the umbrella term for the conduction disorders, including tachycardia, bradycardia, heart block, fibrillation and an ectopic pacemaker. The word itself means without rhythm, which points to a problem with the heart's electrical timing rather than its plumbing.","notes":["Correct. Arrhythmia covers any abnormal heart rhythm.","A blocked coronary artery cuts off blood to part of the heart wall, which can cause a heart attack. Pick it if the question asked about a disease of the vessels that feed the heart muscle.","A leaking heart valve lets blood flow backward and is a valve problem, not a rhythm problem. It would be the answer if the question asked about valve disease or a heart murmur.","Fluid in the pericardial cavity is a problem of the sac around the heart. Choose it if the question asked about a buildup of fluid between the layers of the pericardium."],"lt":"t-cardiac-conduction","lid":"c13-x2","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L01632","dok":1,"q":"What is tachycardia?","a":"An abnormally fast resting heart rate.","options":["An abnormally fast resting heart rate","An abnormally slow resting heart rate","A signal delayed or stopped at the AV node","A site outside the SA node taking over the rhythm"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Tachycardia is an abnormally fast resting heart rate; tachy means fast. The concern is that when the heart beats too fast, the chambers may not have enough time to fill with blood between beats. It is one of the specific arrhythmias.","notes":["Correct. Tachy means fast, so tachycardia is a fast resting heart rate.","An abnormally slow resting heart rate is bradycardia, the opposite of tachycardia. Pick it if the question asked what bradycardia means.","A signal delayed or stopped at the AV node is heart block. It would be the answer if the question asked about the atria and ventricles beating out of step.","A site outside the SA node taking over is an ectopic pacemaker. Choose it if the question asked about the beat starting from the wrong place."],"lt":"t-cardiac-conduction","lid":"c14-x2","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L01633","dok":1,"q":"What is bradycardia?","a":"An abnormally slow resting heart rate.","options":["Rapid, disorganized signals in the atria","An abnormally slow resting heart rate","Rapid, disorganized signals in the ventricles","An abnormally fast resting heart rate"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Bradycardia is an abnormally slow resting heart rate; brady means slow. The concern is that a heart beating too slowly may not send enough blood to the body. If the SA node is failing, an artificial pacemaker can take over its job.","notes":["Rapid, disorganized signals in the atria describe atrial fibrillation, where the atria quiver. It would be the answer if the question asked about atrial fibrillation.","Correct. Brady means slow, so bradycardia is a slow resting heart rate.","Rapid, disorganized signals in the ventricles describe ventricular fibrillation, a life-threatening emergency. Choose it if the question asked about the ventricles quivering and failing to pump.","An abnormally fast resting heart rate is tachycardia. Pick it if the question asked what tachycardia means."],"lt":"t-cardiac-conduction","lid":"c15-x2","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L01634","dok":2,"q":"After the SA node fires, the signal spreads across the atria. Which conduction structure does it reach next?","a":"The atrioventricular node.","options":["The Purkinje fibers","The bundle branches","The AV node","The AV bundle"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Once the signal has spread across both atria, it collects at the AV node in the floor of the right atrium. The AV node holds the signal briefly so the atria can finish emptying, then passes it on. The pathway continues from there to the AV bundle, the bundle branches, and the Purkinje fibers.","notes":["The Purkinje fibers are the last stop, in the ventricle walls, several steps later. They would be correct if the question asked which structure follows the bundle branches.","The bundle branches run down the interventricular septum, two steps after the AV node. Choose them if the question asked what the AV bundle divides into.","Correct. The AV node is the next stop after the atria.","The AV bundle comes right after the AV node, in the superior interventricular septum. Pick it if the question asked where the signal goes after it leaves the AV node."],"lt":"t-cardiac-conduction","lid":"c16-x2","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L01635","dok":2,"q":"Why does the signal pause briefly at the AV node?","a":"To give the atria time to finish emptying into the ventricles.","options":["To give the ventricles time to empty before the atria contract","To let the ventricles recover electrically before the next beat","To give the atria time to finish emptying into the ventricles","To let the atria and ventricles contract at the same moment"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The atria contract first and push their last portion of blood down into the ventricles. The brief hold at the AV node keeps the ventricles from being signaled until that filling is finished. Without the delay, the ventricles would contract before they were fully loaded with blood.","notes":["This reverses the real order. The atria contract first and the ventricles second, so the delay protects ventricular filling, not ventricular emptying.","Ventricular recovery happens later in the beat and appears as the T wave. Pick it if the question asked what the T wave represents.","Correct. The delay lets the atria finish filling the ventricles before the ventricles contract.","The pause does the opposite, separating atrial and ventricular contraction in time. Students pick this when they assume the whole heart squeezes at once, but the chambers contract in sequence."],"lt":"t-cardiac-conduction","lid":"c17-x2","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L01636","dok":2,"q":"From the AV bundle, the signal travels down both sides of the interventricular septum. Which structures carry it?","a":"The bundle branches.","options":["The Purkinje fibers","The chordae tendineae","The bundle branches","The cardiac plexus"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The AV bundle splits into the right and left bundle branches at the top of the interventricular septum. They run down either side of the septum toward the apex, carrying the signal to both ventricles. At the apex they pass the signal to the Purkinje fibers.","notes":["The Purkinje fibers come next, spreading through the ventricle walls rather than running down the septum. Pick them if the question asked which structures reach the contractile cells.","The chordae tendineae are tendon-like cords that tie the AV valve cusps to the papillary muscles. Students confuse them with conduction fibers because both are cord-like structures inside the ventricles, but the chordae keep the valves from flipping into the atria.","Correct. The right and left bundle branches carry the signal down the septum.","The cardiac plexus is a network of autonomic nerve fibers near the base of the heart, not part of the conduction pathway. It would be the answer if the question asked how sympathetic and parasympathetic nerves reach the nodes."],"lt":"t-cardiac-conduction","lid":"c18-x2","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L01637","dok":2,"q":"Which conduction structure spreads the signal upward from the apex through the ventricle walls, so the ventricles contract from the apex upward?","a":"The Purkinje fibers.","options":["The AV bundle","The Purkinje fibers","The AV node","The SA node"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The bundle branches carry the signal down the septum to the apex, and the Purkinje fibers then spread it upward through the walls of both ventricles. Because the muscle near the apex receives the signal first, the ventricles squeeze from the bottom up. This pushes blood toward the pulmonary trunk and aorta, which leave from the top of the ventricles.","notes":["The AV bundle carries the signal into the top of the interventricular septum, well before it reaches the apex. Choose it if the question asked for the only electrical link between the atria and the ventricles.","Correct. The Purkinje fibers spread the signal up through the ventricle walls from the apex.","The AV node sits in the floor of the right atrium and briefly delays the signal. It would be correct if the question asked where the signal pauses.","The SA node starts the signal at the top of the right atrium and never reaches into the ventricle walls. Pick it if the question asked which structure fires first."],"lt":"t-cardiac-conduction","lid":"c19-x2","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L01639","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly describes the role of the autonomic nerves that reach the heart?","a":"They adjust the rate that the SA node already sets.","options":["They generate each heartbeat, and the SA node relays it","They start the heartbeat only during exercise","They adjust the rate that the SA node already sets","They supply the heart's blood vessels but not its nodes"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The heartbeat is generated by the conduction system itself, starting at the SA node, a property called autorhythmicity. Sympathetic and parasympathetic nerves reach the SA and AV nodes through the cardiac plexus and only speed up or slow down that existing rhythm. A transplanted heart, whose nerves have been cut, still beats for this reason.","notes":["This reverses the roles. The SA node generates each beat on its own, and the nerves only change how fast it fires.","The heart beats on its own at rest and during exercise. During exercise, sympathetic nerves speed up the SA node, but they still are not starting the beats; students pick this when they confuse speeding up the beat with creating it.","Correct. The nerves modulate the rate but do not start the beat.","The autonomic fibers do reach the SA and AV nodes through the cardiac plexus. This choice comes from thinking the heart's own rhythm means it has no nerve supply to its conduction tissue at all."],"lt":"t-cardiac-conduction","lid":"c21-x2","comp":65,"competencyId":"cv-cardiac-nerves"},{"id":"L01640","dok":1,"q":"Which conduction structure sits in the superior part of the interventricular septum?","a":"The atrioventricular bundle.","options":["The SA node","The Purkinje fibers","The AV bundle","The AV node"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The AV bundle, or bundle of His, leaves the AV node and enters the superior part of the interventricular septum, the wall between the ventricles. It is the only electrical bridge across the fibrous skeleton from the atria to the ventricles. A little lower in the septum it divides into the right and left bundle branches.","notes":["The SA node sits high in the right atrial wall near the opening of the superior vena cava. Pick it if the question asked where the pacemaker is.","The Purkinje fibers spread through the walls of both ventricles rather than sitting at the top of the septum. They would be correct if the question asked which structures reach the ventricular muscle.","Correct. The AV bundle lies in the upper interventricular septum.","The AV node is just above the AV bundle, but it is still in the floor of the right atrium near the interatrial septum. Students mix up the two because their names are nearly the same, so remember the node is atrial and the bundle is septal."],"lt":"t-cardiac-conduction","lid":"c22-x2","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L01642","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares atrial fibrillation and ventricular fibrillation?","a":"In atrial fibrillation the ventricles keep pumping, while ventricular fibrillation stops the heart from moving blood.","options":["Both are slow, organized rhythms that differ only in which chamber sets the pace","In atrial fibrillation the ventricles keep pumping, while ventricular fibrillation stops blood flow","In ventricular fibrillation the heart keeps pumping, while atrial fibrillation stops blood flow","Both stop the heart from moving blood and are equally life-threatening"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Both disorders are rapid, disorganized signaling that makes chambers quiver instead of contracting, and the name tells you which chambers are affected. In atrial fibrillation only the atria quiver, so the ventricles still pump and the condition is common and treatable. In ventricular fibrillation the main pumping chambers quiver and move no blood, so it is a life-threatening emergency treated with a defibrillator.","notes":["Fibrillation is fast and chaotic, not slow and organized. A slow, organized rhythm would describe bradycardia, and a different site setting the pace would describe an ectopic pacemaker.","Correct. Quivering atria still allow ventricular pumping, but quivering ventricles do not.","This reverses the two disorders. The ventricles are the main pumps, so losing them in ventricular fibrillation is what stops blood flow, while quivering atria still let blood reach the ventricles.","Only ventricular fibrillation stops blood flow. Students pick this because both share the word fibrillation, but the atria contribute only the final top-up of ventricular filling, so the heart keeps pumping in atrial fibrillation."],"lt":"t-cardiac-conduction","lid":"c24-x2","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L01643","dok":1,"q":"What is an ectopic pacemaker?","a":"A site outside the SA node that takes over the heart's rhythm.","options":["A blocked coronary artery","A small implanted electronic device","The heart's normal pacemaker in the right atrium","A site outside the SA node that takes over the rhythm"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Ectopic means out of place. An ectopic pacemaker is a site outside the SA node that starts firing and takes over the heart's rhythm. When this happens, the normal pacing order of the heart is lost because the beat no longer starts at the SA node.","notes":["A blocked coronary artery reduces blood supply to the heart wall and is a vessel problem, not a rhythm disorder. Choose it if the question asked about the cause of a heart attack.","A small implanted device that delivers timed signals is an artificial pacemaker. Pick it if the question asked what can replace a failing SA node or carry the signal past a block.","The heart's normal pacemaker in the right atrium is the SA node itself. It would be the answer if the question asked which structure normally sets the rhythm.","Correct. An ectopic pacemaker is a site outside the SA node setting the rhythm."],"lt":"t-cardiac-conduction","lid":"c25-x2","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L01644","dok":2,"q":"A patient's SA node is failing. An implanted device delivers a timed electrical signal to restore an ordered beat. What is this device?","a":"An artificial pacemaker.","options":["A stent","An artificial pacemaker","A replacement heart valve","A defibrillator"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"An artificial pacemaker is a small implanted device that delivers a timed electrical signal to the heart. It takes over the job of a failing SA node, or carries the signal past a block at the AV node, so the heart keeps an ordered beat. It does the same work as the natural pacemaker, just electronically.","notes":["A stent is a small mesh tube placed inside a narrowed artery, such as a coronary artery, to hold it open. It would be the answer if the question described a blocked vessel rather than a rhythm problem.","Correct. An artificial pacemaker supplies the timed signal a failing SA node cannot.","A replacement heart valve fixes a valve that leaks or will not open fully. Choose it if the question described a valve problem, not a failing pacemaker.","A defibrillator delivers a single strong shock to stop chaotic signals, as in ventricular fibrillation. Pick it if the question described resetting a quivering heart rather than pacing a slow or failing one."],"lt":"t-cardiac-conduction","lid":"c26-x2","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L01645","dok":2,"q":"In ventricular fibrillation the ventricles only quiver and move no blood. A shock is used to stop all the chaotic signals at once. What is this treatment?","a":"Defibrillation.","options":["Defibrillation","Coronary bypass surgery","Valve repair","Pacemaker implantation"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Defibrillation delivers a shock that stops all the chaotic signals in the ventricles at once. With the disorganized activity cleared, the SA node has a chance to restart the heart in its normal rhythm. The name means removing fibrillation, which is the quivering it treats.","notes":["Correct. Defibrillation uses a shock to stop the chaotic signals so the SA node can take over again.","Coronary bypass surgery reroutes blood around a blocked coronary artery using a graft vessel. It would be the answer if the question described restoring blood supply to the heart wall.","Valve repair fixes a heart valve that leaks or is narrowed. Choose it if the question described a valve problem rather than an electrical one.","Pacemaker implantation places a device that delivers timed signals over the long term for a failing SA node or a heart block. Pick it if the question described a slow or blocked rhythm rather than chaotic quivering."],"lt":"t-cardiac-conduction","lid":"c27-x2","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L01646","dok":2,"q":"Following one heartbeat's signal in order, it has just left the AV node. Which structure carries it next?","a":"The atrioventricular bundle.","options":["The Purkinje fibers","The bundle branches","The AV bundle","The SA node"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"After its brief pause at the AV node, the signal enters the AV bundle, which carries it across the fibrous skeleton into the superior interventricular septum. From there it continues to the bundle branches and then the Purkinje fibers. The AV bundle is the only route from the atria to the ventricles.","notes":["The Purkinje fibers are the last structures in the pathway, two steps after the AV bundle. They would be correct if the question asked what follows the bundle branches.","The bundle branches come after the AV bundle, not directly after the AV node. Choose them if the question asked what the AV bundle divides into.","Correct. The AV bundle is the next stop after the AV node.","The SA node is where the signal started, before it crossed the atria and reached the AV node. Pick it if the question asked which structure fires first."],"lt":"t-cardiac-conduction","lid":"c28-x2","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L01647","dok":2,"q":"An ECG shows a normal P wave that is not followed by a QRS complex, because the signal is delayed or stopped at the AV node. What disorder does this suggest?","a":"Heart block.","options":["Tachycardia","Ectopic pacemaker","Atrial fibrillation","Heart block"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"A normal P wave means the SA node fired and the signal crossed the atria as usual. A missing QRS complex means the signal never reached the ventricles. When the signal is delayed or stopped at the AV node, the disorder is heart block, and the atria and ventricles can beat out of step.","notes":["Tachycardia is a fast resting heart rate, and each P wave would still be followed by a QRS complex. Pick it if the ECG showed normal beats occurring too quickly.","An ectopic pacemaker means a site outside the SA node starts the beat, so the problem is where the signal begins, not a stop at the AV node. It would be the answer if the beat were described as starting in the wrong place.","Atrial fibrillation replaces the normal P waves with chaotic atrial activity, so there would be no normal P wave at all. Choose it if the question described the atria quivering.","Correct. A normal atrial signal that fails to reach the ventricles points to heart block at the AV node."],"lt":"t-cardiac-conduction","lid":"c29-x2","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L01649","dok":2,"q":"A transplanted heart has had its nerves cut, yet it keeps beating. What explains this?","a":"The autorhythmicity of the heart's own conduction system.","options":["The autorhythmicity of the conduction system","The contractility of the ventricular muscle","Input from the vagus nerve","Input from the sympathetic nerves"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Autorhythmicity is the heart's ability to generate its own rhythm, and it belongs to the conduction system, starting with the SA node. Cutting the nerves during a transplant removes only the outside signals that speed up or slow down the rate. The SA node keeps firing on its own, so the heart keeps beating.","notes":["Correct. The conduction system generates the beat on its own, with no nerve input needed.","Contractility is the muscle's ability to squeeze once a signal arrives, but it does not create the signal. Pick it if the question asked what lets the ventricles produce force when stimulated.","The vagus nerve carries parasympathetic fibers that slow the heart, and it has also been cut in a transplant. Choose it if the question asked which nerve slows the heart rate.","The sympathetic nerves only speed up the heart, and in a transplanted heart they have been cut. They would be the answer if the question asked which nerves raise the heart rate in an intact heart."],"lt":"t-cardiac-conduction","lid":"d3-t-cardiac-conduction-1","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L01650","dok":3,"q":"If the AV node passed the signal on with no delay at all, what would most likely go wrong?","a":"The ventricles would contract before the atria finished filling them.","options":["The SA node would stop firing","The Purkinje fibers would send the signal back to the atria","The atria would contract after the ventricles","The ventricles would contract before they finished filling"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Normally the AV node holds the signal for a fraction of a second while the atria finish squeezing their blood into the ventricles. Without that delay, the ventricles would be signaled almost at the same moment as the atria. They would start contracting before they were fully loaded, so less blood would be pumped out with each beat.","notes":["The SA node fires on its own, upstream of the AV node, so a change at the AV node would not stop it. This choice confuses the delaying node with the pacemaker.","The conduction pathway carries the signal one way, from the atria down to the ventricles, and the fibrous skeleton blocks any return route. Students pick this when they imagine the signal looping back through the heart.","The signal still reaches the atria first, so the atria would not contract after the ventricles. Without the delay the two would contract nearly together, which is the real problem.","Correct. The delay exists to let the atria finish filling the ventricles first."],"lt":"t-cardiac-conduction","lid":"d3-t-cardiac-conduction-2","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L01651","dok":3,"q":"The Purkinje fibers make the ventricles contract from the apex upward. Why does starting the squeeze at the bottom help the heart pump?","a":"It drives blood up toward the pulmonary trunk and aorta, which leave from the top of the ventricles.","options":["It drives blood up toward the pulmonary trunk and aorta at the top","It pushes blood back up into the atria through the AV valves","It sends the signal back to the SA node to trigger the next beat","It lets the ventricles keep filling while they contract"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The exits from the ventricles, the pulmonary trunk and the aorta, both leave from the top of the heart near the base. Squeezing from the apex upward works like rolling up a tube of toothpaste from the closed end, pushing blood toward those openings. If the top contracted first, blood would be trapped toward the apex.","notes":["Correct. Squeezing from the bottom up pushes blood toward the great arteries at the top.","The AV valves close when the ventricles contract, which prevents blood from flowing back into the atria. Pick the AV valves if the question asked what keeps blood from moving backward during ventricular contraction.","The signal does not travel back to the SA node; the SA node fires on its own for each new beat. Students pick this when they imagine the conduction pathway as a loop.","The ventricles fill while they are relaxed, not while they contract. This choice mixes up the filling phase with the pumping phase of the heartbeat."],"lt":"t-cardiac-conduction","lid":"d3-t-cardiac-conduction-3","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L01652","dok":2,"q":"Cardiac muscle cells pass the signal directly from one cell to the next so a chamber contracts as one unit. Which junctions within the intercalated discs make this possible?","a":"Gap junctions.","options":["Gap junctions","Sarcoplasmic reticulum","T tubules","Desmosomes"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Intercalated discs are the specialized junctions where neighboring cardiac muscle cells meet end to end. Within them, gap junctions are small channels that let the signal pass directly from one cell to the next. This electrical coupling lets the whole chamber contract together in one coordinated squeeze.","notes":["Correct. Gap junctions let the signal pass directly from cell to cell.","The sarcoplasmic reticulum is the network inside a muscle cell that stores and releases calcium. Choose it if the question asked which organelle stores calcium in a muscle cell.","T tubules are inward folds of the cell membrane that carry the signal deep into a single muscle cell. They would be the answer if the question asked how the signal reaches the interior of one fiber, not how it crosses between cells.","Desmosomes are also found in the intercalated discs, but they are anchoring junctions that keep the cells from pulling apart as they contract. Pick them if the question asked what holds neighboring cardiac cells together mechanically."],"lt":"t-cardiac-conduction","lid":"d3-t-cardiac-conduction-4","comp":60,"competencyId":"cv-cardiac-muscle"},{"id":"L01653","dok":3,"q":"The fibrous skeleton between the atria and ventricles does not conduct signals, leaving the AV node and AV bundle as the only route through. What does this arrangement accomplish?","a":"Every signal passes through the AV node delay, so the atria contract before the ventricles.","options":["It makes all four chambers contract at the same moment","Every signal passes the AV node delay, so the atria contract first","It lets the signal reach the ventricles at many points at once","It keeps the signal from ever reaching the ventricular muscle"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The fibrous skeleton is a framework of dense connective tissue that electrically insulates the atria from the ventricles. Because the signal cannot cross anywhere else, it must pass through the AV node, where it is delayed, and then the AV bundle. This single gateway guarantees the atria contract first and the ventricles second, in the right order.","notes":["The arrangement does the opposite, separating atrial and ventricular contraction in time. Students pick this when they assume the whole heart squeezes at once.","Correct. The single gateway forces the signal through the AV node delay, keeping the chambers in order.","Without the fibrous skeleton the signal might leak across at many points, but the insulation prevents exactly that. This choice describes what would happen if the barrier were missing.","The signal does reach the ventricles, through the AV bundle, bundle branches and Purkinje fibers. A complete stop at the AV node would describe severe heart block, not the normal arrangement."],"lt":"t-cardiac-conduction","lid":"d3-t-cardiac-conduction-5","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L01654","dok":3,"q":"During exercise, a person's heart rate climbs from 70 to 140 beats per minute. Which statement best explains the change?","a":"Sympathetic nerves made the SA node fire faster.","options":["The AV node took over as the pacemaker","The nervous system began generating each heartbeat directly","Sympathetic nerves made the SA node fire faster","The vagus nerve made the SA node fire faster"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The SA node keeps setting the rhythm during exercise, but sympathetic nerves reaching it through the cardiac plexus speed up its firing. The nerves adjust the rate to match the body's needs; they do not create the beats. This split lets the heart keep a steady beat on its own while the nervous system fine-tunes the pace.","notes":["The AV node takes over only as a slower backup if the SA node fails, so it would lower the rate, not double it. Choose it if the question described a failing SA node.","The nervous system never generates the heartbeat; the SA node does, through autorhythmicity. Students pick this when they confuse adjusting the rate with creating the beat.","Correct. Sympathetic input speeds up the SA node.","The vagus nerve carries parasympathetic fibers that slow the heart, not speed it up. Pick it if the question asked what brings the heart rate back down at rest."],"lt":"t-cardiac-conduction","lid":"d3-t-cardiac-conduction-6","comp":65,"competencyId":"cv-cardiac-nerves"},{"id":"L01655","dok":2,"q":"If the SA node stops firing, which structure is next in line to take over as the heart's pacemaker?","a":"The atrioventricular node.","options":["The vagus nerve","The AV node","The medulla oblongata","The cardiac plexus"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Cells of the conduction system other than the SA node can also fire on their own, only more slowly. The AV node is the next fastest, so if the SA node fails it takes over as a backup pacemaker at a slower rate, about 40 to 60 beats a minute. This keeps the heart beating, though more slowly, until the problem is treated.","notes":["The vagus nerve carries parasympathetic fibers that slow the heart and cannot start beats. Choose it if the question asked which nerve slows the heart rate.","Correct. The AV node is the backup pacemaker when the SA node fails.","The medulla oblongata holds the cardiac centers that adjust the rate through autonomic nerves, but it cannot generate the heartbeat. Pick it if the question asked where the brain's cardiac centers are.","The cardiac plexus is a network of autonomic nerve fibers that carries signals to the nodes; it does not generate a rhythm. It would be the answer if the question asked how nerves reach the SA and AV nodes."],"lt":"t-cardiac-conduction","lid":"d3x-t-cardiac-conduction-1","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L01656","dok":2,"q":"An ECG uses electrodes on the skin to record the heartbeat. What are the electrodes actually detecting?","a":"The heart's electrical currents, which spread through the body's tissues and fluids to the skin.","options":["The signals traveling in the vagus nerve","The pressure of each beat pushing on the chest wall","The sound of the heart valves closing","The heart's electrical currents spreading to the skin"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Each heartbeat produces electrical currents as the signal spreads through the conduction system and heart muscle. Body tissues and fluids conduct electricity, so these currents spread outward and can be picked up by electrodes on the skin. The ECG tracing is a record of those currents over each heartbeat.","notes":["Nerve signals in the vagus are far too small to show up on an ECG, and the vagus only adjusts the rate. The ECG reflects the much larger electrical activity of the heart muscle itself.","The force of each beat is felt as the pulse in an artery or measured as blood pressure, not detected by ECG electrodes. This choice mixes up the heart's mechanical action with its electrical activity.","The sounds of the valves closing are heard with a stethoscope, not recorded by ECG electrodes. Pick this if the question asked what a clinician listens to when checking heart sounds.","Correct. The electrodes pick up electrical currents from the heart."],"lt":"t-cardiac-conduction","lid":"d3x-t-cardiac-conduction-2","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"N-m3-conduction-1","dok":3,"q":"A blockage in which coronary artery is most likely to cause a rhythm problem by cutting off blood to the SA node, in most people?","a":"The right coronary artery.","options":["The coronary sinus","The left marginal artery","The anterior interventricular artery","The right coronary artery"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"In most people, the SA node receives its blood supply from the right coronary artery, which also usually supplies the AV node. If the right coronary artery is blocked, the SA node can be starved of oxygen along with the heart wall it supplies. That is why a right coronary artery occlusion can cause a rhythm problem as well as damage to the heart muscle.","notes":["The coronary sinus is a large vein that drains blood away from the heart wall into the right atrium, so it delivers no blood to the SA node. Choose it if the question asked where the cardiac veins empty.","The left marginal artery is a branch along the left border of the heart, supplying the wall of the left ventricle. It would be the answer if the question asked which branch runs along the left margin.","The anterior interventricular artery, also called the LAD, runs down the front of the heart and supplies the anterior septum and much of the left ventricle. Pick it if the question asked which artery runs in the anterior interventricular sulcus.","Correct. The right coronary artery usually supplies the SA node."],"comp":63,"competencyId":"cv-coronary"},{"id":"L03373","dok":1,"q":"What does the moderator band carry?","a":"Part of the conduction system to the anterior papillary muscle.","options":["Arterial blood to the interventricular septum","Conduction fibers to the anterior papillary muscle","Chordae tendineae of the tricuspid valve","Venous blood from the heart wall"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The moderator band carries part of the heart's conduction system, from the right bundle branch, across the right ventricle to the anterior papillary muscle. This shortcut lets the papillary muscle contract at the right time to hold the tricuspid valve shut as the ventricle contracts. It is a muscular band, not a vessel or a cord.","notes":["Arterial blood to the septum comes from the interventricular branches of the coronary arteries. Pick the anterior interventricular artery for the front of the septum.","Correct. The moderator band carries conduction fibers to the anterior papillary muscle.","The chordae tendineae run from the papillary muscles to the valve cusps on their own. The moderator band attaches to the base of a papillary muscle, not to the cords.","Venous blood from the heart wall is carried by the cardiac veins and coronary sinus. Pick the coronary sinus for that drainage."],"lt":"t-heart-gross-anatomy","lid":"W3-047","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L03475","dok":1,"q":"What is a cardiomyocyte?","a":"A cardiac muscle cell.","options":["A fibroblast of the pericardium","A cardiac muscle cell","A smooth muscle cell","A skeletal muscle fiber"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"A cardiomyocyte is a cardiac muscle cell. The name breaks down as cardio (heart), myo (muscle), and cyte (cell). Cardiomyocytes are short, branching cells, usually with a single central nucleus, and they join end to end at intercalated discs to build the myocardium.","notes":["A fibroblast is a connective tissue cell that makes fibers, and fibroblasts are found in the fibrous pericardium. It fits a question about the cells that build connective tissue, but it is not a muscle cell and does not contract.","Correct. Cardiomyocyte literally means heart muscle cell.","A smooth muscle cell is a spindle-shaped, nonstriated cell found in the walls of blood vessels and hollow organs. Pick it if the question asked about the cells of the tunica media, not the heart wall.","A skeletal muscle fiber is a long, cylindrical cell with many nuclei, found in a skeletal muscle such as the biceps brachii. It would be the answer for the cell of voluntary muscle, not the cell of the heart wall."],"lt":"t-cardiac-muscle","lid":"W3-119","comp":60,"competencyId":"cv-cardiac-muscle"},{"id":"L03478","dok":1,"q":"What specialized junctions connect neighboring cardiomyocytes end to end?","a":"Intercalated discs.","options":["Tight junctions","Neuromuscular junctions","Intercalated discs","Z discs"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Intercalated discs are the specialized junctions where neighboring cardiomyocytes meet end to end. On a slide they show up as dark lines crossing the fibers, and they are the feature that identifies cardiac muscle. Each disc holds desmosomes, which anchor the cells together, and gap junctions, which let the contraction signal pass from cell to cell.","notes":["Tight junctions seal neighboring epithelial cells so substances cannot leak between them, as in the lining of the intestine. Choose them for a question about a leakproof epithelial seal, not about cardiac muscle.","A neuromuscular junction is where a motor neuron meets a skeletal muscle fiber to trigger contraction. It would be the answer for the nerve-to-muscle connection in skeletal muscle, not a cell-to-cell link between heart cells.","Correct. Intercalated discs join cardiomyocytes end to end.","Z discs are the boundaries of each sarcomere inside a single muscle cell. They would be the answer for where thin filaments anchor within a sarcomere, not for the junctions between two cells."],"lt":"t-cardiac-muscle","lid":"W3-122","comp":60,"competencyId":"cv-cardiac-muscle"},{"id":"L03479","dok":1,"q":"Within the intercalated discs, which junctions keep cardiomyocytes from pulling apart during contraction?","a":"Desmosomes.","options":["Tight junctions","Desmosomes","Hemidesmosomes","Gap junctions"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Desmosomes are anchoring junctions that rivet neighboring cells together, much like spot welds. In cardiac muscle they sit in the intercalated discs and hold the cells together as each beat pulls hard on them. Without them, the force of contraction would tear the cells apart.","notes":["Tight junctions seal epithelial cells to block leaks between them. They are not the anchoring junction of the intercalated disc, and they fit a question about a leakproof lining such as the intestinal epithelium.","Correct. Desmosomes anchor cardiomyocytes to one another so they do not separate.","Hemidesmosomes anchor an epithelial cell to the basement membrane beneath it, not to another cell. They would be the answer for how the epidermis stays attached to the layer below it.","Gap junctions are the other junction in the intercalated disc. They are channels that let the electrical signal pass directly between cells, so pick them for a question about spreading the signal, not holding cells together."],"lt":"t-cardiac-muscle","lid":"W3-123","comp":60,"competencyId":"cv-cardiac-muscle"},{"id":"L03480","dok":1,"q":"Which junctions in the intercalated discs let the electrical signal pass directly from one cardiomyocyte to the next?","a":"Gap junctions.","options":["Gap junctions","Desmosomes","Hemidesmosomes","Tight junctions"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Gap junctions are tiny protein channels that connect the cytoplasm of neighboring cells. In the intercalated discs they let the electrical signal for contraction flow straight from one cardiomyocyte to the next, so the signal spreads quickly through the heart wall. This is why a whole chamber contracts as one coordinated unit.","notes":["Correct. Gap junctions pass the signal directly from cell to cell.","Desmosomes are the anchoring junctions in the intercalated disc that hold cells together mechanically. Choose them for a question about cells pulling apart, not about passing the signal.","Hemidesmosomes attach an epithelial cell to its basement membrane. They would fit a question about anchoring the epidermis to the tissue below, not about communication between heart cells.","Tight junctions seal the spaces between epithelial cells to stop leaks. They are the answer for a leakproof lining, and they do not carry electrical signals between heart cells."],"lt":"t-cardiac-muscle","lid":"W3-124","comp":60,"competencyId":"cv-cardiac-muscle"},{"id":"L03481","dok":2,"q":"How do gap junctions help the heart contract?","a":"They rapidly spread the electrical signal between cells.","options":["They insulate the atria from the ventricles","They spread the electrical signal rapidly from cell to cell","They store calcium to power each contraction","They anchor cells so contraction does not tear them apart"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Gap junctions are channels that connect the insides of neighboring cardiomyocytes. The electrical signal flows straight through them, so it spreads quickly across the whole chamber and the cells contract nearly together. This rapid spread is what turns millions of separate cells into one coordinated pump.","notes":["Insulating the atria from the ventricles is the job of the fibrous skeleton, the rings of dense connective tissue around the valves. Choose that idea for why the AV bundle is the only electrical bridge between the upper and lower chambers.","Correct. Gap junctions let the signal pass quickly between cells so they contract together.","Calcium is stored in the sarcoplasmic reticulum inside each muscle cell, not in gap junctions. This choice mixes up a junction between cells with a storage organelle within the cell.","Anchoring cells against the pull of contraction is the job of desmosomes, the other junction in the intercalated disc. This would be right if the question asked why the cells do not separate."],"lt":"t-cardiac-muscle","lid":"W3-142","comp":60,"competencyId":"cv-cardiac-muscle"},{"id":"L03482","dok":2,"q":"Why do cardiomyocytes need desmosomes?","a":"To hold the cells together during forceful contractions.","options":["To set the pace of the heartbeat","To hold cells together during forceful contractions","To pass the electrical signal to the next cell","To keep the valve cusps from flipping backward"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Every heartbeat pulls hard on the cardiomyocytes, and the heart keeps beating for a lifetime. Desmosomes are anchoring junctions in the intercalated discs that rivet neighboring cells together, so the force of contraction is shared across the wall instead of pulling cells apart.","notes":["Setting the pace is the job of the SA node, the pacemaker in the wall of the right atrium. Choose it for what starts each heartbeat, not for what holds cells together.","Correct. Desmosomes keep cardiomyocytes attached under the strain of contraction.","Passing the signal is the job of gap junctions, the channel junctions in the same intercalated discs. This would be the answer for how the signal spreads between cells.","Keeping the atrioventricular valve cusps from flipping back is the job of the chordae tendineae and papillary muscles. Pick them for a question about stopping backflow into the atria."],"lt":"t-cardiac-muscle","lid":"W3-143","comp":60,"competencyId":"cv-cardiac-muscle"},{"id":"L03483","dok":2,"q":"How does the branching of cardiomyocytes help the heart work?","a":"It links each cell to several neighbors, forming a connected network.","options":["It lets each cell contract independently of the others","It anchors the myocardium firmly to the fibrous pericardium","It links each cell to several neighbors in a connected network","It gives each cell several nuclei for faster repair"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Each cardiomyocyte splits into branches that join several neighboring cells at intercalated discs. This builds an interconnected network, so the contraction signal spreads in many directions at once and the wall squeezes in a coordinated way. A row of unbranched cells could pass the signal along only a single line.","notes":["This is the opposite of what branching does. Cardiac cells are linked so they contract together; separate units contracting on their own is closer to how skeletal muscle uses its motor units.","The myocardium is not attached to the fibrous pericardium; the serous pericardium and the pericardial cavity lie between them. The fibrous pericardium anchors the whole heart in the mediastinum, which would be the answer for what holds the heart in place.","Correct. Branching connects each cell to several others so the signal spreads through a network.","Cardiomyocytes usually have a single central nucleus, and branching does not add nuclei. Several nuclei per cell is a feature of skeletal muscle fibers, so this choice mixes up the two tissues."],"lt":"t-cardiac-muscle","lid":"W3-144","comp":60,"competencyId":"cv-cardiac-muscle"},{"id":"L03485","dok":2,"q":"A disease weakens the desmosomes in a patient's intercalated discs. What is the most likely result in the heart wall?","a":"Cardiomyocytes pull apart from one another during contraction.","options":["The chordae tendineae detach from the valves","The cells lose their striations","Cardiomyocytes pull apart during contraction","The SA node stops generating impulses"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Desmosomes are the anchoring junctions in the intercalated discs that hold cardiomyocytes together. Each contraction pulls on these connections, so if the desmosomes are weak the cells can be pulled apart under the strain. The wall then loses its strength as one continuous sheet of muscle.","notes":["Chordae tendineae are fibrous cords tying the atrioventricular valve cusps to the papillary muscles, and desmosomes do not attach them. They would be the answer for a question about a valve flipping back into the atrium.","Striations come from the ordered sarcomeres inside each cell, not from the junctions between cells. They would be affected by a problem with the contractile filaments themselves, not with desmosomes.","Correct. Without strong desmosomes, the pull of each beat can separate the cells.","The SA node is a cluster of specialized pacemaker cells in the right atrial wall that starts each beat. Choose it for a question about where the heartbeat begins, which does not depend on the anchoring junctions."],"lt":"t-cardiac-muscle","lid":"W3-170","comp":60,"competencyId":"cv-cardiac-muscle"},{"id":"L03486","dok":2,"q":"Which pair of structures gives cardiomyocytes both electrical and mechanical coordination with their neighbors?","a":"Gap junctions and desmosomes in the intercalated discs.","options":["T tubules and sarcoplasmic reticulum inside each cell","Chordae tendineae and papillary muscles in the ventricles","Gap junctions and desmosomes in the intercalated discs","SA node and AV node in the right atrium"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The intercalated disc contains two kinds of junction with complementary jobs. Gap junctions are channels that pass the electrical signal from cell to cell, which gives electrical coordination, and desmosomes rivet the cells together so they pull as one, which gives mechanical coordination. Together they let the myocardium behave like a single unit.","notes":["T tubules and the sarcoplasmic reticulum work inside a single muscle cell, carrying the signal inward and storing calcium. They would be the answer for structures within one cell, not for links between neighboring cells.","Chordae tendineae and papillary muscles hold the atrioventricular valves shut when the ventricles contract. Pick them for a question about preventing backflow into the atria.","Correct. Gap junctions coordinate the cells electrically and desmosomes coordinate them mechanically.","The SA and AV nodes are parts of the conduction system that start and delay the heartbeat. They set the timing of the whole heart, but they do not join individual cells to one another."],"lt":"t-cardiac-muscle","lid":"W3-198","comp":60,"competencyId":"cv-cardiac-muscle"},{"id":"L03487","dok":2,"q":"Cardiac muscle acts as a functional syncytium, meaning its separate cells contract together as if they were one large cell. Which feature makes this possible?","a":"Gap junctions within the intercalated discs.","options":["Desmosomes within the intercalated discs","Abundant mitochondria in each cell","A single central nucleus in each cell","Gap junctions within the intercalated discs"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"A syncytium is a mass of cytoplasm that works as one cell. Cardiomyocytes are separate cells, but the gap junctions in their intercalated discs connect their cytoplasm so the electrical signal passes straight through. Because every cell receives the signal almost at once, the whole chamber contracts together, which is why the myocardium is called a functional syncytium.","notes":["Desmosomes hold the cells together mechanically, but they do not pass the signal, so on their own they could not make the cells contract in unison. Choose them for what stops cells from pulling apart.","Abundant mitochondria, about a quarter of the cell volume, supply the energy for constant contraction. Pick them for a question about how heart muscle keeps working without tiring, not about how cells act as one unit.","Having one central nucleus helps you recognize a cardiomyocyte on a slide, but it does nothing to link cells. It would be the answer for how cardiac cells differ from multinucleated skeletal muscle fibers.","Correct. Gap junctions pass the signal through the cells so they contract as one unit."],"lt":"t-cardiac-muscle","lid":"W3-199","comp":60,"competencyId":"cv-cardiac-muscle"},{"id":"L03561","dok":1,"q":"What is the cardiac conduction system?","a":"Specialized cardiac muscle cells that generate and spread each heartbeat.","options":["Nerves from the brainstem that start each heartbeat","Valves and cords that keep blood moving in one direction","Coronary vessels that supply oxygen to the heart wall","Specialized heart muscle cells that start and spread each beat"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The cardiac conduction system is a network of specialized cardiac muscle cells, about one percent of the cardiac fibers, that generates the signal for each heartbeat and spreads it in a fixed order. It runs from the SA node across the atria to the AV node, AV bundle, bundle branches, and Purkinje fibers. This order makes the atria contract first and the ventricles second.","notes":["Nerves from the cardiac centers in the medulla oblongata only speed up or slow down the heart. They do not start the beat, which is why a transplanted heart with its nerves cut still beats.","The valves, chordae tendineae, and papillary muscles keep blood flowing in one direction. Choose them for a question about preventing backflow, not about the electrical signal.","The coronary vessels deliver oxygen to the heart wall and drain it. They would be the answer for coronary circulation, not for what generates the heartbeat.","Correct. The conduction system is specialized cardiac muscle that starts and spreads the beat."],"lt":"t-heart-conduction","lid":"W3-228","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L03562","dok":1,"q":"What is autorhythmicity?","a":"The heart's ability to generate its own rhythm.","options":["Skeletal muscle's ability to contract when we choose","The heart's ability to set its own rhythm without nerve input","The steady, rhythmic closing of the heart valves","The brainstem's control of heart rate through the vagus nerve"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Autorhythmicity means self-rhythm: the heart's built-in ability to generate its own beat without a signal from the nervous system. It belongs to the conducting cells of the conduction system, especially the SA node, which fires on its own about sixty to one hundred times a minute. Nerves can adjust this rate, but they do not create it.","notes":["Contracting on command describes voluntary skeletal muscle, which needs a nerve signal for every contraction. Cardiac muscle is different because it starts its own beat.","Correct. Autorhythmicity is the heart generating its own beat.","Valves open and close passively as pressures change in the chambers; they do not create a rhythm. Choose the valves for a question about one-way blood flow.","The vagus nerve carries parasympathetic fibers that slow the heart rate. That is nervous adjustment of the rate, the opposite of the heart setting its own rhythm."],"lt":"t-heart-conduction","lid":"W3-229","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L03563","dok":1,"q":"What are the conducting cells of the heart?","a":"Specialized non-contractile cells that generate and carry the electrical signal.","options":["Endothelial cells that line the heart chambers","Ordinary working cardiomyocytes that contract when signaled","Neurons in the cardiac plexus that start each heartbeat","Non-contractile cells that generate and carry the signal"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Conducting cells are specialized cardiac muscle cells that do little or no contracting. Their job is to generate the electrical signal and carry it through the heart along the conduction pathway. They make up the SA node, AV node, AV bundle, bundle branches, and Purkinje fibers.","notes":["Endothelial cells form the smooth endocardium lining the chambers and covering the valves. They would be the answer for the inner layer of the heart wall.","Ordinary working cardiomyocytes are the contractile cells. They make up most of the myocardium and contract when the signal reaches them, so they would be the answer for the cells that generate force.","The cardiac plexus is a network of autonomic nerve fibers near the base of the heart that adjusts the rate. Its fibers change the pace but do not start the heartbeat.","Correct. Conducting cells generate and carry the signal rather than contract."],"lt":"t-heart-conduction","lid":"W3-230","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L03564","dok":1,"q":"What are the contractile cells of the heart?","a":"Working cardiomyocytes that contract when signaled.","options":["Purkinje fibers lining the ventricle walls","Pacemaker cells clustered in the SA node","Fibroblasts that build the fibrous skeleton","Working cardiomyocytes that contract when signaled"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Contractile cells are the ordinary working cardiomyocytes that make up most of the myocardium. They do not start the signal themselves; they contract when the signal from the conduction system reaches them. Their combined contraction is what squeezes blood out of the chambers.","notes":["Purkinje fibers are conducting cells that deliver the signal to the contractile cells of the ventricle walls. Choose them for the last part of the conduction pathway.","Pacemaker cells of the SA node are conducting cells that generate the signal. They are the answer for where each heartbeat starts, not for the cells that do the squeezing.","Fibroblasts make the dense connective tissue of the fibrous skeleton, which anchors the valves and insulates the atria from the ventricles. They are not muscle cells and do not contract.","Correct. Contractile cells are the working cardiomyocytes that generate force."],"lt":"t-heart-conduction","lid":"W3-231","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L03565","dok":1,"q":"Which structure is the heart's natural pacemaker?","a":"SA node.","options":["Purkinje fibers","SA node","AV bundle","AV node"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The SA node, or sinoatrial node, is the heart's natural pacemaker. It sits in the wall of the right atrium near the opening of the superior vena cava and fires faster than any other part of the conduction system, about sixty to one hundred times a minute. Because it fires first, it sets the pace for the whole heart.","notes":["Purkinje fibers spread the signal through the ventricle walls at the end of the pathway. Choose them for the structures that deliver the signal to the ventricular contractile cells.","Correct. The SA node fires first and sets the heart rate.","The AV bundle (bundle of His) carries the signal from the AV node into the interventricular septum. It is the only electrical bridge between atria and ventricles, not the starting point.","The AV node, in the floor of the right atrium, receives the signal from the atria and delays it briefly before passing it on. It can take over as a slower backup pacemaker if the SA node fails, but it is not the normal pacemaker."],"lt":"t-heart-conduction","lid":"W3-232","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L03566","dok":1,"q":"What does SA node stand for?","a":"Sinoatrial node.","options":["Sinoatrial node","Septal arterial node","Atrioventricular node","Semilunar atrial node"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"SA stands for sinoatrial. The name points to its location: the sino part refers to the sinus venarum, the smooth-walled part of the right atrium where the venae cavae empty, and atrial tells you the node sits in an atrium. It lies in the right atrial wall near the opening of the superior vena cava.","notes":["Correct. SA stands for sinoatrial.","There is no septal arterial node. The S in SA comes from sinus, and the node is made of specialized muscle cells, not an artery.","Atrioventricular is what AV stands for in the AV node, the second node in the pathway, at the junction between the right atrium and the ventricles. Choose it for the AV node.","Semilunar describes the half-moon cusps of the pulmonary and aortic valves, not a node. This mix-up comes from seeing the letter S in both terms."],"lt":"t-heart-conduction","lid":"W3-233","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L03567","dok":1,"q":"What does AV node stand for?","a":"Atrioventricular node.","options":["Apical ventricular node","Aortic valve node","Arteriovenous node","Atrioventricular node"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"AV stands for atrioventricular, meaning it relates to both an atrium and a ventricle. The AV node sits in the floor of the right atrium near the interatrial septum, at the junction where the signal must cross from the atria to the ventricles. It delays the signal briefly and then passes it into the AV bundle.","notes":["There is no apical ventricular node. The apex is where the bundle branches carry the signal, but the AV node itself sits in the floor of the right atrium.","AV is also used as an abbreviation for the aortic valve, which is why this mix-up is common. The aortic valve is the semilunar valve between the left ventricle and the aorta, not part of the conduction system.","Arteriovenous describes a direct connection from an artery to a vein, as in an arteriovenous anastomosis. Choose it for a vessel that bypasses capillaries, not for a node.","Correct. AV stands for atrioventricular."],"lt":"t-heart-conduction","lid":"W3-235","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L03568","dok":1,"q":"What is the AV bundle also called?","a":"Bundle of His.","options":["Bundle branches","Crista terminalis","Bundle of His","Moderator band"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The AV bundle, or atrioventricular bundle, is also called the bundle of His. It picks up the signal from the AV node and carries it into the superior part of the interventricular septum. It is the only electrical link between the atria and the ventricles, and it soon splits into the right and left bundle branches.","notes":["The bundle branches are the next step after the bundle of His, carrying the signal down each side of the septum. They are what the AV bundle divides into, not another name for it.","The crista terminalis is a muscular ridge in the right atrium separating its smooth posterior wall from the rough, pectinate anterior wall. It is a surface landmark, not part of the conduction pathway.","Correct. The AV bundle is the bundle of His.","The moderator band, or septomarginal trabecula, is a muscular band in the right ventricle that carries part of the right bundle branch to the anterior papillary muscle. Choose it for that band, not for the AV bundle."],"lt":"t-heart-conduction","lid":"W3-237","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L03569","dok":1,"q":"Where is the AV bundle located?","a":"Superior part of the interventricular septum.","options":["Floor of the right atrium","Right atrial wall near the superior vena cava","Superior interventricular septum","Walls of both ventricles"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The AV bundle lies in the superior part of the interventricular septum, just below the AV node. It is the one place where the signal can pass through the fibrous skeleton that otherwise insulates the atria from the ventricles. From there it divides into the right and left bundle branches, which run down the septum.","notes":["The floor of the right atrium, near the interatrial septum, is where the AV node sits. Choose this location for the AV node, one step earlier in the pathway.","The wall of the right atrium near the opening of the superior vena cava is the location of the SA node. It would be the answer for where the pacemaker sits.","Correct. The AV bundle runs in the upper interventricular septum.","The walls of both ventricles hold the Purkinje fibers, which spread the signal to the contractile cells. Choose this location for the last step of the pathway."],"lt":"t-heart-conduction","lid":"W3-238","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L03570","dok":1,"q":"The right and left bundle branches carry the signal toward which part of the heart?","a":"The apex.","options":["AV node","Base of the heart","Apex of the heart","Both atria"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The right and left bundle branches carry the signal down both sides of the interventricular septum toward the apex, the inferior tip of the heart. From there the Purkinje fibers spread it up through the ventricle walls. This route makes the ventricles contract from the apex upward.","notes":["The AV node lies upstream of the bundle branches, and the signal has already passed through it. Choose the AV node for the step that delays the signal before the AV bundle.","The base is the broad superior surface where the great vessels attach. The Purkinje fibers eventually spread the signal back up toward the base, but the bundle branches carry it the other way, down toward the apex.","Correct. The bundle branches carry the signal down the septum toward the apex.","The atria received the signal from the SA node at the start of the beat. The bundle branches are in the ventricular septum and carry the signal away from the atria."],"lt":"t-heart-conduction","lid":"W3-239","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L03571","dok":1,"q":"What do the Purkinje fibers do?","a":"Spread the signal through the ventricle walls.","options":["Delay the signal so the atria can empty","Spread the signal through the ventricle walls","Set the pace of the heartbeat","Carry the signal across the fibrous skeleton"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Purkinje fibers are the last part of the conduction system. They branch from the bundle branches and run throughout the walls of both ventricles, delivering the signal to the contractile cells. Because they reach the apex first and then spread upward, the ventricles contract from the apex toward the base.","notes":["Delaying the signal so the atria can finish emptying is the job of the AV node. It would be the answer for the brief pause between atrial and ventricular contraction.","Correct. Purkinje fibers deliver the signal to the ventricular contractile cells.","Setting the pace is the job of the SA node in the wall of the right atrium. Choose it for the structure that starts each beat.","Carrying the signal across the fibrous skeleton from the atria to the ventricles is the job of the AV bundle, or bundle of His. Pick it for the only electrical bridge between the upper and lower chambers."],"lt":"t-heart-conduction","lid":"W3-240","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L03572","dok":2,"q":"Which sequence traces the cardiac conduction pathway in the correct order?","a":"SA node, AV node, AV bundle, bundle branches, Purkinje fibers.","options":["SA node, AV node, Purkinje fibers, bundle branches, AV bundle","SA node, AV node, AV bundle, bundle branches, Purkinje fibers","AV node, SA node, AV bundle, bundle branches, Purkinje fibers","SA node, AV bundle, AV node, Purkinje fibers, bundle branches"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The SA node fires first and the signal spreads across both atria, which contract. The signal then reaches the AV node, where it pauses briefly, and passes into the AV bundle in the upper interventricular septum. The right and left bundle branches carry it down the septum toward the apex, and the Purkinje fibers spread it through the ventricle walls so the ventricles contract.","notes":["This sends the signal backward through the ventricles. The AV bundle comes right after the AV node, and the Purkinje fibers are the last step, not the middle.","Correct. SA node, AV node, AV bundle, bundle branches, Purkinje fibers.","This swaps the two nodes. The SA node is the pacemaker and always fires first; the AV node receives the signal only after it has crossed the atria.","This puts the AV bundle before the AV node and the Purkinje fibers before the bundle branches. The AV node must pass the signal to the bundle, and the bundle branches must carry it down the septum before the Purkinje fibers spread it."],"lt":"t-heart-conduction","lid":"W3-253","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L03573","dok":2,"q":"Why does the SA node, rather than another part of the conduction system, normally set the heart rate?","a":"It fires fastest and so starts each beat.","options":["It lies closest to the ventricles","It receives a direct signal from the brain for each beat","It is the largest node in the heart","It fires fastest, so it starts each beat before other sites can"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Several parts of the conduction system can generate signals on their own, but the SA node fires fastest, about sixty to one hundred times a minute. Because it fires first, its signal sweeps through the heart before the slower sites reach their own firing point. That is why the SA node normally sets the pace for the whole heart.","notes":["The SA node sits in the wall of the right atrium near the superior vena cava, farther from the ventricles than the AV node. Its position near the top of the heart helps the atria contract first, but distance to the ventricles is not why it sets the rate.","Nerves from the brainstem only speed up or slow down the SA node; they do not start each beat. A transplanted heart with its nerves cut still beats at its own rate.","Size does not decide which site sets the pace; firing speed does. This choice treats the pacemaker as a matter of size, which is a common guess but not the reason.","Correct. The fastest site fires first and sets the pace."],"lt":"t-heart-conduction","lid":"W3-254","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L03575","dok":2,"q":"Why is the AV bundle so important to the heartbeat?","a":"It is the only electrical bridge from the atria to the ventricles.","options":["It holds the atrioventricular valves shut during contraction","It is the only electrical bridge from the atria to the ventricles","It sets the heart rate for all four chambers","It spreads the signal through the ventricle walls"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The fibrous skeleton, the dense connective tissue around the valves, separates the atria from the ventricles and does not conduct electrical signals. The AV bundle passes through this barrier in the upper interventricular septum, so it is the only route by which the signal can reach the ventricles. Without it, the ventricles would not receive the signal from the atria.","notes":["Holding the atrioventricular valves shut is the job of the chordae tendineae and papillary muscles. Choose them for a question about preventing backflow into the atria.","Correct. The AV bundle is the only electrical link through the fibrous skeleton.","Setting the heart rate is the job of the SA node, which fires fastest. The AV bundle only carries the signal it is given.","Spreading the signal through the ventricle walls is the job of the Purkinje fibers, at the end of the pathway. The AV bundle comes earlier, carrying the signal into the septum."],"lt":"t-heart-conduction","lid":"W3-256","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L03576","dok":2,"q":"The right and left bundle branches run down which structure?","a":"The interventricular septum.","options":["Wall of the right atrium","Outer surface of the ventricles","Interatrial septum","Interventricular septum"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"After the AV bundle enters the top of the interventricular septum, it splits into the right and left bundle branches. These run down the right and left sides of the septum toward the apex. Because they travel inside the septum, damage to the septum, such as an infarction there, can interrupt the signal to the ventricles.","notes":["The wall of the right atrium holds the SA node, near the opening of the superior vena cava. Choose it for the location of the pacemaker.","The outer surface of the ventricles is where the coronary arteries and cardiac veins run in their grooves. The bundle branches run inside the septum, not on the surface.","The interatrial septum is the wall between the two atria, where the fossa ovalis sits. The AV node lies near it, but the bundle branches are lower, in the ventricular septum.","Correct. The bundle branches run down both sides of the interventricular septum."],"lt":"t-heart-conduction","lid":"W3-257","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L03577","dok":2,"q":"The ventricles contract from the apex upward. What does this accomplish?","a":"It pushes blood upward toward the pulmonary trunk and aorta.","options":["It pushes blood upward toward the outflow valves at the base","It lets the ventricles fill from below","It keeps the atrioventricular valves open during contraction","It pushes blood back into the atria"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The pulmonary trunk and aorta leave the ventricles at the base of the heart, at the top. Because the Purkinje fibers deliver the signal to the apex first, contraction starts at the bottom and moves upward, squeezing blood toward the semilunar valves much like squeezing a toothpaste tube from the closed end. This wringing action empties the ventricles efficiently into the great arteries.","notes":["Correct. Apex-to-base contraction drives blood toward the exits at the base.","The ventricles fill while they are relaxed, from the atria above through the atrioventricular valves. Contraction is for emptying, not filling.","The atrioventricular valves close, not open, when the ventricles contract. Keeping them open would let blood leak back into the atria.","Blood is kept from going back into the atria by the closed atrioventricular valves, held by the chordae tendineae and papillary muscles. Pushing blood back into the atria would be backflow, which the heart is built to prevent."],"lt":"t-heart-conduction","lid":"W3-258","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L03578","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares conducting cells and contractile cells in the heart?","a":"Conducting cells generate and carry the signal; contractile cells generate force.","options":["Conducting cells generate and carry the signal; contractile cells generate force","Conducting cells generate force; contractile cells carry the signal","Conducting cells are nerve cells; contractile cells are cardiac muscle cells","Both contract strongly, but only contractile cells have gap junctions"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Both kinds of cell are cardiac muscle, but they have different jobs. Conducting cells, such as those in the SA node, AV node, and Purkinje fibers, generate the electrical signal and carry it through the heart with little or no contraction. Contractile cells are the ordinary working cardiomyocytes that make up most of the myocardium and generate the force that pumps blood when the signal reaches them.","notes":["Correct. Conducting cells handle the signal and contractile cells do the pumping.","This swaps the two jobs. Contractile cells are named for what they do, contracting, while conducting cells conduct the signal.","Conducting cells are specialized cardiac muscle cells, not neurons. Nerves reach the heart through the cardiac plexus, but they only adjust the rate set by the conducting cells.","Conducting cells do little or no contracting, and gap junctions link cardiac cells of both kinds so the signal can pass between them. This choice gets both halves wrong."],"lt":"t-heart-conduction","lid":"W3-259","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L03579","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares the SA node and the AV node?","a":"The SA node starts each beat; the AV node delays the signal and passes it on.","options":["The AV node starts each beat; the SA node delays the signal and passes it on","The SA node signals the ventricles directly; the AV node controls only the atria","The SA node starts each beat; the AV node delays the signal and passes it on","Both nodes sit in the interventricular septum and send the signal to the apex"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The SA node, in the wall of the right atrium near the superior vena cava, is the pacemaker that fires first and starts each heartbeat. The signal spreads across the atria to the AV node, in the floor of the right atrium, which holds it briefly so the atria can finish emptying. The AV node then passes the signal into the AV bundle and on to the ventricles.","notes":["This swaps the two nodes. The SA node fires fastest and so starts the beat; the AV node is the second stop, where the signal pauses.","The SA node's signal cannot reach the ventricles directly because the fibrous skeleton insulates them from the atria. It must pass through the AV node and AV bundle first.","Correct. The SA node starts the beat and the AV node delays and relays it.","Both nodes are in the right atrium, not the interventricular septum. The structures in the septum are the AV bundle and the bundle branches."],"lt":"t-heart-conduction","lid":"W3-260","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L03580","dok":2,"q":"Which conduction structure passes through the fibrous skeleton to connect the atria electrically to the ventricles?","a":"AV bundle.","options":["Purkinje fibers","SA node","Moderator band","AV bundle"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The fibrous skeleton is a set of dense connective tissue rings around the valves that electrically insulates the atria from the ventricles. The AV bundle, or bundle of His, is the one conduction pathway that crosses it, carrying the signal from the AV node into the top of the interventricular septum. Every signal the ventricles receive comes through this bridge.","notes":["Purkinje fibers are in the ventricle walls, beyond the fibrous skeleton. They spread the signal after it has already crossed over.","The SA node starts the signal in the wall of the right atrium, but it lies entirely within the atrium. It is the answer for the pacemaker, not the bridge.","The moderator band is a muscular band inside the right ventricle that carries part of the right bundle branch to the anterior papillary muscle. It lies within the ventricle and does not cross the fibrous skeleton.","Correct. The AV bundle is the bridge through the fibrous skeleton."],"lt":"t-heart-conduction","lid":"W3-261","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L03581","dok":2,"q":"Which pair of conduction structures spreads the signal through the ventricles?","a":"Bundle branches and Purkinje fibers.","options":["SA node and bundle branches","AV node and AV bundle","Bundle branches and Purkinje fibers","SA node and AV node"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Once the signal crosses into the ventricles through the AV bundle, the right and left bundle branches carry it down both sides of the interventricular septum toward the apex. The Purkinje fibers then spread it through the walls of both ventricles to the contractile cells. These two structures are the ventricular part of the conduction system.","notes":["The bundle branches are ventricular, but the SA node is in the right atrium. This pair mixes the first and fourth steps of the pathway.","The AV node and AV bundle carry the signal from the atria to the top of the septum, the bridge into the ventricles. They hand the signal off to the bundle branches but do not spread it through the ventricle walls.","Correct. The bundle branches and Purkinje fibers distribute the signal through the ventricles.","The SA and AV nodes are both in the right atrium. They start and delay the signal, so they are the answer for the atrial part of the pathway."],"lt":"t-heart-conduction","lid":"W3-262","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L03582","dok":2,"q":"Why must the atria contract before the ventricles?","a":"So the ventricles are filled before they eject blood.","options":["So the atria can receive blood from the ventricles","So the semilunar valves stay closed","So the coronary arteries can supply the atria first","So the ventricles are filled before they eject blood"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The ventricles are the main pumps, and they can only push out the blood they hold. When the atria contract first, they top off the ventricles with a final push of blood. The ventricles then contract while full, which is why the conduction system is arranged so the atria go first and the ventricles second.","notes":["Blood never flows from the ventricles back into the atria in a normal heart; the atrioventricular valves prevent it. The atria receive blood from veins, not from the ventricles.","The semilunar valves stay closed while the ventricles fill and open when the ventricles contract. The order of atrial and ventricular contraction is about filling the ventricles, not holding these valves shut.","The coronary arteries supply all of the heart wall and are not the reason for the contraction order. This choice links two unrelated systems.","Correct. Atrial contraction tops off the ventricles before they pump."],"lt":"t-heart-conduction","lid":"W3-263","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L03583","dok":2,"q":"What would happen if the ventricles contracted before the atria had emptied into them?","a":"The ventricles would eject less blood because they were not fully filled.","options":["The aortic valve would stay closed permanently","The ventricles would eject less blood, being underfilled","Blood would flow from the aorta back into the lungs","The ventricles would eject more blood than usual"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The atria finish filling the ventricles with their final contraction. If the ventricles contracted first, they would squeeze down before that last push of blood arrived, so each beat would send out less blood. The atria would also be pushing against closed atrioventricular valves, so their contraction would be wasted.","notes":["The aortic valve still opens whenever the left ventricle contracts with enough force. The problem would be a smaller volume of blood, not a valve that never opens.","Correct. Underfilled ventricles pump out less blood.","There is no direct route from the aorta back to the lungs in an adult heart, and the aortic valve stops backflow into the ventricle. This choice invents a path blood cannot take.","Less filling means less blood to eject, not more. A ventricle can only push out what it holds."],"lt":"t-heart-conduction","lid":"W3-264","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L03584","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly describes how autorhythmicity differs from nervous control of the heart?","a":"Autorhythmicity starts the beat; nerves only modify the rate.","options":["Nerves start each beat; autorhythmicity only adjusts the rate","Autorhythmicity starts the beat; nerves only speed it up or slow it down","Autorhythmicity controls the atria; nerves control the ventricles","Both start the beat, so the heart stops if either is lost"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Autorhythmicity is the heart's built-in ability to generate its own beat, centered on the SA node. The autonomic nerves, sympathetic fibers that speed the rate and parasympathetic fibers in the vagus nerve that slow it, only adjust the pace the SA node already sets. A transplanted heart, whose nerves have been cut, still beats on its own.","notes":["This reverses the roles. The conduction system starts every beat, and the nerves change how fast it happens.","Correct. The heart starts its own beat, and nerves adjust the rate.","Both systems affect the whole heart rather than splitting it into atria and ventricles. The SA node starts the beat for all four chambers, and the nerves reach the SA and AV nodes to adjust the overall rate.","The heart does not stop when its nerves are lost, as heart transplants show. Only the conduction system is needed to start the beat."],"lt":"t-heart-conduction","lid":"W3-265","comp":65,"competencyId":"cv-cardiac-nerves"},{"id":"L03585","dok":3,"q":"A patient's AV node is damaged, so the signal is delayed or stopped there. What timing problem can result?","a":"The atria and ventricles may beat out of step.","options":["The SA node is forced to fire faster","The atria stop receiving the signal","The atria and ventricles beat out of step","The atrioventricular valves stay open"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"A block at the AV node is called heart block. The SA node still fires and the atria still contract, but the signal is delayed or stopped before it reaches the ventricles. The ventricles may then beat late, skip beats, or beat at a slower rate of their own, so the atria and ventricles fall out of step.","notes":["AV node damage does not change how fast the SA node fires. A fast resting rate would be tachycardia, a different disorder.","The atria receive the signal from the SA node before it ever reaches the AV node, so they still contract. The problem lies downstream, between the atria and the ventricles.","Correct. A block at the AV node disconnects atrial and ventricular timing.","The atrioventricular valves close because of pressure changes when the ventricles contract, and the chordae tendineae hold them shut. AV node damage affects the timing signal, not the valves themselves."],"lt":"t-heart-conduction","lid":"W3-279","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L03586","dok":3,"q":"If the brief delay at the AV node disappeared, how would ventricular filling be affected?","a":"The ventricles would contract before the atria finished emptying into them.","options":["The ventricles would contract before the atria finished emptying","Filling would not change, because the delay has no purpose","The ventricles would fill more completely than usual","The atria would contract after the ventricles"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The pause at the AV node holds the signal just long enough for the atria to finish pushing blood into the ventricles. Without it, the signal would race into the ventricles while the atria were still contracting, and the ventricles would squeeze down before they were completely full. Each beat would then pump out less blood.","notes":["Correct. Without the delay, the ventricles would contract before they were full.","The delay is essential timing, not a flaw. It is what lets the atria finish emptying before the ventricles contract.","Removing the delay would cut filling short, not improve it. The delay exists to give the ventricles extra filling time.","The atria would still fire first, since the SA node sits in the atrium. The problem is that the ventricles would follow too soon, overlapping with atrial contraction."],"lt":"t-heart-conduction","lid":"W3-280","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L03587","dok":2,"q":"If the AV bundle is blocked, what major conduction problem occurs?","a":"Signals cannot pass normally from the atria to the ventricles.","options":["The Purkinje fibers fire faster to compensate","The SA node stops firing altogether","The atria no longer receive the signal","The signal cannot reach the ventricles normally"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The AV bundle is the only electrical bridge through the fibrous skeleton between the atria and the ventricles. If it is blocked, the signal from the SA node still spreads across the atria but cannot reach the ventricles normally. The atria and ventricles then lose their coordination, a form of heart block.","notes":["If the ventricles lose the signal from above, cells lower in the conduction system may take over, but they fire more slowly than the SA node, not faster. This choice reverses that pattern.","The SA node lies upstream in the right atrium and keeps firing normally. A block in the AV bundle does not reach back to the pacemaker.","The atria get the signal from the SA node before it reaches the AV bundle, so they still contract. The loss is on the ventricular side of the block.","Correct. With the bridge blocked, the signal cannot cross to the ventricles normally."],"lt":"t-heart-conduction","lid":"W3-281","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L03588","dok":2,"q":"Damage to the bundle branches would most directly affect which chambers?","a":"The ventricles.","options":["Right atrium only","Both ventricles","Both atria","Left atrium only"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The bundle branches run down the right and left sides of the interventricular septum and feed the Purkinje fibers in the ventricle walls. Damage to them, called a bundle branch block, delays or blocks the signal to the ventricle on the affected side. The atria are upstream and still receive the signal normally.","notes":["The right atrium holds the SA and AV nodes, which lie above the bundle branches. Damage to the bundle branches would not change how the right atrium receives its signal.","Correct. The bundle branches serve the ventricles.","The atria get the signal from the SA node before it reaches the AV node and bundle. Since the bundle branches are downstream of the atria, damage to them leaves atrial contraction intact.","The left atrium receives the signal as it spreads across both atria from the SA node. It is upstream of the bundle branches and would contract normally."],"lt":"t-heart-conduction","lid":"W3-282","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L03589","dok":2,"q":"Damage to the Purkinje fibers would most directly impair what?","a":"Coordinated contraction of the ventricles.","options":["Coordinated contraction of the ventricle walls","Contraction of the atria","The delay of the signal at the AV node","The pacing of the heartbeat by the SA node"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Purkinje fibers run throughout the walls of both ventricles and deliver the signal to the contractile cells, starting at the apex and spreading upward. If they are damaged, parts of the ventricle wall receive the signal late or out of order, so the ventricles cannot squeeze in a smooth, coordinated way and pump less effectively.","notes":["Correct. Purkinje damage disrupts the orderly squeeze of the ventricles.","The atria contract when the signal spreads from the SA node across their walls. Purkinje fibers are only in the ventricles, so atrial contraction would continue.","The AV node delay happens before the signal reaches the ventricles. Purkinje fibers are downstream of it, so their damage would not change that pause.","The SA node sits at the very start of the pathway in the right atrium. Damage at the far end, in the ventricle walls, does not change how the SA node paces the heart."],"lt":"t-heart-conduction","lid":"W3-283","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L03590","dok":2,"q":"A transplanted heart has had all of its nerves cut, yet it still beats. What explains this?","a":"Autorhythmic conduction cells generate the signal within the heart.","options":["The papillary muscles set the heart's rhythm","The vagus nerve regrows into the heart within minutes","The conduction system generates its own signals","The brain sends signals through the blood to the heart"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The heartbeat starts inside the heart itself. The SA node and the rest of the conduction system are autorhythmic, meaning they generate their own signals without input from the nervous system. Cutting the nerves removes only the ability to speed up or slow down the rate, so a transplanted heart beats at the SA node's own pace.","notes":["Papillary muscles anchor the chordae tendineae to hold the atrioventricular valves shut. They are contractile muscle, not pacemaker tissue.","Cut nerves do not regrow within minutes, and the vagus nerve only slows the heart anyway. The transplanted heart beats right away because it never needed nerves to start each beat.","Correct. The conduction system starts each beat on its own.","Some hormones carried in the blood can raise the heart rate, but they adjust the pace rather than starting each beat. The signal for each beat comes from the SA node."],"lt":"t-heart-conduction","lid":"W3-284","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L03591","dok":2,"q":"If the SA node fails, what can take over pacing the heart?","a":"An ectopic pacemaker elsewhere in the heart, or an artificial pacemaker.","options":["The vagus nerve, which then starts each beat","The cardiac centers of the medulla oblongata","The fibrous skeleton around the valves","Another conduction site or an artificial pacemaker"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Other parts of the conduction system, such as the AV node, can generate signals on their own, though more slowly than the SA node. If the SA node fails, one of these sites can take over as an ectopic pacemaker, meaning a pacemaker outside its normal location. When this backup is not enough, an implanted artificial pacemaker can deliver timed signals instead.","notes":["The vagus nerve carries parasympathetic fibers that slow the heart rate. It can only adjust the pace, never start the beat.","The cardiac centers in the medulla oblongata adjust the rate through autonomic nerves. They do not generate the beat, which is why a heart beats even when its nerves are cut.","The fibrous skeleton is dense connective tissue that insulates the atria from the ventricles. It cannot conduct a signal, let alone start one.","Correct. A backup site in the conduction system or an implanted device can take over."],"lt":"t-heart-conduction","lid":"W3-287","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L03592","dok":2,"q":"Why is an ectopic pacemaker considered abnormal?","a":"A site outside the SA node controls the rhythm.","options":["The delay at the AV node grows longer","The nerves to the heart have been cut","A site outside the SA node takes over the rhythm","The SA node fires faster than its usual rate"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Ectopic means out of place. An ectopic pacemaker is any site outside the SA node that takes over setting the heart's rhythm. Because the beat no longer starts in the normal place, the signal may spread in an unusual order, and the normal pacing sequence of the heart is lost.","notes":["A longer delay at the AV node is a form of heart block. The beat still starts in the SA node, so this is a conduction problem, not an ectopic pacemaker.","Cutting the heart's nerves, as in a transplant, leaves the SA node in charge. The heart still beats from its normal pacemaker.","Correct. Ectopic means the rhythm starts somewhere other than the SA node.","An SA node firing too fast is tachycardia, an abnormal rate. The pacemaker is still in its normal place, so it is not ectopic."],"lt":"t-heart-conduction","lid":"W3-288","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L03593","dok":3,"q":"Suppose the ventricles contracted from the base downward instead of from the apex upward. Why would this be inefficient?","a":"It would push blood toward the apex, away from the outflow vessels.","options":["The atrioventricular valves would never open","The coronary arteries would carry blood backward","Blood would be pushed away from the outflow valves","The atria would contract after the ventricles"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The pulmonary and aortic valves sit at the base of the ventricles, at the top of the heart. Contracting from the apex upward squeezes blood toward those exits. If contraction began at the base, it would push blood down toward the closed apex, away from the valves, so less blood would leave with each beat.","notes":["The atrioventricular valves open when the ventricles relax and fill, which would still happen. The problem would be emptying the ventricles, not filling them.","Coronary blood flow runs from the aorta into the heart wall regardless of how the ventricles contract. The direction of ventricular contraction does not reverse it.","Correct. Base-first contraction would push blood away from the exits at the base.","The order of atrial and ventricular contraction is set by the SA node and the AV node delay, not by the direction the ventricles squeeze. This choice mixes up two different timing ideas."],"lt":"t-heart-conduction","lid":"W3-289","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L03594","dok":3,"q":"A patient's atria contract normally, but the signal never reaches the ventricles. Where is the block most likely to be?","a":"At the AV node or AV bundle.","options":["At the AV node or AV bundle","At the SA node","In the Purkinje fibers","In the walls of the atria"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"If the atria contract normally, the SA node fired and the signal crossed the atrial walls. The ventricles receive that signal only through the AV node and then the AV bundle, the single bridge through the fibrous skeleton. A signal that stops between the atria and the ventricles must therefore be blocked at the AV node or AV bundle, which is what heart block means.","notes":["Correct. The AV node and AV bundle are the only route from atria to ventricles.","If the SA node had failed, the atria would not receive a normal signal either. The atria are contracting here, so the pacemaker is working.","Purkinje fibers are the last step, inside the ventricle walls. Damage there would make ventricular contraction disorganized, but the signal would already have crossed into the ventricles.","The signal has already spread across the atrial walls, since the atria are contracting. A block there would affect the atria themselves."],"lt":"t-heart-conduction","lid":"W3-290","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L03595","dok":2,"q":"A student says nerves from the brain start each heartbeat. Which correction is accurate?","a":"The conduction system starts each heartbeat; nerves only adjust the rate.","options":["The conduction system starts each beat; nerves only adjust the rate","Hormones in the blood start each heartbeat instead","Nerves start the beat in the atria, and the SA node starts it in the ventricles","The papillary muscles start each beat by pulling on the valves"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The heartbeat starts in the SA node, part of the heart's own conduction system. Autonomic nerves reach the SA and AV nodes through the cardiac plexus and only speed up or slow down the rate the SA node already sets. That is why a transplanted heart, with its nerves cut, still beats.","notes":["Correct. The SA node starts the beat; nerves only change its pace.","Some hormones in the blood can raise the heart rate, but like the nerves they adjust the pace rather than start the beat. The signal for each beat comes from the SA node.","Nerves do not start the beat in any chamber. The SA node starts each beat, and the signal travels through the atria and on to the ventricles through the conduction pathway.","Papillary muscles anchor the chordae tendineae to hold the atrioventricular valves shut during ventricular contraction. They respond to the signal; they do not create it."],"lt":"t-heart-conduction","lid":"W3-291","comp":65,"competencyId":"cv-cardiac-nerves"},{"id":"L03596","dok":2,"q":"Why is the AV bundle described as the only electrical bridge between the atria and the ventricles?","a":"The fibrous skeleton insulates the atria from the ventricles everywhere except at the AV bundle.","options":["The fibrous skeleton blocks the signal everywhere else","It is the largest bundle of nerves in the heart","It carries blood between the atria and the ventricles","It is the only cardiac tissue with gap junctions"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The fibrous skeleton is a set of dense connective tissue rings around the four valves. Connective tissue does not conduct the electrical signal, so this layer insulates the atria from the ventricles all the way around. The AV bundle is the one strand of conducting tissue that passes through it, so every signal to the ventricles has to cross there.","notes":["Correct. The insulating fibrous skeleton leaves the AV bundle as the only path.","The AV bundle is specialized cardiac muscle, not a bundle of nerves. The nerves that reach the heart only adjust the rate and do not carry the beat from atria to ventricles.","Blood passes from the atria to the ventricles through the atrioventricular valves, not through the AV bundle. The AV bundle carries an electrical signal, not blood.","Gap junctions link cardiomyocytes throughout the heart, in both the atria and the ventricles. They are not unique to the AV bundle, so they cannot explain why it is the only bridge."],"lt":"t-heart-conduction","lid":"W3-292","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L03597","dok":2,"q":"The SA node sits in the wall of the right atrium. Which chambers does its signal reach first?","a":"Both atria.","options":["Left ventricle only","Both atria","Right ventricle only","Both ventricles"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Because the SA node sits in the wall of the right atrium, its signal spreads across both atria first, and the atria contract. Only after that does the signal reach the AV node, pause, and travel through the AV bundle to the ventricles. Placing the pacemaker in an atrium is what makes the atria contract before the ventricles.","notes":["The left ventricle receives the signal through the left bundle branch and Purkinje fibers, after the atria. It is reached late, not first.","Correct. The signal spreads across both atria before anything else.","The right ventricle receives the signal through the right bundle branch, late in the pathway. It is never the first chamber to receive the signal from the SA node.","The ventricles receive the signal last, after it has crossed the atria and passed through the AV node and AV bundle. They would be the answer for where the Purkinje fibers deliver the signal."],"lt":"t-heart-conduction","lid":"W3-293","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L03598","dok":3,"q":"In atrial fibrillation the atria quiver instead of contracting, but the ventricles keep pumping. What is the main effect?","a":"The ventricles fill less completely, but blood still circulates.","options":["The ventricles fill less completely, but blood still circulates","The ventricles stop receiving any blood","All blood flow stops immediately","The pulmonary circuit stops while the systemic circuit continues"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Much of ventricular filling happens as blood flows from the atria into the relaxed ventricles, and atrial contraction adds a final top-off. If the atria only quiver, that last push is lost, so the ventricles fill less completely and pump somewhat less. Because the ventricles still contract, blood keeps circulating, which is why atrial fibrillation is common and treatable rather than an immediate emergency.","notes":["Correct. Filling is less efficient, but the ventricles keep the blood moving.","Blood still flows from the atria into the ventricles through the open atrioventricular valves while the ventricles relax. Losing the atrial contraction reduces filling but does not stop it.","Blood flow stops only when the ventricles fail to pump, as in ventricular fibrillation. Here the ventricles are still contracting, so blood keeps moving.","Both circuits are driven by the ventricles, which are still working. Losing atrial contraction affects both sides and does not shut down one circuit."],"lt":"t-heart-conduction","lid":"W3-294","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L03599","dok":2,"q":"In ventricular fibrillation, the signals in the ventricles are rapid and disorganized. Why is this life-threatening?","a":"The ventricles cannot pump blood effectively.","options":["The atria stop receiving blood from the veins","The coronary sinus overfills with blood","The valves seal shut and cannot open again","The ventricles quiver and move almost no blood"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Pumping depends on the ventricle walls squeezing together in order, from the apex upward. In ventricular fibrillation the signals are chaotic, so the muscle only quivers and moves little or no blood into the aorta and pulmonary trunk. The body and the heart wall itself lose their blood supply within minutes, which is why it is treated as an emergency with a defibrillator.","notes":["The atria still receive blood from the veins in ventricular fibrillation. The failure is in the ventricles, which cannot push blood out.","The coronary sinus drains the heart wall and is not the reason this rhythm is deadly. The danger is that the ventricles stop sending blood to the body.","The valves open and close with pressure changes and are not sealed shut by disorganized signals. The problem is a lack of effective contraction, not stuck valves.","Correct. Quivering ventricles move almost no blood."],"lt":"t-heart-conduction","lid":"W3-295","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L03600","dok":3,"q":"How does the order of the conduction pathway match the pathway of blood through the heart?","a":"Electrical order makes the atria fill the ventricles before the ventricles eject blood.","options":["The atria are signaled first, filling the ventricles before they pump","All four chambers are signaled at once so blood moves in both directions","The signal follows the blood from the aorta back to the venae cavae","The ventricles are signaled first so they push blood up into the atria"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Blood moves from the atria into the ventricles and then out into the great arteries. The conduction system mirrors this: the SA node fires the atria first, the AV node delays the signal while the atria empty, and then the bundle branches and Purkinje fibers fire the ventricles from the apex upward. Electrical order produces mechanical order, so blood keeps moving forward.","notes":["Correct. The atria fire first and fill the ventricles, then the ventricles fire and pump.","The atria and ventricles are deliberately signaled at different times, with the AV node delay between them. Contracting all four chambers together would leave the ventricles underfilled.","The signal travels through conduction tissue inside the heart, not along the blood vessels. It starts in the SA node in the right atrium, not in the aorta.","Blood never moves from the ventricles up into the atria in a normal heart; the atrioventricular valves prevent it. Signaling the ventricles first would mean they contract before they are full."],"lt":"t-heart-conduction","lid":"W3-296","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L03601","dok":2,"q":"Why does damage near the interventricular septum threaten the conduction system?","a":"The AV bundle and bundle branches run through the septum.","options":["The AV bundle and bundle branches run through the septum","The SA node lies deep within the ventricular septum","The coronary sinus runs down the inside of the septum","The fossa ovalis lies in the ventricular septum"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The AV bundle enters the top of the interventricular septum and splits into the right and left bundle branches, which run down both sides of it toward the apex. Damage to the septum, such as an infarction after an LAD blockage, can cut these pathways and block the signal to one or both ventricles.","notes":["Correct. The septum carries the AV bundle and both bundle branches.","The SA node sits in the wall of the right atrium near the opening of the superior vena cava, not in the ventricular septum. Choose that location for the pacemaker.","The coronary sinus runs on the posterior surface of the heart in the groove between the atria and ventricles. It is a vein draining the heart wall, not a conduction pathway.","The fossa ovalis is a depression in the interatrial septum, the remnant of the fetal foramen ovale. It lies in the wall between the atria and has nothing to do with conduction."],"lt":"t-heart-conduction","lid":"W3-297","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L03602","dok":3,"q":"Which is more immediately life-threatening, failure of atrial conduction or failure of ventricular conduction?","a":"Ventricular failure, because the ventricles pump blood out of the heart.","options":["Neither, because nerves can take over the heartbeat","Both equally, because all four chambers pump equally","Ventricular failure, since ventricles pump blood out","Atrial failure, because the atria hold the pacemaker"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The ventricles are the main pumps that send blood into the pulmonary trunk and aorta. If atrial conduction fails, the ventricles still contract and blood still circulates, as in atrial fibrillation, which is common and treatable. If ventricular conduction fails, as in ventricular fibrillation, the ventricles cannot pump and blood flow stops, which is a life-threatening emergency.","notes":["Nerves cannot take over the heartbeat; they only adjust the rate set by the conduction system. Fibrillating ventricles need a defibrillator, not nervous input.","The chambers do not pump equally; the thick-walled ventricles do most of the work, while the thin-walled atria mainly fill the ventricles. That difference is why ventricular failure is worse.","Correct. Without effective ventricles, blood stops leaving the heart.","The SA node is in the right atrium, but the atria themselves only top off the ventricles. Losing atrial contraction reduces filling while the ventricles keep the blood moving."],"lt":"t-heart-conduction","lid":"W3-299","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L03918","dok":2,"q":"In the cardiac conduction pathway, after the SA node fires, where does the signal travel next?","a":"Across both atria.","options":["Across both atria","Into the AV node","Through the Purkinje fibers","Down the bundle branches"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The SA node, the sinoatrial node in the wall of the right atrium, is the pacemaker that fires first. Its signal spreads through both atria, and the atria contract. Only after crossing the atria does the signal reach the AV node, where it pauses before passing on to the ventricles.","notes":["Correct. The signal from the SA node spreads across both atria first.","The AV node is the next named structure, but the signal first has to spread across the atrial walls to reach it. The AV node would be the answer if the question started after the signal had crossed the atria.","The Purkinje fibers are the last part of the pathway, spreading the signal through the ventricle walls. They would be the answer if the question started at the bundle branches.","The bundle branches run down the interventricular septum and come after the AV node and AV bundle. They would be the answer if the question started at the AV bundle."],"lt":"t-pathway-conduction-sequence","lid":"W3-724","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L03919","dok":2,"q":"In the cardiac conduction pathway, after the signal spreads across the atria, which structure does it reach next?","a":"The AV node.","options":["AV bundle","SA node","Bundle branches","AV node"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The AV node, the atrioventricular node in the floor of the right atrium near the interatrial septum, collects the signal after it crosses the atria. The signal pauses there briefly, which gives the atria time to finish emptying into the ventricles. Then the signal passes into the AV bundle.","notes":["The AV bundle comes one step after the AV node, carrying the signal into the interventricular septum. It would be the answer if the question started at the AV node.","The SA node is where the signal starts, before it spreads across the atria. It would be the answer if the question asked which structure fires first.","The bundle branches run down both sides of the interventricular septum, two steps after the AV node. They would be the answer if the question started at the AV bundle.","Correct. The signal crosses the atria and then reaches the AV node."],"lt":"t-pathway-conduction-sequence","lid":"W3-725","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L03920","dok":2,"q":"In the cardiac conduction pathway, after the brief delay at the AV node, the signal passes into which structure?","a":"The AV bundle (bundle of His).","options":["Bundle branches","SA node","Purkinje fibers","AV bundle"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The AV bundle, also called the bundle of His, carries the signal from the AV node into the upper part of the interventricular septum. The fibrous skeleton insulates the atria from the ventricles, so the AV bundle is the only electrical bridge between them. It then splits into the right and left bundle branches.","notes":["The bundle branches come after the AV bundle, carrying the signal down both sides of the septum. They would be the answer if the question started at the AV bundle.","The SA node is the pacemaker where the signal begins, at the very start of the pathway. It would be the answer if the question asked which structure fires first.","The Purkinje fibers are the final part of the pathway in the ventricle walls. They would be the answer if the question started at the bundle branches.","Correct. The AV node passes the signal into the AV bundle."],"lt":"t-pathway-conduction-sequence","lid":"W3-726","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L03921","dok":2,"q":"In the cardiac conduction pathway, the AV bundle passes the signal to which structures next?","a":"The right and left bundle branches.","options":["Right and left bundle branches","AV node","Atrial walls","Purkinje fibers"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The AV bundle divides at the top of the interventricular septum into the right and left bundle branches. The bundle branches carry the signal down both sides of the septum toward the apex of the heart. From there the signal enters the Purkinje fibers.","notes":["Correct. The AV bundle splits into the right and left bundle branches.","The AV node comes before the AV bundle, not after it. It would be the answer if the question asked where the signal goes after crossing the atria.","The atrial walls receive the signal at the start of the pathway, right after the SA node fires. They would be the answer if the question started at the SA node.","The Purkinje fibers come after the bundle branches and spread the signal through the ventricle walls. They would be the answer if the question started at the bundle branches."],"lt":"t-pathway-conduction-sequence","lid":"W3-727","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L03922","dok":2,"q":"In the cardiac conduction pathway, the bundle branches pass the signal to which structures?","a":"The Purkinje fibers.","options":["Purkinje fibers","SA node","AV bundle","AV node"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The bundle branches carry the signal down the interventricular septum toward the apex. There they connect with the Purkinje fibers, which run throughout the walls of both ventricles. The Purkinje fibers deliver the signal to the contractile cells, so the ventricles contract from the apex upward.","notes":["Correct. The bundle branches hand the signal to the Purkinje fibers.","The SA node is the pacemaker at the very start of the pathway. It would be the answer if the question asked which structure sets the heart rate.","The AV bundle comes before the bundle branches, carrying the signal into the septum. It would be the answer if the question started at the AV node.","The AV node is two steps earlier, where the signal pauses on the floor of the right atrium. It would be the answer if the question started at the atria."],"lt":"t-pathway-conduction-sequence","lid":"W3-728","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L03923","dok":2,"q":"In the cardiac conduction pathway, the Purkinje fibers deliver the signal to which cells?","a":"The contractile cells of the ventricular walls.","options":["Conducting cells of the AV node","Conducting cells of the SA node","Contractile cells of the ventricular walls","Contractile cells of the atrial walls"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The Purkinje fibers are the last part of the conduction system. They spread through the walls of both ventricles and hand the signal to the contractile cells, the ordinary working cardiac muscle cells. Those cells then contract, starting at the apex and moving upward, which pushes blood toward the semilunar valves.","notes":["The AV node is a conducting structure in the floor of the right atrium, near the middle of the pathway. It would be the answer if the question started at the atria.","The SA node is where each heartbeat's signal begins, not where it ends. It would be the answer if the question asked which structure acts as the pacemaker.","Correct. The Purkinje fibers deliver the signal to the contractile cells of the ventricles.","The contractile cells of the atrial walls receive the signal at the start of the pathway, straight from the SA node. They would be the answer if the question started at the SA node."],"lt":"t-pathway-conduction-sequence","lid":"W3-729","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L03967","dok":2,"q":"Which sequence correctly traces the cardiac conduction pathway from the pacemaker to ventricular contraction?","a":"SA node, across the atria, AV node, AV bundle, bundle branches, Purkinje fibers.","options":["SA node, across the atria, AV bundle, AV node, bundle branches, Purkinje fibers","SA node, across the atria, AV node, AV bundle, bundle branches, Purkinje fibers","SA node, across the atria, AV node, AV bundle, Purkinje fibers, bundle branches","AV node, across the atria, SA node, AV bundle, bundle branches, Purkinje fibers"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The SA node in the right atrium fires first and the signal spreads across both atria. It reaches the AV node, pauses briefly, and then enters the AV bundle in the interventricular septum. The bundle branches carry it down toward the apex, and the Purkinje fibers spread it through the ventricle walls so the ventricles contract.","notes":["This sequence swaps the AV node and AV bundle. The AV node collects the signal from the atria first and then passes it into the AV bundle in the septum.","Correct. This is the conduction pathway in order.","This sequence swaps the bundle branches and Purkinje fibers. The bundle branches run down the septum first, and the Purkinje fibers at the end spread the signal into the ventricle walls.","This sequence starts at the AV node. The SA node is the pacemaker that fires first, so it must lead the pathway, and the AV node comes after the atria."],"lt":"t-pathway-integrated-tracing","lid":"W3-774","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L03978","dok":3,"q":"Damage to the AV node prevents it from passing signals normally to the ventricles. What is the most likely result?","a":"The atria and ventricles beat out of step (heart block).","options":["The atria quiver from rapid, disorganized signals","The ventricles quiver and pump no blood","The atria and ventricles beat out of step","The SA node stops firing and the atria stop"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The AV node collects the signal from the atria and, through the AV bundle, is the only electrical route to the ventricles, because the fibrous skeleton insulates everything else. When the signal is delayed or stopped at the AV node, the atria and ventricles can no longer beat in their normal order. This is called heart block.","notes":["Rapid, disorganized signals that make the atria quiver describe atrial fibrillation. It would be the answer if the question asked what happens when chaotic signals arise in the atria.","Quivering ventricles that pump no blood describe ventricular fibrillation, a life-threatening emergency. It would be the answer if the question asked what a defibrillator is used to treat.","Correct. A damaged AV node disconnects the atria from the ventricles, so they beat out of step.","The SA node lies upstream of the AV node, in the wall of the right atrium near the superior vena cava, so damage at the AV node does not stop it. The atria keep receiving signals from the SA node. This would fit a question about a failing SA node."],"lt":"t-pathway-integrated-tracing","lid":"W3-786","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L04019","dok":1,"q":"Which part of the cardiac conduction system is the natural pacemaker of the heart?","a":"SA node","options":["Purkinje fibers","AV bundle","AV node","SA node"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The sinoatrial (SA) node sits in the wall of the right atrium near the opening of the superior vena cava. It fires faster than any other part of the conduction system, about 60 to 100 times a minute, so it sets the pace for the whole heart. Its signal spreads across both atria and then on to the AV node.","notes":["Purkinje fibers are the last step in the pathway, spreading the signal through the walls of both ventricles. They would be the answer if the question asked which fibers deliver the signal to the ventricular muscle.","The AV bundle carries the signal from the AV node into the interventricular septum. Choose it if the question asked which structure is the only electrical bridge from the atria to the ventricles.","The AV node sits in the floor of the right atrium and briefly delays the signal before passing it to the ventricles. Pick it if the question asked where the signal pauses.","Correct. The SA node is the pacemaker that sets the heart rate."],"lt":"t-imagefree-structure-recognition","lid":"W3-827","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L04020","dok":2,"q":"At which point in the conduction pathway does the signal pause briefly so the atria can finish emptying before the ventricles contract?","a":"AV node","options":["SA node","Purkinje fibers","Bundle branches","AV node"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The atrioventricular (AV) node lies in the floor of the right atrium near the interatrial septum. When the signal from the atria reaches it, the AV node holds it for a moment before passing it on. That short delay gives the atria time to push their last blood into the ventricles before the ventricles contract.","notes":["The SA node starts each heartbeat in the wall of the right atrium, one step earlier in the pathway. Pick it if the question asked which structure is the natural pacemaker.","Purkinje fibers spread the signal through the ventricle walls at the end of the pathway, with no delay. Choose them if the question asked which fibers trigger the ventricular muscle to contract.","The bundle branches carry the signal down the right and left sides of the interventricular septum toward the apex. They would be the answer if the question asked which structures run down both sides of the septum.","Correct. The AV node delays the signal so the atria can finish emptying."],"lt":"t-imagefree-structure-recognition","lid":"W3-828","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L04021","dok":2,"q":"Which part of the conduction system is the only electrical bridge from the atria to the ventricles?","a":"AV bundle","options":["SA node","Purkinje fibers","Bundle branches","AV bundle"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The atria and ventricles are separated by the fibrous skeleton, rings of dense connective tissue that do not conduct electrical signals. The AV bundle, also called the bundle of His, is the one route through that insulation, carrying the signal from the AV node into the top of the interventricular septum. A block at this point can disconnect the beat of the atria from the beat of the ventricles.","notes":["The SA node starts the heartbeat in the right atrium but does not cross into the ventricles. Pick it if the question asked which structure sets the heart rate.","Purkinje fibers lie in the walls of the ventricles and are the final step of the pathway. Choose them if the question asked which fibers deliver the signal to the ventricular muscle cells.","The bundle branches are already on the ventricular side, carrying the signal down both sides of the septum after the AV bundle. They would be the answer if the question asked which structures run toward the apex.","Correct. The AV bundle is the only electrical link through the fibrous skeleton."],"lt":"t-imagefree-structure-recognition","lid":"W3-829","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L04022","dok":2,"q":"Which conduction fibers spread the signal through the walls of both ventricles?","a":"Purkinje fibers","options":["Bundle branches","AV bundle","Purkinje fibers","SA node"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Purkinje fibers are the last part of the conduction pathway. They branch out from the bundle branches near the apex and run through the walls of both ventricles, delivering the signal to the contractile muscle cells. Because the signal reaches the apex first, the ventricles contract from the apex upward and squeeze blood toward the arteries at the top.","notes":["The bundle branches carry the signal down the right and left sides of the interventricular septum toward the apex, one step before the Purkinje fibers. They would be the answer if the question asked which structures run down the septum.","The AV bundle carries the signal from the AV node into the top of the interventricular septum, well before it reaches the ventricle walls. Pick it if the question asked for the only electrical bridge between atria and ventricles.","Correct. Purkinje fibers spread the signal through the ventricular walls.","The SA node starts the signal in the wall of the right atrium. Choose it if the question asked which structure is the pacemaker."],"lt":"t-imagefree-structure-recognition","lid":"W3-830","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L04094","dok":2,"q":"After disease damages the AV node, the atria and ventricles beat out of step with each other. Which disorder fits?","a":"Heart block.","options":["Heart block","Cardiac tamponade","Atrial fibrillation","Ventricular fibrillation"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The fibrous skeleton insulates the atria from the ventricles, so the AV node and AV bundle are the only electrical route between them. Heart block is the disorder in which the signal is delayed or stopped at the AV node. When the signal cannot get through reliably, the atria keep following the SA node while the ventricles fall out of step with them.","notes":["Correct. Heart block is a delay or stop of the signal at the AV node, which lets the atria and ventricles beat out of step.","Cardiac tamponade is fluid in the pericardial cavity that squeezes the heart from outside. It limits filling but does not disconnect the atrial and ventricular rhythms.","Atrial fibrillation is rapid, disorganized signaling within the atria themselves, so the atria quiver instead of contracting. Pick it if the problem started in the atrial walls rather than at the AV node.","Ventricular fibrillation is chaotic signaling in the ventricles, so they quiver and pump no blood. It would be the answer for a sudden collapse with no effective heartbeat, not a steady mismatch between atria and ventricles."],"lt":"t-clinical-application-and-misconceptions","lid":"W3-905","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L04095","dok":2,"q":"A patient's ventricles quiver rapidly and move no blood at all. Which rhythm disorder is this?","a":"Ventricular fibrillation.","options":["Ventricular fibrillation","Heart block","Atrial fibrillation","Tachycardia"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Fibrillation means rapid, disorganized electrical signals that make heart muscle quiver instead of contracting together. When this happens in the ventricles, the pumping chambers cannot squeeze in a coordinated way, so no blood is pushed into the aorta or pulmonary trunk. That is why ventricular fibrillation is a life-threatening emergency.","notes":["Correct. Chaotic signals make the ventricles quiver, so they pump no blood.","Heart block is a delay or stop of the signal at the AV node, so the atria and ventricles beat out of step. The ventricles still contract, just not in time with the atria.","Atrial fibrillation is the same kind of chaotic quivering, but in the atria. The ventricles can still pump, so choose it when the upper chambers quiver and blood is still moving.","Tachycardia is an abnormally fast resting heart rate in which the beats are still organized, so blood keeps moving. It would fit a question about a heart beating too fast to fill well between beats."],"lt":"t-clinical-application-and-misconceptions","lid":"W3-906","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L04096","dok":2,"q":"A patient's SA node can no longer pace the heart normally. Which treatment takes over its job?","a":"An artificial pacemaker.","options":["A replacement heart valve","A coronary artery bypass","An artificial pacemaker","A defibrillator shock"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The SA node is the natural pacemaker, a cluster of conducting cells in the right atrial wall that sets the pace of every heartbeat. An artificial pacemaker is a small implanted device that delivers a timed electrical signal to the heart, so it can take over for a failing SA node or carry the signal past a block at the AV node. It restores an ordered beat by supplying the signal the heart is no longer making on its own.","notes":["A replacement valve fixes a valve that leaks or is narrowed, which is a problem of one-way blood flow. Pick it for regurgitation or stenosis, not for a pacing failure.","A coronary bypass reroutes blood around a blocked coronary artery to feed the myocardium. It would be the answer for coronary artery disease, where heart muscle is starved of blood, not for a pacing problem.","Correct. An implanted pacemaker supplies the timed signal that the failing SA node can no longer provide.","A defibrillator delivers one strong shock that stops chaotic signals all at once so the SA node can restart the rhythm. It is the answer for ventricular fibrillation, but it does not provide an ongoing beat when the SA node itself fails."],"lt":"t-clinical-application-and-misconceptions","lid":"W3-907","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L04127","dok":2,"q":"A student says that nerves from the brain start each heartbeat. Which statement corrects this?","a":"The SA node starts it, and nerves only adjust the rate.","options":["The AV node starts each beat, and the SA node delays it","The SA node starts it, and nerves only adjust the rate","The Purkinje fibers start each beat, and the atria follow","The vagus nerve starts each beat, and the SA node relays it"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The heart has autorhythmicity, the built-in ability to generate its own rhythm. The SA node, in the wall of the right atrium, fires first and sets the pace of the whole heart without any signal from the nervous system. Autonomic nerves reaching the heart through the cardiac plexus only adjust that pace, with sympathetic nerves speeding it and the vagus nerve slowing it. A transplanted heart, whose nerves have been cut, still beats.","notes":["The AV node, in the floor of the right atrium, delays the signal briefly before passing it to the ventricles, and the SA node fires before it. Choose the AV node for a question about the built-in delay.","Correct. The SA node sets the beat on its own, and autonomic nerves only speed it up or slow it down.","Purkinje fibers are the last part of the pathway, spreading the signal through the ventricle walls. Pick them for a question about how the signal reaches the ventricular muscle, not where it begins.","The vagus nerve carries parasympathetic fibers that slow the heart rate, but it does not start the beat. Pick the vagus nerve for a question about which nerve slows the heart."],"lt":"t-clinical-application-and-misconceptions","lid":"W3-941","comp":65,"competencyId":"cv-cardiac-nerves"},{"id":"L04134","dok":2,"q":"Why is the brief delay of the signal at the AV node useful for pumping?","a":"It lets the atria finish emptying before the ventricles contract.","options":["It lets the ventricles finish emptying before the atria contract","It lets the atria finish emptying before the ventricles contract","It gives the SA node time to recover between beats","It keeps the semilunar valves shut until the atria fill"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"In the conduction pathway, the signal from the SA node spreads across the atria, which contract, and then reaches the AV node in the floor of the right atrium. The AV node holds the signal for a brief moment before passing it to the AV bundle and the ventricles. That pause gives the atria time to finish emptying their blood into the ventricles, so the ventricles are full before they contract.","notes":["This reverses the order. The atria contract first and the ventricles second, and the delay protects that atria-then-ventricles order.","Correct. The pause lets the ventricles fill completely before they contract.","The SA node fires on its own schedule at the start of the pathway. The AV node delay happens after the SA node has already fired, so it is not there to give the SA node a rest.","The semilunar valves open and close with the pressure of blood in the ventricles and great arteries, not with the AV node signal. Choose them for a question about preventing backflow from the aorta or pulmonary trunk."],"lt":"t-clinical-application-and-misconceptions","lid":"W3-948","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L04154","dok":2,"q":"Why are the gap junctions in the intercalated discs important for the myocardium?","a":"They pass the signal directly between cells.","options":["They anchor cells so they do not pull apart","They insulate the atria from the ventricles","They pass the signal directly between cells","They set the pace of the heartbeat"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Intercalated discs are the specialized junctions where neighboring cardiac muscle cells meet end to end. The gap junctions within them are channels that let the electrical signal pass directly from one cell to the next. Because the signal spreads so quickly from cell to cell, the myocardium contracts as one coordinated unit rather than cell by cell.","notes":["Anchoring cells so they do not pull apart is the job of desmosomes, the other junction in the intercalated disc. Pick desmosomes for a question about holding cells together during forceful contraction.","Insulating the atria from the ventricles is the job of the fibrous skeleton, the dense connective tissue rings around the valves. Choose it for a question about why the AV bundle is the only electrical route between the chambers.","Correct. Gap junctions pass the signal cell to cell so the myocardium contracts as a unit.","Setting the pace of the heartbeat is the job of the SA node, the pacemaker in the right atrial wall. Gap junctions spread the signal but do not start it."],"lt":"t-clinical-application-and-misconceptions","lid":"W3-970","comp":60,"competencyId":"cv-cardiac-muscle"},{"id":"L04155","dok":2,"q":"Why are the desmosomes in the intercalated discs important for the myocardium?","a":"They keep cells attached during contraction.","options":["They pass the signal from cell to cell","They keep cells attached during contraction","They anchor the chordae tendineae","They form rings around the heart valves"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Desmosomes are anchoring junctions within the intercalated discs, the places where neighboring cardiac muscle cells meet end to end. The heart contracts forcefully with every beat for an entire lifetime, which tends to pull the cells apart. Desmosomes bind the cells tightly together, so the myocardium holds together and pulls as a single unit.","notes":["Passing the electrical signal between cells is the job of gap junctions, the other junction in the intercalated disc. Pick gap junctions for a question about how the myocardium contracts as one unit.","Correct. Desmosomes hold cardiac muscle cells together against the pull of each beat.","The chordae tendineae are anchored to the ventricle wall by the papillary muscles. Choose papillary muscles for a question about what holds the cords of the AV valves in place.","The rings of dense connective tissue around the valves are the fibrous skeleton. It would be the answer for a question about what anchors the valves and insulates the atria from the ventricles."],"lt":"t-clinical-application-and-misconceptions","lid":"W3-971","comp":60,"competencyId":"cv-cardiac-muscle"},{"id":"L04163","dok":2,"q":"Why does ventricular fibrillation need a shock from a defibrillator?","a":"It stops the chaos so the SA node can take over.","options":["It removes fluid from the pericardial cavity","It stops the chaos so the SA node can take over","It permanently replaces the SA node as the pacemaker","It reopens a blocked coronary artery"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"In ventricular fibrillation, rapid, disorganized signals make the ventricles quiver instead of contracting, so they move no blood. A defibrillator delivers one strong shock that stops all the chaotic electrical activity at the same moment. With that chaos cleared, the SA node, the heart's natural pacemaker, has a chance to restart the heart in its normal rhythm.","notes":["Removing fluid from the pericardial cavity treats cardiac tamponade, where the heart is squeezed from outside. Choose it for a filling problem, not chaotic electrical signals.","Correct. The shock clears the chaos so the SA node can restart a normal rhythm.","Taking over permanently as the pacemaker is the job of an implanted artificial pacemaker, which delivers a timed signal with every beat. A defibrillator gives a single shock rather than an ongoing beat.","Reopening a blocked coronary artery treats a heart attack by restoring blood to the myocardium. Pick it for coronary artery disease, not a rhythm problem."],"lt":"t-clinical-application-and-misconceptions","lid":"W3-981","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L04164","dok":2,"q":"Why is atrial fibrillation usually less immediately life-threatening than ventricular fibrillation?","a":"The ventricles can still pump blood.","options":["The AV node blocks every atrial signal","The SA node keeps full control of the atria","The ventricles can still pump blood","The atria pump most of the blood to the body"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"In atrial fibrillation, chaotic signals make the atria quiver instead of contracting, but signals still pass through the AV node and the ventricles keep contracting. The ventricles are the main pumps, and the atria mainly top them off, so blood still moves to the lungs and body. In ventricular fibrillation, the ventricles themselves quiver and no blood is pumped at all, which is an immediate emergency.","notes":["If the AV node blocked every atrial signal, the ventricles would lose their normal signal entirely, which describes complete heart block. In atrial fibrillation, many signals still pass through the AV node, so the ventricles keep beating, though irregularly.","In atrial fibrillation, disorganized signals throughout the atria override the SA node's normal pace. Students choose this when they assume the pacemaker still controls the atria, which is exactly what is lost.","Correct. In atrial fibrillation the ventricles keep pumping, so blood still moves.","The atria are thin-walled chambers that mainly receive blood and top off the ventricles. The ventricles do the real pumping to the lungs and body, which is why losing them is far more dangerous."],"lt":"t-clinical-application-and-misconceptions","lid":"W3-982","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L04206","dok":1,"q":"Where is the SA node, the heart's pacemaker, located?","a":"In the wall of the right atrium near the superior vena cava.","options":["In the superior part of the interventricular septum","Throughout the walls of both ventricles","In the wall of the right atrium near the superior vena cava","In the floor of the right atrium near the interatrial septum"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The SA (sinoatrial) node sits in the wall of the right atrium near the opening of the superior vena cava. It fires faster than any other part of the conduction system, about 60 to 100 times a minute, so it sets the pace for the whole heart. From there the signal spreads across both atria toward the AV node.","notes":["That is where the AV bundle (bundle of His) runs, carrying the signal from the AV node into the septum. Choose it for the only electrical bridge between the atria and the ventricles.","That describes the Purkinje fibers, the last part of the pathway, which deliver the signal to the contractile cells of the ventricles. They would be the answer for the structures that spread the signal through the ventricle walls.","Correct. The SA node is in the right atrial wall near where the superior vena cava enters.","That is the location of the AV node, the second stop in the pathway, which delays the signal before passing it to the ventricles. Pick it if the question asked where the signal pauses."],"lt":"t-cv-conduction-anat","lid":"cv-conduction-anat-r1","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L04207","dok":1,"q":"What does the AV node do in the cardiac conduction pathway?","a":"It delays the signal briefly, then passes it on to the ventricles.","options":["Carries the signal down both sides of the interventricular septum","Delays the signal briefly, then passes it on to the ventricles","Spreads the signal through the walls of both ventricles","Fires first and sets the pace of the whole heart"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The AV (atrioventricular) node sits in the floor of the right atrium near the interatrial septum. The signal from the SA node reaches it after crossing the atria, and the AV node holds that signal for a brief moment before sending it into the AV bundle. That delay gives the atria time to finish emptying into the ventricles before the ventricles contract.","notes":["That is the job of the right and left bundle branches, which run down the septum toward the apex. They would be the answer for the step between the AV bundle and the Purkinje fibers.","Correct. The AV node is the brief holding point between the atria and the ventricles.","That is the job of the Purkinje fibers, which deliver the signal to the contractile cells of the ventricle walls. Choose them for the final step of the pathway.","That is the job of the SA node, the pacemaker in the wall of the right atrium. Pick it if the question asked which structure starts each heartbeat."],"lt":"t-cv-conduction-anat","lid":"cv-conduction-anat-r2","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L04208","dok":1,"q":"The atria and ventricles are electrically insulated from each other. Which structure is the only electrical bridge that carries the signal across?","a":"The AV bundle (bundle of His).","options":["The fibrous skeleton","The AV bundle","The Purkinje fibers","The moderator band"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The fibrous skeleton, a set of dense connective tissue rings around the valves, insulates the atria from the ventricles, so the signal cannot spread directly from atrial muscle to ventricular muscle. The AV bundle, also called the bundle of His, is the one route through. It carries the signal from the AV node into the superior part of the interventricular septum, which is why a block here disconnects the atria from the ventricles.","notes":["The fibrous skeleton is the insulator, not the bridge. Its rings of dense connective tissue anchor the valves and stop the signal from crossing anywhere except the AV bundle; pick it if the question asked what electrically separates the atria from the ventricles.","Correct. The AV bundle is the single electrical path from the atria into the ventricles.","The Purkinje fibers spread the signal through the ventricle walls at the end of the pathway, well after it has crossed from the atria. Choose them for the structures that deliver the signal to the ventricular muscle cells.","The moderator band is a muscular band in the right ventricle that carries part of the conduction system to the anterior papillary muscle. It lies entirely within the ventricle, so it would be the answer only for a question about that band in the right ventricle."],"lt":"t-cv-conduction-anat","lid":"cv-conduction-anat-r3","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L04209","dok":2,"q":"Which sequence gives the cardiac conduction pathway from the pacemaker to the ventricle walls?","a":"SA node, AV node, AV bundle, bundle branches, Purkinje fibers.","options":["SA node, AV node, AV bundle, bundle branches, Purkinje fibers","AV node, SA node, AV bundle, bundle branches, Purkinje fibers","SA node, AV node, AV bundle, Purkinje fibers, bundle branches","SA node, AV bundle, AV node, bundle branches, Purkinje fibers"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The signal starts at the SA node in the right atrium and spreads across both atria to the AV node, where it pauses briefly. It then travels through the AV bundle into the interventricular septum, down the right and left bundle branches toward the apex, and out through the Purkinje fibers into the ventricle walls. This order makes the atria contract first and the ventricles second, with the ventricles squeezing from the apex upward.","notes":["Correct. Pacemaker, pause, bridge, branches, then the ventricle walls.","This starts at the AV node, but the SA node is the pacemaker that fires first. Students pick this when they mix up the two nodes because their names look alike.","This puts the Purkinje fibers before the bundle branches. The branches carry the signal down the septum first, and the Purkinje fibers spread it through the ventricle walls at the very end.","This puts the AV bundle before the AV node. The node is the holding point in the floor of the right atrium, and the bundle carries the signal out of it into the septum."],"lt":"t-cv-conduction-anat","lid":"cv-conduction-anat-r4","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L04210","dok":2,"q":"Why is the brief pause at the AV node essential to a normal heartbeat?","a":"It holds the signal long enough for the atria to finish emptying into the ventricles before the ventricles contract.","options":["It keeps the signal from spreading across the walls of the atria","It lets the atria finish emptying before the ventricles contract","It lets the ventricles contract before the atria begin to contract","It lets the SA node rest so it can fire again on the next beat"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The atria contract first to top off the ventricles, and the ventricles contract second to pump blood out. If the signal passed straight to the ventricles, both would contract almost at once and the ventricles would squeeze before they were full. The AV node's short delay holds the signal just long enough for the atria to finish emptying, which keeps the two halves of the heartbeat in the right order.","notes":["The signal has already spread across both atria by the time it reaches the AV node, and that spread is what makes the atria contract. Pick a statement about atrial spread if the question asked what happens between the SA node and the AV node.","Correct. The delay keeps atrial contraction ahead of ventricular contraction.","This reverses the normal order. The atria contract first and the ventricles second, and the delay exists to protect that order, not to flip it.","The SA node fires again on its own rhythm and does not need the AV node to pause for it. Students choose this when they think of the delay as a rest period for the pacemaker rather than timing between chambers."],"lt":"t-cv-conduction-anat","lid":"cv-conduction-anat-r5","comp":64,"competencyId":"cv-conduction-anat"},{"id":"L04214","dok":1,"q":"What are intercalated discs?","a":"The specialized junctions where neighboring cardiac muscle cells meet end to end.","options":["Junctions where neighboring cardiac muscle cells meet end to end","Fibrous cords that tie the valve cusps to the papillary muscles","Rings of dense connective tissue around the four valve openings","Clusters of conducting cells that set the pace of the heartbeat"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Intercalated discs are the specialized junctions where neighboring cardiomyocytes (cardiac muscle cells) join end to end. Under the microscope they appear as dark lines crossing the fibers, and they are one of the features that identify cardiac muscle tissue. They hold the cells together and let the signal to contract pass from one cell to the next, so the heart contracts as one coordinated unit.","notes":["Correct. Intercalated discs are the end-to-end junctions between cardiac muscle cells.","That describes the chordae tendineae of the AV valves. They would be the answer for the structures that keep the AV valve cusps from flipping back into the atria.","That describes the fibrous skeleton of the heart, which anchors the valves and insulates the atria from the ventricles. Pick it if the question asked what electrically separates the upper and lower chambers.","That describes the SA node, the pacemaker in the wall of the right atrium. Choose it for a question about which structure starts each heartbeat."],"lt":"t-cv-cardiac-muscle","lid":"cv-cardiac-muscle-r1","comp":60,"competencyId":"cv-cardiac-muscle"},{"id":"L04215","dok":2,"q":"An intercalated disc contains two kinds of cell junction. Which statement correctly matches each junction to its job?","a":"Desmosomes anchor the cells so they do not pull apart; gap junctions let the contraction signal pass from cell to cell.","options":["Desmosomes anchor the cells together; gap junctions let the signal pass between cells","Desmosomes anchor the cells together; tight junctions let the signal pass between cells","Tight junctions seal the cells together; desmosomes let the signal pass between cells","Gap junctions anchor the cells together; desmosomes let the signal pass between cells"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"An intercalated disc holds two kinds of junction with two different jobs. Desmosomes are anchoring junctions that rivet neighboring cells together so they do not pull apart when the heart contracts. Gap junctions are channels that connect the insides of neighboring cells, so the contraction signal passes directly from cell to cell and the myocardium contracts as a unit.","notes":["Correct. Desmosomes hold the cells together, and gap junctions pass the signal.","Desmosomes are right, but tight junctions seal cells and do not carry a signal. The signal-passing junction in the intercalated disc is the gap junction.","Tight junctions seal neighboring epithelial cells to block leaks between them, and they are not the junctions of the intercalated disc. Pick tight junctions for a question about how epithelium keeps material from slipping between its cells.","This swaps the two jobs. Gap junctions are open channels between cells, which suits passing a signal, while desmosomes are mechanical anchors."],"lt":"t-cv-cardiac-muscle","lid":"cv-cardiac-muscle-r2","comp":60,"competencyId":"cv-cardiac-muscle"},{"id":"L04216","dok":2,"q":"Which description fits a cardiomyocyte, a cardiac muscle cell?","a":"A short, branching cell with usually one central nucleus, packed with mitochondria to fuel its constant work.","options":["A spindle-shaped cell with one central nucleus and no visible striations","A short, branching cell with many peripheral nuclei and few mitochondria","A long, cylindrical cell with many nuclei pushed to the edge of the cell","A short, branching cell with usually one central nucleus and many mitochondria"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"A cardiomyocyte is a short cell that branches to connect with its neighbors, usually with a single central nucleus, and it is striated like skeletal muscle. It is packed with mitochondria, about a quarter of the cell volume, because the heart works constantly for a lifetime and needs a steady energy supply. These features, plus intercalated discs, are how you recognize cardiac muscle on a slide.","notes":["That describes a smooth muscle cell, found in the walls of hollow organs and blood vessels. It would be the answer for a muscle cell that has no striations.","Short and branching is right, but cardiomyocytes usually have one central nucleus and are rich in mitochondria. Students choose this when they blend the features of cardiac and skeletal muscle.","That describes a skeletal muscle fiber, which is long, cylindrical, and multinucleated with its nuclei at the edge. Pick it if the question asked about the muscle cells attached to bones.","Correct. Short, branching, one central nucleus, and rich in mitochondria."],"lt":"t-cv-cardiac-muscle","lid":"cv-cardiac-muscle-r3","comp":60,"competencyId":"cv-cardiac-muscle"},{"id":"L04217","dok":1,"q":"How do the sympathetic and parasympathetic nerves affect the heart rate?","a":"Sympathetic nerves speed the heart rate; parasympathetic fibers in the vagus nerve slow it.","options":["Parasympathetic fibers in the phrenic nerve slow the rate; sympathetic nerves speed it","Sympathetic nerves speed the rate; parasympathetic fibers in the vagus nerve slow it","These nerves start each heartbeat, so the heart stops beating if they are cut","Sympathetic nerves slow the rate; parasympathetic fibers in the vagus nerve speed it"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The heart sets its own beat through the SA node, but autonomic nerves reaching the SA and AV nodes through the cardiac plexus can adjust the pace. Sympathetic nerves speed the heart rate, as during exercise or stress, and parasympathetic fibers carried in the vagus nerve slow it. These nerves only adjust the rate; they do not start the heartbeat.","notes":["The phrenic nerve runs to the diaphragm, not the heart. The parasympathetic fibers to the heart travel in the vagus nerve; pick the phrenic nerve for a question about which nerve reaches the diaphragm.","Correct. Sympathetic speeds the heart, and the vagus nerve slows it.","The heart is autorhythmic, meaning the SA node starts each beat on its own. A transplanted heart has had its nerves cut and still beats, which shows the nerves only adjust the rate.","This reverses the two divisions. The sympathetic division prepares the body for activity and speeds the heart, while the vagus nerve carries the parasympathetic fibers that slow it."],"lt":"t-cv-cardiac-nerves","lid":"cv-cardiac-nerves-r1","comp":65,"competencyId":"cv-cardiac-nerves"},{"id":"L04319","dok":2,"q":"What joins neighboring cardiac muscle cells, and which two kinds of cell junctions does it contain?","a":"Intercalated discs, which contain desmosomes and gap junctions.","options":["Intercalated discs, containing desmosomes and gap junctions","Intercalated discs, containing tight junctions and hemidesmosomes","T tubules, containing desmosomes and gap junctions","The endomysium, containing tight junctions and gap junctions"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Cardiac muscle cells are joined end to end by intercalated discs, which show up on a slide as dark lines crossing the fibers. Desmosomes in the discs hold the cells together so they do not pull apart as the heart contracts. Gap junctions are small channels that let the electrical signal pass directly from cell to cell, so the heart muscle contracts as a coordinated unit.","notes":["Correct. Intercalated discs join cardiac cells with desmosomes for adhesion and gap junctions for electrical coupling.","Intercalated discs are right, but tight junctions seal epithelial cells to stop leakage between them, and hemidesmosomes anchor epithelial cells to the basement membrane. Pick those junctions if the question asked about epithelium.","T tubules are inward tunnels of the sarcolemma that carry the signal into a single muscle cell, not connections between cells. They would be the answer if the question asked how a signal reaches deep inside a fiber.","The endomysium is the connective tissue sheath around each muscle fiber, not a junction between cells. Choose it if the question asked what wraps each skeletal muscle fiber."],"lt":"t-w4-muscle-tissue-types","lid":"w4-muscle-tissue-types-r3","comp":60,"competencyId":"cv-cardiac-muscle"}]},{"id":"m3-vessel-wall","title":"Blood vessel histology and vessel types","summary":"tunica intima, media, externa; elastic and muscular arteries, arterioles, capillary types, venules, veins and valves","mw":"2026-10-05","tr":"2026-10-01","cards":[{"id":"L01657","dok":1,"q":"What is the lumen of a blood vessel?","a":"The hollow interior of a vessel, the channel the blood flows through.","options":["The outermost connective tissue layer of the wall","The smooth muscle layer of the wall","The hollow channel the blood flows through","The endothelial lining of the wall"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The lumen is the hollow interior of a vessel, the open channel the blood flows through. The vessel wall surrounds it in layers, starting with the endothelium that faces the lumen. On a slide, an artery's lumen is small and round, while a vein's lumen is wider and often looks collapsed.","notes":["The outermost connective tissue layer is the tunica externa, made mostly of collagen. Pick it if the question asked which layer anchors the vessel to the surrounding tissue.","The smooth muscle layer is the tunica media, which changes the vessel's diameter. It would be the answer if the question asked which layer is thickest in an artery.","Correct. The lumen is the open space inside the vessel.","The endothelial lining is the simple squamous epithelium of the tunica intima, which faces the lumen but is part of the wall. Choose it if the question asked what tissue lines the inside of a vessel."],"lt":"t-blood-vessels","lid":"c1","comp":66,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-tunics"},{"id":"L01658","dok":1,"q":"Which type of blood vessel carries blood away from the heart?","a":"An artery.","options":["An artery","A capillary","A venule","A vein"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Arteries carry blood away from the heart, and the definition is about direction, not oxygen content. Most arteries carry oxygen-rich blood, but the pulmonary arteries carry oxygen-poor blood to the lungs and are still arteries. A handy link is that artery and away both start with the letter a.","notes":["Correct. Arteries carry blood away from the heart.","A capillary is the tiny exchange vessel between an arteriole and a venule. It would be the answer if the question asked where gases and nutrients cross between blood and tissue.","A venule is the smallest vein, collecting blood from a capillary bed on its way back toward the heart. Choose it if the question asked which vessel drains a capillary bed.","A vein carries blood toward the heart. Pick it if the question asked which vessel returns blood to the heart."],"lt":"t-blood-vessels","lid":"c2","comp":67,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-types"},{"id":"L01659","dok":1,"q":"Which type of blood vessel carries blood toward the heart?","a":"A vein.","options":["A vein","An arteriole","An artery","A capillary"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Veins carry blood toward the heart, under low pressure. As with arteries, the definition is about direction, not oxygen: most veins carry oxygen-poor blood, but the pulmonary veins carry oxygen-rich blood from the lungs to the heart and are still veins. Because the pressure is low, many veins have valves to keep blood moving the right way.","notes":["Correct. Veins return blood to the heart.","An arteriole is the smallest artery, still carrying blood away from the heart toward a capillary bed. It would be the answer if the question asked which vessel controls flow into a capillary bed.","An artery carries blood away from the heart, under high pressure. Pick it if the question asked which vessel leaves the heart.","A capillary connects arterioles to venules and is where exchange happens, so it carries blood neither directly away from nor toward the heart. Choose it if the question asked about the exchange vessel."],"lt":"t-blood-vessels","lid":"c3","comp":67,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-types"},{"id":"L01660","dok":1,"q":"Which is the smallest type of blood vessel, where exchange between the blood and the tissues takes place?","a":"A capillary.","options":["A venule","An arteriole","A capillary","A muscular artery"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Capillaries are the smallest blood vessels and the only ones thin enough for exchange. Their wall is just the tunica intima, a single layer of endothelium on a basement membrane. Gases, nutrients and wastes cross this thin wall between the blood and the surrounding tissue.","notes":["A venule is the smallest vein, collecting blood after it leaves the capillaries. It would be the answer if the question asked which vessel drains a capillary bed.","An arteriole is the smallest artery, feeding a capillary bed, but its wall has smooth muscle and is too thick for exchange. Pick it if the question asked which vessel controls how much blood enters a capillary bed.","Correct. Capillaries are the exchange vessels.","A muscular artery is a medium-sized artery, such as the brachial or femoral artery, that distributes blood to organs. Choose it if the question asked which artery type has a tunica media that is mostly smooth muscle."],"lt":"t-blood-vessels","lid":"c4","comp":67,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-types"},{"id":"L01661","dok":1,"q":"What is the innermost tunic of a vessel wall called?","a":"The tunica intima.","options":["The tunica intima","The tunica media","The tunica externa","The tunica adventitia"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The tunica intima is the innermost layer of the vessel wall, facing the lumen. It is a lining of simple squamous endothelium on a basement membrane, and it is continuous with the endocardium inside the heart. Intima comes from the same root as intimate, meaning innermost.","notes":["Correct. The tunica intima is the innermost tunic.","The tunica media is the middle layer, built of smooth muscle and elastic fibers. It would be the answer if the question asked which layer sets the vessel's diameter.","The tunica externa is the outermost layer, made mostly of collagen, which anchors the vessel. Pick it if the question asked for the outer layer of the wall.","Tunica adventitia is another name for the tunica externa, the outermost layer. Students pick it because it sounds unfamiliar, but it is just an alternate name for the outer tunic."],"lt":"t-blood-vessels","lid":"c5","comp":66,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-tunics"},{"id":"L01662","dok":1,"q":"Which tunic is the middle layer of a vessel wall?","a":"The tunica media.","options":["The tunica externa","The basement membrane","The tunica media","The tunica intima"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The tunica media is the middle layer, between the tunica intima and the tunica externa. It is made of smooth muscle and elastic fibers, and its smooth muscle sets the vessel's diameter. It is the thickest layer in arteries, which is why arteries hold their round shape on a slide.","notes":["The tunica externa is the outermost layer, mostly collagen, anchoring the vessel to nearby tissue. It would be the answer if the question asked which layer is thickest in a vein.","The basement membrane is the thin layer the endothelium rests on, part of the tunica intima rather than a separate tunic. Choose it if the question asked what the endothelial cells sit on.","Correct. The tunica media is the middle layer.","The tunica intima is the innermost layer, an endothelial lining on a basement membrane. Pick it if the question asked which layer faces the lumen."],"lt":"t-blood-vessels","lid":"c6","comp":66,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-tunics"},{"id":"L01663","dok":1,"q":"What tissue makes up the tunica media?","a":"Smooth muscle and elastic fibers.","options":["Skeletal muscle fibers","Simple squamous endothelium","Smooth muscle and elastic fibers","Mostly collagen fibers"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The tunica media is built of smooth muscle and elastic fibers. The smooth muscle contracts or relaxes to narrow or widen the vessel, and the elastic fibers let the wall stretch and recoil with each heartbeat. This is the layer that does the active work of controlling vessel diameter.","notes":["Skeletal muscle is voluntary muscle attached to bones and is not found in vessel walls. Students pick it because they know the media is muscular, but vessel walls use involuntary smooth muscle.","Simple squamous endothelium lines the tunica intima, the innermost layer. Pick it if the question asked what tissue lines the lumen.","Correct. Smooth muscle and elastic fibers make up the middle tunic.","Mostly collagen fibers describe the tunica externa, the outer anchoring layer. It would be the answer if the question asked what the outermost tunic is made of."],"lt":"t-blood-vessels","lid":"c7","comp":66,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-tunics"},{"id":"L01664","dok":1,"q":"What type of epithelium lines the tunica intima?","a":"Simple squamous epithelium, called endothelium.","options":["Simple columnar epithelium","Simple squamous epithelium (endothelium)","Stratified squamous epithelium","Pseudostratified columnar epithelium"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The tunica intima is lined by simple squamous epithelium, a single layer of flat cells, which in blood vessels is called endothelium. A single flat layer gives blood a smooth surface to flow over and, in capillaries, makes exchange easy because the wall is so thin. It rests on a basement membrane.","notes":["Simple columnar epithelium is a single layer of tall cells specialized for absorption and secretion, as in the stomach and intestines. It would be the answer if the question asked what lines the digestive tract.","Correct. Endothelium is simple squamous epithelium lining the vessels.","Stratified squamous epithelium is many layers thick and protects surfaces that face friction, like the skin and the esophagus. Pick it if the question asked what lines a surface exposed to wear.","Pseudostratified columnar epithelium, usually ciliated, lines most of the respiratory airways such as the trachea. Choose it if the question asked what lines the trachea."],"lt":"t-blood-vessels","lid":"c8","comp":66,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-tunics"},{"id":"L01665","dok":1,"q":"Which tunic is made mostly of collagen fibers and anchors the vessel to the surrounding tissue?","a":"The tunica externa.","options":["The tunica media","The tunica externa","The tunica intima","The endothelium"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The tunica externa is the outermost layer, made mostly of collagen fibers with some nerves and small vessels. Collagen is tough and resists stretch, so this layer protects the vessel and anchors it to nearby tissue. It is the thickest layer in veins.","notes":["The tunica media is the middle layer of smooth muscle and elastic fibers. Pick it if the question asked which layer controls vessel diameter.","Correct. The collagen-rich tunica externa anchors the vessel.","The tunica intima is the innermost layer, an endothelial lining on a basement membrane. It would be the answer if the question asked which layer faces the lumen.","The endothelium is the simple squamous lining of the tunica intima. Choose it if the question asked what single layer of cells lines the lumen."],"lt":"t-blood-vessels","lid":"c9","comp":66,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-tunics"},{"id":"L01666","dok":1,"q":"Which vessels are the smallest arteries?","a":"Arterioles.","options":["Arterioles","Venules","Sinusoids","Capillaries"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Arterioles are the smallest arteries, with walls of just one or two layers of smooth muscle. They lead into capillary beds and adjust how much blood enters each bed by narrowing or widening. The ending -ole means small, so arteriole means small artery.","notes":["Correct. Arterioles are the smallest arteries.","Venules are the smallest veins, on the return side after the capillaries. Pick them if the question asked which vessels collect blood from a capillary bed.","Sinusoids are a leaky type of capillary found in organs like the liver and spleen. Choose them if the question asked which capillary type lets whole cells cross.","Capillaries are smaller than arterioles, but they are exchange vessels, not arteries. They would be the answer if the question asked for the smallest blood vessel overall."],"lt":"t-blood-vessels","lid":"c10","comp":67,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-types"},{"id":"L01667","dok":1,"q":"Which arteries are the largest, including the aorta?","a":"Elastic arteries.","options":["Muscular arteries","Arterioles","Elastic arteries","Metarterioles"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Elastic arteries are the largest arteries, including the aorta, pulmonary trunk, brachiocephalic, subclavian, common carotid and common iliac arteries. Their thick tunica media is dominated by elastic tissue, so they stretch with each heartbeat and recoil between beats. That recoil keeps blood moving while the ventricles relax.","notes":["Muscular arteries are medium-sized, such as the brachial, radial and femoral arteries, with a tunica media that is mostly smooth muscle. Pick them if the question asked which arteries distribute blood to specific organs.","Arterioles are the smallest arteries, controlling flow into capillary beds. They would be the answer if the question asked for the smallest arteries.","Correct. Elastic arteries are the largest, and the aorta is the classic example.","A metarteriole is the tapered terminal end of an arteriole where it meets a capillary bed, so it is even smaller. Choose it if the question asked which vessel segment joins an arteriole to its capillaries."],"lt":"t-blood-vessels","lid":"c11","comp":68,"competencyId":"bvn-artery-cap-vein-kinds"},{"id":"L01668","dok":1,"q":"What is a capillary bed?","a":"A network of capillaries fed by one arteriole.","options":["A direct channel from an artery to a vein","The tapered terminal end of an arteriole","A network of capillaries fed by one arteriole","A ring of smooth muscle at a capillary entrance"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"A capillary bed is a network of roughly ten to one hundred capillaries supplied by a single arteriole, entering through its tapered end, the metarteriole. Blood flows through the bed and drains into a venule. Spreading the blood through many fine capillaries gives a large surface for exchange with the tissue.","notes":["A direct channel from an artery to a vein that skips the capillaries is an arteriovenous anastomosis. Pick it if the question asked what lets blood bypass capillaries entirely.","The tapered terminal end of an arteriole is the metarteriole, which feeds the capillary bed rather than being the bed itself. Choose it if the question asked which vessel segment joins an arteriole to its capillaries.","Correct. A capillary bed is the network of capillaries supplied by one arteriole.","A ring of smooth muscle at a capillary entrance is a precapillary sphincter. It would be the answer if the question asked what opens or closes the entrance to a capillary."],"lt":"t-blood-vessels","lid":"c12","comp":67,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-types"},{"id":"L01669","dok":1,"q":"What is a precapillary sphincter?","a":"A ring of smooth muscle that opens or closes the entrance to a capillary.","options":["The endothelial lining of a sinusoid","A flap of the tunica intima inside a vein","A ring of smooth muscle at a capillary entrance","A network of capillaries fed by one arteriole"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"A precapillary sphincter is a ring of smooth muscle where a capillary branches off the metarteriole. When it contracts, it closes the capillary's entrance, and when it relaxes, blood flows in. This lets the body open or close parts of a capillary bed depending on the tissue's needs.","notes":["The endothelial lining of a sinusoid is a leaky, gapped wall, not a muscular ring. It would be the answer if the question asked what lines the wide capillaries of the liver.","A flap of the tunica intima inside a vein is a venous valve, which keeps blood from flowing backward. Pick it if the question asked what prevents backflow in limb veins.","Correct. A precapillary sphincter is a smooth muscle ring guarding a capillary entrance.","A network of capillaries fed by one arteriole is a capillary bed. Choose it if the question asked what a capillary bed is."],"lt":"t-blood-vessels","lid":"c13","comp":67,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-types"},{"id":"L01670","dok":1,"q":"What are venous valves made from?","a":"Flaps of the tunica intima.","options":["Rings of smooth muscle from the tunica media","Flaps of the tunica intima","Folds of skeletal muscle around the vein","Bundles of collagen from the tunica externa"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Venous valves are thin flaps of the tunica intima, the endothelial lining, that project into the lumen of a vein. They open as blood moves toward the heart and fall closed if blood starts to slide backward. They are most common in the limb veins, where blood must travel uphill against gravity.","notes":["Rings of smooth muscle from the tunica media change the diameter of a vessel, and at a capillary entrance they form a precapillary sphincter. Pick this if the question asked what opens or closes a capillary's entrance.","Correct. Venous valves are folds of the innermost tunic.","Skeletal muscle around the deep veins squeezes them during movement, which is the skeletal muscle pump, but it is outside the vein and does not form the valves. Choose it if the question asked what pushes venous blood toward the heart when you walk.","Collagen bundles make up the tunica externa, which anchors the vessel to nearby tissue. It would be the answer if the question asked what the outermost layer of a vein is made of."],"lt":"t-blood-vessels","lid":"c14","comp":68,"competencyId":"bvn-artery-cap-vein-kinds"},{"id":"L01671","dok":2,"q":"Which tunic is the thickest layer in an artery?","a":"The tunica media.","options":["The tunica intima","The tunica media","The basement membrane","The tunica externa"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"In an artery the tunica media, the middle layer of smooth muscle and elastic fibers, is the thickest layer. Arteries carry blood under high pressure, and a thick muscular, elastic media lets the wall withstand that pressure and control the vessel's diameter. This thick media is why an artery holds its round shape on a slide.","notes":["The tunica intima is a thin endothelial lining on a basement membrane and is never the thickest layer. Pick it if the question asked which tunic is the only one in a capillary wall.","Correct. The tunica media is the thickest tunic in an artery.","The basement membrane is a very thin layer under the endothelium, part of the tunica intima. Choose it if the question asked what the endothelial cells rest on.","The tunica externa is the thickest layer in a vein, not an artery. It would be correct if the question asked about the thickest tunic of a vein."],"lt":"t-blood-vessels","lid":"c15","comp":67,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-types"},{"id":"L01672","dok":2,"q":"Which tunic makes up the entire wall of a capillary?","a":"The tunica intima.","options":["The tunica intima","The tunica media","The tunica externa","All three tunics, each very thin"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"A capillary wall is only the tunica intima: a single layer of endothelium on a basement membrane. Capillaries lack a tunica media and a tunica externa entirely. This one-cell-thick wall is what makes exchange possible between the blood and the tissues.","notes":["Correct. The capillary wall is just the tunica intima.","The tunica media is the smooth muscle and elastic layer of arteries and veins. Capillaries have none, so pick the media if the question asked which layer is thickest in an artery.","The tunica externa is the collagen-rich outer layer of arteries and veins, and capillaries lack it. It would be the answer if the question asked which tunic is thickest in a vein.","Students often assume every vessel has all three tunics, just scaled down. Capillaries are the exception: they keep only the inner lining so the wall stays thin enough for exchange."],"lt":"t-blood-vessels","lid":"c16","comp":66,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-tunics"},{"id":"L01673","dok":2,"q":"Compared with its matching artery, how do a vein's wall and lumen differ?","a":"A vein has a thinner wall and a wider lumen.","options":["A thicker wall and a wider lumen","A thinner wall and a wider lumen","A thicker wall and a narrower lumen","A thinner wall and a narrower lumen"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Veins carry blood back to the heart under low pressure, so they do not need the thick muscular tunica media of an artery. They have the same three tunics but a thinner media, a thicker externa, and a wider lumen. On a slide the artery holds its round shape while the vein often looks collapsed.","notes":["A vein does have a wider lumen, but its wall is thinner, not thicker. Students mix this up because the tunica externa is thicker in veins, yet the overall wall is still thinner.","Correct. A vein has a thinner wall and a wider lumen than its artery.","This describes the artery, not the vein. Arteries have thick walls and narrower lumens because they carry blood under high pressure.","A vein's wall is thinner, but its lumen is wider, not narrower. The wide lumen is why veins hold so much of the body's blood at rest."],"lt":"t-blood-vessels","lid":"c17","comp":67,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-types"},{"id":"L01674","dok":2,"q":"Which vessels have valves in their walls?","a":"Veins.","options":["Arterioles","Arteries","Veins","Capillaries"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Veins, especially those in the limbs, have valves formed from flaps of the tunica intima. Blood in veins moves at low pressure and often has to travel uphill against gravity, so the valves keep it from sliding backward. Arteries do not need valves because the high pressure from the heart keeps their blood moving forward.","notes":["Arterioles are the smallest arteries and control flow into capillary beds with smooth muscle, not valves. They would be the answer if the question asked which vessels act as gatekeepers to capillary beds.","Arteries carry blood under high pressure from the heart, which keeps it moving forward without valves. Pick them if the question asked which vessels have the thickest tunica media.","Correct. Veins have valves to prevent backflow.","Capillaries have walls only one cell thick and no valves. Choose them if the question asked where exchange between blood and tissue happens."],"lt":"t-blood-vessels","lid":"c18","comp":68,"competencyId":"bvn-artery-cap-vein-kinds"},{"id":"L01675","dok":2,"q":"Which vessel type has a wall made of a single tunic, the tunica intima only?","a":"Capillaries.","options":["Venules","Muscular arteries","Capillaries","Arterioles"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Capillaries are the only vessels whose wall is just the tunica intima, an endothelium on a basement membrane. They lack both the tunica media and the tunica externa. A wall this thin is what allows gases, nutrients and wastes to cross between the blood and the tissues.","notes":["Venules have thin, porous walls, and beyond the first postcapillary venules they gain smooth muscle. They would be correct if the question asked which vessels collect blood from capillaries.","Muscular arteries have a thick tunica media of smooth muscle, up to forty layers. Choose them if the question asked which arteries distribute blood to specific organs.","Correct. Capillaries have only the tunica intima.","Arterioles have one or two layers of smooth muscle in their tunica media, so their wall is more than the intima. Pick them if the question asked which vessels control blood flow into a capillary bed."],"lt":"t-blood-vessels","lid":"c19","comp":66,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-tunics"},{"id":"L01676","dok":1,"q":"Which vessels are the smallest veins?","a":"Venules.","options":["Venules","Capillaries","Sinusoids","Arterioles"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Venules are the smallest veins. They collect blood as it drains from a capillary bed and merge to form larger veins. The ending -ule means small, just as arteriole means small artery.","notes":["Correct. Venules are the smallest veins.","Capillaries are the exchange vessels between arterioles and venules, and they are neither arteries nor veins. Choose them if the question asked for the smallest blood vessel overall.","Sinusoids are a wide, leaky type of capillary, not veins. They would be the answer if the question asked which capillary type lets whole cells cross.","Arterioles are the smallest arteries, on the delivery side before the capillaries. Pick them if the question asked for the smallest arteries."],"lt":"t-blood-vessels","lid":"c20","comp":67,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-types"},{"id":"L01677","dok":2,"q":"Which vessel type carries blood under high pressure and has a thick wall with a heavy tunica media?","a":"Arteries.","options":["Venules","Arteries","Veins","Capillaries"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Arteries receive blood pushed out by the ventricles, so they carry it under the highest pressure in the system. Their thick tunica media of smooth muscle and elastic fibers lets them withstand that pressure and control their diameter. The thick wall is why arteries keep a round shape when cut.","notes":["Venules are the smallest veins, with thin, porous walls and low pressure. Choose them if the question asked which vessels collect blood from capillary beds.","Correct. Arteries are the high-pressure vessels with a thick tunica media.","Veins carry blood under low pressure and have thinner walls with a lighter tunica media. Pick them if the question asked which vessels have valves and a wide lumen.","Capillaries have no tunica media at all, just a single layer of endothelium. They would be the answer if the question asked which vessels are built for exchange."],"lt":"t-blood-vessels","lid":"c21","comp":67,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-types"},{"id":"L01678","dok":2,"q":"Which capillary type has an unbroken endothelial lining and is the most common type?","a":"The continuous capillary.","options":["The fenestrated capillary","The continuous capillary","The sinusoid","The postcapillary venule"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Continuous capillaries have an unbroken endothelial lining, interrupted only by narrow clefts between the cells. They are the most common type, found in muscle, skin, the lungs and the central nervous system. Their tight wall keeps most substances from leaking out, which is especially important in the brain.","notes":["Fenestrated capillaries have small pores called fenestrations in the endothelium for rapid filtration or absorption. Pick them if the question asked about the capillaries of the kidney or small intestine.","Correct. Continuous capillaries have an unbroken lining and are the most common.","Sinusoids are wide, leaky capillaries with large gaps and an incomplete basement membrane. They would be the answer if the question asked which capillaries let whole cells pass, as in the liver.","The postcapillary venule is the first venule after a capillary bed, loosely joined and very porous. Students pick it because its name contains capillary, but it is a small vein, not a capillary type."],"lt":"t-blood-vessels","lid":"c22","comp":68,"competencyId":"bvn-artery-cap-vein-kinds"},{"id":"L01679","dok":2,"q":"Which capillary type has an endothelium with small pores called fenestrations?","a":"The fenestrated capillary.","options":["The metarteriole","The sinusoid","The continuous capillary","The fenestrated capillary"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Fenestrated capillaries have small pores, called fenestrations (from the Latin word for windows), through their endothelial cells. These pores let fluid and small molecules move quickly, so these capillaries are found where rapid filtration or absorption happens. Examples include the kidney, the small intestine, the choroid plexus and most endocrine glands.","notes":["A metarteriole is the tapered end of an arteriole that feeds a capillary bed, not a type of capillary. Choose it if the question asked which vessel segment joins an arteriole to its capillaries.","Sinusoids also have openings, but theirs are large, with wide gaps between cells and an incomplete basement membrane. They would be the answer if the question asked which capillaries let whole blood cells cross.","Continuous capillaries have an unbroken lining with no pores, only narrow clefts between cells. Pick them if the question asked which capillaries are most common or which serve the brain.","Correct. Fenestrated capillaries have small pores in the endothelium."],"lt":"t-blood-vessels","lid":"c23","comp":68,"competencyId":"bvn-artery-cap-vein-kinds"},{"id":"L01680","dok":2,"q":"Which capillary type is a wide, leaky vessel with large gaps between its cells?","a":"The sinusoid.","options":["The metarteriole","The sinusoid","The fenestrated capillary","The continuous capillary"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Sinusoids are wide, winding capillaries with large fenestrations, wide clefts between cells, and an incomplete basement membrane. They are the leakiest capillary type, built for tissues where whole cells or large proteins must cross. They are found in the liver, spleen, red bone marrow, anterior pituitary and adrenal glands.","notes":["A metarteriole is the tapered end of an arteriole leading into a capillary bed, not a capillary type. Choose it if the question asked which vessel segment feeds a capillary bed.","Correct. Sinusoids are the wide, leaky capillaries.","Fenestrated capillaries have small pores but an intact basement membrane, so they are leaky to fluid and small molecules but not to cells. Pick them if the question asked about the kidney or small intestine.","Continuous capillaries are the tightest type, with an unbroken lining. They would be the answer if the question asked which capillaries keep most substances out of the brain."],"lt":"t-blood-vessels","lid":"c24","comp":68,"competencyId":"bvn-artery-cap-vein-kinds"},{"id":"L01681","dok":2,"q":"Which arteries have a tunica media that is mostly smooth muscle and distribute blood to specific organs?","a":"Muscular arteries.","options":["Elastic arteries","Muscular arteries","Arterioles","Metarterioles"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Muscular arteries are medium-sized arteries, such as the femoral, axillary, brachial and radial arteries. Their tunica media is mostly smooth muscle, up to forty layers thick, which lets them distribute blood to specific organs and hold vascular tone. They sit between the large elastic arteries and the tiny arterioles in size.","notes":["Elastic arteries, such as the aorta, have a tunica media dominated by elastic tissue rather than smooth muscle. Pick them if the question asked which arteries stretch and recoil with each heartbeat.","Correct. Muscular arteries have a smooth-muscle media and deliver blood to organs.","Arterioles also have smooth muscle, but only one or two layers, and they control flow into capillary beds rather than delivering blood to whole organs. They would be the answer if the question asked which vessels are the gatekeepers of capillary beds.","Metarterioles are the tapered terminal ends of arterioles, where they join a capillary bed. Choose them if the question asked which segment leads directly into capillaries."],"lt":"t-blood-vessels","lid":"c25","comp":68,"competencyId":"bvn-artery-cap-vein-kinds"},{"id":"L01682","dok":2,"q":"What is the correct order of the five vessel types as blood flows from the heart and back?","a":"Artery, arteriole, capillary, venule, vein.","options":["Artery, arteriole, capillary, venule, vein","Artery, capillary, arteriole, vein, venule","Arteriole, artery, capillary, vein, venule","Vein, venule, capillary, arteriole, artery"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Arteries leave the heart and branch into smaller arterioles, which feed the capillaries where exchange happens. The capillaries then merge into venules, which join to form veins that return blood to the heart. The sequence gets smaller toward the capillaries and larger again on the way back.","notes":["Correct. Blood flows artery, arteriole, capillary, venule, vein.","This order puts the capillary before the arteriole and the vein before the venule. Arterioles feed capillaries, and venules drain into veins, so both pairs are reversed.","This order starts with an arteriole and ends with a venule. Blood leaves the heart in a large artery first and returns through a large vein last.","This is the correct sequence run backward, from the vein side to the artery side. It would describe the order if blood flowed toward the tissues through veins, which it does not."],"lt":"t-blood-vessels","lid":"c26","comp":67,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-types"},{"id":"L01683","dok":3,"q":"A guard stands motionless for hours, and blood pools in the veins of his legs. What normally helps push this blood back toward the heart that is missing here?","a":"Contractions of the leg muscles squeezing the deep veins.","options":["Closing of the precapillary sphincters in the legs","Contraction of the smooth muscle in the leg arteries","Contractions of the leg muscles squeezing the deep veins","Recoil of the aorta between heartbeats"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Veins carry blood at low pressure, so they rely on help to move it uphill from the legs. When you walk, contracting leg muscles squeeze the deep veins, and the venous valves let the blood move only toward the heart; this is the skeletal muscle pump. Standing still turns that pump off, so blood pools in the leg veins.","notes":["Precapillary sphincters open or close capillary entrances to control flow into a capillary bed. They would be the answer if the question asked what regulates blood entering individual capillaries.","Smooth muscle in the leg arteries narrows or widens them to adjust delivery, but it works on the arterial side and does not push venous blood upward. Pick it if the question asked what causes vasoconstriction of an artery.","Correct. The skeletal muscle pump, idle while standing still, normally pushes leg vein blood upward.","The aorta's elastic recoil keeps blood moving forward through the arteries between beats, but its push is mostly used up by the time blood reaches the veins. Choose it if the question asked what keeps arterial blood moving while the ventricles relax."],"lt":"t-blood-vessels","lid":"c27","comp":68,"competencyId":"bvn-artery-cap-vein-kinds"},{"id":"L01684","dok":2,"q":"Which group of vessels, taken together, holds the largest share of the body's blood at rest?","a":"The veins.","options":["The arteries","The arterioles","The capillaries","The veins"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"At rest, the veins hold roughly sixty percent of the body's blood, so they are called blood reservoirs. Their thin walls and wide lumens let them stretch and hold a large volume at low pressure. When the body needs more circulating blood, some of this stored volume can be moved back toward the heart.","notes":["Arteries have thick walls and narrower lumens and hold a much smaller share of the blood at any moment. Pick them if the question asked which vessels carry blood under the highest pressure.","Arterioles are small vessels that regulate flow into capillary beds and hold little blood. Choose them if the question asked which vessels are the gatekeepers of capillary beds.","Capillaries are enormous in number, but each is tiny, so together they hold only a small share of the blood. They would be the answer if the question asked where exchange takes place.","Correct. The veins act as the body's blood reservoir."],"lt":"t-blood-vessels","lid":"c28","comp":67,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-types"},{"id":"L01687","dok":2,"q":"On a slide, a vessel wall is a single layer of endothelium on a basement membrane, with no tunica media and no tunica externa. Which vessel type is it?","a":"A capillary.","options":["A vein","A muscular artery","A capillary","An arteriole"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"A wall made of only endothelium on a basement membrane is the tunica intima alone, and the capillary is the only vessel built this way. Lacking the other two tunics keeps the wall as thin as possible. That thinness is what lets gases, nutrients and wastes pass between the blood and the tissue.","notes":["A vein has all three tunics, with a thin media, a thick externa and a wide lumen. Choose it if the slide showed a collapsed-looking vessel with a thick outer layer.","A muscular artery has a thick tunica media of many smooth muscle layers. It would be the answer if the slide showed a round vessel with a heavy muscular wall.","Correct. Only a capillary has an endothelium with no media or externa.","An arteriole has one or two layers of smooth muscle in a tunica media around its endothelium. Pick it if the slide showed a tiny vessel with a thin ring of muscle."],"lt":"t-blood-vessels","lid":"c31","comp":66,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-tunics"},{"id":"L01688","dok":2,"q":"A vessel has a thinner wall and a wider lumen than its matching artery, with valves along its course. Which vessel type is it?","a":"A vein.","options":["An arteriole","An elastic artery","A vein","A capillary"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Three clues point to a vein: a thinner wall, a wider lumen, and valves. Veins carry blood back to the heart under low pressure, so they need less muscle, and their valves keep the slow-moving blood from flowing backward. Arteries lack valves and have thicker, more muscular walls.","notes":["An arteriole is a small artery with smooth muscle and no valves. Choose it if the description mentioned the vessel that controls flow into a capillary bed.","An elastic artery, such as the aorta, has a thick wall rich in elastic tissue and no valves. Pick it if the description mentioned a large artery that stretches and recoils with each heartbeat.","Correct. A thin wall, wide lumen and valves identify a vein.","A capillary has a wall only one cell thick and no valves. It would be the answer if the description mentioned an endothelium with no tunica media or externa."],"lt":"t-blood-vessels","lid":"c32","comp":67,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-types"},{"id":"L01689","dok":2,"q":"A capillary in the liver is wide and leaky, with large gaps between its endothelial cells so whole blood cells can cross. Which capillary type is it?","a":"A sinusoid.","options":["A fenestrated capillary","A continuous capillary","A metarteriole","A sinusoid"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Sinusoids are the leakiest capillaries, with large fenestrations, wide gaps between cells, and an incomplete basement membrane. In the liver, this lets large proteins and even whole cells move between the blood and the liver tissue. Capillary type always follows the job of the tissue.","notes":["Fenestrated capillaries have small pores but an intact basement membrane, so they pass fluid and small molecules but not cells. They would be the answer if the question described the kidney or small intestine.","Continuous capillaries have an unbroken lining and are the tightest type. Pick them if the question described the capillaries of the brain, skin or muscle.","A metarteriole is the tapered end of an arteriole that feeds a capillary bed, not a capillary itself. Choose it if the question asked which vessel segment leads into the capillaries.","Correct. The wide, leaky capillaries of the liver are sinusoids."],"lt":"t-blood-vessels","lid":"c33","comp":68,"competencyId":"bvn-artery-cap-vein-kinds"},{"id":"L01690","dok":2,"q":"A capillary in the kidney has an endothelium pierced by small pores for rapid filtration. Which capillary type is it?","a":"A fenestrated capillary.","options":["A metarteriole","A continuous capillary","A fenestrated capillary","A sinusoid"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Fenestrated capillaries have small pores, called fenestrations, through their endothelium. These pores let fluid and small molecules pass quickly, which suits places where filtration or absorption must happen fast. The kidney, small intestine, choroid plexus and most endocrine glands all use this type.","notes":["A metarteriole is the tapered end of an arteriole leading into a capillary bed. Choose it if the question asked which vessel segment feeds a capillary bed.","Continuous capillaries have no pores, only narrow clefts between cells. Pick them if the question described the tight capillaries of the brain.","Correct. Small endothelial pores for filtration identify a fenestrated capillary.","Sinusoids have large gaps and an incomplete basement membrane, much leakier than small pores. They would be the answer if the question described capillaries that let whole cells cross, as in the liver or spleen."],"lt":"t-blood-vessels","lid":"c34","comp":68,"competencyId":"bvn-artery-cap-vein-kinds"},{"id":"L01691","dok":2,"q":"Which of these is an example of an elastic artery?","a":"The common carotid artery.","options":["The femoral artery","The brachial artery","The common carotid artery","The radial artery"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Elastic arteries are the largest arteries, close to the heart, with a tunica media dominated by elastic tissue. The aorta, pulmonary trunk, brachiocephalic, subclavian, common carotid and common iliac arteries are all elastic arteries. They stretch with each heartbeat and recoil between beats to keep blood moving.","notes":["The femoral artery is a muscular artery, a medium-sized vessel with a smooth-muscle media that delivers blood to the thigh. Pick it if the question asked for an example of a muscular artery.","The brachial artery is a muscular artery in the arm, used when measuring blood pressure. Choose it if the question asked for an example of a muscular artery.","Correct. The common carotid is a large elastic artery near the heart.","The radial artery is a muscular artery in the forearm, the one used to take a pulse at the wrist. It would be correct if the question asked for a muscular artery example."],"lt":"t-blood-vessels","lid":"c35","comp":68,"competencyId":"bvn-artery-cap-vein-kinds"},{"id":"L01692","dok":2,"q":"Which vessel is the smallest type of artery and acts as a gatekeeper that controls how much blood enters a capillary bed?","a":"An arteriole.","options":["An arteriole","A capillary","A venule","A muscular artery"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Arterioles are the smallest arteries, with one or two layers of smooth muscle in their walls. By narrowing or widening, they control how much blood flows into the capillary bed they supply. They are also the main site of resistance to blood flow in the circulation.","notes":["Correct. Arterioles are the smallest arteries and the gatekeepers of capillary beds.","A capillary is the exchange vessel inside the bed, downstream of the gate, and has no smooth muscle to control flow. Choose it if the question asked where exchange takes place.","A venule is the smallest vein, collecting blood after it leaves the capillaries. Pick it if the question asked which vessel drains a capillary bed.","A muscular artery is a medium-sized artery that distributes blood to whole organs. It would be the answer if the question asked about arteries like the brachial or femoral."],"lt":"t-blood-vessels","lid":"c36","comp":67,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-types"},{"id":"L01695","dok":2,"q":"Which structure is a direct channel that lets blood bypass the capillaries of a bed when exchange is not needed?","a":"A vascular shunt.","options":["A portal system","A precapillary sphincter","A vascular shunt","A capillary bed"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"A vascular shunt is a direct channel through a capillary bed that lets blood travel from the arteriole side to the venule side without entering the capillaries. When the precapillary sphincters close, blood takes the shunt instead. This lets the body send blood past tissues that do not need exchange at the moment.","notes":["A portal system sends blood through two capillary beds in series, adding exchange rather than skipping it. Choose it if the question described the route from the digestive organs through the liver.","A precapillary sphincter is a ring of smooth muscle that opens or closes the entrance to a capillary. It decides whether blood enters a capillary, so pick it if the question asked what controls a capillary's entrance.","Correct. A vascular shunt carries blood past the capillaries.","A capillary bed is the network of capillaries where exchange happens, which the shunt goes around. Pick it if the question asked what a single arteriole supplies."],"lt":"t-blood-vessels","lid":"c39","comp":67,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-types"},{"id":"L01696","dok":2,"q":"At which vessel type do white blood cells usually leave the bloodstream to enter the tissues?","a":"Venules.","options":["Vein","Venule","Arteriole","Elastic artery"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Venules, especially the postcapillary venules that receive blood straight from the capillaries, have thin walls whose cells are loosely joined and very porous. White blood cells slip between these cells to leave the blood and enter the surrounding tissue. This is why venules are described as the main exit point for white blood cells.","notes":["Veins have thicker walls with all three tunics and carry blood back to the heart. They would be the answer if the question asked which vessels have valves and act as blood reservoirs.","Correct. White blood cells usually leave the blood through the porous walls of venules.","Arterioles have a ring of smooth muscle and control flow into capillary beds, not the exit of white blood cells. Pick them if the question asked which vessels regulate blood flow into a bed.","Elastic arteries, like the aorta, have thick elastic walls and carry blood under high pressure. Choose them if the question asked which arteries stretch and recoil with each heartbeat."],"lt":"t-blood-vessels","lid":"c40","comp":67,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-types"},{"id":"L01697","dok":2,"q":"Which tunic is the middle layer whose smooth muscle sets the diameter of the vessel?","a":"The tunica media.","options":["The tunica media","The tunica externa","The tunica intima","The basement membrane"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The tunica media is the middle layer, made of smooth muscle and elastic fibers. When its smooth muscle contracts, the vessel narrows (vasoconstriction), and when it relaxes, the vessel widens (vasodilation). This is how vessels adjust how much blood flows through them.","notes":["Correct. The smooth muscle of the tunica media sets vessel diameter.","The tunica externa is the outer collagen layer that anchors the vessel, with no smooth muscle to squeeze it. It would be the answer if the question asked which layer is thickest in veins.","The tunica intima is the innermost endothelial lining and has no muscle to change the diameter. Pick it if the question asked which layer faces the lumen.","The basement membrane is the thin layer the endothelium rests on, part of the tunica intima. Choose it if the question asked what supports the endothelial cells."],"lt":"t-blood-vessels","lid":"c41","comp":66,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-tunics"},{"id":"L01699","dok":2,"q":"What is the order of the three tunics of a vessel wall, from the lumen outward?","a":"Tunica intima, tunica media, tunica externa.","options":["Tunica intima, tunica media, tunica externa","Tunica intima, tunica externa, tunica media","Tunica media, tunica intima, tunica externa","Tunica externa, tunica media, tunica intima"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Starting at the lumen, the tunica intima comes first as the endothelial lining. The tunica media of smooth muscle and elastic fibers surrounds it, and the collagen-rich tunica externa forms the outer covering. The names match the order: intima for innermost, media for middle, externa for external.","notes":["Correct. Intima, media, then externa, from the lumen outward.","This order swaps the media and the externa. The muscular media lies in the middle, with the collagen externa wrapped around it.","This order puts the media first, but the endothelial lining of the intima always faces the lumen. Students sometimes pick it because the media is the thickest and most noticeable layer in an artery.","This is the correct order read from the outside in. It would be right if the question asked for the tunics from the outer surface toward the lumen."],"lt":"t-blood-vessels","lid":"c43","comp":66,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-tunics"},{"id":"L01701","dok":2,"q":"What is the order of the three capillary types from the tightest, least leaky wall to the leakiest wall?","a":"Continuous capillary, fenestrated capillary, sinusoid.","options":["Continuous capillary, sinusoid, fenestrated capillary","Fenestrated capillary, continuous capillary, sinusoid","Continuous capillary, fenestrated capillary, sinusoid","Sinusoid, fenestrated capillary, continuous capillary"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Continuous capillaries have an unbroken lining and are the tightest. Fenestrated capillaries add small pores that pass fluid and small molecules quickly. Sinusoids are the leakiest, with large gaps and an incomplete basement membrane so even whole cells can cross. The capillary type is matched to how much the tissue needs to let through.","notes":["This order places sinusoids in the middle, but their large gaps make them leakier than fenestrated capillaries with small pores.","This order puts fenestrated capillaries ahead of continuous ones. Continuous capillaries have no pores at all, so they are tighter than fenestrated ones.","Correct. Continuous, then fenestrated, then sinusoid.","This is the correct order reversed, from leakiest to tightest. It would be right if the question asked for the leakiest type first."],"lt":"t-blood-vessels","lid":"c45","comp":68,"competencyId":"bvn-artery-cap-vein-kinds"},{"id":"L01702","dok":2,"q":"What is the order of the three sizes of artery, from largest to smallest?","a":"Elastic artery, muscular artery, arteriole.","options":["Muscular artery, elastic artery, arteriole","Arteriole, muscular artery, elastic artery","Elastic artery, muscular artery, arteriole","Elastic artery, arteriole, muscular artery"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Elastic arteries such as the aorta are the largest and sit closest to the heart. They branch into medium-sized muscular arteries, such as the brachial and femoral, which distribute blood to organs. Muscular arteries then branch into the microscopic arterioles that feed capillary beds.","notes":["This order puts muscular arteries ahead of elastic ones. The elastic arteries, like the aorta, are the largest because they receive blood straight from the heart.","This is the correct order reversed, from smallest to largest. It would be right if the question asked for the sizes from smallest to largest.","Correct. Elastic arteries, then muscular arteries, then arterioles.","This order places arterioles in the middle, but arterioles are the smallest arteries, the last branches before the capillaries."],"lt":"t-blood-vessels","lid":"c46","comp":68,"competencyId":"bvn-artery-cap-vein-kinds"},{"id":"L01704","dok":2,"q":"Which choice correctly matches each tunic to its main tissue?","a":"Intima is endothelium on a basement membrane, media is smooth muscle and elastic fibers, externa is mostly collagen.","options":["Intima is elastic fibers, media is collagen, externa is endothelium","Intima is mostly collagen, media is endothelium, externa is smooth muscle","Intima is endothelium, media is smooth muscle, externa is collagen","Intima is smooth muscle, media is collagen, externa is endothelium"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The tunica intima is a lining of simple squamous endothelium on a basement membrane, facing the lumen. The tunica media is smooth muscle and elastic fibers, which set the vessel's diameter. The tunica externa is mostly collagen, which anchors the vessel to the tissue around it.","notes":["This match places the endothelium on the outside of the vessel, but endothelium always lines the lumen. Elastic fibers are part of the media, not the intima's main tissue.","This match puts collagen on the inside and endothelium in the middle. The endothelium must face the lumen to give blood a smooth surface, so it belongs to the intima.","Correct. Endothelium, then smooth muscle and elastic fibers, then collagen.","This match moves every tissue one layer inward. Smooth muscle belongs in the middle layer, and the endothelium lines the inside, not the outside."],"lt":"t-blood-vessels","lid":"c48","comp":66,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-tunics"},{"id":"L01706","dok":2,"q":"On a slide, an artery and its matching vein sit side by side. Which description fits the pair?","a":"The artery is round with a thick tunica media; the vein looks collapsed with a thick tunica externa.","options":["The vein is round with a thick media; the artery looks collapsed with a thick externa","The artery is round with a thick media; the vein looks collapsed with a thick externa","The artery looks collapsed because its lumen is wider than the vein's","Both are round, but the vein has the thicker tunica media"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Arteries and veins have the same three tunics in different proportions. The artery's thick, muscular tunica media holds it open and round, while the vein has a thin media, a thick collagen externa and a wide lumen, so it often looks flattened or collapsed. This is usually the fastest way to tell the pair apart on a slide.","notes":["This reverses the two vessels. The artery's thick media keeps it round, and the vein's thin wall lets it collapse.","Correct. A round, thick-media artery beside a collapsed, thick-externa vein is the classic pair.","The vein is the one with the wider lumen, and it is the vein that collapses. Students pick this when they link a wide lumen to the artery instead of the vein.","The artery, not the vein, has the thicker tunica media, because it carries blood under higher pressure. The vein's thickest layer is the tunica externa."],"lt":"t-blood-vessels","lid":"c50","comp":67,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-types"},{"id":"L01707","dok":2,"q":"What is the order of these vessels by wall thickness, from thickest to thinnest: capillary, vein, artery?","a":"Artery, vein, capillary.","options":["Vein, artery, capillary","Capillary, vein, artery","Artery, vein, capillary","Artery, capillary, vein"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Arteries have the thickest walls, with a heavy tunica media to handle high pressure. Veins have all three tunics but a thinner media, so their walls are thinner than those of matching arteries. Capillaries have the thinnest walls of all, just one layer of endothelium, so exchange can happen across them.","notes":["This order puts the vein ahead of the artery. Veins carry blood at low pressure and have thinner walls than their matching arteries.","This is the correct order reversed, from thinnest to thickest. It would be right if the question asked for the thinnest wall first.","Correct. Artery, then vein, then capillary.","This order places the capillary in the middle, but with a wall only one cell thick it is thinner than any vein."],"lt":"t-blood-vessels","lid":"c51","comp":67,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-types"},{"id":"L01712","dok":1,"q":"How many tunics make up the wall of an artery or a vein?","a":"Three.","options":["Two","Five","Three","Four"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Arteries and veins both have three tunics: the tunica intima, the tunica media and the tunica externa. The two vessel types differ in the thickness of these layers, not in their number. Capillaries are the exception, with only the tunica intima.","notes":["Two might come from counting only the muscle and connective tissue layers and forgetting the endothelial lining. Arteries and veins have all three tunics.","Five matches the number of vessel types (artery, arteriole, capillary, venule, vein), a common mix-up. The question asks about layers in one wall, and there are three.","Correct. Intima, media and externa make three tunics.","Four may come from counting the basement membrane or an elastic lamina as a separate tunic. Those are parts of or borders between the three tunics, not extra tunics."],"lt":"t-blood-vessels","lid":"c4-x2","comp":66,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-tunics"},{"id":"L01714","dok":2,"q":"Which tunic is the thickest layer in a vein?","a":"The tunica externa.","options":["The basement membrane","The tunica externa","The tunica intima","The tunica media"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"In a vein, the tunica externa, the outer collagen layer, is the thickest tunic. Veins carry blood under low pressure, so they need only a thin tunica media, and their collagen externa provides most of the support. This is the reverse of an artery, where the muscular tunica media is thickest.","notes":["The basement membrane is a thin sheet under the endothelium, part of the tunica intima. Choose it if the question asked what the endothelial cells rest on.","Correct. The tunica externa is the thickest layer in a vein.","The tunica intima is a thin endothelial lining in every vessel and is never the thickest layer of a vein. Pick it if the question asked which layer forms the venous valves.","The tunica media is the thickest layer in an artery, not a vein. Students pick it because they carry over the artery rule, but veins have a thin media."],"lt":"t-blood-vessels","lid":"c6-x2","comp":67,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-types"},{"id":"L01715","dok":1,"q":"Which small vessels within the tunica externa supply the wall of a large vessel such as the aorta?","a":"The vasa vasorum.","options":["The vascular sinuses","The vasa vasorum","The coronary arteries","The metarterioles"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The vasa vasorum, meaning vessels of the vessels, are small blood vessels in the tunica externa that supply the wall of a large vessel. The wall of a large artery like the aorta is too thick to be nourished only by the blood flowing through its lumen. These small vessels are easy to see in the outer layer of the aorta on a slide.","notes":["Vascular sinuses are thin-walled veins with no smooth muscle, such as the dural venous sinuses and the coronary sinus. Choose them if the question asked about veins supported by surrounding dense connective tissue.","Correct. The vasa vasorum supply the walls of large vessels.","The coronary arteries supply the heart wall, not the walls of other vessels. Pick them if the question asked which vessels feed the myocardium.","Metarterioles are the tapered ends of arterioles that feed capillary beds in tissues. They would be the answer if the question asked which segment joins an arteriole to its capillaries."],"lt":"t-blood-vessels","lid":"c7-x2","comp":66,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-tunics"},{"id":"L01716","dok":1,"q":"Blood flows through the hollow central channel of a vessel. What is this channel called?","a":"The lumen.","options":["The anastomosis","The tunic","The lumen","The fenestration"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The lumen is the hollow interior of a vessel, the channel blood flows through. It is surrounded by the vessel wall, starting with the endothelium of the tunica intima. Comparing lumens on a slide helps tell vessels apart, since veins have a wider lumen than their matching arteries.","notes":["An anastomosis is a place where two vessels join to give blood an alternate route. Choose it if the question asked what protects a tissue when one vessel is blocked.","A tunic is one of the layers of the vessel wall around the lumen, not the space itself. Pick it if the question asked for the general name of a wall layer.","Correct. The lumen is the channel inside the vessel.","A fenestration is a small pore in the endothelium of certain capillaries. It would be the answer if the question asked about the windows in kidney or intestinal capillaries."],"lt":"t-blood-vessels","lid":"c8-x2","comp":66,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-tunics"},{"id":"L01720","dok":1,"q":"What are the flaps of the tunica intima in limb veins that keep blood from flowing backward called?","a":"Venous valves.","options":["Fenestrations","Anastomoses","Venous valves","Precapillary sphincters"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Venous valves are flaps of the tunica intima that project into the lumen of a vein. They open as blood moves toward the heart and close if it starts to flow backward. They are most common in limb veins, where blood must travel uphill at low pressure, and they can sometimes be seen as small bumps along a forearm vein.","notes":["Fenestrations are small pores in the endothelium of some capillaries, which let fluid pass quickly. Pick them if the question asked about the pores in kidney capillaries.","Anastomoses are junctions between vessels that give blood an alternate route. Choose them if the question asked what keeps a tissue supplied when one vessel is blocked.","Correct. Venous valves are the intimal flaps that stop backflow.","Precapillary sphincters are rings of smooth muscle that open or close the entrance to a capillary. They would be the answer if the question asked what controls blood entering a single capillary."],"lt":"t-blood-vessels","lid":"c13-x2","comp":68,"competencyId":"bvn-artery-cap-vein-kinds"},{"id":"L01722","dok":1,"q":"What is the narrowing of a vessel caused by contraction of the smooth muscle in its wall called?","a":"Vasoconstriction.","options":["Vasodilation","Atherosclerosis","Vasoconstriction","Aneurysm"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Vasoconstriction is the narrowing of a vessel as the smooth muscle of its tunica media contracts. Vaso means vessel and constriction means narrowing. It reduces blood flow through that vessel and is one way arterioles control how much blood reaches a capillary bed.","notes":["Vasodilation is the opposite, the widening of a vessel as its smooth muscle relaxes. Pick it if the question asked what happens when the tunica media relaxes.","Atherosclerosis also narrows an artery, but the cause is fatty plaque building up in the wall, not muscle contraction. It would be the answer if the question asked about narrowing caused by plaque.","Correct. Vasoconstriction is narrowing from smooth muscle contraction.","An aneurysm is a weakened area of a vessel wall that balloons outward, the opposite of narrowing. Choose it if the question asked about a bulge in an artery wall."],"lt":"t-blood-vessels","lid":"c15-x2","comp":66,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-tunics"},{"id":"L01724","dok":1,"q":"What is the tapered terminal end of an arteriole, where it joins a capillary bed, called?","a":"The metarteriole.","options":["The postcapillary venule","The metarteriole","The precapillary sphincter","The muscular artery"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The metarteriole is the tapered end of an arteriole, where it meets a capillary bed. Capillaries branch off it, and precapillary sphincters at those branch points open or close each capillary's entrance. Its far end has no smooth muscle and forms a thoroughfare channel that leads straight to a venule.","notes":["The postcapillary venule is on the opposite side of the capillary bed, receiving blood as it leaves the capillaries. Pick it if the question asked which vessel first drains a capillary bed.","Correct. The metarteriole is the arteriole's tapered end at the capillary bed.","The precapillary sphincter is a ring of smooth muscle on the metarteriole at the entrance to a capillary, not the arteriole's end itself. It would be the answer if the question asked what opens or closes a capillary.","A muscular artery is a much larger, medium-sized artery that distributes blood to organs. Choose it if the question asked about arteries like the brachial or femoral."],"lt":"t-blood-vessels","lid":"c17-x2","comp":67,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-types"},{"id":"L01725","dok":1,"q":"Which structure is a ring of smooth muscle that opens or closes the entrance to a capillary?","a":"A precapillary sphincter.","options":["A precapillary sphincter","A fenestration","A venous valve","An anastomosis"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"A precapillary sphincter is a ring of smooth muscle on the metarteriole at the point where a capillary branches off. When it contracts, blood is kept out of that capillary, and when it relaxes, blood flows in. A sphincter is any ring of muscle that closes an opening, which is exactly its job here.","notes":["Correct. A precapillary sphincter guards a capillary's entrance.","A fenestration is a small pore in the endothelium of some capillaries. Choose it if the question asked about the windows in fenestrated capillaries.","A venous valve is a flap of the tunica intima in a vein that prevents backflow, with no muscle in it. Pick it if the question asked what keeps limb vein blood moving toward the heart.","An anastomosis is a junction where two vessels meet, giving an alternate route. It would be the answer if the question asked what keeps a tissue supplied when one vessel is blocked."],"lt":"t-blood-vessels","lid":"c18-x2","comp":67,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-types"},{"id":"L01726","dok":2,"q":"Sinusoids are found where even whole blood cells must cross the capillary wall. Which of these tissues contains sinusoids?","a":"Red bone marrow.","options":["Skeletal muscle","The brain","The small intestine","Red bone marrow"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Sinusoids are wide, leaky capillaries with large gaps and an incomplete basement membrane. Red bone marrow uses them so newly made blood cells can move from the marrow into the bloodstream. Sinusoids are also found in the liver, spleen, anterior pituitary and adrenal glands.","notes":["Skeletal muscle has continuous capillaries, the most common type. It would be the answer if the question asked where continuous capillaries are found.","The brain has continuous capillaries, the tightest type, which keep most substances out of brain tissue. Pick it if the question asked where continuous capillaries matter most.","The small intestine has fenestrated capillaries, whose small pores speed the absorption of nutrients. Choose it if the question asked where fenestrated capillaries are found.","Correct. Red bone marrow has sinusoids so new blood cells can enter the blood."],"lt":"t-blood-vessels","lid":"c19-x2","comp":68,"competencyId":"bvn-artery-cap-vein-kinds"},{"id":"L01727","dok":2,"q":"Contracting limb muscles squeeze the deep veins and push blood toward the heart. What is this mechanism called?","a":"The skeletal muscle pump.","options":["Elastic recoil","The skeletal muscle pump","Vasoconstriction","The precapillary sphincter"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The skeletal muscle pump is the squeezing of deep veins by the surrounding skeletal muscles as they contract during movement. Each squeeze pushes blood forward, and the venous valves stop it from sliding back. This is one of the main ways low-pressure venous blood returns from the limbs to the heart.","notes":["Elastic recoil is the springing back of elastic arteries like the aorta between heartbeats. Choose it if the question asked what keeps arterial blood moving while the ventricles relax.","Correct. The skeletal muscle pump moves blood through the deep veins.","Vasoconstriction is narrowing of a vessel by the smooth muscle in its own wall, not squeezing by nearby skeletal muscle. Pick it if the question asked what happens when a vessel's tunica media contracts.","A precapillary sphincter is a smooth muscle ring that controls entry into a capillary, far from the deep veins. It would be the answer if the question asked what opens or closes a capillary entrance."],"lt":"t-blood-vessels","lid":"c20-x2","comp":68,"competencyId":"bvn-artery-cap-vein-kinds"},{"id":"L01731","dok":2,"q":"In the vascular sequence, what do capillaries merge into as blood starts back toward the heart?","a":"Venules.","options":["Arterioles","Elastic arteries","Metarterioles","Venules"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"After exchange in the capillary bed, the capillaries merge into venules, the smallest veins. Venules then join to form larger veins that return blood to the heart. The full sequence is artery, arteriole, capillary, venule, vein.","notes":["Arterioles come before the capillaries, feeding blood into the bed. Pick them if the question asked which vessels supply a capillary bed.","Elastic arteries, like the aorta, are the largest arteries near the heart, at the very start of the sequence. They would be the answer if the question asked which arteries stretch and recoil with each heartbeat.","Metarterioles are the tapered ends of arterioles on the arterial side of the bed. Choose them if the question asked which vessel segment leads into the capillaries.","Correct. Capillaries drain into venules."],"lt":"t-blood-vessels","lid":"c24-x2","comp":67,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-types"},{"id":"L01732","dok":2,"q":"Blood is drawn and an IV line is placed in a forearm vessel because it is close to the surface, thin-walled, and at low pressure. Which vessel type is used?","a":"A vein.","options":["An arteriole","A vein","An artery","A capillary"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Veins are used for blood draws and IV lines because they sit close to the surface, have thin walls with wide lumens, and carry blood at low pressure. That makes them easy to enter with a needle and slow to bleed afterward. Their valves can even be seen as small bumps along a forearm vein.","notes":["Arterioles are microscopic arteries buried in the tissues, too small and too deep to use. Choose them if the question asked which vessels control flow into a capillary bed.","Correct. A superficial, thin-walled, low-pressure vein is used.","Arteries carry blood under high pressure, have thick walls, and usually lie deeper, so puncturing one bleeds more and is harder to control. Pick an artery if the question asked where you feel a pulse.","Capillaries are far too small for a needle or IV line, though a finger stick does draw a drop of capillary blood. They would be the answer if the question asked where exchange with the tissues happens."],"lt":"t-blood-vessels","lid":"c26-x2","comp":67,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-types"},{"id":"L01733","dok":2,"q":"The brain needs capillaries that keep most substances in the blood out of the brain tissue. Which capillary type fits this job?","a":"The continuous capillary.","options":["The postcapillary venule","The sinusoid","The continuous capillary","The fenestrated capillary"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Continuous capillaries have an unbroken endothelial lining with only narrow clefts between cells, so they are the tightest type. In the brain the cells are joined especially tightly, which keeps most substances in the blood from reaching brain tissue. Capillary type is always matched to what the tissue needs to let through.","notes":["The postcapillary venule is a small, very porous vein that drains a capillary bed, not a capillary type, and it is where white blood cells leave the blood. Choose it if the question asked where white blood cells exit the bloodstream.","Sinusoids are the leakiest capillaries, letting even whole cells cross. They would be the answer if the question described the liver, spleen or red bone marrow.","Correct. Tight continuous capillaries protect the brain.","Fenestrated capillaries have pores built for fast filtration and absorption, the opposite of what the brain needs. Pick them if the question described the kidney or small intestine."],"lt":"t-blood-vessels","lid":"c27-x2","comp":68,"competencyId":"bvn-artery-cap-vein-kinds"},{"id":"L01734","dok":2,"q":"Following blood from the heart to a tissue, it has just left an arteriole. Which vessel does it enter next?","a":"A capillary.","options":["An elastic artery","A venule","A capillary","A vein"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Arterioles are the last vessels on the arterial side, and they feed directly into the capillaries of a capillary bed. In the capillaries, gases, nutrients and wastes are exchanged with the tissue. From there blood drains into venules and then veins.","notes":["An elastic artery, like the aorta, is at the start of the sequence near the heart, before the arterioles. It would be the answer if the question asked which vessel blood enters when it leaves the left ventricle.","A venule comes after the capillaries, collecting the blood that has passed through the bed. Choose it if the question asked which vessel blood enters after leaving a capillary.","Correct. Arterioles empty into capillaries.","A vein comes at the end of the sequence, after the capillaries and venules. Pick it if the question asked which vessel carries blood back to the heart."],"lt":"t-blood-vessels","lid":"c28-x2","comp":67,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-types"},{"id":"L01736","dok":2,"q":"The far end of a metarteriole has no smooth muscle and runs straight from the arteriole to a venule. What is this channel called?","a":"A thoroughfare channel.","options":["A precapillary sphincter","An arteriovenous anastomosis","A thoroughfare channel","A postcapillary venule"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"A thoroughfare channel is the distal end of a metarteriole, which has no smooth muscle and leads straight to a venule. It gives blood a direct route through the capillary bed. When the precapillary sphincters close, blood can pass through this channel instead of entering the capillaries.","notes":["A precapillary sphincter is a ring of smooth muscle at a capillary entrance, while this channel has no muscle at all. Pick it if the question asked what opens or closes a capillary.","An arteriovenous anastomosis connects an artery directly to a vein, outside the capillary bed. Choose it if the question described an artery linked straight to a vein with no capillaries involved.","Correct. The muscle-free end of the metarteriole is the thoroughfare channel.","A postcapillary venule is the first small vein receiving blood from the capillaries, where the thoroughfare channel empties. It would be the answer if the question asked which vessel first drains a capillary bed."],"lt":"t-blood-vessels","lid":"c30-x2","comp":67,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-types"},{"id":"L01737","dok":2,"q":"A capillary wall is a single layer of flat endothelial cells, with no muscle or connective tissue layers. Why does this thin wall suit the capillary's job?","a":"It makes the distance for diffusion between blood and tissue as short as possible.","options":["It keeps all blood cells and proteins inside the vessel","It makes the distance for diffusion as short as possible","It makes the vessel strong enough for high arterial pressure","It lets the capillary narrow and widen to control flow"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Capillaries are the exchange vessels, and gases, nutrients and wastes cross their walls mainly by diffusion, the movement of substances from higher to lower concentration. Diffusion is fastest over short distances. A wall only one flat cell thick keeps the distance between the blood and the tissue as short as possible.","notes":["Thinness does not decide what stays inside; that depends on the capillary type, and sinusoids are thin yet let whole cells cross. This choice confuses the thickness of the wall with how tightly its cells are joined.","Correct. A one-cell wall gives the shortest diffusion distance.","A thin wall is weaker, not stronger, which is why capillaries sit downstream of arterioles where pressure has already dropped. Thick walls for high pressure describe arteries.","Narrowing and widening require smooth muscle, which capillaries lack. That job belongs to arterioles and precapillary sphincters upstream of the capillaries."],"lt":"t-blood-vessels","lid":"d3-t-blood-vessels-1","comp":66,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-tunics"},{"id":"L01738","dok":2,"q":"Arteries have thick, elastic, muscular walls, while veins of similar size have thinner walls. Why does this difference fit each vessel's job?","a":"Arteries handle the high pressure of blood leaving the heart, while veins carry blood back at low pressure.","options":["Arteries face high pressure from the heart; veins carry low-pressure blood back","Veins carry blood at higher pressure, so their thin walls stretch to absorb it","Arteries need thick walls to hold the larger share of the body's blood","Arteries need thick walls so exchange can happen across them"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Arteries receive blood forced out by the ventricles, so they must withstand high pressure, and their thick tunica media of smooth muscle and elastic fibers lets them stretch, recoil and control their diameter. By the time blood reaches the veins, most of that pressure is gone. Veins can therefore have thinner walls, relying on valves and nearby muscles to keep blood moving.","notes":["Correct. Wall thickness matches the pressure each vessel carries.","This reverses the pressures. Arteries carry the high-pressure blood, and veins carry low-pressure blood back to the heart.","Veins, not arteries, hold the largest share of the body's blood, about sixty percent at rest. Their thin walls and wide lumens are what let them act as reservoirs.","Exchange happens only across thin capillary walls, and a thick wall would block it. Pick capillaries if the question asked which vessels are built for exchange."],"lt":"t-blood-vessels","lid":"d3-t-blood-vessels-2","comp":67,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-types"},{"id":"L01739","dok":2,"q":"Veins, especially in the legs, contain one-way valves that arteries lack. Why do veins need valves while arteries do not?","a":"Venous blood moves at low pressure, often against gravity, so valves stop it from flowing backward.","options":["Valves in veins filter debris out of the returning blood","Valves in veins speed blood up to match arterial pressure","Valves keep blood from flowing too quickly into the capillaries","Low-pressure venous blood, often moving uphill, can slide backward"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Arterial blood is pushed forward by high pressure from the heart, so it does not tend to flow backward. Venous blood moves at low pressure and, in the legs, has to travel uphill against gravity. Valves made from flaps of the tunica intima close if blood starts to slide back, so each push from nearby muscles moves blood only toward the heart.","notes":["Venous valves are simple flaps with no filtering role. Filtering of blood happens in organs like the spleen and the kidneys, not in vein walls.","Valves do not add pressure or speed; they only prevent backward flow. Students pick this when they confuse a valve with a pump.","Veins are downstream of the capillaries, so their valves cannot control flow into capillaries. That job belongs to arterioles and precapillary sphincters on the arterial side.","Correct. Low pressure and gravity make valves necessary in veins."],"lt":"t-blood-vessels","lid":"d3-t-blood-vessels-3","comp":68,"competencyId":"bvn-artery-cap-vein-kinds"},{"id":"L01740","dok":2,"q":"Arterioles have smooth muscle in their walls that can tighten or relax. Why does this muscular control matter for the body?","a":"By narrowing or widening, arterioles send more blood to the tissues that need it most.","options":["The muscle pumps blood forward like a small heart","The muscle lets nutrients pass through the arteriole wall","It lets arterioles send more blood to tissues that need it","The muscle keeps blood from flowing backward toward the heart"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Arterioles are the gatekeepers of capillary beds. When their smooth muscle relaxes (vasodilation), more blood flows into that bed, and when it contracts (vasoconstriction), less does. This lets the body shift blood to active tissues, such as working muscles during exercise, and away from tissues that need less at the moment.","notes":["Vessel smooth muscle holds or changes diameter; it does not beat rhythmically to pump blood. The heart is the only pump in the system.","Arteriole walls are too thick for exchange, and their muscle does not help substances cross. Nutrients cross in the capillaries, whose walls are one cell thick.","Correct. Adjusting their diameter lets arterioles direct blood where it is needed.","Preventing backflow is the job of venous valves in veins. Arterial blood is already pushed forward by the pressure from the heart."],"lt":"t-blood-vessels","lid":"d3-t-blood-vessels-4","comp":67,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-types"},{"id":"L01741","dok":2,"q":"The aorta has an especially high proportion of elastic tissue in its wall. Why is this elasticity valuable right where blood leaves the heart?","a":"The aorta stretches to absorb each surge of blood, then recoils to keep blood moving between beats.","options":["It lets oxygen and nutrients pass through the aortic wall","It lets the aorta contract on its own to pump blood like a second heart","It keeps the aorta rigid so it cannot expand","It stretches with each surge, then recoils to push blood between beats"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"With each contraction, the left ventricle pushes a surge of blood into the aorta. The elastic wall stretches to absorb that surge, then recoils while the ventricles relax, pushing blood onward between beats. This smooths the flow, and when elastic arteries stiffen with age, that cushioning is lost and the strain shows up in smaller vessels downstream.","notes":["The aortic wall is far too thick for exchange, and its own outer layers are supplied by the vasa vasorum. Oxygen and nutrients reach tissues only across capillary walls.","Elastic tissue recoils passively but cannot contract on its own, and the aorta does not pump. Students pick this when they confuse elastic recoil with muscle contraction.","Elastic tissue does the opposite of making a wall rigid; it lets it stretch and spring back. Stiffness is what happens when elastic arteries lose that tissue with age.","Correct. Stretch and recoil keep blood moving between heartbeats."],"lt":"t-blood-vessels","lid":"d3-t-blood-vessels-5","comp":68,"competencyId":"bvn-artery-cap-vein-kinds"},{"id":"L01742","dok":2,"q":"Blood flow slows dramatically as it passes through capillary beds compared with arteries. Why is slow flow through capillaries useful?","a":"It gives more time for substances to move between the blood and the tissue.","options":["It allows more time for exchange with the tissue","Capillaries actually have the fastest flow in the body","It lets the capillaries hold most of the body's blood","It gives platelets time to seal the capillary wall"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Capillaries are where exchange happens, and exchange takes time because substances cross the wall by diffusion. Blood slows in the capillaries because it is spread across an enormous number of tiny vessels. That slower pace gives oxygen, nutrients and wastes more time to cross between the blood and the tissue.","notes":["Correct. Slow flow allows more time for exchange.","Flow is slowest, not fastest, in the capillaries. Students pick this because each capillary is so narrow, but the total cross-section of all capillaries together is huge, which slows the flow.","The veins, not the capillaries, hold the largest share of the blood, about sixty percent at rest. Choose the veins if the question asked which vessels act as blood reservoirs.","Platelets seal a vessel only after it is injured, and slow flow is not about clotting. This choice mixes up normal exchange with the response to bleeding."],"lt":"t-blood-vessels","lid":"d3-t-blood-vessels-6","comp":67,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-types"},{"id":"L01744","dok":2,"q":"Capillaries are so narrow that red blood cells pass through almost single file. Why is this tight fit useful?","a":"It brings each red cell close to the wall, shortening the distance for gas exchange.","options":["It squeezes oxygen out of the red cells by pressure","It keeps white blood cells from entering the capillary","It keeps each red cell close to the wall for gas exchange","It filters worn-out red blood cells out of circulation"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Oxygen and carbon dioxide move between red blood cells and tissue by diffusion, which works best over short distances. When red cells pass through a capillary almost single file, each one presses close to the thin endothelial wall. That keeps the path for gases between the cell and the tissue very short.","notes":["Oxygen leaves red cells by diffusion toward the tissue, not because the cells are squeezed. Students pick this when they picture the capillary wringing the cells out like a sponge.","White blood cells do pass through capillaries and usually leave the blood at venules. The tight fit is about exchange, not about keeping white cells out.","Correct. Single-file flow keeps each red cell close to the wall for exchange.","Worn-out red blood cells are removed mainly in the spleen, which uses leaky sinusoids for that job. Pick the spleen if the question asked where old red cells are filtered out."],"lt":"t-blood-vessels","lid":"d3-t-blood-vessels-8","comp":67,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-types"},{"id":"N-m3-vessel-wall-1","dok":1,"q":"Which structure is a fenestrated sheet of elastic fibers at the boundary between the tunica intima and the tunica media?","a":"The internal elastic lamina.","options":["The vasa vasorum","The external elastic lamina","The basement membrane","The internal elastic lamina"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The internal elastic lamina is a sheet of elastic fibers with small openings that marks the boundary between the tunica intima and the tunica media. Internal tells you it is the inner of the two elastic sheets. It is easy to see in arteries as a wavy line just outside the endothelium.","notes":["The vasa vasorum are small blood vessels in the tunica externa that supply the walls of large vessels. Choose them if the question asked how the wall of the aorta gets its own blood supply.","The external elastic lamina is the outer elastic sheet, separating the tunica media from the tunica externa. Pick it if the question asked which boundary lies between the media and the externa.","The basement membrane is the thin layer the endothelium rests on, inside the tunica intima, and it is not made of elastic fibers. It would be the answer if the question asked what supports the endothelial cells.","Correct. The internal elastic lamina separates the intima from the media."],"comp":66,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-tunics"},{"id":"N-m3-vessel-wall-2","dok":2,"q":"On an artery slide, you find the internal and external elastic laminae. Which layer lies between them?","a":"The tunica media.","options":["The tunica externa","The tunica media","The tunica intima","The lumen"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The internal elastic lamina marks the inner edge of the tunica media, and the external elastic lamina marks its outer edge. So everything between the two elastic sheets is the tunica media. Measuring how thick that band is helps you decide whether you are looking at an artery, with a thick media, or a vein, with a thin one.","notes":["The tunica externa lies outside the external elastic lamina. It would be the answer if the question asked which layer is found beyond the outer elastic sheet.","Correct. The two elastic laminae border the tunica media.","The tunica intima lies inside the internal elastic lamina, between it and the lumen. Pick it if the question asked which layer is found on the lumen side of the internal elastic lamina.","The lumen is the open channel in the center of the vessel, inside the tunica intima. Choose it if the question asked where the blood flows."],"comp":66,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-tunics"},{"id":"N-m3-vessel-wall-3","dok":2,"q":"A vascular sinus is a vein with a thin endothelial wall and no smooth muscle, supported by the dense connective tissue around it. Which of these is a vascular sinus?","a":"The coronary sinus.","options":["The coronary sinus","The maxillary sinus","The femoral vein","The great saphenous vein"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The coronary sinus on the back of the heart is a vascular sinus. It collects blood from the cardiac veins and empties it into the right atrium, and its wall is thin endothelium without smooth muscle. The dural venous sinuses of the brain are the other main example.","notes":["Correct. The coronary sinus is a vascular sinus.","The maxillary sinus is an air-filled paranasal sinus in the maxilla, not a blood vessel at all. Students pick it because of the shared word sinus, so choose it only for a question about the paranasal sinuses.","The femoral vein is a large deep vein of the thigh with a normal three-layered wall. It would be the answer if the question asked which deep vein runs with the femoral artery.","The great saphenous vein is a long superficial vein of the leg with all three tunics, including smooth muscle, and valves along its length. Pick it if the question asked for the longest superficial vein of the lower limb."],"comp":68,"competencyId":"bvn-artery-cap-vein-kinds"},{"id":"N-m3-vessel-wall-4","dok":2,"q":"Avascular tissues have no capillaries at all. Which of these tissues is avascular?","a":"Cartilage.","options":["The liver","Skeletal muscle","Cartilage","Red bone marrow"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Avascular means without vessels. Cartilage, the cornea and lens of the eye, and the covering and lining epithelia have no capillaries of their own. They get nutrients by diffusion from nearby tissue, which is one reason cartilage heals so slowly.","notes":["The liver is very well supplied with blood and contains sinusoids, the leakiest capillary type. It would be the answer if the question asked which organ has sinusoids that receive blood from the hepatic portal vein.","Skeletal muscle is richly supplied with continuous capillaries to meet its high demand for oxygen. Pick it if the question asked where continuous capillaries are commonly found.","Correct. Cartilage has no capillaries.","Red bone marrow contains sinusoids so new blood cells can enter the bloodstream. Choose it if the question asked where blood cells are made."],"comp":26,"competencyId":"w2-cartilage-types"},{"id":"N-m3-vessel-wall-5","dok":1,"q":"What is the widening of a vessel as the smooth muscle in its wall relaxes called?","a":"Vasodilation.","options":["Vasodilation","Vasoconstriction","Aneurysm","Vascular tone"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Vasodilation is the widening of a vessel when the smooth muscle of its tunica media relaxes. A wider lumen lets more blood flow through. Arterioles use vasodilation to send extra blood into capillary beds of tissues that need it, such as working muscles.","notes":["Correct. Vasodilation is widening from smooth muscle relaxation.","Vasoconstriction is the opposite, narrowing of a vessel as its smooth muscle contracts. Pick it if the question asked what happens when the tunica media contracts.","An aneurysm is a bulge in a weakened artery wall, a disorder rather than a normal response of smooth muscle. Choose it if the question asked about a balloon-like swelling that can rupture.","Vascular tone is the partial contraction a vessel holds at rest, which stiffens the wall and keeps up pressure. It would be the answer if the question asked about the vessel's resting state rather than a change in diameter."],"comp":66,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-tunics"},{"id":"L01820","dok":2,"q":"Chronically high blood sugar damages capillaries, harming capillary-rich organs such as the retina and kidneys. What makes up the wall of a capillary, the part that is injured?","a":"A single layer of endothelium on a basement membrane.","options":["A thick layer of smooth muscle and elastic fibers","Three layers, like the wall of a small artery","A single layer of endothelium on a basement membrane","An outer coat of loose connective tissue"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"A capillary wall is only the tunica intima: one layer of simple squamous endothelium resting on a basement membrane. That thinness is what makes capillaries the exchange vessels, since gases and nutrients cross easily. It also means there is little protecting them, so damage to the endothelium directly harms organs that depend on dense capillary beds.","notes":["Smooth muscle and elastic fibers make up the tunica media, the thick middle layer of arteries. Capillaries have no tunica media, so pick this for a question about the layer that lets arteries constrict.","Arteries and veins have three tunics, but capillaries have only one. Students choose this when they assume every vessel shares the same layered plan.","Correct. The capillary wall is a single layer of endothelium on a basement membrane, the tunica intima alone.","An outer coat of connective tissue is the tunica externa, found on arteries and veins. Capillaries lack it, so choose this for a question about the outermost tunic of larger vessels."],"lt":"t-blood-vessel-disorders","lid":"d3x-t-blood-vessel-disorders-3","comp":66,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-tunics"},{"id":"L03603","dok":1,"q":"What is a blood vessel?","a":"A tube that carries blood through the body.","options":["A cord of dense tissue linking muscle to bone","A tube that carries lymph back to the veins","A tube that carries air into the lungs","A tube that carries blood through the body"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"A blood vessel is a tube that carries blood through the body. Arteries carry blood away from the heart, capillaries allow exchange with the tissues, and veins carry blood back toward the heart. Together with the heart, the vessels form the cardiovascular system.","notes":["A cord of dense connective tissue linking muscle to bone is a tendon. Choose it for a question about how muscles attach to bones.","A tube that carries lymph is a lymphatic vessel. Lymphatic vessels return excess tissue fluid to the veins and would be the answer for the lymphatic system.","Tubes that carry air into the lungs are airways such as the trachea and bronchi. They belong to the respiratory system.","Correct. A blood vessel is a tube for blood."],"lt":"t-vessel-structure","lid":"W3-319","comp":67,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-types"},{"id":"L03604","dok":1,"q":"What is an artery?","a":"A vessel that carries blood away from the heart.","options":["A vessel that always carries oxygen-rich blood","The smallest vessel, where exchange occurs","A vessel that carries blood toward the heart","A vessel that carries blood away from the heart"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"An artery is any vessel that carries blood away from the heart. Most arteries carry oxygen-rich blood, but the definition is about direction, not oxygen; the pulmonary arteries carry oxygen-poor blood and are still arteries. Arteries have thick walls with a heavy tunica media to handle high pressure.","notes":["This is a common misconception. The pulmonary arteries carry oxygen-poor blood, so oxygen content cannot define an artery; direction away from the heart does.","The smallest vessel, where exchange occurs, is a capillary. It would be the answer for the vessel whose wall is only the tunica intima.","A vessel that carries blood toward the heart is a vein. Choose this definition for the venae cavae or the pulmonary veins.","Correct. Arteries carry blood away from the heart."],"lt":"t-vessel-structure","lid":"W3-320","comp":67,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-types"},{"id":"L03605","dok":1,"q":"What is a vein?","a":"A vessel that carries blood toward the heart.","options":["A vessel that carries blood away from the heart","A vessel that carries blood toward the heart","A vessel that always carries oxygen-poor blood","A vessel whose wall is only endothelium"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"A vein is any vessel that carries blood toward the heart. Most veins carry oxygen-poor blood, but the pulmonary veins carry oxygen-rich blood and are still veins, because the definition depends on direction. Veins have thinner walls and wider lumens than arteries, and many have valves.","notes":["A vessel that carries blood away from the heart is an artery. Choose this definition for the aorta or the pulmonary trunk.","Correct. Veins carry blood toward the heart.","This is a common misconception. The pulmonary veins carry oxygen-rich blood, so oxygen content cannot define a vein; direction toward the heart does.","A vessel whose wall is only endothelium on a basement membrane is a capillary. It would be the answer for the exchange vessels."],"lt":"t-vessel-structure","lid":"W3-321","comp":67,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-types"},{"id":"L03606","dok":1,"q":"What is a capillary?","a":"The smallest vessel, where exchange occurs between blood and tissues.","options":["A small artery that controls flow into capillaries","The largest artery, leaving the left ventricle","The smallest vessel, where exchange with tissues occurs","A small vein that collects blood from capillaries"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"A capillary is the smallest blood vessel, connecting an arteriole to a venule. Its wall is a single layer of endothelium on a basement membrane, so it is thin enough for gases, nutrients, and wastes to pass between the blood and the tissues. This exchange is the whole point of the circulation.","notes":["A small artery that controls flow into capillaries is an arteriole. Its smooth muscle narrows or widens it to adjust how much blood enters a capillary bed.","The largest artery, leaving the left ventricle, is the aorta. It is a thick-walled elastic artery built to handle high pressure, the opposite of a capillary.","Correct. Capillaries are the tiny exchange vessels.","A small vein that collects blood from capillaries is a venule. Choose it for the vessel that drains a capillary bed."],"lt":"t-vessel-structure","lid":"W3-322","comp":67,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-types"},{"id":"L03608","dok":2,"q":"Which sequence correctly lists the vessels blood passes through, starting from the heart?","a":"Arteries, arterioles, capillaries, venules, veins.","options":["Arteries, venules, capillaries, arterioles, veins","Arteries, arterioles, capillaries, venules, veins","Arteries, capillaries, arterioles, venules, veins","Veins, venules, capillaries, arterioles, arteries"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Blood leaves the heart in arteries, which branch into smaller arterioles. Arterioles feed the capillaries, where exchange with the tissues happens. Capillaries merge into venules, and venules join to form veins, which carry blood back to the heart.","notes":["This swaps arterioles and venules. Arterioles are the small arteries leading into capillaries, and venules are the small veins leading out of them.","Correct. Arteries, arterioles, capillaries, venules, veins.","This places capillaries before arterioles. Arteries must branch into arterioles before reaching the capillaries.","This is the sequence run backward, from veins to arteries. Blood flows away from the heart in arteries first and returns in veins, not the reverse."],"lt":"t-vessel-structure","lid":"W3-324","comp":67,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-types"},{"id":"L03609","dok":1,"q":"Which set names the three tunics of an artery or vein wall?","a":"Tunica intima, tunica media, tunica externa.","options":["Mucosa, submucosa, muscularis externa","Epicardium, myocardium, endocardium","Epidermis, dermis, hypodermis","Tunica intima, tunica media, tunica externa"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The wall of an artery or vein is built in three concentric layers called tunics. From the inside out they are the tunica intima, a lining of endothelium; the tunica media, smooth muscle and elastic fibers; and the tunica externa, mostly collagen. A capillary keeps only the tunica intima.","notes":["The mucosa, submucosa, and muscularis externa are layers of the digestive tract wall. Choose them for a question about the wall of the stomach or intestine.","The epicardium, myocardium, and endocardium are the three layers of the heart wall. The endocardium is continuous with the tunica intima, but this set names the heart's layers.","The epidermis, dermis, and hypodermis are the layers of the skin and the tissue beneath it. They belong to the integumentary system.","Correct. These are the three tunics of a vessel wall."],"lt":"t-vessel-structure","lid":"W3-325","comp":66,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-tunics"},{"id":"L03610","dok":1,"q":"Which tunic is the innermost layer of a vessel wall?","a":"Tunica intima.","options":["Tunica externa","Tunica media","Tunica intima","Tunica albuginea"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The tunica intima is the innermost tunic, the one in direct contact with the blood. It is a lining of simple squamous endothelium on a basement membrane, and it is continuous with the endocardium of the heart. Intima shares a root with intimate, meaning innermost.","notes":["The tunica externa is the outermost tunic, mostly collagen, that anchors the vessel. It would be the answer for the outer layer.","The tunica media is the middle tunic, made of smooth muscle and elastic fibers. Choose it for the layer that sets the vessel's diameter.","Correct. The tunica intima is the innermost layer.","The tunica albuginea is the dense connective tissue capsule around the testis and the ovary, not a layer of a vessel wall. The shared word tunica makes it easy to confuse."],"lt":"t-vessel-structure","lid":"W3-326","comp":66,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-tunics"},{"id":"L03612","dok":1,"q":"Which tunic is the outermost layer of a vessel wall?","a":"Tunica externa.","options":["Basement membrane","Tunica externa","Tunica media","Tunica intima"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The tunica externa is the outermost tunic of a vessel wall. It is made mostly of collagen fibers, with nerves and small vessels, and it anchors the vessel to the surrounding tissue. In large vessels it holds the vasa vasorum, small vessels that supply the wall itself.","notes":["The basement membrane is the thin layer under the endothelium, part of the tunica intima. It sits on the inner side of the wall, not the outside.","Correct. The tunica externa is the outermost layer.","The tunica media is the middle layer of smooth muscle and elastic fibers. It would be the answer for the layer that controls the vessel's diameter.","The tunica intima is the innermost layer, lining the lumen. Choose it for the endothelium that touches the blood."],"lt":"t-vessel-structure","lid":"W3-328","comp":66,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-tunics"},{"id":"L03613","dok":1,"q":"What tissue lines the tunica intima?","a":"Simple squamous endothelium on a basement membrane.","options":["Stratified squamous epithelium","Simple squamous endothelium","Dense collagen fibers","Smooth muscle with elastic fibers"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The tunica intima is lined by endothelium, a simple squamous epithelium: one layer of flat cells resting on a basement membrane. Its smooth surface lets blood flow with little friction, and its thinness is what makes exchange possible in capillaries, where the intima is the only layer.","notes":["Stratified squamous epithelium is many layers thick and resists abrasion, as in the epidermis and the lining of the esophagus. It would be far too thick for a vessel lining.","Correct. The intima is a single layer of flat endothelial cells on a basement membrane.","Dense collagen fibers make up most of the tunica externa, the outermost layer. It would be the answer for the layer that anchors the vessel.","Smooth muscle with elastic fibers makes up the tunica media, the middle layer. Choose it for the layer that changes vessel diameter."],"lt":"t-vessel-structure","lid":"W3-329","comp":66,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-tunics"},{"id":"L03614","dok":1,"q":"Which tunic is the only layer of a capillary wall?","a":"Tunica intima.","options":["Tunica media","Tunica intima","Vasa vasorum","Tunica externa"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"A capillary wall is only the tunica intima: a single layer of endothelium on a basement membrane. It has no tunica media and no tunica externa. That extreme thinness is what lets gases, nutrients, and wastes cross between the blood and the tissues.","notes":["The tunica media is the smooth muscle layer of arteries and veins. Capillaries have none; flow into them is controlled upstream by arterioles and precapillary sphincters.","Correct. A capillary is just the tunica intima.","The vasa vasorum are small vessels in the tunica externa that supply the walls of large vessels such as the aorta. A capillary is far too thin to need them.","The tunica externa is the collagen-rich outer layer of arteries and veins. Capillaries lack it entirely, so choose it for the layer that anchors larger vessels."],"lt":"t-vessel-structure","lid":"W3-330","comp":66,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-tunics"},{"id":"L03615","dok":1,"q":"What does the tunica media contain?","a":"Smooth muscle and elastic fibers.","options":["Smooth muscle and elastic fibers","Mostly collagen fibers","Skeletal muscle fibers","Simple squamous endothelium"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The tunica media is made of smooth muscle cells and elastic fibers arranged in rings around the vessel. The smooth muscle contracts or relaxes to narrow or widen the vessel, and the elastic fibers let the wall stretch and recoil with each heartbeat. Elastic arteries such as the aorta have a media dominated by elastic tissue, while muscular arteries have a media made mostly of smooth muscle.","notes":["Correct. The tunica media is smooth muscle and elastic fibers.","Mostly collagen fibers describes the tunica externa, the outermost layer that anchors the vessel. It would be the answer for that layer.","Skeletal muscle is voluntary muscle attached to bones, and it is not found in vessel walls. Vessel walls use smooth muscle, which works without conscious control.","Simple squamous endothelium lines the tunica intima, the innermost layer. Choose it for the layer touching the blood."],"lt":"t-vessel-structure","lid":"W3-331","comp":66,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-tunics"},{"id":"L03616","dok":1,"q":"Which tunic is the thickest layer in an artery wall?","a":"Tunica media.","options":["Tunica intima","Tunica externa","Tunica media","Basement membrane"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"In arteries the tunica media is the thickest layer. Arteries carry blood under high pressure, and the thick band of smooth muscle and elastic fibers lets them withstand that pressure, stretch with each beat, and control their diameter. In veins, by contrast, the tunica externa is usually the thickest layer.","notes":["The tunica intima is a thin lining of endothelium and is the thinnest tunic in arteries. It would be the answer for the only layer a capillary has.","The tunica externa is usually the thickest layer in veins, not arteries. Choose it for a question about vein walls.","Correct. Arteries have a thick tunica media.","The basement membrane is a very thin sheet beneath the endothelium, part of the tunica intima. It is far from the thickest layer."],"lt":"t-vessel-structure","lid":"W3-332","comp":67,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-types"},{"id":"L03617","dok":1,"q":"What does the smooth muscle of the tunica media control?","a":"Vessel diameter.","options":["Vessel diameter","Direction of flow through valves","Production of blood cells","Exchange of gases with tissues"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The smooth muscle of the tunica media wraps around the vessel. When it contracts, the vessel narrows (vasoconstriction); when it relaxes, the vessel widens (vasodilation). Changing the diameter adjusts how much blood flows through the vessel and helps maintain blood pressure.","notes":["Correct. Smooth muscle in the media narrows or widens the vessel.","One-way flow in veins is maintained by venous valves, which are folds of the tunica intima. The smooth muscle of the media does not form valves.","Blood cells are produced in red bone marrow, not in vessel walls. That would be the answer for a question about hematopoiesis.","Gas exchange happens across the thin walls of capillaries, which have no tunica media at all. Choose capillaries for a question about where exchange occurs."],"lt":"t-vessel-structure","lid":"W3-333","comp":66,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-tunics"},{"id":"L03618","dok":1,"q":"What does the tunica externa mostly contain?","a":"Collagen fibers.","options":["Endothelial cells","Collagen fibers","Smooth muscle cells","Cardiac muscle cells"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The tunica externa is made mostly of collagen fibers, along with nerves and small blood vessels. Collagen is strong and resists stretching, so this layer protects the vessel and anchors it to surrounding tissues. In large vessels it also carries the vasa vasorum, which supply the outer part of the wall.","notes":["Endothelial cells form the lining of the tunica intima, the innermost layer. They would be the answer for the layer in contact with blood.","Correct. The tunica externa is mostly collagen.","Smooth muscle is the main component of the tunica media, the middle layer. Choose it for the layer that changes vessel diameter.","Cardiac muscle is found in the heart wall, not in vessel walls. Choose it for the myocardium."],"lt":"t-vessel-structure","lid":"W3-334","comp":66,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-tunics"},{"id":"L03619","dok":1,"q":"What is the main job of the tunica externa?","a":"Anchoring the vessel to surrounding tissue.","options":["Changing the vessel's diameter","Allowing exchange with the tissues","Anchoring the vessel to surrounding tissue","Lining the lumen with a smooth surface"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The tunica externa is the outer, collagen-rich layer, and its main job is to anchor the vessel to the surrounding tissue and protect it from overstretching. Its collagen fibers blend into the connective tissue around the vessel, holding it in place. It also carries nerves and, in large vessels, the vasa vasorum.","notes":["Changing the vessel's diameter is the job of the smooth muscle in the tunica media. That would be the answer for vasoconstriction and vasodilation.","Exchange with tissues happens across capillary walls, which are only the tunica intima. The thick collagen of the externa would block exchange, which is why capillaries lack it.","Correct. The tunica externa anchors and protects the vessel.","Lining the lumen with a smooth surface is the job of the endothelium of the tunica intima. Choose the intima for the layer that touches blood."],"lt":"t-vessel-structure","lid":"W3-335","comp":66,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-tunics"},{"id":"L03620","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares arteries and veins by direction of flow?","a":"Arteries carry blood away from the heart; veins carry blood toward it.","options":["Arteries carry blood toward the heart; veins carry blood away from it","Arteries carry oxygen-rich blood and veins carry oxygen-poor blood, in every case","Both carry blood away from the heart, at different pressures","Arteries carry blood away from the heart; veins carry blood toward it"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Direction relative to the heart is what defines arteries and veins. Arteries carry blood away from the heart, and veins carry blood toward it. This rule holds without exception, while oxygen content does not, since the pulmonary arteries carry oxygen-poor blood and the pulmonary veins carry oxygen-rich blood.","notes":["This reverses the two definitions. A handy check is that artery and away both start with the same letter.","This is true for most vessels but fails for the pulmonary circuit. The pulmonary arteries carry oxygen-poor blood and the pulmonary veins carry oxygen-rich blood, so oxygen content cannot be the defining rule.","Only arteries carry blood away from the heart; veins return it. Arteries do run at higher pressure, but that is a difference in pressure, not in direction.","Correct. Arteries go away from the heart and veins return to it."],"lt":"t-vessel-structure","lid":"W3-349","comp":67,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-types"},{"id":"L03621","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares the walls of arteries and veins?","a":"Arteries have thicker walls; veins have thinner walls and wider lumens.","options":["Veins have thicker walls; arteries have thinner walls and wider lumens","Arteries have only a tunica intima; veins have all three tunics","Arteries have thicker walls; veins have thinner walls and wider lumens","Both have identical walls and differ only in the direction of flow"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Arteries carry blood under high pressure, so they have thick walls with a heavy tunica media, and on a slide they keep a round shape. Veins carry blood under low pressure, so they have thinner walls, a thinner media, and a wider lumen, and they often look collapsed on a slide. Both have the same three tunics, just in different proportions.","notes":["This reverses the pattern. Arteries need thick walls to withstand high pressure, while veins run at low pressure and have thinner walls.","A wall made of only the tunica intima belongs to a capillary. Arteries and veins both have all three tunics.","Correct. Arteries are thick-walled; veins are thinner-walled with wider lumens.","Arteries and veins have the same three tunics, but the proportions differ, especially the thickness of the tunica media. Those differences are how you tell them apart on a slide."],"lt":"t-vessel-structure","lid":"W3-351","comp":67,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-types"},{"id":"L03622","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares capillaries with arteries and veins?","a":"Capillaries are thin exchange vessels; arteries and veins are transport vessels.","options":["Capillaries carry blood only toward the heart; arteries and veins carry it away","Capillaries have the thickest walls; arteries and veins are the exchange vessels","Capillaries have all three tunics; arteries and veins have only the tunica intima","Capillaries are thin exchange vessels; arteries and veins are transport vessels"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Arteries and veins are transport vessels: their three-layered walls carry blood to and from the tissues but are too thick for exchange. Capillaries have only the tunica intima, a single layer of endothelium, so gases, nutrients, and wastes can cross between blood and tissue. Exchange happens only at the capillary level.","notes":["Capillaries connect arterioles to venules, so blood in them moves from the arterial side to the venous side. Carrying blood toward the heart describes veins, not capillaries.","Capillaries have the thinnest walls of any vessel, a single layer of endothelium. Arteries and veins are too thick for exchange, so this reverses both halves.","This reverses the layers. Arteries and veins have all three tunics, while capillaries have only the tunica intima.","Correct. Capillaries exchange; arteries and veins transport."],"lt":"t-vessel-structure","lid":"W3-352","comp":67,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-types"},{"id":"L03623","dok":2,"q":"Why do capillaries have only a tunica intima?","a":"A thin wall allows exchange between blood and tissues.","options":["A thin wall lets materials pass between blood and tissues","A thin wall lets capillaries withstand the highest pressure","Lacking a media lets capillaries contract more strongly","Capillaries need no lining because they hold no blood"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"A capillary wall is just endothelium on a basement membrane, a single cell thick. That short distance lets O2, CO2, nutrients, and wastes pass quickly between the blood and the tissue fluid. A tunica media or externa would add layers that block this exchange, so capillaries lack them.","notes":["Correct. The thin wall is what makes exchange possible.","Thin walls withstand less pressure, not more. Arteries handle the highest pressure, which is why they have the thickest tunica media.","Without a tunica media, capillaries have no smooth muscle layer to contract. Flow into them is controlled upstream by the arterioles and precapillary sphincters.","Capillaries are full of blood, and their endothelial lining is exactly what holds it. This choice misreads the single layer as no lining at all."],"lt":"t-vessel-structure","lid":"W3-353","comp":66,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-tunics"},{"id":"L03624","dok":2,"q":"Why is the tunica externa built mainly of collagen fibers?","a":"Collagen is strong, so it anchors and supports the vessel.","options":["Collagen lets gases pass through the wall","Collagen recoils to push blood along between heartbeats","Collagen contracts to change the vessel's diameter","Collagen is strong, so it anchors and protects the vessel"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Collagen fibers are strong and resist being stretched, much like the fibers of a rope. In the tunica externa they protect the vessel from overstretching and blend into the surrounding connective tissue, which anchors the vessel in place. Stretch and recoil come from elastic fibers in the media, and changes in diameter come from smooth muscle.","notes":["Gases pass through the thin endothelium of capillaries, which have no tunica externa at all. A thick collagen layer would block exchange rather than allow it.","Stretching and recoiling to keep blood moving between beats is the job of elastic fibers, which are concentrated in the tunica media of elastic arteries such as the aorta. Choose elastic fibers for that recoil.","Collagen is a protein fiber and cannot contract. Changing diameter is the job of the smooth muscle in the tunica media.","Correct. Strong collagen fibers anchor and protect the vessel."],"lt":"t-vessel-structure","lid":"W3-356","comp":66,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-tunics"},{"id":"L03625","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares arterioles with larger arteries?","a":"Arterioles are the smallest arteries and regulate how much blood enters capillary beds.","options":["Arterioles are larger than arteries and carry blood to the lungs","Arterioles are the smallest arteries and control flow into capillary beds","Arterioles have no smooth muscle and serve as the exchange vessels","Arterioles are the smallest veins and collect blood from capillaries"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Arterioles are the smallest arteries, with thin walls holding only one or two layers of smooth muscle. As arteries branch down into arterioles, the arterioles become the control valves of the circulation: by constricting or dilating, they adjust how much blood flows into each capillary bed. Larger arteries mainly carry and distribute blood under high pressure.","notes":["Arterioles are smaller than arteries, as the ending -ole (small) suggests. Blood reaches the lungs through the pulmonary trunk and pulmonary arteries.","Correct. Arterioles are the smallest arteries and regulate entry into capillary beds.","Arterioles do have smooth muscle, one or two layers, and that is how they control flow. The exchange vessels with no smooth muscle are the capillaries.","The smallest veins, which collect blood from capillaries, are venules. Choose venules for the vessels draining a capillary bed."],"lt":"t-vessel-structure","lid":"W3-357","comp":67,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-types"},{"id":"L03626","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares venules and veins?","a":"Venules collect blood from capillaries; veins return blood toward the heart.","options":["Venules carry blood away from the heart; veins carry it toward the heart","Venules feed blood into capillaries; veins drain the capillaries","Venules are larger than veins and have the thickest walls","Venules collect blood from capillaries; veins carry it on toward the heart"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Venules are the smallest veins. They collect blood as it leaves a capillary bed and merge into larger veins, which carry the blood back toward the heart under low pressure. Venule walls are thin and porous, while veins have all three tunics, a wide lumen, and often valves.","notes":["Venules are veins, so they carry blood toward the heart, not away from it. Vessels carrying blood away from the heart are arteries and arterioles.","Feeding blood into capillaries is the job of arterioles. Venules are the vessels that drain capillaries, which this choice wrongly gives to veins.","Venules are the smallest veins, as the ending -ule (small) suggests, and their walls are thin and porous. Larger veins come after them.","Correct. Venules gather blood from capillaries and veins carry it home to the heart."],"lt":"t-vessel-structure","lid":"W3-358","comp":67,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-types"},{"id":"L03627","dok":2,"q":"Why are capillaries called exchange vessels?","a":"Gases, nutrients, and wastes cross their thin walls.","options":["They pass blood between the right and left sides of the heart","Gases, nutrients, and wastes cross their thin walls","They trade blood cells directly with lymphatic vessels","They are where the heart exchanges blood with the great vessels"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Capillaries are called exchange vessels because their walls, a single layer of endothelium, are thin enough for materials to cross. O2 and nutrients leave the blood and enter the tissues, while CO2 and wastes move from the tissues into the blood. Arteries and veins only carry blood; the actual delivery happens in the capillaries.","notes":["The right and left sides of the heart are linked through the lungs by the pulmonary circuit, not by capillaries passing blood between them. In an adult heart no blood passes directly between the two sides.","Correct. Exchange happens across the thin capillary wall.","Lymphatic capillaries pick up excess tissue fluid that leaks out of blood capillaries, but blood capillaries do not trade blood cells directly with them. That idea fits a question about how lymph forms, not why capillaries are exchange vessels.","The heart connects to the great vessels through its valves, not through capillaries. Choose the semilunar valves for where blood passes from the ventricles into the great arteries."],"lt":"t-vessel-structure","lid":"W3-359","comp":67,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-types"},{"id":"L03628","dok":2,"q":"Which vessel type carries blood directly into a capillary bed?","a":"Arterioles.","options":["Veins","Elastic arteries","Venules","Arterioles"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Arterioles are the smallest arteries, and they deliver blood into capillary beds. Their smooth muscle, along with precapillary sphincters at the entrance to the capillaries, adjusts how much blood enters each bed. On the other side of the bed, venules collect the blood and carry it on toward the veins.","notes":["Veins receive blood from venules and carry it back toward the heart. They are two steps downstream of the capillaries.","Elastic arteries, such as the aorta and common carotid arteries, are the large arteries near the heart. They branch into muscular arteries and then arterioles long before blood reaches the capillaries.","Venules are on the other side of the capillary bed, draining it. Choose them for the vessels that receive blood leaving the capillaries.","Correct. Arterioles feed the capillary beds."],"lt":"t-vessel-structure","lid":"W3-360","comp":67,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-types"},{"id":"L03629","dok":2,"q":"Which vessel type directly drains a capillary bed?","a":"Venules.","options":["Venules","Arterioles","Muscular arteries","Large veins"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Venules are the smallest veins, and they collect blood as it leaves a capillary bed. The first ones, the postcapillary venules, have thin, porous walls; they then merge into larger venules and veins. This makes venules the direct drain of every capillary bed.","notes":["Correct. Venules collect blood from the capillaries.","Arterioles feed blood into a capillary bed rather than draining it. Choose them for the vessels that control how much blood enters the capillaries.","Muscular arteries, such as the brachial and femoral arteries, distribute blood to organs well upstream of the capillaries. They carry blood away from the heart, not out of a capillary bed.","Large veins receive blood only after venules have collected it from the capillaries. They are farther downstream on the return trip to the heart."],"lt":"t-vessel-structure","lid":"W3-362","comp":67,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-types"},{"id":"L03630","dok":2,"q":"Which tunic is most involved in vasoconstriction?","a":"Tunica media.","options":["Tunica media","Tunica intima","Tunica externa","Basement membrane"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Vasoconstriction is the narrowing of a vessel when the smooth muscle in its wall contracts. That smooth muscle is in the tunica media, arranged in rings around the vessel, so contracting it squeezes the lumen smaller. Relaxing the same muscle widens the vessel, which is vasodilation.","notes":["Correct. The smooth muscle of the tunica media narrows the vessel.","The tunica intima is a thin lining of endothelium with no smooth muscle layer. It provides the smooth surface for blood flow but does not squeeze the vessel.","The tunica externa is mostly collagen, which cannot contract. It anchors the vessel to the surrounding tissue.","The basement membrane is a thin sheet beneath the endothelium, part of the tunica intima. It has no contractile cells."],"lt":"t-vessel-structure","lid":"W3-363","comp":66,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-tunics"},{"id":"L03631","dok":2,"q":"Which tunic forms the valves found in many veins?","a":"Tunica intima.","options":["Tunica intima","Tunica media","Tunica externa","Vasa vasorum"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Venous valves are flaps of the tunica intima that project into the lumen of many veins, especially in the limbs. Blood moving toward the heart pushes the flaps open, while blood that starts to slip backward fills them and pushes them shut. This keeps low-pressure venous blood moving in one direction.","notes":["Correct. Venous valves are folds of the tunica intima.","The tunica media is the smooth muscle layer that changes vessel diameter. It does not fold inward to form valves.","The tunica externa is the outer collagen layer that anchors the vessel. It lies on the outside of the wall and cannot form flaps inside the lumen.","The vasa vasorum are small vessels in the tunica externa that supply the wall of large vessels. They are blood vessels, not valve flaps."],"lt":"t-vessel-structure","lid":"W3-364","comp":68,"competencyId":"bvn-artery-cap-vein-kinds"},{"id":"L03632","dok":2,"q":"A large vessel must be held firmly in place among the surrounding tissues. Which part of its wall does this?","a":"Tunica externa.","options":["Internal elastic lamina","Tunica externa","Tunica intima","Tunica media"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The tunica externa is the outermost tunic, made mostly of collagen fibers that blend into the connective tissue around the vessel. These fibers tie the vessel to its surroundings and keep it from shifting or overstretching. In large vessels this layer is thick and also carries the vasa vasorum.","notes":["The internal elastic lamina is a sheet of elastic fibers at the boundary between the tunica intima and the tunica media. It marks the inner edge of the media and plays no anchoring role.","Correct. The collagen of the tunica externa anchors the vessel.","The tunica intima is the innermost lining touching the blood. It is on the wrong side of the wall to anchor the vessel to its surroundings.","The tunica media is the smooth muscle and elastic layer that changes vessel diameter and lets the wall stretch and recoil. It sits in the middle of the wall and does not attach to surrounding tissue."],"lt":"t-vessel-structure","lid":"W3-365","comp":66,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-tunics"},{"id":"L03633","dok":2,"q":"A vessel has a thick tunica media and carries blood away from the heart. What type of vessel is it?","a":"An artery.","options":["Venule","Capillary","Vein","Artery"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Two clues point to an artery. Carrying blood away from the heart is the definition of an artery, and a thick tunica media of smooth muscle and elastic fibers is the wall arteries need to handle high pressure. On a slide, this thick media also helps an artery keep its round shape.","notes":["A venule is a small vein that drains a capillary bed, with thin, porous walls. It carries blood toward the heart, not away.","A capillary has no tunica media at all, only the tunica intima. Choose it for a vessel whose wall is a single layer of endothelium.","A vein carries blood toward the heart and has a thin tunica media with a thicker tunica externa. It would be the answer for a thin-walled vessel with a wide lumen and valves.","Correct. A thick media and flow away from the heart mark an artery."],"lt":"t-vessel-structure","lid":"W3-376","comp":67,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-types"},{"id":"L03634","dok":2,"q":"A vessel on a slide has a thin wall, a wide lumen, and valves. What type of vessel is it?","a":"A vein.","options":["Vein","Arteriole","Capillary","Artery"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"A thin wall, a wide lumen, and valves are the signature of a vein. Veins carry blood at low pressure, so they need less muscle in the tunica media, and their valves, flaps of the tunica intima, stop blood from flowing backward. On a slide, a vein often looks collapsed next to its round partner artery.","notes":["Correct. Thin wall, wide lumen, and valves identify a vein.","An arteriole is a tiny artery with one or two layers of smooth muscle that controls flow into capillaries. It has no valves and a narrow lumen.","A capillary is a single layer of endothelium, far smaller than a vein, with no valves. It would be the answer for the exchange vessel.","An artery has a thick wall with a heavy tunica media and a relatively narrow lumen, and it has no valves along its length. Choose it for a thick-walled vessel carrying blood away from the heart."],"lt":"t-vessel-structure","lid":"W3-377","comp":67,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-types"},{"id":"L03635","dok":2,"q":"A vessel wall consists only of simple squamous endothelium on a basement membrane. What type of vessel is it?","a":"Capillary.","options":["Vein","Arteriole","Capillary","Elastic artery"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"A wall made of only simple squamous endothelium on a basement membrane is the tunica intima alone, and the vessel built that way is a capillary. Arteries and veins add a tunica media and a tunica externa. The single thin layer is what lets capillaries exchange materials with the tissues.","notes":["A vein has all three tunics, with a thin media and a thicker externa, plus valves in many limb veins. It would be the answer for a thin-walled vessel with a wide lumen and valves.","An arteriole has endothelium plus one or two layers of smooth muscle in its tunica media. That muscle layer is what lets it control flow into the capillaries.","Correct. A wall of endothelium alone is a capillary.","An elastic artery, such as the aorta, has a thick tunica media full of elastic sheets and a well-developed tunica externa. It is the opposite extreme from a single-layered wall."],"lt":"t-vessel-structure","lid":"W3-378","comp":66,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-tunics"},{"id":"L03637","dok":2,"q":"A student says veins always carry oxygen-poor blood. What actually defines a vessel as an artery or a vein?","a":"The direction it carries blood relative to the heart.","options":["The direction it carries blood relative to the heart","The oxygen content of the blood it carries","The overall thickness of its wall","Whether or not it has valves along its length"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Arteries carry blood away from the heart and veins carry blood toward it, and that direction is the defining rule. Oxygen content does not work as a rule, because the pulmonary arteries carry oxygen-poor blood and the pulmonary veins carry oxygen-rich blood. Wall thickness and valves are typical features but not the definition.","notes":["Correct. Direction relative to the heart defines arteries and veins.","Oxygen content works for most vessels but fails in the pulmonary circuit, where the arteries carry oxygen-poor blood and the veins carry oxygen-rich blood. That exception is why the student's rule is wrong.","Arteries usually have thicker walls than veins, and that helps you tell them apart on a slide. But wall thickness follows from the pressure each carries; it is a feature, not the definition.","Many veins, especially in the limbs, have valves, but not all do, so valves are a feature rather than the definition. Choose valves for a question about how veins prevent backflow."],"lt":"t-vessel-structure","lid":"W3-389","comp":67,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-types"},{"id":"L03639","dok":2,"q":"Why does the vascular sequence place capillaries between arterioles and venules?","a":"Capillaries perform exchange before blood returns through the venous vessels.","options":["Capillaries oxygenate the blood in every tissue","Exchange with tissues must happen before blood returns to the heart","Capillaries add pressure to push blood into the veins","Capillaries filter blood cells out before they reach the veins"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Arterioles deliver blood to the capillaries, and venules carry it away. Placing the capillaries in the middle means blood always passes through the exchange vessels, unloading O2 and nutrients and picking up CO2 and wastes, before it starts back to the heart. Without that middle step, blood would return to the heart without having served the tissues.","notes":["Blood picks up oxygen only in the capillaries of the lungs. In other tissues the capillaries do the reverse, releasing oxygen to the cells.","Correct. Capillaries sit in the middle so exchange happens before blood heads back.","Pressure drops as blood passes through the capillaries; they do not add any. The pressure that moves blood comes from the heart's pumping.","Capillaries do not filter blood cells out; red blood cells pass through them single file and continue into the venules. Removing old blood cells is the job of the spleen and liver."],"lt":"t-vessel-structure","lid":"W3-394","comp":67,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-types"},{"id":"L03640","dok":2,"q":"Why is the tunica intima important in both large vessels and capillaries?","a":"It provides the endothelial surface that blood contacts in every vessel.","options":["It is the endothelial lining blood touches in every vessel","It contains the smooth muscle that controls diameter","It anchors every vessel to the surrounding tissue","It forms the valves found in all arteries"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The tunica intima is the one tunic every vessel has, from the aorta down to the smallest capillary. Its endothelium forms a smooth, continuous lining that blood flows over with little friction, and it is continuous with the endocardium of the heart. In capillaries this same layer is the whole wall, so it is also the surface where exchange happens.","notes":["Correct. Every vessel, large or small, is lined by the endothelium of the intima.","Smooth muscle that controls diameter is in the tunica media, which capillaries lack. It cannot be the reason the intima matters in every vessel.","Anchoring a vessel to surrounding tissue is the job of the tunica externa, which capillaries also lack. Choose the externa for that role in larger vessels.","Valves made from the tunica intima are found in many veins, especially in the limbs, not in arteries. Choose venous valves for a question about stopping backflow in veins."],"lt":"t-vessel-structure","lid":"W3-397","comp":66,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-tunics"},{"id":"L03641","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly matches each tunic to its function?","a":"Intima lines the lumen, media regulates diameter, externa anchors and supports the vessel.","options":["Intima changes diameter, media anchors the vessel, externa lines the lumen","Intima anchors the vessel, media lines the lumen, externa changes diameter","Intima lines the lumen, media changes diameter, externa anchors the vessel","Intima lines the lumen, media anchors the vessel, externa changes diameter"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Each tunic's tissue explains its job. The tunica intima is smooth endothelium, so it lines the lumen and gives blood a low-friction surface. The tunica media is smooth muscle and elastic fibers, so it narrows or widens the vessel and lets it stretch and recoil. The tunica externa is mostly collagen, so it anchors and protects the vessel.","notes":["This shifts every function one layer over. Changing diameter needs smooth muscle, which is in the media, not in the thin endothelium of the intima.","This also scrambles all three. The lining that touches blood has to be the innermost layer, the intima, and anchoring has to be done by the outermost layer, the externa.","Correct. Intima lines, media adjusts diameter, externa anchors.","The intima is matched correctly, but the media and externa are swapped. Collagen in the externa cannot contract to change diameter, and smooth muscle in the media does not anchor the vessel."],"lt":"t-vessel-structure","lid":"W3-400","comp":66,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-tunics"},{"id":"L03642","dok":1,"q":"Which description matches an elastic artery?","a":"One of the largest arteries near the heart, with a tunica media rich in elastic fibers.","options":["A thin-walled vessel with a wide lumen and valves","A large artery near the heart with a media rich in elastic fibers","A microscopic artery with one or two layers of smooth muscle","A medium-sized artery with a media made mostly of smooth muscle"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Elastic arteries are the largest arteries, sitting closest to the heart, such as the aorta, pulmonary trunk, and common carotid arteries. Their tunica media is packed with elastic lamellae (sheets of elastic fibers), so the wall stretches when the ventricles push blood in and recoils between beats. That recoil keeps blood moving forward while the heart relaxes.","notes":["This describes a vein, which carries blood back to the heart under low pressure. Choose it if the question asked how a vein differs from the artery it travels with.","Correct. Elastic arteries like the aorta are large, close to the heart, and full of elastic fibers that let them stretch and recoil.","This describes an arteriole, the smallest artery, which adjusts how much blood enters a capillary bed. It would be the answer for the vessel that regulates flow into capillaries.","This describes a muscular artery, such as the femoral or brachial artery, whose media can hold dozens of smooth muscle layers. Pick it if the question asked which arteries distribute blood to specific organs."],"lt":"t-vessel-types","lid":"W3-401","comp":68,"competencyId":"bvn-artery-cap-vein-kinds"},{"id":"L03643","dok":1,"q":"Which statement describes muscular arteries?","a":"Medium-sized arteries that distribute blood to specific organs.","options":["Low-pressure vessels that carry blood back toward the heart","Medium-sized arteries that distribute blood to specific organs","Very large arteries that stretch and recoil with each heartbeat","Microscopic arteries that open and close individual capillary beds"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Muscular arteries are the medium-sized arteries that carry blood from the elastic arteries to individual organs and body regions. The femoral, axillary, brachial, and radial arteries are examples. Their thick smooth muscle layer lets them hold vascular tone (a steady partial contraction) and adjust their diameter, which is why they are also called distributing arteries.","notes":["This describes veins, which return blood to the heart. Choose it if the question asked which vessels rely on valves and the skeletal muscle pump.","Correct. Muscular arteries such as the brachial and femoral arteries distribute blood to particular organs and regions.","This describes elastic arteries such as the aorta and pulmonary trunk. Pick it if the question asked which arteries keep blood moving between heartbeats by recoiling.","This describes arterioles, the smallest arteries, along with the precapillary sphincters at their ends. They would be the answer for the vessels that control blood flow into a capillary bed."],"lt":"t-vessel-types","lid":"W3-403","comp":68,"competencyId":"bvn-artery-cap-vein-kinds"},{"id":"L03644","dok":1,"q":"Which layer makes up most of the wall of a muscular artery?","a":"The tunica media, made mostly of smooth muscle.","options":["Tunica externa","Tunica media","External elastic lamina","Tunica intima"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The tunica media is the middle layer of a vessel wall, made of smooth muscle and elastic fibers. In a muscular artery this layer can hold up to forty layers of smooth muscle, making it by far the thickest part of the wall. That heavy muscle is what lets these arteries narrow or widen to control how much blood reaches an organ.","notes":["The tunica externa is the outer layer of mostly collagen fibers that anchors the vessel. It would be the answer if the question asked which layer is thickest in a vein.","Correct. The tunica media, packed with smooth muscle, is the dominant layer of a muscular artery.","The external elastic lamina is a thin elastic sheet marking the boundary between the media and the externa, and it is thin in muscular arteries. Choose it if the question asked what separates the middle layer from the outer layer.","The tunica intima is the thin inner lining of simple squamous endothelium on a basement membrane. Pick it if the question asked which layer is the only one a capillary has."],"lt":"t-vessel-types","lid":"W3-404","comp":68,"competencyId":"bvn-artery-cap-vein-kinds"},{"id":"L03645","dok":1,"q":"What is the main job of arterioles?","a":"They regulate how much blood flows into capillary beds.","options":["Stretching and recoiling to push blood between heartbeats","Regulating how much blood enters a capillary bed","Serving as the main site of exchange with tissue cells","Collecting blood as it drains out of a capillary bed"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Arterioles are the smallest arteries, with only one or two layers of smooth muscle in their walls. When that muscle contracts (vasoconstriction) the arteriole narrows and less blood enters the capillary bed downstream; when it relaxes (vasodilation) more blood flows in. Because of this, arterioles are the chief site of resistance in the circulation.","notes":["This is the job of elastic arteries such as the aorta. Pick it if the question asked which vessels cushion each heartbeat and keep blood flowing while the ventricles relax.","Correct. Arterioles set how much blood reaches each capillary bed by narrowing or widening.","This is the job of capillaries, whose walls are only endothelium thin enough for gases and nutrients to cross. Choose it if the question asked where exchange with tissues happens.","This is the job of venules, the smallest veins. They would be the answer if the question asked which vessels receive blood leaving the capillaries."],"lt":"t-vessel-types","lid":"W3-405","comp":67,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-types"},{"id":"L03646","dok":1,"q":"Why is the wall of a capillary only a single layer of cells thick?","a":"So materials can be exchanged quickly between the blood and the tissues.","options":["So the vessel can hold most of the body's blood at rest","So the vessel can stretch and recoil under high pressure","So the vessel can constrict and set resistance to flow","So materials can cross quickly between blood and tissue"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Capillaries are the exchange vessels of the body. Their wall is just the tunica intima, a single layer of endothelial cells on a basement membrane, with no media or externa. The shorter the distance, the faster oxygen, carbon dioxide, nutrients, and wastes can move between the blood and the surrounding cells.","notes":["The veins as a group act as blood reservoirs, holding about sixty percent of the blood at rest because of their wide lumens. This would fit a question about why veins are called capacitance or reservoir vessels.","Stretch and recoil depend on a thick, elastic tunica media, which is the design of elastic arteries like the aorta. Pick that idea if the question asked why the aorta has so many elastic fibers.","Constricting to set resistance requires smooth muscle, which capillaries lack. This describes arterioles, the answer if the question asked which vessels are the chief site of resistance.","Correct. A one-cell-thick wall gives the shortest possible path for exchange."],"lt":"t-vessel-types","lid":"W3-408","comp":66,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-tunics"},{"id":"L03647","dok":1,"q":"Which list names the three types of capillary?","a":"Continuous, fenestrated, and sinusoid.","options":["Elastic, muscular, and arteriole","Continuous, fenestrated, and sinusoid","Postcapillary venule, muscular venule, and vein","Tunica intima, tunica media, and tunica externa"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Capillaries come in three builds that differ in how leaky they are. Continuous capillaries have an unbroken lining, fenestrated capillaries have small pores (fenestrations, from the Latin word for windows), and sinusoids are wide, winding capillaries with large gaps. The type found in a tissue matches what that tissue needs to move in or out of the blood.","notes":["These are the three sizes of artery, from largest to smallest. Pick this list if the question asked how arteries are classified.","Correct. Continuous, fenestrated, and sinusoid are the three capillary types, from tightest to leakiest.","These are the vessels on the venous side, in the order blood reaches them after leaving the capillaries. They would be the answer for the sequence of vessels draining a capillary bed.","These are the three tunics of an artery or vein wall, from inside to outside. Choose this list if the question asked about the layers of a vessel wall."],"lt":"t-vessel-types","lid":"W3-409","comp":68,"competencyId":"bvn-artery-cap-vein-kinds"},{"id":"L03648","dok":1,"q":"Which description fits a continuous capillary?","a":"A capillary with an unbroken endothelial lining.","options":["A wide, winding vessel with large gaps and a patchy basement membrane","A metarteriole end with no smooth muscle that runs straight to a venule","An endothelium perforated by small pores called fenestrations","An unbroken endothelial lining interrupted only by small clefts"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"A continuous capillary has endothelial cells joined edge to edge in an unbroken sheet. The only openings are intercellular clefts, narrow gaps between neighboring cells that let water and small solutes through. This is the most common and the least leaky capillary type, found in muscle, skin, lungs, and the central nervous system.","notes":["This describes a sinusoid, the leakiest capillary type. It would be the answer if the question asked which capillaries let whole cells cross, as in the liver, spleen, and red bone marrow.","This describes a thoroughfare channel, the distal end of a metarteriole that acts as a direct route from arteriole to venule. Choose it if the question asked how blood can pass through a capillary bed without entering the true capillaries.","Pores in the endothelium define a fenestrated capillary. Pick this if the question asked which capillary type is found in the kidney and small intestine.","Correct. Continuous capillaries have a complete lining broken only by intercellular clefts."],"lt":"t-vessel-types","lid":"W3-410","comp":68,"competencyId":"bvn-artery-cap-vein-kinds"},{"id":"L03649","dok":1,"q":"In which group of tissues are continuous capillaries most common?","a":"Skeletal muscle, skin, and lungs.","options":["Skeletal muscle, skin, and lungs","Kidney, small intestine, and endocrine glands","Cartilage, cornea, and covering epithelia","Liver, spleen, and red bone marrow"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Continuous capillaries are the most common type in the body. They are found in skeletal muscle, skin, the lungs, and the central nervous system, where steady, controlled exchange is needed without large leaks. Their unbroken lining lets small molecules and gases cross while keeping larger proteins and cells inside the vessel.","notes":["Correct. Muscle, skin, and lungs are classic locations of continuous capillaries.","These tissues have fenestrated capillaries, whose pores allow rapid filtration or absorption. Pick this group if the question asked where fenestrated capillaries are found.","These tissues are avascular, meaning they have no capillaries at all and get nutrients by diffusion from nearby tissue. Choose this group if the question asked which tissues lack a blood supply.","These organs have sinusoids, the leakiest capillaries, wide enough for whole cells to cross. They would be the answer if the question asked where sinusoids are located."],"lt":"t-vessel-types","lid":"W3-411","comp":68,"competencyId":"bvn-artery-cap-vein-kinds"},{"id":"L03650","dok":1,"q":"What is a fenestrated capillary?","a":"A capillary whose endothelium has small pores called fenestrations.","options":["A direct channel that lets blood skip a capillary bed","A capillary whose endothelium has small pores","A wide, leaky capillary with large gaps between cells","A capillary with an unbroken endothelial lining"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Fenestra is Latin for window, and a fenestrated capillary has small pores through its endothelial cells. These pores let fluid and small solutes pass much faster than through a continuous capillary, while blood cells and most proteins stay inside. Fenestrated capillaries sit where rapid filtration or absorption happens, such as the kidney and small intestine.","notes":["A direct channel that bypasses capillaries is a vascular shunt, not a type of capillary. Choose it if the question asked how blood can skip a capillary bed when exchange is not needed.","Correct. Fenestrated capillaries have small pores, the fenestrations, in their endothelium.","Large gaps describe a sinusoid. It would be the answer if the question asked which capillary lets whole cells cross the wall.","An unbroken lining describes a continuous capillary. Pick it if the question asked about the most common capillary type, found in muscle, skin, and the brain."],"lt":"t-vessel-types","lid":"W3-412","comp":68,"competencyId":"bvn-artery-cap-vein-kinds"},{"id":"L03651","dok":1,"q":"Where are fenestrated capillaries found?","a":"The kidney and the small intestine.","options":["Brain and skeletal muscle","Liver and spleen","Kidney and small intestine","Cartilage and cornea"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Fenestrated capillaries are placed where fluid must move quickly across the vessel wall. In the kidney they allow rapid filtration of plasma, and in the small intestine they allow absorbed nutrients to enter the blood quickly. They are also found in the choroid plexus of the brain ventricles and in most endocrine glands.","notes":["The brain and skeletal muscle have continuous capillaries with an unbroken lining. This pair would be the answer if the question asked where the tightest capillaries are found.","The liver and spleen have sinusoids, wider and leakier than fenestrated capillaries. Pick this pair if the question asked where whole cells cross capillary walls.","Correct. The kidney and small intestine both rely on fenestrated capillaries for rapid filtration and absorption.","Cartilage and the cornea are avascular and have no capillaries at all. Choose this pair if the question asked which tissues lack blood vessels."],"lt":"t-vessel-types","lid":"W3-413","comp":68,"competencyId":"bvn-artery-cap-vein-kinds"},{"id":"L03652","dok":1,"q":"Which description matches a sinusoid?","a":"A wide, winding, leaky capillary with large gaps between cells.","options":["A capillary with small pores in an otherwise intact endothelium","A capillary with an unbroken lining joined by narrow clefts","A thin-walled vein with no smooth muscle, held open by connective tissue","A wide, winding capillary with large gaps and a patchy basement membrane"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"A sinusoid is the leakiest kind of capillary. It is wide and winding, with large fenestrations, wide clefts between cells, and an incomplete basement membrane. These openings are big enough for large proteins and even whole blood cells to pass, which suits organs like the liver, spleen, and red bone marrow.","notes":["Small pores in an intact lining describe a fenestrated capillary. Pick it if the question asked which capillary type allows rapid filtration in the kidney.","An unbroken lining with narrow clefts describes a continuous capillary. It would be the answer if the question asked about capillaries in skin, muscle, or the brain.","This describes a vascular sinus, such as the dural venous sinuses or the coronary sinus. The names sound alike, but a vascular sinus is a vein, not a capillary. Choose it if the question asked about a vein supported by dense connective tissue.","Correct. Sinusoids are wide, winding capillaries with large gaps and an incomplete basement membrane."],"lt":"t-vessel-types","lid":"W3-414","comp":68,"competencyId":"bvn-artery-cap-vein-kinds"},{"id":"L03653","dok":1,"q":"In which group of organs are sinusoids found?","a":"The liver, spleen, and red bone marrow.","options":["Skin, skeletal muscle, and lungs","Liver, spleen, and red bone marrow","Cartilage, cornea, and lens","Kidney, small intestine, and choroid plexus"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Sinusoids are found where large materials or whole cells must cross the vessel wall. In red bone marrow newly made blood cells enter the bloodstream, in the spleen blood cells move between the blood and the splenic tissue, and in the liver plasma proteins and other large molecules pass freely. Sinusoids also occur in the anterior pituitary and adrenal glands.","notes":["These tissues have continuous capillaries, the tightest and most common type. This group would be the answer for where continuous capillaries are found.","Correct. The liver, spleen, and red bone marrow all have sinusoids.","These tissues are avascular and have no capillaries. Choose this group if the question asked which tissues get nutrients only by diffusion.","These sites have fenestrated capillaries, built for rapid filtration and absorption. Pick this group if the question asked where fenestrated capillaries are found."],"lt":"t-vessel-types","lid":"W3-415","comp":68,"competencyId":"bvn-artery-cap-vein-kinds"},{"id":"L03654","dok":1,"q":"What is a vascular shunt?","a":"A direct channel that lets blood bypass the capillaries of a bed.","options":["An arrangement that routes blood through two capillary beds in a row","A direct channel that lets blood bypass the capillaries of a bed","A network of capillaries supplied by a single metarteriole","A ring of smooth muscle that opens or closes a capillary entrance"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"A vascular shunt is a direct channel connecting the arteriole side of a capillary bed to the venule side. When exchange is not needed, blood can travel through the shunt and skip the true capillaries. This lets the body send blood past a tissue that does not need it at the moment.","notes":["This describes a portal system, such as the hepatic portal system. Choose it if the question asked about blood passing through two capillary beds before returning to the heart.","Correct. A vascular shunt lets blood travel past a capillary bed without passing through its capillaries.","This describes a capillary bed, the network of ten to one hundred capillaries fed by one metarteriole. It would be the answer if the question asked what a metarteriole supplies.","This describes a precapillary sphincter, the ring of smooth muscle that controls whether blood enters a capillary. Pick it if the question asked what opens or closes the entrance to an individual capillary."],"lt":"t-vessel-types","lid":"W3-418","comp":67,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-types"},{"id":"L03655","dok":1,"q":"What is a venule?","a":"The smallest vein, collecting blood as it leaves a capillary bed.","options":["The smallest artery, controlling flow into a capillary bed","The smallest vein, collecting blood as it leaves a capillary bed","The tapered end of an arteriole at the edge of a capillary bed","The exchange vessel whose wall is only endothelium"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Venules are the smallest veins. The postcapillary venule receives blood straight from the capillaries, and it is loosely joined and very porous, which makes it the site where white blood cells leave the bloodstream. Venules then merge into larger veins that carry blood back toward the heart.","notes":["The smallest artery is the arteriole. Pick it if the question asked which vessel regulates how much blood enters a capillary bed.","Correct. A venule is the smallest vein, draining blood from a capillary bed.","This describes a metarteriole, the tapered end of an arteriole where it meets the capillary bed. It would be the answer if the question asked which vessel directly supplies a capillary bed.","This describes a capillary, whose wall is only the tunica intima. Choose it if the question asked which vessel is the main site of exchange."],"lt":"t-vessel-types","lid":"W3-419","comp":67,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-types"},{"id":"L03656","dok":1,"q":"Compared with the artery it travels beside, how is a vein built?","a":"Thinner wall, wider lumen, and valves.","options":["Thinner wall, wider lumen, and valves","Only a tunica intima, with no media or externa","Thinner wall, narrower lumen, and no valves","Thicker wall, narrower lumen, and no valves"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"A vein has the same three tunics as an artery, but in different proportions. Because it carries blood under low pressure, its tunica media is thin, its tunica externa is the thickest layer, and its lumen is wide. Many veins, especially in the limbs, also have valves that keep blood from flowing backward. On a slide the vein often looks collapsed next to its round artery.","notes":["Correct. Veins have thinner walls, wider lumens, and valves compared with their matching arteries.","A wall of only tunica intima describes a capillary. Choose it if the question asked which vessel lacks a tunica media and tunica externa.","This mixes the two vessels. A vein does have a thinner wall, but its lumen is wider than the artery's, not narrower, and many veins have valves.","This is the artery side of the comparison, built thick to handle high pressure. Pick it if the question asked how an artery differs from its matching vein."],"lt":"t-vessel-types","lid":"W3-420","comp":67,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-types"},{"id":"L03657","dok":1,"q":"What are venous valves?","a":"Flaps of the tunica intima that keep blood from flowing backward.","options":["Cusps anchored by chordae tendineae inside the heart","Folds of the tunica intima that keep blood from flowing backward","Rings of smooth muscle that open and close capillary entrances","Bands of elastic fibers that help the vein wall recoil"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Venous valves are small flaps formed from the tunica intima, the inner lining of the vein. They point toward the heart, so blood can push them open while moving forward, but backflow catches them and closes them. This matters because blood in veins is under low pressure and, in the limbs, must often travel upward against gravity.","notes":["These are the atrioventricular valves of the heart, the tricuspid and mitral valves. They would be the answer if the question asked which heart valves are held by chordae tendineae.","Correct. Venous valves are flaps of the tunica intima that block backward flow.","These are precapillary sphincters, found at the entrance to capillaries on the arterial side. Pick them if the question asked what controls blood entering an individual capillary.","Veins do not depend on elastic recoil, and valves are not elastic bands. Elastic recoil is the job of the elastic arteries, the answer if the question asked how the aorta keeps blood moving between beats."],"lt":"t-vessel-types","lid":"W3-421","comp":68,"competencyId":"bvn-artery-cap-vein-kinds"},{"id":"L03658","dok":1,"q":"In which vessels are valves most common?","a":"The veins of the limbs.","options":["Arterioles","Elastic arteries","Capillary beds","Veins of the limbs"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Valves are a feature of veins, and they are most common in the veins of the arms and especially the legs. Blood in these veins is under low pressure and must return to the heart against gravity. The valves, together with the squeezing of nearby skeletal muscles, keep that blood moving toward the heart instead of sliding back down.","notes":["Arterioles carry blood toward capillaries under enough pressure that backflow is not a problem, so they lack valves. They would be the answer for the vessels that set resistance to flow.","Elastic arteries such as the aorta have no valves along their length; the aortic and pulmonary valves belong to the heart. Elastic arteries would be the answer if the question asked which vessels stretch and recoil with each heartbeat.","Capillaries have only an endothelial wall and no valves. Pick them if the question asked where exchange between blood and tissue happens.","Correct. Limb veins carry low-pressure blood against gravity, so they have many valves."],"lt":"t-vessel-types","lid":"W3-422","comp":68,"competencyId":"bvn-artery-cap-vein-kinds"},{"id":"L03659","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares elastic and muscular arteries?","a":"Elastic arteries stretch and recoil near the heart; muscular arteries distribute blood to organs.","options":["Elastic arteries have more layers of smooth muscle in their media than muscular arteries do","Elastic arteries regulate flow into capillary beds, while muscular arteries collect blood from them","Muscular arteries stretch and recoil near the heart, while elastic arteries distribute blood to organs","Elastic arteries stretch and recoil near the heart, while muscular arteries distribute blood to organs"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Elastic arteries, like the aorta and common carotid, are the large vessels closest to the heart. Their elastic-rich media stretches with each heartbeat and recoils between beats, keeping blood moving. Muscular arteries, like the brachial and femoral, come next and deliver blood to specific organs, using their thick smooth muscle to adjust diameter.","notes":["It is the reverse. Muscular arteries have the most smooth muscle, up to about forty layers, while elastic arteries are dominated by elastic lamellae.","Regulating flow into capillary beds is the job of arterioles, and collecting blood from capillaries is the job of venules. Pick those vessels if the question asked about the two ends of a capillary bed.","This swaps the two types. It is a common mix-up because both are arteries, but the elastic fibers are what let the aorta stretch and recoil, not smooth muscle.","Correct. Elastic arteries cushion and propel blood near the heart, and muscular arteries distribute it to organs."],"lt":"t-vessel-types","lid":"W3-431","comp":68,"competencyId":"bvn-artery-cap-vein-kinds"},{"id":"L03660","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares continuous and fenestrated capillaries?","a":"Continuous capillaries have an unbroken lining; fenestrated capillaries have small pores.","options":["Continuous capillaries have an unbroken lining, while fenestrated capillaries have small pores","Both types are leaky enough to let whole blood cells cross the wall","Fenestrated capillaries have an unbroken lining, while continuous capillaries have small pores","Continuous capillaries have large gaps for cells, while fenestrated capillaries have none"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"A continuous capillary has an unbroken endothelial lining with only narrow clefts between cells, so exchange is controlled and slow. A fenestrated capillary has small pores through its endothelial cells, which lets fluid and small solutes move across much faster. That is why continuous capillaries suit muscle and skin, while fenestrated capillaries suit the kidney and small intestine.","notes":["Correct. The unbroken lining versus the small pores is the defining difference.","Neither type lets whole blood cells cross. Only sinusoids, found in the liver, spleen, and red bone marrow, have gaps that large.","This swaps the two. The word fenestrated means windowed, which is the clue that those capillaries are the ones with pores.","Large gaps that let cells pass belong to sinusoids, not continuous capillaries. Pick a statement like this if it compared sinusoids with tighter capillaries."],"lt":"t-vessel-types","lid":"W3-438","comp":68,"competencyId":"bvn-artery-cap-vein-kinds"},{"id":"L03661","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares fenestrated capillaries and sinusoids?","a":"Fenestrated capillaries have small pores; sinusoids have large gaps and are leakier.","options":["Sinusoids have small pores, while fenestrated capillaries have large gaps and are leakier","Both have a complete basement membrane that keeps whole cells inside","Sinusoids have an unbroken lining, while fenestrated capillaries have small pores","Fenestrated capillaries have small pores, while sinusoids have large gaps and are leakier"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Both fenestrated capillaries and sinusoids have openings in their walls, but the size differs. Fenestrated capillaries have small pores that let fluid and small solutes through. Sinusoids are wide and winding, with large gaps and an incomplete basement membrane, so they let large proteins and even whole cells cross. Sinusoids are therefore the leakiest capillary type.","notes":["This swaps the two. Sinusoids are the leakiest capillaries, which is why the liver, spleen, and bone marrow use them.","Sinusoids have an incomplete basement membrane, which is part of what lets whole cells pass. A complete basement membrane fits continuous and fenestrated capillaries only.","An unbroken lining describes a continuous capillary, not a sinusoid. Pick a statement like this if it compared continuous and fenestrated capillaries.","Correct. Small pores in fenestrated capillaries, large gaps in sinusoids."],"lt":"t-vessel-types","lid":"W3-439","comp":68,"competencyId":"bvn-artery-cap-vein-kinds"},{"id":"L03662","dok":2,"q":"Which order ranks the capillary types from least leaky to most leaky?","a":"Continuous, then fenestrated, then sinusoid.","options":["Sinusoid, fenestrated, continuous","Fenestrated, continuous, sinusoid","Continuous, sinusoid, fenestrated","Continuous, fenestrated, sinusoid"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Continuous capillaries have an unbroken lining and are the tightest. Fenestrated capillaries add small pores, so they are leakier and allow rapid filtration. Sinusoids have large gaps and an incomplete basement membrane, so they are the leakiest and can pass whole cells. Leakiness rises as you move from tissues that need a tight barrier to tissues where cells must cross.","notes":["This is the right sequence read backward, from most leaky to least leaky. It would be the answer if the question asked for the order from leakiest to tightest.","This places fenestrated capillaries as the tightest, but their pores make them leakier than continuous capillaries, which have an unbroken lining.","This puts sinusoids in the middle, but sinusoids are the leakiest type because their gaps are larger than the small pores of fenestrated capillaries.","Correct. Leakiness increases from continuous to fenestrated to sinusoid."],"lt":"t-vessel-types","lid":"W3-440","comp":68,"competencyId":"bvn-artery-cap-vein-kinds"},{"id":"L03663","dok":2,"q":"Why are fenestrated capillaries a good fit for the kidney?","a":"Their pores allow rapid filtration of fluid out of the blood.","options":["Their large gaps let whole blood cells leave the vessel","Their pores let fluid filter out of the blood quickly","Their unbroken lining keeps most substances in the blood","Their thick smooth muscle wall withstands arterial pressure"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The kidney filters a large volume of plasma every day to begin forming urine. Fenestrated capillaries have small pores that let water and small solutes leave the blood quickly, while blood cells and most proteins stay inside. That combination of speed and selectivity is exactly what filtration requires.","notes":["Large gaps that let whole cells leave describe sinusoids. Pick this if the question asked why the liver, spleen, or red bone marrow uses sinusoids.","Correct. Small pores make rapid filtration possible while keeping cells in the blood.","An unbroken lining describes continuous capillaries, which limit leakage. This would be the answer if the question asked why the brain or skeletal muscle uses continuous capillaries.","Capillaries have no smooth muscle at all; their wall is only endothelium. A thick muscular wall describes muscular arteries, the answer if the question asked which vessels distribute blood to organs."],"lt":"t-vessel-types","lid":"W3-441","comp":68,"competencyId":"bvn-artery-cap-vein-kinds"},{"id":"L03664","dok":2,"q":"Why are fenestrated capillaries a good fit for the small intestine?","a":"Their pores allow absorbed nutrients to enter the blood quickly.","options":["Their pores let absorbed nutrients enter the blood quickly","Their valves keep absorbed nutrients from flowing backward","Their wide gaps let blood cells pass into the gut wall","Their tight, unbroken lining blocks absorbed nutrients"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"After a meal, the small intestine absorbs large amounts of digested nutrients and water. The capillaries in its villi are fenestrated, so their small pores let these absorbed materials move into the blood rapidly. This is the same design the kidney uses for filtration, simply working in the opposite direction.","notes":["Correct. The pores of fenestrated capillaries let absorbed nutrients reach the blood quickly.","Capillaries do not have valves. Valves are found in veins, especially limb veins, and would be the answer if the question asked what prevents backflow in the legs.","Gaps wide enough for blood cells describe sinusoids. Pick this if the question asked about capillaries in the spleen or red bone marrow.","A tight, unbroken lining describes continuous capillaries, which would slow absorption. This fits a question about why brain capillaries keep most substances out."],"lt":"t-vessel-types","lid":"W3-442","comp":68,"competencyId":"bvn-artery-cap-vein-kinds"},{"id":"L03665","dok":2,"q":"Why are sinusoids useful in the liver, spleen, and red bone marrow?","a":"Their large gaps let large substances and even whole cells cross the wall.","options":["Their thick tunica media lets them handle arterial pressure","Their unbroken lining keeps most substances from leaving","Their large gaps let big molecules and whole cells cross the wall","Their small pores allow rapid filtration of fluid only"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Each of these organs moves large materials between blood and tissue. Red bone marrow releases newly made blood cells, the spleen handles blood cells moving in and out of its tissue, and the liver exchanges large proteins with the blood. Sinusoids have large gaps and an incomplete basement membrane, so these large materials can pass easily.","notes":["Capillaries, including sinusoids, have no tunica media. A thick media describes arteries, the answer if the question asked which vessels carry blood under high pressure.","An unbroken lining describes continuous capillaries. This would be the answer if the question asked why muscle and skin capillaries limit leakage.","Correct. Large gaps make sinusoids the right capillary where cells and big molecules must cross.","Small pores that filter fluid describe fenestrated capillaries. Pick this if the question asked why the kidney or small intestine uses fenestrated capillaries."],"lt":"t-vessel-types","lid":"W3-443","comp":68,"competencyId":"bvn-artery-cap-vein-kinds"},{"id":"L03666","dok":2,"q":"Why are continuous capillaries the type found in skeletal muscle and skin?","a":"They allow controlled exchange while limiting leakage.","options":["They allow steady exchange while limiting leakage","They let blood bypass the tissue when exchange is not needed","They filter large volumes of fluid out of the blood quickly","They let whole blood cells move freely into the tissue"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Muscle and skin need a steady supply of oxygen and nutrients, but they do not need to filter large volumes of fluid or move cells across the vessel wall. Continuous capillaries have an unbroken lining with only narrow clefts, so gases and small molecules cross while proteins and cells stay inside. This keeps exchange controlled and prevents unnecessary fluid loss into the tissue.","notes":["Correct. Continuous capillaries give controlled exchange with little leakage.","Bypassing a tissue is the job of a vascular shunt, not a type of capillary. Choose it if the question asked how blood skips a capillary bed.","Rapid filtration of large fluid volumes is the job of fenestrated capillaries. This would be the answer if the question asked about capillaries in the kidney.","Letting whole cells cross is the job of sinusoids. Pick this if the question asked why red bone marrow or the spleen uses sinusoids."],"lt":"t-vessel-types","lid":"W3-444","comp":68,"competencyId":"bvn-artery-cap-vein-kinds"},{"id":"L03667","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares capillaries and venules?","a":"Capillaries are the exchange vessels; venules collect blood draining from capillary beds.","options":["Capillaries regulate flow into a bed, while venules carry blood under high pressure","Both have a tunica media of several smooth muscle layers","Capillaries are where exchange happens, while venules collect blood draining from capillary beds","Venules are where exchange happens, while capillaries collect blood draining from venules"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Capillaries connect arterioles to venules and are the main exchange vessels, with walls only one cell thick. Venules are the smallest veins and come right after the capillaries, collecting the blood that leaves a capillary bed. Postcapillary venules are porous and are where white blood cells leave the bloodstream, but the main job of exchange belongs to capillaries.","notes":["Regulating flow into a capillary bed is the job of arterioles and precapillary sphincters, and veins and venules carry blood under low pressure, not high. Pick arterioles if the question asked what controls entry into a bed.","Capillaries have no tunica media, and venules have at most one or two layers of smooth muscle. Several layers of smooth muscle would describe arteries.","Correct. Exchange happens in capillaries, and venules drain them.","This reverses the sequence. Blood flows from capillaries into venules, not the other way around."],"lt":"t-vessel-types","lid":"W3-447","comp":67,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-types"},{"id":"L03668","dok":2,"q":"A superficial vein on a patient's forearm shows a row of small bumps along its length. What do the bumps most likely mark?","a":"Venous valves.","options":["Lymph nodes","Arteriovenous anastomoses","Precapillary sphincters","Venous valves"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Veins in the limbs have valves, flaps of the tunica intima that keep blood flowing toward the heart. Blood collects slightly just behind each valve, so in a superficial vein the valves can show up as small bumps along the vessel. Phlebotomists notice these when choosing where to draw blood.","notes":["Lymph nodes are bean-shaped organs that filter lymph and cluster in places like the axilla and groin. They are not strung along a vein, and they would be the answer if the question asked where lymph is filtered.","Arteriovenous anastomoses are direct artery-to-vein connections that bypass capillaries, mostly in places like the skin of the fingers. They would be the answer if the question asked how blood can skip a capillary bed.","Precapillary sphincters are microscopic rings of smooth muscle at the entrance to capillaries. They cannot be seen through the skin, and they would be the answer if the question asked what opens or closes a capillary.","Correct. The small bumps along a superficial vein mark the positions of its valves."],"lt":"t-vessel-types","lid":"W3-460","comp":68,"competencyId":"bvn-artery-cap-vein-kinds"},{"id":"L03669","dok":2,"q":"A tissue must move fluid out of the blood quickly but should keep blood cells inside the vessel. Which capillary type fits best?","a":"Fenestrated capillary.","options":["Continuous capillary","Sinusoid","Fenestrated capillary","Arteriole"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Fenestrated capillaries have small pores in their endothelium. These pores let water and small solutes leave the blood rapidly, but they are too small for blood cells to pass. That is the combination this tissue needs, and it is why fenestrated capillaries are found in the kidney, small intestine, and choroid plexus.","notes":["Continuous capillaries have an unbroken lining that limits leakage, so fluid would move too slowly. Pick them if the tissue needed a tight barrier, as in the brain.","Sinusoids are leaky enough to let whole cells pass, which this tissue wants to prevent. They would be the answer if the tissue needed cells to cross, as in red bone marrow.","Correct. Fenestrated capillaries allow rapid fluid movement while keeping cells inside.","An arteriole is a small artery with smooth muscle, not an exchange vessel. It would be the answer if the question asked which vessel controls how much blood reaches the capillaries."],"lt":"t-vessel-types","lid":"W3-467","comp":68,"competencyId":"bvn-artery-cap-vein-kinds"},{"id":"L03670","dok":2,"q":"In red bone marrow, newly made blood cells must pass through the vessel wall into the bloodstream. Which capillary type fits this job?","a":"Sinusoid.","options":["Postcapillary venule","Fenestrated capillary","Sinusoid","Continuous capillary"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Whole blood cells are far too large to fit through the small pores of a fenestrated capillary or the narrow clefts of a continuous capillary. Sinusoids are wide and winding, with large gaps between cells and an incomplete basement membrane. Those openings are big enough for new blood cells to squeeze into the bloodstream, which is why red bone marrow, the spleen, and the liver use sinusoids.","notes":["Postcapillary venules are where white blood cells leave the blood to enter tissues, which is the opposite direction from this question. They would be the answer if the question asked where leukocytes exit the bloodstream.","Fenestrated capillaries have small pores that pass fluid and small solutes but not cells. Pick them if the tissue needed rapid filtration, as in the kidney.","Correct. Only sinusoids have gaps large enough for whole cells to pass.","Continuous capillaries have an unbroken lining and are the tightest type. They would be the answer if the tissue needed to keep most substances out, as in the brain."],"lt":"t-vessel-types","lid":"W3-468","comp":68,"competencyId":"bvn-artery-cap-vein-kinds"},{"id":"L03671","dok":2,"q":"A tissue needs to keep most substances inside the blood and allow only controlled exchange. Which capillary type fits best?","a":"Continuous capillary.","options":["Fenestrated capillary","Continuous capillary","Sinusoid","Thoroughfare channel"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Continuous capillaries have an unbroken endothelial lining, broken only by narrow intercellular clefts. Gases and small molecules can cross, but proteins and cells stay in the blood. This tight, controlled barrier suits skin, skeletal muscle, the lungs, and especially the central nervous system.","notes":["Fenestrated capillaries have pores that let fluid move quickly, so they are leakier than this tissue wants. Pick them if the tissue needed rapid filtration or absorption.","Correct. Continuous capillaries form the tightest exchange barrier.","Sinusoids are the leakiest capillaries, wide enough for whole cells. They would be the answer if large materials needed to cross, as in the liver.","A thoroughfare channel is the distal end of a metarteriole that carries blood straight from arteriole to venule, not a barrier type. Choose it if the question asked about a direct route through a capillary bed."],"lt":"t-vessel-types","lid":"W3-469","comp":68,"competencyId":"bvn-artery-cap-vein-kinds"},{"id":"L03672","dok":2,"q":"Why are the capillaries of the brain built as especially tight continuous capillaries?","a":"To keep most substances in the blood away from delicate neural tissue.","options":["To let blood bypass the brain when it is resting","To keep most blood substances away from neurons","To let new blood cells enter the bloodstream quickly","To filter large volumes of fluid, as the kidney does"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Neurons are very sensitive to changes in their chemical surroundings. Brain capillaries are continuous capillaries with very tight connections between their endothelial cells, which keeps most substances in the blood from reaching the neural tissue. This protective arrangement is called the blood-brain barrier.","notes":["The brain needs a constant blood supply and does not shut it off when resting. Bypassing capillaries is the job of vascular shunts, the answer if the question asked how blood skips a capillary bed.","Correct. Tight brain capillaries protect neural tissue from most substances in the blood.","Releasing new blood cells is the job of sinusoids in red bone marrow. Pick this if the question asked why marrow capillaries are so leaky.","Filtering large volumes of fluid is the job of fenestrated capillaries in the kidney. This would be the answer if the question asked why kidney capillaries have pores."],"lt":"t-vessel-types","lid":"W3-470","comp":68,"competencyId":"bvn-artery-cap-vein-kinds"},{"id":"L03673","dok":2,"q":"Why are the capillaries of the liver built as leaky sinusoids?","a":"The liver processes blood contents, so large materials must pass easily between blood and liver cells.","options":["So blood can skip the liver on its way to the heart","So the liver can keep plasma proteins out of its tissue","So large molecules move freely to and from liver cells","So the liver can filter urine out of the blood"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The liver processes much of what is in the blood, taking up nutrients and other absorbed materials and releasing large plasma proteins it makes. Sinusoids have large gaps and an incomplete basement membrane, so blood comes into close contact with liver cells and large molecules move freely in both directions.","notes":["Skipping the liver happens in the fetus through the ductus venosus, a shunt, not through sinusoids. Choose that idea if the question asked how fetal blood bypasses the liver.","The liver actually releases plasma proteins into the blood, so it needs leaky capillaries, not a barrier. Keeping substances out is the role of tight continuous capillaries, as in the brain.","Correct. Leaky sinusoids let large materials move freely between blood and liver cells.","Filtering urine from the blood is the job of the kidney, which uses fenestrated capillaries. Pick the kidney if the question asked where blood is filtered to start forming urine."],"lt":"t-vessel-types","lid":"W3-471","comp":68,"competencyId":"bvn-artery-cap-vein-kinds"},{"id":"L03674","dok":2,"q":"The precapillary sphincters of a capillary bed close. Where does the blood arriving at that bed go?","a":"Through a vascular shunt to the venule, bypassing the capillaries.","options":["Out through the vessel wall into the tissue","Backward into the arteriole toward the heart","Through the closed capillaries at higher speed","Through a vascular shunt straight to the venule"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Precapillary sphincters are rings of smooth muscle at the entrance to each capillary. When they close, blood cannot enter the true capillaries. Instead it travels through the vascular shunt, the direct channel through the bed (the metarteriole and its thoroughfare channel), and drains into the venule without exchange taking place.","notes":["Blood stays inside the vessels. Movement of fluid out into tissue happens across open capillary walls, the answer if the question asked where exchange occurs.","Blood keeps moving forward, driven by pressure from the arterial side, and does not flow back toward the heart. Backward flow would be a problem in veins with failed valves, the answer if the question asked about varicose veins.","A closed sphincter blocks the capillary, so no blood enters it at all. This would only fit if the sphincters were open.","Correct. With the sphincters closed, blood takes the shunt directly to the venule."],"lt":"t-vessel-types","lid":"W3-472","comp":67,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-types"},{"id":"L03676","dok":3,"q":"One valve in a leg vein no longer closes properly and lets blood flow backward. What happens locally?","a":"Blood pools in the vein segment below the failed valve.","options":["Blood leaks out into the tissue above the valve","The vein wall thickens and narrows its lumen","Blood pools in the vein segment below the failed valve","Blood pools in the artery that supplies the leg"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"In the leg, venous blood is moving upward toward the heart. A working valve catches any blood that slides back. When a valve fails, blood falls back through it and collects in the segment of vein below (farther from the heart), raising pressure there and stretching that part of the vein.","notes":["Blood falls back below the failed valve, not above it, because it is pulled down by gravity. The segment above still has working valves holding blood in place.","Wall thickening and a narrowed lumen describe arteriosclerosis, which affects arteries. Pick that if the question asked about stiff, thickened artery walls.","Correct. Backflow through a failed valve makes blood pool in the segment below it.","Arteries do not depend on venous valves, so the artery supplying the leg is not affected. Pooling in an artery would not fit any common disorder; narrowing of a leg artery is peripheral artery disease."],"lt":"t-vessel-types","lid":"W3-476","comp":68,"competencyId":"bvn-artery-cap-vein-kinds"},{"id":"L03677","dok":2,"q":"Why is a vein more likely than an artery to collapse when it is cut?","a":"Its wall is thinner and the pressure inside it is lower.","options":["Its valves close and pull the wall inward","It has no tunica externa to hold its shape","Its wall has more elastic fibers than an artery wall","Its wall is thinner and the pressure inside it is lower"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Arteries have a thick tunica media of smooth muscle and elastic tissue, so they hold their round shape even when empty. Veins have a thin media and carry blood under low pressure, so there is little to keep the wall open. That is why a vein looks collapsed next to its artery on a slide and why a cut vein flattens.","notes":["Valves are flaps that block backflow; they do not pull the wall inward. Choose valves if the question asked what keeps blood moving toward the heart in the limbs.","Veins do have a tunica externa; in fact it is the thickest layer of a vein. Pick the tunica externa if the question asked which layer is thickest in a vein.","Arteries, not veins, have the most elastic tissue, which helps them keep their shape. More elastic fibers would make a vessel less likely to collapse, which describes the aorta.","Correct. A thin wall and low internal pressure let a vein collapse."],"lt":"t-vessel-types","lid":"W3-477","comp":67,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-types"},{"id":"L03679","dok":2,"q":"A student says that every blood vessel has three tunics. Which correction is accurate?","a":"Arteries and veins have three tunics, but capillaries have only the tunica intima.","options":["Arteries have three tunics, but veins have only the intima and externa","Capillaries have only a tunica externa, which gives them their strength","Arteries and veins have three, but capillaries have only the tunica intima","All vessels, including the smallest capillaries, have the same three tunics"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The walls of arteries and veins are built from three layers: the tunica intima (endothelial lining), the tunica media (smooth muscle and elastic fibers), and the tunica externa (collagen). A capillary wall is only the tunica intima, endothelium on a basement membrane. That single thin layer is what makes capillaries suited for exchange.","notes":["Veins have all three tunics, just in different proportions, with a thin media and a thick externa. Pick a statement about proportions if the question asked how veins differ from arteries.","A capillary's single layer is the tunica intima, not the externa, and capillaries are not built for strength. The tunica externa is the outer collagen layer that anchors larger vessels.","Correct. Capillaries are the exception, with only the tunica intima.","This repeats the student's error. If capillaries had a media and externa, their walls would be too thick for efficient exchange."],"lt":"t-vessel-types","lid":"W3-486","comp":66,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-tunics"},{"id":"L03680","dok":2,"q":"A student says capillaries are strong, high-pressure vessels. Which correction is accurate?","a":"Capillaries are thin exchange vessels; arteries are the high-pressure vessels.","options":["Capillaries are the strongest vessels because blood moves slowly in them","Capillaries have the thickest tunica media of any vessel","Veins, not arteries, carry blood at the highest pressure","Capillaries are thin exchange vessels, and arteries carry the high pressure"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Capillaries have walls only one endothelial cell thick, built for exchange rather than strength. By the time blood reaches them, the arterioles upstream have already lowered its pressure. The high-pressure vessels are the arteries, whose thick tunica media of smooth muscle and elastic fibers can withstand the force of each heartbeat.","notes":["Slow flow does not make a vessel strong. Capillaries are the thinnest vessels, and the slow flow simply gives more time for exchange.","Capillaries have no tunica media at all. The thickest media belongs to arteries, the answer if the question asked which vessels resist high pressure.","Veins carry blood under the lowest pressure, which is why they need valves. Pick veins if the question asked which vessels rely on valves and the skeletal muscle pump.","Correct. Capillaries are thin exchange vessels, and the arteries handle high pressure."],"lt":"t-vessel-types","lid":"W3-487","comp":67,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-types"},{"id":"L03681","dok":2,"q":"A student says veins push blood back to the heart with thick muscular walls. Which correction is accurate?","a":"Veins have thin walls and rely on valves and the skeletal muscle pump.","options":["Veins rely on elastic recoil, as the aorta does","Veins have the thickest tunica media of any vessel","Veins are thin-walled and rely on valves and muscle pumps","Veins rely on precapillary sphincters to push blood forward"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Veins carry blood under low pressure and have a thin tunica media, so they cannot push blood on their own. Instead, contracting skeletal muscles squeeze the deep veins (the skeletal muscle pump), and valves make sure the squeezed blood can only move toward the heart. Together these keep venous return going, especially from the legs.","notes":["Elastic recoil is how elastic arteries like the aorta keep blood moving between heartbeats. It would be the answer if the question asked about the aorta.","Arteries have the thickest tunica media, not veins. Pick arteries if the question asked which vessels have the most smooth muscle.","Correct. Thin-walled veins depend on valves and the skeletal muscle pump.","Precapillary sphincters control entry into capillaries on the arterial side and do not push blood. Choose them if the question asked what opens or closes a capillary."],"lt":"t-vessel-types","lid":"W3-488","comp":67,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-types"},{"id":"L03682","dok":2,"q":"A student says arterioles mainly collect blood leaving capillaries. Which correction is accurate?","a":"Arterioles deliver blood to capillary beds and regulate how much enters.","options":["Arterioles return blood to the heart under low pressure","Arterioles are the exchange vessels where gases cross the wall","Arterioles feed capillary beds and regulate how much enters","Arterioles stretch and recoil to cushion each heartbeat"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Arterioles are on the arterial side of the capillary bed. They are the smallest arteries, and their one or two layers of smooth muscle narrow or widen to control how much blood reaches the capillaries. The vessels that collect blood leaving capillaries are the venules, on the other side of the bed.","notes":["Returning blood to the heart under low pressure is the job of veins. They would be the answer if the question asked which vessels have valves and wide lumens.","Exchange happens in capillaries, whose walls are only endothelium. Pick capillaries if the question asked where gases and nutrients cross into tissue.","Correct. Arterioles feed capillary beds and regulate the flow into them.","Stretching and recoiling is the job of elastic arteries like the aorta. Choose them if the question asked which vessels keep blood moving between heartbeats."],"lt":"t-vessel-types","lid":"W3-489","comp":67,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-types"},{"id":"L03683","dok":2,"q":"A student says venules regulate how much blood enters a capillary bed. Which correction is accurate?","a":"Venules collect blood as it leaves capillary beds.","options":["Venules stretch and recoil to propel blood forward","Venules carry blood away from the heart into capillaries","Venules are the main vessels where exchange happens","Venules collect blood as it leaves capillary beds"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Venules are the smallest veins, located on the outflow side of the capillary bed. They receive blood after it has passed through the capillaries and carry it on toward larger veins. Entry into the bed is controlled upstream by arterioles and precapillary sphincters.","notes":["Stretch and recoil is the job of elastic arteries like the aorta. Pick them if the question asked how blood keeps moving between heartbeats.","Carrying blood away from the heart into capillaries is the job of arterioles. They would be the answer if the question asked which vessels feed a capillary bed.","Capillaries are the main exchange vessels, although postcapillary venules are porous and are where white blood cells leave the blood. Choose capillaries if the question asked where most exchange happens.","Correct. Venules drain capillary beds; they do not control entry into them."],"lt":"t-vessel-types","lid":"W3-490","comp":67,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-types"},{"id":"L03684","dok":2,"q":"Capillaries in the brain, kidney, and liver are all called capillaries, yet they differ greatly in how leaky they are. What explains this?","a":"Capillaries come in three structural types, each matched to the tissue's job.","options":["Capillaries in different organs have different numbers of tunics","Leakier capillaries are older capillaries that have worn thin","Capillaries come in three structural types matched to each tissue's job","Leakiness depends only on how hard the heart is pumping"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"All capillaries have a wall of endothelium on a basement membrane, but that wall is built three different ways. Continuous capillaries in the brain are tight, fenestrated capillaries in the kidney have small pores for filtration, and sinusoids in the liver have large gaps for large molecules and cells. The structure follows what each tissue needs to move across the wall.","notes":["Every capillary has only one tunic, the tunica intima. The number of tunics distinguishes capillaries from arteries and veins, not one capillary from another.","Leakiness is a built-in design feature, not a sign of wear. Sinusoids are leaky in healthy young organs because the tissue needs large materials to cross.","Correct. Continuous, fenestrated, and sinusoid capillaries are built differently for different jobs.","Pumping force affects blood pressure, but it does not explain why brain capillaries stay tight while liver capillaries stay leaky. The difference is in the wall structure itself."],"lt":"t-vessel-types","lid":"W3-494","comp":68,"competencyId":"bvn-artery-cap-vein-kinds"},{"id":"L03685","dok":2,"q":"Why are venous valves especially important in veins below the level of the heart?","a":"They oppose gravity and prevent blood from flowing backward.","options":["Veins below the heart carry oxygen-rich blood that must not mix with arterial blood","Blood must rise against gravity at low pressure, and valves stop it from sliding back","Arteries below the heart carry blood at such high pressure that veins must resist it","Capillaries below the heart lack endothelium, so valves replace the lining"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Blood in the veins of the legs and lower body is under low pressure and has to travel upward to reach the heart. Without help, gravity would pull it back down. Venous valves let blood move only toward the heart, and each squeeze from nearby skeletal muscles pushes blood up past the next valve.","notes":["Systemic veins below the heart carry oxygen-poor blood, not oxygen-rich blood. Oxygen-rich venous blood is found in the pulmonary veins and, before birth, the umbilical vein.","Correct. Valves keep low-pressure blood from falling back against gravity.","Venous valves have nothing to do with arterial pressure. High arterial pressure is the reason arteries have thick walls, the answer if the question asked why arteries have a heavy tunica media.","All capillaries have an endothelial lining, and capillaries have no valves. The endothelium would be the answer if the question asked what forms the entire capillary wall."],"lt":"t-vessel-types","lid":"W3-497","comp":68,"competencyId":"bvn-artery-cap-vein-kinds"},{"id":"L03686","dok":2,"q":"How does vessel wall structure match the pressure each vessel carries?","a":"Arteries have a thick media for high pressure; veins have thinner walls for low pressure.","options":["Arteries and veins have identical walls, and only valves differ","Capillaries have the thickest walls because they hold the highest pressure","Veins have a thick media for high pressure, while arteries have thin walls for low pressure","Arteries have a thick media for high pressure, while veins have thin walls for low pressure"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Blood leaves the heart under high pressure, so arteries have a thick tunica media of smooth muscle and elastic fibers that can withstand and smooth out each heartbeat. By the time blood reaches the veins, its pressure is low, so veins have thinner walls, a thin media, and a wide lumen. Veins make up for low pressure with valves and the skeletal muscle pump.","notes":["Arteries and veins have the same three tunics but in very different proportions. The difference in tunica media thickness is the main way to tell them apart on a slide.","Capillaries have the thinnest walls of all, only endothelium, and they do not hold the highest pressure. Pick capillaries if the question asked where exchange happens.","This swaps arteries and veins. The thick media belongs to arteries because they receive blood directly from the heart.","Correct. Thick arterial walls handle high pressure, and thin venous walls carry low-pressure blood."],"lt":"t-vessel-types","lid":"W3-498","comp":67,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-types"},{"id":"N-m3-vessel-wall-1-chunk26","dok":1,"q":"What are the vasa vasorum?","a":"Small vessels in the tunica externa that supply the wall of a large vessel.","options":["Elastic sheets at the border of the tunica intima and media","Flaps of the tunica intima that block backward flow","Rings of smooth muscle at the entrance to capillaries","Small vessels in the externa that supply a large vessel's wall"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Vasa vasorum means vessels of the vessels. The wall of a large artery or vein, such as the aorta, is too thick to be nourished only by the blood flowing through its lumen. Tiny vessels running in the tunica externa supply the outer parts of the wall, and they are easy to see on a section of the aorta.","notes":["This describes the internal elastic lamina. Pick it if the question asked what marks the boundary between the tunica intima and the tunica media.","This describes venous valves. Choose them if the question asked what keeps blood moving toward the heart in limb veins.","This describes precapillary sphincters. They would be the answer if the question asked what opens or closes the entrance to a capillary.","Correct. The vasa vasorum are small vessels in the tunica externa that nourish a large vessel's wall."],"comp":66,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-tunics"},{"id":"N-m3-vessel-wall-2-chunk26","dok":2,"q":"Which pair are examples of vascular sinuses?","a":"The dural venous sinuses and the coronary sinus.","options":["Paranasal sinuses and the maxillary sinus","Liver sinusoids and spleen sinusoids","Dural venous sinuses and the coronary sinus","Aortic arch and pulmonary trunk"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"A vascular sinus is a vein whose wall is only thin endothelium with no smooth muscle, so it relies on surrounding dense connective tissue for support. The dural venous sinuses, held within the dura mater around the brain, and the coronary sinus on the back of the heart are the classic examples. Both are veins despite the word sinus in their names.","notes":["Paranasal sinuses are air-filled spaces in the skull bones, not blood vessels. They would be the answer if the question asked about spaces that open into the nasal cavity.","Sinusoids are the leakiest type of capillary, not veins. Pick them if the question asked which capillaries let whole cells cross in the liver and spleen.","Correct. The dural venous sinuses and the coronary sinus are vascular sinuses.","The aortic arch and pulmonary trunk are elastic arteries, not veins. Choose them if the question asked for examples of elastic arteries."],"comp":68,"competencyId":"bvn-artery-cap-vein-kinds"},{"id":"N-m3-vessel-wall-3-chunk26","dok":2,"q":"Why are blood draws and IV lines placed in veins rather than arteries?","a":"Superficial veins are close to the surface, thin-walled, wide, and under low pressure, so they are easy to enter and slow to bleed.","options":["Veins are near the surface, thin-walled, and under low pressure","Veins have no valves that could block the needle","Veins have thicker walls that seal tightly around a needle","Veins carry oxygen-rich blood that gives more accurate results"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Many veins run just under the skin, while most arteries lie deeper. A vein has a thin wall and a wide lumen and carries blood under low pressure, so a needle enters it easily and the puncture bleeds slowly afterward. An artery's high pressure would make a puncture bleed much more forcefully.","notes":["Correct. Surface position, thin walls, and low pressure make veins the safer choice.","Many limb veins do have valves; they can even be seen as small bumps along a forearm vein. The valves are a reason to pick a straight stretch of vein, not a reason veins are chosen.","Veins have thinner walls than arteries, not thicker ones. Thick walls describe arteries, which is part of why they are harder to puncture.","Systemic veins carry oxygen-poor blood, not oxygen-rich blood. Oxygen-rich blood is found in systemic arteries and the pulmonary veins."],"comp":67,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-types"},{"id":"L03946","dok":3,"q":"The precapillary sphincters guarding a capillary bed contract and close. What happens to exchange between blood and tissue in that bed?","a":"Exchange decreases, because blood is diverted away from those capillaries.","options":["Exchange stays the same because capillaries fill from the venules","Exchange increases because blood is forced into those capillaries","Exchange decreases because blood is diverted away from those capillaries","Exchange stays the same because arterioles exchange gases too"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"A precapillary sphincter is a ring of smooth muscle at the entrance to a capillary. When it closes, blood cannot enter that capillary and flows past the bed through other channels instead. Since capillaries are the only vessels thin enough for exchange, less blood in the bed means less exchange with the surrounding tissue.","notes":["Venules drain blood out of capillaries and do not refill them from the far end. This choice reflects the mistaken idea that blood can flow backward into a capillary bed from the venous side.","This reverses the effect. A closed sphincter blocks the entrance to the capillary, so blood is kept out rather than forced in. More exchange would happen if the sphincters opened.","Correct. Closed sphincters send blood around the bed, so exchange in it drops.","Arterioles have walls of smooth muscle and are not thin enough for exchange; only capillaries are. This choice confuses the vessel that controls flow with the vessel where exchange happens."],"lt":"t-pathway-applied-sequencing","lid":"W3-753","comp":67,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-types"},{"id":"L03956","dok":2,"q":"In the systemic circuit, which type of vessel receives blood directly from a capillary bed?","a":"A venule.","options":["Arteriole","Vein","Venule","Artery"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Capillaries merge into venules, the smallest veins, as blood leaves a capillary bed. The first of these, the postcapillary venule, has a thin and very porous wall. Venules then join to form veins, which carry the blood back toward the heart.","notes":["Arterioles feed blood into a capillary bed from the arterial side. They would be the answer if the question asked which vessel delivers blood to a capillary bed.","Veins are one step later, formed as venules merge. They would be the answer if the question asked which vessels venules drain into.","Correct. Venules collect blood as it leaves the capillaries.","Arteries carry blood away from the heart and branch into arterioles before any capillary is reached. They would be the answer if the question asked which vessel the aorta branches into."],"lt":"t-pathway-integrated-tracing","lid":"W3-763","comp":67,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-types"},{"id":"L03968","dok":2,"q":"In an ordinary systemic route (artery, arteriole, ?, venule, vein), which vessel fills the gap between the arteriole and the venule?","a":"A capillary.","options":["Capillary","Vein","Muscular artery","Elastic artery"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Arterioles deliver blood to capillaries, and capillaries drain into venules. The capillary is the smallest vessel, with a wall made only of the tunica intima (endothelium on a basement membrane). That thin wall is what makes it the only vessel where exchange with the tissues happens.","notes":["Correct. The capillary sits between the arteriole and the venule.","A vein comes after the venule, as venules merge on the way back to the heart. It would be the answer if the question asked what venules drain into.","A muscular artery, such as the brachial or femoral artery, is larger than an arteriole and comes before it. It would be the answer if the question asked which vessel type distributes blood to specific organs.","An elastic artery, such as the aorta, is the largest kind of artery and sits right next to the heart. It would be the answer if the question asked which vessel stretches and recoils with each heartbeat."],"lt":"t-pathway-integrated-tracing","lid":"W3-776","comp":67,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-types"},{"id":"L04023","dok":1,"q":"Which type of blood vessel is defined as carrying blood away from the heart?","a":"Artery","options":["Artery","Capillary","Venule","Vein"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Arteries are named by direction of flow: they carry blood away from the heart. Most carry oxygen-rich blood, but the pulmonary arteries carry oxygen-poor blood to the lungs, which shows that the name depends on direction and not on oxygen content. Because arteries carry blood under high pressure, they have thick walls with a heavy tunica media.","notes":["Correct. Arteries carry blood away from the heart.","A capillary connects an arteriole to a venule and is where exchange with the tissues happens. Choose it if the question asked which vessel trades gases and nutrients with the tissues.","A venule is the smallest kind of vein, collecting blood as it leaves a capillary bed. It would be the answer if the question asked which vessel first receives blood from the capillaries.","A vein carries blood toward the heart under low pressure. Pick it if the question asked about vessels that return blood to the heart."],"lt":"t-imagefree-structure-recognition","lid":"W3-834","comp":67,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-types"},{"id":"L04024","dok":1,"q":"Which type of blood vessel is defined as carrying blood toward the heart?","a":"Vein","options":["Artery","Capillary","Vein","Arteriole"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Veins are named by direction of flow: they carry blood toward the heart. Most carry oxygen-poor blood, but the pulmonary veins carry oxygen-rich blood from the lungs to the left atrium, so oxygen content is not what makes a vessel a vein. Veins work under low pressure, so their walls are thinner than those of the matching arteries.","notes":["An artery carries blood away from the heart under high pressure. Pick it if the question asked about vessels leaving the heart.","A capillary is the exchange vessel between an arteriole and a venule. Choose it if the question asked where gases and nutrients cross into the tissues.","Correct. Veins carry blood toward the heart.","An arteriole is the smallest kind of artery, still carrying blood away from the heart, and it controls how much blood enters a capillary bed. It would be the answer if the question asked which vessel regulates flow into the capillaries."],"lt":"t-imagefree-structure-recognition","lid":"W3-835","comp":67,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-types"},{"id":"L04025","dok":2,"q":"Which vessel has a wall made of only a single layer of endothelium on a basement membrane, which makes it the site of exchange with the tissues?","a":"Capillary","options":["Muscular artery","Venule","Arteriole","Capillary"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"A capillary wall is just the tunica intima, a single layer of flat endothelial cells on a basement membrane, with no tunica media or tunica externa. That thin wall lets gases, nutrients, and wastes cross between the blood and the tissue fluid. Capillaries connect arterioles to venules and are the only vessels thin enough for this exchange.","notes":["A muscular artery has a thick tunica media of many smooth muscle layers and distributes blood to specific organs. Choose it if the question asked about a medium-sized artery such as the brachial or femoral.","A venule is the smallest vein, collecting blood as it leaves the capillaries, and the larger venules add smooth muscle to the wall. It would be the answer if the question asked which vessel drains a capillary bed.","An arteriole is the smallest artery, with one or two layers of smooth muscle around its lining. Pick it if the question asked which vessel controls how much blood enters a capillary bed.","Correct. The capillary wall is a single layer of endothelium, built for exchange."],"lt":"t-imagefree-structure-recognition","lid":"W3-836","comp":66,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-tunics"},{"id":"L04026","dok":2,"q":"Which layer of a blood vessel wall is the innermost lining of simple squamous endothelium on a basement membrane?","a":"Tunica intima","options":["Tunica intima","Tunica media","Tunica externa","External elastic lamina"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The tunica intima is the innermost of the three tunics and the layer the blood touches. It is a smooth lining of endothelium (simple squamous epithelium) on a basement membrane, and it is continuous with the endocardium of the heart. It is also the only layer a capillary has.","notes":["Correct. The tunica intima is the endothelial lining of the vessel.","The tunica media is the middle layer of smooth muscle and elastic fibers. Pick it if the question asked which layer changes the diameter of the vessel.","The tunica externa is the outermost layer, mostly collagen fibers, that anchors the vessel. It would be the answer if the question asked which layer is the thickest in a vein.","The external elastic lamina is a sheet of elastic fibers between the tunica media and the tunica externa, not a lining. Choose it if the question asked what marks the outer boundary of the tunica media."],"lt":"t-imagefree-structure-recognition","lid":"W3-837","comp":66,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-tunics"},{"id":"L04027","dok":2,"q":"Which layer of a blood vessel wall is made mainly of smooth muscle and elastic fibers and sets the diameter of the vessel?","a":"Tunica media","options":["Tunica intima","Tunica media","Tunica externa","Endothelium"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The tunica media is the middle layer of the vessel wall, built of smooth muscle and elastic fibers. When its smooth muscle contracts the vessel narrows (vasoconstriction), and when it relaxes the vessel widens (vasodilation). It is the thickest layer in arteries, which is why arteries hold their round shape on a slide.","notes":["The tunica intima is the innermost endothelial lining, with no smooth muscle of its own. Pick it if the question asked which layer the blood touches.","Correct. The tunica media is the smooth muscle layer that controls vessel diameter.","The tunica externa is the outer collagen layer that anchors the vessel and carries small nerves and vessels. It would be the answer if the question asked which layer is the thickest in a vein.","The endothelium is the single layer of flat cells that makes up the tunica intima. Choose it if the question asked what kind of cells line every vessel and form the whole wall of a capillary."],"lt":"t-imagefree-structure-recognition","lid":"W3-838","comp":66,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-tunics"},{"id":"L04028","dok":2,"q":"Which layer of a blood vessel wall is made mostly of collagen fibers and anchors the vessel to the surrounding tissue?","a":"Tunica externa","options":["Tunica externa","Tunica media","Tunica intima","Internal elastic lamina"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The tunica externa is the outermost layer of a vessel wall. It is mostly collagen fibers, with small nerves and vessels running through it, and it ties the vessel to the tissue around it. It is the thickest layer in veins, and in large vessels it carries the vasa vasorum, the tiny vessels that supply the wall itself.","notes":["Correct. The tunica externa is the outer collagen layer that anchors the vessel.","The tunica media is the middle layer of smooth muscle and elastic fibers. Pick it if the question asked which layer is the thickest in an artery.","The tunica intima is the endothelial lining on the inside of the vessel. It would be the answer if the question asked which layer is continuous with the endocardium.","The internal elastic lamina is a sheet of elastic fibers at the boundary between the tunica intima and the tunica media. Choose it if the question asked what marks the inner edge of the middle layer."],"lt":"t-imagefree-structure-recognition","lid":"W3-839","comp":66,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-tunics"},{"id":"L04029","dok":2,"q":"Which type of artery is the largest, with a tunica media dominated by elastic fibers that stretch and recoil with each heartbeat?","a":"Elastic artery","options":["Metarteriole","Arteriole","Muscular artery","Elastic artery"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Elastic arteries are the large arteries closest to the heart, such as the aorta, pulmonary trunk, brachiocephalic trunk, and common carotid arteries. Their tunica media is packed with elastic fibers, so they stretch when the ventricles push blood into them and recoil between beats. That recoil keeps blood moving forward even while the ventricles are relaxing.","notes":["A metarteriole is the tapered end of an arteriole where it joins a capillary bed. Choose it if the question asked which vessel directly supplies a network of capillaries.","An arteriole is the smallest artery, with only one or two layers of smooth muscle. It would be the answer if the question asked which vessel controls flow into a capillary bed.","A muscular artery is a medium-sized artery whose tunica media is mostly smooth muscle, such as the brachial or femoral artery. Pick it if the question asked which artery type distributes blood to specific organs.","Correct. Elastic arteries are the large, stretchy arteries near the heart."],"lt":"t-imagefree-structure-recognition","lid":"W3-840","comp":68,"competencyId":"bvn-artery-cap-vein-kinds"},{"id":"L04030","dok":2,"q":"Which type of artery is medium-sized, has a tunica media made mostly of smooth muscle, and distributes blood to specific organs?","a":"Muscular artery","options":["Elastic artery","Metarteriole","Arteriole","Muscular artery"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Muscular arteries, also called distributing arteries, carry blood from the elastic arteries out to individual organs and regions. Examples include the axillary, brachial, radial, and femoral arteries. Their thick tunica media of smooth muscle, up to about forty layers, lets them hold steady tone and adjust how much blood each region receives.","notes":["An elastic artery is a large artery near the heart whose tunica media is dominated by elastic fibers. Pick it if the question asked about the aorta or the pulmonary trunk.","A metarteriole is the tapered end of an arteriole at the start of a capillary bed. Choose it if the question asked where the precapillary sphincters sit.","An arteriole is much smaller, with only one or two layers of smooth muscle. It would be the answer if the question asked which vessel controls flow into a capillary bed.","Correct. Muscular arteries distribute blood to specific organs."],"lt":"t-imagefree-structure-recognition","lid":"W3-841","comp":68,"competencyId":"bvn-artery-cap-vein-kinds"},{"id":"L04031","dok":2,"q":"Which vessel is the smallest type of artery, with one or two layers of smooth muscle, and controls how much blood enters a capillary bed?","a":"Arteriole","options":["Arteriole","Capillary","Muscular artery","Venule"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Arterioles are microscopic arteries with a thin tunica media of one or two smooth muscle layers. When that muscle contracts or relaxes, the arteriole narrows or widens and changes how much blood flows into the capillary bed downstream. Because they can change their diameter so easily, arterioles are the main site of resistance in the circulation.","notes":["Correct. Arterioles regulate flow into the capillary beds.","A capillary has no smooth muscle at all, only an endothelial lining, so it cannot control flow. Choose it if the question asked which vessel is the site of exchange.","A muscular artery is a medium-sized artery with many layers of smooth muscle that distributes blood to whole organs. It would be the answer if the question asked about the brachial or femoral artery.","A venule is the smallest vein, collecting blood after it leaves a capillary bed rather than controlling what enters it. Pick it if the question asked which vessel drains the capillaries."],"lt":"t-imagefree-structure-recognition","lid":"W3-842","comp":67,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-types"},{"id":"L04032","dok":2,"q":"Which capillary type has an unbroken endothelial lining, interrupted only by small intercellular clefts?","a":"Continuous capillary","options":["Thoroughfare channel","Sinusoid","Continuous capillary","Fenestrated capillary"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Continuous capillaries are the most common type. Their endothelial cells form an unbroken lining, with only narrow clefts between neighboring cells, so they let less through than the other types. They are found in muscle, skin, the lungs, and the central nervous system, where the tight capillaries of the brain help keep most substances out.","notes":["A thoroughfare channel is the end of a metarteriole that acts as a direct route from arteriole to venule, not a type of capillary wall. Choose it if the question asked which channel lets blood bypass a capillary bed.","A sinusoid is a wide, leaky capillary with large gaps and an incomplete basement membrane. It would be the answer if the question asked which capillary lets whole cells cross, as in the liver and spleen.","Correct. Continuous capillaries have an unbroken endothelial lining.","A fenestrated capillary has small pores, called fenestrations, through its endothelium. Pick it if the question asked which capillary type is found where rapid filtration or absorption happens, such as the kidney."],"lt":"t-imagefree-structure-recognition","lid":"W3-843","comp":68,"competencyId":"bvn-artery-cap-vein-kinds"},{"id":"L04033","dok":2,"q":"Which capillary type has small pores in its endothelium and is found where rapid filtration or absorption happens, such as the kidney and small intestine?","a":"Fenestrated capillary","options":["Sinusoid","Fenestrated capillary","Postcapillary venule","Continuous capillary"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Fenestrated capillaries have small pores, called fenestrations (from the Latin for windows), through their endothelial cells. The pores let fluid and small solutes move across quickly. They are found where fast filtration or absorption is the job, including the kidney, the small intestine, the choroid plexus, and most endocrine glands.","notes":["A sinusoid has much larger gaps and an incomplete basement membrane, wide enough for whole cells to pass. It would be the answer if the question asked about the capillaries of the liver, spleen, or red bone marrow.","Correct. Fenestrated capillaries have pores for rapid filtration and absorption.","A postcapillary venule is the first vein-side vessel to receive blood from a capillary bed, not a capillary. Choose it if the question asked where white blood cells leave the bloodstream.","A continuous capillary has an unbroken lining with no pores. Pick it if the question asked about the most common capillary type, found in muscle, skin, and the brain."],"lt":"t-imagefree-structure-recognition","lid":"W3-844","comp":68,"competencyId":"bvn-artery-cap-vein-kinds"},{"id":"L04034","dok":2,"q":"Which capillary type is wide and winding, with large gaps and an incomplete basement membrane, so that whole cells can cross its wall?","a":"Sinusoid","options":["Continuous capillary","Postcapillary venule","Sinusoid","Fenestrated capillary"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Sinusoids, also called sinusoidal capillaries, are the leakiest capillaries. They are wide and winding, with large fenestrations, wide clefts, and an incomplete basement membrane. That build lets proteins and even whole blood cells pass, which is why sinusoids are found in the liver, spleen, red bone marrow, anterior pituitary, and adrenal glands.","notes":["A continuous capillary has an unbroken lining and lets the least through. It would be the answer if the question asked about the most common capillary type or the tight capillaries of the brain.","A postcapillary venule receives blood from a capillary bed and has porous walls, but it is the start of the venous side, not a capillary. Choose it if the question asked which vessel collects blood just after the capillaries.","Correct. Sinusoids are the wide, leaky capillaries that let whole cells cross.","A fenestrated capillary has small pores that let fluid and small solutes through, but not whole cells. Pick it if the question asked about the capillaries of the kidney or small intestine."],"lt":"t-imagefree-structure-recognition","lid":"W3-845","comp":68,"competencyId":"bvn-artery-cap-vein-kinds"},{"id":"L04035","dok":2,"q":"Which vessel carries blood under low pressure, has a thin tunica media and a wide lumen, and contains valves in the limbs?","a":"Vein","options":["Muscular artery","Elastic artery","Vein","Arteriole"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Veins return blood to the heart under low pressure, so they do not need a thick muscular wall. They have a thin tunica media, a relatively thick tunica externa, and a wide lumen, and on a slide they often look collapsed next to their round companion artery. Venous valves, flaps of the tunica intima, keep blood from sliding backward, especially in the limbs where blood must travel up against gravity.","notes":["A muscular artery has a thick tunica media of smooth muscle and carries blood under high pressure to organs. It would be the answer if the question asked about a distributing artery such as the brachial artery.","An elastic artery is a large, high-pressure artery near the heart with a thick, elastic tunica media. Pick it if the question asked about the aorta or pulmonary trunk.","Correct. Veins are thin-walled, low-pressure vessels with valves.","An arteriole is a tiny artery that controls flow into capillaries, and it has no valves. Choose it if the question asked which vessel is the main site of resistance."],"lt":"t-imagefree-structure-recognition","lid":"W3-846","comp":67,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-types"},{"id":"L04084","dok":2,"q":"Which structures keep blood in the limb veins from flowing backward as it returns to the heart under low pressure?","a":"Venous valves","options":["Precapillary sphincters","Venous valves","Semilunar valves","Elastic laminae"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Blood in the veins moves under low pressure, and in the limbs it often has to travel upward against gravity. Venous valves are flaps of the tunica intima that open as blood moves toward the heart and close if it starts to slide back. Working with the skeletal muscle pump, which squeezes the deep veins as limb muscles contract, they keep venous blood moving one way.","notes":["Precapillary sphincters are rings of smooth muscle at the entrance to a capillary bed that open or close to control inflow. Pick them if the question asked what controls whether blood enters a particular capillary bed.","Correct. Venous valves keep blood in the limb veins moving toward the heart.","Semilunar valves are the pulmonary and aortic valves of the heart, at the exits of the ventricles. They would be the answer if the question asked which valves keep blood from falling back into the ventricles.","The internal and external elastic laminae are sheets of elastic fibers at the boundaries of the tunica media, and they do not control the direction of flow. Choose them if the question asked what marks the edges of the middle layer of an artery on a slide."],"lt":"t-imagefree-mixed-recognition","lid":"W3-895","comp":68,"competencyId":"bvn-artery-cap-vein-kinds"},{"id":"L04087","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares the wall of a capillary with the wall of an artery?","a":"A capillary has only the tunica intima, while an artery has all three tunics.","options":["A capillary has only the tunica intima, while an artery has all three tunics","A capillary has all three tunics, but each is thinner than in an artery","A capillary has a tunica media of smooth muscle but no tunica externa","A capillary has only a tunica externa, while an artery has all three tunics"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"An artery wall has three tunics: the tunica intima lining, a thick tunica media of smooth muscle and elastic fibers, and an outer tunica externa of collagen. A capillary keeps only the innermost layer, the endothelium on its basement membrane. That single thin layer is what lets gases, nutrients, and wastes cross between the blood and the tissues.","notes":["Correct. A capillary wall is only the tunica intima.","Capillaries do not have thinner versions of all three tunics; they lack the tunica media and tunica externa entirely. Students choose this when they picture a capillary as a scaled-down artery rather than a single cell layer.","Capillaries have no smooth muscle at all, which is why they cannot change their own diameter. Students choose this when they confuse a capillary with an arteriole, which does have one or two layers of smooth muscle.","The tunica externa is the outer collagen layer, and capillaries do not have it. Students choose this when they mix up the innermost and outermost tunics; the layer capillaries keep is the inner endothelial lining."],"lt":"t-imagefree-mixed-recognition","lid":"W3-898","comp":66,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-tunics"},{"id":"L04097","dok":2,"q":"On a slide, a vessel has a thick tunica media packed with smooth muscle. What job is this wall built for?","a":"Withstanding high pressure and changing diameter.","options":["Withstanding high pressure and changing diameter","Exchanging gases and nutrients with the tissues","Letting whole blood cells pass through its wall","Holding a large reserve of blood at low pressure"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The tunica media is the middle layer of a vessel wall, made of smooth muscle and elastic fibers. It is the thickest layer in arteries, because arteries carry blood away from the heart under high pressure and need a strong wall to withstand it. Its smooth muscle can also contract or relax to narrow or widen the vessel (vasoconstriction and vasodilation), which adjusts how much blood flows through.","notes":["Correct. A thick, muscular media is the mark of an artery, built for high pressure and for changing diameter.","Exchange happens in capillaries, whose wall is only the tunica intima, a single layer of endothelium on a basement membrane. Pick exchange for a vessel with no media at all, the thinnest wall in the body.","Letting whole cells cross is the job of sinusoids, the leaky capillaries of the liver, spleen, and red bone marrow. They have no tunica media, so this does not match a thick muscular wall.","Veins hold the largest share of the body's blood at rest and run at low pressure, with a thin media and a thick tunica externa. Choose this for a vessel with a wide, collapsed-looking lumen and a thin middle layer."],"lt":"t-clinical-application-and-misconceptions","lid":"W3-911","comp":67,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-types"},{"id":"L04098","dok":2,"q":"The kidney must filter fluid out of its blood rapidly while keeping blood cells inside the vessels. Which capillary type fits this job?","a":"Fenestrated capillary.","options":["Sinusoid","Fenestrated capillary","Postcapillary venule","Continuous capillary"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Fenestrated capillaries have small pores, called fenestrations, through their endothelial cells. The pores let fluid and small dissolved substances move out quickly, while their small size and the intact basement membrane keep blood cells inside. That combination fits places that filter or absorb rapidly, such as the kidney, the small intestine, and most endocrine glands.","notes":["Sinusoids are wide, winding capillaries with large gaps and an incomplete basement membrane, leaky enough to let whole cells through. Choose them for the liver, spleen, or red bone marrow, but not where blood cells must stay inside.","Correct. Its small pores allow rapid filtration of fluid while keeping blood cells in the vessel.","A postcapillary venule is the small vessel that collects blood after it leaves a capillary bed, and it is where white blood cells leave the bloodstream. It is not a capillary type, so it would fit a question about where blood drains from a capillary bed.","Continuous capillaries have an unbroken endothelial lining with only narrow clefts between cells, so they are the least leaky type. Pick them for muscle, skin, lungs, or the brain, where movement across the wall is slower and more controlled."],"lt":"t-clinical-application-and-misconceptions","lid":"W3-912","comp":68,"competencyId":"bvn-artery-cap-vein-kinds"},{"id":"L04099","dok":2,"q":"In red bone marrow, newly made blood cells must move from the tissue into the bloodstream. Which capillary type makes this possible?","a":"Sinusoid.","options":["Arteriole","Sinusoid","Fenestrated capillary","Continuous capillary"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Sinusoids are wide, winding capillaries with large openings in the endothelium, wide gaps between cells, and an incomplete basement membrane. These gaps are big enough for whole cells to pass through the wall. That is why sinusoids are found in red bone marrow, where new blood cells enter the circulation, as well as in the liver and spleen.","notes":["An arteriole is the smallest artery, with one or two layers of smooth muscle that adjust flow into a capillary bed. It is not an exchange vessel, so it would fit a question about controlling blood flow, not about cells crossing a wall.","Correct. Sinusoids have gaps large enough for whole blood cells to cross the wall.","Fenestrated capillaries have small pores that let fluid and small molecules through quickly, but the pores are far too small for cells. Pick them for the kidney or small intestine, where fast filtration or absorption is the job.","Continuous capillaries have an unbroken lining and are the tightest type, found in muscle, skin, lungs, and the brain. They would be the answer if the goal were to keep most substances inside the blood."],"lt":"t-clinical-application-and-misconceptions","lid":"W3-913","comp":68,"competencyId":"bvn-artery-cap-vein-kinds"},{"id":"L04129","dok":2,"q":"A student says capillaries have all three tunics, just thinner. Which statement correctly describes the capillary wall?","a":"It has only the tunica intima.","options":["It has only the tunica externa","It has only the tunica intima","It has all three tunics, each one cell thick","It has only the tunica media"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"A capillary wall is only the innermost layer of a vessel, the tunica intima: a single layer of simple squamous endothelium on a basement membrane. Capillaries lack a tunica media and a tunica externa entirely. That makes their wall thin enough for gases, nutrients, and wastes to cross between the blood and the tissues.","notes":["The tunica externa is the outer collagen layer that anchors a vessel and is thickest in veins. Choose it for a question about the outermost layer of a vein, not a capillary.","Correct. A capillary wall is just endothelium on a basement membrane, the tunica intima.","This is the misconception in the question. Arteries and veins have all three tunics, but a capillary has only the innermost one.","The tunica media is the smooth muscle and elastic layer that is thickest in arteries. Pick it for a question about what lets an artery change its diameter; capillaries have none."],"lt":"t-clinical-application-and-misconceptions","lid":"W3-943","comp":66,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-tunics"},{"id":"L04145","dok":2,"q":"Why are superficial veins, rather than arteries, used for IV lines?","a":"They are superficial, thin, wide, and low pressure.","options":["They carry oxygen-rich blood straight to the tissues","They lack valves, so fluid can flow either way","They are deep, thick-walled, and under high pressure","They are superficial, thin, wide, and low pressure"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Veins return blood to the heart under low pressure, so their walls are thinner and their lumens wider than those of the matching arteries. Many limb veins also run just under the skin, where they are easy to see and reach. A needle enters a thin, wide vein easily, and because the pressure is low, the site bleeds slowly when the needle comes out. The same features make veins the usual choice for routine blood draws.","notes":["Systemic veins carry oxygen-poor blood toward the heart, not oxygen-rich blood to the tissues. This choice describes systemic arteries.","Limb veins actually have many valves, flaps of the tunica intima that keep blood flowing toward the heart. They can even be seen as small bumps along a forearm vein, so this choice is a misconception about vein anatomy.","Deep, thick-walled, high-pressure vessels are arteries. Those features are why arteries are avoided for routine IV lines, so pick this description for a question about artery walls.","Correct. Superficial veins are easy to reach, easy to enter, and slow to bleed."],"lt":"t-clinical-application-and-misconceptions","lid":"W3-960","comp":67,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-types"},{"id":"L04146","dok":2,"q":"Why are arteries not the usual choice for routine blood draws?","a":"They are deep, thick-walled, and high pressure.","options":["They carry too little blood to sample from","They are deep, thick-walled, and high pressure","They have valves inside that block the needle","They are thin-walled and collapse too easily"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Arteries carry blood away from the heart under high pressure, so they have a thick tunica media of smooth muscle and elastic fibers. Most run deeper in the body than the superficial veins, which makes them harder to find and enter. Because of the high pressure, a puncture also bleeds more forcefully, so arteries are saved for special tests rather than routine draws.","notes":["Arteries carry plenty of blood under high pressure. This misconception confuses arteries with tiny vessels like capillaries, which hold too little blood to draw from.","Correct. Depth, thick walls, and high pressure make arteries harder to enter and quicker to bleed.","Arteries do not have valves; valves are found in veins, especially in the limbs. This choice comes from mixing up the two vessel types.","A thin wall that looks collapsed on a slide describes a vein, not an artery. Pick it for a question about why veins look flattened next to their matching arteries."],"lt":"t-clinical-application-and-misconceptions","lid":"W3-961","comp":67,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-types"},{"id":"L04147","dok":2,"q":"Why are valves especially important in the veins below the heart?","a":"They stop blood sliding back down against gravity.","options":["They raise venous pressure to arterial levels","They stop blood sliding back down against gravity","They let blood exchange gases through the vein wall","They stop blood from entering the capillary beds"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Venous valves are flaps of the tunica intima that let blood move only toward the heart. Blood in the veins below the heart, especially in the legs, travels upward against gravity under low pressure. Each valve closes if blood starts to slide back down, so blood moves up the vein in short steps toward the heart. When these valves fail, blood pools and causes varicose veins.","notes":["Valves only control direction; they do not raise venous pressure. Pressure in veins stays low, which is why they need valves in the first place.","Correct. Valves stop backflow where blood must climb against gravity at low pressure.","Exchange of gases happens across the thin wall of capillaries, not veins. Pick capillaries for a question about where gases and nutrients cross.","Entry into capillary beds is controlled by arterioles and precapillary sphincters on the arterial side. Choose them for a question about directing flow into a tissue."],"lt":"t-clinical-application-and-misconceptions","lid":"W3-962","comp":68,"competencyId":"bvn-artery-cap-vein-kinds"},{"id":"L04148","dok":2,"q":"Why does walking help move blood back to the heart from the legs?","a":"Contracting leg muscles squeeze the deep veins.","options":["Walking makes the veins contract on their own like the heart","Contracting leg muscles squeeze the deep veins","Walking widens the leg arteries","Walking makes the venous valves open both ways"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Deep veins of the legs run between the leg muscles. When the muscles contract during walking, they squeeze those veins and push blood forward, and the venous valves keep it from flowing back down. This is called the skeletal muscle pump, and it is a major help to venous return from the legs, where blood must move upward against gravity at low pressure.","notes":["Veins have a thin tunica media and do not pump rhythmically the way the heart does. Students choose this when they assume veins pump blood on their own.","Correct. The skeletal muscle pump squeezes deep veins, and the valves keep blood moving toward the heart.","Wider arteries bring more blood into the legs, which is a separate effect. It does not push blood back up the veins, so pick it only for a question about delivering blood to working muscles.","Venous valves only open toward the heart, and their whole job is to close when blood flows backward. This misconception would undo what the valves do."],"lt":"t-clinical-application-and-misconceptions","lid":"W3-963","comp":68,"competencyId":"bvn-artery-cap-vein-kinds"},{"id":"L04151","dok":2,"q":"Why are sinusoids the capillary type found in the liver, spleen, and red bone marrow?","a":"Their wide gaps let large molecules and cells cross.","options":["Their wide gaps let large molecules and cells cross","Their unbroken lining keeps most substances in the blood","Their smooth muscle controls how much blood enters","Their small pores allow rapid filtration of fluid"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Sinusoids are wide, winding capillaries with large openings in the endothelium, wide gaps between cells, and an incomplete basement membrane. Those gaps let large molecules, and even whole cells, pass through the wall. The liver exchanges large proteins with the blood, the spleen handles whole blood cells, and red bone marrow releases newly made blood cells, so all three need this leaky build.","notes":["Correct. The wide gaps of sinusoids let large molecules and whole cells pass.","An unbroken lining describes continuous capillaries, the tightest type, found in muscle, skin, lungs, and the brain. Pick it for tissues that must keep most substances inside the blood.","Capillaries have no smooth muscle at all; their wall is only endothelium on a basement membrane. Smooth muscle that controls flow describes arterioles and precapillary sphincters.","Small pores describe fenestrated capillaries, found in the kidney, small intestine, and most endocrine glands. They would be the answer for rapid filtration or absorption, but their pores are too small for cells."],"lt":"t-clinical-application-and-misconceptions","lid":"W3-966","comp":68,"competencyId":"bvn-artery-cap-vein-kinds"},{"id":"L04152","dok":2,"q":"Why are continuous capillaries the least leaky capillary type?","a":"Their lining is unbroken except for narrow clefts.","options":["Their lining is unbroken except for narrow clefts","Their basement membrane is incomplete","Their endothelial cells contain small pores","They have a thick tunica media around the endothelium"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Continuous capillaries have an unbroken endothelial lining, with neighboring cells joined closely and interrupted only by narrow intercellular clefts. With no pores and no large gaps, only small substances can pass, so most material stays inside the blood. They are the most common type, found in muscle, skin, lungs, and the central nervous system, where the tight lining helps protect the brain.","notes":["Correct. An unbroken lining with only narrow clefts makes continuous capillaries the tightest type.","An incomplete basement membrane is a feature of sinusoids, the leakiest type. It would be the answer for a question about how whole cells cross in the liver, spleen, or bone marrow.","Small pores, called fenestrations, describe fenestrated capillaries. Pick them for a question about rapid filtration in the kidney or absorption in the small intestine.","No capillary has a tunica media; the wall is only endothelium on a basement membrane. A thick tunica media describes an artery."],"lt":"t-clinical-application-and-misconceptions","lid":"W3-967","comp":68,"competencyId":"bvn-artery-cap-vein-kinds"},{"id":"L04153","dok":2,"q":"Why are fenestrated capillaries found in the kidney and the small intestine?","a":"Their pores allow fast filtration and absorption.","options":["Their large gaps let whole blood cells pass through","Their thick walls withstand high pressure","Their pores allow fast filtration and absorption","Their unbroken lining keeps substances in"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Fenestrated capillaries have small pores, called fenestrations, in their endothelial cells. These pores let fluid and small dissolved substances move across the wall much faster than in continuous capillaries. The kidney filters large volumes of fluid out of the blood, and the small intestine absorbs digested nutrients into the blood, so both depend on this rapid movement.","notes":["Letting whole blood cells pass describes sinusoids, the leakiest capillaries, found in the liver, spleen, and red bone marrow. The kidney and intestine need fluid to move, not cells.","Capillaries have thin walls made of a single layer of endothelium, never thick walls. Thick walls that withstand high pressure describe arteries.","Correct. Fenestrations let fluid and small solutes cross quickly, which filtration and absorption require.","An unbroken lining describes continuous capillaries, which keep most substances in the blood. Choose them for muscle, skin, lungs, or the brain."],"lt":"t-clinical-application-and-misconceptions","lid":"W3-968","comp":68,"competencyId":"bvn-artery-cap-vein-kinds"},{"id":"L04169","dok":2,"q":"Why are capillary walls so thin?","a":"A thin wall lets materials cross quickly.","options":["A thin wall lets materials cross quickly","A thin wall lets capillaries withstand high pressure","A thin wall lets capillaries hold most of the blood","A thin wall lets capillaries change diameter quickly"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Capillaries are the exchange vessels, where gases, nutrients, and wastes move between the blood and the tissues. Their wall is only the tunica intima, a single layer of flat endothelial cells on a basement membrane. That keeps the distance between blood and tissue fluid very short, so materials can cross quickly.","notes":["Correct. A one-cell-thick wall keeps the exchange distance short.","Withstanding high pressure takes a thick wall, like the heavy tunica media of an artery. Pick arteries for a question about which vessels handle high pressure.","The veins, not the capillaries, hold the largest share of the body's blood at rest, roughly sixty percent. Choose veins for a question about blood reservoirs.","Changing diameter takes smooth muscle, which capillaries lack. Arterioles, with one or two layers of smooth muscle, are the answer for a question about adjusting flow into a capillary bed."],"lt":"t-clinical-application-and-misconceptions","lid":"W3-987","comp":66,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-tunics"},{"id":"L04223","dok":2,"q":"Which list gives the three tunics of a blood vessel wall in order from innermost to outermost?","a":"Tunica intima, tunica media, tunica externa.","options":["Tunica media, tunica intima, tunica externa","Tunica intima, tunica externa, tunica media","Tunica externa, tunica media, tunica intima","Tunica intima, tunica media, tunica externa"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The tunica intima is the innermost layer, an endothelial lining in direct contact with the blood. The tunica media is the middle layer of smooth muscle and elastic fibers, and the tunica externa is the outer layer of mostly collagen fibers that anchors the vessel. The names follow the order: intima means innermost, media means middle, and externa means outer.","notes":["This places the muscle layer innermost, but the tunica media sits in the middle, between the endothelial lining and the outer collagen layer. Its name, media, means middle.","This places the tunica externa in the middle, but externa means outer. The muscular tunica media is the middle layer.","These are the right three tunics, but listed from the outside in. Pick this order if the question asked for the layers from outermost to innermost.","Correct. Intima, media, externa, from the lumen outward."],"lt":"t-bvn-vessel-tunics","lid":"bvn-vessel-tunics-r1","comp":66,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-tunics"},{"id":"L04224","dok":1,"q":"What tissue forms the tunica intima of a blood vessel?","a":"A lining of simple squamous endothelium on a basement membrane.","options":["Simple squamous endothelium on a basement membrane","Smooth muscle and elastic fibers","Stratified squamous epithelium on a basement membrane","Mostly collagen fibers with nerves and small vessels"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The tunica intima is the innermost layer of a vessel wall, and it is lined by endothelium, a single layer of flat (simple squamous) cells resting on a basement membrane. This smooth, thin lining reduces friction as blood flows past and is continuous with the endocardium inside the heart. In a capillary, this layer is the entire wall.","notes":["Correct. The intima is a single layer of flat endothelial cells on a basement membrane.","Smooth muscle and elastic fibers make up the tunica media, the middle layer. Pick this if the question asked which layer changes a vessel's diameter.","Stratified squamous epithelium has many layers and protects surfaces that take friction, such as the skin and the lining of the esophagus. It would be the answer for a protective lining, not a thin vessel lining that blood flows across.","Collagen fibers with nerves and small vessels make up the tunica externa, the outer layer that anchors the vessel. Choose it for the layer that contains the vasa vasorum."],"lt":"t-bvn-vessel-tunics","lid":"bvn-vessel-tunics-r2","comp":66,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-tunics"},{"id":"L04225","dok":1,"q":"What does the tunica media contain, and what does it control?","a":"Smooth muscle and elastic fibers; its smooth muscle sets the vessel's diameter.","options":["Collagen fibers and small vessels; it anchors the vessel in place","Endothelium on a basement membrane; it reduces friction with the blood","Cardiac muscle and elastic fibers; it pumps blood along the vessel","Smooth muscle and elastic fibers; it controls the vessel's diameter"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The tunica media is the middle layer of the vessel wall, made of smooth muscle and elastic fibers. When its smooth muscle contracts, the vessel narrows (vasoconstriction), and when it relaxes, the vessel widens (vasodilation). It is the thickest layer in arteries, which helps them handle blood under high pressure.","notes":["Collagen fibers with small vessels form the tunica externa, which anchors the vessel to surrounding tissue. It would be the answer for the outermost tunic, the thickest layer in veins.","Endothelium on a basement membrane is the tunica intima, the smooth inner lining. Pick it if the question asked which layer is in direct contact with the blood.","Cardiac muscle is found only in the heart wall, and blood vessels do not pump blood. Students choose this when they confuse the smooth muscle in vessel walls with heart muscle.","Correct. The smooth muscle of the media narrows or widens the vessel."],"lt":"t-bvn-vessel-tunics","lid":"bvn-vessel-tunics-r3","comp":66,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-tunics"},{"id":"L04226","dok":2,"q":"A capillary wall is built from only one of the three tunics. Which one, and why only that layer?","a":"The tunica intima only, because exchange needs the thinnest possible wall.","options":["Tunica intima and media, because the wall must be thin but strong","Tunica externa only, because the capillary must be anchored in tissue","Tunica media only, because the capillary must control its own diameter","Tunica intima only, because exchange needs the thinnest possible wall"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"A capillary wall is just the tunica intima: a single layer of endothelium on a basement membrane. Capillaries are the exchange vessels, where gases, nutrients, and wastes cross between the blood and the tissues, and that exchange works best across the thinnest possible barrier. Adding smooth muscle or a collagen coat would only slow the crossing.","notes":["A capillary has no tunica media, so its wall is not two layers. Students choose this when they assume every vessel needs muscle; the precapillary sphincters that control entry into a capillary sit on the metarteriole, not the capillary itself.","The tunica externa is the collagen layer that anchors larger vessels, and capillaries lack it too. Choose it for the thickest layer of a vein.","The tunica media is the smooth muscle layer that changes vessel diameter, and capillaries lack it. Pick it for a question about how arterioles adjust blood flow into a capillary bed.","Correct. The capillary wall is endothelium on a basement membrane, which keeps the exchange barrier as thin as possible."],"lt":"t-bvn-vessel-tunics","lid":"bvn-vessel-tunics-r4","comp":66,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-tunics"},{"id":"L04227","dok":2,"q":"A vessel on a slide has a thick middle layer packed with smooth muscle and elastic fibers, and it holds a round shape. Which vessel type is it most likely to be?","a":"An artery, because arteries carry high-pressure blood and have a thick tunica media.","options":["A vein","A venule","A capillary","An artery"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Arteries carry blood away from the heart under high pressure, so their tunica media is thick, with plenty of smooth muscle and elastic fibers. That thick middle layer is why an artery keeps its round shape on a slide. A vein has the same three tunics, but its media is thin and its outer externa is the thickest layer, so it usually looks collapsed.","notes":["A vein has a thin tunica media, a thick tunica externa, and a wide lumen, so it often looks collapsed on a slide. Pick it if the vessel had a thin wall and an irregular, flattened shape.","A venule is a small vein with a thin, porous wall that collects blood from capillaries. It would be the answer for a tiny thin-walled vessel just downstream of a capillary bed.","A capillary wall is only endothelium and a basement membrane, with no tunica media at all. Choose it for a vessel whose wall is a single layer of flat cells.","Correct. A thick muscular media and a round shape point to an artery."],"lt":"t-bvn-vessel-tunics","lid":"bvn-vessel-tunics-r5","comp":67,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-types"},{"id":"L04228","dok":2,"q":"Which list gives the five vessel types in the order blood passes through them, starting from the heart?","a":"Arteries, arterioles, capillaries, venules, veins.","options":["Arteries, venules, capillaries, arterioles, veins","Arterioles, arteries, capillaries, veins, venules","Veins, venules, capillaries, arterioles, arteries","Arteries, arterioles, capillaries, venules, veins"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Blood leaves the heart in arteries, which branch into smaller arterioles. Arterioles feed the capillaries, where exchange with the tissues happens, and the capillaries merge into venules, the smallest veins. Venules join into veins, which carry blood back to the heart.","notes":["This swaps arterioles and venules. Arterioles are the small arteries leading into a capillary bed, and venules are the small veins draining out of it.","This puts the small vessels before the large ones on each side. Blood goes from the large arteries into the smaller arterioles, and on the return trip the small venules merge into larger veins.","This is the correct sequence run backward, from veins to arteries. Blood leaves the heart in arteries and returns in veins, so this order fits only a question that asked for the sequence in reverse.","Correct. Arteries branch down to capillaries, and venules merge back up into veins."],"lt":"t-bvn-vessel-types","lid":"bvn-vessel-types-r1","comp":67,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-types"},{"id":"L04229","dok":1,"q":"Which statement correctly defines arteries and veins?","a":"Arteries carry blood away from the heart; veins carry blood toward the heart.","options":["Arteries carry oxygen-rich blood; veins carry oxygen-poor blood","Arteries carry blood at low pressure; veins carry blood at high pressure","Arteries carry blood away from the heart; veins carry blood toward the heart","Arteries carry blood toward the heart; veins carry blood away from the heart"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Arteries and veins are defined by the direction of blood flow relative to the heart. Arteries carry blood away from the heart, and veins carry blood back toward it. This is why the pulmonary arteries count as arteries even though they carry oxygen-poor blood to the lungs.","notes":["This is true in the systemic circuit, but it is not the definition. The pulmonary arteries carry oxygen-poor blood and the pulmonary veins carry oxygen-rich blood, so oxygen content cannot be the rule.","This reverses the pressures. Arteries carry blood under high pressure, which is why they have thick muscular walls, while veins carry blood under low pressure and rely on valves.","Correct. Direction of flow is what defines the two vessel types.","This reverses the two definitions. A helpful memory aid is that artery and away both start with the letter a."],"lt":"t-bvn-vessel-types","lid":"bvn-vessel-types-r2","comp":67,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-types"},{"id":"L04230","dok":1,"q":"Which vessel type is the main exchange vessel, where gases and nutrients cross between blood and tissue?","a":"The capillary.","options":["Capillaries","Arterioles","Veins","Elastic arteries"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Capillaries are the exchange vessels of the body. Their walls are only the tunica intima, a single layer of endothelium on a basement membrane, so oxygen, nutrients, carbon dioxide, and wastes can cross easily between the blood and the surrounding tissue. Every other vessel type has a thicker wall built for carrying or controlling blood rather than exchanging with the tissues.","notes":["Correct. Capillaries have the thin walls that make exchange possible.","Arterioles are the smallest arteries, with one or two layers of smooth muscle. Pick them if the question asked which vessels adjust how much blood enters a capillary bed.","Veins carry blood back to the heart under low pressure and hold most of the blood volume at rest. Choose them for a question about blood reservoirs or vessels with valves.","Elastic arteries, like the aorta and pulmonary trunk, stretch with each heartbeat and recoil between beats. They would be the answer for vessels that keep blood moving while the ventricles relax."],"lt":"t-bvn-vessel-types","lid":"bvn-vessel-types-r3","comp":67,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-types"},{"id":"L04231","dok":2,"q":"Arterioles have walls made mostly of smooth muscle. What does that allow them to do?","a":"Adjust how much blood enters a capillary bed.","options":["Adjust how much blood enters a capillary bed","Let gases cross between blood and tissue","Hold the largest share of the blood at rest","Stretch and recoil to keep blood moving between beats"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Arterioles are the smallest arteries, and their walls are mostly one or two layers of smooth muscle. When that muscle contracts, the arteriole narrows and less blood flows into the capillary bed it feeds; when it relaxes, the arteriole widens and more blood flows in. This lets the body send more blood to active tissues and less to resting ones.","notes":["Correct. Their smooth muscle lets arterioles open or narrow the entrance to a capillary bed.","Gas exchange happens across capillary walls, which are only endothelium. Arterioles have muscle in their walls, so they control flow rather than exchange material.","Veins, not arterioles, hold the largest share of the blood at rest, roughly 60 percent. Choose this for a question about the blood reservoirs of the body.","Stretching and recoiling to keep blood moving between beats is the job of elastic arteries like the aorta, whose walls are rich in elastic fibers. Pick this for a question about the largest arteries near the heart."],"lt":"t-bvn-vessel-types","lid":"bvn-vessel-types-r4","comp":67,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-types"},{"id":"L04232","dok":2,"q":"A phlebotomist draws blood from a vein in the arm rather than an artery. Which set of features makes the vein the safer choice?","a":"A vein lies closer to the surface, has a thinner wall, and runs at low pressure, so it is easy to enter and slow to bleed.","options":["Its thick tunica media clamps shut around the needle to stop the bleeding","It lies closer to the surface, has a thinner wall, and carries blood at low pressure","Its wall is a single layer of endothelium, so the needle passes through easily","It lies deeper, has a thicker wall, and carries blood at high pressure"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Veins used for blood draws sit close to the surface, so they are easy to find and reach. Their walls are thinner, with a thin tunica media, and their lumen is wide, so a needle enters easily. Because veins carry blood under low pressure, the puncture bleeds slowly and is easy to stop with gentle pressure, unlike an artery.","notes":["Veins have a thin tunica media, not a thick one, and vessel walls do not clamp around a needle. Students choose this when they confuse veins with muscular arteries.","Correct. Surface position, a thin wall, and low pressure make a vein easy to enter and slow to bleed.","A wall made only of endothelium describes a capillary, which is far too small to hold a needle. Choose this description for a question about the exchange vessels.","That describes an artery, which generally lies deeper, has a thick muscular wall, and carries blood under high pressure. Pick this set of features if the question asked why an arterial puncture is harder and bleeds more."],"lt":"t-bvn-vessel-types","lid":"bvn-vessel-types-r5","comp":67,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-types"},{"id":"L04247","dok":2,"q":"Which list names the three kinds of artery in order from largest to smallest?","a":"Elastic arteries, muscular arteries, and arterioles.","options":["Elastic arteries, muscular arteries, arterioles","Elastic arteries, arterioles, muscular arteries","Arterioles, muscular arteries, elastic arteries","Muscular arteries, elastic arteries, arterioles"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Elastic arteries are the largest, near the heart (the aorta, pulmonary trunk, and their major branches), with a tunica media rich in elastic fibers that stretches and recoils with each beat. Muscular arteries are medium-sized, like the brachial and femoral arteries, with a media made mostly of smooth muscle that distributes blood to specific organs. Arterioles are the smallest, with one or two layers of smooth muscle that control flow into capillary beds.","notes":["Correct. Elastic arteries branch into muscular arteries, which branch into arterioles.","This puts arterioles in the middle, but arterioles are the smallest arteries, the last before the capillaries. Muscular arteries come between the elastic arteries and the arterioles.","These are the right three kinds listed from smallest to largest. Pick this order if the question asked for the arteries from smallest to largest.","This swaps the first two. The largest arteries, right next to the heart, are the elastic ones, and the muscular arteries branch from them to reach specific organs."],"lt":"t-bvn-artery-cap-vein-kinds","lid":"bvn-artery-cap-vein-kinds-r1","comp":68,"competencyId":"bvn-artery-cap-vein-kinds"},{"id":"L04249","dok":2,"q":"Which capillary type is the leakiest, letting even whole blood cells cross, and where is it found?","a":"The sinusoid, found in the liver, spleen, and red bone marrow.","options":["Continuous, in the liver, spleen, and red bone marrow","Continuous, in muscle, skin, and the brain","Fenestrated, in the kidney and small intestine","Sinusoid, in the liver, spleen, and red bone marrow"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Sinusoids are wide, winding capillaries with large openings in the endothelium, an incomplete basement membrane, and wide gaps between cells. They are found where whole cells or large molecules must pass between the blood and the tissue, such as the liver, spleen, and red bone marrow. Newly made blood cells leaving the red bone marrow, for example, cross into the blood through sinusoid walls.","notes":["The locations belong to sinusoids, but continuous capillaries are the tightest type, not the leakiest. Students pick this when they remember the organs but mix up the capillary name.","Continuous capillaries are the least leaky type, with an unbroken lining, and these are their correct locations. Choose this pairing for the most common capillary type, or the tight capillaries of the brain.","Fenestrated capillaries have small pores that let fluid and small molecules through quickly, but not whole cells. Pick this pairing if the question asked about the capillaries built for rapid filtration and absorption.","Correct. Sinusoids are the leakiest capillaries, found where whole cells must cross."],"lt":"t-bvn-artery-cap-vein-kinds","lid":"bvn-artery-cap-vein-kinds-r3","comp":68,"competencyId":"bvn-artery-cap-vein-kinds"}]},{"id":"m3-muscle-organization","title":"Muscle tissue types and muscle organization","summary":"three muscle tissue types, epimysium, perimysium, endomysium, fascicles, fascicle arrangements, tendons and aponeuroses","mw":"2026-10-07","tr":"2026-10-06","cards":[{"id":"L00937","dok":1,"q":"Which three types of muscle tissue are found in the body?","a":"Skeletal, cardiac, and smooth muscle.","options":["Striated, voluntary, and involuntary","Fast, slow, and intermediate","Skeletal, cardiac, and smooth","Skeletal, visceral, and connective"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Muscle tissue comes in three types, and each is named for where it is found or how it looks: skeletal muscle attaches to the skeleton, cardiac muscle forms the heart wall, and smooth muscle lines the walls of hollow organs and blood vessels. All three can contract, but they differ in striations, control, and cell shape, and those differences are how you tell them apart on a slide.","notes":["Striated, voluntary, and involuntary are words used to describe and compare the tissues, not the names of the tissues. They would fit a question asking how skeletal muscle (striated and voluntary) differs from smooth muscle (nonstriated and involuntary).","Fast, slow, and intermediate describe how quickly individual skeletal muscle fibers contract, so they sort fibers within one tissue rather than naming tissue types. Students choose this when they mix up fiber speed categories with tissue categories.","Correct. Skeletal, cardiac, and smooth are the three muscle tissue types.","Visceral is an older name for smooth muscle in organ walls, but connective tissue is a separate primary tissue, not a muscle. Connective tissue would be the answer if the question asked which tissue forms the sheaths and tendons around a muscle."],"lt":"t-muscle-types","lid":"c1","comp":77,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-tissue-types"},{"id":"L00938","dok":1,"q":"Which tissue is attached to the skeleton and is described as striated and voluntary?","a":"Skeletal muscle.","options":["Cardiac muscle","Smooth muscle","Skeletal muscle","Dense regular connective tissue"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Skeletal muscle is the muscle tissue attached to the bones of the skeleton. Its fibers show striations, the light and dark stripes made by lined-up contractile proteins, and it is voluntary, meaning you can contract it on purpose. Those two features, striated and voluntary, are the hallmarks of skeletal muscle.","notes":["Cardiac muscle is striated like skeletal muscle, but it is involuntary and found only in the heart wall. Pick it if the question describes striated muscle that contracts without conscious control.","Smooth muscle has no striations and is involuntary, and it lines hollow organs and blood vessels rather than attaching to bone. It would be the answer for the muscle layer in the stomach or bladder wall.","Correct. Skeletal muscle is attached to the skeleton and is striated and voluntary.","Dense regular connective tissue does attach to bone, because it forms tendons and ligaments, but it cannot contract and has no striations. Pick it if the question asks what tissue makes up a tendon."],"lt":"t-muscle-types","lid":"c2","comp":77,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-tissue-types"},{"id":"L00939","dok":1,"q":"Which pair of features describes skeletal muscle tissue?","a":"Striated and voluntary.","options":["Nonstriated and voluntary","Striated and voluntary","Striated and involuntary","Nonstriated and involuntary"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Skeletal muscle is striated, meaning its fibers show alternating light and dark bands under the microscope, and it is voluntary, meaning it contracts when you consciously decide to move. Comparing the muscle types by these two features is the fastest way to sort them: skeletal is striated and voluntary, cardiac is striated and involuntary, and smooth is nonstriated and involuntary.","notes":["No muscle tissue is both nonstriated and voluntary. Students land on this when they remember that skeletal muscle is voluntary but forget that it is also the most obviously striated tissue.","Correct. Skeletal muscle is striated and under voluntary control.","Striated and involuntary describes cardiac muscle, which has stripes but beats without conscious control. Pick this pair if the question is about the heart wall.","Nonstriated and involuntary describes smooth muscle in the walls of hollow organs and blood vessels. Pick this pair if the question asks about the muscle of the stomach or bladder."],"lt":"t-muscle-types","lid":"c3","comp":77,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-tissue-types"},{"id":"L00940","dok":1,"q":"Which tissue makes up the bulk of the heart wall?","a":"Cardiac muscle.","options":["Smooth muscle","Elastic connective tissue","Cardiac muscle","Skeletal muscle"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The thick middle layer of the heart wall, the myocardium, is made of cardiac muscle, and it makes up most of the wall's thickness. Cardiac muscle is found only in the heart. It is striated like skeletal muscle but involuntary, so the heart keeps beating without conscious effort.","notes":["Smooth muscle lines the walls of hollow organs and blood vessels, including the coronary arteries that supply the heart, but it does not form the heart wall itself. Pick it for the muscle layer of a blood vessel or the stomach.","Elastic connective tissue is rich in stretchy elastin fibers and is found in the walls of large arteries such as the aorta, where it lets the wall stretch and recoil. It would be the answer for what allows the aorta to stretch, not for the contracting heart wall.","Correct. Cardiac muscle forms the myocardium, the bulk of the heart wall.","Skeletal muscle attaches to the skeleton and is under voluntary control, so it is not found in the heart wall. Pick it if the question asks about the muscles that move the bones of the limbs."],"lt":"t-muscle-types","lid":"c4","comp":77,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-tissue-types"},{"id":"L00943","dok":1,"q":"Which tissue forms the contracting layer in the walls of hollow organs such as the stomach and urinary bladder, and in the walls of blood vessels?","a":"Smooth muscle.","options":["Dense regular connective tissue","Smooth muscle","Skeletal muscle","Cardiac muscle"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Smooth muscle lines the walls of hollow organs and blood vessels, where it squeezes contents along or changes the size of the opening. It is called smooth because it has no striations, and it is involuntary, so it works without conscious control. When you see muscle in the wall of the stomach, bladder, or an artery, think smooth muscle.","notes":["Dense regular connective tissue is made of tightly packed parallel collagen fibers and forms tendons and ligaments, but it cannot contract. Pick it if the question asks what tissue attaches a muscle to a bone.","Correct. Smooth muscle forms the contracting layer of hollow organs and blood vessels.","Skeletal muscle attaches to bones and is voluntary, so it does not form the walls of these organs. Pick it for a question about the muscles that move the limbs.","Cardiac muscle is found only in the heart wall, not in the stomach, bladder, or blood vessels. Pick it if the question asks about the myocardium."],"lt":"t-muscle-types","lid":"c7","comp":77,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-tissue-types"},{"id":"L00944","dok":1,"q":"What is the characteristic shape of a smooth muscle cell?","a":"Spindle shaped, tapering at both ends.","options":["Long and cylindrical","Flat and scale-like","Short and branched","Spindle shaped"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"A smooth muscle cell is spindle shaped, meaning it is widest in the middle where its single nucleus sits and tapers to a point at each end. These cells pack together in sheets in the walls of hollow organs and blood vessels. The spindle shape and the lack of striations are the two features that identify smooth muscle on a slide.","notes":["Long and cylindrical describes a skeletal muscle fiber, which can run much of the length of the muscle. Pick this shape for a question about skeletal muscle cells.","Flat and scale-like describes squamous epithelial cells, such as those lining the air sacs of the lungs. Students choose it when they mix up epithelial cell shapes with muscle cell shapes.","Short and branched describes cardiac muscle cells, which branch and join their neighbors at intercalated discs. Pick this shape for a question about the heart wall.","Correct. Smooth muscle cells are spindle shaped and taper at both ends."],"lt":"t-muscle-types","lid":"c8","comp":77,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-tissue-types"},{"id":"L00945","dok":1,"q":"Under the microscope, which muscle tissue shows no striations?","a":"Smooth muscle.","options":["Smooth muscle","Skeletal muscle","Cardiac muscle","Both skeletal and cardiac muscle"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Striations are the alternating light and dark bands made when the contractile proteins inside muscle cells line up in repeating units. In smooth muscle those proteins are not arranged in neat rows, so the cells look smooth and even under the microscope. That is where the name smooth muscle comes from.","notes":["Correct. Smooth muscle lacks striations.","Skeletal muscle is the most obviously striated tissue, with bold light and dark bands across every fiber. Pick it if the question asks which muscle is striated and voluntary.","Cardiac muscle is striated, even though it is involuntary. Pick it if the question asks which muscle is striated but not under conscious control.","Skeletal and cardiac muscle are the two striated tissues, so this pairing names exactly the muscles that do have stripes. It would be the answer if the question asked which muscle tissues are striated."],"lt":"t-muscle-types","lid":"c9","comp":77,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-tissue-types"},{"id":"L00946","dok":1,"q":"How many nuclei does a smooth muscle cell have, and where do they sit?","a":"One nucleus in the center of the cell.","options":["One nucleus in the center of the cell","Two nuclei, one near each tapered end","Many nuclei pushed to the edge of the cell","One nucleus pushed against the edge of the cell"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Each smooth muscle cell has a single nucleus located in the center of the cell, in the widest part of the spindle. This sets it apart from a skeletal muscle fiber, which has many nuclei pushed to the edge. Cardiac cells also have one central nucleus, so to separate cardiac from smooth muscle you look for striations and intercalated discs.","notes":["Correct. A smooth muscle cell has one nucleus in its center.","No muscle cell places its nuclei at its tips. Students pick this when they picture the tapered spindle shape and guess that something must sit at each end.","Many nuclei pushed to the edge describes a skeletal muscle fiber, which forms when many cells fuse during development. Pick this answer for a question about skeletal muscle nuclei.","One nucleus pushed against the cell edge is how a fat cell (adipocyte) looks, because its large fat droplet pushes the nucleus aside. Pick it for a question about adipose tissue, not smooth muscle."],"lt":"t-muscle-types","lid":"c10","comp":77,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-tissue-types"},{"id":"L00947","dok":1,"q":"Which muscle property is the ability to receive and respond to a stimulus?","a":"Excitability.","options":["Contractility","Elasticity","Excitability","Extensibility"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Excitability, also called responsiveness, is a muscle cell's ability to detect a stimulus, such as a signal from a nerve, and respond to it. It is the first step in every contraction, because a muscle cannot shorten until it has responded to the signal telling it to. Nerve cells share this property, which is why it does not by itself set muscle apart from other tissues.","notes":["Contractility is the ability to shorten forcibly when stimulated, which is the step that follows the response to the stimulus. Pick it for a question about the property that sets muscle apart from all other tissues.","Elasticity is the ability to recoil back to resting length after being stretched. Pick it for a question about a muscle springing back after a stretch.","Correct. Excitability is the ability to receive and respond to a stimulus.","Extensibility is the ability to be stretched beyond resting length, as when one muscle is pulled long by the contraction of its opposing muscle. Pick it for a question about stretching."],"lt":"t-muscle-types","lid":"c11"},{"id":"L00948","dok":1,"q":"Which muscle property is the ability to shorten forcibly when stimulated?","a":"Contractility.","options":["Extensibility","Elasticity","Excitability","Contractility"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Contractility is the ability of a muscle cell to shorten with force once it has been stimulated. This shortening is what pulls on tendons and bones to move the body, squeezes blood out of the heart, and narrows hollow organs. Contractility is the property that makes muscle tissue different from every other tissue.","notes":["Extensibility is the ability to be stretched, which is the opposite of shortening. Pick it if the question asks how a muscle can be pulled beyond its resting length.","Elasticity is the ability to recoil to resting length after a stretch. It returns the muscle to its normal length rather than shortening it forcibly, so pick it for a question about springing back.","Excitability is the ability to receive and respond to a stimulus, which comes before the shortening. Pick it for a question about how a muscle detects the signal to contract.","Correct. Contractility is the ability to shorten forcibly."],"lt":"t-muscle-types","lid":"c12"},{"id":"L00949","dok":1,"q":"Which muscle property is the ability to be stretched beyond resting length?","a":"Extensibility.","options":["Contractility","Excitability","Elasticity","Extensibility"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Extensibility is the ability of a muscle to be stretched, or extended, beyond its resting length. This happens every time one muscle contracts and pulls its opposing muscle long, such as the triceps brachii being stretched when the biceps brachii bends the elbow. Extensibility lets muscles in opposing pairs work across a joint without tearing.","notes":["Contractility is the ability to shorten forcibly, which is the opposite of being stretched. Pick it for the property that sets muscle apart from all other tissues.","Excitability is the ability to receive and respond to a stimulus. Pick it for a question about how a muscle detects the signal to contract.","Elasticity is the ability to recoil to resting length after a stretch, so it describes what happens after the stretch ends. Students mix the two up because both words deal with stretching, so pick elasticity only when the question is about springing back.","Correct. Extensibility is the ability to be stretched."],"lt":"t-muscle-types","lid":"c13"},{"id":"L00950","dok":1,"q":"Which muscle property lets a muscle recoil and return to its resting length after being stretched?","a":"Elasticity.","options":["Contractility","Elasticity","Excitability","Extensibility"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Elasticity is the ability of a muscle to recoil and resume its resting length once a stretching force is removed, much like a rubber band snapping back. It works as the partner of extensibility: extensibility allows the stretch, and elasticity brings the muscle back. Together they keep muscles from staying overstretched after movement.","notes":["Contractility is the ability to shorten forcibly when stimulated. Returning to resting length after a stretch is a passive recoil, not a forceful contraction, so pick contractility only for a question about active shortening.","Correct. Elasticity is the ability to recoil to resting length.","Excitability is the ability to receive and respond to a stimulus. Pick it for a question about the first step in starting a contraction.","Extensibility is the ability to be stretched in the first place, so it describes the stretch rather than the return. Pick it for a question about how a muscle can be pulled beyond resting length."],"lt":"t-muscle-types","lid":"c14"},{"id":"L00951","dok":1,"q":"Which list names the four functional properties of muscle tissue?","a":"Excitability, contractility, extensibility, and elasticity.","options":["Contractility, elasticity, absorption, and secretion","Excitability, conductivity, contractility, and secretion","Excitability, contractility, extensibility, and elasticity","Movement, posture, joint stability, and heat production"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The four functional properties describe what muscle cells are able to do. Excitability is responding to a stimulus, contractility is shortening forcibly, extensibility is being stretched, and elasticity is recoiling to resting length. Properties describe the abilities of the tissue, while functions describe the jobs muscle does for the body, such as movement and heat production.","notes":["Absorption and secretion are functions of epithelial tissue, such as the lining of the small intestine. This list mixes two real muscle properties with two epithelial functions.","Conductivity is a property of neurons, which carry signals along their length, and secretion is a job of glandular epithelium. Students choose this list when they blend the properties of nervous and epithelial tissue into muscle.","Correct. Excitability, contractility, extensibility, and elasticity are the four functional properties.","These are the four functions of muscle tissue, the jobs it performs for the body. Pick this list if the question asks what muscle does for the body rather than what abilities its cells have."],"lt":"t-muscle-types","lid":"c15"},{"id":"L00952","dok":1,"q":"Which list names the four general functions of muscle tissue?","a":"Movement, posture, joint stability, and heat production.","options":["Movement, absorption, secretion, and filtration","Movement, protection, blood cell formation, and mineral storage","Movement, posture, joint stability, and heat production","Excitability, contractility, extensibility, and elasticity"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Muscle tissue does four general jobs for the body. It produces movement, maintains posture and body position, stabilizes joints, and generates heat as a byproduct of contraction. These functions all come from muscle's ability to contract and pull, whether that pull moves a bone, holds the body upright, or simply keeps a joint firm.","notes":["Absorption, secretion, and filtration are functions of epithelial tissue, such as the lining of the gut and kidney tubules. Students pick this list when they confuse tissue functions across the four primary tissues.","Protection, blood cell formation, and mineral storage are functions of the skeletal system. Pick this list for a question about what bones do for the body.","Correct. Movement, posture, joint stability, and heat production are the four general functions of muscle.","These are the four functional properties of muscle cells, the abilities that make contraction possible. Pick this list if the question asks about properties such as responding to a stimulus or recoiling after a stretch."],"lt":"t-muscle-types","lid":"c16"},{"id":"L00953","dok":1,"q":"Which muscle function keeps the body upright and holds its position, even while you are sitting or standing still?","a":"Maintenance of posture and body position.","options":["Generation of heat","Maintenance of posture and body position","Production of movement","Stabilization of joints"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Maintaining posture means muscles are working constantly, making small adjustments that hold the body upright against the pull of gravity. This happens even when you seem to be still, such as when sitting or standing. Muscles of the back, neck, and legs are especially busy with this job.","notes":["Generation of heat is a byproduct of muscle contraction that helps keep body temperature normal. Pick it for a question about shivering or warming the body.","Correct. Maintaining posture keeps the body upright and holds its position.","Production of movement is the function used when muscles pull on bones to walk, lift, or reach. Pick it when the question describes visible motion of a body part.","Stabilization of joints is the function where muscles and their tendons hold the bones of a joint firmly together. Pick it if the question describes muscles keeping a particular joint, such as the shoulder, from slipping out of place."],"lt":"t-muscle-types","lid":"c17"},{"id":"L00954","dok":1,"q":"Which muscle function is a byproduct of contraction that helps keep body temperature near 37 C (98.6 F)?","a":"Generation of heat.","options":["Generation of heat","Stabilization of joints","Maintenance of posture","Production of movement"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Every time muscle cells contract, they use energy, and much of that energy is released as heat. Because skeletal muscle makes up a large share of body mass, this heat is a major source of warmth that helps keep body temperature near 37 C (98.6 F). Shivering is the body using rapid contractions on purpose to produce extra heat.","notes":["Correct. Heat is a byproduct of contraction that helps maintain body temperature.","Stabilization of joints is the function where muscles and tendons hold a joint's bones firmly together. Pick it if the question describes keeping a joint steady during weight bearing.","Maintenance of posture is the steady muscle work that holds the body upright against gravity. Pick it for a question about sitting or standing still.","Production of movement is the main purpose of contraction, such as moving a limb, not a byproduct of it. Pick it when the question describes walking, lifting, or other visible motion."],"lt":"t-muscle-types","lid":"c18"},{"id":"L00955","dok":2,"q":"A tissue is striated, contracts without conscious control, and has cells joined by intercalated discs. Which tissue is it?","a":"Cardiac muscle.","options":["Cardiac muscle","Smooth muscle","Nervous tissue","Skeletal muscle"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Cardiac muscle is the only muscle tissue that is striated but involuntary, and it is the only one with intercalated discs, the junctions that join its branching cells end to end. Any one of those intercalated discs on a slide is enough to identify the tissue as cardiac muscle. It is found only in the heart wall.","notes":["Correct. Striated, involuntary, and joined by intercalated discs describes cardiac muscle.","Smooth muscle is involuntary, but it has no striations and no intercalated discs. Pick it if the description says nonstriated cells in the wall of a hollow organ.","Nervous tissue is made of neurons and supporting neuroglia, and it carries signals rather than contracting. Pick it if the description mentions cells with long branching processes such as axons and dendrites.","Skeletal muscle is striated, but it is voluntary and has no intercalated discs. Pick it if the description says striated and under conscious control."],"lt":"t-muscle-types","lid":"c19","comp":77,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-tissue-types"},{"id":"L00956","dok":2,"q":"Cardiac and smooth muscle are both involuntary, and each of their cells has one central nucleus. Which feature does cardiac muscle have that smooth muscle lacks?","a":"Striations.","options":["Striations","Spindle-shaped cells","One central nucleus","Involuntary control"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Cardiac muscle and smooth muscle share involuntary control and a single central nucleus per cell, so those features cannot tell them apart. Cardiac muscle, however, is striated, with the same light and dark banding seen in skeletal muscle, while smooth muscle has no striations at all. Striations, along with intercalated discs, are how you separate cardiac from smooth muscle on a slide.","notes":["Correct. Cardiac muscle is striated, while smooth muscle is not.","Spindle-shaped cells belong to smooth muscle, not cardiac muscle, whose cells are short and branched. Pick this feature if the question asks what smooth muscle has that cardiac muscle lacks.","Both cardiac and smooth muscle cells have one central nucleus, so this feature does not separate them. A single central nucleus would help only if the question asked how either one differs from skeletal muscle, which has many nuclei at the edge.","Both cardiac and smooth muscle are involuntary, so this is a shared feature. Pick involuntary control if the question asks how either tissue differs from skeletal muscle."],"lt":"t-muscle-types","lid":"c20","comp":77,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-tissue-types"},{"id":"L00957","dok":2,"q":"Which muscle tissue type has the longest cells?","a":"Skeletal muscle.","options":["Cardiac muscle","Skeletal muscle","All three have cells of similar length","Smooth muscle"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Skeletal muscle fibers are long, cylindrical cells that form when many embryonic cells fuse together, which is also why each fiber has many nuclei. A single fiber can run a long way along the length of a muscle. Cardiac and smooth muscle cells are much shorter, each with a single nucleus.","notes":["Cardiac muscle cells are short and branched, joined end to end by intercalated discs. Pick cardiac muscle for a question about branching cells, not long ones.","Correct. Skeletal muscle has the longest cells of the three muscle types.","The three types differ a great deal in cell size and shape, which is one of the ways you tell them apart. Students choose this when they assume all muscle cells look alike because they all contract.","Smooth muscle cells are spindle shaped and short compared with skeletal fibers. Pick smooth muscle for a question about tapered cells with one central nucleus."],"lt":"t-muscle-types","lid":"c21","comp":77,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-tissue-types"},{"id":"L00958","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares smooth muscle with skeletal muscle?","a":"Smooth muscle is nonstriated and involuntary, while skeletal muscle is striated and voluntary.","options":["Smooth muscle is nonstriated and involuntary, while skeletal muscle is striated and voluntary","Smooth muscle is striated and involuntary, while skeletal muscle is nonstriated and voluntary","Both are voluntary, but only smooth muscle lacks striations","Both are striated, but only skeletal muscle is under voluntary control"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Skeletal and smooth muscle differ on both features you use to sort muscle tissue. Skeletal muscle is striated, with bold light and dark bands, and voluntary, so you control it on purpose. Smooth muscle has no striations and is involuntary, working in the walls of hollow organs and blood vessels without conscious effort.","notes":["Correct. Smooth muscle is nonstriated and involuntary, and skeletal muscle is striated and voluntary.","This reverses the striations. Striated and involuntary actually describes cardiac muscle, so pick that description for a question about the heart wall.","Smooth muscle is not voluntary; you cannot consciously squeeze your stomach or tighten a blood vessel. The part about smooth muscle lacking striations is true, which is what makes this choice tempting.","Smooth muscle is not striated. This statement would be correct if it compared skeletal muscle with cardiac muscle, since both of those are striated and only skeletal is voluntary."],"lt":"t-muscle-types","lid":"c22","comp":77,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-tissue-types"},{"id":"L00959","dok":1,"q":"The word roots myo and mys, as in myocardium and epimysium, refer to what?","a":"Muscle.","options":["Flesh","Muscle","Cell","Fiber"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Myo and mys both come from the Greek word for muscle. You see them throughout the muscle chapter: myocardium is the muscle layer of the heart, and epimysium, perimysium, and endomysium are the sheaths around and within a muscle. Recognizing the root tells you a term has something to do with muscle before you know its full meaning.","notes":["Flesh is the meaning of the prefix sarco, as in sarcolemma and sarcoplasm. Pick flesh for a question about the sarco prefix.","Correct. Myo and mys both mean muscle.","Cell is the meaning of the root cyto, as in cytoplasm. Students pick this because many muscle terms describe parts of a muscle cell.","Fiber is the meaning of the root fibr, as in fibroblast and fibrocartilage. It is tempting because skeletal muscle cells are called muscle fibers, but myo itself means muscle."],"lt":"t-muscle-types","lid":"c23"},{"id":"L00961","dok":2,"q":"Before a muscle can shorten, it must first detect the signal telling it to contract. Which property makes this first step possible?","a":"Excitability.","options":["Elasticity","Contractility","Extensibility","Excitability"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Excitability is the ability to receive and respond to a stimulus, such as a signal arriving from a nerve. It has to come first, because a muscle cell cannot shorten until it has responded to the signal. Contractility, the shortening itself, follows only after excitability has done its job.","notes":["Elasticity is the ability to recoil to resting length after a stretch, which happens after the muscle has been stretched. Pick it for a question about springing back.","Contractility is the shortening that follows the stimulus, so it is the second step, not the first. Pick it for a question about the property that sets muscle apart from other tissues.","Extensibility is the ability to be stretched beyond resting length. Pick it for a question about how a muscle lengthens when its opposing muscle contracts.","Correct. Excitability lets the muscle detect and respond to the stimulus."],"lt":"t-muscle-types","lid":"c25"},{"id":"L00962","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares extensibility and elasticity?","a":"Extensibility lets a muscle be stretched, and elasticity lets it recoil to resting length.","options":["Both terms mean the same ability, the ability to be stretched","Extensibility lets a muscle be stretched, and elasticity lets it recoil to resting length","Extensibility lets a muscle shorten forcibly, and elasticity lets it respond to a stimulus","Elasticity lets a muscle be stretched, and extensibility lets it recoil to resting length"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Extensibility and elasticity are partners. Extensibility is the ability to be stretched, or extended, beyond resting length, and elasticity is the ability to recoil back to resting length once the stretch is released. A useful memory link is that extensibility sounds like extend, and elasticity works like an elastic band snapping back.","notes":["The two terms describe different abilities: one allows the stretch and the other allows the return. Students choose this because both words deal with stretching and sound alike.","Correct. Extensibility allows the stretch, and elasticity allows the recoil.","Shortening forcibly is contractility, and responding to a stimulus is excitability. Pick those terms for questions about the active steps of contraction rather than stretching.","This swaps the two definitions. Linking extensibility with extend helps keep them straight."],"lt":"t-muscle-types","lid":"c26"},{"id":"L00963","dok":2,"q":"Which match of muscle tissue to cell shape is correct?","a":"Cardiac muscle has short, branching cells.","options":["Skeletal muscle: short, branching cells","Cardiac muscle: spindle-shaped cells","Cardiac muscle: short, branching cells","Smooth muscle: long, cylindrical cells"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Each muscle tissue has its own cell shape. Cardiac muscle cells are short and branched, joined to their neighbors at intercalated discs so they form a connected network in the heart wall. Skeletal muscle fibers are long and cylindrical, and smooth muscle cells are spindle shaped, tapering at both ends.","notes":["Skeletal muscle fibers are long and cylindrical with many nuclei at the edge. Short, branching cells belong to cardiac muscle, so pick skeletal muscle only for long cylindrical fibers.","Spindle-shaped cells belong to smooth muscle, not cardiac. Pick this shape for a question about the muscle in the wall of a hollow organ or blood vessel.","Correct. Cardiac muscle cells are short and branched.","Smooth muscle cells are spindle shaped with one central nucleus. Long, cylindrical cells belong to skeletal muscle."],"lt":"t-muscle-types","lid":"c27","comp":77,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-tissue-types"},{"id":"L00964","dok":2,"q":"Which muscle function makes walking, picking up objects, pumping blood, and pushing food through the gut possible?","a":"Production of movement.","options":["Generation of heat","Stabilization of joints","Maintenance of posture","Production of movement"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Production of movement is the main job of all three muscle types. Skeletal muscle moves the body by pulling on bones, cardiac muscle moves blood by pumping it through the heart, and smooth muscle moves contents such as food along the digestive tract. Each example in the question involves something being moved from one place to another.","notes":["Generation of heat is a byproduct of contraction that helps maintain body temperature. Pick it for a question about shivering.","Stabilization of joints holds the bones of a joint firmly in place rather than moving anything. Pick it if the question describes muscles keeping a joint steady.","Maintenance of posture is the steady muscle work that holds the body upright. Pick it for a question about sitting or standing still against gravity.","Correct. Producing movement covers locomotion, handling objects, and moving blood and digestive contents."],"lt":"t-muscle-types","lid":"c28"},{"id":"L00965","dok":2,"q":"A muscle is stretched beyond its resting length and then springs back once it is released. Which two properties are at work, in order?","a":"Extensibility, then elasticity.","options":["Extensibility, then elasticity","Contractility, then extensibility","Excitability, then contractility","Elasticity, then extensibility"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The stretch comes first, and that depends on extensibility, the ability to be pulled beyond resting length. The return comes second, and that depends on elasticity, the ability to recoil to resting length once the stretching force is gone. These two properties always pair up whenever a muscle is stretched and released.","notes":["Correct. Extensibility allows the stretch, then elasticity allows the recoil.","Contractility is active shortening, which is the opposite of being stretched. Students choose this pair when they confuse a muscle being pulled long with a muscle contracting.","Excitability then contractility is the sequence for an active contraction: the muscle responds to a stimulus and then shortens. Pick this pair for a question about what happens when a nerve signal arrives.","This reverses the order. The muscle must be stretched first (extensibility) before it can spring back (elasticity)."],"lt":"t-muscle-types","lid":"c29"},{"id":"L00967","dok":3,"q":"A histology slide shows short, branched, striated cells joined end to end by dark bands, with one central nucleus in each cell. Which tissue is on the slide, and what are the dark bands?","a":"Cardiac muscle, and the dark bands are intercalated discs.","options":["Cardiac muscle, and the bands are intercalated discs","Skeletal muscle, and the bands are Z discs","Smooth muscle, and the bands are terminal cisterns","Skeletal muscle, and the bands are intercalated discs"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Short, branched, striated cells with a single central nucleus describe cardiac muscle. The dark bands crossing the fibers where one cell meets the next are intercalated discs, junctions that hold the cells together with desmosomes and pass the electrical signal through gap junctions. Intercalated discs are found only in cardiac muscle, so they confirm the identification.","notes":["Correct. The tissue is cardiac muscle, and the dark end bands are intercalated discs.","Skeletal muscle fibers are long and cylindrical with many nuclei at the edge, not short and branched. Z discs are thin lines inside every sarcomere rather than thick bands where cells meet, so pick them for a question about the borders of a sarcomere.","Smooth muscle has no striations, and terminal cisterns are tiny sacs of the sarcoplasmic reticulum that cannot be seen as bands with a light microscope. Pick smooth muscle for nonstriated spindle-shaped cells.","The dark bands are intercalated discs, but skeletal muscle does not have them, because its fibers are single long cells rather than chains of short cells. Pick skeletal muscle only for long fibers with many nuclei at the edge."],"lt":"t-muscle-types","lid":"c31","comp":77,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-tissue-types"},{"id":"L00968","dok":2,"q":"A tissue in the wall of a blood vessel is nonstriated, contracts slowly and steadily, and is not under conscious control. Which tissue is it?","a":"Smooth muscle.","options":["Smooth muscle","Cardiac muscle","Elastic connective tissue","Skeletal muscle"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Smooth muscle forms the middle layer of blood vessel walls, where it contracts slowly and can hold that contraction for a long time to narrow or widen the vessel. It has no striations and works involuntarily, so you cannot consciously control it. Every clue in the description points to smooth muscle.","notes":["Correct. Nonstriated, slow, involuntary muscle in a vessel wall is smooth muscle.","Cardiac muscle is involuntary but striated, and it is found only in the heart wall. Pick it for striated involuntary muscle with intercalated discs.","Elastic connective tissue is also found in the walls of large arteries, where it lets the wall stretch and recoil, but it cannot contract. Pick it if the question asks what lets the aorta stretch with each heartbeat.","Skeletal muscle is striated and voluntary, and it attaches to bones rather than forming vessel walls. Pick it for a description of striated muscle you control on purpose."],"lt":"t-muscle-types","lid":"c32","comp":77,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-tissue-types"},{"id":"L00969","dok":2,"q":"Nervous tissue can also receive and respond to a stimulus. Which property is unique to muscle tissue and sets it apart from all other tissues?","a":"Contractility.","options":["Excitability","Elasticity","Contractility","Extensibility"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Contractility is the ability to shorten forcibly when stimulated, and no other tissue can do this. Excitability is shared with nervous tissue, and stretching and recoiling are shared with tissues such as elastic connective tissue. Only muscle can actively pull, which is why contractility is the defining property of muscle.","notes":["Excitability is shared with neurons, which also receive and respond to stimuli, so it cannot be what sets muscle apart. Pick excitability for a question about the first step before a contraction.","Elasticity, the ability to recoil after a stretch, is also a feature of elastic connective tissue in the skin, lungs, and artery walls. Pick it for a question about a muscle springing back to resting length.","Correct. Contractility is the property found only in muscle tissue.","Extensibility, the ability to be stretched, is shared with other soft tissues such as skin. Pick it for a question about a muscle being pulled long by its opposing muscle."],"lt":"t-muscle-types","lid":"c33"},{"id":"L00970","dok":2,"q":"A hiker caught in the cold begins to shiver, and the shivering warms her body. Which muscle function does shivering show?","a":"Generation of heat.","options":["Maintenance of posture","Production of movement","Generation of heat","Stabilization of joints"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Muscle contraction always releases heat as a byproduct, because much of the energy used by working muscle cells escapes as warmth. Shivering is rapid, repeated contraction of skeletal muscles that the body uses specifically to produce extra heat. That heat helps bring body temperature back toward 37 C (98.6 F).","notes":["Maintenance of posture is the steady muscle work that holds the body upright against gravity. Pick it for a question about sitting or standing still.","Shivering does involve small movements, but their purpose is not to move the body from place to place. Pick production of movement for walking, lifting, or reaching.","Correct. Shivering uses muscle contraction to generate heat.","Stabilization of joints means muscles hold the bones of a joint firmly together. Pick it for a question about keeping a joint such as the shoulder from slipping out of place."],"lt":"t-muscle-types","lid":"c34"},{"id":"L00971","dok":3,"q":"Two striated muscle tissues are compared on slides. Tissue 1 is voluntary, with many nuclei at the edge of each fiber. Tissue 2 is involuntary, with cells joined by intercalated discs. Which identification is correct?","a":"Tissue 1 is skeletal muscle, and Tissue 2 is cardiac muscle.","options":["Tissue 1 is skeletal muscle, and Tissue 2 is smooth muscle","Tissue 1 is cardiac muscle, and Tissue 2 is skeletal muscle","Tissue 1 is smooth muscle, and Tissue 2 is cardiac muscle","Tissue 1 is skeletal muscle, and Tissue 2 is cardiac muscle"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Only skeletal and cardiac muscle are striated, so both slides must be one of those two. Skeletal muscle is voluntary and its long fibers carry many nuclei pushed to the edge, which matches Tissue 1. Cardiac muscle is involuntary and its cells are joined by intercalated discs, which matches Tissue 2.","notes":["Tissue 1 is skeletal muscle, but smooth muscle has no striations and no intercalated discs, so it cannot be Tissue 2. Pick smooth muscle for nonstriated spindle-shaped cells.","This swaps the two. Cardiac muscle is involuntary with intercalated discs, and skeletal muscle is voluntary with many nuclei at the edge.","Tissue 2 is cardiac muscle, but smooth muscle is neither striated nor voluntary and has one central nucleus, so it cannot be Tissue 1.","Correct. The voluntary tissue with peripheral nuclei is skeletal muscle, and the involuntary tissue with intercalated discs is cardiac muscle."],"lt":"t-muscle-types","lid":"c35","comp":77,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-tissue-types"},{"id":"L00972","dok":2,"q":"While you carry a heavy bag, the rotator cuff muscles pull the head of the humerus snugly into its socket so the shoulder does not slip out of place. Which muscle function is this?","a":"Stabilization of joints.","options":["Generation of heat","Production of movement","Stabilization of joints","Maintenance of posture"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Stabilizing a joint means muscles and their tendons hold the bones of that joint firmly together, often without producing any visible motion. The shoulder joint is very mobile but shallow, so it depends heavily on the rotator cuff muscles to keep the head of the humerus seated in its socket. Here the muscles are working to hold a joint steady, not to move it.","notes":["Generation of heat is a byproduct of all contraction, but it is not the purpose described here. Pick it for a question about shivering.","Production of movement means a muscle pulls a bone through a motion. In this example the arm is not being moved; the muscles are holding the joint still.","Correct. Holding the humerus in its socket is stabilization of the joint.","Maintenance of posture is about holding the whole body upright against gravity, mainly with muscles of the back, neck, and legs. Pick it for a question about sitting or standing still rather than securing one joint."],"lt":"t-muscle-types","lid":"c36"},{"id":"L00973","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares the cells of skeletal muscle and smooth muscle?","a":"Skeletal fibers are long and cylindrical with many nuclei, while smooth muscle cells are spindle shaped with one nucleus.","options":["Skeletal fibers are spindle shaped with one nucleus, while smooth muscle cells are long and cylindrical with many nuclei","Both are long and cylindrical, but only skeletal fibers have many nuclei","Skeletal fibers are long and cylindrical with many nuclei, while smooth muscle cells are spindle shaped with one nucleus","Skeletal fibers are short and branched with one nucleus, while smooth muscle cells are spindle shaped with many nuclei"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Skeletal muscle fibers are long, cylindrical cells that form when many embryonic cells fuse, which leaves each fiber with many nuclei pushed to its edge. Smooth muscle cells are much smaller and spindle shaped, tapering at both ends, with a single nucleus in the center. Cell shape and number of nuclei together make these two tissues easy to tell apart on a slide.","notes":["This swaps the two descriptions. Long and cylindrical with many nuclei belongs to skeletal muscle, and spindle shaped with one nucleus belongs to smooth muscle.","Smooth muscle cells are spindle shaped and tapered, not long cylinders. Only the part about skeletal fibers having many nuclei is accurate.","Correct. Skeletal fibers are long, cylindrical, and multinucleated, and smooth muscle cells are spindle shaped with one nucleus.","Short and branched describes cardiac muscle, not skeletal, and smooth muscle cells have one nucleus, not many. Pick short and branched for a question about heart muscle cells."],"lt":"t-muscle-types","lid":"c37","comp":77,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-tissue-types"},{"id":"L00974","dok":2,"q":"A slide shows long, cylindrical, striated cells, each with many nuclei pressed against the edge just under the cell membrane. Which tissue is it?","a":"Skeletal muscle.","options":["Cardiac muscle","Smooth muscle","Dense regular connective tissue","Skeletal muscle"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Long, cylindrical, striated fibers with many nuclei at the edge are the signature of skeletal muscle. Each fiber forms from many fused cells, which explains the many nuclei, and the nuclei sit just under the sarcolemma, the fiber's plasma membrane. No other muscle tissue has more than one or two nuclei per cell.","notes":["Cardiac muscle is striated, but its cells are short and branched with one central nucleus, and it has intercalated discs. Pick it for a slide showing branching striated cells.","Smooth muscle has no striations and its spindle-shaped cells each have one central nucleus. Pick it for a slide of nonstriated, tapered cells.","Dense regular connective tissue also shows long parallel strands at low power, but they are collagen fibers with flattened fibroblast nuclei squeezed between them, and there are no striations. Pick it for a slide of a tendon or ligament.","Correct. Long cylindrical striated fibers with many peripheral nuclei are skeletal muscle."],"lt":"t-muscle-types","lid":"c38","comp":77,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-tissue-types"},{"id":"L00975","dok":1,"q":"What is the thick, fleshy middle region of a skeletal muscle called?","a":"The belly.","options":["The belly","The tendon","The origin","The insertion"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The belly, also called the body, is the thick, fleshy middle portion of a muscle that lies between its two attachments. It is where most of the muscle fibers are and where the muscle bulges when it contracts. The tendons at each end attach the belly to bone.","notes":["Correct. The belly is the fleshy middle of the muscle.","The tendon is the cord of dense regular connective tissue at the end of a muscle that attaches it to bone. Pick it for a question about what anchors a muscle to bone, not its fleshy middle.","The origin is the attachment of a muscle to the more stationary bone, at one end of the muscle. Pick it for a question about which attachment stays still during contraction.","The insertion is the attachment of a muscle to the more movable bone, at the other end from the origin. Pick it for a question about which attachment is pulled toward the other."],"lt":"t-muscle-gross","lid":"c1"},{"id":"L00976","dok":1,"q":"In general, what blood and nerve supply serves a single skeletal muscle?","a":"One nerve, one artery, and one or more veins.","options":["Several nerves and one artery, with no veins","One artery and one or more veins, with no nerve","One nerve and one vein, with no artery of its own","One nerve, one artery, and one or more veins"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"As a general rule, each skeletal muscle is served by one nerve, one artery, and one or more veins. The nerve is needed because skeletal muscle is voluntary and contracts only when a nerve signal arrives. The artery and veins are needed because contracting muscle uses large amounts of oxygen and fuel and produces wastes that must be carried away.","notes":["Without veins, the blood delivered by the artery would have no way to leave the muscle, so wastes would build up. Students choose this when they forget that every tissue needs blood flowing both in and out.","Skeletal muscle is voluntary, so it contracts only when a nerve signal reaches it. A muscle with no nerve supply could not be moved on purpose, which is what happens when a nerve is cut.","A muscle cannot rely only on a vein, because veins carry blood away from tissues. Without an artery, no fresh oxygen-rich blood would reach the working fibers.","Correct. A typical skeletal muscle has one nerve, one artery, and one or more veins."],"lt":"t-muscle-gross","lid":"c2"},{"id":"L00981","dok":1,"q":"Which list gives the three connective tissue sheaths of a skeletal muscle in order from outside to inside?","a":"Epimysium, perimysium, endomysium.","options":["Epimysium, endomysium, perimysium","Endomysium, perimysium, epimysium","Epimysium, perimysium, endomysium","Perimysium, epimysium, endomysium"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The epimysium wraps the whole muscle on the outside, the perimysium wraps each fascicle (bundle of fibers) inside it, and the endomysium wraps each individual muscle fiber at the deepest level. The prefixes help: epi means upon, peri means around, and endo means within. All three are continuous with each other and with the tendon.","notes":["The endomysium wraps single fibers, the smallest units, so it must be the deepest layer. This order puts the endomysium in the middle, which mixes up the two inner layers.","This is the correct order read from inside to outside. Pick it if the question asks for the sheaths starting from a single fiber and moving outward.","Correct. From outside to inside, the order is epimysium, perimysium, endomysium.","The perimysium wraps fascicles inside the muscle, so it cannot be outside the epimysium, which wraps the whole muscle. This order mixes up the two outer layers."],"lt":"t-muscle-gross","lid":"c7","comp":72,"competencyId":"w4-ct-coverings"},{"id":"L00982","dok":1,"q":"Which of the three connective tissue sheaths surrounds the whole muscle?","a":"The epimysium.","options":["The epimysium","The endomysium","The perimysium","The aponeurosis"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The epimysium is the outermost sheath, a layer of dense irregular connective tissue that wraps the entire muscle. The prefix epi means upon, so it sits upon the outside of the muscle. At the ends of the muscle it blends with the other sheaths to form the tendon.","notes":["Correct. The epimysium surrounds the whole muscle.","The endomysium wraps each individual muscle fiber, the deepest level. Pick it for a question about the sheath around a single muscle cell.","The perimysium wraps each fascicle, a bundle of fibers inside the muscle. Pick it for a question about what surrounds a fascicle.","An aponeurosis is a broad, flat sheet that attaches a muscle to bone or to another muscle, not a sheath wrapping the muscle. Pick it for a question about the sheet-like attachment of a wide muscle such as an abdominal muscle."],"lt":"t-muscle-gross","lid":"c8","comp":72,"competencyId":"w4-ct-coverings"},{"id":"L00983","dok":1,"q":"Which tissue makes up the epimysium?","a":"Dense irregular connective tissue.","options":["Dense irregular connective tissue","Dense regular connective tissue","Adipose tissue","Areolar connective tissue"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The epimysium is made of dense irregular connective tissue, in which thick collagen fibers run in many different directions. That arrangement lets the epimysium resist pulling from any direction as the muscle contracts and changes shape. The perimysium is made of the same tissue type.","notes":["Correct. The epimysium is dense irregular connective tissue.","Dense regular connective tissue has collagen fibers running in parallel, which suits pulling in one direction. Pick it for a question about what makes up a tendon or ligament.","Adipose tissue stores fat, and it is found in the superficial fascia between the skin and the muscles. Pick it for a question about the subcutaneous layer.","Areolar connective tissue is loose and delicate, and it forms the fine endomysium around each individual fiber. Pick it for a question about the endomysium."],"lt":"t-muscle-gross","lid":"c9","comp":72,"competencyId":"w4-ct-coverings"},{"id":"L00984","dok":1,"q":"Which connective tissue sheath surrounds each fascicle?","a":"The perimysium.","options":["The endomysium","The epimysium","The periosteum","The perimysium"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"A fascicle is a bundle of muscle fibers, and each fascicle is wrapped in perimysium. The prefix peri means around, so the perimysium goes around each bundle. It is the middle of the three sheaths, between the epimysium outside and the endomysium inside.","notes":["The endomysium wraps each single muscle fiber inside a fascicle. Pick it for a question about the sheath around one muscle cell.","The epimysium wraps the whole muscle, not individual fascicles. Pick it for a question about the outermost sheath.","The periosteum is the connective tissue membrane covering the outer surface of a bone. Pick it for a question about what a direct muscle attachment fuses to, or what covers a bone.","Correct. The perimysium surrounds each fascicle."],"lt":"t-muscle-gross","lid":"c10","comp":72,"competencyId":"w4-ct-coverings"},{"id":"L00985","dok":1,"q":"What is a fascicle?","a":"A bundle of muscle fibers wrapped in perimysium.","options":["A contractile rod inside a muscle fiber","A single muscle cell wrapped in endomysium","A whole muscle wrapped in epimysium","A bundle of muscle fibers wrapped in perimysium"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"A fascicle is a bundle of muscle fibers held together by perimysium. Fascicles are the level between the whole muscle and the individual fiber, and they are large enough to see as the grain of a piece of meat. The way fascicles are arranged also gives each muscle its overall shape.","notes":["A contractile rod inside a muscle fiber is a myofibril, one level smaller than the fiber. Pick it for a question about the structures that fill most of the sarcoplasm.","A single muscle cell wrapped in endomysium is a muscle fiber. Pick this answer for a question about what the endomysium surrounds.","A whole muscle wrapped in epimysium is the muscle organ itself. Pick it for a question about the highest level of muscle organization.","Correct. A fascicle is a bundle of fibers wrapped in perimysium."],"lt":"t-muscle-gross","lid":"c11","comp":73,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-organization"},{"id":"L00986","dok":1,"q":"Which connective tissue sheath surrounds each individual muscle fiber?","a":"The endomysium.","options":["The sarcolemma","The perimysium","The epimysium","The endomysium"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The endomysium is the deepest of the three sheaths, a fine layer of areolar connective tissue wrapped around each single muscle fiber. The prefix endo means within, because this sheath lies within the fascicle. It holds the capillaries and nerve branches that reach each fiber.","notes":["The sarcolemma is the plasma membrane of the muscle fiber itself, part of the cell rather than a connective tissue sheath. The endomysium lies just outside it, so pick the sarcolemma for a question about the fiber's own cell membrane.","The perimysium wraps each fascicle, a bundle of many fibers. Pick it for a question about what surrounds a bundle.","The epimysium wraps the whole muscle on the outside. Pick it for a question about the outermost sheath.","Correct. The endomysium surrounds each individual muscle fiber."],"lt":"t-muscle-gross","lid":"c12","comp":72,"competencyId":"w4-ct-coverings"},{"id":"L00987","dok":1,"q":"Which tissue makes up the endomysium?","a":"A fine layer of areolar connective tissue.","options":["Dense regular connective tissue","Dense irregular connective tissue","Elastic connective tissue","Areolar connective tissue"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The endomysium is a fine, delicate sheath of areolar connective tissue with reticular fibers around each muscle fiber. Areolar tissue is loose, so it leaves room for the capillaries and nerve endings that must reach every fiber. The outer two sheaths are tougher dense irregular connective tissue.","notes":["Dense regular connective tissue, with parallel collagen fibers, forms tendons and ligaments. Pick it for a question about what makes up a tendon.","Dense irregular connective tissue forms the epimysium and perimysium, the two outer sheaths. Pick it for a question about either of those.","Elastic connective tissue is rich in elastin and found in the walls of large arteries and in the lungs. Pick it for a question about the tissue that lets the aorta stretch and recoil.","Correct. The endomysium is a fine layer of areolar connective tissue."],"lt":"t-muscle-gross","lid":"c13","comp":72,"competencyId":"w4-ct-coverings"},{"id":"L00988","dok":1,"q":"In a direct (fleshy) attachment, the epimysium of the muscle is fused to which structure?","a":"The periosteum of the bone.","options":["The joint capsule","An aponeurosis","A tendon","The periosteum of the bone"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"In a direct attachment, the muscle has no tendon in between. Its epimysium fuses straight onto the periosteum, the connective tissue membrane covering the bone (or onto the perichondrium if the muscle attaches to cartilage). Direct attachments are less common than indirect ones, which use a tendon or aponeurosis.","notes":["The joint capsule is the connective tissue sleeve that encloses a synovial joint. Pick it for a question about the parts of a synovial joint, not about how a muscle attaches to bone.","An aponeurosis is a broad, flat sheet used in an indirect attachment, not a direct one. Pick it for a question about how a wide flat muscle attaches.","A tendon is used in an indirect attachment, where the connective tissue extends beyond the muscle as a cord. Pick it for a question about indirect attachments.","Correct. In a direct attachment the epimysium fuses to the periosteum."],"lt":"t-muscle-gross","lid":"c14","comp":72,"competencyId":"w4-ct-coverings"},{"id":"L00989","dok":1,"q":"In an indirect attachment, what connects the muscle to the bone?","a":"A tendon or an aponeurosis.","options":["The epimysium fused straight to the periosteum","A tendon or an aponeurosis","A ligament","Articular cartilage"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"In an indirect attachment, the connective tissue sheaths of the muscle extend beyond the muscle fibers as a tendon (a cord) or an aponeurosis (a broad flat sheet), and that structure anchors to the bone. Indirect attachments are much more common than direct ones. Most of the muscles you identify in lab attach this way.","notes":["The epimysium fusing straight to the periosteum is a direct attachment, also called a fleshy attachment. Pick it for a question about direct attachments.","Correct. An indirect attachment uses a tendon or an aponeurosis.","A ligament connects bone to bone across a joint, not muscle to bone. Pick it for a question about what holds the bones of a joint together.","Articular cartilage is the smooth hyaline cartilage covering bone ends inside a synovial joint. Pick it for a question about the surfaces that glide against each other in a joint."],"lt":"t-muscle-gross","lid":"c15","comp":72,"competencyId":"w4-ct-coverings"},{"id":"L00990","dok":1,"q":"What is the rope-like cord that anchors a muscle to bone called?","a":"A tendon.","options":["A ligament","A fascia","An aponeurosis","A tendon"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"A tendon is a cord of dense regular connective tissue that forms where the three muscle sheaths merge and extend beyond the muscle. Its collagen fibers run in parallel, which makes it very strong when pulled in one direction. The calcaneal (Achilles) tendon at the back of the ankle is a familiar example.","notes":["A ligament is also dense regular connective tissue, but it connects bone to bone across a joint. Pick it for a question about what holds a joint's bones together.","Fascia is a sheet of connective tissue that wraps and separates muscles, but it does not form the cord-like anchor to bone. Pick deep fascia for a question about what groups muscles into compartments.","An aponeurosis is a broad, flat sheet rather than a cord. Pick it for a question about the sheet-like attachment of a wide muscle.","Correct. A tendon is the cord that anchors muscle to bone."],"lt":"t-muscle-gross","lid":"c16","comp":72,"competencyId":"w4-ct-coverings"},{"id":"L00991","dok":1,"q":"What is the broad, sheet-like connective tissue attachment of a muscle called?","a":"An aponeurosis.","options":["A tendon","A ligament","A retinaculum","An aponeurosis"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"An aponeurosis is a broad, flat sheet of tendon-like connective tissue that attaches a muscle where the muscle is wide. It does the same job as a tendon but is spread out like a sheet instead of gathered into a cord. Aponeuroses are prominent in the abdominal wall and on the top of the skull.","notes":["A tendon does the same job but is a rope-like cord rather than a flat sheet. Pick it for a question about the cord that anchors a muscle to bone.","A ligament connects bone to bone across a joint rather than attaching a muscle. Pick it for a question about stabilizing a joint.","A retinaculum is a band of connective tissue that holds tendons in place as they cross the wrist or ankle. Pick it for a question about the band that keeps tendons from bowing out at the wrist.","Correct. An aponeurosis is a broad, sheet-like attachment."],"lt":"t-muscle-gross","lid":"c17","comp":72,"competencyId":"w4-ct-coverings"},{"id":"L00992","dok":1,"q":"Where do the nerve and blood vessels usually enter or leave a skeletal muscle?","a":"Near the central part of the muscle.","options":["At the tendon of insertion","Near the central part of the muscle","At the tendon of origin","Evenly along the whole muscle surface"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The nerve, artery, and veins of a skeletal muscle usually enter or leave near the central part of the muscle. From there they branch extensively through the connective tissue sheaths so that every fiber receives a nerve ending and capillaries. Tendons, by contrast, have a poor blood supply.","notes":["The tendon of insertion anchors the muscle to the moving bone and, like other tendons, has a poor blood supply. This is the same mix-up as choosing the origin.","Correct. Nerves and vessels usually enter near the center of the muscle.","The tendon of origin anchors the muscle to the stationary bone, and tendons have relatively few blood vessels. Students choose this because the origin sounds like a starting point for everything.","The vessels and nerve enter at a limited spot and then branch inside the muscle, rather than entering all along its surface. Students choose this when they picture the branching inside the muscle instead of the point of entry."],"lt":"t-muscle-gross","lid":"c18"},{"id":"L00993","dok":2,"q":"Moving from the outside of a whole muscle inward, which sheath is reached immediately after the epimysium?","a":"The perimysium.","options":["The sarcolemma","The endomysium","The deep fascia","The perimysium"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The epimysium wraps the whole muscle, and just inside it the muscle is divided into fascicles, each wrapped in perimysium. The perimysium is therefore the next sheath reached after the epimysium. The endomysium comes after that, around each individual fiber.","notes":["The sarcolemma is the fiber's own plasma membrane, found inside all three sheaths. Pick it for a question about the membrane of the muscle cell.","The endomysium is the deepest sheath, around individual fibers, so it comes after the perimysium, not right after the epimysium. Pick it if the question asks for the innermost sheath.","Deep fascia lies outside the epimysium, wrapping and separating whole muscles, so you would pass it before reaching the epimysium. Pick it for a question about what binds muscles into compartments.","Correct. The perimysium lies just inside the epimysium."],"lt":"t-muscle-gross","lid":"c19","comp":72,"competencyId":"w4-ct-coverings"},{"id":"L00994","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly matches each connective tissue sheath to what it wraps?","a":"The epimysium wraps the whole muscle, the perimysium wraps each fascicle, and the endomysium wraps each fiber.","options":["The epimysium wraps the whole muscle, the endomysium wraps each fascicle, and the perimysium wraps each fiber","The epimysium wraps the whole muscle, the perimysium wraps each fascicle, and the endomysium wraps each fiber","The endomysium wraps the whole muscle, the perimysium wraps each fascicle, and the epimysium wraps each fiber","The perimysium wraps the whole muscle, the epimysium wraps each fascicle, and the endomysium wraps each fiber"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The three sheaths wrap the muscle at three scales. The epimysium (epi, upon) wraps the whole muscle, the perimysium (peri, around) wraps each fascicle, and the endomysium (endo, within) wraps each individual fiber. Matching the prefix to the level is the easiest way to keep them straight.","notes":["This swaps the two inner sheaths. The perimysium wraps fascicles and the endomysium wraps individual fibers.","Correct. Epimysium, perimysium, and endomysium wrap the muscle, the fascicle, and the fiber.","This swaps the outermost and innermost sheaths. The endomysium is the deepest layer around single fibers, and the epimysium is the outer layer around the whole muscle.","This swaps the two outer sheaths. The epimysium is the outermost layer around the whole muscle, and the perimysium wraps each fascicle inside it."],"lt":"t-muscle-gross","lid":"c20","comp":72,"competencyId":"w4-ct-coverings"},{"id":"L00995","dok":2,"q":"When all three connective tissue sheaths merge and continue beyond the muscle as a cord that attaches to bone, what do they form?","a":"A tendon.","options":["A tendon","The deep fascia","The periosteum","A ligament"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The epimysium, perimysium, and endomysium are continuous with each other, and at the ends of the muscle they merge and extend beyond the fibers. When this merged tissue forms a cord, it is a tendon, which anchors the muscle to bone. Because the sheaths are continuous with the tendon, the pull of every fiber is carried all the way to the bone.","notes":["Correct. The merged sheaths form a tendon.","Deep fascia is a sheet that wraps and separates whole muscles and groups them by function. Pick it for a question about what binds muscles into compartments.","The periosteum is the membrane covering the bone itself, which a tendon attaches to. Pick it for a question about what the epimysium fuses to in a direct attachment.","A ligament is a cord of dense regular connective tissue too, but it connects bone to bone and is not formed from muscle sheaths. Pick it for a question about what holds the bones of a joint together."],"lt":"t-muscle-gross","lid":"c21","comp":72,"competencyId":"w4-ct-coverings"},{"id":"L00996","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares a tendon and a ligament?","a":"A tendon connects muscle to bone, and a ligament connects bone to bone.","options":["A tendon connects muscle to bone, and a ligament connects bone to bone","A ligament connects muscle to bone, and a tendon connects bone to bone","A tendon connects muscle to muscle, and a ligament connects muscle to bone","Both connect muscle to bone, but a ligament is a flat sheet"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Tendons and ligaments are both made of dense regular connective tissue, with parallel collagen fibers that resist pulling. The difference is what they connect. A tendon attaches a muscle to a bone and carries the muscle's pull, while a ligament attaches one bone to another and holds a joint together.","notes":["Correct. Tendons connect muscle to bone, and ligaments connect bone to bone.","This swaps the two structures. A tendon carries a muscle's pull to bone, and a ligament joins bone to bone.","Neither statement here is right. A tendon runs from muscle to bone, and a ligament joins bone to bone, so students who pick this have shifted both definitions one step.","A flat sheet that attaches muscle is an aponeurosis, not a ligament. Pick aponeurosis for a question about the broad attachment of a wide muscle."],"lt":"t-muscle-gross","lid":"c22","comp":72,"competencyId":"w4-ct-coverings"},{"id":"L00998","dok":2,"q":"Why are indirect attachments by tendons more common than direct (fleshy) attachments?","a":"Tendons are small and durable, so many can cross a joint without taking up much space.","options":["Tendons are made of elastic tissue that stretches with each movement","Tendons are small and durable, so many can cross a joint without taking much space","Tendons have a richer blood supply than muscle fibers","Tendons can contract and add force to the muscle"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Tendons are made of tough, densely packed collagen, so they hold up well when they rub over bony projections. They are also much narrower than a fleshy muscle belly, so several tendons can pass over a single joint, such as the wrist, without crowding it. Durability and small size are why most muscles attach indirectly.","notes":["Tendons are dense regular connective tissue built mainly of collagen, which resists stretching rather than allowing it. Elastic connective tissue, found in large artery walls, is the tissue that stretches and recoils.","Correct. Tendons are durable and compact, so many can cross a joint in a small space.","Tendons have a relatively poor blood supply compared with muscle, which is why tendon injuries heal slowly. This option reverses the real difference between the two tissues.","Tendons cannot contract; only muscle fibers can. Students choose this when they forget that a tendon simply transmits the pull that the muscle fibers produce."],"lt":"t-muscle-gross","lid":"c24","comp":72,"competencyId":"w4-ct-coverings"},{"id":"L00999","dok":2,"q":"Which structure is a sheet of dense connective tissue that wraps muscles, separates them, and binds muscles with a common action into functional groups?","a":"The deep fascia.","options":["The epimysium","An aponeurosis","The superficial fascia","The deep fascia"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Deep fascia is a sheet of dense connective tissue that lies outside the epimysium. It wraps muscles, separates neighboring muscles from each other, and binds muscles that share a common action into groups called compartments. That is why the flexors and extensors of the forearm sit in separate fascial compartments.","notes":["The epimysium wraps a single whole muscle rather than binding several muscles into a group. Pick it for a question about the outermost sheath of one muscle.","An aponeurosis is a broad, flat sheet that attaches a muscle to bone or to another muscle, rather than wrapping muscles into groups. Pick it for a question about the attachment of a wide abdominal muscle.","The superficial fascia is the subcutaneous layer of areolar and adipose tissue between the skin and the muscles. Pick it for a question about the fatty layer just under the skin.","Correct. Deep fascia wraps and separates muscles and binds them into functional groups."],"lt":"t-muscle-gross","lid":"c25","comp":72,"competencyId":"w4-ct-coverings"},{"id":"L01000","dok":2,"q":"Which pair names two functions of a muscle's connective tissue sheaths?","a":"They reinforce the muscle and provide routes for its blood vessels and nerves.","options":["They secrete synovial fluid and cushion the joint the muscle crosses","They produce new muscle fibers and generate most of the muscle's heat","They reinforce the muscle and provide routes for its blood vessels and nerves","They contract alongside the fibers and store calcium for them"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The epimysium, perimysium, and endomysium support and reinforce the muscle so it does not tear apart during strong contractions. They also carry the blood vessels and nerve branches that must reach every fiber. Because they are continuous with the tendon, they also transmit the pull of the fibers to the bone.","notes":["Synovial fluid is secreted by the synovial membrane lining a synovial joint cavity. Pick the synovial membrane for a question about where joint fluid comes from.","The sheaths do not make new fibers, and heat comes from the contracting fibers themselves. Students pick this when they credit the wrappings with the work of the muscle cells inside them.","Correct. The sheaths reinforce the muscle and carry its vessels and nerves.","Connective tissue cannot contract, and calcium is stored inside each fiber by the sarcoplasmic reticulum. Pick the sarcoplasmic reticulum for a question about where calcium is stored."],"lt":"t-muscle-gross","lid":"c26","comp":72,"competencyId":"w4-ct-coverings"},{"id":"L01001","dok":2,"q":"Why are the capillaries in a skeletal muscle long and winding, with many cross-links?","a":"So they can adjust to changes in muscle length, straightening when the muscle is stretched.","options":["To wrap the myofibrils inside each fiber","To carry nerve signals to each fiber","To store oxygen for the fibers","To adjust to changes in muscle length"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"A skeletal muscle constantly changes length as it contracts and is stretched. Its capillaries are long, wavy, and cross-linked, so when the muscle lengthens they straighten out instead of tearing, and when it shortens they fold back up. This keeps blood flowing to the fibers no matter what length the muscle is.","notes":["Capillaries run in the endomysium outside the fibers, not inside them. The network wrapping each myofibril inside the fiber is the sarcoplasmic reticulum.","Capillaries carry blood, not nerve signals. Nerve signals reach each fiber through branches of the muscle's nerve, so pick a nerve for that question.","Oxygen is stored inside the fiber by myoglobin, a red pigment in the sarcoplasm. Pick myoglobin for a question about the muscle's oxygen store.","Correct. The winding shape lets capillaries accommodate changes in muscle length."],"lt":"t-muscle-gross","lid":"c27"},{"id":"L01002","dok":2,"q":"Which sheath can blend with the deep fascia between neighboring muscles or with the superficial fascia under the skin?","a":"The epimysium.","options":["The perimysium","The endomysium","The epimysium","The periosteum"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The epimysium is the outermost sheath of a muscle, so it is the one that touches the connective tissue around the muscle. It can blend with the deep fascia that separates neighboring muscles, or with the superficial fascia that lies deep to the skin. The inner sheaths are buried within the muscle and cannot reach these layers.","notes":["The perimysium wraps fascicles inside the muscle, so it is enclosed by the epimysium. Pick it for a question about the sheath around a bundle of fibers.","The endomysium wraps single fibers deep inside the muscle, far from the fascia outside it. Pick it for a question about the sheath around one muscle cell.","Correct. The epimysium, as the outer sheath, can blend with the surrounding fascia.","The periosteum covers bone, not muscle. Pick it for a question about what the epimysium fuses to in a direct attachment."],"lt":"t-muscle-gross","lid":"c28","comp":72,"competencyId":"w4-ct-coverings"},{"id":"L01003","dok":2,"q":"A muscle attaches by having its epimysium fused directly to the periosteum of a bone, with no tendon in between. Which type of attachment is this?","a":"A direct (fleshy) attachment.","options":["An aponeurotic attachment","A direct (fleshy) attachment","An indirect attachment","A tendinous attachment"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"In a direct, or fleshy, attachment the epimysium of the muscle fuses straight onto the periosteum of the bone. There is no tendon or aponeurosis between the muscle and the bone. Direct attachments are less common than indirect ones.","notes":["An aponeurotic attachment uses a broad flat sheet, which makes it a kind of indirect attachment. Pick it if the description mentions a wide sheet of tendon-like tissue.","Correct. Epimysium fused to periosteum is a direct attachment.","An indirect attachment uses a tendon or aponeurosis between the muscle and bone. Pick it if the description includes a cord or sheet extending beyond the muscle.","A tendinous attachment uses a cord-like tendon, which makes it a kind of indirect attachment. Pick it if the description mentions a rope-like cord anchoring the muscle."],"lt":"t-muscle-gross","lid":"c29","comp":72,"competencyId":"w4-ct-coverings"},{"id":"L01005","dok":2,"q":"Trace the pull of one muscle fiber outward to the bone. Which sequence of connective tissue is correct?","a":"Endomysium, perimysium, epimysium, then the tendon to the bone.","options":["Epimysium, perimysium, endomysium, then the tendon to the bone","Tendon, epimysium, perimysium, endomysium, then bone","Endomysium, perimysium, epimysium, then the tendon to the bone","Perimysium, endomysium, epimysium, then the tendon to the bone"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The pull starts at the fiber, which is wrapped in endomysium. It passes outward to the perimysium around the fascicle, then to the epimysium around the whole muscle. These sheaths are continuous with the tendon, which carries the pull to the bone.","notes":["This lists the sheaths from outside to inside, the order you would meet them while cutting into a muscle. The pull from a fiber travels the opposite way, from inside to outside.","This lists the layers from the outside in and puts the tendon first, which is backward. The tendon is the last structure before the bone, not the first.","Correct. The pull travels from endomysium to perimysium to epimysium and then through the tendon to the bone.","The endomysium wraps the fiber directly, so it must come first. This order mixes up the two inner sheaths."],"lt":"t-muscle-gross","lid":"c31","comp":72,"competencyId":"w4-ct-coverings"},{"id":"L01006","dok":2,"q":"During a strong contraction, a muscle bulges but does not burst. Which function of its connective tissue sheaths prevents the bursting?","a":"Supporting and reinforcing the muscle.","options":["Storing calcium ions","Generating heat","Supporting and reinforcing the muscle","Providing routes for blood vessels and nerves"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The epimysium, perimysium, and endomysium wrap the muscle at every level, from the whole organ down to each fiber. That layered wrapping supports and reinforces the muscle, holding it together when strong contractions make it bulge. Without these sheaths, forceful contractions could tear the muscle apart.","notes":["Calcium ions are stored by the sarcoplasmic reticulum inside each fiber, not by the connective tissue sheaths. Pick the sarcoplasmic reticulum for that question.","Heat is a byproduct of the contracting fibers, not something the sheaths produce or use to hold the muscle together. Pick heat generation for a question about shivering.","Correct. The sheaths support and reinforce the muscle so it does not burst.","Providing routes for vessels and nerves is a real function of the sheaths, but it keeps the fibers supplied rather than holding the muscle together. Pick it for a question about how blood reaches every fiber."],"lt":"t-muscle-gross","lid":"c32","comp":72,"competencyId":"w4-ct-coverings"},{"id":"L01007","dok":2,"q":"A forearm muscle passes its pull across the wrist through several narrow cords that fit easily over the joint. Which attachment type allows this space-saving arrangement?","a":"Indirect attachment by several tendons.","options":["Attachment by ligaments across the wrist","Indirect attachment by several tendons","Indirect attachment by one broad aponeurosis","Direct attachment, with the epimysium fused to bone"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Tendons are narrow and durable, so many of them can cross a joint without crowding it. The forearm muscles that move the wrist and fingers keep their bulky bellies up in the forearm and send long tendons across the wrist. This indirect attachment by tendons keeps the wrist slim while still letting many muscles act on the hand.","notes":["Ligaments connect bone to bone and hold the wrist joint together, but they do not carry a muscle's pull. Pick ligaments for a question about joint stability.","Correct. Several narrow tendons cross the wrist in an indirect attachment.","An aponeurosis is a broad flat sheet, the opposite of several narrow cords. Pick an aponeurosis for a question about a wide flat muscle, such as those of the abdominal wall.","In a direct attachment the muscle belly itself fuses to bone, so the bulky fleshy part would have to sit right at the joint. That would crowd the wrist, which is exactly what tendons avoid."],"lt":"t-muscle-gross","lid":"c33","comp":72,"competencyId":"w4-ct-coverings"},{"id":"L01008","dok":2,"q":"A student dissecting a muscle frees one bundle of muscle fibers by cutting the single sheath around it, while the bundle stays inside the whole muscle. Which sheath was cut, and what is the bundle?","a":"The perimysium was cut, and the bundle is a fascicle.","options":["The epimysium was cut, and the bundle is a myofibril","The perimysium was cut, and the bundle is a myofibril","The perimysium was cut, and the bundle is a fascicle","The endomysium was cut, and the bundle is a fascicle"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"A bundle of muscle fibers inside a whole muscle is a fascicle, and the sheath that wraps each fascicle is the perimysium. Cutting the perimysium frees one fascicle from its neighbors. The epimysium is outside all the fascicles, and the endomysium is inside each one around single fibers.","notes":["The epimysium wraps the whole muscle, and a myofibril is a contractile rod inside a single fiber, too small to dissect by hand. Pick the epimysium for a question about the outermost sheath.","The perimysium is the right sheath, but a myofibril is inside a muscle fiber, not a bundle of fibers. Pick a myofibril for a question about the rods that fill most of the sarcoplasm.","Correct. Cutting the perimysium frees a fascicle.","The bundle is a fascicle, but the endomysium wraps single fibers, not bundles. Pick the endomysium for a question about freeing one individual fiber."],"lt":"t-muscle-gross","lid":"c34","comp":72,"competencyId":"w4-ct-coverings"},{"id":"L01009","dok":2,"q":"A muscle's nerve and blood vessels enter near its center and branch outward. Through which structures do these branches travel to reach individual fibers?","a":"Through the connective tissue sheaths.","options":["Through the tendons","Through the connective tissue sheaths","Through the myofibrils","Through the T tubules"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"After entering the muscle, the nerve and blood vessels branch through the epimysium, then into the perimysium between fascicles, and finally into the endomysium around each fiber. These sheaths act as pathways that deliver capillaries and nerve endings to every fiber. This is one of the main functions of the connective tissue sheaths.","notes":["Tendons anchor the muscle to bone and have a relatively poor blood supply, so they are not the main route for vessels into the muscle. Pick tendons for a question about what carries the muscle's pull to bone.","Correct. Vessels and nerves travel through the connective tissue sheaths.","Myofibrils are the contractile rods inside a fiber, and vessels do not pass through them. Pick myofibrils for a question about what fills most of the sarcoplasm.","T tubules are inward folds of the sarcolemma inside a fiber that carry the surface signal deep into the cell. Pick them for a question about how a signal reaches the interior of a fiber."],"lt":"t-muscle-gross","lid":"c35","comp":72,"competencyId":"w4-ct-coverings"},{"id":"L01011","dok":2,"q":"A wide, flat abdominal muscle attaches along the midline of the abdomen by a broad sheet rather than a cord. Which attachment structure is it using?","a":"An aponeurosis.","options":["An aponeurosis","A direct fleshy attachment","A ligament","A tendon"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"An aponeurosis is a broad, flat sheet of tendon-like connective tissue used where a muscle is wide. The flat muscles of the abdominal wall attach this way, and their aponeuroses meet at the midline. A sheet spreads the pull of a wide muscle evenly, where a single cord would concentrate it.","notes":["Correct. A broad, sheet-like attachment is an aponeurosis.","In a direct fleshy attachment the epimysium fuses straight to bone with no sheet or cord in between. The description here includes a sheet, so it is an indirect attachment.","A ligament connects bone to bone across a joint and does not attach muscles. Pick it for a question about what holds a joint together.","A tendon is a rope-like cord, not a broad sheet. Pick it for a question about a narrow attachment, such as the calcaneal tendon."],"lt":"t-muscle-gross","lid":"c37","comp":72,"competencyId":"w4-ct-coverings"},{"id":"L01012","dok":2,"q":"Which statement explains how the pull of a single muscle fiber deep inside a muscle reaches the bone?","a":"The sheaths are continuous with each other and with the tendon, so the pull passes outward to the bone.","options":["The pull travels through the T tubules to the tendon","The sheaths and tendon form one continuous network","Each fiber attaches to bone by its own sarcolemma","Ligaments carry the pull from the fibers to the bone"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The endomysium around each fiber is continuous with the perimysium around the fascicle, which is continuous with the epimysium around the whole muscle, and all three merge into the tendon. Because these layers are one connected network, the pull of a fiber deep in the muscle is passed outward through the sheaths to the tendon and then to the bone. This continuity also explains why a strain can tear fibers inside the belly while the tendon stays intact.","notes":["T tubules carry the surface signal inward into the fiber, not the mechanical pull outward. Pick T tubules for a question about how a signal reaches the interior of the fiber.","Correct. The continuous sheaths and tendon carry each fiber's pull to the bone.","Most fibers never touch the bone at all; they end inside the muscle and rely on the connective tissue to transmit their pull. The sarcolemma is simply the fiber's plasma membrane.","Ligaments join bone to bone across a joint and do not connect to muscle fibers. Pick them for a question about joint stability."],"lt":"t-muscle-gross","lid":"c38","comp":72,"competencyId":"w4-ct-coverings"},{"id":"N-m3-muscle-organization-701","dok":1,"q":"Which fascicle arrangement has its fascicles in concentric rings around an opening, as in the orbicularis oris?","a":"Circular.","options":["Circular","Pennate","Parallel","Convergent"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"In a circular muscle, the fascicles form concentric rings around an opening. When the muscle contracts, the rings tighten and close the opening, so circular muscles act as sphincters. The orbicularis oris around the mouth and the orbicularis oculi around the eye are the classic examples.","notes":["Correct. Fascicles in rings around an opening form a circular muscle.","In a pennate muscle short fascicles attach at an angle to a central tendon, like the barbs of a feather. Pick it for the deltoid or rectus femoris.","In a parallel muscle the fascicles run parallel to the long axis of the muscle, as in the sartorius. Pick it for a long, straplike muscle.","In a convergent muscle a broad origin narrows onto a single tendon, giving a fan or triangle shape. Pick it for the pectoralis major."],"comp":78,"competencyId":"w4-fascicle-patterns"},{"id":"N-m3-muscle-organization-702","dok":2,"q":"The pectoralis major has a broad origin, and its fascicles narrow onto a single tendon, giving the muscle a fan shape. Which fascicle arrangement is this?","a":"Convergent.","options":["Convergent","Circular","Pennate","Fusiform"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"A convergent muscle has a broad origin whose fascicles come together onto a single tendon of insertion. This gives the muscle a triangular or fan shape. The pectoralis major spreads across the chest from the clavicle and sternum and converges on a tendon that inserts on the humerus.","notes":["Correct. A broad origin narrowing onto one tendon is a convergent arrangement.","A circular muscle has fascicles in rings around an opening, like the orbicularis oris. Pick it for a sphincter muscle.","A pennate muscle has short fascicles attached at an angle to a central tendon, like a feather. Pick it for the deltoid or the rectus femoris.","A fusiform muscle has parallel fascicles with a thick central belly that tapers to a tendon at each end, like the biceps brachii. Pick it for a spindle-shaped muscle."],"comp":78,"competencyId":"w4-fascicle-patterns"},{"id":"N-m3-muscle-organization-703","dok":1,"q":"Which fascicle arrangement has short fascicles attached at an angle to a central tendon, like the barbs of a feather?","a":"Pennate.","options":["Parallel","Convergent","Pennate","Circular"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"In a pennate muscle, short fascicles attach at an angle to a central tendon, much like the barbs on a feather. Unipennate muscles have fascicles on one side of the tendon, bipennate muscles have them on both sides, and multipennate muscles have them on a branched tendon. The rectus femoris is bipennate, and the deltoid is multipennate.","notes":["In a parallel muscle the fascicles run the full length of the muscle along its long axis, not at an angle to a tendon. Pick it for the sartorius or rectus abdominis.","In a convergent muscle the fascicles spread from a broad origin and come together on one tendon in a fan shape. Pick it for the pectoralis major.","Correct. Short fascicles angled onto a central tendon form a pennate muscle.","In a circular muscle the fascicles form rings around an opening. Pick it for the orbicularis oris or orbicularis oculi."],"comp":78,"competencyId":"w4-fascicle-patterns"},{"id":"N-m3-muscle-organization-704","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares a parallel muscle with a pennate muscle?","a":"A parallel muscle has a larger range of motion but less power, while a pennate muscle has more power but a smaller range of motion.","options":["A parallel muscle has more power but a smaller range of motion, while a pennate muscle has a larger range of motion but less power","Both have the same power, but only the pennate muscle has a large range of motion","A parallel muscle has a larger range of motion but less power, while a pennate muscle has more power but a smaller range of motion","Both have the same range of motion, but only the parallel muscle has much power"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Fascicle arrangement trades range of motion against power. A parallel muscle has long fascicles, so it can shorten a long distance and move its insertion far, but fewer fibers pull on the tendon. A pennate muscle packs many short fascicles at an angle onto its tendon, so more fibers pull and it is more powerful, but each fascicle shortens only a short distance.","notes":["This reverses the trade-off. Long fascicles give range, and many short, angled fascicles give power.","The arrangements differ in power because a pennate muscle packs many more fibers onto its tendon. Range of motion is actually the strength of a parallel muscle, not a pennate one.","Correct. Parallel muscles favor range of motion, and pennate muscles favor power.","The arrangements differ in range because longer fascicles can shorten a greater distance. A pennate muscle is the more powerful of the two, not the weaker one."],"comp":78,"competencyId":"w4-fascicle-patterns"},{"id":"L01232","dok":1,"q":"Which description fits a skeletal muscle fiber?","a":"A long, cylindrical cell with many nuclei pushed to its edge.","options":["A branching cell with one central nucleus","A spindle-shaped cell with one central nucleus","A long, cylindrical cell with many nuclei at its edge","A long contractile rod inside the cell"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"A skeletal muscle fiber is a single muscle cell. It is long and cylindrical, often running a great length of the muscle, and it has many nuclei flattened against the inside of the sarcolemma. Cardiac cells branch and have one central nucleus, and smooth muscle cells are spindle-shaped with one central nucleus.","notes":["This describes a cardiac muscle cell, joined to its neighbors by intercalated discs. Pick it for heart muscle.","This describes a smooth muscle cell, found in the walls of hollow organs and vessels. It would be the answer for non-striated, involuntary muscle.","Correct. This is the skeletal muscle fiber, a single multinucleated cell.","This describes a myofibril, which lies inside a fiber. It is a part of the cell, not the cell itself."],"lt":"t-muscle-structure","lid":"c1","comp":77,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-tissue-types"},{"id":"L01234","dok":1,"q":"Which connective tissue sheath wraps the entire skeletal muscle?","a":"The epimysium.","options":["The epimysium","The perimysium","The endomysium","The periosteum"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Three connective tissue sheaths wrap a skeletal muscle at three scales. The epimysium, dense irregular connective tissue, surrounds the whole muscle as its outermost layer. The perimysium wraps each fascicle, and the endomysium wraps each fiber. All three are continuous with one another and with the tendon.","notes":["Correct. Epi means upon, and the epimysium is the outermost layer around the whole muscle.","The perimysium wraps each fascicle, a bundle of fibers within the muscle. Pick it for the sheath around a fascicle.","The endomysium wraps each individual muscle fiber. It would be the answer for the finest, innermost sheath.","The periosteum covers the outer surface of a bone, not a muscle. Pick it for the membrane where tendons attach to bone."],"lt":"t-muscle-structure","lid":"c3","comp":72,"competencyId":"w4-ct-coverings"},{"id":"L01235","dok":1,"q":"Which connective tissue sheath wraps each individual muscle fiber?","a":"The endomysium.","options":["The endomysium","The epimysium","The deep fascia","The perimysium"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The endomysium is a fine sheath of areolar connective tissue with reticular fibers that surrounds each individual muscle fiber. The prefixes give the order: epi (upon) for the whole muscle, peri (around) for each fascicle, and endo (within) for each fiber.","notes":["Correct. Endo means within, and the endomysium is the innermost sheath around each fiber.","The epimysium wraps the entire muscle. It would be the answer for the outermost sheath.","Deep fascia is a sheet of dense connective tissue that wraps and separates whole muscles into functional groups. It lies outside the epimysium, far from single fibers.","The perimysium wraps a fascicle, a bundle of many fibers. Pick it for the middle sheath."],"lt":"t-muscle-structure","lid":"c4","comp":72,"competencyId":"w4-ct-coverings"},{"id":"L01236","dok":1,"q":"What is a cord of dense regular connective tissue that attaches a muscle to bone called?","a":"A tendon.","options":["A fascicle","A ligament","A tendon","An aponeurosis"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"A tendon is a cord of dense regular connective tissue that attaches a muscle to bone. It forms from the merged continuation of the three muscle sheaths, the epimysium, perimysium, and endomysium, so the pull of every fiber is carried through the tendon to the bone. Ligaments use similar tissue but join bone to bone.","notes":["A fascicle is a bundle of muscle fibers wrapped in perimysium. It is part of the muscle, not its attachment.","A ligament is dense regular connective tissue that connects bone to bone across a joint. It would be the answer for a joint-stabilizing band.","Correct. A tendon is the cord connecting muscle to bone.","An aponeurosis is also tendon-like tissue, but it is a broad flat sheet, used where a muscle is wide. Pick it for a sheet-like attachment."],"lt":"t-muscle-structure","lid":"c5","comp":72,"competencyId":"w4-ct-coverings"},{"id":"L01246","dok":2,"q":"Which sequence orders the levels of skeletal muscle organization from largest to smallest?","a":"Muscle, fascicle, muscle fiber, myofibril, myofilament.","options":["Muscle, fascicle, fiber, myofibril, myofilament","Myofilament, myofibril, fiber, fascicle, muscle","Muscle, fascicle, fiber, myofilament, myofibril","Muscle, fiber, fascicle, myofibril, myofilament"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"A skeletal muscle is a set of nested structures. The whole muscle is wrapped in epimysium and divided into fascicles wrapped in perimysium. Each fascicle holds muscle fibers, single cells wrapped in endomysium. Each fiber is packed with myofibrils, and each myofibril is built of thick and thin myofilaments.","notes":["Correct. Organ, bundle, cell, rod, protein strand.","This is the correct order run from smallest to largest. It would be right if the question asked you to start with the smallest level.","This swaps the last two levels. Myofibrils are built of myofilaments, so the myofibril is the larger of the two.","This swaps the fascicle and the fiber. A fascicle is a bundle of many fibers, so it must be larger than a single fiber."],"lt":"t-muscle-structure","lid":"c16","comp":73,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-organization"},{"id":"L01251","dok":1,"q":"Which muscle tissue types are striated?","a":"Skeletal and cardiac muscle.","options":["Skeletal and cardiac","Skeletal, cardiac, and smooth","Skeletal and smooth","Cardiac and smooth"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Skeletal and cardiac muscle are both striated, because their myofilaments are organized into sarcomeres whose dark and light bands line up. Smooth muscle is non-striated; its filaments are not arranged in sarcomeres, so it looks smooth under the microscope. Cardiac and smooth muscle are both involuntary, which is a different comparison.","notes":["Correct. Both are built of aligned sarcomeres that show stripes.","Smooth muscle lacks striations, so it cannot be included. All three would be the answer only for a question about which tissues contract.","Skeletal muscle is striated, but smooth muscle is not; its filaments are not arranged in sarcomeres. Pick smooth muscle for the non-striated type.","Cardiac muscle is striated, but smooth muscle is not. This pair would only fit a question about involuntary muscle."],"lt":"t-muscle-structure","lid":"c21","comp":77,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-tissue-types"},{"id":"L01254","dok":1,"q":"What is a broad, flat sheet of tendon-like connective tissue that attaches a wide muscle called?","a":"An aponeurosis.","options":["An aponeurosis","A tendon","A retinaculum","A ligament"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"An aponeurosis is a broad, flat sheet of dense connective tissue that does the job of a tendon where a muscle is wide. Instead of narrowing into a cord, the muscle's connective tissue spreads into a sheet, as in the aponeuroses of the abdominal wall muscles.","notes":["Correct. An aponeurosis is a sheet-like tendon for a wide muscle.","A tendon is the same kind of tissue, but shaped as a cord rather than a broad sheet. Pick it for a cord-like attachment.","A retinaculum is a band of thickened deep fascia that holds tendons in place near a joint, such as at the wrist. It does not attach a muscle to bone.","A ligament connects bone to bone across a joint. It would be the answer for a band that stabilizes a joint."],"lt":"t-muscle-structure","lid":"c24","comp":72,"competencyId":"w4-ct-coverings"},{"id":"L01257","dok":3,"q":"A muscle strain tears fibers within the belly of a muscle, while a different injury separates the muscle from the bone entirely. Which structure is torn in that second injury?","a":"The tendon.","options":["The endomysium","The tendon","The perimysium","The sarcolemma"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The three connective tissue sheaths are continuous with one another and with the tendon, so a fiber's pull is carried all the way to the bone. A strain tears fibers and their endomysium inside the belly, but the muscle stays attached. A tendon rupture breaks the cord that joins muscle to bone, separating them entirely, and the two injuries heal on very different timelines.","notes":["The endomysium wraps single fibers inside the belly and tears along with them in a strain. Pick it for the sheath damaged in a strain.","Correct. A tendon rupture separates the muscle from its bone.","The perimysium wraps fascicles within the muscle. Damage there stays inside the muscle and does not detach it from bone.","The sarcolemma is the membrane of a single fiber. Tearing it injures that cell but does not separate the whole muscle from the bone."],"lt":"t-muscle-structure","lid":"c27","comp":72,"competencyId":"w4-ct-coverings"},{"id":"L01259","dok":2,"q":"Tracing a skeletal muscle from the outside inward, past the whole muscle and its fascicles, which single cell is reached next?","a":"The muscle fiber.","options":["The muscle fiber","The myofibril","The myofilament","The sarcomere"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"A fascicle is a bundle of muscle fibers wrapped in perimysium. Inside it, each muscle fiber is a single long cell wrapped in its own endomysium. Going further in, each fiber is filled with myofibrils, which are built of sarcomeres made from thick and thin myofilaments.","notes":["Correct. The fiber is the muscle cell inside each fascicle.","The myofibril is a contractile rod inside the fiber, one level further in. It is part of a cell, not a whole cell.","The myofilament is a protein strand, the smallest level. It would be the answer for the last level of organization.","The sarcomere is a segment of a myofibril, two levels further in. Pick it for the contractile unit between Z discs."],"lt":"t-muscle-structure","lid":"c29","comp":73,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-organization"},{"id":"L01263","dok":1,"q":"How does a tendon form at the end of a skeletal muscle?","a":"It is the merged continuation of the epimysium, perimysium, and endomysium.","options":["As bundled myofibrils leaving the muscle","As the merged continuation of all three muscle sheaths","As an extension of the sarcolemma of each fiber","As the periosteum growing out from the bone"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The epimysium, perimysium, and endomysium are continuous with one another. At the end of a muscle they merge into a single cord of dense regular connective tissue, the tendon, which attaches to bone. Because this connective tissue is continuous from every fiber to the bone, the pull of each fiber reaches the skeleton.","notes":["Myofibrils stay inside the muscle fibers. They are contractile rods, not the dense connective tissue of a tendon.","Correct. The three sheaths join to form the tendon.","The sarcolemma is a cell membrane and ends with each fiber. The connective tissue around the fibers, not their membranes, forms the tendon.","The periosteum covers the bone, and tendon fibers blend into it at the attachment. The tendon itself comes from the muscle's sheaths, not from the bone."],"lt":"t-muscle-structure","lid":"d3-t-muscle-structure-3","comp":72,"competencyId":"w4-ct-coverings"},{"id":"L01265","dok":1,"q":"Which set of features describes smooth muscle cells?","a":"Spindle-shaped, non-striated, with one central nucleus.","options":["Spindle-shaped, non-striated, with one central nucleus","Branching, striated, with one central nucleus","Long, cylindrical, striated, with many nuclei at the edge","Spindle-shaped, striated, with many nuclei"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The three muscle tissues are told apart by cell shape, striations, and nuclei. Smooth muscle cells are spindle-shaped, thick in the middle and tapered at the ends, with one central nucleus and no striations. They line the walls of hollow organs and vessels and are involuntary, suited to slow, sustained contractions.","notes":["Correct. These are the features of smooth muscle.","These are the features of cardiac muscle cells, joined by intercalated discs. They would be the answer for the heart wall.","These are the features of a skeletal muscle fiber. Pick them for voluntary muscle attached to the skeleton.","This mixes features. Smooth muscle is spindle-shaped but non-striated, with a single nucleus."],"lt":"t-muscle-structure","lid":"d3-t-muscle-structure-5","comp":77,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-tissue-types"},{"id":"L01269","dok":1,"q":"In a muscle with the parallel fascicle pattern, how do the fascicles run?","a":"Parallel to the long axis of the muscle.","options":["At an angle to a central tendon","Converging from a broad origin onto one tendon","In concentric rings around an opening","Parallel to the long axis of the muscle"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"A fascicle is a bundle of muscle fibers wrapped in perimysium, and the way fascicles are laid out gives a muscle its shape. In the parallel pattern the fascicles run side by side along the long axis of the muscle, from one end to the other, so the muscle looks like a strap. The sartorius and the rectus abdominis are the classic examples.","notes":["Short fascicles set at an angle to a central tendon describe a pennate muscle, such as the rectus femoris. Pick this if the question asked about the feather-shaped pattern.","A broad, fan-shaped spread of fascicles that narrows to one tendon describes a convergent muscle, such as the pectoralis major. Choose this for a triangular, fan-shaped muscle.","Rings of fascicles around an opening describe a circular muscle, which forms a sphincter like the orbicularis oris. That would be the answer for a muscle that closes the mouth or eye.","Correct. Parallel fascicles run lengthwise, like the strap-shaped sartorius."],"lt":"t-muscle-fascicles","lid":"c2","comp":78,"competencyId":"w4-fascicle-patterns"},{"id":"L01270","dok":1,"q":"Which fascicle pattern forms a sphincter, with fascicles arranged in concentric rings around an opening?","a":"Circular.","options":["Circular","Parallel","Convergent","Pennate"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"In a circular muscle the fascicles form rings, one inside another, around a body opening. When the rings contract they squeeze the opening shut, which is why circular muscles act as sphincters. The orbicularis oris around the mouth and the orbicularis oculi around the eye are the examples to know.","notes":["Correct. Ring-shaped fascicles around an opening make a sphincter, like the orbicularis oris.","Parallel fascicles run straight along the long axis of a strap-like muscle such as the sartorius. They would be the answer for a long muscle built for range of motion, not for one that closes an opening.","Convergent fascicles fan out from a broad origin and come together on a single tendon, as in the pectoralis major. Pick this for a triangular, fan-shaped muscle.","Pennate fascicles are short and attach at an angle to a central tendon, like the barbs of a feather. This fits a muscle such as the deltoid or rectus femoris, not a ring around an opening."],"lt":"t-muscle-fascicles","lid":"c3","comp":78,"competencyId":"w4-fascicle-patterns"},{"id":"L01271","dok":1,"q":"How are the fascicles arranged in a pennate muscle?","a":"Short fascicles attach at an angle to a central tendon.","options":["Fascicles fan from a broad origin onto one tendon","Short fascicles attach at an angle to a central tendon","Fascicles form concentric rings around an opening","Long fascicles run parallel to the long axis"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Pennate comes from the Latin word for feather. In a pennate muscle, short fascicles attach at an angle to a tendon that runs through the muscle, the way barbs attach to the shaft of a feather. This angled packing lets many fibers fit into the muscle, which is why pennate muscles are built for power.","notes":["A broad origin that narrows to one tendon describes a convergent muscle such as the pectoralis major. Pick this for a triangular, fan-shaped muscle.","Correct. The angled, feather-like attachment to a central tendon is what defines a pennate muscle.","Concentric rings describe a circular muscle, the sphincter pattern seen in the orbicularis oris. That answers a question about closing an opening.","Long fascicles running along the length of the muscle describe the parallel pattern, as in the sartorius. Choose this if the question asked about a strap-like muscle built for range."],"lt":"t-muscle-fascicles","lid":"c4","comp":78,"competencyId":"w4-fascicle-patterns"},{"id":"L01272","dok":1,"q":"A pennate muscle has fascicles attaching to only one side of its tendon. What is this type called?","a":"Unipennate.","options":["Unipennate","Multipennate","Bipennate","Fusiform"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Pennate muscles are subdivided by how many sides of the tendon the fascicles attach to. Uni means one, so a unipennate muscle has fascicles on just one side of the tendon, as in the extensor digitorum. Bipennate muscles have fascicles on both sides, and multipennate muscles have fascicles on a branched tendon.","notes":["Correct. Fascicles on one side of the tendon make a unipennate muscle, such as the extensor digitorum.","A multipennate muscle has fascicles attached to a branched tendon, giving many feather patterns in one muscle. The deltoid is the example to choose for this pattern.","A bipennate muscle has fascicles attaching on both sides of the central tendon, like a full feather. The rectus femoris is the example, so pick this when fascicles come in from two sides.","Fusiform is not a pennate type at all. It describes a muscle with parallel fascicles and a thick central belly that tapers at each end, like the biceps brachii."],"lt":"t-muscle-fascicles","lid":"c5","comp":78,"competencyId":"w4-fascicle-patterns"},{"id":"L01283","dok":2,"q":"A muscle is built for power, with many short fascicles packed at an angle around a central tendon. Which fascicle pattern is this?","a":"Pennate.","options":["Circular","Convergent","Parallel","Pennate"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"A pennate muscle packs many short fascicles at an angle around a tendon, like the barbs of a feather. Because so many fibers fit into the muscle and all pull on the tendon, the muscle produces more power. The trade-off is a smaller range of motion, because short fascicles cannot shorten very far.","notes":["A circular muscle has fascicles in rings around an opening and acts as a sphincter. It fits a question about the orbicularis oris or orbicularis oculi.","A convergent muscle fans from a broad origin onto a single tendon. Pick it for a triangular muscle such as the pectoralis major.","A parallel muscle has long fascicles running its full length, which gives a large range of motion but less power. Choose it for a strap-like muscle such as the sartorius.","Correct. Many short, angled fascicles on a central tendon make a pennate muscle built for power."],"lt":"t-muscle-fascicles","lid":"c16","comp":78,"competencyId":"w4-fascicle-patterns"},{"id":"L01284","dok":2,"q":"A muscle has long fascicles running its full length, giving a large range of motion but less power. Which architecture is this?","a":"Parallel.","options":["Parallel","Pennate","Bipennate","Multipennate"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Parallel muscles have long fascicles running the whole length of the muscle. Because a muscle fiber can shorten to about two thirds of its resting length, a long fascicle shortens by a large distance and moves the insertion far. Fewer fibers fit across the muscle, though, so it pulls with less power than a pennate muscle of the same size.","notes":["Correct. Long fascicles along the full length give range of motion at the cost of power.","A pennate muscle has short fascicles packed at an angle around a tendon, which gives more power but a smaller range. It is the opposite end of the trade-off.","Bipennate is a pennate muscle with fascicles on both sides of the tendon, such as the rectus femoris. Like all pennate muscles it favors power over range.","Multipennate is a pennate muscle with fascicles on a branched tendon, such as the deltoid. It is built for power, not for a large range of motion."],"lt":"t-muscle-fascicles","lid":"c17","comp":78,"competencyId":"w4-fascicle-patterns"},{"id":"L01293","dok":3,"q":"A surgeon planning a tendon transfer needs a muscle built for power to take over a demanding job. Which fascicle architecture should the surgeon look for?","a":"A pennate muscle.","options":["A pennate muscle","A circular muscle","A parallel muscle","A fusiform muscle"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"In a tendon transfer, a working muscle is rerouted to take over the job of a muscle that no longer works, so its architecture should match that job. A pennate muscle packs many short fascicles at an angle around a tendon, so more fibers pull and it produces more power. Parallel and fusiform muscles favor range of motion instead.","notes":["Correct. Many short, angled fascicles give the most power for a demanding job.","A circular muscle forms a sphincter around an opening. It closes a passage rather than pulling a tendon on a bone, so it would fit a job like closing the mouth.","A parallel muscle has long fascicles that give a large range of motion but less power. It would be the choice if the job needed a long, sweeping movement.","A fusiform muscle has parallel fascicles in a thick belly that tapers at each end, like the biceps brachii. Like other parallel muscles it trades power for range."],"lt":"t-muscle-fascicles","lid":"c26","comp":78,"competencyId":"w4-fascicle-patterns"},{"id":"L01296","dok":3,"q":"A muscle fiber can shorten to about two thirds of its resting length. Why does a muscle with longer fascicles move its insertion farther?","a":"Longer fascicles shorten by a greater absolute distance.","options":["They contract with more force","They contain more nuclei","They shorten by a greater distance","They attach at a steeper angle"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"If every fiber shortens by about one third of its length, a long fiber shortens by more centimeters than a short fiber. A muscle with long fascicles therefore pulls its insertion a greater distance toward its origin. That is why parallel muscles, with long fascicles, have a larger range of motion than pennate muscles.","notes":["Force depends mainly on how many fibers pull on the tendon, not on how long they are. Pick a force answer for a question about pennate muscles, which pack in more fibers.","Skeletal muscle fibers are multinucleated, and a long fiber may have many nuclei, but nuclei do not set how far a fiber shortens.","Correct. The same fraction of a longer length is a bigger distance.","A steep angle to the tendon is a feature of pennate muscles, which have short fascicles. Long fascicles in a parallel muscle run along the axis, not at an angle."],"lt":"t-muscle-fascicles","lid":"c29","comp":78,"competencyId":"w4-fascicle-patterns"},{"id":"L01298","dok":3,"q":"A strap-like muscle with parallel fascicles shortens a great deal but with modest force. Which explanation accounts for both features?","a":"Its long fascicles shorten by a large distance, but fewer fibers fit across the muscle to pull.","options":["Parallel fibers pull at an angle, so part of each pull is lost","Long fascicles pull harder but tire before shortening fully","Long fascicles shorten far, but fewer fibers fit across the muscle to pull","Parallel muscles insert far from the joint, so they move bones farther"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Parallel fascicles run the full length of the muscle, so when each fiber shortens by about a third, the whole muscle shortens a long way and moves its insertion far. Because the fibers run lengthwise, relatively few of them fit across the width of the muscle, so fewer fibers pull on the tendon at once. Long length explains the range, and the smaller number of fibers explains the modest force.","notes":["An angled pull on the tendon is a feature of pennate muscles, not parallel ones. Parallel fibers pull in line with the muscle.","Fascicle length does not make a fiber pull harder, and fatigue does not explain range. This choice mixes up length with strength.","Correct. Fascicle length sets the range and fiber number sets the force.","Fascicle pattern does not decide where a muscle inserts. An insertion farther from the joint would actually give more force and less speed, the opposite of what is described."],"lt":"t-muscle-fascicles","lid":"d3-t-muscle-fascicles-1","comp":78,"competencyId":"w4-fascicle-patterns"},{"id":"L01299","dok":3,"q":"A pennate muscle has short fascicles attached at an angle to a central tendon. Why does this design produce more force than a parallel muscle of the same size?","a":"Angled packing fits many more fibers into the same volume, and more fibers pulling means more force.","options":["Angled packing fits more fibers into the same volume","Its fascicles shorten by a greater distance","Its fascicles are longer, so each one pulls harder","Its angled fibers pull straight along the tendon line"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The force a muscle can produce depends mostly on how many fibers pull on the tendon at once. Setting short fibers at an angle along a tendon lets far more of them fit into a given volume than running long fibers straight through. The cost is range, because short fibers shorten by only a short distance.","notes":["Correct. More fibers pulling on the tendon means more force.","Short pennate fascicles shorten by a smaller distance, which is why pennate muscles have less range. A greater shortening distance belongs to long parallel fascicles.","Pennate fascicles are actually shorter than parallel ones, and length does not make a fiber stronger. Longer fascicles explain range of motion in a parallel muscle, not force.","The fibers are angled, so they do not pull straight along the tendon. Pulling in line with the muscle is a feature of parallel fascicles."],"lt":"t-muscle-fascicles","lid":"d3-t-muscle-fascicles-2","comp":78,"competencyId":"w4-fascicle-patterns"},{"id":"L01300","dok":3,"q":"The deltoid of the shoulder is a multipennate muscle that lifts the arm against heavy loads. What does this architecture give the deltoid?","a":"High force from many short fibers packed into a compact muscle.","options":["A very wide range from long fibers running its full length","Extra shortening distance because short fibers shorten more","High force from many short fibers packed into a compact muscle","A ring of fibers that closes the joint like a sphincter"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"A multipennate muscle has short fascicles attached to a branched tendon, which creates several feather patterns inside one muscle. That arrangement packs a very large number of fibers into a compact space, and all of them pull on the tendon. The result is a strong muscle, which suits the deltoid because raising the arm against gravity and loads takes considerable force.","notes":["Long fibers running the full length describe a parallel muscle such as the sartorius, which is built for range. The deltoid has short, angled fascicles.","Short fibers shorten by less distance, not more, so pennate muscles have a smaller range. Students who pick this reverse the trade-off between length and range.","Correct. Multipennate packing gives power in a manageable size.","A ring of fibers around an opening is a circular muscle, such as the orbicularis oris. A joint is not an opening that a muscle closes."],"lt":"t-muscle-fascicles","lid":"d3-t-muscle-fascicles-3","comp":78,"competencyId":"w4-fascicle-patterns"},{"id":"L01301","dok":3,"q":"A circular muscle, such as a sphincter, has fascicles arranged in rings around an opening. Why is this ring arrangement suited to controlling a passage?","a":"Contracting the ring narrows or closes the opening, acting as a valve.","options":["Contracting the ring widens the opening","Contracting the ring pulls a bone toward the origin","Contracting the ring narrows or closes the opening","Ring fibers pack at an angle around a central tendon"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"When the concentric fibers of a circular muscle shorten, the ring gets smaller and the opening in the center narrows or closes. That lets the muscle control what passes through, as the orbicularis oris does at the mouth. Relaxing the ring lets the opening widen again.","notes":["Shortening a ring makes it smaller, not larger. A sphincter opens when it relaxes, so this choice reverses what contraction does.","Pulling an insertion bone toward an origin bone describes how most skeletal muscles move a lever. A sphincter does not have that bone-to-bone arrangement.","Correct. Shortening the ring tightens it, so the muscle works like a valve.","Fibers packed at an angle to a central tendon describe a pennate muscle built for power. A circular muscle has no central tendon."],"lt":"t-muscle-fascicles","lid":"d3-t-muscle-fascicles-4","comp":78,"competencyId":"w4-fascicle-patterns"},{"id":"L01302","dok":3,"q":"A convergent muscle, such as the pectoralis major, has a broad origin that narrows to a single tendon. Why does this fan shape make the muscle versatile?","a":"Different parts of the muscle can pull in slightly different directions on the same tendon.","options":["Its fibers form rings that close an opening","All of its fibers pull along one straight line","Its parts pull in slightly different directions on one tendon","Its fibers attach at an angle to a tendon running through the muscle"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"A convergent muscle spreads over a wide origin but inserts at one point. Fibers from the upper, middle, and lower parts of the fan approach the tendon from different angles, so activating different parts pulls the insertion in different directions. This lets one muscle, like the pectoralis major, take part in several related movements at the shoulder.","notes":["Rings of fibers around an opening describe a circular muscle, which acts as a sphincter. The pectoralis major attaches to bones rather than surrounding an opening.","A single straight line of pull describes a parallel muscle, whose fascicles all run along the long axis. A fan of fibers cannot all pull in exactly the same line.","Correct. Fibers from different parts of the fan reach the tendon at different angles.","Short fibers angled onto a tendon that runs through the muscle describe a pennate muscle. Pick this when the question is about packing fibers for power."],"lt":"t-muscle-fascicles","lid":"d3-t-muscle-fascicles-5","comp":78,"competencyId":"w4-fascicle-patterns"},{"id":"L01305","dok":3,"q":"A fusiform muscle such as the biceps brachii has a thick belly that tapers to a tendon at each end. Where does it fall in the trade-off between range of motion and power?","a":"With the parallel muscles, favoring range of motion over power.","options":["Outside the trade-off, maximizing both range and power","With pennate muscles, favoring power over range","With parallel muscles, favoring range over power","With circular muscles, closing an opening"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"In a fusiform muscle the fascicles still run parallel to the long axis, but the belly is thick in the middle and tapers to a tendon at each end. Because its fascicles are long and parallel, it is grouped with parallel muscles, which have a larger range of motion and less power. A muscle cannot be built for maximum range and maximum power at the same time.","notes":["No fascicle pattern maximizes both at once. Students who pick this overlook that architecture always trades one for the other.","Pennate muscles favor power because short fascicles pack in at an angle to a tendon. A fusiform muscle has long parallel fascicles, so this does not fit.","Correct. Fusiform fascicles run parallel, so the muscle shares the parallel trade-off.","Circular muscles form sphincters around openings such as the mouth. A fusiform muscle runs between bones and moves a joint."],"lt":"t-muscle-fascicles","lid":"d3x-t-muscle-fascicles-1","comp":78,"competencyId":"w4-fascicle-patterns"},{"id":"L04262","dok":1,"q":"Which list gives the three connective tissue coverings of a skeletal muscle in order, starting with the whole muscle and moving in to the single fiber?","a":"Epimysium, perimysium, endomysium.","options":["Epimysium, perimysium, endomysium","Epimysium, endomysium, perimysium","Perimysium, epimysium, endomysium","Endomysium, perimysium, epimysium"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The epimysium wraps the entire muscle, the perimysium wraps each fascicle (a bundle of muscle fibers), and the endomysium wraps each individual muscle fiber. The prefixes help: epi means upon, so it sits on the outside; peri means around, so it surrounds the bundles; endo means within, so it lies deepest, around each cell.","notes":["Correct. The order runs from the outside wrapping of the whole organ to the fine sheath around each cell.","This swaps the two inner sheaths. The endomysium wraps single fibers, which sit inside fascicles, so it must come after the perimysium, not before it.","This puts the perimysium outside the epimysium, but the perimysium wraps fascicles inside the muscle. Students choose it when they mix up the prefixes peri (around) and epi (upon).","This is the right three sheaths listed from the inside out, starting at the single fiber. It would be correct if the question asked you to start at the fiber and work outward to the whole muscle."],"lt":"t-w4-ct-coverings","lid":"w4-ct-coverings-r1","comp":72,"competencyId":"w4-ct-coverings"},{"id":"L04263","dok":1,"q":"What does the epimysium wrap, and what type of connective tissue is it?","a":"The entire muscle; dense irregular connective tissue.","options":["Each fascicle; dense irregular connective tissue","The entire muscle; dense regular connective tissue","The entire muscle; dense irregular connective tissue","Each muscle fiber; areolar connective tissue with reticular fibers"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The epimysium is the outermost sheath, wrapping the whole muscle organ. It is dense irregular connective tissue, meaning its collagen fibers run in many directions, which lets it resist pulling from any angle as the muscle changes shape. At the ends of the muscle it blends with the other sheaths into the tendon.","notes":["This describes the perimysium, which wraps each fascicle and is also dense irregular connective tissue. Pick it if the question asked which sheath surrounds a bundle of fibers.","The location is right, but dense regular connective tissue, with collagen fibers in parallel rows, is the tissue of a tendon, not the epimysium. Choose dense regular if the question asked what a tendon is made of.","Correct. The epimysium covers the entire muscle and is made of dense irregular connective tissue.","This describes the endomysium, the fine areolar sheath with reticular fibers around each muscle fiber. It would be the answer for the covering of a single muscle cell."],"lt":"t-w4-ct-coverings","lid":"w4-ct-coverings-r2","comp":72,"competencyId":"w4-ct-coverings"},{"id":"L04264","dok":1,"q":"What does the endomysium wrap, and what type of connective tissue is it?","a":"Each individual muscle fiber; fine areolar connective tissue with reticular fibers.","options":["Each fascicle; dense irregular connective tissue","Each myofibril; areolar connective tissue","The entire muscle; dense irregular connective tissue","Each muscle fiber; areolar connective tissue with reticular fibers"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The endomysium is the innermost and thinnest sheath, surrounding each single muscle fiber (muscle cell). It is a fine areolar connective tissue reinforced with reticular fibers, a delicate mesh that holds capillaries and nerve endings right against each cell. You can see it on a cross section as the thin pale line between neighboring fibers.","notes":["This describes the perimysium, which bundles fibers into fascicles. Pick it if the question asked what wraps a fascicle.","Myofibrils are contractile rods inside the muscle cell, so no connective tissue can wrap them individually. Students choose this when they confuse the fiber (the cell) with the myofibril (a structure within the cell).","This describes the epimysium, the outer sheath around the whole muscle. It would be the answer if the question asked about the outermost covering.","Correct. The endomysium wraps each muscle fiber and is areolar connective tissue with reticular fibers."],"lt":"t-w4-ct-coverings","lid":"w4-ct-coverings-r3","comp":72,"competencyId":"w4-ct-coverings"},{"id":"L04265","dok":2,"q":"How does the pull of a single muscle fiber get carried all the way to the bone?","a":"The three sheaths are continuous with one another and merge into the tendon, which attaches to the bone.","options":["Each muscle fiber has its own small tendon that attaches separately to bone","The sarcolemma of each fiber fuses directly with the bone at the insertion","Only the epimysium reaches the tendon, and the inner sheaths end within the belly","The three sheaths are continuous with one another and merge into the tendon"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The endomysium around each fiber is continuous with the perimysium around the fascicle, which is continuous with the epimysium around the whole muscle. At the end of the muscle all three blend together into the tendon, a cord of dense regular connective tissue that anchors to bone. Because the sheaths form one connected framework, the tug of every fiber is passed outward and on to the skeleton.","notes":["Muscle fibers do not each have a private tendon. Their pull is gathered by the shared sheaths into one tendon or aponeurosis, so this is a misconception about how force is collected.","The sarcolemma is the plasma membrane of the muscle cell and never attaches to bone directly. Muscle reaches bone only through connective tissue, the tendon or an aponeurosis.","The epimysium does merge into the tendon, but so do the perimysium and endomysium. If the inner sheaths stopped in the belly, the pull of individual fibers would never reach the bone.","Correct. The sheaths form one continuous connective tissue framework that becomes the tendon."],"lt":"t-w4-ct-coverings","lid":"w4-ct-coverings-r4","comp":72,"competencyId":"w4-ct-coverings"},{"id":"L04266","dok":3,"q":"A sprinter can strain a hamstring by tearing fibers in the belly, or rupture its tendon so the muscle separates from bone. Which structural feature explains why a single forceful pull can injure either site?","a":"The endomysium, perimysium, and epimysium are continuous with the tendon.","options":["The endomysium, perimysium, and epimysium are continuous with the tendon","The sarcoplasmic reticulum runs from the fibers into the tendon","The deep fascia binds neighboring muscles into one functional group","The superficial fascia connects the skin to the underlying muscle"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The three connective tissue sheaths are continuous with one another and with the tendon, so force travels in one connected line from each fiber to the bone. A sudden overload stresses that whole line at once, and it fails at its weakest point. Sometimes that is the fibers and endomysium in the belly (a strain), and sometimes it is the tendon or its attachment (a rupture).","notes":["Correct. Because the sheaths and tendon form one continuous chain, a pull loads both the belly and the tendon.","The sarcoplasmic reticulum is the calcium storing smooth ER inside each muscle fiber. It stays within the cell and has no part in the tendon, so it cannot transmit force to bone.","Deep fascia is the dense sheet that wraps and separates whole muscles and groups them by action. It would be the answer if the question asked what divides the limb into compartments, not what links a fiber to bone.","Superficial fascia is the subcutaneous layer of areolar and adipose tissue under the skin. It is the answer for what lies between the skin and muscles, but it does not carry muscle force to bone."],"lt":"t-w4-ct-coverings","lid":"w4-ct-coverings-r5","comp":72,"competencyId":"w4-ct-coverings"},{"id":"L04267","dok":1,"q":"Which list orders the levels of skeletal muscle organization from the whole organ down to the protein strands?","a":"Muscle, fascicle, muscle fiber, myofibril, myofilament.","options":["Muscle, fascicle, muscle fiber, myofibril, myofilament","Muscle, fascicle, muscle fiber, myofilament, myofibril","Muscle, muscle fiber, fascicle, myofibril, myofilament","Muscle, fascicle, myofibril, muscle fiber, myofilament"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"A whole muscle is made of fascicles, which are bundles of muscle fibers. Each muscle fiber is a single cell packed with myofibrils, long contractile rods. Each myofibril is in turn built from myofilaments, the thick and thin protein strands whose overlap creates the sarcomere.","notes":["Correct. Each level is a bundle of the next smaller one.","This swaps the last two levels. Myofilaments are the protein strands that make up a myofibril, so the myofilament is the smallest level and comes last.","This puts the fiber before the fascicle, but a fascicle is a bundle of fibers, so it must be the larger unit. Students choose this when they forget that fascicles are groups of cells.","This places the myofibril outside the muscle fiber, but myofibrils sit inside the fiber. The mix-up comes from the similar names fiber and fibril; a fibril is the smaller structure."],"lt":"t-w4-muscle-organization","lid":"w4-muscle-organization-r1","comp":73,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-organization"},{"id":"L04270","dok":2,"q":"Moving inward from the whole muscle, which structure is the first one you reach that is a single cell?","a":"The muscle fiber.","options":["The myofilament","The myofibril","The muscle fiber","The fascicle"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The whole muscle and each fascicle are groups of many cells, so neither is a single cell. The next level in, the muscle fiber, is one long multinucleated muscle cell. Everything below it, the myofibrils and myofilaments, are structures inside that cell rather than cells of their own.","notes":["A myofilament is a protein strand, far smaller than a cell. Pick it if the question asked for the smallest level of muscle organization.","A myofibril is a contractile rod inside the muscle cell, so you have already passed the cell level by the time you reach it. Students pick it because fibril sounds like fiber.","Correct. The muscle fiber is the single muscle cell, the first cell-level structure moving inward.","A fascicle is a bundle of many muscle fibers wrapped in perimysium, so it is a group of cells. It would be the answer if the question asked for the first bundle you reach inside the muscle."],"lt":"t-w4-muscle-organization","lid":"w4-muscle-organization-r5","comp":73,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-organization"},{"id":"L04317","dok":1,"q":"Which list names the three types of muscle tissue?","a":"Skeletal, cardiac, and smooth.","options":["Skeletal, cardiac, and smooth","Skeletal, smooth, and dense regular","Striated, cardiac, and smooth","Skeletal, cardiac, and elastic"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The three muscle tissues are skeletal muscle, attached to the skeleton and under voluntary control; cardiac muscle, found only in the heart wall; and smooth muscle, found in the walls of hollow organs and vessels. Skeletal and cardiac muscle are both striated, while smooth muscle is not. Each type is defined by where it is and how its cells look.","notes":["Correct. Skeletal, cardiac, and smooth are the three muscle tissues.","Dense regular connective tissue makes up tendons and ligaments, which attach to muscle but are not muscle tissue. It would be the answer if the question asked what a tendon is made of.","Striated describes a feature that skeletal and cardiac muscle share, not a separate tissue type. Pick striated if the question asked which feature skeletal and cardiac muscle have in common.","Elastic is a type of cartilage and a fiber of connective tissue, not a muscle tissue. Students pick it because elastic arteries and elastic recoil come up near muscle topics."],"lt":"t-w4-muscle-tissue-types","lid":"w4-muscle-tissue-types-r1","comp":77,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-tissue-types"},{"id":"L04318","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares skeletal and cardiac muscle?","a":"Both are striated; skeletal is voluntary with many peripheral nuclei, while cardiac is involuntary with a central nucleus and intercalated discs.","options":["Neither is striated; both are involuntary, and both have long cylindrical cells joined by intercalated discs","Both are striated; skeletal is voluntary with many peripheral nuclei, while cardiac is involuntary with a central nucleus and intercalated discs","Both are striated; skeletal is involuntary with one central nucleus, while cardiac is voluntary with many peripheral nuclei","Only skeletal is striated; skeletal is voluntary with many peripheral nuclei, while cardiac is involuntary with a central nucleus"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Skeletal and cardiac muscle both show striations because both are built from sarcomeres. Skeletal muscle is under voluntary control and its long cylindrical fibers have many nuclei pushed to the edge. Cardiac muscle works involuntarily, and its branching cells each have a central nucleus and are joined end to end by intercalated discs.","notes":["Both types are striated, skeletal muscle is voluntary, and intercalated discs are found only in cardiac muscle. Long cylindrical cells describe skeletal fibers, not the branching cells of the heart.","Correct. Both are striated, but they differ in control, nuclei, and the presence of intercalated discs.","This swaps the control and the nuclei between the two types. Skeletal muscle is the voluntary one with many peripheral nuclei, and cardiac is the involuntary one with a central nucleus.","Cardiac muscle is striated too, because its cells contain sarcomeres just like skeletal fibers. Smooth muscle is the type that lacks striations, so pick no striations if the question asked about smooth muscle."],"lt":"t-w4-muscle-tissue-types","lid":"w4-muscle-tissue-types-r2","comp":77,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-tissue-types"},{"id":"L04320","dok":1,"q":"Which list names the patterns of fascicle arrangement in skeletal muscle?","a":"Parallel, fusiform, circular, convergent, and pennate.","options":["Parallel, fusiform, circular, convergent, and pennate","Rectus, fusiform, circular, convergent, and pennate","Parallel, fusiform, circular, convergent, and rhomboid","Parallel, deltoid, circular, convergent, and pennate"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Fascicles are arranged in five basic patterns: parallel, fusiform, circular, convergent, and pennate. Pennate muscles are further split into unipennate, bipennate, and multipennate, depending on whether the fascicles attach to one side, both sides, or a branched central tendon. The arrangement gives each muscle its overall shape and affects its power and range of motion.","notes":["Correct. These are the five fascicle arrangement patterns.","Rectus means straight and is a fiber direction word used in muscle names, such as rectus abdominis. Pick rectus if the question asked which naming criterion describes fiber direction.","Rhomboid is a shape used in naming muscles, such as rhomboid major, not a fascicle pattern. It would be the answer if the question asked for an example of a muscle named for its shape.","Deltoid is a muscle named for its triangular shape, and it happens to be a multipennate example. Choose deltoid if the question asked for an example of a multipennate muscle."],"lt":"t-w4-fascicle-patterns","lid":"w4-fascicle-patterns-r1","comp":78,"competencyId":"w4-fascicle-patterns"},{"id":"L04321","dok":2,"q":"Which pair gives a correct example muscle for a circular arrangement and for a convergent arrangement?","a":"Circular: orbicularis oris; convergent: pectoralis major.","options":["Circular: pectoralis major; convergent: orbicularis oris","Circular: orbicularis oris; convergent: pectoralis major","Circular: deltoid; convergent: biceps brachii","Circular: orbicularis oris; convergent: sartorius"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"In a circular muscle the fascicles form concentric rings around an opening, acting as a sphincter, as in orbicularis oris around the mouth. In a convergent muscle a broad origin narrows onto a single tendon, giving a fan shape, as in pectoralis major. Matching each pattern to a familiar muscle makes the shapes easy to recognize on models.","notes":["This swaps the two examples. Pectoralis major fans out from the chest to one tendon, which is convergent, while orbicularis oris rings the mouth, which is circular.","Correct. Orbicularis oris is circular and pectoralis major is convergent.","The deltoid is multipennate, with short fascicles on a branched tendon, and the biceps brachii is fusiform, with a thick belly tapering at each end. Pick these if the question asked for multipennate and fusiform examples.","Orbicularis oris is the right circular example, but sartorius is a long strap muscle with parallel fascicles. Choose sartorius if the question asked for a parallel muscle."],"lt":"t-w4-fascicle-patterns","lid":"w4-fascicle-patterns-r2","comp":78,"competencyId":"w4-fascicle-patterns"},{"id":"L04322","dok":3,"q":"Two muscles are the same size, but one has pennate fascicles and the other has parallel fascicles. Which one produces more power, and why?","a":"The pennate muscle, because many short, angled fascicles pack more fibers pulling on its tendon.","options":["The parallel muscle, because its long fascicles shorten over a greater distance","The parallel muscle, because its long fascicles pull straight along the line of the tendon","The pennate muscle, because many short, angled fascicles pack more fibers onto its tendon","Neither, because muscles of equal size always produce equal power"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"In a pennate muscle, short fascicles attach at an angle to a central tendon like the barbs of a feather, so many more fibers fit into the same volume of muscle. More fibers pulling on the tendon means more power. The trade-off is range of motion: short, angled fascicles cannot move the insertion as far as the long fascicles of a parallel muscle.","notes":["Shortening over a greater distance is a real advantage of parallel muscles, but it gives a larger range of motion, not more power. Pick this reasoning if the question asked which muscle moves its insertion farther.","Long, straight fascicles do pull in line with the tendon, but there are fewer of them per unit of muscle, so the total force is lower. Students pick this because a straight pull sounds more efficient.","Correct. Pennate muscles trade range of motion for power by packing in more fibers.","Size alone does not set power; how the fascicles are arranged decides how many fibers pull on the tendon. This choice ignores fascicle architecture entirely."],"lt":"t-w4-fascicle-patterns","lid":"w4-fascicle-patterns-r3","comp":78,"competencyId":"w4-fascicle-patterns"}]},{"id":"m3-muscle-microanatomy","title":"Muscle fiber microanatomy and the sarcomere","summary":"sarcolemma, sarcoplasmic reticulum, T tubules, triad, myofibrils, sarcomere bands and lines, thick and thin filaments, myofilament proteins, neuromuscular junction","mw":"2026-10-07","tr":"2026-10-06","cards":[{"id":"L00960","dok":2,"q":"The prefix sarco means flesh. Which statement correctly matches two sarco terms to the ordinary cell parts they name?","a":"The sarcolemma is the plasma membrane, and the sarcoplasm is the cytoplasm.","options":["The sarcolemma is the plasma membrane, and the sarcoplasm is the cytoplasm","The sarcomere is the plasma membrane, and the sarcoplasm is the nucleus","The sarcolemma is the cytoplasm, and the sarcoplasm is the plasma membrane","The sarcolemma is the nucleus, and the sarcomere is the cytoplasm"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Muscle fibers have the same basic parts as other cells, but many of them carry the sarco prefix. The sarcolemma is the plasma membrane that wraps the fiber, and the sarcoplasm is the cytoplasm inside it. A helpful link is that lemma means husk or sheath, and plasm means the fluid material of the cell.","notes":["Correct. The sarcolemma is the plasma membrane, and the sarcoplasm is the cytoplasm.","The sarcomere is the contractile unit of a myofibril, running from one Z disc to the next, and the sarcoplasm is cytoplasm, not a nucleus. Pick sarcomere for a question about the repeating unit that creates striations.","This swaps the two terms. The sarcolemma is the membrane on the outside of the fiber, and the sarcoplasm is the cytoplasm inside it.","The sarcolemma is a membrane, not a nucleus, and the sarcomere is a contractile unit, not cytoplasm. The nuclei of a muscle fiber are simply called nuclei (myonuclei) and sit just under the sarcolemma."],"lt":"t-muscle-types","lid":"c24","comp":74,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-fiber-parts"},{"id":"L00966","dok":1,"q":"The prefix sarco in sarcolemma, sarcoplasm, and sarcomere comes from the Greek word for what?","a":"Flesh.","options":["Fiber","Cell","Flesh","Muscle"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Sarco comes from the Greek word for flesh, and it is attached to many structures of the muscle fiber. The sarcolemma is the fiber's plasma membrane, the sarcoplasm is its cytoplasm, and the sarcomere is the contractile unit of a myofibril. When you see sarco, you know the term names part of a muscle cell.","notes":["Fiber is the meaning of the root fibr, as in fibroblast. It is tempting because skeletal muscle cells are called fibers, but sarco itself means flesh.","Cell is the meaning of the root cyto, as in cytoplasm. Students pick it because the sarco terms rename ordinary cell parts, but the prefix itself means flesh.","Correct. Sarco means flesh.","Muscle is the meaning of the roots myo and mys, as in myocardium and epimysium. Pick muscle for a question about those roots."],"lt":"t-muscle-types","lid":"c30","comp":74,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-fiber-parts"},{"id":"L01056","dok":1,"q":"Where are the many nuclei of a skeletal muscle fiber located?","a":"Just under the sarcolemma, at the edge of the fiber.","options":["Just under the sarcolemma","In the center of the fiber","Inside the sarcoplasmic reticulum","Inside the myofibrils"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"A skeletal muscle fiber is multinucleated, and its many nuclei are flattened against the inner surface of the sarcolemma, the fiber's plasma membrane. The middle of the fiber is packed with myofibrils, which leaves little room for nuclei there. Peripheral nuclei are one of the features that identify skeletal muscle on a slide.","notes":["Correct. Skeletal muscle nuclei sit just under the sarcolemma.","A central nucleus is typical of cardiac and smooth muscle cells, each of which has one. Pick the center of the cell for a question about those tissues.","The sarcoplasmic reticulum is a network of tubules that stores calcium ions, and it does not hold nuclei. Pick it for a question about calcium storage in the fiber.","Myofibrils are contractile rods made of protein filaments, and they contain no nuclei. Pick myofibrils for a question about what fills most of the sarcoplasm."],"lt":"t-muscle-microanatomy","lid":"c1","comp":74,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-fiber-parts"},{"id":"L01057","dok":1,"q":"What is the cytoplasm of a muscle fiber called?","a":"Sarcoplasm.","options":["Sarcomere","Sarcoplasm","Sarcolemma","Sarcoplasmic reticulum"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The sarcoplasm is the cytoplasm of a muscle fiber, the material that fills the cell inside its plasma membrane. It holds the myofibrils, mitochondria, glycogen granules, and myoglobin. The name combines sarco, meaning flesh, with plasm, the term for the fluid contents of a cell.","notes":["The sarcomere is the contractile unit of a myofibril, from one Z disc to the next. Pick it for a question about the repeating unit that creates striations.","Correct. The sarcoplasm is the cytoplasm of a muscle fiber.","The sarcolemma is the plasma membrane that wraps the fiber, not the material inside it. Pick it for a question about the muscle fiber's cell membrane.","The sarcoplasmic reticulum is the network of smooth endoplasmic reticulum inside the fiber that stores calcium. It sits within the sarcoplasm but is an organelle, not the cytoplasm itself."],"lt":"t-muscle-microanatomy","lid":"c2","comp":74,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-fiber-parts"},{"id":"L01058","dok":1,"q":"What is the plasma membrane of a muscle fiber called?","a":"The sarcolemma.","options":["The sarcolemma","The sarcoplasm","The endomysium","The sarcoplasmic reticulum"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The sarcolemma is the plasma membrane of a muscle fiber, the boundary of the cell. Its surface dives inward at regular intervals to form the T tubules, which carry signals deep into the fiber. Just outside the sarcolemma lies the endomysium, the connective tissue sheath around each fiber.","notes":["Correct. The sarcolemma is the plasma membrane of the muscle fiber.","The sarcoplasm is the cytoplasm inside the fiber, not its membrane. Pick it for a question about the fluid material that holds the myofibrils.","The endomysium is a connective tissue sheath just outside the sarcolemma, not part of the cell itself. Pick it for a question about the sheath around each individual fiber.","The sarcoplasmic reticulum is an internal network of membrane tubules that stores calcium, not the outer cell membrane. Pick it for a question about calcium storage."],"lt":"t-muscle-microanatomy","lid":"c3","comp":74,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-fiber-parts"},{"id":"L01059","dok":1,"q":"What are glycosomes in the sarcoplasm of a muscle fiber?","a":"Granules of stored glycogen that supply glucose for energy.","options":["Organelles that produce most of the cell's ATP","Red pigment molecules that store oxygen","Sacs that store calcium ions","Granules of stored glycogen that supply glucose for energy"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Glycosomes are glycogen granules, small stores of carbohydrate fuel held in the sarcoplasm. When the muscle is active, the glycogen is broken down to glucose, which the cell uses to make ATP, the energy molecule contraction depends on. Muscle fibers hold far more of these granules than ordinary cells because their energy demand is so high.","notes":["The organelles that produce most of the cell's ATP are mitochondria, packed in rows between the myofibrils. Glycosomes supply the fuel, but mitochondria do most of the ATP production.","A red pigment that stores oxygen is myoglobin, which also gives muscle its color. Pick myoglobin for a question about the muscle's oxygen store.","Sacs that store calcium ions are part of the sarcoplasmic reticulum, especially its terminal cisterns. Pick those for a question about calcium storage.","Correct. Glycosomes are granules of stored glycogen."],"lt":"t-muscle-microanatomy","lid":"c4","comp":74,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-fiber-parts"},{"id":"L01060","dok":1,"q":"Which red pigment in the sarcoplasm stores oxygen and is similar to hemoglobin?","a":"Myoglobin.","options":["Glycogen","Myosin","Myoglobin","Melanin"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Myoglobin is a red, oxygen-binding pigment found in the sarcoplasm of muscle fibers. Like hemoglobin in red blood cells, it holds onto oxygen, but it stores the oxygen inside the muscle fiber where it can be used during activity. Myoglobin is also what gives muscle its red color.","notes":["Glycogen is stored carbohydrate fuel held as granules in the sarcoplasm, and it is not a pigment. Pick glycogen for a question about the muscle's stored glucose.","Myosin is the protein that makes up the thick filaments inside a myofibril. The names look alike, but myosin is a contractile protein, not an oxygen store.","Correct. Myoglobin stores oxygen in the sarcoplasm.","Melanin is the brown to black pigment made by melanocytes in the epidermis of the skin. Pick it for a question about skin color."],"lt":"t-muscle-microanatomy","lid":"c5","comp":74,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-fiber-parts"},{"id":"L01061","dok":1,"q":"Which three specialized structures inside a skeletal muscle fiber are highly modified for contraction?","a":"Myofibrils, sarcoplasmic reticulum, and T tubules.","options":["Myofibrils, sarcoplasmic reticulum, and T tubules","Nuclei, ribosomes, and centrioles","Myofibrils, Golgi apparatus, and lysosomes","Sarcomeres, fascicles, and endomysium"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Three structures in a muscle fiber are specialized for contraction. Myofibrils are the contractile rods, the sarcoplasmic reticulum is the network that stores calcium around each myofibril, and the T tubules are inward folds of the sarcolemma that carry the surface signal deep into the fiber. Together they let a large fiber contract quickly and as a unit.","notes":["Correct. Myofibrils, sarcoplasmic reticulum, and T tubules are the specialized structures of the fiber.","Nuclei, ribosomes, and centrioles are general cell structures. Mature skeletal muscle fibers do have many nuclei, but none of these three is specially modified for contraction.","Myofibrils belong on the list, but the Golgi apparatus and lysosomes are ordinary organelles found in most cells. They are not the structures specially modified for contraction.","Fascicles and endomysium are outside the fiber: a fascicle is a bundle of fibers, and the endomysium is the sheath around each fiber. Sarcomeres are units within a myofibril rather than separate organelles."],"lt":"t-muscle-microanatomy","lid":"c6","comp":74,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-fiber-parts"},{"id":"L01062","dok":1,"q":"Myofibrils fill most of a skeletal muscle fiber. About what share of the fiber's volume do they make up?","a":"About 80 percent.","options":["Nearly 100 percent","About 50 percent","About 20 percent","About 80 percent"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Myofibrils make up about 80 percent of a skeletal muscle fiber's volume. They run the full length of the fiber, packed side by side, with hundreds to thousands in each fiber. This dense packing is why the nuclei are pushed to the edge and the mitochondria are squeezed into rows between the myofibrils.","notes":["The fiber still needs room for nuclei, mitochondria, sarcoplasmic reticulum, and glycogen, so myofibrils cannot fill all of it. Students choose this when they picture the fiber as nothing but contractile rods.","Half the volume underestimates how tightly myofibrils are packed. The other organelles are crowded into a much smaller share of the space.","Twenty percent is far too low for the contractile rods that fill the fiber. Students who pick this may be thinking of a minor organelle rather than the main contents of the cell.","Correct. Myofibrils make up about 80 percent of the fiber's volume."],"lt":"t-muscle-microanatomy","lid":"c7","comp":74,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-fiber-parts"},{"id":"L01063","dok":1,"q":"What is a myofibril?","a":"A long contractile rod inside a muscle fiber, made of repeating sarcomeres.","options":["A single muscle cell wrapped in endomysium","A contractile rod built of repeating sarcomeres","A bundle of fibers wrapped in perimysium","A single strand of actin or myosin protein"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"A myofibril is a long, rod-shaped bundle of contractile proteins that runs the length of a muscle fiber. Each myofibril is a chain of sarcomeres, the repeating contractile units, and hundreds to thousands of myofibrils fill each fiber. In the levels of organization, the myofibril sits between the muscle fiber and the myofilament.","notes":["A single muscle cell wrapped in endomysium is a muscle fiber, the level above the myofibril. Pick it for a question about what the endomysium surrounds.","Correct. A myofibril is a contractile rod inside the fiber, built of sarcomeres.","A bundle of fibers wrapped in perimysium is a fascicle, two levels above the myofibril. Pick it for a question about what the perimysium surrounds.","A single strand of actin or myosin is a myofilament, the level below the myofibril. Pick it for a question about the thick and thin filaments."],"lt":"t-muscle-microanatomy","lid":"c8","comp":74,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-fiber-parts"},{"id":"L01064","dok":1,"q":"The sarcoplasmic reticulum is a specialized form of which organelle?","a":"Smooth endoplasmic reticulum.","options":["Golgi apparatus","Mitochondrion","Rough endoplasmic reticulum","Smooth endoplasmic reticulum"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The sarcoplasmic reticulum is the smooth endoplasmic reticulum of the muscle fiber, renamed with the sarco prefix. It has no ribosomes on its surface, which is what makes it smooth. In muscle it is specialized to wrap each myofibril and store calcium ions.","notes":["The Golgi apparatus modifies, sorts, and packages proteins into vesicles. Pick it for a question about packaging cell products for secretion.","Mitochondria produce most of the cell's ATP and sit in rows between the myofibrils, but they are not related to the sarcoplasmic reticulum. Pick them for a question about energy production.","Rough endoplasmic reticulum is studded with ribosomes and makes proteins for export. Pick it for a question about the organelle that builds secreted proteins.","Correct. The sarcoplasmic reticulum is specialized smooth endoplasmic reticulum."],"lt":"t-muscle-microanatomy","lid":"c9","comp":74,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-fiber-parts"},{"id":"L01065","dok":1,"q":"How is the sarcoplasmic reticulum arranged relative to each myofibril?","a":"As a network of tubules running lengthwise around each myofibril, like a sleeve.","options":["As one large sac in the center of the fiber","As a network of tubules running lengthwise around each myofibril, like a sleeve","As a thin layer of connective tissue around the whole fiber","As tubes that dive straight inward from the sarcolemma across the fiber"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The sarcoplasmic reticulum forms a network of interconnecting tubules that wraps each myofibril like a loose sleeve, with most tubules running along the length of the myofibril. This arrangement keeps the calcium store close to every part of every myofibril. At regular intervals the network widens into terminal cisterns that sit beside the T tubules.","notes":["A single central sac would leave most myofibrils far from the calcium store. The network instead surrounds every myofibril, which is why it works as a sleeve.","Correct. The sarcoplasmic reticulum wraps each myofibril like a sleeve.","A connective tissue layer around the whole fiber is the endomysium, which lies outside the cell. The sarcoplasmic reticulum is an organelle inside the fiber.","Tubes diving straight inward from the sarcolemma are T tubules, which run across the fiber rather than along each myofibril. Pick this description for a question about T tubules."],"lt":"t-muscle-microanatomy","lid":"c10","comp":74,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-fiber-parts"},{"id":"L01066","dok":1,"q":"What are the enlarged end sacs of the sarcoplasmic reticulum called?","a":"Terminal cisterns.","options":["T tubules","Terminal cisterns","Myofibrils","Glycosomes"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Terminal cisterns (also called terminal cisternae) are the enlarged sacs at the ends of the sarcoplasmic reticulum's tubules. They always occur in pairs, one on each side of a T tubule, and together with that T tubule they form a triad. Because they are part of the sarcoplasmic reticulum, they hold stored calcium ions.","notes":["T tubules are inward folds of the sarcolemma, not part of the sarcoplasmic reticulum. A T tubule sits between two terminal cisterns, so pick it for a question about the middle tube of a triad.","Correct. The enlarged end sacs of the sarcoplasmic reticulum are terminal cisterns.","Myofibrils are the contractile rods that the sarcoplasmic reticulum wraps around. Pick them for a question about what fills most of the fiber.","Glycosomes are granules of stored glycogen in the sarcoplasm, not membrane sacs. Pick them for a question about the muscle's carbohydrate fuel."],"lt":"t-muscle-microanatomy","lid":"c11","comp":74,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-fiber-parts"},{"id":"L01067","dok":1,"q":"In skeletal muscle, the terminal cisterns form cross channels at which location along the sarcomere?","a":"At each junction of an A band and an I band.","options":["At the center of the Z disc","At each junction of an A band and an I band","At the M line","Within the H zone"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The terminal cisterns form channels that run across the myofibril where each dark A band meets a light I band. A T tubule also dives into the fiber at each of these junctions, so the paired terminal cisterns and the T tubule form a triad there. Each sarcomere therefore has two triads, one at each A band and I band junction.","notes":["The Z disc is the protein plate at each end of the sarcomere that anchors the thin filaments. Pick it for a question about the border of a sarcomere.","Correct. Terminal cisterns sit at the A band and I band junctions.","The M line is the protein line at the center of the H zone that anchors the thick filaments. Pick it for a question about what holds the thick filaments together.","The H zone is the lighter region in the center of the A band with thick filaments only. It is where the sarcoplasmic reticulum tubules connect with each other, not where the terminal cisterns form their cross channels."],"lt":"t-muscle-microanatomy","lid":"c12","comp":74,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-fiber-parts"},{"id":"L01068","dok":1,"q":"In one line, what is the main role of the sarcoplasmic reticulum in a muscle fiber?","a":"It stores calcium ions and releases them into the sarcoplasm.","options":["It carries the surface signal deep into the fiber","It produces most of the fiber's ATP","It stores oxygen for the fiber","It stores calcium ions and releases them into the sarcoplasm"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The sarcoplasmic reticulum is the calcium store of the muscle fiber. It holds calcium ions (Ca2+) inside its tubules and terminal cisterns and releases them into the sarcoplasm when the fiber is stimulated, which allows contraction to happen. Its sleeve-like arrangement keeps this store close to every myofibril.","notes":["Carrying the surface signal deep into the fiber is the job of the T tubules, which are inward folds of the sarcolemma. Pick T tubules for that question.","Most of the fiber's ATP comes from mitochondria, packed in rows between the myofibrils. Pick mitochondria for a question about energy production.","Oxygen is stored by myoglobin, a red pigment in the sarcoplasm. Pick myoglobin for a question about the fiber's oxygen store.","Correct. The sarcoplasmic reticulum stores and releases calcium ions."],"lt":"t-muscle-microanatomy","lid":"c13","comp":74,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-fiber-parts"},{"id":"L01069","dok":1,"q":"What is a T tubule?","a":"An inward extension of the sarcolemma that runs deep into the fiber.","options":["A contractile rod running the length of the fiber","An inward extension of the sarcolemma that runs deep into the fiber","A capillary running between neighboring muscle fibers","An enlarged end sac of the sarcoplasmic reticulum"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"A T tubule, or transverse tubule, is a narrow tunnel formed where the sarcolemma dives inward, running across the fiber at right angles to its length. Because it is continuous with the sarcolemma, it carries the signal that arrives at the cell surface deep into the interior of the fiber. Each T tubule runs between a pair of terminal cisterns.","notes":["A contractile rod running the length of the fiber is a myofibril. Pick it for a question about what fills most of the sarcoplasm.","Correct. A T tubule is an inward extension of the sarcolemma.","Capillaries run outside the fibers in the endomysium and carry blood, not signals. Pick capillaries for a question about blood supply to the fibers.","An enlarged end sac of the sarcoplasmic reticulum is a terminal cistern. Pick it for a question about the sacs on either side of a T tubule."],"lt":"t-muscle-microanatomy","lid":"c14","comp":74,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-fiber-parts"},{"id":"L01070","dok":1,"q":"Between which structures does a T tubule run?","a":"Between two terminal cisterns of the sarcoplasmic reticulum.","options":["Between two Z discs of one sarcomere","Between two neighboring muscle fibers","Between two terminal cisterns of the sarcoplasmic reticulum","Between the thick and thin filaments of a myofibril"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Each T tubule passes between a pair of terminal cisterns, the enlarged sacs of the sarcoplasmic reticulum. One T tubule plus the two terminal cisterns beside it forms a triad. This places the surface signal carried by the T tubule right next to the calcium stored in the sarcoplasmic reticulum.","notes":["The space between two Z discs is one sarcomere, the contractile unit of a myofibril. Pick this for a question about the length of a sarcomere.","The space between neighboring fibers is filled by endomysium, the connective tissue sheath around each fiber. T tubules are inside a single fiber.","Correct. A T tubule runs between two terminal cisterns.","Thick and thin filaments overlap inside the myofibril, and no T tubule runs between them. Pick this for a question about the zone of overlap in a sarcomere."],"lt":"t-muscle-microanatomy","lid":"c15","comp":74,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-fiber-parts"},{"id":"L01071","dok":1,"q":"Which structures make up a triad?","a":"One T tubule and two terminal cisterns.","options":["One terminal cistern and two mitochondria","One T tubule and two terminal cisterns","Two T tubules and one terminal cistern","One myofibril and two Z discs"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"A triad is a set of three structures: one T tubule in the middle with a terminal cistern of the sarcoplasmic reticulum on each side. It is the meeting point where the surface signal carried by the T tubule reaches the calcium store of the sarcoplasmic reticulum. Triads repeat along every myofibril at each A band and I band junction.","notes":["Mitochondria lie in rows between the myofibrils, but they are not part of a triad. A triad is made only of the T tubule and the two sacs of the sarcoplasmic reticulum.","Correct. A triad is one T tubule flanked by two terminal cisterns.","This reverses the count. Terminal cisterns always come in pairs, and the single T tubule sits between them.","A myofibril with Z discs describes the contractile rod and the borders of its sarcomeres. Pick this for a question about the structure of a myofibril, not a triad."],"lt":"t-muscle-microanatomy","lid":"c16","comp":74,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-fiber-parts"},{"id":"L01072","dok":2,"q":"A structure is described as a single long, cylindrical cell with many peripheral nuclei, wrapped in endomysium. Which level of muscle organization is it?","a":"The muscle fiber.","options":["The myofibril","The fascicle","The muscle fiber","The sarcomere"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"A muscle fiber is one skeletal muscle cell. It is long and cylindrical, carries many nuclei just under the sarcolemma, and is wrapped in its own endomysium. It sits between the fascicle (a bundle of fibers) above it and the myofibril (a contractile rod inside it) below it.","notes":["A myofibril is a contractile rod inside the fiber, and it has no nuclei of its own. Pick it for a question about the level just below the fiber.","A fascicle is a bundle of many fibers wrapped in perimysium, not a single cell. Pick it for a question about the level above the fiber.","Correct. A single multinucleated cell wrapped in endomysium is a muscle fiber.","A sarcomere is the repeating contractile unit of a myofibril, from one Z disc to the next. Pick it for a question about the smallest unit that contracts."],"lt":"t-muscle-microanatomy","lid":"c17","comp":73,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-organization"},{"id":"L01073","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares myoglobin and glycosomes in a muscle fiber?","a":"Myoglobin stores oxygen, and glycosomes store glycogen as a source of glucose.","options":["Myoglobin stores oxygen, and glycosomes store glycogen as a source of glucose","Myoglobin stores calcium ions, and glycosomes store oxygen","Both store oxygen, but only myoglobin is red","Myoglobin stores glycogen, and glycosomes store oxygen"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"A working muscle fiber needs both oxygen and fuel to make ATP. Myoglobin, a red pigment in the sarcoplasm, stores oxygen. Glycosomes are granules of glycogen that are broken down to glucose when the fiber is active, supplying the fuel side of energy production.","notes":["Correct. Myoglobin stores oxygen and glycosomes store glycogen.","Calcium ions are stored in the sarcoplasmic reticulum, not by myoglobin, and glycosomes store fuel rather than oxygen. Pick the sarcoplasmic reticulum for a question about calcium storage.","Only myoglobin binds oxygen. Glycosomes are fuel stores made of glycogen, so this statement gives them a job they do not have.","This swaps the two. Myoglobin is the oxygen-binding pigment, and glycosomes are the glycogen granules."],"lt":"t-muscle-microanatomy","lid":"c18","comp":74,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-fiber-parts"},{"id":"L01074","dok":2,"q":"Why do the mitochondria and other organelles look squashed in a skeletal muscle fiber?","a":"Densely packed myofibrils crowd the other organelles into the spaces between them.","options":["Densely packed myofibrils crowd the other organelles into the spaces between them","The endomysium presses in on the fiber from outside","The many nuclei take up most of the center of the fiber","The sarcolemma shrinks around the organelles during contraction"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Myofibrils make up about 80 percent of a skeletal muscle fiber's volume. With so much space taken by these contractile rods, the mitochondria and other organelles are squeezed into the narrow gaps between them. This crowding is why the organelles look flattened and why the mitochondria line up in rows.","notes":["Correct. Densely packed myofibrils crowd the other organelles.","The endomysium is a delicate areolar sheath that does not compress the fiber. The crowding comes from inside the cell, from the myofibrils.","The nuclei of a skeletal muscle fiber are pushed to the edge, just under the sarcolemma, not in the center. They are themselves crowded aside by the myofibrils.","The sarcolemma does not shrink around the organelles; the fiber keeps its volume. Students choose this when they picture contraction squeezing the cell from the outside."],"lt":"t-muscle-microanatomy","lid":"c19","comp":74,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-fiber-parts"},{"id":"L01075","dok":2,"q":"Which structure carries the signal that arrives at the sarcolemma deep into the interior of the muscle fiber?","a":"The T tubule.","options":["The endomysium","The T tubule","The terminal cistern","The myofibril"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"T tubules are narrow tunnels of the sarcolemma that dive into the fiber at right angles to its length. Because they are continuous with the surface membrane, they carry the surface signal deep into the cell. This lets the signal reach the sarcoplasmic reticulum around every myofibril at almost the same moment.","notes":["The endomysium is a connective tissue sheath outside the fiber. It carries capillaries and nerve branches to the fiber, but it does not carry signals into the cell's interior.","Correct. The T tubule carries the surface signal deep into the fiber.","A terminal cistern is an enlarged sac of the sarcoplasmic reticulum that stores calcium. It receives the signal brought by the T tubule beside it rather than carrying it inward.","A myofibril is a contractile rod that shortens in response to the signal, not the pathway that carries it. Pick it for a question about what fills most of the fiber."],"lt":"t-muscle-microanatomy","lid":"c20","comp":74,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-fiber-parts"},{"id":"L01077","dok":2,"q":"Which sarco term names the contractile unit of a myofibril, running from one Z disc to the next?","a":"The sarcomere.","options":["The sarcolemma","The sarcomere","The sarcoplasmic reticulum","The sarcoplasm"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The sarcomere is the contractile unit of muscle, the segment of a myofibril from one Z disc to the next. Thousands of sarcomeres line up end to end along each myofibril, and their repeating light and dark bands create the striations of skeletal muscle. The name combines sarco, flesh, with mere, meaning part.","notes":["The sarcolemma is the plasma membrane of the muscle fiber. Pick it for a question about the fiber's cell membrane.","Correct. The sarcomere is the unit from one Z disc to the next.","The sarcoplasmic reticulum is the smooth endoplasmic reticulum that wraps each myofibril and stores calcium. Pick it for a question about calcium storage.","The sarcoplasm is the cytoplasm of the muscle fiber. Pick it for a question about the fluid that holds the myofibrils."],"lt":"t-muscle-microanatomy","lid":"c22","comp":75,"competencyId":"w4-sarcomere"},{"id":"L01078","dok":2,"q":"Which even smaller structures make up a myofibril?","a":"Myofilaments.","options":["T tubules","Terminal cisterns","Myofilaments","Fascicles"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Each myofibril is built from myofilaments, the thick and thin protein strands that are the smallest level of muscle organization. Thick filaments are made mainly of myosin and thin filaments mainly of actin. Their orderly overlap forms the sarcomeres that repeat along the myofibril.","notes":["T tubules are inward folds of the sarcolemma that run between myofibrils, not inside them. Pick them for a question about how the surface signal reaches the fiber's interior.","Terminal cisterns are sacs of the sarcoplasmic reticulum that sit outside the myofibril, wrapped around it. Pick them for a question about the parts of a triad.","Correct. Myofibrils are made of myofilaments.","Fascicles are bundles of muscle fibers, which makes them much larger than a myofibril. Pick fascicles for a question about what the perimysium wraps."],"lt":"t-muscle-microanatomy","lid":"c23","comp":73,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-organization"},{"id":"L01079","dok":2,"q":"Most tubules of the sarcoplasmic reticulum run lengthwise along the myofibril. At which region of the sarcomere do they connect with one another?","a":"At the H zone.","options":["At the H zone","At each A band and I band junction","At the Z disc","Within the I band"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The longitudinal tubules of the sarcoplasmic reticulum run along the myofibril and connect with each other in the region of the H zone, the lighter center of the A band. At the A band and I band junctions, the network widens into terminal cisterns that sit beside the T tubules. Knowing these two landmarks helps you picture the sleeve of sarcoplasmic reticulum around each sarcomere.","notes":["Correct. The sarcoplasmic reticulum tubules connect with each other at the H zone.","The A band and I band junctions are where the terminal cisterns and T tubules form triads. Pick this location for a question about where triads are found.","The Z disc is the protein plate at each end of the sarcomere that anchors the thin filaments. Pick it for a question about the border of a sarcomere.","The I band is the light band of thin filaments only, with a Z disc through its center. Pick it for a question about the region of a sarcomere that contains only thin filaments."],"lt":"t-muscle-microanatomy","lid":"c24","comp":74,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-fiber-parts"},{"id":"L01080","dok":2,"q":"Which pair of structures is found in large numbers around the myofibrils and sarcoplasmic reticulum, supplying energy for contraction?","a":"Mitochondria and glycogen granules.","options":["Ribosomes and lysosomes","Mitochondria and glycogen granules","Nuclei and centrioles","T tubules and terminal cisterns"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Contraction uses a great deal of ATP, so the fiber packs energy-producing structures close to where they are needed. Mitochondria, which make most of the ATP, sit in rows between the myofibrils, and glycogen granules supply the glucose fuel. Both cluster around the myofibrils and sarcoplasmic reticulum.","notes":["Ribosomes build proteins and lysosomes digest worn-out material. They are found in muscle fibers but are not the structures supplying energy for contraction.","Correct. Mitochondria and glycogen granules supply the energy for contraction.","Nuclei in a skeletal muscle fiber are pushed to the edge under the sarcolemma, and centrioles organize cell division. Neither supplies energy for contraction.","T tubules and terminal cisterns form the triads that bring the signal next to the calcium store. They help start contraction but do not supply its energy."],"lt":"t-muscle-microanatomy","lid":"c25","comp":74,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-fiber-parts"},{"id":"L01081","dok":2,"q":"Terminal cisterns are enlarged parts of which structure?","a":"The sarcoplasmic reticulum.","options":["The sarcolemma","The sarcoplasmic reticulum","The T tubule","The myofibril"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Terminal cisterns are the enlarged end sacs of the sarcoplasmic reticulum, the smooth endoplasmic reticulum of the muscle fiber. They sit in pairs on either side of a T tubule. Students often confuse which part of the triad belongs to which membrane system, so remember that the two side sacs belong to the sarcoplasmic reticulum and the middle tube is an inward fold of the sarcolemma.","notes":["The sarcolemma is the plasma membrane, and its inward folds form the T tubules, not the terminal cisterns. Pick it for a question about what the T tubules are continuous with.","Correct. Terminal cisterns are part of the sarcoplasmic reticulum.","The T tubule is the middle tube of the triad, flanked by the terminal cisterns but not part of the same membrane system. Pick it for a question about the inward folds of the sarcolemma.","A myofibril is the contractile rod that the sarcoplasmic reticulum wraps around. It contains myofilaments, not membrane sacs."],"lt":"t-muscle-microanatomy","lid":"c26","comp":74,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-fiber-parts"},{"id":"L01082","dok":2,"q":"Which list orders the levels of organization from a whole muscle fiber down to the smallest level?","a":"Muscle fiber, myofibril, myofilament.","options":["Myofilament, myofibril, muscle fiber","Muscle fiber, myofilament, myofibril","Muscle fiber, myofibril, myofilament","Myofibril, muscle fiber, myofilament"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"A muscle fiber is a single cell, and inside it are hundreds to thousands of myofibrils, the contractile rods. Each myofibril, in turn, is built of myofilaments, the thick and thin protein strands. Going from the fiber downward, the order is fiber, myofibril, myofilament.","notes":["This list is the right sequence read from smallest to largest. Pick it if the question asks you to build up from the smallest level to the whole fiber.","Myofilaments make up myofibrils, so they are smaller and must come last. This order swaps the last two levels.","Correct. The order from fiber downward is muscle fiber, myofibril, myofilament.","The myofibril is inside the fiber, so it cannot come before the fiber when moving downward in size. This order swaps the first two levels."],"lt":"t-muscle-microanatomy","lid":"c27","comp":73,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-organization"},{"id":"L01083","dok":2,"q":"On an electron micrograph, a technician finds one narrow central tube flanked by two larger sacs at an A band and I band junction. Which structure is this?","a":"A triad.","options":["A myofibril","A triad","A sarcomere","An intercalated disc"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"One central tube with a larger sac on each side is a triad. The central tube is a T tubule, an inward fold of the sarcolemma, and the two sacs are terminal cisterns of the sarcoplasmic reticulum. Triads sit at each A band and I band junction, which matches where the technician found this one.","notes":["A myofibril is a long contractile rod made of sarcomeres, much longer than the small cluster described here. Pick it for a question about what the sarcoplasmic reticulum wraps around.","Correct. One T tubule flanked by two terminal cisterns is a triad.","A sarcomere is the segment of a myofibril from one Z disc to the next, made of overlapping filaments rather than a tube and sacs. Pick it for a question about the repeating contractile unit.","An intercalated disc is the junction joining two cardiac muscle cells end to end. Pick it for a question about how cardiac cells connect, not about structures inside a skeletal muscle fiber."],"lt":"t-muscle-microanatomy","lid":"c28","comp":74,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-fiber-parts"},{"id":"L01084","dok":3,"q":"A skeletal muscle fiber is a very large cell, yet the whole fiber responds almost at once when stimulated. Why is the triad important for this?","a":"It brings the surface signal carried by the T tubule right next to the calcium stored in the sarcoplasmic reticulum.","options":["It anchors the thick filaments at the sarcomere center","It connects the muscle fiber to its tendon","It joins neighboring fibers so they contract together","It puts the T tubule's signal beside the calcium store"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The triad is the structural meeting point where a surface signal reaches the calcium store. The T tubule carries the signal from the sarcolemma deep into the fiber, and the two terminal cisterns beside it hold the calcium. Because triads repeat tightly along every myofibril, the signal reaches the calcium store throughout the whole fiber almost at the same moment.","notes":["Anchoring the thick filaments at the center of the sarcomere is the job of the M line. Pick the M line for that question.","The connection from muscle to tendon is made by the connective tissue sheaths, which merge into the tendon. Pick the sheaths for a question about how a fiber's pull reaches the bone.","Skeletal muscle fibers are not joined to each other electrically. Cells joined so they contract together describes the intercalated discs of cardiac muscle.","Correct. The triad places the T tubule's signal beside the calcium store of the sarcoplasmic reticulum."],"lt":"t-muscle-microanatomy","lid":"c29","comp":74,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-fiber-parts"},{"id":"L01085","dok":2,"q":"A muscle that resists fatigue looks deep red when sectioned. Which component of its sarcoplasm chiefly accounts for the red color and the stored oxygen?","a":"Myoglobin.","options":["Collagen","Myoglobin","Glycogen","Myosin"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Myoglobin is a red, oxygen-binding pigment in the sarcoplasm. Muscles with a lot of myoglobin look deep red and can keep working for a long time, because they have a steady supply of stored oxygen for making ATP. Muscle with little myoglobin looks paler.","notes":["Collagen is the main fiber of the connective tissue sheaths and tendons, which look white rather than red. Pick it for a question about what makes tendons strong.","Correct. Myoglobin gives muscle its red color and stores oxygen.","Glycogen is stored carbohydrate fuel and has no red color. Pick it for a question about the muscle's stored glucose.","Myosin is the protein of the thick filaments, and it is not a pigment. The similar name makes it easy to confuse with myoglobin."],"lt":"t-muscle-microanatomy","lid":"c30","comp":74,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-fiber-parts"},{"id":"L01086","dok":2,"q":"A student confuses the T tubule with the sarcoplasmic reticulum. Which statement correctly tells them apart?","a":"The T tubule is an inward extension of the sarcolemma that carries the surface signal inward, while the sarcoplasmic reticulum is smooth endoplasmic reticulum that stores calcium.","options":["The T tubule is smooth endoplasmic reticulum that stores calcium, while the sarcoplasmic reticulum is an inward extension of the sarcolemma","The T tubule is an inward extension of the sarcolemma that carries the surface signal inward, while the sarcoplasmic reticulum is smooth endoplasmic reticulum that stores calcium","Both are parts of the sarcoplasmic reticulum, but only the T tubule stores calcium","Both are inward extensions of the sarcolemma, but only the sarcoplasmic reticulum reaches the myofibrils"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The T tubule and the sarcoplasmic reticulum come from different membranes and do different jobs. The T tubule is part of the sarcolemma, folded inward to carry the surface signal deep into the fiber. The sarcoplasmic reticulum is the fiber's smooth endoplasmic reticulum, wrapped around each myofibril to store calcium. At a triad, one T tubule sits between two terminal cisterns of the sarcoplasmic reticulum.","notes":["This swaps the two structures. The T tubule is the inward fold of the sarcolemma, and the sarcoplasmic reticulum is the calcium store.","Correct. The T tubule comes from the sarcolemma and carries the signal, while the sarcoplasmic reticulum stores calcium.","The T tubule is not part of the sarcoplasmic reticulum, and it does not store calcium. Only the sarcoplasmic reticulum, including its terminal cisterns, stores calcium.","Only the T tubule is an extension of the sarcolemma. The sarcoplasmic reticulum is a separate internal organelle."],"lt":"t-muscle-microanatomy","lid":"c31","comp":74,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-fiber-parts"},{"id":"L01087","dok":2,"q":"Which structural protein links the thin filaments to the sarcolemma and is faulty in Duchenne muscular dystrophy?","a":"Dystrophin.","options":["Titin","Dystrophin","Myomesin","Nebulin"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Dystrophin links the thin filaments of the sarcomere to the sarcolemma, so the force of contraction is passed from the sarcomere out to the connective tissue. In Duchenne muscular dystrophy the gene for dystrophin is faulty, so this link fails. The fiber then damages itself with ordinary use, which causes progressive weakness.","notes":["Titin spans from the Z disc to the M line, anchoring the thick filament and giving the sarcomere elastic recoil. Pick it for a question about what keeps the sarcomere from overstretching.","Correct. Dystrophin links the thin filaments to the sarcolemma and is faulty in Duchenne muscular dystrophy.","Myomesin is found at the M line, where it binds the thick filaments together. Pick it for a question about the protein of the M line.","Nebulin runs along the thin filament, anchoring it and setting its length. Pick it for a question about what determines thin filament length."],"lt":"t-muscle-microanatomy","lid":"c32","comp":76,"competencyId":"w4-myofilament-proteins"},{"id":"L01088","dok":2,"q":"Which structural protein spans from the Z disc to the M line, anchors the thick filament, and gives the sarcomere elastic recoil?","a":"Titin.","options":["Nebulin","Titin","Dystrophin","Alpha-actinin"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Titin is a giant structural protein that stretches from the Z disc to the M line. It anchors each thick filament in place, and its spring-like structure gives the sarcomere elastic recoil and keeps it from being overstretched. Titin helps explain the property of elasticity at the level of the sarcomere.","notes":["Nebulin runs along the thin filament, anchoring it and setting its length. Pick it for a question about thin filament length.","Correct. Titin spans from the Z disc to the M line and gives the sarcomere elastic recoil.","Dystrophin links the thin filaments to the sarcolemma and is faulty in Duchenne muscular dystrophy. Pick it for a question about that disease.","Alpha-actinin is located in the Z disc, where it binds the thin filaments and titin to the disc. Pick it for a question about the protein of the Z disc."],"lt":"t-muscle-microanatomy","lid":"c33","comp":76,"competencyId":"w4-myofilament-proteins"},{"id":"L01090","dok":2,"q":"A histologist wants to confirm that a cell is a skeletal muscle fiber rather than a cardiac muscle cell. Which nuclear feature identifies the skeletal fiber?","a":"Many nuclei just under the sarcolemma.","options":["No nuclei at all","One nucleus in the center of the cell","Two nuclei, one at each end of the cell","Many nuclei just under the sarcolemma"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Skeletal muscle fibers form when many embryonic cells fuse, so each fiber has many nuclei, and these are pushed to the edge just under the sarcolemma. A cardiac muscle cell has one central nucleus. Counting and locating the nuclei is one of the quickest ways to tell the two striated tissues apart.","notes":["Mature red blood cells have no nucleus, but every muscle cell does. Students choose this when they confuse muscle cells with formed elements of the blood.","One central nucleus is the pattern of a cardiac muscle cell, and of a smooth muscle cell too. Pick it for a question that asks how to identify cardiac muscle.","No muscle cell places its nuclei at its two ends. Students choose this when they picture a long fiber and guess that its nuclei must sit at the tips.","Correct. Many peripheral nuclei identify a skeletal muscle fiber."],"lt":"t-muscle-microanatomy","lid":"c35","comp":77,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-tissue-types"},{"id":"L01091","dok":2,"q":"Where are the mitochondria located in a skeletal muscle fiber?","a":"In rows between the myofibrils.","options":["Inside the terminal cisterns","Clustered at the center of each myofibril","In rows between the myofibrils","Inside the T tubules"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Mitochondria in a skeletal muscle fiber are packed in rows between the myofibrils. The myofibrils take up about 80 percent of the fiber's volume, so the mitochondria are squeezed into the narrow spaces between them. This places the ATP they produce right beside the contractile machinery that uses it.","notes":["Terminal cisterns are sacs of the sarcoplasmic reticulum that store calcium, not mitochondria. Pick them for a question about the parts of a triad.","A myofibril is a tightly packed rod of myofilaments, with no room for organelles inside it. The mitochondria lie alongside the myofibrils, not within them.","Correct. Mitochondria lie in rows between the myofibrils.","T tubules are narrow inward folds of the sarcolemma that carry the surface signal, and they do not contain organelles. Pick them for a question about how the signal reaches the fiber's interior."],"lt":"t-muscle-microanatomy","lid":"c36","comp":74,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-fiber-parts"},{"id":"L01092","dok":1,"q":"What is a sarcomere?","a":"The contractile unit of muscle, the segment of a myofibril from one Z disc to the next.","options":["One T tubule flanked by two terminal cisternae","A bundle of muscle fibers wrapped in perimysium","A single long, multinucleated muscle cell","The segment of a myofibril from one Z disc to the next"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"A sarcomere is the contractile unit of skeletal muscle. It is one segment of a myofibril, running from one Z disc (the protein plate at its border) to the next. Thousands of sarcomeres repeat end to end along every myofibril, and their bands line up to produce the stripes seen in skeletal muscle.","notes":["This describes a triad, the meeting point of a T tubule and the sarcoplasmic reticulum. It would be the answer for a question about where the surface signal reaches the calcium store.","This describes a fascicle, a level of organization far larger than a sarcomere. Pick the fascicle if the question asked what the perimysium wraps.","This describes a muscle fiber, the whole cell. A fiber contains myofibrils, and each myofibril is built of thousands of sarcomeres, so the fiber is many levels larger.","Correct. The sarcomere is the repeating contractile unit, bordered by a Z disc at each end."],"lt":"t-muscle-sarcomere","lid":"c1","comp":75,"competencyId":"w4-sarcomere"},{"id":"L01093","dok":1,"q":"A sarcomere extends between which two structures?","a":"Between two Z discs.","options":["Two M lines","Two H zones","Two A bands","Two Z discs"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The sarcomere is defined by its borders, the Z discs. Each Z disc is a protein plate that anchors the thin filaments, and the stretch of myofibril from one Z disc to the next is one sarcomere. Everything else, the A band, H zone, and M line, sits inside that segment.","notes":["The M line sits in the center of the sarcomere, where it anchors the thick filaments. Students mix it up with the Z disc because both are thin dark lines, but the M line marks the middle, not the border.","The H zone is the lighter region in the center of the A band, holding thick filaments only. Each sarcomere has just one H zone, in its middle, so it cannot mark the borders.","The A band is the dark band in the middle of the sarcomere. It would be the answer if the question asked which band is as wide as the thick filaments are long.","Correct. A Z disc forms each border of the sarcomere."],"lt":"t-muscle-sarcomere","lid":"c2","comp":75,"competencyId":"w4-sarcomere"},{"id":"L01094","dok":1,"q":"What produces the striations (stripes) seen along a skeletal muscle myofibril?","a":"Alternating dark A bands and light I bands of the aligned sarcomeres.","options":["Rows of mitochondria between myofibrils","Alternating dark A bands and light I bands","Branching fibers joined end to end","Layers of endomysium around each fiber"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Striations come from the sarcomeres. Each sarcomere has a dark A band, where the thick filaments lie, and light I bands, where only thin filaments lie. Because thousands of sarcomeres are lined up in register across the fiber, these bands appear as continuous stripes under the microscope.","notes":["Mitochondria do sit in rows between myofibrils, where they supply ATP for contraction, but they do not create the banding pattern. They would be the answer if the question asked what supplies the energy for contraction.","Correct. The repeating A and I bands of aligned sarcomeres create the stripes.","Branching fibers joined by intercalated discs describe cardiac muscle. Cardiac muscle is also striated, but the branching is not what makes the stripes; its sarcomeres do.","The endomysium is the fine connective tissue sheath around each muscle fiber. It wraps the outside of the cell and has nothing to do with the bands inside it. Pick it if the question asked which sheath wraps a single fiber."],"lt":"t-muscle-sarcomere","lid":"c3","comp":75,"competencyId":"w4-sarcomere"},{"id":"L01095","dok":1,"q":"Which band of the sarcomere is the dark band?","a":"The A band.","options":["The I band","The A band","The Z disc","The H zone"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The A band is the dark band of the sarcomere. It is as wide as the thick filaments are long, and the dense myosin of those filaments, overlapped at each edge by thin filaments, makes it stain darkly. The alternating dark A bands and light I bands produce the striations of skeletal muscle.","notes":["The I band is the light band, containing thin filaments only. Pick it if the question asked which band is pale or which band a Z disc runs through.","Correct. The A band is the dark band because it holds the thick filaments.","The Z disc is a protein plate at the border of the sarcomere, seen as a thin line, not a band. Pick it if the question asked what anchors the thin filaments or marks each end of the sarcomere.","The H zone is a lighter region in the center of the A band, where thick filaments lie without thin filament overlap. It would be the answer for the region of thick filaments only."],"lt":"t-muscle-sarcomere","lid":"c4","comp":75,"competencyId":"w4-sarcomere"},{"id":"L01096","dok":1,"q":"Which band of the sarcomere is the light band?","a":"The I band.","options":["The A band","The I band","The H zone","The M line"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The I band is the light band of the sarcomere. It contains only thin filaments, which are less dense than the thick filaments of the A band, so it appears pale. A Z disc runs through the center of each I band, which means one I band is shared between two neighboring sarcomeres.","notes":["The A band is the dark band, holding the thick filaments across its full width. It would be the answer for the dark band or the band that never changes width during contraction.","Correct. The I band is light because it contains thin filaments only.","The H zone is a lighter area, but it lies in the center of the dark A band, not between A bands. Pick it for the region that contains thick filaments only.","The M line is a protein line in the middle of the H zone that anchors the thick filaments to one another. It is a line, not a band, and it marks the center of the sarcomere."],"lt":"t-muscle-sarcomere","lid":"c5","comp":75,"competencyId":"w4-sarcomere"},{"id":"L01097","dok":1,"q":"What is the lighter region in the center of the dark A band called?","a":"The H zone.","options":["The I band","The zone of overlap","The Z disc","The H zone"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The H zone is the lighter region in the middle of the A band. Here the thick filaments lie without any thin filaments overlapping them, so the region looks paler than the edges of the A band, where both filaments overlap. The M line runs through the center of the H zone.","notes":["The I band is also light, but it lies outside the A band, between two A bands, with a Z disc through its center. Pick it for the light band that contains thin filaments only.","The zone of overlap is at each end of the A band, where thick and thin filaments interleave. Because it holds both filaments, it is the darkest part of the A band, the opposite of the pale H zone.","The Z disc is the protein plate at the border of the sarcomere. It runs through the I band, not the A band, and anchors the thin filaments.","Correct. The H zone is the paler center of the A band."],"lt":"t-muscle-sarcomere","lid":"c6","comp":75,"competencyId":"w4-sarcomere"},{"id":"L01098","dok":1,"q":"Which line runs through the center of the H zone?","a":"The M line.","options":["The A band","The zone of overlap","The Z disc","The M line"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The M line is a protein line in the exact center of the sarcomere, running through the middle of the H zone. Its job is structural: it anchors the thick filaments to one another so they stay aligned. The protein myomesin forms much of the M line.","notes":["The A band is the dark band that contains the H zone, so it surrounds the H zone rather than running through its middle. It would be the answer for the band as wide as the thick filaments are long.","The zone of overlap lies at each edge of the A band, where thick and thin filaments interleave. It flanks the H zone on both sides instead of bisecting it.","The Z disc runs through the center of the I band and marks the border of the sarcomere. Pick it if the question asked which line bisects the I band.","Correct. The M line bisects the H zone and marks the center of the sarcomere."],"lt":"t-muscle-sarcomere","lid":"c7","comp":75,"competencyId":"w4-sarcomere"},{"id":"L01099","dok":1,"q":"Which protein is found at the M line, binding the thick filaments together?","a":"Myomesin.","options":["Myomesin","Alpha-actinin","Titin","Tropomyosin"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Myomesin is a structural protein located at the M line, in the center of the sarcomere. It binds neighboring thick filaments together so they stay evenly spaced and lined up. Remember the pairing by the shared letter: Myomesin at the M line, while alpha-actinin works at the Z disc.","notes":["Correct. Myomesin sits at the M line and holds the thick filaments together.","Alpha-actinin is found at the Z disc, where it binds the thin filaments and titin. It is the right answer for the protein at the border of the sarcomere, not at its center.","Titin attaches at the M line, but it spans the whole half sarcomere from the Z disc to the M line, acting as an elastic spring. Pick titin if the question asked which protein gives the sarcomere elastic recoil.","Tropomyosin is a regulatory protein lying along the thin filament, covering the myosin-binding sites on actin in relaxed muscle. It never reaches the M line, because thin filaments do not extend into the H zone."],"lt":"t-muscle-sarcomere","lid":"c8","comp":76,"competencyId":"w4-myofilament-proteins"},{"id":"L01100","dok":1,"q":"The Z disc runs through the center of which band?","a":"The I band.","options":["The I band","The zone of overlap","The H zone","The A band"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The Z disc is the protein plate at each border of a sarcomere, and it runs through the middle of the light I band. That placement means half of every I band belongs to one sarcomere and half to the next. Thin filaments extend from the Z disc in both directions, which is why the I band on either side contains thin filaments only.","notes":["Correct. The Z disc bisects the I band, so each I band is split between two sarcomeres.","The zone of overlap is where thick and thin filaments interleave at each end of the A band. No line runs through it; the Z disc lies farther out, in the middle of the I band.","The H zone is the pale center of the A band, and the line through its middle is the M line. Students mix up the two central lines; the M line belongs to the H zone, while the Z disc belongs to the I band.","The A band is the dark central band of the sarcomere, bisected by the H zone and M line, not by the Z disc. Pick it if the question asked which band holds the thick filaments."],"lt":"t-muscle-sarcomere","lid":"c9","comp":75,"competencyId":"w4-sarcomere"},{"id":"L01101","dok":1,"q":"What are myofilaments?","a":"The thick and thin protein strands within a myofibril.","options":["Tunnels of sarcolemma that dive into the fiber","The thick and thin protein strands within a myofibril","The long contractile rods that fill a muscle fiber","Bundles of muscle fibers wrapped in perimysium"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Myofilaments are the protein strands inside each myofibril. The thick filaments are made mostly of myosin and the thin filaments mostly of actin. Their regular, overlapping arrangement creates the sarcomere, and they are the smallest level in the organization of a skeletal muscle, below the muscle, fascicle, fiber, and myofibril.","notes":["This describes the transverse (T) tubules, which carry the surface signal deep into the fiber. They are membrane tunnels, not protein strands.","Correct. Myofilaments are the smallest level of muscle organization, the protein strands whose overlap forms the sarcomere.","This describes myofibrils, one level larger. Each myofibril is built of myofilaments, so pick myofibril if the question asked what fills most of the sarcoplasm.","This describes fascicles, visible bundles of whole cells. A fascicle is several levels above the myofilaments, which are proteins inside a single cell."],"lt":"t-muscle-sarcomere","lid":"c10","comp":73,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-organization"},{"id":"L01102","dok":1,"q":"Which protein makes up the thick filament?","a":"Myosin.","options":["Actin","Titin","Myosin","Tropomyosin"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The thick filament is built of myosin. Many myosin molecules bundle together, each with a long tail and a pair of heads, and the heads project outward from the filament as cross bridges. Thick filaments lie in the center of the sarcomere, spanning the A band, and are anchored at the M line.","notes":["Actin makes up the thin filament, two twisted strands of actin subunits anchored at the Z disc. Pick actin if the question asked about the thin filament.","Titin is a giant structural protein that runs from the Z disc to the M line and gives the sarcomere elastic recoil. It anchors the thick filament but does not form it.","Correct. The thick filament is a bundle of myosin molecules.","Tropomyosin is a rod-shaped regulatory protein lying along the thin filament. Its name contains myosin, which is why students pick it, but it belongs to the thin filament."],"lt":"t-muscle-sarcomere","lid":"c11","comp":76,"competencyId":"w4-myofilament-proteins"},{"id":"L01103","dok":1,"q":"Where do the thick filaments lie within the sarcomere?","a":"In the center, spanning the full width of the A band, joined at the M line.","options":["Centered in the A band, joined at the M line","At each end, anchored to the Z disc","Only within the I band","Along the sarcolemma, outside the myofibril"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Thick filaments sit in the middle of the sarcomere. Their length sets the width of the A band, and their centers are joined at the M line, where myomesin binds them together. Thin filaments reach inward from the Z discs and overlap the ends of the thick filaments, forming the zone of overlap at each edge of the A band.","notes":["Correct. The thick filaments define the A band and are tied together at the M line.","This describes the thin filaments, which are anchored at the Z disc and extend inward. Pick this placement if the question asked where the thin filaments attach.","The I band contains thin filaments only, which is why it is light. Thick filaments never extend into the I band.","Thick filaments are inside the myofibril, not on the cell membrane. The protein linking the myofibril to the sarcolemma is dystrophin, which would be the answer for that connection."],"lt":"t-muscle-sarcomere","lid":"c12","comp":75,"competencyId":"w4-sarcomere"},{"id":"L01104","dok":1,"q":"Which protein makes up the thin filament?","a":"Actin.","options":["Myomesin","Actin","Myosin","Dystrophin"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The thin filament is made mainly of actin. Two strands of actin subunits twist around each other, and each subunit carries a binding site for a myosin head. The thin filament also carries the regulatory proteins tropomyosin and troponin, and it is anchored at the Z disc.","notes":["Myomesin is a structural protein at the M line that binds thick filaments together. It is not part of the thin filament, which never reaches the M line.","Correct. The thin filament is two twisted strands of actin subunits.","Myosin forms the thick filament, a bundle of molecules with projecting heads. Pick myosin if the question asked about the thick filament.","Dystrophin links the thin filaments to the sarcolemma, but it does not build the filament itself. It would be the answer for the protein that is faulty in Duchenne muscular dystrophy."],"lt":"t-muscle-sarcomere","lid":"c13","comp":76,"competencyId":"w4-myofilament-proteins"},{"id":"L01105","dok":1,"q":"How far do the thin filaments extend within a sarcomere?","a":"From the Z disc across the I band and partway into the A band, stopping at the edge of the H zone.","options":["From the Z disc partway into the A band","From one Z disc all the way to the next Z disc","Only within the H zone","From the M line to the edge of the A band"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Each thin filament is anchored at a Z disc and extends inward toward the center of the sarcomere. It crosses the I band, where it is the only filament present, then continues partway into the A band, where it overlaps the thick filaments. It stops before the center, leaving the H zone free of thin filaments.","notes":["Correct. The thin filaments reach into the A band to form the zone of overlap, then stop at the H zone.","If thin filaments ran the full sarcomere there would be no H zone, the region of thick filaments only. This option is a misconception that ignores the gap at the center of a relaxed sarcomere.","The H zone is defined as the region containing thick filaments only, so thin filaments are absent there. It is the answer for where thin filaments are missing, not where they lie.","This is roughly the reach of half a thick filament, which is anchored at the M line. Pick this if the question asked where the thick filaments lie."],"lt":"t-muscle-sarcomere","lid":"c14","comp":75,"competencyId":"w4-sarcomere"},{"id":"L01106","dok":2,"q":"Which intermediate filament protein surrounds the Z discs and ties neighboring myofibrils together side by side?","a":"Desmin.","options":["Titin","Myomesin","Nebulin","Desmin"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Desmin is an intermediate filament protein that wraps around the Z discs and connects each myofibril to its neighbors. By tying the myofibrils together at their Z discs, it keeps the sarcomeres of neighboring myofibrils lined up, which is part of why the bands look continuous across the whole fiber. Desmin is not the elastic filament of the sarcomere; that role belongs to titin.","notes":["Titin is the elastic filament that runs lengthwise inside each sarcomere, from the Z disc to the M line. Pick titin if the question asked which protein gives the sarcomere elastic recoil.","Myomesin sits at the M line and binds thick filaments together within one sarcomere. It would be the answer for the protein at the center of the sarcomere.","Nebulin runs along the length of the thin filament, anchoring it and setting its length. It works within a single myofibril, not between neighboring ones.","Correct. Desmin links adjacent myofibrils at their Z discs, keeping them in register across the fiber."],"lt":"t-muscle-sarcomere","lid":"c15","comp":76,"competencyId":"w4-myofilament-proteins"},{"id":"L01107","dok":1,"q":"A myosin molecule is built of how many heavy and light polypeptide chains?","a":"Two heavy chains and four light chains.","options":["Two heavy chains and four light chains","Four heavy chains and two light chains","Two heavy chains and two light chains","One heavy chain and two light chains"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"A myosin molecule has two heavy chains and four light chains. The two heavy chains twist around each other to form the long tail and then each ends in a globular head, so one molecule has a pair of heads. Two light chains are associated with each head, giving four light chains in all.","notes":["Correct. The two heavy chains form the tail and heads, and the light chains sit at the heads.","This reverses the numbers. Students who remember both numbers but not which is which choose it. There are two heavy chains, matching the two heads.","This gets the heavy chains right but undercounts the light chains. Each of the two heads carries two light chains, for four in total.","A single heavy chain could not form the paired heads and the intertwined tail of myosin. This choice comes from forgetting that the tail is two chains wound together."],"lt":"t-muscle-sarcomere","lid":"c16","comp":76,"competencyId":"w4-myofilament-proteins"},{"id":"L01108","dok":1,"q":"What do the heads of the myosin molecules do in a contracting muscle?","a":"They bind to the thin filament, forming cross bridges that pull it toward the center of the sarcomere.","options":["They cover the active sites on actin in relaxed muscle","They bind the thin filament as cross bridges and pull on it","They store calcium ions near the myofibrils","They anchor the thick filaments at the M line"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Each myosin molecule ends in a pair of globular heads that project outward from the thick filament. During contraction these heads attach to binding sites on the actin of the thin filament, forming cross bridges, and they pivot to pull the thin filaments toward the M line. The heads are the force-producing part of the thick filament.","notes":["Covering the active sites is the job of tropomyosin, a regulatory protein on the thin filament. It would be the answer if the question asked what blocks cross bridges at rest.","Correct. The heads project from the thick filament and link to actin as cross bridges.","Calcium is stored in the sarcoplasmic reticulum, the smooth endoplasmic reticulum wrapping each myofibril. Pick the sarcoplasmic reticulum for the calcium store.","Anchoring at the M line is done by structural proteins such as myomesin, near the center of the thick filament where there are no heads. Pick myomesin for that role."],"lt":"t-muscle-sarcomere","lid":"c17","comp":76,"competencyId":"w4-myofilament-proteins"},{"id":"L01109","dok":1,"q":"What is the shape of a single myosin molecule?","a":"A long tail with a pair of heads at one end, shaped like two golf clubs twisted together.","options":["A rod lying end to end along a filament","A small globular complex of three subunits","A long tail with a pair of heads at one end","Two twisted strands of round subunits"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"A myosin molecule has a long tail made of two intertwined heavy chains, and at one end the chains fold into two globular heads, so the molecule looks like two golf clubs twisted together. Hundreds of these molecules bundle tail to tail to build a thick filament, with the heads sticking out to form cross bridges.","notes":["This describes tropomyosin, a rod-shaped regulatory protein on the thin filament. It has no heads.","This describes troponin, the three-part regulatory complex bound to tropomyosin. It would be the answer for the protein with TnI, TnT, and TnC subunits.","Correct. The intertwined tail and two heads give myosin its golf club shape.","This describes the thin filament, built from strands of globular actin subunits. Pick it if the question asked about the structure of the thin filament."],"lt":"t-muscle-sarcomere","lid":"c18","comp":76,"competencyId":"w4-myofilament-proteins"},{"id":"L01110","dok":1,"q":"What does each actin subunit of the thin filament carry?","a":"A binding site where a myosin head attaches.","options":["A pair of heads that project outward","A binding site for a myosin head","A link to the sarcolemma","An elastic spring segment"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The thin filament is built from actin subunits strung in two twisted strands. Each subunit carries an active site, a myosin-binding site where a myosin head can attach to form a cross bridge. In relaxed muscle, the regulatory protein tropomyosin lies over these sites and keeps the heads from binding.","notes":["Projecting heads belong to myosin molecules in the thick filament. Pick this if the question asked what forms the cross bridges.","Correct. Each actin subunit has an active site where a myosin head can bind.","The link between the thin filaments and the sarcolemma is made by dystrophin, a separate structural protein. Individual actin subunits do not attach to the membrane.","An elastic spring describes titin, which runs from the Z disc to the M line. It would be the answer for the protein that gives the sarcomere recoil."],"lt":"t-muscle-sarcomere","lid":"c19","comp":76,"competencyId":"w4-myofilament-proteins"},{"id":"L01111","dok":1,"q":"Which regulatory protein is rod-shaped and lies end to end along the thin filament?","a":"Tropomyosin.","options":["Troponin","Tropomyosin","Titin","Nebulin"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The thin filament carries two regulatory proteins. Tropomyosin is the rod-shaped one, lying end to end along the groove of the actin strands. In relaxed muscle it covers the myosin-binding sites on actin, and the second regulatory protein, troponin, is bound at intervals along it.","notes":["Troponin is the other regulatory protein, but it is a small three-part globular complex bound at intervals to tropomyosin, not a long rod. Pick troponin for the three-subunit complex.","Correct. Tropomyosin rods lie end to end along the actin strands.","Titin is a long structural protein, but it runs from the Z disc to the M line within the sarcomere, not along the thin filament. It would be the answer for elastic recoil.","Nebulin does run along the thin filament, but it is a structural protein that anchors the filament and sets its length, not a regulatory protein. Pick nebulin if the question asked what sets thin filament length."],"lt":"t-muscle-sarcomere","lid":"c20","comp":76,"competencyId":"w4-myofilament-proteins"},{"id":"L01112","dok":1,"q":"Troponin is a complex of how many polypeptide subunits?","a":"Three.","options":["Four","Two","Three","Six"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Troponin is a small globular complex of three subunits bound at intervals along tropomyosin on the thin filament. Each subunit is named for what it binds: TnI binds actin, TnT binds tropomyosin, and TnC binds calcium ions. Together with tropomyosin, troponin regulates whether myosin heads can reach actin.","notes":["Four is the number of light chains in a myosin molecule. It does not describe troponin, which has three subunits.","Two is the number of regulatory proteins on the thin filament (tropomyosin and troponin). Students who mix up that count with the troponin subunits choose this.","Correct. Troponin has three subunits, called TnI, TnT, and TnC.","Six is the total number of polypeptide chains in one myosin molecule (two heavy and four light). It is the wrong protein for this count."],"lt":"t-muscle-sarcomere","lid":"c21","comp":76,"competencyId":"w4-myofilament-proteins"},{"id":"L01114","dok":1,"q":"Which structural protein forms the elastic filament that runs from the Z disc to the M line?","a":"Titin.","options":["Nebulin","Dystrophin","Myomesin","Titin"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Titin is the largest known protein and forms the elastic filament of the sarcomere. It runs from the Z disc through the core of the thick filament to the M line, holding the thick filament in place. Like a spring, it lets the sarcomere recoil after stretching and keeps it from being overstretched.","notes":["Nebulin runs along the thin filament and sets its length. It does not span from the Z disc to the M line or act as a spring.","Dystrophin links the thin filaments to the sarcolemma and is faulty in muscular dystrophy. Pick dystrophin for that link to the cell membrane.","Myomesin sits only at the M line, binding thick filaments together. It would be the answer for the protein located at the center of the sarcomere.","Correct. Titin is the giant elastic protein spanning each half sarcomere."],"lt":"t-muscle-sarcomere","lid":"c23","comp":76,"competencyId":"w4-myofilament-proteins"},{"id":"L01115","dok":1,"q":"Which structural protein links the thin filaments to the proteins of the sarcolemma?","a":"Dystrophin.","options":["Myomesin","Dystrophin","Nebulin","Titin"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Dystrophin is the structural protein that links the thin filaments at the outer edge of the myofibrils to integral proteins of the sarcolemma, the muscle cell membrane. Through this link the force of the sarcomeres is passed out to the connective tissue around the fiber. When the dystrophin gene is faulty, as in Duchenne muscular dystrophy, fibers damage themselves with ordinary use.","notes":["Myomesin binds thick filaments together at the M line, deep in the center of the sarcomere and far from the membrane.","Correct. Dystrophin connects the myofibrils to the cell membrane.","Nebulin lies along the thin filament and sets its length, but it does not reach the sarcolemma. Pick it for the protein that measures thin filament length.","Titin connects the Z disc to the M line inside the sarcomere. It would be the answer for the elastic filament, not for the link to the sarcolemma."],"lt":"t-muscle-sarcomere","lid":"c24","comp":76,"competencyId":"w4-myofilament-proteins"},{"id":"L01116","dok":2,"q":"Which region of the sarcomere contains only thin filaments?","a":"The I band.","options":["The zone of overlap","The H zone","The I band","The A band"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The I band is the region at each end of a sarcomere where thin filaments extend from the Z disc but have not yet reached the thick filaments. Because it contains only thin filaments, it is less dense and appears light. Its mirror image is the H zone in the center, which contains only thick filaments.","notes":["The zone of overlap contains both thick and thin filaments interleaved. It would be the answer for the region where cross bridges can form.","The H zone is the mirror image of the I band: it holds thick filaments only. Students swap the two because both are pale regions.","Correct. The I band holds thin filaments only, which is why it is light.","The A band contains the thick filaments across its full width, with thin filaments overlapping at its edges. Pick it for the band as wide as the thick filaments are long."],"lt":"t-muscle-sarcomere","lid":"c25","comp":75,"competencyId":"w4-sarcomere"},{"id":"L01117","dok":2,"q":"Which region of the sarcomere contains only thick filaments?","a":"The H zone.","options":["The I band","The Z disc","The H zone","The zone of overlap"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The H zone is the central part of the A band where the thin filaments do not reach, so only thick filaments are present. It looks lighter than the edges of the A band, where both filament types overlap. The M line runs through the middle of the H zone and anchors the thick filaments together.","notes":["The I band holds thin filaments only, the reverse of this question. Pick it if the question asked for the region of thin filaments alone.","The Z disc is a protein plate at the border of the sarcomere that anchors thin filaments. No thick filaments reach it.","Correct. The center of the A band, where thin filaments do not reach, holds thick filaments only.","The zone of overlap holds both thick and thin filaments interleaved, at each end of the A band. It would be the answer for the region containing both filament types."],"lt":"t-muscle-sarcomere","lid":"c26","comp":75,"competencyId":"w4-sarcomere"},{"id":"L01118","dok":2,"q":"Which protein anchors the thin filaments and titin to the Z disc?","a":"Alpha-actinin.","options":["Titin","Myomesin","Desmin","Alpha-actinin"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Alpha-actinin is a structural protein of the Z disc. It binds the ends of the thin filaments and the ends of titin, holding them firmly at the border of the sarcomere. Its name hints at its partner, actin, which is the main protein of the thin filaments it anchors.","notes":["Titin is anchored at the Z disc, but it is the elastic filament being held there, not the anchor itself. It would be the answer for the spring that runs to the M line.","Myomesin is the anchoring protein at the opposite end, the M line, where it binds the thick filaments. Pick it for the protein at the center of the sarcomere.","Desmin wraps around the Z discs to tie neighboring myofibrils together, but it does not anchor the thin filaments within the disc. Pick desmin for the link between adjacent myofibrils.","Correct. Alpha-actinin is the Z disc protein that binds the thin filaments and titin."],"lt":"t-muscle-sarcomere","lid":"c27","comp":76,"competencyId":"w4-myofilament-proteins"},{"id":"L01119","dok":2,"q":"Which two proteins are the regulatory proteins of the thin filament?","a":"Tropomyosin and troponin.","options":["Actin and myosin","Titin and nebulin","Tropomyosin and troponin","Myomesin and alpha-actinin"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The thin filament carries two regulatory proteins that control whether myosin can bind actin. Tropomyosin is a rod lying along the actin strands, covering the myosin-binding sites at rest, and troponin is a three-part complex attached to tropomyosin. Contractile proteins (actin, myosin) and structural proteins (titin, nebulin, myomesin, alpha-actinin, dystrophin) are separate groups.","notes":["Actin and myosin are the contractile proteins that make up the thin and thick filaments. Pick this pair if the question asked which proteins form the two main myofilaments.","Titin and nebulin are structural proteins. Titin gives elastic recoil and nebulin sets thin filament length, so they would be the answer for proteins that hold the sarcomere in shape.","Correct. These two proteins sit on the thin filament and control access to actin.","These are anchoring proteins: myomesin at the M line and alpha-actinin at the Z disc. Pick this pair for the proteins at the center and the border of the sarcomere."],"lt":"t-muscle-sarcomere","lid":"c28","comp":76,"competencyId":"w4-myofilament-proteins"},{"id":"L01122","dok":2,"q":"A filament sits in the center of the sarcomere, spans the full width of the A band, and is joined to its neighbors at the M line. Which filament is it, and what protein makes it?","a":"The thick filament, made of myosin.","options":["The thin filament, made of tropomyosin","The thin filament, made of actin","The elastic filament, made of titin","The thick filament, made of myosin"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The thick filament is built of myosin and lies in the middle of the sarcomere. Its length sets the width of the A band, and the M line at its center ties neighboring thick filaments together. The thin filaments, made of actin, come in from the Z discs at each end and overlap the thick filaments only at the edges of the A band.","notes":["Tropomyosin lies along the thin filament as a regulatory protein; it does not form a filament of its own in the A band. This choice mixes a regulatory protein with the wrong filament.","The thin filament is anchored at the Z disc and crosses the I band, so it lies toward the ends of the sarcomere. Pick it for a filament described as anchored at the Z disc.","Titin does reach the M line, but it starts at the Z disc and runs the whole half sarcomere, so it is not confined to the A band. It would be the answer for the filament that gives elastic recoil.","Correct. Central placement, the full A band, and the M line all point to the thick filament."],"lt":"t-muscle-sarcomere","lid":"c31","comp":76,"competencyId":"w4-myofilament-proteins"},{"id":"L01123","dok":2,"q":"A myosin molecule has a long tail joined to two globular heads. What forms the tail?","a":"Two heavy polypeptide chains wound around each other.","options":["Four light chains bundled together","Two twisted strands of actin subunits","Two heavy chains wound around each other","A strand of titin running through its core"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"A myosin molecule is built of two heavy chains and four light chains. The two heavy chains wind around each other like a rope to form the long tail, and at one end each heavy chain folds into a globular head. The light chains are small and associated with the heads, so the tail is made of heavy chains alone.","notes":["The four light chains are small and sit at the heads, two per head. They do not form the tail; pick them if the question asked what is associated with the myosin heads.","This describes the thin filament, a separate structure. Students choose it because both involve twisted strands, but actin is not part of myosin.","Correct. The two heavy chains intertwine to form the tail, then each ends in a head.","Titin runs through the core of the thick filament, the bundle of many myosin molecules, but it is not part of a single myosin molecule's tail."],"lt":"t-muscle-sarcomere","lid":"c32","comp":76,"competencyId":"w4-myofilament-proteins"},{"id":"L01124","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly matches the Z disc and the M line to what each anchors?","a":"The Z disc anchors the thin filaments, and the M line anchors the thick filaments.","options":["The Z disc anchors thin filaments; the M line anchors thick filaments","Both anchor the thin filaments, at opposite ends","Both anchor the thick filaments, at opposite ends","The Z disc anchors thick filaments; the M line anchors thin filaments"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The sarcomere has two anchoring lines. The Z disc, at each border, is where the thin filaments attach, held by the protein alpha-actinin. The M line, at the center, ties the thick filaments together, helped by the protein myomesin. A simple way to keep them apart is that thin filaments start at the edges and thick filaments are centered.","notes":["Correct. Thin filaments attach at the border and thick filaments are tied together at the center.","Only the Z disc anchors thin filaments. The M line lies in the H zone, where thin filaments are absent, so it cannot anchor them.","Only the M line anchors thick filaments. The Z disc is at the border, beyond the reach of the thick filaments, and it holds the thin filaments instead.","This reverses the pairs. Thick filaments never reach the Z disc, and thin filaments stop before the M line, so neither attachment is possible."],"lt":"t-muscle-sarcomere","lid":"c33","comp":76,"competencyId":"w4-myofilament-proteins"},{"id":"L01125","dok":2,"q":"What does titin do for the sarcomere?","a":"It gives the sarcomere elastic recoil, holds the thick filament in place, and keeps the sarcomere from overstretching.","options":["It links the thin filaments to the sarcolemma","It gives elastic recoil and prevents overstretching","It sets the length of the thin filament","It covers the myosin-binding sites on actin"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Titin is a giant structural protein that runs from the Z disc to the M line. It holds each thick filament centered in the sarcomere, and because it is elastic, it lets a stretched sarcomere spring back to its resting length. It also resists stretching beyond a safe limit, protecting the sarcomere from being pulled apart.","notes":["This is the role of dystrophin, the protein faulty in Duchenne muscular dystrophy. It would be the answer for the connection between myofibrils and the cell membrane.","Correct. Titin acts as a molecular spring between the Z disc and the M line.","Setting thin filament length is the job of nebulin, which runs along the thin filament. Pick nebulin if the question asked which protein measures the thin filament.","Covering the binding sites at rest is the role of tropomyosin, a regulatory protein on the thin filament. Titin is structural, not regulatory."],"lt":"t-muscle-sarcomere","lid":"c34","comp":76,"competencyId":"w4-myofilament-proteins"},{"id":"L01126","dok":2,"q":"Which structural protein runs along the length of the thin filament, anchoring it and setting its length?","a":"Nebulin.","options":["Dystrophin","Nebulin","Titin","Tropomyosin"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Nebulin is a structural protein that runs the full length of the thin filament. It anchors the thin filament and sets how long it is, so every thin filament in the sarcomere is the same length. Along with myomesin at the M line and alpha-actinin at the Z disc, it helps keep the sarcomere in register.","notes":["Dystrophin connects the thin filaments to the sarcolemma at the edge of the myofibril. It does not run along the filament to set its length.","Correct. Nebulin lies along the thin filament and acts like a ruler for its length.","Titin is also a long structural protein, but it runs from the Z disc to the M line with the thick filament. Pick titin for the elastic filament.","Tropomyosin lies along the thin filament too, but it is a regulatory protein that covers myosin-binding sites. It would be the answer for the rod-shaped regulatory protein."],"lt":"t-muscle-sarcomere","lid":"c35","comp":76,"competencyId":"w4-myofilament-proteins"},{"id":"L01127","dok":2,"q":"Moving from the border of a sarcomere toward its center, which landmark is reached last?","a":"The M line.","options":["The M line","The zone of overlap","The I band","The Z disc"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"A sarcomere is symmetrical around its center. Starting at the Z disc on the border, you cross the light I band, enter the A band at the zone of overlap, pass into the H zone, and reach the M line at the very middle. From the M line the same landmarks repeat in reverse to the next Z disc.","notes":["Correct. The M line marks the exact center of the sarcomere.","The zone of overlap is at the edge of the A band, partway in. Past it lie the H zone and finally the M line at the center.","The I band is reached just after the Z disc, near the border. It would be the answer for the region next to the Z disc.","The Z disc is the first landmark, at the border of the sarcomere. Pick it for where the sarcomere begins and ends."],"lt":"t-muscle-sarcomere","lid":"c36","comp":75,"competencyId":"w4-sarcomere"},{"id":"L01128","dok":2,"q":"Moving across half a sarcomere, from its border to its center, which sequence of landmarks is correct?","a":"Z disc, I band, zone of overlap, H zone, M line.","options":["Z disc, zone of overlap, I band, H zone, M line","Z disc, I band, zone of overlap, H zone, M line","Z disc, I band, H zone, zone of overlap, M line","M line, H zone, zone of overlap, I band, Z disc"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The sarcomere is bordered by Z discs and centered on the M line. From the Z disc inward, the thin filaments alone form the I band, then they meet the thick filaments in the zone of overlap at the edge of the A band. Beyond the reach of the thin filaments is the H zone, thick filaments only, and the M line runs through its middle.","notes":["This places the zone of overlap before the I band. The I band, with thin filaments only, comes first; overlap begins only where thin filaments reach the thick filaments.","Correct. This follows the sarcomere from its border to its middle.","This swaps the zone of overlap and the H zone. The H zone sits in the center of the A band, so it is reached after the overlap region.","This is the correct order run backward, from center to border. It would be right if the question asked you to move from the M line outward."],"lt":"t-muscle-sarcomere","lid":"c37","comp":75,"competencyId":"w4-sarcomere"},{"id":"L01129","dok":2,"q":"In a relaxed muscle fiber, what does tropomyosin do?","a":"It lies along the thin filament and covers the myosin-binding sites on actin.","options":["It covers the myosin-binding sites on actin","It anchors the thin filament at the Z disc","It forms the heads of the thick filament","It binds calcium ions"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Tropomyosin is a rod-shaped regulatory protein lying end to end along the groove of the thin filament. In relaxed muscle it sits over the active sites on actin, so the myosin heads cannot attach and form cross bridges. Troponin, bound to tropomyosin, controls when tropomyosin is moved off those sites.","notes":["Correct. With the sites covered, myosin heads cannot bind and the muscle stays relaxed.","Anchoring at the Z disc is done by alpha-actinin. It would be the answer for the protein holding thin filaments at the border.","The heads belong to myosin molecules in the thick filament. Tropomyosin is on the thin filament and has no heads.","Binding calcium is the job of TnC, a subunit of troponin. Pick troponin if the question asked which regulatory protein binds calcium."],"lt":"t-muscle-sarcomere","lid":"c38","comp":76,"competencyId":"w4-myofilament-proteins"},{"id":"L01131","dok":2,"q":"Duchenne muscular dystrophy is caused by a faulty gene for which structural protein?","a":"Dystrophin.","options":["Nebulin","Dystrophin","Myomesin","Titin"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Muscular dystrophy is a group of inherited diseases of muscle structure. In Duchenne muscular dystrophy the gene for dystrophin is faulty. Dystrophin normally links the thin filaments to the sarcolemma, so without it the fiber tears itself with ordinary use and weakness gets steadily worse. The similar names, dystrophin and dystrophy, make this pairing easy to remember.","notes":["Nebulin runs along the thin filament and sets its length. Pick it for the protein that measures thin filament length.","Correct. Dystrophin is the protein missing or faulty in Duchenne muscular dystrophy.","Myomesin binds thick filaments together at the M line. It is the answer for the protein at the center of the sarcomere.","Titin is the elastic filament from the Z disc to the M line. It would be the answer for the protein that gives the sarcomere recoil, not for Duchenne muscular dystrophy."],"lt":"t-muscle-sarcomere","lid":"c40","comp":76,"competencyId":"w4-myofilament-proteins"},{"id":"L01132","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares the thick and thin filaments?","a":"The thick filament is made of myosin and joined at the M line; the thin filament is made of actin and anchored at the Z disc.","options":["Thick is myosin anchored at the Z disc; thin is actin joined at the M line","Thick is titin across the I band; thin is actin across the H zone","Thick is actin joined at the M line; thin is myosin anchored at the Z disc","Thick is myosin joined at the M line; thin is actin anchored at the Z disc"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The two myofilaments differ in protein and position. The thick filament is a bundle of myosin molecules in the center of the sarcomere, spanning the A band and tied together at the M line. The thin filament is two twisted strands of actin, anchored at the Z disc, extending across the I band and partway into the A band.","notes":["The proteins are right, but the anchors are swapped. Thick filaments never reach the Z disc, and thin filaments stop before the M line.","Titin is the elastic filament, not the thick filament, and the H zone has no thin filaments. This mixes structural proteins with the regions where they are absent.","The anchors are right, but the proteins are swapped. Myosin forms the thick filament and actin the thin one.","Correct. Each filament's protein and anchor point are matched properly."],"lt":"t-muscle-sarcomere","lid":"c41","comp":76,"competencyId":"w4-myofilament-proteins"},{"id":"L01133","dok":2,"q":"A student needs to tell titin from myomesin. Which statement correctly compares them?","a":"Titin spans from the Z disc to the M line and gives elastic recoil; myomesin sits only at the M line and binds the thick filaments together.","options":["Both proteins anchor the thin filaments at the Z disc","Titin links thin filaments to the sarcolemma; myomesin sets thin filament length","Titin spans Z disc to M line for recoil; myomesin binds thick filaments at the M line","Titin sits only at the M line; myomesin spans Z disc to M line"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Titin and myomesin both attach at the M line, which is why students confuse them. Titin is a giant elastic protein that runs all the way from the Z disc to the M line, holding the thick filament in place and letting the sarcomere spring back after stretching. Myomesin is confined to the M line, where it binds neighboring thick filaments together.","notes":["The Z disc anchor for thin filaments is alpha-actinin. Myomesin is at the opposite end, and titin anchors the thick filament, not the thin.","These are the roles of dystrophin and nebulin. Pick those proteins if the question asked about the membrane link or the thin filament ruler.","Correct. Titin is the long spring, and myomesin is the local anchor at the center.","This reverses their locations. Myomesin is the short protein confined to the M line, while titin stretches across the half sarcomere."],"lt":"t-muscle-sarcomere","lid":"c42","comp":76,"competencyId":"w4-myofilament-proteins"},{"id":"L01134","dok":2,"q":"A filament is built of many molecules shaped like golf clubs, with their tails bundled together and their heads projecting outward. Which filament and protein are described?","a":"The thick filament, made of myosin.","options":["The thick filament, made of myosin","The elastic filament, made of titin","The thin filament, made of tropomyosin","The thin filament, made of actin"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The golf club shape belongs to myosin. Each myosin molecule has a long tail of two intertwined heavy chains and two globular heads at one end. Hundreds of these molecules bundle tail to tail to build the thick filament, and their heads stick out to form the cross bridges that grip the thin filament.","notes":["Correct. Myosin molecules, with a long tail and paired heads, bundle to form the thick filament.","Titin is a single giant spring running from the Z disc to the M line. It has no projecting heads.","Tropomyosin is a rod lying along the thin filament, not a bundle of headed molecules. It would be the answer for a rod-shaped regulatory protein.","Actin subunits are small and globular, strung like two twisted strands of beads. Pick the thin filament for a description of twisted strands with myosin-binding sites."],"lt":"t-muscle-sarcomere","lid":"c43","comp":76,"competencyId":"w4-myofilament-proteins"},{"id":"L01135","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares titin and desmin?","a":"Titin runs lengthwise inside each sarcomere from the Z disc to the M line as its elastic filament; desmin ties neighboring myofibrils together at their Z discs.","options":["Titin is the sarcomere's elastic filament; desmin links neighboring myofibrils at the Z discs","Both proteins sit at the M line and bind thick filaments","Desmin is the sarcomere's elastic filament; titin links neighboring myofibrils","Titin sets thin filament length; desmin links the thin filaments to the sarcolemma"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Titin and desmin both connect to the Z disc but work at different scales. Titin runs lengthwise inside each sarcomere, from the Z disc to the M line, and acts as the elastic filament that gives recoil. Desmin is an intermediate filament that wraps the Z discs and ties each myofibril to its neighbors, keeping their sarcomeres lined up across the fiber.","notes":["Correct. Titin works within a sarcomere, and desmin works between myofibrils.","Binding thick filaments at the M line is the job of myomesin. Pick myomesin for the M line protein.","This reverses the two roles. Titin is the elastic filament from the Z disc to the M line, and desmin is the intermediate filament between myofibrils.","These are the roles of nebulin and dystrophin. They would be the answers for the thin filament ruler and the membrane link."],"lt":"t-muscle-sarcomere","lid":"c44","comp":76,"competencyId":"w4-myofilament-proteins"},{"id":"L01238","dok":1,"q":"What is the smooth endoplasmic reticulum of a muscle fiber, which stores calcium ions, called?","a":"The sarcoplasmic reticulum.","options":["The triad","The transverse tubule","The sarcolemma","The sarcoplasmic reticulum"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The sarcoplasmic reticulum is the smooth endoplasmic reticulum of a muscle fiber. It forms a network of tubules wrapping each myofibril and stores calcium ions. Its enlarged end sacs, the terminal cisternae, sit on either side of each T tubule.","notes":["A triad is one T tubule flanked by two terminal cisternae of the sarcoplasmic reticulum. It includes part of the reticulum but is not the whole calcium store.","Transverse (T) tubules are tunnels of the sarcolemma that carry the surface signal into the fiber. Pick them for the inward extensions of the membrane.","The sarcolemma is the plasma membrane of the fiber. It would be the answer for the cell's outer membrane.","Correct. The sarcoplasmic reticulum wraps each myofibril and stores calcium ions."],"lt":"t-muscle-structure","lid":"c8","comp":74,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-fiber-parts"},{"id":"L01239","dok":1,"q":"What is the structural unit of one T tubule flanked by two terminal cisternae called?","a":"A triad.","options":["A fascicle","A myofibril","A triad","A sarcomere"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"A triad is made of three parts: one transverse (T) tubule in the middle and a terminal cisterna of the sarcoplasmic reticulum on each side. It is where the signal traveling in from the cell surface meets the calcium store, and triads repeat along every myofibril.","notes":["A fascicle is a bundle of muscle fibers wrapped in perimysium. Pick it for a level of whole-muscle organization.","A myofibril is a long contractile rod made of sarcomeres. It would be the answer for what fills most of the sarcoplasm.","Correct. Tri means three: one T tubule and two terminal cisternae.","A sarcomere is the contractile unit from one Z disc to the next. Pick it for the segment of a myofibril."],"lt":"t-muscle-structure","lid":"c9","comp":74,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-fiber-parts"},{"id":"L01240","dok":1,"q":"Which red, oxygen-binding pigment in the sarcoplasm stores oxygen and gives muscle its color?","a":"Myoglobin.","options":["Myoglobin","Hemoglobin","Glycogen","Myosin"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Myoglobin is a red pigment in the sarcoplasm that binds and stores oxygen inside the muscle fiber. It is related to hemoglobin, which carries oxygen in red blood cells. The amount of myoglobin is a major reason muscle looks red, and muscles rich in it look darker.","notes":["Correct. Myoglobin stores oxygen inside muscle fibers.","Hemoglobin is the oxygen-carrying pigment inside red blood cells. Pick it for oxygen transport in the blood.","Glycogen is stored carbohydrate fuel in the sarcoplasm. It would be the answer for the muscle's energy reserve, not its oxygen store.","Myosin is the protein of the thick filament. Its name starts the same way, but it does not bind oxygen."],"lt":"t-muscle-structure","lid":"c10","comp":74,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-fiber-parts"},{"id":"L01241","dok":1,"q":"Which description fits the thick filament?","a":"A bundle of myosin molecules with their heads projecting outward.","options":["Rod-shaped proteins lying end to end","A giant elastic strand from the Z disc to the M line","Two twisted strands of actin subunits","A bundle of myosin molecules with heads projecting outward"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The thick filament is built from hundreds of myosin molecules bundled together. Each molecule has a long tail and a pair of heads, and the heads project outward from the filament as cross bridges. Thick filaments lie in the center of the sarcomere, spanning the A band, and are anchored at the M line.","notes":["This describes tropomyosin, a regulatory protein along the thin filament. It would be the answer for the protein that covers the myosin-binding sites.","This describes titin, a structural protein. Pick it for the protein that gives the sarcomere elastic recoil.","This describes the thin filament. Pick it for the filament anchored at the Z disc.","Correct. The thick filament is bundled myosin with projecting cross bridges."],"lt":"t-muscle-structure","lid":"c11","comp":76,"competencyId":"w4-myofilament-proteins"},{"id":"L01242","dok":2,"q":"A filament is built of two twisted strands of subunits, and each subunit carries a binding site for myosin. Which protein forms it?","a":"Actin.","options":["Nebulin","Myosin","Titin","Actin"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The thin filament is made of actin. Small actin subunits string together into two strands that twist around each other, and every subunit carries an active site where a myosin head can attach. Tropomyosin and troponin lie along these strands as regulatory proteins.","notes":["Nebulin runs alongside the thin filament and sets its length, but it does not form the twisted strands. Pick it for the thin filament ruler.","Myosin forms the thick filament and carries the heads that bind actin. It would be the answer for the protein with heads, not the one with binding sites.","Titin is a single giant elastic strand from the Z disc to the M line, not twisted strands of subunits. Pick it for the elastic filament.","Correct. Two twisted strands of actin subunits make the thin filament."],"lt":"t-muscle-structure","lid":"c12","comp":76,"competencyId":"w4-myofilament-proteins"},{"id":"L01243","dok":1,"q":"Which structure is made of sarcomeres repeated end to end along its length?","a":"The myofibril.","options":["The sarcoplasmic reticulum","The myofibril","The myofilament","The fascicle"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"A myofibril is a long contractile rod that runs the length of a muscle fiber. It is built of thousands of sarcomeres lined up end to end, each one running from a Z disc to the next. Hundreds to thousands of myofibrils fill each fiber, and their aligned sarcomeres produce the striations.","notes":["The sarcoplasmic reticulum wraps around each myofibril and stores calcium ions. It surrounds the sarcomeres rather than being built from them.","Correct. Each myofibril is a chain of sarcomeres.","Myofilaments are the thick and thin protein strands inside a sarcomere. They are smaller than a sarcomere, so they cannot be made of sarcomeres.","A fascicle is a bundle of whole muscle fibers wrapped in perimysium. Pick it for the bundle level of organization."],"lt":"t-muscle-structure","lid":"c13","comp":74,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-fiber-parts"},{"id":"L01244","dok":2,"q":"Which band contains thick filaments across its full width, with thin filaments overlapping at each edge?","a":"The A band.","options":["The H zone","The I band","The A band","The Z disc"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The A band is the dark band of the sarcomere, as wide as the thick filaments are long. Thick filaments run across its whole width. At each edge, thin filaments slide in from the Z discs and overlap the thick filaments, forming the zones of overlap, while the center, the H zone, has thick filaments only.","notes":["The H zone is the center of the A band, where only thick filaments lie and no thin filaments overlap. It would be the answer for the thick-filaments-only region.","The I band holds thin filaments only, with no thick filaments at all. Pick it for the light band.","Correct. The A band spans the thick filaments, overlapped by thin filaments at its edges.","The Z disc is the protein plate at the sarcomere border that anchors thin filaments. No thick filaments reach it."],"lt":"t-muscle-structure","lid":"c14","comp":75,"competencyId":"w4-sarcomere"},{"id":"L01245","dok":1,"q":"What anchors the thin filaments at each end of the sarcomere?","a":"The Z disc.","options":["The Z disc","The H zone","The A band","The M line"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The Z disc is a protein plate at each end of the sarcomere. The protein alpha-actinin in the Z disc binds the thin filaments, which then extend inward across the I band toward the center. The thick filaments are anchored separately, at the M line.","notes":["Correct. Thin filaments attach to the Z discs at the sarcomere's borders.","The H zone is a region of thick filaments only, so thin filaments are absent there. It would be the answer for where thin filaments do not reach.","The A band is a band defined by the thick filaments, not an anchor. Pick it for the dark band.","The M line anchors the thick filaments, in the center of the sarcomere. Pick it for the thick filament anchor."],"lt":"t-muscle-structure","lid":"c15","comp":76,"competencyId":"w4-myofilament-proteins"},{"id":"L01247","dok":1,"q":"A skeletal muscle fiber contains many nuclei pressed against the inner sarcolemma. What is this property called?","a":"Multinucleated.","options":["Anucleate","Multinucleated","Branching","Binucleate"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Skeletal muscle fibers are multinucleated. A single fiber can contain hundreds of nuclei, called myonuclei, flattened against the inner surface of the sarcolemma. This is one of the main ways to tell skeletal muscle from cardiac and smooth muscle, which usually have a single central nucleus.","notes":["Anucleate means having no nucleus, as in a mature red blood cell. Pick it for a cell that has lost its nucleus.","Correct. A skeletal muscle fiber has many nuclei.","Branching describes the shape of cardiac muscle cells, not their nuclei. It would be the answer for a feature of heart muscle.","Binucleate means having exactly two nuclei, which some cardiac muscle cells and liver cells have. A skeletal fiber has far more than two."],"lt":"t-muscle-structure","lid":"c17","comp":74,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-fiber-parts"},{"id":"L01248","dok":2,"q":"Which structure carries the surface signal deep into the interior of a muscle fiber?","a":"The transverse (T) tubules.","options":["The sarcoplasmic reticulum","The terminal cisternae","The myofibrils","The transverse (T) tubules"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Transverse (T) tubules are narrow tunnels formed by the sarcolemma that dive inward from the cell surface and wrap around the myofibrils. Because they are continuous with the surface membrane, they carry the signal from the surface deep into the fiber, so the inner myofibrils respond at the same time as the outer ones.","notes":["The sarcoplasmic reticulum stores calcium ions around each myofibril; it receives the signal rather than carrying it in from the surface. Pick it for the calcium store.","Terminal cisternae are the end sacs of the sarcoplasmic reticulum beside each T tubule. They are part of the calcium store, not the inward path from the surface.","Myofibrils are the contractile rods that respond to the signal by shortening. They would be the answer for what fills most of the sarcoplasm.","Correct. T tubules are tunnels of sarcolemma that dive into the fiber."],"lt":"t-muscle-structure","lid":"c18","comp":74,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-fiber-parts"},{"id":"L01249","dok":2,"q":"Which band is shared by two neighboring sarcomeres, because a Z disc runs through its center?","a":"The I band.","options":["The zone of overlap","The H zone","The I band","The A band"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The Z disc marks the border between two sarcomeres and runs through the middle of the I band. That means each I band is split in half, with one half belonging to the sarcomere on each side. The A band, by contrast, sits entirely within one sarcomere.","notes":["The zone of overlap is at each edge of the A band, inside one sarcomere. Pick it for where thick and thin filaments interleave.","The H zone is in the center of a single sarcomere, bisected by the M line, not the Z disc. It would be the answer for the region of thick filaments only.","Correct. Half of each I band belongs to each of the two sarcomeres meeting at its Z disc.","The A band lies wholly within one sarcomere, centered on the M line. Pick it for the dark band that holds the thick filaments."],"lt":"t-muscle-structure","lid":"c19","comp":75,"competencyId":"w4-sarcomere"},{"id":"L01250","dok":2,"q":"When a muscle fiber shortens, which band keeps the same width?","a":"The A band.","options":["The whole sarcomere","The H zone","The A band","The I band"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The A band is as wide as the thick filaments are long, and the filaments themselves do not shorten. During contraction the thin filaments slide inward over the thick filaments, so the I band and the H zone narrow and the Z discs move closer together, but the A band stays the same width.","notes":["The sarcomere itself shortens as the Z discs are pulled closer together. It is what changes length, not what stays the same.","The H zone also narrows, and can disappear, as the thin filaments slide toward the center. It would be the answer for the central region that shrinks.","Correct. The A band equals the length of the thick filaments, which do not change.","The I band narrows as the thin filaments slide inward over the thick ones. Pick it for a band that gets narrower during contraction."],"lt":"t-muscle-structure","lid":"c20","comp":75,"competencyId":"w4-sarcomere"},{"id":"L01252","dok":2,"q":"Where does titin run within the sarcomere?","a":"From the Z disc to the M line.","options":["Along the length of the thin filament only","From the Z disc to the M line","At the M line only","From the thin filaments to the sarcolemma"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Titin is anchored at the Z disc and runs through the core of the thick filament to the M line, spanning half of the sarcomere. Placed there, it holds the thick filament centered and acts like a spring, giving elastic recoil after stretching and keeping the sarcomere from overstretching.","notes":["This is the position of nebulin, which sets thin filament length. Pick it for the thin filament ruler.","Correct. Titin spans each half sarcomere.","This is the position of myomesin, which binds thick filaments together. It would be the answer for the M line protein.","This is the position of dystrophin, which links the myofibril to the cell membrane. Pick it for the protein faulty in muscular dystrophy."],"lt":"t-muscle-structure","lid":"c22","comp":76,"competencyId":"w4-myofilament-proteins"},{"id":"L01253","dok":2,"q":"What role does dystrophin play in a muscle fiber?","a":"It transfers the force of contraction from the sarcomeres out to the sarcolemma and the connective tissue.","options":["It passes sarcomere force to the sarcolemma","It covers the myosin-binding sites on actin","It gives the sarcomere elastic recoil","It anchors the thick filaments at the M line"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Dystrophin links the thin filaments at the edge of the myofibrils to proteins in the sarcolemma. Through that link, the pull of the sarcomeres is passed to the cell membrane and from there to the endomysium and the rest of the connective tissue. When dystrophin is faulty, as in Duchenne muscular dystrophy, the fiber is damaged by its own contractions.","notes":["Correct. Dystrophin links the thin filaments to the cell membrane so force reaches the connective tissue.","This is the role of tropomyosin, a regulatory protein. It would be the answer for what keeps relaxed muscle from contracting.","This is the role of titin. Pick titin for the spring between the Z disc and the M line.","This is the role of myomesin. Pick it for the protein at the center of the sarcomere."],"lt":"t-muscle-structure","lid":"c23","comp":76,"competencyId":"w4-myofilament-proteins"},{"id":"L01255","dok":1,"q":"Where in the sarcomere is myomesin found?","a":"At the M line.","options":["At the Z disc","Throughout the I band","At the M line","Along the sarcolemma"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Myomesin is a structural protein located at the M line, the center of the sarcomere. It binds neighboring thick filaments to one another, keeping them evenly spaced. Remembering that myomesin and the M line share the letter M helps keep it apart from alpha-actinin at the Z disc.","notes":["The Z disc holds alpha-actinin, which anchors the thin filaments and titin. Pick it for alpha-actinin.","The I band holds thin filaments only, and its structural protein is nebulin along each thin filament. It would be the answer for nebulin's region.","Correct. Myomesin binds the thick filaments together at the M line.","The sarcolemma is linked to the myofibrils by dystrophin. Pick it for where dystrophin works."],"lt":"t-muscle-structure","lid":"c25","comp":76,"competencyId":"w4-myofilament-proteins"},{"id":"L01256","dok":3,"q":"A signal arriving at the surface of a muscle fiber must reach the calcium store throughout the fiber almost at once. Which repeating structure makes this possible?","a":"The triad.","options":["The triad","The myofibril","The Z disc","The sarcomere"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The triad is one transverse (T) tubule flanked by two terminal cisternae of the sarcoplasmic reticulum. The T tubule brings the surface signal deep into the fiber, and right beside it the cisternae hold calcium. Because triads repeat along every myofibril, the signal reaches the calcium store throughout the fiber almost at the same moment.","notes":["Correct. Each triad is a meeting point of a T tubule and the calcium store.","The myofibril is the contractile rod made of sarcomeres. It would be the answer for what the sarcoplasmic reticulum wraps around.","The Z disc anchors the thin filaments at the sarcomere border. It has no role in carrying the signal to the calcium store.","The sarcomere is the contractile unit that shortens once calcium is released. It responds to the signal but does not deliver it. Pick it for the unit between two Z discs."],"lt":"t-muscle-structure","lid":"c26","comp":74,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-fiber-parts"},{"id":"L01258","dok":2,"q":"In Duchenne muscular dystrophy, fibers damage themselves with ordinary use. Which structural link fails?","a":"The link between the thin filaments and the sarcolemma.","options":["The link between the T tubule and the terminal cisternae","The link between the thick filaments at the M line","The link between the tendon and the bone","The link between the thin filaments and the sarcolemma"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Dystrophin normally connects the thin filaments at the edge of the myofibrils to the sarcolemma, passing the force of contraction out to the connective tissue. In Duchenne muscular dystrophy the dystrophin gene is faulty, so that link fails. Each contraction then stresses the membrane, the fiber damages itself with ordinary use, and weakness gets worse over time.","notes":["This is the triad, where the surface signal meets the calcium store. It would be the answer for the structure that coordinates calcium release.","This link is made by myomesin and is not the defect in Duchenne muscular dystrophy. Pick it for the M line connection.","This link fails in a tendon rupture, an injury rather than an inherited disease. Pick it for an injury that separates muscle from bone.","Correct. This is the link made by dystrophin."],"lt":"t-muscle-structure","lid":"c28","comp":76,"competencyId":"w4-myofilament-proteins"},{"id":"L01260","dok":2,"q":"At each end of the A band, thick and thin filaments lie interleaved. What is this region called?","a":"The zone of overlap.","options":["The H zone","The zone of overlap","The M line","The I band"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The zone of overlap is at each end of the A band, where thin filaments from the Z disc slide in between the thick filaments. Because both filaments are present, it is the darkest part of the sarcomere, and it is where myosin heads can reach actin to form cross bridges. The center of the A band, beyond the reach of the thin filaments, is the H zone.","notes":["The H zone is the center of the A band, with thick filaments only. Pick it for the region thin filaments do not reach.","Correct. Both filament types are present here.","The M line is the protein line in the center of the H zone that anchors thick filaments. It is not where thin filaments are found.","The I band lies outside the A band and holds thin filaments only. It would be the answer for the light band."],"lt":"t-muscle-structure","lid":"c30","comp":75,"competencyId":"w4-sarcomere"},{"id":"L01261","dok":2,"q":"Why does a skeletal muscle fiber have many nuclei?","a":"Each fiber forms from many embryonic cells fusing together, and its many nuclei together maintain a very long cell.","options":["The extra nuclei let the fiber contract without nerve input","The extra nuclei store calcium for contraction","It has divided its nucleus repeatedly to prepare for cell division","It forms from many fused embryonic cells, and the nuclei maintain a very long cell"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"A skeletal muscle fiber is unusually long, and it forms during development when many embryonic muscle cells fuse into one. Each of those cells contributes its nucleus, so the mature fiber has hundreds of nuclei flattened along the sarcolemma. Spread along its length, these nuclei direct the protein production needed to maintain such a large cell.","notes":["Skeletal muscle is voluntary and contracts only when a motor neuron signals it. The number of nuclei has nothing to do with that control.","Calcium is stored in the sarcoplasmic reticulum, not in nuclei. Pick the sarcoplasmic reticulum for the calcium store.","Mature skeletal muscle fibers do not divide. This misconception treats the many nuclei as a sign of a cell about to split.","Correct. Fusion supplies the nuclei, and a long cell needs many to maintain it."],"lt":"t-muscle-structure","lid":"d3-t-muscle-structure-1","comp":74,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-fiber-parts"},{"id":"L01262","dok":2,"q":"A myofibril is built of thousands of sarcomeres lined up end to end. Why does this arrangement produce a large overall shortening?","a":"Each sarcomere shortens a little, and the changes add up along the chain","options":["Each sarcomere shortens a little, and the changes add up along the chain","The stripes are pigment and play no role in contraction","Neighboring sarcomeres pull against each other and cancel out","The sarcomeres take turns contracting so motion never stops"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Each sarcomere shortens only a tiny distance as its Z discs are pulled toward the M line. Because thousands of sarcomeres are joined end to end along a myofibril, all of those tiny shortenings add together. The result is a noticeable shortening of the whole myofibril, the fiber, and finally the muscle.","notes":["Correct. Sarcomeres in series sum their shortening.","The stripes are the bands of the sarcomeres themselves, the actual contractile machinery. Pigment in muscle comes from myoglobin, not the striations.","Neighboring sarcomeres are linked end to end through their shared Z discs, so their shortening adds together instead of canceling. If they canceled each other out, the myofibril could not shorten at all.","Sarcomeres along a myofibril shorten together, not in turns. This misconception confuses muscle with a wave-like process."],"lt":"t-muscle-structure","lid":"d3-t-muscle-structure-2","comp":75,"competencyId":"w4-sarcomere"},{"id":"L01264","dok":2,"q":"The sarcoplasmic reticulum forms a network wrapped around each myofibril. Why does placing the calcium store this close to the myofibrils matter?","a":"Calcium only has to travel a tiny distance, so contraction starts and stops quickly.","options":["A short distance lets contraction start and stop fast","The location of the store has no effect on response time","Closeness slows calcium down so contraction is gentle","The store must be far away so calcium can build up"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The sarcoplasmic reticulum is the smooth endoplasmic reticulum of a muscle fiber, and it wraps every myofibril like a sleeve. Because the calcium is stored right next to the myofilaments, it reaches them almost instantly when released and can be pulled back just as fast. This short distance lets a muscle fiber contract and relax quickly.","notes":["Correct. A close store means fast release and fast reuptake.","A store far from the filaments would delay every contraction. The wrapping arrangement exists precisely because distance matters.","Closeness does the opposite; a short distance speeds the response. This choice reverses the effect of distance.","Calcium builds up inside the sarcoplasmic reticulum itself, wherever it is. Distance from the filaments would only slow release."],"lt":"t-muscle-structure","lid":"d3-t-muscle-structure-4","comp":74,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-fiber-parts"},{"id":"L01267","dok":3,"q":"The muscles that move the eye can make very fine adjustments, while large thigh muscles produce strong but coarse movements. How does the number of fibers in each motor unit explain this?","a":"Fewer fibers per motor neuron means each signal moves a smaller amount of muscle, allowing finer control.","options":["Precision depends only on overall muscle size","More fibers per motor neuron always gives finer control","Motor unit size affects muscle color but not control","Fewer fibers per neuron allows finer steps"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"A motor unit is one motor neuron and all the muscle fibers it controls. In the eye muscles each neuron controls only a few fibers, so each signal moves a tiny amount of muscle and adjustments can be very fine. In large thigh muscles each neuron controls many fibers, so each signal recruits a large block of muscle, producing strength rather than precision.","notes":["Muscle size alone does not set precision. A small muscle with large motor units would still move coarsely.","This reverses the relationship. Large motor units move a lot of muscle at once, which suits power rather than precision.","Muscle color comes mainly from myoglobin. Motor unit size determines how finely a muscle can be controlled.","Correct. Small motor units allow precise control."],"lt":"t-muscle-structure","lid":"d3-t-muscle-structure-7"},{"id":"L01268","dok":2,"q":"Muscles used for endurance, such as postural muscles, are rich in mitochondria. Why does an endurance muscle benefit from many mitochondria?","a":"Mitochondria supply the steady ATP that long-lasting contraction demands.","options":["Mitochondria store oxygen inside the fiber","Mitochondria store the calcium that triggers contraction","Mitochondria form the striations of the fiber","Mitochondria supply the steady ATP that sustained contraction needs"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Mitochondria are packed in rows between the myofibrils, where they supply the ATP that contraction demands. They make ATP using oxygen, which provides a steady energy supply over long periods. Muscles that work for hours, like the postural muscles of the back, rely on that steady supply and so contain many mitochondria.","notes":["Oxygen is stored by myoglobin, the red pigment in the sarcoplasm. It would be the answer for the fiber's oxygen reserve.","Calcium is stored in the sarcoplasmic reticulum. Pick the sarcoplasmic reticulum for the calcium store.","The striations come from the A and I bands of the sarcomeres. Mitochondria lie in rows between myofibrils but do not create the stripes.","Correct. Mitochondria produce ATP aerobically for long-lasting work."],"lt":"t-muscle-structure","lid":"d3-t-muscle-structure-8","comp":74,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-fiber-parts"},{"id":"L04269","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly describes myofilaments and how they relate to the sarcomere?","a":"They are the thick and thin protein strands in a myofibril, and their ordered overlap forms the sarcomere.","options":["They are connective tissue fibers that tie each sarcomere to the tendon","They are membrane tubules that wrap each myofibril and store calcium for the sarcomere","They are the thick and thin protein strands whose ordered overlap forms the sarcomere","They are bundles of myofibrils whose ordered overlap forms the fascicle"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Myofilaments are the thick filaments (mostly myosin) and thin filaments (mostly actin) packed inside each myofibril. They are arranged in a precise, repeating pattern, and that pattern of overlap is what defines the sarcomere, the segment from one Z disc to the next. The bands you see under the microscope are simply regions where thick filaments, thin filaments, or both are present.","notes":["Connective tissue never enters the cell to reach sarcomeres. The link from sarcomere to membrane is made by proteins such as dystrophin, and the link from fiber to tendon is made by the endomysium and other sheaths.","This describes the sarcoplasmic reticulum, the smooth ER that wraps each myofibril and stores calcium ions. Pick it if the question asked which structure stores calcium in a muscle fiber.","Correct. The ordered overlap of thick and thin filaments is the sarcomere.","Myofilaments are smaller than myofibrils, not bundles of them, and a fascicle is a bundle of whole muscle fibers. This choice reverses the size order of the levels."],"lt":"t-w4-muscle-organization","lid":"w4-muscle-organization-r4","comp":73,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-organization"},{"id":"L04271","dok":1,"q":"What is the sarcolemma?","a":"The plasma membrane of the muscle fiber.","options":["The smooth endoplasmic reticulum of the muscle fiber","The connective tissue sheath around each muscle fiber","The plasma membrane of the muscle fiber","The cytoplasm of the muscle fiber"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Parts of a muscle fiber carry the prefix sarco, from the Greek for flesh, added to familiar cell terms. The sarcolemma is the plasma membrane that encloses the fiber, and it dives inward at intervals to form the T tubules. On a slide it is the thin boundary of each fiber, with the flattened nuclei lying just beneath it.","notes":["This describes the sarcoplasmic reticulum, which stores calcium ions. It would be the answer if the question asked about the calcium store of the fiber.","This describes the endomysium, which lies outside the sarcolemma. Choose it if the question asked what connective tissue wraps each fiber; students mix the two up because both form the edge of a fiber on a slide.","Correct. The sarcolemma is the muscle fiber's plasma membrane.","This describes the sarcoplasm, the cytoplasm of the muscle fiber. Pick it if the question asked for the fluid interior that holds the myofibrils, glycogen, and myoglobin."],"lt":"t-w4-muscle-fiber-parts","lid":"w4-muscle-fiber-parts-r1","comp":74,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-fiber-parts"},{"id":"L04272","dok":1,"q":"Which description matches the sarcoplasmic reticulum of a muscle fiber?","a":"The smooth endoplasmic reticulum of the fiber, which wraps each myofibril and stores calcium ions.","options":["The fluid cytoplasm of the fiber, holding glycogen granules and myoglobin","Rows of organelles between the myofibrils that supply ATP","Inward tunnels of the sarcolemma that carry the surface signal deep into the fiber","The smooth endoplasmic reticulum of the fiber, which stores calcium ions"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The sarcoplasmic reticulum is the muscle fiber's version of smooth endoplasmic reticulum, a network of tubules wrapping each myofibril. Its job is to store calcium ions and release them when the fiber is signaled. Its enlarged end sacs, the terminal cisternae, sit on either side of each T tubule to form a triad.","notes":["This describes the sarcoplasm, the cytoplasm of the fiber. It would be the answer if the question asked where glycogen and myoglobin are held.","This describes the mitochondria, packed in rows between myofibrils. Choose it if the question asked which organelle supplies the ATP that contraction needs.","This describes the transverse (T) tubules, which are extensions of the sarcolemma. Pick it if the question asked which structure carries the signal from the surface into the fiber.","Correct. The sarcoplasmic reticulum is the smooth ER of the fiber and its calcium store."],"lt":"t-w4-muscle-fiber-parts","lid":"w4-muscle-fiber-parts-r2","comp":74,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-fiber-parts"},{"id":"L04273","dok":1,"q":"What is a triad in a skeletal muscle fiber?","a":"One T tubule flanked by two terminal cisternae of the sarcoplasmic reticulum.","options":["One terminal cisterna flanked by two T tubules","One T tubule flanked by two terminal cisternae","One A band flanked by two I bands","Three myofibrils bundled together by endomysium"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"A triad is a three-part structural unit: a single transverse (T) tubule in the middle with an enlarged sac of sarcoplasmic reticulum, a terminal cisterna, pressed against it on each side. This is the meeting point where a signal traveling down the T tubule reaches the calcium store. Repeating triads along each myofibril let the whole fiber respond almost at once.","notes":["This reverses the arrangement. There is one T tubule in the center, with the sarcoplasmic reticulum sacs on the outside, so students who pick this have the two parts swapped.","Correct. A triad is one T tubule between two terminal cisternae.","The A band and I bands are regions of the sarcomere seen under the microscope, not a triad. Pick the A band and I bands if the question asked which bands produce the striations.","Myofibrils are not wrapped individually by endomysium; the endomysium wraps the whole fiber. This choice comes from guessing that triad means three of something larger."],"lt":"t-w4-muscle-fiber-parts","lid":"w4-muscle-fiber-parts-r3","comp":74,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-fiber-parts"},{"id":"L04274","dok":2,"q":"What do the transverse (T) tubules of a skeletal muscle fiber do?","a":"They are inward tunnels of the sarcolemma that carry the surface signal deep into the fiber.","options":["They store calcium ions in sacs wrapped around each myofibril","They anchor the thick filaments to one another at the center of the sarcomere","They are inward tunnels of the sarcolemma that carry the surface signal deep into the fiber","They are enlarged end sacs of the sarcoplasmic reticulum beside each tubule"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"T tubules are narrow tunnels of the sarcolemma that dive inward, crossing the fiber at right angles to its length. Because they are continuous with the surface membrane, they carry the signal that starts at the surface down into the middle of a thick fiber. Each one runs between two terminal cisternae, forming a triad, so the signal reaches the calcium store everywhere at once.","notes":["This describes the sarcoplasmic reticulum, the calcium store of the fiber. Pick it if the question asked where calcium ions are held.","This describes the M line and its protein myomesin, which bind the thick filaments together in the center of the sarcomere. It would be the answer if the question asked where thick filaments are anchored.","Correct. T tubules extend the sarcolemma inward to carry the signal deep into the fiber.","This describes the terminal cisternae, the end sacs of the sarcoplasmic reticulum that flank each T tubule. Choose them if the question asked what lies on either side of a T tubule in a triad."],"lt":"t-w4-muscle-fiber-parts","lid":"w4-muscle-fiber-parts-r4","comp":74,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-fiber-parts"},{"id":"L04275","dok":2,"q":"On a slide of skeletal muscle in longitudinal section, a student sees many flattened nuclei along the edge of a single long fiber. What explains this appearance?","a":"Each fiber forms from many fused embryonic cells, so it is multinucleated with nuclei pressed against the inner sarcolemma.","options":["Each fiber forms from many fused embryonic cells, so its many nuclei lie against the sarcolemma","Each nucleus belongs to an endomysium cell, while the fiber itself has none","Each nucleus sits inside one sarcomere, spaced along the myofibrils","Each nucleus belongs to a separate short cell, joined end to end by intercalated discs"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"A skeletal muscle fiber develops when many embryonic muscle cells fuse into one long cell, so a single fiber contains many nuclei. The myofibrils fill most of the cell, which pushes these nuclei out to the periphery, where they lie flattened just under the sarcolemma. Peripheral nuclei are one of the features that let you tell skeletal muscle from cardiac and smooth muscle, which have central nuclei.","notes":["Correct. Fusion of many cells makes the fiber multinucleated, and the myofibrils push the nuclei to the edge.","The endomysium does contain a few connective tissue cells, but a skeletal muscle fiber is multinucleated in its own right. Students choose this when they assume every cell must have just one nucleus.","Nuclei never sit inside sarcomeres, which are made of filaments within the myofibrils. This choice comes from mistaking the regular spacing of nuclei for part of the striation pattern.","Intercalated discs join separate short cells in cardiac muscle, where each cell has one central nucleus. Pick this description if the slide showed branching cells with dark cross lines between them."],"lt":"t-w4-muscle-fiber-parts","lid":"w4-muscle-fiber-parts-r5","comp":74,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-fiber-parts"},{"id":"L04276","dok":1,"q":"Which structures mark the two boundaries of a sarcomere?","a":"The Z discs, one at each end.","options":["The A bands","The Z discs","The M lines","The H zones"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"A sarcomere is the segment of a myofibril from one Z disc to the next. The Z disc is a protein plate that anchors the thin filaments, and it runs through the center of each I band. Because each Z disc is shared, every sarcomere borders the next one in a continuous chain along the myofibril.","notes":["The A band is the dark band in the middle of the sarcomere, as wide as the thick filaments are long. Choose it if the question asked which band never changes length during contraction.","Correct. A sarcomere runs from one Z disc to the next.","The M line runs through the center of the sarcomere, not its edges, and it anchors the thick filaments. Pick it if the question asked what marks the middle of a sarcomere.","The H zone is the lighter region in the center of the A band, where only thick filaments are found. It would be the answer for the central zone with thick filaments only."],"lt":"t-w4-sarcomere","lid":"w4-sarcomere-r1","comp":75,"competencyId":"w4-sarcomere"},{"id":"L04277","dok":1,"q":"Which filaments are found in the I band of a sarcomere?","a":"Thin filaments only.","options":["No filaments, only the Z disc protein","Thick filaments only","Thin filaments only","Thick and thin filaments overlapping"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The I band is the light band at each end of the sarcomere, the stretch where thin (actin) filaments extend from the Z disc but no thick filaments reach. Having only thin filaments makes it look pale under the microscope. A Z disc runs through the center of each I band, so one I band is shared by two neighboring sarcomeres.","notes":["The Z disc runs through the I band, but the band itself is filled with thin filaments anchored to that disc. Students pick this when they think the light color means the band is empty.","Thick filaments only is the content of the H zone in the center of the A band. Pick it if the question asked about the H zone.","Correct. The I band contains thin filaments only.","Both kinds overlapping is the zone of overlap at each end of the A band. It would be the answer for the region where thick and thin filaments interleave."],"lt":"t-w4-sarcomere","lid":"w4-sarcomere-r2","comp":75,"competencyId":"w4-sarcomere"},{"id":"L04278","dok":1,"q":"Which filaments are found in the H zone of a sarcomere?","a":"Thick filaments only.","options":["Thick filaments only","Thick and thin filaments overlapping","Thin filaments only","No filaments, only the M line protein"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The H zone is the lighter region in the center of the A band. Thick (myosin) filaments run through it, but the thin filaments coming in from each Z disc do not reach that far at rest, so only thick filaments are present. The M line runs down the middle of the H zone and holds the thick filaments together.","notes":["Correct. The H zone holds thick filaments only.","Overlapping thick and thin filaments make up the zone of overlap at each edge of the A band. It would be the answer for the darkest part of the A band.","Thin filaments only is the content of the I band at the ends of the sarcomere. Pick it if the question asked about the I band.","The M line does run through the H zone, but the zone itself is filled with thick filaments. Students choose this when they mistake the lighter shade of the H zone for an empty space."],"lt":"t-w4-sarcomere","lid":"w4-sarcomere-r3","comp":75,"competencyId":"w4-sarcomere"},{"id":"L04279","dok":2,"q":"Which description matches the A band of a sarcomere?","a":"The dark band, as wide as the thick filaments are long, with thin filaments overlapping at each edge.","options":["The protein line in the center that anchors the thick filaments","The light band with thin filaments only, split down the middle by a Z disc","The lighter central region with thick filaments only","The dark band, as wide as the thick filaments are long, with thin filaments overlapping at each edge"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The A band is the dark band of the sarcomere, and its width is set by the length of the thick filaments, which run across its full width. Thin filaments slide into each end of it, creating zones of overlap, while its center (the H zone) has thick filaments only. Because its width equals the thick filament length, the A band does not change length when the muscle shortens.","notes":["This describes the M line, which runs down the center of the H zone. Choose it if the question asked where the thick filaments are anchored.","This describes the I band, the light band of thin filaments with a Z disc through its center. Pick it if the question asked about the light band.","This describes the H zone, which sits in the middle of the A band. It would be the answer for the central region where only thick filaments are found.","Correct. The A band spans the full length of the thick filaments, with overlap at each edge."],"lt":"t-w4-sarcomere","lid":"w4-sarcomere-r4","comp":75,"competencyId":"w4-sarcomere"},{"id":"L04280","dok":1,"q":"Under the microscope, skeletal muscle shows alternating dark and light stripes called striations. Which bands produce them?","a":"Dark A bands alternating with light I bands.","options":["Dark Z discs alternating with light M lines","Dark A bands alternating with light I bands","Dark I bands alternating with light A bands","Dark intercalated discs alternating with light sarcoplasm"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Striations are the repeating pattern of thousands of sarcomeres lined up across neighboring myofibrils. The A band, where the thick filaments lie, looks dark, and the I band, which has thin filaments only, looks light. Because the sarcomeres are in register across the whole fiber, these bands line up into visible stripes.","notes":["Z discs and M lines are thin lines within the bands, not the broad stripes themselves. Pick the Z disc if the question asked what marks the border of a sarcomere.","Correct. Dark A bands and light I bands repeat to form the striations.","This reverses the shading. The I band is the light band (it has thin filaments only) and the A band is the dark band, so students who pick this have the letters swapped.","Intercalated discs are found only in cardiac muscle, where they appear as dark cross lines joining neighboring cells. Choose them if the question asked what joins cardiac muscle cells end to end."],"lt":"t-w4-sarcomere","lid":"w4-sarcomere-r5","comp":75,"competencyId":"w4-sarcomere"},{"id":"L04314","dok":1,"q":"What is the main protein of the thick filament, and where is the thick filament anchored?","a":"Myosin, anchored at the M line.","options":["Actin, anchored at the Z disc","Myosin, anchored at the M line","Myosin, anchored at the Z disc","Actin, anchored at the M line"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The thick filament is a bundle of myosin molecules, each with a long tail and a pair of heads that project outward as cross bridges. Thick filaments sit in the center of the sarcomere and are bound to one another at the M line. Titin then runs from the Z disc to the M line, holding each thick filament in position.","notes":["Actin anchored at the Z disc describes the thin filament. Pick it if the question asked about the thin filament.","Correct. The thick filament is made of myosin and anchored at the M line.","Myosin is right, but the Z disc anchors the thin filaments at the ends of the sarcomere. Students pick this when they remember the Z disc as the anchor for every filament.","Actin is the thin filament protein, and the M line holds the thick filaments, not thin ones. This choice pairs each half with the wrong filament."],"lt":"t-w4-myofilament-proteins","lid":"w4-myofilament-proteins-r1","comp":76,"competencyId":"w4-myofilament-proteins"},{"id":"L04315","dok":1,"q":"What is the main protein of the thin filament, and where is the thin filament anchored?","a":"Actin, anchored at the Z disc.","options":["Actin, anchored at the Z disc","Myosin, anchored at the Z disc","Actin, anchored at the M line","Myosin, anchored at the M line"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The thin filament is made of two twisted strands of actin subunits, each with a binding site for myosin. Thin filaments extend inward from the Z disc at each end of the sarcomere toward the center. The regulatory proteins tropomyosin and troponin lie along the thin filament as well.","notes":["Correct. The thin filament is made of actin and anchored at the Z disc.","Myosin forms the thick filament, and the thick filaments are not attached to the Z disc. This choice pairs the thick filament protein with the thin filament anchor.","Actin is right, but thin filaments come in from the ends of the sarcomere and do not reach the M line at rest. That is why the H zone around the M line has thick filaments only.","Myosin anchored at the M line describes the thick filament. Pick it if the question asked about the thick filament."],"lt":"t-w4-myofilament-proteins","lid":"w4-myofilament-proteins-r2","comp":76,"competencyId":"w4-myofilament-proteins"},{"id":"L04316","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly describes the roles of titin and dystrophin?","a":"Titin anchors the thick filament and gives elastic recoil; dystrophin links the thin filaments to the sarcolemma and is faulty in muscular dystrophy.","options":["Titin links the thin filaments to the sarcolemma, and dystrophin anchors the thick filament and gives elastic recoil","Titin and dystrophin both lie along the thin filament as regulatory proteins","Titin forms the core of the thin filament, and dystrophin binds the thick filaments together at the M line","Titin anchors the thick filament and gives elastic recoil, and dystrophin links the thin filaments to the sarcolemma"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Titin is a huge elastic protein that spans from the Z disc to the M line, holding the thick filament in place and springing the sarcomere back after it is stretched. Dystrophin links the thin filaments to the sarcolemma, passing the force of contraction out to the connective tissue. In Duchenne muscular dystrophy the dystrophin gene is faulty, so the fiber damages itself with ordinary use.","notes":["This swaps the two proteins. Titin is the elastic anchor of the thick filament, and dystrophin is the link to the cell membrane.","The regulatory proteins on the thin filament are tropomyosin and troponin. They would be the answer if the question asked which proteins lie along the thin filament.","The core of the thin filament is actin, and the protein that binds thick filaments at the M line is myomesin. Pick actin or myomesin if the question asked about those roles.","Correct. Titin anchors the thick filament and gives recoil, and dystrophin ties the thin filaments to the sarcolemma."],"lt":"t-w4-myofilament-proteins","lid":"w4-myofilament-proteins-r3","comp":76,"competencyId":"w4-myofilament-proteins"},{"id":"N-m3-muscle-microanatomy-1","dok":2,"q":"When a skeletal muscle shortens, which region of the sarcomere stays the same length?","a":"The A band.","options":["The A band","The distance between Z discs","The I band","The H zone"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The width of the A band is set by the length of the thick filaments, and the filaments themselves do not get shorter when a muscle contracts. Instead, the thin filaments slide farther over the thick filaments toward the M line. As a result, the I band and H zone narrow and the Z discs move closer together, while the A band keeps its width.","notes":["Correct. The A band equals the length of the thick filaments, so it does not change.","The Z discs move closer together as the muscle shortens, so the sarcomere as a whole gets shorter. Students pick this when they think the sarcomere stays the same and only the filaments move.","The I band narrows during shortening because the thin filaments slide deeper into the A band, leaving less thin-filament-only space. Pick it if the question asked which band gets narrower.","The H zone narrows and can disappear as the thin filaments slide in toward the M line. It would be the answer if the question asked which central region shrinks the most."],"comp":75,"competencyId":"w4-sarcomere"}]},{"id":"m3-muscle-naming-levers","title":"Muscle roles, naming and lever systems","summary":"origin and insertion, prime mover, synergist, antagonist, fixator, naming criteria, first, second and third class levers","mw":"2026-10-07","tr":"2026-10-06","cards":[{"id":"L00977","dok":1,"q":"The origin of a muscle is its attachment to which bone?","a":"The more stationary bone.","options":["The more stationary bone","The more movable bone","The more distal bone","The larger bone"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"A muscle crosses a joint and attaches to the bones on either side. When it contracts, one bone stays relatively still and the other moves. The attachment to the stationary bone is the origin, and the attachment to the moving bone is the insertion, which is pulled toward the origin.","notes":["Correct. The origin is the attachment to the more stationary bone.","The attachment to the more movable bone is the insertion. Pick this answer for a question about the insertion.","In the limbs the origin is usually proximal, not distal, so this reverses the usual rule of thumb. The more distal attachment is usually the insertion.","Bone size does not decide which attachment is the origin. What matters is which bone stays still during the movement, so students who pick this are guessing from size rather than function."],"lt":"t-muscle-gross","lid":"c3","comp":79,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-roles-naming"},{"id":"L00978","dok":1,"q":"The insertion of a muscle is its attachment to which bone?","a":"The more movable bone.","options":["The larger bone","The more movable bone","The more proximal bone","The more stationary bone"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The insertion is where a muscle attaches to the bone that moves when the muscle contracts. During contraction, the muscle pulls the insertion toward the origin, which stays relatively still. For example, the biceps brachii inserts on the radius, so when it contracts the forearm moves up toward the shoulder.","notes":["Bone size does not decide which attachment is the insertion. What matters is which bone moves during the action.","Correct. The insertion is the attachment to the more movable bone.","In the limbs the more proximal attachment is usually the origin, not the insertion. The insertion is usually the more distal attachment, so this reverses the rule of thumb.","The attachment to the more stationary bone is the origin. Pick this answer for a question about the origin."],"lt":"t-muscle-gross","lid":"c4","comp":79,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-roles-naming"},{"id":"L00979","dok":1,"q":"As a general rule of thumb for limb muscles, which pairing of origin and insertion is usual?","a":"Origin proximal, insertion distal.","options":["Origin proximal, insertion distal","Origin medial, insertion lateral","Origin superficial, insertion deep","Origin distal, insertion proximal"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"In the limbs, the origin is usually the proximal attachment, closer to the trunk, and the insertion is usually the distal attachment, farther from the trunk. That fits how limbs work: the proximal bone usually stays steady while the distal bone moves. The biceps brachii follows this rule, with its origin on the scapula and its insertion on the radius.","notes":["Correct. The origin is usually proximal and the insertion usually distal.","Medial and lateral describe position relative to the midline, and they do not determine which attachment is the origin. Students pick this when they mix up directional term pairs.","Superficial and deep describe depth from the body surface, not which bone moves. This pairing has nothing to do with defining origin and insertion.","This reverses the rule of thumb. The distal bone is usually the one that moves, so it usually carries the insertion."],"lt":"t-muscle-gross","lid":"c5","comp":79,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-roles-naming"},{"id":"L00980","dok":1,"q":"What is the movement a muscle produces when it contracts called?","a":"The action.","options":["The lever","The action","The origin","The insertion"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"A muscle's action is the movement it produces when it contracts and pulls its insertion toward its origin. Actions are named with movement terms such as flexion, extension, abduction, and adduction. For example, the action of the biceps brachii is flexion of the elbow.","notes":["In a lever system the lever is the rigid bar that moves, which in the body is a bone. Pick it for a question about what plays the role of the bar in a body lever.","Correct. The movement produced by a contracting muscle is its action.","The origin is the attachment to the more stationary bone, not a movement. Pick it for a question about which attachment stays still.","The insertion is the attachment to the more movable bone, the point that gets pulled. Pick it for a question about which end moves toward the other."],"lt":"t-muscle-gross","lid":"c6","comp":79,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-roles-naming"},{"id":"L00997","dok":2,"q":"The brachialis attaches to the distal humerus and to the ulna, and it flexes the elbow. During elbow flexion the forearm moves while the arm stays steady. Which attachment is its origin?","a":"Its attachment on the humerus.","options":["Its attachment on the scapula","Its attachment on the radius","Its attachment on the ulna","Its attachment on the humerus"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The origin is the attachment on the bone that stays relatively still, and in the limbs it is usually the proximal one. When the brachialis flexes the elbow, the humerus stays steady and the ulna moves up toward it. That makes the humerus attachment the origin and the ulna attachment the insertion.","notes":["The brachialis does not reach the scapula. The scapula carries the origins of the biceps brachii and the long head of the triceps brachii, so pick it for questions about those muscles.","The brachialis does not attach to the radius. The radius carries the insertion of the biceps brachii, so pick it for a question about where the biceps inserts.","The ulna is the bone that moves during elbow flexion, so the brachialis attachment there is its insertion. Pick the ulna if the question asks for the insertion of the brachialis.","Correct. The humerus stays steady, so the brachialis attachment there is the origin."],"lt":"t-muscle-gross","lid":"c23","comp":79,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-roles-naming"},{"id":"L01004","dok":2,"q":"When a muscle contracts, which statement describes the usual movement of its attachments?","a":"The insertion is pulled toward the origin.","options":["The insertion is pulled toward the origin","Both attachments are pushed apart","The origin is pulled toward the insertion","The belly lengthens while both attachments stay still"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"A contracting muscle shortens and pulls on both of its attachments, but the bone carrying the origin is held steady. As a result, the insertion on the more movable bone is pulled toward the origin. This is why the insertion is defined as the attachment that moves.","notes":["Correct. The insertion is pulled toward the origin.","Muscles can only pull; they never push. Students choose this when they picture a muscle bulging outward and assume it pushes the bones apart.","In a usual movement the origin stays steady and the insertion moves. The origin moves only in a reversed action, such as a pull-up, where the limb is fixed and the trunk is pulled toward it.","A belly that lengthens while being pulled describes a muscle being stretched, as the antagonist is during a movement. Pick this idea for a question about what happens to the triceps brachii when the elbow bends."],"lt":"t-muscle-gross","lid":"c30","comp":79,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-roles-naming"},{"id":"L01010","dok":2,"q":"The biceps brachii runs from the scapula to the radius. When it flexes the elbow, the forearm rises while the scapula stays steady. Which bone carries its insertion?","a":"The radius.","options":["The humerus","The scapula","The radius","The ulna"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The insertion is the attachment on the bone that moves, which is pulled toward the origin. During elbow flexion the forearm rises while the scapula stays steady, so the attachment on the radius (at the radial tuberosity) is the insertion. The scapula attachments are the origins of its two heads.","notes":["The biceps brachii crosses the humerus but does not attach to it. The humerus carries the origin of the brachialis, so pick it for a question about that muscle.","The scapula stays steady during elbow flexion, so it carries the origins of the biceps brachii, not its insertion. Pick the scapula if the question asks for the origin.","Correct. The radius moves, so it carries the insertion of the biceps brachii.","The ulna is a forearm bone that moves during elbow flexion, but the biceps brachii does not insert on it. The brachialis inserts on the ulna, so pick the ulna for a question about the brachialis."],"lt":"t-muscle-gross","lid":"c36","comp":79,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-roles-naming"},{"id":"L01013","dok":1,"q":"Which list contains only criteria used to name skeletal muscles?","a":"Direction of fibers, relative size, shape, and number of origins.","options":["Direction of fibers, nerve supply, blood supply, and color","Direction of fibers, relative size, shape, and number of origins","Action, location, contraction speed, and fiber color","Relative size, number of nuclei, shape, and striation pattern"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Skeletal muscles are named for features you can see or describe: the direction of their fibers, their relative size, their shape, their action, their number of origins, their location, and their origin and insertion. Most names combine two or more of these, such as rectus femoris, which gives direction and location. Learning the criteria lets the name tell you something about the muscle.","notes":["Direction of fibers is a real naming criterion, but nerve supply, blood supply, and color are not used to name muscles. Students pick this list when they mix up how muscles are described with how they are named.","Correct. Direction of fibers, relative size, shape, and number of origins are all naming criteria.","Action and location are real naming criteria, but contraction speed and fiber color describe fiber types inside a muscle. This list mixes two real criteria with two fiber characteristics.","Number of nuclei and striation pattern describe muscle tissue under the microscope, which is the same for every skeletal muscle. They help identify the tissue type, not name individual muscles."],"lt":"t-muscle-naming-mechanics","lid":"c1","comp":79,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-roles-naming"},{"id":"L01014","dok":1,"q":"In a muscle name, what does the direction term rectus tell you about the fascicles?","a":"They run parallel to the midline.","options":["They run perpendicular to the midline","They run parallel to the midline","They run in rings around an opening","They run diagonal to the midline"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Rectus means straight, and in a muscle name it tells you the fascicles run straight up and down, parallel to the midline of the body or to the long axis of a limb. The rectus abdominis, which runs vertically down the front of the abdomen, is a clear example. Direction terms describe how the fascicles run relative to the midline.","notes":["Fascicles running perpendicular to the midline are described as transversus, as in the transversus abdominis. Pick perpendicular for a question about that term.","Correct. Rectus means the fascicles run parallel to the midline.","Fascicles arranged in rings around an opening describe a circular muscle such as the orbicularis oris, which is named for its shape. Pick rings for a question about the term orbicularis.","Fascicles running diagonally are described as oblique, as in the external and internal oblique muscles. Pick diagonal for a question about the term oblique."],"lt":"t-muscle-naming-mechanics","lid":"c2","comp":79,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-roles-naming"},{"id":"L01015","dok":1,"q":"In a muscle name, what does the direction term oblique tell you about the fascicles?","a":"They run diagonal to the midline.","options":["They run parallel to the midline","They run perpendicular to the midline","They run in rings around an opening","They run diagonal to the midline"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Oblique means slanted, and in a muscle name it tells you the fascicles run at an angle, diagonal to the midline. The external and internal obliques of the abdominal wall are named for this slanted fiber direction. Comparing them with the rectus abdominis, whose fibers run straight, shows how direction terms work.","notes":["Fascicles running parallel to the midline are described as rectus, as in the rectus abdominis. Pick parallel for a question about the term rectus.","Fascicles running perpendicular to the midline are described as transversus, as in the transversus abdominis. Pick perpendicular for a question about that term.","Fascicles in rings around an opening describe a circular muscle such as the orbicularis oculi. Pick rings for a question about the term orbicularis.","Correct. Oblique means the fascicles run diagonal to the midline."],"lt":"t-muscle-naming-mechanics","lid":"c3","comp":79,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-roles-naming"},{"id":"L01016","dok":1,"q":"In muscle names, what do the size terms maximus and minimus mean?","a":"Maximus means largest, and minimus means smallest.","options":["Maximus means most superficial, and minimus means deepest","Maximus means strongest, and minimus means weakest","Maximus means longest, and minimus means shortest","Maximus means largest, and minimus means smallest"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Maximus and minimus describe relative size, meaning how big a muscle is compared with its neighbors. Maximus means largest and minimus means smallest, as in the gluteus maximus and gluteus minimus. The gluteus medius, named with medius, is the middle-sized muscle between them.","notes":["The gluteus maximus does happen to be superficial and the gluteus minimus deep, which makes this tempting, but the terms themselves describe size. Depth is described with words such as superficialis and profundus.","Size terms do not describe strength. A muscle's power depends on many things, including how its fascicles are arranged, so students who pick this are reading meaning into the name that it does not carry.","Longest and shortest are expressed by longus and brevis, as in the fibularis longus and fibularis brevis. Pick this pairing for a question about those terms.","Correct. Maximus means largest and minimus means smallest."],"lt":"t-muscle-naming-mechanics","lid":"c4","comp":79,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-roles-naming"},{"id":"L01017","dok":1,"q":"In muscle names, what do the size terms longus and brevis mean?","a":"Longus means long, and brevis means short.","options":["Longus means lateral, and brevis means medial","Longus means superficial, and brevis means deep","Longus means large, and brevis means small","Longus means long, and brevis means short"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Longus and brevis are relative size terms that describe length. Longus means long and brevis means short, so the fibularis longus is the longer of the two fibularis muscles and the fibularis brevis is the shorter one. The word brief comes from the same root as brevis.","notes":["Lateral and medial describe position relative to the midline, and they appear in names as lateralis and medialis, as in the vastus lateralis and vastus medialis. Pick this pairing for a question about those terms.","Depth is expressed by superficialis and profundus, as in the flexor digitorum superficialis and flexor digitorum profundus. Pick this pairing for a question about those terms.","Large and small are expressed by maximus and minimus, as in the gluteus maximus and minimus. Pick this pairing for a question about those terms.","Correct. Longus means long and brevis means short."],"lt":"t-muscle-naming-mechanics","lid":"c5","comp":79,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-roles-naming"},{"id":"L01018","dok":1,"q":"The shape term deltoid describes which shape?","a":"Triangular.","options":["Circular","Triangular","Trapezoid","Diamond shaped"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Deltoid means triangular, from the Greek letter delta, which is shaped like a triangle. The deltoid muscle caps the shoulder with a broad base along the clavicle and scapula and narrows to a point on the lateral humerus. Shape terms let a muscle name describe its outline.","notes":["Circular is described by orbicularis, as in the orbicularis oris around the mouth. Pick circular for a question about the term orbicularis.","Correct. Deltoid means triangular.","Trapezoid is the shape described by trapezius, the large muscle of the upper back. Pick trapezoid for a question about the trapezius.","Diamond shaped is described by rhomboid, as in the rhomboid major and minor between the scapulae. Pick diamond shaped for a question about the rhomboids."],"lt":"t-muscle-naming-mechanics","lid":"c6","comp":79,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-roles-naming"},{"id":"L01019","dok":1,"q":"The shape term orbicularis describes which shape?","a":"Circular.","options":["Saw-toothed","Triangular","Diamond shaped","Circular"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Orbicularis means circular, from the Latin word for a small circle or disc. The orbicularis oris surrounds the mouth and the orbicularis oculi surrounds the eye, and both have fascicles arranged in rings that close the opening like a sphincter. The name tells you the muscle encircles something.","notes":["Saw-toothed is described by serratus, as in the serratus anterior on the side of the chest. Pick saw-toothed for a question about the term serratus.","Triangular is described by deltoid. Pick it for a question about the muscle that caps the shoulder.","Diamond shaped is described by rhomboid. Pick it for a question about the rhomboid muscles between the scapulae.","Correct. Orbicularis means circular."],"lt":"t-muscle-naming-mechanics","lid":"c7","comp":79,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-roles-naming"},{"id":"L01020","dok":1,"q":"When a muscle is named for its action, what does the term flexor tell you?","a":"It decreases the angle of a joint.","options":["It moves a bone toward the midline","It increases the angle of a joint","It decreases the angle of a joint","It moves a bone away from the midline"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Flexion is a movement that decreases the angle between two bones at a joint, such as bending the elbow or the fingers. A muscle named flexor performs flexion. The flexor carpi radialis, for example, flexes the wrist.","notes":["Moving a bone toward the midline is adduction, done by muscles named adductor. Pick this for a question about the term adductor.","Increasing a joint angle is extension, done by muscles named extensor. Pick this for a question about the term extensor.","Correct. A flexor decreases a joint angle.","Moving a bone away from the midline is abduction, done by muscles named abductor. Pick this for a question about the term abductor."],"lt":"t-muscle-naming-mechanics","lid":"c8","comp":79,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-roles-naming"},{"id":"L01021","dok":1,"q":"When muscles are named for their action, which statement correctly describes an abductor and an adductor?","a":"An abductor moves a bone away from the midline, and an adductor moves it toward the midline.","options":["An abductor raises a body part, and an adductor lowers it","An abductor decreases a joint angle, and an adductor increases it","An abductor moves a bone away from the midline, and an adductor moves it toward the midline","An abductor moves a bone toward the midline, and an adductor moves it away from the midline"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Abduction moves a bone away from the midline of the body, and adduction moves it back toward the midline. A helpful memory link is that to abduct means to take away, while adduction adds the limb back to the body. Muscles named abductor and adductor perform these actions, such as the adductor muscles on the medial thigh.","notes":["Raising and lowering a body part are done by muscles named levator and depressor, such as the levator scapulae. Pick this pairing for a question about those terms.","Decreasing and increasing a joint angle are flexion and extension, performed by flexors and extensors. Pick this pairing for a question about those terms.","Correct. Abductors move a bone away from the midline, and adductors move it toward the midline.","This swaps the two terms. Abduction takes the limb away from the midline, and adduction brings it back."],"lt":"t-muscle-naming-mechanics","lid":"c9","comp":79,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-roles-naming"},{"id":"L01022","dok":1,"q":"The names biceps, triceps, and quadriceps are based on which naming criterion?","a":"Number of origins.","options":["Number of origins","Location","Shape","Relative size"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The ending ceps comes from the Latin word for head, so these names count how many heads, or origins, a muscle has. Biceps has two, triceps has three, and quadriceps has four. Each head starts from its own origin and they join to share a common insertion.","notes":["Correct. Biceps, triceps, and quadriceps name the number of origins.","Location is shown by terms that name a bone or region, such as temporalis or tibialis anterior. Pick it for a question about a muscle named for where it lies.","Shape is shown by terms such as deltoid, trapezius, and rhomboid. Pick it for a question about a muscle named for its outline.","Relative size is shown by terms such as maximus, minimus, longus, and brevis. Pick it for a question about the gluteus maximus or fibularis longus."],"lt":"t-muscle-naming-mechanics","lid":"c10","comp":79,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-roles-naming"},{"id":"L01023","dok":1,"q":"The muscle name temporalis is based on which naming criterion?","a":"Location.","options":["Location","Shape","Number of origins","Action"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The temporalis is named for its location, because it lies over the temporal bone on the side of the skull. Many muscles are named this way, such as the frontalis over the frontal bone and the tibialis anterior along the front of the tibia. The name points you to where the muscle sits.","notes":["Correct. Temporalis is named for its location over the temporal bone.","Shape names describe a muscle's outline, such as deltoid (triangular) or trapezius (trapezoid). Pick shape for a question about one of those muscles.","Number of origins is shown by names like biceps and triceps. Pick it for a question about how many heads a muscle has.","Action names describe the movement a muscle produces, such as flexor or adductor. Pick action for a question about a muscle like the adductor longus."],"lt":"t-muscle-naming-mechanics","lid":"c11","comp":79,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-roles-naming"},{"id":"L01024","dok":1,"q":"In a lever system in the body, what does the effort represent?","a":"The force applied to move the lever, supplied by a contracting muscle.","options":["The resistance being moved","The rigid bar that moves","The force applied to move the lever","The fixed pivot point"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"A lever has four parts: the lever itself, the fulcrum, the effort, and the load. The effort is the force applied to move the lever, and in the body it is the pull of a contracting muscle on a bone. The effort acts at the point where the muscle inserts on the bone.","notes":["The resistance being moved is the load, such as the weight of the forearm plus anything held in the hand. Pick it for a question about the load.","The rigid bar that moves is the lever itself, which in the body is a bone. Pick it for a question about what acts as the lever.","Correct. The effort is the force applied to move the lever, the pull of a muscle.","The fixed pivot point is the fulcrum, which in the body is a joint. Pick it for a question about the fulcrum."],"lt":"t-muscle-naming-mechanics","lid":"c12","comp":80,"competencyId":"w4-lever-systems"},{"id":"L01025","dok":1,"q":"In a lever system in the body, what does the load represent?","a":"The resistance moved by the lever, such as the weight of a body part plus anything it carries.","options":["The force applied by the contracting muscle","The rigid bar that moves","The fixed point the lever turns around","The resistance moved by the lever"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The load, also called the resistance, is what the lever moves or works against. In the body it is the weight of the body part being moved plus anything that part is carrying, such as a book held in the hand. The muscle's effort must overcome the load to produce movement.","notes":["The force applied by the contracting muscle is the effort. Pick it for a question about the effort.","The rigid bar that moves is the lever, which in the body is a bone. Pick it for a question about what acts as the lever.","The fixed point the lever turns around is the fulcrum, which in the body is a joint. Pick it for a question about the fulcrum.","Correct. The load is the resistance the lever moves."],"lt":"t-muscle-naming-mechanics","lid":"c13","comp":80,"competencyId":"w4-lever-systems"},{"id":"L01026","dok":1,"q":"In a body lever system, which structure acts as the fulcrum?","a":"A joint.","options":["A tendon","A joint","A muscle","A bone"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The fulcrum is the fixed point that a lever turns around. In the body, bones pivot at joints, so the joint acts as the fulcrum. For example, the elbow joint is the fulcrum when the biceps brachii lifts the forearm.","notes":["A tendon transmits the muscle's pull to the bone at the point where the effort is applied. It is part of how the effort reaches the lever, not the pivot point.","Correct. A joint acts as the fulcrum.","A contracting muscle supplies the effort, the force that moves the lever. Pick a muscle for a question about the source of the effort.","A bone is the rigid bar that moves, so it acts as the lever, not the fulcrum. Pick a bone for a question about what acts as the lever."],"lt":"t-muscle-naming-mechanics","lid":"c14","comp":80,"competencyId":"w4-lever-systems"},{"id":"L01027","dok":1,"q":"In a body lever system, which structure acts as the lever?","a":"A bone.","options":["A tendon","A bone","A joint","A ligament"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"A lever is a rigid bar that moves around a fixed point. In the body, bones are the rigid bars, so a bone acts as the lever. When a muscle pulls on a bone, the bone pivots at a joint and moves the body part.","notes":["A tendon carries the muscle's pull to the bone where the effort is applied. It delivers the effort to the lever rather than acting as the lever itself.","Correct. A bone acts as the lever.","A joint is the fixed point the bone pivots around, so it acts as the fulcrum. Pick a joint for a question about the fulcrum.","A ligament connects bone to bone and holds a joint together, but it is not the rigid bar that moves. Pick it for a question about what stabilizes a joint."],"lt":"t-muscle-naming-mechanics","lid":"c15","comp":80,"competencyId":"w4-lever-systems"},{"id":"L01028","dok":1,"q":"In a first-class lever, how are the parts arranged along the lever?","a":"The fulcrum lies between the effort and the load.","options":["The effort lies between the fulcrum and the load","The load lies between the fulcrum and the effort","The effort and the load act at the fulcrum itself","The fulcrum lies between the effort and the load"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Lever classes are sorted by which part sits in the middle. In a first-class lever the fulcrum is in the middle, with the effort on one side and the load on the other, like a seesaw. In the body, nodding the head works this way, with the atlanto-occipital joint between the posterior neck muscles and the weight of the face.","notes":["An effort in the middle describes a third-class lever, like tweezers or flexing the elbow. Pick this arrangement for a third-class lever question.","A load in the middle describes a second-class lever, like a wheelbarrow or standing on tiptoe. Pick this arrangement for a second-class lever question.","If the effort and load acted right at the fulcrum, the lever could not turn, because force at the pivot point produces no rotation. Students choose this when they are unsure what the fulcrum does.","Correct. In a first-class lever the fulcrum is in the middle."],"lt":"t-muscle-naming-mechanics","lid":"c16","comp":80,"competencyId":"w4-lever-systems"},{"id":"L01029","dok":1,"q":"In a second-class lever, how are the parts arranged along the lever?","a":"The load lies between the fulcrum and the effort.","options":["The effort lies between the fulcrum and the load","The load lies between the fulcrum and the effort","The effort and the load act at the fulcrum itself","The fulcrum lies between the effort and the load"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"In a second-class lever the load sits in the middle, with the fulcrum at one end and the effort at the other, like a wheelbarrow. In the body, standing on tiptoe is the classic example: the ball of the foot is the fulcrum, the body's weight is the load, and the calf muscles pull up on the heel as the effort.","notes":["An effort in the middle describes a third-class lever, like tweezers or flexing the elbow. Pick this arrangement for a third-class lever question.","Correct. In a second-class lever the load is in the middle.","Force applied right at the pivot point cannot turn a lever. Students choose this when they are unsure what the fulcrum does.","A fulcrum in the middle describes a first-class lever, like a seesaw or nodding the head. Pick this arrangement for a first-class lever question."],"lt":"t-muscle-naming-mechanics","lid":"c17","comp":80,"competencyId":"w4-lever-systems"},{"id":"L01030","dok":1,"q":"In a third-class lever, how are the parts arranged along the lever?","a":"The effort lies between the fulcrum and the load.","options":["The effort lies between the fulcrum and the load","The fulcrum lies between the effort and the load","The effort and the load act at the fulcrum itself","The load lies between the fulcrum and the effort"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"In a third-class lever the effort sits in the middle, between the fulcrum and the load, like a pair of tweezers. This is the most common lever in the body. Flexing the elbow is the classic example: the elbow joint is the fulcrum, the biceps brachii pulls on the forearm just below the elbow, and the load is the weight held in the hand.","notes":["Correct. In a third-class lever the effort is in the middle.","A fulcrum in the middle describes a first-class lever, like a seesaw or nodding the head. Pick this arrangement for a first-class lever question.","Force applied right at the pivot point cannot turn a lever. Students choose this when they are unsure what the fulcrum does.","A load in the middle describes a second-class lever, like a wheelbarrow or standing on tiptoe. Pick this arrangement for a second-class lever question."],"lt":"t-muscle-naming-mechanics","lid":"c18","comp":80,"competencyId":"w4-lever-systems"},{"id":"L01031","dok":1,"q":"Which muscle role is the prime mover, the muscle chiefly responsible for producing a movement?","a":"The agonist.","options":["The agonist","The synergist","The antagonist","The fixator"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The agonist, also called the prime mover, is the muscle that contracts to produce a given movement and does most of the work. For example, the biceps brachii is the agonist during elbow flexion. Other muscles help or oppose it, but the agonist is the one chiefly responsible for the action.","notes":["Correct. The agonist is the prime mover.","A synergist assists the prime mover by adding force or preventing unwanted movement, but it is not the main muscle doing the job. Pick it for the brachialis helping the biceps brachii flex the elbow.","The antagonist opposes the prime mover, relaxing and stretching as the agonist contracts. Pick it for the triceps brachii during elbow flexion.","A fixator holds the origin bone steady so the prime mover can work efficiently. Pick it for muscles steadying the scapula during arm movements."],"lt":"t-muscle-naming-mechanics","lid":"c19","comp":79,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-roles-naming"},{"id":"L01032","dok":1,"q":"Which muscle role opposes the prime mover, relaxing and stretching as the prime mover contracts?","a":"The antagonist.","options":["The agonist","The synergist","The antagonist","The fixator"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The antagonist sits on the opposite side of the joint from the prime mover and produces the opposite action. When the prime mover contracts, the antagonist relaxes and lengthens so the movement can happen, and it can also slow or reverse the movement. The triceps brachii is the antagonist when the biceps brachii flexes the elbow.","notes":["The agonist is the prime mover itself, the muscle doing the contracting. Pick it for the biceps brachii during elbow flexion.","A synergist works with the prime mover rather than against it, adding force or preventing unwanted movement. Pick it for the brachialis assisting elbow flexion.","Correct. The antagonist opposes and yields to the prime mover.","A fixator holds the origin bone steady so the prime mover acts efficiently. Pick it for muscles stabilizing the scapula during arm movements."],"lt":"t-muscle-naming-mechanics","lid":"c20","comp":79,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-roles-naming"},{"id":"L01033","dok":1,"q":"Which muscle role assists the prime mover by adding force to the same movement?","a":"The synergist","options":["The agonist","The synergist","The fixator","The antagonist"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"A synergist works together with the prime mover. It can add its own pull to the same movement, or it can prevent unwanted movements that would waste the prime mover's effort. A fixator is a special kind of synergist whose job is to hold the origin bone steady rather than add force.","notes":["The agonist is the prime mover itself, the muscle chiefly responsible for the movement. Pick it if the question asked which muscle does most of the work.","Correct. A muscle that adds force to the prime mover's action is a synergist.","A fixator is a synergist that holds the origin bone steady rather than adding force to the movement. Pick it for muscles steadying the scapula during arm movements.","The antagonist opposes the movement, relaxing and lengthening as the prime mover contracts, as the triceps brachii does during elbow flexion. Pick it if the question asked which muscle works against the movement."],"lt":"t-muscle-naming-mechanics","lid":"c21","comp":79,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-roles-naming"},{"id":"L01034","dok":1,"q":"Some synergists have a special job: they hold the origin bone steady so the prime mover can act efficiently on the insertion. What are these muscles called?","a":"Fixators.","options":["Agonists","Flexors","Fixators","Antagonists"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"A fixator is a synergist that stabilizes the origin of the prime mover. If the origin bone wobbled, part of the prime mover's pull would be wasted moving it, so fixators hold it firm. Muscles that steady the scapula while the arm moves are a common example.","notes":["Agonists are the prime movers themselves, the muscles that produce the movement. Pick agonists for a question about the muscle chiefly responsible for an action.","Flexor is an action term in a muscle's name, meaning it decreases a joint angle. It describes a movement, not the stabilizing role in this question.","Correct. Muscles that hold the origin bone steady for the prime mover are fixators.","Antagonists oppose the prime mover and stretch as it contracts. Pick antagonists for a question about the muscle on the opposite side of the joint."],"lt":"t-muscle-naming-mechanics","lid":"c22","comp":79,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-roles-naming"},{"id":"L01035","dok":1,"q":"What is a muscle compartment?","a":"A group of muscles, with their vessels and nerves, that share a common function.","options":["A bundle of muscle fibers wrapped in perimysium","A single muscle wrapped in its epimysium","A group of muscles with a shared function, vessels, and nerves","A pair of muscles with opposite actions at a joint"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"A compartment is a group of skeletal muscles, along with the blood vessels and nerves that serve them, bound together by deep fascia and sharing a common function. The limbs are divided into compartments such as the anterior flexor compartment and posterior extensor compartment of the forearm. Knowing a muscle's compartment tells you a lot about its likely action.","notes":["A bundle of muscle fibers wrapped in perimysium is a fascicle, which is a part of one muscle. Pick it for a question about what the perimysium surrounds.","A single muscle wrapped in epimysium is one whole muscle organ, not a group. Pick it for a question about the outermost sheath.","Correct. A compartment is a functional group of muscles with their vessels and nerves.","A pair of muscles producing opposite actions describes an agonist and its antagonist, which usually sit in opposite compartments. Pick this idea for a question about opposing muscle pairs."],"lt":"t-muscle-naming-mechanics","lid":"c23","comp":86,"competencyId":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments"},{"id":"L01036","dok":2,"q":"In the name gluteus maximus, which naming criterion does the word maximus use?","a":"Relative size.","options":["Action","Shape","Relative size","Direction of fibers"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Maximus means largest, so it describes the muscle's relative size compared with its neighbors. The gluteus maximus is the largest of the three gluteal muscles, with the gluteus medius and gluteus minimus being smaller. The other word, gluteus, names the location in the buttock region.","notes":["Action is described by terms such as flexor, extensor, and adductor. Pick action for a muscle like the adductor longus.","Shape is described by terms such as deltoid, trapezius, and rhomboid. Pick shape for a question about one of those names.","Correct. Maximus describes relative size.","Direction of fibers is described by terms such as rectus, oblique, and transversus. Pick direction for a muscle like the rectus abdominis."],"lt":"t-muscle-naming-mechanics","lid":"c24","comp":79,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-roles-naming"},{"id":"L01037","dok":2,"q":"Which naming criterion does the name sternocleidomastoid use?","a":"Origin and insertion.","options":["Shape","Action","Direction of fibers","Origin and insertion"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Sternocleidomastoid is built from the names of the muscle's attachments. Sterno refers to the sternum and cleido to the clavicle, where the muscle originates, and mastoid refers to the mastoid process of the temporal bone, where it inserts. Reading the name tells you exactly where the muscle runs on the side of the neck.","notes":["Shape names describe a muscle's outline, such as deltoid or trapezius. Nothing in sternocleidomastoid describes a shape.","Action names describe the movement a muscle produces, such as flexor or levator. Sternocleidomastoid names bones, not a movement.","Direction names describe how fibers run, such as rectus or oblique. Pick direction for a question about the rectus abdominis or external oblique.","Correct. Sternocleidomastoid names its origin and insertion."],"lt":"t-muscle-naming-mechanics","lid":"c25","comp":79,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-roles-naming"},{"id":"L01038","dok":2,"q":"In the name rectus abdominis, what does each word tell you?","a":"Rectus gives the fiber direction, and abdominis gives the location.","options":["Rectus gives the fiber direction, and abdominis gives the location","Rectus gives the shape, and abdominis gives the action","Rectus gives the relative size, and abdominis gives the number of origins","Rectus gives the location, and abdominis gives the fiber direction"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Rectus means straight, so it tells you the fibers run parallel to the midline. Abdominis tells you the muscle is located in the abdomen. Put together, the name describes a muscle with straight vertical fibers on the front of the abdomen.","notes":["Correct. Rectus is direction and abdominis is location.","Rectus describes the direction of fibers, not an outline, and abdominis names a region, not a movement. Pick shape for a name like deltoid and action for a name like flexor.","Size is described by terms such as maximus or longus, and number of origins by terms such as biceps. Neither word in rectus abdominis carries that information.","This swaps the two words. Rectus is the direction term and abdominis is the location term."],"lt":"t-muscle-naming-mechanics","lid":"c26","comp":79,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-roles-naming"},{"id":"L01039","dok":2,"q":"The term transversus in a muscle name tells you the fascicles run in which direction?","a":"Perpendicular to the midline.","options":["Perpendicular to the midline","Parallel to the midline","In rings around an opening","Diagonal to the midline"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Transversus means across, so its fascicles run horizontally, at a right angle to the midline. The transversus abdominis, the deepest of the flat abdominal muscles, wraps around the abdomen like a belt with fibers running side to side. Direction terms always describe the fibers relative to the midline.","notes":["Correct. Transversus means the fascicles run perpendicular to the midline.","Fibers parallel to the midline are described as rectus, as in the rectus abdominis. Pick parallel for a question about the term rectus.","Fibers in rings around an opening belong to circular muscles such as the orbicularis oris. Pick rings for a question about the term orbicularis.","Fibers running diagonally are described as oblique, as in the external and internal obliques. Pick diagonal for a question about the term oblique."],"lt":"t-muscle-naming-mechanics","lid":"c27","comp":79,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-roles-naming"},{"id":"L01040","dok":2,"q":"The shape term serratus describes what appearance?","a":"Saw-toothed.","options":["Slender","Trapezoid","Square","Saw-toothed"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Serratus means saw-toothed, from the Latin word for a saw. The serratus anterior on the side of the chest attaches to the ribs by a row of finger-like slips, which gives its edge a jagged, saw-like look. The name describes that outline.","notes":["Slender is described by gracilis, as in the gracilis of the medial thigh. Pick slender for a question about the gracilis.","Trapezoid is described by trapezius, the large muscle of the upper back. Pick trapezoid for a question about the trapezius.","Square or four-sided is described by quadratus, as in the quadratus lumborum. Pick square for a question about that term.","Correct. Serratus means saw-toothed."],"lt":"t-muscle-naming-mechanics","lid":"c28","comp":79,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-roles-naming"},{"id":"L01041","dok":2,"q":"When you stand on tiptoe, the ball of the foot is the fulcrum, the body's weight is the load, and the calf muscles pull up on the heel as the effort. Which lever class is this?","a":"A second-class lever.","options":["A third-class lever","A second-class lever","A fourth-class lever","A first-class lever"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"To classify a lever, find which part is in the middle. On tiptoe, the fulcrum is at the ball of the foot in front, the effort is at the heel in back, and the body's weight passes down through the ankle in between. With the load in the middle, this is a second-class lever, like a wheelbarrow.","notes":["A third-class lever has the effort in the middle, like tweezers. Pick it for flexing the elbow, where the biceps brachii pulls between the elbow and the hand.","Correct. With the load between the fulcrum and the effort, standing on tiptoe is a second-class lever.","There is no fourth class of lever. Only three arrangements are possible, depending on whether the fulcrum, the load, or the effort sits in the middle.","A first-class lever has the fulcrum in the middle, like a seesaw. Pick it for nodding the head, where the joint sits between the neck muscles and the face."],"lt":"t-muscle-naming-mechanics","lid":"c29","comp":80,"competencyId":"w4-lever-systems"},{"id":"L01042","dok":2,"q":"Nodding the head uses the atlanto-occipital joint, the posterior neck muscles, and the weight of the face. Which lever class is this?","a":"A first-class lever.","options":["A first-class lever","A third-class lever","It is not a lever system","A second-class lever"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"In head nodding, the atlanto-occipital joint between the skull and the atlas is the fulcrum. The weight of the face sits in front of the joint as the load, and the posterior neck muscles pull from behind as the effort. With the fulcrum in the middle, this is a first-class lever, like a seesaw.","notes":["Correct. The joint sits between the effort and the load, making it a first-class lever.","A third-class lever has the effort in the middle. Pick it for flexing the elbow.","Any time a muscle pulls a bone around a joint, a lever system is at work. Students choose this when they think levers apply only to the limbs.","A second-class lever has the load in the middle. Pick it for standing on tiptoe."],"lt":"t-muscle-naming-mechanics","lid":"c30","comp":80,"competencyId":"w4-lever-systems"},{"id":"L01043","dok":2,"q":"When the biceps brachii flexes the elbow to lift a weight in the hand, which lever class is at work?","a":"A third-class lever.","options":["A fourth-class lever","A second-class lever","A first-class lever","A third-class lever"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"In elbow flexion, the elbow joint is the fulcrum, the weight in the hand is the load at the far end, and the biceps brachii pulls on the radius just below the elbow as the effort. With the effort in the middle, this is a third-class lever. Third-class levers are the most common in the body.","notes":["There is no fourth class of lever. Only three arrangements are possible, depending on which part sits in the middle.","A second-class lever has the load in the middle. Pick it for standing on tiptoe.","A first-class lever has the fulcrum in the middle. Pick it for nodding the head.","Correct. With the effort between the fulcrum and the load, elbow flexion is a third-class lever."],"lt":"t-muscle-naming-mechanics","lid":"c31","comp":80,"competencyId":"w4-lever-systems"},{"id":"L01044","dok":2,"q":"During elbow flexion, the biceps brachii contracts while the triceps brachii relaxes and stretches. What role is the triceps brachii playing?","a":"Antagonist.","options":["Antagonist","Fixator","Synergist","Agonist"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The triceps brachii lies on the opposite side of the elbow from the biceps brachii and produces the opposite action, elbow extension. During flexion it relaxes and lengthens to allow the movement, which is the job of an antagonist. If the movement reversed to extension, the triceps would become the agonist.","notes":["Correct. The triceps brachii is the antagonist during elbow flexion.","A fixator holds the origin bone steady for the prime mover. Pick it for muscles steadying the scapula during arm movements.","A synergist helps the prime mover, as the brachialis does in elbow flexion. The triceps works against flexion, not with it.","The agonist in elbow flexion is the biceps brachii, the muscle doing the contracting. The triceps would be the agonist only during elbow extension."],"lt":"t-muscle-naming-mechanics","lid":"c32","comp":79,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-roles-naming"},{"id":"L01045","dok":2,"q":"The biceps brachii lifts a book held in the hand. Which statement correctly identifies the parts of this lever?","a":"The fulcrum is the elbow joint, the effort is the biceps pulling on the radius, and the load is the forearm plus the book.","options":["The fulcrum is the shoulder joint, the effort is the triceps brachii, and the load is the forearm","The fulcrum is the elbow joint, the effort is the biceps pulling on the radius, and the load is the forearm plus the book","The fulcrum is the biceps brachii, the effort is the elbow joint, and the load is the book","The fulcrum is the hand, the effort is the book, and the load is the biceps brachii"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"In a body lever, a joint is the fulcrum, a muscle's pull is the effort, and the weight being moved is the load. Here the forearm pivots at the elbow joint, the biceps brachii pulls on the radius to supply the effort, and the load is the weight of the forearm plus the book. Because the effort lies between the fulcrum and the load, this is a third-class lever.","notes":["The forearm pivots at the elbow, not the shoulder, and the triceps brachii is the antagonist during this movement rather than the source of the effort.","Correct. The elbow is the fulcrum, the biceps pull is the effort, and the forearm plus book is the load.","This swaps the fulcrum and the effort. A muscle supplies the force, and a joint is the pivot point.","This scrambles all three parts. The book is part of the load being lifted, and the biceps brachii supplies the effort."],"lt":"t-muscle-naming-mechanics","lid":"c33","comp":80,"competencyId":"w4-lever-systems"},{"id":"L01046","dok":2,"q":"Whether a lever works at a mechanical advantage or a mechanical disadvantage depends on what?","a":"How far the effort and the load each act from the fulcrum.","options":["The length of the muscle's tendon","The size of the muscle supplying the effort","How far the effort and the load each act from the fulcrum","The number of origins the muscle has"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Mechanical advantage comes from comparing distances. When the effort acts farther from the fulcrum than the load, a small effort can move a large load, which is a mechanical advantage. When the effort acts closer to the fulcrum than the load, a larger effort is needed, which is a mechanical disadvantage, but the load moves faster and farther.","notes":["Tendon length does not decide advantage. What matters is where the effort is applied relative to the fulcrum and the load, which depends on where the tendon inserts, not how long it is.","A larger muscle can pull harder, but that changes how much force is available, not whether the lever arrangement gives an advantage. Students pick this when they mix up strength with leverage.","Correct. Advantage or disadvantage depends on the distances of the effort and load from the fulcrum.","The number of origins is a naming criterion, as in biceps and triceps, and has nothing to do with leverage. Pick it for a question about muscle names."],"lt":"t-muscle-naming-mechanics","lid":"c34","comp":80,"competencyId":"w4-lever-systems"},{"id":"L01047","dok":2,"q":"The forearm muscles are grouped into two main compartments by position and function. Which pair names them correctly?","a":"An anterior flexor compartment and a posterior extensor compartment.","options":["An anterior extensor compartment and a posterior flexor compartment","A medial adductor compartment and a lateral abductor compartment","A superficial flexor layer and a deep flexor layer","An anterior flexor compartment and a posterior extensor compartment"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The forearm is divided into an anterior compartment, whose muscles mainly flex the wrist and fingers, and a posterior compartment, whose muscles mainly extend them. Each compartment holds its own muscles, vessels, and nerves wrapped by deep fascia. Knowing which compartment a muscle sits in tells you its likely action.","notes":["This swaps the two compartments. The flexors sit on the anterior side of the forearm and the extensors on the posterior side.","A medial adductor compartment exists in the thigh, where the adductor muscles pull the thigh toward the midline. Pick it for a question about the medial thigh.","Superficial and deep layers are subdivisions inside the anterior flexor compartment, not the two main compartments. Pick these layers for a question about how the anterior forearm muscles are stacked.","Correct. The forearm has an anterior flexor compartment and a posterior extensor compartment."],"lt":"t-muscle-naming-mechanics","lid":"c35","comp":86,"competencyId":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments"},{"id":"L01048","dok":2,"q":"A muscle is named flexor carpi radialis. Reading only the name, which three kinds of information does it give, in order?","a":"The action, the region, and the position.","options":["Origin, insertion, and action","Shape, number of origins, and size","Action, region, and position","Direction of fibers, shape, and size"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Each word in flexor carpi radialis carries a clue. Flexor is the action, meaning it decreases a joint angle; carpi is the region, the wrist; and radialis is the position, the radial (thumb) side of the forearm. Before you ever see the muscle, the name tells you it flexes the wrist from the radial side.","notes":["Flexor is an action, but carpi and radialis name a region and a side rather than specific origin and insertion points. Pick origin and insertion for a name like sternocleidomastoid.","None of the three words describes a shape, a count of heads, or a size. Those would be terms such as deltoid, biceps, or longus.","Correct. Flexor is the action, carpi the region, and radialis the position.","Direction, shape, and size terms such as rectus, deltoid, and maximus do not appear in this name. Students choose this when they recognize a muscle name but not the meaning of its parts."],"lt":"t-muscle-naming-mechanics","lid":"c36","comp":79,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-roles-naming"},{"id":"L01049","dok":3,"q":"It is easier to crack a hard nut with the back teeth than with the front teeth. Using lever principles, which explanation is correct?","a":"The back teeth are closer to the jaw joint, which is the fulcrum, so less effort is needed to move the same load.","options":["Biting with the back teeth moves the fulcrum forward","The back teeth sit farther from the fulcrum, giving more leverage","The back teeth sit closer to the fulcrum, so less effort is needed","The back teeth are harder, so less muscle force is needed"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"When you bite, the jaw joint is the fulcrum, the jaw-closing muscles supply the effort, and the nut is the load. The back teeth sit much closer to the jaw joint than the front teeth, so the load acts over a shorter distance from the fulcrum. A load closer to the fulcrum needs less effort to move, which is why the back teeth crack hard foods more easily.","notes":["The fulcrum is always the jaw joint, no matter which teeth do the biting. Only the position of the load changes.","The back teeth are closer to the jaw joint, not farther from it. A load placed farther from the fulcrum would take more effort, which is why biting with the front teeth is harder.","Correct. A load closer to the fulcrum requires less effort.","Tooth hardness is not the reason; the explanation is about where the load sits along the lever. Students choose this when they look for a reason in the teeth rather than in the lever."],"lt":"t-muscle-naming-mechanics","lid":"c37","comp":80,"competencyId":"w4-lever-systems"},{"id":"L01050","dok":3,"q":"The biceps brachii is the prime mover during elbow flexion but acts as an antagonist during elbow extension. What does this show about muscle roles?","a":"A muscle's role depends on the movement, so the same muscle can play different roles in different actions.","options":["Each muscle has one fixed role for life","Only flexor muscles can be prime movers","Its role depends on which movement is happening","A muscle changes roles only after an injury"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Agonist, antagonist, synergist, and fixator are job titles for a specific movement, not permanent labels. The biceps brachii is the prime mover when the elbow bends, but when the triceps brachii straightens the elbow, the biceps relaxes and stretches as the antagonist. The same muscle can even act as a synergist or fixator in other movements.","notes":["Muscle roles are assigned movement by movement, so they change whenever the movement changes. The biceps example in the question shows one muscle in two different roles.","Extensors, abductors, and other muscles serve as prime movers for their own actions. The triceps brachii, for example, is the prime mover during elbow extension.","Correct. A muscle's role depends on the movement being performed.","Roles change with normal movement, not with injury. Every time you bend and straighten your arm, the biceps and triceps trade roles."],"lt":"t-muscle-naming-mechanics","lid":"c38","comp":79,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-roles-naming"},{"id":"L01051","dok":2,"q":"When the deltoid abducts the arm, muscles attached to the scapula contract to hold the scapula steady so the deltoid can pull efficiently on the humerus. What role are the scapular muscles playing?","a":"Fixator.","options":["Fixator","Agonist","Abductor","Antagonist"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The deltoid originates on the scapula and clavicle and inserts on the humerus. If the scapula were free to slide around, some of the deltoid's pull would be wasted moving it. Muscles that hold the origin bone steady so the prime mover can act efficiently are fixators.","notes":["Correct. Muscles that steady the origin bone of the prime mover are fixators.","The agonist in this movement is the deltoid, the muscle producing abduction. The scapular muscles are not moving the arm; they are holding the origin bone still.","Abductor is an action term that describes the deltoid's job of moving the arm away from the body. The scapular muscles are stabilizing the scapula, not abducting the arm.","An antagonist would oppose abduction by pulling the arm back toward the body, and it relaxes during the movement. The scapular muscles here are contracting to stabilize, not opposing the action."],"lt":"t-muscle-naming-mechanics","lid":"c39","comp":79,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-roles-naming"},{"id":"L01052","dok":2,"q":"A muscle is named biceps femoris. Which statement correctly breaks the name into its two criteria?","a":"Biceps gives the number of origins, and femoris gives the location.","options":["Biceps gives the relative size, and femoris gives the action","Biceps gives the number of origins, and femoris gives the location","Biceps gives the number of origins, and femoris gives the fiber direction","Biceps gives the shape, and femoris gives the origin and insertion"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Biceps means two heads, so it tells you the number of origins. Femoris refers to the femur, so it tells you the muscle's location in the thigh. Together the name describes a two-headed muscle of the thigh, which is one of the hamstrings on the posterior thigh.","notes":["Biceps counts heads rather than describing size, and femoris names a region rather than a movement. Size would be a term like maximus, and action a term like flexor.","Correct. Biceps is the number of origins and femoris is the location.","Biceps is correctly the number of origins, but femoris names the thigh region rather than how the fibers run. Direction would be a term like rectus or oblique.","Biceps does not describe a shape, and femoris names a region, not two attachment points. Pick origin and insertion for a name like sternocleidomastoid."],"lt":"t-muscle-naming-mechanics","lid":"c40","comp":79,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-roles-naming"},{"id":"L01053","dok":2,"q":"In a lever, the effort acts farther from the fulcrum than the load does, so a small effort can move a large load. What is this condition called?","a":"Mechanical advantage.","options":["Mechanical disadvantage","Increased range of motion","A third-class lever","Mechanical advantage"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"A lever works at a mechanical advantage when the effort is applied farther from the fulcrum than the load. The longer distance lets a small effort balance or move a large load. The trade-off is that the load moves only a short distance and fairly slowly.","notes":["Mechanical disadvantage is the opposite case, when the effort acts closer to the fulcrum than the load, so a large effort is needed. Pick it for most limb movements, such as elbow flexion.","Increased range of motion is the benefit of a mechanical disadvantage, where the load moves fast and far. It is the opposite of what this arrangement provides.","A third-class lever places the effort between the fulcrum and the load, so the effort is always closer to the fulcrum and the lever works at a mechanical disadvantage. Pick third-class for a question about elbow flexion.","Correct. When the effort is farther from the fulcrum than the load, the lever works at a mechanical advantage."],"lt":"t-muscle-naming-mechanics","lid":"c41","comp":80,"competencyId":"w4-lever-systems"},{"id":"L01054","dok":2,"q":"Match these three movements to their lever classes: nodding the head, standing on tiptoe, and flexing the elbow.","a":"Nodding is first class, tiptoe is second class, and elbow flexion is third class.","options":["Nodding is first class, tiptoe is third class, and elbow flexion is second class","Nodding is third class, tiptoe is second class, and elbow flexion is first class","Nodding is second class, tiptoe is first class, and elbow flexion is third class","Nodding is first class, tiptoe is second class, and elbow flexion is third class"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Each class is defined by the part in the middle. In nodding, the joint (fulcrum) lies between the face and the neck muscles, so it is first class. On tiptoe, the body's weight (load) lies between the ball of the foot and the calf's pull, so it is second class. In elbow flexion, the biceps pull (effort) lies between the elbow and the hand, so it is third class.","notes":["Nodding is correctly first class, but this swaps tiptoe and elbow flexion. On tiptoe the load is in the middle, and in elbow flexion the effort is in the middle.","This swaps nodding and elbow flexion. Nodding has the fulcrum in the middle, and elbow flexion has the effort in the middle.","This swaps nodding and tiptoe. Nodding has the fulcrum in the middle, which is first class, and tiptoe has the load in the middle, which is second class.","Correct. Nodding is first class, tiptoe is second class, and elbow flexion is third class."],"lt":"t-muscle-naming-mechanics","lid":"c42","comp":80,"competencyId":"w4-lever-systems"},{"id":"L01055","dok":2,"q":"A group of anterior forearm muscles is wrapped together by deep fascia, shares its blood vessels and nerves, and has the common function of flexion. What is this grouping called?","a":"A compartment.","options":["A fascicle","An aponeurosis","A retinaculum","A compartment"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"A compartment is a group of muscles, together with their blood vessels and nerves, bound by deep fascia and sharing a common function. The anterior forearm muscles form the flexor compartment, and the posterior forearm muscles form the extensor compartment. Grouping by compartment is how the limb muscles are organized in lab.","notes":["A fascicle is a bundle of muscle fibers inside a single muscle, wrapped in perimysium. Pick it for a question about what the perimysium surrounds.","An aponeurosis is a broad, flat sheet that attaches a wide muscle, not a group of muscles. Pick it for a question about the attachment of a flat abdominal muscle.","A retinaculum is a band of connective tissue that holds tendons in place where they cross the wrist or ankle. Pick it for a question about what keeps the forearm tendons from bowing out at the wrist.","Correct. A functional group of muscles with shared vessels and nerves is a compartment."],"lt":"t-muscle-naming-mechanics","lid":"c43","comp":86,"competencyId":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments"},{"id":"L01233","dok":1,"q":"What is the attachment of a muscle to the more stationary bone called?","a":"The origin.","options":["The belly","The action","The origin","The insertion"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"A skeletal muscle crosses at least one joint and attaches to bone at both ends. The origin is the attachment to the more stationary bone, usually the proximal one, and the insertion is the attachment to the more movable bone, usually the distal one. When the muscle contracts, the insertion is pulled toward the origin.","notes":["The belly is the thick, fleshy middle region of the muscle, between its attachments. It would be the answer for the part that bulges when the muscle contracts.","The action is the movement the muscle produces, not an attachment. Pick it for what happens when the muscle contracts.","Correct. The origin is the attachment that stays put during the action.","The insertion is the attachment to the more movable bone, the one pulled toward the origin. Pick it for the bone that moves."],"lt":"t-muscle-structure","lid":"c2","comp":79,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-roles-naming"},{"id":"L01273","dok":1,"q":"In a lever system, what is the fixed point that the lever turns around called?","a":"The fulcrum.","options":["The fulcrum","The lever","The load","The effort"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Every lever has four parts: the lever itself, the fulcrum, the effort, and the load. The fulcrum is the fixed pivot point the lever rotates around. In the body, a joint serves as the fulcrum, a bone serves as the lever, a contracting muscle supplies the effort, and the body part being moved is the load.","notes":["Correct. The fulcrum is the pivot, and in the body that pivot is a joint.","The lever is the rigid bar that moves around the fulcrum. In the body a bone plays this role, so it fits a question about the rigid bar rather than the pivot.","The load is the resistance the lever moves, such as the weight of the forearm and anything held in the hand. Pick it when the question asks what is being moved.","The effort is the force applied to move the lever. In the body it is the pull of a contracting muscle, so choose it when the question asks what supplies the force."],"lt":"t-muscle-fascicles","lid":"c6","comp":80,"competencyId":"w4-lever-systems"},{"id":"L01274","dok":1,"q":"In a body lever system, what serves as the effort that moves the lever?","a":"The pull of a contracting muscle.","options":["The joint where the bones meet","The bone that the muscle pulls on","The weight of the body part being moved","The pull of a contracting muscle"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The effort is the force that moves a lever. In the body, that force comes from a skeletal muscle that contracts and pulls on its insertion. The muscle pull is applied to a bone (the lever), which pivots at a joint (the fulcrum) to move a body part (the load).","notes":["The joint is the fulcrum, the fixed point the bone pivots around. Choose it for a question about the pivot point.","The bone is the lever, the rigid bar that moves. It would be the answer if the question asked which structure acts as the bar.","The weight of the body part, plus anything it carries, is the load. It is the answer when the question asks what resistance the lever moves.","Correct. Muscle contraction supplies the force, so it is the effort."],"lt":"t-muscle-fascicles","lid":"c7","comp":80,"competencyId":"w4-lever-systems"},{"id":"L01275","dok":1,"q":"In a body lever system, which structure acts as the rigid bar that moves around a fixed point?","a":"A bone.","options":["A muscle","A tendon","A joint","A bone"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"A lever is a rigid bar that rotates around a fixed point. In the body, bones are the rigid bars, joints are the fixed points, and muscles supply the pulling force. When the biceps brachii flexes the elbow, for example, the forearm bones act as the lever pivoting at the elbow joint.","notes":["A muscle supplies the effort, the pulling force that moves the lever. Pick it if the question asked what provides the force.","A tendon is the cord of dense connective tissue that transmits the muscle pull to the bone. It carries the effort but is flexible, so it is not the rigid bar.","A joint is the fulcrum, the fixed pivot point. It fits a question about where the lever turns.","Correct. Bones are rigid enough to act as the lever bar."],"lt":"t-muscle-fascicles","lid":"c8","comp":80,"competencyId":"w4-lever-systems"},{"id":"L01276","dok":1,"q":"In a first-class lever, where does the fulcrum lie?","a":"Between the effort and the load.","options":["At one end, with the effort between it and the load","Between the effort and the load","At one end, with the load between it and the effort","At the point where the muscle attaches to the bone"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Levers are classified by which part sits in the middle. In a first-class lever, the fulcrum sits in the middle, with the effort on one side and the load on the other, like a seesaw. Nodding the head is the body example: the atlanto-occipital joint is the fulcrum between the posterior neck muscles and the weight of the face.","notes":["This arrangement puts the effort in the middle, which is a third-class lever, like tweezers. It is the answer for flexing the elbow.","Correct. A fulcrum in the middle, like a seesaw, defines a first-class lever.","This arrangement puts the load in the middle, which is a second-class lever, like a wheelbarrow. Choose it for standing on tiptoe.","The point where a muscle attaches is where the effort is applied, not the fulcrum. Students who pick this are mixing up the insertion with the pivot, which is always the joint."],"lt":"t-muscle-fascicles","lid":"c9","comp":80,"competencyId":"w4-lever-systems"},{"id":"L01277","dok":1,"q":"Which lever class is the most common in the body, and what lies in the middle of it?","a":"Third class, with the effort in the middle.","options":["Third class, with the load in the middle","First class, with the fulcrum in the middle","Second class, with the load in the middle","Third class, with the effort in the middle"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"In a third-class lever the effort lies between the fulcrum and the load, like a pair of tweezers. Most skeletal muscles insert close to the joint they move, which places their pull between the joint and the load. That makes the third-class lever the most common lever in the body, with flexing the elbow as the standard example.","notes":["Third class is the right class, but the load does not sit in the middle. A load in the middle describes a second-class lever, so this mixes the two definitions.","A fulcrum in the middle is a first-class lever, the seesaw arrangement seen when nodding the head. It occurs in the body but is not the most common.","A load in the middle is a second-class lever, the wheelbarrow arrangement seen when standing on tiptoe. It is uncommon in the body.","Correct. Most muscles insert near the joint, so the effort sits between the fulcrum and the load."],"lt":"t-muscle-fascicles","lid":"c10","comp":80,"competencyId":"w4-lever-systems"},{"id":"L01278","dok":1,"q":"What is the agonist in a movement?","a":"The prime mover, the muscle chiefly responsible for producing the movement.","options":["The muscle that assists by adding force","The muscle that opposes the movement and relaxes","The muscle that holds the origin bone steady","The prime mover, chiefly responsible for the movement"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Muscles work in teams, and each muscle in the team plays a role. The agonist, also called the prime mover, is the muscle chiefly responsible for producing a given movement. During elbow flexion, for example, the biceps brachii is the agonist, while other muscles assist, oppose, or steady the movement.","notes":["A muscle that assists the agonist by adding force or preventing unwanted movement is a synergist, such as the brachialis during elbow flexion.","A muscle that opposes the agonist and relaxes as it contracts is the antagonist, such as the triceps brachii during elbow flexion.","A muscle that steadies the origin bone so the agonist can work efficiently is a fixator, such as the scapular muscles during arm movements.","Correct. Agonist and prime mover are two names for the same role."],"lt":"t-muscle-fascicles","lid":"c11","comp":79,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-roles-naming"},{"id":"L01279","dok":1,"q":"Which muscle role opposes the agonist, relaxing and lengthening as the agonist contracts?","a":"The antagonist.","options":["The synergist","The antagonist","The prime mover","The fixator"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The antagonist works on the opposite side of the joint from the agonist. As the agonist contracts, the antagonist relaxes and lengthens, and it can slow the movement or reverse it. During elbow flexion the biceps brachii is the agonist and the triceps brachii is the antagonist.","notes":["A synergist assists the agonist by adding force or preventing an unwanted movement. Pick it for a helper muscle such as the brachialis during elbow flexion.","Correct. The antagonist opposes the agonist and lengthens while it contracts.","The prime mover is another name for the agonist, the muscle chiefly responsible for the movement. It is the muscle the antagonist works against, not the antagonist itself.","A fixator is a synergist that holds the origin bone steady. It fits a question about scapular muscles stabilizing the scapula during arm movements."],"lt":"t-muscle-fascicles","lid":"c12","comp":79,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-roles-naming"},{"id":"L01280","dok":1,"q":"What does the term 'biceps' in a muscle name tell you about the muscle?","a":"It has two heads, or origins.","options":["It produces flexion of a joint","It is the largest muscle in its region","It has two heads, or origins","It is located on the arm"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"One of the naming criteria for muscles is the number of origins, meaning the number of heads or origin tendons. Bi means two, so the biceps brachii has two heads. Triceps means three heads and quadriceps means four, so the name alone tells you how many origin tendons to look for.","notes":["Action is shown by terms such as flexor, extensor, and abductor. The biceps does flex the elbow, but the name biceps itself describes its heads, not its action.","Relative size is shown by terms such as maximus, minimus, longus, and brevis. Pick this if the name contained one of those size words.","Correct. Biceps names the number of origins, two heads.","Location is shown by terms such as brachii, which means arm. In biceps brachii, the word brachii tells you the location, not the word biceps."],"lt":"t-muscle-fascicles","lid":"c13","comp":79,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-roles-naming"},{"id":"L01281","dok":1,"q":"What does the naming term 'maximus' describe about a muscle?","a":"Its relative size.","options":["Its action","Its shape","Its relative size","Its number of origins"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Relative size compares a muscle with its neighbors. Maximus means largest, so the gluteus maximus is the largest of the gluteal muscles, while the gluteus minimus is the smallest. Longus and brevis are size terms too, meaning long and short.","notes":["Action is shown by terms such as flexor, extensor, adductor, and levator. Choose action when the name tells you the movement the muscle produces.","Shape is shown by terms such as deltoid (triangular), trapezius (trapezoid), and rhomboid. Choose shape when the name describes a geometric form.","Correct. Maximus, minimus, longus, and brevis all describe size compared with neighboring muscles.","The number of origins is shown by biceps, triceps, and quadriceps. Choose this when the name counts heads."],"lt":"t-muscle-fascicles","lid":"c14","comp":79,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-roles-naming"},{"id":"L01282","dok":1,"q":"What is meant by the action of a muscle?","a":"The movement it produces when it contracts and pulls the insertion toward the origin.","options":["The fixed point the bone turns around","Its attachment to the more movable bone","Its attachment to the more stationary bone","The movement it produces when it contracts"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"When a muscle contracts it pulls its two attachments toward each other. The insertion, on the more movable bone, is pulled toward the origin, on the more stationary bone. The movement that results, such as flexion of the elbow, is called the action of the muscle.","notes":["The fixed point a bone turns around is the fulcrum, which in the body is a joint. That is a lever term rather than a property of the muscle.","The attachment to the more movable bone is the insertion. Pick it when the question asks which end is pulled toward the other.","The attachment to the more stationary bone is the origin. Pick it when the question asks which end of the muscle stays relatively fixed.","Correct. The action is the movement that results from that pull."],"lt":"t-muscle-fascicles","lid":"c15","comp":79,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-roles-naming"},{"id":"L01285","dok":2,"q":"During elbow flexion the biceps brachii is the agonist. What role does the triceps brachii play in this movement?","a":"Antagonist.","options":["Fixator","Synergist","Antagonist","Agonist"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The triceps brachii lies on the posterior side of the arm, opposite the biceps brachii. During elbow flexion it relaxes and lengthens while the biceps contracts, so it is the antagonist. If the triceps contracts instead, the elbow extends, and the roles switch, which shows that a muscle role depends on the movement.","notes":["A fixator holds the origin bone steady. Scapular muscles that stabilize the scapula during arm movements are fixators.","A synergist assists the agonist. The brachialis plays this role during elbow flexion, so pick synergist for a muscle that helps rather than opposes.","Correct. The triceps opposes the biceps and lengthens during elbow flexion.","The agonist is the prime mover. During elbow flexion that is the biceps brachii, while the triceps would be the agonist for elbow extension."],"lt":"t-muscle-fascicles","lid":"c18","comp":79,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-roles-naming"},{"id":"L01286","dok":2,"q":"Which example shows muscles acting as fixators?","a":"Scapular muscles holding the scapula steady during arm movements.","options":["The brachialis adding force to elbow flexion","Scapular muscles steadying the scapula","The triceps brachii relaxing during elbow flexion","The biceps brachii producing elbow flexion"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"A fixator is a special kind of synergist that holds the origin bone steady so the agonist can act efficiently on the insertion bone. Many arm muscles originate on the scapula, so scapular muscles contract to anchor the scapula while those arm muscles move the humerus. Without that stable base, the pull of the arm muscles would partly move the scapula instead.","notes":["The brachialis adding force to elbow flexion is acting as a synergist. Choose this example when the question asks for a muscle that assists the prime mover.","Correct. Steadying the origin bone is exactly what a fixator does.","The triceps relaxing and lengthening while the biceps flexes the elbow is acting as the antagonist. Pick this example for the opposing role.","The biceps producing elbow flexion is the agonist, the prime mover. Pick this example for the muscle chiefly responsible for the movement."],"lt":"t-muscle-fascicles","lid":"c19","comp":79,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-roles-naming"},{"id":"L01287","dok":2,"q":"The name 'sternocleidomastoid' is built from which naming criterion?","a":"Origin and insertion.","options":["Shape","Origin and insertion","Direction of fascicles","Relative size"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Sternocleidomastoid combines three attachment sites: sterno for the sternum, cleido for the clavicle, and mastoid for the mastoid process of the temporal bone. The muscle runs from the sternum and clavicle up to the mastoid process, so its name lists its origin and insertion. Reading the name tells you where to find both ends of the muscle.","notes":["Shape names describe a geometric form, such as deltoid (triangular), trapezius, or rhomboid. Pick shape when the name sounds like a geometry word.","Correct. The name lists the sternum, clavicle, and mastoid process, the attachments of the muscle.","Direction of fascicles names describe how the fibers run, such as rectus (straight), oblique (at an angle), or transversus (across).","Relative size names compare a muscle with its neighbors, such as maximus, minimus, longus, or brevis."],"lt":"t-muscle-fascicles","lid":"c20","comp":79,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-roles-naming"},{"id":"L01288","dok":2,"q":"Nodding the head uses the posterior neck muscles, the atlanto-occipital joint, and the weight of the face. Which lever class is this, and why?","a":"First class, because the fulcrum lies between the effort and the load.","options":["First class, because the fulcrum lies between the effort and the load","First class, because the effort lies between the fulcrum and the load","Second class, because the load lies between the fulcrum and the effort","Third class, because the effort lies between the fulcrum and the load"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"In head nodding, the atlanto-occipital joint (between the skull and the first cervical vertebra) is the fulcrum. The weight of the face sits in front of that joint and the posterior neck muscles pull behind it. With the fulcrum in the middle, like a seesaw, this is a first-class lever.","notes":["Correct. The joint sits between the neck muscle pull and the weight of the face.","The class is right but the reason describes a third-class lever. A first-class lever is defined by the fulcrum, not the effort, sitting in the middle.","A load in the middle is a second-class lever, like standing on tiptoe. In nodding, the face is in front of the joint, not between the joint and the muscles.","An effort in the middle is a third-class lever, like flexing the elbow. The neck muscles pull behind the joint, so they are not between the joint and the face."],"lt":"t-muscle-fascicles","lid":"c21","comp":80,"competencyId":"w4-lever-systems"},{"id":"L01289","dok":2,"q":"Standing on tiptoe places the body weight between the ball of the foot and the pull of the calf muscles on the heel. Which lever class is this?","a":"Second class.","options":["First class","Third class","Second class","Fourth class"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"When you rise on tiptoe, the ball of the foot is the fulcrum, the calf muscles pull up on the heel as the effort, and the body weight is the load in between. A load in the middle defines a second-class lever, like a wheelbarrow. This arrangement always works at a mechanical advantage, but it is uncommon in the body.","notes":["A first-class lever has the fulcrum in the middle, like a seesaw. Nodding the head is the body example.","A third-class lever has the effort in the middle, like tweezers. Flexing the elbow is the body example, and it is the most common class.","Correct. The load sits between the fulcrum and the effort, like a wheelbarrow.","There is no fourth class of lever. Levers are sorted into only three classes, by whether the fulcrum, the load, or the effort is in the middle."],"lt":"t-muscle-fascicles","lid":"c22","comp":80,"competencyId":"w4-lever-systems"},{"id":"L01290","dok":2,"q":"Flexing the elbow places the pull of the biceps between the elbow joint and the weight held in the hand. Which lever class is this?","a":"Third class.","options":["First class","Fourth class","Third class","Second class"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"In elbow flexion the elbow joint is the fulcrum, the weight in the hand is the load at the far end, and the biceps pulls on the radius just below the elbow. The effort therefore lies between the fulcrum and the load, which defines a third-class lever, like tweezers. This is the most common lever class in the body.","notes":["A first-class lever has the fulcrum in the middle, like a seesaw. It fits nodding the head, not elbow flexion.","There is no fourth lever class. Students sometimes invent one when a body example does not look like a textbook drawing, but all body levers fit one of the three classes.","Correct. The effort sits between the fulcrum and the load.","A second-class lever has the load in the middle, like a wheelbarrow. It fits standing on tiptoe."],"lt":"t-muscle-fascicles","lid":"c23","comp":80,"competencyId":"w4-lever-systems"},{"id":"L01291","dok":2,"q":"Which statement defines mechanical advantage in a lever system?","a":"The effort acts farther from the fulcrum than the load does.","options":["The effort acts farther from the fulcrum than the load does","The muscle pulls with more force than the load weighs","The effort acts closer to the fulcrum than the load does","The fulcrum lies exactly between the effort and the load"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Mechanical advantage depends on distances from the fulcrum. When the effort acts farther from the fulcrum than the load, a small effort can move a large load. The reverse, where the effort acts closer to the fulcrum than the load, is mechanical disadvantage, which needs a large effort but moves the load fast and far.","notes":["Correct. A longer distance for the effort lets a small force move a large load.","A muscle pulling harder than the load it lifts is a sign of mechanical disadvantage, not advantage. Students who choose this confuse a strong pull with an efficient lever.","This is the definition of mechanical disadvantage. It describes most body levers, where a muscle inserts near the joint and must pull hard to move the load.","This describes a first-class lever, which can work at an advantage or a disadvantage depending on the distances. It is a lever class, not the definition of advantage."],"lt":"t-muscle-fascicles","lid":"c24","comp":80,"competencyId":"w4-lever-systems"},{"id":"L01292","dok":1,"q":"Which naming criterion produces the term 'rectus', meaning straight?","a":"Direction of fascicles.","options":["Action of the muscle","Shape of the muscle","Direction of fascicles","Location of the muscle"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The direction of fascicles criterion describes how the fibers run relative to the body midline. Rectus means the fibers run straight, parallel to the midline, oblique means they run at an angle, and transversus means they run across. The rectus abdominis, external oblique, and transversus abdominis are named this way.","notes":["Action names describe the movement produced, such as flexor, extensor, or levator. Choose action when the name tells you what the muscle does.","Shape names describe a geometric form, such as deltoid, trapezius, or rhomboid. Choose shape when the name sounds like a geometric figure.","Correct. Rectus, oblique, and transversus all describe fiber direction.","Location names describe the bone or region the muscle is near, such as tibialis anterior, frontalis, or intercostals."],"lt":"t-muscle-fascicles","lid":"c25","comp":79,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-roles-naming"},{"id":"L01294","dok":2,"q":"A clinician reads the name 'flexor carpi radialis' and learns a lot about the muscle before seeing it. What does the word 'flexor' tell the clinician?","a":"The action the muscle produces.","options":["The region the muscle acts on","The number of origins","The action the muscle produces","The shape of the muscle"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Muscle names usually combine several naming criteria. In flexor carpi radialis, flexor names the action, carpi names the region (the wrist), and radialis names the position (the radial, or thumb, side). Reading the name piece by piece tells you the muscle flexes the wrist and lies on the radial side of the forearm.","notes":["The region is named by carpi, meaning the wrist. Pick region if the question asked about the word carpi instead of flexor.","The number of origins is shown by names such as biceps, triceps, or quadriceps. Flexor does not count heads.","Correct. Flexor names the movement, flexion.","Shape is shown by names such as deltoid or rhomboid. None of the words in flexor carpi radialis describes a geometric shape."],"lt":"t-muscle-fascicles","lid":"c27","comp":79,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-roles-naming"},{"id":"L01295","dok":3,"q":"Most body movements run on third-class levers, which work at a mechanical disadvantage. What does the body gain in return?","a":"Speed and a wide range of motion.","options":["A smaller force needed from the muscle","A greater load the limb can lift","Speed and a wide range of motion","A more stable joint"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"In a third-class lever the muscle inserts close to the joint, so the effort acts closer to the fulcrum than the load. That is a mechanical disadvantage, meaning the muscle must pull much harder than the weight it lifts. In exchange, a small shortening of the muscle swings the far end of the bone through a large arc quickly, so the body gains speed and range of motion.","notes":["This is the reverse of what happens. At a mechanical disadvantage the muscle must generate more force than the load, not less.","Lifting heavy loads with little effort is the benefit of mechanical advantage, as in a second-class lever. A third-class lever gives that up.","Correct. A short muscle pull near the joint moves the far end of the limb fast and far.","Joint stability comes from the shape of the bones, the ligaments, and muscle tone around the joint. It is not what a lever trades force for."],"lt":"t-muscle-fascicles","lid":"c28","comp":80,"competencyId":"w4-lever-systems"},{"id":"L01297","dok":2,"q":"During a movement, one muscle does most of the work while a second muscle crossing the same joint adds its own pull to the same action. Which role is the second muscle playing?","a":"Synergist","options":["Agonist","Synergist","Antagonist","Fixator"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"A synergist assists the agonist, either by adding force to the same movement or by preventing an unwanted movement. Here the first muscle is the agonist, doing most of the work, and the second muscle adds its own pull to the same action. That helping role is what makes the second muscle a synergist.","notes":["The agonist is the prime mover, the muscle doing most of the work. In this question that is the first muscle, not the one helping it.","Correct. A muscle that adds force to the agonist's action is a synergist.","An antagonist opposes the movement, relaxing and lengthening while the agonist contracts, as the triceps brachii does during elbow flexion. Pick it if the second muscle worked against the action.","A fixator is a synergist that holds the origin bone steady, like the scapular muscles during arm movements. Pick it if the second muscle stabilized a bone rather than adding force to the action."],"lt":"t-muscle-fascicles","lid":"c30","comp":79,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-roles-naming"},{"id":"L01303","dok":3,"q":"The biceps brachii shortens only a little, yet the hand moves through a large arc. Why does a small muscle shortening create a large movement at the end of the limb?","a":"The muscle inserts near the joint, so its short pull swings the far end of the lever through a wide arc.","options":["The muscle shortens farther than the hand moves","It inserts close to the elbow, the pivot","The forearm works as a second-class lever","It inserts near the wrist, far from the elbow"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The forearm is a lever that pivots at the elbow, and the biceps inserts on the radius close to that pivot. A small shortening close to the fulcrum rotates the whole forearm, and the hand at the far end travels a much longer distance. This is the speed and range that a third-class lever gains in exchange for its mechanical disadvantage.","notes":["This reverses what happens. The hand moves much farther than the muscle shortens, which is the point of the question.","Correct. A small movement near the fulcrum becomes a large arc at the hand.","In elbow flexion the effort is between the joint and the load, which is a third-class lever. A second-class lever has the load in the middle, as in standing on tiptoe.","The biceps inserts on the radial tuberosity, just below the elbow, not near the wrist. An insertion far from the joint would give more force and less range."],"lt":"t-muscle-fascicles","lid":"d3-t-muscle-fascicles-6","comp":80,"competencyId":"w4-lever-systems"},{"id":"L01304","dok":3,"q":"Muscles usually work in antagonistic pairs, such as the biceps brachii and triceps brachii at the elbow. Why can one muscle not both bend and straighten a joint by itself?","a":"A muscle can only pull, not push, so an opposing muscle is needed to move the joint the other way.","options":["A muscle with two heads can bend and straighten a joint alone","A muscle pushes the bone back by actively lengthening","The opposing muscle only steadies the joint and does not move it","A muscle can only pull, so an opposing muscle must move the joint back"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Muscle tissue produces force only by shortening, which pulls its insertion toward its origin. It cannot actively lengthen to push the bone back. So bending the elbow takes the biceps on the anterior side, and straightening it takes the triceps on the posterior side, with one relaxing while the other contracts.","notes":["Having two heads, as the biceps does, gives two origins, not two directions of pull. Both heads pull the same insertion, so the biceps still only flexes the elbow.","Muscles do not push. A muscle lengthens only passively, when the opposing muscle contracts and stretches it.","Steadying a bone is the job of a fixator. In an antagonistic pair the opposing muscle can actively move the joint the other way, as the triceps extends the elbow.","Correct. Contraction only shortens a muscle, so reversing a movement takes a muscle on the other side."],"lt":"t-muscle-fascicles","lid":"d3-t-muscle-fascicles-7","comp":79,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-roles-naming"},{"id":"L04281","dok":1,"q":"Which description fits the agonist, also called the prime mover, in a movement?","a":"The muscle chiefly responsible for producing the movement.","options":["The muscle that assists the movement by adding force or preventing unwanted motion","The muscle chiefly responsible for producing the movement","The muscle that opposes the movement, relaxing and lengthening as it happens","The muscle that holds the origin bone steady during the movement"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The agonist, or prime mover, is the muscle that does most of the work for a given movement. For example, during elbow flexion the biceps brachii acts as an agonist. The other roles, antagonist, synergist, and fixator, are defined by how they relate to the agonist.","notes":["This describes a synergist, such as the brachialis assisting elbow flexion. It would be the answer if the question asked which muscle helps the prime mover.","Correct. The agonist is the main muscle producing the movement.","This describes the antagonist, such as the triceps brachii during elbow flexion. Pick it if the question asked which muscle opposes the prime mover.","This describes a fixator, a synergist that steadies the origin bone, such as the scapular muscles during arm movements. Choose it if the question asked which muscle stabilizes the origin."],"lt":"t-w4-muscle-roles-naming","lid":"w4-muscle-roles-naming-r1","comp":79,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-roles-naming"},{"id":"L04282","dok":2,"q":"During elbow flexion, the triceps brachii relaxes and lengthens while the biceps brachii contracts. What role is the triceps playing?","a":"Antagonist.","options":["Agonist","Antagonist","Synergist","Fixator"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"An antagonist is the muscle on the opposite side of the joint from the agonist, and it relaxes and lengthens as the agonist contracts. The triceps extends the elbow, so during elbow flexion it gives way to the biceps. The antagonist can also slow the movement or reverse it, which is how the triceps controls and then undoes elbow flexion.","notes":["The agonist in elbow flexion is the muscle producing the movement, here the biceps. The triceps would be the agonist if the movement were elbow extension.","Correct. The triceps opposes elbow flexion and lengthens as the biceps contracts.","A synergist helps the agonist, as the brachialis helps the biceps flex the elbow. Pick synergist if the question described a muscle adding force to the same movement.","A fixator holds the origin bone steady so the agonist can pull efficiently. Choose it if the question described a muscle stabilizing the scapula or another origin bone."],"lt":"t-w4-muscle-roles-naming","lid":"w4-muscle-roles-naming-r2","comp":79,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-roles-naming"},{"id":"L04283","dok":1,"q":"What is a fixator?","a":"A synergist that holds the origin bone steady so the agonist can act efficiently.","options":["A muscle that opposes the agonist by relaxing as the agonist contracts","A muscle that holds the origin bone steady so the agonist can act efficiently","The muscle chiefly responsible for producing a movement","A muscle that crosses the same joint and adds force in the same direction as the agonist"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"A fixator is a special kind of synergist whose job is stabilization. It holds the bone where the agonist originates still, so the agonist's pull moves the insertion bone instead of wasting force by shifting the origin. A common example is the group of scapular muscles that steady the scapula while arm muscles move the humerus.","notes":["This describes the antagonist, which yields to the agonist on the opposite side of the joint. Pick it if the question asked about the triceps during elbow flexion.","Correct. A fixator stabilizes the origin bone for the agonist.","This describes the agonist, or prime mover. It would be the answer if the question asked which muscle does most of the work in a movement.","This describes a synergist that adds force, such as the brachialis helping the biceps flex the elbow. A fixator is also a synergist, but its job is to steady the origin, not to add force to the movement."],"lt":"t-w4-muscle-roles-naming","lid":"w4-muscle-roles-naming-r3","comp":79,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-roles-naming"},{"id":"L04284","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly distinguishes a synergist from a fixator?","a":"A synergist assists the agonist or prevents unwanted movement; a fixator is a synergist that specifically steadies the origin bone.","options":["A synergist assists the agonist or prevents unwanted movement, and a fixator is a synergist that steadies the origin bone","A fixator adds force to the agonist, and a synergist holds the insertion bone steady","A synergist works only at the elbow, and a fixator works only at the shoulder","A synergist opposes the agonist, and a fixator assists the agonist"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"A synergist is any muscle that helps the agonist, either by adding force to the movement or by preventing an unwanted movement at the same time. A fixator is one particular kind of synergist that holds the origin bone steady, so the agonist pulls efficiently on the insertion. Every fixator is a synergist, but not every synergist is a fixator.","notes":["Correct. Synergists help in general, and fixators help by stabilizing the origin.","This reverses the two roles and also names the wrong bone. Fixators steady the origin, the more stationary attachment, while the insertion is the bone that moves.","Muscle roles are not tied to a particular joint. Any muscle can act as a synergist or fixator depending on the movement, so this is a made-up rule.","Opposing the agonist is the job of the antagonist, not a synergist. Students pick this when they mix up synergist (working together) with antagonist (working against)."],"lt":"t-w4-muscle-roles-naming","lid":"w4-muscle-roles-naming-r4","comp":79,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-roles-naming"},{"id":"L04285","dok":2,"q":"The name flexor carpi radialis tells you three things about the muscle. Which choice reads the name correctly?","a":"Action (flexor), region (carpi, the wrist), and position (radialis, the radial side).","options":["Action (flexor), region (carpi, the wrist), and position (radialis, the radial side)","Action (flexor), origin (carpi, the carpal bones), and insertion (radialis, the radius)","Fiber direction (flexor), region (carpi, the wrist), and origin (radialis, the radius)","Action (flexor), number of heads (carpi), and size (radialis)"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Flexor names the action, so the muscle bends a joint. Carpi refers to the carpus, the wrist, which is the joint it flexes, and radialis tells you it lies on the radial (thumb) side of the forearm. Reading the name this way predicts that the muscle flexes the wrist and sits laterally in the anterior forearm.","notes":["Correct. The name gives the action, the region, and the side of the forearm.","Flexor carpi radialis actually arises from the medial epicondyle of the humerus and inserts on the bases of metacarpals 2 and 3, so carpi and radialis are not its attachments. A name built on origin and insertion looks like sternocleidomastoid.","Flexor is an action, not a fiber direction; direction words are rectus, oblique, and transversus. Radialis also does not mean the muscle arises from the radius, since this muscle starts at the medial epicondyle.","Number of heads is shown by words like biceps and triceps, and size by words like longus and brevis. Neither carpi nor radialis carries that meaning."],"lt":"t-w4-muscle-roles-naming","lid":"w4-muscle-roles-naming-r5","comp":79,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-roles-naming"},{"id":"L04323","dok":1,"q":"When a muscle moves a body part, which choice correctly matches each part of the lever?","a":"The bone is the lever, the joint is the fulcrum, the muscle pull is the effort, and the body part's weight is the load.","options":["The bone is the lever, the joint is the fulcrum, the body part is the effort, and the muscle pull is the load","The joint is the lever, the bone is the fulcrum, the muscle pull is the effort, and the body part is the load","The muscle is the lever, the tendon is the fulcrum, the joint is the effort, and the bone is the load","The bone is the lever, the joint is the fulcrum, the muscle pull is the effort, and the body part is the load"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"A lever is a rigid bar that turns around a fixed point. In the body the bone is the rigid bar, the joint is the fixed point (fulcrum) it turns around, the pull of the contracting muscle is the effort, and the weight of the body part plus anything it holds is the load. Once these four are identified, you can sort any movement into a lever class.","notes":["This swaps the effort and the load. The muscle supplies the force (effort), and the body part is the weight being moved (load).","This swaps the lever and the fulcrum. The bone is the rigid bar that moves, and the joint is the pivot point it turns on.","A muscle is soft tissue that pulls, so it supplies the effort rather than acting as the rigid bar, and a tendon only transmits that pull. Students pick this when they mix up the parts that move with the parts that pull.","Correct. Bone, joint, muscle pull, and body part weight are the lever, fulcrum, effort, and load."],"lt":"t-w4-lever-systems","lid":"w4-lever-systems-r1","comp":80,"competencyId":"w4-lever-systems"},{"id":"L04324","dok":2,"q":"Which lever class is the most common in the body, and how are its parts arranged?","a":"Third class, with the effort between the fulcrum and the load.","options":["Second class, with the load between the fulcrum and the effort","Third class, with the load between the fulcrum and the effort","Third class, with the effort between the fulcrum and the load","First class, with the fulcrum between the effort and the load"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Most body movements use third class levers, in which the muscle attaches between the joint and the load, as the biceps does between the elbow and the hand. Because the effort acts closer to the fulcrum than the load, these levers work at a mechanical disadvantage, meaning the muscle must pull much harder than the weight it lifts. In return, the load moves fast and through a wide range.","notes":["A second class lever has the load in the middle, like a wheelbarrow. It would be the answer if the question asked about standing on tiptoe.","Third class is the right class, but load in the middle is the arrangement of a second class lever. In a third class lever the effort is in the middle.","Correct. Third class levers, with the effort in the middle, are the most common in the body.","A first class lever has the fulcrum in the middle, like a seesaw. Pick it if the question asked about nodding the head at the atlanto-occipital joint."],"lt":"t-w4-lever-systems","lid":"w4-lever-systems-r2","comp":80,"competencyId":"w4-lever-systems"},{"id":"L04325","dok":2,"q":"The biceps brachii flexes the elbow to lift a weight held in the hand. Which lever class is this, and why?","a":"Third class, because the biceps pulls between the elbow and the weight.","options":["Second class, because the weight sits between the elbow and the biceps","First class, because the triceps pulls on the olecranon behind the elbow","Third class, because the biceps pulls between the elbow and the weight","First class, because the elbow joint sits between the biceps and the weight"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"In elbow flexion the elbow joint is the fulcrum, the weight in the hand is the load at the far end, and the biceps inserts on the radial tuberosity just below the elbow. That puts the effort between the fulcrum and the load, which defines a third class lever. This is the standard example of a third class lever in the body.","notes":["The weight is held in the hand at the far end of the forearm, not between the elbow and the biceps. A second class lever puts the load in the middle, as in standing on tiptoe.","The triceps pulling on the olecranon does form a first class lever, but that movement is elbow extension, not flexion. Pick this if the question asked about straightening the elbow.","Correct. The biceps pulls between the fulcrum (elbow) and the load (weight in the hand).","The elbow is the fulcrum here, but it sits at one end of the forearm, not between the biceps and the weight. A first class lever needs the pivot in the middle, as in nodding the head."],"lt":"t-w4-lever-systems","lid":"w4-lever-systems-r3","comp":80,"competencyId":"w4-lever-systems"}]},{"id":"m3-back-thorax-muscles","title":"Muscles of the back, thorax and shoulder","summary":"chest, back and shoulder muscles including rotator cuff, per the Module 3 lab structure list","mw":"2026-10-07","tr":"2026-10-08","cards":[{"id":"L04304","dok":1,"q":"Which large, fan-shaped chest muscle flexes, adducts, and medially rotates the arm?","a":"Pectoralis major.","options":["Serratus anterior","Pectoralis minor","Pectoralis major","Subclavius"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Pectoralis major arises from the medial clavicle, the sternum, and costal cartilages 1 to 6, and its fibers converge onto the lateral lip of the intertubercular sulcus of the humerus. Because it inserts on the humerus, it moves the arm, flexing, adducting, and medially rotating it. Its broad origin and single tendon make it the classic convergent muscle.","notes":["Serratus anterior runs from the ribs to the medial border of the scapula, so it acts on the scapula. It would be the answer for the muscle that protracts the scapula and holds it against the chest wall.","Pectoralis minor lies deep to pectoralis major and inserts on the coracoid process, so it moves the scapula, not the arm. Pick it if the question asked which chest muscle depresses and protracts the scapula.","Correct. Pectoralis major inserts on the humerus and flexes, adducts, and medially rotates the arm.","Subclavius is a small muscle from rib 1 to the underside of the clavicle that depresses and steadies the clavicle. Choose it if the question asked which muscle anchors the clavicle."],"lt":"t-w4-lab-chest-anterior-arm","lid":"w4-lab-chest-anterior-arm-r1","comp":85,"competencyId":"w4-lab-chest-anterior-arm"},{"id":"L04305","dok":1,"q":"Which muscle runs from the upper eight or nine ribs to the medial border of the scapula, protracts the scapula as in throwing a punch, and holds it flat against the chest wall?","a":"Serratus anterior.","options":["Pectoralis minor","Serratus anterior","Rhomboid major","Serratus posterior superior"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Serratus anterior arises by finger-like slips from ribs 1 to 8 or 9 on the lateral chest wall and inserts on the anterior surface of the medial border of the scapula. It pulls the scapula forward (protraction), which is why it is called the boxer's muscle, and it rotates the glenoid upward. Its pull also keeps the scapula pressed flat against the ribs; when it fails, the scapula lifts off the back as a winged scapula.","notes":["Pectoralis minor also protracts the scapula, but it runs from ribs 3 to 5 to the coracoid process, not to the medial border. Pick it if the question asked which chest muscle depresses the scapula from the coracoid process.","Correct. Serratus anterior protracts the scapula and holds it against the chest wall.","Rhomboid major attaches to the medial border of the scapula but pulls it toward the spine, retracting it. It would be the answer for the muscle that squeezes the shoulder blades together.","Serratus posterior superior runs from the C7 to T3 spinous processes to ribs 2 to 5 and elevates the ribs. It does not reach the scapula; students pick it because of the shared name serratus."],"lt":"t-w4-lab-chest-anterior-arm","lid":"w4-lab-chest-anterior-arm-r2","comp":85,"competencyId":"w4-lab-chest-anterior-arm"},{"id":"L04309","dok":1,"q":"Which list names the four rotator cuff muscles (SITS)?","a":"Supraspinatus, infraspinatus, teres minor, and subscapularis.","options":["Supraspinatus, infraspinatus, teres major, and subscapularis","Supraspinatus, infraspinatus, teres minor, and subclavius","Supraspinatus, infraspinatus, teres minor, and deltoid","Supraspinatus, infraspinatus, teres minor, and subscapularis"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The rotator cuff is made of supraspinatus, infraspinatus, teres minor, and subscapularis, remembered as SITS. All four run from the scapula to the head of the humerus and hold it in the shallow glenoid cavity. Three insert on the greater tubercle, and only subscapularis reaches the lesser tubercle.","notes":["Teres major runs from the inferior angle of the scapula to the intertubercular sulcus and extends, adducts, and medially rotates the arm, but it is not part of the cuff. Students pick it because its name looks almost the same as teres minor.","Subclavius is a small muscle under the clavicle that steadies the clavicle. Students choose it because its name starts like subscapularis, the S that belongs in SITS.","The deltoid covers the shoulder and abducts the arm, but it sits superficial to the cuff and does not wrap the humeral head. Pick the deltoid if the question asked which muscle forms the rounded contour of the shoulder.","Correct. These are the four SITS muscles of the rotator cuff."],"lt":"t-w4-lab-posterior-shoulder-cuff","lid":"w4-lab-posterior-shoulder-cuff-r1","comp":87,"competencyId":"w4-lab-posterior-shoulder-cuff"},{"id":"L04310","dok":1,"q":"Which broad superficial back muscle, arising from the lower spine, thoracolumbar fascia, and iliac crest, extends, adducts, and medially rotates the arm?","a":"Latissimus dorsi.","options":["Latissimus dorsi","Rhomboid major","Trapezius","Teres major"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Latissimus dorsi arises from the spinous processes of T7 to L5, the thoracolumbar fascia, the iliac crest, and the lower ribs, and it inserts in the intertubercular sulcus of the humerus. Pulling the humerus down and back, it extends, adducts, and medially rotates the arm. That powerful downward pull is why it is called the swimmer's muscle.","notes":["Correct. Latissimus dorsi arises from the lower back and pelvis and extends, adducts, and medially rotates the arm.","Rhomboid major runs from the T2 to T5 spinous processes to the medial border of the scapula and retracts the scapula. Choose it if the question asked which muscle pulls the scapula toward the spine.","Trapezius is a broad superficial back muscle, but it inserts on the clavicle, acromion, and spine of the scapula, so it moves the scapula rather than the arm. Pick it if the question asked which muscle elevates, retracts, and depresses the scapula.","Teres major has the same actions on the arm, but it arises from the inferior angle of the scapula, not from the spine and iliac crest. It would be the answer if the question named the inferior angle of the scapula as the origin."],"lt":"t-w4-lab-posterior-shoulder-cuff","lid":"w4-lab-posterior-shoulder-cuff-r2","comp":87,"competencyId":"w4-lab-posterior-shoulder-cuff"},{"id":"L04311","dok":1,"q":"Which rotator cuff muscle initiates abduction of the arm, the first 15 degrees?","a":"Supraspinatus.","options":["Supraspinatus","Subscapularis","Teres minor","Infraspinatus"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Supraspinatus arises from the supraspinous fossa above the spine of the scapula, passes under the acromion, and inserts on the greater tubercle. From that position on top of the joint it starts lifting the arm out to the side. The middle fibers of the deltoid then take over to carry abduction further.","notes":["Correct. Supraspinatus initiates abduction of the arm.","Subscapularis lies on the anterior surface of the scapula, inserts on the lesser tubercle, and is the only cuff muscle that medially rotates the arm. It would be the answer for the cuff muscle reaching the lesser tubercle.","Teres minor runs from the lateral border of the scapula to the greater tubercle and laterally rotates the arm. Choose it if the question asked which small cuff muscle below infraspinatus rotates the arm outward.","Infraspinatus arises from the infraspinous fossa below the spine of the scapula and laterally rotates the arm. Pick it if the question asked which cuff muscle in the infraspinous fossa turns the arm outward."],"lt":"t-w4-lab-posterior-shoulder-cuff","lid":"w4-lab-posterior-shoulder-cuff-r3","comp":87,"competencyId":"w4-lab-posterior-shoulder-cuff"},{"id":"L04312","dok":2,"q":"What job do all four rotator cuff muscles share?","a":"They hold the head of the humerus in the glenoid cavity, stabilizing the shoulder joint.","options":["They flex the elbow and supinate the forearm","They hold the head of the humerus in the glenoid cavity, stabilizing the shoulder","They produce all of the abduction of the arm above shoulder height","They hold the scapula flat against the posterior chest wall"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The glenoid cavity of the scapula is shallow, so the shoulder relies on muscles rather than a deep socket to stay together. The four cuff muscles run from the scapula to the humeral head and wrap it on the top, front, and back, pulling it snugly into the glenoid cavity. This gives the shoulder its wide range of motion but also explains why it dislocates more than any other joint.","notes":["Elbow flexion and supination are the actions of biceps brachii, which crosses the elbow. The cuff muscles end on the humeral head and do not reach the forearm.","Correct. The cuff muscles together keep the humeral head seated in the glenoid cavity.","Among the cuff muscles only supraspinatus abducts, and its main job is starting the movement; the deltoid and the upward rotation of the scapula carry the arm higher. This choice gives the whole cuff the job of the deltoid.","Holding the scapula flat against the chest wall is the job of serratus anterior. Pick this if the question asked what goes wrong in a winged scapula."],"lt":"t-w4-lab-posterior-shoulder-cuff","lid":"w4-lab-posterior-shoulder-cuff-r4","comp":87,"competencyId":"w4-lab-posterior-shoulder-cuff"},{"id":"L04313","dok":3,"q":"A house painter who works overhead every day develops shoulder pain from a cuff tendon pinched between the head of the humerus and the acromion. Which tendon is most likely affected?","a":"The supraspinatus tendon.","options":["The infraspinatus tendon","The subscapularis tendon","The teres minor tendon","The supraspinatus tendon"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The supraspinatus tendon runs from the supraspinous fossa across the top of the shoulder, passing under the acromion to reach the greater tubercle. The acromion is a bony roof with no give, so repeated overhead arm raising pinches the tendon against it. This is why impingement and rotator cuff tears are concentrated in the supraspinatus.","notes":["Infraspinatus passes behind the shoulder from the infraspinous fossa, below the spine of the scapula, so it is not trapped under the acromion. Choose it if the question asked which cuff muscle covers most of the back of the scapula.","Subscapularis crosses the front of the shoulder to the lesser tubercle, not under the acromion. Pick it if the question asked which cuff muscle medially rotates the arm.","Teres minor runs from the lateral border of the scapula to the lowest part of the greater tubercle, passing behind the joint rather than under the acromion. It would be the answer for a small cuff muscle that laterally rotates the arm.","Correct. Only the supraspinatus tendon runs under the acromion, where overhead work pinches it."],"lt":"t-w4-lab-posterior-shoulder-cuff","lid":"w4-lab-posterior-shoulder-cuff-r5","comp":87,"competencyId":"w4-lab-posterior-shoulder-cuff"},{"id":"L04580","dok":1,"q":"Which broad muscle of the upper back and neck elevates the scapula with its upper fibers and retracts it with its middle fibers?","a":"The trapezius.","options":["Latissimus dorsi","Trapezius","Pectoralis minor","Serratus anterior"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The trapezius is a large, flat, diamond-shaped muscle that runs from the occipital bone, the ligamentum nuchae, and the spinous processes of C7 to T12 out to the lateral clavicle, acromion, and spine of the scapula. Its fibers run in different directions, so different parts do different jobs: upper fibers elevate the scapula, middle fibers retract it, and lower fibers depress it. Upper and lower fibers together rotate the glenoid upward.","notes":["The latissimus dorsi covers the lower back and inserts on the humerus, so it moves the arm, extending, adducting, and medially rotating it. Pick it for a question about pulling the arm down and back, as in a swimming stroke.","Correct. The trapezius elevates, retracts, and depresses the scapula with different fiber groups.","The pectoralis minor runs from ribs 3 to 5 to the coracoid process and depresses and protracts the scapula. Choose it for a question about pulling the scapula down and forward.","The serratus anterior lies on the lateral chest wall and protracts the scapula, pulling it forward around the ribs, the opposite of retraction. It would be the answer for the muscle that holds the scapula flat, whose loss causes a winged scapula."],"lt":"t-w7-lab-neck","lid":"w7-lab-neck-r2","comp":87,"competencyId":"w4-lab-posterior-shoulder-cuff"},{"id":"N-m3-back-thorax-muscles-1","dok":1,"q":"Where does the trapezius insert?","a":"The lateral clavicle, the acromion, and the spine of the scapula.","options":["The floor of the intertubercular sulcus of the humerus","The coracoid process on the front of the scapula","The lateral clavicle, acromion, and spine of the scapula","The medial border of the scapula, from the spine down"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The trapezius is the large diamond-shaped muscle covering the upper back and neck. It arises from the occipital bone, the ligamentum nuchae, and the spinous processes of C7 to T12, and its fibers converge laterally onto one continuous bony line: the lateral third of the clavicle, the acromion, and the spine of the scapula. Because it inserts on the pectoral girdle and not the humerus, all of its actions move the scapula.","notes":["The intertubercular sulcus is the groove between the two tubercles of the humerus where the latissimus dorsi inserts. It would be the answer for the latissimus dorsi, the other large superficial back muscle.","The coracoid process is the hook on the front of the scapula where the pectoralis minor inserts and the short head of the biceps and the coracobrachialis arise. It would fit a question about the pectoralis minor.","Correct. The trapezius converges on the lateral clavicle, acromion, and scapular spine, the same line the deltoid arises from.","The medial border of the scapula is where the rhomboid major and minor insert, with the levator scapulae at its upper end. Pick it if the question asked where the rhomboids attach."],"comp":87,"competencyId":"w4-lab-posterior-shoulder-cuff"},{"id":"N-m3-back-thorax-muscles-2","dok":1,"q":"Which broad sheet of connective tissue in the lower back is a major origin of the latissimus dorsi?","a":"The thoracolumbar fascia.","options":["The thoracolumbar fascia","The ligamentum nuchae","The bicipital aponeurosis","The clavipectoral fascia"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The latissimus dorsi arises from the spinous processes of T7 to L5, the thoracolumbar fascia, the iliac crest, and the lower ribs. The thoracolumbar fascia is a tough diamond-shaped sheet of deep fascia covering the lower back, and it gives the latissimus its wide, flat lower origin. From there the muscle sweeps up and laterally to a narrow tendon in the intertubercular sulcus of the humerus.","notes":["Correct. The thoracolumbar fascia anchors the lower origin of the latissimus dorsi across the lumbar region.","The ligamentum nuchae is the midline ligament on the back of the neck that gives origin to the upper trapezius and the splenius capitis. Pick it for a question about the neck origin of those muscles.","The bicipital aponeurosis is a flat band from the biceps tendon that fans into the fascia of the medial forearm. It belongs at the elbow, not the back, and fits a question about the biceps insertion.","The clavipectoral fascia is a sheet on the front of the chest that encloses the subclavius and pectoralis minor. It would be the answer for a question about the fascia deep to the pectoralis major."],"comp":87,"competencyId":"w4-lab-posterior-shoulder-cuff"},{"id":"N-m3-back-thorax-muscles-3","dok":2,"q":"The ligamentum nuchae gives origin to which pair of back muscles?","a":"The trapezius and the splenius capitis.","options":["Levator scapulae and rhomboid major","Trapezius and splenius capitis","Iliocostalis and longissimus","Latissimus dorsi and quadratus lumborum"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The ligamentum nuchae is a thick midline ligament running from the occipital bone down the back of the neck along the cervical spinous processes. It gives the neck muscles a midline anchor where the cervical spinous processes are short. The upper fibers of the trapezius arise from it superficially, and the splenius capitis arises from it in a deeper layer before wrapping up to the mastoid process and occipital bone.","notes":["The levator scapulae arises from the transverse processes of C1 to C4, and the rhomboid major from the spinous processes of T2 to T5. They would fit a question about muscles that insert on the medial border of the scapula.","Correct. Both the trapezius and the splenius capitis take part of their origin from the ligamentum nuchae.","The iliocostalis and longissimus are columns of the erector spinae that run along the ribs and transverse processes. They would be the answer for a question about the lateral and intermediate erector spinae columns.","The latissimus dorsi and quadratus lumborum are lower back muscles that arise from the thoracolumbar fascia, iliac crest, and lumbar region. Pick them for a question about origins in the lower back, not the neck."],"comp":87,"competencyId":"w4-lab-posterior-shoulder-cuff"},{"id":"N-m3-back-thorax-muscles-4","dok":1,"q":"Which set of actions does the latissimus dorsi produce at the shoulder?","a":"It extends, adducts, and medially rotates the arm.","options":["Elevates and retracts the scapula","Flexes, adducts, and medially rotates the arm","Extends, adducts, and medially rotates the arm","Abducts and laterally rotates the arm"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The latissimus dorsi runs from the lower back up to the intertubercular sulcus on the front of the humerus. Because it pulls the humerus down and back from that anterior attachment, it extends, adducts, and medially rotates the arm. This is the movement of a swimming stroke or of pushing yourself up out of a chair.","notes":["Elevating and retracting the scapula describes scapular muscles such as the upper trapezius and the rhomboids. The latissimus dorsi inserts on the humerus, so its actions are arm actions.","Flexion, adduction, and medial rotation are the actions of the pectoralis major, which lies on the front of the chest and pulls the arm forward. Pick this set for the pectoralis major.","Correct. The latissimus dorsi brings the arm down and back while turning it inward.","Abduction with lateral rotation does not describe the latissimus dorsi, which pulls the arm down and turns it inward. Abduction belongs mainly to the middle deltoid and supraspinatus, and lateral rotation to the infraspinatus and teres minor."],"comp":87,"competencyId":"w4-lab-posterior-shoulder-cuff"},{"id":"N-m3-back-thorax-muscles-5","dok":1,"q":"Where does the levator scapulae take its origin?","a":"The transverse processes of C1 to C4.","options":["The spinous processes of C7 to T1","The outer surfaces of ribs 1 to 8","The transverse processes of C1 to C4","The spinous processes of T2 to T5"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The levator scapulae is a strap-like muscle on the side and back of the neck. It arises from the transverse processes of the first four cervical vertebrae and descends to the superior part of the medial border of the scapula. As its name says, it lifts (levates) the scapula, and it also rotates the glenoid downward.","notes":["The spinous processes of C7 to T1 are the origin of the rhomboid minor, which inserts on the medial border at the level of the scapular spine. Pick this for the rhomboid minor.","The outer surfaces of the upper ribs on the lateral chest wall are the origin of the serratus anterior. Pick this for the muscle that protracts the scapula.","Correct. The levator scapulae comes off the transverse processes of C1 to C4.","The spinous processes of T2 to T5 are the origin of the rhomboid major. It would be the answer for the larger rhomboid, which inserts on the medial border below the spine."],"comp":87,"competencyId":"w4-lab-posterior-shoulder-cuff"},{"id":"N-m3-back-thorax-muscles-6","dok":1,"q":"Which muscle arises from the spinous processes of T2 to T5 and inserts on the medial border of the scapula below the spine?","a":"The rhomboid major.","options":["Serratus anterior","Levator scapulae","Rhomboid major","Rhomboid minor"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The rhomboid major is the larger of the two rhomboids and lies just below the rhomboid minor, deep to the trapezius. It runs from the spinous processes of T2 to T5 down and laterally to the medial border of the scapula below the spine. It retracts the scapula toward the spine and rotates the glenoid downward.","notes":["The serratus anterior also inserts along the medial border of the scapula, but on its anterior surface, and it arises from the ribs on the lateral chest wall. Pick it for the muscle that protracts the scapula.","The levator scapulae arises from the transverse processes of C1 to C4 and reaches only the upper medial border. It would be the answer for the muscle that elevates the scapula from the neck.","Correct. The rhomboid major spans T2 to T5 and the medial border below the spine.","The rhomboid minor arises higher, from the spinous processes of C7 and T1, and inserts on the medial border at the level of the scapular spine. Pick it if the origin were C7 to T1."],"comp":87,"competencyId":"w4-lab-posterior-shoulder-cuff"},{"id":"N-m3-back-thorax-muscles-7","dok":1,"q":"Which muscle or muscle part depresses the scapula?","a":"The lower fibers of the trapezius.","options":["Levator scapulae","Upper fibers of the trapezius","Lower fibers of the trapezius","Rhomboid minor"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The trapezius has three sets of fibers that pull in different directions. The upper fibers elevate the scapula, the middle fibers retract it, and the lower fibers, which run upward from the lower thoracic spinous processes to the scapular spine, pull it down. That downward pull is depression of the scapula.","notes":["The levator scapulae runs from the upper cervical vertebrae to the top of the medial border, so it elevates the scapula. It would be the answer for a shrugging muscle, not a depressing one.","The upper fibers of the trapezius run down from the skull and neck to the clavicle and acromion, so they elevate the scapula, as in a shrug. Pick them for elevation.","Correct. The lower fibers of the trapezius pull the scapula down.","The rhomboid minor runs from C7 and T1 to the medial border and retracts the scapula. Pick it for pulling the shoulder blades together, not down."],"comp":87,"competencyId":"w4-lab-posterior-shoulder-cuff"},{"id":"N-m3-back-thorax-muscles-8","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares the trapezius and the rhomboids?","a":"Both retract the scapula, but the trapezius rotates the glenoid upward and the rhomboids rotate it downward.","options":["The trapezius protracts the scapula, while the rhomboids retract it","Both retract the scapula, and both rotate the glenoid upward","Both retract the scapula, but they rotate the glenoid in opposite directions","Both elevate the scapula, but only the trapezius retracts it"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The middle trapezius and the rhomboids both pull the scapula toward the spine, so they agree on retraction. They disagree on rotation: the upper and lower trapezius fibers together turn the glenoid cavity upward, while the rhomboids pull the medial border up and in, which turns the glenoid downward. That makes them synergists for retraction and antagonists for rotation.","notes":["The trapezius does not protract the scapula. Protraction, pulling the scapula forward around the chest, is the job of the serratus anterior and pectoralis minor.","The first half is right, since both retract, but the rhomboids rotate the glenoid downward, not upward. The upward rotators are the trapezius and the serratus anterior.","Correct. They share retraction, but the trapezius turns the glenoid up and the rhomboids turn it down.","This is wrong because the rhomboids are retractors, and retraction is the action they share with the middle trapezius. Elevation is mainly the job of the upper trapezius and the levator scapulae."],"comp":87,"competencyId":"w4-lab-posterior-shoulder-cuff"},{"id":"N-m3-back-thorax-muscles-9","dok":2,"q":"On a cadaver, you reflect the trapezius from the upper back. At the level of T2 to T5, which muscle lies directly deep to it, between the medial border of the scapula and the spine?","a":"The rhomboid major.","options":["Latissimus dorsi","Serratus anterior","Rhomboid major","Longissimus"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The back muscles are dissected in layers. The trapezius is the most superficial muscle of the upper back, and once it is lifted away the next layer between the scapula and the spine is the rhomboids and the levator scapulae. At T2 to T5 the muscle exposed is the rhomboid major, which runs from those spinous processes to the medial border of the scapula.","notes":["The latissimus dorsi is superficial too, but it arises from T7 downward and covers the lower back, not the T2 to T5 region. Pick it for the superficial muscle of the lower back.","The serratus anterior runs from the lateral ribs to the anterior surface of the medial border, so it lies between the scapula and the chest wall, not between the scapula and the spine. Pick it for the muscle deep to the scapula on the side of the chest.","Correct. The rhomboid major sits immediately deep to the trapezius between the scapula and T2 to T5.","The longissimus is a column of the erector spinae, which lies in a deeper layer beneath the rhomboids. It would be the answer only after the rhomboids are also reflected."],"comp":87,"competencyId":"w4-lab-posterior-shoulder-cuff"},{"id":"N-m3-back-thorax-muscles-10","dok":2,"q":"Three muscles attach in or beside the intertubercular sulcus of the humerus. Which arrangement is correct?","a":"Pectoralis major on the lateral lip, latissimus dorsi in the floor, teres major on the medial lip.","options":["Pectoralis major on the lateral lip, teres major in the floor, latissimus dorsi on the medial lip","Teres major on the lateral lip, latissimus dorsi in the floor, pectoralis major on the medial lip","Latissimus dorsi on the lateral lip, pectoralis major in the floor, teres major on the medial lip","Pectoralis major on the lateral lip, latissimus dorsi in the floor, teres major on the medial lip"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The intertubercular sulcus is the groove between the greater and lesser tubercles on the front of the humerus. The pectoralis major inserts on its lateral lip, the latissimus dorsi inserts in its floor, and the teres major inserts on its medial lip. Students often remember this as a lady (latissimus) lying between two majors.","notes":["This swaps the latissimus dorsi and the teres major. The teres major is on the medial lip, and the latissimus dorsi lies in the floor between the two majors.","This reverses the two lips. The teres major attaches to the medial lip and the pectoralis major to the lateral lip.","This swaps the latissimus dorsi and the pectoralis major. The latissimus tendon lies in the floor of the groove, and the pectoralis major reaches the lateral lip.","Correct. Pectoralis major lateral, latissimus dorsi in the floor, teres major medial."],"comp":87,"competencyId":"w4-lab-posterior-shoulder-cuff"},{"id":"N-m3-back-thorax-muscles-11","dok":3,"q":"During a chin-up, the arms are pulled down and back from an overhead position to lift the body. Which back muscle is the prime mover of that arm movement?","a":"The latissimus dorsi.","options":["Trapezius","Serratus anterior","Latissimus dorsi","Levator scapulae"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"A chin-up brings the humerus from overhead down toward the side of the body, which is adduction and extension of the arm. The latissimus dorsi is the large back muscle that runs from the lower back to the humerus and produces exactly those movements. Because the hands are fixed on the bar, its pull lifts the trunk up toward the arms instead of moving the arms.","notes":["The trapezius inserts on the clavicle and scapula, not the humerus, so it moves the scapula rather than pulling the arm down. Pick it for elevating, retracting, or depressing the scapula.","The serratus anterior pulls the scapula forward around the chest wall. It would fit a pushing or punching movement, not a pull.","Correct. The latissimus dorsi adducts and extends the arm, which is the main work of a chin-up.","The levator scapulae runs from the neck to the top of the scapula and elevates it. It would be the answer for a shrug, not a pull-up."],"comp":87,"competencyId":"w4-lab-posterior-shoulder-cuff"},{"id":"N-m3-back-thorax-muscles-12","dok":1,"q":"Which column of the erector spinae is the most lateral?","a":"The iliocostalis.","options":["Multifidus","Spinalis","Iliocostalis","Longissimus"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The erector spinae is made of three vertical columns on each side of the spine. From lateral to medial they are the iliocostalis, the longissimus, and the spinalis, remembered as I Love Spaghetti. The iliocostalis is the most lateral column, and as its name suggests (ilio for the ilium, costalis for the ribs), it attaches to the ribs.","notes":["The multifidus is not part of the erector spinae. It is a deeper muscle beneath it that spans two to four vertebral segments.","The spinalis is the most medial column, running right along the spinous processes. Pick it for the column closest to the midline.","Correct. The iliocostalis is the lateral column and attaches to the ribs.","The longissimus is the intermediate column between the other two. It would be the answer for the middle column or for the column that reaches the mastoid process."]},{"id":"N-m3-back-thorax-muscles-13","dok":1,"q":"Which column of the erector spinae is the most medial, running along the spinous processes?","a":"The spinalis.","options":["Spinalis","Iliocostalis","Semispinalis","Longissimus"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The spinalis is the smallest and most medial of the three erector spinae columns. It lies right next to the spinous processes, which is where its name comes from. Going outward from it are the longissimus and then the iliocostalis.","notes":["Correct. The spinalis sits closest to the spinous processes.","The iliocostalis is the most lateral column, attaching to the ribs. Pick it for the column farthest from the midline.","The semispinalis is a deeper back muscle that runs from transverse processes up to the occipital bone and spinous processes above. It is not one of the three erector spinae columns, even though its name looks similar to spinalis.","The longissimus is the intermediate column and the longest of the three. Pick it for the middle column."]},{"id":"N-m3-back-thorax-muscles-14","dok":2,"q":"Which sequence lists the erector spinae columns from medial to lateral?","a":"Spinalis, longissimus, iliocostalis.","options":["Iliocostalis, longissimus, spinalis","Spinalis, longissimus, iliocostalis","Spinalis, iliocostalis, longissimus","Longissimus, spinalis, iliocostalis"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The usual mnemonic, I Love Spaghetti, lists the columns from lateral to medial: iliocostalis, longissimus, spinalis. This question asks for the opposite direction, starting at the midline. Starting next to the spinous processes you meet the spinalis, then the intermediate longissimus, then the lateral iliocostalis.","notes":["This is the correct order, but from lateral to medial. It would be the answer if the question asked for lateral to medial, the direction of the I Love Spaghetti mnemonic.","Correct. Spinalis is medial, longissimus is intermediate, and iliocostalis is lateral.","This order starts correctly with the spinalis but then jumps to the lateral column. The longissimus always sits between the other two.","This puts the longissimus at the midline, but the longissimus is the intermediate column. The spinalis is the one next to the spinous processes."]},{"id":"N-m3-back-thorax-muscles-15","dok":2,"q":"What does the erector spinae do when the columns on both sides contract together, and when only one side contracts?","a":"Both sides extend the vertebral column; one side bends it laterally toward that side.","options":["Both sides extend the spine; one side bends it to that side","Both sides bend the spine sideways; one side extends it","Both sides flex the spine; one side bends it to that side","Both sides retract the scapulae; one side elevates the scapula"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The erector spinae columns run vertically behind the axis of the vertebral column. When both sides pull together, they straighten and arch the back, which is extension. When only one side pulls, the spine bends toward that side, which is lateral flexion. This is the main muscle mass that keeps the trunk upright.","notes":["Correct. Bilateral contraction extends the spine, and one-sided contraction bends it laterally.","This reverses the two situations. Lateral bending needs one side pulling alone, and extension needs both sides pulling together.","Flexion of the trunk, bending forward, is done by muscles in front of the spine such as the rectus abdominis. The erector spinae lies behind the spine, so it does the opposite.","Retracting and elevating the scapulae are actions of the rhomboids, trapezius, and levator scapulae. The erector spinae attaches to vertebrae, ribs, and skull, not the scapula."]},{"id":"N-m3-back-thorax-muscles-16","dok":1,"q":"Which deep back muscle wraps up the side of the neck from the ligamentum nuchae to the mastoid process and occipital bone?","a":"The splenius capitis.","options":["Levator scapulae","Splenius cervicis","Semispinalis","Splenius capitis"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The splenius capitis arises from the ligamentum nuchae and the spinous processes of C7 to T3 and runs up and laterally to the mastoid process and the occipital bone. Splenius means bandage, which describes how it wraps around the deeper neck muscles, and capitis means head. Acting together, the two sides extend the head, and acting alone, one side turns the head toward the same side.","notes":["The levator scapulae also lies on the side of the neck, but it runs from the transverse processes of C1 to C4 down to the scapula. Pick it for the muscle that elevates the scapula.","The splenius cervicis is its lower partner. It arises from the spinous processes of T3 to T6 and stops at the transverse processes of C1 to C3, so it never reaches the skull. Pick it for the splenius that moves the neck.","The semispinalis runs from transverse processes up to the occipital bone and spinous processes above, in a layer deep to the splenius. It would be the answer for a muscle arising from transverse processes C7 to T12.","Correct. The splenius capitis runs from the ligamentum nuchae to the mastoid process and occipital bone."]},{"id":"N-m3-back-thorax-muscles-17","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares the splenius capitis and the splenius cervicis?","a":"The capitis reaches the skull, while the cervicis stops at the upper cervical transverse processes.","options":["The capitis reaches the skull, while the cervicis stops at the upper cervical transverse processes","The cervicis reaches the skull, while the capitis stops at the upper cervical transverse processes","Both insert on the mastoid process, and only their origins differ","Both arise from the lumbar spine and insert on the occipital bone"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The two splenius muscles are named for where they end. Capitis means head: the splenius capitis inserts on the mastoid process and occipital bone, so it moves the head. Cervicis means neck: the splenius cervicis arises lower, from T3 to T6, and inserts on the transverse processes of C1 to C3, so it extends and rotates the neck.","notes":["Correct. The capitis inserts on the skull and the cervicis on the transverse processes of C1 to C3.","This reverses the two names. The word capitis always signals a head attachment, and cervicis a neck attachment.","Only the splenius capitis reaches the mastoid process. The splenius cervicis inserts on cervical transverse processes, so their insertions differ, not just their origins.","Neither splenius arises from the lumbar spine. Both come from the upper thoracic and lower cervical spinous processes, and the capitis also from the ligamentum nuchae."]},{"id":"N-m3-back-thorax-muscles-18","dok":1,"q":"Which deep back muscle runs from the transverse processes of C7 to T12 up to the occipital bone and the spinous processes above?","a":"The semispinalis.","options":["Multifidus","Spinalis","Splenius capitis","Semispinalis"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The semispinalis belongs to the deep layer of back muscles that run from transverse processes up toward spinous processes. Its fibers start on the transverse processes of C7 to T12 and climb several segments to reach the spinous processes above, with its uppermost part inserting on the occipital bone. It extends the head and the vertebral column.","notes":["The multifidus also runs from lower attachments up to spinous processes, but it starts on the sacrum, ilium, and transverse processes and spans only two to four segments. Pick it for the short segmental stabilizer of the spine.","The spinalis is the medial erector spinae column, running along the spinous processes themselves rather than from transverse processes. Pick it for the most medial erector spinae column.","The splenius capitis reaches the occipital bone too, but it arises from the ligamentum nuchae and spinous processes of C7 to T3, not from transverse processes.","Correct. The semispinalis runs from transverse processes of C7 to T12 to the occipital bone and spinous processes above."]},{"id":"N-m3-back-thorax-muscles-19","dok":1,"q":"Which deep back muscle spans two to four vertebral segments from the sacrum, ilium, and transverse processes to the spinous processes above, stabilizing the spine segment by segment?","a":"The multifidus.","options":["Quadratus lumborum","Longissimus","Multifidus","Interspinales"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The multifidus lies deep to the erector spinae, packed into the groove beside the spinous processes. Its many bundles each run from the sacrum, ilium, or a transverse process up to a spinous process two to four segments higher. That short span lets it extend and rotate the spine while holding each vertebra steady on the one below.","notes":["The quadratus lumborum runs from the iliac crest to rib 12 and the lumbar transverse processes, and it laterally flexes the trunk. It would be the answer for the muscle of the posterior abdominal wall.","The longissimus is the intermediate erector spinae column, a long vertical muscle that reaches up to the mastoid process. Pick it for the long middle column, not a short segmental one.","Correct. The multifidus is the short, deep, segmental stabilizer of the vertebral column.","The interspinales are tiny muscles linking each spinous process to the next one only. Pick them for a muscle spanning a single segment between spinous processes."]},{"id":"N-m3-back-thorax-muscles-20","dok":1,"q":"Which small deep back muscles connect the spinous process of one vertebra to the spinous process of the next?","a":"The interspinales.","options":["Interspinales","Rotatores","Intertransversarii","Multifidus"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The interspinales are among the smallest and deepest muscles of the back. Their name is a direct description: inter means between, and spinales refers to the spinous processes, so each one runs between two neighboring spinous processes. They help steady and extend the spine one segment at a time.","notes":["Correct. The interspinales link adjacent spinous processes.","The rotatores are small deep muscles that run from a transverse process up and medially to the base of the spinous process above. Pick them for the deepest muscles that help rotate the vertebrae.","The intertransversarii run between neighboring transverse processes, not spinous processes. Pick them if the muscle bridged one transverse process to the next.","The multifidus is larger and spans two to four segments from transverse processes, sacrum, and ilium to spinous processes. It would be the answer for a multi-segment stabilizer."]},{"id":"N-m3-back-thorax-muscles-21","dok":2,"q":"A lab tag sits on a tiny muscle bridging the transverse process of one lumbar vertebra to the transverse process of the next. Which muscle is tagged?","a":"An intertransversarius (the intertransversarii).","options":["Rotatores","Intertransversarii","Interspinales","Quadratus lumborum"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The intertransversarii take their name from their position: between (inter) the transverse processes. Each short slip spans one segment from one transverse process to the next, so a muscle bridging two neighboring transverse processes is an intertransversarius. They help bend the spine to the side and steady it.","notes":["The rotatores run from a transverse process to the spinous process of the vertebra above, so they cross from a lateral point toward the midline. Pick them for a muscle linking a transverse process to the spinous process above.","Correct. The intertransversarii link adjacent transverse processes.","The interspinales connect neighboring spinous processes in the midline, not transverse processes. Pick them if the tag were between two spinous processes.","The quadratus lumborum does attach to the lumbar transverse processes, but it is a large muscle that runs from the iliac crest to rib 12, not a tiny single-segment slip. Pick it for the deep muscle between the iliac crest and the last rib."]},{"id":"N-m3-back-thorax-muscles-22","dok":2,"q":"A patient has pain when bending the trunk to one side. The tender muscle lies deep to the erector spinae and runs from the iliac crest only as far as rib 12 and the transverse processes of L1 to L4. Which muscle is it?","a":"The quadratus lumborum.","options":["Quadratus lumborum","Latissimus dorsi","Iliocostalis","Serratus posterior inferior"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The quadratus lumborum is a four-sided muscle in the posterior abdominal wall, deep to the erector spinae. It arises from the iliac crest and iliolumbar ligament and inserts on rib 12 and the transverse processes of L1 to L4. When one side contracts it bends the trunk laterally, which is why side-bending hurts when it is strained.","notes":["Correct. The quadratus lumborum connects the iliac crest to rib 12 and the lumbar transverse processes and bends the trunk to the side.","The latissimus dorsi is a superficial muscle that runs from the lower back and iliac crest up to the humerus. Pick it for the muscle that extends and adducts the arm.","The iliocostalis is the lateral column of the erector spinae, so it lies superficial to the tender muscle, and it continues up the back along the angles of many ribs. Pick it for the most lateral erector spinae column.","The serratus posterior inferior runs from the spinous processes of T11 to L2 to ribs 9 to 12 and depresses those ribs. Pick it for the thin muscle deep to the latissimus dorsi on the lower ribs."]},{"id":"N-m3-back-thorax-muscles-23","dok":2,"q":"Of these back muscles, which lies deepest, closest to the vertebrae?","a":"The multifidus.","options":["Rhomboid major","Longissimus","Trapezius","Multifidus"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The back is built in layers. The trapezius is superficial, the rhomboids lie just under it, the erector spinae columns such as the longissimus lie deeper along the spine, and the multifidus is deeper still, tucked against the laminae and spinous processes. Only the tiny rotatores, interspinales, and intertransversarii lie closer to the bone.","notes":["The rhomboid major lies just deep to the trapezius but is still a superficial back muscle that attaches to the scapula. Pick it for the second layer of the upper back.","The longissimus is an erector spinae column, deep to the scapular muscles but superficial to the multifidus. Pick it for the intermediate column of the erector spinae.","The trapezius is the most superficial muscle of the upper back, lying just under the skin and fascia. Pick it for the outermost layer.","Correct. The multifidus lies beneath the erector spinae, directly against the vertebrae."]},{"id":"N-m3-back-thorax-muscles-24","dok":1,"q":"In which direction do the fibers of the external intercostals run?","a":"Downward and forward, like hands in front pockets.","options":["Horizontally along the rib","Downward and backward","Straight up and down","Downward and forward"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The external intercostals run from the inferior border of one rib to the superior border of the rib below. Their fibers slant downward and forward, toward the front of the body, the same direction your fingers point when you slide your hands into your front pockets. That slant lets them lift the ribs during inspiration.","notes":["A horizontal course would follow the rib rather than cross the space between ribs, so it could not connect one rib to the next. This is a misreading of how the intercostal space is spanned.","Downward and backward is the direction of the internal intercostals, which lie just deep and run at right angles to the external layer. Pick it for the internal intercostals.","No intercostal layer runs straight vertically. Students sometimes guess this because the muscle simply connects one rib to the next, but the fibers slant.","Correct. External intercostal fibers run downward and forward, the hands-in-pockets direction."],"comp":103,"competencyId":"resp-thoracic-diaphragm"},{"id":"N-m3-back-thorax-muscles-25","dok":1,"q":"In which direction do the fibers of the internal intercostals run?","a":"Downward and backward, at right angles to the external intercostals.","options":["Downward and backward","Straight up and down","Horizontally along the rib","Downward and forward"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The internal intercostals lie just deep to the external layer and run from the superior border of the rib below to the inferior border of the rib above. Their fibers run at right angles to the external intercostals, so they slant downward and backward. Crossing the external layer this way, like the plies of plywood, also strengthens the wall of each intercostal space.","notes":["Correct. Internal intercostal fibers run downward and backward, perpendicular to the external layer.","No intercostal layer runs straight vertically. The fibers of each layer slant across the intercostal space.","A horizontal course would follow the rib instead of spanning the gap between two ribs. Intercostal fibers always cross the space at a slant.","Downward and forward is the hands-in-pockets direction of the external intercostals. Pick it for the superficial layer of the intercostal space."],"comp":103,"competencyId":"resp-thoracic-diaphragm"},{"id":"N-m3-back-thorax-muscles-26","dok":2,"q":"During a forceful exhalation, such as blowing out birthday candles, which muscle layer pulls the ribs down?","a":"The internal intercostals.","options":["Diaphragm","Internal intercostals","Serratus posterior superior","External intercostals"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Quiet exhalation is mostly passive, but a forceful exhalation needs muscles that pull the ribs down and shrink the chest. The internal intercostals depress the ribs, and their downward and backward fibers suit that job. The innermost intercostals and the transversus thoracis act with them.","notes":["The diaphragm is the chief muscle of inspiration. When it contracts it flattens and enlarges the chest, so it would be the answer for quiet breathing in.","Correct. The internal intercostals depress the ribs during forced exhalation.","The serratus posterior superior elevates ribs 2 to 5, which helps inspiration. It would be the answer for a thin upper back muscle that lifts the upper ribs.","The external intercostals elevate the ribs, so they work during inspiration. Pick them for the layer that helps you breathe in."],"comp":103,"competencyId":"resp-thoracic-diaphragm"},{"id":"N-m3-back-thorax-muscles-27","dok":1,"q":"Which intercostal layer is separated from the internal intercostals by the neurovascular bundle?","a":"The innermost intercostals.","options":["External intercostals","Innermost intercostals","Transversus thoracis","Serratus posterior inferior"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Each intercostal space has three muscle layers: external, internal, and innermost. The intercostal vein, artery, and nerve travel in the costal groove between the internal and innermost layers. The innermost intercostals act with the internal intercostals but are separated from them by this neurovascular bundle.","notes":["The external intercostals are the most superficial layer, outside the internal intercostals. The neurovascular bundle does not run beneath them, so they would be the answer for the outer layer that elevates the ribs.","Correct. The innermost intercostals lie deep to the neurovascular bundle.","The transversus thoracis also lies on the inner surface of the chest wall, but it is on the back of the sternum, fanning to costal cartilages 2 to 6. Pick it for the deep muscle of the anterior chest wall.","The serratus posterior inferior is a thin back muscle on the outside of the lower ribs, deep to the latissimus dorsi. Pick it for the muscle that depresses ribs 9 to 12."],"comp":103,"competencyId":"resp-thoracic-diaphragm"},{"id":"N-m3-back-thorax-muscles-28","dok":3,"q":"When placing a needle through an intercostal space to drain fluid from the chest, clinicians insert it just above the upper border of the rib below. Why?","a":"The neurovascular bundle runs in the costal groove along the lower border of each rib.","options":["The neurovascular bundle runs along the lower border of each rib","The external intercostals are missing along the lower border of each rib","The innermost intercostals block the needle near the upper border of each rib","The pleura is absent just above the upper border of each rib"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The intercostal vein, artery, and nerve travel together in the costal groove, a shallow channel on the inner lower edge of each rib. A needle passed just under the rib above would run right into that bundle. Passing just over the top of the rib below keeps the needle as far as possible from the main vessels and nerve of that space.","notes":["Correct. The neurovascular bundle lies in the costal groove on the lower border of each rib, so clinicians stay near the top of the rib below.","The external intercostals attach along the inferior border of the rib above, so they are present there. This choice is a misconception, and the muscle layers are not what clinicians are trying to avoid anyway.","The innermost intercostals are a thin layer that does not block a needle. The real concern is the vessels and nerve, not the muscle layer.","The pleura lines the whole inner chest wall, so a needle entering the pleural cavity always passes through it. Thinking the pleura has gaps leads students to this choice."],"comp":103,"competencyId":"resp-thoracic-diaphragm"},{"id":"N-m3-back-thorax-muscles-29","dok":2,"q":"Which sequence lists the muscle layers of an intercostal space from superficial to deep?","a":"External intercostals, internal intercostals, innermost intercostals.","options":["External, internal, innermost intercostals","Innermost, internal, external intercostals","Internal, external, innermost intercostals","External, innermost, internal intercostals"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The intercostal space between two ribs is filled by three sheets of muscle. The external intercostals lie outermost, the internal intercostals lie just deep to them with fibers at right angles, and the innermost intercostals form the deepest layer, separated from the internal layer by the neurovascular bundle. Their names match their order from the outside in.","notes":["Correct. External, then internal, then innermost, from the skin inward.","This is the correct list read from deep to superficial. It would be the answer if the question asked for the order from the inside of the chest outward.","This places the internal layer outside the external layer. The external intercostals are always the most superficial, as their name says.","This swaps the two deeper layers. The innermost intercostals are the deepest, and the neurovascular bundle runs between them and the internal layer."],"comp":103,"competencyId":"resp-thoracic-diaphragm"},{"id":"N-m3-back-thorax-muscles-30","dok":2,"q":"As you take a deep breath in, which intercostal layer helps lift the ribs, and which way do its fibers run?","a":"The external intercostals, with fibers running downward and forward.","options":["Internal intercostals, fibers downward and backward","External intercostals, fibers downward and backward","External intercostals, fibers downward and forward","Internal intercostals, fibers downward and forward"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The external intercostals elevate the ribs, which widens the chest and helps draw air in. Their fibers run downward and forward, the hands-in-pockets direction. The internal intercostals, whose fibers run at right angles downward and backward, do the opposite and depress the ribs in forced exhalation.","notes":["This pairing is accurate for the internal intercostals, but they depress the ribs during forced exhalation. Pick it for the layer that helps you breathe out hard.","The muscle is right but the direction is not. Downward and backward is the fiber direction of the internal intercostals.","Correct. The external intercostals lift the ribs, and their fibers run downward and forward.","The internal intercostals do not lift the ribs, and their fibers do not run downward and forward. This mixes the internal layer with the external fiber direction."],"comp":103,"competencyId":"resp-thoracic-diaphragm"},{"id":"N-m3-back-thorax-muscles-31","dok":1,"q":"Which thoracic wall muscle lies on the inner surface of the anterior chest wall, fanning from the back of the lower sternum to costal cartilages 2 to 6?","a":"The transversus thoracis.","options":["Innermost intercostals","Subclavius","Pectoralis minor","Transversus thoracis"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The transversus thoracis is a thin muscle you only see from inside the chest. It arises from the posterior surface of the lower sternum and fans upward and laterally to the costal cartilages of ribs 2 to 6. It depresses the ribs, so it works with the internal and innermost intercostals during forced exhalation.","notes":["The innermost intercostals are also deep, but they run between the internal surfaces of neighboring ribs within each intercostal space, not from the sternum. Pick them for the deepest layer of an intercostal space.","The subclavius is a small muscle under the clavicle that runs from rib 1 to the clavicle. Pick it for the muscle that steadies the clavicle.","The pectoralis minor lies on the outside of the chest wall, deep to the pectoralis major, running from ribs 3 to 5 to the coracoid process. Pick it for the muscle that depresses and protracts the scapula.","Correct. The transversus thoracis fans from the back of the sternum to costal cartilages 2 to 6."],"comp":103,"competencyId":"resp-thoracic-diaphragm"},{"id":"N-m3-back-thorax-muscles-32","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares the serratus posterior superior and the serratus posterior inferior?","a":"The superior elevates ribs 2 to 5, while the inferior depresses ribs 9 to 12.","options":["The superior depresses ribs 2 to 5, while the inferior elevates ribs 9 to 12","The superior elevates ribs 2 to 5, while the inferior depresses ribs 9 to 12","Both elevate the ribs, the superior on ribs 2 to 5 and the inferior on ribs 9 to 12","Both depress the ribs, the superior on ribs 2 to 5 and the inferior on ribs 9 to 12"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Both serratus posterior muscles are thin sheets on the back of the rib cage, named for their sawtooth slips. The superior one runs from the spinous processes of C7 to T3 down and laterally to ribs 2 to 5, so it pulls those ribs up. The inferior one runs from the spinous processes of T11 to L2 up and laterally to ribs 9 to 12, so it pulls those ribs down. Their actions follow the direction each muscle pulls.","notes":["This reverses both actions. The superior muscle pulls upward on the upper ribs, and the inferior muscle pulls downward on the lower ribs.","Correct. The superior elevates the upper ribs, and the inferior depresses the lower ribs.","The superior muscle does elevate ribs 2 to 5, but the inferior muscle pulls ribs 9 to 12 down toward its lower origin.","The inferior muscle does depress ribs 9 to 12, but the superior muscle pulls ribs 2 to 5 up toward its higher origin."],"comp":103,"competencyId":"resp-thoracic-diaphragm"},{"id":"N-m3-back-thorax-muscles-33","dok":2,"q":"A lab tag sits on a thin muscle deep to the latissimus dorsi, where the thoracic and lumbar regions meet. Its slips run up and laterally from the spinous processes of T11 to L2 to the lowest four ribs. Which muscle is tagged?","a":"The serratus posterior inferior.","options":["Quadratus lumborum","Serratus posterior superior","Serratus posterior inferior","External oblique"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The serratus posterior inferior lies just deep to the latissimus dorsi in the lower thoracic region. It arises from the spinous processes of T11 to L2 and its slips angle upward and laterally to ribs 9 to 12. Its position at the thoracolumbar junction and its attachment to the lowest four ribs identify it.","notes":["The quadratus lumborum lies deeper, in the posterior abdominal wall, and runs from the iliac crest to rib 12 and the lumbar transverse processes. Pick it for the muscle that bends the trunk to the side.","The serratus posterior superior has the same thin, slipped shape, but it lies high on the back, deep to the rhomboids, running from C7 to T3 to ribs 2 to 5. Pick it for the upper partner.","Correct. The serratus posterior inferior runs from T11 to L2 to ribs 9 to 12, deep to the latissimus dorsi.","The external oblique is an abdominal wall muscle on the lateral side of the trunk, arising from the outer surfaces of the lower ribs. Pick it for the most superficial lateral abdominal muscle, not a muscle arising from spinous processes."],"comp":103,"competencyId":"resp-thoracic-diaphragm"},{"id":"N-m3-back-thorax-muscles-34","dok":1,"q":"Which structure passes through the caval opening of the diaphragm at vertebral level T8?","a":"The inferior vena cava.","options":["The inferior vena cava","The aorta","The thoracic duct","The esophagus"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The diaphragm has three main openings at the even levels T8, T10, and T12. The caval opening at T8 is the highest, and its name tells you what passes through it: the inferior vena cava, the large vein returning blood from the lower body to the right atrium. The right phrenic nerve also passes through or near this opening.","notes":["Correct. The caval opening at T8 transmits the inferior vena cava.","The aorta passes through the aortic hiatus at T12, the lowest opening. Pick it for T12.","The thoracic duct travels with the aorta through the aortic hiatus at T12. Pick it if the question asked about the lowest opening.","The esophagus passes through the esophageal hiatus at T10, with the vagus nerves. Pick it for the middle opening."],"comp":103,"competencyId":"resp-thoracic-diaphragm"},{"id":"N-m3-back-thorax-muscles-35","dok":1,"q":"At which vertebral level does the esophageal hiatus pass through the diaphragm?","a":"T10.","options":["T8","T12","T10","L1"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The esophageal hiatus is the middle of the three main diaphragm openings, at T10. It transmits the esophagus along with the vagus nerves on their way to the stomach. The openings follow a simple count: vena cava at 8, esophagus at 10, aorta at 12.","notes":["T8 is the level of the caval opening for the inferior vena cava. Pick it for the highest opening.","T12 is the level of the aortic hiatus, which transmits the aorta, thoracic duct, and azygos vein. Pick it for the lowest opening.","Correct. The esophagus passes through the diaphragm at T10.","L1 lies below all three main openings. Students may choose it because the crura anchor to the upper lumbar vertebrae, but no main opening sits there."],"comp":103,"competencyId":"resp-thoracic-diaphragm"},{"id":"N-m3-back-thorax-muscles-36","dok":1,"q":"Which set of structures passes through the aortic hiatus at T12?","a":"The aorta, thoracic duct, and azygos vein.","options":["The esophagus and the vagus nerves","The trachea, aorta, and pulmonary trunk","The aorta, thoracic duct, and azygos vein","The inferior vena cava and right phrenic nerve"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The aortic hiatus is the lowest of the three main openings, at T12, set behind the diaphragm between the two crura. The descending aorta passes through it into the abdomen. Two structures heading up into the thorax travel with it: the thoracic duct carrying lymph, and the azygos vein.","notes":["The esophagus and vagus nerves pass through the esophageal hiatus at T10. Pick them for the middle opening.","The trachea and pulmonary trunk stay in the thorax and never cross the diaphragm. Students sometimes pick this because the aorta is listed, but the other two do not belong.","Correct. The aortic hiatus transmits the aorta, the thoracic duct, and the azygos vein.","The inferior vena cava and the right phrenic nerve pass through the caval opening at T8. Pick them for the highest opening."],"comp":103,"competencyId":"resp-thoracic-diaphragm"},{"id":"N-m3-back-thorax-muscles-37","dok":1,"q":"What is the central tendon of the diaphragm?","a":"The flat aponeurosis at the top of the dome where the muscle fibers insert.","options":["The pair of tendons anchored to the lumbar vertebrae","The muscle slip that arises from the xiphoid process","The opening at T12 that transmits the aorta","The aponeurosis where the muscle fibers insert"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The diaphragm's muscle fibers arise around the margin of the lower opening of the rib cage, from the xiphoid process, the inner surfaces of ribs 7 to 12, and the lumbar vertebrae. All of them converge on the central tendon, a flat sheet of dense connective tissue (an aponeurosis) at the top of the dome. When the fibers contract, they pull the central tendon down and flatten the dome.","notes":["The tendons anchored to the upper lumbar vertebrae are the right and left crura, the lumbar origin of the diaphragm. Pick them for the posterior attachments.","The slip from the xiphoid process is the sternal part of the diaphragm, one of its origins. Pick it if the question asked which part arises from the sternum.","The opening at T12 for the aorta is the aortic hiatus, which lies between the crura. Pick it for a question about what the aorta passes through.","Correct. The central tendon is the aponeurosis that receives all the diaphragm's muscle fibers."],"comp":103,"competencyId":"resp-thoracic-diaphragm"},{"id":"N-m3-back-thorax-muscles-38","dok":1,"q":"Where do the right and left crura anchor the diaphragm?","a":"To the bodies of the upper lumbar vertebrae.","options":["To the central tendon","To the inner surfaces of ribs 7 to 12","To the upper lumbar vertebrae","To the xiphoid process"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The diaphragm has three origins: sternal (the xiphoid process), costal (the inner surfaces of the lower ribs), and lumbar. The lumbar origin is formed by the right and left crura, two tendinous legs (crus means leg) that anchor to the bodies of the upper lumbar vertebrae. The right crus is the larger of the two.","notes":["The central tendon is where the muscle fibers insert, not where they anchor to bone. Pick it for the insertion of the diaphragm.","The inner surfaces of ribs 7 to 12 are the origin of the costal part, the largest muscular part of the diaphragm. Pick it for the costal part.","Correct. The crura anchor the diaphragm to the upper lumbar vertebrae.","The xiphoid process is the origin of the sternal part of the diaphragm, at the front. Pick it for the sternal part."],"comp":103,"competencyId":"resp-thoracic-diaphragm"},{"id":"N-m3-back-thorax-muscles-39","dok":2,"q":"What happens to the diaphragm and the thoracic cavity when the diaphragm contracts?","a":"The diaphragm flattens and descends, increasing the vertical dimension of the thoracic cavity.","options":["It pulls the ribs inward and narrows the cavity","It domes upward and shortens the thoracic cavity","It flattens and lengthens the thoracic cavity","It relaxes and pushes air out of the lungs"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"At rest the diaphragm is a dome bulging up into the thorax. When its fibers contract they pull the central tendon down, so the dome flattens and moves inferiorly. That increases the height of the thoracic cavity, the lungs expand with it, and air flows in, which is why the diaphragm is the chief muscle of quiet inspiration.","notes":["Pulling the ribs inward and down is the job of muscles of forced exhalation such as the internal intercostals. The diaphragm acts vertically, not by squeezing the ribs.","Doming upward is what happens when the diaphragm relaxes, which shortens the cavity during exhalation. Pick this for relaxation, not contraction.","Correct. Contraction flattens the dome and makes the thoracic cavity taller.","A contracting muscle is not relaxing. Relaxation of the diaphragm does help push air out, but that describes exhalation, not contraction."],"comp":103,"competencyId":"resp-thoracic-diaphragm"},{"id":"N-m3-back-thorax-muscles-40","dok":3,"q":"A mass presses on the opening of the diaphragm at vertebral level T10. Which structure passing through that opening is most directly compressed, causing trouble swallowing?","a":"The esophagus.","options":["The thoracic duct","The inferior vena cava","The esophagus","The aorta"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The opening at T10 is the esophageal hiatus, which transmits the esophagus and the vagus nerves. A mass narrowing this opening squeezes the esophagus as food passes from the thorax to the stomach, which makes swallowing difficult. Linking the vertebral level to the opening, and the opening to its contents, solves this kind of question.","notes":["The thoracic duct also passes through the aortic hiatus at T12, with the aorta. It would be the answer for a lymph flow problem at T12.","The inferior vena cava passes through the caval opening at T8, higher than this mass. Compression there would affect venous return from the lower body, not swallowing.","Correct. The esophagus passes through the esophageal hiatus at T10.","The aorta passes through the aortic hiatus at T12, below this level. Pick it for a mass at T12."],"comp":103,"competencyId":"resp-thoracic-diaphragm"},{"id":"N-m3-back-thorax-muscles-41","dok":2,"q":"In a hiatal hernia, part of the stomach slides up into the thorax through an opening in the diaphragm. Which opening is it?","a":"The esophageal hiatus.","options":["An intercostal space","The esophageal hiatus","The caval opening","The aortic hiatus"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The stomach lies just below the diaphragm, connected to the esophagus where the esophagus passes through the esophageal hiatus at T10. Because the stomach already sits at this opening, a widened hiatus lets part of the stomach push up alongside the esophagus into the thorax. The word hiatal in hiatal hernia points to this esophageal hiatus.","notes":["An intercostal space is the gap between two ribs, filled by the intercostal muscles. It is not an opening in the diaphragm, so pick it only for a question about where a chest needle is inserted.","Correct. A hiatal hernia pushes the stomach up through the esophageal hiatus.","The caval opening at T8 lies in the central tendon and transmits the inferior vena cava. The stomach does not sit beside it, so it would be the answer only for the vein's passage.","The aortic hiatus at T12 lies behind the diaphragm between the crura and transmits the aorta. Pick it for the opening used by the aorta and thoracic duct."],"comp":103,"competencyId":"resp-thoracic-diaphragm"},{"id":"N-m3-back-thorax-muscles-42","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares the pectoralis major and the pectoralis minor?","a":"The major inserts on the humerus and moves the arm; the minor inserts on the coracoid process and moves the scapula.","options":["The major inserts on the humerus and moves the arm; the minor inserts on the coracoid and moves the scapula","Both insert on the coracoid, and the major simply lies superficial to the minor","The major inserts on the coracoid and moves the scapula; the minor inserts on the humerus and moves the arm","Both insert on the humerus, and the minor simply lies deep to the major"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The pectoralis major arises from the medial clavicle, sternum, and costal cartilages 1 to 6, and inserts on the lateral lip of the intertubercular sulcus of the humerus, so it flexes, adducts, and medially rotates the arm. The pectoralis minor lies deep to it, running from ribs 3 to 5 to the coracoid process. Because it attaches to the scapula, it depresses and protracts the scapula rather than moving the arm.","notes":["Correct. The major reaches the humerus and moves the arm, and the minor reaches the coracoid process and moves the scapula.","The major does lie superficial to the minor, but it does not insert on the coracoid process. It reaches the intertubercular sulcus of the humerus.","This swaps the two insertions. The larger muscle reaches the humerus, and the smaller, deeper muscle stops at the coracoid process.","The minor does lie deep to the major, but it does not reach the humerus. It inserts on the coracoid process."],"comp":85,"competencyId":"w4-lab-chest-anterior-arm"},{"id":"N-m3-back-thorax-muscles-43","dok":1,"q":"Where does the pectoralis minor insert?","a":"The coracoid process of the scapula.","options":["The inferior surface of the clavicle","The lateral lip of the intertubercular sulcus","The coracoid process of the scapula","The anterior surface of the medial border of the scapula"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The pectoralis minor is a thin triangular muscle hidden deep to the pectoralis major. It arises from ribs 3 to 5 and converges upward on the coracoid process, the hook-shaped projection on the front of the scapula. Pulling on the coracoid, it depresses and protracts the scapula.","notes":["The inferior surface of the clavicle is the insertion of the subclavius. Pick it for the small muscle running from rib 1 to the clavicle.","The lateral lip of the intertubercular sulcus is the insertion of the pectoralis major. Pick it for the large superficial chest muscle.","Correct. The pectoralis minor inserts on the coracoid process.","The anterior surface of the medial border of the scapula is the insertion of the serratus anterior. Pick it for the muscle that holds the scapula against the chest wall."],"comp":85,"competencyId":"w4-lab-chest-anterior-arm"},{"id":"N-m3-back-thorax-muscles-44","dok":2,"q":"Which actions does the pectoralis minor produce?","a":"It depresses and protracts the scapula, and can elevate the ribs when the scapula is fixed.","options":["Depresses and protracts the scapula","Depresses and steadies the clavicle","Retracts the scapula and rotates the glenoid down","Flexes, adducts, and medially rotates the arm"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The pectoralis minor runs from ribs 3 to 5 up to the coracoid process. Pulling the coracoid down and forward, it depresses the scapula and draws it forward around the chest wall, which is protraction. When the scapula is held still, the same pull works in reverse and lifts the ribs, which is why it can assist forced inspiration.","notes":["Correct. The pectoralis minor depresses and protracts the scapula.","Depressing and steadying the clavicle is the job of the subclavius, the small muscle under the clavicle. Pick it for the subclavius.","Retraction with downward rotation of the glenoid is the action of the rhomboids on the back. The pectoralis minor pulls the scapula forward, the opposite of retraction.","Flexion, adduction, and medial rotation of the arm belong to the pectoralis major, which inserts on the humerus. Pick this set for the larger chest muscle."],"comp":85,"competencyId":"w4-lab-chest-anterior-arm"},{"id":"N-m3-back-thorax-muscles-45","dok":3,"q":"When a patient pushes against a wall, the medial border of one scapula lifts away from the ribs like a wing. Which muscle is not doing its job?","a":"The serratus anterior.","options":["Pectoralis major","Serratus anterior","Latissimus dorsi","Pectoralis minor"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The serratus anterior runs from the outer surfaces of the upper eight or nine ribs to the anterior surface of the medial border of the scapula. That position lets it pull the scapula forward and hold it flat against the chest wall. When it fails, pushing forward makes the medial border lift off the back, a sign called a winged scapula.","notes":["The pectoralis major moves the humerus, not the scapula, so weakness would affect flexing and adducting the arm. Pick it for a weak bench press or hug.","Correct. Loss of the serratus anterior lets the medial border of the scapula wing away from the chest wall.","The latissimus dorsi extends, adducts, and medially rotates the arm. Weakness would make pulling down hard, not cause the scapula to wing.","The pectoralis minor pulls the coracoid process down and forward, but it does not hold the medial border against the ribs. Pick it for the muscle that depresses and protracts the scapula from the coracoid."],"comp":85,"competencyId":"w4-lab-chest-anterior-arm"},{"id":"N-m3-back-thorax-muscles-46","dok":1,"q":"Which small muscle runs from rib 1 to the inferior surface of the clavicle and depresses and steadies the clavicle?","a":"The subclavius.","options":["Pectoralis minor","Serratus anterior","Subclavius","Levator scapulae"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The subclavius is named for its position under (sub) the clavicle. It arises from rib 1 near its costal cartilage and inserts on the inferior surface of the clavicle. It pulls the clavicle down and steadies it at the sternoclavicular joint during shoulder movement.","notes":["The pectoralis minor also lies deep to the pectoralis major, but it runs from ribs 3 to 5 to the coracoid process. Pick it for the muscle that depresses and protracts the scapula.","The serratus anterior runs from the lateral ribs to the medial border of the scapula. Pick it for the muscle that holds the scapula against the chest wall.","Correct. The subclavius runs from rib 1 to the underside of the clavicle.","The levator scapulae runs from the cervical transverse processes to the scapula and elevates it. It moves the scapula upward, the opposite direction from this muscle's pull on the clavicle."],"comp":85,"competencyId":"w4-lab-chest-anterior-arm"},{"id":"N-m3-back-thorax-muscles-47","dok":1,"q":"Which sheet of deep fascia encloses the subclavius and the pectoralis minor, lying deep to the pectoralis major?","a":"The clavipectoral fascia.","options":["Ligamentum nuchae","Bicipital aponeurosis","Clavipectoral fascia","Thoracolumbar fascia"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The clavipectoral fascia hangs down from the clavicle, wrapping the subclavius and then the pectoralis minor. Its name describes its span from the clavicle (clavi) down over the chest (pectoral). It lies in the layer just deep to the pectoralis major, so it is exposed when the pectoralis major is reflected.","notes":["The ligamentum nuchae is the midline ligament on the back of the neck that gives origin to the trapezius and splenius capitis. Pick it for the neck ligament.","The bicipital aponeurosis is a flat band from the biceps tendon that fans into the fascia of the medial forearm. Pick it for the structure at the front of the elbow.","Correct. The clavipectoral fascia encloses the subclavius and the pectoralis minor.","The thoracolumbar fascia covers the deep muscles of the lower back and gives origin to the latissimus dorsi. Pick it for the large fascia of the lumbar region."],"comp":85,"competencyId":"w4-lab-chest-anterior-arm"},{"id":"N-m3-back-thorax-muscles-48","dok":2,"q":"On the lateral chest wall, the lower slips of the serratus anterior interlock with the upper slips of which muscle?","a":"The external oblique.","options":["Internal intercostals","External oblique","Pectoralis minor","Latissimus dorsi"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The external oblique, the most superficial lateral abdominal muscle, arises by fleshy slips from the outer surfaces of the lower ribs. Its upper slips alternate with the lower slips of the serratus anterior along the lateral chest wall, like interlocking fingers. That zigzag line is a useful landmark for telling the two muscles apart on a model or cadaver.","notes":["The internal intercostals lie deep within the intercostal spaces, between the ribs, not on the outer surface where the serratus slips attach. Pick them for the muscles of forced exhalation.","Correct. The upper slips of the external oblique interlock with the lower slips of the serratus anterior.","The pectoralis minor arises from ribs 3 to 5 on the front of the chest, deep to the pectoralis major. It does not form a zigzag with the serratus anterior, so pick it for the muscle inserting on the coracoid process.","The latissimus dorsi covers the lower back and sweeps toward the axilla over the inferior angle of the scapula. Pick it for the large superficial muscle of the lower back."],"comp":90,"competencyId":"w5-lab-abdominal-wall"},{"id":"N-m3-back-thorax-muscles-49","dok":2,"q":"After reflecting the pectoralis major on a cadaver, you find a thin triangular muscle running from ribs 3 to 5 up to a hook-like process on the front of the scapula. Which muscle is it?","a":"The pectoralis minor.","options":["Serratus anterior","Subclavius","Pectoralis minor","Coracobrachialis"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The pectoralis minor lies immediately deep to the pectoralis major. It arises from ribs 3 to 5 and its fibers converge upward on the coracoid process, the hook-like projection on the front of the scapula. Its triangular shape and coracoid insertion identify it.","notes":["The serratus anterior arises from the ribs on the lateral chest wall and inserts on the medial border of the scapula, not the coracoid process. Pick it for the sawtooth muscle on the side of the chest.","The subclavius also lies deep to the pectoralis major, but it is a small cylinder under the clavicle running from rib 1. Pick it for the muscle attached to the underside of the clavicle.","Correct. The pectoralis minor runs from ribs 3 to 5 to the coracoid process.","The coracobrachialis also attaches to the coracoid process, but it arises there and runs down to the medial shaft of the humerus. Pick it for the arm muscle that flexes and adducts the arm."],"comp":85,"competencyId":"w4-lab-chest-anterior-arm"},{"id":"N-m3-back-thorax-muscles-50","dok":3,"q":"To reach high overhead, the scapula must rotate so the glenoid cavity turns upward. Which pair of muscles produces this upward rotation?","a":"The trapezius and the serratus anterior.","options":["Rhomboid major and levator scapulae","Trapezius and serratus anterior","Latissimus dorsi and teres major","Pectoralis minor and subclavius"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Raising the arm fully overhead needs the scapula to swing so the glenoid cavity faces upward. The upper and lower fibers of the trapezius pull the scapula from above and from the spine, while the serratus anterior pulls its inferior angle forward around the chest wall. Working together, they rotate the glenoid upward.","notes":["The rhomboids and levator scapulae rotate the glenoid downward, so they are the antagonists here. Pick this pair for lowering the arm back down against resistance.","Correct. The trapezius and the serratus anterior rotate the glenoid upward together.","The latissimus dorsi and teres major act on the humerus, extending, adducting, and medially rotating the arm. Pick them for pulling the arm down from overhead.","The pectoralis minor pulls the scapula down and forward, and the subclavius steadies the clavicle. Neither produces upward rotation, so pick them for depression of the shoulder girdle."],"comp":87,"competencyId":"w4-lab-posterior-shoulder-cuff"}]},{"id":"m3-arm-muscles","title":"Muscles of the arm (brachium)","summary":"anterior and posterior arm muscles, compartments, actions at shoulder and elbow","mw":"2026-10-07","tr":"2026-10-08","cards":[{"id":"L04306","dok":1,"q":"Which anterior arm muscle flexes the elbow and also supinates the forearm?","a":"Biceps brachii.","options":["Brachialis","Biceps brachii","Triceps brachii","Coracobrachialis"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Biceps brachii has a long head from the supraglenoid tubercle and a short head from the coracoid process, and it inserts on the radial tuberosity and the bicipital aponeurosis. Because it attaches to the radius, it can rotate the forearm into the palm-up position (supination) as well as flex the elbow. This is why the biceps is strongest at elbow flexion when the palm faces up.","notes":["Brachialis flexes the elbow but inserts on the ulna, which cannot rotate, so it has no role in supination. Pick it if the question asked for the pure elbow flexor that lies deep to the biceps.","Correct. Biceps brachii inserts on the radius, so it flexes the elbow and supinates the forearm.","Triceps brachii is in the posterior compartment and extends the elbow, the opposite movement. Choose it if the question asked which arm muscle extends the elbow.","Coracobrachialis runs from the coracoid process to the humeral shaft and flexes and adducts the arm at the shoulder. It does not cross the elbow, so it would be the answer only for a shoulder movement."],"lt":"t-w4-lab-chest-anterior-arm","lid":"w4-lab-chest-anterior-arm-r3","comp":85,"competencyId":"w4-lab-chest-anterior-arm"},{"id":"L04307","dok":2,"q":"Which anterior arm muscle lies deep to the biceps brachii, inserts on the ulna, and acts only to flex the elbow?","a":"Brachialis.","options":["Brachialis","Pronator teres","Coracobrachialis","Anconeus"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Brachialis arises from the distal anterior humerus, lies directly under the biceps, and inserts on the coronoid process and tuberosity of the ulna. Since the ulna does not rotate, the brachialis cannot supinate or pronate; it only flexes the elbow. That is why it flexes the elbow with the same strength whether the palm faces up or down.","notes":["Correct. Brachialis lies under the biceps, inserts on the ulna, and is a pure elbow flexor.","Pronator teres is in the anterior forearm and inserts on the radius, so it pronates the forearm and only assists elbow flexion. Choose it if the question asked which forearm muscle turns the palm down.","Coracobrachialis lies in the anterior arm, but it runs from the coracoid process to the humeral shaft and acts at the shoulder. Pick it if the question asked which anterior arm muscle flexes and adducts the arm.","Anconeus is a small posterior muscle near the olecranon that assists elbow extension. It would be the answer if the question asked which small muscle helps the triceps."],"lt":"t-w4-lab-chest-anterior-arm","lid":"w4-lab-chest-anterior-arm-r4","comp":85,"competencyId":"w4-lab-chest-anterior-arm"},{"id":"L04308","dok":2,"q":"A student lifts a grocery bag by bending the elbow, palm up. Which two anterior arm muscles work together to flex the elbow?","a":"Biceps brachii and brachialis.","options":["Coracobrachialis and anconeus","Biceps brachii and brachialis","Brachialis and triceps brachii","Biceps brachii and coracobrachialis"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The anterior compartment of the arm holds the elbow flexors. Biceps brachii and brachialis both cross the front of the elbow and pull the forearm toward the arm, so they work together when lifting a bag. Coracobrachialis also lies in the anterior arm, but it ends on the humerus and acts only at the shoulder.","notes":["Neither of these flexes the elbow. Coracobrachialis acts at the shoulder, and anconeus is a small posterior muscle that assists elbow extension.","Correct. Biceps brachii and brachialis are the elbow flexors of the anterior arm.","Brachialis is correct, but triceps brachii is the posterior arm muscle that extends the elbow, so it acts as the antagonist here. Choose the triceps if the question asked which muscle opposes elbow flexion.","Biceps brachii is correct, but coracobrachialis inserts on the humeral shaft and never crosses the elbow. Pick coracobrachialis if the question asked which anterior arm muscle flexes and adducts the arm at the shoulder."],"lt":"t-w4-lab-chest-anterior-arm","lid":"w4-lab-chest-anterior-arm-r5","comp":85,"competencyId":"w4-lab-chest-anterior-arm"},{"id":"N-m3-arm-muscles-1","dok":1,"q":"Which rotator cuff muscle fills the fossa above the spine of the scapula?","a":"The supraspinatus.","options":["Subscapularis","Infraspinatus","Supraspinatus","Teres minor"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The supraspinatus is named for its position: supra means above, and spinatus refers to the spine of the scapula. It arises from the supraspinous fossa, passes under the acromion, and inserts on the greater tubercle of the humerus. It starts the movement of abduction, lifting the arm out to the side.","notes":["The subscapularis lies on the anterior surface of the scapula, in the subscapular fossa against the ribs. Pick it for the only cuff muscle on the front of the scapula.","The infraspinatus fills the fossa below (infra) the scapular spine. Pick it for the muscle in the infraspinous fossa.","Correct. The supraspinatus arises from the supraspinous fossa above the scapular spine.","The teres minor arises from the lateral border of the scapula, not a fossa. Pick it for the small cuff muscle along the lateral border."],"comp":87,"competencyId":"w4-lab-posterior-shoulder-cuff"},{"id":"N-m3-arm-muscles-2","dok":1,"q":"Which rotator cuff muscle inserts on the lesser tubercle of the humerus?","a":"The subscapularis.","options":["Teres minor","Infraspinatus","Supraspinatus","Subscapularis"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Three of the four rotator cuff muscles insert on the greater tubercle, the large lateral bump on the proximal humerus. Only the subscapularis, which lies on the anterior surface of the scapula, reaches the lesser tubercle on the front of the humerus. Pulling from the front, it medially rotates the arm.","notes":["The teres minor inserts on the greater tubercle, at its lowest part. Pick it for the lateral rotator from the lateral border of the scapula.","The infraspinatus inserts on the greater tubercle, just below the supraspinatus. Pick it for a lateral rotator from the infraspinous fossa.","The supraspinatus inserts on the greater tubercle, at its highest part. Pick it for the cuff muscle that starts abduction.","Correct. The subscapularis is the only cuff muscle that inserts on the lesser tubercle."],"comp":87,"competencyId":"w4-lab-posterior-shoulder-cuff"},{"id":"N-m3-arm-muscles-3","dok":1,"q":"What is the main action of the infraspinatus?","a":"It laterally rotates the arm.","options":["Extends the elbow","Initiates abduction of the arm","Laterally rotates the arm","Medially rotates the arm"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The infraspinatus arises from the infraspinous fossa on the back of the scapula and inserts on the greater tubercle. Because it wraps around the back of the humeral head, its pull turns the arm outward, which is lateral rotation. It shares this action with the teres minor just below it.","notes":["Extending the elbow is the action of the triceps brachii, which crosses the elbow to the olecranon. The infraspinatus does not cross the elbow, so pick this for the triceps.","Initiating abduction is the job of the supraspinatus, which sits above the scapular spine. Pick it for the supraspinatus.","Correct. The infraspinatus laterally rotates the arm.","Medial rotation is the action of the subscapularis, the cuff muscle that pulls from the front of the humerus. Pick it for the subscapularis."],"comp":87,"competencyId":"w4-lab-posterior-shoulder-cuff"},{"id":"N-m3-arm-muscles-4","dok":1,"q":"Where does the teres minor take its origin?","a":"The lateral border of the scapula.","options":["The inferior angle of the scapula","The lateral border of the scapula","The coracoid process","The infraspinous fossa"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The teres minor is a small, round (teres) rotator cuff muscle that arises from the lateral border of the scapula, just below the infraspinatus. It runs up and laterally behind the shoulder joint to the lowest part of the greater tubercle. Like the infraspinatus, it laterally rotates the arm.","notes":["The inferior angle of the scapula is the origin of the teres major, the larger muscle just below the teres minor. Pick it for the teres major.","Correct. The teres minor arises from the lateral border of the scapula.","The coracoid process is the origin of the short head of the biceps and the coracobrachialis. Pick it for those anterior arm muscles.","The infraspinous fossa is the origin of the infraspinatus, which lies just above the teres minor. Pick it for the infraspinatus."],"comp":87,"competencyId":"w4-lab-posterior-shoulder-cuff"},{"id":"N-m3-arm-muscles-5","dok":2,"q":"Moving from superior to inferior, in what order do the rotator cuff tendons attach to the greater tubercle?","a":"Supraspinatus, infraspinatus, teres minor.","options":["Teres minor, infraspinatus, supraspinatus","Supraspinatus, infraspinatus, teres minor","Supraspinatus, teres minor, subscapularis","Infraspinatus, supraspinatus, teres minor"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Three cuff tendons land on the greater tubercle, and they stack in the same order as their origins on the back of the scapula. The supraspinatus, from above the spine, takes the highest spot. The infraspinatus, from below the spine, attaches in the middle, and the teres minor, from the lateral border, attaches lowest.","notes":["This is the right list in reverse. It would be the answer if the question asked for the order from inferior to superior.","Correct. Supraspinatus, then infraspinatus, then teres minor, from top to bottom.","The subscapularis does not attach to the greater tubercle at all. It is the one cuff muscle that inserts on the lesser tubercle.","This puts the infraspinatus on top, but the supraspinatus comes from above the scapular spine and inserts highest."],"comp":87,"competencyId":"w4-lab-posterior-shoulder-cuff"},{"id":"N-m3-arm-muscles-6","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly describes how the subscapularis differs from the other three rotator cuff muscles?","a":"It lies on the anterior scapula, inserts on the lesser tubercle, and medially rotates the arm.","options":["It lies on the anterior scapula, inserts on the lesser tubercle, and medially rotates the arm","It lies on the posterior scapula, inserts on the lesser tubercle, and laterally rotates the arm","It lies on the anterior scapula, inserts on the greater tubercle, and abducts the arm","It lies on the posterior scapula, inserts on the greater tubercle, and medially rotates the arm"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The subscapularis is the odd one out in every way. It is the only cuff muscle on the anterior surface of the scapula, the only one that reaches the lesser tubercle, and the only one that medially rotates the arm. The other three lie on the back of the scapula, insert on the greater tubercle, and either start abduction or rotate the arm laterally.","notes":["Correct. Anterior surface, lesser tubercle, and medial rotation all set the subscapularis apart.","The subscapularis does insert on the lesser tubercle, but it lies on the anterior surface and rotates the arm medially. Posterior position and lateral rotation describe the infraspinatus and teres minor.","Anterior position is right, but the greater tubercle and abduction belong to the supraspinatus.","Posterior position and the greater tubercle describe the other three cuff muscles, and none of them rotates the arm medially."],"comp":87,"competencyId":"w4-lab-posterior-shoulder-cuff"},{"id":"N-m3-arm-muscles-7","dok":2,"q":"Which statement is true of all four rotator cuff muscles?","a":"Each arises from the scapula and inserts on a tubercle of the humerus.","options":["Each laterally rotates the arm at the shoulder","Each arises from the scapula and inserts on a tubercle of the humerus","Each arises from the posterior surface of the scapula","Each inserts on the greater tubercle of the humerus"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The four cuff muscles (supraspinatus, infraspinatus, teres minor, and subscapularis) all run from the scapula to the greater or lesser tubercle of the humerus. Their tendons blend around the head of the humerus like a cuff, holding it in the shallow glenoid cavity. That shared layout is what defines the group, while their individual actions differ.","notes":["Only the infraspinatus and teres minor are lateral rotators. The subscapularis rotates medially, and the supraspinatus starts abduction.","Correct. All four run from the scapula to a tubercle of the humerus.","Three arise from the posterior scapula, but the subscapularis arises from the anterior surface, in the subscapular fossa.","Three insert on the greater tubercle, but the subscapularis inserts on the lesser tubercle."],"comp":87,"competencyId":"w4-lab-posterior-shoulder-cuff"},{"id":"N-m3-arm-muscles-8","dok":3,"q":"A patient has weak lateral rotation of the arm, and imaging shows wasting of the muscle that fills the fossa below the scapular spine. Which muscle is affected?","a":"The infraspinatus.","options":["Supraspinatus","Subscapularis","Teres minor","Infraspinatus"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Two clues point to the same muscle. The fossa below the scapular spine is the infraspinous fossa, which is filled by the infraspinatus. The infraspinatus is also a lateral rotator of the arm, so its wasting would weaken that movement.","notes":["The supraspinatus fills the fossa above the spine and starts abduction. Pick it if the weakness were in lifting the arm out to the side.","The subscapularis lies on the front of the scapula and rotates the arm medially. Pick it if the patient had trouble turning the arm inward.","The teres minor also laterally rotates the arm, but it arises from the lateral border of the scapula, not the fossa below the spine. Pick it for the smaller lateral rotator along the lateral border.","Correct. The infraspinatus fills the infraspinous fossa and laterally rotates the arm."],"comp":87,"competencyId":"w4-lab-posterior-shoulder-cuff"},{"id":"N-m3-arm-muscles-9","dok":3,"q":"A patient cannot turn the arm inward against resistance. Imaging shows a tear in the one rotator cuff tendon that crosses in front of the shoulder joint. Which muscle is torn?","a":"The subscapularis.","options":["Subscapularis","Supraspinatus","Infraspinatus","Teres minor"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Turning the arm inward is medial rotation. The subscapularis is the only cuff muscle on the anterior surface of the scapula, and its tendon crosses the front of the shoulder joint to reach the lesser tubercle. A tear here weakens medial rotation while the other cuff muscles still work.","notes":["Correct. The subscapularis crosses the front of the joint and medially rotates the arm.","The supraspinatus tendon crosses the top of the joint under the acromion and starts abduction. Pick it for trouble starting to lift the arm sideways.","The infraspinatus crosses the back of the joint and rotates the arm laterally, outward rather than inward. Pick it for weak lateral rotation.","The teres minor also crosses the back of the joint and rotates the arm laterally. Pick it for weak outward rotation with a problem along the lateral border of the scapula."],"comp":87,"competencyId":"w4-lab-posterior-shoulder-cuff"},{"id":"N-m3-arm-muscles-10","dok":1,"q":"Where does the deltoid insert?","a":"The deltoid tuberosity of the humerus.","options":["The medial lip of the intertubercular sulcus","The deltoid tuberosity of the humerus","The radial tuberosity","The greater tubercle of the humerus"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The deltoid is the thick triangular muscle that rounds the shoulder. Its anterior, middle, and posterior fibers arise from the lateral clavicle, the acromion, and the spine of the scapula, then converge on one roughened area on the lateral side of the humeral shaft, the deltoid tuberosity. That single insertion lets each set of fibers swing the arm in a different direction.","notes":["The medial lip of the intertubercular sulcus is the insertion of the teres major. Pick it for the teres major.","Correct. All three parts of the deltoid converge on the deltoid tuberosity.","The radial tuberosity is on the proximal radius and receives the biceps brachii tendon. Pick it for the biceps insertion.","The greater tubercle receives the supraspinatus, infraspinatus, and teres minor. Pick it for those rotator cuff muscles."],"comp":85,"competencyId":"w4-lab-chest-anterior-arm"},{"id":"N-m3-arm-muscles-11","dok":2,"q":"The deltoid arises from the lateral clavicle, the acromion, and the spine of the scapula. Which muscle inserts along this same bony line?","a":"The trapezius.","options":["Pectoralis minor","Trapezius","Rhomboid major","Levator scapulae"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The trapezius inserts on the lateral clavicle, the acromion, and the spine of the scapula, and the deltoid arises from exactly the same line. The two muscles meet on either side of this bony ridge, the trapezius coming in from the neck and back and the deltoid continuing down to the humerus. Remembering one attachment gives you the other.","notes":["The pectoralis minor inserts on the coracoid process. Pick it for the muscle deep to the pectoralis major that depresses the scapula.","Correct. The trapezius inserts on the same line from which the deltoid arises.","The rhomboid major inserts on the medial border of the scapula below the spine. Pick it for a muscle that retracts the scapula.","The levator scapulae inserts on the superior part of the medial border of the scapula. Pick it for the muscle that elevates the scapula from the neck."],"comp":87,"competencyId":"w4-lab-posterior-shoulder-cuff"},{"id":"N-m3-arm-muscles-12","dok":1,"q":"From which bony landmark do the middle fibers of the deltoid arise?","a":"The acromion.","options":["The lateral clavicle","The spine of the scapula","The coracoid process","The acromion"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The deltoid arises from a continuous bony line: the lateral clavicle in front, the acromion at the tip of the shoulder, and the spine of the scapula behind. The middle fibers come from the acromion, directly above the shoulder joint, and run straight down to the deltoid tuberosity. Pulling from directly above, they abduct the arm.","notes":["The lateral clavicle is the origin of the anterior fibers of the deltoid, which flex and medially rotate the arm. Pick it for the front part of the deltoid.","The spine of the scapula is the origin of the posterior fibers, which extend and laterally rotate the arm. Pick it for the back part of the deltoid.","The coracoid process is not a deltoid attachment. It is the origin of the short head of the biceps and the coracobrachialis and the insertion of the pectoralis minor, so pick it for those muscles.","Correct. The middle deltoid fibers arise from the acromion."],"comp":85,"competencyId":"w4-lab-chest-anterior-arm"},{"id":"N-m3-arm-muscles-13","dok":1,"q":"What do the posterior fibers of the deltoid do?","a":"They extend and laterally rotate the arm.","options":["Flex and medially rotate the arm","Extend and laterally rotate the arm","Abduct the arm","Adduct and medially rotate the arm"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The posterior fibers of the deltoid arise from the spine of the scapula and pass behind the shoulder joint to the deltoid tuberosity. Pulling from behind, they swing the arm backward (extension) and turn it outward (lateral rotation). They are the mirror image of the anterior fibers.","notes":["Flexion with medial rotation is the action of the anterior deltoid fibers, which pass in front of the joint. Pick it for the anterior fibers.","Correct. The posterior deltoid extends and laterally rotates the arm.","Abduction is the action of the middle fibers, which pull from directly above the joint. Pick it for the middle fibers.","Adduction with medial rotation is not a deltoid action. It describes muscles such as the pectoralis major and latissimus dorsi that pull the arm toward the body."],"comp":85,"competencyId":"w4-lab-chest-anterior-arm"},{"id":"N-m3-arm-muscles-14","dok":2,"q":"In which pairs of movements are the anterior and posterior fibers of the deltoid antagonists?","a":"Flexion versus extension, and medial versus lateral rotation.","options":["Elevation versus depression of the scapula","Flexion versus extension, and medial versus lateral rotation","Pronation versus supination of the forearm","Abduction versus adduction of the arm"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The anterior deltoid passes in front of the shoulder joint and flexes and medially rotates the arm. The posterior deltoid passes behind the joint and extends and laterally rotates it. Because they sit on opposite sides of the joint, they oppose each other in both the flexion and extension pair and the rotation pair, even though they belong to the same muscle.","notes":["Elevation and depression of the scapula are done by scapular muscles such as the trapezius and levator scapulae. The deltoid inserts on the humerus, so it moves the arm.","Correct. The front and back fibers oppose each other in flexion and extension and in rotation.","Pronation and supination happen at the forearm. The deltoid does not cross the elbow, so pick this for muscles such as the biceps brachii and pronator teres.","Abduction is done by the middle fibers, and neither the anterior nor posterior fibers are the main adductors. This pair does not describe how the front and back fibers oppose each other."],"comp":85,"competencyId":"w4-lab-chest-anterior-arm"},{"id":"N-m3-arm-muscles-15","dok":2,"q":"A person lifts the arm out to the side up to shoulder height. Once the movement is under way, which muscle becomes the prime mover of the abduction?","a":"The middle fibers of the deltoid.","options":["Middle fibers of the deltoid","Supraspinatus","Anterior fibers of the deltoid","Infraspinatus"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Abduction is shared between two muscles. The supraspinatus initiates the movement from the resting position, and then the middle fibers of the deltoid take over as the prime mover as the arm rises. This hand-off is why a supraspinatus tear causes trouble starting abduction while the deltoid can still raise the arm once it is partly lifted.","notes":["Correct. The middle deltoid is the prime mover once abduction is under way.","The supraspinatus initiates abduction, so it would be the answer if the question asked which muscle starts the movement from the resting position.","The anterior deltoid flexes and medially rotates the arm, lifting it forward rather than out to the side. Pick it for raising the arm in front of the body.","The infraspinatus laterally rotates the arm and does not drive abduction. Pick it for turning the arm outward."],"comp":85,"competencyId":"w4-lab-chest-anterior-arm"},{"id":"N-m3-arm-muscles-16","dok":3,"q":"On a model, a tag sits on deltoid fibers that arise from the lateral clavicle and pass in front of the shoulder joint. What movement do these fibers produce?","a":"They flex and medially rotate the arm.","options":["Flex and medially rotate the arm","Extend and laterally rotate the arm","Adduct and extend the arm","Abduct the arm"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Fibers arising from the lateral clavicle are the anterior fibers of the deltoid. Because they pass in front of the shoulder joint to the deltoid tuberosity, they swing the arm forward (flexion) and turn it inward (medial rotation). Working out where fibers start and which side of the joint they cross lets you predict the action.","notes":["Correct. The anterior deltoid fibers flex and medially rotate the arm.","Extension with lateral rotation is the action of the posterior fibers, which arise from the scapular spine and pass behind the joint. Pick it for a tag on the posterior fibers.","Adduction with extension describes muscles such as the latissimus dorsi and teres major that pull the arm down and back. No part of the deltoid adducts as its main action.","Abduction is the action of the middle fibers, which arise from the acromion directly above the joint. Pick it for a tag on the middle fibers."],"comp":85,"competencyId":"w4-lab-chest-anterior-arm"},{"id":"N-m3-arm-muscles-17","dok":1,"q":"What are the origin and insertion of the teres major?","a":"From the inferior angle of the scapula to the medial lip of the intertubercular sulcus.","options":["From the inferior angle of the scapula to the medial lip of the intertubercular sulcus","From the coracoid process to the medial surface of the humeral shaft","From the lateral border of the scapula to the greater tubercle of the humerus","From the subscapular fossa to the lesser tubercle of the humerus"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The teres major is a thick, round muscle that arises from the inferior angle of the scapula. It runs up and laterally, passing in front of the humerus, to insert on the medial lip of the intertubercular sulcus. Because it follows the same path as the latissimus dorsi, it shares the latissimus dorsi's actions.","notes":["Correct. The teres major runs from the inferior angle to the medial lip of the intertubercular sulcus.","The coracoid process to the medial humeral shaft is the coracobrachialis. Pick it for the arm muscle that flexes and adducts the arm.","The lateral border to the greater tubercle is the teres minor, the smaller cuff muscle just above the teres major. Pick it for the teres minor.","The subscapular fossa to the lesser tubercle is the subscapularis. Pick it for the cuff muscle on the front of the scapula."],"comp":87,"competencyId":"w4-lab-posterior-shoulder-cuff"},{"id":"N-m3-arm-muscles-18","dok":2,"q":"Which muscle shares all three of the latissimus dorsi's actions at the shoulder: extension, adduction, and medial rotation of the arm?","a":"The teres major.","options":["Infraspinatus","Pectoralis major","Teres minor","Teres major"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The teres major runs from the inferior angle of the scapula to the medial lip of the intertubercular sulcus, right beside the latissimus dorsi tendon. Because the two muscles pull on the front of the humerus from below and behind, they produce the same movements: extension, adduction, and medial rotation. The teres major is often called the latissimus dorsi's little helper.","notes":["The infraspinatus laterally rotates the arm. Pick it for turning the arm outward.","The pectoralis major adducts and medially rotates the arm, but it flexes rather than extends, since it pulls from the front of the chest. Pick it for raising the arm forward and across the body.","The teres minor is a rotator cuff muscle that laterally rotates the arm, the opposite rotation. Pick it for a lateral rotator from the lateral border of the scapula.","Correct. The teres major extends, adducts, and medially rotates the arm, just like the latissimus dorsi."],"comp":87,"competencyId":"w4-lab-posterior-shoulder-cuff"},{"id":"N-m3-arm-muscles-19","dok":2,"q":"The teres major arises from the scapula, yet it is not part of the rotator cuff. Which fact explains this?","a":"It inserts on the medial lip of the intertubercular sulcus, not on a tubercle of the humerus.","options":["It laterally rotates the arm","It lies on the anterior surface of the scapula","It arises from the clavicle rather than the scapula","It inserts on the shaft of the humerus, not on a tubercle"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The rotator cuff muscles all insert on the greater or lesser tubercle, where their tendons blend with the joint capsule around the humeral head. The teres major instead inserts lower, on the medial lip of the intertubercular sulcus on the humeral shaft. Without that cuff of tendon around the head, it does not help hold the humerus in the glenoid cavity the way the SITS muscles do.","notes":["The teres major medially rotates the arm, and even if it did rotate laterally, that would not exclude it, since cuff muscles rotate in both directions.","The teres major lies on the posterior side, at the inferior angle. The subscapularis is the muscle on the anterior surface, and it is a cuff muscle.","The teres major arises from the inferior angle of the scapula, not the clavicle. The deltoid is the shoulder muscle with a clavicular origin.","Correct. Its insertion on the shaft rather than a tubercle keeps it out of the cuff."],"comp":87,"competencyId":"w4-lab-posterior-shoulder-cuff"},{"id":"N-m3-arm-muscles-20","dok":2,"q":"Two muscles run from the lateral part of the scapula toward the humerus. A tag is on the smaller, upper one, whose tendon passes behind the humerus to the greater tubercle. Which muscle is tagged?","a":"The teres minor.","options":["Triceps brachii, long head","Infraspinatus","Teres minor","Teres major"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The teres minor and teres major lie one above the other along the lateral side of the scapula. The teres minor is the smaller, upper muscle; it arises from the lateral border and passes behind the humerus to the lowest part of the greater tubercle, which makes it a rotator cuff muscle. The teres major is larger, arises lower from the inferior angle, and passes in front of the humerus to the medial lip of the intertubercular sulcus.","notes":["The long head of the triceps arises from the infraglenoid tubercle and runs down between the two teres muscles to the elbow. Pick it for the vertical muscle crossing between them.","The infraspinatus also reaches the greater tubercle, but it fills the infraspinous fossa rather than running along the lateral border. Pick it for the broad muscle below the scapular spine.","Correct. The teres minor is the smaller upper muscle that reaches the greater tubercle.","The teres major is the larger, lower muscle from the inferior angle, and it passes in front of the humerus to the intertubercular sulcus. Pick it for the lower muscle of the pair."],"comp":87,"competencyId":"w4-lab-posterior-shoulder-cuff"},{"id":"N-m3-arm-muscles-21","dok":1,"q":"Which muscle runs from the coracoid process to the medial surface of the humeral shaft and flexes and adducts the arm?","a":"The coracobrachialis.","options":["Teres major","Pectoralis minor","Brachialis","Coracobrachialis"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The coracobrachialis is named for its two attachments: coraco for the coracoid process and brachialis for the arm. It arises from the coracoid process alongside the short head of the biceps and inserts on the medial surface of the humeral shaft. It lies in the anterior compartment of the arm, where it flexes and adducts the arm at the shoulder.","notes":["The teres major inserts on the medial lip of the intertubercular sulcus but arises from the inferior angle of the scapula. Pick it for the muscle that extends, adducts, and medially rotates the arm.","The pectoralis minor inserts on the coracoid process, but it comes from ribs 3 to 5 and moves the scapula. Pick it for the chest muscle deep to the pectoralis major.","The brachialis arises from the distal anterior humerus and inserts on the ulna, so it crosses the elbow and flexes the forearm. Pick it for the muscle deep to the biceps.","Correct. The coracobrachialis runs from the coracoid process to the medial humeral shaft."],"comp":85,"competencyId":"w4-lab-chest-anterior-arm"},{"id":"N-m3-arm-muscles-22","dok":2,"q":"Which two arm muscles arise from the coracoid process of the scapula?","a":"The short head of the biceps brachii and the coracobrachialis.","options":["Short head of the biceps brachii and brachialis","Long head of the biceps brachii and coracobrachialis","Short head of the biceps brachii and coracobrachialis","Long head of the triceps brachii and brachialis"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The coracoid process is the hook on the front of the scapula. Two arm muscles take origin there side by side: the short head of the biceps brachii and the coracobrachialis. The long head of the biceps arises separately, from the supraglenoid tubercle above the glenoid cavity.","notes":["The short head of the biceps is right, but the brachialis arises from the distal anterior humerus. Pick the brachialis for a muscle starting on the lower humerus.","The coracobrachialis is right, but the long head of the biceps arises from the supraglenoid tubercle, not the coracoid process. Pick the long head for the supraglenoid tubercle.","Correct. The short head of the biceps and the coracobrachialis share the coracoid process.","The long head of the triceps arises from the infraglenoid tubercle, and the brachialis from the distal humerus. Neither touches the coracoid process."],"comp":85,"competencyId":"w4-lab-chest-anterior-arm"},{"id":"N-m3-arm-muscles-23","dok":1,"q":"Where does the long head of the biceps brachii arise?","a":"The supraglenoid tubercle of the scapula.","options":["The coracoid process","The supraglenoid tubercle","The radial tuberosity","The infraglenoid tubercle"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The biceps brachii has two heads (bi means two). The long head arises from the supraglenoid tubercle, a small bump just above (supra) the glenoid cavity, and its tendon travels over the head of the humerus and down the intertubercular sulcus. The short head arises from the coracoid process, and the two heads join into one belly in the anterior arm.","notes":["The coracoid process is the origin of the short head of the biceps. Pick it for the short head.","Correct. The long head of the biceps arises from the supraglenoid tubercle.","The radial tuberosity is where the biceps inserts, on the proximal radius, not where it arises. Pick it for the biceps insertion.","The infraglenoid tubercle, just below the glenoid cavity, is the origin of the long head of the triceps. Pick it for the triceps long head."],"comp":85,"competencyId":"w4-lab-chest-anterior-arm"},{"id":"N-m3-arm-muscles-24","dok":1,"q":"The tendon of the long head of the biceps brachii runs down the front of the humerus in which landmark?","a":"The intertubercular sulcus.","options":["The olecranon fossa","The intertubercular sulcus","The radial groove","The deltoid tuberosity"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"After arising from the supraglenoid tubercle, the tendon of the long head passes over the head of the humerus and drops into the intertubercular sulcus, the groove between the greater and lesser tubercles. The groove guides the tendon down the front of the arm. This is also called the bicipital groove for that reason.","notes":["The olecranon fossa is the deep pit on the posterior distal humerus that receives the olecranon when the elbow straightens. Pick it for that elbow landmark.","Correct. The long head tendon runs in the intertubercular sulcus.","The radial groove spirals across the posterior humeral shaft and carries the radial nerve and deep brachial artery. Pick it for the landmark between the origins of the lateral and medial triceps heads.","The deltoid tuberosity is the roughened area on the lateral humeral shaft where the deltoid inserts. Pick it for the deltoid insertion."],"comp":85,"competencyId":"w4-lab-chest-anterior-arm"},{"id":"N-m3-arm-muscles-25","dok":1,"q":"Where does the biceps brachii insert?","a":"The radial tuberosity, with the bicipital aponeurosis fanning into the forearm fascia.","options":["The olecranon of the ulna","The styloid process of the radius","The radial tuberosity","The coronoid process of the ulna"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The biceps brachii ends in a strong tendon that crosses the front of the elbow and attaches to the radial tuberosity, a bump on the medial side of the proximal radius. A flat band from the tendon, the bicipital aponeurosis, also fans into the deep fascia of the medial forearm. Pulling on the radius lets the biceps both flex the elbow and supinate the forearm.","notes":["The olecranon is the insertion of the triceps brachii on the back of the elbow. Pick it for the elbow extensor.","The styloid process of the radius is the insertion of the brachioradialis, near the wrist. Pick it for that muscle.","Correct. The biceps inserts on the radial tuberosity.","The coronoid process of the ulna, with the ulnar tuberosity, is the insertion of the brachialis. Pick it for the muscle deep to the biceps."],"comp":85,"competencyId":"w4-lab-chest-anterior-arm"},{"id":"N-m3-arm-muscles-26","dok":1,"q":"Which pair of actions does the biceps brachii produce at the elbow and forearm?","a":"It flexes the elbow and supinates the forearm.","options":["Flexes the elbow and supinates the forearm","Extends the elbow and pronates the forearm","Extends the elbow and supinates the forearm","Flexes the elbow and pronates the forearm"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The biceps crosses the front of the elbow, so it flexes it. Because its tendon wraps around the radius to reach the radial tuberosity, contracting it also spins the radius so the palm turns up, which is supination. It is strongest at supination when the elbow is bent, and it also helps flex the arm at the shoulder.","notes":["Correct. The biceps flexes the elbow and supinates the forearm.","Both halves are reversed. Extension of the elbow belongs to the triceps, and pronation belongs to the pronator teres and pronator quadratus.","Supination is right, but the biceps lies in front of the elbow and flexes it. Elbow extension belongs to the triceps and anconeus.","Elbow flexion is right, but pronation is the opposite rotation. Flexing the elbow while pronating describes the pronator teres."],"comp":85,"competencyId":"w4-lab-chest-anterior-arm"},{"id":"N-m3-arm-muscles-27","dok":2,"q":"Where does the bicipital aponeurosis go after leaving the biceps tendon?","a":"It fans medially into the deep fascia over the forearm flexor muscles.","options":["Down to the coronoid process of the ulna","Laterally to the styloid process of the radius","Up to the supraglenoid tubercle of the scapula","Medially into the deep fascia over the forearm flexors"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The bicipital aponeurosis is a flat sheet of tendon (an aponeurosis) that splits off the biceps tendon at the front of the elbow. Instead of reaching bone, it angles medially and blends into the deep fascia covering the flexor muscles of the medial forearm. It forms part of the roof over the cubital fossa and adds a second, soft-tissue insertion to the biceps.","notes":["The coronoid process of the ulna is the insertion of the brachialis. Pick it for the deep elbow flexor beneath the biceps.","The styloid process of the radius is the insertion of the brachioradialis, on the lateral side near the wrist. The aponeurosis runs medially, not laterally.","The supraglenoid tubercle is the origin of the long head of the biceps, at the opposite end of the muscle. Pick it for where the long head starts.","Correct. The aponeurosis fans medially into the fascia over the forearm flexors."],"comp":85,"competencyId":"w4-lab-chest-anterior-arm"},{"id":"N-m3-arm-muscles-28","dok":1,"q":"Which muscle lies deep to the biceps, arising from the distal anterior humerus and inserting on the coronoid process and tuberosity of the ulna?","a":"The brachialis.","options":["Anconeus","Coracobrachialis","Brachioradialis","Brachialis"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The brachialis lies directly under the biceps brachii on the lower half of the anterior humerus. It arises from the distal anterior humerus and inserts on the coronoid process and tuberosity of the ulna. Since it crosses only the elbow and attaches to the ulna, its only action is flexing the elbow.","notes":["The anconeus is a small muscle on the back of the elbow, running from the lateral epicondyle to the olecranon and posterior ulna. Pick it for the muscle that assists elbow extension.","The coracobrachialis lies higher in the arm, running from the coracoid process to the medial humeral shaft. Pick it for the muscle that flexes and adducts the arm at the shoulder.","The brachioradialis arises from the lateral supracondylar ridge of the humerus and inserts on the styloid process of the radius. Pick it for the elbow flexor along the thumb side of the forearm.","Correct. The brachialis lies deep to the biceps and inserts on the ulna."],"comp":85,"competencyId":"w4-lab-chest-anterior-arm"},{"id":"N-m3-arm-muscles-29","dok":2,"q":"Which elbow flexor works just as well whether the palm faces up or down?","a":"The brachialis, because it pulls on the ulna, which does not rotate.","options":["Brachialis","Coracobrachialis","Triceps brachii","Biceps brachii"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"When the forearm pronates and supinates, the radius rotates around the ulna while the ulna stays put. The brachialis inserts on the ulna, so its line of pull is the same in any forearm position, and it flexes the elbow equally well with the palm up or down. The biceps pulls on the radius, so turning the palm down twists its tendon and lessens its flexing power.","notes":["Correct. The brachialis pulls on the ulna, so forearm rotation does not change its flexion.","The coracobrachialis does not cross the elbow at all. It flexes and adducts the arm at the shoulder.","The triceps brachii extends the elbow rather than flexing it. It is the antagonist of the elbow flexors.","The biceps flexes the elbow, but it inserts on the radius, so its flexing power changes as the forearm rotates. It is strongest with the palm up."],"comp":85,"competencyId":"w4-lab-chest-anterior-arm"},{"id":"N-m3-arm-muscles-30","dok":3,"q":"A weightlifter ruptures the distal biceps tendon at the radial tuberosity, yet can still bend the elbow fairly well. Which muscle, lying deep to the biceps, is mainly responsible?","a":"The brachialis.","options":["Coracobrachialis","Triceps brachii, medial head","Brachialis","Anconeus"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The brachialis lies directly beneath the biceps and inserts separately on the ulna. When the biceps tendon tears away from the radius, the brachialis is untouched and still flexes the elbow. What the patient loses most is the biceps' contribution, especially strong supination.","notes":["The coracobrachialis lies in the anterior arm too, but it ends on the humeral shaft and never crosses the elbow. It cannot flex the elbow.","The medial head of the triceps lies deep in the posterior arm and extends the elbow. It works against elbow flexion, not for it.","Correct. The brachialis, deep to the biceps, keeps flexing the elbow after the biceps tendon ruptures.","The anconeus is a small muscle at the back of the elbow that assists extension. It cannot replace an elbow flexor."],"comp":85,"competencyId":"w4-lab-chest-anterior-arm"},{"id":"N-m3-arm-muscles-31","dok":2,"q":"A right-handed person tightens a screw by twisting a screwdriver clockwise with the elbow bent, turning the palm from down to up. Which arm muscle provides most of the power?","a":"The biceps brachii.","options":["Triceps brachii","Coracobrachialis","Brachialis","Biceps brachii"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Turning the palm from down to up is supination. The biceps brachii inserts on the radial tuberosity, so when it contracts it spins the radius and supinates the forearm, and it does this most powerfully when the elbow is bent. That is why tightening a screw with the right hand feels strong.","notes":["The triceps brachii extends the elbow and does not rotate the forearm. Pick it for straightening the elbow.","The coracobrachialis acts at the shoulder, flexing and adducting the arm, and does not reach the forearm. Pick it for moving the arm forward and toward the body.","The brachialis inserts on the ulna, which does not rotate, so it cannot supinate. Pick it for pure elbow flexion.","Correct. The biceps is the most powerful supinator when the elbow is flexed."],"comp":85,"competencyId":"w4-lab-chest-anterior-arm"},{"id":"N-m3-arm-muscles-32","dok":1,"q":"What divides the arm into anterior and posterior compartments?","a":"The medial and lateral intermuscular septa.","options":["The flexor retinaculum","The interosseous membrane","The medial and lateral intermuscular septa","The bicipital aponeurosis"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The arm has two compartments wrapped in deep fascia. Sheets of fascia called the medial and lateral intermuscular septa run inward from the fascia to the humerus, splitting the arm into an anterior (flexor) compartment and a posterior (extensor) compartment. Muscles in the same compartment tend to share an action.","notes":["The flexor retinaculum is a band across the front of the wrist that forms the roof of the carpal tunnel. Pick it for the wrist.","The interosseous membrane joins the radius and ulna in the forearm and helps separate the forearm compartments. Pick it for the forearm, not the arm.","Correct. The intermuscular septa separate the anterior and posterior compartments of the arm.","The bicipital aponeurosis is a flat band from the biceps tendon into the forearm fascia. It does not divide compartments."],"comp":85,"competencyId":"w4-lab-chest-anterior-arm"},{"id":"N-m3-arm-muscles-33","dok":1,"q":"Where does the long head of the triceps brachii arise?","a":"The infraglenoid tubercle of the scapula.","options":["The supraglenoid tubercle","The posterior humerus above the radial groove","The posterior humerus below the radial groove","The infraglenoid tubercle"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The triceps brachii has three heads (tri means three). The long head arises from the infraglenoid tubercle, a small bump just below (infra) the glenoid cavity of the scapula. The lateral and medial heads arise from the posterior humerus, above and below the radial groove.","notes":["The supraglenoid tubercle, just above the glenoid cavity, is the origin of the long head of the biceps. Pick it for the biceps long head.","The posterior humerus above the radial groove is the origin of the lateral head of the triceps. Pick it for the lateral head.","The posterior humerus below the radial groove is the origin of the medial head of the triceps. Pick it for the medial head.","Correct. The long head of the triceps arises from the infraglenoid tubercle."],"comp":85,"competencyId":"w4-lab-chest-anterior-arm"},{"id":"N-m3-arm-muscles-34","dok":1,"q":"Where do all three heads of the triceps brachii insert?","a":"The olecranon of the ulna.","options":["The radial tuberosity","The lateral epicondyle of the humerus","The olecranon of the ulna","The coronoid process of the ulna"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The three heads of the triceps join into one tendon that crosses the back of the elbow. That tendon attaches to the olecranon, the point of the elbow on the proximal ulna. Pulling on the olecranon straightens the elbow, so the triceps is the main extensor of the forearm.","notes":["The radial tuberosity is the insertion of the biceps brachii on the front of the forearm. Pick it for the biceps.","The lateral epicondyle is an origin, not an insertion here. It is where the anconeus and several forearm extensors arise.","Correct. The triceps inserts on the olecranon.","The coronoid process of the ulna is on the front of the elbow and receives the brachialis. Pick it for the brachialis."],"comp":85,"competencyId":"w4-lab-chest-anterior-arm"},{"id":"N-m3-arm-muscles-35","dok":2,"q":"Which part of the triceps brachii can also extend the arm at the shoulder?","a":"The long head, because it is the only head that crosses the shoulder joint.","options":["Lateral head of the triceps","Long head of the triceps","Medial head of the triceps","Anconeus"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"A muscle can only move a joint it crosses. The long head of the triceps arises from the infraglenoid tubercle of the scapula, so it crosses both the shoulder and the elbow, and it extends both. The lateral and medial heads arise from the humerus itself, so they cross only the elbow.","notes":["The lateral head arises from the posterior humerus above the radial groove, so it also crosses only the elbow. Pick it for the head on the lateral side of the arm.","Correct. The long head crosses the shoulder and can extend the arm.","The medial head arises from the posterior humerus below the radial groove, so it crosses only the elbow. Pick it for the deep head that only extends the forearm.","The anconeus runs from the lateral epicondyle to the ulna and only assists elbow extension. It does not reach the shoulder."],"comp":85,"competencyId":"w4-lab-chest-anterior-arm"},{"id":"N-m3-arm-muscles-36","dok":1,"q":"Which humeral landmark spirals across the posterior shaft between the origins of the lateral and medial heads of the triceps?","a":"The radial groove.","options":["The deltoid tuberosity","The olecranon fossa","The radial groove","The intertubercular sulcus"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The radial groove is a shallow channel that winds diagonally across the back of the humeral shaft. The lateral head of the triceps arises above it and the medial head below it. The groove is named for the radial nerve, which runs in it together with the deep brachial artery.","notes":["The deltoid tuberosity is the roughened area on the lateral shaft where the deltoid inserts. Pick it for the deltoid.","The olecranon fossa is the deep pit on the posterior distal humerus that receives the olecranon. Pick it for the landmark at the back of the elbow.","Correct. The radial groove separates the origins of the lateral and medial heads.","The intertubercular sulcus is on the front of the proximal humerus, between the two tubercles, and holds the biceps long head tendon. Pick it for that tendon."],"comp":85,"competencyId":"w4-lab-chest-anterior-arm"},{"id":"N-m3-arm-muscles-37","dok":2,"q":"Which head of the triceps brachii lies deepest, largely covered by the other two heads?","a":"The medial head.","options":["Medial head","Long head","Short head","Lateral head"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The medial head arises from the posterior humerus below the radial groove, right on the bone. The long head and lateral head lie over it, so in a posterior view the medial head is mostly hidden, showing only near the elbow. Its name refers to its position on the medial side of the posterior humerus, not to being superficial.","notes":["Correct. The medial head lies deep, covered by the long and lateral heads.","The long head is superficial, running down the medial side of the back of the arm from the infraglenoid tubercle. Pick it for the head that crosses the shoulder.","The triceps has no short head. Short head is a part of the biceps brachii, which arises from the coracoid process, so choosing it mixes up the two muscles.","The lateral head is also superficial, forming the bulge on the lateral side of the back of the arm. Pick it for the head that arises above the radial groove."],"comp":85,"competencyId":"w4-lab-chest-anterior-arm"},{"id":"N-m3-arm-muscles-38","dok":1,"q":"Which small muscle at the back of the elbow runs from the lateral epicondyle to the lateral olecranon and posterior ulna and assists elbow extension?","a":"The anconeus.","options":["Anconeus","Supinator","Brachialis","Brachioradialis"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The anconeus is a small triangular muscle on the posterolateral side of the elbow, almost continuous with the lower triceps. It arises from the lateral epicondyle of the humerus and inserts on the lateral olecranon and posterior ulna. It helps the triceps extend the elbow.","notes":["Correct. The anconeus runs from the lateral epicondyle to the olecranon and assists extension.","The supinator also arises from the lateral epicondyle and ulna, but it wraps around the proximal radius and supinates the forearm. Pick it for turning the palm up.","The brachialis lies on the front of the elbow and flexes it, inserting on the coronoid process and tuberosity of the ulna. Pick it for the deep elbow flexor.","The brachioradialis arises from the lateral supracondylar ridge but runs down to the styloid process of the radius and flexes the elbow. Pick it for the muscle along the thumb side of the forearm."],"comp":85,"competencyId":"w4-lab-chest-anterior-arm"},{"id":"N-m3-arm-muscles-39","dok":3,"q":"A fracture through the middle of the humeral shaft damages the structures that lie in a groove on its posterior surface. Which structures are at risk?","a":"The radial nerve and the deep brachial artery.","options":["The ulnar nerve and the basilic vein","The median nerve and the brachial artery","The axillary nerve and the posterior circumflex humeral artery","The radial nerve and the deep brachial artery"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The groove on the posterior surface of the mid humeral shaft is the radial groove. The radial nerve and the deep brachial artery travel in it, pressed against the bone between the lateral and medial heads of the triceps. A mid-shaft fracture can stretch or cut them because they lie right on the bone at this level.","notes":["The ulnar nerve runs down the medial side of the arm and passes behind the medial epicondyle, and the basilic vein is a superficial vein on the medial arm. Pick them for an injury near the medial epicondyle.","The median nerve and brachial artery run down the medial side of the anterior arm, beside the biceps. Pick them for an injury on the front of the arm or at the cubital fossa.","The axillary nerve and posterior circumflex humeral artery wrap around the surgical neck of the humerus. Pick them for a fracture just below the humeral head.","Correct. The radial nerve and deep brachial artery run in the radial groove."],"comp":89,"competencyId":"w4-lab-ue-nerves"},{"id":"N-m3-arm-muscles-40","dok":2,"q":"A muscle you have not studied lies in the posterior compartment of the arm. Based on the compartment, what is its most likely action at the elbow?","a":"Extension of the elbow.","options":["Supination of the forearm","Flexion of the elbow","Pronation of the forearm","Extension of the elbow"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The arm's compartments sort muscles by action. The posterior compartment holds the triceps brachii, which crosses the back of the elbow to the olecranon and extends it, so a muscle in that compartment most likely extends the elbow. The anterior compartment holds the biceps, brachialis, and coracobrachialis, the flexors.","notes":["Supination is done by the biceps from the anterior arm and the supinator in the forearm. It is not the shared action of the posterior arm compartment.","Flexion of the elbow is the shared action of the anterior compartment, where the biceps and brachialis lie. Pick it for a muscle on the front of the arm.","Pronation is done mainly by forearm muscles such as the pronator teres and pronator quadratus. Pick it for a muscle in the anterior forearm.","Correct. Posterior arm muscles extend the elbow."],"comp":85,"competencyId":"w4-lab-chest-anterior-arm"}]},{"id":"m3-blood-cells","title":"Formed elements: blood cells and platelets","summary":"erythrocytes, the five leukocytes, platelets, identification on a smear","mw":"2026-10-12","tr":"2026-10-20","cards":[{"id":"L01343","dok":1,"q":"What is the characteristic shape of a mature erythrocyte?","a":"A biconcave disc.","options":["A biconcave disc","A small irregular fragment","A sickle-shaped crescent","A smooth sphere"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"A mature erythrocyte is a biconcave disc, a round disc that is thinner in the center than at the rim. This shape gives the cell about 30 percent more surface area than a sphere of the same volume, which speeds gas exchange. It also lets the cell bend and fold through the narrowest capillaries.","notes":["Correct. The red cell is a disc thinner at its center.","A small fragment of cytoplasm describes a platelet, which breaks off a megakaryocyte. Pick it for a question about thrombocytes.","A rigid, curved sickle shape is seen in sickle cell disease, where abnormal hemoglobin distorts the cell. It is a disease shape, not the normal one.","A sphere would have less surface area for the same volume and could not fold as easily. The biconcave shape gains about 30 percent more surface than a sphere."],"lt":"t-blood-erythrocytes","lid":"c1","comp":82,"competencyId":"w4-formed-elements"},{"id":"L01344","dok":1,"q":"What is the approximate diameter of an erythrocyte?","a":"About 7.5 um.","options":["About 7.5 um","About 10 to 12 um","About 1 um","About 2 to 4 um"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"An erythrocyte is about 7.5 micrometers (um) across. That is a useful ruler on a blood smear, because other cells can be sized against it. Platelets are much smaller, and the leukocytes are larger.","notes":["Correct. A red cell is about 7.5 micrometers across.","About 10 to 12 um is roughly the size of a neutrophil, which is larger than a red cell. Leukocytes are bigger than erythrocytes.","About 1 um is roughly how thin a red cell is at its center, not how wide it is. Students who choose it mix up thickness with diameter.","About 2 to 4 um is the size of a platelet, a small cell fragment. Choose it for the smallest formed element."],"lt":"t-blood-erythrocytes","lid":"c2","comp":82,"competencyId":"w4-formed-elements"},{"id":"L01345","dok":1,"q":"Where is a mature erythrocyte thinnest?","a":"At its center.","options":["At its center","It is evenly thick throughout","Over its nucleus","At its rim"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"An erythrocyte is a biconcave disc, which means both faces are pressed inward. That makes the cell thinnest in the center and thickest at the rim. On a stained smear this shows up as a pale center, because less hemoglobin is stacked there.","notes":["Correct. Both faces dip inward, so the center is the thinnest part.","A disc of even thickness would be a flat coin. The red cell is thinner in the middle, which is what gives it extra surface area.","A mature erythrocyte has no nucleus, having ejected it during development. A nucleus would be the answer for a leukocyte.","The rim is the thickest part of the disc. Students who pick it reverse the biconcave shape."],"lt":"t-blood-erythrocytes","lid":"c3","comp":82,"competencyId":"w4-formed-elements"},{"id":"L01346","dok":2,"q":"What advantage does the biconcave disc shape give an erythrocyte?","a":"More surface area for gas exchange and the flexibility to fold through capillaries.","options":["Extra room inside for organelles","A rigid shape that holds its form in narrow vessels","A smaller surface that slows the loss of gases","More surface area and flexibility"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The biconcave shape gives an erythrocyte about 30 percent more surface area than a sphere of the same volume, so gases move in and out more quickly. The thin, flexible disc can also bend and fold to squeeze single file through capillaries narrower than itself. Both features serve the job of carrying oxygen.","notes":["A mature red cell has ejected its nucleus and most organelles. The space goes to hemoglobin instead.","A rigid red cell is a problem, not an advantage. In sickle cell disease rigid cells cannot fold and jam the capillaries.","This reverses the advantage. The biconcave shape increases surface area so gases move faster.","Correct. Extra surface speeds gas exchange and flexibility lets the cell pass narrow capillaries."],"lt":"t-blood-erythrocytes","lid":"c4","comp":82,"competencyId":"w4-formed-elements"},{"id":"L01347","dok":1,"q":"Which statement about the nucleus of a mature erythrocyte is correct?","a":"There is none; a mature erythrocyte is anucleate.","options":["It has one large round nucleus","It has no nucleus, because it was ejected","It has a kidney-shaped nucleus","It has a nucleus with three to five lobes"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"During development the erythrocyte ejects its nucleus, so a mature red cell is anucleate, meaning it has no nucleus. This frees more space for hemoglobin. On a smear, a red cell shows a pale center but no stained nucleus, which sets it apart from every leukocyte.","notes":["A large round nucleus filling most of the cell is the look of a lymphocyte. Pick it for that agranulocyte.","Correct. Mature red cells are anucleate.","A kidney-shaped nucleus belongs to a monocyte, the largest leukocyte.","A nucleus with three to five lobes belongs to a neutrophil, the most numerous leukocyte."],"lt":"t-blood-erythrocytes","lid":"c5","comp":82,"competencyId":"w4-formed-elements"},{"id":"L01348","dok":1,"q":"Besides a nucleus, what else does a mature erythrocyte lack?","a":"Mitochondria and most other organelles.","options":["Its hemoglobin molecules","A flexible plasma membrane","Mitochondria and other organelles","The iron in its heme groups"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"As an erythrocyte matures, it ejects its nucleus and loses most of its organelles, including its mitochondria. What is left is a flexible membrane packed with hemoglobin. Losing these structures makes room for more hemoglobin, but it also means the cell cannot divide or repair itself.","notes":["Hemoglobin is what fills the mature erythrocyte. It is the one thing the cell keeps in abundance.","Every cell needs a plasma membrane. The red cell membrane is flexible, which lets it fold through capillaries.","Correct. The red cell gives up its organelles to make room for hemoglobin.","Iron sits in the heme groups of hemoglobin, so a red cell is full of it. Iron is what lets hemoglobin bind oxygen."],"lt":"t-blood-erythrocytes","lid":"c6","comp":82,"competencyId":"w4-formed-elements"},{"id":"L01349","dok":2,"q":"Because it lacks a nucleus and organelles, a mature erythrocyte cannot do which of the following?","a":"Divide or make new proteins.","options":["Divide or make new proteins","Carry oxygen","Hold hemoglobin","Bend through capillaries"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Cell division and protein synthesis both depend on a nucleus, which holds the DNA instructions, and on organelles such as ribosomes. A mature erythrocyte has ejected its nucleus and most organelles, so it cannot divide or replace its proteins. That is why red cells wear out after about 120 days and must be replaced by the bone marrow.","notes":["Correct. Without a nucleus or ribosomes the cell cannot divide or build proteins.","Carrying oxygen is the main job of the red cell, done by hemoglobin. No nucleus is needed for it.","Holding hemoglobin is exactly what the red cell is built for. Losing the nucleus makes more room for it.","Bending through capillaries depends on the flexible plasma membrane and biconcave shape, not on a nucleus."],"lt":"t-blood-erythrocytes","lid":"c7","comp":82,"competencyId":"w4-formed-elements"},{"id":"L01350","dok":1,"q":"What is the average lifespan of an erythrocyte?","a":"About 120 days.","options":["About 10 days","About 1 year","About 120 days","About 120 hours"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"An erythrocyte lives about 120 days, roughly four months. Without a nucleus it cannot repair itself, so it gradually wears out and becomes less flexible. Worn cells are then broken down in the spleen and liver, and new ones are made in the red bone marrow.","notes":["About 10 days is closer to the lifespan of a platelet. Red cells last much longer.","A year is too long. Without a nucleus to repair it, a red cell wears out in about four months.","Correct. Red cells last about four months.","About 120 hours is only five days. Students who pick it have the right number with the wrong unit."],"lt":"t-blood-erythrocytes","lid":"c8","comp":82,"competencyId":"w4-formed-elements"},{"id":"L01377","dok":2,"q":"A blood cell is an anucleate biconcave disc about 7.5 um across, with no organelles and a lifespan of about 120 days. What is it?","a":"An erythrocyte.","options":["A platelet","An erythrocyte","A neutrophil","A lymphocyte"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Each clue points to the erythrocyte. It has no nucleus and no organelles, it is a biconcave disc about 7.5 um across, and it lives about 120 days before the spleen and liver remove it. No other formed element has this combination.","notes":["A platelet is a small fragment of cytoplasm, about 2 to 4 um, not a biconcave disc. Pick it for a fragment from a megakaryocyte.","Correct. Every clue matches the red blood cell.","A neutrophil is a complete cell with a three to five lobed nucleus and fine pale granules. It has a nucleus, so it cannot be anucleate.","A lymphocyte is a nucleated agranulocyte with a large round nucleus. Its nucleus rules it out."],"lt":"t-blood-erythrocytes","lid":"c35","comp":82,"competencyId":"w4-formed-elements"},{"id":"L01417","dok":1,"q":"What feature is used to divide the leukocytes into two structural classes?","a":"Whether visible granules are present in the cytoplasm.","options":["Whether a nucleus is present","Whether the cell carries hemoglobin","Whether the cell is larger than a red cell","Whether visible cytoplasmic granules are present"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Leukocytes are sorted into granulocytes, which have granules in their cytoplasm that are visible with a light microscope after staining, and agranulocytes, which do not. The granulocytes are the neutrophils, eosinophils, and basophils. The agranulocytes are the lymphocytes and monocytes.","notes":["All leukocytes are complete cells with a nucleus. Lacking a nucleus is a feature of the mature erythrocyte.","Hemoglobin is carried by erythrocytes, not leukocytes. No leukocyte is classed by hemoglobin content.","All leukocytes are larger than red cells, so size cannot split them into two classes.","Correct. Granulocytes have visible granules and agranulocytes do not."],"lt":"t-blood-leukocytes","lid":"c1","comp":82,"competencyId":"w4-formed-elements"},{"id":"L01418","dok":1,"q":"To which class of leukocytes do the neutrophils, eosinophils, and basophils belong?","a":"The granulocytes.","options":["The granulocytes","The erythrocytes","The thrombocytes","The agranulocytes"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Neutrophils, eosinophils, and basophils all have cytoplasmic granules that are visible after staining, so they are called granulocytes. They all develop from the myeloblast and have lobed nuclei. The other two leukocytes, lymphocytes and monocytes, are agranulocytes.","notes":["Correct. All three have visible cytoplasmic granules.","Erythrocytes are the red blood cells, which have no nucleus and no granules. They are not leukocytes at all.","Thrombocyte is another name for the platelet, a cell fragment. It is not a class of leukocyte.","The agranulocytes are the lymphocytes and monocytes, which lack visible granules. Pick this class for those two cells."],"lt":"t-blood-leukocytes","lid":"c2","comp":82,"competencyId":"w4-formed-elements"},{"id":"L01419","dok":1,"q":"Which two leukocytes are classified as agranulocytes?","a":"Lymphocytes and monocytes.","options":["Neutrophils and eosinophils","Lymphocytes and monocytes","Eosinophils and basophils","Neutrophils and basophils"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Agranulocytes are the leukocytes without visible cytoplasmic granules after staining. There are two of them: the lymphocyte, with a large round nucleus, and the monocyte, the largest leukocyte, with a kidney-shaped nucleus. The other three leukocytes are granulocytes.","notes":["Neutrophils have fine pale granules and eosinophils have red-orange ones. Both are granulocytes.","Correct. These two lack visible granules.","Eosinophils have coarse red-orange granules and basophils have large dark granules. Both are granulocytes.","Neutrophils and basophils both have visible granules, so they are granulocytes. Pick this pair for a question about granulocytes."],"lt":"t-blood-leukocytes","lid":"c3","comp":82,"competencyId":"w4-formed-elements"},{"id":"L01420","dok":1,"q":"Which leukocyte is the most numerous in normal blood?","a":"The neutrophil.","options":["The neutrophil","The basophil","The lymphocyte","The monocyte"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Neutrophils make up more than half of all circulating leukocytes, which makes them the most numerous. They are the first responders to infection, engulfing and digesting bacteria. The mnemonic Never Let Monkeys Eat Bananas lists the leukocytes from most to least numerous, starting with neutrophils.","notes":["Correct. Neutrophils are more than half of all leukocytes.","Basophils are the rarest leukocyte, at the end of the mnemonic. Pick them for the least numerous white cell.","Lymphocytes are the second most numerous leukocyte. They come right after neutrophils in the mnemonic.","Monocytes are in the middle of the count, third in the mnemonic. They are the largest leukocyte, not the most numerous."],"lt":"t-blood-leukocytes","lid":"c4","comp":82,"competencyId":"w4-formed-elements"},{"id":"L01421","dok":1,"q":"Roughly what percentage of circulating leukocytes are neutrophils?","a":"About 50 to 70 percent.","options":["Less than 1 percent","About 20 to 40 percent","About 2 to 8 percent","About 50 to 70 percent"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Neutrophils make up roughly 50 to 70 percent of the leukocytes in a normal differential count, which is more than all the other types combined. Exact ranges vary slightly between labs and textbooks, but neutrophils are always the clear majority. A rise in neutrophils often signals a bacterial infection.","notes":["This is the share of basophils, the rarest leukocyte.","This is the share of lymphocytes, the second most numerous leukocyte.","This is the share of monocytes, the largest leukocyte.","Correct. Neutrophils are more than half of all leukocytes."],"lt":"t-blood-leukocytes","lid":"c5","comp":82,"competencyId":"w4-formed-elements"},{"id":"L01422","dok":1,"q":"What is the characteristic nuclear shape of a neutrophil?","a":"A nucleus with three to five lobes connected by thin strands.","options":["Three to five connected lobes","A kidney-shaped nucleus","A two-lobed nucleus","A single large round nucleus"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"A mature neutrophil has a nucleus divided into three to five lobes joined by thin strands. This multi-lobed nucleus, along with fine pale granules, makes it easy to pick out on a smear. Because the nucleus takes many shapes, the neutrophil is also called a polymorphonuclear cell.","notes":["Correct. The multi-lobed nucleus is the signature of a neutrophil.","A kidney-shaped nucleus belongs to a monocyte, the largest leukocyte.","A two-lobed, or bilobed, nucleus belongs to an eosinophil, which also has coarse red-orange granules.","A large round nucleus filling most of the cell belongs to a lymphocyte."],"lt":"t-blood-leukocytes","lid":"c6","comp":82,"competencyId":"w4-formed-elements"},{"id":"L01423","dok":1,"q":"Because of its many-lobed nucleus, the neutrophil is also called what?","a":"A polymorphonuclear cell.","options":["A macrophage","A mast cell","A polymorphonuclear cell","A plasma cell"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Poly means many and morpho means shape, so polymorphonuclear means having a nucleus of many shapes. The neutrophil earns this name because its nucleus is divided into three to five lobes that vary from cell to cell. Clinicians often shorten the term to PMN.","notes":["A macrophage is a monocyte that has left the blood and matured in a tissue. Pick it for the large phagocyte of the tissues.","A mast cell is a tissue cell similar to the basophil that releases histamine. It is not another name for the neutrophil.","Correct. The name describes its many-lobed nucleus.","A plasma cell is an activated B lymphocyte that makes antibodies. Pick it for antibody production."],"lt":"t-blood-leukocytes","lid":"c7","comp":82,"competencyId":"w4-formed-elements"},{"id":"L01424","dok":1,"q":"Roughly what percentage of circulating leukocytes are eosinophils?","a":"About 1 to 4 percent.","options":["About 50 to 70 percent","About 20 to 40 percent","Less than 1 percent","About 1 to 4 percent"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Eosinophils make up only a small share of leukocytes, roughly 1 to 4 percent. They are fourth in the most-to-least order of Never Let Monkeys Eat Bananas. Their numbers rise in parasitic infections and allergic reactions.","notes":["This is the share of neutrophils, the most numerous leukocyte.","This is the share of lymphocytes, the second most numerous leukocyte.","This is the share of basophils, the rarest leukocyte, which come after eosinophils in the count.","Correct. Eosinophils are a small share of leukocytes."],"lt":"t-blood-leukocytes","lid":"c8","comp":82,"competencyId":"w4-formed-elements"},{"id":"L01425","dok":1,"q":"What is the characteristic nuclear shape of an eosinophil?","a":"A two-lobed (bilobed) nucleus.","options":["A two-lobed nucleus","A large round nucleus filling most of the cell","A nucleus with three to five lobes","A kidney-shaped nucleus"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"An eosinophil has a nucleus with two lobes, often described as looking like a pair of earmuffs or a telephone receiver. Together with its coarse red-orange granules, this makes the eosinophil easy to recognize on a stained smear.","notes":["Correct. The bilobed nucleus plus red-orange granules marks an eosinophil.","A large round nucleus with a thin rim of cytoplasm belongs to a lymphocyte.","Three to five lobes is the neutrophil nucleus. Pick it for the most numerous leukocyte.","A kidney-shaped nucleus belongs to the monocyte, the largest leukocyte."],"lt":"t-blood-leukocytes","lid":"c9","comp":82,"competencyId":"w4-formed-elements"},{"id":"L01427","dok":1,"q":"Which leukocyte is the rarest in normal blood, at less than 1 percent?","a":"The basophil.","options":["The eosinophil","The basophil","The monocyte","The lymphocyte"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Basophils make up less than 1 percent of leukocytes, so they are the rarest and come last in Never Let Monkeys Eat Bananas. They have large dark granules that release histamine and other mediators of inflammation. Finding one on a smear usually takes some searching.","notes":["Eosinophils are uncommon, about 1 to 4 percent, but they are more numerous than basophils.","Correct. Basophils are the rarest leukocyte.","Monocytes are about 2 to 8 percent of leukocytes. They are the largest leukocyte, not the rarest.","Lymphocytes are the second most numerous leukocyte. They are common on any smear."],"lt":"t-blood-leukocytes","lid":"c11","comp":82,"competencyId":"w4-formed-elements"},{"id":"L01428","dok":1,"q":"Which two substances are found in the granules of a basophil?","a":"Histamine and heparin.","options":["Fibrinogen and serotonin","Major basic protein and peroxidase","Histamine and heparin","Perforin and granzymes"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Basophil granules contain histamine, which widens blood vessels and makes them leaky during inflammation, and heparin, which prevents clotting. Releasing these substances helps start an inflammatory response. Mast cells in the tissues carry the same chemicals.","notes":["These are carried in platelet granules, fibrinogen in alpha granules and serotonin in dense granules. Pick them for platelets.","Major basic protein and related enzymes are in eosinophil granules. Pick them for the parasite-fighting granulocyte.","Correct. These are the classic contents of basophil granules.","Perforin and granzymes are stored by natural killer cells and cytotoxic T cells, which use them to kill infected cells."],"lt":"t-blood-leukocytes","lid":"c12","comp":82,"competencyId":"w4-formed-elements"},{"id":"L01429","dok":2,"q":"Why is the nucleus of a basophil often difficult to see on a stained smear?","a":"It is hidden by large, dark-staining granules.","options":["It has been ejected from the cell","It is very small and stains pale","Large dark granules cover it","It is hidden by hemoglobin"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"A basophil has a lobed nucleus, but its cytoplasm is packed with large granules that stain dark purple to blue-black. These granules sit over the nucleus and hide much of it. The dark, crowded look of the cell is what identifies it.","notes":["Ejecting the nucleus happens only in the red cell line. Basophils are complete cells and keep their nucleus.","The basophil nucleus is not tiny or pale. It is simply covered by the dark granules.","Correct. The dark granules cover much of the nucleus.","Hemoglobin is found in erythrocytes, not basophils. Students who pick this mix up the red pigment with the dark granules."],"lt":"t-blood-leukocytes","lid":"c13","comp":82,"competencyId":"w4-formed-elements"},{"id":"L01430","dok":2,"q":"Which tissue cell is structurally similar to the circulating basophil?","a":"The mast cell.","options":["The macrophage","The plasma cell","The mast cell","The fibroblast"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Mast cells live in connective tissues, especially near blood vessels, and contain granules of histamine and heparin much like basophils. Both cells release these chemicals to start inflammation and allergic reactions. The basophil circulates in blood, while the mast cell stays in the tissues.","notes":["A macrophage is the tissue form of the monocyte, a large phagocyte. Pick it for the cell a monocyte becomes.","A plasma cell is the antibody-making cell that develops from a B lymphocyte.","Correct. Mast cells resemble basophils and carry histamine and heparin.","A fibroblast is the cell of connective tissue proper that makes fibers and ground substance. It is not related to basophils."],"lt":"t-blood-leukocytes","lid":"c14","comp":82,"competencyId":"w4-formed-elements"},{"id":"L01431","dok":1,"q":"Roughly what percentage of circulating leukocytes are lymphocytes?","a":"About 20 to 40 percent.","options":["About 2 to 8 percent","About 50 to 70 percent","About 20 to 40 percent","Less than 1 percent"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Lymphocytes make up roughly 20 to 40 percent of leukocytes, second only to neutrophils. Exact ranges vary a little between sources, but lymphocytes are always the second most common white cell. They are the core of specific immunity and include the B cells and T cells.","notes":["This is the share of monocytes, the largest leukocyte.","This is the share of neutrophils, the most numerous leukocyte.","Correct. Lymphocytes are the second most numerous leukocyte.","This is the share of basophils, the rarest leukocyte."],"lt":"t-blood-leukocytes","lid":"c15","comp":82,"competencyId":"w4-formed-elements"},{"id":"L01432","dok":1,"q":"What does the nucleus of a lymphocyte look like?","a":"A large round nucleus that fills almost the whole cell, leaving a thin rim of cytoplasm.","options":["A kidney-shaped nucleus","A nucleus with three to five lobes","A two-lobed nucleus","A large round nucleus"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"A lymphocyte has a large, round, dark-staining nucleus that fills nearly the entire cell. Only a thin rim of pale blue cytoplasm shows around the edge. This look sets it apart from the monocyte, which is larger and has a kidney-shaped nucleus.","notes":["A kidney-shaped nucleus belongs to the monocyte, the largest leukocyte.","Three to five lobes belongs to the neutrophil, the most numerous leukocyte.","A two-lobed nucleus belongs to the eosinophil, which also has red-orange granules.","Correct. A big round nucleus with a thin rim of cytoplasm marks a lymphocyte."],"lt":"t-blood-leukocytes","lid":"c16","comp":82,"competencyId":"w4-formed-elements"},{"id":"L01433","dok":1,"q":"Which list names the three kinds of lymphocytes?","a":"B lymphocytes, T lymphocytes, and natural killer (NK) cells.","options":["Erythrocytes, leukocytes, and platelets","B cells, T cells, and natural killer cells","Neutrophils, eosinophils, and basophils","Monocytes, macrophages, and mast cells"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Lymphocytes come in three kinds. B lymphocytes become plasma cells that make antibodies, T lymphocytes direct and carry out cell-based immune responses, and natural killer cells destroy infected or abnormal cells. All three come from the lymphoid line and look similar on a smear.","notes":["These are the three kinds of formed elements in blood. Pick this list for the cellular part of blood.","Correct. These are the three kinds of lymphocytes.","These are the three granulocytes. Pick this list for leukocytes with visible granules.","A monocyte matures into a macrophage, and the mast cell resembles a basophil. None of these is a lymphocyte."],"lt":"t-blood-leukocytes","lid":"c17","comp":82,"competencyId":"w4-formed-elements"},{"id":"L01434","dok":1,"q":"When a B lymphocyte is activated, into what cell does it develop?","a":"A plasma cell.","options":["A mast cell","A plasma cell","A macrophage","A megakaryocyte"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"An activated B lymphocyte develops into a plasma cell. Plasma cells make and release antibodies, the gamma globulins of the plasma. This is why gamma globulins are the one group of plasma proteins not made in the liver.","notes":["A mast cell is a tissue cell similar to a basophil, with histamine and heparin granules.","Correct. Activated B cells become antibody-making plasma cells.","A macrophage develops from a monocyte that leaves the blood. Pick it for the large tissue phagocyte.","A megakaryocyte is the giant marrow cell that breaks into platelets."],"lt":"t-blood-leukocytes","lid":"c18","comp":82,"competencyId":"w4-formed-elements"},{"id":"L01436","dok":1,"q":"Roughly what percentage of circulating leukocytes are monocytes?","a":"About 2 to 8 percent.","options":["About 20 to 40 percent","Less than 1 percent","About 50 to 70 percent","About 2 to 8 percent"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Monocytes make up roughly 2 to 8 percent of leukocytes. They are third in the most-to-least order of Never Let Monkeys Eat Bananas. Although they are not very numerous, they are the largest leukocytes and become macrophages in the tissues.","notes":["This is the share of lymphocytes, the second most numerous leukocyte.","This is the share of basophils, the rarest leukocyte.","This is the share of neutrophils, the most numerous leukocyte.","Correct. Monocytes are a small share of leukocytes."],"lt":"t-blood-leukocytes","lid":"c20","comp":82,"competencyId":"w4-formed-elements"},{"id":"L01437","dok":1,"q":"What is the characteristic nuclear shape of a monocyte?","a":"A kidney-shaped (indented) nucleus.","options":["A large round nucleus filling most of the cell","A nucleus with three to five lobes","A two-lobed nucleus","A kidney-shaped nucleus"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"A monocyte has a large nucleus with an indentation on one side, giving it a kidney or horseshoe shape. Combined with its large size and pale gray-blue cytoplasm with no visible granules, this identifies the monocyte on a smear.","notes":["A large round nucleus with a thin rim of cytoplasm belongs to a lymphocyte, the other agranulocyte.","Three to five lobes belongs to a neutrophil.","A two-lobed nucleus belongs to an eosinophil.","Correct. The indented, kidney-shaped nucleus marks a monocyte."],"lt":"t-blood-leukocytes","lid":"c21","comp":82,"competencyId":"w4-formed-elements"},{"id":"L01438","dok":1,"q":"Which leukocyte is the largest of the five?","a":"The monocyte.","options":["The lymphocyte","The monocyte","The basophil","The neutrophil"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The monocyte is the largest leukocyte, noticeably bigger than the red cells around it on a smear. Its size fits its job, since after leaving the blood it matures into a macrophage that engulfs debris and pathogens.","notes":["Most lymphocytes are small, about the size of a red cell. Pick the lymphocyte for the large round nucleus, not the largest size.","Correct. The monocyte is the largest leukocyte.","The basophil is the rarest leukocyte. It is not the largest.","The neutrophil is the most numerous leukocyte, but it is smaller than a monocyte."],"lt":"t-blood-leukocytes","lid":"c22","comp":82,"competencyId":"w4-formed-elements"},{"id":"L01439","dok":1,"q":"When a monocyte leaves the blood and enters a tissue, into what cell does it mature?","a":"A macrophage","options":["A neutrophil","A macrophage","A plasma cell","A mast cell"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Monocytes circulate in the blood for a short time, then leave it, mostly through the loosely joined walls of the venules, and enter the tissues. There they enlarge and mature into macrophages, large phagocytes that engulf debris and pathogens. Macrophages in the spleen and liver also remove worn-out red cells.","notes":["A neutrophil is a separate granulocyte that develops from the myeloblast. Monocytes do not turn into neutrophils.","Correct. Monocytes become macrophages in the tissues.","A plasma cell develops from an activated B lymphocyte. Pick it for antibody production.","A mast cell is a tissue cell similar to the basophil, carrying histamine and heparin."],"lt":"t-blood-leukocytes","lid":"c23","comp":82,"competencyId":"w4-formed-elements"},{"id":"L01440","dok":2,"q":"Which list puts the five leukocytes in order from most to least numerous?","a":"Neutrophils, lymphocytes, monocytes, eosinophils, basophils.","options":["Neutrophils, lymphocytes, eosinophils, monocytes, basophils","Neutrophils, lymphocytes, monocytes, eosinophils, basophils","Lymphocytes, neutrophils, monocytes, eosinophils, basophils","Basophils, eosinophils, monocytes, lymphocytes, neutrophils"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The mnemonic Never Let Monkeys Eat Bananas gives the order from most to least numerous: neutrophils, lymphocytes, monocytes, eosinophils, basophils. Neutrophils are more than half of all leukocytes, while basophils are less than 1 percent. This order is the answer to the differential count question.","notes":["This swaps monocytes and eosinophils. Monocytes are more numerous and come third.","Correct. This matches Never Let Monkeys Eat Bananas.","This swaps the first two. Neutrophils, not lymphocytes, are the most numerous.","This is the correct sequence written backward, from least to most numerous."],"lt":"t-blood-leukocytes","lid":"c24","comp":82,"competencyId":"w4-formed-elements"},{"id":"L01441","dok":2,"q":"A leukocyte has fine pale granules and a nucleus with three to five lobes, and it is the most numerous white cell. Which cell is it?","a":"The neutrophil.","options":["The neutrophil","The eosinophil","The basophil","The monocyte"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Each clue fits the neutrophil. It has fine, pale granules that are hard to see, a nucleus with three to five lobes, and it outnumbers every other leukocyte. It is the first responder to infection, engulfing and digesting bacteria.","notes":["Correct. Pale granules, a multi-lobed nucleus, and the highest count identify the neutrophil.","The eosinophil has coarse red-orange granules and a two-lobed nucleus, and it is uncommon.","The basophil has large dark granules that hide its nucleus, and it is the rarest leukocyte.","The monocyte is an agranulocyte with a kidney-shaped nucleus. It has no visible granules."],"lt":"t-blood-leukocytes","lid":"c25","comp":82,"competencyId":"w4-formed-elements"},{"id":"L01442","dok":2,"q":"A granulocyte has a two-lobed nucleus and coarse red-orange granules. Which cell is it?","a":"The eosinophil","options":["The neutrophil","The eosinophil","The basophil","The lymphocyte"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"A two-lobed nucleus and coarse red-orange granules are the two features that identify an eosinophil on a smear. Eosinophils attack parasites and help moderate allergic reactions.","notes":["The neutrophil has fine pale granules and a nucleus with three to five lobes.","Correct. A bilobed nucleus and red-orange granules mark the eosinophil.","The basophil has large dark purple granules that hide its nucleus.","The lymphocyte is an agranulocyte with a large round nucleus and no visible granules."],"lt":"t-blood-leukocytes","lid":"c26","comp":82,"competencyId":"w4-formed-elements"},{"id":"L01443","dok":2,"q":"On a blood smear, which statement correctly distinguishes a lymphocyte from a monocyte?","a":"The lymphocyte has a large round nucleus with little cytoplasm; the monocyte is larger, with a kidney-shaped nucleus.","options":["Lymphocyte has a three to five lobed nucleus; monocyte has a two-lobed nucleus","Lymphocyte has visible granules; monocyte has none","Lymphocyte is larger with a kidney-shaped nucleus; monocyte has a round nucleus","Lymphocyte has a round nucleus; monocyte is larger with a kidney-shaped one"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Both cells are agranulocytes, so granules cannot tell them apart. The lymphocyte has a round nucleus that fills almost the whole cell, leaving only a thin rim of cytoplasm. The monocyte is the largest leukocyte and has more cytoplasm around an indented, kidney-shaped nucleus.","notes":["These nuclear shapes belong to the neutrophil and the eosinophil. Neither fits a lymphocyte or a monocyte.","Neither cell has visible granules, since both are agranulocytes. Granules separate granulocytes from agranulocytes, not these two cells.","This swaps the two cells. The monocyte is the larger cell with the kidney-shaped nucleus.","Correct. Nucleus shape and cell size separate the two agranulocytes."],"lt":"t-blood-leukocytes","lid":"c27","comp":82,"competencyId":"w4-formed-elements"},{"id":"L01444","dok":1,"q":"What is a platelet, in structural terms?","a":"A membrane-bound fragment of a megakaryocyte, not a complete cell.","options":["A type of granulocyte","An immature red cell","A complete nucleated cell","A fragment of a megakaryocyte"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"A platelet is a small piece of cytoplasm wrapped in membrane that has broken off a megakaryocyte, a giant cell in red bone marrow. It has no nucleus, so it is a cell fragment rather than a whole cell. Platelets gather at a vessel injury to form a plug and help build a clot.","notes":["Granulocytes are leukocytes with lobed nuclei and visible granules, such as neutrophils. Platelets are fragments, not leukocytes.","An immature red cell in the blood is a reticulocyte, which still has ribosome remnants.","Complete nucleated cells in blood are the leukocytes. Platelets lack a nucleus.","Correct. Platelets are pieces of megakaryocyte cytoplasm."],"lt":"t-blood-leukocytes","lid":"c28","comp":82,"competencyId":"w4-formed-elements"},{"id":"L01445","dok":1,"q":"What is another name for a platelet?","a":"A thrombocyte.","options":["A leukocyte","A thrombocyte","An erythrocyte","A megakaryocyte"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Platelets are also called thrombocytes, from thrombo, meaning clot. The name reflects their job, since platelets gather at a vessel injury, form a plug, and help the clotting proteins build a clot. Despite the cyte ending, a thrombocyte is a cell fragment, not a whole cell.","notes":["A leukocyte is a white blood cell, a complete nucleated cell that defends the body.","Correct. Thrombocyte is the other name for a platelet.","An erythrocyte is a red blood cell, the oxygen carrier. Pick it for the most numerous formed element.","A megakaryocyte is the giant marrow cell that breaks apart to form platelets. It is the parent cell, not the platelet itself."],"lt":"t-blood-leukocytes","lid":"c29","comp":82,"competencyId":"w4-formed-elements"},{"id":"L01446","dok":1,"q":"What is the approximate diameter of a platelet?","a":"About 2 to 4 um.","options":["About 2 to 4 um","About 7.5 um","About 50 um","About 10 to 12 um"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Platelets are tiny, about 2 to 4 micrometers (um) across, much smaller than the 7.5 um erythrocyte. On a smear they look like small purple specks scattered between the red cells. Their small size fits the fact that they are fragments of a larger cell.","notes":["Correct. Platelets are the smallest formed elements.","About 7.5 um is the diameter of an erythrocyte. Platelets are much smaller than red cells.","About 50 um is larger than any circulating blood cell. Students who pick it confuse the platelet with the giant megakaryocyte that produces it.","About 10 to 12 um is roughly the size of a neutrophil, which is larger than a red cell."],"lt":"t-blood-leukocytes","lid":"c30","comp":82,"competencyId":"w4-formed-elements"},{"id":"L01447","dok":1,"q":"What is the normal platelet count in blood?","a":"About 150,000 to 400,000 per microliter.","options":["About 150,000 to 400,000 per microliter","About 4 to 6 million per microliter","About 20,000 to 40,000 per microliter","About 4,000 to 11,000 per microliter"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"A normal platelet count is about 150,000 to 400,000 platelets per microliter of blood. Platelets are intermediate in number, fewer than red cells but more than white cells. A count well below the normal range is called thrombocytopenia and raises the risk of bleeding.","notes":["Correct. Platelets are intermediate in number among the formed elements.","This is roughly the normal red cell count. Erythrocytes are by far the most numerous formed element.","A count this low is well below normal. It would be thrombocytopenia, with a high risk of bleeding.","This is roughly the normal white cell count. Leukocytes are the least numerous formed element."],"lt":"t-blood-leukocytes","lid":"c31","comp":82,"competencyId":"w4-formed-elements"},{"id":"L01451","dok":1,"q":"What is the normal total white blood cell count?","a":"About 4,000 to 11,000 per microliter.","options":["About 4 to 6 million per microliter","About 40,000 to 110,000 per microliter","About 4,000 to 11,000 per microliter","About 150,000 to 400,000 per microliter"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"A normal total leukocyte count is roughly 4,000 to 11,000 per microliter of blood, with slight differences between labs. Leukocytes are the least numerous formed element. A count well above this range can point toward infection or leukemia, and a count well below it is leukopenia.","notes":["This is roughly the normal red cell count. Erythrocytes are by far the most numerous formed element.","This is ten times the normal range. A count this high would point toward a serious problem such as leukemia.","Correct. Leukocytes are the least numerous formed element.","This is the normal platelet count. Platelets outnumber white cells."],"lt":"t-blood-leukocytes","lid":"c35","comp":82,"competencyId":"w4-formed-elements"},{"id":"L01452","dok":2,"q":"A white cell has a lobed nucleus almost hidden by large dark granules of histamine and heparin, and it is the rarest leukocyte. Which cell is it?","a":"The basophil.","options":["The eosinophil","The monocyte","The basophil","The neutrophil"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Each clue fits the basophil. Its large, dark granules contain histamine and heparin and cover much of its lobed nucleus, and it makes up less than 1 percent of leukocytes. Its job is to release histamine and other mediators during inflammation.","notes":["The eosinophil has coarse red-orange granules and a clearly visible two-lobed nucleus.","The monocyte is an agranulocyte with a kidney-shaped nucleus and no visible granules.","Correct. Dark histamine granules and rarity identify the basophil.","The neutrophil has fine pale granules and is the most numerous leukocyte."],"lt":"t-blood-leukocytes","lid":"c36","comp":82,"competencyId":"w4-formed-elements"},{"id":"L01453","dok":2,"q":"A leukocyte is the largest of the five, is an agranulocyte with a kidney-shaped nucleus, and matures into a macrophage. Which cell is it?","a":"The monocyte.","options":["The lymphocyte","The neutrophil","The monocyte","The eosinophil"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Each clue points to the monocyte. It is the largest leukocyte, has no visible granules, and has an indented, kidney-shaped nucleus. After it leaves the blood and enters the tissues, it matures into a macrophage that engulfs debris and pathogens.","notes":["The lymphocyte is also an agranulocyte, but it is smaller and has a round nucleus. Activated B lymphocytes become plasma cells, not macrophages.","The neutrophil is a granulocyte with a three to five lobed nucleus. It does not become a macrophage.","Correct. Size, nucleus shape, and the macrophage fate identify the monocyte.","The eosinophil is a granulocyte with a two-lobed nucleus and red-orange granules."],"lt":"t-blood-leukocytes","lid":"c37","comp":82,"competencyId":"w4-formed-elements"},{"id":"L01454","dok":2,"q":"A formed element is a small membrane-bound fragment about 2 to 4 um across, with no nucleus, that breaks off a megakaryocyte. What is it?","a":"A platelet","options":["A platelet","An erythrocyte","A reticulocyte","A neutrophil"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Each clue points to the platelet. It is a small fragment of megakaryocyte cytoplasm, about 2 to 4 um across, with no nucleus. Platelets gather at a vessel injury, form a plug, and help the clotting proteins build a clot.","notes":["Correct. A small, nucleus-free fragment of a megakaryocyte is a platelet.","An erythrocyte is a whole biconcave cell about 7.5 um across, packed with hemoglobin. Pick it for a question about the oxygen-carrying cell.","A reticulocyte is an immature red cell with ribosome remnants. It forms in the red cell line, not from a megakaryocyte.","A neutrophil is a complete cell with a lobed nucleus and fine pale granules. It is not a fragment."],"lt":"t-blood-leukocytes","lid":"c38","comp":82,"competencyId":"w4-formed-elements"},{"id":"L01455","dok":2,"q":"When the five leukocytes are listed from most to least numerous, which one falls in the middle position?","a":"The monocyte.","options":["The monocyte","The lymphocyte","The neutrophil","The eosinophil"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The order from most to least numerous is neutrophils, lymphocytes, monocytes, eosinophils, basophils, remembered as Never Let Monkeys Eat Bananas. The third of five is the middle position, and that is the monocyte. Monkeys sits in the middle of the mnemonic.","notes":["Correct. Monocytes are third of five.","Lymphocytes are second in the order, just ahead of monocytes.","Neutrophils are first, the most numerous leukocyte.","Eosinophils are fourth in the order, just after monocytes."],"lt":"t-blood-leukocytes","lid":"c39","comp":82,"competencyId":"w4-formed-elements"},{"id":"L01845","dok":1,"q":"Lymphocytes are the main cells of lymphatic tissue. How is a lymphocyte classified as a blood cell?","a":"As an agranular white blood cell.","options":["A granular white blood cell","A red blood cell","An agranular white blood cell","A platelet"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Lymphocytes are leukocytes, or white blood cells, and they belong to the agranular group because their cytoplasm lacks visible granules on a stained smear. On a slide a lymphocyte shows a large, round nucleus that fills most of the cell, with only a thin rim of cytoplasm. Monocytes are the other agranular white blood cell.","notes":["Granular white blood cells are the neutrophils, eosinophils, and basophils, which show stained granules in their cytoplasm. Pick this for a question about one of those three.","Red blood cells carry oxygen, have no nucleus, and look like biconcave discs. Choose this for a question about erythrocytes, not cells of lymphatic tissue.","Correct. A lymphocyte is an agranular white blood cell.","Platelets are small cell fragments that help blood clot, not whole cells with a nucleus. They fit a question about clotting."],"lt":"t-lymph-overview-vessels","lid":"c25","comp":82,"competencyId":"w4-formed-elements"},{"id":"L02029","dok":1,"q":"Which formed element is also called the red blood cell?","a":"The erythrocyte.","options":["The megakaryocyte","The erythrocyte","The platelet","The leukocyte"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Erythrocyte is the formal name for a red blood cell, from the Greek erythro, meaning red, and cyte, meaning cell. Erythrocytes are by far the most numerous formed elements, and their job is to carry oxygen bound to hemoglobin. Learning these word parts helps with the other formed elements too: leuko means white.","notes":["A megakaryocyte is a giant cell in red bone marrow whose cytoplasm breaks apart into platelets, and it is not found circulating as a formed element. It would be the answer for where platelets come from.","Correct. The erythrocyte is the red blood cell.","Platelets, also called thrombocytes, are small cell fragments that help with clotting. Choose platelets for a question about plugging a damaged vessel.","Leukocyte means white cell, so leukocytes are the white blood cells that defend the body. Pick the leukocyte for a question about the formed elements that fight infection."],"lt":"t-blood","lid":"c7","comp":82,"competencyId":"w4-formed-elements"},{"id":"L02030","dok":1,"q":"What is the shape of a mature erythrocyte?","a":"A biconcave disc.","options":["A flat plate","A branching cell","A biconcave disc","A sphere"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"A mature erythrocyte is a biconcave disc, a round disc that is thinner in the center and thicker at the rim, pinched in on both sides. This shape gives about 30 percent more surface area than a sphere of the same volume, which speeds gas exchange. Its flexible membrane also lets the disc fold to squeeze through narrow capillaries.","notes":["A flat plate is a misreading of the word platelet, which is a small cell fragment, not a red cell shape. Pick platelets for a question about the formed element named for its small plate-like form.","A branching cell describes cells such as neurons or dendritic cells with long processes. Choose a branching shape for a question about the shape of a neuron.","Correct. A mature erythrocyte is a biconcave disc.","A sphere would give less surface area for gas exchange than a biconcave disc of the same volume. Students who pick it are picturing a generic round cell rather than the pinched-in disc seen on a smear."],"lt":"t-blood","lid":"c8","comp":82,"competencyId":"w4-formed-elements"},{"id":"L02032","dok":1,"q":"Which formed elements are the white blood cells?","a":"Leukocytes.","options":["Leukocytes","Platelets","Erythrocytes","Megakaryocytes"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Leukocytes are the white blood cells, from leuko, meaning white. They are the only formed elements that are complete cells with a nucleus, and they defend the body against infection. There are five types: neutrophils, lymphocytes, monocytes, eosinophils, and basophils.","notes":["Correct. Leukocytes are the white blood cells.","Platelets are cell fragments that help with clotting, not white blood cells. Choose them for a question about plugging a damaged vessel.","Erythrocytes are the red blood cells that carry oxygen. Pick them for a question about the most numerous formed element.","Megakaryocytes are giant cells in red bone marrow that produce platelets, and they do not circulate as white blood cells. They would be the answer for the source of platelets."],"lt":"t-blood","lid":"c10","comp":82,"competencyId":"w4-formed-elements"},{"id":"L02033","dok":1,"q":"Which leukocyte is the most numerous and the first responder to infection?","a":"The neutrophil.","options":["The basophil","The lymphocyte","The neutrophil","The monocyte"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Neutrophils are the most numerous leukocytes and the first to arrive at a site of bacterial infection, where they engulf and digest bacteria. On a smear they have a nucleus with three to five lobes and fine pale granules. The memory aid Never Let Monkeys Eat Bananas lists the leukocytes from most to least numerous, starting with neutrophils.","notes":["Basophils are the rarest leukocytes and release histamine in inflammation. Choose the basophil for a question about the least numerous leukocyte.","Lymphocytes are the second most numerous leukocytes and form the core of specific immunity, including T cells and B cells. Pick the lymphocyte for a question about the leukocyte with a large round nucleus filling most of the cell.","Correct. The neutrophil is the most numerous leukocyte and the first responder.","Monocytes are the largest leukocytes and mature into macrophages in the tissues. They would be the answer for the leukocyte with a kidney-shaped nucleus."],"lt":"t-blood","lid":"c11","comp":82,"competencyId":"w4-formed-elements"},{"id":"L02034","dok":1,"q":"Which leukocyte is the rarest and releases histamine during inflammation?","a":"The basophil.","options":["The neutrophil","The basophil","The eosinophil","The lymphocyte"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Basophils are the least numerous leukocytes. Their large dark granules contain histamine and other chemicals that they release during inflammation, and the granules are so dense they often hide the lobed nucleus. On a smear, those dark purple granules are the quickest way to spot one.","notes":["Neutrophils are the most numerous leukocytes, the opposite end of the count from basophils. Choose the neutrophil for a question about the first responder to bacterial infection.","Correct. The basophil is the rarest leukocyte and releases histamine.","Eosinophils have a two-lobed nucleus and coarse red-orange granules, and they attack parasites and moderate allergic reactions. Pick the eosinophil for a question about defense against parasitic worms.","Lymphocytes are agranulocytes with a large round nucleus, and they include the T and B cells. They would be the answer for the core cells of specific immunity."],"lt":"t-blood","lid":"c12","comp":82,"competencyId":"w4-formed-elements"},{"id":"L02035","dok":1,"q":"Which leukocyte is the largest and matures into a macrophage after leaving the blood?","a":"The monocyte.","options":["The neutrophil","The lymphocyte","The eosinophil","The monocyte"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Monocytes are the largest leukocytes, with a kidney-shaped or horseshoe-shaped nucleus and no visible granules. They leave the blood and enter the tissues, where they mature into macrophages that engulf debris and pathogens. Macrophages in lymph nodes, the spleen, and other organs all trace back to monocytes.","notes":["Neutrophils also engulf bacteria, but they stay neutrophils and do not become macrophages. Pick the neutrophil for a question about the most numerous leukocyte with a three-to-five-lobed nucleus.","Lymphocytes are agranulocytes like monocytes, but most are small, with a round nucleus filling nearly the whole cell. They would be the answer for the leukocytes that include T cells and B cells.","Eosinophils have a two-lobed nucleus and red-orange granules and fight parasites. Choose the eosinophil for a question about defense against parasites.","Correct. The monocyte is the largest leukocyte and becomes a macrophage."],"lt":"t-blood","lid":"c13","comp":82,"competencyId":"w4-formed-elements"},{"id":"L02036","dok":1,"q":"A lab report uses the term thrombocyte. Which formed element is being named?","a":"The platelet.","options":["The platelet","The leukocyte","The erythrocyte","The hemocytoblast"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Thrombocyte is another name for a platelet, from thrombo, meaning clot. Platelets are small fragments of megakaryocyte cytoplasm that gather and stick at a vessel injury, forming a plug and helping build a clot. A low platelet count is called thrombocytopenia, which uses the same root.","notes":["Correct. A thrombocyte is a platelet.","The leukocyte is the white blood cell, named from leuko, meaning white. Choose it for a question about the formed elements that fight infection.","The erythrocyte is the red blood cell, named from erythro, meaning red. Pick it for a question about the oxygen-carrying cell.","The hemocytoblast is the stem cell in red bone marrow that gives rise to every formed element, and it does not circulate. It would be the answer for the cell all blood cells come from."],"lt":"t-blood","lid":"c14","comp":82,"competencyId":"w4-formed-elements"},{"id":"L02038","dok":1,"q":"Which formed element is a small cell fragment rather than a complete cell?","a":"The platelet.","options":["The leukocyte","The platelet","The erythrocyte","The reticulocyte"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"A platelet is a small, membrane-bound fragment of cytoplasm that breaks off a megakaryocyte, a giant cell in the red bone marrow. Because it is a fragment, it has no nucleus and is not a whole cell. Platelets gather at a vessel injury, stick together to form a plug, and help the clotting proteins build a clot.","notes":["A leukocyte is a complete cell with a nucleus. Choose it for a question about which formed elements are the only complete, nucleated cells.","Correct. The platelet is a cell fragment, not a complete cell.","An erythrocyte has no nucleus as a mature cell, but it is a whole cell that ejected its nucleus during development, not a fragment. Pick the erythrocyte for a question about the oxygen-carrying cell.","A reticulocyte is a whole immature red blood cell that still holds ribosome remnants. It would be the answer for the stage just before a mature erythrocyte."],"lt":"t-blood","lid":"c16","comp":82,"competencyId":"w4-formed-elements"},{"id":"L02042","dok":1,"q":"Which leukocytes are agranulocytes, lacking visible cytoplasmic granules?","a":"Lymphocytes and monocytes.","options":["Eosinophils and basophils","Neutrophils and eosinophils","Neutrophils and basophils","Lymphocytes and monocytes"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Leukocytes are sorted into two groups by whether granules show in their cytoplasm on a stained smear. Granulocytes, which are neutrophils, eosinophils, and basophils, have visible granules and lobed nuclei. Agranulocytes, which are lymphocytes and monocytes, lack visible granules and have a single round or kidney-shaped nucleus.","notes":["Eosinophils and basophils are both granulocytes with very visible granules, red-orange and dark purple. Choose this pair for a question about leukocytes involved in allergic reactions.","Neutrophils and eosinophils are both granulocytes, with fine pale granules and coarse red-orange granules. Pick this pair for a question about granulocytes with lobed nuclei.","Neutrophils and basophils are both granulocytes, the most numerous and the rarest leukocytes. This pair would answer a question about the two ends of the differential count.","Correct. Lymphocytes and monocytes are the agranulocytes."],"lt":"t-blood","lid":"c20","comp":82,"competencyId":"w4-formed-elements"},{"id":"L02045","dok":2,"q":"Which statement explains why a mature erythrocyte has no nucleus?","a":"It ejects its nucleus during development, leaving more room for hemoglobin.","options":["Its nucleus breaks into three to five small lobes","It ejects its nucleus to make room for hemoglobin","It never had a nucleus at any stage of development","Its nucleus is hidden under dense dark granules"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Developing red blood cells in the marrow do have a nucleus, but they eject it, along with most organelles, before entering circulation. This leaves more space inside the cell for hemoglobin, the oxygen-carrying protein. The trade-off is that a mature red cell cannot divide or repair itself, so it lasts only about 120 days.","notes":["A nucleus with three to five lobes describes a neutrophil. Choose this for a question about identifying the most numerous leukocyte.","Correct. The erythrocyte ejects its nucleus to make room for hemoglobin.","Developing red cells start out with a nucleus and lose it during maturation. This choice confuses a red cell with a platelet, which is a fragment and never had its own nucleus.","A nucleus hidden by dense dark granules describes a basophil. Pick this for a question about identifying a basophil on a smear."],"lt":"t-blood","lid":"c23","comp":82,"competencyId":"w4-formed-elements"},{"id":"L02046","dok":1,"q":"About how long does an erythrocyte circulate before it is broken down?","a":"About 120 days.","options":["About 1 year","About 120 days","About 7 days","About 10 years"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"An erythrocyte lives about 120 days, roughly four months. Because it has no nucleus, it cannot make new proteins to repair itself, so its membrane gradually wears out. Worn cells are then removed by macrophages in the spleen and liver, and the iron is saved for reuse.","notes":["A year is about three times too long for a red cell. Without a nucleus to repair itself, an erythrocyte wears out in about four months.","Correct. An erythrocyte lives about 120 days.","About a week is far too short for a red cell. Students who pick this may be thinking of platelets, which last only about 5 to 9 days.","Ten years is far beyond any red cell's life. This choice confuses circulating blood cells with long-lived cells such as some memory lymphocytes or bone cells."],"lt":"t-blood","lid":"c24","comp":82,"competencyId":"w4-formed-elements"},{"id":"L02053","dok":2,"q":"Mature red blood cells are biconcave discs, pinched in on both sides. How does this shape help them do their job?","a":"It adds surface area for gas exchange and lets the cell bend through narrow capillaries.","options":["It adds surface area and lets the cell bend through capillaries","It makes the cell rigid so it keeps its shape in narrow vessels","It leaves room for a large nucleus in the center of the cell","It gives the cell a sticky surface that helps form clots"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"A biconcave disc has about 30 percent more surface area than a sphere of the same volume, so oxygen and carbon dioxide move in and out faster. The thin, flexible disc can also fold to squeeze single file through capillaries narrower than itself. Both features serve the red cell's job of picking up and delivering gases in every tissue.","notes":["Correct. The shape adds surface area and flexibility.","Rigidity is a problem, not a benefit, for red cells. A rigid, curved red cell is what happens in sickle cell disease, so pick rigidity for a question about why sickled cells block capillaries.","A mature red cell has no nucleus at all, and the pinched-in center is just thin cytoplasm. This choice comes from assuming every cell keeps a nucleus; leukocytes are the formed elements that do.","Sticking together at an injury is the job of platelets, not red cells. Choose a sticky surface for a question about how platelets form a plug."],"lt":"t-blood","lid":"d3-t-blood-1","comp":82,"competencyId":"w4-formed-elements"},{"id":"L02054","dok":2,"q":"White blood cells can squeeze out of blood vessels into the tissues, while red blood cells stay inside. Why is this ability essential to white cells?","a":"To reach infection in the tissues and fight it there","options":["To form clots on the outside of damaged vessels","To deliver oxygen to tissues outside the vessels","To return excess tissue fluid to the lymphatics","To reach infection in the tissues and fight it there"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Most infections and injuries happen in the tissues, not inside the bloodstream. Leukocytes use the blood mainly as a highway, then squeeze between the loosely joined cells of the postcapillary venules to reach the problem. Monocytes, for example, leave the blood and mature into macrophages that engulf debris and pathogens in the tissues.","notes":["Plugging and clotting a damaged vessel is the job of platelets and the clotting proteins. Choose this for a question about what platelets do at a cut.","Oxygen delivery is the job of red cells, and they do it from inside the capillaries as oxygen diffuses out. Pick oxygen delivery for a question about erythrocytes.","Returning excess tissue fluid is the job of lymphatic capillaries, which take up interstitial fluid as lymph. This would be the answer for a question about the lymphatic system's fluid balance role.","Correct. White cells leave the blood to fight infection where it is."],"lt":"t-blood","lid":"d3-t-blood-2","comp":82,"competencyId":"w4-formed-elements"},{"id":"L02055","dok":2,"q":"Platelets gather within seconds at the site of a cut. What do they do there?","a":"They stick together to plug the damaged vessel and help build a clot.","options":["They engulf bacteria that entered the wound","They stick together to plug the damaged vessel","They secrete antibodies against invading microbes","They carry oxygen to the injured tissue"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Platelets gather and stick at a vessel injury, piling up to form a plug that slows bleeding almost at once. They also help the clotting proteins, such as fibrinogen, build a firmer clot around the plug. Because they are small, numerous fragments already circulating everywhere, they can respond faster than any process that needs new cells.","notes":["Engulfing bacteria is the job of phagocytes such as neutrophils and macrophages. Choose this for a question about the first responder to bacterial infection.","Correct. Platelets stick together to plug the vessel and support clotting.","Secreting antibodies is the job of plasma cells, which come from B cells. This would be the answer for a question about humoral immunity.","Carrying oxygen is the job of erythrocytes, using hemoglobin. Pick this for a question about red blood cells."],"lt":"t-blood","lid":"d3-t-blood-3","comp":82,"competencyId":"w4-formed-elements"},{"id":"L02057","dok":3,"q":"Mature red blood cells have no mitochondria. Why is this helpful for a cell whose job is carrying oxygen?","a":"Without mitochondria, the cell does not use up the oxygen it is carrying.","options":["It lets the cell make more hemoglobin as it travels","It lets the cell store oxygen in its membrane","It means the cell does not use up the oxygen it carries","It lets the cell divide faster in the bloodstream"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Mitochondria are the organelles that use oxygen to make most of a cell's energy. A mature red cell has ejected its mitochondria, along with its nucleus, so it makes its small energy supply without oxygen. As a result, nearly all the oxygen it picks up in the lungs is delivered to the tissues instead of being consumed on the way.","notes":["A mature red cell cannot make new hemoglobin, because it has no nucleus or ribosomes left to build proteins. Its hemoglobin is all made while it is still developing in the marrow.","Oxygen is carried by hemoglobin inside the cell, not stored in the membrane. This choice misplaces the oxygen; the membrane's important trait is its flexibility.","Correct. Without mitochondria, the red cell does not burn the oxygen it carries.","A mature red cell cannot divide at all, because it has no nucleus. New red cells come only from stem cells in red bone marrow."],"lt":"t-blood","lid":"d3-t-blood-5","comp":82,"competencyId":"w4-formed-elements"},{"id":"L04299","dok":1,"q":"Which list names the three formed elements of blood?","a":"Erythrocytes, leukocytes, and platelets.","options":["Erythrocytes, leukocytes, and plasma","Erythrocytes, leukocytes, and fibrinogen","Erythrocytes, leukocytes, and platelets","Erythrocytes, platelets, and megakaryocytes"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The formed elements are the cells and cell fragments suspended in plasma. They are erythrocytes (red blood cells), leukocytes (white blood cells), and platelets (thrombocytes, which are cell fragments). Together they make up about 45 percent of blood volume.","notes":["Plasma is the liquid matrix that the formed elements float in, not a formed element itself. Pick plasma if the question asked for the other main component of blood.","Fibrinogen is a plasma protein dissolved in the fluid, not a cell or cell fragment. It would be the answer if the question asked which plasma protein forms a clot.","Correct. Erythrocytes, leukocytes, and platelets are the three formed elements.","Megakaryocytes are giant cells that stay in the red bone marrow and break apart to make platelets. Choose them if the question asked which cell produces platelets."],"lt":"t-w4-formed-elements","lid":"w4-formed-elements-r1","comp":82,"competencyId":"w4-formed-elements"},{"id":"L04300","dok":1,"q":"What is the shape of a mature erythrocyte, and what does it lack?","a":"A biconcave disc with no nucleus.","options":["A small cell fragment with no nucleus or disc shape","A biconcave disc with no nucleus","A biconcave disc with one central nucleus","A round cell with a multi-lobed nucleus"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"A mature erythrocyte is a biconcave disc, thinner in the center than at the rim, which gives it more surface area for gas exchange and lets it fold through narrow capillaries. It ejects its nucleus and most organelles as it matures, leaving more room for hemoglobin. On a smear the thin center shows up as a pale central area.","notes":["This describes a platelet, a fragment of megakaryocyte cytoplasm. It would be the answer if the question asked which formed element is not a whole cell.","Correct. The erythrocyte is a biconcave disc without a nucleus.","The shape is right, but a mature red cell has no nucleus at all. Students pick this when they assume every blood cell must have one; the pale center on a smear is the thin middle of the disc, not a nucleus.","A round cell with a multi-lobed nucleus is a neutrophil, the most numerous leukocyte. Pick this description if the question asked how to recognize a neutrophil on a smear."],"lt":"t-w4-formed-elements","lid":"w4-formed-elements-r2","comp":82,"competencyId":"w4-formed-elements"},{"id":"L04301","dok":1,"q":"Which list names the five types of leukocytes?","a":"Neutrophil, lymphocyte, monocyte, eosinophil, and basophil.","options":["Neutrophil, lymphocyte, monocyte, eosinophil, and basophil","Neutrophil, lymphocyte, monocyte, eosinophil, and reticulocyte","Neutrophil, lymphocyte, monocyte, eosinophil, and platelet","Neutrophil, lymphocyte, monocyte, eosinophil, and megakaryocyte"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The five leukocytes are neutrophils, lymphocytes, monocytes, eosinophils, and basophils. Neutrophils, eosinophils, and basophils are granulocytes with visible granules, while lymphocytes and monocytes are agranulocytes. The phrase Never Let Monkeys Eat Bananas lists them from most to least numerous.","notes":["Correct. These are the five leukocytes.","A reticulocyte is an immature erythrocyte that still holds ribosome remnants. Pick it if the question asked which young red cell is released from the marrow.","A platelet is a fragment of cytoplasm, a separate formed element, not a leukocyte. It would be the answer if the question asked which formed element helps form clots.","A megakaryocyte is the giant marrow cell that fragments into platelets; it does not circulate as a white blood cell. Choose it if the question asked where platelets come from."],"lt":"t-w4-formed-elements","lid":"w4-formed-elements-r3","comp":82,"competencyId":"w4-formed-elements"},{"id":"L04302","dok":2,"q":"Which formed element is the most numerous in blood, and which is the least numerous?","a":"Erythrocytes are the most numerous; leukocytes are the least numerous.","options":["Erythrocytes most, platelets least","Leukocytes most, erythrocytes least","Platelets most, leukocytes least","Erythrocytes most, leukocytes least"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Erythrocytes are by far the most numerous formed element, which is why the red cell layer fills nearly half of a spun tube. Leukocytes are the least numerous, and together with the platelets they form only the thin buffy coat. Platelets fall in between: far fewer than red cells, but more numerous than white cells.","notes":["Erythrocytes are the most numerous, but platelets outnumber leukocytes. Students pick this because platelets are the smallest, mixing up size with number.","This reverses the true order. Leukocytes are the rarest formed element, which is why a spun tube shows only a thin pale buffy coat.","Platelets are intermediate in number, far fewer than erythrocytes. This choice confuses the smallest formed element with the most common one.","Correct. Erythrocytes are most numerous and leukocytes least numerous."],"lt":"t-w4-formed-elements","lid":"w4-formed-elements-r4","comp":82,"competencyId":"w4-formed-elements"},{"id":"L04303","dok":1,"q":"A platelet is counted as a formed element even though it is not a whole cell. What is a platelet, and what does it do?","a":"A fragment of megakaryocyte cytoplasm that helps form a clot.","options":["A whole cell with a kidney-shaped nucleus that becomes a macrophage","A fragment of megakaryocyte cytoplasm that helps form a clot","A plasma protein that turns into fibrin threads in a clot","An immature erythrocyte that still holds ribosome remnants"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"A platelet, also called a thrombocyte, is a small membrane-bound fragment of cytoplasm, not a complete cell. Platelets form when a megakaryocyte, a giant cell in the red bone marrow, breaks its cytoplasm into thousands of pieces. At a vessel injury, platelets gather and stick to form a plug and help the clotting proteins build a clot.","notes":["This describes a monocyte, the largest leukocyte, which leaves the blood to become a macrophage. It would be the answer if the question asked which white cell has a kidney-shaped nucleus.","Correct. A platelet is a piece of megakaryocyte cytoplasm that acts in clotting.","This describes fibrinogen, a plasma protein dissolved in the fluid rather than a formed element. Choose it if the question asked which protein forms the threads of a clot.","This describes a reticulocyte, a young red blood cell. Pick it if the question asked which immature cell makes up about 1 to 2 percent of circulating red cells."],"lt":"t-w4-formed-elements","lid":"w4-formed-elements-r5","comp":82,"competencyId":"w4-formed-elements"},{"id":"N-m3-blood-cells-1","dok":1,"q":"On a blood smear, which leukocyte has a nucleus with three to five lobes and fine, pale granules, and is the most numerous white blood cell?","a":"The neutrophil.","options":["The monocyte","The basophil","The eosinophil","The neutrophil"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The neutrophil is identified by its multi-lobed nucleus, usually three to five lobes, and its fine, pale granules. It is the most numerous leukocyte and the first responder to infection, engulfing and digesting bacteria. On a smear you should look at the shape of the nucleus first and the granules second.","notes":["The monocyte is the largest leukocyte, with a kidney-shaped nucleus and no visible granules. Choose it if the smear showed a large cell with a curved, kidney-shaped nucleus.","The basophil has a nucleus hidden by large, dark granules and is the rarest leukocyte. It would be the answer if the granules were so dark you could barely see the nucleus.","The eosinophil has a two-lobed nucleus and coarse red-orange granules. Pick it if the smear showed a bilobed nucleus with bright red-orange granules.","Correct. A multi-lobed nucleus with fine pale granules marks a neutrophil."],"comp":82,"competencyId":"w4-formed-elements"}]},{"id":"m3-vessel-routes","title":"Circulatory routes, fetal circulation and vessel disorders","summary":"pulmonary and systemic circuits, portal systems, anastomoses, fetal shunts and their adult remnants, aneurysm, atherosclerosis, varicose veins","mw":"2026-10-14","tr":"2026-10-15","cards":[{"id":"L01574","dok":2,"q":"The fossa ovalis is an oval depression in the wall between the two atria. Why does it exist in the adult heart?","a":"It is the remnant of the foramen ovale, a fetal opening that let blood bypass the lungs.","options":["It is the remnant of the ductus arteriosus, a fetal vessel that let blood bypass the lungs","It is the site of the SA node, where each heartbeat begins","It is the opening where the coronary sinus drains into the right atrium","It is the remnant of the foramen ovale, a fetal opening that let blood bypass the lungs"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Before birth the lungs are filled with fluid and not yet used for breathing, so the fetus has shortcuts that send blood around them. The foramen ovale is an opening in the interatrial septum that lets blood pass straight from the right atrium to the left atrium. After birth it normally closes, leaving a shallow oval dip called the fossa ovalis.","notes":["The ductus arteriosus is a different fetal shunt, a short vessel connecting the pulmonary trunk to the aorta. Its adult remnant is the ligamentum arteriosum, so pick it for that ligament.","The SA node sits in the wall of the right atrium near where the superior vena cava enters, not in the fossa ovalis. Pick it for the pacemaker of the heart.","The coronary sinus does open into the right atrium, but its opening is a hole for draining blood, not a sealed depression. Pick it for where the cardiac veins empty.","Correct. The fossa ovalis marks the closed foramen ovale, a fetal shortcut between the atria."],"lt":"t-heart","lid":"d3-t-heart-3","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L01685","dok":1,"q":"What is an anastomosis?","a":"A place where two vessels join, giving blood an alternate route.","options":["A flap of the tunica intima inside a vein","A junction of two vessels that offers an alternate route","Two capillary beds connected in series by a vein","A ring of smooth muscle at a capillary entrance"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"An anastomosis is a place where two vessels join, so blood has more than one path to reach a tissue. If one route is blocked, blood can still arrive through the other. Anastomoses are common around joints and in the brain, and an arteriovenous anastomosis is a special type that connects an artery directly to a vein.","notes":["A flap of the tunica intima inside a vein is a venous valve. Choose it if the question asked what keeps blood from flowing backward in a limb vein.","Correct. An anastomosis is a junction of vessels that offers an alternate route.","Two capillary beds connected in series by a vein describes a portal system, such as the hepatic portal system. Pick it if the question asked about blood passing through two capillary beds before returning to the heart.","A ring of smooth muscle at a capillary entrance is a precapillary sphincter. It would be the answer if the question asked what opens or closes a capillary's entrance."],"lt":"t-blood-vessels","lid":"c29","comp":69,"competencyId":"bvn-circulatory-routes"},{"id":"L01686","dok":1,"q":"Which circuit runs from the right side of the heart to the lungs and back to the left side?","a":"The pulmonary circuit.","options":["The hepatic portal circulation","The coronary circulation","The systemic circuit","The pulmonary circuit"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The pulmonary circuit carries oxygen-poor blood from the right ventricle through the pulmonary trunk and arteries to the lungs. There the blood picks up oxygen and returns through the pulmonary veins to the left atrium. Pulmonary refers to the lungs, which is the destination of this short loop.","notes":["The hepatic portal circulation carries blood from the digestive organs through the liver before it returns to the heart. It would be the answer if the question asked how absorbed nutrients reach the liver.","The coronary circulation supplies the heart wall itself through the coronary arteries and cardiac veins. Choose it if the question asked which vessels feed the heart muscle.","The systemic circuit runs from the left side of the heart out to the body and back to the right side. Pick it if the question asked which circuit supplies the body's tissues.","Correct. The pulmonary circuit links the right heart, the lungs and the left heart."],"lt":"t-blood-vessels","lid":"c30","comp":69,"competencyId":"bvn-circulatory-routes"},{"id":"L01693","dok":2,"q":"A vessel structure is a direct connection from an artery to a vein that bypasses the capillaries. Which structure is it?","a":"An arteriovenous anastomosis.","options":["A venous valve","A capillary bed","An arteriovenous anastomosis","A portal system"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"An arteriovenous anastomosis connects an artery directly to a vein, so blood skips the capillaries entirely. The name tells you its ends: arterio for the artery and venous for the vein, joined by an anastomosis, a junction between vessels. Because no capillaries are involved, no exchange happens along this route.","notes":["A venous valve is a flap of the tunica intima that keeps blood from flowing backward in a vein. Choose it if the question asked what prevents backflow in limb veins.","A capillary bed is the network of capillaries where exchange happens, which this connection bypasses. It would be the answer if the question asked what one arteriole feeds.","Correct. An arteriovenous anastomosis links an artery straight to a vein.","A portal system passes blood through two capillary beds in series, the opposite of skipping capillaries. Pick it if the question described the route through the liver from the digestive organs."],"lt":"t-blood-vessels","lid":"c37","comp":69,"competencyId":"bvn-circulatory-routes"},{"id":"L01694","dok":2,"q":"Blood passes through two capillary beds in series before returning to the heart, as in the route through the liver. What is this arrangement called?","a":"A portal system.","options":["An anastomosis","The pulmonary circuit","A portal system","A vascular shunt"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"In a portal system, blood flows through one capillary bed, collects into a portal vein, and then passes through a second capillary bed before heading back to the heart. The hepatic portal system carries blood from the digestive organs to the liver this way, so the liver can process absorbed nutrients first. Most routes have only one capillary bed between an artery and a vein.","notes":["An anastomosis is a junction where two vessels join to give blood an alternate route. Pick it if the question asked what keeps blood flowing to a tissue when one path is blocked.","The pulmonary circuit carries blood from the right heart to the lungs and back to the left heart, through one set of capillaries. It would be the answer if the question asked which circuit brings blood to the lungs.","Correct. Two capillary beds in series define a portal system.","A vascular shunt lets blood bypass the capillaries of a bed, which is the opposite of adding a second bed. Choose it if the question described blood skipping exchange when it is not needed."],"lt":"t-blood-vessels","lid":"c38","comp":69,"competencyId":"bvn-circulatory-routes"},{"id":"L01705","dok":2,"q":"In the hepatic portal system, in what order does blood pass these structures after leaving the arteries of the digestive organs?","a":"Digestive organ capillaries, hepatic portal vein, liver sinusoids, hepatic veins.","options":["Liver sinusoids, hepatic portal vein, digestive organ capillaries, hepatic veins","Digestive organ capillaries, hepatic portal vein, liver sinusoids, hepatic veins","Digestive organ capillaries, hepatic veins, liver sinusoids, hepatic portal vein","Hepatic portal vein, digestive organ capillaries, liver sinusoids, hepatic veins"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"A portal system has two capillary beds in series. Blood first passes through the capillaries of the digestive organs, where it picks up absorbed nutrients, then drains into the hepatic portal vein. That vein carries it to the second capillary bed, the sinusoids of the liver, and the hepatic veins finally return it toward the heart through the inferior vena cava.","notes":["This order puts the liver first, but blood reaches the liver only after collecting nutrients in the digestive organs. Starting at the liver would skip the reason the portal system exists.","Correct. Digestive capillaries, portal vein, liver sinusoids, then hepatic veins.","This order swaps the hepatic portal vein and the hepatic veins. The portal vein brings blood into the liver, while the hepatic veins carry it out toward the heart.","This order starts with the portal vein, but a portal vein always connects two capillary beds, so a capillary bed has to come before it."],"lt":"t-blood-vessels","lid":"c49","comp":69,"competencyId":"bvn-circulatory-routes"},{"id":"L01708","dok":2,"q":"Which choice correctly matches each circuit or arrangement to its route: the pulmonary circuit, the systemic circuit, and the hepatic portal system?","a":"Pulmonary to the lungs, systemic to the body, hepatic portal through the liver.","options":["Pulmonary to the body, systemic to the lungs, hepatic portal to the heart","Pulmonary to the lungs, systemic to the body, hepatic portal through the liver","Pulmonary to the liver, systemic to the lungs, hepatic portal to the body","Pulmonary to the body, systemic to the liver, hepatic portal to the lungs"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The pulmonary circuit carries blood from the right heart to the lungs and back to the left heart. The systemic circuit carries blood from the left heart out to the body and back to the right heart. The hepatic portal system is a detour within the systemic circuit that routes blood from the digestive organs through the liver.","notes":["This swaps the pulmonary and systemic circuits. Pulmonary refers to the lungs, so the pulmonary circuit goes to the lungs, and the hepatic portal system does not go to the heart.","Correct. Lungs, body, and liver, in that order.","This sends the pulmonary circuit to the liver and the systemic circuit to the lungs. Only the hepatic portal system involves the liver, and only the pulmonary circuit involves the lungs.","This match gets all three routes wrong. The word pulmonary, meaning lungs, and the word hepatic, meaning liver, give away the correct matches."],"lt":"t-blood-vessels","lid":"c52","comp":69,"competencyId":"bvn-circulatory-routes"},{"id":"L01729","dok":1,"q":"Which portal system links the hypothalamus to the anterior pituitary?","a":"The hypophyseal portal system.","options":["The hepatic portal system","The hypophyseal portal system","The coronary circulation","The pulmonary circuit"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The hypophyseal portal system connects capillaries in the hypothalamus to a second capillary bed in the anterior pituitary. Like any portal system, it passes blood through two capillary beds in series before the blood returns to the heart. Hypophysis is another name for the pituitary gland, which gives the system its name.","notes":["The hepatic portal system routes blood from the digestive organs through the liver. Pick it if the question asked which portal system carries absorbed nutrients to the liver.","Correct. The hypophyseal portal system links the hypothalamus and anterior pituitary.","The coronary circulation supplies the heart wall through the coronary arteries and cardiac veins. Choose it if the question asked which vessels feed the heart muscle.","The pulmonary circuit carries blood from the right heart to the lungs and back, with one capillary bed. It would be the answer if the question asked which circuit brings blood to the lungs for oxygen."],"lt":"t-blood-vessels","lid":"c22-x2","comp":69,"competencyId":"bvn-circulatory-routes"},{"id":"L01730","dok":1,"q":"What is a place where two vessels join, giving blood an alternate route if one path is blocked, called?","a":"An anastomosis.","options":["A fenestration","An anastomosis","A precapillary sphincter","A portal system"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"An anastomosis is a junction where two vessels join, so a tissue can be reached by more than one path. If one vessel is blocked, blood can still arrive through the other. This backup arrangement protects tissues such as those around joints and in the brain.","notes":["A fenestration is a small pore in the endothelium of some capillaries. Choose it if the question asked about the windows in kidney or intestinal capillaries.","Correct. An anastomosis is a junction that gives blood an alternate route.","A precapillary sphincter is a smooth muscle ring that opens or closes a capillary's entrance. It would be the answer if the question asked what controls blood entering a single capillary.","A portal system passes blood through two capillary beds in series, as in the route through the liver. Pick it if the question asked about blood crossing two capillary beds before returning to the heart."],"lt":"t-blood-vessels","lid":"c23-x2","comp":69,"competencyId":"bvn-circulatory-routes"},{"id":"L01743","dok":2,"q":"Atherosclerotic plaque slowly narrows the lumen of an artery. Why does this narrowing threaten the tissue downstream?","a":"A narrower lumen reduces blood flow, so the tissue beyond it receives less oxygen.","options":["The plaque weakens the wall until it balloons outward and bursts","Less blood gets through, so the tissue receives less oxygen","The plaque blocks the veins that drain the tissue","The narrowing speeds flow and floods the tissue with oxygen"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"In atherosclerosis, fatty plaque builds up in the artery wall and bulges inward, narrowing the lumen. Less blood can pass through a narrower channel, so the tissue supplied by that artery receives less oxygen. A clot can also form on the rough plaque and suddenly block the vessel, which is how plaques in the coronary arteries cause heart attacks.","notes":["A wall that weakens and balloons outward describes an aneurysm, not a narrowing plaque. Pick it if the question asked about a bulge in an artery wall that can rupture.","Correct. Reduced flow through the narrowed lumen starves the tissue of oxygen.","Atherosclerosis is a disease of arteries, not veins, and the problem is reduced delivery into the tissue. A blocked drainage vein would fit a question about varicose veins or a venous clot.","A narrower channel lets less blood through, not more. This choice confuses a faster jet through a tight spot with more total delivery to the tissue."],"lt":"t-blood-vessels","lid":"d3-t-blood-vessels-7","comp":70,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-disorders"},{"id":"L01745","dok":1,"q":"In atherosclerosis, what is an atheroma?","a":"A plaque, a deposit of fatty material, cells, and debris within the artery wall.","options":["A clot of fibrin and platelets sitting in the vessel lumen","A balloon-like outward bulge of a weakened artery wall","A deposit of fatty material, cells, and debris within the artery wall","A twisted, swollen superficial vein with failed valves"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"An atheroma, also called a plaque, is the lesion that defines atherosclerosis. It is a deposit of fatty material, cells, and debris that builds up inside the artery wall itself, starting where the smooth inner lining (the tunica intima) has been injured. As it grows it bulges into the lumen, the open channel of the vessel, and narrows it.","notes":["A clot of fibrin and platelets in the lumen is a thrombus. A thrombus can form on top of a roughened plaque, but it sits in the channel rather than in the wall, so choose it if the question asks what a thrombus is.","A balloon-like bulge of a weakened artery wall is an aneurysm. The wall pushes outward rather than filling with plaque, so pick this if the question asks what defines an aneurysm.","Correct. An atheroma is the fatty plaque that builds up within the artery wall.","A twisted, swollen superficial vein with failed valves is a varicose vein. That is a disorder of a vein, not of an artery wall, so it fits a question about pooled blood in the legs."],"lt":"t-blood-vessel-disorders","lid":"c1","comp":70,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-disorders"},{"id":"L01746","dok":1,"q":"Which structural change defines an aneurysm?","a":"A balloon-like bulge in a weakened artery wall.","options":["A clot lodged in an artery of the lung","A balloon-like bulge in a weakened artery wall","Inflammation along the wall of a vein","A fatty plaque growing within an artery wall"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"An aneurysm is a weak spot in an artery wall that balloons outward. Arteries carry blood under high pressure, so a weakened area stretches and bulges instead of holding its shape. Aneurysms are most common in the aorta and in the arteries of the brain, and the danger is that the thin bulge can rupture.","notes":["A clot lodged in a lung artery is a pulmonary embolism. It blocks the vessel rather than stretching its wall, so it fits a question about a clot that traveled from a deep leg vein.","Correct. An aneurysm is an outward, balloon-like bulge of a weakened artery wall.","Inflammation along a vein wall is phlebitis, which causes pain, redness, and tenderness along the vein. Choose it for a question about an inflamed vein, not a weakened artery.","A fatty plaque growing within the wall is an atheroma, the lesion of atherosclerosis. It bulges inward and narrows the lumen, so pick it if the question asks what defines atherosclerosis."],"lt":"t-blood-vessel-disorders","lid":"c2","comp":70,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-disorders"},{"id":"L01747","dok":1,"q":"In fetal circulation, where does the umbilical vein carry blood?","a":"From the placenta to the fetus.","options":["From the fetus to the placenta","From the placenta to the fetus","From the pulmonary trunk to the aorta","From the right atrium to the left atrium"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The placenta is where fetal blood picks up oxygen and nutrients from the mother's blood, so the vessel leaving the placenta carries the richest blood in the fetus. That vessel is the single umbilical vein, which runs through the umbilical cord to the fetus. It is called a vein because it carries blood toward the fetal heart, even though its blood is oxygen-rich.","notes":["Blood traveling from the fetus back to the placenta is carried by the two umbilical arteries, and it is oxygen-poor. Pick this direction if the question asks about the umbilical arteries.","Correct. The umbilical vein brings oxygen-rich blood from the placenta to the fetus.","Blood moving from the pulmonary trunk into the aorta uses the ductus arteriosus, a short vessel that lets blood skip the lungs. It fits a question about that shunt, not the umbilical vein.","Blood passing from the right atrium to the left atrium uses the foramen ovale, an opening in the interatrial septum. Choose this if the question asks what the foramen ovale does."],"lt":"t-blood-vessel-disorders","lid":"c3","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L01748","dok":1,"q":"In which part of the fetal heart is the foramen ovale located?","a":"The interatrial septum.","options":["The interatrial septum","The interventricular septum","The wall of the inferior vena cava","The wall of the pulmonary trunk"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The foramen ovale is an opening in the interatrial septum, the wall between the right and left atria. It lets blood pass directly from the right atrium to the left atrium, so that blood skips the right ventricle and the lungs, which are not yet used for gas exchange. After birth it closes and leaves a shallow depression called the fossa ovalis.","notes":["Correct. The foramen ovale is an opening in the wall between the two atria.","The interventricular septum separates the right and left ventricles and has no normal fetal opening. Pick it if the question asks which wall divides the ventricles; a hole there is a defect, not a fetal shunt.","The inferior vena cava is where the ductus venosus empties after carrying blood through the liver. It fits a question about the end of the ductus venosus, not the foramen ovale.","The pulmonary trunk is where the ductus arteriosus begins, carrying blood from the trunk into the aorta. Choose it for a question about where the ductus arteriosus attaches."],"lt":"t-blood-vessel-disorders","lid":"c4","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L01749","dok":1,"q":"Which description matches varicose veins?","a":"Superficial veins that become twisted and swollen when their valves fail.","options":["Arteries narrowed by fatty plaque","Superficial veins twisted and swollen from failed valves","Deep veins blocked by a stationary blood clot","Veins that are inflamed, red, and tender"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Veins carry blood under low pressure and rely on one-way valves to keep it moving toward the heart. When the valves in superficial veins fail, blood flows backward and pools, so the veins stretch, twist, and bulge visibly under the skin. This is most common in the legs, where blood has to climb against gravity.","notes":["Arteries narrowed by fatty plaque describe atherosclerosis, a disease of the high-pressure side of the circulation. Choose it for a question about plaque in an artery wall.","Correct. Varicose veins are superficial veins that twist and swell when their valves fail.","A clot blocking a deep vein is a deep vein thrombosis, usually in the leg. Pick it if the question asks about a clot that could break free and travel to the lungs.","Inflamed, red, tender veins describe phlebitis. It is an inflammation of the vein wall rather than pooled blood from failed valves, so it fits a question about an inflamed vein."],"lt":"t-blood-vessel-disorders","lid":"c5","comp":70,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-disorders"},{"id":"L01750","dok":1,"q":"The ductus arteriosus is a short fetal vessel that connects which two structures?","a":"The pulmonary trunk and the aorta.","options":["The pulmonary veins and the left atrium","The right atrium and the left atrium","The umbilical vein and the inferior vena cava","The pulmonary trunk and the aorta"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The ductus arteriosus runs from the pulmonary trunk to the aorta. Blood that enters the right ventricle and is pumped into the pulmonary trunk can cross straight into the aorta instead of going to the collapsed fetal lungs. After birth it closes and becomes the ligamentum arteriosum, a short cord between the same two vessels.","notes":["The pulmonary veins drain into the left atrium in both the fetus and the adult. That is a normal connection rather than a shunt, so it fits a question about where blood returning from the lungs enters the heart.","The right and left atria are connected by the foramen ovale, an opening in the interatrial septum. Choose this pair for a question about the foramen ovale.","The umbilical vein is connected to the inferior vena cava by the ductus venosus, the shunt that lets blood bypass the liver. Pick this pair if the question asks what the ductus venosus links.","Correct. The ductus arteriosus joins the pulmonary trunk to the aorta."],"lt":"t-blood-vessel-disorders","lid":"c6","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L01751","dok":1,"q":"What is a thrombus?","a":"A clot that can form on a roughened plaque and block the vessel.","options":["A clot fragment that breaks free and lodges elsewhere","A fatty deposit within the artery wall","A balloon-like bulge in an artery wall","A clot that forms in place and can block the vessel"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"A thrombus is a blood clot that forms and stays attached inside a vessel. In atherosclerosis, the roughened surface of a plaque is a common place for one to form, and it can suddenly block the vessel. If a piece breaks off and travels, that moving clot is then called an embolus.","notes":["A clot fragment that breaks free and travels is an embolus. Pick it if the question asks what forms when part of a thrombus breaks loose, as in a pulmonary embolism.","A fatty deposit within the wall is an atheroma, or plaque. A thrombus can form on top of a plaque, but the plaque itself is not a clot, so choose this for a question about atheromas.","A balloon-like bulge in an artery wall is an aneurysm, a weakening of the wall rather than a clot. Pick it if the question asks what an aneurysm is.","Correct. A thrombus is a clot that forms in place inside a vessel and can block it."],"lt":"t-blood-vessel-disorders","lid":"c7","comp":70,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-disorders"},{"id":"L01752","dok":1,"q":"How many umbilical arteries does a fetus have, and what do they carry?","a":"Two umbilical arteries carrying oxygen-poor blood from the fetus back to the placenta.","options":["Two arteries carrying oxygen-rich blood from the placenta to the fetus","One artery carrying oxygen-poor blood from the fetus to the placenta","Two arteries carrying oxygen-poor blood from the fetus to the placenta","One artery carrying oxygen-rich blood from the placenta to the fetus"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The umbilical cord normally holds two umbilical arteries and one umbilical vein. The arteries carry blood away from the fetal heart, so they take oxygen-poor, waste-laden blood from the fetus to the placenta, where it is refreshed. The single umbilical vein brings the oxygen-rich blood back.","notes":["This mixes the correct number with the umbilical vein's direction and oxygen content. Students choose it when they assume arteries always carry oxygen-rich blood, but the name follows direction from the heart, not oxygen level.","This has the right direction and oxygen content but the wrong number. The single vessel in the cord is the vein; there are two umbilical arteries.","Correct. Two umbilical arteries carry oxygen-poor blood from the fetus to the placenta.","One vessel carrying oxygen-rich blood from the placenta to the fetus describes the umbilical vein, not the arteries. Pick it if the question asks about the umbilical vein."],"lt":"t-blood-vessel-disorders","lid":"c8","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L01753","dok":1,"q":"What is the general term for the stiffening and thickening of artery walls?","a":"Arteriosclerosis.","options":["Arteriosclerosis","Atherosclerosis","Phlebitis","Hypertension"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Arteriosclerosis is the broad term for artery walls that become thicker and stiffer, so they lose their ability to stretch and recoil. The name means hardening of the arteries. Atherosclerosis is its most common form, the one caused specifically by fatty plaque in the wall.","notes":["Correct. Arteriosclerosis is the general term for stiff, thickened artery walls.","Atherosclerosis is the most common form of arteriosclerosis, the specific one caused by fatty plaque in the wall. Pick it if the question asks about plaque buildup rather than the general term.","Phlebitis is inflammation of a vein, causing pain, redness, and tenderness along the vein. Choose it for a question about an inflamed vein, not stiff arteries.","Hypertension is chronically high pressure within the arteries. It strains vessel walls over time, but it names the pressure, not the wall change, so pick it for a question about high blood pressure."],"lt":"t-blood-vessel-disorders","lid":"c9","comp":70,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-disorders"},{"id":"L01754","dok":1,"q":"What is a deep vein thrombosis?","a":"A blood clot in a deep vein, usually of the leg.","options":["A blood clot in a deep vein, usually of the leg","A twisted, swollen vein near the skin","A clot lodged in an artery of the lung","Inflammation along the wall of a vein"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"A deep vein thrombosis is a clot that forms in one of the deep veins, most often in the leg, where blood moves slowly under low pressure. It matters because of where the clot can go. If it breaks free, it rides the normal venous return to the right side of the heart and on to the lungs.","notes":["Correct. A deep vein thrombosis is a clot in a deep vein, usually of the leg.","A twisted, swollen vein near the skin is a varicose vein, caused by failed valves rather than a clot. Choose it for a question about superficial veins that bulge visibly.","A clot lodged in a lung artery is a pulmonary embolism, often the result of a deep vein thrombosis that broke loose. Pick it if the question asks where a traveling leg clot ends up.","Inflammation along a vein wall is phlebitis. It can accompany a clot, but it names the inflammation, so pick it for a question about a red, tender vein."],"lt":"t-blood-vessel-disorders","lid":"c10","comp":70,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-disorders"},{"id":"L01755","dok":1,"q":"An atherosclerotic plaque starts with injury to which layer of the artery wall?","a":"The tunica intima, the endothelial lining.","options":["The tunica intima, the endothelial lining","The valves that line the inside of the vessel","The tunica externa, the outer connective tissue layer","The tunica media, the smooth muscle layer"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Atherosclerosis begins with damage to the endothelium, the smooth simple squamous lining of the tunica intima. That lining is the layer in direct contact with the blood. Once it is injured, fatty material, cells, and debris collect in the wall at that spot and the plaque begins to grow inward toward the lumen.","notes":["Correct. The injury that starts a plaque is in the tunica intima, the endothelial lining.","Arteries away from the heart do not have valves; one-way valves are a feature of veins. This fits a question about what fails in varicose veins or chronic venous insufficiency.","The tunica externa is the outer connective tissue layer that anchors the vessel to nearby structures. Choose it for a question about the outermost tunic, not the one touching the blood.","The tunica media is the middle layer of smooth muscle and elastic fibers, the thickest layer in arteries. Pick it if the question asks which layer lets an artery constrict and dilate."],"lt":"t-blood-vessel-disorders","lid":"c11","comp":70,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-disorders"},{"id":"L01756","dok":2,"q":"A fetus routes blood around its collapsed lungs. Which two shunts together accomplish this lung bypass?","a":"The foramen ovale and the ductus arteriosus.","options":["The umbilical vein and the ductus venosus","The foramen ovale and the ductus arteriosus","The ductus venosus and the foramen ovale","The ductus venosus and the ductus arteriosus"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Two shunts keep most blood away from the fetal lungs. The foramen ovale lets blood cross from the right atrium to the left atrium before it ever reaches the right ventricle. Blood that does enter the pulmonary trunk can cross through the ductus arteriosus into the aorta. The third shunt, the ductus venosus, bypasses the liver, not the lungs.","notes":["The umbilical vein and the ductus venosus are the route oxygen-rich blood takes from the placenta through the liver toward the inferior vena cava. Pick this pair for a question about the path from the placenta, not the lung bypass.","Correct. The foramen ovale and the ductus arteriosus together route blood around the lungs.","This pair includes the ductus venosus, which bypasses the liver. Only the foramen ovale in this pair skips the lungs, so it would fit a question about one liver shunt and one lung shunt.","This pair also includes the ductus venosus, the liver bypass. Students pick it when they remember that both names start with ductus and assume both skip the lungs."],"lt":"t-blood-vessel-disorders","lid":"c12","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L01757","dok":2,"q":"The ductus venosus passes through which fetal organ, letting most blood bypass it?","a":"The liver.","options":["The kidneys","The spleen","The lungs","The liver"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The ductus venosus is a shunt that runs through the fetal liver. Oxygen-rich blood arriving in the umbilical vein can flow through it straight toward the inferior vena cava instead of passing through the liver tissue. The placenta handles much of the work the liver will do after birth, so this detour costs the fetus very little.","notes":["The kidneys have no fetal bypass shunt. This choice comes from guessing an abdominal organ; the fetal shunts only skip the liver and the lungs.","The spleen has no fetal shunt. Students may choose it because it sits near the liver in the upper abdomen, but the ductus venosus is named for its connection to the venous system through the liver.","The lungs are bypassed by two other shunts, the foramen ovale and the ductus arteriosus. Pick the lungs if the question asks what organ those two shunts route blood around.","Correct. The ductus venosus carries blood through the liver so most of it skips the liver tissue."],"lt":"t-blood-vessel-disorders","lid":"c13","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L01758","dok":2,"q":"Atherosclerosis narrowing the coronary arteries produces which named disorder?","a":"Coronary artery disease.","options":["Peripheral artery disease","Stroke","Hypertension","Coronary artery disease"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The coronary arteries supply the myocardium, the muscle of the heart wall. When atherosclerotic plaque narrows them, the disorder is called coronary artery disease. It can starve the heart muscle of oxygen and lead to a heart attack. The same plaque in other arteries gets a different name based on location.","notes":["Peripheral artery disease is the same plaque narrowing the arteries of the limbs, usually the legs. Pick it if the narrowed vessel is a leg artery.","A stroke is a loss of blood supply to part of the brain from a blocked or burst artery. Choose it when the narrowed or ruptured vessel supplies the brain.","Hypertension is chronically high pressure within the arteries. It names a pressure problem, not narrowing of one vessel, so pick it for a question about high blood pressure.","Correct. Plaque narrowing the coronary arteries is coronary artery disease."],"lt":"t-blood-vessel-disorders","lid":"c14","comp":70,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-disorders"},{"id":"L01759","dok":2,"q":"Hemorrhoids are best classified as which kind of vessel disorder?","a":"Varicose veins of the rectal and anal region.","options":["Atherosclerosis of the rectal arteries","Varicose veins of the rectal and anal region","An aneurysm of a rectal artery","Inflammation of a rectal vein"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Hemorrhoids are varicose veins in the rectal and anal region. Just as in the legs, the veins there stretch, swell, and bulge when blood pools in them. They are a venous disorder, which fits the general pattern that vein problems involve pooled blood under low pressure.","notes":["Atherosclerosis is fatty plaque narrowing an artery. Students choose it because hemorrhoids involve swollen vessels, but hemorrhoids are veins, and plaque is an artery problem.","Correct. Hemorrhoids are varicose veins of the rectal and anal region.","An aneurysm is a balloon-like bulge of a weakened artery wall, most common in the aorta and brain arteries. Pick it for a question about a weakened artery that could rupture.","Inflammation of a vein is phlebitis. Choose it if the question describes pain, redness, and tenderness along a vein rather than swollen, pooled veins."],"lt":"t-blood-vessel-disorders","lid":"c15","comp":70,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-disorders"},{"id":"L01760","dok":2,"q":"A clot from a leg vein breaks free and lodges in an artery of the lung. What is this disorder called?","a":"Pulmonary embolism.","options":["Phlebitis","Pulmonary embolism","Chronic venous insufficiency","Deep vein thrombosis"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"An embolus is a clot or fragment that travels through the bloodstream and lodges somewhere else. When that clot ends up in an artery of the lung, the disorder is a pulmonary embolism. It is a life-threatening emergency because it blocks blood flow to part of the lung, and the clot most often comes from a deep vein of the leg.","notes":["Phlebitis is inflammation of a vein, with pain, redness, and tenderness along it. Choose it for a question about an inflamed vein, not a traveling clot.","Correct. A traveling clot that lodges in a lung artery is a pulmonary embolism.","Chronic venous insufficiency is long-term failure of the leg vein valves, causing swelling and skin changes in the lower leg. It fits a question about persistent leg swelling from failed valves.","A deep vein thrombosis is the clot while it is still in the deep leg vein. Pick it if the question describes where the clot first forms rather than where it lodges."],"lt":"t-blood-vessel-disorders","lid":"c16","comp":70,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-disorders"},{"id":"L01761","dok":2,"q":"After birth the umbilical vein closes. Which adult structure does it leave behind?","a":"The ligamentum teres, the round ligament of the liver.","options":["The ligamentum arteriosum","The fossa ovalis","The ligamentum venosum","The ligamentum teres"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Once the cord is cut, the umbilical vein no longer carries blood and closes into a fibrous cord called the ligamentum teres, also known as the round ligament of the liver. It runs from the umbilicus to the liver in the free edge of the falciform ligament, following the path the vein once took.","notes":["The ligamentum arteriosum is the remnant of the ductus arteriosus, a short cord between the pulmonary trunk and the aorta. Choose it for a question about that shunt.","The fossa ovalis is the shallow depression left in the interatrial septum when the foramen ovale closes. It fits a question about the foramen ovale.","The ligamentum venosum is the remnant of the ductus venosus, the shunt that ran through the liver. Pick it if the question asks what the ductus venosus becomes.","Correct. The umbilical vein becomes the ligamentum teres, the round ligament of the liver."],"lt":"t-blood-vessel-disorders","lid":"c17","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L01762","dok":2,"q":"Peripheral artery disease is the narrowing of arteries in which region?","a":"The limbs, usually the legs.","options":["The coronary arteries of the heart wall","The aorta and its main trunk","The limbs, usually the legs","The arteries of the brain"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Peripheral artery disease is atherosclerotic narrowing of the arteries that supply the limbs, most often the legs. The word peripheral points to vessels away from the body's core. Because the leg muscles are starved of blood, the typical sign is pain in the legs during walking that eases with rest.","notes":["Narrowing of the coronary arteries is coronary artery disease, which can starve the heart wall. Choose it for a question about plaque in the vessels of the heart.","The aorta is a common site for an aneurysm, a balloon-like bulge of a weakened wall. Pick it if the question asks where aneurysms commonly form.","Correct. Peripheral artery disease narrows the arteries of the limbs, usually the legs.","Narrowing of the brain arteries can lead to a stroke, a loss of blood supply to part of the brain. Pick the brain arteries if the question asks where narrowing causes a stroke."],"lt":"t-blood-vessel-disorders","lid":"c18","comp":70,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-disorders"},{"id":"L01763","dok":2,"q":"The foramen ovale closes after birth. Which adult landmark does it leave?","a":"The fossa ovalis, a shallow oval depression in the interatrial septum.","options":["The ligamentum arteriosum","The medial umbilical ligaments","The ligamentum venosum","The fossa ovalis"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"When the lungs inflate at birth, pressure in the left atrium rises and the flap over the foramen ovale is pressed shut. The sealed opening leaves a shallow oval depression in the interatrial septum called the fossa ovalis, visible from the right atrium. It is the one fetal remnant that is a depression rather than a cord, because the foramen ovale was a hole, not a vessel.","notes":["The ligamentum arteriosum is the remnant of the ductus arteriosus, a short cord between the pulmonary trunk and aorta. Choose it for a question about that shunt.","The medial umbilical ligaments are the remnants of the two umbilical arteries, paired cords on the inner surface of the anterior abdominal wall. They fit a question about the umbilical arteries.","The ligamentum venosum is the remnant of the ductus venosus, a fibrous cord on the inferior surface of the liver. Pick it for a question about the liver shunt.","Correct. The foramen ovale leaves the fossa ovalis, a shallow depression in the interatrial septum."],"lt":"t-blood-vessel-disorders","lid":"c19","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L01764","dok":2,"q":"Which adult structures are the remnants of the paired umbilical arteries?","a":"The medial umbilical ligaments.","options":["The ligamentum arteriosum","The ligamentum teres","The medial umbilical ligaments","The ligamentum venosum"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"After birth the two umbilical arteries no longer carry blood to the placenta, and most of each one closes into a fibrous cord. These cords are the medial umbilical ligaments, which run up the inner surface of the anterior abdominal wall toward the umbilicus. There are two of them because there were two arteries.","notes":["The ligamentum arteriosum is the remnant of the ductus arteriosus. Students choose it because the word arteriosum sounds like artery, but it lies between the pulmonary trunk and the aorta, not in the abdominal wall.","The ligamentum teres is the remnant of the single umbilical vein, running to the liver in the falciform ligament. Pick it for a question about the umbilical vein.","Correct. The two umbilical arteries become the medial umbilical ligaments.","The ligamentum venosum is the remnant of the ductus venosus, a cord on the inferior surface of the liver. Choose it for a question about the liver shunt."],"lt":"t-blood-vessel-disorders","lid":"c20","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L01765","dok":2,"q":"As an atheroma grows in an artery wall, how does it change the vessel's channel?","a":"It bulges inward and narrows the lumen.","options":["It blocks the valves that keep arterial blood moving one way","It stiffens only the outer layer and leaves the lumen unchanged","It bulges outward and widens the lumen","It bulges inward and narrows the lumen"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"An atheroma builds up within the wall just beneath the endothelium, so as it grows it pushes into the lumen, the open channel where blood flows. A narrower lumen carries less blood to the tissue downstream. That reduced flow is what links atherosclerosis to coronary artery disease, stroke, and peripheral artery disease.","notes":["Arteries away from the heart have no valves; one-way valves belong to veins. This fits a question about what fails in varicose veins.","Plaque forms in the inner part of the wall, not the outer layer, and it does change the lumen. Students choose this when they picture arteriosclerosis as only a hardened outer coat.","An outward bulge describes an aneurysm, where a weakened wall balloons outward. Pick it if the question asks how an aneurysm changes the vessel's shape.","Correct. A growing plaque bulges inward and narrows the lumen."],"lt":"t-blood-vessel-disorders","lid":"c21","comp":70,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-disorders"},{"id":"L01766","dok":2,"q":"Chronic venous insufficiency results from the long-term failure of which structures?","a":"The valves of the leg veins.","options":["The walls of the aorta and large arteries","The valves of the leg veins","The interatrial septum of the heart","The endothelial lining of the coronary arteries"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Leg veins depend on one-way valves to keep blood moving up toward the heart against gravity. In chronic venous insufficiency those valves fail over a long period, so blood pools in the lower leg. The result is persistent swelling and skin changes around the ankles and lower leg.","notes":["Weakness or stiffening of the aorta and large artery walls fits an aneurysm or arteriosclerosis. Choose it for a question about the high-pressure arterial side.","Correct. Chronic venous insufficiency is long-term failure of the leg vein valves.","The interatrial septum holds the fossa ovalis; if the foramen ovale fails to seal, the result is a patent foramen ovale. It fits a question about that fetal opening.","Injury to the endothelial lining of the coronary arteries is where atherosclerosis begins, leading to coronary artery disease. Pick it for a question about how coronary plaque starts."],"lt":"t-blood-vessel-disorders","lid":"c22","comp":70,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-disorders"},{"id":"L01767","dok":2,"q":"The ductus arteriosus closes after birth. Which adult cord does it become?","a":"The ligamentum arteriosum.","options":["The ligamentum venosum","The ligamentum teres","The fossa ovalis","The ligamentum arteriosum"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Once the lungs take over gas exchange, blood no longer needs to skip them, and the ductus arteriosus closes. It becomes the ligamentum arteriosum, a short fibrous cord that still connects the pulmonary trunk to the aortic arch. The remnant keeps a piece of the original name, which makes the pair easy to match.","notes":["The ligamentum venosum is the remnant of the ductus venosus, the liver shunt. Pick it when the question names the ductus venosus.","The ligamentum teres is the remnant of the umbilical vein, running to the liver in the falciform ligament. Choose it for a question about the umbilical vein.","The fossa ovalis is the depression left by the foramen ovale in the interatrial septum. It fits a question about the opening between the atria.","Correct. The ductus arteriosus becomes the ligamentum arteriosum."],"lt":"t-blood-vessel-disorders","lid":"c23","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L01768","dok":2,"q":"On a diagram of a fetal heart, a short vessel runs from the pulmonary trunk to the aorta. Which shunt is it?","a":"The ductus arteriosus.","options":["The ductus venosus","The foramen ovale","The umbilical vein","The ductus arteriosus"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The ductus arteriosus is the short vessel linking the pulmonary trunk to the aorta. Because fetal lung tissue resists blood flow, most blood pumped into the pulmonary trunk takes this shortcut into the aorta and out to the body. Its location between two great arteries is the clue to its name.","notes":["The ductus venosus is a shunt inside the liver, carrying blood from the umbilical vein toward the inferior vena cava. Pick it for a diagram of the fetal liver.","The foramen ovale is an opening in the interatrial septum, not a vessel. Choose it if the diagram shows a hole between the two atria.","The umbilical vein runs in the umbilical cord, bringing oxygen-rich blood from the placenta. It fits a question about the vessel arriving from the placenta.","Correct. The vessel between the pulmonary trunk and aorta is the ductus arteriosus."],"lt":"t-blood-vessel-disorders","lid":"c24","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L01769","dok":2,"q":"A fetal shunt is described as an opening that lets blood pass directly from one atrium to the other. Which shunt is it?","a":"The foramen ovale.","options":["The ductus venosus","An umbilical artery","The ductus arteriosus","The foramen ovale"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The foramen ovale is an opening in the interatrial septum. Blood entering the right atrium from the inferior vena cava can pass straight through it into the left atrium, skipping the right ventricle and the lungs. The word foramen means opening, which fits a hole in a wall rather than a vessel.","notes":["The ductus venosus is a vessel that carries blood through the liver toward the inferior vena cava. Choose it for a description of the liver bypass.","An umbilical artery carries oxygen-poor blood from the fetus to the placenta in the umbilical cord. It fits a description of a vessel in the cord leading away from the fetus.","The ductus arteriosus is a short vessel between the pulmonary trunk and the aorta, not an opening between the atria. Pick it if the description names those two great arteries.","Correct. The opening between the two atria is the foramen ovale."],"lt":"t-blood-vessel-disorders","lid":"c25","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L01770","dok":3,"q":"An adult liver shows a fibrous cord on its inferior surface that ends at the inferior vena cava. Which fetal shunt did this cord once carry blood as?","a":"The ductus venosus.","options":["An umbilical artery","The ductus arteriosus","The umbilical vein","The ductus venosus"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The ductus venosus carried blood through the fetal liver toward the inferior vena cava. After birth it closes and becomes the ligamentum venosum, a fibrous cord on the inferior surface of the liver that still reaches the inferior vena cava. Its position on the liver and its connection to the vena cava both point back to the ductus venosus.","notes":["An umbilical artery leaves a medial umbilical ligament on the inner surface of the anterior abdominal wall. Choose it for a cord on the abdominal wall, not the liver.","The ductus arteriosus leaves the ligamentum arteriosum, a cord between the pulmonary trunk and the aorta in the thorax. Pick it if the cord were found above the heart.","The umbilical vein leaves the ligamentum teres, which reaches the liver from the umbilicus in the free edge of the falciform ligament. Pick it for a cord running from the umbilicus to the liver, not one ending at the vena cava.","Correct. The cord on the inferior liver surface ending at the inferior vena cava is the ligamentum venosum, left by the ductus venosus."],"lt":"t-blood-vessel-disorders","lid":"c26","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L01771","dok":2,"q":"A vessel disorder is described as a balloon-like outpouching commonly found in the aorta or brain arteries. Which disorder is it?","a":"An aneurysm.","options":["Hypertension","A stroke","Atherosclerosis","An aneurysm"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"An aneurysm is a weakened area of an artery wall that bulges outward like a balloon. The aorta and the arteries of the brain are the most common sites. The high pressure in arteries keeps stretching the thin bulge, which is why an aneurysm can rupture and bleed.","notes":["Hypertension is chronically high pressure within the arteries. It can stress a weak wall, but it is not a bulge itself, so choose it for a description of high blood pressure.","A stroke is a loss of blood supply to part of the brain. A ruptured brain aneurysm can cause one, but the stroke is the result, so pick it for a description of brain tissue losing its supply.","Atherosclerosis is fatty plaque that bulges inward and narrows an artery. Pick it if the description is of a narrowed vessel rather than an outward bulge.","Correct. A balloon-like outpouching of an artery wall is an aneurysm."],"lt":"t-blood-vessel-disorders","lid":"c27","comp":70,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-disorders"},{"id":"L01772","dok":2,"q":"A vessel disorder is described as a loss of blood supply to part of the brain from a blocked or burst artery. Which disorder is it?","a":"A stroke.","options":["A stroke","Coronary artery disease","Phlebitis","Peripheral artery disease"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"A stroke happens when part of the brain loses its blood supply, either because an artery is blocked, often by atherosclerosis or a clot, or because it bursts. Brain tissue begins to die within minutes without blood. It is one of the main diseases that atherosclerosis causes, named for its location in the brain.","notes":["Correct. Loss of blood supply to part of the brain is a stroke.","Coronary artery disease is narrowing of the coronary arteries that supply the heart wall. Pick it if the description involves the heart muscle rather than the brain.","Phlebitis is inflammation of a vein, causing pain, redness, and tenderness. It fits a description of an inflamed vein, not an artery supplying the brain.","Peripheral artery disease is narrowing of the arteries of the limbs, usually the legs. Choose it for a description of leg pain during walking."],"lt":"t-blood-vessel-disorders","lid":"c28","comp":70,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-disorders"},{"id":"L01773","dok":3,"q":"In a cadaver, paired fibrous cords run up the inner surface of the anterior abdominal wall toward the umbilicus. Which fetal vessels are these the remnants of?","a":"The umbilical arteries.","options":["The ductus venosus","The umbilical vein","The umbilical arteries","The ductus arteriosus"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The two umbilical arteries carried oxygen-poor blood from the fetus out through the umbilicus to the placenta. After birth they close into the medial umbilical ligaments, which run up the inner surface of the anterior abdominal wall toward the umbilicus. The fact that the cords are paired matches the two arteries.","notes":["The ductus venosus leaves the ligamentum venosum on the inferior surface of the liver. Choose it if the cord were found on the liver.","The umbilical vein is single, and its remnant, the ligamentum teres, runs from the umbilicus up to the liver. Pick it for a single cord heading to the liver, not a pair on the abdominal wall.","Correct. Paired cords on the inner anterior abdominal wall are the medial umbilical ligaments, left by the umbilical arteries.","The ductus arteriosus leaves the ligamentum arteriosum in the thorax, between the pulmonary trunk and the aorta. It fits a cord found above the heart."],"lt":"t-blood-vessel-disorders","lid":"c29","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L01774","dok":2,"q":"A fetal shunt is described as passing through the liver and emptying toward the inferior vena cava. Which shunt is it?","a":"The ductus venosus.","options":["The foramen ovale","The ductus venosus","The ductus arteriosus","An umbilical artery"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The ductus venosus carries oxygen-rich blood from the umbilical vein through the liver and on toward the inferior vena cava, so most of it skips the liver tissue. From the vena cava the blood enters the right atrium. Its name, venosus, reflects that it links two parts of the venous system.","notes":["The foramen ovale is an opening in the interatrial septum, letting blood pass from the right atrium to the left atrium. Pick it for a description of a hole between the atria.","Correct. The shunt through the liver toward the inferior vena cava is the ductus venosus.","The ductus arteriosus is a short vessel from the pulmonary trunk to the aorta, letting blood skip the lungs. Choose it for a description involving the great arteries.","An umbilical artery carries oxygen-poor blood from the fetus to the placenta. It fits a description of a vessel in the umbilical cord leading away from the fetus."],"lt":"t-blood-vessel-disorders","lid":"c30","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L01775","dok":2,"q":"A patient's superficial leg veins have become twisted and bulge visibly under the skin. Which disorder is this?","a":"Varicose veins.","options":["An aortic aneurysm","Varicose veins","Deep vein thrombosis","Pulmonary embolism"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Varicose veins are superficial veins whose one-way valves have failed. Blood flows backward and pools, so the veins stretch, twist, and bulge where they can be seen under the skin. The legs are the usual site because blood there has to return to the heart against gravity.","notes":["An aortic aneurysm is a balloon-like bulge of a weakened aortic wall. It is an artery disorder deep in the trunk, so it fits a description of a bulging aorta, not leg veins.","Correct. Twisted, bulging superficial veins in the legs are varicose veins.","A deep vein thrombosis is a clot in a deep vein, usually of the leg. It is hidden in the deep tissues rather than visible under the skin, so pick it if the description is a clot in a deep vein.","A pulmonary embolism is a clot lodged in an artery of the lung, often one that traveled from a deep leg vein. Choose it for a description of a clot blocking blood flow to the lung."],"lt":"t-blood-vessel-disorders","lid":"c31","comp":70,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-disorders"},{"id":"L01776","dok":3,"q":"In an adult heart, the interatrial septum shows a shallow oval depression where an opening once sat. Which fetal structure left this depression?","a":"The foramen ovale.","options":["The umbilical vein","The ductus venosus","The ductus arteriosus","The foramen ovale"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The shallow depression is the fossa ovalis, and it marks where the foramen ovale used to be. In the fetus the foramen ovale was an opening in the interatrial septum that let blood pass from the right atrium to the left. Because it was a hole rather than a vessel, it closes into a depression instead of a fibrous cord.","notes":["The umbilical vein leaves the ligamentum teres, which runs from the umbilicus to the liver. It fits a cord in the falciform ligament, not a mark inside the heart.","The ductus venosus leaves the ligamentum venosum on the inferior surface of the liver. Choose it if the remnant were found on the liver.","The ductus arteriosus leaves a cord, the ligamentum arteriosum, between the pulmonary trunk and the aorta. Pick it if the remnant were a cord outside the heart near the great arteries.","Correct. The fossa ovalis in the interatrial septum is the remnant of the foramen ovale."],"lt":"t-blood-vessel-disorders","lid":"c32","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L01777","dok":2,"q":"A vessel runs in the umbilical cord and carries oxygen-rich blood toward the fetus. Which vessel is it?","a":"The umbilical vein.","options":["The ductus venosus","The ductus arteriosus","An umbilical artery","The umbilical vein"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The umbilical vein carries blood from the placenta, where it has picked up oxygen and nutrients, through the cord to the fetus. It is a vein because it carries blood toward the fetal heart, even though its blood is oxygen-rich. This is a good reminder that vessels are named by direction of flow, not by oxygen content.","notes":["The ductus venosus continues on from the umbilical vein inside the fetal liver, not in the cord. Choose it for a vessel that carries blood through the liver toward the inferior vena cava.","The ductus arteriosus lies in the fetal chest between the pulmonary trunk and the aorta. It fits a description of the lung bypass, not a vessel in the cord.","An umbilical artery also runs in the cord, but it carries oxygen-poor blood away from the fetus to the placenta. Pick it if the question describes flow toward the placenta.","Correct. The umbilical vein carries oxygen-rich blood from the placenta toward the fetus."],"lt":"t-blood-vessel-disorders","lid":"c33","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L01778","dok":2,"q":"In a newborn, the ductus arteriosus fails to seal, leaving an abnormal connection between the pulmonary trunk and the aorta. What is this condition called?","a":"Patent ductus arteriosus.","options":["Patent ductus arteriosus","Ligamentum arteriosum","Patent foramen ovale","Aortic aneurysm"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Patent means open. When the ductus arteriosus stays open after birth instead of closing, the condition is named patent ductus arteriosus, after the fetal structure that failed to seal. It leaves an abnormal connection between the pulmonary and systemic sides of the circulation.","notes":["Correct. A ductus arteriosus that stays open after birth is a patent ductus arteriosus.","The ligamentum arteriosum is the normal cord left when the ductus arteriosus closes as expected. Choose it for a question about the healthy adult remnant, not a failure to close.","A patent foramen ovale is the opening in the interatrial septum that fails to seal. Pick it if the open connection is between the two atria.","An aortic aneurysm is a balloon-like bulge of a weakened aortic wall. It fits a question about a weakened artery that could rupture, not a fetal shunt that stayed open."],"lt":"t-blood-vessel-disorders","lid":"c34","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L01779","dok":2,"q":"A disorder is described as fatty plaques building up within artery walls, beginning at an injured inner lining. Which disorder is it?","a":"Atherosclerosis.","options":["Atherosclerosis","Varicose veins","An aneurysm","Phlebitis"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Atherosclerosis begins with injury to the endothelium of the tunica intima, the smooth inner lining of an artery. Fatty material, cells, and debris then collect in the wall at that spot as a plaque, or atheroma, which bulges into the lumen and narrows it. It is the root of most heart attacks and strokes.","notes":["Correct. Fatty plaque building up in artery walls is atherosclerosis.","Varicose veins are superficial veins twisted and swollen from failed valves. They fit a description of pooled blood in veins, not plaque in arteries.","An aneurysm is a balloon-like bulge in a weakened artery wall. Pick it if the description is an outward bulge rather than a plaque.","Phlebitis is inflammation of a vein, causing pain, redness, and tenderness. Choose it for a description of an inflamed vein, not plaque in an artery."],"lt":"t-blood-vessel-disorders","lid":"c35","comp":70,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-disorders"},{"id":"L01780","dok":2,"q":"Trace oxygen-rich fetal blood from the placenta to the heart. Which sequence is correct?","a":"Umbilical vein, then ductus venosus, then inferior vena cava, then right atrium.","options":["Umbilical vein, then foramen ovale, then ductus venosus, then left atrium","Ductus venosus, then umbilical vein, then aorta, then right atrium","Umbilical artery, then ductus arteriosus, then aorta, then right atrium","Umbilical vein, then ductus venosus, then inferior vena cava, then right atrium"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Oxygen-rich blood leaves the placenta in the umbilical vein and travels through the cord to the fetus. At the liver, most of it takes the ductus venosus, which skips the liver tissue and empties toward the inferior vena cava. The inferior vena cava then delivers it to the right atrium, where much of it will cross the foramen ovale.","notes":["The foramen ovale is inside the heart, between the atria, so blood cannot reach it before passing through the liver shunt. This order would fit only if the foramen ovale came after the right atrium.","This reverses the first two steps. Blood must arrive in the umbilical vein before it can enter the ductus venosus, and it reaches the heart by the inferior vena cava, not the aorta.","Umbilical arteries carry blood away from the fetus to the placenta, and the ductus arteriosus joins the pulmonary trunk to the aorta. This sequence runs in the wrong direction and mixes in the lung bypass.","Correct. Umbilical vein, ductus venosus, inferior vena cava, then right atrium."],"lt":"t-blood-vessel-disorders","lid":"c36","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L01781","dok":2,"q":"Match each fetal structure to its adult remnant: umbilical vein, ductus venosus, foramen ovale. Which set of pairings is fully correct?","a":"Umbilical vein to ligamentum teres, ductus venosus to ligamentum venosum, foramen ovale to fossa ovalis.","options":["Umbilical vein to ligamentum teres, ductus venosus to ligamentum venosum, foramen ovale to fossa ovalis","Umbilical vein to ligamentum venosum, ductus venosus to fossa ovalis, foramen ovale to ligamentum teres","Umbilical vein to ligamentum arteriosum, ductus venosus to fossa ovalis, foramen ovale to ligamentum teres","Umbilical vein to fossa ovalis, ductus venosus to ligamentum teres, foramen ovale to ligamentum venosum"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Each remnant keeps a clue to the original. The ductus venosus becomes the ligamentum venosum, and the foramen ovale, an oval hole, becomes the fossa ovalis, an oval depression. The umbilical vein is the odd name out: it becomes the ligamentum teres, the round ligament of the liver, running in the free edge of the falciform ligament.","notes":["Correct. Ligamentum teres, ligamentum venosum, and fossa ovalis, in that order.","This set swaps the umbilical vein and ductus venosus remnants. The ligamentum venosum belongs to the ductus venosus, since the names match, and the fossa ovalis belongs to the foramen ovale.","This set uses the ligamentum arteriosum, which is the remnant of the ductus arteriosus, a structure not in this list. Choose that pairing only for a question that includes the ductus arteriosus.","This set sends the umbilical vein to the fossa ovalis, but the fossa ovalis is a depression in the heart left by the foramen ovale. The umbilical vein becomes a cord running to the liver, the ligamentum teres."],"lt":"t-blood-vessel-disorders","lid":"c37","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L01782","dok":2,"q":"A clot breaks free from a deep leg vein and lodges in a lung artery. Which route does it follow?","a":"Deep leg vein, then inferior vena cava, then right atrium and right ventricle, then pulmonary artery.","options":["Deep leg vein, then thoracic duct, then right atrium and right ventricle, then pulmonary artery","Deep leg vein, then inferior vena cava, then right atrium and right ventricle, then pulmonary artery","Deep leg vein, then aorta, then left atrium and left ventricle, then pulmonary artery","Deep leg vein, then pulmonary vein, then left atrium and left ventricle, then pulmonary artery"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"A loose clot simply follows the normal venous return. Leg veins drain upward into the inferior vena cava, which empties into the right atrium. The right ventricle then pumps blood into the pulmonary trunk and arteries, and the first vessels narrow enough to trap the clot are in the lungs, causing a pulmonary embolism.","notes":["The thoracic duct is a lymphatic vessel that returns lymph to the left subclavian vein, not a path for venous blood from the leg. Pick it for a question about where lymph from the leg drains.","Correct. The clot travels up the inferior vena cava, through the right heart, and into a pulmonary artery.","The aorta carries blood away from the left ventricle to the body, so venous blood from the leg never enters it directly. This route would fit an arterial clot heading out to the body, not one returning from a vein.","Pulmonary veins carry blood from the lungs to the left atrium. A clot can only reach them after passing through the lungs, so this order runs the pulmonary circuit backward."],"lt":"t-blood-vessel-disorders","lid":"c38","comp":69,"competencyId":"bvn-circulatory-routes"},{"id":"L01783","dok":2,"q":"Oxygen-rich blood travels from the placenta into the fetal body. In what order does it reach the three fetal shunts: ductus venosus, foramen ovale, and ductus arteriosus?","a":"Ductus venosus first, then foramen ovale, then ductus arteriosus.","options":["Foramen ovale first, then ductus venosus, then ductus arteriosus","Ductus venosus first, then ductus arteriosus, then foramen ovale","Ductus arteriosus first, then foramen ovale, then ductus venosus","Ductus venosus first, then foramen ovale, then ductus arteriosus"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Blood from the placenta arrives in the umbilical vein and meets the ductus venosus first, at the liver. It then flows through the inferior vena cava into the right atrium, where the foramen ovale lets much of it cross to the left atrium. Blood that instead enters the right ventricle is pumped into the pulmonary trunk, where the ductus arteriosus carries it on to the aorta.","notes":["The foramen ovale is in the heart, so blood cannot reach it before passing the liver. The ductus venosus has to come first because the umbilical vein reaches the liver before the heart.","This puts the ductus arteriosus before the foramen ovale. The atria come before the ventricle and pulmonary trunk, so blood meets the opening between the atria before the vessel between the great arteries.","This is the full reverse of the real path. It would describe blood traveling back from the great arteries toward the placenta, which is not how fetal blood flows.","Correct. Ductus venosus, then foramen ovale, then ductus arteriosus."],"lt":"t-blood-vessel-disorders","lid":"c39","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L01784","dok":2,"q":"Atherosclerosis gets a different disease name depending on which artery it narrows. Match a coronary artery, a leg artery, and a brain artery to the named disorder.","a":"Coronary artery to coronary artery disease, leg artery to peripheral artery disease, brain artery to stroke.","options":["Coronary artery to coronary artery disease, leg artery to stroke, brain artery to peripheral artery disease","Coronary artery to peripheral artery disease, leg artery to stroke, brain artery to coronary artery disease","Coronary artery to stroke, leg artery to coronary artery disease, brain artery to peripheral artery disease","Coronary artery to coronary artery disease, leg artery to peripheral artery disease, brain artery to stroke"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The wall change is the same in every case, a fatty plaque narrowing the lumen, but the disease is named for the tissue that loses blood. Narrowed coronary arteries starve the heart wall, causing coronary artery disease. Narrowed limb arteries cause peripheral artery disease, and a blocked or burst brain artery causes a stroke.","notes":["This set gets the coronary artery right but swaps the other two. Peripheral artery disease belongs to the leg, and a stroke to the brain.","This set is fully scrambled. Peripheral means away from the body's core, so peripheral artery disease belongs to the limbs, not the heart.","This set scrambles all three. Stroke always involves the brain, and coronary artery disease always involves the arteries of the heart wall.","Correct. Coronary artery disease, peripheral artery disease, then stroke."],"lt":"t-blood-vessel-disorders","lid":"c40","comp":70,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-disorders"},{"id":"L01785","dok":1,"q":"What is atherosclerosis?","a":"A disease in which fatty plaques build up within the walls of arteries.","options":["Fatty plaque building up within artery walls","A balloon-like bulge in a weakened artery wall","A blood clot lodged in a deep vein of the leg","Inflammation of the wall of a vein"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Atherosclerosis is a slow, structural disease of arteries in which fatty plaques, called atheromas, build up within the wall. It begins where the endothelium of the tunica intima is injured. As plaques grow they narrow the lumen, which is why atherosclerosis underlies coronary artery disease, peripheral artery disease, and most strokes.","notes":["Correct. Atherosclerosis is fatty plaque building up within artery walls.","A balloon-like bulge of a weakened artery wall is an aneurysm. Pick it if the question asks what an aneurysm is.","A clot in a deep leg vein is a deep vein thrombosis, a venous disorder. Choose it if the question asks about a clot that could travel to the lungs.","Inflammation of a vein wall is phlebitis. It fits a question about a red, tender vein, not plaque in an artery."],"lt":"t-blood-vessel-disorders","lid":"c1-x2","comp":70,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-disorders"},{"id":"L01789","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly describes how arteriosclerosis and atherosclerosis are related?","a":"Atherosclerosis is the most common form of arteriosclerosis.","options":["Atherosclerosis is the most common form of arteriosclerosis","Arteriosclerosis affects arteries, while atherosclerosis affects veins","They are two names for the same disorder of veins","Arteriosclerosis is a form of atherosclerosis found only in the coronary arteries"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Arteriosclerosis is the general term for artery walls that become stiff and thick, the broad hardening of the arteries. Atherosclerosis is one specific kind, the form caused by fatty plaque building up in the wall, and it is by far the most common. So every case of atherosclerosis is a kind of arteriosclerosis, but not the other way around.","notes":["Correct. Atherosclerosis is the most common form of the broader condition, arteriosclerosis.","Both disorders affect arteries; the arter- and athero- roots do not mark artery versus vein. Venous disorders include varicose veins, phlebitis, and deep vein thrombosis.","Both terms describe arteries, not veins, and they are not exact synonyms. Students pick this because the names look so alike, but one is the category and the other is a type within it.","This reverses the relationship. Arteriosclerosis is the broad category and atherosclerosis is the specific form, and neither term is limited to the coronary arteries."],"lt":"t-blood-vessel-disorders","lid":"c6-x2","comp":70,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-disorders"},{"id":"L01791","dok":1,"q":"What is phlebitis?","a":"Inflammation of a vein.","options":["Inflammation of a vein","Failure of the valves in the leg veins","Twisted, swollen superficial veins","A clot in a deep vein of the leg"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The root phleb- means vein and the suffix -itis means inflammation, so phlebitis is inflammation of a vein. It causes pain, redness, and tenderness along the course of the vein. Like other venous disorders, it involves the low-pressure side of the circulation.","notes":["Correct. Phlebitis is inflammation of a vein.","Long-term failure of the leg vein valves is chronic venous insufficiency, which causes lasting swelling of the lower leg. Choose it for a question about failed valves.","Twisted, swollen superficial veins are varicose veins, caused by valve failure and pooled blood. They fit a question about veins that bulge visibly under the skin.","A clot in a deep leg vein is a deep vein thrombosis. Inflammation can accompany a clot, but pick this if the question asks about the clot itself."],"lt":"t-blood-vessel-disorders","lid":"c9-x2","comp":70,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-disorders"},{"id":"L01792","dok":1,"q":"Which three fetal shunts route blood around the liver and the lungs?","a":"The ductus venosus, foramen ovale, and ductus arteriosus.","options":["The umbilical vein, umbilical arteries, and placenta","The ductus venosus, foramen ovale, and ductus arteriosus","The umbilical vein, ductus venosus, and ductus arteriosus","The fossa ovalis, ligamentum venosum, and ligamentum arteriosum"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"A shunt is a vessel or opening that routes blood around an organ. The fetus has three: the ductus venosus skips most of the liver, while the foramen ovale and the ductus arteriosus both route blood around the collapsed lungs. All three close after birth when the lungs and liver take over their normal work.","notes":["The umbilical vessels and the placenta carry blood between the fetus and mother, but they are not shunts around an organ. Pick this set if the question asks which structures connect the fetus to the placenta.","Correct. The ductus venosus, foramen ovale, and ductus arteriosus are the three fetal shunts.","The umbilical vein is not a shunt; it brings blood from the placenta to the fetus. This set swaps it in for the foramen ovale, which is the shunt between the atria.","These are the adult remnants of the shunts after they close. Choose this set for a question about what the fetal shunts become after birth."],"lt":"t-blood-vessel-disorders","lid":"c10-x2","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L01799","dok":2,"q":"A patient feels pain in the calf muscles when walking that eases with rest, because narrowed arteries cannot deliver enough blood to the working muscles. Which disorder is this?","a":"Peripheral artery disease.","options":["Phlebitis","Varicose veins","Coronary artery disease","Peripheral artery disease"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Peripheral artery disease is atherosclerotic narrowing of the arteries of the limbs, usually the legs. At rest the narrowed vessels can still keep up, but walking raises the muscles' demand for blood beyond what the narrow lumen can carry, so the muscles hurt. When the person rests, demand drops and the pain eases.","notes":["Phlebitis is inflammation of a vein, causing redness and tenderness along the vein. It fits a red, tender vein, not muscle pain that comes and goes with walking.","Varicose veins are twisted superficial veins from failed valves. They can make legs ache, but they are a vein problem, so choose them for visibly bulging veins rather than starved muscles.","Coronary artery disease is the same plaque narrowing the arteries of the heart wall. Pick it if the pain were in the chest from a starved myocardium.","Correct. Leg pain on walking from narrowed limb arteries is peripheral artery disease."],"lt":"t-blood-vessel-disorders","lid":"c17-x2","comp":70,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-disorders"},{"id":"L01806","dok":2,"q":"Which adult remnant is a fibrous cord on the inferior surface of the liver that reaches the inferior vena cava?","a":"The ligamentum venosum.","options":["The fossa ovalis","The ligamentum arteriosum","The ligamentum venosum","The medial umbilical ligaments"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The ligamentum venosum is what remains of the ductus venosus, the fetal shunt that carried blood through the liver toward the inferior vena cava. After birth the shunt closes into a fibrous cord that sits on the inferior surface of the liver. The name keeps the venosum from ductus venosus, which makes the pair easy to match.","notes":["The fossa ovalis is a shallow depression in the interatrial septum of the heart, left by the foramen ovale. Choose it for a question about a mark inside the heart.","The ligamentum arteriosum lies in the thorax, a short cord between the pulmonary trunk and the aorta. Pick it if the question asks what the ductus arteriosus becomes.","Correct. The ligamentum venosum is the cord on the inferior surface of the liver, left by the ductus venosus.","The medial umbilical ligaments are paired cords on the inner surface of the anterior abdominal wall, left by the umbilical arteries. They fit a question about cords on the abdominal wall."],"lt":"t-blood-vessel-disorders","lid":"c24-x2","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L01807","dok":2,"q":"A clot forms in a deep vein of the leg, breaks free, and travels through the venous return. Where does it most likely lodge?","a":"In an artery of the lung, as a pulmonary embolism.","options":["In a coronary artery of the heart wall","In an artery of the other leg","In an artery of the brain","In an artery of the lung"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"A clot from a leg vein follows the normal route of venous blood. It rides up the inferior vena cava into the right atrium and right ventricle and is pumped into the pulmonary arteries. As those arteries branch and narrow in the lungs, the clot gets stuck, which is a pulmonary embolism.","notes":["Coronary arteries branch from the aorta, which is on the left side of the heart. A venous clot reaches the lungs before it could ever reach the aorta, so this fits a clot that started in the left heart.","Arteries of the legs are reached from the aorta, not from the veins. A clot from a leg vein has to pass through the right heart and lungs first, so it cannot travel straight to another leg.","Brain arteries are also supplied from the aorta. A clot that blocks a brain artery causes a stroke, so pick this for a clot that began on the arterial side, such as on a plaque in a neck artery.","Correct. A loose venous clot from the leg lodges in an artery of the lung."],"lt":"t-blood-vessel-disorders","lid":"c25-x2","comp":70,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-disorders"},{"id":"L01808","dok":2,"q":"An atherosclerotic plaque narrows an artery of the leg. What is the named disorder in this location?","a":"Peripheral artery disease.","options":["Coronary artery disease","Peripheral artery disease","Hypertension","Stroke"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Atherosclerosis takes its disease name from where it happens. When plaque narrows the arteries of the limbs, usually the legs, the disorder is peripheral artery disease. The leg muscles are starved of blood, so the classic sign is pain on walking.","notes":["Coronary artery disease is plaque narrowing the arteries of the heart wall. Pick it if the narrowed artery were a coronary artery.","Correct. Plaque narrowing a leg artery is peripheral artery disease.","Hypertension is chronically high pressure within the arteries throughout the body, not narrowing of one leg artery. It fits a question about high blood pressure.","A stroke is a loss of blood supply to part of the brain. Choose it if the blocked or burst artery supplied the brain."],"lt":"t-blood-vessel-disorders","lid":"c26-x2","comp":70,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-disorders"},{"id":"L01809","dok":2,"q":"In a newborn, the opening in the interatrial septum fails to seal after birth. What is this condition called?","a":"Patent foramen ovale.","options":["Fossa ovalis","Patent foramen ovale","Ligamentum venosum","Patent ductus arteriosus"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The opening in the interatrial septum is the foramen ovale, and patent means open. When it stays open after birth, the condition is called patent foramen ovale, named for the fetal structure that failed to seal. It leaves an abnormal connection between the right and left atria.","notes":["The fossa ovalis is the normal depression left when the foramen ovale closes as it should. Choose it for a question about the healthy adult remnant.","Correct. An interatrial opening that stays open is a patent foramen ovale.","The ligamentum venosum is the normal remnant of the ductus venosus on the liver. It fits a question about what the liver shunt becomes.","A patent ductus arteriosus is an open connection between the pulmonary trunk and the aorta. Pick it if the vessel between the great arteries failed to close."],"lt":"t-blood-vessel-disorders","lid":"c27-x2","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L01811","dok":2,"q":"A weakened spot in the wall of the aorta balloons outward. Which disorder is this, and why is it dangerous?","a":"An aneurysm, because it can rupture.","options":["Atherosclerosis, because it narrows the lumen","A pulmonary embolism, because it blocks a lung artery","An aneurysm, because it can rupture","Phlebitis, because the vessel wall is inflamed"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"A weakened artery wall that bulges outward is an aneurysm, and the aorta is one of the most common sites. The danger is rupture: the thin, stretched bulge can burst under arterial pressure and cause severe internal bleeding. The disorder widens the vessel rather than narrowing it.","notes":["Atherosclerosis narrows an artery with plaque that bulges inward. Pick it if the vessel were narrowed rather than ballooned outward.","A pulmonary embolism is a clot lodged in a lung artery, usually from a deep leg vein. It fits a question about a traveling clot, not a weakened aortic wall.","Correct. An outward bulge of a weakened aortic wall is an aneurysm, dangerous because it can rupture.","Phlebitis is inflammation of a vein, not a bulging artery. Choose it for a vein that is red, tender, and painful."],"lt":"t-blood-vessel-disorders","lid":"c29-x2","comp":70,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-disorders"},{"id":"L01812","dok":2,"q":"At birth the lungs inflate and the placenta is cut off. What normally happens to the fetal shunts?","a":"They close and are left behind as small ligaments or remnants.","options":["They widen to carry more blood into the inflated lungs","They close and are left behind as ligaments or remnants","They disappear completely and leave no trace","They stay open for life as backup routes around the lungs"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Once the newborn breathes and the placenta is gone, there is no longer a reason to route blood around the lungs or liver. Each shunt closes in the hours and weeks after birth and leaves a remnant: the ductus venosus becomes the ligamentum venosum, the ductus arteriosus becomes the ligamentum arteriosum, and the foramen ovale becomes the fossa ovalis.","notes":["The shunts carry blood away from the lungs, not into them, so widening them would work against the newborn. After birth, blood reaches the lungs through the pulmonary arteries once the shunts close.","Correct. The shunts close and remain as small ligaments or, for the foramen ovale, a depression.","Each shunt leaves something behind that can be found in an adult, such as the fossa ovalis in the heart or the ligamentum venosum on the liver. Students pick this when they forget the remnants.","Shunts that stay open after birth are abnormal and get their own names, such as patent ductus arteriosus and patent foramen ovale. This describes a defect, not the normal outcome."],"lt":"t-blood-vessel-disorders","lid":"c30-x2","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L01813","dok":2,"q":"Before birth, the foramen ovale and ductus arteriosus route blood away from the lungs, and both close after birth. Why is bypassing the lungs appropriate only before birth?","a":"The fetal lungs are collapsed and not used for gas exchange, so the placenta does that job until birth.","options":["The fetal lungs already exchange gases with the amniotic fluid","Blood reaching the fetal lungs would carry oxygen away from them","The fetal lungs are collapsed and not used for gas exchange","The fetal heart is too weak to pump blood into the lungs"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"A fetus does not breathe air, so its lungs are collapsed and filled with fluid, and the placenta handles gas exchange. Sending all the blood through the lungs would be wasted effort, so most of it is shunted past them, with only a small share reaching the lungs to supply the growing tissue. At birth the lungs inflate and take over gas exchange, so the shunts are no longer needed.","notes":["The fetal lungs do not exchange gases with amniotic fluid; all fetal gas exchange happens at the placenta. This misconception comes from the fact that the lungs are filled with fluid.","This misreads the reason for the bypass. The lungs are skipped because they are not yet exchanging gases, so sending blood there would not help oxygenate it.","Correct. Because the fetal lungs are not used for gas exchange, blood is shunted around them until birth.","The fetal heart pumps blood strongly through the whole body and out to the placenta. The shunts exist because the lungs are not in use, not because the heart lacks strength."],"lt":"t-blood-vessel-disorders","lid":"d3-t-blood-vessel-disorders-1","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L01814","dok":2,"q":"Before birth, where does fetal blood pick up oxygen and nutrients and give up its wastes?","a":"The placenta.","options":["The fetal liver","The fetal lungs","The placenta","The amniotic fluid"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The placenta is the organ where fetal blood and maternal blood come close enough to exchange materials, without mixing. Fetal blood picks up oxygen and nutrients there and gives up carbon dioxide and wastes. The umbilical arteries bring oxygen-poor blood to the placenta, and the umbilical vein carries the refreshed blood back to the fetus.","notes":["The fetal liver is largely bypassed by the ductus venosus, since the placenta handles much of its filtering. Choose it for a question about which organ the ductus venosus skips.","The fetal lungs are collapsed and not used for gas exchange before birth, which is why blood is shunted around them. Pick the lungs if the question asks where gas exchange happens after birth.","Correct. The placenta is where fetal blood exchanges oxygen, nutrients, and wastes with the mother's blood.","The amniotic fluid surrounds and cushions the fetus but is not where its blood exchanges gases. Students choose it because the fetus is bathed in it, but no blood vessels exchange materials with it."],"lt":"t-blood-vessel-disorders","lid":"d3-t-blood-vessel-disorders-2","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L01815","dok":2,"q":"In atherosclerosis, plaque narrows and roughens an artery. Why does this put the tissue downstream at risk?","a":"The narrowed, roughened artery carries less blood and can be fully blocked by a clot, cutting off oxygen to the tissue it feeds.","options":["Plaque affects only the artery wall itself and never the tissue it supplies","A narrowed artery speeds blood up, flooding the tissue with too much oxygen","Less blood gets through, and a clot on the rough plaque can block it completely","Narrowing forces arterial blood backward into the veins of that tissue"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"A plaque bulges into the lumen, so less blood can reach the tissue the artery supplies. The plaque surface is also rough, and a thrombus, or clot, can form on it and suddenly block the vessel completely. Either way, the downstream tissue loses its oxygen supply, which is how plaque leads to heart attacks, strokes, and leg pain.","notes":["The whole point of the named diseases, such as coronary artery disease and stroke, is that the tissue downstream suffers. Students choose this when they think of plaque only as a wall problem.","Narrowing does not deliver extra oxygen; the tissue gets less blood, not more. This misconception treats a narrowed pipe like a nozzle that boosts delivery.","Correct. Narrowing reduces flow, and a clot on the rough plaque can block the artery entirely.","Arteries do not empty backward into veins; blood still has to pass forward through capillaries to reach the veins. This mixes up arterial narrowing with the backflow that happens when vein valves fail."],"lt":"t-blood-vessel-disorders","lid":"d3-t-blood-vessel-disorders-3","comp":70,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-disorders"},{"id":"L01816","dok":2,"q":"Aneurysms are a disorder of arteries rather than veins. Which statement best explains why a weak spot is so risky in an artery wall?","a":"Arteries carry blood under high pressure, so a weakened wall balloons outward and can burst.","options":["Arteries lack a muscular middle layer, so any weak spot leaks","Arteries carry blood under high pressure, so a weak spot balloons and can burst","Arteries have one-way valves that tear apart at a weak spot","Arteries carry blood under low pressure, so a weak spot collapses inward"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Blood leaves the heart under high pressure, and the arteries take that pressure with every beat. A weakened area cannot resist it, so the wall stretches outward into a balloon-like bulge, and the thin bulge can rupture and bleed severely. Veins carry blood under low pressure, so their disorders tend to involve pooled blood and failed valves instead.","notes":["Arteries have a thick tunica media of smooth muscle and elastic fibers, the thickest layer of their wall. A vessel without a tunica media is a capillary, which has only the tunica intima.","Correct. High arterial pressure stretches a weak spot into a bulge that can burst.","One-way valves are a feature of veins, not arteries away from the heart. Pick valves for a question about varicose veins or chronic venous insufficiency.","Low pressure describes veins, not arteries, and a weak spot under pressure bulges outward rather than collapsing. This mixes up the two sides of the circulation."],"lt":"t-blood-vessel-disorders","lid":"d3-t-blood-vessel-disorders-4","comp":70,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-disorders"},{"id":"L01817","dok":2,"q":"Varicose veins develop when the one-way valves in leg veins fail. Using the valves' normal role, why does their failure make blood pool?","a":"The valves normally prevent backflow against gravity, so when they fail blood falls back and collects.","options":["The valves normally stop backflow, so failure lets blood fall back and collect","The valves normally narrow the vein, so failure lets it widen slowly","The valves normally block arterial blood, so failure lets it leak in","The valves normally pump blood upward, so failure removes the pump"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Blood in the leg veins has to travel upward against gravity under low pressure. One-way valves divide each vein into short segments and close whenever blood starts to slip backward. When the valves fail, blood falls back down the vein and collects, stretching the superficial veins until they twist and bulge.","notes":["Correct. The valves prevent backflow, so their failure lets blood fall back and pool.","Valves do not narrow the vein to control flow; smooth muscle in the vessel wall changes diameter. This mixes up the valves with the tunica media.","Arteries and veins are joined through capillary beds, not through valves. Vein valves only control the direction of venous blood, so their failure does not let arterial blood leak in.","Valves do not pump; they only keep blood from flowing backward. The squeezing that pushes venous blood upward comes from nearby skeletal muscles, the skeletal muscle pump."],"lt":"t-blood-vessel-disorders","lid":"d3-t-blood-vessel-disorders-5","comp":70,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-disorders"},{"id":"L01818","dok":3,"q":"Why does a clot that breaks free from a deep leg vein end up in the lungs rather than in the brain?","a":"Venous blood returns to the right side of the heart, which pumps it into the pulmonary arteries, the first narrow vessels the clot meets.","options":["Venous blood goes to the right heart, which pumps it into the lung arteries","The clot travels in the aorta, which sends its first branches to the lungs","Leg veins drain directly into the pulmonary veins","The left ventricle pumps venous blood from the legs straight to the lungs"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"A loose clot follows the normal venous return. Blood from the legs travels up the inferior vena cava to the right atrium and right ventricle, which pumps it into the pulmonary trunk and arteries. The pulmonary arteries branch into smaller and smaller vessels in the lungs, and the clot lodges there long before it could reach the left heart and the arteries to the brain.","notes":["Correct. Venous blood returns to the right heart and is pumped into the pulmonary arteries, where the clot lodges.","Venous blood does not enter the aorta until after it has passed through the lungs and the left heart. The aorta's first branches are the coronary arteries, which fit a question about blood supply to the heart wall.","Pulmonary veins carry blood from the lungs to the left atrium; leg veins never connect to them directly. Pick the pulmonary veins for a question about where blood goes after leaving the lungs.","The left ventricle pumps blood into the aorta and out to the body, not to the lungs. The right ventricle is the chamber that pumps blood to the lungs."],"lt":"t-blood-vessel-disorders","lid":"d3x-t-blood-vessel-disorders-1","comp":69,"competencyId":"bvn-circulatory-routes"},{"id":"L01819","dok":3,"q":"Chronic hypertension makes the heart pump against higher pressure in the systemic arteries. Which chamber's wall is most likely to thicken over time?","a":"The left ventricle.","options":["The right atrium","The left ventricle","The left atrium","The right ventricle"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Hypertension is chronically high pressure in the arteries, and the systemic arteries are fed by the left ventricle through the aorta. Like any muscle that is made to work harder, the myocardium of the left ventricle thickens to generate the extra force. The left ventricle already has the thickest wall of the four chambers because it pumps blood to the whole body.","notes":["The right atrium receives blood from the venae cavae and pushes it only into the right ventricle. Pick it for a question about where systemic venous blood returns to the heart.","Correct. The left ventricle pumps into the systemic arteries, so its wall thickens against chronically high pressure.","The left atrium receives blood from the pulmonary veins and pushes it only into the left ventricle. It fits a question about where oxygenated blood returns from the lungs.","The right ventricle pumps blood to the lungs through the pulmonary trunk. It would thicken if pressure in the pulmonary circuit stayed high, so choose it for a question about the pulmonary side."],"lt":"t-blood-vessel-disorders","lid":"d3x-t-blood-vessel-disorders-2","comp":70,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-disorders"},{"id":"N-m3-vessel-routes-1","dok":1,"q":"A piece of a clot breaks free from an artery wall and travels in the bloodstream until it lodges in a smaller vessel. What is the traveling clot called?","a":"An embolus.","options":["An atheroma","An embolus","An aneurysm","A thrombus"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"An embolus is a clot or fragment that breaks free and travels through the bloodstream to lodge somewhere else. It usually starts as a thrombus, a clot fixed in place, often on the rough surface of a plaque or in a deep vein. Where the embolus lodges decides the disease, such as a pulmonary embolism in the lung or a stroke in the brain.","notes":["An atheroma is a fatty plaque within the artery wall, not a clot. Choose it for a question about the lesion of atherosclerosis.","Correct. A traveling clot or fragment is an embolus.","An aneurysm is a balloon-like bulge of a weakened artery wall. It fits a question about a wall that could rupture, not a moving clot.","A thrombus is a clot that forms and stays in place inside a vessel. Pick it if the clot had not broken free, as in a clot sitting on a roughened plaque."],"comp":70,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-disorders"},{"id":"N-m3-vessel-routes-2","dok":3,"q":"In a cadaver, a round fibrous cord runs in the free edge of the falciform ligament from the umbilicus to the liver. Which fetal vessel was this cord?","a":"The umbilical vein.","options":["The umbilical vein","The ductus venosus","The ductus arteriosus","An umbilical artery"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The cord is the ligamentum teres, the round ligament of the liver. Before birth it was the umbilical vein, which carried oxygen-rich blood from the placenta through the umbilicus to the liver. Its path in the free edge of the falciform ligament traces exactly where the vein once ran.","notes":["Correct. The cord in the free edge of the falciform ligament is the ligamentum teres, left by the umbilical vein.","The ductus venosus leaves the ligamentum venosum on the inferior surface of the liver, reaching the inferior vena cava. It fits a cord on the liver's underside, not one coming from the umbilicus.","The ductus arteriosus leaves the ligamentum arteriosum in the thorax, between the pulmonary trunk and the aorta. Choose it for a cord found above the heart.","The umbilical arteries leave the medial umbilical ligaments, paired cords on the inner surface of the anterior abdominal wall. Pick them for paired cords heading down from the umbilicus, not one running to the liver."],"comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"N-m3-vessel-routes-3","dok":1,"q":"Which disorder is defined as chronically high pressure within the arteries?","a":"Hypertension.","options":["Arteriosclerosis","Hypertension","Chronic venous insufficiency","Aneurysm"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Hypertension means chronically high blood pressure within the arteries. It does not have to narrow or bulge any single vessel, but over time it strains the heart and damages vessel walls throughout the body. That long-term strain is why hypertension raises the risk of stroke, aneurysm, and a thickened left ventricle.","notes":["Arteriosclerosis is the stiffening and thickening of artery walls. It is a change in the wall rather than in the pressure, so pick it for a question about hardened arteries.","Correct. Chronically high arterial pressure is hypertension.","Chronic venous insufficiency is long-term failure of the leg vein valves, a problem on the low-pressure venous side. It fits a question about persistent lower leg swelling.","An aneurysm is a balloon-like bulge of a weakened artery wall. High pressure can make one worse, but choose it for a question about a bulging wall that can rupture."],"comp":70,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-disorders"},{"id":"N-m3-vessel-routes-4","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares arterial and venous disorders?","a":"Arterial disorders tend to narrow or weaken the wall under high pressure, while venous disorders tend to involve failed valves and pooled or clotted blood under low pressure.","options":["Arterial and venous disorders are the same, since both vessels carry blood at the same pressure","Arterial disorders narrow or weaken the wall, while venous disorders involve failed valves and pooled blood","Arterial disorders affect only the brain, while venous disorders affect only the legs","Arterial disorders mostly involve failed valves, while venous disorders mostly involve plaque"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Arteries carry blood under high pressure, so their disorders tend to narrow the channel, as in atherosclerosis, or weaken the wall, as in an aneurysm. Veins carry blood under low pressure and depend on valves, so their disorders tend to involve failed valves and pooled or clotted blood, as in varicose veins and deep vein thrombosis. The pressure each vessel carries shapes what goes wrong with it.","notes":["Arteries carry blood under much higher pressure than veins, which is exactly why their disorders differ. Students pick this when they forget that pressure drops as blood moves through the capillaries.","Correct. Arterial disorders narrow or weaken the wall, and venous disorders involve failed valves and pooled blood.","Arterial disorders occur throughout the body, such as coronary artery disease in the heart and peripheral artery disease in the legs. Venous disorders also occur outside the legs, as hemorrhoids show.","This reverses the two sides. Valves are a feature of veins, and plaque builds up in arteries, so valve failure belongs to venous disorders and plaque to arterial ones."],"comp":70,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-disorders"},{"id":"N-m3-vessel-routes-5","dok":2,"q":"Most fetal shunts close into fibrous cords, but the foramen ovale leaves a shallow depression instead. Why?","a":"The foramen ovale was an opening in a wall, not a vessel, so sealing it leaves a depression rather than a cord.","options":["It stays partly open in every adult to help the newborn lungs","It was the largest shunt, so it shrinks down into a shallow pit","It was a hole in a wall, not a tube, so it seals into a depression","It was an artery, and arteries always close into shallow depressions"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The ductus venosus, the ductus arteriosus, and the umbilical vessels were all tubes, and a closed tube collapses into a fibrous cord, or ligament. The foramen ovale was different: it was a hole in the interatrial septum. When a hole in a wall seals, it leaves a shallow mark in that wall, the fossa ovalis, rather than a cord.","notes":["A foramen ovale that stays open after birth is an abnormal condition called patent foramen ovale. Normally it seals, so this describes a defect rather than the reason for the depression.","The fossa ovalis is not explained by size. The shape of the remnant depends on whether the original was a hole or a tube.","Correct. Because the foramen ovale was a hole rather than a vessel, it leaves a depression.","The foramen ovale was not an artery; it was an opening between the atria. Arteries such as the umbilical arteries close into cords, the medial umbilical ligaments."],"comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"N-m3-vessel-routes-191","dok":2,"q":"The medial umbilical ligaments on the inside of the anterior abdominal wall are the remnants of which fetal vessels?","a":"The umbilical arteries.","options":["The umbilical arteries","The ductus venosus","The ductus arteriosus","The umbilical vein"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Before birth, two umbilical arteries branch from the internal iliac arteries and carry blood from the fetus to the placenta. After birth they close, and their distal parts become fibrous cords called the medial umbilical ligaments, which run up the inner surface of the abdominal wall toward the navel.","notes":["Correct. The medial umbilical ligaments are the closed umbilical arteries.","The ductus venosus let blood bypass the liver. It becomes the ligamentum venosum. Pick it for the remnant on the liver.","The ductus arteriosus connected the pulmonary trunk to the aorta. It becomes the ligamentum arteriosum. Pick it for the remnant between the pulmonary trunk and aortic arch.","The umbilical vein carried oxygen-rich blood from the placenta to the fetus. After birth it becomes the round ligament of the liver (ligamentum teres). Pick it for that remnant."],"comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L03367","dok":1,"q":"The fossa ovalis is the adult remnant of which fetal structure?","a":"The foramen ovale.","options":["The ductus arteriosus","The umbilical vein","The foramen ovale","The ductus venosus"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Before birth, the foramen ovale is an opening in the interatrial septum that lets blood pass from the right atrium to the left atrium, bypassing the lungs. After birth it closes, leaving a shallow depression called the fossa ovalis. It is the one fetal shunt that becomes a depression instead of a cord, because it was a hole and not a vessel.","notes":["The ductus arteriosus connects the pulmonary trunk to the aorta in the fetus and becomes the ligamentum arteriosum. Pick it for the remnant cord between the pulmonary trunk and aortic arch.","The umbilical vein carries oxygen-rich blood from the placenta and becomes the round ligament of the liver (ligamentum teres). Pick it for that liver remnant.","Correct. The foramen ovale closes to leave the fossa ovalis.","The ductus venosus carries blood past the liver in the fetus and becomes the ligamentum venosum. Pick it for the remnant on the liver."],"lt":"t-heart-gross-anatomy","lid":"W3-041","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L03425","dok":2,"q":"Before birth, the foramen ovale let blood pass directly between which two chambers?","a":"From the right atrium to the left atrium.","options":["From the right ventricle to the aorta","From the right atrium to the left atrium","From the left atrium to the right atrium","From the right ventricle to the left ventricle"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The fetal lungs are not yet used for breathing, so most blood is routed around them. The foramen ovale, an opening in the interatrial septum, lets blood pass directly from the right atrium into the left atrium, bypassing the pulmonary circuit. After birth it closes, and its site remains as the fossa ovalis.","notes":["The fetal shunt toward the aorta is the ductus arteriosus, which connects the pulmonary trunk to the aorta. Pick it for the vessel that becomes the ligamentum arteriosum.","Correct. The foramen ovale carried blood from the right atrium to the left atrium.","This reverses the direction. Fetal blood flows from right to left, because the shunt exists to send blood around the unused lungs.","There is no normal fetal opening between the ventricles. A hole there would be a ventricular septal defect."],"lt":"t-heart-gross-anatomy","lid":"W3-099","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L03636","dok":2,"q":"If the valves in the leg veins fail, what problem can develop?","a":"Blood flows backward and pools, leading to varicose veins.","options":["Capillaries in the legs lose their tunica intima","The veins develop a thicker tunica media","Blood flows backward and pools, stretching the veins","Blood pressure in the arteries rises sharply"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Venous valves are flaps of the tunica intima that keep low-pressure blood moving toward the heart. If they fail, blood slips backward and pools in the veins, especially in the legs where gravity pulls it down. Over time the pooled blood stretches the vein walls, producing swollen, twisted varicose veins.","notes":["Capillaries keep their tunica intima regardless of what happens in the veins; it is their only layer. This choice links two unrelated structures.","Failed valves let veins stretch and become swollen and thin-walled, not thicker. A thick tunica media is a feature of arteries.","Correct. Failed valves let blood pool and stretch the veins.","Valve failure affects the low-pressure venous side, where blood is returning to the heart. It does not cause a sharp rise in arterial pressure."],"lt":"t-vessel-structure","lid":"W3-383","comp":70,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-disorders"},{"id":"L03675","dok":2,"q":"The valves in a person's superficial leg veins fail over many years. Which condition is most likely to develop?","a":"Varicose veins.","options":["Varicose veins","Atherosclerosis","Aneurysm","Peripheral artery disease"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Leg veins carry low-pressure blood upward against gravity, and their valves keep that blood from sliding back. When the valves fail, blood flows backward and pools in the vein, stretching it. Over time the superficial veins become twisted, swollen, and visibly bulging, which is the condition called varicose veins.","notes":["Correct. Failed valves in superficial leg veins let blood pool and produce varicose veins.","Atherosclerosis is fatty plaque buildup in artery walls, not a valve problem in veins. Choose it if the question asked what narrows arteries and leads to heart attack or stroke.","An aneurysm is a balloon-like bulge in a weakened artery wall, most often the aorta or brain arteries. Pick it if the question asked about an artery at risk of rupture.","Peripheral artery disease is narrowing of the arteries of the limbs, usually from plaque. It would be the answer if the person had leg pain on walking."],"lt":"t-vessel-types","lid":"W3-473","comp":70,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-disorders"},{"id":"L03678","dok":1,"q":"A patient is diagnosed with deep vein thrombosis. Which kind of vessel holds the clot?","a":"A deep vein, usually of the leg.","options":["A deep vein of the leg","A superficial vein of the leg","An artery of the lung","A deep artery of the leg"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Thrombosis means the formation of a clot (a thrombus) inside a vessel. Deep vein thrombosis is a clot in one of the deep veins, usually in the leg, where slow, low-pressure blood flow makes clotting more likely. The danger is that the clot can break free and travel to the lungs.","notes":["Correct. A deep vein thrombosis is a clot in a deep vein, most often in the leg.","Superficial veins near the skin are the ones affected by varicose veins and often by phlebitis. They would be the answer if the question asked about twisted, bulging veins.","A clot lodged in an artery of the lung is a pulmonary embolism, the dangerous result if a DVT breaks free. Choose it if the question asked where a traveling leg clot ends up.","Narrowing of a leg artery is peripheral artery disease, not DVT. Pick the leg artery if the question asked what causes pain on walking."],"lt":"t-vessel-types","lid":"W3-479","comp":70,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-disorders"},{"id":"L03687","dok":1,"q":"Which route describes the pulmonary circuit?","a":"From the right side of the heart to the lungs and back to the left side.","options":["Right side of the heart to the lungs and back to the left side","Left side of the heart to the body and back to the right side","Right side of the heart to the lungs and back to the right side","Digestive organs through the liver before reaching the heart"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The pulmonary circuit carries oxygen-poor blood from the right ventricle through the pulmonary trunk and pulmonary arteries to the lungs. There the blood drops off carbon dioxide and picks up oxygen. The oxygen-rich blood then returns through the pulmonary veins to the left atrium, ready to be pumped to the body.","notes":["Correct. The pulmonary circuit runs from the right heart to the lungs and back to the left heart.","This describes the systemic circuit, which delivers oxygen-rich blood to the body. Pick it if the question asked which circuit starts at the left ventricle.","Blood returning from the lungs enters the left atrium, not the right side. Returning to the right side would skip the left heart entirely and leave no way for oxygen-rich blood to reach the body.","This describes the hepatic portal circulation, which routes blood from the digestive organs through the liver. It would be the answer if the question asked about a portal system."],"lt":"t-vascular-routes","lid":"W3-425","comp":69,"competencyId":"bvn-circulatory-routes"},{"id":"L03688","dok":1,"q":"Which route describes the systemic circuit?","a":"From the left side of the heart to the body and back to the right side.","options":["Left side of the heart to the body and back to the right side","Right side of the heart to the body and back to the left side","Left side of the heart to the lungs and back to the right side","Right side of the heart to the lungs and back to the left side"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The systemic circuit begins when the left ventricle pumps oxygen-rich blood into the aorta. The blood travels through arteries to capillary beds throughout the body, where it delivers oxygen and picks up carbon dioxide. Oxygen-poor blood then returns through the venae cavae to the right atrium.","notes":["Correct. The systemic circuit runs from the left heart to the body and back to the right heart.","This reverses the systemic circuit. The right side of the heart receives oxygen-poor blood and sends it to the lungs, not to the body.","The left side of the heart sends blood to the body, not the lungs. This route mixes the starting point of the systemic circuit with the destination of the pulmonary circuit.","This is the pulmonary circuit, which sends blood to the lungs for gas exchange. Pick it if the question asked which circuit starts at the right ventricle."],"lt":"t-vascular-routes","lid":"W3-426","comp":69,"competencyId":"bvn-circulatory-routes"},{"id":"L03689","dok":1,"q":"What is a portal system?","a":"An arrangement in which blood passes through two capillary beds in series before returning to the heart.","options":["Blood flows straight from an artery into a vein without a capillary bed","Blood passes through two capillary beds in a row before reaching the heart","Blood flows from the heart to the lungs and back to the heart","Two vessels join so blood has an alternate route if one is blocked"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"In most routes, blood passes through one capillary bed between artery and vein. In a portal system, the veins draining the first capillary bed lead into a second capillary bed before the blood heads back to the heart. This lets the second organ handle what the first bed picked up, as in the hepatic portal system, where blood from the digestive organs passes through the liver.","notes":["This describes an arteriovenous anastomosis, which bypasses capillaries altogether. Pick it if the question asked about a direct artery-to-vein connection.","Correct. A portal system has two capillary beds in a row.","This describes the pulmonary circuit. Choose it if the question asked how blood reaches the lungs for gas exchange.","This describes an anastomosis, a junction of vessels that provides a backup route. It would be the answer if the question asked how tissue can stay supplied when one vessel is blocked."],"lt":"t-vascular-routes","lid":"W3-427","comp":69,"competencyId":"bvn-circulatory-routes"},{"id":"L03690","dok":1,"q":"Which of these is an example of a portal system?","a":"The hepatic portal system.","options":["Pulmonary circuit","Coronary circulation","Cerebral arterial circle","Hepatic portal system"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The hepatic portal system collects blood from the capillaries of the stomach, intestines, spleen, and pancreas and carries it through the hepatic portal vein into the liver's sinusoids, a second capillary bed. Only after the liver has processed this blood does it drain to the inferior vena cava and the heart. The hypophyseal portal system, between the hypothalamus and anterior pituitary, is another example.","notes":["The pulmonary circuit has a single capillary bed in the lungs between the heart and back again. Pick it if the question asked which route carries blood to the lungs.","The coronary circulation supplies the heart wall itself through one capillary bed. It would be the answer if the question asked which vessels feed the myocardium.","The cerebral arterial circle is an arterial anastomosis at the base of the brain that provides backup routes. Choose it if the question asked for an example of an anastomosis.","Correct. The hepatic portal system passes blood through the digestive capillaries and then the liver sinusoids."],"lt":"t-vascular-routes","lid":"W3-428","comp":69,"competencyId":"bvn-circulatory-routes"},{"id":"L03691","dok":1,"q":"What is an arteriovenous anastomosis?","a":"A direct connection from an artery to a vein that bypasses the capillaries.","options":["A direct artery-to-vein link that skips the capillaries","An opening between the right and left atria","A junction of two arteries that gives an alternate route","Two capillary beds connected in series by a vein"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"An anastomosis is any place where vessels join. In an arteriovenous anastomosis, an artery connects directly to a vein, so blood can pass from the arterial side to the venous side without going through capillaries. Because capillaries are skipped, no exchange with the tissue happens along that route.","notes":["Correct. An arteriovenous anastomosis links an artery directly to a vein.","An opening between the atria is the foramen ovale of the fetal heart. Choose it if the question asked about a fetal shunt that bypasses the lungs.","A junction of two arteries is an arterial anastomosis, which provides a backup route to a tissue. Pick it if the question asked how blood can still reach a tissue when one artery is blocked.","Two capillary beds in series describe a portal system. It would be the answer if the question asked about the hepatic portal system."],"lt":"t-vascular-routes","lid":"W3-430","comp":69,"competencyId":"bvn-circulatory-routes"},{"id":"L03692","dok":2,"q":"Which chamber starts each circuit?","a":"The right ventricle starts the pulmonary circuit; the left ventricle starts the systemic circuit.","options":["Pulmonary starts at the right atrium, systemic at the left atrium","Both circuits start at the left ventricle","Pulmonary starts at the right ventricle, systemic at the left ventricle","Pulmonary starts at the left ventricle, systemic at the right ventricle"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Each circuit begins with the ventricle that pumps blood out into it. The right ventricle pumps oxygen-poor blood into the pulmonary trunk, starting the pulmonary circuit to the lungs. The left ventricle pumps oxygen-rich blood into the aorta, starting the systemic circuit to the body.","notes":["Atria receive blood returning to the heart; they do not start a circuit. Pick the atria if the question asked where each circuit ends.","The left ventricle starts only the systemic circuit. The pulmonary circuit needs its own pump, the right ventricle.","Correct. The right ventricle feeds the pulmonary circuit, and the left ventricle feeds the systemic circuit.","This swaps the two ventricles. The right side of the heart handles oxygen-poor blood headed to the lungs."],"lt":"t-vascular-routes","lid":"W3-453","comp":69,"competencyId":"bvn-circulatory-routes"},{"id":"L03693","dok":2,"q":"Which chamber receives the blood at the end of each circuit?","a":"The pulmonary circuit returns to the left atrium; the systemic circuit returns to the right atrium.","options":["Both circuits return to the right atrium","Pulmonary returns to the right ventricle, systemic to the left ventricle","Pulmonary returns to the left atrium, systemic to the right atrium","Pulmonary returns to the right atrium, systemic to the left atrium"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Each circuit ends in an atrium on the opposite side of the heart from where it began. Oxygen-rich blood from the lungs returns through the pulmonary veins to the left atrium. Oxygen-poor blood from the body returns through the superior and inferior venae cavae and the coronary sinus to the right atrium.","notes":["Only the systemic circuit returns to the right atrium. If the pulmonary circuit did too, oxygen-rich blood would never reach the left ventricle and the body.","Blood returns to the atria first, then passes through the AV valves into the ventricles. Pick the ventricles if the question asked where each circuit starts.","Correct. The lungs drain to the left atrium, and the body drains to the right atrium.","This swaps the two atria. Blood coming back from the lungs must reach the left side so it can be pumped to the body."],"lt":"t-vascular-routes","lid":"W3-454","comp":69,"competencyId":"bvn-circulatory-routes"},{"id":"L03694","dok":2,"q":"Why does a portal system include two capillary beds?","a":"So blood leaving one organ can be processed by a second organ before returning to the heart.","options":["So blood can skip all exchange on its way back to the heart","So a second capillary bed can process blood from the first","So blood can pick up oxygen twice before reaching the body","So the heart can pump less blood with each beat"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"In a portal system, blood picks up materials in the first capillary bed and then carries them straight to a second capillary bed. In the hepatic portal system, nutrients and other substances absorbed by the intestines go directly to the liver, which processes them before they enter the general circulation.","notes":["Skipping exchange is what an arteriovenous anastomosis does. Pick that idea if the question asked about a direct artery-to-vein connection.","Correct. The second capillary bed lets another organ process what the first bed collected.","Blood picks up oxygen only in the lungs. Neither capillary bed in the hepatic portal system adds oxygen.","Portal systems do not change how much blood the heart pumps per beat. They change the route blood takes, not the heart's output."],"lt":"t-vascular-routes","lid":"W3-455","comp":69,"competencyId":"bvn-circulatory-routes"},{"id":"L03695","dok":2,"q":"What does an anastomosis provide if one vessel path is blocked?","a":"An alternate route for blood flow.","options":["A direct path to a vein that skips exchange","A valve that stops blood from flowing backward","A backup route for blood to reach the tissue","A second capillary bed to process the blood"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"An anastomosis is a place where two vessels join. If one path to a tissue becomes blocked, blood can travel through the joining vessel and still reach the tissue by another route. The cerebral arterial circle at the base of the brain and the palmar arches of the hand are examples.","notes":["A direct path to a vein that skips exchange is an arteriovenous anastomosis. It would be the answer if the question asked which connection bypasses capillaries.","Valves stop backflow in veins and are not formed by anastomoses. Choose valves if the question asked what keeps blood moving toward the heart in the legs.","Correct. An anastomosis gives blood a backup route around a blockage.","A second capillary bed in series describes a portal system. Pick it if the question asked about the hepatic portal system."],"lt":"t-vascular-routes","lid":"W3-456","comp":69,"competencyId":"bvn-circulatory-routes"},{"id":"L03696","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares an arteriovenous anastomosis and a capillary bed?","a":"An arteriovenous anastomosis bypasses exchange; a capillary bed is where exchange happens.","options":["Both carry blood away from the heart under high pressure","An arteriovenous anastomosis skips exchange, while a capillary bed is where exchange happens","An arteriovenous anastomosis is where exchange happens, while a capillary bed skips it","Both connect two capillary beds in series, as in a portal system"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"In a capillary bed, blood passes through thin-walled capillaries where gases, nutrients, and wastes are exchanged with the tissue. An arteriovenous anastomosis connects an artery directly to a vein, so blood moving through it skips the capillaries and no exchange takes place. Arteriovenous anastomoses are common in places like the skin of the fingers.","notes":["Capillary beds are low-pressure exchange areas, and an arteriovenous anastomosis carries blood toward a vein. High-pressure flow away from the heart describes arteries.","Correct. The anastomosis bypasses exchange, while the capillary bed performs it.","This reverses the two. Exchange needs the thin capillary wall, which an artery-to-vein shortcut does not have.","Two capillary beds in series describe a portal system, which is different from both of these. Pick a portal system if the question asked about blood passing through the intestines and then the liver."],"lt":"t-vascular-routes","lid":"W3-457","comp":69,"competencyId":"bvn-circulatory-routes"},{"id":"L03697","dok":2,"q":"Why are capillary beds positioned between arterioles and venules?","a":"So exchange with tissues happens before blood starts its return to the heart.","options":["So valves can stop blood from flowing backward","So blood pressure rises before blood enters the veins","So exchange happens before blood heads back to the heart","So blood picks up oxygen before entering the arteries"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The vascular sequence runs from arteries to arterioles, then capillaries, then venules and veins. Arterioles deliver blood to the capillaries at a controlled rate, and the capillaries exchange oxygen, nutrients, and wastes with the tissue. Only after this exchange do venules collect the blood and start it back toward the heart.","notes":["Capillaries have no valves. Valves are found in veins, the answer if the question asked what prevents backflow in the limbs.","Pressure falls, not rises, as blood moves through arterioles and capillaries. By the time blood reaches the veins, its pressure is low.","Correct. Capillary beds sit between the delivery side and the return side so exchange can happen.","Blood picks up oxygen in the lungs, not in tissue capillary beds. Tissue capillaries give oxygen away to cells."],"lt":"t-vascular-routes","lid":"W3-458","comp":67,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-types"},{"id":"L03698","dok":2,"q":"Blood reaches a vein through an arteriovenous anastomosis instead of a capillary bed. What function is skipped?","a":"Exchange of materials with the tissue.","options":["Return of blood to the heart","Oxygenation of blood in the lungs","Propulsion of blood by elastic recoil","Exchange of materials with the tissue"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Capillaries are the only vessels with walls thin enough for exchange. An arteriovenous anastomosis connects an artery straight to a vein, so the blood never enters the capillaries of that tissue. It still returns to the heart, but it does so without delivering oxygen and nutrients or picking up wastes along the way.","notes":["The blood still returns to the heart, because it ends up in a vein. Pick venous return if the question asked what veins and their valves accomplish.","Oxygenation happens in the pulmonary capillaries, not in systemic tissue beds. This would be the answer for a question about what the pulmonary circuit does.","Elastic recoil happens in the large elastic arteries near the heart, upstream of any anastomosis. Choose it if the question asked how the aorta keeps blood moving between beats.","Correct. Bypassing the capillaries means no exchange with that tissue."],"lt":"t-vascular-routes","lid":"W3-480","comp":69,"competencyId":"bvn-circulatory-routes"},{"id":"L03699","dok":2,"q":"Why can anastomoses reduce tissue damage when an artery is blocked?","a":"They provide collateral routes for blood flow.","options":["They send blood through a second capillary bed for processing","They give blood a collateral route around the blockage","They dissolve the clot that caused the blockage","They raise pressure enough to push through the blockage"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"A collateral route is a side path that runs alongside the main one. Where vessels join in an anastomosis, blood can detour through the connecting vessel and reach tissue beyond the blockage. Areas with many anastomoses, such as the hand with its palmar arches or the base of the brain with its cerebral arterial circle, are better protected when one vessel is blocked.","notes":["Passing blood through a second capillary bed describes a portal system. Choose it if the question asked about the hepatic portal system.","Correct. Anastomoses supply a collateral, or side, route that keeps blood reaching the tissue.","Anastomoses are just vessel connections; they do not break down clots. Pick a clot-dissolving process if the question were about treating a blockage rather than routing around it.","Anastomoses route blood around a blockage, not through it. Raising pressure against a blocked vessel would not restore flow beyond the block."],"lt":"t-vascular-routes","lid":"W3-481","comp":69,"competencyId":"bvn-circulatory-routes"},{"id":"L03700","dok":3,"q":"An artery whose region has no anastomoses is suddenly blocked. What is most likely to happen?","a":"The tissue downstream loses its blood supply and may be damaged.","options":["Blood reroutes through a collateral vessel with no harm","The tissue begins drawing its blood from lymphatic vessels","Blood flows backward through veins to reach the tissue","The tissue it supplies loses blood flow and may die"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Without an anastomosis, the blocked artery is the only path to the tissue it supplies. Blood cannot detour around the block, so the downstream tissue is starved of oxygen. If the block lasts, the tissue dies, which is what happens to heart muscle in a heart attack or brain tissue in a stroke.","notes":["Rerouting through a collateral vessel requires an anastomosis, which this region lacks. This would be the answer if the region had one, as the hand does with its palmar arches.","Lymphatic vessels carry lymph away from tissues and do not deliver blood. Pick lymphatic vessels if the question asked what drains excess tissue fluid.","Venous valves keep blood moving toward the heart, so veins cannot deliver blood backward to a tissue. Veins drain tissues; they do not supply them.","Correct. With no collateral route, the downstream tissue loses its blood supply."],"lt":"t-vascular-routes","lid":"W3-482","comp":69,"competencyId":"bvn-circulatory-routes"},{"id":"L03701","dok":2,"q":"Which sequence traces systemic blood from the left ventricle to a tissue capillary?","a":"Left ventricle, aorta, artery, arteriole, capillary.","options":["Left ventricle, aorta, artery, venule, capillary","Left ventricle, pulmonary trunk, artery, arteriole, capillary","Left ventricle, aorta, arteriole, artery, capillary","Left ventricle, aorta, artery, arteriole, capillary"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The left ventricle pumps oxygen-rich blood into the aorta, the largest elastic artery. From there blood moves into smaller muscular arteries that serve each region, then into arterioles, which control how much enters each capillary bed. Vessels get smaller at each step on the way out to the tissues.","notes":["Venules come after capillaries and carry blood toward the heart. They would appear in a sequence tracing blood from a capillary back to the right atrium.","The pulmonary trunk leaves the right ventricle, not the left, and carries blood to the lungs. Pick it if the question traced blood from the right ventricle.","This places the arteriole before the artery. Arterioles are the smallest arteries and come last, right before the capillaries.","Correct. Blood moves from large to small: aorta, artery, arteriole, capillary."],"lt":"t-vascular-routes","lid":"W3-483","comp":67,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-types"},{"id":"L03702","dok":2,"q":"Which sequence traces systemic blood from a tissue capillary back to the heart?","a":"Capillary, venule, vein, vena cava, right atrium.","options":["Capillary, venule, vein, pulmonary vein, left atrium","Capillary, vein, venule, vena cava, right atrium","Capillary, arteriole, vein, vena cava, right atrium","Capillary, venule, vein, vena cava, right atrium"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"After exchange in the capillaries, blood drains into venules, the smallest veins. Venules merge into larger veins, which join the superior or inferior vena cava. The venae cavae empty oxygen-poor blood into the right atrium, finishing the systemic circuit.","notes":["Pulmonary veins carry oxygen-rich blood from the lungs to the left atrium. This path fits the end of the pulmonary circuit, not the systemic circuit.","Veins are larger than venules, so blood reaches venules first. Swapping them reverses the size order of the venous side.","Arterioles carry blood toward capillaries, not away from them. They would appear in a sequence from the left ventricle to a capillary.","Correct. Blood moves from small to large on the way back: capillary, venule, vein, vena cava, right atrium."],"lt":"t-vascular-routes","lid":"W3-484","comp":67,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-types"},{"id":"L03703","dok":2,"q":"Why does the hepatic portal system send blood from the digestive organs through the liver before it reaches the heart?","a":"So the liver can process absorbed materials before they enter the general circulation.","options":["So the liver can filter urine out of the blood","So the liver can process nutrients absorbed by the gut","So the liver can add oxygen to blood leaving the intestines","So blood can skip the digestive organs on its way back"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Blood leaving the stomach and intestines carries newly absorbed nutrients and other substances. The hepatic portal vein delivers this blood straight to the liver's sinusoids, where liver cells store, change, or remove many of those materials. Only then does the blood drain through the hepatic veins to the inferior vena cava and the heart.","notes":["Filtering urine is the job of the kidneys. Pick the kidneys if the question asked which organ filters blood to form urine.","Correct. The liver processes absorbed materials before they reach the rest of the body.","The liver does not add oxygen to blood; oxygen is added only in the lungs. The liver actually receives its oxygen from the hepatic artery.","The hepatic portal system collects blood from the digestive organs, so it does not skip them. Skipping the liver is what the fetal ductus venosus does."],"lt":"t-vascular-routes","lid":"W3-485","comp":69,"competencyId":"bvn-circulatory-routes"},{"id":"L03704","dok":1,"q":"Which statement best defines fetal circulation?","a":"The circulatory plan of a fetus, which gets its oxygen from the placenta instead of its lungs.","options":["The adult circulatory plan that forms after the shunts close","The flow of the mother's blood through the body of the fetus","The flow of blood through the umbilical cord and nowhere else","A fetal circulatory plan that gets oxygen from the placenta"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Before birth, the fetal lungs are collapsed and do not exchange gases. Fetal blood picks up oxygen and nutrients at the placenta and returns to the fetus through the umbilical vein. Fetal circulation is arranged around this fact, with shunts that send much of the blood around the lungs and liver.","notes":["After the shunts close at birth, the baby is on the adult plan, which is the opposite of fetal circulation. Pick this if the question asked what happens once the lungs inflate.","The mother's blood and the fetal blood normally stay separate; materials pass between them across the placenta. This misconception comes from thinking the two blood supplies mix.","The umbilical cord carries only part of the route. Fetal blood circulates through the whole fetal body, the heart, and the shunts as well.","Correct. Fetal circulation is built around the placenta as the source of oxygen."],"lt":"t-fetal-circulation","lid":"W3-531","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L03705","dok":1,"q":"What is the placenta?","a":"The organ where fetal blood picks up oxygen and nutrients and gives up wastes.","options":["An opening in the fetal heart that lets blood bypass the lungs","The cord that connects the fetus to the mother's uterine wall","The organ where fetal blood exchanges gases, nutrients, and wastes","A shunt that carries fetal blood past the liver"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The placenta is the organ of exchange between mother and fetus. Fetal blood flows through it and picks up oxygen and nutrients from the mother's blood while handing off carbon dioxide and other wastes, without the two blood supplies mixing. Before birth, the placenta does the jobs that the lungs, digestive tract, and kidneys will take over later.","notes":["This describes the foramen ovale, an opening in the interatrial septum. It would be the answer if the question asked about the fetal shunt between the atria.","This describes the umbilical cord, which links the fetus to the placenta and carries the umbilical vessels. Pick it if the question asked what carries blood between the fetus and the placenta.","Correct. The placenta is where fetal blood exchanges materials with the mother's blood.","This describes the ductus venosus. Choose it if the question asked which fetal shunt bypasses the liver."],"lt":"t-fetal-circulation","lid":"W3-532","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L03706","dok":1,"q":"What is the umbilical cord?","a":"The cord connecting the fetus to the placenta and carrying the umbilical vessels.","options":["A fibrous cord on the inferior surface of the liver","The cord linking the fetus to the placenta, holding the umbilical vessels","The organ where fetal blood exchanges materials with the mother's blood","A short vessel joining the pulmonary trunk to the aorta"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The umbilical cord links the fetus to the placenta. It carries one umbilical vein, which brings oxygen-rich blood to the fetus, and two umbilical arteries, which take oxygen-poor blood back to the placenta. When the cord is cut at birth, these vessels close and later become ligaments.","notes":["This describes the ligamentum venosum, the adult remnant of the ductus venosus. Choose it if the question asked what the ductus venosus becomes after birth.","Correct. The umbilical cord holds the umbilical vein and arteries.","This describes the placenta, the organ of exchange. Pick it if the question asked where fetal blood picks up oxygen.","This describes the ductus arteriosus, a fetal shunt. It would be the answer if the question asked which vessel diverts blood away from the lungs."],"lt":"t-fetal-circulation","lid":"W3-533","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L03707","dok":1,"q":"Why is most fetal blood routed around the lungs?","a":"The fetal lungs are collapsed and not used for gas exchange before birth.","options":["The lungs already get oxygen from the amniotic fluid","The lungs receive oxygen-rich blood from the umbilical arteries","The lungs are collapsed and not used for gas exchange","The pulmonary trunk does not form until after birth"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Before birth, the fetal lungs are filled with fluid and collapsed, and the placenta handles gas exchange. Sending all the blood through the lungs would waste effort, so the foramen ovale and ductus arteriosus route most of it around them. The lungs still receive a small flow, enough to supply their own developing tissue.","notes":["The fetus does not get oxygen from amniotic fluid; all its oxygen comes through the placenta. This misconception comes from the fact that the fetal lungs contain fluid.","The umbilical arteries carry oxygen-poor blood away from the fetus to the placenta, not to the lungs. They would be the answer if the question asked which vessels return fetal blood to the placenta.","Correct. Collapsed, nonfunctional lungs are the reason the fetus bypasses them.","The pulmonary trunk is present before birth; in fact the ductus arteriosus branches from it. The lungs are bypassed because they are not working, not because the vessels are missing."],"lt":"t-fetal-circulation","lid":"W3-534","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L03708","dok":1,"q":"Why does much fetal blood bypass the liver?","a":"The placenta handles much of the filtering the liver would do after birth.","options":["The ductus arteriosus carries liver blood to the aorta","The fetal liver is collapsed like the fetal lungs","The placenta does much of the liver's later work","The fetal liver has not formed yet"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Before birth, the fetus does not digest food, and the placenta takes care of much of the processing and filtering that the liver will handle after birth. So the ductus venosus, a shunt through the liver, lets most of the oxygen-rich blood from the umbilical vein pass straight toward the inferior vena cava. This gets that blood to the heart quickly.","notes":["The ductus arteriosus connects the pulmonary trunk to the aorta and bypasses the lungs, not the liver. The liver shunt is the ductus venosus.","Collapse applies to the lungs, which are filled with fluid and not used for gas exchange. Pick the lungs if the question asked which organ the foramen ovale and ductus arteriosus bypass.","Correct. The placenta covers much of the liver's later work, so much blood can skip the liver.","The fetal liver is present and large; it is bypassed, not missing. The shunt runs through the liver itself."],"lt":"t-fetal-circulation","lid":"W3-535","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L03709","dok":1,"q":"In fetal circulation, what is a shunt?","a":"A special vessel or opening that routes blood around an organ.","options":["A valve that keeps fetal blood from flowing backward","A special vessel or opening that routes blood around an organ","A capillary bed that exchanges materials with the mother","A ligament left behind after a fetal vessel closes"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"A shunt is a detour. The fetus has three: the ductus venosus routes blood around the liver, and the foramen ovale and ductus arteriosus route blood around the lungs. Each one is needed only before birth, while the placenta handles the jobs those organs will take on later.","notes":["A valve blocks backflow, which is the job of heart valves and venous valves. Shunts are detours, not one-way gates.","Correct. A fetal shunt routes blood around an organ that is not yet in full use.","The capillaries that exchange materials with the mother are in the placenta. Choose the placenta if the question asked where fetal blood picks up oxygen.","A ligament left behind after closure is a remnant, such as the ligamentum arteriosum. Pick it if the question asked what a shunt becomes after birth."],"lt":"t-fetal-circulation","lid":"W3-536","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L03710","dok":1,"q":"Which list names the three fetal shunts?","a":"Ductus venosus, foramen ovale, and ductus arteriosus.","options":["Foramen ovale, ductus arteriosus, and coronary sinus","Fossa ovalis, ligamentum arteriosum, and ligamentum venosum","Umbilical vein, umbilical arteries, and ductus venosus","Ductus venosus, foramen ovale, and ductus arteriosus"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The fetus uses three shunts to send blood around organs that are not yet working fully. The ductus venosus bypasses the liver. The foramen ovale, an opening between the atria, and the ductus arteriosus, a vessel from the pulmonary trunk to the aorta, both bypass the lungs.","notes":["The coronary sinus is a normal vein on the back of the heart that drains the heart wall into the right atrium, not a fetal shunt. Choose it if the question asked where cardiac veins empty.","These are the adult remnants of the shunts. They would be the answer if the question asked what the shunts become after birth.","The umbilical vein and arteries are fetal vessels in the cord, not shunts. Pick this list if the question asked which vessels connect the fetus to the placenta.","Correct. These three are the fetal shunts."],"lt":"t-fetal-circulation","lid":"W3-537","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L03711","dok":1,"q":"What does the umbilical vein carry?","a":"Oxygen-rich blood from the placenta to the fetus.","options":["Oxygen-poor blood from the placenta to the fetus","Oxygen-poor blood from the fetus to the placenta","Oxygen-rich blood from the placenta to the fetus","Oxygen-rich blood from the fetus to the placenta"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Veins are defined by direction: they carry blood toward the heart. The umbilical vein brings blood from the placenta toward the fetal heart, and because that blood has just picked up oxygen at the placenta, it is the most oxygen-rich blood in the fetus.","notes":["This has the direction right but the oxygen wrong. Blood coming from the placenta has just been loaded with oxygen.","This describes the umbilical arteries, which return fetal blood to the placenta. Pick it if the question asked what the umbilical arteries carry.","Correct. The umbilical vein brings oxygen-rich blood from the placenta to the fetus.","This has the oxygen right but the direction backward. Blood leaving the fetus for the placenta has already given up its oxygen to fetal tissues."],"lt":"t-fetal-circulation","lid":"W3-538","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L03712","dok":1,"q":"What is the ductus venosus?","a":"A shunt that passes through the fetal liver.","options":["An opening in the interatrial septum","A short vessel between the pulmonary trunk and aorta","A vessel in the umbilical cord carrying blood to the fetus","A shunt that passes through the fetal liver"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The ductus venosus is a fetal shunt that runs through the liver. It carries most of the blood arriving from the umbilical vein past the liver tissue and on toward the inferior vena cava. After birth it closes and becomes the ligamentum venosum.","notes":["This is the foramen ovale, which lets blood pass from the right atrium to the left atrium. Pick it if the question asked which shunt is a hole in the heart.","This is the ductus arteriosus, which bypasses the lungs. It would be the answer if the question asked which shunt connects to the aorta.","This is the umbilical vein, which brings oxygen-rich blood from the placenta. Choose it if the question asked which vessel carries the most oxygen-rich fetal blood.","Correct. The ductus venosus is the liver bypass of the fetus."],"lt":"t-fetal-circulation","lid":"W3-539","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L03713","dok":1,"q":"Where does the ductus venosus send most of the blood that would otherwise pass through the fetal liver?","a":"Toward the inferior vena cava.","options":["Into the arch of the aorta","Back through the umbilical arteries","Into the left atrium of the heart","Toward the inferior vena cava"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The umbilical vein brings oxygen-rich blood from the placenta to the liver. The ductus venosus lets most of this blood bypass the liver tissue and flow straight toward the inferior vena cava. From there it enters the right atrium, where much of it then crosses through the foramen ovale.","notes":["Blood reaches the aorta from the pulmonary trunk through the ductus arteriosus, a different shunt. Pick the aorta if the question asked where the ductus arteriosus sends blood.","Blood returns to the placenta through the umbilical arteries. Choose the placenta if the question asked where the umbilical arteries go.","Blood reaches the left atrium from the right atrium through the foramen ovale. That would be the answer if the question asked where the foramen ovale sends blood.","Correct. The ductus venosus empties toward the inferior vena cava."],"lt":"t-fetal-circulation","lid":"W3-540","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L03714","dok":1,"q":"What is the foramen ovale?","a":"An opening in the interatrial septum of the fetal heart.","options":["A short vessel between the pulmonary trunk and aorta","A shunt that passes through the fetal liver","An opening in the interventricular septum","An opening in the interatrial septum"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Foramen means opening, and the foramen ovale is an oval opening in the interatrial septum, the wall between the right and left atria. In the fetus it lets blood pass directly from the right atrium to the left atrium, bypassing the lungs. After birth it closes and leaves a shallow depression called the fossa ovalis.","notes":["This describes the ductus arteriosus, the other shunt that bypasses the lungs. Pick it if the question asked which shunt is a vessel rather than an opening.","This describes the ductus venosus. Choose it if the question asked which shunt bypasses the liver.","The interventricular septum separates the ventricles, and the fetus has no normal opening there. An opening in that wall would be a ventricular septal defect, a birth defect, not a fetal shunt.","Correct. The foramen ovale is an opening between the two atria."],"lt":"t-fetal-circulation","lid":"W3-541","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L03715","dok":1,"q":"In the fetus, which way does blood move through the foramen ovale?","a":"From the right atrium to the left atrium.","options":["From the right ventricle to the left ventricle","From the left atrium to the right atrium","From the right atrium to the left atrium","From the pulmonary trunk to the aorta"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"In the fetus, blood arriving from the inferior vena cava enters the right atrium. Instead of all of it going to the right ventricle and lungs, much of it passes through the foramen ovale into the left atrium. From there it goes to the left ventricle and out the aorta to the body, skipping the lungs.","notes":["The foramen ovale connects the atria, not the ventricles. A right-to-left connection between ventricles is not a normal fetal shunt.","This reverses the direction. Right-to-left flow is what lets blood skip the lungs.","Correct. Fetal blood crosses the foramen ovale from right atrium to left atrium.","This is the route through the ductus arteriosus. Pick it if the question asked which way blood moves through the ductus arteriosus."],"lt":"t-fetal-circulation","lid":"W3-542","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L03716","dok":1,"q":"What is the ductus arteriosus?","a":"A short vessel between the pulmonary trunk and the aorta.","options":["One of the paired vessels carrying blood back to the placenta","A short vessel between the pulmonary trunk and the aorta","A shunt that passes through the fetal liver","An opening in the interatrial septum"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The ductus arteriosus is a short fetal vessel that connects the pulmonary trunk to the aorta. It lets blood pumped by the right ventricle skip the collapsed lungs and enter the systemic circulation directly. After birth it closes and becomes the ligamentum arteriosum.","notes":["This describes the umbilical arteries. Choose them if the question asked which vessels carry oxygen-poor blood from the fetus to the placenta.","Correct. The ductus arteriosus links the pulmonary trunk to the aorta.","This is the ductus venosus, the liver bypass. It would be the answer if the question asked which shunt sends blood toward the inferior vena cava.","This is the foramen ovale, the lung bypass between the atria. Pick it if the question asked which shunt is a hole in the heart wall."],"lt":"t-fetal-circulation","lid":"W3-543","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L03717","dok":1,"q":"In the fetus, what does the ductus arteriosus do?","a":"Shunts blood from the pulmonary trunk into the aorta, bypassing the lungs.","options":["Shunts blood from the pulmonary trunk into the aorta","Shunts blood from the umbilical vein toward the vena cava","Shunts blood from the aorta into the pulmonary trunk","Shunts blood from the right atrium into the left atrium"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"In the fetus, the right ventricle pumps blood into the pulmonary trunk, but the lungs are collapsed and resist flow. The ductus arteriosus provides an easier path, carrying most of this blood straight into the aorta. That way, blood that left the right ventricle still reaches the body without going through the lungs.","notes":["Correct. The fetal ductus arteriosus carries blood from the pulmonary trunk to the aorta.","This is the route of the ductus venosus. Choose it if the question asked how fetal blood bypasses the liver.","This reverses the fetal direction. Before birth, blood moves from the pulmonary trunk into the aorta, away from the lungs.","This is the route through the foramen ovale. Pick it if the question asked what the opening between the atria does."],"lt":"t-fetal-circulation","lid":"W3-544","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L03718","dok":1,"q":"What do the umbilical arteries carry?","a":"Oxygen-poor blood from the fetus back to the placenta.","options":["Oxygen-poor blood from the fetus to the placenta","Oxygen-rich blood from the placenta to the fetus","Oxygen-poor blood from the placenta to the fetus","Oxygen-rich blood from the fetus to the placenta"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Arteries carry blood away from the heart. The two umbilical arteries branch from the fetal internal iliac arteries and carry blood away from the fetus to the placenta. That blood has already given up its oxygen to fetal tissues, so it is oxygen-poor and loaded with carbon dioxide and wastes for the placenta to remove.","notes":["Correct. The umbilical arteries return oxygen-poor fetal blood to the placenta.","This describes the umbilical vein. Pick it if the question asked which umbilical vessel carries oxygen-rich blood.","This has the oxygen right but the direction backward. Blood from the placenta is freshly oxygenated and travels in the umbilical vein.","This has the direction right but the oxygen wrong. Blood leaving the fetus has already delivered its oxygen to fetal tissues."],"lt":"t-fetal-circulation","lid":"W3-545","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L03719","dok":1,"q":"What does the umbilical vein become after birth?","a":"The ligamentum teres, the round ligament of the liver.","options":["Ligamentum arteriosum","Ligamentum venosum","Medial umbilical ligament","Ligamentum teres"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"After the umbilical cord is cut, the umbilical vein no longer carries blood and closes into a fibrous cord. This remnant is the ligamentum teres, also called the round ligament of the liver. It runs in the free edge of the falciform ligament toward the liver.","notes":["The ligamentum arteriosum is the remnant of the ductus arteriosus. It would be the answer if the question asked what connects the pulmonary trunk and aorta in an adult.","The ligamentum venosum is the remnant of the ductus venosus. Pick it if the question asked what the liver shunt becomes.","The medial umbilical ligaments are the remnants of the umbilical arteries. Choose them if the question asked what the umbilical arteries become.","Correct. The umbilical vein becomes the ligamentum teres."],"lt":"t-fetal-circulation","lid":"W3-546","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L03720","dok":1,"q":"What does the ductus venosus become after birth?","a":"The ligamentum venosum.","options":["Fossa ovalis","Ligamentum venosum","Ligamentum arteriosum","Ligamentum teres"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Once the placenta is cut off at birth, there is no longer a stream of umbilical blood to send past the liver. The ductus venosus closes and becomes the ligamentum venosum, a fibrous cord on the inferior surface of the liver. Its name keeps the venosus part of the original shunt.","notes":["The fossa ovalis is the remnant of the foramen ovale. Choose it if the question asked about the opening between the atria.","Correct. The ductus venosus becomes the ligamentum venosum.","The ligamentum arteriosum comes from the ductus arteriosus. It would be the answer if the question asked about the lung bypass vessel.","The ligamentum teres comes from the umbilical vein. It is also on the liver, which makes it easy to mix up, so pick it if the question asked about the umbilical vein."],"lt":"t-fetal-circulation","lid":"W3-547","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L03721","dok":1,"q":"What does the foramen ovale become after birth?","a":"The fossa ovalis.","options":["Fossa ovalis","Ligamentum arteriosum","Ligamentum venosum","Ligamentum teres"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"At birth, pressure in the left atrium rises as blood returns from the newly working lungs, pushing a flap against the foramen ovale and closing it. The opening seals over and leaves the fossa ovalis, a shallow oval depression in the interatrial septum seen from the right atrium. It is the one remnant that becomes a depression rather than a ligament, because it was a hole rather than a vessel.","notes":["Correct. The foramen ovale becomes the fossa ovalis.","The ligamentum arteriosum is the remnant of the ductus arteriosus. Pick it if the question asked about the other lung bypass.","The ligamentum venosum is the remnant of the ductus venosus. It would be the answer if the question asked about the liver bypass.","The ligamentum teres is the remnant of the umbilical vein. Choose it if the question asked what the vessel bringing blood from the placenta becomes."],"lt":"t-fetal-circulation","lid":"W3-548","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L03722","dok":1,"q":"What does the ductus arteriosus become after birth?","a":"The ligamentum arteriosum.","options":["Ligamentum arteriosum","Ligamentum venosum","Ligamentum teres","Medial umbilical ligament"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"After birth the lungs inflate and blood flows easily into them, so the ductus arteriosus is no longer needed. It constricts and closes, becoming the ligamentum arteriosum, a short fibrous cord between the pulmonary trunk and the aortic arch. Its name keeps the arteriosus part of the original vessel.","notes":["Correct. The ductus arteriosus becomes the ligamentum arteriosum.","The ligamentum venosum comes from the ductus venosus, the liver bypass. Pick it if the question asked about the shunt through the liver.","The ligamentum teres comes from the umbilical vein. It would be the answer if the question asked about the vessel from the placenta.","The medial umbilical ligaments come from the umbilical arteries. Choose them if the question asked about the vessels returning blood to the placenta."],"lt":"t-fetal-circulation","lid":"W3-549","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L03723","dok":1,"q":"What do the umbilical arteries become after birth?","a":"The medial umbilical ligaments.","options":["Median umbilical ligament","Ligamentum teres","Medial umbilical ligaments","Ligamentum arteriosum"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Once the cord is cut, the parts of the umbilical arteries that ran toward the umbilicus close into fibrous cords. These are the medial umbilical ligaments, paired cords on the inner surface of the anterior abdominal wall. There are two of them because there were two umbilical arteries.","notes":["The median umbilical ligament is a single midline cord that is the remnant of the urachus, a fetal canal from the bladder, not of a blood vessel. The names differ by one letter, so read carefully.","The ligamentum teres is the remnant of the umbilical vein. Pick it if the question asked about the vessel carrying blood from the placenta.","Correct. The paired umbilical arteries become the paired medial umbilical ligaments.","The ligamentum arteriosum is the remnant of the ductus arteriosus. It would be the answer if the question asked about the cord between the pulmonary trunk and the aorta."],"lt":"t-fetal-circulation","lid":"W3-550","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L03724","dok":2,"q":"Which single difference explains why fetal circulation is arranged differently from the adult plan?","a":"The fetus gets its oxygen from the placenta rather than its own lungs.","options":["Fetal arteries carry blood toward the heart instead of away","The fetus has no liver until shortly after birth","The fetal heart has only two chambers instead of four","The fetus gets its oxygen from the placenta rather than its lungs"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Every special feature of fetal circulation follows from where the oxygen comes from. Because the placenta, not the lungs, supplies oxygen, the umbilical vein carries the richest blood, and the foramen ovale and ductus arteriosus send most blood past the nonworking lungs. Once the lungs take over at birth, these features are no longer needed.","notes":["Arteries carry blood away from the heart in both the fetus and the adult. The umbilical arteries are unusual only because their blood is oxygen-poor.","The fetal liver is present and large before birth. Blood partly bypasses it through the ductus venosus, but the liver itself exists.","The fetal heart has four chambers, the same as the adult heart. The difference is the opening between the atria, the foramen ovale.","Correct. Relying on the placenta for oxygen is the reason for the whole fetal arrangement."],"lt":"t-fetal-circulation","lid":"W3-566","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L03725","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares the ductus venosus and the ductus arteriosus?","a":"The ductus venosus bypasses the liver; the ductus arteriosus bypasses the lungs.","options":["The ductus venosus joins the atria, while the ductus arteriosus joins the ventricles","The ductus venosus bypasses the liver, while the ductus arteriosus bypasses the lungs","The ductus venosus bypasses the lungs, while the ductus arteriosus bypasses the liver","Both bypass the lungs, one in the heart wall and one in the liver"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Both are fetal shunts named ductus, meaning duct. The ductus venosus is on the venous side, in the liver, carrying umbilical vein blood past the liver toward the inferior vena cava. The ductus arteriosus is on the arterial side, linking the pulmonary trunk to the aorta so blood skips the lungs.","notes":["Neither ductus is in the heart wall. The atria are joined by the foramen ovale, the answer if the question asked about the opening between the atria.","Correct. Venosus bypasses the liver, and arteriosus bypasses the lungs.","This swaps the two. A helpful clue is that the ductus arteriosus joins two great arteries, the pulmonary trunk and the aorta.","Only the ductus arteriosus bypasses the lungs. The ductus venosus is in the liver and bypasses the liver."],"lt":"t-fetal-circulation","lid":"W3-567","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L03726","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares the foramen ovale and the ductus arteriosus?","a":"Both bypass the lungs; the foramen ovale joins the atria and the ductus arteriosus joins the pulmonary trunk to the aorta.","options":["Both bypass the lungs, one joining the ventricles and the other joining the aorta to the vena cava","Both bypass the liver, one joining the atria and the other joining the umbilical vein to the vena cava","Both bypass the lungs, one joining the atria and the other joining the pulmonary trunk to the aorta","One bypasses the lungs and the other bypasses the liver, and both are openings in a septum"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The fetus has two lung bypasses that work at different levels. The foramen ovale is an opening in the interatrial septum that moves blood from the right atrium to the left atrium. The ductus arteriosus is a vessel that moves blood from the pulmonary trunk to the aorta. Between them, most blood never passes through the collapsed lungs.","notes":["The foramen ovale joins the atria, not the ventricles, and the ductus arteriosus joins the pulmonary trunk to the aorta, not to the vena cava.","Neither one bypasses the liver; that is the ductus venosus. A shunt from the umbilical vein toward the vena cava describes the ductus venosus.","Correct. Both are lung bypasses, one between the atria and one between the great arteries.","Both bypass the lungs, and only the foramen ovale is an opening in a septum. The ductus arteriosus is a vessel outside the heart."],"lt":"t-fetal-circulation","lid":"W3-568","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L03727","dok":2,"q":"Why is the umbilical vein unusual compared with most systemic veins?","a":"It carries oxygen-rich blood.","options":["It carries blood from the fetus to the placenta","It carries blood away from the heart, unlike other veins","It has no tunica intima, unlike other veins","It carries oxygen-rich blood, unlike most systemic veins"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Most systemic veins carry oxygen-poor blood back from the tissues. The umbilical vein is still a vein because it carries blood toward the fetal heart, but that blood comes straight from the placenta and is full of oxygen. The pulmonary veins are the other well-known veins that carry oxygen-rich blood, for the same reason: they drain the organ of gas exchange.","notes":["Blood traveling from the fetus to the placenta moves in the umbilical arteries. Pick them if the question asked which vessels return fetal blood to the placenta.","Veins are defined by carrying blood toward the heart, and the umbilical vein does that. A vessel carrying blood away from the heart would be an artery.","All veins have a tunica intima, the endothelial lining. Only the proportions of the media and externa vary between vessels.","Correct. The umbilical vein carries oxygen-rich blood, which is unusual for a vein."],"lt":"t-fetal-circulation","lid":"W3-569","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L03728","dok":2,"q":"Why are the umbilical arteries unusual compared with most systemic arteries?","a":"They carry oxygen-poor blood.","options":["They have valves along their length, like limb veins","They carry blood toward the heart, unlike other arteries","They carry oxygen-poor blood, unlike most systemic arteries","They carry blood from the placenta to the fetus"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Most systemic arteries carry oxygen-rich blood from the heart to the tissues. The umbilical arteries are still arteries because they carry blood away from the fetal heart, but that blood has already given its oxygen to fetal tissues and is heading to the placenta to be refreshed. The pulmonary arteries are the other arteries that carry oxygen-poor blood, since they also lead to the organ of gas exchange.","notes":["Arteries do not have valves along their length. Valves are a feature of veins, especially in the limbs.","Arteries are defined by carrying blood away from the heart, and the umbilical arteries do exactly that. A vessel carrying blood toward the heart would be a vein.","Correct. The umbilical arteries carry oxygen-poor blood, which is unusual for arteries.","Blood traveling from the placenta to the fetus moves in the umbilical vein. Pick it if the question asked which vessel carries oxygen-rich blood into the fetus."],"lt":"t-fetal-circulation","lid":"W3-570","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L03729","dok":2,"q":"What is the purpose of moving blood from the right atrium to the left atrium through the foramen ovale?","a":"It sends blood past the nonfunctional fetal lungs.","options":["It returns oxygen-rich blood from the lungs to the right heart","It sends blood to the left heart without passing through the lungs","It lets blood skip the liver on its way to the heart","It sends oxygen-poor blood back to the placenta"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Blood normally reaches the left atrium only after passing from the right ventricle through the lungs. In the fetus the lungs are collapsed and not exchanging gases, so the foramen ovale offers a shortcut. Blood crosses directly from the right atrium to the left atrium and then goes out the aorta to the body, saving a trip through the lungs.","notes":["Blood returning from working lungs enters the left atrium through the pulmonary veins, not the right heart. That route belongs to the adult pulmonary circuit.","Correct. The foramen ovale lets blood reach the left heart while skipping the lungs.","Skipping the liver is the job of the ductus venosus. It would be the answer if the question asked about the liver bypass.","Returning oxygen-poor blood to the placenta is the job of the umbilical arteries. Pick them if the question asked which vessels carry blood back to the placenta."],"lt":"t-fetal-circulation","lid":"W3-571","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L03730","dok":2,"q":"Why does the fetal ductus arteriosus connect the pulmonary trunk to the aorta?","a":"It diverts blood away from the collapsed fetal lungs.","options":["It diverts blood from the pulmonary trunk away from the collapsed lungs","It moves blood between the atria without entering the ventricles","It lets blood from the placenta skip the liver","It sends blood from the aorta to the lungs to pick up oxygen"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Before birth, the right ventricle still pumps blood into the pulmonary trunk, but the collapsed, fluid-filled lungs resist flow. The ductus arteriosus gives that blood an easier path into the aorta, so it reaches the body instead of the lungs. Only a small share of blood continues into the pulmonary arteries to nourish the developing lung tissue.","notes":["Correct. The ductus arteriosus moves blood from the pulmonary trunk into the aorta, away from the lungs.","Moving blood between the atria is the job of the foramen ovale. It would be the answer if the question asked about the opening in the interatrial septum.","Skipping the liver is the job of the ductus venosus. Pick it if the question asked about the liver bypass.","The fetal lungs do not oxygenate blood, so sending blood to them for oxygen would not help. Oxygen comes from the placenta through the umbilical vein."],"lt":"t-fetal-circulation","lid":"W3-572","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L03731","dok":2,"q":"What is the advantage of routing umbilical vein blood through the ductus venosus?","a":"Most of the oxygen-rich placental blood reaches the inferior vena cava without passing through liver tissue.","options":["Blood from the liver is sent straight into the aorta","Blood skips the lungs by moving between the atria","Oxygen-poor fetal blood reaches the placenta faster","Oxygen-rich blood skips the liver on its way to the heart"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The umbilical vein carries the most oxygen-rich blood in the fetus. If all of it passed through the liver's sinusoids, liver tissue would use some of that oxygen and slow the trip. The ductus venosus lets most of it travel straight toward the inferior vena cava and on to the heart, so well-oxygenated blood reaches the body quickly.","notes":["Blood reaches the aorta from the pulmonary trunk through the ductus arteriosus, not from the liver. The ductus venosus empties toward the inferior vena cava.","Moving between the atria is the job of the foramen ovale, a lung bypass. It would be the answer if the question asked how blood skips the lungs inside the heart.","Oxygen-poor blood returns to the placenta through the umbilical arteries, which do not pass through the liver. Pick them if the question asked how fetal blood gets back to the placenta.","Correct. The ductus venosus speeds oxygen-rich blood past the liver to the vena cava."],"lt":"t-fetal-circulation","lid":"W3-573","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L03732","dok":2,"q":"In an adult heart, the fossa ovalis is the remnant of which fetal structure?","a":"The foramen ovale.","options":["Ductus venosus","Ductus arteriosus","Umbilical vein","Foramen ovale"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The fossa ovalis is a shallow oval depression in the interatrial septum, seen from inside the right atrium. It marks the spot where the foramen ovale, the fetal opening between the atria, sealed shut after birth. Fossa means a shallow pit, which fits because the old opening is now covered but still visible.","notes":["The ductus venosus becomes the ligamentum venosum on the liver. It would be the answer if the question asked about the liver shunt's remnant.","The ductus arteriosus becomes the ligamentum arteriosum between the pulmonary trunk and the aorta. Pick it if the question asked about that ligament.","The umbilical vein becomes the ligamentum teres of the liver. Choose it if the question asked about the round ligament of the liver.","Correct. The fossa ovalis marks where the foramen ovale closed."],"lt":"t-fetal-circulation","lid":"W3-574","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L03733","dok":2,"q":"The ligamentum arteriosum is a short fibrous cord between the pulmonary trunk and the aortic arch. Which fetal structure did it come from?","a":"The ductus arteriosus.","options":["Ductus venosus","Umbilical arteries","Foramen ovale","Ductus arteriosus"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"In the fetus, the ductus arteriosus carried blood from the pulmonary trunk into the aorta to bypass the lungs. After birth it closes and shrinks into the ligamentum arteriosum, which still connects the same two vessels. The name keeps the arteriosus part of the original shunt.","notes":["The ductus venosus becomes the ligamentum venosum on the inferior surface of the liver. Pick it if the question asked about a ligament on the liver.","The umbilical arteries become the medial umbilical ligaments on the anterior abdominal wall. Choose them if the question asked about remnants on the abdominal wall.","The foramen ovale becomes the fossa ovalis, a depression rather than a cord. It would be the answer if the question asked about the interatrial septum.","Correct. The ligamentum arteriosum is the closed ductus arteriosus."],"lt":"t-fetal-circulation","lid":"W3-575","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L03734","dok":2,"q":"The ligamentum teres runs in the free edge of the falciform ligament of the liver. Which fetal vessel did it come from?","a":"The umbilical vein.","options":["Ductus venosus","Umbilical vein","Ductus arteriosus","Umbilical arteries"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"In the fetus, the umbilical vein carried oxygen-rich blood from the placenta through the umbilicus to the liver. After the cord is cut, the vessel closes into a fibrous cord, the ligamentum teres or round ligament of the liver. It still runs from the umbilicus to the liver, following the old path of the vein.","notes":["The ductus venosus also involves the liver, which makes it a tempting choice, but it becomes the ligamentum venosum. Pick it if the question asked about the fibrous cord on the inferior surface of the liver.","Correct. The ligamentum teres is the closed umbilical vein.","The ductus arteriosus becomes the ligamentum arteriosum near the heart. Choose it if the question asked about the cord between the pulmonary trunk and aorta.","The umbilical arteries become the medial umbilical ligaments. They would be the answer if the question asked about cords on the inner surface of the anterior abdominal wall."],"lt":"t-fetal-circulation","lid":"W3-576","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L03735","dok":2,"q":"The ligamentum venosum is a fibrous cord on the inferior surface of the liver. Which fetal structure did it come from?","a":"The ductus venosus.","options":["Foramen ovale","Umbilical vein","Ductus arteriosus","Ductus venosus"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The ductus venosus was the fetal shunt through the liver that carried umbilical vein blood toward the inferior vena cava. After birth, with no placental blood arriving, it closes into the ligamentum venosum. Its name keeps the venosus part of the original shunt.","notes":["The foramen ovale becomes the fossa ovalis in the heart. Choose it if the question asked about the depression in the interatrial septum.","The umbilical vein becomes the ligamentum teres, another liver remnant. Pick it if the question asked about the round ligament of the liver.","The ductus arteriosus becomes the ligamentum arteriosum near the heart. It would be the answer if the question asked about a cord between the pulmonary trunk and aorta.","Correct. The ligamentum venosum is the closed ductus venosus."],"lt":"t-fetal-circulation","lid":"W3-577","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L03736","dok":2,"q":"The medial umbilical ligaments lie on the inner surface of the anterior abdominal wall. Which fetal vessels did they come from?","a":"The umbilical arteries.","options":["Umbilical vein","Ductus arteriosus","Umbilical arteries","Ductus venosus"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The two umbilical arteries carried oxygen-poor blood from the fetus out through the umbilicus to the placenta. After the cord is cut, the parts of these arteries running up the inside of the abdominal wall close into fibrous cords, the medial umbilical ligaments. There are two of them, one for each umbilical artery.","notes":["The umbilical vein becomes the ligamentum teres, which runs to the liver. Pick it if the question asked about the round ligament of the liver.","The ductus arteriosus becomes the ligamentum arteriosum near the heart. Choose it if the question asked about the lung bypass vessel.","Correct. The medial umbilical ligaments are the closed umbilical arteries.","The ductus venosus becomes the ligamentum venosum on the liver. It would be the answer if the question asked about the liver shunt."],"lt":"t-fetal-circulation","lid":"W3-578","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L03737","dok":2,"q":"Which changes at birth make the fetal shunts unnecessary?","a":"The lungs inflate and the placental circulation is cut off.","options":["The lungs inflate and the placenta is cut off","The liver starts working and the heart gains a chamber","The lungs collapse and the placenta takes over","The umbilical cord lengthens and the foramen ovale widens"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"With the first breaths, the lungs fill with air and blood can flow through them easily, so there is no reason to route blood around them. At the same time, cutting the umbilical cord ends placental flow, so there is no stream of umbilical blood for the ductus venosus to carry past the liver. Each shunt then closes and leaves behind a ligament or remnant.","notes":["Correct. Inflated lungs and the loss of the placenta remove the need for the shunts.","The heart already has four chambers before birth, and the liver is present. The change at birth is in where oxygen comes from, not in the number of chambers.","This is the reverse of what happens. The lungs inflate at birth, and the placenta stops working once the cord is cut.","The cord is cut at birth, and the foramen ovale closes rather than widens. A widening foramen ovale would keep blood bypassing the lungs."],"lt":"t-fetal-circulation","lid":"W3-579","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L03738","dok":2,"q":"What is a patent ductus arteriosus?","a":"A ductus arteriosus that remains open after birth.","options":["A foramen ovale that stays open after birth","A ductus arteriosus that stays open after birth","A ductus venosus that stays open after birth","A ductus arteriosus that closes before birth"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Patent means open. Normally the ductus arteriosus closes in the first days after birth and becomes the ligamentum arteriosum. If it stays open, a patent ductus arteriosus leaves an abnormal connection between the aorta and the pulmonary trunk, linking the systemic and pulmonary sides of the circulation.","notes":["An open foramen ovale is a patent foramen ovale. Pick it if the question described a lasting connection between the atria.","Correct. A patent ductus arteriosus is a ductus arteriosus that fails to close.","The ductus venosus is the liver shunt, and the term patent ductus arteriosus refers only to the shunt between the pulmonary trunk and aorta. A lasting open ductus venosus would be named for the ductus venosus.","Closing early is the opposite of patent. Patent always means remaining open."],"lt":"t-fetal-circulation","lid":"W3-580","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L03739","dok":2,"q":"What is a patent foramen ovale?","a":"A foramen ovale that remains open after birth.","options":["A foramen ovale that stays open after birth","A ductus arteriosus that stays open after birth","An opening between the ventricles present at birth","A foramen ovale that seals into the fossa ovalis on schedule"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Patent means open. Normally the foramen ovale closes soon after birth and leaves the fossa ovalis in the interatrial septum. When it stays open, a patent foramen ovale leaves an abnormal connection between the right and left atria.","notes":["Correct. A patent foramen ovale is a foramen ovale that fails to seal.","An open ductus arteriosus is a patent ductus arteriosus. Pick it if the question described a lasting connection between the pulmonary trunk and aorta.","An opening between the ventricles is a ventricular septal defect, a different birth defect. The foramen ovale is between the atria, not the ventricles.","Sealing on schedule into the fossa ovalis is normal closure, the opposite of patent. This would be the answer if the question asked what normally happens to the foramen ovale."],"lt":"t-fetal-circulation","lid":"W3-581","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L03740","dok":2,"q":"Why is a fetal shunt that stays open after birth considered abnormal?","a":"It leaves an abnormal connection between the two sides of the circulation.","options":["It makes the umbilical vessels reopen after the cord is cut","It links two circuits that should be separate after birth","It turns the shunt into a ligament much too early","It blocks all blood from reaching the lungs after birth"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"After birth the pulmonary and systemic circuits are meant to be separate, with the right heart serving the lungs and the left heart serving the body. A shunt that stays open, such as a patent ductus arteriosus or a patent foramen ovale, links these two circuits where they should be sealed apart. That abnormal connection lets blood cross between sides that should no longer mix.","notes":["The umbilical vessels close once the cord is cut, and an open heart shunt does not reopen them. Their closure produces the ligamentum teres and the medial umbilical ligaments.","Correct. An open shunt connects circuits that should be separate after birth.","Becoming a ligament is the normal, healthy outcome. A patent shunt is a problem because it fails to close, not because it closes too soon.","An open shunt diverts some blood, but it does not stop all blood from reaching the lungs. The lungs still receive blood through the pulmonary arteries."],"lt":"t-fetal-circulation","lid":"W3-582","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L03741","dok":2,"q":"A newborn still has an open vessel connecting the pulmonary trunk and the aorta. Which fetal structure failed to close?","a":"The ductus arteriosus.","options":["Foramen ovale","Umbilical vein","Ductus venosus","Ductus arteriosus"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The ductus arteriosus is the fetal vessel joining the pulmonary trunk and the aorta. It normally closes shortly after birth and becomes the ligamentum arteriosum. When it stays open, the condition is a patent ductus arteriosus, named for the structure that failed to seal.","notes":["The foramen ovale is an opening between the atria, not a vessel between the great arteries. Pick it if the newborn had a lasting connection between the atria.","The umbilical vein runs from the umbilicus to the liver and closes into the ligamentum teres. Choose it if the question asked about the vessel that carried blood from the placenta.","The ductus venosus is in the liver and connects to the inferior vena cava side. It would be the answer if the question described a shunt through the liver.","Correct. An open vessel between the pulmonary trunk and aorta is a patent ductus arteriosus."],"lt":"t-fetal-circulation","lid":"W3-593","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L03742","dok":2,"q":"A newborn has a persistent opening between the right and left atria. Which fetal structure stayed open?","a":"The foramen ovale.","options":["Ductus arteriosus","Foramen ovale","Ductus venosus","Fossa ovalis"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The foramen ovale is the fetal opening in the interatrial septum that lets blood pass from the right atrium to the left atrium. It normally seals after birth and leaves the fossa ovalis. An opening that persists between the atria is a patent foramen ovale.","notes":["The ductus arteriosus is a vessel between the pulmonary trunk and the aorta, outside the atria. Pick it if the newborn had an open connection between those two great arteries.","Correct. A lasting opening between the atria is a patent foramen ovale.","The ductus venosus is the liver bypass and is not part of the heart. It would be the answer if the question described a shunt through the liver.","The fossa ovalis is what remains after the foramen ovale closes normally, so it is a sealed depression, not an opening. Choose it if the question asked what the foramen ovale becomes."],"lt":"t-fetal-circulation","lid":"W3-594","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L03743","dok":2,"q":"If the ductus venosus failed to form, which organ would fetal blood no longer bypass efficiently?","a":"The liver.","options":["Liver","Kidneys","Lungs","Heart"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The ductus venosus is the shunt that carries umbilical vein blood through the liver and toward the inferior vena cava without passing through the liver's sinusoids. Without it, much more of this blood would have to pass through the liver tissue itself. The lung bypasses, the foramen ovale and ductus arteriosus, would be unaffected.","notes":["Correct. The ductus venosus is the liver bypass.","The fetus has no shunt that bypasses the kidneys. Kidneys would not be the answer for any of the fetal shunts.","The lungs are bypassed by the foramen ovale and the ductus arteriosus. Pick the lungs if the question asked about either of those shunts.","Blood still passes through the heart in every fetal route; the shunts change which chambers and vessels it uses. The heart would not be the bypassed organ."],"lt":"t-fetal-circulation","lid":"W3-595","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L03744","dok":2,"q":"If the foramen ovale failed to form, which fetal bypass route would be lost?","a":"The lung bypass between the atria.","options":["Liver bypass toward the inferior vena cava","Return of fetal blood to the placenta","Lung bypass between the atria","Lung bypass from the pulmonary trunk to the aorta"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The foramen ovale is an opening in the interatrial septum that lets blood move from the right atrium to the left atrium without going through the lungs. Without it, that atrial-level lung bypass would be gone. The ductus arteriosus would still provide the second lung bypass between the pulmonary trunk and the aorta.","notes":["The liver bypass is the ductus venosus, which would still be present. Pick it if the question asked what the ductus venosus does.","Return to the placenta happens through the umbilical arteries, not the foramen ovale. Choose it if the question asked what the umbilical arteries do.","Correct. The foramen ovale is the lung bypass between the atria.","The pulmonary trunk to aorta route is the ductus arteriosus, a separate shunt that would still work. It would be the answer if the ductus arteriosus were missing."],"lt":"t-fetal-circulation","lid":"W3-596","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L03745","dok":2,"q":"If the ductus arteriosus failed to form, which fetal bypass route would be lost?","a":"The lung bypass from the pulmonary trunk to the aorta.","options":["Lung bypass from the pulmonary trunk to the aorta","Return of fetal blood to the placenta","Liver bypass toward the inferior vena cava","Lung bypass between the atria"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The ductus arteriosus is the short vessel that carries blood from the pulmonary trunk into the aorta, sending blood from the right ventricle past the lungs. Without it, that route would be lost, and more blood leaving the right ventricle would be pushed toward the collapsed lungs. The foramen ovale would still provide the atrial-level bypass.","notes":["Correct. The ductus arteriosus is the lung bypass between the pulmonary trunk and the aorta.","Return to the placenta happens through the umbilical arteries. Choose it if the question asked which vessels carry oxygen-poor blood away from the fetus.","The liver bypass is the ductus venosus. It would be the answer if the question asked which shunt runs through the liver.","The bypass between the atria is the foramen ovale, which would still be present. Pick it if the foramen ovale were missing."],"lt":"t-fetal-circulation","lid":"W3-597","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L03746","dok":2,"q":"Why does the umbilical vein carry the most oxygen-rich blood in the fetus?","a":"Its blood comes directly from the placenta.","options":["Its blood has mixed with blood returning from the lower body","Its blood has just passed through the fetal lungs","Its blood has just picked up oxygen at the placenta","Its blood has just left the left ventricle"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The placenta is where fetal blood picks up oxygen, and the umbilical vein is the first vessel the blood enters after leaving it. Once this blood reaches the fetal body, it starts to mix with oxygen-poor blood, for example in the inferior vena cava and the right atrium. So the umbilical vein holds the richest blood before any mixing happens.","notes":["Mixing with returning blood lowers oxygen content rather than raising it. This happens in the inferior vena cava, after the umbilical vein blood has arrived.","The fetal lungs are collapsed and do not add oxygen. In the adult, freshly oxygenated blood would come from the lungs through the pulmonary veins.","Correct. The umbilical vein carries blood fresh from the placenta.","Blood leaving the left ventricle has already mixed with less oxygenated blood in the fetal heart. In the adult, the left ventricle would be the source of the most oxygen-rich blood pumped to the body."],"lt":"t-fetal-circulation","lid":"W3-598","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L03747","dok":2,"q":"Why do the umbilical arteries carry oxygen-poor blood away from the fetus?","a":"They return fetal blood to the placenta for gas and waste exchange.","options":["They take used fetal blood to the placenta for exchange","They carry blood to the fetal liver for processing","They return blood to the fetal right atrium","They carry blood to the fetal lungs to pick up oxygen"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"By the time blood reaches the umbilical arteries, it has already delivered oxygen to fetal tissues and picked up carbon dioxide and other wastes. The umbilical arteries carry this used blood out through the cord to the placenta, where wastes pass to the mother's blood and fresh oxygen is picked up. The refreshed blood then returns in the umbilical vein.","notes":["Correct. The umbilical arteries take used blood to the placenta for exchange.","The liver receives blood from the umbilical vein, partly bypassed by the ductus venosus. It would be the answer if the question asked which organ the ductus venosus routes blood past.","Blood returns to the right atrium through the venae cavae, not the umbilical arteries. Arteries carry blood away from the heart.","The fetal lungs do not exchange gases before birth. Pick the lungs if the question asked about gas exchange after birth."],"lt":"t-fetal-circulation","lid":"W3-599","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L03748","dok":2,"q":"Which sentence best summarizes how fetal circulation works?","a":"Oxygen-rich placental blood enters the fetus, and shunts route much of it around the liver and lungs until birth.","options":["Placental blood passes fully through the liver and lungs before reaching the body","Oxygen-poor blood from the placenta enters the fetus and picks up oxygen in the fetal liver","Placental oxygen-rich blood enters, and shunts route much of it past the liver and lungs","Oxygen-rich blood from the fetal lungs is pumped to the placenta for storage"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The fetus depends on the placenta for oxygen and nutrients. Oxygen-rich blood enters through the umbilical vein, the ductus venosus carries much of it past the liver, and the foramen ovale and ductus arteriosus carry much of it past the collapsed lungs. Used blood returns to the placenta through the umbilical arteries, and the shunts close once the lungs take over at birth.","notes":["Sending all placental blood through the liver and lungs is exactly what the shunts prevent. This describes a route without any shunts.","Blood coming from the placenta is already oxygen-rich, and the liver does not add oxygen. Only the placenta before birth, and the lungs after birth, load blood with oxygen.","Correct. Placental oxygen plus three shunts around the liver and lungs sums up fetal circulation.","The fetal lungs do not oxygenate blood, and the placenta is an exchange organ, not a storage site. This reverses the direction of oxygen supply."],"lt":"t-fetal-circulation","lid":"W3-600","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L03749","dok":1,"q":"Which features describe a healthy blood vessel?","a":"A smooth endothelial lining and a wall that can stretch and recoil.","options":["A lining with fatty deposits and a narrowed lumen","A smooth lining and a wall that stretches and recoils","A smooth lining and a thin wall bulging outward","A rough, damaged lining and a thick, stiff wall"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"A healthy vessel has an intact, smooth endothelium lining its tunica intima, which lets blood flow without sticking or clotting. Its wall, especially in arteries, can stretch with each pulse of blood and then recoil. Most vascular disorders damage one of these two features, either roughening the lining or stiffening and weakening the wall.","notes":["Fatty deposits and a narrowed lumen describe atherosclerosis. Choose this if the question asked how plaque changes an artery.","Correct. A smooth lining and an elastic wall mark a healthy vessel.","An outward bulge describes an aneurysm, a weakened wall that can rupture. It would be the answer if the question asked about a balloon-like swelling of an artery.","A rough lining and a stiff, thick wall describe a diseased artery, as in arteriosclerosis. Pick this if the question asked about general stiffening of artery walls."],"lt":"t-vessel-disorders","lid":"W3-501","comp":70,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-disorders"},{"id":"L03750","dok":1,"q":"What is a vascular disorder?","a":"A disease of a blood vessel, most often one that narrows the lumen, weakens the wall, or lets blood pool.","options":["A disease of the heart valves that lets blood leak backward","A vessel disease that narrows, weakens, or lets blood pool","A blockage of lymphatic vessels that causes tissue swelling","A shortage of red blood cells or hemoglobin in the blood"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Vascular means relating to blood vessels. A vascular disorder is a disease of the vessel itself, and most of them change the vessel in one of three ways: they narrow the lumen, as atherosclerosis does; they weaken the wall, as in an aneurysm; or they let blood pool, as in varicose veins.","notes":["Leaky heart valves are a heart disorder, involving valves inside the heart. This would be the answer if the question asked about valve problems of the heart.","Correct. Vascular disorders narrow, weaken, or let blood pool in vessels.","Blocked lymphatic vessels cause lymphedema, a lymphatic disorder. Choose it if the question asked about swelling from poor lymph drainage.","A shortage of red blood cells or hemoglobin is anemia, a blood disorder rather than a vessel disorder. Pick it if the question asked about the blood's oxygen-carrying ability."],"lt":"t-vessel-disorders","lid":"W3-502","comp":70,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-disorders"},{"id":"L03751","dok":1,"q":"Which layer of an artery wall is damaged at the start of plaque formation?","a":"The tunica intima, specifically its endothelial lining.","options":["Tunica externa","Tunica media","Tunica intima","External elastic lamina"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Atherosclerosis begins with injury to the endothelium, the smooth lining of the tunica intima. Once this lining is damaged, fatty material, cells, and debris start to collect in the wall at that spot, forming a plaque. Because the plaque starts in the innermost layer, it bulges into the lumen as it grows.","notes":["The tunica externa is the outer collagen layer that anchors the vessel. It would be the answer if the question asked which layer is thickest in a vein.","The tunica media is the middle smooth muscle and elastic layer. Pick it if the question asked which layer is thickest in an artery.","Correct. Plaque starts with damage to the endothelium of the tunica intima.","The external elastic lamina separates the media from the externa, well away from the lining. Choose it if the question asked about the boundary between the middle and outer layers."],"lt":"t-vessel-disorders","lid":"W3-504","comp":70,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-disorders"},{"id":"L03752","dok":1,"q":"What is an atheroma?","a":"A plaque of fatty material, cells, and debris within an artery wall.","options":["A balloon-like bulge in a weakened artery wall","A clot that forms on the surface of a roughened plaque","A clot or fragment that breaks free and travels in the blood","A plaque of fatty material, cells, and debris in an artery wall"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"An atheroma, also called a plaque, is the deposit that builds up in atherosclerosis. It forms in the artery wall after injury to the endothelium and contains fatty material, cells, and debris. As it grows, it bulges into the lumen and narrows the channel that blood flows through.","notes":["A balloon-like bulge is an aneurysm. Choose it if the question asked about a weakened artery wall at risk of rupture.","A clot on a roughened plaque is a thrombus. Pick it if the question asked what can suddenly block an artery that already has a plaque.","A clot or fragment that travels is an embolus. It would be the answer if the question asked what lodges in a lung artery in a pulmonary embolism.","Correct. An atheroma is the fatty plaque within an artery wall."],"lt":"t-vessel-disorders","lid":"W3-505","comp":70,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-disorders"},{"id":"L03753","dok":1,"q":"What happens to the lumen of an artery as an atheroma grows?","a":"It narrows as the plaque bulges inward.","options":["It widens as the plaque pushes the wall outward","It narrows as the plaque bulges inward","It fills with fluid that leaks from the plaque","It stays the same because the plaque grows outward"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"A plaque forms in the tunica intima, the innermost layer of the artery wall. As it enlarges, it bulges into the lumen, the hollow channel where blood flows. The narrower channel lets less blood through to the tissues downstream.","notes":["A widened vessel with an outward bulge describes an aneurysm. Pick it if the question asked about a weakened artery wall that balloons out.","Correct. A growing plaque bulges inward and narrows the lumen.","The lumen narrows because of the solid plaque, not because of fluid leaking out of it. Leaking fluid is not part of how a plaque narrows an artery.","Because the plaque starts in the innermost layer, it pushes into the channel rather than away from it. A plaque that left the lumen unchanged would not reduce blood flow."],"lt":"t-vessel-disorders","lid":"W3-506","comp":70,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-disorders"},{"id":"L03754","dok":1,"q":"What is arteriosclerosis?","a":"General stiffening and thickening of artery walls.","options":["General stiffening and thickening of artery walls","A blood clot in a deep vein of the leg","A balloon-like bulge in a weakened artery wall","Inflammation of a vein, causing redness and pain"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Sclerosis means hardening, so arteriosclerosis literally means hardening of the arteries. It is a general term for artery walls that become thick and stiff and lose their ability to stretch and recoil. Atherosclerosis, caused by fatty plaques, is its most common form.","notes":["Correct. Arteriosclerosis is the general stiffening and thickening of artery walls.","A clot in a deep leg vein is a deep vein thrombosis. Choose it if the question asked about a clot that can travel to the lungs.","A balloon-like bulge is an aneurysm. Pick it if the question asked which disorder can rupture in the aorta or brain.","Inflammation of a vein is phlebitis. It would be the answer if the question asked what causes pain, redness, and tenderness along a vein."],"lt":"t-vessel-disorders","lid":"W3-507","comp":70,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-disorders"},{"id":"L03755","dok":1,"q":"How is atherosclerosis related to arteriosclerosis?","a":"Atherosclerosis is the most common form of arteriosclerosis.","options":["Atherosclerosis is the most common form of arteriosclerosis","They are unrelated, since one affects arteries and the other veins","Arteriosclerosis is the most common form of atherosclerosis","Atherosclerosis is the healed stage that follows arteriosclerosis"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Arteriosclerosis is the broad term for stiff, thickened artery walls. Atherosclerosis is one specific kind, in which the stiffening comes from fatty plaques (atheromas) building up in the wall. Because it is by far the most frequent cause, atherosclerosis is the most common form of arteriosclerosis.","notes":["Correct. Atherosclerosis is one type, and the most common type, of arteriosclerosis.","Both terms describe arteries, as the word root arterio tells you. Neither one is a vein disorder.","This flips the relationship. Arteriosclerosis is the broad category, and atherosclerosis is the specific plaque-driven type within it.","Atherosclerosis is not a healed stage; it is an ongoing disease that worsens as plaque grows. This misconception treats the two names as steps in time instead of a category and a type."],"lt":"t-vessel-disorders","lid":"W3-508","comp":70,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-disorders"},{"id":"L03756","dok":1,"q":"Where are aneurysms most common?","a":"In the aorta and the arteries of the brain.","options":["The superficial veins of the legs","The aorta and the arteries of the brain","The deep veins of the legs","The veins of the rectal and anal region"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"An aneurysm is a balloon-like bulge in a weakened artery wall. They are most common in the aorta, the largest artery carrying blood under high pressure, and in the arteries of the brain. Both locations are dangerous because a rupture can cause severe bleeding.","notes":["The superficial leg veins are the usual site of varicose veins. They would be the answer if the question asked where twisted, swollen veins appear.","Correct. Aneurysms most often form in the aorta and brain arteries.","The deep leg veins are the usual site of deep vein thrombosis, a clot rather than a bulging wall. Pick them if the question asked where DVT forms.","The rectal and anal veins are the site of hemorrhoids. Choose them if the question asked which varicose veins are found in the anal region."],"lt":"t-vessel-disorders","lid":"W3-511","comp":70,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-disorders"},{"id":"L03757","dok":1,"q":"Why does an aneurysm matter clinically?","a":"It can rupture.","options":["It lets blood pool behind failed valves","It forms clots that travel to the lungs","It can rupture and bleed","It narrows the lumen with fatty plaque"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"An aneurysm is a weakened section of artery wall that has ballooned outward. Arteries carry blood under high pressure, and a thin, stretched wall may not hold it. If the aneurysm ruptures, blood escapes rapidly from the artery, which can be life-threatening, especially in the aorta or the brain.","notes":["Pooling behind failed valves describes varicose veins, a venous disorder. It would be the answer if the question asked about twisted, swollen leg veins.","Clots that travel to the lungs come from deep vein thrombosis. Choose that if the question asked what causes a pulmonary embolism.","Correct. The main danger of an aneurysm is rupture.","Narrowing by fatty plaque is atherosclerosis. Pick it if the question asked what causes coronary artery disease or peripheral artery disease."],"lt":"t-vessel-disorders","lid":"W3-512","comp":70,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-disorders"},{"id":"L03758","dok":1,"q":"What is coronary artery disease?","a":"Narrowing of the coronary arteries that supply the heart wall.","options":["Narrowing of the arteries that supply the heart wall","Loss of blood supply to part of the brain","Narrowing of the arteries of the limbs","Chronically high pressure within the arteries"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The coronary arteries branch from the base of the aorta and supply the myocardium, the muscle of the heart wall. In coronary artery disease, usually caused by atherosclerosis, these arteries become narrowed. The heart muscle then receives less blood than it needs, especially when it is working hard.","notes":["Correct. Coronary artery disease is narrowing of the arteries that feed the heart wall.","Loss of blood supply to the brain is a stroke. It would be the answer if the question asked about a blocked or burst brain artery.","Narrowing of limb arteries is peripheral artery disease. Pick it if the question asked about leg pain on walking.","Chronically high arterial pressure is hypertension. Choose it if the question asked which condition strains the heart and damages vessel walls over time."],"lt":"t-vessel-disorders","lid":"W3-513","comp":70,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-disorders"},{"id":"L03759","dok":1,"q":"What can coronary artery disease cause?","a":"Myocardial ischemia and a heart attack.","options":["Lower-leg swelling and skin changes","Leg pain that comes on with walking","Myocardial ischemia and a heart attack","A stroke from a blocked brain artery"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Ischemia means a tissue is not getting enough blood. When narrowed coronary arteries cannot supply enough blood to the myocardium, the heart muscle becomes ischemic. If a coronary artery becomes blocked completely, the heart muscle it supplies begins to die, which is a heart attack (myocardial infarction).","notes":["Lower-leg swelling and skin changes come from chronic venous insufficiency, a vein problem. Choose it if the question asked about long-term failure of leg vein valves.","Leg pain on walking is the classic symptom of peripheral artery disease. It would be the answer if the plaque were in a leg artery.","Correct. Narrowed coronary arteries can starve the myocardium and cause a heart attack.","A stroke comes from a blocked or burst artery in the brain, not the coronary arteries. Pick it if the question asked what happens when a brain artery loses flow."],"lt":"t-vessel-disorders","lid":"W3-514","comp":70,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-disorders"},{"id":"L03760","dok":1,"q":"What is peripheral artery disease?","a":"Narrowing of the arteries of the limbs, usually the legs.","options":["Narrowing of the arteries that supply the heart wall","A blood clot in a deep vein of the leg","Long-term failure of the valves of the leg veins","Narrowing of the arteries of the limbs, usually the legs"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Peripheral means away from the center of the body. Peripheral artery disease is narrowing of the arteries that supply the limbs, most often the legs, usually because of atherosclerosis. The narrowed arteries cannot deliver enough blood to the muscles, especially during activity.","notes":["Narrowing of the arteries to the heart wall is coronary artery disease. Choose it if the question asked what can lead to a heart attack.","A clot in a deep leg vein is deep vein thrombosis. It would be the answer if the question asked about a clot that could travel to the lungs.","Long-term valve failure in leg veins is chronic venous insufficiency. Pick it if the question asked what causes persistent lower-leg swelling and skin changes.","Correct. Peripheral artery disease is narrowing of limb arteries, most often in the legs."],"lt":"t-vessel-disorders","lid":"W3-515","comp":70,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-disorders"},{"id":"L03761","dok":1,"q":"Which symptom is typical of peripheral artery disease?","a":"Pain on walking due to reduced blood supply to the leg muscles.","options":["Redness and tenderness along a vein","Twisted, bulging veins under the skin","Sudden weakness on one side of the body","Leg pain that comes on with walking"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"In peripheral artery disease, the arteries of the legs are narrowed. At rest, enough blood may still get through, but walking increases the muscles' need for oxygen. The narrowed arteries cannot keep up, so the muscles are starved of blood and become painful until the person rests.","notes":["Redness and tenderness along a vein are signs of phlebitis, inflammation of a vein. Choose it if the question asked about an inflamed vein.","Twisted, bulging veins are varicose veins, caused by failed venous valves. Pick them if the question asked about pooling in superficial leg veins.","Sudden weakness on one side of the body is a warning sign of stroke. It would be the answer if the question asked about a blocked or burst brain artery.","Correct. Pain with walking is the classic sign of peripheral artery disease."],"lt":"t-vessel-disorders","lid":"W3-516","comp":70,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-disorders"},{"id":"L03762","dok":1,"q":"What is hypertension?","a":"Chronically high pressure within the arteries.","options":["Chronically high pressure within the arteries","A balloon-like bulge in an artery wall","Narrowing of the coronary arteries","Stiffening and thickening of artery walls"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Hyper means above normal and tension refers to pressure, so hypertension is high blood pressure. It means the pressure inside the arteries stays above normal over a long period, not just briefly during exercise or stress. This constant extra force strains the heart and wears on the vessel walls.","notes":["Correct. Hypertension is chronically high arterial pressure.","A balloon-like bulge is an aneurysm. Pick it if the question asked which disorder is common in the aorta and brain arteries and can rupture.","Narrowed coronary arteries describe coronary artery disease. It would be the answer if the question asked what reduces blood supply to the heart wall.","Stiff, thick artery walls describe arteriosclerosis. Choose it if the question asked about general hardening of the arteries."],"lt":"t-vessel-disorders","lid":"W3-517","comp":70,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-disorders"},{"id":"L03763","dok":1,"q":"Why does hypertension matter for health?","a":"It strains the heart and damages vessel walls over time.","options":["It causes brain tissue to die within minutes","It forms clots that travel straight to the lungs","It makes blood pool behind failed valves in the legs","It strains the heart and damages vessel walls over time"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"When arterial pressure stays high, the heart must work harder with every beat to push blood out. At the same time, the constant extra force injures the walls of the arteries, damaging the endothelium where plaques can then begin. Over years, this raises the risk of heart disease, stroke, and other vessel problems.","notes":["Brain tissue dying within minutes describes a stroke, a sudden event. Choose it if the question asked why a stroke is an emergency.","Clots traveling to the lungs come from deep vein thrombosis and cause a pulmonary embolism. That would be the answer if the question asked why a DVT is dangerous.","Pooling behind failed valves describes varicose veins and chronic venous insufficiency, which are low-pressure vein problems. Pick that if the question asked about swollen leg veins.","Correct. Long-term high pressure strains the heart and harms vessel walls."],"lt":"t-vessel-disorders","lid":"W3-518","comp":70,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-disorders"},{"id":"L03764","dok":1,"q":"What is a stroke?","a":"Loss of blood supply to part of the brain from a blocked or burst artery.","options":["Loss of blood supply to part of the brain from a blocked or burst artery","A clot that lodges in an artery of the lung","A bulge in the wall of a brain artery that has not ruptured","Loss of blood supply to the heart wall from a narrowed coronary artery"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"A stroke happens when part of the brain stops receiving blood. This can occur because an artery is blocked, often by a plaque or a clot, or because an artery bursts and bleeds. Either way, the brain tissue supplied by that artery is cut off from oxygen.","notes":["Correct. A stroke is loss of blood supply to part of the brain from a blocked or burst artery.","A clot in a lung artery is a pulmonary embolism. It would be the answer if the question asked where a traveling DVT clot lodges.","An unruptured bulge in a brain artery is an aneurysm. It can cause a stroke if it bursts, but the bulge alone has not cut off blood supply. Choose it if the question asked about a weakened artery wall.","Loss of blood to the heart wall is a heart attack, from coronary artery disease. Pick it if the question asked about the myocardium."],"lt":"t-vessel-disorders","lid":"W3-519","comp":70,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-disorders"},{"id":"L03765","dok":1,"q":"Why is a stroke treated as an emergency?","a":"Brain tissue begins to die within minutes without blood flow.","options":["Brain tissue begins to die within minutes without blood","The brain can store a full day's oxygen supply","Leg muscles lose their blood supply at the same time","The clot always travels on to the lungs next"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Neurons use a lot of oxygen and have almost no reserve. When blood flow to part of the brain stops, those neurons begin to die within minutes. The faster blood flow is restored, the more brain tissue can be saved.","notes":["Correct. Brain tissue dies within minutes without blood flow.","The brain stores very little oxygen, which is exactly why a stroke is so urgent. This misconception reverses the reason for the emergency.","A stroke affects the brain's blood supply, not the legs. Loss of blood to the leg muscles describes peripheral artery disease.","A clot that blocks a brain artery stays in the brain; it does not move on to the lungs. Clots reach the lungs when they come from the deep veins, as in a pulmonary embolism."],"lt":"t-vessel-disorders","lid":"W3-520","comp":70,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-disorders"},{"id":"L03766","dok":1,"q":"Where do varicose veins most often occur?","a":"In the superficial veins of the legs.","options":["Superficial veins of the legs","Coronary arteries of the heart","Arteries at the base of the brain","Pulmonary arteries of the lungs"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Varicose veins are superficial veins that have become twisted and swollen because their valves have failed. They are most common in the legs, where veins carry low-pressure blood upward against gravity. When the valves fail, blood pools and stretches the veins until they bulge visibly under the skin.","notes":["Correct. Varicose veins are most common in the superficial veins of the legs.","The coronary arteries are the site of coronary artery disease. They would be the answer if the question asked which vessels narrow in a heart attack.","The arteries of the brain are a common site of aneurysms. Pick them if the question asked where a weakened, bulging artery might rupture.","The pulmonary arteries are where a pulmonary embolism lodges. Choose them if the question asked where a clot from a deep vein ends up."],"lt":"t-vessel-disorders","lid":"W3-522","comp":70,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-disorders"},{"id":"L03767","dok":1,"q":"What is deep vein thrombosis?","a":"A blood clot in a deep vein, usually of the leg.","options":["A clot lodged in an artery of the lung","A blood clot in a deep vein, usually of the leg","Twisted, swollen veins just under the skin","Inflammation of a vein, with redness and pain"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"A thrombus is a clot that forms inside a vessel. Deep vein thrombosis, or DVT, is a thrombus in one of the deep veins, most often in the leg, where blood flows slowly under low pressure. The clot can break free and travel through the venous return to the lungs.","notes":["A clot lodged in a lung artery is a pulmonary embolism, which can result when a DVT breaks loose. Pick it if the question asked where a traveling leg clot ends up.","Correct. DVT is a clot in a deep vein, usually of the leg.","Twisted, swollen superficial veins are varicose veins. Choose them if the question asked about failed valves in surface leg veins.","Inflammation of a vein is phlebitis. It would be the answer if the question asked what causes pain, redness, and tenderness along a vein."],"lt":"t-vessel-disorders","lid":"W3-523","comp":70,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-disorders"},{"id":"L03768","dok":1,"q":"What is a pulmonary embolism?","a":"A clot, often from a deep vein, that lodges in an artery of the lung.","options":["A plaque that narrows the pulmonary veins","A ruptured bulge in the wall of the aorta","A clot, often from a deep vein, that lodges in a lung artery","A clot that forms and stays in a deep vein of the leg"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"An embolus is a clot or fragment that breaks free and travels in the bloodstream. A pulmonary embolism is an embolus that lodges in an artery of the lung, most often a clot that started as a deep vein thrombosis in the leg. It blocks blood flow to part of the lung and is a life-threatening emergency.","notes":["Plaque in vessels is atherosclerosis, which affects arteries, and a pulmonary embolism is a clot rather than a plaque. Plaque narrowing would fit a question about coronary artery disease.","A ruptured bulge in the aorta is a ruptured aortic aneurysm. Choose it if the question asked about massive bleeding from the body's largest artery.","Correct. A pulmonary embolism is a traveling clot stuck in a lung artery.","A clot that stays in the leg vein is a deep vein thrombosis. Pick it if the question asked about a clot that has not moved yet."],"lt":"t-vessel-disorders","lid":"W3-524","comp":70,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-disorders"},{"id":"L03769","dok":1,"q":"Why is a pulmonary embolism dangerous?","a":"It blocks blood flow to the lung and can be life-threatening.","options":["It makes blood pool in the leg veins","It weakens the aortic wall until it ruptures","It blocks blood flow to part of the lung","It blocks blood flow out of the brain"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"All the blood returning from the body must pass through the pulmonary arteries to reach the lungs. A clot lodged in one of these arteries blocks that flow, so part of the lung receives no blood and the blood cannot pick up oxygen there. A large embolism can be fatal, which is why it is treated as an emergency.","notes":["Pooling in leg veins describes varicose veins or chronic venous insufficiency. Pick that if the question asked about failed valves in the legs.","Weakening of the aortic wall describes an aortic aneurysm. It would be the answer if the question asked about a bulge in the aorta that could rupture.","Correct. A pulmonary embolism blocks blood flow into the lung.","A clot in a pulmonary artery affects the lungs, not the brain. Loss of blood supply to brain tissue describes a stroke."],"lt":"t-vessel-disorders","lid":"W3-525","comp":70,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-disorders"},{"id":"L03770","dok":1,"q":"Which symptoms are typical of phlebitis?","a":"Pain, redness, and tenderness along the vein.","options":["Pain, redness, and tenderness along a vein","Sudden chest pain and shortness of breath","Leg pain that comes on only with walking","Sudden weakness on one side of the body"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Phleb refers to a vein and itis means inflammation, so phlebitis is inflammation of a vein. Like inflammation anywhere, it produces pain, redness, and tenderness, and these follow the course of the affected vein under the skin.","notes":["Correct. Phlebitis causes pain, redness, and tenderness along the vein.","Sudden chest pain and shortness of breath are warning signs of a pulmonary embolism. Choose them if the question asked what happens when a clot lodges in a lung artery.","Pain with walking is typical of peripheral artery disease, where narrowed leg arteries starve the muscles. Pick it if the question asked about narrowed limb arteries.","Sudden one-sided weakness is a warning sign of stroke. It would be the answer if the question asked about loss of blood supply to the brain."],"lt":"t-vessel-disorders","lid":"W3-527","comp":70,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-disorders"},{"id":"L03771","dok":1,"q":"What is chronic venous insufficiency?","a":"Long-term failure of the valves of the leg veins.","options":["A single clot in a deep vein of the leg","Long-term valve failure in the leg veins","Inflammation of a vein near the skin","Narrowing of the arteries of the legs"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Chronic means long-lasting, and venous insufficiency means the veins are not returning blood well. In chronic venous insufficiency, the valves of the leg veins have failed over a long period. Blood is not moved effectively back toward the heart, so it collects in the lower legs.","notes":["A single clot in a deep leg vein is deep vein thrombosis. Pick it if the question asked about a clot that could travel to the lungs.","Correct. Chronic venous insufficiency is long-term valve failure in the leg veins.","Inflammation of a vein is phlebitis. Choose it if the question asked what causes redness and tenderness along a vein.","Narrowed leg arteries describe peripheral artery disease. It would be the answer if the question asked about pain on walking."],"lt":"t-vessel-disorders","lid":"W3-529","comp":70,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-disorders"},{"id":"L03772","dok":1,"q":"What can chronic venous insufficiency cause?","a":"Persistent lower-leg swelling and skin changes.","options":["Loss of blood supply to part of the brain","Leg pain that comes on with walking","Persistent lower-leg swelling and skin changes","Sudden chest pain and shortness of breath"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"When the valves of the leg veins fail over a long time, blood pools in the lower legs and pressure in those veins stays high. Fluid is pushed out into the surrounding tissue, causing persistent swelling. Over months and years, the skin of the lower leg also changes in appearance from this constant congestion.","notes":["Loss of blood to part of the brain is a stroke. Choose it if the question asked about a blocked or burst brain artery.","Pain on walking comes from peripheral artery disease, an artery problem. Pick it if the question asked about narrowed leg arteries.","Correct. Long-term valve failure causes lasting lower-leg swelling and skin changes.","Sudden chest pain and shortness of breath suggest a pulmonary embolism. It would be the answer if the question asked about a clot lodged in a lung artery."],"lt":"t-vessel-disorders","lid":"W3-530","comp":70,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-disorders"},{"id":"L03773","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares arteriosclerosis and atherosclerosis?","a":"Arteriosclerosis is general artery stiffening; atherosclerosis is the plaque-forming type and its most common form.","options":["Arteriosclerosis affects veins, while atherosclerosis affects arteries","Atherosclerosis is general artery stiffening, and arteriosclerosis is its plaque-forming type","Arteriosclerosis is general artery stiffening, and atherosclerosis is its plaque-forming type","Both terms describe a weakened artery wall that bulges outward"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Arteriosclerosis is the broad term for thick, stiff artery walls. Atherosclerosis is a specific type in which the stiffening and narrowing come from fatty plaques, called atheromas, building up in the wall. The athero part of the name, which refers to the fatty plaque, is the clue to which term is the specific one.","notes":["Both terms describe arteries, as the arterio root shows. Vein disorders include varicose veins and deep vein thrombosis.","This swaps the two terms. The word athero points to fatty plaque, so atherosclerosis is the specific, plaque-forming type.","Correct. Arteriosclerosis is the general category, and atherosclerosis is the plaque-forming type.","A weakened wall that bulges outward is an aneurysm. Pick it if the question asked about an artery at risk of rupture."],"lt":"t-vessel-disorders","lid":"W3-551","comp":70,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-disorders"},{"id":"L03774","dok":2,"q":"How does injury to the endothelium contribute to plaque formation?","a":"Damage to the smooth intima creates the starting point for plaque buildup.","options":["It relaxes the tunica media so the vessel widens","It weakens the tunica externa so the wall bulges outward","It roughens the intima and gives plaque a place to start","It breaks the venous valves so blood pools in the legs"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The endothelium is the smooth inner lining of the tunica intima. When it is injured, by high blood pressure for example, that spot is no longer smooth and protected. Fatty material, cells, and debris begin to collect in the wall there, and the deposit grows into an atheroma, or plaque.","notes":["Relaxing the smooth muscle of the media is vasodilation, a normal way to increase flow. It would be the answer if the question asked how a vessel widens.","A wall that bulges outward describes an aneurysm, which involves weakening of the wall rather than plaque. Pick that if the question asked how an aneurysm forms.","Correct. Endothelial injury is where a plaque begins.","Broken venous valves cause varicose veins and chronic venous insufficiency, not arterial plaque. Choose that if the question asked about pooling in leg veins."],"lt":"t-vessel-disorders","lid":"W3-552","comp":70,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-disorders"},{"id":"L03775","dok":2,"q":"Why does a growing plaque reduce blood flow through an artery?","a":"It bulges inward and narrows the lumen.","options":["It bulges into the lumen and narrows the channel","It thins the wall so blood leaks out of the artery","It closes the artery's valves against forward flow","It widens the lumen so blood slows and pools"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"A plaque grows in the innermost layer of the artery wall, so as it enlarges it pushes into the lumen. A narrower channel lets less blood pass at a time, so the tissue downstream receives less blood. The more the plaque grows, the greater the reduction in flow.","notes":["Correct. The plaque narrows the lumen, so less blood gets through.","Plaque thickens and stiffens the wall rather than thinning it, and blood does not leak out through it. A thin, weakened wall that can burst describes an aneurysm.","Arteries do not have valves along their length. Valves are found in veins, the answer if the question asked what prevents backflow in the legs.","A plaque narrows the lumen rather than widening it. Widened, pooling vessels describe varicose veins."],"lt":"t-vessel-disorders","lid":"W3-553","comp":70,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-disorders"},{"id":"L03776","dok":2,"q":"Why can a roughened plaque suddenly make blood flow much worse?","a":"A thrombus can form on it and block the vessel.","options":["It can turn into an aneurysm and widen the lumen","It can cause nearby valves to fail and allow backflow","It can dissolve quickly and release fat into the lumen","A thrombus can form on it and suddenly block the vessel"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"A plaque that has a rough or broken surface no longer has smooth endothelium covering it. Platelets and clotting factors collect on the rough spot and form a thrombus, a clot attached to the vessel wall. Because the lumen is already narrowed by the plaque, a clot on top of it can block the vessel suddenly, triggering a heart attack or stroke.","notes":["An aneurysm is a bulging, weakened wall and would widen the lumen rather than block it. Pick it if the question asked about a wall at risk of rupture.","Arteries do not have valves along their length, so valve failure is not part of this process. Failed valves describe varicose veins.","Plaques do not simply dissolve and release fat; they tend to grow over time. A sudden worsening comes from a clot forming on the plaque.","Correct. A clot on a roughened plaque can suddenly close an already narrowed artery."],"lt":"t-vessel-disorders","lid":"W3-554","comp":70,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-disorders"},{"id":"L03777","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares an aneurysm and atherosclerosis?","a":"An aneurysm is a weakened wall that bulges outward; atherosclerosis is plaque that narrows the lumen.","options":["An aneurysm is a weak wall that bulges outward, while atherosclerosis is plaque that narrows the lumen","Both are vein disorders caused by failed valves in the legs","An aneurysm is plaque that narrows the lumen, while atherosclerosis is a weak wall that bulges outward","An aneurysm narrows the lumen, while atherosclerosis widens it"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Both are artery disorders, but they change the wall in opposite directions. In an aneurysm, a weakened section of wall balloons outward, and the danger is rupture. In atherosclerosis, plaque builds up inside the wall and bulges inward, and the danger is reduced or blocked blood flow.","notes":["Correct. An aneurysm bulges outward, while atherosclerosis narrows the lumen.","Both are artery disorders, not vein disorders. Failed valves in the legs cause varicose veins and chronic venous insufficiency.","This swaps the two disorders. The plaque belongs to atherosclerosis, and the outward bulge belongs to an aneurysm.","This reverses the effect on the lumen. An aneurysm enlarges the vessel, and atherosclerosis narrows it."],"lt":"t-vessel-disorders","lid":"W3-555","comp":70,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-disorders"},{"id":"L03778","dok":2,"q":"Why do arterial disorders usually involve narrowing of the channel or weakening of the wall?","a":"Arteries carry blood under high pressure, which strains the wall.","options":["Arteries depend on valves that can fail and let blood pool","Arteries carry blood at low pressure, so it tends to clot","Arteries have walls only one cell thick that tear easily","Arteries carry blood under high pressure, which strains the wall"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Arteries receive blood directly from the heart under high pressure. Over time that pressure can injure the lining, which starts plaque formation and narrowing, or it can weaken a section of wall until it bulges into an aneurysm. So the problems typical of arteries come from the force they must handle.","notes":["Arteries do not have valves along their length. Valve failure and pooling describe vein disorders, the answer if the question asked about varicose veins.","Low pressure and slow flow describe veins, where clots such as DVT form more easily. Pick that idea if the question asked why venous disorders involve pooling or clotting.","Walls one cell thick belong to capillaries. Arteries have the thickest walls of any vessel, built to resist high pressure.","Correct. High pressure explains why arterial disorders narrow or weaken the wall."],"lt":"t-vessel-disorders","lid":"W3-556","comp":70,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-disorders"},{"id":"L03779","dok":2,"q":"Why do venous disorders usually involve pooled or clotted blood?","a":"Veins are low-pressure vessels that depend on valves and the skeletal muscle pump.","options":["Veins carry low-pressure blood and depend on valves","Veins have thick, stiff walls that cannot stretch","Veins carry blood under the highest pressure in the body","Veins lack a tunica intima to keep blood moving"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"By the time blood reaches the veins, its pressure is low, so it depends on valves and the squeezing of skeletal muscles to keep moving toward the heart. When valves fail or muscles are inactive for long periods, blood slows and collects. Slow, pooled blood stretches the veins, as in varicose veins, and is more likely to clot, as in deep vein thrombosis.","notes":["Correct. Low pressure and dependence on valves make veins prone to pooling and clotting.","Veins have thin walls that stretch easily, which is why pooled blood can make them bulge. Thick, stiff walls describe arteriosclerosis in arteries.","The highest pressure is in the arteries near the heart, not the veins. High pressure explains arterial disorders such as aneurysms.","Veins do have a tunica intima, and their valves are made from it. The lining itself does not push blood forward."],"lt":"t-vessel-disorders","lid":"W3-557","comp":70,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-disorders"},{"id":"L03780","dok":2,"q":"How does valve failure lead to varicose veins?","a":"Failed valves allow backward flow, so blood pools and the veins stretch and bulge.","options":["Failed valves let blood fall back, pool, and stretch the vein","Failed valves raise the pressure in the arteries of the leg","Failed valves let fatty plaque build up inside the vein wall","Failed valves let blood escape from the vein into nearby arteries"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Healthy valves in the leg veins let blood move only toward the heart. When they fail, gravity pulls blood back down, and it collects in the superficial veins. The extra blood stretches the thin vein walls until the veins become twisted, swollen, and visible under the skin.","notes":["Correct. Backflow through failed valves makes blood pool and the veins bulge.","Venous valve failure affects the veins, not arterial pressure. Chronically high arterial pressure is hypertension.","Fatty plaque buildup is atherosclerosis, which affects arteries rather than veins. Pick it if the question asked what narrows the coronary or leg arteries.","Veins and arteries are not directly connected by valves, and blood does not escape into arteries. A direct artery-to-vein connection is an arteriovenous anastomosis."],"lt":"t-vessel-disorders","lid":"W3-558","comp":70,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-disorders"},{"id":"L03781","dok":2,"q":"A clot breaks free from a deep vein in the leg. Which path does it follow to reach the lung?","a":"Deep leg vein, inferior vena cava, right atrium, right ventricle, pulmonary trunk, pulmonary artery.","options":["Leg vein, femoral artery, aorta, left ventricle, pulmonary trunk","Leg vein, inferior vena cava, right atrium, right ventricle, pulmonary trunk","Leg vein, inferior vena cava, right atrium, left atrium, pulmonary vein","Leg vein, inferior vena cava, left atrium, left ventricle, aorta"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"A clot that breaks loose from a deep leg vein simply follows normal venous return. It travels up the inferior vena cava into the right atrium, passes into the right ventricle, and is pumped into the pulmonary trunk and then a pulmonary artery. The pulmonary arteries branch into smaller and smaller vessels, so the clot eventually lodges in one, causing a pulmonary embolism.","notes":["A clot in a vein cannot jump into an artery; veins return blood to the heart. The femoral artery carries blood down the leg, away from the heart.","Correct. The clot follows venous return through the right heart to the pulmonary arteries.","Blood does not normally pass from the right atrium to the left atrium after birth, and the pulmonary veins carry blood from the lungs to the heart. Right-to-left atrial flow is the fetal route through the foramen ovale.","Blood from the body enters the right side of the heart, not the left. This route skips the lungs entirely, which is why it does not explain a pulmonary embolism."],"lt":"t-vessel-disorders","lid":"W3-559","comp":69,"competencyId":"bvn-circulatory-routes"},{"id":"L03782","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares deep vein thrombosis and pulmonary embolism?","a":"A DVT is a clot in a deep vein; a pulmonary embolism is a clot lodged in a lung artery.","options":["A DVT is a clot in a leg artery, while a pulmonary embolism is a clot in a lung vein","Both are clots that form and stay in the right atrium","A DVT is a clot in a deep vein, while a pulmonary embolism is a clot lodged in a lung artery","A DVT is a clot lodged in a lung artery, while a pulmonary embolism is a clot in a deep vein"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"A deep vein thrombosis is a clot that forms in a deep vein, usually in the leg. If part of it breaks free, it becomes an embolus and travels through the right heart into the pulmonary arteries. When it lodges there, the condition is a pulmonary embolism. The two are often stages of the same event.","notes":["DVT is in a vein, not an artery, and a pulmonary embolism lodges in a pulmonary artery, not a vein. Pick a leg artery if the question asked about peripheral artery disease.","Neither condition is defined as a clot staying in the heart. The right atrium is only a stop along the route from the leg veins to the lungs.","Correct. DVT is where the clot forms, and pulmonary embolism is where it can end up.","This swaps the two. The word pulmonary refers to the lung, so the pulmonary embolism is the one in the lung."],"lt":"t-vessel-disorders","lid":"W3-560","comp":70,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-disorders"},{"id":"L03783","dok":2,"q":"How can the same atherosclerotic process cause coronary artery disease, stroke, or peripheral artery disease?","a":"The location of the plaque determines which disease it causes.","options":["The disease depends only on how fast the heart is beating","The location of the plaque determines which disease results","Each disease comes from a different kind of plaque material","Plaque in veins causes stroke, and plaque in arteries causes heart attack"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Atherosclerosis is the same wall change wherever it happens: plaque builds up and narrows an artery. What differs is which tissue loses blood. Plaque in a coronary artery starves the heart muscle and can cause a heart attack, plaque in a brain artery can cause a stroke, and plaque in a leg artery causes peripheral artery disease.","notes":["Heart rate does not decide which disease a plaque causes. The disease is named for the tissue that loses its blood supply.","Correct. The same plaque produces different diseases depending on which artery it affects.","The plaque material is basically the same in each case: fatty material, cells, and debris. The difference is the artery involved, not the plaque type.","Atherosclerosis affects arteries, not veins. A stroke happens when a brain artery is blocked or bursts."],"lt":"t-vessel-disorders","lid":"W3-561","comp":70,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-disorders"},{"id":"L03784","dok":2,"q":"How does coronary artery disease lead to a myocardial infarction?","a":"Narrowed coronary arteries starve the myocardium of oxygen.","options":["Narrowed coronary arteries starve the myocardium of oxygen","Narrowed coronary veins stop blood returning to the right atrium","Narrowed pulmonary arteries stop the heart from receiving blood","Narrowed coronary arteries block blood leaving the ventricles"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"A myocardial infarction, or heart attack, is the death of a region of heart muscle. The myocardium gets its own blood from the coronary arteries, not from the blood passing through the chambers. When these arteries are narrowed or blocked, the muscle they supply is starved of oxygen, and if the blockage lasts, that muscle dies.","notes":["Correct. Without enough coronary blood flow, heart muscle is starved of oxygen and can die.","Coronary artery disease involves arteries, not cardiac veins. The cardiac veins drain into the coronary sinus, the answer if the question asked how blood leaves the heart wall.","The pulmonary arteries carry blood to the lungs. Blocking them describes a pulmonary embolism, not a heart attack.","The coronary arteries feed the heart wall; they do not control blood leaving the ventricles. Blood leaves through the aortic and pulmonary valves."],"lt":"t-vessel-disorders","lid":"W3-562","comp":70,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-disorders"},{"id":"L03785","dok":2,"q":"Why does peripheral artery disease cause pain during walking but often not at rest?","a":"Exercising leg muscles need more blood than the narrowed arteries can deliver.","options":["Walking makes blood pool in the leg veins and stretch them","Working muscles need more blood than narrowed arteries supply","Walking inflames the leg veins and presses on the muscles","Walking loosens clots that travel from the leg to the lungs"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"At rest, leg muscles need only a modest blood supply, and a narrowed artery may still deliver enough. During walking, the muscles work harder and need much more oxygen. The narrowed arteries cannot increase flow to match, so the muscles become starved of blood and painful until the person stops and rests.","notes":["Pooling in leg veins describes varicose veins or chronic venous insufficiency. Walking actually helps venous return through the skeletal muscle pump.","Correct. The gap between muscle demand and limited supply causes the pain.","Inflamed veins describe phlebitis. It would be the answer if the question asked about redness and tenderness along a vein.","Clots traveling to the lungs come from deep vein thrombosis, a venous problem. Pick that if the question asked how a pulmonary embolism forms."],"lt":"t-vessel-disorders","lid":"W3-563","comp":70,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-disorders"},{"id":"L03786","dok":2,"q":"How does hypertension damage blood vessels over time?","a":"Constant high pressure strains and injures vessel walls.","options":["Low pressure lets blood pool and clot in the arteries","High pressure makes venous valves close for good","High pressure widens every vessel and keeps walls healthy","Constant high pressure strains and injures the vessel walls"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"In hypertension, blood presses harder than normal against the artery walls with every heartbeat, year after year. That steady extra force injures the endothelium and strains the wall. An injured lining is where plaques can start, so hypertension also raises the risk of atherosclerosis, heart disease, and stroke.","notes":["Hypertension is high pressure, not low. Low-pressure pooling and clotting describe veins, as in deep vein thrombosis.","Hypertension affects the arteries and does not close venous valves. Failed or damaged venous valves describe varicose veins.","High pressure harms vessel walls rather than keeping them healthy. This misconception treats more pressure as better flow.","Correct. Long-term high pressure strains and injures the walls."],"lt":"t-vessel-disorders","lid":"W3-564","comp":70,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-disorders"},{"id":"L03787","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares a blocked-artery stroke and a burst-artery stroke?","a":"Both cut off blood to part of the brain; one blocks flow, and the other ruptures and bleeds.","options":["Both are caused by clots that travel up from the leg veins","A blocked-artery stroke affects the heart, while a burst-artery stroke affects the brain","A blocked-artery stroke affects veins, while a burst-artery stroke affects arteries","Both cut off brain blood supply, one by a blockage and the other by a rupture that bleeds"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Every stroke means part of the brain has lost its blood supply. In one type, an artery is blocked, often by a plaque or a clot, and blood cannot get through. In the other, a weakened artery, such as one with an aneurysm, bursts and bleeds, so the tissue beyond it loses its supply and blood leaks into or around the brain.","notes":["A burst artery bleeds; it is not caused by a clot. Clots from the leg veins normally lodge in the lungs as a pulmonary embolism.","Both kinds of stroke affect the brain. Loss of blood to the heart muscle is a heart attack.","Both kinds of stroke involve arteries supplying the brain. Veins are not the vessels at fault in either one.","Correct. Both types cut off brain blood supply, by blockage or by rupture."],"lt":"t-vessel-disorders","lid":"W3-565","comp":70,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-disorders"},{"id":"L03788","dok":2,"q":"A plaque gradually narrows one of a patient's coronary arteries. Which disease process does this describe, and what can it lead to?","a":"Coronary artery disease, which can lead to a heart attack.","options":["Stroke, from loss of blood supply to the brain","Peripheral artery disease, which causes leg pain on walking","Deep vein thrombosis, from a clot in a leg vein","Coronary artery disease, which can lead to a heart attack"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The coronary arteries supply the myocardium, the muscle of the heart wall. When atherosclerotic plaque narrows them, the condition is coronary artery disease. If the narrowing becomes severe or a clot forms on the plaque, the heart muscle downstream can be starved of oxygen and die, which is a myocardial infarction.","notes":["A stroke involves loss of blood to the brain. It would be the answer if the plaque were in a brain artery.","Peripheral artery disease involves the arteries of the limbs, usually the legs. Pick it if the plaque were in a leg artery.","Deep vein thrombosis is a clot in a vein, not plaque in an artery. Choose it if the question described a clot in a deep leg vein.","Correct. A narrowed coronary artery is coronary artery disease, which can lead to a heart attack."],"lt":"t-vessel-disorders","lid":"W3-583","comp":70,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-disorders"},{"id":"L03789","dok":2,"q":"A plaque narrows an artery in a patient's leg. Which disorder does this suggest?","a":"Peripheral artery disease.","options":["Deep vein thrombosis","Chronic venous insufficiency","Peripheral artery disease","Varicose veins"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Peripheral artery disease is narrowing of the arteries of the limbs, usually the legs, and atherosclerotic plaque is the usual cause. The narrowed artery delivers less blood to the leg muscles, which typically causes pain on walking.","notes":["Deep vein thrombosis is a clot in a deep leg vein. It would be the answer if the question described a clot rather than a plaque.","Chronic venous insufficiency is long-term failure of the leg vein valves. Choose it if the question described persistent lower-leg swelling and skin changes.","Correct. Plaque narrowing a leg artery is peripheral artery disease.","Varicose veins come from failed valves in superficial leg veins, not plaque in an artery. Pick them if the question described twisted, bulging surface veins."],"lt":"t-vessel-disorders","lid":"W3-584","comp":70,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-disorders"},{"id":"L03790","dok":2,"q":"A plaque blocks an artery supplying part of a patient's brain, and that tissue loses its blood supply. What event has occurred?","a":"A stroke.","options":["Heart attack","Stroke","Peripheral artery disease","Pulmonary embolism"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"A stroke is the loss of blood supply to part of the brain, caused by a blocked or burst artery. Here the artery is blocked by plaque, so the brain tissue it supplies is starved of oxygen. Because neurons begin to die within minutes, a stroke is a medical emergency.","notes":["A heart attack is the death of heart muscle from a blocked coronary artery. Pick it if the plaque were in a coronary artery.","Correct. Loss of blood supply to brain tissue is a stroke.","Peripheral artery disease is narrowing of the limb arteries. Choose it if the plaque were in a leg artery.","A pulmonary embolism is a clot lodged in a lung artery, usually from a deep vein. It would be the answer if the blockage were in the lungs."],"lt":"t-vessel-disorders","lid":"W3-585","comp":70,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-disorders"},{"id":"L03791","dok":2,"q":"Why can a ruptured aortic aneurysm be catastrophic?","a":"The aorta carries high-pressure blood, so rupture can cause massive bleeding.","options":["The aorta has valves along its length that fail when it bulges","The aorta carries high-pressure blood, so a rupture bleeds massively","An aortic aneurysm sends clots directly into the lungs","The aorta carries low-pressure blood that pools inside the bulge"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The aorta is the largest artery in the body and receives blood directly from the left ventricle under high pressure. When a weakened, bulging section of its wall tears open, blood pours out very quickly. This rapid, massive blood loss can be fatal within a short time.","notes":["The aorta has no valves along its length. The aortic valve sits at the junction of the left ventricle and the aorta, and it would be the answer if the question asked what prevents backflow into the left ventricle.","Correct. High pressure in the aorta means a rupture causes massive bleeding.","Clots that travel to the lungs come from the deep veins and pass through the right heart. Blood leaving the aorta goes to the body, not the lungs.","The aorta carries the highest-pressure blood in the body, not low-pressure blood. Low-pressure pooling describes veins, as in varicose veins."],"lt":"t-vessel-disorders","lid":"W3-586","comp":70,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-disorders"},{"id":"L03792","dok":2,"q":"Why can a ruptured brain aneurysm cause sudden damage to the nervous system?","a":"Rupture bleeds into or around the brain and disrupts its blood supply.","options":["Rupture causes plaque to form in the coronary arteries","Blood escapes into or around the brain and tissue loses supply","Rupture stops the heart's chambers from filling with blood","Rupture blocks venous blood returning from the lower limbs"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"A brain aneurysm is a weakened, bulging section of an artery that supplies the brain. When it ruptures, blood escapes into or around the brain, and the tissue that artery supplied loses its blood. Both the bleeding and the lost supply can damage neurons quickly, which is why the effects are sudden.","notes":["A ruptured brain aneurysm does not create plaque elsewhere. Plaque in the coronary arteries is coronary artery disease.","Correct. Bleeding plus lost blood supply quickly damages brain tissue.","A brain artery does not control filling of the heart. The heart fills with blood returning through the venae cavae and pulmonary veins.","Venous return from the legs travels through the inferior vena cava, far from the brain arteries. Problems with leg venous return describe chronic venous insufficiency."],"lt":"t-vessel-disorders","lid":"W3-587","comp":70,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-disorders"},{"id":"L03793","dok":2,"q":"A patient has a superficial leg vein that is swollen and twisted. Which valve problem explains this?","a":"Failed venous valves allow blood to flow backward and pool.","options":["Failed valves let blood flow backward and pool","Overactive valves block blood from leaving the vein","Valves in the leg arteries have closed too tightly","Valves in the vein have hardened with fatty plaque"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Valves in leg veins normally keep blood moving upward toward the heart. When they fail, blood slips back and collects in the superficial veins. The extra volume stretches the thin walls, and over time the veins become swollen and twisted, which are varicose veins.","notes":["Correct. Valve failure causes backflow and pooling, producing varicose veins.","Valves do not become overactive and trap blood; the problem is valves that no longer close. A blockage in a vein would more likely come from a clot, as in deep vein thrombosis.","Arteries do not have valves along their length. Narrowed leg arteries are peripheral artery disease, the answer if the patient had pain on walking.","Fatty plaque forms in artery walls, not in venous valves. Plaque in an artery describes atherosclerosis."],"lt":"t-vessel-disorders","lid":"W3-588","comp":70,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-disorders"},{"id":"L03794","dok":2,"q":"A clot breaks loose from a deep leg vein. Which side of the heart does it pass through before reaching the lung?","a":"The right side of the heart.","options":["Right side of the heart","Both sides equally","Neither side, since it travels straight to the lung","Left side of the heart"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Blood from the legs returns through the inferior vena cava to the right atrium, then flows into the right ventricle, which pumps it into the pulmonary trunk toward the lungs. A clot riding in that blood follows the same path. This is why a deep vein clot ends up in a lung artery rather than in the brain or heart wall.","notes":["Correct. Venous blood, and any clot in it, passes through the right heart on its way to the lungs.","After birth, the two sides of the heart are separated, so the clot does not pass through both. Normal venous return goes only through the right side before the lungs.","Every vessel carrying blood from the body to the lungs passes through the right side of the heart first. There is no direct path from the vena cava to the lungs.","The left side of the heart receives blood after it has passed through the lungs. A clot from a leg vein normally lodges in the lungs before ever reaching the left side."],"lt":"t-vessel-disorders","lid":"W3-589","comp":69,"competencyId":"bvn-circulatory-routes"},{"id":"L03795","dok":2,"q":"Why does a deep vein thrombosis become dangerous when the clot breaks free?","a":"It can travel to the lungs and lodge in a pulmonary artery as a pulmonary embolism.","options":["It can block a leg artery and cause peripheral artery disease","It can settle in a superficial vein and cause varicose veins","It can enter the hepatic portal vein and block the liver","It can lodge in a pulmonary artery as a pulmonary embolism"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"While a DVT stays in place, it mainly affects the leg vein it sits in. Once it breaks free, it becomes an embolus and is carried by normal venous return up the inferior vena cava and through the right heart into the pulmonary arteries. There it lodges and blocks blood flow to part of the lung, a life-threatening pulmonary embolism.","notes":["Venous clots travel toward the heart, not into leg arteries. Peripheral artery disease is plaque narrowing a limb artery.","Clots in deep veins travel toward the heart, not back out to superficial veins. Varicose veins come from failed valves in superficial veins.","The hepatic portal vein drains the digestive organs, not the legs. Blood from a leg vein enters the inferior vena cava instead.","Correct. A traveling DVT can become a pulmonary embolism."],"lt":"t-vessel-disorders","lid":"W3-590","comp":70,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-disorders"},{"id":"L03796","dok":2,"q":"Why are hemorrhoids grouped with varicose veins?","a":"They are swollen varicose veins in the rectal and anal region.","options":["They are plaques in the arteries of the colon","They are aneurysms of the rectal arteries","They are clots lodged in the deep pelvic veins","They are varicose veins of the rectal and anal region"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Varicose veins are veins that have become swollen and twisted as blood pools in them. Hemorrhoids are the same kind of change in the veins of the rectal and anal region. They are a common cause of rectal discomfort and bleeding.","notes":["Plaque in arteries is atherosclerosis. It would be the answer if the question asked what narrows arteries over time.","An aneurysm is a bulge in a weakened artery wall, common in the aorta and brain. Hemorrhoids involve veins, not arteries.","A clot in a deep vein is a deep vein thrombosis, most often in the leg. Pick it if the question asked about a clot that could travel to the lungs.","Correct. Hemorrhoids are varicose veins in the rectal and anal region."],"lt":"t-vessel-disorders","lid":"W3-591","comp":70,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-disorders"},{"id":"L03797","dok":2,"q":"Why does chronic venous insufficiency produce lasting swelling of the lower legs?","a":"Failed valves impair venous return and allow persistent pooling.","options":["Failed vein valves let blood and fluid pool in the lower legs","Narrowed leg arteries deliver too little blood to the legs","A ruptured artery bleeds into the tissues of the leg","Clots from the legs travel to the lungs and back"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"In chronic venous insufficiency, the valves of the leg veins have failed over a long time, so blood is not returned effectively to the heart. Blood collects in the lower leg veins, and the raised pressure pushes fluid out into the surrounding tissue. Because the valve failure is long-term, the swelling persists rather than coming and going.","notes":["Correct. Long-term valve failure lets blood and fluid pool, causing lasting swelling.","Narrowed leg arteries describe peripheral artery disease, which causes pain on walking rather than swelling. Pick it if the question asked about too little blood reaching the leg muscles.","A ruptured artery causes sudden bleeding, not slow, lasting swelling. Rupture is the main danger of an aneurysm.","Clots that leave the legs lodge in the lungs as a pulmonary embolism; they do not travel back. That would be the answer if the question asked about sudden chest pain and breathlessness."],"lt":"t-vessel-disorders","lid":"W3-592","comp":70,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-disorders"},{"id":"N-m3-vessel-routes-1-chunk26","dok":1,"q":"Besides the hepatic portal system, which portal system is named in the course notes?","a":"The hypophyseal portal system, linking the hypothalamus to the anterior pituitary.","options":["Cerebral arterial circle","Coronary sinus","Palmar arterial arches","Hypophyseal portal system"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"A portal system passes blood through two capillary beds in series before it returns to the heart. In the hypophyseal portal system, blood picks up substances in a capillary bed in the hypothalamus and carries them through portal veins to a second capillary bed in the anterior pituitary. This lets the hypothalamus send chemical signals straight to the pituitary.","notes":["The cerebral arterial circle is an arterial anastomosis at the base of the brain, giving alternate routes if one artery is blocked. Pick it if the question asked for an example of an anastomosis.","The coronary sinus is a vein on the back of the heart that drains the cardiac veins into the right atrium. Choose it if the question asked where blood from the heart wall returns.","The palmar arches are arterial anastomoses in the hand formed by the radial and ulnar arteries. They would be the answer if the question asked how blood still reaches the fingers when one forearm artery is blocked.","Correct. The hypophyseal portal system links the hypothalamus and anterior pituitary through two capillary beds."],"comp":69,"competencyId":"bvn-circulatory-routes"},{"id":"N-m3-vessel-routes-2-chunk26","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares a thrombus and an embolus?","a":"A thrombus is a clot attached where it formed; an embolus is a clot or fragment that has broken free and travels.","options":["A thrombus is a fatty plaque, while an embolus is a bulging artery wall","Both are bulges in a weakened vessel wall that can rupture","An embolus stays where it formed, while a thrombus breaks free and travels","A thrombus stays where it formed, while an embolus breaks free and travels"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"A thrombus is a clot that forms inside a vessel and stays attached there, for example on a roughened plaque or in a deep leg vein. If a thrombus, or a piece of it, breaks loose and is carried in the blood, it becomes an embolus. An embolus lodges wherever the vessel becomes too narrow, as in a pulmonary embolism.","notes":["A fatty plaque is an atheroma, and a bulging wall is an aneurysm. Pick those terms if the question asked about atherosclerosis or an artery at risk of rupture.","A bulge in a weakened wall is an aneurysm, not a clot. Choose it if the question asked which disorder is common in the aorta and brain arteries.","This swaps the two terms. Embolism always describes something that has moved, as in a pulmonary embolism from a leg clot.","Correct. A thrombus is stationary, and an embolus travels."],"comp":70,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-disorders"},{"id":"L03913","dok":2,"q":"Blood leaves a capillary bed in the forearm and returns to the heart. Which sequence does it follow?","a":"Capillary, venule, vein, superior vena cava, right atrium.","options":["Capillary, venule, vein, superior vena cava, right atrium","Capillary, venule, vein, inferior vena cava, right atrium","Capillary, arteriole, artery, superior vena cava, right atrium","Capillary, venule, vein, pulmonary veins, left atrium"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Capillaries drain into venules, the smallest veins, which merge into larger veins. Veins of the upper limb drain toward the superior vena cava, which returns blood from the head, neck, and upper limbs to the right atrium. Arteries and arterioles carry blood away from the heart, so they are never part of the return trip.","notes":["Correct. Forearm blood drains through venules and veins into the superior vena cava and then the right atrium.","The inferior vena cava drains the trunk and lower limbs. This sequence would be right if the capillary bed were in the leg instead of the forearm.","Arterioles and arteries carry blood away from the heart toward the capillaries. They would be the answer for the trip from the aorta down to a capillary bed, which runs in the opposite direction.","The pulmonary veins and left atrium receive blood only from the lungs. This ending would be right for blood leaving a lung capillary bed, not a capillary bed in the forearm."],"lt":"t-pathway-full-tracing","lid":"W3-719","comp":67,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-types"},{"id":"L03915","dok":2,"q":"A clot breaks free from a deep vein in the leg. Which route does it follow to reach the lung?","a":"Inferior vena cava, right atrium, right ventricle, pulmonary trunk, pulmonary artery.","options":["Inferior vena cava, right atrium, left atrium, left ventricle, aorta","Inferior vena cava, right atrium, right ventricle, pulmonary trunk, pulmonary artery","Inferior vena cava, right atrium, right ventricle, pulmonary veins, pulmonary artery","Superior vena cava, right atrium, right ventricle, pulmonary trunk, pulmonary artery"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"A clot that breaks loose from a deep vein of the leg simply rides the normal venous return. Leg veins drain into the inferior vena cava, which empties into the right atrium. The clot passes into the right ventricle, which pumps it into the pulmonary trunk and a pulmonary artery, where it lodges as a pulmonary embolism.","notes":["This route crosses from the right atrium to the left atrium, which does not happen in a normal adult heart because the interatrial septum is closed. The left atrium, left ventricle, and aorta would be the route for blood that has already returned from the lungs.","Correct. A leg clot follows venous return through the right heart into the pulmonary arteries.","This route puts the pulmonary veins between the pulmonary trunk and the pulmonary arteries. The pulmonary veins actually carry blood out of the lungs to the left atrium, so they come after the lungs, not before.","The superior vena cava drains the head, neck, and upper limbs. This route would be right for a clot that started in an arm vein, not a leg vein."],"lt":"t-pathway-full-tracing","lid":"W3-721","comp":69,"competencyId":"bvn-circulatory-routes"},{"id":"L03924","dok":2,"q":"In fetal circulation, which sequence carries oxygen-rich blood from the placenta to the inferior vena cava?","a":"Placenta, umbilical vein, ductus venosus, inferior vena cava.","options":["Placenta, umbilical vein, ductus venosus, inferior vena cava","Placenta, umbilical vein, foramen ovale, inferior vena cava","Placenta, umbilical vein, ductus arteriosus, inferior vena cava","Placenta, umbilical arteries, ductus venosus, inferior vena cava"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"A fetus gets its oxygen from the placenta, not its lungs. Oxygen-rich blood travels from the placenta to the fetus in the single umbilical vein. Most of it then passes through the ductus venosus, a shunt through the liver, which delivers it straight into the inferior vena cava.","notes":["Correct. The umbilical vein and ductus venosus carry placental blood into the inferior vena cava.","The foramen ovale is an opening in the interatrial septum between the right and left atria. It would fit a trace from the right atrium to the left atrium, which happens after blood reaches the heart.","The ductus arteriosus is a short vessel between the pulmonary trunk and the aorta that lets blood bypass the lungs. It would fit a trace from the right ventricle to the aorta, not from the umbilical vein.","The umbilical arteries carry oxygen-poor blood from the fetus back to the placenta, the opposite direction. They would be the answer if the question asked how fetal blood returns to the placenta."],"lt":"t-pathway-fetal-circulation","lid":"W3-730","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L03925","dok":2,"q":"In fetal circulation, blood in the right atrium can bypass the lungs by passing through the foramen ovale. Which chamber does it enter?","a":"The left atrium.","options":["Left atrium","Pulmonary trunk","Left ventricle","Right ventricle"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The foramen ovale is an opening in the interatrial septum, the wall between the two atria. In the fetus it lets blood pass directly from the right atrium into the left atrium, skipping the right ventricle and the collapsed lungs. From the left atrium the blood goes on to the left ventricle and out the aorta.","notes":["Correct. The foramen ovale connects the right atrium to the left atrium.","The pulmonary trunk leaves the right ventricle, so blood reaches it only by the ordinary route. It would be the starting point of the other lung bypass, the ductus arteriosus.","The left ventricle is one step beyond the left atrium, reached through the mitral valve. It would be the answer if the question asked where the blood goes after the left atrium.","The right ventricle receives blood from the right atrium through the tricuspid valve, which is the normal route and does not bypass the lungs. It would be the answer if the blood took the tricuspid valve instead of the foramen ovale."],"lt":"t-pathway-fetal-circulation","lid":"W3-731","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L03926","dok":2,"q":"In fetal circulation, blood from the right ventricle enters the pulmonary trunk and then crosses the ductus arteriosus. Which vessel does it enter at the far end of that shunt?","a":"The aorta.","options":["Aorta","Pulmonary veins","Inferior vena cava","Left pulmonary artery"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The ductus arteriosus is a short vessel connecting the pulmonary trunk to the aorta. Because the fetal lungs are collapsed and not used for gas exchange, most blood pumped into the pulmonary trunk is shunted through the ductus arteriosus into the aorta instead of going to the lungs.","notes":["Correct. The ductus arteriosus empties into the aorta.","The pulmonary veins drain the lungs into the left atrium. They are on the far side of the lungs, so blood that bypasses the lungs never enters them first.","The inferior vena cava is where the ductus venosus empties, carrying placental blood past the liver. It would be the answer if the question asked about the liver bypass.","The left pulmonary artery is a branch of the pulmonary trunk that leads into the left lung. Blood going there is not bypassing the lungs, so it would be the answer for the normal adult route, not for the ductus arteriosus."],"lt":"t-pathway-fetal-circulation","lid":"W3-732","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L03927","dok":2,"q":"In fetal circulation, oxygen-poor blood returns from the fetus to the placenta through which vessels?","a":"The two umbilical arteries.","options":["Pulmonary arteries","Umbilical arteries","Ductus venosus","Umbilical vein"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"After circulating through the fetal body, oxygen-poor blood leaves through the two umbilical arteries, which run in the umbilical cord back to the placenta. They are called arteries because they carry blood away from the fetal heart, even though the blood is low in oxygen. At the placenta the blood unloads wastes and picks up oxygen again.","notes":["The pulmonary arteries carry blood from the pulmonary trunk to the lungs. In an adult they send oxygen-poor blood to be refreshed, but in a fetus that job belongs to the placenta, not the lungs.","Correct. The umbilical arteries return oxygen-poor blood to the placenta.","The ductus venosus is a shunt inside the fetal liver that passes blood from the umbilical vein to the inferior vena cava. It would be the answer if the question asked how fetal blood bypasses the liver.","The umbilical vein carries oxygen-rich blood the other way, from the placenta to the fetus. It would be the answer if the question asked how oxygen reaches the fetus."],"lt":"t-pathway-fetal-circulation","lid":"W3-733","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L03932","dok":2,"q":"In fetal circulation, most of the blood pumped into the pulmonary trunk enters which structure next?","a":"The ductus arteriosus.","options":["Ductus arteriosus","Foramen ovale","Ductus venosus","Right and left pulmonary arteries"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The fetal lungs are collapsed and do not exchange gases, so most blood in the pulmonary trunk is diverted away from them. It passes through the ductus arteriosus, a short vessel joining the pulmonary trunk to the aorta. Only a small share continues into the pulmonary arteries to the lungs.","notes":["Correct. Most fetal blood leaves the pulmonary trunk through the ductus arteriosus.","The foramen ovale is the opening between the right and left atria, the other lung bypass. It would be the answer if the question started in the right atrium.","The ductus venosus is the shunt through the fetal liver, carrying blood from the umbilical vein to the inferior vena cava. It would be the answer if the question started at the umbilical vein.","The right and left pulmonary arteries are where blood goes after the pulmonary trunk in an adult. In a fetus only a small share goes this way, because the lungs are not yet used for gas exchange."],"lt":"t-pathway-next-step-recall","lid":"W3-738","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L03933","dok":2,"q":"In fetal circulation, oxygen-rich blood in the umbilical vein bypasses the liver by passing through which shunt?","a":"The ductus venosus.","options":["Ductus arteriosus","Foramen ovale","Umbilical arteries","Ductus venosus"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The ductus venosus is a shunt that runs through the fetal liver. It lets most of the oxygen-rich blood arriving in the umbilical vein skip the liver tissue and flow straight toward the inferior vena cava. After birth it closes and becomes the ligamentum venosum.","notes":["The ductus arteriosus connects the pulmonary trunk to the aorta and is a lung bypass. It would be the answer if the question started at the pulmonary trunk.","The foramen ovale is the opening in the interatrial septum that lets blood skip the lungs, not the liver. It would be the answer if the question asked how blood passes from the right atrium to the left atrium.","The umbilical arteries are vessels in the cord, not a shunt, and they carry oxygen-poor blood back to the placenta. They would be the answer if the question asked how blood returns to the placenta.","Correct. The ductus venosus carries umbilical vein blood past the liver."],"lt":"t-pathway-next-step-recall","lid":"W3-740","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L03934","dok":2,"q":"In fetal circulation, blood that leaves the ductus venosus flows next into which vessel?","a":"The inferior vena cava.","options":["Inferior vena cava","Umbilical arteries","Superior vena cava","Aorta"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The ductus venosus carries blood from the umbilical vein through the liver and delivers it to the inferior vena cava. From there the blood enters the right atrium. Because this placental blood is oxygen-rich, the inferior vena cava of a fetus carries a mix of oxygen-rich and oxygen-poor blood.","notes":["Correct. The ductus venosus empties into the inferior vena cava.","The umbilical arteries carry oxygen-poor blood from the fetus back to the placenta at the end of the fetal route. They would be the answer if the question asked how blood returns to the placenta.","The superior vena cava drains the head, neck, and upper limbs and enters the right atrium from above. It would be the answer if the question asked about venous return from the upper body.","The aorta is where the ductus arteriosus empties, not the ductus venosus. It would be the answer if the question started at the ductus arteriosus."],"lt":"t-pathway-next-step-recall","lid":"W3-741","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L03944","dok":2,"q":"One artery supplying a region is blocked, yet the tissue still receives blood through a connection with a neighboring artery. What structure explains this?","a":"An anastomosis.","options":["Venous valve","Precapillary sphincter","Anastomosis","Portal system"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"An anastomosis is a place where two vessels join. When arteries supplying the same region are connected this way, blood can take an alternate route if one path is blocked. That backup route is why some tissues survive a blocked artery while tissues supplied by a single artery are more vulnerable.","notes":["Venous valves are flaps of the tunica intima that keep blood in veins from flowing backward. They would be the answer if the question asked why blood in leg veins does not pool.","A precapillary sphincter is a ring of smooth muscle that opens or closes the entrance to a single capillary. It would be the answer if the question asked what controls flow into an individual capillary.","Correct. An anastomosis gives blood an alternate route around a blockage.","A portal system sends blood through two capillary beds in a row before it returns to the heart, as in the hepatic portal circulation. It would be the answer if the question described blood passing through the intestines and then the liver."],"lt":"t-pathway-applied-sequencing","lid":"W3-751","comp":69,"competencyId":"bvn-circulatory-routes"},{"id":"L03945","dok":2,"q":"In some tissues, blood flows from a small artery directly into a vein without passing through any capillaries. What is this direct connection called?","a":"An arteriovenous anastomosis.","options":["Postcapillary venule","Arteriovenous anastomosis","Portal system","Precapillary sphincter"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"An arteriovenous anastomosis is a direct connection from an artery to a vein that bypasses the capillaries. The name tells you what it joins: arterio for the artery side and venous for the vein side. Because the blood skips the capillary bed, little exchange with the tissue happens along this route.","notes":["A postcapillary venule is the first venule to receive blood from the capillaries, so blood reaching it has already passed through a capillary bed. It would be the answer if the question asked which vessel drains a capillary bed.","Correct. An arteriovenous anastomosis links an artery directly to a vein.","A portal system is the opposite arrangement, with blood passing through two capillary beds in series instead of none. It would be the answer if the question described blood flowing from the intestines to the liver.","A precapillary sphincter is a ring of smooth muscle that opens or closes the entrance to a capillary. It controls flow into capillaries rather than connecting an artery to a vein."],"lt":"t-pathway-applied-sequencing","lid":"W3-752","comp":69,"competencyId":"bvn-circulatory-routes"},{"id":"L03955","dok":2,"q":"Which sequence correctly traces systemic blood from the left ventricle to a capillary bed?","a":"Left ventricle, aortic valve, aorta, artery, arteriole, capillary bed.","options":["Left ventricle, pulmonary valve, aorta, artery, arteriole, capillary bed","Left ventricle, aortic valve, aorta, arteriole, artery, capillary bed","Left ventricle, aortic valve, aorta, artery, venule, capillary bed","Left ventricle, aortic valve, aorta, artery, arteriole, capillary bed"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The left ventricle pumps blood through the aortic valve into the aorta. The aorta branches into smaller arteries, which branch into arterioles, the smallest arteries. Arterioles control how much blood enters each capillary bed, where exchange with the tissue takes place.","notes":["The pulmonary valve is the exit from the right ventricle into the pulmonary trunk. It would belong in a trace from the right ventricle to the lungs, not from the left ventricle to the aorta.","This sequence puts arterioles before arteries. Vessels get smaller as blood moves away from the heart, so the larger arteries come first and branch into the smaller arterioles.","Venules carry blood away from capillaries toward the heart, so they come after a capillary bed. This sequence puts a venous vessel on the arterial side of the route.","Correct. This is the systemic route from the left ventricle down to a capillary bed."],"lt":"t-pathway-integrated-tracing","lid":"W3-762","comp":67,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-types"},{"id":"L03961","dok":2,"q":"Where is the foramen ovale, one of the fetal lung bypasses, located?","a":"In the interatrial septum.","options":["In the interventricular septum","Within the fetal liver","In the interatrial septum","Between the pulmonary trunk and the aorta"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The foramen ovale is an opening in the interatrial septum, the wall between the right and left atria. In the fetus it lets blood pass from the right atrium into the left atrium so it can skip the lungs. After birth it closes, leaving a shallow depression called the fossa ovalis.","notes":["The interventricular septum is the thick wall between the two ventricles, and it has no fetal shunt. An opening there would be an abnormal defect, not the foramen ovale. It would be the answer if the question asked where the AV bundle and bundle branches run.","The shunt within the fetal liver is the ductus venosus, which bypasses the liver rather than the lungs. It would be the answer if the question asked where the ductus venosus is.","Correct. The foramen ovale is an opening in the wall between the two atria.","The connection between the pulmonary trunk and the aorta is the ductus arteriosus, the other fetal lung bypass. It would be the answer if the question asked where the ductus arteriosus is."],"lt":"t-pathway-integrated-tracing","lid":"W3-768","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L03962","dok":2,"q":"In the fetus, the ductus arteriosus connects which two vessels?","a":"The pulmonary trunk and the aorta.","options":["Pulmonary veins and pulmonary trunk","Superior vena cava and aorta","Umbilical vein and inferior vena cava","Pulmonary trunk and aorta"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The ductus arteriosus is a short vessel running from the pulmonary trunk to the aorta. Because the fetal lungs are not used for gas exchange, it diverts most of the blood leaving the right ventricle into the aorta instead. After birth it closes and becomes the ligamentum arteriosum.","notes":["The pulmonary veins carry blood out of the lungs to the left atrium and are not joined to the pulmonary trunk by any shunt. This pairing mixes the vessels on the two ends of the lungs.","The superior vena cava returns blood to the right atrium and is not connected to the aorta by any fetal shunt. Students may pick this because both vessels sit at the base of the heart, but no fetal shunt joins them.","The umbilical vein and inferior vena cava are joined by the ductus venosus, the liver bypass. That pair would be the answer if the question asked about the ductus venosus.","Correct. The ductus arteriosus joins the pulmonary trunk to the aorta."],"lt":"t-pathway-integrated-tracing","lid":"W3-769","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L03963","dok":2,"q":"The ductus venosus is a fetal shunt that passes through which organ?","a":"The liver.","options":["Liver","Heart","Placenta","Lungs"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The ductus venosus runs through the fetal liver. It carries most of the oxygen-rich blood from the umbilical vein past the liver tissue and into the inferior vena cava. The fetus can skip much of the liver because the placenta handles that filtering before birth.","notes":["Correct. The ductus venosus is the shunt through the liver.","The heart holds the foramen ovale, the opening in the interatrial septum. The heart would be the answer if the question asked where the foramen ovale is.","The placenta is where fetal blood picks up oxygen and gives up wastes. It connects to the fetus through the umbilical cord, so it would be the answer if the question asked where the umbilical vessels lead.","The lungs are bypassed by the foramen ovale and the ductus arteriosus, but no shunt passes through the lungs themselves. They would be the answer if the question asked which organ those two shunts bypass."],"lt":"t-pathway-integrated-tracing","lid":"W3-770","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L03964","dok":2,"q":"Which statement about the umbilical vessels in fetal circulation is correct?","a":"Two umbilical arteries carry oxygen-poor blood to the placenta.","options":["Two umbilical veins carry oxygen-poor blood to the placenta","Two umbilical arteries carry oxygen-poor blood to the placenta","Two umbilical arteries carry oxygen-rich blood to the fetus","One umbilical vein carries oxygen-poor blood to the placenta"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The umbilical cord holds three vessels: one umbilical vein and two umbilical arteries. The two arteries carry oxygen-poor blood away from the fetus to the placenta, and the single vein brings oxygen-rich blood back. As with the pulmonary vessels, artery and vein are named by direction relative to the heart, not by oxygen content.","notes":["There is only one umbilical vein, and it carries oxygen-rich blood toward the fetus. This choice mixes up both the number of veins and their direction.","Correct. The two umbilical arteries return oxygen-poor blood to the placenta.","This reverses the job of the umbilical arteries. Blood moving toward the fetus with oxygen from the placenta travels in the umbilical vein, so that would be the true statement about the vein.","The number is right, since there is one umbilical vein, but the direction and oxygen content are reversed. The umbilical vein brings oxygen-rich blood from the placenta to the fetus."],"lt":"t-pathway-integrated-tracing","lid":"W3-771","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L03966","dok":2,"q":"Blood leaves the left ventricle through the aorta and returns to the right atrium through the venae cavae. In what order does it pass through the vessel types in between?","a":"Arteries, arterioles, capillaries, venules, veins.","options":["Arteries, arterioles, venules, capillaries, veins","Arterioles, arteries, capillaries, veins, venules","Arteries, arterioles, capillaries, venules, veins","Arteries, venules, capillaries, arterioles, veins"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The systemic circuit follows a fixed vascular sequence. Arteries branch into arterioles, which feed capillaries, where exchange happens. Capillaries merge into venules, which merge into veins, and the veins finally drain into the superior and inferior venae cavae.","notes":["This places venules before capillaries. Venules collect blood after it leaves the capillaries, so the capillaries come first.","This reverses the size order on both sides. Large arteries branch into small arterioles, and small venules merge into larger veins, so arteries come first and veins come last.","Correct. This is the standard vascular sequence from artery to vein.","This puts venules and arterioles on the wrong sides of the capillaries. Arterioles deliver blood to capillaries and venules drain it away, so the arterioles must come before the capillaries."],"lt":"t-pathway-integrated-tracing","lid":"W3-773","comp":67,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-types"},{"id":"L03969","dok":2,"q":"Which sequence describes the route blood takes through a portal system, such as the hepatic portal circulation?","a":"First capillary bed, portal vein, second capillary bed, veins back to the heart.","options":["Artery, directly into a vein, back to the heart","First capillary bed, artery, second capillary bed, veins back to the heart","Artery, capillary bed, vein, back to the heart","First capillary bed, portal vein, second capillary bed, veins back to the heart"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"A portal system passes blood through two capillary beds in a row before it returns to the heart. A portal vein links the two beds. In the hepatic portal circulation, blood from the capillaries of the digestive organs travels in the hepatic portal vein to a second set of capillaries in the liver, then drains toward the inferior vena cava.","notes":["A direct artery-to-vein connection with no capillaries is an arteriovenous anastomosis. It would be the answer if the question asked about blood bypassing a capillary bed.","The vessel linking the two capillary beds is a vein, because it collects blood from the first capillary bed. An artery carries blood away from the heart toward capillaries, so it would not drain a capillary bed.","This is the ordinary route with only one capillary bed. It describes most of the systemic circuit, not a portal system.","Correct. A portal vein links two capillary beds in series."],"lt":"t-pathway-integrated-tracing","lid":"W3-777","comp":69,"competencyId":"bvn-circulatory-routes"},{"id":"L03970","dok":3,"q":"A deep vein thrombosis in the leg breaks free. Why does it usually end up as a pulmonary embolism in the lung rather than blocking an artery in the brain?","a":"Venous blood from the leg returns to the right heart, which pumps it only to the lungs.","options":["The left ventricle pumps venous blood to the lungs before sending it to the brain","Venous blood from the leg returns to the right heart, which pumps it only to the lungs","The clot crosses the interatrial septum and is pumped into the pulmonary trunk","Leg veins drain directly into the pulmonary veins, which carry it to the lungs"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"A clot that leaves a leg vein rides the normal venous return: up the inferior vena cava into the right atrium and right ventricle. The right ventricle sends its blood only into the pulmonary trunk and pulmonary arteries. As those arteries branch and narrow in the lung, the clot lodges there before it could ever reach the left heart and the arteries of the brain.","notes":["The left ventricle pumps oxygen-rich blood into the aorta for the body, not to the lungs. Pumping to the lungs is the job of the right ventricle.","Correct. The right heart sends venous return only to the lungs, so the clot lodges there.","In a normal adult the interatrial septum is closed, so a clot cannot cross it. Crossing would take it to the left side and away from the pulmonary trunk, which is the opposite of what this choice claims.","The pulmonary veins carry blood out of the lungs to the left atrium and receive nothing from the legs. Leg veins drain into the inferior vena cava, which empties into the right atrium."],"lt":"t-pathway-integrated-tracing","lid":"W3-778","comp":69,"competencyId":"bvn-circulatory-routes"},{"id":"L03971","dok":2,"q":"Which sequence explains how coronary artery disease leads to a myocardial infarction (heart attack)?","a":"Plaque narrows a coronary artery, a clot blocks it, and the myocardium beyond it dies from lack of oxygen.","options":["Plaque narrows a brain artery, a clot blocks it, and brain tissue beyond it dies","Plaque narrows a leg artery, and the leg muscles hurt during walking","A clot from a leg vein blocks a pulmonary artery, and blood flow to the lung stops","Plaque narrows a coronary artery, a clot blocks it, and the myocardium beyond it dies"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Coronary artery disease is atherosclerosis of the coronary arteries, the narrow vessels that supply the heart wall. A fatty plaque narrows the artery, and a clot (thrombus) can form on the rough plaque and suddenly block it. The patch of myocardium beyond the block is starved of oxygen and begins to die, which is a myocardial infarction.","notes":["This is the same plaque-and-clot process in an artery of the brain, which causes a stroke. Pick it if the question asked how atherosclerosis leads to a stroke.","Plaque narrowing the arteries of the legs is peripheral artery disease, which causes pain on walking. It would be the answer if the question asked about leg pain from narrowed arteries.","A clot traveling from a leg vein into a pulmonary artery is a pulmonary embolism. It would be the answer if the question asked how a deep vein thrombosis becomes dangerous.","Correct. A blocked coronary artery starves the myocardium beyond it, causing an infarction."],"lt":"t-pathway-integrated-tracing","lid":"W3-779","comp":70,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-disorders"},{"id":"L03972","dok":2,"q":"Which sequence explains how failing valves in leg veins lead to varicose veins?","a":"Valves fail, blood flows backward and pools, and the superficial veins swell and twist.","options":["Valves fail, blood flows backward and pools, and the superficial veins swell and twist","Plaque builds up in the vessel wall, the lumen narrows, and blood flow drops","A clot forms in a deep vein, breaks free, and travels to the lung","An artery wall weakens, the wall bulges outward, and the bulge may rupture"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Veins carry blood under low pressure, so they depend on valves (flaps of the tunica intima) to keep blood moving toward the heart. When the valves in superficial leg veins fail, blood slips backward and pools. The pooled blood stretches the vein walls until the veins become swollen, twisted, and visible under the skin.","notes":["Correct. Failed valves let blood pool and stretch the superficial veins.","Plaque buildup that narrows a vessel is atherosclerosis, which affects arteries rather than veins. It would be the answer if the question asked how an artery becomes narrowed.","A clot in a deep vein that travels to the lung is a deep vein thrombosis becoming a pulmonary embolism. It would be the answer if the question asked about a clot, not about failed valves.","A weakened artery wall that bulges outward is an aneurysm, an arterial disorder. It would be the answer if the question asked how an aneurysm forms."],"lt":"t-pathway-integrated-tracing","lid":"W3-780","comp":70,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-disorders"},{"id":"L03991","dok":2,"q":"After birth the fetal shunts close and leave remnants. Which pairing of fetal structure and adult remnant is correct?","a":"Foramen ovale and fossa ovalis.","options":["Ductus arteriosus and ligamentum venosum","Ductus venosus and ligamentum arteriosum","Foramen ovale and fossa ovalis","Umbilical vein and medial umbilical ligament"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"When the lungs inflate at birth and the placenta is cut off, the fetal shunts close. The foramen ovale, a hole in the interatrial septum, seals to leave a shallow depression called the fossa ovalis. The others become cords: the ductus arteriosus becomes the ligamentum arteriosum, and the ductus venosus becomes the ligamentum venosum.","notes":["The ductus arteriosus becomes the ligamentum arteriosum, and the ligamentum venosum comes from the ductus venosus. Each remnant keeps part of its original name, so matching the names catches this swap.","The ductus venosus becomes the ligamentum venosum, not the ligamentum arteriosum. The ligamentum arteriosum is the remnant of the ductus arteriosus, between the pulmonary trunk and the aorta.","Correct. The foramen ovale becomes the fossa ovalis.","The umbilical vein becomes the ligamentum teres, the round ligament of the liver. The medial umbilical ligaments are the remnants of the two umbilical arteries."],"lt":"t-pathway-integrated-tracing","lid":"W3-799","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L03992","dok":2,"q":"After birth, the umbilical vein closes and becomes which structure?","a":"The ligamentum teres (round ligament of the liver).","options":["Medial umbilical ligaments","Ligamentum teres","Ligamentum arteriosum","Ligamentum venosum"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The umbilical vein carried oxygen-rich blood from the placenta to the fetus. After birth it closes and becomes the ligamentum teres, the round ligament of the liver, which runs in the free edge of the falciform ligament. The two umbilical arteries become the medial umbilical ligaments on the inner anterior abdominal wall.","notes":["The medial umbilical ligaments are the remnants of the two umbilical arteries, not the vein. They would be the answer if the question asked what the umbilical arteries become.","Correct. The umbilical vein becomes the ligamentum teres.","The ligamentum arteriosum is the remnant of the ductus arteriosus, between the pulmonary trunk and the aorta. It would be the answer if the question asked what the ductus arteriosus becomes.","The ligamentum venosum is the remnant of the ductus venosus, the shunt through the liver. It would be the answer if the question asked what the ductus venosus becomes."],"lt":"t-pathway-integrated-tracing","lid":"W3-800","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L04036","dok":2,"q":"Which disorder develops when the valves of superficial veins fail, so blood pools and the veins become twisted and swollen, most often in the legs?","a":"Varicose veins","options":["Varicose veins","Phlebitis","Deep vein thrombosis","Aneurysm"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Varicose veins form when the valves in superficial veins stop closing properly. Blood that should move up toward the heart slides back and pools, stretching the vein walls until the veins bulge and twist under the skin. The legs are most often affected because blood there has to climb against gravity.","notes":["Correct. Failed valves in superficial veins cause varicose veins.","Phlebitis is inflammation of a vein, causing pain, redness, and tenderness along it. Choose it if the question described an inflamed, tender vein rather than one swollen from pooled blood.","Deep vein thrombosis is a blood clot in a deep vein, usually of the leg, not a stretched superficial vein. Pick it if the question asked about a clot that could break free and travel to the lungs.","An aneurysm is a balloon-like bulge in a weakened artery wall, not a vein. It would be the answer if the question asked about a bulging artery that could rupture, such as in the aorta or brain."],"lt":"t-imagefree-structure-recognition","lid":"W3-847","comp":70,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-disorders"},{"id":"L04037","dok":2,"q":"Which disorder is a blood clot in a deep vein, usually in the leg?","a":"Deep vein thrombosis","options":["Deep vein thrombosis","Varicose veins","Pulmonary embolism","Atherosclerosis"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Deep vein thrombosis (DVT) is a clot, or thrombus, that forms in a deep vein, most often in the leg. It is dangerous because of where the clot can go. If a piece breaks free, it rides the normal venous return up the inferior vena cava, through the right side of the heart, and into the lungs.","notes":["Correct. A deep vein thrombosis is a clot in a deep vein, usually of the leg.","Varicose veins are superficial veins that swell and twist when their valves fail, without a clot being the defining problem. Pick them if the question asked about bulging veins under the skin of the legs.","A pulmonary embolism is what happens when a clot, often from a DVT, lodges in an artery of the lung. It would be the answer if the question asked where a traveling leg clot ends up.","Atherosclerosis is fatty plaque building up in artery walls, not a clot in a vein. Choose it if the question asked about the disease behind most heart attacks and strokes."],"lt":"t-imagefree-structure-recognition","lid":"W3-848","comp":70,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-disorders"},{"id":"L04038","dok":2,"q":"Which disorder occurs when a clot, often from a deep leg vein, lodges in an artery of the lung?","a":"Pulmonary embolism","options":["Pulmonary embolism","Stroke","Pneumothorax","Deep vein thrombosis"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"A pulmonary embolism is a blockage of a lung artery by an embolus, a clot or fragment that has traveled through the bloodstream. Most start as a deep vein thrombosis in the leg, then travel up the venae cavae, through the right atrium and right ventricle, and out the pulmonary trunk until they wedge in a pulmonary artery branch. It blocks blood flow to part of the lung and is a medical emergency.","notes":["Correct. A clot lodged in a lung artery is a pulmonary embolism.","A stroke is loss of blood supply to part of the brain from a blocked or burst artery. Choose it if the clot had lodged in an artery of the brain instead of the lung.","A pneumothorax is air in the pleural cavity that lets the lung collapse, not a blocked vessel. It would be the answer if the question described a punctured chest wall and a collapsed lung.","A deep vein thrombosis is the clot while it is still sitting in a deep leg vein, before it travels. Pick it if the question asked about the original clot in the leg."],"lt":"t-imagefree-structure-recognition","lid":"W3-849","comp":70,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-disorders"},{"id":"L04039","dok":2,"q":"Which disease is the buildup of fatty plaques within the walls of arteries, narrowing the lumen?","a":"Atherosclerosis","options":["Atherosclerosis","Varicose veins","Hypertension","Aneurysm"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Atherosclerosis begins with injury to the tunica intima, the smooth inner lining of an artery. Fatty material, cells, and debris then collect in the wall as a plaque (atheroma), which bulges inward and narrows the channel. A clot can also form on the rough plaque and suddenly block the artery, which is why atherosclerosis is behind most heart attacks and strokes.","notes":["Correct. Atherosclerosis is fatty plaque buildup in artery walls.","Varicose veins are swollen superficial veins caused by failed valves, a venous problem rather than an arterial one. Choose them if the question asked about twisted, bulging leg veins.","Hypertension is chronically high pressure inside the arteries, not a plaque in the wall, though it can damage the wall over time. It would be the answer if the question asked about long-term high blood pressure.","An aneurysm is a balloon-like bulge in a weakened artery wall, which widens rather than narrows the vessel. Pick it if the question asked about an artery wall that balloons outward and may rupture."],"lt":"t-imagefree-structure-recognition","lid":"W3-850","comp":70,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-disorders"},{"id":"L04040","dok":2,"q":"Which disorder is a balloon-like bulge in a weakened artery wall?","a":"Aneurysm","options":["Deep vein thrombosis","Varicose veins","Atherosclerosis","Aneurysm"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"An aneurysm is a weak spot in an artery wall that balloons outward under the high pressure inside the vessel. The thinned wall can rupture and cause severe bleeding. Aneurysms are most common in the aorta and in the arteries of the brain.","notes":["A deep vein thrombosis is a blood clot in a deep vein, usually of the leg. Choose it if the question asked about a clot that could travel to the lungs.","Varicose veins are swollen, twisted superficial veins caused by failed valves. They would be the answer if the question asked about bulging veins in the legs instead of a bulging artery.","Atherosclerosis is fatty plaque that builds up inside an artery wall and narrows the lumen. Pick it if the question asked about a narrowed artery rather than a bulging one.","Correct. An aneurysm is a bulge in a weakened artery wall."],"lt":"t-imagefree-structure-recognition","lid":"W3-851","comp":70,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-disorders"},{"id":"L04041","dok":2,"q":"Which disorder is chronically high pressure within the arteries, which strains the heart and damages vessel walls over time?","a":"Hypertension","options":["Atherosclerosis","Hypertension","Peripheral artery disease","Aneurysm"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Hypertension means blood pressure in the arteries stays high over a long period. The heart has to work harder to push blood against that pressure, and the constant force wears on the artery walls. Over time this damage can contribute to other vessel disorders, which is why it is described as a disorder of pressure rather than of one specific structure.","notes":["Atherosclerosis is fatty plaque buildup within the artery wall that narrows the lumen. It would be the answer if the question asked about plaques rather than the pressure itself.","Correct. Hypertension is chronically high arterial pressure.","Peripheral artery disease is narrowing of the arteries of the limbs, usually the legs, causing pain on walking. Choose it if the question described leg pain from muscles starved of blood.","An aneurysm is a specific balloon-like bulge in a weakened artery wall. Pick it if the question described a bulging artery that could rupture."],"lt":"t-imagefree-structure-recognition","lid":"W3-852","comp":70,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-disorders"},{"id":"L04042","dok":2,"q":"In fetal circulation, which vessel carries oxygen-rich blood from the placenta to the fetus?","a":"Umbilical vein","options":["Umbilical arteries","Ductus arteriosus","Umbilical vein","Ductus venosus"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The fetus gets its oxygen from the placenta, not its own lungs. The single umbilical vein runs through the umbilical cord and carries oxygen-rich, nutrient-rich blood from the placenta toward the fetal liver and heart. It is a vein because it carries blood toward the fetal heart, even though its blood is the most oxygen-rich in the fetus.","notes":["The umbilical arteries carry oxygen-poor blood the other way, from the fetus back to the placenta. Pick them if the question asked which vessels return blood to the placenta.","The ductus arteriosus is a short vessel that shunts blood from the pulmonary trunk to the aorta, bypassing the lungs. Choose it if the question asked which fetal vessel connects those two great arteries.","Correct. The umbilical vein brings oxygen-rich blood from the placenta.","The ductus venosus is the shunt that lets blood from the umbilical vein bypass most of the liver. It would be the answer if the question asked how fetal blood skips the liver."],"lt":"t-imagefree-structure-recognition","lid":"W3-853","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L04043","dok":2,"q":"In fetal circulation, which vessels carry oxygen-poor blood from the fetus back to the placenta?","a":"Umbilical arteries","options":["Umbilical arteries","Pulmonary veins","Umbilical vein","Ductus venosus"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The two umbilical arteries run through the umbilical cord and carry oxygen-poor blood and wastes from the fetus back to the placenta. They are arteries because they carry blood away from the fetal heart. After birth they close and become the medial umbilical ligaments on the inner surface of the anterior abdominal wall.","notes":["Correct. The two umbilical arteries return oxygen-poor blood to the placenta.","The pulmonary veins return blood from the lungs to the left atrium, and the fetal lungs are not yet used for gas exchange. They would be the answer if the question asked which vessels bring oxygen-rich blood from the lungs after birth.","The umbilical vein carries oxygen-rich blood the opposite direction, from the placenta to the fetus. Pick it if the question asked which vessel delivers oxygen to the fetus.","The ductus venosus is a shunt inside the fetal liver that lets blood bypass the liver tissue. Choose it if the question asked how umbilical vein blood skips the liver."],"lt":"t-imagefree-structure-recognition","lid":"W3-854","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L04044","dok":2,"q":"Which fetal shunt lets most blood from the umbilical vein bypass the liver and flow toward the inferior vena cava?","a":"Ductus venosus","options":["Ligamentum venosum","Ductus venosus","Ductus arteriosus","Foramen ovale"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Before birth, the placenta handles much of the work the liver will later do, so most fetal blood does not need to pass through the liver tissue. The ductus venosus is a shunt through the fetal liver that carries blood from the umbilical vein straight toward the inferior vena cava. After birth it closes and becomes the ligamentum venosum.","notes":["The ligamentum venosum is the fibrous cord the ductus venosus becomes after birth, so it carries no blood. Choose it if the question asked for the adult remnant of this shunt.","Correct. The ductus venosus carries blood past the liver.","The ductus arteriosus shunts blood from the pulmonary trunk into the aorta, bypassing the lungs, not the liver. Pick it if the question asked which shunt connects the two great arteries.","The foramen ovale is an opening in the interatrial septum that lets blood pass from the right atrium to the left atrium, bypassing the lungs. It would be the answer if the question asked about the shunt inside the heart."],"lt":"t-imagefree-structure-recognition","lid":"W3-855","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L04045","dok":2,"q":"Which fetal shunt is an opening in the interatrial septum that lets blood pass from the right atrium to the left atrium, bypassing the lungs?","a":"Foramen ovale","options":["Fossa ovalis","Ductus venosus","Ductus arteriosus","Foramen ovale"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The foramen ovale is an opening in the wall between the two atria of the fetal heart. Because the fetal lungs are collapsed and not used for gas exchange, much of the blood entering the right atrium passes straight through this opening into the left atrium and out to the body. After birth the opening seals, leaving the fossa ovalis.","notes":["The fossa ovalis is the shallow depression left after the foramen ovale closes, so no blood passes through it. It would be the answer if the question asked for the adult remnant of this opening.","The ductus venosus is the shunt that lets blood bypass the liver, not the lungs. Choose it if the question asked how umbilical vein blood skips the liver.","The ductus arteriosus also bypasses the lungs, but it is a vessel between the pulmonary trunk and the aorta, outside the atria. Pick it if the question asked about the shunt between the two great arteries.","Correct. The foramen ovale lets blood pass from the right atrium to the left atrium."],"lt":"t-imagefree-structure-recognition","lid":"W3-856","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L04046","dok":2,"q":"Which fetal shunt is a short vessel that carries blood from the pulmonary trunk into the aorta, bypassing the lungs?","a":"Ductus arteriosus","options":["Foramen ovale","Ligamentum arteriosum","Ductus arteriosus","Ductus venosus"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The ductus arteriosus is a short vessel connecting the pulmonary trunk to the aorta in the fetus. Blood that the right ventricle pumps into the pulmonary trunk mostly crosses this shunt into the aorta instead of going to the collapsed fetal lungs. After birth it closes and becomes the ligamentum arteriosum; if it stays open, the condition is called patent ductus arteriosus.","notes":["The foramen ovale also bypasses the lungs, but it is an opening between the right and left atria, not a vessel between arteries. Pick it if the question asked about the shunt in the interatrial septum.","The ligamentum arteriosum is the fibrous cord the ductus arteriosus becomes after birth, so it no longer carries blood. Choose it if the question asked for the adult remnant of this shunt.","Correct. The ductus arteriosus shunts blood from the pulmonary trunk to the aorta.","The ductus venosus is the shunt that lets blood bypass the liver. It would be the answer if the question asked how umbilical vein blood reaches the inferior vena cava without passing through liver tissue."],"lt":"t-imagefree-structure-recognition","lid":"W3-857","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L04047","dok":2,"q":"After birth, which structure is the adult remnant of the foramen ovale?","a":"Fossa ovalis","options":["Ligamentum arteriosum","Fossa ovalis","Ligamentum teres","Ligamentum venosum"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"At birth the lungs inflate and the foramen ovale seals shut. What remains is the fossa ovalis, a shallow oval depression in the interatrial septum. It is the one fetal shunt that becomes a depression instead of a ligament, because it was a hole rather than a vessel.","notes":["The ligamentum arteriosum is the remnant of the ductus arteriosus, a cord between the pulmonary trunk and the aorta. Pick it if the question asked what the ductus arteriosus becomes.","Correct. The foramen ovale becomes the fossa ovalis.","The ligamentum teres is the remnant of the umbilical vein, also called the round ligament of the liver. Choose it if the question asked what the umbilical vein becomes.","The ligamentum venosum is the remnant of the ductus venosus, a fibrous cord on the inferior surface of the liver. It would be the answer if the question asked what the liver shunt becomes."],"lt":"t-imagefree-structure-recognition","lid":"W3-858","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L04048","dok":2,"q":"Which adult structure is the remnant of the ductus arteriosus?","a":"Ligamentum arteriosum","options":["Ligamentum teres","Ligamentum venosum","Ligamentum arteriosum","Medial umbilical ligament"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"After birth the lungs begin working, so the ductus arteriosus is no longer needed and it closes. It becomes the ligamentum arteriosum, a short fibrous cord between the pulmonary trunk and the aortic arch. The name keeps a piece of the original, which makes the pair easy to match.","notes":["The ligamentum teres is the remnant of the umbilical vein, the round ligament of the liver. It would be the answer if the question asked what the umbilical vein becomes.","The ligamentum venosum is the remnant of the ductus venosus, found on the inferior surface of the liver. Pick it if the question asked what the liver shunt becomes.","Correct. The ductus arteriosus becomes the ligamentum arteriosum.","The medial umbilical ligaments are the remnants of the umbilical arteries on the inner surface of the anterior abdominal wall. Choose them if the question asked what the umbilical arteries become."],"lt":"t-imagefree-structure-recognition","lid":"W3-859","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L04049","dok":2,"q":"Which adult structure is the remnant of the ductus venosus?","a":"Ligamentum venosum","options":["Ligamentum teres","Ligamentum venosum","Fossa ovalis","Ligamentum arteriosum"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The ductus venosus carried fetal blood through the liver and past its tissue. Once the umbilical cord is cut, no blood flows through it and it closes into the ligamentum venosum, a fibrous cord on the inferior surface of the liver. Like the ductus arteriosus, its adult name keeps part of the fetal name.","notes":["The ligamentum teres is the remnant of the umbilical vein, which also ends up on the liver. Choose it if the question asked which liver ligament runs in the free edge of the falciform ligament.","Correct. The ductus venosus becomes the ligamentum venosum.","The fossa ovalis is the remnant of the foramen ovale, a depression in the interatrial septum. It would be the answer if the question asked what the opening between the atria becomes.","The ligamentum arteriosum is the remnant of the ductus arteriosus, lying between the pulmonary trunk and the aorta. Pick it if the question asked what the shunt between the great arteries becomes."],"lt":"t-imagefree-structure-recognition","lid":"W3-860","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L04050","dok":2,"q":"Which adult structure is the remnant of the umbilical vein?","a":"Ligamentum teres","options":["Medial umbilical ligaments","Ligamentum arteriosum","Ligamentum teres","Ligamentum venosum"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"After birth the umbilical vein no longer carries blood from the placenta, so it closes into a fibrous cord called the ligamentum teres, or round ligament of the liver. It runs from the umbilicus to the liver in the free edge of the falciform ligament. Its name does not echo the fetal vessel the way ligamentum venosum and ligamentum arteriosum do, so it has to be learned on its own.","notes":["The medial umbilical ligaments are the remnants of the two umbilical arteries, not the single umbilical vein. They would be the answer if the question asked what the umbilical arteries become.","The ligamentum arteriosum is the remnant of the ductus arteriosus between the pulmonary trunk and the aorta. Choose it if the question asked what the shunt between the great arteries becomes.","Correct. The umbilical vein becomes the ligamentum teres.","The ligamentum venosum is the remnant of the ductus venosus, the shunt that passed through the liver. Pick it if the question asked what the liver shunt becomes."],"lt":"t-imagefree-structure-recognition","lid":"W3-861","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L04100","dok":2,"q":"An atherosclerotic plaque narrows the lumen of an artery. What happens to the tissue downstream of the narrowing?","a":"It receives less blood and oxygen.","options":["It receives more blood because pressure rises","Its blood flow reverses direction","It receives less blood and oxygen","It is unaffected because its veins supply it instead"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Atherosclerosis is a buildup of fatty plaque (an atheroma) within the artery wall. As the plaque grows, it bulges inward and narrows the lumen, the channel the blood flows through. A narrower channel lets less blood through, so the tissue beyond the narrowing receives less blood and less oxygen. Depending on the artery, this shows up as chest pain, leg pain on walking, or a stroke.","notes":["This mixes up pressure with flow. Pressure may build behind a narrowing, but the smaller channel lets less blood through, so the tissue downstream gets less, not more.","Blood in an artery keeps flowing away from the heart even when the vessel narrows; it simply flows in a smaller amount. Backward flow is a problem of failed valves in veins or the heart, as in varicose veins or regurgitation.","Correct. The narrowed lumen lets less blood through, so the tissue beyond it gets less blood and oxygen.","Veins carry blood away from a tissue and back to the heart, so they cannot deliver fresh oxygen-rich blood to it. Students choose this when they forget the direction of flow in veins."],"lt":"t-clinical-application-and-misconceptions","lid":"W3-914","comp":70,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-disorders"},{"id":"L04101","dok":3,"q":"A patient with a slowly growing plaque in a coronary artery suddenly has much worse chest pain, and a clot is found on the plaque. Why did symptoms worsen so suddenly?","a":"The clot can block the narrowed vessel all at once.","options":["The clot can block the narrowed vessel all at once","The artery's valves closed and stopped the flow","The clot dissolved the plaque and widened the vessel","The plaque suddenly turned into an aneurysm"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"A plaque roughens the smooth tunica intima, and a clot, called a thrombus, can form on that rough surface. The plaque grew over years, but a thrombus can form in minutes, and because the lumen was already narrowed, the added clot can block it completely. Blood flow to the tissue beyond stops suddenly, which can cause a heart attack in a coronary artery or a stroke in a brain artery.","notes":["Correct. A thrombus on top of a plaque can close an already narrowed lumen in minutes.","Arteries do not have valves; valves are found in veins and in the heart. This choice comes from mixing up arteries with veins, where failed valves cause varicose veins.","A clot adds material to the lumen; it does not break down the plaque or widen the channel. Choosing this reverses what a thrombus does.","An aneurysm is a bulge in a weakened artery wall that pushes outward and may rupture. It widens the vessel rather than closing it, so pick it for a question about a ballooning wall, not a sudden blockage."],"lt":"t-clinical-application-and-misconceptions","lid":"W3-915","comp":70,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-disorders"},{"id":"L04102","dok":2,"q":"A weakened section of the aortic wall balloons outward. What is this disorder called?","a":"Aneurysm.","options":["Aneurysm","Varicose vein","Embolus","Atherosclerosis"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"An aneurysm is a balloon-like bulge in a weakened artery wall. The weak section can no longer hold its shape against the high pressure inside the artery, so it stretches outward. Aneurysms are most common in the aorta and in the arteries of the brain, and the main danger is that the thinned wall can rupture.","notes":["Correct. An aneurysm is an outward bulge in a weakened artery wall, common in the aorta.","A varicose vein is a superficial vein, usually in the leg, that becomes twisted and swollen when its valves fail. It also bulges, but it is a low-pressure vein, so pick it when the question describes a vein rather than the aorta.","An embolus is a clot or fragment that breaks free and travels through the bloodstream until it lodges in a smaller vessel. Choose it for a question about something traveling and blocking a distant vessel, like a pulmonary embolism.","Atherosclerosis is fatty plaque building up inside the artery wall and narrowing the lumen. It is the answer when the channel narrows; the disorder named for an outward bulge of the wall is an aneurysm."],"lt":"t-clinical-application-and-misconceptions","lid":"W3-916","comp":70,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-disorders"},{"id":"L04103","dok":2,"q":"A patient gets cramping pain in the calves while walking that eases with rest. The arteries of the legs are narrowed by plaque. Which disorder fits?","a":"Peripheral artery disease.","options":["Varicose veins","Peripheral artery disease","Coronary artery disease","Deep vein thrombosis"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Peripheral artery disease is narrowing of the arteries of the limbs, usually the legs, most often by atherosclerotic plaque. At rest the narrowed arteries can deliver enough blood, but during walking the leg muscles need more than can get through. The muscles are starved of blood and hurt, and the pain fades once they rest.","notes":["Varicose veins are swollen, twisted superficial veins caused by failed valves. They involve veins and pooled blood, not narrowed arteries starving the muscles.","Correct. Narrowed limb arteries starve the leg muscles during walking, which causes the pain.","Coronary artery disease is the same narrowing process in the coronary arteries that supply the heart wall. It would be the answer if the pain were in the chest during exertion rather than in the legs.","Deep vein thrombosis is a clot in a deep vein of the leg, a problem on the venous side. Pick it when a question describes a clot in a leg vein that could break free and travel to the lungs."],"lt":"t-clinical-application-and-misconceptions","lid":"W3-917","comp":70,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-disorders"},{"id":"L04105","dok":2,"q":"A clot forms in a deep vein of the calf, breaks free, and lodges in an artery of the lung. What has happened?","a":"A pulmonary embolism from a DVT.","options":["A varicose vein from a failed valve","A pulmonary embolism from a DVT","A stroke from a blocked brain artery","A myocardial infarction from a coronary thrombus"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"A deep vein thrombosis (DVT) is a clot in a deep vein, usually of the leg. If part of it breaks free, it becomes an embolus, a traveling clot, and it follows the normal path of venous return: up the inferior vena cava, into the right atrium and right ventricle, and out the pulmonary trunk into a pulmonary artery. When it lodges there and blocks blood flow to part of the lung, it is called a pulmonary embolism.","notes":["A varicose vein is a swollen superficial vein whose valves have failed, so blood pools in it. Choose it for a bulging leg vein, since it does not involve a clot traveling to another organ.","Correct. A clot from a deep leg vein that lodges in a lung artery is a pulmonary embolism.","A stroke is loss of blood supply to part of the brain. It would be the answer if the clot lodged in a brain artery rather than a lung artery.","A myocardial infarction is death of heart muscle when a coronary artery is blocked. A clot from a leg vein does not reach the coronary arteries by the normal venous route, so pick this only when the blocked vessel feeds the heart wall."],"lt":"t-clinical-application-and-misconceptions","lid":"W3-919","comp":70,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-disorders"},{"id":"L04106","dok":2,"q":"A superficial vein in the leg becomes twisted and bulging because blood flows backward and pools in it. Which structure has failed?","a":"The venous valves.","options":["The capillary endothelium","The venous valves","The precapillary sphincters","The semilunar valves of the heart"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Venous valves are flaps of the tunica intima, common in the veins of the limbs, that let blood move only toward the heart. In the legs, blood must travel upward against gravity at low pressure, so the valves keep it from sliding back down. When they fail, blood flows backward and pools, and the superficial vein stretches into a twisted, swollen varicose vein.","notes":["The capillary endothelium is the single cell layer that forms a capillary wall and allows exchange. Damage to it does not cause backward flow in a vein, so it fits questions about exchange instead.","Correct. Failed venous valves let blood fall back and pool, producing a varicose vein.","Precapillary sphincters are rings of smooth muscle that open or close the entrance to a capillary bed. They would be the answer for a question about directing blood into a tissue, not about backflow in veins.","The semilunar valves are the pulmonary and aortic valves at the exits of the ventricles. They prevent backflow into the heart, so pick them for a question about blood leaking back into a ventricle, not about a leg vein."],"lt":"t-clinical-application-and-misconceptions","lid":"W3-920","comp":70,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-disorders"},{"id":"L04107","dok":2,"q":"Over many years, the valves of a patient's leg veins fail, leading to persistent swelling and skin changes in the lower leg. Which disorder fits?","a":"Chronic venous insufficiency.","options":["Phlebitis","Chronic venous insufficiency","Deep vein thrombosis","Peripheral artery disease"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Chronic venous insufficiency is long-term failure of the valves in the leg veins. Without working valves, blood is not moved efficiently back toward the heart and pools in the lower leg. Over time the pooling leads to persistent swelling and changes in the skin of the lower leg. Chronic signals a long-lasting condition, and insufficiency signals that the valves are no longer doing enough.","notes":["Phlebitis is inflammation of a vein, which causes pain, redness, and tenderness along the vein. It would be the answer for an inflamed, tender vein rather than years of valve failure.","Correct. Long-term valve failure in the leg veins causes swelling and skin changes.","A deep vein thrombosis is a clot in a deep leg vein, a sudden event that can break free and travel to the lungs. Choose it when the question describes a clot, not long-term failing valves.","Peripheral artery disease is narrowing of the leg arteries, which starves the muscles and causes pain on walking. It is an arterial problem, so pick it when too little blood reaches the leg, not when blood pools in it."],"lt":"t-clinical-application-and-misconceptions","lid":"W3-921","comp":70,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-disorders"},{"id":"L04108","dok":2,"q":"The opening in the fetal interatrial septum fails to seal after birth. What is this defect called?","a":"Patent foramen ovale.","options":["Ligamentum arteriosum","Patent foramen ovale","Patent ductus arteriosus","Fossa ovalis"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Before birth, the foramen ovale is an opening in the interatrial septum that lets blood pass straight from the right atrium to the left atrium, bypassing the lungs. Normally it seals after birth and leaves a shallow depression, the fossa ovalis. If it stays open, the defect is called a patent foramen ovale, since patent means open.","notes":["The ligamentum arteriosum is the normal adult cord left when the ductus arteriosus closes. It would be the answer for the remnant between the pulmonary trunk and aorta, not for a defect.","Correct. A foramen ovale that stays open after birth is a patent foramen ovale.","A patent ductus arteriosus is the other common shunt defect, an open vessel between the pulmonary trunk and the aorta. Pick it when the question describes a vessel outside the heart, not an opening in the atrial wall.","The fossa ovalis is the normal shallow depression left when the foramen ovale closes properly. Choose it for the adult remnant, not for an opening that failed to seal."],"lt":"t-clinical-application-and-misconceptions","lid":"W3-922","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L04109","dok":2,"q":"The fetal vessel that connects the pulmonary trunk to the aorta stays open after birth. What is this defect called?","a":"Patent ductus arteriosus.","options":["Ligamentum venosum","Patent foramen ovale","Patent ductus arteriosus","Ligamentum arteriosum"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The ductus arteriosus is a short fetal vessel between the pulmonary trunk and the aorta. Before birth it shunts blood away from the collapsed lungs and into the aorta. It should close after birth and become the ligamentum arteriosum, and when it stays open the defect is called a patent ductus arteriosus.","notes":["The ligamentum venosum is the remnant of the ductus venosus, the fetal shunt through the liver. Choose it for the fibrous cord on the inferior surface of the liver.","A patent foramen ovale is an opening in the interatrial septum that did not seal. Pick it when the leftover connection is between the two atria, inside the heart.","Correct. A ductus arteriosus that stays open after birth is a patent ductus arteriosus.","The ligamentum arteriosum is the normal fibrous cord the ductus arteriosus becomes after it closes. It is the answer for the adult remnant, not for a vessel that stayed open."],"lt":"t-clinical-application-and-misconceptions","lid":"W3-923","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L04139","dok":2,"q":"In a fetus, why does the umbilical vein carry oxygen-rich blood?","a":"It carries blood from the placenta to the fetus.","options":["It carries blood from the fetus to the placenta","It carries blood from the fetal lungs to the heart","It carries blood from the placenta to the fetus","It carries blood from the fetal liver to the heart"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Before birth, the fetal lungs are collapsed and do not exchange gases, so the fetus gets its oxygen from the placenta. The umbilical vein carries blood from the placenta, where it has just picked up oxygen and nutrients, through the umbilical cord to the fetus. It is a vein because it carries blood toward the fetal heart, and it is oxygen-rich because of where it comes from.","notes":["Blood traveling from the fetus to the placenta is carried by the two umbilical arteries, and it is oxygen-poor. Pick this direction for a question about the umbilical arteries.","The fetal lungs are collapsed and not used for gas exchange, so they are not the source of oxygen before birth. After birth, the pulmonary veins would be the answer for oxygen-rich blood coming from the lungs.","Correct. The umbilical vein brings freshly oxygenated blood from the placenta.","Blood leaving the liver does flow on toward the heart, but the liver is not where fetal blood gets its oxygen. The ductus venosus, which lets umbilical blood bypass the liver, would be the answer for a question about that route."],"lt":"t-clinical-application-and-misconceptions","lid":"W3-954","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L04140","dok":2,"q":"In a fetus, why do the umbilical arteries carry oxygen-poor blood?","a":"They carry fetal blood back to the placenta.","options":["They carry blood from the fetal lungs","They carry blood from the placenta to the fetus","They carry blood through the ductus arteriosus","They carry fetal blood back to the placenta"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The two umbilical arteries carry blood away from the fetus, which is why they are called arteries. That blood has already circulated through the fetal body, delivering oxygen and picking up wastes, so it is oxygen-poor. It travels through the umbilical cord to the placenta, where it gives up wastes and picks up a fresh supply of oxygen.","notes":["The fetal lungs are collapsed and not used for gas exchange, and blood does not travel from them to the umbilical cord. Choose the lungs for a question about what the fetal shunts bypass.","Blood from the placenta to the fetus travels in the umbilical vein, which is oxygen-rich. Pick this for a question about the umbilical vein.","The ductus arteriosus is a short vessel shunting blood from the pulmonary trunk into the aorta to bypass the lungs. It would be the answer for a question about the lung bypass, not the vessels in the umbilical cord.","Correct. The umbilical arteries return used, oxygen-poor fetal blood to the placenta."],"lt":"t-clinical-application-and-misconceptions","lid":"W3-955","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L04141","dok":2,"q":"Why do the fetal shunts close after birth?","a":"The lungs inflate and the placental supply ends.","options":["The coronary arteries open for the first time","The heart begins beating for the first time","The lungs inflate and the placental supply ends","The umbilical vein reverses its direction of flow"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Before birth, oxygen comes from the placenta and the collapsed lungs are bypassed, so the shunts route blood around the lungs and liver. At birth the lungs inflate and begin exchanging gases, and cutting the umbilical cord ends the placental supply. The detours are no longer needed, so the ductus venosus, foramen ovale, and ductus arteriosus close, leaving ligaments or, for the foramen ovale, the fossa ovalis.","notes":["The coronary arteries supply the fetal heart wall before birth, just as they do after. Nothing about them changes at birth, so this choice confuses the heart's own supply with the fetal shunts.","The fetal heart beats long before birth; it is what pushes blood through the shunts. Students choose this when they assume the heart starts working at birth.","Correct. Once the lungs work and the placenta is cut off, the detours are no longer needed.","The umbilical vein does not reverse; it stops carrying blood once the cord is cut and later becomes the ligamentum teres of the liver. Pick the umbilical vein for a question about which remnant runs in the falciform ligament."],"lt":"t-clinical-application-and-misconceptions","lid":"W3-956","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L04144","dok":2,"q":"A clot breaks free from a deep vein in the leg. Which route does it follow to become a pulmonary embolism?","a":"Inferior vena cava, right atrium, right ventricle, pulmonary trunk.","options":["Inferior vena cava, left atrium, left ventricle, aorta","Superior vena cava, right atrium, right ventricle, aorta","Inferior vena cava, right atrium, left atrium, pulmonary veins","Inferior vena cava, right atrium, right ventricle, pulmonary trunk"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"A clot from a leg vein follows the normal path of venous return. It travels up the inferior vena cava into the right atrium, through the tricuspid valve into the right ventricle, and out through the pulmonary valve into the pulmonary trunk. The pulmonary arteries branch into smaller and smaller vessels inside the lung, so the clot eventually lodges in one and blocks blood flow to part of the lung. That blockage is a pulmonary embolism.","notes":["The inferior vena cava empties into the right atrium, not the left. A clot in the left heart would be pumped out the aorta to the body, which is how a clot can cause a stroke.","The superior vena cava drains the head, neck, and upper limbs, not the legs, and the right ventricle pumps into the pulmonary trunk, not the aorta. Students choose this when they mix up which great vessel leaves each ventricle.","The pulmonary veins carry blood from the lungs to the left atrium, so a clot cannot travel through them toward the lungs. Pick the pulmonary veins for a question about where oxygen-rich blood enters the heart.","Correct. The clot rides normal venous return through the right heart into the pulmonary arteries."],"lt":"t-clinical-application-and-misconceptions","lid":"W3-959","comp":69,"competencyId":"bvn-circulatory-routes"},{"id":"L04149","dok":2,"q":"What makes a portal system, such as the hepatic portal system, different from the usual circulatory route?","a":"Blood passes through two capillary beds in a row.","options":["Blood flows from the right heart to the lungs and back","Blood skips the capillaries and flows from artery to vein","Two arteries join so blood has an alternate route","Blood passes through two capillary beds in a row"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"In most of the body, blood goes from an artery to one capillary bed and then into veins headed back to the heart. In a portal system, blood passes through a capillary bed, collects into a portal vein, and then passes through a second capillary bed before returning to the heart. In the hepatic portal system, blood from the digestive organs goes to the liver's capillaries next, so the liver can process absorbed nutrients and remove harmful substances before that blood reaches the rest of the body.","notes":["Right heart to lungs to left heart is the pulmonary circuit. It is the answer for a question about the loop that carries blood to the lungs for gas exchange.","A direct artery-to-vein connection that skips the capillaries is an arteriovenous anastomosis. Pick it for a question about blood bypassing a capillary bed.","Two vessels joining to give an alternate route is an anastomosis. Choose it for a question about what protects tissue when one vessel is blocked.","Correct. A portal system sends blood through two capillary beds in series before it returns to the heart."],"lt":"t-clinical-application-and-misconceptions","lid":"W3-964","comp":69,"competencyId":"bvn-circulatory-routes"},{"id":"L04150","dok":2,"q":"Why does an anastomosis between two arteries help protect the tissue they supply?","a":"It gives blood a second route if one is blocked.","options":["It lets blood skip the capillaries entirely","It keeps blood from flowing backward","It sends blood through two capillary beds in a row","It gives blood a second route if one is blocked"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"An anastomosis is a place where two vessels join. When two arteries supplying the same region connect, blood can reach the tissue through either one. If one path is blocked, blood can still arrive by the other route, which protects the tissue from losing its blood supply. The cerebral arterial circle at the base of the brain is a well-known example.","notes":["Skipping the capillaries describes an arteriovenous anastomosis, a direct artery-to-vein connection. It routes blood away from the tissue's capillaries, so it would not protect the tissue's supply.","Preventing backward flow is the job of valves, which are found in veins and the heart. Choose valves for a question about one-way flow.","Passing through two capillary beds in a row describes a portal system, like the hepatic portal system. Pick it for a question about blood going from the digestive organs to the liver.","Correct. Joined arteries let blood reach the tissue by a second path."],"lt":"t-clinical-application-and-misconceptions","lid":"W3-965","comp":69,"competencyId":"bvn-circulatory-routes"},{"id":"L04158","dok":2,"q":"Why does the ductus arteriosus route fetal blood around the lungs?","a":"The fetal lungs are collapsed and not yet in use.","options":["The pulmonary trunk is too narrow to carry blood","The fetal lungs are collapsed and not yet in use","The fetal liver needs the blood instead","The fetal lungs are already full of oxygen-rich blood"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Before birth, the fetus gets its oxygen from the placenta, and its lungs are collapsed and not used for gas exchange. Sending all the blood through them would be wasted effort. The ductus arteriosus is a short vessel between the pulmonary trunk and the aorta, so it shunts most of the blood leaving the right ventricle into the aorta and out to the body instead.","notes":["The pulmonary trunk is a large vessel, and it actually feeds the ductus arteriosus. Students choose this when they think the shunt exists because of a blocked vessel rather than unused lungs.","Correct. With the lungs not yet working, blood is shunted past them into the aorta.","The ductus arteriosus empties into the aorta, not the liver. A question about blood bypassing the liver would point to the ductus venosus instead.","The fetal lungs do not oxygenate blood before birth; the placenta does. This choice comes from assuming the fetus breathes the way it will after birth."],"lt":"t-clinical-application-and-misconceptions","lid":"W3-974","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L04159","dok":2,"q":"Why does the ductus venosus route much fetal blood around the liver?","a":"The placenta handles most filtering before birth.","options":["The ductus venosus carries blood to the lungs","The fetal liver has no blood supply","The fetal liver is collapsed like the lungs","The placenta handles most filtering before birth"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"In the fetus, oxygen-rich blood arrives from the placenta in the umbilical vein. Because the placenta handles much of the filtering that the liver will do after birth, most of that blood does not need to pass through the liver. The ductus venosus is a shunt through the liver that lets this blood flow straight toward the inferior vena cava and on to the heart.","notes":["The ductus venosus leads toward the inferior vena cava, not the lungs. The shunts that bypass the lungs are the foramen ovale and the ductus arteriosus.","The fetal liver does receive blood; only part of the umbilical blood bypasses it. Students choose this when they think bypassed means completely cut off.","Only the fetal lungs are collapsed; the liver is a working organ before birth. This choice borrows the reason the lungs are bypassed and applies it to the wrong organ.","Correct. With the placenta doing much of the filtering, most umbilical blood can skip the liver."],"lt":"t-clinical-application-and-misconceptions","lid":"W3-975","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L04160","dok":2,"q":"Why can atherosclerosis cause coronary artery disease, peripheral artery disease, or a stroke?","a":"The same plaque forms in different arteries.","options":["Each disease comes from a different kind of plaque","The same plaque forms in different arteries","Plaques form in veins and travel to each artery","Plaque forms in the heart and spreads outward"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Atherosclerosis is the buildup of fatty plaque within artery walls, and it can occur in arteries throughout the body. The disease a patient develops depends on which artery the plaque narrows. In the coronary arteries it causes coronary artery disease and heart attacks, in a brain artery it can cause a stroke, and in the leg arteries it causes peripheral artery disease.","notes":["The same process, fatty deposits in the artery wall, underlies all three diseases. Only the location differs, so this choice overcomplicates what is one disease in different places.","Correct. One plaque process produces different diseases depending on which artery it affects.","Atherosclerosis is a disease of arteries, not veins. Clots that start in veins, like a deep vein thrombosis, travel to the lungs and cause a pulmonary embolism instead.","Plaque forms locally in the wall of each affected artery; it does not start in the heart and spread. Students choose this when they picture the disease as one growing mass."],"lt":"t-clinical-application-and-misconceptions","lid":"W3-976","comp":70,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-disorders"},{"id":"L04161","dok":2,"q":"Why is an aneurysm in a large artery such as the aorta so dangerous?","a":"The weak wall can burst under high pressure.","options":["The weak wall can burst under high pressure","The bulge makes the wall too thick to stretch","The bulge sends clots into the veins","The bulge blocks the artery completely"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"An aneurysm is a balloon-like bulge in a weakened section of artery wall. Arteries carry blood under high pressure, and the thinned, stretched wall of the bulge has less and less strength to resist it. If the wall gives way, the aneurysm ruptures and blood escapes rapidly, which can be fatal in a large artery like the aorta or in an artery of the brain.","notes":["Correct. The thinned wall of an aneurysm can rupture under arterial pressure.","An aneurysm thins and stretches the wall rather than thickening it. A thickened, stiffened artery wall describes arteriosclerosis instead.","Material leaving an artery travels onward in arteries, away from the heart, not into the veins. Students choose this when they mix up an aneurysm with a deep vein thrombosis.","An aneurysm widens the vessel outward rather than closing it. Complete blockage describes a thrombus forming on a plaque, which can trigger a heart attack or stroke."],"lt":"t-clinical-application-and-misconceptions","lid":"W3-977","comp":70,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-disorders"},{"id":"L04162","dok":2,"q":"Why is a stroke treated as a time-sensitive emergency?","a":"Brain tissue starts dying within minutes.","options":["Brain tissue starts dying within minutes","The heart stops beating soon after a stroke","Brain tissue can survive for days without blood","The blocked artery always reopens within an hour"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"A stroke is a loss of blood supply to part of the brain, caused by a blocked or burst artery. Brain tissue depends on a constant flow of oxygen-rich blood, and it begins to die within minutes once that supply is lost. The faster blood flow is restored, the less brain tissue is lost, which is why every minute counts.","notes":["Correct. Brain tissue starts to die within minutes of losing its blood supply.","A stroke affects the brain's blood supply, not the heart's pumping. Pick the heart for a question about ventricular fibrillation or a myocardial infarction.","Brain tissue has almost no tolerance for lost blood supply, so it cannot wait days. This choice underestimates how quickly brain cells are damaged.","A blocked brain artery does not reliably reopen on its own, which is why treatment aims to restore flow quickly. Students choose this when they confuse a stroke with a brief, passing symptom."],"lt":"t-clinical-application-and-misconceptions","lid":"W3-978","comp":70,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-disorders"},{"id":"L04180","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly describes how the heart, blood vessels, and lungs work together to deliver oxygen to the body?","a":"The right heart sends blood to the lungs, and the left heart pumps it to the body.","options":["The lungs add oxygen in the arteries, and the capillaries pump it to the body","The left heart sends blood to the lungs, and the right heart pumps it to the body","The right heart sends blood to the lungs, and the left heart pumps it to the body","The heart adds oxygen to the blood, and the lungs pump it through the vessels"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Oxygen-poor blood returning from the body enters the right atrium and is pumped by the right ventricle through the pulmonary trunk to the lungs. In the lungs, blood in the pulmonary capillaries picks up oxygen from the air in the alveoli and returns through the pulmonary veins to the left atrium. The left ventricle then pumps this oxygen-rich blood into the aorta, and the arteries carry it to capillaries throughout the body, where oxygen moves into the tissues.","notes":["Oxygen is loaded in the pulmonary capillaries around the alveoli, not in arteries, and capillaries do not pump. Pick capillaries for a question about where exchange with the tissues happens.","This reverses the two sides. The right ventricle drives the pulmonary circuit to the lungs, and the left ventricle, with its thicker wall, drives the systemic circuit to the body.","Correct. The right side drives the pulmonary circuit and the left side drives the systemic circuit.","The heart is a pump and does not add oxygen to blood; oxygen is picked up at the alveoli. Students choose this when they mix up the job of the pump with the job of the exchange surface."],"lt":"t-clinical-application-and-misconceptions","lid":"W3-998","comp":69,"competencyId":"bvn-circulatory-routes"},{"id":"L04250","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares the pulmonary and systemic circuits?","a":"The pulmonary circuit carries blood to the lungs and back to the left atrium; the systemic circuit carries blood to the body and back to the right atrium.","options":["Pulmonary supplies the heart wall; systemic supplies the lungs and the rest of the body","Pulmonary goes to the lungs and returns to the right atrium; systemic goes to the body and returns to the left atrium","Pulmonary goes to the lungs and returns to the left atrium; systemic goes to the body and returns to the right atrium","Pulmonary goes to the body and returns to the left atrium; systemic goes to the lungs and returns to the right atrium"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The pulmonary circuit starts at the right ventricle, carries blood to the lungs to pick up oxygen, and returns it through the pulmonary veins to the left atrium. The systemic circuit starts at the left ventricle, carries blood through the aorta to the body tissues, and returns it through the venae cavae to the right atrium. Each circuit ends on the opposite side of the heart from where it began, which links the two into one continuous loop.","notes":["The heart wall is supplied by the coronary arteries, which branch from the aorta and are part of the systemic circuit. Students pick this when they treat the coronary vessels as a separate circuit; choose a coronary answer for a question about how the myocardium gets its blood.","This swaps the return chambers. Blood coming back from the lungs enters the left atrium so it can be pumped out to the body, and blood from the body enters the right atrium so it can be sent to the lungs.","Correct. Each circuit returns to the side of the heart that pumps the other circuit.","This swaps where the two circuits go. Pulmonary refers to the lungs, so the pulmonary circuit is the one that carries blood to them."],"lt":"t-bvn-circulatory-routes","lid":"bvn-circulatory-routes-r3","comp":69,"competencyId":"bvn-circulatory-routes"},{"id":"L04251","dok":2,"q":"How do arterial disorders differ from venous disorders in what tends to go wrong?","a":"Arterial disorders tend to narrow the channel or weaken the wall; venous disorders tend to involve failed valves and pooled or clotted blood.","options":["Arterial disorders affect only the tunica externa; venous disorders affect only the tunica intima","Arterial disorders involve failed valves and pooled blood; venous disorders narrow or weaken the wall","Arterial disorders come from low pressure; venous disorders come from high pressure","Arterial disorders narrow or weaken the wall; venous disorders involve failed valves and pooled or clotted blood"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Arteries carry blood under high pressure, so their disorders tend to narrow the channel, as in atherosclerosis, or weaken the wall until it bulges, as in an aneurysm. Veins carry blood under low pressure and depend on valves, so their disorders tend to involve failed valves and pooled blood, as in varicose veins, or clots, as in deep vein thrombosis. The wall each vessel has, and the pressure it carries, predict how it tends to fail.","notes":["Atherosclerosis begins with injury to the tunica intima of an artery, so arterial disease is not limited to the outer layer. Students choose this when they try to tie each group of disorders to a single tunic.","This swaps the two groups. Valves are a feature of veins, especially in the limbs, so valve failure and pooling belong to venous disorders like varicose veins.","This reverses the pressures. Arteries are the high-pressure vessels, and chronically high arterial pressure (hypertension) is itself an arterial disorder that strains vessel walls.","Correct. High-pressure arteries narrow or weaken, and low-pressure veins pool or clot."],"lt":"t-bvn-vessel-disorders","lid":"bvn-vessel-disorders-r2","comp":70,"competencyId":"bvn-vessel-disorders"},{"id":"L04252","dok":2,"q":"A deep vein thrombosis (a clot in a deep leg vein) breaks free. Which route does the clot follow to become a pulmonary embolism?","a":"Up the inferior vena cava, through the right atrium and right ventricle, out the pulmonary trunk, and into a lung artery.","options":["Leg artery, aorta, left ventricle, left atrium, pulmonary veins, lung artery","Leg vein, inferior vena cava, right atrium, right ventricle, aorta, brain artery","Leg vein, inferior vena cava, right atrium, right ventricle, pulmonary trunk, lung artery","Leg vein, inferior vena cava, left atrium, left ventricle, aorta, lung artery"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"A clot that breaks free from a deep leg vein simply follows normal venous return. It travels up the inferior vena cava into the right atrium, through the right ventricle, and out the pulmonary trunk into the pulmonary arteries. As those arteries branch and narrow, the clot lodges in one, blocking blood flow to part of the lung.","notes":["A deep vein thrombosis forms in a vein, not an artery, and blood does not flow backward from the aorta into the left ventricle. This route runs against the normal direction of flow.","The right ventricle pumps into the pulmonary trunk, not the aorta, so a clot from a leg vein heads to the lungs. Pick a route through the left heart and aorta for a question about how a clot reaches a brain artery and causes a stroke.","Correct. The clot follows the normal route of venous blood back through the right heart to the lungs.","Venous blood from the body returns to the right side of the heart, not the left. The left atrium receives only the pulmonary veins, so a clot from a leg vein cannot reach it first."],"lt":"t-bvn-vessel-disorders","lid":"bvn-vessel-disorders-r3","comp":69,"competencyId":"bvn-circulatory-routes"},{"id":"L04253","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly matches each fetal shunt to the organ it lets blood bypass?","a":"The ductus venosus bypasses the liver; the foramen ovale and ductus arteriosus bypass the lungs.","options":["Ductus venosus bypasses the liver; foramen ovale and ductus arteriosus bypass the lungs","Ductus arteriosus bypasses the liver; ductus venosus and foramen ovale bypass the lungs","Ductus venosus bypasses the lungs; foramen ovale and ductus arteriosus bypass the liver","All three shunts bypass the lungs, since the fetal lungs are not yet in use"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Before birth, the placenta handles gas exchange and much of the filtering, so the fetal lungs and liver are largely bypassed. The ductus venosus passes through the liver and lets most blood from the umbilical vein skip it on the way to the inferior vena cava. The foramen ovale (an opening between the atria) and the ductus arteriosus (a vessel from the pulmonary trunk to the aorta) both route blood around the lungs.","notes":["Correct. One shunt skips the liver and two skip the lungs.","The ductus arteriosus connects the pulmonary trunk to the aorta, so it bypasses the lungs, not the liver. Students choose this when they mix up the two ductus names; venosus goes with the venous side and the liver.","This swaps the two organs. The ductus venosus runs through the fetal liver toward the inferior vena cava, and the other two shunts sit at the heart and great vessels, where they route blood around the lungs.","Two shunts do bypass the lungs, but the ductus venosus bypasses the liver. Students pick this when they focus on the collapsed fetal lungs and forget that the liver is also largely skipped."],"lt":"t-bvn-fetal-remnants","lid":"bvn-fetal-remnants-r2","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"},{"id":"L04254","dok":2,"q":"Which pairing of a fetal structure with its adult remnant is correct?","a":"The ductus arteriosus becomes the ligamentum arteriosum (and the foramen ovale becomes the fossa ovalis).","options":["Umbilical vein becomes the medial umbilical ligaments","Foramen ovale becomes the ligamentum venosum","Ductus arteriosus becomes the ligamentum arteriosum","Ductus venosus becomes the fossa ovalis"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"After birth, each fetal shunt closes and leaves a remnant whose name often echoes the original. The ductus arteriosus, which linked the pulmonary trunk to the aorta, becomes the ligamentum arteriosum, a short cord between those same two vessels. The foramen ovale, which was a hole rather than a vessel, becomes a depression instead of a cord: the fossa ovalis in the interatrial septum.","notes":["The umbilical vein becomes the ligamentum teres, the round ligament of the liver. The medial umbilical ligaments are the remnants of the two umbilical arteries, on the inner surface of the anterior abdominal wall.","The foramen ovale becomes the fossa ovalis, a depression in the interatrial septum. The ligamentum venosum is the remnant of the ductus venosus; pick it for the fibrous cord on the inferior surface of the liver.","Correct. The ductus arteriosus closes into the ligamentum arteriosum between the pulmonary trunk and the aorta.","The ductus venosus becomes the ligamentum venosum on the liver. The fossa ovalis is the remnant of the foramen ovale, so choose it for the depression in the wall between the atria."],"lt":"t-bvn-fetal-remnants","lid":"bvn-fetal-remnants-r3","comp":71,"competencyId":"bvn-fetal-remnants"}]},{"id":"m3-forearm-anterior","title":"Anterior forearm muscles","summary":"superficial and deep flexor compartment muscles, pronators","mw":"2026-10-14","tr":"2026-10-08","cards":[{"id":"L04332","dok":1,"q":"What is the general action of the muscles in the anterior compartment of the forearm?","a":"Flexion of the wrist and fingers, along with pronation of the forearm.","options":["Extending the wrist and fingers and supinating the forearm","Flexing the wrist and fingers and pronating the forearm","Flexing the elbow and supinating the forearm","Extending the elbow and abducting the arm"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The anterior forearm compartment holds the flexors and pronators. Most of its superficial muscles share a common origin on the medial epicondyle of the humerus and flex the wrist and fingers, while pronator teres and pronator quadratus turn the palm down. Knowing the compartment lets you predict the action of a muscle before you have learned it individually.","notes":["Extending the wrist and fingers and supinating the forearm is the job of the posterior forearm compartment. It would be the answer for the muscles that share the lateral epicondyle origin.","Correct. The anterior forearm flexes the wrist and fingers and pronates the forearm.","Flexing the elbow and supinating the forearm is the job of biceps brachii in the anterior arm, not the anterior forearm. Pick this if the question asked about the anterior compartment of the arm.","Extending the elbow is the job of the posterior arm (triceps brachii), and abducting the arm is done at the shoulder by the deltoid and supraspinatus. Neither belongs to the forearm compartments."],"lt":"t-w4-lab-forearm-compartments","lid":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments-r1","comp":86,"competencyId":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments"},{"id":"N-m3-forearm-anterior-1","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares where flexor digitorum superficialis and flexor digitorum profundus insert on the fingers?","a":"The superficialis inserts on the middle phalanges, and the profundus inserts on the distal phalanges.","options":["The superficialis inserts on the distal phalanges, and the profundus inserts on the middle phalanges","Both insert on the distal phalanges, with the profundus lying deeper","The superficialis inserts on the middle phalanges, and the profundus inserts on the distal phalanges","Both insert on the proximal phalanges, with the superficialis lying more superficial"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Flexor digitorum superficialis lies in a more superficial layer and stops at the middle phalanges of digits 2 to 5, so it flexes the proximal interphalangeal joints. Flexor digitorum profundus lies deep to it, and its tendons pass through split tendons of the superficialis to reach the distal phalanges, so it flexes the distal interphalangeal joints. Clinicians test them separately by asking the patient to bend one finger joint at a time.","notes":["This swaps the two insertions. The deeper muscle, profundus, actually travels farther out to the fingertips.","Only the profundus reaches the distal phalanges; the superficialis stops one bone short at the middle phalanges. This is why the profundus alone can bend the fingertip joint.","Correct. Superficialis reaches the middle phalanges and profundus continues on to the distal phalanges.","Neither muscle inserts on the proximal phalanges. Students pick this when they assume the names superficialis and profundus describe depth only and say nothing about how far each tendon travels."],"comp":86,"competencyId":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments"},{"id":"N-m3-forearm-anterior-1-new-m3-forearm-hand","dok":1,"q":"Most of the superficial muscles of the anterior forearm share one common flexor origin. Which bony landmark is it?","a":"The medial epicondyle of the humerus.","options":["Coronoid process of the ulna","Medial epicondyle of the humerus","Radial tuberosity","Lateral epicondyle of the humerus"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The medial epicondyle is the knob on the medial side of the distal humerus, and it serves as the common flexor origin. Pronator teres, flexor carpi radialis, palmaris longus and flexor carpi ulnaris all arise from it through a shared tendon, which is why the anterior forearm muscles fan out from the medial side of the elbow. Irritation of this tendon is called golfer's elbow (medial epicondylitis).","notes":["The coronoid process is the anterior lip of the ulna's trochlear notch. It gives a small second head to pronator teres, but it is better known as the insertion of brachialis, so choose it if the question asked where brachialis attaches.","Correct. The medial epicondyle is the common flexor origin for the superficial anterior forearm muscles.","The radial tuberosity is a bump on the proximal radius where the biceps brachii tendon inserts. Pick it if the question asked for the insertion of biceps brachii.","The lateral epicondyle is the knob on the opposite side of the distal humerus. It is the common extensor origin for the superficial posterior forearm muscles, so pick it if the question asked where the wrist and finger extensors begin."],"comp":86,"competencyId":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments"},{"id":"N-m3-forearm-anterior-2","dok":1,"q":"Which anterior forearm muscle inserts on the palmar aponeurosis, the sheet of deep fascia in the palm?","a":"Palmaris longus.","options":["Pronator teres","Flexor carpi radialis","Palmaris longus","Flexor carpi ulnaris"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Palmaris longus is a small, slender muscle of the superficial layer that arises from the medial epicondyle. Its long, thin tendon passes in front of the flexor retinaculum and spreads out into the palmar aponeurosis, so when it contracts it tightens that sheet and weakly assists wrist flexion. The name says the same thing: palmaris means palm and longus means long.","notes":["Pronator teres runs from the medial epicondyle to the lateral mid-shaft of the radius and never reaches the hand. It would be right if the question asked which round muscle pronates the forearm near the elbow.","Flexor carpi radialis inserts on the bases of metacarpals 2 and 3 on the palmar side. Choose it if the question asked which superficial flexor bends the wrist and pulls it toward the thumb side.","Correct. The palmaris longus tendon continues into the palmar aponeurosis.","Flexor carpi ulnaris inserts on the pisiform, hamate and metacarpal 5 on the medial side of the wrist. Pick it if the question asked which flexor bends the wrist and pulls it toward the little finger side."],"comp":86,"competencyId":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments"},{"id":"N-m3-forearm-anterior-3","dok":1,"q":"Which muscle runs straight across the front of the distal forearm, from the ulna to the radius, and is the prime mover of pronation?","a":"Pronator quadratus.","options":["Supinator","Pronator teres","Brachioradialis","Pronator quadratus"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Pronator quadratus is a flat, four-sided muscle in the deep layer of the anterior forearm, lying right on the bones just above the wrist. Its fibers run from the distal anterior ulna across to the distal anterior radius, so when it shortens it pulls the radius across the front of the ulna and turns the palm to face posteriorly. The course notes name it the prime mover of pronation, with pronator teres assisting.","notes":["Supinator wraps around the proximal radius in the deep posterior forearm and does the opposite movement, turning the palm to face anteriorly. Choose it if the question asked which forearm muscle supinates.","Pronator teres also pronates, but it lies in the proximal forearm and runs diagonally from the medial epicondyle to the lateral mid-shaft of the radius. Pick it if the question described the round pronator near the elbow.","Brachioradialis runs from the lateral supracondylar ridge of the humerus to the styloid process of the radius along the thumb side of the forearm. Pick it if the question asked which muscle flexes the elbow most strongly with the forearm in mid-position.","Correct. Pronator quadratus runs horizontally between the distal ulna and radius and is the prime mover of pronation."],"comp":86,"competencyId":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments"},{"id":"N-m3-forearm-anterior-4","dok":1,"q":"Where does pronator teres insert?","a":"The lateral mid-shaft of the radius.","options":["Styloid process of the radius","Radial tuberosity","Coronoid process of the ulna","Lateral mid-shaft of the radius"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Pronator teres arises from the medial epicondyle and the coronoid process of the ulna, then crosses the front of the forearm diagonally to insert on the lateral surface of the radius about halfway down its shaft. Because it pulls the lateral radius toward the medial elbow, it rolls the radius over the ulna and pronates the forearm. Crossing the front of the elbow also lets it assist elbow flexion.","notes":["The styloid process of the radius is the pointed tip of the radius at the wrist, where brachioradialis inserts. Pick it if the question asked for the insertion of brachioradialis.","The radial tuberosity is the bump on the proximal radius where biceps brachii inserts. Choose it if the question asked where the biceps tendon attaches.","The coronoid process is one of the two origins of pronator teres, not its insertion, so this choice reverses the muscle's attachments. It is also where brachialis inserts, so pick it if the question asked for the insertion of brachialis.","Correct. Pronator teres inserts on the lateral surface of the radius at mid-shaft."],"comp":86,"competencyId":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments"},{"id":"N-m3-forearm-anterior-5","dok":1,"q":"Where does flexor digitorum superficialis insert?","a":"The middle phalanges of digits 2 to 5.","options":["Middle phalanges of digits 2 to 5","Proximal phalanges of digits 2 to 5","Bases of metacarpals 2 and 3","Distal phalanges of digits 2 to 5"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Flexor digitorum superficialis sends four tendons through the carpal tunnel, one to each finger from the index to the little finger. Each tendon splits near the base of the finger and attaches to the sides of the middle phalanx, so the muscle bends the fingers at the proximal interphalangeal joints (the middle knuckles). It cannot bend the fingertips because it stops before the distal phalanx.","notes":["Correct. Flexor digitorum superficialis inserts on the middle phalanges of digits 2 to 5.","No long forearm flexor ends on the proximal phalanges of the fingers, so this choice usually comes from guessing that the superficial muscle stops at the first bone. The long finger flexors pass the proximal phalanx and insert farther out on the middle and distal phalanges.","The bases of metacarpals 2 and 3 receive the tendon of flexor carpi radialis. Choose them if the question asked where the radial wrist flexor inserts.","The distal phalanges of digits 2 to 5 are where flexor digitorum profundus inserts. Pick this if the question asked about the deep finger flexor that bends the fingertips."],"comp":86,"competencyId":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments"},{"id":"N-m3-forearm-anterior-6","dok":1,"q":"Where does flexor digitorum profundus insert?","a":"The distal phalanges of digits 2 to 5.","options":["Middle phalanges of digits 2 to 5","Distal phalanx of the thumb","Pisiform, hamate and metacarpal 5","Distal phalanges of digits 2 to 5"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Flexor digitorum profundus is the deep finger flexor. Its four tendons pass through the carpal tunnel beneath the tendons of flexor digitorum superficialis, slip through the split in each superficialis tendon, and attach to the base of the distal phalanx of digits 2 to 5. That makes it the only muscle that bends the fingertips at the distal interphalangeal joints.","notes":["The middle phalanges of digits 2 to 5 are where flexor digitorum superficialis inserts. Pick this if the question asked about the superficial finger flexor that bends the middle knuckles.","The distal phalanx of the thumb receives flexor pollicis longus, the long thumb flexor. Choose it if the question asked which deep anterior muscle bends the tip of the thumb.","The pisiform, hamate and metacarpal 5 are the insertion of flexor carpi ulnaris on the medial side of the wrist. Pick them if the question asked which muscle flexes and adducts the wrist.","Correct. Flexor digitorum profundus inserts on the distal phalanges of digits 2 to 5."],"comp":86,"competencyId":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments"},{"id":"N-m3-forearm-anterior-7","dok":1,"q":"Where does flexor pollicis longus insert?","a":"The distal phalanx of the thumb.","options":["Distal phalanx of the thumb","Base of metacarpal 2","Base of metacarpal 1","Proximal phalanx of the thumb"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Flexor pollicis longus arises from the anterior radius and interosseous membrane in the deep layer of the anterior forearm. Its tendon runs through the carpal tunnel to the base of the distal phalanx of the thumb, so it bends the tip of the thumb at the interphalangeal joint. The name gives this away: flexor for the action, pollicis for the thumb, and longus because it is the long thumb flexor that starts in the forearm.","notes":["Correct. Flexor pollicis longus inserts on the distal phalanx of the thumb.","The base of metacarpal 2 receives flexor carpi radialis on the palmar side and extensor carpi radialis longus on the dorsal side. Pick it if the question asked about a radial wrist muscle rather than a thumb muscle.","The base of metacarpal 1 is where abductor pollicis longus inserts, on the thumb side of the back of the wrist. Pick it if the question asked which deep posterior forearm muscle moves the thumb away from the palm.","The proximal phalanx of the thumb receives extensor pollicis brevis from the posterior forearm. Choose it if the question asked which muscle extends the thumb at its knuckle (the metacarpophalangeal joint)."],"comp":86,"competencyId":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments"},{"id":"N-m3-forearm-anterior-8","dok":1,"q":"Where does flexor carpi ulnaris insert?","a":"The pisiform, hamate and metacarpal 5.","options":["Base of metacarpal 1","Pisiform, hamate and metacarpal 5","Bases of metacarpals 2 and 3","Palmar aponeurosis"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Flexor carpi ulnaris is the most medial muscle of the superficial anterior forearm. It arises from the medial epicondyle and the olecranon, runs down the ulnar (little finger) side of the forearm, and inserts on the pisiform, the hook of the hamate and the base of metacarpal 5. Pulling on the medial side of the wrist lets it flex the wrist and adduct it toward the little finger side.","notes":["The base of metacarpal 1 is the insertion of abductor pollicis longus from the posterior forearm. Choose it if the question asked which muscle abducts the thumb from the forearm.","Correct. Flexor carpi ulnaris inserts on the pisiform, hamate and metacarpal 5.","The bases of metacarpals 2 and 3 receive flexor carpi radialis, the wrist flexor on the thumb side. Pick them if the question named the radial wrist flexor.","The palmar aponeurosis is the insertion of palmaris longus, the slender superficial muscle in the middle of the forearm. Pick it if the question asked which muscle tenses the fascia of the palm."],"comp":86,"competencyId":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments"},{"id":"N-m3-forearm-anterior-9","dok":1,"q":"What does flexor carpi radialis do at the wrist?","a":"It flexes and abducts the wrist.","options":["Flexes and adducts the wrist","Extends and adducts the wrist","Extends and abducts the wrist","Flexes and abducts the wrist"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The name spells out the action. Flexor means it bends the joint, carpi means the wrist, and radialis places it on the radial (thumb) side. Its tendon crosses the front of the wrist on that side and inserts on the bases of metacarpals 2 and 3, so it flexes the wrist and pulls the hand toward the thumb side, which is abduction (also called radial deviation).","notes":["Flexing and adducting the wrist is the job of flexor carpi ulnaris, which crosses the wrist on the little finger side. Pick this if the question named the ulnar wrist flexor.","Extending and adducting the wrist is the action of extensor carpi ulnaris, the extensor on the little finger side of the back of the forearm. Pick it if the question named that muscle.","Extending and abducting the wrist is the action of extensor carpi radialis longus and brevis on the back of the forearm. Choose it if the question named a radial wrist extensor.","Correct. Flexor carpi radialis flexes the wrist and abducts it toward the thumb side."],"comp":86,"competencyId":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments"},{"id":"N-m3-forearm-anterior-10","dok":1,"q":"Which three muscles make up the deep layer of the anterior forearm?","a":"Flexor digitorum profundus, flexor pollicis longus and pronator quadratus.","options":["Pronator teres, flexor carpi radialis, palmaris longus","Supinator, abductor pollicis longus, extensor pollicis longus","Flexor digitorum profundus, flexor pollicis longus, pronator quadratus","Flexor digitorum superficialis, flexor carpi ulnaris, pronator teres"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The deep layer of the anterior forearm lies right against the radius, ulna and interosseous membrane. Flexor digitorum profundus covers the ulnar side, flexor pollicis longus covers the radial side, and pronator quadratus lies under both of them just above the wrist. These deep muscles arise from the bones and the membrane rather than from the medial epicondyle.","notes":["Pronator teres, flexor carpi radialis and palmaris longus are superficial muscles that arise from the medial epicondyle. Pick this group if the question asked which muscles share the common flexor origin.","Supinator, abductor pollicis longus and extensor pollicis longus belong to the deep layer of the posterior forearm. Choose them if the question asked about deep muscles on the back of the forearm.","Correct. Flexor digitorum profundus, flexor pollicis longus and pronator quadratus form the deep anterior layer.","None of these three lie against the bones. Flexor digitorum superficialis sits on top of the deep finger flexor, and flexor carpi ulnaris and pronator teres arise from the medial epicondyle, so this group fits a question about muscles lying superficial to flexor digitorum profundus."],"comp":86,"competencyId":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments"},{"id":"N-m3-forearm-anterior-11","dok":1,"q":"In a forearm muscle name such as flexor carpi ulnaris, what does the word carpi refer to?","a":"The wrist.","options":["The wrist","The forearm","The thumb","The fingers"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Carpi comes from carpus, the wrist, which is built from the eight carpal bones. Muscles with carpi in their name, such as flexor carpi radialis, flexor carpi ulnaris and the extensor carpi muscles, cross the wrist and insert on carpal or metacarpal bones, so their main job is moving the hand at the wrist rather than moving the fingers.","notes":["Correct. Carpi refers to the wrist.","The forearm is the antebrachium, and no muscle in this group uses carpi for it. Students sometimes pick this because the muscle belly sits in the forearm, but carpi names the joint the muscle moves, not where its belly lies.","The thumb is named by pollicis, as in flexor pollicis longus and abductor pollicis longus. Choose it if the name contained pollicis.","The fingers are named by digitorum, as in flexor digitorum superficialis and extensor digitorum. Pick this if the name contained digitorum."],"comp":79,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-roles-naming"},{"id":"N-m3-forearm-anterior-12","dok":1,"q":"What does the word teres in pronator teres describe?","a":"A round, cylindrical shape.","options":["A deep position","A round, cylindrical shape","A long length","A square, four-sided shape"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Teres means round, and it describes the shape of the muscle belly. Pronator teres is a rounded muscle that runs diagonally across the proximal forearm from the medial epicondyle to the lateral radius. Comparing it with pronator quadratus shows how names use shape to tell apart two muscles that have the same action.","notes":["Depth is named by profundus, as in flexor digitorum profundus, while superficialis names the shallower muscle. Pick this if the name contained profundus.","Correct. Teres means round and describes the shape of the muscle.","Length is named by longus or brevis, as in flexor pollicis longus or extensor carpi radialis brevis. Choose it if the name contained one of those words.","Square or four-sided is quadratus, as in pronator quadratus in the distal forearm. Pick this if the name contained quadratus."],"comp":79,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-roles-naming"},{"id":"N-m3-forearm-anterior-13","dok":1,"q":"From which two sites does pronator teres originate?","a":"The medial epicondyle of the humerus and the coronoid process of the ulna.","options":["Lateral epicondyle and proximal ulna","Medial epicondyle and olecranon","Anterior radius and interosseous membrane","Medial epicondyle and coronoid process of the ulna"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Pronator teres has two heads. The larger head comes from the medial epicondyle as part of the common flexor origin, and a smaller head comes from the coronoid process of the ulna. Both heads join and run diagonally to the lateral mid-shaft of the radius, which is why the muscle pronates the forearm.","notes":["The lateral epicondyle and proximal ulna are the origins of supinator, the deep posterior muscle that does the opposite movement. Choose it if the question asked where supinator arises.","The medial epicondyle and olecranon are the origins of flexor carpi ulnaris, the most medial superficial flexor. Pick this if the question named flexor carpi ulnaris.","The anterior radius and interosseous membrane give origin to flexor pollicis longus in the deep anterior layer. Pick this if the question asked where the long thumb flexor begins.","Correct. Pronator teres arises from the medial epicondyle and the coronoid process of the ulna."],"comp":86,"competencyId":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments"},{"id":"N-m3-forearm-anterior-14","dok":1,"q":"Where does flexor pollicis longus originate?","a":"The anterior radius and the interosseous membrane.","options":["Anterior and medial ulna and interosseous membrane","Distal anterior ulna","Anterior radius and interosseous membrane","Medial epicondyle of the humerus"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Flexor pollicis longus is a deep anterior forearm muscle that lies on the radial (thumb) side. It arises from the anterior surface of the radius and the neighboring interosseous membrane, the sheet of dense connective tissue that joins the radius and ulna. Starting on the radius lines it up with the thumb, which it flexes.","notes":["The anterior and medial ulna and interosseous membrane are the origin of flexor digitorum profundus, the deep flexor beside it on the ulnar side. Pick this if the question asked where the deep finger flexor begins.","The distal anterior ulna is the origin of pronator quadratus, the square muscle just above the wrist. Pick it if the question asked where pronator quadratus begins.","Correct. Flexor pollicis longus arises from the anterior radius and interosseous membrane.","The medial epicondyle is the common flexor origin used by the superficial muscles such as flexor carpi radialis. Choose it if the question asked about a superficial anterior forearm muscle."],"comp":86,"competencyId":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments"},{"id":"N-m3-forearm-anterior-15","dok":1,"q":"What is the main action of palmaris longus?","a":"It tenses the palmar aponeurosis.","options":["Flexes the thumb at its tip","Abducts the wrist toward the thumb","Pronates the forearm at the radioulnar joints","Tenses the palmar aponeurosis"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Palmaris longus has a slender tendon that runs down the middle of the wrist and fans out into the palmar aponeurosis. When it contracts it pulls that sheet tight and gives a weak assist to wrist flexion. It is a small muscle that many people lack on one or both sides, and its absence causes no noticeable weakness because the other wrist flexors do the work.","notes":["Flexing the thumb is the job of flexor pollicis longus in the deep layer, along with the short thumb flexor in the hand. Choose this if the question named a muscle with pollicis in its name.","Abducting the wrist is done by flexor carpi radialis working with the radial wrist extensors on the thumb side. Palmaris longus runs down the middle of the wrist, so it does not tilt the hand to either side.","Pronation is done by pronator teres and pronator quadratus, which pull the radius across the ulna. Pick this if the question named one of the pronators.","Correct. Palmaris longus tenses the palmar aponeurosis and weakly assists wrist flexion."],"comp":86,"competencyId":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments"},{"id":"N-m3-forearm-anterior-16","dok":1,"q":"Where does flexor digitorum profundus originate?","a":"The anterior and medial ulna and the interosseous membrane.","options":["Anterior radius and interosseous membrane","Lateral epicondyle of the humerus","Anterior and medial ulna and interosseous membrane","Medial epicondyle, coronoid process and radius"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Flexor digitorum profundus is the deep finger flexor, so it does not reach up to the medial epicondyle. It arises from the anterior and medial surfaces of the ulna and the interosseous membrane, lying on the ulnar side of the deep layer next to flexor pollicis longus. From there its four tendons run to the distal phalanges of digits 2 to 5.","notes":["The anterior radius and interosseous membrane give origin to flexor pollicis longus, the deep muscle on the thumb side. Choose it if the question asked about the long thumb flexor.","The lateral epicondyle is the common extensor origin for the superficial posterior muscles, such as extensor digitorum. Pick it if the question asked where the finger extensor begins.","Correct. Flexor digitorum profundus arises from the anterior and medial ulna and the interosseous membrane.","The medial epicondyle, coronoid process and radius are the origins of flexor digitorum superficialis, the finger flexor that lies on top. Pick this if the question asked where the superficial finger flexor begins."],"comp":86,"competencyId":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments"},{"id":"N-m3-forearm-anterior-17","dok":1,"q":"In flexor digitorum superficialis, what does superficialis tell you about the muscle?","a":"It lies closer to the surface than its partner, flexor digitorum profundus.","options":["It is the stronger of the two finger flexors","It is the shorter of the two finger flexors","It lies closer to the surface than its partner","It lies on the radial side of the forearm"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Superficialis means nearer the surface. Flexor digitorum superficialis lies on top of flexor digitorum profundus, whose name means deep. The two muscles share the same action on the same fingers, so their names separate them by depth, and their insertions follow the same logic: the superficial one stops at the middle phalanges and the deep one reaches the distal phalanges.","notes":["Names rarely describe strength, and superficialis says nothing about force. Students choose this when they read superficial as dominant, but the word only places the muscle closer to the skin.","Relative length is named by brevis or longus, as in extensor carpi radialis brevis and longus. Choose this if the name contained brevis.","Correct. Superficialis means the muscle lies superficial to flexor digitorum profundus.","The side of the forearm is named by radialis or ulnaris, as in flexor carpi radialis. Pick this if the name contained radialis."],"comp":79,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-roles-naming"},{"id":"N-m3-forearm-anterior-18","dok":1,"q":"What are the origin and insertion of brachioradialis?","a":"From the lateral supracondylar ridge of the humerus to the styloid process of the radius.","options":["Medial epicondyle to the lateral mid-shaft of the radius","Lateral supracondylar ridge to the styloid process of the radius","Lateral epicondyle to the proximal lateral radius","Lateral epicondyle to the base of metacarpal 3"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Brachioradialis begins on the lateral supracondylar ridge, the sharp edge of the humerus just above the lateral epicondyle, and runs down the thumb side of the forearm to the styloid process of the radius. Its name joins brachio (arm) and radialis (radius), which matches a muscle that spans from the arm to the distal radius. It forms the fleshy lateral edge of the forearm you can feel just below the elbow.","notes":["Medial epicondyle to the lateral mid-shaft of the radius describes pronator teres. Pick this if the question asked for the attachments of the round pronator.","Correct. Brachioradialis runs from the lateral supracondylar ridge to the styloid process of the radius.","Lateral epicondyle to the proximal lateral radius describes supinator, which wraps around the upper radius. Pick it if the question asked which muscle supinates the forearm.","Lateral epicondyle to the base of metacarpal 3 describes extensor carpi radialis brevis. Choose it if the question asked about the shorter radial wrist extensor."],"comp":86,"competencyId":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments"},{"id":"N-m3-forearm-anterior-19","dok":2,"q":"Which list gives the superficial anterior forearm muscles in order from lateral (thumb side) to medial (little finger side)?","a":"Pronator teres, flexor carpi radialis, palmaris longus, flexor carpi ulnaris.","options":["Flexor carpi radialis, pronator teres, flexor carpi ulnaris, palmaris longus","Pronator teres, palmaris longus, flexor carpi radialis, flexor carpi ulnaris","Flexor carpi ulnaris, palmaris longus, flexor carpi radialis, pronator teres","Pronator teres, flexor carpi radialis, palmaris longus, flexor carpi ulnaris"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"All four muscles fan out from the medial epicondyle, but they end up side by side across the front of the forearm. Pronator teres is the most lateral because it heads for the radius, flexor carpi radialis lies next to it on the thumb side, palmaris longus runs down the middle, and flexor carpi ulnaris is the most medial on the little finger side. The words radialis and ulnaris in two of the names confirm which side each belongs on.","notes":["This list swaps two pairs of neighbors. Flexor carpi radialis lies medial to pronator teres, not lateral to it, and palmaris longus sits between the two carpi muscles in the middle of the forearm rather than at the medial edge.","This list puts palmaris longus on the thumb side of flexor carpi radialis. Palmaris longus actually runs down the middle of the forearm, between flexor carpi radialis and flexor carpi ulnaris.","This is the correct sequence written backward, from medial to lateral. Pick it if the question asked for the order starting on the little finger side.","Correct. From lateral to medial the order is pronator teres, flexor carpi radialis, palmaris longus, flexor carpi ulnaris."],"comp":86,"competencyId":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments"},{"id":"N-m3-forearm-anterior-20","dok":2,"q":"Why is flexor digitorum profundus the only muscle that can bend the fingertips of digits 2 to 5 at the distal interphalangeal joints?","a":"It is the only finger flexor that inserts on the distal phalanges.","options":["It is the only finger flexor that lies in the superficial layer","It is the only finger flexor that inserts on the distal phalanges","It is the only finger flexor that passes through the carpal tunnel","It is the only finger flexor that arises from the medial epicondyle"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"A muscle can only move the joints its tendon crosses. Flexor digitorum profundus is the only muscle whose tendons reach the distal phalanges of digits 2 to 5, so it is the only one that crosses and flexes the distal interphalangeal joints. Flexor digitorum superficialis stops at the middle phalanges, so it can bend the middle knuckles but not the fingertips.","notes":["Profundus means deep, and the muscle lies in the deep layer beneath flexor digitorum superficialis. This choice reverses the meaning of its name.","Correct. Only profundus reaches the distal phalanges of digits 2 to 5.","Flexor digitorum superficialis and flexor pollicis longus also pass through the carpal tunnel, so this does not set profundus apart. This idea fits a question about why swelling in the tunnel can affect all the long flexor tendons at once.","Profundus arises from the ulna and interosseous membrane, not the medial epicondyle. Flexor digitorum superficialis is the finger flexor with a medial epicondyle origin, so pick this kind of answer for a question about superficialis."],"comp":86,"competencyId":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments"},{"id":"N-m3-forearm-anterior-21","dok":2,"q":"Flexor digitorum superficialis lies on top of flexor digitorum profundus, yet the deep muscle's tendons reach farther out in each finger. How does the profundus tendon get past the superficialis tendon?","a":"The superficialis tendon splits, and the profundus tendon passes through the gap.","options":["The profundus tendon loops around the outer side of the proximal phalanx","The superficialis tendon splits and the profundus tendon passes through the gap","The profundus tendon runs along the back of the finger to reach the tip","The superficialis tendon ends at the wrist, well before reaching the fingers"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Near the base of each finger, the superficialis tendon divides into two slips that pass around the profundus tendon and attach to the sides of the middle phalanx. The profundus tendon slides through this split and continues on to the distal phalanx. This arrangement lets two stacked muscles share the same fingers while each one controls a different joint.","notes":["Both long flexor tendons stay together on the palmar side of the finger. They do not detour around the bone, so this choice comes from guessing how two tendons might avoid each other rather than from the actual anatomy.","Correct. The superficialis tendon splits so the profundus tendon can pass through to the distal phalanx.","The back of the finger carries the extensor expansion, which receives the extensor digitorum tendon. A tendon on the back of the finger would straighten it, so pick this only if the question asked how the extensor tendons reach the fingers.","Superficialis does reach the fingers and inserts on the middle phalanges of digits 2 to 5. A muscle that ended at the wrist would act only on the wrist, like flexor carpi radialis."],"comp":86,"competencyId":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments"},{"id":"N-m3-forearm-anterior-22","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares flexor digitorum superficialis and flexor digitorum profundus?","a":"Superficialis inserts on the middle phalanges, and profundus inserts on the distal phalanges.","options":["Both insert on the distal phalanges and differ only in where they arise","Superficialis inserts on the distal phalanges, and profundus inserts on the middle phalanges","Superficialis acts on the thumb, and profundus acts on digits 2 to 5","Superficialis inserts on the middle phalanges, and profundus inserts on the distal phalanges"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Both muscles flex digits 2 to 5, but they are stacked in two layers and reach different bones. Superficialis lies on top and stops at the middle phalanges, so it bends the proximal interphalangeal joints. Profundus lies deeper and reaches the distal phalanges, so it is the muscle that bends the fingertips. The deeper muscle is the one that reaches farther.","notes":["They do differ in origin, but they also differ in insertion. If both reached the distal phalanges, a clinician could not test them separately by asking the patient to bend one joint at a time.","This reverses the two insertions. It would be correct only if the names were swapped, so remember that the deep muscle reaches the more distal bone.","Neither muscle acts on the thumb. The long thumb flexor is flexor pollicis longus, so pick a statement about the thumb only if it named that muscle.","Correct. Superficialis ends on the middle phalanges and profundus on the distal phalanges."],"comp":86,"competencyId":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments"},{"id":"N-m3-forearm-anterior-23","dok":2,"q":"Which pair of muscles works together to abduct the wrist, tilting the hand toward the thumb side?","a":"Flexor carpi radialis and extensor carpi radialis longus.","options":["Flexor carpi radialis and flexor carpi ulnaris","Extensor carpi radialis longus and extensor carpi ulnaris","Flexor carpi radialis and extensor carpi radialis longus","Flexor carpi ulnaris and extensor carpi ulnaris"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Abduction of the wrist (radial deviation) needs muscles on the thumb side of the wrist. Flexor carpi radialis crosses the front of the wrist on that side and extensor carpi radialis longus crosses the back. Each one alone would also flex or extend, but when they contract together the flexion and extension cancel out and the hand tilts straight toward the thumb.","notes":["Flexor carpi radialis and flexor carpi ulnaris both cross the front of the wrist, one on each side, so together they flex the wrist straight forward while their side-to-side pulls cancel. Choose them for a question about pure wrist flexion.","Extensor carpi radialis longus and extensor carpi ulnaris both cross the back of the wrist, one on each side, so together they extend the wrist straight back. Pick them for a question about pure wrist extension.","Correct. These two radial muscles, one flexor and one extensor, combine to abduct the wrist.","Flexor carpi ulnaris and extensor carpi ulnaris both cross the wrist on the little finger side, so together they adduct the wrist. Pick this pair if the question asked about tilting the hand toward the little finger."],"comp":86,"competencyId":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments"},{"id":"N-m3-forearm-anterior-24","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares pronator teres and pronator quadratus?","a":"Pronator teres runs diagonally near the elbow, and pronator quadratus runs straight across the distal forearm.","options":["Pronator teres runs straight across the distal forearm, and pronator quadratus runs diagonally near the elbow","Pronator teres runs diagonally near the elbow, and pronator quadratus runs straight across the distal forearm","Pronator teres lies in the deep layer, and pronator quadratus lies in the superficial layer","Pronator teres supinates the forearm, and pronator quadratus pronates it"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Both muscles pronate the forearm by pulling the radius across the ulna, but they sit at opposite ends of the forearm and have different shapes. Pronator teres is a round muscle that runs diagonally from the medial epicondyle and coronoid process to the lateral mid-shaft of the radius. Pronator quadratus is a flat, square muscle that runs horizontally between the distal ulna and distal radius, deep to the flexor tendons.","notes":["This swaps the positions of the two muscles. The square muscle (quadratus) is the horizontal one at the wrist end, and the round one (teres) is the diagonal one near the elbow.","Correct. Teres is the diagonal, round pronator near the elbow, and quadratus is the square pronator just above the wrist.","This reverses the layers. Pronator teres is a superficial muscle from the medial epicondyle, while pronator quadratus is in the deep layer lying directly on the bones.","Both muscles pronate, as their names say. Supination belongs to supinator and biceps brachii, so pick a statement about supination only if it named one of those."],"comp":86,"competencyId":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments"},{"id":"N-m3-forearm-anterior-25","dok":2,"q":"Besides pronating the forearm, pronator teres also assists which movement, because it crosses the front of the elbow?","a":"Elbow flexion.","options":["Forearm supination","Wrist flexion","Elbow extension","Elbow flexion"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"A muscle acts on every joint it crosses, and the side it crosses on decides the movement. Pronator teres arises above the elbow on the medial epicondyle and inserts below it on the radius, crossing the anterior side of the elbow. Pulling across the front of a hinge joint bends it, so the muscle gives a small assist to elbow flexion along with its main job of pronation.","notes":["Supination is the opposite of pronation and is done by supinator and biceps brachii. A muscle cannot both pronate and supinate, so pick this if the question asked about supinator.","Pronator teres ends on the mid-shaft of the radius and never crosses the wrist, so it cannot move the wrist. Choose wrist flexion for muscles such as flexor carpi radialis that reach the hand.","Elbow extension needs a muscle crossing the back of the elbow, like triceps brachii inserting on the olecranon. Pick this if the question described a posterior muscle.","Correct. Crossing the front of the elbow lets pronator teres assist elbow flexion."],"comp":86,"competencyId":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments"},{"id":"N-m3-forearm-anterior-26","dok":2,"q":"Flexor carpi radialis and flexor carpi ulnaris both flex the wrist. Why does one abduct the wrist while the other adducts it?","a":"Their tendons cross the wrist on opposite sides, the thumb side and the little finger side.","options":["Their tendons cross the wrist on opposite sides","They arise from different epicondyles of the humerus","One inserts on the fingers and the other on the carpal bones","One lies in the superficial layer and the other in the deep layer"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Side-to-side movement at the wrist depends on which side of the wrist a tendon crosses. Flexor carpi radialis crosses on the radial (thumb) side and inserts on metacarpals 2 and 3, so it tilts the hand toward the thumb, which is abduction. Flexor carpi ulnaris crosses on the ulnar (little finger) side and inserts on the pisiform, hamate and metacarpal 5, so it tilts the hand toward the little finger, which is adduction.","notes":["Correct. The side of the wrist each tendon crosses decides whether it abducts or adducts.","Both muscles arise from the medial epicondyle as part of the common flexor origin, so origin cannot explain the difference. Different epicondyles would explain why a flexor and an extensor differ, not two flexors.","Neither inserts on the fingers. Both insert on carpal and metacarpal bones, which is why their names contain carpi rather than digitorum.","Both are superficial muscles of the anterior forearm. Layer explains why flexor digitorum profundus differs from flexor digitorum superficialis, not why these two tilt the wrist in opposite directions."],"comp":86,"competencyId":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments"},{"id":"N-m3-forearm-anterior-27","dok":2,"q":"A muscle arises from the anterior radius and interosseous membrane and inserts on the distal phalanx of the thumb. Which muscle is it?","a":"Flexor pollicis longus.","options":["Abductor pollicis longus","Flexor pollicis longus","Flexor pollicis brevis","Extensor pollicis longus"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Starting on the anterior radius places this muscle in the deep layer of the anterior forearm, so it is a flexor, and reaching the distal phalanx of the thumb means it bends the thumb tip. That matches flexor pollicis longus. The word longus tells you it is the long thumb flexor that starts in the forearm, unlike the short thumb flexor that lives in the hand.","notes":["Abductor pollicis longus arises from the posterior radius, ulna and interosseous membrane and inserts on the base of metacarpal 1. Pick it if the question asked which posterior muscle moves the thumb away from the palm.","Correct. Flexor pollicis longus runs from the anterior radius and interosseous membrane to the distal phalanx of the thumb.","Flexor pollicis brevis is a short thenar muscle that lies entirely in the hand at the base of the thumb. It cannot arise from the radius, so pick it if the question asked about an intrinsic thenar muscle.","Extensor pollicis longus also reaches the distal phalanx of the thumb, but it arises from the posterior ulna and interosseous membrane and runs on the back of the wrist. Choose it if the question asked which muscle straightens the tip of the thumb."],"comp":86,"competencyId":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments"},{"id":"N-m3-forearm-anterior-28","dok":2,"q":"Which two anterior forearm muscles arise partly from the interosseous membrane between the radius and ulna?","a":"Flexor digitorum profundus and flexor pollicis longus.","options":["Flexor digitorum profundus and flexor pollicis longus","Flexor carpi radialis and palmaris longus","Pronator teres and flexor carpi radialis","Palmaris longus and flexor carpi ulnaris"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The interosseous membrane is a sheet of dense connective tissue that spans the gap between the radius and ulna, and deep forearm muscles use it as extra attachment surface. In the anterior forearm, flexor digitorum profundus arises from the ulna and the membrane, and flexor pollicis longus arises from the radius and the membrane, so both lie directly on it.","notes":["Correct. Both deep finger and thumb flexors arise partly from the interosseous membrane.","Flexor carpi radialis and palmaris longus both come from the medial epicondyle as part of the common flexor origin. The deeper finger flexors separate them from the membrane.","Pronator teres and flexor carpi radialis arise from the medial epicondyle, and pronator teres also from the coronoid process. They are superficial and do not reach the membrane, so choose them for a question about the common flexor origin.","Palmaris longus and flexor carpi ulnaris are superficial muscles from the medial epicondyle, with flexor carpi ulnaris also arising from the olecranon. Pick them if the question asked about superficial flexors on the medial side."],"comp":86,"competencyId":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments"},{"id":"N-m3-forearm-anterior-29","dok":2,"q":"When a person touches the tips of the thumb and little finger together and slightly flexes the wrist, a thin tendon often stands out in the middle of the front of the wrist. Which muscle does it belong to?","a":"Palmaris longus.","options":["Palmaris longus","Flexor pollicis longus","Flexor carpi radialis","Flexor carpi ulnaris"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Palmaris longus has a slender tendon that runs down the center of the anterior wrist and passes in front of the flexor retinaculum into the palmar aponeurosis. Cupping the palm and flexing the wrist tightens it, so it stands out in the midline. Because the muscle is absent in many people, some will see no tendon in this spot even though their other flexors are normal.","notes":["Correct. The thin midline tendon belongs to palmaris longus.","Flexor pollicis longus lies in the deep layer and its tendon passes through the carpal tunnel, so it does not stand out under the skin. Pick it if the question asked which tendon bends the thumb tip.","Flexor carpi radialis has a thicker tendon just to the thumb side of the midline. Choose it if the question described the tendon used as a landmark for the radial pulse.","Flexor carpi ulnaris has its tendon at the medial (little finger) edge of the wrist, heading to the pisiform. Pick it if the question described the tendon at the ulnar edge."],"comp":86,"competencyId":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments"},{"id":"N-m3-forearm-anterior-30","dok":2,"q":"To take a radial pulse, you press on the radial artery where it lies directly lateral to a prominent tendon at the front of the wrist. Which muscle does that tendon belong to?","a":"Flexor carpi radialis.","options":["Flexor digitorum superficialis","Flexor carpi radialis","Palmaris longus","Flexor carpi ulnaris"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"At the wrist the radial artery runs on the thumb side between the tendon of flexor carpi radialis and the distal radius. Pressing just lateral to that tendon pushes the artery against the bone, which is why the pulse is easy to feel there. The tendon makes a reliable landmark because it stands out when the wrist is flexed against resistance.","notes":["The flexor digitorum superficialis tendons lie deeper and closer to the middle of the wrist and pass through the carpal tunnel, so they are not the palpable landmark for the radial pulse.","Correct. The radial pulse is felt just lateral to the flexor carpi radialis tendon.","Palmaris longus runs down the midline of the wrist, medial to flexor carpi radialis, so the radial artery is not directly beside it. Pick it if the question asked about the thin tendon in the center of the wrist.","Flexor carpi ulnaris lies on the little finger side of the wrist, and the ulnar artery runs just lateral to its tendon. Choose it if the question asked where to feel the ulnar pulse."],"comp":86,"competencyId":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments"},{"id":"N-m3-forearm-anterior-31","dok":2,"q":"Which superficial anterior forearm muscle does not cross the wrist?","a":"Pronator teres.","options":["Flexor carpi radialis","Palmaris longus","Flexor carpi ulnaris","Pronator teres"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Pronator teres inserts on the lateral mid-shaft of the radius, partway down the forearm, so it never reaches the wrist. That is why it acts only at the forearm and elbow, pronating the forearm and assisting elbow flexion. The other superficial muscles from the medial epicondyle all send tendons across the wrist to the hand.","notes":["Flexor carpi radialis crosses the front of the wrist on the thumb side to reach metacarpals 2 and 3. Pick it if the question asked which superficial flexor abducts the wrist.","Palmaris longus crosses the middle of the wrist to reach the palmar aponeurosis. Choose it if the question asked which muscle tenses the fascia of the palm.","Flexor carpi ulnaris crosses the wrist on the little finger side to reach the pisiform, hamate and metacarpal 5. Pick it if the question asked which superficial flexor adducts the wrist.","Correct. Pronator teres stops on the mid-shaft of the radius and does not cross the wrist."],"comp":86,"competencyId":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments"},{"id":"N-m3-forearm-anterior-32","dok":2,"q":"Brachioradialis crosses the front of the elbow but does not cross the wrist. Which action does it produce?","a":"It flexes the elbow, most strongly with the forearm in mid-position.","options":["Extends the elbow along with triceps brachii","Extends the wrist and abducts the hand toward the thumb","Flexes the elbow, most strongly with the forearm in mid-position","Flexes the wrist and adducts the hand toward the little finger"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Brachioradialis runs from the lateral supracondylar ridge of the humerus to the styloid process of the radius, so the only joint it crosses is the elbow, and it crosses on the anterior side. That makes it an elbow flexor. The course notes point out that it pulls hardest with the forearm in mid-position, halfway between pronation and supination, as when you lift a mug by its handle with the thumb up.","notes":["Elbow extension comes from muscles crossing the back of the elbow, such as triceps brachii inserting on the olecranon. Pick this if the question asked about the posterior arm.","Extending and abducting the wrist is done by extensor carpi radialis longus, which crosses the wrist to metacarpal 2. Brachioradialis stops on the radius and cannot move the wrist.","Correct. Brachioradialis flexes the elbow and is strongest with the forearm in mid-position.","Flexing and adducting the wrist is the action of flexor carpi ulnaris, which crosses the wrist on the little finger side. Brachioradialis does not reach the hand."],"comp":86,"competencyId":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments"},{"id":"N-m3-forearm-anterior-33","dok":3,"q":"After a knife cut across the palm side of a finger, a patient can bend the middle knuckle of that finger but cannot bend the fingertip, even when the other fingers are held straight. Which tendon was most likely cut?","a":"The flexor digitorum profundus tendon.","options":["Flexor digitorum superficialis","Flexor digitorum profundus","Extensor digitorum","Flexor pollicis longus"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Bending the fingertip happens at the distal interphalangeal joint, and the only muscle that reaches the distal phalanx of digits 2 to 5 is flexor digitorum profundus. The patient can still bend the middle knuckle because flexor digitorum superficialis, which inserts on the middle phalanx, is intact. Losing only the fingertip movement points to a cut profundus tendon beyond the point where superficialis attaches.","notes":["If the superficialis tendon were cut, the patient would be unable to bend the middle knuckle when the other fingers were held straight. Pick it if the scenario described loss of middle knuckle flexion with the fingertip still working.","Correct. Only the profundus tendon bends the fingertip, so its loss explains this finding.","Extensor digitorum runs on the back of the hand and straightens the fingers. A cut extensor tendon would leave the finger unable to straighten, so choose it if the cut were on the back of the finger.","Flexor pollicis longus bends the tip of the thumb, not the fingers. Pick it if the patient could not bend the tip of the thumb."],"comp":86,"competencyId":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments"},{"id":"N-m3-forearm-anterior-34","dok":3,"q":"A patient can bend the thumb at its knuckle but cannot bend the tip of the thumb. Which muscle's tendon is most likely damaged?","a":"Flexor pollicis longus.","options":["Extensor pollicis longus","Flexor pollicis longus","Abductor pollicis longus","Flexor pollicis brevis"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The tip of the thumb bends at the interphalangeal joint, and the only muscle that inserts on the palmar side of the distal phalanx of the thumb is flexor pollicis longus. The knuckle (metacarpophalangeal joint) can still bend because flexor pollicis brevis, a short thenar muscle in the hand, inserts on the proximal phalanx and crosses that joint. Losing tip flexion alone points to the long flexor.","notes":["Extensor pollicis longus straightens the tip of the thumb from the back of the hand. Damage to it would leave the thumb tip unable to straighten, so choose it for that scenario.","Correct. Flexor pollicis longus is the only muscle that bends the thumb tip.","Abductor pollicis longus inserts on the base of metacarpal 1 and moves the thumb away from the palm. Pick it if the patient could not pull the thumb away from the hand.","Flexor pollicis brevis is the short thenar muscle that bends the thumb at its knuckle, and its working is why the knuckle still bends here. Pick it if the question asked which thenar muscle flexes the metacarpophalangeal joint of the thumb."],"comp":86,"competencyId":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments"},{"id":"N-m3-forearm-anterior-35","dok":3,"q":"A golfer has pain at the medial epicondyle that gets worse when he flexes his wrist against resistance. Which structure is most likely irritated?","a":"The common flexor tendon of the wrist and finger flexors at the medial epicondyle.","options":["The triceps brachii tendon at the olecranon","The biceps brachii tendon at the radial tuberosity","The common extensor tendon at the lateral epicondyle","The common flexor tendon at the medial epicondyle"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Golfer's elbow (medial epicondylitis) is irritation of the shared tendon where the superficial anterior forearm muscles attach to the medial epicondyle. Pronator teres, flexor carpi radialis, palmaris longus and flexor carpi ulnaris all pull on that one spot, so resisted wrist flexion loads the sore tendon and reproduces the pain. Knowing the common flexor origin lets you predict which movements will hurt.","notes":["The triceps tendon inserts on the olecranon at the back of the elbow. Irritation there would hurt with resisted elbow extension, so choose it for posterior elbow pain.","The biceps tendon inserts on the radial tuberosity at the front of the elbow. Irritation there would hurt with resisted elbow flexion and supination, so pick it for pain in the front of the elbow.","The common extensor tendon on the lateral epicondyle is the site of tennis elbow, which hurts on the outer elbow with resisted wrist extension. Pick it if the pain were lateral.","Correct. Medial elbow pain with resisted wrist flexion points to the common flexor origin."],"comp":86,"competencyId":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments"},{"id":"N-m3-forearm-anterior-36","dok":3,"q":"A person pours water from a pitcher by turning the palm from facing up to facing down while keeping the elbow straight. Which two muscles produce this movement?","a":"Pronator teres and pronator quadratus.","options":["Brachialis and brachioradialis","Supinator and biceps brachii","Flexor carpi radialis and flexor carpi ulnaris","Pronator teres and pronator quadratus"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Turning the palm from facing up to facing down is pronation, in which the radius rolls across the front of the ulna. Pronator teres and pronator quadratus both pull the radius toward the ulna to make that happen, with pronator quadratus as the prime mover. Keeping the elbow straight rules out the elbow flexors and leaves rotation of the forearm as the only movement.","notes":["Brachialis and brachioradialis flex the elbow. Pick them if the person were lifting the pitcher toward the body by bending the elbow.","Supinator and biceps brachii turn the palm the other way, from facing down to facing up. Pick them if the person were turning the hand over to catch water in the palm.","Flexor carpi radialis and flexor carpi ulnaris bend the wrist forward together. Choose them if the person tipped the pitcher by bending the wrist instead of rotating the forearm.","Correct. Pronator teres and pronator quadratus pronate the forearm."],"comp":86,"competencyId":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments"},{"id":"N-m3-forearm-anterior-37","dok":3,"q":"A surgeon removes the palmaris longus tendon to use as a graft for repairing a tendon elsewhere. Why does the patient have little or no loss of hand function afterward?","a":"Palmaris longus mainly tenses the palmar aponeurosis, and stronger wrist flexors do the work it assisted.","options":["Its job is minor, and stronger wrist flexors take over","Palmaris longus mainly pronates the forearm, and pronator quadratus takes over","Palmaris longus regrows within a few weeks","Palmaris longus only acts on the thumb, which has other muscles"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Palmaris longus is a small muscle whose main job is tightening the palmar aponeurosis, with only a weak assist to wrist flexion. Flexor carpi radialis and flexor carpi ulnaris are much stronger wrist flexors and cover that movement completely. This is also why many people born without palmaris longus never notice it is missing, and why its long, thin tendon makes a convenient graft.","notes":["Correct. Its action is minor and the other wrist flexors are much stronger.","Palmaris longus does not pronate the forearm. Pronation belongs to pronator teres and pronator quadratus, so this choice fits a question about losing one of the pronators.","A removed tendon does not regrow into a working muscle. This choice comes from assuming the body simply replaces the tissue, when the real reason is that other muscles already cover its job.","Palmaris longus does not reach the thumb. It ends in the palmar aponeurosis in the center of the palm, so a thumb-related answer would fit a muscle like flexor pollicis longus instead."],"comp":86,"competencyId":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments"},{"id":"N-m3-forearm-anterior-38","dok":3,"q":"When a patient flexes the wrist, the hand drifts toward the thumb side instead of bending straight forward. Weakness of which muscle best explains this?","a":"Flexor carpi ulnaris.","options":["Palmaris longus","Flexor carpi radialis","Flexor carpi ulnaris","Extensor carpi ulnaris"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Straight wrist flexion depends on balanced pull from flexor carpi radialis on the thumb side and flexor carpi ulnaris on the little finger side. If flexor carpi ulnaris is weak, the radial flexor's pull is unopposed, so the hand both flexes and abducts toward the thumb. Thinking of each muscle as a pull on one corner of the wrist lets you predict the drift.","notes":["Palmaris longus pulls down the middle of the wrist and is only a weak flexor, so losing it does not tilt the hand to either side. Many people lack it with no change in wrist movement.","Weak flexor carpi radialis would leave flexor carpi ulnaris unopposed, so the hand would drift toward the little finger side during flexion. Pick it if the scenario described that ulnar drift.","Correct. Without flexor carpi ulnaris, the radial flexor pulls the flexing hand toward the thumb.","Extensor carpi ulnaris is on the back of the forearm and extends the wrist, so it does not take part in wrist flexion. Choose it if the hand drifted toward the thumb during wrist extension."],"comp":86,"competencyId":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments"},{"id":"N-m3-forearm-anterior-39","dok":3,"q":"A climber grips a hold tightly, curling all four fingers fully at both the middle knuckles and the fingertips. Which two forearm muscles provide this finger flexion?","a":"Flexor digitorum superficialis and flexor digitorum profundus.","options":["Flexor carpi radialis and flexor carpi ulnaris","Pronator teres and pronator quadratus","Extensor digitorum and extensor indicis","Flexor digitorum superficialis and flexor digitorum profundus"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Curling the fingers at the middle knuckles and fingertips needs muscles that reach the middle and distal phalanges. Flexor digitorum superficialis inserts on the middle phalanges and flexes the proximal interphalangeal joints, and flexor digitorum profundus inserts on the distal phalanges and flexes the distal interphalangeal joints. Both also help flex the knuckles and wrist because their tendons cross those joints on the way.","notes":["Flexor carpi radialis and flexor carpi ulnaris insert on wrist and metacarpal bones, as carpi in their names says, so they bend the wrist but not the fingers. Pick them if the climber were bending the wrist.","Pronator teres and pronator quadratus rotate the forearm to turn the palm down. Choose them if the climber were rotating the forearm rather than curling the fingers.","Extensor digitorum and extensor indicis straighten the fingers, which is what the climber does to let go of the hold. Pick them for releasing a grip.","Correct. The two long finger flexors curl the fingers at both interphalangeal joints."],"comp":86,"competencyId":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments"},{"id":"N-m3-forearm-anterior-40","dok":3,"q":"A muscle arises from the medial epicondyle and runs diagonally across the front of the forearm to attach on the lateral side of the radius. Without knowing its name, which action would you predict?","a":"Pronation of the forearm.","options":["Pronation of the forearm","Extension of the wrist","Supination of the forearm","Extension of the elbow"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Pulling the lateral side of the radius toward the medial side of the elbow rolls the radius across the front of the ulna, and that rotation turns the palm to face posteriorly, which is pronation. The muscle also crosses the front of the elbow, which fits an anterior compartment muscle rather than an extensor. These are the attachments of pronator teres, so reasoning from origin and insertion leads straight to its name.","notes":["Correct. These attachments pull the radius across the ulna and pronate the forearm.","Wrist extension needs a muscle that crosses the back of the wrist. This muscle stops on the radius, so it cannot move the wrist at all.","Supination is the opposite rotation, produced by supinator, which wraps around the proximal radius from the lateral epicondyle and ulna. Pick it if the attachments described that wrap from the back.","Elbow extension needs a muscle that crosses the back of the elbow, such as triceps brachii inserting on the olecranon. Choose it if the muscle ran down the back of the arm."],"comp":86,"competencyId":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments"}]},{"id":"m3-upper-limb-vessels","title":"Arteries and veins of the thorax and upper limb","summary":"aortic arch branches, subclavian, axillary, brachial, radial, ulnar, palmar arches, cephalic, basilic, median cubital, brachiocephalic veins","mw":"2026-10-14","tr":"2026-10-08","cards":[{"id":"L04335","dok":2,"q":"Which sequence correctly traces the main artery of the upper limb from the axilla into the forearm?","a":"Axillary artery, brachial artery, then the radial and ulnar arteries.","options":["Axillary artery, brachial artery, then the cephalic and basilic arteries","Axillary artery, brachial artery, then the radial and ulnar arteries","Axillary artery, radial artery, then the brachial and ulnar arteries","Brachial artery, axillary artery, then the radial and ulnar arteries"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The subclavian artery becomes the axillary artery at the lateral border of the first rib, and the axillary becomes the brachial artery at the lower border of teres major. The brachial artery runs down the medial arm and, in the cubital fossa, divides into the radial artery on the thumb side and the ulnar artery on the little finger side. One vessel simply changes its name as it passes each landmark.","notes":["The cephalic and basilic are superficial veins of the upper limb, not arteries. Pick them if the question asked which superficial veins run up the lateral and medial arm.","Correct. Axillary, then brachial, then the radial and ulnar branches in the forearm.","The radial artery is a forearm branch of the brachial artery, so it cannot come before the brachial. Students pick this when they lose track of where the brachial artery divides.","This puts the brachial artery before the axillary. The axillary artery lies in the armpit and becomes the brachial artery in the arm, so the order is reversed."],"lt":"t-w4-lab-ue-arteries-veins","lid":"w4-lab-ue-arteries-veins-r1","comp":88,"competencyId":"w4-lab-ue-arteries-veins"},{"id":"L04336","dok":1,"q":"Which group lists the superficial veins of the upper limb that are commonly used for blood draws?","a":"The cephalic, basilic, and median cubital veins.","options":["The cephalic, basilic, and great saphenous veins","The axillary, subclavian, and brachiocephalic veins","The brachial, radial, and ulnar veins","The cephalic, basilic, and median cubital veins"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The superficial veins of the upper limb run in the subcutaneous tissue, where they can be seen and reached with a needle. The cephalic vein ascends the lateral side, the basilic vein ascends the medial side, and the median cubital vein connects them across the front of the elbow. The median cubital vein is the usual site for drawing blood.","notes":["The cephalic and basilic are correct, but the great saphenous vein is a superficial vein of the lower limb, running up the medial leg and thigh. Choose it if the question asked about the longest superficial vein of the leg.","These are deep veins of the shoulder and root of the neck that carry blood toward the superior vena cava. They would be the answer if the question traced venous return from the upper limb to the heart.","The brachial, radial, and ulnar veins are deep veins that travel alongside the arteries of the same names. Pick them if the question asked which veins accompany the brachial, radial, and ulnar arteries.","Correct. The cephalic, basilic, and median cubital veins are the superficial veins used for blood draws."],"lt":"t-w4-lab-ue-arteries-veins","lid":"w4-lab-ue-arteries-veins-r2","comp":88,"competencyId":"w4-lab-ue-arteries-veins"},{"id":"L04337","dok":2,"q":"Which superficial vein crosses the cubital fossa to connect the cephalic and basilic veins, making it the usual site for a blood draw?","a":"The median cubital vein.","options":["The basilic vein","The median antebrachial vein","The median cubital vein","The cephalic vein"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The median cubital vein runs diagonally across the front of the elbow, linking the cephalic vein on the lateral side to the basilic vein on the medial side. It is large, close to the surface, and fairly well anchored, which makes it easy to reach with a needle. The bicipital aponeurosis lies just deep to it, separating it from the brachial artery and median nerve.","notes":["The basilic vein ascends the medial forearm and arm and joins the brachial veins to form the axillary vein. It would be the answer for the superficial vein on the little finger side.","The median antebrachial vein ascends the middle of the anterior forearm, below the elbow rather than across it. Choose it if the question asked which superficial vein runs up the center of the forearm.","Correct. The median cubital vein bridges the cephalic and basilic veins in the cubital fossa.","The cephalic vein ascends the lateral forearm and arm and joins the axillary vein at the deltopectoral groove. Pick it if the question asked which superficial vein runs up the thumb side of the limb."],"lt":"t-w4-lab-ue-arteries-veins","lid":"w4-lab-ue-arteries-veins-r3","comp":88,"competencyId":"w4-lab-ue-arteries-veins"},{"id":"N-m3-upper-limb-vessels-1","dok":1,"q":"Which artery is the first and largest branch to leave the aortic arch?","a":"The brachiocephalic trunk.","options":["Right subclavian artery","Left subclavian artery","Brachiocephalic trunk","Left common carotid artery"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The aortic arch gives off three branches, and the brachiocephalic trunk is the first one, on the right, and the largest. It is a short shared stem that serves the right side of the head and neck and the right upper limb. It soon divides into the right common carotid and right subclavian arteries.","notes":["The right subclavian artery does not come off the arch at all. It is one of the two vessels the brachiocephalic trunk divides into, so pick it for a question about what the trunk splits into.","The left subclavian artery is the third and last branch of the arch. It would be the answer if the question asked which arch branch supplies the left upper limb.","Correct. The brachiocephalic trunk leaves the arch first and is the largest of the three branches.","The left common carotid artery is the second branch of the arch, arising directly from it between the other two. Pick it if the question asked for the middle branch of the arch or the artery to the left side of the head and neck."],"comp":62,"competencyId":"bvn-great-vessels"},{"id":"N-m3-upper-limb-vessels-2","dok":2,"q":"Moving from the right side of the body to the left, what is the correct order of the branches of the aortic arch?","a":"Brachiocephalic trunk, left common carotid artery, left subclavian artery.","options":["Left subclavian artery, left common carotid artery, brachiocephalic trunk","Brachiocephalic trunk, left common carotid artery, left subclavian artery","Right common carotid artery, brachiocephalic trunk, left subclavian artery","Brachiocephalic trunk, left subclavian artery, left common carotid artery"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"As the arch curves from right to left over the heart, it gives off the brachiocephalic trunk first, then the left common carotid artery, then the left subclavian artery. The right side gets one shared stem while the left side gets two separate arteries, which is why the branches are not symmetrical.","notes":["This is the correct list read backward, from left to right. Students choose it when they lose track of which direction the question asked for.","Correct. Brachiocephalic trunk, then left common carotid, then left subclavian is the right to left order.","The right common carotid artery is not an arch branch. It comes from the brachiocephalic trunk, so placing it on the arch is the mix-up of treating the right side as if it had separate branches like the left.","This order swaps the two left-sided branches. The left common carotid leaves the arch before the left subclavian, because the carotid sits closer to the midline."],"comp":62,"competencyId":"bvn-great-vessels"},{"id":"N-m3-upper-limb-vessels-3","dok":1,"q":"The brachiocephalic trunk divides into which two arteries?","a":"The right common carotid and right subclavian arteries.","options":["Right vertebral and right internal thoracic arteries","Right internal and right external carotid arteries","Right common carotid and right subclavian arteries","Left common carotid and left subclavian arteries"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The brachiocephalic trunk exists only on the right side. It runs up and to the right from the arch and divides into the right common carotid artery, which heads up the neck, and the right subclavian artery, which heads toward the right upper limb. Its name reflects that job: brachio means arm and cephalic means head.","notes":["The vertebral and internal thoracic arteries are branches of the subclavian artery, not of the trunk. They would be the answer for a question about branches of the subclavian.","The internal and external carotid arteries are formed when the common carotid artery divides in the neck, one level beyond the trunk. Pick them if the question asked what the right common carotid splits into.","Correct. The trunk splits into the right common carotid and right subclavian arteries.","The left common carotid and left subclavian arteries arise separately and directly from the aortic arch. They would be the answer if the question asked which two arteries leave the arch on the left side."],"comp":62,"competencyId":"bvn-great-vessels"},{"id":"N-m3-upper-limb-vessels-4","dok":1,"q":"Where does the left subclavian artery arise?","a":"Directly from the aortic arch.","options":["From the descending thoracic aorta","From the left common carotid artery","From the brachiocephalic trunk","Directly from the aortic arch"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The left subclavian artery is the third branch of the aortic arch and comes straight off the arch. On the right, the subclavian comes from the brachiocephalic trunk instead. This difference in origin is the asymmetry of the arch, since there is no left brachiocephalic trunk.","notes":["The descending thoracic aorta begins after the arch and gives off branches such as the posterior intercostal, bronchial, and esophageal arteries. Pick it for those vessels, not for any upper limb artery.","The left common carotid is a separate arch branch that runs up the neck and does not give off the subclavian. It would be the answer only for the origin of the left internal and external carotid arteries.","The brachiocephalic trunk gives rise to the right subclavian artery, not the left. This would be the answer if the question asked about the origin of the right subclavian artery.","Correct. The left subclavian is the last of the three branches leaving the arch."],"comp":62,"competencyId":"bvn-great-vessels"},{"id":"N-m3-upper-limb-vessels-5","dok":1,"q":"At which landmark does the subclavian artery change its name to the axillary artery?","a":"The lateral border of the first rib.","options":["The lower border of teres major","The sternoclavicular joint","The cubital fossa","The lateral border of the first rib"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The artery to the upper limb is one continuous vessel that changes name at landmarks. It is called the subclavian artery until it crosses the lateral (outer) border of the first rib, and from there through the armpit it is the axillary artery. Learning the landmark is what lets you name the segment you are looking at.","notes":["The lower border of teres major is the next landmark down the limb, where the axillary artery becomes the brachial artery. Pick it if the question asked where the brachial artery begins.","The right sternoclavicular joint is roughly where the brachiocephalic trunk divides into the right common carotid and right subclavian arteries. It marks where the right subclavian begins, not where it ends.","The cubital fossa, the hollow at the front of the elbow, is where the brachial artery divides into the radial and ulnar arteries. It would be the answer for where the brachial artery ends.","Correct. The subclavian becomes the axillary at the lateral border of the first rib."],"comp":88,"competencyId":"w4-lab-ue-arteries-veins"},{"id":"N-m3-upper-limb-vessels-6","dok":1,"q":"At which landmark does the axillary artery become the brachial artery?","a":"The lower border of teres major.","options":["The lower border of teres major","The lateral border of the first rib","The surgical neck of the humerus","The cubital fossa"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The axillary artery runs through the armpit and is renamed the brachial artery once it passes the lower (inferior) border of the teres major muscle. From there the brachial artery travels down the medial side of the arm to the elbow. Teres major is the muscle landmark that separates the armpit segment from the arm segment.","notes":["Correct. The axillary artery becomes the brachial artery at the lower border of teres major.","The lateral border of the first rib is where the subclavian artery becomes the axillary artery, one step earlier along the vessel. Pick it if the question asked where the axillary artery begins.","The surgical neck of the humerus is a bony landmark that the circumflex humeral arteries wrap around. It does not mark a name change for the main artery, so it fits a question about where the circumflex humeral arteries run.","The cubital fossa is where the brachial artery ends by dividing into the radial and ulnar arteries. It would be correct for the end of the brachial artery, not its start."],"comp":88,"competencyId":"w4-lab-ue-arteries-veins"},{"id":"N-m3-upper-limb-vessels-7","dok":1,"q":"Where does the brachial artery divide into the radial and ulnar arteries?","a":"In the cubital fossa, just below the elbow.","options":["In the radial groove of the humerus","At the wrist, just above the carpal bones","In the cubital fossa, just below the elbow","In the axilla"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The brachial artery runs down the medial arm and enters the cubital fossa, the triangular hollow at the front of the elbow. Just below the elbow it divides into the radial artery on the thumb side of the forearm and the ulnar artery on the little finger side. These two arteries then carry blood down the forearm to the hand.","notes":["The radial groove on the back of the humerus holds the deep brachial artery and the radial nerve, not the division of the brachial artery. Students pick it because of the look-alike word radial.","At the wrist the radial and ulnar arteries already exist as separate vessels and are heading into the hand to form the palmar arches. The wrist would fit a question about where the radial pulse is felt.","Correct. The brachial artery splits in the cubital fossa just below the elbow.","The axilla, or armpit, is the region of the axillary artery, which has not yet become the brachial artery. Pick the axilla for questions about the axillary artery or the cords of the brachial plexus."],"comp":88,"competencyId":"w4-lab-ue-arteries-veins"},{"id":"N-m3-upper-limb-vessels-8","dok":1,"q":"When a blood pressure cuff is inflated around the upper arm, which artery does it compress?","a":"The brachial artery.","options":["Deep brachial artery","Brachial artery","Axillary artery","Radial artery"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The brachial artery is the main artery of the arm, running from the lower border of teres major down to the cubital fossa. A cuff wrapped around the upper arm squeezes it against the humerus, and the sounds are listened for over the artery at the front of the elbow. That is why blood pressure readings are taken on the arm.","notes":["The deep brachial artery is a branch that runs on the back of the humerus in the radial groove with the radial nerve. It is not the vessel used to read blood pressure, so pick it only for questions about the radial groove.","Correct. The cuff compresses the brachial artery in the arm.","The axillary artery lies up in the armpit, above where a cuff sits. It would be the answer for the artery between the first rib and teres major.","The radial artery runs on the thumb side of the forearm and is used for the pulse at the wrist. Pick it for a question about taking a pulse at the wrist, not about a cuff on the upper arm."],"comp":88,"competencyId":"w4-lab-ue-arteries-veins"},{"id":"N-m3-upper-limb-vessels-9","dok":1,"q":"A pulse is felt on the anterior wrist, on the thumb side. Which artery is being felt?","a":"The radial artery.","options":["Radial artery","Ulnar artery","Common interosseous artery","Brachial artery"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The radial artery runs down the lateral (thumb) side of the forearm and lies close to the surface at the front of the wrist. It can be pressed against the radius there, which is why it is the standard site for taking a pulse. The ulnar artery runs on the opposite, little finger side.","notes":["Correct. The radial artery is felt on the thumb side of the anterior wrist.","The ulnar artery runs on the medial (little finger) side of the forearm, so it would be felt on the opposite side of the wrist. Pick it if the question described the little finger side.","The common interosseous artery is a short branch of the ulnar artery deep in the upper forearm. It lies too deep to be felt and does not reach the wrist as a named pulse.","The brachial artery ends at the elbow, so it cannot be felt at the wrist. It is the pulse felt at the front of the elbow or the artery compressed by a blood pressure cuff."],"comp":88,"competencyId":"w4-lab-ue-arteries-veins"},{"id":"N-m3-upper-limb-vessels-10","dok":1,"q":"Which is the largest branch of the axillary artery?","a":"The subscapular artery.","options":["Subscapular artery","Anterior circumflex humeral artery","Thoracoacromial artery","Lateral thoracic artery"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The axillary artery gives off several named branches in the armpit, and the subscapular artery is the largest of them. It runs down along the lateral border of the scapula toward the muscles on the back of the shoulder. The other branches are smaller and supply the chest wall, the shoulder, and the area around the humerus.","notes":["Correct. The subscapular artery is the largest branch of the axillary artery.","The anterior circumflex humeral artery is a small vessel that wraps the front of the surgical neck of the humerus. It fits a question about arteries around the neck of the humerus, not the largest branch.","The thoracoacromial artery is a short axillary branch that supplies the pectoral region and the deltoid. It would be the answer for the branch to the front of the shoulder and chest muscles.","The lateral thoracic artery runs down the lateral chest wall alongside the long thoracic nerve. Pick it if the question asked which axillary branch follows the side of the chest wall."],"comp":88,"competencyId":"w4-lab-ue-arteries-veins"},{"id":"N-m3-upper-limb-vessels-11","dok":1,"q":"Which artery travels with the radial nerve in the radial groove on the back of the humerus?","a":"The deep brachial artery.","options":["Deep brachial artery","Brachial artery","Radial artery","Posterior circumflex humeral artery"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The deep brachial artery is a branch of the brachial artery that swings around the back of the humerus. It runs in the radial groove, an oblique groove on the posterior shaft of the humerus, together with the radial nerve. A nerve and artery that share a path like this are often injured together.","notes":["Correct. The deep brachial artery runs with the radial nerve in the radial groove.","The brachial artery itself runs down the medial side of the arm with the median nerve, not in the groove on the back of the bone. It would be the answer for the main artery of the arm.","The radial artery is in the forearm on the thumb side, below the elbow, and has nothing to do with the radial groove of the humerus. Students choose it because of the shared word radial.","The posterior circumflex humeral artery wraps around the back of the surgical neck of the humerus with the axillary nerve. Pick it if the question asked which artery travels with the axillary nerve."],"comp":88,"competencyId":"w4-lab-ue-arteries-veins"},{"id":"N-m3-upper-limb-vessels-12","dok":1,"q":"The superficial palmar arch is supplied chiefly by which artery?","a":"The ulnar artery.","options":["Radial artery","Brachial artery","Ulnar artery","Common interosseous artery"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"In the hand, the radial and ulnar arteries join each other to form two arches across the palm. The superficial palmar arch, the more distal of the two, is formed mainly by the ulnar artery. The deep palmar arch, the more proximal one, is formed mainly by the radial artery.","notes":["The radial artery is the chief supply of the deep palmar arch, the more proximal arch. Pick it if the question asked about the deep arch.","The brachial artery ends at the elbow by dividing into the radial and ulnar arteries, so it never reaches the hand. It would be the answer for the artery compressed by a blood pressure cuff.","Correct. The superficial palmar arch is chiefly a continuation of the ulnar artery.","The common interosseous artery is a short branch of the ulnar artery in the upper forearm that splits into anterior and posterior interosseous arteries. It does not form either palmar arch."],"comp":88,"competencyId":"w4-lab-ue-arteries-veins"},{"id":"N-m3-upper-limb-vessels-13","dok":1,"q":"The vertebral artery, the internal thoracic artery, the thyrocervical trunk, and the costocervical trunk all branch from which artery?","a":"The subclavian artery.","options":["Common carotid artery","Axillary artery","Subclavian artery","Descending thoracic aorta"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Before it crosses the first rib, the subclavian artery gives off several branches to the neck, brain, and chest wall. The vertebral artery climbs to the brain, the internal thoracic artery runs down the inside of the front chest wall, and the thyrocervical and costocervical trunks supply the neck and upper chest. All of them leave the subclavian before it becomes the axillary artery.","notes":["The common carotid artery runs up the neck and divides into the internal and external carotid arteries, with no branches to the chest wall. Pick it for the arteries that supply the face and the front of the brain.","The axillary artery begins at the first rib, after these branches have already left. Its own branches include the thoracoacromial, lateral thoracic, subscapular, and circumflex humeral arteries.","Correct. All four vessels are branches of the subclavian artery.","The descending thoracic aorta gives off the posterior intercostal, bronchial, and esophageal arteries. It would be the answer for those vessels, which supply the chest wall from behind and the organs of the thorax."],"comp":88,"competencyId":"w4-lab-ue-arteries-veins"},{"id":"N-m3-upper-limb-vessels-14","dok":1,"q":"As the vertebral artery ascends the neck toward the brain, which openings does it pass through?","a":"The transverse foramina of the cervical vertebrae.","options":["Carotid canals of the temporal bones","Transverse foramina of cervical vertebrae","Intervertebral foramina","Vertebral foramina"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The vertebral artery leaves the subclavian artery and climbs the neck through the transverse foramina, the small holes in the transverse processes that are found only in cervical vertebrae. It then enters the skull through the foramen magnum to help supply the brain. The transverse foramina are the feature that identifies a cervical vertebra.","notes":["The carotid canals are openings in the temporal bones that carry the internal carotid arteries into the skull. Choose them for the internal carotid artery, not the vertebral artery.","Correct. The vertebral artery rises through the transverse foramina of the cervical vertebrae.","The intervertebral foramina are the openings between neighboring vertebrae where spinal nerves exit. Pick them if the question asked where spinal nerves leave the vertebral column.","The vertebral foramina line up to form the vertebral canal, which holds the spinal cord. They would be the answer for the passage of the spinal cord, not an artery."],"comp":88,"competencyId":"w4-lab-ue-arteries-veins"},{"id":"N-m3-upper-limb-vessels-15","dok":1,"q":"Which superficial vein ascends the lateral (thumb) side of the forearm and arm and passes through the deltopectoral groove to join the axillary vein?","a":"The cephalic vein.","options":["Cephalic vein","Median antebrachial vein","Basilic vein","Median cubital vein"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The cephalic vein starts on the back of the hand and climbs the lateral side of the forearm and arm just under the skin. Near the shoulder it runs in the deltopectoral groove, the furrow between the deltoid and pectoralis major, and then dives deep to join the axillary vein. Its lateral course is what tells it apart from the basilic vein.","notes":["Correct. The cephalic vein runs up the lateral side and through the deltopectoral groove.","The median antebrachial vein runs up the middle of the anterior forearm and stays below the elbow. It would be the answer for the vein along the center of the front of the forearm.","The basilic vein ascends the medial (little finger) side of the forearm and arm and joins the brachial veins to form the axillary vein. Pick it if the question described the medial side.","The median cubital vein is a short connecting vein that crosses the front of the elbow between the cephalic and basilic veins. It is the usual blood draw site, not a vein that climbs to the shoulder."],"comp":88,"competencyId":"w4-lab-ue-arteries-veins"},{"id":"N-m3-upper-limb-vessels-16","dok":1,"q":"Which superficial vein ascends the medial side of the forearm and arm and then joins the brachial veins to form the axillary vein?","a":"The basilic vein.","options":["Median cubital vein","Cephalic vein","Subclavian vein","Basilic vein"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The basilic vein runs up the medial (little finger) side of the forearm and arm. Partway up the arm it passes deep and joins the paired brachial veins, and together they form the axillary vein. The cephalic vein is its mirror image on the lateral side.","notes":["The median cubital vein is the short link between the cephalic and basilic veins at the front of the elbow. It would be the answer for the usual site of a blood draw.","The cephalic vein is the lateral superficial vein that passes through the deltopectoral groove to reach the axillary vein. Pick it if the question described the thumb side.","The subclavian vein is a deep vein near the clavicle, formed when the axillary vein crosses the first rib. It fits a question about the vein that joins the internal jugular vein.","Correct. The basilic vein ascends medially and joins the brachial veins to form the axillary vein."],"comp":88,"competencyId":"w4-lab-ue-arteries-veins"},{"id":"N-m3-upper-limb-vessels-17","dok":1,"q":"The median cubital vein connects which two veins across the front of the elbow?","a":"The cephalic and basilic veins.","options":["The cephalic and basilic veins","The radial and ulnar veins","The subclavian and internal jugular veins","The brachial and axillary veins"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The median cubital vein is a short superficial vein that runs diagonally across the cubital fossa, the hollow at the front of the elbow. It links the cephalic vein on the lateral side to the basilic vein on the medial side. Because it is large and close to the skin, it is the usual site for drawing blood.","notes":["Correct. The median cubital vein links the cephalic and basilic veins.","The radial and ulnar veins are deep, paired veins that travel beside the arteries of the same names in the forearm. Pick them for a question about deep veins of the forearm.","The subclavian and internal jugular veins join near the clavicle to form the brachiocephalic vein. They would be the answer for a question about how a brachiocephalic vein forms.","The brachial veins join the basilic vein to form the axillary vein, so they are connected in series, not by a crossing vein. This pair fits a question about how the axillary vein forms."],"comp":88,"competencyId":"w4-lab-ue-arteries-veins"},{"id":"N-m3-upper-limb-vessels-18","dok":1,"q":"Each brachiocephalic vein is formed by the union of which two veins?","a":"The subclavian vein and the internal jugular vein.","options":["Subclavian and internal jugular veins","Azygos and hemiazygos veins","Basilic and brachial veins","Axillary and cephalic veins"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Behind each sternoclavicular joint, the subclavian vein from the upper limb meets the internal jugular vein from the head and neck. Their union forms the brachiocephalic vein on that side. This happens on both the right and the left, so there are two brachiocephalic veins.","notes":["Correct. The subclavian and internal jugular veins unite to form each brachiocephalic vein.","The hemiazygos vein crosses the midline to drain into the azygos vein, which then empties into the superior vena cava. Pick this pair for a question about veins draining the posterior thoracic wall.","The basilic vein and the brachial veins join to form the axillary vein, two steps before the brachiocephalic vein. They would be the answer for how the axillary vein forms.","The cephalic vein empties into the axillary vein, and the axillary then continues as the subclavian vein. This pair fits a question about where the cephalic vein ends."],"comp":62,"competencyId":"bvn-great-vessels"},{"id":"N-m3-upper-limb-vessels-19","dok":1,"q":"The right and left brachiocephalic veins join to form which vessel?","a":"The superior vena cava.","options":["Inferior vena cava","Pulmonary trunk","Azygos vein","Superior vena cava"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The two brachiocephalic veins meet behind the right side of the sternum to form the superior vena cava. The superior vena cava carries blood from the head, neck, upper limbs, and chest wall down into the right atrium. It is the final step for venous blood returning from the upper limb.","notes":["The inferior vena cava drains the lower limbs, pelvis, and abdomen and enters the right atrium from below. Pick it if the question asked about blood returning from below the diaphragm.","The pulmonary trunk carries blood out of the right ventricle to the lungs. It is an artery leaving the heart, so it would fit a question about the vessel leaving the right ventricle.","The azygos vein drains the posterior thoracic wall and empties into the superior vena cava, so it is a tributary, not the vessel formed by the brachiocephalic veins.","Correct. The two brachiocephalic veins form the superior vena cava."],"comp":62,"competencyId":"bvn-great-vessels"},{"id":"N-m3-upper-limb-vessels-20","dok":1,"q":"Which arteries, branching from the thoracic aorta, supply the tissue of the lungs themselves rather than carrying blood for gas exchange?","a":"The bronchial arteries.","options":["Posterior intercostal arteries","Esophageal arteries","Pulmonary arteries","Bronchial arteries"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Lung tissue needs its own oxygen-rich supply, just like any other organ. The bronchial arteries branch from the thoracic aorta and feed the walls of the bronchi and the supporting tissue of the lungs. The pulmonary arteries carry blood to the alveoli for gas exchange, which is a separate job.","notes":["The posterior intercostal arteries leave the thoracic aorta to run in the intercostal spaces with the intercostal nerve and vein. Pick them for the blood supply of the chest wall between the ribs.","The esophageal arteries also branch from the thoracic aorta, but they supply the esophagus. They would be the answer for the blood supply of the esophagus in the thorax.","The pulmonary arteries come from the pulmonary trunk and carry oxygen-poor blood to the lungs for gas exchange. Pick them if the question asked which vessels bring blood to the lungs to pick up oxygen.","Correct. The bronchial arteries supply the lung tissue itself."]},{"id":"N-m3-upper-limb-vessels-21","dok":1,"q":"Which vein ascends along the right side of the vertebral column, drains the posterior thoracic wall, and empties into the superior vena cava?","a":"The azygos vein.","options":["Internal thoracic vein","Azygos vein","Left brachiocephalic vein","Hemiazygos vein"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The azygos vein runs up the right side of the vertebral bodies in the posterior thorax, collecting blood from the posterior intercostal veins. At the top it arches forward over the root of the right lung and empties into the superior vena cava. It is the main drain of the back of the chest wall.","notes":["The internal thoracic vein runs down the inside of the anterior chest wall beside the internal thoracic artery. It would fit a question about veins of the front of the chest wall.","Correct. The azygos vein runs on the right and empties into the superior vena cava.","The left brachiocephalic vein crosses the upper chest from left to right in front of the arch branches, carrying blood from the left head and upper limb. Pick it for a question about the vein that crosses the midline to help form the superior vena cava.","The hemiazygos vein runs on the left side of the vertebral column and crosses the midline to empty into the azygos vein. Pick it if the question described the left side."]},{"id":"N-m3-upper-limb-vessels-22","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly explains why there is one brachiocephalic artery but two brachiocephalic veins?","a":"The left side gets separate arteries off the arch, but both sides form their own brachiocephalic vein.","options":["The brachiocephalic artery serves both upper limbs, so only one is needed, while each vein drains only one side","Arteries always occur singly in the midline, while veins always occur in pairs, so every artery has two matching veins","The left upper limb is fed by the thoracic aorta instead, while each side drains through its own brachiocephalic vein","The left upper limb and left head get separate arteries off the arch, but both sides form their own brachiocephalic vein"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"On the arterial side, the arch gives the right side one shared stem, the brachiocephalic trunk, while the left common carotid and left subclavian arteries leave the arch separately, so there is no left trunk. On the venous side, both the right and the left subclavian veins meet their internal jugular veins to form a brachiocephalic vein. The asymmetry of the arch shows up as one artery against two veins.","notes":["The brachiocephalic trunk supplies only the right upper limb and the right side of the head and neck. Students pick this when they forget that the left upper limb has its own subclavian artery.","This is an overgeneralization. Many arteries are paired, such as the two subclavian and two common carotid arteries, so the rule does not exist.","The left upper limb is supplied by the left subclavian artery from the arch, not by the thoracic aorta. This option gets the venous half right but invents the wrong arterial source, which is the descending aorta mix-up.","Correct. The left side has separate arterial branches, but both sides build a brachiocephalic vein."],"comp":62,"competencyId":"bvn-great-vessels"},{"id":"N-m3-upper-limb-vessels-23","dok":2,"q":"Blood travels from the aortic arch to the left radial artery. Which sequence of arteries does it pass through?","a":"Left subclavian, axillary, brachial, radial.","options":["Left subclavian, axillary, brachial, radial","Left common carotid, left subclavian, axillary, radial","Brachiocephalic trunk, left subclavian, axillary, brachial, radial","Left subclavian, brachial, axillary, radial"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"On the left, the subclavian artery leaves the aortic arch directly, so there is no brachiocephalic step. The vessel is renamed axillary at the lateral border of the first rib and brachial at the lower border of teres major, and the brachial artery then divides in the cubital fossa into the radial and ulnar arteries.","notes":["Correct. Left subclavian, axillary, brachial, then radial is the left side route.","The left common carotid artery runs up the neck and does not give rise to the subclavian. This sequence also skips the brachial artery, which every route to the forearm must pass through.","The brachiocephalic trunk exists only on the right. This sequence would be correct for blood heading to the right upper limb, where the subclavian comes from the trunk.","This puts brachial before axillary, which reverses the order. The axillary artery is in the armpit and comes first, then the brachial artery in the arm."],"comp":88,"competencyId":"w4-lab-ue-arteries-veins"},{"id":"N-m3-upper-limb-vessels-24","dok":2,"q":"Blood travels from the aortic arch to the right ulnar artery. Which sequence of arteries does it pass through?","a":"Brachiocephalic trunk, right subclavian, axillary, brachial, ulnar.","options":["Brachiocephalic trunk, right common carotid, axillary, brachial, ulnar","Brachiocephalic trunk, right subclavian, axillary, brachial, ulnar","Brachiocephalic trunk, right subclavian, brachial, axillary, ulnar","Right subclavian, axillary, brachial, ulnar"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"On the right, blood first enters the brachiocephalic trunk, which divides into the right common carotid and right subclavian arteries. The subclavian becomes the axillary at the first rib and the brachial at teres major, and the brachial divides below the elbow into the radial and ulnar arteries. The right side has one more named step than the left because of the trunk.","notes":["The right common carotid artery goes up the neck to the head, not to the upper limb. Pick it only for a route from the arch to the right side of the face or brain.","Correct. Brachiocephalic trunk, right subclavian, axillary, brachial, then ulnar is the right side route.","This reverses axillary and brachial. The axillary artery lies in the armpit and comes before the brachial artery of the arm.","This sequence leaves out the brachiocephalic trunk. It would be correct for the left side, where the subclavian artery comes straight off the arch."],"comp":88,"competencyId":"w4-lab-ue-arteries-veins"},{"id":"N-m3-upper-limb-vessels-25","dok":2,"q":"In the lab you point to the segment of the main upper limb artery that lies between the lateral border of the first rib and the lower border of teres major. What is this segment called?","a":"The axillary artery.","options":["Axillary artery","Subclavian artery","Deep brachial artery","Brachial artery"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The name of the main artery of the upper limb depends on where you are along it. Between the lateral border of the first rib and the lower border of teres major, it passes through the axilla (armpit) and is called the axillary artery. Above that segment it is the subclavian artery, and below it is the brachial artery.","notes":["Correct. Between the first rib and teres major the vessel is the axillary artery.","The subclavian artery is the segment before the first rib, arching over the rib behind the clavicle. Pick it for the part of the vessel medial to the first rib.","The deep brachial artery is a branch of the brachial artery that runs in the radial groove. It is a side branch, not a segment of the main vessel, so it fits a question about the radial groove.","The brachial artery begins at the lower border of teres major and runs down the arm. It would be the answer for the segment between teres major and the cubital fossa."],"comp":88,"competencyId":"w4-lab-ue-arteries-veins"},{"id":"N-m3-upper-limb-vessels-26","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares the superficial and deep palmar arches?","a":"The superficial arch is more distal and mainly ulnar; the deep arch is more proximal and mainly radial.","options":["The superficial arch lies on the back of the hand, and the deep arch lies in the palm","The superficial arch is more distal and mainly ulnar; the deep arch is more proximal and mainly radial","Both arches are formed only by the ulnar artery, one lying on top of the other","The superficial arch is more proximal and mainly radial; the deep arch is more distal and mainly ulnar"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Both palmar arches are joins between the radial and ulnar arteries in the palm. The superficial palmar arch lies nearer the fingers (more distal) and is formed mainly by the ulnar artery. The deep palmar arch lies nearer the wrist (more proximal) and is formed mainly by the radial artery.","notes":["Both arches are in the palm, as their name palmar says. The back of the hand drains through the dorsal venous network, which would fit a question about superficial veins of the hand.","Correct. The superficial arch is distal and mainly ulnar, and the deep arch is proximal and mainly radial.","Both arches are formed by the radial and ulnar arteries together, which is the whole point of an arch. This option misses that each arch is a connection between the two arteries.","This statement swaps every feature of the two arches. Students pick it when they try to match radial with superficial because the radial pulse is superficial at the wrist."],"comp":88,"competencyId":"w4-lab-ue-arteries-veins"},{"id":"N-m3-upper-limb-vessels-27","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares the superficial and deep veins of the upper limb?","a":"Superficial veins run under the skin on their own paths, while deep veins are paired and travel beside the arteries of the same names.","options":["Superficial veins carry blood away from the heart, while deep veins carry blood back toward the heart","Superficial veins take their own paths under the skin, while deep veins are paired beside the arteries of the same names","Superficial veins travel deep beside the arteries of the same names, while the deep veins run alone just under the skin","The upper limb has only deep veins, because large superficial veins are found only in the lower limb"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The superficial veins of the upper limb, such as the cephalic, basilic, and median cubital veins, run in the tissue just under the skin and follow their own routes. The deep veins, such as the radial, ulnar, and brachial veins, are paired and travel alongside the artery with the same name. In the upper limb the superficial veins are the large, visible ones, which is the reverse of the lower limb.","notes":["All veins carry blood toward the heart; carrying blood away from the heart is what arteries do. This is the mix-up of thinking depth decides direction of flow.","Correct. Superficial veins take their own paths, and deep veins are paired beside arteries of the same name.","This statement reverses the two systems. The deep veins are the ones that accompany named arteries, while the cephalic and basilic veins take their own paths under the skin.","The upper limb has well developed superficial veins, including the cephalic, basilic, and median cubital veins. They are in fact the large veins used for blood draws."],"comp":88,"competencyId":"w4-lab-ue-arteries-veins"},{"id":"N-m3-upper-limb-vessels-28","dok":2,"q":"Blood in the cephalic vein of the arm returns to the heart. Which sequence of veins does it follow?","a":"Cephalic, axillary, subclavian, brachiocephalic, superior vena cava.","options":["Cephalic, basilic, brachial, axillary, superior vena cava","Cephalic, axillary, subclavian, internal jugular, superior vena cava","Cephalic, axillary, subclavian, brachiocephalic, superior vena cava","Cephalic, subclavian, axillary, brachiocephalic, superior vena cava"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The cephalic vein passes through the deltopectoral groove and empties into the axillary vein. The axillary vein becomes the subclavian vein at the first rib, the subclavian joins the internal jugular to form the brachiocephalic vein, and the two brachiocephalic veins form the superior vena cava, which enters the right atrium.","notes":["The cephalic vein does not drain into the basilic or brachial veins on its way up; it joins the axillary vein directly. This route also skips the subclavian and brachiocephalic veins.","The internal jugular vein carries blood down from the head and meets the subclavian vein, so blood from the arm does not flow into it. The vessel formed at that meeting, the brachiocephalic vein, is the correct next step.","Correct. Cephalic, axillary, subclavian, brachiocephalic, then superior vena cava.","This swaps the axillary and subclavian veins. The axillary vein lies in the armpit and comes first, then the subclavian vein near the clavicle."],"comp":88,"competencyId":"w4-lab-ue-arteries-veins"},{"id":"N-m3-upper-limb-vessels-29","dok":2,"q":"A medication is injected into the basilic vein in the arm. As the blood rises toward the shoulder, which named vein does it enter next?","a":"Axillary vein","options":["Subclavian vein","Cephalic vein","Axillary vein","Internal jugular vein"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The basilic vein runs up the medial arm and joins the paired brachial veins, and their union forms the axillary vein. So the next named vein after the basilic is the axillary vein, which then becomes the subclavian vein at the first rib.","notes":["The subclavian vein comes one step later, after the axillary vein crosses the first rib. It would be the answer if the question asked where blood goes after the axillary vein.","The cephalic vein is the other superficial vein, on the lateral side of the arm, and the basilic vein does not drain into it. Pick it for the lateral vein that passes through the deltopectoral groove to reach the axillary vein.","Correct. The basilic vein joins the brachial veins to form the axillary vein.","The internal jugular vein drains the head and neck downward and meets the subclavian vein. It would fit a question about which vein joins the subclavian to form the brachiocephalic vein."],"comp":88,"competencyId":"w4-lab-ue-arteries-veins"},{"id":"N-m3-upper-limb-vessels-30","dok":2,"q":"The subclavian artery becomes the axillary artery at the lateral border of the first rib. Where does the axillary vein become the subclavian vein?","a":"At the lateral border of the first rib.","options":["At the lower border of teres major","At the deltopectoral groove","At the sternoclavicular joint","At the lateral border of the first rib"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The deep veins follow the arteries, so the same landmark changes the name of both vessels. The axillary vein becomes the subclavian vein as it crosses the lateral border of the first rib, just as the subclavian artery becomes the axillary artery there. The only difference is direction: blood in the vein is flowing toward the heart.","notes":["The lower border of teres major is where the brachial veins and basilic vein become the axillary vein, and where the axillary artery becomes the brachial artery. Pick it for the lower end of the axillary vessels.","The deltopectoral groove is where the cephalic vein runs before it dives to join the axillary vein. It would be the answer for the path of the cephalic vein.","The sternoclavicular joint is roughly where the subclavian vein joins the internal jugular vein to form the brachiocephalic vein. It marks the end of the subclavian vein, not its start.","Correct. The axillary vein becomes the subclavian vein at the lateral border of the first rib."],"comp":88,"competencyId":"w4-lab-ue-arteries-veins"},{"id":"N-m3-upper-limb-vessels-31","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares the anterior and posterior intercostal arteries?","a":"The anterior intercostals branch from the internal thoracic artery, and the posterior intercostals branch from the thoracic aorta.","options":["Both sets branch from the subclavian artery before it crosses the first rib","Both sets branch from the axillary artery as it passes through the armpit","The anterior intercostals branch from the internal thoracic artery, and the posterior intercostals branch from the thoracic aorta","The anterior intercostals branch from the thoracic aorta, and the posterior intercostals branch from the internal thoracic artery"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Each intercostal space gets blood from both ends. The anterior intercostal arteries come from the internal thoracic artery, which runs down the inside of the front chest wall beside the sternum. The posterior intercostal arteries come from the thoracic aorta, which lies against the vertebral column at the back, and they run in the intercostal space with the intercostal nerve and vein.","notes":["The subclavian artery gives rise to the internal thoracic artery, so it is only the indirect source of the anterior set. This option is the mix-up of naming the parent vessel instead of the direct branch.","The axillary artery supplies the shoulder, the lateral chest wall, and the pectoral region through branches like the lateral thoracic artery. It would fit a question about the arteries of the armpit, not the intercostal spaces.","Correct. Anterior intercostals come from the internal thoracic artery, and posterior intercostals come from the thoracic aorta.","This statement swaps the two sources. Think about location: the internal thoracic artery runs down the front of the chest, so it feeds the front ends of the spaces, and the aorta lies at the back."]},{"id":"N-m3-upper-limb-vessels-32","dok":2,"q":"A cardiac surgeon needs an artery that runs down the inside of the anterior chest wall, just beside the sternum, to use as a coronary bypass graft. Which artery is it?","a":"The internal thoracic artery.","options":["Posterior intercostal artery","Internal thoracic artery","Lateral thoracic artery","Thoracoacromial artery"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The internal thoracic artery branches from the subclavian artery and runs down the deep surface of the anterior chest wall, just lateral to the sternum. Because it lies so close to the heart, surgeons can redirect it to carry blood past a blocked coronary artery. It also gives off the anterior intercostal arteries along the way.","notes":["The posterior intercostal arteries arise from the thoracic aorta at the back of the chest and run in the intercostal spaces. They would fit a question about the arteries that run with the intercostal nerve from behind.","Correct. The internal thoracic artery runs beside the sternum and is used as a bypass graft.","The lateral thoracic artery is a branch of the axillary artery that runs down the side of the chest wall with the long thoracic nerve. Pick it if the question described the lateral chest wall.","The thoracoacromial artery is a short branch of the axillary artery that supplies the pectoral region and the deltoid. It lies near the shoulder, not along the sternum."],"comp":88,"competencyId":"w4-lab-ue-arteries-veins"},{"id":"N-m3-upper-limb-vessels-33","dok":2,"q":"Which statement best explains why the median cubital vein is the usual site for drawing blood?","a":"It is a large superficial vein over the cubital fossa, easy to see and reach.","options":["It is a deep vein, so it is protected from bruising after the needle is removed","It has no structures beneath it, so the needle cannot hit anything if it goes too deep","It is a large superficial vein over the cubital fossa, easy to see and reach","It carries oxygen-rich blood straight from the heart, so the sample is fresher"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The median cubital vein crosses the front of the elbow just under the skin, linking the cephalic and basilic veins. It is large, easy to see and feel, and sits over the cubital fossa where the arm can be held straight and still. The brachial artery and median nerve lie just deep to it, separated only by the bicipital aponeurosis, a flat sheet of tendon, so the draw is done carefully.","notes":["The median cubital vein is superficial, not deep. A deep vein would be hard to find through the skin, which is the opposite of what a blood draw needs.","The brachial artery and median nerve lie just deep to the vein, separated only by the bicipital aponeurosis. That is exactly why the needle must not go too deep.","Correct. It is large, superficial, and easy to reach over the cubital fossa.","Veins carry blood toward the heart, and blood in the arm veins has already given up oxygen to the tissues. This is the mix-up of thinking a blood draw samples an artery."],"comp":88,"competencyId":"w4-lab-ue-arteries-veins"},{"id":"N-m3-upper-limb-vessels-34","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares the right and left brachiocephalic veins?","a":"The left one is longer because it must cross the midline to meet the right one and form the superior vena cava.","options":["Both are the same length and join in the exact midline to form the inferior vena cava","The left one is longer because it must cross the midline to reach the superior vena cava","Only the right side has a brachiocephalic vein, matching the single brachiocephalic artery","The right one is longer because it must cross the midline to reach the heart"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The superior vena cava lies on the right side of the upper chest. The right brachiocephalic vein is short and runs almost straight down into it, while the left brachiocephalic vein must cross the midline from left to right to reach it, so the left one is longer. Both are formed by a subclavian vein joining an internal jugular vein.","notes":["The brachiocephalic veins form the superior vena cava, not the inferior vena cava, which drains the body below the diaphragm. They also differ in length, because the superior vena cava sits to the right of the midline.","Correct. The left brachiocephalic vein is longer because it crosses the midline.","There are two brachiocephalic veins, one on each side, even though there is only one brachiocephalic artery. This is the mix-up of assuming the veins copy the arterial asymmetry.","The superior vena cava is on the right, so it is the left vein that has to cross the midline. This option reverses the sides."],"comp":62,"competencyId":"bvn-great-vessels"},{"id":"N-m3-upper-limb-vessels-35","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares the azygos and hemiazygos veins?","a":"The azygos runs on the right and empties into the superior vena cava; the hemiazygos runs on the left and crosses over to join the azygos.","options":["The azygos runs on the right into the superior vena cava; the hemiazygos crosses from the left to join it","Both veins run along the front of the sternum and drain the breast","The azygos runs on the left and empties into the inferior vena cava; the hemiazygos runs on the right","Both veins drain the upper limb and empty into the axillary vein near the first rib"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Both veins drain the posterior thoracic wall through the posterior intercostal veins. The azygos vein runs up the right side of the vertebral column and arches forward to empty into the superior vena cava. The hemiazygos vein runs on the left side and crosses the midline behind the aorta to drain into the azygos.","notes":["Correct. The azygos is on the right and ends in the superior vena cava, and the hemiazygos crosses from the left to join it.","The vein running down the inside of the front chest wall beside the sternum is the internal thoracic vein. Pick it if the question described the anterior chest wall.","This reverses the sides and sends the azygos to the wrong vena cava. The azygos is on the right and ends in the superior vena cava.","Veins from the upper limb, such as the cephalic and basilic veins, drain into the axillary vein. The azygos system drains the back of the chest wall instead."]},{"id":"N-m3-upper-limb-vessels-36","dok":2,"q":"Tracing the ulnar artery just below the cubital fossa, you find a short branch that soon splits into anterior and posterior interosseous arteries. What is this short branch?","a":"The common interosseous artery.","options":["Radial artery","Posterior circumflex humeral artery","Deep brachial artery","Common interosseous artery"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The common interosseous artery is a short branch of the ulnar artery in the upper forearm. It quickly divides into an anterior interosseous artery and a posterior interosseous artery, which run on either side of the interosseous membrane, the sheet of connective tissue between the radius and ulna. The name interosseous means between bones.","notes":["The radial artery is the other terminal branch of the brachial artery, not a branch of the ulnar artery. Pick it for the artery on the thumb side of the forearm.","The posterior circumflex humeral artery is a branch of the axillary artery that wraps the surgical neck of the humerus. It is far above the forearm, so it fits only a question about the shoulder.","The deep brachial artery branches from the brachial artery in the arm and runs in the radial groove of the humerus. It would fit a question about the artery traveling with the radial nerve.","Correct. The common interosseous artery branches from the ulnar artery and splits into anterior and posterior interosseous arteries."],"comp":88,"competencyId":"w4-lab-ue-arteries-veins"},{"id":"N-m3-upper-limb-vessels-37","dok":3,"q":"While taking a blood pressure, a nurse places the stethoscope in the cubital fossa just medial to the biceps brachii tendon, below the cuff. Which artery is the nurse listening over?","a":"The brachial artery.","options":["Axillary artery","Ulnar artery","Radial artery","Brachial artery"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The brachial artery runs down the medial side of the arm and enters the cubital fossa just medial to the biceps tendon, before it divides into the radial and ulnar arteries. That is where the stethoscope is placed, below the cuff that compresses the same artery higher up the arm. Knowing that the brachial artery has not yet divided at this point is what makes it the answer.","notes":["The axillary artery is in the armpit, above the cuff and far from the elbow. Pick it for the segment between the first rib and teres major.","The ulnar artery also begins below the elbow and runs on the little finger side of the forearm. It would fit a question about the main supply of the superficial palmar arch.","The radial artery begins below the elbow and is felt at the thumb side of the wrist. Pick it if the question described a pulse taken at the wrist.","Correct. The stethoscope sits over the brachial artery medial to the biceps tendon."],"comp":88,"competencyId":"w4-lab-ue-arteries-veins"},{"id":"N-m3-upper-limb-vessels-38","dok":3,"q":"A person cuts the anterior wrist on the thumb side with broken glass, and bright red blood spurts in rhythm with the heartbeat. Which artery has most likely been cut?","a":"The radial artery.","options":["Radial artery","Median cubital vein","Ulnar artery","Cephalic vein"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Bright red blood that spurts with each heartbeat comes from an artery, not a vein. On the anterior wrist, the artery on the thumb side is the radial artery, the same vessel used to take the pulse. It lies close to the surface there, which is why it can be felt easily and also cut easily.","notes":["Correct. The radial artery lies near the surface on the thumb side of the anterior wrist.","The median cubital vein is at the front of the elbow, not the wrist, and it is a vein. Pick it for a question about the usual blood draw site.","The ulnar artery is on the little finger side of the wrist. It would be the answer if the cut were on the opposite side of the wrist.","The cephalic vein does start on the thumb side, but blood from a cut vein is darker and flows steadily rather than spurting. Pick it if the wound oozed dark blood on the lateral forearm."],"comp":88,"competencyId":"w4-lab-ue-arteries-veins"},{"id":"N-m3-upper-limb-vessels-39","dok":3,"q":"A catheter is placed in a patient's radial artery at the wrist, and the vessel becomes temporarily blocked. Why does the hand still receive blood?","a":"The ulnar artery still supplies the hand, and the palmar arches connect it to the radial side.","options":["The deep brachial artery takes over and runs directly down into the hand","The cephalic vein reverses direction and carries blood into the hand","The hand receives its blood from the anterior intercostal arteries","The ulnar artery still feeds the palmar arches, which connect to the radial side"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The radial and ulnar arteries join each other in the palm to form the superficial and deep palmar arches. A connection like this between two arteries is called an anastomosis, and it means each artery can fill the arches if the other is blocked. This second supply from the ulnar side is why the radial artery can be used for a catheter fairly safely.","notes":["The deep brachial artery stays in the arm, running in the radial groove of the humerus. It does not reach the hand, so it fits a question about the radial groove.","Veins carry blood toward the heart and have valves that prevent backward flow, so the cephalic vein cannot deliver blood to the hand. This is the mix-up of thinking a vein can stand in for an artery.","The anterior intercostal arteries supply the front of the intercostal spaces in the chest wall. They would fit a question about the branches of the internal thoracic artery.","Correct. The ulnar artery keeps feeding the hand through the palmar arches."],"comp":69,"competencyId":"bvn-circulatory-routes"},{"id":"N-m3-upper-limb-vessels-40","dok":3,"q":"A fall fractures the surgical neck of the humerus. Which artery, wrapping around the back of that neck with the axillary nerve, is most at risk?","a":"The posterior circumflex humeral artery.","options":["Deep brachial artery","Subscapular artery","Posterior circumflex humeral artery","Anterior circumflex humeral artery"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The anterior and posterior circumflex humeral arteries are branches of the axillary artery that circle the surgical neck, the narrowing just below the tubercles of the humerus. The posterior circumflex humeral artery travels around the back of the neck together with the axillary nerve. A fracture at this site can injure both the artery and the nerve that run with it.","notes":["The deep brachial artery runs in the radial groove along the middle of the humeral shaft. It would be the vessel at risk in a mid-shaft fracture, not a fracture of the surgical neck.","The subscapular artery is the largest branch of the axillary artery and runs down along the scapula. It does not wrap the humerus, so it fits a question about the largest axillary branch.","Correct. The posterior circumflex humeral artery wraps the surgical neck with the axillary nerve.","The anterior circumflex humeral artery is the smaller vessel that wraps the front of the surgical neck. It does not travel with the axillary nerve around the back, so pick it if the question asked about the artery on the front of the neck."],"comp":88,"competencyId":"w4-lab-ue-arteries-veins"},{"id":"N-m3-upper-limb-vessels-41","dok":3,"q":"A fracture through the middle of the humeral shaft damages the radial nerve in the radial groove. Which artery, running in the same groove, could be torn as well?","a":"The deep brachial artery.","options":["Posterior circumflex humeral artery","Deep brachial artery","Axillary artery","Radial artery"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The deep brachial artery branches from the brachial artery and spirals around the back of the humerus in the radial groove, right beside the radial nerve. Both lie pressed against the bone at mid-shaft, so a fracture line there can injure the artery and the nerve together. Structures that share a groove share the same risk.","notes":["The posterior circumflex humeral artery wraps the surgical neck higher up the bone, with the axillary nerve. Pick it for a fracture of the surgical neck.","Correct. The deep brachial artery runs with the radial nerve in the radial groove.","The axillary artery lies in the armpit above the humeral shaft. It would fit a question about the segment of the main artery between the first rib and teres major.","The radial artery is in the forearm on the thumb side and is not in the radial groove of the humerus. Students choose it because it shares the word radial with the nerve and the groove."],"comp":88,"competencyId":"w4-lab-ue-arteries-veins"},{"id":"N-m3-upper-limb-vessels-42","dok":2,"q":"A medication is injected into the cephalic vein on the forearm. Which great vein will deliver it into the right atrium?","a":"The superior vena cava.","options":["Pulmonary vein","Superior vena cava","Inferior vena cava","Coronary sinus"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The cephalic vein drains into the axillary vein, which becomes the subclavian vein, which joins the internal jugular to form the brachiocephalic vein. The two brachiocephalic veins form the superior vena cava, which empties into the right atrium. Everything from the upper limbs, head, and neck returns to the heart this way.","notes":["The pulmonary veins carry oxygen-rich blood from the lungs into the left atrium. They would be the answer for blood returning from the lungs, not from the arm.","Correct. Blood from the upper limb reaches the right atrium through the superior vena cava.","The inferior vena cava returns blood from the body below the diaphragm. It would be the answer for a medication injected into a vein of the leg.","The coronary sinus drains the heart wall itself into the right atrium. Pick it for a question about where the cardiac veins empty."],"comp":88,"competencyId":"w4-lab-ue-arteries-veins"},{"id":"N-m3-upper-limb-vessels-43","dok":3,"q":"A clot blocks the brachiocephalic trunk near its origin. Which regions lose much of their arterial supply?","a":"The right upper limb and the right side of the head and neck.","options":["The left upper limb and the left side of the head and neck","Both upper limbs, but not the head and neck","The left side of the head and neck only","The right upper limb and the right side of the head and neck"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The brachiocephalic trunk is the shared stem for the right side only, dividing into the right common carotid artery to the head and neck and the right subclavian artery to the upper limb. A block at its origin cuts off both of those branches. The left side is unaffected because the left common carotid and left subclavian arteries leave the arch on their own.","notes":["The left side of the head, neck, and upper limb is fed by the left common carotid and left subclavian arteries straight off the arch. This option would be right if the question described blocks in both of those arteries.","The left upper limb has its own subclavian artery from the arch, and the trunk does feed the right head and neck. This is the mix-up of thinking the trunk serves both arms.","The left side of the head and neck is fed by the left common carotid artery. Pick this option for a block in the left common carotid artery.","Correct. The trunk serves the right upper limb and the right side of the head and neck."],"comp":62,"competencyId":"bvn-great-vessels"},{"id":"N-m3-upper-limb-vessels-44","dok":3,"q":"During a blood draw from the median cubital vein, the needle goes too deep. Which structures lie just beneath the vein and are at risk?","a":"The brachial artery and the median nerve.","options":["The radial artery and the radial nerve","The brachial artery and the median nerve","The deep brachial artery and the axillary nerve","The ulnar artery and the ulnar nerve"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"In the cubital fossa, the brachial artery and the median nerve lie immediately deep to the median cubital vein. The only thing separating them is the bicipital aponeurosis, a flat sheet of tendon from the biceps. That is why a blood draw is taken at this site, and why it is taken carefully.","notes":["The radial artery begins below the cubital fossa and runs down the thumb side of the forearm, and the radial nerve lies on the lateral side of the elbow. Pick this pair for a deep cut on the lateral side.","Correct. The brachial artery and median nerve lie just deep to the vein.","The deep brachial artery is on the back of the humerus in the radial groove, and the axillary nerve wraps the surgical neck. Neither is in the cubital fossa.","The ulnar nerve runs behind the medial epicondyle, not in front of the elbow under the vein. This pair fits a question about an injury on the medial side of the elbow."],"comp":88,"competencyId":"w4-lab-ue-arteries-veins"},{"id":"N-m3-upper-limb-vessels-45","dok":3,"q":"A stab wound on the medial side of the middle of the arm produces bright red, pulsing bleeding. Which artery is most likely injured?","a":"The brachial artery.","options":["Brachial artery","Deep brachial artery","Cephalic vein","Axillary artery"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Pulsing, bright red blood means an artery has been cut. The main artery of the arm, the brachial artery, runs down the medial side of the arm from the lower border of teres major to the cubital fossa, alongside the median nerve. A wound at mid-arm on the medial side is right over its path.","notes":["Correct. The brachial artery runs down the medial side of the arm.","The deep brachial artery winds around the back of the humerus in the radial groove. It would be the answer for a wound or fracture at the back of the mid-arm.","The cephalic vein runs up the lateral side of the arm, and bleeding from a vein is darker and steady rather than pulsing. Pick it for a superficial cut on the lateral arm with dark, oozing blood.","The axillary artery lies in the armpit, above the arm. It would fit a wound deep in the axilla, between the first rib and teres major."],"comp":88,"competencyId":"w4-lab-ue-arteries-veins"}]},{"id":"m3-upper-limb-nerves","title":"Nerves of the upper limb (brachial plexus)","summary":"brachial plexus roots, trunks, cords, terminal nerves (musculocutaneous, axillary, radial, median, ulnar) and where they run. Identify nerves by location; do NOT ask which nerve innervates a given muscle","mw":"2026-10-14","tr":"2026-10-08","cards":[{"id":"L00739","dok":3,"q":"Bumping the funny bone sends a tingling jolt into the little finger. Which nerve is being struck, and where does it run?","a":"The ulnar nerve, behind the medial epicondyle.","options":["The median nerve, through the carpal tunnel","The ulnar nerve, behind the medial epicondyle","The axillary nerve, beside the surgical neck","The radial nerve, in the radial groove"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The ulnar nerve passes just behind the medial epicondyle of the humerus, where it lies under the skin with little padding. A knock there compresses the nerve, and because it carries sensation from the little-finger side of the hand, the tingle is felt in the little finger. The funny bone is really this nerve, not a bone.","notes":["The median nerve does pass through the carpal tunnel at the wrist. It would be right for a question about carpal tunnel syndrome, not the elbow.","Correct. The ulnar nerve runs behind the medial epicondyle.","The axillary nerve does run beside the surgical neck, but that is near the shoulder, not the elbow. Choose this pairing for a question about a fracture of the surgical neck.","The radial nerve does run in the radial groove on the back of the humeral shaft. Pick this pairing for a question about a fracture of the middle of the humerus."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-upper","lid":"c29-x2","comp":89,"competencyId":"w4-lab-ue-nerves"},{"id":"L03006","dok":1,"q":"Which disorder is caused by compression of the median nerve as it passes through a narrow tunnel at the wrist?","a":"Carpal tunnel syndrome.","options":["Thoracic outlet syndrome","Carpal tunnel syndrome","Bell palsy","Sciatica"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Carpal tunnel syndrome is compression of the median nerve inside the carpal tunnel, the narrow passage formed by the carpal bones and the flexor retinaculum (a band of connective tissue across the front of the wrist). The median nerve shares this tight space with the flexor tendons, so swelling presses on the nerve. The result is numbness and tingling in the thumb side of the hand.","notes":["Thoracic outlet syndrome is compression of the brachial plexus where it passes between the clavicle and the first rib. Pick it if the compression were at the base of the neck rather than at the wrist.","Correct. Median nerve compression at the wrist is carpal tunnel syndrome.","Bell palsy is a sudden weakness of the facial nerve (VII) that makes one side of the face droop. Pick it for a cranial nerve problem, not a wrist problem.","Sciatica is irritation or compression of the sciatic nerve or its roots, causing pain down the back of the thigh and leg. Pick it for the lower limb."],"lt":"t-pns","lid":"c37","comp":89,"competencyId":"w4-lab-ue-nerves"},{"id":"L04338","dok":1,"q":"Which list names the five terminal nerves of the brachial plexus?","a":"Musculocutaneous, axillary, radial, median, and ulnar nerves.","options":["Musculocutaneous, suprascapular, radial, median, and ulnar","Musculocutaneous, axillary, radial, median, and long thoracic","Musculocutaneous, axillary, radial, median, and ulnar","Musculocutaneous, axillary, radial, median, and phrenic"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The brachial plexus, built from spinal nerves C5 to T1, ends in five terminal branches. The musculocutaneous nerve comes from the lateral cord, the median nerve from the lateral and medial cords, the ulnar nerve from the medial cord, and the radial and axillary nerves from the posterior cord. Other named nerves leave the plexus higher up, from its roots, trunks, or cords.","notes":["The suprascapular nerve leaves from the upper trunk, well above the terminal branches, and runs to the back of the scapula. Pick it if the question asked which nerve arises from the upper trunk.","The long thoracic nerve arises from the C5 to C7 roots and runs down the lateral chest wall. It would be the answer if the question asked which nerve lies on the chest wall, exposed in axillary surgery.","Correct. These are the five terminal branches of the brachial plexus.","The phrenic nerve comes from C3 to C5 in the neck as part of the cervical plexus and descends through the thorax to the diaphragm. Choose it if the question asked which nerve passes through the thorax to reach the diaphragm."],"lt":"t-w4-lab-ue-nerves","lid":"w4-lab-ue-nerves-r1","comp":89,"competencyId":"w4-lab-ue-nerves"},{"id":"L04339","dok":2,"q":"Which terminal branch of the brachial plexus arises from the lateral cord and runs through the anterior arm between the biceps brachii and the brachialis?","a":"The musculocutaneous nerve.","options":["The ulnar nerve","The musculocutaneous nerve","The radial nerve","The axillary nerve"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The musculocutaneous nerve leaves the lateral cord of the brachial plexus, pierces the coracobrachialis, and then descends in the anterior compartment of the arm between the biceps brachii and the brachialis. Near the elbow it emerges as a cutaneous nerve of the lateral forearm. Its path keeps it entirely in front of the humerus, in the flexor compartment of the arm.","notes":["The ulnar nerve comes from the medial cord and passes behind the medial epicondyle at the elbow. Choose it if the question asked which nerve is struck when you hit your funny bone.","Correct. The musculocutaneous nerve runs between the biceps brachii and brachialis in the anterior arm.","The radial nerve comes from the posterior cord and spirals around the back of the humerus in the radial groove. Pick it if the question asked which nerve runs in the radial groove and is at risk in a mid-shaft humeral fracture.","The axillary nerve comes from the posterior cord and wraps around the surgical neck of the humerus. It would be the answer for the nerve threatened by a shoulder dislocation."],"lt":"t-w4-lab-ue-nerves","lid":"w4-lab-ue-nerves-r2","comp":89,"competencyId":"w4-lab-ue-nerves"},{"id":"L04340","dok":2,"q":"Which nerve passes behind the medial epicondyle of the humerus, where a knock sends tingling into the fourth and fifth fingers?","a":"The ulnar nerve.","options":["The median nerve","The ulnar nerve","The radial nerve","The axillary nerve"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The ulnar nerve comes from the medial cord of the brachial plexus and passes behind the medial epicondyle, where it lies just under the skin with little protection. That spot is the funny bone. Because the ulnar nerve carries sensation from the little finger side of the hand, a knock there causes tingling in the fourth and fifth fingers.","notes":["The median nerve passes through the front of the elbow and then through the carpal tunnel at the wrist. Pick it if the question asked which nerve is compressed in carpal tunnel syndrome.","Correct. The ulnar nerve runs behind the medial epicondyle and serves the little finger side of the hand.","The radial nerve spirals around the back of the humerus in the radial groove. It would be the answer if the question asked which nerve is injured in a mid-shaft humeral fracture, causing wrist drop.","The axillary nerve wraps around the surgical neck of the humerus near the shoulder, far from the elbow. Choose it if the question asked which nerve is at risk in a shoulder dislocation."],"lt":"t-w4-lab-ue-nerves","lid":"w4-lab-ue-nerves-r3","comp":89,"competencyId":"w4-lab-ue-nerves"},{"id":"N-m3-upper-limb-nerves-1","dok":1,"q":"Which spinal nerves form the brachial plexus?","a":"C5 through T1.","options":["T2 through T12","C1 through C4","L1 through L4","C5 through T1"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The brachial plexus is built from spinal nerves C5, C6, C7, C8, and T1. These nerves weave together in the neck and axilla and then sort into the nerves of the shoulder and upper limb. Every named nerve of the upper limb comes from this one plexus.","notes":["The anterior rami of T2 through T12 do not form a plexus; they run as the intercostal nerves in the spaces between the ribs. This range would fit a question about the intercostal nerves.","C1 through C4 form the cervical plexus, which serves the neck and gives rise to the phrenic nerve. Pick this range if the question asked about the cervical plexus.","L1 through L4 form the lumbar plexus, which serves the abdominal wall and part of the lower limb. Choose this range for a question about the lumbar plexus.","Correct. The brachial plexus is formed by C5 through T1."],"comp":89,"competencyId":"w4-lab-ue-nerves"},{"id":"N-m3-upper-limb-nerves-2","dok":1,"q":"The roots of the brachial plexus are which part of spinal nerves C5 through T1?","a":"The anterior (ventral) rami.","options":["The dorsal root ganglia","The anterior (ventral) rami","The ventral roots","The posterior (dorsal) rami"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"After a spinal nerve leaves the vertebral column, it splits into a posterior ramus and an anterior ramus. The anterior rami of C5 through T1 are what the brachial plexus calls its roots. All of the plexuses are built from anterior rami, while the posterior rami supply the deep back muscles and the skin of the back.","notes":["The dorsal root ganglia hold the cell bodies of sensory neurons beside the spinal cord. They would be the answer for where sensory cell bodies are found.","Correct. The roots of the plexus are the anterior rami of C5 through T1.","The ventral roots carry motor axons out of the spinal cord before the spinal nerve forms. Students pick them because the plexus uses the word root, but the plexus roots are rami, one step farther out.","The posterior rami supply the deep muscles and skin of the back and do not form plexuses. Pick them for a question about the nerves to the back along the spine."],"comp":89,"competencyId":"w4-lab-ue-nerves"},{"id":"N-m3-upper-limb-nerves-3","dok":1,"q":"Which roots join to form the upper trunk of the brachial plexus?","a":"C5 and C6.","options":["C5 and C6","C8 and T1","C7 alone","C6 and C7"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The five roots merge into three trunks. The upper trunk is formed by C5 and C6, the middle trunk is C7 continuing alone, and the lower trunk is formed by C8 and T1. The two highest roots join to make the highest trunk.","notes":["Correct. C5 and C6 join to form the upper trunk.","C8 and T1 join to form the lower trunk. They would be the answer for the lowest trunk of the plexus.","C7 continues on its own as the middle trunk. Pick it if the question asked about the middle trunk.","C6 and C7 do not join each other; C6 goes into the upper trunk with C5, and C7 forms the middle trunk alone. This is the mix-up of pairing roots evenly instead of following the plexus pattern."],"comp":89,"competencyId":"w4-lab-ue-nerves"},{"id":"N-m3-upper-limb-nerves-4","dok":1,"q":"Which root forms the middle trunk of the brachial plexus?","a":"C7 alone.","options":["C5 and C6","T1 alone","C8 and T1","C7 alone"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The middle trunk is the only trunk built from a single root, C7. The upper trunk takes the two roots above it, C5 and C6, and the lower trunk takes the two below it, C8 and T1. Remembering that C7 sits alone in the middle makes the other two trunks easy to fill in.","notes":["C5 and C6 form the upper trunk. Pick them if the question asked about the highest trunk.","T1 does not form a trunk by itself; it joins C8 to make the lower trunk. Students pick it when they assume the single root must be at one end of the plexus.","C8 and T1 form the lower trunk. They would be the answer for the lowest trunk.","Correct. C7 continues alone as the middle trunk."],"comp":89,"competencyId":"w4-lab-ue-nerves"},{"id":"N-m3-upper-limb-nerves-5","dok":1,"q":"Which roots join to form the lower trunk of the brachial plexus?","a":"C8 and T1.","options":["C6 and C7","C5 and C6","C7 and C8","C8 and T1"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The lower trunk is formed by the two lowest roots of the plexus, C8 and T1. It lies lowest in the neck, close to the first rib. The upper trunk is C5 and C6, and the middle trunk is C7 alone.","notes":["C6 and C7 do not form a trunk together. C6 joins C5 in the upper trunk, and C7 forms the middle trunk on its own.","C5 and C6 form the upper trunk. Pick them if the question asked about the highest trunk.","C7 does not join C8; it continues alone as the middle trunk. This is the mix-up of sliding the pairings down by one root.","Correct. C8 and T1 join to form the lower trunk."],"comp":89,"competencyId":"w4-lab-ue-nerves"},{"id":"N-m3-upper-limb-nerves-6","dok":1,"q":"How many divisions does the brachial plexus have?","a":"Six.","options":["Ten","Three","Six","Five"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Each of the three trunks splits into an anterior division and a posterior division, which makes six divisions. The full count for the plexus is five roots, three trunks, six divisions, three cords, and five terminal branches. These numbers are a quick way to check your own drawing.","notes":["Ten would mean each of the five roots split in two, but the roots merge into trunks before any division happens. This is the mix-up of dividing the roots instead of the trunks.","Three is the number of trunks and also the number of cords. Students pick it when they forget that each trunk splits into two divisions.","Correct. Three trunks each split in two, giving six divisions.","Five is the number of roots and the number of terminal branches. It would be the answer for either of those levels."],"comp":89,"competencyId":"w4-lab-ue-nerves"},{"id":"N-m3-upper-limb-nerves-7","dok":2,"q":"From the spinal cord outward, what is the correct order of the levels of the brachial plexus?","a":"Roots, trunks, divisions, cords, branches.","options":["Roots, trunks, divisions, cords, branches","Roots, divisions, trunks, cords, branches","Trunks, roots, divisions, cords, branches","Roots, trunks, cords, divisions, branches"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The plexus starts as five roots, which merge into three trunks. Each trunk splits into anterior and posterior divisions, the divisions regroup into three cords, and the cords give off the terminal branches. The memory phrase Read That Damn Cadaver Book keeps the order straight.","notes":["Correct. Roots, trunks, divisions, cords, branches is the order from the cord outward.","This puts divisions before trunks. The roots must first merge into trunks before anything can divide.","The roots are the first level, coming straight from the spinal nerves, so they cannot follow the trunks.","This swaps divisions and cords. The cords are formed from the divisions, so the divisions have to come first."],"comp":89,"competencyId":"w4-lab-ue-nerves"},{"id":"N-m3-upper-limb-nerves-8","dok":1,"q":"The lateral, posterior, and medial cords of the brachial plexus are named for their position relative to which structure?","a":"The axillary artery.","options":["The humerus","The first rib","The axillary artery","The clavicle"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"In the axilla, the three cords wrap around the axillary artery. The lateral cord lies on its lateral side, the medial cord on its medial side, and the posterior cord behind it. That is why you identify the artery first in lab, because the artery is the landmark that makes the cord names mean something.","notes":["The humerus is where several terminal nerves run later, such as the radial nerve in the radial groove. The cords lie in the axilla before the nerves reach the humerus.","The first rib marks where the subclavian artery becomes the axillary artery. It is a landmark for the vessel name, not for the cord names.","Correct. The cords are named for where they sit around the axillary artery.","The clavicle is the bone that the divisions pass behind on their way to the axilla. Pick it for a question about where the divisions lie."],"comp":89,"competencyId":"w4-lab-ue-nerves"},{"id":"N-m3-upper-limb-nerves-9","dok":1,"q":"Which cord of the brachial plexus gives rise to both the axillary nerve and the radial nerve?","a":"The posterior cord.","options":["Posterior cord","Upper trunk","Medial cord","Lateral cord"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The posterior cord lies behind the axillary artery and ends by dividing into the axillary nerve and the radial nerve. Both of these nerves head toward the back of the limb: the axillary nerve wraps the surgical neck of the humerus, and the radial nerve spirals in the radial groove. A cord at the back gives the nerves that travel at the back.","notes":["Correct. The posterior cord gives rise to both the axillary and radial nerves.","The upper trunk is a higher level of the plexus, formed by C5 and C6, and it gives off the suprascapular nerve. It does not give rise to terminal branches directly.","The medial cord gives rise to the ulnar nerve and part of the median nerve. It would be the answer for the origin of the ulnar nerve.","The lateral cord gives rise to the musculocutaneous nerve and part of the median nerve. Pick it if the question asked about the musculocutaneous nerve."],"comp":89,"competencyId":"w4-lab-ue-nerves"},{"id":"N-m3-upper-limb-nerves-10","dok":1,"q":"The musculocutaneous nerve arises from which cord of the brachial plexus?","a":"The lateral cord.","options":["Posterior cord","Middle trunk","Medial cord","Lateral cord"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The musculocutaneous nerve is a terminal branch of the lateral cord. It leaves the axilla, passes through the coracobrachialis, and runs down the anterior arm between the biceps brachii and brachialis. Below the elbow it continues as a sensory nerve to the skin of the lateral forearm.","notes":["The posterior cord gives rise to the axillary and radial nerves. It would be the answer for nerves that run toward the back of the arm.","The middle trunk is formed by C7 alone and lies higher in the plexus, before any cords form. It does not give off terminal branches directly.","The medial cord gives rise to the ulnar nerve and contributes to the median nerve. Pick it for the origin of the ulnar nerve.","Correct. The musculocutaneous nerve comes from the lateral cord."],"comp":89,"competencyId":"w4-lab-ue-nerves"},{"id":"N-m3-upper-limb-nerves-11","dok":1,"q":"The ulnar nerve arises from which cord of the brachial plexus?","a":"The medial cord.","options":["Medial cord","Lateral cord","Posterior cord","Lower trunk"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The ulnar nerve is the terminal branch of the medial cord, the cord on the medial side of the axillary artery. It then runs down the medial side of the arm, passes behind the medial epicondyle of the humerus, and continues along the medial forearm into the hand. A medial cord gives a nerve that stays on the medial side of the limb.","notes":["Correct. The ulnar nerve comes from the medial cord.","The lateral cord gives rise to the musculocutaneous nerve and part of the median nerve. Pick it for the origin of the musculocutaneous nerve.","The posterior cord gives rise to the axillary and radial nerves. It would be the answer for nerves that wrap the back of the humerus.","The lower trunk, formed by C8 and T1, does feed the medial cord, but it is an earlier level of the plexus. The question asks for the cord, which is the level the terminal nerve comes from."],"comp":89,"competencyId":"w4-lab-ue-nerves"},{"id":"N-m3-upper-limb-nerves-12","dok":1,"q":"Which terminal nerve of the brachial plexus is formed by contributions from both the lateral and medial cords?","a":"The median nerve.","options":["Ulnar nerve","Axillary nerve","Median nerve","Musculocutaneous nerve"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The median nerve is formed when a branch of the lateral cord and a branch of the medial cord join in front of the axillary artery. That shared origin places it in the middle, which fits its name. It then runs down the arm beside the brachial artery, through the cubital fossa, and through the carpal tunnel at the wrist.","notes":["The ulnar nerve comes from the medial cord only. Pick it if the question asked which nerve is the terminal branch of the medial cord.","The axillary nerve comes from the posterior cord. Pick it for a nerve from the posterior cord that wraps the surgical neck of the humerus.","Correct. The median nerve has roots from both the lateral and medial cords.","The musculocutaneous nerve comes from the lateral cord only. It would be the answer for the terminal branch of the lateral cord."],"comp":89,"competencyId":"w4-lab-ue-nerves"},{"id":"N-m3-upper-limb-nerves-13","dok":1,"q":"Which nerve spirals around the back of the humeral shaft in the radial groove?","a":"The radial nerve.","options":["Ulnar nerve","Axillary nerve","Radial nerve","Median nerve"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The radial nerve leaves the posterior cord and winds around the posterior shaft of the humerus in the radial groove, an oblique groove on the back of the bone. It travels there with the deep brachial artery, pressed against the bone. This is the location that makes it vulnerable to a mid-shaft fracture.","notes":["The ulnar nerve passes behind the medial epicondyle at the lower end of the humerus. It would be the answer for the nerve at the funny bone.","The axillary nerve wraps the surgical neck of the humerus, higher up than the radial groove. Pick it for a question about the surgical neck.","Correct. The radial nerve runs in the radial groove.","The median nerve runs down the medial arm beside the brachial artery and into the cubital fossa, not around the back of the bone. Pick it for the nerve that passes through the carpal tunnel."],"comp":89,"competencyId":"w4-lab-ue-nerves"},{"id":"N-m3-upper-limb-nerves-14","dok":1,"q":"Which nerve passes behind the medial epicondyle of the humerus?","a":"The ulnar nerve.","options":["Ulnar nerve","Radial nerve","Musculocutaneous nerve","Median nerve"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The ulnar nerve runs down the medial arm and passes behind the medial epicondyle, the bony bump on the inner side of the elbow. Here it lies just under the skin against the bone, with little protection. This is the spot people call the funny bone.","notes":["Correct. The ulnar nerve passes behind the medial epicondyle.","The radial nerve runs in the radial groove on the back of the humeral shaft and then crosses to the lateral side of the elbow. Pick it for a question about the radial groove.","The musculocutaneous nerve runs down the anterior arm between the biceps brachii and brachialis and continues to the lateral forearm skin. Pick it for the nerve in the front of the arm.","The median nerve passes through the front of the elbow in the cubital fossa, not behind it. It would be the answer for the nerve that travels with the brachial artery into the cubital fossa."],"comp":89,"competencyId":"w4-lab-ue-nerves"},{"id":"N-m3-upper-limb-nerves-15","dok":1,"q":"Which nerve passes through the carpal tunnel at the wrist?","a":"The median nerve.","options":["Median nerve","Ulnar nerve","Radial nerve","Musculocutaneous nerve"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The carpal tunnel is a narrow passage at the front of the wrist, with the carpal bones as its floor and walls and the flexor retinaculum, a band of connective tissue, as its roof. The median nerve passes through it along with the flexor tendons of the fingers. Swelling in this tight space presses on the nerve, which is carpal tunnel syndrome.","notes":["Correct. The median nerve passes through the carpal tunnel.","The ulnar nerve enters the hand on the little finger side, superficial to the flexor retinaculum, so it stays outside the carpal tunnel. Pick it for the nerve behind the medial epicondyle.","The radial nerve runs on the posterior and lateral side of the forearm and does not pass through the carpal tunnel. It would be the answer for the nerve in the radial groove.","The musculocutaneous nerve ends as a sensory nerve of the lateral forearm skin and never reaches the hand. Pick it for the nerve between biceps brachii and brachialis."],"comp":89,"competencyId":"w4-lab-ue-nerves"},{"id":"N-m3-upper-limb-nerves-16","dok":1,"q":"Which nerve wraps around the surgical neck of the humerus?","a":"The axillary nerve.","options":["Ulnar nerve","Musculocutaneous nerve","Axillary nerve","Radial nerve"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The axillary nerve leaves the posterior cord and passes backward around the surgical neck of the humerus, the narrowing just below the tubercles. It travels there with the posterior circumflex humeral artery. The nerve is named for the axilla, the region it comes from, but its weak point is this part of the bone.","notes":["The ulnar nerve passes behind the medial epicondyle at the elbow end of the humerus. It would be the answer for the funny bone.","The musculocutaneous nerve runs down the front of the arm between the biceps brachii and brachialis. Pick it for the nerve in the anterior arm.","Correct. The axillary nerve wraps the surgical neck of the humerus.","The radial nerve runs lower on the bone, spiraling in the radial groove along the shaft. Pick it for a question about the radial groove or a mid-shaft fracture."],"comp":89,"competencyId":"w4-lab-ue-nerves"},{"id":"N-m3-upper-limb-nerves-17","dok":1,"q":"The suprascapular nerve branches from which level of the brachial plexus?","a":"The upper trunk.","options":["The upper trunk","The posterior cord","The C5 root alone","The medial cord"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"A few nerves leave the plexus before the terminal branches form, and each comes from a particular level. The suprascapular nerve branches from the upper trunk, formed by C5 and C6, and runs back toward the top of the scapula. Knowing which level a branch leaves from helps you predict which injuries will affect it.","notes":["Correct. The suprascapular nerve branches from the upper trunk.","The posterior cord gives off the thoracodorsal and subscapular nerves before ending as the axillary and radial nerves. It would fit a question about those branches.","The C5 root alone gives off the dorsal scapular nerve. Pick it if the question asked about the dorsal scapular nerve.","The medial cord gives off the medial pectoral nerve and the medial cutaneous nerves of the arm and forearm before ending as the ulnar nerve. Pick it for those branches."],"comp":89,"competencyId":"w4-lab-ue-nerves"},{"id":"N-m3-upper-limb-nerves-18","dok":1,"q":"Which nerve runs down the anterior arm between the biceps brachii and the brachialis?","a":"The musculocutaneous nerve.","options":["Radial nerve","Musculocutaneous nerve","Axillary nerve","Ulnar nerve"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The musculocutaneous nerve leaves the lateral cord, passes through the coracobrachialis, and then runs down the front of the arm in the plane between the biceps brachii and the brachialis. Below the elbow it becomes a skin nerve of the lateral forearm. Its path through the anterior arm is the location to know for lab.","notes":["The radial nerve runs on the back of the humerus in the radial groove. Pick it for the nerve on the posterior arm.","Correct. The musculocutaneous nerve runs between the biceps brachii and brachialis.","The axillary nerve wraps the surgical neck of the humerus near the shoulder and does not run down the arm. It would be the answer for a question about the surgical neck.","The ulnar nerve runs down the medial side of the arm to pass behind the medial epicondyle. Pick it for the nerve at the funny bone."],"comp":89,"competencyId":"w4-lab-ue-nerves"},{"id":"N-m3-upper-limb-nerves-19","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly describes how the posterior cord of the brachial plexus is formed?","a":"The posterior divisions of all three trunks join to form it.","options":["The anterior division of the lower trunk continues as it","The C8 and T1 roots join directly to form it","The anterior divisions of the upper and middle trunks join to form it","The posterior divisions of all three trunks join to form it"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Each trunk splits into an anterior and a posterior division. All three posterior divisions come together behind the axillary artery to form the posterior cord, so it carries fibers from every root, C5 through T1. That is why the posterior cord can give rise to nerves for the whole back of the limb.","notes":["The anterior division of the lower trunk continues as the medial cord. This description would be correct for the medial cord.","C8 and T1 join to form the lower trunk, not a cord. Roots never form cords directly, since the trunks and divisions come in between.","The anterior divisions of the upper and middle trunks join to form the lateral cord. Pick this description for the lateral cord.","Correct. All three posterior divisions join to form the posterior cord."],"comp":89,"competencyId":"w4-lab-ue-nerves"},{"id":"N-m3-upper-limb-nerves-20","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares how the lateral and medial cords are formed?","a":"The lateral cord comes from the anterior divisions of the upper and middle trunks; the medial cord comes from the anterior division of the lower trunk.","options":["Lateral from the anterior divisions of the upper and middle trunks; medial from the anterior division of the lower trunk","The lateral cord comes from the posterior divisions of all three trunks; the medial cord comes from the upper trunk","The lateral cord comes from the anterior division of the lower trunk; the medial cord from the upper and middle trunks","Both cords come from the posterior divisions, one on each side of the axillary artery"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The anterior divisions of the upper and middle trunks join to form the lateral cord, and the anterior division of the lower trunk continues as the medial cord. The posterior cord takes all three posterior divisions. The higher roots feed the lateral cord and the lowest roots feed the medial cord, which matches how the cords sit around the axillary artery.","notes":["Correct. Lateral from the upper and middle anterior divisions, medial from the lower anterior division.","All three posterior divisions form the posterior cord, not the lateral cord. This option confuses the lateral cord with the posterior cord.","This reverses the two cords. The lower trunk, from C8 and T1, feeds the medial cord, and the higher trunks feed the lateral cord.","The lateral and medial cords are both built from anterior divisions. Only the posterior cord is built from posterior divisions."],"comp":89,"competencyId":"w4-lab-ue-nerves"},{"id":"N-m3-upper-limb-nerves-21","dok":2,"q":"Tracing the brachial plexus from the neck into the upper limb, in which region do you find the cords?","a":"In the axilla, around the axillary artery.","options":["In the cubital fossa at the elbow","Behind the middle of the clavicle","In the neck, above the clavicle","In the axilla, around the axillary artery"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The roots and trunks lie in the neck, the divisions pass behind the clavicle, and the cords lie in the axilla (armpit). There the three cords sit around the axillary artery, which gives them their names. The terminal branches then leave the axilla to travel down the limb.","notes":["The cubital fossa is at the front of the elbow, far beyond the plexus itself. It is where the median nerve passes with the brachial artery.","Behind the clavicle is where the divisions are found, between the trunks and the cords. It would be the answer for the location of the divisions.","The neck above the clavicle is where the roots and trunks are found. Pick it if the question asked where the trunks lie.","Correct. The cords lie in the axilla around the axillary artery."],"comp":89,"competencyId":"w4-lab-ue-nerves"},{"id":"N-m3-upper-limb-nerves-22","dok":2,"q":"Which level of the brachial plexus passes behind the clavicle?","a":"The divisions.","options":["The roots","The trunks","The terminal branches","The divisions"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The plexus moves from the neck to the axilla in order. The roots and trunks are in the neck, the divisions pass behind the clavicle, and the cords and branches are in the axilla. So the level that sits behind the clavicle is the divisions, which link the trunks above to the cords below.","notes":["The roots emerge from the vertebral column in the neck, well above the clavicle. Pick them for the level closest to the spinal cord.","The trunks form in the neck, above the clavicle. They would be the answer for the level found in the lower neck.","The terminal branches leave the cords in the axilla and travel down the limb, below and beyond the clavicle. Pick them for the five nerves that enter the arm.","Correct. The divisions pass behind the clavicle."],"comp":89,"competencyId":"w4-lab-ue-nerves"},{"id":"N-m3-upper-limb-nerves-23","dok":2,"q":"Which nerve travels beside the brachial artery down the medial arm and lies deep to the median cubital vein in the cubital fossa?","a":"The median nerve.","options":["Median nerve","Radial nerve","Ulnar nerve","Musculocutaneous nerve"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The median nerve forms in the axilla from the lateral and medial cords and runs down the medial arm alongside the brachial artery. In the cubital fossa, both lie just deep to the median cubital vein, separated from it by the bicipital aponeurosis, a flat sheet of tendon. The nerve then continues down the forearm to the carpal tunnel.","notes":["Correct. The median nerve runs with the brachial artery and lies deep to the median cubital vein.","The radial nerve runs on the back of the humerus in the radial groove and reaches the lateral side of the elbow. Pick it for a nerve on the posterior arm.","The ulnar nerve leaves the brachial artery partway down the arm and passes behind the medial epicondyle, not through the front of the elbow. It would be the answer for the funny bone.","The musculocutaneous nerve runs between the biceps brachii and brachialis on the anterior arm and then becomes a skin nerve of the lateral forearm. Pick it for the nerve between those two muscles."],"comp":89,"competencyId":"w4-lab-ue-nerves"},{"id":"N-m3-upper-limb-nerves-24","dok":2,"q":"Which set of counts correctly describes the brachial plexus, from roots to terminal branches?","a":"5 roots, 3 trunks, 6 divisions, 3 cords, 5 terminal branches.","options":["4 roots, 3 trunks, 6 divisions, 3 cords, 4 terminal branches","5 roots, 3 trunks, 3 divisions, 6 cords, 5 terminal branches","5 roots, 3 trunks, 6 divisions, 3 cords, 5 terminal branches","5 roots, 5 trunks, 6 divisions, 3 cords, 3 terminal branches"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Five roots (C5 through T1) merge into three trunks. Each trunk splits into an anterior and a posterior division, giving six divisions, and these regroup into three cords. The cords end in five terminal branches: the musculocutaneous, axillary, radial, median, and ulnar nerves.","notes":["The brachial plexus has five roots, C5, C6, C7, C8, and T1, and five terminal branches. Four of each would leave out one root and one nerve.","This swaps the counts for divisions and cords. Each of three trunks splits in two, so there are six divisions, which then regroup into three cords.","Correct. The pattern is 5, 3, 6, 3, 5.","This has five trunks and three terminal branches. The roots merge into three trunks, and the plexus ends in five terminal nerves."],"comp":89,"competencyId":"w4-lab-ue-nerves"},{"id":"N-m3-upper-limb-nerves-25","dok":2,"q":"Hitting the funny bone sends tingling into the ring and little fingers. Which nerve is struck, and where?","a":"The ulnar nerve, behind the medial epicondyle.","options":["The ulnar nerve, behind the medial epicondyle","The median nerve, in the cubital fossa","The ulnar nerve, behind the lateral epicondyle","The radial nerve, in the radial groove"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The funny bone is not a bone at all but the ulnar nerve where it passes behind the medial epicondyle of the humerus. At that spot the nerve lies just under the skin against bone, with nothing to cushion it. The ulnar nerve carries sensation from the little finger and the medial half of the ring finger, which is why the tingling shows up there.","notes":["Correct. The funny bone is the ulnar nerve behind the medial epicondyle.","The median nerve passes through the front of the elbow, deep to the median cubital vein. It would fit a question about structures at risk during a deep blood draw.","The ulnar nerve is the right nerve, but it passes behind the medial epicondyle, on the little finger side. The lateral epicondyle is on the thumb side, which is the mix-up of reversing medial and lateral.","The radial nerve in the radial groove is on the back of the humeral shaft, well above the elbow. Pick it for the nerve injured in a mid-shaft fracture."],"comp":89,"competencyId":"w4-lab-ue-nerves"},{"id":"N-m3-upper-limb-nerves-26","dok":2,"q":"Three nerves lie against the humerus at vulnerable points. Which set correctly pairs each nerve with its location on the bone?","a":"Axillary at the surgical neck, radial in the radial groove, ulnar behind the medial epicondyle.","options":["Axillary at the surgical neck, radial behind the medial epicondyle, ulnar in the radial groove","Axillary in the radial groove, radial at the surgical neck, ulnar behind the medial epicondyle","Axillary at the surgical neck, radial in the radial groove, ulnar behind the medial epicondyle","Axillary behind the medial epicondyle, radial in the radial groove, ulnar at the surgical neck"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"These three nerves meet the humerus from top to bottom. The axillary nerve wraps the surgical neck near the shoulder, the radial nerve spirals in the radial groove along the shaft, and the ulnar nerve passes behind the medial epicondyle at the elbow. Each location predicts which fracture or blow is most likely to injure that nerve.","notes":["This swaps the radial and ulnar nerves. The ulnar nerve is the funny bone behind the medial epicondyle, while the radial nerve runs in the radial groove.","This swaps the axillary and radial nerves. The radial nerve is the one in the groove that shares its name, and the axillary nerve is higher up at the surgical neck.","Correct. Axillary at the surgical neck, radial in the radial groove, ulnar behind the medial epicondyle.","This swaps the axillary and ulnar nerves. The axillary nerve stays near the shoulder at the surgical neck, and the ulnar nerve is at the elbow."],"comp":89,"competencyId":"w4-lab-ue-nerves"},{"id":"N-m3-upper-limb-nerves-27","dok":2,"q":"The median nerve is compressed in the carpal tunnel. Where would the person most likely feel numbness and tingling?","a":"The thumb, index finger, and middle finger.","options":["The thumb, index finger, and middle finger","The skin over the deltoid","The little finger and medial half of the ring finger","The back of the hand only"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"After passing through the carpal tunnel, the median nerve carries sensation from the palm side of the thumb, index finger, middle finger, and the lateral half of the ring finger. Pressure on the nerve in the tunnel therefore causes numbness and tingling in those fingers. The little finger is spared because the ulnar nerve, which does not pass through the tunnel, serves it.","notes":["Correct. The median nerve serves the thumb, index, and middle fingers on the palm side.","The skin over the deltoid is served by the axillary nerve, which wraps the surgical neck of the humerus. Pick it for an injury at the shoulder.","The little finger and medial half of the ring finger are served by the ulnar nerve. Pick them for a blow to the funny bone or an injury behind the medial epicondyle.","The back of the hand on the thumb side is served by the radial nerve, which does not pass through the carpal tunnel. This is the mix-up of thinking any nerve at the wrist affects the whole hand."],"comp":89,"competencyId":"w4-lab-ue-nerves"},{"id":"N-m3-upper-limb-nerves-28","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares how the median and ulnar nerves enter the hand?","a":"The median nerve passes through the carpal tunnel, while the ulnar nerve passes superficial to the flexor retinaculum on the little finger side.","options":["The ulnar nerve passes through the carpal tunnel, while the median nerve passes superficial to the flexor retinaculum","Both nerves pass together through the carpal tunnel beneath the flexor retinaculum","Neither nerve reaches the hand; both end at the elbow","The median passes through the carpal tunnel; the ulnar passes superficial to the flexor retinaculum"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The flexor retinaculum is a band of connective tissue that forms the roof of the carpal tunnel. The median nerve passes beneath it, through the tunnel, with the flexor tendons. The ulnar nerve enters the hand on the little finger side, superficial to the retinaculum, so it is not squeezed in carpal tunnel syndrome.","notes":["This reverses the two nerves. The median nerve is the one inside the carpal tunnel, which is why carpal tunnel syndrome affects the thumb side of the hand.","Only the median nerve passes through the carpal tunnel. If both did, carpal tunnel syndrome would numb the little finger too, and it does not.","Both nerves continue into the hand. The nerve that ends near the elbow as a skin nerve of the forearm is the musculocutaneous nerve.","Correct. The median nerve goes through the tunnel, and the ulnar nerve passes superficial to the retinaculum."],"comp":89,"competencyId":"w4-lab-ue-nerves"},{"id":"N-m3-upper-limb-nerves-29","dok":2,"q":"In the thorax, the phrenic nerve and the vagus nerve both descend past the root of the lung. Which statement correctly compares their positions?","a":"The phrenic nerve passes anterior to the lung root, and the vagus nerve passes posterior to it.","options":["The phrenic nerve passes anterior to the lung root, and the vagus nerve passes posterior to it","Both nerves pass anterior to the lung root, side by side","The phrenic nerve passes posterior to the lung root, and the vagus nerve passes anterior to it","Both nerves run inside the intercostal spaces, below the ribs"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The phrenic nerve, from C3, C4, and C5, runs down the side of the mediastinum in front of the root of the lung on its way to the diaphragm. The vagus nerve runs behind the root of the lung on its way to the esophagus and the abdomen. Their relationship to the lung root is how you tell them apart in the thorax.","notes":["Correct. The phrenic nerve is anterior to the lung root, and the vagus is posterior.","Only the phrenic nerve passes in front of the lung root. The vagus nerve passes behind it, so they are not side by side.","This reverses the two nerves. A useful check is that the phrenic nerve is heading for the diaphragm along the front of the mediastinum, while the vagus stays farther back near the esophagus.","The nerves in the intercostal spaces are the intercostal nerves, from the anterior rami of the thoracic spinal nerves. Pick this location for the intercostal nerves, not for the phrenic or vagus nerves."]},{"id":"N-m3-upper-limb-nerves-30","dok":2,"q":"An intercostal nerve runs in the costal groove with its artery and vein. From superior to inferior, what is the order of these three structures?","a":"Vein, artery, nerve.","options":["Artery, vein, nerve","Vein, artery, nerve","Artery, nerve, vein","Nerve, artery, vein"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The costal groove runs along the inferior border of each rib and shelters a neurovascular bundle. From top to bottom the order is vein, artery, nerve, often remembered as VAN. Because the nerve is lowest, it is the least protected by the rib, which is why needles placed into the chest are passed just above a rib rather than just below one.","notes":["This swaps the vein and artery. The vein sits highest in the groove, tucked under the rib, with the artery just below it.","Correct. Vein, artery, nerve from superior to inferior.","This places the nerve between the vessels. The nerve lies below both the vein and the artery at the bottom of the bundle.","This is the order read from bottom to top. The nerve is the lowest structure, not the highest, which is why it is the most exposed."],"comp":103,"competencyId":"resp-thoracic-diaphragm"},{"id":"N-m3-upper-limb-nerves-31","dok":2,"q":"During surgery to remove lymph nodes from the axilla, the surgeon protects a nerve that runs down the lateral chest wall on its surface, alongside the lateral thoracic artery. Which nerve is it?","a":"The long thoracic nerve.","options":["Phrenic nerve","Thoracodorsal nerve","Axillary nerve","Long thoracic nerve"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The long thoracic nerve arises from the C5, C6, and C7 roots and runs down the lateral chest wall, lying on the surface of the chest wall muscles below the axilla. Because it is long and superficial in this position, it can be injured during surgery in the axilla or by a blow to the side of the chest. It travels alongside the lateral thoracic artery.","notes":["The phrenic nerve descends through the thorax in front of the lung root to reach the diaphragm. It runs inside the chest cavity, not on the outer chest wall.","The thoracodorsal nerve comes from the posterior cord and runs down the posterior wall of the axilla toward the back. Pick it for a nerve heading toward the back of the trunk from the posterior cord.","The axillary nerve leaves the axilla backward to wrap the surgical neck of the humerus. It would be the answer for a nerve at risk in a fracture of the surgical neck.","Correct. The long thoracic nerve runs down the lateral chest wall with the lateral thoracic artery."],"comp":89,"competencyId":"w4-lab-ue-nerves"},{"id":"N-m3-upper-limb-nerves-32","dok":2,"q":"In lab you find a cord lying on the outer side of the axillary artery and trace its branches. Which terminal nerves will you reach?","a":"The musculocutaneous nerve and the lateral root of the median nerve.","options":["The ulnar nerve and the medial root of the median nerve","The musculocutaneous nerve and lateral root of the median","The axillary and radial nerves","The suprascapular and dorsal scapular nerves"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"A cord on the outer side of the axillary artery is the lateral cord. It ends as the musculocutaneous nerve and the lateral root of the median nerve, which joins the medial root from the medial cord. Identifying the artery first is what lets you name the cord and predict its nerves.","notes":["The ulnar nerve and the medial root of the median nerve come from the medial cord, on the inner side of the artery. They would be the answer for the medial cord.","Correct. The lateral cord gives the musculocutaneous nerve and the lateral root of the median nerve.","The axillary and radial nerves come from the posterior cord, which lies behind the axillary artery. Pick them if the cord were behind the artery.","The suprascapular nerve comes from the upper trunk and the dorsal scapular nerve from the C5 root. Both leave the plexus in the neck, before any cords form."],"comp":89,"competencyId":"w4-lab-ue-nerves"},{"id":"N-m3-upper-limb-nerves-33","dok":3,"q":"A student who studies for hours leaning on both elbows begins to notice tingling in the ring and little fingers. Which nerve is most likely being compressed, and where?","a":"The ulnar nerve, behind the medial epicondyle.","options":["The radial nerve, in the radial groove","The median nerve, in the carpal tunnel","The ulnar nerve, behind the medial epicondyle","The axillary nerve, at the surgical neck"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Leaning on the elbows presses on the back and inner side of the elbow, where the ulnar nerve lies just under the skin behind the medial epicondyle. Pressure there irritates the nerve, and since it carries sensation from the little finger and the medial half of the ring finger, the tingling shows up in those fingers. It is the same spot as the funny bone, only with steady pressure instead of a quick knock.","notes":["The radial nerve in the radial groove lies along the back of the humeral shaft, not at the point of the elbow. It would be the answer for a mid-shaft humeral fracture.","Compression of the median nerve in the carpal tunnel causes tingling in the thumb, index, and middle fingers. Pick it for symptoms on the thumb side of the hand after repetitive wrist use.","Correct. Leaning on the elbows compresses the ulnar nerve behind the medial epicondyle.","The axillary nerve wraps the surgical neck near the shoulder and serves the skin over the deltoid. Pick it for an injury at the shoulder."],"comp":89,"competencyId":"w4-lab-ue-nerves"},{"id":"N-m3-upper-limb-nerves-34","dok":3,"q":"An older adult falls and fractures the surgical neck of the humerus. Afterward, a patch of skin over the deltoid is numb. Which nerve was most likely injured?","a":"The axillary nerve.","options":["Ulnar nerve","Axillary nerve","Musculocutaneous nerve","Radial nerve"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The axillary nerve leaves the posterior cord and wraps around the back of the surgical neck of the humerus, so a fracture there can stretch or tear it. The nerve carries sensation from the skin over the deltoid, which explains the numb patch. The location of the fracture and the location of the numbness both point to the same nerve.","notes":["The ulnar nerve passes behind the medial epicondyle at the elbow. It would be the answer for tingling in the ring and little fingers after a blow to the elbow.","Correct. The axillary nerve wraps the surgical neck and serves the skin over the deltoid.","The musculocutaneous nerve runs down the front of the arm between the biceps brachii and brachialis and serves the skin of the lateral forearm. Pick it for numbness on the lateral forearm.","The radial nerve runs lower on the bone, in the radial groove along the shaft. It would be the nerve at risk in a mid-shaft fracture, with wrist drop as the sign."],"comp":89,"competencyId":"w4-lab-ue-nerves"},{"id":"N-m3-upper-limb-nerves-35","dok":3,"q":"After a fracture through the middle of the humeral shaft, a patient cannot lift the hand at the wrist, and it hangs limply (wrist drop). Which nerve was injured, and where?","a":"The radial nerve, in the radial groove.","options":["The ulnar nerve, behind the medial epicondyle","The radial nerve, in the radial groove","The axillary nerve, at the surgical neck","The median nerve, in the carpal tunnel"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The radial nerve spirals around the back of the humeral shaft in the radial groove, pressed against the bone. A mid-shaft fracture runs right through that spot, so the nerve and the fracture line are in the same place. Wrist drop is the classic sign of radial nerve injury, because the nerve serves the extensors on the back of the forearm.","notes":["The ulnar nerve is at risk behind the medial epicondyle, at the elbow rather than mid-shaft. Its injury causes clawing of the ring and little fingers, not wrist drop.","Correct. The radial nerve in the radial groove is injured in a mid-shaft fracture.","The axillary nerve is at risk at the surgical neck, near the shoulder. Pick it for a fracture just below the head of the humerus with numbness over the deltoid.","The median nerve is at risk in the carpal tunnel at the wrist, far below a humeral fracture. It would be the answer for numbness in the thumb, index, and middle fingers."],"comp":89,"competencyId":"w4-lab-ue-nerves"},{"id":"N-m3-upper-limb-nerves-36","dok":3,"q":"A data entry worker has numbness in the thumb, index, and middle fingers that is worse at night, and the little finger feels normal. Where is a nerve most likely being compressed?","a":"The median nerve in the carpal tunnel.","options":["The radial nerve in the radial groove","The median nerve in the carpal tunnel","The ulnar nerve behind the medial epicondyle","The lower trunk of the brachial plexus in the neck"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The thumb, index, and middle fingers are served by the median nerve, which passes through the narrow carpal tunnel at the wrist. Repetitive wrist use can cause swelling inside the tunnel that squeezes the nerve. The little finger is spared because it is served by the ulnar nerve, which does not pass through the tunnel.","notes":["The radial nerve in the radial groove is injured by a mid-shaft humeral fracture and causes wrist drop. Pick it for a patient who cannot extend the wrist.","Correct. These symptoms point to the median nerve in the carpal tunnel.","The ulnar nerve behind the medial epicondyle would cause tingling in the ring and little fingers. The normal little finger in this case rules it out.","The lower trunk, from C8 and T1, mainly affects the medial side of the hand and forearm, including the little finger. It would fit a question about an arm pulled forcefully upward."],"comp":89,"competencyId":"w4-lab-ue-nerves"},{"id":"N-m3-upper-limb-nerves-37","dok":3,"q":"During a difficult birth, a baby's head is pulled sharply away from the shoulder, stretching the C5 and C6 roots. Which part of the brachial plexus is most likely injured?","a":"The upper trunk.","options":["The lower trunk","The medial cord","The upper trunk","The middle trunk"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Pulling the head away from the shoulder widens the angle between the neck and shoulder and stretches the highest part of the plexus. The C5 and C6 roots join to form the upper trunk, so the upper trunk takes the injury. The affected baby has weakness around the shoulder and upper arm, the regions served by those roots.","notes":["The lower trunk is formed by C8 and T1 and is stretched when the arm is pulled forcefully upward. Pick it for a fall where the person grabs something overhead.","The medial cord is a deeper level in the axilla, continuing from the lower trunk. It fits a question about the cord that gives rise to the ulnar nerve.","Correct. C5 and C6 form the upper trunk, which is stretched when the head is pulled from the shoulder.","The middle trunk is formed by C7 alone. It would be the answer if the injured root were C7."],"comp":89,"competencyId":"w4-lab-ue-nerves"},{"id":"N-m3-upper-limb-nerves-38","dok":3,"q":"A climber slips and grabs a ledge overhead, and the arm is yanked sharply upward, stretching the C8 and T1 roots. Which part of the brachial plexus is most likely injured?","a":"The lower trunk.","options":["The middle trunk","The upper trunk","The lower trunk","The lateral cord"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"When the arm is pulled forcefully upward, the lowest part of the plexus is stretched. The C8 and T1 roots join to form the lower trunk, so the lower trunk is injured. The deficit falls mainly on the hand and the medial side of the forearm, which these roots serve.","notes":["The middle trunk is formed by C7 alone. It would be the answer for an injury limited to the C7 root.","The upper trunk, from C5 and C6, is stretched when the head is pulled away from the shoulder. Pick it for the birth injury pattern.","Correct. C8 and T1 form the lower trunk, which is stretched when the arm is pulled upward.","The lateral cord is built from the anterior divisions of the upper and middle trunks, so it carries fibers from the higher roots. It fits a question about the cord lateral to the axillary artery."],"comp":89,"competencyId":"w4-lab-ue-nerves"},{"id":"N-m3-upper-limb-nerves-39","dok":3,"q":"A tumor in the axilla presses on the cord that lies behind the axillary artery. Which pair of terminal nerves would most likely be affected?","a":"The axillary and radial nerves.","options":["The axillary and radial nerves","The musculocutaneous and median nerves","The ulnar and median nerves","The suprascapular and dorsal scapular nerves"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The cord behind the axillary artery is the posterior cord. Its two terminal branches are the axillary nerve, which wraps the surgical neck, and the radial nerve, which runs in the radial groove. Pressure on the posterior cord would therefore disturb both nerves at once.","notes":["Correct. The posterior cord ends as the axillary and radial nerves.","The musculocutaneous nerve and the lateral root of the median nerve come from the lateral cord, on the outer side of the artery. Pick this pair for a mass pressing on the lateral cord.","The ulnar nerve and the medial root of the median nerve come from the medial cord, on the inner side of the artery. This pair fits a mass on the medial cord.","The suprascapular nerve leaves the upper trunk and the dorsal scapular nerve leaves the C5 root, both up in the neck. A mass in the axilla would be below their origins."],"comp":89,"competencyId":"w4-lab-ue-nerves"},{"id":"N-m3-upper-limb-nerves-40","dok":3,"q":"A glass cut on the palm side of the wrist, on the little finger side, leaves the little finger and the medial half of the ring finger numb, while the thumb feels normal. Which nerve was cut?","a":"The ulnar nerve.","options":["Median nerve","Radial nerve","Musculocutaneous nerve","Ulnar nerve"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The ulnar nerve runs down the medial (little finger) side of the forearm and enters the hand superficial to the flexor retinaculum, outside the carpal tunnel. It carries sensation from the little finger and the medial half of the ring finger. Both the site of the cut and the pattern of numbness point to the ulnar nerve.","notes":["The median nerve crosses the wrist in the middle, through the carpal tunnel, and serves the thumb, index, and middle fingers. Pick it if the thumb side were numb.","The radial nerve runs on the thumb side and back of the forearm, away from a cut on the little finger side. It would be the answer for wrist drop after a humeral fracture.","The musculocutaneous nerve ends as a skin nerve of the lateral forearm and never reaches the wrist or hand. Pick it for the nerve between the biceps brachii and brachialis.","Correct. The ulnar nerve crosses the wrist on the little finger side and serves those fingers."],"comp":89,"competencyId":"w4-lab-ue-nerves"}]},{"id":"m3-blood","title":"Blood: composition, plasma, hematopoiesis, blood types and disorders","summary":"plasma and formed elements, hematocrit, hemoglobin, hematopoiesis, ABO and Rh, blood disorders at the structural level","mw":"2026-10-19","tr":"2026-10-20","cards":[{"id":"L01306","dok":1,"q":"To which of the four primary tissue types does blood belong?","a":"Connective tissue.","options":["Epithelial tissue","Nervous tissue","Muscle tissue","Connective tissue"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The four primary tissue types are epithelial, connective, muscle, and nervous tissue. Blood is classified as connective tissue because, like all connective tissues, it is made of cells suspended in an extracellular matrix. In blood the matrix is a fluid, the plasma, which makes blood the only liquid tissue in the body.","notes":["Epithelial tissue is made of tightly packed cells that cover surfaces and line cavities, with almost no matrix between them. It is the answer for tissue like the lining of blood vessels, not the blood inside.","Nervous tissue is made of neurons and neuroglia that carry and support electrical signals. It fits the brain, spinal cord, and nerves.","Muscle tissue is made of cells that contract, such as cardiac muscle in the heart wall. Pick it if the question asked about the tissue that pumps the blood.","Correct. Blood is cells suspended in a matrix, plasma, so it is a connective tissue."],"lt":"t-blood-plasma","lid":"c1","comp":81,"competencyId":"w4-blood-composition"},{"id":"L01307","dok":1,"q":"Blood is classified as which specific kind of connective tissue?","a":"Fluid connective tissue.","options":["Fluid connective tissue","Loose connective tissue","Supporting connective tissue","Dense connective tissue"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Connective tissues are sorted partly by the nature of their matrix. Blood has a liquid matrix, the plasma, in which the formed elements float, so it is called a fluid connective tissue. This sets it apart from connective tissue proper, cartilage, and bone, which have fibrous, firm, or hard matrices.","notes":["Correct. Plasma is a liquid matrix, so blood is a fluid connective tissue.","Loose connective tissue, such as areolar tissue, has a soft, gel-like matrix with scattered fibers and lies beneath epithelia. Its matrix is not a flowing fluid.","Cartilage and bone are the supporting connective tissues, with firm or calcified matrices. They are the answer for tissues that hold the body up.","Dense connective tissue is packed with collagen fibers and forms tendons and ligaments. Choose it for a structure that resists pulling forces."],"lt":"t-blood-plasma","lid":"c2","comp":81,"competencyId":"w4-blood-composition"},{"id":"L01308","dok":1,"q":"About what percentage of whole blood is made up of formed elements?","a":"About 45 percent.","options":["About 7 percent","About 55 percent","About 45 percent","About 92 percent"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"When a blood sample is spun in a centrifuge, it separates into layers. The formed elements, which are the cells and cell fragments, settle to the bottom and make up about 45 percent of the volume, most of it erythrocytes. The plasma above them makes up the remaining 55 percent.","notes":["About 7 to 8 percent is the share of body weight made up by blood, or the share of plasma made up by proteins. It is not the formed element fraction.","About 55 percent is the plasma fraction of whole blood. Students often swap the two numbers, so match the larger number to the fluid.","Correct. Formed elements are just under half of whole blood.","About 92 percent is the water content of plasma. Pick it for a question about what plasma is mostly made of."],"lt":"t-blood-plasma","lid":"c3","comp":81,"competencyId":"w4-blood-composition"},{"id":"L01309","dok":1,"q":"About what percentage of whole blood is plasma?","a":"About 55 percent.","options":["About 45 percent","About 92 percent","About 8 percent","About 55 percent"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Plasma is the liquid part of blood, and it makes up a little more than half of the volume, about 55 percent. The formed elements make up the other 45 percent. In a spun tube, the plasma is the pale yellow layer at the top.","notes":["About 45 percent is the formed element fraction, mostly erythrocytes. It is the answer when the question asks about the cells and cell fragments.","About 92 percent is the share of plasma that is water. That number describes what plasma contains, not how much of the blood it makes up.","About 8 percent is close to the share of body weight that is blood, and to the share of plasma that is protein. It does not describe the plasma fraction of blood.","Correct. Plasma is a little more than half of whole blood."],"lt":"t-blood-plasma","lid":"c4","comp":81,"competencyId":"w4-blood-composition"},{"id":"L01310","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly describes the two major fractions of whole blood?","a":"Plasma is about 55 percent and formed elements are about 45 percent.","options":["Plasma about 92 percent, formed elements about 8 percent","Plasma about 55 percent, buffy coat about 45 percent","Plasma about 45 percent, formed elements about 55 percent","Plasma about 55 percent, formed elements about 45 percent"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Whole blood separates into two major fractions. Plasma, the fluid matrix, is about 55 percent of the volume, and the formed elements, the erythrocytes, leukocytes, and platelets, are about 45 percent. A thin buffy coat of leukocytes and platelets lies between them in a spun sample, but it is part of the formed elements.","notes":["About 92 percent is the water content of plasma, and about 8 percent is roughly the solute share. These describe plasma itself, not whole blood.","The buffy coat is the thin layer of leukocytes and platelets, less than 1 percent of the sample. The 45 percent fraction is mostly erythrocytes.","This reverses the two numbers. Plasma is the larger fraction, so this is the mix-up to watch for.","Correct. The fluid is slightly more than half and the cells slightly less."],"lt":"t-blood-plasma","lid":"c5","comp":81,"competencyId":"w4-blood-composition"},{"id":"L01311","dok":1,"q":"What structural role does plasma play in blood?","a":"It is the extracellular matrix of blood.","options":["It is the layer of leukocytes and platelets","It is the fluid left after blood clots","It is the cellular fraction of blood","It is the extracellular matrix of blood"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Every connective tissue is made of cells and an extracellular matrix, the material between the cells. In blood that matrix is plasma, a fluid in which the formed elements are suspended and carried. Calling plasma the matrix is what places blood among the connective tissues.","notes":["The thin pale layer of leukocytes and platelets in a spun sample is the buffy coat. Pick it for the middle layer of a centrifuged tube.","The fluid left after blood clots is serum, which is plasma with the clotting proteins removed. Choose serum when the clotting proteins are gone.","The cellular fraction is the formed elements: erythrocytes, leukocytes, and platelets. Plasma is a fluid with no cells in it.","Correct. Plasma is the fluid matrix in which the formed elements float."],"lt":"t-blood-plasma","lid":"c6","comp":81,"competencyId":"w4-blood-composition"},{"id":"L01312","dok":1,"q":"What is the approximate blood volume of an average adult?","a":"About 5 liters.","options":["About 15 liters","About 1 liter","About 40 liters","About 5 liters"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"An average adult has about 5 liters of blood, which is roughly 7 to 8 percent of body weight. This volume circulates continuously through the heart and vessels. Losing a large fraction of it quickly is life threatening, which is why blood volume matters in trauma care.","notes":["About 15 liters is closer to the total amount of fluid outside the cells in an adult, which includes the fluid between cells as well as plasma. Blood is only a part of that.","One liter is far too little for an adult. It is closer to the blood volume of a small child.","About 40 liters is closer to the total water in an adult body. Students who choose it confuse blood volume with all body water.","Correct. An average adult carries about 5 liters of blood."],"lt":"t-blood-plasma","lid":"c7","comp":81,"competencyId":"w4-blood-composition"},{"id":"L01313","dok":1,"q":"Blood makes up approximately what percentage of total body weight?","a":"About 7 to 8 percent.","options":["About 1 to 2 percent","About 60 percent","About 45 percent","About 7 to 8 percent"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Blood accounts for about 7 to 8 percent of body weight. For an average adult, that works out to about 5 liters. It is a small share of the body, but it reaches every tissue through the vessels.","notes":["About 1 to 2 percent is the share of circulating red cells that are reticulocytes. It is much too small for blood as a share of body weight.","About 60 percent is closer to the share of body weight made up of water. Blood is only a small part of that water.","About 45 percent is the share of whole blood made up of formed elements. It describes blood itself, not blood as a part of the body.","Correct. Blood is about 7 to 8 percent of body weight, about 5 liters in an adult."],"lt":"t-blood-plasma","lid":"c8","comp":81,"competencyId":"w4-blood-composition"},{"id":"L01314","dok":1,"q":"What is the normal pH range of blood?","a":"7.35 to 7.45.","options":["6.8 to 7.0","7.0 to 7.2","7.35 to 7.45","7.8 to 8.2"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Blood pH is held in a narrow range of 7.35 to 7.45. Because 7.0 is neutral, this range is slightly alkaline, meaning slightly basic. Values outside this range disturb how cells and proteins work, so the body regulates blood pH closely.","notes":["A range from 6.8 to 7.0 is neutral to slightly acidic. Students who pick it assume body fluids are neutral, but blood is slightly alkaline.","A pH of 7.0 to 7.2 is lower than normal blood. Blood in this range would be too acidic.","Correct. Normal blood is held in this narrow, slightly alkaline range.","A pH of 7.8 to 8.2 is far more alkaline than normal blood. Students who choose it overstate how basic blood is."],"lt":"t-blood-plasma","lid":"c9","comp":81,"competencyId":"w4-blood-composition"},{"id":"L01315","dok":1,"q":"Which description fits the pH of normal blood?","a":"Slightly alkaline, at about 7.35 to 7.45.","options":["Slightly alkaline, about 7.35 to 7.45","Slightly acidic, about 6.5 to 6.9","Strongly alkaline, about 9 to 10","Exactly neutral, at 7.0"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"On the pH scale, 7.0 is neutral, numbers below 7 are acidic, and numbers above 7 are alkaline (basic). Normal blood sits at 7.35 to 7.45, just above neutral, so it is slightly alkaline. Keeping blood in this narrow range is one of the regulatory functions of blood.","notes":["Correct. Just above neutral means slightly alkaline.","Values below 7.0 are acidic, and blood normally stays above 7.0. This choice reverses the side of neutral that blood sits on.","A pH of 9 to 10 is strongly basic, more like a mild cleaning solution. Blood is only slightly alkaline.","A pH of exactly 7.0 is neutral, like pure water. Blood is a little more alkaline than water."],"lt":"t-blood-plasma","lid":"c10","comp":81,"competencyId":"w4-blood-composition"},{"id":"L01316","dok":1,"q":"What is the normal temperature of blood?","a":"About 38°C (100.4°F).","options":["About 42°C (107.6°F)","About 35°C (95°F)","About 40°C (104°F)","About 38°C (100.4°F)"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Blood runs at about 38°C (100.4°F). Blood picks up heat from active organs and carries it around the body, which is part of its role in spreading heat evenly. When blood flows near the skin, it can give off that heat.","notes":["About 42°C (107.6°F) is a dangerously high temperature, well above any normal reading. Blood this hot would signal a medical emergency.","About 35°C (95°F) is cooler than normal and is the threshold usually used for hypothermia. Blood is not normally this cool.","About 40°C (104°F) is a high fever. Blood at this temperature would signal illness, not normal function.","Correct. Blood runs at about 38°C (100.4°F)."],"lt":"t-blood-plasma","lid":"c11","comp":81,"competencyId":"w4-blood-composition"},{"id":"L01317","dok":1,"q":"About how many times more viscous than water is blood?","a":"About 5 times.","options":["About the same as water","About 2 times","About 20 times","About 5 times"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Viscosity is how thick and resistant to flow a fluid is. Blood is about 5 times more viscous than water because it carries plasma proteins and a large number of formed elements. That thickness affects how hard the heart must work to push blood through the vessels.","notes":["Plasma is mostly water, but whole blood also carries proteins and cells, so it is clearly thicker than water. This choice ignores the formed elements.","Two times understates the thickness of whole blood. It is closer to the viscosity of plasma alone, which lacks the formed elements.","Twenty times overstates the thickness of blood. Blood this thick would flow very poorly through capillaries.","Correct. Proteins and formed elements make blood about 5 times thicker than water."],"lt":"t-blood-plasma","lid":"c12","comp":81,"competencyId":"w4-blood-composition"},{"id":"L01318","dok":1,"q":"Which two components of blood are chiefly responsible for its high viscosity?","a":"Its plasma proteins and its formed elements.","options":["Water and dissolved gases","Proteins and formed elements","Urea and other wastes","Electrolytes and glucose"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Viscosity is the thickness of a fluid, its resistance to flowing. Water alone flows easily, so blood gets its extra thickness from what is carried in it: the plasma proteins dissolved in the plasma and the formed elements suspended in it. More red cells, as in polycythemia, make blood even thicker and slower.","notes":["Water is the solvent that lets blood flow, and dissolved gases add little bulk. These parts make blood thinner, not thicker.","Correct. Proteins and cells are what make blood about 5 times thicker than water.","Urea, creatinine, and other wastes are dissolved in small amounts. They are carried in plasma for removal, not a major cause of viscosity.","Electrolytes such as Na+ and nutrients such as glucose are small dissolved solutes. They matter for chemistry but add little to the thickness of blood."],"lt":"t-blood-plasma","lid":"c13","comp":81,"competencyId":"w4-blood-composition"},{"id":"L01319","dok":1,"q":"What color is oxygen-rich blood?","a":"Bright red, also called scarlet.","options":["Blue","Dark red","Bright red","Pale yellow"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Blood gets its color from hemoglobin, the iron-containing protein in erythrocytes. When hemoglobin is loaded with oxygen, the blood is bright red, or scarlet. When it has given up much of its oxygen, the blood is a darker red.","notes":["Blood is never blue. Veins can look bluish through the skin because of how light passes through tissue, which leads students to this mistake.","Dark red is the color of oxygen-poor blood, such as blood returning to the heart in the veins. Pick it for blood after the tissues have taken its oxygen.","Correct. Oxygen-loaded hemoglobin makes blood scarlet.","Pale yellow is the color of plasma once the cells are removed. It does not describe whole blood."],"lt":"t-blood-plasma","lid":"c14","comp":81,"competencyId":"w4-blood-composition"},{"id":"L01320","dok":1,"q":"What color is oxygen-poor blood?","a":"Dark red.","options":["Bright red","Blue","Dark red","Pale yellow"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Hemoglobin changes color depending on how much oxygen it holds. Oxygen-poor blood, such as the blood returning to the heart in the veins, is dark red. It is still red because hemoglobin is a red pigment whether or not oxygen is bound to it.","notes":["Bright red, or scarlet, is the color of oxygen-rich blood, as in the arteries of the systemic circuit. It is the answer for blood just leaving the lungs.","Blood is not blue at any point. Veins only look bluish through the skin, which is an effect of light passing through tissue.","Correct. Hemoglobin that has released much of its oxygen gives blood a darker red color.","Pale yellow is the color of plasma after the cells are removed. Whole blood, with its erythrocytes, is always some shade of red."],"lt":"t-blood-plasma","lid":"c15","comp":81,"competencyId":"w4-blood-composition"},{"id":"L01321","dok":1,"q":"What is the most abundant component of plasma?","a":"Water, about 92 percent of plasma.","options":["Electrolytes","Nutrients","Water","Plasma proteins"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Plasma is mostly water, about 92 percent of its volume. Water is the solvent that carries every other component in solution: the plasma proteins, nutrients, wastes, electrolytes, and gases. The plasma proteins are the most abundant of the dissolved solutes, but they are a small share compared with water.","notes":["Electrolytes such as Na+, K+, and Cl- are important dissolved ions, but they make up only a tiny share of plasma by weight.","Nutrients such as glucose and amino acids are carried in plasma in small amounts. They are not close to the bulk of plasma.","Correct. About 92 percent of plasma is water.","Plasma proteins are the most abundant of the dissolved solutes, about 7 to 8 percent of plasma. Pick them if the question asked which solute is most abundant."],"lt":"t-blood-plasma","lid":"c16","comp":81,"competencyId":"w4-blood-composition"},{"id":"L01322","dok":1,"q":"About what percentage of plasma is water?","a":"About 92 percent.","options":["About 8 percent","About 55 percent","About 92 percent","About 45 percent"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Plasma is roughly 92 percent water, with the rest made up of dissolved solutes. The water acts as a solvent, carrying proteins, nutrients, wastes, electrolytes, and gases. This is why plasma looks like a thin, pale yellow fluid.","notes":["About 8 percent is close to the share of plasma made up of solutes, mostly proteins. It is the other part of plasma, not the water.","About 55 percent is the share of whole blood made up of plasma. It describes how much of the blood is plasma, not how much of the plasma is water.","Correct. Plasma is about 92 percent water.","About 45 percent is the formed element fraction of whole blood. It does not describe plasma at all."],"lt":"t-blood-plasma","lid":"c17","comp":81,"competencyId":"w4-blood-composition"},{"id":"L01323","dok":1,"q":"Plasma proteins make up approximately what percentage of plasma?","a":"About 7 to 8 percent.","options":["About 7 to 8 percent","About 45 percent","About 92 percent","About 55 percent"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"After water, the plasma proteins are the largest part of plasma, at about 7 to 8 percent. They include albumin, the globulins, and fibrinogen. Even though they are a small share by weight, they make up most of the dissolved solutes in plasma.","notes":["Correct. Plasma proteins are about 7 to 8 percent of plasma.","About 45 percent is the formed element share of whole blood. It is not a plasma component.","About 92 percent is the water content of plasma. Pick it for the most abundant component of plasma.","About 55 percent is the plasma share of whole blood. Students who pick this mix up how much blood is plasma with how much plasma is protein."],"lt":"t-blood-plasma","lid":"c18","comp":81,"competencyId":"w4-blood-composition"},{"id":"L01324","dok":1,"q":"Which is the most abundant plasma protein?","a":"Albumin.","options":["Fibrinogen","Albumin","Hemoglobin","Globulins"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Albumin makes up about 60 percent of the plasma proteins, which makes it the most abundant. It is made by the liver. Albumin holds water in the blood by maintaining osmotic pressure and carries hormones, fatty acids, and drugs.","notes":["Fibrinogen is the largest plasma protein and the clotting protein, but it is only about 4 percent of plasma proteins. Pick it for a question about clotting.","Correct. Albumin is about 60 percent of the plasma proteins.","Hemoglobin is found inside erythrocytes, not dissolved in plasma. Students choose it because it is the best-known blood protein.","The globulins are about 35 percent of plasma proteins and include transport proteins and the antibodies. They are the second most abundant group."],"lt":"t-blood-plasma","lid":"c19","comp":81,"competencyId":"w4-blood-composition"},{"id":"L01325","dok":1,"q":"Albumin makes up about what share of the plasma proteins?","a":"About 60 percent.","options":["About 4 percent","About 35 percent","About 60 percent","About 92 percent"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Albumin is the most abundant plasma protein, at about 60 percent of the total. The globulins make up about 35 percent and fibrinogen about 4 percent. Because albumin is so plentiful, it is the main protein holding water in the blood by osmotic pressure.","notes":["About 4 percent is the share of fibrinogen, the clotting protein. It is the smallest of the three main groups.","About 35 percent is the share of the globulins. Pick it if the question asked about the group that includes the antibodies.","Correct. Albumin is about 60 percent of the plasma proteins.","About 92 percent is the water content of plasma, not a protein share. Students who pick it mix up the plasma table."],"lt":"t-blood-plasma","lid":"c20","comp":81,"competencyId":"w4-blood-composition"},{"id":"L01326","dok":1,"q":"Which plasma protein group includes the antibodies, also called immunoglobulins?","a":"Gamma globulins.","options":["Gamma globulins","Alpha globulins","Albumin","Fibrinogen"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The globulins are divided into alpha, beta, and gamma globulins. The gamma globulins are the antibodies, the proteins of immune defense, and they are made by plasma cells rather than the liver. Alpha and beta globulins are transport proteins.","notes":["Correct. The gamma globulins are the antibodies.","Alpha globulins, along with beta globulins, are transport proteins. They carry substances in the plasma rather than defending against pathogens.","Albumin is the most abundant plasma protein, a carrier and the main source of osmotic pressure. It is not an antibody.","Fibrinogen is the clotting protein that converts to fibrin threads. Pick it for a question about clot formation."],"lt":"t-blood-plasma","lid":"c21","comp":81,"competencyId":"w4-blood-composition"},{"id":"L01327","dok":1,"q":"Which plasma protein is the largest and is converted to fibrin during clotting?","a":"Fibrinogen.","options":["Fibrinogen","Albumin","Gamma globulin","Transferrin"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Fibrinogen is the largest plasma protein and the clotting protein. During clotting it is converted to fibrin, which forms threads that make up the framework of a clot. It is made by the liver and makes up about 4 percent of plasma proteins.","notes":["Correct. Fibrinogen becomes the fibrin threads of a clot.","Albumin is the most abundant plasma protein, but not the largest, and it does not form clots. Choose it for osmotic pressure or carrying hormones and drugs.","Gamma globulins are the antibodies made by plasma cells. They act in immune defense, not clotting.","Transferrin is a globulin that carries iron through the plasma. Pick it for a question about iron transport."],"lt":"t-blood-plasma","lid":"c22","comp":81,"competencyId":"w4-blood-composition"},{"id":"L01328","dok":2,"q":"Which list puts the three major classes of plasma proteins in order from most to least abundant?","a":"Albumin, globulins, fibrinogen.","options":["Albumin, fibrinogen, globulins","Globulins, albumin, fibrinogen","Fibrinogen, albumin, globulins","Albumin, globulins, fibrinogen"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Albumin makes up about 60 percent of the plasma proteins, the globulins about 35 percent, and fibrinogen about 4 percent. So the order from most to least abundant is albumin, then globulins, then fibrinogen. Fibrinogen is the largest single molecule but the smallest share.","notes":["Albumin is correctly first, but fibrinogen at about 4 percent is far less abundant than the globulins at about 35 percent.","This puts the globulins first, but at about 35 percent they rank behind albumin at about 60 percent.","Fibrinogen is the largest plasma protein molecule, which leads some students to rank it first. By amount it is the least abundant of the three.","Correct. About 60, 35, and 4 percent."],"lt":"t-blood-plasma","lid":"c23","comp":81,"competencyId":"w4-blood-composition"},{"id":"L01329","dok":1,"q":"Fibrinogen makes up approximately what share of the plasma proteins?","a":"About 4 percent.","options":["About 60 percent","About 4 percent","About 35 percent","About 92 percent"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Fibrinogen is the largest plasma protein, but it is the smallest of the three main groups by amount, about 4 percent of plasma proteins. Albumin makes up about 60 percent and the globulins about 35 percent. A small amount of fibrinogen is enough because it only needs to form the fibrin framework of a clot when a vessel is injured.","notes":["About 60 percent is the share of albumin, the most abundant plasma protein. Pick it for a question about the protein that holds water in the blood.","Correct. Fibrinogen is a small share even though it is the largest molecule.","About 35 percent is the share of the globulins, which include the transport proteins and the antibodies.","About 92 percent is the water content of plasma, not a share of the proteins. Students who pick it are reading from the wrong row of the plasma table."],"lt":"t-blood-plasma","lid":"c24","comp":81,"competencyId":"w4-blood-composition"},{"id":"L01330","dok":1,"q":"The globulins make up approximately what share of the plasma proteins?","a":"About 35 percent.","options":["About 35 percent","About 55 percent","About 4 percent","About 60 percent"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The globulins are the second most abundant group of plasma proteins, at about 35 percent. Alpha and beta globulins are transport proteins, and gamma globulins are the antibodies. Albumin makes up about 60 percent and fibrinogen about 4 percent.","notes":["Correct. The globulins are about a third of the plasma proteins.","About 55 percent is the share of whole blood that is plasma. It describes blood volume, not the protein mix inside plasma.","About 4 percent is fibrinogen, the clotting protein. It is the smallest of the three main groups.","About 60 percent is albumin, the most abundant plasma protein. Choose it for the protein that maintains osmotic pressure."],"lt":"t-blood-plasma","lid":"c25","comp":81,"competencyId":"w4-blood-composition"},{"id":"L01331","dok":1,"q":"Where are all the plasma proteins made, except for the gamma globulins?","a":"In the liver.","options":["In the liver","In the spleen","In the kidney","In the red bone marrow"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The liver makes albumin, fibrinogen, and the alpha and beta globulins, so it produces most of the plasma proteins. The one exception is the gamma globulins, the antibodies, which are made by plasma cells of the immune system. This is why serious liver disease lowers most plasma protein levels.","notes":["Correct. The liver makes every major plasma protein except the antibodies.","The spleen, along with the liver, breaks down worn-out erythrocytes. It is the answer for removing old red cells, not making plasma proteins.","The kidney is the main source of erythropoietin, the hormone that stimulates red cell production. It does not make the plasma proteins.","Red bone marrow is where the formed elements are made by hematopoiesis. Pick it for a question about where blood cells form."],"lt":"t-blood-plasma","lid":"c26","comp":81,"competencyId":"w4-blood-composition"},{"id":"L01332","dok":2,"q":"Which plasma proteins are not made in the liver, and which cells make them?","a":"The gamma globulins (antibodies), made by plasma cells.","options":["Gamma globulins, made by plasma cells","Fibrinogen, made by platelets","Albumin, made by plasma cells","Alpha globulins, made by macrophages"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The gamma globulins are the antibodies, the proteins of immune defense. They are made by plasma cells, which develop from activated B lymphocytes. All the other major plasma proteins, albumin, fibrinogen, and the alpha and beta globulins, come from the liver.","notes":["Correct. Antibodies come from plasma cells, not the liver.","Fibrinogen is made by the liver. Platelets work alongside fibrinogen in clotting, which is why the two get linked, but platelets do not make it.","Albumin is made by the liver. Students who pick this attach the right cell to the wrong protein.","Alpha globulins are transport proteins made by the liver. Macrophages develop from monocytes and engulf debris and pathogens."],"lt":"t-blood-plasma","lid":"c27","comp":81,"competencyId":"w4-blood-composition"},{"id":"L01333","dok":2,"q":"Which globulins act as transport proteins, carrying substances such as iron and lipids in the plasma?","a":"The alpha and beta globulins.","options":["Alpha and beta globulins","Hemoglobin","The gamma globulins","Fibrinogen, the clotting protein"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The globulins fall into three groups. The alpha and beta globulins are transport proteins that carry substances through the plasma; transferrin, which carries iron, is one of them. The gamma globulins are a different job entirely, since they are the antibodies.","notes":["Correct. These globulins carry substances such as iron and lipids.","Hemoglobin transports oxygen, but it stays inside erythrocytes rather than circulating free in the plasma. It is not a plasma globulin.","The gamma globulins are the antibodies of immune defense, made by plasma cells. Pick them for a question about immunity.","Fibrinogen is the clotting protein that converts to fibrin threads. It is not a globulin and is not a transporter."],"lt":"t-blood-plasma","lid":"c28","comp":81,"competencyId":"w4-blood-composition"},{"id":"L01334","dok":2,"q":"Which plasma protein is the main carrier for hormones, fatty acids, and many drugs, and the main protein maintaining the osmotic pressure that holds water in the blood?","a":"Albumin.","options":["Albumin","Gamma globulin","Fibrinogen","Transferrin"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Albumin is the most abundant plasma protein, about 60 percent of the total. Because there is so much of it, it creates most of the osmotic pressure that pulls water into the blood and keeps it there. It also binds and carries hormones, fatty acids, and many drugs through the plasma.","notes":["Correct. Albumin is both the main carrier and the main source of osmotic pressure.","Gamma globulins are the antibodies made by plasma cells. They defend against pathogens rather than carrying hormones or holding water in the blood.","Fibrinogen is the largest plasma protein and the clotting protein. Pick it when the question asks about building a clot.","Transferrin is a transport globulin, but it carries iron specifically. It is too narrow in its job to be the main carrier or the source of osmotic pressure."],"lt":"t-blood-plasma","lid":"c29","comp":81,"competencyId":"w4-blood-composition"},{"id":"L01335","dok":2,"q":"A lab report lists the solutes found in a plasma sample. Which pair shows one nutrient and one nitrogenous waste?","a":"Glucose and urea.","options":["Albumin and fibrinogen","Glucose and urea","Na+ and Cl-","O2 and CO2"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Plasma carries many dissolved solutes besides proteins. Nutrients include glucose, amino acids, fatty acids, and vitamins, which are delivered to cells. Wastes include urea, creatinine, bilirubin, and ammonia, which contain nitrogen and are carried to the liver or kidneys for removal. Glucose and urea give one of each.","notes":["Albumin and fibrinogen are both plasma proteins. Pick this pair for a question about the main proteins made by the liver.","Correct. Glucose is a nutrient and urea is a nitrogenous waste.","Na+ and Cl- are both electrolytes, dissolved ions in plasma. They would answer a question about electrolytes.","O2 and CO2 are both dissolved gases. CO2 is a waste, but neither is a nutrient, and neither is a nitrogenous waste."],"lt":"t-blood-plasma","lid":"c30","comp":81,"competencyId":"w4-blood-composition"},{"id":"L01336","dok":1,"q":"Which of the following is a major electrolyte dissolved in plasma?","a":"Na+.","options":["Glucose","Urea","Albumin","Na+"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Electrolytes are dissolved ions that carry an electrical charge. The major electrolytes in plasma are sodium (Na+), potassium (K+), calcium (Ca2+), chloride (Cl-), and bicarbonate. Sodium is the most abundant positive ion in plasma.","notes":["Glucose is a nutrient carried in plasma. It dissolves but does not carry a charge, so it is not an electrolyte.","Urea is a nitrogenous waste carried to the kidneys. It is not an ion, so it is not an electrolyte.","Albumin is the most abundant plasma protein. Pick it for a question about proteins or osmotic pressure.","Correct. Sodium is a charged ion, a major plasma electrolyte."],"lt":"t-blood-plasma","lid":"c31","comp":81,"competencyId":"w4-blood-composition"},{"id":"L01337","dok":2,"q":"Which list contains only electrolytes normally dissolved in plasma?","a":"Na+, K+, Ca2+, Cl-.","options":["Na+, K+, Ca2+, Cl-","Albumin, globulins, fibrinogen, transferrin","Glucose, amino acids, fatty acids, vitamins","Urea, creatinine, bilirubin, ammonia"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Electrolytes are charged ions dissolved in a fluid. Plasma carries sodium (Na+), potassium (K+), calcium (Ca2+), chloride (Cl-), and bicarbonate as its main electrolytes. Plasma also carries nutrients, wastes, gases, and proteins, but those are separate categories.","notes":["Correct. All four are charged ions.","These are plasma proteins. They answer a question about the proteins, not the ions.","These are the nutrients carried in plasma. Pick this list for a question about what plasma delivers to cells for fuel and building.","These are the wastes carried in plasma. Choose this list for a question about what plasma carries away for removal."],"lt":"t-blood-plasma","lid":"c32","comp":81,"competencyId":"w4-blood-composition"},{"id":"L01338","dok":1,"q":"What is the liquid extracellular matrix of blood called?","a":"Plasma.","options":["Lymph","Plasma","Buffy coat","Serum"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Blood is a connective tissue, so it has cells suspended in a matrix. Its matrix is plasma, a pale yellow fluid that is about 92 percent water and carries proteins, nutrients, wastes, electrolytes, and gases. Plasma makes up about 55 percent of whole blood.","notes":["Lymph is the fluid inside lymphatic vessels, drained from the spaces around cells. It is a separate fluid, not the matrix of blood.","Correct. Plasma is the fluid matrix of blood.","The buffy coat is the thin pale layer of leukocytes and platelets in a spun sample. It is made of cells, not matrix.","Serum is plasma with the clotting proteins removed, the fluid left after blood clots. It is not the matrix of circulating blood."],"lt":"t-blood-plasma","lid":"c33","comp":81,"competencyId":"w4-blood-composition"},{"id":"L01339","dok":2,"q":"Which waste product in plasma travels bound to albumin on its way to the liver?","a":"Bilirubin.","options":["Creatinine","Urea","Glucose","Bilirubin"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Bilirubin is the yellow pigment made when the heme from worn-out red cells is broken down. It does not dissolve well in water, so it rides through the plasma attached to albumin until it reaches the liver. The liver then processes it and releases it in bile.","notes":["Creatinine is a waste from muscle that travels dissolved in plasma to the kidneys for excretion. It does not need albumin to carry it.","Urea is a nitrogenous waste made by the liver and carried dissolved in plasma to the kidneys. It travels toward the kidneys, not toward the liver on albumin.","Glucose is a nutrient, not a waste, and it dissolves freely in plasma. It is the answer for a fuel carried to cells.","Correct. Bilirubin from heme breakdown is carried on albumin to the liver."],"lt":"t-blood-plasma","lid":"c34","comp":81,"competencyId":"w4-blood-composition"},{"id":"L01340","dok":2,"q":"A blood component is a fluid that forms about 55 percent of whole blood, is about 92 percent water, and carries dissolved proteins, nutrients, and electrolytes. What is it?","a":"Plasma.","options":["Formed elements","Buffy coat","Plasma","Serum"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Each clue points to plasma. It is the fluid part of blood, it makes up about 55 percent of whole blood, it is about 92 percent water, and the rest is dissolved solutes such as proteins, nutrients, wastes, electrolytes, and gases. In a spun tube it forms the pale yellow top layer.","notes":["The formed elements are the cells and cell fragments, about 45 percent of whole blood. Pick them when the clues describe cells rather than fluid.","The buffy coat is a thin layer of leukocytes and platelets, less than 1 percent of a spun sample. It is cells, not a watery fluid.","Correct. Every clue matches plasma.","Serum is plasma after clotting, with the clotting proteins removed. It is not a fraction of circulating whole blood, so it does not match the 55 percent clue."],"lt":"t-blood-plasma","lid":"c35","comp":81,"competencyId":"w4-blood-composition"},{"id":"L01341","dok":3,"q":"A patient has severe liver failure, so the liver can no longer make proteins normally. Which group of plasma proteins is least directly affected?","a":"The gamma globulins.","options":["The alpha and beta globulins","Albumin","Fibrinogen","The gamma globulins"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The liver makes albumin, fibrinogen, and the alpha and beta globulins, so all of these fall when the liver fails. The gamma globulins, the antibodies, are made by plasma cells of the immune system instead. Because their source is outside the liver, they are the group least directly affected.","notes":["The alpha and beta globulins are transport proteins made by the liver. They fall in liver failure along with albumin and fibrinogen.","Albumin is made by the liver, so it drops in liver failure. Because it is the most abundant plasma protein, its loss is often the most noticeable.","Fibrinogen is made by the liver, so liver failure lowers it and slows clotting. Pick it for a question about why a patient with liver disease bleeds easily.","Correct. Antibodies come from plasma cells, not the liver."],"lt":"t-blood-plasma","lid":"c36","comp":81,"competencyId":"w4-blood-composition"},{"id":"L01342","dok":2,"q":"A protein group makes up about 35 percent of the plasma proteins and includes both transport proteins and the antibodies. Name the group.","a":"The globulins.","options":["The clotting factors","The globulins","Albumin","Fibrinogen"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The globulins are the only plasma protein group with both jobs. Alpha and beta globulins transport substances such as iron and lipids, and gamma globulins are the antibodies. Together they make up about 35 percent of the plasma proteins.","notes":["Clotting factors are proteins that work in clot formation, such as the one missing in hemophilia. They are not the group that includes antibodies and transporters.","Correct. Alpha and beta globulins transport, and gamma globulins are antibodies.","Albumin is about 60 percent of plasma proteins. It is a carrier, but it does not include the antibodies.","Fibrinogen is about 4 percent of plasma proteins and is the clotting protein. Pick it for a question about clot formation."],"lt":"t-blood-plasma","lid":"c37","comp":81,"competencyId":"w4-blood-composition"},{"id":"L01351","dok":1,"q":"What red, iron-containing protein fills the erythrocyte?","a":"Hemoglobin.","options":["Myoglobin","Ferritin","Hemoglobin","Transferrin"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Hemoglobin is the red, iron-containing protein that fills the erythrocyte and binds oxygen for transport. Each molecule has four subunits, and each subunit carries a heme group that holds one iron. Hemoglobin is also what gives blood its red color.","notes":["Myoglobin is a related oxygen-binding protein found in muscle cells, not in red blood cells. Pick it for a question about oxygen storage in muscle.","Ferritin is the protein that stores iron in tissues such as the liver, spleen, and bone marrow. It holds iron but is not found filling red cells.","Correct. Hemoglobin packs the red cell and carries oxygen.","Transferrin is the plasma protein that carries iron to the bone marrow. It moves iron through the plasma rather than filling red cells."],"lt":"t-blood-erythrocytes","lid":"c9","comp":82,"competencyId":"w4-formed-elements"},{"id":"L01353","dok":1,"q":"Which polypeptide chains make up one molecule of normal adult hemoglobin?","a":"Two alpha chains and two beta chains.","options":["Four alpha chains","One alpha and one beta chain","Two alpha and two beta chains","Three alpha and one beta chain"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Normal adult hemoglobin is made of four polypeptide chains: two alpha chains and two beta chains. Each chain holds one heme group with an iron at its center. Because the beta chains are part of the molecule, a change in the beta chain, as in sickle cell disease, changes the whole hemoglobin.","notes":["Hemoglobin does have alpha chains, but only two. Students who pick this forget that the molecule pairs two kinds of chain.","This gives only two chains. Hemoglobin has four subunits, so it needs two of each kind.","Correct. Adult hemoglobin has two of each.","This adds up to four but in the wrong mix. The chains come in matched pairs, two alpha and two beta."],"lt":"t-blood-erythrocytes","lid":"c11","comp":82,"competencyId":"w4-formed-elements"},{"id":"L01354","dok":1,"q":"How many heme groups does one hemoglobin molecule contain?","a":"Four.","options":["One","Four","Two","Eight"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Hemoglobin has four polypeptide subunits, and each subunit carries one heme group. So one hemoglobin molecule has four heme groups. Each heme holds one iron, and each iron can bind one oxygen molecule.","notes":["One heme group would mean one subunit. Myoglobin in muscle has a single heme, which may be the source of this mix-up.","Correct. One heme group per subunit gives four.","Two matches the number of alpha chains, or the number of beta chains, but not the total. Each of the four chains has its own heme.","Eight would be two hemes per chain. Each chain carries only one heme group."],"lt":"t-blood-erythrocytes","lid":"c12","comp":82,"competencyId":"w4-formed-elements"},{"id":"L01355","dok":1,"q":"Each heme group in hemoglobin holds one ion of which element at its center?","a":"Iron.","options":["Calcium","Magnesium","Iron","Sodium"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"A heme group is a ring-shaped structure with a single iron ion at its center. The iron is the part that actually binds oxygen. This is why iron deficiency reduces hemoglobin and can cause anemia.","notes":["Calcium is an important plasma electrolyte and is needed for clotting and muscle contraction. It is not part of heme.","Magnesium sits at the center of chlorophyll, the green pigment of plants, which has a ring similar to heme. Human heme uses iron instead.","Correct. Each heme holds one iron, which binds oxygen.","Sodium is the main positive electrolyte in plasma. It is dissolved in the fluid, not bound inside hemoglobin."],"lt":"t-blood-erythrocytes","lid":"c13","comp":82,"competencyId":"w4-formed-elements"},{"id":"L01357","dok":2,"q":"Based on its structure, what is the greatest number of oxygen molecules one hemoglobin molecule can carry?","a":"Four, one per heme group.","options":["Two","Four","Eight","One"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Hemoglobin has four subunits, each with one heme group, and each heme holds one iron that can bind one oxygen molecule. So one hemoglobin molecule can carry up to four oxygen molecules. A single red cell holds millions of hemoglobin molecules, which is why red cells carry so much oxygen.","notes":["Two matches the number of alpha chains or beta chains, not the total. Each of the four chains can bind oxygen.","Correct. Four hemes give four oxygen-binding sites.","Eight would require two binding sites per heme. Each heme iron binds only one oxygen molecule.","One oxygen would mean one heme. Students who pick this treat the whole molecule as a single binding site."],"lt":"t-blood-erythrocytes","lid":"c15","comp":82,"competencyId":"w4-formed-elements"},{"id":"L01358","dok":1,"q":"What is hemoglobin called when it is bound to oxygen?","a":"Oxyhemoglobin.","options":["Oxyhemoglobin","Carboxyhemoglobin","Methemoglobin","Deoxyhemoglobin"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Hemoglobin is named by what is attached to it. When oxygen is bound to the heme iron, it is called oxyhemoglobin, and the blood is bright red. When the oxygen has been released to the tissues, it is called deoxyhemoglobin.","notes":["Correct. Oxygen-bound hemoglobin is oxyhemoglobin.","Carboxyhemoglobin is hemoglobin bound to carbon monoxide, the dangerous form in carbon monoxide poisoning.","Methemoglobin has its iron oxidized to Fe3+ and cannot carry oxygen. It is the answer for that abnormal form.","Deoxyhemoglobin is hemoglobin that has released its oxygen. Pick it for blood returning from the tissues."],"lt":"t-blood-erythrocytes","lid":"c16","comp":82,"competencyId":"w4-formed-elements"},{"id":"L01359","dok":1,"q":"What is hemoglobin called after it has released its oxygen?","a":"Deoxyhemoglobin.","options":["Deoxyhemoglobin","Methemoglobin","Carboxyhemoglobin","Oxyhemoglobin"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Hemoglobin loads oxygen in the lungs and releases it in the tissues. Once the oxygen has been given up, the molecule is called deoxyhemoglobin, and the blood is a darker red. Its iron is still Fe2+, so it can pick up oxygen again on its next pass through the lungs.","notes":["Correct. Hemoglobin that has given up its oxygen is deoxyhemoglobin.","Methemoglobin has iron oxidized to Fe3+, so it cannot bind oxygen at all. Deoxyhemoglobin, by contrast, can still reload oxygen.","Carboxyhemoglobin is hemoglobin bound to carbon monoxide. Pick it for carbon monoxide poisoning, not for normal oxygen release.","Oxyhemoglobin is hemoglobin with oxygen bound, the bright red form leaving the lungs. It is the form before oxygen is released."],"lt":"t-blood-erythrocytes","lid":"c17","comp":82,"competencyId":"w4-formed-elements"},{"id":"L01360","dok":1,"q":"What is hemoglobin called when it is bound to carbon monoxide?","a":"Carboxyhemoglobin.","options":["Carboxyhemoglobin","Deoxyhemoglobin","Oxyhemoglobin","Methemoglobin"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Carbon monoxide binds to the heme iron in hemoglobin much more tightly than oxygen does. Hemoglobin with carbon monoxide attached is called carboxyhemoglobin. Because those binding sites are blocked, the blood carries less oxygen, which is why carbon monoxide is so dangerous.","notes":["Correct. Carbon monoxide bound to hemoglobin forms carboxyhemoglobin.","Deoxyhemoglobin is hemoglobin that has released its oxygen. It has nothing bound to its heme sites.","Oxyhemoglobin is hemoglobin bound to oxygen, the normal loaded form. Pick it for blood leaving the lungs.","Methemoglobin is defined by its iron being oxidized to Fe3+, not by carbon monoxide. Choose it for the Fe3+ form."],"lt":"t-blood-erythrocytes","lid":"c18","comp":82,"competencyId":"w4-formed-elements"},{"id":"L01362","dok":1,"q":"Which list names four forms of hemoglobin?","a":"Oxyhemoglobin, deoxyhemoglobin, carboxyhemoglobin, carbaminohemoglobin","options":["Proerythroblast, normoblast, reticulocyte, erythrocyte","Ferritin, transferrin, bilirubin, heme","Oxyhemoglobin, deoxyhemoglobin, carboxyhemoglobin, carbaminohemoglobin","Albumin, globulin, fibrinogen, transferrin"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Hemoglobin is named by what is bound to it. Oxyhemoglobin carries oxygen, deoxyhemoglobin has released its oxygen, carboxyhemoglobin carries carbon monoxide, and carbaminohemoglobin carries carbon dioxide. All four are the same protein carrying different cargo.","notes":["These are stages in red cell development, not forms of hemoglobin. Choose this list for a question about erythropoiesis.","These are substances in iron handling and red cell breakdown. Ferritin stores iron, transferrin carries it, and bilirubin comes from broken-down heme.","Correct. These four are named forms of hemoglobin.","These are plasma proteins, dissolved in the plasma rather than packed inside red cells. Pick this list for a question about plasma proteins."],"lt":"t-blood-erythrocytes","lid":"c20","comp":82,"competencyId":"w4-formed-elements"},{"id":"L01363","dok":2,"q":"Which two forms of hemoglobin differ only in whether oxygen is bound to them?","a":"Oxyhemoglobin and deoxyhemoglobin","options":["Carboxyhemoglobin and deoxyhemoglobin","Oxyhemoglobin and deoxyhemoglobin","Oxyhemoglobin and carboxyhemoglobin","Carbaminohemoglobin and carboxyhemoglobin"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Oxyhemoglobin and deoxyhemoglobin are the normal working pair. Oxyhemoglobin has oxygen bound as blood leaves the lungs, and deoxyhemoglobin is the same molecule after it has released that oxygen in the tissues. Hemoglobin switches back and forth between these two forms on every trip around the body.","notes":["Carboxyhemoglobin has carbon monoxide bound, while deoxyhemoglobin has released its oxygen and carries nothing on its heme. The difference is carbon monoxide, so pick this pair for a question about carbon monoxide poisoning.","Correct. Oxyhemoglobin has oxygen bound and deoxyhemoglobin has released it.","Oxyhemoglobin carries oxygen and carboxyhemoglobin carries carbon monoxide in the same binding sites, so they differ by which gas is bound. Pick this pair for a question about carbon monoxide taking the place of oxygen.","Carbaminohemoglobin carries carbon dioxide and carboxyhemoglobin carries carbon monoxide. Neither is defined by oxygen, so this pair fits a question about the two forms named for carbon gases."],"lt":"t-blood-erythrocytes","lid":"c21","comp":82,"competencyId":"w4-formed-elements"},{"id":"L01364","dok":1,"q":"Roughly what is the normal hemoglobin level in the blood of an adult man?","a":"About 13 to 18 g/dL.","options":["About 13 to 18 g/dL","About 42 to 52 g/dL","About 150 to 400 g/dL","About 4 to 11 g/dL"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Hemoglobin is measured in grams per deciliter (g/dL) of blood. A normal adult man runs roughly 13 to 18 g/dL, and an adult woman runs a little lower. A level well below the normal range points toward anemia, meaning the blood carries less oxygen.","notes":["Correct. Adult men run roughly 13 to 18 grams of hemoglobin per deciliter.","The numbers 42 to 52 are the normal hematocrit for men, given as a percentage of blood volume. Students who pick this mix up hematocrit with hemoglobin.","The numbers 150 to 400 match the normal platelet count, in thousands per microliter. They are far too high for hemoglobin.","The numbers 4 to 11 match the normal white cell count, in thousands per microliter. A hemoglobin level in this range would mean anemia."],"lt":"t-blood-erythrocytes","lid":"c22","comp":82,"competencyId":"w4-formed-elements"},{"id":"L01365","dok":1,"q":"Which statement correctly compares normal hemoglobin levels in adult women and men?","a":"Women run slightly lower, about 12 to 16 g/dL compared with about 13 to 18 g/dL in men.","options":["Women and men have exactly the same range","Women run slightly higher, about 13 to 18 versus 12 to 16 g/dL","Women run slightly lower, about 12 to 16 versus 13 to 18 g/dL","Women run about half the level of men"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Normal hemoglobin ranges differ a little between the sexes. Adult women typically run about 12 to 16 g/dL and adult men about 13 to 18 g/dL. The ranges overlap, but the female range sits slightly lower, so a lab result has to be read against the right range.","notes":["The ranges overlap but are not identical. Treating them as the same can make a normal value look abnormal.","This swaps the two ranges. The higher range belongs to men.","Correct. The female range sits a little below the male range.","The difference is small, about 1 to 2 g/dL. A level half of the male range would be severe anemia."],"lt":"t-blood-erythrocytes","lid":"c23","comp":82,"competencyId":"w4-formed-elements"},{"id":"L01366","dok":1,"q":"Roughly what is the normal hematocrit for an adult man?","a":"About 42 to 52 percent.","options":["About 42 to 52 percent","About 13 to 18 percent","About 55 to 65 percent","About 1 to 2 percent"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Hematocrit is the percentage of blood volume made up of erythrocytes, measured by spinning a blood sample. In adult men it is roughly 42 to 52 percent, which matches the formed elements being a little under half of whole blood. Women run a few points lower.","notes":["Correct. Red cells make up a little under half of a man's blood volume.","The numbers 13 to 18 are the normal hemoglobin range for men in g/dL. Students who pick this mix up hemoglobin with hematocrit.","About 55 percent is the share of blood made up of plasma, not red cells. A hematocrit this high would suggest polycythemia.","About 1 to 2 percent is the share of red cells that are reticulocytes. It is much too small for the whole red cell fraction."],"lt":"t-blood-erythrocytes","lid":"c24","comp":81,"competencyId":"w4-blood-composition"},{"id":"L01367","dok":1,"q":"Roughly what is the normal hematocrit for an adult woman?","a":"About 37 to 47 percent.","options":["About 60 to 70 percent","About 20 to 30 percent","About 7 to 8 percent","About 37 to 47 percent"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Hematocrit is the percentage of blood volume that is erythrocytes. In adult women it is roughly 37 to 47 percent, a few points lower than in men. A hematocrit well below this range is one sign of anemia.","notes":["About 60 to 70 percent is close to the share of leukocytes that are neutrophils. A hematocrit this high would be abnormal.","A hematocrit this low would point to anemia. The numbers 20 to 30 are close to the lymphocyte share of leukocytes.","About 7 to 8 percent is the share of body weight made up of blood. It is far too low for a hematocrit.","Correct. Women run a few points below the male range."],"lt":"t-blood-erythrocytes","lid":"c25","comp":81,"competencyId":"w4-blood-composition"},{"id":"L01368","dok":1,"q":"The hematocrit measures the percentage of blood volume made up of which component?","a":"Erythrocytes.","options":["Leukocytes","Platelets","Plasma","Erythrocytes"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"When blood is spun in a tube, the heavy erythrocytes pack at the bottom, the plasma rises to the top, and a thin buffy coat of leukocytes and platelets lies between. The hematocrit is the height of the red cell layer as a percentage of the whole column. It gives a quick check on whether the blood has too few or too many red cells.","notes":["Leukocytes form part of the thin buffy coat, less than 1 percent of the sample. A white cell count, not the hematocrit, measures them.","Platelets also sit in the thin buffy coat. They are counted separately as the platelet count.","Plasma is the fluid fraction, about 55 percent. It makes up the rest of the tube, but the hematocrit reports the red cells.","Correct. Hematocrit is the red cell fraction of blood volume."],"lt":"t-blood-erythrocytes","lid":"c26","comp":81,"competencyId":"w4-blood-composition"},{"id":"L01369","dok":2,"q":"When worn-out erythrocytes are broken down, which protein stores the recovered iron, and where?","a":"Ferritin, in the liver, spleen, and bone marrow.","options":["Transferrin, circulating in the plasma","Albumin, circulating in the plasma","Fibrinogen, in the red bone marrow","Ferritin, in liver, spleen, and marrow"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Iron from broken-down hemoglobin is too valuable to lose, so the body saves it. Ferritin is the storage protein that holds iron in tissues such as the liver, spleen, and bone marrow. When the marrow needs iron to build new hemoglobin, it is released and carried there through the plasma.","notes":["Transferrin carries iron through the plasma rather than storing it. Pick it for the protein that moves iron to the marrow.","Albumin is a general carrier and the main osmotic protein. It carries bilirubin but is not the iron storage protein.","Fibrinogen is the clotting protein made by the liver. It plays no part in storing iron.","Correct. Ferritin is the iron storage protein in tissues."],"lt":"t-blood-erythrocytes","lid":"c27","comp":82,"competencyId":"w4-formed-elements"},{"id":"L01370","dok":2,"q":"Which protein transports iron through the plasma?","a":"Transferrin.","options":["Albumin","Ferritin","Hemoglobin","Transferrin"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Iron does not travel freely in the blood. Transferrin, one of the transport globulins, binds iron and carries it through the plasma to the red bone marrow, where it is used to build new hemoglobin. Ferritin, by contrast, holds iron in storage inside tissues.","notes":["Albumin carries hormones, fatty acids, drugs, and bilirubin, but iron has its own carrier. Pick albumin for the general carrier protein.","Ferritin stores iron in the liver, spleen, and bone marrow. Choose it for a question about iron storage.","Hemoglobin contains iron, but it stays inside erythrocytes and carries oxygen. It does not deliver iron through the plasma.","Correct. Transferrin carries iron through the plasma."],"lt":"t-blood-erythrocytes","lid":"c28","comp":82,"competencyId":"w4-formed-elements"},{"id":"L01371","dok":2,"q":"How do ferritin and transferrin differ in function?","a":"Ferritin stores iron in tissues; transferrin transports iron in the plasma.","options":["Both store iron, in different organs","Ferritin stores iron; transferrin transports it in plasma","Both transport iron, in different fluids","Ferritin transports iron in plasma; transferrin stores it"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The body handles iron with two different proteins. Ferritin stores iron inside tissues such as the liver, spleen, and bone marrow. Transferrin carries iron through the plasma to where it is needed, mainly the red bone marrow for building hemoglobin.","notes":["Only ferritin is a storage protein. Transferrin moves iron through the plasma.","Correct. Storage and transport are separate jobs.","Only transferrin is a transport protein. Ferritin holds iron in place inside tissues.","This swaps the two roles. A memory cue is that transferrin transfers iron from place to place."],"lt":"t-blood-erythrocytes","lid":"c29","comp":82,"competencyId":"w4-formed-elements"},{"id":"L01372","dok":1,"q":"Which pair of organs breaks down most worn-out erythrocytes?","a":"The spleen and the liver.","options":["The heart and the lungs","The thymus and the lymph nodes","The kidney and the pancreas","The spleen and the liver"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"After about 120 days, erythrocytes become stiff and fragile. As they pass through the spleen and liver, macrophages in these organs engulf the worn cells and break them down. The iron is saved, the globin is recycled into amino acids, and the heme is converted to bilirubin.","notes":["The heart pumps blood and the lungs load it with oxygen. Red cells pass through them constantly but are not broken down there.","The thymus is where T lymphocytes mature, and lymph nodes filter lymph. Neither is the main site for breaking down red cells.","The kidney makes erythropoietin, which stimulates red cell production, so it is involved in making red cells, not removing them. The pancreas is a digestive and endocrine gland.","Correct. Macrophages in these organs remove worn red cells."],"lt":"t-blood-erythrocytes","lid":"c30","comp":82,"competencyId":"w4-formed-elements"},{"id":"L01373","dok":1,"q":"Which cells engulf and break down aged erythrocytes?","a":"Macrophages.","options":["Neutrophils","Plasma cells","Macrophages","Platelets"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Macrophages are large phagocytic cells, meaning cells that engulf and digest particles. They develop from monocytes that leave the blood. Macrophages in the spleen and liver recognize old, stiff red cells and engulf them, then break down their hemoglobin.","notes":["Neutrophils are phagocytes too, but they are the first responders to bacterial infection. They are not the cells that clear old red cells.","Plasma cells develop from B lymphocytes and make antibodies. They do not engulf cells.","Correct. Macrophages engulf worn-out red cells.","Platelets are cell fragments that plug vessel injuries and help form clots. They cannot engulf other cells."],"lt":"t-blood-erythrocytes","lid":"c31","comp":82,"competencyId":"w4-formed-elements"},{"id":"L01374","dok":1,"q":"When hemoglobin is broken down, which two main parts is it first split into?","a":"Heme and globin.","options":["Ferritin and transferrin","Bilirubin and urea","Heme and globin","Iron and albumin"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Hemoglobin is made of a protein part, globin, and the iron-holding heme groups. When macrophages break hemoglobin down, they first split it into these two parts. The globin goes on to become amino acids, while the heme gives up its iron and the rest becomes bilirubin.","notes":["Ferritin and transferrin are proteins that store and carry iron after it is released. They are not pieces of hemoglobin.","Bilirubin is made later from the heme ring, and urea is a nitrogenous waste from protein breakdown in the liver. Neither is a first split product.","Correct. Hemoglobin separates into its heme groups and its globin chains.","Iron is released from heme later in the process, and albumin is a plasma protein that is not part of hemoglobin."],"lt":"t-blood-erythrocytes","lid":"c32","comp":82,"competencyId":"w4-formed-elements"},{"id":"L01375","dok":1,"q":"After hemoglobin is broken down, what happens to the globin chains?","a":"They are broken into amino acids that are recycled.","options":["They are stored as ferritin","They are recycled as amino acids","They are excreted unchanged in urine","They are converted to bilirubin"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Globin is the protein portion of hemoglobin. Like any protein, it can be broken down into its building blocks, the amino acids. These amino acids return to the blood and are reused to build new proteins.","notes":["Ferritin stores the iron from heme, not the globin. Pick ferritin for what happens to the iron.","Correct. The protein part is reduced to reusable amino acids.","Intact globin chains are not passed in urine. The body breaks them down and reuses the amino acids.","Bilirubin comes from the heme ring, not the globin. Choose bilirubin for the fate of the heme."],"lt":"t-blood-erythrocytes","lid":"c33","comp":82,"competencyId":"w4-formed-elements"},{"id":"L01376","dok":1,"q":"When the heme ring is broken down after iron is removed, which yellow pigment is formed and carried to the liver?","a":"Bilirubin.","options":["Bilirubin","Urea","Fibrin","Ferritin"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Once the iron is removed from heme, the remaining ring is converted to bilirubin, a yellow pigment. Bilirubin travels through the plasma bound to albumin to the liver, which secretes it in bile. When bilirubin builds up in the blood, the skin and eyes can turn yellow, a sign called jaundice.","notes":["Correct. Heme breakdown produces the yellow pigment bilirubin.","Urea is a nitrogenous waste made in the liver from amino acid breakdown. It is carried to the kidneys, not made from heme.","Fibrin is the threadlike protein formed from fibrinogen during clotting. It has nothing to do with heme breakdown.","Ferritin is the protein that stores the iron removed from heme. It is not a pigment made from the ring."],"lt":"t-blood-erythrocytes","lid":"c34","comp":82,"competencyId":"w4-formed-elements"},{"id":"L01381","dok":1,"q":"What is the general name for the formation of all the formed elements of blood?","a":"Hematopoiesis.","options":["Erythropoiesis","Hemostasis","Hematopoiesis","Phagocytosis"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Hematopoiesis is the formation of all the formed elements: erythrocytes, leukocytes, and platelets. In adults it takes place in red bone marrow, starting from one kind of stem cell. More specific terms name the formation of each cell type, such as erythropoiesis for red cells.","notes":["Erythropoiesis is the formation of red blood cells only. It is one branch of hematopoiesis.","Hemostasis is the stopping of bleeding, through vessel spasm, a platelet plug, and clotting. Pick it for a question about controlling blood loss.","Correct. Hematopoiesis covers the formation of every formed element.","Phagocytosis is the engulfing of particles by cells such as macrophages and neutrophils. It describes destroying cells, not forming them."],"lt":"t-blood-hematopoiesis","lid":"c1","comp":83,"competencyId":"w4-hematopoiesis"},{"id":"L01382","dok":1,"q":"Where does hematopoiesis take place in an adult?","a":"In red bone marrow.","options":["In the liver","In yellow bone marrow","In red bone marrow","In the spleen"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"In adults, blood cells are formed in red bone marrow, found in the spongy bone of the axial skeleton and the proximal ends of limb bones such as the femur and humerus. This is why a marrow sample to examine blood formation is taken from the iliac crest or sternum.","notes":["The liver forms blood cells in the fetus, but not in a healthy adult. In adults it makes plasma proteins and helps remove old red cells.","Yellow marrow is mostly fat and fills the medullary cavity of adult long bones. It does not normally form blood cells.","Correct. Red marrow in spongy bone is the adult blood-forming tissue.","The spleen removes worn-out red cells and is a lymphatic organ. It is not the normal adult site of blood cell formation."],"lt":"t-blood-hematopoiesis","lid":"c2","comp":83,"competencyId":"w4-hematopoiesis"},{"id":"L01383","dok":1,"q":"What is the name of the stem cell from which every formed element arises?","a":"The hemocytoblast.","options":["The myeloid stem cell","The lymphoid stem cell","The hemocytoblast","The megakaryocyte"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The hemocytoblast is the pluripotent hematopoietic stem cell, meaning it can give rise to every type of formed element. It lives in red bone marrow and divides to produce the myeloid and lymphoid stem cells. Those committed lines then produce red cells, platelets, and the five kinds of leukocytes.","notes":["The myeloid stem cell comes from the hemocytoblast and gives rise to every formed element except lymphocytes. It is one step down the tree.","The lymphoid stem cell comes from the hemocytoblast and gives rise only to lymphocytes. Pick it for the lymphocyte line.","Correct. The hemocytoblast is the stem cell at the top of the blood cell family tree.","The megakaryocyte is the giant marrow cell whose cytoplasm breaks into platelets. It is far down one branch, not the common ancestor."],"lt":"t-blood-hematopoiesis","lid":"c3","comp":83,"competencyId":"w4-hematopoiesis"},{"id":"L01384","dok":1,"q":"The hemocytoblast divides to produce which two committed stem cell lines?","a":"The myeloid stem cell and the lymphoid stem cell.","options":["Erythroid and megakaryocyte stem cells","Myeloid and lymphoid stem cells","Granulocyte and agranulocyte stem cells","B cell and T cell stem cells"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Just below the hemocytoblast, the blood cell family tree splits into two lines. The myeloid line produces erythrocytes, platelets, and all leukocytes except lymphocytes. The lymphoid line produces the lymphocytes.","notes":["The red cell and platelet lineages both branch from the myeloid line further down. They are not the first split.","Correct. These are the two lines that branch from the hemocytoblast.","Granulocyte and agranulocyte are the two groups of leukocytes sorted by visible granules, not the two stem cell lines. Monocytes are agranulocytes but come from the myeloid line.","B and T cells are both lymphocytes and come from the lymphoid line. They are products of one branch, not the two branches."],"lt":"t-blood-hematopoiesis","lid":"c4","comp":83,"competencyId":"w4-hematopoiesis"},{"id":"L01385","dok":1,"q":"Which stem cell line gives rise to the lymphocytes?","a":"The lymphoid line.","options":["The lymphoid line","The myeloid line","The megakaryocyte line","The erythroid line"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The hemocytoblast produces two committed lines. The lymphoid line gives rise to the lymphocytes, which include the B cells, T cells, and natural killer cells. Every other formed element comes from the myeloid line.","notes":["Correct. Lymphocytes come from the lymphoid stem cell.","The myeloid line produces erythrocytes, platelets, and the other leukocytes, everything except lymphocytes. It is the answer for neutrophils or monocytes.","The megakaryocyte line produces platelets. It is also a branch of the myeloid line.","The erythroid line produces red blood cells through the proerythroblast and reticulocyte stages. It is a branch of the myeloid line."],"lt":"t-blood-hematopoiesis","lid":"c5","comp":83,"competencyId":"w4-hematopoiesis"},{"id":"L01386","dok":2,"q":"The myeloid stem cell gives rise to every formed element except which one?","a":"The lymphocyte.","options":["The monocyte","The platelet","The lymphocyte","The erythrocyte"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The myeloid line produces erythrocytes, platelets, and the leukocytes, with one exception. Lymphocytes come from the separate lymphoid line. So neutrophils, eosinophils, basophils, and monocytes are myeloid, while lymphocytes are lymphoid.","notes":["Monocytes are agranulocytes like lymphocytes, which can mislead students, but monocytes come from the myeloid line through the monoblast.","Platelets come from the myeloid line through megakaryocytes. They are a myeloid product.","Correct. Lymphocytes come from the lymphoid line instead.","Erythrocytes come from the myeloid line through the proerythroblast. They are a myeloid product."],"lt":"t-blood-hematopoiesis","lid":"c6","comp":83,"competencyId":"w4-hematopoiesis"},{"id":"L01387","dok":2,"q":"Which lineage branches from the myeloid stem cell?","a":"The megakaryocyte lineage, which makes platelets.","options":["The T lymphocyte lineage","The megakaryocyte lineage","The natural killer cell lineage","The B lymphocyte lineage"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The myeloid stem cell gives rise to several lineages: the red cell line, the granulocyte line, the monocyte line, and the megakaryocyte line that produces platelets. The lymphocytes, including B cells, T cells, and natural killer cells, all come from the separate lymphoid line.","notes":["T lymphocytes come from the lymphoid line and mature in the thymus. They are not myeloid.","Correct. Platelet-forming megakaryocytes come from the myeloid line.","Natural killer cells are lymphocytes, so they come from the lymphoid line. They are not myeloid.","B lymphocytes come from the lymphoid line. Pick this for a question about the cells that become antibody-making plasma cells."],"lt":"t-blood-hematopoiesis","lid":"c7","comp":83,"competencyId":"w4-hematopoiesis"},{"id":"L01388","dok":1,"q":"What is the specific name for the formation of red blood cells?","a":"Erythropoiesis.","options":["Hematopoiesis","Erythropoiesis","Thrombopoiesis","Leukopoiesis"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Erythro means red and poiesis means making, so erythropoiesis is the formation of red blood cells. It is one branch of hematopoiesis, the formation of all blood cells. Erythropoiesis is stimulated by erythropoietin, a hormone made mainly by the kidney.","notes":["Hematopoiesis is the general term for forming all blood cells. It includes erythropoiesis but is broader.","Correct. Erythropoiesis is red cell formation.","Thrombopoiesis is the formation of platelets, also called thrombocytes. Pick it for platelet production.","Leukopoiesis is the formation of white blood cells. Pick it for leukocyte production."],"lt":"t-blood-hematopoiesis","lid":"c8","comp":83,"competencyId":"w4-hematopoiesis"},{"id":"L01389","dok":1,"q":"In erythropoiesis, which cell is the first one committed to becoming a red blood cell?","a":"The proerythroblast.","options":["The monoblast","The megakaryoblast","The proerythroblast","The myeloblast"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The myeloid stem cell can produce several kinds of blood cells. Once it gives rise to a proerythroblast, that cell is committed to the red cell line and will only become an erythrocyte. Each of the other myeloid lines has its own committed blast cell.","notes":["The monoblast is the committed cell of the monocyte line. Pick it for the precursor of monocytes and macrophages.","The megakaryoblast is the committed cell of the platelet line. It develops into a megakaryocyte, which breaks into platelets.","Correct. The proerythroblast is the first cell committed to the red cell line.","The myeloblast is the committed cell of the granulocyte line, which produces neutrophils, eosinophils, and basophils."],"lt":"t-blood-hematopoiesis","lid":"c9","comp":83,"competencyId":"w4-hematopoiesis"},{"id":"L01390","dok":2,"q":"Which sequence correctly traces erythropoiesis from the stem cell to the mature red cell?","a":"Hemocytoblast, myeloid stem cell, proerythroblast, erythroblast, normoblast, reticulocyte, erythrocyte.","options":["Hemocytoblast, myeloid stem cell, proerythroblast, normoblast, erythroblast, reticulocyte, erythrocyte","Hemocytoblast, myeloid stem cell, proerythroblast, erythroblast, normoblast, reticulocyte, erythrocyte","Hemocytoblast, lymphoid stem cell, proerythroblast, erythroblast, normoblast, reticulocyte, erythrocyte","Hemocytoblast, myeloid stem cell, proerythroblast, erythroblast, reticulocyte, normoblast, erythrocyte"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Red cell development starts with the hemocytoblast, the stem cell for all formed elements, and passes to the myeloid stem cell. The proerythroblast is the first committed red cell precursor, followed by the erythroblast stages as hemoglobin builds up. The normoblast ejects its nucleus, the reticulocyte still holds ribosome remnants, and the erythrocyte is the mature cell.","notes":["This swaps the erythroblast and normoblast. The erythroblast comes first and builds hemoglobin, and the normoblast follows and ejects the nucleus.","Correct. This is the full red cell pathway in order.","Red cells come from the myeloid line, not the lymphoid line. The lymphoid stem cell would start a pathway that ends in lymphocytes.","This puts the reticulocyte before the normoblast. The nucleus is ejected at the normoblast stage, so the nucleus-free reticulocyte must come after it."],"lt":"t-blood-hematopoiesis","lid":"c10","comp":83,"competencyId":"w4-hematopoiesis"},{"id":"L01391","dok":1,"q":"At which stage of erythropoiesis is the nucleus ejected as hemoglobin accumulates?","a":"The normoblast.","options":["The reticulocyte","The normoblast","The hemocytoblast","The proerythroblast"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"As a developing red cell fills with hemoglobin, it reaches the normoblast stage and ejects its nucleus. Losing the nucleus makes room for more hemoglobin and produces a reticulocyte, which still carries ribosome remnants. The reticulocyte then matures into an erythrocyte.","notes":["The reticulocyte comes after nucleus ejection. It has no nucleus, only ribosome remnants.","Correct. The normoblast is the stage that ejects the nucleus.","The hemocytoblast is the stem cell at the top of the blood cell tree. It keeps its nucleus and divides to make new cells.","The proerythroblast is the first committed red cell precursor. It still has its nucleus and has barely begun making hemoglobin."],"lt":"t-blood-hematopoiesis","lid":"c11","comp":83,"competencyId":"w4-hematopoiesis"},{"id":"L01392","dok":1,"q":"What is the last immature stage before a fully mature erythrocyte?","a":"The reticulocyte.","options":["The reticulocyte","The proerythroblast","The normoblast","The hemocytoblast"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The reticulocyte is the final immature stage of the red cell line. It has already ejected its nucleus but still contains ribosome remnants. It enters the blood and matures into an erythrocyte within a day or two as those remnants disappear.","notes":["Correct. The reticulocyte is the step right before the mature erythrocyte.","The proerythroblast is the first committed red cell precursor, at the start of the line rather than the end.","The normoblast comes just before the reticulocyte and is the stage that ejects the nucleus. It is one step too early.","The hemocytoblast is the stem cell that every formed element comes from. It is at the very top of the tree."],"lt":"t-blood-hematopoiesis","lid":"c12","comp":83,"competencyId":"w4-hematopoiesis"},{"id":"L01393","dok":1,"q":"What does a reticulocyte still contain that a mature erythrocyte lacks?","a":"Ribosome remnants.","options":["A small nucleus","Ribosome remnants","Visible cytoplasmic granules","Hemoglobin"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"A reticulocyte is an immature red cell that has already ejected its nucleus. It still holds remnants of its ribosomes, which can be stained to show a fine network, and that network gives the cell its name. Once these remnants are lost, the cell is a mature erythrocyte.","notes":["The nucleus is ejected at the normoblast stage, before the reticulocyte. A cell that still has a nucleus is an earlier stage.","Correct. Leftover ribosomes are what mark a reticulocyte.","Visible cytoplasmic granules are the mark of granulocytes, the neutrophils, eosinophils, and basophils. They are not found in the red cell line.","Both reticulocytes and mature erythrocytes contain hemoglobin. It is not something the mature cell lacks."],"lt":"t-blood-hematopoiesis","lid":"c13","comp":83,"competencyId":"w4-hematopoiesis"},{"id":"L01394","dok":1,"q":"About what percentage of circulating red blood cells are normally reticulocytes?","a":"About 1 to 2 percent.","options":["About 1 to 2 percent","About 7 to 8 percent","About 45 percent","About 20 to 30 percent"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Normally about 1 to 2 percent of the red cells in circulation are reticulocytes. They enter the blood from the marrow and mature within a day or two, so only a small share is present at any time. This number gives a rough idea of how fast the marrow is making new red cells.","notes":["Correct. Only a small share of circulating red cells are reticulocytes.","About 7 to 8 percent is the share of body weight that is blood. It is too high for the normal reticulocyte share.","About 45 percent is the share of whole blood made up of formed elements. It describes all the cells, not the young red cells.","This is close to the share of leukocytes that are lymphocytes. A reticulocyte count this high would be far above normal."],"lt":"t-blood-hematopoiesis","lid":"c14","comp":83,"competencyId":"w4-hematopoiesis"},{"id":"L01395","dok":2,"q":"Which is the first stage of erythropoiesis that has no nucleus?","a":"The reticulocyte.","options":["The erythroblast","The proerythroblast","The reticulocyte","The normoblast"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The nucleus is ejected at the end of the normoblast stage. The cell that results, the reticulocyte, is the first stage with no nucleus. It still carries ribosome remnants until it matures into an erythrocyte.","notes":["The erythroblast is an earlier stage that is building hemoglobin and still has its nucleus.","The proerythroblast is the first committed red cell precursor and has a nucleus.","Correct. The reticulocyte is the first stage after the nucleus is ejected.","The normoblast is the stage that ejects the nucleus, so it still has one until the end of that stage."],"lt":"t-blood-hematopoiesis","lid":"c15","comp":83,"competencyId":"w4-hematopoiesis"},{"id":"L01396","dok":2,"q":"Which stage of erythropoiesis comes directly between the proerythroblast and the normoblast?","a":"The erythroblast.","options":["The reticulocyte","The megakaryoblast","The hemocytoblast","The erythroblast"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"After the proerythroblast commits to the red cell line, it develops through the erythroblast stages, during which hemoglobin builds up in the cytoplasm. Next comes the normoblast, which ejects the nucleus. So the order is proerythroblast, erythroblast, normoblast.","notes":["The reticulocyte comes after the normoblast, once the nucleus has been ejected. It is later in the sequence.","The megakaryoblast is on the platelet line, not the red cell line. Pick it for a question about platelet formation.","The hemocytoblast is the stem cell at the very start of hematopoiesis. It comes before the proerythroblast.","Correct. The erythroblast stages lie between the proerythroblast and the normoblast."],"lt":"t-blood-hematopoiesis","lid":"c16","comp":83,"competencyId":"w4-hematopoiesis"},{"id":"L01397","dok":1,"q":"Erythropoietin is the hormone that stimulates red blood cell production. Which organ is its main source?","a":"The kidney.","options":["The spleen","The red bone marrow","The kidney","The liver"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Erythropoietin is made chiefly by the kidney. When tissues are short of oxygen, the kidney releases more erythropoietin, and the red bone marrow responds by making more erythrocytes. This is why people with failing kidneys often become anemic.","notes":["The spleen removes worn-out red cells and is a lymphatic organ. It does not make erythropoietin.","Red bone marrow responds to erythropoietin by making red cells. It is the target of the hormone, not its source.","Correct. The kidney is the main source of erythropoietin.","The liver makes a small amount of erythropoietin, but the kidney is the main source. The liver is better known for making plasma proteins."],"lt":"t-blood-hematopoiesis","lid":"c17","comp":83,"competencyId":"w4-hematopoiesis"},{"id":"L01398","dok":1,"q":"Which committed marrow cell develops into the megakaryocyte that produces platelets?","a":"The megakaryoblast.","options":["The monoblast","The megakaryoblast","The proerythroblast","The myeloblast"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Platelet formation, called thrombopoiesis, begins with a committed cell called the megakaryoblast. It grows into a megakaryocyte, a giant marrow cell, whose cytoplasm breaks into thousands of platelets. Each myeloid line has its own blast cell like this.","notes":["The monoblast is the committed cell of the monocyte line, which leads to monocytes and then macrophages.","Correct. The megakaryoblast is the committed precursor of the platelet line.","The proerythroblast is the committed cell of the red cell line. Pick it for erythropoiesis.","The myeloblast is the committed cell of the granulocyte line, giving rise to neutrophils, eosinophils, and basophils."],"lt":"t-blood-hematopoiesis","lid":"c18","comp":83,"competencyId":"w4-hematopoiesis"},{"id":"L01399","dok":1,"q":"Which blast cell of the myeloid line develops into the granulocytes?","a":"The myeloblast.","options":["The lymphoblast","The monoblast","The myeloblast","The megakaryoblast"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The granulocytes are the neutrophils, eosinophils, and basophils, the leukocytes with visible cytoplasmic granules. All three develop from the myeloblast, a committed cell of the myeloid line. Monocytes, red cells, and platelets each have their own blast cell.","notes":["The lymphoblast comes from the lymphoid line and gives rise to lymphocytes. It is not part of the myeloid line.","The monoblast gives rise to monocytes, which are agranulocytes. Pick it for the cell that becomes a macrophage.","Correct. The myeloblast gives rise to all three granulocytes.","The megakaryoblast gives rise to megakaryocytes and then platelets. It is the platelet line."],"lt":"t-blood-hematopoiesis","lid":"c19","comp":83,"competencyId":"w4-hematopoiesis"},{"id":"L01400","dok":1,"q":"Which blast cell develops into monocytes?","a":"The monoblast.","options":["The proerythroblast","The lymphoblast","The myeloblast","The monoblast"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Monocytes develop from the monoblast, a committed cell of the myeloid line. After a monocyte leaves the blood and enters a tissue, it matures into a macrophage. The name helps: the monoblast is the blast that makes monocytes.","notes":["The proerythroblast produces red blood cells. Pick it for erythropoiesis.","The lymphoblast produces lymphocytes. Monocytes and lymphocytes are both agranulocytes, but they come from different lines.","The myeloblast produces the granulocytes: neutrophils, eosinophils, and basophils. Monocytes have their own blast cell.","Correct. The monoblast leads to monocytes."],"lt":"t-blood-hematopoiesis","lid":"c20","comp":83,"competencyId":"w4-hematopoiesis"},{"id":"L01401","dok":1,"q":"From which stem cell line do the lymphoblast and its products, the B cells, T cells, and natural killer cells, arise?","a":"The lymphoid line.","options":["The myeloid line","The lymphoid line","The erythroid line","The megakaryocyte line"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The lymphoid stem cell produces the lymphoblast, which develops into the lymphocytes: B cells, T cells, and natural killer cells. These are the only formed elements that come from the lymphoid line. Every other formed element comes from the myeloid line.","notes":["The myeloid line produces red cells, platelets, granulocytes, and monocytes, but not lymphocytes.","Correct. All lymphocytes come from the lymphoid stem cell.","The erythroid line is the red cell branch of the myeloid line. It produces erythrocytes.","The megakaryocyte line is the platelet branch of the myeloid line. It produces platelets."],"lt":"t-blood-hematopoiesis","lid":"c21","comp":83,"competencyId":"w4-hematopoiesis"},{"id":"L01402","dok":2,"q":"Which formed elements share the myeloid stem cell as a common ancestor?","a":"Erythrocytes, platelets, granulocytes, and monocytes.","options":["Erythrocytes and lymphocytes only","Erythrocytes, platelets, granulocytes, and monocytes","All five leukocytes, but not red cells or platelets","Lymphocytes only"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The myeloid stem cell gives rise to the red cell line, the platelet line, the granulocyte line, and the monocyte line. The only formed element it does not produce is the lymphocyte, which comes from the lymphoid line. So the myeloid family covers nearly everything in a blood smear except lymphocytes.","notes":["Erythrocytes are myeloid, but lymphocytes are lymphoid. This list also leaves out platelets, granulocytes, and monocytes.","Correct. Everything except lymphocytes comes from the myeloid line.","This includes lymphocytes, which are lymphoid, and leaves out red cells and platelets, which are myeloid.","Lymphocytes are the one group that does not come from the myeloid line. They have their own lymphoid stem cell."],"lt":"t-blood-hematopoiesis","lid":"c22","comp":83,"competencyId":"w4-hematopoiesis"},{"id":"L01403","dok":2,"q":"Which cell is more primitive, meaning higher in the blood cell family tree: the hemocytoblast or the myeloid stem cell?","a":"The hemocytoblast.","options":["The myeloid stem cell","Neither, since both are mature cells","The hemocytoblast","They are the same cell"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The hemocytoblast is the pluripotent stem cell at the top of the tree, able to give rise to every formed element. The myeloid stem cell is one of its two committed descendants and can no longer produce lymphocytes. Moving down the tree, each cell becomes more limited in what it can become.","notes":["The myeloid stem cell comes from the hemocytoblast and is already committed away from lymphocytes, so it is a step lower in the tree.","Both are stem cells, not mature cells. Mature cells such as erythrocytes and neutrophils sit at the bottom of the tree.","Correct. The hemocytoblast is the ancestor of the myeloid stem cell.","They are different cells. The hemocytoblast can make every formed element, while the myeloid stem cell cannot make lymphocytes."],"lt":"t-blood-hematopoiesis","lid":"c23","comp":83,"competencyId":"w4-hematopoiesis"},{"id":"L01404","dok":2,"q":"Which stage of erythropoiesis comes immediately before the reticulocyte?","a":"The normoblast.","options":["The erythrocyte","The proerythroblast","The normoblast","The hemocytoblast"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The order of the later red cell stages is erythroblast, normoblast, reticulocyte, erythrocyte. The normoblast ejects its nucleus, and the nucleus-free cell that results is the reticulocyte. So the normoblast comes immediately before the reticulocyte.","notes":["The erythrocyte is the mature cell that comes after the reticulocyte, not before it.","The proerythroblast is the first committed red cell precursor, several stages before the reticulocyte.","Correct. The normoblast ejects its nucleus and becomes a reticulocyte.","The hemocytoblast is the stem cell at the start of hematopoiesis, far earlier in the sequence."],"lt":"t-blood-hematopoiesis","lid":"c24","comp":83,"competencyId":"w4-hematopoiesis"},{"id":"L01405","dok":2,"q":"The red cell lineage and the granulocyte lineage both branch from which shared stem cell?","a":"The myeloid stem cell.","options":["The proerythroblast","The myeloid stem cell","The lymphoid stem cell","The megakaryoblast"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The myeloid stem cell is the common branch point for the red cell, granulocyte, monocyte, and platelet lineages. The red cell line passes through the proerythroblast and the granulocyte line through the myeloblast. Both trace back to the myeloid stem cell, which in turn comes from the hemocytoblast.","notes":["The proerythroblast is already committed to the red cell line, so it cannot give rise to granulocytes.","Correct. Both lineages branch from the myeloid stem cell.","The lymphoid stem cell gives rise only to lymphocytes. Neither red cells nor granulocytes come from it.","The megakaryoblast is a committed cell of the platelet line, downstream of the branch point. It produces only platelets."],"lt":"t-blood-hematopoiesis","lid":"c25","comp":83,"competencyId":"w4-hematopoiesis"},{"id":"L01406","dok":3,"q":"A bone marrow transplant is meant to restore a patient's ability to make every type of blood cell. Which cell must the donated marrow contain for this to work?","a":"The hemocytoblast.","options":["The reticulocyte","The proerythroblast","The myeloid stem cell","The hemocytoblast"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Only the hemocytoblast, the pluripotent hematopoietic stem cell, can give rise to every formed element. If donated marrow contains these stem cells, they settle in the patient's marrow and rebuild both the myeloid and lymphoid lines. A committed cell further down the tree could restore only one branch.","notes":["The reticulocyte is an almost mature red cell with no nucleus. It cannot divide at all, so it could not rebuild any line.","The proerythroblast is committed to the red cell line and could only make erythrocytes.","The myeloid stem cell could restore red cells, platelets, granulocytes, and monocytes, but not lymphocytes. It would leave the lymphoid line missing.","Correct. Only the pluripotent stem cell can rebuild every blood cell line."],"lt":"t-blood-hematopoiesis","lid":"c26","comp":83,"competencyId":"w4-hematopoiesis"},{"id":"L01407","dok":2,"q":"A red cell precursor has no nucleus but still contains ribosome remnants, and it is the stage just before maturity. Which stage is it?","a":"The reticulocyte.","options":["The reticulocyte","The erythroblast","The normoblast","The erythrocyte"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The reticulocyte is the stage after the normoblast has ejected its nucleus. It has no nucleus but still holds ribosome remnants, which can be stained to show a fine network. Within a day or two in the blood, these remnants disappear and the cell becomes a mature erythrocyte.","notes":["Correct. No nucleus plus ribosome remnants identifies the reticulocyte.","The erythroblast is an earlier stage that still has its nucleus while it builds hemoglobin.","The normoblast is the stage that ejects the nucleus, so it still has a nucleus. It is the stage just before the reticulocyte.","The mature erythrocyte has lost its ribosome remnants too. The clue about remaining ribosomes rules it out."],"lt":"t-blood-hematopoiesis","lid":"c27","comp":83,"competencyId":"w4-hematopoiesis"},{"id":"L01408","dok":2,"q":"A cell sits one step below the hemocytoblast in the blood cell family tree and can no longer become a lymphocyte. Which cell is it?","a":"The myeloid stem cell.","options":["The myeloid stem cell","The myeloblast","The lymphoid stem cell","The proerythroblast"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The hemocytoblast splits into two lines. The lymphoid stem cell makes lymphocytes, and the myeloid stem cell makes everything else. A cell one step below the hemocytoblast that cannot make lymphocytes must therefore be the myeloid stem cell.","notes":["Correct. It is one step down and cannot produce lymphocytes.","The myeloblast cannot make lymphocytes, but it is a committed granulocyte precursor further down the myeloid line.","The lymphoid stem cell is also one step below the hemocytoblast, but it is the cell that does make lymphocytes.","The proerythroblast cannot make lymphocytes, but it sits further down the tree, committed to the red cell line."],"lt":"t-blood-hematopoiesis","lid":"c28","comp":83,"competencyId":"w4-hematopoiesis"},{"id":"L01410","dok":1,"q":"Which pair correctly names the formation of white blood cells and the formation of platelets?","a":"Leukopoiesis for white cells and thrombopoiesis for platelets.","options":["Erythropoiesis for white cells, thrombopoiesis for platelets","Thrombopoiesis for white cells, leukopoiesis for platelets","Leukopoiesis for white cells, thrombopoiesis for platelets","Leukopoiesis for white cells, hemostasis for platelets"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Each branch of hematopoiesis is named for the cell it makes. Leuko means white, so leukopoiesis is the formation of leukocytes. Thrombo refers to the thrombocyte, another name for the platelet, so thrombopoiesis is platelet formation.","notes":["Erythropoiesis is red cell formation, since erythro means red. It does not describe white cells.","This swaps the two terms. Leuko means white, so leukopoiesis must be the white cell term.","Correct. Each name follows the cell being made.","Hemostasis is the stopping of bleeding, which platelets help with. It is not the name for forming platelets."],"lt":"t-blood-hematopoiesis","lid":"c30","comp":83,"competencyId":"w4-hematopoiesis"},{"id":"L01412","dok":1,"q":"Which large marrow cell has cytoplasm that breaks into fragments to form platelets?","a":"The megakaryocyte.","options":["The megakaryocyte","The monocyte","The reticulocyte","The megakaryoblast"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The megakaryocyte is a giant cell in red bone marrow, with a very large, many-lobed nucleus that holds many copies of its DNA. Its cytoplasm breaks off into thousands of small fragments, and each fragment is a platelet. This is why platelets are cell fragments rather than whole cells.","notes":["Correct. Platelets are fragments of megakaryocyte cytoplasm.","The monocyte is the largest leukocyte in the blood, which can lead to this mix-up. It becomes a macrophage, not platelets.","The reticulocyte is an immature red cell with ribosome remnants. It matures into an erythrocyte.","The megakaryoblast is the earlier committed cell that grows into the megakaryocyte. It does not fragment into platelets itself."],"lt":"t-blood-hematopoiesis","lid":"c32","comp":83,"competencyId":"w4-hematopoiesis"},{"id":"L01413","dok":2,"q":"A blood test shows a higher than normal reticulocyte count. What does this most directly reflect?","a":"Increased red cell production by the marrow.","options":["An increase in plasma volume","Suppressed activity of the marrow","Stepped-up red cell production","An increase in stored iron"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Reticulocytes are newly released red cells that mature within a day or two. Normally they are only 1 to 2 percent of red cells, so a higher count means the marrow is releasing new red cells faster than usual. This is what happens when the marrow responds to blood loss or low oxygen by stepping up erythropoiesis.","notes":["Plasma volume affects how concentrated the cells are, but the reticulocyte count reflects new red cell production, not fluid volume.","If the marrow were suppressed, fewer new red cells would be released, so the reticulocyte count would fall, not rise.","Correct. More reticulocytes means more new red cells are being released.","Stored iron is held by ferritin in tissues. A reticulocyte count does not measure iron stores."],"lt":"t-blood-hematopoiesis","lid":"c33","comp":83,"competencyId":"w4-hematopoiesis"},{"id":"L01415","dok":2,"q":"Which set correctly matches each blast cell to the formed element it produces?","a":"Myeloblast to granulocytes, monoblast to monocytes, megakaryoblast to platelets.","options":["Myeloblast to platelets, monoblast to granulocytes, megakaryoblast to monocytes","Myeloblast to granulocytes, monoblast to monocytes, megakaryoblast to platelets","Myeloblast to lymphocytes, monoblast to monocytes, megakaryoblast to platelets","Myeloblast to monocytes, monoblast to platelets, megakaryoblast to granulocytes"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Each myeloid line has its own committed blast cell. The myeloblast produces the granulocytes (neutrophils, eosinophils, and basophils), the monoblast produces monocytes, and the megakaryoblast produces the megakaryocyte that breaks into platelets. The names help: monoblast matches monocyte and megakaryoblast matches megakaryocyte.","notes":["Every pairing here is scrambled. The myeloblast makes granulocytes, and the megakaryoblast makes platelets through the megakaryocyte.","Correct. Each blast is matched to its own line.","The last two pairings are right, but the myeloblast makes granulocytes. Lymphocytes come from the lymphoblast of the lymphoid line.","Every pairing here is shifted. The monoblast makes monocytes, as its name suggests, and the megakaryoblast makes platelets."],"lt":"t-blood-hematopoiesis","lid":"c35","comp":83,"competencyId":"w4-hematopoiesis"},{"id":"L01416","dok":2,"q":"A student traces a neutrophil back through its ancestry. Which sequence of cells is correct?","a":"Hemocytoblast, myeloid stem cell, myeloblast, neutrophil.","options":["Hemocytoblast, lymphoid stem cell, myeloblast, neutrophil","Hemocytoblast, myeloid stem cell, myeloblast, neutrophil","Hemocytoblast, myeloid stem cell, proerythroblast, neutrophil","Hemocytoblast, myeloid stem cell, monoblast, neutrophil"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"A neutrophil is a granulocyte, so it comes from the granulocyte line of the myeloid branch. Its path runs from the hemocytoblast to the myeloid stem cell, then to the myeloblast, the committed granulocyte precursor, and finally to the mature neutrophil.","notes":["The lymphoid stem cell makes only lymphocytes. Granulocytes such as the neutrophil come from the myeloid stem cell.","Correct. Neutrophils come through the myeloid line and the myeloblast.","The proerythroblast is the committed red cell precursor. It makes erythrocytes, not neutrophils.","The monoblast is the committed precursor of monocytes. Neutrophils come from the myeloblast instead."],"lt":"t-blood-hematopoiesis","lid":"c36","comp":83,"competencyId":"w4-hematopoiesis"},{"id":"L01456","dok":1,"q":"The antigens that determine a person's ABO blood type are located where?","a":"On the outer surface of the erythrocyte plasma membrane.","options":["On the outer surface of the erythrocyte plasma membrane","Inside the erythrocyte, attached to hemoglobin","Inside the nucleus of the erythrocyte","Dissolved in the plasma around the cells"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"ABO blood type is named for the antigens a person carries on the outside of their red blood cells. An antigen is a molecule the immune system can recognize, and the A and B antigens are sugar chains sitting on the outer face of the erythrocyte plasma membrane. Because they face outward, antibodies in the plasma can find them, which is why a mismatched transfusion makes red cells clump.","notes":["Correct. The A and B antigens sit on the outside of the red cell membrane, where plasma antibodies can reach them.","Hemoglobin is the iron-containing protein inside the red cell that binds oxygen. It would be the answer for what carries oxygen in the erythrocyte, but blood type antigens are on the cell surface, not on hemoglobin.","A mature erythrocyte has no nucleus at all, since it ejects it to make room for hemoglobin. Students who choose this are picturing a typical cell rather than a red blood cell.","The plasma is where the ABO antibodies, anti-A and anti-B, are found, not the antigens. Pick the plasma if the question asked where the agglutinins of the ABO system circulate."],"lt":"t-blood-groups-disorders","lid":"c1"},{"id":"L01457","dok":1,"q":"What is another name for the A and B surface antigens of the ABO system?","a":"Agglutinogens.","options":["Gamma globulins","Fibrinogen","Agglutinins","Agglutinogens"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The A and B antigens on red cells are called agglutinogens because they are the molecules that trigger agglutination, the clumping of red cells when they meet a matching antibody. The ending -gen means producer, so an agglutinogen is the thing that produces clumping. The antibodies that bind them are the agglutinins.","notes":["Gamma globulins are the plasma protein group that makes up the antibodies of immune defense. They would be the answer for which class of plasma proteins includes antibodies, but antigens on a red cell are not antibodies.","Fibrinogen is the large clotting protein made by the liver that converts to fibrin threads. Pick it for a question about which plasma protein forms the framework of a clot.","Agglutinins are the ABO antibodies dissolved in the plasma, such as anti-A and anti-B. Pick agglutinins if the question asked for the other name of the plasma antibodies rather than the surface antigens.","Correct. Agglutinogens are the A and B antigens on the red cell surface that cause clumping when matched by an antibody."],"lt":"t-blood-groups-disorders","lid":"c2"},{"id":"L01458","dok":1,"q":"What is another name for the anti-A and anti-B antibodies found in the plasma?","a":"Agglutinins.","options":["Agglutinogens","Fibrinogen","Agglutinins","Albumin"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The ABO antibodies in the plasma are called agglutinins because they cause agglutination, the clumping of red cells that carry the matching antigen. Each antibody can bind antigens on more than one red cell at once, linking the cells together into clumps. A useful way to keep the pair straight is that the agglutinogen generates the reaction and sits on the cell, while the agglutinin floats in the plasma.","notes":["Agglutinogens are the A and B antigens on the surface of the red cells. Choose agglutinogens if the question asked about the surface antigens rather than the plasma antibodies.","Fibrinogen is the clotting protein of plasma, converted to fibrin threads at an injury. It would be correct for which plasma protein is removed when plasma becomes serum, not for an antibody.","Correct. Agglutinins are the ABO antibodies in the plasma that clump red cells carrying the matching antigen.","Albumin is the most abundant plasma protein, made by the liver, and it holds water in the blood and carries hormones and drugs. It would be the answer for the main protein that maintains osmotic pressure, not for an antibody."],"lt":"t-blood-groups-disorders","lid":"c3"},{"id":"L01459","dok":1,"q":"Which surface antigen or antigens does a person with type A blood carry on their red cells?","a":"The A antigen only.","options":["The A antigen only","Both the A and B antigens","The B antigen only","Neither the A nor the B antigen"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"An ABO blood type is named directly for the antigens present on the red cells. A person with type A blood has the A antigen on their erythrocytes and lacks the B antigen. The name of the type always tells you the antigen, and type O is the special case meaning neither antigen is present.","notes":["Correct. Type A red cells carry the A antigen and not the B antigen.","Carrying both antigens defines type AB blood, which is why that type gets both letters. Pick it for a type AB person.","Carrying the B antigen only defines type B blood. Pick this if the question asked about a person with type B.","Carrying neither antigen defines type O blood. Pick this if the question asked about a type O person."],"lt":"t-blood-groups-disorders","lid":"c4"},{"id":"L01460","dok":1,"q":"Which surface antigen or antigens does a person with type AB blood carry on their red cells?","a":"Both the A and B antigens.","options":["The A antigen only","Neither the A nor the B antigen","The B antigen only","Both the A and B antigens"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Type AB blood is named for having both antigens on the red cells, the A antigen and the B antigen together. Because both antigens are part of the person's own cells, the immune system treats both as self. That is also why a type AB person makes neither anti-A nor anti-B antibody.","notes":["The A antigen alone defines type A blood. Choose it if the question asked about a person with type A.","Having neither antigen defines type O blood. Choose it for a type O person, whose red cells carry no A or B antigen.","The B antigen alone defines type B blood. Choose it if the question asked about a person with type B.","Correct. Type AB red cells carry both the A and B antigens."],"lt":"t-blood-groups-disorders","lid":"c5"},{"id":"L01461","dok":1,"q":"Which surface antigen or antigens does a person with type O blood carry on their red cells?","a":"Neither the A nor the B antigen.","options":["Neither the A nor the B antigen","The B antigen only","The A antigen only","Both the A and B antigens"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Type O blood has neither the A antigen nor the B antigen on its red cells. Students often read the O as a letter like A and B, but it is better to think of it as a zero, meaning no ABO antigen at all. Because type O cells lack both antigens, they do not trigger anti-A or anti-B antibodies in a recipient.","notes":["Correct. Type O red cells carry neither the A nor the B antigen.","The B antigen alone is found on type B red cells. Pick this if the question asked about a type B person.","The A antigen alone is found on type A red cells. Pick this if the question asked about a type A person.","Both antigens together define type AB, the opposite of type O. Pick this for a person with type AB blood."],"lt":"t-blood-groups-disorders","lid":"c6"},{"id":"L01462","dok":1,"q":"Which ABO antibody is present in the plasma of a person with type A blood?","a":"Anti-B antibody only.","options":["Anti-B antibody only","Anti-A antibody only","Both anti-A and anti-B antibodies","Neither antibody"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"A person makes antibodies only against the ABO antigen they do not have. Type A red cells carry the A antigen, so the body treats A as self and makes anti-B antibody in the plasma. A simple rule is that the plasma antibody is always the opposite letter of the antigen on the cells.","notes":["Correct. Type A plasma carries anti-B, the antibody against the antigen this person lacks.","Anti-A antibody would attack the person's own type A red cells, so type A blood never carries it. Anti-A alone is the antibody found in type B plasma.","Both antibodies together are found in type O plasma, because type O cells carry neither antigen. Pick this for a type O person.","Having neither antibody describes type AB plasma, since both antigens are self for that person. Pick this for a type AB person."],"lt":"t-blood-groups-disorders","lid":"c7"},{"id":"L01463","dok":1,"q":"Which ABO antibody is present in the plasma of a person with type B blood?","a":"Anti-A antibody only.","options":["Anti-B antibody only","Both anti-A and anti-B antibodies","Neither antibody","Anti-A antibody only"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Type B red cells carry the B antigen, so the immune system accepts B as self and makes antibody only against the missing A antigen. The plasma of a type B person therefore holds anti-A antibody. If type A blood were transfused into this person, the anti-A would bind the donor cells and clump them.","notes":["Anti-B antibody would attack this person's own type B red cells, so it is never found in type B plasma. Anti-B alone is the antibody of type A plasma.","Both antibodies together are found in type O plasma, since type O cells carry no ABO antigen. Pick this for a type O person.","Having neither antibody describes type AB plasma, where both antigens are self. Pick this for a type AB person.","Correct. Type B plasma carries anti-A, the antibody against the antigen this person lacks."],"lt":"t-blood-groups-disorders","lid":"c8"},{"id":"L01464","dok":1,"q":"Which ABO antibodies are present in the plasma of a person with type O blood?","a":"Both anti-A and anti-B antibodies.","options":["Neither antibody","Both anti-A and anti-B","Anti-B only","Anti-A only"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Type O red cells carry neither the A nor the B antigen, so neither antigen is treated as self. The plasma of a type O person therefore holds both anti-A and anti-B antibodies. This is why a type O person can receive only type O red cells, since their antibodies would clump A, B, or AB cells.","notes":["Having neither antibody describes type AB plasma, the mirror image of type O. Pick this for a type AB person.","Correct. Type O plasma carries both anti-A and anti-B, because type O cells have neither antigen.","Anti-B alone is the plasma antibody of a type A person, whose cells carry the A antigen. Pick this for type A blood.","Anti-A alone is the plasma antibody of a type B person, whose cells carry the B antigen. Pick this for type B blood."],"lt":"t-blood-groups-disorders","lid":"c9"},{"id":"L01465","dok":1,"q":"Which ABO antibodies are present in the plasma of a person with type AB blood?","a":"Neither anti-A nor anti-B antibody.","options":["Both anti-A and anti-B antibodies","Neither anti-A nor anti-B antibody","Anti-B antibody only","Anti-A antibody only"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Type AB red cells carry both the A and B antigens, so the immune system treats both as self and makes neither antibody. With no anti-A or anti-B in the plasma, a type AB person will not clump donor red cells of any ABO type. Type AB is the mirror image of type O, which has neither antigen and both antibodies.","notes":["Both antibodies together are found in type O plasma, the opposite of type AB. Pick this for a type O person.","Correct. Type AB plasma holds neither antibody, since both antigens are self.","Anti-B alone is the plasma antibody of type A blood. A type AB person cannot make it, because B is one of their own antigens.","Anti-A alone is the plasma antibody of type B blood. Pick it for a type B person, not a type AB person, who carries the A antigen on their own cells."],"lt":"t-blood-groups-disorders","lid":"c10"},{"id":"L01466","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly lists the plasma antibodies for all four ABO blood types?","a":"Type A has anti-B, type B has anti-A, type AB has neither, and type O has both.","options":["Type A has anti-B, type B has anti-A, type AB has both, and type O has neither","Type A has anti-B, type B has anti-A, type AB has neither, and type O has both","Type A has anti-A, type B has anti-B, type AB has neither, and type O has both","Type A has anti-A, type B has anti-B, type AB has both, and type O has neither"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Each person makes antibodies only against the ABO antigens their own red cells lack. Type A lacks B, so it has anti-B; type B lacks A, so it has anti-A. Type AB lacks neither antigen and makes no antibody, while type O lacks both antigens and makes both antibodies.","notes":["Types A and B are right here, but types AB and O are swapped. Students who choose this have mixed up antigens and antibodies for the last two types, since AB carries both antigens and O carries neither.","Correct. The antibody is always against the missing antigen, so AB has none and O has both.","Types AB and O are right here, but types A and B are matched to their own antigen. A type A person making anti-A would destroy their own red cells, so this cannot be correct.","This list gives each type the antigens it carries rather than its antibodies. Read as antibodies, it would make every person attack their own red cells, so pick it only if the question asked which antigens each type carries."],"lt":"t-blood-groups-disorders","lid":"c11"},{"id":"L01469","dok":1,"q":"Whether a person is Rh positive or Rh negative depends on the presence or absence of which red cell antigen?","a":"The D antigen.","options":["The H antigen","The A antigen","The B antigen","The D antigen"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The Rh blood group is a separate system from ABO, and Rh status is set by one antigen, the D antigen, on the red cell surface. A person whose red cells carry the D antigen is Rh positive, and a person whose cells lack it is Rh negative. Blood type is usually written with both systems together, such as A positive or O negative.","notes":["The H antigen is the base sugar chain of the ABO system, the chain the A and B sugars are added onto. It would fit a question about type O cells, not about Rh status.","The A antigen belongs to the ABO system and decides whether a person is type A or AB. Pick it for a question about ABO type, not Rh status.","The B antigen belongs to the ABO system and decides whether a person is type B or AB. Pick it for a question about ABO type, not Rh.","Correct. The D antigen defines Rh status: present means Rh positive, absent means Rh negative."],"lt":"t-blood-groups-disorders","lid":"c14"},{"id":"L01470","dok":1,"q":"What does it mean for a person to be Rh positive?","a":"The D antigen is present on their red cells.","options":["The D antigen is absent from their red cells","Anti-D antibody is in their plasma from birth","Both the A and B antigens are on their red cells","The D antigen is present on their red cells"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Rh positive means the red cells carry the D antigen, the antigen that defines the Rh system. The positive sign simply reports that the antigen is present. Rh positive is the more common status, and a person who is Rh positive does not make anti-D antibody because the D antigen is part of their own cells.","notes":["Red cells without the D antigen are Rh negative. Pick this if the question asked what Rh negative means.","Anti-D antibody is never present from birth. It forms only in an Rh negative person after exposure to Rh positive blood, such as a mismatched transfusion or a pregnancy with an Rh positive baby.","Carrying both the A and B antigens defines type AB in the ABO system. Students sometimes connect the plus sign with having more antigens, but ABO type and Rh status are separate.","Correct. Rh positive means the D antigen is present on the red cell surface."],"lt":"t-blood-groups-disorders","lid":"c15"},{"id":"L01471","dok":1,"q":"In the United States, about what percentage of people are Rh positive?","a":"About 85 percent.","options":["About 50 percent","About 85 percent","About 15 percent","About 99 percent"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Most people carry the D antigen, so about 85 percent of people in the United States are Rh positive. That leaves about 15 percent who are Rh negative. Because Rh negative people are the minority, Rh negative blood is in shorter supply and matters most in transfusion and pregnancy.","notes":["An even split is a guess that assumes the D antigen is equally likely to be present or absent. In reality the D antigen is present in a large majority of people.","Correct. About 85 percent of people are Rh positive.","About 15 percent is the share of people who are Rh negative. Pick it if the question asked how many people lack the D antigen.","Nearly everyone is not Rh positive. Roughly one person in seven lacks the D antigen, which is why Rh negative status is common enough to check before every transfusion."],"lt":"t-blood-groups-disorders","lid":"c16"},{"id":"L01472","dok":2,"q":"How do Rh antibodies differ from ABO antibodies in when they appear?","a":"ABO antibodies form naturally in infancy, while Rh antibodies form only after exposure to Rh positive blood.","options":["ABO antibodies form naturally in infancy, but Rh antibodies need prior exposure","Both appear in the plasma before birth","Both form only after a person receives a mismatched transfusion","Rh antibodies form naturally in infancy, while ABO antibodies form only after a transfusion"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Anti-A and anti-B antibodies appear on their own during the first months of life, without any transfusion. Anti-D antibody is different. An Rh negative person makes it only after being sensitized, meaning after Rh positive red cells enter their blood through a transfusion or a pregnancy. This is why a first mismatched Rh exposure usually causes no reaction, while a second one can.","notes":["Correct. ABO antibodies are naturally occurring, while Rh antibodies require prior sensitization.","Neither type of antibody is a normal part of plasma before birth. ABO antibodies appear in the first months after birth, and Rh antibodies appear only after sensitization.","This fits Rh antibodies but not ABO antibodies. A type O person has anti-A and anti-B even if they have never had a transfusion.","This reverses the two systems. Pick the reverse statement, in which ABO antibodies are natural and Rh antibodies need exposure, because that is the correct pattern."],"lt":"t-blood-groups-disorders","lid":"c17"},{"id":"L01473","dok":2,"q":"A lab report shows that a donor's red cells carry the A antigen and that the donor's plasma contains anti-B antibody. What is the donor's ABO type?","a":"Type A.","options":["Type AB","Type B","Type O","Type A"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The antigen on the red cells names the blood type, and the antibody in the plasma is against the missing antigen. Red cells with the A antigen and plasma with anti-B fit only type A. Reading both pieces of evidence together is a good habit, because they should always agree with each other.","notes":["Type AB cells would carry both antigens, and the plasma would have no anti-A or anti-B. Pick type AB if both antigens appeared with no antibody.","Type B cells would carry the B antigen and the plasma would hold anti-A. Pick type B if the report showed the B antigen with anti-A antibody.","Type O cells would carry no ABO antigen, and the plasma would hold both antibodies. Pick type O if the report showed no antigen with both antibodies.","Correct. The A antigen with anti-B antibody is the pattern of type A blood."],"lt":"t-blood-groups-disorders","lid":"c18"},{"id":"L01474","dok":2,"q":"A lab report shows no A or B antigen on a patient's red cells and both anti-A and anti-B antibodies in the plasma. What is the patient's ABO type?","a":"Type O.","options":["Type B","Type AB","Type O","Type A"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Red cells with no ABO antigen belong to type O. Because neither antigen is part of the patient's own cells, the plasma makes antibodies against both, so anti-A and anti-B are both present. The two findings confirm each other and point clearly to type O.","notes":["Type B would show the B antigen on the cells and anti-A alone in the plasma. Pick type B if the report showed that pattern.","Type AB is the mirror image of this report, with both antigens on the cells and no antibody in the plasma. Pick type AB for that pattern.","Correct. No antigens and both antibodies is the pattern of type O blood.","Type A would show the A antigen on the cells and anti-B alone in the plasma. Pick type A if the report showed that pattern."],"lt":"t-blood-groups-disorders","lid":"c19"},{"id":"L01475","dok":2,"q":"A lab report shows both the A and B antigens on a patient's red cells and no anti-A or anti-B antibody in the plasma. What is the patient's ABO type?","a":"Type AB.","options":["Type A","Type O","Type B","Type AB"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Red cells that carry both the A and B antigens are type AB. Since both antigens are self for this patient, the immune system makes neither antibody, so the plasma has no anti-A or anti-B. The absence of both antibodies is what lets a type AB patient receive red cells of any ABO type.","notes":["Type A would show only the A antigen and anti-B antibody in the plasma. Pick type A if the B antigen were missing and anti-B were present.","Type O is the opposite pattern, with no antigens on the cells and both antibodies in the plasma. Pick type O for that report.","Type B would show only the B antigen and anti-A antibody in the plasma. Pick type B if the A antigen were missing and anti-A were present.","Correct. Both antigens and no antibodies is the pattern of type AB blood."],"lt":"t-blood-groups-disorders","lid":"c20"},{"id":"L01476","dok":2,"q":"A lab report shows the B antigen on a patient's red cells and anti-A antibody in the plasma. What is the patient's ABO type?","a":"Type B.","options":["Type O","Type AB","Type B","Type A"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The B antigen on the red cells names this as type B blood. Since the A antigen is missing, the plasma makes anti-A antibody, which matches the report. Whenever one antigen is present, expect the plasma to carry the antibody of the opposite letter.","notes":["Type O would show no antigen on the cells and both antibodies in the plasma. Pick type O if neither antigen were present.","Type AB would show both antigens on the cells and no antibody in the plasma. Pick type AB if the A antigen were also present and anti-A were absent.","Correct. The B antigen with anti-A antibody is the pattern of type B blood.","Type A is the mirror of this report, with the A antigen on the cells and anti-B in the plasma. Pick type A for that pattern."],"lt":"t-blood-groups-disorders","lid":"c21"},{"id":"L01477","dok":1,"q":"Which description fits anemia?","a":"The blood carries less oxygen because there are too few red cells or too little hemoglobin.","options":["The blood clots too slowly because a clotting factor is missing","The blood carries less oxygen because there are too few red cells or too little hemoglobin","The blood has too few platelets, so the risk of bleeding rises","The blood has too many red cells, so it thickens and flows slowly"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Anemia is a problem of the erythrocytes. Either there are too few red cells, or the red cells hold too little hemoglobin, and in both cases the blood carries less oxygen to the tissues. Because the cause can be a low count or low hemoglobin, anemia is described as a reduced oxygen-carrying capacity rather than simply a low red cell number.","notes":["A missing clotting factor that slows clotting is hemophilia, a disorder of the plasma clotting proteins. Pick it for hemophilia.","Correct. Anemia is a reduced oxygen-carrying capacity from too few red cells or too little hemoglobin.","A low platelet count that raises bleeding risk is thrombocytopenia. Pick it if the question asked about a platelet disorder.","An excess of red cells that thickens the blood is polycythemia. Pick this description if the question asked about polycythemia."],"lt":"t-blood-groups-disorders","lid":"c22","comp":84,"competencyId":"w4-blood-disorders"},{"id":"L01484","dok":1,"q":"In sickle cell disease, which part of the hemoglobin molecule carries the inherited change?","a":"The beta-globin chain.","options":["The beta-globin chain","The heme group","The alpha-globin chain","The red cell plasma membrane"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Hemoglobin is built from four protein chains, two alpha-globin and two beta-globin, each holding a heme group with one iron. Sickle cell disease is caused by a change in the gene for the beta-globin chain. The altered beta chains make the hemoglobin stick together when oxygen is low, which bends the red cell into a sickle shape.","notes":["Correct. Sickle cell disease comes from a change in the beta-globin chain.","The heme group is the iron-holding ring that binds oxygen within each subunit. Pick heme for a question about where oxygen attaches, not for the sickle cell change.","The alpha-globin chain is the other type of chain in adult hemoglobin. Pick it if the question asked which chain is underproduced in alpha-thalassemia.","The plasma membrane is the outer surface of the red cell, where the ABO antigens sit. The sickle shape changes the membrane's outline, but the inherited defect is in hemoglobin inside the cell."],"lt":"t-blood-groups-disorders","lid":"c29","comp":84,"competencyId":"w4-blood-disorders"},{"id":"L01485","dok":1,"q":"What is the abnormal hemoglobin found in sickle cell disease called?","a":"Hemoglobin S.","options":["Hemoglobin A","Hemoglobin S","Hemoglobin F","Methemoglobin"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The abnormal hemoglobin of sickle cell disease is called hemoglobin S, with the S standing for sickle. It differs from normal adult hemoglobin by a change in its beta-globin chains. When oxygen levels drop, hemoglobin S molecules link into long stiff strands that bend the red cell into a crescent.","notes":["Hemoglobin A is the normal adult hemoglobin. Pick it if the question asked which hemoglobin most adult red cells carry.","Correct. Hemoglobin S is the sickle hemoglobin.","Hemoglobin F is fetal hemoglobin, the form made before birth that holds oxygen more tightly. Pick it for a question about the hemoglobin of a fetus.","Methemoglobin is hemoglobin whose iron has been oxidized so it can no longer carry oxygen well. It is a different problem from the inherited sickle cell hemoglobin."],"lt":"t-blood-groups-disorders","lid":"c30","comp":84,"competencyId":"w4-blood-disorders"},{"id":"L01487","dok":1,"q":"What structural change to the erythrocytes gives sickle cell disease its name?","a":"The red cells become stiff, curved, and sickle-shaped.","options":["The red cells enlarge but keep their disc shape","The red cells shrink but keep their disc shape","The red cells swell into rigid spheres","The red cells become stiff, curved, and sickle-shaped"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"In sickle cell disease, the abnormal hemoglobin links into long stiff strands when oxygen is low, and these strands bend the red cell into a curved crescent like a farm sickle. A normal red cell is a flexible biconcave disc that folds to squeeze through capillaries. The rigid sickled cell cannot fold, so it jams small vessels and causes pain and tissue damage downstream.","notes":["Large red cells that keep a normal shape are the picture of a macrocytic anemia, such as vitamin B12 deficiency. Pick this for macrocytic anemia.","Small red cells that keep a normal shape are the picture of a microcytic anemia, such as iron deficiency. Pick this for microcytic anemia.","Rigid round red cells are spherocytes, seen in a different inherited disorder called hereditary spherocytosis. Pick this for a question about spherical red cells, not sickled ones.","Correct. The sickled cell is stiff and crescent-shaped, which is where the disease gets its name."],"lt":"t-blood-groups-disorders","lid":"c32","comp":84,"competencyId":"w4-blood-disorders"},{"id":"L01488","dok":1,"q":"A normal erythrocyte lasts about 120 days. About how long does a sickled erythrocyte survive in the circulation?","a":"About 10 to 20 days.","options":["About 1 year","About 120 days","About 10 to 20 days","About 60 days"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Sickled red cells are stiff and easily damaged, so the spleen and liver remove them quickly. They survive only about 10 to 20 days instead of the normal 120. Because the bone marrow cannot replace cells that fast, people with sickle cell disease are chronically anemic.","notes":["No red cell lasts a year. Students may be thinking of long-lived memory lymphocytes, which can persist for years, but erythrocytes are worn out within months even when healthy.","About 120 days is the lifespan of a normal, healthy red cell. Pick it if the question asked how long an ordinary erythrocyte lasts.","Correct. Sickled cells last only about 10 to 20 days, far shorter than normal red cells.","Sixty days is a guess at a moderate shortening. The real loss is much greater, because sickled cells are removed within a few weeks at most."],"lt":"t-blood-groups-disorders","lid":"c33","comp":84,"competencyId":"w4-blood-disorders"},{"id":"L01492","dok":3,"q":"A child has painful episodes in which small blood vessels in the hands and feet become blocked. A blood smear shows many stiff, crescent-shaped red cells. Which disorder fits best?","a":"Sickle cell disease.","options":["Polycythemia","Iron-deficiency anemia","Beta-thalassemia","Sickle cell disease"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Crescent-shaped red cells are the signature of sickle cell disease. The abnormal hemoglobin S makes the cells rigid and curved, so they cannot fold through narrow capillaries and instead jam them. Blocked small vessels starve the tissue downstream of oxygen, which causes the painful episodes.","notes":["Polycythemia is an excess of red cells that thickens the blood and slows flow, which can also block vessels. However, the cells keep their normal disc shape, so pick polycythemia if the smear showed too many normal-looking cells.","Iron-deficiency anemia gives small, pale red cells and fatigue, not crescent-shaped cells blocking vessels. Pick it for a microcytic anemia from low iron.","Beta-thalassemia is reduced production of the beta chain, which gives small, pale red cells rather than crescents. Pick it if the smear showed small cells and testing showed too little beta-globin.","Correct. Rigid crescent-shaped red cells that block small vessels are the hallmark of sickle cell disease."],"lt":"t-blood-groups-disorders","lid":"c37","comp":84,"competencyId":"w4-blood-disorders"},{"id":"L01495","dok":3,"q":"In a lab test, a person's plasma clumps red cells that carry the B antigen but leaves red cells that carry only the A antigen alone. What is this person's ABO type, and which antibody explains the result?","a":"Type A, because type A plasma carries anti-B antibody.","options":["Type O, because the plasma carries both antibodies","Type AB, because the plasma carries no antibody","Type A, because the plasma carries anti-B","Type B, because the plasma carries anti-A"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Clumping of B cells means the plasma holds anti-B, and leaving A cells alone means it lacks anti-A. Plasma with anti-B and no anti-A belongs to a type A person. A type A person cannot make anti-A, because the A antigen is on their own red cells.","notes":["Type O plasma carries both antibodies, so it would clump the A cells as well as the B cells. Pick type O if both kinds of cells clumped.","Type AB plasma has no ABO antibody, so it would clump neither the A cells nor the B cells. Pick type AB if nothing clumped.","Correct. Anti-B without anti-A is the plasma of a type A person.","Type B plasma carries anti-A, which would clump the A cells and leave the B cells alone. Pick type B if the result were the reverse of this one."],"lt":"t-blood-groups-disorders","lid":"c40"},{"id":"L02024","dok":1,"q":"What type of tissue is blood?","a":"A fluid connective tissue.","options":["A nervous tissue","A muscle tissue","A fluid connective tissue","An epithelial tissue"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Blood is a connective tissue, the only one that is liquid. Like every connective tissue, it is made of cells suspended in an extracellular matrix, and in blood that matrix is a fluid called plasma. The cells and cell fragments in it are called the formed elements.","notes":["Nervous tissue is made of neurons and neuroglia that carry and support electrical signals. It would be the answer for a question about the tissue of the brain and nerves.","Muscle tissue is made of cells specialized to contract, such as skeletal, cardiac, and smooth muscle. Choose muscle tissue for a question about the tunica media of an artery.","Correct. Blood is a fluid connective tissue, with plasma as its matrix.","Epithelial tissue covers surfaces and lines cavities, with cells packed tightly together on a basement membrane and very little matrix. Pick epithelium for a question about the lining of a blood vessel, the endothelium."],"lt":"t-blood","lid":"c1","comp":81,"competencyId":"w4-blood-composition"},{"id":"L02025","dok":1,"q":"What are the cells and cell fragments suspended in plasma called?","a":"The formed elements.","options":["The formed elements","The buffy coat","The plasma proteins","The solutes"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The formed elements are the cellular part of blood: erythrocytes, leukocytes, and platelets. Platelets are cell fragments rather than whole cells, which is why the term says cells and cell fragments. Together the formed elements make up about 45 percent of blood volume, and plasma makes up the other 55 percent.","notes":["Correct. The cells and cell fragments of blood are the formed elements.","The buffy coat is the thin pale layer of leukocytes and platelets in a spun sample, which is only part of the formed elements. Choose it for a question about the middle layer of a centrifuged tube.","Plasma proteins such as albumin, globulins, and fibrinogen are dissolved molecules in the plasma, not cells. Pick them for a question about the most abundant solutes in plasma.","Solutes are the substances dissolved in plasma, such as nutrients, wastes, electrolytes, and proteins. They would be the answer for what makes up the roughly 8 percent of plasma that is not water."],"lt":"t-blood","lid":"c3","comp":81,"competencyId":"w4-blood-composition"},{"id":"L02026","dok":1,"q":"What does the hematocrit measure?","a":"The percentage of blood volume made up of erythrocytes.","options":["The amount of hemoglobin in each cell","The number of leukocytes in a sample","The percentage of blood volume made up of erythrocytes","The percentage of blood volume made up of plasma"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The hematocrit is the percentage of whole blood volume that is erythrocytes (red blood cells). It is measured by spinning a blood sample so the heavy red cells pack at the bottom of the tube, then comparing that red layer with the total height. A low hematocrit can point to anemia, and a high one to polycythemia.","notes":["The hemoglobin content of red cells is a separate measurement in a blood count, not the hematocrit. Choose this for a question about how much oxygen-carrying protein is in the blood.","Counting leukocytes is the white blood cell count, part of a complete blood count. Pick this for a question about testing for infection or leukemia.","Correct. The hematocrit is the percentage of blood volume made up of erythrocytes.","The plasma makes up the top layer of a spun sample, about 55 percent of blood. Students who choose this are reading the wrong layer of the tube; the hematocrit measures the packed red cells at the bottom."],"lt":"t-blood","lid":"c4","comp":81,"competencyId":"w4-blood-composition"},{"id":"L02027","dok":1,"q":"What is the most abundant plasma protein?","a":"Albumin.","options":["Hemoglobin","Fibrinogen","Globulins","Albumin"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Albumin makes up about 60 percent of the plasma proteins, more than any other. It is made by the liver and helps hold water in the blood by maintaining osmotic pressure, the pull that keeps fluid from leaking out of the capillaries. It also carries hormones, fatty acids, and drugs.","notes":["Hemoglobin is inside the red blood cells, not dissolved in the plasma. It would be the answer for the protein that binds oxygen inside erythrocytes.","Fibrinogen is only about 4 percent of the plasma proteins, though it is the largest. Pick it for a question about the protein that forms fibrin threads in a clot.","Globulins are about 35 percent of the plasma proteins, second to albumin. Choose them for a question about the plasma proteins that include the antibodies (gamma globulins).","Correct. Albumin is the most abundant plasma protein."],"lt":"t-blood","lid":"c5","comp":81,"competencyId":"w4-blood-composition"},{"id":"L02028","dok":1,"q":"Which plasma protein converts into fibrin threads that form the framework of a clot?","a":"Fibrinogen.","options":["Myoglobin","Globulins","Fibrinogen","Albumin"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Fibrinogen is the clotting protein of plasma, made by the liver. When a vessel is damaged, fibrinogen is converted into long fibrin threads that form a mesh, trapping blood cells to build the clot. Serum is what is left when fibrinogen and the other clotting proteins have been removed.","notes":["Myoglobin is an oxygen-storing protein inside muscle fibers, not a plasma protein. It would be the answer for a question about what stores oxygen in skeletal muscle.","Globulins include transport proteins and the antibodies of immune defense. Choose them for a question about which plasma proteins are antibodies.","Correct. Fibrinogen is converted to fibrin threads in a clot.","Albumin is the most abundant plasma protein and holds water in the blood, but it does not form clots. Pick it for a question about plasma osmotic pressure."],"lt":"t-blood","lid":"c6","comp":81,"competencyId":"w4-blood-composition"},{"id":"L02031","dok":1,"q":"Which protein fills the erythrocyte and binds oxygen for transport?","a":"Hemoglobin.","options":["Fibrinogen","Albumin","Myoglobin","Hemoglobin"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Hemoglobin is the red, iron-containing protein that fills each erythrocyte. It has four subunits, and each one holds a heme group with an iron atom that binds one oxygen molecule. Nearly everything about the red cell's structure, from its missing nucleus to its shape, serves to pack in and use this protein.","notes":["Fibrinogen is a plasma protein that forms fibrin threads in a clot. Choose it for a question about clotting.","Albumin is a plasma protein dissolved outside the cells that holds water in the blood. Pick it for a question about the most abundant plasma protein.","Myoglobin is an oxygen-storing protein found inside muscle fibers, not red blood cells. It would be the answer for what stores oxygen in skeletal and cardiac muscle.","Correct. Hemoglobin fills the erythrocyte and carries oxygen."],"lt":"t-blood","lid":"c9","comp":82,"competencyId":"w4-formed-elements"},{"id":"L02037","dok":1,"q":"What is the formation of the blood's formed elements called?","a":"Hematopoiesis.","options":["Hematopoiesis","Hemolysis","Hematocrit","Hemostasis"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Hematopoiesis is the formation of blood cells, from hemato, meaning blood, and poiesis, meaning making. In adults it happens in red bone marrow, where hemocytoblasts give rise to every erythrocyte, leukocyte, and platelet. Several look-alike terms start with hem-, so the second half of the word is what tells them apart.","notes":["Correct. Hematopoiesis is the formation of blood cells.","Hemolysis means the rupture of red blood cells, as in a mismatched transfusion. Choose hemolysis for a question about red cells bursting.","The hematocrit is the percentage of blood volume made up of erythrocytes. It would be the answer for the measurement taken from a spun blood sample.","Hemostasis means stopping bleeding, through vessel spasm, a platelet plug, and clotting. Pick hemostasis for a question about how the body stops blood loss."],"lt":"t-blood","lid":"c15","comp":83,"competencyId":"w4-hematopoiesis"},{"id":"L02039","dok":1,"q":"In a centrifuged blood sample, what is the thin pale layer of leukocytes and platelets between the plasma and the erythrocytes called?","a":"The buffy coat.","options":["The plasma","The serum","The buffy coat","The colloid"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"When blood is spun, it separates into three layers by weight. Heavy erythrocytes pack at the bottom, plasma rises to the top, and a thin pale layer of leukocytes and platelets forms between them, called the buffy coat. It is thin because white cells and platelets make up only a tiny fraction of blood volume.","notes":["Plasma is the straw-colored top layer of a spun sample, about 55 percent of blood volume. It would be the answer for the liquid matrix of blood.","Serum is the fluid left after blood has clotted, which is plasma without its clotting proteins. Pick serum for a question about the fluid above a clotted sample.","Correct. The buffy coat is the pale middle layer of leukocytes and platelets.","Colloid is the protein-rich material stored in the follicles of the thyroid gland, not a blood layer. Choose colloid for a question about thyroid histology."],"lt":"t-blood","lid":"c17","comp":83,"competencyId":"w4-hematopoiesis"},{"id":"L02040","dok":1,"q":"Where does hematopoiesis take place in adults?","a":"In the red bone marrow.","options":["In the yellow bone marrow","In the spleen","In the red bone marrow","In the liver"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"In adults, all blood cells are formed in the red bone marrow, found in the spongy bone of the axial skeleton and the proximal ends of the limb bones. Hemocytoblasts there give rise to every formed element. Other organs such as the liver and spleen help make blood cells before birth, but not in a healthy adult.","notes":["Yellow bone marrow is mostly fat and is not an active blood-forming tissue in adults. It would be the answer for what fills the medullary cavity of an adult long bone.","The spleen makes red blood cells in the fetus, and in adults it removes old ones instead. Choose the spleen for a question about filtering blood and storing platelets.","Correct. Adult hematopoiesis happens in red bone marrow.","The liver is an important site of blood cell formation in the fetus, but not in a healthy adult. Pick the liver for a question about where most plasma proteins are made."],"lt":"t-blood","lid":"c18","comp":83,"competencyId":"w4-hematopoiesis"},{"id":"L02041","dok":1,"q":"From which single cell type do all the formed elements arise?","a":"The hemocytoblast.","options":["The reticulocyte","The hemocytoblast","The megakaryocyte","The monocyte"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The hemocytoblast is the pluripotent hematopoietic stem cell of the red bone marrow. Pluripotent means it can become many different cell types, and its descendants split into the myeloid line and the lymphoid line. Every erythrocyte, leukocyte, and platelet traces back to it.","notes":["The reticulocyte is an immature red blood cell, one step before a mature erythrocyte. Choose it for a question about the cell with ribosome remnants that makes up 1 to 2 percent of circulating red cells.","Correct. All formed elements arise from the hemocytoblast.","The megakaryocyte is a giant marrow cell that produces only platelets. Pick it for a question about where platelets come from.","The monocyte is a leukocyte that becomes a macrophage, far down one branch of the family tree. It would be the answer for the precursor of macrophages."],"lt":"t-blood","lid":"c19","comp":83,"competencyId":"w4-hematopoiesis"},{"id":"L02043","dok":1,"q":"Which giant bone marrow cell breaks apart to form thousands of platelets?","a":"The megakaryocyte.","options":["The monocyte","The megakaryocyte","The hemocytoblast","The reticulocyte"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"A megakaryocyte is a giant cell in the red bone marrow, from mega, meaning large, and karyo, meaning nucleus. Its cytoplasm breaks into thousands of small membrane-bound fragments, and each fragment is a platelet. That is why platelets have no nucleus: the nucleus stays behind in the megakaryocyte.","notes":["The monocyte is the largest circulating leukocyte and becomes a macrophage. Choose it for a question about the leukocyte with a kidney-shaped nucleus.","Correct. The megakaryocyte breaks apart into platelets.","The hemocytoblast is the stem cell that gives rise to all formed elements, including the megakaryocyte line. Pick it for a question about the single cell all blood cells come from.","The reticulocyte is an immature red blood cell, not a platelet producer. It would be the answer for the stage right before a mature erythrocyte."],"lt":"t-blood","lid":"c21","comp":83,"competencyId":"w4-hematopoiesis"},{"id":"L02044","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly matches the two hematopoietic lines with what they produce?","a":"The lymphoid line makes lymphocytes, and the myeloid line makes all other formed elements.","options":["The myeloid line makes only platelets; the lymphoid line makes red cells","The lymphoid line makes all leukocytes; the myeloid line makes red cells","The myeloid line makes lymphocytes; the lymphoid line makes the rest","The lymphoid line makes lymphocytes; the myeloid line makes the rest"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Hemocytoblasts split into two lines. The lymphoid line gives rise to the lymphocytes, which is easy to remember because the names match. The myeloid line gives rise to everything else: erythrocytes, platelets, and every leukocyte except lymphocytes, meaning neutrophils, eosinophils, basophils, and monocytes.","notes":["The myeloid line makes platelets, but also red cells and most leukocytes, and the lymphoid line never makes red cells. This choice shrinks the myeloid line to a single product.","The lymphoid line makes only lymphocytes, not all leukocytes. Neutrophils, eosinophils, basophils, and monocytes come from the myeloid line along with the red cells.","This reverses the two lines. Use the shared root to keep them straight: the lymphoid line makes lymphocytes.","Correct. The lymphoid line makes lymphocytes, and the myeloid line makes everything else."],"lt":"t-blood","lid":"c22","comp":83,"competencyId":"w4-hematopoiesis"},{"id":"L02047","dok":1,"q":"After a blood sample has clotted, the fluid left behind has no clotting proteins. What is this fluid called?","a":"Serum.","options":["Plasma","Buffy coat","Lymph","Serum"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Serum is plasma with the clotting proteins removed. When blood clots, fibrinogen is used up to form fibrin threads that trap the cells, and the fluid that remains is serum. Plasma, by contrast, still contains fibrinogen and the other clotting proteins.","notes":["Plasma is the liquid part of unclotted blood, and it still contains fibrinogen. Pick plasma for a question about the top layer of a spun sample that was kept from clotting.","The buffy coat is the thin layer of leukocytes and platelets in a spun sample, not a cell-free fluid. Choose it for a question about the middle layer of a centrifuged tube.","Lymph is interstitial fluid that has entered a lymphatic vessel, not a product of clotted blood. It would be the answer for the fluid carried in lymphatic capillaries.","Correct. Serum is the fluid left after clotting, with no clotting proteins."],"lt":"t-blood","lid":"c25","comp":81,"competencyId":"w4-blood-composition"},{"id":"L02048","dok":1,"q":"A patient's blood has too few erythrocytes and too little hemoglobin, so it carries less oxygen. What is this disorder?","a":"Anemia.","options":["Hemophilia","Leukemia","Polycythemia","Anemia"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Anemia is a shortage of erythrocytes or hemoglobin, so the blood carries less oxygen than the tissues need. It shows up as a low hematocrit or low hemoglobin on a blood count. The word means without blood, which reflects the reduced oxygen-carrying capacity rather than a lack of blood volume.","notes":["Hemophilia is an inherited deficiency of a clotting factor, so the blood clots too slowly. It would be the answer for a question about prolonged bleeding with a normal platelet count.","Leukemia is a cancer of white blood cell formation that crowds the marrow with abnormal leukocytes. Choose it for a question about abnormal white cells filling the marrow.","Polycythemia is the opposite problem, an excess of erythrocytes that thickens the blood and slows its flow. Pick it for a question about a high hematocrit.","Correct. Too few red cells or too little hemoglobin is anemia."],"lt":"t-blood","lid":"c26","comp":84,"competencyId":"w4-blood-disorders"},{"id":"L02049","dok":1,"q":"An inherited abnormal hemoglobin distorts erythrocytes into a rigid curved shape that jams capillaries. Which disorder is this?","a":"Sickle cell disease.","options":["Polycythemia","Thrombocytopenia","Leukopenia","Sickle cell disease"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"In sickle cell disease, a single abnormal hemoglobin makes red cells rigid and curved like a sickle. A normal red cell is a flexible biconcave disc that folds to pass through narrow capillaries, but a sickled cell cannot fold, so it blocks the capillary instead. This causes pain and tissue damage downstream.","notes":["Polycythemia is an excess of erythrocytes that thickens the blood, but the cells keep their normal shape. It would be the answer for a question about a high red cell count.","Thrombocytopenia is an abnormally low platelet count, which raises the risk of bleeding. Pick it for a question about easy bruising from too few platelets.","Leukopenia is an abnormally low white blood cell count, which weakens defense against infection. Choose it for a question about frequent infections from too few leukocytes.","Correct. Rigid curved red cells from abnormal hemoglobin indicate sickle cell disease."],"lt":"t-blood","lid":"c27","comp":84,"competencyId":"w4-blood-disorders"},{"id":"L02050","dok":1,"q":"A patient has a cancer of white blood cell formation that crowds the bone marrow with abnormal leukocytes. Which disorder is this?","a":"Leukemia.","options":["Polycythemia","Hemophilia","Anemia","Leukemia"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Leukemia is a cancer of white blood cell formation in the red bone marrow. The abnormal leukocytes multiply and crowd out the normal blood-forming cells, so the patient can also develop anemia and bleeding problems. In leukemia the spleen may swell with abnormal white cells as well.","notes":["Polycythemia is an excess of red cells that thickens the blood. It would be the answer for a question about a high hematocrit.","Hemophilia is an inherited clotting factor deficiency, not a cancer. Choose it for a question about blood that clots too slowly.","Anemia is a shortage of red cells or hemoglobin. It can follow leukemia when the marrow is crowded, but it is not the cancer itself, so pick anemia for a question about reduced oxygen-carrying capacity.","Correct. A cancer of white blood cell formation is leukemia."],"lt":"t-blood","lid":"c28","comp":84,"competencyId":"w4-blood-disorders"},{"id":"L02051","dok":1,"q":"A patient has an inherited deficiency of one clotting factor, so the blood clots too slowly even though the platelet count is normal. Which disorder is this?","a":"Hemophilia.","options":["Thrombocytopenia","Anemia","Hemophilia","Leukemia"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Hemophilia is an inherited deficiency of a clotting factor, one of the plasma proteins needed to build a clot. Platelets can still form a plug, but without the missing factor the fibrin mesh forms too slowly, so bleeding lasts much longer than normal. It is a disorder of the clotting proteins, not of any formed element.","notes":["Thrombocytopenia also causes bleeding, but because of a low platelet count. This patient's platelet count is normal, so pick thrombocytopenia for a question about bleeding from too few platelets.","Anemia is a shortage of red cells or hemoglobin that reduces oxygen delivery. It would be the answer for fatigue from low oxygen-carrying capacity.","Correct. An inherited clotting factor deficiency is hemophilia.","Leukemia is a cancer of white blood cell formation. Choose it for a question about abnormal leukocytes crowding the marrow."],"lt":"t-blood","lid":"c29","comp":84,"competencyId":"w4-blood-disorders"},{"id":"L02052","dok":2,"q":"A patient's blood count is abnormal, and the clinician needs to examine the blood-forming tissue itself. From which sites is a marrow sample typically drawn?","a":"The iliac crest or the sternum.","options":["The phalanges or the metacarpals","The patella or the calcaneus","The femoral shaft or the radial shaft","The iliac crest or the sternum"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Red bone marrow in adults is found in the spongy bone of the axial skeleton and the limb girdles. The iliac crest of the hip bone and the sternum are rich in red marrow and sit close to the skin, so a needle can reach them easily. That is why marrow samples are drawn from these two sites.","notes":["The phalanges and metacarpals are small bones of the hand that are not used for marrow sampling. They would be the answer for a question about the bones of the fingers and palm.","The patella and the calcaneus are bones of the knee and heel, not the standard red marrow sites. Choose the calcaneus for a question about the bone the calcaneal tendon attaches to.","The shafts of adult long bones contain mostly yellow marrow, which is fat, so they are poor sites for sampling blood-forming tissue. Pick the shaft for a question about where yellow marrow is stored.","Correct. Marrow samples are typically drawn from the iliac crest or the sternum."],"lt":"t-blood","lid":"c30","comp":83,"competencyId":"w4-hematopoiesis"},{"id":"L02056","dok":2,"q":"Plasma is about 92 percent water. Why does that suit its job?","a":"Water dissolves and carries many different substances and flows easily through vessels.","options":["Water carries most of the blood's oxygen to the tissues","Water gives blood its deep red color in the vessels","Water forms the fibrous framework of a blood clot","Water dissolves and carries many substances as it flows"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Water is an excellent solvent, so plasma can dissolve and carry nutrients, wastes, electrolytes, gases, hormones, and proteins all at once. Because it is a liquid, it flows through the vessels and delivers this cargo to every tissue. This is why the notes describe water as the solvent that carries every other component of plasma.","notes":["Most oxygen is carried bound to hemoglobin inside red cells, with only a small amount dissolved in plasma. Pick hemoglobin for a question about how most oxygen is transported.","Blood's red color comes from the hemoglobin in erythrocytes, and plasma by itself is a pale straw color. This would be the answer for a question about hemoglobin.","The framework of a clot is made of fibrin threads formed from fibrinogen, a plasma protein. Choose fibrinogen for a question about clot formation.","Correct. Water dissolves and carries many substances and lets plasma flow."],"lt":"t-blood","lid":"d3-t-blood-4","comp":81,"competencyId":"w4-blood-composition"},{"id":"L02058","dok":3,"q":"A person moves to a high altitude, where each breath brings in less oxygen, and gradually makes more red blood cells. Which change would show up on a blood test?","a":"A higher hematocrit.","options":["A higher hematocrit","A larger buffy coat","A lower hematocrit","A lower plasma albumin level"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"When tissues are short of oxygen, the kidney releases erythropoietin, which signals the red bone marrow to make more erythrocytes. More red cells means a larger share of the blood volume is erythrocytes, so the hematocrit rises. The extra red cells carry more oxygen, which helps make up for the thinner air.","notes":["Correct. More red cells raise the hematocrit.","The buffy coat holds leukocytes and platelets, which are not what the body increases in response to low oxygen. Choose a larger buffy coat for a question about a high white cell count, such as in infection or leukemia.","A lower hematocrit means fewer red cells, which is what happens in anemia. Pick it for a question about a patient with too few erythrocytes.","Albumin is a plasma protein made by the liver and is not the body's response to thin air. A low albumin level would fit a question about liver disease or fluid leaking out of the blood."],"lt":"t-blood","lid":"d3-t-blood-6","comp":81,"competencyId":"w4-blood-composition"},{"id":"L02059","dok":2,"q":"Blood is typed as A, B, AB, or O based on antigens on the red cell surface. Why must donor and recipient blood be matched before a transfusion?","a":"The recipient's antibodies attack donor red cells carrying unfamiliar antigens, causing a dangerous reaction.","options":["Mismatched blood carries much less oxygen to the tissues","Mismatched platelets cannot help form clots at an injury","Mismatched plasma contains too much water for the recipient","Mismatched red cells are attacked by the recipient's antibodies"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Each blood type is defined by antigens, which are marker molecules, on the surface of the red cells. A person's plasma contains antibodies against the ABO antigens they do not have. If mismatched blood is given, those antibodies bind the donor red cells and cause them to clump and burst, a dangerous transfusion reaction.","notes":["All normal red cells carry oxygen the same way with hemoglobin, whatever their type. Students pick this because they assume blood types differ in function, but they differ only in surface antigens.","ABO typing is based on antigens on red cells, not on platelets. Choose platelets for a question about how a vessel is plugged after a cut.","Plasma is about 92 percent water in everyone, regardless of blood type. This choice confuses blood type with plasma composition.","Correct. The recipient's antibodies attack donor red cells with unfamiliar antigens."],"lt":"t-blood","lid":"d3-t-blood-7"},{"id":"L02060","dok":2,"q":"A person with anemia often feels tired and short of breath. Why do these symptoms follow from anemia?","a":"Fewer red cells carry less oxygen, so tissues, including muscles, get less than they need.","options":["Too few leukocytes leave the body open to infection","Too many red cells thicken the blood and slow flow","Too few platelets allow constant slow bleeding","Fewer red cells carry less oxygen to the tissues"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Anemia means too few red cells or too little hemoglobin, so the blood carries less oxygen. Muscles and other tissues then receive less oxygen than they need, which causes fatigue, and the body responds by breathing harder, which feels like shortness of breath. The symptoms follow directly from the red cell's job of carrying oxygen.","notes":["Too few leukocytes is leukopenia, which weakens defense against infection. Choose it for a question about frequent infections.","Too many red cells is polycythemia, the opposite of anemia, which thickens the blood. It would be the answer for a question about a high hematocrit.","Too few platelets is thrombocytopenia, which causes bleeding and bruising rather than oxygen shortage. Pick it for a question about a low platelet count.","Correct. Anemia lowers oxygen delivery, causing fatigue and breathlessness."],"lt":"t-blood","lid":"d3x-t-blood-1","comp":84,"competencyId":"w4-blood-disorders"},{"id":"L04294","dok":1,"q":"What are the two main components of whole blood, and roughly what share of blood volume does each make up?","a":"Plasma, about 55 percent, and formed elements, about 45 percent.","options":["Plasma about 92 percent, formed elements about 8 percent","Plasma about 45 percent, formed elements about 55 percent","Plasma about 55 percent, formed elements about 45 percent","Plasma about 99 percent, formed elements about 1 percent"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"When a tube of blood is spun, it separates into a pale yellow plasma layer on top, about 55 percent of the volume, and the formed elements below, about 45 percent. The formed elements are almost entirely erythrocytes, with a thin buffy coat of leukocytes and platelets between the two layers. Plasma is the liquid extracellular matrix that makes blood a fluid connective tissue.","notes":["About 92 percent is the water content of plasma, not plasma's share of whole blood. Students pick this when they mix up what plasma is made of with how much of the blood it is.","This reverses the two numbers. Plasma is the larger share, which is why the plasma layer sits above a slightly smaller red cell layer in a spun tube.","Correct. Plasma is about 55 percent and formed elements about 45 percent of blood volume.","The leukocytes and platelets of the buffy coat make up only about 1 percent of blood, but the erythrocytes alone make up close to 45 percent. This choice forgets the red cells, the largest group of formed elements."],"lt":"t-w4-blood-composition","lid":"w4-blood-composition-r1","comp":81,"competencyId":"w4-blood-composition"},{"id":"L04296","dok":1,"q":"Which list names the three main plasma proteins?","a":"Albumin, globulins, and fibrinogen.","options":["Albumin, globulins, and fibrinogen","Globulins, fibrinogen, and myoglobin","Albumin, globulins, and hemoglobin","Albumin, fibrinogen, and collagen"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The three main plasma proteins are albumin (about 60 percent), the globulins (about 35 percent), and fibrinogen (about 4 percent). Albumin helps hold water in the blood, globulins include transport proteins and antibodies, and fibrinogen is the clotting protein. All except the antibody-forming gamma globulins are made in the liver.","notes":["Correct. Albumin, globulins, and fibrinogen are the three main plasma proteins.","Myoglobin is the oxygen-storing pigment in the sarcoplasm of muscle fibers, not a plasma protein. It would be the answer if the question asked what gives muscle its red color.","Hemoglobin is the oxygen-carrying protein packed inside erythrocytes, not a protein dissolved in plasma. Pick it if the question asked what fills a red blood cell.","Collagen is the strong fiber of connective tissue proper, cartilage, and bone. It is not dissolved in plasma, though students choose it because blood is classified as a connective tissue."],"lt":"t-w4-blood-composition","lid":"w4-blood-composition-r3","comp":81,"competencyId":"w4-blood-composition"},{"id":"L04297","dok":2,"q":"Which plasma protein is the most abundant, and what is its main job?","a":"Albumin, which helps hold water in the blood by maintaining osmotic pressure.","options":["Albumin, which helps hold water in the blood","Gamma globulins, which act as antibodies","Hemoglobin, which carries oxygen in the plasma","Fibrinogen, which forms the framework of a blood clot"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Albumin makes up about 60 percent of plasma protein, more than any other. Because there is so much of it dissolved in the blood, it creates an osmotic pull (a tendency for water to move toward where more dissolved particles are) that keeps water inside the vessels instead of leaking into the tissues. It also carries hormones, fatty acids, and drugs.","notes":["Correct. Albumin is the most abundant plasma protein and helps keep water in the blood.","Gamma globulins are the antibodies of immune defense, made by plasma cells. They would be the answer if the question asked which plasma proteins fight infection.","Hemoglobin carries oxygen, but it stays inside erythrocytes rather than floating in plasma. Students pick it because it is the best known blood protein.","Fibrinogen is the clotting protein, but it is only about 4 percent of plasma protein. Pick it if the question asked which plasma protein becomes fibrin threads in a clot."],"lt":"t-w4-blood-composition","lid":"w4-blood-composition-r4","comp":81,"competencyId":"w4-blood-composition"},{"id":"L04298","dok":1,"q":"A lab tech lets a blood sample clot and then collects the fluid left behind, which is serum rather than plasma. What is missing from serum that was present in plasma?","a":"The clotting proteins, such as fibrinogen.","options":["Albumin","Water and electrolytes","The gamma globulins (antibodies)","The clotting proteins, such as fibrinogen"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Serum is plasma with the clotting proteins removed. When blood clots, fibrinogen is converted into fibrin threads that form the framework of the clot, so the fibrinogen is used up and stays behind in the clot. The fluid that remains still holds water, electrolytes, albumin, and antibodies.","notes":["Albumin is not used in clotting, so it remains in serum. Pick albumin if the question asked which plasma protein is the most abundant.","Water and electrolytes stay in the fluid after clotting, since they play no part in forming the clot. Water is about 92 percent of plasma and is still the bulk of serum.","Antibodies stay in serum, which is why serum is used for many antibody tests. They would be the answer if the question asked which plasma proteins defend against infection.","Correct. The clotting proteins are used up in the clot, so serum lacks them."],"lt":"t-w4-blood-composition","lid":"w4-blood-composition-r5","comp":81,"competencyId":"w4-blood-composition"},{"id":"L04329","dok":1,"q":"Where does hematopoiesis, the formation of blood cells, take place in an adult?","a":"In red bone marrow.","options":["In yellow bone marrow","In red bone marrow","In the spleen","In the thymus"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"In adults, all the formed elements are produced in red bone marrow, found in the spongy bone of the axial skeleton and the proximal ends of limb bones such as the humerus and femur. Every blood cell traces back to one stem cell type, the hemocytoblast, living in this marrow. That is why a marrow sample from the iliac crest or sternum is used to examine blood formation directly.","notes":["Yellow bone marrow is mostly fat stored in the medullary cavity of adult long bones, and it does not make blood cells under normal conditions. Pick it if the question asked what fills the medullary cavity of an adult long bone.","Correct. Red bone marrow is the site of hematopoiesis in adults.","The spleen makes red blood cells in the fetus, but in adults it removes worn-out red cells and stores platelets. It would be the answer if the question asked where old red cells are broken down.","The thymus is where T cells finish maturing, but the T cell precursors are first formed in red bone marrow. Choose the thymus if the question asked where T cells mature."],"lt":"t-w4-hematopoiesis","lid":"w4-hematopoiesis-r1","comp":83,"competencyId":"w4-hematopoiesis"},{"id":"L04330","dok":1,"q":"What is the stem cell that gives rise to every formed element of blood?","a":"The hemocytoblast, the hematopoietic stem cell.","options":["The reticulocyte","The hemocytoblast","The osteoprogenitor cell","The megakaryocyte"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The hemocytoblast is the pluripotent hematopoietic stem cell in red bone marrow, meaning it can become any type of blood cell. It gives rise to the myeloid line, which produces erythrocytes, platelets, and all leukocytes except lymphocytes, and to the lymphoid line, which produces lymphocytes. Every formed element in circulation traces back to it.","notes":["The reticulocyte is an immature erythrocyte released into the blood, near the end of red cell development rather than the start. It would be the answer if the question asked which young red cell still contains ribosome remnants.","Correct. The hemocytoblast is the stem cell for all formed elements.","The osteoprogenitor cell is the stem cell of bone tissue, which becomes osteoblasts. Students pick it because blood forms inside bone, but it makes bone cells, not blood cells.","The megakaryocyte is a giant marrow cell whose cytoplasm breaks into platelets, so it makes only one formed element. Pick it if the question asked which cell produces platelets."],"lt":"t-w4-hematopoiesis","lid":"w4-hematopoiesis-r2","comp":83,"competencyId":"w4-hematopoiesis"},{"id":"L04342","dok":1,"q":"What is anemia?","a":"Too few erythrocytes or too little hemoglobin, so the blood carries less oxygen.","options":["An inherited lack of a clotting factor, so blood clots too slowly","Too few erythrocytes or too little hemoglobin, so the blood carries less oxygen","An excess of erythrocytes that thickens the blood","An abnormally low platelet count that raises the risk of bleeding"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Anemia is a disorder of the erythrocytes: either there are too few of them or they hold too little hemoglobin. Since hemoglobin is the protein that carries oxygen, either problem lowers the oxygen-carrying capacity of the blood. A low hematocrit on a spun tube is one common sign.","notes":["An inherited lack of a clotting factor is hemophilia, a disorder of the clotting proteins rather than the red cells. Choose it if the question asked which inherited disorder makes blood clot too slowly.","Correct. Anemia means reduced oxygen-carrying capacity from too few red cells or too little hemoglobin.","An excess of erythrocytes is polycythemia, which thickens the blood and slows its flow. Pick it if the question asked about the opposite of anemia.","A low platelet count is thrombocytopenia. It would be the answer if the question asked which disorder causes easy bruising and bleeding from too few platelets."],"lt":"t-w4-blood-disorders","lid":"w4-blood-disorders-r1","comp":84,"competencyId":"w4-blood-disorders"}]},{"id":"m3-forearm-posterior","title":"Posterior forearm muscles","summary":"superficial and deep extensor compartment muscles, supinator","mw":"2026-10-19","tr":"2026-10-15","cards":[{"id":"L04333","dok":1,"q":"What is the general action of the muscles in the posterior compartment of the forearm?","a":"Extension of the wrist and fingers, along with supination of the forearm.","options":["Flexing the wrist and fingers and pronating the forearm","Flexing the elbow and supinating the forearm","Extending the wrist and fingers and supinating the forearm","Extending the elbow and the shoulder"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The posterior forearm compartment holds the extensors and the supinator. Most of its superficial muscles share a common origin on the lateral epicondyle of the humerus and extend the wrist and fingers, and the deep supinator turns the palm up. This is the mirror image of the anterior compartment, which flexes and pronates.","notes":["Flexing the wrist and fingers and pronating the forearm is the job of the anterior forearm compartment. Pick this if the question asked about the muscles that share the medial epicondyle origin.","Flexing the elbow and supinating the forearm is the job of biceps brachii in the anterior arm. Students pick it because supination appears in both answers.","Correct. The posterior forearm extends the wrist and fingers and supinates the forearm.","Extending the elbow and shoulder is done by the triceps brachii in the posterior arm, along with muscles such as latissimus dorsi. It would be the answer for the posterior arm, not the forearm."],"lt":"t-w4-lab-forearm-compartments","lid":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments-r2","comp":86,"competencyId":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments"},{"id":"L04334","dok":2,"q":"Based on its name, in which forearm compartment does extensor carpi ulnaris lie, and what does it do?","a":"The posterior compartment; it extends the wrist and adducts it toward the ulnar side.","options":["Posterior compartment; extends and adducts the wrist","Posterior compartment; extends and abducts the wrist","Anterior compartment; flexes and adducts the wrist","Anterior compartment; flexes and abducts the wrist"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Extensor tells you the action and places the muscle in the posterior (extensor) compartment. Carpi means the wrist, and ulnaris means the ulnar (little finger) side, so the muscle extends the wrist while pulling it toward the ulnar side, which is adduction of the wrist. It arises from the lateral epicondyle and posterior ulna and inserts on the base of metacarpal 5.","notes":["Correct. Extensor carpi ulnaris is a posterior compartment muscle that extends and adducts the wrist.","Extending and abducting the wrist is the action of extensor carpi radialis longus and brevis, on the radial (thumb) side. It would be the answer for a muscle named radialis.","Flexing and adducting the wrist is the action of flexor carpi ulnaris, its anterior partner on the same side. Pick this if the muscle's name began with flexor.","Flexing and abducting the wrist is the action of flexor carpi radialis in the anterior compartment. Choose this for a muscle named flexor carpi radialis."],"lt":"t-w4-lab-forearm-compartments","lid":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments-r3","comp":86,"competencyId":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments"},{"id":"N-m3-forearm-posterior-1","dok":3,"q":"A tennis player develops pain on the outer side of the elbow after many backhand strokes. This tennis elbow is inflammation at which site?","a":"The lateral epicondyle, the common origin of the wrist extensors.","options":["The lateral epicondyle, the common extensor origin","The medial epicondyle, the common flexor origin","The olecranon, where the triceps brachii inserts","The radial tuberosity, where the biceps brachii inserts"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Most of the superficial muscles of the posterior forearm, the wrist and finger extensors, share a common origin on the lateral epicondyle of the humerus. Repeated forceful wrist extension, as in a backhand stroke, strains that shared attachment and inflames it. Golfer's elbow is the same problem on the opposite side, at the common flexor origin on the medial epicondyle.","notes":["Correct. Tennis elbow affects the common extensor origin on the lateral epicondyle.","The medial epicondyle is the common origin of the superficial forearm flexors, and inflammation there is golfer's elbow. Pick it if the pain were on the inner side of the elbow.","The olecranon is the point of the elbow where the triceps brachii attaches to extend the elbow. It would be the answer if the question asked where the triceps inserts.","The radial tuberosity is just below the elbow on the radius, where the biceps brachii inserts to flex the elbow and supinate the forearm. Choose it if the question asked where the biceps tendon attaches."],"comp":86,"competencyId":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments"},{"id":"N-m3-forearm-posterior-1-new-m3-forearm-hand","dok":1,"q":"Most superficial posterior forearm muscles share one common extensor origin. Which bony landmark is it?","a":"The lateral epicondyle of the humerus.","options":["Lateral supracondylar ridge of the humerus","Lateral epicondyle of the humerus","Olecranon of the ulna","Medial epicondyle of the humerus"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The lateral epicondyle is the knob on the lateral side of the distal humerus, and it is the common extensor origin. Extensor carpi radialis brevis, extensor digitorum, extensor digiti minimi and extensor carpi ulnaris all arise there from a shared tendon. Irritation of that tendon is called tennis elbow (lateral epicondylitis).","notes":["The lateral supracondylar ridge is the sharp edge just above the lateral epicondyle. Extensor carpi radialis longus and brachioradialis arise there, so choose it if the question named one of those two muscles.","Correct. The lateral epicondyle is the common extensor origin.","The olecranon is the point of the elbow on the ulna, where triceps brachii inserts. Pick it if the question asked for the insertion of triceps brachii.","The medial epicondyle is the common flexor origin for the superficial anterior forearm muscles. Pick it if the question asked where the wrist and finger flexors begin."],"comp":86,"competencyId":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments"},{"id":"N-m3-forearm-posterior-2","dok":1,"q":"Where does extensor carpi radialis longus originate?","a":"The lateral supracondylar ridge of the humerus.","options":["Lateral epicondyle of the humerus","Medial epicondyle of the humerus","Posterior ulna and interosseous membrane","Lateral supracondylar ridge of the humerus"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Extensor carpi radialis longus is the one radial wrist extensor that starts above the common extensor origin. It arises from the lateral supracondylar ridge, the edge of the humerus just above the lateral epicondyle, and runs down the thumb side of the back of the forearm to the base of metacarpal 2. Its higher starting point is part of why it is longer than extensor carpi radialis brevis.","notes":["The lateral epicondyle is the origin of extensor carpi radialis brevis and the other common extensors. Pick it if the question asked about the shorter radial wrist extensor.","The medial epicondyle is the common flexor origin on the opposite side of the elbow. Pick it if the question asked about a superficial anterior forearm muscle.","The posterior ulna and interosseous membrane give origin to deep muscles such as extensor pollicis longus and extensor indicis. Choose it if the question asked about a deep thumb or index finger extensor.","Correct. Extensor carpi radialis longus arises from the lateral supracondylar ridge."],"comp":86,"competencyId":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments"},{"id":"N-m3-forearm-posterior-3","dok":1,"q":"Where does extensor carpi radialis longus insert?","a":"The base of metacarpal 2.","options":["Base of metacarpal 2","Base of metacarpal 1","Base of metacarpal 5","Base of metacarpal 3"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Extensor carpi radialis longus crosses the back of the wrist on the thumb side and inserts on the dorsal base of metacarpal 2, the metacarpal of the index finger. Pulling there extends the wrist and tilts the hand toward the thumb (abduction). Its partner, extensor carpi radialis brevis, lies just beside it and inserts one bone over, on metacarpal 3.","notes":["Correct. Extensor carpi radialis longus inserts on the base of metacarpal 2.","The base of metacarpal 1 receives abductor pollicis longus from the deep layer. Pick it if the question asked which muscle moves the thumb away from the palm.","The base of metacarpal 5 receives extensor carpi ulnaris on the little finger side. Choose it if the question asked about the ulnar wrist extensor.","The base of metacarpal 3 receives extensor carpi radialis brevis, the shorter partner. Pick it if the question asked where the brevis inserts."],"comp":86,"competencyId":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments"},{"id":"N-m3-forearm-posterior-4","dok":1,"q":"Where does extensor carpi ulnaris insert?","a":"The base of metacarpal 5.","options":["Base of metacarpal 5","Extensor expansion of digit 5","Base of metacarpal 2","Pisiform and hook of the hamate"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Extensor carpi ulnaris arises from the lateral epicondyle and the posterior ulna, runs down the ulnar (little finger) side of the back of the forearm, and inserts on the base of metacarpal 5. Because it pulls on the back and the ulnar side of the wrist, it extends the wrist and adducts the hand toward the little finger.","notes":["Correct. Extensor carpi ulnaris inserts on the base of metacarpal 5.","The extensor expansion of digit 5 receives extensor digiti minimi, which straightens the little finger. Pick it if the question asked about a finger extensor rather than a wrist extensor.","The base of metacarpal 2 receives extensor carpi radialis longus on the thumb side. Choose it if the question asked about the longer radial wrist extensor.","The pisiform and hook of the hamate receive flexor carpi ulnaris on the front of the wrist. Pick them if the question asked about the ulnar wrist flexor."],"comp":86,"competencyId":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments"},{"id":"N-m3-forearm-posterior-5","dok":1,"q":"Where does extensor digitorum insert?","a":"The extensor expansions of digits 2 to 5.","options":["Middle phalanges of digits 2 to 5","Proximal phalanx of the thumb","Bases of metacarpals 2 to 5","Extensor expansions of digits 2 to 5"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Extensor digitorum sits in the middle of the superficial posterior forearm and divides into four tendons that cross the back of the hand to digits 2 to 5. Each tendon spreads into an extensor expansion, a flattened hood of tendon over the back of the finger, which carries the pull out to the middle and distal phalanges. That lets one muscle straighten all four fingers and help extend the wrist.","notes":["The middle phalanges of digits 2 to 5 receive flexor digitorum superficialis on the palmar side. Pick them if the question asked about the superficial finger flexor.","The proximal phalanx of the thumb receives extensor pollicis brevis. Choose it if the question asked which muscle extends the thumb at its knuckle.","No single muscle inserts on all four metacarpal bases. The wrist extensors attach to single metacarpal bases (2, 3 and 5), so this choice blends the wrist extensors with the finger extensor.","Correct. Extensor digitorum inserts on the extensor expansions of digits 2 to 5."],"comp":86,"competencyId":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments"},{"id":"N-m3-forearm-posterior-6","dok":1,"q":"What does the supinator muscle do?","a":"It supinates the forearm.","options":["Pronates the forearm","Supinates the forearm","Extends the wrist","Flexes the elbow"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Supinator is a deep posterior forearm muscle that arises from the lateral epicondyle and proximal ulna and wraps around the back of the proximal radius to insert on its lateral surface. When it shortens, it unwinds the radius from across the ulna so the palm turns to face anteriorly, which is supination. It is one of the few posterior compartment muscles that does not extend anything.","notes":["Pronation is the opposite movement, done by pronator teres and pronator quadratus in the anterior forearm. Pick it if the question named one of the pronators.","Correct. Supinator turns the palm to face anteriorly.","Wrist extension is done by the extensor carpi muscles, which cross the back of the wrist. Supinator stops on the proximal radius and never reaches the wrist.","Elbow flexion is done by muscles that cross the front of the elbow, such as brachialis, biceps brachii and brachioradialis. Choose it if the question named one of those."],"comp":86,"competencyId":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments"},{"id":"N-m3-forearm-posterior-7","dok":1,"q":"Which deep posterior forearm muscle wraps around the proximal radius?","a":"Supinator.","options":["Extensor indicis","Abductor pollicis longus","Supinator","Extensor pollicis longus"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Supinator lies deep in the proximal posterior forearm, under the superficial extensors. Its fibers run from the lateral epicondyle and the proximal ulna and curl around the back and lateral side of the upper radius. That wrapped arrangement is what lets it twist the radius and supinate the forearm.","notes":["Extensor indicis arises from the distal posterior ulna and joins the extensor expansion of the index finger. Pick it if the question asked which muscle lets the index finger straighten on its own.","Abductor pollicis longus arises lower on the posterior radius, ulna and interosseous membrane and sends its tendon to the base of metacarpal 1. Pick it if the question asked which deep muscle moves the thumb away from the palm.","Correct. Supinator wraps around the proximal radius.","Extensor pollicis longus arises from the posterior ulna and interosseous membrane and runs to the distal phalanx of the thumb. Choose it if the question asked which muscle straightens the thumb tip."],"comp":86,"competencyId":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments"},{"id":"N-m3-forearm-posterior-8","dok":1,"q":"Where does abductor pollicis longus insert?","a":"The base of metacarpal 1.","options":["Base of metacarpal 2","Proximal phalanx of the thumb","Distal phalanx of the thumb","Base of metacarpal 1"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Abductor pollicis longus arises from the posterior radius, ulna and interosseous membrane, and its tendon curves around the thumb side of the wrist to the base of metacarpal 1. Pulling on the first metacarpal moves the thumb away from the palm (abduction) and also helps extend it. Of the three thumb muscles of the posterior forearm, it inserts on the most proximal bone.","notes":["The base of metacarpal 2 receives extensor carpi radialis longus on the back of the wrist. Pick it if the question asked about the longer radial wrist extensor.","The proximal phalanx of the thumb receives extensor pollicis brevis. Pick it if the question asked which muscle extends the thumb at its knuckle.","The distal phalanx of the thumb receives extensor pollicis longus on the back and flexor pollicis longus on the front. Choose it if the question asked which muscles act on the thumb tip.","Correct. Abductor pollicis longus inserts on the base of metacarpal 1."],"comp":86,"competencyId":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments"},{"id":"N-m3-forearm-posterior-9","dok":1,"q":"Where does extensor pollicis brevis insert?","a":"The proximal phalanx of the thumb.","options":["Distal phalanx of the thumb","Proximal phalanx of the thumb","Base of metacarpal 1","Extensor expansion of digit 2"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Extensor pollicis brevis arises from the posterior radius and interosseous membrane alongside abductor pollicis longus. Its tendon runs to the base of the proximal phalanx of the thumb, so it extends the thumb at the metacarpophalangeal joint (the thumb's knuckle). Brevis tells you it is the shorter of the two thumb extensors, and it stops one bone short of extensor pollicis longus.","notes":["The distal phalanx of the thumb is where extensor pollicis longus inserts. Pick it if the question asked about the long thumb extensor that straightens the tip.","Correct. Extensor pollicis brevis inserts on the proximal phalanx of the thumb.","The base of metacarpal 1 is where abductor pollicis longus inserts. Choose it if the question asked which muscle moves the thumb away from the palm.","The extensor expansion of digit 2 receives extensor indicis, the extra extensor of the index finger. Pick it if the question asked about the pointing finger."],"comp":86,"competencyId":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments"},{"id":"N-m3-forearm-posterior-10","dok":1,"q":"At which joint does extensor pollicis longus extend the thumb that no other thumb extensor reaches?","a":"The interphalangeal joint near the tip of the thumb.","options":["The metacarpophalangeal joint, the thumb's knuckle","The interphalangeal joint near the tip of the thumb","The wrist joint on the thumb side","The carpometacarpal joint at the base of the thumb"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Extensor pollicis longus inserts on the base of the distal phalanx of the thumb, so it is the only thumb extensor that can straighten the tip at the interphalangeal joint. Its tendon also crosses the knuckle and the base of the thumb on the way, so it helps extend those joints too. Its tendon forms the ulnar border of the anatomical snuffbox.","notes":["The metacarpophalangeal joint is where extensor pollicis brevis does its work, since it stops on the proximal phalanx. Choose it if the question asked about the short thumb extensor.","Correct. Only extensor pollicis longus reaches the distal phalanx to straighten the thumb tip.","The wrist on the thumb side is extended by extensor carpi radialis longus and brevis. Pick it if the question named a muscle with carpi in its name.","The carpometacarpal joint at the base of the thumb is moved by abductor pollicis longus, which inserts on metacarpal 1. Pick it if the question asked where that muscle acts."],"comp":86,"competencyId":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments"},{"id":"N-m3-forearm-posterior-11","dok":1,"q":"What is the action of extensor indicis?","a":"It extends the index finger.","options":["Extends the index finger","Adducts the wrist","Extends the little finger","Abducts the thumb"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Extensor indicis is a small deep posterior muscle that arises from the posterior ulna and interosseous membrane and joins the extensor expansion of digit 2. Indicis refers to the index finger, the pointing finger. Its extra tendon lets the index finger straighten on its own, which is why you can point while the other fingers stay curled.","notes":["Correct. Extensor indicis extends the index finger.","Adducting the wrist is done by extensor carpi ulnaris working with flexor carpi ulnaris. Pick it if the name contained carpi ulnaris.","Extending the little finger on its own is the job of extensor digiti minimi. Pick it if the name contained digiti minimi.","Abducting the thumb is done by abductor pollicis longus in the deep posterior layer, along with the short abductor in the hand. Choose it if the name contained pollicis."],"comp":86,"competencyId":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments"},{"id":"N-m3-forearm-posterior-12","dok":1,"q":"In the muscle name extensor digiti minimi, what does digiti minimi refer to?","a":"The little finger.","options":["The middle finger","The thumb","The little finger","The index finger"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Digiti minimi means the smallest digit, the little finger. Extensor digiti minimi is a slender superficial muscle that lies just medial to extensor digitorum and joins the extensor expansion of digit 5. The same words appear in the hypothenar muscles of the hand, such as abductor digiti minimi, which also act on the little finger.","notes":["No forearm muscle is named for the middle finger alone. It is moved by extensor digitorum along with the other fingers, and in the hand it serves as the reference line for spreading the fingers.","The thumb is named by pollicis, as in extensor pollicis longus. Pick this if the name contained pollicis.","Correct. Digiti minimi means the little finger.","The index finger is named by indicis, as in extensor indicis. Choose it if the name contained indicis."],"comp":79,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-roles-naming"},{"id":"N-m3-forearm-posterior-13","dok":1,"q":"Which group lists the deep layer of the posterior forearm?","a":"Supinator, abductor pollicis longus, extensor pollicis brevis, extensor pollicis longus and extensor indicis.","options":["Extensor carpi radialis longus, extensor carpi radialis brevis, extensor digitorum, extensor digiti minimi, extensor carpi ulnaris","Flexor digitorum profundus, flexor pollicis longus, pronator quadratus","Supinator, abductor pollicis longus, extensor pollicis brevis, extensor pollicis longus, extensor indicis","Pronator teres, flexor carpi radialis, palmaris longus, flexor carpi ulnaris"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The deep posterior layer lies against the radius, ulna and interosseous membrane. Supinator sits proximally around the radius, and below it four muscles run toward the thumb and index finger: abductor pollicis longus, extensor pollicis brevis, extensor pollicis longus and extensor indicis. Most of the deep layer acts on the thumb and index finger, while the superficial layer acts on the wrist and the four fingers together.","notes":["These five muscles form the superficial layer of the posterior forearm, most of them arising from the lateral epicondyle. Pick this group if the question asked about the superficial extensors.","Flexor digitorum profundus, flexor pollicis longus and pronator quadratus form the deep layer of the anterior forearm. Choose them if the question asked about deep muscles on the front of the forearm.","Correct. These five muscles form the deep posterior layer.","Pronator teres, flexor carpi radialis, palmaris longus and flexor carpi ulnaris are the superficial anterior muscles from the medial epicondyle. Pick them if the question asked about the common flexor origin."],"comp":86,"competencyId":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments"},{"id":"N-m3-forearm-posterior-14","dok":1,"q":"Which group of muscles arises from the common extensor origin on the lateral epicondyle?","a":"Extensor carpi radialis brevis, extensor digitorum, extensor digiti minimi and extensor carpi ulnaris.","options":["Abductor pollicis longus, extensor pollicis brevis, extensor pollicis longus, extensor indicis","Extensor carpi radialis longus, abductor pollicis longus, extensor pollicis longus, extensor indicis","Extensor carpi radialis brevis, extensor digitorum, extensor digiti minimi, extensor carpi ulnaris","Pronator teres, flexor carpi radialis, palmaris longus, flexor carpi ulnaris"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The common extensor origin is a shared tendon on the lateral epicondyle. Extensor carpi radialis brevis, extensor digitorum, extensor digiti minimi and extensor carpi ulnaris all start there and fan down the back of the forearm. Extensor carpi radialis longus is the exception in the superficial group, arising slightly higher on the lateral supracondylar ridge.","notes":["These four deep muscles arise from the posterior radius, ulna and interosseous membrane, not the humerus. Pick this group if the question asked which posterior muscles act mainly on the thumb and index finger.","This group mixes extensor carpi radialis longus, which arises from the lateral supracondylar ridge, with three deep muscles that arise from the radius, ulna and membrane. None of them use the common extensor tendon.","Correct. These four superficial extensors share the lateral epicondyle origin.","These four superficial anterior muscles arise from the medial epicondyle, the common flexor origin. Choose them if the question asked about the flexor side of the elbow."],"comp":86,"competencyId":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments"},{"id":"N-m3-forearm-posterior-15","dok":1,"q":"What does extensor carpi radialis brevis do at the wrist?","a":"It extends and abducts the wrist.","options":["Extends and adducts the wrist","Flexes and abducts the wrist","Extends and abducts the wrist","Flexes and adducts the wrist"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The name gives the action: extensor for straightening, carpi for the wrist, radialis for the thumb side, and brevis because it is shorter than its longus partner. Its tendon crosses the back of the wrist on the thumb side and inserts on the base of metacarpal 3, so it extends the wrist and helps tilt the hand toward the thumb. It works alongside extensor carpi radialis longus.","notes":["Extending and adducting the wrist is the action of extensor carpi ulnaris on the little finger side. Pick it if the name contained ulnaris.","Flexing and abducting the wrist is the action of flexor carpi radialis on the front of the forearm. Choose it if the name began with flexor.","Correct. Extensor carpi radialis brevis extends and abducts the wrist.","Flexing and adducting the wrist is the action of flexor carpi ulnaris on the front of the forearm. Pick it if the question named the ulnar wrist flexor."],"comp":86,"competencyId":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments"},{"id":"N-m3-forearm-posterior-16","dok":1,"q":"Extensor carpi radialis longus and extensor carpi radialis brevis share most of their name. What do longus and brevis tell you?","a":"Which of the two muscles is longer.","options":["Which one lies deeper","Which one is longer","Which one is stronger","Which side of the wrist each crosses"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Longus means long and brevis means short, so the names compare the lengths of two muscles that otherwise match. Extensor carpi radialis longus starts higher, on the lateral supracondylar ridge, and is the longer of the pair, while brevis starts on the lateral epicondyle. Both extend and abduct the wrist and lie side by side on the thumb side of the back of the forearm.","notes":["Depth is named by superficialis and profundus, as in the finger flexors. Both radial wrist extensors are superficial, so their names do not need to separate them by depth.","Correct. Longus and brevis compare length.","Muscle names describe strength only indirectly, through size words such as major and minor. Longus and brevis describe length, and the longer muscle is not automatically the stronger one.","The side a muscle crosses is named by radialis or ulnaris. Both muscles contain radialis, so both cross the wrist on the thumb side."],"comp":79,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-roles-naming"},{"id":"N-m3-forearm-posterior-17","dok":1,"q":"Which superficial posterior forearm muscle sends four tendons across the back of the hand to digits 2 to 5?","a":"Extensor digitorum.","options":["Extensor carpi ulnaris","Extensor digitorum","Extensor digiti minimi","Extensor indicis"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Extensor digitorum lies in the middle of the superficial posterior forearm and splits into four tendons above the wrist. These pass under the extensor retinaculum and fan out across the back of the hand to digits 2 to 5, where you can see them stand out when you straighten your fingers. Digitorum means of the digits, which matches its reach to all four fingers.","notes":["Extensor carpi ulnaris ends on the base of metacarpal 5 and does not reach the fingers. Pick it if the question asked which muscle extends and adducts the wrist.","Correct. Extensor digitorum sends four tendons to digits 2 to 5.","Extensor digiti minimi sends a single tendon to the little finger. Choose it if the question asked about the extra extensor of digit 5.","Extensor indicis is a deep muscle with a single tendon to the index finger. Pick it if the question asked which muscle lets the index finger straighten on its own."],"comp":86,"competencyId":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments"},{"id":"N-m3-forearm-posterior-18","dok":1,"q":"Unlike the superficial extensors, the deep posterior forearm muscles that move the thumb and index finger mostly arise from where?","a":"The posterior radius, ulna and interosseous membrane.","options":["The common flexor tendon on the medial epicondyle","The olecranon at the point of the elbow","The common extensor tendon on the lateral epicondyle","The posterior radius, ulna and interosseous membrane"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Abductor pollicis longus, extensor pollicis brevis, extensor pollicis longus and extensor indicis lie in the deep layer against the bones. They arise from the posterior surfaces of the radius and ulna and from the interosseous membrane between them, rather than from the humerus. Because they start partway down the forearm, they do not cross the elbow and act only at the wrist, thumb and index finger.","notes":["The medial epicondyle is the common flexor origin on the anterior side. Choose it if the question asked about the superficial flexors.","The olecranon is where triceps brachii inserts, and it gives a minor origin to flexor carpi ulnaris. Pick it if the question asked for the insertion of triceps.","The lateral epicondyle is the common extensor origin for the superficial layer. Pick it if the question asked where extensor digitorum or extensor carpi ulnaris begins.","Correct. The deep thumb and index muscles arise from the posterior radius, ulna and interosseous membrane."],"comp":86,"competencyId":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments"},{"id":"N-m3-forearm-posterior-19","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares extensor carpi radialis longus and extensor carpi radialis brevis?","a":"Longus arises from the lateral supracondylar ridge and inserts on metacarpal 2; brevis arises from the lateral epicondyle and inserts on metacarpal 3.","options":["Longus arises from the lateral epicondyle and inserts on metacarpal 3; brevis arises from the lateral supracondylar ridge and inserts on metacarpal 2","Longus lies in the deep layer; brevis lies in the superficial layer","Longus arises from the lateral supracondylar ridge and inserts on metacarpal 2; brevis arises from the lateral epicondyle and inserts on metacarpal 3","Longus extends and abducts the wrist; brevis flexes and adducts the wrist"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The two radial wrist extensors lie side by side and do the same job, extending the wrist and tilting it toward the thumb. Longus starts higher, on the lateral supracondylar ridge, and ends on metacarpal 2, while brevis starts on the lateral epicondyle with the other common extensors and ends one bone over on metacarpal 3. The longer muscle starts higher and ends closer to the thumb.","notes":["This swaps the attachments of the two muscles. It would be right only if the names were reversed, so tie longus to the higher origin on the supracondylar ridge.","Both muscles are in the superficial layer of the posterior forearm. The deep layer holds supinator and the thumb and index finger muscles.","Correct. Longus runs from the supracondylar ridge to metacarpal 2, and brevis from the lateral epicondyle to metacarpal 3.","Both muscles extend and abduct the wrist. Flexing and adducting is the action of flexor carpi ulnaris on the front of the forearm."],"comp":86,"competencyId":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments"},{"id":"N-m3-forearm-posterior-20","dok":2,"q":"Which pair of muscles works together to adduct the wrist, tilting the hand toward the little finger side?","a":"Flexor carpi ulnaris and extensor carpi ulnaris.","options":["Flexor carpi radialis and extensor carpi radialis longus","Flexor carpi ulnaris and flexor carpi radialis","Extensor carpi radialis brevis and extensor carpi ulnaris","Flexor carpi ulnaris and extensor carpi ulnaris"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Adduction of the wrist (ulnar deviation) needs muscles on the little finger side. Flexor carpi ulnaris crosses the front of the wrist on that side and extensor carpi ulnaris crosses the back. When both contract together, flexion and extension cancel out and the hand tilts straight toward the little finger, which is why both share ulnaris in their names.","notes":["Flexor carpi radialis and extensor carpi radialis longus both cross the wrist on the thumb side, so together they abduct the wrist. Pick this pair if the question asked about tilting the hand toward the thumb.","Flexor carpi ulnaris and flexor carpi radialis both cross the front of the wrist, one on each side, so together they flex the wrist straight forward. Pick them for pure wrist flexion.","Extensor carpi radialis brevis and extensor carpi ulnaris both cross the back of the wrist, one on each side, so together they extend the wrist straight back. Choose them for pure wrist extension.","Correct. The two ulnaris muscles combine to adduct the wrist."],"comp":86,"competencyId":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments"},{"id":"N-m3-forearm-posterior-21","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares extensor pollicis brevis and extensor pollicis longus?","a":"Brevis inserts on the proximal phalanx of the thumb; longus inserts on the distal phalanx.","options":["Brevis inserts on the proximal phalanx of the thumb; longus inserts on the distal phalanx","Brevis inserts on the distal phalanx of the thumb; longus inserts on the proximal phalanx","Both insert on the distal phalanx and differ only in where they arise","Brevis inserts on metacarpal 1; longus inserts on the proximal phalanx of the thumb"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Both muscles extend the thumb from the deep posterior forearm, but they stop at different bones. Extensor pollicis brevis, the short one, ends on the proximal phalanx and extends the thumb at its knuckle (the metacarpophalangeal joint). Extensor pollicis longus, the long one, continues to the distal phalanx and is the only extensor that straightens the thumb tip at the interphalangeal joint.","notes":["Correct. Brevis ends on the proximal phalanx and longus on the distal phalanx.","This reverses the two insertions. The short muscle (brevis) stops on the nearer bone and the long muscle (longus) reaches the tip.","They differ in insertion as well as origin. If both reached the distal phalanx, losing one would not change which thumb joint could still be straightened.","Metacarpal 1 is the insertion of abductor pollicis longus, not extensor pollicis brevis. Pick a statement about metacarpal 1 only if it named the abductor."],"comp":86,"competencyId":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments"},{"id":"N-m3-forearm-posterior-22","dok":2,"q":"Most people can straighten the index finger or the little finger on its own while the other fingers stay curled. What anatomical feature allows this?","a":"Each of those fingers has an extra extensor muscle besides extensor digitorum.","options":["Those two fingers receive tendons from the extensor retinaculum","Those two fingers have no flexor digitorum profundus tendons","Those two fingers lack an extensor expansion","Each has an extra extensor besides extensor digitorum"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Extensor digitorum sends tendons to all four fingers, so on its own it tends to straighten them together. The index finger also receives extensor indicis and the little finger also receives extensor digiti minimi, and each of these joins the extensor expansion of its finger. That extra, separate tendon is what lets you point with the index finger or lift the little finger by itself.","notes":["The extensor retinaculum is a band across the back of the wrist that holds the extensor tendons down. It does not give rise to tendons, so pick it if the question asked what keeps the extensor tendons from bowstringing.","All four fingers have flexor digitorum profundus tendons, which bend the fingertips. Removing a flexor would not add any extension, so this choice confuses the flexor and extensor sides.","Every finger has an extensor expansion, the hood over the back of the finger that carries the extensor pull to the phalanges. Without one, a finger could not be straightened at all.","Correct. Extensor indicis and extensor digiti minimi give these two fingers independent extension."],"comp":86,"competencyId":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments"},{"id":"N-m3-forearm-posterior-23","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares the two main supinators of the forearm, supinator and biceps brachii?","a":"Supinator lies in the posterior forearm and wraps the proximal radius; biceps brachii lies in the anterior arm and inserts on the radial tuberosity.","options":["Supinator supinates the forearm from the posterior side; biceps brachii pronates it from the anterior arm","Both muscles arise from the lateral epicondyle of the humerus and insert on the styloid process of the radius","Supinator pulls on the radial tuberosity from the anterior arm; biceps brachii wraps the proximal radius from the posterior forearm","Supinator wraps the proximal radius from the posterior forearm; biceps brachii pulls on the radial tuberosity from the anterior arm"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Both muscles turn the palm to face anteriorly, but they come from different regions. Supinator is a deep posterior forearm muscle that curls around the proximal radius from the lateral epicondyle and ulna. Biceps brachii is an anterior arm muscle whose tendon inserts on the radial tuberosity, and it is the stronger supinator, especially when the elbow is bent.","notes":["Biceps brachii supinates as well as flexing the elbow. Pronation belongs to pronator teres and pronator quadratus, so pick a statement about pronation only if it named one of those.","Only supinator uses the lateral epicondyle, and neither muscle reaches the styloid process of the radius, which is where brachioradialis inserts. Choose the styloid process for a question about brachioradialis.","This swaps the two muscles' locations and attachments. Biceps brachii is the anterior arm muscle on the radial tuberosity, and supinator is the posterior forearm muscle that wraps the radius.","Correct. Supinator wraps the proximal radius from the posterior forearm, and biceps brachii pulls on the radial tuberosity from the anterior arm."],"comp":86,"competencyId":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments"},{"id":"N-m3-forearm-posterior-24","dok":2,"q":"Which deep posterior forearm muscle does not move the thumb or any finger?","a":"Supinator.","options":["Abductor pollicis longus","Extensor indicis","Supinator","Extensor pollicis longus"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Supinator attaches entirely within the proximal forearm, running from the lateral epicondyle and proximal ulna to the proximal radius. Because it never crosses the wrist, it cannot move the hand or digits, and its only job is rotating the radius to supinate the forearm. The other four deep posterior muscles all send tendons into the thumb or the index finger.","notes":["Abductor pollicis longus sends its tendon to the base of metacarpal 1 and moves the thumb away from the palm. Pick it if the question asked which deep muscle abducts the thumb.","Extensor indicis joins the extensor expansion of the index finger and straightens it. Choose it if the question asked which muscle lets you point.","Correct. Supinator acts only on the forearm.","Extensor pollicis longus reaches the distal phalanx of the thumb and straightens the thumb tip. Pick it if the question asked which muscle extends the thumb's interphalangeal joint."],"comp":86,"competencyId":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments"},{"id":"N-m3-forearm-posterior-25","dok":2,"q":"Extensor digitorum arises from the lateral epicondyle and inserts on the extensor expansions of digits 2 to 5. Which joints can it extend?","a":"The finger joints and the wrist.","options":["The thumb joints and the wrist","The finger joints only","The finger joints and the wrist","The wrist only"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"A muscle can act on every joint its tendons cross. Extensor digitorum crosses the back of the wrist and then the knuckles and finger joints of digits 2 to 5, so it straightens the fingers and also helps extend the wrist. This is why the course notes list its action as extending both the fingers and the wrist.","notes":["The thumb is extended by extensor pollicis longus and brevis. Extensor digitorum sends no tendon to the thumb, so pick this choice only for a muscle with pollicis in its name.","Its tendons have to cross the back of the wrist to reach the fingers, so the muscle also pulls the wrist into extension. This choice overlooks the joints a tendon passes on its way.","Correct. Its tendons cross the back of the wrist and the finger joints, so it extends both.","A muscle that extended only the wrist would insert on a metacarpal, like the extensor carpi muscles. Extensor digitorum continues into the fingers, so it moves them too."],"comp":86,"competencyId":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments"},{"id":"N-m3-forearm-posterior-26","dok":2,"q":"What does each part of the name abductor pollicis longus tell you?","a":"It moves the thumb away from the palm, acts on the thumb, and is the long member of a pair.","options":["It moves the thumb away from the palm, acts on the thumb, and is the long member of a pair","It moves the wrist away from the body, acts on the wrist, and is the long member of a pair","It moves the index finger away from the palm, acts on the index finger, and lies deep","It moves the thumb toward the palm, acts on the thumb, and is the long member of a pair"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Abductor means it moves a part away, pollicis means the thumb, and longus means long. Abductor pollicis longus starts in the posterior forearm and ends on metacarpal 1, pulling the thumb away from the palm. Its partner, abductor pollicis brevis, is a short thenar muscle in the hand that makes a similar movement, and longus and brevis tell the two apart.","notes":["Correct. Abductor, pollicis and longus give the action, the digit and the relative length.","Wrist muscles carry carpi in their names, as in extensor carpi radialis longus. Pollicis tells you this muscle acts on the thumb, not the wrist.","The index finger is named by indicis, as in extensor indicis. Pollicis always refers to the thumb.","Moving toward is adduction, not abduction. That would describe adductor pollicis in the web space of the hand."],"comp":79,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-roles-naming"},{"id":"N-m3-forearm-posterior-27","dok":2,"q":"A muscle arises from the lateral supracondylar ridge, crosses the back of the wrist on the thumb side and inserts on the base of metacarpal 2. Which action would you predict at the wrist?","a":"Extension and abduction.","options":["Flexion and abduction","Flexion and adduction","Extension and adduction","Extension and abduction"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Crossing the back of the wrist means the muscle extends it, and crossing on the thumb side means it tilts the hand toward the thumb, which is abduction. These attachments belong to extensor carpi radialis longus. Reading the side and the surface a tendon crosses lets you predict the action before you recall the name.","notes":["Flexion would need a tendon crossing the front of the wrist, like flexor carpi radialis. This muscle is on the back.","Flexion and adduction describe flexor carpi ulnaris, which crosses the front of the wrist on the little finger side. Both the surface and the side are wrong for this muscle.","Adduction would need a tendon on the little finger side, like extensor carpi ulnaris. This tendon crosses on the thumb side.","Correct. A tendon on the back and thumb side of the wrist extends and abducts it."],"comp":86,"competencyId":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments"},{"id":"N-m3-forearm-posterior-28","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares the superficial and deep layers of the posterior forearm?","a":"The superficial layer mostly arises from the lateral epicondyle and moves the wrist and fingers; the deep layer mostly arises from the radius, ulna and interosseous membrane and moves the thumb and index finger.","options":["The superficial layer mostly arises from the radius, ulna and interosseous membrane and moves the thumb; the deep layer mostly arises from the lateral epicondyle and moves the wrist","The superficial layer mostly arises from the lateral epicondyle and moves the wrist and fingers; the deep layer arises lower and mostly moves the thumb and index finger","Both layers arise from the lateral epicondyle, and they differ only in which of the four fingers they extend","The superficial layer mostly arises from the medial epicondyle and flexes the wrist; the deep layer mostly arises from the lateral epicondyle and extends it"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The superficial extensors (extensor carpi radialis brevis, extensor digitorum, extensor digiti minimi and extensor carpi ulnaris) share the lateral epicondyle origin and act on the wrist and digits 2 to 5. The deep layer lies against the bones, arising from the posterior radius, ulna and interosseous membrane, and mostly moves the thumb and index finger. Supinator is the one deep muscle that acts on the forearm instead.","notes":["This swaps the two layers. The muscles from the lateral epicondyle are the superficial ones, and the bone and membrane origins belong to the deep layer.","Correct. The two layers differ in where they arise and in which parts of the hand they move.","The deep layer does not arise from the lateral epicondyle, apart from part of supinator. Its thumb and index muscles start lower, on the radius, ulna and membrane.","The medial epicondyle and wrist flexion belong to the anterior forearm. Both layers of the posterior forearm are on the extensor side."],"comp":86,"competencyId":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments"},{"id":"N-m3-forearm-posterior-29","dok":2,"q":"Which list orders the three posterior thumb muscles by where they insert, from the most proximal bone to the most distal?","a":"Abductor pollicis longus, extensor pollicis brevis, extensor pollicis longus.","options":["Abductor pollicis longus, extensor pollicis longus, extensor pollicis brevis","Abductor pollicis longus, extensor pollicis brevis, extensor pollicis longus","Extensor pollicis brevis, abductor pollicis longus, extensor pollicis longus","Extensor pollicis longus, extensor pollicis brevis, abductor pollicis longus"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Abductor pollicis longus stops on the base of metacarpal 1, extensor pollicis brevis continues to the proximal phalanx, and extensor pollicis longus reaches the distal phalanx. Each muscle therefore acts one joint farther out than the last: the base of the thumb, then the knuckle, then the tip. Knowing this order lets you work out which thumb movement is lost when one tendon is cut.","notes":["This swaps the two extensors. Brevis, the short one, ends on the proximal phalanx, and longus continues to the distal phalanx.","Correct. The insertions run from metacarpal 1 to the proximal phalanx to the distal phalanx.","This places extensor pollicis brevis before abductor pollicis longus. The abductor stops on metacarpal 1, which is proximal to the proximal phalanx where brevis inserts.","This is the correct order written backward, from distal to proximal. Pick it if the question asked for the order starting at the tip of the thumb."],"comp":86,"competencyId":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments"},{"id":"N-m3-forearm-posterior-30","dok":2,"q":"What does each part of the name extensor carpi ulnaris tell you?","a":"It straightens a joint, acts on the wrist, and lies on the ulnar side.","options":["It straightens a joint, acts on the fingers, and lies on the ulnar side","It straightens a joint, acts on the wrist, and lies on the ulnar side","It bends a joint, acts on the wrist, and lies on the ulnar side","It straightens a joint, acts on the wrist, and lies on the radial side"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Extensor names the action (straightening a joint), carpi names the wrist, and ulnaris places the muscle on the ulnar (little finger) side. Together they predict exactly what the muscle does: it extends the wrist and, because it pulls on the ulnar side, adducts the hand toward the little finger. It inserts on the base of metacarpal 5.","notes":["Muscles that act on the fingers carry digitorum or a specific finger name, as in extensor digitorum. Carpi means the wrist.","Correct. Extensor, carpi and ulnaris give the action, the joint and the side.","Bending a joint is flexion, named by flexor, as in flexor carpi ulnaris on the front of the forearm. Pick this if the name began with flexor.","The radial side is named by radialis, as in extensor carpi radialis longus. Ulnaris places this muscle on the opposite side."],"comp":79,"competencyId":"w4-muscle-roles-naming"},{"id":"N-m3-forearm-posterior-31","dok":2,"q":"Which of these posterior forearm muscles crosses both the elbow and the wrist?","a":"Extensor carpi radialis brevis.","options":["Abductor pollicis longus","Supinator","Extensor carpi radialis brevis","Extensor pollicis longus"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Extensor carpi radialis brevis arises above the elbow on the lateral epicondyle and inserts below the wrist on metacarpal 3, so its path crosses both joints. Supinator never reaches the wrist, and the deep thumb muscles start on the radius, ulna and interosseous membrane below the elbow. Knowing which joints a muscle crosses tells you which joints it can move.","notes":["Abductor pollicis longus arises from the posterior radius, ulna and interosseous membrane below the elbow. It crosses the wrist on its way to metacarpal 1 but does not cross the elbow.","Supinator starts at the elbow region but stops on the proximal radius, so it never crosses the wrist. Pick it if the question asked which posterior muscle acts only on the forearm.","Correct. It runs from the lateral epicondyle to metacarpal 3, crossing both the elbow and the wrist.","Extensor pollicis longus arises from the posterior ulna and interosseous membrane below the elbow. It crosses the wrist but not the elbow."],"comp":86,"competencyId":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments"},{"id":"N-m3-forearm-posterior-32","dok":2,"q":"Which description of extensor digiti minimi is accurate?","a":"A slender superficial muscle beside extensor digitorum that extends the little finger.","options":["A slender superficial muscle that extends the little finger","A hypothenar muscle in the hand that abducts the little finger","A deep muscle from the posterior ulna that extends the index finger","A superficial muscle that inserts on metacarpal 5 and adducts the wrist"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Extensor digiti minimi is a thin superficial muscle that arises from the common extensor origin and lies just medial to extensor digitorum. Its tendon joins the extensor expansion of digit 5, giving the little finger an extensor of its own. That is why you can lift the little finger by itself.","notes":["Correct. Extensor digiti minimi is a slender superficial extensor of the little finger.","This describes abductor digiti minimi, an intrinsic hypothenar muscle of the hand. Choose it if the name began with abductor.","This describes extensor indicis, the deep extensor of the index finger. Pick it if the name contained indicis.","This describes extensor carpi ulnaris, which ends on metacarpal 5 and moves the wrist rather than the finger. Pick it if the name contained carpi ulnaris."],"comp":86,"competencyId":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments"},{"id":"N-m3-forearm-posterior-33","dok":3,"q":"A tennis player has pain on the lateral side of the elbow that flares when he extends his wrist against resistance or grips his racket. Which structure is most likely irritated?","a":"The common extensor tendon at the lateral epicondyle.","options":["The biceps brachii tendon at the radial tuberosity","The common extensor tendon at the lateral epicondyle","The triceps brachii tendon at the olecranon","The common flexor tendon at the medial epicondyle"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Tennis elbow (lateral epicondylitis) is irritation of the shared tendon where the superficial extensors attach to the lateral epicondyle. Resisted wrist extension loads that tendon directly. Gripping also hurts because the wrist extensors contract to hold the wrist steady while the finger flexors squeeze, so every grip pulls on the sore spot.","notes":["The biceps tendon inserts on the radial tuberosity at the front of the elbow. Irritation there would hurt with resisted elbow flexion and supination, not wrist extension.","Correct. Lateral elbow pain with resisted wrist extension and gripping points to the common extensor origin.","The triceps tendon inserts on the olecranon at the back of the elbow. Irritation there would hurt with resisted elbow extension rather than wrist extension.","The common flexor tendon at the medial epicondyle is the site of golfer's elbow, which hurts on the inner elbow with resisted wrist flexion. Pick it if the pain were medial."],"comp":86,"competencyId":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments"},{"id":"N-m3-forearm-posterior-34","dok":3,"q":"After a cut on the back of the thumb, a patient can straighten the thumb at its knuckle but cannot straighten the tip. Which tendon was most likely cut?","a":"The extensor pollicis longus tendon.","options":["Extensor pollicis longus","Abductor pollicis longus","Flexor pollicis longus","Extensor pollicis brevis"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Straightening the thumb tip happens at the interphalangeal joint, and the only extensor that reaches the distal phalanx is extensor pollicis longus. The knuckle still straightens because extensor pollicis brevis, which inserts on the proximal phalanx, is intact. Losing only the tip movement points to the long extensor tendon.","notes":["Correct. Extensor pollicis longus is the only extensor of the thumb tip.","Abductor pollicis longus inserts on metacarpal 1 and moves the thumb away from the palm. A cut there would weaken thumb abduction rather than tip extension.","Flexor pollicis longus bends the thumb tip from the palm side. A cut there would leave the patient unable to bend the tip, and the cut would be on the front of the thumb.","If extensor pollicis brevis were cut, extension at the knuckle would weaken, but the long extensor could still straighten the tip. Pick it if the scenario described weak knuckle extension with the tip working."],"comp":86,"competencyId":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments"},{"id":"N-m3-forearm-posterior-35","dok":3,"q":"When you squeeze a fist hard, you can feel the muscles on the back of your forearm tighten even though your fingers are bending. What are those posterior muscles doing?","a":"Holding the wrist extended so the finger flexors can pull efficiently on the fingers.","options":["Holding the wrist extended so the flexors pull efficiently","Pronating the forearm so the fist rotates palm down","Relaxing completely so the finger flexors are not opposed","Flexing the fingers together with the anterior forearm muscles"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The long finger flexors cross the wrist before they reach the fingers, so when they contract they would also bend the wrist. The wrist extensors (extensor carpi radialis longus and brevis and extensor carpi ulnaris) contract at the same time to hold the wrist slightly extended, acting as synergists that stabilize the joint. With the wrist held steady, the flexors' pull goes into the fingers and the grip is stronger.","notes":["Correct. The wrist extensors stabilize the wrist so the grip is strong.","Pronation is done by pronator teres and pronator quadratus on the front of the forearm. Making a fist does not require rotating the forearm.","The tightening you feel shows these muscles are contracting, not relaxing. If they relaxed, the finger flexors would pull the wrist into flexion and the grip would weaken.","The posterior muscles are extensors, so they cannot flex the fingers. Finger flexion comes from flexor digitorum superficialis and profundus on the front of the forearm."],"comp":86,"competencyId":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments"},{"id":"N-m3-forearm-posterior-36","dok":3,"q":"A hitchhiker holds the thumb up and back, and tendons stand out around a small hollow at the base of the thumb on the back of the wrist. Which muscles do these tendons belong to?","a":"Abductor pollicis longus, extensor pollicis brevis and extensor pollicis longus.","options":["Extensor carpi radialis longus, extensor carpi radialis brevis, extensor carpi ulnaris","Flexor pollicis longus, flexor pollicis brevis, abductor pollicis brevis","Extensor digitorum, extensor indicis, extensor digiti minimi","Abductor pollicis longus, extensor pollicis brevis, extensor pollicis longus"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Pulling the thumb up and back uses the three deep posterior muscles that reach the thumb. Their tendons frame the anatomical snuffbox, the small hollow at the base of the thumb: abductor pollicis longus and extensor pollicis brevis on one side, and extensor pollicis longus on the other. These muscles arise from the posterior radius, ulna and interosseous membrane and act only on the thumb.","notes":["These muscles extend the wrist and insert on metacarpals 2, 3 and 5. Pick them if the question asked about bending the wrist back rather than moving the thumb.","These muscles bend the thumb or move it from the palm side, and their tendons are on the front of the hand. Pick them if the question asked about gripping with the thumb.","These muscles extend digits 2 to 5, and their tendons run across the back of the hand to the fingers rather than the thumb. Choose them if the question asked about straightening the fingers.","Correct. These three thumb tendons outline the anatomical snuffbox."],"comp":86,"competencyId":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments"},{"id":"N-m3-forearm-posterior-37","dok":3,"q":"A right-handed carpenter tightens a screw by turning the screwdriver clockwise, rotating the palm from facing down to facing up with the elbow bent. Which two muscles drive this movement?","a":"Supinator and biceps brachii.","options":["Pronator teres and pronator quadratus","Brachialis and brachioradialis","Supinator and biceps brachii","Extensor carpi radialis longus and brevis"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Turning the palm from facing down to facing up is supination, which unrolls the radius from across the ulna. Supinator wraps the proximal radius and does this at any elbow angle, while biceps brachii adds a powerful pull on the radial tuberosity, especially with the elbow bent. That is why screws are easier to drive with the right hand and a flexed elbow.","notes":["Pronator teres and pronator quadratus turn the palm from up to down, which would loosen the screw with the right hand. Pick them for the opposite rotation.","Brachialis and brachioradialis bend the elbow but do not rotate the forearm. Choose them if the carpenter were lifting a tool toward the body.","Correct. Supinator and biceps brachii supinate the forearm.","The radial wrist extensors bend the wrist back and tilt it toward the thumb. They do not turn the palm over, so pick them only for a question about wrist movement."],"comp":86,"competencyId":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments"},{"id":"N-m3-forearm-posterior-38","dok":3,"q":"A patient can straighten all four fingers together but cannot lift the index finger alone while the other fingers stay curled into the palm. Which muscle is most likely not working?","a":"Extensor indicis.","options":["Extensor digiti minimi","Extensor indicis","Extensor pollicis longus","Extensor digitorum"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Extensor digitorum straightens all four fingers together, and the patient can still do that, so it is working. Lifting the index finger alone while the others stay curled depends on extensor indicis, the deep muscle that adds a separate tendon to the extensor expansion of the index finger. Losing that independent movement while group extension is normal points to extensor indicis.","notes":["Extensor digiti minimi gives the little finger independent extension. Choose it if the patient could not lift the little finger by itself.","Correct. Extensor indicis gives the index finger independent extension.","Extensor pollicis longus straightens the thumb tip and does not act on the index finger. Pick it if the patient could not straighten the tip of the thumb.","Extensor digitorum is the muscle that straightens all four fingers together, which this patient can still do. Pick it if the patient could not straighten any of the fingers."],"comp":86,"competencyId":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments"},{"id":"N-m3-forearm-posterior-39","dok":3,"q":"A deep cut on the thumb side of the back of the wrist severs the tendon that inserts on the base of metacarpal 1. Which movement is most weakened?","a":"Moving the thumb away from the palm (abduction).","options":["Moving the thumb away from the palm","Bending the tip of the thumb","Tilting the wrist toward the little finger","Straightening the index finger"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The tendon that inserts on the base of metacarpal 1 belongs to abductor pollicis longus. Pulling on the first metacarpal swings the thumb away from the palm, so cutting this tendon weakens thumb abduction. Some abduction remains because abductor pollicis brevis, a short thenar muscle in the hand, makes a similar movement.","notes":["Correct. Abductor pollicis longus moves the thumb away from the palm.","Bending the thumb tip is the job of flexor pollicis longus on the palm side. A cut on the back of the wrist would not affect it.","Tilting the wrist toward the little finger is done by extensor carpi ulnaris and flexor carpi ulnaris on the opposite side of the wrist from this cut.","Straightening the index finger depends on extensor digitorum and extensor indicis, which insert on the extensor expansion of digit 2, not on metacarpal 1."],"comp":86,"competencyId":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments"},{"id":"N-m3-forearm-posterior-40","dok":3,"q":"When a patient extends the wrist, the hand drifts toward the thumb side instead of lifting straight back. Weakness of which muscle best explains this?","a":"Extensor carpi ulnaris.","options":["Extensor digitorum","Extensor carpi radialis longus","Extensor carpi ulnaris","Flexor carpi ulnaris"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Straight wrist extension depends on balanced pull from the radial extensors on the thumb side and extensor carpi ulnaris on the little finger side. If extensor carpi ulnaris is weak, the radial extensors pull unopposed, so the hand extends and tilts toward the thumb at the same time. Picturing each tendon as a pull on one corner of the wrist lets you predict the drift.","notes":["Extensor digitorum pulls through the middle of the back of the hand toward all four fingers, so its weakness would weaken extension without tilting the hand to one side.","Weak extensor carpi radialis longus would reduce the pull on the thumb side, so extensor carpi ulnaris would tend to tilt the extending hand toward the little finger. Pick it if the scenario described that ulnar drift.","Correct. Without extensor carpi ulnaris, the radial extensors tilt the extending hand toward the thumb.","Flexor carpi ulnaris is on the front of the forearm and flexes the wrist, so it does not take part in wrist extension. Choose it if the hand drifted toward the thumb during wrist flexion."],"comp":86,"competencyId":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments"}]},{"id":"m3-hand-muscles","title":"Hand muscles and wrist landmarks","summary":"thenar and hypothenar muscles, lumbricals, interossei, adductor pollicis, cubital fossa, carpal tunnel, retinacula, palmar aponeurosis, anatomical snuffbox, extensor expansion","mw":"2026-10-19","tr":"2026-10-15","cards":[{"id":"L00738","dok":2,"q":"After a fall on an outstretched hand, a clinician presses into the anatomical snuffbox to check for a scaphoid fracture. Where is the anatomical snuffbox?","a":"The hollow on the back of the wrist at the base of the thumb.","options":["The groove between the tubercles of the proximal humerus","The tunnel on the palm side of the wrist under a fibrous band","The hollow on the back of the wrist at the base of the thumb","The pit on the back of the humerus above the elbow"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The anatomical snuffbox is a small hollow on the back of the wrist at the base of the thumb, which shows clearly when the thumb is extended. The scaphoid lies in its floor. Tenderness there after a fall is the classic clue to a scaphoid fracture.","notes":["The groove between the tubercles is the intertubercular sulcus near the shoulder. It would be right for a question about the groove holding a biceps tendon.","The tunnel on the palm side of the wrist under the flexor retinaculum is the carpal tunnel. Pick it for a question about where the median nerve and flexor tendons enter the hand.","Correct. The snuffbox is the hollow at the base of the thumb on the back of the wrist.","The pit on the back of the humerus above the elbow is the olecranon fossa. Choose it for a question about where the olecranon rests when the elbow straightens."],"lt":"t-appendicular-skeleton-upper","lid":"c28-x2","comp":86,"competencyId":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments"},{"id":"N-m3-hand-muscles-1","dok":1,"q":"Which nerve passes through the carpal tunnel along with the long flexor tendons of the fingers and thumb?","a":"The median nerve.","options":["The radial nerve","The median nerve","The axillary nerve","The ulnar nerve"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The carpal tunnel is the passage between the carpal bones and the flexor retinaculum, a strong fibrous band that roofs it on the palm side of the wrist. The long flexor tendons and the median nerve travel through it into the hand. When the tunnel narrows, the median nerve is squeezed, which is carpal tunnel syndrome.","notes":["The radial nerve runs in the radial groove on the back of the humerus. Pick it for a question about a fracture of the humeral shaft.","Correct. The median nerve runs through the carpal tunnel.","The axillary nerve wraps around the surgical neck of the humerus near the shoulder. It would be right for a question about a fracture of the surgical neck.","The ulnar nerve passes behind the medial epicondyle at the elbow and enters the hand outside the carpal tunnel. Choose it for a question about the funny bone."],"comp":89,"competencyId":"w4-lab-ue-nerves"},{"id":"N-m3-hand-muscles-1-chunk32","dok":1,"q":"Which group of intrinsic hand muscles spreads the fingers apart (abducts them) away from the middle finger?","a":"The dorsal interossei.","options":["The lumbricals","The adductor pollicis","The palmar interossei","The dorsal interossei"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The dorsal interossei lie between the metacarpals on the back of the hand and pull the fingers away from the middle finger, which is the reference line for abduction in the hand. The memory aid DAB stands for Dorsal interossei ABduct, and its partner PAD stands for Palmar interossei ADduct. Together the two groups spread and close the fingers.","notes":["The lumbricals flex the knuckles (metacarpophalangeal joints) while extending the fingers. They would be the answer if the question asked which muscles produce the tabletop position of the hand.","The adductor pollicis is a single muscle in the web space that pulls the thumb toward the palm. Choose it if the question asked which muscle adducts the thumb.","The palmar interossei pull the fingers back toward the middle finger, which is adduction. Pick them if the question asked which muscles bring the fingers together.","Correct. The dorsal interossei abduct the fingers."],"comp":86,"competencyId":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments"},{"id":"N-m3-hand-muscles-1-new-m3-forearm-hand","dok":1,"q":"Which three muscles make up the thenar eminence, the bulge at the base of the thumb?","a":"Abductor pollicis brevis, flexor pollicis brevis and opponens pollicis.","options":["Abductor pollicis longus, extensor pollicis brevis, extensor pollicis longus","Abductor digiti minimi, flexor digiti minimi brevis, opponens digiti minimi","Lumbricals, palmar interossei, dorsal interossei","Abductor pollicis brevis, flexor pollicis brevis, opponens pollicis"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The thenar eminence is the fleshy pad at the base of the thumb, formed by three short intrinsic muscles that lie entirely within the hand. Abductor pollicis brevis moves the thumb away from the palm, flexor pollicis brevis bends it at the knuckle, and opponens pollicis rotates it across the palm to meet the fingers. All three carry pollicis in their names because they act on the thumb.","notes":["These three muscles act on the thumb but arise in the posterior forearm, so they are not part of the thenar pad. Choose them if the question asked which tendons outline the anatomical snuffbox.","These three muscles form the hypothenar eminence on the little finger side of the palm. Pick them if the question asked about the bulge at the base of the little finger.","The lumbricals and interossei are the midpalmar muscles that lie between the metacarpals and along the flexor tendons. Pick them if the question asked which intrinsic muscles move digits 2 to 5 at the knuckles or spread the fingers.","Correct. These three short thumb muscles form the thenar eminence."],"comp":86,"competencyId":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments"},{"id":"N-m3-hand-muscles-2","dok":1,"q":"Which muscle belongs to the hypothenar group?","a":"Opponens digiti minimi.","options":["Adductor pollicis","Opponens pollicis","Abductor pollicis brevis","Opponens digiti minimi"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The hypothenar group forms the fleshy bulge on the little finger (ulnar) side of the palm. Its three muscles are abductor digiti minimi, flexor digiti minimi brevis and opponens digiti minimi, and all of them carry digiti minimi in their names because they move the little finger. Opponens digiti minimi draws the little finger toward the thumb to help cup the palm.","notes":["Adductor pollicis lies deep in the web space between the thumb and index finger and pulls the thumb toward the palm. Choose it if the question asked which muscle squeezes the thumb against the side of the hand.","Opponens pollicis is a thenar muscle that rotates the thumb across the palm. Pick it if the question asked about the thumb rather than the little finger.","Abductor pollicis brevis is a thenar muscle that moves the thumb away from the palm. Pick it if the question asked about the thenar eminence.","Correct. Opponens digiti minimi is one of the three hypothenar muscles."],"comp":86,"competencyId":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments"},{"id":"N-m3-hand-muscles-3","dok":1,"q":"How many lumbrical muscles are in each hand?","a":"Four.","options":["Two","Five","Three","Four"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"There are four lumbricals, one for each finger from the index to the little finger. Each is a small, worm-shaped muscle (lumbricus means earthworm) that arises from a tendon of flexor digitorum profundus in the palm and inserts on the extensor expansion of its finger. The thumb has no lumbrical.","notes":["Two is too few, since each finger from digit 2 to digit 5 has its own lumbrical. This number comes from counting only part of the hand.","Five would include the thumb, but the thumb has no lumbrical because flexor digitorum profundus, the tendon the lumbricals arise from, does not go to the thumb.","Three is a common slip if you skip the index or little finger. Every finger from digit 2 to digit 5 has a lumbrical, which makes four.","Correct. There are four lumbricals, one for each of digits 2 to 5."],"comp":86,"competencyId":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments"},{"id":"N-m3-hand-muscles-4","dok":1,"q":"What do the lumbricals do to digits 2 to 5?","a":"They flex the metacarpophalangeal joints and extend the interphalangeal joints.","options":["Extend the metacarpophalangeal joints and flex the interphalangeal joints","Flex both the metacarpophalangeal and interphalangeal joints","Spread the fingers apart from the middle finger","Flex the metacarpophalangeal joints and extend the interphalangeal joints"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The lumbricals bend the knuckles (metacarpophalangeal joints) while straightening the finger joints (interphalangeal joints). The course notes put it simply: they flex the knuckles and extend the fingers. This produces the tabletop position you use to hold a pen or a playing card.","notes":["This is the reverse of the lumbrical action. It describes a claw-like position, which the fingers fall into when the small intrinsic hand muscles are not working.","Flexing every finger joint at once is the job of the long flexors, flexor digitorum superficialis and profundus, as in making a fist. Pick this if the question named those forearm muscles.","Spreading the fingers away from the middle finger is abduction, done by the dorsal interossei. Choose it if the question asked about the dorsal interossei.","Correct. The lumbricals flex the knuckles and straighten the finger joints."],"comp":86,"competencyId":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments"},{"id":"N-m3-hand-muscles-5","dok":1,"q":"Which intrinsic hand muscles lie between the metacarpals and spread the fingers apart?","a":"The dorsal interossei.","options":["Palmar interossei","Dorsal interossei","Lumbricals","Hypothenar muscles"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The dorsal interossei fill the spaces between the metacarpals and are felt from the back of the hand. They abduct the fingers, spreading them away from a line through the middle finger. The course notes give the memory aid DAB: dorsal interossei abduct.","notes":["The palmar interossei also lie between the metacarpals but on the palm side, and they adduct the fingers toward the middle finger. Pick them for squeezing the fingers together (PAD).","Correct. The dorsal interossei abduct the fingers.","The lumbricals lie in the palm alongside the flexor digitorum profundus tendons and flex the knuckles while extending the finger joints. Choose them for the tabletop position.","The hypothenar muscles form the bulge on the little finger side of the palm and move only the little finger. Pick them if the question asked about that bulge."],"comp":86,"competencyId":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments"},{"id":"N-m3-hand-muscles-6","dok":1,"q":"Abduction and adduction of the fingers are measured from which reference line?","a":"A line through the middle finger.","options":["The midline of the body","A line through the thumb","A line through the middle finger","A line through the little finger"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"In the hand, abduction means spreading a finger away from a line through the middle finger, and adduction means bringing it back toward that line. The course notes stress that this reference is the middle finger, not the midline of the body. That is why spreading the fingers is called abduction even for the index finger, which moves toward the thumb.","notes":["The body midline is the reference for most limb movements, such as raising the arm out to the side. Students choose it by habit, but the fingers use their own reference line.","The thumb moves in its own planes and is not the reference for the other fingers. Using it would make every finger movement toward the thumb look like adduction, which is not how the terms are used.","Correct. Finger abduction and adduction are measured from the middle finger.","The little finger sits at the edge of the hand, so it cannot serve as a central reference line. This choice usually comes from mixing up the reference line with the hypothenar side of the hand."],"comp":86,"competencyId":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments"},{"id":"N-m3-hand-muscles-7","dok":1,"q":"Where is adductor pollicis located?","a":"Deep in the web space between the thumb and the index finger.","options":["In the bulge on the little finger side of the palm","Between the metacarpals on the back of the hand","In the deep layer of the anterior forearm","In the web space between the thumb and index finger"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Adductor pollicis is a fan-shaped muscle that lies deep in the palm, in the web space between the thumb and the index finger. It pulls the thumb toward the palm, which is the movement you use to pinch a key between the thumb and the side of the index finger. The course notes list it as a single muscle of its own, separate from the thenar group.","notes":["The bulge on the little finger side is the hypothenar eminence, made of abductor digiti minimi, flexor digiti minimi brevis and opponens digiti minimi. Pick it if the question asked about the little finger muscles.","The spaces between the metacarpals on the back of the hand hold the dorsal interossei. Choose this if the question asked which muscles spread the fingers.","The deep anterior forearm holds flexor digitorum profundus, flexor pollicis longus and pronator quadratus. Adductor pollicis is an intrinsic muscle that lies entirely within the hand.","Correct. Adductor pollicis lies in the thumb web space."],"comp":86,"competencyId":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments"},{"id":"N-m3-hand-muscles-8","dok":1,"q":"Which structure forms the roof of the carpal tunnel?","a":"The flexor retinaculum.","options":["Extensor retinaculum","Flexor retinaculum","Bicipital aponeurosis","Palmar aponeurosis"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The carpal bones form a curved trough on the front of the wrist, and the flexor retinaculum is the strong fibrous band that bridges across the top of it. Together they create the carpal tunnel, a closed passage for the long flexor tendons and the median nerve. Because the retinaculum does not stretch, swelling inside the tunnel has nowhere to go.","notes":["The extensor retinaculum is a band across the back of the wrist that holds down the extensor tendons. Choose it if the question asked about the dorsal side of the wrist.","Correct. The flexor retinaculum is the roof of the carpal tunnel.","The bicipital aponeurosis is a sheet from the biceps brachii tendon that crosses the front of the elbow over the cubital fossa. Pick it if the question asked about the elbow rather than the wrist.","The palmar aponeurosis is a triangular sheet of deep fascia in the palm, lying beyond the tunnel and continuous with the palmaris longus tendon. Pick it if the question asked which fascia covers the palm."],"comp":44,"competencyId":"w2-hand-bones"},{"id":"N-m3-hand-muscles-9","dok":1,"q":"What passes through the carpal tunnel?","a":"The median nerve and the long flexor tendons of the fingers and thumb.","options":["The median nerve and the long flexor tendons of the fingers and thumb","The radial nerve and the extensor tendons of the fingers","The radial artery and the palmaris longus tendon","The ulnar nerve and the ulnar artery"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The carpal tunnel is a narrow passage on the front of the wrist, with the carpal bones forming its floor and sides and the flexor retinaculum forming its roof. Through it run the median nerve and nine tendons: four from flexor digitorum superficialis, four from flexor digitorum profundus and one from flexor pollicis longus. With so much packed into a space with rigid walls, swelling inside it presses on the median nerve.","notes":["Correct. The median nerve and nine long flexor tendons pass through the tunnel.","The extensor tendons run across the back of the wrist under the extensor retinaculum. Choose this if the question asked what the extensor retinaculum holds down.","The radial artery runs on the thumb side of the wrist, and the palmaris longus tendon passes in front of the flexor retinaculum. Neither goes through the tunnel.","The ulnar nerve and ulnar artery cross the front of the wrist on the little finger side, superficial to the flexor retinaculum, so they stay outside the tunnel. Pick them if the question asked what passes near the pisiform outside the tunnel."],"comp":44,"competencyId":"w2-hand-bones"},{"id":"N-m3-hand-muscles-10","dok":1,"q":"What does the extensor retinaculum do?","a":"It holds the extensor tendons against the back of the wrist.","options":["Holds the extensor tendons against the back of the wrist","Covers and protects the tendons in the palm","Forms the roof of the carpal tunnel","Spreads the extensor pull over the back of each finger"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The extensor retinaculum is a band of thickened deep fascia that wraps across the back of the wrist. The extensor tendons pass beneath it on their way to the hand, and it keeps them close to the bones so they do not lift away like a bowstring when the wrist extends. Retinaculum comes from a word meaning a band that holds something back.","notes":["Correct. The extensor retinaculum keeps the extensor tendons in place on the back of the wrist.","Covering and protecting the tendons in the palm is the role of the palmar aponeurosis, the triangular sheet of deep fascia continuous with palmaris longus. Choose it if the question asked about the palm.","The roof of the carpal tunnel is the flexor retinaculum, on the front of the wrist. Pick this if the question named the flexor retinaculum.","Spreading the extensor pull over each finger is the job of the extensor expansion, the tendon hood on the back of each finger. Pick it if the question asked where extensor digitorum inserts."],"comp":86,"competencyId":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments"},{"id":"N-m3-hand-muscles-11","dok":1,"q":"Which description fits the palmar aponeurosis?","a":"A thick triangular sheet of deep fascia in the palm, continuous with the palmaris longus tendon.","options":["A band across the back of the wrist that holds the extensor tendons in place","A flattened tendon hood over the back of each finger","A fibrous band across the front of the carpal bones that roofs the carpal tunnel","A triangular sheet of deep fascia in the palm, continuous with palmaris longus"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The palmar aponeurosis is a tough, triangular layer of deep fascia lying just under the skin of the palm. Its narrow end at the wrist is continuous with the tendon of palmaris longus, and it fans out toward the bases of the fingers. It protects the tendons, vessels and nerves beneath it and gives the palm a firm surface for gripping.","notes":["A band across the back of the wrist holding the extensor tendons is the extensor retinaculum. Choose it if the question asked about the dorsal wrist.","A tendon hood over the back of each finger is the extensor expansion. Pick it if the question asked where extensor digitorum, the lumbricals and the interossei insert.","A band across the front of the carpal bones that roofs the carpal tunnel is the flexor retinaculum. Pick it if the question asked about the carpal tunnel.","Correct. The palmar aponeurosis is the triangular fascia of the palm, tensed by palmaris longus."],"comp":86,"competencyId":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments"},{"id":"N-m3-hand-muscles-12","dok":1,"q":"Which tendon forms the medial (ulnar) border of the anatomical snuffbox?","a":"The extensor pollicis longus tendon.","options":["Flexor carpi radialis","Extensor pollicis brevis","Extensor pollicis longus","Abductor pollicis longus"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The anatomical snuffbox is the small hollow on the thumb side of the back of the wrist that appears when you extend the thumb. Its medial (ulnar) border is the tendon of extensor pollicis longus, and its lateral border is formed by the tendons of abductor pollicis longus and extensor pollicis brevis. The scaphoid lies in its floor and the radial artery crosses it.","notes":["Flexor carpi radialis runs on the front of the wrist, where its tendon is the landmark for the radial pulse. It does not border the snuffbox, which is on the back of the wrist.","Extensor pollicis brevis forms part of the lateral border of the snuffbox, together with abductor pollicis longus. Pick it if the question asked about the lateral border.","Correct. Extensor pollicis longus forms the medial border of the snuffbox.","Abductor pollicis longus forms the lateral border of the snuffbox with extensor pollicis brevis. Choose it if the question asked about the lateral border."],"comp":86,"competencyId":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments"},{"id":"N-m3-hand-muscles-13","dok":1,"q":"What is the extensor expansion?","a":"A flattened tendon hood over the back of each finger that carries the extensor pull to the phalanges.","options":["A tough band over the front of the carpal bones","A band across the back of the wrist that keeps the extensor tendons from lifting away","A triangular sheet of deep fascia across the palm","A flattened tendon hood over the back of each finger"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Each extensor digitorum tendon flattens out over the back of its finger into a hood called the extensor expansion (also called the dorsal digital expansion). The hood wraps the sides of the proximal phalanx and carries the extensor pull out to the middle and distal phalanges. The lumbricals and interossei also attach to it, which is how these small hand muscles help straighten the finger joints.","notes":["A tough band over the front of the carpal bones is the flexor retinaculum, the roof of the carpal tunnel. Pick it if the question asked about the carpal tunnel.","A band across the back of the wrist holding the tendons down is the extensor retinaculum. Pick it if the question asked what keeps the extensor tendons from bowstringing.","A triangular sheet of deep fascia in the palm is the palmar aponeurosis. Choose it if the question asked about the fascia that palmaris longus tenses.","Correct. The extensor expansion is the tendon hood on the back of each finger."],"comp":86,"competencyId":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments"},{"id":"N-m3-hand-muscles-14","dok":2,"q":"When the thumb is extended, which two tendons form the lateral (radial) border of the anatomical snuffbox?","a":"Abductor pollicis longus and extensor pollicis brevis.","options":["Extensor pollicis brevis and extensor pollicis longus","Flexor carpi radialis and palmaris longus","Abductor pollicis longus and extensor pollicis brevis","Extensor pollicis longus and extensor indicis"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Abductor pollicis longus and extensor pollicis brevis arise next to each other on the posterior radius and run together to the thumb, so their tendons lie side by side and form the lateral border of the snuffbox. Extensor pollicis longus takes a separate path from the ulna and forms the opposite, medial border. The hollow between them deepens when the thumb is extended because all three tendons tighten.","notes":["This pair takes one tendon from each border. Extensor pollicis brevis is lateral and extensor pollicis longus is medial, so together they bracket the snuffbox rather than forming one border.","Flexor carpi radialis and palmaris longus are on the front of the wrist. Choose them if the question asked about tendons you can see on the palm side of the wrist.","Correct. Abductor pollicis longus and extensor pollicis brevis form the lateral border.","Extensor pollicis longus forms the medial border alone, and extensor indicis heads to the index finger, not the thumb. Pick extensor pollicis longus by itself if the question asked about the medial border."],"comp":86,"competencyId":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments"},{"id":"N-m3-hand-muscles-15","dok":2,"q":"Which structure crossing the front of the wrist does not pass through the carpal tunnel?","a":"The ulnar nerve.","options":["Flexor digitorum profundus tendons","Median nerve","Flexor pollicis longus tendon","Ulnar nerve"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The ulnar nerve crosses the wrist on the little finger side next to the pisiform, passing superficial to the flexor retinaculum rather than under it. That keeps it outside the carpal tunnel. The median nerve and the long flexor tendons of the fingers and thumb all pass beneath the retinaculum, inside the tunnel.","notes":["The four flexor digitorum profundus tendons pass through the carpal tunnel beneath the superficialis tendons. Pick them if the question asked which tendons in the tunnel bend the fingertips.","The median nerve runs through the carpal tunnel beneath the flexor retinaculum, which is why it is compressed in carpal tunnel syndrome. Pick it if the question asked which nerve passes through the tunnel.","The flexor pollicis longus tendon is one of the nine tendons in the carpal tunnel, running on its thumb side. Choose it if the question asked which tendon in the tunnel bends the thumb tip.","Correct. The ulnar nerve passes superficial to the flexor retinaculum, outside the tunnel."],"comp":44,"competencyId":"w2-hand-bones"},{"id":"N-m3-hand-muscles-16","dok":2,"q":"Which muscles form the medial and lateral borders of the cubital fossa?","a":"Pronator teres medially and brachioradialis laterally.","options":["Biceps brachii medially and triceps brachii laterally","Flexor carpi ulnaris medially and extensor carpi ulnaris laterally","Pronator teres medially and brachioradialis laterally","Brachioradialis medially and pronator teres laterally"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The cubital fossa is the triangular hollow on the front of the elbow. Its upper border is an imaginary line between the medial and lateral epicondyles, its medial border is pronator teres, and its lateral border is brachioradialis. The two muscles converge below the elbow to form the point of the triangle.","notes":["Biceps brachii lies in the anterior arm and its tendon runs through the middle of the fossa, while triceps brachii is on the back of the arm. Neither forms a border.","Flexor carpi ulnaris and extensor carpi ulnaris both run down the ulnar side of the forearm, far from the front of the elbow. They do not outline the cubital fossa.","Correct. Pronator teres forms the medial border and brachioradialis the lateral border.","This swaps the two borders. Pronator teres starts at the medial epicondyle, so it must be the medial border, and brachioradialis runs down the thumb side of the forearm."],"comp":86,"competencyId":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments"},{"id":"N-m3-hand-muscles-17","dok":2,"q":"From lateral to medial, what is the order of the main structures inside the cubital fossa?","a":"Biceps brachii tendon, brachial artery, median nerve.","options":["Biceps brachii tendon, brachial artery, median nerve","Biceps brachii tendon, median nerve, brachial artery","Brachial artery, biceps brachii tendon, median nerve","Median nerve, brachial artery, biceps brachii tendon"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"From lateral to medial, the cubital fossa contains the tendon of biceps brachii, then the brachial artery, then the median nerve. The artery lies right beside the biceps tendon, which is why you feel for the brachial pulse just medial to that tendon when taking a blood pressure. The brachial artery divides into the radial and ulnar arteries just below the elbow.","notes":["Correct. Tendon, artery, nerve runs from lateral to medial.","This swaps the artery and the nerve. The median nerve is the most medial structure, with the brachial artery between it and the biceps tendon.","This places the artery lateral to the biceps tendon. The tendon is the most lateral of the three, and the artery lies on its medial side.","This is the correct order reversed, running from medial to lateral. Pick it if the question asked for the order starting on the medial side."],"comp":88,"competencyId":"w4-lab-ue-arteries-veins"},{"id":"N-m3-hand-muscles-18","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares the palmar and dorsal interossei?","a":"Palmar interossei adduct the fingers toward the middle finger; dorsal interossei abduct them away from it.","options":["Palmar interossei abduct the fingers away from the middle finger; dorsal interossei adduct them toward it","Palmar interossei adduct the fingers toward the middle finger; dorsal interossei abduct them away from it","Both groups flex the fingertips and differ only in location","Palmar interossei move the thumb; dorsal interossei move the little finger"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Both sets of interossei lie between the metacarpals, but they pull in opposite directions. The palmar interossei adduct, drawing the fingers together toward the middle finger, and the dorsal interossei abduct, spreading them away from it. The course notes use PAD and DAB to keep them straight: palmar adduct, dorsal abduct.","notes":["This reverses the two actions. Remember PAD for palmar adduct and DAB for dorsal abduct.","Correct. Palmar adduct and dorsal abduct (PAD and DAB).","Neither group reaches the distal phalanx, so neither flexes the fingertips. That job belongs to flexor digitorum profundus.","The interossei act on the fingers, measured from the middle finger. The thumb is moved by the thenar muscles and adductor pollicis, and the little finger by the hypothenar muscles."],"comp":86,"competencyId":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments"},{"id":"N-m3-hand-muscles-19","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares the thenar and hypothenar groups?","a":"Thenar muscles form the bulge at the base of the thumb and move the thumb; hypothenar muscles form the bulge on the little finger side and move the little finger.","options":["Thenar muscles extend the thumb; hypothenar muscles extend the little finger","Thenar muscles arise in the forearm; hypothenar muscles lie entirely within the hand","Thenar muscles form the thumb pad and move the thumb; hypothenar muscles form the little finger pad and move the little finger","Thenar muscles form the bulge on the little finger side; hypothenar muscles form the bulge at the base of the thumb"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The two groups mirror each other on opposite sides of the palm. The thenar group (abductor pollicis brevis, flexor pollicis brevis, opponens pollicis) forms the pad at the base of the thumb, and the hypothenar group (abductor digiti minimi, flexor digiti minimi brevis, opponens digiti minimi) forms the pad on the little finger side. Each has an abductor, a short flexor and an opponens.","notes":["Neither group contains an extensor. Thumb extension comes from extensor pollicis longus and brevis in the posterior forearm, and little finger extension from extensor digitorum and extensor digiti minimi.","Both groups are intrinsic muscles that lie entirely within the hand. The thumb muscles that arise in the forearm, such as abductor pollicis longus, are not part of the thenar group.","Correct. Thenar is the thumb side and hypothenar is the little finger side, with matching sets of muscles.","This swaps the two groups. The thenar eminence is the pad at the base of the thumb, and the hypothenar eminence is the pad on the little finger side of the palm."],"comp":86,"competencyId":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments"},{"id":"N-m3-hand-muscles-20","dok":2,"q":"Which thenar muscle is named for the movement of bringing the thumb pad across the palm to meet the fingertips?","a":"Opponens pollicis.","options":["Abductor pollicis brevis","Flexor pollicis brevis","Adductor pollicis","Opponens pollicis"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Bringing the thumb pad across the palm to touch the fingertips is called opposition, and opponens means the one that opposes. Opponens pollicis inserts on metacarpal 1 and rotates it so the pad of the thumb faces the fingers. This movement is what lets you pinch and pick up small objects.","notes":["Abductor pollicis brevis moves the thumb away from the palm. Its name describes abduction, so pick it if the question asked which thenar muscle lifts the thumb away from the hand.","Flexor pollicis brevis bends the thumb at its knuckle. Choose it if the question asked which thenar muscle flexes the metacarpophalangeal joint of the thumb.","Adductor pollicis pulls the thumb toward the palm, as in pinching a key against the side of the index finger. Pick it if the question described squeezing the thumb against the hand.","Correct. Opponens pollicis is named for opposition of the thumb."],"comp":86,"competencyId":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments"},{"id":"N-m3-hand-muscles-21","dok":2,"q":"The lumbricals flex the knuckles but extend the finger joints. Which attachments explain this?","a":"They arise from the flexor digitorum profundus tendons and insert on the extensor expansions.","options":["They arise from the metacarpals and insert on the palmar side of the distal phalanges","They arise from the medial epicondyle and insert on the middle phalanges","They arise from flexor digitorum profundus tendons and insert on the extensor expansions","They arise from the extensor digitorum tendons and insert on the flexor retinaculum"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Each lumbrical starts on a tendon of flexor digitorum profundus in the palm and runs along the thumb side of the knuckle to join the extensor expansion on the back of the finger. Because it passes in front of the knuckle (metacarpophalangeal joint), it flexes that joint, and because it pulls on the extensor expansion beyond the knuckle, it straightens the interphalangeal joints. Origin and insertion together explain the split action.","notes":["A muscle inserting on the palmar side of the distal phalanges would bend the fingertips, which is the action of flexor digitorum profundus. The lumbricals straighten the finger joints instead.","The medial epicondyle and middle phalanges are the attachments of flexor digitorum superficialis, a forearm muscle that flexes the finger joints rather than extending them.","Correct. A flexor tendon origin and an extensor expansion insertion give the lumbricals their split action.","The lumbricals arise from flexor tendons, not extensor tendons, and the flexor retinaculum is a band at the wrist that no hand muscle uses as its insertion. This choice reverses their origin and misplaces their insertion."],"comp":86,"competencyId":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments"},{"id":"N-m3-hand-muscles-22","dok":2,"q":"The flexor retinaculum stretches across the front of the wrist between which bony attachments?","a":"The pisiform and hamate medially, and the scaphoid and trapezium laterally.","options":["The styloid processes of the radius and ulna on the back of the wrist","The medial and lateral epicondyles of the humerus","The bases of metacarpals 2 and 5 on the back of the hand","Pisiform and hamate medially, scaphoid and trapezium laterally"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The flexor retinaculum is a strong fibrous band that bridges the carpal bones on the palmar side. It attaches medially to the pisiform and the hook of the hamate and laterally to the scaphoid and trapezium, turning the curved front of the carpal bones into a closed tunnel. Its arch keeps the long flexor tendons close to the wrist so they do not bowstring when the wrist flexes.","notes":["The back of the wrist is where the extensor retinaculum lies, not the flexor retinaculum. Choose a dorsal answer only if the question asked about the band holding the extensor tendons.","The medial and lateral epicondyles are at the elbow, where an imaginary line between them forms the upper border of the cubital fossa. Pick them if the question asked about the cubital fossa.","The bases of the metacarpals are farther out in the hand and on the back they receive the wrist extensors. They are not the anchors of a band that roofs the carpal tunnel.","Correct. The flexor retinaculum spans from the pisiform and hamate to the scaphoid and trapezium."],"comp":44,"competencyId":"w2-hand-bones"},{"id":"N-m3-hand-muscles-23","dok":2,"q":"Besides extensor digitorum, which intrinsic hand muscles insert into the extensor expansions of the fingers?","a":"The lumbricals and the interossei.","options":["Lumbricals and interossei","Flexor digitorum superficialis and profundus","Flexor carpi radialis and flexor carpi ulnaris","Abductor pollicis longus and extensor pollicis brevis"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The extensor expansion is the tendon hood on the back of each finger. Besides receiving the extensor digitorum tendon, it receives the small intrinsic muscles of the palm, the lumbricals and the interossei. Pulling on this hood from the side is how these little muscles help straighten the interphalangeal joints even though they lie in the palm.","notes":["Correct. The lumbricals and interossei join the extensor expansions.","Flexor digitorum superficialis and profundus insert on the palmar side of the middle and distal phalanges. They bend the fingers and never reach the back of the finger.","Flexor carpi radialis and flexor carpi ulnaris are forearm muscles that insert on metacarpal and carpal bones. They move the wrist, not the fingers.","Abductor pollicis longus and extensor pollicis brevis insert on the thumb, at metacarpal 1 and the proximal phalanx. Pick them if the question asked about the snuffbox."],"comp":86,"competencyId":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments"},{"id":"N-m3-hand-muscles-24","dok":2,"q":"The middle finger has a dorsal interosseous on each side but no palmar interosseous. Why does this arrangement make sense?","a":"The middle finger is the reference line, so moving it to either side is abduction.","options":["The middle finger is adducted by the lumbricals instead of the interossei","The middle finger is the reference line, so moving it to either side is abduction","The middle finger is moved sideways only by the extensor digitorum tendon","The middle finger cannot move side to side at all, so it needs no interossei"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Adduction in the hand means moving a finger toward the line through the middle finger. The middle finger cannot move toward itself, so it never adducts, and any sideways movement in either direction takes it away from its resting line, which counts as abduction. That is why it has two dorsal interossei (abductors) and no palmar interosseous (the adductors).","notes":["The lumbricals flex the knuckles and extend the finger joints. They do not move the fingers side to side, and the middle finger has nothing to adduct toward.","Correct. Because it is the reference line, every sideways movement of the middle finger is abduction.","Extensor digitorum straightens the fingers and does not move them sideways. Side-to-side movement of the fingers comes from the interossei.","The middle finger can move side to side, which you can check by wiggling it toward the index and ring fingers. The dorsal interossei on each side produce that movement."],"comp":86,"competencyId":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments"},{"id":"N-m3-hand-muscles-25","dok":3,"q":"A worker's flexor tendon sheaths swell inside the carpal tunnel, and she develops tingling in the thumb, index and middle fingers. Why is the median nerve compressed while the ulnar nerve is spared?","a":"The median nerve runs through the tunnel beneath the flexor retinaculum, while the ulnar nerve passes superficial to it.","options":["The median nerve runs on the back of the wrist, under the extensor retinaculum with the tendons","Only the median nerve runs under the flexor retinaculum; the ulnar nerve passes superficial to it","The ulnar nerve runs through the tunnel too but is cushioned by thicker connective tissue","The ulnar nerve ends in the forearm and never reaches the wrist or the hand"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The carpal tunnel has rigid walls: carpal bones below and the flexor retinaculum above. The median nerve travels through it with nine flexor tendons, so when the tendon sheaths swell, the nerve is squeezed against the retinaculum. The ulnar nerve crosses the wrist superficial to the flexor retinaculum, outside the tunnel, so the swelling does not reach it.","notes":["The median nerve crosses the front of the wrist, not the back. The structures under the extensor retinaculum are the extensor tendons.","Correct. Only the median nerve shares the tunnel with the swollen tendons.","The ulnar nerve does not travel in the carpal tunnel at all. This choice comes from assuming every nerve to the hand passes through the tunnel.","The ulnar nerve continues past the wrist into the hand, passing beside the pisiform. It is spared because of its path, not because it stops early."],"comp":89,"competencyId":"w4-lab-ue-nerves"},{"id":"N-m3-hand-muscles-26","dok":3,"q":"During a blood draw from the median cubital vein, a needle pushed too deep at the front of the elbow could reach which structures lying just under the bicipital aponeurosis?","a":"The brachial artery and the median nerve.","options":["The cephalic vein and the basilic vein","The radial nerve and the deep brachial artery in the radial groove","The brachial artery and the median nerve","The axillary artery and the axillary nerve"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The median cubital vein lies superficially across the cubital fossa. Directly beneath it, separated only by the thin bicipital aponeurosis, lie the brachial artery and the median nerve. That is why a blood draw is taken here and why the needle is angled shallowly and carefully.","notes":["The cephalic and basilic veins are superficial veins that the median cubital vein connects. They lie at the same shallow level as the vein being drawn from, not deep to the aponeurosis.","The radial nerve and deep brachial artery travel together in the radial groove on the back of the humerus. Choose them if the question asked what is at risk with a mid-shaft humerus fracture.","Correct. The brachial artery and median nerve lie just under the bicipital aponeurosis.","The axillary artery and axillary nerve are in the armpit and around the surgical neck of the humerus, far above the elbow. Pick them if the question asked about the shoulder region."],"comp":88,"competencyId":"w4-lab-ue-arteries-veins"},{"id":"N-m3-hand-muscles-27","dok":2,"q":"A student wants to feel the radial artery pulse in the anatomical snuffbox. After extending her thumb, where should she press?","a":"Between the extensor pollicis longus tendon and the tendons of abductor pollicis longus and extensor pollicis brevis.","options":["Between the flexor carpi ulnaris tendon and the pisiform on the little finger side","Between the flexor carpi radialis tendon and the palmaris longus tendon at the front","In the hollow between extensor pollicis longus and the two lateral thumb tendons","Between the extensor digitorum tendons to the index and middle fingers on the back"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Extending the thumb tightens the three thumb tendons and opens the snuffbox between them, with extensor pollicis longus on the medial side and abductor pollicis longus and extensor pollicis brevis on the lateral side. The radial artery crosses the floor of this hollow on its way to the back of the hand, lying over the scaphoid. Pressing gently in the hollow lets you feel its pulse against the bone.","notes":["The flexor carpi ulnaris tendon and pisiform are on the little finger side of the front of the wrist, where the ulnar artery runs. Pick this spot if the question asked where to feel the ulnar pulse.","On the front of the wrist the radial pulse is felt lateral to the flexor carpi radialis tendon, not between it and palmaris longus. This spot is also on the palm side, not in the snuffbox.","Correct. The radial artery crosses the snuffbox between these tendons.","The extensor digitorum tendons fan out over the back of the hand toward the fingers, well away from the base of the thumb. No major pulse is taken between them."],"comp":88,"competencyId":"w4-lab-ue-arteries-veins"},{"id":"N-m3-hand-muscles-28","dok":3,"q":"A clinician asks a patient to grip a sheet of paper between two straightened fingers while she tries to pull it away. Which muscles is this testing?","a":"The palmar interossei.","options":["Lumbricals","Palmar interossei","Dorsal interossei","Hypothenar muscles"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Holding paper between two straightened fingers requires squeezing the fingers together, which is adduction toward the middle finger. Adduction of the fingers is the job of the palmar interossei (PAD: palmar adduct). If they are weak, the paper slides out easily.","notes":["The lumbricals flex the knuckles and straighten the finger joints, producing the tabletop position. Choose them if the patient were asked to hold that position.","Correct. Squeezing the fingers together tests the palmar interossei.","The dorsal interossei do the opposite movement, spreading the fingers apart. Pick them if the clinician asked the patient to spread the fingers against resistance.","The hypothenar muscles move only the little finger. They do not squeeze two fingers together, so pick them only for a test of little finger movement."],"comp":86,"competencyId":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments"},{"id":"N-m3-hand-muscles-29","dok":3,"q":"In an older patient, a band of thickened tissue in the palm slowly pulls the ring and little fingers into a bent position that he cannot straighten. Which structure has thickened and shortened?","a":"The palmar aponeurosis.","options":["Extensor retinaculum","Extensor expansion","Palmar aponeurosis","Flexor retinaculum"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"This is Dupuytren contracture, a gradual thickening and shortening of the palmar aponeurosis, the triangular sheet of deep fascia under the skin of the palm. Its bands extend toward the fingers, so as they tighten they draw the fingers, most often the ring and little fingers, into flexion at the knuckles. The muscles and tendons are not the cause; the shortened fascia itself holds the fingers bent.","notes":["The extensor retinaculum is on the back of the wrist and holds down the extensor tendons. Pick it if the scenario described tendons bowstringing on the back of the wrist.","The extensor expansion is the tendon hood on the back of each finger. Damage to it would leave a finger unable to straighten actively, but it would not create a thick band in the palm.","Correct. A thickened, shortened palmar aponeurosis pulls the fingers into flexion.","The flexor retinaculum is the band across the front of the wrist that roofs the carpal tunnel. Its problems cause nerve compression in the tunnel, not a band in the palm pulling the fingers down."],"comp":86,"competencyId":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments"},{"id":"N-m3-hand-muscles-30","dok":3,"q":"After an injury to the back of the wrist, the extensor tendons lift away from the bones like a bowstring whenever the patient extends the wrist. Which structure was most likely torn?","a":"The extensor retinaculum.","options":["Palmar aponeurosis","Extensor expansion","Flexor retinaculum","Extensor retinaculum"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The extensor retinaculum is a band across the back of the wrist that holds the extensor tendons close to the bones. When the wrist extends, the tendons pull in a straight line between their origin and insertion, and the retinaculum is what stops them from lifting off the curve of the wrist. If it is torn, the tendons spring up under the skin, a sign called bowstringing.","notes":["The palmar aponeurosis is the fascia of the palm on the front of the hand. It does not hold down any tendons on the back of the wrist.","The extensor expansion is the tendon hood over each finger, beyond the wrist. Damage to it would affect straightening of that finger, not the position of the tendons at the wrist.","The flexor retinaculum does the same job on the front of the wrist for the flexor tendons. Pick it if the flexor tendons bowstrung when the wrist flexed.","Correct. A torn extensor retinaculum lets the extensor tendons bowstring."],"comp":86,"competencyId":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments"}]}]},{"id":"m4","n":4,"title":"Module 4. Respiratory, Lymphatic, Endocrine, Lower Limb and Digestive","exam":"Exam 4","extra":false,"topics":[{"id":"m4-upper-respiratory","title":"Nose, pharynx and larynx","summary":"nasal cavity, conchae, paranasal sinuses, pharynx regions, larynx cartilages, vocal folds, conducting vs respiratory zones","mw":"2026-10-26","tr":"2026-10-27","cards":[{"id":"L02097","dok":1,"q":"Which laryngeal cartilage forms the laryngeal prominence, the Adam's apple?","a":"The thyroid cartilage.","options":["The epiglottis","The cricoid cartilage","The arytenoid cartilage","The thyroid cartilage"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The thyroid cartilage is the large, shield-shaped cartilage at the front of the larynx. Its anterior ridge sticks forward as the laryngeal prominence, which can be seen and felt in the neck as the Adam's apple. It is one of the three single cartilages of the larynx and is made of hyaline cartilage.","notes":["The epiglottis is the spoon-shaped flap of elastic cartilage that folds over the laryngeal inlet during swallowing. It sits behind the tongue, not at the front of the neck, so it does not form the Adam's apple.","The cricoid cartilage is the complete ring just below the thyroid cartilage. It is smaller and lower, so it does not form the Adam's apple. Pick it for a question about the only complete ring in the airway.","The arytenoid cartilages are small paired cartilages at the back of the larynx that anchor and move the vocal folds. Because they sit at the back, they cannot form a ridge at the front of the neck.","Correct. The thyroid cartilage forms the laryngeal prominence."],"lt":"t-respiratory","lid":"c1","comp":99,"competencyId":"resp-larynx"},{"id":"L02101","dok":1,"q":"Which term names the vocal folds together with the opening between them?","a":"The glottis.","options":["The carina","The epiglottis","The glottis","The vestibular folds"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The glottis is the part of the larynx made of the true vocal folds and the opening between them. As air passes through this opening, the vocal folds vibrate to produce sound. Food is kept away from the glottis during swallowing when the epiglottis folds over the laryngeal inlet above it.","notes":["The carina is the internal ridge where the trachea forks into the main bronchi, far below the larynx. It has nothing to do with the vocal folds.","The epiglottis is the flap of elastic cartilage above the glottis that covers the laryngeal inlet during swallowing. Its name means above the glottis, which is why students mix the two up.","Correct. The glottis is the vocal folds plus the opening between them.","The vestibular folds are the false vocal cords, the pair just above the true vocal folds. The term asked for names the true vocal folds and the opening between them, so this upper pair is not the answer."],"lt":"t-respiratory","lid":"c5","comp":99,"competencyId":"resp-larynx"},{"id":"L02103","dok":1,"q":"Which flexible flap folds down over the laryngeal inlet during swallowing?","a":"The epiglottis.","options":["The soft palate","The epiglottis","The vocal folds","The thyroid cartilage"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The epiglottis is a spoon-shaped flap of elastic cartilage at the top of the larynx. During a swallow, the larynx rises and the epiglottis folds down over the laryngeal inlet, sending food back toward the esophagus instead of into the airway. Elastic cartilage lets it bend and spring back with each swallow.","notes":["The soft palate lifts to close off the nasopharynx during swallowing, which keeps food from entering the nasal cavity. Pick it if the question asked what blocks food from going up into the nose.","Correct. The epiglottis folds over the laryngeal inlet during swallowing.","The vocal folds vibrate to produce sound and sit below the laryngeal inlet. They are not the flap that covers the airway during swallowing.","The thyroid cartilage is the large shield that forms the Adam's apple. It is rigid hyaline cartilage and does not fold over the airway."],"lt":"t-respiratory","lid":"c7","comp":99,"competencyId":"resp-larynx"},{"id":"L02104","dok":1,"q":"Which scroll-shaped projections sit on the lateral walls of the nasal cavity?","a":"The nasal conchae.","options":["The vocal folds","The paranasal sinuses","The nasal conchae","The nasal septum"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The three nasal conchae (superior, middle, and inferior) are scroll-shaped projections on each lateral wall of the nasal cavity. They swirl incoming air so more of it touches the warm, moist mucosa, which warms, moistens, and filters it. The air passages beneath each concha are the meatuses.","notes":["The vocal folds are in the larynx and vibrate to produce sound. They are folds of tissue in the airway below the pharynx, not in the nasal cavity.","The paranasal sinuses are air-filled cavities inside the frontal, ethmoid, sphenoid, and maxillary bones that drain into the nasal cavity. They are spaces inside bone, not projections into the cavity.","Correct. The nasal conchae are scroll-shaped projections on the lateral walls.","The nasal septum is the midline wall that divides the nasal cavity into right and left halves. Pick it if the question asked what separates the two sides."],"lt":"t-respiratory","lid":"c8","comp":98,"competencyId":"resp-upper-tract"},{"id":"L02106","dok":1,"q":"The paranasal sinuses are named for the skull bones that contain them. Which set names those bones?","a":"The frontal, ethmoid, sphenoid, and maxillary bones.","options":["The nasal, lacrimal, zygomatic, and palatine bones","The vomer, mandible, maxillary, and temporal bones","The frontal, ethmoid, sphenoid, and maxillary bones","The frontal, parietal, occipital, and temporal bones"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The paranasal sinuses are air-filled cavities in the frontal, ethmoid, sphenoid, and maxillary bones, which surround the nasal cavity. They drain into the nasal cavity and share its mucous lining. Each sinus takes its name from the bone that holds it.","notes":["The nasal, lacrimal, zygomatic, and palatine bones are small facial bones without named paranasal sinuses. Pick this set for a question listing facial bones of the orbit and nose.","This set mixes one right bone with three wrong ones. The maxilla does hold a sinus, but the vomer, mandible, and temporal bone have no paranasal sinuses. The vomer forms part of the nasal septum, which may be why students link it to the sinuses.","Correct. The frontal, ethmoid, sphenoid, and maxillary bones hold the paranasal sinuses.","Only the frontal bone in this set holds a paranasal sinus. The parietal, occipital, and temporal bones form the sides, back, and base of the cranium, away from the nasal cavity."],"lt":"t-respiratory","lid":"c10","comp":98,"competencyId":"resp-upper-tract"},{"id":"L02109","dok":1,"q":"Which laryngeal cartilages anchor and move the vocal folds?","a":"The arytenoid cartilages.","options":["The cuneiform cartilages","The arytenoid cartilages","The cricoid cartilage","The corniculate cartilages"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The arytenoid cartilages are a pair of small cartilages at the back of the larynx. The vocal ligaments run from the arytenoids forward to the thyroid cartilage, so when the arytenoids move, they open, close, and tighten the vocal folds. They are the most important of the three pairs of laryngeal cartilages.","notes":["The cuneiform cartilages are a small pair in the fold between the arytenoid and the epiglottis. Pick them only if the question asked which paired cartilages sit in that fold.","Correct. The arytenoid cartilages anchor and move the vocal folds.","The cricoid cartilage is the single, complete ring at the bottom of the larynx that the arytenoids rest on. Pick it for a question about the only complete cartilage ring in the airway.","The corniculate cartilages are a small pair that sit on top of the arytenoids. They are neighbors of the arytenoids but do not anchor the vocal folds."],"lt":"t-respiratory","lid":"c13","comp":99,"competencyId":"resp-larynx"},{"id":"L02111","dok":2,"q":"The nose, nasal cavity, paranasal sinuses, and pharynx together belong to which division of the respiratory system?","a":"The upper respiratory tract.","options":["The upper respiratory tract","The respiratory zone","The lower respiratory tract","The bronchial tree"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The respiratory system is grouped by location into upper and lower tracts. The upper respiratory tract is the nose, nasal cavity, paranasal sinuses, and pharynx, which take in air and condition it. The lower tract begins at the larynx and includes the trachea, bronchi, and lungs.","notes":["Correct. These four structures make up the upper respiratory tract.","The respiratory zone is the group of structures where gas exchange happens: the respiratory bronchioles, alveolar ducts, and alveoli. That grouping is by function, and none of these upper structures exchange gases.","The lower respiratory tract is the larynx, trachea, bronchi, and lungs. Pick it if the list started at the larynx and went down into the lungs.","The bronchial tree is the branching airway system below the larynx, from the trachea down. The nose and pharynx are above it."],"lt":"t-respiratory","lid":"c15","comp":97,"competencyId":"resp-zones"},{"id":"L02112","dok":2,"q":"The respiratory bronchioles, alveolar ducts, and alveoli together make up which functional zone?","a":"The respiratory zone.","options":["The upper respiratory tract","The pleural cavity","The respiratory zone","The conducting zone"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The respiratory system is also grouped by function into two zones. The respiratory zone is made of the structures where gas exchange happens: the respiratory bronchioles, alveolar ducts, and alveoli. Everything from the nose to the terminal bronchioles only carries air and forms the conducting zone.","notes":["The upper respiratory tract is a location grouping: the nose, nasal cavity, paranasal sinuses, and pharynx. These structures sit far above the alveoli.","The pleural cavity is the thin, fluid-filled space between the parietal and visceral pleurae. It surrounds the lung but is not part of the airway.","Correct. These structures make up the respiratory zone, where gas exchange happens.","The conducting zone is the passages that only carry air, from the nose down to the terminal bronchioles. Pick it if the list ended at the terminal bronchioles."],"lt":"t-respiratory","lid":"c16","comp":97,"competencyId":"resp-zones"},{"id":"L02115","dok":2,"q":"Which division contains the passages that only carry air, from the nose to the terminal bronchioles?","a":"The conducting zone.","options":["The lower respiratory tract","The respiratory zone","The bronchial tree","The conducting zone"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The conducting zone includes every passage that carries air without exchanging gases, from the nose to the terminal bronchioles. Along the way it warms, moistens, and filters the incoming air. Gas exchange only begins in the respiratory zone, starting with the respiratory bronchioles.","notes":["The lower respiratory tract runs from the larynx to the lungs and includes the alveoli. It starts below the nose and also contains gas exchange structures, so it does not match this description.","The respiratory zone is where gas exchange happens, in the respiratory bronchioles, alveolar ducts, and alveoli. It begins just after the terminal bronchioles.","The bronchial tree starts below the larynx at the trachea. It does not include the nose, nasal cavity, or pharynx.","Correct. The conducting zone carries air from the nose to the terminal bronchioles."],"lt":"t-respiratory","lid":"c19","comp":97,"competencyId":"resp-zones"},{"id":"L02121","dok":2,"q":"Which region of the pharynx carries air only, and never food?","a":"The nasopharynx.","options":["The esophagus","The oropharynx","The nasopharynx","The laryngopharynx"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The nasopharynx lies behind the nasal cavity and above the soft palate. During swallowing the soft palate lifts to close it off, so food does not enter, and it carries air only. Because it never meets food, it is lined with pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium rather than the tougher stratified squamous of the lower regions.","notes":["The esophagus is a digestive tube carrying food only, below the laryngopharynx. It is not part of the pharynx and does not carry air.","The oropharynx lies behind the oral cavity and carries both air and food. Its stratified squamous lining resists the friction of swallowed food.","Correct. The nasopharynx is the air-only region of the pharynx.","The laryngopharynx carries both air and food, and it is where the air and food paths divide. Pick it for a question about the region opening into both the larynx and the esophagus."],"lt":"t-respiratory","lid":"c25","comp":98,"competencyId":"resp-upper-tract"},{"id":"L02124","dok":2,"q":"Which of these structures belongs to the upper respiratory tract?","a":"The pharynx.","options":["The larynx","The bronchi","The trachea","The pharynx"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The upper respiratory tract is the nose, nasal cavity, paranasal sinuses, and pharynx. The lower tract begins at the larynx and continues with the trachea, bronchi, and lungs. The pharynx is the last structure of the upper tract, and the larynx is the first of the lower tract.","notes":["The larynx is the first structure of the lower respiratory tract, just below the pharynx. Students often place it in the upper tract because it sits in the neck.","The bronchi branch from the trachea in the thorax and belong to the lower respiratory tract. Pick them for a question about the lower tract.","The trachea belongs to the lower respiratory tract, running from the larynx into the thorax. Pick it as part of the lower tract.","Correct. The pharynx is part of the upper respiratory tract."],"lt":"t-respiratory","lid":"c28","comp":97,"competencyId":"resp-zones"},{"id":"L02128","dok":3,"q":"An emergency airway is placed just above a single, ring-shaped cartilage that lies below the thyroid cartilage. Which cartilage is this ring?","a":"The cricoid cartilage.","options":["A tracheal cartilage","The cricoid cartilage","The epiglottis","The arytenoid cartilage"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The cricoid cartilage is a single ring of hyaline cartilage at the bottom of the larynx, directly below the thyroid cartilage. It is the only complete ring of cartilage in the airway. The cricothyroid ligament fills the gap between the thyroid and cricoid cartilages, and it is the landmark for an emergency airway.","notes":["Tracheal cartilages are C-shaped and open at the back, not complete rings. They lie below the cricoid cartilage, in the trachea.","Correct. The cricoid cartilage is the complete ring below the thyroid cartilage.","The epiglottis is the flap of elastic cartilage at the top of the larynx. It sits above the thyroid cartilage, not below it.","The arytenoid cartilages are small paired cartilages that anchor the vocal folds. They are not ring-shaped and are not used as the emergency airway landmark."],"lt":"t-respiratory","lid":"c32","comp":99,"competencyId":"resp-larynx"},{"id":"L02129","dok":3,"q":"Food and air travel together through one region of the pharynx until it opens into both the larynx and the esophagus. Which region is it?","a":"The laryngopharynx.","options":["The oropharynx","The laryngopharynx","The nasal vestibule","The nasopharynx"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The laryngopharynx runs from the epiglottis down to the esophagus. At its lower end, the air path continues forward into the larynx and the food path continues back into the esophagus. It is where the two paths divide.","notes":["The oropharynx lies behind the oral cavity, from the soft palate to the epiglottis. It carries air and food but does not open directly into the larynx and esophagus.","Correct. The laryngopharynx opens into both the larynx and the esophagus.","The nasal vestibule is the skin-lined area just inside the nostrils. Pick it for a question about the coarse hairs that filter air at the nose.","The nasopharynx sits behind the nasal cavity, above the soft palate, and carries air only. Pick it for a question about the air-only region."],"lt":"t-respiratory","lid":"c33","comp":98,"competencyId":"resp-upper-tract"},{"id":"L02142","dok":2,"q":"Which sequence correctly orders the structures a breath travels through from outside the body toward the lungs?","a":"Nasal cavity, pharynx, larynx, trachea.","options":["Nasal cavity, pharynx, trachea, larynx","Nasal cavity, larynx, pharynx, trachea","Nasal cavity, pharynx, larynx, trachea","Pharynx, nasal cavity, larynx, trachea"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Air enters through the nostrils into the nasal cavity, then passes back through the choanae into the pharynx. From the pharynx it moves forward into the larynx, and below the larynx it continues into the trachea. The pharynx is the last part of the upper tract and the larynx is the first part of the lower tract.","notes":["This swaps the larynx and trachea. The larynx sits above the trachea and is entered first.","This swaps the pharynx and larynx. Air reaches the pharynx, the shared throat, before it enters the larynx.","Correct. Nasal cavity, pharynx, larynx, then trachea.","The nasal cavity comes first because air enters through the nostrils. The pharynx lies behind the nasal cavity, so it cannot come before it."],"lt":"t-respiratory","lid":"c46","comp":97,"competencyId":"resp-zones"},{"id":"L02144","dok":2,"q":"Moving downward along the front of the neck, which order of airway structures is correct?","a":"Thyroid cartilage, cricoid cartilage, trachea.","options":["Thyroid cartilage, trachea, cricoid cartilage","Thyroid cartilage, cricoid cartilage, trachea","Trachea, cricoid cartilage, thyroid cartilage","Cricoid cartilage, thyroid cartilage, trachea"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The thyroid cartilage forms the Adam's apple near the top of the larynx. Just below it sits the cricoid cartilage, the complete ring at the bottom of the larynx. Below the cricoid, the trachea begins with its C-shaped cartilage rings.","notes":["The cricoid cartilage is part of the larynx and sits above the trachea. Placing it below the trachea mixes it up with the tracheal rings.","Correct. The thyroid cartilage is above the cricoid cartilage, which sits above the trachea.","This order runs from bottom to top. It would be correct only if the question asked for the order moving upward.","This swaps the two laryngeal cartilages. The cricoid lies below the thyroid cartilage, not above it."],"lt":"t-respiratory","lid":"c48","comp":99,"competencyId":"resp-larynx"},{"id":"L02147","dok":1,"q":"Which structures make up the upper respiratory tract?","a":"The nose, nasal cavity, paranasal sinuses, and pharynx.","options":["The nose, nasal cavity, paranasal sinuses, and pharynx","The trachea, carina, and main bronchi","The larynx, trachea, bronchi, and lungs","The respiratory bronchioles, alveolar ducts, and alveoli"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The upper respiratory tract is the part of the system above the larynx: the nose, nasal cavity, paranasal sinuses, and pharynx. These structures take in air and warm, moisten, and filter it. The lower tract begins at the larynx.","notes":["Correct. The nose, nasal cavity, paranasal sinuses, and pharynx form the upper tract.","The trachea, carina, and main bronchi are the top of the bronchial tree in the lower tract. Pick this set for a question about where the trachea divides.","The larynx, trachea, bronchi, and lungs make up the lower respiratory tract. Pick this set if the question asked about the lower tract.","The respiratory bronchioles, alveolar ducts, and alveoli form the respiratory zone, a functional grouping where gas exchange happens. Pick this set for a question about the respiratory zone."],"lt":"t-respiratory","lid":"c1-x2","comp":97,"competencyId":"resp-zones"},{"id":"L02149","dok":1,"q":"Besides carrying air, what does the conducting zone do to incoming air?","a":"It warms, moistens, and filters the air.","options":["It warms, moistens, and filters the air","It produces sound along its entire length","It secretes surfactant into the air","It exchanges oxygen and carbon dioxide with the blood"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The conducting zone runs from the nose to the terminal bronchioles and carries air without exchanging gases. Along the way its mucosa warms and moistens the air, and mucus and cilia trap and sweep away particles. By the time air reaches the alveoli it has been conditioned and cleaned.","notes":["Correct. The conducting zone warms, moistens, and filters incoming air.","Sound is produced only at the vocal folds in the larynx. The rest of the conducting zone carries air without making sound.","Surfactant is secreted by type II alveolar cells in the alveoli, which are in the respiratory zone. Pick type II cells for a question about surfactant.","Gas exchange happens only in the respiratory zone, in the respiratory bronchioles, alveolar ducts, and alveoli. Pick this job for the respiratory zone."],"lt":"t-respiratory","lid":"c3-x2","comp":97,"competencyId":"resp-zones"},{"id":"L02150","dok":1,"q":"What divides the nasal cavity into right and left halves?","a":"The nasal septum.","options":["The nasal septum","The nasal conchae","The soft palate","The hard palate"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The nasal septum is the midline wall of the nasal cavity. Its front part is septal cartilage, and its back part is bone, formed by the vomer and the perpendicular plate of the ethmoid. It splits the cavity into right and left halves, each opening to the outside through a naris.","notes":["Correct. The nasal septum divides the nasal cavity into right and left halves.","The nasal conchae are scroll-shaped projections on the lateral walls of each half. They swirl the air but do not divide the cavity in two.","The soft palate is the muscular posterior part of the palate that lifts to close the nasopharynx during swallowing. Pick it for a question about keeping food out of the nose.","The hard palate is the bony floor of the nasal cavity, separating it from the oral cavity below. Pick it if the question asked what separates the nasal cavity from the mouth."],"lt":"t-respiratory","lid":"c4-x2","comp":98,"competencyId":"resp-upper-tract"},{"id":"L02151","dok":1,"q":"Which tubes open into the lateral walls of the nasopharynx, the air-only region behind the nasal cavity?","a":"The auditory tubes.","options":["The auditory tubes","The nasolacrimal ducts","The bronchi","The parotid ducts"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The nasopharynx sits behind the nasal cavity and above the soft palate, and it carries air only. The auditory tubes, which connect the middle ear to the throat, open into its lateral walls, and the pharyngeal tonsil sits on its posterior wall. This connection is why throat infections can spread to the middle ear.","notes":["Correct. The auditory tubes open into the nasopharynx.","The nasolacrimal ducts drain tears from the eye into the nasal cavity, below the inferior concha. They open into the nasal cavity, in front of the nasopharynx.","The bronchi branch from the trachea in the thorax, far below the pharynx. Pick them for a question about the airways entering the lungs.","The parotid ducts carry saliva from the parotid glands into the oral cavity next to the upper molars. Pick them for a question about salivary drainage."],"lt":"t-respiratory","lid":"c5-x2","comp":98,"competencyId":"resp-upper-tract"},{"id":"L02152","dok":1,"q":"In which structure is the glottis found?","a":"The larynx.","options":["The trachea","The pharynx","The nasal cavity","The larynx"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The glottis is the true vocal folds together with the opening between them, and the vocal folds are inside the larynx, the voice box. The larynx sits between the pharynx above and the trachea below. Air must pass through the glottis to reach the trachea and lungs.","notes":["The trachea lies below the larynx and has C-shaped cartilage rings but no vocal folds. Pick it for a question about the windpipe.","The pharynx is the throat above the larynx, shared by air and food. It holds the tonsils but not the vocal folds.","The nasal cavity is at the start of the airway and holds the conchae and meatuses. Pick it for a question about where air is first warmed and filtered.","Correct. The glottis is part of the larynx."],"lt":"t-respiratory","lid":"c7-x2","comp":99,"competencyId":"resp-larynx"},{"id":"L02157","dok":1,"q":"What are the paranasal sinuses?","a":"Air-filled cavities in skull bones that drain into the nasal cavity.","options":["Scroll-shaped projections on the lateral walls of the nasal cavity","Air-filled cavities in skull bones that drain into the nasal cavity","Passages beneath the conchae that carry air through the nose","Posterior openings where the nasal cavity becomes the nasopharynx"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The paranasal sinuses are air-filled spaces in the frontal, ethmoid, sphenoid, and maxillary bones, arranged around the nasal cavity. They are lined with the same mucosa as the nasal cavity and drain into it. Because they connect, a nasal infection can spread into the sinuses.","notes":["Scroll-shaped projections on the lateral walls are the nasal conchae. Pick them for a question about the structures that swirl incoming air.","Correct. Paranasal sinuses are air-filled cavities in skull bones that drain into the nasal cavity.","The passages beneath each concha are the nasal meatuses, named superior, middle, and inferior. Pick them for a question about the air channels inside the nasal cavity.","The posterior openings into the nasopharynx are the choanae. Pick them for a question about where the nasal cavity ends."],"lt":"t-respiratory","lid":"c15-x2","comp":98,"competencyId":"resp-upper-tract"},{"id":"L02161","dok":2,"q":"The thyroid and cricoid cartilages are hyaline cartilage, but the epiglottis has to bend with every swallow. What type of cartilage forms the epiglottis?","a":"Elastic cartilage.","options":["Hyaline cartilage","Dense irregular connective tissue","Elastic cartilage","Fibrocartilage"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The epiglottis is made of elastic cartilage, which has many elastic fibers in its matrix. This lets it bend down over the laryngeal inlet with every swallow and then spring back to open the airway. Rigid hyaline cartilage could not bend this way over and over.","notes":["Hyaline cartilage forms the thyroid and cricoid cartilages, most of each arytenoid, and the tracheal rings. It is too stiff for a flap that bends with every swallow. Pick it for a question about cartilage that holds the airway open.","Dense irregular connective tissue forms the dermis and organ capsules. It is not cartilage at all.","Correct. The epiglottis is elastic cartilage, so it can bend and spring back.","Fibrocartilage is tough cartilage with thick collagen bundles, found in the intervertebral discs and the pubic symphysis. Pick it for a question about cartilage that resists compression."],"lt":"t-respiratory","lid":"c19-x2","comp":99,"competencyId":"resp-larynx"},{"id":"L02178","dok":3,"q":"A person laughs while swallowing, food slips into the larynx, and a coughing fit follows. Which structure normally folds over the laryngeal inlet to stop this?","a":"The epiglottis.","options":["The trachealis muscle","The epiglottis","The cricoid cartilage","The soft palate"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"During a normal swallow, the larynx rises and the epiglottis folds down over the laryngeal inlet, so food is directed into the esophagus. If the timing fails, as when someone laughs or talks while swallowing, food can slip into the larynx. The coughing fit is the airway trying to clear it.","notes":["The trachealis muscle closes the back of the C-shaped tracheal rings, below the larynx. It does not guard the laryngeal inlet.","Correct. The epiglottis folds over the laryngeal inlet during swallowing.","The cricoid cartilage is the complete ring at the bottom of the larynx. It holds the airway open but does not move to cover it.","The soft palate lifts to close the nasopharynx, which keeps food from going up into the nose. It does not cover the larynx."],"lt":"t-respiratory","lid":"d3-t-respiratory-6","comp":99,"competencyId":"resp-larynx"},{"id":"N-m4-upper-respiratory-1","dok":1,"q":"Which folds of the larynx are the false vocal cords, lying above the true vocal cords and playing no part in sound?","a":"The vestibular folds.","options":["The vocal folds","The vestibular folds","The aryepiglottic folds","The vocal ligaments"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The vestibular folds are the false vocal cords, sitting just above the true vocal folds. They help close the airway during swallowing but do not vibrate to make sound. The true vocal folds below them are the ones that produce the voice.","notes":["The vocal folds are the true vocal cords. They are pearly white and vibrate as air passes to produce sound, so they are the opposite of the false cords.","Correct. The vestibular folds are the false vocal cords.","The aryepiglottic folds are folds running from the arytenoid cartilages up to the epiglottis, forming the sides of the laryngeal inlet. They are not called the false vocal cords.","The vocal ligaments are elastic bands inside the true vocal folds, running from the arytenoid cartilages to the thyroid cartilage. Pick them for a question about the bands within the vocal folds."],"comp":99,"competencyId":"resp-larynx"},{"id":"N-m4-upper-respiratory-2","dok":1,"q":"What are the air passages beneath each nasal concha called?","a":"The nasal meatuses.","options":["The paranasal sinuses","The choanae","The nasal meatuses","The nares"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Each of the three nasal conchae overhangs a groove-like air passage called a meatus. The passages are named superior, middle, and inferior, matching the concha above each one. Air flowing through the meatuses swirls against the warm, moist mucosa, which helps condition it.","notes":["The paranasal sinuses are air-filled cavities inside skull bones that drain into the nasal cavity. They are spaces within bone, not passages beneath the conchae.","The choanae are the posterior openings where the nasal cavity becomes the nasopharynx. Pick them for a question about where air leaves the nasal cavity.","Correct. The nasal meatuses are the passages beneath the conchae.","The nares are the nostrils, the external openings at the front of the nose. Pick them for a question about where air enters the nose."],"comp":98,"competencyId":"resp-upper-tract"},{"id":"N-m4-upper-respiratory-3","dok":2,"q":"Air passes from the back of the nasal cavity into the nasopharynx. Through which openings does it pass?","a":"The choanae.","options":["The glottis","The choanae","The nares","The auditory tubes"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The choanae are the paired posterior openings of the nasal cavity. Air that entered through the nostrils crosses the nasal cavity and leaves through the choanae into the nasopharynx. From there it continues down through the oropharynx and laryngopharynx to the larynx.","notes":["The glottis is the vocal folds and the opening between them in the larynx. Air reaches it much later, after passing through the pharynx.","Correct. The choanae open from the nasal cavity into the nasopharynx.","The nares are the nostrils at the front of the nose, where air enters the nasal cavity. They are the opposite end of the nasal cavity.","The auditory tubes open into the lateral walls of the nasopharynx and connect it to the middle ear. Air does not pass through them on its way to the lungs."],"comp":98,"competencyId":"resp-upper-tract"},{"id":"N-m4-upper-respiratory-4","dok":2,"q":"The nasopharynx is lined with ciliated epithelium, but the oropharynx is lined with stratified squamous epithelium. Why does the oropharynx need this tougher lining?","a":"It carries food as well as air, and stratified squamous epithelium resists the friction of swallowed food.","options":["It carries air only, so it needs no cilia","It secretes surfactant to keep the throat open","It carries food as well as air and must resist friction","It is where gas exchange begins in the upper tract"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The oropharynx lies behind the oral cavity, so swallowed food passes through it along with air. Stratified squamous epithelium has many layers of cells that can be rubbed away and replaced, which protects against that friction. The nasopharynx carries air only, so it keeps the delicate ciliated lining that sweeps mucus.","notes":["Carrying air only describes the nasopharynx, which is why it keeps its ciliated lining. The oropharynx carries both air and food.","Surfactant is secreted by type II alveolar cells in the alveoli, not in the throat. Pick type II cells for a question about surfactant.","Correct. The oropharynx carries food, so it needs a friction-resistant lining.","No part of the pharynx exchanges gases. Gas exchange starts in the respiratory bronchioles, deep in the lungs."],"comp":98,"competencyId":"resp-upper-tract"},{"id":"L03798","dok":1,"q":"Which statement best describes the overall job of the respiratory system?","a":"It moves air in and out and brings that air close to the blood for gas exchange.","options":["It breaks down food and absorbs the nutrients into the blood","It pumps blood through the lungs and then out to the body tissues","It returns excess tissue fluid to the bloodstream in vessels","It moves air in and out and brings that air close to the blood"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The respiratory system is the set of organs that moves air in and out of the body and brings that air very close to the blood so gases can be exchanged. Everything from the nose to the alveoli is either a passage for air or a surface where air meets blood. The actual exchange of gases is a physiology topic, so in anatomy we focus on the structures that make it possible.","notes":["Breaking down food and absorbing nutrients is the job of the digestive system. Choose this if the question were about the alimentary canal and its accessory organs.","Pumping blood through the lungs and body is the job of the heart and blood vessels, the cardiovascular system. Pick this if the question asked what the heart does in the pulmonary and systemic circuits.","Returning excess tissue fluid to the blood is the job of the lymphatic system, which collects fluid in lymphatic vessels and empties it into veins near the heart. It would be the answer for a question about lymph.","Correct. The respiratory system moves air and brings it close to the blood for gas exchange."],"lt":"t-resp-airways","lid":"W3-601","comp":97,"competencyId":"resp-zones"},{"id":"L03799","dok":1,"q":"Which group of structures makes up the upper respiratory tract?","a":"The nose, nasal cavity, paranasal sinuses, and pharynx.","options":["Every passage from the nose to the terminal bronchioles","Respiratory bronchioles, alveolar ducts, and alveoli","Nose, nasal cavity, paranasal sinuses, and pharynx","Trachea, main bronchi, lobar bronchi, and lungs"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The upper respiratory tract is the set of structures in the head and neck that take in air and condition it: the nose, the nasal cavity, the paranasal sinuses, and the pharynx (throat). These parts warm, moisten, and filter air before it travels deeper. Everything below them, starting with the larynx in this course's grouping, belongs to the lower respiratory tract.","notes":["Every passage from the nose to the terminal bronchioles is the conducting zone, which is a grouping by function rather than by location. Students mix it up with the upper tract because both include the nose.","Respiratory bronchioles, alveolar ducts, and alveoli are the respiratory zone, where gas exchange happens. They would be the answer if the question asked which structures form the respiratory zone.","Correct. The nose, nasal cavity, paranasal sinuses, and pharynx make up the upper respiratory tract.","The trachea, bronchi, and lungs sit in the neck and chest and belong to the lower respiratory tract. Pick this group if the question asked about the lower tract."],"lt":"t-resp-airways","lid":"W3-602","comp":97,"competencyId":"resp-zones"},{"id":"L03801","dok":1,"q":"The conducting zone is made of passages that only carry air. Which stretch of the airway does it include?","a":"From the nose to the terminal bronchioles.","options":["From the trachea down to the alveoli","From the respiratory bronchioles to the alveoli","From the nose down to the larynx only","From the nose to the terminal bronchioles"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The conducting zone is every passage that carries air without exchanging gas, starting at the nose and ending at the terminal bronchioles. Along the way it warms, moistens, and filters the air. The terminal bronchioles are the last conducting airways, and the respiratory zone begins right after them.","notes":["The trachea to the alveoli starts too low and ends too far. It leaves out the nose and pharynx, which do conduct air, and it includes alveoli, which are gas-exchange structures. This is a mix of the lower tract and both zones.","The respiratory bronchioles to the alveoli is the respiratory zone, where gas exchange happens. Pick this if the question asked which structures form the respiratory zone.","The nose to the larynx is too short. The conducting zone keeps going through the trachea and the whole bronchial tree down to the terminal bronchioles. This range roughly matches the upper airway in the head and neck, which is why it can look right.","Correct. The conducting zone runs from the nose to the terminal bronchioles."],"lt":"t-resp-airways","lid":"W3-604","comp":97,"competencyId":"resp-zones"},{"id":"L03802","dok":1,"q":"Which group of structures makes up the respiratory zone, where gas exchange happens?","a":"Respiratory bronchioles, alveolar ducts, and alveoli.","options":["Respiratory bronchioles, alveolar ducts, and alveoli","Bronchioles, terminal bronchioles, and carina","Trachea, main bronchi, lobar bronchi, and segmental bronchi","Nasal cavity, pharynx, and larynx"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The respiratory zone is the part of the airway where the walls are thin enough for gas exchange. It begins with the respiratory bronchioles, which have a few alveoli budding from their walls, continues into alveolar ducts lined with alveoli, and ends in alveolar sacs and alveoli. Everything above the respiratory bronchioles only conducts air.","notes":["Correct. Respiratory bronchioles, alveolar ducts, and alveoli make up the respiratory zone.","Bronchioles and terminal bronchioles are still conducting airways, and the carina is the ridge where the trachea forks. This group is the end of the conducting zone. Students choose it because bronchioles sound close to the respiratory bronchioles.","The trachea and the main, lobar, and segmental bronchi are large conducting airways with cartilage in their walls. They would be the answer if the question asked for the upper part of the bronchial tree.","The nasal cavity, pharynx, and larynx are conducting passages in the head and neck. Pick them if the question asked for structures that warm, moisten, and filter air early in its path."],"lt":"t-resp-airways","lid":"W3-605","comp":97,"competencyId":"resp-zones"},{"id":"L03803","dok":1,"q":"Air entering the respiratory system through the nose passes through which openings first?","a":"The nares (nostrils).","options":["The glottis","The nares","The auditory tube openings","The choanae"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The nares are the external openings of the nose, the nostrils, so they are the first openings air passes through on its way in. Just inside them is the nasal vestibule, and then the air moves back through the nasal cavity. At the back of the nasal cavity the air leaves through the choanae into the nasopharynx.","notes":["The glottis is the vocal folds together with the opening between them, inside the larynx. Air reaches it much later, after the pharynx. Choose it if the question asked for the opening between the vocal folds.","Correct. The nares, or nostrils, are the first openings air passes through.","The auditory tube openings are in the lateral walls of the nasopharynx and connect the throat to the middle ear. They are not part of the air pathway to the lungs. They would be the answer if the question asked what opens into the nasopharynx from the ear.","The choanae are the posterior openings where the nasal cavity becomes the nasopharynx. Air passes through them at the back of the nose, so they come later. Pick the choanae if the question asked where air leaves the nasal cavity."],"lt":"t-resp-airways","lid":"W3-606","comp":98,"competencyId":"resp-upper-tract"},{"id":"L03805","dok":1,"q":"What is the name of the skin-lined area just inside the nostrils?","a":"The nasal vestibule.","options":["The nasopharynx","The nasal meatus","The nasal vestibule","The choanae"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The nasal vestibule is the entrance area just inside the nostrils. Unlike the rest of the nasal cavity, it is lined with skin, and it holds sebaceous (oil) glands and coarse hairs that catch large particles. It is the first part of the nasal cavity that air enters after passing through the nares.","notes":["The nasopharynx is the upper part of the throat behind the nasal cavity. It is reached only after air crosses the whole nasal cavity. Choose it if the question asked where air goes after the choanae.","A nasal meatus is a passage beneath one of the nasal conchae, deeper in the cavity along the lateral wall. Pick a meatus if the question asked about the air channels under the conchae.","Correct. The nasal vestibule is the skin-lined area just inside the nostrils.","The choanae are the posterior openings of the nasal cavity, the opposite end from the nostrils. They would be the answer if the question asked where the nasal cavity opens into the nasopharynx."],"lt":"t-resp-airways","lid":"W3-608","comp":98,"competencyId":"resp-upper-tract"},{"id":"L03806","dok":1,"q":"What are vibrissae?","a":"Coarse hairs in the nasal vestibule that filter particles","options":["Coarse hairs in the nasal vestibule that filter particles","Scroll-shaped bones on the lateral nasal wall","Cilia on the nasal respiratory mucosa that sweep mucus back","Mucus-secreting goblet cells of the nasal lining"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Vibrissae are the coarse hairs in the nasal vestibule, the skin-lined entrance just inside the nostrils. They act as a first, rough filter that catches large particles before air moves deeper into the nasal cavity. Finer particles are trapped later by mucus on the respiratory mucosa.","notes":["Correct. Vibrissae are the coarse hairs of the nasal vestibule that filter large particles.","Scroll-shaped projections on the lateral wall are the nasal conchae. They swirl air rather than filter it with hairs. Choose them if the question asked what increases air contact with the warm, moist lining.","Cilia are tiny hairlike projections on the cells of the respiratory mucosa that sweep mucus toward the pharynx. They are microscopic and sit deeper in the cavity. Pick cilia if the question asked what moves trapped mucus toward the throat.","Goblet cells are single-celled glands in the respiratory epithelium that secrete mucus. Their sticky mucus traps fine particles deeper in the airway, while the coarse hairs of the vestibule catch large particles at the entrance. They would be the answer if the question asked which cells make mucus in the airway lining."],"lt":"t-resp-airways","lid":"W3-609","comp":98,"competencyId":"resp-upper-tract"},{"id":"L03807","dok":1,"q":"What are the three scroll-shaped projections on each lateral wall of the nasal cavity called?","a":"The nasal conchae.","options":["The nasal septa","The paranasal sinuses","The nasal meatuses","The nasal conchae"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The nasal conchae are three curled projections (superior, middle, and inferior) on the lateral wall of each side of the nasal cavity. Their scroll shape makes incoming air swirl, which increases its contact with the warm, moist lining. That contact lets the air be warmed, moistened, and cleaned before it moves on.","notes":["The nasal septum is a single midline wall that divides the cavity into halves, not a set of three lateral projections. Pick the septum if the question asked what separates the right and left sides.","The paranasal sinuses are air-filled cavities inside the frontal, ethmoid, sphenoid, and maxillary bones, outside the nasal cavity. They drain into it but do not project from its wall. Choose them if the question asked about hollow spaces in the skull bones around the nose.","The nasal meatuses are the air passages beneath each concha, not the projections themselves. Pick the meatuses if the question asked for the channels under the conchae.","Correct. The nasal conchae are the scroll-shaped projections on the lateral wall."],"lt":"t-resp-airways","lid":"W3-610","comp":98,"competencyId":"resp-upper-tract"},{"id":"L03808","dok":1,"q":"What are the nasal meatuses?","a":"The air passages beneath each nasal concha.","options":["The external openings of the nose","The air passages beneath each nasal concha","The posterior openings into the nasopharynx","The air-filled cavities inside skull bones"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"A meatus is a passage, and the nasal meatuses are the grooved air channels that run beneath each nasal concha. They are named superior, middle, and inferior to match the concha above each one. Air flowing through these channels is forced against the moist lining, which helps condition it.","notes":["The external openings of the nose are the nares, or nostrils. Pick them if the question asked where air first enters the nose.","Correct. The nasal meatuses are the air passages beneath each concha.","The posterior openings into the nasopharynx are the choanae. They would be the answer if the question asked where the nasal cavity ends at the back.","The air-filled cavities inside skull bones are the paranasal sinuses. Most of them drain into the meatuses, which is why students mix the two up, but the sinuses themselves are spaces inside the bones."],"lt":"t-resp-airways","lid":"W3-611","comp":98,"competencyId":"resp-upper-tract"},{"id":"L03809","dok":2,"q":"How do the nasal conchae and meatuses help prepare incoming air?","a":"They swirl the air so it has more contact with the warm, moist lining.","options":["They divide the airflow into right and left streams","They close the nasopharynx during each swallow","They swirl the air over the warm, moist lining","They are the surface where gas exchange takes place"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The scroll-shaped conchae and the passages beneath them break the airflow into swirling currents. That turbulence pushes more of the air against the warm, moist mucosa, which warms it, adds moisture, and lets mucus trap particles. This is part of how the conducting zone conditions air before it reaches the delicate alveoli.","notes":["Dividing the airflow into right and left streams is done by the nasal septum, the midline wall of the nasal cavity. Pick this if the question asked about the septum.","Closing the nasopharynx during swallowing is the job of the soft palate, which lifts up to block food from entering the nose. Choose this if the question were about the soft palate.","Correct. The conchae and meatuses swirl air over the warm, moist lining.","Gas exchange takes place in the alveoli of the respiratory zone, far below the nose. The nasal cavity is entirely conducting zone. This choice confuses conditioning air with exchanging gases."],"lt":"t-resp-airways","lid":"W3-612","comp":98,"competencyId":"resp-upper-tract"},{"id":"L03810","dok":1,"q":"The paranasal sinuses are air-filled cavities in which four skull bones?","a":"The frontal, ethmoid, sphenoid, and maxillary bones.","options":["Occipital, ethmoid, sphenoid, and maxillary","Frontal, ethmoid, sphenoid, and maxillary","Frontal, parietal, sphenoid, and maxillary","Frontal, ethmoid, sphenoid, and mandible"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The paranasal sinuses are named for the four bones that hold them: the frontal, ethmoid, sphenoid, and maxillary bones. All four sit around the nasal cavity, which is why they are called paranasal (beside the nose). Each one is lined with mucosa and drains into the nasal cavity.","notes":["The occipital bone forms the back and base of the skull around the foramen magnum and has no paranasal sinus. This choice swaps out the frontal bone, whose sinus sits just above the eyes.","Correct. The frontal, ethmoid, sphenoid, and maxillary bones contain the paranasal sinuses.","The parietal bones form the top and sides of the cranium, far from the nasal cavity, and do not contain a paranasal sinus. Students pick this when they remember a four-part list but swap in a cranial bone.","The mandible is the lower jaw and holds the lower teeth, not a sinus. The maxilla, the upper jaw, holds the large maxillary sinus. This choice confuses the two jaw bones."],"lt":"t-resp-airways","lid":"W3-613","comp":98,"competencyId":"resp-upper-tract"},{"id":"L03811","dok":1,"q":"Where do the paranasal sinuses drain?","a":"Into the nasal cavity.","options":["Into the oral cavity","Into the oropharynx","Into the nasal cavity","Into the middle ear"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Each paranasal sinus is lined with mucosa that makes mucus, and that mucus drains through small openings into the nasal cavity. The sinuses sit in the bones surrounding the nose, so the nasal cavity is the nearest shared space. From there, cilia sweep the mucus back toward the pharynx to be swallowed.","notes":["The oral cavity is the mouth, separated from the nasal cavity by the hard and soft palates. The sinuses do not open into it. Pick the oral cavity if the question asked what lies below the hard palate.","The oropharynx is the middle part of the throat behind the mouth. Mucus from the sinuses eventually reaches the pharynx after cilia sweep it back, but the sinuses open directly into the nasal cavity. Pick the oropharynx if the question asked where food and air first share a passage below the soft palate.","Correct. The paranasal sinuses drain into the nasal cavity.","The middle ear connects to the nasopharynx through the auditory tube, not to the paranasal sinuses. Choose it if the question asked where the auditory tube leads."],"lt":"t-resp-airways","lid":"W3-614","comp":98,"competencyId":"resp-upper-tract"},{"id":"L03812","dok":1,"q":"Which structure is the throat, a muscular passage shared by air and food?","a":"The pharynx.","options":["The larynx","The trachea","The pharynx","The esophagus"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The pharynx is the throat, a muscular tube that runs from behind the nasal cavity down to the level of the larynx and esophagus. It carries both air and food, which is why it belongs to both the respiratory and digestive systems. It has three regions: the nasopharynx, oropharynx, and laryngopharynx.","notes":["The larynx is the voice box below the pharynx. It carries air only and works to keep food out. Pick it if the question asked which structure houses the vocal folds.","The trachea is the windpipe below the larynx, held open by C-shaped cartilage rings. It carries air only. It would be the answer if the question asked which airway leads to the main bronchi.","Correct. The pharynx is the throat, shared by air and food.","The esophagus is the muscular tube behind the trachea that carries food to the stomach. It carries food only. Choose it if the question asked where food goes after the laryngopharynx."],"lt":"t-resp-airways","lid":"W3-615","comp":98,"competencyId":"resp-upper-tract"},{"id":"L03813","dok":1,"q":"Which region of the pharynx lies behind the nasal cavity and above the soft palate?","a":"The nasopharynx.","options":["The nasopharynx","The nasal vestibule","The laryngopharynx","The oropharynx"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The nasopharynx is the uppermost region of the pharynx, sitting behind the nasal cavity and above the soft palate. Because the soft palate lifts to seal it off during swallowing, it carries air only. It also holds the pharyngeal tonsil and the openings of the auditory tubes.","notes":["Correct. The nasopharynx lies behind the nasal cavity, above the soft palate.","The nasal vestibule is the skin-lined entrance just inside the nostrils, at the front of the nasal cavity. It is not part of the pharynx. It would be the answer if the question asked where vibrissae are found.","The laryngopharynx is the lowest region, from the epiglottis down to the esophagus. Choose it if the question asked where the air and food paths divide.","The oropharynx is the middle region, behind the oral cavity, running from the soft palate down to the epiglottis. Pick it if the question asked which region lies behind the mouth."],"lt":"t-resp-airways","lid":"W3-616","comp":98,"competencyId":"resp-upper-tract"},{"id":"L03814","dok":1,"q":"Which region of the pharynx lies behind the oral cavity, from the soft palate to the epiglottis?","a":"The oropharynx.","options":["The larynx","The nasopharynx","The laryngopharynx","The oropharynx"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The oropharynx is the middle region of the pharynx, directly behind the mouth. It runs from the soft palate down to the epiglottis and carries both air and food. Because food rubs along it, it is lined with stratified squamous epithelium, and it holds the palatine and lingual tonsils.","notes":["The larynx is the voice box, a separate airway structure in front of the laryngopharynx, not a region of the pharynx. It would be the answer if the question asked which structure connects the pharynx to the trachea.","The nasopharynx sits above the soft palate, behind the nasal cavity, and carries air only. Pick it if the question asked which region holds the pharyngeal tonsil and auditory tube openings.","The laryngopharynx sits below the oropharynx, from the epiglottis to the esophagus. Choose it if the question asked where the pharynx opens into both the larynx and the esophagus.","Correct. The oropharynx lies behind the oral cavity, from the soft palate to the epiglottis."],"lt":"t-resp-airways","lid":"W3-617","comp":98,"competencyId":"resp-upper-tract"},{"id":"L03815","dok":1,"q":"In which region of the pharynx do the air and food paths divide, with openings into both the larynx and the esophagus?","a":"The laryngopharynx.","options":["The oropharynx","The trachea","The laryngopharynx","The nasopharynx"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The laryngopharynx is the lowest region of the pharynx, running from the epiglottis down to the esophagus. At its lower end, air turns forward into the larynx while food continues back into the esophagus. That is why it is described as the point where the air and food paths divide.","notes":["The oropharynx carries both air and food, but it sits higher, behind the mouth. The paths are still shared there. Pick it if the question asked which region holds the palatine tonsils.","The trachea is the windpipe below the larynx and is not part of the pharynx. It carries air only. It would be the answer if the question asked where air goes after the larynx.","Correct. The laryngopharynx opens into both the larynx and the esophagus.","The nasopharynx is the top region behind the nasal cavity and carries air only. Choose it if the question asked which region is sealed off by the soft palate during swallowing."],"lt":"t-resp-airways","lid":"W3-618","comp":98,"competencyId":"resp-upper-tract"},{"id":"L03816","dok":1,"q":"Which structure is the voice box that connects the pharynx to the trachea?","a":"The larynx.","options":["The laryngopharynx","The larynx","The carina","The epiglottis"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The larynx is the voice box, a framework of nine cartilages that connects the pharynx above to the trachea below. It keeps food out of the airway, holds the airway open, and houses the vocal folds that produce sound. In this course it is the first structure of the lower respiratory tract.","notes":["The laryngopharynx is the lowest region of the throat, behind the larynx. Its name sounds similar, but it is part of the pharynx and carries both air and food. Pick it if the question asked where the air and food paths divide.","Correct. The larynx is the voice box between the pharynx and the trachea.","The carina is the internal ridge where the trachea forks into the two main bronchi, well below the larynx. It would be the answer if the question asked where the trachea branches.","The epiglottis is one of the cartilages of the larynx, the flap that folds over the laryngeal inlet during swallowing. It is part of the larynx, not the whole voice box. Choose it if the question asked what covers the airway when you swallow."],"lt":"t-resp-airways","lid":"W3-619","comp":99,"competencyId":"resp-larynx"},{"id":"L03817","dok":1,"q":"Which pair of jobs belongs to the larynx?","a":"Keeping food out of the airway and housing the vocal folds.","options":["Keeping food out of the airway and housing the vocal folds","Exchanging gases and secreting surfactant onto the alveoli","Carrying food down to the stomach and starting the swallow","Swirling incoming air and draining the paranasal sinuses"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The larynx sits at the top of the airway where food and air paths have just separated, so one of its main jobs is guarding the airway, with the epiglottis folding over the inlet during swallowing. It also houses the vocal folds, which vibrate to produce sound, which is why it is called the voice box. Its cartilage framework also holds the airway open.","notes":["Correct. The larynx keeps food out of the airway and houses the vocal folds.","Gas exchange happens in the alveoli, and surfactant is secreted by Type II alveolar cells. Choose this pair if the question were about the alveolus.","Carrying food to the stomach is the job of the esophagus, and swallowing is driven by the muscles of the mouth and pharynx. This pair describes the digestive path, not the larynx.","Swirling incoming air is the job of the nasal conchae and meatuses, and the sinuses drain into the nasal cavity. Pick this pair if the question were about the nasal cavity."],"lt":"t-resp-airways","lid":"W3-620","comp":99,"competencyId":"resp-larynx"},{"id":"L03818","dok":1,"q":"Which laryngeal cartilage is the large shield-shaped one that forms the laryngeal prominence (Adam's apple)?","a":"The thyroid cartilage.","options":["The epiglottis","The thyroid cartilage","The cricoid cartilage","The arytenoid cartilage"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The thyroid cartilage is the largest cartilage of the larynx, a shield-shaped plate at the front of the neck. Its anterior ridge forms the laryngeal prominence, which you can feel as the Adam's apple. Like the cricoid and arytenoid cartilages, it is made of hyaline cartilage.","notes":["The epiglottis is the spoon-shaped flap of elastic cartilage that folds over the laryngeal inlet during swallowing. It would be the answer if the question asked which cartilage protects the airway when you swallow.","Correct. The thyroid cartilage forms the laryngeal prominence.","The cricoid cartilage is the ring-shaped cartilage just below the thyroid cartilage. Pick it if the question asked which cartilage is the only complete ring in the airway.","The arytenoid cartilages are small paired cartilages at the back of the larynx that anchor and move the vocal folds. Choose them if the question asked what controls the position of the vocal folds."],"lt":"t-resp-airways","lid":"W3-621","comp":99,"competencyId":"resp-larynx"},{"id":"L03819","dok":1,"q":"Which laryngeal cartilage sits just below the thyroid cartilage and is the only complete ring in the airway?","a":"The cricoid cartilage.","options":["The cuneiform cartilage","A tracheal cartilage","The cricoid cartilage","The corniculate cartilage"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The cricoid cartilage is a ring-shaped cartilage directly below the thyroid cartilage, at the base of the larynx. It is the only cartilage in the airway that forms a complete ring all the way around. The tracheal cartilages below it are C-shaped, open at the back where the trachealis muscle closes the gap.","notes":["The cuneiform cartilages are small paired cartilages in the fold between the arytenoid and the epiglottis. They would be the answer if the question asked which paired cartilages lie in that fold.","The tracheal cartilages are C-shaped rings of hyaline cartilage, open in the back. They are incomplete rings, which is the exact contrast with the cricoid. Choose them if the question asked what holds the trachea open.","Correct. The cricoid cartilage is the complete ring just below the thyroid cartilage.","The corniculate cartilages are small paired cartilages that sit on top of the arytenoids. They are tiny horn-like pieces, not rings. Pick them if the question asked which cartilages perch on the arytenoids."],"lt":"t-resp-airways","lid":"W3-622","comp":99,"competencyId":"resp-larynx"},{"id":"L03820","dok":1,"q":"Which laryngeal cartilage is the flexible flap that folds over the laryngeal inlet during swallowing?","a":"The epiglottis.","options":["The thyroid cartilage","The epiglottis","The soft palate","The vestibular fold"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The epiglottis is a spoon-shaped flap at the top of the larynx. During swallowing it folds back over the laryngeal inlet, the opening into the larynx, so food and drink slide past into the esophagus. It is made of elastic cartilage, unlike the stiff hyaline thyroid and cricoid cartilages, because it has to bend and spring back with every swallow.","notes":["The thyroid cartilage is the large, rigid shield at the front of the larynx that forms the Adam's apple. It does not fold over anything. Pick it if the question asked which cartilage forms the laryngeal prominence.","Correct. The epiglottis folds over the laryngeal inlet during swallowing.","The soft palate is a muscular flap at the back of the roof of the mouth, not a laryngeal cartilage. It lifts to close the nasopharynx during swallowing. Choose it if the question asked what keeps food out of the nasal cavity.","The vestibular folds are the false vocal cords, mucosal folds above the true vocal folds. They help close the airway but are folds of tissue, not a cartilage flap. They would be the answer if the question asked which folds sit above the true vocal cords."],"lt":"t-resp-airways","lid":"W3-623","comp":99,"competencyId":"resp-larynx"},{"id":"L03821","dok":1,"q":"Which paired laryngeal cartilages anchor the vocal folds and move them?","a":"The arytenoid cartilages.","options":["The cuneiform cartilages","The corniculate cartilages","The arytenoid cartilages","The cricoid cartilages"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The arytenoid cartilages are a pair of small cartilages at the back of the larynx, resting on the cricoid cartilage. The vocal ligaments attach to them, so when the arytenoids shift and pivot, the vocal folds open, close, and tighten. They are considered the most important of the three pairs of laryngeal cartilages.","notes":["The cuneiform cartilages are small paired cartilages in the fold between the arytenoid and the epiglottis. Choose them if the question asked which paired cartilages lie in that fold.","The corniculate cartilages are tiny paired cartilages that sit on top of the arytenoids. Being so close to the arytenoids is why students pick them. They would be the answer if the question asked which cartilages perch on the arytenoids.","Correct. The arytenoid cartilages anchor and move the vocal folds.","The cricoid is a single ring-shaped cartilage, not a pair, and the vocal folds do not attach to it. Pick the cricoid if the question asked for the only complete ring in the airway."],"lt":"t-resp-airways","lid":"W3-624","comp":99,"competencyId":"resp-larynx"},{"id":"L03822","dok":1,"q":"The true vocal cords are also known by which name?","a":"The vocal folds.","options":["The vocal folds","The laryngeal prominence","The vestibular folds","The glottis"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The true vocal cords are called the vocal folds. They are folds of mucous membrane that hold the vocal ligaments, and they look pearly white because their lining has very few blood vessels. They vibrate as air passes between them, and that vibration makes sound.","notes":["Correct. The true vocal cords are the vocal folds.","The laryngeal prominence is the Adam's apple, the ridge of the thyroid cartilage at the front of the neck. It would be the answer if the question asked which feature you can feel on the front of the larynx.","The vestibular folds are the false vocal cords, sitting just above the true folds. They help close the airway during swallowing and play no part in sound. Pick them if the question asked for the false vocal cords.","The glottis is the vocal folds together with the opening between them. It includes the folds but is a larger term. Choose it if the question asked for the folds plus the space between them."],"lt":"t-resp-airways","lid":"W3-625","comp":99,"competencyId":"resp-larynx"},{"id":"L03823","dok":1,"q":"What do the true vocal folds do?","a":"They vibrate as air passes to produce sound.","options":["They close the airway when swallowing but make no sound","They form the ridge of the Adam's apple on the neck","They vibrate as air passes over them to produce sound","They fold over the laryngeal inlet during each swallow"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The true vocal folds are the paired shelves of tissue inside the larynx that contain the vocal ligaments. When air pushed up from the lungs passes between them, they vibrate and produce sound. The arytenoid cartilages change their position and tension, which changes the sound.","notes":["Closing the airway during swallowing while playing no part in sound describes the vestibular folds, the false vocal cords above the true folds. Pick this if the question asked about the false vocal cords.","The ridge of the Adam's apple is the laryngeal prominence of the thyroid cartilage. It would be the answer if the question asked what the thyroid cartilage forms.","Correct. The true vocal folds vibrate as air passes to produce sound.","Folding over the laryngeal inlet during swallowing is the job of the epiglottis. Choose this if the question asked how the epiglottis protects the airway."],"lt":"t-resp-airways","lid":"W3-626","comp":99,"competencyId":"resp-larynx"},{"id":"L03832","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares the conducting zone and the respiratory zone?","a":"The conducting zone only carries and conditions air; the respiratory zone is where gas exchange happens.","options":["The conducting zone carries and conditions air, and the respiratory zone exchanges gases","Both zones exchange gases, but the respiratory zone has more cartilage","The conducting zone ends at the larynx, and the respiratory zone begins at the trachea","The conducting zone exchanges gases, and the respiratory zone only carries and conditions air"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The conducting zone is every passage from the nose to the terminal bronchioles, and its job is to carry air while warming, moistening, and filtering it. The respiratory zone starts at the respiratory bronchioles and includes the alveolar ducts and alveoli, where the walls are thin enough for gas exchange. The two zones are a grouping by function, which is different from the upper and lower tract grouping by location.","notes":["Correct. The conducting zone carries and conditions air, and the respiratory zone exchanges gases.","Only the respiratory zone exchanges gases, and it has no cartilage at all. Cartilage is found in the larger conducting airways and disappears at the bronchioles. Pick a cartilage-based contrast only if the question compared bronchi with bronchioles.","This describes the upper and lower respiratory tracts, which split near the larynx, not the two zones. The conducting zone continues all the way to the terminal bronchioles. Choose a larynx boundary only if the question were about the upper and lower tracts.","This statement reverses the two zones. Students fall for it because the word respiratory sounds like it should mean breathing air in, but the respiratory zone is where gas exchange happens."],"lt":"t-resp-airways","lid":"W3-670","comp":97,"competencyId":"resp-zones"},{"id":"L03833","dok":2,"q":"What does the upper respiratory tract do to incoming air before it reaches the lungs?","a":"It warms, moistens, and filters the air.","options":["It exchanges oxygen and carbon dioxide","It secretes surfactant onto the airway lining","It warms, moistens, and filters the air","It cools and dries the air to protect the alveoli"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The upper respiratory tract conditions air before it travels deeper. The nasal conchae swirl air over a warm, moist mucosa, which warms and humidifies it, while vibrissae, mucus, and cilia trap and remove particles. Conditioned air protects the delicate alveoli, which are easily damaged by cold, dry, or dirty air.","notes":["Exchanging oxygen and carbon dioxide happens in the respiratory zone, at the alveoli. The upper tract is entirely conducting. Pick this if the question asked what happens in the alveoli.","Surfactant is secreted by Type II alveolar cells deep in the lungs, not by the upper tract. Choose it if the question asked what keeps alveoli from collapsing.","Correct. The upper respiratory tract warms, moistens, and filters incoming air.","This is the reverse of what happens. The upper tract warms and moistens air, because cold, dry air is harder on the lower airways. This choice comes from mixing up the direction of the change."],"lt":"t-resp-airways","lid":"W3-671","comp":97,"competencyId":"resp-zones"},{"id":"L03834","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares the three regions of the pharynx by what they carry?","a":"The nasopharynx carries air only; the oropharynx and laryngopharynx carry both air and food.","options":["The nasopharynx carries air only, and the other two carry air and food","The laryngopharynx carries air only, and the other two carry air and food","All three regions carry both air and food the entire time","The nasopharynx and oropharynx carry air only, and the laryngopharynx carries food"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The nasopharynx sits above the soft palate, and the soft palate lifts during swallowing to seal it off, so it carries air only. The oropharynx (behind the mouth) and the laryngopharynx (from the epiglottis to the esophagus) carry both air and food. Their lining reflects this: the nasopharynx has pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium, while the two lower regions have tougher stratified squamous epithelium.","notes":["Correct. The nasopharynx carries air only, and the oropharynx and laryngopharynx carry air and food.","The laryngopharynx carries both air and food, since it is where the two paths divide into the larynx and esophagus. This choice flips the top and bottom regions. The air-only region is the one at the top, the nasopharynx.","The nasopharynx does not carry food, because the soft palate closes it off during swallowing. This choice ignores the job of the soft palate. Pick a both-paths answer only for the two lower regions.","The oropharynx sits right behind the mouth, so food passes through it with every swallow. Students pick this because the oropharynx is near the nose, but only the region above the soft palate is air-only."],"lt":"t-resp-airways","lid":"W3-672","comp":98,"competencyId":"resp-upper-tract"},{"id":"L03835","dok":2,"q":"Why is the epiglottis important during swallowing?","a":"It folds over the laryngeal inlet to keep food and drink out of the airway.","options":["It closes the gap at the back of each tracheal cartilage ring","It tightens the vocal folds so food cannot pass into the trachea","It folds over the laryngeal inlet so food stays out of the airway","It lifts to close off the nasopharynx so food stays out of the nose"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"During a swallow, the larynx rises and the epiglottis folds back over the laryngeal inlet, the opening into the larynx. That directs food and drink back into the esophagus instead of down into the trachea. It is made of flexible elastic cartilage so it can bend and spring back every time you swallow.","notes":["The gap at the back of each tracheal ring is closed by the trachealis muscle, not the epiglottis. It would be the answer if the question asked what lies between the trachea and the esophagus.","The vocal folds are moved by the arytenoid cartilages, and their main job is producing sound. This choice gives the epiglottis a job it does not have. Choose arytenoid movement if the question asked how the vocal folds are positioned.","Correct. The epiglottis folds over the laryngeal inlet to keep food out of the airway.","Lifting to close off the nasopharynx is the job of the soft palate. Pick this if the question asked what keeps food from going up into the nasal cavity."],"lt":"t-resp-airways","lid":"W3-673","comp":99,"competencyId":"resp-larynx"},{"id":"L03836","dok":2,"q":"The vocal ligaments inside the vocal folds run between which two cartilages?","a":"The arytenoid cartilages and the thyroid cartilage.","options":["The corniculate and cuneiform cartilages","The cricoid cartilage and the epiglottis","The thyroid cartilage and the epiglottis","The arytenoids and the thyroid cartilage"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The vocal ligaments are elastic bands inside the vocal folds that stretch from the arytenoid cartilages at the back of the larynx to the thyroid cartilage at the front. Because they are anchored this way, movement of the arytenoids opens, closes, and tightens the vocal folds. That is how the arytenoids control voice production.","notes":["The corniculate cartilages sit on the arytenoids, and the cuneiform cartilages lie in the fold between the arytenoid and epiglottis. They are small supporting pieces. This choice confuses them with the arytenoids because all three are paired.","The cricoid is the complete ring at the base of the larynx, and the epiglottis is the flap at the top. The vocal ligaments do not stretch between them. Pick this pair only if the question asked for the lowest and highest laryngeal cartilages.","The thyroid cartilage is the front anchor of the vocal ligaments, but the epiglottis is not involved in sound. Choose the epiglottis if the question asked what covers the laryngeal inlet during swallowing.","Correct. The vocal ligaments run from the arytenoid cartilages to the thyroid cartilage."],"lt":"t-resp-airways","lid":"W3-674","comp":99,"competencyId":"resp-larynx"},{"id":"L03841","dok":3,"q":"A patient coughs violently after a sip of water goes down the wrong way and enters the laryngeal inlet. Which structure failed to seal the airway during that swallow?","a":"The epiglottis.","options":["The epiglottis","The soft palate","The thyroid cartilage","The trachealis muscle"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"During a normal swallow, the epiglottis folds back over the laryngeal inlet so food and liquid slide past into the esophagus. When something slips past it into the larynx, the airway responds with a coughing fit to clear it. So liquid entering the laryngeal inlet means the epiglottis did not seal the opening in time.","notes":["Correct. The epiglottis normally folds over the laryngeal inlet during swallowing.","The soft palate lifts to close off the nasopharynx during swallowing. If it failed, liquid would go up into the nose instead. Pick the soft palate if the question described water coming out of the nose.","The thyroid cartilage is the rigid shield that forms the Adam's apple. It protects the larynx from the front but does not move to cover the inlet. Choose it if the question asked which cartilage forms the laryngeal prominence.","The trachealis muscle closes the gap at the back of the tracheal rings, below the larynx. It plays no part in sealing the inlet. It would be the answer if the question asked what lies between the trachea and the esophagus."],"lt":"t-resp-airways","lid":"W3-692","comp":99,"competencyId":"resp-larynx"},{"id":"L03985","dok":2,"q":"Air breathed in through the nose passes through the three regions of the pharynx in which order?","a":"Nasopharynx, oropharynx, laryngopharynx.","options":["Nasopharynx, oropharynx, laryngopharynx","Oropharynx, nasopharynx, laryngopharynx","Nasopharynx, laryngopharynx, oropharynx","Laryngopharynx, oropharynx, nasopharynx"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The pharynx is the throat, and its three regions are stacked from top to bottom. The nasopharynx lies behind the nasal cavity above the soft palate, the oropharynx lies behind the oral cavity, and the laryngopharynx runs from the epiglottis down to where the airway and esophagus divide. Air from the nose meets them in that top-to-bottom order.","notes":["Correct. Air from the nose passes the nasopharynx, then the oropharynx, then the laryngopharynx.","This order starts at the oropharynx, behind the mouth, and then climbs back up to the nasopharynx. Air from the nose meets the nasopharynx first because it lies directly behind the nasal cavity, while air breathed through the mouth enters at the oropharynx and never passes the nasopharynx.","This order skips past the oropharynx and comes back to it. The laryngopharynx is the lowest region, so it must come last.","This is the order from bottom to top, the reverse of the path of inhaled air. It would fit air moving upward, such as during exhalation through the nose."],"lt":"t-pathway-integrated-tracing","lid":"W3-793","comp":98,"competencyId":"resp-upper-tract"},{"id":"L03986","dok":2,"q":"As air moves down through the larynx toward the trachea, it passes between the true vocal cords. What is the name for the vocal folds together with the opening between them?","a":"The glottis.","options":["Epiglottis","Glottis","Vestibular folds","Carina"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The glottis is the vocal folds together with the opening between them. The vocal folds, the true vocal cords, vibrate as air passes through the glottis to produce sound. Below the glottis the larynx continues into the trachea.","notes":["The epiglottis is the flexible flap of elastic cartilage that folds over the laryngeal inlet during swallowing. It would be the answer if the question asked what keeps food out of the airway.","Correct. The glottis is the vocal folds and the opening between them.","The vestibular folds are the false vocal cords above the true folds. They close the airway during swallowing and play no part in sound, so they would be the answer if the question asked about the folds above the true vocal cords.","The carina is the internal ridge at the bottom of the trachea where it forks into the main bronchi. It would be the answer if the question asked where the trachea divides."],"lt":"t-pathway-integrated-tracing","lid":"W3-794","comp":99,"competencyId":"resp-larynx"},{"id":"L04051","dok":2,"q":"Which list names the parts of the upper respiratory tract?","a":"Nose, nasal cavity, paranasal sinuses, and pharynx","options":["Nose, pharynx, trachea, and main bronchi","Respiratory bronchioles, alveolar ducts, and alveoli","Larynx, trachea, bronchi, and lungs","Nose, nasal cavity, paranasal sinuses, and pharynx"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The upper respiratory tract is the set of passages above the larynx that take in air and condition it, warming, moistening, and filtering it on the way down. It is made up of the nose, the nasal cavity, the paranasal sinuses that drain into the nasal cavity, and the pharynx. Everything from the larynx down belongs to the lower respiratory tract.","notes":["This list mixes the two tracts, since the trachea and main bronchi lie below the larynx in the lower tract. Students pick it when they forget that the larynx is the dividing line between the upper and lower tracts.","The respiratory bronchioles, alveolar ducts, and alveoli are the respiratory zone, where gas exchange happens deep in the lungs. Choose this list if the question asked for the structures of the respiratory zone.","The larynx, trachea, bronchi, and lungs make up the lower respiratory tract. Pick this list if the question asked for the parts of the lower tract.","Correct. The upper tract is the nose, nasal cavity, paranasal sinuses, and pharynx."],"lt":"t-imagefree-structure-recognition","lid":"W3-862","comp":97,"competencyId":"resp-zones"},{"id":"L04053","dok":2,"q":"Which part of the respiratory system includes all the passages that only carry air, from the nose down to the terminal bronchioles?","a":"Conducting zone","options":["Upper respiratory tract","Lower respiratory tract","Respiratory zone","Conducting zone"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The conducting zone is every passage that moves air without exchanging gases with the blood, from the nose through the pharynx, larynx, trachea, and bronchi down to the terminal bronchioles. Along the way it warms, moistens, and filters the air. The zones and the tracts are two separate ways of grouping the same parts, so the conducting zone crosses from the upper tract into the lower tract.","notes":["The upper respiratory tract stops at the pharynx, so it does not reach the terminal bronchioles. It would be the answer if the question asked for the nose, nasal cavity, paranasal sinuses, and pharynx as a group.","The lower respiratory tract runs from the larynx through the lungs, including the alveoli where gases are exchanged, and it leaves out the nose. Choose it if the question asked for the larynx, trachea, bronchi, and lungs as a group.","The respiratory zone is where gas exchange happens: the respiratory bronchioles, alveolar ducts, and alveoli. Pick it if the question asked which structures exchange gases with the blood.","Correct. The conducting zone carries air from the nose to the terminal bronchioles."],"lt":"t-imagefree-structure-recognition","lid":"W3-864","comp":97,"competencyId":"resp-zones"},{"id":"L04054","dok":2,"q":"Gas exchange happens in the respiratory bronchioles, alveolar ducts, and alveoli. What is this group of structures called?","a":"Respiratory zone","options":["Conducting zone","Upper respiratory tract","Respiratory zone","Respiratory mucosa"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The respiratory zone is the part of the system where air comes close enough to the blood for gases to cross. It begins at the respiratory bronchioles, which have a few alveoli budding from their walls, and continues through the alveolar ducts to the alveoli. Everything above it, down to the terminal bronchioles, is the conducting zone.","notes":["The conducting zone is the set of passages that only carry air, from the nose to the terminal bronchioles. Pick it if the question asked which passages warm, moisten, and filter air without exchanging gases.","The upper respiratory tract is the nose, nasal cavity, paranasal sinuses, and pharynx, far from the alveoli. It would be the answer if the question asked which structures lie above the larynx.","Correct. The respiratory bronchioles, alveolar ducts, and alveoli form the respiratory zone.","The respiratory mucosa is the lining of pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium with goblet cells found in the nasal cavity and airways. Choose it if the question asked which lining traps particles in mucus and sweeps them toward the pharynx."],"lt":"t-imagefree-structure-recognition","lid":"W3-865","comp":97,"competencyId":"resp-zones"},{"id":"L04055","dok":2,"q":"Which scroll-shaped projections on the lateral walls of the nasal cavity swirl incoming air so it contacts the warm, moist lining?","a":"Nasal conchae","options":["Nasal septum","Choanae","Nasal meatuses","Nasal conchae"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The nasal conchae are three scroll-shaped projections, superior, middle, and inferior, on each lateral wall of the nasal cavity. They make air tumble as it passes, which increases its contact with the warm, moist mucosa. That contact warms and moistens the air and lets mucus trap particles before the air moves on.","notes":["The nasal septum is the midline wall that divides the nasal cavity into right and left halves. It would be the answer if the question asked what separates the two sides of the nasal cavity.","The choanae are the posterior openings where the nasal cavity becomes the nasopharynx. Choose them if the question asked where air leaves the back of the nasal cavity.","The nasal meatuses are the air passages beneath each concha, not the projections themselves. Pick them if the question asked about the grooves that air flows through under the conchae.","Correct. The nasal conchae swirl the air against the nasal lining."],"lt":"t-imagefree-structure-recognition","lid":"W3-866","comp":98,"competencyId":"resp-upper-tract"},{"id":"L04056","dok":2,"q":"Which region of the pharynx carries air only and lies behind the nasal cavity, above the soft palate?","a":"Nasopharynx","options":["Laryngopharynx","Larynx","Oropharynx","Nasopharynx"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The nasopharynx is the uppermost region of the pharynx, sitting behind the nasal cavity and above the soft palate. Because food does not normally enter it, it carries air only and is lined with pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium. It holds the pharyngeal tonsil and the openings of the auditory tubes, and the soft palate lifts to close it during swallowing.","notes":["The laryngopharynx is the lowest region, from the epiglottis to the esophagus, where the air and food paths divide. It would be the answer if the question asked for the region just above the larynx and esophagus.","The larynx is the voice box below the pharynx, not a region of the pharynx. Choose it if the question asked which structure connects the pharynx to the trachea.","The oropharynx lies behind the oral cavity, from the soft palate to the epiglottis, and carries both air and food. Pick it if the question asked which region holds the palatine tonsils.","Correct. The nasopharynx carries air only and lies above the soft palate."],"lt":"t-imagefree-structure-recognition","lid":"W3-867","comp":98,"competencyId":"resp-upper-tract"},{"id":"L04057","dok":2,"q":"Which region of the pharynx lies behind the oral cavity, from the soft palate to the epiglottis, and carries both air and food?","a":"Oropharynx","options":["Esophagus","Oropharynx","Nasopharynx","Laryngopharynx"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The oropharynx is the middle region of the pharynx, directly behind the mouth. Because both air and swallowed food pass through it, it is lined with stratified squamous epithelium, which stands up to friction. It holds the palatine and lingual tonsils.","notes":["The esophagus is the muscular tube that carries food from the laryngopharynx to the stomach, not part of the pharynx. Choose it if the question asked which tube food enters after leaving the pharynx.","Correct. The oropharynx is the middle region, shared by air and food.","The nasopharynx lies above the soft palate, behind the nasal cavity, and carries air only. Pick it if the question asked which region holds the pharyngeal tonsil and the auditory tube openings.","The laryngopharynx is the lowest region, below the epiglottis, where the air and food paths divide. It would be the answer if the question asked for the region that leads into both the larynx and the esophagus."],"lt":"t-imagefree-structure-recognition","lid":"W3-868","comp":98,"competencyId":"resp-upper-tract"},{"id":"L04058","dok":2,"q":"Which region of the pharynx runs from the epiglottis down to the esophagus and is where the air and food paths divide?","a":"Laryngopharynx","options":["Laryngopharynx","Oropharynx","Nasopharynx","Larynx"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The laryngopharynx is the lowest region of the pharynx, lying behind the larynx from the level of the epiglottis down to the esophagus. Air turns forward into the larynx here while food continues back into the esophagus. Like the oropharynx, it carries both air and food and is lined with stratified squamous epithelium.","notes":["Correct. The laryngopharynx is where the air and food paths split.","The oropharynx is the middle region behind the mouth, from the soft palate to the epiglottis. It would be the answer if the question asked which region holds the palatine tonsils.","The nasopharynx is the uppermost region, above the soft palate, and carries air only. Pick it if the question asked which region lies behind the nasal cavity.","The larynx is the voice box that air enters after the laryngopharynx, not a region of the pharynx itself. Choose it if the question asked which structure houses the vocal folds."],"lt":"t-imagefree-structure-recognition","lid":"W3-869","comp":98,"competencyId":"resp-upper-tract"},{"id":"L04059","dok":2,"q":"Which structure connects the pharynx to the trachea, keeps food out of the airway, and houses the vocal folds?","a":"Larynx","options":["Laryngopharynx","Larynx","Trachea","Epiglottis"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The larynx, or voice box, sits between the pharynx and the trachea. It is built from nine cartilages that hold the airway open, it closes off the airway during swallowing, and it houses the vocal folds that vibrate to produce sound. Its largest cartilage, the thyroid cartilage, forms the laryngeal prominence or Adam's apple.","notes":["The laryngopharynx is the lowest region of the pharynx, lying behind the larynx and shared by air and food. It would be the answer if the question asked where the air and food paths divide.","Correct. The larynx is the voice box between the pharynx and trachea.","The trachea is the windpipe below the larynx, held open by C-shaped cartilage rings. Pick it if the question asked which airway runs from the larynx to the carina.","The epiglottis is one cartilage of the larynx, the flap that folds over the inlet during swallowing, not the whole voice box. Choose it if the question asked which elastic cartilage keeps food out of the airway."],"lt":"t-imagefree-structure-recognition","lid":"W3-870","comp":99,"competencyId":"resp-larynx"},{"id":"L04060","dok":2,"q":"Which flexible flap of elastic cartilage folds over the laryngeal inlet during swallowing to keep food out of the airway?","a":"Epiglottis","options":["Cricoid cartilage","Epiglottis","Soft palate","Thyroid cartilage"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The epiglottis is a spoon-shaped flap at the top of the larynx. During a swallow it folds down over the laryngeal inlet so food and liquid slide past into the esophagus instead of the airway. It is the one unpaired laryngeal cartilage made of elastic cartilage, because it has to bend and spring back with every swallow.","notes":["The cricoid cartilage is the ring-shaped cartilage just below the thyroid cartilage, the only complete ring in the airway. It would be the answer if the question asked which laryngeal cartilage forms a full circle.","Correct. The epiglottis covers the laryngeal inlet during swallowing.","The soft palate is the muscular back part of the roof of the mouth, and it lifts to close off the nasopharynx during swallowing. Choose it if the question asked what keeps food from entering the nasal cavity.","The thyroid cartilage is the large shield-shaped cartilage at the front of the larynx that forms the Adam's apple. Pick it if the question asked which cartilage forms the laryngeal prominence."],"lt":"t-imagefree-structure-recognition","lid":"W3-871","comp":99,"competencyId":"resp-larynx"},{"id":"L04061","dok":2,"q":"What is the name for the vocal folds together with the opening between them?","a":"Glottis","options":["Glottis","Vestibular folds","Epiglottis","Laryngeal prominence"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The glottis is the sound-producing part of the larynx: the true vocal folds plus the opening between them. As air passes through that opening, the vocal folds vibrate to make sound. The epiglottis is named for sitting above (epi) the glottis.","notes":["Correct. The glottis is the vocal folds and the opening between them.","The vestibular folds are the false vocal cords above the true folds, which help close the airway during swallowing but play no part in sound. They would be the answer if the question asked for the folds that sit just above the vocal folds.","The epiglottis is the elastic cartilage flap above the glottis that covers the airway during swallowing. Pick it if the question asked which structure keeps food out of the larynx.","The laryngeal prominence is the Adam's apple, the bump formed by the thyroid cartilage on the front of the neck. Choose it if the question asked about the visible landmark of the larynx."],"lt":"t-imagefree-structure-recognition","lid":"W3-872","comp":99,"competencyId":"resp-larynx"},{"id":"L04110","dok":3,"q":"During a swallow, a bit of food slips into the laryngeal opening and triggers coughing. Which structure normally folds over the opening to prevent this?","a":"The epiglottis.","options":["The epiglottis","The thyroid cartilage","The soft palate","The cricoid cartilage"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The epiglottis is a flexible, spoon-shaped flap of elastic cartilage at the top of the larynx. During swallowing it folds back over the laryngeal inlet, so food and liquid slide past into the esophagus instead of into the airway. When food slips past it, coughing is the airway's attempt to clear it.","notes":["Correct. The epiglottis folds over the laryngeal inlet during swallowing.","The thyroid cartilage is the large shield-shaped cartilage that forms the Adam's apple. It protects the larynx from the front but does not cover the opening, so choose it for a question about the laryngeal prominence.","The soft palate also moves during swallowing, but it lifts to close off the nasopharynx so food does not go up into the nose. Pick it for a question about food or liquid coming out of the nose.","The cricoid cartilage is the ring below the thyroid cartilage and the only complete cartilage ring in the airway. It would be the answer for a question about the base of the larynx or the landmark for an emergency airway."],"lt":"t-clinical-application-and-misconceptions","lid":"W3-924","comp":99,"competencyId":"resp-larynx"},{"id":"L04173","dok":2,"q":"Besides producing the voice, what other major job does the larynx perform?","a":"It guards the airway during swallowing.","options":["It guards the airway during swallowing","It exchanges gases with the blood","It drains the paranasal sinuses","It warms and moistens incoming air"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The larynx connects the pharynx to the trachea, and it does more than house the vocal folds. During swallowing, the epiglottis folds over the laryngeal inlet and the vestibular folds close the airway, so food and liquid are sent into the esophagus instead of the trachea. Its cartilages also hold the airway open, keeping a clear path for air.","notes":["Correct. The epiglottis and vestibular folds close off the airway when you swallow.","Gas exchange happens in the respiratory zone, in the respiratory bronchioles, alveolar ducts, and alveoli. The larynx is part of the conducting zone and only carries air.","The paranasal sinuses drain into the nasal cavity, not the larynx. Choose the nasal cavity for a question about where the sinuses empty.","Warming and moistening air is mainly the job of the nasal cavity, with its conchae and moist mucosa. Pick the nasal cavity for a question about conditioning incoming air."],"lt":"t-clinical-application-and-misconceptions","lid":"W3-991","comp":99,"competencyId":"resp-larynx"},{"id":"L04174","dok":2,"q":"How do the nasal conchae help condition incoming air?","a":"They swirl air over warm, moist mucosa.","options":["They close the nasopharynx during swallowing","They swirl air over warm, moist mucosa","They filter large particles with coarse hairs","They exchange gases with the blood"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The nasal conchae are three scroll-shaped projections on the lateral wall of each side of the nasal cavity. They make the air tumble and swirl as it passes, which increases its contact with the warm, moist mucosa covering them. More contact means the air is warmed and moistened, and more particles are trapped in mucus, before it moves on toward the lungs.","notes":["Closing the nasopharynx during swallowing is the job of the soft palate, which lifts up. Choose the soft palate for a question about keeping food out of the nose.","Correct. The conchae swirl air across a large area of warm, moist lining.","Coarse hairs that filter large particles are found in the nasal vestibule, just inside the nostrils. Pick the vestibule for a question about the first filter in the nose.","Gas exchange happens only in the respiratory zone of the lungs. The conchae are in the conducting zone and only condition the air."],"lt":"t-clinical-application-and-misconceptions","lid":"W3-992","comp":98,"competencyId":"resp-upper-tract"},{"id":"L04175","dok":2,"q":"Why is the nasal vestibule good at filtering out large particles?","a":"It has skin with coarse hairs.","options":["It is lined with olfactory epithelium","It has skin with coarse hairs","It is lined with alveolar macrophages","It has scroll-shaped conchae"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The nasal vestibule is the area just inside the nostrils. It is lined with skin that has sebaceous glands and coarse hairs, sometimes called vibrissae. These hairs act as a first screen, catching large particles like dust before the air moves deeper into the nasal cavity.","notes":["Olfactory epithelium is a small patch in the roof of the nasal cavity that houses the receptors for smell. Pick it for a question about where smell is detected.","Correct. The coarse hairs of the vestibule catch large particles at the entrance of the nose.","Alveolar macrophages patrol the alveoli deep in the lungs and engulf small particles that get past every earlier filter. Choose them for a question about the last line of defense in the alveoli.","The conchae are on the lateral walls of the nasal cavity, deeper than the vestibule. They swirl air over moist mucosa, so choose them for a question about warming and moistening air."],"lt":"t-clinical-application-and-misconceptions","lid":"W3-993","comp":98,"competencyId":"resp-upper-tract"},{"id":"L04233","dok":1,"q":"Which list names the parts of the upper respiratory tract, as grouped in this course?","a":"The nose, nasal cavity, paranasal sinuses, and pharynx.","options":["Nose, nasal cavity, trachea, and bronchi","Larynx, trachea, bronchi, and lungs","Respiratory bronchioles, alveolar ducts, and alveoli","Nose, nasal cavity, paranasal sinuses, and pharynx"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The upper respiratory tract is the part of the airway in the head and upper neck: the nose, the nasal cavity, the paranasal sinuses, and the pharynx. These structures take in air and condition it by warming, moistening, and filtering it. In this course the larynx begins the lower respiratory tract.","notes":["The nose and nasal cavity are upper tract structures, but the trachea and bronchi lie in the neck and thorax and belong to the lower tract. This list mixes the two groups.","This is the lower respiratory tract, from the larynx down into the lungs. Pick this list if the question asked for the lower tract.","These are respiratory zone structures deep in the lungs, where gas exchange happens. Choose this list for a question about the respiratory zone, not the upper tract.","Correct. The upper tract runs from the nose through the pharynx."],"lt":"t-resp-zones","lid":"resp-zones-r1","comp":97,"competencyId":"resp-zones"},{"id":"L04235","dok":2,"q":"Which structures make up the respiratory zone, where gas exchange happens?","a":"The respiratory bronchioles, alveolar ducts, and alveoli; the zone begins just past the terminal bronchioles.","options":["Nasal cavity, pharynx, and larynx","Respiratory bronchioles, alveolar ducts, and alveoli","Segmental bronchi, bronchioles, and terminal bronchioles","Trachea, main bronchi, and lobar bronchi"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The respiratory zone is the part of the airway where gas exchange actually happens. It begins just past the terminal bronchioles with the respiratory bronchioles, which have a few alveoli budding from their walls, then continues into the alveolar ducts and alveoli. Everything before that point, from the nose to the terminal bronchioles, is the conducting zone, which only carries air.","notes":["These are upper airway structures that warm, moisten, and filter air. Choose them for a question about the upper respiratory tract.","Correct. These are the airways with alveoli, where gases cross into the blood.","These are the last airways of the conducting zone, ending at the terminal bronchioles. Students choose this list because it sits so close to the exchange surface, but these walls have no alveoli; it would be the answer for the end of the conducting zone.","These are large conducting airways supported by cartilage. Pick them if the question asked which airways carry air into the lungs without exchanging gas."],"lt":"t-resp-zones","lid":"resp-zones-r4","comp":97,"competencyId":"resp-zones"},{"id":"L04236","dok":3,"q":"A segment of airway carries air but has no alveoli in its walls. Which of these could it be?","a":"A terminal bronchiole, part of the conducting zone.","options":["An alveolar sac","An alveolar duct","A terminal bronchiole","A respiratory bronchiole"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"An airway with no alveoli belongs to the conducting zone, which only carries air from the nose down to the terminal bronchioles. The terminal bronchiole is the last airway of that zone. Past it, every structure has alveoli in or along its walls: respiratory bronchioles have a few budding from their walls, alveolar ducts are lined entirely with them, and alveolar sacs are clusters of them.","notes":["An alveolar sac is a cluster of alveoli sharing a common space, so it is all exchange surface. Choose it for the grape-like cluster at the very end of the airway.","An alveolar duct is a passage whose walls are lined entirely with alveoli. It would be the answer for the airway that leads from a respiratory bronchiole into the alveolar sacs.","Correct. The terminal bronchiole is the last conducting airway, with no alveoli.","A respiratory bronchiole has a few alveoli budding from its walls, so it is the first airway of the respiratory zone. Pick it if the question asked where gas exchange begins."],"lt":"t-resp-zones","lid":"resp-zones-r5","comp":97,"competencyId":"resp-zones"},{"id":"L04237","dok":1,"q":"Which laryngeal cartilage forms the Adam's apple?","a":"The thyroid cartilage.","options":["Arytenoid cartilage","Epiglottis","Thyroid cartilage","Cricoid cartilage"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The thyroid cartilage is the largest cartilage of the larynx, a shield-shaped piece that forms the front of the voice box. Its anterior ridge is the laryngeal prominence, known as the Adam's apple, which is usually larger in males. It sits just above the cricoid cartilage, the ring at the bottom of the larynx.","notes":["The arytenoid cartilages are small paired cartilages at the back of the larynx that anchor and move the vocal folds. They would be the answer for a question about which cartilages control the vocal cords.","The epiglottis is the flexible flap of elastic cartilage that folds over the laryngeal inlet during swallowing. Choose it for the cartilage that keeps food out of the airway.","Correct. The thyroid cartilage forms the laryngeal prominence.","The cricoid cartilage is the ring just below the thyroid cartilage, the only complete ring of cartilage in the airway. Pick it if the question asked which laryngeal cartilage fully encircles the airway."],"lt":"t-resp-larynx","lid":"resp-larynx-r2","comp":99,"competencyId":"resp-larynx"},{"id":"L04238","dok":1,"q":"What does the epiglottis do?","a":"It folds down over the laryngeal opening during swallowing to keep food and drink out of the airway.","options":["It vibrates as air passes to produce sound","It forms a complete ring that holds the airway open","It lifts to close off the nasopharynx during swallowing","It folds over the laryngeal inlet during swallowing"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The epiglottis is a flexible, spoon-shaped flap of elastic cartilage at the top of the larynx. During swallowing, the larynx rises and the epiglottis folds down over the laryngeal inlet, so food and drink slide past into the esophagus instead of entering the airway. Elastic cartilage lets it bend and spring back with every swallow, which the stiff hyaline thyroid and cricoid cartilages could not do.","notes":["Vibrating to produce sound is the job of the vocal folds, the true vocal cords stretched across the larynx. Pick this if the question asked what makes the voice.","A complete ring holding the airway open describes the cricoid cartilage, just below the thyroid cartilage. It would be the answer for the only complete cartilage ring in the airway.","Lifting to close off the nasopharynx is the job of the soft palate, which keeps food from going up into the nasal cavity. Choose this for a question about why food does not come out of the nose when you swallow.","Correct. The epiglottis is the lid that covers the airway during a swallow."],"lt":"t-resp-larynx","lid":"resp-larynx-r3","comp":99,"competencyId":"resp-larynx"},{"id":"L04239","dok":3,"q":"A student laughs while eating, and a bit of food slips into the larynx instead of the esophagus, setting off a coughing fit. Which structure normally covers the laryngeal inlet during a swallow to prevent this?","a":"The epiglottis, which folds down over the laryngeal opening during swallowing.","options":["Soft palate","Epiglottis","Thyroid cartilage","Trachealis muscle"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"During a swallow the larynx rises and the epiglottis folds down over the laryngeal inlet like a lid, steering food back toward the esophagus. If someone laughs or talks while swallowing, the airway may be open at the wrong moment and food can slip past the epiglottis. The coughing fit is the airway's attempt to push the food back out.","notes":["The soft palate rises to close off the nasopharynx during a swallow, keeping food out of the nasal cavity. Pick it if the food had come up into the nose instead of entering the larynx.","Correct. The epiglottis covers the laryngeal inlet so food goes down the esophagus.","The thyroid cartilage is the large shield-shaped cartilage forming the front of the larynx (the Adam's apple). It protects the larynx but does not cover its opening; choose it for a question about the laryngeal prominence.","The trachealis muscle is the smooth muscle that closes the open back of each C-shaped tracheal ring, next to the esophagus. It would be the answer for a question about the posterior wall of the trachea."],"lt":"t-resp-larynx","lid":"resp-larynx-r5","comp":99,"competencyId":"resp-larynx"},{"id":"L04255","dok":1,"q":"Which list gives the three regions of the pharynx from top to bottom?","a":"Nasopharynx, oropharynx, laryngopharynx.","options":["Nasopharynx, oropharynx, laryngopharynx","Oropharynx, nasopharynx, laryngopharynx","Nasopharynx, laryngopharynx, oropharynx","Laryngopharynx, oropharynx, nasopharynx"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The pharynx is the throat, a muscular tube running from behind the nasal cavity down to the larynx and esophagus. The nasopharynx sits behind the nasal cavity, above the soft palate; the oropharynx sits behind the oral cavity, from the soft palate to the epiglottis; and the laryngopharynx runs from the epiglottis down to the esophagus. Each region is named for the structure it sits behind.","notes":["Correct. Nose, then mouth, then larynx, from top to bottom.","This swaps the top two regions. The nasal cavity sits above the oral cavity, so the nasopharynx behind it is the highest region.","This puts the laryngopharynx in the middle, but it is the lowest region, leading down to the larynx and esophagus. The oropharynx, behind the mouth, sits between the other two.","These are the right three regions listed from bottom to top. Pick this order if the question asked for the regions from inferior to superior."],"lt":"t-resp-upper-tract","lid":"resp-upper-tract-r1","comp":98,"competencyId":"resp-upper-tract"},{"id":"L04256","dok":2,"q":"Which part of the pharynx carries air only, and which parts carry both air and food?","a":"The nasopharynx is air-only; the oropharynx and laryngopharynx are shared by air and food.","options":["The laryngopharynx carries air only; the nasopharynx and oropharynx carry both","The oropharynx carries air only; the nasopharynx and laryngopharynx carry both","All three regions of the pharynx carry both air and food","The nasopharynx carries air only; the oropharynx and laryngopharynx carry both"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The nasopharynx lies above the soft palate, and during a swallow the soft palate lifts to seal it off, so it carries air only. The oropharynx and laryngopharynx lie below that point, where food from the mouth and air from the nose share the same passage. Their linings match: the nasopharynx has pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium like the rest of the airway, while the shared regions have tougher stratified squamous epithelium to resist friction from food.","notes":["The laryngopharynx is where the air and food paths finally divide into the larynx and the esophagus, so food passes through it. Students choose this because its name sounds like part of the airway.","The oropharynx sits directly behind the mouth, so food passes through it with every swallow. Pick the oropharynx for a question about which region holds the palatine tonsils.","Food normally never enters the nasopharynx, because the soft palate lifts to close it off during swallowing. Students choose this when they picture the whole pharynx as one shared tube.","Correct. Only the nasopharynx is closed off from food."],"lt":"t-resp-upper-tract","lid":"resp-upper-tract-r2","comp":98,"competencyId":"resp-upper-tract"},{"id":"L04257","dok":2,"q":"What do the nasal conchae and meatuses do to inhaled air?","a":"They swirl the air over the warm, moist lining to warm, moisten, and filter it.","options":["They vibrate as the air passes through to produce sound","They bring the air close to the blood so oxygen can cross into it","They swirl air against the moist lining to warm and filter it","They close off the nasal cavity from the pharynx during swallowing"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The nasal conchae are three scroll-shaped projections on the lateral wall of the nasal cavity, and the meatuses are the narrow passages beneath them. They turn the airflow into swirls, bringing more air into contact with the warm, moist, mucus-covered lining. That contact warms and moistens the air and traps dust and particles in the mucus before the air reaches the lungs.","notes":["Sound is produced by the vocal folds in the larynx, not by the nasal conchae. Choose this description for a question about the true vocal cords.","Gas exchange happens in the alveoli deep in the lungs, not in the nasal cavity. Pick this for a question about the job of the alveoli and the respiratory membrane.","Correct. The conchae increase contact between the air and the lining so the air is conditioned.","Closing off the nasal route during swallowing is the job of the soft palate, which lifts to seal the nasopharynx. It would be the answer for a question about why food does not enter the nose."],"lt":"t-resp-upper-tract","lid":"resp-upper-tract-r3","comp":98,"competencyId":"resp-upper-tract"},{"id":"N-a17-104-1","dok":1,"q":"Which structure is inflamed in rhinitis?","a":"The nasal mucosa.","options":["The lining of the paranasal sinuses","The vocal folds of the larynx","The lining of the bronchi","The nasal mucosa"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Rhinitis is inflammation of the nasal mucosa, the lining of the nasal cavity. The lining swells and makes extra mucus. Allergies or infections cause it.","notes":["Inflamed linings of the paranasal sinuses are sinusitis, which often starts when the sinus openings into the nasal cavity are blocked.","Inflamed, swollen vocal folds are laryngitis, which turns the voice hoarse.","A bronchial lining that stays inflamed and makes too much mucus is chronic bronchitis, a problem much lower in the airway.","Correct. Rhinitis inflames the nasal mucosa, the lining of the nasal cavity."],"comp":104,"competencyId":"resp-disorders"},{"id":"N-a17-104-2","dok":2,"q":"The openings that drain a patient's paranasal sinuses into the nasal cavity become blocked, and the lining of the sinuses becomes inflamed. Which disorder fits?","a":"Sinusitis.","options":["Rhinitis","Sinusitis","Laryngitis","Pleurisy"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Sinusitis is inflammation of the lining of the paranasal sinuses. It often starts when the sinus openings into the nasal cavity are blocked. Rhinitis is the related problem in the nasal cavity itself, the nasal mucosa.","notes":["Rhinitis inflames the nasal mucosa, the lining of the nasal cavity itself, not the sinuses that drain into it.","Correct. Sinusitis is inflammation of the lining of the paranasal sinuses, often after their openings are blocked.","Laryngitis inflames the vocal folds of the larynx and turns the voice hoarse. It does not involve the sinuses.","Pleurisy is inflammation of the pleurae around the lungs, which causes sharp pain with each breath. It does not involve the sinuses or their openings."],"comp":104,"competencyId":"resp-disorders"},{"id":"N-a17-104-3","dok":3,"q":"Why does laryngitis make the voice hoarse?","a":"The vocal folds are inflamed and swollen, so they cannot vibrate normally.","options":["The nasal mucosa swells and makes extra mucus that muffles the voice","The epiglottis stays open and lets air slip past the vocal folds","The vocal folds are inflamed and swollen, so they cannot vibrate normally","The cricoid cartilage softens, so the larynx cannot hold its shape"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Laryngitis affects the larynx and its vocal folds. The folds become inflamed and swollen, so they cannot vibrate normally, and the voice turns hoarse. The problem is in the folds themselves, not in the cartilage frame of the larynx or in the nasal cavity.","notes":["A swollen nasal mucosa with extra mucus is rhinitis. It affects the nasal cavity, not the vocal folds that make the voice.","The epiglottis covers the laryngeal inlet during a swallow. Laryngitis is inflammation of the vocal folds, not a problem with the epiglottis.","Correct. In laryngitis the inflamed, swollen vocal folds cannot vibrate normally.","The cricoid cartilage is the only complete cartilage ring in the airway. Laryngitis inflames the vocal folds and does not change the cartilage."],"comp":104,"competencyId":"resp-disorders"},{"id":"N-a17d-98-1","dok":3,"q":"A patient with a sinus infection has pus draining from the space above the superior nasal concha, called the sphenoethmoidal recess. Which sinus is most likely infected, and which bone holds it?","a":"The sphenoidal sinus, in the sphenoid bone.","options":["The maxillary sinus, in the maxilla.","The back ethmoidal air cells, in the ethmoid bone.","The frontal sinus, in the frontal bone.","The sphenoidal sinus, in the sphenoid bone."],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Each paranasal sinus is named for the bone it sits in, and each drains through a small opening into the nasal cavity. Most drain into the middle meatus, and the back ethmoidal cells drain into the superior meatus. Only the sphenoidal sinus, deep in the skull behind the nasal cavity, drains into the sphenoethmoidal recess above the superior concha. So pus at that spot points to the sphenoidal sinus in the sphenoid bone.","notes":["The maxillary sinus is the largest sinus, in the cheek below each orbit, but it drains into the middle meatus, not the space above the superior concha.","The back ethmoidal cells drain into the superior meatus, the groove under the superior concha. The recess is the space above that concha.","The frontal sinus sits in the forehead above the orbits and drains into the middle meatus, so it would not send pus into the sphenoethmoidal recess.","Correct. The sphenoidal sinus is the only sinus that drains into the space above the superior concha."],"comp":98,"competencyId":"resp-upper-tract"},{"id":"N-a17d-98-2","dok":3,"q":"A child has enlarged adenoids that crowd the openings on the side walls of the pharyngeal region where the adenoids sit. Which region is it, and what are those openings?","a":"The nasopharynx; the auditory tubes, which lead to the middle ears.","options":["The oropharynx; the auditory tubes, which lead to the middle ears.","The nasopharynx; the auditory tubes, which lead to the middle ears.","The nasopharynx; the choanae, which lead from the nasal cavity.","The laryngopharynx; the openings of the larynx and the esophagus."],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The adenoids are the pharyngeal tonsil when it is enlarged, and it sits on the back wall of the nasopharynx, the air-only region behind the nasal cavity and above the soft palate. The same region has the openings of the auditory tubes on its side walls, and those tubes connect the nasopharynx to the middle ears. That is why swollen adenoids can crowd the auditory tube openings.","notes":["The oropharynx sits behind the mouth and holds the palatine and lingual tonsils. The adenoids and the auditory tube openings are higher, in the nasopharynx.","Correct. The pharyngeal tonsil (adenoid) sits on the back wall of the nasopharynx, and the auditory tubes open on its side walls.","The region is right, but the choanae open into the nasopharynx from the front, from the nasal cavity. The openings on its side walls are the auditory tubes.","The laryngopharynx is where the air and food paths split into the larynx and esophagus. The adenoids and the auditory tube openings sit higher, in the nasopharynx."],"comp":98,"competencyId":"resp-upper-tract"}]},{"id":"m4-lower-respiratory","title":"Trachea, bronchial tree, lungs and pleurae","summary":"trachea, carina, bronchial tree, lobes and fissures, hilum, pleurae, alveoli and respiratory membrane, respiratory histology, diaphragm, respiratory disorders at the structural level","mw":"2026-10-26","tr":"2026-10-27","cards":[{"id":"L00155","dok":3,"q":"The diaphragm separates the thoracic cavity from the abdominopelvic cavity. Beyond dividing the trunk, what role does it play in breathing?","a":"Its contraction enlarges the thoracic cavity, drawing air into the lungs.","options":["It separates the two pleural cavities so each lung can inflate","Its contraction enlarges the thoracic cavity, drawing air into the lungs","It closes off the trachea during swallowing","It squeezes the lungs from below to push air into them"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The diaphragm is a dome-shaped muscle and the main muscle of breathing. When it contracts, it flattens and moves down, making the thoracic cavity larger, and air flows into the lungs. So the same partition that divides the trunk also drives each breath in.","notes":["The two pleural cavities are separated by the mediastinum, the central compartment holding the heart. The diaphragm lies beneath both lungs, not between them.","Correct. Contraction enlarges the chest and draws air in.","Closing off the airway during swallowing is the job of the epiglottis in the larynx. The diaphragm is far below, at the floor of the thorax.","Squeezing the lungs would push air out, not in. Contraction of the diaphragm makes the chest larger, which is the opposite of squeezing."],"lt":"t-body-cavities","lid":"d3-t-body-cavities-4","comp":103,"competencyId":"resp-thoracic-diaphragm"},{"id":"L02098","dok":1,"q":"Which structure is the windpipe, held permanently open by C-shaped rings of cartilage?","a":"The trachea.","options":["The trachea","A main bronchus","The larynx","The pharynx"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The trachea, or windpipe, runs from the larynx down into the thorax, where it forks into the two main bronchi. C-shaped rings of hyaline cartilage keep it permanently open so air can pass at all times. The open back of each C is closed by the trachealis muscle, which lies against the esophagus.","notes":["Correct. The trachea is the windpipe held open by C-shaped cartilage rings.","A main bronchus is one of the two branches the trachea divides into at the carina. Its cartilage changes from rings to plates inside the lung, and it is not called the windpipe.","The larynx is the voice box above the trachea, built from nine cartilages including the thyroid and cricoid. Pick it if the question asked which structure houses the vocal folds.","The pharynx is the throat, a muscular tube shared by air and food. It has no cartilage rings holding it open."],"lt":"t-respiratory","lid":"c2","comp":100,"competencyId":"resp-tree"},{"id":"L02099","dok":1,"q":"Which serous membrane covers the outer surface of the lung itself?","a":"The visceral pleura.","options":["The parietal peritoneum","The parietal pleura","The visceral pleura","The visceral pericardium"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Each lung is wrapped by a double-layered serous membrane called the pleura. The visceral pleura clings directly to the outer surface of the lung, while the parietal pleura lines the thoracic wall. Visceral always means the layer on the organ, and parietal means the layer on the body wall.","notes":["The parietal peritoneum lines the wall of the abdominopelvic cavity. Pick it for a question about the membrane lining the abdominal wall.","The parietal pleura lines the inside of the thoracic wall, not the lung surface. Pick it if the question asked which pleura a surgeon meets first when cutting through the chest wall.","Correct. The visceral pleura covers the surface of the lung.","The visceral pericardium, also called the epicardium, covers the surface of the heart. It follows the same visceral pattern but belongs to the heart."],"lt":"t-respiratory","lid":"c3","comp":101,"competencyId":"resp-lungs-pleura"},{"id":"L02100","dok":1,"q":"How many lobes does the right lung have?","a":"Three lobes.","options":["Five lobes","Two lobes","Three lobes","Four lobes"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The right lung has three lobes: superior, middle, and inferior. A horizontal fissure separates the superior lobe from the middle lobe, and an oblique fissure separates the inferior lobe from the middle and superior lobes above it. The right lung is the larger of the two because the heart takes up room on the left.","notes":["Five is the total number of lobes in both lungs together, three on the right plus two on the left. Pick five only if the question asked how many lobes there are in all.","Two lobes is the count for the left lung, which has only a superior and an inferior lobe. Pick two if the question asked about the left lung.","Correct. The right lung has superior, middle, and inferior lobes.","Four lobes is not the count for either lung. Students sometimes add a lobe for the lingula, but the lingula is part of the left superior lobe, not a separate lobe."],"lt":"t-respiratory","lid":"c4","comp":101,"competencyId":"resp-lungs-pleura"},{"id":"L02102","dok":1,"q":"Which alveolar cells secrete surfactant?","a":"Type II alveolar cells.","options":["Alveolar macrophages","Type II alveolar cells","Pulmonary capillary endothelial cells","Type I alveolar cells"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Type II alveolar cells are cuboidal cells scattered in the alveolar wall that secrete surfactant. Surfactant is a fluid that lowers surface tension and keeps the alveoli from collapsing. Type I cells, by contrast, are the thin, flat cells where gases cross.","notes":["Alveolar macrophages, also called dust cells, patrol the alveolar surface and engulf debris. Pick them for a question about cleaning the alveoli.","Correct. Type II alveolar cells secrete surfactant.","Pulmonary capillary endothelial cells line the capillaries that wrap each alveolus. They form the blood side of the respiratory membrane, not the surfactant-secreting cells.","Type I alveolar cells are thin, flat simple squamous cells that form most of the alveolar wall, where gases cross. Pick them for a question about the gas exchange surface."],"lt":"t-respiratory","lid":"c6","comp":102,"competencyId":"resp-histo"},{"id":"L02105","dok":1,"q":"What is the narrow superior tip of a lung called?","a":"The apex.","options":["The base","The lingula","The apex","The hilum"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The apex is the narrow top of each lung. It rises just above the clavicle, into the root of the neck. The opposite end, the broad inferior surface resting on the diaphragm, is the base.","notes":["The base is the broad inferior surface of the lung that rests on the diaphragm. Pick it if the question asked for the bottom of the lung.","The lingula is the tongue-shaped projection of the left superior lobe, below the cardiac notch. Pick it for a question about the left lung's tongue-like extension.","Correct. The apex is the narrow superior tip of the lung.","The hilum is the indentation on the medial surface where the bronchus and vessels enter and leave. Pick it for a question about where structures pass into the lung."],"lt":"t-respiratory","lid":"c9","comp":101,"competencyId":"resp-lungs-pleura"},{"id":"L02107","dok":1,"q":"Which structure is the indentation on the medial surface of a lung where the bronchus and vessels enter and leave?","a":"The hilum.","options":["The hilum","The base","The apex","The cardiac notch"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The hilum is the indented region on the medial, mediastinal surface of each lung. The main bronchus, pulmonary artery, pulmonary veins, bronchial vessels, nerves, and lymphatics pass through it, and that bundle is called the root of the lung. The same word hilum is used for the indented side of the kidney and lymph node, where vessels enter and leave.","notes":["Correct. The hilum is where the bronchus and vessels enter and leave the lung.","The base is the broad inferior surface resting on the diaphragm. Pick it for a question about the bottom surface of the lung.","The apex is the narrow superior tip of the lung, rising just above the clavicle. Pick it for a question about the top of the lung.","The cardiac notch is the indentation in the left lung that makes room for the heart. It is on the anterior border of the left lung, not the entry point for the bronchus and vessels."],"lt":"t-respiratory","lid":"c11","comp":101,"competencyId":"resp-lungs-pleura"},{"id":"L02108","dok":1,"q":"What is the branching system of airways that runs from the trachea down to the terminal bronchioles called?","a":"The bronchial tree.","options":["The alveolar sac","The bronchial tree","The root of the lung","The respiratory membrane"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Below the larynx the airway branches again and again, like an upside-down tree, from the trachea to the main, lobar, and segmental bronchi and then the bronchioles. This branching system is called the bronchial tree. With each division the airways get narrower and more numerous.","notes":["An alveolar sac is a cluster of alveoli sharing a common space at the very end of the airways. Pick it for a question about the clusters at the ends of the alveolar ducts.","Correct. The bronchial tree is the branching system of airways below the larynx.","The root of the lung is the bundle of the main bronchus, pulmonary vessels, nerves, and lymphatics at the hilum. Pick it for a question about what passes into the lung at the hilum.","The respiratory membrane is the thin barrier between alveolar air and blood. Pick it for a question about where gases cross."],"lt":"t-respiratory","lid":"c12","comp":100,"competencyId":"resp-tree"},{"id":"L02110","dok":2,"q":"Air leaving the trachea passes the carina. Which airways does it enter next?","a":"The main bronchi.","options":["The alveolar ducts","The lobar bronchi","The main bronchi","The terminal bronchioles"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The carina is the internal ridge at the bottom of the trachea where it forks in two. Each fork is a main (primary) bronchus, one entering each lung at the hilum. From there the main bronchi divide into lobar, then segmental, bronchi.","notes":["Alveolar ducts are respiratory zone passages lined entirely with alveoli, near the very end of the tree. Pick them for a question about the airway between the respiratory bronchioles and the alveolar sacs.","The lobar bronchi are the secondary bronchi, one to each lobe. They branch from the main bronchi, one step later than the carina.","Correct. The trachea forks at the carina into the two main bronchi.","Terminal bronchioles are the last airways of the conducting zone, many divisions further down the tree. Pick them if the question asked for the airway just before the respiratory zone."],"lt":"t-respiratory","lid":"c14","comp":100,"competencyId":"resp-tree"},{"id":"L02113","dok":2,"q":"The lobar bronchi are also called the secondary bronchi. What does one lobar bronchus supply?","a":"One lobe of a lung.","options":["One bronchopulmonary segment","One whole lung","One alveolar sac","One lobe of a lung"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Each main bronchus divides into lobar bronchi, one for each lobe. The right lung has three lobes and so receives three lobar bronchi, while the left lung has two lobes and two lobar bronchi. The name lobar comes from the lobe each one supplies.","notes":["A bronchopulmonary segment is supplied by a segmental (tertiary) bronchus, the next branch after the lobar bronchi. Pick this if the question asked about segmental bronchi.","One whole lung is supplied by a main (primary) bronchus. Pick the main bronchus if the question asked which airway enters each lung.","An alveolar sac lies at the very end of the tree, after the alveolar duct. It is reached only after many more divisions.","Correct. Each lobar bronchus supplies one lobe."],"lt":"t-respiratory","lid":"c17","comp":100,"competencyId":"resp-tree"},{"id":"L02114","dok":2,"q":"The left lung has a cardiac notch along its anterior border. What is the structural reason for it?","a":"It makes room for the heart.","options":["It anchors the visceral pleura","It marks where the apex begins","It makes room for the heart","It allows the trachea to fork"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The heart sits in the mediastinum and tilts toward the left side of the thorax. The left lung gives up room for it, and the indentation along its anterior border is called the cardiac notch. This is also why the left lung is smaller and has only two lobes.","notes":["The visceral pleura clings to the whole surface of the lung and needs no notch to anchor it. Pick the visceral pleura for a question about the membrane on the lung surface.","The apex is the narrow superior tip of the lung, above the clavicle. The cardiac notch is lower, along the anterior border near the heart.","Correct. The cardiac notch is where the left lung makes room for the heart.","The trachea forks at the carina, in the mediastinum, before the airways enter either lung. The cardiac notch has nothing to do with that split."],"lt":"t-respiratory","lid":"c18","comp":101,"competencyId":"resp-lungs-pleura"},{"id":"L02116","dok":2,"q":"The trachea has C-shaped cartilage rings, but bronchioles have no cartilage. What tissue makes up much of a bronchiole wall instead?","a":"Smooth muscle.","options":["Elastic cartilage","Smooth muscle","Skeletal muscle","Dense regular connective tissue"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"As the bronchial tree branches, cartilage shrinks and smooth muscle takes over. By the level of the bronchioles the cartilage is gone, and a complete layer of smooth muscle makes up much of the wall. Without cartilage to hold them open, bronchioles narrow a great deal when that muscle contracts, as they do in asthma.","notes":["Elastic cartilage forms the epiglottis, which has to bend and spring back. Bronchioles have no cartilage of any kind.","Correct. Bronchioles have no cartilage, and smooth muscle makes up much of their wall.","Skeletal muscle is voluntary muscle attached to bones, such as the diaphragm and intercostals that move the chest wall. It is not found in the walls of the bronchioles.","Dense regular connective tissue forms tendons and ligaments. Pick it for a question about what attaches muscle to bone."],"lt":"t-respiratory","lid":"c20","comp":100,"competencyId":"resp-tree"},{"id":"L02117","dok":2,"q":"A surgeon cuts inward through the thoracic wall toward a lung. Which pleura is reached first?","a":"The parietal pleura.","options":["The visceral pleura","The pleural fluid","The parietal pleura","The pericardium"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The parietal pleura lines the inner surface of the thoracic wall, so it is the first serous layer reached from outside. Beneath it is the pleural cavity with its thin film of fluid, and then the visceral pleura on the surface of the lung. Parietal means wall and visceral means organ.","notes":["The visceral pleura covers the lung surface, so it is reached last, after the parietal pleura and the pleural cavity. Pick it if the question asked which pleura clings to the lung.","Pleural fluid is the thin film in the pleural cavity between the two layers. It lies deep to the parietal pleura, and it is a fluid rather than a membrane.","Correct. The parietal pleura lines the thoracic wall and is reached first.","The pericardium surrounds the heart in the mediastinum. A cut through the lateral chest wall toward a lung does not pass through it."],"lt":"t-respiratory","lid":"c21","comp":101,"competencyId":"resp-lungs-pleura"},{"id":"L02118","dok":2,"q":"The right lung has both a horizontal fissure and an oblique fissure. Which fissure pattern does the left lung have?","a":"An oblique fissure only.","options":["An oblique fissure only","A horizontal fissure only","No fissures at all","A horizontal fissure and an oblique fissure"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The left lung has two lobes, superior and inferior, separated by a single oblique fissure. The right lung needs two fissures to separate three lobes, and its horizontal fissure sets off the middle lobe. Since the left lung has no middle lobe, it has no horizontal fissure.","notes":["Correct. The left lung has only an oblique fissure.","The horizontal fissure separates the superior and middle lobes of the right lung. The left lung has no middle lobe, so it has no horizontal fissure.","The left lung still has two lobes, so it needs one fissure to divide them. A lung with no fissures would be a single lobe.","Both fissures are found in the right lung, where they separate the superior, middle, and inferior lobes. Pick this pattern for a question about the right lung."],"lt":"t-respiratory","lid":"c22","comp":101,"competencyId":"resp-lungs-pleura"},{"id":"L02119","dok":2,"q":"Which airway is the last airway of the conducting zone, just before gas exchange begins?","a":"The terminal bronchiole.","options":["The segmental bronchus","The terminal bronchiole","The alveolar duct","The respiratory bronchiole"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Terminal bronchioles are the final airways of the conducting zone. They still only carry air, since no alveoli bud from their walls. The next airway, the respiratory bronchiole, starts the respiratory zone because a few alveoli open from its walls.","notes":["The segmental bronchus is the tertiary bronchus supplying one bronchopulmonary segment. It is several divisions above the end of the conducting zone.","Correct. The terminal bronchiole is the last conducting airway.","The alveolar duct is a respiratory zone passage lined entirely with alveoli. It comes after the respiratory bronchiole, deeper into the gas exchange region.","The respiratory bronchiole is the first airway of the respiratory zone, with a few alveoli budding from its walls. Pick it if the question asked where gas exchange first begins."],"lt":"t-respiratory","lid":"c23","comp":97,"competencyId":"resp-zones"},{"id":"L02120","dok":2,"q":"Which description fits an alveolar sac?","a":"A cluster of alveoli that share a common space.","options":["The thin barrier between alveolar air and blood","A cluster of alveoli that share a common space","A single tiny air sac where gases cross","A passage whose walls are lined entirely with alveoli"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"An alveolar sac sits at the end of an alveolar duct and looks like a bunch of grapes. Several alveoli open into one shared central space, the sac. Air reaches the sac through the alveolar duct, and gas exchange happens across the walls of the individual alveoli.","notes":["The thin barrier between alveolar air and blood is the respiratory membrane. Pick it for a question about the layers that gases cross.","Correct. An alveolar sac is a cluster of alveoli sharing a common space.","A single tiny air sac where gases cross is one alveolus. Pick alveolus if the question asked about the individual site of gas exchange.","A passage lined entirely with alveoli is an alveolar duct, which leads into the alveolar sacs. Pick it for the airway just before the sacs."],"lt":"t-respiratory","lid":"c24","comp":102,"competencyId":"resp-histo"},{"id":"L02122","dok":2,"q":"Which respiratory disorder is defined by air entering the pleural cavity and breaking its seal?","a":"Pneumothorax.","options":["Pneumothorax","Pneumonia","Asthma","Emphysema"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"A pneumothorax is air in the pleural cavity, the sealed space between the parietal and visceral pleurae. Normally that space holds only a thin film of fluid. When air gets in through a puncture of the chest wall or lung, the seal is lost and the lung collapses.","notes":["Correct. A pneumothorax is air in the pleural cavity, which lets the lung collapse.","Pneumonia is an infection that inflames the alveoli and fills them with fluid. The problem is inside the lung, not air in the pleural cavity.","Asthma is inflammation and narrowing of the bronchioles, which causes wheezing. Pick it for a question about the small airways.","Emphysema destroys alveolar walls and reduces the surface for gas exchange. Pick it for a question about permanent loss of alveolar surface."],"lt":"t-respiratory","lid":"c26","comp":104,"competencyId":"resp-disorders"},{"id":"L02123","dok":2,"q":"Which respiratory disorder is inflammation and narrowing of the bronchioles, causing wheezing?","a":"Asthma.","options":["Pneumothorax","Asthma","Pneumonia","Emphysema"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Asthma affects the bronchi and bronchioles. The airway lining is inflamed, the smooth muscle tightens, and extra mucus collects, so the airways narrow and air whistles through them as a wheeze. The narrowing is worst in the bronchioles because they have no cartilage to hold them open.","notes":["Pneumothorax is air in the pleural cavity that lets the lung collapse. It involves the space around the lung, not the airways.","Correct. Asthma is inflammation and narrowing of the bronchioles.","Pneumonia is an infection that fills the alveoli with fluid. Pick it for a question about inflamed, fluid-filled air sacs.","Emphysema destroys the alveolar walls rather than narrowing the bronchioles. Pick it for a question about lost surface area for gas exchange."],"lt":"t-respiratory","lid":"c27","comp":104,"competencyId":"resp-disorders"},{"id":"L02125","dok":2,"q":"Which structures form the dense net that wraps each alveolus?","a":"The pulmonary capillaries.","options":["The pulmonary capillaries","The alveolar ducts","The terminal bronchioles","The bronchial arteries"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Each alveolus is wrapped in a dense net of pulmonary capillaries. The walls of the alveolus and the capillary lie so close together that they form the respiratory membrane, where gases cross between air and blood. These capillaries come from branches of the pulmonary arteries and drain into the pulmonary veins.","notes":["Correct. Pulmonary capillaries wrap each alveolus.","Alveolar ducts are air passages lined with alveoli. They lead air to the alveoli but are airways, not a net of blood vessels.","Terminal bronchioles are the last airways of the conducting zone. They carry air toward the alveoli but do not wrap around them.","The bronchial arteries supply oxygen-rich blood to the walls of the bronchi and lung tissue. They nourish the lung itself rather than forming the gas exchange net around the alveoli."],"lt":"t-respiratory","lid":"c29","comp":102,"competencyId":"resp-histo"},{"id":"L02126","dok":3,"q":"On a slide, a student finds a small conducting airway with a wall of smooth muscle, no cartilage, and no alveoli. Which structure is it?","a":"A bronchiole.","options":["An alveolar duct","A segmental bronchus","A bronchiole","A respiratory bronchiole"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Bronchioles are the smallest conducting airways. As the bronchial tree branches, cartilage disappears and smooth muscle takes over, so a bronchiole wall has smooth muscle and no cartilage. Because no alveoli open from its wall, it still only carries air.","notes":["An alveolar duct has walls lined entirely with alveoli. Pick it if the slide showed a passage made of alveoli.","A segmental bronchus is a tertiary bronchus that still has plates of cartilage in its wall. Pick it if the airway had cartilage and supplied one bronchopulmonary segment.","Correct. A bronchiole has smooth muscle, no cartilage, and no alveoli.","A respiratory bronchiole has a few alveoli budding from its wall. The absence of alveoli rules it out."],"lt":"t-respiratory","lid":"c30","comp":100,"competencyId":"resp-tree"},{"id":"L02127","dok":3,"q":"On a slide, an airway has a few alveoli budding from its walls. It is the first airway of the respiratory zone. Which structure is it?","a":"A respiratory bronchiole.","options":["A respiratory bronchiole","An alveolar sac","A terminal bronchiole","An alveolar duct"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The respiratory bronchiole comes right after the terminal bronchiole. A few alveoli bud from its walls, so some gas exchange can happen there, which makes it the first part of the respiratory zone. Farther down, the alveolar ducts are lined entirely with alveoli.","notes":["Correct. A respiratory bronchiole has a few alveoli budding from its walls.","An alveolar sac is a cluster of alveoli sharing a common space at the end of the tree. It is a dead-end cluster rather than an airway with a few buds.","A terminal bronchiole has no alveoli in its wall and is the last airway of the conducting zone. Pick it for the airway just before the respiratory zone.","An alveolar duct is lined entirely with alveoli, not just a few. Pick it if the walls were made completely of alveoli."],"lt":"t-respiratory","lid":"c31","comp":102,"competencyId":"resp-histo"},{"id":"L02130","dok":2,"q":"Which part of the lung is the broad inferior surface that rests on the diaphragm?","a":"The base of the lung.","options":["The cardiac notch","The base of the lung","The hilum of the lung","The apex of the lung"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The base of each lung is its broad, concave lower surface, shaped to sit on the dome of the diaphragm. The opposite end is the narrow apex, which rises above the clavicle. The heart uses the same two words the other way around, with its apex pointing down and its base at the top.","notes":["The cardiac notch is the indentation on the anterior border of the left lung that makes room for the heart. It is not the surface on the diaphragm.","Correct. The base is the broad inferior surface resting on the diaphragm.","The hilum is the indentation on the medial surface where the bronchus and vessels enter and leave. Pick it for a question about the entry point of the root of the lung.","The apex is the narrow superior tip of the lung, above the clavicle. Pick it for a question about the top of the lung."],"lt":"t-respiratory","lid":"c34","comp":101,"competencyId":"resp-lungs-pleura"},{"id":"L02131","dok":2,"q":"Which respiratory passage has walls lined entirely with alveoli?","a":"An alveolar duct.","options":["A respiratory bronchiole","A terminal bronchiole","A segmental bronchus","An alveolar duct"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Alveolar ducts come after the respiratory bronchioles in the respiratory zone. Their walls are made entirely of alveoli opening one after another, and they lead into the alveolar sacs. Moving from respiratory bronchiole to alveolar duct, the number of alveoli increases from a few to complete coverage.","notes":["A respiratory bronchiole has only a few alveoli budding from its walls. Pick it if the airway showed scattered alveoli rather than complete coverage.","A terminal bronchiole has no alveoli at all and is the last conducting airway. Pick it for the airway just before gas exchange begins.","A segmental bronchus is a tertiary bronchus with cartilage in its wall and no alveoli. It belongs to the conducting zone, well above the alveolar ducts.","Correct. An alveolar duct is lined entirely with alveoli."],"lt":"t-respiratory","lid":"c35","comp":102,"competencyId":"resp-histo"},{"id":"L02132","dok":2,"q":"Which thin, sealed, fluid-filled space between two membranes lets the lung glide as it expands?","a":"The pleural cavity.","options":["The pleural cavity","The mediastinum","The alveolar sac","The hilum"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The pleural cavity is the thin space between the parietal pleura on the thoracic wall and the visceral pleura on the lung. It holds only a film of serous fluid, which lets the two layers slide smoothly as the lung expands and recoils. Because it is sealed, letting air in causes the lung to collapse.","notes":["Correct. The pleural cavity is the thin fluid-filled space that lets the lung glide.","The mediastinum is the central compartment of the thorax between the lungs, holding the heart, great vessels, trachea, and esophagus. It is not the space around each lung.","An alveolar sac is a cluster of alveoli that holds air for gas exchange. It is inside the lung, not between two membranes.","The hilum is the indentation on the medial lung surface where the bronchus and vessels enter. It is a region of the lung, not a fluid-filled space."],"lt":"t-respiratory","lid":"c36","comp":101,"competencyId":"resp-lungs-pleura"},{"id":"L02133","dok":2,"q":"Which cell type is thin and flat and forms most of the alveolar wall, where gases cross?","a":"Type I alveolar cells.","options":["Type I alveolar cells","Alveolar macrophages","Type II alveolar cells","Goblet cells"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Type I alveolar cells are simple squamous cells, meaning thin and flat. They cover most of the alveolar surface and form the air side of the respiratory membrane. Their thinness gives gases a very short distance to cross between alveolar air and the blood in the capillaries.","notes":["Correct. Type I alveolar cells form most of the alveolar wall.","Alveolar macrophages, or dust cells, wander over the alveolar surface and engulf debris. Pick them for a question about cleaning the alveoli.","Type II alveolar cells are cuboidal cells that secrete surfactant. Pick them for a question about the cells that keep alveoli from collapsing.","Goblet cells secrete mucus in the respiratory mucosa of the airways, not in the alveolar wall. Pick them for a question about where airway mucus comes from."],"lt":"t-respiratory","lid":"c37","comp":102,"competencyId":"resp-histo"},{"id":"L02134","dok":2,"q":"Which structure is the internal ridge where the trachea forks into the two main bronchi?","a":"The carina.","options":["The laryngeal prominence","The carina","The hilum","The glottis"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The carina is a ridge of cartilage inside the lower end of the trachea, at the point where it splits into the right and left main bronchi. Its lining is very sensitive, so anything touching it triggers strong coughing. It marks the end of the trachea and the start of the bronchi.","notes":["The laryngeal prominence is the Adam's apple, formed by the thyroid cartilage. It is an external landmark on the larynx, not an internal ridge in the trachea.","Correct. The carina is the ridge where the trachea forks.","The hilum is the indentation on the medial lung surface where the main bronchus enters the lung. It is where the bronchus enters, not where the trachea splits.","The glottis is the vocal folds and the opening between them, in the larynx above the trachea. Pick it for a question about voice production."],"lt":"t-respiratory","lid":"c38","comp":100,"competencyId":"resp-tree"},{"id":"L02135","dok":2,"q":"Which bronchus is a tertiary bronchus supplying a single bronchopulmonary segment?","a":"A segmental bronchus.","options":["A main bronchus","A lobar bronchus","A segmental bronchus","A terminal bronchiole"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The bronchial tree branches in order: main (primary) bronchi to each lung, lobar (secondary) bronchi to each lobe, and segmental (tertiary) bronchi to each bronchopulmonary segment. A bronchopulmonary segment is a smaller section of a lobe with its own airway and blood supply. The segmental bronchi then branch further into bronchioles.","notes":["A main bronchus is a primary bronchus that supplies a whole lung. Pick it for a question about the first branch after the carina.","A lobar bronchus is a secondary bronchus that supplies one lobe. Pick it for a question about one lobe.","Correct. A segmental bronchus is a tertiary bronchus that supplies one bronchopulmonary segment.","A terminal bronchiole is the last conducting airway, far smaller than a bronchus and without cartilage. Pick it for the airway just before the respiratory zone."],"lt":"t-respiratory","lid":"c39","comp":100,"competencyId":"resp-tree"},{"id":"L02136","dok":2,"q":"Which respiratory disorder is the destruction of the alveolar walls, reducing the surface available for gas exchange?","a":"Emphysema.","options":["Pneumothorax","Asthma","Emphysema","Lung cancer"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"In emphysema the walls between alveoli break down, and many small sacs merge into a few large ones. Large sacs have far less total surface than many small ones, so the area available for gas exchange shrinks. The change is structural and permanent.","notes":["Pneumothorax is air in the pleural cavity that collapses the lung. Pick it for a question about a puncture that breaks the pleural seal.","Asthma is inflammation and narrowing of the bronchioles, causing wheezing. The alveolar walls stay intact.","Correct. Emphysema destroys alveolar walls and reduces the gas exchange surface.","Lung cancer is a malignant tumor of the lung tissue, strongly linked to smoking. Pick it for a question about a tumor rather than a breakdown of alveolar walls."],"lt":"t-respiratory","lid":"c40","comp":104,"competencyId":"resp-disorders"},{"id":"L02137","dok":2,"q":"Which respiratory disorder is an infection that inflames the alveoli and fills them with fluid?","a":"Pneumonia.","options":["Asthma","Pneumonia","Pneumothorax","Emphysema"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Pneumonia is an infection of the lung in which the alveoli become inflamed and fill with fluid. Fluid-filled alveoli cannot hold air, so less surface is available for gas exchange. Unlike emphysema, the alveolar walls are not destroyed.","notes":["Asthma narrows the bronchioles, not the alveoli. Pick it for a question about wheezing from small airway narrowing.","Correct. Pneumonia is an infection that fills the alveoli with fluid.","Pneumothorax is air in the pleural cavity, outside the lung. Pick it for a question about a collapsed lung after a chest wound.","Emphysema destroys the alveolar walls rather than filling the alveoli with fluid. Pick it for a question about permanent loss of alveolar surface."],"lt":"t-respiratory","lid":"c41","comp":104,"competencyId":"resp-disorders"},{"id":"L02138","dok":2,"q":"Which thin barrier lies between the alveolar air and the blood, and is the structure that gases cross?","a":"The respiratory membrane.","options":["The bronchiole wall","The respiratory membrane","The parietal pleura","The visceral pleura"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The respiratory membrane is the extremely thin barrier between alveolar air and the blood in a pulmonary capillary. It is made of the alveolar epithelium, the fused basement membranes, and the capillary endothelium. Oxygen and carbon dioxide cross it during gas exchange.","notes":["The bronchiole wall has smooth muscle and no cartilage, and it is far too thick for gases to cross, so it only carries air. Pick it for a question about the airways narrowed in asthma.","Correct. The respiratory membrane is the barrier that gases cross between air and blood.","The parietal pleura lines the thoracic wall. Pick it for a question about the outer layer of the pleural membranes.","The visceral pleura covers the outside of the whole lung. It faces the pleural cavity, not the air inside the alveoli."],"lt":"t-respiratory","lid":"c42","comp":102,"competencyId":"resp-histo"},{"id":"L02139","dok":2,"q":"How does the right main bronchus differ in shape from the left main bronchus?","a":"It is wider and more vertical.","options":["It is narrower and more horizontal","It has no cartilage, like a bronchiole","It is wider and more vertical","It is held open by complete cartilage rings"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The right main bronchus is wider and runs more vertically than the left, which is narrower and angles out more horizontally to pass under the aortic arch. This shape makes the right main bronchus the more direct continuation of the trachea. That is why inhaled objects most often lodge in the right lung.","notes":["Narrower and more horizontal describes the left main bronchus, the mirror image of the correct answer. Pick it if the question asked about the left side.","Both main bronchi contain cartilage. Losing all cartilage happens much farther down the tree, at the bronchioles.","Correct. The right main bronchus is wider and more vertical.","The cricoid cartilage is the only complete cartilage ring in the airway. The main bronchi are supported by incomplete cartilage, so this is not what sets the right side apart."],"lt":"t-respiratory","lid":"c43","comp":100,"competencyId":"resp-tree"},{"id":"L02140","dok":2,"q":"Which respiratory disorder is a long-term blockage of airflow that includes both emphysema and chronic bronchitis?","a":"COPD.","options":["COPD","Asthma","Pneumonia","Pneumothorax"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"COPD stands for chronic obstructive pulmonary disease. It is a long-term blockage of airflow that groups together emphysema and chronic bronchitis, and it affects both the airways and the alveoli. Chronic means long-lasting and obstructive means airflow is blocked.","notes":["Correct. COPD includes both emphysema and chronic bronchitis.","Asthma narrows the bronchioles and causes wheezing, but it is a separate disorder from the COPD group. Pick it for a question about inflammation of the small airways.","Pneumonia is an infection that fills the alveoli with fluid. It is usually a short-term illness rather than a long-term blockage.","Pneumothorax is air in the pleural cavity that collapses the lung. Pick it for a question about a punctured chest wall."],"lt":"t-respiratory","lid":"c44","comp":104,"competencyId":"resp-disorders"},{"id":"L02141","dok":2,"q":"Which sequence correctly orders the airways a breath passes through after the larynx?","a":"Trachea, main bronchi, lobar bronchi, segmental bronchi.","options":["Main bronchi, trachea, segmental bronchi, lobar bronchi","Trachea, lobar bronchi, main bronchi, segmental bronchi","Trachea, main bronchi, lobar bronchi, segmental bronchi","Trachea, segmental bronchi, lobar bronchi, main bronchi"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Below the larynx, air enters the trachea, which forks at the carina into the main (primary) bronchi, one per lung. Each main bronchus splits into lobar (secondary) bronchi, one per lobe, and each lobar bronchus splits into segmental (tertiary) bronchi, one per bronchopulmonary segment. The airways get narrower and more numerous at each step.","notes":["The trachea comes before the main bronchi, since the main bronchi are formed by the trachea splitting. This order also puts segmental before lobar, which reverses that step.","This swaps the main and lobar bronchi. The trachea forks into the main bronchi first, and only then do they divide into lobar bronchi.","Correct. Trachea, main bronchi, lobar bronchi, then segmental bronchi.","This keeps the trachea first but then lists the bronchi from smallest to largest. The branches get smaller at every division after the trachea, so the main bronchi must come next."],"lt":"t-respiratory","lid":"c45","comp":100,"competencyId":"resp-tree"},{"id":"L02143","dok":2,"q":"Which sequence correctly orders the deepest airways as a breath reaches gas exchange?","a":"Terminal bronchiole, respiratory bronchiole, alveolar duct, alveolus.","options":["Alveolar duct, terminal bronchiole, respiratory bronchiole, alveolus","Respiratory bronchiole, terminal bronchiole, alveolar duct, alveolus","Terminal bronchiole, respiratory bronchiole, alveolar duct, alveolus","Terminal bronchiole, alveolar duct, respiratory bronchiole, alveolus"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The terminal bronchiole is the last airway of the conducting zone. Next comes the respiratory bronchiole, with a few alveoli budding from its walls, followed by the alveolar duct, lined entirely with alveoli. The duct ends in alveolar sacs made of individual alveoli, where gas exchange happens.","notes":["The alveolar duct is in the respiratory zone, so it cannot come before the terminal bronchiole, which is still in the conducting zone.","This swaps the two bronchioles. The terminal bronchiole has no alveoli and comes first, before the respiratory bronchiole that begins the respiratory zone.","Correct. Terminal bronchiole, respiratory bronchiole, alveolar duct, then alveolus.","This puts the alveolar duct before the respiratory bronchiole. The number of alveoli increases along the path, so the airway with a few alveoli comes before the one lined entirely with them."],"lt":"t-respiratory","lid":"c47","comp":97,"competencyId":"resp-zones"},{"id":"L02145","dok":2,"q":"Which set of features correctly describes the left lung?","a":"Two lobes, an oblique fissure only, and a cardiac notch.","options":["Three lobes, an oblique fissure only, and no notch","Two lobes, an oblique fissure only, and a cardiac notch","Three lobes, two fissures, and a cardiac notch","Two lobes, two fissures, and no notch"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The left lung is the smaller lung because the heart takes up room on the left. It has two lobes, superior and inferior, divided by a single oblique fissure. The cardiac notch on its anterior border makes room for the heart, and the lingula projects below that notch.","notes":["Three lobes need two fissures to separate them, so this combination does not exist in either lung. The right lung has three lobes and two fissures.","Correct. The left lung has two lobes, one oblique fissure, and a cardiac notch.","Three lobes and two fissures describe the right lung, and the right lung has no cardiac notch. This set mixes features of both lungs.","Two lobes are right for the left lung, but it has only one fissure, and it does have a cardiac notch. The second fissure, the horizontal fissure, belongs to the right lung."],"lt":"t-respiratory","lid":"c49","comp":101,"competencyId":"resp-lungs-pleura"},{"id":"L02146","dok":3,"q":"Which set correctly names the last airway of the conducting zone, the first airway of the respiratory zone, and the tiny air sac where gas exchange happens?","a":"Terminal bronchiole, respiratory bronchiole, alveolus.","options":["Respiratory bronchiole, terminal bronchiole, alveolar sac","Terminal bronchiole, respiratory bronchiole, alveolus","Segmental bronchus, terminal bronchiole, alveolus","Bronchiole, terminal bronchiole, alveolar duct"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The terminal bronchiole has no alveoli and ends the conducting zone. The respiratory bronchiole has a few alveoli budding from its walls, so it begins the respiratory zone. The alveolus is the tiny air sac where gas exchange happens across the respiratory membrane.","notes":["This swaps the two bronchioles. The respiratory bronchiole is the first respiratory zone airway, not the last conducting one.","Correct. Terminal bronchiole, respiratory bronchiole, and alveolus mark the boundary and the endpoint.","The segmental bronchus is far above the end of the conducting zone, and the terminal bronchiole has no alveoli, so it does not start the respiratory zone.","The terminal bronchiole still belongs to the conducting zone, so it cannot be the first airway of the respiratory zone. The alveolar duct is also a passage, not the air sac where gases cross."],"lt":"t-respiratory","lid":"c50","comp":97,"competencyId":"resp-zones"},{"id":"L02148","dok":1,"q":"Which structures make up the lower respiratory tract?","a":"The larynx, trachea, bronchi, and lungs.","options":["The nose, nasal cavity, and paranasal sinuses","The pharynx and the paranasal sinuses","The nasopharynx, oropharynx, and laryngopharynx","The larynx, trachea, bronchi, and lungs"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The lower respiratory tract starts at the larynx and continues down through the trachea and bronchi into the lungs. It includes both conducting airways and the alveoli of the respiratory zone. The location grouping (upper and lower tract) does not line up with the functional grouping (conducting and respiratory zones), which is why both are tested.","notes":["The nose, nasal cavity, and paranasal sinuses are upper tract structures. Pick this set for a question about where air is first warmed and filtered.","The pharynx and paranasal sinuses both belong to the upper respiratory tract. Pick them for a question about the upper tract.","These are the three regions of the pharynx, which belongs to the upper tract. Pick this set for a question about how the pharynx is divided.","Correct. The larynx, trachea, bronchi, and lungs form the lower tract."],"lt":"t-respiratory","lid":"c2-x2","comp":97,"competencyId":"resp-zones"},{"id":"L02153","dok":1,"q":"What holds the trachea permanently open?","a":"C-shaped rings of hyaline cartilage.","options":["Complete rings of hyaline cartilage","The trachealis muscle","Plates of elastic cartilage","C-shaped rings of hyaline cartilage"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The trachea is held open by a stack of C-shaped rings of hyaline cartilage, so the airway never collapses. The open part of each C faces backward, toward the esophagus. That gap is closed by the trachealis muscle, a band of smooth muscle.","notes":["The cricoid cartilage of the larynx is the only complete cartilage ring in the airway. Tracheal rings are open at the back, so they are C-shaped rather than complete.","The trachealis muscle is smooth muscle that closes the gap at the back of each C-shaped ring. It spans the gap but is not what holds the airway open.","Elastic cartilage forms the epiglottis, which must bend and spring back. The trachea uses rigid hyaline cartilage to stay open.","Correct. C-shaped rings of hyaline cartilage keep the trachea open."],"lt":"t-respiratory","lid":"c8-x2","comp":100,"competencyId":"resp-tree"},{"id":"L02154","dok":1,"q":"Which structures pass through the hilum of a lung as part of the root of the lung?","a":"The main bronchus and the pulmonary vessels.","options":["The coronary arteries and the cardiac veins","The trachea and the aortic arch","The inferior vena cava and the thoracic duct","The main bronchus and the pulmonary vessels"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The hilum is the indentation on the medial surface of each lung. The bundle passing through it is the root of the lung: the main bronchus, the pulmonary artery, the pulmonary veins, bronchial vessels, nerves, and lymphatics. These structures connect the lung to the trachea and heart in the mediastinum.","notes":["The coronary arteries and cardiac veins supply and drain the heart wall. Pick them for a question about the blood supply of the heart.","The trachea and aortic arch stay in the mediastinum. The trachea divides into the main bronchi before reaching either lung, and the aortic arch curves over the left main bronchus without entering the lung.","The inferior vena cava returns blood from the lower body to the right atrium, and the thoracic duct runs up through the posterior thorax. Neither enters the lung.","Correct. The main bronchus and the pulmonary vessels form the root of the lung at the hilum."],"lt":"t-respiratory","lid":"c10-x2","comp":101,"competencyId":"resp-lungs-pleura"},{"id":"L02155","dok":1,"q":"Where in the lungs does gas exchange take place?","a":"In the alveoli.","options":["In the trachea","In the alveoli","In the terminal bronchioles","In the main bronchi"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Gas exchange happens in the alveoli, the tiny air sacs at the ends of the airways. Their walls are thin type I cells wrapped in pulmonary capillaries, forming the respiratory membrane that oxygen and carbon dioxide cross. The lungs hold hundreds of millions of alveoli, giving a huge exchange surface.","notes":["The trachea is a conducting airway held open by cartilage. Its walls are far too thick for gases to cross into the blood.","Correct. Gas exchange happens in the alveoli.","Terminal bronchioles are the last airways of the conducting zone. They carry air to the respiratory zone but have no alveoli, so no gas exchange happens there.","The main bronchi carry air into each lung through the hilum. They are conducting airways, not exchange surfaces."],"lt":"t-respiratory","lid":"c12-x2","comp":102,"competencyId":"resp-histo"},{"id":"L02156","dok":2,"q":"Which layers make up the respiratory membrane?","a":"Alveolar epithelium, fused basement membranes, and capillary endothelium.","options":["Type II cells, a surfactant film, and alveolar macrophages","Alveolar epithelium, fused basement membranes, and capillary endothelium","Ciliated epithelium, hyaline cartilage plates, and smooth muscle","Visceral pleura, pleural fluid, and parietal pleura"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The respiratory membrane is the thin barrier between the air in an alveolus and the blood in a pulmonary capillary. It has three layers: the alveolar epithelium (mostly type I cells), the fused basement membranes of the alveolus and capillary, and the capillary endothelium. Keeping it this thin gives gases a very short distance to cross.","notes":["Type II cells, surfactant, and alveolar macrophages are all found in or on the alveolus, but they are not the layers that gases cross. Pick them for a question about what keeps alveoli open and clean.","Correct. The respiratory membrane is alveolar epithelium, fused basement membranes, and capillary endothelium.","Ciliated epithelium, hyaline cartilage, and smooth muscle describe the wall of a bronchus. Pick this set for a question about the layers of a large conducting airway.","The visceral pleura, pleural fluid, and parietal pleura make up the pleural membranes around the lung. Pick this set for a question about the layers a surgeon meets around the lung."],"lt":"t-respiratory","lid":"c14-x2","comp":102,"competencyId":"resp-histo"},{"id":"L02158","dok":2,"q":"Where is the carina located?","a":"At the lower end of the trachea, where it forks into the main bronchi.","options":["At the upper end of the trachea, just below the cricoid cartilage","At the hilum of each lung, where the bronchus enters","At the lower end of the trachea, where it forks into the main bronchi","Inside the larynx, between the true vocal folds"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The carina is an internal ridge at the inferior end of the trachea, inside the thorax. It marks the exact point where the trachea divides into the right and left main bronchi. The lining over the carina is very sensitive, so contact there sets off coughing.","notes":["The upper end of the trachea, just below the cricoid cartilage, is where the trachea begins. Students mix the two ends up, but the carina is at the bottom where the trachea forks.","The hilum is the indentation on each lung where the main bronchus enters. The carina is in the mediastinum before the bronchi reach either lung.","Correct. The carina is at the lower end of the trachea, where it splits.","The space between the true vocal folds is part of the glottis in the larynx. Pick the glottis for a question about the opening between the vocal folds."],"lt":"t-respiratory","lid":"c16-x2","comp":100,"competencyId":"resp-tree"},{"id":"L02159","dok":2,"q":"After the main bronchi, which airways branch next, one to each lobe of a lung?","a":"The lobar bronchi.","options":["The terminal bronchioles","The segmental bronchi","The alveolar ducts","The lobar bronchi"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Each main bronchus divides into lobar (secondary) bronchi, one for each lobe. The right main bronchus gives off three lobar bronchi for its three lobes, and the left gives off two. The lobar bronchi then divide into segmental bronchi.","notes":["Terminal bronchioles are the last conducting airways, many divisions below the main bronchi. Pick them for the airway just before the respiratory zone.","The segmental bronchi are tertiary bronchi that branch from the lobar bronchi, one to each bronchopulmonary segment. They come one step later.","Alveolar ducts are respiratory zone passages lined with alveoli, near the very end of the tree. Pick them for the airway after the respiratory bronchiole.","Correct. The lobar bronchi come next, one to each lobe."],"lt":"t-respiratory","lid":"c17-x2","comp":100,"competencyId":"resp-tree"},{"id":"L02160","dok":2,"q":"Which tongue-shaped projection of the left superior lobe lies just below the cardiac notch?","a":"The lingula.","options":["The lingula","The apex","The hilum","The middle lobe"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The lingula is a small, tongue-shaped extension of the left superior lobe, just below the cardiac notch. Its name means little tongue. It sits in roughly the same position on the left that the middle lobe occupies on the right, but it is part of the superior lobe rather than a separate lobe.","notes":["Correct. The lingula is the tongue-shaped part of the left superior lobe.","The apex is the narrow superior tip of the lung, above the clavicle. Pick it for a question about the top of the lung.","The hilum is the indentation on the medial surface where the bronchus and vessels enter. Pick it for a question about the root of the lung.","The middle lobe belongs only to the right lung, between its horizontal and oblique fissures. The left lung has no middle lobe."],"lt":"t-respiratory","lid":"c18-x2","comp":101,"competencyId":"resp-lungs-pleura"},{"id":"L02162","dok":2,"q":"A terminal bronchiole and a respiratory bronchiole sit next to each other in the bronchial tree. Which feature is found in the respiratory bronchiole but not the terminal bronchiole?","a":"Alveoli budding from its walls.","options":["Plates of hyaline cartilage","C-shaped cartilage rings","A lining of stratified squamous epithelium","Alveoli budding from its walls"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The terminal bronchiole is the last conducting airway and has no alveoli. The respiratory bronchiole that follows has a few alveoli budding from its walls, so a little gas exchange happens there, and that is why it begins the respiratory zone. Neither airway has cartilage.","notes":["Plates of hyaline cartilage support the bronchi inside the lung. Cartilage is gone by the bronchiole level, so neither of these airways has it.","C-shaped cartilage rings are found in the trachea. Bronchioles of every kind have no cartilage.","Stratified squamous epithelium lines the oropharynx and laryngopharynx, where food causes friction. Bronchioles are lined by much thinner epithelium.","Correct. A few alveoli bud from the walls of a respiratory bronchiole."],"lt":"t-respiratory","lid":"c20-x2","comp":102,"competencyId":"resp-histo"},{"id":"L02163","dok":2,"q":"As the bronchial tree branches into smaller and smaller airways, how do cartilage and smooth muscle change?","a":"Cartilage decreases and smooth muscle increases.","options":["Cartilage decreases and smooth muscle increases","Both cartilage and smooth muscle increase","Cartilage increases and smooth muscle decreases","Both stay the same at every level"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The large airways need cartilage to stay open, so the trachea has C-shaped rings and the bronchi have plates. As the airways get smaller, the cartilage fades out and smooth muscle takes over. By the bronchioles there is no cartilage at all, so when their smooth muscle contracts they narrow a great deal, while cartilage keeps the larger bronchi open.","notes":["Correct. Cartilage decreases and smooth muscle increases down the tree.","Cartilage does not increase as airways shrink. By the bronchioles it has disappeared completely.","This is the reverse of the real pattern. The trachea has the most cartilage, and the bronchioles have none.","The airway wall changes a lot along the tree, from cartilage rings in the trachea to no cartilage in the bronchioles. If the wall stayed the same, the bronchioles would have cartilage holding them open and could not narrow so much in asthma."],"lt":"t-respiratory","lid":"c21-x2","comp":100,"competencyId":"resp-tree"},{"id":"L02164","dok":1,"q":"What normally fills the pleural cavity?","a":"A thin film of serous pleural fluid.","options":["A thin film of serous pleural fluid","Lymph draining from the lung","Blood from the pulmonary capillaries","Air drawn in with each breath"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The pleural cavity is the thin, sealed space between the parietal and visceral pleurae. It holds only a thin film of serous fluid, which lets the two layers glide smoothly as the lung expands. Anything else in this space, such as air or blood, breaks the seal and can collapse the lung.","notes":["Correct. A thin film of serous fluid fills the pleural cavity.","Lymph from the lung drains through lymphatic vessels to nodes at the hilum and in the mediastinum. It travels inside vessels, not loose in the pleural cavity.","Blood from the pulmonary capillaries stays inside vessels and returns through the pulmonary veins. Blood in the pleural cavity would be a sign of injury.","Air in the pleural cavity is a pneumothorax, which makes the lung collapse. Inhaled air belongs inside the airways and alveoli, not between the pleurae."],"lt":"t-respiratory","lid":"c22-x2","comp":101,"competencyId":"resp-lungs-pleura"},{"id":"L02165","dok":1,"q":"Which structures does emphysema destroy?","a":"The alveolar walls.","options":["The alveolar walls","The bronchiole walls","The pleural membranes","The tracheal cartilage"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Emphysema destroys the walls of the alveoli. As the walls break down, many small alveoli merge into a few large spaces, and the total surface for gas exchange drops. Emphysema is one of the two conditions grouped under COPD.","notes":["Correct. Emphysema destroys the alveolar walls.","The bronchioles are the target of asthma, which inflames and narrows them. Pick the bronchioles for a question about asthma.","The pleural membranes are involved in a pneumothorax, when air enters the space between them. Emphysema damages the lung tissue itself.","The tracheal cartilage is not damaged in emphysema. Pick the trachea for a question about the C-shaped rings that hold the windpipe open."],"lt":"t-respiratory","lid":"c23-x2","comp":104,"competencyId":"resp-disorders"},{"id":"L02166","dok":3,"q":"During an asthma attack, the bronchioles narrow but the trachea and main bronchi stay open. Which structural difference explains this?","a":"Bronchioles have smooth muscle and no cartilage, while larger airways are held open by cartilage.","options":["The trachea and main bronchi have no smooth muscle at all","Bronchioles have complete cartilage rings that squeeze shut","Bronchioles lack cartilage, while larger airways are held open by it","The main bronchi are lined with alveoli that hold them open"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Asthma inflames and narrows the airways, and the narrowing is worst in the bronchioles. A bronchiole has no cartilage, and smooth muscle makes up much of its wall, so when that muscle tightens and the lining swells, nothing holds the airway open. The trachea and bronchi have cartilage rings and plates that keep them open even when their walls are irritated.","notes":["The trachea does have smooth muscle, the trachealis, across the back of its C-shaped rings. What keeps it open is the cartilage, not a lack of muscle.","Bronchioles have no cartilage at all. The only complete cartilage ring in the airway is the cricoid cartilage of the larynx.","Correct. Without cartilage, bronchioles can narrow, while cartilage keeps larger airways open.","The main bronchi are conducting airways with no alveoli. Alveoli first appear in the respiratory bronchioles."],"lt":"t-respiratory","lid":"c24-x2","comp":104,"competencyId":"resp-disorders"},{"id":"L02167","dok":3,"q":"A child inhales a small bead that slips past the epiglottis into the lower airway. Which lung does it most often land in, and why?","a":"The right lung, because the right main bronchus is wider and more vertical.","options":["The left lung, because the left main bronchus is wider and more vertical","The left lung, because it has only two lobes","The right lung, because the right main bronchus is wider and more vertical","The right lung, because it has a cardiac notch that traps objects"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The right main bronchus is wider and runs more vertically than the left, so it lines up more directly with the trachea. An inhaled object falling down the trachea tends to follow this straighter path. That is why aspirated objects most often lodge in the right lung.","notes":["This assigns the right bronchus's shape to the left. The left main bronchus is narrower and more horizontal, which makes objects less likely to enter it.","The number of lobes does not decide where an object lands. The angle and width of the main bronchus does.","Correct. The wider, more vertical right main bronchus lines up with the trachea.","The cardiac notch is on the left lung, not the right. It makes room for the heart and does not trap inhaled objects."],"lt":"t-respiratory","lid":"c25-x2","comp":100,"competencyId":"resp-tree"},{"id":"L02168","dok":3,"q":"A stab wound punctures the chest wall, and the lung on that side collapses. Into which space has air entered?","a":"The pleural cavity.","options":["The alveoli","The mediastinum","The pericardial cavity","The pleural cavity"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The pleural cavity is a sealed space between the parietal and visceral pleurae that normally holds only a thin film of fluid. A wound through the chest wall lets air into this space, breaking the seal, and the lung collapses. This is called a pneumothorax.","notes":["Air belongs in the alveoli, and air there does not collapse the lung. The problem in a pneumothorax is air outside the lung.","The mediastinum is the central compartment of the thorax holding the heart, trachea, and esophagus. Pick it for a question about the space between the two lungs.","The pericardial cavity surrounds the heart. Air or fluid there would press on the heart, not collapse a lung.","Correct. Air in the pleural cavity breaks the seal and collapses the lung."],"lt":"t-respiratory","lid":"c26-x2","comp":104,"competencyId":"resp-disorders"},{"id":"L02169","dok":3,"q":"An infection inflames the air sacs of the lung and fills them with fluid. Which structure is affected, and which disorder is this?","a":"The alveoli, in pneumonia.","options":["The trachea, in COPD","The bronchioles, in asthma","The pleural cavity, in pneumothorax","The alveoli, in pneumonia"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The air sacs of the lung are the alveoli. In pneumonia, an infection inflames the alveoli and fills them with fluid, so they cannot hold air for gas exchange. Naming both the structure and the disorder links the problem to the exact place it happens.","notes":["COPD involves the airways and alveoli and includes emphysema and chronic bronchitis. It is a long-term blockage, not an infection of the trachea.","Asthma inflames and narrows the bronchioles, the small airways, not the air sacs. It causes wheezing rather than fluid-filled alveoli.","Pneumothorax is air in the pleural cavity that collapses the lung. It is not an infection of the air sacs.","Correct. Pneumonia fills the alveoli with fluid."],"lt":"t-respiratory","lid":"c27-x2","comp":104,"competencyId":"resp-disorders"},{"id":"L02170","dok":3,"q":"In emphysema, many small alveoli merge into a few large ones. Which structural feature of the lung is lost?","a":"The large surface area for gas exchange.","options":["The seal of the pleural cavity","The C-shaped tracheal rings","The large surface area for gas exchange","The cartilage of the bronchi"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Many tiny alveoli together have far more wall surface than a few large sacs holding the same volume. When emphysema destroys the walls between alveoli, the small sacs merge and much of that surface disappears. Less surface means less area where gases can cross, and the change is permanent.","notes":["Losing the pleural seal is a pneumothorax, caused by air entering the pleural cavity. Emphysema damages the alveoli, not the pleura.","The tracheal rings are cartilage in the windpipe and are not affected by emphysema. Pick them for a question about what holds the trachea open.","Correct. Merging alveoli lose much of the surface area for gas exchange.","Bronchial cartilage is in the larger airways, not the alveoli. Emphysema destroys alveolar walls, which have no cartilage."],"lt":"t-respiratory","lid":"c28-x2","comp":104,"competencyId":"resp-disorders"},{"id":"L02171","dok":2,"q":"Which two conditions are grouped together under COPD?","a":"Emphysema and chronic bronchitis.","options":["Asthma and pneumonia","Asthma and pneumothorax","Pneumothorax and lung cancer","Emphysema and chronic bronchitis"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"COPD, chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, is a long-term blockage of airflow. It groups emphysema, which destroys alveolar walls, with chronic bronchitis, a long-lasting inflammation of the bronchi. Together they affect both the airways and the alveoli.","notes":["Asthma narrows the bronchi and bronchioles, and pneumonia fills the alveoli with fluid. Both are lung disorders, but this pair is not what COPD groups together.","Asthma and pneumothorax affect the bronchioles and the pleural cavity. Asthma also blocks airflow, which may cause the mix-up, but it is classified separately from COPD.","Pneumothorax is air in the pleural cavity and lung cancer is a malignant tumor. Neither is a long-term blockage of airflow grouped under COPD.","Correct. COPD includes emphysema and chronic bronchitis."],"lt":"t-respiratory","lid":"c29-x2","comp":104,"competencyId":"resp-disorders"},{"id":"L02172","dok":2,"q":"A patient is told a malignant tumor has formed in the tissue of the lung. Which disorder does this describe?","a":"Lung cancer.","options":["Lung cancer","Asthma","Pneumonia","Emphysema"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Lung cancer is a malignant tumor in the lung tissue. It usually starts in the epithelium lining the airways or alveoli, and it is strongly linked to smoking. Unlike the other choices here, it is defined by abnormal tissue growth rather than by destroyed walls, fluid in the air sacs, or narrowed airways.","notes":["Correct. A malignant tumor of the lung is lung cancer.","Asthma is inflammation and narrowing of the bronchioles. Pick it for a question about wheezing from the small airways.","Pneumonia is an infection that fills the alveoli with fluid. Pick it for a question about inflamed, fluid-filled air sacs.","Emphysema destroys the alveolar walls but does not form a tumor. Pick it for a question about lost surface area for gas exchange."],"lt":"t-respiratory","lid":"c30-x2","comp":104,"competencyId":"resp-disorders"},{"id":"L02173","dok":3,"q":"The lungs hold hundreds of millions of tiny alveoli, each wrapped in capillaries. Why does this design suit gas exchange?","a":"Together the tiny sacs create a huge, thin surface in close contact with the blood.","options":["Each alveolus has its own cartilage ring that keeps the lungs from collapsing","Together the tiny sacs create a huge, thin surface in close contact with the blood","The tiny sacs keep blood far from the air to protect the lung","Each alveolus connects directly to its own main bronchus so air arrives faster"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Dividing the lung into hundreds of millions of tiny sacs gives it an enormous total wall surface. Each alveolus is wrapped in pulmonary capillaries, and the wall between air and blood is extremely thin. A huge surface plus a short distance is what allows oxygen and carbon dioxide to cross quickly.","notes":["Alveoli have no cartilage. Their walls are thin type I cells, and surfactant from type II cells is what helps keep them from collapsing.","Correct. Many tiny alveoli give a huge, thin surface next to the blood.","The design does the opposite, bringing blood as close to the air as possible. The respiratory membrane is extremely thin so gases can cross easily.","There are only two main bronchi, one for each lung. Air reaches the alveoli through many branching airways, not through a private bronchus for each sac."],"lt":"t-respiratory","lid":"d3-t-respiratory-1","comp":102,"competencyId":"resp-histo"},{"id":"L02174","dok":3,"q":"Type I alveolar cells are thin, flat, simple squamous cells. Why is this shape well suited to their job?","a":"A thin wall gives gases a very short distance to cross between air and blood.","options":["A thin wall gives gases a very short distance to cross between air and blood","A flat shape lets them contract and push air out of the alveolus","A thin wall lets them secrete surfactant more easily","A flat shape lets them wander over the alveolar surface and engulf dust"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Type I alveolar cells form most of the alveolar wall and the air side of the respiratory membrane. Because they are simple squamous, a single layer of flat cells, gases only have to cross a very thin barrier to move between alveolar air and capillary blood. A thicker wall would slow that crossing.","notes":["Correct. Thin, flat cells keep the distance for gases short.","Alveolar cells do not contract. Air moves in and out because the diaphragm and chest wall change the size of the thoracic cavity and the lungs recoil, not because the alveolar wall squeezes.","Surfactant is secreted by type II alveolar cells, which are cuboidal, not flat. Pick type II cells for a question about surfactant.","Wandering and engulfing debris is the job of alveolar macrophages, the dust cells. Pick them for a question about cleaning the alveoli."],"lt":"t-respiratory","lid":"d3-t-respiratory-2","comp":102,"competencyId":"resp-histo"},{"id":"L02175","dok":3,"q":"The C-shaped cartilage rings of the trachea are open at the back, where the trachea lies against the esophagus. Why is this arrangement useful?","a":"The open back lets the esophagus expand forward when food is swallowed, while the cartilage still keeps the airway open.","options":["The open back faces forward to make room for the thyroid cartilage","The open back lets air pass from the trachea into the esophagus","The esophagus can expand forward during swallowing while the airway stays open","The open back lets the trachea collapse fully during each exhalation"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The trachea sits directly in front of the esophagus. The back of each C-shaped ring is open and closed only by the trachealis, a band of smooth muscle, so the soft back wall can give way when a swallowed mass of food stretches the esophagus. The cartilage in front and on the sides still keeps the airway open.","notes":["The open part of the C faces backward toward the esophagus, not forward. The thyroid cartilage is part of the larynx, above the trachea.","The trachea and esophagus are separate tubes, and the trachealis muscle closes the gap so air cannot cross between them. Air and food share only the pharynx.","Correct. The open back lets the esophagus expand while the cartilage keeps the airway open.","The cartilage rings exist to keep the trachea from collapsing. A trachea that collapsed with each breath would block airflow."],"lt":"t-respiratory","lid":"d3-t-respiratory-3","comp":100,"competencyId":"resp-tree"},{"id":"L02176","dok":3,"q":"The right lung has three lobes, while the left lung is smaller and has only two. Which explanation fits the anatomy of the thorax?","a":"The heart sits toward the left side of the thorax and takes up space the left lung would otherwise fill.","options":["The heart sits toward the left and takes up that space","The liver pushes up under the left lung and takes its space","The stomach fills the lower part of the left thoracic cavity","The left main bronchus is too narrow to supply three lobes"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The heart sits in the mediastinum but tilts toward the left, with its apex pointing down and to the left. The left lung gives up room for it, which is why the left lung is smaller, has only two lobes, and carries a cardiac notch along its anterior border. The right lung has more room and three lobes.","notes":["Correct. The heart takes up space on the left side of the thorax.","The liver lies mostly on the right, under the right dome of the diaphragm. This mirrors the real arrangement and would not explain a smaller left lung.","The stomach lies in the abdominal cavity, below the diaphragm. It does not sit in the thoracic cavity.","The number of lobar bronchi follows the number of lobes, not the other way around. A narrower bronchus does not determine lobe count."],"lt":"t-respiratory","lid":"d3-t-respiratory-4","comp":101,"competencyId":"resp-lungs-pleura"},{"id":"L02177","dok":3,"q":"Airways branch again and again, getting narrower and more numerous from the trachea to the bronchioles. Why does this branching pattern serve breathing well?","a":"Repeated branching carries air to hundreds of millions of alveoli spread throughout both lungs.","options":["Repeated branching carries air to hundreds of millions of alveoli throughout both lungs","Branching lets each airway exchange gases along its full length","Each branch adds more cartilage, making the lungs stiffer","Branching keeps air in the conducting zone so it never reaches the alveoli"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The alveoli are spread through every part of both lungs, so air has to be delivered to all of them. The bronchial tree divides from the trachea into main, lobar, and segmental bronchi and then bronchioles, reaching every region of the lung. The more branches there are, the more alveoli can be reached.","notes":["Correct. Branching delivers air to alveoli throughout both lungs.","The conducting airways have walls too thick for gas exchange. Exchange only happens in the respiratory zone, starting at the respiratory bronchioles.","Cartilage actually decreases as the airways branch and gets replaced by smooth muscle. The bronchioles have no cartilage.","The conducting zone's job is to lead air to the respiratory zone, not to keep it out. Air must reach the alveoli for gas exchange."],"lt":"t-respiratory","lid":"d3-t-respiratory-5","comp":100,"competencyId":"resp-tree"},{"id":"L02179","dok":3,"q":"The airways are lined with ciliated epithelium and mucus-secreting goblet cells. How does this lining protect the delicate alveoli?","a":"Mucus traps inhaled particles and cilia sweep them up toward the pharynx to be swallowed.","options":["Cilia sweep particles down into the alveoli, where macrophages digest them","Goblet cells secrete surfactant that dissolves inhaled dust","Mucus traps inhaled particles and cilia sweep them up toward the pharynx","Cartilage in the lining filters particles out of the air"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The conducting airways are lined by pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium with goblet cells. The goblet cells secrete sticky mucus that traps dust and microbes, and the cilia beat to move the mucus up toward the pharynx, where it is swallowed. This keeps most particles from ever reaching the thin-walled alveoli.","notes":["This reverses the direction the cilia beat. They move mucus up and out, away from the alveoli, so fewer particles reach them.","Surfactant comes from type II alveolar cells and keeps alveoli from collapsing. Goblet cells secrete mucus, not surfactant.","Correct. Mucus traps particles and cilia move them up toward the pharynx.","Cartilage supports the wall of the larger airways but sits beneath the lining, not in it. It holds the airway open but does not filter air."],"lt":"t-respiratory","lid":"d3-t-respiratory-7","comp":100,"competencyId":"resp-tree"},{"id":"L02180","dok":3,"q":"When the diaphragm contracts, it flattens and air flows into the lungs. Why does this contraction cause inhalation?","a":"Flattening the diaphragm enlarges the thoracic cavity, so the lungs expand and air flows in.","options":["It enlarges the thoracic cavity, so the lungs expand and air flows in","Contracting the diaphragm squeezes the lungs, forcing air inward","The diaphragm blows air into the lungs the way a bellows does","Contracting the diaphragm opens the glottis, which pulls air down"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The diaphragm is a dome-shaped skeletal muscle forming the floor of the thoracic cavity, and the base of each lung rests on it. When it contracts, the dome flattens and moves down, making the thoracic cavity taller. The lungs, held against the chest wall by the pleurae, expand with it, and air flows in to fill the larger space.","notes":["Correct. A flatter diaphragm enlarges the thoracic cavity, so the lungs expand and fill with air.","Squeezing the lungs would push air out, not in. That is closer to what happens when the diaphragm relaxes and the cavity gets smaller.","The diaphragm is not connected to the airways and does not push air. It changes the size of the thoracic cavity, and air flows in on its own.","The glottis opens and closes with the vocal folds in the larynx, controlled by the arytenoid cartilages. The diaphragm is far below in the thorax and does not move the glottis."],"lt":"t-respiratory","lid":"d3-t-respiratory-8","comp":103,"competencyId":"resp-thoracic-diaphragm"},{"id":"N-m4-lower-respiratory-1","dok":1,"q":"Which cells, sometimes called dust cells, patrol the alveolar surface and engulf debris?","a":"Alveolar macrophages.","options":["Type II alveolar cells","Goblet cells","Type I alveolar cells","Alveolar macrophages"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Alveolar macrophages are wandering immune cells on the inner surface of the alveoli. They engulf dust, microbes, and other debris that got past the mucus and cilia of the conducting airways. Their nickname, dust cells, comes from this cleanup job.","notes":["Type II alveolar cells are cuboidal cells that secrete surfactant to keep the alveoli from collapsing. Pick them for a question about surfactant.","Goblet cells secrete mucus in the lining of the conducting airways, where the mucus traps particles. They are not found on the alveolar surface.","Type I alveolar cells are thin, flat cells that form most of the alveolar wall, where gases cross. They stay in place and do not engulf debris.","Correct. Alveolar macrophages are the dust cells that engulf debris."],"comp":102,"competencyId":"resp-histo"},{"id":"N-m4-lower-respiratory-2","dok":1,"q":"Which lobe is found only in the right lung?","a":"The middle lobe.","options":["The lingula","The superior lobe","The middle lobe","The inferior lobe"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The right lung has three lobes (superior, middle, and inferior), while the left lung has only superior and inferior lobes. The middle lobe is set off by the horizontal fissure above and the oblique fissure below. The left lung has the lingula in a similar position, but the lingula is part of its superior lobe.","notes":["The lingula is the tongue-shaped projection of the left superior lobe, not a lobe of its own. It belongs to the left lung, not the right.","Both lungs have a superior lobe, so it is not unique to the right lung.","Correct. Only the right lung has a middle lobe.","Both lungs have an inferior lobe, below the oblique fissure. It is shared by both sides."],"comp":101,"competencyId":"resp-lungs-pleura"},{"id":"L03800","dok":1,"q":"In this course's grouping, which structures make up the lower respiratory tract?","a":"The larynx, trachea, bronchi, and lungs.","options":["Nose, nasal cavity, paranasal sinuses, and pharynx","Larynx, trachea, bronchi, and lungs","Nasopharynx, oropharynx, and laryngopharynx","Respiratory bronchioles, alveolar ducts, and alveoli only"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The lower respiratory tract begins at the larynx (voice box) and continues through the trachea, the branching bronchi, and the lungs themselves. It is a grouping by location, separating the airway in the neck and chest from the nose and throat above it. Notice that the lower tract includes both conducting airways and the gas-exchange structures inside the lungs.","notes":["The nose, nasal cavity, paranasal sinuses, and pharynx make up the upper respiratory tract. Pick this group if the question asked about the upper tract.","Correct. The larynx, trachea, bronchi, and lungs make up the lower respiratory tract.","The nasopharynx, oropharynx, and laryngopharynx are the three regions of the pharynx, which belongs to the upper tract. They would be the answer if the question asked for the parts of the throat.","Respiratory bronchioles, alveolar ducts, and alveoli are the respiratory zone. They sit inside the lungs, so they are part of the lower tract, but they are only a small piece of it. Choose this if the question asked for the respiratory zone."],"lt":"t-resp-airways","lid":"W3-603","comp":97,"competencyId":"resp-zones"},{"id":"L03825","dok":1,"q":"What is the carina?","a":"The internal ridge where the trachea forks into the two main bronchi.","options":["The muscle that closes the back of each tracheal ring","The indentation on the medial lung where the bronchus enters","The only complete ring of cartilage in the airway","The internal ridge where the trachea forks into the bronchi"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The carina is a ridge of cartilage inside the lower end of the trachea, right where it splits into the right and left main bronchi. It marks the point where the single windpipe becomes two airways, one for each lung. On a model, you find it by looking down into the trachea at the fork.","notes":["The muscle that closes the back of each C-shaped tracheal ring is the trachealis muscle. It would be the answer if the question asked what lies against the esophagus in the tracheal wall.","The indentation on the medial surface of a lung where the bronchus and vessels enter and leave is the hilum. Pick it if the question asked where the root of the lung attaches.","The only complete ring of cartilage in the airway is the cricoid cartilage at the bottom of the larynx. Choose it if the question asked which cartilage forms a full ring.","Correct. The carina is the internal ridge where the trachea forks into the main bronchi."],"lt":"t-resp-airways","lid":"W3-629","comp":100,"competencyId":"resp-tree"},{"id":"L03826","dok":1,"q":"Which bronchi are the primary bronchi, with one entering each lung?","a":"The main bronchi.","options":["The main bronchi","The lobar bronchi","The segmental bronchi","The terminal bronchioles"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The main bronchi, also called primary bronchi, are the first two branches of the trachea at the carina. One enters the right lung and one enters the left lung, each passing in at the hilum. The right main bronchus is wider and more vertical than the left.","notes":["Correct. The main bronchi are the primary bronchi, one entering each lung.","The lobar bronchi are the secondary bronchi, one to each lobe of a lung. They branch off the main bronchi, one step later. Pick them if the question asked which bronchi supply each lobe.","The segmental bronchi are the tertiary bronchi, one to each bronchopulmonary segment. They are two steps below the main bronchi. Choose them if the question asked about the third level of branching.","Terminal bronchioles are the last airways of the conducting zone, deep inside the lung. They would be the answer if the question asked where the conducting zone ends."],"lt":"t-resp-airways","lid":"W3-630","comp":100,"competencyId":"resp-tree"},{"id":"L03827","dok":1,"q":"Lobar bronchi are also called secondary bronchi. What does each one supply?","a":"One lobe of a lung.","options":["One lobe of a lung","One alveolar sac","One bronchopulmonary segment","One whole lung"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Each main bronchus divides into lobar bronchi, the secondary bronchi, and each lobar bronchus serves one lobe. That means the right lung, with three lobes, has three lobar bronchi, and the left lung, with two lobes, has two. The name lobar is the clue, since it matches the lobe it supplies.","notes":["Correct. Each lobar bronchus supplies one lobe of a lung.","An alveolar sac is fed by an alveolar duct deep in the respiratory zone. It would be the answer if the question asked what an alveolar duct leads into.","One bronchopulmonary segment is supplied by a segmental (tertiary) bronchus, the next level down. Choose this if the question asked what the tertiary bronchi supply.","One whole lung is supplied by a main (primary) bronchus. Pick this if the question asked about the main bronchi."],"lt":"t-resp-airways","lid":"W3-631","comp":100,"competencyId":"resp-tree"},{"id":"L03828","dok":1,"q":"Which bronchi are the tertiary bronchi, one to each bronchopulmonary segment?","a":"The segmental bronchi.","options":["The lobar bronchi","The respiratory bronchioles","The segmental bronchi","The main bronchi"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The segmental bronchi are the tertiary bronchi, the third level of branching in the bronchial tree. Each one supplies a bronchopulmonary segment, a wedge-shaped section of a lobe with its own airway and blood supply. They branch from the lobar bronchi and go on to divide into bronchioles.","notes":["The lobar bronchi are the secondary bronchi, one to each lobe. They come just before the segmental bronchi. Choose them if the question asked which bronchi supply each lobe.","Respiratory bronchioles are the first airways of the respiratory zone, with a few alveoli budding from their walls. They would be the answer if the question asked where gas exchange first becomes possible.","Correct. The segmental bronchi are the tertiary bronchi.","The main bronchi are the primary bronchi, one entering each lung. Pick them if the question asked for the first branches of the trachea."],"lt":"t-resp-airways","lid":"W3-632","comp":100,"competencyId":"resp-tree"},{"id":"L03829","dok":1,"q":"Which airways are the smallest conducting airways, with smooth muscle in their walls and no cartilage?","a":"The bronchioles.","options":["The bronchioles","The alveolar ducts","The segmental bronchi","The lobar bronchi"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Bronchioles are the small airways that branch from the segmental bronchi. As the bronchial tree gets smaller, cartilage decreases and smooth muscle increases, and the bronchioles are where the cartilage disappears. With smooth muscle and no cartilage, bronchioles can change their diameter, which is why they can constrict.","notes":["Correct. Bronchioles are the smallest conducting airways, with smooth muscle and no cartilage.","Alveolar ducts are respiratory zone passages whose walls are lined entirely with alveoli. They do not just conduct air. Choose them if the question asked for passages lined with alveoli.","The segmental bronchi still have cartilage plates in their walls. They are one level above the bronchioles. Pick them if the question asked for the tertiary bronchi.","The lobar bronchi are large secondary bronchi supported by cartilage. They would be the answer if the question asked which bronchi supply each lobe."],"lt":"t-resp-airways","lid":"W3-633","comp":100,"competencyId":"resp-tree"},{"id":"L03830","dok":1,"q":"Which airways are the last airways of the conducting zone, just before gas exchange begins?","a":"The terminal bronchioles.","options":["The segmental bronchi","The terminal bronchioles","The respiratory bronchioles","The alveolar ducts"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Terminal means end, and the terminal bronchioles are the end of the conducting zone. Air in them is still only being carried, not exchanged. The very next airways, the respiratory bronchioles, have alveoli budding from their walls and begin the respiratory zone.","notes":["The segmental bronchi are much higher in the tree, the tertiary bronchi that supply bronchopulmonary segments. Choose them if the question asked about the third level of branching.","Correct. The terminal bronchioles are the last conducting airways.","The respiratory bronchioles come right after the terminal bronchioles and are the first airways of the respiratory zone. Pick them if the question asked where gas exchange first begins.","Alveolar ducts are in the respiratory zone, with walls lined entirely by alveoli. They would be the answer if the question asked which passages lead into the alveolar sacs."],"lt":"t-resp-airways","lid":"W3-634","comp":97,"competencyId":"resp-zones"},{"id":"L03831","dok":1,"q":"Which airways are the first airways of the respiratory zone, with a few alveoli budding from their walls?","a":"The respiratory bronchioles.","options":["The terminal bronchioles","The respiratory bronchioles","The alveolar sacs","The alveolar ducts"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Respiratory bronchioles branch from the terminal bronchioles and are the first airways where gas exchange can occur. Scattered alveoli bud out from their walls, so they are partly airway and partly exchange surface. Further along, the alveolar ducts are lined completely with alveoli.","notes":["The terminal bronchioles are the last airways of the conducting zone, just before the respiratory bronchioles. They have no alveoli. Pick them if the question asked where the conducting zone ends.","Correct. Respiratory bronchioles are the first respiratory zone airways, with a few alveoli on their walls.","Alveolar sacs are clusters of alveoli sharing a common space at the very end of the airway. They would be the answer if the question asked what an alveolar duct opens into.","Alveolar ducts come after the respiratory bronchioles, and their walls are lined entirely with alveoli rather than having just a few. Choose them if the question asked for passages fully lined with alveoli."],"lt":"t-resp-airways","lid":"W3-647","comp":102,"competencyId":"resp-histo"},{"id":"L03837","dok":2,"q":"Which sequence correctly traces air from the trachea to the terminal bronchioles?","a":"Trachea, carina, main bronchi, lobar bronchi, segmental bronchi, bronchioles, terminal bronchioles.","options":["Trachea, carina, lobar bronchi, main bronchi, segmental bronchi, bronchioles, terminal bronchioles","Trachea, carina, main bronchi, lobar bronchi, segmental bronchi, bronchioles, terminal bronchioles","Trachea, carina, main bronchi, lobar bronchi, segmental bronchi, terminal bronchioles, bronchioles","Trachea, carina, main bronchi, segmental bronchi, lobar bronchi, bronchioles, terminal bronchioles"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The bronchial tree branches from largest to smallest. The trachea forks at the carina into the main (primary) bronchi, one per lung, which divide into lobar (secondary) bronchi, one per lobe, and then segmental (tertiary) bronchi, one per segment. These branch into bronchioles, and the last of the bronchioles are the terminal bronchioles.","notes":["This sequence puts the lobar bronchi before the main bronchi. The main bronchi come first because there is one per lung, and each then splits into lobar bronchi, one per lobe.","Correct. Air passes from the trachea through the carina, main, lobar, and segmental bronchi, then bronchioles and terminal bronchioles.","This sequence puts terminal bronchioles before ordinary bronchioles. Terminal means end, so the terminal bronchioles are the last conducting airways, after the other bronchioles.","This sequence swaps the lobar and segmental bronchi. Remember primary, secondary, tertiary: main, lobar, segmental. A lobe is bigger than a segment, so the lobar branch comes first."],"lt":"t-resp-airways","lid":"W3-675","comp":100,"competencyId":"resp-tree"},{"id":"L03838","dok":2,"q":"Bronchioles have smooth muscle in their walls but no cartilage. What does that structure allow them to do?","a":"Change their diameter to regulate airflow.","options":["Exchange gases directly with the capillary blood","Secrete surfactant into the airway","Change their diameter to regulate airflow","Stay rigidly open no matter what"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"As the bronchial tree branches, cartilage decreases and smooth muscle increases, and at the bronchioles the cartilage is gone. Without stiff cartilage holding them open, the smooth muscle can contract to narrow the bronchioles or relax to widen them. This lets them regulate airflow, and it is also why bronchioles can constrict too much, as in asthma.","notes":["Bronchioles are still conducting airways and are too thick-walled for gas exchange. Exchange begins at the respiratory bronchioles and alveoli. Pick gas exchange if the question asked about the alveoli.","Surfactant is secreted by Type II alveolar cells, not by bronchiole smooth muscle. It would be the answer if the question asked what keeps alveoli from collapsing.","Correct. Smooth muscle without cartilage lets bronchioles change diameter and regulate airflow.","Staying rigidly open is what cartilage does, as in the trachea and bronchi. Bronchioles lack cartilage, so this is the opposite of their design. Choose it if the question asked why the trachea does not collapse."],"lt":"t-resp-airways","lid":"W3-676","comp":100,"competencyId":"resp-tree"},{"id":"L03839","dok":2,"q":"Which sequence correctly traces air from a terminal bronchiole to the alveoli?","a":"Terminal bronchiole, respiratory bronchiole, alveolar duct, alveolar sac and alveoli.","options":["Terminal bronchiole, alveolar duct, respiratory bronchiole, alveolar sac","Terminal bronchiole, respiratory bronchiole, alveolar duct, alveolar sac","Terminal bronchiole, respiratory bronchiole, alveolar sac, alveolar duct","Respiratory bronchiole, terminal bronchiole, alveolar duct, alveolar sac"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The terminal bronchiole is the last conducting airway. It leads into a respiratory bronchiole, the first respiratory zone airway with a few alveoli on its walls. That opens into an alveolar duct, lined entirely with alveoli, which ends in an alveolar sac, a cluster of alveoli sharing a common space.","notes":["This sequence puts the alveolar duct before the respiratory bronchiole. The respiratory bronchiole has only a few alveoli, so it comes first, and the duct, lined completely with alveoli, comes after it.","Correct. Air goes from the terminal bronchiole to the respiratory bronchiole, alveolar duct, and alveolar sac.","This sequence puts the alveolar sac before the alveolar duct. The duct is a passage that leads into the sac, so the sac is the dead end at the finish of the path.","This sequence starts with the respiratory bronchiole. The terminal bronchiole is the end of the conducting zone, so air reaches it before the respiratory bronchiole, not after."],"lt":"t-resp-airways","lid":"W3-681","comp":97,"competencyId":"resp-zones"},{"id":"L03840","dok":3,"q":"A child inhales a small bead, and an X-ray shows it lodged in the right lung. Which feature of the airway best explains why it went right?","a":"The right main bronchus is wider and more vertical","options":["The left main bronchus has no cartilage","The right lung has three lobes and the left has two","The left lung has a cardiac notch for the heart","The right main bronchus is wider and more vertical"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"At the carina the trachea splits into the two main bronchi, and they are not mirror images. The right main bronchus is wider, shorter, and more vertical, nearly in line with the trachea, while the left is narrower and angles off more sharply. An inhaled object falling down the trachea follows the straighter, wider path, so it most often lands in the right lung.","notes":["Both main bronchi are supported by cartilage, which is lost only at the level of the bronchioles. This choice describes a bronchiole, so pick a cartilage-free wall if the question asked which airways can constrict in asthma.","The right lung does have three lobes, but the number of lobes does not steer an object entering the airway. The object is directed at the carina, before any lobes are reached. Pick this if the question compared the lobes of the two lungs.","The left lung does have a cardiac notch for the heart, but the notch is on the lung, past the point where the object chose a side at the carina. The shape of the right main bronchus is the explanation. Choose the cardiac notch if the question asked why the left lung is smaller.","Correct. The wider, more vertical right main bronchus directs inhaled objects into the right lung."],"lt":"t-resp-airways","lid":"W3-691","comp":100,"competencyId":"resp-tree"},{"id":"L03842","dok":3,"q":"During an asthma attack the bronchioles constrict. Which statement best describes where the problem is and how it affects the alveoli?","a":"The narrowing is in the conducting zone, so less air reaches the alveoli.","options":["The narrowing is in the pleural cavity, so the lung collapses","The narrowing is in the conducting zone, so less air reaches the alveoli","The narrowing is in the larynx, so air stops before the trachea","The narrowing is in the respiratory zone, so the alveolar walls are destroyed"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Bronchioles are conducting airways, with smooth muscle and no cartilage, so they can constrict. When they narrow, air has a harder time moving through them, and less of it reaches the alveoli in the respiratory zone downstream. The alveoli themselves are not damaged, but they receive less fresh air while the bronchioles stay narrow.","notes":["A problem in the pleural cavity that collapses the lung is a pneumothorax, caused by air entering that space. Choose this if the question described a chest puncture.","Correct. Constricted bronchioles are a conducting zone problem that reduces airflow to the alveoli.","The larynx is above the trachea and is not where asthma acts. Asthma is a disease of the small airways. This choice would fit only an obstruction high in the airway, such as a blocked laryngeal inlet.","Destruction of alveolar walls in the respiratory zone is emphysema, a permanent structural change. Pick this if the question described a permanent loss of exchange surface."],"lt":"t-resp-airways","lid":"W3-693","comp":104,"competencyId":"resp-disorders"},{"id":"L03843","dok":1,"q":"How many lobes does the left lung have?","a":"Two, the superior and inferior lobes.","options":["One lobe","Three lobes","Two lobes","Four lobes"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The left lung has two lobes, a superior lobe and an inferior lobe, separated by a single oblique fissure. It gives up space to the heart, which sits slightly to the left, and it is the smaller of the two lungs. The lingula is a tongue-shaped part of the left superior lobe, not a separate lobe.","notes":["One lobe would mean the left lung had no fissure at all, but it has an oblique fissure dividing it in two. This is a guess from thinking the smaller lung must be undivided.","Three lobes is the right lung, with superior, middle, and inferior lobes. Pick three if the question asked about the right lung.","Correct. The left lung has two lobes, superior and inferior.","Neither lung has four lobes. Students sometimes count the lingula as an extra lobe, but it is part of the left superior lobe."],"lt":"t-resp-lungs","lid":"W3-636","comp":101,"competencyId":"resp-lungs-pleura"},{"id":"L03844","dok":1,"q":"Which fissures divide the right lung into its lobes?","a":"A horizontal fissure and an oblique fissure.","options":["A horizontal fissure only","Two oblique fissures, upper and lower","An oblique fissure only","Horizontal and oblique fissures"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The right lung has three lobes, so it needs two fissures to separate them. The horizontal fissure separates the superior lobe from the middle lobe, and the oblique fissure separates the middle lobe (and part of the superior lobe) from the inferior lobe. The left lung, with only two lobes, has just the oblique fissure.","notes":["A horizontal fissure only would create just two lobes. The right lung needs a second fissure, the oblique fissure, to form its inferior lobe. This choice forgets the fissure the two lungs share.","The right lung has one oblique fissure, not two. Its second fissure runs horizontally, which is how the middle lobe is set apart. This is a made-up combination.","An oblique fissure only is the left lung, which has two lobes. Pick this if the question asked about the left lung.","Correct. The right lung has a horizontal fissure and an oblique fissure."],"lt":"t-resp-lungs","lid":"W3-637","comp":101,"competencyId":"resp-lungs-pleura"},{"id":"L03845","dok":1,"q":"Which fissure does the left lung have?","a":"An oblique fissure only.","options":["A cardiac fissure only","A horizontal fissure only","A horizontal fissure and an oblique fissure","An oblique fissure only"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The left lung has two lobes, superior and inferior, so it needs only one fissure between them, the oblique fissure. It has no horizontal fissure because it has no middle lobe. Where the right lung would have a middle lobe, the left lung instead has the cardiac notch and the lingula.","notes":["There is no cardiac fissure. The left lung has a cardiac notch, an indentation for the heart, which is not a fissure between lobes. This choice confuses the notch with a fissure.","A horizontal fissure is found only in the right lung, where it separates the superior and middle lobes. Pick it if the question asked which fissure is unique to the right lung.","A horizontal fissure and an oblique fissure together belong to the right lung, which has three lobes. Choose this pair if the question asked about the right lung.","Correct. The left lung has only an oblique fissure."],"lt":"t-resp-lungs","lid":"W3-638","comp":101,"competencyId":"resp-lungs-pleura"},{"id":"L03846","dok":1,"q":"Which lung is larger?","a":"The right lung.","options":["They are exactly equal","The right lung","The left lung","It varies with body position"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The right lung is the larger of the two. It has three lobes, while the left lung has two lobes and gives up room for the heart, which sits slightly to the left in the mediastinum. That missing space shows up as the cardiac notch on the left lung.","notes":["The two lungs are not identical. The left lung gives up room for the heart, so the lungs differ in size and in number of lobes. This choice assumes the body is perfectly symmetrical.","Correct. The right lung is larger.","The left lung is the smaller one, with two lobes and a cardiac notch where the heart takes up space. Pick it if the question asked which lung has a cardiac notch.","Lung size is a fixed anatomical difference, not something that changes with posture. This choice is a guess that avoids committing to either side."],"lt":"t-resp-lungs","lid":"W3-639","comp":101,"competencyId":"resp-lungs-pleura"},{"id":"L03847","dok":1,"q":"What is the cardiac notch?","a":"An indentation in the left lung that makes room for the heart.","options":["An indentation in the left lung for the heart","The tongue-shaped part of the left lung","The ridge where the trachea forks in two","The indentation on the medial lung where vessels enter"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The cardiac notch is an indentation along the anterior border of the left lung that makes room for the heart. Because the heart sits slightly to the left in the mediastinum, the left lung curves around it. This is one reason the left lung is smaller than the right.","notes":["Correct. The cardiac notch is the indentation in the left lung that accommodates the heart.","The tongue-shaped projection of the left superior lobe is the lingula, which sits just below the cardiac notch. It would be the answer if the question asked for the part shaped like a tongue.","The ridge where the trachea forks into the main bronchi is the carina. Choose it if the question asked about the branching point of the trachea.","The indentation on the medial surface where the bronchus and vessels enter and leave is the hilum. Pick it if the question asked where the root of the lung attaches."],"lt":"t-resp-lungs","lid":"W3-640","comp":101,"competencyId":"resp-lungs-pleura"},{"id":"L03848","dok":1,"q":"Which part of a lung is the narrow superior tip that rises just above the clavicle?","a":"The apex.","options":["The lingula","The apex","The base","The hilum"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The apex of a lung is its narrow, rounded top. It rises slightly above the clavicle into the root of the neck. The opposite end, the broad base, sits on the diaphragm.","notes":["The lingula is the tongue-shaped projection of the left superior lobe, below the cardiac notch. It would be the answer if the question asked about the tongue-shaped part of the left lung.","Correct. The apex is the narrow superior tip that rises above the clavicle.","The base is the broad inferior surface of the lung that rests on the diaphragm, the opposite end from the apex. Pick it if the question asked which surface sits on the diaphragm.","The hilum is the indentation on the medial surface where the bronchus and vessels enter and leave. Choose it if the question asked where the root of the lung attaches."],"lt":"t-resp-lungs","lid":"W3-641","comp":101,"competencyId":"resp-lungs-pleura"},{"id":"L03849","dok":1,"q":"What does the base of a lung rest on?","a":"The diaphragm.","options":["The heart","The clavicle","The sternum","The diaphragm"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The base of a lung is its broad inferior surface, and it rests on the dome of the diaphragm. The diaphragm is the muscle that separates the thoracic cavity from the abdominal cavity. The opposite end of the lung, the apex, rises just above the clavicle.","notes":["The heart sits medial to the lungs in the mediastinum and fits into the cardiac notch of the left lung. Choose it if the question asked what the cardiac notch accommodates.","The clavicle is the landmark for the apex, the narrow top of the lung that rises just above it. Pick the clavicle if the question asked about the apex.","The sternum is the breastbone at the front of the thoracic cage, anterior to the heart and the medial edges of the lungs. The lungs do not rest on it. It would be the answer if the question asked which bone the costal cartilages join in front.","Correct. The base of a lung rests on the diaphragm."],"lt":"t-resp-lungs","lid":"W3-642","comp":101,"competencyId":"resp-lungs-pleura"},{"id":"L03850","dok":1,"q":"Which serous membrane lines the inside of the thoracic wall?","a":"The parietal pleura.","options":["The visceral pleura","The parietal pericardium","The parietal peritoneum","The parietal pleura"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The pleura is the serous membrane of the lungs, and it has two layers. The parietal pleura lines the inner surface of the thoracic wall (parietal means wall), and it also covers the top of the diaphragm and the sides of the mediastinum. The visceral pleura covers the lung itself.","notes":["The visceral pleura is the layer stuck directly to the outer surface of the lung. Pick it if the question asked which membrane covers the lung.","The parietal pericardium is part of the sac around the heart, in the mediastinum. Choose it if the question asked about the membrane lining the pericardial cavity.","The parietal peritoneum lines the wall of the abdominopelvic cavity, below the diaphragm. It would be the answer if the question asked which membrane lines the abdominal wall.","Correct. The parietal pleura lines the thoracic wall."],"lt":"t-resp-lungs","lid":"W3-644","comp":101,"competencyId":"resp-lungs-pleura"},{"id":"L03851","dok":1,"q":"Which serous membrane covers the outer surface of each lung?","a":"The visceral pleura.","options":["The parietal pleura","The visceral pericardium","The visceral pleura","The mediastinum"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The visceral pleura is the inner layer of the pleura, attached directly to the outer surface of the lung and dipping into its fissures. Visceral refers to an organ, so the visceral layer is the one on the organ itself. It faces the parietal pleura across the thin, fluid-filled pleural cavity.","notes":["The parietal pleura is the outer layer, lining the thoracic wall rather than the lung. Pick it if the question asked which membrane lines the inside of the chest wall.","The visceral pericardium, also called the epicardium, covers the surface of the heart. Choose it if the question asked which serous layer is on the heart.","Correct. The visceral pleura covers the outer surface of the lung.","The mediastinum is the central compartment of the thoracic cavity between the two lungs, holding the heart, great vessels, trachea, and esophagus. It is a region, not a membrane. It would be the answer if the question asked where the heart sits."],"lt":"t-resp-lungs","lid":"W3-645","comp":101,"competencyId":"resp-lungs-pleura"},{"id":"L03852","dok":1,"q":"What is the pleural cavity?","a":"The thin, fluid-filled space between the parietal and visceral pleurae.","options":["The central chest region that holds the heart","The space inside the lungs that holds all of the alveoli","The fluid-filled space between the two pleural layers","The space between the heart and the pericardial sac"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The pleural cavity is the thin space between the parietal pleura on the thoracic wall and the visceral pleura on the lung. It holds only a thin film of serous fluid. That fluid lets the lung glide smoothly against the chest wall as it expands, and the sealed space helps keep the lung expanded.","notes":["The central chest region holding the heart is the mediastinum. Pick it if the question asked about the compartment between the two lungs.","The air spaces inside the lungs are the alveoli and the airways that lead to them. The pleural cavity is outside the lung, not inside it. Students mix these up because both are described as spaces in the chest.","Correct. The pleural cavity is the fluid-filled space between the parietal and visceral pleurae.","The space between the heart and its sac is the pericardial cavity. It is the heart's version of the same serous arrangement. Choose it if the question were about the heart."],"lt":"t-resp-lungs","lid":"W3-646","comp":101,"competencyId":"resp-lungs-pleura"},{"id":"L03853","dok":1,"q":"Which respiratory zone passages have walls lined entirely with alveoli?","a":"The alveolar ducts.","options":["The terminal bronchioles","The alveolar ducts","The segmental bronchi","The respiratory bronchioles"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Alveolar ducts are passages in the respiratory zone whose walls are made almost completely of alveoli opening off them. They branch from the respiratory bronchioles and lead into alveolar sacs. Compared with a respiratory bronchiole, which has only a few alveoli, an alveolar duct is lined with them all the way around.","notes":["Terminal bronchioles have no alveoli at all. They are the last airways of the conducting zone. Choose them if the question asked where the conducting zone ends.","Correct. Alveolar ducts have walls lined entirely with alveoli.","Segmental bronchi are large conducting airways with cartilage in their walls, far from the alveoli. They would be the answer if the question asked for the tertiary bronchi.","Respiratory bronchioles have only a few alveoli budding from their walls, not walls lined entirely with them. Pick them if the question asked for the first airways of the respiratory zone."],"lt":"t-resp-lungs","lid":"W3-648","comp":102,"competencyId":"resp-histo"},{"id":"L03854","dok":1,"q":"Which structures are the tiny air sacs that serve as the site of gas exchange?","a":"The alveoli.","options":["The pleural cavities","The paranasal sinuses","The bronchioles","The alveoli"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Alveoli are tiny, thin-walled air sacs at the end of the respiratory tree, and the lungs hold hundreds of millions of them. Each one is wrapped in pulmonary capillaries, so air and blood are separated by only a very thin barrier. That makes the alveoli the main site of gas exchange.","notes":["The pleural cavities are thin, fluid-filled spaces between the pleural layers around each lung. They hold fluid, not air. They would be the answer if the question asked where air collects in a pneumothorax.","The paranasal sinuses are air-filled cavities in the skull bones around the nose. They are lined with mucosa that drains into the nasal cavity and play no part in gas exchange. Choose them if the question asked about spaces in the frontal or maxillary bones.","Bronchioles are small conducting airways with smooth muscle walls. They carry air but do not exchange gases. Pick them if the question asked which airways lack cartilage.","Correct. The alveoli are the air sacs where gas exchange happens."],"lt":"t-resp-lungs","lid":"W3-649","comp":102,"competencyId":"resp-histo"},{"id":"L03855","dok":1,"q":"What is an alveolar sac?","a":"A cluster of alveoli that share a common space.","options":["A fluid-filled space surrounding the lung","A passage whose walls are lined entirely with alveoli","A cluster of alveoli that share a common space","A single alveolus wrapped in capillaries"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"An alveolar sac is a grape-like cluster of alveoli that open into a shared central space. It sits at the very end of an alveolar duct, so it is the dead end of the airway. Grouping alveoli this way packs a huge amount of exchange surface into a small volume.","notes":["A fluid-filled space surrounding the lung is the pleural cavity. It would be the answer if the question asked about the space between the parietal and visceral pleurae.","A passage lined with alveoli is an alveolar duct, which leads into the alveolar sacs. Choose it if the question asked for the passage before the sac.","Correct. An alveolar sac is a cluster of alveoli sharing a common space.","A single alveolus wrapped in capillaries is one alveolus, the individual unit within the sac. Pick this if the question asked about one alveolus and its pulmonary capillaries."],"lt":"t-resp-lungs","lid":"W3-650","comp":102,"competencyId":"resp-histo"},{"id":"L03856","dok":1,"q":"Which cells are the thin, flat simple squamous cells that form most of the alveolar wall?","a":"Type I alveolar cells.","options":["Goblet cells","Type II alveolar cells","Type I alveolar cells","Alveolar macrophages"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Type I alveolar cells are simple squamous cells, meaning a single layer of very thin, flat cells. They form most of the alveolar wall, and gases cross through them. Their thinness keeps the distance between air and blood as short as possible.","notes":["Goblet cells are mucus-secreting cells in the pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium of the larger airways. They are not found in the alveolar wall. They would be the answer if the question asked what makes mucus in the trachea.","Type II alveolar cells are cuboidal, not flat, and are scattered among the Type I cells. Their job is to secrete surfactant. Pick them if the question asked which cells make surfactant.","Correct. Type I alveolar cells are the thin, flat cells that form most of the alveolar wall.","Alveolar macrophages are the dust cells that wander over the alveolar surface and engulf debris. They are not part of the wall itself. Choose them if the question asked which cells clear inhaled particles."],"lt":"t-resp-lungs","lid":"W3-651","comp":102,"competencyId":"resp-histo"},{"id":"L03857","dok":1,"q":"Which cells in the alveolar wall secrete surfactant?","a":"Type II alveolar cells.","options":["Type II alveolar cells","Type I alveolar cells","Capillary endothelial cells","Alveolar macrophages"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Type II alveolar cells are rounder, cuboidal cells scattered among the flat type I cells of the alveolar wall. Their job is to secrete surfactant, a fluid that lowers surface tension and keeps the alveoli from collapsing. They cover only a small part of the wall, while the type I cells cover nearly all of it.","notes":["Correct. Type II alveolar cells secrete surfactant.","Type I alveolar cells are the thin, flat cells that form most of the wall where gases cross. They do not secrete surfactant. Pick them if the question asked which cells form the gas-exchange surface.","Capillary endothelial cells line the pulmonary capillaries on the blood side of the respiratory membrane. They would be the answer if the question asked which cells form the inner lining of the capillaries around an alveolus.","Alveolar macrophages are the dust cells that patrol the alveolar surface and engulf debris. They clean rather than secrete surfactant. Choose them if the question asked which cells remove inhaled particles."],"lt":"t-resp-lungs","lid":"W3-652","comp":102,"competencyId":"resp-histo"},{"id":"L03858","dok":1,"q":"What does surfactant do in the alveoli?","a":"It lowers surface tension and helps keep the alveoli from collapsing.","options":["It lets the lungs glide against the thoracic wall","It fills the alveoli to fight infection by microbes","It traps dust so cilia can sweep it up toward the pharynx","It lowers surface tension so alveoli do not collapse"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The inside of each alveolus is coated with a thin layer of water, and water molecules pull on each other, creating surface tension that tends to squeeze the tiny sacs shut. Surfactant, secreted by Type II alveolar cells, lowers that surface tension. That keeps the alveoli from collapsing, especially at the end of each breath out.","notes":["Letting the lungs glide against the thoracic wall is the job of the fluid in the pleural cavity, outside the lung. It would be the answer if the question asked about pleural fluid.","Alveoli filling with fluid is what happens in pneumonia, an infection, and it interferes with gas exchange. Choose this if the question described pneumonia.","Trapping dust and sweeping it toward the pharynx is the job of mucus and cilia in the conducting airways. Pick this if the question asked about the respiratory mucosa.","Correct. Surfactant lowers surface tension and helps keep alveoli from collapsing."],"lt":"t-resp-lungs","lid":"W3-653","comp":102,"competencyId":"resp-histo"},{"id":"L03859","dok":1,"q":"Which cells, nicknamed dust cells, patrol the alveolar surface and engulf debris and microbes?","a":"Alveolar macrophages.","options":["Goblet cells","Type II alveolar cells","Alveolar macrophages","Type I alveolar cells"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Alveolar macrophages are immune cells that move across the alveolar surface and engulf (swallow up) inhaled dust, debris, and microbes. They are called dust cells because the particles they collect can be seen inside them. They are the last line of cleaning, since the mucus and cilia of the conducting airways do not reach the alveoli.","notes":["Goblet cells secrete mucus in the larger conducting airways, where mucus traps particles before they reach the alveoli. Choose them if the question asked which cells produce mucus in the trachea.","Type II alveolar cells are cuboidal cells that secrete surfactant. They do not engulf debris. They would be the answer if the question asked which cells keep alveoli from collapsing.","Correct. Alveolar macrophages are the dust cells.","Type I alveolar cells are thin, flat cells that form the gas-exchange surface. They stay in place as part of the wall. Pick them if the question asked which cells form most of the alveolar wall."],"lt":"t-resp-lungs","lid":"W3-654","comp":102,"competencyId":"resp-histo"},{"id":"L03860","dok":1,"q":"What wraps around each alveolus?","a":"A dense net of pulmonary capillaries.","options":["A band of smooth muscle","A supporting ring of hyaline cartilage","A dense net of pulmonary capillaries","A layer of visceral pericardium"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Each alveolus is wrapped in a dense web of pulmonary capillaries, the smallest blood vessels of the pulmonary circuit. The capillary wall and the alveolar wall are pressed together, so air and blood are separated by only the thin respiratory membrane. That close contact is what makes gas exchange possible.","notes":["Smooth muscle bands are found in the walls of bronchioles, where they can narrow the airway. Choose smooth muscle if the question asked what lets bronchioles constrict.","Hyaline cartilage supports the trachea and bronchi but is gone by the bronchioles, long before the alveoli. Pick cartilage if the question asked what holds the trachea open.","Correct. A dense net of pulmonary capillaries wraps each alveolus.","The visceral pericardium covers the heart, not the alveoli. The lung surface is covered by visceral pleura, and the alveoli inside are surrounded by capillaries. This choice mixes up the serous membrane of the heart with the structures inside the lung."],"lt":"t-resp-lungs","lid":"W3-656","comp":102,"competencyId":"resp-histo"},{"id":"L03861","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares the right and left lungs by their lobes?","a":"The right lung has three lobes and the left lung has two.","options":["The right lung has three lobes and the left lung has two","Both lungs have three lobes, but the left ones are smaller","Both lungs have two lobes separated by an oblique fissure","The right lung has two lobes and the left lung has three"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The right lung has three lobes (superior, middle, and inferior), separated by a horizontal fissure and an oblique fissure. The left lung has two lobes (superior and inferior), separated by an oblique fissure only. The left lung gives up room to the heart, which is why it is the smaller of the two.","notes":["Correct. The right lung has three lobes and the left lung has two.","The left lung does not have a middle lobe. It has two lobes, and where a middle lobe would be, it has the cardiac notch and the lingula. This choice assumes the lungs are mirror images.","Only the left lung is limited to two lobes and a single oblique fissure. The right lung adds a horizontal fissure and a middle lobe. Pick this description if the question asked only about the left lung.","This reverses the two lungs. The extra lobe belongs to the right lung, the larger one, and the left lung has fewer because it shares space with the heart."],"lt":"t-resp-lungs","lid":"W3-677","comp":101,"competencyId":"resp-lungs-pleura"},{"id":"L03862","dok":2,"q":"Why is the left lung smaller than the right lung?","a":"It gives up room for the heart, shown by its cardiac notch.","options":["It sits higher because the liver pushes up on the left side","Its main bronchus is narrower, so less lung tissue develops","It is compressed by the esophagus running down its surface","It gives up room for the heart, shown by its cardiac notch"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The heart sits in the mediastinum but tips toward the left side of the chest. The left lung makes room for it, which shows up as the cardiac notch, an indentation along its anterior border. With less room, the left lung ends up the smaller of the two.","notes":["The liver sits mostly on the right side, under the right dome of the diaphragm, not the left. This choice puts the liver on the wrong side, so it cannot explain a smaller left lung.","The left main bronchus is narrower and angles more sharply than the right, but that does not set the size of the lung. The left lung is smaller because of the space it shares with the heart. This choice mixes up a bronchus fact with a lung fact.","The esophagus runs down the mediastinum behind the trachea and heart, not along the surface of the left lung. Choose the esophagus if the question asked what lies directly behind the trachea.","Correct. The left lung gives up room for the heart, which forms the cardiac notch."],"lt":"t-resp-lungs","lid":"W3-678","comp":101,"competencyId":"resp-lungs-pleura"},{"id":"L03863","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares the parietal pleura and the visceral pleura?","a":"The parietal pleura lines the thoracic wall, and the visceral pleura covers the lung surface.","options":["Both layers cover the lung, with the parietal layer outside the visceral layer","The parietal pleura covers the lung, and the visceral pleura lines the thoracic wall","The parietal pleura covers the heart, and the visceral pleura covers the lung","The parietal pleura lines the thoracic wall, and the visceral pleura covers the lung"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The pleura is a double-layered serous membrane. The parietal layer (parietal means wall) lines the inside of the thoracic wall, and the visceral layer (visceral means organ) is attached directly to the surface of the lung. Between them is the pleural cavity, a thin space with a film of fluid.","notes":["Only the visceral pleura is attached to the lung. The parietal pleura is on the chest wall, with the pleural cavity between them. This choice forgets that the two layers are separated by a space.","This reverses the two layers. Remember that parietal refers to a wall and visceral refers to an organ, so the visceral layer is the one on the lung.","The heart is covered by the pericardium, not the pleura. Pick a heart covering if the question asked about the visceral or parietal pericardium.","Correct. The parietal pleura lines the thoracic wall, and the visceral pleura covers the lung."],"lt":"t-resp-lungs","lid":"W3-679","comp":101,"competencyId":"resp-lungs-pleura"},{"id":"L03864","dok":2,"q":"Why does the pleural cavity hold a thin film of fluid?","a":"The fluid lets the lung glide against the thoracic wall as it expands.","options":["It supplies the alveoli with surfactant","It lets the lung glide as it expands","It drains mucus out of the bronchi","It carries oxygen to the lung tissue"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The pleural cavity is a sealed, thin space between the parietal and visceral pleurae, and it holds only a small amount of serous fluid. The fluid acts as a lubricant, letting the lung surface glide smoothly against the thoracic wall as the lung expands and recoils with each breath. The sealed space also helps keep the lung expanded, which is why letting air into it causes collapse.","notes":["Surfactant is made by Type II alveolar cells inside the alveoli, not by the pleural fluid outside the lung. Pick surfactant if the question asked what prevents alveolar collapse.","Correct. Pleural fluid lets the lung glide against the thoracic wall as it expands.","Mucus in the bronchi is moved upward by cilia toward the pharynx, inside the airway. Pleural fluid is outside the lung and never touches the bronchi. Choose cilia if the question asked how mucus leaves the airways.","Oxygen reaches the lung tissue through the air in the airways and the blood in the vessels, not through pleural fluid. This choice gives the fluid a job it does not have."],"lt":"t-resp-lungs","lid":"W3-680","comp":101,"competencyId":"resp-lungs-pleura"},{"id":"L03865","dok":2,"q":"Why are Type I alveolar cells so thin and flat?","a":"Their thinness makes a very short distance for gases to cross between air and blood.","options":["To let them move freely across the alveolar surface","To make room for surfactant in the alveolar wall","To give gases a very short distance to cross","To make the alveolar wall strong and stiff"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Type I alveolar cells are simple squamous cells, the thinnest kind of epithelium. Gases cross the alveolar wall by diffusion (moving from where they are more concentrated to where they are less), and diffusion is fastest across a short distance. Keeping these cells flat means air and blood are separated by only a very thin barrier, the respiratory membrane.","notes":["Moving freely over the alveolar surface describes alveolar macrophages, the dust cells. Type I cells stay in place as part of the wall. Choose this if the question asked about macrophages.","Surfactant is secreted by the separate Type II alveolar cells, which are cuboidal. The flatness of Type I cells is not about surfactant. Pick surfactant if the question asked about Type II cells.","Correct. Thin, flat Type I cells give gases a very short distance to cross.","Thin, flat cells make a delicate wall, not a stiff one. Strength comes from structures like cartilage in the larger airways. This choice confuses thinness with toughness, which is the opposite of how alveoli are built."],"lt":"t-resp-lungs","lid":"W3-682","comp":102,"competencyId":"resp-histo"},{"id":"L03866","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly describes Type II alveolar cells?","a":"They are cuboidal cells that secrete surfactant, which helps prevent alveolar collapse.","options":["They are flat cells that form most of the wall where the gases cross","They are cuboidal cells that secrete surfactant to prevent collapse","They are wandering cells that engulf inhaled debris and microbes","They are ciliated cells that sweep mucus out of the alveoli"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Type II alveolar cells are cuboidal cells scattered among the flat Type I cells. They secrete surfactant, which lowers surface tension in the thin layer of water lining each alveolus. Without enough surfactant, alveoli tend to collapse, which is why these cells are essential even though they make up a small part of the wall.","notes":["Flat cells that form most of the gas-exchange wall are the Type I alveolar cells. Pick this if the question asked about Type I cells.","Correct. Type II alveolar cells are cuboidal and secrete surfactant.","Wandering cells that engulf debris are the alveolar macrophages, or dust cells. Choose this if the question asked which cells clean the alveolar surface.","Ciliated cells are found in the pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium of the conducting airways, not in the alveoli. They would be the answer if the question asked which cells sweep mucus up the trachea."],"lt":"t-resp-lungs","lid":"W3-683","comp":102,"competencyId":"resp-histo"},{"id":"L03867","dok":2,"q":"The mucus and cilia of the conducting airways do not reach the alveoli. What clears debris and microbes that make it all the way to the alveolar surface?","a":"Alveolar macrophages engulf them.","options":["Goblet cells trap them in mucus","Surfactant dissolves them away","Type I cells absorb them into the blood","Alveolar macrophages engulf them"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The conducting airways clean air with mucus from goblet cells and cilia that sweep it toward the pharynx, but the alveoli have no cilia or goblet cells. Instead, alveolar macrophages, the dust cells, patrol the alveolar surface and engulf any debris and microbes that arrive. Their location on the exchange surface puts them exactly where the airway's other defenses end.","notes":["Goblet cells make mucus in the conducting airways, such as the trachea and bronchi. They are not present in the alveoli. Pick goblet cells if the question asked what traps particles in the larger airways.","Surfactant lowers surface tension so alveoli do not collapse. It does not dissolve debris. Choose surfactant if the question asked what keeps alveoli open.","Type I alveolar cells form the thin exchange wall and do not absorb debris. This choice confuses the path of gases across the wall with the clearing of particles.","Correct. Alveolar macrophages engulf debris and microbes on the alveolar surface."],"lt":"t-resp-lungs","lid":"W3-684","comp":102,"competencyId":"resp-histo"},{"id":"L03868","dok":2,"q":"Why are pulmonary capillaries wrapped so tightly around each alveolus?","a":"To bring blood very close to the air for gas exchange.","options":["To carry lymph away from the lung tissue","To hold the alveolus open like a stiff supporting ring","To deliver surfactant into the alveolar space","To bring blood very close to the air for exchange"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Gas exchange needs air and blood to be as close together as possible. Wrapping each alveolus in a dense net of capillaries means blood flows right against the alveolar wall, separated from the air by only the thin respiratory membrane (alveolar epithelium, fused basement membranes, and capillary endothelium). This arrangement packs a huge exchange surface into the lungs.","notes":["Carrying lymph is the job of lymphatic vessels, not blood capillaries. Choose lymphatic vessels if the question asked how excess tissue fluid leaves the lung.","Capillaries are thin, soft vessels and do not hold anything open. Support rings of cartilage are found only in the larger airways. This choice confuses a blood vessel with a supporting structure.","Surfactant is secreted by Type II alveolar cells in the alveolar wall, not delivered by the capillaries. Pick Type II cells if the question asked where surfactant comes from.","Correct. Capillaries wrap the alveoli to bring blood close to the air for gas exchange."],"lt":"t-resp-lungs","lid":"W3-685","comp":102,"competencyId":"resp-histo"},{"id":"L03869","dok":3,"q":"A premature newborn's Type II alveolar cells are not yet making enough surfactant. What is most likely to happen to the baby's alveoli?","a":"They are more likely to collapse because surface tension stays high.","options":["Their walls are permanently destroyed","They leak air out into the pleural cavity","They fill with fluid from an infection","They collapse because surface tension stays high"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Surfactant lowers the surface tension of the thin layer of water lining each alveolus. Without enough of it, that surface tension stays high and tends to pull the tiny sacs closed, especially at the end of each breath out. So a newborn without enough surfactant has alveoli that collapse easily, which makes breathing hard work.","notes":["Permanent destruction of alveolar walls is emphysema, a long-term disease, usually linked to smoking. Choose this if the question described a permanent loss of exchange surface.","Air in the pleural cavity is a pneumothorax, caused by a puncture of the chest wall or lung. It would be the answer if the question described a chest injury.","Alveoli filling with fluid from an infection describes pneumonia. A lack of surfactant is not an infection. Pick this if the question described an infection that inflames the alveoli.","Correct. Without enough surfactant, high surface tension makes alveoli collapse."],"lt":"t-resp-lungs","lid":"W3-694","comp":104,"competencyId":"resp-disorders"},{"id":"L03870","dok":3,"q":"Inhaling a toxic gas damages a patient's Type I alveolar cells. Which process is most directly impaired?","a":"Gas diffusion across the alveolar wall.","options":["Mucus clearance by cilia in the trachea","Removal of debris from the alveolar surface","Surfactant secretion by the alveolar wall","Gas diffusion across the alveolar wall"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Type I alveolar cells are the thin, flat cells that form most of the alveolar wall, and they are the cells that gases cross. If they are damaged, the barrier between air and blood is disrupted, and gas diffusion (movement of gases from high to low concentration across the wall) is impaired. The other jobs listed belong to other cells.","notes":["Mucus clearance by cilia happens in the pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium of the conducting airways, far from the alveoli. Choose this if the question described damage to the tracheal lining.","Removing debris from the alveolar surface is the job of the alveolar macrophages, or dust cells. It would be the answer if the question said the macrophages were damaged.","Surfactant secretion is the job of Type II alveolar cells. Damage to Type II cells would impair it most directly. Pick this if the question said Type II cells were damaged.","Correct. Type I cells form the gas-exchange surface, so damage to them impairs gas diffusion."],"lt":"t-resp-lungs","lid":"W3-695","comp":102,"competencyId":"resp-histo"},{"id":"L03871","dok":3,"q":"Which statement best explains why the alveoli are ideal for gas exchange but also easy to damage?","a":"Their thin, capillary-wrapped walls make exchange efficient, but damage or fluid quickly reduces it.","options":["Their walls are lined with thick cartilage, which protects them but slows the exchange of gases","Their thin, capillary-wrapped walls make exchange fast, but damage or fluid quickly cuts it","They have few capillaries, so exchange is slow but the walls rarely suffer any injury","They are covered in cilia, which clean them well but break easily when air is cold"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Alveoli have extremely thin walls made of flat Type I cells, and each one is wrapped in pulmonary capillaries, so the distance between air and blood is tiny and the total surface is enormous. That design makes gas exchange very efficient. But a wall that thin is fragile, so anything that destroys it (as in emphysema) or fills the space with fluid (as in pneumonia) quickly reduces exchange.","notes":["Alveoli have no cartilage. Cartilage supports the trachea and bronchi and disappears by the bronchioles. This choice describes the larger airways, not the exchange surface.","Correct. The thin, capillary-wrapped wall is efficient for exchange but easily damaged or flooded.","Alveoli are wrapped in a dense net of capillaries, not a few. This statement reverses the actual design. Students might pick it by thinking delicate tissue must be poorly supplied.","Cilia line the conducting airways, not the alveoli. The alveoli are cleaned by macrophages instead. Pick cilia if the question asked how mucus is moved out of the trachea."],"lt":"t-resp-lungs","lid":"W3-700","comp":104,"competencyId":"resp-disorders"},{"id":"L03872","dok":1,"q":"Structurally, what do most common respiratory disorders do?","a":"They narrow the airways, damage the alveoli, or affect the pleurae.","options":["They cause the trachea to grow extra rings","They permanently enlarge the paranasal sinuses of the skull","They narrow the airways, damage the alveoli, or affect the pleurae","They destroy the cartilage of the larynx"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Most respiratory disorders do one of three things to a structure. Some narrow or block the airways, as in asthma and chronic bronchitis. Some damage or fill the alveoli, as in emphysema and pneumonia, and some affect the pleurae and pleural cavity, as in pleurisy and pneumothorax.","notes":["The trachea does not grow extra rings in any common disorder. This change sounds structural, but it is not something any disorder in this unit does.","Sinusitis inflames the lining of the sinuses, but no common disorder enlarges the sinus spaces in the bone. This choice does not describe what most respiratory disorders do.","Correct. Most disorders narrow or block the airways, damage or fill the alveoli, or affect the pleurae.","Laryngitis inflames the vocal folds, but no common disorder destroys the laryngeal cartilages. This choice picks one structure and a change that does not happen, so it does not describe most disorders."],"lt":"t-resp-disorders","lid":"W3-657","comp":104,"competencyId":"resp-disorders"},{"id":"L03873","dok":1,"q":"Which structure is affected in asthma?","a":"The bronchioles.","options":["The larynx","The alveoli","The bronchioles","The pleural cavity"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Asthma affects the bronchi and bronchioles, and the narrowing is worst in the bronchioles. Bronchioles have smooth muscle and no cartilage, so when the lining is inflamed and the muscle tightens, nothing holds them open and they narrow. That narrowing causes the wheezing and breathlessness of an asthma attack.","notes":["The larynx is the voice box in the upper part of the lower tract. It is not the site of asthma. It would be the answer if the question asked where the vocal folds are housed.","The alveoli are affected in emphysema and pneumonia, where walls are destroyed or sacs fill with fluid. Pick the alveoli if the question asked about either of those disorders.","Correct. Asthma affects the bronchioles.","The pleural cavity is affected in pneumothorax, when air enters the space between the pleurae. Choose it if the question asked about a collapsed lung after a chest puncture."],"lt":"t-resp-disorders","lid":"W3-658","comp":104,"competencyId":"resp-disorders"},{"id":"L03874","dok":1,"q":"Which description best defines asthma?","a":"Inflammation and narrowing of the bronchioles, causing wheezing and breathlessness.","options":["Inflammation and narrowing of the bronchioles with wheezing","An infection that inflames the alveoli and fills them with fluid","Destruction of alveolar walls that reduces exchange surface","Air in the pleural cavity that lets the lung collapse"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Asthma is inflammation and narrowing of the bronchioles, the small airways with smooth muscle walls and no cartilage. When they narrow, air moving through them makes a whistling sound called wheezing, and the person feels short of breath. The problem is in the airways, not the alveoli themselves.","notes":["Correct. Asthma is inflammation and narrowing of the bronchioles, causing wheezing.","An infection that inflames the alveoli and fills them with fluid is pneumonia. Choose this if the question asked to define pneumonia.","Destruction of alveolar walls is emphysema, a permanent loss of exchange surface. Pick this if the question asked to define emphysema.","Air in the pleural cavity that lets the lung collapse is a pneumothorax. It would be the answer if the question asked to define pneumothorax."],"lt":"t-resp-disorders","lid":"W3-659","comp":104,"competencyId":"resp-disorders"},{"id":"L03875","dok":1,"q":"Which structures are affected in COPD (chronic obstructive pulmonary disease)?","a":"The airways and the alveoli.","options":["The pleurae and pleural cavity","The airways and the alveoli","The nasal cavity and sinuses","The larynx and vocal folds"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"COPD is a long-term blockage of airflow that includes two conditions: chronic bronchitis, which affects the airways, and emphysema, which destroys the alveolar walls. Because it combines both, COPD affects the airways and the alveoli. This makes it broader than asthma, which centers on the bronchioles, or pneumonia, which centers on the alveoli.","notes":["The pleurae and pleural cavity are affected in pneumothorax, when air breaks the pleural seal. Pick this if the question asked about a collapsed lung.","Correct. COPD affects both the airways and the alveoli.","The nasal cavity and sinuses are in the upper respiratory tract and are not where COPD acts. Choose them if the question asked which structures warm and filter incoming air.","The larynx and vocal folds produce sound and guard the airway, and they are not the site of COPD. They would be the answer if the question asked which structures are involved in speaking."],"lt":"t-resp-disorders","lid":"W3-660","comp":104,"competencyId":"resp-disorders"},{"id":"L03876","dok":1,"q":"COPD is a long-term blockage of airflow. Which two conditions does it include?","a":"Emphysema and chronic bronchitis.","options":["Emphysema and chronic bronchitis","Asthma and pneumonia","Pneumothorax and emphysema","Pneumonia and lung cancer"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"COPD, or chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, is an umbrella term for long-term airflow blockage. It includes emphysema, in which alveolar walls are destroyed, and chronic bronchitis, in which the airways are inflamed over a long period. Both are lasting conditions, which is what the word chronic means.","notes":["Correct. COPD includes emphysema and chronic bronchitis.","Asthma narrows the bronchioles in attacks, and pneumonia is an infection of the alveoli. Neither is one of the two conditions grouped under COPD. Pick asthma if the question asked which disorder narrows the bronchioles.","Emphysema is part of COPD, but pneumothorax is air in the pleural cavity, a separate problem outside the airways. This pair is half right. Pick pneumothorax if the question asked about a collapsed lung after a puncture.","Pneumonia is an infection that fills alveoli with fluid, and lung cancer is a malignant tumor. They are separate disorders, not parts of COPD. Choose lung cancer if the question asked which disorder is a malignant tumor."],"lt":"t-resp-disorders","lid":"W3-661","comp":104,"competencyId":"resp-disorders"},{"id":"L03877","dok":1,"q":"Which structure is damaged in emphysema?","a":"The alveoli.","options":["The bronchioles","The alveoli","The pleural cavity","The trachea"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Emphysema destroys the walls of the alveoli, the tiny air sacs where gas exchange happens. As the walls break down, many small sacs merge into a few large ones, so the total exchange surface shrinks. The change is structural and permanent.","notes":["The bronchioles are the site of asthma, where the small airways narrow. Pick them if the question asked about asthma.","Correct. Emphysema damages the alveoli.","The pleural cavity is the site of pneumothorax, when air enters the space between the pleurae. Choose it if the question asked about a collapsed lung.","The trachea is a large airway held open by C-shaped cartilage rings, and it is not the site of emphysema. It would be the answer if the question asked which airway forks at the carina."],"lt":"t-resp-disorders","lid":"W3-662","comp":104,"competencyId":"resp-disorders"},{"id":"L03878","dok":1,"q":"What happens to the lung in emphysema?","a":"The alveolar walls are destroyed, reducing the surface area for gas exchange.","options":["Air leaks into the pleural cavity, and the lung then collapses","The alveoli fill with fluid from an infection","The alveolar walls are destroyed and exchange surface shrinks","The bronchioles narrow during attacks and then reopen"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"In emphysema the thin walls between alveoli break down. Many small air sacs merge into fewer, larger ones, which leaves much less total surface where air and blood meet. Because the walls do not grow back, the loss of exchange surface is permanent.","notes":["Air leaking into the pleural cavity and collapsing the lung is a pneumothorax. It would be the answer if the question described a chest puncture.","Alveoli filling with fluid from an infection is pneumonia. The alveoli are still intact, just flooded. Choose this if the question asked about pneumonia.","Correct. In emphysema the alveolar walls are destroyed and the exchange surface shrinks.","Bronchioles narrowing in attacks and then reopening describes asthma, which affects the small airways. Pick this if the question asked about asthma."],"lt":"t-resp-disorders","lid":"W3-663","comp":104,"competencyId":"resp-disorders"},{"id":"L03879","dok":1,"q":"Which structures are inflamed in pneumonia?","a":"The alveoli.","options":["The alveoli","The bronchioles","The pleural cavities","The nasal conchae"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Pneumonia is an infection that inflames the alveoli and fills them with fluid. Because the alveoli are the air sacs where gas exchange happens, flooding them leaves less space for air to reach the blood. The alveolar walls are not destroyed as in emphysema, but they cannot work well while they are full of fluid.","notes":["Correct. Pneumonia inflames the alveoli.","The bronchioles are the site of asthma, where the small airways narrow. They would be the answer if the question asked about wheezing from narrowed airways.","The pleural cavities are the site of pneumothorax, where air enters the space between the pleurae. Pick them if the question asked about a collapsed lung after a puncture.","The nasal conchae are scroll-shaped projections in the nasal cavity that swirl incoming air. They are not the site of pneumonia. Choose them if the question asked how the nose conditions air."],"lt":"t-resp-disorders","lid":"W3-664","comp":104,"competencyId":"resp-disorders"},{"id":"L03880","dok":1,"q":"Which description best defines pneumonia?","a":"An infection that inflames the alveoli and fills them with fluid.","options":["A malignant tumor of the lung tissue strongly linked to smoking","Destruction of alveolar walls that merges small sacs into large ones","Long-term narrowing of the airways that includes chronic bronchitis","An infection that inflames the alveoli and fills them with fluid"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Pneumonia is an infection of the lung in which the alveoli become inflamed and fill with fluid. The fluid takes up the space where air should be, so less air reaches the exchange surface. Unlike emphysema, the alveolar walls are not destroyed, so the alveoli can recover once the infection clears.","notes":["A malignant tumor of lung tissue strongly linked to smoking is lung cancer. Choose this if the question asked to define lung cancer.","Destruction of alveolar walls that merges small sacs into large ones is emphysema. Pick this if the question asked to define emphysema.","Long-term airway obstruction that includes chronic bronchitis is COPD. It would be the answer if the question asked to define COPD.","Correct. Pneumonia is an infection that inflames the alveoli and fills them with fluid."],"lt":"t-resp-disorders","lid":"W3-665","comp":104,"competencyId":"resp-disorders"},{"id":"L03881","dok":1,"q":"Which space is affected in a pneumothorax?","a":"The pleural cavity.","options":["The mediastinum","The pleural cavity","The pericardial cavity","The alveolar sacs"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Pneumothorax means air in the thorax, and specifically it means air in the pleural cavity, the thin space between the parietal and visceral pleurae. Normally that space is sealed and holds only a film of fluid. When air gets in, the seal is lost and the lung can collapse.","notes":["The mediastinum is the central compartment of the chest holding the heart, great vessels, trachea, and esophagus. It would be the answer if the question asked which region lies between the two pleural cavities.","Correct. A pneumothorax affects the pleural cavity.","The pericardial cavity is the space around the heart, between the layers of the pericardium. Pick it if the question asked about fluid around the heart.","The alveolar sacs are supposed to hold air, so air in them is normal. They are affected in pneumonia and emphysema. Choose them if the question asked where pneumonia fluid collects."],"lt":"t-resp-disorders","lid":"W3-666","comp":104,"competencyId":"resp-disorders"},{"id":"L03882","dok":1,"q":"Which description best defines a pneumothorax?","a":"Air in the pleural cavity that breaks the seal and lets the lung collapse.","options":["Fluid filling the alveoli during an infection of the lung","Air in the pleural cavity that lets the lung collapse","Narrowing of the bronchioles that causes wheezing","Fluid collecting in the sac around the heart"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"A pneumothorax is air in the pleural cavity, usually after a puncture of the chest wall or the lung. The pleural cavity is normally a sealed space with only a thin film of fluid, and that seal helps hold the lung against the chest wall. When air gets in, the seal is lost and the lung collapses.","notes":["Fluid filling the alveoli during infection is pneumonia. Pick this if the question asked to define pneumonia.","Correct. A pneumothorax is air in the pleural cavity that lets the lung collapse.","Narrowing of the bronchioles with wheezing is asthma. Choose this if the question asked to define asthma.","Fluid collecting in the sac around the heart affects the pericardial cavity, not the pleural cavity. It would be the answer if the question were about the pericardium."],"lt":"t-resp-disorders","lid":"W3-667","comp":104,"competencyId":"resp-disorders"},{"id":"L03883","dok":1,"q":"Which respiratory disorder is a malignant tumor of the lung tissue?","a":"Lung cancer.","options":["Lung cancer","Asthma","Emphysema","Pneumonia"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Lung cancer is a malignant tumor, a growth of abnormal cells that can invade nearby tissue and spread. It usually starts in the epithelium lining the airways or alveoli, and it is strongly linked to smoking. It is the only choice here defined by a tumor rather than narrowing, destruction, or fluid.","notes":["Correct. Lung cancer is a malignant tumor of the lung.","Asthma is inflammation and narrowing of the bronchioles. It would be the answer if the question described wheezing from narrowed small airways.","Emphysema destroys alveolar walls, but it is not a tumor. It is also strongly linked to smoking, which is why students mix it up with lung cancer. Pick emphysema if the question described a permanent loss of exchange surface.","Pneumonia is an infection that inflames the alveoli and fills them with fluid. It is not a tumor. Choose it if the question described fluid-filled, inflamed alveoli."],"lt":"t-resp-disorders","lid":"W3-668","comp":104,"competencyId":"resp-disorders"},{"id":"L03884","dok":1,"q":"Which description best defines lung cancer?","a":"A malignant tumor of lung tissue, strongly linked to smoking.","options":["Destruction of the alveolar walls, also strongly linked to smoking","Air in the pleural cavity after a chest puncture","A malignant tumor of lung tissue, strongly linked to smoking","Inflammation and narrowing of the small airways"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Lung cancer is a malignant tumor of the lung tissue, a growth of abnormal cells that can invade and spread. Smoking is the risk factor most strongly linked to it. What sets it apart from the other disorders in this unit is that it is a tumor, not a blockage, an infection, or a leak.","notes":["Destruction of alveolar walls is emphysema. Smoking is linked to both emphysema and lung cancer, so the smoking link alone does not tell them apart. Pick this if the question asked to define emphysema.","Air in the pleural cavity after a puncture is a pneumothorax. It would be the answer if the question asked to define pneumothorax.","Correct. Lung cancer is a malignant tumor of lung tissue, strongly linked to smoking.","Inflammation and narrowing of the small airways is asthma. Choose this if the question asked to define asthma."],"lt":"t-resp-disorders","lid":"W3-669","comp":104,"competencyId":"resp-disorders"},{"id":"L03885","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares asthma and emphysema by the structure each one affects?","a":"Asthma narrows the bronchioles; emphysema destroys alveolar walls.","options":["Asthma narrows the bronchioles, and emphysema destroys alveolar walls","Both narrow the bronchioles, but emphysema is permanent","Asthma destroys alveolar walls, and emphysema narrows the bronchioles","Asthma affects the pleural cavity, and emphysema affects the alveoli"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Asthma is a disease of the small airways: the bronchioles become inflamed and narrow, which blocks airflow. Emphysema is a disease of the exchange surface: the alveolar walls are destroyed, which shrinks the area where gases cross. So one disorder narrows the passages leading to the alveoli, and the other damages the alveoli themselves.","notes":["Correct. Asthma narrows the bronchioles, and emphysema destroys alveolar walls.","Emphysema does not act by narrowing the bronchioles. Its damage is to the alveolar walls. It is true that emphysema is permanent, which is what makes this choice tempting.","This reverses the two disorders. Asthma acts on the airways, which can tighten because they have smooth muscle, and emphysema acts on the alveoli.","The pleural cavity is affected in pneumothorax, not asthma. Pick the pleural cavity if the question compared pneumothorax with another disorder."],"lt":"t-resp-disorders","lid":"W3-686","comp":104,"competencyId":"resp-disorders"},{"id":"L03886","dok":2,"q":"Pneumonia and emphysema both affect the alveoli. Which statement correctly compares what goes wrong in each?","a":"Pneumonia fills the alveoli with fluid; emphysema destroys the alveolar walls.","options":["Both fill the alveoli with fluid, but only pneumonia is caused by smoking","Pneumonia lets air into the pleural cavity, and emphysema narrows the bronchioles","Pneumonia fills the alveoli with fluid, and emphysema destroys the alveolar walls","Pneumonia destroys the alveolar walls, and emphysema fills the alveoli with fluid"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"In pneumonia, an infection inflames the alveoli and fills them with fluid, so air cannot reach the exchange surface even though the walls are intact. In emphysema, the alveolar walls themselves are destroyed, merging small sacs into large ones and permanently reducing the exchange surface. Both hurt gas exchange at the alveoli, but one floods them and the other breaks them down.","notes":["Emphysema does not fill alveoli with fluid, and pneumonia is an infection rather than a smoking disease. Smoking is linked to emphysema and lung cancer. This choice mixes up the cause and the damage.","Air in the pleural cavity is pneumothorax, and narrowed bronchioles are asthma. This statement describes two other disorders. Pick it only if the question compared pneumothorax and asthma.","Correct. Pneumonia fills the alveoli with fluid, and emphysema destroys the alveolar walls.","This reverses the two disorders. Pneumonia is the infection that floods alveoli, and emphysema is the structural, permanent destruction of their walls."],"lt":"t-resp-disorders","lid":"W3-687","comp":104,"competencyId":"resp-disorders"},{"id":"L03887","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares pneumothorax and pneumonia by the space each one affects?","a":"Pneumothorax affects the pleural cavity; pneumonia affects the alveoli.","options":["Pneumothorax affects the alveoli, and pneumonia affects the pleural cavity","Pneumothorax affects the pleural cavity, and pneumonia affects the alveoli","Pneumothorax affects the bronchioles, and pneumonia affects the trachea","Both affect the pleural cavity, but pneumonia adds fluid instead of air"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"A pneumothorax is air in the pleural cavity, the sealed space outside the lung between the parietal and visceral pleurae, and it can let the lung collapse. Pneumonia is an infection inside the lung that fills the alveoli with fluid. So pneumothorax is a problem around the lung, while pneumonia is a problem inside the air sacs.","notes":["This reverses the two spaces. Students mix them up because both names start with pneumo, which refers to air or the lungs. Pneumothorax is the one in the thorax outside the lung.","Correct. Pneumothorax affects the pleural cavity, and pneumonia affects the alveoli.","Neither disorder is defined by the bronchioles or the trachea. Narrowed bronchioles are asthma. Pick bronchioles if the question asked which structure asthma affects.","Pneumonia does not affect the pleural cavity. Its fluid collects inside the alveoli. This choice moves pneumonia to the wrong side of the visceral pleura."],"lt":"t-resp-disorders","lid":"W3-688","comp":104,"competencyId":"resp-disorders"},{"id":"L03888","dok":2,"q":"In emphysema, many small alveoli merge into a few large air sacs. Why does that reduce gas exchange?","a":"A few large sacs have much less total wall surface than many small ones.","options":["A few large sacs have much less total wall surface","Large sacs fill with fluid more easily than small ones","A few large sacs narrow the bronchioles that feed them","Large sacs push air back into the pleural cavity"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Gas exchange happens across the alveolar walls, so the amount of wall surface sets how much exchange can occur. Many small sacs have far more total wall area than the same volume divided into a few large sacs. When emphysema destroys the walls between alveoli, the lung trades a huge exchange surface for a small one, and the change is permanent.","notes":["Correct. Merging into a few large sacs leaves much less wall surface for gas exchange.","Filling with fluid is what happens in pneumonia, an infection. Emphysema is a loss of wall, not a flooding of the sacs. Pick fluid-filled alveoli if the question asked about pneumonia.","Narrowing of the bronchioles is asthma, which affects the airways before the alveoli. This choice blames the wrong structure. Pick bronchiole narrowing if the question asked about wheezing.","Air in the pleural cavity is a pneumothorax, caused by a puncture. Emphysema does not move air into the pleural cavity by itself. Choose this if the question asked about a collapsed lung."],"lt":"t-resp-disorders","lid":"W3-689","comp":104,"competencyId":"resp-disorders"},{"id":"L03889","dok":2,"q":"Why does air entering the pleural cavity cause a lung to collapse?","a":"It breaks the sealed pleural space that helps keep the lung expanded.","options":["It breaks the pleural seal that holds the lung expanded","It washes the surfactant out of the alveoli so they shut","It narrows the bronchioles so no air can reach the lung","It tears the visceral pericardium away from the heart"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The pleural cavity is a sealed space between the parietal pleura on the chest wall and the visceral pleura on the lung, holding only a thin film of fluid. That sealed space keeps the lung held out against the chest wall. When a puncture lets air in, the seal is lost, the lung is no longer held out, and it collapses.","notes":["Correct. Air breaks the seal of the pleural space, so the lung is no longer held expanded.","Surfactant is inside the alveoli, on the other side of the visceral pleura and the lung tissue. Air in the pleural cavity does not reach it. Pick lost surfactant if the question asked why a premature newborn's alveoli collapse.","Narrowing of the bronchioles is asthma, an airway problem. Pneumothorax acts outside the lung, not inside the airways. Choose this if the question asked about asthma.","The visceral pericardium covers the heart, not the lung, and it is a different serous membrane. This choice mixes up the pleura and the pericardium."],"lt":"t-resp-disorders","lid":"W3-690","comp":104,"competencyId":"resp-disorders"},{"id":"L03890","dok":3,"q":"A patient has episodes of wheezing and breathlessness, and testing shows her bronchioles are inflamed and narrowed. Which disorder best matches?","a":"Asthma.","options":["Asthma","Emphysema","Pneumonia","Pneumothorax"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Asthma is inflammation and narrowing of the bronchioles, the small airways with smooth muscle and no cartilage. Narrowed bronchioles make air whistle as it moves through, which is wheezing, and they limit how much air gets in and out. The episodes and the bronchiole findings point to asthma.","notes":["Correct. Inflamed, narrowed bronchioles with wheezing match asthma.","Emphysema destroys alveolar walls and permanently reduces exchange surface. It affects the alveoli, not mainly the bronchioles. Choose it if the scenario described a permanent loss of alveolar surface.","Pneumonia is an infection that fills the alveoli with fluid. It would be the answer if the scenario described inflamed, fluid-filled alveoli.","Pneumothorax is air in the pleural cavity that lets a lung collapse, usually after a puncture. It does not narrow the bronchioles. Pick it if the scenario described a chest wound and a collapsed lung."],"lt":"t-resp-disorders","lid":"W3-696","comp":104,"competencyId":"resp-disorders"},{"id":"L03891","dok":3,"q":"A longtime smoker's lung tissue shows that many small alveoli have merged into a few large sacs, a permanent loss of exchange surface. Which disorder best matches?","a":"Emphysema.","options":["Pneumonia","Pneumothorax","Emphysema","Asthma"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Emphysema destroys the walls between alveoli, so many small air sacs merge into a few large ones. That leaves much less surface for gas exchange, and because the walls do not regrow, the loss is permanent. A permanent, structural loss of alveolar surface is the hallmark of emphysema.","notes":["Pneumonia fills the alveoli with fluid during an infection, but the walls stay intact and can recover. Choose it if the scenario described inflamed, fluid-filled alveoli.","Pneumothorax is air in the pleural cavity that collapses the lung. It does not change the alveoli themselves. It would be the answer if the scenario described a chest puncture.","Correct. A permanent loss of alveolar surface from merged sacs is emphysema.","Asthma narrows the bronchioles in episodes and does not destroy alveoli. Pick asthma if the scenario described wheezing from narrowed small airways."],"lt":"t-resp-disorders","lid":"W3-697","comp":104,"competencyId":"resp-disorders"},{"id":"L03892","dok":3,"q":"A patient with a fever and cough is found to have inflamed alveoli filled with fluid in one region of the lung. Which disorder best matches?","a":"Pneumonia.","options":["Emphysema","Pneumonia","Pneumothorax","Asthma"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Pneumonia is an infection that inflames the alveoli and fills them with fluid. The fever points to an infection, and the fluid-filled alveoli show where it is. Because fluid takes up the space where air should be, less air reaches the exchange surface in that part of the lung.","notes":["Emphysema destroys alveolar walls but does not fill them with fluid, and it is not an infection. Pick it if the scenario described a permanent loss of alveolar surface.","Correct. Inflamed, fluid-filled alveoli from an infection match pneumonia.","Pneumothorax puts air in the pleural cavity, outside the lung, not fluid in the alveoli. Choose it if the scenario described a puncture and a collapsed lung.","Asthma narrows the bronchioles and does not fill the alveoli. It would be the answer if the scenario described wheezing from narrowed small airways."],"lt":"t-resp-disorders","lid":"W3-698","comp":104,"competencyId":"resp-disorders"},{"id":"L03893","dok":3,"q":"A stab wound to the chest wall lets air enter the space between the parietal and visceral pleurae. What condition can result?","a":"A pneumothorax, with possible collapse of the lung.","options":["Asthma, with narrowed bronchioles","Pneumothorax, with a collapsed lung","Pneumonia, with fluid in the alveoli","Emphysema, with merging of alveoli"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The space between the parietal and visceral pleurae is the pleural cavity, which is normally sealed and holds only a thin film of fluid. A wound that lets air into it breaks the seal, so the lung is no longer held against the chest wall and can collapse. Air in the pleural cavity is called a pneumothorax.","notes":["Asthma narrows the bronchioles and is not caused by a chest wound. It would be the answer if the scenario described wheezing from narrowed small airways.","Correct. Air entering the pleural cavity causes a pneumothorax, which can collapse the lung.","Pneumonia is an infection that fills the alveoli with fluid. A stab wound puts air outside the lung, not fluid inside it. Choose it if the scenario described fever and fluid-filled alveoli.","Emphysema is a slow, permanent destruction of alveolar walls, usually linked to smoking, not the result of a wound. Pick it if the scenario described a long-term loss of exchange surface."],"lt":"t-resp-disorders","lid":"W3-699","comp":104,"competencyId":"resp-disorders"},{"id":"L03894","dok":2,"q":"In asthma the bronchioles narrow, but the trachea does not. Which structural difference explains this?","a":"The trachea is held open by cartilage rings, while bronchioles have smooth muscle and no cartilage.","options":["The trachea is held open by cartilage, which bronchioles lack","The trachea is lined by visceral pleura that resists narrowing","The trachea has more smooth muscle than the bronchioles","The bronchioles are wrapped in capillaries that squeeze them"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Moving down the bronchial tree, cartilage decreases and smooth muscle increases, and the bronchioles are where the cartilage disappears. The trachea is held permanently open by C-shaped rings of hyaline cartilage, so it cannot be squeezed shut. Bronchioles have no cartilage, and smooth muscle makes up much of their wall, so when the lining is inflamed and the muscle tightens, they narrow.","notes":["Correct. Cartilage holds the trachea open, while bronchioles lack cartilage and can constrict.","The visceral pleura covers the outer surface of the lungs, not the trachea, and it does not hold airways open. Pick visceral pleura if the question asked which membrane covers the lung.","This reverses the pattern. Smooth muscle increases as the airways get smaller, so the bronchioles have relatively more of it, not less. Students pick this by thinking a bigger airway must have more of everything.","Pulmonary capillaries wrap the alveoli, not the bronchioles, and they do not squeeze airways. Choose capillaries if the question asked what surrounds each alveolus."],"lt":"t-resp-disorders","lid":"resp-disorders-r1","comp":104,"competencyId":"resp-disorders"},{"id":"L03916","dok":2,"q":"Which sequence correctly traces inhaled air from the nasal cavity to the terminal bronchioles?","a":"Nasal cavity, pharynx, larynx, trachea, main bronchi, lobar bronchi, segmental bronchi, bronchioles, terminal bronchioles.","options":["Nasal cavity, pharynx, larynx, trachea, main bronchi, segmental bronchi, lobar bronchi, bronchioles, terminal bronchioles","Nasal cavity, pharynx, larynx, trachea, main bronchi, lobar bronchi, segmental bronchi, bronchioles, terminal bronchioles","Nasal cavity, larynx, pharynx, trachea, main bronchi, lobar bronchi, segmental bronchi, bronchioles, terminal bronchioles","Nasal cavity, pharynx, trachea, larynx, main bronchi, lobar bronchi, segmental bronchi, bronchioles, terminal bronchioles"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Air passes from the nasal cavity into the pharynx (the throat), then through the larynx (the voice box) into the trachea. The trachea forks into the main bronchi, which divide into lobar bronchi for each lobe, then segmental bronchi for each segment, then bronchioles. The terminal bronchioles are the last airways of the conducting zone, the part that only carries air.","notes":["This sequence swaps the lobar and segmental bronchi. Lobar bronchi supply whole lobes and come first, and each one divides into smaller segmental bronchi for the bronchopulmonary segments.","Correct. This is the conducting path from the nasal cavity to the terminal bronchioles.","This sequence puts the larynx before the pharynx. The pharynx lies directly behind the nasal cavity and leads down to the larynx, so the larynx comes second.","This sequence puts the trachea above the larynx. The larynx sits on top of the trachea and connects the pharynx to it, so air reaches the larynx first."],"lt":"t-pathway-full-tracing","lid":"W3-722","comp":97,"competencyId":"resp-zones"},{"id":"L03917","dok":2,"q":"Which sequence correctly traces air from a terminal bronchiole into the alveoli?","a":"Terminal bronchiole, respiratory bronchioles, alveolar ducts, alveolar sacs, alveoli.","options":["Terminal bronchiole, segmental bronchi, alveolar ducts, alveolar sacs, alveoli","Terminal bronchiole, respiratory bronchioles, alveolar sacs, alveolar ducts, alveoli","Terminal bronchiole, alveolar ducts, respiratory bronchioles, alveolar sacs, alveoli","Terminal bronchiole, respiratory bronchioles, alveolar ducts, alveolar sacs, alveoli"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Past the terminal bronchiole the airway enters the respiratory zone, where gas exchange can happen. Respiratory bronchioles come first, with a few alveoli budding from their walls. They lead into alveolar ducts, whose walls are lined entirely with alveoli, and the ducts end in alveolar sacs, which are clusters of alveoli sharing a common space.","notes":["Segmental bronchi are much larger conducting airways that come well before the bronchioles. They would fit a trace from the main bronchi downward, not one that starts at a terminal bronchiole.","This sequence swaps the alveolar sacs and alveolar ducts. The ducts are the passages, and the sacs are the dead-end clusters at their ends, so the sacs come last before the individual alveoli.","This sequence puts the alveolar ducts before the respiratory bronchioles. The respiratory bronchioles still look like small airways with only a few alveoli, so they come first, and the ducts lined with alveoli come next.","Correct. This is the respiratory zone in order."],"lt":"t-pathway-full-tracing","lid":"W3-723","comp":97,"competencyId":"resp-zones"},{"id":"L03947","dok":3,"q":"Inhaled air has just reached a terminal bronchiole. Has gas exchange with the blood started yet?","a":"No. Terminal bronchioles are the last part of the conducting zone.","options":["Yes, because gas exchange begins as soon as air enters the trachea","Yes, because terminal bronchioles are the first part of the respiratory zone","No, because gas exchange begins only in the alveolar sacs","No, because terminal bronchioles are the last part of the conducting zone"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The airway is divided into a conducting zone, which only carries air, and a respiratory zone, where gas exchange happens. The terminal bronchioles are the final airways of the conducting zone. Gas exchange becomes possible one step later, in the respiratory bronchioles, which have a few alveoli budding from their walls.","notes":["The trachea is part of the conducting zone, which warms, moistens, and filters air but does not exchange gases. Its thick wall with cartilage rings is far too thick for gases to cross.","The first part of the respiratory zone is the respiratory bronchiole, not the terminal bronchiole. That statement would be true if the air had reached a respiratory bronchiole.","Gas exchange actually begins earlier, in the respiratory bronchioles and alveolar ducts, which have alveoli in their walls. This choice treats only the very end of the tree as the respiratory zone.","Correct. The terminal bronchiole still belongs to the conducting zone, so exchange has not begun."],"lt":"t-pathway-applied-sequencing","lid":"W3-754","comp":97,"competencyId":"resp-zones"},{"id":"L03948","dok":3,"q":"Inhaled air has just reached a respiratory bronchiole. Has gas exchange become possible?","a":"Yes. Respiratory bronchioles begin the respiratory zone.","options":["Yes, because a few alveoli bud from its walls, starting the respiratory zone","No, because gas exchange happens only in the alveolar sacs","No, because respiratory bronchioles are the last part of the conducting zone","Yes, because its walls are lined with cartilage rings like the trachea"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The respiratory bronchioles are the first airways of the respiratory zone. A few alveoli bud from their walls, and alveoli are where gases cross between air and blood. So once air reaches a respiratory bronchiole, gas exchange has become possible.","notes":["Correct. The alveoli in its walls make the respiratory bronchiole the start of the respiratory zone.","Gas exchange happens wherever alveoli are present, which includes the respiratory bronchioles and alveolar ducts, not just the sacs. This choice treats only the end of the tree as the respiratory zone.","The last part of the conducting zone is the terminal bronchiole, one step earlier. This statement would be true if the air had only reached a terminal bronchiole.","Bronchioles have no cartilage at all, and cartilage would not allow gas exchange anyway. Cartilage rings describe the trachea, which is part of the conducting zone."],"lt":"t-pathway-applied-sequencing","lid":"W3-755","comp":97,"competencyId":"resp-zones"},{"id":"L03949","dok":3,"q":"An inhaled peanut reaches the carina. Which airways could it enter next?","a":"The right or left main bronchus.","options":["The right or left lobar bronchus","The right or left segmental bronchus","The right or left main bronchus","The larynx or the trachea"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The carina is the internal ridge at the bottom of the trachea where it forks into the two main bronchi, also called the primary bronchi. Anything that reaches the carina must go into either the right or the left main bronchus. Each main bronchus then enters a lung and divides into lobar bronchi.","notes":["The lobar bronchi come one level later, after a main bronchus divides inside the lung. They would be the answer if the object had already entered a main bronchus.","The segmental bronchi are two levels past the carina, branching from the lobar bronchi. They would be the answer if the object had reached a lobar bronchus.","Correct. The carina splits the trachea into the right and left main bronchi.","The larynx and trachea are above the carina, so the object has already passed through them. They would be the answer if the question asked what it passed through after the pharynx."],"lt":"t-pathway-applied-sequencing","lid":"W3-756","comp":100,"competencyId":"resp-tree"},{"id":"L03950","dok":3,"q":"Inhaled objects land in the right lung more often than the left. What structural difference explains this?","a":"The right main bronchus is wider and more vertical than the left.","options":["The left main bronchus is wider and more vertical than the right","The right main bronchus has no cartilage, so it stays open wider","The right lung has more lobes, so it pulls in more air","The right main bronchus is wider and more vertical than the left"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"At the carina the trachea splits into two main bronchi that are not identical. The right main bronchus is wider, shorter, and more vertical, almost in line with the trachea, while the left is narrower and angles off more sharply. An object falling down the trachea therefore tends to drop straight into the right main bronchus.","notes":["This is the mirror image of the truth. The left main bronchus is the narrower, more horizontal one, so objects are less likely to enter it. Pick wider and more vertical only for the right side.","Both main bronchi are supported by cartilage, which holds them open. Cartilage is lost only at the level of the bronchioles, so this choice confuses the main bronchi with much smaller airways.","The right lung does have three lobes and the left has two, but the number of lobes does not steer an object. Students pick this because the right lung is larger, but the angle and width of the bronchus decide where an object goes.","Correct. The wider, more vertical right main bronchus lines up with the trachea."],"lt":"t-pathway-applied-sequencing","lid":"W3-757","comp":100,"competencyId":"resp-tree"},{"id":"L03959","dok":2,"q":"In the bronchial tree, which airways come immediately after the main (primary) bronchi?","a":"The lobar (secondary) bronchi.","options":["Bronchioles","Lobar bronchi","Trachea","Segmental bronchi"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Each main bronchus enters a lung and divides into lobar bronchi, also called secondary bronchi, one for each lobe. The right lung has three lobes and so three lobar bronchi, and the left lung has two. Each lobar bronchus then divides into segmental bronchi for the bronchopulmonary segments.","notes":["Bronchioles are much smaller airways with smooth muscle and no cartilage, reached only after the segmental bronchi. They would be the answer if the question started at the segmental bronchi.","Correct. The main bronchi divide into lobar bronchi.","The trachea is above the main bronchi, splitting into them at the carina. It would be the answer if the question asked which airway leads into the main bronchi.","Segmental bronchi, or tertiary bronchi, come one level after the lobar bronchi. They would be the answer if the question started at the lobar bronchi."],"lt":"t-pathway-integrated-tracing","lid":"W3-766","comp":100,"competencyId":"resp-tree"},{"id":"L03960","dok":2,"q":"In the lower respiratory tract, which airway comes immediately before the alveolar ducts?","a":"The respiratory bronchioles.","options":["Alveolar sacs","Terminal bronchioles","Segmental bronchi","Respiratory bronchioles"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The respiratory bronchioles connect the terminal bronchioles to the alveolar ducts. They are the first airways of the respiratory zone, with only a few alveoli budding from their walls. They lead into alveolar ducts, whose walls are lined entirely with alveoli.","notes":["Alveolar sacs come after the alveolar ducts, as the clusters of alveoli at the ends of the ducts. They would be the answer if the question asked what the alveolar ducts lead into.","Terminal bronchioles come one step earlier, at the end of the conducting zone. They would be the answer if the question asked which airway comes just before the respiratory bronchioles.","Segmental bronchi are larger conducting airways several branchings earlier. They would be the answer if the question asked what the lobar bronchi divide into.","Correct. Respiratory bronchioles lead into the alveolar ducts."],"lt":"t-pathway-integrated-tracing","lid":"W3-767","comp":97,"competencyId":"resp-zones"},{"id":"L03973","dok":3,"q":"A stab wound punctures the chest wall. Which sequence explains how this can collapse the lung (pneumothorax)?","a":"Air enters the pleural cavity, the pleural seal breaks, and the lung collapses.","options":["Bronchioles narrow, airflow is blocked, and breathing becomes wheezy","Fluid fills the alveoli, air cannot reach the blood, and exchange drops","Alveolar walls break down, air spaces enlarge, and surface area shrinks","Air enters the pleural cavity, the pleural seal breaks, and the lung collapses"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The pleural cavity is the thin, sealed, fluid-filled space between the parietal pleura on the chest wall and the visceral pleura on the lung. That seal keeps the lung expanded against the chest wall. When a wound lets air into the pleural cavity, the seal is lost and the lung can collapse, which is a pneumothorax.","notes":["Narrowing of the bronchioles with wheezing describes asthma. It would be the answer if the question asked how asthma reduces airflow.","Fluid filling the alveoli describes pneumonia, an infection of the alveoli. It would be the answer if the question asked how pneumonia reduces gas exchange.","Breakdown of alveolar walls into fewer, larger air spaces describes emphysema. It would be the answer if the question asked how emphysema reduces the exchange surface.","Correct. Air in the pleural cavity breaks the seal and lets the lung collapse."],"lt":"t-pathway-integrated-tracing","lid":"W3-781","comp":104,"competencyId":"resp-disorders"},{"id":"L03974","dok":3,"q":"A long-time smoker has emphysema. Why does this disease reduce gas exchange?","a":"Alveolar walls are destroyed, merging small sacs into fewer large ones with less surface area.","options":["The bronchioles narrow from inflammation, slowing air on its way in","Alveolar walls are destroyed, merging small sacs into fewer large ones with less surface area","The alveoli fill with fluid from an infection, blocking air from the blood","Air leaks into the pleural cavity, letting part of the lung collapse"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Gas exchange depends on the huge surface area of hundreds of millions of tiny alveoli. Emphysema destroys the thin walls between alveoli, so many small sacs merge into a few large ones. The total surface where gases cross shrinks, and the change is structural and permanent.","notes":["Inflamed, narrowed bronchioles describe asthma, a disease of the small airways. It would be the answer if the question asked why asthma causes wheezing.","Correct. Emphysema destroys alveolar walls and shrinks the exchange surface.","Fluid filling inflamed alveoli describes pneumonia. It would be the answer if the patient had a lung infection rather than emphysema.","Air leaking into the pleural cavity is a pneumothorax. It would be the answer if the question described a collapsed lung after a chest injury."],"lt":"t-pathway-integrated-tracing","lid":"W3-782","comp":104,"competencyId":"resp-disorders"},{"id":"L03975","dok":3,"q":"A patient with pneumonia has trouble getting oxygen into the blood. Which structures fill with fluid in pneumonia?","a":"The alveoli.","options":["Paranasal sinuses","Pleural cavity","Bronchioles","Alveoli"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Pneumonia is an infection that inflames the alveoli, the tiny air sacs where gas exchange happens, and fills them with fluid. Oxygen must cross from the air in the alveoli through the respiratory membrane into the blood. When the alveoli are filled with fluid instead of air, that exchange is impaired.","notes":["The paranasal sinuses are air-filled spaces in the skull bones that drain into the nasal cavity. They would be the answer if the question were about sinus congestion, not a lung infection.","The pleural cavity is the space between the two pleurae, outside the lung. It would be the answer if the question asked where air collects in a pneumothorax.","The bronchioles are small airways that narrow in asthma. They would be the answer if the question asked which airways constrict during an asthma attack.","Correct. Pneumonia fills the alveoli with fluid."],"lt":"t-pathway-integrated-tracing","lid":"W3-783","comp":104,"competencyId":"resp-disorders"},{"id":"L03976","dok":3,"q":"During an asthma attack, a patient wheezes and struggles to move air. Which airways narrow the most, because they have no cartilage to hold them open?","a":"The bronchioles.","options":["Alveolar ducts","Bronchioles","Trachea","Main bronchi"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Asthma inflames the bronchi and bronchioles, and the narrowing is worst in the bronchioles. Bronchioles have smooth muscle in their walls and no cartilage, so when the lining swells and the muscle tightens, nothing holds them open. The trachea and main bronchi keep their cartilage, so they stay open while the bronchioles narrow, and the restricted airflow produces wheezing and breathlessness.","notes":["Alveolar ducts belong to the respiratory zone and are lined with alveoli, not with the smooth muscle that narrows in asthma. They would be the answer if the question asked which passages lead into the alveolar sacs.","Correct. Bronchioles have no cartilage, so they narrow the most in asthma.","The trachea is held permanently open by C-shaped rings of hyaline cartilage, so it does not narrow the way bronchioles do. It would be the answer if the question asked which airway leads from the larynx to the main bronchi.","The main bronchi can be inflamed in asthma too, but their cartilage holds them open, so they do not narrow nearly as much as the bronchioles. They would be the answer if the question asked which airways branch from the trachea at the carina."],"lt":"t-pathway-integrated-tracing","lid":"W3-784","comp":104,"competencyId":"resp-disorders"},{"id":"L03977","dok":3,"q":"A premature infant's type II alveolar cells are not yet producing enough surfactant. What structural problem is most likely?","a":"The alveoli are more likely to collapse.","options":["The alveoli are more likely to collapse","The bronchioles narrow and wheeze","The alveolar walls are destroyed","Air leaks into the pleural cavity"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Type II alveolar cells are cuboidal cells in the alveolar wall that secrete surfactant. Surfactant lowers surface tension, the inward pull of the thin film of water lining each alveolus. With too little surfactant that pull is stronger, so the alveoli are more likely to collapse.","notes":["Correct. Without enough surfactant, the alveoli tend to collapse.","Narrowed, wheezing bronchioles describe asthma, a disease of the small airways rather than the alveoli. It would be the answer if the question described an asthma attack.","Destruction of the alveolar walls is emphysema, usually caused by years of smoking. It would be the answer if the question described a long-time smoker losing exchange surface.","Air in the pleural cavity is a pneumothorax, caused by a puncture of the chest wall or lung. It would be the answer if the question described a chest wound."],"lt":"t-pathway-integrated-tracing","lid":"W3-785","comp":104,"competencyId":"resp-disorders"},{"id":"L03987","dok":2,"q":"Moving from the inside of the thoracic wall inward to the surface of the lung, in which order do you meet the pleural layers?","a":"Parietal pleura, pleural cavity, visceral pleura.","options":["Parietal pleura, pleural cavity, visceral pleura","Pleural cavity, parietal pleura, visceral pleura","Visceral pleura, pleural cavity, parietal pleura","Parietal pleura, visceral pleura, pleural cavity"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The pleurae are serous membranes. The parietal pleura lines the thoracic wall, and the visceral pleura covers the outer surface of the lung. Between them is the pleural cavity, a thin, fluid-filled space that lets the lung glide as it expands.","notes":["Correct. The parietal pleura is on the wall, the visceral pleura is on the lung, and the cavity lies between them.","This puts the pleural cavity against the thoracic wall. The parietal pleura is the layer that lines the wall, so it must come first, with the cavity just inside it.","This is the order from the lung outward to the chest wall, the reverse of the path asked. It would be correct if the question started at the lung surface.","This puts the pleural cavity outside both membranes. The cavity is the space between the two layers, so it cannot come after the visceral pleura on the lung."],"lt":"t-pathway-integrated-tracing","lid":"W3-795","comp":101,"competencyId":"resp-lungs-pleura"},{"id":"L03988","dok":2,"q":"Oxygen moves from the air in an alveolus into a pulmonary capillary. Which list names the layers of the respiratory membrane it crosses, in order?","a":"Alveolar epithelium, fused basement membranes, capillary endothelium.","options":["Alveolar epithelium, fused basement membranes, capillary endothelium","Visceral pleura, pleural cavity, parietal pleura","Alveolar epithelium, smooth muscle, hyaline cartilage","Capillary endothelium, fused basement membranes, alveolar epithelium"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The respiratory membrane is the thin barrier between alveolar air and blood. Oxygen first crosses the alveolar epithelium (mostly flat type I cells), then the fused basement membranes of the alveolus and the capillary, and finally the capillary endothelium. Because each layer is so thin, gases cross quickly.","notes":["Correct. Oxygen crosses the alveolar epithelium, the fused basement membranes, and the capillary endothelium.","The pleurae surround the whole lung and line the chest wall. They have nothing to do with gas exchange, so this list would fit a question about the coverings of the lung.","Smooth muscle and cartilage are found in the walls of the conducting airways, such as the bronchi. They would make the barrier far too thick for gas exchange, so this list describes an airway wall, not the respiratory membrane.","These are the right layers in the reverse order. This order would fit carbon dioxide moving from the blood into the alveolus."],"lt":"t-pathway-integrated-tracing","lid":"W3-796","comp":102,"competencyId":"resp-histo"},{"id":"L04052","dok":2,"q":"Which list names the parts of the lower respiratory tract?","a":"Larynx, trachea, bronchi, and lungs","options":["Respiratory bronchioles, alveolar ducts, and alveoli","Pharynx, larynx, trachea, and esophagus","Nose, nasal cavity, paranasal sinuses, and pharynx","Larynx, trachea, bronchi, and lungs"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"In this course the lower respiratory tract begins at the larynx and includes the trachea, the bronchi, and the lungs. These structures lie below the pharynx and carry air down into the chest, ending in the alveoli inside the lungs. The upper tract, by contrast, is the nose, nasal cavity, paranasal sinuses, and pharynx.","notes":["The respiratory bronchioles, alveolar ducts, and alveoli are only the deepest part of the lungs, the respiratory zone. Choose this list if the question asked which structures carry out gas exchange.","This list mixes in the esophagus, which is a digestive organ, and the pharynx, which belongs to the upper tract. Students pick it because the esophagus runs right behind the trachea.","The nose, nasal cavity, paranasal sinuses, and pharynx form the upper respiratory tract. Pick this list if the question asked for the parts of the upper tract.","Correct. The lower tract is the larynx, trachea, bronchi, and lungs."],"lt":"t-imagefree-structure-recognition","lid":"W3-863","comp":97,"competencyId":"resp-zones"},{"id":"L04062","dok":2,"q":"Which airway, the windpipe, runs from the larynx to the carina and is held open by C-shaped rings of hyaline cartilage?","a":"Trachea","options":["Esophagus","Main bronchus","Trachea","Larynx"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The trachea is the windpipe, running from the bottom of the larynx down into the chest, where it forks at the carina. C-shaped rings of hyaline cartilage keep it permanently open. The open part of each ring faces backward, where the trachealis muscle closes the gap against the esophagus.","notes":["The esophagus is the food tube that lies just behind the trachea, against the trachealis muscle, with no cartilage rings. Choose it if the question asked which digestive tube runs behind the windpipe.","A main bronchus is one of the two branches the trachea splits into at the carina, each entering a lung. It would be the answer if the question asked which airway begins below the carina.","Correct. The trachea is the windpipe held open by C-shaped cartilage rings.","The larynx is the voice box above the trachea, built of nine separate cartilages rather than a stack of C-shaped rings. Pick it if the question asked which structure houses the vocal folds."],"lt":"t-imagefree-structure-recognition","lid":"W3-873","comp":100,"competencyId":"resp-tree"},{"id":"L04063","dok":2,"q":"Which internal ridge marks the point where the trachea divides into the two main bronchi?","a":"Carina","options":["Cricoid cartilage","Cardiac notch","Hilum","Carina"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The carina is a ridge of cartilage inside the lower end of the trachea, right at the fork where the right and left main bronchi split off. It sits at the base of the trachea in the mediastinum. It marks the end of the trachea and the start of the bronchial tree.","notes":["The cricoid cartilage is the complete ring of cartilage at the bottom of the larynx, above the trachea. It would be the answer if the question asked which cartilage is the only complete ring in the airway.","The cardiac notch is the indentation in the left lung that makes room for the heart. Choose it if the question asked which feature of the left lung accommodates the heart.","The hilum is the indentation on the medial surface of each lung where the bronchus and vessels enter. Pick it if the question asked where a main bronchus enters a lung.","Correct. The carina is the ridge where the trachea forks."],"lt":"t-imagefree-structure-recognition","lid":"W3-874","comp":100,"competencyId":"resp-tree"},{"id":"L04064","dok":2,"q":"Which bronchi, also called primary bronchi, branch from the trachea with one entering each lung?","a":"Main bronchi","options":["Segmental bronchi","Main bronchi","Terminal bronchioles","Lobar bronchi"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The trachea splits at the carina into the right and left main bronchi, the first branches of the bronchial tree. Each one enters a lung at its hilum. The right main bronchus is wider and more vertical than the left, which is why an inhaled object most often lands in the right lung.","notes":["Segmental bronchi, or tertiary bronchi, branch from the lobar bronchi to supply each bronchopulmonary segment. They would be the answer if the question asked about the third level of branching.","Correct. The main (primary) bronchi branch from the trachea, one to each lung.","Terminal bronchioles are tiny airways at the very end of the conducting zone, many branchings after the main bronchi. Choose them if the question asked for the last conducting airways before gas exchange.","Lobar bronchi, or secondary bronchi, are the next branches, one for each lobe of a lung. Pick them if the question asked which bronchi supply individual lobes."],"lt":"t-imagefree-structure-recognition","lid":"W3-875","comp":100,"competencyId":"resp-tree"},{"id":"L04065","dok":2,"q":"Which bronchi, also called secondary bronchi, each supply one lobe of a lung?","a":"Lobar bronchi","options":["Segmental bronchi","Lobar bronchi","Respiratory bronchioles","Main bronchi"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Each main bronchus divides into lobar bronchi, one for each lobe. The right lung has three lobes, so it has three lobar bronchi, and the left lung has two lobes and two lobar bronchi. These are the second level of the bronchial tree, after the main bronchi and before the segmental bronchi.","notes":["Segmental bronchi are the next level down, each supplying one bronchopulmonary segment within a lobe. They would be the answer if the question asked about the tertiary bronchi.","Correct. Lobar (secondary) bronchi supply one lobe each.","Respiratory bronchioles are tiny airways in the respiratory zone with alveoli budding from their walls. Choose them if the question asked which airways are the first to take part in gas exchange.","The main bronchi are the first branches off the trachea, one for each whole lung rather than each lobe. Pick them if the question asked which bronchi enter the lungs at the hilum."],"lt":"t-imagefree-structure-recognition","lid":"W3-876","comp":100,"competencyId":"resp-tree"},{"id":"L04066","dok":2,"q":"Which bronchi, also called tertiary bronchi, each supply one bronchopulmonary segment?","a":"Segmental bronchi","options":["Segmental bronchi","Bronchioles","Main bronchi","Lobar bronchi"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Segmental bronchi branch from the lobar bronchi, and each one supplies a bronchopulmonary segment, a wedge-shaped section of a lobe with its own air and blood supply. They are the third level of the bronchial tree. Below them the airways keep dividing until cartilage disappears and they become bronchioles.","notes":["Correct. Segmental (tertiary) bronchi supply one bronchopulmonary segment each.","Bronchioles are smaller airways further down the tree, with smooth muscle and no cartilage. Choose them if the question asked which airways have lost their cartilage.","The main bronchi are the primary bronchi, the first branches off the trachea, one to each lung. They would be the answer if the question asked which bronchi begin at the carina.","Lobar bronchi are the secondary bronchi, one level higher, supplying a whole lobe. Pick them if the question asked which bronchi go to each lobe."],"lt":"t-imagefree-structure-recognition","lid":"W3-877","comp":100,"competencyId":"resp-tree"},{"id":"L04067","dok":2,"q":"Which small conducting airways have smooth muscle in their walls but no cartilage?","a":"Bronchioles","options":["Bronchioles","Segmental bronchi","Alveolar ducts","Trachea"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"As the bronchial tree branches, cartilage decreases and smooth muscle increases. By the bronchioles the cartilage is gone, and smooth muscle makes up much of the wall, so the muscle can narrow or widen the airway. Cartilage keeps the larger bronchi open, but bronchioles can narrow a great deal, which is why the narrowing in asthma is worst in the bronchioles.","notes":["Correct. Bronchioles have smooth muscle and no cartilage.","Segmental bronchi still have cartilage plates in their walls, which keep them from collapsing. Pick them if the question asked which bronchi supply the bronchopulmonary segments.","Alveolar ducts are passages lined with alveoli in the respiratory zone, so they take part in gas exchange rather than just conducting air. Choose them if the question asked which passages are lined entirely with alveoli.","The trachea is held open by C-shaped rings of hyaline cartilage, the opposite of a cartilage-free wall. It would be the answer if the question asked which airway has the trachealis muscle across the back of its rings."],"lt":"t-imagefree-structure-recognition","lid":"W3-878","comp":100,"competencyId":"resp-tree"},{"id":"L04068","dok":2,"q":"Which airways are the last passages of the conducting zone, just before gas exchange begins?","a":"Terminal bronchioles","options":["Alveolar ducts","Segmental bronchi","Respiratory bronchioles","Terminal bronchioles"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Terminal bronchioles are the final branches of the conducting zone, the airways that carry air but do not exchange gases. Each one leads into respiratory bronchioles, where the first alveoli appear and the respiratory zone begins. Terminal means end, which points to their place at the end of the conducting airways.","notes":["Alveolar ducts are passages lined entirely with alveoli, well inside the respiratory zone. Choose them if the question asked which passages lead into the alveolar sacs.","Segmental bronchi are much higher in the tree, still carrying cartilage and supplying whole bronchopulmonary segments. They would be the answer if the question asked for the tertiary bronchi.","Respiratory bronchioles are the next step, the first airways of the respiratory zone with a few alveoli on their walls. Pick them if the question asked where gas exchange begins.","Correct. Terminal bronchioles are the last conducting airways."],"lt":"t-imagefree-structure-recognition","lid":"W3-879","comp":97,"competencyId":"resp-zones"},{"id":"L04069","dok":2,"q":"Which airways are the first of the respiratory zone, with a few alveoli budding from their walls?","a":"Respiratory bronchioles","options":["Alveolar sacs","Respiratory bronchioles","Alveolar ducts","Terminal bronchioles"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Respiratory bronchioles branch from the terminal bronchioles and mark the start of the respiratory zone. A few alveoli bud from their walls, so some gas exchange can happen there. Further along, the walls become lined entirely with alveoli in the alveolar ducts, which end in alveolar sacs.","notes":["Alveolar sacs are clusters of alveoli sharing a common space at the very end of the airway. Choose them if the question asked which structure is a cluster of alveoli at the end of an alveolar duct.","Correct. Respiratory bronchioles are the first airways of the respiratory zone.","Alveolar ducts come after the respiratory bronchioles and are lined entirely with alveoli rather than having just a few. They would be the answer if the question asked which passages have walls made completely of alveoli.","Terminal bronchioles are the last airways of the conducting zone, with no alveoli on their walls. Pick them if the question asked for the final conducting airways."],"lt":"t-imagefree-structure-recognition","lid":"W3-880","comp":102,"competencyId":"resp-histo"},{"id":"L04070","dok":2,"q":"Which description matches the right lung?","a":"Three lobes, with a horizontal fissure and an oblique fissure","options":["Three lobes, with a horizontal fissure and an oblique fissure","Three lobes, with an oblique fissure and a cardiac notch","Two lobes, with an oblique fissure and a cardiac notch","Two lobes, with a horizontal fissure only"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The right lung has three lobes, superior, middle, and inferior, separated by two fissures. The horizontal fissure separates the superior lobe from the middle lobe, and the oblique fissure separates the inferior lobe from the lobes above it. The right lung is larger than the left because it does not have to make room for the heart, so it has no cardiac notch.","notes":["Correct. The right lung has three lobes separated by horizontal and oblique fissures.","This mixes the two lungs: three lobes belong to the right lung, but the cardiac notch belongs to the left. Students choose it when they remember the lobe count but forget which side the heart pushes into.","Two lobes, an oblique fissure, and a cardiac notch describe the left lung. Pick this description if the question asked about the left lung.","No lung has two lobes with only a horizontal fissure, since the horizontal fissure is found only in the right lung, which has three lobes. Students choose it when they mix up which fissure each lung keeps."],"lt":"t-imagefree-structure-recognition","lid":"W3-881","comp":101,"competencyId":"resp-lungs-pleura"},{"id":"L04071","dok":2,"q":"Which description matches the left lung?","a":"Two lobes, with an oblique fissure and a cardiac notch","options":["Two lobes, with a horizontal fissure and a cardiac notch","Two lobes, with an oblique fissure and a cardiac notch","Three lobes, with an oblique fissure and a cardiac notch","Three lobes, with a horizontal fissure and an oblique fissure"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The left lung has two lobes, superior and inferior, separated by a single oblique fissure. It is smaller than the right lung because the heart tilts to the left, and its anterior border has a cardiac notch that makes room for the heart. Below the notch, a tongue-shaped piece of the superior lobe called the lingula curves around the heart.","notes":["This gives the left lung the wrong fissure, since the horizontal fissure is found only in the right lung. Students choose it when they remember the cardiac notch but mix up which fissure each lung keeps.","Correct. The left lung has two lobes, an oblique fissure, and a cardiac notch.","This mixes the two lungs: the cardiac notch belongs to the left lung, but three lobes belong to the right. Students choose it when they remember the notch but forget that the left lung gives up a lobe to make room for the heart.","Three lobes with horizontal and oblique fissures describe the right lung. Pick this description if the question asked about the right lung."],"lt":"t-imagefree-structure-recognition","lid":"W3-882","comp":101,"competencyId":"resp-lungs-pleura"},{"id":"L04072","dok":2,"q":"Which indentation on the medial surface of each lung is where the main bronchus, blood vessels, and nerves enter and leave?","a":"Hilum","options":["Lingula","Hilum","Apex","Cardiac notch"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The hilum is a depression on the medial surface of each lung, facing the mediastinum. The main bronchus, pulmonary artery, pulmonary veins, bronchial vessels, nerves, and lymphatics all pass through it. Bundled together at the hilum, these structures are called the root of the lung.","notes":["The lingula is the tongue-shaped projection of the left superior lobe below the cardiac notch. Choose it if the question asked about the small extension of the left lung that curves around the heart.","Correct. The hilum is where the bronchus, vessels, and nerves enter and leave the lung.","The apex is the narrow superior tip of the lung, rising just above the clavicle. It would be the answer if the question asked for the top of the lung.","The cardiac notch is an indentation in the anterior border of the left lung that makes room for the heart, and nothing enters the lung there. Pick it if the question asked which feature accommodates the heart."],"lt":"t-imagefree-structure-recognition","lid":"W3-883","comp":101,"competencyId":"resp-lungs-pleura"},{"id":"L04073","dok":2,"q":"Which serous membrane lines the inner surface of the thoracic wall around each lung?","a":"Parietal pleura","options":["Parietal peritoneum","Parietal pericardium","Parietal pleura","Visceral pleura"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The pleura is the serous membrane around each lung, and like other serous membranes it has two layers. The parietal pleura lines the thoracic wall, while the visceral pleura covers the lung itself. Parietal always refers to the layer on the body wall and visceral to the layer on the organ.","notes":["The parietal peritoneum lines the wall of the abdominopelvic cavity, below the diaphragm. Choose it if the question asked which membrane lines the abdominal wall.","The parietal pericardium is the serous layer lining the fibrous pericardium around the heart. It would be the answer if the question asked about the outer serous layer of the heart's sac.","Correct. The parietal pleura lines the thoracic wall.","The visceral pleura covers the outer surface of the lung itself rather than the chest wall. Pick it if the question asked which membrane is attached directly to the lung."],"lt":"t-imagefree-structure-recognition","lid":"W3-884","comp":101,"competencyId":"resp-lungs-pleura"},{"id":"L04075","dok":2,"q":"What is the thin, fluid-filled space between the parietal and visceral pleurae called?","a":"Pleural cavity","options":["Pericardial cavity","Thoracic cavity","Pleural cavity","Mediastinum"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The pleural cavity is the narrow, sealed space between the parietal pleura on the chest wall and the visceral pleura on the lung. It holds only a thin film of serous fluid, which lets the lung glide as it expands and keeps the two layers stuck together. If air gets into this space, the seal is lost and the lung can collapse.","notes":["The pericardial cavity is the fluid space between the layers of the serous pericardium around the heart. It would be the answer if the question asked about the space around the heart rather than the lungs.","The thoracic cavity is the whole chest cavity, which contains both pleural cavities and the mediastinum. Choose it if the question asked for the large cavity above the diaphragm, not the thin space between two membranes.","Correct. The pleural cavity is the fluid-filled space between the pleurae.","The mediastinum is the central compartment of the thorax between the two lungs, holding the heart, great vessels, trachea, and esophagus. Pick it if the question asked where the heart sits."],"lt":"t-imagefree-structure-recognition","lid":"W3-886","comp":101,"competencyId":"resp-lungs-pleura"},{"id":"L04076","dok":2,"q":"Which alveolar cells are thin and flat (simple squamous) and form most of the alveolar wall where gases cross?","a":"Type I alveolar cells","options":["Alveolar macrophages","Type I alveolar cells","Type II alveolar cells","Goblet cells"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Type I alveolar cells are extremely thin, flat cells that make up most of the alveolar wall. Being so thin keeps the distance between the air and the blood very short, which is exactly what gas exchange needs. They form the epithelial part of the respiratory membrane.","notes":["Alveolar macrophages are wandering immune cells, the dust cells, that engulf debris on the alveolar surface. They would be the answer if the question asked which cells clean the alveoli.","Correct. Type I alveolar cells form the thin wall where gases cross.","Type II alveolar cells are cuboidal cells scattered among the type I cells, and they secrete surfactant. Pick them if the question asked which cells keep the alveoli from collapsing.","Goblet cells are mucus-secreting cells in the respiratory mucosa of the larger airways, not in the alveolar wall. Choose them if the question asked which cells produce the mucus that traps particles in the conducting passages."],"lt":"t-imagefree-structure-recognition","lid":"W3-887","comp":102,"competencyId":"resp-histo"},{"id":"L04077","dok":2,"q":"Which alveolar cells are cuboidal and secrete surfactant, which keeps the alveoli from collapsing?","a":"Type II alveolar cells","options":["Type I alveolar cells","Goblet cells","Type II alveolar cells","Alveolar macrophages"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Type II alveolar cells are cuboidal cells scattered among the flat type I cells of the alveolar wall. They secrete surfactant, a fluid that lowers surface tension, meaning the pull of water molecules on each other that would otherwise make the tiny sacs stick shut. Without enough surfactant the alveoli tend to collapse.","notes":["Type I alveolar cells are the thin, flat cells that form most of the alveolar wall and do not secrete surfactant. Pick them if the question asked which cells form the surface where gases cross.","Goblet cells secrete mucus in the conducting airways, not surfactant in the alveoli. Choose them if the question asked which cells add mucus to the respiratory mucosa.","Correct. Type II alveolar cells secrete surfactant.","Alveolar macrophages are immune cells that engulf dust and debris in the alveoli. They would be the answer if the question asked which cells are called dust cells."],"lt":"t-imagefree-structure-recognition","lid":"W3-888","comp":102,"competencyId":"resp-histo"},{"id":"L04078","dok":2,"q":"Which cells, often called dust cells, patrol the alveolar surface and engulf debris?","a":"Alveolar macrophages","options":["Goblet cells","Type I alveolar cells","Alveolar macrophages","Type II alveolar cells"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Alveolar macrophages are immune cells that move over the inner surface of the alveoli and engulf dust, microbes, and other debris that reach that far. They are needed because the alveoli are beyond the reach of the mucus and cilia that clean the conducting airways. Their nickname, dust cells, comes from this cleanup job.","notes":["Goblet cells secrete mucus in the larger airways, where mucus and cilia trap and sweep away particles. Choose them if the question asked which cells help clean the conducting zone rather than the alveoli.","Type I alveolar cells are the flat cells forming most of the alveolar wall, built for gas exchange rather than cleanup. Pick them if the question asked which cells form the surface gases cross.","Correct. Alveolar macrophages are the dust cells of the alveoli.","Type II alveolar cells are cuboidal cells that secrete surfactant. They would be the answer if the question asked which cells keep the alveoli from collapsing."],"lt":"t-imagefree-structure-recognition","lid":"W3-889","comp":102,"competencyId":"resp-histo"},{"id":"L04079","dok":2,"q":"Which disorder is inflammation and narrowing of the bronchioles, causing wheezing and breathlessness?","a":"Asthma","options":["Pneumothorax","Emphysema","Asthma","Pneumonia"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Asthma is a disorder of the small airways. The bronchioles have smooth muscle and no cartilage, so when they are inflamed and their smooth muscle tightens, they narrow and air has trouble moving through. The narrowed airways produce the wheezing and breathlessness of an asthma attack.","notes":["A pneumothorax is air in the pleural cavity that lets a lung collapse. Choose it if the question described a chest wound or a collapsed lung.","Emphysema destroys the alveolar walls rather than narrowing the bronchioles. Pick it if the question asked about the disorder that reduces the surface for gas exchange.","Correct. Asthma is inflammation and narrowing of the bronchioles.","Pneumonia is an infection that fills the alveoli with fluid. It would be the answer if the question described inflamed, fluid-filled air sacs."],"lt":"t-imagefree-structure-recognition","lid":"W3-890","comp":104,"competencyId":"resp-disorders"},{"id":"L04080","dok":2,"q":"Which disorder destroys the alveolar walls, merging many small air sacs into fewer large ones and reducing the surface for gas exchange?","a":"Emphysema","options":["Emphysema","Pneumonia","Asthma","Lung cancer"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"In emphysema the walls between neighboring alveoli break down, so many small sacs merge into a few large ones. Many small sacs have far more total surface than a few large ones, so the area available for gas exchange shrinks. The change is structural and permanent, and emphysema is one of the conditions grouped as COPD.","notes":["Correct. Emphysema destroys alveolar walls and shrinks the exchange surface.","Pneumonia inflames the alveoli and fills them with fluid from infection, without permanently destroying their walls. It would be the answer if the question described infected, fluid-filled alveoli.","Asthma narrows the bronchioles through inflammation and smooth muscle tightening, leaving the alveolar walls intact. Pick it if the question asked about wheezing from narrowed small airways.","Lung cancer is a malignant tumor of lung tissue. Choose it if the question asked about a tumor strongly linked to smoking."],"lt":"t-imagefree-structure-recognition","lid":"W3-891","comp":104,"competencyId":"resp-disorders"},{"id":"L04081","dok":2,"q":"Which disorder is an infection that inflames the alveoli and fills them with fluid?","a":"Pneumonia","options":["Asthma","Pneumonia","Emphysema","Pneumothorax"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Pneumonia is an infection of the lung in which the alveoli become inflamed and fill with fluid. Fluid in the air sacs blocks air from reaching the thin alveolar wall, so less gas can cross into the blood. The structure affected is the alveolus, the same site where gas exchange normally happens.","notes":["Asthma is inflammation and narrowing of the bronchioles, not an infection of the alveoli. Pick it if the question described wheezing from tightened small airways.","Correct. Pneumonia is an infection that fills the alveoli with fluid.","Emphysema destroys alveolar walls, enlarging the air sacs rather than filling them with fluid. Choose it if the question asked about the permanent loss of exchange surface.","A pneumothorax is air in the pleural cavity, outside the lung, that lets the lung collapse. It would be the answer if the question described a punctured chest wall."],"lt":"t-imagefree-structure-recognition","lid":"W3-892","comp":104,"competencyId":"resp-disorders"},{"id":"L04082","dok":2,"q":"Which disorder is air in the pleural cavity, which breaks the seal and lets the lung collapse?","a":"Pneumothorax","options":["Pneumonia","Emphysema","Pneumothorax","Pulmonary embolism"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Normally the pleural cavity is a sealed space holding only a thin film of fluid, which keeps the lung pressed against the chest wall. If the chest wall or the lung is punctured, air enters this space and the seal is lost. The lung then pulls away from the wall and collapses, which is a pneumothorax (pneumo means air, thorax means chest).","notes":["Pneumonia is an infection that fills the alveoli with fluid, inside the lung rather than in the pleural cavity. Pick it if the question described infected, fluid-filled air sacs.","Emphysema is destruction of the alveolar walls, which happens inside the lung tissue. Choose it if the question asked about the permanent loss of exchange surface.","Correct. Air in the pleural cavity is a pneumothorax.","A pulmonary embolism is a clot lodged in an artery of the lung, a vessel problem rather than air in the pleural cavity. It would be the answer if the question described a leg clot that traveled to the lungs."],"lt":"t-imagefree-structure-recognition","lid":"W3-893","comp":104,"competencyId":"resp-disorders"},{"id":"L04083","dok":2,"q":"Which disorder is a malignant tumor of the lung tissue, strongly linked to smoking?","a":"Lung cancer","options":["Pneumonia","Emphysema","Lung cancer","COPD"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Lung cancer is a malignant tumor, meaning a growth of abnormal cells that can invade nearby tissue and spread. It arises in the lung tissue and is strongly linked to smoking. Smoking is also linked to COPD and emphysema, so the word tumor is what points to lung cancer here.","notes":["Pneumonia is an infection that fills the alveoli with fluid. Choose it if the question described inflamed, infected air sacs.","Emphysema is the destruction of alveolar walls, which is also linked to smoking but involves no tumor. It would be the answer if the question described enlarged air sacs and lost exchange surface.","Correct. Lung cancer is a malignant tumor of the lung.","COPD is a long-term blockage of airflow that includes emphysema and chronic bronchitis, not a tumor. Pick it if the question asked for the umbrella term for chronic obstructive airway disease."],"lt":"t-imagefree-structure-recognition","lid":"W3-894","comp":104,"competencyId":"resp-disorders"},{"id":"L04086","dok":2,"q":"Oxygen moving from the air in an alveolus into the blood must cross which thin barrier?","a":"The respiratory membrane","options":["Visceral pleura","Respiratory membrane","Respiratory mucosa","Bronchiole wall"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The respiratory membrane is the very thin barrier between alveolar air and the blood in the pulmonary capillaries. It has three parts: the alveolar epithelium (mostly flat type I cells), the fused basement membranes of the alveolus and the capillary, and the capillary endothelium. Its thinness is what makes gas exchange possible.","notes":["The visceral pleura is the serous membrane covering the outside of the lung, not the barrier inside each alveolus. Pick it if the question asked which membrane covers the lung surface.","Correct. Oxygen crosses the respiratory membrane to reach the blood.","The respiratory mucosa is the ciliated, mucus-producing lining of the nasal cavity and conducting airways, which filters air rather than exchanging gases. It would be the answer if the question asked which lining traps particles and sweeps them toward the pharynx.","The bronchiole wall belongs to the conducting zone, so air passes along it without gases crossing into the blood. Choose it if the question asked which airway wall has smooth muscle and no cartilage."],"lt":"t-imagefree-mixed-recognition","lid":"W3-897","comp":102,"competencyId":"resp-histo"},{"id":"L04089","dok":2,"q":"Which sequence correctly traces air from the trachea to the sites of gas exchange?","a":"Trachea, main bronchi, lobar bronchi, segmental bronchi, bronchioles, terminal bronchioles, respiratory bronchioles, alveolar ducts, alveoli","options":["Trachea, main bronchi, lobar bronchi, segmental bronchi, bronchioles, respiratory bronchioles, terminal bronchioles, alveolar ducts, alveoli","Trachea, lobar bronchi, main bronchi, segmental bronchi, bronchioles, terminal bronchioles, respiratory bronchioles, alveolar ducts, alveoli","Trachea, main bronchi, segmental bronchi, lobar bronchi, bronchioles, terminal bronchioles, respiratory bronchioles, alveolar ducts, alveoli","Trachea, main bronchi, lobar bronchi, segmental bronchi, bronchioles, terminal bronchioles, respiratory bronchioles, alveolar ducts, alveoli"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The bronchial tree branches from large to small. The trachea splits into main bronchi (one per lung), then lobar bronchi (one per lobe), then segmental bronchi (one per bronchopulmonary segment), then bronchioles that lose their cartilage. The terminal bronchioles end the conducting zone, and the respiratory bronchioles, alveolar ducts, and alveoli form the respiratory zone where gases are exchanged.","notes":["This sequence swaps the terminal and respiratory bronchioles. Terminal bronchioles are the last conducting airways, so they come before the respiratory bronchioles, where the first alveoli appear.","This sequence puts the lobar bronchi before the main bronchi. The trachea first splits into the two main bronchi, and only then does each main bronchus divide into lobar bronchi.","This sequence swaps the lobar and segmental bronchi. Lobar bronchi supply whole lobes and must come first, before the segmental bronchi divide each lobe into segments.","Correct. Air passes through each level of the bronchial tree in this order, from largest to smallest."],"lt":"t-imagefree-mixed-recognition","lid":"W3-900","comp":97,"competencyId":"resp-zones"},{"id":"L04111","dok":3,"q":"A child inhales a small bead, and it lodges in the right lung. Which feature of the airway best explains why?","a":"The right bronchus is wider and more vertical.","options":["The left lung has a cardiac notch","The right bronchus is wider and more vertical","The right main bronchus is longer and narrower","The right lung has three lobes"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The trachea forks at the carina into the right and left main bronchi. The right main bronchus is wider, shorter, and more vertical, almost in line with the trachea, while the left is narrower and angles off more sharply. An inhaled object falling down the trachea follows the straighter, wider path, so it most often lands in the right lung.","notes":["The cardiac notch is the indentation in the left lung that makes room for the heart. It answers a question about why the left lung is smaller, but it does not steer objects into the right bronchus.","Correct. The wider, more vertical right main bronchus is the easier path for an inhaled object.","This reverses the real anatomy. The left main bronchus is the longer, narrower, more horizontal one, which is why objects are less likely to enter it.","The right lung does have three lobes (superior, middle, and inferior), but lobes are divisions of lung tissue beyond the main bronchus. Pick this for a question about how many lobes or fissures the right lung has, not about where an object enters."],"lt":"t-clinical-application-and-misconceptions","lid":"W3-925","comp":100,"competencyId":"resp-tree"},{"id":"L04112","dok":2,"q":"A patient's bronchioles become inflamed and narrowed, causing wheezing and breathlessness. Which disorder fits?","a":"Asthma.","options":["Emphysema","Asthma","Pneumonia","Pneumothorax"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Asthma is inflammation and narrowing of the bronchioles, the small conducting airways. Bronchioles have smooth muscle in their walls and no cartilage, so they can constrict. When they narrow, air has to squeeze through, which produces the whistling sound of wheezing and makes breathing hard.","notes":["Emphysema is permanent destruction of the alveolar walls, which shrinks the surface for gas exchange. Choose it when the damage is in the alveoli rather than the airways leading to them.","Correct. Asthma is inflammation and narrowing of the bronchioles.","Pneumonia is an infection that inflames the alveoli and fills them with fluid. Pick it for fluid-filled air sacs, not tightened bronchioles.","Pneumothorax is air in the pleural cavity that breaks the seal and lets the lung collapse. It would be the answer for a collapsed lung after a chest wall puncture, not narrowed airways."],"lt":"t-clinical-application-and-misconceptions","lid":"W3-926","comp":104,"competencyId":"resp-disorders"},{"id":"L04113","dok":2,"q":"In a long-term smoker, many alveolar walls are destroyed and small air sacs merge into a few large ones. Which disorder fits?","a":"Emphysema.","options":["Asthma","Pneumonia","Pneumothorax","Emphysema"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Emphysema is destruction of the alveolar walls. As the thin walls between neighboring alveoli break down, many small sacs merge into fewer large ones, which greatly reduces the surface area available for gas exchange. The damage is structural and permanent, and emphysema is one of the conditions grouped under COPD (chronic obstructive pulmonary disease).","notes":["Asthma narrows the bronchioles through inflammation, and the airways can open again with treatment. Choose it for wheezing from narrowed small airways, not for destroyed alveoli.","Pneumonia is an infection that fills alveoli with fluid. The alveoli are inflamed but not merged into larger sacs, so pick it for an infection with fluid-filled air spaces.","Pneumothorax is air in the pleural cavity that lets the lung collapse. It affects the space around the lung, so choose it for a punctured chest wall rather than damaged alveolar walls.","Correct. Emphysema destroys alveolar walls and merges small sacs into larger ones."],"lt":"t-clinical-application-and-misconceptions","lid":"W3-927","comp":104,"competencyId":"resp-disorders"},{"id":"L04114","dok":2,"q":"An infection inflames a patient's alveoli and fills them with fluid. Which disorder fits?","a":"Pneumonia.","options":["Emphysema","Lung cancer","Pneumonia","Pneumothorax"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Pneumonia is an infection of the lung that inflames the alveoli and fills them with fluid. Alveoli are meant to hold air pressed against a very thin wall, so fluid inside them gets in the way of gas exchange. Unlike in emphysema, the air sacs are still structurally present, but they are flooded.","notes":["Emphysema is permanent destruction of alveolar walls, not an infection. Choose it when the alveoli have broken down and merged, usually after years of smoking.","Lung cancer is a malignant tumor of the lung tissue, strongly linked to smoking. It would be the answer for an abnormal growth, not for infected, fluid-filled air sacs.","Correct. Pneumonia is an infection that inflames the alveoli and fills them with fluid.","Pneumothorax is air, not fluid, in the pleural cavity around the lung, which makes the lung collapse. Pick it for a punctured chest wall or lung."],"lt":"t-clinical-application-and-misconceptions","lid":"W3-928","comp":104,"competencyId":"resp-disorders"},{"id":"L04115","dok":2,"q":"A stab wound lets air into the space between the parietal and visceral pleurae. Which disorder fits?","a":"Pneumothorax.","options":["Pneumothorax","Emphysema","Pneumonia","Cardiac tamponade"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The pleural cavity is the thin space between the parietal pleura on the chest wall and the visceral pleura on the lung, and it normally holds only a sealed film of fluid. When air enters this space, the seal is broken, the lung is no longer held out against the chest wall, and it collapses. Air in the pleural cavity is called a pneumothorax (pneumo means air, thorax means chest).","notes":["Correct. Air in the pleural cavity is a pneumothorax, and it lets the lung collapse.","Emphysema is destruction of the alveolar walls over many years. It is not caused by a wound and does not involve air entering the pleural cavity.","Pneumonia is infection inside the alveoli, which fill with fluid. It is inside the lung, so pick it when the problem is in the air sacs rather than the space around the lung.","Cardiac tamponade is fluid building up in the pericardial cavity around the heart. It is the matching problem for the heart's serous sac, so choose it when the heart rather than the lung is being compressed."],"lt":"t-clinical-application-and-misconceptions","lid":"W3-929","comp":104,"competencyId":"resp-disorders"},{"id":"L04116","dok":2,"q":"A premature infant's lungs do not yet make enough surfactant. What problem with the alveoli becomes likely?","a":"The alveoli tend to collapse.","options":["The alveoli fill with infected fluid","The alveolar walls are destroyed","Air leaks into the pleural cavity","The alveoli tend to collapse"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Surfactant is secreted by type II alveolar cells and lowers the surface tension of the thin fluid film lining each alveolus. Without enough of it, that surface tension pulls the tiny alveolar walls inward and the air sacs tend to collapse. Premature infants, whose lungs have not yet made enough surfactant, can have serious trouble keeping their alveoli open for this reason.","notes":["Infected fluid in the alveoli describes pneumonia. Pick it for an infection, not for missing surfactant.","Destruction of alveolar walls describes emphysema, usually from years of smoking. It is permanent damage to the walls, not a failure to keep them open.","Air in the pleural cavity is a pneumothorax, which collapses the whole lung from outside. Surfactant works inside each alveolus, so its absence does not let air into the pleural space.","Correct. Without enough surfactant, the alveoli tend to collapse."],"lt":"t-clinical-application-and-misconceptions","lid":"W3-930","comp":104,"competencyId":"resp-disorders"},{"id":"L04117","dok":2,"q":"Type I alveolar cells are damaged. Which alveolar job is most directly impaired?","a":"Gas diffusion across the wall.","options":["Warming and moistening incoming air","Secretion of surfactant","Gas diffusion across the wall","Engulfing inhaled debris"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Type I alveolar cells are thin, flat simple squamous cells that form most of the alveolar wall. Their thinness keeps the distance between air and blood very short, and they are part of the respiratory membrane that gases cross. When they are damaged, that barrier is disrupted and gases cannot move across the wall as easily.","notes":["Warming and moistening air happens in the conducting zone, especially the nasal cavity with its conchae and moist mucosa. It is not an alveolar job, so pick it for a question about the upper airway.","Secreting surfactant is the job of type II alveolar cells, the cuboidal cells scattered among the type I cells. Pick it for a question about type II cell damage.","Correct. Type I cells form the thin wall that gases cross.","Engulfing debris is the job of alveolar macrophages, the dust cells that patrol the alveolar surface. Choose it when those immune cells fail."],"lt":"t-clinical-application-and-misconceptions","lid":"W3-931","comp":102,"competencyId":"resp-histo"},{"id":"L04118","dok":2,"q":"Type II alveolar cells are damaged. What is most directly reduced?","a":"Surfactant secretion.","options":["Surfactant secretion","Pleural fluid production","Mucus secretion","Gas diffusion across the wall"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Type II alveolar cells are cuboidal cells scattered among the flat type I cells of the alveolar wall. Their job is to secrete surfactant, which lowers surface tension and keeps the alveoli from collapsing. Damage to them most directly reduces surfactant, which in turn makes alveolar collapse more likely.","notes":["Correct. Type II alveolar cells are the cells that secrete surfactant.","Pleural fluid is the thin film of serous fluid between the parietal and visceral pleurae, made by those serous membranes. Choose it for a question about what lets the lung glide against the chest wall.","Mucus is secreted by goblet cells in the pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium of the conducting airways, such as the nasal cavity and trachea. Pick it for a question about trapping particles in the upper airways.","Gas diffusion depends mainly on the thin type I cells that form most of the alveolar wall. It would be the answer if the question said type I cells were damaged."],"lt":"t-clinical-application-and-misconceptions","lid":"W3-932","comp":102,"competencyId":"resp-histo"},{"id":"L04119","dok":2,"q":"A patient's alveolar macrophages stop working. Which defense in the alveoli is weakened?","a":"Removal of inhaled debris and microbes.","options":["Removal of inhaled debris and microbes","Prevention of alveolar collapse","Sweeping of mucus toward the pharynx","Filtering of large particles by nasal hairs"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Alveolar macrophages, often called dust cells, are immune cells that patrol the alveolar surface and engulf debris and microbes. The alveoli lie beyond the mucus and cilia of the conducting airways, so whatever reaches them must be cleared by these cells. If the macrophages fail, inhaled particles and germs that make it this deep are not removed.","notes":["Correct. Alveolar macrophages engulf debris and microbes that reach the alveoli.","Preventing alveolar collapse is the job of surfactant from type II alveolar cells. It would be the answer if type II cells failed, not macrophages.","Sweeping mucus toward the pharynx is done by the cilia of the respiratory mucosa lining the conducting airways. Pick it for damage to the ciliated lining of the nasal cavity, trachea, or bronchi, not the alveoli.","Coarse hairs in the nasal vestibule, just inside the nostrils, filter the largest particles. Choose it for a question about the first filter at the entrance of the nose."],"lt":"t-clinical-application-and-misconceptions","lid":"W3-933","comp":102,"competencyId":"resp-histo"},{"id":"L04120","dok":2,"q":"A lesion is found along the edge of the cardiac notch. Which lobe of which lung contains it?","a":"Left superior lobe.","options":["Right middle lobe","Right superior lobe","Left superior lobe","Left inferior lobe"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The cardiac notch is the indentation in the anterior border of the left lung that makes room for the heart. Only the left lung has one, and it is cut into the superior lobe, with the tongue-shaped lingula hanging just below it. A lesion along the edge of the notch is therefore in the left superior lobe.","notes":["The right middle lobe sits against the right side of the heart and has no notch, because the heart lies mostly to the left. Pick it for a question about the lobe that only the right lung has, between the horizontal and oblique fissures.","The right superior lobe lies above the horizontal fissure of the right lung, and the right lung has no cardiac notch. Choose it for a lesion near the apex of the right lung.","Correct. The cardiac notch is cut into the anterior border of the left superior lobe.","The left inferior lobe lies below the oblique fissure and rests on the diaphragm. It would be the answer for a lesion at the base of the left lung, not along the cardiac notch."],"lt":"t-clinical-application-and-misconceptions","lid":"W3-934","comp":101,"competencyId":"resp-lungs-pleura"},{"id":"L04121","dok":2,"q":"A lesion is found in the middle lobe of a lung. Which lung must it be, and why?","a":"The right lung, because only it has three lobes.","options":["The left lung, because its middle lobe sits beside the heart","The right lung, because it has the cardiac notch","Either lung, because both have three lobes","The right lung, because only it has three lobes"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The right lung has three lobes, superior, middle, and inferior, separated by a horizontal fissure and an oblique fissure. The left lung has only two lobes, superior and inferior, separated by a single oblique fissure, because it gives up room for the heart. A middle lobe therefore exists only in the right lung.","notes":["The left lung has only a superior and an inferior lobe. The tongue-shaped lingula of its superior lobe sits roughly where a middle lobe would be, which is why students sometimes mistake it for one.","The right lung does contain the middle lobe, but the cardiac notch is on the left lung. Pick the cardiac notch for a question about which lung makes room for the heart.","Only the right lung has three lobes; the left has two. This choice comes from assuming the lungs are mirror images, when the left lung is smaller to make room for the heart.","Correct. Only the right lung has a middle lobe."],"lt":"t-clinical-application-and-misconceptions","lid":"W3-935","comp":101,"competencyId":"resp-lungs-pleura"},{"id":"L04122","dok":1,"q":"An airway is held open by C-shaped rings of hyaline cartilage, with smooth muscle closing the gap at the back. Which airway is it?","a":"Trachea.","options":["Bronchiole","Alveolar duct","Larynx","Trachea"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The trachea, or windpipe, is held permanently open by C-shaped rings of hyaline cartilage. The open part of each C faces posteriorly, where the trachealis muscle closes the gap against the esophagus. The rings keep the airway from collapsing, while the soft back wall lets the esophagus expand when food passes.","notes":["Bronchioles are small airways with smooth muscle walls and no cartilage at all. Choose them when the question describes an airway that can constrict because cartilage is absent.","Alveolar ducts are passages in the respiratory zone whose walls are lined entirely with alveoli. They have no cartilage rings and would be the answer for an airway made almost entirely of air sacs.","The larynx is built of nine separate cartilages, including the shield-shaped thyroid cartilage and the complete ring of the cricoid cartilage. Pick it for an airway with vocal folds rather than a stack of repeating C-shaped rings.","Correct. C-shaped cartilage rings closed posteriorly by the trachealis muscle mark the trachea."],"lt":"t-clinical-application-and-misconceptions","lid":"W3-936","comp":100,"competencyId":"resp-tree"},{"id":"L04123","dok":1,"q":"A small airway has a wall of smooth muscle with no cartilage, and no alveoli bud from it. Which airway is it?","a":"Bronchiole.","options":["Bronchiole","Respiratory bronchiole","Segmental bronchus","Trachea"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Moving down the bronchial tree, cartilage decreases and smooth muscle increases. The bronchioles are where cartilage disappears entirely, and smooth muscle makes up much of their wall. Because no alveoli bud from its wall, this airway is still a conducting passage rather than part of the respiratory zone.","notes":["Correct. Bronchioles have smooth muscle walls with no cartilage and no alveoli.","Respiratory bronchioles are the first airways of the respiratory zone, with a few alveoli budding from their walls. They would be the answer if the question mentioned alveoli on the wall.","Segmental bronchi, the tertiary bronchi, still have plates of cartilage in their walls. Pick them for the airway that supplies one bronchopulmonary segment.","The trachea is the large airway held open by C-shaped rings of hyaline cartilage. Choose it for an airway with cartilage rings, the opposite of this description."],"lt":"t-clinical-application-and-misconceptions","lid":"W3-937","comp":100,"competencyId":"resp-tree"},{"id":"L04124","dok":1,"q":"An airway has a few alveoli budding from its wall, but its wall is not yet lined entirely with alveoli. Which airway is it?","a":"Respiratory bronchiole.","options":["Alveolar sac","Terminal bronchiole","Respiratory bronchiole","Alveolar duct"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Respiratory bronchioles are the first airways of the respiratory zone, the part of the lungs where gas exchange happens. They branch from the terminal bronchioles and have scattered alveoli budding from their walls. Farther along, the alveolar ducts are lined entirely with alveoli, so a few scattered alveoli mark the respiratory bronchiole.","notes":["An alveolar sac is a cluster of alveoli that share a common space at the end of an alveolar duct. It would be the answer for a grapelike cluster at the very end of the airway.","Terminal bronchioles are the last airways of the conducting zone and have no alveoli on their walls. Pick them for the final airway before gas exchange begins.","Correct. A few scattered alveoli on the wall mark a respiratory bronchiole.","Alveolar ducts are passages whose walls are lined entirely with alveoli. Choose them when the wall is made of air sacs all the way around, not just a few scattered ones."],"lt":"t-clinical-application-and-misconceptions","lid":"W3-938","comp":102,"competencyId":"resp-histo"},{"id":"L04128","dok":2,"q":"A student says gas exchange begins in the terminal bronchioles. Where does it actually begin?","a":"In the respiratory bronchioles.","options":["In the respiratory bronchioles","In the terminal bronchioles","In the segmental bronchi","Only in the alveolar sacs"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The airways are divided into a conducting zone, which only carries air, and a respiratory zone, where gas exchange happens. The terminal bronchioles are the last airways of the conducting zone. Gas exchange begins in the next airways, the respiratory bronchioles, because they are the first to have alveoli budding from their walls.","notes":["Correct. The respiratory bronchioles are the first airways with alveoli, so exchange begins there.","This is the student's mistake. Terminal bronchioles are the last conducting airways and have no alveoli, so choose them for a question about where the conducting zone ends.","Segmental bronchi are well up in the conducting zone and are still supported by cartilage. Pick them for a question about the airway that supplies one bronchopulmonary segment.","Alveolar sacs are a major site of exchange, but they are not the first. Exchange starts wherever alveoli first appear, beginning with the respiratory bronchioles and continuing through the alveolar ducts."],"lt":"t-clinical-application-and-misconceptions","lid":"W3-942","comp":97,"competencyId":"resp-zones"},{"id":"L04130","dok":2,"q":"A student says the pleural cavity normally contains air. What does it actually contain?","a":"A thin film of serous fluid.","options":["Mucus from goblet cells","A thin film of serous fluid","Nothing at all, like a vacuum","Air that has passed through the alveoli"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The pleural cavity is the thin space between the parietal pleura, which lines the chest wall, and the visceral pleura, which covers the lung. It is sealed and normally holds only a thin film of serous fluid, which lets the lung glide against the chest wall as it expands. Air belongs inside the airways and alveoli, and air in the pleural cavity is a disorder called a pneumothorax.","notes":["Mucus is made by goblet cells in the lining of the conducting airways to trap particles. Choose it for a question about the respiratory mucosa of the nasal cavity or trachea, not a serous space.","Correct. The pleural cavity normally holds only a thin film of serous fluid.","The pleural cavity is a very narrow space, but it is not empty. Its thin fluid film is what lets the two pleural layers slide smoothly against each other.","Air belongs inside the lung, in the airways and alveoli. If air gets into the pleural cavity, the seal is lost and the lung collapses, which is a pneumothorax."],"lt":"t-clinical-application-and-misconceptions","lid":"W3-944","comp":101,"competencyId":"resp-lungs-pleura"},{"id":"L04135","dok":2,"q":"Why does emphysema reduce how much oxygen reaches the blood?","a":"Lost alveolar walls leave less exchange surface.","options":["Lost alveolar walls leave less exchange surface","Narrowed bronchioles block air from reaching the alveoli","Air in the pleural cavity collapses the lung","Fluid fills the alveoli and blocks diffusion"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Emphysema destroys the thin walls between alveoli, so many small air sacs merge into a few large ones. Gas exchange happens across the alveolar walls, and many small sacs provide far more wall surface than a few large ones. Losing that surface leaves less area for oxygen to cross into the blood, and the loss is permanent.","notes":["Correct. Emphysema shrinks the exchange surface by destroying alveolar walls.","Narrowed bronchioles describe asthma, a problem of the conducting airways. Pick it for wheezing from tightened small airways, not destroyed alveoli.","Air in the pleural cavity is a pneumothorax, which collapses the lung from outside. It would be the answer for a punctured chest wall, not damage to the alveolar walls.","Fluid-filled alveoli describe pneumonia, an infection. Choose it when the air sacs are flooded rather than destroyed."],"lt":"t-clinical-application-and-misconceptions","lid":"W3-950","comp":104,"competencyId":"resp-disorders"},{"id":"L04136","dok":2,"q":"Why does pneumonia reduce how much oxygen reaches the blood?","a":"Fluid and inflammation fill the alveoli.","options":["The alveolar walls are permanently destroyed","The bronchioles constrict and narrow","Fluid and inflammation fill the alveoli","The pleural seal breaks and the lung collapses"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Pneumonia is an infection that inflames the alveoli and fills them with fluid. Normally the alveoli hold air right against a very thin wall wrapped in capillaries, so oxygen has only a short distance to travel. Fluid and inflammation keep air from reaching much of that surface, so less oxygen crosses into the blood.","notes":["Permanent destruction of alveolar walls describes emphysema. Choose it when the exchange surface itself has been lost, usually after years of smoking.","Constricted bronchioles describe asthma, which narrows the conducting airways. Pick it for wheezing and difficulty moving air, not fluid-filled alveoli.","Correct. Fluid in inflamed alveoli keeps air away from the exchange surface.","A broken pleural seal is a pneumothorax, which collapses the lung from outside. It would be the answer for air entering the pleural cavity."],"lt":"t-clinical-application-and-misconceptions","lid":"W3-951","comp":104,"competencyId":"resp-disorders"},{"id":"L04137","dok":3,"q":"Why does asthma mainly affect airflow rather than the gas exchange surface itself?","a":"It narrows bronchioles, which only conduct air.","options":["It narrows bronchioles, which only conduct air","It collapses the lung from the outside","It fills the alveoli with fluid","It destroys the alveoli, which only conduct air"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Asthma is inflammation and narrowing of the bronchioles. Bronchioles belong to the conducting zone, the airways that carry air to the alveoli but do not exchange gases themselves. Narrowing them makes it hard to move air in and out, while the alveoli, the actual exchange surface, are left structurally intact.","notes":["Correct. Asthma targets conducting airways, so it limits airflow while the alveoli stay intact.","Collapsing the lung from outside describes a pneumothorax, air in the pleural cavity. Choose it when the pleural seal is broken, not when the airways narrow.","Fluid in the alveoli describes pneumonia, which affects the exchange surface directly. Pick it for an infection that floods the air sacs.","This mixes up two things. Destroying alveoli describes emphysema, and alveoli are the exchange surface of the respiratory zone, not conducting airways."],"lt":"t-clinical-application-and-misconceptions","lid":"W3-952","comp":104,"competencyId":"resp-disorders"},{"id":"L04138","dok":2,"q":"Why does air entering the pleural cavity make the lung collapse?","a":"It breaks the pleural seal that holds the lung out.","options":["It fills the alveoli and pushes them apart","It breaks the pleural seal that holds the lung out","It narrows the bronchioles so air cannot enter","It destroys the type II cells that make surfactant"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The pleural cavity is a sealed space between the parietal pleura on the chest wall and the visceral pleura on the lung, and it holds only a thin film of fluid. That sealed film keeps the lung surface held against the chest wall, so the lung expands when the chest expands. When air gets in, the seal is broken, the lung is no longer held out, and it collapses. This is a pneumothorax.","notes":["Air in the pleural cavity is outside the lung, not inside the alveoli. Air belongs in the alveoli normally, so this choice mixes up the pleural cavity with the air spaces.","Correct. Air breaks the pleural seal, so the lung is no longer held out and collapses.","Narrowed bronchioles describe asthma, which limits airflow but leaves the pleural seal intact. Choose it for wheezing, not a collapsed lung.","Loss of type II cells reduces surfactant and lets individual alveoli collapse. Pick it for a question about premature infants or surfactant, not for air in the pleural space."],"lt":"t-clinical-application-and-misconceptions","lid":"W3-953","comp":104,"competencyId":"resp-disorders"},{"id":"L04170","dok":2,"q":"Why does the respiratory membrane need to be extremely thin?","a":"To keep the path for diffusing gases short.","options":["To keep the path for diffusing gases short","To warm and moisten the incoming air","To trap dust before it reaches the blood","To keep the alveoli from collapsing"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The respiratory membrane is the barrier between the air in an alveolus and the blood in a pulmonary capillary. It is made of the alveolar epithelium (mostly thin type I cells), fused basement membranes, and the capillary endothelium. Gases cross it by diffusion, which is fast only over very short distances, so the thinner the membrane, the more easily oxygen and carbon dioxide can pass.","notes":["Correct. A thin membrane keeps the path for diffusing gases very short.","Warming and moistening air happens in the conducting zone, especially the nasal cavity with its conchae and moist mucosa. It is the answer for a question about conditioning air in the nose.","Trapping dust is the job of mucus in the conducting airways and of alveolar macrophages in the alveoli. A thin membrane does nothing to trap particles, so choose those defenses for a question about cleaning the air.","Keeping alveoli from collapsing is the job of surfactant from type II alveolar cells. Pick surfactant for a question about what keeps the air sacs open."],"lt":"t-clinical-application-and-misconceptions","lid":"W3-988","comp":102,"competencyId":"resp-histo"},{"id":"L04171","dok":2,"q":"What is the job of the thin film of fluid in the pleural cavity?","a":"It lets the lungs glide smoothly as they expand.","options":["It cushions the heart as it beats","It lets the lungs glide smoothly as they expand","It traps inhaled particles before they reach the alveoli","It carries oxygen from the alveoli to the blood"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The pleural cavity is the thin space between the parietal pleura, which lines the chest wall, and the visceral pleura, which covers the lung. Its film of serous fluid reduces friction, so the two layers slide smoothly past each other each time the lungs expand and recoil. The sealed film also keeps the lung surface held against the chest wall, which is why air entering this space lets the lung collapse.","notes":["The fluid that lets the heart beat with little friction is in the pericardial cavity, the matching serous space around the heart. Pick the pericardial cavity for a question about the heart.","Correct. The pleural fluid reduces friction so the lung glides against the chest wall.","Trapping inhaled particles is done by mucus and cilia in the conducting airways. Choose the respiratory mucosa for a question about filtering air.","Oxygen moves from the alveoli into the blood across the respiratory membrane, not through the pleural fluid. Pick the respiratory membrane for a question about where gases cross."],"lt":"t-clinical-application-and-misconceptions","lid":"W3-989","comp":101,"competencyId":"resp-lungs-pleura"},{"id":"L04176","dok":2,"q":"Why are the bronchioles the airways that regulate airflow?","a":"Their smooth muscle can change their diameter.","options":["Their alveoli can expand to pull in air","Their smooth muscle can change their diameter","Their cilia can push air toward the lungs","Their C-shaped cartilage rings can open and close"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"As the bronchial tree branches, cartilage decreases and smooth muscle increases, and at the bronchioles the cartilage disappears entirely. With no cartilage and smooth muscle making up much of the wall, bronchioles can constrict to narrow the airway or relax to widen it. Cartilage keeps the larger bronchi open, so they cannot narrow nearly as much, which is why the narrowing in asthma is worst in the bronchioles.","notes":["Alveoli are the air sacs of the respiratory zone, where gas exchange happens. Bronchioles of the conducting zone have no alveoli, so choose alveoli for a question about the exchange surface.","Correct. Smooth muscle without cartilage lets bronchioles narrow or widen.","Cilia sweep mucus and trapped particles toward the pharynx; they do not move air or change airway size. Pick them for a question about clearing debris from the conducting airways.","C-shaped cartilage rings are found in the trachea, and their job is to hold the airway permanently open, not to adjust it. Pick them for a question about what keeps the trachea from collapsing."],"lt":"t-clinical-application-and-misconceptions","lid":"W3-994","comp":100,"competencyId":"resp-tree"},{"id":"L04177","dok":2,"q":"Why don't the terminal bronchioles exchange gases with the blood?","a":"They are conducting airways with no alveoli.","options":["They are conducting airways with no alveoli","They lie beyond the respiratory zone","They are lined only by alveolar macrophages","Their walls are reinforced with cartilage rings"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The terminal bronchioles are the last airways of the conducting zone, the passages that only carry air. Gas exchange needs alveoli, the thin-walled air sacs wrapped in capillaries, and the terminal bronchioles have none in their walls. Exchange begins in the next airways, the respiratory bronchioles, which have the first alveoli budding from them.","notes":["Correct. Without alveoli, the terminal bronchioles only carry air.","This reverses the order. Terminal bronchioles come just before the respiratory zone, which starts with the respiratory bronchioles and continues through the alveolar ducts and alveoli.","Alveolar macrophages are dust cells that patrol the alveoli, not a lining of the terminal bronchioles. Pick them for a question about clearing debris from the alveolar surface.","Cartilage has already disappeared by the bronchiole level, so terminal bronchioles have smooth muscle walls, not cartilage rings. C-shaped rings belong to the trachea."],"lt":"t-clinical-application-and-misconceptions","lid":"W3-995","comp":97,"competencyId":"resp-zones"},{"id":"L04178","dok":2,"q":"Why is each alveolus wrapped in a dense net of pulmonary capillaries?","a":"To bring blood close to alveolar air.","options":["To supply the bronchi with oxygen-rich blood","To produce surfactant for the alveolar wall","To hold the lung against the chest wall","To bring blood close to alveolar air"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Gas exchange happens by diffusion, which works only across very short distances. Wrapping each alveolus in pulmonary capillaries brings the blood extremely close to the air, separated only by the thin respiratory membrane. With hundreds of millions of alveoli, this arrangement gives the blood an enormous surface over which to pick up oxygen and release carbon dioxide.","notes":["The walls of the bronchi and other lung tissue get their own blood from the bronchial vessels, part of the root of the lung. Pick the bronchial vessels for a question about feeding the lung tissue itself.","Surfactant is secreted by type II alveolar cells in the alveolar wall, not by capillaries. Choose type II cells for a question about what keeps alveoli from collapsing.","The lung is held against the chest wall by the sealed pleural cavity and its thin film of fluid. Pick the pleurae for a question about how the lung follows the chest wall as it expands.","Correct. The capillary net puts blood right against the alveolar air."],"lt":"t-clinical-application-and-misconceptions","lid":"W3-996","comp":102,"competencyId":"resp-histo"},{"id":"L04179","dok":3,"q":"Respiratory disorders can narrow the airways, damage the alveoli, or affect the pleurae. Which disorder mainly affects the alveoli?","a":"Pneumonia.","options":["Asthma","Chronic bronchitis","Pneumonia","Pneumothorax"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Most respiratory disorders can be sorted by the structure they hit: the airways, the alveoli, or the pleurae. Pneumonia is an infection that inflames the alveoli and fills them with fluid, so it damages the exchange surface itself. Asthma and chronic bronchitis narrow or block the airways, and a pneumothorax affects the pleural cavity.","notes":["Asthma narrows the bronchioles, which are conducting airways, so it is a problem of blocked airflow. Pick it as the example of an airway disorder.","Chronic bronchitis is long-term inflammation of the bronchi, the larger conducting airways, and it is grouped with emphysema under COPD. It blocks airflow rather than mainly damaging the alveoli.","Correct. Pneumonia inflames and floods the alveoli, the exchange surface.","Pneumothorax is air in the pleural cavity, which collapses the lung from outside. It affects the space around the lung rather than the airways or the alveolar walls."],"lt":"t-clinical-application-and-misconceptions","lid":"W3-997","comp":104,"competencyId":"resp-disorders"},{"id":"L04234","dok":1,"q":"Which group lists only structures of the lower respiratory tract?","a":"The trachea, bronchi, and lungs (the larynx also belongs to the lower tract in this course).","options":["Nasopharynx, oropharynx, and laryngopharynx","Trachea, bronchi, and lungs","Nasal conchae, nasal meatuses, and choanae","Nasal cavity, paranasal sinuses, and pharynx"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The lower respiratory tract runs from the larynx down through the trachea and bronchi into the lungs. These structures carry air into the thorax and, deep in the lungs, bring it close to the blood for gas exchange. Everything above the larynx, from the nose through the pharynx, belongs to the upper tract.","notes":["These are the three regions of the pharynx, all part of the upper tract. Choose this group for a question about the pharynx from top to bottom.","Correct. The trachea, bronchi, and lungs are all part of the lower tract.","These are features of the nasal cavity in the upper tract: the conchae and meatuses on its lateral wall and the choanae opening into the nasopharynx. They would be the answer for a question about how the nasal cavity is shaped to condition air.","These are upper respiratory tract structures in the head and throat. Pick this group if the question asked for the upper tract."],"lt":"t-resp-zones","lid":"resp-zones-r2","comp":97,"competencyId":"resp-zones"},{"id":"L04240","dok":1,"q":"Which sequence follows the bronchial tree from the main bronchi to the end of the conducting zone?","a":"Main (primary) bronchi, lobar (secondary) bronchi, segmental (tertiary) bronchi, bronchioles, terminal bronchioles.","options":["Lobar bronchi, main bronchi, segmental bronchi, bronchioles, terminal bronchioles","Main bronchi, lobar bronchi, segmental bronchi, bronchioles, terminal bronchioles","Main bronchi, segmental bronchi, lobar bronchi, bronchioles, terminal bronchioles","Main bronchi, lobar bronchi, segmental bronchi, terminal bronchioles, bronchioles"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The trachea splits at the carina into two main (primary) bronchi, one to each lung. Each main bronchus splits into lobar (secondary) bronchi, one per lobe, and those split into segmental (tertiary) bronchi, one per bronchopulmonary segment. The segmental bronchi branch into bronchioles, which end as terminal bronchioles, the last airways of the conducting zone.","notes":["This puts the lobar bronchi first, but the main bronchi come straight off the trachea and only then divide into lobar bronchi. Students pick this when they forget that each lung gets a single main bronchus before any lobe is served.","Correct. The tree narrows from lung to lobe to segment, then into bronchioles.","This swaps lobar and segmental bronchi. Lobes are the larger units, so the lobar (secondary) bronchi come before the segmental (tertiary) bronchi that serve the smaller segments.","This puts terminal bronchioles before bronchioles. Terminal means last, so terminal bronchioles are the final branches of the conducting zone."],"lt":"t-resp-tree","lid":"resp-tree-r3","comp":100,"competencyId":"resp-tree"},{"id":"L04241","dok":2,"q":"How do the walls of bronchioles differ from the walls of bronchi?","a":"Bronchioles have smooth muscle walls and no cartilage; bronchi are supported by cartilage.","options":["Bronchioles have smooth muscle walls and no cartilage; bronchi are supported by cartilage","Bronchioles have C-shaped cartilage rings closed by the trachealis muscle","Bronchioles are lined by stratified squamous epithelium to resist friction","Bronchioles are supported by cartilage; bronchi have smooth muscle walls and no cartilage"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"As the bronchial tree branches, cartilage decreases and smooth muscle increases. Bronchi are still held open by cartilage, but bronchioles have lost their cartilage entirely and have walls of smooth muscle. That is why bronchioles can constrict, and why asthma, which narrows the bronchioles, is a disease of the small airways.","notes":["Correct. Bronchioles trade cartilage for smooth muscle.","C-shaped cartilage rings with the trachealis muscle closing the gap describe the trachea. Pick this description for a question about what holds the windpipe open.","Stratified squamous epithelium lines the oropharynx and laryngopharynx, where food rubs past. Choose it for a question about the lining of the parts of the pharynx shared with food, not the small airways.","This reverses the pattern. Cartilage is greatest in the large airways and disappears by the bronchiole, where smooth muscle takes over."],"lt":"t-resp-tree","lid":"resp-tree-r4","comp":100,"competencyId":"resp-tree"},{"id":"L04242","dok":3,"q":"A tumor blocks one segmental (tertiary) bronchus. Which part of the lung loses its air supply?","a":"One bronchopulmonary segment, since a segmental bronchus supplies one segment.","options":["One bronchopulmonary segment","One entire lung","One single alveolus","One entire lobe"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Each segmental (tertiary) bronchus supplies one bronchopulmonary segment, a distinct section of a lobe with its own airway. Blocking that bronchus cuts off air to that one segment while the neighboring segments keep working. This is why each segment is treated as a separate functional unit of the lung.","notes":["Correct. One segmental bronchus serves one bronchopulmonary segment.","A whole lung would lose air if a main (primary) bronchus were blocked, since each main bronchus enters one lung. Choose this for a blockage just past the carina.","A single alveolus sits at the very end of the airway tree, far beyond a segmental bronchus. A blocked segmental bronchus affects a huge number of alveoli at once, not just one.","A whole lobe would lose air if a lobar (secondary) bronchus were blocked, since each lobar bronchus supplies one lobe. Pick this if the tumor were in a lobar bronchus."],"lt":"t-resp-tree","lid":"resp-tree-r5","comp":100,"competencyId":"resp-tree"},{"id":"L04243","dok":1,"q":"How many lobes does each lung have?","a":"The right lung has three lobes; the left has two.","options":["Right lung three, left lung two","Both lungs have two","Both lungs have three","Right lung two, left lung three"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The right lung has three lobes (superior, middle, and inferior), separated by a horizontal fissure and an oblique fissure. The left lung has only two lobes (superior and inferior), separated by an oblique fissure. The left lung is smaller because it shares the left side of the thorax with the heart.","notes":["Correct. Three lobes on the right and two on the left.","Two lobes is right only for the left lung. The right lung also has a middle lobe, set off by its horizontal fissure.","Three lobes is right for the right lung, but the left lung has only two because it gives up room for the heart. Students choose this when they assume the lungs are mirror images.","This swaps the two lungs. The heart sits mostly to the left of the midline, so the left lung is the smaller one with fewer lobes."],"lt":"t-resp-lungs-pleura","lid":"resp-lungs-pleura-r1","comp":101,"competencyId":"resp-lungs-pleura"},{"id":"L04244","dok":1,"q":"What is the cardiac notch, and which lung has it?","a":"An indentation in the left lung that makes room for the heart.","options":["An indentation in the right lung that makes room for the heart","The medial indentation where the bronchus and vessels enter a lung","The tongue-shaped projection of the left superior lobe","An indentation in the left lung that makes room for the heart"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The cardiac notch is an indentation in the anterior border of the left lung that makes room for the heart. About two thirds of the heart's mass lies to the left of the midline, so the left lung is shaped around it. Just below the notch, the left superior lobe forms a small tongue-shaped projection called the lingula.","notes":["The heart lies mostly to the left of the midline, so it is the left lung, not the right, that is notched around it. Students choose this when they forget which way the heart tilts.","That describes the hilum, the indentation on the medial surface of each lung where the bronchus, pulmonary vessels, and nerves enter and leave. Pick it if the question asked where the root of the lung attaches.","That describes the lingula, which sits just below the cardiac notch on the left superior lobe. Choose it for a question about the tongue-shaped part of the left lung.","Correct. The cardiac notch is the space in the left lung where the heart fits."],"lt":"t-resp-lungs-pleura","lid":"resp-lungs-pleura-r2","comp":101,"competencyId":"resp-lungs-pleura"},{"id":"L04245","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares the parietal and visceral pleura?","a":"The parietal pleura lines the thoracic wall; the visceral pleura covers the outer surface of the lung.","options":["The parietal pleura lines the abdominal wall; the visceral pleura covers the abdominal organs","The visceral pleura lines the thoracic wall; the parietal pleura covers the lung","The parietal pleura lines the thoracic wall; the visceral pleura covers the lung","The parietal pleura surrounds the heart; the visceral pleura covers the lung"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The pleura is a serous membrane with two layers. The parietal pleura lines the inner thoracic wall (parietal refers to a body wall), and the visceral pleura clings to the outer surface of the lung (visceral refers to an organ). The thin, fluid-filled pleural cavity between them lets the lung glide against the chest wall as it expands.","notes":["Those are the parietal and visceral peritoneum, the serous membranes of the abdominopelvic cavity. They would be the answer for a question about the membranes lining the abdomen and covering its organs.","This swaps the two layers. Parietal always means the layer on the body wall and visceral always means the layer on the organ, in the pleura, pericardium, and peritoneum alike.","Correct. Parietal is on the wall and visceral is on the lung.","The membrane around the heart is the pericardium, not the pleura. Pick the pericardium for a question about the serous sac that encloses the heart."],"lt":"t-resp-lungs-pleura","lid":"resp-lungs-pleura-r4","comp":101,"competencyId":"resp-lungs-pleura"},{"id":"L04246","dok":3,"q":"Why does the left lung have only two lobes and a cardiac notch, while the right lung has three lobes?","a":"The heart sits mostly to the left of the midline, so the left lung gives up room for it.","options":["The heart sits mostly to the left of the midline, so the left lung gives up room for it","The aorta passes through the left lung and divides it into fewer lobes","The left main bronchus is wider, so it supplies fewer and larger lobes","The liver sits beneath the left lung and pushes it upward into a smaller space"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"About two thirds of the heart's mass lies to the left of the midline. The left lung shares that side of the thorax with the heart, so it is smaller, has only two lobes, and carries a cardiac notch along its anterior border where the heart sits. The right lung has more room, so it is larger and has three lobes.","notes":["Correct. The left lung is smaller because the heart takes up part of the left side of the thorax.","The aorta arches over the heart and descends in the mediastinum, between the lungs, not through either lung. The number of lobes reflects the room left by the heart, not a vessel passing through.","It is the right main bronchus that is wider and more vertical, which is why inhaled objects usually lodge in the right lung. Pick this bronchus fact for a question about where an inhaled object is most likely to go.","The liver lies on the right side, under the right dome of the diaphragm, not under the left lung. Students choose this when they misplace the liver; it would fit a question about which abdominal organ lies beneath the right lung."],"lt":"t-resp-lungs-pleura","lid":"resp-lungs-pleura-r5","comp":101,"competencyId":"resp-lungs-pleura"},{"id":"L04258","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares type I and type II alveolar cells?","a":"Type I cells are thin and flat and form most of the wall where gases cross; type II cells secrete surfactant that keeps the alveoli from collapsing.","options":["Type I cells are thin and form most of the wall; type II cells secrete surfactant","Type I cells secrete surfactant; type II cells are thin and form most of the wall","Type I cells secrete mucus; type II cells sweep it away with cilia","Type I cells engulf debris on the surface; type II cells form most of the wall"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Type I alveolar cells are thin, flat, simple squamous cells that form most of the alveolar wall, so gases have only a short distance to cross. Type II alveolar cells are cuboidal cells scattered among them that secrete surfactant, a fluid that lowers surface tension and keeps the alveoli from collapsing. A third cell, the alveolar macrophage, patrols the surface and engulfs debris.","notes":["Correct. Type I cells form the thin exchange wall, and type II cells make surfactant.","This swaps the two jobs. The thin type I cells are built for gas exchange, while the plumper cuboidal type II cells make and secrete surfactant.","Mucus secretion and ciliary sweeping belong to the goblet cells and ciliated cells of the conducting airways, such as the trachea. Choose this for a question about how the respiratory mucosa traps and clears particles.","Engulfing debris is the job of alveolar macrophages, also called dust cells, which are not type I cells. Pick macrophages for a question about which cells clean the alveolar surface."],"lt":"t-resp-histo","lid":"resp-histo-r2","comp":102,"competencyId":"resp-histo"},{"id":"L04259","dok":1,"q":"What is the apex of a lung?","a":"The narrow superior tip, rising just above the clavicle.","options":["The medial indentation where the bronchus and vessels enter","The tongue-shaped projection of the left superior lobe","The broad inferior surface, resting on the diaphragm","The narrow superior tip, rising just above the clavicle"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The apex of each lung is its narrow superior tip, which rises just above the clavicle into the root of the neck. The broad base sits at the opposite end, resting on the diaphragm. Knowing that the apex reaches above the clavicle explains why a deep wound at the base of the neck can reach the top of the lung.","notes":["That describes the hilum, on the medial surface of each lung, where the root of the lung enters. Choose it for a question about where the main bronchus and pulmonary vessels join the lung.","That describes the lingula of the left lung, which sits below the cardiac notch. It would be the answer for a question about the tongue-shaped part of the left superior lobe.","That describes the base of the lung, the end opposite the apex. Pick it if the question asked which surface rests on the diaphragm.","Correct. The apex is the top of the lung, reaching just above the clavicle."],"lt":"t-resp-thoracic-diaphragm","lid":"resp-thoracic-diaphragm-r1","comp":101,"competencyId":"resp-lungs-pleura"},{"id":"L04260","dok":1,"q":"The base of each lung is its broad inferior surface. What does it rest on?","a":"The diaphragm.","options":["The clavicle","The diaphragm","The heart","The first rib"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The base of each lung is its broad, concave inferior surface, and it rests on the dome of the diaphragm. Because the diaphragm separates the thoracic cavity from the abdominal cavity, the lung bases sit just above the abdominal organs. The apex, at the opposite end, rises just above the clavicle.","notes":["The clavicle is at the top of the thorax, and the apex of the lung rises just above it. Pick the clavicle if the question asked about the apex rather than the base.","Correct. Each lung base sits on the dome of the diaphragm.","The heart sits between the lungs in the mediastinum, pressing into their medial surfaces and making the cardiac notch in the left lung. Choose it for a question about what the medial surface of the left lung is shaped around.","The first rib curves around the top of the thoracic cavity, near the apex of each lung. It would be a reasonable landmark for a question about the apex, not the base."],"lt":"t-resp-thoracic-diaphragm","lid":"resp-thoracic-diaphragm-r2","comp":101,"competencyId":"resp-lungs-pleura"},{"id":"L04261","dok":2,"q":"What does the pleural cavity contain, and what does that allow?","a":"A thin film of fluid that lets the lung glide as it expands.","options":["A thin film of serous fluid that lets the lung glide","Loose fat that cushions the lung against the ribs","The main bronchus and pulmonary vessels entering the lung","Air that holds the lung open between breaths"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The pleural cavity is the thin, sealed space between the parietal pleura on the thoracic wall and the visceral pleura on the lung. It holds only a thin film of serous fluid, which lets the lung slide smoothly against the chest wall as it expands and recoils with each breath. If air gets into this space, the seal is lost and the lung can collapse, a condition called pneumothorax.","notes":["Correct. The fluid film lets the two pleural layers slide over each other.","The pleural cavity holds no fat or cushioning tissue, only a thin fluid film. Students choose this when they picture the space as padding rather than a lubricated gap between two membranes.","The main bronchus and pulmonary vessels enter at the hilum as the root of the lung, not within the pleural cavity. Choose them for a question about what passes through the hilum.","Air in the pleural cavity is not normal; it is a pneumothorax, which breaks the seal and lets the lung collapse. Pick air for a question about what enters the pleural cavity after a chest wound."],"lt":"t-resp-thoracic-diaphragm","lid":"resp-thoracic-diaphragm-r3","comp":101,"competencyId":"resp-lungs-pleura"},{"id":"N-a17-103-1","dok":2,"q":"Why does the right dome of the diaphragm sit a little higher than the left?","a":"The liver lies under the right dome.","options":["The liver lies under the right dome","The heart rests on top of the right dome","Only the right side has a central tendon","The right phrenic nerve comes from more spinal nerves"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The diaphragm is a dome-shaped sheet of muscle that forms the floor of the thoracic cavity. The liver sits just under its right side, so the right dome sits a little higher than the left. Both sides share one central tendon, and both are supplied by phrenic nerves from C3, C4, and C5.","notes":["Correct. The liver lies under the right dome, so that side sits a little higher.","Weight resting on top of a dome would press it down, not lift it. The right dome sits higher because the liver lies under it.","There is one central tendon, the flat sheet of tendon at the top of the whole dome that all the muscle fibers pull toward. It is not a right-side feature, so it does not explain the difference in height.","Both phrenic nerves come from the same spinal nerves, C3, C4, and C5, so the two sides are supplied the same way. The difference in height comes from the liver underneath."],"comp":103,"competencyId":"resp-thoracic-diaphragm"},{"id":"N-a17-103-2","dok":1,"q":"Which pair of structures both pass through the aortic hiatus of the diaphragm?","a":"The aorta and the thoracic duct.","options":["The esophagus and the vagus nerves","The inferior vena cava and the aorta","The esophagus and the thoracic duct","The aorta and the thoracic duct"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The aortic hiatus lies behind the diaphragm at T12, between its two crura. The aorta passes through it, and the thoracic duct passes with it. Above the diaphragm the vessel is the thoracic aorta, and below it the vessel is called the abdominal aorta.","notes":["The esophagus and the vagus nerves pass together through the esophageal hiatus at T10. That pair answers a question about the T10 opening.","The inferior vena cava has its own opening, the caval opening in the central tendon at T8, so it does not travel with the aorta.","The thoracic duct does pass with the aorta, but the esophagus goes through a separate opening, the esophageal hiatus at T10.","Correct. The aorta and the thoracic duct pass together through the aortic hiatus at T12."],"comp":103,"competencyId":"resp-thoracic-diaphragm"},{"id":"N-a17-103-3","dok":2,"q":"In the cadaver, you follow the vagus nerves down from the thorax into the abdomen. Through which diaphragm opening do they pass?","a":"The esophageal hiatus at T10.","options":["The caval opening at T8","The esophageal hiatus at T10","The aortic hiatus at T12","The esophageal hiatus at T12"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The vagus nerves pass through the esophageal hiatus along with the esophagus. This opening is in the muscle of the diaphragm at the level of T10. So when you follow the esophagus through the diaphragm, you are following the path of the vagus nerves too.","notes":["The caval opening at T8 carries the inferior vena cava through the central tendon. The vagus nerves do not use it.","Correct. The vagus nerves pass with the esophagus through the esophageal hiatus at T10.","The aortic hiatus at T12 carries the aorta and the thoracic duct, not the vagus nerves.","The esophageal hiatus is the right opening, but it sits at T10. T12 is the level of the aortic hiatus."],"comp":103,"competencyId":"resp-thoracic-diaphragm"},{"id":"N-a17-103-4","dok":2,"q":"You trace the inferior vena cava down from the heart toward the abdomen. Where does it pass through the diaphragm?","a":"Through the central tendon at T8.","options":["Through the muscle at T10","Behind the diaphragm, between the crura, at T12","Through the muscle at T8","Through the central tendon at T8"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The inferior vena cava passes through the caval opening, which sits in the central tendon at the level of T8. The central tendon is the flat sheet of tendon at the top of the dome that all the muscle fibers pull toward. The other two openings sit lower: the esophageal hiatus in the muscle at T10 and the aortic hiatus behind the diaphragm at T12.","notes":["The opening in the muscle at T10 is the esophageal hiatus, which carries the esophagus and the vagus nerves.","The opening behind the diaphragm between the crura at T12 is the aortic hiatus, which carries the aorta and the thoracic duct.","T8 is the right level, but the caval opening sits in the central tendon, not in the muscle. The opening in the muscle is the esophageal hiatus at T10.","Correct. The caval opening for the inferior vena cava is in the central tendon at T8."],"comp":103,"competencyId":"resp-thoracic-diaphragm"},{"id":"N-a17-103-5","dok":1,"q":"Which muscle has fibers that run downward and forward, the same direction as your fingers with your hands in your front pockets?","a":"External intercostals.","options":["Internal intercostals","External intercostals","Diaphragm","Scalenes"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The external intercostals are the outer layer of muscle between the ribs, and their fibers run downward and forward. They lift the ribs during inspiration. The internal intercostals lie just deep to them, with fibers at right angles that run downward and backward.","notes":["The internal intercostals run at right angles to the external layer, downward and backward. Pick them for a question about the deeper layer.","Correct. The external intercostal fibers run downward and forward, like hands in front pockets.","The diaphragm is a dome whose fibers rise toward the central tendon. It closes the bottom of the thoracic cavity rather than filling the spaces between the ribs.","The scalenes are accessory muscles that help lift the rib cage when you breathe in harder than normal. They are not one of the layers between the ribs."],"comp":103,"competencyId":"resp-thoracic-diaphragm"},{"id":"N-a17-103-6","dok":1,"q":"When you need to breathe in harder than normal, which accessory muscles help lift the rib cage?","a":"The scalenes, sternocleidomastoid, and pectoralis minor.","options":["The internal intercostals and the abdominal muscles","The scalenes, sternocleidomastoid, and pectoralis minor","The two crura of the diaphragm","None, because a harder breath in comes from recoil alone"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The diaphragm and the external intercostals do the work of normal breathing in. When you need to breathe in harder than normal, accessory muscles help lift the rib cage: the scalenes, the sternocleidomastoid, and the pectoralis minor. Forced breathing out uses a different set, the internal intercostals and the abdominal muscles.","notes":["The internal intercostals and the abdominal muscles work in forced expiration, the hard breath out. They are the answer for blowing out candles, not for a harder breath in.","Correct. The scalenes, sternocleidomastoid, and pectoralis minor help lift the rib cage when you breathe in harder than normal.","The crura are the muscular legs that anchor the diaphragm to the lumbar vertebrae. They are part of the diaphragm itself, not accessory muscles that lift the ribs.","Recoil of the lungs and chest wall moves air out during quiet expiration. It does not pull air in, so a harder breath in needs extra muscles."],"comp":103,"competencyId":"resp-thoracic-diaphragm"},{"id":"N-a17-103-7","dok":3,"q":"A classmate says the internal intercostals must contract every time you breathe out. Why is this wrong for quiet breathing at rest?","a":"Quiet expiration needs no muscle effort, because the lungs and chest wall recoil.","options":["The external intercostals contract to pull the ribs down in every quiet breath out","The diaphragm contracts and pushes upward to force the air out of the lungs","Quiet expiration needs no muscle effort, because the lungs and chest wall recoil","The scalenes lower the rib cage a little during each quiet breath out at rest"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Quiet expiration needs no muscle effort. The lungs and chest wall recoil on their own, and that recoil moves air out at rest. The internal intercostals and the abdominal muscles join in only for forced expiration, such as a hard cough.","notes":["The external intercostals lift the ribs during inspiration. They are not the muscles that pull the ribs down.","When the diaphragm contracts, it flattens and moves down, which is inspiration. It does not contract to push air out.","Correct. At rest, breathing out needs no muscle effort because the lungs and chest wall recoil on their own.","The scalenes are accessory muscles that help lift the rib cage in a harder than normal breath in. They do not lower the ribs."],"comp":103,"competencyId":"resp-thoracic-diaphragm"},{"id":"N-a17-103-8","dok":3,"q":"Moving from the highest diaphragm opening to the lowest, which list gives where each opening sits?","a":"Central tendon, then muscle, then between the crura.","options":["Muscle, then central tendon, then between the crura","Between the crura, then muscle, then central tendon","Central tendon, then between the crura, then muscle","Central tendon, then muscle, then between the crura"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The highest opening is the caval opening for the inferior vena cava, in the central tendon at T8. Next is the esophageal hiatus, in the muscle at T10. Lowest is the aortic hiatus at T12, which lies behind the diaphragm between its two crura.","notes":["This swaps the top two. The highest opening, at T8, is in the central tendon, and the esophageal hiatus in the muscle comes next at T10.","This is the order from lowest to highest. The opening between the crura is the lowest, at T12, not the highest.","This swaps the bottom two. The esophageal hiatus in the muscle at T10 comes before the aortic hiatus between the crura at T12.","Correct. The order is the caval opening (central tendon, T8), the esophageal hiatus (muscle, T10), then the aortic hiatus (between the crura, T12)."],"comp":103,"competencyId":"resp-thoracic-diaphragm"},{"id":"N-a17-104-4","dok":1,"q":"Which structure does chronic bronchitis affect, and what happens to it?","a":"The bronchi, whose lining stays inflamed and makes too much mucus.","options":["The alveoli, whose walls break down and merge into larger spaces","The alveoli, which an infection inflames and fills with fluid","The bronchi, whose lining stays inflamed and makes too much mucus","The pleurae, whose layers become rough and rub with each breath"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Chronic bronchitis affects the bronchi. Their lining stays inflamed and makes too much mucus, and the mucus blocks the airways. It is one of the two main parts of COPD, along with emphysema.","notes":["Alveolar walls that break down and merge into fewer, larger spaces describe emphysema, the other main part of COPD.","Alveoli inflamed by an infection and filled with fluid describe pneumonia, an alveolar problem rather than a bronchial one.","Correct. In chronic bronchitis the bronchial lining stays inflamed and makes too much mucus, which blocks the airways.","Rough, inflamed pleural layers that rub with each breath describe pleurisy, a problem of the pleurae, not the airways."],"comp":104,"competencyId":"resp-disorders"},{"id":"N-a17-104-5","dok":3,"q":"Why do the bronchi and bronchioles clog in cystic fibrosis?","a":"An inherited disorder makes the mucus so thick and sticky that the cilia cannot clear it.","options":["The airway smooth muscle tightens and squeezes the bronchioles narrow","The alveolar walls break down and merge into a few large spaces","Air enters the pleural cavity, and the lung on that side collapses","An inherited disorder makes the mucus so thick and sticky that the cilia cannot clear it"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Cystic fibrosis is an inherited disorder that affects the bronchi and bronchioles. Its mucus is thick and sticky, so the cilia lining the airways cannot sweep it away. The mucus builds up and clogs the airways.","notes":["Tightening airway smooth muscle, with an inflamed lining and extra mucus, is asthma. In cystic fibrosis the problem is the thick mucus itself.","Alveolar walls that break down and merge describe emphysema, which affects the alveoli rather than clogging the bronchi and bronchioles.","Air in the pleural cavity that collapses the lung is a pneumothorax, a pleural problem rather than an airway one.","Correct. In cystic fibrosis, an inherited disorder, the mucus is so thick and sticky that the cilia cannot clear it, so it clogs the airways."],"comp":104,"competencyId":"resp-disorders"},{"id":"N-a17-104-6","dok":1,"q":"Which description matches tuberculosis?","a":"A bacterial infection that forms tubercles and scar tissue in the lung.","options":["An infection that inflames the alveoli and fills them with fluid","A bacterial infection that forms tubercles and scar tissue in the lung","A malignant tumor that usually starts in the epithelium of the airways","Extra fluid in the pleural cavity that presses on the lung"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Tuberculosis is a bacterial infection of the lung tissue. It forms small nodules called tubercles, along with scar tissue. Pneumonia is also an infection, but it inflames the alveoli and fills them with fluid instead.","notes":["An infection that inflames the alveoli and fills them with fluid is pneumonia. Fluid in the air sacs, not nodules and scarring, is its sign.","Correct. Tuberculosis is a bacterial infection that forms small nodules (tubercles) and scar tissue in the lung tissue.","A malignant tumor that usually starts in the epithelium lining the airways or alveoli is lung cancer, which is strongly linked to smoking.","Extra fluid in the pleural cavity that presses on the lung is a pleural effusion, a problem of the pleural cavity rather than the lung tissue."],"comp":104,"competencyId":"resp-disorders"},{"id":"N-a17-104-7","dok":2,"q":"A patient sitting upright has a pleural effusion. Which space holds the extra fluid, and where in that space does it pool?","a":"The pleural cavity, in the costodiaphragmatic recess.","options":["The pleural cavity, in the costodiaphragmatic recess","The pleural cavity, at the top near the apex of the lung","The alveoli, inside the air sacs of the lung","The bronchi, inside the lumen of the airways"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"A pleural effusion is extra fluid in the pleural cavity, and the fluid presses on the lung. In a person sitting or standing, it pools in the costodiaphragmatic recess. This recess is the low pocket where the parietal pleura turns from the ribs onto the diaphragm.","notes":["Correct. The fluid is in the pleural cavity, and in an upright person it pools in the costodiaphragmatic recess.","The fluid is in the pleural cavity, but it does not collect at the top. In a person sitting or standing, it pools in the costodiaphragmatic recess at the bottom edge.","Fluid filling the alveoli is pneumonia, where an infection inflames the air sacs. A pleural effusion collects outside the lung, in the pleural cavity.","Mucus in the lumen of the bronchi is the problem in chronic bronchitis and cystic fibrosis. A pleural effusion is fluid outside the lung, in the pleural cavity."],"comp":104,"competencyId":"resp-disorders"},{"id":"N-a17d-101-1","dok":3,"q":"On the cadaver, you study a removed lung. At its hilum the pulmonary artery is the highest structure and the pulmonary veins are the lowest, and its front edge has a deep notch. Which lung is it, and how many lobes should you find?","a":"The left lung, with two lobes separated by an oblique fissure.","options":["The right lung, with two lobes separated by an oblique fissure.","The right lung, with three lobes separated by two fissures.","The left lung, with three lobes separated by two fissures.","The left lung, with two lobes separated by an oblique fissure."],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Two clues work together here. In the left hilum the pulmonary artery is the highest structure, while in the right hilum a bronchus sits at or above the artery, and the veins are lowest in both. The cardiac notch in the front edge makes room for the heart, which tilts to the left. Both clues point to the left lung, which has a superior and an inferior lobe separated by an oblique fissure.","notes":["Two lobes and an oblique fissure only describe the left lung. The right lung has three lobes, and the cardiac notch is found only on the left.","The right lung has three lobes and both a horizontal and an oblique fissure, but in its hilum a bronchus sits at or above the artery, and it has no cardiac notch.","The side is right but the lobe count is wrong. The left lung gave up its middle lobe to the heart, so it has only two lobes and one fissure.","Correct. The artery on top at the hilum and the cardiac notch both mark the left lung, which has two lobes and an oblique fissure only."],"comp":101,"competencyId":"resp-lungs-pleura"}]},{"id":"m4-lymph-vessels","title":"Lymph, lymphatic vessels, trunks and ducts","summary":"lymph, lymphatic capillaries, collecting vessels, trunks, thoracic and right lymphatic ducts, drainage regions, lymphedema","mw":"2026-10-28","tr":"2026-10-29","cards":[{"id":"L01821","dok":1,"q":"Which list names parts of the lymphatic system?","a":"Lymph, lymphatic vessels, lymphatic tissue and organs, and red bone marrow.","options":["Interstitial fluid, blood capillaries, the liver, and the kidneys","Lymph, lymphatic vessels, lymphatic tissue and organs, and red bone marrow","Plasma, arteries, veins, and the spleen","Lymph, lymphatic vessels, the heart, and the venae cavae"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The lymphatic system is made of lymph (the fluid), the lymphatic vessels that carry it, the tissues and organs that contain lymphatic tissue such as lymph nodes, the spleen, the thymus, and the tonsils, and the red bone marrow where lymphocytes are made. It has no pump of its own, which is why the heart is not on the list.","notes":["Interstitial fluid and blood capillaries are where lymph comes from, and the liver and kidneys are organs of other systems. This list fits a question about where fluid leaks out before lymphatics pick it up.","Correct. Lymph, lymphatic vessels, lymphatic tissue and organs, and red bone marrow make up the lymphatic system.","The spleen is a lymphatic organ, but plasma, arteries, and veins belong to the cardiovascular system. Students pick this when they confuse the two circulations.","Lymph and lymphatic vessels belong, but the heart and venae cavae are parts of the cardiovascular system. Students pick this because lymph does end up in the blood heading to the heart, but it rejoins the blood at the subclavian veins and is no longer part of the lymphatic system there."],"lt":"t-lymph-overview-vessels","lid":"c1","comp":105,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-pathway"},{"id":"L01823","dok":1,"q":"What is the main difference between interstitial fluid and lymph?","a":"Their location: interstitial fluid lies between cells, and lymph is the same fluid once it is inside lymphatic vessels.","options":["Where the fluid is located","Whether the heart pumps the fluid","Whether the fluid contains red blood cells","Which organ secretes the fluid"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Interstitial fluid is the fluid that leaks out of blood capillaries and bathes the cells. Once that same fluid enters a lymphatic capillary, it is called lymph. So the difference is location rather than makeup: the name changes when the fluid moves from the tissue spaces into the lymphatic vessels.","notes":["Correct. Interstitial fluid and lymph are the same fluid in different places.","The heart pumps neither fluid; lymph moves without a pump of its own. Being pumped by the heart is what separates blood from both of these fluids.","Neither interstitial fluid nor ordinary lymph normally carries red blood cells, since those stay inside blood vessels. Pick red blood cells as the difference for a question comparing blood with lymph.","Neither fluid is secreted by an organ; both come from fluid that filtered out of blood capillaries. Students choose this when they picture lymph nodes making lymph, but nodes only filter it."],"lt":"t-lymph-overview-vessels","lid":"c3","comp":105,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-pathway"},{"id":"L01824","dok":1,"q":"What is chyle?","a":"Milky, fat-rich lymph that comes from the lacteals of the small intestine.","options":["The liquid part of blood left after the cells are removed","Clear, watery lymph draining from the limbs","Milky, fat-rich lymph from the lacteals of the small intestine","Fluid in the spaces between cells before it enters a vessel"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Chyle is lymph that carries absorbed dietary fat. It forms in the lacteals, the lymphatic capillaries in the center of each intestinal villus, and the fat gives it a milky color. Chyle drains toward the cisterna chyli and then up the thoracic duct.","notes":["The liquid part of blood is plasma. Plasma leaks out to form interstitial fluid, but it is not lymph, so pick it for a question about the fluid portion of blood.","Clear, watery lymph is ordinary lymph, like the lymph draining the limbs. Pick it if the question asks what lymph looks like when it carries no dietary fat.","Correct. Chyle is milky, fat-rich lymph from the lacteals.","Fluid between cells before it enters a vessel is interstitial fluid. It becomes lymph only after it enters a lymphatic capillary, so choose this for a question about interstitial fluid."],"lt":"t-lymph-overview-vessels","lid":"c4","comp":105,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-pathway"},{"id":"L01828","dok":1,"q":"Where do lymphatic capillaries begin?","a":"In the tissue spaces between cells, woven among the blood capillaries.","options":["Inside the lymph nodes","Inside the lumen of blood capillaries","At the cisterna chyli","In the tissue spaces between cells"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Lymphatic capillaries start as tiny dead-end tubes in the tissue spaces, woven among the blood capillaries in nearly every tissue. Their job is to take up the excess interstitial fluid that leaks out of the blood capillaries. From there, lymph moves into collecting vessels, through lymph nodes, and on to the trunks and ducts.","notes":["Lymph nodes lie along the collecting vessels, later in the route. Pick them if the question asks where lymph is filtered.","Lymphatic capillaries are separate from blood capillaries; they collect fluid that has already left the blood. Students pick this because the two kinds of capillary lie side by side.","The cisterna chyli is a sac in the abdomen at the start of the thoracic duct, near the end of the route. Choose it for a question about where the thoracic duct begins.","Correct. Lymphatic capillaries begin in the tissue spaces between cells."],"lt":"t-lymph-overview-vessels","lid":"c8","comp":105,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-pathway"},{"id":"L01829","dok":2,"q":"Which group lists tissues that all lack lymphatic capillaries?","a":"Cartilage, the cornea, and the central nervous system.","options":["The dermis, skeletal muscle, and the cornea","The intestinal villi, the dermis, and the lungs","Cartilage, the dermis, and the intestinal wall","Cartilage, the cornea, and the central nervous system"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Lymphatic capillaries are found in nearly every tissue, but a few lack them. These include the central nervous system, cartilage, bone and red bone marrow, the cornea, and avascular tissue such as the epidermis. Cartilage, the cornea, and the central nervous system are the only set here where every item is on that list.","notes":["The cornea fits, but the dermis and skeletal muscle both have lymphatic capillaries. Pick the dermis and muscle for a question about tissues that do drain lymph.","All three of these contain lymphatics. The intestinal villi even hold the specialized lacteals, so this set fits a question about tissues rich in lymphatic drainage.","Cartilage fits, but the dermis and the intestinal wall both contain lymphatic capillaries. Students choose this when they remember only one tissue from the list.","Correct. Cartilage, the cornea, and the central nervous system all lack lymphatic capillaries."],"lt":"t-lymph-overview-vessels","lid":"c9","comp":106,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-vessels-node"},{"id":"L01830","dok":1,"q":"Anchoring filaments tie a lymphatic capillary to what?","a":"The surrounding tissue, which holds the capillary open.","options":["The surrounding tissue","The nearest lymph node","A nearby blood capillary","The basement membrane of the epidermis"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Anchoring filaments are fine filaments that attach the endothelial cells of a lymphatic capillary to the surrounding tissue. Because a lymphatic capillary has no blood pressure inside to hold it open, these filaments keep it from collapsing. When fluid builds up and the tissue swells, they pull on the cells and widen the gaps between them so fluid can enter.","notes":["Correct. Anchoring filaments attach the capillary to the surrounding tissue.","Lymph nodes lie along collecting vessels farther down the route, not at the capillary. Choose a node for a question about where lymph is filtered.","Lymphatic and blood capillaries lie side by side but are not tied together; fluid passes from one to the other only by first entering the tissue spaces. Pick a blood capillary for a question about where interstitial fluid comes from.","The epidermis is avascular and has no lymphatic capillaries at all. Pick the epidermis for a question about tissues that lack lymphatics."],"lt":"t-lymph-overview-vessels","lid":"c10","comp":106,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-vessels-node"},{"id":"L01831","dok":1,"q":"What are lacteals?","a":"Lymphatic capillaries in the intestinal villi that absorb dietary fat.","options":["Blood capillaries in the intestinal villi that absorb sugars","Lymphatic capillaries in the intestinal villi that absorb dietary fat","Ducts that carry milk through the mammary glands","Lymph nodes along the intestine that filter chyle"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Lacteals are specialized lymphatic capillaries in the center of each villus of the small intestine. Dietary fats are too large to enter the blood capillaries, so the lacteals absorb them instead. The fat turns the lymph milky, and this milky lymph is called chyle.","notes":["Each villus also contains blood capillaries, which take up sugars and amino acids. Pick these for a question about where absorbed sugars enter the blood.","Correct. Lacteals are the lymphatic capillaries of the villi that absorb dietary fat.","Ducts carrying milk in the breast are lactiferous ducts. The names look alike because both come from the Latin word for milk, but those ducts belong to the mammary glands.","Lymph nodes do filter lymph from the intestine, but they lie along collecting vessels, not in the villi. Choose nodes for a question about where intestinal lymph is filtered."],"lt":"t-lymph-overview-vessels","lid":"c11","comp":106,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-vessels-node"},{"id":"L01832","dok":1,"q":"How does a lymphatic capillary end in the tissues?","a":"It is closed at one end, a blind ending.","options":["Open at both ends as part of a loop","Closed at one end, a blind ending","Joined at one end to an arteriole","Ending in a valve that opens into a vein"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"A lymphatic capillary is a dead-end tube, closed at the end that sits in the tissue. Fluid enters through gaps between its overlapping endothelial cells rather than from an upstream vessel. Lymph then flows one way only, away from the tissues and toward the heart.","notes":["Open at both ends describes a blood capillary, which links an arteriole to a venule as part of a loop. Pick it for a question about blood capillaries.","Correct. A lymphatic capillary has a blind ending, closed at one end.","Arterioles feed blood capillaries, not lymphatic capillaries. Students choose this when they assume lymphatic capillaries are built like blood capillaries.","Lymph does not enter veins at the capillary level; it is returned to the blood only at the end of the route, where the ducts join the subclavian veins."],"lt":"t-lymph-overview-vessels","lid":"c12","comp":106,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-vessels-node"},{"id":"L01833","dok":1,"q":"Compared with blood capillaries, how permeable are lymphatic capillaries?","a":"More permeable, so they take up proteins, cells, and even pathogens.","options":["Impermeable until a lymph node opens them","More permeable, admitting proteins, cells, and pathogens","Less permeable, admitting only water","Equally permeable, admitting the same small molecules"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Lymphatic capillaries are built with loosely overlapping endothelial cells, so they are more permeable than blood capillaries. They take up fluid along with large items such as proteins, cells, pathogens, and even cancer cells. Blood capillaries, with more tightly joined cells, mostly let small molecules through.","notes":["Lymphatic capillaries take in fluid directly from the tissues; lymph nodes lie farther along the route and only filter what arrives. Pick nodes for a question about filtering lymph.","Correct. Lymphatic capillaries are more permeable than blood capillaries.","Lymphatic capillaries are more permeable, not less. A wall that admits mostly small molecules describes a blood capillary.","Blood capillaries mostly pass small molecules, but lymphatic capillaries also take in proteins and cells. Students choose this when they assume all capillaries are alike."],"lt":"t-lymph-overview-vessels","lid":"c13","comp":106,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-vessels-node"},{"id":"L01834","dok":1,"q":"How does the diameter of a lymphatic capillary compare with that of a blood capillary?","a":"The lymphatic capillary is wider.","options":["The lymphatic capillary is narrower","The lymphatic capillary is as wide as a lymphatic duct","The lymphatic capillary is wider","The two are the same width"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Lymphatic capillaries are slightly wider than blood capillaries. Their larger size, together with their loosely overlapping endothelial cells, suits their job of taking up fluid along with large particles such as proteins and cells. Blood capillaries are narrow enough that red blood cells pass through nearly single file.","notes":["Narrower describes the blood capillary, which is just wide enough for red blood cells to pass in single file. Pick narrower if the question asked about the blood capillary.","Lymphatic ducts are the largest lymphatic vessels, at the end of the route, and far wider than any capillary. Choose a duct for a question about the largest lymphatic vessel.","Correct. A lymphatic capillary is wider than a blood capillary.","They are not the same width. Students pick this when they assume all capillaries share one size."],"lt":"t-lymph-overview-vessels","lid":"c14","comp":106,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-vessels-node"},{"id":"L01835","dok":1,"q":"Which structural feature keeps lymph moving one way in lymphatic collecting vessels?","a":"Valves.","options":["Lacteals","Anchoring filaments","Lymph nodes","Valves"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Lymphatic collecting vessels contain many one-way valves, even more than veins have. The valves let lymph move forward toward the heart and close if it starts to slip backward. Because the lymphatic system has no pump of its own, the valves are what turn every squeeze from muscles or breathing into forward movement.","notes":["Lacteals are the lymphatic capillaries in the intestinal villi that absorb dietary fat. Choose them for a question about where chyle forms.","Anchoring filaments tie lymphatic capillaries to the surrounding tissue and hold them open. Pick them for a question about what keeps a lymphatic capillary from collapsing.","Lymph nodes filter lymph along the collecting vessels, but they do not set its direction. Pick them for a question about where lymph is cleaned before returning to the blood.","Correct. Valves keep lymph flowing one way in the collecting vessels."],"lt":"t-lymph-overview-vessels","lid":"c15","comp":106,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-vessels-node"},{"id":"L01836","dok":2,"q":"In what order does lymph travel from the tissues toward the lymphatic trunks?","a":"Lymphatic capillaries, then collecting vessels, then lymph nodes, then lymphatic trunks.","options":["Lymph nodes, then lymphatic capillaries, then collecting vessels, then lymphatic trunks","Collecting vessels, then lymphatic capillaries, then lymph nodes, then lymphatic trunks","Lymphatic capillaries, then collecting vessels, then lymph nodes, then lymphatic trunks","Lymphatic capillaries, then lymph nodes, then lymphatic trunks, then collecting vessels"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Lymph moves into larger and larger vessels as it goes. It starts in the lymphatic capillaries in the tissue spaces, flows into collecting vessels, and passes through lymph nodes, which filter it along the way. Collecting vessels then merge into the lymphatic trunks, which empty into the two ducts and finally the subclavian veins.","notes":["Lymph nodes filter lymph that has already been collected, so they cannot be the starting point. Lymph has to be picked up by capillaries before it reaches a node.","Collecting vessels receive lymph from the capillaries, so they cannot come first. The route always begins where fluid is taken up in the tissues.","Correct. Capillaries, collecting vessels, nodes, then trunks.","This puts collecting vessels last, but nodes sit along the collecting vessels, and trunks are formed when collecting vessels merge. Collecting vessels must come before both."],"lt":"t-lymph-overview-vessels","lid":"c16","comp":105,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-pathway"},{"id":"L01837","dok":1,"q":"Where does the fluid that becomes lymph come from?","a":"Interstitial fluid that leaked out of the blood capillaries.","options":["Cerebrospinal fluid draining from the brain","Plasma pumped directly into lymphatic vessels by the heart","Fluid secreted by the lymph nodes","Interstitial fluid that leaked out of blood capillaries"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Blood capillaries constantly leak some fluid and plasma proteins into the tissue spaces, where it is called interstitial fluid. Not all of it returns to the blood capillaries, so lymphatic capillaries take up the extra. Once inside a lymphatic vessel it is called lymph, and it is eventually returned to the blood.","notes":["Cerebrospinal fluid fills the ventricles and surrounds the brain and spinal cord. The central nervous system has no lymphatic capillaries, so choose this for a question about the fluid made by the choroid plexus.","The heart pumps blood through blood vessels only; it does not push plasma into lymphatic vessels. The lymphatic system has no pump of its own.","Lymph nodes filter lymph but do not produce the fluid. Pick nodes for a question about where lymph is cleaned along its route.","Correct. Lymph begins as interstitial fluid that leaked from blood capillaries."],"lt":"t-lymph-overview-vessels","lid":"c17","comp":105,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-pathway"},{"id":"L01838","dok":1,"q":"Which statement best describes lymph?","a":"Interstitial fluid once it has entered a lymphatic vessel; clear and watery.","options":["Fluid secreted into the small intestine by the liver","Interstitial fluid once it has entered a lymphatic vessel","Fluid that circulates through the ventricles of the brain","The liquid part of blood flowing in the arteries"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Lymph is interstitial fluid that has entered a lymphatic vessel. Ordinary lymph is clear and watery and carries proteins, cells such as lymphocytes, and anything else it picked up in the tissues. It travels one way through the lymphatic vessels and is returned to the blood at the subclavian veins.","notes":["Fluid sent into the small intestine by the liver is bile, which helps break up fats. It fits a question about the liver's digestive secretion.","Correct. Lymph is interstitial fluid that has entered a lymphatic vessel.","Fluid in the brain's ventricles is cerebrospinal fluid, made by the choroid plexus. Choose it for a question about the fluid around the brain and spinal cord.","The liquid part of blood is plasma. Some plasma leaks out to become interstitial fluid, but in the arteries it is still plasma, so pick this for a question defining plasma."],"lt":"t-lymph-overview-vessels","lid":"c18","comp":105,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-pathway"},{"id":"L01839","dok":2,"q":"Which vessels carry chyle?","a":"The lacteals of the small intestine.","options":["The lacteals of the small intestine","The lymphatic capillaries of the lower limbs","The blood capillaries of the intestinal villi","The lymphatic capillaries of the lungs"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Chyle is milky, fat-rich lymph, and it forms in the lacteals, the lymphatic capillaries in the center of each villus of the small intestine. Dietary fats are too large to enter the blood capillaries, so the lacteals pick them up. Chyle then drains to the cisterna chyli and up the thoracic duct.","notes":["Correct. The lacteals of the small intestine carry chyle.","Lymphatic capillaries of the lower limbs carry ordinary lymph, which is clear and watery. Choose them for a question about lymph that drains into the lumbar trunks.","The blood capillaries of the villi absorb sugars and amino acids into the blood. Fats are too large for them, so pick these for a question about where sugars are absorbed.","Lymphatic capillaries of the lungs carry ordinary lymph to the bronchomediastinal trunks. They fit a question about lymph from the thoracic organs, not fat-rich chyle."],"lt":"t-lymph-overview-vessels","lid":"c19","comp":106,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-vessels-node"},{"id":"L01840","dok":2,"q":"What lets interstitial fluid flow into a lymphatic capillary but not back out?","a":"Overlapping endothelial cells that act as one-way flap valves.","options":["The valves of the collecting vessels downstream","A thick smooth muscle wall around the capillary","Overlapping endothelial cells that act as flap valves","Anchoring filaments that seal the capillary shut"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The endothelial cells of a lymphatic capillary overlap loosely at their edges like shingles. When fluid pressure in the tissue is higher, the edges swing inward and fluid enters. When pressure inside the capillary rises, the edges are pressed together and close, so fluid cannot leak back out into the tissue.","notes":["Valves in the collecting vessels stop lymph from flowing backward along the vessels, but they are farther down the route. They do not control entry into the capillary from the tissue.","Lymphatic capillaries have a very thin wall of endothelium with no smooth muscle layer. Smooth muscle appears in the walls of larger lymphatic vessels, so pick it for a question about what helps move lymph along larger vessels.","Correct. Overlapping endothelial cells act as one-way flap valves at the capillary wall.","Anchoring filaments hold the capillary open and pull the gaps wider when fluid builds up. They do the opposite of sealing it, so choose them for a question about what keeps the capillary from collapsing."],"lt":"t-lymph-overview-vessels","lid":"c20","comp":106,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-vessels-node"},{"id":"L01841","dok":2,"q":"Which statement about lymphatic capillaries is true?","a":"They are held open by anchoring filaments tied to the surrounding tissue.","options":["They are held open by anchoring filaments tied to the tissue","They have a smaller diameter than blood capillaries","They are less permeable than blood capillaries","They are open at both ends like a continuous tube"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"A lymphatic capillary has no blood pressure inside to hold it open, so anchoring filaments attach its endothelial cells to the surrounding tissue. When fluid collects and the tissue swells, the filaments pull the cells apart and let more fluid in. The capillary is also blind-ended, wider than a blood capillary, and more permeable.","notes":["Correct. Anchoring filaments tie lymphatic capillaries to the surrounding tissue and hold them open.","Lymphatic capillaries are slightly wider than blood capillaries, not narrower. A smaller diameter describes the blood capillary, which red blood cells pass through nearly single file.","Lymphatic capillaries are more permeable than blood capillaries, letting in proteins, cells, and pathogens. This reverses the comparison.","Being open at both ends describes a blood capillary, which links an arteriole to a venule. Lymphatic capillaries are closed at one end, so pick this for a question about blood capillaries."],"lt":"t-lymph-overview-vessels","lid":"c21","comp":106,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-vessels-node"},{"id":"L01842","dok":2,"q":"What do anchoring filaments do when interstitial fluid builds up in a tissue?","a":"They pull the endothelial cells apart so more fluid can enter the capillary.","options":["They seal the capillary so no fluid can enter","They pull the endothelial cells apart so more fluid enters","They pump lymph toward the heart","They form the valves inside the collecting vessels"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"When fluid collects in a tissue, the tissue swells and stretches the anchoring filaments. Because the filaments are tied to the endothelial cells of the lymphatic capillary, they pull those cells apart and widen the gaps between them. More fluid can then flow in, which helps drain the swelling.","notes":["Sealing the capillary would stop drainage just when it is needed most. Anchoring filaments do the opposite, opening the capillary wider.","Correct. Anchoring filaments pull the endothelial cells apart so more fluid can enter.","Anchoring filaments do not pump. Lymph is moved by outside forces such as the skeletal muscle pump and the respiratory pump, so pick those for a question about what pushes lymph along.","The valves in collecting vessels are folds of the vessel lining, not anchoring filaments. Choose valves for a question about what keeps lymph from flowing backward."],"lt":"t-lymph-overview-vessels","lid":"c22","comp":106,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-vessels-node"},{"id":"L01843","dok":2,"q":"Which vessels carry lymph directly out of the lymphatic capillaries?","a":"Lymphatic collecting vessels.","options":["The subclavian veins","The thoracic duct","Lymphatic collecting vessels","Lymphatic trunks"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Lymphatic capillaries drain into collecting vessels, the next step on the route. Collecting vessels are built like small veins, with thin walls and many valves, and they pass through lymph nodes as they go. Only later do they merge to form the larger lymphatic trunks.","notes":["The subclavian veins are where the lymphatic ducts finally return lymph to the blood. They fit a question about the last step of the route.","The thoracic duct is the largest lymphatic vessel, near the end of the route. Choose it for a question about where most trunks empty.","Correct. Collecting vessels carry lymph directly from the lymphatic capillaries.","Lymphatic trunks form when many collecting vessels merge, after the lymph has passed through nodes. Pick trunks for a question about the large vessels that each drain a major region."],"lt":"t-lymph-overview-vessels","lid":"c23","comp":105,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-pathway"},{"id":"L01844","dok":2,"q":"Which of these is NOT one of the three main jobs of the lymphatic system?","a":"Pumping oxygenated blood to the tissues.","options":["Transporting dietary fats","Carrying out immune defense","Draining excess interstitial fluid","Pumping oxygenated blood to the tissues"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The lymphatic system has three jobs: returning leaked fluid and proteins to the blood (fluid balance), absorbing dietary fats through the lacteals, and defending the body through lymph nodes, the spleen, tonsils, and MALT. Pumping oxygenated blood is the work of the heart and arteries. The lymphatic system has no pump at all.","notes":["Transporting dietary fats is a real lymphatic job, done by the lacteals of the small intestine. It would be the answer to a question about what lacteals do.","Immune defense is a real lymphatic job, carried out in lymph nodes, the spleen, tonsils, and MALT. It would answer a question about what lymphocytes and macrophages do there.","Draining excess interstitial fluid is a real lymphatic job, called fluid balance, done by lymphatic capillaries in nearly every tissue. It would be the answer if the question asked which job keeps blood volume steady.","Correct. Pumping oxygenated blood is the job of the heart and arteries, not the lymphatic system."],"lt":"t-lymph-overview-vessels","lid":"c24","comp":105,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-pathway"},{"id":"L01847","dok":3,"q":"A vessel is described as a blind-ended tube in the tissue spaces that is more permeable than a blood capillary. Which vessel is it?","a":"A lymphatic capillary.","options":["A lymphatic collecting vessel","A lymphatic trunk","A blood capillary","A lymphatic capillary"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Lymphatic capillaries are the only vessels in this list that begin as dead-end tubes in the tissue spaces. Their loosely overlapping endothelial cells make them more permeable than blood capillaries, so they can take up fluid along with proteins and cells. Every other lymphatic vessel receives lymph from somewhere upstream.","notes":["A collecting vessel carries lymph onward from the capillaries and has valves. It is not blind-ended, so pick it for a question about the vessels that pass through lymph nodes.","A lymphatic trunk is a large vessel formed by merging collecting vessels, draining a whole region. Choose it for a question about the vessels that empty into the ducts.","A blood capillary is open at both ends, linking an arteriole to a venule, and is less permeable. It fits a question about exchange vessels of the blood.","Correct. A blind-ended, highly permeable tube in the tissue spaces is a lymphatic capillary."],"lt":"t-lymph-overview-vessels","lid":"c27","comp":106,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-vessels-node"},{"id":"L01848","dok":3,"q":"After a fatty meal, a vessel in the center of an intestinal villus fills with milky fluid. What is the vessel, and what is the fluid?","a":"A lacteal carrying chyle.","options":["A lymph node holding lymph","A collecting vessel carrying bile","A lacteal carrying chyle","A blood capillary carrying plasma"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The lymphatic capillary in the center of each villus is a lacteal. Dietary fats are too large to enter the blood capillaries of the villus, so the lacteal absorbs them, and the fat turns the lymph milky. That milky, fat-rich lymph is called chyle.","notes":["Lymph nodes sit along collecting vessels and filter lymph, not in the center of a villus. Choose a node for a question about where intestinal lymph is filtered.","Bile is made by the liver and carried in bile ducts to the duodenum, never in lymphatic vessels. It fits a question about the liver's digestive secretion.","Correct. The milky fluid in the center of a villus is chyle in a lacteal.","Blood capillaries in the villus carry plasma and absorb sugars and amino acids, but the fat is too large for them. Pick this pair for a question about where absorbed sugars enter the blood."],"lt":"t-lymph-overview-vessels","lid":"c28","comp":106,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-vessels-node"},{"id":"L01849","dok":2,"q":"Which of these tissues has no lymphatic capillaries?","a":"The cornea.","options":["Skeletal muscle","The dermis of the skin","The wall of the intestine","The cornea"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Lymphatic capillaries are found in nearly every tissue, but the cornea is one of the few without them. The others include the central nervous system, cartilage, bone and red bone marrow, and avascular tissue such as the epidermis. The cornea has no blood vessels either, which helps keep it transparent.","notes":["Skeletal muscle has lymphatic capillaries in the connective tissue around its fibers. Choose it for a question about tissues where the skeletal muscle pump pushes lymph along.","The dermis is a vascular connective tissue layer with many lymphatic capillaries. Pick it for a question about which skin layer drains lymph.","The intestinal wall is rich in lymphatics, including the lacteals in the villi. It fits a question about where dietary fat enters the lymph.","Correct. The cornea has no lymphatic capillaries."],"lt":"t-lymph-overview-vessels","lid":"c29","comp":106,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-vessels-node"},{"id":"L01851","dok":1,"q":"Which lymphatic job is carried out by the lymphatic capillaries in nearly every tissue?","a":"Returning excess interstitial fluid and proteins to the blood.","options":["Returning excess interstitial fluid to the blood","Absorbing dietary fats","Producing red blood cells","Filtering lymph and housing lymphocytes"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Blood capillaries leak more fluid and protein into the tissues than they take back. Lymphatic capillaries in nearly every tissue pick up that excess and return it to the blood, about 3 liters a day, which keeps fluid from building up in the tissues and keeps blood volume steady. This job is called fluid balance.","notes":["Correct. Lymphatic capillaries in nearly every tissue drain excess interstitial fluid back to the blood.","Absorbing dietary fats is done only by the lacteals in the small intestine, not by lymphatic capillaries everywhere. Pick it for a question about what lacteals do.","Red blood cells are made in the red bone marrow, not by lymphatic capillaries. Pick this for a question about where blood cells form.","Filtering lymph and housing lymphocytes happens in lymph nodes, the spleen, tonsils, and MALT. Choose it for a question about the defense job of the system."],"lt":"t-lymph-overview-vessels","lid":"c31","comp":105,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-pathway"},{"id":"L01852","dok":2,"q":"How does the wall of a lymphatic collecting vessel compare with the wall of a vein?","a":"Thinner walls with more valves.","options":["Thicker walls with fewer valves","The same walls but with no valves","A muscular pumping chamber in place of valves","Thinner walls with more valves"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Collecting vessels are built like veins, with three thin layers, but their walls are thinner and they have more valves. The extra valves matter because lymph moves under very low pressure with no pump of its own. Each valve stops lymph from slipping backward between squeezes from muscles and breathing.","notes":["This reverses both features. Thicker walls with fewer valves would describe a vein compared with a collecting vessel.","Collecting vessels have many valves, more than veins, so they cannot be valveless. Students choose this when they confuse collecting vessels with lymphatic capillaries, which rely on overlapping endothelial cells instead.","The lymphatic system has no pumping chamber; lymph is moved by outside forces like the skeletal muscle and respiratory pumps. A muscular pumping chamber describes the heart.","Correct. Collecting vessels have thinner walls and more valves than veins."],"lt":"t-lymph-overview-vessels","lid":"c32","comp":106,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-vessels-node"},{"id":"L01856","dok":1,"q":"Where are lacteals located?","a":"In the center of each villus of the small intestine.","options":["In the villi of the small intestine","In the lobules of the liver","In the gastric pits of the stomach lining","In the mucosa of the large intestine"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Lacteals are lymphatic capillaries that run up the center of each villus, the finger-like folds of the small intestine's lining. That is where dietary fats are absorbed, and fats are too large to enter the blood capillaries of the villus. The lacteals take them up instead, forming chyle.","notes":["Correct. Lacteals run up the center of each intestinal villus.","Liver lobules are the functional units of the liver, built around a central vein. They fit a question about liver structure, not fat absorption.","Gastric pits are openings in the stomach lining that lead to the gastric glands. Pick them for a question about where stomach secretions are released.","The large intestine lacks villi, so it has no lacteals. Choose the large intestine for a question about where water is absorbed from the remaining waste."],"lt":"t-lymph-overview-vessels","lid":"c36","comp":106,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-vessels-node"},{"id":"L01857","dok":2,"q":"Which of these tissues would you expect to contain lymphatic capillaries?","a":"The dermis of the skin.","options":["The central nervous system","The epidermis","The dermis of the skin","Hyaline cartilage"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The dermis is a vascular connective tissue layer with blood capillaries, and lymphatic capillaries lie among them to drain the fluid that leaks out. The other tissues listed are among the few that lack lymphatic capillaries: avascular tissue such as the epidermis, cartilage, and the central nervous system.","notes":["The central nervous system has no lymphatic capillaries. It fits a question about tissues where lymphatic capillaries are absent.","The epidermis is avascular epithelium, with no blood vessels or lymphatic capillaries. Pick it for a question about a skin layer that gets nutrients by diffusion from the dermis.","Correct. The dermis contains lymphatic capillaries.","Hyaline cartilage is avascular and has no lymphatic capillaries. Choose it for a question about a tissue that heals slowly because it lacks blood vessels."],"lt":"t-lymph-overview-vessels","lid":"c37","comp":106,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-vessels-node"},{"id":"L01858","dok":2,"q":"Chyle looks milky white rather than clear. What in it explains this?","a":"Its high dietary fat content.","options":["Its high dietary fat content","Dissolved oxygen","A high red blood cell count","A large number of platelets"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Chyle is lymph from the lacteals of the small intestine, and it carries absorbed dietary fats. The fat droplets scatter light and give the fluid its milky color. Ordinary lymph, which carries little fat, is clear and watery.","notes":["Correct. The dietary fat in chyle makes it milky.","Dissolved oxygen does not change the color of a fluid this way. Oxygen bound to hemoglobin is what makes arterial blood bright red.","Red blood cells would color the fluid red, and they stay inside blood vessels rather than entering lymph. Pick red cells for a question about why blood is red.","Platelets are tiny cell fragments in blood that help it clot. They do not make lymph milky, so choose them for a question about clotting."],"lt":"t-lymph-overview-vessels","lid":"c38","comp":105,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-pathway"},{"id":"L01860","dok":2,"q":"Which statement best explains why the lymphatic system is closely linked with the circulatory system?","a":"It drains excess interstitial fluid that came from the blood and returns it to the venous blood.","options":["It replaces the veins as the return route from the legs","It pumps arterial blood out to the tissues alongside the heart","It drains fluid leaked from the blood and returns it to the veins","It makes red blood cells inside its own vessels"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The fluid in lymphatic vessels starts as plasma that leaked out of blood capillaries. The lymphatic system collects that excess fluid and its proteins and returns them to the blood where the lymphatic ducts empty into the subclavian veins. Without this return, fluid would build up in the tissues and blood volume would fall.","notes":["Lymphatic vessels run alongside veins, but they do not replace them. Superficial collecting vessels travel with the superficial veins, which fits a question about where those vessels run.","The lymphatic system has no pump and carries no arterial blood. Lymph moves through outside forces such as the skeletal muscle and respiratory pumps.","Correct. The lymphatic system returns fluid that leaked from the blood back into the venous blood.","Red blood cells are made in red bone marrow, not inside lymphatic vessels. Red bone marrow fits a question about where blood cells and lymphocytes form."],"lt":"t-lymph-overview-vessels","lid":"c40","comp":105,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-pathway"},{"id":"L01861","dok":1,"q":"How does ordinary lymph (not chyle) look?","a":"Clear and watery.","options":["Bright red","Clear and watery","Milky white","Greenish yellow"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Ordinary lymph is interstitial fluid that has entered a lymphatic vessel, so it looks much like the clear, watery fluid that bathes the cells. It carries proteins and white blood cells but no red blood cells. Only lymph from the small intestine, chyle, turns milky because of absorbed fat.","notes":["Bright red is the color of oxygen-rich blood, colored by hemoglobin in red blood cells. Lymph normally has no red blood cells.","Correct. Ordinary lymph is clear and watery.","Milky white describes chyle, the fat-rich lymph from the lacteals of the small intestine. Pick it for a question about lymph after a fatty meal.","Greenish yellow is the color of bile, made by the liver and stored in the gallbladder. It fits a question about the fluid released into the duodenum."],"lt":"t-lymph-overview-vessels","lid":"c41","comp":105,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-pathway"},{"id":"L01862","dok":1,"q":"Which list names the five kinds of lymphatic trunks?","a":"Lumbar, intestinal, bronchomediastinal, subclavian, and jugular.","options":["Inguinal, axillary, cervical, thoracic, and cisterna chyli","Femoral, mesenteric, pulmonary, brachial, and jugular","Lumbar, intestinal, thoracic, axillary, and cervical","Lumbar, intestinal, bronchomediastinal, subclavian, and jugular"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Collecting vessels merge into large lymphatic trunks, each draining a major region. There are five kinds: lumbar, intestinal, bronchomediastinal, subclavian, and jugular. All but the intestinal trunk are paired, left and right, which gives nine trunks in all.","notes":["Inguinal, axillary, and cervical are lymph node regions, thoracic names a duct, and the cisterna chyli is a sac. Choose this list for a question about where clinicians feel for swollen nodes.","These names belong to blood vessels and nerves, such as the femoral artery and brachial plexus. Students pick them because they share regional words, but only jugular here is also a lymphatic trunk.","This starts right but swaps in thoracic, axillary, and cervical. Thoracic names a duct, and axillary and cervical name groups of lymph nodes, so pick those for a question about node clusters.","Correct. The five kinds are lumbar, intestinal, bronchomediastinal, subclavian, and jugular."],"lt":"t-lymph-trunks-ducts","lid":"c1","comp":105,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-pathway"},{"id":"L01863","dok":1,"q":"What region do the lumbar trunks drain?","a":"The lower limbs, pelvis, and abdominal wall.","options":["The lower limbs, pelvis, and abdominal wall","The head and neck","The thoracic organs and wall","The upper limbs"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The paired lumbar trunks collect lymph from the lower body: the lower limbs, the pelvis, and the abdominal wall. Both lumbar trunks empty into the thoracic duct at its start, the cisterna chyli. That is why lymph from either leg ends up in the thoracic duct.","notes":["Correct. The lumbar trunks drain the lower limbs, pelvis, and abdominal wall.","The head and neck are drained by the jugular trunks. Choose this region for a question about the jugular trunks.","The thoracic organs and wall are drained by the bronchomediastinal trunks. It fits a question about lymph from the lungs and heart.","The upper limbs are drained by the subclavian trunks. Pick the upper limbs for a question about the subclavian trunks."],"lt":"t-lymph-trunks-ducts","lid":"c2","comp":105,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-pathway"},{"id":"L01864","dok":1,"q":"What region does the intestinal trunk drain?","a":"The digestive organs of the abdomen.","options":["The digestive organs of the abdomen","The thoracic organs and wall","The lower limbs and pelvis","The head and neck"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The intestinal trunk is the only unpaired lymphatic trunk. It collects lymph, including fat-rich chyle, from the digestive organs of the abdomen such as the stomach and intestines. It empties into the thoracic duct at the cisterna chyli.","notes":["Correct. The intestinal trunk drains the digestive organs of the abdomen.","The thoracic organs and wall are drained by the bronchomediastinal trunks. Choose this for a question about lymph from the lungs and heart.","The lower limbs and pelvis are drained by the lumbar trunks. Pick this region for a question about the lumbar trunks.","The head and neck are drained by the jugular trunks. It fits a question about the jugular trunks."],"lt":"t-lymph-trunks-ducts","lid":"c3","comp":105,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-pathway"},{"id":"L01865","dok":1,"q":"What region do the bronchomediastinal trunks drain?","a":"The thoracic organs and wall, including the lungs and heart.","options":["The digestive organs of the abdomen","The upper limbs","The thoracic organs and wall","The lower limbs and pelvis"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The name points to the region: broncho- refers to the airways and lungs, and mediastinal refers to the mediastinum, the central compartment of the thorax that holds the heart. The paired bronchomediastinal trunks drain the thoracic organs and the thoracic wall. The left one empties into the thoracic duct and the right one into the right lymphatic duct.","notes":["The digestive organs of the abdomen are drained by the single intestinal trunk. Pick this region for a question about the intestinal trunk.","The upper limbs are drained by the subclavian trunks. Choose this region for a question about the subclavian trunks.","Correct. The bronchomediastinal trunks drain the thoracic organs and wall.","The lower limbs and pelvis are drained by the lumbar trunks. It fits a question about the lumbar trunks."],"lt":"t-lymph-trunks-ducts","lid":"c4","comp":105,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-pathway"},{"id":"L01866","dok":1,"q":"What region do the subclavian trunks drain?","a":"The upper limbs.","options":["The thoracic organs and wall","The digestive organs of the abdomen","The upper limbs","The head and neck"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The subclavian trunks are named for their position near the subclavian veins, just beneath the clavicles. Each one drains lymph from the upper limb on its side. The left subclavian trunk empties into the thoracic duct and the right one into the right lymphatic duct.","notes":["The thoracic organs and wall are drained by the bronchomediastinal trunks. Choose this region for a question about lymph from the lungs and heart.","The digestive organs of the abdomen are drained by the intestinal trunk. It fits a question about the only unpaired trunk.","Correct. The subclavian trunks drain the upper limbs.","The head and neck are drained by the jugular trunks, which run near the internal jugular veins. Pick this region for a question about the jugular trunks."],"lt":"t-lymph-trunks-ducts","lid":"c5","comp":105,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-pathway"},{"id":"L01867","dok":1,"q":"What region do the jugular trunks drain?","a":"The head and neck.","options":["The upper limbs","The lower limbs and pelvis","The head and neck","The thoracic organs and wall"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The jugular trunks run alongside the internal jugular veins in the neck, and like those veins they drain the head and neck. The left jugular trunk empties into the thoracic duct and the right jugular trunk into the right lymphatic duct. That split is why the right side of the head drains differently from the left.","notes":["The upper limbs are drained by the subclavian trunks. Pick this region for a question about the subclavian trunks.","The lower limbs and pelvis are drained by the lumbar trunks. Choose this region for a question about the lumbar trunks.","Correct. The jugular trunks drain the head and neck.","The thoracic organs and wall are drained by the bronchomediastinal trunks. It fits a question about lymph from the lungs and heart."],"lt":"t-lymph-trunks-ducts","lid":"c6","comp":105,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-pathway"},{"id":"L01868","dok":2,"q":"The cisterna chyli is the sac at the start of the thoracic duct. Which trunks empty into it?","a":"The two lumbar trunks and the intestinal trunk.","options":["The two bronchomediastinal trunks","The left jugular and left subclavian trunks","The two lumbar trunks and the intestinal trunk","The right jugular, right subclavian, and right bronchomediastinal trunks"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The cisterna chyli sits in the abdomen, so it collects lymph from the trunks that drain the lower body. The two lumbar trunks bring lymph from the lower limbs, pelvis, and abdominal wall, and the intestinal trunk brings chyle from the digestive organs. That fat-rich chyle is where the cisterna chyli gets its name.","notes":["The bronchomediastinal trunks drain the thorax and join the ducts up near the neck, far above the abdomen. The left one joins the thoracic duct and the right one the right lymphatic duct.","The left jugular and left subclavian trunks join the thoracic duct near its upper end in the neck, not at the cisterna chyli. Pick them for a question about trunks that join the thoracic duct close to where it empties.","Correct. The lumbar trunks and the intestinal trunk empty into the cisterna chyli.","These three right-side trunks form or empty into the right lymphatic duct. Choose them for a question about the right lymphatic duct."],"lt":"t-lymph-trunks-ducts","lid":"c7","comp":105,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-pathway"},{"id":"L01871","dok":1,"q":"Which regions does the thoracic duct drain?","a":"The upper left side of the body and everything below the diaphragm.","options":["Both upper limbs and the entire head and neck","The upper left side of the body and everything below the diaphragm","Only the lower limbs, pelvis, and abdomen","The right side of the head, neck, and thorax and the right upper limb"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The thoracic duct is the largest lymphatic vessel and drains about three quarters of the body. It receives both lumbar trunks and the intestinal trunk, which bring lymph from everything below the diaphragm, plus the left jugular, left subclavian, and left bronchomediastinal trunks from the upper left side. Only the upper right quarter of the body drains elsewhere, into the right lymphatic duct.","notes":["The right upper limb and the right side of the head and neck drain into the right lymphatic duct, not the thoracic duct. This set splits the body top and bottom instead of right and left.","Correct. The thoracic duct drains the upper left side and everything below the diaphragm.","The lower body is only part of the thoracic duct's territory. It also receives the left jugular, left subclavian, and left bronchomediastinal trunks from the upper left side.","The right side of the head, neck, and thorax and the right upper limb are drained by the right lymphatic duct. Pick this for a question about the right lymphatic duct."],"lt":"t-lymph-trunks-ducts","lid":"c10","comp":105,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-pathway"},{"id":"L01872","dok":1,"q":"Where does the right lymphatic duct empty lymph into the blood?","a":"At the junction of the right subclavian and right internal jugular veins.","options":["Into the superior vena cava","Directly into the right atrium","At the junction of the left subclavian and left internal jugular veins","At the junction of the right subclavian and right internal jugular veins"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Both lymphatic ducts return lymph where a subclavian vein meets an internal jugular vein, low in the neck on each side. The right lymphatic duct is short and empties at the junction on the right side. From there the lymph joins venous blood heading to the heart.","notes":["The superior vena cava receives blood after the brachiocephalic veins join, downstream of where the ducts empty. Choose it for a question about the large vein that returns blood from the upper body to the heart.","The right atrium receives blood from the venae cavae, not directly from any lymphatic vessel. It fits a question about where systemic venous blood enters the heart.","The junction of the left subclavian and left internal jugular veins is where the thoracic duct empties. Pick it for a question about the thoracic duct.","Correct. The right lymphatic duct empties at the junction of the right subclavian and right internal jugular veins."],"lt":"t-lymph-trunks-ducts","lid":"c11","comp":105,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-pathway"},{"id":"L01873","dok":1,"q":"Which three trunks empty into the right lymphatic duct?","a":"The right jugular, right subclavian, and right bronchomediastinal trunks.","options":["The right jugular, right subclavian, and right bronchomediastinal trunks","The right lumbar, right jugular, and right subclavian trunks","The intestinal, right jugular, and right subclavian trunks","The left jugular, left subclavian, and left bronchomediastinal trunks"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The right lymphatic duct drains only the upper right quarter of the body: the right upper limb and the right side of the head, neck, and thorax. The three trunks that serve that region are the right jugular, right subclavian, and right bronchomediastinal trunks. Every other trunk ends in the thoracic duct.","notes":["Correct. The right jugular, right subclavian, and right bronchomediastinal trunks empty into the right lymphatic duct.","The right lumbar trunk drains the right lower limb and empties into the thoracic duct at the cisterna chyli. Both lumbar trunks belong to the thoracic duct, so pick this trunk for a question about the lower body.","The intestinal trunk drains the abdominal digestive organs and empties into the thoracic duct. Students pick it when they forget that everything below the diaphragm drains to the thoracic duct.","The three left-side trunks empty into the thoracic duct. Choose this set for a question about the trunks that join the thoracic duct in the neck."],"lt":"t-lymph-trunks-ducts","lid":"c12","comp":105,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-pathway"},{"id":"L01874","dok":1,"q":"Which is the largest lymphatic vessel in the body?","a":"The thoracic duct.","options":["The intestinal trunk","The right lymphatic duct","The lumbar trunk","The thoracic duct"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The thoracic duct is the largest lymphatic vessel. It begins at the cisterna chyli in the abdomen, runs up through the thorax, and empties at the junction of the left subclavian and left internal jugular veins. It drains about three quarters of the body, far more than the short right lymphatic duct.","notes":["The intestinal trunk drains the abdominal digestive organs and empties into the thoracic duct. It fits a question about the only unpaired trunk.","The right lymphatic duct is short and small, draining only the upper right quarter of the body. Pick it for a question about the duct that drains the right arm.","A lumbar trunk drains one lower limb and the pelvis, and it is smaller than the duct it empties into. Choose it for a question about lymph from the legs.","Correct. The thoracic duct is the largest lymphatic vessel."],"lt":"t-lymph-trunks-ducts","lid":"c13","comp":105,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-pathway"},{"id":"L01875","dok":1,"q":"Lymph has no heart of its own. Which two named pumps help keep it moving?","a":"The skeletal muscle pump and the respiratory pump.","options":["The left ventricle and the right ventricle","The skeletal muscle pump and the lymph node pump","The respiratory pump and the splenic pump","The skeletal muscle pump and the respiratory pump"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The skeletal muscle pump works when contracting muscles squeeze nearby lymphatic vessels. The respiratory pump works through pressure changes in the thorax with each breath. Smooth muscle in larger lymphatic walls and the pulsing of nearby arteries help too, and valves make sure all of this pushes lymph one way, toward the heart.","notes":["The ventricles pump blood, not lymph; the lymphatic system has no pump of its own. Pick the ventricles for a question about which chambers pump blood out of the heart.","The skeletal muscle pump is real, but lymph nodes filter lymph rather than pump it. Pick nodes for a question about where lymph is cleaned.","The respiratory pump is real, but the spleen filters blood and does not pump lymph. Choose the spleen for a question about removing old red blood cells.","Correct. The skeletal muscle pump and the respiratory pump help move lymph."],"lt":"t-lymph-trunks-ducts","lid":"c14","comp":105,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-pathway"},{"id":"L01876","dok":1,"q":"How does the skeletal muscle pump move lymph?","a":"Contracting skeletal muscles squeeze nearby lymphatic vessels and push lymph forward.","options":["Contracting skeletal muscles squeeze nearby lymphatic vessels","Nearby arteries pulse against the lymphatic vessels","Pressure in the thorax changes with each breath","Smooth muscle in the walls of larger lymphatic vessels contracts in rhythm"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"When skeletal muscles contract, they bulge and squeeze the lymphatic vessels running between them. That squeeze pushes lymph along, and the valves make sure it can only move forward toward the heart. This is why movement and exercise speed lymph flow, and why long periods of sitting still let fluid collect in the legs.","notes":["Correct. The skeletal muscle pump works by contracting muscles squeezing lymphatic vessels.","Pulsing of nearby arteries also nudges lymph along, but that push comes from the heartbeat, not from skeletal muscles. Pick it for a question about how arteries next to lymphatic vessels help.","Pressure changes in the thorax with each breath describe the respiratory pump. Pick this for a question about how breathing moves lymph.","Rhythmic contraction of smooth muscle in the walls of larger lymphatic vessels is a real mechanism, but it comes from the vessel itself, not from skeletal muscle. Choose it for a question about how lymphatic vessels help move lymph on their own."],"lt":"t-lymph-trunks-ducts","lid":"c15","comp":105,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-pathway"},{"id":"L01877","dok":1,"q":"How does the respiratory pump move lymph?","a":"Pressure changes in the thorax with each breath move lymph along the vessels.","options":["The lungs pump lymph out through the bronchomediastinal trunks","Contracting leg muscles squeeze the lymphatic vessels","Each breath pushes air into the lymphatic vessels","Pressure changes in the thorax with each breath move lymph upward"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"With each breath, pressure in the thorax rises and falls. During inhalation the pressure in the thorax drops, and lymph is drawn from the abdominal vessels up into the thoracic duct. Valves keep it from sliding back down, so every breath helps move lymph toward the heart.","notes":["The lungs do not pump lymph; the bronchomediastinal trunks simply drain the thoracic organs and wall. Pick those trunks for a question about lymph from the lungs and heart.","Contracting leg muscles squeezing the vessels is the skeletal muscle pump. Choose this for a question about how exercise speeds lymph flow.","Air stays in the airways and never enters lymphatic vessels. The respiratory pump works through pressure changes, not by moving air into the vessels.","Correct. Pressure changes in the thorax with breathing move lymph toward the heart."],"lt":"t-lymph-trunks-ducts","lid":"c16","comp":105,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-pathway"},{"id":"L01878","dok":2,"q":"Which trunk drains lymph from the upper limbs?","a":"The subclavian trunk.","options":["The jugular trunk","The bronchomediastinal trunk","The lumbar trunk","The subclavian trunk"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Each subclavian trunk drains the upper limb on its own side. The name comes from its position near the subclavian vein, just beneath the clavicle at the root of the limb. The left trunk ends in the thoracic duct and the right trunk in the right lymphatic duct.","notes":["The jugular trunk drains the head and neck. Pick it if the question asked about lymph from the face or scalp.","The bronchomediastinal trunk drains the thoracic organs and wall. It fits a question about lymph from the lungs and heart.","The lumbar trunk drains the lower limbs, pelvis, and abdominal wall. Choose it for a question about lymph from the legs.","Correct. The subclavian trunk drains the upper limb."],"lt":"t-lymph-trunks-ducts","lid":"c17","comp":105,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-pathway"},{"id":"L01879","dok":2,"q":"Which trunk drains lymph from the head and neck?","a":"The jugular trunk.","options":["The intestinal trunk","The jugular trunk","The subclavian trunk","The lumbar trunk"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The jugular trunks drain the head and neck, running alongside the internal jugular veins that drain the same region. Each side has its own jugular trunk. The left one ends in the thoracic duct and the right one in the right lymphatic duct.","notes":["The intestinal trunk drains the digestive organs of the abdomen. Choose it for a question about where chyle travels after leaving the intestine.","Correct. The jugular trunk drains the head and neck.","The subclavian trunk drains the upper limb. Pick it for a question about lymph from the arm.","The lumbar trunk drains the lower limbs, pelvis, and abdominal wall. It fits a question about lymph from the legs."],"lt":"t-lymph-trunks-ducts","lid":"c18","comp":105,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-pathway"},{"id":"L01880","dok":2,"q":"Which trunk drains the lower limbs, pelvis, and abdominal wall?","a":"The lumbar trunk.","options":["The subclavian trunk","The lumbar trunk","The bronchomediastinal trunk","The intestinal trunk"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The paired lumbar trunks drain the lower body: the lower limbs, the pelvis, and the abdominal wall. They are named for the lumbar region where they lie. Both empty into the cisterna chyli at the start of the thoracic duct.","notes":["The subclavian trunk drains the upper limb. Choose it for a question about lymph from the arm.","Correct. The lumbar trunk drains the lower limbs, pelvis, and abdominal wall.","The bronchomediastinal trunk drains the thoracic organs and wall. It fits a question about lymph from the lungs and heart.","The intestinal trunk drains the digestive organs of the abdomen, not the limbs or the abdominal wall. Pick it for a question about lymph from the stomach and intestines."],"lt":"t-lymph-trunks-ducts","lid":"c19","comp":105,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-pathway"},{"id":"L01881","dok":2,"q":"Which trunk drains the stomach and intestines?","a":"The intestinal trunk.","options":["The jugular trunk","The intestinal trunk","The bronchomediastinal trunk","The lumbar trunk"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The intestinal trunk drains the digestive organs of the abdomen, including the stomach and intestines. It carries chyle, the fat-rich lymph from the lacteals, and empties into the cisterna chyli. It is the only unpaired lymphatic trunk.","notes":["The jugular trunk drains the head and neck. It fits a question about lymph from the face or scalp.","Correct. The intestinal trunk drains the stomach and intestines.","The bronchomediastinal trunk drains the thoracic organs and wall. Choose it for a question about lymph from the lungs and heart.","The lumbar trunk drains the lower limbs, pelvis, and abdominal wall. Students pick it because it also lies in the abdomen, but it does not drain the digestive organs."],"lt":"t-lymph-trunks-ducts","lid":"c20","comp":105,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-pathway"},{"id":"L01882","dok":2,"q":"Which trunk drains the thoracic wall, lungs, and heart?","a":"The bronchomediastinal trunk.","options":["The lumbar trunk","The subclavian trunk","The bronchomediastinal trunk","The intestinal trunk"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The bronchomediastinal trunks drain the thoracic organs and the thoracic wall. Broncho- points to the lungs and airways, and mediastinal points to the mediastinum, where the heart sits. The left trunk ends in the thoracic duct and the right trunk in the right lymphatic duct.","notes":["The lumbar trunk drains the lower limbs, pelvis, and abdominal wall. It fits a question about lymph from the legs.","The subclavian trunk drains the upper limb. Pick it for a question about lymph from the arm.","Correct. The bronchomediastinal trunk drains the thoracic wall, lungs, and heart.","The intestinal trunk drains the digestive organs of the abdomen. Choose it for a question about where chyle goes."],"lt":"t-lymph-trunks-ducts","lid":"c21","comp":105,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-pathway"},{"id":"L01883","dok":2,"q":"Which vessel receives the right jugular, right subclavian, and right bronchomediastinal trunks?","a":"The right lymphatic duct.","options":["The intestinal trunk","The thoracic duct","The right lymphatic duct","The cisterna chyli"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The right lymphatic duct collects lymph from the three trunks on the upper right side of the body. Together they drain the right upper limb and the right side of the head, neck, and thorax. The short duct then empties at the junction of the right subclavian and right internal jugular veins.","notes":["The intestinal trunk is itself a trunk; it drains the abdominal digestive organs and does not receive other trunks. It fits a question about lymph from the stomach and intestines.","The thoracic duct receives the matching left-side trunks plus both lumbar trunks and the intestinal trunk. Pick it if the question listed the left jugular, left subclavian, and left bronchomediastinal trunks.","Correct. The right lymphatic duct receives the three right-side upper trunks.","The cisterna chyli is the sac at the start of the thoracic duct, receiving the lumbar and intestinal trunks. Choose it for a question about where lymph from the lower body first collects."],"lt":"t-lymph-trunks-ducts","lid":"c22","comp":105,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-pathway"},{"id":"L01884","dok":2,"q":"Where does the cisterna chyli lie?","a":"In the abdomen, in front of the upper lumbar vertebrae.","options":["In the mediastinum, behind the sternum","In the upper left abdomen, behind the stomach","In the abdomen, in front of the upper lumbar vertebrae","In the neck, where the subclavian and internal jugular veins meet"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The cisterna chyli is a sac in the abdomen that sits in front of the upper lumbar vertebrae, deep against the posterior body wall. It is the starting point of the thoracic duct and receives the lumbar trunks and the intestinal trunk. Its name refers to the chyle that drains into it from the intestine.","notes":["The mediastinum behind the sternum is where the thymus lies. Pick it for a question about where T cells mature.","The upper left abdomen behind the stomach is the location of the spleen. Choose it for a question about the largest lymphatic organ.","Correct. The cisterna chyli lies in the abdomen in front of the upper lumbar vertebrae.","The junction of the subclavian and internal jugular veins is where the lymphatic ducts empty, at the end of the route. It fits a question about where lymph returns to the blood."],"lt":"t-lymph-trunks-ducts","lid":"c23","comp":105,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-pathway"},{"id":"L01885","dok":2,"q":"The thoracic duct begins as which structure?","a":"The cisterna chyli.","options":["The left jugular trunk","A lacteal","The cisterna chyli","The right lymphatic duct"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The thoracic duct begins in the abdomen as the cisterna chyli, a dilated sac that receives the lumbar trunks and the intestinal trunk. From there the duct runs up through the thorax and ends at the junction of the left subclavian and left internal jugular veins. It is the largest lymphatic vessel in the body.","notes":["The left jugular trunk joins the thoracic duct near its upper end in the neck, not at its start. It fits a question about lymph from the left side of the head.","A lacteal is a lymphatic capillary in an intestinal villus, at the very start of the route for chyle. Choose it for a question about where dietary fat enters the lymph.","Correct. The thoracic duct begins as the cisterna chyli.","The right lymphatic duct is the separate, short duct that drains the upper right quarter of the body. It never joins the thoracic duct, so pick it for a question about the right arm's lymph."],"lt":"t-lymph-trunks-ducts","lid":"c24","comp":105,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-pathway"},{"id":"L01888","dok":3,"q":"A patient has an infected cut on the right hand. Lymph from the right upper limb reaches the blood through which duct?","a":"The right lymphatic duct.","options":["The right lumbar trunk","The right lymphatic duct","The cisterna chyli","The thoracic duct"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Lymph from the right upper limb travels in the right subclavian trunk. That trunk empties into the right lymphatic duct, which drains the upper right quarter of the body. The duct then returns the lymph to the blood at the junction of the right subclavian and right internal jugular veins.","notes":["The right lumbar trunk drains the right lower limb and pelvis. It fits a question about an infection in the right foot.","Correct. Lymph from the right upper limb returns through the right lymphatic duct.","The cisterna chyli collects lymph from the lower body and abdominal organs, not from an upper limb. Choose it for a question about where the lumbar and intestinal trunks meet.","The thoracic duct drains the left upper limb and everything below the diaphragm, but not the right arm. Pick it for an infection in the left hand or either leg."],"lt":"t-lymph-trunks-ducts","lid":"c27","comp":105,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-pathway"},{"id":"L01889","dok":3,"q":"Lymph from the left leg reaches the bloodstream through which duct?","a":"The thoracic duct.","options":["The intestinal trunk","The right lymphatic duct","The left lumbar trunk","The thoracic duct"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Lymph from the left leg travels in the left lumbar trunk to the cisterna chyli, the start of the thoracic duct. The thoracic duct carries it up through the thorax and empties at the junction of the left subclavian and left internal jugular veins. Both legs drain this way, since everything below the diaphragm belongs to the thoracic duct.","notes":["The intestinal trunk drains the abdominal digestive organs, not the legs. It fits a question about lymph from the stomach and intestines.","The right lymphatic duct drains only the right upper limb and the right side of the head, neck, and thorax. Pick it for lymph from the right arm.","The left lumbar trunk does carry this lymph, but it is a trunk, not a duct, and it empties into the thoracic duct. Choose it if the question asked which trunk drains the left leg.","Correct. Lymph from the left leg returns through the thoracic duct."],"lt":"t-lymph-trunks-ducts","lid":"c28","comp":105,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-pathway"},{"id":"L01890","dok":3,"q":"After a meal, lymph draining from the stomach and small intestine travels through which trunk?","a":"The intestinal trunk.","options":["The intestinal trunk","The lumbar trunk","The bronchomediastinal trunk","The subclavian trunk"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The intestinal trunk drains the digestive organs of the abdomen, so lymph from the stomach and small intestine passes through it. After a meal that lymph includes chyle from the lacteals. The intestinal trunk empties into the cisterna chyli at the start of the thoracic duct.","notes":["Correct. Lymph from the stomach and small intestine travels through the intestinal trunk.","The lumbar trunk drains the lower limbs, pelvis, and abdominal wall. Students choose it because it also lies in the abdomen, but it does not drain the digestive organs.","The bronchomediastinal trunk drains the thoracic organs and wall. Pick it for lymph from the lungs and heart.","The subclavian trunk drains the upper limb. Choose it for lymph from the arm."],"lt":"t-lymph-trunks-ducts","lid":"c29","comp":105,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-pathway"},{"id":"L01891","dok":3,"q":"A tumor blocks the thoracic duct at its end, where it joins the veins in the left side of the neck. Lymph from which region would still return to the blood normally?","a":"The right side of the head and neck.","options":["The left upper limb","The left leg","The abdominal organs","The right side of the head and neck"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Lymph from the right side of the head and neck travels in the right jugular trunk to the right lymphatic duct, which is a separate vessel that empties on the right side of the neck. A block at the end of the thoracic duct does not touch that route. Every other region listed sends its lymph through the thoracic duct, so it would back up behind the tumor.","notes":["The left upper limb drains through the left subclavian trunk, which joins the thoracic duct before it empties. With the block at the duct's end, this lymph would back up too.","The left leg drains through the left lumbar trunk into the cisterna chyli and up the thoracic duct. Its lymph would be blocked, so pick it for a question about a region drained by the thoracic duct.","The abdominal organs drain through the intestinal trunk into the cisterna chyli and the thoracic duct. They would be affected, so choose them for a question about where chyle travels.","Correct. The right side of the head and neck drains through the right lymphatic duct and is unaffected."],"lt":"t-lymph-trunks-ducts","lid":"c30","comp":105,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-pathway"},{"id":"L01892","dok":3,"q":"Lymph from the right side of the scalp enters which trunk before reaching its duct?","a":"The right jugular trunk.","options":["The left jugular trunk","The right jugular trunk","The right subclavian trunk","The right bronchomediastinal trunk"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The scalp is part of the head, and the head and neck are drained by the jugular trunks. Because the scalp region is on the right, its lymph enters the right jugular trunk. That trunk then empties into the right lymphatic duct.","notes":["The left jugular trunk drains the left side of the head and neck and empties into the thoracic duct. It fits lymph from the left side of the scalp.","Correct. Lymph from the right side of the head enters the right jugular trunk.","The right subclavian trunk drains the right upper limb. Pick it for lymph from the right hand or arm.","The right bronchomediastinal trunk drains the right side of the thoracic organs and wall. Choose it for lymph from the right lung."],"lt":"t-lymph-trunks-ducts","lid":"c31","comp":105,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-pathway"},{"id":"L01893","dok":2,"q":"Which of these is a lymphatic duct rather than a trunk?","a":"The right lymphatic duct.","options":["The jugular trunk","The lumbar trunk","The subclavian trunk","The right lymphatic duct"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"There are only two lymphatic ducts, the thoracic duct and the right lymphatic duct. They are the last vessels on the lymph route, receiving lymph from the trunks and emptying it into the veins of the neck. The trunks come one step earlier, each draining one major region.","notes":["The jugular trunk is a trunk that drains the head and neck. Choose it for a question about lymph from the face or scalp.","The lumbar trunk is a trunk that drains the lower limbs, pelvis, and abdominal wall. It fits a question about lymph from the legs.","The subclavian trunk is a trunk that drains the upper limb. It would be the answer for a question about which trunk drains the arm.","Correct. The right lymphatic duct is one of the two lymphatic ducts."],"lt":"t-lymph-trunks-ducts","lid":"c32","comp":105,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-pathway"},{"id":"L01894","dok":2,"q":"Which region does the thoracic duct NOT drain?","a":"The right upper limb.","options":["Everything below the diaphragm","The right upper limb","The left upper limb","The left side of the head and neck"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The thoracic duct drains about three quarters of the body: everything below the diaphragm plus the upper left side. The upper right quarter, which includes the right upper limb and the right side of the head, neck, and thorax, drains into the right lymphatic duct instead. So the right arm is the region outside the thoracic duct's territory.","notes":["Everything below the diaphragm drains into the thoracic duct through the lumbar and intestinal trunks. It fits a question about the cisterna chyli.","Correct. The right upper limb drains into the right lymphatic duct, not the thoracic duct.","The left upper limb drains through the left subclavian trunk into the thoracic duct. It would be the answer for a question about a region the thoracic duct does drain.","The left side of the head and neck drains through the left jugular trunk into the thoracic duct. Choose it for a question about lymph that enters the thoracic duct in the neck."],"lt":"t-lymph-trunks-ducts","lid":"c33","comp":105,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-pathway"},{"id":"L01895","dok":1,"q":"Where do the lymphatic ducts return lymph to the bloodstream?","a":"At the junctions of the subclavian and internal jugular veins.","options":["Into the inferior vena cava in the abdomen","Directly into the right atrium of the heart","Where the subclavian and internal jugular veins meet","Into the hepatic portal vein near the liver"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The last step on the lymph route is the return to the blood. Each lymphatic duct empties where a subclavian vein meets an internal jugular vein, low in the neck: the thoracic duct on the left and the right lymphatic duct on the right. From there the lymph travels with venous blood to the heart.","notes":["The inferior vena cava returns blood from the lower body, but the lymphatic ducts do not empty into it. Pick it for a question about where blood from the legs returns.","The right atrium receives blood from the venae cavae and coronary sinus, not directly from lymphatic vessels. Choose it for a question about where systemic venous blood enters the heart.","Correct. The ducts empty at the junctions of the subclavian and internal jugular veins.","The hepatic portal vein carries blood from the digestive organs to the liver. It fits a question about the portal system, not the lymph route."],"lt":"t-lymph-trunks-ducts","lid":"c34","comp":105,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-pathway"},{"id":"L01896","dok":3,"q":"During a workout, a weightlifter's repeated muscle contractions speed lymph return from the limbs. Which mechanism is at work?","a":"The skeletal muscle pump.","options":["The respiratory pump","The skeletal muscle pump","Smooth muscle in the lymphatic vessel walls","Pulsing of nearby arteries"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Each time a skeletal muscle contracts, it bulges and squeezes the lymphatic vessels around it, pushing lymph forward. The valves in the vessels keep it from flowing back when the muscle relaxes. Repeated contractions, as in lifting or walking, act like a series of squeezes that speed lymph toward the heart.","notes":["The respiratory pump works through pressure changes in the thorax with each breath, not through the squeeze of working limb muscles. Pick it for a question about how breathing moves lymph.","Correct. Contracting skeletal muscles squeezing the vessels is the skeletal muscle pump.","Smooth muscle in the walls of larger lymphatic vessels contracts in its own rhythm, so it does not depend on the weightlifter's muscle contractions. Pick it for a question about how the vessels themselves help move lymph.","Pulsing of nearby arteries does help move lymph, but that push comes from the heartbeat, not from the weightlifter's skeletal muscles. Choose it for a question about how arteries next to lymphatic vessels help."],"lt":"t-lymph-trunks-ducts","lid":"c35","comp":105,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-pathway"},{"id":"L01897","dok":2,"q":"About how much of the body does the thoracic duct drain?","a":"About three quarters.","options":["About half","About three quarters","All of it","About one quarter"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The thoracic duct drains everything below the diaphragm plus the upper left side of the body, which adds up to about three quarters. The remaining quarter, the right upper limb and the right side of the head, neck, and thorax, drains into the right lymphatic duct. That uneven split is why the thoracic duct is the largest lymphatic vessel.","notes":["Students choose half when they picture the body split evenly into right and left sides. The split is uneven, because the thoracic duct takes the whole lower body from both sides.","Correct. The thoracic duct drains about three quarters of the body.","The right upper limb and the right side of the head, neck, and thorax drain into the right lymphatic duct, so the thoracic duct does not drain all of the body.","About one quarter is the share drained by the right lymphatic duct, the upper right quarter of the body. Pick it for a question about the right lymphatic duct."],"lt":"t-lymph-trunks-ducts","lid":"c36","comp":105,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-pathway"},{"id":"L01899","dok":3,"q":"Lymph from the left lung passes through which trunk on its way to a duct?","a":"The left bronchomediastinal trunk.","options":["The intestinal trunk","The left jugular trunk","The left subclavian trunk","The left bronchomediastinal trunk"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The lungs are thoracic organs, and the bronchomediastinal trunks drain the thoracic organs and wall. Lymph from the left lung therefore enters the left bronchomediastinal trunk. That trunk empties into the thoracic duct.","notes":["The intestinal trunk drains the digestive organs of the abdomen. It fits a question about lymph from the stomach and intestines.","The left jugular trunk drains the left side of the head and neck. Choose it for lymph from the left side of the face or scalp.","The left subclavian trunk drains the left upper limb. Pick it for lymph from the left arm.","Correct. Lymph from the left lung passes through the left bronchomediastinal trunk."],"lt":"t-lymph-trunks-ducts","lid":"c38","comp":105,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-pathway"},{"id":"L01900","dok":2,"q":"Which structure is a dilated sac rather than a duct or a trunk?","a":"The cisterna chyli.","options":["The right lymphatic duct","The thoracic duct","The jugular trunk","The cisterna chyli"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The cisterna chyli is an enlarged, sac-like start to the thoracic duct, lying in the abdomen in front of the upper lumbar vertebrae. Cisterna means a reservoir, and chyli refers to chyle, the fat-rich lymph from the intestine that drains into it. The lumbar trunks and the intestinal trunk empty into this sac.","notes":["The right lymphatic duct is the short duct that drains the upper right quarter of the body. Choose it for a question about lymph from the right arm.","The thoracic duct is the largest lymphatic duct; the cisterna chyli is the sac at its lower end. Pick the thoracic duct for a question about the vessel that drains three quarters of the body.","The jugular trunk is a trunk that drains the head and neck. It fits a question about lymph from the face or scalp.","Correct. The cisterna chyli is a dilated sac."],"lt":"t-lymph-trunks-ducts","lid":"c39","comp":105,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-pathway"},{"id":"L01901","dok":3,"q":"Lymph from the left foot passes through a lumbar trunk, the cisterna chyli, the thoracic duct, and a subclavian vein. Which sequence is correct?","a":"Lumbar trunk, cisterna chyli, thoracic duct, subclavian vein.","options":["Thoracic duct, cisterna chyli, lumbar trunk, subclavian vein","Lumbar trunk, thoracic duct, cisterna chyli, subclavian vein","Cisterna chyli, lumbar trunk, thoracic duct, subclavian vein","Lumbar trunk, cisterna chyli, thoracic duct, subclavian vein"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Lymph from the foot first reaches the left lumbar trunk, which drains the lower limb. The lumbar trunk empties into the cisterna chyli, the sac at the start of the thoracic duct. The thoracic duct then carries the lymph up through the thorax to the junction of the left subclavian and left internal jugular veins.","notes":["This starts at the thoracic duct, the largest and last lymphatic vessel. Lymph moves from smaller to larger vessels, so the trunk comes before the duct.","This puts the cisterna chyli after the thoracic duct, but the cisterna chyli is the start of the duct, not something beyond it.","The cisterna chyli receives the lumbar trunk, so the trunk has to come first. Lymph from the limb reaches its trunk before it reaches the sac.","Correct. Lumbar trunk, cisterna chyli, thoracic duct, then subclavian vein."],"lt":"t-lymph-trunks-ducts","lid":"c40","comp":105,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-pathway"},{"id":"L01902","dok":2,"q":"Lymph has no pump of its own. Which of these is NOT one of the ways lymph keeps moving?","a":"Direct pumping by the left ventricle.","options":["Direct pumping by the left ventricle","Rhythmic contraction of smooth muscle in larger lymphatic walls","Contracting skeletal muscles squeezing the vessels","Pressure changes in the thorax with each breath"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Several forces work together to move lymph: skeletal muscles squeeze the vessels, pressure in the thorax changes with each breath, smooth muscle in the walls of larger lymphatic vessels contracts in rhythm, and nearby arteries pulse against the vessels. Valves keep all of it moving one way, toward the heart. The left ventricle pumps blood into the aorta and has no connection to lymphatic vessels.","notes":["Correct. The left ventricle pumps blood, not lymph; the lymphatic system has no pump of its own.","Rhythmic contraction of smooth muscle in larger lymphatic walls is a real way lymph moves. It would be the answer for a question about how the vessels themselves help.","Contracting skeletal muscles squeezing the vessels is the skeletal muscle pump, a real way lymph moves. It would be the answer for a question about how exercise speeds lymph flow.","Pressure changes in the thorax with breathing are the respiratory pump, a real way lymph moves. It would be the answer for a question about how breathing helps."],"lt":"t-lymph-trunks-ducts","lid":"c41","comp":105,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-pathway"},{"id":"N-m4-lymph-vessels-1","dok":3,"q":"Months after surgery that removed many lymph nodes from her armpit, a patient has long-lasting swelling of that arm. Which disorder is most likely?","a":"Lymphedema.","options":["Varicose veins","Lymphedema","Lymphoma","Lymphadenitis"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Lymphedema is long-term swelling caused by blocked or missing lymphatic vessels, so fluid builds up in the tissues instead of draining away. Removing many axillary lymph nodes also removes the lymphatic vessels that pass through them. The arm then cannot drain its interstitial fluid well, and it stays swollen.","notes":["Varicose veins are twisted superficial veins from failed valves, most often in the legs. They fit a question about visibly bulging leg veins, not arm swelling after node removal.","Correct. Long-lasting swelling after lymph node removal is lymphedema.","Lymphoma is cancer of the lymphocytes in lymphatic tissue. Choose it for a question about a cancer arising in lymph nodes or other lymphatic tissue.","Lymphadenitis is swollen, tender lymph nodes that are fighting an infection. Pick it for tender nodes during an infection, not for swelling after the nodes were removed."],"comp":108,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-disorders"},{"id":"N-m4-lymph-vessels-2","dok":1,"q":"Which statement describes the direction lymph flows?","a":"One way only, from the tissues toward the heart.","options":["Outward from the heart to the tissues","Back and forth in the vessels with each breath","In a closed loop from the heart to the tissues and back","One way only, from the tissues toward the heart"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The lymphatic system is a one-way drainage network. Lymph starts in blind-ended capillaries in the tissues, moves through larger and larger vessels, and ends where the ducts empty into the subclavian veins near the heart. Valves along the way keep it from flowing backward.","notes":["Flow outward from the heart describes arteries. Lymph moves the opposite way, returning fluid to the blood.","Breathing helps push lymph forward through the respiratory pump, but valves stop it from moving backward. Students pick this when they confuse the pump with the direction of flow.","A closed loop from the heart and back describes blood in the cardiovascular system. The lymphatic system has no loop because its capillaries begin as dead ends.","Correct. Lymph flows one way, from the tissues toward the heart."],"comp":105,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-pathway"},{"id":"N-m4-lymph-vessels-3","dok":2,"q":"Where do the superficial lymphatic collecting vessels run?","a":"In the subcutaneous layer, alongside the superficial veins.","options":["Inside the epidermis","Alongside the deep arteries, between the muscles","In the subcutaneous layer, alongside the superficial veins","Inside the lumen of the veins"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Collecting vessels travel in two sets. The superficial ones run in the subcutaneous layer next to the superficial veins, while the deep ones run with the deep arteries. Both sets have valves and pass through lymph nodes along the way.","notes":["The epidermis is avascular and has no lymphatic vessels at all. Choose it for a question about a tissue without lymphatic capillaries.","Running with the deep arteries describes the deep collecting vessels. Pick this for a question about lymphatic vessels that drain muscles and deeper organs.","Correct. Superficial collecting vessels run in the subcutaneous layer with the superficial veins.","Lymphatic vessels are separate tubes that run beside veins, not inside them. Lymph enters a vein only at the end of the route, where the ducts empty in the neck."],"comp":106,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-vessels-node"},{"id":"L02061","dok":1,"q":"What is interstitial fluid?","a":"The fluid in the spaces between cells, filtered out of the blood capillaries.","options":["The milky, fat-rich fluid that drains from the lacteals of the small intestine","The fluid in the spaces between cells, filtered out of the blood capillaries","The fluid inside a lymphatic vessel after it has been collected from the tissues","The liquid part of blood that stays inside the blood vessels"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Interstitial fluid is the fluid that bathes the cells, sitting in the tissue spaces between them. It forms when fluid filters out of the blood capillaries. The blood capillaries take most of it back, and the lymphatic system collects the rest. Once that fluid enters a lymphatic vessel, its name changes to lymph.","notes":["This describes chyle, the lymph that turns milky after the lacteals absorb dietary fat. Pick chyle if the question asked about the lymph draining from the small intestine after a meal.","Correct. Interstitial fluid fills the spaces between cells and comes from fluid that leaves the blood capillaries.","This describes lymph, which is the same fluid after it has entered a lymphatic capillary. Pick lymph if the question asked what interstitial fluid is called once it is inside the lymphatic vessels.","This describes plasma, the liquid matrix of blood that stays inside the vessels. Pick plasma if the question asked about the fluid portion of whole blood."],"lt":"t-lymphatic-system","lid":"c2","comp":105,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-pathway"},{"id":"L02062","dok":1,"q":"In which direction does the lymphatic system move lymph?","a":"In one direction only, toward the heart.","options":["Outward from the heart to the tissues","Back and forth with each heartbeat","In a continuous loop, like the blood","In one direction only, toward the heart"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The lymphatic system is a one-way drainage network rather than a circuit. It begins with dead-end lymphatic capillaries in the tissues and carries lymph through larger and larger vessels until the ducts empty it into the veins near the heart. Valves along the vessels keep lymph from flowing backward.","notes":["This is the direction arteries carry blood, away from the heart and out to the tissues. Lymph moves the opposite way, from the tissues back toward the heart.","This mixes up lymph with the pulsing of arterial blood. The heart does not pump lymph, and valves in the lymphatic vessels stop any backward flow.","The blood travels in a closed loop, leaving the heart in arteries and returning in veins. The lymphatic system is not a loop, because its capillaries start blind in the tissues and it only drains toward the heart.","Correct. Lymph flows one way, from the tissues toward the heart, and valves keep it from backing up."],"lt":"t-lymphatic-system","lid":"c3","comp":105,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-pathway"},{"id":"L02063","dok":1,"q":"Which lymphatic duct drains most of the body?","a":"The thoracic duct.","options":["The cisterna chyli","The right lymphatic duct","The thoracic duct","The cystic duct"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The thoracic duct is the largest lymphatic vessel and drains about three quarters of the body: both lower limbs, the abdomen, the left arm, and the left side of the head, neck, and thorax. It begins at the cisterna chyli in the abdomen, runs up through the thorax, and empties where the left subclavian and left internal jugular veins meet.","notes":["The cisterna chyli is the sac in the abdomen where the thoracic duct begins. It collects lymph from the lumbar and intestinal trunks, but it is the starting point of the duct, not the duct itself.","The right lymphatic duct is short and small and drains only the right arm and the right side of the head, neck, and thorax. Pick it if the question asked which duct drains the upper right part of the body.","Correct. The thoracic duct drains everything except the upper right part of the body.","The cystic duct belongs to the digestive system and connects the gallbladder to the common bile duct. It is a look-alike name and would be the answer for a question about the bile pathway."],"lt":"t-lymphatic-system","lid":"c5","comp":105,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-pathway"},{"id":"L02064","dok":1,"q":"Which region of the body does the right lymphatic duct drain?","a":"The right arm and the right side of the head, neck, and thorax.","options":["The left arm and the left side of the head and neck","The right arm and the right side of the head, neck, and thorax","Everything below the diaphragm on both sides","The lower limbs and the abdomen"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The right lymphatic duct is a short vessel that receives the right jugular, right subclavian, and right bronchomediastinal trunks. Those trunks drain the right side of the head and neck, the right arm, and the right side of the thorax, so the duct covers only the upper right part of the body. It empties where the right subclavian and right internal jugular veins meet.","notes":["The left arm and the left side of the head and neck drain through the left subclavian and left jugular trunks into the thoracic duct. This is the mirror-image region of the correct answer.","Correct. The right lymphatic duct drains only the upper right part of the body.","Everything below the diaphragm drains into the thoracic duct by way of the cisterna chyli. This region belongs to the thoracic duct, not the right lymphatic duct.","The lower limbs and abdomen drain through the lumbar and intestinal trunks into the cisterna chyli and then the thoracic duct. Pick this region if the question asked what the thoracic duct receives from below the diaphragm."],"lt":"t-lymphatic-system","lid":"c6","comp":105,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-pathway"},{"id":"L02073","dok":1,"q":"What is the smallest type of lymphatic vessel?","a":"The lymphatic capillary.","options":["The lymphatic trunk","The thoracic duct","The lymphatic capillary","The collecting vessel"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Lymphatic capillaries are the smallest lymphatic vessels and the starting point of the whole system. They are dead-end tubes woven between the blood capillaries, and their overlapping endothelial cells let excess interstitial fluid in. From there lymph moves into progressively larger vessels on its way back to the blood.","notes":["Lymphatic trunks are large vessels formed when collecting vessels merge, each draining a major body region. They are near the end of the pathway, just before the ducts.","The thoracic duct is the largest lymphatic vessel in the body. Pick it if the question asked for the biggest lymphatic vessel rather than the smallest.","Correct. Lymphatic capillaries are the smallest vessels and where lymph first forms.","Collecting vessels are the next step after the capillaries. They have valves and pass through lymph nodes, so pick them if the question asked where lymph goes after the capillaries."],"lt":"t-lymphatic-system","lid":"c15","comp":106,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-vessels-node"},{"id":"L02074","dok":2,"q":"Lymph leaves the tissue spaces in the lymphatic capillaries. Which vessels does it enter next?","a":"The lymphatic collecting vessels.","options":["The lymphatic ducts","The lymphatic collecting vessels","The lymphatic trunks","The subclavian veins"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The pathway of lymph runs from lymphatic capillaries to collecting vessels, through lymph nodes, into lymphatic trunks, then into a lymphatic duct, and finally into a subclavian vein. Collecting vessels come right after the capillaries. They are built like thin veins with even more valves, and they carry lymph to the nodes for filtering.","notes":["The lymphatic ducts are the last lymphatic vessels in the pathway, receiving lymph from the trunks. Pick them if the question asked where the trunks empty.","Correct. Collecting vessels receive lymph directly from the lymphatic capillaries.","The lymphatic trunks form when collecting vessels merge after passing through nodes. They come two steps later than the capillaries, so pick them for a question about what the collecting vessels drain into.","The subclavian veins are where lymph finally rejoins the blood, at their junctions with the internal jugular veins. They are the end of the pathway, not the step after the capillaries."],"lt":"t-lymphatic-system","lid":"c16","comp":105,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-pathway"},{"id":"L02077","dok":2,"q":"The lymphatic system has no pump of its own. What mainly moves lymph along the vessels?","a":"The squeeze of skeletal muscles and the pressure changes of breathing.","options":["Gravity pulling lymph down toward the feet","Cilia on the vessel walls sweeping it along","Contractions of the heart pushing it forward","Skeletal muscle squeeze and breathing movements"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Lymph is moved by forces outside the vessels. Contracting skeletal muscles squeeze nearby lymphatic vessels (the skeletal muscle pump), and pressure changes in the thorax with each breath pull lymph upward (the respiratory pump). Smooth muscle in larger vessel walls and the pulsing of nearby arteries help, and valves keep all of this flow moving one way toward the heart.","notes":["Gravity would pull lymph away from the heart in the legs, which is the opposite of the direction it needs to travel. Lymph moves upward against gravity because muscle squeeze and valves push it.","Cilia are found on the lining of the airways, where they sweep mucus. Lymphatic vessels are lined by endothelium without cilia, so this mixes up the respiratory lining with the lymphatic wall.","The heart pumps blood, not lymph. Lymph only reaches the heart after the ducts empty into the veins, so heart contractions are not what push lymph through the lymphatic vessels.","Correct. The skeletal muscle pump and the respiratory pump are the main forces that move lymph."],"lt":"t-lymphatic-system","lid":"c19","comp":105,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-pathway"},{"id":"L02080","dok":2,"q":"How do the walls of a lymphatic capillary let fluid in but not back out?","a":"Overlapping endothelial cells act as one-way flap valves.","options":["Overlapping endothelial cells act as one-way flap valves","Tight junctions seal the cells completely together","Anchoring filaments squeeze the capillary shut after each beat","Smooth muscle in the wall pumps fluid inward"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The endothelial cells of a lymphatic capillary overlap loosely at their edges like shingles. When fluid pressure outside is higher, the edges push inward and fluid enters, and when pressure inside rises, the edges press shut. This flap-valve arrangement lets in fluid, proteins, cells, and even pathogens, but keeps them from leaking back out.","notes":["Correct. The overlapping endothelial cells work as one-way flap valves.","Tight junctions would seal the wall and keep most material out. Blood capillary cells are joined more tightly than lymphatic ones, so this describes the opposite of how a lymphatic capillary works.","Anchoring filaments tie the lymphatic capillary to the surrounding tissue and hold it open, so they help fluid enter rather than closing the vessel. Pick them if the question asked what keeps a lymphatic capillary from collapsing.","Lymphatic capillaries are a single layer of endothelium with no smooth muscle. Smooth muscle appears only in the walls of larger lymphatic vessels, where it helps move lymph along."],"lt":"t-lymphatic-system","lid":"c22","comp":106,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-vessels-node"},{"id":"L02082","dok":2,"q":"Which sac in the abdomen marks the beginning of the thoracic duct?","a":"The cisterna chyli.","options":["The subcapsular sinus","The cisterna chyli","A lacteal","The right lymphatic duct"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The cisterna chyli is an enlarged sac in the abdomen that collects lymph from the lumbar and intestinal trunks. The thoracic duct begins at this sac and runs upward through the thorax. Its name refers to chyle, the milky, fat-rich lymph that drains into it from the intestine.","notes":["The subcapsular sinus is the space just under the capsule of a lymph node where lymph first enters. Pick it if the question asked where lymph goes after the afferent vessels.","Correct. The thoracic duct begins at the cisterna chyli.","A lacteal is a lymphatic capillary in an intestinal villus that absorbs dietary fat. Lacteals feed chyle toward the cisterna chyli, but they are tiny vessels, not a large sac.","The right lymphatic duct is the smaller of the two ducts, starting in the right side of the neck and thorax. It has no abdominal sac and drains only the upper right part of the body."],"lt":"t-lymphatic-system","lid":"c24","comp":105,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-pathway"},{"id":"L02084","dok":3,"q":"After surgery that removes many lymph nodes from the armpit, a patient develops long-term swelling of that arm. Which disorder is this?","a":"Lymphedema.","options":["Lymphedema","Tonsillitis","Splenomegaly","Lymphadenopathy"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Lymphedema is long-term swelling caused by blocked or missing lymphatic vessels. When many nodes and the vessels running through them are removed, fluid from that limb can no longer drain well, so interstitial fluid builds up. The swelling lasts because the drainage pathway itself has been lost.","notes":["Correct. Lymphedema is long-term limb swelling after lymphatic drainage is blocked or removed.","Tonsillitis is infection and inflammation of the tonsils, common in childhood. Pick it for a question about a sore, swollen throat with enlarged tonsils.","Splenomegaly is an enlarged spleen, seen in mononucleosis and leukemia. Pick it for a question about swelling of the organ in the upper left abdomen.","Lymphadenopathy means enlargement of the lymph nodes themselves. Here the nodes were removed, and the swelling is in the whole limb, so the problem is drainage, not node enlargement."],"lt":"t-lymphatic-system","lid":"c26","comp":108,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-disorders"},{"id":"L02088","dok":2,"q":"Tissue swells because fluid leaks out of the blood capillaries faster than the lymphatic vessels can drain it. Which condition is this?","a":"Edema.","options":["Lymphoma","Lymphadenopathy","Tonsillitis","Edema"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Edema is swelling of the interstitial space, the tissue spaces between cells. It happens when fluid leaves the blood capillaries faster than the lymphatics can return it. The lymphatics normally carry about 3 liters of leaked fluid back to the blood each day, so any imbalance shows up as swelling.","notes":["Lymphoma is a cancer of lymphocytes in lymphatic tissue. Pick it for a question about Hodgkin or non-Hodgkin disease.","Lymphadenopathy is enlargement of the lymph nodes. Pick it if the question described swollen nodes rather than swollen tissue.","Tonsillitis is inflammation of infected tonsils. It causes a swollen throat, but it is an infection of lymphatic tissue rather than general fluid buildup.","Correct. Edema is swelling of the interstitial spaces when drainage cannot keep up."],"lt":"t-lymphatic-system","lid":"c30","comp":108,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-disorders"},{"id":"L02089","dok":3,"q":"Lymphatic capillaries have overlapping cells that act as one-way flaps. What does this one-way design accomplish?","a":"Excess tissue fluid can enter the capillary but cannot leak back into the tissue spaces.","options":["Only water and small ions are allowed in","Lymph can flow back and forth between nodes","Fluid is actively pumped into the capillary","Tissue fluid can enter but cannot leak back out"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The overlapping cell edges open inward when fluid builds up in the tissue spaces and press closed when pressure inside the capillary rises. That arrangement lets interstitial fluid, proteins, and even cells enter, while stopping them from flowing back out. Once inside, the fluid is lymph and can only move onward toward the heart.","notes":["Lymphatic capillaries are more permeable than blood capillaries, so they take in proteins, cells, pathogens, and even cancer cells, not only small molecules. Students sometimes mix this up with blood capillaries, which mostly pass small molecules.","Lymph never flows back and forth. The flap valves and the valves of the collecting vessels keep it moving only one way, toward the heart.","The capillary wall does not pump. Fluid enters passively when pressure outside is higher, and the flaps only control direction.","Correct. The flaps let excess fluid in and keep it from leaking back out."],"lt":"t-lymphatic-system","lid":"d3-t-lymphatic-system-1","comp":106,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-vessels-node"},{"id":"L02091","dok":3,"q":"A patient on long bed rest develops puffy, swollen ankles. Why does inactivity slow the flow of lymph out of the legs?","a":"Without contracting leg muscles squeezing the vessels, less lymph is pushed upward past the valves.","options":["The lymphatic valves open in both directions when muscles relax","The heart stops pumping lymph while the body is lying at rest","The inguinal lymph nodes close off when the legs are not moving","Idle leg muscles stop squeezing the vessels, so less lymph is pushed upward"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The lymphatic system has no central pump, so lymph in the legs depends on the skeletal muscle pump. Each time leg muscles contract, they squeeze nearby lymphatic vessels, and the valves make sure the lymph can only move upward. With little muscle activity, this push weakens and fluid collects in the lowest tissues.","notes":["Lymphatic valves stay one-way at all times, which is what keeps lymph from sliding back down. The problem during bed rest is too little push, not leaky valves.","The heart never pumps lymph, at rest or during activity. Lymph only meets the heart after the ducts empty it into the veins in the neck.","Lymph nodes do not open and close with movement. Lymph passes through them continuously, whether the legs are active or still.","Correct. Inactive muscles stop squeezing the vessels, so lymph moves upward more slowly."],"lt":"t-lymphatic-system","lid":"d3-t-lymphatic-system-3","comp":105,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-pathway"},{"id":"L02095","dok":3,"q":"When lymphatic drainage from a limb is blocked, the limb swells. Based on the normal job of the lymphatic vessels, why does this happen?","a":"Fluid that leaks from the blood capillaries is no longer carried away, so it builds up in the tissue spaces.","options":["Leaked fluid is no longer carried away, so it builds up in the tissues","The lymph nodes swell and take up the extra room inside the limb","Blood can no longer leave the arteries to reach the limb tissues","Lymph backs up into the veins of the limb and stretches their walls"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Blood capillaries constantly leak fluid into the tissue spaces, and the lymphatic vessels normally return the excess to the blood. When those vessels are blocked, the leaking continues but the return does not, so interstitial fluid accumulates. That swelling is called lymphedema when it lasts.","notes":["Correct. Without drainage, leaked fluid collects in the tissue spaces and causes swelling.","Swollen nodes are lymphadenopathy, which makes lumps at node clusters. The swelling here spreads through the tissue of the whole limb because fluid is not being drained.","Arterial blood flow into the limb is unaffected by a lymphatic blockage. Swelling comes from fluid that has already left the blood and cannot be returned.","Lymph does not drain into the limb veins. It only reaches the venous system at the subclavian and internal jugular veins in the neck."],"lt":"t-lymphatic-system","lid":"d3-t-lymphatic-system-7","comp":108,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-disorders"},{"id":"L02096","dok":3,"q":"Most absorbed nutrients enter the blood capillaries of the intestinal villi, but absorbed dietary fats enter the lacteals instead. Why do fats take the lymphatic route?","a":"Dietary fat is packaged into particles too large to enter blood capillaries, but they can pass into the more permeable lacteals.","options":["Fat particles are too large for blood capillaries but fit into lacteals","Fat must first be filtered by the spleen before it can enter the blood","The lacteals digest the fat before it can be allowed to reach the blood","Blood capillaries in the villi are blind-ended and cannot take up nutrients"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Lacteals are lymphatic capillaries in the center of each intestinal villus. Absorbed fats are packed into particles too big to cross into blood capillaries, but lymphatic capillaries are wider and more permeable, so the fat enters the lacteals. The fat turns the lymph milky (chyle), which drains to the cisterna chyli and up the thoracic duct.","notes":["Correct. Fat particles are too large for blood capillaries but can enter the lacteals.","The spleen filters blood, not fat. Fat travels with lymph through the thoracic duct and reaches the blood at the junction of the left subclavian and internal jugular veins.","Digestion of fat happens in the lumen of the small intestine before absorption. Lacteals only carry absorbed fat away.","This swaps the two kinds of capillaries. Lymphatic capillaries are the blind-ended ones, while blood capillaries are open at both ends and absorb most other nutrients."],"lt":"t-lymphatic-system","lid":"d3x-t-lymphatic-system-1","comp":106,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-vessels-node"},{"id":"N-m4-lymph-vessels-1-chunk14","dok":1,"q":"Which lymphatic trunks drain the lower limbs, pelvis, and abdominal wall?","a":"The lumbar trunks.","options":["The bronchomediastinal trunks","The lumbar trunks","The intestinal trunk","The subclavian trunks"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Collecting vessels merge into nine large lymphatic trunks, each named for the region it drains. The paired lumbar trunks collect lymph from the lower limbs, pelvis, and abdominal wall. Both lumbar trunks empty into the cisterna chyli at the start of the thoracic duct.","notes":["The bronchomediastinal trunks drain the thoracic organs and the thoracic wall. Pick them for a question about lymph from the lungs and mediastinum.","Correct. The lumbar trunks drain the lower limbs, pelvis, and abdominal wall.","The intestinal trunk is a single trunk that drains the digestive organs of the abdomen. Pick it if the question asked which trunk carries chyle from the intestine.","The subclavian trunks drain the upper limbs. Pick them for a question about lymph from the arms."],"comp":105,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-pathway"},{"id":"N-m4-lymph-vessels-2-chunk14","dok":2,"q":"Lymph from the left arm travels through the left subclavian trunk. Which vessel does that trunk empty into?","a":"The thoracic duct.","options":["The left lumbar trunk","The right lymphatic duct","The cisterna chyli","The thoracic duct"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The right lymphatic duct only receives the trunks from the upper right part of the body. Every other trunk, including the left subclavian trunk from the left arm, empties into the thoracic duct. The thoracic duct then returns that lymph to the blood where the left subclavian and left internal jugular veins meet.","notes":["The lumbar trunks drain the lower limbs and pelvis and empty into the cisterna chyli. Trunks do not drain into other trunks.","The right lymphatic duct receives only the right jugular, right subclavian, and right bronchomediastinal trunks. Pick it if the question asked about lymph from the right arm.","The cisterna chyli is the abdominal sac that receives the lumbar and intestinal trunks. Lymph from the arm enters the thoracic duct much higher, in the neck region.","Correct. The left subclavian trunk empties into the thoracic duct."],"comp":105,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-pathway"},{"id":"L04286","dok":1,"q":"Which list traces the path of lymph from the tissues back to the blood in the correct order?","a":"Lymphatic capillaries, collecting vessels, lymph nodes, lymphatic trunks, lymphatic ducts, subclavian veins.","options":["Collecting vessels, lymphatic capillaries, lymph nodes, lymphatic ducts, lymphatic trunks, subclavian veins","Lymphatic capillaries, collecting vessels, lymph nodes, lymphatic trunks, lymphatic ducts, subclavian arteries","Lymphatic capillaries, collecting vessels, lymph nodes, lymphatic trunks, lymphatic ducts, subclavian veins","Lymphatic capillaries, lymphatic trunks, lymph nodes, collecting vessels, lymphatic ducts, subclavian veins"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Lymph starts when excess interstitial fluid enters blind-ended lymphatic capillaries. These drain into collecting vessels, which pass through lymph nodes for filtering, then merge into the large lymphatic trunks. The trunks empty into the right lymphatic duct or the thoracic duct, which return lymph to the blood where the subclavian and internal jugular veins meet.","notes":["This starts with collecting vessels and swaps the ducts and trunks. Lymph always enters capillaries first, and trunks drain into ducts, not the other way around.","The steps are right until the end, but lymph returns to veins, not arteries. Students pick this when they forget that the lymphatic system is a drainage system that feeds into the low pressure venous side.","Correct. Lymph moves into larger and larger vessels, with nodes filtering it along the way.","This puts the trunks before the nodes and the collecting vessels. Trunks are the large vessels formed after collecting vessels have passed through nodes, so this order runs from large to small in the middle of the route."],"lt":"t-w4-lymph-pathway","lid":"w4-lymph-pathway-r2","comp":105,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-pathway"},{"id":"L04287","dok":1,"q":"Which two vessels return lymph to the blood, and where do they empty?","a":"The right lymphatic duct and the thoracic duct, where the subclavian and internal jugular veins meet.","options":["The right lymphatic duct and the thoracic duct, directly into the right atrium","The right lymphatic duct and the thoracic duct, where the subclavian and internal jugular veins meet","The right and left lumbar trunks, into the common iliac veins","The cisterna chyli and the thoracic duct, into the inferior vena cava"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"All lymph ends up in one of two lymphatic ducts. The right lymphatic duct empties at the junction of the right subclavian and right internal jugular veins, and the thoracic duct empties at the junction of the left subclavian and left internal jugular veins. From there the returned fluid joins venous blood heading to the heart through the brachiocephalic veins and superior vena cava.","notes":["The two ducts are named correctly, but they join large veins in the root of the neck, not the heart itself. Students pick this when they assume all returning fluid must enter the heart directly.","Correct. Both ducts empty at the junction of a subclavian vein and an internal jugular vein.","The lumbar trunks drain the lower limbs, pelvis, and abdominal wall, and they empty into the thoracic duct, not into veins. They would be the answer if the question asked which trunks carry lymph from the legs.","The cisterna chyli is the sac in the abdomen where the thoracic duct begins, not a separate duct. Pick the cisterna chyli if the question asked where the thoracic duct starts."],"lt":"t-w4-lymph-pathway","lid":"w4-lymph-pathway-r3","comp":105,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-pathway"},{"id":"L04288","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares the drainage areas of the right lymphatic duct and the thoracic duct?","a":"The right lymphatic duct drains the right arm and right side of the head, neck, and thorax; the thoracic duct drains the rest of the body.","options":["The thoracic duct drains the right arm and right side of the head, neck, and thorax, and the right lymphatic duct drains the rest","The right lymphatic duct drains the right arm and right side of the head, neck, and thorax, and the thoracic duct drains the rest","The right lymphatic duct drains everything above the diaphragm, and the thoracic duct drains everything below it","The right lymphatic duct drains the entire right half of the body, and the thoracic duct drains the entire left half"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The right lymphatic duct is short and small, and it receives only the right jugular, right subclavian, and right bronchomediastinal trunks. That limits its territory to the right arm and the right side of the head, neck, and thorax. The thoracic duct is the largest lymphatic vessel and drains everything else, about three quarters of the body, including both legs and the abdomen.","notes":["This swaps the two ducts. The thoracic duct is the large one, so it drains the larger territory, not the small upper right region.","Correct. The right lymphatic duct drains the upper right region only, and the thoracic duct drains about three quarters of the body.","The diaphragm is not the dividing line. The thoracic duct also drains the left arm and the left side of the head, neck, and thorax, which are above the diaphragm.","The drainage is not split evenly down the midline. The right leg and the right side of the abdomen drain through the lumbar and intestinal trunks into the thoracic duct, not the right lymphatic duct."],"lt":"t-w4-lymph-pathway","lid":"w4-lymph-pathway-r4","comp":105,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-pathway"},{"id":"L04289","dok":3,"q":"The lymphatic system has no pump of its own, yet lymph still moves toward the heart. Which explanation is correct?","a":"Nearby skeletal muscle contractions and breathing squeeze the vessels, and valves keep lymph flowing one way.","options":["The heart pumps lymph through the vessels, and valves keep it flowing one way","Skeletal muscle contractions and breathing squeeze the vessels, and valves keep lymph flowing one way","Gravity carries lymph toward the heart, so the vessels need no valves","Lymph nodes contract in rhythm to push lymph, and sphincters keep it flowing one way"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Lymph is moved by forces outside the vessels. Contracting skeletal muscles squeeze nearby lymphatic vessels (the skeletal muscle pump), pressure changes in the thorax with each breath pull lymph upward (the respiratory pump), and smooth muscle in larger vessel walls and pulsing nearby arteries also help. Collecting vessels have even more valves than veins, so every squeeze can push lymph only toward the heart.","notes":["The heart pumps blood, but it is not connected to the start of the lymphatic vessels, which begin as blind-ended capillaries in the tissues. Students pick this because blood and lymph both return to the heart.","Correct. Outside squeezing moves lymph, and valves make the flow one way.","Most lymph from the legs and abdomen has to move upward against gravity, so gravity cannot be the driving force. The vessels also have many valves, so this choice is wrong on both counts.","Lymph nodes are filters with a fibrous capsule, not pumps, and lymphatic vessels use valves rather than sphincters. This choice mixes up nodes with the muscular parts of the system."],"lt":"t-w4-lymph-pathway","lid":"w4-lymph-pathway-r5","comp":105,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-pathway"},{"id":"L04326","dok":1,"q":"What is a lacteal?","a":"A lymphatic capillary in an intestinal villus that absorbs dietary fat.","options":["A blood capillary in an intestinal villus that absorbs sugars","A cluster of lymphatic follicles in the wall of the ileum","A lymphatic capillary in an intestinal villus that absorbs dietary fat","A sac in the abdomen where the thoracic duct begins"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Lacteals are specialized lymphatic capillaries in the center of each villus of the small intestine. Dietary fats are packaged into particles too large to enter blood capillaries, so they enter the lacteals instead. The fat turns the lymph milky white, and this fat-rich lymph, called chyle, drains to the cisterna chyli and up the thoracic duct.","notes":["Blood capillaries in the villus do take up absorbed nutrients such as sugars, but they are not lacteals. Students pick this because both vessels sit side by side in the villus.","This describes Peyer patches, a form of MALT that watches the intestinal contents. It would be the answer if the question asked about lymphatic tissue in the wall of the ileum.","Correct. A lacteal is the lymphatic capillary of a villus, and it absorbs fat.","This describes the cisterna chyli, which receives chyle from the intestinal trunk. Pick it if the question asked where the thoracic duct starts."],"lt":"t-w4-lymph-vessels-node","lid":"w4-lymph-vessels-node-r1","comp":106,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-vessels-node"},{"id":"L04327","dok":2,"q":"How does a lymphatic capillary let fluid in but keep it from leaking back out?","a":"Its endothelial cells overlap loosely and act as one-way flap valves.","options":["Its endothelial cells overlap loosely and act as one-way flap valves","Its endothelial cells are joined by tight seals that admit only small molecules","Cup-shaped valves made of muscle close the open end of the capillary","Smooth muscle in its wall squeezes fluid forward in one direction"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"A lymphatic capillary is blind at one end, and its endothelial cells overlap at their edges without being tightly joined. When fluid pressure in the tissue is higher, it pushes the flaps inward and fluid enters; when pressure inside the capillary rises, the flaps are pressed shut. Anchoring filaments tie the cells to the surrounding tissue so the capillary stays open.","notes":["Correct. The overlapping endothelial cells work as minivalves that open inward only.","Tightly joined endothelial cells are a feature of blood capillaries, which mostly let small molecules through. Lymphatic capillaries are looser, which is why proteins, cells, and even cancer cells can enter them.","Lymphatic capillaries are closed at one end, so there is no open end to cover, and their valves are not made of muscle. Collecting vessels do have valves, but those are folds of the lining, not muscle.","Smooth muscle is found in the walls of larger lymphatic vessels, not the capillaries, which are just a single layer of endothelium. Pick smooth muscle if the question asked how larger vessels help move lymph."],"lt":"t-w4-lymph-vessels-node","lid":"w4-lymph-vessels-node-r2","comp":106,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-vessels-node"},{"id":"L04341","dok":1,"q":"What is lymphedema?","a":"Long-term tissue swelling from blocked or missing lymphatic vessels, sometimes after lymph node removal.","options":["Enlargement of the spleen, as seen in mononucleosis","Long-term tissue swelling from blocked or missing lymphatic vessels","Swollen, tender lymph nodes that are fighting an infection","Cancer of the lymphocytes within lymphatic tissue"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Lymphedema is long-lasting swelling that develops when lymphatic vessels are blocked, damaged, or missing, so interstitial fluid cannot drain back to the blood. It can follow surgery that removes many lymph nodes, such as in breast cancer treatment, and it often affects a limb. In its most extreme form, when parasitic roundworms block the vessels, it is called elephantiasis.","notes":["An enlarged spleen is splenomegaly, seen in mononucleosis and leukemia. Choose it if the question asked why a patient with mono should avoid contact sports.","Correct. Lymphedema is chronic swelling from failed lymphatic drainage.","Swollen, tender nodes fighting an infection is lymphadenitis. Pick it if the question asked what swollen glands in the neck during a sore throat are called.","Cancer of the lymphocytes in lymphatic tissue is lymphoma, which can be Hodgkin or non-Hodgkin. It would be the answer if the question asked about a cancer arising in lymph nodes."],"lt":"t-w4-lymphatic-disorders","lid":"w4-lymphatic-disorders-r1","comp":108,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-disorders"},{"id":"N-m4-lymph-vessels-1-chunk32","dok":1,"q":"Where does the thoracic duct begin?","a":"At the cisterna chyli, a sac in the abdomen.","options":["At a lacteal, in the center of an intestinal villus","At the right lymphatic duct, in the right side of the neck","At the cisterna chyli, a sac in the abdomen","At the junction of the left subclavian and left internal jugular veins"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The thoracic duct starts at the cisterna chyli, a sac in the abdomen that receives lymph from the lumbar trunks and the intestinal trunk. From there the duct runs up through the thorax, collecting more trunks along the way. It is the largest lymphatic vessel and drains about three quarters of the body.","notes":["A lacteal is a lymphatic capillary in an intestinal villus, the very start of fat-rich lymph, far upstream of any duct. Choose it if the question asked where chyle is first formed.","The right lymphatic duct is a separate, small duct that drains the upper right region of the body. It would be the answer if the question asked which duct receives the right jugular and right subclavian trunks.","Correct. The thoracic duct begins at the cisterna chyli.","The junction of the left subclavian and left internal jugular veins is where the thoracic duct ends, not where it begins. Pick it if the question asked where the thoracic duct returns lymph to the blood."],"comp":105,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-pathway"}]},{"id":"m4-lymph-organs","title":"Lymphatic organs and tissues","summary":"red bone marrow, thymus, lymph nodes, spleen, MALT, tonsils, Peyer patches, appendix","mw":"2026-10-28","tr":"2026-10-29","cards":[{"id":"L01826","dok":1,"q":"Which tissue forms lymphatic tissue?","a":"Reticular connective tissue packed with lymphocytes.","options":["Hyaline cartilage packed with chondrocytes","Adipose tissue packed with fat cells","Reticular connective tissue packed with lymphocytes","Dense regular connective tissue packed with fibroblasts"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Lymphatic tissue is built on reticular connective tissue, a fine meshwork of reticular fibers that acts like a soft internal scaffold. Large numbers of lymphocytes sit within that mesh. This same framework supports lymph nodes, the spleen, and red bone marrow.","notes":["Hyaline cartilage, with chondrocytes in lacunae, covers the ends of long bones and forms the costal cartilages. It fits a question about cartilage, and cartilage has no lymphatic capillaries at all.","Adipose tissue stores fat and is found in the hypodermis and around organs. Choose it for a question about fat storage or insulation.","Correct. Lymphatic tissue is reticular connective tissue packed with lymphocytes.","Dense regular connective tissue, with parallel collagen fibers and fibroblasts, forms tendons and ligaments. Pick it for a question about what connects muscle to bone."],"lt":"t-lymph-overview-vessels","lid":"c6","comp":106,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-vessels-node"},{"id":"L01827","dok":1,"q":"All lymphocytes form in red bone marrow. Which lymphocytes travel to the thymus to finish maturing?","a":"T cells.","options":["T cells","Plasma cells","B cells","NK cells"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"T cells start in red bone marrow, but their precursors travel to the thymus to mature, which is where the T in their name comes from. The thymus sits in the mediastinum behind the sternum and is a primary lymphatic organ. T cells make up about 80% of circulating lymphocytes and attack infected cells directly.","notes":["Correct. T cells finish maturing in the thymus.","Plasma cells develop from activated B cells and make antibodies. They are not a group that matures in the thymus, so pick them for a question about what B cells become.","B cells stay in the red bone marrow to mature. Pick them if the question asks which lymphocytes mature in the marrow or become antibody-making plasma cells.","NK (natural killer) cells also mature in the red bone marrow. Choose them for a question about lymphocytes that attack virus-infected and cancer cells as part of innate defense."],"lt":"t-lymph-overview-vessels","lid":"c7","comp":107,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-organs"},{"id":"L01846","dok":1,"q":"Reticular connective tissue is the framework of lymphatic tissue. What kind of fibers form its meshwork?","a":"Reticular fibers.","options":["Thick parallel collagen bundles","Reticular fibers","Fibrin threads","Elastic fibers"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Reticular connective tissue is named for its reticular fibers, very thin fibers that branch and interweave into a fine net. That net forms a soft internal skeleton, called a stroma, for lymph nodes, the spleen, and red bone marrow. Lymphocytes and other cells sit in the spaces of the mesh.","notes":["Thick parallel collagen bundles make up dense regular connective tissue in tendons and ligaments. Pick them for a question about tissue that resists pulling in one direction.","Correct. Reticular fibers form the meshwork of reticular connective tissue.","Fibrin threads form a blood clot, not a connective tissue framework. They fit a question about how a clot traps blood cells.","Elastic fibers dominate elastic connective tissue, such as in the walls of large arteries. Choose them for a question about tissue that stretches and recoils."],"lt":"t-lymph-overview-vessels","lid":"c26","comp":106,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-vessels-node"},{"id":"L01850","dok":3,"q":"A breast tumor sheds cancer cells into nearby lymphatic capillaries. Where are those cells most likely to lodge first?","a":"In the first lymph node that region drains into.","options":["In the first lymph node the region drains into","In the thoracic duct","In the right atrium of the heart","In the spleen"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Lymphatic capillaries are permeable enough to take in cells, including cancer cells. Lymph then flows through collecting vessels to the lymph nodes, which filter it before it moves on. The first node a region drains into, called the sentinel node, is where cancer cells usually lodge first, which is why surgeons check it to see whether a cancer has begun to spread.","notes":["Correct. Cancer cells in lymph usually lodge first in the sentinel lymph node.","The thoracic duct is near the end of the lymph route. Lymph must pass through nodes before reaching it, so pick it for a question about where most trunks empty.","Lymph returns to the blood at the subclavian veins, and nodes filter it long before that point. The heart fits a question about where a clot from a leg vein travels.","The spleen filters blood, not lymph, so cancer cells carried in lymph do not pass through it on the way. Choose the spleen for a question about where old red blood cells are removed."],"lt":"t-lymph-overview-vessels","lid":"c30","comp":108,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-disorders"},{"id":"L01854","dok":3,"q":"A histology slide shows a fine mesh of reticular fibers packed with small round cells that have large nuclei and no visible granules. What tissue is this?","a":"Lymphatic tissue.","options":["Lymphatic tissue","Dense regular connective tissue","Hyaline cartilage","Adipose tissue"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The cells described are lymphocytes, agranular white blood cells with a large round nucleus. A reticular fiber mesh packed with lymphocytes is lymphatic tissue, the tissue found in lymph nodes, the spleen, tonsils, and MALT. The reticular fibers provide the framework and the lymphocytes fill the spaces.","notes":["Correct. A reticular mesh packed with lymphocytes is lymphatic tissue.","Dense regular connective tissue shows tightly packed parallel collagen fibers with flattened fibroblasts between them. Pick it for a slide of a tendon or ligament.","Hyaline cartilage shows chondrocytes in lacunae within a smooth, glassy matrix. It fits a slide of articular cartilage or the trachea.","Adipose tissue shows large empty-looking fat cells with the nucleus pushed to the edge. Choose it for a slide of fat from the hypodermis."],"lt":"t-lymph-overview-vessels","lid":"c34","comp":106,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-vessels-node"},{"id":"L01859","dok":1,"q":"Which lymphocytes become plasma cells that make antibodies?","a":"B cells.","options":["NK cells","B cells","Neutrophils","T cells"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"B cells form and mature in the red bone marrow. When a B cell is activated by an invader, it can become a plasma cell, which makes antibodies. This antibody-based defense is called humoral immunity, and it complements the direct attack carried out by T cells.","notes":["NK (natural killer) cells attack virus-infected and cancer cells as part of innate defense, without making antibodies. Choose them for a question about the third type of lymphocyte.","Correct. B cells become plasma cells that make antibodies.","Neutrophils are granular white blood cells that engulf bacteria. They are not lymphocytes, so pick them for a question about the most common white blood cell.","T cells mature in the thymus and attack infected cells directly or direct other immune cells. Pick them for a question about cell-mediated immunity."],"lt":"t-lymph-overview-vessels","lid":"c39","comp":107,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-organs"},{"id":"L01903","dok":1,"q":"Which pair names the two primary lymphatic organs?","a":"Red bone marrow and the thymus.","options":["The thymus and the spleen","Red bone marrow and the lymph nodes","The spleen and the tonsils","Red bone marrow and the thymus"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Primary lymphatic organs are where lymphocytes are made and where they mature. All lymphocytes form in red bone marrow, and B cells also mature there, while T cell precursors travel to the thymus to finish maturing. Every other lymphatic organ is secondary, meaning it is a place where mature lymphocytes meet invaders.","notes":["The thymus is primary, but the spleen is a secondary organ that filters blood and houses mature lymphocytes. Pick the spleen if the question asked which organ filters the blood.","Red bone marrow is primary, but lymph nodes are secondary organs that filter lymph along the collecting vessels. Lymph nodes would be the answer for an organ that cleans lymph before it returns to the blood.","Both of these are secondary lymphatic tissue where mature lymphocytes meet pathogens. The tonsils would fit a question about lymphatic tissue guarding the entrance to the throat, and the spleen a question about filtering blood.","Correct. Lymphocytes form in red bone marrow and finish maturing either there (B cells) or in the thymus (T cells)."],"lt":"t-lymph-primary-organs","lid":"c1","comp":107,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-organs"},{"id":"L01904","dok":1,"q":"Where is red bone marrow found in an adult?","a":"In the spongy bone of the axial skeleton, the limb girdles, and the proximal ends of the humerus and femur.","options":["In the articular cartilage at the ends of long bones","In the compact bone of the outer skull surface","In the medullary cavity of every long bone shaft","In the spongy bone of the axial skeleton and limb girdles"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"In adults, red bone marrow fills the spaces of spongy bone in the axial skeleton (skull, vertebrae, ribs, sternum) and the limb girdles, plus the proximal ends of the humerus and femur. This is why marrow samples are drawn from the sternum or the iliac crest of the hip bone. Over childhood, the marrow in the shafts of long bones is replaced by fatty yellow marrow.","notes":["Articular cartilage is hyaline cartilage that covers joint surfaces, and cartilage has no marrow or blood vessels inside it. It would be the answer for the smooth tissue that reduces friction in a synovial joint.","Compact bone is the dense outer layer of bone built from osteons, with no marrow-filled spaces. Choose compact bone if the question asked which bone tissue resists bending and forms the hard outer shell.","The medullary cavity of a long bone shaft holds red marrow in children, but in adults it is filled mostly with yellow marrow, which is fat. Pick the medullary cavity if the question asked where yellow marrow is stored in an adult.","Correct. Adult red marrow sits in spongy bone of the axial skeleton and the girdles."],"lt":"t-lymph-primary-organs","lid":"c2","comp":107,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-organs"},{"id":"L01905","dok":1,"q":"Which cells in red bone marrow give rise to lymphocytes?","a":"Pluripotent stem cells (hemocytoblasts).","options":["Megakaryocytes","Reticulocytes","Pluripotent stem cells","Plasma cells"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Red bone marrow holds pluripotent stem cells, also called hemocytoblasts. Pluripotent means one stem cell can become many different cell types, so these cells give rise to every formed element of blood, including the lymphocytes. The lymphoid line of these stem cells produces the B cells, T cell precursors, and NK cells.","notes":["Megakaryocytes are giant marrow cells whose cytoplasm breaks apart into platelets. Pick megakaryocytes if the question asked which cell produces platelets.","Reticulocytes are immature red blood cells that still hold ribosome remnants. They would be the answer for the cell stage just before a mature erythrocyte.","Correct. Pluripotent stem cells in red marrow give rise to all blood cells, lymphocytes included.","Plasma cells are what activated B cells become, and they make antibodies. They are the end of the B cell story, not the start, so choose them for a question about which cells secrete antibodies."],"lt":"t-lymph-primary-organs","lid":"c3","comp":107,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-organs"},{"id":"L01906","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly describes the B cells and T cells that leave the red bone marrow?","a":"B cells leave mature, while T cells leave as immature pre-T cells.","options":["Both leave immature and finish maturing in the spleen","T cells leave mature, while B cells leave as immature pre-B cells","Both B cells and T cells leave the marrow fully mature","B cells leave mature, while T cells leave as immature pre-T cells"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Both B cells and T cells start from stem cells in the red bone marrow. B cells stay in the marrow until they are mature, so the marrow is where B cells both form and mature. T cells leave the marrow as pre-T cells and travel to the thymus to finish maturing, which is why the thymus is also a primary lymphatic organ.","notes":["The spleen is a secondary organ where mature lymphocytes meet blood-borne invaders, not a place where lymphocytes mature. Pick the spleen for a question about filtering blood or removing old red cells.","This reverses the two cell types. Students often mix them up, so link the letters to places: B cells mature in the bone marrow, and T cells mature in the thymus.","T cells cannot be fully mature when they leave the marrow, because they still need the thymus. This choice would be true only of B cells, which do complete their maturation in the marrow.","Correct. B cells mature in the marrow, and pre-T cells finish maturing in the thymus."],"lt":"t-lymph-primary-organs","lid":"c4","comp":107,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-organs"},{"id":"L01907","dok":1,"q":"Where do pre-T cells travel after they leave the red bone marrow?","a":"To the thymus.","options":["The thymus","The spleen","The tonsils","The lymph nodes"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Pre-T cells are immature T cells made in the red bone marrow. They travel in the blood to the thymus, the primary lymphatic organ in the mediastinum behind the sternum, where they multiply and learn to recognize foreign antigens. Only after maturing in the thymus do T cells move on to secondary organs.","notes":["Correct. The thymus is where pre-T cells go to mature into T cells.","The spleen is a secondary organ in the upper left abdomen that filters blood. Mature T cells do live in its white pulp, so pick the spleen if the question asked where mature lymphocytes respond to blood-borne pathogens.","The tonsils are secondary lymphatic tissue in a ring around the throat that meet inhaled and swallowed pathogens. They would be the answer for lymphatic tissue guarding the entrance to the pharynx.","Lymph nodes are secondary organs along the collecting vessels, and their deep cortex holds mostly T cells, but only after the T cells have matured. Choose lymph nodes for a question about filtering lymph."],"lt":"t-lymph-primary-organs","lid":"c5","comp":107,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-organs"},{"id":"L01908","dok":2,"q":"Which description matches the internal organization of the thymus?","a":"Two lobes divided into lobules, each with an outer cortex and an inner medulla.","options":["Clusters of follicles scattered through a mucous membrane with no capsule","Two lobes divided into lobules, each with a cortex and a medulla","An outer cortex, a deep cortex, and a medulla fed by afferent vessels","Small islands of lymphocytes around central arteries within red pulp"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The thymus is bilobed, meaning it has two lobes, and a connective tissue capsule sends extensions inward that split each lobe into smaller lobules. Each lobule has a darker outer cortex packed with immature T cells and a paler inner medulla with fewer, more mature T cells. Recognizing this pattern lets you tell the thymus apart from the spleen, lymph nodes, and MALT on a slide.","notes":["This describes MALT, such as Peyer patches in the ileum, which is lymphatic tissue in the mucosa without a surrounding capsule. Choose it for a question about unencapsulated lymphatic tissue in the gut wall.","Correct. Two lobes, lobules, and a cortex and medulla in each lobule is the pattern of the thymus.","This describes a lymph node, with B cell follicles in the outer cortex, T cells in the deep cortex, and lymph arriving through afferent vessels. The thymus has no afferent vessels because it does not filter lymph.","This describes the spleen, where white pulp forms small islands of lymphocytes around central arteries and red pulp makes up most of the organ. Pick it for a question about the spleen's internal structure."],"lt":"t-lymph-primary-organs","lid":"c7","comp":107,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-organs"},{"id":"L01909","dok":1,"q":"What are the two regions of each thymic lobule?","a":"An outer cortex and an inner medulla.","options":["An outer cortex and an inner medulla","A capsule and a hilum","An outer cortex and a deep cortex","White pulp and red pulp"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Each lobule of the thymus has an outer cortex and an inner medulla. The cortex is densely packed with immature T cells, so it stains darker, while the medulla holds fewer, more mature T cells and the thymic corpuscles. T cells develop as they move from the cortex toward the medulla.","notes":["Correct. Every thymic lobule has an outer cortex and an inner medulla.","A capsule is the covering of an organ, and a hilum is the indented spot where vessels enter or leave, as on a lymph node or the spleen. These are surface features, not the two internal regions of a thymic lobule.","The outer cortex and deep cortex are regions of a lymph node, holding mostly B cells and mostly T cells. Choose this pair for a question about the zones of a lymph node.","White pulp and red pulp are the two tissues of the spleen, not the thymus. Pick them if the question asked about the regions of the spleen."],"lt":"t-lymph-primary-organs","lid":"c8","comp":107,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-organs"},{"id":"L01910","dok":1,"q":"Which cells are most densely packed in the cortex of the thymus?","a":"Immature T cells.","options":["Red blood cells","Immature T cells","Mature B cells","Plasma cells"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The thymic cortex is crowded with immature T cells that arrived from the red bone marrow as pre-T cells. Dendritic cells, epithelial cells, and macrophages are scattered among them and support their development. Because the cortex is so densely packed with these lymphocytes, it stains darker than the medulla.","notes":["Red blood cells are not a feature of thymic tissue. Choose red blood cells for a question about what fills the splenic cords and sinuses of the red pulp.","Correct. The thymic cortex is packed with immature T cells that are multiplying and maturing.","B cells mature in the red bone marrow, not in the thymus. Mature B cells fill the follicles of the outer cortex of a lymph node, so pick them for a question about that region.","Plasma cells are activated B cells that make antibodies, and they are not residents of the thymic cortex. They would be the answer for cells found in the medulla of a lymph node."],"lt":"t-lymph-primary-organs","lid":"c9","comp":107,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-organs"},{"id":"L01911","dok":1,"q":"What happens to pre-T cells in the thymic cortex?","a":"They multiply and begin maturing into T cells.","options":["They are filtered out of lymph and destroyed","They multiply and begin maturing into T cells","They are first formed from pluripotent stem cells","They become plasma cells that make antibodies"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Pre-T cells arrive in the thymic cortex from the red bone marrow and multiply there. Thymic epithelial cells and dendritic cells support them as they mature and learn to recognize foreign antigens. The cells that pass this process move on to the medulla and then leave the thymus as mature T cells.","notes":["Filtering lymph is the job of lymph nodes, and the thymus has no afferent lymphatic vessels at all. This would be the answer for what macrophages in a lymph node do to bacteria and debris.","Correct. The cortex is where pre-T cells proliferate and mature.","Pre-T cells are formed from pluripotent stem cells in the red bone marrow, before they ever reach the thymus. Pick this for a question about what happens to lymphocytes in the red bone marrow.","Becoming a plasma cell is what an activated B cell does, for example in the follicles of a lymph node. T cells never become plasma cells, so choose this for a question about B cell activation."],"lt":"t-lymph-primary-organs","lid":"c10","comp":107,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-organs"},{"id":"L01914","dok":2,"q":"Compared with the thymic cortex, what kind of T cells does the thymic medulla hold?","a":"Fewer T cells that are more mature.","options":["Only B cells and plasma cells, with no T cells","More T cells that are less mature","No lymphocytes at all, only epithelial cells","Fewer T cells that are more mature"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"T cells develop in the cortex and then move inward to the medulla as they mature. The medulla therefore holds fewer T cells than the cortex, but the ones there are more mature and nearly ready to leave the thymus. The medulla also contains epithelial cells, dendritic cells, macrophages, and the thymic corpuscles.","notes":["B cells and plasma cells are typical of the medulla of a lymph node, not the thymus. Choose this for a question about the inner region of a lymph node.","This describes the cortex, which is densely packed with immature T cells. Pick it for a question about the outer region of a thymic lobule.","The medulla does contain epithelial cells, but it also holds mature T cells and other support cells. Thinking it has no lymphocytes comes from confusing it with a thymic corpuscle, which is made only of epithelial cells.","Correct. The medulla has fewer T cells, and they are more mature."],"lt":"t-lymph-primary-organs","lid":"c13","comp":107,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-organs"},{"id":"L01915","dok":1,"q":"What is another name for the thymic corpuscles?","a":"Hassall's corpuscles.","options":["Hassall's corpuscles","Peyer patches","Billroth's cords","Germinal centers"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Thymic corpuscles are also called Hassall's corpuscles, named for the scientist who first described them. They are rounded clusters of epithelial cells arranged in concentric layers, like the layers of an onion, and they are found only in the medulla of the thymus. They are one of the easiest features for identifying thymus tissue on a slide.","notes":["Correct. Thymic corpuscles and Hassall's corpuscles are the same structure.","Peyer patches are clusters of lymphatic follicles in the wall of the ileum, a form of MALT. Pick them if the question asked about lymphatic tissue in the small intestine.","Billroth's cords are the splenic cords of the red pulp, found between the venous sinuses of the spleen. They would be the answer for a question about red pulp structure.","Germinal centers are the pale centers of lymphatic follicles where B cells multiply, as in a lymph node's outer cortex. Choose germinal centers for a question about where B cells proliferate in a follicle."],"lt":"t-lymph-primary-organs","lid":"c14","comp":107,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-organs"},{"id":"L01916","dok":1,"q":"Which description fits a Hassall's (thymic) corpuscle?","a":"Epithelial cells arranged in concentric layers.","options":["A cord of red cells and macrophages between sinuses","Epithelial cells arranged in concentric layers","A cluster of B cells with a pale germinal center","A sleeve of lymphocytes around a central artery"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"A Hassall's corpuscle is a rounded whorl of flattened epithelial cells wrapped in concentric layers. It sits in the medulla of the thymus and has no match in other lymphatic organs. On a slide it looks like a small pink onion slice among the T cells.","notes":["This describes a splenic cord (Billroth's cord) in the red pulp of the spleen. It would be the answer for the tissue that removes worn-out red cells.","Correct. A thymic corpuscle is a whorl of epithelial cells in concentric layers.","This is a lymphatic follicle (nodule) with a germinal center, as in the outer cortex of a lymph node. Pick it for a question about where B cells multiply in a node.","This is splenic white pulp, where lymphocytes cluster around central arteries. Choose it for a question about the immune tissue of the spleen."],"lt":"t-lymph-primary-organs","lid":"c15","comp":107,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-organs"},{"id":"L01917","dok":1,"q":"In which region of the thymus are Hassall's corpuscles found?","a":"The medulla.","options":["The cortex","The medulla","The connective tissue septa","The capsule"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Hassall's corpuscles, also called thymic corpuscles, are found in the medulla, the paler inner region of each thymic lobule. The medulla holds fewer, more mature T cells, so the corpuscles stand out clearly there. Finding them is a reliable way to locate the medulla on a thymus slide.","notes":["The cortex is the outer region packed with immature T cells, and it has no thymic corpuscles. Pick the cortex for a question about where pre-T cells first multiply and mature.","Correct. Thymic corpuscles are a feature of the medulla.","The septa are inward extensions of the capsule that divide each lobe into lobules. They would be the answer for what separates one thymic lobule from the next.","The capsule is the connective tissue covering of the whole thymus. Choose it for a question about what surrounds the organ and sends extensions inward."],"lt":"t-lymph-primary-organs","lid":"c16","comp":107,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-organs"},{"id":"L01918","dok":2,"q":"Which structure is a primary lymphatic organ?","a":"The thymus.","options":["A lymph node","A palatine tonsil","The thymus","The spleen"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Primary lymphatic organs are where lymphocytes form and mature, and there are only two of them: red bone marrow and the thymus. The thymus is primary because T cells finish maturing there. The spleen, lymph nodes, tonsils, and MALT are secondary organs where mature lymphocytes meet invaders.","notes":["A lymph node is a secondary organ that filters lymph along the collecting vessels. It would be the answer for where lymph is cleaned before returning to the blood.","The palatine tonsils are secondary lymphatic tissue in the lateral walls of the oropharynx. Choose them for a question about the tonsils most often infected and removed.","Correct. The thymus is a primary lymphatic organ where T cells mature.","The spleen is the largest lymphatic organ, but it is secondary because mature lymphocytes there respond to pathogens in the blood. Pick the spleen if the question asked which organ filters the blood."],"lt":"t-lymph-primary-organs","lid":"c17","comp":107,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-organs"},{"id":"L01919","dok":2,"q":"Which organ produces both mature B cells and pre-T cells?","a":"Red bone marrow.","options":["The spleen","Red bone marrow","The tonsils","The thymus"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"All lymphocytes form from stem cells in red bone marrow. B cells stay in the marrow until they are mature, and T cell precursors (pre-T cells) leave the marrow and travel to the thymus. That makes red bone marrow the starting point for every lymphocyte and the maturation site for B cells.","notes":["The spleen is a secondary organ that filters blood and houses mature lymphocytes. It would be the answer for where old red blood cells are removed.","Correct. Red bone marrow makes the stem-cell-derived lymphocytes, releasing mature B cells and pre-T cells.","The tonsils are secondary lymphatic tissue guarding the throat. Choose them for a question about lymphatic tissue that traps inhaled and swallowed pathogens.","The thymus receives pre-T cells but does not produce them, and it does not make B cells. Pick the thymus if the question asked where T cells finish maturing."],"lt":"t-lymph-primary-organs","lid":"c18","comp":107,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-organs"},{"id":"L01920","dok":2,"q":"In which thymic region do pre-T cells multiply and begin to mature?","a":"The cortex.","options":["The capsule","The medulla","The cortex","A thymic corpuscle"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Pre-T cells arriving from the red bone marrow settle in the outer cortex of each thymic lobule. There they multiply and mature with help from thymic epithelial cells and dendritic cells. The cells that survive this process move inward to the medulla before leaving the thymus.","notes":["The capsule is the connective tissue covering of the thymus, not a place where cells develop. Choose it for a question about what surrounds the organ and divides it into lobules.","The medulla is where T cells go after they have matured in the cortex. Pick the medulla if the question asked which region holds fewer, more mature T cells.","Correct. The cortex is where pre-T cells proliferate and begin maturing.","A thymic corpuscle is a whorl of epithelial cells in the medulla, not a region where T cells multiply. It would be the answer for the concentric structure used to identify the thymic medulla."],"lt":"t-lymph-primary-organs","lid":"c20","comp":107,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-organs"},{"id":"L01921","dok":2,"q":"Which feature lets you tell a thymus slide apart from a lymph node slide?","a":"Thymic corpuscles in the medulla.","options":["Germinal centers in the outer cortex","A capsule of dense connective tissue","Trabeculae extending inward from the capsule","Thymic corpuscles in the medulla"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Thymic (Hassall's) corpuscles, whorls of epithelial cells in concentric layers, are found only in the thymic medulla. Both the thymus and a lymph node have a connective tissue capsule with inward extensions, so those features do not separate them. Germinal centers point the other way, toward a lymph node, because the thymus has no follicles.","notes":["Germinal centers inside follicles are typical of a lymph node's outer cortex, and the thymus has no follicles. Pick germinal centers if the question asked which feature identifies a lymph node.","Both the thymus and lymph nodes have a dense connective tissue capsule, so it cannot tell them apart. The capsule would be the answer for what covers the outside of either organ.","Both organs have inward extensions of the capsule, called trabeculae in a lymph node and septa in the thymus. Choose trabeculae for a question about what divides a lymph node into compartments.","Correct. Thymic corpuscles are unique to the thymic medulla."],"lt":"t-lymph-primary-organs","lid":"c21","comp":107,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-organs"},{"id":"L01922","dok":2,"q":"A T cell has finished developing in the thymic cortex. Where does it move next before it leaves the thymus?","a":"The medulla.","options":["A connective tissue septum","The capsule","The medulla","A thymic corpuscle"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"T cells mature as they move from the outer cortex inward to the medulla of each thymic lobule. The medulla therefore holds the more mature T cells, which then leave the thymus and travel to secondary organs such as lymph nodes and the spleen. This inward path explains why the cortex holds immature cells and the medulla holds mature ones.","notes":["Septa are connective tissue partitions between lobules, not a place where T cells collect. Choose a septum for a question about what divides a thymic lobe into lobules.","The capsule is the connective tissue covering on the outside of the thymus, the opposite direction from where maturing T cells travel. Pick it for a question about what encloses the whole organ.","Correct. Maturing T cells move inward from the cortex to the medulla.","A thymic corpuscle is a whorl of epithelial cells inside the medulla, not a region T cells move into. It would be the answer for the structure that identifies the medulla on a slide."],"lt":"t-lymph-primary-organs","lid":"c22","comp":107,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-organs"},{"id":"L01923","dok":2,"q":"Which thymic cells form a barrier around developing T cells and release thymic hormones?","a":"Epithelial cells.","options":["Plasma cells","Epithelial cells","Dendritic cells","Macrophages"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Thymic epithelial cells make up the supporting framework of the thymus. They form a barrier around the developing T cells and release thymic hormones that help the T cells mature. In the medulla, some of these same epithelial cells are wrapped into the concentric thymic corpuscles.","notes":["Plasma cells are antibody-making cells that come from activated B cells, and they are not part of the thymic cortex. They would be the answer for the antibody-secreting cells in a lymph node medulla.","Correct. Thymic epithelial cells surround developing T cells and release thymic hormones.","Dendritic cells in the thymic cortex assist pre-T cells as they mature, but they do not release thymic hormones. Pick dendritic cells if the question asked which cells help present antigens to developing T cells.","Macrophages engulf debris, including the many T cells that die by apoptosis in the thymus. Choose macrophages for a question about which cells clean up dead cells."],"lt":"t-lymph-primary-organs","lid":"c23","comp":107,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-organs"},{"id":"L01924","dok":2,"q":"What divides the thymus into lobes and lobules?","a":"Connective tissue extensions from the capsule.","options":["Connective tissue septa from the capsule","Whorls of thymic corpuscles","Venous sinuses of the red pulp","Germinal centers of follicles"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"A capsule of connective tissue surrounds the thymus, separating its two lobes. Extensions of this capsule reach inward and divide each lobe into lobules. Each lobule then has its own cortex and medulla.","notes":["Correct. Connective tissue from the capsule extends inward to divide the thymus into lobes and lobules.","Thymic corpuscles are whorls of epithelial cells in the medulla, not partitions. Choose them for a question about a feature unique to the thymic medulla.","Venous sinuses are wide blood channels in the red pulp of the spleen. Pick them for a question about where blood collects after passing through the splenic cords.","Germinal centers are the pale centers of follicles where B cells multiply, and the thymus has none. They would be the answer for a feature of the outer cortex of a lymph node."],"lt":"t-lymph-primary-organs","lid":"c24","comp":107,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-organs"},{"id":"L01925","dok":3,"q":"A lymphatic organ in the mediastinum, above the heart and behind the sternum, is large in a child but shrinks after puberty. Which organ is it?","a":"The thymus.","options":["The spleen","Red bone marrow","The pharyngeal tonsil","The thymus"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The thymus sits in the mediastinum, above the heart and behind the sternum. It is large in infants and children, when large numbers of T cells are being produced, and then shrinks after puberty. In older adults it is mostly replaced by fat.","notes":["The spleen sits in the upper left abdomen behind the stomach, not in the mediastinum. Pick the spleen for a question about a lymphatic organ protected by the lower left ribs.","Red bone marrow is in the spongy bone of the axial skeleton and limb girdles, not a single organ in the mediastinum. Choose it for a question about where all lymphocytes form.","The pharyngeal tonsil (adenoid) is on the posterior wall of the nasopharynx. It also tends to shrink with age, but it is in the throat, so pick it for a question about tissue that blocks the back of the nose in children.","Correct. The thymus lies behind the sternum and shrinks after puberty."],"lt":"t-lymph-primary-organs","lid":"c25","comp":107,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-organs"},{"id":"L01926","dok":2,"q":"What has happened to most of the thymus in an older adult?","a":"It has been largely replaced by fat.","options":["It has turned into red bone marrow","It has doubled in size","It has been largely replaced by fat","It has moved down into the abdomen"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The thymus is largest in infants and children, when the body is building its supply of T cells. After puberty it shrinks steadily, and in older adults most of its tissue has been replaced by fat. The T cells it produced earlier in life continue to live and divide in the secondary lymphatic organs.","notes":["The thymus does not become marrow. Red bone marrow is a separate primary organ located in spongy bone, which is where all lymphocytes form throughout life.","The thymus gets smaller with age, not larger. Students sometimes assume organs grow with the body, but the thymus is at its relative largest in childhood.","Correct. After puberty the thymus shrinks and is mostly fat in older adults.","The thymus stays in the mediastinum, above the heart and behind the sternum, for life. Aging changes its size and makeup, not its location, so an abdominal location would fit the spleen instead."],"lt":"t-lymph-primary-organs","lid":"c26","comp":107,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-organs"},{"id":"L01928","dok":3,"q":"A slide of the thymic medulla shows rounded whorls of flattened epithelial cells in concentric layers. What are these whorls?","a":"Hassall's corpuscles.","options":["Billroth's cords","Hassall's corpuscles","Germinal centers","Lymphatic follicles"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Concentric whorls of epithelial cells in the thymic medulla are Hassall's corpuscles, also called thymic corpuscles. They are found nowhere else in the lymphatic system, so seeing them confirms both that the tissue is thymus and that you are looking at the medulla. The surrounding cells are mature T cells, macrophages, and dendritic cells.","notes":["Billroth's cords are the splenic cords of the red pulp, packed with red cells and macrophages. Choose them for a question about the tissue between the venous sinuses of the spleen.","Correct. Whorls of epithelial cells in the thymic medulla are Hassall's corpuscles.","Germinal centers are the pale centers of follicles where B cells multiply. They are made of lymphocytes, not epithelial cells, so pick them for a question about B cell proliferation in a node.","Lymphatic follicles are round clusters of lymphocytes, mostly B cells, found in lymph nodes, tonsils, the spleen, and Peyer patches. Pick follicles for a question about the dense B cell clusters in a node's outer cortex."],"lt":"t-lymph-primary-organs","lid":"c28","comp":107,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-organs"},{"id":"L01929","dok":3,"q":"A baby is born without a thymus. Which cells would fail to mature normally?","a":"T cells.","options":["B cells","Erythrocytes","Platelets","T cells"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The thymus is the primary lymphatic organ where T cells mature. Pre-T cells are still made in the red bone marrow, but without a thymus they have nowhere to finish developing, so the child would have very few functional T cells. B cells, red cells, and platelets all form and mature in the red bone marrow, so they would not depend on the thymus.","notes":["B cells form and mature in the red bone marrow, so they do not need the thymus. Pick B cells if the question described a child with a red bone marrow defect.","Erythrocytes are produced entirely in the red bone marrow from the myeloid line. They would be the answer for a question about a marrow failure causing anemia.","Platelets come from megakaryocytes in the red bone marrow. Choose platelets for a question about a marrow problem that raises the risk of bleeding.","Correct. T cells need the thymus to mature."],"lt":"t-lymph-primary-organs","lid":"c29","comp":107,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-organs"},{"id":"L01930","dok":2,"q":"Which cell type is NOT normally found in the thymic cortex?","a":"Plasma cells.","options":["Dendritic cells","Immature T cells","Plasma cells","Epithelial cells"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The thymic cortex holds densely packed immature T cells along with dendritic cells, epithelial cells, and macrophages that support and clean up after them. Plasma cells are activated B cells that secrete antibodies, and they belong in places where B cells respond to antigens, such as the medulla of a lymph node. They are not part of the T cell nursery in the thymic cortex.","notes":["Dendritic cells are present in the thymic cortex, where they assist pre-T cells as they mature. Choose them for a question about cells that help T cells learn to recognize antigens.","Immature T cells are the most numerous cells of the thymic cortex. Pick them if the question asked which cells crowd the outer region of a thymic lobule.","Correct. Plasma cells are not residents of the thymic cortex.","Epithelial cells form the framework of the thymic cortex and release thymic hormones. They would be the answer for the cells that make thymic hormones."],"lt":"t-lymph-primary-organs","lid":"c30","comp":107,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-organs"},{"id":"L01932","dok":2,"q":"Why is red bone marrow classified as a primary lymphatic organ?","a":"Lymphocytes form there, and B cells mature there.","options":["Lymphocytes form there, and B cells mature there","Worn-out red blood cells are removed there","Lymphocytes there meet swallowed pathogens","Lymph passing through it is filtered of bacteria"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"A primary lymphatic organ is a place where lymphocytes are made and mature. Every lymphocyte starts from stem cells in red bone marrow, and B cells also complete their maturation there. The other jobs listed belong to secondary organs, where mature lymphocytes do their work.","notes":["Correct. Lymphocyte formation and B cell maturation make red marrow a primary organ.","Removing worn-out red cells is a job of the spleen's red pulp. This would be the answer for a question about what the spleen does to old erythrocytes.","Meeting swallowed pathogens is the job of the tonsils and MALT, which guard mucosal surfaces. Choose this for a question about lymphatic tissue in the throat or gut lining.","Filtering lymph is the job of lymph nodes, which are secondary organs along the collecting vessels. Pick this for a question about why lymph nodes swell during an infection."],"lt":"t-lymph-primary-organs","lid":"c32","comp":107,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-organs"},{"id":"L01933","dok":2,"q":"Red bone marrow fills the spaces inside which kind of bone tissue?","a":"Spongy bone.","options":["Spongy bone","Compact bone","Dense regular connective tissue","Hyaline cartilage"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Spongy bone is a lattice of thin bony plates called trabeculae with open spaces between them. In the axial skeleton, the limb girdles, and the proximal ends of the humerus and femur, those spaces are filled with red bone marrow, where blood cells and lymphocytes are made. Compact bone is solid and has no room for marrow.","notes":["Correct. Red bone marrow fills the spaces between the trabeculae of spongy bone.","Compact bone is the dense outer layer of bone built from tightly packed osteons, with only tiny canals for vessels. Pick compact bone for a question about the bone tissue that resists bending and forms the outer shell.","Dense regular connective tissue forms tendons and ligaments, built of parallel collagen fibers with no marrow spaces. It would be the answer for the tissue that attaches muscle to bone.","Hyaline cartilage covers joint surfaces and forms the costal cartilages, and it has no blood vessels or marrow. Choose it for a question about the tissue at the ends of long bones in a synovial joint."],"lt":"t-lymph-primary-organs","lid":"c33","comp":107,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-organs"},{"id":"L01934","dok":2,"q":"Which lymphatic organ has two lobes, each divided into lobules with a cortex and a medulla?","a":"The thymus.","options":["The spleen","A lymph node","The thymus","A palatine tonsil"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The thymus is bilobed, meaning it has two lobes, and connective tissue from its capsule divides each lobe into lobules. Each lobule has an outer cortex of immature T cells and an inner medulla with mature T cells and thymic corpuscles. A lymph node also has a cortex and medulla, but it is a single small bean-shaped organ, not two lobes.","notes":["The spleen is organized into red pulp and white pulp, not lobes with a cortex and medulla. Pick the spleen for a question about red pulp and white pulp.","A lymph node does have a cortex and a medulla, but it is a single bean-shaped structure with no lobes. Choose a lymph node for a question about an organ with afferent and efferent lymphatic vessels.","Correct. The thymus has two lobes divided into lobules, each with a cortex and a medulla.","A palatine tonsil is a mass of lymphatic tissue with deep pits called crypts, without lobes or a medulla. It would be the answer for lymphatic tissue in the lateral walls of the oropharynx."],"lt":"t-lymph-primary-organs","lid":"c34","comp":107,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-organs"},{"id":"L01935","dok":2,"q":"On a thymus slide, each lobule has a darker outer region. What is this region, and why does it stain darker?","a":"The cortex, because it is densely packed with immature T cells.","options":["The medulla, because it is crowded with mature T cells","The capsule, because it is made of dense collagen","The medulla, because it holds the thymic corpuscles","The cortex, because it is packed with immature T cells"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The outer region of each thymic lobule is the cortex. It is crowded with immature T cells, and their tightly packed nuclei take up a lot of stain, so the cortex looks darker. The medulla in the center has fewer T cells and looks paler, which is the opposite of what students often expect.","notes":["The medulla is the inner region, and it actually holds fewer T cells than the cortex, so it stains lighter. It would be the answer for the paler central region with mature T cells.","The capsule is a thin covering around the whole thymus, not the outer region of each lobule. Choose the capsule for a question about what encloses the thymus and divides it into lobules.","The medulla is the inner, paler region, not the outer one, although it does hold the thymic corpuscles. Pick the medulla for a question about where Hassall's corpuscles are found.","Correct. The cortex is the darker outer region, packed with immature T cells."],"lt":"t-lymph-primary-organs","lid":"c35","comp":107,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-organs"},{"id":"L01936","dok":1,"q":"What is the paler inner region of a thymic lobule called?","a":"The medulla.","options":["The germinal center","The hilum","The cortex","The medulla"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The inner region of each thymic lobule is the medulla. It looks paler than the cortex because it holds fewer T cells, and those T cells are the more mature ones. The medulla also contains the thymic (Hassall's) corpuscles.","notes":["A germinal center is the pale center of a lymphatic follicle where B cells multiply, and the thymus has no follicles. It would be the answer for the pale area inside a follicle of a lymph node.","A hilum is the indented spot where vessels enter or leave an organ, such as a lymph node or the spleen. Choose the hilum for a question about where efferent vessels leave a lymph node.","The cortex is the darker outer region packed with immature T cells. Pick the cortex for a question about where pre-T cells first multiply.","Correct. The medulla is the paler inner region of each thymic lobule."],"lt":"t-lymph-primary-organs","lid":"c36","comp":107,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-organs"},{"id":"L01938","dok":2,"q":"Which cell type is found in both the cortex and the medulla of the thymus?","a":"Epithelial cells.","options":["Megakaryocytes","Plasma cells","Epithelial cells","Erythrocytes"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Thymic epithelial cells form the supporting framework of the whole thymus, so they are found in both the cortex and the medulla. In the medulla, some of them are wrapped into the concentric thymic corpuscles. The two regions differ mainly in their T cells: immature and crowded in the cortex, fewer and more mature in the medulla.","notes":["Megakaryocytes are giant cells of the red bone marrow that break apart to form platelets. They would be the answer for a question about where platelets come from.","Plasma cells are antibody-making cells that come from activated B cells, and they are not residents of the thymus. Pick plasma cells for a question about the cells in the medulla of a lymph node.","Correct. Thymic epithelial cells are found in both the cortex and the medulla.","Erythrocytes are red blood cells that travel inside blood vessels, not resident cells of thymic tissue. Choose them for a question about what fills the cords and sinuses of the splenic red pulp."],"lt":"t-lymph-primary-organs","lid":"c38","comp":107,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-organs"},{"id":"L01939","dok":3,"q":"Unlike a lymph node, the thymus has no afferent lymphatic vessels and no follicles. What does this tell you about the thymus?","a":"It does not filter lymph.","options":["It is not a lymphatic organ","It does not contain any lymphocytes","It does not filter lymph","It filters blood like the spleen"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Afferent vessels are the vessels that carry lymph into a lymph node so it can be filtered. Because the thymus has none, lymph never flows through it, and the thymus is not a filter. Its job is to mature T cells, which makes it a primary lymphatic organ rather than a filtering organ like a lymph node.","notes":["The thymus is a lymphatic organ, in fact a primary one, because T cells mature there. Not filtering lymph does not remove it from the lymphatic system; red bone marrow does not filter lymph either.","The thymus is packed with lymphocytes, especially immature T cells in its cortex. This choice confuses having no follicles with having no lymphocytes; follicles are just one way lymphocytes can be clustered.","Correct. With no afferent vessels, the thymus does not filter lymph.","Filtering blood is the job of the spleen, which has red pulp and venous sinuses that the thymus lacks. Pick this for a question about how the spleen differs from a lymph node."],"lt":"t-lymph-primary-organs","lid":"c39","comp":107,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-organs"},{"id":"L01940","dok":2,"q":"Which sequence correctly describes where T cells develop?","a":"They form in red bone marrow as pre-T cells, then mature in the thymus.","options":["Form in the thymus, then mature in red bone marrow","Form in the tonsils, then mature in lymph nodes","Form and mature entirely in the spleen","Form in red bone marrow, then mature in the thymus"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"All lymphocytes, T cells included, form from stem cells in the red bone marrow. T cell precursors, called pre-T cells, leave the marrow and travel to the thymus, where they mature. Mature T cells then leave the thymus for the secondary organs, such as lymph nodes and the spleen.","notes":["This reverses the order. The marrow is the starting point for every lymphocyte, and the thymus is the finishing school for T cells.","The tonsils and lymph nodes are secondary organs where mature lymphocytes meet invaders, not where they form. Choose these for a question about where T cells respond to an infection.","The spleen is a secondary organ where mature lymphocytes respond to blood-borne pathogens. Pick the spleen for a question about filtering blood, not about T cell development.","Correct. T cells form in the red bone marrow and mature in the thymus."],"lt":"t-lymph-primary-organs","lid":"c40","comp":107,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-organs"},{"id":"L01941","dok":1,"q":"Where are the hemocytoblasts, the stem cells that give rise to every blood cell including lymphocytes?","a":"In red bone marrow.","options":["In the spleen","In the thymus","In red bone marrow","In yellow bone marrow"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Hemocytoblasts are pluripotent stem cells, meaning each one can become many kinds of blood cell. They live in the red bone marrow, which is why red marrow is the site of hematopoiesis (blood cell formation) and a primary lymphatic organ. Every erythrocyte, leukocyte, and platelet traces back to these cells.","notes":["The spleen makes red blood cells in the fetus, but in adults it filters blood and stores platelets rather than holding the stem cells. Choose the spleen for a question about removing worn-out red cells.","The thymus receives pre-T cells from the marrow but has no hemocytoblasts. Pick the thymus for a question about where T cells mature.","Correct. Hemocytoblasts are in the red bone marrow.","Yellow bone marrow is mostly fat and is not an active blood-forming tissue in adults. It would be the answer for what fills the medullary cavity of an adult long bone shaft."],"lt":"t-lymph-primary-organs","lid":"c41","comp":107,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-organs"},{"id":"L01942","dok":2,"q":"A lymph node has more afferent vessels bringing lymph in than efferent vessels carrying it out. What is the effect of this arrangement?","a":"Lymph slows down inside the node, giving time for filtering.","options":["Lymph speeds up and leaves the node faster","Lymph bypasses the node and enters the blood directly","Lymph slows down inside the node for filtering","Lymph flows backward out of the afferent vessels"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Afferent vessels carry lymph into a lymph node, and fewer efferent vessels carry it out at the hilum. Because more lymph comes in than can quickly leave, the flow slows down inside the node. That gives macrophages time to engulf bacteria and debris and gives lymphocytes time to meet invaders.","notes":["Narrowing the exit does the opposite of speeding lymph up. Students who pick this are thinking of a nozzle, but here the many incoming vessels feed into fewer outgoing ones, so lymph backs up and slows.","Lymph must pass through nodes before it reaches the ducts and the subclavian veins. This choice ignores the fixed route of lymph: capillaries, collecting vessels, nodes, trunks, ducts, and then the veins.","Correct. More afferent than efferent vessels slows lymph so the node can filter it.","Valves in the lymphatic vessels keep lymph moving one way, toward the heart. Backflow is what valves prevent, so pick valves as the answer to a question about how lymph is kept from flowing backward."],"lt":"t-lymph-nodes-spleen","lid":"c1","comp":106,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-vessels-node"},{"id":"L01943","dok":1,"q":"Where are the large clusters of lymph nodes that a clinician can feel during an exam?","a":"The neck, armpits, and groin.","options":["The brain, spinal cord, and eyes","The palms, soles, and scalp","The fingertips, toes, and lips","The neck, armpits, and groin"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Lymph nodes cluster in the cervical (neck), axillary (armpit), and inguinal (groin) regions, as well as deep in the thorax and abdomen. The superficial clusters in the neck, armpits, and groin are the ones a clinician can feel through the skin. When they are swollen and tender, it usually means the nodes are fighting an infection in the region they drain.","notes":["The central nervous system has no lymphatic capillaries, so there are no node clusters in the brain or spinal cord. Pick these structures for a question about places where lymphatic capillaries are absent.","These are not sites of node clusters. Students sometimes pick them because they are easy to touch, but the palpable clusters sit where limbs and the head join the trunk.","These areas drain toward nodes farther up the limb or in the neck, but they do not hold node clusters themselves. Choosing them confuses the area a node drains with where the node actually sits.","Correct. The cervical, axillary, and inguinal clusters are the ones felt during an exam."],"lt":"t-lymph-nodes-spleen","lid":"c2","comp":107,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-organs"},{"id":"L01944","dok":1,"q":"What tissue forms the capsule around a lymph node?","a":"Dense connective tissue.","options":["Stratified squamous epithelium","Dense connective tissue","Hyaline cartilage","Smooth muscle"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"A lymph node is wrapped in a capsule of dense connective tissue, which is rich in collagen fibers that make it tough. Extensions of the capsule called trabeculae reach inward and divide the node into compartments. Afferent lymphatic vessels pierce the capsule to bring lymph in.","notes":["Stratified squamous epithelium is a protective lining in areas of abrasion, such as the epidermis, mouth, and esophagus. Pick it for a question about the lining of the oral cavity, not a capsule around an organ.","Correct. The capsule of a lymph node is dense connective tissue.","Hyaline cartilage covers joint surfaces and supports the trachea, and it has no role in lymph nodes. It would be the answer for the C-shaped rings that hold the trachea open.","Smooth muscle is found in the walls of larger lymphatic vessels and helps push lymph along, but it does not form the capsule of a node. Choose smooth muscle for a question about how lymph moves through larger vessels."],"lt":"t-lymph-nodes-spleen","lid":"c3","comp":106,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-vessels-node"},{"id":"L01945","dok":1,"q":"What are the trabeculae of a lymph node?","a":"Inward extensions of the capsule that divide the node into compartments.","options":["Vessels that carry lymph out at the hilum","Inward extensions of the capsule that form compartments","Clusters of B cells with pale germinal centers","Channels just beneath the capsule where lymph enters"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Trabeculae are strands of dense connective tissue that extend inward from the capsule of a lymph node. They divide the node into compartments and carry blood vessels into the interior. The same word is used in bone and the spleen for internal supporting strands, so the idea of an inner framework carries over.","notes":["Vessels leaving at the hilum are the efferent lymphatic vessels. Pick efferent vessels for a question about how filtered lymph exits a node.","Correct. Trabeculae are inward extensions of the capsule that divide the node into compartments.","Clusters of B cells with pale germinal centers are lymphatic follicles in the outer cortex. Choose follicles for a question about where B cells multiply in a node.","The channel just beneath the capsule is the subcapsular sinus, where lymph first enters. It would be the answer for the first space lymph reaches after the afferent vessels."],"lt":"t-lymph-nodes-spleen","lid":"c4","comp":106,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-vessels-node"},{"id":"L01946","dok":1,"q":"Which cells dominate the outer cortex of a lymph node?","a":"B cells.","options":["Erythrocytes","T cells","Plasma cells","B cells"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The outer cortex of a lymph node holds the lymphatic follicles, which are dense, round clusters made mostly of B cells. When an antigen arrives, B cells in a follicle multiply in a pale germinal center. The deep cortex beneath it holds mostly T cells.","notes":["Erythrocytes are red blood cells that stay in blood vessels and are not a resident cell of lymph node tissue. They would be the answer for the cells that fill the red pulp of the spleen.","T cells dominate the deep cortex of a lymph node, just inside the outer cortex. Pick T cells for a question about the deep cortex.","Plasma cells are concentrated in the medulla of a lymph node. Choose plasma cells for a question about the inner region of a node.","Correct. The follicles of the outer cortex are made mostly of B cells."],"lt":"t-lymph-nodes-spleen","lid":"c5","comp":106,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-vessels-node"},{"id":"L01947","dok":1,"q":"Which cells make up most of the deep cortex of a lymph node?","a":"T cells (along with dendritic cells).","options":["Plasma cells","T cells","Macrophages","B cells"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The deep cortex of a lymph node lies between the B cell follicles of the outer cortex and the medulla. It is made mostly of T cells, with dendritic cells that present antigens to them. This layered arrangement lets B cells and T cells each have their own zone while staying close enough to work together.","notes":["Plasma cells are found mainly in the medulla. They would be the answer for the antibody-secreting cells of the inner node.","Correct. The deep cortex is mostly T cells.","Macrophages are concentrated along the medullary sinuses, where they engulf bacteria and debris. Choose them for a question about the cells lining the sinuses of the medulla.","B cells dominate the follicles of the outer cortex, not the deep cortex. Pick B cells for a question about the outer cortex."],"lt":"t-lymph-nodes-spleen","lid":"c6","comp":106,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-vessels-node"},{"id":"L01948","dok":2,"q":"The medulla of a lymph node contains medullary cords and medullary sinuses. Which cells line the medullary sinuses?","a":"Macrophages.","options":["Thymic epithelial cells","Megakaryocytes","Macrophages","Erythrocytes"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The medullary sinuses are channels that lymph flows through on its way to the efferent vessels at the hilum. They are lined with macrophages, which engulf bacteria, debris, and even cancer cells as the lymph passes. The medullary cords between the sinuses hold B cells and plasma cells.","notes":["Thymic epithelial cells are found only in the thymus, where they support developing T cells. Pick them for a question about the cells that release thymic hormones.","Megakaryocytes are giant cells in red bone marrow that break apart into platelets. They would be the answer for a question about platelet formation.","Correct. Macrophages line the medullary sinuses and clean the lymph as it passes.","Erythrocytes are red blood cells, which stay in the blood and are not part of a lymph node's sinuses. Choose them for a question about the splenic red pulp."],"lt":"t-lymph-nodes-spleen","lid":"c7","comp":106,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-vessels-node"},{"id":"L01949","dok":1,"q":"Where in a lymph node are the lymphatic nodules (follicles) found?","a":"In the outer cortex.","options":["In the medulla","In the outer cortex","In the hilum","In the deep cortex"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Lymphatic nodules, also called follicles, are round clusters of mostly B cells located in the outer cortex of a lymph node. When an antigen reaches the node, B cells in a follicle multiply in a pale germinal center. The deeper layers of the node have different jobs: T cells in the deep cortex and macrophages along the medullary sinuses.","notes":["The medulla is the inner region of cords and sinuses with B cells, plasma cells, and macrophages, not follicles. Pick the medulla for a question about where plasma cells collect.","Correct. Follicles sit in the outer cortex of a lymph node.","The hilum is the indented side where blood vessels enter and efferent vessels leave. It would be the answer for where filtered lymph exits the node.","The deep cortex holds mostly T cells and dendritic cells in a more diffuse arrangement. Choose it for a question about the T cell zone of a node."],"lt":"t-lymph-nodes-spleen","lid":"c8","comp":106,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-vessels-node"},{"id":"L01951","dok":1,"q":"What does a primary lymphatic nodule mainly contain?","a":"B cells.","options":["T cells","Plasma cells","B cells","Macrophages"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"A primary lymphatic nodule is a round cluster made mainly of B cells that have not yet met their antigen. When an antigen arrives, the nodule develops a pale germinal center of multiplying B cells and becomes a secondary nodule. The B cells there then become plasma cells and memory B cells.","notes":["T cells are mainly found in the deep cortex, outside the nodules. Pick T cells for a question about the deep cortex of a node.","Plasma cells form later, after B cells are activated, and they collect in the medulla. They would be the answer for the antibody-secreting cells of a node.","Correct. A primary nodule is made mainly of B cells.","Macrophages are concentrated along the sinuses of the medulla. Choose them for a question about the cells that engulf bacteria in lymph."],"lt":"t-lymph-nodes-spleen","lid":"c10","comp":106,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-vessels-node"},{"id":"L01952","dok":2,"q":"What makes a lymphatic nodule a secondary nodule?","a":"It formed after antigen exposure and has a germinal center of multiplying B cells.","options":["It is found only in the medulla of the thymus","It lacks lymphocytes and contains only macrophages","It sits in the deep cortex and contains mostly T cells","It formed after antigen exposure and has a germinal center"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"A secondary nodule develops when B cells in a primary nodule meet an antigen. The B cells multiply in a pale central area called a germinal center and become plasma cells and memory B cells. A primary nodule, by contrast, is a cluster of B cells without a germinal center.","notes":["The thymus has no nodules at all, and its medulla holds mature T cells and thymic corpuscles. This would fit a question about where Hassall's corpuscles are found.","Nodules are made of lymphocytes, mostly B cells. Macrophages line the medullary sinuses, so choose this for a question about the cells that clean lymph in the medulla.","The deep cortex is the T cell zone of a node, and nodules are B cell clusters in the outer cortex. Pick this for a question about the deep cortex.","Correct. A secondary nodule forms in response to an antigen and has a germinal center."],"lt":"t-lymph-nodes-spleen","lid":"c11","comp":106,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-vessels-node"},{"id":"L01953","dok":1,"q":"In which abdominopelvic region is the spleen located?","a":"The left hypochondriac region.","options":["The left hypochondriac region","The epigastric region","The left lumbar region","The right hypochondriac region"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The spleen sits in the upper left abdomen, against the diaphragm and behind the stomach, which places it in the left hypochondriac region. Hypochondriac means below the cartilage of the ribs, and the lower left ribs protect the spleen. This is why a hard blow to the left upper abdomen can rupture it.","notes":["Correct. The spleen is in the left hypochondriac region.","The epigastric region is the upper middle region, holding much of the stomach and part of the liver. Choose it for a question about pain just below the sternum.","The left lumbar region is below the left hypochondriac region, beside the umbilical region, and contains the descending colon. It would be the answer for a question about the region lateral to the umbilicus on the left.","The right hypochondriac region holds most of the liver and the gallbladder. Pick it for a question about where the liver mainly sits."],"lt":"t-lymph-nodes-spleen","lid":"c12","comp":107,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-organs"},{"id":"L01954","dok":1,"q":"Which is the largest lymphatic organ in the body?","a":"The spleen.","options":["The spleen","The thymus","A palatine tonsil","A lymph node"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The spleen is the largest lymphatic organ in the body. It sits in the upper left abdomen behind the stomach and filters blood rather than lymph. Its white pulp carries out immune functions, and its red pulp removes worn-out red blood cells and platelets.","notes":["Correct. The spleen is the largest lymphatic organ.","The thymus is large in children but shrinks after puberty, and even at its largest it is smaller than the spleen. Pick the thymus for a question about where T cells mature.","The palatine tonsils are the largest of the tonsils, but they are much smaller than the spleen. They would be the answer for the largest tonsil, which is usually the one removed in a tonsillectomy.","Lymph nodes are small bean-shaped organs, numbering in the hundreds. Choose a lymph node for a question about the organs that filter lymph."],"lt":"t-lymph-nodes-spleen","lid":"c13","comp":107,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-organs"},{"id":"L01955","dok":1,"q":"What forms the covering and internal framework of the spleen?","a":"A dense connective tissue capsule with trabeculae extending inward.","options":["A layer of stratified squamous epithelium","A dense connective tissue capsule and trabeculae","Plates of hyaline cartilage and perichondrium","A sheet of skeletal muscle with tendons"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Like a lymph node, the spleen is wrapped in a capsule of dense connective tissue. Strands called trabeculae extend inward from the capsule and form part of the internal framework, carrying the trabecular arteries and veins. Inside this framework are the red pulp and the white pulp.","notes":["Stratified squamous epithelium is a protective lining in places of abrasion such as the skin, mouth, and esophagus. Pick it for a question about the lining of the esophagus.","Correct. The spleen has a dense connective tissue capsule with trabeculae extending inward.","Hyaline cartilage supports structures such as the trachea and covers joint surfaces. It would be the answer for the tissue forming the C-shaped tracheal rings.","Skeletal muscle forms the muscles that move the skeleton, and it is not part of the spleen. Choose skeletal muscle for a question about the skeletal muscle pump that squeezes lymphatic vessels."],"lt":"t-lymph-nodes-spleen","lid":"c14","comp":107,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-organs"},{"id":"L01956","dok":1,"q":"Which description fits the white pulp of the spleen?","a":"Small islands of lymphocytes clustered around central arteries.","options":["Concentric whorls of epithelial cells in a medulla","Follicles with germinal centers beneath a subcapsular sinus","Small islands of lymphocytes clustered around central arteries","Venous sinuses and cords full of red cells and macrophages"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"White pulp is lymphatic tissue made of lymphocytes, both B cells and T cells, clustered around the central arteries. It appears as small islands scattered through the much larger red pulp. White pulp handles the spleen's immune function, responding to pathogens carried in the blood.","notes":["Concentric whorls of epithelial cells are thymic (Hassall's) corpuscles in the medulla of the thymus. Choose them for a question about identifying thymus tissue.","Follicles beneath a subcapsular sinus describe the outer cortex of a lymph node. This would be the answer for a question about lymph node structure.","Correct. White pulp is islands of lymphocytes around central arteries.","This describes red pulp, which makes up most of the spleen and filters old red cells from the blood. Pick it for a question about red pulp."],"lt":"t-lymph-nodes-spleen","lid":"c15","comp":107,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-organs"},{"id":"L01957","dok":1,"q":"What is the red pulp of the spleen made of?","a":"Venous sinuses and splenic cords full of red blood cells and macrophages.","options":["Dense connective tissue strands that extend from the capsule","Venous sinuses and splenic cords with red cells and macrophages","Lymphocytes clustered in islands around the central arteries","Medullary cords and sinuses that drain to efferent vessels"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Red pulp makes up most of the spleen. It is built of wide blood channels called venous sinuses and the splenic cords (Billroth's cords) between them, which are full of red blood cells, macrophages, lymphocytes, and plasma cells. Here macrophages remove worn-out red cells and platelets as blood moves from the cords into the sinuses.","notes":["Connective tissue strands from the capsule are trabeculae, which form part of the spleen's framework. They would be the answer for what carries the trabecular arteries and veins into the spleen.","Correct. Red pulp is venous sinuses and splenic cords packed with red cells and macrophages.","Lymphocytes around central arteries form the white pulp, the spleen's immune tissue. Pick this for a question about white pulp.","Medullary cords and sinuses form the medulla of a lymph node, where filtered lymph heads toward the efferent vessels. Choose this for a question about the inner region of a lymph node."],"lt":"t-lymph-nodes-spleen","lid":"c16","comp":107,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-organs"},{"id":"L01958","dok":1,"q":"What are Billroth's cords?","a":"The splenic cords of the red pulp, found between the venous sinuses.","options":["The splenic cords of the red pulp, between the venous sinuses","Whorls of epithelial cells inside the thymic medulla","Medullary cords inside a lymph node","Sleeves of lymphocytes around the central arteries"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Billroth's cords is another name for the splenic cords, the strips of tissue in the red pulp that lie between the venous sinuses. They are packed with red blood cells, macrophages, lymphocytes, and plasma cells. As blood squeezes from the cords into the sinuses, old and damaged red cells get caught and are eaten by macrophages.","notes":["Correct. Billroth's cords are the splenic cords of the red pulp.","Whorls of epithelial cells in the thymic medulla are Hassall's (thymic) corpuscles. They would be the answer for the feature used to identify the thymic medulla.","Medullary cords are in the medulla of a lymph node and hold B cells and plasma cells. Choose them for a question about lymph node structure, since Billroth's cords belong to the spleen.","Lymphocyte sleeves around central arteries are the white pulp of the spleen. Pick this for a question about white pulp."],"lt":"t-lymph-nodes-spleen","lid":"c17","comp":107,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-organs"},{"id":"L01959","dok":2,"q":"Which list correctly gives functions of the splenic red pulp?","a":"Removing worn-out blood cells, storing platelets, and making red cells in the fetus.","options":["Removing worn-out blood cells, storing platelets, and fetal red cell formation","Maturing T cells, releasing thymic hormones, and forming corpuscles","Absorbing dietary fats, forming chyle, and draining to the cisterna chyli","Filtering lymph, forming follicles, and draining to efferent vessels"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The red pulp is where the spleen handles the blood itself. Macrophages in its cords remove worn-out red blood cells and platelets, the red pulp stores a supply of platelets, and in the fetus it makes red blood cells. The white pulp, in contrast, carries out the immune response to blood-borne pathogens.","notes":["Correct. Red pulp removes worn-out cells, stores platelets, and makes red cells in the fetus.","These are functions of the thymus, where T cells mature with help from epithelial cells and thymic hormones. Pick this list for a question about the thymus.","These are the jobs of the lacteals of the small intestine, which absorb dietary fat as milky chyle. This list would answer a question about lacteals.","These are features of a lymph node, which filters lymph and holds follicles in its outer cortex. Choose this list for a question about lymph node function."],"lt":"t-lymph-nodes-spleen","lid":"c18","comp":107,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-organs"},{"id":"L01960","dok":1,"q":"What is the dense connective tissue covering of a lymph node called?","a":"The capsule.","options":["The capsule","The hilum","The cortex","The trabecula"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The outer covering of a lymph node is its capsule, a layer of dense connective tissue. Afferent lymphatic vessels pass through the capsule to deliver lymph into the subcapsular sinus just beneath it. Trabeculae extend inward from the capsule to divide the node into compartments.","notes":["Correct. The capsule is the dense connective tissue covering of a lymph node.","The hilum is the indented side of the node where blood vessels enter and efferent vessels leave. It would be the answer for where filtered lymph exits.","The cortex is the region just inside the capsule, holding B cell follicles and the T cell zone. Pick the cortex for a question about where follicles are found.","A trabecula is an inward extension of the capsule, not the covering itself. Choose trabeculae for a question about what divides the node into compartments."],"lt":"t-lymph-nodes-spleen","lid":"c19","comp":106,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-vessels-node"},{"id":"L01961","dok":2,"q":"Which region of a lymph node contains mostly T cells and dendritic cells?","a":"The deep cortex.","options":["The deep cortex","The capsule","The medullary sinus","The outer cortex"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The deep cortex, sometimes called the inner cortex, lies between the B cell follicles of the outer cortex and the medulla. It holds mostly T cells, with dendritic cells that present antigens to them. The node is layered so that each type of lymphocyte has its own zone.","notes":["Correct. The deep cortex is the T cell zone of a lymph node.","The capsule is the dense connective tissue covering, not a region full of lymphocytes. Choose the capsule for a question about what covers the outside of the node.","The medullary sinuses are channels lined with macrophages that lymph passes through before leaving. They would be the answer for where macrophages clean lymph near the exit.","The outer cortex holds the follicles, which are mostly B cells. Pick the outer cortex for a question about where B cells cluster."],"lt":"t-lymph-nodes-spleen","lid":"c20","comp":106,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-vessels-node"},{"id":"L01962","dok":2,"q":"Where in a lymph node are plasma cells concentrated?","a":"In the medulla.","options":["In the trabeculae","In the capsule","In the medulla","In the deep cortex"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"After B cells are activated in the follicles of the outer cortex, many become plasma cells that collect in the medullary cords of the medulla. There they secrete antibodies into the lymph that leaves the node. The medulla also holds B cells and macrophages along its sinuses.","notes":["Trabeculae are connective tissue strands that divide the node into compartments. They would be the answer for the inward extensions of the capsule.","The capsule is a connective tissue covering with no lymphocyte zones. Choose the capsule for a question about the outer covering of a node.","Correct. Plasma cells are concentrated in the medulla.","The deep cortex holds mostly T cells and dendritic cells. Pick it for a question about the T cell zone."],"lt":"t-lymph-nodes-spleen","lid":"c21","comp":106,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-vessels-node"},{"id":"L01963","dok":2,"q":"A lymphatic nodule that formed in response to an antigen and shows multiplying B cells is called what?","a":"A secondary nodule.","options":["A primary nodule","A thymic corpuscle","A secondary nodule","A splenic cord"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Lymphatic nodules start as primary nodules, clusters of B cells that have not yet met their antigen. Once an antigen arrives, the B cells multiply in a pale germinal center, and the nodule is then called a secondary nodule. The B cells in a secondary nodule become plasma cells and memory B cells.","notes":["A primary nodule is a cluster of mainly B cells before antigen exposure, without a germinal center. Pick it for a question about a resting B cell cluster.","A thymic corpuscle is a whorl of epithelial cells in the thymic medulla, not a cluster of B cells. Choose it for a question about identifying thymus tissue.","Correct. A secondary nodule forms after antigen exposure and has proliferating B cells.","A splenic cord (Billroth's cord) is a strip of red pulp between venous sinuses in the spleen. It would be the answer for where old red cells are caught and removed."],"lt":"t-lymph-nodes-spleen","lid":"c22","comp":106,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-vessels-node"},{"id":"L01964","dok":1,"q":"Which bones protect the spleen?","a":"The lower left ribs.","options":["The lower left ribs","The left hip bone","The sternum","The lower right ribs"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The spleen sits in the upper left abdomen against the diaphragm, behind the stomach. The lower left ribs curve around it and protect it. When those ribs are fractured, or the left upper abdomen takes a hard blow, the spleen can tear and bleed heavily.","notes":["Correct. The lower left ribs protect the spleen.","The left hip bone surrounds the lower abdominopelvic cavity, far below the spleen. It would be the answer for a question about the bone that houses the iliac crest, a site for marrow samples.","The sternum is in the midline of the anterior chest and lies in front of the thymus and heart. Choose it for a question about which bone sits in front of the thymus.","The lower right ribs protect the liver, which fills most of the right upper abdomen. Pick them for a question about the bones that shield the liver."],"lt":"t-lymph-nodes-spleen","lid":"c23","comp":107,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-organs"},{"id":"L01965","dok":2,"q":"Which tissue of the spleen carries out its immune functions?","a":"White pulp.","options":["The capsule","White pulp","Red pulp","The venous sinuses"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"White pulp is the spleen's lymphatic tissue, made of B cells and T cells clustered around the central arteries. As blood flows past, these lymphocytes respond to pathogens carried in the blood. Red pulp, which makes up most of the organ, handles the blood itself by removing old cells.","notes":["The capsule is the dense connective tissue covering of the spleen and has no lymphocyte clusters. Choose the capsule for a question about what surrounds the organ.","Correct. White pulp carries out the spleen's immune function.","Red pulp filters blood and removes worn-out red cells and platelets, rather than mounting immune responses. Pick red pulp for a question about removing old erythrocytes.","Venous sinuses are wide blood channels within the red pulp. They would be the answer for where blood collects after leaving the splenic cords."],"lt":"t-lymph-nodes-spleen","lid":"c24","comp":107,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-organs"},{"id":"L01966","dok":2,"q":"Which tissue of the spleen contains the venous sinuses and Billroth's cords?","a":"Red pulp.","options":["White pulp","The medulla","Red pulp","The cortex"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Red pulp is made of venous sinuses and the splenic cords (Billroth's cords) between them. Blood moves from the cords into the sinuses, and macrophages remove old and damaged red cells along the way. Red pulp makes up most of the spleen, with white pulp scattered through it as small islands.","notes":["White pulp is lymphocytes clustered around central arteries, with no venous sinuses. Pick white pulp for a question about the spleen's immune tissue.","The medulla is a region of a lymph node or the thymus, not of the spleen. It would be the answer for where plasma cells collect in a lymph node.","Correct. Venous sinuses and Billroth's cords make up the red pulp.","The spleen is not divided into a cortex and medulla; those terms describe a lymph node, the thymus, or the kidney. Choose the cortex for a question about where follicles sit in a lymph node."],"lt":"t-lymph-nodes-spleen","lid":"c25","comp":107,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-organs"},{"id":"L01967","dok":2,"q":"Which job belongs to the red pulp of the spleen rather than the white pulp?","a":"Removing worn-out red blood cells.","options":["Mounting immune responses to blood-borne pathogens","Absorbing dietary fats","Maturing pre-T cells","Removing worn-out red blood cells"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Red pulp is where the spleen deals with the blood itself. Macrophages in its splenic cords catch and destroy worn-out red blood cells and platelets, and the iron from old red cells is saved for reuse. White pulp, by contrast, is lymphatic tissue that mounts immune responses.","notes":["Mounting immune responses is the job of the white pulp, where lymphocytes surround the central arteries. Pick this for a question about white pulp.","Dietary fats are absorbed by the lacteals of the small intestine. This would be the answer for a question about what lacteals do.","Pre-T cells mature in the thymus, not the spleen. Choose this for a question about the job of the thymic cortex.","Correct. Red pulp removes worn-out red blood cells."],"lt":"t-lymph-nodes-spleen","lid":"c26","comp":107,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-organs"},{"id":"L01968","dok":2,"q":"During a physical exam, a clinician feels the neck, armpits, and groin for swollen glands. What structures is she checking?","a":"Lymph nodes.","options":["Lymph nodes","Tonsils","Sweat glands","Lymphatic trunks"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Swollen glands are enlarged lymph nodes. The large superficial clusters of nodes sit in the cervical (neck), axillary (armpit), and inguinal (groin) regions, close enough to the skin to be felt. Nodes swell when they are fighting an infection in the area they drain, or when cancer cells have spread into them.","notes":["Correct. The clinician is feeling the cervical, axillary, and inguinal lymph nodes.","The tonsils are in the throat, not the armpits or groin. Pick the tonsils for a question about lymphatic tissue seen at the back of the mouth.","Sweat glands are in the dermis all over the body and are far too small to feel. Students pick them because of the word glands, but in this setting glands is an informal name for lymph nodes.","Lymphatic trunks are large vessels deep in the trunk, formed by merging collecting vessels, and they cannot be felt through the skin. Choose them for a question about the vessels that empty into the lymphatic ducts."],"lt":"t-lymph-nodes-spleen","lid":"c27","comp":108,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-disorders"},{"id":"L01969","dok":2,"q":"A small, bean-shaped structure sits along a lymphatic collecting vessel and filters the lymph. What is it?","a":"A lymph node.","options":["A lymph node","A lacteal","The thymus","The spleen"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Lymph nodes are small bean-shaped organs spaced along the collecting vessels. Lymph enters through afferent vessels, slows down inside, and is cleaned by macrophages while lymphocytes check it for invaders. Filtered lymph then leaves through efferent vessels at the hilum.","notes":["Correct. A lymph node is a small bean-shaped filter along the collecting vessels.","A lacteal is a lymphatic capillary in an intestinal villus that absorbs dietary fat. It would be the answer for where chyle begins.","The thymus is a bilobed organ in the mediastinum where T cells mature, and it does not filter lymph. Choose it for a question about T cell maturation.","The spleen is a single large organ in the upper left abdomen that filters blood, not lymph. Pick the spleen for a question about filtering blood."],"lt":"t-lymph-nodes-spleen","lid":"c28","comp":106,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-vessels-node"},{"id":"L01970","dok":3,"q":"Old red blood cells broken down in the spleen release products into the splenic vein, which joins the hepatic portal vein. Which organ receives these products next?","a":"The liver.","options":["The kidneys","The pancreas","The lungs","The liver"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Blood leaving the spleen drains into the splenic vein, which joins the hepatic portal vein. The hepatic portal vein carries blood straight to the liver before it returns to the heart. As a result, the breakdown products of old red cells reach the liver first, where they can be processed.","notes":["The kidneys receive blood from the renal arteries off the abdominal aorta, not from the hepatic portal vein. Choose the kidneys for a question about the renal blood supply.","The splenic vein runs along the back of the pancreas and receives small veins from it, but it does not deliver blood into the pancreas. Pick the pancreas for a question about which organ the splenic vein passes behind.","The lungs receive blood through the pulmonary trunk after it returns to the right side of the heart, well after the liver. They would be the answer for where blood is sent to pick up oxygen.","Correct. The splenic vein feeds the hepatic portal vein, which delivers blood to the liver."],"lt":"t-lymph-nodes-spleen","lid":"c29","comp":126,"competencyId":"w5-lab-abdominal-vessels"},{"id":"L01971","dok":3,"q":"A slide of a lymph node's outer cortex shows a round cluster of B cells with a pale center where cells are multiplying. What is the pale center called?","a":"A germinal center.","options":["A thymic corpuscle","A medullary sinus","The subcapsular sinus","A germinal center"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The pale center of a lymphatic follicle is called the germinal center. It forms after an antigen arrives, when B cells in the follicle begin multiplying rapidly. The dividing cells there become plasma cells and memory B cells, and a follicle with a germinal center is a secondary nodule.","notes":["A thymic corpuscle is a whorl of epithelial cells found only in the thymic medulla. Choose it for a question about identifying the thymus.","A medullary sinus is a channel in the medulla, deep in the node, lined with macrophages. Pick it for a question about where macrophages clean lymph before it leaves.","The subcapsular sinus is the space just under the capsule where lymph first enters the node. It would be the answer for the first space lymph reaches after the afferent vessels.","Correct. The pale center of a follicle is the germinal center."],"lt":"t-lymph-nodes-spleen","lid":"c30","comp":106,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-vessels-node"},{"id":"L01972","dok":3,"q":"A patient takes a hard blow to the left upper abdomen and develops heavy internal bleeding from a torn lymphatic organ. Which organ is it?","a":"The spleen.","options":["The spleen","An inguinal lymph node","A palatine tonsil","The thymus"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The spleen sits in the left upper abdomen behind the stomach, protected by the lower left ribs. Its soft tissue tears easily and it holds a large volume of blood, so a tear causes heavy internal bleeding, called a ruptured spleen. Patients with mononucleosis are told to avoid contact sports because their enlarged spleen ruptures more easily.","notes":["Correct. A blow to the left upper abdomen can rupture the spleen.","Inguinal lymph nodes are in the groin, far from the left upper abdomen, and they do not hold enough blood to cause heavy bleeding. Choose them for a question about swollen nodes after a leg infection.","The palatine tonsils are in the lateral walls of the oropharynx. They would be the answer for a question about bleeding after a tonsillectomy.","The thymus is in the mediastinum behind the sternum, not the left upper abdomen. Pick the thymus for a question about chest trauma near the sternum."],"lt":"t-lymph-nodes-spleen","lid":"c31","comp":108,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-disorders"},{"id":"L01973","dok":2,"q":"Which structures divide a lymph node into compartments?","a":"Trabeculae.","options":["Anchoring filaments","Billroth's cords","Trabeculae","Hassall's corpuscles"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Trabeculae are inward extensions of the dense connective tissue capsule of a lymph node. They reach into the node and divide it into compartments, also carrying blood vessels inward. The spleen has trabeculae too, built the same way from its capsule.","notes":["Anchoring filaments tie lymphatic capillaries to the surrounding tissue so the capillaries stay open. They would be the answer for what holds a lymphatic capillary open.","Billroth's cords are the splenic cords in the red pulp of the spleen. Pick them for a question about where old red cells are caught in the spleen.","Correct. Trabeculae divide a lymph node into compartments.","Hassall's corpuscles are whorls of epithelial cells in the thymic medulla. Choose them for a question about a feature unique to the thymus."],"lt":"t-lymph-nodes-spleen","lid":"c32","comp":106,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-vessels-node"},{"id":"L01975","dok":1,"q":"What is the inner region of a lymph node, made of cords and sinuses, called?","a":"The medulla.","options":["The deep cortex","The medulla","The subcapsular sinus","The hilum"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The medulla is the inner region of a lymph node. It is made of medullary cords, which hold B cells and plasma cells, and medullary sinuses lined with macrophages. Lymph passes through the medullary sinuses last before leaving through the efferent vessels at the hilum.","notes":["The deep cortex lies between the outer cortex and the medulla and holds mostly T cells. Pick it for a question about the T cell zone.","Correct. The medulla is the inner region of cords and sinuses.","The subcapsular sinus is the space just under the capsule, at the outer edge of the node. It would be the answer for where lymph first enters.","The hilum is the indented side of the node where vessels enter and leave, not the internal region. Choose it for a question about where efferent vessels exit."],"lt":"t-lymph-nodes-spleen","lid":"c34","comp":106,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-vessels-node"},{"id":"L01976","dok":1,"q":"At which part of a lymph node do the efferent vessels leave and the blood vessels enter?","a":"The hilum.","options":["The outer cortex","The subcapsular sinus","The capsule","The hilum"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The hilum is the indented side of a bean-shaped lymph node. Efferent lymphatic vessels carry filtered lymph out at the hilum, and blood vessels enter and leave there too. Afferent vessels, in contrast, enter at many points around the convex surface through the capsule.","notes":["The outer cortex holds B cell follicles just inside the capsule. Choose it for a question about where follicles with germinal centers are found.","The subcapsular sinus is the first space lymph enters after the afferent vessels. It would be the answer for the start of lymph's path through the node.","The capsule is where the afferent vessels enter, all around the curved surface of the node. Pick the capsule for a question about where lymph comes into the node.","Correct. Efferent vessels leave and blood vessels enter at the hilum."],"lt":"t-lymph-nodes-spleen","lid":"c35","comp":106,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-vessels-node"},{"id":"L01977","dok":2,"q":"Which structure belongs to the spleen rather than to a lymph node?","a":"Billroth's cords.","options":["Billroth's cords","The deep cortex","The subcapsular sinus","Afferent lymphatic vessels"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Billroth's cords, the splenic cords, are strips of red pulp between the venous sinuses of the spleen. They hold red cells and macrophages that remove old blood cells. Lymph nodes have no red pulp, so they have no splenic cords; their own cords are the medullary cords of the medulla.","notes":["Correct. Billroth's cords are part of the spleen's red pulp.","The deep cortex is the T cell zone of a lymph node. Pick it for a question about where T cells concentrate in a node.","The subcapsular sinus is the space under a lymph node's capsule where lymph first enters. Choose it for a question about the first stop for lymph in a node.","Afferent lymphatic vessels bring lymph into a lymph node. The spleen has none because it filters blood, so these would be the answer for a structure found only in a node."],"lt":"t-lymph-nodes-spleen","lid":"c36","comp":107,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-organs"},{"id":"L01978","dok":1,"q":"Splenic white pulp is arranged around which vessels?","a":"The central arteries.","options":["The trabecular veins","The afferent lymphatic vessels","The central arteries","The venous sinuses"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"White pulp forms sleeves of lymphocytes around the central arteries, which branch from the trabecular arteries. As blood travels through these arteries, the surrounding lymphocytes can detect blood-borne pathogens. Blood then moves on into the cords and sinuses of the red pulp.","notes":["Trabecular veins carry blood from the venous sinuses toward the splenic vein. Choose them for a question about blood leaving the spleen.","The spleen has no afferent lymphatic vessels because it filters blood, not lymph. These would be the answer for vessels carrying lymph into a lymph node.","Correct. White pulp surrounds the central arteries.","Venous sinuses are channels in the red pulp, downstream of the white pulp. Pick them for a question about where blood collects after the splenic cords."],"lt":"t-lymph-nodes-spleen","lid":"c37","comp":107,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-organs"},{"id":"L01979","dok":2,"q":"Lymph enters a lymph node through the afferent vessels. Which space does it flow into first?","a":"The subcapsular sinus.","options":["The subcapsular sinus","The medullary sinuses","The efferent vessels","The cortical sinuses"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Lymph moves through a node in a fixed order: afferent vessels, subcapsular sinus, cortical sinuses, medullary sinuses, and then efferent vessels at the hilum. The subcapsular sinus is the space just under the capsule, so it is the first space the lymph reaches. From there the lymph trickles inward past the lymphocytes and macrophages.","notes":["Correct. The subcapsular sinus is the first space lymph enters.","The medullary sinuses are the last sinuses lymph passes through before leaving. Pick them for a question about the final sinuses before the efferent vessels.","Efferent vessels carry lymph out at the hilum, at the very end of the path. Choose them for a question about how filtered lymph leaves a node.","The cortical sinuses come second, after the subcapsular sinus. They would be the answer for the step between the subcapsular sinus and the medullary sinuses."],"lt":"t-lymph-nodes-spleen","lid":"c38","comp":106,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-vessels-node"},{"id":"L01980","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares what lymph nodes and the spleen filter?","a":"Lymph nodes filter lymph, and the spleen filters blood.","options":["Lymph nodes filter lymph, and the spleen filters blood","Both lymph nodes and the spleen filter only lymph","Both lymph nodes and the spleen filter only blood","Lymph nodes filter blood, and the spleen filters lymph"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Lymph nodes sit along the lymphatic collecting vessels, so they clean lymph before it returns to the bloodstream. The spleen has no afferent lymphatic vessels; instead it receives the splenic artery and filters blood, removing old red cells and responding to blood-borne pathogens. Both are secondary lymphatic organs, but they sit on different fluid routes.","notes":["Correct. Nodes filter lymph, and the spleen filters blood.","The spleen receives blood through the splenic artery and has no afferent lymphatic vessels. This would be true only of lymph nodes.","Lymph nodes receive lymph through afferent vessels, so they do not filter only blood. This would be true only of the spleen.","This reverses the two organs. A lymph node receives afferent lymphatic vessels, while the spleen receives the splenic artery, so match each organ to the fluid that flows into it."],"lt":"t-lymph-nodes-spleen","lid":"c39","comp":107,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-organs"},{"id":"L01982","dok":2,"q":"Why does the red pulp of the spleen look red?","a":"Its venous sinuses and splenic cords are full of red blood cells.","options":["It stores oxygen-rich fat droplets in its cords","Its venous sinuses and cords are full of red blood cells","Its lymphocytes contain a red pigment of their own","Its capsule is made of red-staining elastic fibers"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Red pulp is built from venous sinuses and splenic cords that are packed with red blood cells, along with macrophages, lymphocytes, and plasma cells. All those red cells give the tissue its color. White pulp looks different because it is made mostly of lymphocytes around the central arteries.","notes":["The spleen does not store fat or oxygen as droplets. This choice confuses the spleen with fatty tissue such as yellow marrow; oxygen in blood is carried by hemoglobin inside red cells.","Correct. The red color comes from the red blood cells filling the sinuses and cords.","Lymphocytes have no red pigment; the red pigment of blood is hemoglobin, found in erythrocytes. Lymphocytes are what make white pulp look different from red pulp.","The capsule of the spleen is dense connective tissue, and it covers the outside rather than giving the inner pulp its color. Choose the capsule for a question about what surrounds the spleen."],"lt":"t-lymph-nodes-spleen","lid":"c41","comp":107,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-organs"},{"id":"L01983","dok":1,"q":"What does MALT stand for?","a":"Mucosa-associated lymphatic tissue.","options":["Medulla-associated lymph tubules","Mucosa-associated lymphatic tissue","Muscle-attached lymphatic tract","Membrane-anchored lymphocyte tissue"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"MALT stands for mucosa-associated lymphatic tissue. It is lymphatic tissue scattered in the mucous membranes that line the digestive, respiratory, urinary, and reproductive tracts. Because these tracts open to the outside, MALT is positioned to meet pathogens right where they try to enter.","notes":["MALT is not tied to the medulla of any organ, and it is tissue rather than tubules. Students who pick this are mixing it up with the medulla of a lymph node or thymus.","Correct. MALT is mucosa-associated lymphatic tissue.","MALT has nothing to do with muscle or a tract of vessels. This choice comes from guessing at the letters rather than remembering that MALT lives in mucous membranes.","MALT is not anchored to a membrane by filaments. Anchoring filaments belong to lymphatic capillaries, which would be the answer for what holds those capillaries open."],"lt":"t-lymph-malt-tonsils","lid":"c1","comp":107,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-organs"},{"id":"L01984","dok":1,"q":"In the wall of the digestive tract, in which layer is MALT scattered?","a":"The lamina propria of the mucosa.","options":["The adventitia","The muscularis externa","The lamina propria of the mucosa","The serosa"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The mucosa is the innermost layer of the gut wall, made of an epithelium, a lamina propria of areolar connective tissue beneath it, and a thin muscularis mucosae. MALT is scattered in the lamina propria, just under the epithelium that contacts food and microbes. That position lets lymphocytes meet pathogens as soon as they cross the lining.","notes":["The adventitia is a fibrous outer layer that replaces the serosa on the esophagus. It would be the answer for the outer layer of the esophagus.","The muscularis externa is the smooth muscle layer that moves food along, with the myenteric plexus between its layers. Pick it for a question about peristalsis.","Correct. MALT sits in the lamina propria of the mucosa.","The serosa is the outermost layer of most of the gut, a serous membrane also called the visceral peritoneum. Choose it for a question about the outer covering of the stomach or intestines."],"lt":"t-lymph-malt-tonsils","lid":"c2","comp":109,"competencyId":"w5-canal-wall"},{"id":"L01985","dok":1,"q":"MALT lines the mucous membranes of which body systems?","a":"The digestive, respiratory, urinary, and reproductive tracts.","options":["The bones, cartilages, and synovial joints","The heart chambers, arteries, veins, and capillaries","The brain, spinal cord, and peripheral nerves","The digestive, respiratory, urinary, and reproductive tracts"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"MALT guards mucous membranes, which line the body tracts that open to the outside: the digestive, respiratory, urinary, and reproductive tracts. These are the entry points where pathogens are most likely to get in. Placing lymphatic tissue right in these linings lets the body respond at the point of entry.","notes":["Bones and synovial joints have no mucous membranes; synovial joints are lined by a synovial membrane instead. These would be the answer for a question about where synovial fluid is made.","The heart and blood vessels are lined by endothelium, not a mucous membrane, so they have no MALT. Pick them for a question about the tunica intima.","The brain and spinal cord have no mucous membranes and no lymphatic capillaries. Choose them for a question about places where lymphatic capillaries are absent.","Correct. MALT lines the digestive, respiratory, urinary, and reproductive tracts."],"lt":"t-lymph-malt-tonsils","lid":"c3","comp":107,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-organs"},{"id":"L01986","dok":1,"q":"Lymphatic tissue scattered through the mucous membranes, rather than gathered into an encapsulated organ, is called what?","a":"MALT.","options":["MALT","Lymph nodes","White pulp","Thymic lobules"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"MALT, mucosa-associated lymphatic tissue, is lymphatic tissue in the mucous membranes of the body's tracts. It has no surrounding capsule, which sets it apart from encapsulated organs like lymph nodes, the spleen, and the thymus. Peyer patches in the ileum, the appendix, and the tonsils are larger collections of this kind of tissue.","notes":["Correct. MALT is unencapsulated lymphatic tissue in mucous membranes.","Lymph nodes are encapsulated organs along the collecting vessels. Pick them for a question about the organs that filter lymph.","White pulp is lymphatic tissue inside the encapsulated spleen, around the central arteries. Choose it for a question about the immune tissue of the spleen.","Thymic lobules are subdivisions of the encapsulated thymus. They would be the answer for the units that each contain a cortex and medulla in the thymus."],"lt":"t-lymph-malt-tonsils","lid":"c4","comp":107,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-organs"},{"id":"L01987","dok":1,"q":"The tonsils form a ring of lymphatic tissue around the entrance to which passage?","a":"The pharynx (throat).","options":["The esophagus","The pharynx","The trachea","The larynx"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The tonsils form a ring around the entrance to the pharynx, the throat, where the nasal cavity and the oral cavity open into it. The pharyngeal tonsil sits on the back wall of the nasopharynx, the palatine tonsils in the lateral walls of the oropharynx, and the lingual tonsils at the base of the tongue. This ring guards against pathogens that are inhaled or swallowed.","notes":["The esophagus begins below the pharynx and carries food to the stomach. It would be the answer for the tube that passes through the esophageal hiatus of the diaphragm.","Correct. The tonsils ring the entrance to the pharynx.","The trachea is the airway below the larynx, held open by C-shaped cartilage rings. Choose it for a question about the airway that splits at the carina.","The larynx is the voice box below the pharynx, guarded by the epiglottis rather than the tonsils. Pick the larynx for a question about the vocal folds."],"lt":"t-lymph-malt-tonsils","lid":"c5","comp":107,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-organs"},{"id":"L01988","dok":1,"q":"What is the pharyngeal tonsil also called?","a":"The adenoid.","options":["The uvula","The lingual tonsil","The adenoid","The epiglottis"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The pharyngeal tonsil is also called the adenoid. It is a single mass of lymphatic tissue on the posterior wall of the nasopharynx, the part of the throat behind the nasal cavity. When it swells, especially in children, it can block airflow through the nose.","notes":["The uvula is the fleshy tab hanging from the back of the soft palate, not lymphatic tissue. Pick the uvula for a question about the soft palate.","The lingual tonsils are a separate set of tonsils at the base of the tongue. They would be the answer for the tonsils that guard swallowed material at the tongue's root.","Correct. The pharyngeal tonsil is the adenoid.","The epiglottis is a cartilage flap that covers the larynx during swallowing. Choose it for a question about what keeps food out of the airway."],"lt":"t-lymph-malt-tonsils","lid":"c6","comp":107,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-organs"},{"id":"L01989","dok":1,"q":"Where is the pharyngeal tonsil (adenoid) located?","a":"On the posterior wall of the nasopharynx.","options":["In the lateral walls of the oropharynx","At the base of the tongue","On the posterior wall of the nasopharynx","At the openings of the auditory tubes"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The pharyngeal tonsil, or adenoid, sits on the posterior wall of the nasopharynx, the uppermost part of the pharynx behind the nasal cavity. Because it is in the path of air coming through the nose, it guards against inhaled pathogens. It is the highest of the tonsils in the ring around the throat.","notes":["The lateral walls of the oropharynx, between the palatal arches, hold the palatine tonsils. Pick this for a question about the palatine tonsils.","The base of the tongue holds the lingual tonsils. Choose this location for a question about the lingual tonsils.","Correct. The adenoid sits on the back wall of the nasopharynx.","The openings of the auditory tubes hold the small paired tubal tonsils. This would be the answer for a question about the tubal tonsils."],"lt":"t-lymph-malt-tonsils","lid":"c7","comp":107,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-organs"},{"id":"L01990","dok":1,"q":"Where are the palatine tonsils located?","a":"In the lateral walls of the oropharynx.","options":["At the openings of the auditory tubes","In the lateral walls of the oropharynx","On the posterior wall of the nasopharynx","At the base of the tongue"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The palatine tonsils are a pair of lymphatic masses in the lateral walls of the oropharynx, tucked between the palatal arches. They are the largest tonsils and the ones you can see when someone opens wide and says ah. They are also the tonsils most often infected and removed.","notes":["The openings of the auditory tubes hold the tubal tonsils. This would be the answer for the tonsils near the tube connecting the middle ear to the throat.","Correct. The palatine tonsils sit in the lateral walls of the oropharynx.","The posterior wall of the nasopharynx holds the single pharyngeal tonsil, or adenoid. Pick this for a question about the adenoid.","The base of the tongue holds the lingual tonsils. Choose this location for a question about the lingual tonsils."],"lt":"t-lymph-malt-tonsils","lid":"c8","comp":107,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-organs"},{"id":"L01991","dok":1,"q":"Where are the lingual tonsils located?","a":"At the base of the tongue.","options":["In the lateral walls of the oropharynx","At the base of the tongue","At the openings of the auditory tubes","On the posterior wall of the nasopharynx"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The lingual tonsils sit at the base, or root, of the tongue. Lingual means relating to the tongue, which makes the name a direct clue to the location. They are the lowest tonsils in the ring and guard swallowed material on its way into the pharynx.","notes":["The lateral walls of the oropharynx hold the palatine tonsils. Choose this for a question about the tonsils usually removed in a tonsillectomy.","Correct. The lingual tonsils are at the base of the tongue.","The openings of the auditory tubes hold the tubal tonsils. This would be the answer for a question about the tubal tonsils.","The posterior wall of the nasopharynx holds the pharyngeal tonsil (adenoid). Pick this for a question about the adenoid."],"lt":"t-lymph-malt-tonsils","lid":"c9","comp":107,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-organs"},{"id":"L01992","dok":1,"q":"How many pharyngeal tonsils (adenoids) are there?","a":"One.","options":["Three","Two","Four","One"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"There is a single pharyngeal tonsil, the adenoid, on the posterior wall of the nasopharynx in the midline. The palatine tonsils, by contrast, are paired, one in each lateral wall of the oropharynx. Remembering that the midline tonsil is single and the side tonsils are paired keeps the counts straight.","notes":["There are not three adenoids. This guess comes from mixing up the count of tonsil types (pharyngeal, palatine, and lingual) with the count of the pharyngeal tonsil itself.","Two is the number of palatine tonsils, one in each lateral wall of the oropharynx. Pick two for a question about how many palatine tonsils there are.","There are not four adenoids. Students who pick this are usually adding up paired tonsils from different groups rather than counting the single midline adenoid.","Correct. The pharyngeal tonsil is a single midline structure."],"lt":"t-lymph-malt-tonsils","lid":"c10","comp":107,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-organs"},{"id":"L01993","dok":1,"q":"How many palatine tonsils are there?","a":"Two.","options":["One","Four","Three","Two"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The palatine tonsils are paired, with one in each lateral wall of the oropharynx between the palatal arches. They are the largest tonsils and the ones you can see in a throat exam. The single tonsil is the pharyngeal tonsil (adenoid) in the midline of the nasopharynx.","notes":["One is the count for the pharyngeal tonsil, the single adenoid in the midline. Pick one for a question about how many adenoids there are.","There are not four palatine tonsils. Students pick this when they add two paired groups together, such as the palatine and tubal tonsils, instead of counting one pair.","There are not three palatine tonsils. This guess comes from confusing the count of tonsil types with the count of one type.","Correct. The palatine tonsils are a pair."],"lt":"t-lymph-malt-tonsils","lid":"c11","comp":107,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-organs"},{"id":"L01995","dok":2,"q":"Which tonsil lies on the posterior wall of the nasopharynx?","a":"The pharyngeal tonsil (adenoid).","options":["A tubal tonsil","The pharyngeal tonsil","A palatine tonsil","A lingual tonsil"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The pharyngeal tonsil, also called the adenoid, is a single mass of lymphatic tissue on the back wall of the nasopharynx. It sits in the path of air entering through the nose, so it guards against inhaled pathogens. Its name comes from the pharynx, the part of the throat it lines.","notes":["The tubal tonsils are in the nasopharynx too, but at the openings of the auditory tubes on the side walls rather than on the posterior wall. Pick them for a question about tonsils near the auditory tubes.","Correct. The pharyngeal tonsil sits on the posterior nasopharyngeal wall.","The palatine tonsils are in the lateral walls of the oropharynx, below the nasopharynx. Pick a palatine tonsil for a question about the tonsils visible when the mouth is open.","The lingual tonsils are at the base of the tongue. Choose them for a question about the lowest tonsils in the ring."],"lt":"t-lymph-malt-tonsils","lid":"c13","comp":107,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-organs"},{"id":"L01996","dok":2,"q":"Which tonsils sit in the lateral walls of the oropharynx, between the palatal arches?","a":"The palatine tonsils.","options":["The tubal tonsils","The pharyngeal tonsil","The palatine tonsils","The lingual tonsils"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The palatine tonsils are paired masses of lymphatic tissue in each lateral wall of the oropharynx, between the palatal arches. They are named for their position near the palate. Being the largest tonsils, they are the ones most often infected and removed.","notes":["The tubal tonsils are at the openings of the auditory tubes in the nasopharynx. They would be the answer for tonsils next to the tube leading to the middle ear.","The pharyngeal tonsil is on the posterior wall of the nasopharynx, above the soft palate. Choose it for a question about the adenoid.","Correct. The palatine tonsils sit in the lateral walls of the oropharynx.","The lingual tonsils are at the base of the tongue, not in the lateral walls of the oropharynx. Pick them for a question about lymphatic tissue on the root of the tongue."],"lt":"t-lymph-malt-tonsils","lid":"c14","comp":107,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-organs"},{"id":"L01997","dok":2,"q":"Which tonsils lie at the base of the tongue?","a":"The lingual tonsils.","options":["The pharyngeal tonsil","The tubal tonsils","The palatine tonsils","The lingual tonsils"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The lingual tonsils sit at the base of the tongue. Lingual means relating to the tongue, so the name gives away the location. They are the lowest part of the tonsil ring and guard food and drink as they are swallowed.","notes":["The pharyngeal tonsil is on the back wall of the nasopharynx, the highest point of the ring. Choose it for a question about the adenoid.","The tubal tonsils are at the openings of the auditory tubes in the nasopharynx. They would be the answer for tonsils near the auditory tube openings.","The palatine tonsils are in the lateral walls of the oropharynx, above and lateral to the base of the tongue. Pick them for a question about the tonsils removed in a tonsillectomy.","Correct. The lingual tonsils are at the base of the tongue."],"lt":"t-lymph-malt-tonsils","lid":"c15","comp":107,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-organs"},{"id":"L01998","dok":2,"q":"What is the main job of the ring of tonsils around the throat?","a":"To guard against inhaled and swallowed pathogens.","options":["To guard against inhaled and swallowed pathogens","To mature T cells for the whole body","To filter lymph returning from the head","To filter blood and remove old red cells"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The tonsils sit where the nasal and oral cavities open into the pharynx, the path taken by everything we breathe in or swallow. Their lymphatic tissue traps pathogens, partly in deep pits called crypts, so lymphocytes can recognize them and start an immune response. That makes the tonsils a secondary lymphatic tissue that guards an entry point.","notes":["Correct. The tonsils guard against pathogens that are inhaled or swallowed.","Maturing T cells is the job of the thymus, a primary lymphatic organ. This would be the answer for a question about the thymus.","Filtering lymph is the job of lymph nodes, such as the cervical nodes in the neck. Pick this for a question about what the cervical lymph nodes do.","Filtering blood and removing old red cells is the job of the spleen. Choose this for a question about the splenic red pulp."],"lt":"t-lymph-malt-tonsils","lid":"c16","comp":107,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-organs"},{"id":"L01999","dok":2,"q":"Which organ, attached to the cecum, has a wall packed with lymphatic follicles and counts as MALT?","a":"The appendix.","options":["The appendix","The sigmoid colon","The gallbladder","The pancreas"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The appendix is a narrow tube hanging off the cecum, the first part of the large intestine. Its wall is packed with lymphatic follicles, so it is counted as part of the MALT that guards the digestive tract. Along with the Peyer patches of the ileum, it monitors the contents of the gut.","notes":["Correct. The appendix is packed with lymphatic follicles and counts as MALT.","The sigmoid colon is the S-shaped last part of the colon before the rectum, far from the cecum. It would be the answer for the segment of large intestine just above the rectum.","The gallbladder stores and concentrates bile under the liver, and it is not known for lymphatic follicles. Pick it for a question about where bile is stored.","The pancreas is an accessory digestive gland that makes digestive enzymes and contains the pancreatic islets. Choose it for a question about the gland whose duct joins the bile duct."],"lt":"t-lymph-malt-tonsils","lid":"c17","comp":107,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-organs"},{"id":"L02000","dok":3,"q":"A child snores and breathes through the mouth because enlarged lymphatic tissue blocks the back of the nasal passage. Which tonsil is most likely enlarged?","a":"The pharyngeal tonsil (adenoid).","options":["The pharyngeal tonsil","A tubal tonsil","A palatine tonsil","A lingual tonsil"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The pharyngeal tonsil, or adenoid, sits on the posterior wall of the nasopharynx, right where air from the nose passes into the throat. When it swells, which is common in children, it blocks airflow through the nose and forces mouth breathing. The other tonsils sit lower or off to the side and do not block the nasal airway this way.","notes":["Correct. An enlarged adenoid blocks the back of the nasal passage.","The tubal tonsils sit at the openings of the auditory tubes, and swelling there tends to affect the ear tubes rather than block the nasal airway. They would be the answer for tonsils near the auditory tube openings.","The palatine tonsils are in the lateral walls of the oropharynx, so swelling there narrows the throat behind the mouth rather than the nasal passage. Pick a palatine tonsil for a sore throat with visible swollen masses.","The lingual tonsils are at the base of the tongue, well below the nasal passage. Choose them for a question about lymphatic tissue on the root of the tongue."],"lt":"t-lymph-malt-tonsils","lid":"c18","comp":107,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-organs"},{"id":"L02001","dok":3,"q":"A surgeon performs a tonsillectomy, removing the largest tonsils, the pair most often infected. Which tonsils are removed?","a":"The palatine tonsils.","options":["The tubal tonsils","The lingual tonsils","The palatine tonsils","The pharyngeal tonsil"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The palatine tonsils are the largest tonsils, paired in the lateral walls of the oropharynx. Because they are large and directly in the path of food and air, they are the ones most often infected, and they are what a tonsillectomy usually removes. Removing the pharyngeal tonsil is a separate procedure called an adenoidectomy.","notes":["The tubal tonsils are small paired tonsils at the auditory tube openings. They would be the answer for tonsils located in the side walls of the nasopharynx.","The lingual tonsils are at the base of the tongue and are not the usual target of a tonsillectomy. Pick them for a question about lymphatic tissue on the tongue's root.","Correct. A tonsillectomy usually removes the paired palatine tonsils.","The pharyngeal tonsil is single, not a pair, and its removal is called an adenoidectomy. Choose it for a question about an enlarged adenoid blocking the nose."],"lt":"t-lymph-malt-tonsils","lid":"c19","comp":107,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-organs"},{"id":"L02002","dok":3,"q":"Lymphatic tissue scattered in the lining of the intestine guards against swallowed pathogens. What is this tissue?","a":"MALT.","options":["The deep cortex of a lymph node","The thymic cortex","Splenic white pulp","MALT"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Lymphatic tissue in the mucous membrane of the intestine is MALT, mucosa-associated lymphatic tissue. It sits in the lamina propria just beneath the epithelium, where it meets microbes that arrive with food. Larger collections, such as the Peyer patches of the ileum and the appendix, are part of the same system.","notes":["The deep cortex is the T cell zone inside an encapsulated lymph node, not the gut lining. It would be the answer for where T cells concentrate in a node.","The thymic cortex is inside the thymus in the mediastinum, where immature T cells develop. Pick it for a question about where pre-T cells multiply.","Splenic white pulp is lymphatic tissue inside the spleen, around the central arteries. Choose it for a question about immune responses to blood-borne pathogens.","Correct. Lymphatic tissue in the intestinal lining is MALT."],"lt":"t-lymph-malt-tonsils","lid":"c20","comp":107,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-organs"},{"id":"L02003","dok":2,"q":"Which of these is NOT lined by MALT?","a":"The inside of the blood vessels.","options":["The urinary tract","The inside of the blood vessels","The respiratory tract","The gastrointestinal tract"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"MALT lines the mucous membranes of tracts that open to the outside of the body: the digestive, respiratory, urinary, and reproductive tracts. Blood vessels are a closed system lined by endothelium, a simple squamous epithelium, not a mucous membrane. Since pathogens do not enter the body through the inside of a blood vessel, there is no MALT there.","notes":["The urinary tract is lined by a mucous membrane with MALT. It would be a correct choice for a question asking which tracts MALT does guard.","Correct. Blood vessels are lined by endothelium, not a mucous membrane, so they have no MALT.","The respiratory tract is lined by a mucous membrane with MALT, guarding against inhaled pathogens. Choose it for a question about lymphatic tissue that meets inhaled microbes.","The gastrointestinal tract has abundant MALT, including Peyer patches and the appendix. Pick it if the question asked where Peyer patches are found."],"lt":"t-lymph-malt-tonsils","lid":"c21","comp":107,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-organs"},{"id":"L02005","dok":2,"q":"The tonsil ring guards which two openings into the pharynx?","a":"Where the nasal cavity opens into the nasopharynx and the oral cavity opens into the oropharynx.","options":["The pharynx into the esophagus and the esophagus into the stomach","The nasal cavity into the nasopharynx and the mouth into the oropharynx","The larynx into the trachea and the trachea into the bronchi","The stomach into the duodenum and the ileum into the cecum"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Air enters the pharynx from the nasal cavity at the nasopharynx, and food and air enter from the mouth at the oropharynx. The tonsils are placed at these two entrances: the pharyngeal tonsil at the nasopharynx, and the palatine and lingual tonsils around the oropharynx. Everything inhaled or swallowed passes them first.","notes":["These junctions are guarded by the upper and lower esophageal sphincters, not by tonsils. Choose this pair for a question about the sphincters of the esophagus.","Correct. The tonsils guard where the nasal and oral cavities open into the pharynx.","These junctions lie below the pharynx in the lower respiratory tract, where there is no ring of tonsils. Pick this pair for a question about the path of air below the larynx.","These are junctions inside the digestive tract guarded by the pyloric sphincter and the ileocecal valve. Lymphatic tissue near the ileocecal junction is Peyer patches and the appendix, so pick this for a question about gut MALT."],"lt":"t-lymph-malt-tonsils","lid":"c23","comp":107,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-organs"},{"id":"L02006","dok":2,"q":"How does the pharyngeal tonsil differ from the palatine tonsils?","a":"The pharyngeal tonsil is single, while the palatine tonsils are paired.","options":["It is paired, while the palatine tonsils are single","It is the largest tonsil and the one usually removed","It sits at the base of the tongue, below them","It is single, while the palatine tonsils are paired"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The pharyngeal tonsil, or adenoid, is one mass on the posterior wall of the nasopharynx, right in the midline. The palatine tonsils come as a pair, one in each lateral wall of the oropharynx. A structure sitting in the midline is often single, while structures on the side walls usually come in pairs.","notes":["This reverses the counts. The midline adenoid is single, and the palatine tonsils sit one on each side, so they are paired.","The palatine tonsils, not the pharyngeal tonsil, are the largest and the ones usually removed in a tonsillectomy. This choice would describe the palatine tonsils.","The base of the tongue is the location of the lingual tonsils, and the pharyngeal tonsil is actually the highest tonsil, up in the nasopharynx. Pick the base of the tongue for a question about the lingual tonsils.","Correct. The pharyngeal tonsil is single and the palatine tonsils are a pair."],"lt":"t-lymph-malt-tonsils","lid":"c24","comp":107,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-organs"},{"id":"L02007","dok":3,"q":"A slide of the ileum wall shows large clusters of lymphatic follicles in the mucosa, with no capsule around them. What are these clusters?","a":"Peyer patches.","options":["Thymic lobules","Splenic white pulp","Lymph nodes","Peyer patches"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Peyer patches are clusters of lymphatic follicles in the wall of the ileum, the last part of the small intestine. They are a type of MALT, so they have no capsule, and they monitor the intestinal contents for pathogens. Their location in the ileum and their lack of a capsule separate them from encapsulated organs like lymph nodes.","notes":["Thymic lobules are subdivisions of the thymus in the mediastinum, each with a cortex and medulla. They would be the answer for the units of the thymus.","White pulp is inside the spleen, around the central arteries. Choose it for a question about the spleen's immune tissue.","Lymph nodes are encapsulated organs along lymphatic collecting vessels, not clusters inside the intestinal mucosa. Pick them for a question about encapsulated filters of lymph.","Correct. Clusters of follicles in the ileum wall are Peyer patches."],"lt":"t-lymph-malt-tonsils","lid":"c25","comp":107,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-organs"},{"id":"L02008","dok":1,"q":"Peyer patches are found in the wall of which part of the digestive tract?","a":"The ileum.","options":["The esophagus","The ileum","The rectum","The stomach"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Peyer patches are clusters of lymphatic follicles in the wall of the ileum, the final segment of the small intestine. They are part of MALT and watch the intestinal contents for pathogens before material passes into the large intestine. The appendix, attached to the nearby cecum, is another gut structure packed with follicles.","notes":["The esophagus carries food from the pharynx to the stomach and is not the site of Peyer patches. Pick the esophagus for a question about the tube that passes through the diaphragm.","Correct. Peyer patches are in the ileum.","The rectum is the last part of the large intestine before the anal canal. It would be the answer for where feces are stored before defecation.","The stomach churns food and has rugae and gastric pits, but Peyer patches are not a feature of it. Choose the stomach for a question about rugae."],"lt":"t-lymph-malt-tonsils","lid":"c26","comp":107,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-organs"},{"id":"L02010","dok":3,"q":"During a throat exam, a clinician sees a mass of lymphatic tissue on each side at the back of the mouth, between the palatal arches. Which tonsils are these?","a":"The palatine tonsils.","options":["The lingual tonsils","The pharyngeal tonsil","The palatine tonsils","The tubal tonsils"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The paired masses between the palatal arches are the palatine tonsils. They sit in the lateral walls of the oropharynx and are the tonsils you can see when a patient opens wide. The pharyngeal and tubal tonsils are hidden up in the nasopharynx, and the lingual tonsils are lower, on the base of the tongue.","notes":["The lingual tonsils are on the base of the tongue, lower than the palatal arches and harder to see without a mirror. Pick them for a question about the root of the tongue.","The pharyngeal tonsil is single and sits up in the nasopharynx, out of view in a normal throat exam. Choose it for a question about the adenoid.","Correct. The visible paired masses between the palatal arches are the palatine tonsils.","The tubal tonsils are in the nasopharynx at the auditory tube openings, also out of view. They would be the answer for the tonsils near the auditory tubes."],"lt":"t-lymph-malt-tonsils","lid":"c28","comp":107,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-organs"},{"id":"L02012","dok":3,"q":"MALT and the tonsils are both secondary lymphatic tissue. What do they have in common?","a":"Both are lymphatic tissue placed in mucosal linings to guard entry points into the body.","options":["Both guard mucosal entry points into the body","Both filter blood like the spleen","Both are primary organs where lymphocytes mature","Both are encapsulated filters along lymph vessels"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The tonsils and MALT are both lymphatic tissue embedded in mucous membranes. They sit where pathogens are most likely to enter: the tonsils at the entrance to the throat, and MALT along the linings of the digestive, respiratory, urinary, and reproductive tracts. Mature lymphocytes there meet invaders at the point of entry.","notes":["Correct. Both guard mucosal surfaces where pathogens enter.","Filtering blood is the job of the spleen, which receives the splenic artery. Choose this for a question about the spleen.","The primary organs are red bone marrow and the thymus, where lymphocytes form and mature. Pick this for a question about the thymus or red marrow.","Encapsulated filters along lymphatic vessels are lymph nodes. MALT has no capsule, so this would be the answer for a question describing lymph nodes."],"lt":"t-lymph-malt-tonsils","lid":"c30","comp":107,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-organs"},{"id":"L02013","dok":1,"q":"Which structure seen at the back of the mouth is NOT a tonsil?","a":"The uvula.","options":["The pharyngeal tonsil","The lingual tonsil","The palatine tonsil","The uvula"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The three tonsils on the lab list are the pharyngeal tonsil (adenoid) in the nasopharynx, the palatine tonsils in the lateral walls of the oropharynx, and the lingual tonsils at the base of the tongue. The uvula is the small fleshy projection hanging from the back of the soft palate. It is muscle and connective tissue covered by mucosa, not a mass of lymphatic tissue.","notes":["The pharyngeal tonsil, or adenoid, is a tonsil on the posterior wall of the nasopharynx. Pick it for a question about the tonsil that blocks nasal breathing when enlarged.","The lingual tonsils are tonsils at the base of the tongue. They would be the answer for the lowest tonsils in the ring.","The palatine tonsils are tonsils in the lateral walls of the oropharynx. Choose them for a question about the tonsils usually removed in a tonsillectomy.","Correct. The uvula hangs from the soft palate and is not a tonsil."],"lt":"t-lymph-malt-tonsils","lid":"c31","comp":107,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-organs"},{"id":"L02014","dok":2,"q":"Which choice correctly pairs a tonsil with its location?","a":"Lingual tonsil: base of the tongue.","options":["Lingual tonsil: openings of the auditory tubes","Lingual tonsil: base of the tongue","Pharyngeal tonsil: base of the tongue","Palatine tonsil: posterior wall of the nasopharynx"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The lingual tonsils are at the base of the tongue, which matches their name, since lingual means of the tongue. The pharyngeal tonsil is on the posterior wall of the nasopharynx, the palatine tonsils are in the lateral walls of the oropharynx, and the tubal tonsils are at the auditory tube openings. Matching each name to its location is the core of the tonsil identification task in lab.","notes":["The openings of the auditory tubes are the site of the tubal tonsils. This location would be right for a question about the tubal tonsils.","Correct. The lingual tonsils sit at the base of the tongue.","The pharyngeal tonsil is up in the nasopharynx, not on the tongue. The base of the tongue would be right for the lingual tonsils.","The posterior wall of the nasopharynx is the location of the pharyngeal tonsil, not the palatine tonsil. Pick this location for a question about the adenoid."],"lt":"t-lymph-malt-tonsils","lid":"c32","comp":107,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-organs"},{"id":"L02015","dok":2,"q":"Tonsils have deep pits in their surface that trap bacteria so lymphocytes can learn to recognize them. What are these pits called?","a":"Crypts.","options":["Crypts","Sinuses","Alveoli","Rugae"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Tonsillar crypts are deep pits that dip inward from the surface of a tonsil. Bacteria and debris get trapped in them, which brings the microbes into close contact with the lymphocytes inside the tonsil. This lets the immune system learn to recognize pathogens entering through the mouth and nose.","notes":["Correct. The pits in the tonsils are crypts.","Sinuses are open spaces or channels, such as the sinuses of a lymph node that lymph flows through, or the paranasal sinuses in the skull. Pick sinuses for a question about the path of lymph through a node.","Alveoli are the tiny air sacs of the lungs where gas exchange happens. They would be the answer for the site of the respiratory membrane.","Rugae are folds in the lining of an organ that let it stretch, such as the folds of the empty stomach. Choose rugae for a question about the stomach lining."],"lt":"t-lymph-malt-tonsils","lid":"c33","comp":107,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-organs"},{"id":"L02017","dok":1,"q":"Infected, inflamed tonsils, common in childhood, are called what?","a":"Tonsillitis.","options":["Lymphadenitis","Tonsillitis","Lymphedema","Splenomegaly"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Tonsillitis is infection and inflammation of the tonsils, most often the palatine tonsils. The suffix -itis means inflammation, so the name says exactly what is happening. Repeated bouts of tonsillitis are a common reason for a tonsillectomy.","notes":["Lymphadenitis is inflammation of lymph nodes, which become swollen and tender while fighting an infection. Pick it for a question about tender nodes in the neck.","Correct. Inflamed, infected tonsils are tonsillitis.","Lymphedema is long-term swelling caused by blocked or missing lymphatic vessels, sometimes after nodes are removed. It would be the answer for a swollen arm after breast cancer surgery.","Splenomegaly is an enlarged spleen, seen in mononucleosis and leukemia. Choose it for a question about why patients with mono avoid contact sports."],"lt":"t-lymph-malt-tonsils","lid":"c35","comp":108,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-disorders"},{"id":"L02019","dok":2,"q":"Which lymphatic tissue lines the mucous membranes of the reproductive tract?","a":"MALT.","options":["Red bone marrow","The thymic medulla","MALT","White pulp"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The reproductive tract opens to the outside of the body, so its mucous membrane is guarded by MALT, mucosa-associated lymphatic tissue. The same kind of tissue lines the digestive, respiratory, and urinary tracts. In each of these tracts, MALT lets lymphocytes meet pathogens at the point of entry.","notes":["Red bone marrow is a primary lymphatic organ inside spongy bone. It would be the answer for where all lymphocytes form.","The thymic medulla is the inner region of the thymus in the mediastinum. Choose it for a question about where Hassall's corpuscles are found.","Correct. MALT lines the reproductive tract mucosa.","White pulp is lymphatic tissue inside the spleen, around the central arteries. Pick it for a question about the spleen's immune tissue."],"lt":"t-lymph-malt-tonsils","lid":"c37","comp":107,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-organs"},{"id":"L02021","dok":3,"q":"Starting in the nasopharynx and moving down toward the base of the tongue, in which order do you meet the pharyngeal, palatine, and lingual tonsils?","a":"Pharyngeal, then palatine, then lingual.","options":["Palatine, then pharyngeal, then lingual","Lingual, then palatine, then pharyngeal","Pharyngeal, then palatine, then lingual","Pharyngeal, then lingual, then palatine"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The pharyngeal tonsil is highest, on the back wall of the nasopharynx behind the nasal cavity. Below it, the palatine tonsils sit in the lateral walls of the oropharynx between the palatal arches. The lingual tonsils are lowest, on the base of the tongue where food enters the oropharynx.","notes":["The palatine tonsils are in the oropharynx, below the nasopharynx, so they cannot come before the pharyngeal tonsil. This mistake comes from forgetting that the adenoid sits above the soft palate.","This is the correct order read backward, from the tongue up to the nasopharynx. Pick it only if the question asked you to start at the base of the tongue and move up.","Correct. The order going down is pharyngeal, palatine, and then lingual.","This swaps the palatine and lingual tonsils. The lingual tonsils are on the base of the tongue, which is the lowest point of the ring."],"lt":"t-lymph-malt-tonsils","lid":"c39","comp":107,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-organs"},{"id":"L02023","dok":1,"q":"Which tonsils are paired and sit at the openings of the auditory tubes?","a":"The tubal tonsils.","options":["The pharyngeal tonsil","The palatine tonsils","The lingual tonsils","The tubal tonsils"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The tubal tonsils are small paired masses of lymphatic tissue at the openings of the auditory tubes in the side walls of the nasopharynx. The auditory tubes connect the middle ear to the throat, so these tonsils guard that passage. They sit close to the pharyngeal tonsil, which is on the posterior wall of the nasopharynx.","notes":["The pharyngeal tonsil is in the nasopharynx near the auditory tubes, but it is single and sits on the posterior wall. It would be the answer for a question about the adenoid.","The palatine tonsils are paired too, but they sit in the lateral walls of the oropharynx. Pick them for a question about the tonsils removed in a tonsillectomy.","The lingual tonsils are on the base of the tongue, far below the auditory tubes. Choose them for a question about the lowest tonsils in the ring.","Correct. The tubal tonsils are paired and sit at the auditory tube openings."],"lt":"t-lymph-malt-tonsils","lid":"c41","comp":107,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-organs"},{"id":"L02065","dok":1,"q":"What is the general shape of a lymph node?","a":"Small and bean-shaped.","options":["Flat and disc-shaped","Small and bean-shaped","Long and tubular","Branching and tree-like"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"A lymph node is a small, bean-shaped organ wrapped in a connective tissue capsule. The indented side of the bean is the hilum, where blood vessels enter and the efferent lymphatic vessels leave. Afferent vessels enter around the rounded outer surface, so lymph passes all the way through the node before leaving.","notes":["A flat disc shape describes an erythrocyte, the biconcave red blood cell. It would fit a question about red cell shape, not a lymphatic organ.","Correct. Lymph nodes are small bean-shaped organs with a hilum on the indented side.","A long, tubular shape describes a lymphatic vessel such as a collecting vessel or the thoracic duct. Pick it if the question asked about the vessels that carry lymph between nodes.","A branching, tree-like pattern describes the network of lymphatic capillaries and collecting vessels, or the bronchial tree of the lungs. A single node is a compact organ, not a branching system."],"lt":"t-lymphatic-system","lid":"c7","comp":106,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-vessels-node"},{"id":"L02066","dok":1,"q":"Which region of a lymph node lies just inside the capsule and is packed with lymphocytes in rounded clusters called follicles?","a":"The cortex.","options":["The cortex","The medulla","The capsule","The hilum"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The cortex is the outer region of a lymph node, just beneath the capsule. Its outer part holds follicles, rounded clusters of lymphocytes with pale germinal centers where B cells multiply, and its deeper part holds mostly T cells. Lymph filters through the cortex before moving into the medulla.","notes":["Correct. The cortex is the outer region of the node, filled with lymphocyte follicles.","The medulla is the inner region of the node, made of medullary cords and sinuses lined with macrophages. Pick it if the question asked about the deep region where lymph collects before leaving the node.","The capsule is the dense connective tissue covering around the node, with trabeculae extending inward. It wraps the node but holds no follicles of lymphocytes.","The hilum is the indented side of the node where blood vessels enter and efferent vessels leave. Pick it if the question asked where filtered lymph exits the node."],"lt":"t-lymphatic-system","lid":"c8","comp":106,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-vessels-node"},{"id":"L02067","dok":1,"q":"Where do lymph nodes cluster most heavily in the body?","a":"In the cervical, axillary, and inguinal regions.","options":["Inside the vertebral canal around the spinal cord","In the palms and the soles of the feet","In the cervical, axillary, and inguinal regions","Within the wall of the heart"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Lymph nodes gather in large groups in the neck (cervical), the armpit (axillary), and the groin (inguinal), with more deep groups in the thorax and abdomen. These regions sit where lymph from the head, the limbs, and the trunk collects on its way back to the blood. This is why a clinician feels the neck, armpits, and groin when checking for swollen nodes.","notes":["The central nervous system does not have lymphatic capillaries, so nodes do not cluster around the spinal cord. Pick the vertebral canal for a question about where the spinal cord and its meninges sit.","The palms and soles have lymphatic capillaries draining them, but the nodes that filter that lymph sit farther up the limbs, mainly in the axillary and inguinal regions.","Correct. The cervical, axillary, and inguinal regions hold the largest superficial clusters of lymph nodes.","The heart wall is cardiac muscle with coronary vessels. Lymph from the heart drains to nodes in the mediastinum, not to clusters inside the heart wall."],"lt":"t-lymphatic-system","lid":"c9","comp":107,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-organs"},{"id":"L02068","dok":1,"q":"Which two organs are the primary lymphatic organs?","a":"Red bone marrow and the thymus.","options":["The spleen and the tonsils","The spleen and the lymph nodes","The lymph nodes and MALT","Red bone marrow and the thymus"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Primary lymphatic organs are where lymphocytes form and mature. All lymphocytes are made in red bone marrow, and B cells finish maturing there, while T cell precursors travel to the thymus to mature. The remaining lymphatic organs are secondary, meaning they are the places where mature lymphocytes meet invaders.","notes":["The spleen and tonsils are secondary organs. The spleen filters blood and the tonsils guard the entrance to the throat, but neither is where lymphocytes mature.","The spleen and lymph nodes are both secondary organs. This pair would be right if the question asked which organs filter blood and lymph, respectively.","Lymph nodes and MALT are secondary lymphatic tissue where mature lymphocytes meet pathogens. Pick this pair for a question about where lymph is filtered and mucous membranes are guarded.","Correct. Red bone marrow and the thymus are where lymphocytes form and mature."],"lt":"t-lymphatic-system","lid":"c10","comp":107,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-organs"},{"id":"L02069","dok":1,"q":"Where do T cells mature?","a":"In the thymus.","options":["In the tonsils","In the spleen","In the thymus","In the lymph nodes"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"T cells start as stem cells in red bone marrow, but their precursors travel to the thymus to finish maturing. The thymus sits in the mediastinum, above the heart and behind the sternum. The T in T cell stands for thymus.","notes":["The tonsils are secondary lymphatic tissue around the entrance to the throat. They hold mature lymphocytes that meet inhaled and swallowed pathogens, so pick them for that guarding role.","The spleen is a secondary organ in the upper left abdomen that filters blood. Mature T cells gather in its white pulp, but they did not mature there.","Correct. T cells mature in the thymus, which gives them their name.","Lymph nodes are secondary organs along the collecting vessels. Mature T cells settle in the deep cortex of a node, but they finished maturing in the thymus first."],"lt":"t-lymphatic-system","lid":"c11","comp":107,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-organs"},{"id":"L02070","dok":1,"q":"Where is the spleen located?","a":"In the upper left abdomen, behind the stomach.","options":["In the upper left abdomen, behind the stomach","In a ring around the entrance to the throat","Behind the sternum in the mediastinum","Along the collecting vessels of the groin"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The spleen is the largest lymphatic organ and sits in the upper left abdomen against the diaphragm, behind the stomach. The lower left ribs protect it, which is why a hard blow to that area can rupture it. The splenic artery and vein pass through its hilum.","notes":["Correct. The spleen lies in the upper left abdomen, behind the stomach and under the lower left ribs.","A ring around the entrance to the throat describes the tonsils. Pick it for a question about the lymphatic tissue that guards inhaled and swallowed material.","Behind the sternum in the mediastinum is the location of the thymus. Pick this location if the question asked where T cells mature.","Lymph nodes cluster along collecting vessels in the groin as the inguinal nodes. Pick this location for a question about the nodes that filter lymph from the lower limb."],"lt":"t-lymphatic-system","lid":"c12","comp":107,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-organs"},{"id":"L02071","dok":1,"q":"Which tissue of the spleen filters the blood and removes worn-out red blood cells?","a":"Red pulp.","options":["The capsule","The hilum","Red pulp","White pulp"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Red pulp makes up most of the spleen and is built of venous sinuses and splenic cords full of red cells and macrophages. As blood squeezes through it, macrophages remove old and damaged red cells and platelets and save their iron for reuse. That is where the red pulp gets its color and its filtering job.","notes":["The capsule is the connective tissue covering around the spleen. It shapes and protects the organ but does not filter blood.","The hilum is where the splenic artery and vein enter and leave the spleen. Pick it for a question about where vessels pass into the organ.","Correct. Red pulp is the spleen's filtering tissue, where macrophages remove worn-out red cells.","White pulp is made of lymphocytes clustered around central arteries and handles the spleen's immune function. Pick white pulp if the question asked which part of the spleen mounts immune responses."],"lt":"t-lymphatic-system","lid":"c13","comp":107,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-organs"},{"id":"L02072","dok":1,"q":"What is another name for the pharyngeal tonsil?","a":"The adenoid","options":["The adenoid","The lingual tonsil","The palatine tonsil","The tubal tonsil"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The pharyngeal tonsil is a single tonsil on the posterior wall of the nasopharynx, and it is also called the adenoid. When it swells, especially in children, it can block airflow through the nose. It is one of the tonsils that form a protective ring around the entrance to the throat.","notes":["Correct. The pharyngeal tonsil is also called the adenoid.","The lingual tonsils sit at the base of the tongue and guard swallowed material. Pick them if the question asked which tonsils lie on the back of the tongue.","The palatine tonsils are the paired tonsils in the lateral walls of the oropharynx, the ones usually infected and removed. Pick them for a question about tonsillitis or a tonsillectomy.","The tubal tonsils are paired patches at the openings of the auditory tubes in the nasopharynx. They are neighbors of the pharyngeal tonsil but are not called the adenoid."],"lt":"t-lymphatic-system","lid":"c14","comp":107,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-organs"},{"id":"L02076","dok":2,"q":"Which vessels carry lymph into a lymph node?","a":"The afferent lymphatic vessels.","options":["The afferent lymphatic vessels","The efferent lymphatic vessels","The lymphatic trunks","The renal collecting ducts"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Afferent vessels carry lymph into a node through several points on its capsule, and afferent means carrying toward. Lymph then passes through the subcapsular, cortical, and medullary sinuses before leaving through fewer efferent vessels at the hilum. Because more vessels enter than leave, lymph slows down inside the node, giving macrophages time to clean it.","notes":["Correct. Afferent vessels bring lymph into the node through the capsule.","Efferent vessels carry filtered lymph out of the node at the hilum. Pick them if the question asked which vessels leave the node.","Lymphatic trunks are large vessels formed farther along the pathway, after lymph has passed through the nodes. They drain whole body regions into the ducts.","Collecting ducts belong to the kidney, where they carry urine toward the renal papilla. The name looks like lymphatic collecting vessels, but it is part of the urinary system."],"lt":"t-lymphatic-system","lid":"c18","comp":106,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-vessels-node"},{"id":"L02078","dok":2,"q":"Which lymphatic organ filters the blood rather than the lymph?","a":"The spleen.","options":["The thymus","A palatine tonsil","A lymph node","The spleen"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The spleen is the only lymphatic organ that filters blood. Blood enters through the splenic artery, and in the red pulp macrophages remove worn-out red cells while lymphocytes in the white pulp watch for blood-borne pathogens. Lymph nodes do the matching job for lymph.","notes":["The thymus is a primary organ where T cells mature, and it does not filter either blood or lymph. Pick it for a question about T cell maturation.","The palatine tonsils sit in the lateral walls of the oropharynx and guard against swallowed and inhaled pathogens. They trap bacteria in their crypts but do not filter the blood.","Lymph nodes filter lymph, not blood. Pick a lymph node if the question asked which organ cleans lymph before it returns to the bloodstream.","Correct. The spleen is the lymphatic organ that filters blood."],"lt":"t-lymphatic-system","lid":"c20","comp":107,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-organs"},{"id":"L02079","dok":2,"q":"Which secondary lymphatic tissue guards the mucous membranes of the digestive and respiratory tracts?","a":"MALT.","options":["Red bone marrow","The spleen","MALT","The thymus"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"MALT stands for mucosa-associated lymphatic tissue. It is lymphatic tissue placed in the linings of the digestive, respiratory, urinary, and reproductive tracts, where pathogens are most likely to enter. Examples include the tonsils, the Peyer patches of the ileum, and the follicles in the wall of the appendix.","notes":["Red bone marrow is a primary organ where all lymphocytes form and B cells mature. Pick it for a question about where lymphocytes are made.","The spleen is a secondary organ, but it filters blood in the upper left abdomen instead of guarding mucosal linings. Pick it for a question about blood filtering.","Correct. MALT guards the mucous membranes of the body's tracts.","The thymus is a primary organ in the mediastinum where T cells mature. It sits behind the sternum, not in a mucous membrane."],"lt":"t-lymphatic-system","lid":"c21","comp":107,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-organs"},{"id":"L02081","dok":2,"q":"Where do B cells mature?","a":"In red bone marrow.","options":["In the thymus","In the spleen","In red bone marrow","In the tonsils"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"All lymphocytes form from stem cells in red bone marrow. B cells stay in the marrow to finish maturing, while T cell precursors leave for the thymus. Once mature, B cells move to secondary organs, and when they meet an invader some become plasma cells that make antibodies.","notes":["The thymus is where T cells mature, not B cells. Pick the thymus if the question asked about T cell maturation.","The spleen is a secondary organ where mature B cells gather in the white pulp. They arrive there already mature from the marrow.","Correct. B cells form and mature in red bone marrow.","The tonsils hold follicles where mature B cells multiply when they meet a pathogen. They are secondary tissue, not a place of maturation."],"lt":"t-lymphatic-system","lid":"c23","comp":107,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-organs"},{"id":"L02083","dok":3,"q":"During a physical exam, a clinician feels tender, enlarged lumps in a patient's neck and armpit. Which structures are enlarged?","a":"The lymph nodes.","options":["The lymph nodes","The lymphatic capillaries","The thoracic duct","The lacteals"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Swollen glands are enlarged lymph nodes. The cervical and axillary regions hold large clusters of nodes, and when the lymphocytes inside meet an infection they multiply, making the nodes swell and often become tender. That is why clinicians feel the neck, armpits, and groin during an exam.","notes":["Correct. Tender, enlarged lumps in the neck and armpit are swollen lymph nodes.","Lymphatic capillaries are microscopic dead-end tubes in the tissues. They are far too small to feel, and they do not swell into lumps during an infection.","The thoracic duct runs deep in the thorax and abdomen and cannot be felt in the neck and armpit. Pick it for a question about the vessel that drains most of the body.","Lacteals are lymphatic capillaries inside the villi of the small intestine. Pick them for a question about fat absorption, not swelling in the neck or armpit."],"lt":"t-lymphatic-system","lid":"c25","comp":108,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-disorders"},{"id":"L02085","dok":3,"q":"A hard blow to the lower left ribs tears a soft lymphatic organ that holds a large volume of blood, causing heavy internal bleeding. Which organ is most at risk?","a":"The spleen.","options":["The thymus","A palatine tonsil","An inguinal lymph node","The spleen"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The spleen sits in the upper left abdomen under the lower left ribs, and it is the lymphatic organ that filters blood. Because it is soft and holds a large volume of blood, a tear causes heavy internal bleeding. Its location explains why rib injuries on the left side raise concern for a ruptured spleen.","notes":["The thymus lies in the mediastinum behind the sternum, not under the lower left ribs. It also does not filter blood.","The palatine tonsils sit in the lateral walls of the oropharynx, far from the ribs. Pick them for a question about tonsillitis.","Inguinal lymph nodes lie in the groin and filter lymph, not blood. Pick them if the question asked which nodes drain the lower limb.","Correct. The spleen lies under the lower left ribs and holds a large volume of blood, so a tear bleeds heavily."],"lt":"t-lymphatic-system","lid":"c27","comp":108,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-disorders"},{"id":"L02086","dok":3,"q":"A patient is diagnosed with a cancer of the lymphocytes in lymphatic tissue, classified as Hodgkin or non-Hodgkin type. Which disorder is this?","a":"Lymphoma.","options":["Lymphoma","Lymphedema","Splenomegaly","Edema"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Lymphoma is a cancer of the lymphocytes that arises in lymphatic tissue such as the lymph nodes. It is divided into Hodgkin and non-Hodgkin types. The suffix -oma means tumor, which separates it from the swelling disorders with similar names.","notes":["Correct. Lymphoma is cancer of lymphocytes in lymphatic tissue.","Lymphedema is long-term swelling from blocked or missing lymphatic vessels. Its name shares the lymph- root, but it is a drainage problem, not a cancer.","Splenomegaly is an enlarged spleen. It can happen in some blood cancers such as leukemia, but the term names the enlargement, not a cancer of lymphatic tissue.","Edema is swelling in the interstitial spaces when fluid leaks out faster than the lymphatics can drain it. It is fluid buildup, not cancer."],"lt":"t-lymphatic-system","lid":"c28","comp":108,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-disorders"},{"id":"L02087","dok":3,"q":"To learn whether a cancer has begun to spread, a surgeon checks the first lymph node that a region drains into. What is this node called?","a":"The sentinel node.","options":["The sentinel node","The afferent node","The efferent node","The germinal node"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Lymph passes through nodes in a set order, so cancer cells traveling in lymph reach the first node along the route before any others. That first node is called the sentinel node, meaning the guard. If it is free of cancer, the surgeon has good evidence the cancer has not spread through the lymph.","notes":["Correct. The sentinel node is the first node a region drains into.","Afferent is the name of the vessels that carry lymph into a node, not a type of node. Students choose this because afferent means toward, but no node is called the afferent node.","Efferent is the name of the vessels that carry lymph out of a node at the hilum. Pick efferent vessels for a question about where filtered lymph leaves a node.","Germinal centers are the pale centers of lymphatic follicles where B cells multiply. The term describes a region inside a node, not the first node in a drainage route."],"lt":"t-lymphatic-system","lid":"c29","comp":108,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-disorders"},{"id":"L02090","dok":3,"q":"Lymph nodes sit along the collecting vessels and are packed with immune cells. Why is this placement effective for fighting infection?","a":"Lymph from the tissues must pass through the nodes, so pathogens carried in it are caught and attacked before the lymph reaches the blood.","options":["The nodes store extra lymph and release it when blood volume drops","The nodes send cleaned lymph back out into the tissue spaces to reuse it","The nodes add fresh fluid to the lymph so it flows faster toward the heart","Lymph must pass through them, so pathogens are caught before reaching the blood"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Every collecting vessel passes through one or more nodes on its way to the trunks, so all lymph is screened before it rejoins the blood. Inside a node, lymph slows down because more afferent vessels enter than efferent vessels leave. Macrophages engulf bacteria and debris, and lymphocytes meet invaders and start an immune response.","notes":["Nodes filter lymph rather than storing it. The spleen stores platelets and monocytes, which may be the source of this mix-up.","Lymph never returns to the tissue spaces. After filtering, it leaves the node through efferent vessels and continues toward the trunks and ducts.","Nodes do not add fluid. Lymph actually slows down inside a node so the immune cells have time to clean it.","Correct. Placing nodes along the route means pathogens in lymph are filtered out before reaching the blood."],"lt":"t-lymphatic-system","lid":"d3-t-lymphatic-system-2","comp":107,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-organs"},{"id":"L02092","dok":3,"q":"A bacterial infection is spreading in the bloodstream. Why is the spleen, rather than the lymph nodes, positioned to catch these pathogens directly?","a":"Blood flows through the spleen, while lymph nodes filter only lymph.","options":["The spleen sits along the thoracic duct and filters its contents","Lymph nodes contain no lymphocytes to fight infection","Blood flows through the spleen, while lymph nodes filter only lymph","The spleen receives afferent lymphatic vessels that carry blood into it"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The splenic artery brings blood into the spleen, where it passes the lymphocytes of the white pulp and the macrophages of the red pulp before leaving by the splenic vein. That lets the spleen screen blood for pathogens while it also removes worn-out red cells. Lymph nodes sit on lymphatic vessels and only see lymph, so they catch pathogens that enter from the tissues.","notes":["The thoracic duct runs through the thorax and empties into the veins at the base of the neck. The spleen lies in the upper left abdomen and is not on the duct's path.","Lymph nodes are packed with lymphocytes in their cortex. They fight infection well, but only in lymph, not in the bloodstream.","Correct. The spleen filters blood, so it can catch pathogens circulating in the bloodstream.","Afferent lymphatic vessels belong to lymph nodes and carry lymph, not blood. The spleen receives blood through the splenic artery at its hilum."],"lt":"t-lymphatic-system","lid":"d3-t-lymphatic-system-4","comp":107,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-organs"},{"id":"L02093","dok":3,"q":"The thymus is large in children but shrinks after puberty and is mostly fat in older adults. Which explanation fits its job?","a":"It is where T cells mature, and most of that maturing happens while the immune system is being established early in life.","options":["It is where B cells mature, and that happens mostly in childhood","It stores fat for growth, which is used up after puberty","It is where T cells mature, and most of that maturing happens early in life","It filters lymph, and children produce more lymph than adults"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The thymus is a primary lymphatic organ in the mediastinum where T cell precursors from the marrow mature. A large supply of mature T cells is built up in infancy and childhood, so the gland is biggest and busiest then. After puberty the thymus shrinks and is gradually replaced by fat, while the mature T cells it produced live on in the secondary organs.","notes":["B cells mature in red bone marrow, not the thymus. Pick red bone marrow if the question asked where B cells finish maturing.","This reverses the story. The thymus becomes fatty as it shrinks in adults, but fat storage was never its job.","Correct. The thymus matures T cells, and most of that work happens early in life.","The thymus does not filter lymph. It has no afferent vessels and no follicles, which separates it from a lymph node."],"lt":"t-lymphatic-system","lid":"d3-t-lymphatic-system-5","comp":107,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-organs"},{"id":"L02094","dok":3,"q":"The tonsils form a ring around the entrance to the throat, where food and air pass. Why does this location suit their job?","a":"They guard the path of inhaled and swallowed material and meet pathogens early.","options":["They drain lymph from the head straight into the thoracic duct","They filter the blood before it travels on to reach the brain","They are where T cells mature before entering the bloodstream","They meet inhaled and swallowed pathogens early, at the entry point"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Tonsils are secondary lymphatic tissue that sit where food and air enter the body. Their deep pits, called crypts, trap bacteria so the lymphocytes inside can learn to recognize them and mount a defense. Placing them at this shared entry point lets them meet pathogens before those pathogens reach deeper tissues.","notes":["Lymph from the head drains through cervical nodes into the jugular trunks and then a duct. Tonsils are lymphatic tissue, not drainage vessels.","Filtering blood is the job of the spleen in the upper left abdomen. The tonsils sit in mucous membranes and sample what passes through the throat.","T cells mature in the thymus in the mediastinum. Pick the thymus for a question about T cell maturation.","Correct. Tonsils guard the entry point for inhaled and swallowed pathogens."],"lt":"t-lymphatic-system","lid":"d3-t-lymphatic-system-6","comp":107,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-organs"},{"id":"N-m4-lymph-organs-2","dok":1,"q":"Peyer patches are clusters of lymphatic follicles that watch the intestinal contents. Where are they found?","a":"In the wall of the ileum.","options":["In the wall of the ileum","In the wall of the esophagus","In the wall of the rectum","In the wall of the stomach"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Peyer patches are part of MALT, the lymphatic tissue that guards mucous membranes. They sit in the wall of the ileum, the last part of the small intestine, where they sample the intestinal contents for pathogens. The appendix, just beyond the ileum, also has a wall packed with lymphatic follicles.","notes":["Correct. Peyer patches are in the wall of the ileum.","The esophagus is a muscular tube carrying food to the stomach, lined with stratified squamous epithelium. Pick it for a question about the tube behind the trachea.","The rectum is the last part of the large intestine, storing feces before elimination. Pick it for a question about the end of the large intestine.","The stomach wall has gastric pits and glands that secrete acid and enzymes. It does not hold the named clusters called Peyer patches."],"comp":107,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-organs"},{"id":"N-m4-lymph-organs-3","dok":1,"q":"Which lymphatic organ lies in the mediastinum, above the heart and behind the sternum?","a":"The thymus.","options":["The thymus","A palatine tonsil","The cisterna chyli","The spleen"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The thymus sits in the mediastinum, above the heart and just behind the sternum. It is a primary lymphatic organ where T cells mature. It is large in children and shrinks after puberty, so in older adults it is mostly fat.","notes":["Correct. The thymus lies behind the sternum, above the heart.","The palatine tonsils sit in the lateral walls of the oropharynx. Pick them for a question about the tonsils usually removed in a tonsillectomy.","The cisterna chyli is a lymphatic sac in the abdomen where the thoracic duct begins. It is a vessel, not an organ, and it lies below the diaphragm.","The spleen lies in the upper left abdomen, behind the stomach and under the lower left ribs. Pick it for a question about the organ that filters blood."],"comp":107,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-organs"},{"id":"N-m4-lymph-organs-4","dok":3,"q":"A teenager with mononucleosis is told to avoid contact sports for several weeks. Which enlarged lymphatic organ could rupture from a hard hit?","a":"The spleen.","options":["A palatine tonsil","An axillary lymph node","The thymus","The spleen"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Mononucleosis, caused by the Epstein-Barr virus, enlarges the spleen, a condition called splenomegaly. An enlarged spleen extends beyond the protection of the lower left ribs and is easier to tear. Because the spleen holds a large volume of blood, a rupture causes heavy internal bleeding.","notes":["The palatine tonsils can be inflamed in throat infections, but they sit in the throat and do not bleed heavily into the abdomen. Pick them for a question about tonsillitis.","Axillary lymph nodes can swell during infection, but a swollen node does not rupture with heavy internal bleeding. Pick them for a question about swollen glands in the armpit.","The thymus lies behind the sternum and is not the organ enlarged and endangered in mononucleosis. Pick it for a question about T cell maturation.","Correct. The spleen enlarges in mononucleosis and can rupture."],"comp":108,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-disorders"},{"id":"L02321","dok":1,"q":"Which feature of the appendix wall links it to the body's immune defenses?","a":"Its wall is packed with lymphatic follicles.","options":["Its wall is lined with bile-producing cells","Its wall contains clusters of hormone-producing islets","Its wall is packed with lymphatic follicles","Its wall is made mostly of skeletal muscle"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The appendix is a narrow tube off the cecum whose wall is packed with lymphatic follicles, clusters of lymphocytes. This makes it part of the mucosa-associated lymphatic tissue (MALT), along with the tonsils and Peyer patches, which guards the openings and lining of the digestive tract. The lymphocytes in these follicles respond to bacteria in the intestine.","notes":["Bile-producing cells are hepatocytes, found in the liver. Pick this feature for the liver, not the appendix.","Hormone-producing islets are the pancreatic islets, the endocrine part of the pancreas. This feature would be the answer for the pancreas.","Correct. Lymphatic follicles in the wall make the appendix part of MALT.","The wall of the digestive tract below the esophagus is smooth muscle, not skeletal muscle. Skeletal muscle appears at the two ends of the tract, such as the external anal sphincter, so pick it for voluntary control at those ends."],"lt":"t-accessory-digestive-organs","lid":"c26","comp":107,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-organs"},{"id":"L04290","dok":1,"q":"Which two lymphatic organs are primary organs, where lymphocytes form and mature?","a":"Red bone marrow and the thymus.","options":["Red bone marrow and the spleen","The thymus and the lymph nodes","The spleen and the tonsils","Red bone marrow and the thymus"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Primary lymphatic organs are where lymphocytes are produced and become mature. All lymphocytes form in red bone marrow, where B cells also mature, while T cell precursors travel to the thymus to mature there. The other lymphatic organs are secondary, meaning they are where mature lymphocytes meet invaders.","notes":["Red bone marrow is primary, but the spleen is secondary, a filter of the blood where mature lymphocytes respond to blood-borne pathogens. Pick the spleen if the question asked which organ filters blood.","The thymus is primary, but lymph nodes are secondary organs that filter lymph. They would be the answer if the question asked where lymph is cleaned before it returns to the blood.","Both are secondary organs. The spleen filters blood and the tonsils guard the entrance to the throat; pick the tonsils if the question asked which tissue traps inhaled and swallowed pathogens.","Correct. Red bone marrow and the thymus are the primary lymphatic organs."],"lt":"t-w4-lymphatic-organs","lid":"w4-lymphatic-organs-r1","comp":107,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-organs"},{"id":"L04291","dok":1,"q":"What does the spleen filter, and where is it located?","a":"It filters blood, and it sits in the upper left abdomen behind the stomach.","options":["It filters lymph, in the mediastinum behind the sternum","It filters blood, in the upper right abdomen beneath the liver","It filters lymph, in the upper left abdomen behind the stomach","It filters blood, in the upper left abdomen behind the stomach"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The spleen is the largest lymphatic organ, and unlike a lymph node it filters blood rather than lymph. It sits in the upper left abdomen against the diaphragm, behind the stomach, protected by the lower left ribs. Its red pulp removes worn-out red blood cells and platelets, and its white pulp mounts immune responses to blood-borne pathogens.","notes":["The mediastinum behind the sternum is the location of the thymus, which does not filter lymph or blood. Choose that location if the question asked where T cells mature.","The spleen filters blood, but it lies on the left side. The upper right abdomen beneath the liver is where the gallbladder sits, so pick that location if the question asked about the gallbladder.","The location is right, but the spleen has no afferent lymphatic vessels bringing lymph in to be filtered. Filtering lymph is the job of the lymph nodes.","Correct. The spleen filters blood in the upper left abdomen."],"lt":"t-w4-lymphatic-organs","lid":"w4-lymphatic-organs-r3","comp":107,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-organs"},{"id":"L04292","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares a lymph node and the spleen by what each one filters?","a":"A lymph node filters lymph; the spleen filters blood.","options":["Both filter lymph, but the spleen handles a larger volume","A lymph node filters lymph, and the spleen filters blood","Both filter blood, but lymph nodes are spread throughout the body","A lymph node filters blood, and the spleen filters lymph"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Lymph nodes sit along the collecting vessels, and lymph enters them through afferent vessels so macrophages and lymphocytes can clean it before it reaches the blood. The spleen has no afferent lymphatic vessels; blood arrives through the splenic artery instead, and the spleen filters that blood. Both are secondary lymphatic organs, but they guard two different fluids.","notes":["The spleen is the largest lymphatic organ, but it does not receive lymph to filter. Students pick this because the spleen is a lymphatic organ and assume it must handle lymph.","Correct. Nodes clean lymph and the spleen cleans blood.","Lymph nodes do not filter blood; they lie along lymphatic vessels and clean lymph. Only the spleen filters blood, so this choice gives the nodes the spleen's job.","This swaps the two fluids. Lymph nodes receive lymph through afferent lymphatic vessels, while the spleen receives blood from the splenic artery."],"lt":"t-w4-lymphatic-organs","lid":"w4-lymphatic-organs-r4","comp":107,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-organs"},{"id":"L04293","dok":3,"q":"A football player takes a hard blow to the lower left ribs and develops signs of heavy internal bleeding. Which lymphatic organ is most likely injured?","a":"The spleen.","options":["The spleen","The thymus","The appendix","The Peyer patches"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The spleen sits in the upper left abdomen against the diaphragm, just under the lower left ribs, so a blow there can tear it. It is a soft, blood-filled organ whose red pulp is packed with venous sinuses, and a large volume of blood passes through it from the splenic artery. A tear therefore bleeds heavily, which is why a ruptured spleen is a surgical emergency.","notes":["Correct. The spleen lies under the lower left ribs and bleeds heavily when torn.","The thymus lies in the mediastinum behind the sternum, above the heart, not under the lower left ribs. Pick it if the question asked where T cells mature.","The appendix hangs from the cecum in the lower right abdomen, far from the lower left ribs. It would be the answer for a lymphatic organ that can rupture and cause peritonitis.","Peyer patches are clusters of lymphatic follicles in the wall of the ileum, the end of the small intestine. Choose them if the question asked which MALT watches the intestinal contents."],"lt":"t-w4-lymphatic-organs","lid":"w4-lymphatic-organs-r5","comp":108,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-disorders"},{"id":"L04328","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly describes the regions of a lymph node and where lymph enters and leaves?","a":"A capsule surrounds a cortex and a medulla; lymph enters through afferent vessels and leaves through efferent vessels at the hilum.","options":["A capsule surrounds a cortex and a medulla; lymph enters through efferent vessels and leaves through afferent vessels at the hilum","A capsule surrounds a cortex and a medulla; lymph enters at the hilum and leaves through vessels across the capsule","A capsule surrounds red pulp and white pulp; blood enters and leaves through vessels at the hilum","A capsule surrounds a cortex and a medulla; lymph enters through afferent vessels and leaves through efferent vessels at the hilum"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"A lymph node is wrapped in a dense connective tissue capsule, with an outer cortex of follicles (mostly B cells), a deeper cortex of T cells, and an inner medulla lined with macrophages. Several afferent vessels pierce the capsule to bring lymph in, and fewer efferent vessels carry it out at the hilum, the indented side of the node. Because more vessels enter than leave, lymph slows down inside the node, giving time for filtering.","notes":["This swaps afferent and efferent. Afferent means carrying toward (lymph comes in), and efferent means carrying away (lymph goes out).","The sites are reversed. Many afferent vessels enter all around the capsule, and the hilum is where the efferent vessels leave and blood vessels enter.","Red pulp and white pulp are the regions of the spleen, which filters blood. Pick this description if the question asked about the structure of the spleen.","Correct. Afferent vessels bring lymph in through the capsule, and efferent vessels carry it out at the hilum."],"lt":"t-w4-lymph-vessels-node","lid":"w4-lymph-vessels-node-r3","comp":106,"competencyId":"w4-lymph-vessels-node"},{"id":"L04331","dok":2,"q":"Which primary lymphatic organ is the site where all lymphocytes, including future T cells, first form?","a":"Red bone marrow.","options":["The spleen","Red bone marrow","The lymph nodes","The thymus"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"All lymphocytes form from stem cells in red bone marrow. B cells and NK cells stay in the marrow to mature, while T cell precursors leave and travel to the thymus to finish maturing. That is why both red bone marrow and the thymus count as primary lymphatic organs, but only the marrow is where every lymphocyte begins.","notes":["The spleen is a secondary organ where mature lymphocytes respond to blood-borne pathogens. It would be the answer if the question asked which organ filters blood.","Correct. Every lymphocyte forms in red bone marrow, even the ones that mature elsewhere.","Lymph nodes are secondary organs where mature lymphocytes meet invaders carried in the lymph. Choose them if the question asked where lymph is filtered.","The thymus is a primary organ, but it is where T cells mature after arriving from the marrow, not where they first form. Pick the thymus if the question asked where T cells mature."],"lt":"t-w4-hematopoiesis","lid":"w4-hematopoiesis-r3","comp":107,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-organs"},{"id":"N-a17d-108-1","dok":1,"q":"What does the term splenomegaly mean?","a":"An enlarged spleen.","options":["Enlarged lymph nodes.","An enlarged spleen.","Inflamed palatine tonsils.","Swelling from blocked lymph vessels."],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Splenomegaly names its structure in the word itself: the spleen, made larger. It can happen with an infection such as mononucleosis or with a blood disorder such as leukemia. An enlarged spleen ruptures more easily, which matters because the spleen sits just under the lower left ribs and holds a large volume of blood.","notes":["Enlarged lymph nodes are lymphadenopathy, the swollen glands a clinician feels in the neck, armpit, and groin.","Correct. Splenomegaly is enlargement of the spleen, for example in mononucleosis or leukemia.","Inflamed tonsils, usually the palatine tonsils, are tonsillitis, which is common in childhood.","Long-term swelling from blocked, damaged, or missing lymphatic vessels is lymphedema."],"comp":108,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-disorders"},{"id":"N-a17d-108-2","dok":1,"q":"What is lymphadenopathy?","a":"Enlargement of the lymph nodes.","options":["Excess fluid in the interstitial space.","Enlargement of the lymph nodes.","Infection and swelling of the tonsils.","Enlargement of the spleen."],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Lymphadenopathy means the lymph nodes are enlarged. Nodes swell as they trap and fight pathogens, and they can also enlarge when cancer cells lodge in them. These are the swollen glands a clinician feels for in the neck, armpit, and groin.","notes":["Excess interstitial fluid is edema, which happens when fluid leaks out faster than the lymphatics can carry it away.","Correct. Lymphadenopathy means enlarged lymph nodes, often while they fight a nearby infection.","Infected, swollen tonsils are tonsillitis. Lymphadenopathy is about the lymph nodes.","An enlarged spleen is splenomegaly. Lymphadenopathy affects the lymph nodes, not the spleen."],"comp":108,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-disorders"},{"id":"N-a17d-108-3","dok":2,"q":"Edema and lymphedema both cause tissue swelling. Which statement correctly compares the structure each one affects?","a":"Edema is fluid built up in the interstitial space; lymphedema comes from blocked or damaged lymphatic vessels.","options":["Edema comes from blocked or damaged lymphatic vessels; lymphedema is fluid built up in the lymph nodes.","Both affect the lymph nodes, which enlarge; lymphedema is simply the longer-lasting form of the same thing.","Edema is fluid built up in the interstitial space; lymphedema comes from blocked or damaged lymphatic vessels.","Edema affects the spleen as it enlarges; lymphedema affects the tonsils as they swell with infection."],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Both disorders show up as swelling, but they start in different places. Edema is excess fluid in the interstitial space, which builds up when fluid leaks out of the blood capillaries faster than the lymphatics can drain it. Lymphedema is a specific kind of edema caused by blocked, damaged, or missing lymphatic vessels, which is why it can follow surgery that removes lymph nodes.","notes":["This mixes up the two. Edema is excess interstitial fluid, not a vessel problem, and lymphedema is caused by blocked, damaged, or missing lymphatic vessels, not fluid in the nodes.","Enlarged lymph nodes are lymphadenopathy. Edema affects the interstitial space and lymphedema affects the lymphatic vessels.","Correct. Edema is about the interstitial space, while lymphedema is caused by failed lymphatic vessels.","An enlarged spleen is splenomegaly and swollen tonsils are tonsillitis. Neither one is edema or lymphedema."],"comp":108,"competencyId":"w4-lymphatic-disorders"}]},{"id":"m4-endocrine","title":"Endocrine glands","summary":"hypothalamus, pituitary, pineal, thyroid, parathyroid, thymus, adrenal zones, pancreatic islets, gonads, other organs with endocrine tissue; location and structure","mw":"2026-11-02","tr":"2026-11-03","cards":[{"id":"L01136","dok":1,"q":"Which description correctly gives the location of the pituitary gland and the names of its two lobes?","a":"In the sella turcica of the sphenoid bone; an anterior lobe (adenohypophysis) and a posterior lobe (neurohypophysis).","options":["In the anterior neck; two lobes joined by an isthmus","In the sella turcica; cortex and medulla","In the posterior roof of the third ventricle; adenohypophysis and neurohypophysis","In the sella turcica; adenohypophysis and neurohypophysis"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The pituitary gland, or hypophysis, is about the size of a pea and sits in the sella turcica, the saddle-shaped pocket of the sphenoid bone. It hangs from the hypothalamus by a stalk called the infundibulum. It has two lobes made of different tissues: the anterior lobe, or adenohypophysis, which is glandular, and the posterior lobe, or neurohypophysis, which is nervous tissue.","notes":["The pituitary is in the skull, not the neck. An anterior neck gland with two lobes joined by an isthmus is the thyroid gland.","The location is right, but cortex and medulla are the two regions of the adrenal gland, not the lobes of the pituitary.","The lobe names are right, but the pituitary sits below the brain in the sella turcica. The posterior roof of the third ventricle is where the pineal gland sits.","Correct. The pituitary sits in the sphenoid's saddle and has an anterior lobe (adenohypophysis) and a posterior lobe (neurohypophysis)."],"lt":"t-endocrine","lid":"fr1","comp":129,"competencyId":"w6-pituitary"},{"id":"L01137","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares how the two pituitary lobes connect to the hypothalamus?","a":"The anterior lobe is linked by the hypophyseal portal system (blood vessels); the posterior lobe is linked by a tract of nerve fibers through the infundibulum.","options":["Both lobes by a nerve fiber tract","Anterior by a nerve fiber tract; posterior by portal blood vessels","Both lobes by the hypophyseal portal system","Anterior by portal blood vessels; posterior by a nerve fiber tract"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The type of tissue in each lobe tells you how it connects to the hypothalamus. The anterior lobe is glandular epithelium, so the hypothalamus reaches it through blood, using a set of vessels called the hypophyseal portal system. The posterior lobe is nervous tissue, an extension of the hypothalamus, so it is connected by a tract of nerve fibers running down through the infundibulum.","notes":["Only the posterior lobe is linked by nerve fibers, because it is an extension of the hypothalamus. The anterior lobe is glandular and connects through blood vessels.","This reverses the two links. The posterior lobe is itself nervous tissue, so it is the one connected by nerve fibers.","Only the anterior lobe receives the portal vessels. Pick the portal system if the question asked about the anterior lobe alone.","Correct. Glandular tissue is linked by blood, and nervous tissue by nerve fibers."],"lt":"t-endocrine","lid":"fr2","comp":129,"competencyId":"w6-pituitary"},{"id":"L01138","dok":1,"q":"Which two cell types are found in the thyroid gland?","a":"Follicular cells and parafollicular cells (also called C cells).","options":["Follicular cells and parafollicular cells","Alpha cells and beta cells","Chief cells and follicular cells","Chromaffin cells and chief cells"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The thyroid gland is made of microscopic hollow spheres called follicles. Follicular cells form the wall of each follicle and surround the stored colloid. Parafollicular cells, also called C cells, are scattered in the spaces between the follicles.","notes":["Correct. Follicular cells form the follicle walls, and parafollicular (C) cells sit between the follicles.","Alpha and beta cells are not in the thyroid. They are endocrine cells of the pancreatic islets in the abdomen.","Chief cells are the main hormone-making cells of the parathyroid glands, not the thyroid. Only the follicular cells in this pair belong to the thyroid.","Neither cell belongs to the thyroid. Chromaffin cells are in the adrenal medulla, and chief cells are in the parathyroid glands."],"lt":"t-endocrine","lid":"fr3","comp":130,"competencyId":"w6-thyroid-parathyroid"},{"id":"L01139","dok":1,"q":"How many parathyroid glands does a person usually have, and where are they?","a":"Usually four, on the posterior surface of the thyroid gland.","options":["Usually four, on the posterior surface of the thyroid","Usually two, on the anterior surface of the thyroid","Usually one, in the mediastinum behind the sternum","Usually four, one capping each kidney"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Most people have four small, round parathyroid glands, a superior and an inferior gland on the posterior surface of each thyroid lobe. Each one has its own thin capsule that separates it from the thyroid tissue. Because they are so small and sit right on the thyroid, surgeons must take care to spare them during thyroid surgery.","notes":["Correct. Usually four small glands sit on the back of the thyroid lobes, two on each side.","Both the number and the side are wrong. The front of the thyroid is crossed by the isthmus, and the parathyroids are on the back, usually four of them.","The parathyroids are in the neck, not the chest. A single organ in the mediastinum behind the sternum is the thymus.","The parathyroids are in the neck, not on the kidneys. The glands on the superior pole of each kidney are the two adrenal glands."],"lt":"t-endocrine","lid":"fr4","comp":128,"competencyId":"w6-endocrine-locate"},{"id":"L01140","dok":1,"q":"Which description gives the location of an adrenal gland and its two main regions?","a":"Capping the superior pole of each kidney; an outer adrenal cortex and an inner adrenal medulla.","options":["On the posterior surface of the thyroid; outer cortex and inner medulla","Behind the stomach; islets and acini","On top of each kidney; outer cortex and inner medulla","On top of each kidney; anterior lobe and posterior lobe"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Each adrenal gland, also called a suprarenal gland, caps the superior pole of a kidney. It is really two glands in one: an outer adrenal cortex made of glandular tissue arranged in three zones, and an inner adrenal medulla made of modified nervous tissue that belongs to the sympathetic nervous system.","notes":["The regions are right, but the back of the thyroid is where the parathyroid glands sit, not the adrenal glands.","Islets and acini belong to the pancreas, which lies behind the stomach. The adrenal glands sit on the kidneys and have a cortex and a medulla instead.","Correct. Each adrenal gland caps a kidney and is built as a cortex around a medulla.","The location is right, but anterior and posterior lobes describe the pituitary gland, not the adrenal gland."],"lt":"t-endocrine","lid":"fr5","comp":131,"competencyId":"w6-adrenal"},{"id":"L01141","dok":1,"q":"Which zone of the adrenal cortex lies deepest, directly against the adrenal medulla?","a":"The zona reticularis.","options":["Zona reticularis","Zona fasciculata","Zona glomerulosa","Adrenal medulla"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The adrenal cortex has three zones, in order from the surface inward: the zona glomerulosa, the zona fasciculata, and the zona reticularis. The zona reticularis is the deepest, a branching network of cells lying against the medulla.","notes":["Correct. The zona reticularis is the inner zone, bordering the medulla.","The zona fasciculata is the thick middle zone, the widest of the three. It would be the answer for the zone with cells in straight columns.","The zona glomerulosa is the thin outer zone, just beneath the capsule. Pick it for the most superficial zone.","The medulla is the inner core itself, made of modified nervous tissue, so it is not a zone of the cortex. Pick it if the question asked for the innermost part of the whole adrenal gland."],"lt":"t-endocrine","lid":"fr6","comp":131,"competencyId":"w6-adrenal"},{"id":"L01147","dok":1,"q":"In a thyroid follicle, which cells form the wall around the colloid?","a":"Follicular cells.","options":["Chief cells","Parafollicular cells","Chromaffin cells","Follicular cells"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The thyroid is built of microscopic hollow spheres called follicles. A single layer of follicular cells, a simple cuboidal epithelium, forms the wall of each follicle, and the center is filled with colloid, a protein-rich fluid that stains pink. Parafollicular (C) cells sit between follicles or at the outer edge of a follicle wall, and they never touch the colloid.","notes":["Chief cells are not in the thyroid at all. They are the main cells of the parathyroid glands on the back of the thyroid.","Parafollicular (C) cells sit between follicles or at the outer edge of a follicle wall, and they never touch the colloid. The cells that line the colloid are the follicular cells.","Chromaffin cells are not in the thyroid. They make up the adrenal medulla, the inner core of the adrenal gland.","Correct. Follicular cells line each follicle and surround its colloid."],"lt":"t-endocrine","lid":"fr12","comp":130,"competencyId":"w6-thyroid-parathyroid"},{"id":"L01149","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares the pancreatic islets and the acini?","a":"The islets are endocrine and release hormones into the blood; the acini are exocrine and release digestive enzymes into ducts.","options":["Both release hormones directly into the blood","Islets release enzymes into ducts; acini release hormones into blood","Both release digestive enzymes through ducts","Islets release hormones into blood; acini release enzymes into ducts"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The pancreas is a mixed organ that sits behind the stomach. Most of its tissue is exocrine acini, clusters of cells that make digestive enzymes and send them through ducts to the small intestine. Scattered among them are the pancreatic islets, or islets of Langerhans, small clusters of endocrine cells that release hormones directly into the blood.","notes":["Only the islets are endocrine. The acini are exocrine clusters that secrete enzymes through ducts.","This reverses the two roles. The islets are the scattered hormone-producing clusters, while the acini make digestive enzymes.","Only the acini are exocrine. The islets are ductless and secrete hormones into the blood.","Correct. The islets are the endocrine part, and the acini are the exocrine part."],"lt":"t-endocrine","lid":"fr14","comp":132,"competencyId":"w6-endocrine-tissue-organs"},{"id":"L01150","dok":1,"q":"The hypothalamus is part of which division of the brain?","a":"The diencephalon.","options":["The cerebrum","The cerebellum","The brainstem","The diencephalon"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The hypothalamus lies in the diencephalon, below the thalamus, where it forms the floor and walls of the third ventricle. It links the nervous and endocrine systems and directs the pituitary gland, which hangs below it on the infundibulum. The pineal gland is also in the diencephalon, in the roof of the third ventricle.","notes":["The cerebrum is the large upper part of the brain with its lobes and cortex. The hypothalamus lies below it, in the diencephalon.","The cerebellum sits behind the brainstem and coordinates movement. It is a separate division, not the one that holds the hypothalamus.","The brainstem (midbrain, pons, and medulla oblongata) lies below the diencephalon. The hypothalamus sits just above it.","Correct. The hypothalamus forms the floor and lower walls of the third ventricle in the diencephalon."],"lt":"t-endocrine","lid":"fr15","comp":128,"competencyId":"w6-endocrine-locate"},{"id":"L01152","dok":1,"q":"Where is the pituitary gland located?","a":"In the sella turcica of the sphenoid bone, hanging below the hypothalamus.","options":["In the sella turcica of the sphenoid bone","In the mediastinum behind the sternum","Capping the superior pole of each kidney","On the posterior surface of the thyroid gland"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The pituitary gland sits in the sella turcica, a saddle-shaped pocket in the body of the sphenoid bone in the middle of the base of the skull. It hangs from the hypothalamus by a stalk, the infundibulum. Bone forms the floor and the front and back walls of the pocket, so a growing pituitary tumor usually expands upward toward the optic chiasm.","notes":["Correct. The pituitary rests in the sphenoid's saddle and hangs from the hypothalamus.","The pituitary is in the skull, not the chest. The mediastinum behind the sternum is where the thymus lies.","The pituitary is in the skull, not the abdomen. The superior pole of each kidney is where the adrenal glands sit.","The pituitary is in the skull, not the neck. The posterior surface of the thyroid is where the parathyroid glands sit."],"lt":"t-endocrine","lid":"c1","comp":129,"competencyId":"w6-pituitary"},{"id":"L01153","dok":1,"q":"Where is the thyroid gland located?","a":"In the anterior neck, below the larynx.","options":["In the anterior neck, below the larynx","Scattered through the pancreas behind the stomach","In the pelvis on each side of the uterus","In the posterior roof of the third ventricle"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The thyroid gland lies in the anterior neck, just below the larynx. Its two lobes lie beside the trachea, and a bridge of tissue called the isthmus joins them across the front. Because it sits just under the skin, an enlarged thyroid can be seen and felt.","notes":["Correct. The thyroid's lobes lie beside the trachea just below the larynx.","The thyroid is one gland in the neck, not tissue scattered in the abdomen. Clusters scattered through the pancreas are the pancreatic islets.","The thyroid is in the neck, not the pelvis. One on each side of the uterus describes the ovaries.","The thyroid is in the neck, not the brain. The posterior roof of the third ventricle is where the pineal gland sits."],"lt":"t-endocrine","lid":"c2","comp":128,"competencyId":"w6-endocrine-locate"},{"id":"L01154","dok":1,"q":"What is the infundibulum?","a":"The slender stalk connecting the pituitary gland to the hypothalamus.","options":["The stalk connecting the pituitary to the hypothalamus","The bridge of tissue joining the two thyroid lobes","The fluid stored in the center of a thyroid follicle","The saddle-shaped pocket of the sphenoid bone"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The infundibulum is the slender stalk that connects the pituitary gland to the hypothalamus above it. A tract of nerve fibers runs down through it to the posterior lobe, and the vessels of the hypophyseal portal system run along it to the anterior lobe, so it is the physical link between brain and pituitary.","notes":["Correct. The pituitary hangs from the hypothalamus by the infundibulum.","That bridge is the isthmus of the thyroid in the neck. It joins the thyroid lobes and has no link to the pituitary.","That fluid is colloid, inside thyroid follicles. It is not a stalk and is not part of the pituitary.","That pocket is the sella turcica, the bone the pituitary sits in. The infundibulum is the soft stalk above it, not the bone."],"lt":"t-endocrine","lid":"c3","comp":129,"competencyId":"w6-pituitary"},{"id":"L01155","dok":1,"q":"What is another name for the pituitary gland?","a":"The hypophysis.","options":["The hypophysis","The hypothalamus","The infundibulum","The pineal gland"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The pituitary gland is also called the hypophysis. That is why its lobes are called the adenohypophysis (anterior, glandular) and the neurohypophysis (posterior, nervous), and why the vessels that serve the anterior lobe are called the hypophyseal portal system.","notes":["Correct. Hypophysis is another name for the pituitary gland.","The hypothalamus is the brain region above the pituitary that controls it. The names look alike, which is why students mix them up, but they are separate structures.","The infundibulum is the stalk connecting the pituitary to the hypothalamus, not the gland itself.","The pineal gland is a different endocrine gland, a small cone in the posterior roof of the third ventricle, not the pituitary."],"lt":"t-endocrine","lid":"c4","comp":129,"competencyId":"w6-pituitary"},{"id":"L01156","dok":1,"q":"What is the isthmus of the thyroid gland?","a":"The bridge of tissue connecting the two thyroid lobes.","options":["The bridge of tissue connecting the two thyroid lobes","The microscopic hollow spheres that make up the gland","The protein-rich fluid stored inside each follicle","A small gland on the back of the thyroid"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The thyroid gland has two lateral lobes, one on each side of the trachea. The isthmus is the narrow bridge of thyroid tissue that crosses the front of the trachea and joins the two lobes, giving the gland its butterfly or H shape.","notes":["Correct. The isthmus crosses the front of the trachea between the two lobes.","Those hollow spheres are the thyroid follicles, the microscopic units inside the gland. The isthmus is a visible bridge of tissue, not a microscopic unit.","That fluid is colloid, found inside each follicle. The isthmus is solid gland tissue joining the lobes.","A small gland on the back of the thyroid is a parathyroid gland. The isthmus is part of the thyroid itself and lies in front of the trachea."],"lt":"t-endocrine","lid":"c5","comp":130,"competencyId":"w6-thyroid-parathyroid"},{"id":"L01157","dok":1,"q":"Where are the parathyroid glands located?","a":"On the posterior surface of the thyroid gland.","options":["On the posterior surface of the thyroid gland","In the mediastinum behind the sternum","On the superior pole of each kidney","Inside the sella turcica of the sphenoid bone"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The parathyroid glands are usually four small, round glands on the posterior surface of the thyroid lobes, two on each side. Each has its own thin capsule. Their name means beside the thyroid, and because they are tiny and sit right on the thyroid, they must be found and spared during thyroid surgery.","notes":["Correct. The small parathyroid glands sit on the back of the thyroid lobes.","The parathyroids are in the neck, not the chest. The mediastinum behind the sternum is where the thymus lies.","The parathyroids are in the neck, not the abdomen. The superior pole of each kidney is where the adrenal glands sit.","The parathyroids are in the neck, not the skull. The sella turcica holds the pituitary gland."],"lt":"t-endocrine","lid":"c6","comp":128,"competencyId":"w6-endocrine-locate"},{"id":"L01158","dok":1,"q":"An adrenal gland has an outer region and an inner core. What is the outer region called?","a":"The adrenal cortex.","options":["The zona reticularis","The adrenal cortex","The adrenal capsule","The adrenal medulla"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Each adrenal gland is two glands in one. The outer adrenal cortex is glandular tissue that forms the bulk of the gland and is arranged in three zones. The inner adrenal medulla is modified nervous tissue belonging to the sympathetic nervous system. The words cortex (bark or outer layer) and medulla (marrow or core) are used the same way in the kidney and brain.","notes":["The zona reticularis is one zone of the cortex, the deepest one against the medulla. It is only part of the outer region, not its name.","Correct. The cortex is the outer region and the bulk of the gland.","The capsule is the thin connective tissue covering around the whole gland. It surrounds the cortex but is not glandular tissue itself.","The medulla is the inner core, made of modified nervous tissue. Pick it for the inner part of the adrenal gland."],"lt":"t-endocrine","lid":"c7","comp":131,"competencyId":"w6-adrenal"},{"id":"L01159","dok":1,"q":"What term describes a gland that secretes its product directly into the surrounding fluid and blood, with no duct?","a":"An endocrine gland.","options":["A follicular cell","A target cell","An endocrine gland","An exocrine gland"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"An endocrine gland is a ductless gland that releases its product, a hormone, directly into the surrounding tissue fluid and blood. The blood then carries the hormone throughout the body. Exocrine glands are the contrast: they release their products through ducts onto a surface or into a cavity.","notes":["A follicular cell is one specific cell type, forming the wall of a thyroid follicle. It would be the answer for the cells around the colloid.","A target cell is a cell that carries a receptor for a hormone and responds to it. It receives the hormone rather than secreting it.","Correct. Endocrine glands are ductless and release hormones into the blood.","An exocrine gland secretes through a duct onto a surface, such as a sweat gland or the pancreatic acini. Pick it for a gland with a duct."],"lt":"t-endocrine","lid":"c8","comp":128,"competencyId":"w6-endocrine-locate"},{"id":"L01160","dok":1,"q":"Where is the thymus located?","a":"In the mediastinum, behind the sternum.","options":["In the anterior neck below the larynx","On the superior pole of each kidney","In the mediastinum, behind the sternum","In the diencephalon of the brain"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The thymus lies in the mediastinum, the central compartment of the thoracic cavity, behind the sternum and above the heart. It is large in childhood and shrinks after puberty. It belongs to both the lymphatic and the endocrine systems.","notes":["The thymus is in the chest, not the neck. The anterior neck below the larynx is where the thyroid gland sits.","The thymus is in the chest, not the abdomen. The superior pole of each kidney is where the adrenal glands sit.","Correct. The thymus lies in the mediastinum behind the sternum.","The thymus is in the chest, not the brain. The diencephalon holds the hypothalamus and the pineal gland."],"lt":"t-endocrine","lid":"c9","comp":128,"competencyId":"w6-endocrine-locate"},{"id":"L01161","dok":1,"q":"Where is the pineal gland located?","a":"In the posterior roof of the third ventricle of the brain.","options":["In the sella turcica of the sphenoid bone","In the mediastinum behind the sternum","In the floor and walls of the third ventricle","In the posterior roof of the third ventricle"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The pineal gland is a small, cone-shaped gland in the posterior roof of the third ventricle. It is part of the epithalamus of the diencephalon. The hypothalamus lies below it in the floor of the same ventricle, which is why the two are easy to confuse by location.","notes":["The sella turcica holds the pituitary gland, which hangs below the brain. The pineal gland is up in the roof of the third ventricle instead.","The pineal gland is in the brain, not the chest. The mediastinum behind the sternum is where the thymus lies.","The floor and lower walls of the third ventricle are formed by the hypothalamus. The pineal gland sits on the opposite side, in the roof.","Correct. The pineal gland projects from the posterior roof of the third ventricle."],"lt":"t-endocrine","lid":"c10","comp":128,"competencyId":"w6-endocrine-locate"},{"id":"L01162","dok":1,"q":"What are the thyroid follicles?","a":"The microscopic hollow spheres that make up the thyroid gland.","options":["The connective tissue capsule around the gland","The four small glands behind the thyroid","The microscopic hollow spheres that make up the thyroid","The endocrine cell clusters of the pancreas"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The thyroid gland is built of thousands of microscopic hollow spheres called follicles. Each follicle has a wall of follicular cells around a center filled with colloid, the protein-rich fluid where the thyroid stores its product. Parafollicular cells sit in the spaces between follicles.","notes":["The capsule is the outer covering of the thyroid, not its working units. It would be the answer for the gland's protective wrapping.","These are the parathyroid glands. Pick them for the glands on the posterior surface of the thyroid.","Correct. Follicles are the functional units of the thyroid.","These are the pancreatic islets. Pick them for the endocrine tissue of the pancreas."],"lt":"t-endocrine","lid":"c11","comp":130,"competencyId":"w6-thyroid-parathyroid"},{"id":"L01163","dok":1,"q":"Which gland is the largest purely endocrine gland in the body?","a":"The thyroid gland.","options":["The thyroid gland","The pineal gland","The adrenal gland","The pituitary gland"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The thyroid gland is the largest gland in the body whose whole job is making hormones. Larger organs such as the pancreas contain endocrine tissue, but they are mixed organs with other jobs. The thyroid sits in the anterior neck just below the larynx, with a lobe on each side of the trachea.","notes":["Correct. The thyroid is the largest gland whose only job is making hormones.","The pineal gland is tiny and cone-shaped, in the posterior roof of the third ventricle. It is far smaller than the thyroid.","Each adrenal gland is a small cap on a kidney, much smaller than the thyroid.","The pituitary is about the size of a pea. It is a major endocrine gland but far smaller than the thyroid."],"lt":"t-endocrine","lid":"c12","comp":128,"competencyId":"w6-endocrine-locate"},{"id":"L01164","dok":1,"q":"A gland caps the superior pole of each kidney. Which gland is it?","a":"The adrenal gland.","options":["The pancreas","The thyroid gland","The thymus","The adrenal gland"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Each adrenal gland caps the superior pole of a kidney, which is why it is also called the suprarenal gland (supra means above, renal means kidney). Inside, it has an outer cortex of glandular tissue and an inner medulla of modified nervous tissue.","notes":["The pancreas lies behind the stomach. It is near the kidneys in the abdomen but does not cap them; pick it for the mixed exocrine and endocrine organ.","The thyroid is in the anterior neck, wrapped around the trachea. Pick it for the gland just below the larynx.","The thymus lies in the mediastinum behind the sternum. It would be the answer for the chest organ that shrinks after puberty.","Correct. Each adrenal gland sits on top of a kidney."],"lt":"t-endocrine","lid":"c13","comp":128,"competencyId":"w6-endocrine-locate"},{"id":"L01165","dok":1,"q":"Which brain structure forms the floor of the third ventricle and directs the pituitary gland?","a":"The hypothalamus.","options":["The pineal gland","The pituitary gland","The hypothalamus","The thalamus"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The hypothalamus is part of the diencephalon. It forms the floor and lower walls of the third ventricle, and the pituitary hangs from it by the infundibulum. It links the nervous and endocrine systems, directing the pituitary through nerve fibers to the posterior lobe and through portal blood vessels to the anterior lobe.","notes":["The pineal gland sits in the posterior roof of the third ventricle, not its floor, and it does not control the pituitary.","The pituitary is the gland being controlled, hanging below the hypothalamus in the sella turcica. It is not part of the third ventricle.","Correct. The hypothalamus forms the floor of the third ventricle and controls the pituitary.","The thalamus sits above the hypothalamus and forms the upper part of the side walls of the third ventricle. It does not form the floor or control the pituitary."],"lt":"t-endocrine","lid":"c14","comp":128,"competencyId":"w6-endocrine-locate"},{"id":"L01166","dok":1,"q":"The anterior lobe of the pituitary is also known by which name?","a":"The adenohypophysis.","options":["The adenohypophysis","The hypophyseal portal system","The neurohypophysis","The infundibulum"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The anterior lobe of the pituitary is called the adenohypophysis. The prefix adeno means gland, which fits its glandular epithelial tissue. The posterior lobe is the neurohypophysis, with neuro pointing to its nervous tissue. Reading the prefixes is the fastest way to match each name to its lobe.","notes":["Correct. Adeno means gland, matching the glandular anterior lobe.","This is the set of blood vessels linking the hypothalamus to the anterior lobe. It serves the anterior lobe but is not another name for it.","The neurohypophysis is the posterior lobe, made of nervous tissue. Pick it for the lobe connected by nerve fibers.","The infundibulum is the stalk connecting the pituitary to the hypothalamus. It would be the answer for the stalk, not a lobe."],"lt":"t-endocrine","lid":"c15","comp":129,"competencyId":"w6-pituitary"},{"id":"L01167","dok":2,"q":"Which structure is nervous tissue that is a direct extension of the hypothalamus itself?","a":"The posterior lobe of the pituitary.","options":["The posterior lobe of the pituitary","The adrenal medulla","The pineal gland","The anterior lobe of the pituitary"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The posterior lobe of the pituitary, the neurohypophysis, is nervous tissue that grows down from the hypothalamus itself. Axons from hypothalamic neurons run through the infundibulum and end in the posterior lobe, where their hormones are stored. That is why its link to the hypothalamus is a nerve tract rather than blood vessels.","notes":["Correct. The posterior lobe, or neurohypophysis, is an extension of the hypothalamus.","The adrenal medulla is modified nervous tissue too, but it sits on the kidney and belongs to the sympathetic nervous system. It is not part of the hypothalamus.","The pineal gland is in the roof of the third ventricle and belongs to the epithalamus, not the hypothalamus.","The anterior lobe is glandular epithelial tissue, linked to the hypothalamus by blood vessels rather than being part of it."],"lt":"t-endocrine","lid":"c16","comp":129,"competencyId":"w6-pituitary"},{"id":"L01168","dok":1,"q":"The parafollicular cells scattered between the thyroid follicles are also known by which name?","a":"C cells.","options":["Acinar cells","Chief cells","Follicular cells","C cells"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The thyroid has two cell types. Follicular cells form the walls of the follicles, while parafollicular cells sit between the follicles or at the outer edge of a follicle wall, never touching the colloid. Parafollicular cells are also called C cells. They are larger and paler than follicular cells.","notes":["Acinar cells are not in the thyroid. They form the exocrine acini of the pancreas, which make digestive enzymes.","Chief cells are the main hormone-making cells of the parathyroid glands. Chief and C cells both start with C, which is why students mix them up, but chief cells belong to a different gland.","Follicular cells form the wall of each follicle around the colloid. Parafollicular cells are a separate type that sit between follicles.","Correct. Parafollicular cells are also called C cells."],"lt":"t-endocrine","lid":"c17","comp":130,"competencyId":"w6-thyroid-parathyroid"},{"id":"L01169","dok":2,"q":"A slide of the pancreas shows pale, rounded clusters of cells surrounded by many darker-staining glandular clusters. What are the pale clusters?","a":"The pancreatic islets (islets of Langerhans).","options":["The pancreatic islets","The acini","The thyroid follicles","The chief cells"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"On a pancreas slide, most of the tissue is acini, exocrine clusters whose cells stain darkly. Scattered among them are the pancreatic islets, also called the islets of Langerhans, which stain more lightly and look like pale islands in a dark sea. The islets are the endocrine portion of the pancreas.","notes":["Correct. The islets appear as pale islands among the darker acini.","The acini are the darker-staining exocrine clusters that make up most of the pancreas. Pick them for the surrounding tissue that makes digestive enzymes.","Thyroid follicles are hollow spheres of follicular cells filled with colloid, found in the neck, not the pancreas. They would be the answer on a thyroid slide.","Chief cells make up most of a parathyroid gland. They are not found in the pancreas."],"lt":"t-endocrine","lid":"c18","comp":132,"competencyId":"w6-endocrine-tissue-organs"},{"id":"L01170","dok":1,"q":"The adrenal medulla, the inner core of the adrenal gland, is modified tissue of which system?","a":"The sympathetic nervous system.","options":["The somatic nervous system","The parasympathetic nervous system","The sympathetic nervous system","The lymphatic system"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The adrenal medulla is modified nervous tissue that belongs to the sympathetic nervous system. It develops from the neural crest, the same embryonic tissue that forms the sympathetic ganglia. Its chromaffin cells are modified sympathetic neurons with no axons or dendrites, and they are supplied by preganglionic sympathetic fibers, so the medulla works like a sympathetic ganglion.","notes":["The somatic nervous system controls skeletal muscle. It has no tissue in the adrenal gland.","The parasympathetic division is the other branch of the autonomic nervous system. The adrenal medulla belongs to the sympathetic branch instead.","Correct. The adrenal medulla is modified sympathetic nervous tissue.","The lymphatic system includes the thymus, spleen, and lymph nodes. The adrenal medulla is nervous tissue, not lymphatic tissue."],"lt":"t-endocrine","lid":"c19","comp":131,"competencyId":"w6-adrenal"},{"id":"L01171","dok":1,"q":"Chief cells are the main hormone-producing cells of which gland?","a":"The parathyroid glands.","options":["The thyroid gland","The pineal gland","The parathyroid glands","The adrenal cortex"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The parathyroid glands are usually four small glands on the posterior surface of the thyroid. Their main hormone-producing cells are called chief cells. The thyroid beside them has a different pair of cells, follicular cells and parafollicular cells, so the cell name tells you which of the two neighboring glands you are looking at.","notes":["The thyroid's cells are follicular cells and parafollicular (C) cells, not chief cells. Chief cells belong to the small glands on its back.","The pineal gland is a small cone in the roof of the third ventricle. It is not built of chief cells.","Correct. Chief cells are the main hormone-making cells of the parathyroids.","The adrenal cortex is glandular tissue in three zones (glomerulosa, fasciculata, reticularis), not chief cells."],"lt":"t-endocrine","lid":"c20","comp":130,"competencyId":"w6-thyroid-parathyroid"},{"id":"L01172","dok":1,"q":"The pancreas is both an exocrine and an endocrine organ. Which part of it is the endocrine tissue?","a":"The pancreatic islets.","options":["The follicular cells","The colloid","The pancreatic islets","The acini"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The pancreas is a mixed organ. Most of it is exocrine acini, which make digestive enzymes, but scattered through it are the pancreatic islets, or islets of Langerhans. These small clusters of endocrine cells release hormones such as insulin and glucagon directly into the blood.","notes":["Follicular cells form the walls of thyroid follicles, in the neck. They would be the answer for the thyroid.","Colloid is the protein-rich fluid stored inside thyroid follicles. It is not found in the pancreas.","Correct. The islets are the hormone-producing part of the pancreas.","The acini are the exocrine clusters that make digestive enzymes and send them through ducts. Pick them for the exocrine tissue."],"lt":"t-endocrine","lid":"c21","comp":132,"competencyId":"w6-endocrine-tissue-organs"},{"id":"L01173","dok":2,"q":"Which structure carries hypothalamic signals to the anterior lobe of the pituitary?","a":"The hypophyseal portal system.","options":["A tract of nerve fibers through the infundibulum","The cerebrospinal fluid of the third ventricle","The sella turcica","The hypophyseal portal system"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The anterior lobe is glandular tissue, so the hypothalamus reaches it through the blood. The hypophyseal portal system is a set of vessels in which blood passes through capillaries in the hypothalamus, then travels down to a second capillary bed in the anterior lobe. This carries hypothalamic signals straight to the anterior pituitary without first passing through the rest of the body.","notes":["This is the link to the posterior lobe, which is nervous tissue. Pick it if the question asked how the hypothalamus connects to the neurohypophysis.","The third ventricle sits above the pituitary, but its fluid does not carry the hypothalamus's signals to the gland. This choice comes from confusing location with connection.","The sella turcica is the bony saddle that holds the pituitary. It protects the gland but carries no signals.","Correct. These blood vessels carry hypothalamic signals to the anterior lobe."],"lt":"t-endocrine","lid":"c22","comp":129,"competencyId":"w6-pituitary"},{"id":"L01174","dok":1,"q":"Besides being reproductive organs, what else are the ovaries and testes?","a":"Endocrine glands.","options":["Lymphatic organs","Parts of the urinary system","Endocrine glands","Organs of the digestive system"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The ovaries and testes, the gonads, are mixed organs. They make the sex cells for reproduction, and they are also endocrine glands that secrete hormones into the blood: estrogen and progesterone from the ovaries, and testosterone from the testes.","notes":["Lymphatic organs include the thymus, spleen, and lymph nodes. Pick this group for organs that house immune cells.","The urinary system includes the kidneys, ureters, bladder, and urethra. The gonads are nearby in the pelvis but belong to the reproductive system.","Correct. The gonads secrete sex hormones into the blood.","Digestive organs include the stomach and intestines, which do contain scattered endocrine cells. The gonads, though, are not digestive organs."],"lt":"t-endocrine","lid":"c23","comp":132,"competencyId":"w6-endocrine-tissue-organs"},{"id":"L01175","dok":1,"q":"Which organ contains hormone-producing cells in the wall of its atria?","a":"The heart.","options":["The kidneys","The heart","The skin","The placenta"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Many organs whose main job is something else still contain endocrine tissue. The heart is one of them: cells in the wall of its atria release a hormone into the blood. Atria are the two upper chambers of the heart, so the clue in the question points straight to it.","notes":["The kidneys do contain endocrine cells, in the renal cortex, including the juxtaglomerular cells. Kidneys have no atria.","Correct. Cardiac muscle cells in the atrial walls of the heart release a hormone.","The skin's endocrine cells are keratinocytes of the epidermis, which begin making a hormone in sunlight. Skin has no atria.","The placenta is a temporary organ of pregnancy that contains endocrine cells. It has no atria."],"lt":"t-endocrine","lid":"c24","comp":132,"competencyId":"w6-endocrine-tissue-organs"},{"id":"L01176","dok":1,"q":"Which structure is the saddle-shaped pocket of the sphenoid bone that cradles and protects the pituitary gland?","a":"The sella turcica.","options":["The foramen magnum","The sella turcica","The infundibulum","The third ventricle"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The sella turcica, Latin for Turkish saddle, is a depression on the superior surface of the sphenoid bone. The pituitary gland sits in it, protected by bone below, in front, and behind. That bony enclosure is why a pituitary tumor tends to grow upward toward the optic chiasm.","notes":["The foramen magnum is the large opening in the occipital bone where the spinal cord joins the brainstem. It does not hold the pituitary.","Correct. The sella turcica is the bony seat of the pituitary.","The infundibulum is the soft stalk connecting the pituitary to the hypothalamus. Pick it for the stalk, not the bony pocket.","The third ventricle is a fluid-filled space in the diencephalon, above the pituitary. It would be the answer for the space bordered by the hypothalamus and thalamus."],"lt":"t-endocrine","lid":"c25","comp":129,"competencyId":"w6-pituitary"},{"id":"L01177","dok":1,"q":"Which organs have scattered endocrine cells in the lining of the digestive tract?","a":"The stomach and small intestine.","options":["Adipose tissue","The stomach and small intestine","The thymus","The kidneys"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The stomach and small intestine are organs of digestion, but their lining contains scattered endocrine cells. These cells release hormones into the blood that help coordinate digestion. They are an example of endocrine tissue inside an organ whose main job is something else.","notes":["Adipose tissue does contain endocrine cells (the adipocytes), but it is fat tissue, not the lining of the digestive tract.","Correct. Enteroendocrine cells are scattered through the mucosa of the stomach and small intestine.","The thymus is in the mediastinum behind the sternum and is not part of the digestive tract.","The kidneys contain endocrine cells in the renal cortex, but they are urinary organs, not part of the digestive tract."],"lt":"t-endocrine","lid":"c26","comp":132,"competencyId":"w6-endocrine-tissue-organs"},{"id":"L01178","dok":2,"q":"A gland lies in the sella turcica of the sphenoid bone, hanging from a stalk below the hypothalamus. Which gland is it?","a":"The pituitary gland.","options":["The pineal gland","The pituitary gland","The hypothalamus","The thyroid gland"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The pituitary gland is identified by its position. It sits in the sella turcica of the sphenoid bone and hangs from the hypothalamus by a stalk called the infundibulum. No other endocrine gland lies in the skull base, so the sella turcica alone points to the pituitary.","notes":["The pineal gland is in the brain too, but it sits in the posterior roof of the third ventricle, not in the sphenoid bone. Pick it for that location.","Correct. The sella turcica and the stalk below the hypothalamus identify the pituitary.","The hypothalamus is the structure above, from which the gland hangs. It forms the floor of the third ventricle and is not in the sella turcica.","The thyroid is in the anterior neck, around the trachea. It would be the answer for a gland with two lobes joined by an isthmus."],"lt":"t-endocrine","lid":"c27","comp":129,"competencyId":"w6-pituitary"},{"id":"L01179","dok":2,"q":"A gland wraps around the trachea just below the larynx and has two lateral lobes joined across the front. Which gland is described?","a":"The thyroid gland.","options":["The adrenal glands","The parathyroid glands","The thymus","The thyroid gland"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The thyroid gland sits in the anterior neck, just below the larynx. Its two lateral lobes lie against the trachea, and the isthmus joins them across the front. The parathyroid glands are on its posterior surface, and the thymus is lower down, in the chest.","notes":["The adrenal glands are in the abdomen, on top of the kidneys, not in the neck.","The parathyroids are small glands on the back of the thyroid, not two large lobes beside the trachea.","The thymus is lower, in the mediastinum behind the sternum, not wrapped around the trachea below the larynx.","Correct. The lobes beside the trachea and the isthmus across the front identify the thyroid."],"lt":"t-endocrine","lid":"c28","comp":128,"competencyId":"w6-endocrine-locate"},{"id":"L01180","dok":2,"q":"A pituitary lobe is made of glandular epithelial tissue and is linked to the hypothalamus by a set of blood vessels. Which lobe is it?","a":"The anterior lobe.","options":["The posterior lobe","The infundibulum","The anterior lobe","The neurohypophysis"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The anterior lobe, or adenohypophysis, is glandular epithelial tissue. Because it is not nervous tissue, the hypothalamus cannot reach it with nerve fibers; it reaches it through blood, using the hypophyseal portal system. The tissue type and the type of link always go together.","notes":["The posterior lobe is nervous tissue linked by a tract of nerve fibers. Pick it for the lobe that is an extension of the hypothalamus.","The infundibulum is the stalk joining the pituitary to the hypothalamus, not a lobe. It would be the answer for the connecting stalk.","Correct. The glandular anterior lobe connects to the hypothalamus through portal blood vessels.","The neurohypophysis is another name for the posterior lobe. Its name points to nervous tissue, the opposite of glandular epithelium."],"lt":"t-endocrine","lid":"c29","comp":129,"competencyId":"w6-pituitary"},{"id":"L01181","dok":2,"q":"An adrenal cortex zone is the thick middle zone, with its cells arranged in straight columns. Which zone is it?","a":"The zona fasciculata.","options":["Adrenal medulla","Zona reticularis","Zona fasciculata","Zona glomerulosa"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The adrenal cortex has three zones. The zona fasciculata is the middle zone and the widest of the three, and its cells line up in long straight columns, which is what fasciculata (bundled) describes. It sits between the thin outer zona glomerulosa and the inner zona reticularis.","notes":["The medulla is the inner core of modified nervous tissue, not a zone of the cortex. Pick it for the part made of chromaffin cells.","The zona reticularis is the inner zone, a branching network against the medulla. It would be the answer for the deepest zone.","Correct. Fasciculata means bundled, describing its straight columns of cells.","The zona glomerulosa is the thin outer zone just beneath the capsule. Pick it for the most superficial zone."],"lt":"t-endocrine","lid":"c30","comp":131,"competencyId":"w6-adrenal"},{"id":"L01182","dok":2,"q":"The pineal gland belongs to which part of the diencephalon?","a":"The epithalamus.","options":["The thalamus","The midbrain","The epithalamus","The hypothalamus"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The diencephalon has three parts named by position: the thalamus in the middle, the hypothalamus below (hypo means under), and the epithalamus above and behind (epi means upon). The pineal gland projects from the posterior roof of the third ventricle as part of the epithalamus.","notes":["The thalamus is the large central mass of the diencephalon that relays sensory information to the cerebrum. It forms the upper walls of the third ventricle but does not include the pineal gland.","The midbrain is the top part of the brainstem, below the diencephalon. It would be the answer for structures such as the cerebral peduncles, not the pineal gland.","Correct. The pineal gland is part of the epithalamus, the roof region of the diencephalon.","The hypothalamus is the floor region of the diencephalon, below the third ventricle. Pick it for the structure that directs the pituitary."],"lt":"t-endocrine","lid":"c31","comp":128,"competencyId":"w6-endocrine-locate"},{"id":"L01183","dok":2,"q":"A part of the adrenal gland is modified nervous tissue forming the inner core. Which part is it?","a":"The adrenal medulla.","options":["The adrenal cortex","The adrenal medulla","The adrenal capsule","The zona glomerulosa"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Each adrenal gland is two glands in one. The outer adrenal cortex is glandular epithelial tissue arranged in three zones, and the inner adrenal medulla is modified nervous tissue belonging to the sympathetic nervous system. The medulla is made of chromaffin cells, large cells in clusters around blood vessels that are modified sympathetic neurons.","notes":["The cortex is the outer region, made of glandular tissue in three zones. Pick it for the bulk of the gland.","Correct. The medulla is the nervous tissue core of the adrenal gland.","The capsule is the connective tissue covering around the outside of the gland. It would be the answer for the outer wrapping.","The zona glomerulosa is the thin outer zone of the cortex, just beneath the capsule. It is glandular tissue at the surface, the opposite of the core."],"lt":"t-endocrine","lid":"c32","comp":131,"competencyId":"w6-adrenal"},{"id":"L01184","dok":2,"q":"A pituitary lobe is nervous tissue connected to the hypothalamus by a tract of nerve fibers running down through the infundibulum. Which lobe is it?","a":"The posterior lobe (neurohypophysis).","options":["The infundibulum","The adenohypophysis","The anterior lobe","The posterior lobe"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The posterior lobe, or neurohypophysis, is nervous tissue that extends down from the hypothalamus. Axons of hypothalamic neurons form a tract that runs through the infundibulum and ends in the posterior lobe, where their hormones are stored and released. Nervous tissue goes with a nerve connection, and glandular tissue goes with a blood connection.","notes":["The infundibulum is the stalk that the nerve tract runs through on its way down. It is the route of the tract, not the lobe where the fibers end; pick it for the pituitary stalk.","The adenohypophysis is another name for the anterior lobe. Its prefix adeno, meaning gland, signals glandular tissue rather than nervous tissue.","The anterior lobe is glandular epithelium linked by portal blood vessels. Pick it for the lobe served by the hypophyseal portal system.","Correct. The nervous posterior lobe is linked by a nerve fiber tract."],"lt":"t-endocrine","lid":"c33","comp":129,"competencyId":"w6-pituitary"},{"id":"L01185","dok":2,"q":"Small round glands, usually four of them, sit on the posterior surface of a larger neck gland. Which glands are these?","a":"The parathyroid glands.","options":["The pancreatic islets","The adrenal glands","The thyroid follicles","The parathyroid glands"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The parathyroid glands are usually four small, round glands on the posterior surface of the thyroid lobes, two on each side, each in its own thin capsule. Their main hormone-making cells are chief cells. Their position on the back of the thyroid is why surgeons must take care to spare them during thyroid surgery.","notes":["The pancreatic islets are microscopic clusters scattered through the pancreas in the abdomen, not glands in the neck.","The adrenal glands are two glands on top of the kidneys in the abdomen, not four glands in the neck.","Thyroid follicles are microscopic hollow spheres inside the thyroid, not separate glands on its surface.","Correct. The parathyroids sit on the back of the thyroid lobes."],"lt":"t-endocrine","lid":"c34","comp":128,"competencyId":"w6-endocrine-locate"},{"id":"L01186","dok":2,"q":"An adrenal cortex zone is made of cells arranged in a branching network. Which zone is it?","a":"The zona reticularis.","options":["Zona glomerulosa","Zona fasciculata","Adrenal medulla","Zona reticularis"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The names of the three cortical zones describe how their cells are arranged. The zona glomerulosa has rounded clusters, the zona fasciculata has straight columns, and the zona reticularis has a branching network (reticulum means little net). The zona reticularis is also the deepest zone, lying against the medulla.","notes":["The zona glomerulosa is the thin outer zone, with cells in rounded clusters. Pick it for the zone just beneath the capsule.","The zona fasciculata has cells in straight columns, not a network. It would be the answer for the thick middle zone.","The medulla is the core of modified nervous tissue, not a zone of the cortex. Pick it for the part made of chromaffin cells.","Correct. Reticularis means network, describing its branching cords of cells."],"lt":"t-endocrine","lid":"c35","comp":131,"competencyId":"w6-adrenal"},{"id":"L01187","dok":2,"q":"A soft gland lies behind the stomach and does both an exocrine and an endocrine job. Which organ is it?","a":"The pancreas.","options":["The pancreas","The thymus","The liver","The adrenal gland"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The pancreas is a soft, elongated gland that lies behind the stomach. It is a mixed organ: most of it is exocrine acini, which make digestive enzymes and send them through ducts, and scattered among them are the endocrine pancreatic islets, which release hormones into the blood.","notes":["Correct. The pancreas lies behind the stomach and has both exocrine acini and endocrine islets.","The thymus is in the mediastinum behind the sternum, not in the abdomen. Pick it for the organ that shrinks after puberty.","The liver is a large organ in the upper right abdomen, under the diaphragm, not tucked behind the stomach. Pick it for the organ that makes bile.","The adrenal gland caps the kidney and is purely endocrine. It would be the answer for a gland with a cortex and a medulla."],"lt":"t-endocrine","lid":"c36","comp":132,"competencyId":"w6-endocrine-tissue-organs"},{"id":"L01188","dok":2,"q":"Which paired endocrine organs lie in the female pelvis, one on each side of the uterus?","a":"The ovaries.","options":["The ovaries","The testes","The placenta","The adrenal glands"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The ovaries are the paired female gonads. They lie in the female pelvis, one on each side of the uterus. Like the testes, they are mixed organs, producing sex cells and also acting as endocrine glands that secrete estrogen and progesterone.","notes":["Correct. The ovaries are the female gonads, beside the uterus.","The testes are the male gonads, located in the scrotum. Pick them if the question asked about the male gonads.","The placenta is a single temporary organ of pregnancy attached to the uterine wall, not a pair beside the uterus. It would be the answer for the organ that releases hormones supporting pregnancy.","The adrenal glands are paired too, but they cap the kidneys in the upper abdomen. Pick them for the suprarenal glands."],"lt":"t-endocrine","lid":"c37","comp":132,"competencyId":"w6-endocrine-tissue-organs"},{"id":"L01189","dok":2,"q":"Which temporary organ of pregnancy releases hormones that support the pregnancy?","a":"The placenta.","options":["The placenta","Adipose tissue","The uterus","The ovaries"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The placenta is a temporary organ that forms during pregnancy, attached to the uterine wall. Besides exchanging materials between mother and fetus, it contains endocrine tissue that releases hormones supporting the pregnancy. It is delivered after the baby, which is why it is called temporary.","notes":["Correct. The placenta exists only during pregnancy and contains endocrine cells.","Adipose tissue does contain endocrine cells (the adipocytes), but it is a permanent tissue found all over the body, not a temporary organ of pregnancy.","The uterus is the permanent muscular organ where the fetus develops. The placenta attaches to its wall, but the uterus itself is not the temporary organ.","The ovaries are permanent paired gonads in the pelvis, present long before and after any pregnancy."],"lt":"t-endocrine","lid":"c38","comp":132,"competencyId":"w6-endocrine-tissue-organs"},{"id":"L01190","dok":2,"q":"Which sequence orders these endocrine glands from most superior to most inferior in the body: adrenal gland, thyroid gland, pineal gland?","a":"Pineal gland, thyroid gland, adrenal gland.","options":["Adrenal, thyroid, pineal","Pineal, thyroid, adrenal","Thyroid, pineal, adrenal","Pineal, adrenal, thyroid"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Order these glands by body region. The pineal gland is in the brain, in the roof of the third ventricle, so it is the most superior. The thyroid is in the anterior neck, below the larynx. The adrenal glands cap the kidneys in the abdomen, so they are the most inferior of the three.","notes":["This is the correct order run backward, from inferior to superior. It would be right if the question asked for the lowest gland first.","Correct. Brain, then neck, then abdomen.","This places the thyroid above the pineal gland. The pineal gland is inside the brain, so it is higher than any gland in the neck.","This places the adrenal gland above the thyroid. The adrenals sit on the kidneys in the abdomen, well below the neck."],"lt":"t-endocrine","lid":"c39","comp":128,"competencyId":"w6-endocrine-locate"},{"id":"L01191","dok":2,"q":"Which sequence orders the three zones of the adrenal cortex from the most superficial, just under the capsule, to the deepest, against the medulla?","a":"Zona glomerulosa, zona fasciculata, zona reticularis.","options":["Glomerulosa, reticularis, fasciculata","Fasciculata, glomerulosa, reticularis","Glomerulosa, fasciculata, reticularis","Reticularis, fasciculata, glomerulosa"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The adrenal cortex has three zones in order from the surface inward. The zona glomerulosa is a thin outer layer just under the capsule. The zona fasciculata is the thick middle zone, the widest of the three. The zona reticularis is the inner zone, a branching network of cells against the medulla.","notes":["This swaps the last two zones. The reticularis is the deepest zone, against the medulla, so it must come last.","This swaps the first two zones. The thin glomerulosa lies just under the capsule, above the thick fasciculata.","Correct. Outer, middle, and inner zones in order.","This is the correct order run backward, from deep to superficial. It would be right if the question started at the medulla."],"lt":"t-endocrine","lid":"c40","comp":131,"competencyId":"w6-adrenal"},{"id":"L01192","dok":2,"q":"An endocrine organ in the mediastinum behind the sternum is large in childhood and shrinks after puberty. Which organ is it?","a":"The thymus.","options":["The pineal gland","The thyroid gland","The thymus","The parathyroid glands"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The thymus lies in the mediastinum, behind the sternum. It is large and active in childhood, when T cells are maturing, and it shrinks after puberty as much of it is replaced by fat. It belongs to both the lymphatic and the endocrine systems.","notes":["The pineal gland is in the brain, in the roof of the third ventricle. It would be the answer for the gland of the epithalamus.","The thyroid is in the anterior neck, around the trachea, above the mediastinum. Pick it for the largest purely endocrine gland.","Correct. The thymus is large in children and shrinks after puberty.","The parathyroids are small glands on the back of the thyroid, in the neck. Pick them for the glands with chief cells."],"lt":"t-endocrine","lid":"c41","comp":128,"competencyId":"w6-endocrine-locate"},{"id":"L01193","dok":1,"q":"The adrenal glands are also called by which other name?","a":"The suprarenal glands.","options":["The suprarenal glands","The hypophysis","The pancreatic islets","The parathyroid glands"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The adrenal glands are also called the suprarenal glands. Both names describe location: ad-renal means toward the kidney and supra-renal means above the kidney. Each gland caps the superior pole of a kidney and has an outer cortex and an inner medulla.","notes":["Correct. Supra means above and renal means kidney, describing where they sit.","The hypophysis is another name for the pituitary gland. It would be the answer for the gland in the sella turcica.","The pancreatic islets are the endocrine clusters of the pancreas, also called islets of Langerhans. They are not on the kidneys.","The parathyroids are the small glands on the back of the thyroid. Their name means beside the thyroid, so pick them for that location."],"lt":"t-endocrine","lid":"c42","comp":128,"competencyId":"w6-endocrine-locate"},{"id":"L01195","dok":2,"q":"Which sequence orders these structures of the pituitary region from superior to inferior: anterior pituitary lobe, hypothalamus, infundibulum?","a":"Hypothalamus, infundibulum, anterior pituitary lobe.","options":["Hypothalamus, infundibulum, anterior pituitary lobe","Infundibulum, hypothalamus, anterior pituitary lobe","Anterior pituitary lobe, infundibulum, hypothalamus","Hypothalamus, anterior pituitary lobe, infundibulum"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The hypothalamus forms the floor of the third ventricle. The infundibulum is a slender stalk that hangs down from it, and the pituitary gland, with its anterior and posterior lobes, hangs from the bottom of the stalk in the sella turcica. So from top to bottom the order is hypothalamus, infundibulum, pituitary.","notes":["Correct. The gland hangs from the hypothalamus by the stalk.","This places the stalk above the hypothalamus. The infundibulum hangs down from the hypothalamus, so it must come second.","This is the correct order run backward, from inferior to superior. It would be right if the question started at the gland.","This places the gland above its own stalk. The infundibulum connects the two, so it lies between them."],"lt":"t-endocrine","lid":"c44","comp":129,"competencyId":"w6-pituitary"},{"id":"L01196","dok":2,"q":"Which organ is not an endocrine gland, but has cells in its epidermis that begin making a hormone when exposed to sunlight?","a":"The skin.","options":["Adipose tissue","The skin","The liver","The kidneys"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The skin is one of several organs whose main job is something else but that still contain endocrine cells. Its endocrine cells are keratinocytes, the main cells of the epidermis, and they begin making a hormone when the skin is exposed to sunlight. Only the skin has an epidermis, so that clue points straight to it.","notes":["Adipose tissue has endocrine cells too, but they are the adipocytes (fat cells), not cells of an epidermis.","Correct. Keratinocytes of the epidermis begin making a hormone in sunlight.","The liver's endocrine cells are its hepatocytes. The liver has no epidermis.","The kidneys' endocrine cells are in the renal cortex, including the juxtaglomerular cells. The kidneys have no epidermis."],"lt":"t-endocrine","lid":"c45","comp":132,"competencyId":"w6-endocrine-tissue-organs"},{"id":"L01197","dok":1,"q":"What is an endocrine gland?","a":"A ductless gland that secretes its product directly into the surrounding fluid and blood.","options":["A cell that carries the receptor for a hormone","A chemical messenger carried in the blood","A ductless gland that secretes into the blood","A gland that secretes through a duct onto a surface"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"An endocrine gland is a ductless gland. It secretes its product, a hormone, straight into the tissue fluid around it, and from there the hormone enters the blood. Exocrine glands are the contrast, because they use ducts to deliver their products onto a surface.","notes":["This defines a target cell, the cell that responds to a hormone. It receives a signal rather than making one.","This defines a hormone, the product of an endocrine gland. The gland is the organ that makes it.","Correct. No duct is involved; the blood carries the product away.","This defines an exocrine gland, such as a sweat gland or the pancreatic acini. Pick it if the question asked about exocrine glands."],"lt":"t-endocrine","lid":"c1-x2","comp":128,"competencyId":"w6-endocrine-locate"},{"id":"L01198","dok":2,"q":"Why can a clinician feel the thyroid gland during a neck exam, and why is an enlarged thyroid often visible?","a":"It lies just under the skin of the anterior neck.","options":["It rests on the superior pole of the kidney","It lies just under the skin of the anterior neck","It lies deep in the mediastinum behind the sternum","It sits inside a bony pocket of the sphenoid bone"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The thyroid gland sits in the anterior neck, just below the larynx and just under the skin. Because it is so superficial, a clinician can palpate it, and an enlarged thyroid, called a goiter, can often be seen as a swelling at the front of the neck. The same position makes thyroid surgery accessible.","notes":["This is the position of the adrenal gland, deep in the abdomen. Pick it for the gland often found by chance on kidney imaging.","Correct. Its superficial position makes it easy to see and palpate.","This is the position of the thymus, which is hidden behind bone. A structure in that location could not be felt in a neck exam.","This is the position of the pituitary gland, deep in the skull. It would be the answer for the gland whose tumors press on the optic chiasm."],"lt":"t-endocrine","lid":"c3-x2","comp":128,"competencyId":"w6-endocrine-locate"},{"id":"L01199","dok":1,"q":"Where are the adrenal glands located?","a":"Capping the superior pole of each kidney.","options":["On the back of the thyroid","Behind the stomach","In the sella turcica","Capping the superior pole of each kidney"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The adrenal glands are paired glands, one capping the superior pole of each kidney. That position is why they are also called the suprarenal glands. Each has an outer cortex of glandular tissue and an inner medulla of modified nervous tissue.","notes":["The back of the thyroid is in the neck, where the parathyroid glands sit. The adrenal glands are in the abdomen.","Behind the stomach is where the pancreas lies. The adrenal glands sit higher and farther back, on the kidneys.","The sella turcica is in the skull and holds the pituitary gland, not the adrenal glands.","Correct. Each adrenal gland sits on top of a kidney."],"lt":"t-endocrine","lid":"c4-x2","comp":128,"competencyId":"w6-endocrine-locate"},{"id":"L01200","dok":1,"q":"What is the slender stalk connecting the pituitary gland to the hypothalamus called?","a":"The infundibulum.","options":["The infundibulum","The sella turcica","The isthmus","The colloid"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The infundibulum, a name meaning funnel, is the slender stalk that connects the pituitary gland to the hypothalamus. A nerve fiber tract runs through it to the posterior lobe, and portal blood vessels run along it to the anterior lobe.","notes":["Correct. The infundibulum is the pituitary stalk.","The sella turcica is the bony saddle that holds the pituitary. It would be the answer for the pocket of the sphenoid bone.","The isthmus is the bridge of tissue joining the two thyroid lobes. Pick it for the thyroid.","Colloid is the fluid inside thyroid follicles. Pick it for the stored product of the thyroid."],"lt":"t-endocrine","lid":"c6-x2","comp":129,"competencyId":"w6-pituitary"},{"id":"L01201","dok":1,"q":"What is the posterior lobe of the pituitary gland also called?","a":"The neurohypophysis.","options":["The neurohypophysis","The infundibulum","The epithalamus","The adenohypophysis"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The posterior lobe of the pituitary is called the neurohypophysis because it is made of nervous tissue, an extension of the hypothalamus. The anterior lobe is the adenohypophysis, from adeno meaning gland. Together the two lobes make up the hypophysis, the pituitary gland.","notes":["Correct. Neuro points to the nervous tissue of the posterior lobe.","The infundibulum is the stalk that connects the pituitary to the hypothalamus. It would be the answer for the stalk.","The epithalamus is the roof region of the diencephalon that contains the pineal gland. It is not part of the pituitary.","The adenohypophysis is the anterior lobe, made of glandular tissue. Pick it for the anterior lobe."],"lt":"t-endocrine","lid":"c7-x2","comp":129,"competencyId":"w6-pituitary"},{"id":"L01202","dok":1,"q":"What type of tissue makes up the posterior lobe of the pituitary gland?","a":"Nervous tissue.","options":["Nervous tissue","Glandular epithelial tissue","Dense regular connective tissue","Smooth muscle tissue"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The posterior lobe is nervous tissue. It is made largely of the axon endings of hypothalamic neurons and their supporting cells, which is why it is called the neurohypophysis and why its link to the hypothalamus is a nerve tract. The anterior lobe, by contrast, is glandular epithelium.","notes":["Correct. The posterior lobe is an extension of the hypothalamus.","Glandular epithelium makes up the anterior lobe. Pick it for the adenohypophysis.","Dense regular connective tissue forms tendons and ligaments. It would be the answer for a structure built to resist pulling in one direction.","Smooth muscle lines the walls of hollow organs and vessels. It is not found in the pituitary lobes."],"lt":"t-endocrine","lid":"c8-x2","comp":129,"competencyId":"w6-pituitary"},{"id":"L01203","dok":1,"q":"What is the bridge of thyroid tissue that connects the two thyroid lobes called?","a":"The isthmus.","options":["The follicle","The infundibulum","The colloid","The isthmus"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The isthmus is the narrow bridge of thyroid tissue that crosses the front of the trachea and connects the right and left lobes. The word isthmus means a narrow strip joining two larger parts, the same idea as a land isthmus joining two land masses.","notes":["A follicle is one microscopic hollow sphere of the thyroid. Pick it for the gland's microscopic unit.","The infundibulum is the stalk joining the pituitary to the hypothalamus. Pick it for the pituitary stalk.","Colloid is the protein-rich fluid inside each follicle. It would be the answer for the thyroid's stored product.","Correct. The isthmus crosses the front of the trachea."],"lt":"t-endocrine","lid":"c9-x2","comp":130,"competencyId":"w6-thyroid-parathyroid"},{"id":"L01204","dok":1,"q":"What is the protein-rich fluid stored in the center of each thyroid follicle called?","a":"Colloid.","options":["Cerebrospinal fluid","Plasma","Colloid","Synovial fluid"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Each thyroid follicle is a hollow sphere whose wall is made of follicular cells. The center is filled with colloid, a thick protein-rich fluid in which the thyroid stores the material it uses to make its hormones. The thyroid is unusual among endocrine glands for storing its product outside its cells like this.","notes":["Cerebrospinal fluid fills the ventricles of the brain and surrounds the CNS. It is not found in the thyroid.","Plasma is the liquid part of blood. Pick it for the fluid in which blood cells are suspended.","Correct. Colloid fills the center of each follicle.","Synovial fluid fills the cavity of a synovial joint and lubricates it. It would be the answer for joints, not glands."],"lt":"t-endocrine","lid":"c10-x2","comp":130,"competencyId":"w6-thyroid-parathyroid"},{"id":"L01205","dok":1,"q":"What are the two main regions of an adrenal gland?","a":"An outer cortex and an inner medulla.","options":["An outer cortex and an inner medulla","An anterior lobe and a posterior lobe","A head and a tail","Two lateral lobes joined by an isthmus"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"An adrenal gland has two main regions that are really two glands in one. The outer adrenal cortex is glandular tissue arranged in three zones, and the inner adrenal medulla is modified nervous tissue of the sympathetic nervous system.","notes":["Correct. The adrenal gland is a cortex wrapped around a medulla.","These are the two lobes of the pituitary gland. Pick this pair for the pituitary.","Head and tail are regions of the pancreas. It would be the answer for that organ.","This describes the thyroid gland. Pick it for the gland around the trachea."],"lt":"t-endocrine","lid":"c11-x2","comp":131,"competencyId":"w6-adrenal"},{"id":"L01206","dok":1,"q":"How many zones make up the adrenal cortex?","a":"Three.","options":["Two","Three","Five","Four"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The adrenal cortex has three zones. From the surface inward they are the zona glomerulosa, the thin outer zone; the zona fasciculata, the thick middle zone; and the zona reticularis, the inner zone against the medulla. The medulla itself is a separate region, not a fourth zone.","notes":["Two is the number of main regions of the whole adrenal gland, the cortex and the medulla. Students mix up that count with the zones of the cortex.","Correct. The zona glomerulosa, zona fasciculata, and zona reticularis.","Five comes from counting the capsule and the medulla along with the three zones, but neither one is a zone of the cortex. The capsule is the covering and the medulla is the core.","Four is the usual number of parathyroid glands. It does not describe the adrenal cortex."],"lt":"t-endocrine","lid":"c12-x2","comp":131,"competencyId":"w6-adrenal"},{"id":"L01207","dok":1,"q":"What are the exocrine clusters of the pancreas that make digestive enzymes called?","a":"The acini.","options":["The follicles","The chief cells","The pancreatic islets","The acini"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The pancreas is mostly made of acini, small clusters of exocrine cells that make digestive enzymes and release them into ducts leading to the small intestine. The acini are not endocrine tissue. The endocrine tissue is the scattered pancreatic islets.","notes":["Follicles are the hollow spheres of the thyroid gland. They would be the answer for the thyroid's microscopic units.","Chief cells are the main cells of the parathyroid glands. Pick them for the parathyroids.","The islets are the endocrine clusters of the pancreas, which release hormones into the blood. Pick them for the endocrine tissue.","Correct. The acini are the enzyme-making exocrine clusters."],"lt":"t-endocrine","lid":"c13-x2","comp":132,"competencyId":"w6-endocrine-tissue-organs"},{"id":"L01208","dok":1,"q":"What is the main hormone-producing cell of the parathyroid glands called?","a":"The chief cell.","options":["The parafollicular cell","The follicular cell","The chief cell","The acinar cell"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The parathyroid glands are usually four small glands on the posterior surface of the thyroid. Their main hormone-producing cell is the chief cell. The thyroid next door has follicular cells and parafollicular cells instead, so the cell names separate the two glands.","notes":["Parafollicular cells, or C cells, lie between thyroid follicles. They would be the answer for the cells scattered between follicles.","Follicular cells form the walls of thyroid follicles. Pick them for the thyroid.","Correct. Chief cells are the main cells of the parathyroids.","Acinar cells make up the exocrine acini of the pancreas. Pick them for the cells that make digestive enzymes."],"lt":"t-endocrine","lid":"c14-x2","comp":130,"competencyId":"w6-thyroid-parathyroid"},{"id":"L01209","dok":2,"q":"A small, cone-shaped endocrine gland sits in the posterior roof of the third ventricle. Which gland is it?","a":"The pineal gland.","options":["The pituitary gland","An adrenal gland","The thyroid gland","The pineal gland"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The pineal gland is a small, cone-shaped gland, named for its likeness to a pine cone. It sits in the posterior roof of the third ventricle as part of the epithalamus of the diencephalon. The hypothalamus lies below it, in the floor of the same ventricle.","notes":["The pituitary hangs below the brain in the sella turcica, not in the roof of a ventricle.","The adrenal glands cap the kidneys in the abdomen, nowhere near the brain.","The thyroid is in the anterior neck below the larynx, not in the brain.","Correct. The pineal gland is the cone-shaped gland in the roof of the third ventricle."],"lt":"t-endocrine","lid":"c15-x2","comp":128,"competencyId":"w6-endocrine-locate"},{"id":"L01210","dok":1,"q":"Which structure links the nervous and endocrine systems and directs the pituitary gland?","a":"The hypothalamus.","options":["The pineal gland","The thymus","The hypothalamus","The thyroid gland"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The hypothalamus is a region of the diencephalon that acts as a bridge between the nervous and endocrine systems. Its neurosecretory cells send signals to the anterior pituitary through portal blood vessels and send axons into the posterior pituitary. This control is why the hypothalamus is called a neuroendocrine organ.","notes":["The pineal gland is in the brain too, in the roof of the third ventricle, but it does not control the pituitary.","The thymus sits in the mediastinum behind the sternum. It is not part of the brain and does not control the pituitary.","Correct. The hypothalamus is the neuroendocrine link that controls the pituitary.","The thyroid is a gland in the anterior neck. It is not part of the brain and does not control the pituitary."],"lt":"t-endocrine","lid":"c16-x2","comp":128,"competencyId":"w6-endocrine-locate"},{"id":"L01211","dok":2,"q":"The hypophyseal portal system carries hypothalamic signals to which part of the pituitary gland?","a":"The anterior lobe.","options":["The sella turcica","The infundibulum only","The posterior lobe","The anterior lobe"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The hypophyseal portal system is a set of blood vessels running from capillaries in the hypothalamus down to a second capillary bed in the anterior lobe of the pituitary. It carries hypothalamic signals directly to the anterior lobe, which is glandular and cannot be reached by nerve fibers. The posterior lobe is linked by a nerve tract instead.","notes":["The sella turcica is the bony pocket around the gland, not a part of the pituitary. It would be the answer for the gland's protective seat.","The portal vessels run along the infundibulum, but their destination is the anterior lobe. The stalk is the route, not the target.","The posterior lobe receives a tract of nerve fibers, not portal vessels. Pick it for the lobe linked by axons.","Correct. The portal vessels serve the glandular anterior lobe."],"lt":"t-endocrine","lid":"c17-x2","comp":129,"competencyId":"w6-pituitary"},{"id":"L01212","dok":1,"q":"Which adrenal cortex zone lies just beneath the connective tissue capsule?","a":"The zona glomerulosa.","options":["Zona reticularis","Zona glomerulosa","Zona fasciculata","Adrenal medulla"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The adrenal gland is wrapped in a connective tissue capsule. Just beneath it is the zona glomerulosa, the thin outer zone of the cortex, whose cells form rounded clusters. Below it come the zona fasciculata and then the zona reticularis, which borders the medulla.","notes":["The zona reticularis is the deepest zone, against the medulla. It would be the answer for the zone with a branching network of cells.","Correct. The glomerulosa is the thin outer zone under the capsule.","The zona fasciculata is the thick middle zone, with cells in straight columns. Pick it for the widest zone.","The medulla is the inner core of modified nervous tissue, as far from the capsule as possible. Pick it for the part made of chromaffin cells."],"lt":"t-endocrine","lid":"c18-x2","comp":131,"competencyId":"w6-adrenal"},{"id":"L01213","dok":2,"q":"Viewing a slide of the adrenal cortex from the capsule inward, which zone do you reach immediately after the zona glomerulosa?","a":"The zona fasciculata.","options":["Zona fasciculata","Adrenal medulla","Adrenal capsule","Zona reticularis"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The adrenal cortex is layered in a fixed order from outside in: capsule, zona glomerulosa, zona fasciculata, zona reticularis, then the medulla. The zona fasciculata comes right after the glomerulosa and is the thickest of the three zones, with its cells lined up in straight columns.","notes":["Correct. The fasciculata is the middle zone, directly under the glomerulosa.","The medulla is reached only after passing all three cortical zones. It would be the answer for the inner core.","The capsule is the outer covering, found before the zona glomerulosa, not after it. Pick it for the first layer at the surface.","The zona reticularis comes after the fasciculata, as the deepest zone against the medulla. Pick it for the zone reached last."],"lt":"t-endocrine","lid":"c19-x2","comp":131,"competencyId":"w6-adrenal"},{"id":"L01214","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares the tissue of the adrenal cortex and the adrenal medulla?","a":"The cortex is glandular tissue arranged in three zones; the medulla is modified nervous tissue of the sympathetic nervous system.","options":["Both are glandular tissue arranged in zones","Both are modified nervous tissue","Cortex is modified nervous tissue; medulla is glandular tissue in three zones","Cortex is glandular in three zones; medulla is modified sympathetic nervous tissue"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The adrenal gland is two glands in one because its two regions are made of different tissues. The outer cortex is glandular tissue, organized into the zona glomerulosa, zona fasciculata, and zona reticularis. The inner medulla is modified nervous tissue belonging to the sympathetic nervous system, made of chromaffin cells.","notes":["Only the cortex is zoned glandular tissue. The medulla is modified nervous tissue with chromaffin cells.","Only the medulla is nervous tissue. The cortex is glandular, which is why the adrenal gland is described as two glands in one.","This reverses the two regions. The zones belong to the outer cortex, and the nervous tissue is in the core.","Correct. The two regions come from different tissues."],"lt":"t-endocrine","lid":"c20-x2","comp":131,"competencyId":"w6-adrenal"},{"id":"L01215","dok":2,"q":"What runs down through the infundibulum to connect the hypothalamus with the posterior lobe of the pituitary?","a":"A tract of nerve fibers (axons of hypothalamic neurons).","options":["A lymphatic duct","Portal blood vessels","Cerebrospinal fluid","A tract of nerve fibers"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The posterior lobe of the pituitary is nervous tissue, an extension of the hypothalamus. Neurons in the hypothalamus send their axons down through the infundibulum as a tract of nerve fibers, and these axons end in the posterior lobe. Hormones made in the hypothalamus travel down these axons and are stored in the posterior lobe until release.","notes":["Lymphatic ducts return lymph to the veins in the neck and chest. They play no part in connecting the hypothalamus to the pituitary.","Portal vessels link the hypothalamus to the anterior lobe, not the posterior lobe. Pick them for the hypophyseal portal system.","Cerebrospinal fluid fills the third ventricle above the pituitary but does not run down the stalk to the posterior lobe. This choice mixes up nearby space with a physical connection.","Correct. Hypothalamic axons run through the stalk and end in the posterior lobe."],"lt":"t-endocrine","lid":"c21-x2","comp":129,"competencyId":"w6-pituitary"},{"id":"L01216","dok":1,"q":"The pancreas does an endocrine job. What is its other major role?","a":"It is also an exocrine organ that makes digestive enzymes.","options":["It is an organ that makes bile","It is an exocrine organ that makes digestive enzymes","It is a lymphatic organ that filters blood","It is an organ that absorbs nutrients"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The pancreas is a mixed organ. Its endocrine part is the pancreatic islets, which release hormones into the blood. Its exocrine part, which makes up most of the organ, is the acini, clusters of cells that make digestive enzymes and send them through ducts into the small intestine.","notes":["Bile is made by the liver and stored in the gallbladder. It would be the answer for the liver.","Correct. The acini make digestive enzymes and send them through ducts.","Filtering blood as a lymphatic organ is the role of the spleen, which lies near the pancreas. Pick the spleen for that job.","Absorbing nutrients is the main job of the small intestine. Pick the small intestine for that role."],"lt":"t-endocrine","lid":"c22-x2","comp":132,"competencyId":"w6-endocrine-tissue-organs"},{"id":"L01217","dok":1,"q":"The kidneys are not endocrine glands, but they contain endocrine cells. Where in the kidney are these cells found?","a":"In the renal cortex, including the juxtaglomerular cells.","options":["In the renal cortex, including the juxtaglomerular cells","In the adrenal medulla that caps the kidney","In the renal pelvis, where urine collects","In the fibrous renal capsule around the kidney"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The kidneys are urinary organs, but they are one of several organs that also contain endocrine cells. These cells sit in the renal cortex, the outer region of the kidney, and include the juxtaglomerular cells in the walls of the afferent arterioles. The adrenal gland rests on top of the kidney, but it is a separate gland and not part of the kidney.","notes":["Correct. The kidney's endocrine cells are in the renal cortex, including the juxtaglomerular cells in the walls of the afferent arterioles.","The adrenal gland sits on the superior pole of the kidney, but it is a separate endocrine gland with its own capsule, not part of the kidney. Its medulla is modified sympathetic nervous tissue.","The renal pelvis is the funnel inside the kidney that collects urine and passes it to the ureter. It drains urine and is not where the kidney's endocrine cells are.","The renal capsule is the fibrous connective tissue covering of the kidney. It protects the organ and does not hold its endocrine cells."],"lt":"t-endocrine","lid":"c24-x2","comp":132,"competencyId":"w6-endocrine-tissue-organs"},{"id":"L01218","dok":1,"q":"What are the male gonads called?","a":"The testes.","options":["The prostate glands","The testes","The ovaries","The adrenal glands"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The testes are the paired male gonads, located in the scrotum. Like the ovaries, they are mixed organs: they produce sperm and also act as endocrine glands, secreting testosterone into the blood.","notes":["The prostate is a single accessory gland of the male reproductive system below the bladder, not a gonad. Pick it for the gland around the urethra.","Correct. The testes are the male gonads, in the scrotum.","The ovaries are the female gonads, in the pelvis beside the uterus. Pick them for the female gonads.","The adrenal glands cap the kidneys. They would be the answer for the suprarenal glands."],"lt":"t-endocrine","lid":"c25-x2","comp":132,"competencyId":"w6-endocrine-tissue-organs"},{"id":"L01219","dok":3,"q":"A pituitary tumor enlarges and presses upward on the optic chiasm, causing loss of peripheral vision. What anatomical feature forces the tumor to expand upward?","a":"The gland sits in the bony sella turcica, so the open path for growth is upward.","options":["The gland lies in the third ventricle","The gland has two lobes of different tissues","The gland hangs from the infundibulum","The gland is boxed inside the bony sella turcica"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The pituitary gland sits in the sella turcica, a pocket of the sphenoid bone with a bony floor and bony front and back walls. When a pituitary tumor grows, that bone holds it in below, in front, and behind, so it usually expands upward. Just above the pituitary lies the optic chiasm, and pressure there can cause a loss of peripheral vision.","notes":["The pituitary does not lie in the third ventricle; the third ventricle is above it, bordered by the hypothalamus. This choice confuses the pituitary's position with that of the hypothalamus.","The two lobes explain how each connects to the hypothalamus, not the direction a tumor grows. This would be the answer for why the lobes link by different routes.","The infundibulum is a soft stalk and does not restrict growth in any direction. Pick it for what connects the pituitary to the hypothalamus.","Correct. Bone surrounds the gland on most sides, so growth goes upward."],"lt":"t-endocrine","lid":"c26-x2","comp":128,"competencyId":"w6-endocrine-locate"},{"id":"L01220","dok":3,"q":"A patient develops a goiter, a swelling visible at the front of the neck. Which gland has enlarged?","a":"The thyroid gland.","options":["The thyroid gland","The thymus","The adrenal gland","The pituitary gland"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The thyroid gland lies in the anterior neck just under the skin, below the larynx and wrapped around the trachea. Because it is so superficial, an enlarged thyroid, called a goiter, shows as a visible and palpable swelling at the front of the neck.","notes":["Correct. A goiter is an enlarged thyroid.","The thymus is behind the sternum in the mediastinum, hidden by bone. It would be the answer for the organ that shrinks after puberty.","The adrenal glands sit on the kidneys deep in the abdomen. An adrenal mass is usually found on imaging, not seen.","The pituitary is deep in the skull, in the sella turcica, so its enlargement cannot be seen in the neck. Pick it for a tumor that affects vision."],"lt":"t-endocrine","lid":"c27-x2","comp":128,"competencyId":"w6-endocrine-locate"},{"id":"L01221","dok":3,"q":"An imaging study ordered to view a kidney also shows a mass sitting on top of it. Which endocrine gland was imaged by chance?","a":"The adrenal gland.","options":["The thyroid gland","The pineal gland","The adrenal gland","The thymus"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Each adrenal gland rests on the superior pole of a kidney. Because the two organs are so close, imaging ordered for the kidney usually captures the adrenal too, and an adrenal mass is sometimes discovered by accident this way.","notes":["The thyroid is in the neck, far from the kidney. Pick it for a swelling at the front of the neck.","The pineal gland is in the brain, in the roof of the third ventricle. It would be the answer for a brain image.","Correct. The adrenal gland rests on the superior pole of the kidney.","The thymus is in the mediastinum behind the sternum. Pick it for chest imaging, not kidney imaging."],"lt":"t-endocrine","lid":"c28-x2","comp":128,"competencyId":"w6-endocrine-locate"},{"id":"L01222","dok":3,"q":"During thyroid surgery, the surgeon carefully spares several tiny glands on the back surface of the thyroid. Which glands are they?","a":"The parathyroid glands.","options":["The pancreatic islets","The adrenal glands","The thyroid follicles","The parathyroid glands"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The parathyroid glands, usually four, are tiny glands on the posterior surface of the thyroid lobes, each in its own thin capsule. The thyroid's position just under the skin of the anterior neck makes surgery on it accessible, but because the parathyroids sit on its back, the surgeon must find and protect them.","notes":["The pancreatic islets are scattered inside the pancreas in the abdomen, not on the thyroid.","The adrenal glands sit on the kidneys, far from the neck.","The thyroid follicles are inside the thyroid itself, the organ being operated on. They are microscopic units, not separate glands on its surface.","Correct. The parathyroids sit on the back of the thyroid lobes."],"lt":"t-endocrine","lid":"c29-x2","comp":128,"competencyId":"w6-endocrine-locate"},{"id":"L01223","dok":2,"q":"A gland sits below the hypothalamus in a bony saddle and has one lobe of glandular epithelium and one lobe of nervous tissue. Which gland is it?","a":"The pituitary gland.","options":["The thyroid gland","The adrenal gland","The pineal gland","The pituitary gland"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The pituitary gland sits in the sella turcica of the sphenoid bone, below the hypothalamus. It has an anterior lobe of glandular epithelial tissue, the adenohypophysis, and a posterior lobe of nervous tissue, the neurohypophysis. The adrenal gland also combines glandular and nervous tissue, but as a cortex around a medulla rather than as two lobes.","notes":["The thyroid has two lobes, but both are glandular and it sits in the neck, not in a bony saddle. Pick it for the gland joined by an isthmus.","The adrenal gland also has a glandular part and a nervous part, but they are an outer cortex and inner medulla, and the gland sits on the kidney. Pick it for that layout.","The pineal gland is in the brain but sits in the roof of the third ventricle and has no lobes. It would be the answer for the cone-shaped gland of the epithalamus.","Correct. The sella turcica and the two different lobes identify the pituitary."],"lt":"t-endocrine","lid":"c30-x2","comp":129,"competencyId":"w6-pituitary"},{"id":"L01224","dok":2,"q":"Endocrine glands release their hormones into the blood rather than through ducts. Why does this suit hormones that must reach organs far from the gland?","a":"The blood circulates through the whole body, so it can carry a hormone to distant target organs.","options":["Hormones act only on the gland that makes them","The blood carries the hormone throughout the body","Hormones reach only cells touching the gland","Ducts would carry hormones to every organ faster"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"An endocrine gland has no duct. It secretes its hormone into the tissue fluid around it, the hormone enters nearby capillaries, and the circulating blood carries it to nearly every part of the body. This is how a gland in the neck or the skull can signal target cells in organs far away.","notes":["A hormone that acted only on its own gland would not need the blood at all. This misconception ignores that hormones signal distant target cells.","Correct. Circulating blood reaches nearly every tissue.","Signals limited to neighboring cells are local signals, not hormones. Hormones are defined by traveling in the blood.","Ducts deliver a product to one place, such as a surface or a cavity. That is how exocrine glands work, and it would not spread a hormone to distant organs."],"lt":"t-endocrine","lid":"d3-t-endocrine-1","comp":128,"competencyId":"w6-endocrine-locate"},{"id":"L01225","dok":2,"q":"Hormones travel in the blood to nearly every cell, yet each hormone affects only certain cells. What makes a cell a target cell for a hormone?","a":"It carries the receptor for that hormone.","options":["It has a duct leading to the bloodstream","It makes the same hormone itself","It carries the receptor for that hormone","It is the cell closest to the gland"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"A hormone in the blood passes nearly every cell, but only target cells respond to it. A target cell is one that carries the receptor for that particular hormone. Cells without the receptor are exposed to the hormone but do not react, which is how one hormone can have very specific effects.","notes":["Ducts belong to exocrine glands, and no duct leads to the blood. This choice mixes up gland structure with how cells respond.","Making a hormone describes an endocrine cell, not a target cell. A target cell responds to the hormone rather than producing it.","Correct. Only cells with the matching receptor respond.","Distance from the gland does not decide a response, because the blood carries hormones everywhere. This misconception treats hormones like local signals."],"lt":"t-endocrine","lid":"d3-t-endocrine-2","comp":128,"competencyId":"w6-endocrine-locate"},{"id":"L01227","dok":3,"q":"One part of the adrenal gland is supplied by preganglionic sympathetic nerve fibers and works like a sympathetic ganglion. Which part is it, and what explains its tie to the sympathetic system?","a":"The adrenal medulla, which is modified sympathetic nervous tissue.","options":["The adrenal medulla, modified sympathetic nervous tissue","The adrenal cortex, glandular tissue in three zones","The adrenal capsule, the connective tissue covering","The zona glomerulosa, the outer zone of the cortex"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The adrenal medulla is the inner core of the adrenal gland, and it is modified nervous tissue that develops from the neural crest, the same embryonic tissue that forms the sympathetic ganglia. Its chromaffin cells are modified sympathetic neurons with no axons or dendrites. Preganglionic sympathetic fibers reach them directly, and they release their product straight into the blood.","notes":["Correct. The medulla is modified sympathetic nervous tissue made of chromaffin cells.","The cortex is the outer region, made of glandular epithelial tissue from mesoderm in three zones. It is not nervous tissue and does not work like a ganglion.","The capsule is the connective tissue covering of the gland. It is not nervous tissue.","The zona glomerulosa is the thin outer zone of the cortex, made of glandular tissue just under the capsule. It is the part farthest from the nervous core."],"lt":"t-endocrine","lid":"d3-t-endocrine-4","comp":131,"competencyId":"w6-adrenal"},{"id":"L01228","dok":3,"q":"A blockage in the main pancreatic duct means pancreatic juice cannot reach the small intestine. Which part of the pancreas still delivers its product normally, and why?","a":"The pancreatic islets, because they are ductless and release hormones straight into the blood.","options":["The islets, because they release hormones into the blood","The acini, because they bypass the duct through capillaries","Neither part, because both depend on the duct","The acini, because they release enzymes into the blood"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The pancreas has two kinds of tissue with two delivery routes. The exocrine acini make digestive enzymes and send them through the pancreatic ducts to the small intestine, so a blocked duct stops that flow. The endocrine pancreatic islets are ductless and release hormones directly into the blood, so the blockage does not affect them.","notes":["Correct. The islets do not use ducts, so a duct blockage does not stop them.","Acini have no route around the ducts; their enzymes must travel through them. This misconception gives exocrine tissue an endocrine pathway.","Only the exocrine acini depend on the duct. The endocrine islets are ductless and keep delivering hormones to the blood.","The acini send their digestive enzymes through ducts, so they are the part blocked here. Pick the acini for the exocrine tissue affected by the obstruction."],"lt":"t-endocrine","lid":"d3-t-endocrine-5","comp":132,"competencyId":"w6-endocrine-tissue-organs"},{"id":"L01229","dok":2,"q":"Unlike most endocrine glands, the thyroid stores its product in bulk outside its cells. Where is it stored?","a":"In the colloid at the center of each thyroid follicle.","options":["In the parathyroid glands on its back surface","In the isthmus that joins the two lobes","Inside the parafollicular cells","In the colloid at the center of each follicle"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Each thyroid follicle is a hollow sphere of follicular cells surrounding a central space filled with colloid. The thyroid stores the material it uses to make its hormones in this colloid, outside the cells, which is unusual among endocrine glands. This store lets the gland hold a large reserve.","notes":["The parathyroids are separate glands with their own chief cells. They do not store thyroid products.","The isthmus is just a bridge of ordinary thyroid tissue across the trachea. It would be the answer for the part connecting the two lobes.","Parafollicular cells, or C cells, lie between follicles and are not the thyroid's storage site. Pick them for the cells scattered in the spaces between follicles.","Correct. The follicle's center is the thyroid's storage space."],"lt":"t-endocrine","lid":"d3-t-endocrine-6","comp":130,"competencyId":"w6-thyroid-parathyroid"},{"id":"L01230","dok":1,"q":"Adipose tissue is not an endocrine gland, but it contains endocrine cells. Which cells are they?","a":"Adipocytes (fat cells).","options":["Juxtaglomerular cells","Keratinocytes","Adipocytes (fat cells)","Enteroendocrine cells"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Many organs and tissues whose main job is something else still contain endocrine cells. In adipose tissue, the endocrine cells are the adipocytes, the same fat cells that store fat, so one cell type does both jobs. Other examples are the keratinocytes of the skin, the enteroendocrine cells of the stomach and small intestine, and the juxtaglomerular cells of the kidney.","notes":["Juxtaglomerular cells are endocrine cells of the kidney, in the walls of the afferent arterioles in the renal cortex. They are not part of adipose tissue.","Keratinocytes are the endocrine cells of the skin, in the epidermis, which begin making a hormone when exposed to sunlight. They are not found in adipose tissue.","Correct. In adipose tissue the endocrine cells are the adipocytes themselves.","Enteroendocrine cells are scattered in the mucosa of the stomach and small intestine. They belong to the digestive lining, not to fat tissue."],"lt":"t-endocrine","lid":"d3-t-endocrine-7","comp":132,"competencyId":"w6-endocrine-tissue-organs"},{"id":"L01231","dok":2,"q":"Which of these is a pure endocrine gland, rather than a mixed organ with another major role?","a":"The thyroid gland.","options":["The pancreas","The ovary","The thyroid gland","The testis"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Pure endocrine glands do nothing but make hormones; examples are the pituitary, thyroid, parathyroid, and adrenal glands. Mixed organs do an endocrine job alongside another role, so only part of the organ is endocrine tissue. The pancreas also makes digestive enzymes, and the gonads also make sex cells.","notes":["The pancreas is mixed, with exocrine acini that make digestive enzymes alongside its endocrine islets. Pick it for a mixed organ in the abdomen.","The ovary is mixed, producing eggs as well as hormones. It would be the answer for a female gonad.","Correct. The thyroid's whole job is making hormones.","The testis is mixed, producing sperm as well as testosterone. Pick it for a male gonad."],"lt":"t-endocrine","lid":"d3x-t-endocrine-1","comp":132,"competencyId":"w6-endocrine-tissue-organs"},{"id":"L02431","dok":2,"q":"On a kidney model, a small cap-shaped gland sits on the superior pole of each kidney. What is it?","a":"The suprarenal (adrenal) gland.","options":["The spleen","The liver","The pancreas","The suprarenal (adrenal) gland"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The suprarenal glands, also called adrenal glands, sit like caps on the superior pole of each kidney. Supra means above and renal means kidney, and ad-renal means near the kidney, so both names describe the location. They are endocrine glands, but they are identified on the kidney model in the urinary lab.","notes":["The spleen is a lymphatic organ in the upper left abdomen, beside the left kidney, not on top of either kidney. It would be the answer for the organ near the left kidney that filters blood.","The liver fills the upper right abdomen above the right kidney and is the reason the right kidney sits lower. It is a large organ, not a small cap on each kidney.","The pancreas lies across the posterior abdomen in front of the kidneys, with its tail reaching toward the spleen. Pick it if the question asked for the gland that sits behind the stomach.","Correct. The suprarenal (adrenal) gland caps the superior pole of each kidney."],"lt":"t-urinary-system","lid":"c37","comp":128,"competencyId":"w6-endocrine-locate"},{"id":"L02672","dok":2,"q":"The ovaries are listed both with the reproductive organs and with the endocrine glands. Which statement explains why?","a":"They produce oocytes and also secrete hormones.","options":["They produce oocytes and also produce milk","They house the embryo and also secrete hormones","They store sperm and also secrete hormones","They produce oocytes and also secrete hormones"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The ovaries are the female gonads, so they make the gametes (oocytes), which places them in the reproductive system. They also secrete sex hormones directly into the blood, which makes them endocrine glands as well. The testes have the same double role in males.","notes":["Milk is made by the mammary glands, not the ovaries. Pick milk production for the mammary glands.","The embryo is housed in the uterus, not the ovary. Pick this role for the uterus, which holds the developing embryo and fetus.","The ovaries do not store sperm. Sperm are stored in the male epididymis. Pick sperm storage for a question about the epididymis.","Correct. The ovaries make oocytes and secrete hormones."],"lt":"t-reproductive-female","lid":"d3-t-reproductive-female-5","comp":132,"competencyId":"w6-endocrine-tissue-organs"},{"id":"L04472","dok":2,"q":"Which is the largest purely endocrine gland in the body?","a":"The thyroid gland, in the anterior neck.","options":["The adrenal gland","The pancreas","The thyroid gland","The pituitary gland"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"A purely endocrine gland is one whose only job is to make hormones. Among those, the thyroid gland is the largest, with two lobes lying against the trachea in the anterior neck. Larger organs such as the pancreas contain endocrine tissue, but they also have other jobs, so they are not purely endocrine.","notes":["The adrenal glands are purely endocrine but much smaller than the thyroid. Pick them if the question asked which gland has an outer cortex and an inner medulla.","The pancreas is larger than the thyroid, but it is a mixed gland. Most of it is exocrine tissue that makes digestive enzymes, and only its scattered islets are endocrine, so it is not purely endocrine.","Correct. The thyroid is the largest gland whose only job is making hormones.","The pituitary is purely endocrine but only about the size of a pea. Choose it if the question asked which gland sits in the sella turcica."],"lt":"t-w6-endocrine-locate","lid":"w6-endocrine-locate-r4","comp":128,"competencyId":"w6-endocrine-locate"},{"id":"L04473","dok":3,"q":"A patient's first sign of a pituitary tumor is a loss of peripheral vision. Which fact about the gland's location best explains this?","a":"It is boxed in by the bony sella turcica, so it can only expand upward and press on the optic chiasm.","options":["Its bony pocket forces it to grow up into the optic chiasm","It sits behind the eye, pressing directly on the retina","It lies in the occipital lobe, next to the visual cortex","It sits inside the orbit, right beside the optic nerve"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The pituitary sits in the sella turcica, a pocket of the sphenoid bone with a bony floor and bony front and back walls. A growing tumor is held in by that bone, so it usually expands upward, and just above the gland lies the optic chiasm, where the optic nerves cross. Pressure on the chiasm can cause a loss of peripheral vision, and that can be the first sign the gland has enlarged.","notes":["Correct. Trapped by the sella turcica, the growing gland pushes up on the optic chiasm.","The retina lines the back of the eyeball, far from the pituitary. A problem there would point to the eye itself, not a gland in the skull base.","The occipital lobe of the cerebrum holds the visual cortex, but it is at the back of the head, far above and behind the pituitary. Pick the occipital lobe if the question asked where visual information is processed.","The pituitary lies in the middle of the cranial floor, not in the orbit. The optic nerve does run through the orbit, so this would describe a tumor of the eye socket rather than the pituitary."],"lt":"t-w6-endocrine-locate","lid":"w6-endocrine-locate-r5","comp":128,"competencyId":"w6-endocrine-locate"},{"id":"L04485","dok":1,"q":"Which slender stalk connects the pituitary gland to the hypothalamus?","a":"The infundibulum.","options":["The optic chiasm","The sella turcica","The infundibulum","The mammillary body"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The infundibulum is the slender stalk that hangs from the floor of the hypothalamus and attaches to the pituitary gland below it. Nerve fibers to the posterior lobe and the portal blood vessels to the anterior lobe both run along this stalk. The word means funnel, and the same name is used for the funnel-shaped end of the uterine tube.","notes":["The optic chiasm is where the optic nerves cross, just in front of and above the infundibulum. Choose it if the question asked what structure a pituitary tumor presses on to cause vision loss.","The sella turcica is the saddle-shaped pocket of the sphenoid bone that cradles the pituitary. It holds the gland from below rather than connecting it to the brain; pick it if the question asked what bony pocket protects the pituitary.","Correct. The infundibulum is the stalk joining the hypothalamus and the pituitary.","The mammillary bodies are two small round bumps on the underside of the hypothalamus, just behind the infundibulum. Pick them if the question asked which paired bumps lie on the floor of the hypothalamus."],"lt":"t-w6-pituitary","lid":"w6-pituitary-r1","comp":129,"competencyId":"w6-pituitary"},{"id":"L04486","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares the anterior and posterior pituitary lobes by tissue type?","a":"The anterior lobe (adenohypophysis) is glandular epithelial tissue; the posterior lobe (neurohypophysis) is nervous tissue.","options":["Both lobes are glandular epithelium, joined by a stalk of nerve fibers","Both lobes are nervous tissue that grew down from the hypothalamus","Anterior lobe is glandular epithelium; posterior lobe is nervous tissue","Anterior lobe is nervous tissue; posterior lobe is glandular epithelium"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The two lobes of the pituitary come from two different tissues. The anterior lobe, or adenohypophysis (adeno means gland), is glandular epithelial tissue. The posterior lobe, or neurohypophysis (neuro means nerve), is nervous tissue, an extension of the hypothalamus itself.","notes":["Only the anterior lobe is glandular epithelium. The posterior lobe is itself nervous tissue, continuous with the nerve fibers in the stalk.","Only the posterior lobe is an extension of the hypothalamus. The anterior lobe is glandular epithelium, which is why it needs blood vessels rather than nerve fibers to receive signals.","Correct. The adenohypophysis is glandular and the neurohypophysis is nervous tissue.","This swaps the lobes. The prefixes in the other names give it away: the neurohypophysis is the nervous lobe, and it is the posterior one."],"lt":"t-w6-pituitary","lid":"w6-pituitary-r2","comp":129,"competencyId":"w6-pituitary"},{"id":"L04487","dok":2,"q":"How does the hypothalamus connect to each lobe of the pituitary gland?","a":"To the anterior lobe by the hypophyseal portal blood vessels; to the posterior lobe by a tract of nerve fibers.","options":["Anterior lobe by portal blood vessels; posterior lobe by a nerve fiber tract","Both lobes by nerve fiber tracts running through the optic chiasm","Anterior lobe by a nerve fiber tract; posterior lobe by portal blood vessels","Both lobes by portal blood vessels running through the sella turcica"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The tissue of each lobe tells you its link. The anterior lobe is glandular epithelium, so the hypothalamus reaches it through blood vessels, the hypophyseal portal system that runs down the infundibulum. The posterior lobe is nervous tissue, so the hypothalamus reaches it through a tract of nerve fibers that runs down the same stalk.","notes":["Correct. Blood vessels serve the glandular anterior lobe, and nerve fibers serve the nervous posterior lobe.","Only the posterior lobe is linked by nerve fibers, and they run down the infundibulum, not through the optic chiasm. The optic chiasm is the crossing of the optic nerves just in front of the stalk.","This swaps the two links. The posterior lobe is the nervous lobe, so it is the one connected by nerve fibers, and the glandular anterior lobe is reached by blood vessels.","Only the anterior lobe is linked by the portal vessels, and those vessels run down the infundibulum, not through the bone of the sella turcica. Pick the portal system if the question asked how the anterior lobe receives hypothalamic signals."],"lt":"t-w6-pituitary","lid":"w6-pituitary-r3","comp":129,"competencyId":"w6-pituitary"},{"id":"L04488","dok":1,"q":"Which part of the thyroid gland is the bridge of tissue crossing the front of the trachea that connects the two lobes?","a":"The isthmus.","options":["The thyroid follicle","The infundibulum","The isthmus","The parathyroid gland"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The thyroid has two lateral lobes, one on each side of the trachea. The isthmus is the narrow band of thyroid tissue that crosses the front of the trachea and joins them, giving the gland its butterfly or H shape. The word isthmus means a narrow strip connecting two larger parts, which is why the uterine tube also has an isthmus.","notes":["A thyroid follicle is a microscopic hollow sphere of cells that stores colloid. It is far too small to bridge the two lobes; choose it if the question asked which units make up the gland under the microscope.","The infundibulum is the stalk that connects the pituitary gland to the hypothalamus. Pick it if the question asked what links the pituitary to the brain.","Correct. The isthmus is the bridge of tissue connecting the two thyroid lobes.","The parathyroid glands are tiny separate glands on the back of the thyroid lobes, not a bridge across the front. Pick them if the question asked which glands sit on the posterior thyroid surface."],"lt":"t-w6-thyroid-parathyroid","lid":"w6-thyroid-parathyroid-r1","comp":130,"competencyId":"w6-thyroid-parathyroid"},{"id":"L04489","dok":1,"q":"Where are the parathyroid glands, and how many are there usually?","a":"On the posterior surface of the thyroid lobes; usually four.","options":["On the posterior surface of the thyroid; usually four","On the anterior surface of the thyroid; usually two","On the posterior surface of the thyroid; usually two","In the mediastinum behind the sternum; usually one"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The parathyroid glands are small, round glands on the posterior surface of the thyroid lobes. Most people have four, a superior and an inferior gland on each lobe, and each has its own thin capsule. Because they are so small and sit right on the back of the thyroid, surgeons must take care to spare them during thyroid surgery.","notes":["Correct. There are usually four parathyroid glands on the back of the thyroid.","The front of the thyroid is covered by the isthmus and the lobes, not by the parathyroids. Students who choose this know the glands are tied to the thyroid but forget they sit behind it.","The location is right, but the usual number is four, two on each lobe. Picking two comes from counting one gland per lobe instead of two.","This describes the thymus, a single gland in the chest behind the sternum. Pick it if the question asked which gland is large in childhood and shrinks after puberty."],"lt":"t-w6-thyroid-parathyroid","lid":"w6-thyroid-parathyroid-r2","comp":128,"competencyId":"w6-endocrine-locate"},{"id":"L04490","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly describes thyroid follicles and colloid?","a":"Follicles are the hollow spheres that make up the gland; colloid is the protein-rich fluid stored in each follicle's center.","options":["Follicles hold developing oocytes; colloid is the fluid that fills a mature follicle","Follicles are the four small glands; colloid is the tissue joining them together","Follicles are solid cords of cells; colloid is the fluid between those cords","Follicles are hollow spheres of cells; colloid is the fluid stored in their centers"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Under the microscope, the thyroid is made of many small hollow spheres called thyroid follicles. Each follicle is a ring of follicular cells around a central space filled with colloid, a thick, protein-rich fluid where the thyroid's hormone material is stored. Parafollicular cells (C cells) sit scattered between the follicles.","notes":["This describes ovarian follicles in the ovary, which hold oocytes, and the fluid inside a mature ovarian follicle is not called colloid. The word follicle means a small sac in both organs, which is the source of the mix-up.","The four small glands are the parathyroid glands on the back of the thyroid. Follicles are microscopic units inside the thyroid, and colloid is a fluid, not a connecting tissue.","Thyroid follicles are hollow, not solid cords, and the colloid sits inside them, not between them. Packed cords of cells with no colloid describe parathyroid tissue.","Correct. Follicles are hollow spheres of cells, and colloid fills their centers."],"lt":"t-w6-thyroid-parathyroid","lid":"w6-thyroid-parathyroid-r3","comp":130,"competencyId":"w6-thyroid-parathyroid"},{"id":"L04496","dok":1,"q":"Which statement correctly names the two regions of an adrenal gland and the tissue of each?","a":"An outer cortex of glandular tissue and an inner medulla of modified nervous tissue.","options":["Outer cortex and inner medulla, both of glandular tissue","Outer cortex of glandular tissue; inner medulla of nervous tissue","Outer cortex of nervous tissue; inner medulla of glandular tissue","Outer medulla of nervous tissue; inner cortex of glandular tissue"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Each adrenal gland is really two glands in one. The outer adrenal cortex forms most of the gland and is glandular tissue arranged in three zones. The inner adrenal medulla is modified nervous tissue that belongs to the sympathetic nervous system, so it is built from a different tissue than the cortex around it.","notes":["Only the cortex is glandular tissue. The medulla is modified nervous tissue, which is why the adrenal is described as two glands in one.","Correct. The cortex is outer glandular tissue, and the medulla is inner modified nervous tissue.","The positions are right, but the tissues are swapped. The medulla is the part that belongs to the sympathetic nervous system, so it is the nervous tissue.","This puts the medulla on the outside. As in the kidney, cortex means the outer layer and medulla means the inner core."],"lt":"t-w6-adrenal","lid":"w6-adrenal-r1","comp":131,"competencyId":"w6-adrenal"},{"id":"L04497","dok":1,"q":"Which list names only organs whose main job is not endocrine but that still contain endocrine tissue?","a":"The heart, the kidneys, and the stomach (also the small intestine, liver, skin, adipose tissue, and placenta).","options":["Heart, thyroid, kidneys","Thyroid, adrenal glands, pituitary","Pineal gland, parathyroids, skin","Heart, kidneys, stomach"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Many organs whose main job is something else still contain endocrine cells. The heart has hormone-making cells in the muscle of its atrial walls, the kidneys have endocrine cells in the renal cortex, and the stomach and small intestine have enteroendocrine cells scattered in their mucosa. The liver, skin, adipose tissue, and placenta also belong to this group.","notes":["The heart and kidneys fit, but the thyroid is a purely endocrine gland, the largest one in the body. One pure gland in the list makes it wrong.","These are all pure endocrine glands, whose only job is making hormones. Pick this list if the question asked for glands that do nothing but make hormones.","The skin fits, but the pineal gland and parathyroid glands are purely endocrine glands. Choose them if the question asked for small glands whose only job is making hormones.","Correct. The heart, kidneys, and stomach each have other main jobs but contain endocrine tissue."],"lt":"t-w6-endocrine-tissue-organs","lid":"w6-endocrine-tissue-organs-r1","comp":132,"competencyId":"w6-endocrine-tissue-organs"},{"id":"N-a17d-129-1","dok":3,"q":"A pituitary tumor grows upward out of the sella turcica. Which structure, lying just above and in front of the gland, is it most likely to press on?","a":"The optic chiasm.","options":["The hypophyseal portal veins.","The optic chiasm.","The body of the sphenoid bone.","The nasal cavity."],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The pituitary sits in the sella turcica, a saddle-shaped pocket of the sphenoid bone. The bone forms the floor and the front and back walls of the pocket, so the open side faces up. The optic chiasm lies just above and in front of the gland. A tumor that grows up out of the pocket meets the optic chiasm first.","notes":["The portal veins run down along the infundibulum to the anterior lobe. They are part of the gland's own connection, not the structure above and in front of it.","Correct. The optic chiasm lies just above and in front of the pituitary, so an upward-growing tumor reaches it.","The sphenoid bone forms the floor and walls of the sella turcica, below and around the gland. A tumor growing upward moves away from it.","The nasal cavity lies below the sphenoid bone, whose body forms the floor of the sella turcica (the sphenoid also helps form the roof of the nasal cavity). A tumor growing upward moves away from it."],"comp":129,"competencyId":"w6-pituitary"},{"id":"N-a17d-129-2","dok":3,"q":"On a pituitary slide, one lobe looks pale and fibrous, with scattered nuclei of glial cells among many axons. Which lobe is it, and how is it linked to the hypothalamus?","a":"The posterior lobe, linked by the hypothalamo-hypophyseal tract.","options":["The posterior lobe, linked by the hypophyseal portal system.","The anterior lobe, linked by the hypothalamo-hypophyseal tract.","The anterior lobe, linked by the hypophyseal portal system.","The posterior lobe, linked by the hypothalamo-hypophyseal tract."],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The slide tells you the tissue type. Pale, fibrous-looking tissue of axons with scattered pituicyte nuclei is nervous tissue, so it is the posterior lobe (neurohypophysis), an extension of the hypothalamus. Because it is nervous tissue, it connects by nerve fibers, the hypothalamo-hypophyseal tract, which runs down through the infundibulum. The anterior lobe is glandular epithelium linked by the hypophyseal portal system.","notes":["The lobe is right, but the portal system links the hypothalamus to the anterior lobe. The posterior lobe connects by nerve fibers.","The tract runs to the posterior lobe, and the anterior lobe on a slide is packed gland cells, not pale fibrous tissue.","The anterior lobe is glandular epithelium, densely packed gland cells in cords, and it is linked by blood vessels. The pale, fibrous lobe is the posterior lobe.","Correct. Pale, fibrous tissue of axons and pituicytes is the posterior lobe, linked to the hypothalamus by nerve fibers through the infundibulum."],"comp":129,"competencyId":"w6-pituitary"},{"id":"N-a17d-129-3","dok":3,"q":"A head injury tears the infundibulum completely. Which links between the hypothalamus and the pituitary are cut?","a":"Both the nerve tract to the posterior lobe and the portal veins to the anterior lobe.","options":["Both the nerve tract to the posterior lobe and the portal veins to the anterior lobe.","Only the nerve tract to the posterior lobe; the anterior lobe has no link there.","Neither link, since both lobes connect through the sella turcica instead.","Only the portal veins to the anterior lobe; the posterior lobe links elsewhere."],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The infundibulum is the stalk that hangs the pituitary from the hypothalamus. The posterior lobe connects by nerve fibers, the hypothalamo-hypophyseal tract, which runs down through the stalk. The anterior lobe connects by blood vessels: the hypophyseal portal veins carry blood down along the stalk from the primary capillary plexus to the secondary plexus in the anterior lobe. A complete tear of the stalk cuts both links.","notes":["Correct. The tract runs down through the infundibulum and the portal veins run down along it, so tearing the stalk cuts both.","The anterior lobe is linked by the hypophyseal portal veins, which run down along the infundibulum, so they are torn as well.","The sella turcica is the bony pocket that holds the gland. The links to the hypothalamus run through and along the infundibulum.","The posterior lobe is linked by the hypothalamo-hypophyseal tract, which runs through the infundibulum, so it is torn as well."],"comp":129,"competencyId":"w6-pituitary"},{"id":"N-a17d-130-1","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares the chief cells and the oxyphil cells of the parathyroid glands?","a":"Chief cells are small and numerous; oxyphil cells are larger, fewer, and stain dark pink.","options":["Chief cells are larger and stain dark pink; oxyphil cells are small and make up most of the gland.","Chief cells and oxyphil cells look alike, but only oxyphil cells make the gland's hormone.","Chief cells form the walls of follicles; oxyphil cells sit in the colloid at the center.","Chief cells are small and numerous; oxyphil cells are larger, fewer, and stain dark pink."],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Parathyroid tissue is packed cords and clusters of cells with capillaries between them. Chief cells (principal cells) are the small, numerous cells with round nuclei and pale cytoplasm that make up most of the gland, and they are its hormone-making cells. Oxyphil cells are larger and much fewer, and their cytoplasm is packed with mitochondria, so it stains dark pink. Oxyphil cells become more common with age.","notes":["This swaps the two. The small, numerous cells are chief cells, and the larger dark pink cells are oxyphil cells.","The two look different on a slide, and the chief cells are the hormone-making cells. The function of oxyphil cells is not fully understood.","Follicles and colloid belong to the thyroid. Parathyroid tissue has no follicles and no colloid, only packed cords of cells.","Correct. Chief cells make up most of the gland, while oxyphil cells are larger, fewer, and packed with mitochondria that stain dark pink."],"comp":130,"competencyId":"w6-thyroid-parathyroid"},{"id":"N-a17d-130-2","dok":3,"q":"A slide shows packed cords of small cells with capillaries between them, no follicles, and no colloid. A few larger cells with dark pink, granular cytoplasm are scattered among them. Which gland is this, and what are the larger cells?","a":"The parathyroid gland; the larger cells are oxyphil cells.","options":["The parathyroid gland; the larger cells are oxyphil cells.","The parathyroid gland; the larger cells are chief cells.","The thyroid gland; the larger cells are follicular cells.","The thyroid gland; the larger cells are parafollicular cells."],"correctIndex":0,"why":"First identify the gland. Follicles and colloid mean thyroid, so packed cords of cells with no colloid mean parathyroid. Then identify the cells. On a parathyroid slide the small, plentiful cells are chief cells, and the larger cells with dark pink, granular cytoplasm (packed with mitochondria) are oxyphil cells.","notes":["Correct. Packed cords with no colloid mean parathyroid, and the larger dark pink cells are oxyphil cells.","The gland is right, but chief cells are the small, numerous cells that make up most of the tissue. The larger dark pink cells are oxyphil cells.","Follicular cells are simple cuboidal cells lining each follicle around the colloid. A slide with no follicles is not thyroid.","Parafollicular cells are larger and paler, but they sit between thyroid follicles. This slide has no follicles or colloid, so it is not thyroid."],"comp":130,"competencyId":"w6-thyroid-parathyroid"},{"id":"N-a17d-130-3","dok":3,"q":"During neck surgery, a surgeon removes tissue from the back surface of the right thyroid lobe only. Which glands are most at risk, and how many of them usually sit on that one lobe?","a":"The parathyroid glands; usually two, a superior and an inferior.","options":["The parathyroid glands; usually four, all on the right lobe.","The parafollicular cells; usually two clusters, each in a capsule.","The parathyroid glands; usually two, a superior and an inferior.","The thyroid isthmus; one bridge that crosses behind the lobe."],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The parathyroid glands are small, round glands, each about the size of a grain of rice. Most people have four: a superior and an inferior gland on the posterior surface of each thyroid lobe, each in its own thin capsule. Surgery on the back of one lobe puts the two glands on that lobe at risk. Their number and exact position vary from person to person.","notes":["The glands are right, but most people have four in total, two on each lobe. Only the superior and inferior glands on the right lobe are at risk here.","Parafollicular cells (C cells) sit alone or in small groups between the thyroid follicles, inside the thyroid. They do not form separate glands with their own capsules.","Correct. Most people have a superior and an inferior parathyroid gland on the posterior surface of each thyroid lobe, so two sit on the right lobe.","The isthmus is thyroid tissue, not a separate gland, and it crosses in front of the upper trachea in the midline, not behind a lobe."],"comp":130,"competencyId":"w6-thyroid-parathyroid"},{"id":"N-a17d-130-4","dok":3,"q":"On a thyroid slide, you find a large, pale cell tucked at the outer edge of a follicle wall. It does not touch the pink material in the center. What is the pink material, and what is the cell?","a":"The pink material is colloid; the cell is a parafollicular cell.","options":["The pink material is cytoplasm; the cell is a chief cell.","The pink material is colloid; the cell is a follicular cell.","The pink material is cytoplasm; the cell is an oxyphil cell.","The pink material is colloid; the cell is a parafollicular cell."],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Each thyroid follicle is a ring of cells around a center filled with colloid, a thick, protein-rich fluid that stains pink. The wall is a single layer of simple cuboidal follicular cells that sit around the colloid. Parafollicular cells (C cells) are larger and paler, sit between the follicles or at the outer edge of a follicle wall, and do not touch the colloid.","notes":["Chief cells are the small, numerous cells of the parathyroid, which has no follicles. The pink center of a thyroid follicle is colloid.","Follicular cells form the single cuboidal layer that lines the colloid, so they touch it. The pale cell that does not touch the colloid is a parafollicular cell.","Oxyphil cells belong to the parathyroid, which has no follicles. The pink center of a thyroid follicle is colloid.","Correct. The pink center is colloid, and a larger, pale cell at the edge of the wall that does not touch it is a parafollicular cell (C cell)."],"comp":130,"competencyId":"w6-thyroid-parathyroid"},{"id":"N-a17d-131-1","dok":3,"q":"Moving inward from the capsule on an adrenal slide, you pass a thin band of rounded clusters, then a thick band of pale, foamy cells in straight columns. Which layer comes next, and what lies beyond it?","a":"The zona reticularis, then the medulla with its chromaffin cells.","options":["The zona reticularis, then the medulla with its chromaffin cells.","The zona reticularis, then the capsule of dense connective tissue.","The zona glomerulosa, then the medulla with its chromaffin cells.","The medulla, then the zona reticularis around the central vessels."],"correctIndex":0,"why":"From the capsule inward, the cortex has three zones: the thin zona glomerulosa of rounded clusters, the thick zona fasciculata of pale, foamy columns, and the thin zona reticularis of cells in a branching network. The reticularis sits against the medulla, the inner core made of chromaffin cells clustered around blood vessels. GFR, then the medulla, gives the order.","notes":["Correct. After the glomerulosa and fasciculata comes the zona reticularis, and the medulla of chromaffin cells lies inside it.","The zone is right, but the capsule is the outer covering. Inside the zona reticularis lies the medulla.","The zona glomerulosa is the thin band of rounded clusters just under the capsule, which you have already passed.","The zona reticularis lies between the fasciculata and the medulla, so you reach it before the medulla, not after."],"comp":131,"competencyId":"w6-adrenal"},{"id":"N-a17d-131-2","dok":3,"q":"During dissection, you find a crescent-shaped gland on the superior pole of a kidney, behind the peritoneum. Which side of the body is it on, and what cells make up its inner core?","a":"The left side; chromaffin cells, which are modified sympathetic neurons.","options":["The left side; zona reticularis cells in a branching network.","The left side; chromaffin cells, which are modified sympathetic neurons.","The right side; chromaffin cells, which are modified sympathetic neurons.","The right side; glandular epithelial cells in three zones."],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Each adrenal gland sits on the superior pole of a kidney, behind the peritoneum. The right gland is shaped like a pyramid and the left like a crescent, so a crescent-shaped gland is the left adrenal. Its outer cortex is glandular epithelium in three zones. Its inner core, the medulla, is made of chromaffin cells, which are modified sympathetic neurons with no axons or dendrites.","notes":["The side is right, but the zona reticularis is the inner zone of the cortex. The inner core, the medulla, is made of chromaffin cells.","Correct. The left adrenal gland is crescent-shaped, and its medulla is made of chromaffin cells, modified sympathetic neurons.","The core is right, but the right adrenal gland is shaped like a pyramid. The crescent-shaped gland is on the left.","The right gland is pyramid-shaped, and the three zones of glandular epithelium form the outer cortex, not the inner core."],"comp":131,"competencyId":"w6-adrenal"},{"id":"N-a17d-132-1","dok":3,"q":"A slide of a testis shows clusters of cells in the spaces between the seminiferous tubules. What are these cells, and what kind of tissue are they?","a":"Interstitial (Leydig) cells, the endocrine tissue of the testis.","options":["Islet cells, the endocrine tissue of the testis.","Corpus luteum cells, the endocrine tissue of the testis.","Interstitial (Leydig) cells, the endocrine tissue of the testis.","Interstitial (Leydig) cells, the gamete-making tissue of the testis."],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The gonads are mixed organs: they make gametes, and they also contain endocrine cells. The testes sit in the scrotum, and their endocrine tissue is the interstitial cells (Leydig cells), which lie in the spaces between the seminiferous tubules. In the ovaries, the endocrine tissue is the cells of the developing follicles and the corpus luteum.","notes":["Islet cells form the pancreatic islets, scattered among the acini of the pancreas. They are not found in the testis.","The corpus luteum forms in the ovary after ovulation. It is endocrine tissue of the female gonad, not the testis.","Correct. The interstitial cells between the seminiferous tubules are the endocrine tissue that makes the testis a mixed organ.","The cells are named correctly, but they are endocrine cells. Gametes are made inside the seminiferous tubules, not in the spaces between them."],"comp":132,"competencyId":"w6-endocrine-tissue-organs"},{"id":"N-a17d-132-2","dok":3,"q":"A surgeon removes the tail of the pancreas, near the spleen. What happens to the pancreatic islets?","a":"Some islets are removed, since they are scattered throughout the gland.","options":["No islets are removed, since they are all in the head of the gland.","Some islets are removed, since they are scattered throughout the gland.","All the islets are removed, since they are all in the tail of the gland.","No islets are removed, since they lie outside the gland in the duodenum."],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The pancreas lies behind the stomach, with its head in the curve of the duodenum and its tail reaching toward the spleen. About 1 to 2 million pancreatic islets are scattered among the acini throughout the whole gland, not gathered in one region. Removing the tail removes the islets in the tail, while the islets in the head and body remain.","notes":["The islets are not collected in the head. They are scattered throughout the pancreas, including the tail.","Correct. The islets are scattered throughout the pancreas, so removing any part of it removes the islets in that part.","The islets are not gathered in the tail. They are scattered throughout the pancreas, so the head and body still hold islets.","The islets lie inside the pancreas, scattered among the acini. The duodenal mucosa has its own enteroendocrine cells, which are different."],"comp":132,"competencyId":"w6-endocrine-tissue-organs"}]},{"id":"m4-hip-thigh-muscles","title":"Gluteal and thigh muscles","summary":"gluteal muscles, anterior, medial and posterior thigh compartments","mw":"2026-11-02","tr":"2026-11-03","cards":[{"id":"L04394","dok":1,"q":"Which list names the four muscles of the quadriceps femoris?","a":"Rectus femoris, vastus lateralis, vastus medialis, and vastus intermedius.","options":["Rectus femoris, vastus lateralis, vastus medialis, sartorius","Rectus femoris, vastus lateralis, vastus medialis, vastus intermedius","Biceps femoris, vastus lateralis, vastus medialis, vastus intermedius","Rectus femoris, vastus lateralis, gracilis, vastus intermedius"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Quadriceps means four heads. The quadriceps femoris on the front of the thigh is made of the rectus femoris in the middle and three vastus muscles: the vastus lateralis on the outer side, the vastus medialis on the inner side, and the vastus intermedius deep to the rectus femoris. All four join in the quadriceps tendon, which surrounds the patella and continues as the patellar ligament to the tibial tuberosity.","notes":["The sartorius is the long strap muscle that crosses the front of the thigh diagonally from the hip to the medial knee. It is in the anterior thigh but is not part of the quadriceps, so it fits a question about the longest muscle in the body.","Correct. These are the four heads of the quadriceps femoris.","The biceps femoris is a hamstring muscle on the back of the thigh. Students pick it because its name ends in femoris, but it would be the answer for the lateral hamstring.","The gracilis is a slender muscle of the medial thigh, in the adductor group. It would fit a question about the medial compartment, not the quadriceps."],"lt":"t-w5-lab-thigh-gluteal","lid":"w5-lab-thigh-gluteal-r1","comp":91,"competencyId":"w5-lab-thigh-gluteal"},{"id":"L04395","dok":1,"q":"Which list names the three hamstring muscles?","a":"Biceps femoris, semitendinosus, and semimembranosus.","options":["Biceps femoris, semitendinosus, gastrocnemius","Rectus femoris, semitendinosus, semimembranosus","Biceps femoris, semitendinosus, semimembranosus","Biceps femoris, gracilis, semimembranosus"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The hamstrings are the three muscles of the posterior thigh. The biceps femoris lies on the lateral side, while the semitendinosus and the semimembranosus lie on the medial side, with the semitendinosus more superficial. Running behind both the hip and the knee, the group extends the thigh at the hip and flexes the knee.","notes":["The gastrocnemius is a calf muscle of the posterior leg. It lies just below the hamstrings and also helps flex the knee, but it belongs to the leg and is the answer for a calf question.","The rectus femoris is part of the quadriceps on the front of the thigh, the group that opposes the hamstrings. Pick it for a question about the quadriceps.","Correct. The biceps femoris, semitendinosus and semimembranosus are the hamstrings.","The gracilis is a slender muscle of the medial thigh in the adductor group. It sits near the semitendinosus at the knee, which is why students mix them up, but it is not a hamstring."],"lt":"t-w5-lab-thigh-gluteal","lid":"w5-lab-thigh-gluteal-r2","comp":91,"competencyId":"w5-lab-thigh-gluteal"},{"id":"L04396","dok":1,"q":"Which gluteal muscle is the largest, and what is its main action?","a":"The gluteus maximus, which extends the thigh.","options":["Gluteus medius; extends the thigh","Gluteus maximus; flexes the thigh","Gluteus medius; abducts the thigh","Gluteus maximus; extends the thigh"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The gluteus maximus is the largest and most superficial gluteal muscle, forming most of the bulk of the buttock. It runs from the posterior ilium, sacrum, and coccyx to the gluteal tuberosity of the femur and the iliotibial (IT) band, passing behind the hip, so its main action is to extend the thigh at the hip. It is the main extensor for climbing stairs and rising from a chair.","notes":["The gluteus medius lies partly under the gluteus maximus and is smaller. Its main action is abduction, so pairing it with extension gets both the muscle and the action wrong.","The gluteus maximus is the right muscle, but it extends the thigh rather than flexing it. Flexing the thigh at the hip is the job of the iliopsoas on the front, which is the answer for a hip flexion question.","The gluteus medius does abduct the thigh, but it is not the largest gluteal muscle. Choose this pairing for a question about which gluteal muscle keeps the pelvis level when you stand on one leg.","Correct. The gluteus maximus is the largest gluteal muscle and extends the thigh."],"lt":"t-w5-lab-thigh-gluteal","lid":"w5-lab-thigh-gluteal-r3","comp":91,"competencyId":"w5-lab-thigh-gluteal"},{"id":"L04397","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares the actions of the quadriceps and the hamstrings at the knee?","a":"The quadriceps extend the knee; the hamstrings flex the knee.","options":["Quadriceps flex the knee; hamstrings extend the knee","Both groups flex the knee as synergists","Both groups extend the knee as synergists","Quadriceps extend the knee; hamstrings flex the knee"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The quadriceps lie on the front of the thigh and pull on the tibia through the patellar ligament, straightening (extending) the knee. The hamstrings lie on the back of the thigh and pass behind the knee to attach on the tibia and fibula, so they bend (flex) it. Because they produce opposite actions at the same joint, the two groups are antagonists of each other.","notes":["This reverses the two groups. A muscle on the front of the knee pulls the leg straight, so the quadriceps are the extensors, and the hamstrings behind the knee are the flexors.","The hamstrings do flex the knee, but the quadriceps do the opposite. Students pick this when they remember that both groups cross the knee but forget they pull on opposite sides of it.","Synergists are muscles that help each other with the same action, but these two groups work in opposite directions at the knee. Only the quadriceps extend it.","Correct. The quadriceps extend the knee and the hamstrings flex it."],"lt":"t-w5-lab-thigh-gluteal","lid":"w5-lab-thigh-gluteal-r4","comp":91,"competencyId":"w5-lab-thigh-gluteal"},{"id":"L04398","dok":3,"q":"As you lower yourself slowly from standing into a chair, your knees bend under your body weight. Which muscle group works to control that bending and keep you from dropping?","a":"The quadriceps femoris.","options":["Adductor group","Gastrocnemius and soleus","Hamstrings","Quadriceps femoris"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"When you sit down slowly, gravity is what bends your knees. To keep from dropping, the knee extensors, the quadriceps, stay active while lengthening, braking the knee flexion that gravity produces. That is why your thighs feel the effort in a slow squat even though your knees are bending.","notes":["The adductor group on the medial thigh pulls the thighs toward the midline. It would be the answer for squeezing the knees together, not controlling knee bend.","The gastrocnemius and soleus are calf muscles that plantarflex the foot. They are the answer for rising onto your toes, not for controlling the knee as you sit.","The hamstrings flex the knee, which is the movement gravity is already producing as you sit. They would be the answer if the question asked which group actively bends the knee against resistance, as in a leg curl.","Correct. The quadriceps resist knee flexion and control the descent."],"lt":"t-w5-lab-thigh-gluteal","lid":"w5-lab-thigh-gluteal-r5","comp":91,"competencyId":"w5-lab-thigh-gluteal"},{"id":"N-m4-hip-thigh-muscles-1","dok":1,"q":"Which muscle is the largest of the gluteal group and the most powerful extensor of the hip?","a":"The gluteus maximus.","options":["Gluteus medius","Gluteus maximus","Tensor fasciae latae","Gluteus minimus"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The gluteus maximus forms most of the bulk of the buttock. It runs from the back of the ilium, sacrum, and coccyx to the gluteal tuberosity of the femur and the iliotibial (IT) band, so when it contracts it pulls the thigh backward. That makes it the main extensor of the hip, the muscle you rely on to climb stairs or rise from a chair.","notes":["The gluteus medius lies partly under the gluteus maximus on the upper outer buttock and inserts on the greater trochanter. It is smaller, and its main action is abduction, not extension.","Correct. The gluteus maximus is the largest gluteal muscle and the main extensor of the hip.","The tensor fasciae latae is a small muscle on the lateral hip that tightens the IT band. It flexes the thigh, the opposite of extension.","The gluteus minimus is the smallest and deepest of the three gluteal muscles, lying under the gluteus medius. It helps abduct the thigh rather than extend it."],"comp":91,"competencyId":"w5-lab-thigh-gluteal"},{"id":"N-m4-hip-thigh-muscles-2","dok":1,"q":"What is the main action of the gluteus medius?","a":"Abduction of the thigh at the hip.","options":["Flexion of the knee","Extension of the thigh","Adduction of the thigh","Abduction of the thigh"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The gluteus medius runs from the outer surface of the ilium down to the greater trochanter on the lateral side of the femur. Because it sits on the outside of the hip joint, it pulls the thigh away from the midline, which is abduction. It also medially rotates the thigh and steadies the pelvis each time you stand on one leg.","notes":["Flexion of the knee is done mainly by the hamstrings on the back of the thigh. The gluteus medius ends at the greater trochanter and never crosses the knee, so it cannot move that joint.","Extension of the thigh is the main job of the gluteus maximus, helped by the hamstrings. Pick extension if the question named the gluteus maximus or asked what happens when you push your leg backward.","Adduction pulls the thigh toward the midline and belongs to the medial thigh muscles, such as the adductor longus, brevis, and magnus. It is the opposite of what the gluteus medius does.","Correct. The gluteus medius is the main abductor of the thigh."],"comp":91,"competencyId":"w5-lab-thigh-gluteal"},{"id":"N-m4-hip-thigh-muscles-3","dok":3,"q":"A patient stands on her right leg only, and the left side of her pelvis drops instead of staying level. Weakness of which muscle best explains this?","a":"The right gluteus medius.","options":["Left gluteus medius","Right gluteus medius","Right adductor longus","Right gluteus maximus"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"When you stand on one leg, the gluteus medius on the standing side pulls on the pelvis from its attachment at the greater trochanter and keeps the opposite side from sagging. If that muscle is weak, the unsupported side of the pelvis drops. Here she is standing on the right leg, so the right gluteus medius is the muscle failing to hold the pelvis level. Clinicians call this a positive Trendelenburg sign.","notes":["The left gluteus medius belongs to the leg that is lifted off the ground, so it is not holding up the pelvis at that moment. It would be the weak muscle if she stood on her left leg and the right side of her pelvis dropped.","Correct. The stance-side gluteus medius holds the pelvis level, so a drop on the left points to weakness on the right.","The right adductor longus pulls the thigh toward the midline from the medial side. It does not act as the lateral support that keeps the pelvis level, so its weakness would not cause the opposite side to drop.","The right gluteus maximus is the main extensor of the hip, used to push the thigh backward when climbing or rising from a chair. Weakness there shows up as trouble with those movements, not as a pelvis that tilts while standing on one leg."],"comp":91,"competencyId":"w5-lab-thigh-gluteal"},{"id":"N-m4-hip-thigh-muscles-4","dok":1,"q":"Which gluteal muscle is the smallest and lies deepest, under the gluteus medius?","a":"The gluteus minimus.","options":["Gluteus maximus","Gluteus minimus","Tensor fasciae latae","Gluteus medius"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The three gluteal muscles stack from superficial to deep: gluteus maximus on the surface, gluteus medius beneath it, and gluteus minimus deepest of all. The names follow size as well, since minimus means smallest. The gluteus minimus runs from the ilium to the greater trochanter and works with the gluteus medius to abduct and medially rotate the thigh.","notes":["The gluteus maximus is the largest and most superficial gluteal muscle, the one that gives the buttock its shape. It lies on top of the other two, not under them.","Correct. The gluteus minimus is the smallest and deepest of the gluteal muscles.","The tensor fasciae latae is not deep in the buttock at all. It sits on the front of the lateral hip and pulls on the IT band.","The gluteus medius is the middle layer, directly under the gluteus maximus. The muscle under the gluteus medius is a different, smaller one."],"comp":91,"competencyId":"w5-lab-thigh-gluteal"},{"id":"N-m4-hip-thigh-muscles-5","dok":1,"q":"The iliopsoas is formed by which two muscles?","a":"The iliacus and the psoas major.","options":["Psoas major and sartorius","Iliacus and psoas major","Iliacus and pectineus","Pectineus and sartorius"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The iliopsoas is a combined muscle. The iliacus fills the iliac fossa on the inner surface of the ilium, and the psoas major arises from the bodies and transverse processes of vertebrae T12 to L5. The two pass together under the inguinal ligament and insert on the lesser trochanter of the femur.","notes":["The sartorius is the long strap muscle that crosses the front of the thigh from the anterior superior iliac spine to the medial tibia. It helps flex the hip, but it is a separate muscle, not part of the iliopsoas.","Correct. The iliacus and the psoas major join to form the iliopsoas.","The pectineus is a flat muscle of the medial compartment that lies right beside the iliopsoas at the top of the thigh. That closeness makes it easy to mix up, but it is not part of the iliopsoas.","Neither of these is part of the iliopsoas. The pectineus is in the medial compartment, and the sartorius is the strap muscle crossing the front of the thigh."],"comp":91,"competencyId":"w5-lab-thigh-gluteal"},{"id":"N-m4-hip-thigh-muscles-6","dok":1,"q":"Where does the iliopsoas insert on the femur?","a":"On the lesser trochanter.","options":["Lesser trochanter","Linea aspera","Gluteal tuberosity","Greater trochanter"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The iliopsoas comes down from vertebrae T12 to L5 and the iliac fossa, passes in front of the hip joint, and ends on the lesser trochanter, the small projection on the medial side of the upper femur. Pulling on this point from above and in front swings the thigh forward, so the iliopsoas flexes the thigh at the hip.","notes":["Correct. The iliopsoas tendon attaches to the lesser trochanter.","The linea aspera is the rough ridge running down the back of the femoral shaft. It would be the answer for where most of the adductor muscles insert.","The gluteal tuberosity is a roughened area on the upper posterior femur. Choose it for part of the insertion of the gluteus maximus.","The greater trochanter is the large projection on the lateral side of the upper femur. Pick it for the insertion of the gluteus medius and gluteus minimus."],"comp":91,"competencyId":"w5-lab-thigh-gluteal"},{"id":"N-m4-hip-thigh-muscles-7","dok":2,"q":"The iliopsoas runs from vertebrae T12 to L5 and the iliac fossa, passes in front of the hip joint, and inserts on the lesser trochanter. What action does this arrangement produce?","a":"Flexion of the thigh at the hip.","options":["Abduction of the thigh","Extension of the thigh","Extension of the knee","Flexion of the thigh"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"A muscle that crosses the front of a joint usually flexes it. The iliopsoas crosses the front of the hip and pulls the lesser trochanter upward and forward, lifting the thigh toward the trunk. It is the prime mover of hip flexion, the movement used when you raise your knee to march or climb.","notes":["Abduction moves the thigh out to the side and is the job of muscles on the lateral hip, such as the gluteus medius. The iliopsoas lies in front of the joint, not beside it.","Extension of the thigh moves it backward and is done by muscles that cross the back of the hip, mainly the gluteus maximus and the hamstrings. That is the opposite action of the iliopsoas.","Extension of the knee is done by the quadriceps, which reach the tibia. The iliopsoas stops at the lesser trochanter of the femur and never crosses the knee.","Correct. Crossing the front of the hip, the iliopsoas flexes the thigh."],"comp":91,"competencyId":"w5-lab-thigh-gluteal"},{"id":"N-m4-hip-thigh-muscles-8","dok":1,"q":"Which part of the quadriceps femoris lies deep to the rectus femoris, between the vastus lateralis and vastus medialis?","a":"The vastus intermedius.","options":["Vastus intermedius","Vastus lateralis","Sartorius","Vastus medialis"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The quadriceps femoris has four parts. The rectus femoris runs down the middle of the front of the thigh, the vastus lateralis lies on the outer side, the vastus medialis on the inner side, and the vastus intermedius lies hidden beneath the rectus femoris, directly on the femur. The name intermedius tells you it sits between the other two vasti.","notes":["Correct. The vastus intermedius lies under the rectus femoris, between the other two vasti.","The vastus lateralis is the large quadriceps muscle on the outer side of the thigh. Pick it for the lateral part of the quadriceps or for the thigh muscle often used for injections in infants.","The sartorius is a long strap muscle crossing the front of the thigh diagonally, and it is not part of the quadriceps. Pick it for the longest muscle in the body or the lateral border of the femoral triangle.","The vastus medialis is the quadriceps muscle on the inner side of the thigh, forming the bulge just above and medial to the knee. It would be the answer for the medial part of the group."],"comp":91,"competencyId":"w5-lab-thigh-gluteal"},{"id":"N-m4-hip-thigh-muscles-9","dok":1,"q":"Which quadriceps muscle arises from the anterior inferior iliac spine rather than from the femur?","a":"The rectus femoris.","options":["Vastus medialis","Rectus femoris","Vastus intermedius","Vastus lateralis"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Three parts of the quadriceps, the three vasti, arise from the shaft of the femur. The rectus femoris is different because it starts higher, on the anterior inferior iliac spine of the hip bone. Since it begins above the hip joint, it crosses both the hip and the knee, so it can flex the hip as well as extend the knee.","notes":["The vastus medialis arises from the medial side of the femur along the linea aspera. Pick it for the inner quadriceps muscle that bulges above the medial knee.","Correct. The rectus femoris arises from the anterior inferior iliac spine.","The vastus intermedius arises from the front and side of the femoral shaft, under the rectus femoris. Pick it for the deep quadriceps muscle lying directly on the femur.","The vastus lateralis arises from the upper lateral femur, including near the greater trochanter and the linea aspera. Choose it for the outer quadriceps muscle, which crosses only the knee."],"comp":91,"competencyId":"w5-lab-thigh-gluteal"},{"id":"N-m4-hip-thigh-muscles-10","dok":1,"q":"The four parts of the quadriceps femoris share a common insertion. Where is it?","a":"The tibial tuberosity, through the patella and patellar ligament.","options":["Lesser trochanter","Tibial tuberosity","Head of the fibula","Medial condyle of the tibia"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"All four quadriceps muscles join into the quadriceps tendon, which wraps around the patella. Below the patella the same line of pull continues as the patellar ligament and anchors to the tibial tuberosity on the front of the tibia. Pulling on that point straightens the knee.","notes":["The lesser trochanter is on the upper medial femur, far above the knee. Pick it for the insertion of the iliopsoas.","Correct. The quadriceps inserts on the tibial tuberosity by way of the patella and patellar ligament.","The head of the fibula is the knob at the top of the lateral leg bone. Pick it for the insertion of the biceps femoris, the lateral hamstring.","The medial condyle of the tibia is on the inner side of the top of the tibia. It would be the answer for where the semimembranosus inserts on the back of the medial knee."],"comp":91,"competencyId":"w5-lab-thigh-gluteal"},{"id":"N-m4-hip-thigh-muscles-11","dok":1,"q":"What is the main action shared by all four parts of the quadriceps femoris?","a":"Extension of the knee.","options":["Flexion of the knee","Adduction of the thigh","Extension of the knee","Plantar flexion of the foot"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Every part of the quadriceps ends on the tibial tuberosity through the patellar ligament, on the front of the knee. Pulling on the front of the tibia straightens the leg at the knee, so knee extension is the action all four share. Only the rectus femoris adds a second action, flexion of the hip.","notes":["Flexion of the knee bends it and is the job of the hamstrings on the back of the thigh. The hamstrings are the antagonists of the quadriceps.","Adduction of the thigh pulls it toward the midline and belongs to the medial thigh group, such as the adductor longus and adductor magnus.","Correct. The quadriceps extends the knee.","Plantar flexion points the foot downward and is done by the calf muscles, mainly the gastrocnemius and soleus. The quadriceps does not cross the ankle."],"comp":91,"competencyId":"w5-lab-thigh-gluteal"},{"id":"N-m4-hip-thigh-muscles-12","dok":2,"q":"The patella is a sesamoid bone, meaning it forms inside a tendon. Inside the tendon of which muscle group does it sit?","a":"The quadriceps femoris.","options":["Adductor group","Hamstrings","Quadriceps femoris","Gluteal group"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The patella develops within the quadriceps tendon at the front of the knee. It lets that tendon glide smoothly over the knee joint and improves the leverage of the quadriceps as it extends the knee. Below the patella, the tendon continues as the patellar ligament to the tibial tuberosity.","notes":["The adductor group sits on the medial thigh and inserts mainly on the linea aspera of the femur, well above the knee. It has no tendon crossing the front of the knee.","The hamstrings run down the back of the thigh and insert behind and beside the knee, on the tibia and fibula. They have no sesamoid bone at the front of the knee.","Correct. The patella lies within the quadriceps tendon.","The gluteal group acts at the hip and inserts on the upper femur and the IT band. It has no tendon at the front of the knee."],"comp":91,"competencyId":"w5-lab-thigh-gluteal"},{"id":"N-m4-hip-thigh-muscles-13","dok":1,"q":"Which muscle is the longest in the body, running diagonally across the front of the thigh?","a":"The sartorius.","options":["Gracilis","Sartorius","Rectus femoris","Biceps femoris"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The sartorius is a long, narrow strap muscle that starts at the anterior superior iliac spine, crosses the front of the thigh at an angle, and ends on the medial side of the upper tibia. Its length and diagonal course make it the longest muscle in the body. It also forms the lateral border of the femoral triangle.","notes":["The gracilis is also long and strap-like, but it runs down the medial side of the thigh from the pubis. Choose it for the medial thigh muscle that crosses the knee.","Correct. The sartorius is the longest muscle in the body.","The rectus femoris runs straight down the middle of the front of the thigh, not diagonally. Pick it for the quadriceps muscle that arises from the anterior inferior iliac spine.","The biceps femoris is on the back of the thigh, running to the head of the fibula. It would be the answer for the lateral hamstring."],"comp":91,"competencyId":"w5-lab-thigh-gluteal"},{"id":"N-m4-hip-thigh-muscles-14","dok":3,"q":"A tailor sits on the floor with legs crossed, each ankle resting on the opposite knee. The muscle named for this posture flexes, abducts, and laterally rotates the thigh while also flexing the knee. Which muscle is it?","a":"The sartorius.","options":["Tensor fasciae latae","Gracilis","Semitendinosus","Sartorius"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The name sartorius comes from the Latin word for tailor. Because it runs from the anterior superior iliac spine, across the front of the thigh, to the medial tibia, it crosses both the hip and the knee. Its pull flexes, abducts, and laterally rotates the thigh and also bends the knee, which together place the leg in the cross-legged position.","notes":["The tensor fasciae latae flexes and abducts the thigh, but it medially rotates it and ends in the IT band on the lateral side. It does not flex the knee or rotate the thigh outward the way this posture needs.","The gracilis also crosses the hip and knee and ends near the sartorius on the medial tibia, but it adducts the thigh rather than abducting it.","The semitendinosus is a hamstring that extends the thigh and flexes the knee. Thigh extension is the opposite of what the cross-legged position needs.","Correct. The sartorius produces the cross-legged position it is named for."],"comp":91,"competencyId":"w5-lab-thigh-gluteal"},{"id":"N-m4-hip-thigh-muscles-15","dok":1,"q":"Which small muscle on the lateral side of the hip pulls on the iliotibial (IT) band?","a":"The tensor fasciae latae.","options":["Gluteus minimus","Tensor fasciae latae","Vastus lateralis","Biceps femoris"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The tensor fasciae latae arises from the anterior iliac crest and the anterior superior iliac spine and ends in the iliotibial (IT) band, which runs to the lateral condyle of the tibia. Its name describes its job: tensor means tightener, and fasciae latae refers to the wide fascia of the thigh. It also flexes, abducts, and medially rotates the thigh, and tightening the IT band steadies the knee.","notes":["The gluteus minimus is a deep gluteal muscle that inserts on the greater trochanter, not into the IT band.","Correct. The tensor fasciae latae tightens the IT band.","The vastus lateralis lies under the IT band on the outer thigh, but it is a quadriceps muscle that inserts on the tibial tuberosity, not into the band.","The biceps femoris is on the back of the thigh and inserts on the head of the fibula, not into the IT band."],"comp":91,"competencyId":"w5-lab-thigh-gluteal"},{"id":"N-m4-hip-thigh-muscles-16","dok":1,"q":"What is the thick band of fascia that runs down the lateral thigh from the iliac crest to the lateral side of the tibia?","a":"Iliotibial (IT) band.","options":["Calcaneal tendon","Patellar ligament","Iliotibial (IT) band","Inguinal ligament"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The iliotibial (IT) band is not a muscle. It is a thick band of fascia that runs from the iliac crest down the outer thigh to the lateral condyle of the tibia, just below the knee. The tensor fasciae latae and the gluteus maximus pull on it, which helps steady the knee.","notes":["The calcaneal (Achilles) tendon attaches the calf muscles to the heel bone at the back of the ankle, not the thigh.","The patellar ligament runs from the patella to the tibial tuberosity on the front of the knee. It is short and anterior, not a long band down the lateral thigh.","Correct. The IT band runs down the lateral thigh from the iliac crest to the lateral condyle of the tibia.","The inguinal ligament stretches across the groin from the anterior superior iliac spine to the pubic tubercle. It does not run down the thigh."],"comp":91,"competencyId":"w5-lab-thigh-gluteal"},{"id":"N-m4-hip-thigh-muscles-17","dok":3,"q":"A distance runner has pain along the outside of the thigh and knee where a tight band rubs over the lateral femoral epicondyle. Which structure is most likely irritated?","a":"Iliotibial (IT) band.","options":["Iliotibial (IT) band","Adductor longus tendon","Patellar ligament","Semitendinosus tendon"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The iliotibial (IT) band runs down the lateral thigh and crosses the outer side of the knee near the lateral femoral epicondyle. With repeated bending and straightening of the knee during running, a tight band can rub over this bony bump and become painful. Pain on the outer thigh and knee points to this band.","notes":["Correct. The IT band crosses the lateral femoral epicondyle and can be irritated there.","The adductor longus tendon is high on the inner thigh near the pubis. Pain there would be in the groin, not the outer knee.","The patellar ligament is on the front of the knee, below the kneecap, not on the outer side.","The semitendinosus tendon passes along the back and inner side of the knee to the medial tibia. It is on the medial side, not the lateral side."],"comp":91,"competencyId":"w5-lab-thigh-gluteal"},{"id":"N-m4-hip-thigh-muscles-18","dok":1,"q":"What is the main action shared by the muscles of the medial thigh compartment?","a":"Adduction of the thigh.","options":["Flexion of the knee","Adduction of the thigh","Extension of the knee","Abduction of the thigh"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The medial compartment holds the adductor longus, adductor brevis, adductor magnus, pectineus, and gracilis. They arise from the pubis and ischium and insert on the femur or medial tibia, so they pull the thigh toward the midline. That movement, adduction, is the shared job of the group.","notes":["Flexion of the knee is mainly done by the hamstrings in the posterior compartment. Only the gracilis of the medial group crosses the knee, so knee flexion is not the shared action.","Correct. The medial thigh muscles adduct the thigh.","Extension of the knee is the job of the quadriceps in the anterior compartment. The medial muscles mostly stop on the femur and do not straighten the knee.","Abduction moves the thigh away from the midline and is done by the gluteus medius and minimus on the lateral hip. It is the opposite of the medial group's action."],"comp":91,"competencyId":"w5-lab-thigh-gluteal"},{"id":"N-m4-hip-thigh-muscles-19","dok":1,"q":"Which medial thigh muscle is a long, thin strap that runs from the pubis to the medial tibia and is the only member of its group to cross the knee?","a":"The gracilis.","options":["Adductor magnus","Adductor longus","Pectineus","Gracilis"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Most medial thigh muscles end on the femur, along the linea aspera or near the adductor tubercle. The gracilis is the exception. It is a slender strap that runs down the inner thigh from the pubis and inserts on the medial side of the upper tibia, so it crosses the knee and can help flex it as well as adduct the thigh.","notes":["The adductor magnus is the largest adductor and inserts on the linea aspera and adductor tubercle of the femur, above the knee. Choose it for the biggest muscle of the medial group.","The adductor longus is a triangular muscle of the upper medial thigh that inserts on the linea aspera. Pick it for the medial border of the femoral triangle.","The pectineus is a short, flat muscle at the top of the medial thigh that inserts on the upper femur. It would be the answer for the medial muscle lying in the floor of the femoral triangle beside the iliopsoas.","Correct. The gracilis is the long medial strap muscle that crosses the knee."],"comp":91,"competencyId":"w5-lab-thigh-gluteal"},{"id":"N-m4-hip-thigh-muscles-20","dok":1,"q":"Which is the largest muscle of the medial (adductor) compartment of the thigh?","a":"The adductor magnus.","options":["Adductor magnus","Adductor longus","Gracilis","Adductor brevis"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Magnus means large, and the adductor magnus is the biggest and deepest muscle of the medial thigh. It arises from the pubis and the ischium, including the ischial tuberosity, and fans out to insert along the linea aspera and on the adductor tubercle of the femur. Its broad shape makes it a powerful adductor.","notes":["Correct. The adductor magnus is the largest adductor.","The adductor longus is long but narrow, a triangular muscle near the surface of the upper medial thigh. Pick it for the medial border of the femoral triangle.","The gracilis is a long, thin strap, not a large muscle. Choose it for the medial thigh muscle that crosses the knee to reach the tibia.","The adductor brevis is short, as brevis means, and lies between the adductor longus and adductor magnus. It would be the answer for the short middle adductor."],"comp":91,"competencyId":"w5-lab-thigh-gluteal"},{"id":"N-m4-hip-thigh-muscles-21","dok":1,"q":"Most of the adductor muscles insert along which ridge on the back of the femur?","a":"The linea aspera.","options":["Linea aspera","Lateral epicondyle","Intertrochanteric line","Gluteal tuberosity"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The linea aspera is a long, rough ridge running down the middle of the posterior femoral shaft. Rough bone markings like this anchor muscles, and the adductor longus, adductor brevis, and adductor magnus all insert along it. From there they pull the femur toward the midline.","notes":["Correct. The adductor longus, brevis, and magnus insert along the linea aspera.","The lateral epicondyle is a bump on the outer side of the femur just above the knee, not a ridge on the back of the shaft. The IT band passes over it.","The intertrochanteric line runs across the front of the upper femur between the two trochanters. It is where the vastus medialis begins, not where the adductors insert.","The gluteal tuberosity is a rough area high on the back of the femur where part of the gluteus maximus inserts, not the adductors."],"comp":91,"competencyId":"w5-lab-thigh-gluteal"},{"id":"N-m4-hip-thigh-muscles-22","dok":2,"q":"Which flat muscle lies in the floor of the femoral triangle, between the iliopsoas laterally and the adductor longus medially?","a":"The pectineus.","options":["Sartorius","Pectineus","Gracilis","Adductor magnus"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The floor of the femoral triangle is formed, from lateral to medial, by the iliopsoas, the pectineus, and the adductor longus. The pectineus runs from the superior ramus of the pubis to the pectineal line of the femur, just below the lesser trochanter, and it adducts and flexes the thigh. Because it lies right next to the iliopsoas, it is often mistaken for part of it.","notes":["The sartorius forms the lateral border of the femoral triangle, not its floor. Choose it when the question asks which muscle runs along the lateral edge of the triangle.","Correct. The pectineus forms the medial part of the floor of the femoral triangle.","The gracilis runs down the medial edge of the thigh to the tibia and is not part of the triangle's floor. Pick it for the long strap of the medial thigh that crosses the knee.","The adductor magnus is a large, deep muscle lower and further back in the medial thigh. It would be the answer for the largest adductor."],"comp":91,"competencyId":"w5-lab-thigh-gluteal"},{"id":"N-m4-hip-thigh-muscles-23","dok":3,"q":"A soccer player feels a sudden pull high on the inner thigh while sweeping the leg across the body to kick. Which muscle group was most likely strained?","a":"The adductor group of the medial thigh.","options":["Gluteal group","Hamstring group","Quadriceps group","Adductor group"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Sweeping the leg across the front of the body pulls the thigh toward and past the midline, which is adduction. The adductors on the medial thigh produce that movement and attach high on the pubis, right at the groin. A sudden forceful contraction or stretch there is the classic groin pull, an adductor strain.","notes":["The gluteal muscles lie in the buttock and act mainly to extend and abduct the hip. A strain there would be felt in the buttock rather than on the inner thigh.","The hamstrings are on the back of the thigh and are usually strained during sprinting, when they work hard to slow the swinging leg. Pick them for pain in the back of the thigh.","The quadriceps fill the front of the thigh and extend the knee. They would be the answer for a strain felt on the front of the thigh, such as during a hard kick that straightens the knee.","Correct. A groin pull during a cross-body kick is an adductor strain."],"comp":91,"competencyId":"w5-lab-thigh-gluteal"},{"id":"N-m4-hip-thigh-muscles-24","dok":1,"q":"Which set names the three hamstring muscles?","a":"Biceps femoris, semitendinosus, and semimembranosus.","options":["Biceps femoris, semitendinosus, semimembranosus","Rectus femoris, semitendinosus, semimembranosus","Semitendinosus, adductor magnus, gracilis","Biceps femoris, gracilis, sartorius"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The hamstrings are the three muscles of the posterior thigh: the biceps femoris on the lateral side and the semitendinosus and semimembranosus on the medial side. All three arise mainly from the ischial tuberosity and run down the back of the thigh to the leg, so they extend the hip and flex the knee.","notes":["Correct. These are the three hamstrings.","The rectus femoris is a quadriceps muscle on the front of the thigh. Swapping it in for the biceps femoris mixes the anterior compartment with the posterior one.","The adductor magnus and gracilis belong to the medial compartment. Part of the adductor magnus arises from the ischial tuberosity, which causes confusion, but it is classed with the adductors.","The gracilis and sartorius are not hamstrings. The gracilis is in the medial compartment and the sartorius in the anterior compartment, though both share an insertion on the medial tibia with the semitendinosus."],"comp":91,"competencyId":"w5-lab-thigh-gluteal"},{"id":"N-m4-hip-thigh-muscles-25","dok":1,"q":"From which bone marking do the hamstrings mainly arise?","a":"The ischial tuberosity.","options":["Greater trochanter","Ischial tuberosity","Pubic crest","Anterior superior iliac spine"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The hamstrings share a common origin on the ischial tuberosity, the rough knob of the hip bone that you sit on. From there they run down the back of the thigh. Because they start behind the hip and end below the knee, they extend the hip and flex the knee.","notes":["The greater trochanter is a large projection on the lateral upper femur. It is where the gluteus medius inserts, not where the hamstrings arise.","Correct. The hamstrings arise from the ischial tuberosity.","The pubic crest is on the front of the pubis, near where medial thigh muscles such as the adductor longus arise. The hamstrings start farther back, on the ischium.","The anterior superior iliac spine is the bony point at the front of the iliac crest. The sartorius and tensor fasciae latae arise there, not the hamstrings."],"comp":91,"competencyId":"w5-lab-thigh-gluteal"},{"id":"N-m4-hip-thigh-muscles-26","dok":2,"q":"The hamstrings run from the ischial tuberosity, behind the hip, down to the tibia and fibula below the knee. Which pair of actions does this course produce?","a":"Extension of the hip and flexion of the knee.","options":["Hip flexion and knee extension","Hip extension and knee extension","Hip extension and knee flexion","Hip flexion and knee flexion"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"A muscle acts on every joint it crosses. The hamstrings cross the back of the hip, so they pull the thigh backward, which is hip extension. They also cross the back of the knee, so they bend it, which is knee flexion. This makes them the antagonists of the quadriceps at the knee.","notes":["This is the reverse combination, produced by the rectus femoris on the front of the thigh, which flexes the hip and extends the knee. Pick it for the one quadriceps muscle that crosses both joints.","No muscle of the posterior thigh extends both joints. Extension at the hip comes from the back of the thigh, but extension at the knee comes from the quadriceps on the front.","Correct. The hamstrings extend the hip and flex the knee.","Flexing both the hip and the knee together is what the sartorius does as it crosses the front of the hip and the medial knee. The hamstrings lie behind the hip, so they cannot flex it."],"comp":91,"competencyId":"w5-lab-thigh-gluteal"},{"id":"N-m4-hip-thigh-muscles-27","dok":1,"q":"Which hamstring lies on the lateral side of the posterior thigh and inserts on the head of the fibula?","a":"The biceps femoris.","options":["Semitendinosus","Semimembranosus","Gracilis","Biceps femoris"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The biceps femoris has a long head from the ischial tuberosity and a short head from the linea aspera. The two heads join and run down the outer side of the back of the thigh to insert on the head of the fibula. That makes it the lateral hamstring, while the other two hamstrings lie medially.","notes":["The semitendinosus is a medial hamstring with a long cord-like tendon that inserts on the medial surface of the upper tibia. Pick it for the superficial medial hamstring.","The semimembranosus is the other medial hamstring, lying deep to the semitendinosus and inserting on the medial condyle of the tibia. Choose it for the deep medial hamstring.","The gracilis is a medial thigh muscle, not a hamstring, and it inserts on the medial tibia. It would be the answer for the adductor that crosses the knee.","Correct. The biceps femoris is the lateral hamstring and inserts on the fibular head."],"comp":91,"competencyId":"w5-lab-thigh-gluteal"},{"id":"N-m4-hip-thigh-muscles-28","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares the semitendinosus and the semimembranosus?","a":"The semitendinosus is superficial with a long, cord-like tendon; the semimembranosus lies deep to it with a flat, membrane-like upper tendon.","options":["Both lie on the lateral side of the posterior thigh","Semimembranosus is superficial, semitendinosus lies deep to it","Both insert on the head of the fibula","Semitendinosus is superficial, semimembranosus lies deep to it"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The two medial hamstrings are stacked. The semitendinosus is on top, and its name points to its long, rope-like tendon in the lower thigh. The semimembranosus lies under it, named for its flat, membrane-like tendon of origin. Both run along the medial side of the back of the thigh and insert on the medial tibia.","notes":["The lateral side of the posterior thigh belongs to the biceps femoris. The semitendinosus and semimembranosus both lie on the medial side, which is why they are called the medial hamstrings.","This reverses their layers. Remember that the semitendinosus, with its long tendon, rests on top of the broader semimembranosus.","The head of the fibula receives the biceps femoris, the lateral hamstring. The two semi muscles insert on the medial side of the tibia instead.","Correct. The semitendinosus is superficial and the semimembranosus lies deep to it."],"comp":91,"competencyId":"w5-lab-thigh-gluteal"},{"id":"N-m4-hip-thigh-muscles-29","dok":2,"q":"Which part of the hamstring group crosses only the knee, so it can flex the knee but cannot extend the hip?","a":"The short head of the biceps femoris.","options":["Semimembranosus","Long head of biceps femoris","Short head of biceps femoris","Semitendinosus"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"A muscle can only move a joint it crosses. The long head of the biceps femoris, the semitendinosus, and the semimembranosus all start on the ischial tuberosity, above the hip, so they act at both the hip and knee. The short head of the biceps femoris starts lower, on the linea aspera of the femur, so it crosses only the knee and acts only as a knee flexor.","notes":["The semimembranosus also arises from the ischial tuberosity and crosses both joints. Choose it for the deep medial hamstring.","The long head of the biceps femoris arises from the ischial tuberosity and crosses the hip, so it does extend the hip. Pick it for the part of the biceps femoris that acts at two joints.","Correct. The short head of the biceps femoris arises from the femur and crosses only the knee.","The semitendinosus arises from the ischial tuberosity and crosses both the hip and knee. It would be the answer for a superficial medial hamstring that acts at both joints."],"comp":91,"competencyId":"w5-lab-thigh-gluteal"},{"id":"N-m4-hip-thigh-muscles-30","dok":3,"q":"A sprinter feels a sharp pain in the back of the thigh during a race and cannot continue. Which muscle group was most likely strained?","a":"The hamstrings.","options":["Hamstrings","Quadriceps","Iliopsoas","Adductors"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The back of the thigh is occupied by the hamstrings. During sprinting they work hard both to drive the hip into extension and to slow the swinging leg before the foot lands, which puts them under heavy load while they are being stretched. That is why hamstring strains are so common in sprinters and why the pain is felt behind the thigh.","notes":["Correct. Pain in the back of the thigh during sprinting points to a hamstring strain.","The quadriceps lie on the front of the thigh. A strain there would cause pain on the anterior thigh, not the back.","The iliopsoas runs from vertebrae T12 to L5 and the iliac fossa to the lesser trochanter, deep in the front of the hip. A strain there would be felt in the groin or front of the hip, not the back of the thigh.","The adductors lie on the inner thigh near the groin. A strain there is a groin pull, felt high on the medial thigh, not the back of the thigh."],"comp":91,"competencyId":"w5-lab-thigh-gluteal"},{"id":"N-m4-hip-thigh-muscles-31","dok":1,"q":"What forms the superior border of the femoral triangle?","a":"The inguinal ligament.","options":["Adductor longus","Inguinal ligament","Sartorius","Iliac crest"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The femoral triangle is a hollow on the upper front of the thigh, just below the groin. Its upper border is the inguinal ligament, which stretches from the anterior superior iliac spine to the pubic tubercle. The sartorius forms the lateral border and the adductor longus forms the medial border.","notes":["The adductor longus forms the medial border of the triangle. Choose it for the medial side.","Correct. The inguinal ligament is the superior border of the femoral triangle.","The sartorius forms the lateral border of the femoral triangle as it angles down and inward across the thigh. Pick it for the lateral side.","The iliac crest is the upper rim of the ilium, well above the groin. It is a landmark of the hip bone, not a border of the femoral triangle."],"comp":127,"competencyId":"w5-lab-lower-limb-vessels-nerves"},{"id":"N-m4-hip-thigh-muscles-32","dok":2,"q":"Which pair correctly gives the lateral and medial borders of the femoral triangle?","a":"Sartorius laterally and adductor longus medially.","options":["Sartorius lateral, adductor longus medial","Iliopsoas lateral, pectineus medial","Rectus femoris lateral, gracilis medial","Adductor longus lateral, sartorius medial"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The femoral triangle has three borders: the inguinal ligament above, the sartorius on the lateral side, and the adductor longus on the medial side. The sartorius runs down and inward from the anterior superior iliac spine, while the adductor longus comes down and outward from the pubis, so the two meet at the lower point of the triangle.","notes":["Correct. The sartorius is the lateral border and the adductor longus is the medial border.","The iliopsoas and pectineus are real parts of the femoral triangle, but they form its floor, not its borders. Pick this pair if the question asked which muscles lie in the floor of the triangle.","The rectus femoris lies in the middle of the front of the thigh and the gracilis on the far medial side. Neither one outlines the femoral triangle.","These are the right two muscles placed on the wrong sides. The sartorius starts at the anterior superior iliac spine on the outside, so it must be the lateral border."],"comp":127,"competencyId":"w5-lab-lower-limb-vessels-nerves"},{"id":"N-m4-hip-thigh-muscles-33","dok":2,"q":"Which muscle belongs to the anterior compartment of the thigh?","a":"The vastus medialis.","options":["Vastus medialis","Semimembranosus","Gracilis","Adductor brevis"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The thigh is divided into anterior, medial, and posterior compartments. The anterior compartment holds the quadriceps femoris and the sartorius. The vastus medialis is one of the four parts of the quadriceps, so it belongs to the anterior compartment even though its name includes the word medial.","notes":["Correct. The vastus medialis is part of the quadriceps in the anterior compartment.","The semimembranosus is a hamstring in the posterior compartment, on the back of the thigh.","The gracilis is in the medial compartment with the adductors, on the inner thigh. Do not let the word medial in vastus medialis pull you toward the medial group.","The adductor brevis is in the medial compartment and adducts the thigh. It is not an anterior muscle."],"comp":91,"competencyId":"w5-lab-thigh-gluteal"},{"id":"N-m4-hip-thigh-muscles-34","dok":2,"q":"A thigh muscle arises from the pubis and inserts along the linea aspera of the femur. Which compartment does it belong to?","a":"The medial compartment.","options":["Anterior compartment","Medial compartment","Posterior compartment","Lateral compartment"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Muscles that start on the pubis and attach along the back of the femur lie on the inner thigh and pull the femur toward the midline. That pattern fits the adductors, such as the adductor longus and adductor brevis, which make up the medial compartment.","notes":["The anterior compartment muscles, the quadriceps and sartorius, arise from the ilium or the femur and insert on the tibia, at or below the knee. None of them runs from the pubis to the linea aspera.","Correct. A muscle from the pubis to the linea aspera is an adductor in the medial compartment.","The posterior compartment muscles, the hamstrings, arise mainly from the ischial tuberosity and insert below the knee on the tibia or fibula. None of them arises from the pubis.","The thigh is divided into anterior, medial, and posterior compartments. A lateral compartment belongs to the leg, where it holds the fibularis muscles."],"comp":91,"competencyId":"w5-lab-thigh-gluteal"},{"id":"N-m4-hip-thigh-muscles-35","dok":3,"q":"After a thigh injury, a patient can bend his knee but cannot straighten it against resistance or rise from a chair without using his arms. Which muscle group is most likely damaged?","a":"The quadriceps femoris.","options":["Adductors","Quadriceps femoris","Gluteus medius and minimus","Hamstrings"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Straightening the knee is knee extension, and the muscle group that extends the knee is the quadriceps femoris on the front of the thigh. Rising from a chair also depends heavily on the quadriceps. Since knee bending still works, the hamstrings are intact and the problem lies on the front of the thigh.","notes":["The adductors pull the thigh toward the midline. Damage to them would weaken squeezing the knees together, not straightening the knee.","Correct. Loss of knee extension points to the quadriceps.","The gluteus medius and minimus abduct the thigh and keep the pelvis level when walking. Weakness there causes a tilting gait, not loss of knee extension.","The hamstrings flex the knee, and this patient can still bend it. Pick the hamstrings if the patient could straighten the knee but not bend it."],"comp":91,"competencyId":"w5-lab-thigh-gluteal"},{"id":"N-m4-hip-thigh-muscles-36","dok":3,"q":"Climbing a steep flight of stairs, a hiker drives the body upward by forcefully pulling the thigh backward at the hip. Which muscle provides most of this power?","a":"The gluteus maximus.","options":["Iliopsoas","Gluteus medius","Rectus femoris","Gluteus maximus"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Pulling the thigh backward at the hip is extension of the thigh. The gluteus maximus is the largest gluteal muscle and the main extensor of the thigh, the muscle used most for climbing stairs and rising from a chair. It runs from the back of the pelvis to the posterior femur and the IT band, behind the hip, which is why it pulls the thigh backward.","notes":["The iliopsoas is the prime mover of hip flexion, lifting the knee to place the foot on the next step. That is the opposite movement from the push that drives the body upward.","The gluteus medius abducts the thigh and keeps the pelvis level on the stance leg. It helps you stay balanced on each step but is not the main source of upward drive.","The rectus femoris flexes the hip and extends the knee. It helps straighten the knee during the climb but works against hip extension, not for it.","Correct. The gluteus maximus powers hip extension when climbing."],"comp":91,"competencyId":"w5-lab-thigh-gluteal"},{"id":"N-m4-hip-thigh-muscles-37","dok":2,"q":"Which two muscles pull on the iliotibial (IT) band?","a":"The gluteus maximus and the tensor fasciae latae.","options":["Sartorius and rectus femoris","Biceps femoris and semitendinosus","Gluteus medius and gracilis","Gluteus maximus and tensor fasciae latae"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The iliotibial (IT) band is a thick band of fascia on the lateral thigh, running from the iliac crest to the lateral condyle of the tibia. The tensor fasciae latae inserts into it from the front of the hip, and the gluteus maximus inserts into it from behind. The tensor fasciae latae tightens the band to steady the knee.","notes":["The sartorius ends on the medial tibia, and the rectus femoris ends on the tibial tuberosity through the patellar ligament. Neither attaches to the IT band.","These are two hamstrings. The biceps femoris inserts on the head of the fibula and the semitendinosus on the medial tibia, not into the IT band.","The gluteus medius inserts on the greater trochanter and the gracilis on the medial tibia. Neither attaches to the IT band.","Correct. The gluteus maximus and the tensor fasciae latae both insert into the IT band."],"comp":91,"competencyId":"w5-lab-thigh-gluteal"},{"id":"N-m4-hip-thigh-muscles-38","dok":2,"q":"Three muscles from three different thigh compartments insert together on the medial side of the upper tibia. Which set is it?","a":"Sartorius, gracilis, and semitendinosus.","options":["Sartorius, adductor longus, semimembranosus","Biceps femoris, semimembranosus, adductor magnus","Rectus femoris, vastus medialis, gracilis","Sartorius, gracilis, semitendinosus"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The sartorius from the anterior compartment, the gracilis from the medial compartment, and the semitendinosus from the posterior compartment all end side by side on the medial surface of the upper tibia. Their joined tendons are called the pes anserinus, meaning goose's foot, because of the way they spread out. Each comes from a different compartment, which makes this a useful landmark.","notes":["The sartorius belongs, but the adductor longus inserts on the linea aspera of the femur, and the semimembranosus inserts on the medial condyle of the tibia rather than joining the other two.","The biceps femoris inserts on the head of the fibula on the lateral side, and the adductor magnus stops on the femur above the knee. Neither belongs to this medial tibial group.","The rectus femoris and vastus medialis insert on the tibial tuberosity through the patellar ligament, on the front of the tibia, not its medial side. Both are also from the same compartment.","Correct. The sartorius, gracilis, and semitendinosus share an insertion on the medial tibia."],"comp":91,"competencyId":"w5-lab-thigh-gluteal"},{"id":"N-m4-hip-thigh-muscles-39","dok":1,"q":"Which quadriceps muscle, on the outer side of the thigh, is a common site for intramuscular injections, especially in infants?","a":"The vastus lateralis.","options":["Gracilis","Rectus femoris","Vastus lateralis","Vastus medialis"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The vastus lateralis is the large quadriceps muscle on the lateral side of the thigh, running from the greater trochanter and the lateral lip of the linea aspera to the tibial tuberosity. It is thick and well developed even in infants, so it is a standard injection site, especially for babies whose gluteal muscles are still small.","notes":["The gracilis is a thin strap on the inner thigh, far too narrow for injections. It would be the answer for the medial thigh muscle that crosses the knee.","The rectus femoris runs down the middle of the front of the thigh. It is sometimes used as an alternative site, but the outer thigh muscle is the standard choice. Choose rectus femoris for the quadriceps muscle that crosses the hip.","Correct. The vastus lateralis is a common injection site, especially in infants.","The vastus medialis is on the inner side of the thigh near the knee. Major vessels run along the medial thigh, which makes it a poor injection site. Pick it for the medial part of the quadriceps."],"comp":91,"competencyId":"w5-lab-thigh-gluteal"},{"id":"N-m4-hip-thigh-muscles-40","dok":3,"q":"A patient lies face down and bends the knee to pull the heel toward the buttock while the examiner pushes against the ankle. Which muscle group is being tested?","a":"The hamstrings.","options":["Hamstrings","Quadriceps","Adductors","Gluteal group"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Bringing the heel toward the buttock is flexion of the knee. The muscles that flex the knee against resistance are the hamstrings on the back of the thigh, which run from the ischial tuberosity to the tibia and fibula. Testing in the face-down position isolates this movement.","notes":["Correct. Knee flexion against resistance tests the hamstrings.","The quadriceps extend the knee, straightening it. They would be tested by asking the patient to kick the leg straight while sitting.","The adductors pull the thigh toward the midline. To test them, an examiner would ask the patient to squeeze the knees together.","The gluteal group acts at the hip, mainly extending and abducting the thigh. They do not cross the knee, so they cannot bring the heel toward the buttock."],"comp":91,"competencyId":"w5-lab-thigh-gluteal"},{"id":"N-m4-hip-thigh-muscles-41","dok":3,"q":"A doctor taps the patellar ligament just below the kneecap and the lower leg kicks forward. Which muscle group contracted to produce the kick?","a":"The quadriceps femoris.","options":["Quadriceps femoris","Hamstrings","Gastrocnemius and soleus","Adductors"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The patellar ligament is the lower continuation of the quadriceps tendon. Tapping it briefly stretches the quadriceps, which contracts in response and extends the knee, so the lower leg kicks forward. This is the knee jerk, or patellar, reflex.","notes":["Correct. The quadriceps contracts to extend the knee in the knee jerk.","The hamstrings flex the knee, which would pull the leg backward rather than forward. They are the antagonists of the muscle tested here.","The gastrocnemius and soleus attach to the heel through the calcaneal tendon. Pick them for the ankle reflex, tested by tapping the calcaneal tendon.","The adductors pull the thigh toward the midline and do not attach to the patellar ligament. Their contraction would not kick the leg forward."],"comp":91,"competencyId":"w5-lab-thigh-gluteal"},{"id":"N-m4-hip-thigh-muscles-42","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares the gluteus maximus and the gluteus medius?","a":"The maximus is superficial and mainly extends the hip; the medius lies partly beneath it and mainly abducts the thigh.","options":["Both insert on the lesser trochanter and adduct the thigh","Medius is superficial and extends; maximus is deeper and abducts","Maximus is superficial and extends; medius is deeper and abducts","Both lie deep to the gluteus minimus and flex the hip"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The gluteus maximus is the largest, most superficial gluteal muscle and the main hip extensor. The gluteus medius lies on the upper outer buttock, partly covered by the gluteus maximus, and inserts on the greater trochanter, where it abducts the thigh and keeps the pelvis level when you stand on one leg.","notes":["The lesser trochanter receives the iliopsoas, and adduction is done by the medial thigh muscles. The gluteus maximus and medius insert on the gluteal tuberosity, the IT band, and the greater trochanter instead.","This swaps the two muscles. The gluteus maximus is the large surface muscle that extends, and the gluteus medius is the abductor partly beneath it.","Correct. The maximus is superficial and extends; the medius is deeper and abducts.","The gluteus minimus is the deepest of the three, so the other two lie on top of it, not beneath it. The gluteus maximus and medius also extend and abduct rather than flex the hip."],"comp":91,"competencyId":"w5-lab-thigh-gluteal"}]},{"id":"m4-leg-muscles","title":"Leg and foot muscles","summary":"anterior, lateral and posterior leg compartments, calcaneal tendon","mw":"2026-11-02","tr":"2026-11-03","cards":[{"id":"L04399","dok":1,"q":"Which two muscles form the bulk of the calf and share the calcaneal (Achilles) tendon?","a":"The gastrocnemius and soleus.","options":["Gastrocnemius and soleus","Tibialis anterior and soleus","Tibialis posterior and gastrocnemius","Fibularis longus and brevis"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The gastrocnemius is the superficial two-headed calf muscle, and the soleus is the broad, flat muscle just deep to it. Both are in the posterior compartment of the leg, and both insert on the calcaneus through the calcaneal tendon. Pulling on the heel bone, they plantarflex the foot, pointing the toes down.","notes":["Correct. The gastrocnemius and soleus share the calcaneal tendon.","The tibialis anterior lies on the front of the leg beside the shin and dorsiflexes the foot. The soleus is right, but this pair mixes a front muscle with a calf muscle.","The tibialis posterior is a deep posterior muscle whose tendon passes behind the medial malleolus to the bones of the foot. Only the gastrocnemius in this pair uses the calcaneal tendon.","The fibularis longus and brevis lie on the lateral side of the leg and evert the foot. Their tendons pass behind the lateral malleolus rather than into the calcaneal tendon."],"lt":"t-w5-lab-leg-muscles","lid":"w5-lab-leg-muscles-r1","comp":92,"competencyId":"w5-lab-leg-muscles"},{"id":"L04400","dok":1,"q":"Which muscle of the anterior leg compartment is the main dorsiflexor of the foot?","a":"The tibialis anterior.","options":["Soleus","Tibialis posterior","Fibularis longus","Tibialis anterior"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The tibialis anterior lies along the lateral side of the shin in the anterior compartment. It runs from the tibia down across the front of the ankle to the medial side of the foot, so it pulls the top of the foot up toward the shin, which is dorsiflexion. It also inverts the foot.","notes":["The soleus is a calf muscle of the posterior compartment that plantarflexes the foot. It does the opposite of dorsiflexion and is the answer for pointing the toes.","The tibialis posterior is a deep muscle of the posterior compartment, behind the tibia. It plantarflexes and inverts the foot, so it fits a question about inversion from the back of the leg.","The fibularis longus lies in the lateral compartment and everts the foot. It would be the answer for turning the sole outward.","Correct. The tibialis anterior is the main dorsiflexor of the foot."],"lt":"t-w5-lab-leg-muscles","lid":"w5-lab-leg-muscles-r2","comp":92,"competencyId":"w5-lab-leg-muscles"},{"id":"L04401","dok":1,"q":"Which muscles of the lateral leg compartment evert the foot?","a":"The fibularis (peroneus) longus and brevis.","options":["Tibialis anterior and posterior","Flexor digitorum and hallucis longus","Gastrocnemius and soleus","Fibularis longus and brevis"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The fibularis longus and fibularis brevis run down the lateral side of the leg along the fibula, and their tendons pass behind the lateral malleolus into the foot. From that lateral path they pull the sole of the foot outward, which is eversion. Some texts call them the peroneus longus and brevis.","notes":["The tibialis anterior and tibialis posterior both invert the foot, turning the sole inward. Pick this pair for a question about inversion, the opposite of eversion.","The flexor digitorum longus and flexor hallucis longus are deep posterior muscles that flex the toes. They would be the answer for curling the toes, including the big toe.","The gastrocnemius and soleus are the calf muscles that plantarflex the foot through the calcaneal tendon. They are the answer for standing on tiptoe.","Correct. The fibularis longus and brevis are the lateral compartment evertors."],"lt":"t-w5-lab-leg-muscles","lid":"w5-lab-leg-muscles-r3","comp":92,"competencyId":"w5-lab-leg-muscles"},{"id":"L04402","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares the actions of the anterior and posterior leg compartments?","a":"The anterior compartment dorsiflexes the foot; the posterior compartment plantarflexes it.","options":["Anterior flexes the knee; posterior extends it","Anterior dorsiflexes the foot; posterior plantarflexes it","Anterior everts the foot; posterior inverts it","Anterior plantarflexes the foot; posterior dorsiflexes it"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Muscles in front of the ankle, in the anterior compartment, pull the top of the foot up toward the shin, which is dorsiflexion. Muscles behind the ankle, in the posterior compartment, pull the heel up and point the toes down, which is plantarflexion. The two compartments are antagonists across the ankle, much as the quadriceps and hamstrings are across the knee.","notes":["Flexing and extending the knee are thigh actions, done by the hamstrings and quadriceps. The leg compartments act mainly at the ankle and toes.","Correct. The anterior compartment dorsiflexes and the posterior compartment plantarflexes.","Eversion is the job of the lateral compartment, the fibularis muscles. This option skips the lateral compartment, which is the answer for turning the sole outward.","This reverses the two compartments. A muscle in front of the ankle lifts the top of the foot, so the anterior compartment must be the dorsiflexor group."],"lt":"t-w5-lab-leg-muscles","lid":"w5-lab-leg-muscles-r4","comp":92,"competencyId":"w5-lab-leg-muscles"},{"id":"L04403","dok":3,"q":"Standing on tiptoe lifts the heel off the ground. Which tendon carries the pull of the calf muscles to the heel bone?","a":"Calcaneal tendon.","options":["Calcaneal tendon","Patellar ligament","Iliotibial (IT) band","Tibialis posterior tendon"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Rising onto your toes is plantarflexion, done mainly by the gastrocnemius and soleus in the posterior compartment of the leg. Both muscles end in the calcaneal (Achilles) tendon, the thickest tendon in the body, which attaches to the calcaneus, the heel bone. When the calf contracts, this tendon pulls the heel up.","notes":["Correct. The gastrocnemius and soleus lift the heel through the calcaneal tendon.","The patellar ligament runs from the patella to the tibial tuberosity and carries the pull of the quadriceps. It is the answer for a question about straightening the knee, not lifting the heel.","The iliotibial (IT) band is a thick band of fascia on the lateral thigh, running from the iliac crest to the lateral condyle of the tibia. It is pulled on by the tensor fasciae latae and gluteus maximus and does not reach the heel.","The tibialis posterior tendon passes behind the medial malleolus to the bones of the foot and helps invert the foot. It does not attach to the heel bone, so it fits a question about the deep posterior muscles."],"lt":"t-w5-lab-leg-muscles","lid":"w5-lab-leg-muscles-r5","comp":92,"competencyId":"w5-lab-leg-muscles"},{"id":"N-m4-leg-muscles-1","dok":1,"q":"Which muscle runs along the lateral side of the tibial shaft and is the most superficial muscle of the anterior leg?","a":"The tibialis anterior.","options":["Tibialis posterior","Extensor digitorum longus","Tibialis anterior","Fibularis longus"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The tibialis anterior lies right against the lateral surface of the tibia, the shin bone, and you can feel it tighten just outside the sharp anterior border of the tibia when you lift your toes. It arises from the upper lateral tibia and its tendon crosses the front of the ankle to the medial side of the foot.","notes":["The tibialis posterior is the deepest muscle on the back of the leg, lying on the interosseous membrane. Pick it for the deepest muscle of the posterior compartment.","The extensor digitorum longus is also in the anterior compartment, but it lies lateral to the tibialis anterior, closer to the fibula. Choose it for the muscle that extends the four lateral toes.","Correct. The tibialis anterior lies along the lateral side of the tibia on the front of the leg.","The fibularis longus lies on the lateral side of the leg along the fibula. It would be the answer for the larger muscle of the lateral compartment."],"comp":92,"competencyId":"w5-lab-leg-muscles"},{"id":"N-m4-leg-muscles-2","dok":1,"q":"What two actions does the tibialis anterior perform at the foot?","a":"Dorsiflexion and inversion.","options":["Dorsiflexion and inversion","Plantar flexion and inversion","Plantar flexion and eversion","Dorsiflexion and eversion"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The tibialis anterior runs down the front of the leg, so it lifts the top of the foot toward the shin, which is dorsiflexion. Its tendon then crosses to the medial side of the foot and attaches to the medial cuneiform and the base of the first metatarsal, so it also pulls the sole to face inward, which is inversion.","notes":["Correct. The tibialis anterior dorsiflexes and inverts the foot.","Plantar flexion with inversion describes the tibialis posterior in the deep posterior compartment. The tibialis anterior is on the front of the leg, so it dorsiflexes rather than plantar flexes.","Plantar flexion with eversion is the work of the fibularis longus and brevis in the lateral compartment. Pick this pair for those muscles.","Dorsiflexion with eversion describes the fibularis tertius, the small anterior compartment muscle that inserts on the lateral side of the foot. The tibialis anterior pulls on the medial side, so it inverts."],"comp":92,"competencyId":"w5-lab-leg-muscles"},{"id":"N-m4-leg-muscles-3","dok":1,"q":"What is the main shared action of the muscles in the anterior compartment of the leg?","a":"Dorsiflexion of the foot (and extension of the toes).","options":["Knee flexion","Dorsiflexion","Plantar flexion","Eversion"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The anterior compartment holds the tibialis anterior, extensor digitorum longus, extensor hallucis longus, and fibularis tertius. All of their tendons cross the front of the ankle, so they lift the top of the foot toward the shin, which is dorsiflexion. The two long extensors also straighten the toes.","notes":["Knee flexion is done by the hamstrings and the gastrocnemius. The anterior leg muscles arise below the knee and do not cross it.","Correct. The anterior compartment muscles dorsiflex the foot.","Plantar flexion points the foot downward and is done by the posterior compartment, especially the gastrocnemius and soleus. It is the opposite of the anterior group's action.","Eversion turns the sole outward and is the main job of the lateral compartment, the fibularis longus and brevis. Only the small fibularis tertius in the anterior group helps with it."],"comp":92,"competencyId":"w5-lab-leg-muscles"},{"id":"N-m4-leg-muscles-4","dok":1,"q":"Which muscle extends the great toe and helps dorsiflex the foot?","a":"The extensor hallucis longus.","options":["Extensor hallucis longus","Fibularis tertius","Flexor hallucis longus","Extensor digitorum longus"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Hallux is the anatomical name for the great toe, so a muscle with hallucis in its name acts on the great toe. The extensor hallucis longus lies in the anterior compartment, between the tibialis anterior and the extensor digitorum longus, and its tendon runs over the top of the foot to the great toe. Because it crosses the front of the ankle, it also helps dorsiflex the foot.","notes":["Correct. The extensor hallucis longus extends the great toe.","The fibularis tertius is a small anterior compartment muscle that inserts on the fifth metatarsal. It dorsiflexes and everts the foot but does not move any toe.","The flexor hallucis longus also acts on the great toe, but it lies in the deep posterior compartment and flexes the toe, curling it down. It is the antagonist of the correct answer.","The extensor digitorum longus extends the other four toes, numbers two through five. Pick it if the question asked about the lateral four toes."],"comp":92,"competencyId":"w5-lab-leg-muscles"},{"id":"N-m4-leg-muscles-5","dok":1,"q":"Which muscle extends toes two through five and helps dorsiflex the foot?","a":"The extensor digitorum longus.","options":["Flexor digitorum longus","Extensor hallucis longus","Tibialis anterior","Extensor digitorum longus"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Digitorum refers to the digits, here the four lateral toes. The extensor digitorum longus lies in the anterior compartment along the fibula, and its tendon splits on the top of the foot into four slips, one for each of toes two through five. Pulling on them straightens those toes, and because the muscle crosses the front of the ankle it also dorsiflexes the foot.","notes":["The flexor digitorum longus reaches the same four toes but from the deep posterior compartment, so it curls them down. It is the antagonist of the correct answer.","The extensor hallucis longus extends only the great toe. Pick it when the question names the hallux or big toe.","The tibialis anterior dorsiflexes and inverts the foot but ends on the medial cuneiform and first metatarsal. It does not move the toes.","Correct. The extensor digitorum longus extends the four lateral toes."],"comp":92,"competencyId":"w5-lab-leg-muscles"},{"id":"N-m4-leg-muscles-6","dok":2,"q":"Which muscle carries the fibularis name but belongs to the anterior compartment of the leg?","a":"The fibularis tertius.","options":["Tibialis anterior","Fibularis longus","Fibularis tertius","Fibularis brevis"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The fibularis tertius is a small muscle that looks like a fifth slip of the extensor digitorum longus. It lies in the anterior compartment and inserts on the top of the base of the fifth metatarsal, so it dorsiflexes and everts the foot. The other two fibularis muscles sit in the lateral compartment.","notes":["The tibialis anterior is in the anterior compartment, but it does not carry the fibularis name. It would be the answer for the main dorsiflexor and invertor.","The fibularis longus is the larger, more superficial muscle of the lateral compartment. Pick it for the lateral muscle whose tendon crosses the sole of the foot.","Correct. The fibularis tertius lies in the anterior compartment despite its name.","The fibularis brevis is the smaller, deeper muscle of the lateral compartment and inserts on the base of the fifth metatarsal. Choose it for the short fibularis muscle in the lateral group."],"comp":92,"competencyId":"w5-lab-leg-muscles"},{"id":"N-m4-leg-muscles-7","dok":1,"q":"Which muscles make up the lateral compartment of the leg?","a":"The fibularis longus and fibularis brevis.","options":["Gastrocnemius and soleus","Tibialis posterior and flexor hallucis longus","Tibialis anterior and fibularis tertius","Fibularis longus and brevis"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The lateral compartment lies along the outer surface of the fibula and holds just two muscles, the fibularis longus and the fibularis brevis. These muscles may also be called the peroneus longus and brevis. Their tendons pass behind the lateral malleolus and they evert the foot.","notes":["The gastrocnemius and soleus form the superficial posterior compartment, the calf. Choose them for the muscles that join the calcaneal tendon.","The tibialis posterior and flexor hallucis longus are in the deep posterior compartment. They would be the answer for muscles whose tendons pass behind the medial malleolus.","The tibialis anterior and fibularis tertius are in the anterior compartment and dorsiflex the foot. Pick this pair for front-of-leg muscles.","Correct. The fibularis longus and brevis form the lateral compartment."],"comp":92,"competencyId":"w5-lab-leg-muscles"},{"id":"N-m4-leg-muscles-8","dok":1,"q":"What is the main action of the lateral compartment muscles of the leg?","a":"Eversion of the foot.","options":["Toe flexion","Dorsiflexion","Eversion","Inversion"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The fibularis longus and brevis run down the outer side of the leg and their tendons hook behind the lateral malleolus before reaching the foot. Pulling from the lateral side, they turn the sole of the foot outward, which is eversion. Because the tendons pass behind the ankle, they also help plantar flex.","notes":["Toe flexion is done by the flexor digitorum longus and flexor hallucis longus in the deep posterior compartment. The fibularis muscles end on the metatarsals and cuneiform, not on the toes.","Dorsiflexion lifts the top of the foot and is the job of the anterior compartment. The fibularis tendons pass behind the ankle, so they plantar flex instead.","Correct. The lateral compartment everts the foot.","Inversion turns the sole inward and is done by the tibialis anterior and tibialis posterior, which pull on the medial side of the foot. It is the opposite of the lateral group's action."],"comp":92,"competencyId":"w5-lab-leg-muscles"},{"id":"N-m4-leg-muscles-9","dok":2,"q":"The tendons of which two muscles curve behind the lateral malleolus?","a":"The fibularis longus and fibularis brevis.","options":["Tibialis posterior and flexor digitorum longus","Gastrocnemius and soleus","Tibialis anterior and extensor hallucis longus","Fibularis longus and brevis"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The lateral malleolus is the bump at the lower end of the fibula on the outside of the ankle. The tendons of the lateral compartment, the fibularis longus and brevis, run down the fibula and turn forward just behind this bump, held in place by the fibular retinacula. Using the malleolus as a pulley lets them evert the foot.","notes":["The tibialis posterior and flexor digitorum longus tendons curve behind the medial malleolus on the inner side of the ankle. Pick them for the medial side.","The gastrocnemius and soleus end in the calcaneal tendon, which attaches to the back of the heel. They pass between the two malleoli rather than around either one.","The tibialis anterior and extensor hallucis longus tendons cross the front of the ankle under the extensor retinacula. Choose them for tendons on the top of the ankle.","Correct. The fibularis tendons pass behind the lateral malleolus."],"comp":92,"competencyId":"w5-lab-leg-muscles"},{"id":"N-m4-leg-muscles-10","dok":1,"q":"Where does the fibularis brevis insert?","a":"On the base (tuberosity) of the fifth metatarsal.","options":["Base of the fifth metatarsal","Navicular bone","Calcaneus","Base of the first metatarsal"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The fibularis brevis arises from the lower lateral fibula, passes behind the lateral malleolus, and runs forward along the outer edge of the foot. It ends on the tuberosity at the base of the fifth metatarsal, the bump you can feel halfway along the lateral border of the foot. Pulling there everts the foot.","notes":["Correct. The fibularis brevis inserts on the base of the fifth metatarsal.","The navicular bone receives the main insertion of the tibialis posterior on the medial side of the foot. Choose it for the deepest posterior muscle.","The calcaneus is the heel bone and receives the calcaneal tendon of the gastrocnemius and soleus. It would be the answer for the calf muscles.","The base of the first metatarsal, together with the medial cuneiform, receives the fibularis longus and the tibialis anterior. Pick it for the fibularis longus, whose tendon crosses the sole."],"comp":92,"competencyId":"w5-lab-leg-muscles"},{"id":"N-m4-leg-muscles-11","dok":2,"q":"Which leg muscle has a tendon that runs under the sole from lateral to medial and inserts near the same bones as the tibialis anterior?","a":"The fibularis longus.","options":["Fibularis brevis","Flexor hallucis longus","Tibialis posterior","Fibularis longus"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The fibularis longus tendon passes behind the lateral malleolus, turns under the cuboid bone, and crosses the sole to the medial side, where it inserts on the medial cuneiform and the base of the first metatarsal. The tibialis anterior reaches the same bones from above. Together the two tendons form a sling under the foot that helps support its arches.","notes":["The fibularis brevis stays on the lateral side and inserts on the base of the fifth metatarsal. Pick it for the lateral muscle that does not cross the sole.","The flexor hallucis longus tendon runs along the sole to the great toe, but it enters from behind the medial malleolus and ends on the toe, not on the cuneiform and metatarsal base.","The tibialis posterior enters the foot on the medial side, behind the medial malleolus, and inserts mainly on the navicular. It does not cross the sole from the lateral side.","Correct. The fibularis longus tendon crosses the sole to the medial side."],"comp":92,"competencyId":"w5-lab-leg-muscles"},{"id":"N-m4-leg-muscles-12","dok":1,"q":"The two heads of the gastrocnemius arise from which structures?","a":"The medial and lateral condyles of the femur.","options":["Head of fibula and soleal line","Ischial tuberosity and linea aspera","Medial and lateral tibial condyles","Medial and lateral femoral condyles"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The gastrocnemius has a medial head and a lateral head that begin on the back of the medial and lateral condyles of the femur, just above the knee joint. The two bellies form the bulge of the calf and join into the calcaneal tendon. Because they start on the femur, the gastrocnemius crosses the knee as well as the ankle.","notes":["The head of the fibula and the soleal line of the tibia are where the soleus arises. Pick them for the muscle lying deep to the gastrocnemius.","The ischial tuberosity and linea aspera are origins for the hamstrings, including the long and short heads of the biceps femoris. Choose them for posterior thigh muscles.","The tibial condyles lie just below the knee joint. If the gastrocnemius started there it would not cross the knee, so this is the mistake of placing it one bone too low.","Correct. The gastrocnemius heads arise from the femoral condyles."],"comp":92,"competencyId":"w5-lab-leg-muscles"},{"id":"N-m4-leg-muscles-13","dok":2,"q":"Because the gastrocnemius crosses both the knee and the ankle, which pair of actions can it perform?","a":"Flexion of the knee and plantar flexion of the foot.","options":["Knee flexion and dorsiflexion","Knee extension and dorsiflexion","Knee flexion and plantar flexion","Knee extension and plantar flexion"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The gastrocnemius starts on the back of the femoral condyles and ends on the calcaneus through the calcaneal tendon. Crossing the back of the knee, it helps bend the knee. Crossing the back of the ankle, it pulls the heel up and points the foot down, which is plantar flexion.","notes":["Dorsiflexion lifts the top of the foot and is the job of the anterior leg muscles, such as the tibialis anterior. The gastrocnemius pulls on the heel, which points the foot down.","This pair describes front-of-limb actions. Extending the knee and dorsiflexing the ankle come from the quadriceps and the anterior leg compartment, not from the calf.","Correct. The gastrocnemius flexes the knee and plantar flexes the foot.","Knee extension is done by the quadriceps on the front of the thigh. The gastrocnemius lies behind the knee, so it bends the knee rather than straightening it."],"comp":92,"competencyId":"w5-lab-leg-muscles"},{"id":"N-m4-leg-muscles-14","dok":1,"q":"Which calf muscle lies directly deep to the gastrocnemius?","a":"The soleus.","options":["Plantaris","Soleus","Tibialis posterior","Fibularis longus"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The soleus is a broad, flat muscle that lies just under the gastrocnemius. It arises from the upper fibula and the soleal line of the tibia and joins the gastrocnemius in the calcaneal tendon. Its flat shape is where the name comes from, since it resembles the sole fish.","notes":["The plantaris is a small muscle with a long, thin tendon. Its tendon runs between the gastrocnemius and soleus, but the muscle itself is not the broad layer under the gastrocnemius.","Correct. The soleus lies directly deep to the gastrocnemius.","The tibialis posterior is in the deep posterior compartment, beneath the soleus, lying on the interosseous membrane. Pick it for the deepest muscle on the back of the leg.","The fibularis longus is on the lateral side of the leg, not in the calf. Choose it for the lateral compartment muscle that everts the foot."],"comp":92,"competencyId":"w5-lab-leg-muscles"},{"id":"N-m4-leg-muscles-15","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares the soleus and the gastrocnemius?","a":"The soleus arises below the knee and acts only at the ankle; the gastrocnemius arises above the knee and acts at both joints.","options":["Both cross only the ankle and dorsiflex the foot","Both cross the knee and insert on the tibial tuberosity","Gastrocnemius crosses only the ankle; soleus crosses knee and ankle","Soleus crosses only the ankle; gastrocnemius crosses knee and ankle"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Both muscles join the calcaneal tendon and plantar flex the foot. The difference is where they begin. The gastrocnemius starts on the femoral condyles, above the knee, so it also flexes the knee. The soleus starts on the tibia and fibula, below the knee, so it acts only at the ankle.","notes":["Both calf muscles plantar flex, pointing the foot down. Dorsiflexion is the job of the anterior compartment, and the gastrocnemius also crosses the knee.","The tibial tuberosity receives the patellar ligament from the quadriceps. Both calf muscles insert on the calcaneus, and only the gastrocnemius crosses the knee.","This reverses the two muscles. The gastrocnemius is the one that reaches up to the femur, and the soleus starts on the leg bones.","Correct. The soleus crosses only the ankle, while the gastrocnemius crosses both."],"comp":92,"competencyId":"w5-lab-leg-muscles"},{"id":"N-m4-leg-muscles-16","dok":3,"q":"A gym-goer does calf raises while seated with the knees bent at 90 degrees. Which muscle does most of the work of plantar flexion in this position?","a":"The soleus.","options":["Fibularis tertius","Tibialis anterior","Soleus","Gastrocnemius"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"With the knee bent, the gastrocnemius is already shortened across the back of the knee because it arises from the femur. A shortened muscle cannot pull as hard, so it adds little force at the ankle. The soleus starts below the knee on the tibia and fibula, so knee position does not affect it, and it carries most of the load in a seated calf raise.","notes":["The fibularis tertius dorsiflexes and everts the foot from the anterior compartment. It cannot raise the heel.","The tibialis anterior dorsiflexes the foot, lifting the toes. It works against a calf raise rather than producing it.","Correct. With the knee flexed, the soleus is the main plantar flexor.","The gastrocnemius is the main calf muscle in a standing calf raise with straight knees. With the knees bent it is slack across the knee, so it contributes less here."],"comp":92,"competencyId":"w5-lab-leg-muscles"},{"id":"N-m4-leg-muscles-17","dok":1,"q":"The calcaneal (Achilles) tendon attaches which muscles to which bone?","a":"The gastrocnemius and soleus to the calcaneus.","options":["Gastrocnemius and soleus to the talus","Tibialis posterior and soleus to the calcaneus","Quadriceps to the tibial tuberosity","Gastrocnemius and soleus to the calcaneus"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The calcaneal tendon is the thick tendon at the back of the ankle, the strongest tendon in the body. It forms where the gastrocnemius and the soleus merge in the lower calf, and it anchors to the back of the calcaneus, the heel bone. Pulling on the heel lifts it and points the foot down.","notes":["The talus sits above the calcaneus and forms the ankle joint with the tibia and fibula. No muscle tendon anchors to it, so it is the wrong bone for the calcaneal tendon.","The tibialis posterior is a deep muscle whose tendon passes behind the medial malleolus to the navicular. It does not join the calcaneal tendon.","The quadriceps tendon and patellar ligament attach to the tibial tuberosity at the front of the knee. That is the knee's tendon system, not the heel's.","Correct. The calcaneal tendon joins the gastrocnemius and soleus to the calcaneus."],"comp":92,"competencyId":"w5-lab-leg-muscles"},{"id":"N-m4-leg-muscles-18","dok":3,"q":"During a basketball game a player feels a snap at the back of the heel and afterward cannot rise onto the toes on that side. Which structure was most likely torn?","a":"Calcaneal tendon.","options":["Patellar ligament","Extensor retinaculum","Calcaneal tendon","Iliotibial (IT) band"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Rising onto the toes is plantar flexion, and the strongest plantar flexors, the gastrocnemius and soleus, act through the calcaneal tendon at the back of the heel. When that tendon ruptures, the calf can no longer pull on the calcaneus, so the heel cannot be lifted against body weight. The location of the snap fits as well.","notes":["The patellar ligament is at the front of the knee and carries the pull of the quadriceps. A tear there would stop the person from straightening the knee, not rising onto the toes.","The extensor retinaculum is a band across the front of the ankle that holds down the dorsiflexor tendons. Damage there would not stop plantar flexion.","Correct. A snap at the back of the heel with loss of tiptoe rising points to the calcaneal tendon.","The IT band runs down the lateral thigh to the lateral condyle of the tibia. It is nowhere near the heel and does not raise it."],"comp":92,"competencyId":"w5-lab-leg-muscles"},{"id":"N-m4-leg-muscles-19","dok":1,"q":"Which small posterior leg muscle has a short belly behind the knee and a very long, thin tendon running down between the gastrocnemius and soleus?","a":"The plantaris.","options":["Plantaris","Soleus","Fibularis brevis","Flexor hallucis longus"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The plantaris arises just above the lateral femoral condyle, has a small belly behind the knee, and then becomes one of the longest, thinnest tendons in the body. The tendon runs down between the gastrocnemius and soleus to reach the calcaneus. It is a weak helper in plantar flexion and knee flexion, and it is sometimes absent.","notes":["Correct. The plantaris has a small belly and a very long, thin tendon.","The soleus is a broad, flat muscle with a large belly in the lower calf. Pick it for the muscle lying directly under the gastrocnemius.","The fibularis brevis is in the lateral compartment along the lower fibula. It would be the answer for the muscle that inserts on the base of the fifth metatarsal.","The flexor hallucis longus is a deep posterior muscle that arises from the fibula and ends on the great toe. Choose it for the muscle that flexes the hallux."],"comp":92,"competencyId":"w5-lab-leg-muscles"},{"id":"N-m4-leg-muscles-20","dok":1,"q":"Which three deep posterior leg muscles send their tendons behind the medial malleolus into the foot?","a":"Tibialis posterior, flexor digitorum longus, flexor hallucis longus","options":["Gastrocnemius, soleus, plantaris","Fibularis longus, fibularis brevis, fibularis tertius","Tibialis posterior, flexor digitorum longus, flexor hallucis longus","Tibialis anterior, extensor digitorum longus, extensor hallucis longus"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The posterior leg has two layers. The superficial layer holds the gastrocnemius, soleus, and plantaris, which end on the calcaneus. Beneath them, the deep layer holds the tibialis posterior and the two long toe flexors, the flexor digitorum longus and flexor hallucis longus. The tendons of these three pass behind the medial malleolus into the foot.","notes":["The gastrocnemius, soleus, and plantaris form the superficial posterior compartment, the calf. Their tendons end on the calcaneus rather than passing behind the medial malleolus, so pick them for the muscles that act through or beside the calcaneal tendon.","This mixes compartments. The fibularis longus and brevis are lateral, but the fibularis tertius is anterior. None of the three is in the posterior compartment.","Correct. These three deep posterior muscles send their tendons behind the medial malleolus into the foot.","These are the main anterior compartment muscles, which dorsiflex the foot and extend the toes. They are the toe extensors, not the flexors."],"comp":92,"competencyId":"w5-lab-leg-muscles"},{"id":"N-m4-leg-muscles-21","dok":1,"q":"Which muscle is the deepest muscle on the back of the leg and both inverts and plantar flexes the foot?","a":"The tibialis posterior.","options":["Soleus","Fibularis longus","Tibialis anterior","Tibialis posterior"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The tibialis posterior lies on the interosseous membrane between the tibia and fibula, deeper than any other posterior leg muscle. Its tendon passes behind the medial malleolus and attaches mainly to the navicular on the medial side of the foot. Pulling from behind the ankle and from the medial side, it plantar flexes and inverts the foot, and it helps support the medial arch.","notes":["The soleus plantar flexes but is in the superficial posterior layer, under the gastrocnemius, and does not invert. Choose it for the flat calf muscle deep to the gastrocnemius.","The fibularis longus plantar flexes but everts the foot and lies in the lateral compartment. It is the antagonist of the correct answer for inversion.","The tibialis anterior also inverts the foot, but it lies on the front of the leg and dorsiflexes. Pick it for the superficial muscle beside the shin.","Correct. The tibialis posterior is the deepest posterior muscle and inverts and plantar flexes."],"comp":92,"competencyId":"w5-lab-leg-muscles"},{"id":"N-m4-leg-muscles-22","dok":1,"q":"Which muscle flexes the great toe?","a":"The flexor hallucis longus.","options":["Flexor hallucis longus","Flexor digitorum longus","Extensor hallucis longus","Tibialis posterior"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The flexor hallucis longus arises from the back of the fibula in the deep posterior compartment. Its tendon passes behind the medial malleolus and runs along the sole to the distal phalanx of the great toe, so it curls the hallux down. This gives the strong push-off from the great toe in walking and running.","notes":["Correct. The flexor hallucis longus flexes the great toe.","The flexor digitorum longus flexes the other four toes, numbers two through five. Pick it when the question asks about the lateral toes.","The extensor hallucis longus acts on the same toe from the front of the leg and lifts it rather than curling it. It is the antagonist of the correct answer.","The tibialis posterior is in the same deep compartment but ends on the navicular and other tarsal bones. It inverts and plantar flexes the foot without moving the toes."],"comp":92,"competencyId":"w5-lab-leg-muscles"},{"id":"N-m4-leg-muscles-23","dok":1,"q":"Which muscle flexes toes two through five?","a":"The flexor digitorum longus.","options":["Flexor digitorum longus","Extensor digitorum longus","Flexor hallucis longus","Fibularis brevis"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The flexor digitorum longus arises from the back of the tibia in the deep posterior compartment. Its tendon passes behind the medial malleolus, enters the sole, and splits into four tendons that reach the distal phalanges of toes two through five. Pulling on them curls those toes downward.","notes":["Correct. The flexor digitorum longus flexes the four lateral toes.","The extensor digitorum longus reaches the same four toes from the anterior compartment and straightens them. It is the antagonist of the correct answer.","The flexor hallucis longus flexes only the great toe. Pick it when the question names the hallux.","The fibularis brevis is a lateral compartment muscle that inserts on the fifth metatarsal and everts the foot. It does not reach the toes."],"comp":92,"competencyId":"w5-lab-leg-muscles"},{"id":"N-m4-leg-muscles-24","dok":2,"q":"Several tendons pass behind the medial malleolus. Which tendon lies closest to the malleolus, in front of the others?","a":"The tibialis posterior tendon.","options":["Tibialis posterior","Flexor digitorum longus","Flexor hallucis longus","Fibularis longus"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Behind the medial malleolus, the structures line up in a set order from front to back: the tibialis posterior tendon, the flexor digitorum longus tendon, the posterior tibial artery and veins, the tibial nerve, and finally the flexor hallucis longus tendon. Students often remember this as Tom, Dick, And Very Nervous Harry. The tibialis posterior tendon sits in a groove right against the malleolus.","notes":["Correct. The tibialis posterior tendon lies closest to the medial malleolus.","The flexor digitorum longus tendon is second in line, just behind the tibialis posterior. It would be the answer if the question asked which tendon comes next.","The flexor hallucis longus tendon is last in line, behind the artery, veins, and tibial nerve. Pick it for the most posterior structure behind the medial malleolus.","The fibularis longus tendon passes behind the lateral malleolus on the outer side of the ankle. It does not travel on the medial side at all."],"comp":92,"competencyId":"w5-lab-leg-muscles"},{"id":"N-m4-leg-muscles-25","dok":2,"q":"Which muscle acts as the antagonist of the fibularis longus for movement of the sole inward and outward?","a":"The tibialis anterior.","options":["Fibularis tertius","Extensor digitorum longus","Fibularis brevis","Tibialis anterior"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The fibularis longus everts the foot, turning the sole outward. An antagonist produces the opposite movement, so the answer must invert the foot, turning the sole inward. The tibialis anterior inserts on the medial side of the foot and inverts it, making it the antagonist for this movement.","notes":["The fibularis tertius dorsiflexes and everts the foot, so it also helps with eversion. Pick it for the fibularis muscle in the anterior compartment.","The extensor digitorum longus extends the lateral four toes and dorsiflexes the foot. It does not invert the foot, so it cannot oppose eversion.","The fibularis brevis also everts the foot, so it works with the fibularis longus as a synergist rather than against it.","Correct. The tibialis anterior inverts the foot and opposes the eversion of the fibularis longus."],"comp":92,"competencyId":"w5-lab-leg-muscles"},{"id":"N-m4-leg-muscles-26","dok":2,"q":"Which structure separates the anterior compartment of the leg from the deep posterior compartment?","a":"The interosseous membrane.","options":["Interosseous membrane","Calcaneal tendon","Plantar aponeurosis","Extensor retinaculum"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The interosseous membrane is a sheet of dense connective tissue stretched between the tibia and fibula along their length. Together with the two bones, it forms a wall between the front and back of the leg. Muscles of the anterior compartment arise from its front surface and the tibialis posterior arises from its back surface.","notes":["Correct. The interosseous membrane divides the anterior and posterior compartments.","The calcaneal tendon attaches the calf muscles to the heel. It lies at the back of the ankle and does not divide compartments.","The plantar aponeurosis is a thick sheet of fascia on the sole of the foot. Choose it for the structure that supports the longitudinal arch.","The extensor retinaculum is a band across the front of the ankle that holds the anterior tendons in place. Pick it for the structure that keeps those tendons from bowing out."],"comp":92,"competencyId":"w5-lab-leg-muscles"},{"id":"N-m4-leg-muscles-27","dok":1,"q":"What holds the tendons of the anterior leg muscles against the front of the ankle so they do not bow outward when the foot dorsiflexes?","a":"The superior and inferior extensor retinacula.","options":["Fibular retinacula","Plantar aponeurosis","Flexor retinaculum","Extensor retinacula"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"A retinaculum is a band of thickened deep fascia that holds tendons in place where they cross a joint. At the front of the ankle, the superior and inferior extensor retinacula strap down the tendons of the tibialis anterior, extensor hallucis longus, extensor digitorum longus, and fibularis tertius. Without them, the tendons would lift away from the ankle like a bowstring.","notes":["The fibular retinacula hold the fibularis longus and brevis tendons behind the lateral malleolus. Pick them for the band on the outside of the ankle.","The plantar aponeurosis is the thick fascia on the sole of the foot. It supports the arches rather than holding tendons at the front of the ankle.","The flexor retinaculum spans the medial side of the ankle from the medial malleolus to the calcaneus. Choose it for the band that roofs the tarsal tunnel.","Correct. The extensor retinacula hold the anterior tendons at the ankle."],"comp":92,"competencyId":"w5-lab-leg-muscles"},{"id":"N-m4-leg-muscles-28","dok":1,"q":"Which bands hold the fibularis longus and brevis tendons in place behind the lateral malleolus?","a":"The fibular (peroneal) retinacula.","options":["Fibular retinacula","Flexor retinaculum","Extensor retinacula","Interosseous membrane"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The fibularis tendons curve behind the lateral malleolus, using it as a pulley. The superior and inferior fibular retinacula, also called the peroneal retinacula, hold these tendons in their groove on the outside of the ankle so they stay in place as the foot moves.","notes":["Correct. The fibular retinacula hold the fibularis tendons.","The flexor retinaculum lies on the medial side of the ankle and covers the tendons of the deep posterior muscles. It is the mirror image of the correct answer on the opposite side.","The extensor retinacula cross the front of the ankle and hold the anterior compartment tendons. Pick them for the tendons of the tibialis anterior and the toe extensors.","The interosseous membrane joins the tibia and fibula along the leg. It separates compartments rather than holding tendons at the ankle."],"comp":92,"competencyId":"w5-lab-leg-muscles"},{"id":"N-m4-leg-muscles-29","dok":2,"q":"On the medial side of the ankle, which band forms the roof of a tunnel that carries the deep posterior tendons, the posterior tibial vessels, and the tibial nerve?","a":"The flexor retinaculum.","options":["Flexor retinaculum","Superior extensor retinaculum","Superior fibular retinaculum","Plantar aponeurosis"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The flexor retinaculum stretches from the medial malleolus to the calcaneus. It covers the passage called the tarsal tunnel, through which the tendons of the tibialis posterior, flexor digitorum longus, and flexor hallucis longus travel into the sole, along with the posterior tibial artery and veins and the tibial nerve.","notes":["Correct. The flexor retinaculum roofs the tarsal tunnel on the medial ankle.","The superior extensor retinaculum lies across the front of the leg just above the ankle. Pick it for the band holding down the dorsiflexor tendons.","The superior fibular retinaculum lies on the lateral ankle behind the lateral malleolus. It holds the fibularis tendons, not the deep posterior ones.","The plantar aponeurosis spreads across the sole from the calcaneus toward the toes. It supports the arch but does not form a tunnel at the medial ankle."],"comp":92,"competencyId":"w5-lab-leg-muscles"},{"id":"N-m4-leg-muscles-35","dok":3,"q":"A patient's toes drag on the ground when he walks, so he lifts the knee high with each step. Weakness of which group of leg muscles best explains this?","a":"The anterior compartment muscles (the dorsiflexors).","options":["Anterior compartment","Deep posterior compartment","Lateral compartment","Superficial posterior compartment"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"To clear the ground while the leg swings forward, the foot must be held in dorsiflexion, with the toes lifted. The anterior compartment muscles, led by the tibialis anterior, do this. When they are weak the foot hangs down, a problem called foot drop, and the person lifts the knee unusually high so the toes do not catch.","notes":["Correct. Weak dorsiflexors in the anterior compartment cause the toes to drag.","The deep posterior compartment inverts the foot and flexes the toes. Weakness there would affect push-off and the medial arch rather than lifting of the toes.","The lateral compartment everts the foot. Weakness there makes the ankle prone to rolling inward but does not stop the toes from lifting.","The superficial posterior compartment, the calf, plantar flexes the foot. Weakness there would make it hard to push off or rise onto the toes, not cause the toes to drag."],"comp":92,"competencyId":"w5-lab-leg-muscles"},{"id":"N-m4-leg-muscles-36","dok":3,"q":"A dancer has weak lateral compartment muscles and keeps rolling her ankle so the sole turns inward. Which movement are the weak muscles failing to produce?","a":"Eversion of the foot.","options":["Inversion","Knee flexion","Eversion","Dorsiflexion"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Rolling the ankle with the sole turning inward is excessive inversion. The muscles that resist it are the evertors, which turn the sole outward. The fibularis longus and brevis of the lateral compartment are the main evertors, so when they are weak, nothing pulls back against a sudden inward roll.","notes":["Inversion is the movement that is happening too much. It is produced by the tibialis anterior and tibialis posterior, not by the weak lateral muscles.","Knee flexion is done by the hamstrings and gastrocnemius. The lateral compartment muscles arise from the fibula below the knee and do not cross it.","Correct. The lateral compartment muscles evert the foot and resist inward rolling.","Dorsiflexion lifts the top of the foot and is the job of the anterior compartment. The fibularis muscles pass behind the ankle and plantar flex instead."],"comp":92,"competencyId":"w5-lab-leg-muscles"},{"id":"N-m4-leg-muscles-37","dok":3,"q":"To screen a patient's leg muscles, an examiner asks her to walk on her heels with her toes lifted off the floor. Which muscle group is mainly being tested?","a":"The anterior compartment (dorsiflexors).","options":["Superficial posterior compartment","Deep posterior compartment","Anterior compartment","Lateral compartment"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Walking on the heels requires holding the foot in dorsiflexion so the front of the foot stays off the floor. That is the job of the anterior compartment muscles, especially the tibialis anterior. Walking on the toes would instead test the plantar flexors of the calf.","notes":["The superficial posterior compartment, the gastrocnemius and soleus, would be tested by walking on the toes, which needs strong plantar flexion.","The deep posterior compartment inverts the foot and flexes the toes. It is not what keeps the forefoot lifted during heel walking.","Correct. Heel walking tests the dorsiflexors of the anterior compartment.","The lateral compartment everts the foot. Heel walking does not depend on turning the sole outward."],"comp":92,"competencyId":"w5-lab-leg-muscles"},{"id":"N-m4-leg-muscles-38","dok":2,"q":"A muscle arises from the interosseous membrane and the backs of the tibia and fibula, and its tendon passes behind the medial malleolus to the navicular. Which compartment holds this muscle?","a":"The deep posterior compartment.","options":["Anterior compartment","Deep posterior compartment","Superficial posterior compartment","Lateral compartment"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"A muscle that arises from the back of the interosseous membrane lies in the deepest layer of the posterior leg. A tendon passing behind the medial malleolus to the navicular describes the tibialis posterior, the deepest muscle of the deep posterior compartment.","notes":["Anterior compartment muscles arise from the front of the interosseous membrane and their tendons cross the front of the ankle, not behind the medial malleolus.","Correct. This is the tibialis posterior in the deep posterior compartment.","The superficial posterior compartment holds the gastrocnemius, soleus, and plantaris, which reach the calcaneus through or beside the calcaneal tendon, not the navicular.","Lateral compartment muscles arise from the outer fibula and their tendons pass behind the lateral malleolus. They are the mirror image of this muscle on the other side of the ankle."],"comp":92,"competencyId":"w5-lab-leg-muscles"},{"id":"N-m4-leg-muscles-39","dok":2,"q":"Pressing down on a car's gas pedal points the foot downward. Which movement is this, and which group mainly produces it?","a":"Plantar flexion, by the posterior compartment (calf) muscles.","options":["Plantar flexion by the anterior leg muscles","Dorsiflexion by the anterior leg muscles","Plantar flexion by the calf muscles","Dorsiflexion by the calf muscles"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Pointing the foot downward is plantar flexion. It is produced mainly by the gastrocnemius and soleus, which pull up on the calcaneus through the calcaneal tendon and so tip the front of the foot down. The anterior leg muscles do the reverse, lifting the foot in dorsiflexion as you ease off the pedal.","notes":["The movement is named correctly, but the anterior leg muscles lie in front of the ankle and lift the foot. They are the antagonists of plantar flexion.","Dorsiflexion by the anterior leg muscles is a correct pairing, but it describes lifting the foot off the pedal. Pick it for the movement of releasing the gas.","Correct. Pressing the pedal is plantar flexion by the calf muscles.","Dorsiflexion lifts the top of the foot, which is easing off the pedal, and the calf muscles cannot produce it because they pull on the heel."],"comp":92,"competencyId":"w5-lab-leg-muscles"},{"id":"N-m4-leg-muscles-40","dok":1,"q":"Which muscle forms most of the visible bulge of the calf?","a":"The gastrocnemius.","options":["Soleus","Plantaris","Tibialis posterior","Gastrocnemius"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The gastrocnemius is the most superficial muscle on the back of the leg. Its two heads form the rounded, diamond-shaped bulge of the upper calf just below the knee. The soleus lies beneath it and shows mainly at the sides of the lower calf.","notes":["The soleus is large but lies deep to the gastrocnemius and is mostly hidden by it. Pick it for the broad flat muscle under the gastrocnemius.","The plantaris is a small muscle with a thin tendon and adds almost nothing to the shape of the calf. Choose it for the muscle with a very long, slender tendon.","The tibialis posterior is the deepest posterior leg muscle, lying on the interosseous membrane. It cannot be seen or felt as part of the calf bulge.","Correct. The gastrocnemius forms the main bulge of the calf."],"comp":92,"competencyId":"w5-lab-leg-muscles"},{"id":"N-m4-leg-muscles-41","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares the fibularis longus and the fibularis brevis?","a":"The longus arises higher on the fibula and its tendon crosses the sole; the brevis lies deeper and lower and ends on the fifth metatarsal.","options":["Brevis arises higher and crosses the sole; longus ends on the fifth metatarsal","Both lie in the anterior compartment and dorsiflex the foot","Longus arises higher and crosses the sole; brevis ends on the fifth metatarsal","Both pass behind the medial malleolus and invert the foot"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The fibularis longus starts on the head and upper shaft of the fibula and is the more superficial of the two. Its tendon passes behind the lateral malleolus, turns under the cuboid, and crosses the sole to the medial cuneiform and first metatarsal. The fibularis brevis lies under it on the lower fibula and ends on the base of the fifth metatarsal. Both evert the foot.","notes":["This swaps the two muscles. Longus means long, so the fibularis longus has the longer course, starting higher and crossing the whole sole.","The anterior compartment holds the fibularis tertius, not these two. The fibularis longus and brevis are in the lateral compartment and evert rather than dorsiflex.","Correct. The longus is higher and crosses the sole; the brevis ends on the fifth metatarsal.","The medial malleolus is passed by the deep posterior tendons, which invert the foot. The fibularis tendons pass behind the lateral malleolus and evert."],"comp":92,"competencyId":"w5-lab-leg-muscles"}]},{"id":"m4-lower-limb-nav","title":"Nerves and vessels of the lower limb","summary":"lumbar and sacral plexus nerves of the limb (femoral, obturator, sciatic, tibial, common fibular), femoral, popliteal, tibial arteries, great and small saphenous veins. Identify nerves by location; do NOT ask which nerve innervates a given muscle","mw":"2026-11-02","tr":"2026-11-03","cards":[{"id":"L04422","dok":1,"q":"Tracing the main artery of the lower limb from the thigh into the leg, which artery comes between the femoral artery and the tibial arteries?","a":"The popliteal artery.","options":["Fibular artery","External iliac artery","Popliteal artery","Deep femoral artery"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The femoral artery runs down the front and medial thigh and passes to the back of the knee, where it is renamed the popliteal artery as it crosses the popliteal fossa. Below the knee the popliteal artery gives rise to the anterior and posterior tibial arteries, which supply the leg and foot. So the order from the thigh to the leg is femoral, popliteal, then tibial.","notes":["The fibular artery is a branch of the posterior tibial artery in the leg, so it comes after the tibial arteries, not before them. Pick it for the artery along the fibula.","The external iliac artery comes before the femoral artery, in the pelvis. It becomes the femoral artery as it passes under the inguinal ligament, which is the answer for the vessel just above the femoral.","Correct. The popliteal artery behind the knee links the femoral artery to the tibial arteries.","The deep femoral artery is a large branch of the femoral artery high in the thigh that supplies the thigh muscles. It branches off the main channel rather than continuing it into the leg."],"lt":"t-w5-lab-lower-limb-vessels-nerves","lid":"w5-lab-lower-limb-vessels-nerves-r1","comp":127,"competencyId":"w5-lab-lower-limb-vessels-nerves"},{"id":"L04423","dok":1,"q":"The sciatic nerve, the largest nerve in the body, divides above the knee into which two nerves?","a":"The tibial nerve and the common fibular nerve.","options":["Tibial and saphenous nerves","Tibial and common fibular nerves","Superficial and deep fibular nerves","Femoral and obturator nerves"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The sciatic nerve comes from the sacral plexus, leaves the pelvis through the greater sciatic foramen, and runs down the back of the thigh deep to the hamstrings. Usually just above the knee it divides into the tibial nerve, which continues down the back of the leg, and the common fibular nerve, which follows the biceps femoris tendon and wraps around the neck of the fibula toward the lateral and front of the leg.","notes":["The tibial nerve is correct, but the saphenous nerve is a branch of the femoral nerve that runs down the medial leg. Students pick it because its name matches the great saphenous vein.","Correct. The sciatic nerve splits into the tibial and common fibular nerves.","The superficial and deep fibular nerves are the two branches of the common fibular nerve, one step further down. Pick this pair for a question about how the common fibular nerve divides.","The femoral and obturator nerves come from the lumbar plexus and run in the front and medial thigh. They are separate nerves, not branches of the sciatic, and would be the answer for lumbar plexus nerves of the thigh."],"lt":"t-w5-lab-lower-limb-vessels-nerves","lid":"w5-lab-lower-limb-vessels-nerves-r2","comp":127,"competencyId":"w5-lab-lower-limb-vessels-nerves"},{"id":"L04424","dok":2,"q":"Which long superficial vein runs up the medial side of the leg and thigh and is often harvested for bypass grafts?","a":"The great saphenous vein.","options":["Great saphenous vein","Small saphenous vein","Popliteal vein","Femoral vein"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The great saphenous vein is the longest vein in the body. It begins on the medial side of the foot, passes in front of the medial malleolus, and runs up the medial leg and thigh just under the skin to drain into the femoral vein near the groin. Because it is long, superficial, and not essential for returning blood from the limb, surgeons often use pieces of it for coronary bypass grafts.","notes":["Correct. The great saphenous vein runs up the medial leg and thigh and is used for grafts.","The small saphenous vein is also superficial, but it runs up the back of the calf and drains into the popliteal vein behind the knee. Pick it for the superficial vein of the posterior leg.","The popliteal vein is a deep vein behind the knee that continues upward as the femoral vein. It is the answer for the vein in the popliteal fossa that receives the small saphenous vein.","The femoral vein is a deep vein that travels with the femoral artery in the thigh. It receives the great saphenous vein near the groin and is the answer for the main deep vein of the thigh."],"lt":"t-w5-lab-lower-limb-vessels-nerves","lid":"w5-lab-lower-limb-vessels-nerves-r3","comp":127,"competencyId":"w5-lab-lower-limb-vessels-nerves"},{"id":"N-m4-lower-limb-nav-1","dok":1,"q":"The lumbar plexus is formed by the anterior rami of which spinal nerves?","a":"L1 to L4.","options":["C5 to T1","L1 to L4","T2 to T12","L4 to S4"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"A plexus is a network where the anterior rami of neighboring spinal nerves join and regroup before heading out to the body. The lumbar plexus forms inside the psoas major from the anterior rami of L1 to L4. Its branches cross the posterior abdominal wall and serve the lower abdominal wall, the groin, and the front, lateral, and medial thigh, and they include the femoral and obturator nerves.","notes":["C5 to T1 forms the brachial plexus, which supplies the upper limb, not the lumbar region.","Correct. The lumbar plexus comes from L1 to L4.","The thoracic anterior rami do not form a plexus at all. They run separately as the intercostal nerves, and T12 runs as the subcostal nerve.","L4 to S4 forms the sacral plexus, which gives rise to the sciatic nerve. It overlaps the lumbar plexus only at L4."],"comp":191,"competencyId":"w8-nerve-plexuses"},{"id":"N-m4-lower-limb-nav-2","dok":1,"q":"The sacral plexus is formed by the anterior rami of which spinal nerves?","a":"L4 to S4.","options":["L4 to S4","C1 to C4","S4 to S5 and coccygeal","L1 to L4"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The sacral plexus forms from the anterior rami of L4 and L5, which join as the lumbosacral trunk, together with S1 through S4. It lies on the back wall of the pelvis and serves the buttock, the perineum, and most of the lower limb. Its largest branch is the sciatic nerve.","notes":["Correct. The sacral plexus comes from L4 to S4.","C1 to C4, with a small part from C5, forms the cervical plexus, which serves the neck and gives rise to the phrenic nerve. It has nothing to do with the lower limb.","S4, S5, and the coccygeal nerve form the tiny coccygeal plexus below the sacral plexus. They are only the lowest part of the range, not the sacral plexus itself.","L1 to L4 forms the lumbar plexus, which gives rise to the femoral and obturator nerves. The sacral plexus starts lower, at L4."],"comp":191,"competencyId":"w8-nerve-plexuses"},{"id":"N-m4-lower-limb-nav-3","dok":1,"q":"Which large nerve of the lumbar plexus passes under the inguinal ligament into the front of the thigh?","a":"The femoral nerve.","options":["Femoral nerve","Sciatic nerve","Tibial nerve","Obturator nerve"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The femoral nerve is the largest branch of the lumbar plexus. It emerges from the lateral border of the psoas major, runs in the groove between the psoas major and iliacus, then passes deep to the inguinal ligament into the femoral triangle, where it fans out into branches across the anterior thigh.","notes":["Correct. The femoral nerve passes under the inguinal ligament into the anterior thigh.","The sciatic nerve comes from the sacral plexus, not the lumbar plexus, and leaves the pelvis at the back through the greater sciatic foramen.","The tibial nerve is a branch of the sciatic nerve that runs through the popliteal fossa and down the back of the leg. It never passes in front of the hip.","The obturator nerve also comes from the lumbar plexus, but it leaves the pelvis through the obturator foramen into the medial thigh, not under the inguinal ligament."],"comp":127,"competencyId":"w5-lab-lower-limb-vessels-nerves"},{"id":"N-m4-lower-limb-nav-4","dok":2,"q":"In the femoral triangle, which structure lies most lateral?","a":"The femoral nerve.","options":["Great saphenous vein","Femoral nerve","Femoral vein","Femoral artery"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Just below the inguinal ligament, the main structures of the femoral triangle line up in a set order from lateral to medial: femoral nerve, femoral artery, femoral vein, an empty space, then the lymphatics. The memory tip NAVEL (Nerve, Artery, Vein, Empty space, Lymphatics) reads from lateral to medial, so the nerve is the outermost.","notes":["The great saphenous vein comes up the medial side of the thigh and drains into the femoral vein near the triangle. It joins from the medial side, not the lateral side.","Correct. The femoral nerve is the most lateral structure in the femoral triangle.","The femoral vein lies medial to the artery. It would be the answer for the most medial of the three large structures.","The femoral artery lies in the middle, between the nerve and the vein. Pick it for the structure you feel pulsing in the center of the triangle."],"comp":127,"competencyId":"w5-lab-lower-limb-vessels-nerves"},{"id":"N-m4-lower-limb-nav-5","dok":2,"q":"Which sequence gives the main contents of the femoral triangle in order from lateral to medial?","a":"Femoral nerve, femoral artery, femoral vein.","options":["Nerve, artery, vein","Nerve, vein, artery","Vein, artery, nerve","Artery, nerve, vein"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The femoral nerve enters the thigh lateral to the femoral vessels. The femoral artery sits in the middle, and the femoral vein lies medial to it. The memory tip NAVEL (Nerve, Artery, Vein, Empty space, Lymphatics) follows this lateral to medial order, which lets a clinician find the artery for a pulse without hitting the vein or nerve.","notes":["Correct. Lateral to medial, the order is nerve, artery, vein.","This places the vein in the middle, but the artery lies between the nerve and the vein. The vein is the most medial of the three.","This is the correct order read the wrong way, from medial to lateral. Pick it if the question asked for the order starting from the medial side.","This puts the artery on the outside, but the femoral nerve is the most lateral of the three. The artery sits between the nerve and the vein."],"comp":127,"competencyId":"w5-lab-lower-limb-vessels-nerves"},{"id":"N-m4-lower-limb-nav-6","dok":1,"q":"Which nerve passes through the obturator foramen to reach the medial thigh?","a":"The obturator nerve.","options":["Sciatic nerve","Common fibular nerve","Femoral nerve","Obturator nerve"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The obturator foramen is the large opening in the hip bone framed by the pubis and ischium. The obturator nerve, a branch of the lumbar plexus, passes through a small canal at the top of this opening and enters the medial compartment of the thigh. The nerve is named for the opening it travels through.","notes":["The sciatic nerve leaves the pelvis at the back through the greater sciatic foramen and runs down the posterior thigh, not the medial thigh.","The common fibular nerve is a branch of the sciatic nerve near the knee that wraps around the neck of the fibula. It never passes through the hip bone.","The femoral nerve also comes from the lumbar plexus, but it passes under the inguinal ligament into the front of the thigh, not through the obturator foramen.","Correct. The obturator nerve passes through the obturator foramen to the medial thigh."],"comp":127,"competencyId":"w5-lab-lower-limb-vessels-nerves"},{"id":"N-m4-lower-limb-nav-7","dok":1,"q":"Which is the largest nerve in the body?","a":"The sciatic nerve.","options":["Femoral nerve","Tibial nerve","Obturator nerve","Sciatic nerve"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The sciatic nerve is the largest nerve in the body. It comes from the sacral plexus, leaves the pelvis through the greater sciatic foramen, passes deep to the gluteus maximus, and runs down the back of the thigh before dividing into the tibial and common fibular nerves.","notes":["The femoral nerve is the largest branch of the lumbar plexus, but it is much smaller than the sciatic nerve. Pick it for the largest nerve of the anterior thigh.","The tibial nerve is one of the two branches of the sciatic nerve, so it is smaller than its parent. Choose it for the nerve in the back of the leg.","The obturator nerve is a smaller lumbar plexus branch that serves the medial thigh. It would be the answer for the nerve passing through the obturator foramen.","Correct. The sciatic nerve is the largest nerve in the body."],"comp":127,"competencyId":"w5-lab-lower-limb-vessels-nerves"},{"id":"N-m4-lower-limb-nav-8","dok":1,"q":"The sciatic nerve leaves the pelvis through which opening?","a":"Greater sciatic foramen.","options":["Acetabular notch","Inguinal canal","Greater sciatic foramen","Obturator foramen"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"On the back of the hip bone, ligaments turn the deep greater sciatic notch into an opening called the greater sciatic foramen. The sciatic nerve leaves the pelvis through this foramen and passes deep to the gluteus maximus into the back of the thigh. The opening and the nerve share the name sciatic.","notes":["The acetabular notch is a gap in the rim of the hip socket. It is part of the hip joint, not a way out of the pelvis for a nerve.","The inguinal canal runs through the lower front of the abdominal wall. The ilioinguinal nerve passes through it, but the sciatic nerve leaves at the back of the pelvis.","Correct. The sciatic nerve leaves the pelvis through the greater sciatic foramen.","The obturator foramen is the large opening between the pubis and ischium. The obturator nerve uses it to reach the medial thigh, not the sciatic nerve."],"comp":127,"competencyId":"w5-lab-lower-limb-vessels-nerves"},{"id":"N-m4-lower-limb-nav-9","dok":2,"q":"Where does the sciatic nerve travel as it passes down the thigh?","a":"Posterior thigh, deep to the hamstrings.","options":["Anterior thigh, in the femoral triangle","Lateral thigh, under the IT band","Medial thigh, with the adductors","Posterior thigh, deep to the hamstrings"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"After leaving the buttock, the sciatic nerve runs straight down the middle of the back of the thigh. It lies deep to the hamstring muscles, resting on the adductor magnus. Near the knee it divides into the tibial and common fibular nerves.","notes":["The femoral triangle at the front of the thigh holds the femoral nerve, artery, and vein, not the sciatic nerve.","The IT band is a band of fascia on the lateral thigh. The sciatic nerve runs down the back of the thigh, not the side.","The medial thigh is where the obturator nerve runs after passing through the obturator foramen. The sciatic nerve is behind, not medial.","Correct. The sciatic nerve runs down the posterior thigh under the hamstrings."],"comp":127,"competencyId":"w5-lab-lower-limb-vessels-nerves"},{"id":"N-m4-lower-limb-nav-10","dok":1,"q":"The sciatic nerve divides into which two nerves?","a":"The tibial nerve and the common fibular nerve.","options":["Superficial and deep fibular","Medial and lateral plantar","Femoral and obturator","Tibial and common fibular"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The sciatic nerve is really two nerves wrapped together in one sheath. Usually near the top of the popliteal fossa behind the knee, they separate into the tibial nerve, which continues down the back of the leg, and the common fibular nerve, which heads toward the lateral side of the knee.","notes":["The superficial and deep fibular nerves are the two branches of the common fibular nerve, one step further down the pathway. Choose them for what the common fibular nerve splits into.","The medial and lateral plantar nerves are the end branches of the tibial nerve in the sole of the foot. They lie at the far end of the pathway.","The femoral and obturator nerves are branches of the lumbar plexus, not of the sciatic nerve. Pick them for the nerves of the front and inner thigh.","Correct. The sciatic nerve divides into the tibial and common fibular nerves."],"comp":127,"competencyId":"w5-lab-lower-limb-vessels-nerves"},{"id":"N-m4-lower-limb-nav-11","dok":2,"q":"The sciatic nerve usually divides into its two branches at which location?","a":"Near the upper end of the popliteal fossa, just above the knee.","options":["At the neck of the fibula","In the femoral triangle","Behind the medial malleolus","Just above the knee"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The two parts of the sciatic nerve usually separate near the top of the popliteal fossa, the diamond-shaped space behind the knee. From there the tibial nerve continues straight down through the fossa, while the common fibular nerve angles laterally along the biceps femoris tendon. The level of division varies between people, but just above the knee is the usual site.","notes":["The neck of the fibula is where the common fibular nerve wraps around the bone. That point is below the division, after the common fibular nerve has already formed.","The femoral triangle is at the front of the upper thigh and holds the femoral nerve, not the sciatic nerve. Pick it for where the femoral nerve enters the thigh.","Behind the medial malleolus is where the tibial nerve passes into the foot. That is the far end of the tibial nerve, not the place the sciatic nerve divides.","Correct. The sciatic nerve usually divides just above the knee, at the top of the popliteal fossa."],"comp":127,"competencyId":"w5-lab-lower-limb-vessels-nerves"},{"id":"N-m4-lower-limb-nav-12","dok":3,"q":"A nurse gives an intramuscular injection into the buttock and places the needle in the upper outer quadrant. Which structure is the nurse mainly trying to avoid?","a":"The sciatic nerve.","options":["Obturator nerve","Great saphenous vein","Sciatic nerve","Femoral nerve"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The sciatic nerve leaves the pelvis through the greater sciatic foramen and runs deep to the gluteus maximus through the lower and medial part of the buttock before entering the back of the thigh. An injection in the upper outer quadrant stays well away from that path. A needle placed too low or too medial could injure the nerve and cause weakness and numbness down the back of the limb.","notes":["The obturator nerve passes through the obturator foramen into the medial thigh. It lies inside the pelvis and inner thigh, not in the buttock.","The great saphenous vein runs along the medial leg and thigh. It is nowhere near the buttock, so it is not a concern for a gluteal injection.","Correct. The upper outer quadrant keeps the needle away from the sciatic nerve.","The femoral nerve is in the front of the thigh, in the femoral triangle. Pick it as the structure to avoid when accessing the femoral artery in the groin."],"comp":127,"competencyId":"w5-lab-lower-limb-vessels-nerves"},{"id":"N-m4-lower-limb-nav-13","dok":1,"q":"After the sciatic nerve divides, which nerve continues straight down through the popliteal fossa into the back of the leg?","a":"The tibial nerve.","options":["Femoral nerve","Saphenous nerve","Tibial nerve","Common fibular nerve"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The tibial nerve is the larger branch of the sciatic nerve. It runs straight down the middle of the popliteal fossa, passes deep to the calf muscles, and travels down the back of the leg with the posterior tibial vessels. It ends by passing behind the medial malleolus into the sole of the foot.","notes":["The femoral nerve serves the front of the thigh and does not reach the popliteal fossa. Choose it for the nerve in the femoral triangle.","The saphenous nerve is a skin branch of the femoral nerve that runs down the medial leg with the great saphenous vein. It does not pass through the middle of the popliteal fossa.","Correct. The tibial nerve continues down through the popliteal fossa into the posterior leg.","The common fibular nerve leaves the popliteal fossa along its lateral edge and wraps around the neck of the fibula. Pick it for the branch that heads to the lateral and anterior leg."],"comp":127,"competencyId":"w5-lab-lower-limb-vessels-nerves"},{"id":"N-m4-lower-limb-nav-14","dok":2,"q":"At the ankle, the tibial nerve passes behind which bony landmark to enter the sole of the foot?","a":"The medial malleolus.","options":["Head of the fibula","Medial malleolus","Tibial tuberosity","Lateral malleolus"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The tibial nerve runs down the back of the leg with the posterior tibial artery and veins. At the ankle it passes behind the medial malleolus, under the flexor retinaculum through the tarsal tunnel, and then divides into the medial and lateral plantar nerves in the sole.","notes":["The head of the fibula is near the knee, where the common fibular nerve wraps around the neck just below it. Pick it for the common fibular nerve.","Correct. The tibial nerve passes behind the medial malleolus.","The tibial tuberosity is the bump on the front of the tibia where the patellar ligament attaches. No major nerve passes it.","The lateral malleolus is passed by the fibularis tendons and the small saphenous vein. It is the mirror image of the correct answer on the outer ankle."],"comp":127,"competencyId":"w5-lab-lower-limb-vessels-nerves"},{"id":"N-m4-lower-limb-nav-15","dok":1,"q":"Which nerve wraps around the neck of the fibula just below the knee, where it lies close to the skin?","a":"The common fibular nerve.","options":["Common fibular nerve","Tibial nerve","Saphenous nerve","Obturator nerve"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"After the sciatic nerve divides, the common fibular nerve follows the biceps femoris tendon to the lateral side of the knee. It then curves around the neck of the fibula, just below the fibular head, where only skin and thin tissue cover it. There it divides into the superficial and deep fibular nerves.","notes":["Correct. The common fibular nerve wraps around the neck of the fibula.","The tibial nerve stays deep in the back of the leg and passes behind the medial malleolus. Pick it for the nerve running through the middle of the popliteal fossa.","The saphenous nerve runs along the medial side of the knee and leg with the great saphenous vein. It is on the opposite side from the fibula.","The obturator nerve is in the medial thigh and ends above the knee. Choose it for the nerve that passes through the obturator foramen."],"comp":127,"competencyId":"w5-lab-lower-limb-vessels-nerves"},{"id":"N-m4-lower-limb-nav-16","dok":3,"q":"A soccer player is kicked on the outside of the leg just below the knee and fractures the neck of the fibula. Which nerve lies against the bone at this spot and is most at risk?","a":"Common fibular nerve","options":["Sciatic nerve at the buttock","Tibial nerve","Common fibular nerve","Femoral nerve"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"After the sciatic nerve divides above the knee, the common fibular nerve follows the biceps femoris tendon to the lateral side of the knee and curves around the neck of the fibula, covered only by skin and thin tissue. A blow or fracture at that exact spot can stretch or crush it, so the location of the break points straight to this nerve.","notes":["The sciatic nerve has already divided above the knee, so it is not at the fibular neck. Pick it for an injury in the buttock or the back of the thigh.","The tibial nerve runs down the middle of the popliteal fossa and deep in the back of the leg, protected by the calf muscles. It does not pass the fibular neck, so pick it for the nerve that goes behind the medial malleolus.","Correct. The common fibular nerve wraps around the fibular neck just under the skin.","The femoral nerve is in the front of the thigh and ends above the knee in its main branches. Pick it for an injury in the groin or front of the thigh."],"comp":127,"competencyId":"w5-lab-lower-limb-vessels-nerves"},{"id":"N-m4-lower-limb-nav-17","dok":1,"q":"The common fibular nerve divides into which two nerves?","a":"The superficial fibular and deep fibular nerves.","options":["Femoral and saphenous","Superficial and deep fibular","Tibial and sural","Medial and lateral plantar"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"After curving around the neck of the fibula, the common fibular nerve splits into two branches. The superficial fibular nerve continues down the lateral compartment of the leg, and the deep fibular nerve passes into the anterior compartment, running alongside the anterior tibial artery.","notes":["The femoral nerve is a lumbar plexus nerve and the saphenous nerve is its skin branch. They belong to the front of the thigh and medial leg, not to the fibular pathway.","Correct. The common fibular nerve divides into the superficial and deep fibular nerves.","The tibial nerve is the sister branch of the common fibular nerve, not one of its branches. The sural nerve is a separate skin nerve on the back of the calf.","The medial and lateral plantar nerves are the end branches of the tibial nerve in the sole. Pick them for what the tibial nerve becomes in the foot."],"comp":127,"competencyId":"w5-lab-lower-limb-vessels-nerves"},{"id":"N-m4-lower-limb-nav-18","dok":2,"q":"Which nerve leaves the popliteal fossa along its lateral edge, following the tendon of the biceps femoris?","a":"The common fibular nerve.","options":["Common fibular nerve","Femoral nerve","Saphenous nerve","Tibial nerve"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The biceps femoris tendon forms the upper lateral border of the popliteal fossa on its way to the head of the fibula. The common fibular nerve runs along the inner edge of this tendon to reach the lateral side of the knee, then wraps around the neck of the fibula. The tibial nerve, by contrast, stays in the middle of the fossa.","notes":["Correct. The common fibular nerve follows the biceps femoris tendon laterally.","The femoral nerve stays in the front of the thigh and does not enter the popliteal fossa.","The saphenous nerve runs on the medial side of the knee with the great saphenous vein. It is on the opposite side from the biceps femoris.","The tibial nerve runs straight down the middle of the popliteal fossa. Pick it for the nerve in the center of the fossa."],"comp":127,"competencyId":"w5-lab-lower-limb-vessels-nerves"},{"id":"N-m4-lower-limb-nav-19","dok":1,"q":"Which skin nerve, a branch of the femoral nerve, runs down the medial side of the leg alongside the great saphenous vein?","a":"The saphenous nerve.","options":["Sural nerve","Obturator nerve","Saphenous nerve","Tibial nerve"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The saphenous nerve is the longest branch of the femoral nerve and carries only sensation. It travels down the medial side of the knee, leg, and ankle, right beside the great saphenous vein. Both share the name because they run together.","notes":["The sural nerve is a skin nerve on the back and lateral side of the calf that runs with the small saphenous vein. It is the companion of the other saphenous vein.","The obturator nerve serves the medial thigh but ends above the knee. Pick it for the nerve that passes through the obturator foramen.","Correct. The saphenous nerve accompanies the great saphenous vein on the medial leg.","The tibial nerve runs deep in the posterior leg with the posterior tibial vessels, not with a superficial vein."],"comp":127,"competencyId":"w5-lab-lower-limb-vessels-nerves"},{"id":"N-m4-lower-limb-nav-20","dok":1,"q":"The external iliac artery becomes the femoral artery when it passes which structure?","a":"Inguinal ligament.","options":["Popliteal fossa","Adductor hiatus","Inguinal ligament","Greater sciatic foramen"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The external iliac artery runs along the pelvic brim. Where it passes under the inguinal ligament into the thigh, it takes the name femoral artery. The vessel is continuous, and the name changes only because it has entered a new region.","notes":["The popliteal fossa is behind the knee, where the popliteal artery runs. It lies two name changes further down the limb.","The adductor hiatus is the opening in the adductor magnus where the femoral artery passes to the back of the knee and becomes the popliteal artery. That is the next name change down the limb, not this one.","Correct. The external iliac artery becomes the femoral artery at the inguinal ligament.","The greater sciatic foramen is where the sciatic nerve leaves the pelvis at the back, toward the buttock. The external iliac artery stays in front and does not pass through it."],"comp":127,"competencyId":"w5-lab-lower-limb-vessels-nerves"},{"id":"N-m4-lower-limb-nav-21","dok":2,"q":"Where is the femoral pulse felt?","a":"In the groin, just below the inguinal ligament, about midway between the anterior superior iliac spine and the pubic symphysis.","options":["Behind the medial malleolus","Behind the knee, deep in the popliteal fossa","Top of the foot, beside the great toe tendon","Groin, just below the inguinal ligament"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The femoral artery enters the thigh under the inguinal ligament and lies near the surface in the femoral triangle. Pressing just below the ligament, about midway between the anterior superior iliac spine and the pubic symphysis, compresses the artery against the hip bone so the pulse can be felt.","notes":["Behind the medial malleolus is where the posterior tibial pulse is felt. Choose it for the posterior tibial artery.","Deep behind the knee is where the popliteal pulse is felt. Pick it for the popliteal artery.","The top of the foot beside the great toe tendon is where the dorsalis pedis pulse is felt. It would be the answer for the artery on the dorsum of the foot.","Correct. The femoral pulse is felt in the groin just below the inguinal ligament."],"comp":127,"competencyId":"w5-lab-lower-limb-vessels-nerves"},{"id":"N-m4-lower-limb-nav-22","dok":1,"q":"Which branch of the femoral artery is the main supply to the muscles of the thigh?","a":"The deep femoral artery (deep artery of the thigh).","options":["External iliac artery","Popliteal artery","Anterior tibial artery","Deep femoral artery"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Shortly after entering the femoral triangle, the femoral artery gives off a large branch called the deep femoral artery, or deep artery of the thigh. It runs deep and sends branches around the femur and into the muscles of the thigh, while the main femoral artery continues down toward the knee.","notes":["The external iliac artery is the vessel above the femoral artery, in the pelvis. It gives rise to the femoral artery rather than branching from it.","The popliteal artery is the continuation of the femoral artery behind the knee. Pick it for the artery in the popliteal fossa.","The anterior tibial artery is a branch of the popliteal artery that supplies the front of the leg. It lies below the knee, not in the thigh.","Correct. The deep femoral artery is the main supply to the thigh muscles."],"comp":127,"competencyId":"w5-lab-lower-limb-vessels-nerves"},{"id":"N-m4-lower-limb-nav-23","dok":2,"q":"The femoral artery passes through which opening to reach the back of the knee, where it becomes the popliteal artery?","a":"Adductor hiatus.","options":["Femoral triangle","Greater sciatic foramen","Obturator foramen","Adductor hiatus"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"In the lower thigh, the femoral artery runs in a groove between the muscles called the adductor canal. At its end, the artery passes through the adductor hiatus, a gap in the tendon of the adductor magnus, and reaches the back of the knee. From that point it is called the popliteal artery.","notes":["The femoral triangle is where the femoral artery first enters the thigh, just below the inguinal ligament. It is the start of the femoral artery, not where it becomes the popliteal artery.","The greater sciatic foramen is where the sciatic nerve leaves the pelvis for the buttock. The femoral artery never passes through it.","The obturator foramen is the opening in the hip bone that the obturator nerve uses to reach the medial thigh. It is at the hip, far above the knee.","Correct. The femoral artery passes through the adductor hiatus and becomes the popliteal artery."],"comp":127,"competencyId":"w5-lab-lower-limb-vessels-nerves"},{"id":"N-m4-lower-limb-nav-24","dok":1,"q":"Where does the popliteal artery run?","a":"Behind the knee, in the popliteal fossa.","options":["Behind the medial malleolus","Behind the knee","On the top of the foot","In the femoral triangle"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The popliteal artery is the continuation of the femoral artery after it passes through the adductor hiatus. It runs through the popliteal fossa, the diamond-shaped space at the back of the knee, lying deep against the femur and knee joint. At the lower end of the fossa it divides into the anterior and posterior tibial arteries.","notes":["Behind the medial malleolus is the path of the posterior tibial artery. It would be the answer for the artery that reaches the sole.","Correct. The popliteal artery runs behind the knee in the popliteal fossa.","The top of the foot is the path of the dorsalis pedis artery, the continuation of the anterior tibial artery.","The femoral triangle is in the upper front thigh and holds the femoral artery. Pick it for the artery just below the inguinal ligament."],"comp":127,"competencyId":"w5-lab-lower-limb-vessels-nerves"},{"id":"N-m4-lower-limb-nav-25","dok":2,"q":"In the popliteal fossa, which of these structures lies deepest, closest to the knee joint?","a":"The popliteal artery.","options":["Popliteal artery","Tibial nerve","Popliteal vein","Small saphenous vein"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The main contents of the popliteal fossa are stacked from superficial to deep. The tibial nerve is the most superficial, the popliteal vein lies beneath it, and the popliteal artery is deepest, resting against the back of the femur and knee joint capsule. That is why the popliteal pulse is hard to feel unless the knee is slightly bent.","notes":["Correct. The popliteal artery is the deepest of these structures in the fossa.","The tibial nerve is the most superficial of the three main contents. It would be the answer for the structure closest to the skin.","The popliteal vein lies between the nerve and the artery, in the middle layer. Pick it for the intermediate structure.","The small saphenous vein is a superficial vein that pierces the fascia to drain into the popliteal vein. It lies near the surface, not deep against the joint."],"comp":127,"competencyId":"w5-lab-lower-limb-vessels-nerves"},{"id":"N-m4-lower-limb-nav-26","dok":1,"q":"The popliteal artery divides into which two arteries?","a":"The anterior tibial and posterior tibial arteries.","options":["Anterior and posterior tibial","Medial and lateral plantar","Dorsalis pedis and fibular","Femoral and deep femoral"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"At the lower edge of the popliteal fossa, the popliteal artery divides into the anterior tibial artery, which passes forward into the front of the leg, and the posterior tibial artery, which continues down the back of the leg. These two vessels supply the leg and foot.","notes":["Correct. The popliteal artery divides into the anterior and posterior tibial arteries.","The medial and lateral plantar arteries are the end branches of the posterior tibial artery in the sole. They are at the far end of the pathway.","The dorsalis pedis and fibular arteries come later. The dorsalis pedis continues the anterior tibial on the foot, and the fibular branches from the posterior tibial.","The femoral and deep femoral arteries are in the thigh, above the popliteal artery. The deep femoral is a branch of the femoral, one step earlier in the pathway."],"comp":127,"competencyId":"w5-lab-lower-limb-vessels-nerves"},{"id":"N-m4-lower-limb-nav-27","dok":2,"q":"After branching from the popliteal artery, how does the anterior tibial artery reach the front of the leg?","a":"Through a gap above the interosseous membrane.","options":["Behind the medial malleolus","Around the neck of the fibula","Through a gap above the interosseous membrane","Under the inguinal ligament"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The anterior tibial artery starts on the back of the leg. It passes forward through a gap at the upper edge of the interosseous membrane, between the tibia and fibula, and then runs down the anterior compartment on the front of the membrane. At the ankle it continues onto the foot as the dorsalis pedis artery.","notes":["Behind the medial malleolus is the path of the posterior tibial artery and tibial nerve on their way to the sole, not a route to the front of the leg.","Wrapping around the neck of the fibula is the course of the common fibular nerve, not an artery.","Correct. The anterior tibial artery passes forward through a gap above the interosseous membrane into the front of the leg.","The inguinal ligament is in the groin, where the external iliac artery becomes the femoral artery. It is far above the leg."],"comp":127,"competencyId":"w5-lab-lower-limb-vessels-nerves"},{"id":"N-m4-lower-limb-nav-28","dok":1,"q":"On the top of the foot, the anterior tibial artery continues as which artery?","a":"The dorsalis pedis artery.","options":["Posterior tibial artery","Fibular artery","Dorsalis pedis artery","Popliteal artery"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The anterior tibial artery runs down the front of the leg and crosses the front of the ankle. Once it reaches the dorsum, the top of the foot, it is renamed the dorsalis pedis artery. The name means artery of the back of the foot, since dorsum refers to the upper surface.","notes":["The posterior tibial artery is the other branch of the popliteal artery. It runs down the back of the leg and passes behind the medial malleolus into the sole.","The fibular artery branches from the posterior tibial artery and runs along the fibula in the back of the leg. It does not reach the top of the foot as a main vessel.","Correct. The anterior tibial artery continues as the dorsalis pedis artery.","The popliteal artery is above the anterior tibial artery, behind the knee. It gives rise to the anterior tibial rather than continuing from it."],"comp":127,"competencyId":"w5-lab-lower-limb-vessels-nerves"},{"id":"N-m4-lower-limb-nav-29","dok":2,"q":"Where is the dorsalis pedis pulse felt?","a":"On the top of the foot, between the first and second metatarsals, just lateral to the extensor hallucis longus tendon.","options":["Just behind the medial malleolus of the ankle","In the groin, just below the inguinal ligament","Top of the foot, beside the great toe tendon","Deep behind the knee in the popliteal fossa"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The dorsalis pedis artery runs over the top of the foot toward the gap between the first and second metatarsals. It lies just lateral to the tendon of the extensor hallucis longus, which stands out when the great toe is lifted. Pressing gently there against the tarsal bones lets you feel the pulse, which is routinely checked to assess blood flow to the foot.","notes":["Behind the medial malleolus is where the posterior tibial pulse is felt. It would be the answer for the artery heading into the sole.","The groin just below the inguinal ligament is where the femoral pulse is felt.","Correct. The dorsalis pedis pulse is on the top of the foot beside the great toe extensor tendon.","Behind the knee is where the popliteal pulse is felt, deep in the popliteal fossa."],"comp":127,"competencyId":"w5-lab-lower-limb-vessels-nerves"},{"id":"N-m4-lower-limb-nav-30","dok":2,"q":"The posterior tibial pulse is felt behind which landmark?","a":"The medial malleolus.","options":["Calcaneal tendon insertion","Head of the fibula","Lateral malleolus","Medial malleolus"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The posterior tibial artery runs down the back of the leg and passes behind the medial malleolus on its way into the sole. Here it lies close to the surface between the malleolus and the calcaneal tendon, so pressing just behind and slightly below the medial malleolus lets you feel its pulse.","notes":["The calcaneal tendon insertion is at the back of the heel. The pulse lies in front of the tendon, between it and the medial malleolus, not on the tendon itself.","The head of the fibula is near the knee where the common fibular nerve lies. There is no standard pulse point there.","The lateral malleolus is passed by the fibularis tendons and the small saphenous vein, not a main pulse point. It is the mirror image of the correct answer.","Correct. The posterior tibial pulse is felt behind the medial malleolus."],"comp":127,"competencyId":"w5-lab-lower-limb-vessels-nerves"},{"id":"N-m4-lower-limb-nav-31","dok":1,"q":"Which artery branches from the posterior tibial artery and runs down the leg close to the fibula?","a":"The fibular (peroneal) artery.","options":["Fibular artery","Dorsalis pedis artery","Anterior tibial artery","Deep femoral artery"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Shortly below its origin, the posterior tibial artery gives off the fibular artery, also called the peroneal artery. It runs down the back of the leg along the fibula, supplying nearby muscles of the posterior and lateral compartments. Its name tells you which bone it follows.","notes":["Correct. The fibular artery branches from the posterior tibial artery and follows the fibula.","The dorsalis pedis artery is the continuation of the anterior tibial artery on the top of the foot. Pick it for the pulse on the dorsum.","The anterior tibial artery branches from the popliteal artery, not the posterior tibial, and runs in the front of the leg.","The deep femoral artery branches from the femoral artery in the thigh. It supplies thigh muscles, far above the fibula."],"comp":127,"competencyId":"w5-lab-lower-limb-vessels-nerves"},{"id":"N-m4-lower-limb-nav-32","dok":2,"q":"Which sequence correctly traces blood from the external iliac artery to the top of the foot?","a":"External iliac, femoral, popliteal, anterior tibial, dorsalis pedis.","options":["External iliac, femoral, popliteal, posterior tibial, dorsalis pedis","Internal iliac, femoral, popliteal, anterior tibial, dorsalis pedis","External iliac, femoral, popliteal, anterior tibial, dorsalis pedis","External iliac, popliteal, femoral, anterior tibial, dorsalis pedis"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The main arterial pathway of the lower limb is one continuous vessel that changes names as it enters new regions. The external iliac becomes the femoral at the inguinal ligament, the femoral becomes the popliteal at the adductor hiatus, and the popliteal divides into the anterior and posterior tibial arteries. The anterior tibial continues onto the top of the foot as the dorsalis pedis.","notes":["The posterior tibial artery goes to the sole, not the top of the foot. Pick this route if the question ended at the medial and lateral plantar arteries.","The internal iliac artery supplies the pelvic organs and the buttock. The femoral artery continues from the external iliac, not the internal iliac.","Correct. This is the arterial pathway to the dorsum of the foot.","The femoral artery must come before the popliteal. The femoral is in the thigh and the popliteal is behind the knee, so this order skips backward."],"comp":127,"competencyId":"w5-lab-lower-limb-vessels-nerves"},{"id":"N-m4-lower-limb-nav-33","dok":1,"q":"Which superficial vein begins on the medial side of the foot, passes in front of the medial malleolus, and runs up the medial leg and thigh?","a":"The great saphenous vein.","options":["Great saphenous vein","Popliteal vein","Small saphenous vein","Femoral vein"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The great saphenous vein is the longest vein in the body. It starts from the medial end of the venous arch on the top of the foot, passes just in front of the medial malleolus, and climbs the medial side of the leg and thigh. Near the groin it drains into the femoral vein.","notes":["Correct. The great saphenous vein runs up the medial side of the limb.","The popliteal vein is a deep vein behind the knee. Pick it for the vessel that receives the small saphenous vein.","The small saphenous vein starts on the lateral side of the foot, passes behind the lateral malleolus, and runs up the back of the calf. It is the lateral counterpart of the correct answer.","The femoral vein is a deep vein that runs with the femoral artery in the thigh. It receives the great saphenous vein rather than running on the surface."],"comp":127,"competencyId":"w5-lab-lower-limb-vessels-nerves"},{"id":"N-m4-lower-limb-nav-34","dok":1,"q":"The small saphenous vein drains into which vein?","a":"The popliteal vein.","options":["Great saphenous vein","Femoral vein","External iliac vein","Popliteal vein"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The small saphenous vein starts on the lateral side of the foot, passes behind the lateral malleolus, and runs up the middle of the back of the calf. At the popliteal fossa it pierces the deep fascia and empties into the popliteal vein behind the knee.","notes":["The great saphenous vein runs on the medial side of the limb. Although small connections link the two saphenous veins, the small saphenous vein ends in a deep vein behind the knee.","The femoral vein receives the great saphenous vein near the groin. Pick it for the drainage point of the longer, medial saphenous vein.","The external iliac vein is the continuation of the femoral vein above the inguinal ligament. It is too far up to receive a calf vein directly.","Correct. The small saphenous vein drains into the popliteal vein."],"comp":127,"competencyId":"w5-lab-lower-limb-vessels-nerves"},{"id":"N-m4-lower-limb-nav-35","dok":3,"q":"A surgeon needs a long length of vein to bypass blocked coronary arteries and harvests one from the medial side of the patient's leg and thigh. Which vein is most often used?","a":"The great saphenous vein.","options":["Femoral vein","Small saphenous vein","Great saphenous vein","Popliteal vein"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The great saphenous vein is long, runs near the surface along the medial leg and thigh, and is a superficial vein whose job can be taken over by the deep veins. That combination makes it the standard vein to remove and use as a graft in coronary artery bypass surgery. Its location on the medial side matches the description.","notes":["The femoral vein is a deep vein that carries most of the blood back from the limb. Removing it would seriously impair drainage, so it is not harvested.","The small saphenous vein is also superficial but is shorter and runs up the back of the calf, not along the medial thigh.","Correct. The great saphenous vein is the usual graft for coronary bypass.","The popliteal vein is a deep vein behind the knee. It is short and essential for drainage, so it would not be taken for a graft."],"comp":127,"competencyId":"w5-lab-lower-limb-vessels-nerves"},{"id":"N-m4-lower-limb-nav-36","dok":2,"q":"The deep veins of the lower limb follow the arteries. After passing under the inguinal ligament, the femoral vein continues as which vein?","a":"The external iliac vein.","options":["Great saphenous vein","Internal iliac vein","Inferior vena cava","External iliac vein"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The deep veins of the limb run beside the arteries of the same name. The femoral vein lies medial to the femoral artery in the femoral triangle, and when it passes under the inguinal ligament into the pelvis it becomes the external iliac vein, just as the external iliac artery became the femoral artery going the other way.","notes":["The great saphenous vein is a superficial vein that drains into the femoral vein. It comes before the femoral vein in the pathway, not after it.","The internal iliac vein drains the pelvic organs and buttock. It joins the external iliac vein to form the common iliac vein but is not the continuation of the femoral vein.","The inferior vena cava forms where the two common iliac veins join, higher in the abdomen. Blood from the femoral vein reaches it only after passing through the external and common iliac veins.","Correct. The femoral vein becomes the external iliac vein at the inguinal ligament."],"comp":127,"competencyId":"w5-lab-lower-limb-vessels-nerves"},{"id":"N-m4-lower-limb-nav-37","dok":3,"q":"A clot forms in a patient's popliteal vein and breaks loose. Traveling toward the heart, which vein does it enter next?","a":"The femoral vein.","options":["External iliac vein","Femoral vein","Small saphenous vein","Great saphenous vein"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Blood in the deep veins of the lower limb flows upward in the reverse order of the arteries: popliteal vein, femoral vein, external iliac vein, common iliac vein, inferior vena cava, and then the right atrium. A loose clot follows the same path. From the popliteal vein, the next vessel is the femoral vein, which it enters through the adductor hiatus.","notes":["The external iliac vein comes after the femoral vein, above the inguinal ligament. The clot would reach it one step later.","Correct. The popliteal vein continues upward as the femoral vein.","The small saphenous vein drains into the popliteal vein, so blood flows from it into the popliteal vein, not the other way. It is one step before the popliteal vein, not after.","The great saphenous vein is a superficial vein that drains into the femoral vein. Blood does not flow from the deep popliteal vein into it."],"comp":127,"competencyId":"w5-lab-lower-limb-vessels-nerves"},{"id":"N-m4-lower-limb-nav-38","dok":3,"q":"A cardiologist inserts a catheter into the right femoral artery in the groin and advances it toward the heart. Which artery does the catheter enter first after leaving the femoral artery?","a":"The external iliac artery.","options":["Abdominal aorta","Common iliac artery","External iliac artery","Internal iliac artery"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Moving toward the heart means traveling backward along the arterial pathway. The femoral artery is the continuation of the external iliac artery below the inguinal ligament, so the catheter enters the external iliac artery first. From there it passes into the common iliac artery and then the abdominal aorta.","notes":["The abdominal aorta is reached third, after the external and common iliac arteries. Pick it as the vessel the catheter enters once it leaves the common iliac.","The common iliac artery is reached second, after the external iliac. It is one step too far along.","Correct. The catheter enters the external iliac artery first.","The internal iliac artery is a separate branch of the common iliac that dives into the pelvis. The catheter would not enter it while traveling from the femoral artery toward the heart."],"comp":127,"competencyId":"w5-lab-lower-limb-vessels-nerves"},{"id":"N-m4-lower-limb-nav-39","dok":3,"q":"A patient with poor circulation has a strong popliteal pulse, but no pulse can be felt on the top of the foot. Along which artery is a blockage most likely?","a":"The anterior tibial artery.","options":["Femoral artery","External iliac artery","Anterior tibial artery","Posterior tibial artery"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The pulse on the top of the foot is the dorsalis pedis, which continues from the anterior tibial artery. A strong popliteal pulse shows that blood is getting past the knee normally. So the narrowing must lie below the popliteal artery along the route to the dorsum of the foot, which is the anterior tibial artery.","notes":["A blockage in the femoral artery would weaken the popliteal pulse as well, because the femoral artery feeds the popliteal. The strong popliteal pulse rules it out.","The external iliac artery lies above the femoral artery. Blockage there would weaken every pulse below it, including the popliteal.","Correct. A blockage in the anterior tibial artery fits a strong popliteal pulse and a missing dorsalis pedis pulse.","The posterior tibial artery supplies the sole and is felt behind the medial malleolus. A blockage there would remove that pulse, not the one on top of the foot."],"comp":127,"competencyId":"w5-lab-lower-limb-vessels-nerves"},{"id":"N-m4-lower-limb-nav-40","dok":3,"q":"A patient has twisted, bulging superficial veins running along the inner side of the leg and thigh. Which vein is most likely affected?","a":"The great saphenous vein.","options":["Small saphenous vein","Great saphenous vein","Femoral vein","Anterior tibial vein"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Varicose veins are superficial veins that have become stretched and twisted, often because their valves fail and blood pools. The great saphenous vein is the large superficial vein that runs along the medial side of the leg and thigh, so bulging veins in that position point to it and its tributaries.","notes":["The small saphenous vein is superficial too, but it runs up the back of the calf and ends behind the knee. Pick it for varicose veins on the back of the calf.","Correct. Bulging superficial veins on the inner leg and thigh involve the great saphenous vein.","The femoral vein is a deep vein lying beside the femoral artery. Deep veins are surrounded by muscle and do not form visible bulges under the skin.","The anterior tibial vein is a deep vein running with the anterior tibial artery between the muscles of the front of the leg. It cannot be seen on the surface."],"comp":127,"competencyId":"w5-lab-lower-limb-vessels-nerves"},{"id":"N-m4-lower-limb-nav-41","dok":3,"q":"A patient has shooting pain that starts in the buttock and runs down the back of the thigh into the leg. Along which nerve does this pain follow?","a":"The sciatic nerve.","options":["Saphenous nerve","Sciatic nerve","Obturator nerve","Femoral nerve"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The sciatic nerve leaves the pelvis through the greater sciatic foramen, passes deep to the gluteus maximus in the buttock, and runs down the back of the thigh before dividing into the nerves of the leg. Pain that follows this path, from the buttock down the back of the thigh and into the leg, traces the course of the sciatic nerve. This pattern is commonly called sciatica.","notes":["The saphenous nerve runs along the medial side of the knee and leg. It is a skin nerve on the inner leg, not the back of the thigh.","Correct. Pain from the buttock down the back of the thigh follows the sciatic nerve.","The obturator nerve serves the inner thigh. Pain along the medial thigh would point to it.","The femoral nerve runs down the front of the thigh from the groin. Pick it for pain on the anterior thigh."],"comp":127,"competencyId":"w5-lab-lower-limb-vessels-nerves"},{"id":"N-m4-lower-limb-nav-42","dok":2,"q":"Damage to which plexus would most likely cause weakness and loss of sensation in the buttock, the back of the thigh, and the leg and foot?","a":"The sacral plexus.","options":["Sacral plexus","Brachial plexus","Lumbar plexus","Cervical plexus"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Each plexus serves its own region. The sacral plexus, from L4 to S4, serves the buttock, perineum, and most of the lower limb through branches such as the sciatic nerve. Injury to it therefore affects the buttock, back of the thigh, and the leg and foot.","notes":["Correct. The sacral plexus serves the buttock, back of the thigh, leg, and foot.","The brachial plexus serves the shoulder and upper limb. Damage there affects the arm and hand.","The lumbar plexus serves the lower abdominal wall and the front and inner thigh. Pick it for weakness on the front and medial side of the thigh.","The cervical plexus serves the neck and upper shoulders and gives rise to the phrenic nerve to the diaphragm."],"comp":191,"competencyId":"w8-nerve-plexuses"},{"id":"N-m4-lower-limb-nav-43","dok":3,"q":"After pelvic surgery, a patient has numbness over the inner thigh and weakness when squeezing her knees together. A nerve passing through the obturator foramen is suspected. Which nerve is it?","a":"The obturator nerve.","options":["Common fibular nerve","Sciatic nerve","Obturator nerve","Femoral nerve"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The obturator nerve runs along the side wall of the pelvis and leaves through the obturator foramen into the medial thigh. Because it travels through the pelvis, it can be stretched or injured during pelvic surgery. Its course to the inner thigh fits both the numbness there and the weakness in pulling the thighs together.","notes":["The common fibular nerve is at the lateral knee, wrapping around the fibular neck. It is far from the inner thigh and the obturator foramen.","The sciatic nerve leaves the pelvis through the greater sciatic foramen to the back of the thigh, not through the obturator foramen to the inner thigh.","Correct. The obturator nerve passes through the obturator foramen to the medial thigh.","The femoral nerve passes under the inguinal ligament into the front of the thigh, not through the obturator foramen."],"comp":127,"competencyId":"w5-lab-lower-limb-vessels-nerves"}]},{"id":"m4-gi-wall","title":"Digestive tract wall and peritoneum","summary":"mucosa, submucosa, muscularis externa, serosa, enteric plexuses, peritoneum, mesenteries, omenta, retroperitoneal organs","mw":"2026-11-04","tr":"2026-11-10","cards":[{"id":"L00098","dok":2,"q":"In the course notes, what is the fan-shaped fold of peritoneum that binds the jejunum and ileum to the posterior abdominal wall?","a":"The mesentery.","options":["The mesentery","The parietal peritoneum","The lesser omentum","The greater omentum"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The mesentery is a fan-shaped double fold of peritoneum that binds the jejunum and ileum to the posterior abdominal wall. Its two layers carry blood vessels, lymphatic vessels, and nerves to the small intestine. It holds the long small intestine in place while still letting it move.","notes":["Correct. The mesentery binds the jejunum and ileum to the posterior wall.","The parietal peritoneum is the single layer that lines the abdominal wall. It does not form a fold that suspends an organ.","The lesser omentum connects the stomach and duodenum to the liver. It would be correct for the fold between the stomach and the liver.","The greater omentum is the largest fold, a fatty apron hanging from the stomach over the intestines. Pick it for a question about the apron draped over the abdominal organs."],"lt":"t-body-cavities","lid":"c17","comp":111,"competencyId":"w5-peritoneum-folds"},{"id":"L00103","dok":2,"q":"The greater omentum hangs from which part of the stomach?","a":"The greater curvature.","options":["The pylorus","The fundus","The greater curvature","The lesser curvature"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The greater omentum is the largest fold of peritoneum, a fatty apron that hangs from the greater curvature of the stomach and drapes down over the intestines. The greater curvature is the long, convex left border of the stomach. Its partner, the lesser omentum, runs from the short lesser curvature up to the liver.","notes":["The pylorus is the narrow outlet of the stomach leading into the duodenum. It is a region of the stomach, not the long border where the apron attaches.","The fundus is the rounded top of the stomach, above the level of the esophageal opening. It is not the border from which the greater omentum hangs.","Correct. The greater omentum hangs from the greater curvature.","The lesser curvature is the short, concave right border of the stomach, where the lesser omentum attaches and runs to the liver. Pick it for a question about the lesser omentum."],"lt":"t-body-cavities","lid":"c22","comp":111,"competencyId":"w5-peritoneum-folds"},{"id":"L00144","dok":2,"q":"In the course notes, which fold of peritoneum binds the transverse and sigmoid colon to the posterior abdominal wall?","a":"The mesocolon.","options":["The mesocolon","The falciform ligament","The greater omentum","The lesser omentum"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The mesocolon is the fold of peritoneum that binds the transverse colon and the sigmoid colon to the posterior abdominal wall. Because these two parts of the colon are held by a fold rather than pressed against the wall, they are intraperitoneal and fairly mobile. The ascending and descending colon have no mesocolon and are retroperitoneal.","notes":["Correct. The mesocolon binds the transverse and sigmoid colon.","The falciform ligament attaches the liver to the anterior abdominal wall and the diaphragm. Choose it for the fold that holds the liver in place.","The greater omentum is a fatty apron hanging from the stomach over the intestines. Pick it for the large fold draped over the abdominal organs.","The lesser omentum connects the stomach and duodenum to the liver. It would be correct for the fold between the stomach and the liver."],"lt":"t-body-cavities","lid":"c23-x2","comp":111,"competencyId":"w5-peritoneum-folds"},{"id":"L02181","dok":2,"q":"The mucosa is the innermost tunic of the alimentary canal wall. What tissue forms the part of the mucosa that directly touches food in the lumen?","a":"An epithelium.","options":["A serous membrane","Areolar connective tissue","An epithelium","A thin layer of smooth muscle"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The canal wall has four tunics (layers), running mucosa, submucosa, muscularis, and serosa from the lumen outward. The mucosa itself has three parts: an epithelium facing the lumen, the lamina propria of areolar connective tissue beneath it, and a thin muscularis mucosae at its outer edge. Only the epithelium is in direct contact with food, so it is the tissue that lines the lumen.","notes":["A serous membrane forms the serosa, the outermost tunic, which faces the peritoneal cavity rather than the lumen. Choose it if the question asked what covers the outside surface of the stomach or intestines.","Areolar connective tissue forms the lamina propria, which sits just beneath the epithelium, and it also makes up the submucosa. Pick it if the question asked which tissue supports the epithelium from below.","Correct. The epithelium of the mucosa is the surface layer that meets food in the lumen.","A thin layer of smooth muscle, the muscularis mucosae, is the deepest part of the mucosa, so it never faces the lumen. It would be the answer if the question asked what forms the outer edge of the mucosa."],"lt":"t-alimentary-canal","lid":"fr1","comp":109,"competencyId":"w5-canal-wall"},{"id":"L02184","dok":2,"q":"Which part of the mucosa is a layer of areolar connective tissue lying just beneath the epithelium?","a":"The lamina propria.","options":["The lamina propria","The submucosa","The muscularis mucosae","The serosa"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The mucosa has three parts from the lumen outward: the epithelium that touches food, the lamina propria beneath it, and the thin muscularis mucosae. The lamina propria is areolar connective tissue, a loose tissue that supports the epithelium and carries small vessels close to the surface. Because it belongs to the mucosa itself, it is separate from the submucosa, the next tunic out.","notes":["Correct. The lamina propria is the areolar connective tissue layer of the mucosa, right under the epithelium.","The submucosa is also areolar connective tissue, but it is a separate tunic outside the mucosa that carries larger blood and lymphatic vessels and the submucosal plexus. It would be right if the question asked for the connective tissue tunic between the mucosa and the muscularis.","The muscularis mucosae is a thin sheet of smooth muscle at the outer edge of the mucosa, so it is muscle, not connective tissue. Pick it if the question asked for the deepest part of the mucosa.","The serosa is the outermost tunic, a serous membrane also called the visceral peritoneum, and it lies far from the epithelium. Choose it if the question asked what covers the outer surface of the intestine."],"lt":"t-alimentary-canal","lid":"fr4","comp":109,"competencyId":"w5-canal-wall"},{"id":"L02185","dok":2,"q":"The enteric nervous system has two nerve networks in the gut wall. Where is the submucosal plexus, and what does it control?","a":"It lies in the submucosa and controls the secretions of the gut.","options":["It lies between the muscle layers and controls the movement of the gut","It lies in the submucosa and controls the movement of the gut","It lies in the submucosa and controls the secretions of the gut","It lies between the muscle layers and controls the secretions of the gut"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The enteric nervous system is the nerve supply built into the wall of the gut, and it has two networks. The submucosal plexus lies in the submucosa, close to the mucosa and its glands, and it controls the secretions of the gut. The myenteric plexus lies between the circular and longitudinal layers of the muscularis and controls the movement of the gut. Each plexus controls the tissue it sits closest to.","notes":["This describes the myenteric plexus, the other enteric network, which sits between the circular and longitudinal layers of the muscularis. Pick it if the question asked about the myenteric plexus.","The location is right, but the job belongs to the other plexus. Movement is controlled by the myenteric plexus in the muscle layers, while the submucosal plexus, near the glands of the mucosa, controls secretion.","Correct. The submucosal plexus sits in the submucosa and controls the secretions of the gut.","This pairs the myenteric location with the submucosal job, which mixes up the two plexuses. The plexus between the muscle layers controls movement, and the one in the submucosa controls secretion."],"lt":"t-alimentary-canal","lid":"fr5","comp":110,"competencyId":"w5-enteric-plexuses"},{"id":"L02193","dok":1,"q":"Which list names the five large folds of the peritoneum?","a":"Greater omentum, lesser omentum, falciform ligament, mesentery, and mesocolon.","options":["Greater omentum, lesser omentum, falciform ligament, mesentery, and mesocolon","Greater omentum, lesser omentum, falciform ligament, mesentery, and teniae coli","Parietal peritoneum, visceral peritoneum, greater omentum, mesentery, and mesocolon","Greater omentum, lesser omentum, falciform ligament, mesentery, and lamina propria"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The peritoneum is the large serous membrane of the abdomen, and five big folds of it weave among the organs, anchoring them and carrying their vessels and nerves. The greater omentum hangs like an apron from the stomach, the lesser omentum connects the stomach and duodenum to the liver, and the falciform ligament attaches the liver to the anterior wall and diaphragm. The mesentery holds the jejunum and ileum, and the mesocolon holds the transverse and sigmoid colon, to the posterior wall.","notes":["Correct. These are the five large folds of the peritoneum.","The teniae coli are bands of longitudinal muscle on the colon, part of the colon wall rather than a fold of peritoneum. Pick them if the question asked what gathers the colon into haustra.","The parietal and visceral peritoneum are the two layers of the membrane, not folds of it. They would be the answer if the question asked which layers line the abdominal wall and cover the organs.","The lamina propria is the areolar connective tissue layer of the mucosa, inside the canal wall. Choose it if the question asked which part of the mucosa supports the epithelium."],"lt":"t-alimentary-canal","lid":"fr13","comp":111,"competencyId":"w5-peritoneum-folds"},{"id":"L02194","dok":2,"q":"Which organ is retroperitoneal, lying behind the peritoneum rather than within the peritoneal cavity?","a":"The pancreas.","options":["The pancreas","The transverse colon","The stomach","The jejunum"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Retroperitoneal organs sit behind the parietal peritoneum, pressed against the posterior abdominal wall, rather than hanging inside the peritoneal cavity on folds. The pancreas is one of them, along with the kidneys, the duodenum, and parts of the colon (the ascending and descending colon). Organs that hang on folds, such as the stomach on the omenta or the jejunum on the mesentery, are inside the cavity instead.","notes":["Correct. The pancreas lies behind the peritoneum against the posterior abdominal wall.","The transverse colon hangs within the peritoneal cavity on the mesocolon, unlike the ascending and descending colon, which are retroperitoneal. Choose it if the question asked which colon segment is held by the mesocolon.","The stomach lies within the peritoneal cavity, held in place by the greater and lesser omenta. Pick it if the question asked which organ the greater omentum hangs from.","The jejunum hangs inside the peritoneal cavity on the mesentery, so it is not retroperitoneal. It would be the answer for a question about the middle region of the small intestine held by the mesentery."],"lt":"t-alimentary-canal","lid":"fr14","comp":112,"competencyId":"w5-retroperitoneal"},{"id":"L02195","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares the lesser omentum and the greater omentum?","a":"The lesser omentum connects the stomach and duodenum to the liver; the greater omentum hangs from the stomach over the intestines.","options":["The lesser omentum hangs from the stomach over the intestines; the greater omentum connects the stomach and duodenum to the liver","The lesser omentum binds the jejunum and ileum to the posterior wall; the greater omentum attaches the liver to the anterior wall","The lesser omentum connects the stomach and duodenum to the liver; the greater omentum hangs from the stomach over the intestines","The lesser omentum binds the colon to the posterior wall; the greater omentum hangs from the stomach over the intestines"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Both omenta are folds of peritoneum attached to the stomach, but they go in different directions. The lesser omentum runs up from the lesser curvature of the stomach and the duodenum to the liver and carries vessels to the liver. The greater omentum is the largest fold, a fatty apron that hangs down from the stomach and drapes over the intestines. When you open the abdomen on a model, the greater omentum is the first thing you lift.","notes":["This swaps the two omenta. The greater omentum is the large fatty apron over the intestines, and the smaller lesser omentum is the one that reaches the liver.","These describe two other folds. The mesentery binds the jejunum and ileum to the posterior wall, and the falciform ligament attaches the liver to the anterior wall and diaphragm. Pick those names for questions about the small intestine or the liver's anterior attachment.","Correct. The lesser omentum links the stomach and duodenum to the liver, and the greater omentum hangs from the stomach over the intestines.","The greater omentum is described correctly, but the fold that binds the colon to the posterior wall is the mesocolon, not the lesser omentum. Choose the mesocolon for a question about the transverse or sigmoid colon."],"lt":"t-alimentary-canal","lid":"fr15","comp":111,"competencyId":"w5-peritoneum-folds"},{"id":"L02197","dok":1,"q":"What is the continuous muscular tube that runs from the mouth to the anus called?","a":"The alimentary canal.","options":["The peritoneum","The mesentery","The lumen","The alimentary canal"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The alimentary canal, also called the gastrointestinal or GI tract, is the continuous muscular tube that food travels through from the mouth to the anus. Its organs are the mouth, the pharynx, the esophagus, the stomach, the small intestine, and the large intestine. The accessory organs, such as the liver and pancreas, sit beside the canal and empty their secretions into it, but food never passes through them.","notes":["The peritoneum is the serous membrane that lines the abdominal cavity and covers the organs, not the tube itself. Pick it if the question asked which membrane drapes the digestive organs.","The mesentery is a fan-shaped fold of peritoneum that binds the jejunum and ileum to the posterior abdominal wall. Choose it if the question asked what anchors the small intestine.","The lumen is the open space inside the tube, not the tube. It would be the answer if the question asked what the hollow interior of the canal is called.","Correct. The alimentary canal is the GI tract, the continuous tube from mouth to anus."],"lt":"t-alimentary-canal","lid":"c1","comp":109,"competencyId":"w5-canal-wall"},{"id":"L02198","dok":1,"q":"Which tunic of the alimentary canal wall is innermost, lining the lumen?","a":"The mucosa.","options":["The serosa","The mucosa","The submucosa","The muscularis externa"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The four tunics of the canal wall run mucosa, submucosa, muscularis externa, and serosa from the lumen outward. The mucosa is the innermost one, so its epithelium is the surface that touches food. That position is why the mucosa handles secretion and absorption, and why problems such as a peptic ulcer begin there.","notes":["The serosa is the outermost tunic, a serous membrane also called the visceral peritoneum. Choose it if the question asked which tunic faces the peritoneal cavity.","Correct. The mucosa is the innermost tunic and lines the lumen.","The submucosa is the areolar connective tissue tunic just outside the mucosa, carrying vessels and the submucosal plexus. Pick it if the question asked for the second tunic from the lumen.","The muscularis externa is the smooth muscle tunic outside the submucosa that moves food along. It would be the answer if the question asked which tunic produces the waves that push food through the canal."],"lt":"t-alimentary-canal","lid":"c2","comp":109,"competencyId":"w5-canal-wall"},{"id":"L02205","dok":1,"q":"Which serous membrane lines the abdominal cavity and covers the digestive organs?","a":"The peritoneum.","options":["The adventitia","The pericardium","The pleura","The peritoneum"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The peritoneum is the large serous membrane of the abdomen. Its parietal layer lines the wall of the abdominal cavity, and its visceral layer covers the organs, where it forms their serosa. The slim, fluid-filled space between the two layers is the peritoneal cavity. Folds of the peritoneum also anchor the organs and carry their vessels and nerves.","notes":["The adventitia is a fibrous outer layer, not a serous membrane, and it replaces the serosa on the esophagus. Choose it if the question asked what covers the outside of the esophagus.","The pericardium is the serous sac around the heart in the mediastinum. It would be the answer if the question asked which membrane surrounds the heart.","The pleura is the serous membrane around the lungs in the thoracic cavity. Pick it if the question asked which membrane lines the chest wall and covers the lungs.","Correct. The peritoneum lines the abdominal cavity and covers the digestive organs."],"lt":"t-alimentary-canal","lid":"c9","comp":111,"competencyId":"w5-peritoneum-folds"},{"id":"L02210","dok":1,"q":"The four tunics of the canal wall are the mucosa, submucosa, muscularis externa, and serosa. Which one is outermost?","a":"The serosa.","options":["The muscularis externa","The submucosa","The serosa","The mucosa"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The serosa is the outermost tunic of the canal wall. It is a serous membrane, the visceral peritoneum, so it faces the peritoneal cavity and lets the organs slide smoothly against each other. On the esophagus, which runs mostly through the thorax rather than the abdomen, a fibrous adventitia takes its place.","notes":["The muscularis externa is the third tunic, lying outside the submucosa but inside the serosa. Choose it if the question asked which tunic moves food along the tract.","The submucosa is the second tunic from the lumen, areolar connective tissue with vessels and the submucosal plexus. It would be the answer if the question asked which tunic lies just outside the mucosa.","Correct. The serosa is the outermost tunic, the visceral peritoneum.","The mucosa is the innermost tunic, lining the lumen. Pick it if the question asked which tunic touches food."],"lt":"t-alimentary-canal","lid":"c14","comp":109,"competencyId":"w5-canal-wall"},{"id":"L02213","dok":1,"q":"Which tunic of the canal wall usually contains an inner circular layer and an outer longitudinal layer of smooth muscle?","a":"The muscularis externa.","options":["The submucosa","The muscularis externa","The mucosa","The serosa"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The muscularis externa, often just called the muscularis, is the muscle coat of the canal wall. Through most of the tube it has two layers of smooth muscle, an inner circular layer and an outer longitudinal layer, with the myenteric plexus between them. Contractions of these layers mix food and push it along the tract. The stomach is the exception, with a third, oblique layer.","notes":["The submucosa is areolar connective tissue with blood and lymphatic vessels and the submucosal plexus, not a muscle layer. It would be the answer if the question asked which tunic carries the larger vessels of the wall.","Correct. The muscularis externa has the inner circular and outer longitudinal smooth muscle layers.","The mucosa does contain smooth muscle, but only a thin muscularis mucosae, not the two full layers. Pick the mucosa if the question asked which tunic holds the muscularis mucosae.","The serosa is the outermost tunic, a serous membrane with no thick muscle layers. Choose it if the question asked which tunic is also called the visceral peritoneum."],"lt":"t-alimentary-canal","lid":"c17","comp":109,"competencyId":"w5-canal-wall"},{"id":"L02216","dok":2,"q":"On most of the esophagus, the outermost layer of the wall is a fibrous adventitia. Which tunic does the adventitia replace?","a":"The serosa.","options":["The serosa","The mucosa","The submucosa","The muscularis externa"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"In the abdomen, the outermost tunic of the canal is the serosa, a serous membrane that is the visceral peritoneum. The esophagus runs mostly through the thorax, outside the peritoneum, so it has no serous covering there. Instead a fibrous adventitia, a layer of connective tissue, forms its outer layer and blends with the surrounding tissues.","notes":["Correct. The adventitia takes the place of the serosa as the outer layer of the esophagus.","The mucosa is the innermost tunic lining the lumen, and the esophagus keeps its mucosa. Pick it if the question asked which tunic has stratified squamous epithelium in the esophagus.","The submucosa is the connective tissue tunic just outside the mucosa, and it is present in the esophagus as usual. It would be the answer if the question asked which tunic holds the submucosal plexus.","The muscularis externa is the muscle coat that pushes food down the esophagus, and it is present there as usual. Choose it if the question asked which tunic produces swallowing waves."],"lt":"t-alimentary-canal","lid":"c20","comp":109,"competencyId":"w5-canal-wall"},{"id":"L02223","dok":2,"q":"Which tunic of the canal wall contains the muscularis mucosae, a thin layer of smooth muscle?","a":"The mucosa.","options":["The mucosa","The submucosa","The serosa","The muscularis externa"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The muscularis mucosae is part of the mucosa, as its name says. The mucosa has three parts from the lumen outward: the epithelium, the lamina propria of areolar connective tissue, and the thin muscularis mucosae. It is easy to confuse with the muscularis externa, but that is a separate, much thicker tunic farther out in the wall.","notes":["Correct. The muscularis mucosae is the thin smooth muscle layer of the mucosa.","The submucosa is areolar connective tissue with vessels and the submucosal plexus, lying just outside the muscularis mucosae. Pick it if the question asked which tunic holds the submucosal plexus.","The serosa is the outermost tunic, a serous membrane with no muscle layer. Choose it if the question asked which tunic is the visceral peritoneum.","The muscularis externa is the thick muscle coat with inner circular and outer longitudinal layers, separate from the thin muscularis mucosae. It would be the answer if the question asked which tunic holds the myenteric plexus."],"lt":"t-alimentary-canal","lid":"c27","comp":109,"competencyId":"w5-canal-wall"},{"id":"L02224","dok":2,"q":"A tunic of the canal wall is areolar connective tissue carrying blood and lymphatic vessels and a nerve plexus, lying just outside the mucosa. Which tunic is it?","a":"The submucosa.","options":["The submucosa","The serosa","The mucosa","The muscularis externa"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The submucosa is the second tunic from the lumen, sitting just outside the mucosa. It is areolar connective tissue that carries the larger blood and lymphatic vessels of the wall and the submucosal plexus, the nerve network that controls secretion. Its vessels supply the mucosa just inside it.","notes":["Correct. The submucosa is the connective tissue tunic with vessels and the submucosal plexus, just outside the mucosa.","The serosa is the outermost tunic, a serous membrane rather than a connective tissue layer full of vessels. Choose it if the question asked which tunic faces the peritoneal cavity.","The mucosa is the innermost tunic, made of epithelium, lamina propria, and muscularis mucosae, so it cannot lie outside itself. Pick it if the question asked which tunic lines the lumen.","The muscularis externa is the smooth muscle coat outside the submucosa, holding the myenteric plexus between its layers. It would be the answer if the question described two layers of smooth muscle."],"lt":"t-alimentary-canal","lid":"c28","comp":109,"competencyId":"w5-canal-wall"},{"id":"L02233","dok":2,"q":"Which space of the alimentary canal is continuous with the outside world at the mouth and anus, so its contents are not truly inside the body until they are absorbed?","a":"The lumen.","options":["The retroperitoneal space","The submucosa","The lumen","The peritoneal cavity"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The lumen is the open interior space of the alimentary canal. Because the tube is open at the mouth and the anus, the lumen is continuous with the outside world, much like the hole through a doughnut. Food in the lumen only truly enters the body once it is absorbed across the mucosa into the blood or lymph.","notes":["The retroperitoneal space lies behind the peritoneum and holds organs such as the kidneys and pancreas. Choose it if the question asked where the duodenum and pancreas sit.","The submucosa is a connective tissue tunic within the wall, not an open space. Pick it if the question asked which tunic carries the larger vessels of the wall.","Correct. The lumen is the open interior of the canal, continuous with the outside at both ends.","The peritoneal cavity is the slim, fluid-filled space between the parietal and visceral peritoneum, outside the canal and closed off from the outside world. It would be the answer for where contents spill when an ulcer perforates."],"lt":"t-alimentary-canal","lid":"c37","comp":109,"competencyId":"w5-canal-wall"},{"id":"L02237","dok":2,"q":"Which sequence correctly orders the four tunics of the canal wall from the lumen outward?","a":"Mucosa, submucosa, muscularis externa, serosa.","options":["Mucosa, muscularis externa, submucosa, serosa","Mucosa, submucosa, muscularis externa, serosa","Submucosa, mucosa, muscularis externa, serosa","Serosa, muscularis externa, submucosa, mucosa"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"From the lumen outward the tunics are the mucosa, submucosa, muscularis externa, and serosa. The names help: the submucosa lies just under (sub) the mucosa, the muscle coat comes next, and the serous membrane covers the outside. On the esophagus a fibrous adventitia takes the place of the serosa.","notes":["This swaps the submucosa and the muscularis externa. The submucosa sits directly under the mucosa, and the muscle coat lies outside it.","Correct. The tunics run mucosa, submucosa, muscularis externa, and serosa from the lumen outward.","This puts the submucosa against the lumen, but the mucosa is the innermost tunic, and the submucosa sits beneath it, as its name says.","This is the correct order run backward, from the outside in. It would be right only if the question asked for the order from the serosa inward toward the lumen."],"lt":"t-alimentary-canal","lid":"c41","comp":109,"competencyId":"w5-canal-wall"},{"id":"L02246","dok":1,"q":"Which structure lies between the circular and longitudinal muscle layers of the gut wall and controls the movement of the gut?","a":"The myenteric plexus.","options":["The muscularis mucosae","The submucosal plexus","The lamina propria","The myenteric plexus"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The myenteric plexus is one of the two nerve networks of the enteric nervous system, the nerve supply of the gut. It lies within the muscularis, between the inner circular and outer longitudinal layers of smooth muscle, which is exactly where it can control those muscles. It therefore controls the movement of the gut, such as the waves that push food along.","notes":["The muscularis mucosae is a thin layer of smooth muscle inside the mucosa, not a nerve network. It would be the answer if the question asked which part of the mucosa is muscle.","The submucosal plexus is the other enteric nerve network, lying in the submucosa and controlling the secretions of the gut. Pick it if the question asked which plexus controls secretion.","The lamina propria is the areolar connective tissue of the mucosa, just under the epithelium. Choose it if the question asked which tissue supports the epithelium.","Correct. The myenteric plexus lies between the muscle layers and controls gut movement."],"lt":"t-alimentary-canal","lid":"c5-x2","comp":110,"competencyId":"w5-enteric-plexuses"},{"id":"L02247","dok":1,"q":"Which peritoneal fold is the largest, a fatty apron hanging from the stomach over the intestines?","a":"The greater omentum.","options":["The greater omentum","The falciform ligament","The mesentery","The lesser omentum"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The greater omentum is the largest fold of the peritoneum. It hangs down from the stomach like a fatty apron and drapes over the intestines, so it is the first structure you lift when you open the abdomen on a model. Like the other folds, it carries vessels and nerves.","notes":["Correct. The greater omentum is the large fatty apron hanging from the stomach over the intestines.","The falciform ligament attaches the liver to the anterior abdominal wall and the diaphragm. Choose it if the question asked how the liver is anchored to the front wall.","The mesentery is a fan-shaped fold binding the jejunum and ileum to the posterior abdominal wall. It would be the answer for what anchors the small intestine.","The lesser omentum is a smaller fold that connects the stomach and duodenum to the liver. Pick it if the question asked which fold links the stomach to the liver."],"lt":"t-alimentary-canal","lid":"c6-x2","comp":111,"competencyId":"w5-peritoneum-folds"},{"id":"L02258","dok":2,"q":"A peptic ulcer begins in the tunic of the stomach wall that is in contact with the lumen. Which tunic is that?","a":"The mucosa.","options":["The serosa","The submucosa","The muscularis externa","The mucosa"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"A peptic ulcer is an eroded sore that starts at the surface exposed to stomach acid, which is the mucosa, the innermost tunic lining the lumen. The danger is that the sore keeps eroding outward through the submucosa and then the muscularis. If it perforates the serosa, the contents of the lumen spill into the peritoneal cavity.","notes":["The serosa is the outermost tunic, the last layer an ulcer would reach. Pick it if the question asked which layer must be breached for the ulcer to perforate into the peritoneal cavity.","The submucosa is the second tunic, which an ulcer reaches only after eroding through the mucosa. It would be the answer if the question asked which layer an ulcer enters first after leaving the mucosa.","The muscularis externa is the third tunic, deeper still, reached only by an ulcer that has already passed through the mucosa and submucosa. Choose it if the question asked which layer lies just inside the serosa.","Correct. A peptic ulcer begins in the mucosa, the lining in contact with the lumen."],"lt":"t-alimentary-canal","lid":"c17-x2","comp":119,"competencyId":"w5-canal-disorders"},{"id":"L02262","dok":1,"q":"Which peritoneal fold binds the jejunum and ileum to the posterior abdominal wall?","a":"The mesentery.","options":["The lesser omentum","The mesocolon","The mesentery","The greater omentum"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The mesentery is a fan-shaped fold of peritoneum that binds the jejunum and ileum to the posterior abdominal wall. The fan shape lets the long, coiled small intestine move while staying anchored, and the fold carries the blood vessels, lymphatic vessels, and nerves that reach the intestine.","notes":["The lesser omentum connects the stomach and duodenum to the liver. Choose it if the question asked which fold links the stomach to the liver.","The mesocolon binds the transverse and sigmoid colon, not the small intestine, to the posterior abdominal wall. Pick it if the question asked about the colon.","Correct. The mesentery binds the jejunum and ileum to the posterior abdominal wall.","The greater omentum is the fatty apron hanging from the stomach over the intestines. It would be the answer for the largest peritoneal fold."],"lt":"t-alimentary-canal","lid":"c21-x2","comp":111,"competencyId":"w5-peritoneum-folds"},{"id":"L02267","dok":3,"q":"A peptic ulcer in the stomach has eroded through the mucosa, submucosa, and muscularis. If it then perforates the serosa, where will the contents of the lumen spill?","a":"Into the peritoneal cavity.","options":["Into the mediastinum","Into the peritoneal cavity","Into the pericardial cavity","Into the pleural cavity"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The serosa of the stomach is the visceral peritoneum. Just outside it lies the peritoneal cavity, the slim, fluid-filled space between the visceral and parietal peritoneum. When an ulcer breaks through the serosa, stomach contents leak into that space, which can cause a dangerous infection of the peritoneum.","notes":["The mediastinum is the central compartment of the thorax that holds the heart and esophagus. A perforated esophagus could leak there, but the stomach lies below the diaphragm. Choose it for a tear in the thoracic esophagus.","Correct. A perforated stomach spills its contents into the peritoneal cavity.","The pericardial cavity is the space around the heart, inside the pericardium in the thorax. It would be the answer for fluid building up around the heart.","The pleural cavity surrounds the lungs in the thorax, above the diaphragm, while the stomach lies below it in the abdomen. Pick it if the question asked where air or fluid collects around a lung."],"lt":"t-alimentary-canal","lid":"c26-x2","comp":119,"competencyId":"w5-canal-disorders"},{"id":"L02269","dok":3,"q":"An inflamed vermiform appendix ruptures. Which membrane is the spreading infection most likely to reach?","a":"The peritoneum.","options":["The peritoneum","The pericardium","The meninges","The pleura"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The appendix hangs from the cecum inside the abdominopelvic cavity, which is lined by the peritoneum, the serous membrane of the abdomen. When the appendix ruptures, its contents spill into the peritoneal cavity, so the infection spreads to the peritoneum. This is why appendicitis is treated quickly, before rupture.","notes":["Correct. A ruptured appendix spreads infection to the peritoneum.","The pericardium is the sac around the heart in the mediastinum. It would be the answer for an infection or fluid around the heart.","The meninges are the membranes covering the brain and spinal cord. Choose them for an infection such as meningitis, not for an abdominal organ.","The pleura is the serous membrane around the lungs, separated from the abdomen by the diaphragm. Pick it if the infection started in a lung."],"lt":"t-alimentary-canal","lid":"c28-x2","comp":119,"competencyId":"w5-canal-disorders"},{"id":"L02274","dok":3,"q":"Smooth muscle in the canal wall contracts in waves called peristalsis. A person can still swallow and digest food while lying down. What does this tell you about how food moves through the canal?","a":"Muscular waves push food along the tract, so food moves even when gravity is not helping.","options":["Food moves only by gravity, so digestion stops when lying down","Muscular waves push food along the tract, so food moves even when gravity is not helping","The sphincters pump food forward, so the muscle coat is not needed","The villi sweep food along like cilia, so the muscle coat only stores food"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The muscularis of the canal wall, with its circular and longitudinal layers of smooth muscle, contracts in coordinated waves that squeeze food forward and mix it with digestive secretions. Because the muscle does the work, food keeps moving whether the body is upright, lying down, or even upside down. The myenteric plexus between the muscle layers controls these movements.","notes":["Gravity may help food fall when you are upright, but it is not what moves food through the canal, which is why digestion continues while lying down. This is a common misconception rather than a fact that fits any question here.","Correct. Peristaltic waves of the muscularis push food along regardless of body position.","Sphincters are rings of muscle that open and close to control passage between regions, such as the pyloric sphincter at the stomach exit. They do not pump food along the length of the tube. Pick a sphincter for a question about controlling flow at a junction.","Villi are finger-like projections that increase absorptive surface, and they do not sweep food along the way cilia move mucus in the airway. Choose villi for a question about absorption in the small intestine."],"lt":"t-alimentary-canal","lid":"d3-t-alimentary-canal-3","comp":109,"competencyId":"w5-canal-wall"},{"id":"L02279","dok":3,"q":"The canal wall has four tunics: a lining, a connective tissue layer, a muscle coat, and an outer covering. Which statement best explains why a layered wall suits the canal?","a":"Each tunic handles a different job, so together they line, supply, move, and cover the tube.","options":["The four tunics make the tube rigid so it holds its shape when empty","Each tunic handles a different job, so together they line, supply, move, and cover the tube","Each tunic digests a different type of food, so four tunics handle four food groups","All four tunics are identical copies, so one can take over if another fails"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The canal must do several jobs at once, and each tunic takes on one. The mucosa lines the lumen and handles secretion and absorption. The submucosa carries the blood and lymphatic vessels and the submucosal plexus. The muscularis moves and mixes the contents, and the serosa covers the outside so the organs slide smoothly against each other.","notes":["Most of the canal is soft and flexible, and the esophagus even collapses when empty. A rigid tube held open by cartilage would be the trachea, which is the answer for a question about the airway.","Correct. The four tunics each do a different job, together lining, supplying, moving, and covering the tube.","Tunics are layers of tissue, not stations for food groups, and digestion of all foods happens in the lumen. This idea mixes up wall layers with the chemical work of digestion.","The four tunics are made of different tissues and do different jobs, so none can stand in for another. This is a misconception about why the wall has layers."],"lt":"t-alimentary-canal","lid":"d3x-t-alimentary-canal-1","comp":109,"competencyId":"w5-canal-wall"},{"id":"N-m4-gi-wall-1","dok":1,"q":"Which layer of the peritoneum covers the surface of the abdominal organs and forms their serosa?","a":"The visceral peritoneum.","options":["The adventitia","The visceral peritoneum","The parietal peritoneum","The mesentery"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The peritoneum has two layers. The parietal peritoneum lines the wall of the abdominal cavity, and the visceral peritoneum covers the organs themselves. On the stomach and intestines, the visceral peritoneum is the serosa, the outermost tunic of the wall. The word visceral refers to organs, and parietal refers to a wall.","notes":["The adventitia is a fibrous outer layer found on the esophagus in place of a serosa, not a layer of the peritoneum. Choose it if the question asked what covers the outside of the esophagus.","Correct. The visceral peritoneum covers the organs and is their serosa.","The parietal peritoneum lines the wall of the abdominal cavity rather than covering the organs. Pick it if the question asked which layer lines the inside of the abdominal wall.","The mesentery is a fan-shaped fold of peritoneum that binds the jejunum and ileum to the posterior wall, not the covering of each organ. It would be the answer for what anchors the small intestine."],"comp":111,"competencyId":"w5-peritoneum-folds"},{"id":"N-m4-gi-wall-2","dok":1,"q":"What is the slim, fluid-filled space between the parietal and visceral layers of the peritoneum called?","a":"The peritoneal cavity.","options":["The retroperitoneal space","The lumen","The pleural cavity","The peritoneal cavity"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The peritoneal cavity is the narrow space between the parietal peritoneum on the abdominal wall and the visceral peritoneum on the organs. It holds only a thin film of serous fluid, which lets the organs slide against each other and the wall without friction. It is a potential space, not an open chamber, until fluid, blood, or the contents of a perforated organ collect there.","notes":["The retroperitoneal space lies behind the parietal peritoneum, where organs such as the kidneys and pancreas sit, outside the peritoneal cavity. Choose it for a question about where retroperitoneal organs lie.","The lumen is the open space inside the alimentary canal, where food travels. Pick it if the question asked about the interior of the digestive tube.","The pleural cavity is the similar fluid-filled space between the two layers of the pleura around each lung. It would be the answer for the thorax rather than the abdomen.","Correct. The peritoneal cavity lies between the parietal and visceral peritoneum."],"comp":111,"competencyId":"w5-peritoneum-folds"},{"id":"N-m4-gi-wall-3","dok":1,"q":"Which fold of peritoneum attaches the liver to the anterior abdominal wall and the diaphragm?","a":"The falciform ligament.","options":["The falciform ligament","The mesocolon","The greater omentum","The lesser omentum"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The falciform ligament is a thin, sickle-shaped fold of peritoneum that attaches the liver to the anterior abdominal wall and the diaphragm. It runs between the right and left lobes of the liver on the front surface, so on a model it marks the boundary you use to tell the two lobes apart.","notes":["Correct. The falciform ligament anchors the liver to the anterior abdominal wall and diaphragm.","The mesocolon binds the transverse and sigmoid colon to the posterior abdominal wall. It would be the answer for a question about anchoring the colon.","The greater omentum is the fatty apron hanging from the stomach over the intestines. Choose it for the largest peritoneal fold.","The lesser omentum also attaches to the liver, but it connects the liver to the stomach and duodenum rather than to the anterior wall. Pick it if the question asked which fold links the stomach to the liver."],"comp":111,"competencyId":"w5-peritoneum-folds"},{"id":"N-m4-gi-wall-4","dok":1,"q":"Which fold of peritoneum binds the transverse and sigmoid colon to the posterior abdominal wall?","a":"The mesocolon.","options":["The mesocolon","The mesentery","The falciform ligament","The lesser omentum"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The mesocolon is the fold of peritoneum that holds the transverse colon and the sigmoid colon to the posterior abdominal wall, carrying their vessels and nerves. The ascending and descending colon have no mesocolon, because they are retroperitoneal and lie pressed against the posterior wall. Meso means middle, and the fold holds the colon out from the back wall like a hammock.","notes":["Correct. The mesocolon binds the transverse and sigmoid colon to the posterior abdominal wall.","The mesentery is the similar fan-shaped fold for the small intestine, binding the jejunum and ileum to the posterior wall. Pick it if the question asked about the small intestine.","The falciform ligament attaches the liver to the anterior abdominal wall and the diaphragm. It would be the answer for a question about anchoring the liver to the front wall.","The lesser omentum connects the stomach and duodenum to the liver. Choose it if the question asked which fold runs from the stomach to the liver."],"comp":111,"competencyId":"w5-peritoneum-folds"},{"id":"L02287","dok":2,"q":"The pancreas lies behind the parietal peritoneum instead of hanging inside the peritoneal cavity. Which term describes this position?","a":"Retroperitoneal.","options":["Retroperitoneal","Intraperitoneal","Mediastinal","Subcutaneous"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Retro means behind, so a retroperitoneal organ sits behind the peritoneum, pressed against the posterior abdominal wall, rather than being wrapped in visceral peritoneum and suspended in the cavity. The pancreas is retroperitoneal, along with the kidneys, most of the duodenum, and parts of the colon. Its head sits in the curve of the duodenum and its body and tail stretch toward the spleen.","notes":["Correct. The pancreas is retroperitoneal.","Intraperitoneal organs are fully wrapped in visceral peritoneum and hang inside the peritoneal cavity, like the stomach, jejunum, and ileum. Pick this term for those organs, not the pancreas.","Mediastinal describes structures in the mediastinum, the central compartment of the thoracic cavity that holds the heart and esophagus. It would fit the heart, not an abdominal gland.","Subcutaneous means beneath the skin, in the hypodermis. It describes a layer of fat and connective tissue, so it would fit a question about the layer under the dermis, not an abdominal organ."],"lt":"t-accessory-digestive-organs","lid":"fr8","comp":112,"competencyId":"w5-retroperitoneal"},{"id":"L04343","dok":1,"q":"Which sequence lists the four layers of the alimentary canal wall from the lumen outward?","a":"Mucosa, submucosa, muscularis, serosa.","options":["Mucosa, submucosa, muscularis, serosa","Mucosa, muscularis, submucosa, serosa","Submucosa, mucosa, muscularis, serosa","Serosa, muscularis, submucosa, mucosa"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"From the esophagus to the anal canal, the digestive tube is built from the same four layers. The mucosa lines the lumen (the open space inside the tube) and touches the food, the submucosa beneath it carries blood vessels, lymphatic vessels and a nerve plexus, the muscularis moves the food along, and the serosa wraps the outside as visceral peritoneum. Reading from the inside out, the order is mucosa, submucosa, muscularis, serosa.","notes":["Correct. This is the order from the lining that touches food out to the serous covering.","This swaps the submucosa and the muscularis. The submucosa is the connective tissue layer directly under the mucosa, and the muscle layers sit outside it, so the muscularis cannot come second.","This puts the submucosa against the lumen, but the mucosa is always the innermost layer because its epithelium is the surface that contacts the food. The submucosa supports the mucosa from underneath.","These are the right four layers, but listed from the outside in. Choose this order if a question asks you to start at the outer surface of the tube and work toward the lumen."],"lt":"t-w5-canal-wall","lid":"w5-canal-wall-r1","comp":109,"competencyId":"w5-canal-wall"},{"id":"L04344","dok":1,"q":"Which set of parts makes up the mucosa of the alimentary canal?","a":"An epithelium, a lamina propria of areolar connective tissue, and a thin muscularis mucosae.","options":["Epithelium, lamina propria, and muscularis mucosae","Inner circular and outer longitudinal smooth muscle","Areolar tissue, blood vessels, and the submucosal plexus","A serous membrane called the visceral peritoneum"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The mucosa is the innermost layer of the wall and has three parts. Its epithelium is the surface that contacts the food, the lamina propria under it is a layer of areolar (loose) connective tissue, and the muscularis mucosae is a thin sheet of smooth muscle at its base that lets the lining shift and fold. Together these three make up the lining of the tube.","notes":["Correct. The mucosa is the epithelium, the lamina propria, and the thin muscularis mucosae.","Two layers of smooth muscle, inner circular and outer longitudinal, make up the muscularis, the third layer of the wall. That is the answer for the layer that moves food along, not for the lining.","Areolar tissue with blood vessels and the submucosal nerve plexus describes the submucosa, the layer just outside the mucosa. Pick this set if the question asks what the second layer of the wall contains.","The visceral peritoneum is the serosa, the outermost layer of most of the tube. It would be the answer if the question asked about the covering on the outside of the intestine."],"lt":"t-w5-canal-wall","lid":"w5-canal-wall-r2","comp":109,"competencyId":"w5-canal-wall"},{"id":"L04345","dok":1,"q":"On most of the alimentary canal the outer layer is a serosa. Which outer layer replaces the serosa on the esophagus above the diaphragm?","a":"A fibrous adventitia.","options":["Parietal peritoneum","Visceral peritoneum","A muscularis mucosae","A fibrous adventitia"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The serosa is a serous membrane, the visceral peritoneum, and it covers organs that sit inside the abdominal cavity. Most of the esophagus runs through the neck and thorax, outside the peritoneal cavity, so it has no peritoneum to cover it. Instead its outer layer is a fibrous adventitia, a layer of connective tissue that binds it to the surrounding structures. Only the short segment below the diaphragm has a serosa.","notes":["Parietal peritoneum lines the inner surface of the abdominal wall rather than covering an organ. Pick it if the question asks which layer of peritoneum lines the abdominal cavity.","Visceral peritoneum is the serosa itself, the layer the question says is replaced. It is the right answer for the outer covering of the stomach or small intestine.","The muscularis mucosae is the thin band of smooth muscle at the base of the mucosa, deep inside the wall. It is the answer if the question asks for the muscle layer that belongs to the mucosa.","Correct. The esophagus is covered by a fibrous adventitia in place of a serosa."],"lt":"t-w5-canal-wall","lid":"w5-canal-wall-r3","comp":109,"competencyId":"w5-canal-wall"},{"id":"L04346","dok":2,"q":"Which description fits the muscularis of most of the alimentary canal?","a":"An inner circular and an outer longitudinal layer of smooth muscle, with the myenteric plexus between them.","options":["Inner circular and outer longitudinal smooth muscle, submucosal plexus between","Inner longitudinal and outer circular smooth muscle, myenteric plexus between","Inner oblique, middle circular, and outer longitudinal smooth muscle layers","Inner circular and outer longitudinal smooth muscle, myenteric plexus between"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"For most of its length the muscularis has two sheets of smooth muscle: an inner circular layer that squeezes the tube narrower and an outer longitudinal layer that shortens it. The myenteric plexus, a nerve network that controls movement of the gut, sits between these two sheets. Working together, the layers push food along the canal.","notes":["The submucosal plexus is the nerve network in the submucosa, not between the muscle layers. It would be the answer if the question asked which plexus lies in the layer just outside the mucosa.","This reverses the two layers. The circular layer is the inner one and the longitudinal layer is the outer one, which is easy to remember because the circular fibers sit closest to the lumen they squeeze.","Three layers with an inner oblique layer describes the stomach only, which has one more layer than the rest of the tube. Pick this if the question asks about the stomach wall specifically.","Correct. Two smooth muscle layers, inner circular and outer longitudinal, with the myenteric plexus between them."],"lt":"t-w5-canal-wall","lid":"w5-canal-wall-r4","comp":109,"competencyId":"w5-canal-wall"},{"id":"L04348","dok":1,"q":"What does it mean when an organ is described as retroperitoneal?","a":"It lies behind the peritoneum, not within the peritoneal cavity.","options":["It hangs inside the peritoneal cavity on a mesentery","It lines the inner surface of the abdominal wall","It lies behind the peritoneum, outside its cavity","It hangs in front of the intestines like an apron"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The prefix retro means behind. A retroperitoneal organ sits against the posterior abdominal wall, behind the parietal peritoneum, so the peritoneum covers only its front surface and the organ is not suspended in the peritoneal cavity. The kidneys, pancreas, duodenum and parts of the colon are retroperitoneal.","notes":["An organ that hangs on a mesentery inside the cavity, such as the jejunum or ileum, is the opposite of retroperitoneal. This description fits intraperitoneal organs, which are wrapped by visceral peritoneum.","A layer lining the inner surface of the abdominal wall is the parietal peritoneum itself. Choose this if the question asks what the parietal peritoneum is.","Correct. Retroperitoneal organs lie behind the peritoneum rather than inside its cavity.","An apron hanging in front of the intestines describes the greater omentum, the large fatty fold that drapes from the stomach. It is the answer for a question about that fold, not about organ position."],"lt":"t-w5-retroperitoneal","lid":"w5-retroperitoneal-r1","comp":112,"competencyId":"w5-retroperitoneal"},{"id":"L04349","dok":1,"q":"Which group of abdominal organs is retroperitoneal?","a":"The pancreas, the duodenum, and the kidneys.","options":["Gallbladder, stomach, and ileum","Liver, ileum, and transverse colon","Pancreas, duodenum, and kidneys","Stomach, jejunum, and spleen"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Retroperitoneal organs lie behind the peritoneum against the posterior abdominal wall. In this course the named retroperitoneal organs are the kidneys, the pancreas, the duodenum and parts of the colon. Each organ in the correct group sits flat against the back wall rather than hanging in the peritoneal cavity.","notes":["The gallbladder sits on the underside of the liver, and the stomach and ileum hang on peritoneal folds, so none of them is retroperitoneal. Students pick this group because the gallbladder is small and tucked out of sight, which feels like being behind something.","The liver is wrapped in peritoneum and anchored by the falciform ligament, the ileum hangs on the mesentery, and the transverse colon hangs on the mesocolon. All three are in the peritoneal cavity, so this group fits intraperitoneal organs.","Correct. The pancreas, most of the duodenum, and the kidneys all lie behind the peritoneum.","The stomach and jejunum are held inside the peritoneal cavity by peritoneal folds (the omenta and the mesentery), and the spleen is also covered by peritoneum. This group would fit a question asking for intraperitoneal organs."],"lt":"t-w5-retroperitoneal","lid":"w5-retroperitoneal-r2","comp":112,"competencyId":"w5-retroperitoneal"},{"id":"L04350","dok":1,"q":"Which of these intestinal segments is mostly retroperitoneal?","a":"The duodenum.","options":["Ileum","Jejunum","Transverse colon","Duodenum"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The duodenum is the first and shortest part of the small intestine, curving from the pyloric sphincter around the head of the pancreas. Most of it is pressed against the posterior abdominal wall behind the peritoneum, so it is mostly retroperitoneal. The rest of the small intestine hangs freely on the mesentery.","notes":["The ileum is the last and longest part of the small intestine, also held by the mesentery inside the peritoneal cavity. Pick it if the question asks which segment joins the large intestine at the ileocecal sphincter.","The jejunum is the middle part of the small intestine and hangs on the fan-shaped mesentery, so it is inside the peritoneal cavity. It would be the answer for the middle segment, about 3 feet long.","The transverse colon hangs across the abdomen on the transverse mesocolon, so it is inside the peritoneal cavity. It is the right answer for a question about which colon segment the mesocolon binds.","Correct. The duodenum is the mostly retroperitoneal part of the small intestine."],"lt":"t-w5-retroperitoneal","lid":"w5-retroperitoneal-r3","comp":112,"competencyId":"w5-retroperitoneal"},{"id":"L04351","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares the parietal and visceral peritoneum?","a":"Parietal peritoneum lines the abdominal wall; visceral peritoneum covers the organs as their serosa.","options":["Parietal is the organ's mucosa; visceral is the organ's submucosa","Visceral lines the abdominal wall; parietal covers the organs as their serosa","Parietal lines the chest wall; visceral covers the surface of each lung","Parietal lines the abdominal wall; visceral covers the organs as their serosa"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Every serous membrane has two layers. Parietal refers to a wall, so the parietal peritoneum lines the wall of the abdominal cavity, and visceral refers to organs, so the visceral peritoneum covers the organs and forms their serosa. The slim, fluid-filled peritoneal cavity lies between the two layers and lets the organs slide without friction.","notes":["The mucosa and submucosa are the two inner layers of the digestive tube wall, not layers of the peritoneum. Students choose this when they mix up the peritoneum with the layers of the gut wall itself.","This swaps the two layers. Parietal always goes with the body wall and visceral with the organs, for the peritoneum as well as for the pleura and pericardium.","A parietal layer on the chest wall and a visceral layer on the lungs describes the pleura, the serous membrane of the thoracic cavity. Pick this if the question is about the lungs rather than the abdomen.","Correct. Parietal lines the wall and visceral covers the organs."],"lt":"t-w5-retroperitoneal","lid":"w5-retroperitoneal-r4","comp":111,"competencyId":"w5-peritoneum-folds"},{"id":"L04352","dok":3,"q":"A surgeon reaches the pancreas from behind without opening the peritoneal cavity. Which fact about the pancreas makes this possible?","a":"The pancreas is retroperitoneal, lying behind the peritoneum.","options":["It is held to the liver by the lesser omentum","It hangs on the mesentery proper","It is retroperitoneal, behind the peritoneum","It is wrapped inside the greater omentum"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The pancreas lies posterior to the stomach and is retroperitoneal, meaning it sits against the posterior abdominal wall behind the parietal peritoneum. Because it is outside the peritoneal cavity, a surgeon can approach it from the back without passing through the peritoneum. Organs that hang inside the cavity could not be reached this way.","notes":["The lesser omentum connects the stomach and duodenum to the liver and carries vessels to the liver. Choose it if the question asks which fold links the stomach to the liver.","The mesentery proper is the fan-shaped fold that binds the jejunum and ileum to the posterior wall. An organ on the mesentery lies inside the peritoneal cavity, which is the answer for those two segments of small intestine.","Correct. The pancreas lies behind the peritoneum, so it can be reached without entering the peritoneal cavity.","The greater omentum is the fatty apron that hangs from the stomach over the intestines. It does not wrap the pancreas, and it would be the answer for a question about that apron of peritoneum."],"lt":"t-w5-retroperitoneal","lid":"w5-retroperitoneal-r5","comp":112,"competencyId":"w5-retroperitoneal"},{"id":"L04404","dok":1,"q":"Which description fits the greater omentum?","a":"The largest peritoneal fold, a fatty apron hanging from the stomach over the intestines.","options":["A fan-shaped fold binding the jejunum and ileum to the back wall","A fold joining the stomach and duodenum to the liver","A fatty apron hanging from the stomach over the intestines","A fold attaching the liver to the anterior abdominal wall"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The greater omentum is the largest fold of peritoneum. It hangs down from the greater curvature of the stomach like an apron, draping over the coils of the intestines, and it stores a good deal of fat. It is usually the first thing a surgeon sees on opening the abdomen.","notes":["A fan-shaped fold binding the jejunum and ileum to the posterior wall is the mesentery. Choose it for a question about how the small intestine is anchored.","A fold joining the stomach and duodenum to the liver is the lesser omentum. Pick it for a question about the fold that carries vessels to the liver.","Correct. The greater omentum is the fatty apron that hangs from the stomach over the intestines.","A fold attaching the liver to the anterior abdominal wall and diaphragm is the falciform ligament. It is the answer for the fold that separates the right and left lobes of the liver."],"lt":"t-w5-peritoneum-folds","lid":"w5-peritoneum-folds-r1","comp":111,"competencyId":"w5-peritoneum-folds"},{"id":"L04405","dok":1,"q":"What does the lesser omentum connect?","a":"The stomach and duodenum to the liver.","options":["The stomach and duodenum to the liver","The jejunum and ileum to the posterior wall","The liver to the anterior abdominal wall","The transverse and sigmoid colon to the back wall"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The lesser omentum is a fold of peritoneum that runs from the lesser curvature of the stomach and the first part of the duodenum up to the underside of the liver. Its free edge carries vessels and ducts to and from the liver. It is much smaller than the greater omentum, which hangs from the opposite curvature of the stomach.","notes":["Correct. The lesser omentum links the stomach and duodenum to the liver.","The mesentery binds the jejunum and ileum to the posterior abdominal wall. It is the answer for the fan-shaped fold of the small intestine.","The falciform ligament attaches the liver to the anterior abdominal wall and diaphragm. Pick it for the fold that suspends the liver from the front.","The mesocolon binds the transverse and sigmoid colon to the posterior abdominal wall. Choose it for a question about how the colon is held in place."],"lt":"t-w5-peritoneum-folds","lid":"w5-peritoneum-folds-r2","comp":111,"competencyId":"w5-peritoneum-folds"},{"id":"L04406","dok":2,"q":"A mesentery binds an organ to the posterior abdominal wall and carries its vessels. Which organs does the fan-shaped mesentery proper bind?","a":"The jejunum and ileum.","options":["Transverse and sigmoid colon","Jejunum and ileum","Duodenum and pancreas","Liver and gallbladder"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The mesentery proper is a fan-shaped fold of peritoneum. Its short root attaches to the posterior abdominal wall, and its long, ruffled outer edge holds the many coils of the jejunum and ileum. Blood vessels, lymphatic vessels and nerves run between its two layers to reach the intestine, which lets the small intestine move freely while staying supplied.","notes":["The transverse and sigmoid colon are bound by the mesocolon, a separate fold. Pick them for a question about the mesocolon.","Correct. The mesentery proper anchors the jejunum and ileum.","The duodenum and pancreas are mostly retroperitoneal, pressed against the posterior wall with no mesentery. They would be the answer for organs that lie behind the peritoneum.","The liver is held by the falciform ligament and the lesser omentum, and the gallbladder sits on its underside. Neither hangs on the mesentery, so they fit a question about liver ligaments."],"lt":"t-w5-peritoneum-folds","lid":"w5-peritoneum-folds-r3","comp":111,"competencyId":"w5-peritoneum-folds"},{"id":"L04425","dok":1,"q":"Which statement correctly describes what the submucosal and myenteric plexuses each control?","a":"The submucosal plexus controls gut secretions; the myenteric plexus controls gut movement.","options":["Both control movement, from inside the muscularis","Submucosal controls movement; myenteric controls secretions","Submucosal controls secretions; myenteric controls movement","Both control secretions, from inside the mucosa"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The enteric nervous system is the gut's own network of nerves, built from two plexuses in the wall. The submucosal plexus lies in the submucosa, close to the glands of the mucosa, and controls secretion. The myenteric plexus lies between the circular and longitudinal muscle layers of the muscularis and controls movement. Each plexus sits next to the tissue it controls.","notes":["Only the myenteric plexus lies in the muscularis. The submucosal plexus lies in the submucosa and controls secretion rather than movement.","This reverses the two plexuses. The myenteric plexus sits between the muscle layers, so it is the one that controls movement.","Correct. The submucosal plexus controls secretion and the myenteric plexus controls movement.","Neither plexus lies in the mucosa, and only the submucosal plexus controls secretion. Students pick this when they lump both plexuses together."],"lt":"t-w5-enteric-plexuses","lid":"w5-enteric-plexuses-r1","comp":110,"competencyId":"w5-enteric-plexuses"},{"id":"N-a17-110-1","dok":1,"q":"What is another name for the myenteric plexus?","a":"Auerbach plexus.","options":["Auerbach plexus","Meissner plexus","Submucosal plexus","Enteric nervous system"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The myenteric plexus is also called the Auerbach plexus. It sits in the muscularis, between the inner circular and outer longitudinal layers. The other enteric plexus, in the submucosa, is the submucosal or Meissner plexus.","notes":["Correct. The myenteric plexus is also called the Auerbach plexus.","Meissner plexus is the other name for the submucosal plexus, which lies in the submucosa.","The submucosal plexus is the other enteric plexus, so it is not another name for the myenteric plexus. It lies in the submucosa, not in the muscularis.","The enteric nervous system is the whole nerve supply of the gut wall. It is made of both plexuses, so it is not another name for just one."],"comp":110,"competencyId":"w5-enteric-plexuses"},{"id":"N-a17-110-2","dok":2,"q":"Moving outward from the lumen of the alimentary canal, in which order do you meet these layers and plexuses?","a":"Submucosal plexus, inner circular muscle, myenteric plexus, outer longitudinal muscle.","options":["Myenteric plexus, inner circular muscle, submucosal plexus, outer longitudinal muscle","Submucosal plexus, outer longitudinal muscle, myenteric plexus, inner circular muscle","Submucosal plexus, inner circular muscle, myenteric plexus, outer longitudinal muscle","Inner circular muscle, submucosal plexus, outer longitudinal muscle, myenteric plexus"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The submucosal plexus lies in the submucosa, just deep to the mucosa, so you reach it first. Deeper is the muscularis, starting with its inner circular layer. The myenteric plexus sits between the two muscle layers, and the outer longitudinal layer comes last.","notes":["This puts the myenteric plexus first. It sits between the two muscle layers, not next to the mucosa, and the submucosal plexus is not inside the muscularis.","This flips the muscle layers. The circular layer is the inner one and the longitudinal layer is the outer one.","Correct. The submucosal plexus comes first, then the inner circular layer, the myenteric plexus, and the outer longitudinal layer.","This puts muscle before the submucosal plexus. The submucosa, with its plexus, lies just deep to the mucosa, before you reach any muscle."],"comp":110,"competencyId":"w5-enteric-plexuses"},{"id":"N-a17-110-3","dok":2,"q":"A student forgets which layer holds the myenteric plexus. How does the name itself answer the question?","a":"Myo means muscle, so it sits in the muscularis.","options":["Myo means mucus, so it sits in the mucosa","Myo means muscle, so it sits in the muscularis","Myo means outer, so it sits in the serosa","Myo means under, so it sits in the submucosa"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Myenteric comes from myo, which means muscle. So the myenteric plexus sits in the muscularis, between the inner circular and outer longitudinal layers. The submucosal plexus is named the same way, since sub-mucosal sits in the submucosa.","notes":["Myo means muscle, not mucus. The plexus that sits next to the mucosa is the submucosal plexus, in the submucosa.","Correct. Myo means muscle, so the myenteric plexus sits in the muscularis.","Myo means muscle, not outer. The myenteric plexus sits in the muscularis, not in the outer covering of the tube.","The sub in submucosal is what points to the submucosa. Myo means muscle, so the myenteric plexus is in the muscularis."],"comp":110,"competencyId":"w5-enteric-plexuses"},{"id":"N-a17-110-4","dok":2,"q":"On a slide of the gut wall, you find a network of neurons in the connective tissue layer that lies just deep to the mucosa. Which structure is it?","a":"Submucosal (Meissner) plexus.","options":["Myenteric (Auerbach) plexus","Submucosal (Meissner) plexus","Celiac plexus","Vagus nerve"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The submucosa is the connective tissue layer just deep to the mucosa, and the submucosal (Meissner) plexus runs inside it. It controls the glands of the mucosa and the blood flow to it. The other enteric plexus, the myenteric plexus, sits deeper, between the two muscle layers of the muscularis.","notes":["The myenteric plexus lies deeper, inside the muscularis, between the inner circular and outer longitudinal layers. Pick it for neurons found between the two muscle layers.","Correct. The submucosal plexus lies in the submucosa, the connective tissue layer just deep to the mucosa.","The celiac plexus is the large autonomic plexus that surrounds the celiac trunk and supplies the upper abdominal organs. It lies outside the gut wall, not inside the submucosa.","The vagus nerve sends parasympathetic fibers to the gut wall from outside. It is a cranial nerve, not a network of neurons inside the submucosa."],"comp":110,"competencyId":"w5-enteric-plexuses"},{"id":"N-a17-112-1","dok":1,"q":"What covers the back surface of a retroperitoneal organ?","a":"An adventitia.","options":["An adventitia","A serosa","A mesentery","The parietal peritoneum"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"A retroperitoneal organ lies behind the parietal peritoneum, against the posterior abdominal wall. Peritoneum covers only its front surface, so its back surface is covered by an adventitia instead of a serosa. It also has no mesentery, which is why it is held firmly in place.","notes":["Correct. The back surface of a retroperitoneal organ is covered by an adventitia, not a serosa.","A serosa is the visceral peritoneum that wraps an intraperitoneal organ. A retroperitoneal organ has peritoneum only on its front surface.","A mesentery is the fold that lets an intraperitoneal organ such as the jejunum hang and move. Retroperitoneal organs have no mesentery.","The parietal peritoneum lies in front of a retroperitoneal organ and covers its front surface, not its back."],"comp":112,"competencyId":"w5-retroperitoneal"},{"id":"N-a17-112-2","dok":2,"q":"The duodenum and the pancreas are both retroperitoneal, each with one exception. Which parts are the exceptions?","a":"The first part of the duodenum and the tip of the pancreatic tail.","options":["The first part of the duodenum and the tip of the pancreatic tail","The last part of the duodenum and the head of the pancreas","The first part of the duodenum and the head of the pancreas","The last part of the duodenum and the tip of the pancreatic tail"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The duodenum is retroperitoneal except for its short first part, just past the stomach. The pancreas is retroperitoneal except for the tip of its tail. The rest of the duodenum is fixed to the posterior wall in a C-shape around the head of the pancreas.","notes":["Correct. The short first part of the duodenum and the tip of the pancreatic tail are the exceptions.","Both halves are wrong. The rest of the duodenum, including its last part, is retroperitoneal, and the head of the pancreas sits fixed inside the duodenal C.","The duodenum half is right, but the head of the pancreas is retroperitoneal. Only the tip of the tail is the exception.","The pancreas half is right, but the exception in the duodenum is its short first part, just past the stomach, not its last part."],"comp":112,"competencyId":"w5-retroperitoneal"},{"id":"N-a17-112-3","dok":3,"q":"On the cadaver, you can lift the transverse and sigmoid colon away from the body wall, but the ascending and descending colon stay fixed. What explains this?","a":"The transverse and sigmoid colon hang on a mesocolon, and the other two are retroperitoneal.","options":["The ascending and descending colon hang on a mesocolon that holds them against the wall","The transverse and sigmoid colon are retroperitoneal, which leaves them free to swing","The transverse and sigmoid colon hang on a mesocolon, and the other two are retroperitoneal","All four parts are intraperitoneal, but only the transverse and sigmoid have a serosa"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The colon alternates. The ascending and descending colon are retroperitoneal, fixed to the right and left posterior walls with no mesentery. The transverse and sigmoid colon are intraperitoneal and hang on their own mesocolons, and that fold is what lets them move.","notes":["This reverses them. The ascending and descending colon have no mesocolon; they are retroperitoneal and fixed to the posterior wall.","Retroperitoneal organs are held firmly in place, not free to swing. The transverse and sigmoid colon move because they are intraperitoneal.","Correct. The transverse and sigmoid colon hang on mesocolons, while the ascending and descending colon are retroperitoneal and fixed.","Only two parts of the colon are intraperitoneal. The ascending and descending colon are retroperitoneal, fixed to the posterior wall."],"comp":112,"competencyId":"w5-retroperitoneal"},{"id":"N-a17d-109-1","dok":3,"q":"A biopsy needle passes from the lumen of the stomach through the epithelium, the lamina propria, and then the muscularis mucosae. Which layer does it reach next, and what is its dominant tissue?","a":"The submucosa, made mostly of areolar connective tissue.","options":["The serosa, made of mesothelium over loose connective tissue.","The adventitia, made of fibrous connective tissue.","The muscularis, made mostly of layers of smooth muscle.","The submucosa, made mostly of areolar connective tissue."],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The mucosa has three parts: the epithelium that touches the food, the lamina propria of loose connective tissue, and the thin muscularis mucosae. Once the needle passes the muscularis mucosae it has left the mucosa. The next layer outward is the submucosa, which is areolar connective tissue (OpenStax describes it as dense connective tissue) carrying larger blood and lymphatic vessels and the submucosal plexus.","notes":["The serosa is the outermost layer. The needle would cross the submucosa and the muscularis before reaching it.","An adventitia replaces the serosa as the outer layer on the esophagus above the diaphragm. It is never the layer just outside the mucosa.","The muscularis is the third layer. The needle has to cross the submucosa before it reaches the smooth muscle layers of the muscularis.","Correct. The muscularis mucosae is the last part of the mucosa, so the next layer out is the submucosa, which is areolar connective tissue."],"comp":109,"competencyId":"w5-canal-wall"},{"id":"N-a17d-110-1","dok":3,"q":"A biopsy of the colon removes the mucosa and the submucosa but none of the muscularis. Which enteric plexus could appear in the sample?","a":"Only the submucosal plexus.","options":["Neither plexus, since both lie in the serosa.","Only the myenteric plexus.","Only the submucosal plexus.","Both the submucosal and myenteric plexuses."],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Each enteric plexus sits in a different layer of the wall. The submucosal (Meissner) plexus lies in the submucosa, just deep to the mucosa. The myenteric (Auerbach) plexus lies inside the muscularis, between the inner circular and outer longitudinal layers. A sample that ends at the submucosa holds the submucosal plexus but not the myenteric plexus.","notes":["Neither plexus is in the serosa. The submucosal plexus is in the submucosa and the myenteric plexus is in the muscularis.","The myenteric plexus lies inside the muscularis, between its two muscle layers. The sample stops before the muscularis, so it would not contain it.","Correct. The submucosal plexus lies in the submucosa, which is in the sample, while the myenteric plexus lies in the muscularis, which was left behind.","Only one plexus sits in the layers taken. The myenteric plexus is in the muscularis, which this biopsy did not reach."],"comp":110,"competencyId":"w5-enteric-plexuses"},{"id":"N-a17d-110-2","dok":3,"q":"During surgery on the small intestine, the surgeon cuts inward from the outside, through the serosa and then the outer longitudinal muscle layer. Which nerve network does the blade reach next, and which layer comes right after it?","a":"The myenteric plexus, then the inner circular muscle layer.","options":["The submucosal plexus, then the inner circular muscle layer.","The submucosal plexus, then the muscularis mucosae.","The myenteric plexus, then the inner circular muscle layer.","The myenteric plexus, then the submucosa and its vessels."],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Moving inward from the outside, the wall goes serosa, outer longitudinal muscle, myenteric plexus, inner circular muscle, then the submucosa with its submucosal plexus, and finally the mucosa. The myenteric plexus is sandwiched between the two layers of the muscularis. So after cutting through the longitudinal layer, the blade meets the myenteric plexus and then the circular layer.","notes":["The submucosal plexus lies in the submucosa, which is deeper than both muscle layers. The blade would cross the circular layer before reaching it.","The submucosal plexus and the muscularis mucosae are both deeper. The next thing after the longitudinal layer is the myenteric plexus.","Correct. The myenteric plexus sits between the outer longitudinal and inner circular layers, so it comes right after the longitudinal layer.","The plexus is right, but the order is not. The inner circular muscle layer lies between the myenteric plexus and the submucosa."],"comp":110,"competencyId":"w5-enteric-plexuses"},{"id":"N-a17d-110-3","dok":3,"q":"A tumor starts in the mucosa of the colon and grows outward until it fills the submucosa. Which enteric plexus has it already reached, and what must it cross before it reaches the other plexus?","a":"The submucosal plexus; it must cross the inner circular muscle layer.","options":["The myenteric plexus; it must cross the outer longitudinal muscle layer.","The submucosal plexus; it must cross the outer longitudinal muscle layer.","The myenteric plexus; it must cross the muscularis mucosae.","The submucosal plexus; it must cross the inner circular muscle layer."],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Moving outward from the lumen, the wall goes mucosa, submucosa with the submucosal plexus, inner circular muscle, myenteric plexus, outer longitudinal muscle, then serosa. A tumor that fills the submucosa has reached the submucosal (Meissner) plexus. To reach the myenteric (Auerbach) plexus, which sits between the two muscle layers of the muscularis, it must first cross the inner circular layer.","notes":["The myenteric plexus lies in the muscularis, deeper than the submucosa, so the tumor has not reached it yet. The outer longitudinal layer lies outside the myenteric plexus, not between it and the submucosa.","The plexus is right, but the outer longitudinal layer lies outside the myenteric plexus. The layer between the submucosa and the myenteric plexus is the inner circular layer.","The muscularis mucosae is the thin muscle layer of the mucosa, which the tumor has already passed. The myenteric plexus sits deeper, inside the muscularis, so the tumor has not reached it.","Correct. The submucosal plexus lies in the submucosa, and the inner circular layer of the muscularis separates the submucosa from the myenteric plexus."],"comp":110,"competencyId":"w5-enteric-plexuses"},{"id":"N-a17d-111-1","dok":3,"q":"On the cadaver, the first thing you see after opening the abdominal wall is a fatty apron. You lift it upward toward the head. Which fold are you holding, and which organ is attached to its back surface?","a":"The greater omentum, with the transverse colon on its back.","options":["The greater omentum, with the jejunum and ileum on its back.","The greater omentum, with the transverse colon on its back.","The mesentery, with the coils of the small intestine on it.","The lesser omentum, with the transverse colon on its back."],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The greater omentum hangs from the greater curvature of the stomach, drapes down in front of the small intestine, then folds back up and attaches to the transverse colon. It is the fatty apron you see first when the abdominal wall is opened. Lifting it toward the head shows the transverse colon on its back surface and the coils of small intestine below it.","notes":["The fold is right, but the jejunum and ileum lie below it on their own mesentery. The greater omentum folds back up to attach to the transverse colon.","Correct. The greater omentum is the fatty apron, and lifting it shows the transverse colon on its back surface.","The mesentery holds the jejunum and ileum to the posterior wall. It lies deep, behind the apron, not on the surface when you open the abdomen.","The lesser omentum runs from the lesser curvature of the stomach up to the liver. It is not the apron that covers the intestines."],"comp":111,"competencyId":"w5-peritoneum-folds"},{"id":"N-a17d-111-2","dok":3,"q":"A tumor spreads along the peritoneal fold that runs from the lesser curvature of the stomach up to the liver. Which structures in the free edge of this fold could it reach?","a":"The hepatic artery, hepatic portal vein, and common bile duct.","options":["The blood vessels and nerves of the transverse colon.","The ligamentum teres, the remnant of the umbilical vein.","The hepatic artery, hepatic portal vein, and common bile duct.","The blood vessels and nerves of the jejunum and ileum."],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Two steps solve this. First, the fold from the lesser curvature of the stomach and the first part of the duodenum up to the inferior surface of the liver is the lesser omentum. Second, its free edge carries the vessels and duct that serve the liver: the hepatic artery, the hepatic portal vein, and the common bile duct.","notes":["The vessels of the transverse colon run in the transverse mesocolon, which attaches it to the posterior abdominal wall.","The ligamentum teres runs in the free lower edge of the falciform ligament, which attaches the liver to the anterior abdominal wall, not to the stomach.","Correct. This fold is the lesser omentum, and its free edge carries the hepatic artery, the hepatic portal vein, and the common bile duct.","The vessels and nerves of the jejunum and ileum run in the mesentery, which attaches them to the posterior abdominal wall."],"comp":111,"competencyId":"w5-peritoneum-folds"},{"id":"N-a17d-111-3","dok":3,"q":"In the pelvis you lift an S-shaped loop of large intestine and follow the fold that holds it back to the posterior abdominal wall. Which fold are you following?","a":"The sigmoid mesocolon.","options":["The sigmoid mesocolon.","The transverse mesocolon.","The mesentery.","The greater omentum."],"correctIndex":0,"why":"First identify the organ: the S-shaped part of the large intestine in the pelvis is the sigmoid colon. Then find its fold: the sigmoid colon hangs on the sigmoid mesocolon, which attaches it to the posterior abdominal wall and carries its vessels and nerves. That fold is why this part of the colon can be lifted.","notes":["Correct. The S-shaped loop is the sigmoid colon, and the sigmoid mesocolon attaches it to the posterior abdominal wall.","The transverse mesocolon holds the transverse colon, which crosses the abdomen below the stomach, not the S-shaped loop in the pelvis.","The mesentery is the fan-shaped fold that attaches the jejunum and ileum to the posterior wall. It does not hold the colon.","The greater omentum hangs from the stomach and attaches to the transverse colon. It does not attach the colon to the posterior wall."],"comp":111,"competencyId":"w5-peritoneum-folds"},{"id":"N-a17d-112-1","dok":3,"q":"On the cadaver, a part of the large intestine on the left side of the abdomen has teniae coli and haustra, but it stays fixed to the posterior wall and you cannot lift it on a fold. Which part is it, and what is its position?","a":"The descending colon, which is retroperitoneal.","options":["The transverse colon, which is intraperitoneal.","The ascending colon, which is retroperitoneal.","The sigmoid colon, which is retroperitoneal.","The descending colon, which is retroperitoneal."],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The teniae coli and haustra tell you this is colon. A retroperitoneal organ lies behind the parietal peritoneum with no mesentery, so it is held firmly in place. The colon alternates: the ascending colon (right) and descending colon (left) are retroperitoneal, while the transverse and sigmoid colon hang on mesocolons. A fixed colon segment on the left is the descending colon.","notes":["The transverse colon is intraperitoneal, but it crosses the abdomen on its mesocolon and can be lifted, so it does not fit a fixed segment on the left.","The ascending colon is retroperitoneal, but it is fixed to the right posterior wall, not the left.","The sigmoid colon hangs on the sigmoid mesocolon, so it can be lifted. It is intraperitoneal.","Correct. The descending colon runs down the left side, fixed to the posterior wall with no mesocolon, so it is retroperitoneal."],"comp":112,"competencyId":"w5-retroperitoneal"}]},{"id":"m4-mouth-esophagus","title":"Mouth, teeth, pharynx and esophagus","summary":"oral cavity, tongue, teeth, pharynx, esophagus, sphincters","mw":"2026-11-04","tr":"2026-11-10","cards":[{"id":"L02182","dok":3,"q":"Trace a bolus (a swallowed ball of food) from the oral cavity to the stomach. Which sequence is correct?","a":"Oral cavity, oropharynx, laryngopharynx, esophagus, lower esophageal sphincter, stomach.","options":["Oral cavity, oropharynx, laryngopharynx, esophagus, lower esophageal sphincter, stomach","Oral cavity, laryngopharynx, oropharynx, esophagus, lower esophageal sphincter, stomach","Oral cavity, oropharynx, laryngopharynx, esophagus, pyloric sphincter, stomach","Oral cavity, nasopharynx, oropharynx, esophagus, lower esophageal sphincter, stomach"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Food leaves the mouth through the fauces into the oropharynx, then drops into the laryngopharynx, which sits behind the larynx and leads into the esophagus. The esophagus carries the bolus down through the diaphragm, and the lower esophageal sphincter at its bottom opens to let the food into the stomach. The nasopharynx is above the soft palate and carries only air, so food never passes through it.","notes":["Correct. The bolus passes the oropharynx and laryngopharynx, travels the esophagus, and enters the stomach through the lower esophageal sphincter.","This sequence reverses the two lower pharynx regions. The oropharynx sits just behind the mouth and comes first, and the laryngopharynx lies below it, just above the esophagus.","The pyloric sphincter guards the exit of the stomach into the duodenum, not the entrance. Pick it if the question traced food leaving the stomach rather than entering it.","This sequence sends food through the nasopharynx, which lies above the soft palate and carries only air. During swallowing the uvula and soft palate close it off. The nasopharynx would belong in the answer only for the path of inhaled air through the nose."],"lt":"t-alimentary-canal","lid":"fr2","comp":118,"competencyId":"w5-food-pathway"},{"id":"L02188","dok":1,"q":"What type of epithelium lines the esophagus?","a":"Nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium.","options":["Keratinized stratified squamous epithelium","Nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium","Pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium","Simple columnar epithelium"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The esophagus carries rough, swallowed food, so its lining must resist abrasion. Stratified squamous epithelium has many layers of cells, so worn surface cells can be lost without exposing the tissue below. It is nonkeratinized, meaning it lacks the tough, dry protein layer of the skin, because the esophagus is kept moist. The same epithelium lines the mouth, and the lining changes to simple columnar where the esophagus meets the stomach.","notes":["Keratinized stratified squamous epithelium is the dry, waterproof epidermis of the skin. Pick it if the question asked about the outer surface of the body rather than a moist internal tube.","Correct. The esophagus is lined by moist, many-layered nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium that withstands abrasion.","Pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium lines the respiratory tract, such as the trachea, where cilia sweep mucus. Choose it for an airway question, not for the food tube.","Simple columnar epithelium is a single layer of tall cells built for secretion and absorption. It lines the stomach and the intestines, so it would be the answer for those organs, not the esophagus."],"lt":"t-alimentary-canal","lid":"fr8","comp":113,"competencyId":"w5-mouth-pharynx-esophagus"},{"id":"L02189","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares the hard palate and the soft palate?","a":"The hard palate is bony and forms the anterior roof of the mouth; the soft palate is muscular and forms the posterior roof.","options":["The hard palate is cartilage and forms the anterior roof; the soft palate is muscular and forms the posterior roof","The hard palate is bony and forms the posterior roof; the soft palate is muscular and forms the anterior roof","The hard palate is muscular and forms the anterior roof; the soft palate is bony and forms the posterior roof","The hard palate is bony and forms the anterior roof; the soft palate is muscular and forms the posterior roof"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Together the two palates form the roof of the mouth. The hard palate is the bony anterior part, formed by the maxillae and palatine bones and covered by mucous membrane. The soft palate is the muscular posterior part, and its finger-like uvula closes off the nasopharynx during swallowing. You can feel this with your tongue: the front of the roof is firm and the back is soft.","notes":["The hard palate is bone, formed by the maxillae and palatine bones, not cartilage. Cartilage would fit a question about the framework of the larynx or the external nose, not the roof of the mouth.","This swaps the positions. The hard palate is in front, right behind the upper teeth, and the soft palate lies behind it, ending in the uvula.","This swaps the tissues. The hard palate is the one built on bone, which is why it feels firm, and the soft palate is the muscular one that can move during swallowing.","Correct. The bony hard palate forms the front of the roof of the mouth and the muscular soft palate forms the back."],"lt":"t-alimentary-canal","lid":"fr9","comp":113,"competencyId":"w5-mouth-pharynx-esophagus"},{"id":"L02196","dok":1,"q":"What is the lingual frenulum?","a":"The midline fold of mucous membrane that anchors the tongue to the floor of the mouth.","options":["The finger-like projection of the soft palate at the back of the mouth","The midline fold of mucous membrane that anchors the tongue to the floor of the mouth","The projections on the tongue surface that carry taste buds","The opening from the mouth into the oropharynx"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The tongue is skeletal muscle covered with mucous membrane, and it needs to move freely while staying attached. The lingual frenulum is the thin midline fold of mucous membrane on the underside of the tongue that anchors it to the floor of the mouth. You can see it by lifting the tip of your tongue in a mirror.","notes":["The finger-like projection of the soft palate is the uvula, which closes off the nasopharynx during swallowing. Pick it if the question asked what hangs from the back of the soft palate.","Correct. The lingual frenulum is the midline fold under the tongue that anchors it to the floor of the mouth.","The projections on the tongue surface that carry taste buds and help grip food are the papillae. Choose papillae for a question about the rough upper surface of the tongue.","The opening from the mouth into the oropharynx is the fauces. It would be the answer if the question asked where food passes from the mouth into the throat."],"lt":"t-alimentary-canal","lid":"fr16","comp":120,"competencyId":"w5-teeth-tongue"},{"id":"L02202","dok":1,"q":"Which structure is the muscular tube that carries food from the laryngopharynx down to the stomach?","a":"The esophagus.","options":["The pharynx","The trachea","The esophagus","The duodenum"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The esophagus is a collapsible muscular tube, about 25 cm (10 inches) long, that carries food from the laryngopharynx to the stomach. It runs posterior to the trachea through the thorax and passes through the diaphragm at the esophageal hiatus. It is collapsed when empty and opens as a bolus passes.","notes":["The pharynx is the funnel-shaped throat passage that food passes through before reaching the esophagus. Choose it if the question asked where swallowed food goes right after the mouth.","The trachea is the airway that lies directly anterior to the esophagus and carries air to the lungs. Pick it if the question asked which tube is held open by C-shaped cartilage rings.","Correct. The esophagus carries food from the laryngopharynx to the stomach.","The duodenum is the first region of the small intestine, just past the stomach. It would be the answer if the question asked where food goes after the stomach."],"lt":"t-alimentary-canal","lid":"c6","comp":113,"competencyId":"w5-mouth-pharynx-esophagus"},{"id":"L02220","dok":1,"q":"The hard palate and the soft palate together form which part of the oral cavity?","a":"The roof of the mouth.","options":["The opening into the oropharynx","The lateral walls of the mouth","The floor of the mouth","The roof of the mouth"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The palate is the roof of the mouth. The hard palate forms the bony anterior part, and the soft palate forms the muscular posterior part, ending in the uvula. Together they separate the oral cavity below from the nasal cavity above, and the soft palate rises during swallowing to close off the nasopharynx.","notes":["The fauces is the opening between the mouth and the oropharynx, framed by the palatal arches. It is a passage at the back of the mouth, not the roof, so choose it if the question asked where food passes from the mouth into the throat.","The lateral walls of the mouth are the cheeks. They would be the answer if the question asked what bounds the oral cavity on each side.","The floor of the mouth is the mylohyoid muscle, and the tongue rests on it. The palates lie above the tongue, so they cannot form the floor.","Correct. The hard and soft palates together form the roof of the mouth."],"lt":"t-alimentary-canal","lid":"c24","comp":113,"competencyId":"w5-mouth-pharynx-esophagus"},{"id":"L02221","dok":2,"q":"Which two regions of the pharynx carry swallowed food toward the esophagus?","a":"The oropharynx and laryngopharynx.","options":["The laryngopharynx and larynx","The nasopharynx and oropharynx","The oropharynx and laryngopharynx","The nasopharynx and laryngopharynx"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The pharynx is a funnel-shaped passage with three regions stacked top to bottom: the nasopharynx, oropharynx, and laryngopharynx. Food passes from the mouth through the fauces into the oropharynx, then down into the laryngopharynx, which leads into the esophagus. The nasopharynx, behind the nasal cavity, carries only air and is closed off by the soft palate and uvula during swallowing.","notes":["The larynx is the voice box, part of the airway that leads to the trachea, not a region of the pharynx. Food entering the larynx is choking. The larynx would be the answer for a question about where air goes after the laryngopharynx.","The oropharynx does carry food, but the nasopharynx carries only air and is sealed off during swallowing. Pick this pair for the path of air from the nose into the throat.","Correct. The oropharynx and laryngopharynx carry swallowed food toward the esophagus.","The laryngopharynx carries food, but the nasopharynx does not, and this pair skips the oropharynx that food enters first from the mouth."],"lt":"t-alimentary-canal","lid":"c25","comp":113,"competencyId":"w5-mouth-pharynx-esophagus"},{"id":"L02229","dok":2,"q":"Which ring of muscle at the bottom of the esophagus closes to keep stomach contents from rising into the esophagus?","a":"The lower esophageal sphincter.","options":["The pyloric sphincter","The upper esophageal sphincter","The lower esophageal sphincter","The ileocecal sphincter"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The lower esophageal sphincter, also called the cardiac sphincter, is the ring of muscle at the bottom of the esophagus where it meets the stomach. It opens to let a bolus into the stomach and closes to keep acidic stomach contents out of the esophagus. When it is weak, reflux occurs, as in GERD.","notes":["The pyloric sphincter is at the exit of the stomach into the duodenum, not the entrance. Pick it if the question asked which sphincter controls food leaving the stomach.","The upper esophageal sphincter guards the top of the esophagus, where it meets the laryngopharynx. It would be the answer if the question asked which sphincter a bolus passes right after the pharynx.","Correct. The lower esophageal sphincter closes to keep stomach contents from rising into the esophagus.","The ileocecal sphincter sits where the ileum meets the large intestine. Choose it if the question asked about the junction of the small and large intestines."],"lt":"t-alimentary-canal","lid":"c33","comp":114,"competencyId":"w5-canal-sphincters"},{"id":"L02256","dok":1,"q":"Which sphincter is also called the cardiac sphincter?","a":"The lower esophageal sphincter.","options":["The upper esophageal sphincter","The lower esophageal sphincter","The ileocecal sphincter","The pyloric sphincter"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The lower esophageal sphincter guards the bottom of the esophagus, where it opens into the cardia of the stomach. Because it sits at the cardia, it is also called the cardiac sphincter. The name has nothing to do with the heart's own muscle, even though the cardia lies just below the heart.","notes":["The upper esophageal sphincter guards the top of the esophagus, where it meets the laryngopharynx. Pick it if the question asked which sphincter lies at the start of the esophagus.","Correct. The lower esophageal sphincter is also called the cardiac sphincter.","The ileocecal sphincter sits where the ileum meets the large intestine. Choose it for a question about the junction of the small and large intestines.","The pyloric sphincter guards the exit of the stomach into the duodenum, at the opposite end from the cardia. It would be the answer if the question asked which sphincter controls food leaving the stomach."],"lt":"t-alimentary-canal","lid":"c15-x2","comp":114,"competencyId":"w5-canal-sphincters"},{"id":"L02257","dok":1,"q":"A swallowed bolus has just passed through the lower esophageal sphincter. Which region does it enter?","a":"The stomach.","options":["The laryngopharynx","The oropharynx","The duodenum","The stomach"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Food travels from the mouth through the oropharynx and laryngopharynx into the esophagus. The lower esophageal sphincter sits at the bottom of the esophagus and opens into the cardia of the stomach. So once the bolus passes this sphincter, it is in the stomach.","notes":["The laryngopharynx is the lowest part of the pharynx, just above the esophagus, so the bolus passes it before entering the esophagus. Choose it if the question asked which region leads into the esophagus.","The oropharynx is passed early in swallowing, right after the mouth. It would be the answer if the question asked where food goes after the fauces.","The duodenum comes after the stomach, past the pyloric sphincter. Pick it if the question said the food had just passed the pyloric sphincter.","Correct. The lower esophageal sphincter opens into the stomach."],"lt":"t-alimentary-canal","lid":"c16-x2","comp":114,"competencyId":"w5-canal-sphincters"},{"id":"L02278","dok":3,"q":"The esophagus is a simple muscular tube with no villi or absorptive folds, while the small intestine is covered in them. Which statement best explains this difference?","a":"The esophagus only transports food to the stomach, so it needs muscle to push food, not surface area to absorb it.","options":["The esophagus must stay permanently open, and folds would block its lumen","The esophagus only transports food to the stomach, so it needs muscle to push food, not surface area to absorb it","The esophagus absorbs water from swallowed food, a job that needs no folds","The esophagus stores food between meals, so it needs a smooth wall that stretches"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Structure matches function along the canal. The esophagus simply carries swallowed food from the laryngopharynx to the stomach, so it needs a strong muscular wall to push each bolus down and a tough, abrasion-resistant lining. It does no absorbing, so it has no use for the villi and folds that give the small intestine its huge absorptive surface.","notes":["The esophagus is actually collapsible, flattened when empty and opening only as a bolus passes. A tube held permanently open by rings would be the trachea, which is the answer for a question about the airway.","Correct. The esophagus is built for transport, not absorption.","Absorbing water from waste is the main job of the large intestine, not the esophagus. Choose the large intestine for a question about where water is reclaimed and feces form.","Storing food between meals is the job of the stomach, whose rugae flatten so it can stretch. Pick the stomach for a question about holding a meal."],"lt":"t-alimentary-canal","lid":"d3-t-alimentary-canal-7","comp":116,"competencyId":"w5-small-intestine"},{"id":"N-m4-mouth-esophagus-1","dok":1,"q":"Which finger-like projection of the soft palate closes off the nasopharynx during swallowing?","a":"The uvula.","options":["The lingual frenulum","The uvula","The palatine tonsil","The epiglottis"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The uvula hangs from the back edge of the soft palate. During swallowing, the soft palate and uvula rise to close off the nasopharynx so that food and liquid head down toward the esophagus instead of up into the nasal cavity. You can see it at the back of your mouth in a mirror.","notes":["The lingual frenulum is the midline fold of mucous membrane that anchors the tongue to the floor of the mouth. Pick it if the question asked what holds the tongue down.","Correct. The uvula closes off the nasopharynx during swallowing.","The palatine tonsil is lymphatic tissue between the palatal arches at the sides of the back of the mouth, not a projection of the soft palate. It would be the answer for lymphatic tissue guarding the oropharynx.","The epiglottis is a flap of elastic cartilage on the larynx that covers the airway below during swallowing, not the nasopharynx above. Choose it if the question asked what keeps food out of the larynx."],"comp":113,"competencyId":"w5-mouth-pharynx-esophagus"},{"id":"N-m4-mouth-esophagus-2","dok":1,"q":"What is the opening from the mouth into the oropharynx called?","a":"The fauces.","options":["The uvula","The esophageal hiatus","The fauces","The pyloric canal"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The fauces is the opening at the back of the oral cavity that leads into the oropharynx. Swallowed food passes through it on the way from the mouth to the throat. The palatine tonsils sit on either side of the fauces, between the palatal arches, where they guard this entrance.","notes":["The uvula is the finger-like projection of the soft palate, hanging just above the fauces rather than being the opening itself. It would be the answer for what closes off the nasopharynx during swallowing.","The esophageal hiatus is the opening in the diaphragm that the esophagus passes through, much farther down. Pick it if the question asked where the esophagus passes from the thorax into the abdomen.","Correct. The fauces is the opening from the mouth into the oropharynx.","The pyloric canal is part of the pyloric region of the stomach, leading toward the pylorus. Choose it for a question about the exit end of the stomach."],"comp":113,"competencyId":"w5-mouth-pharynx-esophagus"},{"id":"N-m4-mouth-esophagus-3","dok":1,"q":"What is the opening in the diaphragm that the esophagus passes through called?","a":"The esophageal hiatus.","options":["The fauces","The cardia","The lower esophageal sphincter","The esophageal hiatus"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The esophagus runs down through the thorax behind the trachea and must cross the diaphragm to reach the stomach in the abdomen. It passes through an opening in the diaphragm called the esophageal hiatus. Just below the hiatus, the lower esophageal sphincter opens into the cardia of the stomach. If part of the stomach pushes up through this opening, the result is a hiatal hernia.","notes":["The fauces is the opening from the mouth into the oropharynx, at the top of the swallowing path. Pick it if the question asked where food leaves the mouth.","The cardia is the region of the stomach that surrounds the opening from the esophagus, below the diaphragm. Choose it if the question asked which stomach region the esophagus opens into.","The lower esophageal sphincter is a ring of muscle at the bottom of the esophagus, not an opening in the diaphragm. It would be the answer for what keeps stomach contents out of the esophagus.","Correct. The esophagus passes through the diaphragm at the esophageal hiatus."],"comp":113,"competencyId":"w5-mouth-pharynx-esophagus"},{"id":"L02343","dok":1,"q":"What anchors the tongue to the floor of the mouth?","a":"The lingual frenulum.","options":["The uvula","The lingual frenulum","The fauces","The papillae"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The lingual frenulum is the thin midline fold of mucous membrane under the tongue that anchors it to the floor of the mouth. You can see it by lifting the tip of your tongue toward the roof of your mouth. The submandibular ducts open on either side of it.","notes":["The uvula is the small fleshy projection that hangs from the back of the soft palate. It would be the answer for the structure dangling at the back of the mouth.","Correct. The lingual frenulum anchors the tongue to the floor of the mouth.","The fauces is the opening from the mouth into the oropharynx. Pick it for the passage at the back of the oral cavity.","Papillae are the small projections on the upper surface of the tongue that carry taste buds and help grip food. Pick them for the structures that hold taste buds."],"lt":"t-accessory-digestive-organs","lid":"c3-x2","comp":120,"competencyId":"w5-teeth-tongue"},{"id":"L02361","dok":1,"q":"Which muscles originate outside the tongue and move it side to side and in and out?","a":"The extrinsic tongue muscles.","options":["The intrinsic tongue muscles","The buccinator muscles","The extrinsic tongue muscles","The masseter muscles"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Extrinsic means outside, so the extrinsic tongue muscles start on bone or on the soft palate outside the tongue and end inside it. Because they anchor elsewhere, they can move the whole tongue side to side and in and out. The intrinsic muscles, which start and end inside the tongue, change its shape for speech and swallowing.","notes":["The intrinsic tongue muscles lie entirely within the tongue and change its shape and size for speech and swallowing. Pick them for reshaping the tongue rather than moving it as a whole.","The buccinator muscles form the wall of the cheeks and press food back between the teeth while chewing. Pick them for the cheek muscle, not a tongue muscle.","Correct. The extrinsic tongue muscles move the tongue as a whole.","The masseter muscles are chewing muscles that run from the zygomatic arch to the mandible and close the jaw. They would be the answer for a question about the muscle that elevates the mandible."],"lt":"t-accessory-digestive-organs","lid":"c21-x2","comp":120,"competencyId":"w5-teeth-tongue"},{"id":"L02378","dok":2,"q":"Incisors at the front of the mouth have a flat, chisel-like edge, while molars at the back have a broad surface with several cusps. What does this difference allow?","a":"Incisors cut food and molars grind it.","options":["Incisors grind food and molars cut it","Incisors and molars secrete different digestive enzymes","Incisors cut food and molars grind it","Only molars chew, while incisors serve only for speech"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Tooth shape matches tooth job. The chisel-like edge of the incisors cuts and bites off pieces of food, while the broad, cusped surface of the molars crushes and grinds those pieces. Molars do the heaviest grinding in the mouth, which turns food into a soft mass the tongue can shape into a bolus.","notes":["This reverses the jobs. A thin chisel edge is suited to cutting, and a broad surface with several cusps is suited to grinding.","Teeth do not secrete enzymes; digestive enzymes in the mouth come from the salivary glands. Pick the salivary glands for the source of salivary amylase.","Correct. Incisors cut and molars grind.","Incisors do help shape some speech sounds, but their main digestive job is biting and cutting food. Students pick this when they think only the back teeth take part in chewing."],"lt":"t-accessory-digestive-organs","lid":"d3x-t-accessory-digestive-organs-1","comp":120,"competencyId":"w5-teeth-tongue"},{"id":"L04353","dok":1,"q":"The hard palate is the bony front part of the roof of the mouth. Which bones form it?","a":"The maxillae and the palatine bones.","options":["The maxillae and the vomer","The palatine bones and sphenoid","The maxillae and palatine bones","The mandible and palatine bones"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The hard palate is the bony anterior roof of the mouth and the floor of the nasal cavity. The palatine processes of the two maxillae form its front portion, and the horizontal plates of the two palatine bones form its back portion. Behind it, the soft palate continues as a muscular flap that ends in the uvula.","notes":["The vomer is the thin bone that forms the lower part of the nasal septum. It sits on top of the hard palate rather than forming it, and it is the answer for a question about the bony nasal septum.","The sphenoid is the bat-shaped bone in the middle of the cranial floor, behind the nasal cavity. Only the palatine half of this pair is correct, and the sphenoid would be the answer for a question about the sella turcica.","Correct. The maxillae in front and the palatine bones behind form the hard palate.","The mandible is the lower jaw and forms no part of the roof of the mouth. Students pick it because it is the most familiar bone of the mouth, but it would be the answer for the bone holding the lower teeth."],"lt":"t-w5-mouth-pharynx-esophagus","lid":"w5-mouth-pharynx-esophagus-r1","comp":113,"competencyId":"w5-mouth-pharynx-esophagus"},{"id":"L04354","dok":1,"q":"During swallowing, the uvula and soft palate rise. Which space do they close off?","a":"The nasopharynx.","options":["The larynx","The laryngopharynx","The nasopharynx","The oropharynx"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The uvula is the finger-like projection hanging from the back of the soft palate. When you swallow, the soft palate and uvula move upward and close off the nasopharynx, the part of the pharynx behind the nasal cavity. This keeps food and liquid from being pushed up into the nose.","notes":["The larynx is protected during swallowing by the epiglottis, which folds over its opening. Choose the larynx if the question asks what the epiglottis covers, not what the uvula closes.","The laryngopharynx is the lowest part of the pharynx, which leads into the esophagus. Food passes through it on the way down, so it stays open during swallowing.","Correct. The soft palate and uvula seal off the nasopharynx so food does not enter the nose.","The oropharynx is the part of the pharynx behind the mouth, and food has to pass through it to be swallowed. It would be the answer for the region you see at the back of the throat when you open your mouth."],"lt":"t-w5-mouth-pharynx-esophagus","lid":"w5-mouth-pharynx-esophagus-r2","comp":113,"competencyId":"w5-mouth-pharynx-esophagus"},{"id":"L04355","dok":1,"q":"The esophagus is a collapsible muscular tube. Between which two structures does it carry food?","a":"From the laryngopharynx to the stomach.","options":["From the nasopharynx to the stomach","From the laryngopharynx to the stomach","From the oral cavity to the oropharynx","From the stomach to the duodenum"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The esophagus is about 25 cm (10 inches) long. It begins where the laryngopharynx ends, runs down through the thorax behind the trachea, passes through the esophageal hiatus of the diaphragm, and opens into the stomach. Muscular contractions of its wall carry swallowed food down this path.","notes":["The nasopharynx lies above the soft palate and carries air, not food, so the esophagus does not start there. Students pick this when they forget that the pharynx has three regions and only the lower two carry food.","Correct. The esophagus runs from the laryngopharynx to the stomach.","Food moves from the oral cavity into the oropharynx through the fauces, an opening rather than a tube. This would describe the first step of swallowing, not the esophagus.","The passage from the stomach to the duodenum is guarded by the pyloric sphincter and leads into the small intestine. It would be the answer for where food goes after the stomach."],"lt":"t-w5-mouth-pharynx-esophagus","lid":"w5-mouth-pharynx-esophagus-r3","comp":113,"competencyId":"w5-mouth-pharynx-esophagus"},{"id":"L04356","dok":2,"q":"What opening does the lower esophageal (cardiac) sphincter guard?","a":"The opening from the esophagus into the stomach.","options":["The opening from the esophagus into the stomach","The opening from the pharynx into the esophagus","The opening from the ileum into the cecum","The opening from the stomach into the duodenum"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The lower esophageal sphincter is a ring of muscle at the bottom of the esophagus. It guards the opening into the stomach, relaxing to let food pass and closing to keep stomach contents from flowing back up. It is also called the cardiac sphincter because it opens into the cardia, the region of the stomach around the esophageal opening.","notes":["Correct. The lower esophageal sphincter guards the entrance to the stomach.","The opening from the pharynx into the esophagus is guarded by the upper esophageal sphincter. Pick this if the question asks about the sphincter at the top of the esophagus.","The ileocecal sphincter guards the junction where the ileum empties into the cecum of the large intestine. That is the answer for the valve between the small and large intestines.","The pyloric sphincter guards the opening from the stomach into the duodenum. It would be the answer for the sphincter at the far end of the stomach."],"lt":"t-w5-mouth-pharynx-esophagus","lid":"w5-mouth-pharynx-esophagus-r4","comp":114,"competencyId":"w5-canal-sphincters"},{"id":"L04357","dok":3,"q":"A patient has burning pain behind the breastbone after meals as stomach contents rise into the esophagus. Which sphincter has most likely weakened?","a":"The lower esophageal sphincter.","options":["Pyloric sphincter","Ileocecal sphincter","Lower esophageal sphincter","Upper esophageal sphincter"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The lower esophageal sphincter normally closes the bottom of the esophagus so acidic stomach contents stay in the stomach. When it weakens, stomach contents flow back up and irritate the esophageal lining, which is felt as heartburn behind the sternum because the esophagus runs through the thorax there. Long-term reflux of this kind is called GERD.","notes":["The pyloric sphincter controls the passage from the stomach into the duodenum, at the far end of the stomach. It is the answer if the question involves food leaving the stomach too slowly or too fast.","The ileocecal sphincter sits between the small and large intestines in the lower right abdomen, far from the chest. It would be the answer for backflow from the cecum into the ileum.","Correct. A weak lower esophageal sphincter lets stomach contents back up into the esophagus.","The upper esophageal sphincter sits at the top of the esophagus, where it joins the pharynx. It would matter for a problem where food or liquid comes up into the throat, not for acid entering the lower esophagus."],"lt":"t-w5-mouth-pharynx-esophagus","lid":"w5-mouth-pharynx-esophagus-r5","comp":119,"competencyId":"w5-canal-disorders"},{"id":"L04413","dok":1,"q":"Which description matches gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD)?","a":"Stomach contents flow back up through a weak lower esophageal sphincter and irritate the esophagus.","options":["An eroded sore forms in the wall of the stomach or duodenum","Stomach contents flow back through a weak lower esophageal sphincter","Small pouches bulge outward through weak spots in the colon wall","Part of the stomach pushes up through the esophageal hiatus of the diaphragm"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Gastroesophageal reflux disease, or GERD, happens when the lower esophageal sphincter at the bottom of the esophagus is weak. Acidic stomach contents flow back up into the esophagus and irritate its lining, which is felt as heartburn behind the sternum. The name itself tells you the two organs involved, gastro for stomach and esophageal for esophagus.","notes":["An eroded sore in the wall of the stomach or duodenum is a peptic ulcer. It is the answer for a sore that begins in the mucosa and can perforate the wall.","Correct. GERD is reflux of stomach contents through a weak lower esophageal sphincter.","Small pouches bulging through weak spots in the colon wall describe diverticulosis. Choose it for a question about the large intestine.","Part of the stomach pushing up through the esophageal hiatus of the diaphragm is a hiatal hernia. Pick it for a question about the stomach moving into the thorax."],"lt":"t-w5-canal-disorders","lid":"w5-canal-disorders-r1","comp":119,"competencyId":"w5-canal-disorders"},{"id":"N-m4-mouth-esophagus-1-new-m4-limb-gi","dok":1,"q":"Which structure forms the floor of the oral cavity?","a":"Mylohyoid muscle.","options":["Hard palate","Cheeks","Uvula","Mylohyoid muscle"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The oral cavity is bounded by the lips in front, the cheeks on the sides, the hard and soft palate above, and the mylohyoid muscle below. The tongue rests on this muscular floor and fills most of it, which is why students often name the tongue as the floor. The lingual frenulum anchors the underside of the tongue to the floor of the mouth.","notes":["The hard palate is the bony front part of the roof of the mouth. It sits above the tongue, so it is the answer for the roof, not the floor.","The cheeks form the lateral walls of the oral cavity, built around the buccinator muscle. Choose them for the sides of the mouth, not the floor.","The uvula hangs from the back edge of the soft palate, at the top of the mouth. It would be the answer for the projection that helps close off the nasopharynx during swallowing.","Correct. The mylohyoid muscle forms the floor of the oral cavity."],"comp":113,"competencyId":"w5-mouth-pharynx-esophagus"},{"id":"N-m4-mouth-esophagus-2-new-m4-limb-gi","dok":2,"q":"Which pairing correctly matches a wall of the oral cavity with the structure that forms it?","a":"The lateral walls are formed by the cheeks.","options":["Lateral walls: the cheeks","Roof: the tongue","Anterior wall: the uvula of the soft palate","Floor: the hard palate"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The walls of the oral cavity are the lips in front, the cheeks on each side, the hard and soft palate above, and the mylohyoid muscle below, with the tongue resting on it. Only the pairing of the cheeks with the lateral walls matches this layout. The other choices put real mouth structures in the wrong position.","notes":["Correct. The cheeks form the lateral walls of the mouth.","The tongue rests on the floor of the mouth, above the mylohyoid muscle, so it is not the roof. The roof is formed by the hard and soft palate.","The uvula hangs at the back of the soft palate, near the opening to the oropharynx. The anterior wall of the mouth is formed by the lips.","The hard palate is part of the roof of the mouth, above the tongue. Pick it for the bony front part of the roof."],"comp":113,"competencyId":"w5-mouth-pharynx-esophagus"},{"id":"N-m4-mouth-esophagus-3-new-m4-limb-gi","dok":1,"q":"How many teeth are in a complete adult (permanent) set?","a":"32.","options":["20","24","28","32"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The permanent set has 32 teeth, 16 in each jaw. In each half of each jaw there are two incisors, one canine, two premolars, and three molars, which adds up to eight per quarter and 32 in all. The count includes the third molars, or wisdom teeth.","notes":["Twenty is the number of deciduous, or baby, teeth. Pick it for a child's first set.","Twenty-four has no basis in either set. It usually comes from mixing up the number of teeth per jaw or forgetting a whole tooth type.","Twenty-eight is the count students get if they leave out the four wisdom teeth. Many adults do have 28 teeth after wisdom teeth are removed, but the full set has 32.","Correct. A complete permanent set has 32 teeth."],"comp":120,"competencyId":"w5-teeth-tongue"},{"id":"N-m4-mouth-esophagus-4","dok":1,"q":"How many teeth are in the deciduous (baby) set?","a":"20.","options":["16","20","28","32"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The deciduous teeth are the first set, which erupt in early childhood and are later shed. There are 20 of them, five in each half of each jaw: two incisors, one canine, and two molars. They are gradually replaced by the 32 permanent teeth.","notes":["Sixteen is the number of permanent teeth in one jaw, half of the adult set. It is a common mix-up between one jaw and the whole mouth.","Correct. The deciduous set has 20 teeth.","Twenty-eight is the adult count without the four wisdom teeth. It does not apply to the baby set.","Thirty-two is the number of permanent teeth in a complete adult set. Pick it for the adult count."],"comp":120,"competencyId":"w5-teeth-tongue"},{"id":"N-m4-mouth-esophagus-5","dok":1,"q":"Which substance covers the crown of a tooth and is the hardest material in the body?","a":"Enamel.","options":["Cementum","Pulp","Dentin","Enamel"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Enamel is a heavily mineralized layer that covers the crown, the part of the tooth above the gum. It is the hardest substance in the body, which lets it withstand the forces of chewing. Enamel contains no living cells, so once it is lost it cannot grow back.","notes":["Cementum is a calcified layer that covers the root, not the crown. Choose it for the covering of the part of the tooth inside the socket.","Pulp is the soft tissue in the center of the tooth, holding nerves and blood vessels. It is the softest part, not the hardest.","Dentin forms the bulk of the tooth beneath the enamel. It is hard and bone-like but softer than enamel. Pick it for the main body of the tooth.","Correct. Enamel covers the crown and is the hardest substance in the body."],"comp":120,"competencyId":"w5-teeth-tongue"},{"id":"N-m4-mouth-esophagus-6","dok":1,"q":"What does the pulp cavity of a tooth contain?","a":"Pulp: connective tissue with blood vessels and nerves.","options":["Bone marrow and fat cells","Enamel and dentin","Saliva and mucus","Blood vessels and nerves"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The pulp cavity is the hollow center of the tooth. It is filled with pulp, a soft connective tissue that carries blood vessels and nerves. The pulp extends down into each root as the root canal, where the vessels and nerves enter through a small opening at the tip of the root.","notes":["Bone marrow and fat are found inside bones, not teeth. Teeth sit in bony sockets, but the tooth itself has pulp at its center.","Enamel and dentin are the hard outer layers of the tooth that surround the pulp cavity. They form its walls, not its contents.","Saliva and mucus are secretions of the salivary and lingual glands that bathe the outside of the teeth. They do not fill the inside of a tooth.","Correct. The pulp cavity holds the blood vessels and nerves of the tooth."],"comp":120,"competencyId":"w5-teeth-tongue"},{"id":"N-m4-mouth-esophagus-7","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares dentin and enamel?","a":"Dentin forms the bulk of the tooth beneath the enamel; enamel is harder and covers only the crown.","options":["Dentin forms the bulk; enamel is harder and covers the crown","Enamel forms the bulk; dentin is harder and covers the crown","Both cover only the root and attach to the socket","Both are soft tissues that hold the nerves of the tooth"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Most of a tooth is made of dentin, a hard, bone-like tissue that surrounds the pulp cavity. Over the crown, the dentin is capped by enamel, the hardest substance in the body. Over the root, the dentin is covered by cementum instead.","notes":["Correct. Dentin is the bulk of the tooth and enamel is the harder cap on the crown.","This reverses the two layers. Enamel is the thin, very hard covering of the crown, and dentin lies beneath it as the main body of the tooth.","The root is covered by cementum, which helps attach the tooth to its socket. Enamel stays on the crown, and dentin runs through both crown and root.","The soft tissue holding the nerves is the pulp, at the center of the tooth. Dentin and enamel are both hard mineralized tissues."],"comp":120,"competencyId":"w5-teeth-tongue"},{"id":"N-m4-mouth-esophagus-8","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares the crown and the root of a tooth?","a":"The crown is the exposed part above the gingiva; the root is embedded in the bony socket of the jaw.","options":["Crown is above the gum; root sits in the bony socket","Root is above the gum; crown sits in the bony socket","Both lie in the socket and are covered by cementum","Both lie above the gum and are covered by enamel"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"A tooth has two main regions. The crown is the visible part that projects above the gingiva, or gum, and is covered by enamel. The root is the part anchored in the bony socket of the maxilla or mandible and is covered by cementum. The narrow region where they meet, at the gum line, is the neck.","notes":["Correct. The crown is above the gum and the root is in the socket.","This swaps the two regions. The part you can see when someone smiles is the crown.","Only the root lies in the socket and is covered by cementum. The crown is exposed and covered by enamel.","Only the crown lies above the gum and wears enamel. The root is hidden in the socket."],"comp":120,"competencyId":"w5-teeth-tongue"},{"id":"N-m4-mouth-esophagus-9","dok":2,"q":"What anchors the root of a tooth to the bone of its socket?","a":"The periodontal ligament.","options":["Pulp","Gingiva","Lingual frenulum","Periodontal ligament"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The periodontal ligament is a layer of dense fibrous connective tissue between the cementum of the root and the bony wall of the socket. Its fibers anchor the tooth firmly while allowing a tiny amount of give during chewing. This tooth-in-socket arrangement is a fibrous joint called a gomphosis.","notes":["The pulp is the soft tissue inside the tooth with its nerves and blood vessels. It lies within the tooth rather than attaching it to the jaw.","The gingiva, or gum, is the mucous membrane covering the jaw bone and surrounding the neck of each tooth. It seals around the tooth but does not hold the root in the bone.","The lingual frenulum is a fold of mucous membrane that anchors the tongue to the floor of the mouth. Pick it for the tongue, not the teeth.","Correct. The periodontal ligament anchors the root to the socket."],"comp":120,"competencyId":"w5-teeth-tongue"},{"id":"N-m4-mouth-esophagus-10","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly matches a type of tooth with its main job?","a":"Molars have broad crowns with cusps for grinding food.","options":["Incisors: broad crowns that grind","Molars: broad crowns that grind","Premolars: chisel edges that cut","Canines: flat edges that grind"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Tooth shape follows function. Incisors at the front have chisel-shaped edges for cutting, canines are pointed for tearing, and premolars and molars have broad crowns with raised cusps for crushing and grinding. Molars, at the back of the jaw, have the broadest grinding surfaces.","notes":["Incisors are the front teeth with thin, chisel-shaped edges used for cutting and biting off food. The broad grinding crown belongs to the back teeth.","Correct. Molars have broad, cusped crowns for grinding.","Premolars sit between the canines and molars and have cusps for crushing and grinding. The chisel edge for cutting belongs to the incisors.","Canines are the single pointed teeth beside the incisors, used for tearing and piercing. A flat grinding surface does not fit their shape."],"comp":120,"competencyId":"w5-teeth-tongue"},{"id":"N-m4-mouth-esophagus-11","dok":1,"q":"The teeth commonly called wisdom teeth are which teeth?","a":"The third molars.","options":["Third molars","Canines","Second premolars","First molars"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Each half of each jaw in the permanent set has three molars. The third molars are the last teeth at the back of the jaw and usually erupt last, in the late teens or early twenties, which is why they are called wisdom teeth. They are often removed when there is not enough room in the jaw.","notes":["Correct. Wisdom teeth are the third molars.","The canines are the pointed teeth beside the incisors, near the front of the mouth. Pick them for the teeth used for tearing.","The second premolars sit just in front of the first molars. They are not the last teeth in the jaw.","The first molars are the most forward of the three molars and erupt in childhood. Choose them for the first permanent molars, not the last ones."],"comp":120,"competencyId":"w5-teeth-tongue"},{"id":"N-m4-mouth-esophagus-12","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares the intrinsic and extrinsic muscles of the tongue?","a":"Intrinsic muscles lie within the tongue and change its shape; extrinsic muscles arise outside it and move its position.","options":["Both arise outside the tongue and only move it forward","Intrinsic change its shape; extrinsic move its position","Extrinsic change its shape; intrinsic move its position","Both lie within the tongue and only change its shape"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Intrinsic means within. The intrinsic tongue muscles begin and end inside the tongue and alter its shape and size, which matters for speech and swallowing. The extrinsic muscles arise from structures outside the tongue and insert into it, so they move the whole tongue side to side and in and out.","notes":["Only the extrinsic muscles arise outside the tongue, and they move it in several directions, not just forward. The intrinsic muscles lie within it.","Correct. Intrinsic muscles change shape and extrinsic muscles change position.","This swaps the two groups. Muscles that are entirely within the tongue can reshape it, while muscles anchored outside can pull it into new positions.","Only the intrinsic muscles lie entirely within the tongue. The extrinsic muscles reach it from outside and move it as a whole."],"comp":120,"competencyId":"w5-teeth-tongue"},{"id":"N-m4-mouth-esophagus-13","dok":1,"q":"What are the small projections on the surface of the tongue that carry taste buds and help it grip food?","a":"Papillae.","options":["Villi","Rugae","Teniae coli","Papillae"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The upper surface of the tongue is covered with tiny projections called papillae. They give the tongue its rough texture, which helps it grip and move food during chewing, and many of them carry taste buds.","notes":["Villi are finger-like projections of the small intestine lining that increase surface area for absorption. They are in the small intestine, not on the tongue.","Rugae are the folds of the inner lining of the stomach that flatten as the stomach fills. Pick them for the stomach, not the tongue.","The teniae coli are three bands of longitudinal muscle running along the colon. They would be the answer for the large intestine.","Correct. Papillae carry taste buds and help grip food."],"comp":120,"competencyId":"w5-teeth-tongue"},{"id":"N-m4-mouth-esophagus-14","dok":2,"q":"Which sequence lists the regions of the pharynx from superior to inferior?","a":"Nasopharynx, oropharynx, laryngopharynx.","options":["Nasopharynx, oropharynx, laryngopharynx","Nasopharynx, laryngopharynx, oropharynx","Laryngopharynx, oropharynx, nasopharynx","Oropharynx, nasopharynx, laryngopharynx"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The pharynx is a funnel-shaped tube behind the nasal cavity, mouth, and larynx. Its regions are named for what lies in front of them. The nasopharynx is highest, behind the nasal cavity; the oropharynx is in the middle, behind the mouth; and the laryngopharynx is lowest, behind the larynx, where it leads into the esophagus.","notes":["Correct. From top to bottom the regions are the nasopharynx, oropharynx, and laryngopharynx.","The laryngopharynx is the lowest region, behind the larynx. It cannot lie between the nasopharynx and the oropharynx.","This is the correct order read from bottom to top. Pick it if the question asked for inferior to superior.","The oropharynx lies behind the mouth, below the nasopharynx. Putting it first places the mouth above the nasal cavity."],"comp":113,"competencyId":"w5-mouth-pharynx-esophagus"},{"id":"N-m4-mouth-esophagus-15","dok":1,"q":"Which region of the pharynx carries only air and not food?","a":"The nasopharynx.","options":["Oropharynx","Nasopharynx","Esophagus","Laryngopharynx"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The nasopharynx lies behind the nasal cavity, above the soft palate. During swallowing, the soft palate and uvula rise to seal it off, so food never passes through it. The oropharynx and laryngopharynx, by contrast, carry both air and swallowed food.","notes":["The oropharynx lies behind the mouth and carries both air and food. Pick it for the region that food enters through the fauces.","Correct. The nasopharynx carries only air.","The esophagus is not a region of the pharynx. It carries only food, the opposite of the correct answer.","The laryngopharynx is the lowest region and also carries both air and food before they separate. Choose it for the region that leads into the esophagus."],"comp":113,"competencyId":"w5-mouth-pharynx-esophagus"},{"id":"N-m4-mouth-esophagus-16","dok":2,"q":"At its lower end, the laryngopharynx opens into which two tubes?","a":"The esophagus behind and the larynx in front.","options":["Esophagus and larynx","Oral cavity and esophagus","Larynx and nasal cavity","Trachea and nasopharynx"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The laryngopharynx is where the paths for food and air separate. Behind, it continues downward as the esophagus, which carries food to the stomach. In front, it opens into the larynx, which leads to the trachea and lungs. During swallowing the larynx is covered so food goes into the esophagus.","notes":["Correct. The laryngopharynx leads into the esophagus and the larynx.","The oral cavity opens into the oropharynx through the fauces, higher up. Only the esophagus in this pair is correct.","The nasal cavity opens into the nasopharynx at the top of the pharynx. It does not connect to the laryngopharynx.","The trachea lies below the larynx, so the laryngopharynx reaches it only by way of the larynx. The nasopharynx lies above, not below."],"comp":113,"competencyId":"w5-mouth-pharynx-esophagus"},{"id":"N-m4-mouth-esophagus-17","dok":2,"q":"In the neck and thorax, where does the esophagus lie relative to the trachea?","a":"Posterior to the trachea, in front of the vertebral column.","options":["Lateral to the trachea","Anterior to the trachea","Posterior to the trachea","Inside the trachea"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The esophagus runs down the neck and through the mediastinum directly behind the trachea and in front of the vertebral column. The back wall of the trachea has no cartilage, which lets the esophagus bulge forward slightly as a swallowed bite passes. Lower down, the esophagus passes through the esophageal hiatus of the diaphragm to reach the stomach.","notes":["Lateral to the trachea is where the common carotid arteries run on each side. The esophagus is in the midline behind the trachea.","Anterior to the trachea would place the esophagus just under the skin of the front of the neck, where the thyroid gland lies instead.","Correct. The esophagus lies behind the trachea.","The esophagus and trachea are separate tubes. Food and air share a path only in the oropharynx and laryngopharynx, above them."],"comp":113,"competencyId":"w5-mouth-pharynx-esophagus"},{"id":"N-m4-mouth-esophagus-18","dok":2,"q":"Most of the esophagus, in the neck and thorax, lacks a serosa. What forms its outermost layer instead?","a":"A fibrous adventitia.","options":["Adventitia","Submucosa","Visceral peritoneum","Mucosa"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Most of the digestive tube is wrapped in a serosa, the visceral peritoneum, because those organs lie in the peritoneal cavity. The esophagus runs through the neck and thorax, outside that cavity, so its outer covering is instead an adventitia, a layer of fibrous connective tissue that blends with the surrounding tissues.","notes":["Correct. The esophagus in the neck and thorax has an adventitia instead of a serosa.","The submucosa is the connective tissue layer just outside the mucosa, carrying vessels and the submucosal plexus. It lies deep in the wall, not on the outside.","The visceral peritoneum is the serosa that covers abdominal organs such as the stomach and intestines. Pick it for the outer layer of organs inside the peritoneal cavity.","The mucosa is the innermost layer, the lining in contact with food. It is the opposite end of the wall from the correct answer."],"comp":113,"competencyId":"w5-mouth-pharynx-esophagus"},{"id":"N-m4-mouth-esophagus-19","dok":2,"q":"Where is the upper esophageal sphincter?","a":"At the junction of the laryngopharynx and the esophagus.","options":["Where the esophagus joins the cardia of the stomach","Where the laryngopharynx meets the esophagus","Where the mouth meets the oropharynx","Where the pylorus of the stomach meets the duodenum"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The esophagus is guarded by a ring of muscle at each end. The upper esophageal sphincter is at the top, where the laryngopharynx joins the esophagus, and it stays closed between swallows so air does not enter the esophagus. The lower esophageal sphincter is at the bottom, where the esophagus joins the stomach.","notes":["The junction of the esophagus and stomach is guarded by the lower esophageal sphincter, also called the cardiac sphincter. Pick it for the bottom end of the esophagus.","Correct. The upper esophageal sphincter is where the laryngopharynx meets the esophagus.","The opening from the mouth into the oropharynx is the fauces. It is an opening, not a sphincter of the esophagus.","The junction of the stomach and duodenum is guarded by the pyloric sphincter. Choose it for the outlet of the stomach."],"comp":114,"competencyId":"w5-canal-sphincters"},{"id":"N-m4-mouth-esophagus-20","dok":2,"q":"In the permanent set, moving back from the front of the jaw, which type of tooth comes directly after the canine?","a":"The premolars.","options":["Incisors","Premolars","Molars","Wisdom teeth"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"In each half of each jaw, the permanent teeth run from front to back as two incisors, one canine, two premolars, and three molars. The premolars therefore sit right behind the canine. Their cusped crowns are the start of the crushing and grinding surface that continues back through the molars.","notes":["The incisors are in front of the canine, at the very front of the jaw. Pick them for the chisel-shaped cutting teeth.","Correct. The premolars come directly after the canine.","The molars come after the premolars, at the back of the jaw. They are one step too far back.","The wisdom teeth are the third molars, the last teeth at the very back of the jaw. They are the farthest teeth from the canine, not the next ones."],"comp":120,"competencyId":"w5-teeth-tongue"},{"id":"N-m4-mouth-esophagus-21","dok":3,"q":"A cavity has worn through a patient's enamel and dentin, and the tooth now aches constantly. Which part of the tooth has the decay most likely reached?","a":"The pulp.","options":["Gingiva","Pulp","Enamel","Cementum"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"A tooth has layers from the outside in: enamel over the crown, dentin beneath it, and the pulp cavity at the center. Enamel has no nerves, so decay there is often painless. Once decay passes through the dentin and reaches the pulp, it contacts the nerves and blood vessels there, which causes the constant ache.","notes":["The gingiva is the gum tissue around the tooth, outside it entirely. Gum disease affects it, but tooth decay works inward through the hard layers.","Correct. Decay through enamel and dentin reaches the pulp and its nerves.","The enamel is the outermost layer and has already been worn through. It contains no nerves, so it is not the source of the ache.","Cementum covers the root, below the gum. Decay coming down through the crown passes enamel and dentin to the pulp rather than heading for the root surface."],"comp":120,"competencyId":"w5-teeth-tongue"},{"id":"N-m4-mouth-esophagus-22","dok":3,"q":"Below the gum line, a dentist begins cleaning the outer surface of a tooth's root. Which covering does the instrument touch first?","a":"Cementum.","options":["Pulp","Enamel","Dentin","Cementum"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The root of a tooth is the part held in the bony socket below the gum. Its outer surface is covered by cementum, a calcified, bone-like layer that also gives attachment to the periodontal ligament. Beneath the cementum lies dentin, and at the center is the pulp.","notes":["The pulp lies at the center of the tooth, inside the dentin. It is the deepest part, not the outer surface.","Enamel covers the crown above the gum line, not the root. Pick it if the dentist were working on the visible chewing surface.","Dentin is the bulk of the tooth beneath the cementum. The instrument would reach it only after passing through the cementum.","Correct. Cementum covers the outer surface of the root."],"comp":120,"competencyId":"w5-teeth-tongue"},{"id":"N-m4-mouth-esophagus-23","dok":3,"q":"A newborn cannot push her tongue past her gums, and the fold of tissue tying the underside of her tongue to the floor of the mouth looks unusually short. Which structure is involved?","a":"The lingual frenulum.","options":["Uvula","Lingual frenulum","Palatine tonsil","Soft palate"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The lingual frenulum is a midline fold of mucous membrane that anchors the underside of the tongue to the floor of the mouth. When it is unusually short or attached too far forward, it restricts how far the tongue can move, a condition commonly called tongue-tie. This can interfere with feeding and, later, with speech.","notes":["The uvula hangs from the back of the soft palate and helps seal off the nasopharynx during swallowing. It is not attached to the tongue.","Correct. A short lingual frenulum restricts the tongue and causes tongue-tie.","The palatine tonsil is lymphatic tissue on the side wall of the oropharynx, between the palatal arches. It does not tie down the tongue.","The soft palate is the muscular back part of the roof of the mouth. It forms the roof, not a fold beneath the tongue."],"comp":120,"competencyId":"w5-teeth-tongue"},{"id":"N-m4-mouth-esophagus-24","dok":3,"q":"Imaging shows that the top of a patient's stomach has slid upward through the opening in the diaphragm that normally passes the esophagus. Through which opening did it move?","a":"The esophageal hiatus.","options":["Fauces","Vena caval foramen","Aortic hiatus","Esophageal hiatus"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The esophagus passes from the thorax into the abdomen through the esophageal hiatus, an opening in the diaphragm. In a hiatal hernia, part of the stomach pushes up through this same opening into the thorax. This can weaken the barrier at the lower esophageal sphincter and is linked with heartburn.","notes":["The fauces is the opening from the mouth into the oropharynx, far above the diaphragm. It would be the answer for the back of the mouth.","The vena caval foramen passes the inferior vena cava through the diaphragm on its way to the right atrium. It carries a vein, not the digestive tube.","The aortic hiatus is the opening at the back of the diaphragm that passes the aorta. Pick it for the path of the aorta from the thorax into the abdomen.","Correct. A hiatal hernia occurs through the esophageal hiatus."],"comp":119,"competencyId":"w5-canal-disorders"},{"id":"N-m4-mouth-esophagus-25","dok":3,"q":"A patient with a sore throat opens wide, and swollen, red masses of lymphatic tissue are visible on the side walls of the throat between the palatal arches. In which region of the pharynx are they located?","a":"The oropharynx (these are the palatine tonsils).","options":["Nasopharynx","Oropharynx","Oral cavity proper","Laryngopharynx"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The palatine tonsils sit in the side walls of the throat between the two palatal arches, just behind the fauces. That places them in the oropharynx, the part of the pharynx directly behind the mouth. Because the oropharynx can be seen through an open mouth, swollen palatine tonsils are easy to spot in tonsillitis.","notes":["The nasopharynx lies behind the nasal cavity, above the soft palate, and holds the pharyngeal tonsil. It cannot be seen just by opening the mouth.","Correct. The palatine tonsils are in the oropharynx.","The oral cavity proper is the space inside the teeth. The tonsils lie behind it, past the fauces, in the throat rather than in the mouth itself.","The laryngopharynx is the lowest region, behind the larynx. It lies too deep to be seen through an open mouth."],"comp":113,"competencyId":"w5-mouth-pharynx-esophagus"},{"id":"N-m4-mouth-esophagus-26","dok":3,"q":"A dentist examines a 5-year-old and counts 20 teeth: incisors, canines, and molars, but no premolars. What does this tell the dentist?","a":"The child still has a complete set of deciduous (baby) teeth.","options":["A complete permanent set","A permanent set without wisdom teeth","A complete deciduous set","Missing premolars from injury"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The deciduous set has 20 teeth made up of incisors, canines, and molars only, with no premolars. The permanent premolars come in later, replacing the deciduous molars. A count of 20 with this mix of tooth types is exactly what a complete baby set looks like.","notes":["A complete permanent set has 32 teeth and includes eight premolars. Twenty teeth with no premolars cannot be a permanent set.","A permanent set without wisdom teeth still has 28 teeth, including premolars. This choice confuses the two sets.","Correct. Twenty teeth with no premolars is a complete deciduous set.","Premolars are not missing here. They are simply not part of the deciduous set, so their absence is normal at this age."],"comp":120,"competencyId":"w5-teeth-tongue"},{"id":"N-a17d-113-1","dok":3,"q":"A swallowed bolus moves down the esophagus and passes through the esophageal hiatus. At about which vertebral level is this, and how does the outer layer of the esophagus change below it?","a":"At about T10; the adventitia gives way to a serosa.","options":["At the cricoid cartilage; the adventitia gives way to a serosa.","At about T10; the adventitia gives way to a serosa.","At the cricoid cartilage; the serosa gives way to an adventitia.","At about T10; the serosa gives way to an adventitia."],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The esophagus begins at the level of the cricoid cartilage and runs down through the mediastinum. It reaches the stomach by passing through the diaphragm at the esophageal hiatus, at about the level of T10. Above the diaphragm, in the neck and thorax, its outer layer is an adventitia. Below the diaphragm the short abdominal segment lies in the abdominal cavity, so it has a serosa.","notes":["The cricoid cartilage marks where the esophagus begins, at the lower end of the laryngopharynx, not where it passes through the diaphragm.","Correct. The hiatus is at about T10, and the short segment below the diaphragm has a serosa instead of an adventitia.","The cricoid level is the top of the esophagus, and the outer layer there is already an adventitia. The hiatus is at about T10.","The level is right, but the change runs the other way. The esophagus has an adventitia above the diaphragm and a serosa below it."],"comp":113,"competencyId":"w5-mouth-pharynx-esophagus"},{"id":"N-a17d-113-2","dok":3,"q":"A child is born with a gap in the bony front part of the roof of the mouth. Which structure is affected, and which space would the mouth then open into?","a":"The hard palate; the mouth would open into the nasal cavity.","options":["The uvula; the mouth would open into the nasopharynx.","The soft palate; the mouth would open into the nasal cavity.","The hard palate; the mouth would open into the nasal cavity.","The hard palate; the mouth would open into the oropharynx."],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The roof of the oral cavity is the palate. The hard palate is its bony front part, formed by the palatine processes of the maxillae and the horizontal plates of the palatine bones. The soft palate behind it is skeletal muscle. Because the palate separates the nasal cavity from the mouth, a gap in the hard palate would leave the mouth open to the nasal cavity above.","notes":["The uvula is the soft finger-shaped tab hanging from the back edge of the soft palate. It is not bone and is not part of the front of the roof.","The soft palate is the back of the roof and is skeletal muscle, not bone. The bony front part is the hard palate.","Correct. The hard palate is the bony front of the roof of the mouth, and it separates the oral cavity from the nasal cavity above.","The structure is right, but the mouth already opens into the oropharynx through the fauces. The palate separates the mouth from the nasal cavity."],"comp":113,"competencyId":"w5-mouth-pharynx-esophagus"}]},{"id":"m4-stomach-intestines","title":"Stomach, small intestine and large intestine","summary":"stomach regions and features, small intestine segments and surface area features, large intestine regions and features, pathway of food, canal disorders","mw":"2026-11-04","tr":"2026-11-10","cards":[{"id":"L02183","dok":2,"q":"The muscularis of the stomach has one more layer of smooth muscle than the rest of the canal. Which layer is the extra one?","a":"The inner oblique layer.","options":["The outer longitudinal layer","The middle circular layer","The muscularis mucosae","The inner oblique layer"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Most of the canal has a muscularis of two smooth muscle layers, an inner circular layer and an outer longitudinal layer. The stomach adds a third, an inner oblique layer whose fibers run at an angle, so its muscularis runs oblique, circular, then longitudinal from the inside out. The extra layer lets the stomach churn and mix its contents, not just squeeze them forward.","notes":["The outer longitudinal layer runs along the whole canal, so it is not unique to the stomach. It would be the answer if the question asked which muscularis layer of the stomach is outermost.","The stomach does have a circular layer, but so does every other region of the canal, so it is not the extra one. Pick it if the question asked which stomach muscle layer sits between the other two.","The muscularis mucosae is a thin sheet of smooth muscle inside the mucosa, not part of the muscularis, and every region of the canal has one. Choose it if the question asked which part of the mucosa is made of smooth muscle.","Correct. The inner oblique layer is found in the stomach and nowhere else in the canal."],"lt":"t-alimentary-canal","lid":"fr3","comp":115,"competencyId":"w5-stomach"},{"id":"L02186","dok":1,"q":"Which list names the four regions of the stomach?","a":"Cardia, fundus, body, and pyloric part.","options":["Fundus, body, pyloric part, and duodenum","Cardia, body, lesser curvature, and pyloric part","Cardia, fundus, rugae, and pyloric part","Cardia, fundus, body, and pyloric part"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The stomach is divided into four regions. The cardia surrounds the opening from the esophagus, the fundus bulges above and to the left of the cardia, the body is the large central portion, and the pyloric part connects the body to the duodenum. Named features such as the rugae and the curvatures are found on these regions but are not regions themselves.","notes":["The duodenum is the first region of the small intestine, just past the pyloric sphincter, so it is not part of the stomach, and this list leaves out the cardia. It would fit a question about where food goes after leaving the stomach.","The lesser curvature is the short, concave medial border of the stomach, an edge rather than a region, and this list also leaves out the fundus. The lesser curvature would be the answer if the question asked where the lesser omentum attaches to the stomach.","Rugae are the folds of the inner stomach lining that flatten as the stomach fills, a feature of the lining rather than a region. Pick rugae if the question asked what lets the stomach lining expand with a meal.","Correct. The cardia, fundus, body, and pyloric part are the four regions of the stomach."],"lt":"t-alimentary-canal","lid":"fr6","comp":115,"competencyId":"w5-stomach"},{"id":"L02187","dok":2,"q":"The pyloric part of the stomach has three subdivisions. In what order does food meet them as it moves toward the duodenum?","a":"Pyloric antrum, pyloric canal, pylorus.","options":["Pyloric antrum, pyloric canal, pylorus","Pylorus, pyloric canal, pyloric antrum","Pyloric canal, pyloric antrum, pylorus","Pyloric antrum, pylorus, pyloric canal"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The pyloric part funnels food from the body of the stomach toward the small intestine. It begins with the wider pyloric antrum next to the body, narrows into the pyloric canal, and ends at the pylorus. The pylorus is guarded by the pyloric sphincter, which opens into the duodenum.","notes":["Correct. Food passes the pyloric antrum, then the pyloric canal, then the pylorus at the junction with the duodenum.","This is the correct order run backward, from the duodenum toward the body of the stomach. It would be right only if the question asked for the order moving away from the duodenum.","This puts the narrow canal before the wide antrum. The antrum is the broad first part next to the body, and it narrows into the canal, not the other way around.","This places the pylorus in the middle, but the pylorus is the final part of the stomach, where the pyloric sphincter meets the duodenum. The canal must come before it."],"lt":"t-alimentary-canal","lid":"fr7","comp":115,"competencyId":"w5-stomach"},{"id":"L02190","dok":2,"q":"Which region of the small intestine begins at the pyloric sphincter and is mostly retroperitoneal?","a":"The duodenum.","options":["The jejunum","The stomach","The duodenum","The ileum"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The small intestine runs duodenum, jejunum, then ileum. The duodenum is the first and shortest region, about 25 cm (10 inches), and it curves in a C shape from the pyloric sphincter around the head of the pancreas. It lies mostly behind the peritoneum, pressed against the posterior abdominal wall, which is what retroperitoneal means. The jejunum and ileum, by contrast, hang within the peritoneal cavity on the mesentery.","notes":["The jejunum is the middle region, about 1 m (3 feet) long, and it hangs from the posterior wall on the mesentery inside the peritoneal cavity. Pick it if the question asked for the middle region of the small intestine.","The stomach sits just before the pyloric sphincter and lies within the peritoneal cavity, held by the omenta. Choose it if the question asked which organ the greater omentum hangs from.","Correct. The duodenum starts at the pyloric sphincter and lies mostly behind the peritoneum.","The ileum is the last and longest region, about 2 m (6 feet), and it ends at the ileocecal sphincter, not the pyloric sphincter. It would be the answer if the question asked which region joins the large intestine."],"lt":"t-alimentary-canal","lid":"fr10","comp":116,"competencyId":"w5-small-intestine"},{"id":"L02191","dok":2,"q":"Which three surface features are found on the large intestine and on no other part of the canal?","a":"Teniae coli, haustra, and omental appendices.","options":["Teniae coli, rugae, and circular folds","Teniae coli, haustra, and omental appendices","Rugae, haustra, and teniae coli","Circular folds, villi, and microvilli"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Three features identify the large intestine. The teniae coli are three flat bands of longitudinal smooth muscle running along the colon. They are shorter than the wall, so the wall bunches up between them into pouches called haustra. Small fat-filled pouches of visceral peritoneum, the omental appendices, hang from the surface of the colon, mostly along the teniae coli.","notes":["Only the teniae coli belong to the large intestine here. Rugae are stomach folds and circular folds are permanent ridges of the small intestine wall.","Correct. The teniae coli, haustra, and omental appendices are found only on the large intestine.","Haustra and teniae coli belong to the colon, but rugae are folds of the stomach lining that flatten as it fills. Pick rugae for a question about the stomach.","Circular folds, villi, and microvilli are the three nested surface features of the small intestine that increase its surface area for absorption. Choose this set if the question asked about the small intestine."],"lt":"t-alimentary-canal","lid":"fr11","comp":117,"competencyId":"w5-large-intestine"},{"id":"L02192","dok":1,"q":"What are gastric pits?","a":"Openings in the stomach lining that lead down to the gastric glands.","options":["Folds of the stomach lining that flatten as the stomach fills","Openings in the stomach lining that lead down to the gastric glands","Finger-like projections of the small intestine mucosa","Pouches of the colon wall formed by the teniae coli"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The stomach lining is dotted with tiny pits called gastric pits. Each pit leads down into gastric glands, which are the secreting glands of the stomach, so the secretions made in the glands empty into the bottom of the pits and rise to the surface. The pits are microscopic and are seen on slides, unlike the large rugae seen on a model.","notes":["Folds of the stomach lining that flatten as the stomach fills are the rugae. Pick rugae if the question asked how the stomach expands to hold a meal.","Correct. Gastric pits are openings in the stomach lining that lead to the gastric glands.","Finger-like projections of the small intestine mucosa are the villi. Choose villi for a question about absorptive surface area in the small intestine.","Pouches of the colon wall formed by the teniae coli are the haustra. They would be the answer for a question about the puckered look of the large intestine."],"lt":"t-alimentary-canal","lid":"fr12","comp":115,"competencyId":"w5-stomach"},{"id":"L02199","dok":1,"q":"Which region of the stomach surrounds the opening where the esophagus enters?","a":"The cardia.","options":["The cardia","The pyloric part","The fundus","The body"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The cardia is the small region of the stomach that surrounds the opening from the esophagus. Its name comes from its position near the heart, just below the diaphragm. The lower esophageal sphincter, also called the cardiac sphincter, guards this opening.","notes":["Correct. The cardia surrounds the opening where the esophagus enters the stomach.","The pyloric part is the region that connects the body to the duodenum at the exit end of the stomach. Choose it if the question asked which region leads to the small intestine.","The fundus is the rounded portion that bulges above and to the left of the cardia. Pick it if the question asked for the dome-shaped top of the stomach.","The body is the large central portion of the stomach. It would be the answer if the question asked for the biggest region of the stomach."],"lt":"t-alimentary-canal","lid":"c3","comp":115,"competencyId":"w5-stomach"},{"id":"L02200","dok":1,"q":"What is the first and shortest region of the small intestine?","a":"The duodenum.","options":["The duodenum","The jejunum","The cecum","The ileum"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The duodenum is the first and shortest region of the small intestine, about 25 cm (10 inches) long, and it curves from the pyloric sphincter of the stomach. Bile and pancreatic juice enter the small intestine here at the major duodenal papilla. After it come the jejunum, about 1 m (3 feet) long, and the ileum, the longest region at about 2 m (6 feet).","notes":["Correct. The duodenum is the first and shortest region, about 25 cm (10 inches) long.","The jejunum is the middle region of the small intestine, about 1 m (3 feet) long. Pick it if the question asked for the region between the duodenum and the ileum.","The cecum is the pouch at the start of the large intestine, on the far side of the ileocecal valve. Choose it if the question asked where the large intestine begins.","The ileum is the last and longest region, about 2 m (6 feet), ending at the ileocecal sphincter. It would be the answer if the question asked for the longest region or the one that joins the large intestine."],"lt":"t-alimentary-canal","lid":"c4","comp":116,"competencyId":"w5-small-intestine"},{"id":"L02201","dok":1,"q":"What are the finger-like projections of the small intestine mucosa called?","a":"Villi.","options":["Teniae coli","Villi","Rugae","Haustra"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Villi are finger-like projections of the mucosa that give the small intestine lining a velvety look. They greatly increase the surface area available for absorbing nutrients. Each villus holds a lacteal, a lymphatic capillary that absorbs dietary fats, and its surface cells carry even smaller microvilli.","notes":["Teniae coli are three bands of longitudinal muscle running along the colon, part of the muscle layer rather than the mucosa. Choose them if the question asked what forms the haustra.","Correct. Villi are the finger-like projections of the small intestine mucosa.","Rugae are folds of the inner stomach lining that flatten as the stomach fills. Pick rugae if the question asked about the stomach lining rather than the small intestine.","Haustra are the pouches that give the colon its puckered look. They would be the answer for a question about the wall of the large intestine."],"lt":"t-alimentary-canal","lid":"c5","comp":116,"competencyId":"w5-small-intestine"},{"id":"L02203","dok":1,"q":"What are the folds of the inner stomach lining that flatten out as the stomach fills?","a":"Rugae.","options":["Villi","Haustra","Plicae circulares","Rugae"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Rugae are folds of the inner stomach lining. When the stomach is empty they lie in ridges, and as a meal arrives they flatten out so the stomach can stretch and hold more. This fits the stomach's role as a holding reservoir and mixing chamber.","notes":["Villi are finger-like projections of the small intestine mucosa that increase surface area for absorption. Pick villi for a question about the absorptive lining of the small intestine.","Haustra are the pouches of the colon formed by the teniae coli. Choose them for a question about the puckered look of the large intestine.","Plicae circulares, or circular folds, are permanent ridges of the mucosa and submucosa in the small intestine, and they do not flatten out. They would be the answer for the largest folds of the small intestine wall.","Correct. Rugae are the stomach folds that flatten as the stomach fills."],"lt":"t-alimentary-canal","lid":"c7","comp":115,"competencyId":"w5-stomach"},{"id":"L02204","dok":1,"q":"What is the ring of smooth muscle that controls the exit from the stomach into the duodenum?","a":"The pyloric sphincter.","options":["The upper esophageal sphincter","The lower esophageal sphincter","The pyloric sphincter","The ileocecal sphincter"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The pyloric sphincter is the ring of smooth muscle between the pylorus and the duodenum. It controls the exit from the stomach, letting the liquefied stomach contents into the small intestine a little at a time. Each sphincter in the canal is named for where it sits, so the pyloric sphincter belongs at the pylorus.","notes":["The upper esophageal sphincter guards the top of the esophagus, where it meets the laryngopharynx. Choose it if the question asked which sphincter a bolus passes first after the pharynx.","The lower esophageal sphincter guards the entrance to the stomach from the esophagus, not the exit. Pick it if the question asked which sphincter keeps stomach contents out of the esophagus.","Correct. The pyloric sphincter controls the passage from the stomach into the duodenum.","The ileocecal sphincter sits where the ileum meets the large intestine, far downstream. It would be the answer if the question asked where the small intestine ends."],"lt":"t-alimentary-canal","lid":"c8","comp":114,"competencyId":"w5-canal-sphincters"},{"id":"L02206","dok":1,"q":"What is the pouch where the large intestine begins, just below the ileocecal valve?","a":"The cecum.","options":["The ascending colon","The rectum","The cecum","The ileum"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The cecum is the blind pouch at the start of the large intestine, just below the ileocecal valve where the ileum enters. The vermiform appendix hangs from it. From the cecum, material moves up into the ascending colon.","notes":["The ascending colon is the first part of the colon, which rises from the cecum up the right side of the abdomen. It would be the answer if the question asked where material goes right after the cecum.","The rectum is the straight segment near the end of the large intestine, anterior to the sacrum and coccyx. Choose it if the question asked where feces are stored before elimination.","Correct. The cecum is the pouch at the start of the large intestine.","The ileum is the last region of the small intestine, which empties into the large intestine at the ileocecal valve. Pick it if the question asked which region lies just before the valve."],"lt":"t-alimentary-canal","lid":"c10","comp":117,"competencyId":"w5-large-intestine"},{"id":"L02207","dok":1,"q":"What are the three bands of longitudinal muscle that run the length of the colon called?","a":"Teniae coli.","options":["Teniae coli","Rugae","Haustra","Omental appendices"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"In the cecum and colon, the outer longitudinal layer of the muscularis is gathered into three flat bands called the teniae coli instead of forming a complete sheet. The bands are shorter than the wall, so the wall bunches up between them into pouches called haustra. The teniae coli, haustra, and omental appendices together mark the large intestine.","notes":["Correct. The teniae coli are the three bands of longitudinal muscle along the colon.","Rugae are folds of the inner stomach lining that flatten as the stomach fills. Choose rugae for a question about the stomach.","Haustra are the pouches of the colon wall, formed by the pull of the teniae coli. Pick haustra if the question asked about the puckered pouches rather than the muscle bands.","Omental appendices are small fat-filled pouches of peritoneum that hang from the surface of the colon, mostly along the teniae coli. They are fat, not muscle, so they would be the answer for the fatty tags on the colon surface."],"lt":"t-alimentary-canal","lid":"c11","comp":117,"competencyId":"w5-large-intestine"},{"id":"L02208","dok":1,"q":"What are the microscopic projections of the absorptive cells that form the brush border called?","a":"Microvilli.","options":["Rugae","Plicae circulares","Villi","Microvilli"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Microvilli are microscopic projections of the plasma membrane on each absorptive cell of the small intestine. Packed together, they look like the bristles of a brush under the microscope, which is why they are called the brush border. They are the smallest of the three surface features, after the circular folds and villi, that expand the absorptive surface.","notes":["Rugae are folds of the stomach lining that flatten as the stomach fills. Choose rugae for a question about the stomach.","Plicae circulares are permanent circular ridges of the mucosa and submucosa, the largest of the small intestine's folds. They would be the answer if the question asked for the biggest surface feature of the small intestine wall.","Villi are finger-like projections of the whole mucosa, each made of many cells. Pick villi if the question asked about the projections that give the lining its velvety look.","Correct. Microvilli are the tiny projections on each absorptive cell that form the brush border."],"lt":"t-alimentary-canal","lid":"c12","comp":116,"competencyId":"w5-small-intestine"},{"id":"L02209","dok":1,"q":"Food leaving the stomach passes through the pyloric sphincter. Which region does it enter next?","a":"The duodenum.","options":["The cecum","The jejunum","The duodenum","The esophagus"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The pyloric sphincter sits between the pylorus of the stomach and the duodenum. Food that passes it enters the duodenum, the first region of the small intestine, where bile and pancreatic juice are added. From there it continues into the jejunum and then the ileum.","notes":["The cecum is the pouch at the start of the large intestine, reached only after the whole small intestine. Choose it if the question asked where food goes after passing the ileocecal valve.","The jejunum is the middle region of the small intestine, reached only after the duodenum. Pick it if the question asked where food goes after the duodenum.","Correct. Food passing the pyloric sphincter enters the duodenum.","The esophagus comes before the stomach and delivers food to it through the lower esophageal sphincter. It would be the answer if the question asked which region food leaves to enter the stomach."],"lt":"t-alimentary-canal","lid":"c13","comp":114,"competencyId":"w5-canal-sphincters"},{"id":"L02211","dok":1,"q":"The duodenum, jejunum, and ileum are the three regions of which organ?","a":"The small intestine.","options":["The pharynx","The large intestine","The small intestine","The stomach"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The small intestine is the long, coiled region of the canal where most absorption happens, and it has three regions. The duodenum comes first, curving from the pyloric sphincter, the jejunum is the middle region, and the ileum is the last and longest, joining the large intestine at the ileocecal sphincter.","notes":["The pharynx also has three regions, but they are the nasopharynx, oropharynx, and laryngopharynx. Choose it if the question listed those throat regions.","The large intestine is made of the cecum, colon, rectum, and anal canal. Pick it if the question listed those regions instead.","Correct. The duodenum, jejunum, and ileum make up the small intestine.","The stomach is divided into the cardia, fundus, body, and pyloric part. It would be the answer if the question listed those four regions."],"lt":"t-alimentary-canal","lid":"c15","comp":116,"competencyId":"w5-small-intestine"},{"id":"L02212","dok":1,"q":"The ascending, transverse, descending, and sigmoid segments are the four parts of which structure?","a":"The colon.","options":["The stomach","The colon","The rectum","The small intestine"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The colon is the long main portion of the large intestine, and it has four parts named for their direction or shape. The ascending colon rises on the right, the transverse colon crosses the abdomen, the descending colon drops on the left, and the S-shaped sigmoid colon leads into the rectum. The large intestine as a whole also includes the cecum, rectum, and anal canal, which are not parts of the colon.","notes":["The stomach also has four regions, but they are the cardia, fundus, body, and pyloric part. It would be the answer if the question listed those.","Correct. The ascending, transverse, descending, and sigmoid segments make up the colon.","The rectum is a single straight segment that follows the sigmoid colon, not one of the four parts of the colon. Choose it if the question asked for the straight portion anterior to the sacrum and coccyx.","The small intestine is divided into the duodenum, jejunum, and ileum. Pick it if the question listed those three regions."],"lt":"t-alimentary-canal","lid":"c16","comp":117,"competencyId":"w5-large-intestine"},{"id":"L02215","dok":1,"q":"The ileum joins the large intestine at which structure?","a":"The ileocecal valve (ileocecal sphincter).","options":["The internal anal sphincter","The pyloric sphincter","The lower esophageal sphincter","The ileocecal valve"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The ileum is the last and longest region of the small intestine. It ends at the ileocecal sphincter, a thickening of smooth muscle where it opens into the cecum of the large intestine. Together with folds of mucosa, this sphincter forms the ileocecal valve, which keeps the contents of the large intestine from moving back into the ileum.","notes":["The internal anal sphincter is involuntary smooth muscle around the anal canal, at the very end of the tract, far from the ileum. Choose it if the question asked which smooth muscle sphincter surrounds the anal canal.","The pyloric sphincter is at the exit of the stomach into the duodenum, at the start of the small intestine. Pick it if the question asked where the small intestine begins.","The lower esophageal sphincter guards the entrance of the stomach from the esophagus. It would be the answer if the question asked which sphincter weakens in GERD.","Correct. The ileum joins the cecum at the ileocecal valve."],"lt":"t-alimentary-canal","lid":"c19","comp":114,"competencyId":"w5-canal-sphincters"},{"id":"L02217","dok":1,"q":"Which region of the stomach is the rounded portion that bulges above and to the left of the cardia?","a":"The fundus.","options":["The cardia","The body","The pyloric part","The fundus"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The fundus is the rounded, dome-shaped portion of the stomach that bulges above and to the left of the cardia, just under the diaphragm. Because it sits higher than the opening from the esophagus, it often holds swallowed air. The four regions in order are the cardia, fundus, body, and pyloric part.","notes":["The cardia is the region that surrounds the opening from the esophagus, just below and to the right of the fundus. Choose it if the question asked where food enters the stomach.","The body is the large central portion of the stomach, below the fundus. Pick it if the question asked for the biggest region of the stomach.","The pyloric part is the region that connects the body to the duodenum, at the opposite end from the fundus. It would be the answer if the question asked which region leads to the small intestine.","Correct. The fundus is the rounded portion above and to the left of the cardia."],"lt":"t-alimentary-canal","lid":"c21","comp":115,"competencyId":"w5-stomach"},{"id":"L02218","dok":1,"q":"Which disorder of the canal is stomach contents flowing back up through a weak lower esophageal sphincter and irritating the esophagus?","a":"Gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD).","options":["Gastroesophageal reflux disease","Hiatal hernia","Peptic ulcer","Diverticulosis"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Gastroesophageal reflux disease, or GERD, happens when the lower esophageal sphincter is weak and lets acidic stomach contents flow back up into the esophagus. The esophagus has no protection against stomach acid, so it becomes irritated, and the person feels heartburn behind the breastbone. The name itself traces the path: gastro (stomach) to esophageal (esophagus), with reflux meaning backflow.","notes":["Correct. GERD is backflow of stomach contents through a weak lower esophageal sphincter.","A hiatal hernia is part of the stomach pushing up through the esophageal hiatus of the diaphragm. It can occur alongside reflux, but it is a hernia, not the backflow itself. It would be the answer for a question about the stomach moving through the diaphragm.","A peptic ulcer is an eroded sore in the wall of the stomach or duodenum, not a backflow problem. Pick it if the question described a sore eating into the lining.","Diverticulosis is small pouches bulging outward through weak spots in the colon wall. Choose it for a question about the large intestine wall."],"lt":"t-alimentary-canal","lid":"c22","comp":119,"competencyId":"w5-canal-disorders"},{"id":"L02219","dok":2,"q":"A patient has pain in the lower right abdomen from inflammation of the narrow, worm-like tube attached to the cecum. Which disorder is this?","a":"Appendicitis.","options":["Colorectal cancer","Gastroesophageal reflux disease","Peptic ulcer","Appendicitis"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The narrow, worm-like tube attached to the cecum is the vermiform appendix, a tube of lymphatic tissue. The cecum sits in the lower right part of the abdomen, so inflammation of the appendix, called appendicitis, brings pain there. If the inflamed appendix ruptures, infection can spread to the peritoneum.","notes":["Colorectal cancer is a malignant tumor of the colon or rectum, not inflammation of the appendix. It would be the answer for a question about a tumor of the large intestine.","Gastroesophageal reflux disease is backflow of stomach contents into the esophagus, felt as heartburn behind the breastbone. Pick it for a weak lower esophageal sphincter, not for pain at the cecum.","A peptic ulcer is an eroded sore in the wall of the stomach or duodenum, which lie in the upper abdomen. Choose it for a question about a sore in the stomach lining.","Correct. Inflammation of the vermiform appendix is appendicitis."],"lt":"t-alimentary-canal","lid":"c23","comp":119,"competencyId":"w5-canal-disorders"},{"id":"L02222","dok":1,"q":"Which disorder is chronic inflammation of the intestinal wall and includes Crohn disease and ulcerative colitis?","a":"Inflammatory bowel disease.","options":["Hemorrhoids","Peptic ulcer","Diverticulosis","Inflammatory bowel disease"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Inflammatory bowel disease is a group of disorders marked by long-lasting (chronic) inflammation of the intestinal wall. Its two main forms are Crohn disease and ulcerative colitis. The word bowel refers to the intestines, which is where this inflammation is found.","notes":["Hemorrhoids are swollen, dilated veins in the wall of the anal canal and rectum. Choose them if the question described swollen veins near the anus.","A peptic ulcer is an eroded sore in the wall of the stomach or duodenum. It would be the answer for a single sore in the upper digestive tract.","Diverticulosis is small pouches bulging outward through weak spots in the colon wall, a structural change rather than chronic inflammation. Pick it if the question described pouches in the colon.","Correct. Inflammatory bowel disease includes Crohn disease and ulcerative colitis."],"lt":"t-alimentary-canal","lid":"c26","comp":119,"competencyId":"w5-canal-disorders"},{"id":"L02225","dok":2,"q":"A region of the stomach narrows like a funnel and connects the body of the stomach to the duodenum. Which region is it?","a":"The pyloric part.","options":["The fundus","The pyloric part","The cardia","The body"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The pyloric part is the exit region of the stomach. It narrows from the wide pyloric antrum through the pyloric canal to the pylorus, which is guarded by the pyloric sphincter where the stomach meets the duodenum. Its funnel shape guides the liquefied stomach contents toward the small intestine.","notes":["The fundus is the rounded portion that bulges above and to the left of the cardia. It would be the answer if the question asked for the dome-shaped top of the stomach.","Correct. The pyloric part narrows and connects the body of the stomach to the duodenum.","The cardia surrounds the opening from the esophagus, at the entrance end of the stomach. Pick it if the question asked where food enters the stomach.","The body is the large central portion of the stomach, between the fundus and the pyloric part. Choose it if the question asked for the biggest region of the stomach."],"lt":"t-alimentary-canal","lid":"c29","comp":115,"competencyId":"w5-stomach"},{"id":"L02226","dok":1,"q":"Which region of the small intestine is the last and longest, joining the large intestine at the ileocecal sphincter?","a":"The ileum.","options":["The duodenum","The cecum","The jejunum","The ileum"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The ileum is the final region of the small intestine and also the longest, about 2 m (6 feet). It ends at the ileocecal sphincter, a thickening of smooth muscle where it opens into the cecum of the large intestine, and together with folds of mucosa this sphincter forms the ileocecal valve. The spelling helps: the ileum of the intestine is different from the ilium of the hip bone.","notes":["The duodenum is the first and shortest region, about 25 cm (10 inches), starting at the pyloric sphincter. Pick it if the question asked where the small intestine begins.","The cecum is the pouch at the start of the large intestine, on the far side of the ileocecal sphincter, so it is not part of the small intestine. Choose it if the question asked what the ileum empties into.","The jejunum is the middle region, about 1 m (3 feet) long. It would be the answer if the question asked which region lies between the duodenum and the ileum.","Correct. The ileum is the last and longest region and joins the large intestine at the ileocecal sphincter."],"lt":"t-alimentary-canal","lid":"c30","comp":116,"competencyId":"w5-small-intestine"},{"id":"L02227","dok":2,"q":"Which surface feature of the small intestine is a permanent circular ridge of the mucosa and submucosa?","a":"The circular folds (plicae circulares).","options":["Rugae","Microvilli","Villi","Circular folds"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The circular folds, or plicae circulares, are the largest of the small intestine's three surface features. They are permanent ridges that involve both the mucosa and the submucosa, so they do not flatten out when the intestine fills. Villi project from the folds, and microvilli cover the cells of each villus, so the surface is increased three times over.","notes":["Rugae are folds of the stomach lining, and unlike the circular folds they flatten out as the stomach fills. Choose rugae for a question about the stomach.","Microvilli are microscopic projections of single absorptive cells that form the brush border. They would be the answer for the smallest of the three surface features.","Villi are finger-like projections of the mucosa only, smaller than the circular folds and sitting on top of them. Pick villi if the question asked about the projections that give the lining a velvety look.","Correct. The circular folds are permanent ridges of the mucosa and submucosa."],"lt":"t-alimentary-canal","lid":"c31","comp":116,"competencyId":"w5-small-intestine"},{"id":"L02228","dok":2,"q":"Which region of the large intestine is the straight portion lying anterior to the sacrum and coccyx?","a":"The rectum.","options":["The sigmoid colon","The rectum","The anal canal","The cecum"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The rectum is the straight portion of the large intestine, running down in the pelvis in front of the sacrum and coccyx. Its name comes from the Latin word for straight. It follows the sigmoid colon and stores feces before they pass into the anal canal.","notes":["The sigmoid colon is the S-shaped last part of the colon, just before the rectum. Its curves set it apart from the straight rectum. Pick it if the question asked which segment of the colon leads into the rectum.","Correct. The rectum is the straight portion anterior to the sacrum and coccyx.","The anal canal is the last few centimeters of the tract, after the rectum, ending at the anus. It would be the answer if the question asked for the final short segment of the canal.","The cecum is the pouch at the start of the large intestine, in the lower right abdomen, far from the sacrum. Choose it if the question asked where the large intestine begins."],"lt":"t-alimentary-canal","lid":"c32","comp":117,"competencyId":"w5-large-intestine"},{"id":"L02230","dok":2,"q":"Which feature of the colon is a series of pouches formed by the pull of the teniae coli?","a":"Haustra.","options":["Rugae","Circular folds","Haustra","Villi"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Haustra are the pouches that give the colon its puckered appearance. The three teniae coli, bands of longitudinal muscle, are shorter than the colon wall and gather it into a row of sacs. Haustra, teniae coli, and omental appendices together identify the large intestine.","notes":["Rugae are folds of the stomach lining that flatten as the stomach fills. They would be the answer for a question about the stomach.","Circular folds are permanent ridges of the mucosa and submucosa in the small intestine. Choose them for the largest surface feature of the small intestine.","Correct. Haustra are the pouches of the colon formed by the teniae coli.","Villi are finger-like projections of the small intestine mucosa that increase surface area for absorption. Pick villi for a question about the small intestine lining."],"lt":"t-alimentary-canal","lid":"c34","comp":117,"competencyId":"w5-large-intestine"},{"id":"L02231","dok":1,"q":"Which disorder is an eroded sore in the wall of the stomach or duodenum?","a":"Peptic ulcer.","options":["Gastroesophageal reflux disease","Appendicitis","Peptic ulcer","Diverticulosis"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"A peptic ulcer is an eroded sore in the wall of the stomach or the duodenum, the regions exposed to stomach acid. It begins in the mucosa, and the danger is that it erodes deeper through the submucosa and muscularis. If it perforates the serosa, the contents of the lumen spill into the peritoneal cavity.","notes":["Gastroesophageal reflux disease is backflow of stomach contents into the esophagus through a weak lower esophageal sphincter, not a sore in the stomach. Pick it for a question about heartburn from reflux.","Appendicitis is inflammation of the vermiform appendix attached to the cecum. Choose it for a question about pain in the lower right abdomen.","Correct. A peptic ulcer is an eroded sore in the stomach or duodenal wall.","Diverticulosis is small pouches bulging outward through weak spots in the colon wall. It would be the answer for a question about the large intestine wall."],"lt":"t-alimentary-canal","lid":"c35","comp":119,"competencyId":"w5-canal-disorders"},{"id":"L02232","dok":1,"q":"Which disorder is small pouches bulging outward through weak spots in the colon wall?","a":"Diverticulosis.","options":["Colorectal cancer","Hiatal hernia","Diverticulosis","Inflammatory bowel disease"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Diverticulosis is the presence of small pouches, called diverticula, that bulge outward through weak spots in the colon wall. It is a change in the structure of the wall rather than an inflammation or tumor. It is different from the haustra, which are the normal pouches of the colon formed by the teniae coli.","notes":["Colorectal cancer is a malignant tumor of the colon or rectum. It would be the answer for a question about a tumor rather than pouches.","A hiatal hernia is part of the stomach pushing up through the esophageal hiatus of the diaphragm. Choose it for a question about the stomach and diaphragm.","Correct. Diverticulosis is small pouches bulging through weak spots in the colon wall.","Inflammatory bowel disease is chronic inflammation of the intestinal wall, as in Crohn disease and ulcerative colitis, not outward pouches. Pick it if the question described long-lasting inflammation of the intestine."],"lt":"t-alimentary-canal","lid":"c36","comp":119,"competencyId":"w5-canal-disorders"},{"id":"L02234","dok":1,"q":"Which region of the small intestine is the middle region, about 1 m (3 feet) long?","a":"The jejunum.","options":["The colon","The jejunum","The ileum","The duodenum"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The small intestine runs duodenum, jejunum, then ileum. The jejunum is the middle region, about 1 m (3 feet) long, lying between the short duodenum (about 25 cm, or 10 inches) and the long ileum (about 2 m, or 6 feet). Along with the ileum, it hangs within the peritoneal cavity on the mesentery.","notes":["The colon is part of the large intestine, not the small intestine. Choose it if the question asked about the ascending, transverse, descending, and sigmoid segments.","Correct. The jejunum is the middle region of the small intestine.","The ileum is the last and longest region, about 2 m (6 feet). It would be the answer if the question asked which region joins the large intestine.","The duodenum is the first and shortest region, about 25 cm (10 inches). Pick it if the question asked which region starts at the pyloric sphincter."],"lt":"t-alimentary-canal","lid":"c38","comp":116,"competencyId":"w5-small-intestine"},{"id":"L02235","dok":1,"q":"The stomach has a short concave medial border and a long convex lateral border. What are these two borders called?","a":"The lesser and greater curvatures.","options":["The cardia and the pylorus","The fundus and the body","The pyloric antrum and pyloric canal","The lesser and greater curvatures"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The stomach is J-shaped, so it has two curved borders. The lesser curvature is the short, concave border on the right (medial) side, facing the liver, and the greater curvature is the long, convex border on the left (lateral) side. The lesser omentum attaches along the lesser curvature, and the greater omentum hangs from the greater curvature.","notes":["The cardia and pylorus are the entrance and exit regions of the stomach, not its borders. Pick them if the question asked where food enters and leaves the stomach.","The fundus and body are regions of the stomach, the dome on top and the large central portion. They would be the answer for a question about the upper and central regions.","The pyloric antrum and pyloric canal are subdivisions of the pyloric part of the stomach. Choose them if the question asked what makes up the funnel leading to the pylorus.","Correct. The lesser curvature is the short concave medial border and the greater curvature is the long convex lateral border."],"lt":"t-alimentary-canal","lid":"c39","comp":115,"competencyId":"w5-stomach"},{"id":"L02236","dok":2,"q":"Which sequence correctly orders these organs as food travels through the canal: esophagus, stomach, small intestine, large intestine?","a":"Esophagus, stomach, small intestine, large intestine.","options":["Esophagus, small intestine, stomach, large intestine","Stomach, esophagus, small intestine, large intestine","Esophagus, stomach, small intestine, large intestine","Esophagus, stomach, large intestine, small intestine"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Food moves through the canal in one direction. The esophagus carries it to the stomach, where it is churned into a liquid. It then passes into the small intestine, where most absorption happens, and finally into the large intestine, where water is absorbed and feces form.","notes":["This puts the small intestine before the stomach. The stomach empties through the pyloric sphincter into the duodenum, so the stomach must come before the small intestine.","This puts the stomach before the esophagus. The esophagus is the tube that delivers food to the stomach, so it must come first.","Correct. Food travels from the esophagus to the stomach, then the small intestine, then the large intestine.","This puts the large intestine before the small intestine. Students sometimes order them by the word large, but the small intestine always comes first, and the large intestine is the final, wider region."],"lt":"t-alimentary-canal","lid":"c40","comp":118,"competencyId":"w5-food-pathway"},{"id":"L02238","dok":2,"q":"Which sequence correctly orders the regions of the small intestine as food moves through it, followed by the structure the ileum empties into?","a":"Duodenum, jejunum, ileum, then the cecum.","options":["Duodenum, jejunum, ileum, then the rectum","Duodenum, jejunum, ileum, then the cecum","Jejunum, duodenum, ileum, then the cecum","Duodenum, ileum, jejunum, then the cecum"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The small intestine runs duodenum, jejunum, then ileum. The ileum empties through the ileocecal valve into the cecum, the pouch at the start of the large intestine. From the cecum, material moves through the colon and only later reaches the rectum.","notes":["The rectum is near the end of the large intestine, after the whole colon, so the ileum does not empty into it. The rectum would be the answer for where the sigmoid colon leads.","Correct. Food passes the duodenum, jejunum, and ileum, then enters the cecum.","This puts the jejunum first, but the duodenum is the first region, starting right at the pyloric sphincter.","This swaps the jejunum and ileum. The jejunum is the middle region, and the ileum is the last and longest region, the one that reaches the large intestine."],"lt":"t-alimentary-canal","lid":"c42","comp":118,"competencyId":"w5-food-pathway"},{"id":"L02239","dok":2,"q":"Which sequence correctly orders the four parts of the colon as material travels through it?","a":"Ascending, transverse, descending, sigmoid.","options":["Ascending, transverse, sigmoid, descending","Ascending, transverse, descending, sigmoid","Transverse, ascending, descending, sigmoid","Ascending, descending, transverse, sigmoid"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The colon frames the small intestine on three sides. Material rises from the cecum up the right side in the ascending colon, turns at the hepatic flexure to cross in the transverse colon, turns at the splenic flexure to drop down the left side in the descending colon, and then curves through the S-shaped sigmoid colon into the rectum.","notes":["This puts the sigmoid colon before the descending colon. The sigmoid colon is the last part, connecting the descending colon to the rectum.","Correct. The colon runs ascending, transverse, descending, then sigmoid.","This puts the transverse colon first, but the colon starts by rising from the cecum on the right, so the ascending colon is first.","This puts the descending colon before the transverse colon. The colon has to cross the abdomen before it can descend on the left side, so the transverse colon comes second."],"lt":"t-alimentary-canal","lid":"c43","comp":118,"competencyId":"w5-food-pathway"},{"id":"L02240","dok":2,"q":"Which sequence correctly traces the end of the canal, from the straight segment that holds feces, to the short terminal segment, to the exit?","a":"Rectum, anal canal, anus.","options":["Rectum, cecum, anus","Rectum, anal canal, anus","Anal canal, rectum, anus","Sigmoid colon, rectum, anus"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The final stretch of the canal begins with the rectum, the straight portion anterior to the sacrum and coccyx that stores feces. Next comes the anal canal, the last few centimeters of the tract, which passes through the pelvic floor. It ends at the anus, the exit, and the internal and external anal sphincters surround the anal canal.","notes":["The cecum is the pouch at the start of the large intestine, not near its end. It would be the answer for where the ileum empties.","Correct. The rectum, anal canal, and anus form the end of the canal in that order.","This swaps the rectum and the anal canal. The rectum comes first, and the anal canal is the short final segment that ends at the anus.","The sigmoid colon is the S-shaped part just before the rectum, so this sequence starts one step too early and skips the anal canal. Pick the sigmoid colon if the question asked what leads into the rectum."],"lt":"t-alimentary-canal","lid":"c44","comp":118,"competencyId":"w5-food-pathway"},{"id":"L02241","dok":2,"q":"Which sequence names the three surface features that expand the absorptive surface of the small intestine, from largest to smallest?","a":"Circular folds, villi, microvilli.","options":["Microvilli, villi, circular folds","Circular folds, villi, microvilli","Circular folds, rugae, microvilli","Villi, circular folds, microvilli"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The small intestine increases its surface three times over, each feature smaller than the last. The circular folds (plicae circulares) are permanent ridges of the mucosa and submucosa. Villi are finger-like projections of the mucosa that stand on those folds. Microvilli are microscopic projections of the single absorptive cells covering each villus, forming the brush border.","notes":["This is the correct list run backward, from smallest to largest. It would be right only if the question asked for the order starting with the smallest feature.","Correct. Circular folds hold villi, and villi are covered by cells with microvilli.","Rugae are folds of the stomach lining, not a feature of the small intestine. They would be the answer for a question about how the stomach expands to hold a meal.","This puts the villi first, but villi are projections of the mucosa that sit on top of the much larger circular folds of the wall."],"lt":"t-alimentary-canal","lid":"c45","comp":116,"competencyId":"w5-small-intestine"},{"id":"L02243","dok":1,"q":"Which list names only organs of the alimentary canal itself, the tube that food passes through?","a":"The mouth, pharynx, esophagus, stomach, small intestine, and large intestine.","options":["The pharynx, esophagus, stomach, pancreas, small intestine, and large intestine","The mouth, esophagus, stomach, liver, small intestine, and large intestine","The teeth, tongue, salivary glands, liver, gallbladder, and pancreas","The mouth, pharynx, esophagus, stomach, small intestine, and large intestine"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The organs of the alimentary canal are the ones food actually travels through: the mouth, the pharynx, the esophagus, the stomach, and the small and large intestines. The accessory organs sit alongside the tube and send their secretions into it, but food never passes through them. The liver and pancreas, for example, empty bile and pancreatic juice into the duodenum.","notes":["This list slips in the pancreas, an accessory organ that empties pancreatic juice into the duodenum. Food never travels through the pancreas.","This list slips in the liver, an accessory organ that makes bile and sends it to the duodenum through ducts. Food never travels through the liver.","These are the accessory digestive organs, which help digestion without being part of the tube. Pick this list if the question asked for the accessory organs.","Correct. Food passes through each of these organs in turn."],"lt":"t-alimentary-canal","lid":"c2-x2","comp":109,"competencyId":"w5-canal-wall"},{"id":"L02252","dok":1,"q":"Which structure is the narrow, worm-like tube of lymphatic tissue attached to the cecum?","a":"The vermiform appendix.","options":["The ileum","The vermiform appendix","The palatine tonsil","The sigmoid colon"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The vermiform appendix is a narrow tube attached to the cecum, and vermiform means worm-shaped. Its wall is rich in lymphatic tissue, so it belongs to the immune defenses of the gut. When it becomes inflamed, the condition is appendicitis.","notes":["The ileum is the last region of the small intestine, which opens into the cecum at the ileocecal valve rather than hanging from it. Choose it if the question asked which region joins the large intestine.","Correct. The vermiform appendix is the worm-like lymphatic tube attached to the cecum.","The palatine tonsil is also lymphatic tissue, but it sits between the palatal arches at the back of the mouth, not on the cecum. Pick it if the question asked about lymphatic tissue guarding the oropharynx.","The sigmoid colon is the S-shaped last part of the colon, leading into the rectum. It would be the answer if the question asked which colon segment is curved like an S."],"lt":"t-alimentary-canal","lid":"c11-x2","comp":117,"competencyId":"w5-large-intestine"},{"id":"L02255","dok":1,"q":"How many layers of smooth muscle are in the muscularis of the stomach?","a":"Three.","options":["Four","Three","One","Two"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The stomach muscularis has three layers of smooth muscle: an inner oblique layer, a middle circular layer, and an outer longitudinal layer. That is one more layer than the rest of the canal, which usually has only the circular and longitudinal layers. The extra oblique layer lets the stomach churn food as well as push it.","notes":["Four is the number of tunics in the canal wall (mucosa, submucosa, muscularis, serosa), not the number of muscle layers. Students often carry that number over by mistake.","Correct. The stomach has three smooth muscle layers in its muscularis.","One layer is too few for any part of the muscularis. A single thin layer of smooth muscle does exist in the mucosa, the muscularis mucosae, which may be the source of this mix-up.","Two layers, inner circular and outer longitudinal, is the usual count for the rest of the canal. Pick two if the question asked about the muscularis of the small intestine or colon."],"lt":"t-alimentary-canal","lid":"c14-x2","comp":115,"competencyId":"w5-stomach"},{"id":"L02264","dok":1,"q":"Which structure inside each villus is a lymphatic capillary that absorbs dietary fats?","a":"The lacteal.","options":["A blood capillary","A gastric pit","A microvillus","The lacteal"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Each villus contains a lacteal, a lymphatic capillary in its core. Dietary fats are absorbed into the lacteal and travel in the lymph rather than going straight into the blood. Its name comes from the milky look of the fat-filled lymph after a meal.","notes":["Villi also hold blood capillaries, which take up most other absorbed nutrients, but they are part of the blood system, not the lymphatic system. Pick a blood capillary if the question asked where sugars and amino acids enter the villus.","A gastric pit is an opening in the stomach lining that leads to the gastric glands, not a structure inside a villus. It would be the answer for a question about the stomach lining.","A microvillus is a microscopic projection on the surface of an absorptive cell, part of the brush border, not a vessel inside the villus. Choose it if the question asked what forms the brush border.","Correct. The lacteal is the lymphatic capillary in each villus that absorbs dietary fats."],"lt":"t-alimentary-canal","lid":"c23-x2","comp":116,"competencyId":"w5-small-intestine"},{"id":"L02270","dok":3,"q":"Imaging shows part of a patient's stomach pushed up through the opening in the diaphragm that the esophagus passes through. Which disorder is this, and which opening is involved?","a":"A hiatal hernia, through the esophageal hiatus.","options":["An inguinal hernia, through the esophageal hiatus","A hiatal hernia, through the pyloric sphincter","A hiatal hernia, through the esophageal hiatus","Gastroesophageal reflux disease, through the pyloric canal"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The esophagus passes from the thorax into the abdomen through an opening in the diaphragm called the esophageal hiatus. In a hiatal hernia, part of the stomach pushes up through that same opening into the thorax. The word hiatal comes from hiatus, which names the opening involved.","notes":["The opening is right, but an inguinal hernia is a different disorder, in which intestine pushes through the inguinal canal in the groin. Choose an inguinal hernia for a bulge in the groin.","The disorder is right, but the pyloric sphincter is the ring of muscle between the stomach and duodenum, not an opening in the diaphragm. The pyloric sphincter would be the answer for where food leaves the stomach.","Correct. A hiatal hernia is the stomach pushing up through the esophageal hiatus.","Gastroesophageal reflux disease is backflow of stomach contents through a weak lower esophageal sphincter, not the stomach moving through the diaphragm, and the pyloric canal is at the stomach's exit. Pick GERD for heartburn from reflux."],"lt":"t-alimentary-canal","lid":"c29-x2","comp":119,"competencyId":"w5-canal-disorders"},{"id":"L02272","dok":3,"q":"The small intestine is lined with villi, and each villus is covered in cells bearing microvilli. Why does this lining suit the small intestine's main job?","a":"It greatly increases the surface area for absorbing nutrients.","options":["It greatly increases the surface area for absorbing nutrients","It adds a muscle layer that churns and mixes the contents","It lets the small intestine stretch to store a large meal","It forms pouches that hold waste while water is absorbed"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The small intestine is where most absorption happens, and absorption depends on how much lining the digested food can touch. Villi multiply the surface of the mucosa, and the microvilli on each absorptive cell multiply it again. Along with the circular folds, these features give the small intestine an enormous absorptive surface packed into a tube that fits in the abdomen.","notes":["Correct. Villi and microvilli greatly increase the surface area for absorption.","An extra muscle layer that churns food is the oblique layer of the stomach's muscularis. Villi and microvilli are part of the mucosa and contain no extra muscle layer. Choose this for a question about the stomach muscularis.","Stretching to store a meal is the job of the stomach, whose rugae flatten as it fills. Pick this for a question about rugae.","Pouches that hold waste while water is absorbed describe the haustra of the colon. This would be the answer for a question about the large intestine."],"lt":"t-alimentary-canal","lid":"d3-t-alimentary-canal-1","comp":116,"competencyId":"w5-small-intestine"},{"id":"L02273","dok":3,"q":"The stomach lining is folded into rugae and coated with a thick layer of mucus. Which statement best explains why each feature matters to the stomach?","a":"The rugae let the stomach expand with a meal, and the mucus shields the lining from the stomach's own acid.","options":["The rugae anchor the stomach to the liver, and the mucus fills the peritoneal cavity","The rugae let the stomach expand with a meal, and the mucus shields the lining from the stomach's own acid","The rugae are permanent ridges that slow food, and the mucus speeds absorption","The rugae squeeze food toward the duodenum, and the mucus breaks food down"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The stomach works as a holding reservoir and a mixing chamber full of strong acid. The rugae are folds of the lining that flatten out as a meal arrives, so the stomach can stretch to hold it. The thick mucus coats the lining so the acid works on the food rather than on the stomach wall itself. When that protection breaks down, a peptic ulcer can form.","notes":["Anchoring the stomach to the liver is the job of the lesser omentum, and the slim fluid in the peritoneal cavity comes from the serous membrane, not stomach mucus. Choose the lesser omentum for a question about that attachment.","Correct. The rugae allow expansion, and the mucus protects the lining from acid.","Permanent ridges that slow the contents are the circular folds of the small intestine. Rugae are not permanent, since they flatten as the stomach fills. This would fit a question about the small intestine wall.","Squeezing food forward is the job of the muscularis, the smooth muscle tunic, not the folds of the lining, and mucus protects rather than digests. Pick the muscularis for a question about what moves stomach contents."],"lt":"t-alimentary-canal","lid":"d3-t-alimentary-canal-2","comp":115,"competencyId":"w5-stomach"},{"id":"L02275","dok":3,"q":"The small intestine absorbs most nutrients, while the large intestine mainly absorbs water and forms feces. Which statement best explains this division of labor?","a":"Most nutrients are already absorbed upstream, so what reaches the large intestine is mostly water and indigestible waste.","options":["Bile enters the large intestine and blocks nutrient absorption there","The large intestine has more villi than the small intestine, which suits it to water","The large intestine adds water to the waste to soften it before elimination","Most nutrients are already absorbed upstream, so what reaches the large intestine is mostly water and indigestible waste"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Food passes through the small intestine first, and its huge absorptive surface takes up most of the nutrients along the way. What enters the large intestine through the ileocecal valve is largely water and indigestible material. The large intestine reclaims much of that water and compacts what is left into feces, which the rectum holds until elimination.","notes":["Bile enters the duodenum at the major duodenal papilla, at the start of the small intestine, not the large intestine. Pick the duodenum for a question about where bile and pancreatic juice enter the canal.","The large intestine lacks villi entirely, which is one way to tell it from the small intestine on a slide. Villi belong to the small intestine, so this would be the answer only in a question about the small intestine's absorptive surface.","The large intestine removes water from the waste rather than adding it, which is why feces become more solid as they move along the colon. This mix-up reverses the large intestine's real role.","Correct. The small intestine absorbs most nutrients first, leaving water and waste for the large intestine."],"lt":"t-alimentary-canal","lid":"d3-t-alimentary-canal-4","comp":117,"competencyId":"w5-large-intestine"},{"id":"L02276","dok":2,"q":"The pyloric sphincter sits between the stomach and the duodenum. What is its job?","a":"It lets the liquefied stomach contents into the duodenum a little at a time.","options":["It lets the liquefied stomach contents into the duodenum a little at a time","It keeps material in the large intestine from flowing back into the ileum","It controls when feces leave the body through the anus","It keeps stomach contents from flowing back into the esophagus"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"In the stomach, food is churned and held until it becomes a liquid. The pyloric sphincter, a ring of smooth muscle between the pylorus and the duodenum, opens to release that liquid into the small intestine in small amounts. This metering keeps the duodenum from being flooded so the small intestine can process each portion.","notes":["Correct. The pyloric sphincter meters stomach contents into the duodenum.","Limiting backflow from the large intestine into the ileum is the job of the ileocecal valve. It would be the answer for a question about the junction of the small and large intestines.","Controlling when feces leave the body is the job of the internal and external anal sphincters at the end of the canal. Choose them for a question about the anus.","Keeping stomach contents out of the esophagus is the job of the lower esophageal sphincter at the stomach's entrance. Pick it for a question about reflux or GERD."],"lt":"t-alimentary-canal","lid":"d3-t-alimentary-canal-5","comp":114,"competencyId":"w5-canal-sphincters"},{"id":"N-m4-stomach-intestines-1","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares the internal and external anal sphincters?","a":"The internal anal sphincter is involuntary smooth muscle; the external anal sphincter is voluntary skeletal muscle.","options":["Both anal sphincters are voluntary skeletal muscle","Both anal sphincters are involuntary smooth muscle","The internal anal sphincter is involuntary smooth muscle; the external anal sphincter is voluntary skeletal muscle","The internal anal sphincter is voluntary skeletal muscle; the external anal sphincter is involuntary smooth muscle"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Two sphincters guard the anus. The internal anal sphincter is a thickening of the smooth muscle of the canal wall, so it is involuntary and works without conscious control. The external anal sphincter is skeletal muscle, so it is voluntary and lets a person decide when to release feces. Having one of each gives both automatic and conscious control.","notes":["The external sphincter is voluntary skeletal muscle, but the internal sphincter is smooth muscle like the rest of the canal wall. This mix-up comes from assuming both sphincters at one exit must be the same tissue.","The internal sphincter is involuntary smooth muscle, but the external sphincter is skeletal muscle that you control at will. If both were involuntary, there would be no conscious control over elimination.","Correct. The internal sphincter is involuntary smooth muscle and the external sphincter is voluntary skeletal muscle.","This swaps the two. The internal sphincter is a continuation of the gut's own smooth muscle, which is involuntary, while the external one is skeletal muscle under conscious control."],"comp":114,"competencyId":"w5-canal-sphincters"},{"id":"N-m4-stomach-intestines-2","dok":1,"q":"What are the small fat-filled pouches of peritoneum that hang from the surface of the colon, mostly along the teniae coli?","a":"Omental appendices.","options":["Omental appendices","Lacteals","Diverticula","Haustra"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Omental appendices, also called epiploic appendages, are small fat-filled pouches of visceral peritoneum that hang from the surface of the colon, mostly along the teniae coli. Along with the teniae coli and the haustra, they are one of the three features found only on the large intestine, so they help you identify the colon on a model.","notes":["Correct. Omental appendices are the fat-filled pouches of peritoneum on the surface of the colon.","Lacteals are lymphatic capillaries inside the villi of the small intestine that absorb dietary fats. Choose them for a question about fat absorption, not for structures on the colon surface.","Diverticula are abnormal pouches that bulge outward through weak spots in the colon wall, the defining feature of diverticulosis. They would be the answer for a question about that disorder.","Haustra are the pouches of the colon wall itself, formed by the pull of the teniae coli, and they are not filled with fat. Pick haustra if the question asked what gives the colon its puckered look."],"comp":117,"competencyId":"w5-large-intestine"},{"id":"N-m4-stomach-intestines-3","dok":2,"q":"Which bend of the colon lies near the liver, where the ascending colon turns to become the transverse colon?","a":"The hepatic flexure (right colic flexure).","options":["The sigmoid colon","The splenic flexure","The hepatic flexure","The ileocecal valve"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"As the ascending colon rises on the right side of the abdomen, it reaches the underside of the liver and turns to run across the abdomen as the transverse colon. This bend is the hepatic flexure, also called the right colic flexure, named for the liver (hepatic). The matching bend on the left, near the spleen, is the splenic flexure.","notes":["The sigmoid colon is the S-shaped last part of the colon, low in the pelvis, leading into the rectum. It would be the answer for the colon segment just before the rectum.","The splenic flexure, or left colic flexure, is the bend near the spleen where the transverse colon turns down to become the descending colon. Pick it if the question asked about the bend on the left side.","Correct. The hepatic flexure is the bend near the liver between the ascending and transverse colon.","The ileocecal valve is where the ileum opens into the cecum, at the very start of the large intestine, below the ascending colon. Choose it for the junction of the small and large intestines."],"comp":117,"competencyId":"w5-large-intestine"},{"id":"N-m4-stomach-intestines-4","dok":1,"q":"Which disorder of the canal is swollen, dilated veins in the wall of the anal canal and rectum?","a":"Hemorrhoids.","options":["Inflammatory bowel disease","Diverticulosis","Colorectal cancer","Hemorrhoids"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Hemorrhoids are veins in the wall of the anal canal and rectum that have become swollen and dilated. They are a vascular problem, much like varicose veins elsewhere in the body, rather than a problem of the intestinal lining or muscle. Their location at the end of the canal explains why they are felt near the anus.","notes":["Inflammatory bowel disease is chronic inflammation of the intestinal wall, as in Crohn disease and ulcerative colitis. Choose it for long-lasting inflammation of the intestine.","Diverticulosis is small pouches bulging outward through weak spots in the colon wall, not swollen veins. Pick it for a question about pouches in the colon.","Colorectal cancer is a malignant tumor of the colon or rectum. It would be the answer for a question about a tumor rather than dilated veins.","Correct. Hemorrhoids are swollen, dilated veins of the anal canal and rectum."],"comp":119,"competencyId":"w5-canal-disorders"},{"id":"L02301","dok":1,"q":"To which part of the large intestine is the vermiform appendix attached?","a":"The cecum.","options":["The cecum","The ileum","The sigmoid colon","The transverse colon"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The cecum is the pouch at the very start of the large intestine, just below the ileocecal valve in the lower right abdomen. The vermiform appendix, a narrow worm-shaped tube of lymphatic tissue, hangs from the cecum. That is why appendicitis causes pain in the lower right quadrant.","notes":["Correct. The appendix hangs from the cecum.","The ileum is the last part of the small intestine, not the large intestine. It joins the cecum at the ileocecal valve, so it would be the answer for the segment that empties into the cecum.","The sigmoid colon is the S-shaped last part of the colon in the lower left abdomen, leading to the rectum. Pick it for the part of the colon just before the rectum.","The transverse colon crosses the upper abdomen from right to left. It would fit a question about the part of the colon held by the transverse mesocolon."],"lt":"t-accessory-digestive-organs","lid":"c6","comp":117,"competencyId":"w5-large-intestine"},{"id":"L02329","dok":1,"q":"Which part of the digestive tract is a narrow, worm-shaped, dead-end tube whose wall is rich in lymphatic tissue?","a":"The vermiform appendix.","options":["The cecum","The ileum","The sigmoid colon","The vermiform appendix"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Vermiform means worm-shaped, which describes the appendix well. It is a narrow, dead-end tube that hangs from the cecum at the start of the large intestine, and its wall is packed with lymphatic follicles. It belongs to the large intestine, not to the accessory digestive organs.","notes":["The cecum is the wide pouch at the start of the large intestine that the appendix hangs from. Pick it for the structure just below the ileocecal valve.","The ileum is the long, final part of the small intestine, and its wall does carry lymphatic Peyer patches. It is not a narrow dead end, so pick it for the segment that joins the cecum.","The sigmoid colon is the S-shaped last part of the colon, leading to the rectum. It would be the answer for the part of the colon in the lower left abdomen.","Correct. The vermiform appendix is the worm-shaped lymphatic tube off the cecum."],"lt":"t-accessory-digestive-organs","lid":"c34","comp":117,"competencyId":"w5-large-intestine"},{"id":"L04347","dok":3,"q":"A peptic ulcer begins in the mucosa of the stomach and erodes outward until it perforates the serosa. What is the main danger at that point?","a":"The contents of the lumen spill into the peritoneal cavity.","options":["Bile backs up into the gallbladder and liver","Stomach contents back up into the lower esophagus","Lumen contents spill into the peritoneal cavity","Lumen contents spill into the pleural cavity"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"A peptic ulcer is an eroded sore in the wall of the stomach or duodenum. It starts in the mucosa and can work outward through the submucosa and muscularis. The serosa is the visceral peritoneum, so once the ulcer breaks through it, food, acid and bacteria leak into the peritoneal cavity, which can cause a serious infection of the peritoneum.","notes":["Bile backing up toward the gallbladder and liver happens when a gallstone blocks the bile ducts. That would be the answer for a blocked common bile duct, not a perforated stomach.","Stomach contents moving up into the esophagus is reflux, caused by a weak lower esophageal sphincter rather than a hole in the wall. Choose it for a question about GERD or heartburn.","Correct. Breaking through the serosa opens the stomach into the peritoneal cavity.","The pleural cavity surrounds the lungs and lies above the diaphragm, so a hole in the stomach does not open into it. Students pick this when they mix up the serous membrane of the lungs with the peritoneum of the abdomen."],"lt":"t-w5-canal-wall","lid":"w5-canal-wall-r5","comp":119,"competencyId":"w5-canal-disorders"},{"id":"L04359","dok":1,"q":"The pyloric sphincter is a thickened ring at the outlet of the stomach. Which tissue forms it?","a":"Smooth muscle.","options":["Smooth muscle","Dense regular connective tissue","Skeletal muscle","Hyaline cartilage"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The pyloric sphincter is a thickened ring of the circular smooth muscle in the stomach wall, located between the pylorus and the duodenum. Because it is smooth muscle, it works involuntarily, tightening and relaxing to control how fast stomach contents move into the small intestine. You cannot open or close it by choice.","notes":["Correct. The pyloric sphincter is a ring of smooth muscle.","Dense regular connective tissue forms tendons and ligaments, which have parallel collagen fibers and no ability to contract. It would be the answer for a structure that resists pulling, not one that squeezes shut.","Skeletal muscle is the voluntary tissue that forms sphincters you can control, such as the external anal sphincter. Pick it for a question about a sphincter under conscious control.","Hyaline cartilage forms the C-shaped rings of the trachea and covers the ends of bones in joints. It holds a passage open rather than closing it, so it fits a question about the trachea, not a sphincter."],"lt":"t-w5-stomach","lid":"w5-stomach-r3","comp":114,"competencyId":"w5-canal-sphincters"},{"id":"L04360","dok":2,"q":"How does the muscularis of the stomach differ from the muscularis of the rest of the canal?","a":"It has a third smooth muscle layer, an inner oblique layer, in addition to the circular and longitudinal layers.","options":["It is made of skeletal rather than smooth muscle","It has only a single circular layer of smooth muscle","It adds a third, inner oblique muscle layer","It has a fibrous adventitia in place of a serosa"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Most of the canal has two layers of smooth muscle in its muscularis, an inner circular and an outer longitudinal layer. The stomach has three: an inner oblique layer, a middle circular layer, and an outer longitudinal layer. The extra oblique layer lets the stomach twist and churn its contents instead of only squeezing them forward.","notes":["The stomach wall is smooth muscle like the rest of the tube, and it works without conscious control. Skeletal muscle in the digestive tract is found at the two ends, such as the external anal sphincter.","The stomach has more muscle layers than the rest of the tube, not fewer. Students pick this when they remember that the stomach is different but forget which way the difference goes.","Correct. The stomach adds an inner oblique layer, giving it three layers instead of two.","A fibrous adventitia replaces the serosa on the esophagus, not on the stomach. Pick this if the question asks how the outer layer of the esophagus differs from the rest of the canal."],"lt":"t-w5-stomach","lid":"w5-stomach-r4","comp":115,"competencyId":"w5-stomach"},{"id":"L04361","dok":3,"q":"The stomach must churn food into a liquid, not just push it along. Which structural feature best supports this churning?","a":"The inner oblique layer of its muscularis.","options":["Villi that cover its mucosa","Gastric pits that lead to gastric glands","An inner oblique layer in its muscularis","Rugae that flatten as it fills"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Two layers of muscle can squeeze a tube narrower and shorter, which pushes contents forward. The stomach's third, inner oblique layer runs at an angle to the other two, so contracting all three together twists and kneads the contents. This churning mixes food with gastric juice until it becomes a liquid.","notes":["Villi are finger-like projections of the mucosa in the small intestine, not the stomach. They increase surface area for absorption, which is the answer for a question about the small intestine lining.","Gastric pits lead down to the gastric glands, which secrete gastric juice. They are the answer for where the stomach's secretions come from, not for how it moves.","Correct. The extra oblique muscle layer lets the stomach churn as well as squeeze.","Rugae are folds of the stomach lining that flatten out as the stomach fills. They let the stomach expand to hold a meal, so they are the answer for a question about storage capacity, not mixing."],"lt":"t-w5-stomach","lid":"w5-stomach-r5","comp":115,"competencyId":"w5-stomach"},{"id":"L04362","dok":1,"q":"Which sequence lists the three regions of the small intestine in the order food passes through them?","a":"Duodenum, jejunum, ileum.","options":["Duodenum, ileum, jejunum","Duodenum, jejunum, ileum","Ileum, jejunum, duodenum","Jejunum, duodenum, ileum"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Food leaves the stomach through the pyloric sphincter and enters the duodenum, the first and shortest region, about 25 cm (10 inches) long. It then moves through the jejunum, the middle region of about 1 m (3 feet), and finally the ileum, the longest region at about 2 m (6 feet). The ileum ends at the ileocecal sphincter, where it joins the large intestine.","notes":["This swaps the last two regions. The ileum is the final region and the one that joins the large intestine, so it must come after the jejunum.","Correct. Food passes through the duodenum, then the jejunum, then the ileum.","This is the right sequence read backward, from the large intestine toward the stomach. Choose it only if a question asks you to trace the small intestine in reverse.","The jejunum is the middle region, so it cannot come first. The duodenum receives food directly from the stomach and must lead the sequence."],"lt":"t-w5-small-intestine","lid":"w5-small-intestine-r1","comp":118,"competencyId":"w5-food-pathway"},{"id":"L04363","dok":1,"q":"What are the circular folds (plicae circulares) of the small intestine?","a":"Permanent ridges of the mucosa and submucosa that increase surface area.","options":["Folds of stomach lining that flatten when full","Microscopic projections on absorptive cells","Permanent ridges of the mucosa and submucosa","Finger-like projections of the mucosa alone"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Circular folds are permanent ridges that run around the inside of the small intestine. They are folds of both the mucosa and the submucosa, so unlike the rugae of the stomach, they do not flatten when the intestine fills. They are the largest of the three levels of folding that expand the absorptive surface, and villi cover their surface.","notes":["Folds of the stomach lining that flatten as the stomach fills are rugae. Pick rugae for a question about how the stomach expands to hold a meal.","Microscopic projections on the surface of single absorptive cells are microvilli, which form the brush border. They are the smallest level of folding and the answer for a question about the brush border.","Correct. Circular folds are permanent ridges of both the mucosa and the submucosa.","Finger-like projections of the mucosa alone are villi, the middle level of folding that sits on top of the circular folds. They are the answer for the structures that give the lining its velvety look."],"lt":"t-w5-small-intestine","lid":"w5-small-intestine-r3","comp":116,"competencyId":"w5-small-intestine"},{"id":"L04364","dok":2,"q":"What is a lacteal?","a":"The lymphatic capillary inside each villus that absorbs dietary fats.","options":["A nerve network in the submucosa that controls secretion","A blood capillary in a villus that carries off sugars","A pouch of the colon wall formed by the teniae coli","A lymphatic capillary in a villus that absorbs fats"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Each villus of the small intestine has a core of lamina propria that holds a network of blood capillaries and one central lymphatic capillary, the lacteal. Digested fats are packaged into particles too large to enter the blood capillaries, so they enter the lacteal instead and travel in the lymph. The milky look of this fat-rich lymph gives the lacteal its name.","notes":["A nerve network in the submucosa that controls secretions is the submucosal plexus. Choose it for a question about the enteric nervous system, not about absorbing fats.","Blood capillaries in the villus do absorb sugars and amino acids, but they are a separate network around the lacteal. Pick them if a question asks where most absorbed nutrients other than fats enter.","A pouch of the colon wall formed by the teniae coli is a haustrum. It would be the answer for the puckered pouches of the large intestine.","Correct. A lacteal is the lymphatic capillary in a villus that takes up dietary fats."],"lt":"t-w5-small-intestine","lid":"w5-small-intestine-r4","comp":116,"competencyId":"w5-small-intestine"},{"id":"L04365","dok":3,"q":"The lining of the small intestine is folded at three scales to expand its surface for absorption. Which sequence lists them from largest to smallest?","a":"Circular folds, villi, microvilli.","options":["Circular folds, villi, microvilli","Rugae, villi, microvilli","Microvilli, villi, circular folds","Villi, circular folds, microvilli"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The small intestine amplifies its surface three times over. Circular folds are ridges of the whole wall lining (mucosa and submucosa), villi are finger-like projections of the mucosa that cover those folds, and microvilli are microscopic projections on each absorptive cell that cover the villi. Each level sits on top of the one before it, which multiplies the area available for absorption.","notes":["Correct. Circular folds are largest, villi cover them, and microvilli on single cells are smallest.","Rugae are folds of the stomach lining, not the small intestine, and they flatten when the stomach fills. The missing largest level of the small intestine is the circular folds.","This is the correct set read from smallest to largest. Choose it only if a question asks you to start with the microscopic level.","This puts villi ahead of circular folds, but villi are smaller and sit on top of the circular folds. Students pick this because villi are the best known of the three."],"lt":"t-w5-small-intestine","lid":"w5-small-intestine-r5","comp":116,"competencyId":"w5-small-intestine"},{"id":"L04366","dok":1,"q":"Which sequence lists the four parts of the colon in the order feces pass through them?","a":"Ascending, transverse, descending, and sigmoid colon.","options":["Ascending, descending, transverse, sigmoid","Ascending, transverse, descending, sigmoid","Sigmoid, descending, transverse, ascending","Transverse, ascending, descending, sigmoid"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The colon begins at the cecum in the lower right abdomen. The ascending colon rises along the right side, the transverse colon crosses the upper abdomen, the descending colon runs down the left side, and the S-shaped sigmoid colon curves toward the midline to join the rectum. Following that frame around the abdomen gives the order.","notes":["This swaps the transverse and descending colon. The colon has to cross the abdomen before it can come down the left side, so the transverse colon comes second.","Correct. Ascending, transverse, descending, then sigmoid.","This is the correct sequence written backward, starting at the rectum. Pick it only if a question asks you to trace the colon in reverse.","The transverse colon cannot come first because the colon starts at the cecum in the lower right and must rise before it crosses. The ascending colon is the first part."],"lt":"t-w5-large-intestine","lid":"w5-large-intestine-r1","comp":118,"competencyId":"w5-food-pathway"},{"id":"L04367","dok":1,"q":"What is the cecum?","a":"The pouch at the start of the large intestine, just below the ileocecal valve.","options":["The S-shaped final curve of the colon in the pelvis","The last few centimeters ending at the anus","The straight part lying in front of the sacrum","The pouch at the start of the large intestine"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The cecum is the blind-ended pouch where the large intestine begins, in the lower right abdomen just below the ileocecal valve. The ileum empties into it, and the narrow, worm-like vermiform appendix hangs from it. From the cecum, the large intestine continues upward as the ascending colon.","notes":["The S-shaped final curve of the colon is the sigmoid colon, which joins the rectum. It is the answer for the last part of the colon on the left side of the pelvis.","The last few centimeters of the tract, ending at the anus, is the anal canal, not the cecum. Choose it for a question about where the internal and external anal sphincters sit.","The straight portion of the large intestine in front of the sacrum and coccyx is the rectum, not the cecum. Pick it for a question about the part that stores feces just before the anal canal.","Correct. The cecum is the first part of the large intestine, a pouch with the appendix attached."],"lt":"t-w5-large-intestine","lid":"w5-large-intestine-r2","comp":117,"competencyId":"w5-large-intestine"},{"id":"L04368","dok":2,"q":"The haustra are the pouches that give the colon its puckered look. What forms them?","a":"The teniae coli.","options":["The omental appendices","The circular folds","The rugae","The teniae coli"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The teniae coli are three thickened bands of longitudinal muscle that run the length of the colon. They are shorter than the colon wall they attach to, so their steady tone gathers the wall into a row of pouches, the haustra. That is why the colon looks puckered rather than smooth.","notes":["The omental appendices are small fat-filled pouches of peritoneum that hang from the colon surface, mostly along the teniae coli. They sit on the outside of the colon but do not form the haustra, so they are the answer for those fatty tags.","Circular folds are permanent ridges of the small intestine lining, not the colon. They are the answer for the largest level of folding in the small intestine.","Rugae are folds in the stomach lining that flatten as the stomach fills. Students pick them because they also give a wall a wrinkled look, but they belong to the stomach.","Correct. The teniae coli gather the colon wall into haustra."],"lt":"t-w5-large-intestine","lid":"w5-large-intestine-r4","comp":117,"competencyId":"w5-large-intestine"},{"id":"L04369","dok":3,"q":"Which job belongs to the large intestine rather than the small intestine?","a":"Absorbing the remaining water and forming and storing the feces.","options":["Absorbing leftover water and forming the feces","Churning food with acid into a liquid","Taking up dietary fats into lacteals","Absorbing most nutrients across the villi and microvilli"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"By the time material reaches the large intestine, most nutrients have already been absorbed in the small intestine. The large intestine absorbs much of the water that is left and compacts the remaining material into feces, which it stores until elimination. It has no villi, which fits a job that is about water and waste rather than nutrient absorption.","notes":["Correct. The large intestine absorbs the remaining water and forms and stores feces.","Churning food with acid until it becomes a liquid is the job of the stomach, with its three muscle layers and gastric glands. Choose it for a question about the stomach.","Lacteals are the lymphatic capillaries inside the villi of the small intestine. They are the answer for where dietary fats are absorbed, which is a small intestine job.","Absorbing most nutrients across villi is the job of the small intestine, whose circular folds, villi and microvilli give it a huge surface. Pick it for a question about where most absorption happens."],"lt":"t-w5-large-intestine","lid":"w5-large-intestine-r5","comp":117,"competencyId":"w5-large-intestine"},{"id":"L04407","dok":1,"q":"What does the pyloric sphincter separate?","a":"The pylorus of the stomach from the duodenum.","options":["The ileum from the cecum","The pylorus of the stomach from the duodenum","The esophagus from the cardia of the stomach","The duodenum from the jejunum"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The pyloric sphincter is a ring of smooth muscle at the outlet of the stomach. It sits between the pylorus, the last part of the stomach, and the duodenum, the first part of the small intestine. By tightening and relaxing it controls how quickly stomach contents pass into the small intestine.","notes":["The junction between the ileum and the cecum is guarded by the ileocecal sphincter. That is the answer for the passage from the small to the large intestine.","Correct. The pyloric sphincter lies between the pylorus and the duodenum.","The junction of the esophagus and the cardia is guarded by the lower esophageal sphincter. Pick it for the sphincter at the entrance of the stomach.","The duodenum continues into the jejunum without a named sphincter. Students pick this because both are parts of the small intestine near the stomach, but no sphincter separates them."],"lt":"t-w5-canal-sphincters","lid":"w5-canal-sphincters-r1","comp":114,"competencyId":"w5-canal-sphincters"},{"id":"L04408","dok":1,"q":"What does the ileocecal sphincter separate?","a":"The ileum from the large intestine at the cecum.","options":["The sigmoid colon from the rectum","The ileum from the cecum","The jejunum from the ileum","The stomach from the duodenum"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The ileocecal sphincter is a thickening of smooth muscle where the ileum, the last part of the small intestine, empties into the cecum, the first part of the large intestine. Together with folds of mucosa it forms the ileocecal valve, which keeps the contents of the large intestine from moving back into the ileum. It sits in the lower right abdomen.","notes":["The sigmoid colon joins the rectum without a named sphincter. The sphincters at the end of the tract are the internal and external anal sphincters, which guard the anus.","Correct. The ileocecal sphincter lies between the ileum and the cecum.","The jejunum blends into the ileum without a sphincter, and both lie within the small intestine. Students pick this when they remember the ileum is involved but forget which neighbor it meets.","The stomach and duodenum are separated by the pyloric sphincter. That is the answer for the outlet of the stomach."],"lt":"t-w5-canal-sphincters","lid":"w5-canal-sphincters-r2","comp":114,"competencyId":"w5-canal-sphincters"},{"id":"L04410","dok":1,"q":"Which sequence traces food from the mouth to the anus through the named regions of the canal?","a":"Mouth, pharynx, esophagus, stomach, small intestine, large intestine, anus.","options":["Mouth, pharynx, stomach, esophagus, small intestine, large intestine","Mouth, pharynx, esophagus, stomach, small intestine, large intestine","Mouth, esophagus, pharynx, stomach, small intestine, large intestine","Mouth, pharynx, esophagus, stomach, large intestine, small intestine"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Food moves through the alimentary canal in one direction. It is chewed in the mouth, swallowed through the pharynx, carried down the esophagus, churned in the stomach, absorbed mainly in the small intestine, and turned into feces in the large intestine before leaving through the anus. Each region hands its contents to the next.","notes":["This swaps the stomach and esophagus. The esophagus is the tube that carries food down to the stomach, so it has to come first.","Correct. The pathway runs mouth, pharynx, esophagus, stomach, small intestine, large intestine, then anus.","This puts the esophagus ahead of the pharynx, but food must pass through the pharynx at the back of the throat before it can enter the esophagus.","This swaps the two intestines. The small intestine receives food directly from the stomach, and the large intestine comes last, which is easy to forget because the names describe width, not order."],"lt":"t-w5-food-pathway","lid":"w5-food-pathway-r1","comp":118,"competencyId":"w5-food-pathway"},{"id":"L04412","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly matches the main jobs of the small and large intestines?","a":"The small intestine is where most absorption happens; the large intestine absorbs the remaining water and forms the feces.","options":["Small: most nutrient absorption; large: absorbs leftover water, forms feces","Small: stores the feces; large: absorbs fats into the lacteals","Small: churns food into a liquid; large: most nutrient absorption","Small: absorbs leftover water, forms feces; large: most nutrient absorption"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The small intestine, with its circular folds, villi and microvilli, has an enormous surface and is where most absorption occurs. By the time material reaches the large intestine, most nutrients are gone, so the large intestine absorbs much of the water that remains and forms and stores the feces. The large intestine has no villi, which fits a job about water and waste rather than nutrient absorption.","notes":["Correct. Most absorption is in the small intestine, and the large intestine reclaims leftover water and forms feces.","Lacteals are in the villi of the small intestine, and feces are stored in the large intestine. This pairing puts both jobs in the wrong organ.","Churning food into a liquid is the job of the stomach, not the small intestine. Pick churning for a question about the stomach and its three muscle layers.","This swaps the two intestines. Most nutrient absorption happens in the small intestine, whose circular folds and villi give it a huge surface, while the large intestine has no villi and mainly reclaims water and forms feces."],"lt":"t-w5-food-pathway","lid":"w5-food-pathway-r3","comp":117,"competencyId":"w5-large-intestine"},{"id":"L04414","dok":1,"q":"Appendicitis is inflammation of the vermiform appendix. Why is a ruptured appendix especially dangerous?","a":"Rupture can spread infection into the peritoneal cavity and the peritoneum.","options":["It pushes part of the stomach up through the diaphragm","It can block the flow of bile into the duodenum","It lets stomach acid back up into the esophagus","It can spread infection into the peritoneal cavity"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The vermiform appendix is a narrow, worm-like tube of lymphatic tissue attached to the cecum. When it is inflamed it can swell and burst, releasing bacteria-laden contents into the peritoneal cavity. Infection can then spread across the peritoneum, the large serous membrane lining the abdomen, which is why appendicitis is treated as an emergency.","notes":["Part of the stomach pushing up through the diaphragm is a hiatal hernia. That is the answer for a question about the esophageal hiatus, not the appendix.","Blocking the flow of bile into the duodenum is caused by a gallstone in the bile ducts. Pick it for a question about gallstones or jaundice.","Stomach acid backing up into the esophagus is reflux through a weak lower esophageal sphincter, as in GERD. It is the answer for heartburn behind the sternum.","Correct. A ruptured appendix can spread infection through the peritoneal cavity."],"lt":"t-w5-canal-disorders","lid":"w5-canal-disorders-r2","comp":119,"competencyId":"w5-canal-disorders"},{"id":"L04415","dok":2,"q":"A patient has pain that settles in the lower right abdomen. Which structure of the canal is classically involved?","a":"The vermiform appendix, suggesting appendicitis.","options":["Sigmoid colon","Gallbladder","Appendix","Stomach"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Where a problem sits in the abdomen predicts where the patient feels it. The vermiform appendix hangs from the cecum, which lies in the lower right abdomen, so pain that settles there classically points to appendicitis. This is one reason anatomists divide the abdomen into quadrants and regions.","notes":["The sigmoid colon curves through the lower left abdomen into the pelvis. Pick it for lower left pain, such as inflammation of pouches in the colon wall.","The gallbladder sits under the liver in the upper right abdomen. It would be the answer for upper right pain after a fatty meal, as with gallstones.","Correct. The appendix lies in the lower right abdomen, so pain there suggests appendicitis.","The stomach lies in the upper abdomen, mostly to the left of the midline below the diaphragm. It is the answer for upper abdominal pain, as with a peptic ulcer."],"lt":"t-w5-canal-disorders","lid":"w5-canal-disorders-r3","comp":119,"competencyId":"w5-canal-disorders"},{"id":"N-a17d-114-1","dok":3,"q":"A swallowed camera pill travels from the pylorus to the anus. Which is the first sphincter made of skeletal muscle that it passes?","a":"The external anal sphincter.","options":["The external anal sphincter.","The ileocecal sphincter.","The pyloric sphincter.","The internal anal sphincter."],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Only the two ends of the canal have skeletal muscle sphincters: the upper esophageal sphincter at the top and the external anal sphincter at the bottom. Starting at the pylorus, the pill passes the pyloric sphincter, the ileocecal sphincter, and the internal anal sphincter, all smooth muscle. The external anal sphincter, outside the internal one, is the first skeletal muscle sphincter it meets, which is why it is the one under voluntary control.","notes":["Correct. Every sphincter from the pylorus onward is smooth muscle until the external anal sphincter at the very end.","The ileocecal sphincter is a thickening of smooth muscle at the end of the ileum, where it opens into the cecum.","The pyloric sphincter is a thick ring of circular smooth muscle around the pylorus, between the stomach and the duodenum.","The internal anal sphincter is a thickening of the circular smooth muscle of the anal canal, so it is involuntary smooth muscle."],"comp":114,"competencyId":"w5-canal-sphincters"},{"id":"N-a17d-114-2","dok":3,"q":"A swallowed coin stops in the esophagus inside the thorax, above the diaphragm. Which sphincter did it most recently pass through, and what tissue forms that sphincter?","a":"The upper esophageal sphincter, made of skeletal muscle.","options":["The upper esophageal sphincter, made of smooth muscle.","The pyloric sphincter, made of smooth muscle.","The lower esophageal sphincter, made of smooth muscle.","The upper esophageal sphincter, made of skeletal muscle."],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The upper esophageal sphincter sits at the top of the esophagus, at the level of the cricoid cartilage, and separates the laryngopharynx from the esophagus. It is skeletal muscle, continuous with the muscles of the pharynx. The next sphincter, the lower esophageal sphincter, is at the bottom of the esophagus below the diaphragm. A coin stuck in the thoracic esophagus has passed only the upper one.","notes":["The sphincter is right, but it is skeletal muscle. Only the two ends of the canal have skeletal muscle sphincters, and this is the top one.","The pyloric sphincter is at the outlet of the stomach. A coin still in the esophagus is far above it.","The lower esophageal sphincter is at the bottom of the esophagus, just below the diaphragm. A coin stuck above the diaphragm has not reached it.","Correct. The coin entered the esophagus through the upper esophageal sphincter, which is skeletal muscle continuous with the pharynx."],"comp":114,"competencyId":"w5-canal-sphincters"},{"id":"N-a17d-114-3","dok":3,"q":"A camera pill shows a thickened ring of smooth muscle with folds of mucosa, forming a valve. On the far side is a blind pouch. Which sphincter is this, and which two regions does it separate?","a":"The ileocecal sphincter, between the ileum and the cecum.","options":["The internal anal sphincter, between the anal canal and the outside.","The lower esophageal sphincter, between the esophagus and stomach.","The ileocecal sphincter, between the ileum and the cecum.","The pyloric sphincter, between the stomach and the duodenum."],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Two clues identify it. The ileocecal sphincter is a thickening of smooth muscle at the end of the ileum that, together with folds of mucosa, forms the ileocecal valve. The cecum, the first part of the large intestine, is a blind pouch below that valve. So this sphincter separates the small intestine (ileum) from the large intestine (cecum).","notes":["The internal anal sphincter opens to the outside of the body at the anus, so nothing lies beyond it inside the canal.","The lower esophageal sphincter opens into the cardia of the stomach. The stomach is a J-shaped widening of the tube, not a blind pouch.","Correct. The ileocecal sphincter and mucosal folds form the ileocecal valve, and the blind pouch beyond it is the cecum.","The pyloric sphincter is a ring of circular smooth muscle at the pylorus. The duodenum beyond it is a C-shaped tube, not a blind pouch."],"comp":114,"competencyId":"w5-canal-sphincters"},{"id":"N-a17d-115-1","dok":3,"q":"An ulcer sits on the short, concave border of the stomach, on the right side facing the liver. Which border is this, and which peritoneal fold attaches along it?","a":"The lesser curvature, where the lesser omentum attaches.","options":["The lesser curvature, where the lesser omentum attaches.","The greater curvature, where the lesser omentum attaches.","The greater curvature, where the greater omentum hangs.","The lesser curvature, where the greater omentum hangs."],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The stomach has two borders. The lesser curvature is the short, concave border on the right (medial) side, facing the liver, and the lesser omentum runs from it up to the liver. The greater curvature is the long, convex border on the left (lateral) side, and the greater omentum hangs from it. Matching the size words, lesser goes with lesser and greater goes with greater.","notes":["Correct. The short, concave right border is the lesser curvature, and the lesser omentum attaches along it.","This mixes the two. The lesser omentum runs from the lesser curvature to the liver, and the greater curvature is the long, convex border.","The greater curvature is the long, convex left border, and the greater omentum hangs from it. The ulcer is on the short, concave border.","The border is right, but the greater omentum hangs from the greater curvature. The lesser omentum attaches to the lesser curvature."],"comp":115,"competencyId":"w5-stomach"},{"id":"N-a17d-117-1","dok":3,"q":"On the cadaver, you follow the three teniae coli down the ascending colon to the point where they meet. What do you find there, and which part of the large intestine is it attached to?","a":"The base of the appendix, on the cecum.","options":["The ileocecal valve, on the cecum.","The hepatic flexure, under the liver.","The base of the appendix, on the rectum.","The base of the appendix, on the cecum."],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The teniae coli are three flat ribbons of smooth muscle along the cecum and colon. Following them down the ascending colon leads to the cecum, the blind pouch at the start of the large intestine. The three teniae meet at the base of the vermiform appendix, which is one way to find the appendix on a specimen.","notes":["The ileocecal valve is where the ileum opens into the large intestine. The teniae coli meet at the base of the appendix, not at the valve.","The hepatic flexure is the bend at the top of the ascending colon. Following the teniae down the ascending colon leads away from it, to the cecum.","The landmark is right, but the appendix is attached to the cecum. The teniae spread out into a full layer at the rectum, which is in the pelvis.","Correct. The three teniae meet at the base of the vermiform appendix, which hangs from the cecum."],"comp":117,"competencyId":"w5-large-intestine"},{"id":"N-a17d-118-1","dok":1,"q":"As material moves through the colon, which bend does it pass between the transverse colon and the descending colon?","a":"The splenic (left colic) flexure.","options":["The splenic (left colic) flexure.","The hepatic (right colic) flexure.","The sigmoid colon.","The ileocecal valve."],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The colon frames the small intestine. Material rises in the ascending colon, turns at the hepatic flexure under the liver, crosses the abdomen in the transverse colon from right to left, then turns down at the splenic flexure near the spleen into the descending colon. The two flexures are bends between the parts of the colon, not separate parts.","notes":["Correct. The transverse colon crosses the abdomen from right to left and turns down at the splenic flexure, the bend near the spleen, into the descending colon.","The hepatic flexure is the bend under the liver between the ascending and transverse colon. Material passes it before the transverse colon, not after.","The sigmoid colon is the S-shaped curve into the pelvis. It comes after the descending colon, so material reaches it later.","The ileocecal valve sits between the ileum and the cecum, at the start of the large intestine, well before the transverse colon."],"comp":118,"competencyId":"w5-food-pathway"},{"id":"N-a17d-118-2","dok":3,"q":"A swallowed seed is found in the transverse colon. In order, which named structures did it pass after leaving the ileum?","a":"Ileocecal valve, cecum, ascending colon, hepatic flexure.","options":["Pyloric sphincter, cecum, ascending colon, hepatic flexure.","Ileocecal valve, appendix, ascending colon, hepatic flexure.","Ileocecal valve, cecum, ascending colon, hepatic flexure.","Ileocecal valve, cecum, descending colon, splenic flexure."],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The colon frames the small intestine. Leaving the ileum, material passes the ileocecal sphincter (ileocecal valve) into the cecum, then travels up the right side in the ascending colon. The bend under the liver, the hepatic flexure, turns it left into the transverse colon. The appendix is off the main path, and the splenic flexure and descending colon come later.","notes":["The pyloric sphincter sits between the stomach and the duodenum. The sphincter at the end of the ileum is the ileocecal sphincter, which forms the ileocecal valve.","The appendix is a blind tube hanging from the cecum, so food never travels through it. The seed passes through the cecum instead.","Correct. From the ileum, material crosses the ileocecal valve into the cecum, rises in the ascending colon, and turns at the hepatic flexure into the transverse colon.","The descending colon and splenic flexure come after the transverse colon, on the left side. The seed reached the transverse colon from the right side."],"comp":118,"competencyId":"w5-food-pathway"},{"id":"N-a17d-118-3","dok":3,"q":"A bolus is in the laryngopharynx. Which structure covers the larynx so the bolus does not enter it, and which named structure does the bolus pass through next?","a":"The epiglottis; next, the upper esophageal sphincter.","options":["The epiglottis; next, the upper esophageal sphincter.","The epiglottis; next, the lower esophageal sphincter.","The uvula; next, the fauces into the oropharynx.","The soft palate; next, the upper esophageal sphincter."],"correctIndex":0,"why":"In the laryngopharynx the air and food paths split. The epiglottis covers the larynx, so the bolus passes behind it toward the esophagus. The upper esophageal sphincter, at the level of the cricoid cartilage, then relaxes to let the bolus into the esophagus. The soft palate and uvula worked earlier, closing off the nasopharynx while the bolus was in the oropharynx.","notes":["Correct. The epiglottis covers the larynx, and the bolus passes behind it and through the upper esophageal sphincter into the esophagus.","The epiglottis is right, but the lower esophageal sphincter is at the bottom of the esophagus. The bolus meets the upper one first.","The uvula helps close the nasopharynx, and the fauces lead from the mouth into the oropharynx. A bolus in the laryngopharynx has already passed both.","The soft palate and uvula close off the nasopharynx, higher up. They do not cover the larynx."],"comp":118,"competencyId":"w5-food-pathway"}]},{"id":"m4-accessory-organs","title":"Salivary glands, liver, gallbladder and pancreas","summary":"salivary glands, liver lobes and lobule, bile pathway, gallbladder, pancreas structure, accessory organ disorders","mw":"2026-11-04","tr":"2026-11-10","cards":[{"id":"L02214","dok":1,"q":"The teeth, tongue, salivary glands, liver, gallbladder, and pancreas are grouped together as what?","a":"The accessory digestive organs.","options":["The accessory digestive organs","Lymphatic organs of the gut","Retroperitoneal organs","Organs of the alimentary canal"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The digestive system has two groups of organs. The alimentary canal is the tube that food passes through, while the accessory digestive organs sit alongside it and help digestion without being part of the tube. The teeth and tongue work on food in the mouth, and the salivary glands, liver, gallbladder, and pancreas empty their secretions into the canal.","notes":["Correct. These six help digestion from outside the food tube, which is what makes them the accessory digestive organs.","Lymphatic tissue of the gut, such as the tonsils and the appendix, holds immune cells. None of the six organs listed is lymphatic tissue, so this group does not fit.","Retroperitoneal organs lie behind the peritoneum, such as the kidneys, pancreas, and duodenum. The pancreas fits, but the liver, gallbladder, and tongue do not, so this group does not match the list.","The alimentary canal is the tube food passes through, from the mouth to the anus. None of these six organs is part of that tube, so this group is the opposite of the right answer."],"lt":"t-alimentary-canal","lid":"c18","comp":109,"competencyId":"w5-canal-wall"},{"id":"L02280","dok":2,"q":"Which list correctly matches each of the three large pairs of salivary glands to its location?","a":"Parotid in front of and below the ear, submandibular along the inner mandible, sublingual beneath the tongue.","options":["Parotid along the inner mandible, submandibular beneath the tongue, sublingual in front of and below the ear","Parotid beneath the tongue, submandibular in front of and below the ear, sublingual along the inner mandible","Parotid in front of and below the ear, submandibular beneath the tongue, sublingual along the inner mandible","Parotid in front of and below the ear, submandibular along the inner mandible, sublingual beneath the tongue"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The parotid glands sit anterior and inferior to each ear, between the skin and the masseter muscle. The submandibular glands lie in the floor of the mouth along the inner side of the mandible, and the sublingual glands sit beneath the tongue, just above the submandibular glands. The names help: sub-mandibular means under the jaw, and sub-lingual means under the tongue.","notes":["This list puts every gland in the wrong spot. The parotid sits by the ear, the gland along the inner mandible is the submandibular, and the sublingual sits under the tongue.","This list moves every gland to the wrong spot. The parotid is the gland by the ear, not under the tongue, so use the name parotid (para means beside, otic means ear) to anchor it next to the ear.","The parotid is placed correctly, but the other two are swapped. The submandibular lies along the inner mandible, and the sublingual lies beneath the tongue, just above the submandibular.","Correct. Each name gives the spot: parotid means beside the ear, submandibular means below the mandible, and sublingual means below the tongue."],"lt":"t-accessory-digestive-organs","lid":"fr1","comp":121,"competencyId":"w5-salivary-glands"},{"id":"L02282","dok":1,"q":"Which list names the four lobes of the liver?","a":"Right, left, quadrate, and caudate.","options":["Head, body, tail, and caudate","Right, left, superior, and inferior","Superior, middle, inferior, and quadrate","Right, left, quadrate, and caudate"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The liver has two large principal lobes, the right and left, separated on the anterior surface by the falciform ligament. On the posterior and inferior surface you can also see two small lobes, the quadrate lobe and the caudate lobe. All four are on the lab structure list, so practice finding the two small lobes when the liver is turned over.","notes":["Head, body, and tail are the three regions of the pancreas. That set would be right for a question about pancreas regions, not liver lobes.","Superior and inferior are lobe names for the lungs, not the liver. The left lung has a superior and an inferior lobe, so this list mixes liver lobes with lung lobes.","Superior, middle, and inferior are the three lobes of the right lung. Pick that set if the question asked about the right lung; only the quadrate lobe here belongs to the liver.","Correct. The right and left lobes are seen from the front, and the quadrate and caudate lobes are the two small lobes seen from below and behind."],"lt":"t-accessory-digestive-organs","lid":"fr3","comp":122,"competencyId":"w5-liver-gross-lobule"},{"id":"L02283","dok":2,"q":"Which group of structures passes through the porta hepatis on the underside of the liver?","a":"The hepatic portal vein, hepatic artery, and hepatic ducts.","options":["The hepatic portal vein, inferior vena cava, and pancreatic duct","The hepatic veins, hepatic portal vein, and common bile duct","The hepatic portal vein, hepatic artery, and hepatic ducts","The hepatic veins, hepatic artery, and cystic duct"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The porta hepatis is the gateway on the inferior surface of the liver. Two vessels bring blood in through it, the hepatic portal vein (nutrient-rich blood from the digestive organs) and the hepatic artery (oxygen-rich blood), while the right and left hepatic ducts carry bile out. The common bile duct does not pass through it, because that duct forms below the liver where the common hepatic duct meets the cystic duct.","notes":["The inferior vena cava runs along the back of the liver, not through the porta hepatis, and the pancreatic duct never reaches the liver. The vena cava would be the answer for the vessel that receives the hepatic veins.","This mixes in two structures that do not belong. The hepatic veins leave from the back of the liver, and the common bile duct forms outside the liver, below the porta hepatis, where the common hepatic and cystic ducts join.","Correct. The hepatic portal vein and hepatic artery bring blood in at the porta hepatis, and the right and left hepatic ducts carry bile out.","The hepatic veins drain blood out of the liver into the inferior vena cava from the back of the liver, not through the porta hepatis, and the cystic duct belongs to the gallbladder. Pick the hepatic veins for a question about where blood leaves the liver."],"lt":"t-accessory-digestive-organs","lid":"fr4","comp":122,"competencyId":"w5-liver-gross-lobule"},{"id":"L02284","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares the hepatic sinusoids and the bile canaliculi?","a":"Sinusoids carry blood between the hepatocyte plates; canaliculi collect bile between the hepatocytes.","options":["Both carry blood, but sinusoids carry arterial blood and canaliculi carry portal blood","Sinusoids carry blood between the plates of hepatocytes, and canaliculi collect bile between the hepatocytes","Both carry bile, but sinusoids drain to the gallbladder and canaliculi drain to the duodenum","Sinusoids collect bile between the hepatocytes, and canaliculi carry blood between the plates of hepatocytes"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Hepatocytes, the liver's working cells, are stacked in thin plates called hepatic laminae. The hepatic sinusoids are wide, leaky capillaries that run between the plates, so blood flows right past the cells. The bile canaliculi are tiny channels between neighboring hepatocytes that collect the bile those cells make, so blood and bile travel in separate channels.","notes":["Only the sinusoids carry blood; they receive a mix of arterial and portal blood. The canaliculi never carry blood, since their job is to collect bile from the hepatocytes.","Correct. Sinusoids are leaky capillaries that carry blood past the plates, while canaliculi are tiny bile channels between neighboring hepatocytes.","Only the canaliculi carry bile. Sinusoids are blood capillaries, and bile reaches the gallbladder and duodenum through the duct system, not directly from either channel.","This swaps the two jobs. Sinusoids are capillaries carrying blood and canaliculi carry bile, so remember that canaliculi (little canals) are the bile channels."],"lt":"t-accessory-digestive-organs","lid":"fr5","comp":122,"competencyId":"w5-liver-gross-lobule"},{"id":"L02285","dok":2,"q":"Bile made by hepatocytes leaves the liver through a series of widening channels. Which sequence is correct, starting at the hepatocytes?","a":"Bile canaliculi, right and left hepatic ducts, common hepatic duct.","options":["Hepatic sinusoids, right and left hepatic ducts, common hepatic duct","Bile canaliculi, right and left hepatic ducts, common hepatic duct","Right and left hepatic ducts, bile canaliculi, common bile duct","Bile canaliculi, common hepatic duct, right and left hepatic ducts"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Bile drains from small channels into larger ones, like streams joining a river. The tiny bile canaliculi between hepatocytes collect it first, then the bile is gathered into the right and left hepatic ducts, and those two merge into the single common hepatic duct as they leave the liver. From there bile can detour up the cystic duct to the gallbladder or continue in the common bile duct.","notes":["Hepatic sinusoids are blood capillaries between the hepatocyte plates, not bile channels. Pick the sinusoids if the question asked where blood flows past the hepatocytes.","Correct. Bile starts in the canaliculi, gathers into the right and left hepatic ducts, and those merge into the common hepatic duct.","The canaliculi are the very first channels, right between the hepatocytes, so they cannot come after the hepatic ducts. The common bile duct also forms later, below the liver, once the cystic duct joins.","This reverses the last two steps. The right and left hepatic ducts come first and merge into the common hepatic duct, so one common duct cannot split back into two on the way out."],"lt":"t-accessory-digestive-organs","lid":"fr6","comp":123,"competencyId":"w5-bile-path"},{"id":"L02286","dok":1,"q":"Which region of the gallbladder is the broad, rounded end at its inferior tip?","a":"The fundus.","options":["The fundus","The cardia","The body","The neck"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The gallbladder has three regions: the fundus, the body, and the neck. The fundus is the broad, rounded inferior end, the body is the middle, and the neck tapers at the superior end where the sac narrows into the cystic duct. The stomach also has a region called the fundus, so the word simply means the rounded end or base of a hollow organ.","notes":["Correct. The fundus is the wide, rounded end that sticks out past the lower edge of the liver.","The cardia is the region of the stomach that surrounds the opening from the esophagus. It is not part of the gallbladder, though the stomach does share the term fundus with it.","The body is the middle portion of the gallbladder, between the fundus and the neck. Pick it for the central part of the sac, not the rounded tip.","The neck is the tapered superior end of the gallbladder that leads into the cystic duct. It would be the answer if the question asked which region connects to the cystic duct."],"lt":"t-accessory-digestive-organs","lid":"fr7","comp":123,"competencyId":"w5-bile-path"},{"id":"L02288","dok":1,"q":"Which pancreatic duct empties separately into the duodenum instead of joining the common bile duct?","a":"The accessory pancreatic duct (duct of Santorini).","options":["The cystic duct","The common hepatic duct","The accessory pancreatic duct","The main pancreatic duct"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The pancreas usually drains through two ducts. The main pancreatic duct, also called the duct of Wirsung, joins the common bile duct at the hepatopancreatic ampulla. The smaller accessory duct, also called the duct of Santorini, drains part of the head and empties into the duodenum on its own at the minor duodenal papilla, so only the main duct shares an opening with bile.","notes":["The cystic duct connects the gallbladder to the bile duct system and carries no pancreatic juice. It would be the answer for the duct that carries bile to and from the gallbladder.","The common hepatic duct is a bile duct, not a pancreatic duct. It carries bile out of the liver and joins the cystic duct to make the common bile duct.","Correct. The accessory duct (duct of Santorini) opens on its own at the minor duodenal papilla, a little above the major papilla.","The main pancreatic duct (duct of Wirsung) is the one that does join the common bile duct at the hepatopancreatic ampulla. Pick it if the question asked which pancreatic duct meets the bile duct."],"lt":"t-accessory-digestive-organs","lid":"fr9","comp":124,"competencyId":"w5-pancreas"},{"id":"L02290","dok":1,"q":"Pancreatic juice made by the acini drains through the pancreatic ducts. Where does it finally empty?","a":"Into the duodenum.","options":["The stomach","The gallbladder","The bloodstream","The duodenum"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The acini are the exocrine clusters of the pancreas. Exocrine glands release their products through ducts onto a surface, and for the pancreas that surface is the lining of the duodenum, the first part of the small intestine. The main pancreatic duct delivers juice through the hepatopancreatic ampulla, and the accessory duct empties separately, but both end in the duodenum.","notes":["The stomach sits in front of the pancreas, but no pancreatic duct opens into it. The stomach would be the answer if the question asked where gastric juice is made.","The gallbladder stores bile from the liver and receives no pancreatic juice. Pick it if the question asked where bile is stored and concentrated between meals.","The bloodstream is where the endocrine pancreatic islets release their hormones, without ducts. It would be the answer for islet hormones, not for pancreatic juice from the acini.","Correct. Both pancreatic ducts end in the duodenum, so that is where pancreatic juice is delivered."],"lt":"t-accessory-digestive-organs","lid":"fr11","comp":124,"competencyId":"w5-pancreas"},{"id":"L02292","dok":2,"q":"Starting at the common bile duct, which sequence shows the rest of bile's route into the small intestine?","a":"Common bile duct, hepatopancreatic ampulla, major duodenal papilla, duodenum.","options":["Common bile duct, cystic duct, gallbladder, duodenum","Common bile duct, hepatopancreatic ampulla, major duodenal papilla, duodenum","Common bile duct, pancreatic duct, pyloric sphincter, stomach","Common bile duct, major duodenal papilla, hepatopancreatic ampulla, duodenum"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The common bile duct runs down to meet the main pancreatic duct, and the two join in a short widened passage called the hepatopancreatic ampulla. The ampulla opens into the duodenum at the major duodenal papilla, a small raised bump on the duodenal lining, with the sphincter of Oddi controlling the flow. The papilla is the doorway, so it has to come last before the duodenum.","notes":["The cystic duct and gallbladder lie upstream of the common bile duct, so this route runs backward. It would describe bile backing up into the gallbladder for storage, not bile heading into the intestine.","Correct. The common bile duct empties into the ampulla, and the ampulla opens into the duodenum at the major duodenal papilla.","Bile never flows into the stomach through a normal route, and the pyloric sphincter guards the exit from the stomach into the duodenum. Pick the pyloric sphincter for a question about stomach emptying.","The ampulla and the papilla are in the wrong order. Bile and pancreatic juice meet in the ampulla first and then pass out through the papilla, which is the opening on the duodenal wall."],"lt":"t-accessory-digestive-organs","lid":"fr13","comp":123,"competencyId":"w5-bile-path"},{"id":"L02293","dok":1,"q":"Hepatocytes are arranged in thin plates with blood channels running between them. What are these plates called?","a":"Hepatic laminae.","options":["Acini","Portal triads","Bile canaliculi","Hepatic laminae"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Hepatocytes, the functional cells of the liver, are lined up in plates called hepatic laminae (lamina means a thin sheet). Wide, leaky capillaries called hepatic sinusoids run between the plates, so nearly every hepatocyte has blood flowing right past it. This arrangement lets the cells pull nutrients from the blood and add products back to it.","notes":["Acini are the exocrine clusters of the pancreas that make pancreatic juice. They would be the answer for the main tissue of the pancreas, not the liver.","A portal triad is the trio of a bile duct, a hepatic artery branch, and a hepatic portal vein branch at each corner of a liver lobule. Pick it if the question asked what sits at the corners of a lobule.","Bile canaliculi are tiny channels between hepatocytes that collect bile. Pick them if the question asked where bile first drains, not what the cell plates are called.","Correct. Lamina means a thin sheet, and the plates of hepatocytes are called the hepatic laminae."],"lt":"t-accessory-digestive-organs","lid":"fr14","comp":122,"competencyId":"w5-liver-gross-lobule"},{"id":"L02295","dok":2,"q":"Which set correctly matches each large salivary gland to where its duct opens into the mouth?","a":"Parotid beside the upper second molar, submandibular near the lingual frenulum, sublingual along the floor of the mouth.","options":["Parotid beside the upper second molar, submandibular near the lingual frenulum, sublingual along the floor of the mouth","Parotid near the lingual frenulum, submandibular beside the upper second molar, sublingual along the floor of the mouth","Parotid along the floor of the mouth, submandibular near the lingual frenulum, sublingual beside the upper second molar","Parotid beside the lower incisors, submandibular beside the upper second molar, sublingual near the lingual frenulum"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Each duct opens close to its gland. The parotid gland sits by the ear, so its duct crosses the cheek and opens on the inside of the cheek beside the upper second molar. The submandibular ducts open near the lingual frenulum under the tongue, and the sublingual glands drain through several small ducts onto the floor of the mouth.","notes":["Correct. The parotid duct crosses the cheek to the upper second molar, the submandibular duct opens beside the frenulum, and the sublingual ducts open along the floor of the mouth.","This swaps the parotid and submandibular openings. The parotid duct opens high in the cheek beside the upper second molar, while the submandibular ducts open low, near the lingual frenulum.","The parotid duct does not open on the floor of the mouth, and the sublingual ducts do not reach the upper molars. Only the submandibular opening is placed correctly here.","No large salivary duct opens beside the lower incisors in the cheek, and the submandibular duct opens under the tongue, not by the upper molar. This set scrambles all three."],"lt":"t-accessory-digestive-organs","lid":"fr16","comp":121,"competencyId":"w5-salivary-glands"},{"id":"L02296","dok":1,"q":"Which of these structures is NOT one of the six accessory digestive organs?","a":"The vermiform appendix.","options":["The pancreas","The vermiform appendix","The gallbladder","The tongue"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The six accessory digestive organs are the teeth, tongue, salivary glands, liver, gallbladder, and pancreas. The vermiform appendix is a small tube of lymphatic tissue attached to the cecum, so it is part of the large intestine, which belongs to the alimentary canal itself. Accessory organs help digestion from outside the food tube, while the appendix is a branch of the tube.","notes":["The pancreas is one of the six accessory organs, since it sends pancreatic juice to the duodenum through its ducts. Because it belongs to the group, it cannot be the exception.","Correct. The appendix hangs from the cecum, so it is part of the large intestine, not one of the accessory organs.","The gallbladder is one of the six accessory organs. It stores and concentrates bile, so it belongs to the group and is not the exception.","The tongue is one of the six accessory organs. Along with the teeth it works on food in the mouth, so it belongs to the group and is not the exception."],"lt":"t-accessory-digestive-organs","lid":"c1","comp":109,"competencyId":"w5-canal-wall"},{"id":"L02297","dok":1,"q":"Which is the largest of the three large pairs of salivary glands?","a":"The parotid glands.","options":["The parotid glands","The submandibular glands","The sublingual glands","The buccal glands"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The parotid glands are the largest salivary glands. Each one sits anterior and inferior to the ear, between the skin and the masseter muscle, and its duct crosses the cheek to open beside the upper second molar. Their size and position are why swelling from mumps shows so clearly at the angle of the jaw.","notes":["Correct. The parotids are the largest of the three pairs, bigger than both the submandibular and sublingual glands.","The submandibular glands are one of the three large pairs, but they are smaller than the parotids. They would be the answer for the glands that lie along the inner side of the mandible.","The sublingual glands are the smallest of the three large pairs and sit beneath the tongue. Pick them for the glands that drain through several small ducts onto the floor of the mouth.","The buccal glands are minor salivary glands scattered in the lining of the cheek, not one of the three large pairs. They would fit a question about the small glands of the mouth lining."],"lt":"t-accessory-digestive-organs","lid":"c2","comp":121,"competencyId":"w5-salivary-glands"},{"id":"L02298","dok":1,"q":"Which structure stores and concentrates bile between meals?","a":"The gallbladder.","options":["The gallbladder","The liver","The duodenum","The pancreas"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The gallbladder is a pear-shaped sac on the posterior surface of the liver. Bile made in the liver backs up through the cystic duct into the gallbladder, where it is stored and concentrated until a meal arrives. It is the only accessory organ that works as a storage organ rather than as a gland.","notes":["Correct. The gallbladder stores and concentrates bile.","The liver makes bile but does not store it. Pick the liver if the question asked which organ produces bile.","The duodenum is where bile is delivered, through the major duodenal papilla. Pick it for the part of the small intestine that receives bile and pancreatic juice.","The pancreas makes pancreatic juice and hormones, not bile. It would be the answer for the accessory organ that has both acini and islets."],"lt":"t-accessory-digestive-organs","lid":"c3","comp":123,"competencyId":"w5-bile-path"},{"id":"L02299","dok":1,"q":"Which organ is the largest gland in the body?","a":"The liver.","options":["The liver","The pancreas","The thyroid gland","The parotid gland"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The liver is the largest gland in the body. It sits just below the diaphragm, mostly in the right upper quadrant, and its main digestive job is producing bile. It counts as a gland because its cells make and release products, including bile into the ducts and plasma proteins into the blood.","notes":["Correct. The liver is the largest gland in the body.","The pancreas is a large gland behind the stomach, but it is much smaller than the liver. Pick it if the question asked which gland is both exocrine and endocrine.","The thyroid is a butterfly-shaped endocrine gland in the neck, far smaller than the liver. It would be the answer for the gland that wraps the front of the trachea just below the larynx.","The parotid is the largest salivary gland, not the largest gland overall. Pick it for the salivary gland in front of and below the ear."],"lt":"t-accessory-digestive-organs","lid":"c4","comp":122,"competencyId":"w5-liver-gross-lobule"},{"id":"L02300","dok":1,"q":"Where is the pancreas located?","a":"Posterior to the stomach","options":["On the posterior surface of the liver","Posterior to the stomach","In the lower right quadrant beside the cecum","Anterior to the stomach"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The pancreas is a soft, tapering gland that lies posterior to the stomach, against the posterior abdominal wall in a retroperitoneal position. Its head sits in the C-shaped curve of the duodenum and its tail reaches toward the spleen. Because it hides behind the stomach, you usually have to lift the stomach to see it on a model.","notes":["The posterior surface of the liver is where the gallbladder sits. Pick that location for the gallbladder, not the pancreas.","Correct. The pancreas lies posterior to the stomach.","The lower right quadrant beside the cecum is where the appendix lies. That location would be the answer for the appendix, not a gland in the upper abdomen.","The pancreas is hidden behind the stomach, not in front of it. In front of the stomach is the anterior abdominal wall, with the left lobe of the liver reaching over the stomach higher up."],"lt":"t-accessory-digestive-organs","lid":"c5","comp":124,"competencyId":"w5-pancreas"},{"id":"L02302","dok":1,"q":"What is the name of the main functional cell of the liver?","a":"The hepatocyte.","options":["Goblet cells","Hepatocytes","Islet cells","Acinar cells"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Hepatocytes (hepat means liver, cyte means cell) are the functional cells of the liver. They are arranged in plates called hepatic laminae, they make bile, and they process the nutrients delivered by the blood flowing past them in the sinusoids. Almost every liver job you learn traces back to hepatocytes.","notes":["Goblet cells are mucus-secreting cells scattered in the epithelium lining the intestines and airways. Pick them for a question about mucus production in a simple columnar epithelium.","Correct. Hepat means liver and cyte means cell, so hepatocytes are the working cells of the liver.","Islet cells form the pancreatic islets, the endocrine clusters of the pancreas. They would be the answer for the pancreatic cells that release hormones into the blood.","Acinar cells make up the acini, the exocrine clusters of the pancreas. Pick them for the cells that produce pancreatic juice."],"lt":"t-accessory-digestive-organs","lid":"c7","comp":122,"competencyId":"w5-liver-gross-lobule"},{"id":"L02303","dok":1,"q":"Under the microscope, the liver is built of many small, roughly hexagonal units. What is each unit called?","a":"A liver lobule.","options":["A portal triad","A liver lobe","A hepatic sinusoid","A liver lobule"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"A liver lobule is a small, roughly six-sided unit of plates of hepatocytes arranged around a central vein. At each corner of the lobule sits a portal triad. Thousands of lobules packed together make up each of the large lobes you see with the naked eye.","notes":["A portal triad is the bile duct, hepatic artery branch, and hepatic portal vein branch found at each corner of a lobule. It is part of the lobule, so it would be the answer for what sits at the corners.","A lobe is a large division of the whole liver, such as the right or left lobe, visible without a microscope. Pick it for the big named parts of the liver, not the tiny units.","A hepatic sinusoid is one of the leaky capillaries running between plates of hepatocytes inside the lobule. Pick it for the blood channels within the lobule.","Correct. The liver lobule is the small six-sided unit, with a central vein in the middle and a portal triad at each corner."],"lt":"t-accessory-digestive-organs","lid":"c8","comp":122,"competencyId":"w5-liver-gross-lobule"},{"id":"L02304","dok":1,"q":"Which duct carries pancreatic juice from the pancreas and joins the common bile duct?","a":"The main pancreatic duct.","options":["The common bile duct","The main pancreatic duct","The cystic duct","The common hepatic duct"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The main pancreatic duct, also called the duct of Wirsung, runs the length of the pancreas collecting pancreatic juice from the acini. Near the duodenum it joins the common bile duct at the hepatopancreatic ampulla, and the combined fluid enters the duodenum at the major duodenal papilla. This shared ending is why a stone at the ampulla can affect the pancreas.","notes":["The common bile duct is the duct that the pancreatic duct joins, but it carries bile, not pancreatic juice. It would be the answer for the duct formed by the common hepatic and cystic ducts.","Correct. The main pancreatic duct runs the length of the pancreas collecting juice and joins the common bile duct at the hepatopancreatic ampulla.","The cystic duct carries bile to and from the gallbladder. It would be the answer for the duct that connects the gallbladder to the bile duct system.","The common hepatic duct carries bile out of the liver after the right and left hepatic ducts merge. Pick it for the duct that the cystic duct joins."],"lt":"t-accessory-digestive-organs","lid":"c9","comp":124,"competencyId":"w5-pancreas"},{"id":"L02307","dok":1,"q":"Which fold of peritoneum attaches the liver to the anterior abdominal wall and diaphragm?","a":"The falciform ligament.","options":["The greater omentum","The falciform ligament","The lesser omentum","The mesentery"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The falciform ligament is a thin, sickle-shaped fold of peritoneum (falx means sickle) that suspends the liver from the anterior abdominal wall and the diaphragm. On the anterior surface it also marks the boundary between the right and left lobes. Its free lower edge carries the ligamentum teres, the remnant of the fetal umbilical vein.","notes":["The greater omentum is the fatty apron that hangs from the stomach over the intestines. It does not attach the liver to the body wall, so pick it for the largest peritoneal fold.","Correct. The falciform ligament is the sickle-shaped fold that holds the liver to the diaphragm and the anterior abdominal wall.","The lesser omentum connects the liver to the stomach and duodenum and carries vessels to the liver. Pick it for the fold between the liver and the stomach, not the body wall.","The mesentery is the fan-shaped fold binding the jejunum and ileum to the posterior abdominal wall. It would be the answer for the fold that holds the small intestine."],"lt":"t-accessory-digestive-organs","lid":"c12","comp":122,"competencyId":"w5-liver-gross-lobule"},{"id":"L02308","dok":2,"q":"A single large duct from each side opens on a small bump right beside the lingual frenulum, under the tongue. Which gland does it drain?","a":"The submandibular gland.","options":["The submandibular gland","A minor labial gland","The sublingual gland","The parotid gland"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The submandibular glands lie along the inner side of the mandible, and each has one main duct that runs forward under the tongue to open near the lingual frenulum, the midline fold that anchors the tongue. The sublingual glands, by contrast, drain through several small ducts spread along the floor of the mouth. One large duct beside the frenulum points to the submandibular gland.","notes":["Correct. Each submandibular gland has one main duct that opens on a small bump beside the lingual frenulum.","Minor labial glands are small salivary glands in the lining of the lips with tiny openings of their own. Pick them for the small glands scattered in the lip, not a large paired duct.","The sublingual gland drains through several small ducts spread along the floor of the mouth rather than one large duct beside the frenulum. It would be the answer if the question described many small openings under the tongue.","The parotid duct opens high in the cheek, beside the upper second molar. Pick the parotid if the duct opening were inside the cheek rather than under the tongue."],"lt":"t-accessory-digestive-organs","lid":"c13","comp":121,"competencyId":"w5-salivary-glands"},{"id":"L02309","dok":1,"q":"Where does the parotid duct open into the mouth?","a":"Beside the upper second molar.","options":["On the soft palate near the uvula","Beside the upper second molar","Along the floor of the mouth","Beside the lingual frenulum"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The parotid gland sits in front of and below the ear, outside the mouth. Its duct runs forward across the masseter muscle, turns inward through the cheek, and opens on the inside of the cheek beside the upper second molar. You can feel the small bump of this opening with your tongue.","notes":["No large salivary duct opens on the soft palate. Only small minor palatal glands open there, so this would fit a question about minor salivary glands.","Correct. The parotid duct crosses the masseter, passes through the buccinator, and opens inside the cheek beside the upper second molar.","The floor of the mouth is where the sublingual glands drain through several small ducts. It would be the answer for the sublingual glands.","The openings beside the lingual frenulum belong to the submandibular ducts. Pick this location for the submandibular glands."],"lt":"t-accessory-digestive-organs","lid":"c14","comp":121,"competencyId":"w5-salivary-glands"},{"id":"L02310","dok":2,"q":"Why are the teeth, tongue, salivary glands, liver, gallbladder, and pancreas called accessory organs rather than parts of the alimentary canal?","a":"They are not part of the tube that food travels through, though they help digestion.","options":["None of them makes contact with food at any point","They all release hormones into the blood instead of secretions into ducts","They are not part of the tube that food travels through, though they help digestion","They all lie outside the abdominopelvic cavity"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The alimentary canal is the continuous tube from the mouth to the anus that food actually travels through. The accessory organs help digestion without being part of that tube. The teeth and tongue work on food in the mouth, and the salivary glands, liver, and pancreas add secretions to the canal through ducts, with the gallbladder storing bile along the way.","notes":["The teeth and tongue make direct contact with food in the mouth, so this is false. Students pick it by focusing only on the glands, which add secretions without touching the food directly.","Most accessory organs release their products through ducts, which makes them exocrine. Only the pancreatic islets release hormones into the blood, so this describes endocrine tissue, not the accessory organs as a group.","Correct. Food never passes through these organs; they help digestion from beside the tube.","The liver, gallbladder, and pancreas all sit inside the abdominopelvic cavity. Only the teeth, tongue, and salivary glands lie in the head, so location does not separate the two groups."],"lt":"t-accessory-digestive-organs","lid":"c15","comp":109,"competencyId":"w5-canal-wall"},{"id":"L02311","dok":2,"q":"The liver, salivary glands, and pancreas release their digestive secretions through ducts onto the lining of the digestive tract. This makes them which kind of gland?","a":"Exocrine glands.","options":["Lymphatic organs","Exocrine glands","Endocrine glands","Storage organs"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Exocrine glands (exo means outward) release their products through ducts onto a surface, such as the lining of the mouth or the duodenum. Saliva, bile, and pancreatic juice all reach the digestive tract this way. Endocrine glands, by contrast, have no ducts and release hormones into the blood.","notes":["Lymphatic organs, such as lymph nodes, the spleen, and the thymus, house immune cells and filter lymph or blood. They would fit a question about immune defense, not digestive secretions.","Correct. Exocrine glands release their products through ducts onto a surface, which is how saliva, bile, and pancreatic juice reach the tract.","Endocrine glands release hormones directly into the blood without ducts. Pick this term for the pancreatic islets or the thyroid, not for glands that empty digestive juices through ducts.","A storage organ holds a secretion made somewhere else, which describes the gallbladder. The liver, salivary glands, and pancreas make their own secretions, so they are glands, not storage organs."],"lt":"t-accessory-digestive-organs","lid":"c16","comp":109,"competencyId":"w5-canal-wall"},{"id":"L02313","dok":1,"q":"The right and left hepatic ducts merge to form which single duct?","a":"The common hepatic duct.","options":["The common bile duct","The common hepatic duct","The main pancreatic duct","The cystic duct"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The right and left hepatic ducts drain bile from the two halves of the liver and merge just outside the liver into the common hepatic duct. The common hepatic duct is then joined by the cystic duct from the gallbladder, and only below that junction is the duct called the common bile duct. Follow the names in order: hepatic, then common hepatic, then common bile.","notes":["The common bile duct forms one step later, where the common hepatic duct meets the cystic duct. Pick it for the duct formed by those two, not by the hepatic ducts alone.","Correct. The two hepatic ducts merge just outside the liver into the single common hepatic duct.","The main pancreatic duct drains pancreatic juice and has nothing to do with the hepatic ducts until it meets the common bile duct at the ampulla. Pick it for the duct of Wirsung.","The cystic duct connects the gallbladder to the duct system. It would be the answer for the duct that carries bile to and from the gallbladder."],"lt":"t-accessory-digestive-organs","lid":"c18","comp":123,"competencyId":"w5-bile-path"},{"id":"L02314","dok":1,"q":"The pancreatic islets are also known by which name?","a":"The islets of Langerhans.","options":["The acini","The islets of Langerhans","The hepatic laminae","The portal triads"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The pancreatic islets, also called the islets of Langerhans, are small clusters of endocrine cells scattered among the much more numerous acini. They make up only about 1 percent of the pancreas and release hormones into the blood. The name islets fits because they look like little islands surrounded by a sea of acini.","notes":["The acini are the exocrine clusters of the pancreas that make pancreatic juice, about 99 percent of the gland. They are the sea around the islets, not another name for them.","Correct. The pancreatic islets are the islets of Langerhans.","Hepatic laminae are the plates of hepatocytes in the liver. They would be the answer for how liver cells are arranged, not for pancreatic tissue.","Portal triads are the bile duct, hepatic artery branch, and portal vein branch at the corners of liver lobules. Pick them for a question about liver lobule structure."],"lt":"t-accessory-digestive-organs","lid":"c19","comp":124,"competencyId":"w5-pancreas"},{"id":"L02315","dok":1,"q":"Which structure on the underside of the liver serves as the gateway where vessels and ducts enter and leave the liver?","a":"The porta hepatis.","options":["The renal hilum","The falciform ligament","The major duodenal papilla","The porta hepatis"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Porta means door, and the porta hepatis is the doorway on the inferior surface of the liver. The hepatic portal vein and hepatic artery enter here, and the hepatic ducts carry bile out. Many organs have a similar gateway, but each has its own name.","notes":["The renal hilum is the indented gateway on the medial side of the kidney where the renal vessels and ureter enter and leave. It would be the answer for the kidney's equivalent of the porta hepatis.","The falciform ligament is a fold of peritoneum on the anterior surface that attaches the liver to the abdominal wall and diaphragm. Pick it for the ligament between the right and left lobes.","The major duodenal papilla is the raised opening in the duodenum where bile and pancreatic juice enter. It is a gateway into the intestine, not into the liver.","Correct. Porta means door, and the porta hepatis is where the portal vein and hepatic artery enter and the hepatic ducts leave."],"lt":"t-accessory-digestive-organs","lid":"c20","comp":122,"competencyId":"w5-liver-gross-lobule"},{"id":"L02316","dok":1,"q":"The pancreas is called a mixed gland. Which pair of tissues makes it mixed?","a":"Exocrine acini that make digestive juice and endocrine islets that make hormones.","options":["Endocrine islets that make hormones and a sac that stores bile","Lymphatic follicles that trap microbes and acini that make digestive juice","Exocrine acini that make digestive juice and endocrine islets that make hormones","Hepatocytes that make bile and sinusoids that carry blood"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The pancreas does two jobs with two kinds of tissue. About 99 percent of it is exocrine acini, which make pancreatic juice and send it through ducts to the duodenum. The other 1 percent is endocrine pancreatic islets, which release hormones directly into the blood, and having both kinds is what makes it a mixed gland.","notes":["The pancreas does have endocrine islets, but the sac that stores bile is the gallbladder, a separate organ. This pair mixes the pancreas with the gallbladder.","Lymphatic follicles are clusters of immune cells found in places like the appendix and tonsils, not a main tissue of the pancreas. Only the acini part of this pair belongs to the pancreas.","Correct. Exocrine acini plus endocrine islets make the pancreas a mixed gland.","Hepatocytes and sinusoids are the cells and blood channels of the liver. Pick this pair for a question about liver histology."],"lt":"t-accessory-digestive-organs","lid":"c21","comp":124,"competencyId":"w5-pancreas"},{"id":"L02317","dok":1,"q":"Which two lobes make up most of the liver?","a":"The right and left lobes.","options":["The caudate and quadrate lobes","The right and left lobes","The anterior and posterior lobes","The superior and inferior lobes"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The right and left lobes are the two principal lobes of the liver, and the right lobe is the larger of the two. The falciform ligament marks the boundary between them on the anterior surface. The caudate and quadrate lobes are two much smaller lobes seen only on the posterior and inferior surface.","notes":["The caudate and quadrate lobes are real liver lobes, but they are the two small ones on the posterior and inferior surface. Pick them if the question asked for the small lobes.","Correct. The right and left lobes are the two large lobes seen from the front, and the right lobe is the largest.","Anterior and posterior lobes describe the pituitary gland, not the liver. Pick this pair for a question about the two parts of the pituitary.","Superior and inferior lobes are lobes of the lungs, such as the two lobes of the left lung. They would be the answer for a lung question, not the liver."],"lt":"t-accessory-digestive-organs","lid":"c22","comp":122,"competencyId":"w5-liver-gross-lobule"},{"id":"L02318","dok":1,"q":"The head, body, and tail are the three regions of which accessory organ?","a":"The pancreas.","options":["The liver","The parotid gland","The pancreas","The gallbladder"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The pancreas is divided into a head, body, and tail. The wide head sits in the C-shaped curve of the duodenum, the body crosses behind the stomach, and the tapered tail reaches toward the spleen. Picture it lying sideways across the upper abdomen with its head on the right.","notes":["The liver is divided into lobes (right, left, quadrate, and caudate), not a head, body, and tail. Pick it for a question about lobes.","The parotid gland is a salivary gland in front of the ear and is not divided into named regions in this course. It would be the answer for the largest salivary gland.","Correct. The pancreas has a head, body, and tail.","The gallbladder has a fundus, body, and neck. The shared word body makes this an easy mix-up, so remember that the gallbladder ends in a neck rather than a head and tail."],"lt":"t-accessory-digestive-organs","lid":"c23","comp":124,"competencyId":"w5-pancreas"},{"id":"L02320","dok":1,"q":"Mumps is a viral infection best known for swelling which glands?","a":"The parotid glands.","options":["The palatine tonsils","The gallbladder","The liver","The parotid glands"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Mumps is a viral infection that inflames and enlarges the parotid glands, the largest salivary glands, which sit in front of and below each ear. Because the parotids lie over the angle of the jaw, the swelling gives the face a puffy look below the ears. Each accessory organ disorder is easiest to remember by the structure it affects.","notes":["The palatine tonsils are lymphatic tissue in the throat, and their swelling is tonsillitis. Students pick them because both conditions cause swelling near the jaw, but tonsils sit inside the throat, not in front of the ear.","Mumps does not affect the gallbladder. Inflammation of the gallbladder is cholecystitis, often from a blocking gallstone.","Mumps does not affect the liver. Inflammation of the liver, usually from a virus, is hepatitis, so pick the liver for hepatitis or cirrhosis.","Correct. The mumps virus infects the parotid glands, so the face swells in front of and below the ears."],"lt":"t-accessory-digestive-organs","lid":"c25","comp":125,"competencyId":"w5-accessory-disorders"},{"id":"L02322","dok":2,"q":"Between meals, bile flows into the gallbladder for storage, and during a meal it flows back out the same way. Which duct carries bile both into and out of the gallbladder?","a":"The cystic duct.","options":["The common hepatic duct","The cystic duct","The common bile duct","The main pancreatic duct"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The gallbladder has only one duct, the cystic duct, which joins the common hepatic duct to form the common bile duct. When the sphincter of Oddi is closed between meals, bile backs up the cystic duct into the gallbladder for storage. When a fatty meal arrives, the gallbladder contracts and sends bile back down the same cystic duct into the common bile duct.","notes":["The common hepatic duct carries bile one way only, out of the liver toward the junction with the cystic duct. Pick it for the duct formed by the right and left hepatic ducts.","Correct. The cystic duct carries bile into and out of the gallbladder.","Bile reaches the gallbladder from the common hepatic duct by way of the cystic duct. The common bile duct carries bile on down to the duodenum, so it is not the gallbladder's way in and out.","The main pancreatic duct carries pancreatic juice, not bile, from the pancreas to the ampulla. Pick it for the duct of Wirsung."],"lt":"t-accessory-digestive-organs","lid":"c27","comp":123,"competencyId":"w5-bile-path"},{"id":"L02323","dok":1,"q":"Which duct forms where the common hepatic duct and the cystic duct join?","a":"The common bile duct.","options":["The main pancreatic duct","The common bile duct","The right hepatic duct","The hepatopancreatic ampulla"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The common hepatic duct brings bile down from the liver, and the cystic duct connects to the gallbladder. Where these two meet, the single duct that continues downward is the common bile duct. It runs behind the duodenum and through the head of the pancreas to meet the main pancreatic duct.","notes":["The main pancreatic duct drains the pancreas and meets the common bile duct farther down. It would be the answer for the duct of Wirsung.","Correct. The common hepatic and cystic ducts form the common bile duct.","The right hepatic duct drains bile from the right side of the liver and lies upstream of this junction. Pick it for one of the two ducts that form the common hepatic duct.","The hepatopancreatic ampulla is the short widened passage where the common bile duct and pancreatic duct meet, downstream of this junction. Pick it for where bile and pancreatic juice combine."],"lt":"t-accessory-digestive-organs","lid":"c28","comp":123,"competencyId":"w5-bile-path"},{"id":"L02324","dok":1,"q":"What is the short, widened passage where the common bile duct and the main pancreatic duct meet just before the duodenum?","a":"The hepatopancreatic ampulla.","options":["The cystic duct","The major duodenal papilla","The porta hepatis","The hepatopancreatic ampulla"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"An ampulla is a widened section of a duct. The hepatopancreatic ampulla gets its name from the two ducts it joins, hepato for the bile duct from the liver and pancreatic for the pancreatic duct. It empties into the duodenum through the major duodenal papilla, with the sphincter of Oddi controlling the flow.","notes":["The cystic duct connects the gallbladder to the bile duct system, far upstream of the duodenum. It would be the answer for the duct that carries bile to and from the gallbladder.","The major duodenal papilla is the raised opening on the duodenal lining where the ampulla empties. It is the exit of the ampulla, so pick it for the opening into the duodenum.","The porta hepatis is the gateway on the underside of the liver where vessels enter and the hepatic ducts leave. Pick it for the doorway into the liver.","Correct. The hepatopancreatic ampulla is the short widened chamber in the duodenal wall where the common bile duct and main pancreatic duct meet."],"lt":"t-accessory-digestive-organs","lid":"c29","comp":123,"competencyId":"w5-bile-path"},{"id":"L02325","dok":1,"q":"An accessory organ is a soft, tapering gland whose head sits in the curve of the duodenum and whose tail reaches toward the spleen. Which organ is it?","a":"The pancreas.","options":["The gallbladder","The spleen","The pancreas","The liver"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The pancreas lies behind the stomach in a retroperitoneal position. Its wide head nestles in the C-shaped curve of the duodenum, its body crosses the posterior abdominal wall, and its tapered tail ends near the spleen. Those landmarks at each end are the quickest way to recognize it on a model.","notes":["The gallbladder is a small pear-shaped sac on the posterior surface of the liver, with a fundus, body, and neck. It would be the answer for the organ that stores bile.","The spleen is the lymphatic organ the tail of the pancreas points toward, not the organ described. Pick it for the organ in the left upper quadrant that filters blood.","Correct. The pancreas has its head in the duodenal curve and its tail by the spleen.","The liver sits in the right upper quadrant under the diaphragm and is divided into lobes, not a head and tail. Pick it for the largest gland in the body."],"lt":"t-accessory-digestive-organs","lid":"c30","comp":124,"competencyId":"w5-pancreas"},{"id":"L02326","dok":1,"q":"A patient has inflammation of the liver caused by a viral infection. Which disorder is this?","a":"Hepatitis.","options":["Hepatitis","Cholecystitis","Cirrhosis","Pancreatitis"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The suffix itis means inflammation, and hepat refers to the liver, so hepatitis is inflammation of the liver. It is most commonly caused by a viral infection. Reading the root of each disorder name tells you which accessory organ is affected.","notes":["Correct. Hepat means liver and itis means inflammation, so viral inflammation of the liver is hepatitis.","Cholecystitis is inflammation of the gallbladder (chole means bile, cyst means bladder). It would be the answer if the gallbladder were inflamed.","Cirrhosis is the replacement of healthy liver tissue with scar tissue after long-term damage, not an inflammation. Pick it for scarring with loss of liver function.","Pancreatitis is inflammation of the pancreas. Pick it if the inflamed organ were the pancreas, for example after a gallstone blocks the ampulla."],"lt":"t-accessory-digestive-organs","lid":"c31","comp":125,"competencyId":"w5-accessory-disorders"},{"id":"L02327","dok":1,"q":"Which disorder replaces healthy liver tissue with scar tissue, with a loss of liver function?","a":"Cirrhosis.","options":["Hepatitis","Cirrhosis","Jaundice","Cholecystitis"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Cirrhosis develops after long-term damage to the liver. Healthy hepatocytes are gradually replaced by scar tissue, which hardens the liver and leaves fewer working cells, so liver function is lost. It differs from hepatitis, which is inflammation, though repeated inflammation can eventually lead to cirrhosis.","notes":["Hepatitis is inflammation of the liver, commonly viral. Pick it for an inflamed liver rather than a scarred one.","Correct. Cirrhosis is scarring of the liver with loss of function.","Jaundice is yellowing of the skin and eyes from a buildup of bilirubin. It is a sign that can accompany liver disease, so pick it for the yellow color, not the scarring.","Cholecystitis is inflammation of the gallbladder, often from a blocking gallstone. It would be the answer for an inflamed gallbladder."],"lt":"t-accessory-digestive-organs","lid":"c32","comp":125,"competencyId":"w5-accessory-disorders"},{"id":"L02328","dok":1,"q":"Which salivary glands lie in the floor of the mouth along the inner side of the mandible?","a":"The submandibular glands.","options":["The parotid glands","The buccal glands","The sublingual glands","The submandibular glands"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Sub means below and mandibular refers to the mandible, so the submandibular glands sit just below and inside the body of the mandible, in the floor of the mouth. Their ducts run forward to open near the lingual frenulum. You can feel them as soft lumps under the jaw on each side.","notes":["The parotid glands sit in front of and below the ears, outside the mouth over the masseter. Pick them for the largest salivary gland.","The buccal glands are minor salivary glands scattered in the lining of the cheek. Pick them for the small glands of the cheek wall.","The sublingual glands lie beneath the tongue, just above the submandibular glands. They would be the answer for the glands directly under the tongue.","Correct. The submandibular glands lie along the inner mandible."],"lt":"t-accessory-digestive-organs","lid":"c33","comp":121,"competencyId":"w5-salivary-glands"},{"id":"L02330","dok":1,"q":"Which disorder is inflammation of the gallbladder, often caused by a blocking gallstone?","a":"Cholecystitis.","options":["Hepatitis","Cholecystitis","Cirrhosis","Pancreatitis"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The name breaks down into chole (bile), cyst (bladder or sac), and itis (inflammation), so cholecystitis is inflammation of the bile sac, the gallbladder. A gallstone blocking the cystic duct traps bile inside the gallbladder, which often triggers the inflammation. Pain is felt in the right upper quadrant.","notes":["Hepatitis is inflammation of the liver, commonly viral. It would be the answer for an inflamed liver, not the gallbladder.","Correct. Cholecystitis is inflammation of the gallbladder.","Cirrhosis is scarring of the liver with loss of function after long-term damage. Pick it for a scarred, hardened liver.","Pancreatitis is inflammation of the pancreas. A gallstone can cause it too, but only when the stone blocks the shared ampulla, so pick it when the pancreas is the inflamed organ."],"lt":"t-accessory-digestive-organs","lid":"c35","comp":125,"competencyId":"w5-accessory-disorders"},{"id":"L02331","dok":1,"q":"Which exocrine clusters of the pancreas produce pancreatic juice rich in digestive enzymes?","a":"The acini.","options":["The pancreatic islets","The hepatocytes","The liver lobules","The acini"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Acini (singular acinus, from the Latin for grape) are berry-like clusters of secretory cells that make up about 99 percent of the pancreas. They produce pancreatic juice, which drains through small ducts into the pancreatic ducts and on to the duodenum. Because they secrete through ducts, they are the exocrine part of the gland.","notes":["The pancreatic islets are the endocrine clusters that release hormones into the blood, about 1 percent of the gland. Pick them for the hormone-producing part of the pancreas.","Hepatocytes are the functional cells of the liver, which make bile. They would be the answer for the liver's main cell type.","Liver lobules are the small hexagonal units of the liver. Pick them for the building blocks of the liver, not the pancreas.","Correct. The acini make pancreatic juice."],"lt":"t-accessory-digestive-organs","lid":"c36","comp":124,"competencyId":"w5-pancreas"},{"id":"L02332","dok":1,"q":"Hardened deposits that form from bile and can lodge in and block the bile ducts are called what?","a":"Gallstones.","options":["Jaundice","Gallstones","Cholecystitis","Kidney stones"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Gallstones are hardened deposits that form in the gallbladder from components of bile. A stone can slip into the cystic duct, the common bile duct, or the hepatopancreatic ampulla and block the flow of bile. That blockage is behind several other disorders, including cholecystitis, jaundice, and pancreatitis.","notes":["Jaundice is yellowing of the skin and eyes from a buildup of bilirubin, which can follow a blocked bile duct. It is a sign, not a deposit.","Correct. Gallstones are hardened bile deposits.","Cholecystitis is inflammation of the gallbladder, which a gallstone often causes. It is the result of the blockage, not the deposit itself.","Kidney stones are hardened deposits that form in the urinary system and can block a ureter. Pick them for a stone that blocks the flow of urine, not bile."],"lt":"t-accessory-digestive-organs","lid":"c37","comp":125,"competencyId":"w5-accessory-disorders"},{"id":"L02333","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares the roles of the liver and the gallbladder in handling bile?","a":"The liver makes bile, and the gallbladder stores and concentrates it.","options":["The liver makes bile, and the gallbladder stores and concentrates it","Both organs make bile, but only the gallbladder releases it into the duodenum","The liver makes bile, and the gallbladder adds digestive enzymes to it","The gallbladder makes bile, and the liver stores and concentrates it"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Hepatocytes in the liver produce bile continuously. Between meals, much of that bile backs up the cystic duct into the gallbladder, which stores it and concentrates it by removing water. When a fatty meal arrives, the gallbladder contracts and sends a concentrated supply down to the duodenum.","notes":["Correct. The liver produces bile and the gallbladder stores and concentrates it.","Only the liver makes bile, and bile reaches the duodenum whether it comes straight from the liver or from storage. Students choose this when they assume any organ holding bile must also produce it.","Bile contains no digestive enzymes, and the gallbladder adds none. Digestive enzymes come from the pancreas, so pick the pancreas for the organ that supplies enzymes to the duodenum.","This reverses the two roles. The liver is the gland that makes bile, and the gallbladder is the storage sac, which is why the notes classify the gallbladder as a storage organ."],"lt":"t-accessory-digestive-organs","lid":"c38","comp":123,"competencyId":"w5-bile-path"},{"id":"L02334","dok":1,"q":"Which salivary glands sit in the floor of the mouth beneath the tongue and drain through several small ducts?","a":"The sublingual glands.","options":["The buccal glands","The submandibular glands","The sublingual glands","The parotid glands"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Sub means below and lingual means tongue, so the sublingual glands sit directly beneath the tongue, above the submandibular glands. Unlike the parotid and submandibular glands, which each drain through one main duct, the sublingual glands drain through several small ducts that open onto the floor of the mouth. They are the smallest of the three large pairs.","notes":["The buccal glands are minor salivary glands in the lining of the cheek. Pick them for the small glands of the cheek wall.","The submandibular glands lie along the inner side of the mandible, below the sublingual glands, and each drains through one main duct near the lingual frenulum. They would be the answer for a single duct beside the frenulum.","Correct. The sublingual glands lie beneath the tongue and use several small ducts.","The parotid glands sit in front of and below the ears and drain through one duct that opens beside the upper second molar. Pick them for the largest salivary gland."],"lt":"t-accessory-digestive-organs","lid":"c39","comp":121,"competencyId":"w5-salivary-glands"},{"id":"L02335","dok":2,"q":"Which sequence traces bile from the liver to the gallbladder for storage between meals?","a":"Liver, hepatic ducts, common hepatic duct, cystic duct, gallbladder.","options":["Liver, hepatic ducts, common hepatic duct, cystic duct, gallbladder","Liver, hepatic ducts, common bile duct, cystic duct, gallbladder","Liver, hepatic ducts, main pancreatic duct, cystic duct, gallbladder","Liver, common bile duct, hepatic ducts, gallbladder"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Bile leaves the liver through the right and left hepatic ducts, which merge into the common hepatic duct. When the sphincter of Oddi is closed between meals, bile cannot enter the duodenum, so it backs up and flows through the cystic duct into the gallbladder. The cystic duct branches off where the common hepatic duct becomes the common bile duct, so bile turns up into the gallbladder at that junction.","notes":["Correct. Bile leaves through the hepatic ducts and the common hepatic duct, then turns up the cystic duct into the gallbladder.","This route skips the common hepatic duct, where the right and left hepatic ducts merge. Bile cannot reach the cystic duct without passing through the common hepatic duct first.","The main pancreatic duct carries pancreatic juice from the pancreas, not bile from the liver, and it does not connect to the cystic duct. Pick it for the duct that joins the common bile duct at the ampulla.","The hepatic ducts come first, right at the liver, and the common bile duct forms later. This sequence puts the downstream duct before the upstream ones."],"lt":"t-accessory-digestive-organs","lid":"c40","comp":123,"competencyId":"w5-bile-path"},{"id":"L02336","dok":2,"q":"During a fatty meal, which sequence traces bile from the gallbladder to the duodenum?","a":"Gallbladder, cystic duct, common bile duct, hepatopancreatic ampulla, duodenum.","options":["Gallbladder, cystic duct, common bile duct, hepatopancreatic ampulla, duodenum","Gallbladder, cystic duct, main pancreatic duct, hepatopancreatic ampulla, duodenum","Gallbladder, common hepatic duct, common bile duct, hepatopancreatic ampulla, duodenum","Gallbladder, common bile duct, cystic duct, hepatopancreatic ampulla, duodenum"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The gallbladder contracts and pushes bile out through its only exit, the cystic duct. The cystic duct joins the common hepatic duct to form the common bile duct, which carries bile down to the hepatopancreatic ampulla. From the ampulla, bile passes the sphincter of Oddi and enters the duodenum at the major duodenal papilla.","notes":["Correct. Bile leaves through the cystic duct, runs down the common bile duct to the ampulla, and then enters the duodenum.","Bile does not travel through the main pancreatic duct. That duct carries pancreatic juice and meets the common bile duct only at the ampulla.","The common hepatic duct brings bile down from the liver and is upstream of the gallbladder's junction. Bile leaving the gallbladder never flows up into it, so pick it for the route out of the liver.","The cystic duct comes first because it is the gallbladder's own duct. The common bile duct only forms after the cystic duct joins the common hepatic duct."],"lt":"t-accessory-digestive-organs","lid":"c41","comp":123,"competencyId":"w5-bile-path"},{"id":"L02337","dok":3,"q":"A drop of bile is made in the liver, stored in the gallbladder overnight, and released into the duodenum at breakfast. Which duct does it pass through twice, once in each direction?","a":"The cystic duct.","options":["The common hepatic duct","The cystic duct","The main pancreatic duct","The common bile duct"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The full route is liver, hepatic ducts, common hepatic duct, then up the cystic duct into the gallbladder. At breakfast the gallbladder contracts, and the bile goes back down the same cystic duct, into the common bile duct, through the hepatopancreatic ampulla, and into the duodenum. The cystic duct is the gallbladder's only connection, so stored bile must use it both ways.","notes":["The common hepatic duct is used only once, on the way out of the liver. Pick it for the duct formed by the right and left hepatic ducts.","Correct. The cystic duct carries bile into the gallbladder and back out.","The main pancreatic duct carries pancreatic juice, and bile never travels through it. Pick it for the duct of Wirsung that joins the common bile duct.","The common bile duct carries the drop on its final trip down to the ampulla. The gallbladder's own two-way connection is the cystic duct, not the common bile duct."],"lt":"t-accessory-digestive-organs","lid":"c42","comp":123,"competencyId":"w5-bile-path"},{"id":"L02338","dok":3,"q":"A small gallstone lodges at the hepatopancreatic ampulla. Which two ducts can it block at the same time?","a":"The common bile duct and the main pancreatic duct.","options":["The right and left hepatic ducts","The common bile duct and the main pancreatic duct","The cystic duct and the common hepatic duct","The accessory pancreatic duct and the cystic duct"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The hepatopancreatic ampulla is where the common bile duct and the main pancreatic duct meet before opening into the duodenum. A stone stuck there blocks the shared passage, so neither bile nor pancreatic juice can drain. This shared anatomy is why a gallstone can cause pancreatitis as well as jaundice.","notes":["The right and left hepatic ducts are near the liver, far upstream of the ampulla. They would be the answer for the ducts that merge into the common hepatic duct.","Correct. The common bile duct and main pancreatic duct share the ampulla.","The cystic and common hepatic ducts meet higher up, at the start of the common bile duct, well above the ampulla. Pick this pair if the stone were stuck where those two ducts join.","The accessory pancreatic duct empties into the duodenum separately and does not pass through the ampulla, and the cystic duct is far upstream. A stone at the ampulla would leave both of these open."],"lt":"t-accessory-digestive-organs","lid":"c43","comp":125,"competencyId":"w5-accessory-disorders"},{"id":"L02339","dok":2,"q":"Which set correctly pairs the liver, the pancreas, and the gallbladder with the way each organ is subdivided?","a":"Liver into lobes, pancreas into head, body, and tail, gallbladder into fundus, body, and neck.","options":["Liver into cardia, body, and pylorus, pancreas into head, body, and tail, gallbladder into lobes","Liver into lobes, pancreas into fundus, body, and neck, gallbladder into head, body, and tail","Liver into lobes, pancreas into head, body, and tail, gallbladder into fundus, body, and neck","Liver into head, body, and tail, pancreas into lobes, gallbladder into fundus, body, and neck"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The liver is divided into lobes: the large right and left lobes plus the small quadrate and caudate lobes. The pancreas runs from a head in the curve of the duodenum through a body to a tail near the spleen. The gallbladder has a rounded fundus, a middle body, and a narrow neck that leads into the cystic duct.","notes":["Cardia, body, and pylorus are regions of the stomach, not the liver, and the gallbladder has no lobes. Only the pancreas is subdivided correctly in this set.","This swaps the pancreas and gallbladder. The gallbladder is the one with a fundus and neck, and the pancreas has the head and tail; only the liver is correct here.","Correct. Lobes for the liver, head, body, and tail for the pancreas, and fundus, body, and neck for the gallbladder.","This swaps the liver and pancreas. The pancreas is the organ with a head, body, and tail, while the liver is divided into lobes; only the gallbladder is correct here."],"lt":"t-accessory-digestive-organs","lid":"c44","comp":124,"competencyId":"w5-pancreas"},{"id":"L02340","dok":2,"q":"Which accessory organ lies in the right upper quadrant, drains its secretion through the right and left hepatic ducts, and has a storage sac attached to its posterior surface?","a":"The liver.","options":["The liver","The gallbladder","The duodenum","The pancreas"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Every clue points to the liver. It sits under the diaphragm mostly in the right upper quadrant, its bile drains out through the right and left hepatic ducts, and the gallbladder, which stores that bile, is attached to its posterior surface. Putting several clues together like this is how you confirm a structure on a model.","notes":["Correct. The liver sits mostly in the right upper quadrant, drains bile through the right and left hepatic ducts, and has the gallbladder on its underside.","The gallbladder is the storage sac in the question, not the organ it is attached to. It would be the answer for the organ that stores and concentrates bile.","The duodenum is the first part of the small intestine, part of the alimentary canal rather than an accessory organ. Pick it for where bile and pancreatic juice are delivered.","The pancreas lies behind the stomach and drains pancreatic juice through the pancreatic ducts, not the hepatic ducts. Pick it for the gland with a head in the curve of the duodenum."],"lt":"t-accessory-digestive-organs","lid":"c45","comp":122,"competencyId":"w5-liver-gross-lobule"},{"id":"L02341","dok":1,"q":"Which list names the six accessory digestive organs?","a":"The teeth, tongue, salivary glands, liver, gallbladder, and pancreas.","options":["Mouth, pharynx, esophagus, stomach, small intestine, and large intestine","Teeth, tongue, salivary glands, liver, gallbladder, and appendix","Teeth, tongue, salivary glands, liver, gallbladder, and pancreas","Liver, spleen, pancreas, stomach, duodenum, and colon"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The six accessory digestive organs are the teeth, tongue, salivary glands, liver, gallbladder, and pancreas. The teeth and tongue work on food directly in the mouth, the salivary glands, liver, and pancreas add secretions through ducts, and the gallbladder stores bile. None of them is part of the tube that food travels through.","notes":["This is the alimentary canal, the tube food actually passes through from mouth to anus. Pick this list for the organs of the digestive tract itself.","This list is close, but it swaps the pancreas for the appendix. The appendix hangs from the cecum and is part of the large intestine, while the pancreas is the accessory organ that belongs here.","Correct. These six help digestion without being part of the tube that food travels through.","This list mixes the spleen, a lymphatic organ, and canal organs such as the stomach, duodenum, and colon in with two accessory organs. It would not match either group cleanly."],"lt":"t-accessory-digestive-organs","lid":"c1-x2","comp":109,"competencyId":"w5-canal-wall"},{"id":"L02342","dok":2,"q":"Most accessory organs are glands that make a secretion. Which accessory organ is classified as a storage organ instead, because it holds a secretion made somewhere else?","a":"The gallbladder.","options":["The gallbladder","The liver","The pancreas","The parotid gland"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The glandular accessory organs, the salivary glands, liver, and pancreas, each produce their own secretion and deliver it through ducts. The gallbladder produces no secretion of its own. It stores bile made by the liver and concentrates it until a meal arrives, so the notes classify it as a storage organ.","notes":["Correct. The gallbladder stores bile made by the liver.","The liver is a gland that makes bile, the very secretion the storage organ holds. Pick it for the organ that produces bile.","The pancreas is a gland that makes its own pancreatic juice and hormones. It would be the answer for the accessory organ that is both exocrine and endocrine.","The parotid gland makes its own saliva and delivers it through the parotid duct. Pick it for the largest salivary gland."],"lt":"t-accessory-digestive-organs","lid":"c2-x2","comp":123,"competencyId":"w5-bile-path"},{"id":"L02344","dok":1,"q":"Which salivary glands lie anterior and inferior to the ears, between the skin and the masseter muscle?","a":"The parotid glands.","options":["The lingual glands","The parotid glands","The sublingual glands","The submandibular glands"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Para means beside and otic refers to the ear, so the parotid glands sit beside the ear, anterior and inferior to it, lying over the masseter muscle just under the skin. They are the largest salivary glands. Their ducts cross the masseter and open inside the cheek beside the upper second molar.","notes":["The lingual glands are small glands within the tongue that secrete mucus and watery fluid. Pick them for glands inside the tongue itself.","Correct. The parotid glands lie in front of and below the ears.","The sublingual glands lie beneath the tongue in the floor of the mouth. They would be the answer for the glands directly under the tongue.","The submandibular glands lie in the floor of the mouth along the inner side of the mandible. Pick them for the glands under the jaw whose ducts open near the frenulum."],"lt":"t-accessory-digestive-organs","lid":"c4-x2","comp":121,"competencyId":"w5-salivary-glands"},{"id":"L02345","dok":1,"q":"The sublingual glands sit beneath the tongue. Which other pair of salivary glands lies just below them?","a":"The submandibular glands.","options":["The lingual glands","The parotid glands","The buccal glands","The submandibular glands"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"In the floor of the mouth, the salivary glands are stacked. The sublingual glands lie just beneath the tongue, and the submandibular glands sit below them, along the inner side of the mandible. The parotid glands are separate, out by the ears.","notes":["The lingual glands are small glands within the tongue itself, above the floor of the mouth. Pick them for glands that secrete inside the tongue.","The parotid glands sit in front of and below the ears, outside the floor of the mouth. Pick them for the glands over the masseter muscle.","The buccal glands are minor salivary glands in the lining of the cheeks. They would be the answer for small glands scattered in the cheek wall.","Correct. The submandibular glands lie below the sublingual glands."],"lt":"t-accessory-digestive-organs","lid":"c5-x2","comp":121,"competencyId":"w5-salivary-glands"},{"id":"L02346","dok":1,"q":"Which digestive secretion does the liver produce?","a":"Bile.","options":["Insulin","Saliva","Pancreatic juice","Bile"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The liver is the largest gland in the body, and its digestive product is bile. Hepatocytes make bile and release it into the bile canaliculi, which drain into the hepatic ducts. The gallbladder stores the bile, but it is the liver that makes it.","notes":["Insulin is a hormone released by the pancreatic islets into the blood. Pick it for an endocrine product of the pancreas, not a product of the liver.","Saliva is made by the salivary glands, mainly the parotid, submandibular, and sublingual pairs. Pick saliva for a question about the glands that drain into the mouth.","Pancreatic juice is made by the acini of the pancreas and delivered to the duodenum. It would be the answer for the exocrine product of the pancreas.","Correct. The liver produces bile."],"lt":"t-accessory-digestive-organs","lid":"c6-x2","comp":122,"competencyId":"w5-liver-gross-lobule"},{"id":"L02347","dok":1,"q":"On the anterior surface of the liver, which structure marks the boundary between the right and left lobes?","a":"The falciform ligament.","options":["The mesentery","The greater omentum","The porta hepatis","The falciform ligament"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The falciform ligament is a thin fold of peritoneum that runs from the anterior abdominal wall and diaphragm onto the front of the liver. Where it attaches, it divides the large right lobe from the smaller left lobe. On a model, find the thin vertical sheet on the front of the liver and you have found the dividing line.","notes":["The mesentery is the fan-shaped fold that binds the jejunum and ileum to the posterior abdominal wall. It would be the answer for the fold that holds the small intestine.","The greater omentum is the fatty apron of peritoneum that hangs from the stomach over the intestines. Pick it for the largest fold of peritoneum.","The porta hepatis is the gateway on the inferior surface of the liver where vessels and ducts enter and leave. Pick it for the liver's doorway, not the line between the lobes on the front.","Correct. The falciform ligament separates the right and left lobes."],"lt":"t-accessory-digestive-organs","lid":"c7-x2","comp":122,"competencyId":"w5-liver-gross-lobule"},{"id":"L02348","dok":1,"q":"Which three structures make up the portal triad at each corner of a liver lobule?","a":"A bile duct, a branch of the hepatic artery, and a branch of the hepatic portal vein.","options":["The cystic duct, the common bile duct, and the main pancreatic duct","A central vein, a hepatic sinusoid, and a bile canaliculus","A bile duct, a hepatic artery branch, and a hepatic portal vein branch","Three small branches of the hepatic veins that drain the lobule"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Each liver lobule has a portal triad at its corners. The branch of the hepatic artery and the branch of the hepatic portal vein bring blood into the lobule, and the bile duct carries bile out. Traffic in the triad therefore runs both ways, with blood coming in and bile going out.","notes":["These are large ducts outside the liver that carry bile and pancreatic juice toward the duodenum. Pick them for the duct system below the liver, not microscopic lobule structures.","These are all real lobule structures, but they sit inside the lobule, not at its corners. The central vein is at the center, the sinusoids run between the plates, and the canaliculi lie between the cells.","Correct. A bile duct, a hepatic artery branch, and a hepatic portal vein branch.","The hepatic veins carry blood out of the liver to the inferior vena cava, and they begin from the central veins, not the corners of the lobule. Pick them for the vessels that drain the liver."],"lt":"t-accessory-digestive-organs","lid":"c8-x2","comp":122,"competencyId":"w5-liver-gross-lobule"},{"id":"L02349","dok":1,"q":"Where does the gallbladder sit?","a":"On the posterior surface of the liver.","options":["Within the curve of the duodenum","Posterior to the stomach","Beside the cecum in the lower right quadrant","On the posterior surface of the liver"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The gallbladder is a pear-shaped sac tucked against the posterior and inferior surface of the liver, in the right upper quadrant. Its broad fundus often peeks out below the lower edge of the liver. On a model, lift the liver's lower edge and look underneath to find it.","notes":["The curve of the duodenum holds the head of the pancreas. It would be the answer for the widest region of the pancreas.","The space posterior to the stomach holds the pancreas. Pick this location for the pancreas, not the gallbladder.","The lower right quadrant beside the cecum is where the appendix hangs. Pick this location for the appendix.","Correct. The gallbladder sits on the posterior surface of the liver."],"lt":"t-accessory-digestive-organs","lid":"c9-x2","comp":123,"competencyId":"w5-bile-path"},{"id":"L02350","dok":1,"q":"What are the three regions of the gallbladder?","a":"The fundus, body, and neck.","options":["Cortex, medulla, and pelvis","Cardia, body, and pylorus","Fundus, body, and neck","Head, body, and tail"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The gallbladder has a fundus, a body, and a neck. The fundus is the broad, rounded inferior end, the body is the middle, and the neck is the tapered superior end that continues as the cystic duct. Picturing a pear with its narrow end pointing up toward the liver helps you place each region.","notes":["Cortex, medulla, and pelvis are regions of the kidney. Pick this set for a kidney cut in a frontal section.","Cardia, body, and pylorus are regions of the stomach. This set would be the answer for the stomach, which also has its own fundus.","Correct. The gallbladder has a fundus, body, and neck.","Head, body, and tail are the regions of the pancreas. Pick this set for the gland whose head sits in the curve of the duodenum."],"lt":"t-accessory-digestive-organs","lid":"c10-x2","comp":123,"competencyId":"w5-bile-path"},{"id":"L02351","dok":1,"q":"Which two ducts join to form the common bile duct?","a":"The common hepatic duct and the cystic duct.","options":["The common hepatic duct and the cystic duct","The right and left hepatic ducts","The cystic duct and the main pancreatic duct","The main and accessory pancreatic ducts"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The common hepatic duct carries bile down from the liver, and the cystic duct connects the gallbladder. Where these two meet, the duct continues as the common bile duct, which carries bile from both sources down toward the duodenum. The pancreatic duct joins later, at the hepatopancreatic ampulla.","notes":["Correct. The common hepatic and cystic ducts form the common bile duct.","The right and left hepatic ducts join to form the common hepatic duct, one step earlier. Pick this pair for the formation of the common hepatic duct.","The main pancreatic duct does meet the bile system, but it joins the common bile duct at the ampulla, not the cystic duct. This pair mixes two different junctions.","The main and accessory pancreatic ducts both drain the pancreas, and they do not join to form any bile duct. Pick them for a question about how the pancreas drains."],"lt":"t-accessory-digestive-organs","lid":"c11-x2","comp":123,"competencyId":"w5-bile-path"},{"id":"L02352","dok":1,"q":"The hepatopancreatic ampulla is also known by which name?","a":"The ampulla of Vater.","options":["The ampulla of Vater","The duct of Santorini","The duct of Wirsung","The sphincter of Oddi"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The hepatopancreatic ampulla, also called the ampulla of Vater, is the short widened passage where the common bile duct and the main pancreatic duct meet before emptying into the duodenum. Several structures in this area carry a person's name, so it helps to pair each name with its plain anatomical term. The ampulla is the meeting chamber, and the other names belong to the ducts and the sphincter around it.","notes":["Correct. The hepatopancreatic ampulla is the ampulla of Vater.","The duct of Santorini is the accessory pancreatic duct, which empties into the duodenum separately. It would be the answer for the smaller pancreatic duct.","The duct of Wirsung is the main pancreatic duct, one of the two ducts that meet in the ampulla. Pick it for the main pancreatic duct.","The sphincter of Oddi is the ring of muscle that controls flow from the ampulla into the duodenum. Pick it for the muscle at the exit, not the chamber itself."],"lt":"t-accessory-digestive-organs","lid":"c12-x2","comp":123,"competencyId":"w5-bile-path"},{"id":"L02353","dok":1,"q":"What is another name for the main pancreatic duct?","a":"The duct of Wirsung.","options":["The duct of Wirsung","The duct of Santorini","The sphincter of Oddi","The ampulla of Vater"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The main pancreatic duct is also called the duct of Wirsung. It runs through the length of the pancreas and joins the common bile duct at the hepatopancreatic ampulla. One way to keep the names straight is that only the main duct, Wirsung, joins the bile duct, while Santorini empties on its own.","notes":["Correct. The main pancreatic duct is the duct of Wirsung.","The duct of Santorini is the smaller accessory pancreatic duct, which empties separately into the duodenum. Pick it for the accessory duct.","The sphincter of Oddi is the muscular ring controlling flow from the ampulla into the duodenum. It would be the answer for a question about what regulates bile and pancreatic juice entering the duodenum.","The ampulla of Vater is the hepatopancreatic ampulla, where the main pancreatic duct and common bile duct meet. Pick it for the shared chamber, not the duct itself."],"lt":"t-accessory-digestive-organs","lid":"c13-x2","comp":124,"competencyId":"w5-pancreas"},{"id":"L02354","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly describes how the pancreas is divided between its two kinds of tissue?","a":"About 99 percent is exocrine acini and about 1 percent is endocrine islets.","options":["The head is made only of acini, and the tail is made only of islets","The tissue is split evenly, with half acini and half islets","About 99 percent is exocrine acini, and about 1 percent is endocrine islets","About 99 percent is endocrine islets, and about 1 percent is exocrine acini"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Nearly all of the pancreas, about 99 percent, is made of acini, the exocrine clusters that produce pancreatic juice. The pancreatic islets, the endocrine clusters, make up only about 1 percent and are scattered among the acini like small islands. So although the pancreas is famous for its hormones, most of its tissue is devoted to making digestive juice.","notes":["The islets are scattered among the acini through the gland rather than collected into one region. Head, body, and tail are regions of location, and acini are found in all of them.","The two tissues are far from equal. Students pick this when they know the pancreas has two jobs and assume each gets half the tissue.","Correct. The acini make up about 99 percent of the gland, and the islets are small scattered patches making up about 1 percent.","This reverses the proportions. The islets are the small scattered islands, about 1 percent, and the acini form the bulk of the gland."],"lt":"t-accessory-digestive-organs","lid":"c14-x2","comp":124,"competencyId":"w5-pancreas"},{"id":"L02355","dok":1,"q":"What does the sphincter of Oddi control?","a":"Flow through the hepatopancreatic ampulla into the duodenum.","options":["Flow from the stomach into the duodenum","Flow from the esophagus into the stomach","Flow from the ileum into the cecum","Flow from the ampulla into the duodenum"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The sphincter of Oddi is a muscular ring around the hepatopancreatic ampulla where it opens at the major duodenal papilla. It controls when bile and pancreatic juice enter the duodenum. When it stays closed between meals, bile backs up the cystic duct into the gallbladder for storage.","notes":["Flow from the stomach into the duodenum is controlled by the pyloric sphincter. It would be the answer for the valve at the end of the stomach.","Flow from the esophagus into the stomach is controlled by the lower esophageal sphincter, also called the cardiac sphincter. Pick it for a question about heartburn and reflux.","Flow from the ileum into the cecum is controlled by the ileocecal sphincter, also called the ileocecal valve. Pick it for the junction of the small and large intestines.","Correct. The sphincter of Oddi controls flow from the ampulla into the duodenum."],"lt":"t-accessory-digestive-organs","lid":"c15-x2","comp":124,"competencyId":"w5-pancreas"},{"id":"L02356","dok":1,"q":"Bile is first collected from the hepatocytes by which tiny channels?","a":"The bile canaliculi.","options":["The right and left hepatic ducts","The bile canaliculi","The cystic duct","The hepatic sinusoids"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Bile canaliculi (canaliculus means little canal) are tiny channels squeezed between neighboring hepatocytes. They collect the bile as the cells make it and carry it toward the bile ducts at the edges of the lobule. They are the very first step on the path of bile.","notes":["The right and left hepatic ducts collect bile later, after it has drained from the canaliculi and the small bile ducts. They would be the answer for the ducts that leave the liver.","Correct. The bile canaliculi collect bile from the hepatocytes.","The cystic duct is far downstream and connects the gallbladder. Pick it for the duct that carries bile to and from storage.","The hepatic sinusoids are wide, leaky capillaries that carry blood between the plates of hepatocytes. Pick them for the blood channels of the lobule."],"lt":"t-accessory-digestive-organs","lid":"c16-x2","comp":123,"competencyId":"w5-bile-path"},{"id":"L02357","dok":1,"q":"After leaving the bile canaliculi and small bile ducts, bile gathers into which ducts as it exits the liver?","a":"The right and left hepatic ducts.","options":["The common bile duct","The cystic duct","The right and left hepatic ducts","The main pancreatic duct"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Bile flows from the canaliculi into small bile ducts and then gathers into the right and left hepatic ducts, which drain the two sides of the liver. These two merge into the common hepatic duct just outside the liver. From there the bile can detour into the gallbladder or continue toward the duodenum.","notes":["The common bile duct forms farther along, where the common hepatic duct meets the cystic duct. Pick it for the duct that carries bile down to the ampulla.","The cystic duct is the detour to the gallbladder, after the hepatic ducts have merged. It would be the answer for the duct that carries bile to storage.","Correct. Bile gathers into the right and left hepatic ducts.","The main pancreatic duct carries pancreatic juice and never receives bile from the liver. Pick it for the duct of Wirsung."],"lt":"t-accessory-digestive-organs","lid":"c17-x2","comp":123,"competencyId":"w5-bile-path"},{"id":"L02358","dok":3,"q":"A gallstone completely blocks the cystic duct. What happens to the flow of bile?","a":"Bile is cut off from the gallbladder but still reaches the duodenum","options":["Pancreatic juice can no longer reach the duodenum","Bile can no longer leave the liver by any route","Bile flows backward into the main pancreatic duct","Bile is cut off from the gallbladder but still reaches the duodenum"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The cystic duct is a side branch that connects only the gallbladder to the duct system. If it is blocked, the gallbladder is cut off, but the main route from the hepatic ducts to the common hepatic duct, common bile duct, and duodenum stays open. Trapped bile in the gallbladder is what often leads to cholecystitis.","notes":["The pancreatic duct does not pass through the cystic duct, so pancreatic juice still drains. This would be the answer if the stone were at the hepatopancreatic ampulla, where the two systems share a passage.","Bile leaves the liver through the hepatic ducts, which are above the blockage and still open. This would happen only if the stone blocked the common hepatic duct, the single duct that carries all bile out of the liver.","The cystic duct and the pancreatic duct do not connect, so bile has no path into the pancreatic duct from here. Students pick this when they picture all the ducts as one connected tube.","Correct. The gallbladder is cut off, but the main bile route stays open."],"lt":"t-accessory-digestive-organs","lid":"c18-x2","comp":125,"competencyId":"w5-accessory-disorders"},{"id":"L02359","dok":1,"q":"At which raised opening in the duodenal lining do bile and pancreatic juice enter the small intestine?","a":"The major duodenal papilla.","options":["The pyloric sphincter","The ileocecal valve","The renal papilla","The major duodenal papilla"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"A papilla is a small nipple-shaped bump. The major duodenal papilla is the raised opening on the inner wall of the duodenum where the hepatopancreatic ampulla empties bile and pancreatic juice, with the sphincter of Oddi controlling the flow. It is the last stop on the path of bile.","notes":["The pyloric sphincter is the ring of smooth muscle between the stomach and the duodenum. Pick it for what controls stomach emptying.","The ileocecal valve is where the ileum empties into the cecum, at the end of the small intestine. It would be the answer for the junction of the small and large intestines.","A renal papilla is the tip of a renal pyramid in the kidney, where urine drips into a minor calyx. Pick it for a kidney question; it shares only the word papilla.","Correct. Bile and pancreatic juice enter at the major duodenal papilla."],"lt":"t-accessory-digestive-organs","lid":"c19-x2","comp":124,"competencyId":"w5-pancreas"},{"id":"L02360","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares the acini and the pancreatic islets?","a":"Acini send pancreatic juice through ducts to the duodenum; islets release hormones into the blood.","options":["Both send their products through the pancreatic duct to the duodenum","Acini send pancreatic juice through ducts to the duodenum, and islets release hormones into the blood","Acini release bile into the duodenum, and islets store it between meals","Islets send pancreatic juice through ducts to the duodenum, and acini release hormones into the blood"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The pancreas is both an exocrine and an endocrine gland. The acini are the exocrine part, sending pancreatic juice through ducts that empty into the duodenum. The islets are the endocrine part, with no ducts, so their hormones pass directly into the blood.","notes":["Only the acini use the duct system. Endocrine tissue such as the islets has no ducts, which is the defining difference between endocrine and exocrine glands.","Correct. Acini are exocrine and islets are endocrine.","Neither part of the pancreas handles bile. Bile is made by the liver and stored in the gallbladder, so pick those organs for bile production and storage.","This swaps the two jobs. Acini are the exocrine clusters that use ducts, and the islets are the endocrine clusters that release into the blood."],"lt":"t-accessory-digestive-organs","lid":"c20-x2","comp":124,"competencyId":"w5-pancreas"},{"id":"L02362","dok":1,"q":"Which region of the pancreas lies within the C-shaped curve of the duodenum?","a":"The head.","options":["The head","The fundus","The tail","The body"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The pancreas has a head, body, and tail. The head is the widest part, and it sits nestled in the C-shaped curve of the duodenum on the right. The body crosses behind the stomach, and the tail tapers toward the spleen on the left.","notes":["Correct. The head of the pancreas sits in the curve of the duodenum.","The fundus is a region of the gallbladder and of the stomach, not the pancreas. Students pick it by mixing up the regions of the three organs that share the word body.","The tail is the tapered left end of the pancreas, reaching toward the spleen. It would be the answer for the region nearest the spleen.","The body is the central part of the pancreas, crossing behind the stomach. Pick it for the middle region between the head and tail."],"lt":"t-accessory-digestive-organs","lid":"c22-x2","comp":124,"competencyId":"w5-pancreas"},{"id":"L02363","dok":1,"q":"Which wide, leaky capillaries run between the plates of hepatocytes?","a":"The hepatic sinusoids.","options":["The pulmonary capillaries","The hepatic sinusoids","The bile canaliculi","The lacteals"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Sinusoids are a special type of capillary with wide, winding channels and large gaps in their walls. In the liver, the hepatic sinusoids run between the plates of hepatocytes and carry blood from the portal triads toward the central vein. Their leakiness lets hepatocytes exchange materials easily with the blood flowing past.","notes":["Pulmonary capillaries wrap around the alveoli in the lungs for gas exchange. Pick them for the capillaries of the respiratory membrane.","Correct. The hepatic sinusoids run between the hepatocyte plates.","The bile canaliculi are tiny channels between hepatocytes that collect bile, not blood. Pick them for the first channels on the path of bile.","Lacteals are lymphatic capillaries inside the villi of the small intestine that absorb fats. They would be the answer for a question about fat absorption in the intestine."],"lt":"t-accessory-digestive-organs","lid":"c23-x2","comp":122,"competencyId":"w5-liver-gross-lobule"},{"id":"L02364","dok":1,"q":"The ligamentum teres, the round ligament of the liver, is a remnant of which fetal vessel?","a":"The umbilical vein.","options":["The umbilical vein","An umbilical artery","The ductus arteriosus","The ductus venosus"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Before birth, the umbilical vein carries oxygen-rich blood from the placenta to the fetus, running up to the liver. After birth it closes and becomes a fibrous cord, the ligamentum teres, which runs in the free edge of the falciform ligament. Each fetal shunt and vessel leaves a named remnant, so it helps to learn them as pairs.","notes":["Correct. The ligamentum teres is the remnant of the umbilical vein.","The umbilical arteries become the medial umbilical ligaments on the inner surface of the anterior abdominal wall. They would be the answer for those paired cords.","The ductus arteriosus connected the pulmonary trunk to the aorta and becomes the ligamentum arteriosum. Pick it for the remnant between the pulmonary trunk and aorta.","The ductus venosus becomes the ligamentum venosum, a fibrous cord on the inferior surface of the liver. Students confuse it with the teres because both remnants are on the liver."],"lt":"t-accessory-digestive-organs","lid":"c24-x2","comp":122,"competencyId":"w5-liver-gross-lobule"},{"id":"L02365","dok":3,"q":"A gallstone lodges at the hepatopancreatic ampulla, and the patient develops pancreatitis as well as blocked bile. Why can one stone affect the pancreas?","a":"The common bile duct and main pancreatic duct share that final passage, so pancreatic juice is trapped too.","options":["The bile duct and main pancreatic duct share that passage, so pancreatic juice is trapped too","The pancreas and gallbladder are one organ joined by the cystic duct","The stone blocks the pyloric sphincter, so stomach contents back up into the pancreas","Gallstones form inside the pancreas and travel down to the ampulla"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The common bile duct and the main pancreatic duct meet at the hepatopancreatic ampulla before emptying into the duodenum. A stone stuck there blocks the shared passage, so pancreatic juice cannot drain and backs up in the pancreas, which can inflame it. This shared anatomy is one of the main ways gallstones trigger pancreatitis.","notes":["Correct. The shared ampulla means one stone blocks both bile and pancreatic juice.","The pancreas and gallbladder are separate organs, and the cystic duct connects only the gallbladder. This mixes up the bile and pancreatic duct systems, which meet only at the ampulla.","The pyloric sphincter is between the stomach and duodenum, upstream of the ampulla, and the stomach does not drain into the pancreas. Pick the pyloric sphincter for a question about stomach emptying.","Gallstones form in the gallbladder, not the pancreas. Students pick this because the stone ends up near the pancreas, but it traveled there from the gallbladder through the cystic and common bile ducts."],"lt":"t-accessory-digestive-organs","lid":"c25-x2","comp":125,"competencyId":"w5-accessory-disorders"},{"id":"L02366","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares hepatitis and cirrhosis?","a":"Hepatitis is inflammation of the liver; cirrhosis is replacement of liver tissue by scar tissue.","options":["Hepatitis affects the gallbladder, and cirrhosis affects the liver","Cirrhosis is inflammation of the liver, and hepatitis is replacement of liver tissue by scar tissue","Hepatitis is inflammation of the liver, and cirrhosis is replacement of liver tissue by scar tissue","Both are names for yellowing of the skin from a buildup of bilirubin"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Both disorders affect the liver, but they describe different processes. Hepatitis (hepat for liver, itis for inflammation) is inflammation, commonly from a viral infection. Cirrhosis is what can follow long-term damage, when healthy liver tissue is replaced by scar tissue and liver function is lost.","notes":["Both disorders affect the liver. Inflammation of the gallbladder is cholecystitis, so pick that name for an inflamed gallbladder.","This swaps the two definitions. The itis ending in hepatitis is the clue that it is the inflammation, while cirrhosis is the scarring.","Correct. Hepatitis is inflammation and cirrhosis is scarring.","Yellowing of the skin and eyes from bilirubin buildup is jaundice, a sign that liver disease can cause. Pick jaundice for the yellow color, not for either of these disorders."],"lt":"t-accessory-digestive-organs","lid":"c26-x2","comp":125,"competencyId":"w5-accessory-disorders"},{"id":"L02367","dok":1,"q":"Gallstones are hardened deposits that can block the bile ducts. In which organ do they usually form?","a":"The gallbladder.","options":["The pancreas","The gallbladder","The kidney","The urinary bladder"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Gallstones form in the gallbladder, where bile is stored and concentrated. As the gallbladder removes water from bile, some bile components can harden into stones. A stone that slips out can lodge in the cystic duct, the common bile duct, or the ampulla and block the flow of bile.","notes":["The pancreas does not store bile and is not where gallstones form, though a gallstone at the ampulla can cause pancreatitis. Pick the pancreas for the organ inflamed in pancreatitis.","Correct. Gallstones form in the gallbladder.","The kidney is where kidney stones form, from substances in urine. Pick it for a stone that blocks a ureter, not a bile duct.","The urinary bladder stores urine, and stones there come from urine, not bile. Students pick it because of the shared word bladder."],"lt":"t-accessory-digestive-organs","lid":"c27-x2","comp":125,"competencyId":"w5-accessory-disorders"},{"id":"L02368","dok":2,"q":"A child with mumps has swelling of the glands the virus infects. Where would that swelling show?","a":"In front of and below the ears.","options":["In the right upper quadrant of the abdomen","At the back of the throat beside the uvula","Under the tongue in the floor of the mouth","In front of and below the ears"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Mumps is a viral infection that inflames and enlarges the parotid glands. The parotids sit anterior and inferior to each ear, over the masseter muscle and the angle of the jaw. When they swell, the face looks puffy in front of and below the ears, which is the classic look of mumps.","notes":["The right upper quadrant holds the liver and gallbladder. Pick it for pain or swelling from hepatitis or cholecystitis.","The back of the throat near the uvula is where the palatine tonsils sit. Swelling there would suggest tonsillitis, not mumps.","Under the tongue is where the sublingual glands lie. Swelling there would point to the sublingual or submandibular glands, not the classic site of mumps.","Correct. The parotid glands are in front of and below the ears."],"lt":"t-accessory-digestive-organs","lid":"c28-x2","comp":125,"competencyId":"w5-accessory-disorders"},{"id":"L02369","dok":2,"q":"Cholecystitis, inflammation of the gallbladder, is most often caused by what?","a":"A gallstone blocking the cystic duct.","options":["A gallstone blocking the cystic duct","Scarring of the liver tissue","A viral infection of the liver","A buildup of bilirubin in the skin"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The gallbladder drains only through the cystic duct. When a gallstone blocks that duct, bile is trapped inside the gallbladder, and the trapped bile irritates and inflames its wall. That is why cholecystitis and gallstones so often appear together, with pain in the right upper quadrant.","notes":["Correct. A gallstone blocking the cystic duct is the usual cause.","Scarring of liver tissue is cirrhosis, the result of long-term liver damage. It would be the answer for a scarred liver with lost function.","A viral infection of the liver causes hepatitis. Pick it for the most common cause of liver inflammation.","A buildup of bilirubin causes jaundice, the yellowing of the skin and eyes. Jaundice can follow a blocked bile duct, but it is a sign, not the cause of gallbladder inflammation."],"lt":"t-accessory-digestive-organs","lid":"c29-x2","comp":125,"competencyId":"w5-accessory-disorders"},{"id":"L02370","dok":1,"q":"A patient develops yellowing of the skin and eyes from a buildup of bilirubin. What is this condition called?","a":"Jaundice.","options":["Mumps","Cirrhosis","Pancreatitis","Jaundice"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Jaundice is a yellowing of the skin and the whites of the eyes caused by a buildup of bilirubin, a yellow pigment that is normally cleared by the liver into bile. It often signals liver disease or a blocked bile duct, such as from a gallstone. It is a sign that points to a problem rather than a disease of one organ.","notes":["Mumps is a viral infection that swells the parotid glands. It would be the answer for swelling in front of the ears.","Cirrhosis is scarring of the liver that can lead to jaundice, but it is the underlying damage, not the yellow color itself. Pick it for liver tissue replaced by scar tissue.","Pancreatitis is inflammation of the pancreas. Pick it for an inflamed pancreas, for example after a gallstone blocks the ampulla.","Correct. Yellowing from bilirubin buildup is jaundice."],"lt":"t-accessory-digestive-organs","lid":"c30-x2","comp":125,"competencyId":"w5-accessory-disorders"},{"id":"L02372","dok":2,"q":"Fat arrives in the duodenum mainly with meals. Why is it useful that the gallbladder stores and concentrates bile between meals?","a":"A concentrated supply of bile can be released when fatty food actually arrives.","options":["Stored bile is released into the blood to reach the small intestine","The gallbladder adds fat-digesting enzymes to bile while it is stored","A concentrated supply of bile can be released when fatty food arrives","Storing bile lets the gallbladder make more bile between meals"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The liver makes bile all the time, but bile is needed mainly when a fatty meal reaches the duodenum. Storing bile in the gallbladder and concentrating it by removing water builds up a strong supply between meals. When fat arrives, the gallbladder contracts and sends that concentrated bile down the cystic and common bile ducts right when it is needed.","notes":["Bile travels through ducts, not the blood, from the gallbladder to the duodenum. Students choose this when they confuse exocrine secretion through ducts with endocrine release into the blood.","Bile contains no digestive enzymes, and the gallbladder adds none. The fat-digesting enzymes come from the pancreas, so pick the pancreas for the source of enzymes.","Correct. Storage lets a concentrated supply be released with a fatty meal.","The gallbladder makes no bile; it only stores and concentrates what the liver produces. Pick the liver for the organ that makes bile."],"lt":"t-accessory-digestive-organs","lid":"d3-t-accessory-digestive-organs-2","comp":123,"competencyId":"w5-bile-path"},{"id":"L02375","dok":2,"q":"The main pancreatic duct and the common bile duct join at the hepatopancreatic ampulla. What does this mean for where their secretions arrive?","a":"Bile and pancreatic juice enter the duodenum together at the major duodenal papilla.","options":["Bile and pancreatic juice enter the jejunum rather than the duodenum","Bile and pancreatic juice enter the duodenum together at the major duodenal papilla","Pancreatic juice flows up the cystic duct to be stored in the gallbladder","Bile enters the stomach while pancreatic juice enters the duodenum"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Because the two ducts merge at the ampulla, bile and pancreatic juice exit through the same opening, the major duodenal papilla on the wall of the duodenum. The sphincter of Oddi controls that shared exit. Both secretions are therefore delivered together to the first part of the small intestine, right where food leaves the stomach.","notes":["The jejunum is the middle part of the small intestine, beyond the duodenum. Pick it for the segment between the duodenum and the ileum, not for where these ducts open.","Correct. Both secretions enter the duodenum at the major duodenal papilla.","The cystic duct carries only bile to and from the gallbladder, and pancreatic juice is never stored. Pick the cystic duct for bile storage.","No bile duct opens into the stomach; both secretions enter the duodenum. Students pick this when they assume bile must work in the stomach because the stomach comes first."],"lt":"t-accessory-digestive-organs","lid":"d3-t-accessory-digestive-organs-5","comp":123,"competencyId":"w5-bile-path"},{"id":"L02377","dok":1,"q":"Which statement describes what saliva does in the mouth?","a":"It moistens, cleanses, and lubricates food and begins its breakdown.","options":["It absorbs nutrients from food directly into the blood","It moistens and lubricates food and begins its breakdown","It neutralizes the stomach acid that enters the duodenum","It breaks fat into small droplets so enzymes can reach it"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Saliva is the watery secretion of the salivary glands. It keeps the mouth moist and clean, lubricates food so it can be chewed into a soft mass and swallowed, and begins chemical digestion with salivary amylase acting on starch. Dissolving food in saliva also lets the taste buds detect it.","notes":["Absorbing nutrients into the blood happens mainly in the small intestine, through its villi. Pick the small intestine for absorption.","Correct. Saliva moistens, cleanses, and lubricates food and begins its breakdown.","Neutralizing stomach acid in the duodenum is the job of the bicarbonate in pancreatic juice. That would be the answer for the pancreas, not saliva.","Breaking fat into small droplets is the job of bile from the liver. Pick bile for emulsifying fat in the duodenum."],"lt":"t-accessory-digestive-organs","lid":"d3-t-accessory-digestive-organs-7","comp":121,"competencyId":"w5-salivary-glands"},{"id":"L04370","dok":1,"q":"Which list names the three large pairs of salivary glands?","a":"The parotid, submandibular, and sublingual glands.","options":["Buccal, submandibular, and sublingual","Lacrimal, submandibular, and sublingual","Parotid, submandibular, and lingual","Parotid, submandibular, and sublingual"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Three large pairs of glands make most of the saliva. The parotid glands lie in front of and below the ears, the submandibular glands lie along the inner side of the mandible, and the sublingual glands lie beneath the tongue. Many small minor salivary glands scattered through the lining of the mouth add a little more.","notes":["Buccal glands are minor salivary glands in the lining of the cheeks. They are real salivary glands but small ones, and they would fit a question asking for examples of minor salivary glands.","The lacrimal glands make tears and sit above the lateral corner of each eye, so they are not salivary glands. Students pick them because they are another paired gland of the head.","The lingual glands are small glands within the tongue that secrete mucus and watery fluid. They are minor glands, so they would be the answer only for a question about glands in the tongue itself.","Correct. The parotid, submandibular, and sublingual glands are the three large pairs."],"lt":"t-w5-salivary-glands","lid":"w5-salivary-glands-r1","comp":121,"competencyId":"w5-salivary-glands"},{"id":"L04371","dok":1,"q":"The parotid gland lies in front of and below the ear. Where does its duct open into the mouth?","a":"Beside the second upper molar.","options":["Beside the second upper molar","Near the lingual frenulum","On the inside of the lower lip","Onto the floor of the mouth"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The parotid gland is the largest salivary gland and sits between the skin and the masseter muscle, in front of and below the ear. Its duct crosses the cheek and pierces the buccinator to open into the mouth beside the second upper molar. You can feel the small bump of this opening with your tongue on the inside of your cheek.","notes":["Correct. The parotid duct opens beside the second upper molar.","Ducts opening near the lingual frenulum belong to the submandibular glands. Pick this location for a question about where the submandibular ducts open.","The inside of the lip holds small labial glands, which are minor salivary glands with their own tiny openings. No large salivary gland drains there, so students pick this only by guessing.","Ducts opening onto the floor of the mouth belong to the sublingual glands, which lie beneath the tongue. That would be the answer for the sublingual glands."],"lt":"t-w5-salivary-glands","lid":"w5-salivary-glands-r2","comp":121,"competencyId":"w5-salivary-glands"},{"id":"L04372","dok":1,"q":"Which salivary gland lies along the inner side of the mandible, with ducts that open near the lingual frenulum?","a":"The submandibular gland.","options":["Minor lingual glands","Sublingual gland","Parotid gland","Submandibular gland"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The submandibular glands lie in the floor of the mouth along the inner side of the body of the mandible, as their name (below the mandible) suggests. Their ducts run forward and open near the lingual frenulum, the fold under the tongue. They produce a large share of the saliva in the resting mouth.","notes":["Lingual glands are small minor glands within the tongue that secrete mucus and watery fluid. They are the answer for glands inside the tongue itself, not a large gland beside the mandible.","The sublingual glands lie just beneath the tongue, above the submandibular glands, and their ducts open onto the floor of the mouth. Choose them for the smallest of the three large pairs.","The parotid gland lies in front of and below the ear, over the masseter muscle, and its duct opens beside the second upper molar. It is the answer for the largest salivary gland and the one swollen in mumps.","Correct. The submandibular glands lie along the inner mandible and open near the lingual frenulum."],"lt":"t-w5-salivary-glands","lid":"w5-salivary-glands-r3","comp":121,"competencyId":"w5-salivary-glands"},{"id":"L04373","dok":2,"q":"Which statement describes what saliva does?","a":"It moistens, cleanses, and lubricates food and begins its breakdown.","options":["Carries most of the digestive enzymes into the duodenum","Moistens the mouth and starts breaking down food","Breaks fat into tiny droplets so it can be digested","Absorbs water from waste and helps form the feces"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Saliva is the watery secretion of the salivary glands. It keeps the mouth moist and clean, lubricates food so it can be shaped into a ball and swallowed, and begins the chemical breakdown of food while it is still being chewed. The three large pairs of salivary glands make most of it.","notes":["Pancreatic juice from the acini of the pancreas carries most of the digestive enzymes into the duodenum. That is the answer for a question about the exocrine pancreas.","Correct. Saliva moistens, cleanses and lubricates, and it starts the breakdown of food.","Breaking fat into tiny droplets is the job of bile, which the liver makes and the gallbladder stores. Pick it for a question about what bile does in the duodenum.","Absorbing water and forming feces is the job of the large intestine, not a secretion at all. Choose it for a question about the colon."],"lt":"t-w5-salivary-glands","lid":"w5-salivary-glands-r4","comp":121,"competencyId":"w5-salivary-glands"},{"id":"L04374","dok":3,"q":"A child with mumps has painful swelling in front of and just below the ear, over the angle of the jaw. Which salivary gland is inflamed?","a":"The parotid gland.","options":["Sublingual gland","Submandibular gland","Parotid gland","Palatine tonsil"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Mumps is a viral infection that inflames and enlarges the parotid glands. The parotid gland lies anterior and inferior to the ear, between the skin and the masseter muscle, so when it swells the cheek in front of the ear puffs out. The location of the swelling points straight to this gland.","notes":["The sublingual gland lies under the tongue in the floor of the mouth. Swelling of this gland would show inside the mouth beneath the tongue, not on the side of the face.","The submandibular gland lies along the inner side of the mandible, under the jaw rather than in front of the ear. Swelling there would show beneath the jawline, which is where you would point for this gland.","Correct. The parotid gland lies in front of and below the ear and is the gland swollen in mumps.","The palatine tonsils are lymphatic tissue at the back of the mouth between the palatal arches, not salivary glands. They would be the answer for swelling seen at the back of the throat in tonsillitis."],"lt":"t-w5-salivary-glands","lid":"w5-salivary-glands-r5","comp":125,"competencyId":"w5-accessory-disorders"},{"id":"L04375","dok":1,"q":"What does the liver produce, and where does it sit?","a":"It produces bile and sits below the diaphragm, mostly in the upper right abdomen.","options":["Bile; upper left abdomen, behind the stomach","Gastric juice; upper left abdomen, below the diaphragm","Bile; upper right abdomen, below the diaphragm","Pancreatic juice; upper right abdomen, under the ribs"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The liver is the largest gland in the body. It sits just below the diaphragm, mostly in the upper right abdomen under the lower ribs, with its left lobe reaching across the midline. Its digestive product is bile, which drains through the bile ducts to the duodenum.","notes":["Bile is right, but the upper left abdomen behind the stomach is where the pancreas body and tail lie, not the liver. Students choose this when they place the liver on the wrong side.","Gastric juice comes from the gastric glands of the stomach, which sits in the upper left abdomen below the diaphragm. This pairing describes the stomach, not the liver.","Correct. The liver makes bile and lies mostly in the upper right abdomen below the diaphragm.","Pancreatic juice is made by the acini of the pancreas, which lies behind the stomach. Pick pancreatic juice for a question about the exocrine pancreas."],"lt":"t-w5-liver-gross-lobule","lid":"w5-liver-gross-lobule-r1","comp":122,"competencyId":"w5-liver-gross-lobule"},{"id":"L04376","dok":1,"q":"On the anterior surface, which fold of peritoneum separates the right and left lobes of the liver?","a":"The falciform ligament.","options":["Falciform ligament","Lesser omentum","Greater omentum","Transverse mesocolon"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The falciform ligament is a sickle-shaped fold of peritoneum that suspends the liver from the anterior abdominal wall and diaphragm. On the front of the liver it marks the boundary between the large right lobe and the smaller left lobe. A fibrous cord in its free edge, the ligamentum teres, is the remnant of the fetal umbilical vein.","notes":["Correct. The falciform ligament divides the right and left lobes on the front of the liver.","The lesser omentum connects the stomach and duodenum to the underside of the liver and carries vessels to it. Pick it for a question about which fold joins the stomach to the liver.","The greater omentum hangs from the stomach like a fatty apron over the intestines. It is the answer for the largest peritoneal fold, not for a fold on the liver.","The mesocolon binds the transverse and sigmoid colon to the posterior abdominal wall. Choose it for a question about how the colon is anchored."],"lt":"t-w5-liver-gross-lobule","lid":"w5-liver-gross-lobule-r2","comp":122,"competencyId":"w5-liver-gross-lobule"},{"id":"L04377","dok":1,"q":"What are hepatocytes?","a":"The functional cells of the liver, arranged in plates called hepatic laminae.","options":["Leaky capillaries running between liver cell plates","Pancreatic cell clusters that make digestive juice","The liver's working cells, arranged in plates","Tiny channels between liver cells that collect bile"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Hepatocytes are the functional cells of the liver, the cells that make bile and do the liver's processing work. They are arranged in plates, called hepatic laminae, that radiate through each lobule. Blood flows past them in sinusoids on one side, and the bile they make drains into canaliculi between neighboring cells.","notes":["Wide, leaky capillaries running between the plates of liver cells are the hepatic sinusoids. Pick them for a question about the blood channels inside a liver lobule.","Clusters of pancreatic cells that make digestive juice are the acini of the pancreas. That is the answer for the exocrine part of the pancreas, not the liver.","Correct. Hepatocytes are the liver's functional cells, stacked in plates called hepatic laminae.","Tiny channels between hepatocytes that collect bile are bile canaliculi, the first step in the bile pathway. They are the answer for where bile goes right after the cells make it."],"lt":"t-w5-liver-gross-lobule","lid":"w5-liver-gross-lobule-r3","comp":122,"competencyId":"w5-liver-gross-lobule"},{"id":"L04378","dok":2,"q":"Which three structures make up a portal triad at the corner of a liver lobule?","a":"A bile duct, a branch of the hepatic artery, and a branch of the hepatic portal vein.","options":["Cystic duct, hepatic artery branch, hepatic portal vein branch","Bile duct, hepatic artery branch, hepatic portal vein branch","Bile duct, hepatic artery branch, hepatic vein branch","Bile duct, central vein, hepatic portal vein branch"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"A portal triad sits at each corner of a liver lobule and carries traffic in both directions. A branch of the hepatic artery brings oxygenated blood in, a branch of the hepatic portal vein brings nutrient-rich blood from the gut in, and a bile duct carries bile out. The two incoming blood supplies mix in the sinusoids as they flow past the hepatocytes.","notes":["The cystic duct carries bile to and from the gallbladder outside the liver, so it is not found in a lobule. Pick it for a question about the duct that connects the gallbladder to the bile pathway.","Correct. The triad is a bile duct, a hepatic artery branch, and a hepatic portal vein branch.","The hepatic veins drain blood out of the liver into the inferior vena cava and are not part of the triad. They would be the answer for the vessels carrying blood away from the liver.","The central vein runs through the middle of each lobule and collects blood after it passes the hepatocytes. It is the answer for the vessel at the center of the lobule, not at its corners."],"lt":"t-w5-liver-gross-lobule","lid":"w5-liver-gross-lobule-r4","comp":122,"competencyId":"w5-liver-gross-lobule"},{"id":"L04379","dok":3,"q":"The liver receives blood from two sources. Which statement correctly describes what each one delivers?","a":"The hepatic portal vein delivers nutrient-rich blood from the gut, and the hepatic artery delivers oxygenated blood.","options":["Portal vein brings nutrient-rich gut blood; hepatic artery brings oxygenated blood","Both vessels bring oxygenated blood from the aorta, one to each liver lobe","Hepatic veins bring nutrient-rich gut blood; hepatic artery brings oxygenated blood","Portal vein brings oxygenated blood; hepatic artery brings nutrient-rich gut blood"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The liver's blood supply is unusual. The hepatic portal vein carries nutrient-rich blood collected from the stomach, intestines, spleen and pancreas, so the liver can process absorbed substances before they reach the rest of the body. The hepatic artery, which arises from the celiac trunk, brings oxygenated blood to keep the liver tissue alive.","notes":["Correct. The portal vein brings gut blood rich in nutrients, and the hepatic artery brings oxygen.","Only the hepatic artery comes from the aorta. Students pick this when they forget that the liver has a portal vein, a vein that brings blood in rather than taking it out.","The hepatic veins carry blood out of the liver into the inferior vena cava, so they do not deliver anything to it. They would be the answer for the vessels that drain the liver.","This reverses the two vessels. Arteries carry blood from the aorta, so the oxygenated supply comes through the hepatic artery, while the portal vein collects blood that has already passed through the gut."],"lt":"t-w5-liver-gross-lobule","lid":"w5-liver-gross-lobule-r5","comp":122,"competencyId":"w5-liver-gross-lobule"},{"id":"L04380","dok":1,"q":"What does the gallbladder do?","a":"It stores and concentrates bile until it is needed.","options":["Stores and concentrates bile until it is needed","Produces enzyme-rich juice for the duodenum","Produces bile from the blood that passes through it","Absorbs water from waste and helps form feces"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The gallbladder is a small pear-shaped sac on the underside of the liver. It does not make bile. Instead it receives bile from the liver through the cystic duct, stores it, and concentrates it by removing water, then releases it into the duodenum after a meal.","notes":["Correct. The gallbladder stores and concentrates bile.","Enzyme-rich juice for the duodenum comes from the acini of the pancreas. That is the answer for a question about pancreatic juice.","Bile is produced by the liver's hepatocytes, not the gallbladder. Pick this for a question about what the liver makes.","Absorbing water from waste and forming feces is the job of the large intestine. Students pick this because the gallbladder also removes water, but it removes it from bile, not from waste."],"lt":"t-w5-bile-path","lid":"w5-bile-path-r1","comp":123,"competencyId":"w5-bile-path"},{"id":"L04382","dok":2,"q":"Without a detour into the gallbladder, which sequence traces bile from the liver cells to the duodenum?","a":"Bile canaliculi, hepatic ducts, common hepatic duct, common bile duct, hepatopancreatic ampulla, duodenum.","options":["Canaliculi, hepatic ducts, common hepatic duct, common bile duct, ampulla","Hepatic ducts, canaliculi, common hepatic duct, common bile duct, ampulla","Canaliculi, common bile duct, hepatic ducts, common hepatic duct, ampulla","Canaliculi, hepatic ducts, cystic duct, main pancreatic duct, ampulla"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Bile drains along a path of widening ducts. The tiny bile canaliculi between hepatocytes collect it, the right and left hepatic ducts gather it and merge into the common hepatic duct, and the common hepatic duct becomes the common bile duct once the cystic duct joins. The common bile duct meets the pancreatic duct at the hepatopancreatic ampulla, which opens into the duodenum.","notes":["Correct. Bile moves from canaliculi to hepatic ducts, then the common hepatic duct, the common bile duct, and the ampulla.","This puts the hepatic ducts ahead of the canaliculi, but the canaliculi are the smallest channels and collect bile right where the cells make it. The larger ducts come after.","This places the common bile duct second, but the common bile duct only forms after the common hepatic duct is joined by the cystic duct. It is the last duct before the ampulla, not an early one.","The cystic duct leads to and from the gallbladder, which the question excludes, and bile never travels up the main pancreatic duct. Choose the cystic duct only for a question about bile going into storage."],"lt":"t-w5-bile-path","lid":"w5-bile-path-r4","comp":123,"competencyId":"w5-bile-path"},{"id":"L04383","dok":3,"q":"After a patient's gallbladder is removed, bile still reaches the intestine. Using the duct anatomy, how does it get there?","a":"Bile flows from the common hepatic duct straight into the common bile duct, skipping the storage detour through the cystic duct and gallbladder.","options":["It drains through the accessory pancreatic duct and into the duodenum instead","It drains into the stomach along a channel in the lesser omentum","It enters the blood and is carried to the duodenum by the hepatic veins","It drains from the common hepatic duct straight into the common bile duct"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The gallbladder only stores and concentrates bile, and it sits on a side branch, the cystic duct. The liver keeps making bile, which flows from the hepatic ducts into the common hepatic duct and then straight into the common bile duct, the hepatopancreatic ampulla, and the duodenum. Without the gallbladder, bile simply trickles in steadily instead of being stored and released in larger amounts.","notes":["The accessory pancreatic duct drains part of the pancreas separately into the duodenum and is not connected to the bile ducts. Pick it for a question about the smaller pancreatic duct.","The lesser omentum is a fold of peritoneum linking the stomach and duodenum to the liver, not a channel for bile. It is the answer for a question about which fold carries vessels to the liver.","The hepatic veins carry blood from the liver to the inferior vena cava and never carry bile. Students pick this when they confuse the liver's blood vessels with its bile ducts.","Correct. Bile takes the main path from the common hepatic duct into the common bile duct and on to the duodenum."],"lt":"t-w5-bile-path","lid":"w5-bile-path-r5","comp":123,"competencyId":"w5-bile-path"},{"id":"L04384","dok":1,"q":"Where does the pancreas lie, and how is it positioned relative to the peritoneum?","a":"Posterior to the stomach, in a retroperitoneal position.","options":["Under the right lobe of the liver, intraperitoneal","Beside the stomach in the upper left, intraperitoneal","Posterior to the stomach, retroperitoneal","Anterior to the stomach, inside the greater omentum"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The pancreas lies posterior to the stomach, stretching from its head in the curve of the duodenum to its tail near the spleen. It is retroperitoneal, meaning it lies behind the peritoneum against the posterior abdominal wall instead of hanging in the peritoneal cavity. Its ducts empty into the duodenum.","notes":["The underside of the right lobe of the liver is where the gallbladder sits. Pick this location for a question about the gallbladder.","An intraperitoneal organ beside the stomach in the upper left abdomen describes the spleen. That would be the answer for the spleen, which is covered by peritoneum.","Correct. The pancreas is behind the stomach and retroperitoneal.","The greater omentum hangs in front of the intestines from the stomach, and the pancreas is behind the stomach, not in front of it. This description fits the fatty apron of the greater omentum."],"lt":"t-w5-pancreas","lid":"w5-pancreas-r1","comp":124,"competencyId":"w5-pancreas"},{"id":"L04385","dok":1,"q":"Which list names the three regions of the pancreas?","a":"The head, body, and tail.","options":["Right, left, and caudate lobes","Cardia, body, and pylorus","Head, body, and tail","Fundus, body, and neck"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The pancreas is divided into a head, a body and a tail. The wide head sits in the C-shaped curve of the duodenum, the body runs behind the stomach, and the tapered tail reaches toward the spleen. The main pancreatic duct runs through all three regions toward the head.","notes":["The right and left lobes, with the smaller caudate and quadrate lobes, are lobes of the liver. Choose them for a question about liver anatomy.","The cardia, body and pylorus are regions of the stomach. This set would fit a question about the parts of the stomach between the esophagus and the duodenum.","Correct. The pancreas has a head, body, and tail.","Fundus, body and neck are the three regions of the gallbladder, from its broad end to its tapered end. Pick this set for a question about the gallbladder."],"lt":"t-w5-pancreas","lid":"w5-pancreas-r2","comp":124,"competencyId":"w5-pancreas"},{"id":"L04386","dok":1,"q":"What are the acini of the pancreas?","a":"The exocrine clusters that produce pancreatic juice, about 99 percent of the gland.","options":["Ducts that carry pancreatic juice into the duodenum","Plates of liver cells that make bile and process absorbed nutrients","Exocrine clusters that make pancreatic juice, about 99 percent","Endocrine clusters that make hormones, about 1 percent"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Most of the pancreas is made of acini, small grape-like clusters of exocrine cells (cells that release their product into ducts). They produce pancreatic juice, which drains through the pancreatic ducts into the duodenum. The acini make up about 99 percent of the gland, while the scattered pancreatic islets make up the remaining 1 percent.","notes":["The ducts that carry juice into the duodenum are the main and accessory pancreatic ducts. The acini make the juice, and the ducts only carry it.","Plates of liver cells are hepatic laminae made of hepatocytes. They are the answer for the functional tissue of the liver, not the pancreas.","Correct. Acini are the exocrine clusters that make pancreatic juice and form most of the gland.","Endocrine clusters that make hormones, about 1 percent of the gland, are the pancreatic islets. Pick them for a question about the endocrine part of the pancreas."],"lt":"t-w5-pancreas","lid":"w5-pancreas-r3","comp":124,"competencyId":"w5-pancreas"},{"id":"L04387","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares the exocrine and endocrine parts of the pancreas?","a":"The acini are exocrine and secrete juice into the duodenum through ducts; the pancreatic islets are endocrine and release hormones.","options":["Acini and islets both send juice through ducts to the duodenum","Islets send juice through ducts to the duodenum; acini release hormones","Acini send juice through ducts to the duodenum; islets release hormones","Acini release hormones into ducts; islets make bile for the duodenum"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The pancreas is two glands in one. Its exocrine part, the acini, makes pancreatic juice and sends it through ducts into the duodenum. Its endocrine part, the pancreatic islets, makes hormones that pass into the blood rather than into a duct. The acini make up about 99 percent of the gland and the islets about 1 percent.","notes":["Only the acini send their product through ducts. The islets are endocrine, so their hormones go into the blood, which is the whole difference between the two parts.","This reverses the two. Exocrine glands use ducts, and the acini are the clusters that empty through the pancreatic duct, so the islets cannot be the duct-using part.","Correct. Acini are the exocrine part and islets are the endocrine part.","Bile is made by the liver, not the pancreas, and hormones do not travel in ducts. Students pick this when they mix up the pancreas with the liver."],"lt":"t-w5-pancreas","lid":"w5-pancreas-r4","comp":124,"competencyId":"w5-pancreas"},{"id":"L04388","dok":3,"q":"A gallstone lodges at the hepatopancreatic ampulla, and the patient develops pancreatitis. Why can a gallstone there inflame the pancreas?","a":"The bile duct and pancreatic duct share that final passage, so the stone blocks pancreatic juice as well as bile.","options":["Bile and pancreatic ducts share that passage, so pancreatic juice backs up","The gallbladder sits inside the pancreas, so its swelling presses on the gland","The pancreas makes the bile, so blocked bile damages the pancreas","The stone travels up the cystic duct and lodges inside the pancreas"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The common bile duct and the main pancreatic duct meet at the hepatopancreatic ampulla before they open into the duodenum. Because both secretions pass through that short shared channel, a stone lodged there blocks bile and pancreatic juice at once. The trapped pancreatic juice backs up into the pancreas, which can inflame it.","notes":["Correct. The shared ampulla means one stone can block both the bile duct and the pancreatic duct.","The gallbladder sits on the underside of the liver, not inside the pancreas. This is a location mix-up, since the gallbladder and pancreas are linked only through the ducts.","The liver makes bile, and the pancreas makes pancreatic juice. Pick the liver for a question about where bile is produced.","The cystic duct connects the gallbladder to the common bile duct and leads nowhere near the pancreas. Students pick this when they think of the cystic duct as a route into the pancreas."],"lt":"t-w5-pancreas","lid":"w5-pancreas-r5","comp":125,"competencyId":"w5-accessory-disorders"},{"id":"L04411","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly distinguishes the alimentary canal from the accessory digestive organs?","a":"The alimentary canal is the continuous tube food travels through; the accessory organs are not part of that tube and help from alongside it.","options":["The canal is the tube food passes through; accessory organs lie outside it","The canal makes every digestive secretion; accessory organs only absorb food","The canal includes the liver and pancreas; accessory organs are the intestines","Food passes through both; accessory organs are the widest parts of the tube"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The alimentary canal is one continuous muscular tube from the mouth to the anus, and food passes through every part of it. The accessory organs (the teeth, tongue, salivary glands, liver, gallbladder and pancreas) are not part of that tube. The teeth and tongue work on food in the mouth, and the glandular organs add their secretions to the canal through ducts.","notes":["Correct. Food passes through the canal, while the accessory organs help from outside the tube.","Much of the digestive secretion comes from accessory organs such as the salivary glands, liver, and pancreas, and absorption happens in the canal. This choice reverses the roles of the two groups.","The liver and pancreas are accessory organs, and the intestines are part of the canal. This choice reverses the two groups.","Food never passes through the accessory organs, so this cannot be right. Students pick it when they think of the gallbladder or pancreas as pouches of the tube rather than separate organs with ducts."],"lt":"t-w5-food-pathway","lid":"w5-food-pathway-r2","comp":109,"competencyId":"w5-canal-wall"},{"id":"L04416","dok":1,"q":"What are gallstones, and what can they block?","a":"Hardened deposits that form in the gallbladder and can block the bile ducts.","options":["Hardened deposits from the kidney that can block the ureter","Scar tissue in the liver that replaces healthy liver tissue","Hardened deposits from the gallbladder that can block the bile ducts","Hardened deposits in a salivary gland that can block the gland's duct"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Gallstones are hardened deposits that form inside the gallbladder from substances in stored bile. A stone can leave the gallbladder and lodge in the cystic duct, the common bile duct, or the hepatopancreatic ampulla, blocking the flow of bile. A blocking stone can inflame the gallbladder (cholecystitis) or, at the ampulla, the pancreas.","notes":["Hardened deposits that form in the kidney and block the ureter are kidney stones. Pick this for a question about the urinary tract.","Scar tissue replacing healthy liver tissue is cirrhosis. Choose it for a question about long-term liver damage and loss of liver function.","Correct. Gallstones form in the gallbladder and can block the bile ducts.","Stones can form in a salivary gland and block its duct, but they are salivary stones, not gallstones. This would be the answer for a swollen salivary gland during meals."],"lt":"t-w5-accessory-disorders","lid":"w5-accessory-disorders-r1","comp":125,"competencyId":"w5-accessory-disorders"},{"id":"L04417","dok":1,"q":"Which description matches jaundice?","a":"A yellowing of the skin and eyes from a buildup of bilirubin, often from liver or bile duct disease.","options":["Yellowing of skin and eyes from a buildup of bilirubin","Replacement of healthy liver tissue with scar tissue","Inflammation of the gallbladder from a blocking stone","Inflammation of the liver, often from a viral infection"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Bilirubin is a yellow pigment made when old red blood cells are broken down. The liver normally clears it into bile. When the liver is damaged or the bile ducts are blocked, bilirubin builds up in the blood and tints the skin and the whites of the eyes yellow. Jaundice is therefore a sign of a problem rather than a disease of its own.","notes":["Correct. Jaundice is yellowing of the skin and eyes from a buildup of bilirubin.","Replacement of healthy liver tissue with scar tissue is cirrhosis. It can also lead to jaundice, but it is the answer for scarring of the liver.","Inflammation of the gallbladder, often from a blocking gallstone, is cholecystitis. Pick it for a question about an inflamed gallbladder.","Inflammation of the liver, commonly from a viral infection, is hepatitis. Hepatitis can cause jaundice, but the inflammation itself is the answer for a question about hepatitis."],"lt":"t-w5-accessory-disorders","lid":"w5-accessory-disorders-r2","comp":125,"competencyId":"w5-accessory-disorders"},{"id":"L04418","dok":2,"q":"Where would a single gallstone have to lodge to block both bile and pancreatic juice at the same time?","a":"Hepatopancreatic ampulla","options":["Hepatopancreatic ampulla","Common hepatic duct","Accessory pancreatic duct","Cystic duct"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The common bile duct and the main pancreatic duct meet at the hepatopancreatic ampulla, a short widened channel just before the duodenum. This is the only place in the system where bile and pancreatic juice share one passage. A stone stuck there backs bile up toward the liver and pancreatic juice up into the pancreas at the same time, which can cause both jaundice and pancreatitis.","notes":["Correct. The hepatopancreatic ampulla is the shared passage for bile and pancreatic juice.","The common hepatic duct carries bile from the liver before the cystic duct joins it, well above the pancreas. A stone there blocks bile from the liver but not pancreatic juice.","The accessory pancreatic duct empties separately into the duodenum and carries only pancreatic juice. Blocking it would not stop bile at all, so it fits a question about the duct of Santorini.","A stone in the cystic duct blocks the gallbladder's outlet and can cause cholecystitis, but bile from the liver and pancreatic juice can still reach the duodenum. Pick it for a stone that affects only the gallbladder."],"lt":"t-w5-accessory-disorders","lid":"w5-accessory-disorders-r3","comp":125,"competencyId":"w5-accessory-disorders"},{"id":"N-a17d-121-1","dok":3,"q":"A dentist sees saliva coming from a small opening inside the cheek, opposite the upper second molar. Which gland makes this saliva, and which muscle does its duct pierce to reach the mouth?","a":"The parotid gland; the buccinator muscle.","options":["The parotid gland; the buccinator muscle.","The submandibular gland; the buccinator muscle.","The parotid gland; the mylohyoid muscle.","The sublingual gland; the masseter muscle."],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The parotid gland lies in front of and below the ear, over the masseter muscle. Its duct runs forward across the masseter, passes through the buccinator muscle of the cheek, and opens on the inside of the cheek opposite the upper second molar. An opening in the cheek at that spot always points to the parotid.","notes":["Correct. The parotid duct runs across the masseter, pierces the buccinator, and opens opposite the upper second molar.","The submandibular duct runs under the lining of the floor of the mouth and opens beside the lingual frenulum, not in the cheek.","The gland is right, but the mylohyoid forms the floor of the mouth. The parotid duct passes through the buccinator in the cheek.","The sublingual gland drains through many small ducts into the floor of the mouth. Its ducts do not reach the cheek."],"comp":121,"competencyId":"w5-salivary-glands"},{"id":"N-a17d-121-2","dok":3,"q":"A small stone blocks the single duct that opens on a bump beside the lingual frenulum. Which gland swells, and where would you feel it?","a":"The submandibular gland, along the inner surface of the mandible.","options":["The sublingual gland, just under the lining below the tongue.","The submandibular gland, over the masseter muscle.","The parotid gland, in front of and below the ear.","The submandibular gland, along the inner surface of the mandible."],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Start with the opening: the single duct that ends on a small bump on each side of the lingual frenulum is the submandibular duct. Then trace it back. It runs under the lining of the floor of the mouth to the submandibular gland, which lies along the inner surface of the body of the mandible. A blocked duct would make that gland swell.","notes":["The sublingual gland lies under the tongue, but it drains through many small ducts along the floor of the mouth, not one duct beside the frenulum.","The gland is right, but the gland over the masseter is the parotid. The submandibular gland lies along the inner surface of the mandible.","The parotid lies in front of and below the ear, but its duct opens inside the cheek opposite the upper second molar, not beside the frenulum.","Correct. The submandibular duct opens beside the lingual frenulum, and the gland lies along the inner surface of the body of the mandible."],"comp":121,"competencyId":"w5-salivary-glands"},{"id":"N-a17d-121-3","dok":3,"q":"A drawing of the floor of the mouth shows two salivary glands. One drains through a single duct beside the lingual frenulum. The other drains through many small ducts along the floor. Which one is which, and which one sits higher?","a":"The single duct drains the submandibular; the many ducts drain the sublingual, which sits higher.","options":["The single duct drains the submandibular; the many ducts drain the sublingual, which sits higher.","The single duct drains the parotid; the many ducts drain the submandibular, which sits higher.","The single duct drains the sublingual; the many ducts drain the submandibular, which sits higher.","The single duct drains the submandibular; the many ducts drain the sublingual, which sits lower."],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Both glands sit in the floor of the mouth, but they drain differently. The submandibular gland sends its saliva through one submandibular duct that opens beside the lingual frenulum. The sublingual gland, the smallest of the three, drains through many small ducts along the floor of the mouth. It lies just under the lining, above the submandibular gland.","notes":["Correct. The sublingual gland drains through many small ducts and lies above the submandibular gland.","The parotid duct opens inside the cheek, not beside the frenulum. The two glands in the floor of the mouth are the submandibular and sublingual.","This swaps the two. The submandibular has one duct beside the frenulum, and the sublingual, with many small ducts, lies above it.","The ducts are matched correctly, but the sublingual gland lies just under the lining, above the submandibular gland, not below it."],"comp":121,"competencyId":"w5-salivary-glands"},{"id":"N-a17d-122-1","dok":3,"q":"On a liver slide, you find a corner of a lobule with a small vein, a small artery, and a duct. Blood from those two vessels flows through which channels, and into which vessel?","a":"Through the hepatic sinusoids, into the central vein.","options":["Through the bile canaliculi, into the bile duct of the triad.","Through the hepatic sinusoids, into the central vein.","Through the hepatic sinusoids, into the hepatic portal vein.","Through the bile canaliculi, into the central vein."],"correctIndex":1,"why":"A vein, an artery, and a duct together at a lobule corner make a portal triad: a branch of the hepatic portal vein, a branch of the hepatic artery, and a bile duct. Blood from the two vessels mixes in the hepatic sinusoids, the leaky capillaries between the plates of hepatocytes, and flows toward the center. The central vein collects it there. Bile travels the other way, out through the canaliculi to the bile duct.","notes":["This is the path of bile, not blood. Bile moves from the canaliculi out to the bile duct, the opposite direction from blood.","Correct. Blood from the portal triad flows through the sinusoids past the hepatocytes to the central vein in the middle of the lobule.","The channels are right, but the portal vein brings blood into the liver. The sinusoids drain into the central vein at the lobule's center.","The bile canaliculi carry bile between hepatocytes, outward toward the portal triad. They do not carry blood."],"comp":122,"competencyId":"w5-liver-gross-lobule"},{"id":"N-a17d-122-2","dok":3,"q":"Looking at the inferior surface of the liver, you see a small square lobe between the gallbladder and a fibrous cord. Which lobe is it, and what is the cord a remnant of?","a":"The quadrate lobe; the cord is the remnant of the fetal umbilical vein.","options":["The quadrate lobe; the cord is the remnant of the fetal umbilical artery.","The caudate lobe; the cord is the remnant of the fetal umbilical vein.","The quadrate lobe; the cord is the remnant of the fetal umbilical vein.","The left lobe; the cord is the falciform ligament."],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The liver has four lobes. The right and left lobes are seen from the front, while the quadrate and caudate lobes are seen from below and behind. The quadrate lobe is the small square lobe on the inferior surface between the gallbladder and the ligamentum teres. The ligamentum teres (round ligament) is a fibrous cord from the umbilicus to the liver, and it is the remnant of the fetal umbilical vein.","notes":["The lobe is right, but the ligamentum teres is the remnant of the fetal umbilical vein. The remnant of the umbilical artery is the medial umbilical ligament, on the inside of the anterior abdominal wall.","The caudate lobe is on the posterior surface beside the inferior vena cava. The square lobe beside the gallbladder is the quadrate lobe.","Correct. The quadrate lobe sits between the gallbladder and the ligamentum teres, which is what remains of the fetal umbilical vein.","The left lobe is seen from the front, over the stomach. The falciform ligament is a peritoneal fold, and the cord in its free edge is the ligamentum teres."],"comp":122,"competencyId":"w5-liver-gross-lobule"},{"id":"N-a17d-123-1","dok":3,"q":"A gallstone lodges in the common bile duct where it runs through the head of the pancreas. Which ducts lie upstream of the stone and would fill with bile that cannot get past it?","a":"The common hepatic duct, the hepatic ducts, and the cystic duct with the gallbladder.","options":["Only the main pancreatic duct and the hepatopancreatic ampulla below the stone.","The common hepatic duct, the hepatic ducts, and the cystic duct with the gallbladder.","Only the right and left hepatic ducts; the gallbladder still empties freely.","Only the cystic duct and the gallbladder; the hepatic ducts still drain freely."],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Trace the biliary tree from the top. The right and left hepatic ducts join to form the common hepatic duct. The cystic duct from the gallbladder joins it to form the common bile duct, which runs behind the first part of the duodenum and through the head of the pancreas. Every duct above that point drains into the common bile duct, so a stone there blocks the hepatic ducts, the common hepatic duct, and the cystic duct and gallbladder.","notes":["The main pancreatic duct and the ampulla lie downstream, where the common bile duct ends. Bile from above never reaches them past the stone.","Correct. All of these drain into the common bile duct, so all of them lie upstream of a stone lodged in it.","The gallbladder empties through the cystic duct into the common bile duct, so it is blocked by the stone as well.","The hepatic ducts empty into the common hepatic duct, which joins the cystic duct to form the common bile duct. A stone there blocks them too."],"comp":123,"competencyId":"w5-bile-path"},{"id":"N-a17d-124-1","dok":3,"q":"A stone blocks the hepatopancreatic ampulla. Which pancreatic duct can still deliver pancreatic juice to the duodenum, and where does it open?","a":"The accessory pancreatic duct, at the minor duodenal papilla.","options":["The main pancreatic duct, at the major duodenal papilla.","The main pancreatic duct, at the minor duodenal papilla.","The accessory pancreatic duct, at the minor duodenal papilla.","The accessory pancreatic duct, at the major duodenal papilla."],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The main pancreatic duct (duct of Wirsung) runs the length of the gland and joins the common bile duct at the hepatopancreatic ampulla, which opens at the major duodenal papilla. The smaller accessory pancreatic duct (duct of Santorini) drains part of the head and opens on its own at the minor duodenal papilla, a little above the major papilla. A stone in the ampulla blocks the main duct but not the accessory duct.","notes":["The main pancreatic duct joins the common bile duct at the ampulla, so a stone in the ampulla blocks it.","The main pancreatic duct opens through the ampulla at the major papilla. It is the accessory duct that uses the minor papilla.","Correct. The accessory pancreatic duct opens on its own at the minor duodenal papilla, so it bypasses the blocked ampulla.","The duct is right, but the major papilla is where the blocked ampulla opens. The accessory duct opens at the minor papilla."],"comp":124,"competencyId":"w5-pancreas"},{"id":"N-a17d-124-2","dok":3,"q":"A stained pancreas slide shows mostly dark clusters of gland cells around tiny ducts, with scattered pale, round patches. If a disease destroyed only the pale patches, which tissue would be lost?","a":"The pancreatic islets, the endocrine tissue, about 1 percent of the gland.","options":["The pancreatic islets, the endocrine tissue, about 1 percent of the gland.","The acini, the exocrine tissue, about 99 percent of the gland.","The pancreatic islets, the exocrine tissue, about 99 percent of the gland.","The acini, the endocrine tissue, about 1 percent of the gland."],"correctIndex":0,"why":"On a stained slide the pancreas shows two tissues. The acini are the darker clusters of gland cells around tiny ducts, the exocrine part that makes pancreatic juice and forms about 99 percent of the gland. Scattered among them are the pancreatic islets (islets of Langerhans), pale round patches that are the endocrine part and form about 1 percent of the gland. A disease of the pale patches destroys the islets.","notes":["Correct. The pale patches are the islets, the endocrine part, which make up about 1 percent of the gland.","The acini are the darker clusters around tiny ducts that make up most of the gland. The pale patches scattered among them are the islets.","The islets are the pale patches, but they are the endocrine part and only about 1 percent of the gland. The acini are the exocrine part.","The acini are exocrine, sending pancreatic juice through ducts. The endocrine tissue is the islets, the pale patches."],"comp":124,"competencyId":"w5-pancreas"},{"id":"N-a17d-124-3","dok":3,"q":"A tumor grows in the part of the pancreas that sits in the C-shaped curve of the duodenum. Which region is this, and which duct that is not part of the pancreas runs through it?","a":"The head of the pancreas; the common bile duct.","options":["The tail of the pancreas; the common bile duct.","The head of the pancreas; the common bile duct.","The head of the pancreas; the main pancreatic duct.","The body of the pancreas; the cystic duct."],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The head of the pancreas is its wide end, sitting in the C-shaped curve of the duodenum to the right of the midline. The common bile duct, which carries bile from the liver and gallbladder, runs down behind the first part of the duodenum and through the head of the pancreas. It then joins the main pancreatic duct at the hepatopancreatic ampulla. A tumor in the head lies right beside that duct.","notes":["The tail is the narrow left end near the spleen. The region in the curve of the duodenum is the head.","Correct. The head sits in the curve of the duodenum, and the common bile duct runs through the head on its way to the ampulla.","The region is right, but the main pancreatic duct is the pancreas's own duct. The question asks for the duct from outside the gland.","The body crosses the midline behind the stomach. The cystic duct runs from the neck of the gallbladder and ends where it joins the common hepatic duct, so it never reaches the pancreas."],"comp":124,"competencyId":"w5-pancreas"}]},{"id":"m4-abdominal-vessels","title":"Vessels of the abdomen and pelvis","summary":"abdominal aorta branches, celiac trunk, mesenteric arteries, renal and gonadal vessels, common iliacs, inferior vena cava, hepatic portal system","mw":"2026-11-09","tr":"2026-11-10","cards":[{"id":"L02289","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares the two vessels that bring blood into the liver?","a":"The hepatic artery brings oxygen-rich blood; the hepatic portal vein brings nutrient-rich blood from the digestive organs.","options":["The hepatic portal vein brings oxygen-rich blood from the aorta, and the hepatic artery brings nutrient-rich blood from the intestines","The hepatic veins bring blood in from the intestines, and the hepatic portal vein carries blood out to the heart","The hepatic artery and hepatic portal vein both carry blood out of the liver toward the inferior vena cava","The hepatic artery brings oxygen-rich blood, and the hepatic portal vein brings nutrient-rich blood from the digestive organs"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The liver has an unusual double blood supply. The hepatic artery, which traces back to the celiac trunk, brings oxygen-rich blood for the liver tissue itself. The hepatic portal vein brings blood that has already passed through the capillaries of the stomach, intestines, spleen, and pancreas, so it is rich in absorbed nutrients but low in oxygen. Both empty into the sinusoids, and the blood then leaves through the hepatic veins.","notes":["This swaps the two vessels. Arteries carry oxygen-rich blood from the aorta, so the hepatic artery is the oxygen supply, while the portal vein carries nutrient-rich blood drained from the digestive organs.","This confuses the hepatic veins with the hepatic portal vein. The portal vein brings intestinal blood in, and the hepatic veins carry blood out of the liver to the inferior vena cava and on to the heart.","Both of these vessels bring blood into the liver. The vessels that carry blood out toward the inferior vena cava are the hepatic veins, so pick those for a question about drainage from the liver.","Correct. The hepatic artery carries oxygen-rich blood from the aorta, while the portal vein carries nutrient-rich blood drained from the digestive organs."],"lt":"t-accessory-digestive-organs","lid":"fr10","comp":122,"competencyId":"w5-liver-gross-lobule"},{"id":"L02319","dok":1,"q":"Which vessel delivers nutrient-rich blood from the stomach, intestines, and spleen to the liver?","a":"The hepatic portal vein.","options":["The inferior vena cava","The hepatic portal vein","The superior mesenteric artery","The hepatic veins"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The hepatic portal vein forms behind the pancreas where the splenic vein and superior mesenteric vein join, and it carries blood from the digestive organs and spleen into the liver. This blood has already passed through one capillary bed in the gut and then passes through a second one, the liver sinusoids, which is what makes it a portal system. It lets the liver process absorbed nutrients before the blood returns to the heart.","notes":["The inferior vena cava receives blood from the hepatic veins and returns it to the heart. It would be the answer for the large vein behind the liver, not the vessel bringing gut blood in.","Correct. The hepatic portal vein carries gut and spleen blood to the liver.","The superior mesenteric artery carries oxygen-rich blood from the aorta to the small intestine. It supplies the intestines rather than draining them, so pick it for arterial supply to the small intestine.","The hepatic veins carry blood out of the liver into the inferior vena cava. Pick them for the vessels that drain the liver, the opposite direction from the portal vein."],"lt":"t-accessory-digestive-organs","lid":"c24","comp":126,"competencyId":"w5-lab-abdominal-vessels"},{"id":"L02371","dok":3,"q":"Blood from the stomach and intestines travels through the hepatic portal vein to the liver before it returns to the heart. What does this arrangement allow?","a":"The liver can process absorbed nutrients before that blood reaches the rest of the body.","options":["The intestines receive their oxygen-rich blood supply straight from the liver","Bile can be added to the blood so it travels to the duodenum","The liver can process absorbed nutrients before that blood reaches the rest of the body","Intestinal blood can skip the liver's capillaries and return quickly to the heart"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The hepatic portal system is a portal system, meaning blood passes through two capillary beds in a row before returning to the heart. Blood picks up absorbed nutrients in the capillaries of the gut, then flows through the hepatic portal vein into the liver sinusoids, the second capillary bed. This lets hepatocytes take up, store, and process nutrients and filter the blood before it enters the general circulation.","notes":["The intestines get oxygen-rich blood from arteries off the aorta, such as the superior and inferior mesenteric arteries. Pick those arteries for the blood supply to the intestines.","Bile reaches the duodenum through the bile ducts, not through the blood. Students choose this when they mix up the liver's duct system with its blood supply.","Correct. The portal route lets the liver process nutrients first.","This describes the opposite of a portal system. Portal blood is forced through the liver sinusoids, a second capillary bed, rather than bypassing them."],"lt":"t-accessory-digestive-organs","lid":"d3-t-accessory-digestive-organs-1","comp":126,"competencyId":"w5-lab-abdominal-vessels"},{"id":"N-m4-abdominal-vessels-191","dok":2,"q":"The ovarian arteries reach the ovaries through the suspensory ligaments. From which vessel do they branch?","a":"The abdominal aorta.","options":["The abdominal aorta","The renal artery","The internal iliac artery","The external iliac artery"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The ovarian arteries are the gonadal arteries in females. They branch directly from the abdominal aorta just below the renal arteries and travel down into the pelvis, entering the suspensory ligament to reach the ovary. They start high because the ovaries develop high in the abdomen and move down before birth.","notes":["Correct. The ovarian arteries branch from the abdominal aorta.","The renal artery supplies the kidney. The ovarian arteries arise just below it, but from the aorta. Pick the renal artery for the blood supply of the kidney.","The internal iliac artery supplies the pelvic organs, including the uterus through the uterine artery, but it does not give off the ovarian artery. Pick it for the main artery of the pelvic organs.","The external iliac artery continues into the lower limb as the femoral artery. Pick it for the vessel that supplies the leg."],"comp":126,"competencyId":"w5-lab-abdominal-vessels"},{"id":"L04419","dok":1,"q":"Which three unpaired arteries branch from the front of the abdominal aorta to supply the digestive tract?","a":"The celiac trunk, the superior mesenteric artery, and the inferior mesenteric artery.","options":["Celiac trunk and both mesenteric arteries","Celiac trunk, renal, and splenic arteries","Splenic, common hepatic, and left gastric arteries","Renal, gonadal, and common iliac arteries"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Three single, unpaired arteries leave the front of the abdominal aorta for the gut. The celiac trunk, highest of the three, supplies the stomach, liver, spleen and part of the pancreas. The superior mesenteric artery supplies most of the small intestine and the beginning of the large intestine, and the inferior mesenteric artery supplies the final part of the large intestine.","notes":["Correct. The celiac trunk and the superior and inferior mesenteric arteries are the three unpaired gut branches.","The celiac trunk is correct, but the renal arteries are paired branches to the kidneys and the splenic artery is a branch of the celiac trunk, not of the aorta.","The splenic, common hepatic and left gastric arteries are the three branches of the celiac trunk. Pick this list for a question about what the celiac trunk divides into.","The renal and gonadal arteries are paired branches that leave the sides of the aorta, one to each side of the body, and the common iliac arteries are the aorta's terminal split. This list fits a question about paired branches."],"lt":"t-w5-lab-abdominal-vessels","lid":"w5-lab-abdominal-vessels-r1","comp":126,"competencyId":"w5-lab-abdominal-vessels"},{"id":"L04420","dok":1,"q":"What does the hepatic portal vein carry, and where does it take it?","a":"Nutrient-rich blood from the digestive organs to the liver.","options":["Filtered blood from the kidneys to the inferior vena cava","Oxygenated blood straight from the aorta to the liver","Nutrient-rich blood from the gut organs to the liver","Processed blood from the liver to the inferior vena cava"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"A portal system carries blood through two capillary beds in a row. Blood leaving the capillaries of the stomach, intestines, spleen and pancreas collects into the hepatic portal vein, which carries it to the liver instead of straight back to the heart. In the liver it flows through the sinusoids, the second capillary bed, so absorbed nutrients and toxins are processed before reaching the rest of the body.","notes":["Blood from the kidneys drains through the renal veins directly into the inferior vena cava, without passing through the liver. Choose it for a question about kidney drainage.","Oxygenated blood from the aorta reaches the liver through the hepatic artery, which arises from the celiac trunk. Pick it for a question about the liver's oxygen supply.","Correct. The hepatic portal vein brings nutrient-rich blood from the digestive organs to the liver.","Blood leaving the liver drains through the hepatic veins into the inferior vena cava. That is the answer for the vessels that empty the liver."],"lt":"t-w5-lab-abdominal-vessels","lid":"w5-lab-abdominal-vessels-r2","comp":126,"competencyId":"w5-lab-abdominal-vessels"},{"id":"L04421","dok":2,"q":"The hepatic portal vein forms from the union of which two main veins?","a":"The splenic vein and the superior mesenteric vein.","options":["Splenic and superior mesenteric veins","Right, middle, and left hepatic veins","Left renal vein and left gonadal vein","Right and left common iliac veins"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The hepatic portal vein forms behind the neck of the pancreas, where the splenic vein from the left joins the superior mesenteric vein from the intestines. Together these collect blood from the spleen, stomach, pancreas and intestines. The inferior mesenteric vein usually drains into the splenic vein before this junction.","notes":["Correct. The splenic and superior mesenteric veins join to form the hepatic portal vein.","The hepatic veins drain blood out of the liver into the inferior vena cava, the opposite direction from the portal vein. They would be the answer for the vessels that empty the liver.","The left gonadal vein drains into the left renal vein, which then empties into the inferior vena cava. This pair is about drainage to the vena cava, not the portal system.","The right and left common iliac veins join to form the inferior vena cava, which returns blood from the lower body to the heart. Pick this pair for a question about how the inferior vena cava forms."],"lt":"t-w5-lab-abdominal-vessels","lid":"w5-lab-abdominal-vessels-r3","comp":126,"competencyId":"w5-lab-abdominal-vessels"},{"id":"N-m4-abdominal-vessels-1","dok":1,"q":"Where does the descending aorta become the abdominal aorta?","a":"At the aortic hiatus of the diaphragm.","options":["The esophageal hiatus of the diaphragm","The caval opening of the diaphragm","The level of the sternal angle","The aortic hiatus of the diaphragm"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The descending aorta runs down through the thorax as the thoracic aorta and passes through the diaphragm at the aortic hiatus, an opening at about the level of the T12 vertebra. Below that opening it is called the abdominal aorta. The name changes because the vessel has moved from the thoracic cavity into the abdominal cavity, not because it branches.","notes":["The esophageal hiatus is the opening in the diaphragm that lets the esophagus pass into the abdomen to reach the stomach. Pick it for a question about where the esophagus crosses the diaphragm or where a hiatal hernia occurs.","The caval opening is the hole in the diaphragm that carries the inferior vena cava up into the thorax toward the right atrium. It would be the answer for where the inferior vena cava passes through the diaphragm.","The sternal angle marks the level where the aortic arch begins and ends, so it is where the descending thoracic aorta starts, not the abdominal aorta. Choose it if the question asked where the aortic arch ends.","Correct. The aorta becomes the abdominal aorta once it passes through the aortic hiatus."],"comp":126,"competencyId":"w5-lab-abdominal-vessels"},{"id":"N-m4-abdominal-vessels-2","dok":1,"q":"The abdominal aorta ends by dividing into which pair of arteries?","a":"The right and left common iliac arteries.","options":["Internal and external iliac arteries","Right and left femoral arteries","Right and left common iliac arteries","Right and left renal arteries"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Near the level of the L4 vertebra, the abdominal aorta splits into the right and left common iliac arteries. This split is called the aortic bifurcation. Each common iliac artery then carries blood toward the pelvis and lower limb on its own side.","notes":["The internal and external iliac arteries form one step later, when each common iliac artery divides. Choose this pair if the question asked how a common iliac artery ends.","The femoral arteries are in the thighs. Each one is the continuation of an external iliac artery after it passes under the inguinal ligament, two steps beyond the aortic bifurcation. Pick them for the main artery of the thigh.","Correct. The aorta ends at its bifurcation into the two common iliac arteries.","The renal arteries are paired side branches that leave the aorta higher up, near L1 and L2, to supply the kidneys. The aorta keeps going after they branch, so they are not its end. Pick them for the vessels that carry blood into the kidneys at the hilum."],"comp":126,"competencyId":"w5-lab-abdominal-vessels"},{"id":"N-m4-abdominal-vessels-3","dok":1,"q":"Which artery is the first unpaired branch to leave the abdominal aorta just below the diaphragm?","a":"The celiac trunk.","options":["Celiac trunk","Superior mesenteric artery","Common hepatic artery","Inferior mesenteric artery"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The celiac trunk is a short, wide artery that leaves the front of the abdominal aorta almost as soon as the aorta enters the abdomen, at about T12. It is the first of the three unpaired branches that supply the digestive tract. After a short distance it splits into the left gastric, splenic, and common hepatic arteries.","notes":["Correct. The celiac trunk is the highest of the three unpaired branches.","The superior mesenteric artery is the second unpaired branch, arising just below the celiac trunk at about L1. Pick it for the artery that supplies the small intestine and the first part of the large intestine.","The common hepatic artery does not come straight off the aorta. It is one of the three branches of the celiac trunk and carries blood toward the liver, so choose it for the celiac branch that heads to the liver.","The inferior mesenteric artery is the third and lowest unpaired branch, arising at about L3. It would be the answer for the artery to the descending colon, sigmoid colon, and upper rectum."],"comp":126,"competencyId":"w5-lab-abdominal-vessels"},{"id":"N-m4-abdominal-vessels-4","dok":1,"q":"The celiac trunk divides into which three arteries?","a":"The left gastric, splenic, and common hepatic arteries.","options":["Left gastric, splenic, and common hepatic arteries","Splenic, renal, and suprarenal arteries","Common hepatic, renal, and gonadal arteries","Left gastric, splenic, and superior mesenteric arteries"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The celiac trunk is short and splits almost at once into three arteries. The left gastric artery goes to the stomach along its lesser curvature, the splenic artery runs to the left toward the spleen, and the common hepatic artery heads right toward the liver. Together these three supply the stomach, spleen, liver, gallbladder, and parts of the pancreas and duodenum.","notes":["Correct. These are the three branches of the celiac trunk.","The renal and suprarenal arteries are paired side branches of the abdominal aorta that supply the kidneys and adrenal glands. They come straight off the aorta, so they belong in a list of its paired branches, not the celiac trunk's.","The common hepatic artery is a celiac branch, but the renal and gonadal arteries are paired branches straight off the aorta. Choose renal and gonadal arteries for questions about the kidneys and the testes or ovaries.","The left gastric and splenic arteries are celiac branches, but the superior mesenteric artery is a separate unpaired branch that leaves the aorta just below the celiac trunk. Pick it on its own for the artery to the small intestine."],"comp":126,"competencyId":"w5-lab-abdominal-vessels"},{"id":"N-m4-abdominal-vessels-5","dok":1,"q":"Which branch of the celiac trunk runs to the left along the upper border of the pancreas to reach the spleen?","a":"The splenic artery.","options":["Left gastric artery","Common hepatic artery","Superior mesenteric artery","Splenic artery"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The splenic artery is usually the largest branch of the celiac trunk. It runs to the left along the upper edge of the pancreas, often in a winding path, and enters the spleen at its hilum. Along the way it also sends small branches to the pancreas and to part of the stomach.","notes":["The left gastric artery is the smallest celiac branch. It rises toward the lesser curvature of the stomach and also supplies the lower esophagus. Pick it for the celiac branch that feeds the lesser curvature.","The common hepatic artery runs to the right from the celiac trunk, toward the liver and gallbladder. It goes the opposite direction from the splenic artery, so choose it for the celiac branch heading to the liver.","The superior mesenteric artery is not a celiac branch at all. It leaves the aorta separately, just below the celiac trunk, and supplies the small intestine and the first part of the large intestine.","Correct. The splenic artery travels along the pancreas to the spleen."],"comp":126,"competencyId":"w5-lab-abdominal-vessels"},{"id":"N-m4-abdominal-vessels-6","dok":1,"q":"Which artery supplies the jejunum and ileum?","a":"The superior mesenteric artery.","options":["Inferior mesenteric artery","Internal iliac artery","Superior mesenteric artery","Celiac trunk"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The superior mesenteric artery leaves the front of the aorta just below the celiac trunk and fans out through the mesentery, the fold of peritoneum that holds the small intestine. Its branches supply most of the small intestine, including the jejunum and ileum, plus the cecum, appendix, ascending colon, and most of the transverse colon. It also helps supply the head of the pancreas.","notes":["The inferior mesenteric artery supplies the last part of the large intestine, including the descending colon, sigmoid colon, and upper rectum. It would be the answer for those regions.","The internal iliac artery supplies the pelvic organs, such as the urinary bladder, rectum, and uterus or prostate, along with the gluteal muscles. Choose it for structures in the pelvis.","Correct. The superior mesenteric artery supplies the jejunum and ileum.","The celiac trunk supplies the upper digestive organs: the stomach, spleen, liver, gallbladder, and the first part of the duodenum. Pick it for those organs rather than the jejunum and ileum."],"comp":126,"competencyId":"w5-lab-abdominal-vessels"},{"id":"N-m4-abdominal-vessels-7","dok":1,"q":"Which artery supplies the descending colon, sigmoid colon, and upper rectum?","a":"The inferior mesenteric artery.","options":["Superior mesenteric artery","Celiac trunk","External iliac artery","Inferior mesenteric artery"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The inferior mesenteric artery is the lowest of the three unpaired arteries to the gut, leaving the front of the aorta at about L3. It supplies the last stretch of the large intestine: the far end of the transverse colon, the descending colon, the sigmoid colon, and the upper rectum. Lower parts of the rectum get blood from branches of the internal iliac arteries.","notes":["The superior mesenteric artery supplies the small intestine and the first part of the large intestine, from the cecum through most of the transverse colon. Pick it if the question listed the jejunum, ileum, or ascending colon.","The celiac trunk supplies the upper abdominal organs, including the stomach, spleen, and liver. It would be the answer for those organs, not the lower colon.","The external iliac artery carries blood out of the pelvis toward the lower limb, where it becomes the femoral artery. Choose it for the main route of blood into the leg.","Correct. The inferior mesenteric artery supplies the lower large intestine."],"comp":126,"competencyId":"w5-lab-abdominal-vessels"},{"id":"N-m4-abdominal-vessels-8","dok":1,"q":"Which paired branches of the abdominal aorta enter the kidneys at the hilum?","a":"The renal arteries.","options":["Gonadal arteries","Suprarenal arteries","Lumbar arteries","Renal arteries"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The renal arteries leave the sides of the abdominal aorta at about L1 to L2, usually just below the superior mesenteric artery. Each one travels sideways to its kidney and enters at the hilum, the indented medial border where vessels and the ureter enter and leave. The kidneys receive a large share of the blood the heart pumps out, so these are large arteries.","notes":["The gonadal arteries are long, thin paired branches that leave the aorta below the renal arteries and run down to the testes or ovaries. They would be the answer for blood supply to the gonads.","The suprarenal arteries are small paired branches that supply the adrenal glands, which sit on top of the kidneys. Pick them for blood supply to the adrenal glands.","The lumbar arteries are several small pairs that leave the back of the aorta and supply the posterior abdominal wall, including the muscles and vertebrae of the lower back. Choose them for blood supply to the body wall rather than an organ.","Correct. The renal arteries enter each kidney at its hilum."],"comp":126,"competencyId":"w5-lab-abdominal-vessels"},{"id":"N-m4-abdominal-vessels-11","dok":1,"q":"The external iliac artery changes its name to the femoral artery as it passes under which structure?","a":"The inguinal ligament.","options":["The iliac crest","The linea alba","The inguinal ligament","The obturator foramen"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The inguinal ligament stretches from the anterior superior iliac spine to the pubic tubercle and marks the boundary between the abdomen and the thigh. The external iliac artery runs along the pelvic brim and passes beneath this ligament into the thigh. From that point on, the same vessel is called the femoral artery.","notes":["The iliac crest is the curved upper rim of the ilium that you feel at your hip, and no major artery changes its name there. It would be the answer for the landmark used to find the level of the L4 vertebra.","The linea alba is the band of connective tissue running down the midline of the anterior abdominal wall where the abdominal muscle sheaths meet. It contains no major vessels, so choose it for questions about the abdominal wall instead.","Correct. Below the inguinal ligament the external iliac artery becomes the femoral artery.","The obturator foramen is the large opening in the hip bone that the obturator nerve and vessels pass through to reach the medial thigh. Pick it for the route of the obturator vessels, not the femoral artery."],"comp":127,"competencyId":"w5-lab-lower-limb-vessels-nerves"},{"id":"N-m4-abdominal-vessels-12","dok":2,"q":"Which artery is the main blood supply to the urinary bladder and to the uterus or prostate?","a":"The internal iliac artery.","options":["External iliac artery","Internal iliac artery","Inferior mesenteric artery","Femoral artery"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Each common iliac artery splits into an internal and an external iliac artery. The internal iliac artery dives into the pelvis and supplies the pelvic organs, including the urinary bladder, rectum, and uterus or prostate, as well as the gluteal muscles and perineum. The external iliac artery, by contrast, heads out to the lower limb.","notes":["The external iliac artery is the other branch of the common iliac artery. It runs along the pelvic brim and leaves the pelvis to become the femoral artery, so pick it for blood supply to the lower limb.","Correct. The internal iliac artery supplies the pelvic organs.","The inferior mesenteric artery supplies the descending colon, sigmoid colon, and upper rectum. It does not supply the bladder or reproductive organs, so choose it for questions about the lower large intestine.","The femoral artery is the main artery of the thigh, continuing from the external iliac artery below the inguinal ligament. It would be the answer for blood supply to the thigh, not the pelvic organs."],"comp":126,"competencyId":"w5-lab-abdominal-vessels"},{"id":"N-m4-abdominal-vessels-13","dok":1,"q":"The inferior vena cava begins where which two veins join?","a":"The right and left common iliac veins.","options":["Right and left renal veins","Splenic and superior mesenteric veins","Right and left common iliac veins","Internal and external iliac veins"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The right and left common iliac veins join at about the level of L5, just to the right of the midline, to form the inferior vena cava. From there the inferior vena cava runs up the posterior abdominal wall on the right side of the aorta, collects blood from the lower body, passes through the diaphragm, and empties into the right atrium.","notes":["The renal veins drain the kidneys and empty into the inferior vena cava partway up, at about L1 to L2. They join a vena cava that already exists, so pick them for tributaries of the inferior vena cava, not its starting point.","The splenic and superior mesenteric veins join behind the pancreas to form the hepatic portal vein, which carries blood to the liver rather than straight to the heart. Choose them for the formation of the portal vein.","Correct. The two common iliac veins unite to form the inferior vena cava.","The internal and external iliac veins join one step earlier to form each common iliac vein. Pick this pair if the question asked how a common iliac vein forms."],"comp":126,"competencyId":"w5-lab-abdominal-vessels"},{"id":"N-m4-abdominal-vessels-14","dok":1,"q":"Where does the left gonadal (testicular or ovarian) vein drain?","a":"Into the left renal vein.","options":["Into the left common iliac vein","Directly into the inferior vena cava","Into the left renal vein","Into the inferior mesenteric vein"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The two gonadal veins drain differently because the inferior vena cava sits on the right side of the abdomen. The right gonadal vein empties straight into the inferior vena cava, but the left gonadal vein runs up to the left renal vein and empties into it. The left renal vein then crosses in front of the aorta to reach the inferior vena cava.","notes":["The left common iliac vein drains the left lower limb and pelvis and joins the right common iliac vein to form the inferior vena cava. The gonadal vein rises far above it, so pick it for drainage of the pelvis and leg.","The inferior vena cava is where the right gonadal vein drains directly. Choose it if the question asked about the right side.","Correct. The left gonadal vein empties into the left renal vein.","The inferior mesenteric vein drains the lower large intestine and is part of the hepatic portal system, carrying blood toward the liver. It would be the answer for drainage of the descending and sigmoid colon."],"comp":126,"competencyId":"w5-lab-abdominal-vessels"},{"id":"N-m4-abdominal-vessels-15","dok":1,"q":"Blood leaves the liver through the hepatic veins. Where do the hepatic veins empty?","a":"Into the inferior vena cava.","options":["Into the hepatic portal vein","Into the inferior vena cava","Into the superior vena cava","Into the right renal vein"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"After blood filters through the liver sinusoids, it collects into central veins (small veins at the center of each liver lobule) and then into the hepatic veins, usually three large ones. The hepatic veins leave the back of the liver and empty into the inferior vena cava just below the diaphragm. From there the blood travels a short distance up into the right atrium.","notes":["The hepatic portal vein carries blood into the liver from the digestive organs, so it is upstream of the liver, not downstream. Pick it for the vessel that delivers nutrient-rich blood to the liver.","Correct. The hepatic veins drain into the inferior vena cava.","The superior vena cava drains the head, neck, upper limbs, and chest into the right atrium. The liver is below the heart, so its veins join the inferior vena cava instead. Choose the superior vena cava for drainage of the upper body.","The right renal vein drains the right kidney and is a short tributary of the inferior vena cava. It would be the answer for blood leaving the right kidney."],"comp":126,"competencyId":"w5-lab-abdominal-vessels"},{"id":"N-m4-abdominal-vessels-16","dok":1,"q":"The inferior mesenteric vein usually empties into which vein?","a":"The splenic vein.","options":["The inferior vena cava","The left renal vein","The left common iliac vein","The splenic vein"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The inferior mesenteric vein collects blood from the descending colon, sigmoid colon, and upper rectum. It usually runs upward and empties into the splenic vein behind the pancreas, and the splenic vein then joins the superior mesenteric vein to form the hepatic portal vein. This keeps blood from the lower large intestine in the portal system, so it passes through the liver first.","notes":["The inferior vena cava receives veins from the kidneys, body wall, and liver, but veins from the intestines do not drain into it directly. Choose it for veins like the renal or hepatic veins.","The left renal vein drains the left kidney and also receives the left gonadal vein. Pick it for the vein that the left testicular or ovarian vein empties into.","The left common iliac vein drains the left lower limb and pelvis and helps form the inferior vena cava. It would be the answer for drainage of the left leg, not the colon.","Correct. The inferior mesenteric vein usually joins the splenic vein."],"comp":126,"competencyId":"w5-lab-abdominal-vessels"},{"id":"N-m4-abdominal-vessels-17","dok":1,"q":"What makes the hepatic portal system a portal system?","a":"Blood passes through two capillary beds in a row before returning to the heart.","options":["Its veins have no valves and drain straight into the right atrium","Blood passes through two capillary beds before reaching the heart","Blood passes through the liver before entering any capillary bed","Its veins carry oxygen-rich blood straight from the aorta to the liver"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"A portal system links two capillary beds in a row: blood passes through one capillary bed, collects into a vein, and then enters a second capillary bed before heading back to the heart. In the hepatic portal system, the first capillary bed is in the walls of the stomach, intestines, and other digestive organs, and the second is the sinusoids of the liver. Most veins, by contrast, carry blood from one capillary bed straight back toward the heart.","notes":["Whether veins have valves is not what defines a portal system, and portal blood does not drain straight into the right atrium. It first passes through the liver, then the hepatic veins and the inferior vena cava, so this choice mixes up the portal vein with the vena cava.","Correct. Two capillary beds in series define a portal system.","Portal blood has already passed through one capillary bed in the digestive organs before it reaches the liver. This statement leaves out that first capillary bed, which is half of what makes the system a portal system.","The hepatic portal vein carries oxygen-poor blood that has already passed through the digestive organs. Oxygen-rich blood reaches the liver through the hepatic artery, a branch of the celiac trunk, so this statement describes the hepatic artery, not a portal system."],"comp":126,"competencyId":"w5-lab-abdominal-vessels"},{"id":"N-m4-abdominal-vessels-18","dok":1,"q":"The medial umbilical ligament on the inside of the anterior abdominal wall is a remnant of which fetal vessel?","a":"The umbilical artery.","options":["Umbilical vein","Ductus arteriosus","Umbilical artery","Ductus venosus"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Before birth, two umbilical arteries branch from the internal iliac arteries and carry blood from the fetus out to the placenta. After birth, the far portion of each artery closes off and becomes a fibrous cord, the medial umbilical ligament, which runs up the inside of the anterior abdominal wall toward the umbilicus. The part closest to the internal iliac artery usually stays open and supplies the bladder.","notes":["The umbilical vein carried oxygen-rich blood from the placenta to the fetus. After birth it becomes the ligamentum teres, the round ligament in the free edge of the falciform ligament of the liver, so pick it for the origin of the round ligament.","The ductus arteriosus was a fetal shunt between the pulmonary trunk and the aortic arch that let blood bypass the lungs. It becomes the ligamentum arteriosum in the thorax, so pick it for that remnant.","Correct. Each medial umbilical ligament is what remains of an umbilical artery.","The ductus venosus was a fetal shunt in the liver that let blood from the umbilical vein bypass the liver tissue and reach the inferior vena cava. It becomes the ligamentum venosum, so choose it for that remnant."],"comp":126,"competencyId":"w5-lab-abdominal-vessels"},{"id":"N-m4-abdominal-vessels-19","dok":1,"q":"Which vein runs along the back of the pancreas, carrying blood from the spleen toward the hepatic portal vein?","a":"The splenic vein.","options":["Splenic vein","Inferior mesenteric vein","Left gastric vein","Left renal vein"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The splenic vein leaves the spleen at its hilum and runs to the right along the back of the pancreas. Behind the neck of the pancreas it joins the superior mesenteric vein to form the hepatic portal vein. Along the way it collects blood from the pancreas and part of the stomach, and it usually receives the inferior mesenteric vein as well.","notes":["Correct. The splenic vein runs behind the pancreas to help form the portal vein.","The inferior mesenteric vein drains the lower large intestine and usually empties into the splenic vein. It is a tributary of the splenic vein, so pick it for drainage of the descending and sigmoid colon rather than the spleen.","The left gastric vein drains the lesser curvature of the stomach and usually empties into the hepatic portal vein. It would be the answer for drainage of the lesser curvature, not the spleen.","The left renal vein also crosses the abdomen from left to right, but it drains the left kidney, passes in front of the aorta, and empties into the inferior vena cava. Pick it for the vein that carries blood from the left kidney."],"comp":126,"competencyId":"w5-lab-abdominal-vessels"},{"id":"N-m4-abdominal-vessels-20","dok":2,"q":"Moving from superior to inferior along the abdominal aorta, in what order do the three unpaired branches to the digestive tract arise?","a":"Celiac trunk, superior mesenteric artery, inferior mesenteric artery.","options":["Superior mesenteric artery, celiac trunk, inferior mesenteric artery","Inferior mesenteric artery, superior mesenteric artery, celiac trunk","Celiac trunk, superior mesenteric artery, inferior mesenteric artery","Celiac trunk, inferior mesenteric artery, superior mesenteric artery"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The celiac trunk leaves the aorta first, at about T12, just below the diaphragm. The superior mesenteric artery follows at about L1, and the inferior mesenteric artery arises lower, at about L3. The order matches the parts of the gut they supply: the stomach and liver at the top, then the small intestine and first part of the colon, then the last part of the colon and the rectum.","notes":["This order swaps the first two. The superior mesenteric artery arises just below the celiac trunk, not above it. Students often pick this because the word superior sounds like it should come first.","This is the correct sequence read in reverse, from inferior to superior. Pick it only if the question asked for the order moving up the aorta from the bifurcation.","Correct. The celiac trunk leaves first at about T12, the superior mesenteric follows at about L1, and the inferior mesenteric arises at about L3.","This order puts the inferior mesenteric artery in the middle, but as its name says, it is the lowest of the three. The superior mesenteric artery comes before it."],"comp":126,"competencyId":"w5-lab-abdominal-vessels"},{"id":"N-m4-abdominal-vessels-21","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares the unpaired and paired branches of the abdominal aorta?","a":"Unpaired branches supply the digestive organs; paired branches supply the kidneys, adrenals, gonads, and body wall.","options":["Unpaired branches supply the pelvis and lower limbs; paired branches supply all of the abdominal organs","Unpaired branches supply the digestive organs; paired branches supply the kidneys, adrenals, gonads, and body wall","Unpaired branches supply the kidneys and gonads; paired branches supply the stomach, intestines, liver, and spleen","Unpaired branches supply only the liver and spleen; paired branches supply the small and large intestine"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The three unpaired branches (the celiac trunk and the superior and inferior mesenteric arteries) leave the front of the aorta and supply the digestive tract, its accessory organs, and the spleen. The paired branches leave the sides of the aorta in left and right pairs and supply paired structures: the adrenal glands, kidneys, gonads, and, through the lumbar arteries, the posterior abdominal wall.","notes":["The pelvis and lower limbs are supplied by the common iliac arteries, the two branches at the very bottom of the aorta, not by its unpaired branches. This choice confuses the way the aorta ends with its branches along the way.","Correct. Unpaired branches go to the gut; paired branches go to paired organs and the body wall.","This statement reverses the two groups. The kidneys and gonads are paired organs, so they get paired arteries (renal and gonadal), while the stomach, intestines, liver, and spleen get the unpaired midline branches.","The liver and spleen are supplied by the celiac trunk, but the intestines are also supplied by unpaired branches, the superior and inferior mesenteric arteries. No paired branch supplies the intestines."],"comp":126,"competencyId":"w5-lab-abdominal-vessels"},{"id":"N-m4-abdominal-vessels-22","dok":2,"q":"Where do the renal arteries usually arise along the abdominal aorta?","a":"Just below the superior mesenteric artery.","options":["From the common iliac arteries","Just above the celiac trunk","Just below the superior mesenteric artery","Just below the inferior mesenteric artery"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The renal arteries usually leave the sides of the aorta at about L1 to L2, just below the origin of the superior mesenteric artery. This places them high in the abdomen, level with the kidneys, which sit against the posterior abdominal wall between about T12 and L3. Knowing that the renal arteries sit just below the superior mesenteric artery helps you find them on a model or cadaver.","notes":["The common iliac arteries supply the pelvis and lower limbs and begin at the aortic bifurcation, well below the kidneys. Pick them for the vessels that divide into the internal and external iliac arteries.","At or just above the celiac trunk is where the inferior phrenic arteries usually arise to supply the underside of the diaphragm. The renal arteries come off lower, below both the celiac trunk and the superior mesenteric artery.","Correct. The renal arteries usually arise just below the superior mesenteric artery.","Just below the inferior mesenteric artery would put the renal arteries near L3 or L4, close to the aortic bifurcation and below the kidneys. Students pick this when they mix up the superior and inferior mesenteric arteries."],"comp":126,"competencyId":"w5-lab-abdominal-vessels"},{"id":"N-m4-abdominal-vessels-23","dok":2,"q":"On its way to the inferior vena cava, the left renal vein passes in front of which large vessel?","a":"The abdominal aorta.","options":["The inferior vena cava","The hepatic portal vein","The left common iliac artery","The abdominal aorta"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The inferior vena cava lies to the right of the aorta, so the left renal vein has farther to travel than the right one. It crosses from the left kidney toward the right, passing in front of the abdominal aorta just below the origin of the superior mesenteric artery, and then empties into the inferior vena cava. This is why the left renal vein is longer than the right renal vein.","notes":["The inferior vena cava is the left renal vein's destination, not a vessel it passes in front of. Pick it for the vein that both renal veins empty into.","The hepatic portal vein forms behind the pancreas and runs up to the liver, above and in front of the left renal vein's path. Pick it for the vessel that carries blood from the digestive organs to the liver.","The left common iliac artery begins at the aortic bifurcation near L4, far below the kidneys, and the left renal vein never reaches it. Choose it for blood flow to the left side of the pelvis and the left leg.","Correct. The left renal vein crosses in front of the abdominal aorta."],"comp":126,"competencyId":"w5-lab-abdominal-vessels"},{"id":"N-m4-abdominal-vessels-24","dok":2,"q":"The testicular arteries branch from the abdominal aorta near the kidneys, far above the scrotum. What explains this high starting point?","a":"The testes develop high in the abdomen and descend, bringing their arteries along.","options":["The high origin keeps the testes warmer than the other abdominal organs","The testes develop high in the abdomen and descend, bringing their arteries along","The testicular arteries branch from the renal arteries and only appear to start high","Blood must pass through the kidneys first so it is filtered before reaching the testes"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The testes form on the posterior abdominal wall near the developing kidneys, and they receive their blood supply there. Before birth, each testis descends through the inguinal canal into the scrotum, and its artery, vein, and nerves are pulled along behind it inside the spermatic cord. The ovaries follow a similar but shorter descent into the pelvis, which is why the ovarian arteries also start near the kidneys.","notes":["The scrotum keeps the testes slightly cooler than the body core, not warmer, because sperm production works best a little below 37°C (98.6°F). Where the artery branches has nothing to do with temperature.","Correct. The arteries start where the testes first developed.","The testicular arteries usually come straight off the front of the abdominal aorta, just below the renal arteries. The renal arteries enter the kidneys and do not normally give rise to them, so this choice confuses neighboring branches.","Arterial blood does not pass through the kidneys on its way to other organs. Each organ gets its own branch of the aorta, so this choice mixes up the arteries with the filtering path blood takes inside the kidney itself."],"comp":126,"competencyId":"w5-lab-abdominal-vessels"},{"id":"N-m4-abdominal-vessels-25","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares how the right and left gonadal veins drain?","a":"The right drains into the inferior vena cava; the left drains into the left renal vein.","options":["The right drains into the right renal vein; the left drains into the inferior vena cava","Both gonadal veins empty into the hepatic portal vein","Both gonadal veins empty directly into the inferior vena cava","The right drains into the inferior vena cava; the left drains into the left renal vein"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The inferior vena cava runs up the right side of the abdomen, close to the right gonadal vein, so that vein empties into it directly. The left gonadal vein is farther from the vena cava and ends by joining the left renal vein, which then crosses in front of the aorta to reach the inferior vena cava. The same left and right difference applies to the testicular veins in males and the ovarian veins in females.","notes":["This is the drainage pattern reversed. It is the left gonadal vein that ends in a renal vein, because the inferior vena cava sits on the right side, near the right gonadal vein.","The hepatic portal vein collects blood from the digestive organs and spleen, not from the gonads. Pick it for veins such as the splenic and superior mesenteric veins.","Only the right gonadal vein empties straight into the inferior vena cava. This choice treats the two sides as mirror images, which is the most common mistake on this question.","Correct. The inferior vena cava lies on the right, so the right gonadal vein empties straight into it, while the left gonadal vein drains into the left renal vein."],"comp":126,"competencyId":"w5-lab-abdominal-vessels"},{"id":"N-m4-abdominal-vessels-26","dok":2,"q":"Through its branches, one artery supplies the stomach, spleen, liver, and gallbladder. Which artery is it?","a":"The celiac trunk.","options":["Celiac trunk","Splenic artery","Superior mesenteric artery","Common hepatic artery"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The celiac trunk divides into the left gastric, splenic, and common hepatic arteries, and together those three branches reach every organ in this list. The left gastric and splenic arteries supply the stomach and spleen, and the common hepatic artery supplies the liver and, through its branches, the gallbladder and parts of the stomach and duodenum. No single branch of the celiac trunk covers all four organs, but the trunk itself does.","notes":["Correct. The celiac trunk reaches all four organs through its three branches.","The splenic artery supplies the spleen and also sends branches to the pancreas and part of the stomach, but it does not reach the liver or gallbladder. Pick it for blood flow to the spleen alone.","The superior mesenteric artery supplies the small intestine and the first part of the large intestine. Choose it if the list had named the jejunum, ileum, and ascending colon.","The common hepatic artery supplies the liver and gallbladder and parts of the stomach and duodenum, but not the spleen. It would be the answer if the spleen were left out of the list."],"comp":126,"competencyId":"w5-lab-abdominal-vessels"},{"id":"N-m4-abdominal-vessels-27","dok":2,"q":"The transverse colon receives blood from two arteries. Which pair?","a":"The superior and inferior mesenteric arteries.","options":["Superior and inferior mesenteric arteries","Inferior mesenteric and internal iliac arteries","Celiac trunk and inferior mesenteric artery","Celiac trunk and superior mesenteric artery"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The transverse colon sits at the boundary between the territories of the two mesenteric arteries. The superior mesenteric artery supplies most of the transverse colon, and the inferior mesenteric artery supplies its last part. Connecting vessels run along the colon between the two territories, so each artery can help supply the other's area.","notes":["Correct. The superior mesenteric artery supplies most of the transverse colon, and the inferior mesenteric artery supplies its last part.","The inferior mesenteric and internal iliac arteries share the supply of the rectum, at the far end of the large intestine. Pick this pair for a question about the rectum rather than the transverse colon.","The celiac trunk does not reach the colon. The inferior mesenteric artery in this pair is right for the last part of the transverse colon, but its partner should be the superior mesenteric artery.","The celiac trunk supplies the stomach, spleen, liver, and upper duodenum, not the colon. The duodenum is the organ that gets blood from both the celiac trunk and the superior mesenteric artery, so pick this pair for a question about the duodenum."],"comp":126,"competencyId":"w5-lab-abdominal-vessels"},{"id":"N-m4-abdominal-vessels-28","dok":2,"q":"Glucose is absorbed into a capillary in the wall of the ileum. Which route does it follow on its way to the right atrium?","a":"Superior mesenteric vein, hepatic portal vein, liver sinusoids, hepatic veins, inferior vena cava.","options":["Superior mesenteric vein, inferior vena cava, hepatic portal vein, liver sinusoids, hepatic veins","Superior mesenteric vein, hepatic veins, liver sinusoids, hepatic portal vein, inferior vena cava","Superior mesenteric vein, hepatic portal vein, liver sinusoids, hepatic veins, inferior vena cava","Superior mesenteric artery, hepatic artery, liver sinusoids, hepatic veins, inferior vena cava"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Venous blood from the ileum drains into the superior mesenteric vein, which joins the splenic vein to form the hepatic portal vein. The portal vein delivers the glucose into the liver, where blood spreads through the sinusoids (wide, leaky capillaries between plates of liver cells) so the cells can take up nutrients. Blood then leaves through the hepatic veins into the inferior vena cava, which empties into the right atrium.","notes":["Blood from the intestines does not enter the inferior vena cava before reaching the liver. This route skips the whole point of the portal system, which is to send intestinal blood through the liver first.","This route has the liver vessels in reverse order. The hepatic portal vein carries blood into the liver and the hepatic veins carry it out, so the portal vein must come before the sinusoids and the hepatic veins after them.","Correct. This is the portal route from the ileum through the liver to the heart.","Arteries carry blood toward the intestine, not away from it, so absorbed glucose cannot leave the ileum in the superior mesenteric artery. The hepatic artery does bring oxygen-rich blood into the liver, but it comes from the celiac trunk, not from the intestine."],"comp":126,"competencyId":"w5-lab-abdominal-vessels"},{"id":"N-m4-abdominal-vessels-29","dok":2,"q":"Why does blood from the stomach and intestines pass through the liver before it returns to the heart?","a":"So the liver can process absorbed nutrients and remove toxins first.","options":["So the liver can add oxygen to blood that the intestines have used up","So the liver can pump the blood upward against gravity to the heart","So the liver can process absorbed nutrients and remove toxins first","So the liver can filter out water and form urine from the absorbed fluid"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Blood leaving the digestive tract carries everything that was just absorbed, including nutrients, drugs, alcohol, and toxins. Sending this blood through the liver first lets liver cells take up and store nutrients such as glucose, and break down or remove harmful substances before the blood reaches the rest of the body. Macrophages lining the liver sinusoids also remove bacteria that slip in from the gut.","notes":["The liver does not add oxygen to blood. Oxygen is added in the lungs, and the liver receives its own oxygen-rich blood through the hepatic artery. Students choose this when they mix up the portal vein with the pulmonary circuit.","The liver is not a pump. The heart is the only pump in the circulation, and blood in the portal vein is moved by the pressure that remains after it passes through the gut capillaries. Students choose this when they picture the liver as a second heart.","Correct. The liver gets the first look at everything absorbed from the gut.","Filtering blood and forming urine is the work of the kidneys, not the liver. That would be the answer for why so much blood flows through the renal arteries."],"comp":126,"competencyId":"w5-lab-abdominal-vessels"},{"id":"N-m4-abdominal-vessels-30","dok":2,"q":"A catheter is threaded from the right femoral artery upward toward the heart. Which vessels does it pass through, in order, to reach the abdominal aorta?","a":"The right external iliac artery, then the right common iliac artery.","options":["Right external iliac artery, then right common iliac artery","Right external iliac artery, then right internal iliac artery","Right common iliac artery, then right external iliac artery","Right internal iliac artery, then right common iliac artery"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The femoral artery is the continuation of the external iliac artery below the inguinal ligament, so moving upward the catheter enters the external iliac artery first. The external and internal iliac arteries are the two branches of the common iliac artery, so the catheter next passes into the right common iliac artery. The right and left common iliac arteries meet at the aortic bifurcation, which opens into the abdominal aorta.","notes":["Correct. The femoral artery continues upward as the external iliac artery, which comes off the common iliac artery, which leads straight into the aorta.","This route turns into the internal iliac artery after the external iliac artery, which would send the catheter down into the pelvis instead of up to the aorta. The internal and external iliac arteries are two branches that split from the same common iliac artery.","This is the right pair in the wrong order. Moving upward from the thigh, the catheter reaches the external iliac artery before the common iliac artery. This order describes blood flowing downward from the aorta to the leg.","The internal iliac artery is a side branch that dives into the pelvis to supply the pelvic organs and gluteal muscles. It does not connect to the femoral artery, so it would be on the route only for a catheter aimed at the pelvic organs."],"comp":126,"competencyId":"w5-lab-abdominal-vessels"},{"id":"N-m4-abdominal-vessels-31","dok":2,"q":"Which of these veins does not empty directly into the inferior vena cava?","a":"The superior mesenteric vein.","options":["Hepatic vein","Superior mesenteric vein","Right gonadal vein","Right renal vein"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The superior mesenteric vein drains the small intestine and the first part of the large intestine, and it belongs to the hepatic portal system. It joins the splenic vein to form the hepatic portal vein, so its blood goes through the liver before it can reach the inferior vena cava. Veins from the kidneys, the right gonad, and the liver itself empty straight into the inferior vena cava.","notes":["The hepatic veins carry blood out of the liver and empty directly into the inferior vena cava just below the diaphragm. They are the last step of the portal route, so choose them for the vessels that drain the liver.","Correct. The superior mesenteric vein drains into the portal system, not the vena cava.","The right gonadal vein empties directly into the inferior vena cava. Its partner on the left would fit this question, because the left gonadal vein drains into the left renal vein instead.","The right renal vein is short and empties straight into the inferior vena cava, which sits just beside the right kidney. Pick it for the vein that drains the right kidney."],"comp":126,"competencyId":"w5-lab-abdominal-vessels"},{"id":"N-m4-abdominal-vessels-32","dok":2,"q":"Which two capillary beds does blood pass through in the hepatic portal system, in order?","a":"Capillaries of the digestive organs, then the liver sinusoids.","options":["Capillaries of the lungs, then the liver sinusoids","Capillaries of the kidneys, then the liver sinusoids","Capillaries of the digestive organs, then the liver sinusoids","The liver sinusoids, then capillaries of the digestive organs"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Blood first passes through capillaries in the walls of the stomach, intestines, pancreas, and spleen, where it picks up absorbed nutrients and other substances. It then collects into the hepatic portal vein and enters the second capillary bed, the liver sinusoids, which are wide, leaky capillaries running between plates of liver cells. Only after the sinusoids does the blood leave through the hepatic veins toward the heart.","notes":["The lungs belong to the pulmonary circuit, which carries blood from the right ventricle to the lungs and back to the left atrium. Lung capillaries are where blood picks up oxygen, so choose them for a question about gas exchange.","Kidney blood drains through the renal veins straight into the inferior vena cava, so the kidneys are not part of the hepatic portal system. Pick kidney capillaries for a question about where blood is filtered to form urine.","Correct. Digestive capillaries come first and the liver sinusoids second.","This is the right pair in reverse. Blood reaches the liver after the digestive organs, not before, because the liver's job here is to process what the gut has just absorbed."],"comp":126,"competencyId":"w5-lab-abdominal-vessels"},{"id":"N-m4-abdominal-vessels-33","dok":3,"q":"A clot blocks the superior mesenteric artery near its origin. Which part of the digestive tract is most at risk of losing its blood supply?","a":"The jejunum, ileum, and ascending colon.","options":["The jejunum, ileum, and ascending colon","The stomach and the first part of the duodenum","The esophagus and the stomach","The descending colon and the sigmoid colon"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The superior mesenteric artery supplies most of the small intestine, including the jejunum and ileum, along with the cecum, appendix, ascending colon, and most of the transverse colon. A clot near its origin cuts off flow to all of these branches at once. Without blood, the bowel wall can die, which is why a blocked superior mesenteric artery is a surgical emergency.","notes":["Correct. These regions all depend on the superior mesenteric artery.","The stomach and first part of the duodenum are supplied by branches of the celiac trunk, which arises just above the superior mesenteric artery. They would be at risk if the clot blocked the celiac trunk instead.","The lower esophagus and stomach get blood from the left gastric artery and other celiac branches, not from the superior mesenteric artery. Choose these for a problem with the celiac trunk.","The descending and sigmoid colon are supplied by the inferior mesenteric artery, the lowest unpaired branch. Pick them for a blockage of the inferior mesenteric artery."],"comp":126,"competencyId":"w5-lab-abdominal-vessels"},{"id":"N-m4-abdominal-vessels-34","dok":3,"q":"A clot forms at the origin of the celiac trunk. Which of these organs would lose its main arterial supply?","a":"The spleen.","options":["Ileum","Spleen","Left kidney","Sigmoid colon"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The celiac trunk divides into the left gastric, splenic, and common hepatic arteries. The spleen gets its blood from the splenic artery, so a clot at the start of the celiac trunk cuts off its main supply. The stomach and liver would also lose arterial flow from this trunk, although the liver still receives blood through the hepatic portal vein.","notes":["The ileum is supplied by the superior mesenteric artery, which arises separately just below the celiac trunk. It would be the answer for a superior mesenteric artery blockage.","Correct. The spleen depends on the splenic artery, a celiac branch.","The left kidney is supplied by the left renal artery, a paired branch that leaves the side of the aorta below the celiac trunk. Pick it for a blockage of the left renal artery.","The sigmoid colon is supplied by the inferior mesenteric artery, the lowest of the unpaired branches. Choose it for a blockage of the inferior mesenteric artery."],"comp":126,"competencyId":"w5-lab-abdominal-vessels"},{"id":"N-m4-abdominal-vessels-35","dok":3,"q":"Scarring from cirrhosis makes it hard for blood to flow through the liver. Which vessel would back up first as blood struggles to enter the liver?","a":"The hepatic portal vein.","options":["The left renal vein","The hepatic portal vein","The hepatic veins","The inferior vena cava"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The hepatic portal vein delivers blood from the stomach, intestines, pancreas, and spleen into the liver. When scar tissue narrows the paths through the liver, blood has trouble getting through, so pressure rises upstream in the portal vein and its tributaries. This is called portal hypertension, and it can make the splenic vein and spleen swell as blood backs up.","notes":["The left renal vein drains the left kidney into the inferior vena cava and is not part of the portal system, so liver scarring does not back blood up into it. Choose it for a question about the left kidney or the left gonadal vein.","Correct. The portal vein is upstream of the liver, so pressure builds there first.","The hepatic veins carry blood out of the liver, so they are downstream of the scarring, and blood that cannot get through the liver does not pile up in them. Pick them for the vessels that drain the liver into the inferior vena cava.","The inferior vena cava receives blood after it leaves the liver, so a blockage inside the liver does not back blood up into it. It would back up from a problem above it, such as failure of the right side of the heart."],"comp":126,"competencyId":"w5-lab-abdominal-vessels"},{"id":"N-m4-abdominal-vessels-36","dok":3,"q":"A mass presses on the left renal vein just before it reaches the inferior vena cava. Besides the left kidney, drainage from which organ could be affected?","a":"The left testis or ovary.","options":["The descending colon","The right testis or ovary","The spleen","The left testis or ovary"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The left gonadal vein does not reach the inferior vena cava on its own. It empties into the left renal vein, so anything that blocks the left renal vein near its end also slows drainage from the left testis or ovary. This is one reason swollen veins around the testis (a varicocele) are much more common on the left side.","notes":["The descending colon drains through the inferior mesenteric vein into the portal system, not into the renal veins. It would be affected by a blockage of the inferior mesenteric or hepatic portal vein.","The right gonadal vein empties directly into the inferior vena cava, so pressure on the left renal vein would not affect it. Pick the right side for a question about which gonadal vein empties straight into the inferior vena cava.","The spleen drains through the splenic vein into the hepatic portal vein, which goes to the liver. It has no connection to the left renal vein, so choose it for a blockage of the splenic or portal vein.","Correct. The left gonadal vein drains through the left renal vein."],"comp":126,"competencyId":"w5-lab-abdominal-vessels"},{"id":"N-m4-abdominal-vessels-37","dok":3,"q":"During surgery, the abdominal aorta is clamped just above its bifurcation. Which arteries beyond the clamp would lose their blood flow?","a":"Both common iliac arteries.","options":["Both common iliac arteries","Both renal arteries","The superior mesenteric artery","The celiac trunk and its branches"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The bifurcation, at about L4, is where the aorta ends by dividing into the right and left common iliac arteries. A clamp placed just above it sits below the celiac trunk, the superior mesenteric artery, the renal arteries, and the inferior mesenteric artery, so those branches still fill. The common iliac arteries, and everything they feed in the pelvis and lower limbs, lie beyond the clamp.","notes":["Correct. The common iliac arteries start at the bifurcation, below the clamp, while the renal and mesenteric branches all leave above it.","The renal arteries leave the aorta at about L1 to L2, well above the bifurcation, so they are upstream of a clamp placed this low. They would lose flow only if the clamp were placed above the kidneys.","The superior mesenteric artery arises at about L1, far above the clamp, so it keeps its blood supply. It would be affected by a clamp placed high on the aorta near the diaphragm.","The celiac trunk is the highest unpaired branch, arising just below the diaphragm at about T12. It would lose flow only if the clamp were placed above it, right at the top of the abdominal aorta."],"comp":126,"competencyId":"w5-lab-abdominal-vessels"},{"id":"N-m4-abdominal-vessels-38","dok":3,"q":"A pill is swallowed and the drug is absorbed through the wall of the small intestine. Which organ does the drug reach first after it leaves the intestine?","a":"The liver.","options":["The heart","The kidneys","The liver","The lungs"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Drugs absorbed from the small intestine enter capillaries that drain into the superior mesenteric vein and then the hepatic portal vein. The portal vein delivers them to the liver before they can reach the heart or the rest of the body. Liver cells can break down part of the dose on this first pass, which is one reason some drugs need a higher dose when taken by mouth than when injected.","notes":["The heart is the next stop after the liver, since blood leaves the liver through the hepatic veins and the inferior vena cava to reach the right atrium. The heart would come first for a drug injected into a vein in the arm.","The kidneys receive the drug only after it has passed through the liver, heart, and lungs and been pumped out through the aorta to the renal arteries. Pick them for where a drug is filtered out of the blood into the urine.","Correct. The hepatic portal vein carries the drug to the liver first.","The lungs are reached only after the liver and the right side of the heart, when blood is pumped through the pulmonary trunk. They would be the first organ reached by an inhaled drug."],"comp":126,"competencyId":"w5-lab-abdominal-vessels"},{"id":"N-m4-abdominal-vessels-39","dok":3,"q":"An hour after a large meal, which vessel carries blood with the highest concentration of absorbed glucose?","a":"The hepatic portal vein.","options":["The hepatic portal vein","The hepatic artery","The inferior vena cava","The hepatic veins"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"After a meal, glucose absorbed by the small intestine enters the superior mesenteric vein and then the hepatic portal vein, so the portal vein carries nutrient-rich blood straight from the gut. As this blood passes through the liver sinusoids, liver cells take up much of the extra glucose and store it as glycogen. The blood leaving through the hepatic veins therefore has less glucose than the blood that came in.","notes":["Correct. The portal vein carries freshly absorbed nutrients straight from the gut.","The hepatic artery carries oxygen-rich blood from the celiac trunk into the liver. It has not passed through the intestine, so its glucose level matches ordinary arterial blood. Choose it for the vessel that brings oxygen to the liver.","The inferior vena cava carries blood from the lower body and the liver back to the heart. By the time portal blood reaches it, the liver has already removed much of the glucose, and it is mixed with blood from the legs and kidneys. Pick it for the vein that returns lower body blood to the right atrium.","The hepatic veins carry blood out of the liver after liver cells have removed much of the extra glucose. Pick them for the vessels that drain processed blood from the liver into the inferior vena cava."],"comp":126,"competencyId":"w5-lab-abdominal-vessels"},{"id":"N-m4-abdominal-vessels-40","dok":3,"q":"A clot blocks the left common iliac artery. The left leg turns cold and pale while the right leg stays normal. Which statement best explains this?","a":"The left leg depends on the external iliac artery, a branch of the left common iliac artery.","options":["The left leg depends on the inferior mesenteric artery, which joins the common iliac","The left leg depends on the left renal artery, which branches from the common iliac","The left leg depends on the internal iliac artery, which arises above the common iliac","The left leg depends on the external iliac artery, a branch of the left common iliac"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Each common iliac artery divides into an internal iliac artery for the pelvis and an external iliac artery that continues into the thigh as the femoral artery. Because the left external iliac artery branches from the left common iliac artery, a clot in the common iliac cuts off the main route of blood into the left leg. The right leg is fed by the right common iliac artery, a separate branch at the aortic bifurcation.","notes":["The inferior mesenteric artery leaves the front of the aorta at about L3 and supplies the lower colon and upper rectum. It does not join the common iliac artery or feed the leg, so pick it for the descending and sigmoid colon.","The left renal artery leaves the side of the aorta at about L1 to L2 and supplies the left kidney, not the leg. It does not branch from the common iliac artery, so pick it for blood flow to the kidney.","The internal iliac artery is a branch of the common iliac artery, not a vessel above it, and it supplies the pelvic organs and gluteal region rather than the length of the leg. Choose it for blood supply to the bladder or gluteal muscles.","Correct. The external iliac artery, the leg's main supply, comes off the common iliac artery."],"comp":126,"competencyId":"w5-lab-abdominal-vessels"}]}]},{"id":"m5","n":5,"title":"Module 5. Urinary, Reproductive and Nervous Systems","exam":"Exam 5","extra":false,"topics":[{"id":"m5-kidney","title":"Kidney gross anatomy and blood supply","summary":"location, renal capsule and fat, cortex, medulla, pyramids, columns, calyces, pelvis, hilum, renal blood supply sequence","mw":"2026-11-18","tr":"2026-11-19","cards":[{"id":"L02380","dok":2,"q":"Three layers surround each kidney. Which one is the outer connective tissue layer that anchors the kidney to the abdominal wall?","a":"The renal fascia.","options":["The adipose capsule","The renal capsule","The parietal peritoneum","The renal fascia"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"From deep to superficial, the three layers around each kidney are the renal capsule, the adipose capsule, and the renal fascia. The renal fascia is the outermost of the three, a layer of connective tissue that anchors the kidney and its fat to the abdominal wall. The capsule shapes the kidney, the fat cushions it, and the fascia ties it down.","notes":["The adipose capsule is the layer of fat around the renal capsule that cushions the kidney and helps hold it in place. Pick it for the layer whose loss lets the kidney drop in nephroptosis.","The renal capsule is the smooth fibrous layer lying directly on the kidney's surface. It maintains the kidney's shape, so pick it if the question asked for the innermost covering.","The parietal peritoneum lines the abdominal cavity and passes in front of the kidneys, which is why the kidneys are called retroperitoneal. It is not one of the kidney's own three layers; choose it if the question asked which membrane lies anterior to the kidneys.","Correct. The renal fascia is the outer connective tissue layer that anchors the kidney to the abdominal wall."],"lt":"t-urinary-system","lid":"fr2","comp":133,"competencyId":"w6-kidney-gross"},{"id":"L02381","dok":2,"q":"Which kidney usually sits slightly lower, and why?","a":"The right kidney, because the liver sits above it.","options":["The right kidney, because the stomach sits above it","The left kidney, because the spleen sits above it","The right kidney, because the liver sits above it","The left kidney, because the heart sits above it"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Both kidneys lie retroperitoneally between about T12 and L3, but they are not perfectly level. The liver, the largest organ in the abdomen, fills the upper right side, so the right kidney sits slightly lower than the left. This is one of the questions on the lab structure list, so be ready to say both the side and the reason.","notes":["The right kidney is the lower one, but the stomach is mostly on the left side of the upper abdomen, so it is not the reason. The liver above the right kidney is what pushes it down.","The spleen does sit near the upper left kidney, but it is small compared with the liver, and the left kidney is the higher one. Students who reason by symmetry pick this; remember the liver makes the right side the low side.","Correct. The large liver above the right kidney pushes it slightly lower than the left.","The heart sits in the thorax above the diaphragm, well away from the kidneys, and the left kidney is the higher one. This choice comes from mixing up the left-sided heart with the low kidney."],"lt":"t-urinary-system","lid":"fr3","comp":133,"competencyId":"w6-kidney-gross"},{"id":"L02382","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares the renal cortex and the renal medulla?","a":"The cortex is the outer region holding the renal corpuscles; the medulla is the inner region made of renal pyramids.","options":["The cortex is the inner region made of renal pyramids; the medulla is the outer region holding the renal corpuscles.","The cortex is the outer region holding the renal corpuscles; the medulla is the inner region made of renal pyramids.","The cortex is the fat layer around the kidney; the medulla is the funnel that drains into the ureter.","The cortex and the medulla are both made of renal pyramids, separated by the renal capsule."],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The renal cortex is the outer region of kidney tissue, just beneath the capsule. It holds the renal corpuscles and the convoluted tubules, and it sends renal columns down between the pyramids. The renal medulla is the inner region, made of cone-shaped renal pyramids that hold the nephron loops and collecting ducts. Together the cortex and pyramids form the parenchyma, the working tissue that houses the nephrons.","notes":["This statement swaps the two regions. Cortex comes from the Latin word for bark, the outer layer of a tree, while the medulla is the inner core, so the pyramids belong to the medulla.","Correct. The cortex is the outer region with the renal corpuscles, and the medulla is the inner region of pyramids.","These descriptions belong to other structures. The fat layer is the adipose capsule, and the funnel that drains into the ureter is the renal pelvis, so choose those names if the question asked what cushions the kidney or what passes urine to the ureter.","Only the medulla is made of pyramids, and the renal capsule is the fibrous covering on the outside of the kidney, not a divider inside it. Pick the renal capsule if the question asked for the kidney's outer fibrous covering."],"lt":"t-urinary-system","lid":"fr4","comp":134,"competencyId":"w6-kidney-internal"},{"id":"L02387","dok":3,"q":"Which sequence correctly traces blood through the arteries of the kidney, from the renal artery to the glomerulus?","a":"Segmental, interlobar, arcuate, cortical radiate, afferent arteriole.","options":["Segmental, arcuate, interlobar, cortical radiate, afferent arteriole","Segmental, interlobar, arcuate, cortical radiate, afferent arteriole","Segmental, interlobar, cortical radiate, arcuate, afferent arteriole","Interlobar, segmental, arcuate, cortical radiate, efferent arteriole"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The renal artery enters at the hilum and splits into segmental arteries, which give off interlobar arteries that run between the renal pyramids. At the bases of the pyramids these bend into arcuate arteries, which arch along the border between cortex and medulla. Cortical radiate (interlobular) arteries then branch up into the cortex, and each one gives off afferent arterioles that feed the glomeruli. The names help: interlobar means between the lobes, and arcuate means arched.","notes":["This order places the arcuate arteries before the interlobar arteries. The interlobar arteries climb between the pyramids first, and only at the pyramid bases do they arch over as arcuate arteries.","Correct. Segmental, interlobar, arcuate, cortical radiate, then the afferent arteriole into the glomerulus.","This order has the cortical radiate arteries before the arcuate arteries. The cortical radiate arteries branch up into the cortex from the arcuate arteries, so they come after them.","This order starts with interlobar instead of segmental and ends with the efferent arteriole. The efferent arteriole carries blood out of the glomerulus, so it would be the answer for the vessel leaving the glomerulus."],"lt":"t-urinary-system","lid":"fr9","comp":138,"competencyId":"w6-kidney-bloodsupply"},{"id":"L02388","dok":2,"q":"Which group of structures enters or leaves the kidney at the renal hilum?","a":"The renal artery, renal vein, ureter, and nerves.","options":["Renal artery, renal vein, ureter, and nerves","Renal artery, renal vein, suprarenal gland, and ureter","Collecting ducts, nephron loops, and arcuate arteries","Renal artery, renal vein, urethra, and nerves"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The renal hilum is the notch on the medial border of the kidney, and it is the kidney's only doorway. The renal artery enters there, while the renal vein and the ureter (continuing from the renal pelvis) leave there, along with the nerves and lymphatic vessels that serve the kidney. Everything that connects the kidney to the rest of the body passes through this one notch.","notes":["Correct. The renal artery, renal vein, ureter, and nerves all pass through the hilum.","The suprarenal (adrenal) gland sits like a cap on the superior pole of the kidney, not at the hilum. Pick it if the question asked which gland rests on top of each kidney.","These structures lie inside the kidney tissue. Collecting ducts and nephron loops run in the pyramids, and arcuate arteries arch along the pyramid bases, so they never reach the hilum.","The urethra does not touch the kidney at all. It is the tube that carries urine from the bladder to the outside; the tube leaving the hilum is the ureter, which carries urine to the bladder."],"lt":"t-urinary-system","lid":"fr10","comp":133,"competencyId":"w6-kidney-gross"},{"id":"L02395","dok":1,"q":"Which paired organs filter the blood and form urine?","a":"The kidneys.","options":["The urinary bladder","The ureters","The urethra","The kidneys"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The kidneys are the paired, bean-shaped organs against the back wall of the abdomen that filter the blood and form urine. Inside each one, about a million nephrons do the actual filtering. Every other organ of the urinary system only carries or stores the urine the kidneys make.","notes":["The urinary bladder is a single muscular sac that stores urine until it is released. It would be the answer if the question asked where urine is held.","The ureters are paired too, but they are tubes that carry urine from the kidneys to the bladder by peristalsis. Pick them if the question asked what transports urine down to the bladder.","The urethra is a single tube that carries urine from the bladder to the outside. Choose it if the question asked which structure releases urine from the body.","Correct. The kidneys filter the blood and form urine."],"lt":"t-urinary-system","lid":"c1","comp":133,"competencyId":"w6-kidney-gross"},{"id":"L02396","dok":1,"q":"What is the smooth, fibrous layer that lies directly on the kidney's surface and maintains its shape?","a":"The renal capsule.","options":["The renal capsule","The adipose capsule","The renal cortex","The renal fascia"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The renal capsule is the innermost of the three layers around the kidney. It is a smooth, fibrous covering that sits right on the kidney's surface, holds its shape, and helps protect the tissue beneath it. Outside it lie the adipose capsule and then the renal fascia.","notes":["Correct. The renal capsule is the smooth fibrous layer on the kidney's surface.","The adipose capsule is the fat layer between the renal capsule and the renal fascia. Choose it if the question asked which layer cushions the kidney and holds it in place.","The renal cortex is kidney tissue, the outer region just beneath the capsule that holds the renal corpuscles. Pick it if the question asked for the outer tissue region of the kidney rather than its covering.","The renal fascia is also connective tissue, but it is the outermost of the three layers and anchors the kidney to the abdominal wall. It would be the answer for the anchoring layer."],"lt":"t-urinary-system","lid":"c2","comp":133,"competencyId":"w6-kidney-gross"},{"id":"L02400","dok":1,"q":"Which vessel branches directly from the abdominal aorta to bring blood to the kidney?","a":"The renal artery.","options":["The efferent arteriole","The renal artery","The renal vein","The afferent arteriole"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The renal artery branches from the abdominal aorta and enters the kidney at the hilum. Inside the kidney it divides into smaller and smaller arteries until afferent arterioles deliver blood to each glomerulus. Because the kidneys filter the blood, they receive a large share of the blood the heart pumps.","notes":["The efferent arteriole carries blood out of the glomerulus toward the peritubular capillaries. It would be the answer for the vessel leaving the glomerulus.","Correct. The renal artery comes straight off the abdominal aorta.","The renal vein carries blood out of the kidney at the hilum and drains into the inferior vena cava. Choose it if the question asked which vessel drains the kidney.","The afferent arteriole is a tiny vessel at the far end of the arterial chain, delivering blood into one glomerulus. Pick it if the question asked which vessel feeds a glomerulus."],"lt":"t-urinary-system","lid":"c6","comp":138,"competencyId":"w6-kidney-bloodsupply"},{"id":"L02404","dok":1,"q":"What is the tip of a renal pyramid, where urine drains out, called?","a":"The renal papilla.","options":["The renal hilum","The minor calyx","The renal papilla","The renal column"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Each renal pyramid is a cone with its broad base toward the cortex and its tip pointing toward the center of the kidney. That tip is the renal papilla. Papillary ducts open on its surface, and urine drains from there into a minor calyx.","notes":["The renal hilum is the notch on the medial border of the kidney where the vessels, ureter, and nerves pass. It would be the answer for the kidney's doorway, not the tip of a pyramid.","The minor calyx is the cup that catches urine from the papilla, so it sits just beyond the tip rather than being the tip itself. Choose it if the question asked what receives urine from the papilla.","Correct. The renal papilla is the tip of the pyramid.","A renal column is an extension of cortical tissue that dips down between two pyramids. Pick it if the question asked what separates neighboring pyramids."],"lt":"t-urinary-system","lid":"c10","comp":134,"competencyId":"w6-kidney-internal"},{"id":"L02406","dok":1,"q":"What is the notch where the renal artery, renal vein, and ureter connect to the kidney?","a":"The renal hilum.","options":["The renal hilum","The trigone","The renal column","The renal papilla"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The renal hilum is the notch on the kidney's medial border. It is the only place where structures enter or leave the kidney: the renal artery comes in, and the renal vein and ureter go out, along with nerves and lymphatic vessels. Other organs, such as the lungs, also have a hilum that serves the same doorway role.","notes":["Correct. The renal hilum is where the vessels and ureter connect.","The trigone is a triangular area on the floor of the urinary bladder, not part of the kidney. It would be the answer for the region marked by the ureter openings and the urethral opening.","A renal column is cortical tissue that dips down between pyramids inside the kidney. Choose it if the question asked what lies between two renal pyramids.","The renal papilla is the tip of a renal pyramid, deep inside the kidney, where urine drains into a minor calyx. Pick it if the question asked where urine leaves a pyramid."],"lt":"t-urinary-system","lid":"c12","comp":133,"competencyId":"w6-kidney-gross"},{"id":"L02407","dok":2,"q":"The renal pyramids are found in which region of the kidney?","a":"The renal medulla.","options":["The renal pelvis","The renal medulla","The renal capsule","The renal cortex"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The renal medulla is the inner region of kidney tissue, and it is made of cone-shaped renal pyramids. Each pyramid points its base toward the cortex and its tip, the renal papilla, toward the center of the kidney. The pyramids look striped because they are packed with nephron loops and collecting ducts running in parallel.","notes":["The renal pelvis is a hollow funnel at the center of the kidney that collects urine, so it holds urine rather than tissue. Choose it if the question asked where the calyces drain.","Correct. The pyramids make up the renal medulla.","The renal capsule is the fibrous covering on the kidney's surface, not a region of tissue. It would be the answer for the layer that maintains the kidney's shape.","The renal cortex is the outer tissue region, which holds the renal corpuscles and dips between the pyramids as renal columns. Pick it if the question asked where the glomeruli are found."],"lt":"t-urinary-system","lid":"c13","comp":134,"competencyId":"w6-kidney-internal"},{"id":"L02408","dok":2,"q":"The renal columns that dip down between the renal pyramids are extensions of which tissue?","a":"Cortical tissue.","options":["Adipose tissue","Fibrous capsule tissue","Medullary tissue","Cortical tissue"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The renal columns are extensions of the renal cortex that reach down between neighboring pyramids. Because the columns are cortex, the medulla is broken up into separate cone-shaped pyramids rather than forming one solid inner layer. The arteries that run between the pyramids travel in these columns.","notes":["Adipose tissue forms the adipose capsule that cushions the kidney from the outside. Choose it if the question asked which layer holds the kidney in place and is lost in nephroptosis.","Fibrous capsule tissue forms the renal capsule on the outside of the kidney. It would be the answer for the smooth covering that maintains the kidney's shape.","Medullary tissue makes up the pyramids themselves, which the columns separate. Pick it if the question asked what the renal pyramids are made of.","Correct. The renal columns are extensions of the cortex."],"lt":"t-urinary-system","lid":"c14","comp":134,"competencyId":"w6-kidney-internal"},{"id":"L02410","dok":2,"q":"The kidneys are described as retroperitoneal. What does that tell you about where they lie?","a":"They lie behind the parietal peritoneum, against the posterior abdominal wall.","options":["They lie in the thoracic cavity, behind the pleura.","They hang inside the peritoneal cavity, suspended by a mesentery.","They lie behind the parietal peritoneum, against the posterior abdominal wall.","They sit low in the pelvic cavity, behind the urinary bladder."],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Retro means behind, so retroperitoneal organs lie behind the peritoneum, the serous membrane that lines the abdominal cavity. The kidneys sit against the posterior abdominal wall, between about T12 and L3, with only the parietal peritoneum passing in front of them. Instead of hanging from a mesentery, each kidney is held in place by its own fat and fascia.","notes":["The thoracic cavity holds the lungs and heart above the diaphragm, and the pleura covers the lungs. The kidneys are below the diaphragm, so this choice confuses the abdomen with the chest.","Organs that hang inside the peritoneal cavity on a mesentery, such as most of the small intestine, are called intraperitoneal. Pick this description if the question asked about an organ like the jejunum.","Correct. Retroperitoneal means behind the peritoneum, against the posterior abdominal wall.","The kidneys sit high in the abdomen, near the lowest ribs, not in the pelvis. The urinary bladder is the urinary organ found low in the pelvic cavity."],"lt":"t-urinary-system","lid":"c16","comp":133,"competencyId":"w6-kidney-gross"},{"id":"L02411","dok":2,"q":"Which chambers collect urine directly from the renal papillae?","a":"The minor calyces.","options":["The major calyces","The collecting ducts","The minor calyces","The renal pelvis"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Each minor calyx is a small cup that fits around the tip of a renal papilla and catches the urine draining out of it. Several minor calyces join to form a major calyx, and the major calyces empty into the renal pelvis. The sequence runs from small to large: minor calyx, major calyx, renal pelvis.","notes":["The major calyces form when several minor calyces join, so they receive urine one step after the minor calyces. Pick them if the question asked what drains into the renal pelvis.","The collecting ducts carry fluid down through the pyramids toward the papillae, so they deliver urine to the papillae rather than collect it from them. Choose them if the question asked which ducts gather fluid from many nephrons.","Correct. The minor calyces catch urine straight from the papillae.","The renal pelvis is the funnel that gathers urine from the major calyces and passes it to the ureter. It would be the answer for the last collecting space before the ureter."],"lt":"t-urinary-system","lid":"c17","comp":134,"competencyId":"w6-kidney-internal"},{"id":"L02412","dok":2,"q":"Which vessel carries blood directly into a glomerulus?","a":"The afferent arteriole.","options":["The efferent arteriole","The afferent arteriole","The peritubular capillaries","The cortical radiate artery"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The afferent arteriole is the last vessel in the kidney's arterial chain, and it delivers blood straight into the glomerulus. Afferent means carrying toward, the same word used for sensory neurons that carry signals toward the brain. Blood then leaves the glomerulus through the efferent arteriole, meaning carrying away.","notes":["The efferent arteriole carries blood out of the glomerulus, the opposite direction. Pick it if the question asked which vessel leaves the glomerulus and leads to the peritubular capillaries.","Correct. The afferent arteriole carries blood into the glomerulus.","The peritubular capillaries are the second capillary bed, wrapping the renal tubules after blood leaves through the efferent arteriole. Choose them if the question asked which capillaries surround the tubules.","The cortical radiate (interlobular) artery runs up into the cortex and gives off the afferent arterioles, so it is one step upstream. It would be the answer for the artery that branches into afferent arterioles."],"lt":"t-urinary-system","lid":"c18","comp":138,"competencyId":"w6-kidney-bloodsupply"},{"id":"L02413","dok":2,"q":"After blood leaves the efferent arteriole, which capillaries wrap around the convoluted tubules in the cortex?","a":"The peritubular capillaries.","options":["The cortical radiate veins","The glomerular capillaries","The vasa recta","The peritubular capillaries"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Blood in the kidney passes through two capillary beds in a row. The first is the glomerulus, where filtration happens. The efferent arteriole then carries the blood to the second bed, the peritubular capillaries, which wrap the convoluted tubules in the cortex. Peritubular means around the tubules.","notes":["The cortical radiate veins are veins that drain blood away from the capillaries toward the arcuate veins. They would be the answer for the first veins blood enters after the peritubular capillaries.","The glomerular capillaries are the first capillary bed, the filtering tuft inside the glomerular capsule. They come before the efferent arteriole, so pick them if the question asked where filtration happens.","The vasa recta also come from efferent arterioles, but they are long, straight capillaries that run deep into the medulla beside the loops of juxtamedullary nephrons. Choose them if the question asked about capillaries in the medulla.","Correct. The peritubular capillaries wrap the convoluted tubules after the efferent arteriole."],"lt":"t-urinary-system","lid":"c19","comp":138,"competencyId":"w6-kidney-bloodsupply"},{"id":"L02418","dok":2,"q":"Which structure is the funnel that gathers urine from the major calyces and passes it to the ureter?","a":"The renal pelvis.","options":["The renal pelvis","The renal cortex","The renal papilla","The minor calyx"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The renal pelvis is the funnel-shaped cavity at the center of the kidney. The major calyces empty into it, and it narrows at the hilum to become the ureter. It is the last collecting space inside the kidney before urine leaves for the bladder.","notes":["Correct. The renal pelvis funnels urine into the ureter.","The renal cortex is the outer region of kidney tissue, holding the renal corpuscles. Pick it if the question asked where the glomeruli are.","The renal papilla is the tip of a pyramid, where urine first leaves the kidney tissue. It sits upstream of the calyces, so choose it if the question asked where papillary ducts open.","The minor calyx is the small cup that catches urine from a papilla, two steps before the renal pelvis. It would be the answer for the first chamber urine enters after leaving a pyramid."],"lt":"t-urinary-system","lid":"c24","comp":134,"competencyId":"w6-kidney-internal"},{"id":"L02425","dok":2,"q":"Which vessel carries blood out of the glomerulus?","a":"The efferent arteriole.","options":["The efferent arteriole","The cortical radiate artery","The peritubular capillaries","The afferent arteriole"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Blood leaves the glomerulus through the efferent arteriole, meaning the arteriole that carries away. Unusually, blood leaving this capillary bed is still in an arteriole rather than a venule. The efferent arteriole then leads to a second capillary bed, the peritubular capillaries and vasa recta, around the renal tubules.","notes":["Correct. The efferent arteriole carries blood out of the glomerulus.","The cortical radiate (interlobular) artery runs up into the cortex and gives off afferent arterioles, upstream of the glomerulus. It would be the answer for the artery that branches into afferent arterioles.","The peritubular capillaries receive blood from the efferent arteriole, so they are one step downstream. Choose them if the question asked which capillaries wrap the convoluted tubules.","The afferent arteriole carries blood into the glomerulus, the opposite direction. Pick it if the question asked which vessel feeds the glomerulus."],"lt":"t-urinary-system","lid":"c31","comp":138,"competencyId":"w6-kidney-bloodsupply"},{"id":"L02438","dok":3,"q":"Which sequence correctly traces blood from the renal artery through the filtering vessels to the renal vein?","a":"Renal artery, afferent arteriole, glomerulus, efferent arteriole, peritubular capillaries, renal vein.","options":["Renal artery, glomerulus, afferent arteriole, efferent arteriole, peritubular capillaries, renal vein","Renal artery, afferent arteriole, glomerulus, efferent arteriole, peritubular capillaries, renal vein","Renal artery, efferent arteriole, glomerulus, afferent arteriole, peritubular capillaries, renal vein","Renal artery, afferent arteriole, efferent arteriole, glomerulus, peritubular capillaries, renal vein"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Blood reaches each nephron through an afferent arteriole, which empties into the glomerulus, the first capillary bed, where filtration happens. It leaves the glomerulus through the efferent arteriole, flows into the second capillary bed, the peritubular capillaries around the tubules, and then drains through a chain of veins to the renal vein. The efferent arteriole is the vessel between the two capillary beds.","notes":["This order places the glomerulus before the afferent arteriole. The afferent arteriole is what delivers blood into the glomerulus, so it has to come first.","Correct. Afferent arteriole, glomerulus, efferent arteriole, peritubular capillaries.","This order reverses the two arterioles. Afferent means carrying toward and efferent means carrying away, so the afferent arteriole must come before the glomerulus.","This order puts both arterioles before the glomerulus. The glomerulus sits between them, with the afferent arteriole feeding it and the efferent arteriole draining it."],"lt":"t-urinary-system","lid":"c44","comp":138,"competencyId":"w6-kidney-bloodsupply"},{"id":"L02439","dok":3,"q":"A frontal section of the kidney shows the outer tissue region, the inner tissue region, and the funnel that empties into the ureter. Which sequence names them from outermost to innermost?","a":"Renal cortex, renal medulla, renal pelvis.","options":["Renal medulla, renal cortex, renal pelvis","Renal pelvis, renal cortex, renal medulla","Renal cortex, renal pelvis, renal medulla","Renal cortex, renal medulla, renal pelvis"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"On a frontal section, the renal cortex forms the outer band of tissue just under the capsule. Deep to it is the renal medulla, made of cone-shaped pyramids with their tips pointing inward. At the center, the calyces open into the renal pelvis, the funnel that narrows into the ureter at the hilum. Tissue regions are on the outside and the collecting spaces are in the middle.","notes":["This order puts the medulla on the outside. The cortex is the outer region, and the pyramids of the medulla lie deep to it, pointing toward the center.","This order starts with the renal pelvis, which is actually the innermost space. The pelvis sits at the center of the kidney where the calyces drain, so it belongs last.","This order places the renal pelvis between the two tissue regions. The pelvis is a collecting space at the very center, so the medulla must come before it.","Correct. Cortex, then medulla, then renal pelvis."],"lt":"t-urinary-system","lid":"c45","comp":134,"competencyId":"w6-kidney-internal"},{"id":"L02448","dok":1,"q":"Between which vertebral levels do the kidneys lie?","a":"Between T12 and L3.","options":["Between S1 and S3","Between T12 and L3","Between L3 and L5","Between T6 and T10"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The kidneys lie retroperitoneally against the posterior abdominal wall, between about T12 and L3. Their upper ends are protected by the lowest ribs. The right kidney sits slightly lower than the left because the liver lies above it.","notes":["The sacral vertebrae form the back of the pelvis, where the urinary bladder and rectum lie. This choice confuses the kidneys with the pelvic organs of the urinary system.","Correct. The kidneys lie between about T12 and L3.","This range is too low. The lower lumbar vertebrae sit near the level of the iliac crests, below the kidneys, so this choice confuses the kidneys with the lower abdomen.","This range is too high. The T6 to T10 vertebrae lie mostly behind the thoracic cavity, at the level of the heart and lower lungs, above where the kidneys sit."],"lt":"t-urinary-system","lid":"c9-x2","comp":133,"competencyId":"w6-kidney-gross"},{"id":"L02449","dok":1,"q":"On which part of the kidney is the renal hilum found?","a":"The medial border.","options":["The medial border","The inferior pole","The lateral border","The superior pole"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The renal hilum is a notch on the medial (concave) border of the kidney, the side that faces the vertebral column. This is where the ureter, renal vessels, and nerves enter and leave, which keeps them close to the aorta and inferior vena cava running along the spine. The notch is what gives the kidney its bean shape.","notes":["Correct. The hilum is on the medial border, facing the spine.","The inferior pole is the lower, rounded end of the kidney. This choice usually comes from picturing the ureter hanging straight down, but the ureter leaves from the medial notch.","The lateral border is the smooth, convex outer edge of the kidney, facing away from the spine. It has no opening, so pick it only if the question asked for the outer curved edge.","The superior pole is the upper end of the kidney, where the suprarenal (adrenal) gland sits like a cap. It would be the answer for where the adrenal gland rests."],"lt":"t-urinary-system","lid":"c10-x2","comp":133,"competencyId":"w6-kidney-gross"},{"id":"L02450","dok":1,"q":"Which sequence names the three layers around the kidney from innermost to outermost?","a":"Renal capsule, adipose capsule, renal fascia.","options":["Renal fascia, adipose capsule, renal capsule","Renal cortex, renal medulla, renal pelvis","Renal capsule, renal fascia, adipose capsule","Renal capsule, adipose capsule, renal fascia"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Three layers cushion and anchor each kidney. The renal capsule is the smooth fibrous layer on the kidney's surface that maintains its shape. Around it is the adipose capsule, a layer of fat that cushions the kidney and holds it in place. Outermost is the renal fascia, connective tissue that anchors the kidney to the abdominal wall.","notes":["This is the correct list read in the opposite direction, from outermost to innermost. It would be right if the question asked you to start at the abdominal wall.","The cortex, medulla, and renal pelvis are regions inside the kidney, not the layers around it. Pick this list if the question asked what a frontal section shows from outside to center.","This order puts the renal fascia in the middle. The fat of the adipose capsule lies between the capsule and the fascia, so the fascia is the outermost layer.","Correct. Renal capsule, then adipose capsule, then renal fascia."],"lt":"t-urinary-system","lid":"c11-x2","comp":133,"competencyId":"w6-kidney-gross"},{"id":"L02455","dok":2,"q":"Blood enters the kidney through the renal artery, which splits into segmental arteries in the renal sinus. Which arteries come next, running up through the renal columns between the pyramids?","a":"The interlobar arteries.","options":["The afferent arterioles","The cortical radiate arteries","The interlobar arteries","The arcuate arteries"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Inside the renal sinus, the renal artery divides into segmental arteries. These give off interlobar arteries, which run up through the renal columns between the renal pyramids. Interlobar means between the lobes, and each lobe is one pyramid with the cortex around it. At the bases of the pyramids the interlobar arteries turn into arcuate arteries.","notes":["The afferent arterioles are the last arterial vessels in the chain, each delivering blood into one glomerulus. Choose them if the question asked which vessel feeds a glomerulus.","The cortical radiate (interlobular) arteries branch from the arcuate arteries up into the cortex. They would be the answer for the arteries that give off the afferent arterioles.","Correct. The interlobar arteries branch from the segmental arteries and run through the renal columns between the pyramids.","The arcuate arteries come one step later, arching along the bases of the pyramids at the border of the cortex and medulla. Pick them if the question asked which arteries run along the pyramid bases."],"lt":"t-urinary-system","lid":"c16-x2","comp":138,"competencyId":"w6-kidney-bloodsupply"},{"id":"L02456","dok":2,"q":"Which arteries branch from the arcuate arteries up into the cortex and give off the afferent arterioles?","a":"The cortical radiate (interlobular) arteries.","options":["The efferent arterioles","The interlobar arteries","The cortical radiate (interlobular) arteries","The segmental arteries"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The cortical radiate arteries, also called interlobular arteries, radiate outward from the arcuate arteries into the cortex. Along their length they give off the afferent arterioles that feed the glomeruli. Watch the look-alike names: interlobar arteries run between the pyramids, while interlobular arteries run up into the cortex.","notes":["The efferent arterioles carry blood out of the glomeruli, after the afferent arterioles. Choose them if the question asked which vessel leaves the glomerulus.","The interlobar arteries run between the pyramids before the arcuate arteries, so they come earlier in the chain. The name differs from interlobular by only a few letters; pick interlobar if the question asked which arteries pass between pyramids.","Correct. The cortical radiate (interlobular) arteries give off the afferent arterioles.","The segmental arteries are the first branches of the renal artery inside the kidney. They would be the answer for the vessels the renal artery divides into right after the hilum."],"lt":"t-urinary-system","lid":"c17-x2","comp":138,"competencyId":"w6-kidney-bloodsupply"},{"id":"L02457","dok":2,"q":"Several minor calyces join to form which larger chamber?","a":"A major calyx.","options":["The renal pelvis","The renal sinus","A major calyx","The ureter"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Each minor calyx catches urine from one renal papilla, and several minor calyces merge into a major calyx. The major calyces in turn empty into the renal pelvis. Calyx means cup, and the chambers grow larger as urine moves from minor calyx to major calyx to renal pelvis.","notes":["The renal pelvis receives urine from the major calyces, so it is one step beyond the chamber the minor calyces form. Pick it if the question asked where the major calyces drain.","The renal sinus is the space inside the kidney, deep to the hilum, that holds the calyces, renal pelvis, vessels, and fat. It surrounds the calyces rather than being formed by them.","Correct. Minor calyces join to form a major calyx.","The ureter begins where the renal pelvis narrows at the hilum, two steps after the minor calyces. Choose it if the question asked which tube carries urine out of the kidney."],"lt":"t-urinary-system","lid":"c18-x2","comp":134,"competencyId":"w6-kidney-internal"},{"id":"L02470","dok":3,"q":"A kidney biopsy needs to include renal corpuscles for the pathologist to examine. Which region of the kidney must the needle sample?","a":"The renal cortex.","options":["The renal medulla","The renal pelvis","The renal sinus","The renal cortex"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"All of the renal corpuscles, the glomeruli inside their capsules, sit in the renal cortex, the outer region of kidney tissue. The medulla holds the nephron loops and collecting ducts, but no corpuscles. So a sample that must show glomeruli has to come from the cortex.","notes":["The renal medulla is made of pyramids holding the nephron loops and collecting ducts, with no renal corpuscles. Pick it if the question asked where the long loops of juxtamedullary nephrons reach.","The renal pelvis is a hollow funnel that collects urine, so it holds no nephrons at all. It would be the answer if the question asked where kidney stones often form.","The renal sinus is the space deep to the hilum that holds the renal pelvis, calyces, vessels, and fat. It contains no nephron tissue; choose it if the question asked what space the hilum opens into.","Correct. All renal corpuscles are in the renal cortex."],"lt":"t-urinary-system","lid":"d3-t-urinary-system-1","comp":134,"competencyId":"w6-kidney-internal"},{"id":"L02471","dok":3,"q":"Unlike most capillary beds, the glomerulus drains into an arteriole instead of a venule. Where does blood in that efferent arteriole go next?","a":"Into a second capillary bed around the renal tubules.","options":["Back into the afferent arteriole","Directly into the renal vein","Into the capsular space with the filtrate","Into a second capillary bed around the renal tubules"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Blood in the kidney meets two capillary beds, one after the other. The first is the glomerulus, where filtration happens. The efferent arteriole carries the blood to the second bed, the peritubular capillaries and vasa recta that wrap the renal tubules, and only then does the blood drain into veins leading to the renal vein. The efferent arteriole is the vessel that links the two beds.","notes":["The afferent arteriole carries blood into the glomerulus, so blood cannot flow back into it. Pick the afferent arteriole if the question asked which vessel feeds the glomerulus.","The renal vein is reached only after the second capillary bed and a chain of veins. This choice skips the peritubular capillaries, which are the reason the glomerulus drains into an arteriole.","The capsular space receives filtrate, the fluid pushed out of the blood, but blood itself stays inside the vessels. Choose the capsular space if the question asked where filtrate first collects.","Correct. The efferent arteriole leads to the peritubular capillaries and vasa recta."],"lt":"t-urinary-system","lid":"d3-t-urinary-system-2","comp":138,"competencyId":"w6-kidney-bloodsupply"},{"id":"L02476","dok":2,"q":"Which list correctly names the roles of the kidneys?","a":"Filtering wastes from the blood, balancing water, ions, and pH, and helping regulate blood pressure.","options":["Storing urine, then contracting the bladder wall to push it out through the urethra","Filtering wastes, balancing water, ions, and pH, and helping regulate blood pressure","Exchanging oxygen and carbon dioxide between the blood and the air","Producing bile, storing glycogen, and breaking down old and worn-out red blood cells"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The kidneys filter wastes out of the blood and form urine, but their work goes further. By adjusting how much water and salt they keep or let go in the urine, they balance the body's water, ions, and pH. Because the amount of water in the blood affects blood volume, the kidneys also help regulate blood pressure.","notes":["Storing urine and pushing it out is the job of the urinary bladder and its detrusor muscle. Pick this list if the question asked about the bladder.","Correct. These are the roles of the kidneys.","Exchanging oxygen and carbon dioxide is the job of the lungs, at the alveoli. Choose this description if the question asked about the respiratory membrane.","Producing bile and storing glycogen are jobs of the liver, and breaking down old red blood cells is a job of the spleen. This list mixes up the kidneys with other abdominal organs."],"lt":"t-urinary-system","lid":"d3-t-urinary-system-7","comp":133,"competencyId":"w6-kidney-gross"},{"id":"N-m5-kidney-1","dok":1,"q":"What is the space inside the kidney, just deep to the hilum, that holds the renal pelvis, calyces, vessels, and fat?","a":"The renal sinus.","options":["The renal medulla","The capsular space","The renal sinus","The adipose capsule"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The hilum is the notch on the medial border of the kidney, and it opens into a space inside the kidney called the renal sinus. The sinus holds the renal pelvis and calyces along with the branches of the renal vessels, nerves, and fat that cushions them. On a frontal section, it is the central area surrounded by the kidney tissue.","notes":["The renal medulla is solid kidney tissue made of renal pyramids, not an open space holding the pelvis. Pick it if the question asked which region contains the pyramids.","The capsular space is a microscopic space inside one glomerular capsule where filtrate collects. It would be the answer for where filtrate gathers before the proximal tubule.","Correct. The renal sinus is the space deep to the hilum.","The adipose capsule is the fat layer around the outside of the kidney, between the renal capsule and the renal fascia. Choose it if the question asked which outer layer cushions the kidney."],"comp":134,"competencyId":"w6-kidney-internal"},{"id":"N-m5-kidney-2","dok":2,"q":"Which capillaries run deep into the medulla alongside the long loops of juxtamedullary nephrons?","a":"The vasa recta.","options":["The arcuate arteries","The vasa recta","The glomerular capillaries","The peritubular capillaries"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The vasa recta are long, straight capillaries that branch from the efferent arterioles of juxtamedullary nephrons and run deep into the medulla beside their long nephron loops. Vasa recta means straight vessels. Cortical nephrons, with their short loops, are wrapped by peritubular capillaries instead.","notes":["The arcuate arteries are arteries, not capillaries, and they arch along the bases of the pyramids rather than running down into them. Choose them if the question asked which arteries mark the border of cortex and medulla.","Correct. The vasa recta run alongside the long loops of juxtamedullary nephrons.","The glomerular capillaries form the filtering tuft inside the glomerular capsule, upstream of the efferent arteriole. They would be the answer for where filtration happens.","The peritubular capillaries also come from efferent arterioles, but they wrap the convoluted tubules and short loops of cortical nephrons. Pick them if the question asked which capillaries surround tubules in the cortex."],"comp":136,"competencyId":"w6-nephron-types"},{"id":"N-m5-kidney-3","dok":1,"q":"Which arteries arch along the bases of the renal pyramids, at the border between the cortex and medulla?","a":"The arcuate arteries.","options":["The interlobar arteries","The cortical radiate arteries","The arcuate arteries","The segmental arteries"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The arcuate arteries arch over the bases of the renal pyramids, right along the border between the cortex and medulla. Arcuate means arched, which names their shape. They receive blood from the interlobar arteries and send cortical radiate arteries up into the cortex.","notes":["The interlobar arteries run between the pyramids, one step before the arcuate arteries. Pick them if the question asked which arteries pass between neighboring pyramids.","The cortical radiate (interlobular) arteries branch from the arcuate arteries and run up into the cortex. Choose them if the question asked which arteries give off the afferent arterioles.","Correct. The arcuate arteries arch along the pyramid bases.","The segmental arteries are the first branches of the renal artery after it enters at the hilum. They would be the answer for the vessels the renal artery divides into first."],"comp":138,"competencyId":"w6-kidney-bloodsupply"},{"id":"N-m5-kidney-4","dok":2,"q":"Which sequence correctly traces blood from the peritubular capillaries back to the renal vein?","a":"Cortical radiate veins, arcuate veins, interlobar veins, renal vein.","options":["Interlobar veins, arcuate veins, cortical radiate veins, renal vein","Cortical radiate veins, arcuate veins, interlobar veins, renal vein","Arcuate veins, cortical radiate veins, interlobar veins, renal vein","Cortical radiate veins, interlobar veins, arcuate veins, renal vein"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The veins of the kidney retrace the arterial path in reverse. Blood from the peritubular capillaries drains into cortical radiate veins in the cortex, then into arcuate veins along the pyramid bases, then into interlobar veins between the pyramids, and finally into the renal vein, which leaves at the hilum. If you know the arteries in order, read them backward to get the veins.","notes":["This order runs from the hilum outward, the direction of the arteries rather than the veins. It would fit a question about arteries if the names were changed to arteries.","Correct. Cortical radiate, arcuate, interlobar, then the renal vein.","This order starts with the arcuate veins, which sit at the pyramid bases. Blood from the cortex must first collect in cortical radiate veins before reaching the arcuate veins.","This order skips over the arcuate veins and adds them too late. The arcuate veins lie between the cortical radiate and interlobar veins, just as the arcuate arteries do."],"comp":138,"competencyId":"w6-kidney-bloodsupply"},{"id":"L04428","dok":1,"q":"Which list orders the three tissue layers around the kidney from innermost to outermost?","a":"Renal capsule, adipose capsule, renal fascia.","options":["Adipose capsule, renal capsule, renal fascia","Renal fascia, adipose capsule, renal capsule","Renal capsule, adipose capsule, renal fascia","Renal capsule, renal fascia, adipose capsule"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The renal capsule is the smooth fibrous layer that sits right on the kidney surface and keeps its shape. Around it is the adipose capsule, a layer of fat that cushions the kidney and holds it in place. The renal fascia is the outermost layer of dense connective tissue that anchors the kidney and its fat to the back wall of the abdomen.","notes":["This puts the fat directly on the kidney surface, but the fibrous renal capsule is the layer touching the kidney. The fat lies outside that capsule, which is why the two layers share the word capsule but sit in different places.","This is the correct sequence written backward, from outermost to innermost. Students choose it when they miss the words inner to outer in the stem; it would be right if the question asked for the order from the outside in.","Correct. The fibrous capsule touches the kidney, the fat surrounds it, and the fascia anchors everything to the body wall.","This swaps the fat and the fascia. The renal fascia is the outermost anchoring sheet, and the adipose capsule lies between it and the renal capsule, so the fat cannot be the outer layer."],"lt":"t-w6-kidney-gross","lid":"w6-kidney-gross-r3","comp":133,"competencyId":"w6-kidney-gross"},{"id":"L04429","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares the positions of the renal cortex and the renal medulla?","a":"The cortex is the outer region just beneath the capsule; the medulla is the inner region that contains the renal pyramids.","options":["The cortex forms the renal pelvis; the medulla forms the outer shell of the kidney","The cortex and medulla are side by side, with the cortex nearer the hilum","The cortex is the inner region and holds the pyramids; the medulla lies beneath the capsule","The cortex lies under the capsule; the medulla is deeper and holds the pyramids"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Cortex means bark, the outer layer, and medulla means marrow, the inner part, and the kidney follows that pattern. The renal cortex is the outer band of tissue just under the renal capsule, and the renal medulla lies deeper, divided into cone-shaped renal pyramids. Strips of cortex called renal columns dip down between the pyramids.","notes":["Neither region forms the renal pelvis, which is the funnel-shaped space that collects urine from the calyces. The outer shell is the cortex, not the medulla, so this statement gets both halves wrong.","The two regions are layered one inside the other, not set side by side. The structures nearest the hilum are the renal pelvis and the major calyces, which would be the answer if the question asked what lies at the medial notch where the ureter leaves.","This reverses the two regions. The pyramids belong to the medulla, which is the deeper region; students choose this when they mix up which word means outer.","Correct. The cortex is the outer zone under the capsule, and the medulla is the deeper zone made of renal pyramids."],"lt":"t-w6-kidney-gross","lid":"w6-kidney-gross-r4","comp":134,"competencyId":"w6-kidney-internal"},{"id":"L04430","dok":3,"q":"After rapid, severe weight loss, a patient's kidney drops out of its normal position and kinks the ureter. Loss of which layer most likely allowed this?","a":"The adipose capsule.","options":["Renal capsule","Renal fascia","Adipose capsule","Parietal peritoneum"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The adipose capsule is the layer of fat around the kidney that cushions it and helps hold it in place. When a person loses a large amount of body fat quickly, this fat pad can shrink, and the kidney may drop lower in the abdomen. This condition is called nephroptosis, or a floating kidney, and the dropped kidney can kink the ureter and block the flow of urine.","notes":["The renal capsule is the thin fibrous layer on the kidney surface that maintains the kidney's shape. It is not fat, so weight loss does not shrink it; pick it if the question asked which layer touches the kidney directly.","The renal fascia is the outer connective tissue sheet that anchors the kidney to the abdominal wall. It is not lost with weight loss; choose it if the question asked which layer is the outermost anchor of the kidney.","Correct. Loss of the fatty adipose capsule removes the cushion that holds the kidney in place.","The kidneys are retroperitoneal, meaning they lie behind the parietal peritoneum rather than wrapped in it, so the peritoneum is not one of the kidney's own supporting layers. Pick it if a question asked which membrane lies in front of the retroperitoneal organs."],"lt":"t-w6-kidney-gross","lid":"w6-kidney-gross-r5","comp":140,"competencyId":"w6-urinary-disorders"},{"id":"L04431","dok":1,"q":"What are the renal pyramids, and which way do their bases face?","a":"The cone-shaped masses of the medulla, with their bases toward the cortex.","options":["Cup-shaped spaces of the medulla, with their bases toward the hilum","Cone-shaped masses of the medulla, with their bases toward the pelvis","Cone-shaped masses of the cortex, with their bases toward the pelvis","Cone-shaped masses of the medulla, with their bases toward the cortex"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The renal medulla is divided into cone-shaped renal pyramids. Each pyramid has a broad base that faces outward toward the cortex and a pointed tip, the renal papilla, that faces inward toward the renal sinus where it drains into a minor calyx. Picture each pyramid pointing its tip at the center of the kidney.","notes":["This describes the calyces, the cup-shaped spaces that collect urine near the renal sinus, rather than solid tissue. Pick a minor calyx if the question asked what cup receives urine from a papilla.","This gets the tissue right but flips the pyramid. The tip of the pyramid, the papilla, points toward the pelvis and calyces, so the base must face the opposite way, out toward the cortex.","The pyramids are part of the medulla, not the cortex. The cortical tissue that dips between the pyramids forms the renal columns, which would be the answer if the question asked about cortex extending into the medulla.","Correct. The pyramids are medullary tissue, broad at the cortex and narrowing to a papilla."],"lt":"t-w6-kidney-internal","lid":"w6-kidney-internal-r1","comp":134,"competencyId":"w6-kidney-internal"},{"id":"L04432","dok":1,"q":"The renal papilla is the tip of a renal pyramid. Into which space does it drain urine?","a":"A minor calyx.","options":["A major calyx","The ureter","A minor calyx","The renal pelvis"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Each renal papilla, the pointed tip of a pyramid, fits into a small cup called a minor calyx, and urine drips from the papilla into that cup. Several minor calyces join to form a major calyx, and the major calyces empty into the renal pelvis. The minor calyx is therefore the first collecting space urine reaches after it leaves the kidney tissue.","notes":["A major calyx forms where several minor calyces merge, so it is one step later in the path. Pick it if the question asked what the minor calyces drain into.","The ureter leaves the kidney at the hilum and carries urine to the bladder. It is several steps downstream of the papilla, and it would be the answer if the question asked what the renal pelvis drains into.","Correct. The papilla empties straight into a minor calyx.","The renal pelvis is the funnel that collects urine from the major calyces, two steps after the papilla. Choose it if the question asked what passes urine directly into the ureter."],"lt":"t-w6-kidney-internal","lid":"w6-kidney-internal-r2","comp":134,"competencyId":"w6-kidney-internal"},{"id":"L04433","dok":1,"q":"What are the renal columns?","a":"Extensions of the cortex that dip down between the renal pyramids.","options":["Extensions of the cortex that dip down between the renal pyramids","Bundles of collecting ducts that form the tip of each pyramid","Strips of medulla that rise up into the cortex between nephrons","Branches of the renal pelvis that cup the tip of each pyramid"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The renal columns are bands of cortical tissue that reach inward between neighboring renal pyramids, separating one pyramid from the next. They carry blood vessels, such as the interlobar arteries and veins, between the pyramids toward the cortex. Because they are cortex, they look the same as the outer cortex in a sectioned kidney.","notes":["Correct. Renal columns are cortex reaching down between the pyramids.","Collecting ducts gathering at the tip of a pyramid form the renal papilla region, not a column. Pick the renal papilla if the question asked about the tip of a pyramid where urine drains out.","This reverses the direction. The columns are cortex pushing inward, not medulla pushing outward; students choose this when they forget which tissue the columns are made of.","This describes the minor calyces, the cup-shaped branches of the collecting system around each papilla. Choose a minor calyx if the question asked what receives urine from the tip of a pyramid."],"lt":"t-w6-kidney-internal","lid":"w6-kidney-internal-r3","comp":134,"competencyId":"w6-kidney-internal"},{"id":"L04434","dok":2,"q":"What is the renal pelvis, and what does it do?","a":"The funnel-shaped cavity that collects urine and passes it into the ureter.","options":["The funnel that collects urine and passes it to the ureter","The cup that receives urine directly from a single renal papilla","The bony pelvic basin that supports the kidney from below","The muscular sac that stores urine until it is released"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The renal pelvis is a flat, funnel-shaped space inside the renal sinus, near the hilum. The major calyces empty into it, and it narrows as it leaves the kidney to become the ureter. Its job is to gather all the urine from the calyces and channel it into that one tube.","notes":["Correct. The renal pelvis gathers urine from the major calyces and funnels it into the ureter.","This describes a minor calyx, the small cup that fits over a single papilla. Pick it if the question asked what receives urine first after it leaves the pyramid.","The word pelvis means basin, which is why students link it to the bony pelvis, but the renal pelvis is a hollow space inside the kidney. The kidneys sit high on the posterior abdominal wall, well above the bony pelvis.","This describes the urinary bladder, which stores urine until urination. Choose the bladder if the question asked which organ holds urine between trips to the restroom."],"lt":"t-w6-kidney-internal","lid":"w6-kidney-internal-r4","comp":134,"competencyId":"w6-kidney-internal"},{"id":"L04435","dok":3,"q":"Which list orders the urine-collecting spaces from the renal papilla to the ureter?","a":"Minor calyx, major calyx, renal pelvis, then the ureter.","options":["Minor calyx, renal pelvis, major calyx, ureter","Major calyx, minor calyx, renal pelvis, ureter","Minor calyx, major calyx, renal pelvis, ureter","Renal pelvis, minor calyx, major calyx, ureter"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The collecting spaces get larger as they merge. Each papilla drains into a small minor calyx, several minor calyces join to form a major calyx, and the major calyces empty into the single renal pelvis. The renal pelvis then narrows into the ureter as it leaves the kidney at the hilum.","notes":["This places the renal pelvis between the two calyces. The major calyces empty into the pelvis, so both calyces come before it.","This puts the larger cup first. Minor calyces merge to form the major calyx, so the minor calyx must come first; students pick this when they mix up minor and major.","Correct. Small cups merge into larger cups, which merge into the funnel that becomes the ureter.","The renal pelvis is the last space inside the kidney, formed where the major calyces meet, so it cannot come before the calyces. It would be first only if the question traced urine backward from the ureter."],"lt":"t-w6-kidney-internal","lid":"w6-kidney-internal-r5","comp":139,"competencyId":"w6-urine-path"},{"id":"L04477","dok":1,"q":"Which vessel delivers blood into the glomerulus, and which carries it out?","a":"The afferent arteriole delivers it in; the efferent arteriole carries it out.","options":["Interlobular artery in; peritubular capillary out","Efferent arteriole in; afferent arteriole out","Afferent arteriole in; efferent arteriole out","Afferent arteriole in; interlobular vein out"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Afferent means carrying toward, and efferent means carrying away. The afferent arteriole brings blood into the glomerulus, and the efferent arteriole carries it out. Unusually, the vessel leaving the glomerulus is still an arteriole, which then feeds a second capillary bed, the peritubular capillaries and vasa recta around the tubules.","notes":["The interlobular artery feeds the afferent arteriole, one step earlier, and the peritubular capillaries come after the efferent arteriole. Neither one connects directly to the glomerulus.","This reverses the two arterioles. Students mix them up because the words differ by one letter; remember that afferent arrives and efferent exits.","Correct. The afferent arteriole brings blood in and the efferent arteriole takes it out.","The afferent half is right, but blood leaving the glomerulus does not go straight into a vein. It passes through the efferent arteriole and a second capillary bed first."],"lt":"t-w6-kidney-bloodsupply","lid":"w6-kidney-bloodsupply-r1","comp":138,"competencyId":"w6-kidney-bloodsupply"},{"id":"L04478","dok":2,"q":"Which list traces blood from the renal artery to the glomerulus?","a":"Renal artery, segmental and interlobar arteries, arcuate arteries, interlobular arteries, afferent arteriole, glomerulus.","options":["Renal, segmental, interlobar, interlobular, arcuate, afferent arteriole, glomerulus","Renal, interlobar, segmental, arcuate, interlobular, afferent arteriole, glomerulus","Renal, segmental, arcuate, interlobar, interlobular, afferent arteriole, glomerulus","Renal, segmental, interlobar, arcuate, interlobular, afferent arteriole, glomerulus"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The renal artery enters at the hilum and splits into segmental arteries, which give rise to interlobar arteries running between the pyramids. At the bases of the pyramids, the interlobar arteries become arcuate arteries that arch along the border of cortex and medulla. Interlobular arteries (also called cortical radiate arteries) rise from the arcuate arteries into the cortex and give off the afferent arterioles that feed each glomerulus.","notes":["This swaps the arcuate and interlobular arteries. The arcuate arteries arch along the pyramid bases, and the interlobular arteries rise from them into the cortex, where the glomeruli are.","This swaps the segmental and interlobar arteries. The segmental arteries branch first from the renal artery, and the interlobar arteries come from them.","This puts the arcuate arteries before the interlobar arteries. The interlobar arteries travel between the pyramids first and then curve into the arcuate arteries at the pyramid bases.","Correct. The arteries branch from large to small, ending at the afferent arteriole and glomerulus."],"lt":"t-w6-kidney-bloodsupply","lid":"w6-kidney-bloodsupply-r2","comp":138,"competencyId":"w6-kidney-bloodsupply"},{"id":"L04479","dok":2,"q":"Which vessels are long, straight capillaries that follow the loops of juxtamedullary nephrons deep into the medulla?","a":"The vasa recta.","options":["The interlobar veins","The peritubular capillaries","The vasa recta","The arcuate arteries"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Vasa recta means straight vessels. These long capillaries come off the efferent arterioles of juxtamedullary nephrons and run beside their long nephron loops deep into the medulla. Juxtamedullary nephrons sit near the medulla, make up about 15 percent of nephrons, and concentrate the urine.","notes":["The interlobar veins carry blood out between the pyramids toward the renal vein. They are veins, not capillaries; pick them if the question asked which vessels drain blood between the pyramids.","The peritubular capillaries wrap the tubules of the cortical nephrons, the short-looped nephrons that barely enter the medulla. Choose them if the question asked which capillaries serve cortical nephrons.","Correct. The vasa recta run alongside the long loops of juxtamedullary nephrons.","The arcuate arteries arch along the bases of the pyramids at the border of cortex and medulla. They are arteries, not capillaries, so pick them if the question asked which vessels curve along the pyramid bases."],"lt":"t-w6-kidney-bloodsupply","lid":"w6-kidney-bloodsupply-r3","comp":136,"competencyId":"w6-nephron-types"},{"id":"N-a17d-133-1","dok":3,"q":"During a dissection, you cut through the renal fascia around a kidney. Which layer will you meet next, and which layer still lies between that one and the kidney tissue?","a":"The adipose capsule, with the renal capsule still beneath it.","options":["The adipose capsule, with the renal fascia still beneath it","The adipose capsule, with the renal capsule still beneath it","The parietal peritoneum, with the renal capsule beneath it","The renal capsule, with the adipose capsule still beneath it"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"From the kidney outward, the three layers are the renal capsule, the adipose capsule, and the renal fascia. Working inward from the fascia, you reverse that order, so the fat of the adipose capsule comes next. The thin renal capsule is the last layer, lying right on the kidney's surface.","notes":["The adipose capsule is the right next layer, but the renal fascia is the layer you just cut. It is the outermost layer, not one that lies closer to the kidney.","Correct. Under the renal fascia is the fat of the adipose capsule, and the renal capsule sits on the kidney surface below that.","The parietal peritoneum lies in front of the kidneys, which is why they are called retroperitoneal. It is not one of the kidney's three layers, so you would not find it inside the renal fascia.","This reverses the two inner layers. The renal capsule is the innermost layer on the kidney itself, so it comes last, after the fat."],"comp":133,"competencyId":"w6-kidney-gross"},{"id":"N-a17d-133-2","dok":3,"q":"A surgeon removes a kidney together with its renal fascia, without opening the fascia. Which other organ comes out inside the same fascia, and which layer inside it cushions the kidney?","a":"The adrenal gland, with the adipose capsule cushioning the kidney.","options":["The liver, with the adipose capsule cushioning the kidney","The adrenal gland, with the renal capsule cushioning the kidney","The urinary bladder, with the renal capsule cushioning the kidney","The adrenal gland, with the adipose capsule cushioning the kidney"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The renal fascia is the outermost of the three layers around the kidney, and it wraps the suprarenal (adrenal) gland together with the kidney, so the gland comes out with it. Inside the fascia, the adipose capsule (perirenal fat) cushions the kidney and helps hold it in place. The renal capsule lies on the kidney surface itself, where it holds the kidney's shape and protects its surface.","notes":["The liver sits above the right kidney and is why that kidney sits lower, but it is not wrapped in the renal fascia. The only other organ inside the fascia is the adrenal gland.","The adrenal gland is right, but the renal capsule is the thin, tough layer on the kidney surface that holds its shape and protects it. The cushioning layer is the fat of the adipose capsule.","The bladder is the urinary organ that stores urine, far below the kidney, and the ureter connects the two. It does not share the renal fascia, and the renal capsule is not the cushioning layer.","Correct. The renal fascia wraps the adrenal gland together with the kidney, and the adipose capsule inside it cushions the kidney."],"comp":133,"competencyId":"w6-kidney-gross"},{"id":"N-a17d-133-3","dok":3,"q":"A stab wound enters the back beside the vertebral column, just below the lowest ribs, at about the level of L1. Which organ is most likely injured, and must the peritoneal cavity have been opened to reach it?","a":"The kidney, and no, because it lies behind the parietal peritoneum.","options":["The kidney, and no, because it lies behind the parietal peritoneum","The kidney, and yes, because it lies inside the peritoneal cavity","The urinary bladder, and no, because it lies behind the peritoneum","The adrenal gland only, and yes, because it lies in the peritoneal cavity"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The kidneys sit on the posterior abdominal wall beside the vertebral column, between about T12 and L3, with the lowest ribs covering their upper ends. They are retroperitoneal, which means they lie behind the parietal peritoneum and outside the peritoneal cavity. A wound coming in from the back can reach a kidney before it ever reaches the peritoneum.","notes":["Correct. The kidney lies at about T12 to L3 against the back muscles, behind the peritoneum, so a wound from behind can reach it without entering the peritoneal cavity.","The kidney is the right organ, but it is retroperitoneal. It lies behind the parietal peritoneum, so reaching it from behind does not require opening the peritoneal cavity.","The bladder sits low in the pelvis, far below L1. A wound just under the lowest ribs beside the spine is at kidney level, not bladder level.","The adrenal gland caps the upper end of the kidney inside the same renal fascia, so it is also retroperitoneal. At L1 beside the spine, the kidney itself is the organ in the path."],"comp":133,"competencyId":"w6-kidney-gross"},{"id":"N-a17d-134-1","dok":3,"q":"A small stone blocks one minor calyx and urine backs up behind it. Which part of the kidney is the backed-up urine coming from?","a":"The pyramid whose papilla opens into it.","options":["The renal pelvis and the ureter below it","The renal column beside that calyx","The pyramid whose papilla opens into it","Every renal pyramid in the kidney"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Urine drains out of tiny openings on the renal papilla, the tip of a renal pyramid, and each papilla empties into a cup called a minor calyx. A blockage makes urine back up into the parts above it. So the backup comes from the pyramid whose papilla opens into that calyx, while the rest of the kidney keeps draining through its own calyces.","notes":["The renal pelvis and ureter are downstream of the minor calyx, so a stone in the calyx does not back urine up into them. They would back up only if the stone were lower, in the pelvis or ureter.","A renal column is cortex tissue reaching inward between two pyramids. Urine drains from the papillae of the pyramids, not from the columns.","Correct. Each papilla drains into a minor calyx, so the pyramid whose tip opens into the blocked calyx is the one that backs up.","The other pyramids drain into their own minor calyces, which are still open. Only the pyramid draining into the blocked calyx is affected."],"comp":134,"competencyId":"w6-kidney-internal"},{"id":"N-a17d-138-1","dok":1,"q":"Which vessel carries blood out of the kidney at the hilum and empties into the inferior vena cava?","a":"The renal vein.","options":["The interlobar vein","The renal artery","The renal vein","The ureter"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Blood leaving the kidney passes through veins that repeat the artery names in reverse: cortical radiate, arcuate, interlobar, and then the renal vein. The renal vein leaves the kidney at the hilum, alongside the renal artery and ureter. It empties into the inferior vena cava, which returns the blood to the heart.","notes":["Interlobar veins run down through the renal columns toward the renal sinus. They join to form the renal vein, which is the vessel that actually leaves the kidney.","The renal artery also passes through the hilum, but it brings blood in. It branches off the abdominal aorta rather than draining into a vein.","Correct. The renal vein leaves at the hilum and empties into the inferior vena cava.","The ureter leaves at the hilum too, but it is a tube that carries urine to the bladder, not a blood vessel."],"comp":138,"competencyId":"w6-kidney-bloodsupply"}]},{"id":"m5-nephron","title":"The nephron and juxtaglomerular apparatus","summary":"renal corpuscle, tubule segments, collecting duct, cortical vs juxtamedullary nephrons, filtration membrane, JGA","mw":"2026-11-18","tr":"2026-11-19","cards":[{"id":"L02383","dok":2,"q":"Filtrate has just left the nephron loop. Which segment of the nephron does it enter next?","a":"The distal convoluted tubule.","options":["The distal convoluted tubule","The glomerular capsule","The collecting duct","The proximal convoluted tubule"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The segments are named in the order the filtrate meets them: capsular space, proximal convoluted tubule, nephron loop, distal convoluted tubule, and then the collecting duct. After the filtrate climbs the ascending limb of the nephron loop, it enters the distal convoluted tubule, the second coiled segment. Proximal means near the renal corpuscle and distal means farther from it, which makes the order easy to rebuild.","notes":["Correct. The distal convoluted tubule follows the nephron loop.","The glomerular capsule is the cup around the glomerulus where filtrate first collects, at the very start of the nephron. It would be the answer for where filtrate enters the nephron, not where it goes after the loop.","The collecting duct comes one step later. It receives fluid from the distal convoluted tubules of many nephrons, so it is the answer for where filtrate goes after the distal convoluted tubule.","The proximal convoluted tubule is the first coiled segment, the one filtrate enters straight from the capsular space. It comes before the nephron loop, so pick it if the question asked where filtrate goes after leaving the glomerular capsule."],"lt":"t-urinary-system","lid":"fr5","comp":135,"competencyId":"w6-nephron-parts"},{"id":"L02384","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares cortical and juxtamedullary nephrons?","a":"Cortical nephrons are about 85 percent and have short loops; juxtamedullary nephrons have long loops served by the vasa recta.","options":["Cortical nephrons are about 85 percent and have short loops; juxtamedullary nephrons have long loops served by the vasa recta.","Cortical nephrons are about 15 percent and have long loops; juxtamedullary nephrons are about 85 percent and have short loops.","Juxtamedullary nephrons lie entirely within the medulla; cortical nephrons have no nephron loop at all.","Both types have the same loop length; they differ only in which kidney they are found in."],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Nephrons are sorted by how deep their loop reaches. Cortical nephrons make up about 85 percent of all nephrons, have short loops that barely enter the medulla, and are wrapped by peritubular capillaries. Juxtamedullary nephrons make up about 15 percent, have renal corpuscles near the border with the medulla (juxta means next to), and have long loops that reach deep into the medulla, served by the vasa recta. These long loops are what let the kidney concentrate urine.","notes":["Correct. Cortical nephrons are the common short-looped type, and juxtamedullary nephrons are the long-looped type served by the vasa recta.","This statement swaps the numbers and the loop lengths. Most nephrons are cortical with short loops; only about 15 percent are juxtamedullary with long loops.","Every nephron has a nephron loop, and the renal corpuscles of juxtamedullary nephrons sit in the cortex next to the medulla, not inside it. Only their long loops dip deep into the medulla.","The two types differ mainly in loop length and the position of the corpuscle, and both types are found in each kidney. This choice comes from reading cortical and juxtamedullary as places rather than as nephron types."],"lt":"t-urinary-system","lid":"fr6","comp":136,"competencyId":"w6-nephron-types"},{"id":"L02385","dok":2,"q":"What forms the parietal (outer) layer of the glomerular capsule?","a":"Simple squamous epithelium.","options":["Podocytes","Simple cuboidal epithelium","Simple squamous epithelium","Transitional epithelium"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The glomerular capsule has two layers with the capsular space between them. The outer, parietal layer is simple squamous epithelium, a single layer of flat cells that forms the wall of the cup. The inner, visceral layer is made of podocytes, which wrap the glomerular capillaries. Filtrate passes into the capsular space between the two layers.","notes":["Podocytes form the inner, visceral layer that clings to the glomerular capillaries, and their foot-like processes form the filtration slits. Pick them if the question asked about the visceral layer.","Simple cuboidal epithelium lines the renal tubules, such as the proximal and distal convoluted tubules. It is the answer if the question asked about the tubule wall rather than the capsule.","Correct. The parietal layer is a thin wall of simple squamous epithelium.","Transitional epithelium is the stretchy lining of the ureters and urinary bladder. Choose it if the question asked which epithelium lines an organ that has to expand as it fills."],"lt":"t-urinary-system","lid":"fr7","comp":135,"competencyId":"w6-nephron-parts"},{"id":"L02389","dok":2,"q":"Where are the juxtaglomerular (granular) cells located?","a":"In the wall of the afferent arteriole.","options":["In the wall of the distal convoluted tubule","In the wall of the collecting duct","In the wall of the afferent arteriole","In the visceral layer of the glomerular capsule"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Juxtaglomerular cells are modified smooth muscle cells, mostly in the wall of the afferent arteriole, at the spot where the tubule passes beside its own renal corpuscle. Together with the macula densa cells in the tubule wall, they form the juxtaglomerular apparatus, which monitors the tubule fluid and the arteriole and helps adjust filtration. The JG cells release renin when blood pressure is low. Juxtaglomerular means next to the glomerulus.","notes":["Macula densa cells sit in the tubule wall, where the ascending limb of the nephron loop ends and the distal convoluted tubule begins. Pick this location if the question asked where the macula densa is, not the JG cells.","The collecting duct gathers fluid from many nephrons and runs down into the medulla, away from the glomerulus. It is the answer if the question asked which duct receives fluid from many distal tubules.","Correct. The JG cells are modified smooth muscle in the afferent arteriole wall.","The visceral layer of the glomerular capsule is made of podocytes, which wrap the glomerular capillaries. It would be the answer if the question asked where the filtration slits are formed."],"lt":"t-urinary-system","lid":"fr11","comp":137,"competencyId":"w6-jga-filtration"},{"id":"L02393","dok":2,"q":"Near the tip of a renal pyramid, collecting ducts join into larger ducts that open on the renal papilla. What are these larger ducts called?","a":"Papillary ducts.","options":["Papillary ducts","Minor calyces","Distal convoluted tubules","Ureters"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Collecting ducts run down through the renal pyramid and join into larger papillary ducts as they near the tip. The papillary ducts open on the renal papilla, where urine drips out into a minor calyx. The name tells you where they are: papillary ducts are the ducts of the papilla. They are on the lab structure list as the last part of the renal tubule system.","notes":["Correct. The papillary ducts carry urine out onto the renal papilla.","The minor calyces are cups, not ducts, and they wrap around the papillae to catch urine. Pick them if the question asked what receives urine as it leaves the papilla.","The distal convoluted tubules are coiled segments of individual nephrons in the cortex. They empty into collecting ducts, so they come before the collecting ducts, not after.","The ureters are the paired tubes that carry urine from the renal pelvis to the bladder, well outside the pyramids. They would be the answer for the tube leaving the kidney at the hilum."],"lt":"t-urinary-system","lid":"fr15","comp":135,"competencyId":"w6-nephron-parts"},{"id":"L02397","dok":1,"q":"What is the microscopic functional unit of the kidney?","a":"The nephron.","options":["The renal pyramid","The glomerulus","The nephron","The collecting duct"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The nephron is the microscopic structure that actually makes urine, so it is the functional unit of the kidney. Each nephron has a renal corpuscle that filters the blood and a renal tubule that processes the filtrate. Each kidney holds about one million of them.","notes":["A renal pyramid is a large cone of medullary tissue that you can see without a microscope, and it holds parts of thousands of nephrons. Pick it if the question asked for the cone-shaped masses of the medulla.","The glomerulus is only one part of a nephron, the capillary tuft where blood is filtered. It would be the answer for where filtration happens, not for the whole working unit.","Correct. The nephron is the functional unit of the kidney.","The collecting duct gathers fluid from many nephrons, so it serves many units rather than being one. Choose it if the question asked which duct receives fluid from many nephrons."],"lt":"t-urinary-system","lid":"c3","comp":135,"competencyId":"w6-nephron-parts"},{"id":"L02401","dok":1,"q":"What is the tuft of capillaries where the blood is filtered?","a":"The glomerulus.","options":["The glomerulus","The peritubular capillaries","The renal tubule","The glomerular capsule"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The glomerulus is a tuft of leaky, fenestrated capillaries, meaning their walls are full of small pores. Blood enters it through the afferent arteriole, and water and small solutes are pushed out through the pores into the glomerular capsule as filtrate. Blood leaves through the efferent arteriole.","notes":["Correct. The glomerulus is the filtering capillary tuft.","The peritubular capillaries are the second capillary bed, wrapping the renal tubules after blood leaves the glomerulus. Choose them if the question asked which capillaries surround the tubules.","The renal tubule is the long tube that processes the filtrate after it leaves the capsule. It would be the answer for the part of the nephron that changes the filtrate into urine.","The glomerular capsule is the cup that surrounds the glomerulus and collects the filtrate, but it is not made of capillaries. Pick it if the question asked what catches the filtrate."],"lt":"t-urinary-system","lid":"c7","comp":135,"competencyId":"w6-nephron-parts"},{"id":"L02403","dok":1,"q":"What is the cup-shaped structure that surrounds the glomerulus and collects the filtrate?","a":"The glomerular capsule.","options":["The collecting duct","The renal capsule","The renal pelvis","The glomerular capsule"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The glomerular capsule, also called Bowman's capsule, is a double-walled cup around the glomerulus. Filtrate pushed out of the glomerular capillaries collects in the capsular space inside it and then flows into the proximal convoluted tubule. The glomerulus and its capsule together make up the renal corpuscle.","notes":["The collecting duct gathers fluid from many nephrons far downstream of the glomerulus. Choose it if the question asked what receives fluid from the distal convoluted tubules.","The renal capsule is the fibrous covering of the whole kidney, not a cup around one glomerulus. The similar names cause this mix-up; pick the renal capsule if the question asked about the kidney's outer covering.","The renal pelvis is the funnel-shaped cavity that collects urine from the calyces and passes it to the ureter. It would be the answer for the large collecting space at the center of the kidney.","Correct. The glomerular capsule surrounds the glomerulus and collects the filtrate."],"lt":"t-urinary-system","lid":"c9","comp":135,"competencyId":"w6-nephron-parts"},{"id":"L02409","dok":2,"q":"The renal corpuscle is made up of which two structures together?","a":"A glomerulus enclosed in a glomerular capsule.","options":["A glomerulus enclosed in a glomerular capsule","A renal pyramid and a renal column","An afferent arteriole and an efferent arteriole","A renal tubule and a collecting duct"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The renal corpuscle is the filtering head of the nephron. It is a glomerulus, the tuft of capillaries where blood is filtered, sitting inside the glomerular (Bowman's) capsule, which catches the filtrate. The rest of the nephron, the renal tubule, carries and processes that filtrate.","notes":["Correct. The renal corpuscle is the glomerulus inside its capsule.","Renal pyramids and renal columns are large regions of the kidney visible without a microscope. They would be the answer if the question asked what makes up the medulla and the cortex extensions between it.","The afferent and efferent arterioles carry blood into and out of the glomerulus, so they attach to the corpuscle but are not part of it. Choose them if the question asked which vessels serve the glomerulus.","The renal tubule and the collecting duct carry the filtrate after it leaves the corpuscle. Pick the renal tubule if the question asked for the second main part of the nephron."],"lt":"t-urinary-system","lid":"c15","comp":135,"competencyId":"w6-nephron-parts"},{"id":"L02421","dok":2,"q":"Which part of the nephron is its filtering head?","a":"The renal corpuscle.","options":["The renal pelvis","The collecting duct","The renal corpuscle","The renal tubule"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"A nephron has two main parts. The renal corpuscle, a glomerulus inside a glomerular capsule, is the filtering head where fluid is pushed out of the blood. The renal tubule then carries that filtrate and changes it into urine. All the renal corpuscles sit in the cortex.","notes":["The renal pelvis is a large collecting cavity at the center of the kidney, not part of any nephron. It would be the answer for the funnel that passes urine to the ureter.","The collecting duct gathers fluid from many nephrons, so it is downstream of them rather than a part of one. Choose it if the question asked which duct receives fluid from many distal tubules.","Correct. The renal corpuscle is the filtering part of the nephron.","The renal tubule is the second main part of the nephron, the long tube that processes the filtrate after it is formed. Pick it if the question asked which part of the nephron carries the filtrate away from the corpuscle."],"lt":"t-urinary-system","lid":"c27","comp":135,"competencyId":"w6-nephron-parts"},{"id":"L02422","dok":2,"q":"Which segment of the nephron is the U-shaped part that dips down into the medulla?","a":"The nephron loop.","options":["The glomerular capsule","The nephron loop","The distal convoluted tubule","The proximal convoluted tubule"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The nephron loop, also called the loop of Henle, is the U-shaped segment between the two convoluted tubules. It has a descending limb that runs down into the medulla and an ascending limb that climbs back toward the cortex. In juxtamedullary nephrons the loop reaches deep into the medulla, and in cortical nephrons it barely enters it.","notes":["The glomerular capsule is the cup around the glomerulus at the start of the nephron, not a tubule segment. Choose it if the question asked what collects the filtrate first.","Correct. The nephron loop is the U-shaped segment that dips into the medulla.","The distal convoluted tubule is the second coiled segment, back in the cortex after the loop. It would be the answer for the segment that touches its own glomerulus at the juxtaglomerular apparatus.","The proximal convoluted tubule is the first coiled segment, lying in the cortex right after the capsule. Pick it if the question asked which segment has a brush border of microvilli."],"lt":"t-urinary-system","lid":"c28","comp":135,"competencyId":"w6-nephron-parts"},{"id":"L02423","dok":2,"q":"Which duct receives fluid from the distal convoluted tubules of many nephrons and carries it down through the pyramid?","a":"The collecting duct.","options":["The nephron loop","The distal convoluted tubule","The ureter","The collecting duct"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The collecting duct is shared by many nephrons. It receives fluid from their distal convoluted tubules and carries it down through a renal pyramid toward the renal papilla, where it joins into papillary ducts. Because it serves many nephrons, it is the last stage of the tubule system rather than a part of any single nephron.","notes":["The nephron loop is the U-shaped segment of a single nephron between the two convoluted tubules. Choose it if the question asked which segment dips into the medulla and back.","The distal convoluted tubule belongs to a single nephron and empties into the collecting duct. Pick it if the question asked which segment comes right after the nephron loop.","The ureter carries urine from the renal pelvis to the bladder, outside the kidney tissue. It would be the answer for the tube that leaves the kidney at the hilum.","Correct. The collecting duct gathers fluid from many nephrons."],"lt":"t-urinary-system","lid":"c29","comp":135,"competencyId":"w6-nephron-parts"},{"id":"L02424","dok":2,"q":"The proximal convoluted tubule is called proximal because it is the segment closest to which structure?","a":"The renal corpuscle.","options":["The renal papilla","The collecting duct","The renal hilum","The renal corpuscle"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Proximal means nearer to the starting point and distal means farther away, the same words used for limbs. For the renal tubule, the starting point is the renal corpuscle. The proximal convoluted tubule begins right at the glomerular capsule, and the distal convoluted tubule comes later, after the nephron loop. Knowing this lets you rebuild the order of the segments.","notes":["The renal papilla is the tip of a pyramid where urine drains into a minor calyx, near the end of the path. A segment closest to the papilla would be the papillary duct, not the proximal tubule.","The collecting duct is at the far end of the nephron, after the distal convoluted tubule. The segment closest to it is the distal convoluted tubule, which is the opposite of proximal.","The renal hilum is the notch on the kidney's medial border where vessels and the ureter pass. Tubule names are measured from the renal corpuscle, not from the hilum.","Correct. Proximal means closest to the renal corpuscle, where the tubule begins."],"lt":"t-urinary-system","lid":"c30","comp":135,"competencyId":"w6-nephron-parts"},{"id":"L02428","dok":2,"q":"Which part of the glomerular capsule wraps directly around the glomerular capillaries?","a":"The visceral layer.","options":["The renal capsule","The capsular space","The parietal layer","The visceral layer"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The glomerular capsule is a double-walled cup. Its inner, visceral layer is made of podocytes that wrap directly around the glomerular capillaries, and their foot-like processes form filtration slits. The outer, parietal layer forms the wall of the cup, and the capsular space between the two layers collects the filtrate. Visceral layers always cling to the organ, the same pattern as visceral pleura on the lung.","notes":["The renal capsule is the fibrous covering of the whole kidney, not part of a nephron. The names are similar; choose it if the question asked what maintains the kidney's shape.","The capsular space is the gap between the two layers where filtrate collects, not a layer itself. It would be the answer for where filtrate gathers before entering the proximal convoluted tubule.","The parietal layer is the outer wall of the cup, made of simple squamous epithelium, and it does not touch the capillaries. Pick it if the question asked for the outer layer of the capsule.","Correct. The visceral layer of podocytes wraps the glomerular capillaries."],"lt":"t-urinary-system","lid":"c34","comp":135,"competencyId":"w6-nephron-parts"},{"id":"L02432","dok":2,"q":"Which segment is the second coiled part of the renal tubule, coming after the nephron loop?","a":"The distal convoluted tubule.","options":["The proximal convoluted tubule","The nephron loop","The distal convoluted tubule","The collecting duct"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The renal tubule has two coiled (convoluted) segments with the U-shaped nephron loop between them. The distal convoluted tubule is the second coil, lying back in the cortex after the loop climbs out of the medulla. Where the ascending limb ends and the distal convoluted tubule begins, the tubule passes beside its own renal corpuscle, and the macula densa cells there form part of the juxtaglomerular apparatus.","notes":["The proximal convoluted tubule is the first coiled segment, right after the glomerular capsule and before the loop. Pick it if the question asked which segment filtrate enters first.","The nephron loop is the U-shaped, uncoiled segment between the two convoluted tubules. It would be the answer for the segment that dips into the medulla.","Correct. The distal convoluted tubule is the second coiled segment.","The collecting duct is not coiled and is shared by many nephrons, coming after the distal convoluted tubule. Choose it if the question asked where the distal tubules drain."],"lt":"t-urinary-system","lid":"c38","comp":135,"competencyId":"w6-nephron-parts"},{"id":"L02435","dok":3,"q":"Which sequence correctly traces filtrate through the nephron, from where it is captured to the collecting duct?","a":"Glomerular capsule, proximal convoluted tubule, nephron loop, distal convoluted tubule, collecting duct.","options":["Glomerular capsule, distal convoluted tubule, nephron loop, proximal convoluted tubule, collecting duct","Glomerular capsule, proximal convoluted tubule, nephron loop, distal convoluted tubule, collecting duct","Glomerular capsule, nephron loop, proximal convoluted tubule, distal convoluted tubule, collecting duct","Glomerular capsule, distal convoluted tubule, proximal convoluted tubule, nephron loop, collecting duct"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Filtrate is captured in the glomerular capsule and flows next into the proximal convoluted tubule, the coil nearest the capsule. It then runs down and back up the nephron loop, through the distal convoluted tubule, and into the collecting duct. The segments are named in the order the filtrate meets them, so proximal comes before the loop and distal comes after it.","notes":["This order runs the tubule backward, starting with the distal convoluted tubule. Distal means farther from the capsule, so the distal tubule is the last segment before the collecting duct.","Correct. Capsule, proximal convoluted tubule, nephron loop, distal convoluted tubule, collecting duct.","This order sends filtrate into the nephron loop before the proximal convoluted tubule. The proximal tubule is attached directly to the capsule, so it always comes first.","This order places both coiled segments before the loop. The nephron loop sits between the two convoluted tubules, so the proximal tubule leads into it and the distal tubule follows it."],"lt":"t-urinary-system","lid":"c41","comp":135,"competencyId":"w6-nephron-parts"},{"id":"L02441","dok":1,"q":"About how many nephrons does each kidney hold?","a":"About one million.","options":["About one thousand","About one million","About one hundred","About one billion"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Each kidney holds about one million nephrons, the microscopic functional units that make urine. Their renal corpuscles are packed into the cortex, and their loops and the collecting ducts they drain into fill the pyramids of the medulla. Because each nephron is so small, one kidney can hold that many.","notes":["One thousand is far too low. Nephrons are microscopic, so each kidney holds far more of them than students who picture them as visible structures expect.","Correct. Each kidney holds about one million nephrons.","One hundred is far too few. Each kidney holds about ten thousand times that many, because each nephron is microscopic.","One billion overshoots by about a thousand times. Each nephron is built from many cells, so students who think of nephrons as single cells end up with a number that is far too large."],"lt":"t-urinary-system","lid":"c2-x2","comp":135,"competencyId":"w6-nephron-parts"},{"id":"L02442","dok":1,"q":"Which two main parts make up a nephron?","a":"The renal corpuscle and the renal tubule.","options":["The renal corpuscle and the renal tubule","The afferent and efferent arterioles","The glomerulus and the renal pelvis","The cortex and the medulla"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"A nephron has two main parts. The renal corpuscle (a glomerulus inside a glomerular capsule) filters the blood, and the renal tubule (the proximal convoluted tubule, nephron loop, and distal convoluted tubule) carries the filtrate and turns it into urine. Learning these two parts first makes the smaller pieces easier to place.","notes":["Correct. A nephron is a renal corpuscle plus a renal tubule.","The afferent and efferent arterioles carry blood into and out of the glomerulus. They serve the nephron but are blood vessels, so pick them if the question asked which vessels connect to the glomerulus.","The glomerulus is part of the renal corpuscle, but the renal pelvis is a large collecting cavity outside every nephron. Choose the renal pelvis if the question asked what funnels urine to the ureter.","The cortex and medulla are the two tissue regions of the whole kidney, each holding parts of many nephrons. Pick them if the question asked for the regions seen on a kidney section."],"lt":"t-urinary-system","lid":"c3-x2","comp":135,"competencyId":"w6-nephron-parts"},{"id":"L02443","dok":1,"q":"Where does filtrate collect inside the glomerular capsule before it enters the proximal convoluted tubule?","a":"The capsular space.","options":["The renal pelvis","The minor calyx","The capsular space","The renal sinus"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The glomerular capsule has an inner visceral layer of podocytes and an outer parietal layer, with the capsular space between them. Fluid filtered out of the glomerulus passes into this space and then flows straight into the proximal convoluted tubule. The capsular space is the first stop in the path of the filtrate.","notes":["The renal pelvis is the large funnel at the center of the kidney that passes urine to the ureter, far downstream of any nephron. Pick it if the question asked where the major calyces drain.","The minor calyx is the cup that catches urine from a renal papilla, at the end of the nephron's path rather than the start. Choose it if the question asked what receives urine from the papilla.","Correct. Filtrate collects in the capsular space first.","The renal sinus is the space inside the kidney, deep to the hilum, that holds the renal pelvis, calyces, vessels, and fat. It would be the answer for the cavity that the hilum opens into."],"lt":"t-urinary-system","lid":"c4-x2","comp":135,"competencyId":"w6-nephron-parts"},{"id":"L02444","dok":1,"q":"What is another name for the glomerular capsule?","a":"Bowman's capsule.","options":["The renal capsule","The renal corpuscle","Bowman's capsule","The loop of Henle"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The glomerular capsule is also called Bowman's capsule, after the anatomist who described it. It is the double-walled cup around the glomerulus that collects the filtrate in its capsular space. You will see both names on models and in the lab structure list.","notes":["The renal capsule is the fibrous covering of the entire kidney, not the cup around one glomerulus. It would be the answer for the layer that maintains the kidney's shape.","The renal corpuscle is the glomerulus and its capsule together, so it is larger than the capsule alone. Pick it if the question asked for the filtering head of the nephron as a whole.","Correct. Bowman's capsule is the other name for the glomerular capsule.","The loop of Henle is the other name for the nephron loop, the U-shaped segment of the tubule. Choose it if the question asked for another name for the nephron loop."],"lt":"t-urinary-system","lid":"c5-x2","comp":135,"competencyId":"w6-nephron-parts"},{"id":"L02445","dok":1,"q":"Which segment of the renal tubule is lined with a brush border of microvilli?","a":"The proximal convoluted tubule.","options":["The proximal convoluted tubule","The distal convoluted tubule","The nephron loop","The collecting duct"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The proximal convoluted tubule is the first coiled segment of the renal tubule, and its cells carry a dense brush border of microvilli on the side facing the filtrate. Microvilli are tiny finger-like folds of the cell membrane that add surface area. This is one of the features that lets you tell the proximal tubule from the distal tubule on a slide.","notes":["Correct. The proximal convoluted tubule has the brush border of microvilli.","The distal convoluted tubule is the second coiled segment, and its cells have few microvilli, so it has no brush border. Pick it if the question asked which coiled segment comes after the nephron loop.","The nephron loop is the U-shaped segment that dips into the medulla, not the coiled segment with the brush border. It would be the answer for the segment with a descending and an ascending limb.","The collecting duct gathers fluid from many nephrons and is not lined by a brush border. Choose it if the question asked which duct receives fluid from many distal tubules."],"lt":"t-urinary-system","lid":"c6-x2","comp":135,"competencyId":"w6-nephron-parts"},{"id":"L02446","dok":1,"q":"What is another name for the nephron loop?","a":"The loop of Henle.","options":["The renal pelvis","The loop of Henle","Bowman's capsule","The collecting duct"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The nephron loop is also called the loop of Henle, after the anatomist who described it. It is the U-shaped segment between the proximal and distal convoluted tubules, with a descending limb that runs down into the medulla and an ascending limb that returns toward the cortex. Both limbs are on the lab structure list.","notes":["The renal pelvis is the funnel-shaped collecting cavity at the center of the kidney. Pick it if the question asked what passes urine into the ureter.","Correct. The nephron loop is also called the loop of Henle.","Bowman's capsule is the other name for the glomerular capsule, the cup around the glomerulus. Choose it if the question asked for another name for the glomerular capsule.","The collecting duct is a shared duct that receives fluid from many nephrons, not a loop. It would be the answer for where the distal convoluted tubules drain."],"lt":"t-urinary-system","lid":"c7-x2","comp":135,"competencyId":"w6-nephron-parts"},{"id":"L02447","dok":1,"q":"Which cells form the inner, visceral layer of the glomerular capsule?","a":"Podocytes.","options":["Podocytes","Juxtaglomerular cells","Macula densa cells","Endothelial cells"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Podocytes are the cells of the visceral layer of the glomerular capsule. They wrap around the glomerular capillaries with foot-like processes, and the narrow gaps between those processes are the filtration slits. The name means foot cells, which describes their shape.","notes":["Correct. Podocytes form the visceral layer of the capsule.","Juxtaglomerular cells are modified smooth muscle cells in the wall of the afferent arteriole that release renin. Pick them if the question asked which cells of the juxtaglomerular apparatus release renin.","Macula densa cells are tubule cells of the juxtaglomerular apparatus, where the ascending limb meets the distal convoluted tubule, and they sense the sodium chloride in the fluid. They would be the answer for the tubule cells of the juxtaglomerular apparatus.","Endothelial cells line the glomerular capillaries themselves, forming the fenestrated endothelium with its pores. Choose them if the question asked which layer of the filtration membrane blocks blood cells."],"lt":"t-urinary-system","lid":"c8-x2","comp":135,"competencyId":"w6-nephron-parts"},{"id":"L02451","dok":1,"q":"Which type of nephron makes up about 85 percent of all nephrons?","a":"Cortical nephrons.","options":["Medullary nephrons","Papillary nephrons","Cortical nephrons","Juxtamedullary nephrons"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Cortical nephrons make up about 85 percent of nephrons. They have short loops that barely enter the medulla and are wrapped by peritubular capillaries. The other roughly 15 percent are juxtamedullary nephrons, whose long loops reach deep into the medulla.","notes":["There is no nephron type called medullary. All renal corpuscles sit in the cortex, so this name comes from mixing up where the loops reach with where the nephrons are classified.","There is no nephron type called papillary; the papilla is the tip of a renal pyramid, where papillary ducts open. This choice borrows a real kidney word for a nephron type that does not exist.","Correct. Cortical nephrons are about 85 percent of nephrons.","Juxtamedullary nephrons are the minority, about 15 percent, with long loops served by the vasa recta. Pick them if the question asked which nephrons concentrate the urine."],"lt":"t-urinary-system","lid":"c12-x2","comp":136,"competencyId":"w6-nephron-types"},{"id":"L02452","dok":1,"q":"Which cells of the juxtaglomerular apparatus release renin when blood pressure is low?","a":"The juxtaglomerular cells.","options":["The proximal tubule cells","The juxtaglomerular cells","The macula densa cells","The podocytes"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The juxtaglomerular (granular) cells are modified smooth muscle cells in the wall of the afferent arteriole. Their granules hold the enzyme renin, which they release when blood pressure is low. They sit where the tubule passes beside its own renal corpuscle, close to the macula densa cells in the tubule wall.","notes":["Proximal tubule cells line the first coiled segment and carry a brush border of microvilli. They are not part of the juxtaglomerular apparatus; choose them if the question asked which segment has a brush border.","Correct. The juxtaglomerular cells release renin.","The macula densa cells are the tubule half of the juxtaglomerular apparatus, where the ascending limb meets the distal convoluted tubule. They sense the sodium chloride level of the fluid, so pick them for the sensing cells.","Podocytes form the visceral layer of the glomerular capsule and create the filtration slits. They would be the answer for the cells that wrap the glomerular capillaries."],"lt":"t-urinary-system","lid":"c13-x2","comp":137,"competencyId":"w6-jga-filtration"},{"id":"L02459","dok":2,"q":"Which nephrons have long loops that reach deep into the medulla, and what share of nephrons are they?","a":"Juxtamedullary nephrons, about 15 percent of nephrons.","options":["Cortical nephrons, about 85 percent of nephrons","Juxtamedullary nephrons, about 85 percent of nephrons","Juxtamedullary nephrons, about 15 percent of nephrons","Cortical nephrons, about 15 percent of nephrons"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Juxtamedullary nephrons have their renal corpuscles in the cortex right next to the medulla, and their long loops reach deep into the medulla, where they are served by the vasa recta. They make up only about 15 percent of nephrons, but they are the ones that concentrate the urine. The other 85 percent are cortical nephrons with short loops.","notes":["Cortical nephrons are the 85 percent, but they have short loops that barely enter the medulla. Pick this pairing if the question asked about the common short-looped nephrons.","Juxtamedullary is the right type, but they are the minority, not the majority. The 85 percent figure belongs to cortical nephrons.","Correct. Juxtamedullary nephrons have long loops and are about 15 percent of nephrons.","This pairing gives the cortical nephrons the juxtamedullary share. Cortical nephrons are the large majority with short loops, so both parts of this choice are mixed up."],"lt":"t-urinary-system","lid":"c20-x2","comp":136,"competencyId":"w6-nephron-types"},{"id":"L02460","dok":2,"q":"Which layer of the filtration membrane lets plasma pass but blocks blood cells?","a":"The fenestrated endothelium.","options":["The fenestrated endothelium","The slit membranes","The parietal layer of the glomerular capsule","The basement membrane"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Blood is filtered across a three-layer membrane. The first layer is the fenestrated endothelium, the wall of the glomerular capillary, which is full of pores (fenestrations means windows). The pores are large enough for plasma to pass but too small for blood cells, so the cells stay in the blood. The basement membrane and slit membranes then hold back proteins.","notes":["Correct. The fenestrated endothelium blocks blood cells but lets plasma through.","The slit membranes bridge the filtration slits between podocyte feet and hold back most of the smaller proteins that get past the basement membrane. They would be the answer for the last layer of the filtration membrane.","The parietal layer is the outer wall of the capsule, made of simple squamous epithelium, and filtrate never has to cross it. Choose it if the question asked for the outer layer of the glomerular capsule.","The basement membrane is the middle layer, between the capillary and the podocytes, and it blocks large plasma proteins. Pick it if the question asked which layer holds back large proteins."],"lt":"t-urinary-system","lid":"c21-x2","comp":137,"competencyId":"w6-jga-filtration"},{"id":"L02464","dok":2,"q":"Blood has just been filtered in the glomerulus. Which segment of the renal tubule does the filtrate enter first?","a":"The proximal convoluted tubule.","options":["The proximal convoluted tubule","The collecting duct","The distal convoluted tubule","The nephron loop"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Filtrate collects first in the capsular space of the glomerular capsule, and the capsule opens directly into the proximal convoluted tubule. Proximal means closest, and this coil is the segment closest to the renal corpuscle. From there the filtrate moves on to the nephron loop, the distal convoluted tubule, and the collecting duct.","notes":["Correct. Filtrate enters the proximal convoluted tubule first.","The collecting duct is the last stage, gathering fluid from many nephrons after their distal tubules. It would be the answer for where the distal convoluted tubule drains.","The distal convoluted tubule is the second coiled segment, after the nephron loop. Pick it if the question asked where filtrate goes after leaving the loop.","The nephron loop comes after the proximal tubule, not before it. Choose it if the question asked which U-shaped segment dips into the medulla."],"lt":"t-urinary-system","lid":"c25-x2","comp":135,"competencyId":"w6-nephron-parts"},{"id":"L02472","dok":3,"q":"The proximal convoluted tubule is lined with a brush border of microvilli. How does this feature suit its job?","a":"It adds surface area for reclaiming water and nutrients from the filtrate.","options":["It forms filtration slits that hold back blood proteins","It adds surface area for reclaiming water and nutrients from the filtrate","It lets the tubule stretch as urine is stored inside it","It sweeps the filtrate along the length of the tubule the way cilia do"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The glomerulus filters far more fluid than the body can afford to lose, including water, glucose, and ions. The proximal convoluted tubule is the first place that fluid is reclaimed and returned to the blood. Microvilli are tiny finger-like folds of the cell membrane, and a dense brush border of them greatly increases the surface area for this reabsorption, the same design used by the lining of the small intestine.","notes":["Filtration slits are formed by podocytes in the visceral layer of the glomerular capsule, not by tubule cells. Choose podocytes if the question asked which cells form the filtration slits.","Correct. The brush border increases surface area for reabsorption.","Stretching to store urine is the job of transitional epithelium in the bladder. The proximal tubule does not store urine, so pick transitional epithelium if the question asked about a lining that stretches.","Microvilli do not move; cilia are the longer, moving projections that sweep material along, as in the airways. Pick cilia if the question asked how mucus is moved up the trachea."],"lt":"t-urinary-system","lid":"d3-t-urinary-system-3","comp":135,"competencyId":"w6-nephron-parts"},{"id":"L02477","dok":2,"q":"Healthy urine contains almost no protein, even though plasma is full of large proteins. Which layer of the filtration membrane blocks large plasma proteins?","a":"The basement membrane.","options":["The renal capsule","The parietal layer of the glomerular capsule","The fenestrated endothelium","The basement membrane"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The filtration membrane has three layers, and each one holds back something different. The fenestrated endothelium blocks blood cells, the basement membrane between the capillary and the podocytes blocks large plasma proteins, and the filtration slits of the podocytes hold back most of the smaller proteins that get past it. Because large proteins stop at the basement membrane, they stay in the blood instead of entering the filtrate.","notes":["The renal capsule is the fibrous covering of the whole kidney, far from the glomerulus. It would be the answer if the question asked what maintains the kidney's shape.","The parietal layer is the outer wall of the capsule, made of simple squamous epithelium, and it is not part of the filtration membrane. Choose it if the question asked for the outer layer of the glomerular capsule.","The fenestrated endothelium is the pored wall of the glomerular capillary, and its pores block blood cells rather than proteins. Pick it if the question asked which layer keeps blood cells out of the filtrate.","Correct. The basement membrane blocks large plasma proteins."],"lt":"t-urinary-system","lid":"d3x-t-urinary-system-1","comp":137,"competencyId":"w6-jga-filtration"},{"id":"N-m5-nephron-1","dok":1,"q":"Which layer of the filtration membrane holds back most of the smaller proteins that get past the basement membrane?","a":"The filtration slits of the podocytes.","options":["The capsular space","The basement membrane","The filtration slits of the podocytes","The fenestrated endothelium"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The filtration slits are the narrow gaps between the foot-like extensions of the podocytes, and thin slit membranes bridge them. They are the last of the three layers that filtrate crosses, and they hold back most of the smaller proteins that got past the basement membrane. Each layer catches something smaller than the one before it: blood cells at the endothelium, large proteins at the basement membrane, and smaller proteins at the slits.","notes":["The capsular space is where filtrate collects after crossing all three layers, not a barrier itself. Choose it if the question asked where filtrate gathers before entering the proximal convoluted tubule.","The basement membrane is the middle layer, between the capillary and the podocytes, and it holds back large plasma proteins. The smaller proteins in this question have already passed it, so pick it if the question asked which layer stops the large proteins.","Correct. The filtration slits between podocyte feet, bridged by thin slit membranes, hold back most of the smaller proteins.","The fenestrated endothelium is the pored capillary wall, the first layer of the filtration membrane, and it blocks blood cells. Pick it if the question asked which layer keeps cells in the blood."],"comp":137,"competencyId":"w6-jga-filtration"},{"id":"N-m5-nephron-2","dok":1,"q":"Which cells of the juxtaglomerular apparatus sit in the tubule wall, where the ascending limb meets the distal convoluted tubule, and sense the sodium chloride in the fluid?","a":"The macula densa cells.","options":["The macula densa cells","The podocytes","The juxtaglomerular cells","The endothelial cells"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The juxtaglomerular apparatus forms where the tubule passes beside the afferent and efferent arterioles of its own renal corpuscle. On the tubule side, where the ascending limb ends and the distal convoluted tubule begins, a patch of tall, tightly packed cells called the macula densa senses the sodium chloride level of the fluid passing by. Macula densa means dense spot, which describes how crowded these cells look. On the arteriole side, the juxtaglomerular cells release renin.","notes":["Correct. The macula densa cells sit in the tubule wall at that junction and sense the sodium chloride level.","Podocytes form the visceral layer of the glomerular capsule and create the filtration slits. They would be the answer for the cells that wrap the glomerular capillaries.","The juxtaglomerular (granular) cells are the arteriole half of the apparatus, modified smooth muscle in the afferent arteriole wall. Pick them if the question asked which cells release renin.","Endothelial cells line the glomerular capillaries as the fenestrated endothelium. Choose them if the question asked which layer of the filtration membrane blocks blood cells."],"comp":137,"competencyId":"w6-jga-filtration"},{"id":"L04436","dok":1,"q":"What is the microscopic functional unit of the kidney, the structure that actually makes urine?","a":"The nephron.","options":["The renal corpuscle","The nephron","The collecting duct","The renal pyramid"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The nephron is the microscopic functional unit of the kidney, and each kidney holds about one million of them. A nephron filters blood at its renal corpuscle and then adjusts that filtrate along its renal tubule, turning it into urine. All the larger kidney regions are simply organized groups of nephrons and the vessels and ducts that serve them.","notes":["The renal corpuscle is only the filtering head of a nephron, the glomerulus inside its capsule. It filters blood but does not finish making urine, so choose it if the question asked where filtration happens.","Correct. The nephron filters the blood and processes the filtrate into urine.","A collecting duct gathers fluid from many nephrons and carries it toward the papilla. It is shared by several nephrons rather than being the unit itself; pick it if the question asked what drains several nephrons.","A renal pyramid is a visible cone of medulla holding parts of many nephrons and collecting ducts. It is far too large to be a microscopic unit; pick it if the question asked about the cone-shaped regions of the medulla."],"lt":"t-w6-nephron-parts","lid":"w6-nephron-parts-r1","comp":135,"competencyId":"w6-nephron-parts"},{"id":"L04437","dok":1,"q":"What are the two main parts of a nephron?","a":"The renal corpuscle and the renal tubule.","options":["The afferent and efferent arterioles","The glomerulus and the collecting duct","The renal cortex and the renal medulla","The renal corpuscle and the renal tubule"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"A nephron has two main parts. The renal corpuscle is the filtering head, made of the glomerulus (a capillary tuft) inside the glomerular capsule. The renal tubule is the long tube that follows, made of the proximal convoluted tubule, the nephron loop, and the distal convoluted tubule, where the filtrate is processed into urine.","notes":["The afferent and efferent arterioles are the blood vessels that carry blood into and out of the glomerulus. They serve the nephron but are not part of it; choose them if the question asked which vessels supply the glomerulus.","The glomerulus is only part of the renal corpuscle, and the collecting duct is shared by many nephrons rather than belonging to one. This pairing mixes a piece of one part with a structure outside the nephron.","The cortex and medulla are the two regions of the whole kidney, and each nephron stretches across them. Pick them if the question asked for the outer and inner regions of kidney tissue.","Correct. Every nephron is a renal corpuscle followed by a renal tubule."],"lt":"t-w6-nephron-parts","lid":"w6-nephron-parts-r2","comp":135,"competencyId":"w6-nephron-parts"},{"id":"L04438","dok":1,"q":"Which structure is the tuft of leaky, fenestrated capillaries where blood is filtered in the kidney?","a":"The glomerulus.","options":["The vasa recta","The peritubular capillaries","The glomerular capsule","The glomerulus"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The glomerulus is a ball of capillaries inside the glomerular capsule. Its walls are fenestrated, meaning they are full of small pores, which lets water and small solutes pass out of the blood easily. This makes the glomerulus the site of filtration, the first step in making urine.","notes":["The vasa recta are long, straight capillaries that run beside the loops of juxtamedullary nephrons deep in the medulla. Pick them if the question asked which vessels follow the long nephron loops.","The peritubular capillaries come after the glomerulus and wrap around the renal tubules, where they take back useful substances. Pick them if the question asked which capillaries surround the tubules of cortical nephrons.","The glomerular capsule is the cup that surrounds the glomerulus and collects the filtrate. It is not made of capillaries; choose it if the question asked which structure catches the fluid that leaves the blood.","Correct. The glomerulus is the porous capillary tuft where blood is filtered."],"lt":"t-w6-nephron-parts","lid":"w6-nephron-parts-r3","comp":135,"competencyId":"w6-nephron-parts"},{"id":"L04439","dok":2,"q":"In the renal tubule, which segment does the filtrate enter right after it leaves the nephron loop?","a":"The distal convoluted tubule.","options":["The distal convoluted tubule","The glomerular capsule","The collecting duct","The proximal convoluted tubule"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The renal tubule segments are named in the order the filtrate meets them. After the glomerular capsule comes the proximal convoluted tubule, then the U-shaped nephron loop, then the distal convoluted tubule. Proximal means nearer the start of the tubule and distal means farther away, so the distal tubule comes after the loop.","notes":["Correct. The distal convoluted tubule follows the ascending limb of the nephron loop.","The glomerular capsule is where filtrate first collects, before any tubule segment. It would be the answer if the question asked where the filtrate starts.","The collecting duct comes after the distal convoluted tubule and gathers fluid from many nephrons. It is one step too far; choose it if the question asked where the distal tubule drains.","The proximal convoluted tubule is the first coiled segment, right after the glomerular capsule and before the loop. Pick it if the question asked which segment receives filtrate straight from the capsule."],"lt":"t-w6-nephron-parts","lid":"w6-nephron-parts-r4","comp":135,"competencyId":"w6-nephron-parts"},{"id":"L04440","dok":3,"q":"Which list traces filtrate from the glomerulus through the nephron to the collecting duct?","a":"Glomerular capsule, proximal convoluted tubule, nephron loop, distal convoluted tubule, collecting duct.","options":["Glomerular capsule, proximal convoluted tubule, distal convoluted tubule, nephron loop, collecting duct","Glomerular capsule, proximal convoluted tubule, nephron loop, distal convoluted tubule, collecting duct","Glomerular capsule, distal convoluted tubule, nephron loop, proximal convoluted tubule, collecting duct","Glomerular capsule, nephron loop, proximal convoluted tubule, distal convoluted tubule, collecting duct"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Filtrate leaves the glomerulus and collects in the capsular space of the glomerular capsule. It then flows through the proximal convoluted tubule, down and back up the nephron loop (the loop of Henle), and through the distal convoluted tubule. Finally the distal tubule drains into a collecting duct that serves many nephrons.","notes":["This puts both coiled tubules before the loop. The nephron loop sits between them, linking the proximal tubule to the distal tubule.","Correct. The segments are named in the order the filtrate meets them.","This swaps the proximal and distal tubules. Proximal means near the start, so the proximal convoluted tubule must come right after the capsule; students pick this when they mix up the two terms.","This sends filtrate into the loop straight from the capsule. The proximal convoluted tubule always comes first, before the loop dips into the medulla."],"lt":"t-w6-nephron-parts","lid":"w6-nephron-parts-r5","comp":135,"competencyId":"w6-nephron-parts"},{"id":"L04474","dok":1,"q":"What is the juxtaglomerular apparatus?","a":"The structure where the distal convoluted tubule contacts the arterioles of its own renal corpuscle.","options":["Where the collecting duct opens at the tip of a renal papilla","Where the distal tubule meets its own corpuscle's arterioles","Where the proximal tubule leaves its own glomerular capsule","Where a long nephron loop dips down beside the vasa recta"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Juxtaglomerular means next to the glomerulus. After the nephron loop rises back toward the cortex, the tubule passes right beside the afferent and efferent arterioles of its own renal corpuscle, where the ascending limb ends and the distal convoluted tubule begins. At that contact point, the macula densa cells in the tubule wall and the juxtaglomerular cells in the arteriole wall together form the juxtaglomerular apparatus, which monitors the tubule fluid and the arteriole and helps adjust filtration.","notes":["Collecting ducts end as papillary ducts at the renal papilla, deep in the medulla and far from any glomerulus. Choose this if the question asked where urine leaves the pyramid.","Correct. The juxtaglomerular apparatus is the contact point between the distal tubule and its own arterioles.","The proximal convoluted tubule begins where it leaves the glomerular capsule, but that junction has no special name and is not where the tubule touches the arterioles. Pick the proximal tubule if the question asked which segment receives filtrate straight from the capsule.","Long nephron loops run beside the vasa recta in the medulla, but that is not next to the glomerulus. Pick this if the question asked which vessels follow the loops of juxtamedullary nephrons."],"lt":"t-w6-jga-filtration","lid":"w6-jga-filtration-r1","comp":137,"competencyId":"w6-jga-filtration"},{"id":"L04475","dok":1,"q":"Which list names the three layers of the filtration membrane, from the blood side to the capsule side?","a":"Fenestrated endothelium, basement membrane, slit membranes between podocytes.","options":["Fenestrated endothelium, basement membrane, podocyte slit membranes","Renal capsule, adipose capsule, renal fascia","Basement membrane, fenestrated endothelium, podocyte slit membranes","Parietal capsule layer, capsular space, visceral capsule layer"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Blood in the glomerulus is filtered across three layers. First is the fenestrated endothelium, the pored wall of the capillary, which blocks blood cells. Next is the basement membrane, which blocks large plasma proteins. Last are the filtration slits between the feet of the podocytes, the cells of the capsule's inner layer, which hold back most of the smaller proteins.","notes":["Correct. Fluid crosses the pored capillary wall, then the basement membrane, then the slits between podocytes.","These are the three tissue layers around the whole kidney, from inner to outer. Choose this list if the question asked what surrounds and anchors the kidney.","This puts the basement membrane first, but the capillary wall itself, the fenestrated endothelium, is the first layer the blood touches. The basement membrane lies between the capillary and the podocytes.","These are the parts of the glomerular capsule, which catches the filtrate. Only the visceral layer, made of podocytes, contributes to the filtration membrane; pick this list if the question asked for the parts of the capsule."],"lt":"t-w6-jga-filtration","lid":"w6-jga-filtration-r2","comp":137,"competencyId":"w6-jga-filtration"},{"id":"L04476","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly describes the macula densa and the juxtaglomerular cells?","a":"Macula densa cells in the tubule wall sense the fluid; juxtaglomerular cells release renin.","options":["Both cell types sit in the glomerulus and filter the blood as podocytes do","Macula densa cells in the tubule wall sense the fluid; juxtaglomerular cells release renin","Macula densa cells line the afferent arteriole and release renin; juxtaglomerular cells sense the filtrate","Macula densa cells form the filtration slits; juxtaglomerular cells line the collecting duct"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The macula densa is a patch of tightly packed cells in the tubule wall, where the ascending limb meets the distal convoluted tubule, and it senses the sodium chloride level of the fluid passing by. The juxtaglomerular cells are modified smooth muscle cells in the wall of the afferent arteriole, and they release the enzyme renin when blood pressure is low. One is a tubule cell that senses, and the other is a vessel cell that releases an enzyme.","notes":["Neither cell type is part of the filtration membrane. Filtering happens across the glomerular capillary wall, basement membrane, and podocyte slits, which would be the answer if the question asked about the filter itself.","Correct. The macula densa senses the fluid in the tubule, and the juxtaglomerular cells release renin.","This swaps the two cell types. The renin-releasing cells are the juxtaglomerular cells in the arteriole wall, and the macula densa is the tubule patch that senses the fluid.","The filtration slits are formed by podocytes in the glomerular capsule, and neither cell type lines the collecting duct. Pick podocytes if the question asked which cells form the filtration slits."],"lt":"t-w6-jga-filtration","lid":"w6-jga-filtration-r3","comp":137,"competencyId":"w6-jga-filtration"},{"id":"N-a17-136-1","dok":1,"q":"Where does the renal corpuscle of a cortical nephron sit?","a":"In the outer part of the cortex.","options":["In the outer part of the cortex","In the deep cortex, next to the medulla","In the medulla, close to the renal papilla","In the medulla, beside the vasa recta"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Cortical nephrons have their renal corpuscles in the outer part of the cortex, and their short loops barely dip into the outer medulla. Juxtamedullary nephrons are the ones whose corpuscles sit in the deepest part of the cortex, right next to the medulla.","notes":["Correct. The renal corpuscle of a cortical nephron sits in the outer part of the cortex.","The deepest part of the cortex, next to the medulla, is where juxtamedullary renal corpuscles sit. Juxta means next to.","Renal corpuscles sit in the cortex, not the medulla. Only the long loops of juxtamedullary nephrons reach deep, close to the renal papilla.","The vasa recta run deep in the medulla beside the long loops of juxtamedullary nephrons. Renal corpuscles sit in the cortex."],"comp":136,"competencyId":"w6-nephron-types"},{"id":"N-a17-136-2","dok":2,"q":"The word juxtamedullary describes where these nephrons begin. What does the name tell you?","a":"Their renal corpuscles sit next to the medulla.","options":["Their renal corpuscles sit next to the medulla","Their renal corpuscles sit inside the medulla","Their renal corpuscles sit in the outer cortex","They make up about 85 percent of all nephrons"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Juxta means next to, so a juxtamedullary nephron has its renal corpuscle in the deepest part of the cortex, right next to the medulla. From there, its long loop reaches deep into the medulla, close to the renal papilla.","notes":["Correct. Juxta means next to, so the renal corpuscle sits in the deep cortex right next to the medulla.","Juxta means next to, not inside. The renal corpuscle stays in the cortex, at its deepest part beside the medulla.","Renal corpuscles in the outer cortex belong to cortical nephrons, the type with short loops.","About 85 percent is the share of cortical nephrons. Juxtamedullary nephrons are about 15 percent, and the name says nothing about numbers."],"comp":136,"competencyId":"w6-nephron-types"},{"id":"N-a17-136-3","dok":3,"q":"Why do juxtamedullary nephrons have the vasa recta running beside their loops, instead of only a capillary mesh in the cortex?","a":"Their loops reach deep into the medulla, so the capillaries must reach deep too.","options":["Their loops stay in the cortex, so they need straight capillaries to hold them","The vasa recta come straight from the renal papilla to supply the corpuscle","Their loops reach deep into the medulla, so the capillaries must reach deep too","Their corpuscles sit in the outer cortex, far from the peritubular capillaries"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Juxtamedullary nephrons have long loops that reach deep into the medulla, close to the renal papilla. A mesh that wraps the tubules in the cortex would not follow them, so long, straight capillaries, the vasa recta, run down and back up beside the loops. Cortical nephrons, with their short loops, are wrapped by the peritubular capillaries.","notes":["Juxtamedullary loops do not stay in the cortex. They are long and reach deep into the medulla, which is why their capillaries must be long too.","The vasa recta are supplied by efferent arterioles leaving the glomeruli, not by the renal papilla.","Correct. The long loops reach deep into the medulla, so the straight vasa recta run deep beside them.","Juxtamedullary corpuscles sit in the deep cortex, next to the medulla. Corpuscles in the outer cortex belong to cortical nephrons."],"comp":136,"competencyId":"w6-nephron-types"},{"id":"N-a17d-136-1","dok":1,"q":"Which capillary bed wraps around the tubules of cortical nephrons in the cortex?","a":"The peritubular capillaries.","options":["The peritubular capillaries","The straight vasa recta","The glomerular capillaries","The arcuate arteries and veins"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Cortical nephrons have short loops that stay mostly in the cortex. Their tubules are wrapped by the peritubular capillaries, a mesh of capillaries in the cortex. The long straight vasa recta belong instead to the deep loops of juxtamedullary nephrons.","notes":["Correct. The peritubular capillaries form a mesh around the tubules in the cortex, the bed that runs with cortical nephrons.","The vasa recta are the long straight capillaries that run deep into the medulla beside the long loops of juxtamedullary nephrons. Cortical nephrons have short loops, so their bed is the peritubular mesh.","The glomerulus is the first capillary bed, where blood is filtered inside the renal corpuscle. The bed around the tubules is the second bed, which comes after the efferent arteriole.","The arcuate arteries and veins are not capillaries. They arch over the bases of the pyramids at the cortex and medulla border, well upstream of the beds that surround the tubules."],"comp":136,"competencyId":"w6-nephron-types"},{"id":"N-a17d-136-2","dok":1,"q":"Which vessels carry blood into both the peritubular capillaries and the vasa recta?","a":"The efferent arterioles.","options":["The interlobar veins","The efferent arterioles","The afferent arterioles","The segmental arteries"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Blood leaves each glomerulus through an efferent arteriole. Those efferent arterioles then supply the second capillary bed, the peritubular capillaries around cortical tubules and the vasa recta beside the long juxtamedullary loops. This is why the kidney has two capillary beds in a row, with an arteriole between them.","notes":["Interlobar veins run down through the renal columns carrying blood out of the kidney. Veins drain capillary beds; they do not supply them.","Correct. Both capillary beds around the tubules are supplied by efferent arterioles leaving the glomeruli.","Afferent arterioles carry blood into the glomerulus, the first capillary bed. The beds around the tubules come after the glomerulus, so they are fed by the efferent arterioles.","Segmental arteries are branches of the renal artery in the renal sinus, near the start of the chain. Several more arteries and the glomerulus come before the beds around the tubules."],"comp":136,"competencyId":"w6-nephron-types"},{"id":"N-a17d-136-3","dok":3,"q":"A slide shows a renal corpuscle in the outer cortex, just under the renal capsule. How far will its nephron loop reach, and which capillaries will surround its tubule?","a":"Barely into the medulla, with peritubular capillaries.","options":["Deep into the medulla, with the vasa recta beside it","Deep into the medulla, with peritubular capillaries","Barely into the medulla, with the vasa recta beside it","Barely into the medulla, with peritubular capillaries"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Where the renal corpuscle sits tells you the nephron type. A corpuscle in the outer cortex marks a cortical nephron, which makes up about 85 percent of nephrons. Its loop is short and barely dips into the outer medulla, and its tubule is wrapped by the peritubular capillaries in the cortex.","notes":["This describes a juxtamedullary nephron, whose corpuscle sits in the deepest cortex next to the medulla. A corpuscle just under the capsule belongs to a cortical nephron.","A long loop that reaches deep into the medulla belongs to a juxtamedullary nephron, not one starting in the outer cortex. Those long loops are paired with the vasa recta.","The short loop is right, but the vasa recta follow the long loops of juxtamedullary nephrons deep into the medulla. A short cortical loop is served by the peritubular capillaries.","Correct. A corpuscle in the outer cortex belongs to a cortical nephron, which has a short loop and is wrapped by peritubular capillaries."],"comp":136,"competencyId":"w6-nephron-types"},{"id":"N-a17d-136-4","dok":3,"q":"A disease mainly destroys the nephrons whose loops reach deep into the medulla. About what share of nephrons is affected, and which capillary bed loses the loops it runs beside?","a":"About 15 percent, and the vasa recta.","options":["About 85 percent, and the peritubular capillaries","About 85 percent, and the vasa recta","About 15 percent, and the vasa recta","About 15 percent, and the peritubular capillaries"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Nephrons with long loops reaching deep into the medulla are juxtamedullary nephrons. They make up about 15 percent of all nephrons, and their corpuscles sit in the deep cortex next to the medulla. The vasa recta, long straight capillaries, run down and back up beside these loops, so those are the vessels paired with the lost loops.","notes":["This describes cortical nephrons, the majority, with short loops and peritubular capillaries. The question asks about the deep loops of juxtamedullary nephrons.","The vasa recta are right, but about 85 percent is the share of cortical nephrons, the ones with short loops. Juxtamedullary nephrons are the smaller group.","Correct. Long loops belong to juxtamedullary nephrons, about 15 percent of the total, and the vasa recta run beside them.","The share is right, but the peritubular capillaries wrap tubules in the cortex, the bed that goes with cortical nephrons. Deep loops are paired with the vasa recta."],"comp":136,"competencyId":"w6-nephron-types"},{"id":"N-a17d-137-1","dok":3,"q":"A cortex slide shows a patch of tall, crowded cells in a tubule wall pressed against the arterioles of its own renal corpuscle. Where in the nephron is this patch, and what do these cells sense?","a":"At the end of the ascending limb; they sense sodium chloride.","options":["In the glomerular capsule; they form filtration slits","In the afferent arteriole wall; they release renin","In the proximal convoluted tubule; they sense blood pressure","At the end of the ascending limb; they sense sodium chloride"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The tubule passes beside its own renal corpuscle between the afferent and efferent arterioles, forming the juxtaglomerular apparatus. The patch of tall, closely packed tubule cells there is the macula densa, which means dense spot. It sits where the ascending limb of the loop ends and the distal convoluted tubule begins, and its cells sense the sodium chloride in the passing fluid.","notes":["Filtration slits are the gaps between podocyte feet on the glomerulus, part of the filtration membrane. The crowded cells in a tubule wall beside the arterioles are the macula densa.","The JG (granular) cells sit mostly in the afferent arteriole wall and release renin. The patch described here is in the tubule wall, which makes it the macula densa.","The macula densa is at the start of the distal convoluted tubule, after the loop has climbed back to the cortex. Sensing low blood pressure and releasing renin is the job of the JG cells in the arteriole.","Correct. Tall, crowded tubule cells beside the arterioles are the macula densa, at the end of the ascending limb, and they sense sodium chloride."],"comp":137,"competencyId":"w6-jga-filtration"},{"id":"N-a17d-137-2","dok":3,"q":"A particle in glomerular blood slips through the pores of the capillary wall but is stopped at the very next layer. What kind of particle is it most likely, and which layer stopped it?","a":"A large plasma protein, stopped by the basement membrane.","options":["A large plasma protein, stopped by the basement membrane","A blood cell, stopped by the basement membrane","Glucose, stopped by the filtration slits","A smaller protein, stopped by the fenestrated endothelium"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The filtration membrane has three layers in a set order from the blood side: the fenestrated endothelium, the basement membrane, and the filtration slits of the podocytes. Each one holds back something smaller than the layer before it. A particle that passes the pores but stops at the next layer was stopped by the basement membrane, which holds back large plasma proteins.","notes":["Correct. The basement membrane is the layer after the fenestrated endothelium, and it holds back large plasma proteins.","Blood cells and platelets are held back by the first layer, the fenestrated endothelium. A blood cell would not get through the capillary pores in the first place.","Glucose is a small dissolved substance that passes all three layers and becomes part of the filtrate. The filtration slits are also the third layer, not the one right after the pores.","The fenestrated endothelium is the pored layer the particle already passed. Smaller proteins that get past the basement membrane are held back by the filtration slits."],"comp":137,"competencyId":"w6-jga-filtration"},{"id":"N-a17d-137-3","dok":3,"q":"In a kidney disease, the podocyte feet flatten and the filtration slits are lost, while the endothelium and basement membrane stay intact. What would start to appear in the filtrate?","a":"Smaller proteins that pass the basement membrane.","options":["Nothing new, since every protein is stopped earlier","Smaller proteins that pass the basement membrane","Red blood cells and platelets from the blood","Large plasma proteins from the blood plasma"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Each layer of the filtration membrane holds back a different size of particle: the fenestrated endothelium stops blood cells, the basement membrane stops large plasma proteins, and the filtration slits between podocyte feet stop most of the smaller proteins. If only the slits are lost, the first two layers still do their jobs. What gets through is the smaller protein the slits used to catch, which is one way protein ends up in the urine.","notes":["The basement membrane stops large plasma proteins, but smaller proteins get past it. Those smaller proteins are what the missing slits used to catch.","Correct. The filtration slits hold back the smaller proteins that get past the basement membrane, so losing the slits lets those proteins through.","Blood cells and platelets are held back by the fenestrated endothelium, which is still intact here. Losing the slits would not let cells through.","Large plasma proteins are held back by the basement membrane, which is still intact. They never reach the slits, so losing the slits does not release them."],"comp":137,"competencyId":"w6-jga-filtration"}]},{"id":"m5-urinary-tract","title":"Ureters, bladder, urethra and the path of urine","summary":"ureters, bladder wall and trigone, male and female urethra, sphincters, urine pathway, urinary disorders","mw":"2026-11-18","tr":"2026-11-19","cards":[{"id":"L02379","dok":2,"q":"Which pairing of a urinary organ with its job is correct?","a":"Kidney: filters the blood and forms urine.","options":["Kidney: filters the blood and forms urine","Urethra: carries urine from the kidney to the bladder","Urinary bladder: carries urine out of the body","Ureter: stores urine until it is released"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The kidneys are the working organs of the urinary system. They filter wastes out of the blood and form urine. The other three organs only move and hold that urine: the ureters carry it down to the bladder, the bladder stores it, and the urethra carries it to the outside of the body.","notes":["Correct. Filtering the blood and forming urine is the kidney's job; the rest of the system carries and stores the urine.","This pairing describes the ureter, not the urethra. The urethra is the single tube from the bladder to the outside, so match it with carrying urine out of the body.","This pairing gives the bladder the urethra's job. The urinary bladder is the muscular sac that stores urine, and it would be the right match for storage.","This pairing gives the ureter the bladder's job. The ureters are paired tubes that carry urine from the kidneys to the bladder, so match the ureter with transporting urine to the bladder."],"lt":"t-urinary-system","lid":"fr1","comp":133,"competencyId":"w6-kidney-gross"},{"id":"L02386","dok":3,"q":"Fluid leaves the distal convoluted tubule. Which sequence correctly traces it to the minor calyx?","a":"Collecting duct, papillary duct, renal papilla, minor calyx.","options":["Collecting duct, papillary duct, renal papilla, minor calyx","Collecting duct, renal papilla, papillary duct, minor calyx","Papillary duct, collecting duct, renal papilla, minor calyx","Collecting duct, papillary duct, major calyx, minor calyx"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The distal convoluted tubules of many nephrons drain into a collecting duct, which runs down through a renal pyramid. Near the tip of the pyramid, collecting ducts join into larger papillary ducts that open on the renal papilla. From there the urine drips into a minor calyx, the cup that fits around each papilla. The ducts get larger and fewer as you move toward the calyx.","notes":["Correct. Collecting duct, then papillary duct, then out at the renal papilla into a minor calyx.","The papillary duct opens on the renal papilla, so it comes before the papilla, not after. The papilla is the tip of the pyramid where urine leaves the ducts.","This order puts the papillary duct first. Papillary ducts are formed when collecting ducts join near the papilla, so the collecting duct has to come before them.","This order sends urine to the major calyx before the minor calyx. Minor calyces receive urine from the papillae first and then join to form the major calyces."],"lt":"t-urinary-system","lid":"fr8","comp":139,"competencyId":"w6-urine-path"},{"id":"L02390","dok":1,"q":"Which epithelium lines the inside of the ureter?","a":"Transitional epithelium.","options":["Transitional epithelium","Simple columnar epithelium","Simple cuboidal epithelium","Stratified squamous epithelium"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The ureter is lined by transitional epithelium, also called urothelium, the same stretchy lining found in the urinary bladder. Its cells can change from rounded to flattened as the wall stretches, which suits a tube that widens as each wave of urine passes. Beneath the lining are a lamina propria, a layer of smooth muscle that produces peristalsis, and an outer adventitia.","notes":["Correct. Transitional epithelium lines the ureter and lets it stretch.","Simple columnar epithelium lines the stomach and intestines, where it absorbs and secretes. Pick it if the question were about the lining of the digestive tract.","Simple cuboidal epithelium lines the renal tubules inside the kidney. Choose it if the question asked about the wall of the proximal or distal convoluted tubule.","Stratified squamous epithelium protects surfaces that take abrasion, such as the mouth, esophagus, and skin. It would be the answer for the lining of the esophagus, not a stretching urine tube."],"lt":"t-urinary-system","lid":"fr12","comp":139,"competencyId":"w6-urine-path"},{"id":"L02391","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly describes the detrusor muscle?","a":"Smooth muscle in the bladder wall that contracts to empty the bladder.","options":["Smooth muscle in the bladder wall that contracts to empty the bladder","Smooth muscle in the ureter wall that pushes urine to the bladder","Skeletal muscle around the urethra that gives voluntary control","A ring of smooth muscle that keeps the bladder outlet closed"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The detrusor muscle is the thick smooth muscle layer of the urinary bladder wall. When it contracts, it squeezes the bladder and pushes urine out into the urethra. Because it is smooth muscle, it works involuntarily, while conscious control of urination comes from the external urethral sphincter.","notes":["Correct. The detrusor is the smooth muscle of the bladder wall that empties the bladder.","This describes the muscle layer of the ureter, which moves urine to the bladder by peristalsis. Choose it if the question asked how urine travels from the kidney to the bladder.","This describes the external urethral sphincter, the voluntary skeletal muscle ring that gives conscious control over urination. Pick it if the question asked how a person holds urine on purpose.","This describes the internal urethral sphincter, the involuntary smooth muscle ring at the bladder outlet. It would be the answer for the automatic ring that keeps urine from leaking."],"lt":"t-urinary-system","lid":"fr13","comp":139,"competencyId":"w6-urine-path"},{"id":"L02392","dok":2,"q":"Which sequence correctly names the regions of the male urethra, starting at the bladder?","a":"Prostatic, membranous, spongy","options":["Spongy, membranous, prostatic","Prostatic, spongy, membranous","Membranous, prostatic, spongy","Prostatic, membranous, spongy"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Each region of the male urethra is named for what it passes through. Leaving the bladder, it runs through the prostate gland as the prostatic urethra, then through the perineum as the short membranous urethra, and finally through the penis as the spongy (penile) urethra, which lies in the corpus spongiosum. The male urethra is longer than the female urethra and carries both urine and semen.","notes":["This is the correct sequence read backward, from the outside of the body toward the bladder. It would only be right if the question asked you to start at the tip of the penis.","This order moves the membranous region to the end. The short membranous urethra crosses the perineum between the prostate and the penis, so it sits in the middle.","This order puts the membranous urethra first. The prostate sits directly below the bladder, so the urethra passes through it before reaching the perineum.","Correct. Prostatic, then membranous, then spongy."],"lt":"t-urinary-system","lid":"fr14","comp":139,"competencyId":"w6-urine-path"},{"id":"L02394","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares the internal and external urethral sphincters?","a":"The internal sphincter is involuntary smooth muscle at the bladder outlet; the external sphincter is voluntary skeletal muscle around the urethra.","options":["The internal sphincter is involuntary smooth muscle at the bladder outlet; the external sphincter is voluntary skeletal muscle around the urethra.","Both sphincters are involuntary smooth muscle, so neither one can be controlled consciously.","The internal sphincter is voluntary skeletal muscle at the bladder outlet; the external sphincter is involuntary smooth muscle around the urethra.","The internal sphincter lies in the wall of the ureter; the external sphincter lies at the bladder outlet."],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The bladder outlet is guarded by two rings of muscle. The internal urethral sphincter is involuntary smooth muscle where the bladder opens into the urethra, so it stays closed without any thought. The external urethral sphincter is voluntary skeletal muscle that surrounds the urethra farther along its path, and it gives conscious control over when urination happens.","notes":["Correct. Internal is involuntary smooth muscle; external is voluntary skeletal muscle.","Only the internal sphincter is involuntary. The external sphincter is skeletal muscle, which is why people can hold urine on purpose, so this statement leaves out voluntary control entirely.","This statement swaps the two muscle types. The internal sphincter is the automatic smooth muscle ring, and the external sphincter is the skeletal muscle ring you can control.","Neither sphincter lies in the ureter; the ureters enter the bladder without a named sphincter. The internal sphincter is at the bladder outlet, and the external sphincter surrounds the urethra beyond it."],"lt":"t-urinary-system","lid":"fr16","comp":139,"competencyId":"w6-urine-path"},{"id":"L02398","dok":1,"q":"Which paired tubes carry urine from the kidneys to the bladder?","a":"The ureters.","options":["The ureters","The major calyces","The collecting ducts","The urethra"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The ureters are a pair of muscular tubes, one from each kidney, that carry urine from the renal pelvis down to the urinary bladder. Waves of smooth muscle contraction, called peristalsis, push the urine along. The names look alike, but there are two ureters, and the urethra is the single exit.","notes":["Correct. The ureters carry urine from the kidneys to the bladder.","The major calyces are chambers inside the kidney that channel urine from the minor calyces into the renal pelvis. Choose them if the question asked what drains directly into the renal pelvis.","The collecting ducts are microscopic ducts inside the kidney that gather fluid from many nephrons. They would be the answer for a structure inside the renal pyramids, not a tube between organs.","The urethra is a single tube that carries urine from the bladder to the outside of the body. Students often mix up the two names; pick the urethra if the question asked which tube empties the bladder."],"lt":"t-urinary-system","lid":"c4","comp":139,"competencyId":"w6-urine-path"},{"id":"L02399","dok":1,"q":"Which tube carries urine from the bladder to the outside of the body?","a":"The urethra.","options":["The collecting duct","The renal pelvis","The urethra","The ureter"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The urethra is the last structure in the path of urine. It leaves the bladder at the internal urethral orifice and carries urine to the outside of the body, passing the internal and external urethral sphincters on the way. It is longer in males, where it also carries semen, and shorter in females.","notes":["The collecting duct is a microscopic duct inside the kidney that gathers fluid from many nephrons. Choose it if the question asked about a duct in the renal pyramids.","The renal pelvis is the funnel-shaped cavity inside the kidney that collects urine and passes it into the ureter. It would be the answer for the space where the calyces drain.","Correct. The urethra carries urine from the bladder out of the body.","The ureter carries urine into the bladder from the kidney, not out of it. Pick the ureter if the question asked which tube connects each kidney to the bladder."],"lt":"t-urinary-system","lid":"c5","comp":139,"competencyId":"w6-urine-path"},{"id":"L02402","dok":1,"q":"What is the smooth muscle layer of the bladder wall that contracts to empty the bladder?","a":"The detrusor muscle.","options":["The detrusor muscle","The internal urethral sphincter","The external urethral sphincter","The trigone"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The detrusor muscle is the thick smooth muscle layer that makes up most of the bladder wall. When it contracts, it squeezes the bladder and pushes urine into the urethra. The name comes from a Latin word meaning to push down or thrust away, which matches its job.","notes":["Correct. The detrusor muscle empties the bladder.","The internal urethral sphincter is an involuntary smooth muscle ring at the bladder outlet that keeps urine in, not a layer of the whole wall. It would be the answer for the automatic ring at the bladder neck.","The external urethral sphincter is a ring of voluntary skeletal muscle around the urethra. Choose it if the question asked which muscle gives conscious control over urination.","The trigone is a smooth triangular area on the floor of the bladder, marked by the two ureter openings and the urethral opening. Pick it if the question asked about the landmark at the base of the bladder."],"lt":"t-urinary-system","lid":"c8","comp":139,"competencyId":"w6-urine-path"},{"id":"L02405","dok":1,"q":"Which muscular sac stores urine until it is released?","a":"The urinary bladder.","options":["The ureter","The renal pelvis","The urinary bladder","The urethra"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The urinary bladder is a hollow, muscular sac in the pelvic cavity that stores urine. Its wall is lined with stretchy transitional epithelium and folds called rugae, so it can expand as it fills. When it is time to empty, the detrusor muscle in its wall contracts.","notes":["The ureter is a transport tube that moves urine from the kidney to the bladder by peristalsis. It would be the answer for the structure that carries urine down to the bladder.","The renal pelvis is a funnel-shaped cavity inside the kidney that passes urine on to the ureter rather than storing it. Pick it if the question asked what collects urine from the calyces.","Correct. The urinary bladder stores urine.","The urethra is the exit tube from the bladder to the outside. Choose it if the question asked which structure carries urine out of the body."],"lt":"t-urinary-system","lid":"c11","comp":139,"competencyId":"w6-urine-path"},{"id":"L02414","dok":2,"q":"Where is the trigone found?","a":"On the floor of the urinary bladder.","options":["On the floor of the urinary bladder","Within the wall of the ureter","At the tip of a renal pyramid","Inside the renal pelvis"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The trigone is a smooth triangular area on the floor (base) of the urinary bladder. Its three corners are the two ureteral openings, where urine enters, and the internal urethral orifice, where urine leaves. Unlike the rest of the bladder lining, it stays smooth rather than folding into rugae.","notes":["Correct. The trigone is on the floor of the urinary bladder.","The ureter wall has a transitional epithelium lining and a smooth muscle layer for peristalsis, but no triangular landmark. The ureters only meet the trigone where they open into the bladder at two of its corners.","The tip of a renal pyramid is the renal papilla, where urine drains into a minor calyx. Pick that location if the question asked about the renal papilla.","The renal pelvis is the funnel-shaped cavity inside the kidney that collects urine from the calyces. It has no trigone; choose it if the question asked where the major calyces empty."],"lt":"t-urinary-system","lid":"c20","comp":139,"competencyId":"w6-urine-path"},{"id":"L02415","dok":2,"q":"Which urethral sphincter is the voluntary ring of skeletal muscle?","a":"The external urethral sphincter.","options":["The trigone","The detrusor muscle","The external urethral sphincter","The internal urethral sphincter"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The external urethral sphincter is a ring of skeletal muscle around the urethra, and skeletal muscle is under voluntary control. That is why a person can choose to hold urine or let it go. The internal urethral sphincter, closer to the bladder, is smooth muscle and works automatically.","notes":["The trigone is a smooth triangular area on the bladder floor, a landmark rather than a muscle ring. Choose it if the question asked about the area marked by the ureter and urethral openings.","The detrusor muscle is the smooth muscle of the bladder wall that contracts to empty the bladder, not a sphincter. It would be the answer for the muscle that pushes urine out.","Correct. The external urethral sphincter is the voluntary skeletal muscle ring.","The internal urethral sphincter is an involuntary ring of smooth muscle at the bladder outlet. Pick it if the question asked which sphincter keeps the outlet closed without conscious effort."],"lt":"t-urinary-system","lid":"c21","comp":139,"competencyId":"w6-urine-path"},{"id":"L02416","dok":2,"q":"Which statement is true of the female urethra compared with the male urethra?","a":"It is shorter and carries only urine.","options":["It has no external sphincter.","It is shorter and carries only urine.","It opens into the vaginal canal.","It is longer and also carries semen."],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The female urethra is short and carries only urine, opening to the outside between the clitoris and the vaginal opening. The male urethra is longer, passes through the prostate, perineum, and penis, and carries both urine and semen. The short female urethra is one reason urinary tract infections are more common in women.","notes":["Both sexes have an external urethral sphincter of voluntary skeletal muscle. This choice confuses the shorter length of the female urethra with missing parts.","Correct. The female urethra is shorter and carries only urine.","The female urethra opens separately, between the clitoris and the vaginal opening, not into the vagina. This mix-up comes from the two openings sitting close together in the vulva.","This describes the male urethra, which runs through the prostate, perineum, and penis and carries both urine and semen. Pick it if the question asked about the male urethra."],"lt":"t-urinary-system","lid":"c22","comp":139,"competencyId":"w6-urine-path"},{"id":"L02417","dok":2,"q":"Glomerulonephritis is inflammation of which structures?","a":"The glomeruli.","options":["The ureters","The renal pelvis","The glomeruli","The renal pyramids"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Glomerulonephritis is inflammation of the glomeruli, the capillary tufts where blood is filtered. The name breaks down into glomerulo (the glomeruli), nephr (kidney), and itis (inflammation). Because the glomeruli are the filters, inflaming them impairs the kidney's ability to filter the blood.","notes":["The ureters are the tubes from the kidneys to the bladder. They are where a kidney stone causes pain, so pick them if the question asked where a stone becomes painful.","The renal pelvis is the funnel that collects urine and passes it to the ureter, and it is where kidney stones often form. It would be the answer if the question asked where a stone first forms.","Correct. Glomerulonephritis is inflammation of the glomeruli.","The renal pyramids are the cone-shaped masses of the medulla that hold the nephron loops and collecting ducts. Choose them if the question asked what makes up the medulla."],"lt":"t-urinary-system","lid":"c23","comp":140,"competencyId":"w6-urinary-disorders"},{"id":"L02419","dok":2,"q":"Urinary incontinence, the loss of voluntary control over urination, involves which structures?","a":"The urethral sphincters.","options":["The urethral sphincters","The renal pelvis","The glomeruli","The ureters"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Urinary incontinence is the loss of voluntary control over urination. The structures involved are the urethral sphincters, the detrusor muscle, and the pelvic floor. In stress incontinence a weak pelvic floor and external sphincter leak with coughing or lifting, and in urge incontinence the detrusor contracts too readily.","notes":["Correct. The urethral sphincters guard the bladder outlet, so they are among the structures involved in incontinence.","The renal pelvis collects urine inside the kidney and passes it to the ureter. Choose it if the question asked where kidney stones often form.","The glomeruli are the filtering capillary tufts of the nephrons, far upstream of the bladder. Pick them if the question asked about glomerulonephritis.","The ureters carry urine to the bladder by peristalsis and play no part in holding urine in. They would be the answer for where a kidney stone causes pain."],"lt":"t-urinary-system","lid":"c25","comp":140,"competencyId":"w6-urinary-disorders"},{"id":"L02420","dok":2,"q":"How do the ureters move urine from the kidneys to the bladder?","a":"By peristalsis, waves of smooth muscle contraction.","options":["By contraction of the external urethral sphincter","By waves of smooth muscle contraction (peristalsis)","By gravity alone, with no muscle action","By contraction of the detrusor muscle"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The ureter wall has a layer of smooth muscle that contracts in waves, called peristalsis, squeezing urine down toward the bladder. Because the muscle does the pushing, urine still reaches the bladder when a person is lying down. The same kind of wave moves food along the digestive tract.","notes":["The external urethral sphincter is a skeletal muscle ring around the urethra that holds urine in on purpose. It would be the answer for voluntary control of urination, not transport down the ureter.","Correct. Peristalsis in the ureter wall moves urine to the bladder.","Gravity helps when a person is upright, but urine still reaches the bladder when someone lies flat. This choice comes from picturing the ureters as passive drainpipes rather than muscular tubes.","The detrusor muscle is in the bladder wall and contracts to empty the bladder into the urethra. Pick it if the question asked how urine leaves the bladder."],"lt":"t-urinary-system","lid":"c26","comp":139,"competencyId":"w6-urine-path"},{"id":"L02426","dok":2,"q":"Which inherited disorder fills the kidneys with many enlarging, fluid-filled cysts?","a":"Polycystic kidney disease.","options":["Polycystic kidney disease","Kidney stones","Glomerulonephritis","Renal failure"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Polycystic kidney disease is an inherited disorder in which many fluid-filled cysts grow in the kidneys and enlarge them. The name says it: poly means many, and cystic means cysts. As the cysts crowd out working kidney tissue, they can lead to renal failure.","notes":["Correct. Polycystic kidney disease is the inherited cyst disorder.","Kidney stones are hardened mineral deposits that form in the kidney and can lodge in the ureter. Choose them if the question described solid deposits blocking the flow of urine.","Glomerulonephritis is inflammation of the glomeruli that impairs filtration, not an inherited cyst disease. Pick it if the question described inflamed filtering capillaries.","Renal failure is the loss of the kidney's ability to filter the blood, either acute or chronic. It can be the end result of polycystic kidney disease, but it is the outcome rather than the cyst disorder itself."],"lt":"t-urinary-system","lid":"c32","comp":140,"competencyId":"w6-urinary-disorders"},{"id":"L02427","dok":2,"q":"Which disorder consists of hardened mineral deposits that form in the kidney and can lodge in the ureter?","a":"Kidney stones.","options":["Urinary incontinence","Kidney stones","Renal failure","Glomerulonephritis"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Kidney stones are hardened mineral deposits that form in the kidney, often in the renal pelvis. A stone can travel into the ureter, a tube only millimeters wide, where it scrapes the wall and blocks the flow of urine. That is why the pain usually starts when the stone enters the ureter.","notes":["Urinary incontinence is the loss of voluntary control over urination, involving the detrusor, the urethral sphincters, or the pelvic floor. It would be the answer if the question described leaking urine.","Correct. Kidney stones are hardened mineral deposits in the urinary tract.","Renal failure is the loss of the kidney's ability to filter the blood. Choose it if the question described kidneys that can no longer clean the blood.","Glomerulonephritis is inflammation of the glomeruli, not a solid deposit. Pick it if the question described inflamed filtering capillaries that impair filtration."],"lt":"t-urinary-system","lid":"c33","comp":140,"competencyId":"w6-urinary-disorders"},{"id":"L02429","dok":2,"q":"Which urinary disorder is an infection of the urethra, bladder, or kidneys, usually by bacteria?","a":"Urinary tract infection.","options":["Kidney stones","Urinary tract infection","Polycystic kidney disease","Renal failure"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"A urinary tract infection is an infection anywhere along the urinary tract, usually by bacteria that enter through the urethra and climb toward the bladder and sometimes the kidneys. It is more common in women, in part because the female urethra is short and gives bacteria a shorter climb to the bladder.","notes":["Kidney stones are hardened mineral deposits that can lodge in the ureter. Choose them if the question described a solid deposit causing sharp pain along the ureter.","Correct. A urinary tract infection is a bacterial infection of the urinary tract.","Polycystic kidney disease is an inherited disorder of many fluid-filled cysts in the kidneys. It would be the answer if the question described enlarged kidneys full of cysts.","Renal failure is the loss of the kidney's ability to filter the blood, not an infection. Pick it if the question described kidneys that can no longer clean the blood."],"lt":"t-urinary-system","lid":"c35","comp":140,"competencyId":"w6-urinary-disorders"},{"id":"L02430","dok":2,"q":"Which structure is the involuntary smooth muscle ring at the bladder outlet?","a":"The internal urethral sphincter.","options":["The detrusor muscle","The external urethral sphincter","The trigone","The internal urethral sphincter"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The internal urethral sphincter is a ring of smooth muscle where the bladder opens into the urethra. Because it is smooth muscle, it works automatically and keeps the outlet closed without conscious effort. Farther along the urethra, the external urethral sphincter adds voluntary control.","notes":["The detrusor muscle is the smooth muscle of the whole bladder wall, and it contracts to empty the bladder rather than close it. Pick it if the question asked which muscle pushes urine out.","The external urethral sphincter is a ring of voluntary skeletal muscle around the urethra, beyond the bladder outlet. It would be the answer for the sphincter you control on purpose.","The trigone is a smooth triangular area on the bladder floor, a landmark rather than a ring of muscle. Choose it if the question asked about the area marked by the ureter and urethral openings.","Correct. The internal urethral sphincter is the involuntary ring at the bladder outlet."],"lt":"t-urinary-system","lid":"c36","comp":139,"competencyId":"w6-urine-path"},{"id":"L02433","dok":2,"q":"What is the smooth triangular area on the bladder floor, marked by the two ureter openings and the urethral opening?","a":"The trigone.","options":["The renal pelvis","The detrusor muscle","The trigone","The internal urethral orifice"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The trigone is the smooth triangle on the floor of the bladder. Its two back corners are the ureteral openings, where urine enters from the ureters, and its front corner is the internal urethral orifice, where urine leaves. Its name comes from the word for a triangle.","notes":["The renal pelvis is the funnel inside the kidney that passes urine to the ureter, not part of the bladder. Choose it if the question asked where the calyces drain.","The detrusor muscle is the smooth muscle layer throughout the bladder wall, not a marked area on the floor. Pick it if the question asked which muscle contracts to empty the bladder.","Correct. The trigone is the triangle marked by the three openings.","The internal urethral orifice is only one corner of the trigone, the opening where urine leaves the bladder. It would be the answer if the question asked for the opening into the urethra."],"lt":"t-urinary-system","lid":"c39","comp":139,"competencyId":"w6-urine-path"},{"id":"L02434","dok":3,"q":"Which sequence correctly traces urine from the collecting duct to the ureter?","a":"Collecting duct, renal papilla, minor calyx, major calyx, renal pelvis, ureter.","options":["Collecting duct, renal papilla, minor calyx, major calyx, renal pelvis, ureter","Collecting duct, minor calyx, renal papilla, major calyx, renal pelvis, ureter","Collecting duct, renal papilla, major calyx, minor calyx, renal pelvis, ureter","Collecting duct, renal pelvis, major calyx, minor calyx, renal papilla, ureter"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Collecting ducts carry urine down through a pyramid to its tip, the renal papilla. Urine drips from the papilla into a minor calyx, several minor calyces join into a major calyx, and the major calyces empty into the renal pelvis. The renal pelvis narrows at the hilum into the ureter. The collecting spaces get larger at every step.","notes":["Correct. Papilla, minor calyx, major calyx, renal pelvis, then ureter.","This order puts the minor calyx before the papilla. The papilla is the tip of the pyramid where urine leaves the tissue, so it must come first, and the minor calyx catches what drips from it.","This order swaps the minor and major calyces. Minor calyces catch urine from the papillae and then join to form the larger major calyces.","This order runs the collecting spaces backward after the collecting duct. The renal pelvis is the last and largest space, so it comes right before the ureter, not right after the collecting duct."],"lt":"t-urinary-system","lid":"c40","comp":139,"competencyId":"w6-urine-path"},{"id":"L02436","dok":2,"q":"Which sequence correctly traces urine from the renal pelvis until it leaves the body?","a":"Renal pelvis, ureter, urinary bladder, urethra.","options":["Renal pelvis, ureter, urinary bladder, urethra","Renal pelvis, ureter, urethra, urinary bladder","Renal pelvis, urinary bladder, ureter, urethra","Renal pelvis, urethra, urinary bladder, ureter"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"From the renal pelvis, urine flows into the ureter, which carries it down to the urinary bladder by peristalsis. The bladder stores the urine until it is released through the urethra to the outside. A quick check is that the bladder sits between the two look-alike tubes: ureter in, urethra out.","notes":["Correct. Renal pelvis, ureter, urinary bladder, then urethra.","This order puts the urethra before the bladder. The urethra is the exit tube, so urine must be stored in the bladder before it reaches the urethra.","This order skips the ureter between the kidney and the bladder. The renal pelvis is inside the kidney and cannot drain directly into the bladder; the ureter connects the two.","This order swaps the ureter and urethra. The ureter leaves the kidney and enters the bladder, while the urethra leaves the bladder for the outside."],"lt":"t-urinary-system","lid":"c42","comp":139,"competencyId":"w6-urine-path"},{"id":"L02437","dok":3,"q":"Starting at the glomerular capsule, which sequence correctly traces fluid all the way out of the body?","a":"Glomerular capsule, renal tubule, collecting duct, calyces, renal pelvis, ureter, bladder, urethra.","options":["Glomerular capsule, collecting duct, renal tubule, calyces, renal pelvis, ureter, bladder, urethra","Glomerular capsule, renal tubule, collecting duct, renal pelvis, calyces, ureter, bladder, urethra","Glomerular capsule, renal tubule, collecting duct, calyces, renal pelvis, ureter, bladder, urethra","Glomerular capsule, renal tubule, calyces, collecting duct, renal pelvis, bladder, ureter, urethra"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Filtrate captured in the glomerular capsule flows through the renal tubule of its own nephron and then into a collecting duct shared with other nephrons. From the renal papilla it drips into the calyces, gathers in the renal pelvis, and leaves the kidney through the ureter. The ureter carries it to the bladder for storage, and the urethra carries it out of the body.","notes":["This order puts the collecting duct before the renal tubule. The renal tubule belongs to the nephron itself and empties into the collecting duct, so the tubule comes first.","This order swaps the calyces and the renal pelvis. The calyces catch urine from the papillae and then empty into the renal pelvis, which leads to the ureter.","Correct. Tubule, collecting duct, calyces, renal pelvis, ureter, bladder, urethra.","This order places the calyces before the collecting duct and the bladder before the ureter. Collecting ducts deliver urine to the calyces, and the ureter delivers urine to the bladder."],"lt":"t-urinary-system","lid":"c43","comp":139,"competencyId":"w6-urine-path"},{"id":"L02440","dok":1,"q":"Which four organs make up the urinary system?","a":"The kidneys, ureters, urinary bladder, and urethra.","options":["The kidneys, ureters, urinary bladder, and urethra","The kidneys, nephrons, glomeruli, and calyces","The renal artery, renal vein, ureter, and bladder","The cortex, medulla, renal pelvis, and ureter"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The urinary system is made of four organs: the two kidneys, which filter the blood and form urine; the two ureters, which carry urine down; the urinary bladder, which stores it; and the urethra, which carries it out of the body. Everything else in the urinary system, such as nephrons and calyces, is a part inside one of these organs.","notes":["Correct. The kidneys, ureters, urinary bladder, and urethra are the four organs.","Nephrons, glomeruli, and calyces are all parts inside the kidney, not separate organs. Pick a list like this if the question asked for structures found within a kidney.","The renal artery and renal vein are blood vessels that serve the kidney. They belong to the circulatory system, so pick them if the question asked which vessels enter and leave at the hilum.","The cortex, medulla, and renal pelvis are regions inside a single kidney. This list would fit a question about what a frontal section of the kidney shows, not the organs of the system."],"lt":"t-urinary-system","lid":"c1-x2","comp":133,"competencyId":"w6-kidney-gross"},{"id":"L02462","dok":2,"q":"Which three openings mark the corners of the trigone?","a":"The two ureteral openings and the internal urethral orifice.","options":["One ureteral opening and two urethral openings","Two urethral sphincters and one ureteral opening","Two ureteral openings and the external urethral orifice","Two ureteral openings and the internal urethral orifice"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The trigone is the smooth triangular area on the floor of the bladder. Its two back corners are the ureteral openings, where each ureter delivers urine into the bladder, and its front corner is the internal urethral orifice, where urine leaves the bladder for the urethra. Two openings in and one opening out gives the triangle.","notes":["There are two ureters but only one urethra, so the bladder has two ureteral openings and one urethral opening. This choice reverses the count.","The urethral sphincters are muscle rings around the outlet, not openings in the bladder floor. This choice mixes up the muscles that guard an opening with the openings themselves.","The external urethral orifice is at the far end of the urethra, on the outside of the body, not inside the bladder. Pick it if the question asked where urine finally exits the body.","Correct. Two ureteral openings and the internal urethral orifice mark the trigone."],"lt":"t-urinary-system","lid":"c23-x2","comp":139,"competencyId":"w6-urine-path"},{"id":"L02465","dok":3,"q":"After rapid weight loss, a patient's kidney loses its fat support and drops from its normal position, kinking the ureter. Which disorder is this?","a":"Nephroptosis.","options":["Renal failure","Glomerulonephritis","Nephroptosis","Polycystic kidney disease"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Nephroptosis, also called a floating kidney, happens when the kidney drops from its normal position because the adipose capsule that holds it in place is lost. As the kidney sags, the ureter can kink and block the flow of urine. The name means a falling kidney: nephro (kidney) plus ptosis (drooping).","notes":["Renal failure is the loss of the kidney's ability to filter the blood. A badly blocked ureter could eventually harm the kidney, but the dropped kidney itself is nephroptosis.","Glomerulonephritis is inflammation of the glomeruli that impairs filtration, not a change in the kidney's position. Pick it if the question described inflamed filtering capillaries.","Correct. A kidney that drops after losing its fat support is nephroptosis.","Polycystic kidney disease is an inherited disorder in which fluid-filled cysts enlarge the kidneys. It would be the answer if the question described kidneys full of cysts."],"lt":"t-urinary-system","lid":"c26-x2","comp":140,"competencyId":"w6-urinary-disorders"},{"id":"L02466","dok":3,"q":"A kidney stone forms in the renal pelvis and causes no pain until it moves into the next tube along the path of urine, only millimeters wide. Which tube is that?","a":"The ureter.","options":["The urethra","The papillary duct","The ureter","The collecting duct"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Urine leaves the renal pelvis through the ureter, a narrow muscular tube only millimeters wide. A stone sitting in the roomy renal pelvis may cause no symptoms, but once it enters the ureter it scrapes the wall and blocks the flow of urine, causing sharp pain. Knowing the path of urine tells you where the pain will be.","notes":["The urethra carries urine from the bladder to the outside, so a stone would have to pass the ureter and the bladder before reaching it. Pick it if the question asked about the last tube in the path.","The papillary duct opens on the renal papilla before urine reaches the calyces and pelvis, so it is upstream of the stone. It would be the answer for the duct that empties onto the papilla.","Correct. The ureter is the narrow tube that follows the renal pelvis.","The collecting duct is a microscopic duct inside a renal pyramid, upstream of the renal pelvis. A stone in the pelvis cannot move backward into it."],"lt":"t-urinary-system","lid":"c27-x2","comp":140,"competencyId":"w6-urinary-disorders"},{"id":"L02467","dok":3,"q":"A patient's kidney filters are inflamed, and the kidneys cannot filter the blood properly. Testing shows the problem is in the capillary tufts of the nephrons. Which disorder fits?","a":"Glomerulonephritis.","options":["Kidney stones","Glomerulonephritis","Urinary incontinence","Nephroptosis"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The capillary tufts of the nephrons are the glomeruli, so inflammation there is glomerulonephritis. Because the glomeruli are where the blood is filtered, inflaming them impairs filtration. Reading the name in pieces gives the answer: glomerulo (glomeruli), nephr (kidney), itis (inflammation).","notes":["Kidney stones are hardened mineral deposits that can block the ureter, not inflammation of the filters. Pick them if the question described sharp pain as a stone moves down the ureter.","Correct. Inflammation of the glomeruli is glomerulonephritis.","Urinary incontinence is the loss of voluntary control over urination, involving the detrusor, the urethral sphincters, or the pelvic floor. It would be the answer if the question described leaking urine.","Nephroptosis is a kidney that drops out of position after losing its fat support. Choose it if the question described a sagging kidney that kinks the ureter."],"lt":"t-urinary-system","lid":"c28-x2","comp":140,"competencyId":"w6-urinary-disorders"},{"id":"L02468","dok":3,"q":"Urinary tract infections are more common in women. Which structural feature is one reason for this?","a":"The female urethra is shorter","options":["The female bladder is larger","The female ureters are wider","The female kidneys sit lower","The female urethra is shorter"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Most urinary tract infections start when bacteria enter through the urethra and climb up to the bladder. The female urethra is much shorter than the male urethra, so bacteria have a shorter distance to travel before they reach the bladder. The anatomy of the urethra helps predict who is at higher risk.","notes":["Bladder size is not the reason infections are more common in women. This choice comes from assuming a bigger organ is more exposed, but the deciding feature is the length of the entry tube.","Bacteria usually climb in through the urethra, not the ureters, so the width of the ureters does not explain the higher risk. Pick the ureters only if the question asked how urine travels from kidney to bladder.","Kidney position does not differ by sex in a way that explains infection risk; the right kidney usually sits lower in both sexes because of the liver. That fact would answer which kidney sits lower, not why infections are more common.","Correct. The shorter female urethra gives bacteria a shorter climb to the bladder."],"lt":"t-urinary-system","lid":"c29-x2","comp":140,"competencyId":"w6-urinary-disorders"},{"id":"L02474","dok":3,"q":"The bladder is lined with transitional epithelium and has folds called rugae. How do these features suit the bladder's job?","a":"They let the bladder expand as it fills and shrink when empty without tearing.","options":["They keep the bladder at the same size whether it is full or empty","They let the bladder expand as it fills and shrink when empty without tearing","They move urine back into the blood through the thin wall of the bladder","They contract to push urine out of the bladder into the urethra"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The bladder changes volume greatly as it fills and empties. Transitional epithelium has cells that flatten as the wall stretches and round up again when it relaxes, and the rugae are folds that unfold as the bladder fills. Together they let the bladder hold a large volume and then return to a small size without damaging its lining.","notes":["This is the opposite of what these features do. A lining that could not change size would tear as urine filled the bladder.","Correct. The stretchy lining and rugae let the bladder expand and shrink.","The bladder stores urine; it does not return urine to the blood. Reclaiming water and nutrients happens in the renal tubules, so pick the tubules if the question asked where filtrate is reabsorbed.","Contracting to push urine out is the job of the detrusor muscle in the bladder wall, not the epithelium or the rugae. Choose the detrusor if the question asked which layer empties the bladder."],"lt":"t-urinary-system","lid":"d3-t-urinary-system-5","comp":139,"competencyId":"w6-urine-path"},{"id":"L02475","dok":3,"q":"Two sphincters guard the bladder outlet, one involuntary and one under conscious control. Why is it useful to have both?","a":"The internal one keeps the outlet closed automatically, and the external one lets a person choose when to urinate.","options":["The internal one keeps the outlet closed automatically, and the external one lets a person choose when to urinate","Both are voluntary, so a person must consciously hold each one closed at all times","The external one keeps the outlet closed automatically, and the internal one lets a person choose when to urinate","Both are involuntary, so urination happens on its own without any conscious control"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Continence needs both reliable closure and conscious timing. The internal urethral sphincter is smooth muscle, so it keeps the bladder outlet closed automatically and urine does not leak while a person is busy or asleep. The external urethral sphincter is skeletal muscle, so a person can hold urine or release it by choice. Each muscle type matches the kind of control it provides.","notes":["Correct. The internal sphincter gives automatic closure and the external sphincter gives voluntary control.","Only the external sphincter is voluntary. If both needed conscious effort, a person would leak urine whenever their attention drifted or they fell asleep.","This choice swaps the two sphincters. The internal sphincter is smooth muscle and works automatically, while the external sphincter is skeletal muscle under voluntary control.","Only the internal sphincter is involuntary. The external sphincter of skeletal muscle is what lets people decide when to urinate, so this choice leaves out voluntary control."],"lt":"t-urinary-system","lid":"d3-t-urinary-system-6","comp":139,"competencyId":"w6-urine-path"},{"id":"N-m5-urinary-tract-1","dok":1,"q":"Which disorder is the loss of the kidney's ability to filter the blood, either acute or chronic?","a":"Renal failure.","options":["Renal failure","Urinary tract infection","Nephroptosis","Urinary incontinence"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Renal failure is the loss of the kidney's ability to filter the blood. It can be acute, developing quickly, or chronic, developing over a long time. Other kidney disorders, such as glomerulonephritis or polycystic kidney disease, can lead to renal failure when they damage enough working tissue.","notes":["Correct. Renal failure is the loss of the kidney's filtering ability.","A urinary tract infection is a bacterial infection of the urethra, bladder, or kidneys. Choose it if the question described bacteria climbing up the urinary tract.","Nephroptosis is a kidney that drops out of position after losing its fat support. It would be the answer if the question described a floating kidney that kinks the ureter.","Urinary incontinence is the loss of voluntary control over urination, involving the detrusor, the urethral sphincters, or the pelvic floor, while filtering may be normal. Pick it if the question described leaking urine."],"comp":140,"competencyId":"w6-urinary-disorders"},{"id":"N-m5-urinary-tract-2","dok":1,"q":"Where does the female urethra open to the outside of the body?","a":"Between the clitoris and the vaginal opening.","options":["Between the clitoris and the vaginal opening","Between the vaginal opening and the anus","Inside the vaginal canal","On the glans of the clitoris"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The female urethra is short and carries only urine. Its external opening lies in the vulva, between the clitoris in front and the vaginal opening behind it. Knowing this order from front to back (clitoris, urethral opening, vaginal opening, then anus) is useful in the lab and in clinical skills such as placing a urinary catheter.","notes":["Correct. The female urethra opens between the clitoris and the vaginal opening.","The skin between the vaginal opening and the anus is part of the perineum, and no urinary structure opens there. This choice comes from placing the urethra behind the vaginal opening instead of in front of it.","The urethra has its own separate opening and does not empty into the vagina. This choice comes from the two openings sitting close together in the vulva.","The clitoris contains no urethra in females, unlike the penis, which carries the spongy urethra. Choose the glans of the penis if the question asked where the male urethra opens."],"comp":139,"competencyId":"w6-urine-path"},{"id":"L04427","dok":1,"q":"Which list names the four organs of the urinary system?","a":"The kidneys, ureters, urinary bladder, and urethra.","options":["Kidneys, ureters, prostate, urethra","Kidneys, ureters, urinary bladder, urethra","Kidneys, adrenal glands, ureters, urethra","Kidneys, renal pelvis, ureters, bladder"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The urinary system has four organs. The kidneys filter the blood and form urine, the ureters carry urine down to the bladder, the urinary bladder stores it, and the urethra carries it out of the body. Every other urinary structure you learn, such as the calyces or the renal pelvis, is a part of one of these four organs.","notes":["The prostate surrounds the first part of the male urethra, but it is a male reproductive gland, and this list leaves out the bladder. Pick the prostate if the question were about which accessory gland the urethra passes through.","Correct. These are the four organs, in the order urine passes through them.","The adrenal glands sit on top of the kidneys but they are endocrine glands, not urinary organs, so they never handle urine. Pick them if a question asked which glands cap the superior pole of each kidney.","The renal pelvis is a real urine-carrying space, but it is a part inside the kidney rather than a separate organ, so this list counts the kidney twice and leaves out the urethra. Choose the renal pelvis when a question asks what funnels urine from the calyces into the ureter."],"lt":"t-w6-kidney-gross","lid":"w6-kidney-gross-r1","comp":133,"competencyId":"w6-kidney-gross"},{"id":"L04441","dok":1,"q":"Which structure carries urine from the kidney to the bladder, and how is the urine moved?","a":"The ureter, by peristalsis.","options":["The ureter, by gravity alone","The renal pelvis, by cilia","The ureter, by peristalsis","The urethra, by peristalsis"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Each ureter is a muscular tube that runs from the renal pelvis down to the urinary bladder. Waves of smooth muscle contraction called peristalsis push urine along the tube, the same kind of squeezing that moves food through the gut. Because of peristalsis, urine reaches the bladder even when a person is lying down.","notes":["The ureter is right, but gravity alone does not move the urine. Urine still reaches the bladder when a person lies flat or is upside down, because muscle in the ureter wall pushes it along.","The renal pelvis collects urine and funnels it into the ureter, but it does not reach the bladder, and the urinary tract is not lined by cilia that sweep urine along. Choose the renal pelvis if the question asked what passes urine into the ureter.","Correct. The ureter moves urine to the bladder by peristaltic waves of smooth muscle.","The urethra carries urine from the bladder to the outside, not from the kidney to the bladder. Students mix up the two because the names are so close; pick the urethra if the question asked about the last tube of the system."],"lt":"t-w6-urine-path","lid":"w6-urine-path-r1","comp":139,"competencyId":"w6-urine-path"},{"id":"L04442","dok":1,"q":"Which feature of the bladder is the smooth triangular area at its base, marked by the two ureter openings and the urethral opening?","a":"The trigone.","options":["The detrusor","The internal urethral sphincter","The renal papilla","The trigone"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The trigone is a smooth triangle on the floor of the bladder. Its three corners are the two openings where the ureters enter and the single opening where the urethra leaves. Unlike the rest of the bladder lining, which folds into rugae when the bladder is empty, the trigone stays smooth.","notes":["The detrusor is the smooth muscle layer of the whole bladder wall, not a marked area on its floor. Pick it if the question asked which muscle contracts to empty the bladder.","The internal urethral sphincter is a ring of involuntary smooth muscle at the bladder outlet, at only one corner of the trigone. Choose it if the question asked what keeps the bladder outlet closed without conscious effort.","The renal papilla is the tip of a renal pyramid inside the kidney, not part of the bladder. Pick it if the question asked which structure drains urine into a minor calyx.","Correct. The trigone is the triangle outlined by the two ureter openings and the urethral opening."],"lt":"t-w6-urine-path","lid":"w6-urine-path-r2","comp":139,"competencyId":"w6-urine-path"},{"id":"L04443","dok":1,"q":"Which muscle forms the bladder wall and contracts to empty the bladder?","a":"The detrusor muscle.","options":["The internal urethral sphincter","The external urethral sphincter","The detrusor muscle","The cremaster muscle"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The detrusor muscle is the thick layer of smooth muscle in the wall of the urinary bladder. When it contracts, it squeezes the bladder and pushes urine out through the urethra. The sphincters, in contrast, are rings that hold the outlet closed and must relax for urine to leave.","notes":["The internal urethral sphincter is a ring of involuntary smooth muscle at the bladder outlet. It keeps the outlet closed and relaxes when the bladder empties, so choose it if the question asked about the involuntary ring at the bladder neck.","The external urethral sphincter is a ring of voluntary skeletal muscle around the urethra that holds urine in. It relaxes during urination rather than squeezing the bladder; pick it if the question asked which muscle gives conscious control over urination.","Correct. The detrusor is the smooth muscle of the bladder wall that contracts during urination.","The cremaster is a skeletal muscle in the spermatic cord that raises and lowers the testes. It has nothing to do with the bladder; pick it if the question asked which muscle adjusts the position of the testes."],"lt":"t-w6-urine-path","lid":"w6-urine-path-r3","comp":139,"competencyId":"w6-urine-path"},{"id":"L04445","dok":3,"q":"Which list traces urine from the collecting duct to outside the body?","a":"Collecting duct, minor calyx, major calyx, renal pelvis, ureter, bladder, urethra.","options":["Collecting duct, major calyx, minor calyx, renal pelvis, urethra, bladder, ureter","Collecting duct, minor calyx, major calyx, renal pelvis, urethra, bladder, ureter","Collecting duct, renal pelvis, minor calyx, major calyx, ureter, bladder, urethra","Collecting duct, minor calyx, major calyx, renal pelvis, ureter, bladder, urethra"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Collecting ducts carry urine to the renal papilla, which drains into a minor calyx. Minor calyces merge into major calyces, which empty into the renal pelvis, and the pelvis narrows into the ureter. The ureter carries urine to the bladder for storage, and the urethra carries it out of the body.","notes":["This flips the calyces and also swaps the ureter and urethra. Students pick this when they mix up minor with major and ureter with urethra, two of the most common look-alike pairs in this system.","The kidney portion is right, but the tubes after the pelvis are reversed. The ureter leaves the kidney and the urethra leaves the bladder, so the ureter must come before the bladder and the urethra after it.","This puts the renal pelvis before the calyces. The pelvis is formed where the major calyces meet, so both calyces must come first.","Correct. The spaces inside the kidney grow larger as they merge, and then the ureter, bladder, and urethra carry urine out."],"lt":"t-w6-urine-path","lid":"w6-urine-path-r5","comp":139,"competencyId":"w6-urine-path"},{"id":"L04492","dok":1,"q":"Which anatomical difference helps explain why urinary tract infections are more common in women?","a":"The female urethra is short, giving bacteria a shorter climb to the bladder.","options":["The female bladder has no trigone, so bacteria settle more easily","The female urethra passes through the prostate, which traps bacteria","The female ureters are shorter, so bacteria reach the kidney sooner","The female urethra is shorter, so bacteria reach the bladder sooner"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Most urinary tract infections start when bacteria enter at the urethral opening and climb upward. The female urethra is much shorter than the male urethra, which runs the length of the penis, so bacteria have a shorter distance to travel before reaching the bladder. Its opening also lies close to the vaginal and anal openings.","notes":["Both females and males have a trigone, the smooth triangle on the bladder floor. Pick the trigone if the question asked which area of the bladder is marked by the ureter and urethral openings.","Only the male urethra passes through the prostate, as the prostatic urethra. Pick this if the question asked why an enlarged prostate makes urination difficult.","Bacteria in a typical urinary tract infection climb up from the outside through the urethra, not down from the kidney, so ureter length is not the main factor. Students choose this when they mix up the ureter and the urethra.","Correct. The short female urethra gives bacteria a shorter path to the bladder."],"lt":"t-w6-urinary-disorders","lid":"w6-urinary-disorders-r1","comp":140,"competencyId":"w6-urinary-disorders"},{"id":"N-a17d-140-1","dok":1,"q":"When urine backs up behind a blockage, the renal pelvis and calyces swell. What is this swelling called?","a":"Hydronephrosis.","options":["Hydronephrosis","Nephroptosis","Glomerulonephritis","Pyelonephritis"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Urine travels one path from the calyces through the renal pelvis and ureter to the bladder. Any blockage along that path makes urine back up into the parts above it. When the backup reaches the renal pelvis and calyces, they swell, and that swelling is called hydronephrosis.","notes":["Correct. Hydronephrosis is the swelling of the renal pelvis and calyces when urine backs up.","Nephroptosis is the kidney dropping out of its normal position when its fat cushion is lost. It can kink the ureter, but the term names the drop, not the swelling.","Glomerulonephritis is inflammation of the glomeruli, which damages the filtration membrane. It affects the filters, not the collecting spaces that swell with backed-up urine.","Pyelonephritis is an infection that has reached the renal pelvis and kidney tissue. It is named for infection, not for swelling from backed-up urine."],"comp":140,"competencyId":"w6-urinary-disorders"},{"id":"N-a17d-140-2","dok":1,"q":"What is a urinary tract infection called once it reaches the renal pelvis and kidney tissue?","a":"Pyelonephritis.","options":["Glomerulonephritis","Hydronephrosis","Pyelonephritis","Cystitis"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"In a urinary tract infection, bacteria, most often from the intestine, climb up the urine path and inflame its lining. Infection of the bladder is called cystitis. If it keeps climbing through the ureter to the renal pelvis and kidney tissue, it is called pyelonephritis.","notes":["Glomerulonephritis is inflammation of the glomeruli that damages the filtration membrane. It is not named for a climbing infection of the renal pelvis.","Hydronephrosis is swelling of the renal pelvis and calyces when urine backs up behind a blockage. It is caused by blocked flow, not named for infection.","Correct. Pyelonephritis is infection that has climbed to the renal pelvis and kidney tissue.","Cystitis is infection of the urinary bladder, lower on the path. It becomes pyelonephritis only when it climbs to the kidney."],"comp":140,"competencyId":"w6-urinary-disorders"}]},{"id":"m5-male-repro","title":"Male reproductive system","summary":"scrotum, testis, seminiferous tubules, sperm duct pathway, accessory glands, penis, sperm structure, disorders","mw":"2026-11-23","tr":"2026-11-24","cards":[{"id":"L02478","dok":2,"q":"Which pairing correctly matches the seam on the outside of the scrotum with the wall that divides it on the inside?","a":"The raphe outside and the scrotal septum inside.","options":["Scrotal septum outside, raphe inside","Dartos outside, cremaster inside","Tunica vaginalis outside, albuginea inside","Raphe outside, scrotal septum inside"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The scrotum is a pouch of skin and subcutaneous tissue that holds the testes. A raphe, a ridge that looks like a seam, runs down the middle of its outer surface. Inside, the scrotal septum divides the pouch into two compartments, one for each testis, and the raphe on the surface marks where that septum lies underneath.","notes":["This reverses the two terms. The raphe is the visible seam on the skin and the septum is the partition inside, so a student who knows both words but mixes up surface and deep tends to pick this.","The dartos and cremaster are the two muscles that adjust the scrotum and testes, not a surface seam or a dividing wall. Pick them if the question asked which muscles help control the temperature of the testes.","The tunica vaginalis and tunica albuginea are coverings of each testis, not markings of the scrotum. Choose them if the question asked for the serous membrane and the fibrous capsule around a testis.","Correct. The raphe is the seam on the skin, and the scrotal septum is the internal wall between the two testes."],"lt":"t-reproductive-male","lid":"fr1","comp":141,"competencyId":"w6-scrotum-testes"},{"id":"L02479","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares the tunica vaginalis and the tunica albuginea of the testis?","a":"The tunica vaginalis is a serous membrane; the tunica albuginea is a fibrous capsule.","options":["The vaginalis is a serous membrane; the albuginea is a fibrous capsule","The vaginalis divides the testis; the albuginea lines its tubules","The vaginalis is a fibrous capsule; the albuginea is a serous membrane","Both are layers of smooth muscle found in the scrotal wall"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The tunica vaginalis is a serous membrane, a thin double-layered sheet derived from the peritoneum, and it partly covers the front and sides of each testis. Deep to it, the tunica albuginea is a tough white capsule of dense connective tissue that wraps the testis directly. The albuginea also sends walls inward that divide the testis into a few hundred wedge-shaped lobules.","notes":["Correct. The vaginalis is the serous outer covering, and the albuginea is the fibrous capsule beneath it.","The tunica albuginea, not the vaginalis, divides the testis into lobules, and neither tunica lines the seminiferous tubules. This statement scrambles which structure does which job.","This swaps the two layers. The albuginea is the fibrous capsule and the vaginalis is the serous membrane from the peritoneum, which is the mix-up to watch for because the names look alike.","Neither tunica is muscle. The smooth muscle of the scrotal wall is the dartos, so pick smooth muscle if the question asked what wrinkles the scrotal skin."],"lt":"t-reproductive-male","lid":"fr2","comp":141,"competencyId":"w6-scrotum-testes"},{"id":"L02480","dok":2,"q":"Where are the sustentacular (Sertoli) cells of the testis located?","a":"Lining the seminiferous tubules.","options":["Lining the seminiferous tubules","In the spaces between the tubules","In the wall of the epididymis","Within the tunica albuginea"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Sustentacular cells, also called Sertoli cells, form the lining of the seminiferous tubules, the coiled tubes where sperm are produced. They surround and nourish the developing sperm, and tight connections between neighboring Sertoli cells form the blood-testis barrier, which separates the developing sperm from the blood supply. The name sustentacular means supporting, which matches their job.","notes":["Correct. Sertoli cells line the seminiferous tubules and support the developing sperm.","The spaces between the tubules hold the interstitial (Leydig) cells, which produce testosterone. Pick this location if the question asked where the interstitial cells sit.","The epididymis is the coiled duct on the testis where sperm are stored and gain the ability to move. It would be the answer if the question asked where sperm mature after leaving the testis, not where the support cells of sperm production are found.","The tunica albuginea is the white fibrous capsule around the testis, not a layer of cells inside the tubules. Choose it if the question asked which capsule divides the testis into lobules."],"lt":"t-reproductive-male","lid":"fr3","comp":141,"competencyId":"w6-scrotum-testes"},{"id":"L02481","dok":1,"q":"What do the interstitial (Leydig) cells of the testis produce?","a":"Testosterone.","options":["Alkaline mucus","Fructose-rich fluid","Testosterone","Sperm"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The interstitial cells, also called Leydig cells, sit in the spaces between the seminiferous tubules and produce testosterone, the main male sex hormone. This is why the testes are said to do two jobs: the tubules make sperm, and the cells between the tubules make hormones. The word interstitial simply means located in the spaces between other structures.","notes":["Alkaline mucus comes from the bulbourethral glands, which lubricate the urethra and neutralize traces of urine. Choose it if the question asked what the glands below the prostate secrete.","A fructose-rich alkaline fluid comes from the seminal vesicles, the paired glands behind the bladder. It would be the answer if the question asked which secretion makes up the largest share of semen.","Correct. Leydig cells between the tubules produce testosterone.","Sperm are produced inside the seminiferous tubules, not by the cells between them. Pick sperm if the question asked what the seminiferous tubules make."],"lt":"t-reproductive-male","lid":"fr4","comp":141,"competencyId":"w6-scrotum-testes"},{"id":"L02482","dok":1,"q":"Which part of a sperm cell is packed with mitochondria that power its movement?","a":"The middle piece.","options":["The middle piece","The acrosome","The end piece","The head"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The middle piece is the part of the tail just behind the neck, and it is packed with mitochondria, the organelles that make ATP, the energy molecule cells run on. That energy drives the whipping motion of the rest of the tail. In order, the tail runs from the neck to the middle piece, then the principal piece, and finally the end piece.","notes":["Correct. The middle piece holds the mitochondria that supply energy for swimming.","The acrosome is the enzyme-filled cap over the head that helps the sperm get through the layers around the oocyte. It would be the answer if the question asked which part holds those enzymes.","The end piece is the thin, tapering last part of the tail. Choose it if the question asked for the terminal portion of the tail.","The head holds the nucleus with its 23 chromosomes, the genetic material the sperm delivers. Pick it if the question asked where the chromosomes are carried."],"lt":"t-reproductive-male","lid":"fr5","comp":143,"competencyId":"w6-sperm-cell"},{"id":"L02483","dok":2,"q":"Inside the testis, sperm move from the seminiferous tubules into a network of small channels before they pass to the epididymis. What is this network called?","a":"The rete testis.","options":["The rete testis","The tunica vaginalis","The pampiniform plexus","The spermatic cord"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The rete testis is a network of small channels near the back of the testis that collects sperm from the seminiferous tubules. From the rete testis, sperm pass through a few small efferent ducts into the head of the epididymis. The word rete means net, which describes this mesh of channels.","notes":["Correct. The rete testis collects sperm from the tubules and passes them toward the epididymis.","The tunica vaginalis is the serous membrane, derived from the peritoneum, that partly covers the outside of the testis. It carries no sperm, so choose it only if the question asked about a covering of the testis.","The pampiniform plexus is also a network, but it is a network of veins in the spermatic cord that drains the testis. Pick it if the question asked which venous network surrounds the testicular artery in the cord.","The spermatic cord is the bundle outside the testis that holds the ductus deferens, vessels, nerves, and cremaster muscle. It is the answer for what passes through the inguinal canal, not for channels inside the testis."],"lt":"t-reproductive-male","lid":"fr6","comp":141,"competencyId":"w6-scrotum-testes"},{"id":"L02484","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly describes the epididymis?","a":"A comma-shaped duct on the back of the testis that stores sperm.","options":["A short duct through the prostate that empties into the urethra","A pair of glands behind the bladder that make fructose-rich fluid","A comma-shaped duct on the back of the testis that stores sperm","A muscular duct in the spermatic cord that carries sperm upward"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The epididymis is a comma-shaped, tightly coiled duct that sits along the posterior side of each testis. Sperm that leave the testis are stored here and gain the ability to move, a process called maturation. It is the first duct of the male duct system, linking sperm production in the testis to the ductus deferens that carries sperm away.","notes":["This describes the ejaculatory duct, formed where the ductus deferens meets the seminal vesicle duct. It would be correct if the question asked which duct empties into the prostatic urethra.","This describes the seminal vesicles, which add an alkaline, fructose-rich fluid that is the largest share of semen. Choose it if the question asked about the seminal vesicles.","Correct. The epididymis is the comma-shaped duct on the posterior testis where sperm are stored and mature.","This describes the ductus deferens (vas deferens), which carries sperm from the epididymis up out of the scrotum inside the spermatic cord. Pick this statement if the question asked about the ductus deferens."],"lt":"t-reproductive-male","lid":"fr7","comp":142,"competencyId":"w6-sperm-path"},{"id":"L02485","dok":2,"q":"The ductus deferens widens just before it joins the duct of the seminal vesicle. What is this widened end called?","a":"The ampulla.","options":["The rete testis","The ampulla","The bulb","The crus"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"After passing through the inguinal canal, the ductus deferens runs into the pelvis, crosses over the ureter, and travels down the back of the urinary bladder. Its last stretch widens into the ampulla, a word that means flask. The ampulla then joins the duct of the seminal vesicle to form the ejaculatory duct.","notes":["The rete testis is the network of channels inside the testis that collects sperm from the seminiferous tubules. It sits at the start of the route, not at the end of the ductus deferens.","Correct. The ampulla is the widened end of the ductus deferens.","The bulb is part of the root of the penis, the enlarged base of the corpus spongiosum. Pick it if the question asked which structures make up the attached root of the penis.","The crus (plural crura) is the anchored base of each corpus cavernosum, also in the root of the penis. It would be the answer if the question asked how the dorsal erectile columns attach to the pelvic bones."],"lt":"t-reproductive-male","lid":"fr8","comp":142,"competencyId":"w6-sperm-path"},{"id":"L02486","dok":2,"q":"Which structure travels within the spermatic cord?","a":"The pampiniform plexus.","options":["The pampiniform plexus","The ejaculatory duct","The bulbourethral gland","The seminal vesicle"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The spermatic cord is the bundle that suspends each testis and passes through the inguinal canal. It contains the ductus deferens, the testicular artery, the pampiniform plexus of veins that drains the testis, nerves, lymphatic vessels, and the cremaster muscle. The pampiniform plexus wraps around the testicular artery, which helps cool the blood flowing down to the testis.","notes":["Correct. The pampiniform plexus is the network of veins in the spermatic cord.","The ejaculatory duct lies deep in the pelvis, where the ductus deferens meets the seminal vesicle duct, and it empties into the prostatic urethra. Pick it if the question asked which short duct passes through the prostate.","The bulbourethral glands are pea-sized glands below the prostate that release mucus into the urethra. They would be the answer for glands beside the membranous urethra, not for something inside the cord.","The seminal vesicles are glands on the back of the bladder, deep in the pelvis and outside the cord. Choose them if the question asked which gland contributes the largest share of semen."],"lt":"t-reproductive-male","lid":"fr9","comp":142,"competencyId":"w6-sperm-path"},{"id":"L02487","dok":2,"q":"Each ejaculatory duct empties into which region of the urethra?","a":"The prostatic urethra.","options":["The external urethral orifice","The spongy urethra","The membranous urethra","The prostatic urethra"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Each ejaculatory duct forms where the ampulla of the ductus deferens joins the duct of a seminal vesicle. The short duct then passes through the prostate and opens into the prostatic urethra, the first region of the male urethra just below the bladder. From that point on, sperm share the urethra with the urinary tract.","notes":["The external urethral orifice is the opening at the tip of the penis where semen and urine leave the body. It is the end of the route, not where the ejaculatory ducts join it.","The spongy urethra is the longest region, running through the penis inside the corpus spongiosum. Choose it if the question asked where the bulbourethral glands release their mucus.","The membranous urethra is the short middle region that passes through the floor of the pelvis between the prostate and the penis. It comes after the prostatic urethra, so pick it if the question asked which region sperm enter after leaving the prostate.","Correct. The ejaculatory ducts open into the prostatic urethra."],"lt":"t-reproductive-male","lid":"fr10","comp":142,"competencyId":"w6-sperm-path"},{"id":"L02488","dok":1,"q":"Which set lists the three male accessory glands?","a":"Seminal vesicles, prostate, and bulbourethral glands.","options":["Seminal vesicles, prostate, bulbourethral glands","Seminal vesicles, epididymis, bulbourethral glands","Seminal vesicles, prostate, interstitial cells","Testes, prostate, bulbourethral glands"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The male accessory glands are the paired seminal vesicles, the single prostate, and the paired bulbourethral glands. Each adds fluid to the sperm as they move through the ducts, and the mixture of sperm and these fluids is semen. They are called accessory because they support reproduction without producing the gametes themselves.","notes":["Correct. These three glands add the fluid portion of semen.","The epididymis is part of the duct system, the coiled duct on the testis where sperm are stored and mature. It belongs in a list of the male ducts with the ductus deferens, ejaculatory ducts, and urethra.","Interstitial cells are hormone-producing cells between the seminiferous tubules, not a gland that adds fluid to semen. Choose them if the question asked which cells produce testosterone.","The testes are the gonads, the primary reproductive organs that make sperm and hormones, so they are not accessory glands. Pick the testes if the question asked for the male gonads."],"lt":"t-reproductive-male","lid":"fr11","comp":144,"competencyId":"w6-male-glands-penis"},{"id":"L02489","dok":1,"q":"Which description matches the fluid made by the seminal vesicles?","a":"Alkaline and rich in fructose.","options":["Alkaline and rich in fructose","Watery and rich in testosterone","Milky, and helps sperm stay active","Clear, alkaline mucus"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The seminal vesicles are paired glands on the back of the urinary bladder. Their fluid is alkaline, meaning slightly basic, and rich in fructose, a sugar the sperm use for energy. This fluid makes up the largest share of semen.","notes":["Correct. Seminal vesicle fluid is alkaline and rich in fructose.","Testosterone is a hormone released into the blood by the interstitial cells of the testis, not part of any accessory gland fluid. Students who mix up the hormone-making cells with the fluid-making glands tend to pick this.","A milky fluid that helps sperm stay active comes from the prostate, the single gland around the urethra below the bladder. Pick this description if the question asked about prostate fluid.","Clear, alkaline mucus comes from the bulbourethral glands, which lubricate the urethra and neutralize traces of urine. Choose it if the question asked about the glands below the prostate."],"lt":"t-reproductive-male","lid":"fr12","comp":144,"competencyId":"w6-male-glands-penis"},{"id":"L02490","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly describes the prostate?","a":"A single gland below the bladder that surrounds the urethra.","options":["A pair of glands behind the bladder that make fructose-rich fluid","A pair of pea-sized glands that release mucus into the urethra","A single coiled duct on the testis where sperm are stored","A single gland below the bladder that surrounds the urethra"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The prostate is a single gland, about the size of a walnut, that sits just below the urinary bladder and wraps around the first part of the urethra, the prostatic urethra. It adds a milky fluid that helps sperm stay active. Because the urethra passes straight through it, an enlarged prostate can squeeze the urethra and slow urine flow.","notes":["This describes the seminal vesicles, the paired glands on the back of the bladder. Pick this statement if the question asked which glands supply the largest share of semen.","This describes the bulbourethral (Cowper's) glands, which sit below the prostate. Choose it if the question asked which glands lubricate the urethra with alkaline mucus.","This describes the epididymis, a duct rather than a gland. It would be correct if the question asked where sperm are stored and gain the ability to move.","Correct. The prostate is one gland below the bladder wrapped around the prostatic urethra."],"lt":"t-reproductive-male","lid":"fr13","comp":144,"competencyId":"w6-male-glands-penis"},{"id":"L02491","dok":2,"q":"What does the mucus from the bulbourethral glands do?","a":"Lubricates the urethra and neutralizes leftover urine.","options":["Keeps sperm active with a milky fluid","Lubricates the urethra and neutralizes leftover urine","Supplies the fructose that sperm use for energy","Shields developing sperm from the blood supply"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The bulbourethral glands, also called Cowper's glands, are pea-sized glands just below the prostate. Before ejaculation they release a clear, alkaline mucus into the spongy urethra, where it lubricates the passage and neutralizes traces of acidic urine. Alkaline means slightly basic, the opposite of acidic.","notes":["A milky fluid that keeps sperm active comes from the prostate. Choose this if the question asked what the gland around the prostatic urethra secretes.","Correct. Bulbourethral mucus lubricates the urethra and neutralizes acidic urine.","Fructose for energy comes from the seminal vesicles, the paired glands on the back of the bladder. Pick this if the question asked about seminal vesicle fluid.","Shielding developing sperm from the blood is the job of the sustentacular (Sertoli) cells, whose tight connections form the blood-testis barrier. That is a role inside the testis, not a gland secretion."],"lt":"t-reproductive-male","lid":"fr14","comp":144,"competencyId":"w6-male-glands-penis"},{"id":"L02492","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares the corpora cavernosa and the corpus spongiosum?","a":"The two corpora cavernosa are dorsal; the single corpus spongiosum is ventral and surrounds the urethra.","options":["The two cavernosa are dorsal and hold the urethra; the one spongiosum is ventral","The two cavernosa are dorsal; the one spongiosum is ventral and holds the urethra","The two cavernosa are ventral and hold the urethra; the one spongiosum is dorsal","The one cavernosum is dorsal; the two spongiosa are ventral and hold the urethra"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The penis has three columns of erectile tissue, spongy tissue that fills with blood during an erection. The paired corpora cavernosa run side by side along the dorsal side, and the single corpus spongiosum runs along the ventral side and surrounds the spongy urethra. The corpus spongiosum also expands at the tip to form the glans penis.","notes":["The counts and positions are right, but the urethra runs through the corpus spongiosum, not the cavernosa. That is why the penile part of the urethra is called the spongy urethra.","Correct. Two dorsal cavernosa and one ventral spongiosum that carries the spongy urethra.","This flips the positions. The cavernosa are dorsal, and the spongiosum is the ventral column, so the urethra is not inside the cavernosa.","This reverses the counts. There are two corpora cavernosa and only one corpus spongiosum, which is easy to remember because cavernosa is the plural form."],"lt":"t-reproductive-male","lid":"fr15","comp":144,"competencyId":"w6-male-glands-penis"},{"id":"L02493","dok":2,"q":"Semen is a mixture of sperm and gland fluids. Which structure supplies the sperm cells themselves?","a":"The seminiferous tubules.","options":["The bulbourethral glands","The seminiferous tubules","The seminal vesicles","The prostate"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Semen has four sources. The sperm cells are made in the seminiferous tubules of the testes, while the fluid comes from the three accessory glands: the seminal vesicles, the prostate, and the bulbourethral glands. Only the seminiferous tubules produce sperm; every other structure on the route stores, carries, or adds fluid.","notes":["The bulbourethral glands add a small amount of alkaline mucus that lubricates the urethra. They would be the answer if the question asked which glands lie below the prostate.","Correct. Sperm are produced in the seminiferous tubules of the testes.","The seminal vesicles add an alkaline, fructose-rich fluid, the largest share of semen, but no sperm. Pick them if the question asked which gland contributes the most fluid.","The prostate adds a milky fluid that helps sperm stay active, but no sperm. Choose it if the question asked which gland surrounds the urethra below the bladder."],"lt":"t-reproductive-male","lid":"fr16","comp":141,"competencyId":"w6-scrotum-testes"},{"id":"L02494","dok":1,"q":"What is the skin-covered pouch that holds the testes outside the body cavity?","a":"The scrotum.","options":["The scrotum","The perineum","The spermatic cord","The prepuce"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The scrotum is a pouch of skin and subcutaneous tissue that holds the testes outside the abdominopelvic cavity. This position keeps the testes a few degrees cooler than the body core, which sperm production requires. A raphe marks the middle of its surface, and an internal scrotal septum separates the two testes.","notes":["Correct. The scrotum is the pouch that holds the testes.","The perineum is the whole diamond-shaped region between the thighs, not the pouch itself. Choose it if the question asked for the region that contains the external genitalia and the anus.","The spermatic cord is the bundle that suspends each testis and carries the ductus deferens and testicular vessels. It hangs inside the scrotum but is not the pouch that holds the testes.","The prepuce is the foreskin, the loose fold of skin over the glans penis. Pick it if the question asked which fold of skin covers the tip of the penis."],"lt":"t-reproductive-male","lid":"c1","comp":141,"competencyId":"w6-scrotum-testes"},{"id":"L02495","dok":1,"q":"Which structures are the male gonads, the primary reproductive organs?","a":"The testes.","options":["The seminal vesicles","The prostate","The testes","The epididymis"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The testes are the male gonads, the primary reproductive organs. They produce the gametes (sperm) inside the seminiferous tubules and secrete hormones, mainly testosterone, from the interstitial cells. Every other male reproductive structure either carries sperm, adds fluid to them, or helps deliver them.","notes":["The seminal vesicles are accessory glands that add a fructose-rich fluid to semen. Pick them if the question asked which gland makes the largest share of semen.","The prostate is an accessory gland around the urethra below the bladder. It would be the answer if the question asked which gland is enlarged in benign prostatic hyperplasia.","Correct. The testes are the male gonads.","The epididymis is a duct on the back of the testis where sperm are stored and mature. Choose it if the question asked where sperm gain the ability to move."],"lt":"t-reproductive-male","lid":"c2","comp":141,"competencyId":"w6-scrotum-testes"},{"id":"L02496","dok":1,"q":"Where inside the testis are sperm produced?","a":"In the seminiferous tubules.","options":["In the tunica albuginea","In the rete testis","In the seminiferous tubules","Among the interstitial cells"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Sperm are produced in the seminiferous tubules, long, tightly coiled tubes packed into the lobules of each testis. The sustentacular (Sertoli) cells lining these tubules nourish the developing sperm. Once formed, sperm leave the tubules and move on to be stored and matured in the epididymis.","notes":["The tunica albuginea is the fibrous capsule of the testis that divides it into lobules. Pick it if the question asked for the capsule, not the site of sperm production.","The rete testis is the network of channels that collects sperm after they leave the seminiferous tubules. It would be the answer if the question asked where sperm go next inside the testis.","Correct. The seminiferous tubules are the only sperm-producing structures.","The interstitial cells sit between the tubules and produce testosterone, not sperm. Choose them if the question asked which cells of the testis make hormones."],"lt":"t-reproductive-male","lid":"c3","comp":141,"competencyId":"w6-scrotum-testes"},{"id":"L02497","dok":1,"q":"Which organ lies along the posterior side of each testis?","a":"The epididymis.","options":["The epididymis","The seminal vesicle","The bulbourethral gland","The prostate"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The epididymis is a comma-shaped, coiled duct that hugs the posterior side of each testis inside the scrotum. Sperm leave the testis and enter the epididymis, where they are stored and gain the ability to move. Its position right against the testis makes it the first stop after sperm are produced.","notes":["Correct. The epididymis sits on the posterior side of each testis.","The seminal vesicles lie on the back of the urinary bladder, far from the testis. Choose them if the question asked which gland lies behind the bladder.","The bulbourethral glands are small glands below the prostate beside the membranous urethra. They would be the answer if the question asked which glands release mucus into the spongy urethra.","The prostate is a single gland deep in the pelvis, just below the bladder and around the urethra. Pick it if the question asked which gland surrounds the first part of the urethra."],"lt":"t-reproductive-male","lid":"c4","comp":142,"competencyId":"w6-sperm-path"},{"id":"L02498","dok":1,"q":"What is the white fibrous capsule of the testis called?","a":"The tunica albuginea.","options":["The tunica albuginea","The corpus spongiosum","The tunica vaginalis","The dartos muscle"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The tunica albuginea is the tough white capsule of dense connective tissue that wraps each testis. Albuginea means white, which describes its color. It also sends walls inward that divide the testis into a few hundred lobules, each holding seminiferous tubules.","notes":["Correct. The tunica albuginea is the fibrous capsule of the testis.","The corpus spongiosum is the erectile column of the penis that surrounds the spongy urethra. It would be the answer if the question asked which column forms the glans.","The tunica vaginalis is the serous membrane, derived from the peritoneum, that partly covers the testis outside the albuginea. Choose it if the question asked for the serous covering rather than the fibrous capsule.","The dartos is the smooth muscle layer of the scrotum that wrinkles the skin to reduce heat loss. Pick it if the question asked about the muscle in the scrotal wall."],"lt":"t-reproductive-male","lid":"c5","comp":141,"competencyId":"w6-scrotum-testes"},{"id":"L02499","dok":1,"q":"What is the enlarged, acorn-shaped tip of the penis called?","a":"The glans penis.","options":["The prepuce","The glans","The body","The root"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The glans penis is the enlarged, acorn-shaped tip of the penis, and the word glans means acorn. It is an expansion of the corpus spongiosum, the erectile column that surrounds the urethra, so the external urethral orifice opens at its tip. In an uncircumcised male, the prepuce covers it.","notes":["The prepuce is the foreskin, the loose fold of skin that covers the glans. It would be the answer if the question asked what is removed in circumcision.","Correct. The glans is the acorn-shaped tip of the penis.","The body, or shaft, is the free, movable portion between the root and the tip. Choose it if the question asked for the main length of the penis.","The root is the attached portion of the penis, containing the bulb and the crura. Pick it if the question asked which part anchors the penis to the body."],"lt":"t-reproductive-male","lid":"c6","comp":144,"competencyId":"w6-male-glands-penis"},{"id":"L02500","dok":1,"q":"What is the foreskin of the penis called?","a":"The prepuce.","options":["The prepuce","The glans","The root","The raphe"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The prepuce, or foreskin, is the loose fold of skin that covers the glans penis. It is the structure removed in circumcision. Because it is loose, it can slide back to expose the glans.","notes":["Correct. The prepuce is the foreskin.","The glans is the acorn-shaped tip that the prepuce covers. Pick it if the question asked for the tip of the penis itself.","The root is the attached portion of the penis that contains the bulb and the crura. Choose it if the question asked which part anchors the penis to the body.","The raphe is the seam-like ridge down the middle of the scrotum. It would be the answer if the question asked what marks the surface of the scrotum."],"lt":"t-reproductive-male","lid":"c7","comp":144,"competencyId":"w6-male-glands-penis"},{"id":"L02501","dok":1,"q":"Which cell is the male gamete?","a":"The sperm cell.","options":["A Sertoli cell","A sperm cell","A Leydig cell","An oocyte"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"A gamete is a sex cell that carries half the usual number of chromosomes, 23 in humans, and joins with another gamete at fertilization. The male gamete is the sperm cell, made in the seminiferous tubules of the testes. The female gamete is the oocyte, the egg cell made in the ovary.","notes":["Sertoli (sustentacular) cells line the seminiferous tubules and nourish developing sperm. They support sperm production but are not gametes, so pick them if the question asked which cells form the blood-testis barrier.","Correct. The sperm cell is the male gamete.","Leydig (interstitial) cells sit between the seminiferous tubules and produce testosterone. They are body cells of the testis, so choose them if the question asked which cells make the male hormone.","The oocyte is the female gamete, produced in the ovary. Pick it if the question asked for the female sex cell."],"lt":"t-reproductive-male","lid":"c8","comp":143,"competencyId":"w6-sperm-cell"},{"id":"L02502","dok":1,"q":"Which male reproductive structure sits just below the urinary bladder and surrounds the urethra?","a":"The prostate.","options":["The prostate","The seminal vesicles","The epididymis","The bulbourethral glands"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The prostate is a single, walnut-sized gland just inferior to the urinary bladder. The first part of the urethra runs straight through it, which is why that region is called the prostatic urethra. The prostate adds a milky fluid to semen that helps sperm stay active.","notes":["Correct. The prostate lies below the bladder and encircles the urethra.","The seminal vesicles lie on the posterior surface of the bladder, behind it rather than below it, and they do not surround the urethra. Pick them if the question asked which glands lie behind the bladder.","The epididymis is a duct on the back of the testis inside the scrotum, nowhere near the bladder. It would be the answer if the question asked where sperm are stored.","The bulbourethral glands are pea-sized glands farther down, below the prostate beside the membranous urethra. Choose them if the question asked which glands lie inferior to the prostate."],"lt":"t-reproductive-male","lid":"c9","comp":144,"competencyId":"w6-male-glands-penis"},{"id":"L02503","dok":1,"q":"Which bundle suspends each testis and carries the ductus deferens and the testicular vessels?","a":"The spermatic cord.","options":["The spermatic cord","The ejaculatory duct","The inguinal canal","The tunica vaginalis"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The spermatic cord is the bundle that hangs each testis in the scrotum and runs up through the inguinal canal into the abdominal wall. It holds the ductus deferens, the testicular artery, the pampiniform plexus of veins, nerves, lymphatic vessels, and the cremaster muscle. Think of it as the cable that connects each testis to the body.","notes":["Correct. The spermatic cord suspends the testis and carries its duct, vessels, and nerves.","The ejaculatory duct is a short duct deep in the pelvis where the ductus deferens meets the seminal vesicle duct. Choose it if the question asked which duct empties into the prostatic urethra.","The inguinal canal is the passage through the anterior abdominal wall that the cord travels through, not the cord itself. Pick it if the question asked where an inguinal hernia occurs.","The tunica vaginalis is the serous membrane that partly covers each testis. It would be the answer if the question asked where fluid collects in a hydrocele."],"lt":"t-reproductive-male","lid":"c10","comp":142,"competencyId":"w6-sperm-path"},{"id":"L02504","dok":1,"q":"At the end of the male duct system, through which opening do sperm leave the body?","a":"The external urethral orifice.","options":["The external urethral orifice","The inguinal canal","The internal urethral orifice","The ejaculatory duct"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The external urethral orifice is the opening at the tip of the glans penis, at the end of the spongy urethra. Semen leaves the body here, and so does urine, because the male urethra is shared by the reproductive and urinary systems. External means it opens to the outside of the body.","notes":["Correct. Semen exits through the external urethral orifice at the tip of the penis.","The inguinal canal is a passage through the abdominal wall that carries the spermatic cord, not an exit from the body. It would be the answer if the question asked where an inguinal hernia pushes through.","The internal urethral orifice is where the urethra begins, at the outlet of the urinary bladder. Pick it if the question asked where urine leaves the bladder and enters the urethra.","The ejaculatory duct is an internal duct that empties into the prostatic urethra, much earlier in the route. Choose it if the question asked where the ductus deferens and seminal vesicle duct join."],"lt":"t-reproductive-male","lid":"c11","comp":142,"competencyId":"w6-sperm-path"},{"id":"L02505","dok":1,"q":"Which term names the diamond-shaped region between the thighs that contains the external genitalia and the anus?","a":"The perineum.","options":["The pubic region","The inguinal region","The scrotum","The perineum"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The perineum is the diamond-shaped region between the thighs, bounded by the pubic symphysis in front, the coccyx behind, and the ischial tuberosities on the sides. It contains the external genitalia and the anus. The membranous urethra passes through the floor of this region.","notes":["The pubic region is the lower front of the trunk, just above the external genitalia. It would be the answer if the question asked for the regional term over the pubic bones.","The inguinal region is the groin, where the thigh meets the trunk, and it holds the inguinal canal. Choose it if the question asked where the spermatic cord passes through the abdominal wall.","The scrotum is the pouch that holds the testes, one structure within the perineum rather than the whole region. Pick it if the question asked for the sac that holds the testes.","Correct. The perineum is the region between the thighs that contains the external genitalia and anus."],"lt":"t-reproductive-male","lid":"c12","comp":149,"competencyId":"w6-vagina-vulva"},{"id":"L02506","dok":1,"q":"Which structure of the scrotum is a layer of smooth muscle?","a":"The dartos muscle.","options":["The dartos muscle","The scrotal raphe","The tunica vaginalis","The cremaster muscle"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The dartos is a thin layer of smooth muscle in the wall of the scrotum, just under the skin. When it contracts, it wrinkles the scrotal skin and pulls it tighter, which reduces heat loss from the testes. Because it is smooth muscle, it works involuntarily.","notes":["Correct. The dartos is the smooth muscle layer of the scrotum.","The scrotal raphe is the seam-like ridge on the surface of the scrotum, not a muscle layer. It would be the answer if the question asked what marks the midline of the scrotum.","The tunica vaginalis is a serous membrane covering the testis, not muscle. Choose it if the question asked which membrane is derived from the peritoneum.","The cremaster is the other scrotal muscle, but it is skeletal muscle, and it raises and lowers the testes. Pick it if the question asked which muscle is skeletal or which one lifts the testes."],"lt":"t-reproductive-male","lid":"c13","comp":141,"competencyId":"w6-scrotum-testes"},{"id":"L02507","dok":1,"q":"Which muscle raises and lowers the testes to adjust their temperature?","a":"The cremaster muscle.","options":["The cremaster muscle","The dartos muscle","The myometrium","The detrusor muscle"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The cremaster is a skeletal muscle that travels in the spermatic cord and loops around each testis. When it contracts, it pulls the testes up toward the warm body, and when it relaxes, the testes hang lower and cool off. This keeps them near the slightly cooler temperature that sperm production needs.","notes":["Correct. The cremaster raises and lowers the testes.","The dartos is the smooth muscle of the scrotal wall that wrinkles the skin, but it does not lift the testes. Pick it if the question asked which muscle wrinkles the scrotum to reduce heat loss.","The myometrium is the smooth muscle layer of the uterus, a female structure. It would be the answer if the question asked which layer of the uterine wall contracts during labor.","The detrusor is the smooth muscle in the wall of the urinary bladder. Choose it if the question asked which muscle squeezes urine out of the bladder."],"lt":"t-reproductive-male","lid":"c14","comp":141,"competencyId":"w6-scrotum-testes"},{"id":"L02508","dok":2,"q":"Where in the testis are the interstitial (Leydig) cells found?","a":"In the spaces between the seminiferous tubules.","options":["Within the epididymis","Lining the seminiferous tubules","Inside the prostate","Between the seminiferous tubules"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Interstitial cells sit in the connective tissue spaces between the seminiferous tubules, which is exactly what interstitial means. They produce testosterone. Keeping the two cell types apart is easier if you link location to job: the cells inside the tubules support sperm production, and the cells between the tubules make hormone.","notes":["The epididymis is the duct on the back of the testis where sperm are stored and mature, and it holds no Leydig cells. Choose it if the question asked where sperm gain the ability to move.","The cells lining the seminiferous tubules are the sustentacular (Sertoli) cells, which nourish developing sperm. Pick this location if the question asked about the cells that form the blood-testis barrier.","The prostate is an accessory gland below the bladder that adds a milky fluid to semen. It would be the answer if the question asked which gland surrounds the urethra.","Correct. Leydig cells lie between the seminiferous tubules."],"lt":"t-reproductive-male","lid":"c15","comp":141,"competencyId":"w6-scrotum-testes"},{"id":"L02509","dok":1,"q":"The ductus deferens is also known by which other name?","a":"The vas deferens.","options":["The efferent duct","The ejaculatory duct","The lactiferous duct","The vas deferens"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The ductus deferens and the vas deferens are two names for the same duct. It carries sperm from the epididymis up out of the scrotum inside the spermatic cord, through the inguinal canal, and into the pelvis. The word deferens means carrying away.","notes":["The efferent ducts are tiny ducts that carry sperm from the rete testis into the head of the epididymis. Choose them if the question asked how sperm leave the testis to enter the epididymis.","The ejaculatory duct is a separate, shorter duct formed where the ductus deferens meets the seminal vesicle duct. Pick it if the question asked which duct empties into the prostatic urethra.","The lactiferous ducts carry milk in the mammary gland, a female structure. They would be the answer if the question asked which ducts open at the nipple.","Correct. Vas deferens is another name for the ductus deferens."],"lt":"t-reproductive-male","lid":"c16","comp":142,"competencyId":"w6-sperm-path"},{"id":"L02510","dok":2,"q":"The ejaculatory duct is formed by the joining of which two structures?","a":"The ductus deferens and the duct of the seminal vesicle.","options":["Ductus deferens and bulbourethral duct","Seminal vesicle duct and prostatic urethra","Ductus deferens and seminal vesicle duct","Epididymis and ductus deferens"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"On each side, the widened end of the ductus deferens (the ampulla) joins the duct of the seminal vesicle to form one ejaculatory duct. This is the point where sperm first mix with seminal vesicle fluid. The ejaculatory duct then passes through the prostate and empties into the prostatic urethra.","notes":["The bulbourethral glands release mucus separately into the spongy urethra, farther down the route. They would be the answer if the question asked which glands lie below the prostate.","The prostatic urethra is where the ejaculatory duct ends, not one of the structures that forms it. Choose the urethra if the question asked where the ejaculatory duct empties.","Correct. The ductus deferens and the seminal vesicle duct merge to form the ejaculatory duct.","The epididymis does connect to the ductus deferens, but that junction is in the scrotum and simply continues the same tube. Pick this pair if the question asked which two ducts join at the tail of the epididymis."],"lt":"t-reproductive-male","lid":"c17","comp":142,"competencyId":"w6-sperm-path"},{"id":"L02511","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares the positions of the seminal vesicles and the prostate?","a":"The seminal vesicles lie behind the bladder; the prostate lies below it.","options":["The seminal vesicles lie below the bladder; the prostate lies behind it","The seminal vesicles lie behind the bladder; the prostate lies below it","The seminal vesicles lie in the scrotum; the prostate lies below the bladder","Both glands lie side by side on the back wall of the bladder"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The seminal vesicles are a pair of elongated glands on the posterior surface of the urinary bladder. The prostate is a single gland just inferior to the bladder, wrapped around the first part of the urethra. The ducts of the seminal vesicles join the ductus deferens behind the bladder, and the resulting ejaculatory ducts then pass down through the prostate.","notes":["This reverses the two positions. The prostate is the one below the bladder around the urethra, and the seminal vesicles are behind it, so this is the mirror-image mix-up.","Correct. Seminal vesicles are posterior to the bladder, and the prostate is inferior to it.","The seminal vesicles are deep in the pelvis, not in the scrotum. Students sometimes place them near the testes because of the word seminal, but the structures in the scrotum are the testes and epididymides.","Only the seminal vesicles sit on the back wall of the bladder. The prostate is below the bladder, which is why it can squeeze the urethra when it enlarges."],"lt":"t-reproductive-male","lid":"c18","comp":144,"competencyId":"w6-male-glands-penis"},{"id":"L02512","dok":2,"q":"Which paired glands lie just below the prostate in the male?","a":"The bulbourethral glands.","options":["The greater vestibular glands","The testes","The seminal vesicles","The bulbourethral glands"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The bulbourethral glands, also called Cowper's glands, are a pair of pea-sized glands just inferior to the prostate, on either side of the membranous urethra. Their ducts open into the spongy urethra, where they release a clear alkaline mucus. Going from top to bottom, the order is bladder, prostate, then the bulbourethral glands.","notes":["The greater vestibular glands are the female counterparts of the bulbourethral glands, found beside the vaginal opening. They would be the answer if the question asked which female glands lubricate the vestibule.","The testes hang in the scrotum outside the pelvis, far below and in front of the prostate. Choose them if the question asked for the male gonads.","The seminal vesicles lie behind the bladder, above and behind the prostate rather than below it. Pick them if the question asked which glands are on the posterior bladder wall.","Correct. The bulbourethral glands sit just below the prostate."],"lt":"t-reproductive-male","lid":"c19","comp":144,"competencyId":"w6-male-glands-penis"},{"id":"L02513","dok":1,"q":"Which paired columns of erectile tissue run along the dorsal side of the penis?","a":"The corpora cavernosa.","options":["The corpora cavernosa","The corpus spongiosum","The glans penis","The prepuce"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The corpora cavernosa are the two larger columns of erectile tissue, and they run side by side along the dorsal side of the penis. Erectile tissue is a sponge of blood spaces that fills with blood during an erection. The word corpora is plural, which helps you remember there are two of them.","notes":["Correct. The two corpora cavernosa run along the dorsal side.","The corpus spongiosum is the single ventral column that surrounds the spongy urethra. Pick it if the question asked which column holds the urethra or forms the glans.","The glans penis is the acorn-shaped tip, an expansion of the corpus spongiosum, not a paired column. Choose it if the question asked for the enlarged tip of the penis.","The prepuce is the foreskin, a fold of skin over the glans, not erectile tissue. It would be the answer if the question asked what is removed in circumcision."],"lt":"t-reproductive-male","lid":"c20","comp":144,"competencyId":"w6-male-glands-penis"},{"id":"L02514","dok":2,"q":"Which structure expands at the tip of the penis to form the glans?","a":"The corpus spongiosum.","options":["The spongy urethra","The corpora cavernosa","The corpus spongiosum","The prepuce"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The corpus spongiosum is the single ventral column of erectile tissue that surrounds the spongy urethra. At the distal end of the penis it expands into the glans penis, which is why the urethra opens at the tip of the glans. At the other end, in the root, it expands into the bulb.","notes":["The spongy urethra is the tube that runs inside the corpus spongiosum and opens at the tip. It carries urine and semen but does not form the glans, so pick it if the question asked which region of the urethra runs through the penis.","The corpora cavernosa are the two dorsal columns, and they end before the tip, capped by the glans. Pick them if the question asked which columns run along the dorsal side.","Correct. The glans is the expanded end of the corpus spongiosum.","The prepuce is the fold of skin covering the glans, not the tissue that forms it. Choose it if the question asked what the foreskin is called."],"lt":"t-reproductive-male","lid":"c21","comp":144,"competencyId":"w6-male-glands-penis"},{"id":"L02515","dok":1,"q":"The spermatic cord passes through which passage in the lower anterior abdominal wall?","a":"The inguinal canal.","options":["The obturator foramen","The urethra","The inguinal canal","The ejaculatory duct"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The inguinal canal is a short, slanted passage through the lower anterior abdominal wall in the groin. The spermatic cord runs through it, carrying the ductus deferens, testicular vessels, and nerves between the scrotum and the abdominopelvic cavity. Because it is a natural opening in the wall, it is also where an inguinal hernia can push through.","notes":["The obturator foramen is the large opening in the hip bone, surrounded by the pubis and ischium, not a passage through the abdominal wall. Pick it if the question asked for the largest foramen of the hip bone.","The urethra is the tube that carries urine and semen out through the penis, not a passage through the abdominal wall. It would be the answer if the question asked which tube is shared by the urinary and reproductive systems.","Correct. The spermatic cord runs through the inguinal canal.","The ejaculatory duct is a short internal duct deep in the pelvis, formed where the ductus deferens meets the seminal vesicle duct. Choose it if the question asked which duct empties into the prostatic urethra."],"lt":"t-reproductive-male","lid":"c22","comp":142,"competencyId":"w6-sperm-path"},{"id":"L02516","dok":1,"q":"Which part of the penis is the attached portion anchored to the body?","a":"The root.","options":["The root","The body","The glans","The prepuce"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The root is the attached, internal portion of the penis, anchored in the perineum to the pelvic bones and the floor of the pelvis. It contains the bulb, the enlarged base of the corpus spongiosum, and the two crura, the anchored ends of the corpora cavernosa. The rest of the penis, the body and the glans, hangs free.","notes":["Correct. The root is the attached portion of the penis.","The body, or shaft, is the free, movable portion of the penis. Pick it if the question asked for the part between the root and the tip.","The glans is the enlarged, acorn-shaped tip of the penis. Choose it if the question asked which part is an expansion of the corpus spongiosum at the distal end.","The prepuce is the foreskin over the glans. It would be the answer if the question asked which fold of skin is removed in circumcision."],"lt":"t-reproductive-male","lid":"c23","comp":144,"competencyId":"w6-male-glands-penis"},{"id":"L02517","dok":1,"q":"Which description best defines semen?","a":"Sperm plus fluid from the accessory glands.","options":["Fluid from the seminal vesicles alone","Sperm plus fluid from the accessory glands","A mix of sperm and urine in the urethra","Sperm stored in the epididymis before release"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Semen is the mixture of sperm and the fluids of the three accessory glands: the seminal vesicles, the prostate, and the bulbourethral glands. The sperm come from the seminiferous tubules, but most of the volume is gland fluid. These fluids nourish the sperm, give them a liquid to swim in, and help neutralize acid along the way.","notes":["Seminal vesicle fluid is the largest single part of semen, but it is only one part. It contains no sperm on its own, so this would describe seminal vesicle secretion, not semen.","Correct. Semen is sperm combined with the fluids of the accessory glands.","Urine and semen share the male urethra, but semen is the mix of sperm and gland fluids, and urine is not part of it. Pick the urethra only if the question asked which tube carries both.","Sperm stored in the epididymis have not yet mixed with any gland fluid. This describes the stored sperm before ejaculation, not the final mixture called semen."],"lt":"t-reproductive-male","lid":"c24","comp":144,"competencyId":"w6-male-glands-penis"},{"id":"L02518","dok":2,"q":"Into which region of the urethra do the bulbourethral glands release their mucus?","a":"The spongy urethra.","options":["The prostatic urethra","The ejaculatory duct","The membranous urethra","The spongy urethra"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The bulbourethral glands sit on either side of the membranous urethra, just below the prostate, but their small ducts run forward and open into the spongy urethra. Their clear, alkaline mucus lubricates this part of the urethra and neutralizes traces of acidic urine. So the glands lie beside one region and empty into the next one downstream.","notes":["The prostatic urethra receives the ejaculatory ducts and the prostate's own fluid. Pick it if the question asked where the ejaculatory ducts empty.","The ejaculatory duct is formed by the ductus deferens and the seminal vesicle duct, upstream of the bulbourethral glands. It would be the answer if the question asked where seminal vesicle fluid first joins the sperm.","The membranous urethra is the region the bulbourethral glands sit beside, which is why students pick it, but their ducts open farther down. Choose it if the question asked where the glands are located rather than where they empty.","Correct. Bulbourethral ducts open into the spongy urethra."],"lt":"t-reproductive-male","lid":"c25","comp":144,"competencyId":"w6-male-glands-penis"},{"id":"L02519","dok":1,"q":"Which structure contributes the largest share of the fluid in semen?","a":"The seminal vesicles.","options":["The seminal vesicles","The bulbourethral glands","The prostate","The seminiferous tubules"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The seminal vesicles produce the largest share of semen, about 60 percent of its volume, an alkaline fluid rich in fructose that gives the sperm energy. The prostate adds a smaller amount of milky fluid, and the bulbourethral glands add only a little mucus. The sperm cells themselves make up a small part of the total volume.","notes":["Correct. The seminal vesicles supply most of the fluid in semen.","The bulbourethral glands add a small amount of clear, alkaline mucus. Choose them if the question asked which gland lubricates the urethra.","The prostate adds a milky fluid that helps sperm stay active, but less of it than the seminal vesicles. Pick it if the question asked which gland surrounds the urethra below the bladder.","The seminiferous tubules make the sperm cells, not the fluid. They would be the answer if the question asked where sperm are produced."],"lt":"t-reproductive-male","lid":"c26","comp":144,"competencyId":"w6-male-glands-penis"},{"id":"L02520","dok":2,"q":"Which duct carries sperm upward out of the scrotum inside the spermatic cord?","a":"The ductus deferens.","options":["The epididymis","The urethra","The ductus deferens","The ejaculatory duct"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The ductus deferens (vas deferens) begins at the tail of the epididymis and carries sperm up out of the scrotum inside the spermatic cord. It passes through the inguinal canal into the pelvis, runs along the back of the bladder, and ends by joining the seminal vesicle duct. Its thick smooth muscle wall pushes sperm along.","notes":["The epididymis stores sperm on the back of the testis, just before the ductus deferens begins. Pick it if the question asked where sperm gain the ability to move.","The urethra is the final shared tube for urine and semen, running through the prostate and penis. It would be the answer if the question asked which duct carries sperm to the outside.","Correct. The ductus deferens carries sperm up out of the scrotum in the spermatic cord.","The ejaculatory duct is the short duct deep in the pelvis that forms after the ductus deferens meets the seminal vesicle duct. Choose it if the question asked which duct passes through the prostate."],"lt":"t-reproductive-male","lid":"c27","comp":142,"competencyId":"w6-sperm-path"},{"id":"L02521","dok":2,"q":"In which structure do sperm gain the ability to swim?","a":"The epididymis.","options":["The seminal vesicles","The ductus deferens","The epididymis","The seminiferous tubules"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Sperm leaving the seminiferous tubules are fully formed but cannot yet swim. They pass into the epididymis, the comma-shaped duct on the back of the testis, where they are stored and gain motility, the ability to move on their own. This maturation step is why the epididymis comes right after the testis on the route.","notes":["The seminal vesicles add fructose that fuels swimming, but sperm gain the ability to swim before they meet this fluid. They would be the answer if the question asked which gland makes the largest share of semen.","The ductus deferens moves sperm by contractions of its muscular wall, after they have already matured. Choose it if the question asked which duct carries sperm up through the spermatic cord.","Correct. Sperm gain motility in the epididymis.","The seminiferous tubules produce sperm, but sperm leaving them cannot swim yet. Pick them if the question asked where sperm are made."],"lt":"t-reproductive-male","lid":"c28","comp":142,"competencyId":"w6-sperm-path"},{"id":"L02522","dok":2,"q":"On their way to the urethra, the ejaculatory ducts pass through which gland?","a":"The prostate.","options":["The prostate","The testis","The seminal vesicles","The bulbourethral glands"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Each ejaculatory duct forms behind the bladder, then runs down through the substance of the prostate to open into the prostatic urethra. The prostate surrounds both the first part of the urethra and the ejaculatory ducts. This is the meeting point where sperm, seminal vesicle fluid, and prostate fluid all come together.","notes":["Correct. The ejaculatory ducts pass through the prostate to reach the prostatic urethra.","The testis is in the scrotum, where sperm are made, at the start of the route rather than near the urethra. It would be the answer if the question asked which organ contains the seminiferous tubules.","The seminal vesicles help form the ejaculatory ducts, since their ducts join the ductus deferens, but the ejaculatory ducts do not pass through them. Pick them if the question asked which gland's duct joins the ductus deferens.","The bulbourethral glands are below the prostate and empty into the spongy urethra, downstream of where the ejaculatory ducts end. Choose them if the question asked which glands lie below the prostate."],"lt":"t-reproductive-male","lid":"c29","comp":142,"competencyId":"w6-sperm-path"},{"id":"L02523","dok":3,"q":"A 68-year-old man has a weak urine stream because a non-cancerous growth of a gland is squeezing his urethra just below the bladder. Which gland is enlarged?","a":"The prostate.","options":["The prostate","The bulbourethral gland","The epididymis","The seminal vesicle"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The prostate sits just below the bladder and wraps around the prostatic urethra. In benign prostatic hyperplasia, a non-cancerous enlargement common in older men, the growing gland presses inward on the urethra and narrows it. That is how a reproductive gland causes a urinary symptom like a weak stream.","notes":["Correct. An enlarged prostate squeezes the urethra just below the bladder.","The bulbourethral glands are pea-sized glands below the prostate that sit beside the urethra but do not encircle it. They would be the answer if the question asked which glands secrete alkaline mucus.","The epididymis is a duct on the back of the testis in the scrotum, nowhere near the bladder or urethra. Pick it if the question asked where sperm are stored and mature.","The seminal vesicles lie on the back of the bladder and do not surround the urethra, so their enlargement would not squeeze it. Choose them if the question asked which glands make the largest share of semen."],"lt":"t-reproductive-male","lid":"c30","comp":145,"competencyId":"w6-male-disorders"},{"id":"L02524","dok":2,"q":"Which tube carries both urine and semen in a male?","a":"The urethra.","options":["The urethra","The ejaculatory duct","The ureter","The ductus deferens"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The male urethra runs from the bladder through the prostate, the floor of the pelvis, and the penis to the outside. It carries urine from the bladder and semen from the ejaculatory ducts, though never both at the same moment. This shared final stretch is where the urinary and reproductive systems overlap.","notes":["Correct. The urethra is the shared tube for urine and semen.","The ejaculatory duct carries only sperm and seminal vesicle fluid, and only for a short distance through the prostate. It would be the answer if the question asked which duct empties into the prostatic urethra.","The ureter carries urine from the kidney to the bladder and never carries semen. Students mix it up with the urethra because the names look alike; pick ureter if the question asked what drains each kidney.","The ductus deferens carries only sperm, from the epididymis up through the spermatic cord. Choose it if the question asked which duct is cut in a vasectomy."],"lt":"t-reproductive-male","lid":"c31","comp":142,"competencyId":"w6-sperm-path"},{"id":"L02525","dok":2,"q":"A newborn's left testis never moved down into the scrotum and remains in the abdomen. What is this condition called?","a":"Cryptorchidism.","options":["Inguinal hernia","Hydrocele","Varicocele","Cryptorchidism"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Cryptorchidism is the failure of one or both testes to descend into the scrotum. The name means hidden testis, because the testis stays out of sight in the abdomen or the inguinal canal. An undescended testis stays at core body temperature, which impairs sperm production and raises the risk of testicular cancer.","notes":["An inguinal hernia is a loop of intestine pushing through the weak point at the inguinal canal. Pick it if the question described a bulge of intestine in the groin.","A hydrocele is a collection of serous fluid in the tunica vaginalis around a testis. Choose it if the question described a fluid-filled swelling around a testis that is already in the scrotum.","A varicocele is an abnormal swelling of the veins that drain the testis. It would be the answer if the question described enlarged veins in the scrotum.","Correct. A testis that fails to descend is cryptorchidism."],"lt":"t-reproductive-male","lid":"c32","comp":145,"competencyId":"w6-male-disorders"},{"id":"L02526","dok":2,"q":"In an inguinal hernia, what pushes through the weak point at the inguinal canal?","a":"A loop of intestine.","options":["An undescended testis","Fluid from the tunica vaginalis","A swollen testicular vein","A loop of intestine"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The inguinal canal is a natural passage through the lower abdominal wall that carries the spermatic cord in males. Because it is an opening, it is a weak point, and in an inguinal hernia a loop of intestine pushes through it, sometimes down toward the scrotum. This shows up as a bulge in the groin.","notes":["An undescended testis is cryptorchidism, where a testis stays in the abdomen or groin instead of reaching the scrotum. Pick it if the question asked about a testis that never descended.","Fluid collecting in the tunica vaginalis is a hydrocele. Choose it if the question described a fluid-filled swelling around the testis.","A swollen vein draining the testis is a varicocele. It would be the answer if the question described enlarged veins in the scrotum.","Correct. A loop of intestine pushes through the inguinal canal in an inguinal hernia."],"lt":"t-reproductive-male","lid":"c33","comp":145,"competencyId":"w6-male-disorders"},{"id":"L02527","dok":2,"q":"On which side of the penis does the corpus spongiosum lie?","a":"The ventral side.","options":["The dorsal side","The left and right sides","The root only","The ventral side"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The corpus spongiosum is the single column of erectile tissue on the ventral side of the penis, below the two corpora cavernosa. It surrounds the spongy urethra, runs the full length of the penis, and expands into the glans at the tip. Its ventral position is why the urethra lies along the underside of the penis.","notes":["The dorsal side holds the paired corpora cavernosa. Pick dorsal if the question asked where the two cavernosa run.","The two corpora cavernosa lie side by side, which can look like left and right, but they are both dorsal, and the spongiosum is a single midline column. This option comes from picturing the columns from the wrong angle.","The corpus spongiosum does form the bulb in the root, but it continues through the body all the way to the glans. Limiting it to the root leaves out most of its length.","Correct. The corpus spongiosum is the ventral column."],"lt":"t-reproductive-male","lid":"c34","comp":144,"competencyId":"w6-male-glands-penis"},{"id":"L02528","dok":2,"q":"The tunica albuginea divides each testis into a few hundred lobules. Which structures are packed into each lobule?","a":"Seminiferous tubules.","options":["Seminiferous tubules","Pampiniform veins","Erectile tissue","Lactiferous ducts"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Each lobule of the testis holds tightly coiled seminiferous tubules, the tubes where sperm are produced. The interstitial cells that make testosterone sit in the spaces between these tubules. Coiling long tubules into small lobules fits a great length of sperm-producing tissue into a small organ.","notes":["Correct. Seminiferous tubules fill the lobules of the testis.","The pampiniform plexus is a network of veins in the spermatic cord, outside the testis. Choose it if the question asked which veins drain the testis and surround the testicular artery.","Erectile tissue fills the corpora cavernosa and corpus spongiosum of the penis. It would be the answer if the question asked what fills with blood during an erection.","Lactiferous ducts carry milk in the mammary gland, a female structure that is also divided into lobules. Pick them if the question asked which ducts drain the lobes of the breast."],"lt":"t-reproductive-male","lid":"c35","comp":141,"competencyId":"w6-scrotum-testes"},{"id":"L02529","dok":3,"q":"During a self-exam, a 25-year-old man finds a firm lump in one testis. If the lump is malignant, which disorder is it?","a":"Testicular cancer.","options":["Hydrocele","Prostate cancer","Testicular cancer","Varicocele"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Testicular cancer is a malignant tumor of the testis, usually starting in the sperm-forming cells of the seminiferous tubules. Because the testes hang in the scrotum outside the body, a man can feel them directly, which is why self-exams are taught. A firm lump in the testis itself is the warning sign.","notes":["A hydrocele is a collection of serous fluid in the tunica vaginalis around the testis, not a tumor. Pick it if the question described a fluid-filled swelling.","Prostate cancer is a malignant tumor of the prostate, deep in the pelvis below the bladder, so it cannot be felt in the testis. It would be the answer if the question described a malignant growth of the gland around the urethra.","Correct. A malignant tumor of the testis is testicular cancer.","A varicocele is an abnormal swelling of the veins that drain the testis, not a growth in the testis itself. Choose it if the question described enlarged veins in the scrotum."],"lt":"t-reproductive-male","lid":"c36","comp":145,"competencyId":"w6-male-disorders"},{"id":"L02530","dok":2,"q":"On a sagittal model of the male pelvis, you find a pair of elongated glands pressed against the back of the urinary bladder. What are they?","a":"The seminal vesicles.","options":["The seminal vesicles","The ureters","The prostate","The bulbourethral glands"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The seminal vesicles are a pair of elongated glands on the posterior surface of the urinary bladder. Each one's duct joins the ampulla of the ductus deferens to form an ejaculatory duct. On a model, look behind the bladder and above the prostate to find them.","notes":["Correct. The seminal vesicles are the paired glands on the back of the bladder.","The ureters do reach the back of the bladder, but they are tubes carrying urine from the kidneys, not glands. They would be the answer if the question asked which tubes enter the posterior wall of the bladder.","The prostate is a single gland below the bladder around the urethra, not a pair on its back wall. Choose it if the question asked which gland surrounds the first part of the urethra.","The bulbourethral glands are pea-sized and sit below the prostate, not on the back of the bladder. Pick them if the question asked which glands lie beside the membranous urethra."],"lt":"t-reproductive-male","lid":"c37","comp":144,"competencyId":"w6-male-glands-penis"},{"id":"L02531","dok":1,"q":"Which part of the penis is the free, movable shaft?","a":"The body.","options":["The glans","The crura","The root","The body"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The body, or shaft, is the free, movable portion of the penis between the attached root and the glans at the tip. It contains all three columns of erectile tissue: the two corpora cavernosa and the corpus spongiosum with the spongy urethra inside it.","notes":["The glans is the acorn-shaped tip, an expansion of the corpus spongiosum. Choose it if the question asked for the enlarged tip.","The crura are the anchored ends of the corpora cavernosa, found in the root rather than the shaft. They would be the answer if the question asked how the dorsal columns attach to the pelvic bones.","The root is the attached portion anchored in the perineum, containing the bulb and the crura. Pick it if the question asked which part is fixed to the body.","Correct. The body is the free shaft of the penis."],"lt":"t-reproductive-male","lid":"c38","comp":144,"competencyId":"w6-male-glands-penis"},{"id":"L02532","dok":2,"q":"Before birth, each testis moves from the abdomen down to the scrotum. Which passage in the abdominal wall does it travel through?","a":"The inguinal canal.","options":["The esophageal hiatus","The obturator canal","The umbilical ring","The inguinal canal"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The testes form high on the back wall of the abdomen during fetal development and then descend, passing through the inguinal canal in the lower anterior abdominal wall to reach the scrotum. The spermatic cord later runs through this same canal. Because the canal is a natural opening in the wall, it stays a weak point where an inguinal hernia can occur.","notes":["The esophageal hiatus is the opening in the diaphragm that the esophagus passes through, the site of a hiatal hernia. Choose it if the question asked where the esophagus crosses from thorax to abdomen.","The obturator canal is a small gap at the top of the obturator foramen of the hip bone that leads into the thigh. It would be the answer if the question asked about a passage from the pelvis into the medial thigh, not into the scrotum.","The umbilical ring is the opening at the navel where the umbilical cord vessels passed, and it is the site of an umbilical hernia. Pick it if the question asked about the opening at the belly button.","Correct. The testes descend through the inguinal canal."],"lt":"t-reproductive-male","lid":"c39","comp":145,"competencyId":"w6-male-disorders"},{"id":"L02533","dok":2,"q":"Which sequence correctly traces sperm from where they are made, to where they are stored, to the duct that carries them out of the scrotum?","a":"Seminiferous tubules, then epididymis, then ductus deferens.","options":["Seminiferous tubules, then epididymis, then ductus deferens","Seminiferous tubules, then ductus deferens, then epididymis","Ductus deferens, then epididymis, then seminiferous tubules","Epididymis, then seminiferous tubules, then ductus deferens"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Sperm are produced in the seminiferous tubules inside the testis. They then move into the epididymis on the back of the testis, where they are stored and gain the ability to move. Finally the ductus deferens carries them up out of the scrotum inside the spermatic cord.","notes":["Correct. Production, then storage, then transport out of the scrotum.","This swaps the last two steps. The epididymis sits right on the testis and comes before the ductus deferens, which begins at the tail of the epididymis.","This is the whole route in reverse. It traces the path from the pelvis back toward the testis, which is the direction a student might follow when reading a diagram from the top down.","This puts storage before production. Sperm cannot be stored in the epididymis until the seminiferous tubules have made them, so the tubules always come first."],"lt":"t-reproductive-male","lid":"c40","comp":142,"competencyId":"w6-sperm-path"},{"id":"L02534","dok":2,"q":"Sperm have just left the ductus deferens. Which two structures do they pass through next, in order, before leaving the body?","a":"The ejaculatory duct, then the urethra.","options":["The urethra, then the ejaculatory duct","The epididymis, then the urethra","The ejaculatory duct, then the urethra","The rete testis, then the ejaculatory duct"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The ductus deferens joins the seminal vesicle duct to form the ejaculatory duct, so that is the next stop. The ejaculatory duct then empties into the prostatic urethra, and sperm travel through the prostatic, membranous, and spongy urethra to the outside. The full route is seminiferous tubules, epididymis, ductus deferens, ejaculatory duct, and urethra.","notes":["This reverses the two. The ejaculatory duct empties into the urethra, so the urethra must come second.","The epididymis comes before the ductus deferens, not after it. Pick the epididymis if the question asked where sperm go right after leaving the testis.","Correct. Ejaculatory duct first, then the urethra.","The rete testis is the network of channels inside the testis, at the very start of the route. It would be the answer if the question asked where sperm go after the seminiferous tubules."],"lt":"t-reproductive-male","lid":"c41","comp":142,"competencyId":"w6-sperm-path"},{"id":"L02535","dok":3,"q":"Starting at the testis, in what order do sperm reach these three places: the ejaculatory duct, the epididymis, and the inguinal canal?","a":"Epididymis, inguinal canal, ejaculatory duct.","options":["Inguinal canal, epididymis, ejaculatory duct","Ejaculatory duct, epididymis, inguinal canal","Epididymis, inguinal canal, ejaculatory duct","Epididymis, ejaculatory duct, inguinal canal"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Sperm first enter the epididymis on the back of the testis, inside the scrotum. The ductus deferens then carries them up in the spermatic cord, through the inguinal canal in the abdominal wall, and into the pelvis. Deep in the pelvis, behind the bladder, the ductus deferens joins the seminal vesicle duct to form the ejaculatory duct.","notes":["The inguinal canal cannot come first, because sperm are still in the scrotum while in the epididymis. The ductus deferens carries them through the canal only after they leave the epididymis.","The ejaculatory duct is near the end of the route, not the start. This order treats it as if it were in the testis, which is a common mix-up with the small efferent ducts that carry sperm out of the testis.","Correct. Epididymis in the scrotum, then the inguinal canal, then the ejaculatory duct in the pelvis.","The ejaculatory duct lies deep in the pelvis, so sperm must pass through the inguinal canal to reach it. This order skips the trip from the scrotum up into the pelvis."],"lt":"t-reproductive-male","lid":"c42","comp":142,"competencyId":"w6-sperm-path"},{"id":"L02536","dok":2,"q":"The male reproductive system is sorted into gonads, ducts, accessory glands, and supporting structures. Which two make up the supporting structures?","a":"The scrotum and the penis.","options":["The testes and interstitial cells","The scrotum and penis","The epididymis and ductus deferens","The prostate and seminal vesicles"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The supporting structures are the scrotum and the penis. The scrotum holds the testes outside the body at a slightly cooler temperature, and the penis delivers sperm into the female reproductive tract and carries the urethra. They neither make sperm nor add fluid, which is why they are grouped separately from the gonads, ducts, and glands.","notes":["The testes and their interstitial cells belong to the gonads, which produce sperm and hormones. They would be the answer if the question asked where testosterone is made.","Correct. The scrotum and penis are the supporting structures.","The epididymis and ductus deferens belong to the duct system, which stores and carries sperm. Choose them if the question asked for two parts of the duct system.","The prostate and seminal vesicles are accessory glands that add fluid to semen. Pick them if the question asked for two of the accessory glands."],"lt":"t-reproductive-male","lid":"c43","comp":141,"competencyId":"w6-scrotum-testes"},{"id":"L02537","dok":3,"q":"Following the path of sperm downstream, in what order do you pass the places where the three accessory glands empty their fluid?","a":"Seminal vesicles, then prostate, then bulbourethral glands.","options":["Seminal vesicles, then prostate, then bulbourethral glands","Seminal vesicles, then bulbourethral glands, then prostate","Prostate, then seminal vesicles, then bulbourethral glands","Bulbourethral glands, then prostate, then seminal vesicles"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The seminal vesicle ducts come first, because they merge with the ductus deferens to form the ejaculatory ducts behind the bladder. The prostate comes next, with its ducts opening into the prostatic urethra just below the bladder. The bulbourethral glands come last, emptying into the spongy urethra below the prostate. This is the order of the openings along the path, not the order in time that each gland releases its fluid.","notes":["Correct. The seminal vesicles empty at the ejaculatory ducts, the prostate into the prostatic urethra, and the bulbourethral glands into the spongy urethra.","This swaps the last two. The bulbourethral glands sit below the prostate and open into the spongy urethra, so they come after the prostate, not before it.","This puts the prostate first, but the seminal vesicle fluid joins earlier, at the ejaculatory duct behind the bladder, before the sperm ever reach the prostate.","This is the order reversed. It lists the openings from the tip of the penis back toward the bladder, the opposite of the direction sperm travel."],"lt":"t-reproductive-male","lid":"c44","comp":144,"competencyId":"w6-male-glands-penis"},{"id":"L02538","dok":2,"q":"Name the male gonad, the fibrous capsule that divides it into lobules, and the coiled tubes inside where sperm form.","a":"The testis, the tunica albuginea, and the seminiferous tubules.","options":["Testis, dartos muscle, seminiferous tubules","Testis, tunica albuginea, seminiferous tubules","Testis, tunica vaginalis, seminiferous tubules","Epididymis, tunica albuginea, efferent ducts"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The testis is the male gonad. Its tough white fibrous capsule, the tunica albuginea, sends walls inward that divide it into a few hundred lobules. Each lobule holds tightly coiled seminiferous tubules, where sperm are produced.","notes":["The dartos is the smooth muscle layer of the scrotal wall, not a capsule of the testis. Pick it if the question asked which muscle wrinkles the scrotal skin.","Correct. Testis, tunica albuginea, and seminiferous tubules.","The tunica vaginalis is the serous membrane covering the testis, but it does not divide the testis into lobules. Choose it if the question asked for the covering derived from the peritoneum.","The epididymis is a duct on the testis, not the gonad, and the efferent ducts carry sperm out of the testis rather than producing them. This combination describes the exit route, not the sperm factory."],"lt":"t-reproductive-male","lid":"c45","comp":141,"competencyId":"w6-scrotum-testes"},{"id":"L02539","dok":1,"q":"What two jobs do the testes perform?","a":"They produce sperm and secrete hormones.","options":["Produce sperm and secrete hormones","Add fructose fluid and nourish sperm","Carry both sperm and urine","Store sperm and help them mature"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The testes are the male gonads, and they do two jobs. The seminiferous tubules inside them produce sperm, and the interstitial cells between the tubules secrete hormones, mainly testosterone. Every other male reproductive structure stores, carries, or adds fluid to the sperm the testes make.","notes":["Correct. The testes produce sperm and secrete hormones.","Adding a fructose-rich fluid that nourishes sperm is the job of the seminal vesicles. Choose this if the question asked about the glands on the back of the bladder.","Carrying both sperm and urine is the job of the male urethra, the shared final tube. It would be the answer if the question asked what the urethra does.","Storing sperm and helping them mature is the job of the epididymis, the duct on the back of the testis. Pick this if the question asked what the epididymis does."],"lt":"t-reproductive-male","lid":"c1-x2","comp":141,"competencyId":"w6-scrotum-testes"},{"id":"L02540","dok":1,"q":"Which list names the structures of the male duct system?","a":"Epididymis, ductus deferens, ejaculatory duct, and urethra.","options":["Epididymis, ductus deferens, ejaculatory duct, urethra","Seminal vesicles, prostate gland, bulbourethral glands","Epididymis, ductus deferens, ejaculatory duct, ureter","Seminiferous tubules, epididymis, prostate gland, urethra"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The male duct system is the epididymis, ductus deferens, ejaculatory ducts, and urethra, in that order. These ducts store, mature, and carry sperm from the testis to the outside. The seminiferous tubules make the sperm and the accessory glands add fluid, so neither group is counted as part of the duct system.","notes":["Correct. These four ducts make up the male duct system.","These are the three accessory glands, which add fluid to semen rather than carry sperm. Pick this list if the question asked for the accessory glands.","The ureter carries urine from the kidney to the bladder and is part of the urinary system. Replacing the urethra with the ureter is the classic look-alike mix-up.","The seminiferous tubules produce sperm inside the testis, and the prostate is an accessory gland, so this list mixes groups. It would only fit a question asking for structures sperm pass through or near."],"lt":"t-reproductive-male","lid":"c2-x2","comp":142,"competencyId":"w6-sperm-path"},{"id":"L02541","dok":1,"q":"What does the dartos muscle do?","a":"It wrinkles the scrotal skin to reduce heat loss.","options":["Squeezes urine out of the urinary bladder","Wrinkles the scrotal skin to reduce heat loss","Raises and lowers the testes inside the scrotum","Pushes sperm along the ductus deferens"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The dartos is a layer of smooth muscle in the scrotal wall. When it contracts, it wrinkles and tightens the scrotal skin, which shrinks the surface area and reduces heat loss from the testes. Together with the cremaster, it helps keep the testes near the temperature sperm production needs.","notes":["Squeezing urine out of the bladder is the job of the detrusor, the smooth muscle of the bladder wall. Choose this if the question asked about the detrusor.","Correct. The dartos wrinkles the scrotal skin to reduce heat loss.","Raising and lowering the testes is the job of the cremaster, the skeletal muscle in the spermatic cord. Pick this if the question asked what the cremaster does.","The ductus deferens has its own thick smooth muscle wall that pushes sperm along. This would describe that wall, not a muscle of the scrotum."],"lt":"t-reproductive-male","lid":"c3-x2","comp":141,"competencyId":"w6-scrotum-testes"},{"id":"L02542","dok":1,"q":"What type of tissue makes up the cremaster muscle?","a":"Skeletal muscle.","options":["Cardiac muscle","Skeletal muscle","Smooth muscle","Dense regular connective tissue"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The cremaster is skeletal muscle, the striated type of muscle tissue attached to the trunk and limbs. It runs in the spermatic cord and loops around the testis, raising the testis toward the body in the cold and letting it hang lower when warm. The two scrotal muscles are different tissues: the cremaster is skeletal, and the dartos is smooth.","notes":["Cardiac muscle is found only in the wall of the heart. Students who pick it are usually guessing at an involuntary-looking muscle, but the cremaster is not related to the heart.","Correct. The cremaster is skeletal muscle.","Smooth muscle is the tissue of the dartos, the other scrotal muscle, which wrinkles the skin. Pick smooth muscle if the question asked about the dartos.","Dense regular connective tissue forms tendons and ligaments, not a contracting muscle. It would be the answer if the question asked what tissue connects muscle to bone."],"lt":"t-reproductive-male","lid":"c4-x2","comp":141,"competencyId":"w6-scrotum-testes"},{"id":"L02543","dok":2,"q":"Along the route of sperm, which structure is the only one that produces sperm rather than storing or carrying them?","a":"The seminiferous tubules.","options":["The epididymis","The seminiferous tubules","The ejaculatory duct","The ductus deferens"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Only the seminiferous tubules, the coiled tubes inside the testis, make sperm. Every other structure on the route has a different job: the epididymis stores and matures them, and the ductus deferens, ejaculatory duct, and urethra carry them. Sorting the male structures into one producer and several transporters makes the route easier to remember.","notes":["The epididymis stores sperm and lets them gain the ability to move, but it does not make them. Pick it if the question asked where sperm mature.","Correct. The seminiferous tubules are the only sperm producers.","The ejaculatory duct is a short duct that carries sperm and seminal vesicle fluid into the prostatic urethra. It would be the answer if the question asked which duct passes through the prostate.","The ductus deferens carries sperm up out of the scrotum through the spermatic cord. Choose it if the question asked which duct is cut in a vasectomy."],"lt":"t-reproductive-male","lid":"c5-x2","comp":142,"competencyId":"w6-sperm-path"},{"id":"L02544","dok":1,"q":"What is another name for the interstitial cells of the testis?","a":"Leydig cells.","options":["Leydig cells","Granulosa cells","Sertoli cells","Podocytes"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The interstitial cells of the testis are also called Leydig cells. They sit in the spaces between the seminiferous tubules and produce testosterone. Pairing each eponym with its location helps: Leydig cells are between the tubules, and Sertoli cells are inside the tubules.","notes":["Correct. Interstitial cells are also called Leydig cells.","Granulosa cells surround the developing oocyte inside an ovarian follicle, a female structure. Choose them if the question asked which cells form the layers of a follicle in the ovary.","Sertoli cells are the sustentacular cells that line the seminiferous tubules, nourish developing sperm, and form the blood-testis barrier. Pick them if the question asked for another name for the sustentacular cells.","Podocytes are cells in the kidney that wrap around the glomerular capillaries and form part of the filtration membrane. They would be the answer if the question asked about the visceral layer of the glomerular capsule."],"lt":"t-reproductive-male","lid":"c6-x2","comp":141,"competencyId":"w6-scrotum-testes"},{"id":"L02545","dok":1,"q":"The epididymis belongs to which group of male reproductive structures?","a":"The duct system.","options":["The accessory glands","The gonads","The supporting structures","The duct system"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The epididymis is the first part of the male duct system, which also includes the ductus deferens, the ejaculatory ducts, and the urethra. It is a coiled duct on the back of the testis where sperm are stored and gain the ability to move. Even though it sits right on the testis, it does not make sperm, so it is a duct and not part of the gonad.","notes":["The accessory glands are the seminal vesicles, prostate, and bulbourethral glands, which add fluid to semen. Choose this group if the question asked where the prostate belongs.","The gonads are the testes, which produce sperm and hormones. Pick this group if the question asked where the testes belong.","The supporting structures are the scrotum and the penis. They would be the answer if the question asked which group holds the testes and delivers sperm.","Correct. The epididymis is part of the duct system."],"lt":"t-reproductive-male","lid":"c7-x2","comp":142,"competencyId":"w6-sperm-path"},{"id":"L02546","dok":3,"q":"In a vasectomy, a surgeon cuts and ties the duct that carries sperm upward from the scrotum in the spermatic cord. Which duct is cut?","a":"The ductus deferens.","options":["The epididymis","The ejaculatory duct","The ductus deferens","The urethra"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"A vasectomy cuts the ductus deferens, also called the vas deferens, which is where the procedure gets its name. The duct is easy to reach because it runs in the spermatic cord just under the scrotal skin. Once it is cut, sperm cannot travel past the scrotum, but the accessory glands still add their fluid, so semen is still released without sperm.","notes":["The epididymis is the coiled duct on the back of the testis where sperm are stored. It sits before the cut, which is why sperm still form and collect there after a vasectomy.","The ejaculatory duct lies deep in the pelvis and passes through the prostate, so it cannot be reached through the scrotum. Pick it if the question asked where the ductus deferens and seminal vesicle duct join.","Correct. A vasectomy cuts the ductus (vas) deferens.","The urethra carries urine as well as semen, so cutting it would block urination. It would be the answer if the question asked which tube is shared by the urinary and reproductive systems."],"lt":"t-reproductive-male","lid":"c8-x2","comp":142,"competencyId":"w6-sperm-path"},{"id":"L02547","dok":2,"q":"Which list best describes the contents of the spermatic cord?","a":"The ductus deferens, blood vessels, nerves, and the cremaster muscle.","options":["Ductus deferens, blood vessels, nerves, cremaster","Ductus deferens, blood vessels, nerves, dartos","Seminal vesicle ducts, prostate, bulbourethral ducts","Ejaculatory duct, blood vessels, nerves, cremaster"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The spermatic cord is the bundle that suspends each testis and passes through the inguinal canal. It contains the ductus deferens, blood vessels (the testicular artery and the pampiniform plexus of veins), nerves, lymphatic vessels, and the cremaster muscle. Everything a testis needs to connect to the body travels in this cord.","notes":["Correct. The cord holds the ductus deferens, vessels, nerves, and the cremaster.","The dartos is smooth muscle in the wall of the scrotum, not in the spermatic cord. Swapping in the dartos for the cremaster is the common mix-up between the two scrotal muscles.","The seminal vesicles, prostate, and bulbourethral glands are accessory glands in the pelvis, all outside the cord. Pick this list if the question asked which structures add fluid to semen.","The ejaculatory duct lies deep in the pelvis behind the bladder, not in the cord. The duct inside the cord is the ductus deferens, which only later becomes part of the ejaculatory duct."],"lt":"t-reproductive-male","lid":"c9-x2","comp":142,"competencyId":"w6-sperm-path"},{"id":"L02548","dok":1,"q":"Which part of a sperm cell holds the nucleus with its chromosomes?","a":"The head.","options":["The head","The middle piece","The end piece","The principal piece"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The head of the sperm holds the nucleus, which carries 23 chromosomes, half the number in a body cell. At fertilization, these chromosomes join the 23 in the oocyte. The head is capped by the acrosome, which holds enzymes that help the sperm get through the coverings of the oocyte.","notes":["Correct. The head holds the nucleus and its 23 chromosomes.","The middle piece is packed with mitochondria that power the tail. Pick it if the question asked which part supplies energy for swimming.","The end piece is the thin, tapering last part of the tail. It would be the answer if the question asked for the terminal portion of the tail.","The principal piece is the longest part of the tail and drives most of the swimming motion. Choose it if the question asked for the longest segment of the tail."],"lt":"t-reproductive-male","lid":"c10-x2","comp":143,"competencyId":"w6-sperm-cell"},{"id":"L02549","dok":1,"q":"What is the acrosome of a sperm cell?","a":"An enzyme-filled cap over the head that helps the sperm penetrate the oocyte.","options":["An enzyme-filled cap over the head","The longest part of the tail","The part of the tail packed with mitochondria","The neck region that holds the centrioles"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The acrosome is a cap that covers the front of the sperm head, and it is a sac of enzymes. When the sperm reaches the oocyte, these enzymes let it digest a path through the layers that surround the oocyte. It sits on the head because the head carries the nucleus that must get through.","notes":["Correct. The acrosome is the enzyme-filled cap over the head.","The longest part of the tail is the principal piece. It would be the answer if the question asked which segment of the tail is longest.","The part of the tail packed with mitochondria is the middle piece. Pick this description if the question asked about the middle piece.","The neck is the short part just behind the head that contains the centrioles. Choose this description if the question asked about the neck."],"lt":"t-reproductive-male","lid":"c11-x2","comp":143,"competencyId":"w6-sperm-cell"},{"id":"L02550","dok":1,"q":"Which structure adds an alkaline fluid rich in fructose to the sperm?","a":"The seminal vesicles.","options":["The testes","The prostate","The bulbourethral glands","The seminal vesicles"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The seminal vesicles, the paired glands on the back of the bladder, add an alkaline fluid rich in fructose. Fructose is a sugar the sperm use for energy. This secretion is the largest share of semen, about 60 percent of its volume.","notes":["The testes produce the sperm and testosterone, not the fluid that carries the sperm. They would be the answer if the question asked where sperm are made.","The prostate adds a milky fluid that helps sperm stay active, not an alkaline, fructose-rich one. Pick it if the question asked which gland surrounds the prostatic urethra.","The bulbourethral glands add a clear, alkaline mucus that lubricates the urethra, but it is not rich in fructose. Choose them if the question asked which glands neutralize urine in the urethra.","Correct. The seminal vesicles add the alkaline, fructose-rich fluid."],"lt":"t-reproductive-male","lid":"c12-x2","comp":144,"competencyId":"w6-male-glands-penis"},{"id":"L02551","dok":1,"q":"What is another name for the bulbourethral glands?","a":"Cowper's glands.","options":["Bartholin's glands","Leydig cells","Skene's glands","Cowper's glands"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The bulbourethral glands are also called Cowper's glands. They are pea-sized glands below the prostate that release a clear, alkaline mucus into the spongy urethra. The mucus lubricates the urethra and neutralizes traces of urine before sperm pass through.","notes":["Bartholin's glands are the greater vestibular glands of the female, which lubricate the vestibule beside the vaginal opening. Pick them if the question asked for the female counterpart of the bulbourethral glands.","Leydig cells are the interstitial cells of the testis that produce testosterone. They are cells between the seminiferous tubules, not a gland that empties into the urethra.","Skene's glands are small female glands that open near the external urethral orifice, also called the paraurethral glands. Choose them if the question asked about glands beside the female urethra.","Correct. Cowper's glands is another name for the bulbourethral glands."],"lt":"t-reproductive-male","lid":"c13-x2","comp":144,"competencyId":"w6-male-glands-penis"},{"id":"L02552","dok":1,"q":"How many columns of erectile tissue does the penis contain?","a":"Three.","options":["Two","Three","Four","One"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The penis is built around three columns of erectile tissue. Two are the corpora cavernosa, which run along the dorsal side, and one is the corpus spongiosum, which runs along the ventral side and surrounds the spongy urethra. All three fill with blood during an erection.","notes":["Two counts only the paired corpora cavernosa and leaves out the single corpus spongiosum. Students who remember only the two larger columns pick this.","Correct. Two corpora cavernosa plus one corpus spongiosum make three.","Four comes from counting the corpus spongiosum twice, once for its column and once for the glans at its tip. The glans is an expansion of the spongiosum, not a separate column.","One counts only the corpus spongiosum around the urethra and leaves out the two corpora cavernosa. Students who remember only the column with the urethra tend to pick this."],"lt":"t-reproductive-male","lid":"c14-x2","comp":144,"competencyId":"w6-male-glands-penis"},{"id":"L02553","dok":1,"q":"Which column of erectile tissue surrounds the spongy urethra?","a":"The corpus spongiosum.","options":["The prepuce","The crura","The corpus spongiosum","The corpora cavernosa"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The corpus spongiosum is the single ventral column of erectile tissue, and it surrounds the urethra along the full length of the penis. That is why this part of the urethra is named the spongy urethra. The corpus spongiosum ends at the tip by expanding into the glans penis.","notes":["The prepuce is the fold of skin over the glans, not erectile tissue. It would be the answer if the question asked what the foreskin is called.","The crura are the anchored ends of the corpora cavernosa in the root of the penis, and they do not hold the urethra. Choose them if the question asked how the dorsal columns attach to the pelvic bones.","Correct. The corpus spongiosum surrounds the spongy urethra.","The corpora cavernosa are the two dorsal columns, and the urethra does not run through them. Pick them if the question asked which paired columns run along the dorsal side."],"lt":"t-reproductive-male","lid":"c15-x2","comp":144,"competencyId":"w6-male-glands-penis"},{"id":"L02554","dok":2,"q":"Sperm leave the testis. Which duct of the male duct system do they enter first?","a":"The epididymis.","options":["The ejaculatory duct","The ductus deferens","The epididymis","The urethra"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The epididymis is the first duct of the male duct system, sitting right on the back of the testis. Sperm enter it from the testis, are stored there, and gain the ability to move. From the epididymis, the route continues into the ductus deferens, then the ejaculatory duct, and finally the urethra.","notes":["The ejaculatory duct comes after the ductus deferens, deep in the pelvis. Choose it if the question asked which duct the ductus deferens helps form.","The ductus deferens comes after the epididymis, beginning at its tail. Pick it if the question asked which duct sperm enter after the epididymis.","Correct. The epididymis is the first duct sperm enter after leaving the testis.","The urethra is the last duct on the route, the shared tube to the outside. It would be the answer if the question asked which duct sperm pass through just before leaving the body."],"lt":"t-reproductive-male","lid":"c16-x2","comp":142,"competencyId":"w6-sperm-path"},{"id":"L02555","dok":2,"q":"Where along the male duct system does seminal vesicle fluid first mix with the sperm?","a":"In the ejaculatory duct.","options":["In the ejaculatory duct","In the epididymis","In the ductus deferens","In the spongy urethra"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The duct of each seminal vesicle joins the ampulla of the ductus deferens, and the two together form the ejaculatory duct. So the ejaculatory duct is the first place where sperm and seminal vesicle fluid flow together. The mixture then enters the prostatic urethra, where prostate fluid is added.","notes":["Correct. Seminal vesicle fluid first meets the sperm in the ejaculatory duct.","The epididymis stores sperm on the back of the testis, long before any gland fluid is added. Pick it if the question asked where sperm are stored and mature.","The ductus deferens carries only sperm until its end, where the seminal vesicle duct joins it. Choose it if the question asked which duct carries sperm through the inguinal canal.","The spongy urethra is where the bulbourethral glands add their mucus, much later on the route. It would be the answer if the question asked where bulbourethral mucus enters."],"lt":"t-reproductive-male","lid":"c17-x2","comp":142,"competencyId":"w6-sperm-path"},{"id":"L02556","dok":2,"q":"Which cells line the seminiferous tubules and form the blood-testis barrier?","a":"The sustentacular (Sertoli) cells.","options":["The interstitial cells","The juxtaglomerular cells","The sustentacular cells","The podocytes"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The sustentacular cells, also called Sertoli cells, line the seminiferous tubules and surround the developing sperm. Tight connections between neighboring Sertoli cells form the blood-testis barrier, which separates the developing sperm from the blood supply. They also nourish the sperm as they develop.","notes":["The interstitial (Leydig) cells sit between the tubules and produce testosterone. Pick them if the question asked which cells of the testis make the male hormone.","Juxtaglomerular cells are modified smooth muscle cells in the wall of the afferent arteriole of the kidney, part of the juxtaglomerular apparatus. They would be the answer if the question asked about the JGA.","Correct. Sustentacular cells line the tubules and form the blood-testis barrier.","Podocytes are kidney cells that wrap around the glomerular capillaries and form part of the filtration membrane, a different barrier in a different organ. Choose them if the question asked about the filtration membrane of the kidney."],"lt":"t-reproductive-male","lid":"c18-x2","comp":141,"competencyId":"w6-scrotum-testes"},{"id":"L02557","dok":2,"q":"Starting at the bladder, which order correctly lists the three regions of the male urethra?","a":"Prostatic, membranous, spongy.","options":["Membranous, prostatic, spongy","Spongy, prostatic, membranous","Prostatic, membranous, spongy","Prostatic, spongy, membranous"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The male urethra first passes through the prostate just below the bladder (the prostatic urethra). It then crosses the floor of the pelvis in the perineum as the short membranous urethra. Finally, it runs the length of the penis inside the corpus spongiosum as the spongy urethra, the longest region.","notes":["This puts the membranous region first, but the prostate sits directly against the bladder, so the prostatic urethra must come first.","This order begins in the penis and ends in the pelvic floor, which mixes the outermost region with the deep ones. The spongy urethra is the last region, not the first.","Correct. Prostatic, then membranous, then spongy.","This swaps the last two. The membranous urethra is the short middle stretch through the pelvic floor, and the spongy urethra comes after it in the penis."],"lt":"t-reproductive-male","lid":"c19-x2","comp":139,"competencyId":"w6-urine-path"},{"id":"L02558","dok":1,"q":"Which gland adds a milky fluid that helps sperm stay active?","a":"The prostate.","options":["The seminal vesicles","The testes","The prostate","The bulbourethral glands"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The prostate is the single gland below the bladder that surrounds the urethra. Many small ducts carry its milky fluid into the prostatic urethra, where it helps the sperm stay active. The seminal vesicles and the bulbourethral glands, in contrast, add alkaline secretions.","notes":["The seminal vesicles add an alkaline fluid rich in fructose, the largest share of semen. Pick them if the question asked which gland supplies the sugar sperm use for energy.","The testes make sperm and testosterone, not the fluid portion of semen. They would be the answer if the question asked which organs are the male gonads.","Correct. The prostate adds a milky fluid that helps sperm stay active.","The bulbourethral glands add a clear, alkaline mucus. Choose them if the question asked which glands lubricate the urethra and neutralize urine."],"lt":"t-reproductive-male","lid":"c20-x2","comp":144,"competencyId":"w6-male-glands-penis"},{"id":"L02559","dok":2,"q":"Which serous membrane, derived from the peritoneum, partly covers each testis?","a":"The tunica vaginalis.","options":["The visceral pleura","The tunica vaginalis","The parietal peritoneum","The tunica albuginea"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The tunica vaginalis is a serous membrane that came from a pouch of peritoneum carried down with the testis as it descended. It partly covers the front and sides of each testis, outside the tunica albuginea. Like other serous membranes, it has two layers with a thin film of fluid between them, and too much fluid here is called a hydrocele.","notes":["The visceral pleura is the serous membrane on the surface of each lung. Choose it if the question asked which membrane covers the lungs.","Correct. The tunica vaginalis is the serous membrane around the testis.","The parietal peritoneum lines the wall of the abdominopelvic cavity. The tunica vaginalis is derived from it, but the parietal peritoneum itself stays in the abdomen, so pick it only if the question asked what lines the abdominal wall.","The tunica albuginea is the fibrous capsule of the testis, deep to the tunica vaginalis, and it is not a serous membrane. Pick it if the question asked which capsule divides the testis into lobules."],"lt":"t-reproductive-male","lid":"c21-x2","comp":141,"competencyId":"w6-scrotum-testes"},{"id":"L02560","dok":2,"q":"Which part of the penis contains the bulb and the crura?","a":"The root.","options":["The root","The body","The glans","The prepuce"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The root is the attached portion of the penis in the perineum. It contains the bulb, the enlarged base of the corpus spongiosum, and the two crura, the anchored ends of the corpora cavernosa that attach to the pelvic bones. These anchor the penis, while the body and glans hang free.","notes":["Correct. The root contains the bulb and the crura.","The body is the free, movable shaft, where the three erectile columns run side by side. Pick it if the question asked for the part between the root and the tip.","The glans is the acorn-shaped tip, the expansion of the corpus spongiosum at the opposite end from the bulb. Choose it if the question asked which part the external urethral orifice opens on.","The prepuce is the foreskin over the glans, a fold of skin with no erectile columns. It would be the answer if the question asked what is removed in circumcision."],"lt":"t-reproductive-male","lid":"c22-x2","comp":144,"competencyId":"w6-male-glands-penis"},{"id":"L02561","dok":2,"q":"The tunica albuginea sends walls inward into each testis. What does it divide the testis into?","a":"A few hundred wedge-shaped lobules.","options":["A few hundred wedge-shaped lobules","Three erectile columns","Root, body, and glans","Right and left compartments"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The tunica albuginea is the white fibrous capsule of the testis. Walls of this capsule extend inward and divide the testis into a few hundred small, wedge-shaped lobules. Each lobule holds tightly coiled seminiferous tubules, where sperm are made.","notes":["Correct. The tunica albuginea divides each testis into a few hundred lobules.","Three erectile columns describe the penis, with two corpora cavernosa and one corpus spongiosum. Choose this if the question asked how the erectile tissue of the penis is arranged.","Root, body, and glans are the three regions of the penis. They would be the answer if the question asked how the penis is divided from base to tip.","Right and left compartments are made by the scrotal septum, which divides the scrotum, not the testis. Pick this if the question asked what the scrotal septum does."],"lt":"t-reproductive-male","lid":"c23-x2","comp":141,"competencyId":"w6-scrotum-testes"},{"id":"L02562","dok":1,"q":"Which structure is removed in a circumcision?","a":"The prepuce.","options":["The raphe","The prepuce","The glans","The corpus spongiosum"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Circumcision removes the prepuce, the loose fold of skin, also called the foreskin, that covers the glans penis. The glans and the erectile columns beneath it are left intact. After circumcision, the glans is exposed rather than covered.","notes":["The raphe is the seam-like ridge on the surface of the scrotum. Choose it if the question asked what marks the midline of the scrotum.","Correct. Circumcision removes the prepuce.","The glans is the acorn-shaped tip that the prepuce covers, and it stays in place after circumcision. Pick it if the question asked for the expanded end of the corpus spongiosum.","The corpus spongiosum is the erectile column around the urethra, deep to the skin, and it is not touched in circumcision. It would be the answer if the question asked which column surrounds the spongy urethra."],"lt":"t-reproductive-male","lid":"c24-x2","comp":144,"competencyId":"w6-male-glands-penis"},{"id":"L02563","dok":2,"q":"Which region of the male urethra runs through the penis inside the corpus spongiosum?","a":"The spongy urethra.","options":["The spongy urethra","The ejaculatory duct","The membranous urethra","The prostatic urethra"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The spongy urethra, also called the penile urethra, runs through the penis inside the corpus spongiosum, which is how it gets its name. It is the longest of the three regions and ends at the external urethral orifice on the glans. The bulbourethral glands release their mucus into it.","notes":["Correct. The spongy urethra runs inside the corpus spongiosum.","The ejaculatory duct is not part of the urethra; it is the short duct that empties into the prostatic urethra. It would be the answer if the question asked which duct the ductus deferens and seminal vesicle duct form.","The membranous urethra is the short middle region crossing the floor of the pelvis, between the prostate and the penis. Choose it if the question asked which region lies beside the bulbourethral glands.","The prostatic urethra is the first region, running through the prostate just below the bladder. Pick it if the question asked where the ejaculatory ducts empty."],"lt":"t-reproductive-male","lid":"c25-x2","comp":139,"competencyId":"w6-urine-path"},{"id":"L02564","dok":3,"q":"In cryptorchidism, an undescended testis stays at core body temperature. Which cells are at risk if it is not corrected?","a":"The sperm-forming cells of the testis.","options":["The erectile tissue of the penis","The mucus glands beside the urethra","The sperm-forming cells of the testis","The fluid-secreting cells of the prostate"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Sperm production needs a temperature a few degrees below the body core, which is why the testes normally hang in the scrotum. An undescended testis stays inside at core body temperature, and if the problem is not corrected, the warmth can damage the cells that form sperm. The problem is local to the testis that did not descend.","notes":["The erectile tissue of the penis is not affected by where the testis sits. Choose it if the question asked what fills with blood during an erection.","The bulbourethral glands beside the urethra are pelvic glands that normally sit at body temperature. They would be the answer if the question asked which glands secrete alkaline mucus.","Correct. The warmth threatens the sperm-forming cells in the undescended testis.","The prostate is deep in the pelvis at body temperature already and works normally there. Pick it if the question asked which gland is enlarged in benign prostatic hyperplasia."],"lt":"t-reproductive-male","lid":"c26-x2","comp":145,"competencyId":"w6-male-disorders"},{"id":"L02565","dok":3,"q":"An older man's enlarged prostate makes urination difficult. Which tube is the prostate squeezing?","a":"The urethra.","options":["The ureter","The ductus deferens","The urethra","The ejaculatory duct"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The male urethra passes straight through the prostate just below the bladder. When the prostate enlarges, it presses inward on this part of the urethra and narrows it, so urine has trouble flowing out. That is how a reproductive gland causes a urinary symptom.","notes":["The ureters carry urine from the kidneys and enter the back of the bladder above the prostate, so the prostate does not surround them. Pick ureter if the question asked which tubes drain the kidneys.","The ductus deferens carries sperm, not urine, and it ends behind the bladder before reaching the prostate. Choose it if the question asked which duct is cut in a vasectomy.","Correct. The enlarged prostate squeezes the urethra that runs through it.","The ejaculatory ducts do pass through the prostate, but they carry sperm and seminal fluid, not urine, so squeezing them would not make urination difficult. They would be the answer if the question asked which ducts empty into the prostatic urethra."],"lt":"t-reproductive-male","lid":"c27-x2","comp":145,"competencyId":"w6-male-disorders"},{"id":"L02566","dok":2,"q":"Which disorder is an abnormal swelling of the veins that drain the testis?","a":"Varicocele.","options":["Inguinal hernia","Hydrocele","Varicocele","Cryptorchidism"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"A varicocele is an abnormal swelling of the veins that drain the testis, the pampiniform plexus in the spermatic cord. The name links varic-, as in varicose veins, with -cele, meaning swelling. It is the vein disorder of the scrotum, while a hydrocele is the fluid disorder.","notes":["An inguinal hernia is a loop of intestine pushing through the inguinal canal. It would be the answer if the question described intestine bulging in the groin.","A hydrocele is a collection of serous fluid in the tunica vaginalis around the testis, not a vein problem. Pick it if the question described a fluid-filled swelling.","Correct. A varicocele is swelling of the veins that drain the testis.","Cryptorchidism is the failure of a testis to descend into the scrotum. Choose it if the question described a testis that stayed in the abdomen."],"lt":"t-reproductive-male","lid":"c28-x2","comp":145,"competencyId":"w6-male-disorders"},{"id":"L02567","dok":3,"q":"A man's prostate is enlarged and squeezing his urethra, but a biopsy shows no cancer. What is this condition called?","a":"Benign prostatic hyperplasia.","options":["Varicocele","Benign prostatic hyperplasia","Prostate cancer","Hydrocele"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Benign prostatic hyperplasia (BPH) is a non-cancerous enlargement of the prostate. Benign means not cancer, and hyperplasia means growth from an increase in the number of cells. Because the prostate surrounds the urethra, the enlarged gland squeezes it and makes urination difficult.","notes":["A varicocele is a swelling of the veins that drain the testis, nowhere near the urethra. Choose it if the question described enlarged veins in the scrotum.","Correct. A non-cancerous enlargement of the prostate is benign prostatic hyperplasia.","Prostate cancer is a malignant tumor of the prostate. The biopsy showed no cancer, so this does not fit, but pick it if the question described a malignant growth of the prostate.","A hydrocele is serous fluid collecting in the tunica vaginalis around a testis. It would be the answer if the question described a fluid-filled swelling in the scrotum."],"lt":"t-reproductive-male","lid":"c29-x2","comp":145,"competencyId":"w6-male-disorders"},{"id":"L02568","dok":2,"q":"Serous fluid collects in the tunica vaginalis around one testis. Which disorder is this?","a":"Hydrocele.","options":["Inguinal hernia","Hydrocele","Varicocele","Cryptorchidism"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"A hydrocele is a collection of serous fluid in the tunica vaginalis, the serous membrane around the testis. The name combines hydro-, meaning water, with -cele, meaning swelling. Normally only a thin film of fluid sits between the two layers of the tunica vaginalis, so extra fluid makes the scrotum swell.","notes":["An inguinal hernia is a loop of intestine pushing through the inguinal canal. It would be the answer if the question described intestine bulging in the groin.","Correct. Fluid in the tunica vaginalis is a hydrocele.","A varicocele is a swelling of the veins that drain the testis, not a collection of fluid. Pick it if the question described enlarged veins in the scrotum.","Cryptorchidism is a testis that never descended into the scrotum. Choose it if the question described a testis that stayed in the abdomen."],"lt":"t-reproductive-male","lid":"c30-x2","comp":145,"competencyId":"w6-male-disorders"},{"id":"L02569","dok":3,"q":"The testes hang in the scrotum, outside the main body cavity. Why is this outside position important?","a":"Sperm production needs a temperature below core body temperature.","options":["The scrotum shields the testes better than the abdomen does","Sperm production needs a temperature above core body temperature","The testes are too large to fit inside the pelvic cavity","Sperm production needs a temperature below core body temperature"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Sperm form properly only at a temperature a few degrees below the body core of 37°C (98.6°F). Hanging the testes in the scrotum keeps them cooler than they would be in the abdomen. The cremaster and dartos muscles fine-tune that temperature by moving the testes and tightening the scrotal skin.","notes":["The scrotum actually offers less protection than the bony pelvis or muscular abdomen, which is why the testes are vulnerable to injury. The outside position is a trade-off made for temperature, not safety.","This reverses the requirement. Sperm need cooler conditions, not warmer ones, which is why an undescended testis at body temperature can have its sperm-forming cells damaged.","Size is not the reason; the testes would fit in the pelvis, and organs of similar size sit there. This is a guess at a cause, while the real reason for the outside position is temperature.","Correct. The scrotum keeps the testes cooler than the core, which sperm production needs."],"lt":"t-reproductive-male","lid":"d3-t-reproductive-male-1","comp":141,"competencyId":"w6-scrotum-testes"},{"id":"L02570","dok":3,"q":"The seminiferous tubules are very long and tightly coiled inside each testis. Why is this coiled design useful?","a":"Coiling packs a long sperm-making tube into a small space.","options":["Coiling keeps the tubules warmer than the rest of the body","Coiling packs a long sperm-making tube into a small space","Coiling lets the tubules secrete most of the fluid in semen","Coiling stores sperm until they gain the ability to swim"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The testes produce huge numbers of sperm, and that takes a great deal of sperm-forming tissue. Coiling very long tubules tightly inside the lobules fits an enormous total length of tubule, and the lining that makes sperm, into a small organ. This is the same idea as the folds and coils seen in other organs that need a lot of working surface in a small space.","notes":["Sperm production needs cooler conditions, not warmer ones, so making the tubules warmer would harm them. Students who mix up the temperature rule tend to choose this.","Correct. Coiling fits a great length of sperm-producing tubule into the small testis.","Most of the fluid in semen comes from the seminal vesicles, not the seminiferous tubules. This would fit a question about the seminal vesicles, which supply the largest share of semen.","Storing sperm until they can swim is the job of the epididymis, the coiled duct on the back of the testis. Pick this if the question asked why the epididymis is needed."],"lt":"t-reproductive-male","lid":"d3-t-reproductive-male-2","comp":141,"competencyId":"w6-scrotum-testes"},{"id":"L02571","dok":3,"q":"Sperm collected directly from the seminiferous tubules are fully formed but cannot swim. Which step have they not yet gone through?","a":"Maturing in the epididymis.","options":["Maturing in the epididymis","Passing through the ejaculatory duct","Receiving fructose from the seminal vesicles","Mixing with prostate fluid"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Sperm leave the seminiferous tubules with all their parts, but they gain the ability to move only after time in the epididymis. The epididymis stores them while they mature, so a reserve of motile sperm is ready at ejaculation. Sperm taken straight from the tubules have skipped this step.","notes":["Correct. Sperm gain motility while maturing in the epididymis.","The ejaculatory duct is a passageway near the end of the route; passing through it does not give sperm the ability to swim. Choose it if the question asked where seminal vesicle fluid first joins the sperm.","Fructose fuels swimming, but sperm must first be able to swim, which they gain in the epididymis before they ever meet seminal vesicle fluid. This would fit a question about where sperm get their energy.","Prostate fluid helps sperm stay active, but it is added later in the prostatic urethra, after sperm can already move. Pick this if the question asked what the prostate contributes."],"lt":"t-reproductive-male","lid":"d3-t-reproductive-male-3","comp":142,"competencyId":"w6-sperm-path"},{"id":"L02572","dok":2,"q":"Seminal vesicle fluid is rich in fructose. What does the fructose do for sperm?","a":"It gives sperm the energy to swim.","options":["It neutralizes acidic urine in the urethra","It forms the blood-testis barrier","It gives sperm the energy to swim","It helps the sperm penetrate the oocyte"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Fructose is a sugar, and sperm use it as fuel for the whipping movement of the tail. The seminal vesicles supply this fuel in an alkaline fluid that also gives the sperm a liquid to swim in. This nourishment helps the sperm keep swimming on the long trip toward the oocyte.","notes":["Neutralizing acidic urine in the urethra is the job of the alkaline mucus from the bulbourethral glands. Pick this if the question asked what bulbourethral mucus does.","The blood-testis barrier is formed by tight connections between Sertoli cells in the seminiferous tubules, not by any gland fluid. Choose this if the question asked what the sustentacular cells do.","Correct. Fructose is the fuel sperm use to swim.","Getting through the coverings of the oocyte depends on enzymes in the acrosome, the cap on the sperm head. This would fit a question about the acrosome."],"lt":"t-reproductive-male","lid":"d3-t-reproductive-male-4","comp":144,"competencyId":"w6-male-glands-penis"},{"id":"L02574","dok":3,"q":"A mature sperm has almost no cytoplasm and few parts besides a nucleus, mitochondria, and a tail. Which statement best explains how this design fits its job?","a":"A light, streamlined cell swims fast on energy from its mitochondria.","options":["Removing cytoplasm lets the sperm divide again in the female tract","The acrosome replaces the mitochondria as the energy source","The tail carries the chromosomes, which keeps the head small","A light, streamlined cell swims fast on energy from its mitochondria"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"A sperm's job is to deliver its chromosomes to the oocyte, which means swimming a long way. Shedding most of its cytoplasm keeps the cell small and light, the tail provides the swimming motion, and the mitochondria packed into the middle piece supply the energy. Every remaining part serves that trip.","notes":["Sperm do not divide after they are formed; their job ends with fertilization. This statement treats the sperm like a body cell that can keep dividing, which is a misconception.","The acrosome holds enzymes for getting into the oocyte, not energy. Energy comes from the mitochondria of the middle piece, so pick the acrosome only if the question asked about penetrating the egg.","The chromosomes are in the nucleus in the head, not the tail. This mixes up the part that carries genetic material with the part that moves the cell.","Correct. A small, light cell with mitochondria powering its tail is built for fast travel."],"lt":"t-reproductive-male","lid":"d3-t-reproductive-male-6","comp":143,"competencyId":"w6-sperm-cell"},{"id":"L02575","dok":3,"q":"On a cold day the testes are pulled up closer to the body, and on a warm day they hang lower. Why is this adjustment useful?","a":"It keeps the testes near the cooler temperature sperm need.","options":["It keeps the testes at full core body temperature","It protects the testes from injury during movement","It keeps the testes near the cooler temperature sperm need","It moves sperm from the testes into the epididymis"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Sperm form best a few degrees below the body core, so the testes must stay slightly cool but not too cold. In the cold, the cremaster muscle pulls them toward the warm body, and in warm weather it relaxes so they hang lower and lose heat. The dartos helps by tightening or loosening the scrotal skin.","notes":["Holding the testes at full core temperature would harm sperm production, which is the problem in an undescended testis. The adjustment aims for slightly below core temperature.","The scrotum does little to shield the testes from injury, and the warm and cold pattern in the question tracks temperature, not movement. This option guesses at a purpose the question does not describe.","Correct. Raising and lowering the testes keeps them near the cooler temperature sperm need.","Sperm move from the testis into the epididymis through ducts, not by raising or lowering the testes. This would only fit a question about the route sperm take after they form."],"lt":"t-reproductive-male","lid":"d3x-t-reproductive-male-1","comp":141,"competencyId":"w6-scrotum-testes"},{"id":"L02576","dok":2,"q":"In benign prostatic hyperplasia, which region of the urethra is narrowed?","a":"The prostatic urethra.","options":["The prostatic urethra","The spongy urethra","The external urethral orifice","The membranous urethra"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The prostatic urethra is the first region of the male urethra, running straight through the prostate just below the bladder. When the prostate enlarges in benign prostatic hyperplasia, it presses inward on this region and narrows it. That is why the problem shows up as a weak or slow urine stream.","notes":["Correct. The prostatic urethra runs through the prostate, so it is the region narrowed in BPH.","The spongy urethra runs through the penis inside the corpus spongiosum, far from the prostate. Choose it if the question asked which region is the longest.","The external urethral orifice is the opening at the tip of the glans. It would be the answer if the question asked where urine and semen leave the body.","The membranous urethra is the short region below the prostate crossing the floor of the pelvis, outside the gland. Pick it if the question asked which region the bulbourethral glands sit beside."],"lt":"t-reproductive-male","lid":"d3x-t-reproductive-male-2","comp":145,"competencyId":"w6-male-disorders"},{"id":"L04446","dok":1,"q":"Which organs are the male gonads, the organs that produce sperm and secrete hormones?","a":"The testes.","options":["The epididymides","The seminal vesicles","The testes","The prostate and bulbourethral glands"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The testes are the male gonads, the primary reproductive organs. Inside each testis, the seminiferous tubules produce sperm, and the interstitial cells between those tubules produce testosterone. Every other male reproductive structure either stores and carries the sperm or adds fluid to it.","notes":["Each epididymis is a coiled duct on the back of a testis where sperm are stored and gain the ability to move. It does not make sperm; pick it if the question asked where sperm mature after they form.","The seminal vesicles are accessory glands that add an alkaline, fructose-rich fluid to semen. Choose them if the question asked which gland makes the largest share of the semen volume.","Correct. The testes produce sperm and secrete hormones such as testosterone.","These are accessory glands that add fluid to semen, not gonads. Pick them if the question asked which glands add milky fluid and lubricating mucus to the sperm."],"lt":"t-w6-scrotum-testes","lid":"w6-scrotum-testes-r1","comp":141,"competencyId":"w6-scrotum-testes"},{"id":"L04447","dok":1,"q":"Which white fibrous capsule surrounds each testis and divides it into lobules?","a":"The tunica albuginea.","options":["The scrotal septum","The tunica albuginea","The dartos muscle","The tunica vaginalis"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The tunica albuginea is a tough capsule of dense connective tissue wrapped tightly around each testis, and its name means white coat. Extensions of this capsule run inward and divide the testis into a few hundred lobules. Each lobule holds coiled seminiferous tubules, where sperm are produced.","notes":["The scrotal septum divides the scrotum into two compartments, one for each testis. It separates the two testes rather than dividing a single testis; pick it if the question asked what splits the scrotal sac down the middle.","Correct. The tunica albuginea is the white capsule whose extensions divide the testis into lobules.","The dartos is the layer of smooth muscle in the scrotal skin that wrinkles the skin to reduce heat loss. It belongs to the scrotum, not the testis capsule; choose it if the question asked which muscle wrinkles the scrotum.","The tunica vaginalis is a serous membrane, derived from the peritoneum, that partly covers the testis outside the tunica albuginea. Pick it if the question asked where fluid collects in a hydrocele."],"lt":"t-w6-scrotum-testes","lid":"w6-scrotum-testes-r2","comp":141,"competencyId":"w6-scrotum-testes"},{"id":"L04448","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares the sustentacular (Sertoli) cells and the interstitial (Leydig) cells of the testis?","a":"Sertoli cells line the seminiferous tubules and nourish developing sperm; Leydig cells lie between the tubules and make testosterone.","options":["Sertoli cells line the tubules and nourish sperm; Leydig cells lie between the tubules and make testosterone","Both cell types line the tubules, and both develop directly into mature sperm cells","Sertoli cells lie between the tubules and make testosterone; Leydig cells line the tubules and nourish sperm","Sertoli cells line the epididymis and store sperm; Leydig cells line the ductus deferens"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Sustentacular means supporting, and the Sertoli cells sit inside the walls of the seminiferous tubules, where they nourish developing sperm and form the blood-testis barrier. Interstitial means in between, and the Leydig cells sit in the connective tissue between the tubules, where they produce testosterone. The names describe where each cell lives.","notes":["Correct. Sertoli cells support sperm inside the tubules, and Leydig cells make testosterone between them.","Only the Sertoli cells line the tubules, and neither cell type turns into sperm. Sperm develop from separate germ cells in the tubule wall, which the Sertoli cells surround and support.","This swaps the two cell types. Interstitial means in between, so the Leydig cells are the ones between the tubules; students choose this when they mix up the two people's names.","Both cell types are found in the testis, not in the ducts. Sperm storage happens in the epididymis, which would be the answer if the question asked where sperm wait and mature after leaving the testis."],"lt":"t-w6-scrotum-testes","lid":"w6-scrotum-testes-r4","comp":141,"competencyId":"w6-scrotum-testes"},{"id":"L04449","dok":3,"q":"Why do the testes sit in the scrotum, outside the abdominopelvic cavity?","a":"Sperm production needs a temperature a few degrees below body core temperature.","options":["Testosterone can only enter the blood from outside the body cavity","The scrotum shields the testes better than the bony pelvis could","Sperm production needs a temperature a few degrees below the body core","Sperm production needs a temperature a few degrees above the body core"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Sperm develop best at a temperature a few degrees below the body core temperature of 37°C (98.6°F). Hanging the testes in the scrotum keeps them cooler, and two muscles fine-tune that temperature: the cremaster raises or lowers the testes, and the dartos wrinkles the scrotal skin. When a testis fails to descend (cryptorchidism), the extra warmth can damage its sperm-forming cells if it is not corrected.","notes":["Testosterone enters the blood just as well from inside the body, as shown by the ovaries, which make hormones while sitting in the pelvic cavity. The reason for the scrotal position is temperature, not hormone release.","The scrotum actually leaves the testes more exposed to injury than the bony pelvis would. This position is a trade-off accepted for temperature, not a form of protection.","Correct. The scrotum keeps the testes slightly cooler than the core, which sperm production requires.","This reverses the temperature. If warmth were needed, the testes would sit deep in the body; the cremaster pulls them closer to the body only when it is cold, to keep them from cooling too much."],"lt":"t-w6-scrotum-testes","lid":"w6-scrotum-testes-r5","comp":141,"competencyId":"w6-scrotum-testes"},{"id":"L04450","dok":1,"q":"In which structure are sperm stored while they mature and gain the ability to swim?","a":"The epididymis.","options":["The seminiferous tubules","The ductus deferens","The epididymis","The seminal vesicle"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The epididymis is a comma-shaped, tightly coiled duct on the back of each testis. Sperm leave the seminiferous tubules unable to swim, and they gain motility while they are stored in the epididymis. From there they move into the ductus deferens when it is time to leave.","notes":["The seminiferous tubules are where sperm are produced inside the testis. Sperm leave them before they can swim, so pick this if the question asked where sperm form.","The ductus deferens carries sperm from the epididymis up toward the urethra. It is the transport tube that comes next; choose it if the question asked which duct runs in the spermatic cord.","Correct. Sperm are stored in the epididymis and gain motility there.","The seminal vesicle is an accessory gland that adds fluid to semen, not a place where sperm mature. Pick it if the question asked which gland adds a fructose-rich fluid."],"lt":"t-w6-sperm-path","lid":"w6-sperm-path-r1","comp":142,"competencyId":"w6-sperm-path"},{"id":"L04451","dok":1,"q":"Which duct carries sperm from the epididymis up out of the scrotum toward the urethra?","a":"The ductus (vas) deferens.","options":["The ejaculatory duct","The spongy urethra","The spermatic cord","The ductus deferens"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The ductus deferens, also called the vas deferens, begins at the epididymis and runs up through the spermatic cord, through the inguinal canal, and into the pelvis. Behind the bladder it joins the duct of the seminal vesicle to form the ejaculatory duct. It is the long transport tube between storage in the epididymis and the urethra.","notes":["The ejaculatory duct is the short duct formed where the ductus deferens meets the seminal vesicle duct, and it empties into the prostatic urethra. It is one step later; pick it if the question asked which duct passes through the prostate.","The spongy urethra runs through the penis and is the last stretch of the path to the outside. Pick it if the question asked which part of the urethra lies within the corpus spongiosum.","The spermatic cord is a bundle, not a duct. It wraps the ductus deferens together with blood vessels, nerves, and the cremaster muscle, so choose it if the question asked what bundle passes through the inguinal canal.","Correct. The ductus deferens carries sperm from the epididymis toward the urethra."],"lt":"t-w6-sperm-path","lid":"w6-sperm-path-r2","comp":142,"competencyId":"w6-sperm-path"},{"id":"L04452","dok":1,"q":"Which structure is a bundle containing the ductus deferens, blood vessels, nerves, and the cremaster muscle?","a":"The spermatic cord.","options":["The epididymis","The spermatic cord","The corpus spongiosum","The tunica vaginalis"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The spermatic cord is a bundle that runs from the scrotum up through the inguinal canal. It holds the ductus deferens together with the testicular artery and veins, nerves, and lymphatic vessels, all wrapped in layers that include the cremaster muscle. It is the lifeline that connects each testis to the body.","notes":["The epididymis is a single coiled duct on the testis where sperm are stored. It is not a bundle of several structures; pick it if the question asked where sperm gain motility.","Correct. The spermatic cord bundles the ductus deferens with vessels, nerves, and the cremaster muscle.","The corpus spongiosum is the single column of erectile tissue in the penis that surrounds the spongy urethra. Pick it if the question asked which erectile column holds the urethra.","The tunica vaginalis is a serous membrane that partly covers the testis. It surrounds the testis rather than bundling vessels and ducts; choose it if the question asked which membrane fills with fluid in a hydrocele."],"lt":"t-w6-sperm-path","lid":"w6-sperm-path-r3","comp":142,"competencyId":"w6-sperm-path"},{"id":"L04453","dok":2,"q":"How is the ejaculatory duct formed, and where does it empty?","a":"The ductus deferens joins the seminal vesicle duct; it empties into the prostatic urethra.","options":["The seminal vesicle joins the bulbourethral duct; it empties into the urinary bladder","The epididymis joins the ductus deferens inside the testis; it empties into the membranous urethra","The ductus deferens joins a prostatic duct; it empties into the spongy urethra of the penis","The ductus deferens joins the seminal vesicle duct; it empties into the prostatic urethra"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Behind the bladder, each ductus deferens merges with the duct of a seminal vesicle to form a short ejaculatory duct. The two ejaculatory ducts then pass into the prostate and empty into the prostatic urethra, the first part of the urethra. From there sperm travel through the membranous and spongy urethra to the outside.","notes":["The bulbourethral glands sit below the prostate and drain into the spongy urethra, and nothing in the duct system empties into the bladder. Pick the bulbourethral glands if the question asked which glands release lubricating mucus into the urethra.","The epididymis connects to the ductus deferens back at the testis, not where the ejaculatory duct forms. The membranous urethra is the short segment after the prostate, so the location is also one step too far.","The prostate is not one of the ducts that forms the ejaculatory duct; the ejaculatory duct runs through the prostate. The spongy urethra is in the penis, too far along the path.","Correct. The ductus deferens and seminal vesicle duct merge, and the ejaculatory duct empties into the prostatic urethra."],"lt":"t-w6-sperm-path","lid":"w6-sperm-path-r4","comp":142,"competencyId":"w6-sperm-path"},{"id":"L04454","dok":3,"q":"Which list traces sperm from where they are made to the outside of the body?","a":"Seminiferous tubules, epididymis, ductus deferens, ejaculatory duct, urethra.","options":["Seminiferous tubules, ductus deferens, epididymis, ejaculatory duct, urethra","Epididymis, seminiferous tubules, ductus deferens, ejaculatory duct, urethra","Seminiferous tubules, epididymis, ductus deferens, ejaculatory duct, urethra","Seminiferous tubules, epididymis, ejaculatory duct, ductus deferens, urethra"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Sperm are made only in the seminiferous tubules of the testis. They move into the epididymis to be stored and gain motility, then travel up the ductus deferens through the spermatic cord. The ductus deferens joins the seminal vesicle duct to form the ejaculatory duct, which empties into the urethra for the final trip out.","notes":["This swaps the epididymis and the ductus deferens. Sperm reach the epididymis first, directly from the testis, and only then enter the ductus deferens.","This starts with the epididymis, but the epididymis stores sperm rather than making them. Only the seminiferous tubules produce sperm, so they must be first.","Correct. Sperm are made, stored, carried up, joined with seminal fluid, and released through the urethra.","This puts the ejaculatory duct before the ductus deferens, but the ejaculatory duct is formed from the end of the ductus deferens. It must come after the long ductus, just before the urethra."],"lt":"t-w6-sperm-path","lid":"w6-sperm-path-r5","comp":142,"competencyId":"w6-sperm-path"},{"id":"L04480","dok":1,"q":"Which list names the three accessory glands of the male reproductive system?","a":"The seminal vesicles, the prostate, and the bulbourethral glands.","options":["Seminal vesicles, epididymis, prostate","Seminal vesicles, prostate, bulbourethral glands","Prostate, bulbourethral glands, greater vestibular glands","Prostate, testes, bulbourethral glands"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The male accessory glands add fluid to sperm to form semen. The paired seminal vesicles sit behind the bladder, the single prostate surrounds the first part of the urethra, and the paired bulbourethral glands lie just below the prostate. None of them make sperm; they make the fluid that carries and protects it.","notes":["The epididymis is a duct where sperm are stored and gain motility, not a gland that adds fluid. Pick it if the question asked which part of the duct system sits on the testis.","Correct. These three glands add fluid to the sperm to form semen.","The greater vestibular glands are female glands beside the vaginal opening that secrete mucus. Pick them if the question asked which female glands secrete lubricating mucus.","The testes are the gonads that make sperm, not accessory glands. Choose them if the question asked which organs produce sperm and testosterone."],"lt":"t-w6-male-glands-penis","lid":"w6-male-glands-penis-r1","comp":144,"competencyId":"w6-male-glands-penis"},{"id":"L04481","dok":2,"q":"Which accessory gland adds an alkaline, fructose-rich fluid that makes up the largest share of semen?","a":"The seminal vesicles.","options":["The seminal vesicles","The prostate","The epididymis","The bulbourethral glands"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The seminal vesicles produce an alkaline fluid rich in fructose, a sugar the sperm use for energy, and this fluid makes up about 60 percent of semen. The prostate adds a milky fluid that helps sperm stay active. The bulbourethral glands add an alkaline mucus that neutralizes traces of urine in the urethra and lubricates it.","notes":["Correct. The seminal vesicles add the alkaline, fructose-rich fluid that forms most of the semen.","The prostate adds a milky fluid that helps sperm stay active. Pick it if the question asked which gland surrounds the urethra and adds a milky fluid.","The epididymis is a duct that stores sperm, not an accessory gland. Pick it if the question asked where sperm gain the ability to move.","The bulbourethral glands add a small amount of alkaline mucus that neutralizes urine in the urethra and lubricates it. Choose them if the question asked which glands clear the urethra before ejaculation."],"lt":"t-w6-male-glands-penis","lid":"w6-male-glands-penis-r2","comp":144,"competencyId":"w6-male-glands-penis"},{"id":"L04482","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly describes the columns of erectile tissue in the penis?","a":"Two dorsal corpora cavernosa and one ventral corpus spongiosum that surrounds the urethra.","options":["One dorsal corpus spongiosum and two ventral corpora cavernosa around the urethra","Two dorsal corpora spongiosa and one ventral corpus cavernosum around the urethra","Three corpora cavernosa side by side, with the urethra running between them","Two dorsal corpora cavernosa and one ventral corpus spongiosum around the urethra"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The penis has three columns of erectile tissue. The paired corpora cavernosa run along the dorsal side, and the single corpus spongiosum runs along the ventral side and surrounds the spongy urethra. The corpus spongiosum expands at the tip to form the glans penis.","notes":["This flips the columns top to bottom. The single column is ventral, and it is the one that holds the urethra.","This gets the positions right but swaps the names. The urethra runs through the spongiosum, which is how the spongy urethra gets its name.","There are only two corpora cavernosa, and the urethra does not run between columns. It runs inside the single corpus spongiosum.","Correct. Two dorsal corpora cavernosa sit above one ventral corpus spongiosum, which holds the urethra."],"lt":"t-w6-male-glands-penis","lid":"w6-male-glands-penis-r3","comp":144,"competencyId":"w6-male-glands-penis"},{"id":"L04494","dok":1,"q":"Why can an enlarged prostate make urination difficult?","a":"The urethra passes straight through the prostate, so an enlarged prostate squeezes it.","options":["The urethra runs through the prostate, which squeezes it as it grows","The prostate presses on both ureters, blocking urine from the kidneys","The prostate presses on the external urethral sphincter and paralyzes it","The prostate grows into the bladder and blocks the detrusor muscle"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The prostate is a gland that wraps around the first part of the male urethra just below the bladder, a segment called the prostatic urethra. When the prostate enlarges, as in benign prostatic hyperplasia (a non-cancerous overgrowth), it squeezes the urethra running through it. The urine stream weakens and the bladder may not empty fully, which is how a reproductive organ causes a urinary symptom.","notes":["Correct. The urethra runs through the prostate, so prostate enlargement squeezes it.","The ureters enter the bladder at the back of the trigone, above the prostate, so an enlarged prostate does not usually squeeze them. Pick the ureters if the question asked which tubes carry urine from the kidneys to the bladder.","The external urethral sphincter is a ring of skeletal muscle below the prostate that gives voluntary control. Prostate enlargement narrows the urethra itself rather than paralyzing this muscle; pick the external sphincter if the question asked which muscle gives conscious control over urination.","The prostate sits below the bladder and surrounds the urethra; the detrusor in the bladder wall is still able to contract. The problem is a narrowed outlet, not a blocked muscle."],"lt":"t-w6-male-disorders","lid":"w6-male-disorders-r1","comp":145,"competencyId":"w6-male-disorders"},{"id":"N-a17-143-1","dok":1,"q":"Which part of a sperm cell holds the centrioles?","a":"The neck.","options":["The middle piece","The neck","The acrosome","The end piece"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The neck is the short, narrow part just behind the head. It holds the centrioles, and the core of microtubules that runs the length of the tail starts here.","notes":["The middle piece is packed with mitochondria that make ATP to power the tail. It comes just after the neck.","Correct. The neck, just behind the head, holds the centrioles.","The acrosome is the cap over the front of the head. It holds enzymes that help the sperm get through the layers around the oocyte.","The end piece is the short, thin tip at the very end of the tail, the opposite end from the centrioles."],"comp":143,"competencyId":"w6-sperm-cell"},{"id":"N-a17-143-2","dok":2,"q":"Which list gives the parts of the sperm tail in order, from the head to the tip?","a":"Neck, middle piece, principal piece, end piece.","options":["Middle piece, neck, principal piece, end piece","Neck, middle piece, principal piece, end piece","Neck, principal piece, middle piece, end piece","Neck, middle piece, end piece, principal piece"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The tail is a long flagellum with four parts. The short neck sits just behind the head, then comes the middle piece packed with mitochondria, then the long principal piece, and finally the thin end piece at the tip.","notes":["The neck comes first, right behind the head. The middle piece follows it.","Correct. From the head back, the tail runs neck, middle piece, principal piece, end piece.","The middle piece comes before the principal piece. The mitochondria sit in the first long stretch of the tail, right after the neck.","The end piece is the very tip, so it comes last. The principal piece, the longest part, comes before it."],"comp":143,"competencyId":"w6-sperm-cell"},{"id":"N-a17-143-3","dok":3,"q":"A sperm cell has a normal head and a normal middle piece, but it cannot drive itself forward. Which part is most likely defective?","a":"The principal piece.","options":["The principal piece","The acrosome","The nucleus","The end piece"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The principal piece is the longest part of the tail and does most of the whipping that moves the cell forward. The middle piece makes the ATP that powers the tail, and here it is normal. So a sperm that cannot swim forward points to a problem in the principal piece.","notes":["Correct. The principal piece does most of the whipping that moves the sperm forward.","The acrosome is the enzyme cap over the head. It helps the sperm get through the layers around the oocyte, not swim.","The nucleus fills most of the head and holds the 23 chromosomes. The head is normal here, and the nucleus does not move the cell.","The end piece is only the short, thin tip of the tail. The principal piece, the longest part, does most of the whipping."],"comp":143,"competencyId":"w6-sperm-cell"},{"id":"N-a17d-143-1","dok":2,"q":"Which pairing of a sperm cell part with what it holds is correct?","a":"Neck: the centrioles.","options":["Middle piece: the enzymes","End piece: the mitochondria","Acrosome: the chromosomes","Neck: the centrioles"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Each part of a sperm cell holds something specific. The head holds the nucleus, capped by the enzyme-filled acrosome. The neck holds the centrioles, the middle piece is packed with mitochondria, and the principal and end pieces make up the rest of the tail.","notes":["The middle piece holds mitochondria that make ATP for the tail. The enzymes that digest a path to the oocyte are in the acrosome.","The end piece is the short, thin tip of the tail. The mitochondria wind in a spiral around the middle piece, the only part packed with them.","The 23 chromosomes are in the nucleus, which fills most of the head. The acrosome is the cap over the front of the nucleus and holds enzymes.","Correct. The neck, just behind the head, holds the centrioles, and the core of the tail starts there."],"comp":143,"competencyId":"w6-sperm-cell"},{"id":"N-a17d-143-2","dok":2,"q":"On a sperm smear, which part is the dark, densely stained oval at the front, and what makes up most of it?","a":"The head, which is mostly the nucleus.","options":["The middle piece, which is mostly mitochondria","The head, which is mostly the nucleus","The neck, which is mostly centrioles","The acrosome, which is mostly mitochondria"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"On a sperm smear, the head stains as a dark, dense oval and the tail is the long thin line behind it. The head is a flattened oval made mostly of the nucleus, which holds 23 chromosomes, with almost no cytoplasm. The acrosome forms a cap over the front of that nucleus.","notes":["The middle piece is packed with mitochondria, but it is part of the tail, behind the neck. The dark oval at the front is the head.","Correct. The head is the dark oval, and most of it is the nucleus with almost no cytoplasm.","The neck is the short, narrow part just behind the head that holds the centrioles. It is too small to be the dark oval at the front.","The acrosome is the cap over the front of the head, and it holds enzymes, not mitochondria. Most of the dark oval is the nucleus."],"comp":143,"competencyId":"w6-sperm-cell"},{"id":"N-a17d-143-3","dok":3,"q":"A sperm cell swims normally, but the cap over the front of its nucleus never formed. Which part is missing, and what would this sperm be unable to do?","a":"The acrosome; it could not get through to the oocyte.","options":["The middle piece; it could not make ATP to swim","The nucleus; it could not deliver its 23 chromosomes","The acrosome; it could not get through to the oocyte","The neck; it could not start the core of the tail"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The acrosome is the cap that covers the front of the nucleus in the sperm head. It is a sac of enzymes that lets the sperm digest a path through the layers around the oocyte. Swimming depends on the tail and its mitochondria, so this sperm could reach the oocyte but could not get through to it.","notes":["The middle piece holds the mitochondria that power the tail, and this sperm swims normally. The missing cap over the nucleus is the acrosome.","The nucleus is what the cap covers, and it is still present. Losing the cap removes the enzymes, not the chromosomes.","Correct. The missing cap is the acrosome, the sac of enzymes that lets a sperm digest its way through the layers around the oocyte.","The neck is behind the head and holds the centrioles where the tail core starts. It is not a cap over the nucleus, and the tail here works fine."],"comp":143,"competencyId":"w6-sperm-cell"},{"id":"N-a17d-144-1","dok":3,"q":"In a cross section of the penis, you find a small opening surrounded by a single column of erectile tissue, with two larger columns side by side above it. What is the opening, and which surface of the penis is at the top of this view?","a":"The spongy urethra, with the dorsal surface at the top.","options":["The spongy urethra, with the ventral surface at the top","The spongy urethra, with the dorsal surface at the top","The prostatic urethra, with the dorsal surface at the top","The ejaculatory duct, with the ventral surface at the top"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Start a penis cross section by finding the urethra. The single column wrapped around it is the corpus spongiosum, which lies on the ventral side and surrounds the spongy urethra. The two larger columns side by side are the corpora cavernosa, which lie on the dorsal side, so the view has the dorsal surface at the top.","notes":["The opening is right, but the corpus spongiosum around it is ventral. The two corpora cavernosa above it are dorsal, so the top of this view is dorsal.","Correct. The column around the urethra is the corpus spongiosum on the ventral side, so the two corpora cavernosa above it mark the dorsal side.","The prostatic urethra runs through the prostate just below the bladder, not through the penis. The urethra inside the corpus spongiosum is the spongy urethra.","The ejaculatory ducts form where the seminal vesicle ducts join the ductus deferens, inside the pelvis. The opening ringed by erectile tissue in the penis is the spongy urethra."],"comp":144,"competencyId":"w6-male-glands-penis"},{"id":"N-a17d-144-2","dok":3,"q":"On a model, you trace the corpus spongiosum from the tip of the penis back to the perineum. What does it form at the tip, and what does it form in the root?","a":"The glans at the tip and the bulb of the penis in the root.","options":["The crura at the tip and the glans in the root","The prepuce at the tip and the bulb of the penis in the root","The glans at the tip and the crura in the root","The glans at the tip and the bulb of the penis in the root"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The corpus spongiosum is the single ventral column of erectile tissue that surrounds the spongy urethra. At the tip it expands to form the glans penis. At its back end in the root it forms the bulb of the penis, while the crura of the root belong to the two corpora cavernosa.","notes":["This puts the parts backward. The glans is the enlarged tip, and the crura are back in the root, where they belong to the corpora cavernosa.","The prepuce is the loose fold of skin over the glans, not erectile tissue. The corpus spongiosum itself expands into the glans.","The glans is right, but the crura are the back ends of the two corpora cavernosa, attached to the ischial and pubic rami. The corpus spongiosum ends in the bulb.","Correct. The corpus spongiosum expands to form the glans at the tip, and its back end is the bulb of the penis in the root."],"comp":144,"competencyId":"w6-male-glands-penis"},{"id":"N-a17d-145-1","dok":1,"q":"In a varicocele, which network of veins swells and twists?","a":"The pampiniform plexus.","options":["The pampiniform plexus","The seminiferous tubules","The tunica vaginalis","The inguinal canal"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The veins of the testis run up the spermatic cord as a network called the pampiniform plexus. In a varicocele these veins swell and twist, above the testis. Varicoceles are more common on the left, where the testicular vein drains into the left renal vein.","notes":["Correct. A varicocele is swelling and twisting of the veins of the pampiniform plexus in the spermatic cord.","The seminiferous tubules are the sperm-forming tubules inside the testis, where testicular cancer usually begins. They are not veins.","The tunica vaginalis is the two-layered covering around the testis. Fluid collecting between its layers is a hydrocele, not a varicocele.","The inguinal canal is the passage in the abdominal wall that the testis descends through. A loop of intestine pushing into it is an inguinal hernia."],"comp":145,"competencyId":"w6-male-disorders"},{"id":"N-a17d-145-2","dok":1,"q":"In which part of the prostate does prostate cancer most often start?","a":"The outer, posterior part, against the rectum.","options":["The front part, farthest from the rectum","The tissue right around the urethra","The outer, posterior part, against the rectum","The upper part, where it touches the bladder"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The prostate sits just below the bladder and wraps around the prostatic urethra, with its back surface against the rectum. Prostate cancer most often starts in the outer, posterior part of the gland. Because that part lies against the rectum, a clinician can feel it through the rectal wall.","notes":["Cancer most often starts in the posterior part of the gland. That is why a clinician can feel it through the rectal wall.","The tissue around the urethra is where benign prostatic hyperplasia most often happens. That growth squeezes the urethra, while cancer usually starts farther back.","Correct. Prostate cancer most often starts in the outer, posterior part, which lies against the rectum.","The prostate does sit just below the bladder, but cancer most often starts in the posterior part of the gland, against the rectum, not up where it meets the bladder."],"comp":145,"competencyId":"w6-male-disorders"},{"id":"N-a17d-145-3","dok":1,"q":"During development, where do the testes first form?","a":"On the posterior abdominal wall, near the kidneys.","options":["On the posterior abdominal wall, near the kidneys","Low in the pelvis, beside the urinary bladder","Inside the inguinal canal of the abdominal wall","In the scrotum, where they stay throughout life"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The testes form high on the posterior abdominal wall, near the kidneys. That starting point is why each testicular artery branches from the abdominal aorta. Later in development each testis moves down through the inguinal canal into the scrotum, and if it stops along the way the condition is cryptorchidism.","notes":["Correct. The testes develop high on the posterior abdominal wall near the kidneys, then descend into the scrotum.","The testes form higher than the pelvis, near the kidneys, which is why their arteries branch from the abdominal aorta.","The inguinal canal is the passage the testis travels through on its way down. A testis found there has stopped partway, which is one form of cryptorchidism.","The scrotum is where the testes end up, not where they start. They must descend into it, which is why an undescended testis is possible."],"comp":145,"competencyId":"w6-male-disorders"}]},{"id":"m5-female-repro","title":"Female reproductive system","summary":"ovary and follicles, uterine tubes, uterus regions, wall and ligaments, vagina, vulva, mammary glands, pregnancy-related anatomy","mw":"2026-11-23","tr":"2026-11-24","cards":[{"id":"L02577","dok":2,"q":"Which list contains only organs of the female reproductive system?","a":"Ovaries, uterine tubes, uterus, and vagina.","options":["Ovaries, uterine tubes, uterus, vagina","Ovaries, uterine tubes, uterus, urinary bladder","Ovaries, ductus deferens, uterus, vagina","Ovaries, uterine tubes, rectum, vagina"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The female reproductive system is built from the ovaries (the gonads), the genital tract that carries the oocyte and receives sperm (uterine tubes, uterus, and vagina), the external genitalia (the vulva), and the mammary glands. Every organ in the correct list belongs to that system. Each of the other lists slips in one neighbor from the pelvis or from the male system.","notes":["Correct. All four are organs of the female reproductive tract, running from the gonad to the canal that opens to the outside.","The urinary bladder sits just anterior to the uterus, but it stores urine and belongs to the urinary system. It would fit a list of pelvic organs or urinary organs, not reproductive ones.","The ductus deferens is the male sperm duct that carries sperm from the epididymis toward the urethra. Pick it for a list of male reproductive ducts.","The rectum lies just posterior to the uterus and vagina, but it is the last part of the large intestine. It belongs on a list of digestive organs or pelvic organs."],"lt":"t-reproductive-female","lid":"fr1","comp":146,"competencyId":"w6-ovary"},{"id":"L02578","dok":2,"q":"Which order correctly lists the layers of the ovary from the surface inward?","a":"Germinal epithelium, tunica albuginea, cortex, medulla.","options":["Germinal epithelium, tunica albuginea, cortex, medulla","Germinal epithelium, cortex, tunica albuginea, medulla","Germinal epithelium, tunica albuginea, medulla, cortex","Tunica albuginea, germinal epithelium, cortex, medulla"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The ovary is read from the outside in. Its surface is covered by the germinal epithelium, a single layer of low cuboidal cells. Just beneath it is the tunica albuginea, a whitish capsule of dense connective tissue. Deep to the capsule is the cortex, which holds the follicles, and at the center is the medulla, the looser connective tissue that carries blood vessels and nerves.","notes":["Correct. Epithelium on the surface, capsule beneath it, follicles in the cortex, and vessels in the medulla.","This order places the cortex above the capsule. The tunica albuginea wraps the whole ovary just under the surface, so the follicle-filled cortex is always deep to it.","This order flips the two inner regions. The follicles sit in the cortex, which is outside the medulla, and the vessels run through the central medulla.","This order puts the capsule on the surface. The tunica albuginea lies just beneath the germinal epithelium, so the epithelium has to come first. Students who remember the testis capsule as the outer layer often make this swap."],"lt":"t-reproductive-female","lid":"fr2","comp":146,"competencyId":"w6-ovary"},{"id":"L02579","dok":2,"q":"The ovary is the female gonad. Which male organ is its homolog, the structure that develops from the same embryonic tissue?","a":"The testis.","options":["The prostate","The scrotum","The epididymis","The testis"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Homologous structures develop from the same embryonic tissue in males and females, even though they look different in the adult. The ovaries and the testes are both gonads, the primary reproductive organs that make the gametes (oocytes or sperm) and secrete sex hormones. That shared role and origin is why they are counted as homologs.","notes":["The prostate is a male accessory gland that surrounds the urethra below the bladder and adds fluid to semen. It would be the answer for a question about the gland that encircles the first part of the male urethra.","The scrotum is the skin pouch that holds the testes. Its female homolog is the labia majora, so pick the scrotum when the question starts from the labia majora.","The epididymis is the coiled duct on the back of the testis where sperm mature and are stored. It is part of the male duct system, so pick it if the question asked where sperm mature after leaving the testis.","Correct. The testis and the ovary are both gonads that produce gametes and hormones."],"lt":"t-reproductive-female","lid":"fr3","comp":149,"competencyId":"w6-vagina-vulva"},{"id":"L02580","dok":2,"q":"Which order lists the regions of the uterine tube from the end near the ovary to the end at the uterus?","a":"Infundibulum, ampulla, isthmus.","options":["Infundibulum, ampulla, isthmus","Ampulla, infundibulum, isthmus","Infundibulum, isthmus, ampulla","Isthmus, ampulla, infundibulum"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The uterine tube begins at the infundibulum, the funnel-shaped end that opens into the pelvic cavity beside the ovary and is fringed by the fimbriae. Next comes the ampulla, the widest and longest part, where fertilization usually happens. The tube ends at the isthmus, the narrow, thick-walled segment that joins the uterus.","notes":["Correct. Funnel near the ovary, wide middle where fertilization happens, then the narrow segment joining the uterus.","This order starts in the middle of the tube. The ampulla is the wide segment after the funnel, so the infundibulum has to come before it.","This order swaps the last two regions. The narrow isthmus is the segment that joins the uterus, so it comes last, after the wide ampulla.","This is the correct sequence run backward, from the uterus out to the ovary. It would be right if the question asked for the order from the uterus toward the ovary."],"lt":"t-reproductive-female","lid":"fr4","comp":147,"competencyId":"w6-ovum-path"},{"id":"L02581","dok":2,"q":"The cervical canal runs through the cervix. At which opening does the canal open upward into the uterine cavity?","a":"The internal os.","options":["The vaginal orifice","The external os","The vaginal fornix","The internal os"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The cervical canal is the passage through the cervix, the narrow lower part of the uterus. Its upper opening, the internal os, leads into the uterine cavity inside the body of the uterus. Its lower opening, the external os, leads into the vagina. Os means mouth or opening.","notes":["The vaginal orifice is the opening of the vagina into the vestibule of the vulva, at the very bottom of the tract. It would be the answer for where the vagina opens to the outside.","The external os is the lower opening of the cervical canal, where it opens into the vagina. Pick it if the question asked where the canal opens into the vagina.","The vaginal fornix is the recess of the vagina that wraps around the outside of the cervix. It is a space in the vagina, not an opening of the cervical canal. Pick it if the question asked for the vaginal recess surrounding the cervix.","Correct. The internal os is the upper end of the cervical canal, opening into the uterine cavity."],"lt":"t-reproductive-female","lid":"fr5","comp":148,"competencyId":"w6-uterus"},{"id":"L02582","dok":2,"q":"Which paired ligaments connect the uterus posteriorly to the sacrum?","a":"The uterosacral ligaments.","options":["The round ligaments","The broad ligaments","The cardinal ligaments","The uterosacral ligaments"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The uterosacral ligaments run from the cervix, the lower part of the uterus, back to the sacrum. They anchor the uterus from behind. Each uterine ligament is named for what it connects: the round ligaments reach the labia majora, the cardinal ligaments reach the lateral pelvic walls, and the broad ligaments are folds of peritoneum out to the lateral pelvic walls.","notes":["The round ligaments are fibrous cords that run from the uterus forward and down to the labia majora. Pick them if the question asked which ligament reaches the labia majora.","The broad ligaments are folds of peritoneum that drape over the uterus and attach it to the lateral pelvic walls. They would be the answer for the peritoneal fold on each side of the uterus.","The cardinal ligaments run from the lateral pelvic wall to the cervix and vagina. Pick them if the question asked which ligaments anchor the cervix to the side walls of the pelvis.","Correct. The name says it: uterus to sacrum."],"lt":"t-reproductive-female","lid":"fr6","comp":148,"competencyId":"w6-uterus"},{"id":"L02583","dok":2,"q":"Which pairing of a uterine wall layer with its tissue is correct?","a":"Myometrium and smooth muscle.","options":["Endometrium and serous membrane","Perimetrium and smooth muscle","Myometrium and serous membrane","Myometrium and smooth muscle"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The uterine wall has three layers. The perimetrium on the outside is a serous membrane, part of the visceral peritoneum. The myometrium in the middle is the thick layer of smooth muscle, and myo means muscle. The endometrium on the inside is the mucous lining that sheds and rebuilds each cycle.","notes":["The endometrium is the inner vascular mucosal lining, not a serous membrane. Pick the endometrium for a question about the lining that sheds in menstruation or where an embryo implants.","The perimetrium is the outer layer, and it is a serous membrane rather than muscle. Its correct pairing would be perimetrium and serous membrane.","The myometrium is muscle, not a serous membrane. This pairing mixes up the middle layer with the outer perimetrium, which is the serous layer.","Correct. The myometrium is the thick middle layer of smooth muscle."],"lt":"t-reproductive-female","lid":"fr7","comp":148,"competencyId":"w6-uterus"},{"id":"L02584","dok":2,"q":"Which peritoneal pouch lies between the uterus and the rectum and forms the lowest point of the peritoneal cavity in females?","a":"The rectouterine pouch (pouch of Douglas).","options":["The rectouterine pouch","The uterine cavity","The vaginal fornix","The vesicouterine pouch"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The peritoneum dips down between the pelvic organs to form pouches. Behind the uterus it drops between the uterus and the rectum to form the rectouterine pouch, also called the pouch of Douglas. This is the lowest part of the peritoneal cavity in females, so fluid, blood, or pus in the abdomen tends to collect there.","notes":["Correct. The rectouterine pouch sits between the uterus and rectum and is the lowest point of the female peritoneal cavity.","The uterine cavity is the space inside the body of the uterus, where an embryo implants in the lining. It is inside the organ, not a peritoneal pouch outside it.","The vaginal fornix is a recess of the vagina that surrounds the cervix. It is inside the vagina, not a peritoneal pouch. It would be the answer for the vaginal recess around the cervix.","The vesicouterine pouch is the shallower peritoneal pouch in front of the uterus, between the uterus and the urinary bladder. Pick it if the question asked for the pouch between the bladder and uterus."],"lt":"t-reproductive-female","lid":"fr8","comp":148,"competencyId":"w6-uterus"},{"id":"L02585","dok":1,"q":"What is the recess of the vagina that surrounds the cervix called?","a":"The fornix.","options":["The vestibule","The hymen","The fornix","The external os"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The cervix projects down into the top of the vagina. The vaginal wall wraps around it and forms a gutter-like recess all the way around the cervix, called the fornix. It is a space inside the upper vagina, which is why it is listed with the vagina and not with the uterus.","notes":["The vestibule is the region between the labia minora that holds the vaginal and urethral openings. It is at the bottom of the vagina, not around the cervix. Pick it for the area of the vulva where the vagina opens.","The hymen is the thin fold of mucous membrane that partly closes the vaginal opening at the bottom of the vagina. It would be the answer for a question about the vaginal orifice, not the cervix.","Correct. The fornix is the vaginal recess that encircles the cervix.","The external os is the opening of the cervical canal into the vagina. It sits in the middle of the cervix, surrounded by the fornix. Pick it if the question asked where the cervical canal opens into the vagina."],"lt":"t-reproductive-female","lid":"fr9","comp":149,"competencyId":"w6-vagina-vulva"},{"id":"L02586","dok":1,"q":"Which structure is part of the vulva, the external genitalia?","a":"The labia minora.","options":["The labia minora","The isthmus","The fornix","The cervix"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The vulva is the collective name for the external genitalia. It includes the mons pubis, the labia majora and labia minora, the clitoris, the vestibule with the vaginal and urethral openings, and the greater vestibular glands. The labia minora are the two smaller, hairless skin folds that lie within the labia majora and enclose the vestibule.","notes":["Correct. The labia minora are external skin folds and are part of the vulva.","The isthmus is the narrow segment of the uterine tube that joins the uterus. It is deep in the pelvis. Pick it for the part of the uterine tube closest to the uterus.","The fornix is the recess inside the upper vagina around the cervix. It is internal, so it is not part of the vulva. Pick it for a question about the vaginal recess that surrounds the cervix.","The cervix is the narrow lower part of the uterus that opens into the vagina. It is an internal organ region. Pick it for a question about where a Pap test samples cells."],"lt":"t-reproductive-female","lid":"fr10","comp":149,"competencyId":"w6-vagina-vulva"},{"id":"L02587","dok":2,"q":"The greater vestibular glands secrete lubricating mucus beside the vaginal opening. Which male glands are their homologs?","a":"The bulbourethral glands.","options":["The seminal glands","The sebaceous glands","The bulbourethral glands","The prostate gland"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Homologs are male and female structures that develop from the same embryonic tissue. The greater vestibular (Bartholin's) glands and the bulbourethral (Cowper's) glands are both small, paired glands near the lower end of the tract that release mucus during arousal. Their matching position and mucus secretion reflect their shared origin.","notes":["The seminal glands are paired male glands behind the bladder that add a large share of the fluid in semen. Pick them if the question asked which glands produce most of the seminal fluid.","Sebaceous glands are the oil glands of the skin that empty into hair follicles. They are found in both sexes, so they are not a male homolog. Pick them for a skin question about oil secretion.","Correct. The bulbourethral glands are the male homologs of the greater vestibular glands.","The prostate is a single male gland that surrounds the urethra just below the bladder. It would be the answer for the gland that encircles the first part of the male urethra."],"lt":"t-reproductive-female","lid":"fr11","comp":149,"competencyId":"w6-vagina-vulva"},{"id":"L02588","dok":1,"q":"What is the diamond-shaped region between the thighs that holds the external genitalia and the anus called?","a":"The perineum.","options":["The vestibule","The mons pubis","The vulva","The perineum"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The perineum is the diamond-shaped region bounded by the pubic symphysis in front, the ischial tuberosities on the sides, and the coccyx behind. It holds both the external genitalia and the anus, and it is divided into an anterior urogenital triangle and a posterior anal triangle.","notes":["The vestibule is the small area between the labia minora that holds the vaginal and urethral openings. Pick it for the space where the vagina and urethra open.","The mons pubis is the cushion of fatty tissue over the pubic symphysis, at the top front of the vulva. Pick it for the fatty pad over the pubic bone.","The vulva is the collective name for the external genitalia only. It sits within the front part of the perineum but does not include the anus. Pick it for the collective term for the external female genitalia.","Correct. The perineum is the diamond-shaped region holding the external genitalia and the anus."],"lt":"t-reproductive-female","lid":"fr12","comp":149,"competencyId":"w6-vagina-vulva"},{"id":"L02589","dok":1,"q":"Which ligaments support the breast by running through the mammary tissue to the skin?","a":"The suspensory (Cooper's) ligaments.","options":["The round ligaments","The uterosacral ligaments","The broad ligaments","The suspensory ligaments"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The suspensory ligaments (Cooper ligaments) are bands of connective tissue that run from the skin through the breast, between the lobes, to the fascia over the pectoralis major. They support the breast from within. A tumor that pulls on one of these ligaments can dimple the skin above it.","notes":["The round ligaments belong to the uterus, not the breast. They are fibrous cords that run from the uterus through the inguinal canal to the labia majora.","The uterosacral ligaments belong to the uterus, not the breast. They run from the cervix back to the sacrum and anchor the uterus from behind.","The broad ligaments are in the pelvis, not the breast. They are folds of peritoneum that drape over the uterus and uterine tubes and attach them to the lateral pelvic walls.","Correct. The suspensory (Cooper) ligaments run through the breast to the skin and support it."],"lt":"t-reproductive-female","lid":"fr13","comp":150,"competencyId":"w6-mammary"},{"id":"L02590","dok":2,"q":"Which description fits the broad ligament of the uterus?","a":"A fold of peritoneum draped over the uterus and tubes that attaches them to the pelvic walls and carries their vessels and nerves.","options":["A cord of tissue that anchors the ovary to the uterus","A band connecting the back of the uterus to the sacrum","A fold of peritoneum draped over the uterus and its tubes","A fibrous cord running from the uterus to the labia majora"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The broad ligament is not a cord but a wide double sheet of peritoneum. It drapes over the uterus, uterine tubes, and ovaries like a sheet over a clothesline and attaches them to the lateral pelvic walls. Blood vessels, lymphatic vessels, and nerves run between its two layers to reach these organs.","notes":["This describes the ovarian ligament, which ties the ovary to the side of the uterus. Pick this description when the question names the ovarian ligament.","This describes the uterosacral ligament, which connects the uterus to the sacrum. Pick this description when the question names the uterosacral ligament.","Correct. The broad ligament is a peritoneal fold draped over the uterus and tubes.","This describes the round ligament, which runs from the uterus to the labia majora. Pick this description when the question names the round ligament."],"lt":"t-reproductive-female","lid":"fr14","comp":148,"competencyId":"w6-uterus"},{"id":"L02591","dok":1,"q":"What kind of tissue makes up most of the clitoris?","a":"Erectile tissue.","options":["Glandular tissue","Lymphatic tissue","Adipose tissue","Erectile tissue"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The clitoris is a small mass of erectile tissue at the front junction of the labia minora. Erectile tissue is a network of vascular spaces that fill with blood during arousal. This is the same kind of tissue found in the penis, and the clitoris is the female homolog of the penis.","notes":["Glandular tissue secretes a product, and the clitoris is not a gland. Glandular tissue is the answer for the greater vestibular glands beside the vaginal orifice or for the mammary glands.","Lymphatic tissue is immune tissue that filters lymph, as in the lymph nodes, tonsils, and spleen. It does not form the clitoris.","Adipose tissue is fat, and it does not make up the clitoris. It is the answer if the question asked what the mons pubis is made of.","Correct. The clitoris is built of erectile tissue, like its male homolog."],"lt":"t-reproductive-female","lid":"fr15","comp":149,"competencyId":"w6-vagina-vulva"},{"id":"L02592","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares the internal os and the external os of the cervix?","a":"The internal os opens into the uterine cavity, and the external os opens into the vagina.","options":["Both open into the vagina, one on each side of the fornix.","Both open into the uterine cavity at opposite ends of the uterine body.","The internal os opens into the uterine cavity; the external os opens into the vagina.","The internal os opens into the vagina; the external os opens into the uterine cavity."],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The cervical canal is the passage through the cervix, and it has two openings. The internal os is the upper opening, on the inside, where the canal meets the uterine cavity. The external os is the lower opening, facing outward, where the canal meets the vagina. The words internal and external tell you which end is deeper in the body.","notes":["Only the external os opens into the vagina. The fornix is the vaginal recess around the cervix, not a pair of openings, so this mixes up the fornix with the os.","The uterine cavity is reached only at the upper end of the canal. The lower opening leads into the vagina, so the two openings do not both open into the uterine cavity.","Correct. Internal os to the uterine cavity, external os to the vagina.","This reverses the two openings. The internal os is the deeper, upper end, so it opens into the uterine cavity, while the external os faces the vagina."],"lt":"t-reproductive-female","lid":"fr16","comp":148,"competencyId":"w6-uterus"},{"id":"L02593","dok":1,"q":"Which structures are the female gonads, the primary reproductive organs?","a":"The ovaries.","options":["The uterine tubes","The uterus","The mammary glands","The ovaries"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"A gonad is the primary reproductive organ that makes the gametes and secretes sex hormones. In females the gonads are the ovaries, paired almond-shaped organs beside the uterus that produce oocytes and hormones. Every other female reproductive organ is an accessory organ that carries, receives, or supports.","notes":["The uterine tubes catch the released oocyte and carry it toward the uterus. They transport the gamete but do not make it. Pick them for a question about where fertilization usually happens.","The uterus is the muscular organ where an embryo implants and a fetus develops. It supports a pregnancy but does not make gametes. Pick it for a question about where a fetus grows.","The mammary glands are modified sweat glands of the breast that produce milk. They are studied with this system but are not gonads. Pick them for a question about milk production.","Correct. The ovaries are the female gonads."],"lt":"t-reproductive-female","lid":"c1","comp":146,"competencyId":"w6-ovary"},{"id":"L02594","dok":1,"q":"What is the female gamete called?","a":"The oocyte.","options":["The corpus luteum","The follicle","The zygote","The oocyte"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"A gamete is a sex cell that carries half the genetic information and joins with a gamete from the other parent at fertilization. The female gamete is the oocyte, the egg cell. It develops inside an ovarian follicle and is released from the ovary at ovulation.","notes":["The corpus luteum is the structure left in the ovary after the follicle releases its oocyte. Pick it for what remains after ovulation.","A follicle is the structure in the ovarian cortex that holds and supports a developing oocyte. It is the container, not the gamete itself. Pick it for the structure that houses an immature egg.","The zygote is the single cell formed when a sperm and an oocyte unite at fertilization. It is the product of two gametes, not a gamete. Pick it for the first cell of a new individual.","Correct. The oocyte is the female gamete, the egg cell."],"lt":"t-reproductive-female","lid":"c2","comp":146,"competencyId":"w6-ovary"},{"id":"L02595","dok":1,"q":"What is the collective term for the female external genitalia?","a":"The vulva.","options":["The vagina","The perineum","The vestibule","The vulva"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The vulva, also called the pudendum, is the collective name for all the external female genitalia: the mons pubis, labia majora, labia minora, clitoris, vestibule, and greater vestibular glands. It is a group name, so it includes several smaller structures, each with its own name.","notes":["The vagina is the internal fibromuscular canal from the cervix to the outside. It opens into the vulva but is not external genitalia. Pick it for the birth canal.","The perineum is the larger diamond-shaped region that holds the external genitalia and also the anus. Pick it for the whole region between the thighs, from the pubic symphysis to the coccyx.","The vestibule is one part of the vulva, the area between the labia minora that holds the vaginal and urethral openings. Pick it for that specific space, not the whole group.","Correct. The vulva is the collective term for the external genitalia."],"lt":"t-reproductive-female","lid":"c3","comp":149,"competencyId":"w6-vagina-vulva"},{"id":"L02596","dok":1,"q":"What is another name for the uterine tubes?","a":"The fallopian tubes.","options":["The ductus deferens","The fallopian tubes","The lactiferous ducts","The eustachian tubes"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The uterine tubes are also called the fallopian tubes, after the anatomist Fallopius, and some texts call them oviducts. They are the paired tubes that catch the oocyte at the ovary and carry it to the uterus. Eponyms like this are easy to mix up, so link fallopian with the uterus and ovary.","notes":["The ductus deferens is a male duct that carries sperm from the epididymis toward the urethra. Because it is male, it cannot be another name for the female uterine tubes.","Correct. Uterine tubes and fallopian tubes are two names for the same paired tubes.","The lactiferous ducts are the milk ducts of the breast, one for each lobe, carrying milk to the nipple. They are not tubes of the pelvic reproductive tract.","The eustachian tubes are the auditory tubes that connect the middle ear to the pharynx, so they have nothing to do with the uterus. Students mix them up with the fallopian tubes because both names come from anatomists."],"lt":"t-reproductive-female","lid":"c4","comp":147,"competencyId":"w6-ovum-path"},{"id":"L02597","dok":1,"q":"What is the narrow inferior portion of the uterus that opens into the vagina called?","a":"The cervix.","options":["The body","The fornix","The cervix","The fundus"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The uterus has three regions from top to bottom: the dome-shaped fundus above the tube entries, the tapered central body, and the narrow cervix at the bottom. The cervix projects into the top of the vagina, and its canal opens into the vagina at the external os.","notes":["The body is the tapered central portion of the uterus that surrounds the uterine cavity. Pick it for the main middle region, between the fundus and cervix.","The fornix is the recess of the vagina that wraps around the cervix. It is part of the vagina, not the uterus. Pick it for the vaginal recess around the cervix.","Correct. The cervix is the narrow inferior portion that opens into the vagina.","The fundus is the dome-shaped top of the uterus, above where the uterine tubes enter. It is at the opposite end from the vagina. Pick it for the superior portion of the uterus."],"lt":"t-reproductive-female","lid":"c5","comp":148,"competencyId":"w6-uterus"},{"id":"L02598","dok":1,"q":"What is the inner lining layer of the uterine wall called?","a":"The endometrium.","options":["The myometrium","The tunica albuginea","The perimetrium","The endometrium"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The uterine wall has three layers, and the prefixes tell you where each one sits. Endo means within, so the endometrium is the inner lining that faces the uterine cavity. It is a vascular mucosa that thickens each cycle, sheds in menstruation, and is where an embryo implants.","notes":["The myometrium is the thick middle layer of smooth muscle. Myo means muscle. Pick it for the layer that contracts during childbirth.","The tunica albuginea is the whitish connective tissue capsule beneath the surface of the ovary. It is not part of the uterus. Pick it for the capsule of the ovary.","The perimetrium is the outer serous layer of the uterus, part of the visceral peritoneum. Peri means around. Pick it for the outermost layer.","Correct. The endometrium is the inner lining of the uterus."],"lt":"t-reproductive-female","lid":"c6","comp":148,"competencyId":"w6-uterus"},{"id":"L02599","dok":1,"q":"Which structure is the fibromuscular canal that runs from the cervix to the body exterior?","a":"The vagina.","options":["The vestibule","The urethra","The vagina","The uterine tube"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The vagina is the fibromuscular canal that connects the cervix of the uterus to the outside of the body. It receives the penis during intercourse and serves as the birth canal. Its lower opening, the vaginal orifice, opens into the vestibule of the vulva.","notes":["The vestibule is the shallow area between the labia minora where the vagina and urethra open. It is a space of the vulva, not a canal. Pick it for the region that holds those two openings.","The urethra is a separate tube that carries urine from the bladder to the outside. It opens into the vestibule just in front of the vagina. Pick it for the tube that drains the bladder.","Correct. The vagina runs from the cervix to the exterior.","The uterine tube runs from beside the ovary to the uterus. It never reaches the outside of the body. Pick it for the tube that carries the oocyte toward the uterus."],"lt":"t-reproductive-female","lid":"c7","comp":149,"competencyId":"w6-vagina-vulva"},{"id":"L02600","dok":1,"q":"What are the structures in the ovarian cortex that each hold a developing oocyte called?","a":"Ovarian follicles.","options":["Mammary alveoli","Seminiferous tubules","Ovarian follicles","Corpora albicantia"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Ovarian follicles are the structures in the cortex of the ovary, each holding an oocyte at some stage of development. As a follicle matures it enlarges and fills with fluid, and at ovulation the mature follicle releases its oocyte.","notes":["Mammary alveoli are the small sacs of glandular tissue in the breast that secrete milk. Pick them for the milk-producing units of the mammary gland.","Seminiferous tubules are the coiled tubules inside the testis where sperm develop. They are the male counterpart of a place where gametes form. Pick them for the testis.","Correct. Each ovarian follicle holds a developing oocyte.","The corpora albicantia (singular, corpus albicans) are white scars in the ovary left after a corpus luteum degenerates. They hold no oocyte. Pick them for the scar tissue in an older ovary."],"lt":"t-reproductive-female","lid":"c8","comp":146,"competencyId":"w6-ovary"},{"id":"L02601","dok":1,"q":"Which small mass of erectile tissue sits at the anterior junction of the labia minora?","a":"The clitoris.","options":["The mons pubis","The hymen","The vestibule","The clitoris"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The clitoris is a small, sensitive mass of erectile tissue where the labia minora meet at the front of the vulva. It fills with blood during arousal and is the female homolog of the penis.","notes":["The mons pubis is the cushion of fatty tissue over the pubic symphysis, above and in front of the labia. Pick it for the fatty pad over the pubic bone.","The hymen is a thin fold of mucous membrane that partly closes the vaginal opening. Pick it for the membrane at the vaginal orifice.","The vestibule is the area between the labia minora that holds the vaginal and urethral openings. It lies behind the clitoris. Pick it for the space where those openings are found.","Correct. The clitoris sits where the labia minora meet in front."],"lt":"t-reproductive-female","lid":"c9","comp":149,"competencyId":"w6-vagina-vulva"},{"id":"L02602","dok":1,"q":"What are the milk-producing glands of the breast called?","a":"The mammary glands.","options":["The seminal glands","The greater vestibular glands","The bulbourethral glands","The mammary glands"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The mammary glands are the milk-producing glands of the breasts. They lie over the chest muscles and are built from lobes, lobules, and alveoli, with lactiferous ducts carrying milk to the nipple. They are studied with the female reproductive system because they feed the newborn.","notes":["The seminal glands are male accessory glands behind the bladder that add fluid to semen. Pick them for the glands that produce most of the seminal fluid.","The greater vestibular glands are the Bartholin's glands beside the vaginal opening, which secrete lubricating mucus. Pick them for the glands of the vulva.","The bulbourethral glands are small male glands below the prostate that release mucus into the urethra. Pick them for the male homolog of the greater vestibular glands.","Correct. The mammary glands are the milk-producing glands of the breast."],"lt":"t-reproductive-female","lid":"c10","comp":150,"competencyId":"w6-mammary"},{"id":"L02603","dok":1,"q":"What is the circular pigmented area of skin around the nipple called?","a":"The areola.","options":["The nipple","The lobule","The alveolus","The areola"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The areola is the circle of pigmented skin that surrounds the nipple. The nipple itself is the raised projection in the center, where the lactiferous ducts open. Both are on the surface of the breast, while lobules and alveoli are deep inside the gland.","notes":["The nipple is the raised projection in the center of the areola where the lactiferous ducts open. Pick it for the opening point of the milk ducts.","A lobule is a subdivision of a mammary lobe, deep inside the breast, that contains the milk-secreting alveoli. Pick it for the subdivision that holds the alveoli.","An alveolus is one of the small sacs of glandular tissue inside a lobule that secretes milk. Pick it for the milk-producing unit of the gland.","Correct. The areola is the pigmented ring of skin around the nipple."],"lt":"t-reproductive-female","lid":"c11","comp":150,"competencyId":"w6-mammary"},{"id":"L02604","dok":1,"q":"After a mature follicle releases its oocyte at ovulation, which structure forms from what remains of that follicle?","a":"The corpus luteum.","options":["The germinal epithelium","The tunica albuginea","The corpus luteum","The corpus albicans"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"After ovulation, the ruptured follicle left in the ovary is transformed into the corpus luteum, which means yellow body. It is a temporary structure. If no pregnancy occurs, it later degenerates into a white scar called the corpus albicans.","notes":["The germinal epithelium is the layer of low cuboidal cells covering the surface of the ovary. Pick it for the outermost layer of the ovary.","The tunica albuginea is the whitish connective tissue capsule just beneath the germinal epithelium. Pick it for the capsule of the ovary.","Correct. The corpus luteum, the yellow body, forms from the follicle left after ovulation.","The corpus albicans is the white scar that forms later, when the corpus luteum degenerates. It is one step further along. Pick it for what the corpus luteum becomes."],"lt":"t-reproductive-female","lid":"c12","comp":146,"competencyId":"w6-ovary"},{"id":"L02605","dok":1,"q":"Which ligament is the cord that anchors the ovary to the uterus?","a":"The ovarian ligament.","options":["The ovarian ligament","The uterosacral ligament","The round ligament","The suspensory ligament"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The ovary is held in place by more than one attachment, and each one connects it to something different. The ovarian ligament is a short cord that ties the ovary medially to the side of the uterus. The suspensory ligament holds it laterally to the pelvic wall, and the mesovarium attaches it to the broad ligament.","notes":["Correct. The ovarian ligament anchors the ovary to the uterus.","The uterosacral ligament connects the uterus to the sacrum. Pick it for the posterior attachment of the uterus.","The round ligament runs from the uterus to the labia majora. It does not touch the ovary. Pick it for the uterine ligament that reaches the labia majora.","The suspensory ligament attaches the ovary to the lateral pelvic wall and carries the ovarian vessels. Pick it for the lateral attachment of the ovary."],"lt":"t-reproductive-female","lid":"c13","comp":146,"competencyId":"w6-ovary"},{"id":"L02606","dok":2,"q":"Which ligament attaches the ovary to the lateral pelvic wall and carries the ovarian artery and vein?","a":"The suspensory ligament.","options":["The round ligament","The uterosacral ligament","The suspensory ligament","The ovarian ligament"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The suspensory ligament is the fold that holds the ovary to the lateral wall of the pelvis. The ovarian artery and vein travel in this fold to reach the ovary, which is why it is the ligament to look for when you trace the ovarian vessels on a model.","notes":["The round ligament runs from the uterus to the labia majora and does not attach to the ovary. Pick it for the uterine ligament that reaches the labia majora.","The uterosacral ligament connects the uterus to the sacrum. Pick it for the posterior anchor of the uterus.","Correct. The suspensory ligament holds the ovary to the pelvic wall and carries its vessels.","The ovarian ligament anchors the ovary medially to the uterus, not to the pelvic wall. Pick it for the cord between the ovary and the uterus."],"lt":"t-reproductive-female","lid":"c14","comp":146,"competencyId":"w6-ovary"},{"id":"L02607","dok":1,"q":"Which part of the uterine tube is the funnel-shaped end that opens into the pelvic cavity near the ovary?","a":"The infundibulum.","options":["The infundibulum","The fimbriae","The isthmus","The ampulla"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Infundibulum means funnel, and that is its shape. It is the open, flared end of the uterine tube that sits beside the ovary and opens into the pelvic cavity. The tube does not attach directly to the ovary, so the infundibulum has to catch the oocyte after it is released.","notes":["Correct. The infundibulum is the funnel-shaped end near the ovary.","The fimbriae are the finger-like fringes on the rim of the infundibulum. They are attached to the funnel but are not the funnel itself. Pick them for the projections that sweep the oocyte in.","The isthmus is the narrow, thick-walled segment at the far end of the tube, where it joins the uterus. Pick it for the part of the tube closest to the uterus.","The ampulla is the widest and longest part of the tube, just past the funnel, where fertilization usually happens. Pick it for the usual site of fertilization."],"lt":"t-reproductive-female","lid":"c15","comp":147,"competencyId":"w6-ovum-path"},{"id":"L02608","dok":1,"q":"In which part of the female tract does fertilization usually occur?","a":"The ampulla","options":["The infundibulum","The uterine cavity","The isthmus","The ampulla"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The ampulla is the widest and longest part of the uterine tube, between the infundibulum and the isthmus. After the fimbriae sweep the oocyte into the infundibulum, it moves into the ampulla, and this is where sperm usually meet it. The narrow isthmus and the uterus come after the ampulla.","notes":["The infundibulum is the funnel-shaped end of the tube that catches the oocyte from the ovary. The oocyte passes through it first, but fertilization usually happens farther along, in the ampulla.","The uterine cavity is the space inside the body of the uterus, past the end of the tube. Fertilization usually happens before the oocyte ever reaches it, out in the ampulla of the tube.","The isthmus is the narrow part of the tube that joins the uterus. The oocyte reaches it after the ampulla, so it is past the usual site of fertilization.","Correct. Fertilization usually happens in the ampulla, the widest part of the uterine tube."],"lt":"t-reproductive-female","lid":"c16","comp":147,"competencyId":"w6-ovum-path"},{"id":"L02609","dok":1,"q":"Which part of the uterine tube is the narrow, thick-walled segment that joins the uterus?","a":"The isthmus.","options":["The fimbriae","The isthmus","The ampulla","The infundibulum"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"An isthmus is a narrow connecting part, and in the uterine tube it is the short, narrow, thick-walled segment at the medial end where the tube joins the uterus. The oocyte or early embryo passes through it on the way to the uterine cavity.","notes":["The fimbriae are the fringe of finger-like projections around the infundibulum. Pick them for the structures that sweep the oocyte into the tube.","Correct. The isthmus is the narrow segment joining the tube to the uterus.","The ampulla is the wide middle segment of the tube where fertilization usually occurs. Pick it if the question asked for the widest part.","The infundibulum is the funnel-shaped end at the opposite end of the tube, near the ovary. Pick it for the open end that catches the oocyte."],"lt":"t-reproductive-female","lid":"c17","comp":147,"competencyId":"w6-ovum-path"},{"id":"L02610","dok":1,"q":"What are the finger-like projections around the infundibulum that sweep the oocyte into the uterine tube called?","a":"The fimbriae.","options":["The villi","The papillae","The fimbriae","The rugae"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The fimbriae are a fringe of finger-like projections around the rim of the infundibulum. They lie close to the ovary and, at ovulation, sweep over its surface to draw the released oocyte into the funnel of the tube.","notes":["Villi are finger-like projections of the small intestine lining that increase the surface area for absorption. The shape is similar, which is the trap. Pick villi for the small intestine.","Papillae are small bumps, such as those on the tongue or the dermal papillae of the skin. Pick them for the projections on the surface of the tongue.","Correct. The fimbriae are the fringe of projections on the infundibulum.","Rugae are folds in the wall of a hollow organ, such as the stomach or vagina, that let it stretch. Pick rugae for the folds of the stomach lining."],"lt":"t-reproductive-female","lid":"c18","comp":147,"competencyId":"w6-ovum-path"},{"id":"L02611","dok":1,"q":"Which region of the uterus is the dome-shaped top, above where the uterine tubes enter?","a":"The fundus.","options":["The fornix","The fundus","The cervix","The body"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The fundus is the rounded, dome-shaped top of the uterus, above the points where the two uterine tubes enter. Below it is the body, which holds the uterine cavity, and below that is the narrow cervix that projects into the vagina. Use the tube entries as the landmark: anything above them is fundus.","notes":["The fornix is the recess of the vagina that surrounds the cervix. It belongs to the vagina, not the uterus. Pick it for the vaginal recess.","Correct. The fundus is the dome-shaped top of the uterus.","The cervix is the narrow inferior portion that opens into the vagina, at the opposite end from the fundus. Pick it for the lowest region of the uterus.","The body is the tapered central portion of the uterus, below the fundus and above the cervix. Pick it for the main middle region."],"lt":"t-reproductive-female","lid":"c19","comp":148,"competencyId":"w6-uterus"},{"id":"L02612","dok":1,"q":"Which layer of the uterine wall is the outer serous layer?","a":"The perimetrium.","options":["The parietal peritoneum","The myometrium","The perimetrium","The endometrium"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The perimetrium is the outermost layer of the uterine wall. It is a serous membrane, part of the visceral peritoneum that covers the organ. Peri means around, so the perimetrium is the layer wrapped around the outside of the uterus.","notes":["The parietal peritoneum lines the walls of the abdominopelvic cavity rather than covering an organ. The perimetrium is visceral peritoneum. Pick parietal peritoneum for the layer lining the body wall.","The myometrium is the thick middle layer of smooth muscle. Pick it for the layer that contracts in labor.","Correct. The perimetrium is the outer serous layer of the uterus.","The endometrium is the inner vascular lining that sheds in menstruation. Pick it for the innermost layer."],"lt":"t-reproductive-female","lid":"c20","comp":148,"competencyId":"w6-uterus"},{"id":"L02613","dok":1,"q":"Which layer of the uterine wall is the thick smooth muscle that contracts during labor?","a":"The myometrium.","options":["The perimetrium","The stratum basalis","The myometrium","The endometrium"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The myometrium is the thick middle layer of the uterine wall, made of smooth muscle, and it is thickest in the fundus. It is the layer that contracts during childbirth. Myo means muscle, and that prefix is the fastest way to recognize it.","notes":["The perimetrium is the outer serous layer, a thin covering of visceral peritoneum. Pick it for the outermost layer.","The stratum basalis is the deep, permanent part of the endometrium that rebuilds the lining after menstruation. It is not muscle. Pick it for the layer that regenerates the lining.","Correct. The myometrium is the smooth muscle layer that contracts in labor.","The endometrium is the inner vascular lining, not a muscle. Pick it for the layer that sheds in menstruation or where an embryo implants."],"lt":"t-reproductive-female","lid":"c21","comp":148,"competencyId":"w6-uterus"},{"id":"L02614","dok":1,"q":"Which structures of the breast carry milk from the lobes toward the nipple?","a":"The lactiferous ducts.","options":["The alveoli","The lobules","The lactiferous ducts","The suspensory ligaments"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Milk is made in the alveoli, which sit inside lobules, which are grouped into lobes. Each lobe drains through a lactiferous duct that carries the milk toward the nipple and opens on its surface. Lactiferous means milk-carrying, which matches the job.","notes":["The alveoli are the small sacs of glandular tissue where milk is produced. They are the start of the path, not the ducts. Pick them for where milk is made.","The lobules are the subdivisions of each lobe that contain the alveoli. Pick them for the subdivision that holds the milk-secreting sacs.","Correct. The lactiferous ducts carry milk to the nipple.","The suspensory ligaments, or Cooper's ligaments, are connective tissue bands that support the breast. They carry no milk. Pick them for what holds up the breast."],"lt":"t-reproductive-female","lid":"c22","comp":150,"competencyId":"w6-mammary"},{"id":"L02615","dok":1,"q":"In the mammary gland, the milk-secreting alveoli are found within which subdivision?","a":"Within the lobules.","options":["Within the nipple","Within the lobules","Within the lactiferous ducts","Within the areola"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The mammary gland nests in one direction: alveoli sit inside lobules, lobules sit inside lobes, and each lobe drains through a lactiferous duct to the nipple. So the alveoli, the small sacs that secrete milk, are housed within the lobules.","notes":["The nipple is the projection where the lactiferous ducts open. It is the end of the path, not where milk is made. Pick it for where milk exits the breast.","Correct. The alveoli are housed in the lobules.","The lactiferous ducts carry milk away from the lobes to the nipple. They are the drainage, not the compartments that hold the alveoli. Pick them for the ducts that carry milk.","The areola is the pigmented ring of skin around the nipple, on the surface of the breast. Pick it for the skin around the nipple."],"lt":"t-reproductive-female","lid":"c23","comp":150,"competencyId":"w6-mammary"},{"id":"L02616","dok":1,"q":"Which part of the vulva is the cushion of fatty tissue over the pubic symphysis?","a":"The mons pubis.","options":["The mons pubis","The vestibule","The labia majora","The clitoris"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The mons pubis is the rounded cushion of fatty tissue lying over the pubic symphysis, the joint between the two pubic bones. It is the most anterior and superior part of the vulva, and after puberty it is covered with hair.","notes":["Correct. The mons pubis is the fatty cushion over the pubic symphysis.","The vestibule is the area between the labia minora that holds the vaginal and urethral openings. Pick it for the space where those openings are found.","The labia majora are the two larger, hair-covered skin folds that extend back from the mons pubis. Pick them for the outer folds of the vulva or for the homolog of the scrotum.","The clitoris is the small mass of erectile tissue where the labia minora meet in front. Pick it for the homolog of the penis."],"lt":"t-reproductive-female","lid":"c24","comp":149,"competencyId":"w6-vagina-vulva"},{"id":"L02617","dok":2,"q":"Which two openings are found in the vestibule of the vulva?","a":"The urethral orifice and the vaginal orifice.","options":["The urethra and the vagina","The urethra and the anus","The cervix and the urethra","The vagina and the anus"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The vestibule is the area enclosed by the labia minora. Two openings sit in it: the urethral orifice in front and the vaginal orifice behind it. The anus is close by but lies outside the vestibule, in the anal triangle of the perineum.","notes":["Correct. The urethra and the vagina both open into the vestibule.","The urethra does open into the vestibule, but the anus does not. The anus lies farther back in the anal triangle of the perineum. Pick this pair for the openings found in the perineum.","The cervix opens into the top of the vagina at the external os, deep inside the body, not into the vestibule. Pick the cervix for where the uterus opens into the vagina.","The vagina opens into the vestibule, but the anus is separate, in the anal triangle behind it. Both openings lie in the perineum, which is where this pair would fit."],"lt":"t-reproductive-female","lid":"c25","comp":149,"competencyId":"w6-vagina-vulva"},{"id":"L02618","dok":1,"q":"Which folds of the vulva are the two smaller, hairless skin folds that lie within the labia majora?","a":"The labia minora.","options":["The hymen","The labia minora","The labia majora","The mons pubis"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The vulva has two pairs of skin folds. The labia majora are the larger, outer, hair-covered folds. Inside them are the labia minora, smaller and hairless, which enclose the vestibule and meet in front at the clitoris. Majora means larger and minora means smaller.","notes":["The hymen is a thin fold of mucous membrane that partly closes the vaginal opening. It is not a skin fold. Pick it for the membrane at the vaginal orifice.","Correct. The labia minora are the smaller, hairless inner folds.","The labia majora are the larger outer folds, covered with hair, and homologous to the scrotum. Pick them for the outer folds of the vulva.","The mons pubis is a fatty cushion over the pubic symphysis, not a fold. Pick it for the fatty pad over the pubic bone."],"lt":"t-reproductive-female","lid":"c26","comp":149,"competencyId":"w6-vagina-vulva"},{"id":"L02619","dok":2,"q":"Which layer of the uterine wall is made of a stratum functionalis and a stratum basalis?","a":"The endometrium.","options":["The perimetrium","The endometrium","The ampulla","The myometrium"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The endometrium is the inner mucous lining of the uterus, next to the uterine cavity. It has two layers. The stratum functionalis, next to the cavity, is shed during menstruation, and the stratum basalis, next to the myometrium, stays in place and rebuilds it each cycle.","notes":["The perimetrium is the outer serous layer of the uterus, part of the visceral peritoneum. It is a single serous covering with no strata, so it is the answer only for the outermost layer.","Correct. The endometrium is built of a stratum functionalis and a stratum basalis.","The ampulla is not a layer of the uterine wall at all. It is the wide middle part of the uterine tube, where fertilization usually happens.","The myometrium is the thick middle layer of smooth muscle and has no stratum functionalis or basalis. It is the answer for the layer that contracts during childbirth."],"lt":"t-reproductive-female","lid":"c27","comp":148,"competencyId":"w6-uterus"},{"id":"L02623","dok":2,"q":"Endometrial tissue is found growing on the ovaries and the pelvic lining, outside the uterus. What is this disorder called?","a":"Endometriosis.","options":["Endometriosis","Pelvic inflammatory disease","Uterine fibroids","Ovarian cysts"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Endometriosis is the growth of endometrial tissue, the tissue that normally lines the uterus, in places outside the uterus such as the ovaries and the pelvic peritoneum. The name comes from the tissue involved. Fibroids, in contrast, grow in the muscle of the uterine wall.","notes":["Correct. Endometriosis is endometrial tissue growing outside the uterus.","Pelvic inflammatory disease is an infection that spreads through the uterus, tubes, and ovaries. Pick it when the question describes an infection rising through the tract.","Uterine fibroids are non-cancerous tumors of the smooth muscle of the myometrium. They grow in the uterine wall, not outside the uterus. Pick fibroids for a muscle tumor of the uterus.","Ovarian cysts are fluid-filled sacs that form on or in an ovary. They are not displaced uterine lining. Pick them for a fluid sac on the ovary."],"lt":"t-reproductive-female","lid":"c31","comp":148,"competencyId":"w6-uterus"},{"id":"L02624","dok":2,"q":"A patient has non-cancerous tumors arising in the smooth muscle of the uterine wall. What is this condition called?","a":"Uterine fibroids.","options":["Endometriosis","Ovarian cysts","Cervical cancer","Uterine fibroids"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Uterine fibroids are benign (non-cancerous) tumors of the smooth muscle of the myometrium, the thick middle layer of the uterine wall. The clue in the question is smooth muscle, which points straight to the myometrium.","notes":["Endometriosis is endometrial lining tissue growing outside the uterus. It involves the lining, not the muscle layer. Pick it for uterine lining found on the ovaries or pelvic wall.","Ovarian cysts are fluid-filled sacs on or in an ovary, not muscle tumors of the uterus. Pick them for a fluid sac on the ovary.","Cervical cancer is a malignant tumor of the cervix, the lower part of the uterus. It is cancerous and starts in the cervix, not the muscle wall. Pick it for the cancer screened by a Pap test.","Correct. Uterine fibroids are benign tumors of the myometrium."],"lt":"t-reproductive-female","lid":"c32","comp":148,"competencyId":"w6-uterus"},{"id":"L02626","dok":2,"q":"An infection of the upper reproductive tract spreads through the uterus and tubes and can scar the uterine tubes. What is this disorder called?","a":"Pelvic inflammatory disease.","options":["Endometriosis","Cervical cancer","Pelvic inflammatory disease","Ovarian cysts"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Pelvic inflammatory disease (PID) is an infection that climbs from the vagina through the cervix, uterus, and uterine tubes into the pelvic cavity. It can reach the pelvic cavity because the infundibulum is open and does not seal onto the ovary. The infection can leave scarring that narrows a uterine tube. The key word in the question is infection.","notes":["Endometriosis is uterine lining tissue growing outside the uterus. It can also scar pelvic organs, but it is not an infection. Pick it for endometrial tissue on the ovaries or pelvic wall.","Cervical cancer is a malignant tumor of the cervix. It is not an infection that spreads up the tract. Pick it for the cancer screened by a Pap test.","Correct. Pelvic inflammatory disease is an infection that spreads up the tract and can scar the tubes.","Ovarian cysts are fluid-filled sacs on or in an ovary, not a spreading infection. Pick them for a fluid sac on the ovary."],"lt":"t-reproductive-female","lid":"c34","comp":147,"competencyId":"w6-ovum-path"},{"id":"L02627","dok":2,"q":"A Pap test screens for cancer by sampling cells from which structure?","a":"The cervix.","options":["The cervix","The labia minora","The fundus","The ovary"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"A Pap test collects cells from the cervix, the narrow lower part of the uterus that projects into the vagina. Because the cervix can be reached through the vagina, its cells can be sampled in a routine exam and checked for changes that can lead to cervical cancer.","notes":["Correct. The Pap test samples cells from the cervix to screen for cervical cancer.","The labia minora are skin folds of the vulva. They are external, not the site of a Pap sample. Pick them for the inner folds that enclose the vestibule.","The fundus is the dome-shaped top of the uterus, far from the vagina. Pick it for the superior region of the uterus.","The ovary sits deep in the pelvis beside the uterus and cannot be reached through the vagina for a cell sample. Pick it for a question about ovarian cysts."],"lt":"t-reproductive-female","lid":"c35","comp":148,"competencyId":"w6-uterus"},{"id":"L02629","dok":1,"q":"What are the small sacs of glandular tissue inside the lobules that secrete milk called?","a":"The alveoli.","options":["The lobes","The lactiferous ducts","The alveoli","The lobules"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The alveoli are the smallest units of the mammary gland, tiny sacs of glandular tissue that secrete milk. They cluster inside the lobules, the lobules group into lobes, and the lobes drain through lactiferous ducts to the nipple.","notes":["The lobes are the largest compartments of the mammary gland, 15 to 20 of them, each divided into lobules. They are too large to be the small secreting sacs.","The lactiferous ducts carry milk from the lobes to the nipple. They transport milk rather than make it.","Correct. The alveoli are the milk-secreting sacs inside the lobules.","The lobules are the subdivisions of a lobe that contain the alveoli. They hold the sacs but are not the sacs themselves."],"lt":"t-reproductive-female","lid":"c37","comp":150,"competencyId":"w6-mammary"},{"id":"L02630","dok":1,"q":"Which pigmented projection on the breast is where the lactiferous ducts open?","a":"The nipple.","options":["The lobule","The areola","The nipple","The alveolus"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The nipple is the raised, pigmented projection at the center of the breast. The lactiferous ducts converge on it and open on its surface, so it is the exit point for milk. The areola is the ring of pigmented skin around it.","notes":["A lobule is a deep subdivision of a lobe that contains the alveoli. Pick it for the subdivision holding the milk-secreting sacs.","The areola is the circular pigmented skin around the nipple. It surrounds the opening point but is not where the ducts open. Pick it for the ring of skin around the nipple.","Correct. The lactiferous ducts open on the nipple.","An alveolus is a milk-secreting sac deep inside a lobule. It is the start of the milk path, not the exit. Pick it for where milk is produced."],"lt":"t-reproductive-female","lid":"c38","comp":150,"competencyId":"w6-mammary"},{"id":"L02631","dok":1,"q":"What is the area enclosed by the labia minora, into which the vagina opens, called?","a":"The vestibule.","options":["The vestibule","The mons pubis","The fornix","The perineum"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The vestibule is the space between the labia minora. It holds the vaginal orifice and, in front of it, the external urethral orifice. The ducts of the greater vestibular glands also open into the vestibule, which is how those glands got their name.","notes":["Correct. The vestibule is the area between the labia minora.","The mons pubis is the fatty cushion over the pubic symphysis, above and in front of the labia. Pick it for the fatty pad over the pubic bone.","The fornix is the recess at the top of the vagina around the cervix. It is deep inside the body, not between the labia. Pick it for the vaginal recess around the cervix.","The perineum is the larger diamond-shaped region that holds all the external genitalia and the anus. The vestibule is a small part within it. Pick the perineum for the whole region from the pubic symphysis to the coccyx."],"lt":"t-reproductive-female","lid":"c39","comp":149,"competencyId":"w6-vagina-vulva"},{"id":"L02632","dok":2,"q":"Which sequence correctly traces the oocyte from ovulation until it enters the uterine tube?","a":"Ovary, then fimbriae, then infundibulum.","options":["Ovary, then infundibulum, then fimbriae","Fimbriae, then ovary, then infundibulum","Infundibulum, then ovary, then fimbriae","Ovary, then fimbriae, then infundibulum"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"At ovulation the mature follicle releases the oocyte from the surface of the ovary. The tube is not attached to the ovary, so the fimbriae sweep over the ovary and draw the oocyte toward the tube. The oocyte then enters the funnel-shaped infundibulum, the opening of the uterine tube.","notes":["This order has the oocyte enter the funnel before the fimbriae act. The fimbriae are the fringes that guide it in, so they come before the infundibulum.","The oocyte starts in the ovary, so the ovary must be first. The fimbriae cannot act on an oocyte that has not been released yet.","This order starts in the tube, which reverses the direction of travel. The oocyte begins in the ovary and moves into the tube.","Correct. Released from the ovary, swept by the fimbriae, then into the infundibulum."],"lt":"t-reproductive-female","lid":"c40","comp":147,"competencyId":"w6-ovum-path"},{"id":"L02633","dok":2,"q":"After the oocyte enters the infundibulum, which two regions does it pass through, in order, before reaching the uterine cavity?","a":"The ampulla, then the isthmus.","options":["The ampulla, then the isthmus","The fimbriae, then the isthmus","The ampulla, then the cervix","The isthmus, then the ampulla"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The oocyte follows one route down the uterine tube. From the funnel-shaped infundibulum it moves into the ampulla, the widest part, where sperm may fertilize it. It then passes through the isthmus, the narrow part that joins the uterus, and reaches the uterine cavity.","notes":["Correct. Ampulla first, then isthmus, then the uterine cavity.","The fimbriae are on the rim of the infundibulum and act before the oocyte enters the tube. They are behind it at this point, not ahead.","The ampulla is right, but the cervix is not part of the tube. It is the lowest part of the uterus, below the uterine cavity, so the oocyte would not pass through it on the way in.","This reverses the two regions. The wide ampulla comes first after the funnel, and the narrow isthmus comes last, next to the uterus."],"lt":"t-reproductive-female","lid":"c41","comp":147,"competencyId":"w6-ovum-path"},{"id":"L02634","dok":2,"q":"Which order lists the layers of the uterine wall from the outer surface to the inner lining?","a":"Perimetrium, myometrium, endometrium.","options":["Perimetrium, myometrium, endometrium","Myometrium, perimetrium, endometrium","Perimetrium, endometrium, myometrium","Endometrium, myometrium, perimetrium"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"From outside to inside, the uterine wall has the perimetrium (the outer serous layer), the myometrium (the thick smooth muscle in the middle), and the endometrium (the inner vascular lining). The prefixes help: peri means around, myo means muscle, and endo means within.","notes":["Correct. Perimetrium outside, myometrium in the middle, endometrium inside.","This puts the muscle layer on the outside. The perimetrium is the outermost covering, so the myometrium cannot come first.","This swaps the inner two layers. The lining faces the cavity, so the endometrium is innermost, with the myometrium between it and the perimetrium.","This is the correct sequence run backward, from the lining out to the surface. It would be right if the question asked for the order from inside to outside."],"lt":"t-reproductive-female","lid":"c42","comp":148,"competencyId":"w6-uterus"},{"id":"L02635","dok":2,"q":"Which order lists the regions of the uterus from the top down to the opening into the vagina?","a":"Fundus, body, cervix.","options":["Cervix, body, fundus","Body, fundus, cervix","Fundus, cervix, body","Fundus, body, cervix"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The uterus is shaped like an upside-down pear. The fundus is the dome-shaped top above the entries of the uterine tubes, the body is the tapered central portion, and the cervix is the narrow lowest portion that opens into the vagina.","notes":["This is the correct order run from the bottom up. It would be right if the question asked for the order from the vagina upward.","This places the body above the fundus. The fundus is the topmost region, above the tube entries, so it comes first.","This puts the cervix in the middle. The cervix is the narrow neck at the bottom that opens into the vagina, so it comes last.","Correct. Fundus at the top, body in the middle, cervix at the bottom."],"lt":"t-reproductive-female","lid":"c43","comp":148,"competencyId":"w6-uterus"},{"id":"L02636","dok":2,"q":"Which order lists the parts of the mammary gland from the largest division down to the milk-secreting sac?","a":"Lobes, lobules, alveoli.","options":["Lobes, alveoli, lobules","Lobes, lobules, alveoli","Lobules, lobes, alveoli","Alveoli, lobules, lobes"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The mammary gland is nested. Each breast has 15 to 20 lobes, each lobe is divided into smaller lobules, and each lobule holds clusters of alveoli, the small sacs that secrete milk. Going from largest to smallest runs lobes, lobules, alveoli.","notes":["This places the alveoli above the lobules. The alveoli sit inside the lobules, so they are the smallest unit and come last.","Correct. Lobes contain lobules, and lobules contain alveoli.","This puts lobules before lobes, but lobules are subdivisions of a lobe, so the lobe is larger and comes first.","This is the correct order run from smallest to largest. It would be right if the question asked for the order from the milk sac outward."],"lt":"t-reproductive-female","lid":"c44","comp":150,"competencyId":"w6-mammary"},{"id":"L02637","dok":2,"q":"Name, in order, the female gonad, the structure left in it after a follicle releases its oocyte, and the funnel-shaped tube end that receives that oocyte.","a":"The ovary, the corpus luteum, and the infundibulum.","options":["The ovary, the corpus luteum, and the infundibulum","The uterus, the corpus luteum, and the infundibulum","The ovary, the corpus luteum, and the ampulla","The ovary, the corpus albicans, and the infundibulum"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The ovary is the female gonad. After ovulation, the follicle that released the oocyte becomes the corpus luteum, the yellow body. The oocyte is swept by the fimbriae into the infundibulum, the funnel-shaped end of the uterine tube. Each choice changes one of these three pieces, so check each piece in turn.","notes":["Correct. Ovary, corpus luteum, and infundibulum.","The uterus is not a gonad. It is the organ where an embryo implants. The other two pieces are right, but the gonad has to be the ovary.","The ampulla is the wide middle part of the tube where fertilization usually occurs. The oocyte reaches it after the infundibulum, so it is one step too far along.","The corpus albicans is the white scar that forms later, when the corpus luteum degenerates. It is the structure one step after the corpus luteum, so pick it for what the corpus luteum becomes."],"lt":"t-reproductive-female","lid":"c45","comp":146,"competencyId":"w6-ovary"},{"id":"L02640","dok":1,"q":"Which region of the ovary contains the ovarian follicles?","a":"The ovarian cortex.","options":["The ovarian medulla","The ovarian cortex","The germinal epithelium","The tunica albuginea"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The ovarian cortex is the region just beneath the tunica albuginea, and it holds the follicles at their different stages of development. Deeper still is the medulla, which carries the blood vessels and nerves. When you look for follicles on a slide, look in the outer cortex, not the center.","notes":["The ovarian medulla is the deep central region of looser connective tissue that carries blood vessels and nerves. Pick it for where the ovary's vessels run.","Correct. The follicles sit in the ovarian cortex.","The germinal epithelium is the layer of low cuboidal cells covering the surface of the ovary. Despite its name, it does not hold the follicles. Pick it for the surface layer.","The tunica albuginea is the whitish capsule of dense connective tissue just beneath the germinal epithelium. Pick it for the capsule of the ovary."],"lt":"t-reproductive-female","lid":"c3-x2","comp":146,"competencyId":"w6-ovary"},{"id":"L02641","dok":1,"q":"If no pregnancy occurs, the corpus luteum degenerates into a white scar in the ovary. What is that scar called?","a":"The corpus albicans.","options":["The mature follicle","The corpus albicans","The tunica albuginea","The ovarian medulla"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The corpus luteum, or yellow body, is temporary. If no pregnancy occurs, it breaks down and is replaced by a white scar of connective tissue called the corpus albicans, meaning white body. Both are found in the ovary on a slide and are named for their color.","notes":["The mature follicle is the large, fluid-filled follicle that releases the oocyte at ovulation. It comes before the corpus luteum, not after. Pick it for the follicle ready to ovulate.","Correct. The corpus luteum degenerates into the corpus albicans.","The tunica albuginea is also white, and its name shares the root alb for white, but it is the capsule just beneath the ovary's surface, not a scar. Pick it for the capsule of the ovary.","The ovarian medulla is the deep central region that carries vessels and nerves. Pick it for the innermost region of the ovary."],"lt":"t-reproductive-female","lid":"c4-x2","comp":146,"competencyId":"w6-ovary"},{"id":"L02643","dok":1,"q":"Which description fits the fimbriae?","a":"Finger-like projections on the infundibulum that sweep the oocyte into the tube.","options":["Small sacs in the breast that secrete milk","Recesses of the vagina surrounding the cervix","Folds of peritoneum that drape over the uterus","Finger-like projections that sweep the oocyte in"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The fimbriae are a fringe of finger-like projections around the rim of the infundibulum, the funnel-shaped end of the uterine tube. Because the tube is not attached to the ovary, the fimbriae sweep over its surface at ovulation and draw the released oocyte into the tube.","notes":["This describes the alveoli of the mammary gland. Pick this description when the question names the alveoli.","This describes the fornix of the vagina. Pick this description when the question names the fornix.","This describes the broad ligament, the peritoneal fold over the uterus and tubes. Pick this description when the question names the broad ligament.","Correct. The fimbriae are the finger-like projections that sweep the oocyte into the tube."],"lt":"t-reproductive-female","lid":"c6-x2","comp":147,"competencyId":"w6-ovum-path"},{"id":"L02645","dok":1,"q":"Which set names the three regions of the uterus?","a":"The fundus, body, and cervix.","options":["The cortex, medulla, and pelvis","The fundus, body, and cervix","The head, body, and tail","The cardia, fundus, and pylorus"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The uterus has three regions: the fundus, the rounded top above the entry of the uterine tubes; the body, the main central part that holds the uterine cavity; and the cervix, the narrow lower part that projects into the vagina. Other organs reuse some of these words, since the stomach also has a fundus and a body and the pancreas has a head, body, and tail. Only the uterus has a cervix.","notes":["The cortex, medulla, and pelvis are regions of the kidney. Pick this set for a question about kidney regions.","Correct. Fundus, body, and cervix are the regions of the uterus.","The head, body, and tail are the regions of the pancreas. Pick this set for a question about the pancreas.","The cardia, fundus, and pylorus are regions of the stomach. The shared word fundus is the trap. Pick this set for a question about the stomach."],"lt":"t-reproductive-female","lid":"c8-x2","comp":148,"competencyId":"w6-uterus"},{"id":"L02647","dok":1,"q":"Which layer of the uterine wall is partly shed during menstruation?","a":"The endometrium.","options":["The tunica albuginea","The perimetrium","The endometrium","The myometrium"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The endometrium is the inner mucous lining of the uterus, next to the uterine cavity. It has two layers. The stratum functionalis, next to the cavity, is the part shed during menstruation, and the deeper stratum basalis stays in place and rebuilds it each cycle.","notes":["The tunica albuginea is the capsule of the ovary, not a layer of the uterus. Pick it for the whitish capsule beneath the ovary's surface.","The perimetrium is the outer serous covering of the uterus. It is not shed. Pick it for the outermost layer.","Correct. The endometrium is the layer that sheds in menstruation.","The myometrium is the smooth muscle layer. It stays intact each cycle. Pick it for the layer that contracts during labor."],"lt":"t-reproductive-female","lid":"c10-x2","comp":148,"competencyId":"w6-uterus"},{"id":"L02649","dok":2,"q":"Which female external structure is homologous to the penis?","a":"The clitoris.","options":["The vestibule","The clitoris","The mons pubis","The labia majora"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Homologous structures develop from the same embryonic tissue. The clitoris and the penis both form from the same embryonic swelling and both contain erectile tissue that fills with blood during arousal. That is why the clitoris is the female homolog of the penis.","notes":["The vestibule is the area between the labia minora that holds the urethral and vaginal openings. Pick it for that space.","Correct. The clitoris is the homolog of the penis.","The mons pubis is the fatty cushion over the pubic symphysis. Pick it for the fatty pad at the front of the vulva.","The labia majora are homologous to the scrotum, not the penis. Pick them for the female homolog of the scrotum."],"lt":"t-reproductive-female","lid":"c12-x2","comp":149,"competencyId":"w6-vagina-vulva"},{"id":"L02650","dok":1,"q":"What is another name for the greater vestibular glands?","a":"Bartholin's glands.","options":["Montgomery's glands","Bartholin's glands","Skene's glands","Cowper's glands"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The greater vestibular glands are also called Bartholin's glands. They sit on each side of the vaginal opening and secrete lubricating mucus into the vestibule. Eponyms are easy to mix up, so link Bartholin with the vagina and Cowper with the male urethra.","notes":["Montgomery's glands are small glands in the areola of the breast. Pick them for the bumps on the areola around the nipple.","Correct. Bartholin's glands is another name for the greater vestibular glands.","Skene's glands are the paraurethral glands that open near the urethral orifice, not beside the vaginal opening. Pick them if the question asked about the small glands beside the female urethra.","Cowper's glands are the male bulbourethral glands, the homologs of the greater vestibular glands. Pick them for the small glands below the male prostate."],"lt":"t-reproductive-female","lid":"c13-x2","comp":149,"competencyId":"w6-vagina-vulva"},{"id":"L02651","dok":1,"q":"The mammary glands are modified versions of which kind of gland?","a":"Sweat glands.","options":["Sweat glands","Endocrine glands","Sebaceous glands","Salivary glands"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The mammary glands develop from the skin and are modified sweat glands. Like sweat glands, they are exocrine glands that release a product through ducts onto the skin surface, in this case milk through the lactiferous ducts at the nipple.","notes":["Correct. The mammary glands are modified sweat glands.","Endocrine glands release hormones into the blood with no ducts. The mammary glands release milk through ducts, so they are exocrine. Pick endocrine glands for organs like the thyroid or adrenal glands.","Sebaceous glands are oil glands of the skin that usually empty into hair follicles. They are a different skin gland. Pick them for a question about where sebum comes from.","Salivary glands are digestive glands around the mouth that secrete saliva. Pick them for the parotid, submandibular, or sublingual glands."],"lt":"t-reproductive-female","lid":"c14-x2","comp":150,"competencyId":"w6-mammary"},{"id":"L02653","dok":2,"q":"The mature follicle releases the oocyte at ovulation. Which structures catch it first?","a":"The fimbriae and infundibulum.","options":["Isthmus and uterine cavity","Fimbriae and infundibulum","Fundus and uterine cavity","Ampulla and isthmus"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The uterine tube is not attached to the ovary, so the released oocyte has to be caught. The fimbriae sweep over the ovary and draw the oocyte into the infundibulum, the funnel-shaped opening of the tube. Only after that does it move on to the ampulla and the isthmus.","notes":["The isthmus and uterine cavity are at the far end of the route, near and inside the uterus. They would be the last stops for an oocyte or early embryo.","Correct. The fimbriae sweep the oocyte into the infundibulum.","The fundus and uterine cavity are parts of the uterus. The oocyte reaches the uterine cavity only at the end of its trip down the tube.","The ampulla and isthmus come later on the route. The oocyte reaches the ampulla only after it has entered through the infundibulum."],"lt":"t-reproductive-female","lid":"c16-x2","comp":147,"competencyId":"w6-ovum-path"},{"id":"L02654","dok":2,"q":"Which uterine ligament runs from the uterus to the labia majora?","a":"The round ligament.","options":["The uterosacral ligament","The cardinal ligament","The broad ligament","The round ligament"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The round ligaments are paired fibrous cords that leave the uterus just below the uterine tubes, travel forward through the inguinal canal, and end in the labia majora. Each uterine ligament is easiest to learn by naming what it connects the uterus to.","notes":["The uterosacral ligament connects the uterus to the sacrum. Pick it for the posterior anchor of the uterus.","The cardinal ligament runs from the lateral pelvic wall to the cervix and vagina. Pick it for the ligament that anchors the cervix to the side walls.","The broad ligament is the fold of peritoneum that attaches the uterus to the lateral pelvic walls. Pick it for the peritoneal sheet over the uterus and tubes.","Correct. The round ligament runs from the uterus to the labia majora."],"lt":"t-reproductive-female","lid":"c17-x2","comp":148,"competencyId":"w6-uterus"},{"id":"L02655","dok":1,"q":"At which opening does the cervical canal open into the vagina?","a":"The external os.","options":["The internal os","The fornix","The external os","The vaginal orifice"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The cervical canal runs through the cervix and has two openings. The lower one, the external os, opens into the vagina. The upper one, the internal os, opens into the uterine cavity. External means toward the outside, so the external os is the end that faces the vagina.","notes":["The internal os is the upper opening of the cervical canal, into the uterine cavity. Pick it if the question asked where the canal opens into the uterus.","The fornix is the vaginal recess that surrounds the cervix, not an opening of the canal. Pick it for the gutter around the cervix.","Correct. The external os opens into the vagina.","The vaginal orifice is where the vagina opens into the vestibule, at the bottom of the tract. Pick it for the external opening of the vagina."],"lt":"t-reproductive-female","lid":"c18-x2","comp":148,"competencyId":"w6-uterus"},{"id":"L02657","dok":2,"q":"Which female external structure is homologous to the male scrotum?","a":"The labia majora.","options":["The mons pubis","The clitoris","The vestibule","The labia majora"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The labia majora and the scrotum develop from the same paired swellings in the embryo. In males these swellings fuse to form the scrotum. In females they stay separate as the two larger, hair-covered skin folds of the vulva.","notes":["The mons pubis is the fatty cushion over the pubic symphysis. Pick it for the fatty pad at the front of the vulva.","The clitoris is homologous to the penis, not the scrotum. Pick it for the female homolog of the penis.","The vestibule is the area between the labia minora that holds the urethral and vaginal openings. Pick it for that space.","Correct. The labia majora are the homolog of the scrotum."],"lt":"t-reproductive-female","lid":"c20-x2","comp":149,"competencyId":"w6-vagina-vulva"},{"id":"L02659","dok":2,"q":"An oocyte travels through the ampulla. Which part of the uterine tube does it pass through next on the way to the uterus?","a":"The isthmus.","options":["The uterine cavity","The fimbriae","The isthmus","The infundibulum"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The route through the tube runs infundibulum, ampulla, isthmus, and then the uterine cavity. After the wide ampulla, the oocyte or early embryo passes through the isthmus, the narrow segment that joins the tube to the uterus.","notes":["The uterine cavity is the destination, but it comes after the isthmus, not right after the ampulla. It is one step too far.","The fimbriae are on the rim of the infundibulum at the very start of the route. Pick them for what sweeps the oocyte into the tube.","Correct. The isthmus comes right after the ampulla.","The infundibulum is the funnel at the start of the tube, which the oocyte has already passed. Pick it for the structure that receives the oocyte from the ovary."],"lt":"t-reproductive-female","lid":"c22-x2","comp":147,"competencyId":"w6-ovum-path"},{"id":"L02660","dok":1,"q":"Which thin fold of mucous membrane partly closes the vaginal opening?","a":"The hymen.","options":["The labia minora","The hymen","The fornix","The vestibule"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The hymen is a thin fold of mucous membrane at the vaginal orifice that partly closes the opening. It varies widely in shape and thickness from person to person. It sits at the bottom of the vagina, where the vagina opens into the vestibule.","notes":["The labia minora are hairless skin folds that enclose the vestibule. They lie outside the vaginal opening and do not close it. Pick them for the inner folds of the vulva.","Correct. The hymen partly closes the vaginal opening.","The fornix is the recess at the top of the vagina around the cervix, at the opposite end from the opening. Pick it for the vaginal recess around the cervix.","The vestibule is the area between the labia minora into which the vagina opens. It is a space, not a fold of membrane. Pick it for the region holding the vaginal and urethral openings."],"lt":"t-reproductive-female","lid":"c23-x2","comp":149,"competencyId":"w6-vagina-vulva"},{"id":"L02661","dok":2,"q":"The broad ligament attaches to the ovary by which portion?","a":"The mesovarium.","options":["The suspensory ligament","The ovarian ligament","The mesovarium","The round ligament"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The broad ligament is the fold of peritoneum draped over the uterus and tubes. The part of it that attaches to the ovary is called the mesovarium. The prefix meso- is used for peritoneal folds that suspend an organ, just as the mesentery suspends the small intestine.","notes":["The suspensory ligament attaches the ovary to the lateral pelvic wall and carries the ovarian vessels. Pick it for the lateral attachment of the ovary.","The ovarian ligament is a separate cord that anchors the ovary to the uterus. Pick it for the ovary-to-uterus attachment.","Correct. The mesovarium is the part of the broad ligament that attaches to the ovary.","The round ligament runs from the uterus to the labia majora and does not attach to the ovary. Pick it for the cord that reaches the labia majora."],"lt":"t-reproductive-female","lid":"c24-x2","comp":146,"competencyId":"w6-ovary"},{"id":"L02663","dok":3,"q":"In pelvic inflammatory disease, infection can climb from the vagina through the uterus and tubes and reach the pelvic cavity. Which structure provides that open route into the cavity?","a":"The infundibulum of the uterine tube.","options":["The isthmus of the uterine tube","The external os of the cervix","The infundibulum of the tube","The fornix of the vagina"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The infundibulum opens into the pelvic cavity near the ovary instead of sealing onto it. That opening is how the fimbriae catch the oocyte, but it also leaves an open path from the outside into the pelvic cavity: vagina, cervix, uterus, uterine tube, and out through the infundibulum. An infection can climb that same route.","notes":["The isthmus is the narrow part of the tube next to the uterus. Infection passes through it, but it connects the ampulla to the uterus and does not open into the pelvic cavity.","The external os opens from the cervical canal into the vagina. It is part of the route, but it connects two parts of the tract and does not open into the pelvic cavity.","Correct. The open infundibulum connects the tract to the pelvic cavity.","The fornix is the recess of the vagina that rings the cervix. It is a dead end inside the vagina and does not open into the pelvic cavity."],"lt":"t-reproductive-female","lid":"c26-x2","comp":147,"competencyId":"w6-ovum-path"},{"id":"L02665","dok":2,"q":"Which pairing of uterine layer and disorder is correct?","a":"The myometrium, in uterine fibroids.","options":["The endometrium, in uterine fibroids","The myometrium, in endometriosis","The myometrium, in uterine fibroids","The perimetrium, in uterine fibroids"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Uterine fibroids are non-cancerous tumors of smooth muscle, and the smooth muscle of the uterus is the myometrium. Endometriosis, despite sounding similar, involves the endometrium, the lining, growing outside the uterus. Matching each disorder to the tissue it starts in sorts these out.","notes":["The endometrium is the lining, not muscle, so it is not where fibroids grow. The endometrium pairs with endometriosis, where lining tissue grows outside the uterus.","The myometrium is the muscle layer, but endometriosis involves lining tissue. This pairing mixes the layer of one disorder with the name of the other.","Correct. Fibroids are smooth muscle tumors of the myometrium.","The perimetrium is the thin outer serous layer, not muscle. Fibroids start in the myometrium beneath it. Pick the perimetrium for the outer covering of the uterus."],"lt":"t-reproductive-female","lid":"c28-x2","comp":148,"competencyId":"w6-uterus"},{"id":"L02667","dok":2,"q":"The endometrium has two layers. Which one stays in place each cycle and rebuilds the lining after menstruation?","a":"The stratum basalis.","options":["The stratum basalis","The perimetrium","The stratum functionalis","The myometrium"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The endometrium is built in two layers so that it can renew itself. The superficial stratum functionalis thickens each cycle and is shed in menstruation. The deeper stratum basalis is permanent and is never shed, so it regenerates a new functionalis each cycle.","notes":["Correct. The stratum basalis is permanent and rebuilds the lining.","The perimetrium is the outer serous layer of the uterus. Pick it for the outermost covering.","The stratum functionalis is the layer that is shed in menstruation, so it cannot be the one that stays behind. Pick it for the part of the lining lost during menstruation.","The myometrium is the smooth muscle layer outside the endometrium. It is not part of the lining. Pick it for the layer that contracts in labor."],"lt":"t-reproductive-female","lid":"c30-x2","comp":148,"competencyId":"w6-uterus"},{"id":"L02668","dok":2,"q":"Unlike a sperm, the oocyte cannot move on its own. What moves it along the uterine tube toward the uterus?","a":"Cilia and smooth muscle of the tube wall.","options":["Contractions of the uterine myometrium","Pressure from the ruptured follicle","Cilia and smooth muscle of the tube wall","Its own flagellum, as a sperm uses"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The lining of the uterine tube has ciliated cells, and the cilia beat toward the uterus. A layer of smooth muscle in the tube wall also squeezes in waves. Together they move the oocyte toward the uterus over a few days, because the oocyte has no tail of its own.","notes":["The myometrium is the muscle of the uterine wall, not of the tube, so it cannot move the oocyte along the tube. It is the layer that contracts during childbirth.","The rupture of the follicle releases the oocyte from the ovary into the pelvic cavity, but it does not push the oocyte down the tube.","Correct. Beating cilia and waves of smooth muscle in the tube wall carry the oocyte toward the uterus.","Only sperm have a flagellum. The oocyte has no tail, so it cannot swim. This choice comes from assuming both gametes move the same way."],"lt":"t-reproductive-female","lid":"d3-t-reproductive-female-1","comp":147,"competencyId":"w6-ovum-path"},{"id":"L02673","dok":2,"q":"Which kind of tissue makes up the myometrium, the thick middle layer of the uterine wall?","a":"Smooth muscle.","options":["Cardiac muscle","Dense regular connective tissue","Skeletal muscle","Smooth muscle"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The myometrium is the thick middle layer of the uterine wall, and it is made of smooth muscle. It is thickest in the fundus, and it is the layer that contracts during childbirth. Like the smooth muscle in other hollow organs, it contracts without conscious control.","notes":["Cardiac muscle is the branched, striated muscle joined by intercalated discs in the wall of the heart. It is not in the uterine wall.","Dense regular connective tissue forms tendons and ligaments. It resists pulling but cannot contract, so it cannot be a muscle layer.","Skeletal muscle is the voluntary, striated muscle attached to bones. It is not found in the uterine wall.","Correct. The myometrium is a thick layer of smooth muscle."],"lt":"t-reproductive-female","lid":"d3-t-reproductive-female-6","comp":148,"competencyId":"w6-uterus"},{"id":"L02674","dok":2,"q":"After ovulation, which glandular body forms in the ovary from the granulosa and theca cells left behind?","a":"The corpus luteum.","options":["The corpus albicans","The corpus luteum","The ovarian medulla","The germinal epithelium"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"At ovulation the mature follicle ruptures and releases the secondary oocyte. The granulosa and theca cells left behind become the corpus luteum, the yellow body, a large glandular body. If no pregnancy occurs, it later breaks down into the corpus albicans, a pale scar.","notes":["The corpus albicans is the white scar that forms later, when the corpus luteum breaks down. It is one step too far along.","Correct. The cells left behind after ovulation form the corpus luteum.","The ovarian medulla is the central core of loose connective tissue with the blood vessels and nerves. It is a region of the ovary, not a body that forms after ovulation.","The germinal epithelium is the single layer of cuboidal cells covering the ovary. It is the surface layer and does not form from the ruptured follicle."],"lt":"t-reproductive-female","lid":"d3x-t-reproductive-female-1","comp":146,"competencyId":"w6-ovary"},{"id":"N-m5-female-repro-191","dok":1,"q":"Which paired ligaments run from the lateral pelvic wall to the cervix and vagina?","a":"The cardinal ligaments.","options":["The cardinal ligaments","The round ligaments","The uterosacral ligaments","The ovarian ligaments"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The cardinal ligaments, also called the transverse cervical ligaments, run from the cervix and upper vagina out to the lateral pelvic walls. They are the main support that keeps the uterus from sliding down. The other uterine ligaments are named for where they go: the round ligaments to the labia majora and the uterosacral ligaments to the sacrum.","notes":["Correct. The cardinal ligaments anchor the cervix and vagina to the lateral pelvic walls.","The round ligaments run from the uterus forward to the labia majora. Pick them for the uterine ligament that reaches the labia majora.","The uterosacral ligaments run from the uterus back to the sacrum. Pick them for the posterior anchor of the uterus.","The ovarian ligaments tie each ovary to the uterus. Pick them for the attachment between the ovary and the uterus."],"comp":148,"competencyId":"w6-uterus"},{"id":"N-m5-female-repro-192","dok":1,"q":"What is the whitish capsule of dense connective tissue just beneath the surface epithelium of the ovary called?","a":"The tunica albuginea.","options":["The ovarian cortex","The perimetrium","The corpus albicans","The tunica albuginea"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The tunica albuginea is a tough, whitish capsule of dense connective tissue that lies just under the germinal epithelium and wraps the ovary. Albuginea refers to its white color. The testis has a capsule with the same name, which helps you remember that both gonads are wrapped this way.","notes":["The ovarian cortex lies beneath the capsule and contains the follicles. Pick it for the region that holds the follicles.","The perimetrium is the outer serous layer of the uterus, not the ovary. Pick it for the outermost layer of the uterine wall.","The corpus albicans is also white, but it is a scar inside the ovary left when a corpus luteum breaks down. Pick it for what remains of a corpus luteum.","Correct. The tunica albuginea is the whitish capsule of the ovary."],"comp":146,"competencyId":"w6-ovary"},{"id":"N-m5-female-repro-193","dok":1,"q":"Which region of the ovary is the deep core of looser connective tissue that carries blood vessels and nerves?","a":"The ovarian medulla.","options":["The ovarian cortex","The ovarian medulla","The tunica albuginea","The germinal epithelium"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The ovarian medulla is the central region of the ovary. It is made of looser connective tissue and holds the blood vessels and nerves that serve the organ. Medulla means the middle or inner part, the same word used for the inner region of the kidney and the adrenal gland.","notes":["The ovarian cortex is the outer region that holds the follicles. Pick it if the question asked where the follicles are found.","Correct. The medulla is the central core with the vessels and nerves.","The tunica albuginea is the whitish capsule just beneath the surface. Pick it for the capsule of the ovary.","The germinal epithelium is the layer of low cuboidal cells on the outer surface. Pick it for the outermost layer."],"comp":146,"competencyId":"w6-ovary"},{"id":"N-m5-female-repro-194","dok":1,"q":"On an ovary slide, which structure is the large, fluid-filled follicle ready to release its oocyte at ovulation?","a":"The mature follicle.","options":["The germinal epithelium","The corpus albicans","The corpus luteum","The mature follicle"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"As a follicle develops in the ovarian cortex it grows and fills with fluid. The largest stage, the mature follicle, bulges at the surface of the ovary and releases its oocyte at ovulation. What is left of it afterward becomes the corpus luteum.","notes":["The germinal epithelium is the thin layer of cuboidal cells covering the ovary, not a follicle. Pick it for the surface layer of the ovary.","The corpus albicans is the white scar left when the corpus luteum breaks down. Pick it for the scar tissue in the ovary.","The corpus luteum forms from the follicle after ovulation, so it no longer holds an oocyte. Pick it for the structure left behind after the oocyte is released.","Correct. The mature follicle is the large, fluid-filled follicle about to ovulate."],"comp":146,"competencyId":"w6-ovary"},{"id":"N-m5-female-repro-195","dok":2,"q":"Which layer of the endometrium is shed during menstruation?","a":"The stratum functionalis.","options":["The perimetrium","The stratum functionalis","The myometrium","The stratum basalis"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The endometrium has two layers. The stratum functionalis, next to the uterine cavity, is the part shed during menstruation. The stratum basalis, next to the myometrium, stays in place and rebuilds the functionalis each cycle.","notes":["The perimetrium is the outer serous covering of the uterus. Pick it for the outermost layer of the uterine wall.","Correct. The stratum functionalis is shed in menstruation.","The myometrium is the smooth muscle layer outside the endometrium. It is not shed. Pick it for the layer that contracts during labor.","The stratum basalis is the permanent deep layer that stays behind and rebuilds the lining. Pick it for the layer that regenerates the endometrium."],"comp":148,"competencyId":"w6-uterus"},{"id":"N-m5-female-repro-196","dok":1,"q":"The perineum is divided into two triangles. What is the anterior triangle, which holds the external genitalia, called?","a":"The urogenital triangle.","options":["The vestibule","The anal triangle","The femoral triangle","The urogenital triangle"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"A line drawn between the two ischial tuberosities splits the diamond-shaped perineum into two triangles. The anterior one is the urogenital triangle, which holds the external genitalia and the urethral opening. The posterior one is the anal triangle, which holds the anus.","notes":["The vestibule is a small space between the labia minora that lies within the urogenital triangle. Pick it for the area that holds the vaginal and urethral openings.","The anal triangle is the posterior half of the perineum and holds the anus. Pick it for the back triangle.","The femoral triangle is in the upper anterior thigh and holds the femoral vessels and nerve. It is not part of the perineum. Pick it for a lower limb question.","Correct. The urogenital triangle is the anterior part of the perineum."],"comp":149,"competencyId":"w6-vagina-vulva"},{"id":"N-m5-female-repro-197","dok":1,"q":"The breast lies on the anterior chest wall. Which muscle does most of it rest on?","a":"The pectoralis major.","options":["The pectoralis major","The trapezius","The latissimus dorsi","The deltoid"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Each breast lies in the subcutaneous tissue in front of the pectoralis major, the large fan-shaped muscle of the anterior chest. The suspensory ligaments run from the skin through the breast to the fascia over this muscle and support the breast.","notes":["Correct. Most of the breast rests on the pectoralis major.","The trapezius is a large muscle of the upper back and neck, so it lies on the back of the body, not under the breast.","The latissimus dorsi is the broad muscle of the lower back that wraps toward the armpit. It is on the back, not under the breast on the anterior chest.","The deltoid caps the shoulder and covers the shoulder joint. It sits lateral to the chest, not under the breast."],"comp":150,"competencyId":"w6-mammary"},{"id":"N-m5-female-repro-198","dok":1,"q":"What is the interior space inside the body of the uterus called?","a":"The uterine cavity.","options":["The vestibule","The vaginal fornix","The cervical canal","The uterine cavity"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The uterine cavity is the space inside the body of the uterus, lined by the endometrium. The uterine tubes open into it at the top, and below it narrows into the cervical canal at the internal os.","notes":["The vestibule is the area between the labia minora where the vagina and urethra open. Pick it for that external space.","The vaginal fornix is the recess of the vagina around the cervix. Pick it for the vaginal recess, not the inside of the uterus.","The cervical canal is the passage through the cervix, below the uterine cavity. Pick it for the interior of the cervix.","Correct. The uterine cavity is the space inside the body of the uterus."],"comp":148,"competencyId":"w6-uterus"},{"id":"N-m5-female-repro-199","dok":2,"q":"In the female pelvis, the uterus lies between which two organs?","a":"The urinary bladder in front and the rectum behind.","options":["The pubic symphysis in front and the bladder behind","The bladder in front and the kidneys behind","The rectum in front and the bladder behind","The bladder in front and the rectum behind"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The uterus is a thick-walled, pear-shaped organ in the pelvic cavity. The urinary bladder sits anterior to it and the rectum sits posterior to it. The peritoneum dips between them to form the vesicouterine pouch in front and the rectouterine pouch behind.","notes":["The bladder lies directly behind the pubic symphysis, so the bladder, not the uterus, is the next organ back from the pubic bone. The uterus lies behind the bladder.","The kidneys are high in the posterior abdomen, well above the pelvis. The organ behind the uterus is the rectum. Pick the kidneys for a question about retroperitoneal organs of the abdomen.","This is the right pair of organs in the wrong order. The bladder is anterior, just behind the pubic symphysis, and the rectum is posterior, in front of the sacrum.","Correct. Bladder in front, rectum behind."],"comp":148,"competencyId":"w6-uterus"},{"id":"N-m5-female-repro-200","dok":2,"q":"Within the vestibule, how is the urethral orifice positioned relative to the vaginal orifice?","a":"It is anterior to the vaginal orifice.","options":["Lateral to the vaginal orifice","Posterior to the vaginal orifice","Anterior to the vaginal orifice","Inside the vaginal orifice"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Both the urethra and the vagina open into the vestibule, the area between the labia minora. The urethral orifice lies in front, between the clitoris and the vaginal orifice, and the vaginal orifice lies behind it. The anus is farther back still, outside the vestibule.","notes":["The greater vestibular glands open beside the vaginal orifice, on each side. Pick lateral if the question asked where those glands open.","Posterior to the vaginal orifice lies the rest of the perineum and then the anus, not the urethra. This choice reverses the two openings.","Correct. The urethral orifice is anterior to the vaginal orifice.","The urethra has its own separate opening and does not empty into the vagina. This choice comes from assuming the two tubes share an exit."],"comp":149,"competencyId":"w6-vagina-vulva"},{"id":"N-m5-female-repro-201","dok":2,"q":"What type of epithelium forms the germinal epithelium on the surface of the ovary?","a":"Simple cuboidal epithelium.","options":["Stratified squamous epithelium","Transitional epithelium","Simple columnar epithelium","Simple cuboidal epithelium"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The germinal epithelium is a single layer of cuboidal cells covering the ovary, so it is a simple cuboidal epithelium. Despite its name, it does not make oocytes; the oocytes develop in follicles in the cortex beneath it. Under it lies the tunica albuginea, the dense connective tissue capsule.","notes":["Stratified squamous epithelium has many layers with flat cells on top. It lines the vagina and covers the skin. Pick it for a surface that takes friction.","Transitional epithelium stretches and lines the urinary bladder and ureters. Pick it for an organ that fills and empties with urine.","Simple columnar epithelium is a single layer of tall cells. It lines the stomach and intestines. Pick it for the lining of the digestive tract below the esophagus.","Correct. The germinal epithelium is simple cuboidal."],"comp":146,"competencyId":"w6-ovary"},{"id":"L04455","dok":1,"q":"Which organs are the female gonads, the organs that produce oocytes and secrete hormones?","a":"The ovaries.","options":["The ovaries","The greater vestibular glands","The uterine tubes","The mammary glands"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The ovaries are the paired, almond-shaped female gonads, one on each side of the uterus. They produce oocytes, the female sex cells, which develop inside follicles in the ovarian cortex. The ovaries also secrete hormones, so they are endocrine glands as well as reproductive organs.","notes":["Correct. The ovaries produce oocytes and secrete hormones.","The greater vestibular glands sit beside the vaginal opening and secrete lubricating mucus. Choose them if the question asked which glands of the vulva produce mucus.","The uterine tubes catch the released oocyte and carry it toward the uterus, but they do not produce it. Pick them if the question asked where fertilization usually takes place.","The mammary glands are modified sweat glands in the breast that produce milk, not oocytes. Pick them if the question asked which glands are drained by lactiferous ducts."],"lt":"t-w6-ovary","lid":"w6-ovary-r1","comp":146,"competencyId":"w6-ovary"},{"id":"L04456","dok":1,"q":"What is an ovarian follicle?","a":"A structure in the ovarian cortex that holds an oocyte at a stage of development.","options":["A fringe at the end of the uterine tube that sweeps the oocyte inward","A scar of connective tissue left after the corpus luteum breaks down","A fold of peritoneum that attaches the ovary to the broad ligament","A structure in the ovarian cortex holding an oocyte as it develops"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"An ovarian follicle is a small structure in the cortex of the ovary, made of an oocyte surrounded by one or more layers of supporting cells. Follicles grow through stages, and at ovulation a mature follicle releases its oocyte. The follicles are always found in the cortex, while the deeper medulla holds the blood vessels and nerves.","notes":["This describes the fimbriae, the finger-like projections of the infundibulum. Pick them if the question asked what sweeps the released oocyte into the uterine tube.","This describes the corpus albicans, the white scar left when a corpus luteum degenerates. Choose it if the question asked what the corpus luteum becomes.","This describes the mesovarium, the part of the broad ligament that holds the ovary. Pick it if the question asked how the broad ligament attaches to the ovary.","Correct. A follicle is an oocyte and its surrounding cells, found in the ovarian cortex."],"lt":"t-w6-ovary","lid":"w6-ovary-r2","comp":146,"competencyId":"w6-ovary"},{"id":"L04457","dok":1,"q":"Which list orders the stages of an ovarian follicle from earliest to most mature?","a":"Primordial, primary, secondary, then the vesicular (Graafian) follicle.","options":["Primordial, primary, secondary, vesicular","Vesicular, secondary, primary, primordial","Primary, primordial, secondary, vesicular","Primordial, secondary, primary, vesicular"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"A follicle starts as a primordial follicle, a primary oocyte with one layer of flat follicle cells. It becomes a primary follicle when those cells turn cuboidal and the zona pellucida forms, then a secondary follicle with several layers of granulosa cells and small pockets of fluid. The last stage is the vesicular (mature, Graafian) follicle, with one large fluid-filled antrum, ready to release its oocyte at ovulation.","notes":["Correct. The follicle grows from primordial to primary to secondary to vesicular.","This is the right sequence written backward, from mature to earliest. It would be correct only if the question asked for the order from the most mature stage back to the start.","This puts primary before primordial. Primordial means existing from the beginning, so the primordial follicle is the starting stage; students pick this because primary also sounds like first.","This swaps primary and secondary. The follicle is primary before it becomes secondary, just as the words suggest."],"lt":"t-w6-ovary","lid":"w6-ovary-r3","comp":146,"competencyId":"w6-ovary"},{"id":"L04458","dok":2,"q":"What structure is left in the ovary after ovulation, and what does it later become?","a":"The corpus luteum, which later degenerates into a scar called the corpus albicans.","options":["The corpus luteum, which later becomes a new primordial follicle","The corpus luteum, which later becomes the corpus albicans","The corpus albicans, which later becomes the corpus luteum","The vesicular follicle, which later becomes a primary follicle"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"After a mature follicle releases its oocyte, the remaining follicle cells form the corpus luteum, whose name means yellow body. The corpus luteum secretes hormones for a time, and if no pregnancy occurs it degenerates. What remains is a whitish scar of connective tissue called the corpus albicans, which means white body.","notes":["The corpus luteum is right, but it ends as a scar, not a new follicle. Primordial follicles are already present in the ovary and are not made from old corpora lutea.","Correct. The corpus luteum forms after ovulation and degenerates into the corpus albicans.","This reverses the sequence. The yellow body forms first and the white scar is what is left when it breaks down; students mix up which Latin color word comes first.","The vesicular follicle is the mature follicle that ruptures at ovulation, so it is the structure before ovulation, not after. Follicles never go backward to an earlier stage."],"lt":"t-w6-ovary","lid":"w6-ovary-r4","comp":146,"competencyId":"w6-ovary"},{"id":"L04459","dok":3,"q":"Which ligament anchors the ovary to the uterus, and which attaches it to the pelvic wall?","a":"The ovarian ligament anchors it to the uterus; the suspensory ligament attaches it to the pelvic wall.","options":["Suspensory ligament to the uterus; ovarian ligament to the pelvic wall","Ovarian ligament to the uterus; suspensory ligament to the pelvic wall","Round ligament to the uterus; cardinal ligament to the pelvic wall","Broad ligament to the uterus; uterosacral ligament to the pelvic wall"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The ovarian ligament is a short cord that runs from the ovary medially to the side of the uterus. The suspensory ligament is a fold that runs from the ovary laterally to the pelvic wall and carries the ovarian blood vessels. Together they hold the ovary between the uterus and the side wall of the pelvis.","notes":["This swaps the two ligaments. The ovarian ligament is the one that reaches the uterus, which matches its short medial path; students pick this when they forget which end each ligament attaches to.","Correct. The ovarian ligament ties the ovary to the uterus, and the suspensory ligament ties it to the pelvic wall.","The round ligament runs from the uterus to the labia majora, and the cardinal ligament runs from the pelvic wall to the cervix and vagina. Neither one holds the ovary; pick the round ligament if the question asked which ligament ends in the labia majora.","The broad ligament is a fold of peritoneum that drapes over the uterus and tubes, and the uterosacral ligament connects the uterus to the sacrum. Neither is the ovary's own tie to the uterus or the pelvic wall; choose the uterosacral ligament if the question asked what anchors the uterus to the sacrum."],"lt":"t-w6-ovary","lid":"w6-ovary-r5","comp":146,"competencyId":"w6-ovary"},{"id":"L04460","dok":1,"q":"Which structures sweep the released oocyte into the funnel of the uterine tube?","a":"The fimbriae.","options":["The ovarian ligament","The isthmus","The fimbriae","The ampulla"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The fimbriae are finger-like projections that fringe the infundibulum, the funnel-shaped end of the uterine tube. The infundibulum opens into the pelvic cavity rather than sealing onto the ovary. At ovulation, the fimbriae sweep over the ovary and draw the released oocyte into the tube.","notes":["The ovarian ligament anchors the ovary to the uterus. It holds the ovary in place but does not catch the oocyte; pick it if the question asked which ligament ties the ovary to the uterus.","The isthmus is the narrow, thick-walled part of the tube that joins it to the uterus, at the opposite end from the ovary. Choose it if the question asked which part of the tube connects to the uterus.","Correct. The fimbriae sweep the oocyte into the infundibulum.","The ampulla is the widest and longest part of the uterine tube, past the infundibulum. Pick it if the question asked where fertilization usually takes place."],"lt":"t-w6-ovum-path","lid":"w6-ovum-path-r1","comp":147,"competencyId":"w6-ovum-path"},{"id":"L04461","dok":1,"q":"In which part of the female reproductive tract does fertilization usually occur?","a":"The ampulla of the uterine tube.","options":["The uterine cavity","The ampulla of the uterine tube","The isthmus of the uterine tube","The infundibulum"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Fertilization usually takes place in the ampulla, the widest and longest part of the uterine tube. The oocyte is swept into the tube at the infundibulum and moves into the ampulla, where sperm that have traveled up from the uterus can meet it. The early embryo then continues through the isthmus to the uterus.","notes":["The uterine cavity is past the end of the tube. The oocyte reaches it only after the ampulla and isthmus, so fertilization has usually already happened by then.","Correct. The ampulla is the usual site of fertilization.","The isthmus is the narrow segment near the uterus that comes after the ampulla. Pick it if the question asked which part of the tube joins the uterus.","The infundibulum is the funnel-shaped opening of the tube, fringed with fimbriae, that the oocyte passes through before the ampulla. Choose it if the question asked which part of the tube opens into the pelvic cavity near the ovary."],"lt":"t-w6-ovum-path","lid":"w6-ovum-path-r2","comp":147,"competencyId":"w6-ovum-path"},{"id":"L04462","dok":2,"q":"How is the oocyte moved along the uterine tube toward the uterus?","a":"Beating cilia and smooth muscle waves of the tube move it.","options":["Pressure from the ruptured follicle pushes it","It swims with its own flagellum","Beating cilia and smooth muscle waves of the tube move it","Skeletal muscle in the tube wall squeezes it"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The oocyte cannot move on its own. The lining of the uterine tube has ciliated cells whose cilia beat toward the uterus, and a layer of smooth muscle in the wall squeezes in waves. Together they move the oocyte toward the uterus over a few days.","notes":["Ovulation releases the oocyte into the pelvic cavity near the tube, but that release does not push it along the tube. Once inside, the oocyte depends on the cilia and smooth muscle of the tube.","The oocyte has no flagellum. Sperm are the cells that swim with a tail, which is the mix-up behind this choice.","Correct. Cilia beating toward the uterus and waves of smooth muscle in the wall move the oocyte.","The wall of the uterine tube has smooth muscle, not skeletal muscle, so it is never under voluntary control. Skeletal muscle would be the answer for the external urethral sphincter."],"lt":"t-w6-ovum-path","lid":"w6-ovum-path-r4","comp":147,"competencyId":"w6-ovum-path"},{"id":"L04463","dok":3,"q":"Which list traces an oocyte from the ovary to the uterus?","a":"Ovary, fimbriae and infundibulum, ampulla, isthmus, uterus.","options":["Ovary, infundibulum, isthmus, ampulla, uterus","Ovary, ampulla, infundibulum, isthmus, uterus","Ovary, isthmus, ampulla, infundibulum, uterus","Ovary, infundibulum, ampulla, isthmus, uterus"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"At ovulation the oocyte leaves the ovary, and the fimbriae sweep it into the infundibulum, the funnel at the open end of the tube. It moves into the ampulla, the wide part where fertilization usually happens, then through the narrow isthmus. The isthmus joins the uterus, so the oocyte or early embryo enters the uterine cavity last.","notes":["This swaps the ampulla and isthmus. The wide ampulla is closer to the ovary, and the narrow isthmus is the part that connects to the uterus.","This puts the ampulla before the funnel. The oocyte must enter the tube through the infundibulum before it can reach the ampulla.","This lists the tube regions backward, starting from the end that joins the uterus. The infundibulum is the end nearest the ovary, so it must come first.","Correct. The oocyte passes from the funnel to the wide ampulla to the narrow isthmus and into the uterus."],"lt":"t-w6-ovum-path","lid":"w6-ovum-path-r5","comp":147,"competencyId":"w6-ovum-path"},{"id":"L04464","dok":1,"q":"Which list names the three regions of the uterus?","a":"The fundus, body, and cervix.","options":["Fundus, body, pylorus","Perimetrium, myometrium, endometrium","Fundus, ampulla, isthmus","Fundus, body, cervix"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The fundus is the dome-shaped top of the uterus, above where the uterine tubes enter. The body is the tapered central portion that surrounds the uterine cavity. The cervix is the narrow inferior portion that opens into the vagina.","notes":["The pylorus is the outlet region of the stomach. The stomach also has a fundus and a body, which is why this mix-up is tempting; pick this set for stomach regions.","These are the three layers of the uterine wall, from outer to inner, not its regions. Choose this list if the question asked for the wall layers.","The ampulla and isthmus are regions of the uterine tube, not the uterus. Pick this set if the question asked for regions of the uterine tube, along with the infundibulum.","Correct. The uterus is divided into the fundus, body, and cervix."],"lt":"t-w6-uterus","lid":"w6-uterus-r1","comp":148,"competencyId":"w6-uterus"},{"id":"L04465","dok":1,"q":"Which list names the three layers of the uterine wall from outer to inner?","a":"Perimetrium, myometrium, endometrium.","options":["Perimetrium, myometrium, endometrium","Myometrium, perimetrium, endometrium","Endometrium, myometrium, perimetrium","Perimetrium, endometrium, myometrium"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The prefixes give the order. Peri means around, so the perimetrium is the outer serous layer, part of the visceral peritoneum. Myo means muscle, so the myometrium is the thick smooth muscle middle layer, and endo means within, so the endometrium is the inner vascular lining.","notes":["Correct. Around, muscle, within: perimetrium, myometrium, endometrium.","This puts the muscle on the outside, but the serous perimetrium covers the outer surface. Students choose this when they forget that peri means around.","This is the right order written from inner to outer. It would be correct if the question asked for the layers starting at the uterine cavity.","This places the lining in the middle. The endometrium lines the uterine cavity, so it must be the innermost layer."],"lt":"t-w6-uterus","lid":"w6-uterus-r2","comp":148,"competencyId":"w6-uterus"},{"id":"L04466","dok":1,"q":"Which layer of the uterine wall is made of smooth muscle and is thickest in the fundus?","a":"The myometrium.","options":["The detrusor","The endometrium","The myometrium","The perimetrium"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The myometrium is the thick middle layer of the uterine wall, made of smooth muscle, and it is thickest in the fundus. It is the layer of the uterus that contracts during childbirth. Myo means muscle, which is the clue that this is the muscle layer.","notes":["The detrusor is smooth muscle too, but it is the muscle of the urinary bladder wall, not the uterus. Pick it if the question asked which muscle contracts to empty the bladder.","The endometrium is the inner mucous lining, not a muscle layer. Pick it if the question asked which layer sheds during menstruation.","Correct. The myometrium is the smooth muscle layer of the uterus, thickest in the fundus.","The perimetrium is the thin outer serous layer, part of the visceral peritoneum, not muscle. Choose it if the question asked which layer covers the outside of the uterus."],"lt":"t-w6-uterus","lid":"w6-uterus-r3","comp":148,"competencyId":"w6-uterus"},{"id":"L04467","dok":2,"q":"How is the endometrium built in two layers, and why does that matter each cycle?","a":"The stratum functionalis sheds and is rebuilt each cycle from the permanent stratum basalis.","options":["Both strata shed each cycle and are rebuilt from the myometrium","The myometrium sheds each cycle and is rebuilt from the perimetrium","The stratum basalis sheds each cycle and is rebuilt from the stratum functionalis","The stratum functionalis sheds each cycle and is rebuilt from the stratum basalis"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The endometrium has two layers. The stratum functionalis is the thick surface layer that builds up during the cycle and is shed during menstruation. The stratum basalis is the thin, permanent deeper layer that is never shed, and it regrows a new functionalis after each period, so the lining can renew month after month.","notes":["If both strata shed, the lining could not regrow. The myometrium is muscle and does not make new endometrium; the stratum basalis does that job.","The myometrium is the smooth muscle layer and the perimetrium is the outer serous layer, and neither sheds. Shedding happens only in the endometrium.","This reverses the two layers. The basalis must stay in place, because if it shed there would be nothing left to rebuild the lining from.","Correct. The functionalis is shed and the permanent basalis regrows it."],"lt":"t-w6-uterus","lid":"w6-uterus-r4","comp":148,"competencyId":"w6-uterus"},{"id":"L04483","dok":1,"q":"Which of these structures is NOT part of the vulva, the external genitalia?","a":"The cervix.","options":["The vestibule","The labia minora","The cervix","The mons pubis"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The vulva is the external genitalia: the mons pubis, labia majora, labia minora, clitoris, and vestibule. The cervix is the narrow inferior region of the uterus, which opens into the vagina deep inside the body. Because it is part of an internal organ, it is not part of the vulva.","notes":["The vestibule is the region between the labia minora that holds the vaginal and urethral openings, so it is part of the vulva. Pick it if the question asked where the urethral opening is found.","The labia minora are the smaller, hairless folds inside the labia majora and are part of the vulva. Choose them if the question asked which folds enclose the vestibule.","Correct. The cervix is part of the uterus, an internal organ, not the external genitalia.","The mons pubis is the cushion of fat over the pubic symphysis and is part of the vulva, so it does not fit a question asking for the exception. Pick it if the question asked what lies over the pubic symphysis."],"lt":"t-w6-vagina-vulva","lid":"w6-vagina-vulva-r2","comp":149,"competencyId":"w6-vagina-vulva"},{"id":"L04484","dok":2,"q":"Which vulvar structures are homologous to the scrotum and to the penis?","a":"The labia majora match the scrotum; the clitoris matches the penis.","options":["Labia majora match the scrotum; the clitoris matches the penis","The vestibule matches the scrotum; the labia minora match the penis","Labia majora match the penis; the clitoris matches the scrotum","Labia minora match the scrotum; the mons pubis matches the penis"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Homologous structures develop from the same embryonic tissue in males and females. The labia majora are hair-covered folds of skin that form from the same tissue as the scrotum. The clitoris is a small mass of erectile tissue that forms from the same tissue as the penis, which is why both contain erectile tissue.","notes":["Correct. The labia majora match the scrotum, and the clitoris matches the penis.","The vestibule is the space between the labia minora holding the vaginal and urethral openings, not a fold of skin like the scrotum. The erectile match for the penis is the clitoris, not the labia minora.","This swaps the pair. The clitoris is the erectile structure, so it matches the penis, and the skin folds of the labia majora match the scrotal sac.","The labia minora are the inner hairless folds, and the mons pubis is a fat pad over the pubic symphysis. Neither matches the scrotum or penis; the hair-covered outer folds, the labia majora, are the match for the scrotum."],"lt":"t-w6-vagina-vulva","lid":"w6-vagina-vulva-r3","comp":149,"competencyId":"w6-vagina-vulva"},{"id":"L04495","dok":1,"q":"Which statement correctly describes the mammary glands and how their milk reaches the nipple?","a":"They are modified sweat glands; lactiferous ducts carry milk toward the nipple.","options":["Endocrine glands; milk passes into the blood and then to the nipple","Modified sweat glands; the areola carries milk to the nipple","Modified sebaceous glands; suspensory ligaments carry milk to the nipple","Modified sweat glands; lactiferous ducts carry milk to the nipple"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The mammary glands are modified sweat glands that produce milk. Inside each gland, milk-secreting alveoli sit in lobules, the lobules sit in lobes, and each lobe drains through lactiferous ducts that open at the nipple. Naming them in that order also names the path the milk takes.","notes":["Mammary glands are exocrine glands, meaning they release their product through ducts onto a surface rather than into the blood. Choose endocrine if the question were about a ductless gland that releases hormones into the blood.","The areola is the ring of pigmented skin around the nipple; it carries no milk. Pick it if the question asked which circular area of skin surrounds the nipple.","Sebaceous glands secrete oil into hair follicles, and the suspensory (Cooper's) ligaments support the breast rather than carry milk. Pick the suspensory ligaments if the question asked what supports the breast tissue.","Correct. Mammary glands are modified sweat glands drained by lactiferous ducts."],"lt":"t-w6-mammary","lid":"w6-mammary-r1","comp":150,"competencyId":"w6-mammary"},{"id":"N-a17d-146-1","dok":3,"q":"An ovary slide shows an oocyte ringed by several layers of granulosa cells, with a theca outside and small pockets of fluid between the cells, but no single large cavity. Which stage is this, and what stage comes next?","a":"A secondary follicle, which becomes a mature follicle.","options":["A primordial follicle, which becomes a primary follicle","A mature follicle, which ruptures at ovulation","A primary follicle, which becomes a secondary follicle","A secondary follicle, which becomes a mature follicle"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Follicle stages are named by size, layers of cells, and whether there is a fluid space. A secondary follicle has several layers of granulosa cells, a theca around the outside, and small pockets of fluid between the granulosa cells. When those pockets become one large fluid-filled cavity, the antrum, the follicle is a mature (vesicular) follicle.","notes":["A primordial follicle is the smallest stage, a primary oocyte with one layer of flat follicle cells. It has no granulosa layers, theca, or fluid.","A mature follicle has one large antrum and bulges from the ovary surface. Small scattered fluid pockets with no single cavity mean it is not there yet.","A primary follicle has cuboidal granulosa cells around the oocyte and a forming zona pellucida, but no fluid pockets and no theca yet. The fluid pockets here mean it has moved past that stage.","Correct. Several granulosa layers, a theca, and small fluid pockets mark a secondary follicle, and the next stage has one large antrum."],"comp":146,"competencyId":"w6-ovary"},{"id":"N-a17d-146-2","dok":3,"q":"An adult ovary slide shows a pale, white patch of fibrous scar tissue in the cortex with no oocyte. What is this patch, and which structure did it form from?","a":"A corpus albicans, from a corpus luteum that broke down.","options":["A corpus luteum, formed from a ruptured mature follicle","A corpus albicans, from a corpus luteum that broke down","A mature follicle, formed from a secondary follicle","The tunica albuginea, formed from the germinal epithelium"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"After ovulation, the granulosa and theca cells left behind form the corpus luteum, a large glandular body. When the corpus luteum breaks down, it becomes the corpus albicans, which means white body. On a slide it is a pale scar of fibrous tissue in the cortex, with no oocyte.","notes":["The corpus luteum does form from the follicle cells left after ovulation, but it is a large glandular body, not a pale fibrous scar. The scar comes after it breaks down.","Correct. The corpus albicans is the pale fibrous scar left when a corpus luteum breaks down.","A mature follicle is a large structure with an oocyte and a fluid-filled antrum. A patch with no oocyte and only scar tissue is past ovulation.","The tunica albuginea is the dense connective tissue capsule just under the germinal epithelium, covering the whole ovary. It is a layer, not a patch inside the cortex."],"comp":146,"competencyId":"w6-ovary"},{"id":"N-a17d-147-1","dok":3,"q":"On a model, you trace a uterine tube from the end that drapes over the ovary toward the uterus, and your finger stops on the widest, longest stretch. Which regions has your finger already passed, and which region comes next?","a":"Past the fimbriae and infundibulum, with the isthmus next.","options":["Past the fimbriae and isthmus, with the intramural part next","Past the infundibulum only, with the fimbriae next","Past the fimbriae and infundibulum, with the isthmus next","Past the isthmus, with the infundibulum next"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"From the ovary toward the uterus, the tube runs fimbriae and infundibulum, then ampulla, then isthmus, then the short intramural part. The ampulla is the widest and longest part, so your finger is there. It has already passed the fringed funnel at the outer end, and the narrow, thick-walled isthmus is next.","notes":["The isthmus comes after the ampulla, so it cannot already be behind your finger. The intramural part comes after the isthmus.","The fimbriae are the fringe on the edge of the infundibulum at the outer end, so you pass them first. They do not come after the ampulla.","Correct. The widest, longest stretch is the ampulla, which comes after the fimbriae and infundibulum and before the narrow isthmus.","This runs the tube backward. The isthmus is the narrow part next to the uterus, so you would reach it after the ampulla, not before it."],"comp":147,"competencyId":"w6-ovum-path"},{"id":"N-a17d-148-1","dok":3,"q":"In an older patient, the uterus has slid down into the vagina. Weakness of which ligaments most likely allowed this, and where do they run?","a":"The cardinal ligaments, from the cervix to the lateral pelvic walls.","options":["The broad ligaments, a peritoneal fold to the lateral pelvic walls","The cardinal ligaments, from the cervix to the lateral pelvic walls","The round ligaments, from the uterus through the inguinal canal","The cardinal ligaments, from the uterus through the inguinal canal"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Four paired ligaments help hold the uterus, along with the pelvic floor muscles. The cardinal (transverse cervical) ligaments run from the cervix and upper vagina to the lateral pelvic walls, and they are the main support that keeps the uterus from sliding down. When the uterus drops into the vagina, the support that has failed is mainly this one.","notes":["The broad ligaments are a double fold of peritoneum from the sides of the uterus to the pelvic walls. They drape the uterus, tubes, and ovaries, but the main support against sliding down is the cardinal ligaments.","Correct. The cardinal ligaments run from the cervix and upper vagina to the lateral pelvic walls and are the main support that keeps the uterus from sliding down.","The round ligaments run from the uterus through the inguinal canal to the labia majora. They help keep the uterus tipped forward, but they are not the main support against sliding down.","The cardinal ligaments are the right support, but they run from the cervix and upper vagina to the lateral pelvic walls. The route through the inguinal canal belongs to the round ligaments."],"comp":148,"competencyId":"w6-uterus"},{"id":"N-a17d-148-2","dok":3,"q":"A biopsy needle passes through the uterine wall from the peritoneal cavity side into the uterine cavity. Which layer does it cross second, and what tissue is that layer?","a":"The myometrium, a thick layer of smooth muscle.","options":["The perimetrium, a serous layer of peritoneum","The endometrium, a mucous lining with two layers","The stratum basalis, a layer of smooth muscle","The myometrium, a thick layer of smooth muscle"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The uterine wall has three layers from the outside in: the perimetrium, the myometrium, and the endometrium. Coming from the peritoneal cavity, the needle meets the perimetrium first, because it is the outer serous layer and part of the visceral peritoneum. The second layer is the myometrium, the thick middle layer of smooth muscle.","notes":["The perimetrium is the outer serous layer, part of the visceral peritoneum, so a needle coming from the peritoneal cavity crosses it first, not second.","The endometrium is the inner mucous lining next to the uterine cavity, with a functional and a basal layer. It is the last layer the needle crosses, the third, not the second.","The stratum basalis is the deep layer of the endometrium, next to the myometrium, and it is part of the lining, not muscle. It is crossed after the myometrium.","Correct. From the outside, the needle crosses the perimetrium first and the smooth muscle of the myometrium second."],"comp":148,"competencyId":"w6-uterus"},{"id":"N-a17d-148-3","dok":3,"q":"A non-cancerous tumor of smooth muscle is found in the rounded top of the uterus, above where the uterine tubes enter. Which layer and region of the uterus is it in?","a":"The myometrium of the fundus.","options":["The myometrium of the fundus","The perimetrium of the cervix","The myometrium of the cervix","The endometrium of the body"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Uterine fibroids are non-cancerous tumors of the smooth muscle of the myometrium. The fundus is the rounded top of the uterus, above the points where the uterine tubes enter, and it is also where the myometrium is thickest. So a smooth muscle tumor at the top lies in the myometrium of the fundus.","notes":["Correct. A smooth muscle tumor arises in the myometrium, and the rounded top above the tubes is the fundus.","The perimetrium is the outer serous layer, and the cervix is the narrow lower part that projects into the vagina. Neither matches a smooth muscle tumor at the top.","The myometrium is the right layer, but the cervix is the narrow bottom of the uterus. The rounded top above the tubes is the fundus.","The endometrium is the mucous lining, not muscle, and the body is the main central part below the fundus. Tissue from the endometrium growing outside the uterus is endometriosis."],"comp":148,"competencyId":"w6-uterus"},{"id":"N-a17d-149-1","dok":3,"q":"A pea-sized swelling forms on one side of the vaginal orifice, slightly toward the back, where a gland's duct has become blocked. Which gland is it, and where does its duct normally open?","a":"A greater vestibular gland, whose duct opens into the vestibule.","options":["The hymen, which partly covers the vaginal orifice","A greater vestibular gland, whose duct opens into the vestibule","A greater vestibular gland, whose duct opens at the cervix","A bulbourethral gland, whose duct opens into the spongy urethra"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The greater vestibular (Bartholin) glands are a pair of pea-sized glands, one on each side of the vaginal orifice and slightly toward the back. Their ducts open into the vestibule, the space between the labia minora, and their mucus lubricates it. A blocked duct would swell right at that spot.","notes":["The hymen is a thin fold of mucous membrane at the vaginal orifice. It is not a gland and has no duct to block.","Correct. The greater vestibular glands sit on each side of the vaginal orifice, slightly toward the back, and their ducts open into the vestibule.","The gland is right, but its duct opens into the vestibule at the outside, not up at the cervix at the top of the vagina.","Bulbourethral glands are the male glands beside the membranous urethra. They are the male match for the greater vestibular glands, but they are not found in the female."],"comp":149,"competencyId":"w6-vagina-vulva"},{"id":"N-a17d-149-2","dok":3,"q":"On a vulva model, two thin, hairless folds enclose a space that holds two openings. If you follow these folds forward to where they meet, what do they form there, and over which structure?","a":"The prepuce, over the clitoris.","options":["The prepuce, over the clitoris","The labia majora, over the vestibule","The mons pubis, over the pubic symphysis","The hymen, over the vaginal orifice"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The thin, hairless folds that enclose the vestibule are the labia minora. At the front they meet over the clitoris and form its prepuce. The clitoris is the small erectile organ at the front of the vulva, and only its glans shows on the surface.","notes":["Correct. The folds are the labia minora, and where they meet in front they form the prepuce over the clitoris.","The labia majora are the outer folds of skin and fat, outside the labia minora, and they are hair-covered after puberty. They are separate folds, not what the labia minora form.","The mons pubis is a rounded pad of fat over the pubic symphysis, in front of the folds. It is not formed by the labia minora.","The hymen is a thin fold of mucous membrane at the vaginal orifice, at the back of the vestibule. The labia minora meet at the front, not over that opening."],"comp":149,"competencyId":"w6-vagina-vulva"},{"id":"N-a17d-149-3","dok":3,"q":"On a model, a probe placed in the opening at the back of the vestibule slides up a canal until it reaches a recess that rings a structure poking down into the top of the canal. What is the canal, and what is the recess?","a":"The vagina, ending at the fornix around the cervix.","options":["The cervical canal, ending at the internal os","The urethra, ending at the neck of the bladder","The vagina, ending at the vestibule around the cervix","The vagina, ending at the fornix around the cervix"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The vestibule holds two openings, the external urethral orifice in front and the vaginal orifice behind it. A probe in the back opening runs up the vagina, which reaches from the vestibule to the cervix. The cervix pokes into the top of the vagina, and the recess that rings it is the fornix.","notes":["The cervical canal runs through the cervix itself, from the external os to the internal os. The probe has to pass up the vagina before it reaches the cervix.","The urethral orifice is the front opening of the vestibule, not the back one. The urethra also has no recess ringing a structure at its top.","The canal is right, but the vestibule is the space between the labia minora at the outside. The recess around the cervix at the top of the vagina is the fornix.","Correct. The back opening of the vestibule is the vaginal orifice, and the recess ringing the cervix at the top of the vagina is the fornix."],"comp":149,"competencyId":"w6-vagina-vulva"},{"id":"N-a17d-150-1","dok":2,"q":"Which sequence traces milk from where it is made to where it leaves the breast?","a":"Alveoli, lobule ducts, lactiferous duct, lactiferous sinus, nipple.","options":["Alveoli, lactiferous sinus, lobule ducts, lactiferous duct, nipple","Lactiferous duct, alveoli, lobule ducts, lactiferous sinus, nipple","Alveoli, lobule ducts, lactiferous duct, lactiferous sinus, nipple","Areola, lactiferous sinus, lactiferous duct, alveoli, nipple"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Milk is secreted by the gland cells of the alveoli inside each lobule. Small ducts carry it out of the lobule into the one lactiferous duct that drains that lobe. Just before the nipple the duct widens into a lactiferous sinus where milk collects, and then it leaves through openings on the nipple.","notes":["The lactiferous sinus is the widened end of the lactiferous duct, just before the nipple. It comes after the ducts, not before them.","The alveoli are where milk is made, so they come first. The lactiferous duct comes after the small lobule ducts.","Correct. Milk is made in the alveoli, drains through small ducts and a lactiferous duct, collects in the sinus, and leaves at the nipple.","This runs mostly backward and starts at the areola, which is the ring of pigmented skin around the nipple. Milk is made in the alveoli and moves outward to the nipple."],"comp":150,"competencyId":"w6-mammary"},{"id":"N-a17d-150-2","dok":2,"q":"Both the small bumps on the areola and the small sacs deep in the lobules are glands. Which statement correctly compares them?","a":"The bumps are sebaceous areolar glands; the sacs are alveoli that secrete milk.","options":["The bumps are alveoli that secrete milk; the sacs are sebaceous areolar glands.","Both are alveoli that secrete milk, one set on the skin and one set deep inside.","The bumps are lactiferous sinuses; the sacs are lobules that hold the lobes.","The bumps are sebaceous areolar glands; the sacs are alveoli that secrete milk."],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The areola is the ring of pigmented skin around the nipple, and its small bumps are areolar glands, a type of sebaceous gland. The milk-making units sit deep in the breast: each lobule holds clusters of alveoli, small sacs lined with cells that secrete milk. Both are glands, but only the alveoli are part of the milk pathway.","notes":["This swaps the two. Alveoli sit deep inside the lobules and secrete milk, while the bumps on the areola are areolar glands, a type of sebaceous gland.","Alveoli are found only inside the lobules. The bumps on the areola are a different kind of gland, the sebaceous areolar glands.","A lactiferous sinus is the widened end of a lactiferous duct just inside the nipple, not a bump on the areola. Lobules are subdivisions of a lobe, not the other way around, and the sacs inside them are alveoli.","Correct. The areolar bumps are sebaceous glands, while the alveoli inside the lobules are the sacs that secrete milk."],"comp":150,"competencyId":"w6-mammary"},{"id":"N-a17d-150-3","dok":3,"q":"A breast tumor pulls on a band of connective tissue that runs from the skin to the fascia over the pectoralis major, and the skin over it dimples. Which structure is being pulled, and between which units of the gland does it run?","a":"A suspensory ligament, running between the lobes.","options":["A lactiferous duct, running between the lobules","A suspensory ligament, running between the lobes","A suspensory ligament, running inside each alveolus","The areola, running between the nipple and the skin"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The suspensory (Cooper) ligaments are bands of connective tissue that run from the skin through the breast, between the lobes, to the fascia over the pectoralis major. They support the breast. Because they are attached to the skin, a tumor that pulls on one can dimple the skin above it.","notes":["A lactiferous duct carries milk from one lobe to the nipple. It is a duct, not a band of connective tissue tied to the skin and fascia.","Correct. Suspensory ligaments run from the skin between the lobes to the pectoral fascia, so a tumor pulling on one dimples the skin.","The structure is right, but alveoli are tiny secretory sacs inside lobules. The suspensory ligaments run between the much larger lobes.","The areola is the ring of pigmented skin around the nipple. It is skin, not a band that runs through the breast to the fascia."],"comp":150,"competencyId":"w6-mammary"},{"id":"N-a17d-150-4","dok":3,"q":"A lactiferous duct is blocked just before the nipple. How much of the gland can no longer drain milk out through the nipple, and why?","a":"One lobe, because each lobe drains by its own lactiferous duct.","options":["Only the areolar glands, because the duct drains the areola","The whole breast, because all lobes share one duct","One lobe, because each lobe drains by its own lactiferous duct","One lobule, because each lobule has its own lactiferous duct"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The mammary gland has 15 to 20 lobes, and each lobe drains by its own lactiferous duct to the nipple. Blocking one duct just before the nipple cuts off the drainage of that single lobe. The other lobes still drain through their own ducts.","notes":["Areolar glands are sebaceous glands on the areola, not part of the milk pathway. The lactiferous duct carries milk from a lobe.","The lobes do not share a duct. Each of the 15 to 20 lobes has its own lactiferous duct, so the others keep draining.","Correct. There is one lactiferous duct per lobe, so blocking one duct stops drainage from that one lobe.","Lobules drain by small ducts that join into the lactiferous duct for the whole lobe. A lactiferous duct serves an entire lobe, not a single lobule."],"comp":150,"competencyId":"w6-mammary"},{"id":"N-a17d-150-5","dok":3,"q":"A mammary gland slide shows small sacs lined with secretory cells, each wrapped by contractile cells. What are the sacs, and what do the wrapping cells do?","a":"Alveoli, wrapped by myoepithelial cells that push out milk.","options":["Alveoli, wrapped by myoepithelial cells that push out milk","Lobes, wrapped by suspensory ligaments that support them","Alveoli, wrapped by sebaceous cells that oil the nipple","Lactiferous sinuses, wrapped by smooth muscle that stores milk"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Each lobule holds clusters of alveoli, small sacs lined with cells that secrete milk. Myoepithelial cells around the alveoli contract to push the milk out into the ducts. From there it moves through the lactiferous duct toward the nipple.","notes":["Correct. The secretory sacs are alveoli, and the myoepithelial cells around them contract to push milk into the ducts.","Lobes are the 15 to 20 large compartments of the gland, far bigger than a sac on a slide. Suspensory ligaments run between lobes but are not contractile cells.","The sacs are alveoli, but the cells around them are myoepithelial cells that contract. Sebaceous glands on the breast are the areolar glands on the areola.","A lactiferous sinus is the widened end of a lactiferous duct near the nipple where milk collects. The secretory sacs that make milk are the alveoli."],"comp":150,"competencyId":"w6-mammary"}]},{"id":"m5-nervous-tissue","title":"Nervous system organization and nervous tissue","summary":"CNS and PNS divisions, neuron parts and classes, neuroglia, myelin, synapse structures, gray and white matter, nuclei, ganglia, tracts, nerves","mw":"2026-11-30","tr":"2026-12-01","cards":[{"id":"L02676","dok":1,"q":"Which two structures make up the central nervous system?","a":"The brain and the spinal cord.","options":["The brain and the spinal cord","The nerves and the ganglia","The neurons and the neuroglia","The brain and the cranial nerves"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The central nervous system (CNS) is the brain, which sits in the cranial cavity, and the spinal cord, which runs in the vertebral canal. Together they process incoming information and issue commands, so the CNS is the control center of the nervous system. Everything outside these two organs, including the nerves, ganglia, and sensory receptors, belongs to the peripheral nervous system.","notes":["Correct. The brain and the spinal cord together form the CNS.","Nerves (bundles of axons) and ganglia (clusters of cell bodies) sit outside the brain and spinal cord. Choose them for a question asking what makes up the peripheral nervous system.","Neurons and neuroglia are the two cell types of nervous tissue, and they are found in both the CNS and the PNS. Pick them if the question asked which cells make up nervous tissue.","The brain is part of the CNS, but the cranial nerves are bundles of axons that leave the brain and run outside it, so they belong to the PNS. This pairing would fit a question about the brain and the 12 pairs of nerves attached to it."],"lt":"t-nervous-tissue","lid":"c1","comp":173,"competencyId":"w8-ns-organization"},{"id":"L02677","dok":1,"q":"Which part of a neuron contains the nucleus?","a":"The cell body.","options":["The axon","The dendrite","The axon terminal","The cell body"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The cell body, also called the soma or perikaryon, holds the nucleus and most of the cytoplasm, which makes it the metabolic center of the neuron. Its Nissl bodies (clusters of rough ER and ribosomes) make the proteins the whole cell needs, including those used far out in the axon. The processes that extend from the cell body, the dendrites and the axon, do not hold the nucleus.","notes":["The axon is the single long process that carries the impulse away from the cell body toward other cells. It would be the answer for a question about which part conducts the signal away.","Dendrites are the short, branched processes that receive incoming signals and carry them toward the cell body. Pick the dendrite for a question about the receiving part of a neuron.","Axon terminals are the fine branches at the far end of the axon that contact the target cell. They would be right for a question about where the neuron hands its signal to the next cell.","Correct. The cell body holds the nucleus and is the metabolic center of the neuron."],"lt":"t-nervous-tissue","lid":"c2","comp":174,"competencyId":"w8-neuron-parts"},{"id":"L02678","dok":1,"q":"Which glial cell forms the myelin sheath around axons in the central nervous system?","a":"The oligodendrocyte.","options":["The Schwann cell","The microglia","The oligodendrocyte","The astrocyte"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Oligodendrocytes are CNS glial cells whose processes wrap around axons to form the myelin sheath, a fatty covering that insulates the axon and speeds the impulse. One oligodendrocyte can send processes to several axons at once, myelinating a segment of each. In the PNS the same job belongs to a different cell, the Schwann cell, so the location of the axon decides the answer.","notes":["Schwann cells also form myelin, but only around axons in the peripheral nervous system. Pick the Schwann cell when the question places the axon in a peripheral nerve.","Microglia are the small phagocytic glia of the CNS that clear away debris and microbes. They would be the answer for a question about the defense cells of the brain and spinal cord.","Correct. Oligodendrocytes make the myelin in the brain and spinal cord.","Astrocytes are star-shaped CNS glia that support neurons and help form the blood-brain barrier; they do not make myelin. Choose the astrocyte for a question about the blood-brain barrier or structural support."],"lt":"t-nervous-tissue","lid":"c3","comp":178,"competencyId":"w8-myelination"},{"id":"L02679","dok":1,"q":"What are the regular gaps in the myelin sheath along an axon called?","a":"The nodes of Ranvier.","options":["The axon hillocks","The axon terminals","The nodes of Ranvier","The synaptic clefts"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Myelin is laid down in segments, and the short, bare stretches of axon between the segments are the nodes of Ranvier. The impulse jumps from one node to the next, a pattern called saltatory conduction, which is why myelinated axons conduct much faster than unmyelinated ones. Each segment between two nodes is wrapped by one Schwann cell in the PNS or by one process of an oligodendrocyte in the CNS.","notes":["The axon hillock is the cone-shaped region where the axon leaves the cell body, at the very start of the axon. It would be the answer for a question about where the axon joins the cell body.","Axon terminals are the fine branches at the far end of the axon. Choose them for a question about where the neuron contacts its target cell.","Correct. The nodes of Ranvier are the bare gaps between myelin segments.","A synaptic cleft is a gap too, but it is the space between two cells at a chemical synapse, not a gap in the myelin. Pick it for a question about the space that neurotransmitter crosses."],"lt":"t-nervous-tissue","lid":"c4","comp":178,"competencyId":"w8-myelination"},{"id":"L02680","dok":1,"q":"Which glial cell forms the myelin sheath around axons in the peripheral nervous system?","a":"The Schwann cell.","options":["The Schwann cell","The satellite cell","The oligodendrocyte","The ependymal cell"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Schwann cells are the myelin-forming glia of the PNS. Each Schwann cell wraps one segment of one axon, and its outer layer, which holds its nucleus and cytoplasm, forms the neurolemma. That neurolemma guides a cut axon as it regrows, which is why peripheral nerves can regenerate while CNS tracts usually cannot.","notes":["Correct. Schwann cells myelinate axons in peripheral nerves.","Satellite cells are the other PNS glia, but they surround neuron cell bodies inside ganglia instead of wrapping axons. Pick them for a question about the support cells in a ganglion.","Oligodendrocytes form myelin too, but only in the CNS. Choose the oligodendrocyte when the axon is in the brain or spinal cord.","Ependymal cells line the ventricles of the brain and the central canal of the spinal cord and help produce and circulate cerebrospinal fluid. They would be right for a question about the lining of the CNS cavities."],"lt":"t-nervous-tissue","lid":"c5","comp":178,"competencyId":"w8-myelination"},{"id":"L02681","dok":1,"q":"What is the single long process of a neuron that carries the impulse away from the cell body?","a":"The axon.","options":["The axon hillock","The myelin sheath","The axon","The dendrite"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The axon is the single long process of a neuron, and it carries the nerve impulse away from the cell body toward other neurons, muscles, or glands. It begins at the axon hillock and ends in branching axon terminals. A typical neuron has many dendrites bringing signals in but only one axon sending the signal out.","notes":["The axon hillock is the cone-shaped spot on the cell body where the axon starts; it is the beginning of the axon, not the whole process. Choose it for a question about where the cell body joins the axon.","The myelin sheath is a fatty wrapping made by glial cells around many axons, so it is not a process of the neuron itself. It would be right for a question about what insulates the axon and speeds the impulse.","Correct. The axon carries the impulse away from the cell body.","Dendrites are short, branched processes that carry signals toward the cell body, the opposite direction. Pick the dendrite for a question about the receiving processes of a neuron."],"lt":"t-nervous-tissue","lid":"c6","comp":174,"competencyId":"w8-neuron-parts"},{"id":"L02682","dok":1,"q":"Which glial cell acts as the defense cell of the central nervous system?","a":"The microglia.","options":["The satellite cell","The microglia","The ependymal cell","The astrocyte"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Microglia are small phagocytic glial cells of the CNS. Phagocytic means they engulf and digest material, so they remove dead cells, debris, and microbes from the brain and spinal cord. Of the four CNS glia, they are the only ones whose main job is defense; the others support neurons, make myelin, or line the fluid-filled cavities.","notes":["Satellite cells are PNS glia that surround neuron cell bodies inside ganglia, so they are not even in the CNS. They would be right for a question about support cells in a peripheral ganglion.","Correct. Microglia engulf debris and microbes in the CNS.","Ependymal cells line the ventricles and central canal and help produce and circulate cerebrospinal fluid. Choose them for a question about the lining of the CNS cavities.","Astrocytes are star-shaped CNS glia that support neurons, help form the blood-brain barrier, and maintain the chemical environment around neurons. Pick the astrocyte for a question about the blood-brain barrier."],"lt":"t-nervous-tissue","lid":"c7","comp":177,"competencyId":"w8-neuroglia"},{"id":"L02683","dok":1,"q":"What is the tiny gap between the two cells at a chemical synapse called?","a":"The synaptic cleft.","options":["The node of Ranvier","The axon hillock","The synaptic vesicle","The synaptic cleft"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"At a chemical synapse the sending and receiving cells do not touch. The narrow space between them is the synaptic cleft, and the neurotransmitter released by the sending cell diffuses across this gap to reach the receiving cell. That is why the signal crosses a chemical synapse as a chemical message rather than through direct contact.","notes":["A node of Ranvier is a gap in the myelin sheath along an axon, not a gap between two cells. It would be right for a question about where the impulse jumps during saltatory conduction.","The axon hillock is the cone-shaped region where the axon leaves the cell body. Choose it for a question about where the axon begins.","Synaptic vesicles are tiny sacs inside the sending axon terminal that store neurotransmitter before it is released. Pick the vesicle for a question about where neurotransmitter is stored.","Correct. The synaptic cleft is the gap the neurotransmitter crosses."],"lt":"t-nervous-tissue","lid":"c8","comp":175,"competencyId":"w8-synapse-parts"},{"id":"L02684","dok":1,"q":"Which structural class of neuron has many dendrites and one axon and is the most common type?","a":"The multipolar neuron.","options":["The multipolar neuron","The unipolar neuron","The sensory neuron","The bipolar neuron"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Multipolar neurons have many dendrites and one axon, and they are the most common structural class. Almost all neurons in the brain and spinal cord are multipolar, including motor neurons and interneurons. The structural classes are named for how many processes leave the cell body: many for multipolar, two for bipolar, and one for unipolar.","notes":["Correct. Multipolar neurons have many dendrites and one axon.","A unipolar neuron has a single fused process that leaves the cell body, and it is the typical shape of a sensory neuron. Choose it for a question about the shape of most sensory neurons.","Sensory neuron is a functional name for a neuron that carries signals toward the CNS, not a structural class. Most sensory neurons are unipolar, so they do not have many dendrites. Pick it for a question about direction of signaling.","A bipolar neuron has just one dendrite and one axon and is found in the retina, the inner ear, and the olfactory area. Pick it for a question about neurons in the special sense organs."],"lt":"t-nervous-tissue","lid":"c9","comp":176,"competencyId":"w8-neuron-classification"},{"id":"L02685","dok":1,"q":"What is the cone-shaped region of the cell body where the axon begins called?","a":"The axon hillock.","options":["The node of Ranvier","The synaptic end bulb","The axon terminal","The axon hillock"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The axon hillock is the cone-shaped part of the cell body that narrows into the start of the axon. It marks the boundary between the cell body, which receives and processes input, and the axon, which carries the impulse away. On a slide it is the tapered point where the single axon leaves the soma.","notes":["A node of Ranvier is a bare gap in the myelin sheath further along the axon. Pick it for a question about the gaps where the impulse jumps.","Synaptic end bulbs are the swellings at the tips of the axon terminals that hold the synaptic vesicles. Choose them for a question about the signal-sending end of the neuron.","Axon terminals are the fine branches at the far end of the axon, the opposite end from the hillock. They would be right for a question about where the neuron contacts its target.","Correct. The axon hillock is where the cell body narrows into the axon."],"lt":"t-nervous-tissue","lid":"c10","comp":174,"competencyId":"w8-neuron-parts"},{"id":"L02686","dok":1,"q":"What are the two cell types that make up nervous tissue?","a":"Neurons and neuroglia.","options":["Sensory cells and motor cells","Astrocytes and Schwann cells","Neurons and neuroglia","Axons and dendrites"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Nervous tissue is built from two cell types. Neurons are the excitable cells that receive, conduct, and transmit nerve impulses, and neuroglia (glial cells) are the smaller, very numerous support cells that nourish, insulate, and protect them. Unlike neurons, glial cells can still divide.","notes":["Sensory and motor neurons are functional classes of neurons, so both are neurons and the support cells are missing. This would fit a question about neurons that carry signals toward and away from the CNS.","Astrocytes and Schwann cells are both glial cells, one from the CNS and one from the PNS, so the neurons are missing. Choose this pair for a question asking for one CNS glial cell and one PNS glial cell.","Correct. Nervous tissue is built from neurons and their support cells, the neuroglia.","Axons and dendrites are processes of a single neuron, not separate cell types. Pick them for a question about the two kinds of processes that extend from the cell body."],"lt":"t-nervous-tissue","lid":"c11","comp":177,"competencyId":"w8-neuroglia"},{"id":"L02687","dok":1,"q":"What is the small sac in the axon terminal that stores neurotransmitter called?","a":"The synaptic vesicle.","options":["The Nissl body","The synaptic cleft","The synaptic end bulb","The synaptic vesicle"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Synaptic vesicles are tiny membrane sacs packed inside the synaptic end bulbs at the tips of the axon terminals. Each vesicle stores neurotransmitter, the chemical messenger, until a signal arrives and the vesicles release it into the synaptic cleft. Only the sending side of a synapse holds these vesicles.","notes":["Nissl bodies are clusters of rough ER and ribosomes in the cell body that make proteins. They would be right for a question about the site of protein synthesis in a neuron.","The synaptic cleft is the gap between the two cells that the neurotransmitter crosses after release, not a sac that stores it. Pick it for a question about the space between the cells.","The synaptic end bulb is the swelling at the tip of an axon terminal that contains the vesicles; it is the container, not the sac itself. Choose it for a question about the swelling that holds the signal-sending machinery.","Correct. Synaptic vesicles store neurotransmitter until it is released."],"lt":"t-nervous-tissue","lid":"c12","comp":175,"competencyId":"w8-synapse-parts"},{"id":"L02688","dok":2,"q":"The sensory division of the peripheral nervous system is also known by which name?","a":"The afferent division.","options":["The afferent division","The autonomic division","The somatic division","The efferent division"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Afferent means carrying toward, so the afferent (sensory) division carries signals from sensory receptors toward the CNS. Its partner, the efferent (motor) division, carries commands away from the CNS to the muscles and glands. Afferent signals arrive at the CNS, and efferent signals exit it.","notes":["Correct. Afferent means carrying toward, and the sensory division carries signals toward the CNS.","The autonomic nervous system is a subdivision of the motor division that controls smooth muscle, cardiac muscle, and glands. Choose it for a question about involuntary motor commands.","The somatic nervous system is a subdivision of the motor division that carries voluntary commands to skeletal muscle. It would be right for a question about the nerves that move skeletal muscle.","The efferent division is the motor division, which carries commands away from the CNS to muscles and glands. Pick it for a question asking for the other name of the motor division."],"lt":"t-nervous-tissue","lid":"c13","comp":173,"competencyId":"w8-ns-organization"},{"id":"L02689","dok":2,"q":"Which subdivision of the motor division carries voluntary commands to skeletal muscle?","a":"The somatic nervous system","options":["The somatic nervous system","The enteric nervous system","The sensory (afferent) division","The autonomic nervous system"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The motor (efferent) division of the PNS is split by the kind of effector it controls. The somatic nervous system is the voluntary branch, and its motor neurons carry commands from the CNS directly to skeletal muscle. The autonomic nervous system, by contrast, controls effectors that work without conscious control.","notes":["Correct. The somatic nervous system is the voluntary branch that drives skeletal muscle.","The enteric nervous system is the network of neurons in the wall of the GI tract that can work on its own. Choose it for a question about the nervous system of the gut.","The sensory division carries signals toward the CNS from receptors, so it does not carry commands at all. It would be right for a question about input from receptors.","The autonomic nervous system is the involuntary branch, carrying commands to smooth muscle, cardiac muscle, and glands. Pick it for a question about heart rate or gland secretion."],"lt":"t-nervous-tissue","lid":"c14","comp":173,"competencyId":"w8-ns-organization"},{"id":"L02690","dok":2,"q":"Which subdivision of the motor division carries involuntary commands to smooth muscle, cardiac muscle, and glands?","a":"The autonomic nervous system.","options":["The sensory (afferent) division","The central nervous system","The autonomic nervous system","The somatic nervous system"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The autonomic nervous system is the involuntary branch of the motor division of the PNS. Its motor neurons carry commands to smooth muscle, cardiac muscle, and glands, the effectors that work without conscious control. It has two divisions, the sympathetic division for fight or flight and the parasympathetic division for rest and digest, and many organs receive both.","notes":["The sensory division carries input from receptors toward the CNS rather than commands out to effectors. Choose it for a question about signals arriving at the CNS.","The CNS is the brain and spinal cord, where commands are issued, but it is not a subdivision of the motor division of the PNS. It would be right for a question about where information is integrated.","Correct. The autonomic nervous system runs the effectors we do not consciously control.","The somatic nervous system is the voluntary branch of the motor division, carrying commands to skeletal muscle. Pick it for a question about moving a limb on purpose."],"lt":"t-nervous-tissue","lid":"c15","comp":173,"competencyId":"w8-ns-organization"},{"id":"L02691","dok":2,"q":"Nerves and ganglia have one feature in common. Which statement describes it?","a":"Both lie outside the CNS.","options":["Both are bundles of axons","Both are clusters of cell bodies","Both lie inside the CNS","Both lie outside the CNS"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Nerves and ganglia are the two main structures of the peripheral nervous system, which includes every neural structure outside the brain and spinal cord. A nerve is a bundle of axons and a ganglion is a cluster of neuron cell bodies, so they differ in what they contain but share their location outside the CNS. Inside the CNS, the matching structures are called tracts and nuclei.","notes":["Only a nerve is a bundle of axons; a ganglion is a cluster of neuron cell bodies. This statement would fit a comparison of a nerve and a tract, which are both bundles of axons.","Only a ganglion is a cluster of cell bodies; a nerve is a bundle of axons. This statement would fit a comparison of a ganglion and a nucleus.","This is the reverse of the truth. The structures that lie inside the CNS are tracts and nuclei, so pick this statement for a question about those two.","Correct. Nerves and ganglia are both structures of the peripheral nervous system."],"lt":"t-nervous-tissue","lid":"c16","comp":179,"competencyId":"w8-tissue-collections"},{"id":"L02692","dok":2,"q":"Which glial cell helps form the blood-brain barrier?","a":"The astrocyte.","options":["The astrocyte","The oligodendrocyte","The ependymal cell","The microglia"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Astrocytes are star-shaped glial cells of the CNS. Their processes wrap around the capillaries of the brain and help form the blood-brain barrier, which limits what can pass from the blood into brain tissue. They also give structural support, guide the growth of neurons, and keep the chemical environment around neurons stable.","notes":["Correct. Astrocytes help form the blood-brain barrier.","Oligodendrocytes form the myelin sheaths around axons in the CNS. Pick the oligodendrocyte for a question about CNS myelin.","Ependymal cells line the ventricles and central canal and help produce and circulate cerebrospinal fluid. They would be right for a question about the lining of the CNS cavities.","Microglia are the small phagocytic glia that clear debris and microbes from the CNS. Choose them for a question about the defense cells of the brain."],"lt":"t-nervous-tissue","lid":"c17","comp":177,"competencyId":"w8-neuroglia"},{"id":"L02693","dok":2,"q":"Which glial cell lines the cavities of the brain and spinal cord and helps circulate cerebrospinal fluid?","a":"The ependymal cell.","options":["The ependymal cell","The astrocyte","The oligodendrocyte","The microglia"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Ependymal cells are CNS glia that line the ventricles of the brain and the central canal of the spinal cord. They help produce cerebrospinal fluid and help move it through those spaces. Their position as the lining of the fluid-filled cavities is what sets them apart from the other three CNS glia.","notes":["Correct. Ependymal cells line the ventricles and central canal.","Astrocytes are star-shaped CNS glia that support neurons and help form the blood-brain barrier, but they do not line the cavities. Pick the astrocyte for a question about the blood-brain barrier.","Oligodendrocytes wrap CNS axons in myelin. They would be the answer for a question about which cell myelinates axons in the brain or spinal cord.","Microglia are phagocytic CNS glia that remove debris and microbes. Choose them for a question about defense in the brain and spinal cord."],"lt":"t-nervous-tissue","lid":"c18","comp":177,"competencyId":"w8-neuroglia"},{"id":"L02694","dok":2,"q":"Which glial cell surrounds and supports the neuron cell bodies in peripheral ganglia?","a":"The satellite cell.","options":["The oligodendrocyte","The satellite cell","The astrocyte","The Schwann cell"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Satellite cells are PNS glia that form a layer around each neuron cell body inside a ganglion, such as a posterior root ganglion. They support those cell bodies in the same way that astrocytes support neurons in the CNS. The PNS has only two kinds of glia: satellite cells around cell bodies and Schwann cells around axons.","notes":["Oligodendrocytes form myelin around CNS axons. They would be right for a question about myelin in the brain or spinal cord.","Correct. Satellite cells surround neuron cell bodies in ganglia.","Astrocytes are the main support cells around neurons in the CNS, not in peripheral ganglia. Choose the astrocyte for a question about support or the blood-brain barrier in the brain.","Schwann cells are also PNS glia, but they wrap axons in myelin rather than surrounding cell bodies. Pick the Schwann cell for a question about myelin in a peripheral nerve."],"lt":"t-nervous-tissue","lid":"c19","comp":177,"competencyId":"w8-neuroglia"},{"id":"L02695","dok":2,"q":"An interneuron lies entirely within which division of the nervous system?","a":"The central nervous system.","options":["The autonomic nervous system","The somatic nervous system","The central nervous system","The peripheral nervous system"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Interneurons, also called association neurons, lie entirely within the brain and spinal cord. They connect sensory neurons to motor neurons and link neurons to each other, so they carry out much of the processing the CNS performs. Sensory and motor neurons, by contrast, have long processes that extend out into the PNS.","notes":["The autonomic nervous system is the involuntary motor branch of the PNS that runs to smooth muscle, cardiac muscle, and glands. It would be right for a question about control of the viscera.","The somatic nervous system is the voluntary motor branch of the PNS that runs to skeletal muscle. Choose it for a question about the motor neurons that drive skeletal muscle.","Correct. Interneurons lie entirely within the brain and spinal cord.","The PNS holds the nerves, ganglia, and receptors outside the brain and spinal cord. Sensory and motor neurons have processes that run in the PNS, so pick it for a question about where those long processes travel."],"lt":"t-nervous-tissue","lid":"c20","comp":176,"competencyId":"w8-neuron-classification"},{"id":"L02696","dok":2,"q":"By function, which type of neuron carries signals from receptors toward the central nervous system?","a":"The sensory neuron.","options":["The sensory neuron","The interneuron","The motor neuron","The multipolar neuron"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Neurons are classified by function according to the direction they carry signals relative to the CNS. Sensory (afferent) neurons carry input from receptors toward the CNS, and most of them are unipolar in shape. Motor neurons carry commands away from the CNS, and interneurons connect the two inside the CNS.","notes":["Correct. Sensory (afferent) neurons carry input toward the CNS.","Interneurons stay entirely within the CNS and connect sensory neurons to motor neurons. Choose the interneuron for a question about the neurons that link input to output inside the brain or cord.","Motor (efferent) neurons carry commands in the opposite direction, away from the CNS to muscles and glands. Pick the motor neuron for a question about signals leaving the CNS.","Multipolar is a structural class based on shape, not on the direction a neuron carries signals. It would be right for a question about a neuron with many dendrites and one axon."],"lt":"t-nervous-tissue","lid":"c21","comp":176,"competencyId":"w8-neuron-classification"},{"id":"L02697","dok":2,"q":"Neurons are sorted into multipolar, bipolar, and unipolar types according to which feature?","a":"How many processes leave the cell body.","options":["The direction in which they carry signals","The type of glial cell that supports them","Whether their axon has a myelin sheath","How many processes leave the cell body"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The structural classification of neurons is based on shape, specifically how many processes leave the cell body. Multipolar neurons have many (several dendrites and one axon), bipolar neurons have two (one dendrite and one axon), and unipolar neurons have a single process that then splits. The other way to classify neurons is functional, by the direction they carry signals.","notes":["Direction is the basis of the functional classification, which gives sensory, motor, and interneurons. Pick this feature for a question about how those three functional classes are defined.","Glial cells differ between the CNS and the PNS, but neurons are not named for the glia around them. This would fit a question about the difference between Schwann cells and oligodendrocytes.","Myelin divides axons into myelinated and unmyelinated, which affects conduction speed but not the structural class. Choose this feature for a question about what makes white matter white.","Correct. The structural classes count the processes extending from the cell body."],"lt":"t-nervous-tissue","lid":"c22","comp":176,"competencyId":"w8-neuron-classification"},{"id":"L02698","dok":2,"q":"The neuromuscular junction is the synapse between a motor neuron and which structure?","a":"A skeletal muscle fiber.","options":["Another neuron's dendrite","A skeletal muscle fiber","A secretory gland cell","A supporting glial cell"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"A synapse is any site where a neuron communicates with another neuron, a muscle cell, or a gland. The neuromuscular junction is the specific synapse where the axon terminal of a somatic motor neuron meets a skeletal muscle fiber. The neurotransmitter released there crosses the cleft and triggers the muscle fiber to contract.","notes":["A synapse between two neurons is a neuron-to-neuron synapse, such as an axodendritic or axosomatic synapse. Pick this for a question about where an axon meets a dendrite or cell body.","Correct. The neuromuscular junction links a motor neuron to a skeletal muscle fiber.","Neurons do synapse with gland cells, for example in autonomic pathways, but that synapse is not called the neuromuscular junction. Choose a gland cell for a question about autonomic effectors that secrete.","Glial cells support neurons but are not the target of motor commands. This would fit a question about the cells that wrap axons or surround cell bodies."],"lt":"t-nervous-tissue","lid":"c23","comp":175,"competencyId":"w8-synapse-parts"},{"id":"L02699","dok":2,"q":"Within a neuron, which structures receive signals and carry them toward the cell body?","a":"The dendrites.","options":["The axon terminals","The axon hillock","The axon","The dendrites"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Dendrites are the short, branched, tree-like processes of a neuron, and they are its main receiving surface. Signals from other neurons arrive at synapses on the dendrites and travel toward the cell body. From there the signal can continue to the axon hillock and out along the axon.","notes":["Axon terminals are the branches at the end of the axon that pass the signal on to the next cell. Choose them for a question about where a neuron hands off its signal.","The axon hillock is the cone-shaped region where the axon leaves the cell body. It would be right for a question about where the axon begins.","The axon carries the impulse away from the cell body, the opposite direction. Pick the axon for a question about the outgoing process.","Correct. Dendrites are the receiving processes of the neuron."],"lt":"t-nervous-tissue","lid":"c24","comp":174,"competencyId":"w8-neuron-parts"},{"id":"L02700","dok":2,"q":"The motor division of the peripheral nervous system is also known by which name?","a":"The efferent division.","options":["The efferent division","The autonomic division","The somatic division","The afferent division"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Efferent means carrying away, so the efferent (motor) division carries commands from the CNS out to the effectors, the muscles and glands. It includes the somatic nervous system for skeletal muscle, the autonomic nervous system for smooth muscle, cardiac muscle, and glands, and the enteric nervous system in the wall of the digestive tract. Its counterpart is the afferent (sensory) division, which carries input toward the CNS.","notes":["Correct. Efferent means carrying away, and the motor division carries commands away from the CNS.","The autonomic nervous system is one part of the motor division, the involuntary part that reaches smooth muscle, cardiac muscle, and glands. It is a subdivision, not another name for the whole division.","The somatic nervous system is one part of the motor division, the voluntary part that reaches skeletal muscle, not another name for the whole division.","The afferent division is the sensory division, which carries signals toward the CNS, the opposite direction. Pick it for the other name of the sensory division."],"lt":"t-nervous-tissue","lid":"c25","comp":173,"competencyId":"w8-ns-organization"},{"id":"L02701","dok":2,"q":"The cell body of a neuron is also known by which single-word term?","a":"The soma.","options":["The axolemma","The axoplasm","The soma","The neurolemma"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Soma means body, so soma is the one-word name for the cell body of a neuron; perikaryon is another name for the same part. The soma holds the nucleus and the Nissl bodies and is the metabolic center of the neuron. The look-alike terms axoplasm, axolemma, and neurolemma all describe the axon or its wrapping instead.","notes":["The axolemma is the plasma membrane that surrounds the axon. Choose it for a question about the membrane of the axon.","Axoplasm is the cytoplasm inside the axon, not the cell body. Pick it for a question about the fluid contents of the axon.","Correct. Soma means body, and it is another name for the cell body.","The neurolemma is the outer layer of a Schwann cell wrapped around a peripheral axon, so it is part of a glial cell, not the neuron. It would be right for a question about what guides a cut peripheral axon as it regrows."],"lt":"t-nervous-tissue","lid":"c26","comp":174,"competencyId":"w8-neuron-parts"},{"id":"L02702","dok":2,"q":"Pyramidal cells of the cerebral cortex and Purkinje cells of the cerebellum share which structural feature?","a":"Both are multipolar neurons.","options":["Both are glial support cells","Both are multipolar neurons","Both are unipolar neurons","Both are bipolar neurons"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Pyramidal cells in the cerebral cortex and Purkinje cells in the cerebellum are two named examples of multipolar neurons, each with many dendrites and one axon. They have distinctive shapes, which is why they are given their own names, but structurally they still fall in the multipolar class. Multipolar neurons are the most common type in the brain and spinal cord.","notes":["Glial cells are the support cells of nervous tissue, such as astrocytes and Schwann cells. Pyramidal and Purkinje cells are neurons, so pick glial cells only for a question about support cells.","Correct. Pyramidal and Purkinje cells are both distinctive multipolar neurons.","Unipolar neurons have a single process and are the typical shape of sensory neurons. Choose unipolar for a question about the shape of most sensory neurons.","Bipolar neurons have one dendrite and one axon and are found in the retina, the inner ear, and the olfactory area. Pick bipolar for a question about neurons in the special sense organs."],"lt":"t-nervous-tissue","lid":"c27","comp":176,"competencyId":"w8-neuron-classification"},{"id":"L02703","dok":2,"q":"A neuron has a single short process that leaves the cell body and then divides in two, and most sensory neurons have this shape. Which structural class is it?","a":"A unipolar neuron.","options":["A multipolar neuron","A motor neuron","A unipolar neuron","A bipolar neuron"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"A unipolar neuron has only one process leaving the cell body, and that single process splits into two branches, one running toward the periphery and one toward the CNS. This shape is typical of sensory neurons, whose cell bodies sit in ganglia such as the posterior root ganglion. The structural class depends only on how many processes leave the cell body.","notes":["A multipolar neuron has many dendrites and one axon. Choose it for a question about motor neurons or interneurons.","Motor neuron is a functional name for a neuron that carries commands away from the CNS, not a shape class, and motor neurons are multipolar. It would be right for a question about direction of signaling.","Correct. A unipolar neuron has a single process leaving the cell body.","A bipolar neuron has two separate processes, one dendrite and one axon, leaving opposite ends of the cell body. Pick it for a question about neurons in the retina or inner ear."],"lt":"t-nervous-tissue","lid":"c28","comp":176,"competencyId":"w8-neuron-classification"},{"id":"L02704","dok":2,"q":"A glial cell in the spinal cord sends out several processes, and each one wraps a segment of a different axon in myelin. Which glial cell is it?","a":"An oligodendrocyte.","options":["An ependymal cell","An astrocyte","A Schwann cell","An oligodendrocyte"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Oligodendrocytes are the myelin-forming cells of the CNS, and each one has a few long processes. Each process wraps a segment of a different axon, so one oligodendrocyte myelinates parts of several axons. A Schwann cell in the PNS is different: it myelinates one segment of a single axon.","notes":["Ependymal cells line the ventricles and the central canal of the cord and help circulate cerebrospinal fluid. They would be right for a question about the lining of the central canal.","Astrocytes are star-shaped CNS glia with many processes, but those processes support neurons and wrap capillaries rather than forming myelin. Choose the astrocyte for a question about the blood-brain barrier.","A Schwann cell wraps just one segment of one axon, and it works only in the PNS. Pick it for a question about myelin in a peripheral nerve.","Correct. One oligodendrocyte can myelinate segments of several CNS axons."],"lt":"t-nervous-tissue","lid":"c29","comp":178,"competencyId":"w8-myelination"},{"id":"L02705","dok":2,"q":"Which pair names the two types of glial cells found in the peripheral nervous system?","a":"Schwann cells and satellite cells.","options":["Astrocytes and microglia","Satellite cells and ependymal cells","Schwann cells and satellite cells","Schwann cells and oligodendrocytes"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The PNS has two kinds of glia. Schwann cells wrap peripheral axons in myelin and form the neurolemma, and satellite cells surround and support the neuron cell bodies inside ganglia. The other four glial types, astrocytes, oligodendrocytes, microglia, and ependymal cells, are found in the CNS.","notes":["Astrocytes and microglia are both CNS glia, so neither belongs in the PNS. Pick this pair for a question asking for the CNS glia that support neurons and defend the tissue.","Satellite cells are PNS glia, but ependymal cells line the ventricles and central canal of the CNS. Pick ependymal cells for a question about the lining of the ventricles.","Correct. These are the only two glial cells of the PNS.","These are the two myelin-forming cells, but the oligodendrocyte works in the CNS. Choose this pair for a question asking which cells make myelin in the PNS and CNS."],"lt":"t-nervous-tissue","lid":"c30","comp":177,"competencyId":"w8-neuroglia"},{"id":"L02706","dok":2,"q":"On a neuron model, you point to the fine branches at the far end of the axon that pass the signal to the next cell. Which part are you pointing to?","a":"The axon terminals.","options":["The dendrites","The cell body","The axon hillock","The axon terminals"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The axon ends by splitting into fine branches called axon terminals, and each terminal ends in a synaptic end bulb that contacts the next cell. These terminals are where the neuron hands its signal off to another neuron, a muscle cell, or a gland. Dendrites look branched too, but they are at the receiving end, clustered around the cell body.","notes":["Dendrites are also branched, but they sit on the cell body and receive signals rather than pass them on. Pick the dendrites for a question about the receiving branches of a neuron.","The cell body holds the nucleus and is the metabolic center of the neuron. It would be right for a question about the part that contains the nucleus and Nissl bodies.","The axon hillock is at the start of the axon, where it leaves the cell body, not at the far end. Choose it for a question about where the axon begins.","Correct. Axon terminals are the end branches that contact the target cell."],"lt":"t-nervous-tissue","lid":"c31","comp":174,"competencyId":"w8-neuron-parts"},{"id":"L02708","dok":2,"q":"The axon hillock forms the junction between which two parts of a neuron?","a":"The cell body and the axon.","options":["Two neighboring myelin segments","The cell body and the axon","The dendrites and the cell body","The axon and its terminals"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The axon hillock is the cone-shaped region of the cell body that tapers into the beginning of the axon. It is the boundary between the receiving and processing part of the neuron, the cell body, and the conducting part, the axon. Each neuron has one axon, so it has one axon hillock.","notes":["The bare gap between two myelin segments is a node of Ranvier. Choose this for a question about where the impulse jumps during saltatory conduction.","Correct. The axon hillock is where the cell body narrows into the axon.","Dendrites join the cell body directly, with no cone-shaped region between them. This pairing would fit a question about where incoming signals travel after a dendrite receives them.","The axon ends by branching into axon terminals at its far end, which is the opposite end from the hillock. Pick this pairing for a question about how the axon ends."],"lt":"t-nervous-tissue","lid":"c33","comp":174,"competencyId":"w8-neuron-parts"},{"id":"L02709","dok":2,"q":"A neuron in the retina has one dendrite and one axon. Which structural class is it?","a":"A bipolar neuron.","options":["A unipolar neuron","A multipolar neuron","A Purkinje cell","A bipolar neuron"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Bipolar neurons have exactly two processes, one dendrite and one axon, leaving opposite sides of the cell body. They are rare and found in special sense organs: the retina of the eye, the inner ear, and the olfactory area. The structural class is set by how many processes leave the cell body, so two processes means bipolar.","notes":["A unipolar neuron has a single process leaving the cell body, and it is the typical shape of a sensory neuron. Pick unipolar for a question about the shape of most sensory neurons.","A multipolar neuron has many dendrites and one axon and is the most common type in the brain and spinal cord. Choose it for a question about motor neurons or interneurons.","A Purkinje cell is a distinctive multipolar neuron of the cerebellum with a large, branching tree of dendrites. It would be right for a question naming a special neuron of the cerebellum.","Correct. Bipolar neurons have one dendrite and one axon."],"lt":"t-nervous-tissue","lid":"c34","comp":176,"competencyId":"w8-neuron-classification"},{"id":"L02710","dok":1,"q":"A synapse is a site where a neuron passes its signal to another cell. Which list gives the kinds of cells a neuron can synapse with?","a":"A neuron, a muscle cell, or a gland cell.","options":["A glial cell, blood cell, or skin cell","A muscle cell, bone cell, or fat cell","A neuron, muscle cell, or gland cell","Only the dendrite of another neuron"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"A synapse is the junction where a neuron communicates with another cell. The target can be another neuron, a muscle cell, or a gland cell, which is why muscles and glands are grouped together as the effectors of the nervous system. The synapse between a motor neuron and a skeletal muscle fiber has its own name, the neuromuscular junction.","notes":["This list misses all three main targets. A neuron synapses with another neuron, a muscle cell, or a gland cell. Glial cells are the support cells around neurons, and blood cells and skin cells are not on that list.","Muscle cells are real synaptic targets, but this list swaps bone cells and fat cells in for the other two targets, neurons and gland cells. It would only be right if the question asked about muscle alone.","Correct. Neurons synapse with other neurons and with the effectors, muscles and glands.","Dendrites are common targets, but axons also end on cell bodies, on other axons, and on muscle and gland cells. This would fit a question about an axodendritic synapse specifically."],"lt":"t-nervous-tissue","lid":"c35","comp":175,"competencyId":"w8-synapse-parts"},{"id":"L02711","dok":2,"q":"At a synapse, which term names the neuron that sends the signal across the cleft?","a":"The presynaptic neuron.","options":["The afferent neuron","The presynaptic neuron","The postsynaptic neuron","The efferent neuron"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"At a synapse, pre means before and post means after, measured from the synaptic cleft. The presynaptic neuron is the sender, and its synaptic end bulbs release neurotransmitter into the cleft. The postsynaptic cell, which may be a neuron, muscle cell, or gland cell, receives that neurotransmitter on the far side.","notes":["Afferent describes a neuron that carries signals toward the CNS, which is a functional class, not a position at a synapse. Choose it for a question about sensory neurons.","Correct. Pre means before, so the presynaptic neuron sits before the cleft and sends the signal.","The postsynaptic neuron is on the receiving side of the cleft, after the gap. Pick it for a question about the cell that receives the neurotransmitter.","Efferent describes a neuron that carries commands away from the CNS, another functional class. It would be right for a question about motor neurons."],"lt":"t-nervous-tissue","lid":"c36","comp":175,"competencyId":"w8-synapse-parts"},{"id":"L02712","dok":3,"q":"After a small stroke, glial cells in the damaged brain tissue engulf the dead cells and debris. Which glial cells are doing this?","a":"Microglia.","options":["Oligodendrocytes","Astrocytes","Microglia","Ependymal cells"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Microglia are small phagocytic glial cells of the CNS, meaning they engulf and digest material. When brain tissue is damaged, as in a stroke, they move in and remove dead cells, debris, and any microbes. This makes them the defense and cleanup crew of the brain and spinal cord.","notes":["Oligodendrocytes form the myelin sheaths around CNS axons. Choose them for a question about myelin loss in the brain, as in multiple sclerosis.","Astrocytes support neurons, help form the blood-brain barrier, and keep the chemical environment stable, but engulfing debris is not their main role. Pick them for a question about the blood-brain barrier.","Correct. Microglia are the phagocytic cleanup and defense cells of the CNS.","Ependymal cells line the ventricles and central canal and help circulate cerebrospinal fluid. They would be right for a question about the lining of the brain cavities."],"lt":"t-nervous-tissue","lid":"c37","comp":177,"competencyId":"w8-neuroglia"},{"id":"L02713","dok":3,"q":"Trace the path a signal takes through one neuron, from where it is received to where it is handed off. Which order is correct?","a":"Dendrites, cell body, axon hillock, axon, axon terminals.","options":["Dendrites, cell body, axon hillock, axon, axon terminals","Axon terminals, axon, axon hillock, cell body, dendrites","Dendrites, axon, cell body, axon hillock, axon terminals","Axon, dendrites, cell body, axon hillock, axon terminals"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Signals arrive at the dendrites, the receiving processes, and travel toward the cell body. From the cell body the signal reaches the axon hillock, where the axon begins, then runs along the axon to the axon terminals, which pass it to the next cell. The direction is always dendrites in and axon out.","notes":["Correct. The signal is received, passes through the cell body, and leaves along the axon.","This is the correct path run backward, from the sending end to the receiving end. It would fit a question that asked for the order from the axon terminals back to the dendrites.","This sends the signal into the axon before the cell body, but the axon begins at the hillock on the far side of the cell body. Students who choose this often forget that the hillock is where the axon starts.","This starts at the axon, but the axon is the outgoing process, not where signals arrive. Students who choose this mix up the axon, which sends, with the dendrites, which receive."],"lt":"t-nervous-tissue","lid":"c38","comp":174,"competencyId":"w8-neuron-parts"},{"id":"L02715","dok":3,"q":"Each choice pairs a glial cell with its location and its job. Which pairing is fully correct?","a":"The oligodendrocyte is a CNS glial cell that myelinates CNS axons.","options":["The oligodendrocyte is a CNS glial cell that myelinates CNS axons","The satellite cell is a CNS glial cell that lines brain cavities","The Schwann cell is a CNS glial cell that myelinates CNS axons","The astrocyte is a PNS glial cell that supports ganglion cell bodies"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Each glial cell belongs to one division and has one main job. In the CNS, oligodendrocytes form myelin, astrocytes support neurons and help form the blood-brain barrier, microglia defend, and ependymal cells line the cavities. In the PNS, Schwann cells form myelin and satellite cells surround cell bodies in ganglia.","notes":["Correct. Oligodendrocytes are CNS glia, and they form the myelin in the brain and spinal cord.","Satellite cells are PNS glia that surround cell bodies in ganglia, and the cells that line brain cavities are ependymal cells. Pick satellite cells for a question about support in a ganglion.","The Schwann cell does make myelin, but it works in the PNS, not the CNS. This pairing would be correct if it said the Schwann cell is a PNS glial cell that myelinates PNS axons.","Astrocytes are CNS glia that support neurons and help form the blood-brain barrier; the cells around ganglion cell bodies are satellite cells. Choose astrocytes for a question about the blood-brain barrier."],"lt":"t-nervous-tissue","lid":"c40","comp":177,"competencyId":"w8-neuroglia"},{"id":"L02716","dok":2,"q":"You touch a hot pan and pull your hand away. Which sequence lists the three functions of the nervous system in the order they happen?","a":"Sensory, integrative, motor.","options":["Sensory, integrative, motor","Integrative, sensory, motor","Motor, sensory, integrative","Sensory, motor, integrative"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The nervous system handles every task in three steps. In the sensory function, receptors detect a change, here the heat, and send input toward the CNS. In the integrative function, the CNS analyzes the input and decides on a response, and in the motor function, commands go out to the effectors, here the arm muscles that pull the hand away.","notes":["Correct. The stimulus is detected, the CNS processes it, and the muscles respond.","This has the CNS processing information before any sensory input has arrived, which cannot happen. The integrative step always needs incoming information to work on.","This puts the response first, before anything has been detected or processed. Students pick it when they focus on the movement and forget that the heat had to be sensed first.","This sends the response out before the CNS has decided on it. Integration has to come between sensing and responding, because the CNS decides what the response will be."],"lt":"t-nervous-tissue","lid":"c41","comp":173,"competencyId":"w8-ns-organization"},{"id":"L02717","dok":3,"q":"At a chemical synapse, which sequence lists the structures in order from the sending neuron to the receiving cell?","a":"Synaptic vesicles, synaptic cleft, postsynaptic cell.","options":["Synaptic cleft, synaptic vesicles, postsynaptic cell","Synaptic vesicles, synaptic cleft, postsynaptic cell","Postsynaptic cell, synaptic cleft, synaptic vesicles","Synaptic vesicles, postsynaptic cell, synaptic cleft"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The sending neuron stores neurotransmitter in synaptic vesicles inside its synaptic end bulbs. When a signal arrives, the vesicles release the neurotransmitter into the synaptic cleft, the narrow gap between the cells. The neurotransmitter diffuses across the cleft and reaches the postsynaptic cell on the other side.","notes":["This puts the cleft before the vesicles, but the vesicles sit inside the sending terminal, so the neurotransmitter starts there before it enters the cleft.","Correct. Neurotransmitter leaves the vesicles, crosses the cleft, and reaches the postsynaptic cell.","This is the correct order run backward, from the receiving cell to the sender. It would fit a question that asked for the order from the receiving side back to the sending side.","This has the neurotransmitter reach the receiving cell before crossing the gap. The cleft lies between the two cells, so it must be crossed first."],"lt":"t-nervous-tissue","lid":"c42","comp":175,"competencyId":"w8-synapse-parts"},{"id":"L02718","dok":3,"q":"Each choice gives the direction that each functional class of neuron carries signals relative to the CNS. Which set is fully correct?","a":"Sensory toward the CNS, motor away from the CNS, interneuron within the CNS.","options":["Sensory toward the CNS, motor away from the CNS, interneuron within the CNS","Sensory within the CNS, motor toward the CNS, interneuron away from the CNS","Sensory away from the CNS, motor toward the CNS, interneuron within the CNS","Sensory toward the CNS, motor within the CNS, interneuron away from the CNS"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Neurons are classified by function according to the direction they carry signals. Sensory (afferent) neurons carry input from receptors toward the CNS, motor (efferent) neurons carry commands away from the CNS to effectors, and interneurons connect the two entirely within the CNS. Remembering that interneurons never leave the CNS rules out most wrong sets quickly.","notes":["Correct. Sensory neurons bring input in, motor neurons send commands out, and interneurons connect them inside the CNS.","This places the sensory neuron inside the CNS and sends the interneuron out of it, but interneurons are the only class that stays entirely within the CNS.","This swaps the sensory and motor directions. Sensory input has to travel toward the CNS to be processed, and motor commands travel away to reach the effectors.","The sensory direction is right, but motor neurons must leave the CNS to reach muscles and glands, and interneurons never leave it."],"lt":"t-nervous-tissue","lid":"c43","comp":176,"competencyId":"w8-neuron-classification"},{"id":"L02719","dok":3,"q":"Which set correctly matches each of the four CNS glial cells to its main job?","a":"Astrocyte supports neurons; oligodendrocyte forms myelin; microglia defends; ependymal cell lines cavities.","options":["Astrocyte defends; oligodendrocyte lines cavities; microglia forms myelin; ependymal cell supports neurons","Astrocyte lines cavities; oligodendrocyte supports neurons; microglia forms myelin; ependymal cell defends","Astrocyte forms myelin; oligodendrocyte defends; microglia lines cavities; ependymal cell supports neurons","Astrocyte supports neurons; oligodendrocyte forms myelin; microglia defends; ependymal cell lines cavities"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The CNS has four kinds of glia, each with a main job. Astrocytes support neurons and help form the blood-brain barrier, oligodendrocytes form the myelin around CNS axons, microglia are phagocytes that clear debris and microbes, and ependymal cells line the ventricles and central canal and help circulate cerebrospinal fluid. Anchoring one pairing, such as oligodendrocytes with myelin, makes it easier to sort the rest.","notes":["This gives microglia the myelin job, but microglia are small phagocytic cells that clear debris. Myelin in the CNS is made by oligodendrocytes.","This gives the lining of the cavities to astrocytes, but the cells lining the ventricles and central canal are ependymal cells.","Every job here is shifted one cell over. Myelin in the CNS comes from oligodendrocytes, not astrocytes, so this set fails from its first pairing.","Correct. Each CNS glial cell is matched to its main job."],"lt":"t-nervous-tissue","lid":"c44","comp":177,"competencyId":"w8-neuroglia"},{"id":"L02720","dok":3,"q":"You decide to raise your arm. Which path does this voluntary motor command follow through the divisions of the nervous system?","a":"CNS, motor division of the PNS, somatic nervous system, skeletal muscle.","options":["CNS, sensory division of the PNS, somatic nervous system, skeletal muscle","Skeletal muscle, somatic nervous system, motor division of the PNS, CNS","CNS, motor division of the PNS, somatic nervous system, skeletal muscle","CNS, motor division of the PNS, autonomic nervous system, skeletal muscle"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"A voluntary command starts in the CNS, which decides on the response. It leaves through the motor (efferent) division of the PNS and specifically through the somatic nervous system, the voluntary branch whose motor neurons reach skeletal muscle. The skeletal muscle is the effector that carries out the movement.","notes":["The sensory division carries input toward the CNS, so it cannot carry a command out to a muscle. Pick the sensory division for a question about signals arriving from receptors.","This is the correct path run backward, from the muscle to the CNS. Commands travel outward from the CNS, so the CNS must come first.","Correct. A voluntary command leaves the CNS through the somatic part of the motor division.","The autonomic nervous system carries involuntary commands to smooth muscle, cardiac muscle, and glands, not to skeletal muscle. Choose it for a question about an involuntary response such as a change in heart rate."],"lt":"t-nervous-tissue","lid":"c45","comp":173,"competencyId":"w8-ns-organization"},{"id":"L02722","dok":2,"q":"The sensory receptors in the skin belong to which division of the nervous system?","a":"The peripheral nervous system.","options":["The central nervous system","The peripheral nervous system","The motor (efferent) division","The autonomic nervous system"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The peripheral nervous system includes every neural structure outside the brain and spinal cord: the nerves, the ganglia, and the sensory receptors. Receptors in the skin detect changes such as touch and temperature and start signals that sensory neurons carry toward the CNS. They feed the sensory (afferent) side of the PNS.","notes":["The CNS is only the brain and spinal cord, where sensory input is processed. Pick the CNS for a question about where the input from receptors is analyzed.","Correct. Sensory receptors lie outside the brain and spinal cord, so they are part of the PNS.","The motor division carries commands away from the CNS to effectors, while receptors feed the sensory side. It would be right for a question about signals leaving the CNS.","The autonomic nervous system is the involuntary motor branch to smooth muscle, cardiac muscle, and glands, so it carries commands out rather than receiving stimuli. Choose it for a question about control of the viscera."],"lt":"t-nervous-tissue","lid":"c2-x2","comp":173,"competencyId":"w8-ns-organization"},{"id":"L02723","dok":1,"q":"How many pairs of cranial nerves and spinal nerves does the body have?","a":"12 pairs of cranial nerves and 31 pairs of spinal nerves.","options":["10 pairs of cranial nerves and 33 pairs of spinal nerves","12 pairs of cranial nerves and 33 pairs of spinal nerves","12 pairs of cranial nerves and 31 pairs of spinal nerves","31 pairs of cranial nerves and 12 pairs of spinal nerves"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The nerves of the PNS come in pairs, one on each side. Twelve pairs of cranial nerves leave the brain, and 31 pairs of spinal nerves leave the spinal cord: 8 cervical, 12 thoracic, 5 lumbar, 5 sacral, and 1 coccygeal. These nerves are bundles of axons that carry signals between the CNS and the rest of the body.","notes":["Neither number is right. There are 12 pairs of cranial nerves, not 10, and 31 pairs of spinal nerves. The 33 comes from the count of vertebrae before the sacral and coccygeal ones fuse, which is a different number.","The cranial count is right, but there are 31 pairs of spinal nerves. The 33 is a mix-up with the count of vertebrae before fusion.","Correct. There are 12 cranial pairs and 31 spinal pairs.","This swaps the two numbers. The 12 pairs belong to the cranial nerves that attach to the brain, and the 31 pairs belong to the spinal nerves that attach to the spinal cord."],"lt":"t-nervous-tissue","lid":"c3-x2","comp":173,"competencyId":"w8-ns-organization"},{"id":"L02724","dok":1,"q":"Nissl bodies, clusters of rough ER and ribosomes, are found in which part of a neuron?","a":"The cell body.","options":["The synaptic end bulbs","The cell body","The axon","The axon hillock"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Nissl bodies are clusters of rough endoplasmic reticulum and ribosomes in the cell body, and they are where the neuron makes its proteins. Their presence is one reason the cell body is called the metabolic center of the neuron. The axon depends on proteins made in the cell body, which is why an axon cut off from its cell body dies.","notes":["Synaptic end bulbs are the swellings at the tips of the axon terminals that hold the synaptic vesicles. They would be right for a question about where neurotransmitter is stored.","Correct. Nissl bodies sit in the cell body, the metabolic center of the neuron.","The axon carries the impulse away and depends on the cell body for its proteins because it does not make them in bulk. Choose it for a question about the long outgoing process.","The axon hillock is the cone-shaped region where the axon begins, right next to the cell body, but it lacks Nissl bodies. Pick it for a question about where the axon joins the cell body."],"lt":"t-nervous-tissue","lid":"c4-x2","comp":174,"competencyId":"w8-neuron-parts"},{"id":"L02725","dok":1,"q":"What is the plasma membrane of the axon called?","a":"The axolemma.","options":["The axolemma","The axoplasm","The sarcolemma","The neurolemma"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Lemma means husk or covering, and the axolemma is the plasma membrane that surrounds the axon. The cytoplasm inside it is the axoplasm. The neurolemma sounds similar but belongs to the Schwann cell, a glial cell wrapped around the axon, not to the neuron.","notes":["Correct. The axolemma is the plasma membrane of the axon.","Axoplasm is the cytoplasm inside the axon, the contents rather than the membrane. Pick it for a question about the fluid inside the axon.","The sarcolemma is the plasma membrane of a muscle fiber, not of a neuron. It would be right for a question about the membrane surrounding a skeletal muscle cell.","The neurolemma is the outer layer of a Schwann cell, which wraps around a peripheral axon from the outside. Choose it for a question about what guides a cut peripheral axon as it regrows."],"lt":"t-nervous-tissue","lid":"c5-x2","comp":174,"competencyId":"w8-neuron-parts"},{"id":"L02726","dok":1,"q":"What is a bundle of axons within the central nervous system called?","a":"A tract.","options":["A tract","A nucleus","A ganglion","A nerve"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The same kind of structure gets a different name depending on whether it is in the CNS or the PNS. A bundle of axons inside the brain or spinal cord is a tract, and the matching bundle in the PNS is a nerve. Tracts make up much of the white matter, since their axons are usually myelinated.","notes":["Correct. A bundle of axons inside the CNS is a tract.","A nucleus is a cluster of neuron cell bodies in the CNS, not a bundle of axons. Choose it for a question about a group of cell bodies inside the brain or cord.","A ganglion is a cluster of neuron cell bodies in the PNS. It would be right for a question about cell bodies grouped outside the CNS.","A nerve is also a bundle of axons, but it lies in the PNS. Pick nerve when the bundle is outside the brain and spinal cord."],"lt":"t-nervous-tissue","lid":"c6-x2","comp":179,"competencyId":"w8-tissue-collections"},{"id":"L02727","dok":1,"q":"What is a cluster of neuron cell bodies in the peripheral nervous system called?","a":"A ganglion.","options":["A nucleus","A nerve","A tract","A ganglion"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"A ganglion is a cluster of neuron cell bodies located outside the CNS. A good example is the posterior root ganglion beside the spinal cord, which holds the cell bodies of sensory neurons. Inside the CNS the matching cluster of cell bodies is called a nucleus.","notes":["A nucleus is also a cluster of neuron cell bodies, but it lies inside the CNS. Pick nucleus when the cluster is in the brain or spinal cord.","A nerve is a bundle of axons in the PNS. It would be right for a question about a cable of axons running out to the body.","A tract is a bundle of axons inside the CNS, not a cluster of cell bodies. Choose it for a question about axon bundles in the brain or cord.","Correct. A cluster of cell bodies outside the CNS is a ganglion."],"lt":"t-nervous-tissue","lid":"c7-x2","comp":179,"competencyId":"w8-tissue-collections"},{"id":"L02728","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares how Schwann cells and oligodendrocytes myelinate axons?","a":"One Schwann cell wraps one axon segment; one oligodendrocyte wraps segments of many axons.","options":["One Schwann cell wraps many axons; one oligodendrocyte wraps just one axon segment","Both cells wrap exactly one segment of one axon, but in different divisions","Schwann cells myelinate CNS axons; oligodendrocytes myelinate PNS axons","One Schwann cell wraps one axon segment; one oligodendrocyte wraps many axons"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Both cells make myelin, but they do it differently. A Schwann cell, in the PNS, wraps a single segment of one axon and leaves its outer layer, the neurolemma, on the outside. An oligodendrocyte, in the CNS, sends out several processes that each wrap a segment of a different axon, and it leaves no neurolemma.","notes":["This reverses the two cells. Students who choose it often picture the Schwann cell as the larger cell, but it is the oligodendrocyte that sends processes to many axons.","The difference in division is real, Schwann cells in the PNS and oligodendrocytes in the CNS, but only the Schwann cell is limited to one axon segment.","This swaps their locations. Schwann cells work in the PNS and oligodendrocytes in the CNS.","Correct. A Schwann cell serves one segment of one axon, while an oligodendrocyte reaches several axons."],"lt":"t-nervous-tissue","lid":"c8-x2","comp":178,"competencyId":"w8-myelination"},{"id":"L02730","dok":2,"q":"During saltatory conduction along a myelinated axon, the impulse jumps between which structures?","a":"The nodes of Ranvier.","options":["The axon terminals","The Nissl bodies","The synaptic clefts","The nodes of Ranvier"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Saltatory comes from a word meaning to leap. On a myelinated axon the impulse leaps from one node of Ranvier, a bare gap in the myelin, to the next, skipping the insulated stretches in between. That is why myelinated axons conduct impulses much faster than unmyelinated ones.","notes":["Axon terminals are the end branches of the axon that contact the next cell. They would be right for a question about where the signal is handed off.","Nissl bodies are clusters of rough ER and ribosomes in the cell body that make proteins. Choose them for a question about protein synthesis in a neuron.","Synaptic clefts are the gaps between two cells at a synapse, at the end of the axon rather than along it. Pick them for a question about the space that neurotransmitter crosses.","Correct. The impulse jumps from one node of Ranvier to the next."],"lt":"t-nervous-tissue","lid":"c10-x2","comp":178,"competencyId":"w8-myelination"},{"id":"L02731","dok":2,"q":"Motor neurons and interneurons share which structural class?","a":"Both are multipolar.","options":["Motor is unipolar; interneuron is bipolar","Both are unipolar","Both are multipolar","Both are bipolar"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Functional class and structural class are separate ways of naming a neuron, but they line up in a pattern. Motor neurons and interneurons are both multipolar, with many dendrites and one axon, while most sensory neurons are unipolar. Multipolar is the most common structural class in the brain and spinal cord for this reason.","notes":["Neither half is right, since both of these functional classes are multipolar. This mix-up comes from linking the functional names to the wrong shapes.","Unipolar is the typical shape of sensory neurons, not motor neurons or interneurons. Pick unipolar for a question about the shape of most sensory neurons.","Correct. Motor neurons and interneurons both have many dendrites and one axon.","Bipolar neurons have one dendrite and one axon and are found in the retina, inner ear, and olfactory area. Choose bipolar for a question about special sense organs."],"lt":"t-nervous-tissue","lid":"c11-x2","comp":176,"competencyId":"w8-neuron-classification"},{"id":"L02732","dok":2,"q":"Which pairing of a functional class of neuron with its typical structural class is correct?","a":"Sensory neuron, unipolar.","options":["Motor neuron, bipolar","Interneuron, unipolar","Motor neuron, unipolar","Sensory neuron, unipolar"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The two ways of classifying neurons, by function and by shape, line up in a predictable way. Most sensory (afferent) neurons are unipolar, with a single process leaving the cell body that splits into two branches. Motor neurons and interneurons are both multipolar.","notes":["Motor neurons are multipolar, with many dendrites and one axon. Bipolar neurons are the rare type found in the retina, inner ear, and olfactory area.","Interneurons are multipolar, like motor neurons. Unipolar is the shape linked to sensory neurons, so pick unipolar for a question about them.","Motor neurons are multipolar, not unipolar. Students choose this when they remember that unipolar belongs to one functional class but forget which one.","Correct. Most sensory neurons are unipolar."],"lt":"t-nervous-tissue","lid":"c12-x2","comp":176,"competencyId":"w8-neuron-classification"},{"id":"L02734","dok":1,"q":"Which pigment harmlessly builds up in neurons with age?","a":"Lipofuscin.","options":["Nissl substance","Myelin","Melanin","Lipofuscin"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Lipofuscin is a harmless pigment that builds up inside the cell bodies of neurons as a person ages. Neurons are long-lived cells that do not divide, so this pigment gradually collects in them over the years. It is a sign of age, not of disease.","notes":["Nissl substance (Nissl bodies) is clusters of rough ER and ribosomes in the cell body where proteins are made. It would be right for a question about the site of protein synthesis.","Myelin is a fatty wrapping made by glial cells around axons, not a pigment that builds up with age. Choose it for a question about what insulates axons.","Melanin is the pigment made by melanocytes in the epidermis that colors the skin and hair. Pick it for a question about skin color or protection from UV light.","Correct. Lipofuscin is the harmless pigment that collects in aging neurons."],"lt":"t-nervous-tissue","lid":"c14-x2","comp":174,"competencyId":"w8-neuron-parts"},{"id":"L02736","dok":2,"q":"A cluster of neuron cell bodies sits deep inside the brain. What is this cluster called?","a":"A nucleus.","options":["A ganglion","A nerve","A nucleus","A tract"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"In nervous tissue, a cluster of neuron cell bodies inside the CNS is called a nucleus, and the matching cluster outside the CNS is called a ganglion. Because this cluster sits inside the brain, it is a nucleus. This use of nucleus is different from the nucleus of a single cell, which is the organelle that holds its DNA.","notes":["A ganglion is also a cluster of neuron cell bodies, but it lies outside the CNS. Pick ganglion when the cluster sits in the PNS, such as beside the spinal cord on a posterior root.","A nerve is a bundle of axons in the PNS. It would be right for a question about a cable of axons running out to the body.","Correct. A cluster of cell bodies inside the CNS is a nucleus.","A tract is a bundle of axons inside the CNS, not a cluster of cell bodies. Choose it for a question about axon bundles running through the brain or cord."],"lt":"t-nervous-tissue","lid":"c16-x2","comp":179,"competencyId":"w8-tissue-collections"},{"id":"L02737","dok":1,"q":"Interneurons are also known by which other name?","a":"Association neurons.","options":["Bipolar neurons","Association neurons","Efferent neurons","Afferent neurons"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Interneurons are also called association neurons because they associate, or connect, other neurons. They lie entirely within the brain and spinal cord, linking sensory neurons to motor neurons and to each other. Structurally they are multipolar.","notes":["Bipolar is a structural class (one dendrite and one axon) found in special sense organs, not a functional name. It would be right for a question about neurons in the retina.","Correct. Interneurons are also called association neurons because they link other neurons together.","Efferent neurons are motor neurons that carry commands away from the CNS. Choose efferent for a question asking for the other name of motor neurons.","Afferent neurons are sensory neurons that carry signals toward the CNS. Pick afferent for a question asking for the other name of sensory neurons."],"lt":"t-nervous-tissue","lid":"c17-x2","comp":176,"competencyId":"w8-neuron-classification"},{"id":"L02738","dok":2,"q":"A brain tumor is far more likely to arise from glial cells than from neurons. Which property of neurons explains this?","a":"Neurons cannot divide.","options":["Neurons cannot divide","Neurons lack a nucleus","Neurons have no cell membrane","Neurons divide faster than glia"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"A tumor is a mass of cells that keep dividing, so it can only arise from cells that are able to divide. Mature neurons generally do not divide, but glial cells still can. That is why most tumors of nervous tissue come from glia. A tumor of glial cells is a glioma, the most common primary malignant brain tumor.","notes":["Correct. A tumor needs dividing cells, and mature neurons do not divide.","Neurons do have a nucleus, which sits in the cell body. Students choose this when they mix up not dividing with having no nucleus, as with mature red blood cells.","Neurons have a plasma membrane, and the part around the axon is called the axolemma. This choice comes from a misunderstanding of what a neuron is built from.","This is the reverse of the truth. If neurons divided faster, they would be the more likely source of a tumor, not the less likely one."],"lt":"t-nervous-tissue","lid":"c18-x2","comp":178,"competencyId":"w8-myelination"},{"id":"L02739","dok":1,"q":"What is the neurolemma?","a":"The outer layer of a Schwann cell.","options":["The outer layer of a Schwann cell","The plasma membrane that surrounds the axon","The gap between myelin segments","The cytoplasm inside the axon"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"When a Schwann cell wraps an axon, the layers of its membrane form the myelin sheath, and its outer layer, which holds the nucleus and most of the cytoplasm, is left on the outside as the neurolemma. The neurolemma forms a tube that guides a cut peripheral axon as it regrows. Oligodendrocytes in the CNS do not leave a neurolemma, which is one reason CNS tracts rarely regrow.","notes":["Correct. The neurolemma is the outer layer of the Schwann cell, holding its nucleus and cytoplasm.","The plasma membrane of the axon is the axolemma, which belongs to the neuron itself. Pick axolemma for a question about the axon membrane.","A gap between myelin segments is a node of Ranvier. Choose it for a question about where the impulse jumps in saltatory conduction.","The cytoplasm inside the axon is the axoplasm. It would be right for a question about the contents of the axon."],"lt":"t-nervous-tissue","lid":"c19-x2","comp":178,"competencyId":"w8-myelination"},{"id":"L02740","dok":2,"q":"An axon ends on a dendrite of another neuron. What type of synapse is this?","a":"An axodendritic synapse.","options":["An axosomatic synapse","An axodendritic synapse","An axoaxonic synapse","A neuromuscular junction"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Synapses between neurons are named for the two parts that meet, always starting with the axon of the sending neuron. An axon ending on a dendrite is axodendritic, on a cell body is axosomatic, and on another axon is axoaxonic. Dendrites are the most common receiving site because they are the receiving processes of the neuron.","notes":["An axosomatic synapse is an axon ending on the cell body (soma) of another neuron. Pick it when the axon contacts a cell body.","Correct. Axo plus dendritic names an axon ending on a dendrite.","An axoaxonic synapse is an axon ending on another axon. Choose it when both sides of the synapse are axons.","A neuromuscular junction is the synapse between a motor neuron and a skeletal muscle fiber, not between two neurons. It would be right for a question about where a nerve meets muscle."],"lt":"t-nervous-tissue","lid":"c20-x2","comp":175,"competencyId":"w8-synapse-parts"},{"id":"L02741","dok":1,"q":"Which part of the nervous system is a network of neurons in the wall of the GI tract that can work on its own?","a":"The enteric nervous system","options":["The central nervous system","The enteric nervous system","The sensory (afferent) division","The somatic nervous system"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The enteric nervous system, sometimes called the brain of the gut, is a network of neurons in the wall of the GI tract. It controls the smooth muscle and glands of the gut and can operate on its own, even without input from the brain. It is one of the divisions of the motor side of the PNS, alongside the somatic and autonomic systems.","notes":["The CNS is the brain and spinal cord, not a network in the wall of the gut. Choose it for a question about where most information is processed.","Correct. The enteric nervous system is the gut's own network of neurons.","The sensory division carries input from receptors all over the body toward the CNS. It would be right for a question about signals traveling into the brain and cord.","The somatic nervous system is the voluntary motor branch that carries commands to skeletal muscle. Pick it for a question about voluntary movement."],"lt":"t-nervous-tissue","lid":"c21-x2","comp":173,"competencyId":"w8-ns-organization"},{"id":"L02742","dok":2,"q":"An axon ends on the cell body of another neuron. What type of synapse is this?","a":"An axosomatic synapse.","options":["An axoaxonic synapse","A neuromuscular junction","An axosomatic synapse","An axodendritic synapse"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Synapses between neurons are named for the parts that meet, starting with the axon of the sending neuron. The cell body is also called the soma, so an axon ending on a cell body forms an axosomatic synapse. The other two types are axodendritic, ending on a dendrite, and axoaxonic, ending on another axon.","notes":["An axoaxonic synapse is an axon ending on another axon. Choose it when both sides of the synapse are axons.","A neuromuscular junction is the synapse between a motor neuron and a skeletal muscle fiber. It would be right for a question about where a nerve meets a muscle.","Correct. Soma means cell body, so an axon ending on a cell body forms an axosomatic synapse.","An axodendritic synapse is an axon ending on a dendrite, the most common arrangement. Pick it when the axon contacts a dendrite."],"lt":"t-nervous-tissue","lid":"c22-x2","comp":175,"competencyId":"w8-synapse-parts"},{"id":"L02743","dok":2,"q":"On a myelinated axon, the impulse jumps from one node of Ranvier to the next. What is this type of conduction called?","a":"Saltatory conduction.","options":["Saltatory conduction","Continuous conduction","Decussation","Convergence"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Saltatory comes from a word meaning to leap. Myelin insulates the axon, so on a myelinated axon the impulse leaps from one node of Ranvier, a bare gap in the myelin, to the next. This skipping is why myelinated axons conduct much faster than unmyelinated ones.","notes":["Correct. Saltatory conduction is the jumping pattern seen on myelinated axons.","Continuous conduction is the slower, step-by-step spread along an unmyelinated axon, which has no nodes to jump between. Pick it for a question about axons without myelin.","Decussation is the crossing of a pathway to the opposite side of the CNS. Choose it for a question about why one side of the brain serves the other side of the body.","Convergence is a circuit pattern in which many neurons feed onto one neuron. It would be right for a question about how neurons are wired in a neuronal pool."],"lt":"t-nervous-tissue","lid":"c23-x2","comp":178,"competencyId":"w8-myelination"},{"id":"L02744","dok":1,"q":"Which distinctive multipolar neuron is found in the cerebellum?","a":"The Purkinje cell.","options":["The pyramidal cell","The Purkinje cell","The bipolar neuron","The unipolar neuron"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Purkinje cells are large multipolar neurons with an elaborate, fan-shaped tree of dendrites, and they are found in the cortex of the cerebellum. They are given their own name because of that distinctive shape, but structurally they are still multipolar. Their partner name to remember is the pyramidal cell, the distinctive multipolar neuron of the cerebral cortex.","notes":["Pyramidal cells are also distinctive multipolar neurons, but they are found in the cerebral cortex. Pick the pyramidal cell for a question about the cerebral cortex.","Correct. Purkinje cells are the distinctive multipolar neurons of the cerebellum.","Bipolar neurons have one dendrite and one axon and are found in the retina, inner ear, and olfactory area. Choose bipolar for a question about special sense organs.","Unipolar neurons have a single process and are the typical shape of sensory neurons. It would be right for a question about the shape of most sensory neurons."],"lt":"t-nervous-tissue","lid":"c24-x2","comp":176,"competencyId":"w8-neuron-classification"},{"id":"L02745","dok":3,"q":"A patient has a slow-growing mass of dividing cells in the brain. Which cell type most likely gave rise to it?","a":"A glial cell.","options":["A multipolar neuron","A glial cell","An interneuron","A sensory neuron"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"A tumor is a mass of dividing cells, so it must start from a cell type that can still divide. Mature neurons generally do not divide, but glial cells can. A tumor of glial cells is a glioma, the most common primary malignant brain tumor.","notes":["Multipolar neurons are the most common neurons in the brain, but mature neurons cannot divide, so they rarely form tumors. Pick multipolar for a question about the shape of motor neurons and interneurons.","Correct. Glial cells can still divide, so most brain tumors start from glia.","Interneurons connect sensory and motor neurons inside the CNS, but as neurons they cannot divide. It would be right for a question about the neurons that link input to output.","Sensory neurons carry signals toward the CNS, and like all mature neurons they cannot divide. Choose it for a question about input from receptors."],"lt":"t-nervous-tissue","lid":"c26-x2","comp":178,"competencyId":"w8-myelination"},{"id":"L02746","dok":3,"q":"An infant's nerve impulses travel more slowly than an adult's. Which structural difference best explains this?","a":"The infant has less myelination.","options":["The infant has less myelination","The infant's neurons lack dendrites","The infant's neurons lack nuclei","The infant has fewer neurons"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Myelin insulates axons and lets the impulse jump from node to node, which makes conduction much faster. Myelination is not complete at birth; it increases from birth to maturity. With less myelin, an infant's nerve conduction is slower than an adult's.","notes":["Correct. Myelination increases from birth to maturity, so infant axons conduct more slowly.","Infant neurons have dendrites, and dendrites receive signals rather than set the speed of conduction along the axon. Pick dendrites for a question about where a neuron receives input.","Every neuron has a nucleus in its cell body, at any age. This choice comes from mixing up neurons with cells such as mature red blood cells that lose their nucleus.","Neurons cannot divide, so a person does not add large numbers of new neurons after birth. A smaller neuron count would not explain slower conduction along each axon anyway."],"lt":"t-nervous-tissue","lid":"c27-x2","comp":178,"competencyId":"w8-myelination"},{"id":"L02747","dok":3,"q":"A surgeon repairing a severed nerve in the forearm lines up the cut ends so the axons can regrow. Which feature of the PNS makes this regrowth possible?","a":"The neurolemma of the Schwann cells.","options":["The neurolemma of Schwann cells","The end-feet of astrocytes","The phagocytic action of microglia","The myelin of the oligodendrocytes"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"In the PNS, each Schwann cell leaves its outer layer, the neurolemma, wrapped around the axon. After a cut, the neurolemma forms a tube that guides the regrowing axon back toward its target. In the CNS, oligodendrocytes leave no neurolemma, which is why a cut nerve in the arm can regrow but a cut tract in the cord usually cannot.","notes":["Correct. The neurolemma forms a guiding tube for the regrowing axon.","Astrocytes are CNS glia whose end-feet wrap capillaries and help form the blood-brain barrier. They would be right for a question about the blood-brain barrier.","Microglia are CNS cleanup cells, not part of peripheral nerves. Choose them for a question about clearing debris after a brain injury.","Oligodendrocytes are CNS cells and are not found in a forearm nerve, and they leave no neurolemma. Pick them for a question about why a cut tract in the spinal cord usually cannot regrow."],"lt":"t-nervous-tissue","lid":"c28-x2","comp":178,"competencyId":"w8-myelination"},{"id":"L02748","dok":2,"q":"A cluster of neuron cell bodies sits just outside the spinal cord on a posterior root. Which term names it?","a":"A ganglion.","options":["A tract","A nucleus","A nerve","A ganglion"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"A cluster of neuron cell bodies outside the CNS is a ganglion. The cluster described here is the posterior (dorsal) root ganglion, which holds the cell bodies of the sensory neurons for that segment of the cord. Inside the CNS, the same kind of cluster would be called a nucleus.","notes":["A tract is a bundle of axons inside the CNS. Choose it for a question about the axon bundles in the white matter of the cord.","A nucleus is also a cluster of cell bodies, but it lies inside the CNS. Pick nucleus if the cluster were within the gray matter of the cord or brain.","A nerve is a bundle of axons outside the CNS, not a cluster of cell bodies. It would be right for a question about the spinal nerve formed where the roots join.","Correct. A cluster of cell bodies outside the CNS is a ganglion."],"lt":"t-nervous-tissue","lid":"c29-x2","comp":179,"competencyId":"w8-tissue-collections"},{"id":"L02749","dok":3,"q":"In multiple sclerosis, the immune system strips a covering from CNS axons and signals slow or fail. Which structure is being lost?","a":"The myelin sheath.","options":["The dendrites","The cell body","The myelin sheath","The synaptic vesicles"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The myelin sheath is a lipid-rich covering that insulates axons and lets the impulse jump from node to node. In multiple sclerosis, the immune system destroys the myelin around CNS axons, the myelin made by oligodendrocytes. Without it, signals along those axons slow down or fail.","notes":["Dendrites are the branched receiving processes of a neuron, not a covering. Choose them for a question about where a neuron receives signals.","The cell body holds the nucleus and is the metabolic center, but it is not a covering around the axon. Pick it for a question about where Nissl bodies are found.","Correct. Multiple sclerosis destroys the myelin around CNS axons.","Synaptic vesicles are sacs of neurotransmitter inside the axon terminals. They would be right for a question about where neurotransmitter is stored."],"lt":"t-nervous-tissue","lid":"c30-x2","comp":178,"competencyId":"w8-myelination"},{"id":"L02750","dok":2,"q":"A typical neuron has many dendrites but only one axon. Which statement best explains how this shape fits its job?","a":"Dendrites gather input from many cells, and the single axon sends out one output.","options":["Dendrites gather input from many cells; the axon sends one output","Dendrites and the axon both gather input from many other cells","Dendrites make the proteins; the axon stores the neurotransmitter","Dendrites send output to many cells; the axon gathers the input"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Dendrites are the receiving processes of the neuron, and having many of them gives a large surface for synapses from many other cells. The neuron combines this input and, if it fires, sends a single impulse out along its one axon. The axon can then branch at its end into many axon terminals, so that one output can still reach several target cells.","notes":["Correct. Many dendrites collect input, and one axon carries the result away.","Only the dendrites are a receiving surface. The axon carries the impulse away from the cell body toward other cells.","This gives the dendrites and the axon the wrong jobs. Proteins are made mainly at the Nissl bodies of the cell body, and neurotransmitter is stored in vesicles in the synaptic end bulbs at the axon terminals, not along the axon.","This reverses the jobs. Dendrites receive signals and carry them toward the cell body, while the axon carries the impulse away."],"lt":"t-nervous-tissue","lid":"d3-t-nervous-tissue-1","comp":174,"competencyId":"w8-neuron-parts"},{"id":"L02751","dok":2,"q":"Why does a myelin sheath make an axon conduct impulses faster?","a":"The impulse jumps from node to node instead of moving along every part of the membrane.","options":["Myelin shortens the axon, so the impulse has less distance to go","The impulse jumps from node to node, skipping the insulated parts","The nodes of Ranvier release neurotransmitter along the axon","Myelin carries the impulse itself, so the axon membrane is bypassed"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Myelin is a multilayered, lipid-rich covering that insulates the axon. Because of that insulation, the impulse does not spread step by step along the covered stretches. Instead it jumps from one node of Ranvier, a bare gap in the myelin, to the next. This saltatory conduction is much faster than conduction along an unmyelinated axon.","notes":["Myelin wraps around the axon without changing its length. The gain in speed comes from the jumping pattern, not from a shorter path.","Correct. Myelin insulates the axon, so the impulse leaps between the nodes of Ranvier.","Neurotransmitter is released only at the axon terminals, at synapses. The nodes are simply bare gaps in the myelin that the impulse jumps between.","Myelin is an insulating wrap made by glial cells; it does not carry the impulse. The impulse still travels on the axon membrane, just only at the nodes."],"lt":"t-nervous-tissue","lid":"d3-t-nervous-tissue-2","comp":178,"competencyId":"w8-myelination"},{"id":"L02752","dok":2,"q":"At a chemical synapse the two cells do not touch. How does the signal get from one cell to the next?","a":"Neurotransmitter diffuses across the synaptic cleft.","options":["The two cell membranes fuse so the impulse passes directly","Neurotransmitter diffuses across the synaptic cleft","A strip of myelin bridges the gap between the two cells","The impulse jumps across the cleft as it does between nodes"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"At a chemical synapse there is no direct contact between the cells. When the impulse reaches the axon terminal, synaptic vesicles release neurotransmitter into the synaptic cleft. The neurotransmitter molecules diffuse across the cleft and reach the receiving cell, carrying the signal to it as a chemical message.","notes":["At a chemical synapse the membranes stay separate, with the synaptic cleft between them. Students choose this when they picture the two cells as joined.","Correct. The signal crosses the gap as a chemical message.","Myelin wraps axons to insulate them and does not cross from one cell to another. Pick myelin for a question about what speeds conduction along an axon.","Jumping from node to node happens along a myelinated axon, not across a synapse. At a chemical synapse the impulse itself stops at the terminal and a chemical carries the message over."],"lt":"t-nervous-tissue","lid":"d3-t-nervous-tissue-3","comp":175,"competencyId":"w8-synapse-parts"},{"id":"L02753","dok":2,"q":"Mature neurons cannot divide. Why does this make damage to the brain or spinal cord especially serious?","a":"Lost neurons are not readily replaced, so their function may be lost for good.","options":["The remaining neurons divide quickly to fill in the damaged area","Lost neurons are not replaced, so lost function may not return","Schwann cells move into the brain and guide new tracts to grow","Glial cells turn into new neurons and take over the lost function"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Mature neurons cannot divide, so when neurons in the brain or spinal cord die, there are no new neurons to take their place. In addition, CNS axons regrow very poorly because oligodendrocytes leave no neurolemma to guide them. Together these facts mean that a brain or spinal cord injury often causes lasting loss of function.","notes":["This contradicts the starting fact, since mature neurons do not divide. Dividing is something glial cells can do, not neurons.","Correct. Because neurons do not divide, neurons lost to injury are not replaced.","Schwann cells are PNS glia and do not repair CNS tracts. They would be right for a question about how a cut peripheral nerve regrows.","Glial cells can divide, but they do not take over the work of lost neurons. After a CNS injury, astrocytes fill the damaged area with scar tissue instead."],"lt":"t-nervous-tissue","lid":"d3-t-nervous-tissue-4","comp":178,"competencyId":"w8-myelination"},{"id":"L02754","dok":2,"q":"Glial cells do not carry nerve impulses. Which statement best describes why they are still essential?","a":"They support, nourish, insulate, and protect neurons.","options":["They replace dead neurons by dividing into new neurons","They support, nourish, insulate, and protect neurons","They carry nerve impulses from one neuron to the next","They store the neurotransmitter used at every synapse"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Neuroglia are the support cells of nervous tissue, and they make up about half the volume of the CNS. Each type has a job that keeps neurons working: astrocytes support neurons and maintain their environment, oligodendrocytes and Schwann cells form myelin, microglia clear debris, ependymal cells line the cavities, and satellite cells surround cell bodies in ganglia. Without them, neurons could not function or survive.","notes":["Glial cells can divide, but they do not replace lost neurons. After a CNS injury, astrocytes fill the damaged area with scar tissue instead.","Correct. Glia keep neurons supported, supplied, insulated, and protected.","This contradicts the question, since glia do not carry impulses. Neurons carry impulses, and synapses pass them from cell to cell.","Neurotransmitter is stored in synaptic vesicles inside the synaptic end bulbs of neurons, not in glial cells. Pick vesicles for a question about neurotransmitter storage."],"lt":"t-nervous-tissue","lid":"d3-t-nervous-tissue-5","comp":177,"competencyId":"w8-neuroglia"},{"id":"L02756","dok":2,"q":"The cell body of a neuron is packed with Nissl bodies, clusters of rough ER and ribosomes. Why does a neuron need so much protein-making machinery there?","a":"The whole neuron, even a very long axon, relies on proteins made in the cell body.","options":["The proteins are stored in the cell body as an energy reserve","The proteins are used to build new neurons when the cell divides","Nissl bodies make the myelin that wraps the length of the axon","The whole neuron, even a long axon, relies on cell body proteins"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Nissl bodies are where the neuron makes its proteins, and most of them sit in the cell body, the metabolic center of the neuron. Those proteins must supply the dendrites and an axon that can be very long, so the cell body keeps making them throughout the life of the neuron. This dependence is why the part of an axon cut off from its cell body dies.","notes":["Nissl bodies are protein factories, not storage depots for fuel. This choice mixes up making proteins with storing energy.","Mature neurons cannot divide, so the proteins are not used for division. They maintain the existing cell throughout its long life.","Myelin is made by glial cells, oligodendrocytes in the CNS and Schwann cells in the PNS, not by the neuron. Pick those cells for a question about where myelin comes from.","Correct. The cell body makes the proteins that the rest of the neuron depends on."],"lt":"t-nervous-tissue","lid":"d3-t-nervous-tissue-7","comp":174,"competencyId":"w8-neuron-parts"},{"id":"L02757","dok":3,"q":"A nerve in the forearm is cut cleanly. Which part of each cut axon dies?","a":"The part beyond the cut, separated from the cell body.","options":["The part beyond the cut, away from the cell body","The part still attached to the cell body","Only the cell body, while the whole axon survives","Neither part, because an axon can live on its own"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The cell body is the life support of the neuron because its Nissl bodies make the proteins that the axon needs. When an axon is cut, the part beyond the cut is separated from that supply and dies. The part still attached to the cell body survives, and in the PNS it can regrow along the tube formed by the neurolemma.","notes":["Correct. The part cut off from the cell body loses its protein supply and dies.","The part still connected to the cell body keeps receiving proteins, so it survives and is the part that can regrow. Students choose this when they forget that the cell body is the life support of the axon.","The cell body is the part most likely to survive a cut far out in the forearm, because it is still intact. The axon depends on the cell body, not the other way around.","An axon cannot make enough of its own proteins and depends on the cell body for them. Once separated, the distant part cannot survive."],"lt":"t-nervous-tissue","lid":"d3x-t-nervous-tissue-1","comp":178,"competencyId":"w8-myelination"},{"id":"L02758","dok":1,"q":"Gray matter is made mostly of which neural components?","a":"Cell bodies, dendrites, and unmyelinated axons.","options":["Myelinated axons running together in tracts","Cerebrospinal fluid and ependymal cells","Connective tissue sheaths around blood vessels","Cell bodies, dendrites, and unmyelinated axons"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Gray matter is nervous tissue made mostly of neuron cell bodies, dendrites, and unmyelinated axons, along with neuroglia. Because it lacks much myelin, it looks gray. It forms the outer cortex of the brain and the inner core of the spinal cord.","notes":["Myelinated axons in tracts make up white matter, and the fatty myelin is what gives it a white color. Pick this for a question about white matter.","Cerebrospinal fluid fills the ventricles and central canal, which are lined by ependymal cells. This would fit a question about the fluid-filled spaces of the CNS.","Connective tissue sheaths wrap nerves and vessels, but they are not what gray matter is made of. Choose connective tissue for a question about the coverings of a peripheral nerve.","Correct. Gray matter is where the cell bodies and unmyelinated fibers are."],"lt":"t-neuronal-integration","lid":"c1","comp":180,"competencyId":"w8-gray-white-matter"},{"id":"L02759","dok":1,"q":"White matter is made mostly of which structures?","a":"Myelinated axons running in bundles.","options":["Dendrites and their synapses on cell bodies","Clusters of neuron cell bodies","Myelinated axons running in bundles","Unmyelinated fibers and glial nuclei"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"White matter is nervous tissue made mostly of myelinated axons running together in bundles, the tracts of the CNS. The myelin around these axons is fatty, and that lipid is what gives the tissue its white color. It lies beneath the cortex in the brain and forms the outer part of the spinal cord.","notes":["Dendrites and synapses are concentrated in gray matter, where the cell bodies are. Choose them for a question about where neurons receive input.","Clusters of cell bodies are found in gray matter, as nuclei inside the CNS. Pick this for a question about what makes up gray matter.","Correct. White matter is bundles of myelinated axons.","Unmyelinated fibers belong to gray matter, since they lack the white myelin. This would fit a question about why gray matter looks gray."],"lt":"t-neuronal-integration","lid":"c2","comp":180,"competencyId":"w8-gray-white-matter"},{"id":"L02760","dok":2,"q":"Which pair of terms both name bundles of axons?","a":"Tract and nerve.","options":["Nucleus and ganglion","Nerve and ganglion","Tract and nucleus","Tract and nerve"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The nervous system uses four terms that sort into two pairs. A bundle of axons is a tract in the CNS and a nerve in the PNS, while a cluster of cell bodies is a nucleus in the CNS and a ganglion in the PNS. Deciding first whether the structure is axons or cell bodies, and then whether it is inside or outside the CNS, leads to the right term.","notes":["Nucleus and ganglion both name clusters of neuron cell bodies, one in the CNS and one in the PNS. Pick this pair for a question about clusters of cell bodies.","Nerve and ganglion are the two PNS terms, but a ganglion is a cluster of cell bodies. This pair would fit a question asking which terms are used outside the CNS.","Tract and nucleus are the two CNS terms, but a nucleus is a cluster of cell bodies, not a bundle of axons. Choose this pair for a question asking which terms are used inside the CNS.","Correct. A tract is a bundle of axons in the CNS, and a nerve is one in the PNS."],"lt":"t-neuronal-integration","lid":"c3","comp":179,"competencyId":"w8-tissue-collections"},{"id":"L02761","dok":1,"q":"What is a bundle of axons in the peripheral nervous system called?","a":"A nerve.","options":["A nucleus","A tract","A ganglion","A nerve"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"A nerve is a bundle of axons outside the CNS, wrapped in connective tissue. The body has 12 pairs of cranial nerves and 31 pairs of spinal nerves. Inside the brain and spinal cord, a bundle of axons is called a tract instead.","notes":["A nucleus is a cluster of neuron cell bodies inside the CNS. Choose it for a question about a group of cell bodies in the brain.","A tract is also a bundle of axons, but it lies inside the CNS. Pick tract when the bundle runs within the brain or spinal cord.","A ganglion is a cluster of neuron cell bodies in the PNS. It would be right for a question about the cell bodies grouped on a posterior root.","Correct. A bundle of axons in the PNS is a nerve."],"lt":"t-neuronal-integration","lid":"c4","comp":179,"competencyId":"w8-tissue-collections"},{"id":"L02762","dok":1,"q":"When the term nucleus names a structure in the brain, what does it mean?","a":"A cluster of neuron cell bodies in the CNS.","options":["A cluster of neuron cell bodies in the PNS","The organelle that holds a single cell's DNA","A bundle of myelinated axons in the CNS","A cluster of neuron cell bodies in the CNS"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"In the nervous system, nucleus has a second meaning besides the organelle in a single cell. A nucleus here is a cluster of neuron cell bodies inside the CNS, such as a cranial nerve nucleus in the brainstem or the basal nuclei deep in the cerebrum. The matching cluster outside the CNS is a ganglion.","notes":["A cluster of cell bodies in the PNS is a ganglion. Pick this for a question asking what a ganglion is.","This is the meaning of nucleus in cell biology, the control center of one cell. It would be right for a cell question, not for a named structure made of many neurons.","A bundle of axons in the CNS is a tract. Choose this for a question asking what a tract is.","Correct. In this sense a nucleus is a group of cell bodies inside the CNS."],"lt":"t-neuronal-integration","lid":"c5","comp":179,"competencyId":"w8-tissue-collections"},{"id":"L02763","dok":2,"q":"Which pair of terms both name clusters of neuron cell bodies?","a":"Nucleus and ganglion.","options":["Nucleus and tract","Ganglion and nerve","Tract and nerve","Nucleus and ganglion"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"A cluster of neuron cell bodies has two names depending on where it is: a nucleus inside the CNS and a ganglion in the PNS. Their axon-bundle counterparts are the tract in the CNS and the nerve in the PNS. Decide cell bodies or axons first, then inside or outside the CNS.","notes":["Nucleus and tract are the two CNS terms, but a tract is a bundle of axons. Choose this pair for a question asking which terms are used inside the CNS.","Ganglion and nerve are the two PNS terms, but a nerve is a bundle of axons. This pair would fit a question asking which terms are used outside the CNS.","Tract and nerve both name bundles of axons, one in the CNS and one in the PNS. Pick this pair for a question about bundles of axons.","Correct. A nucleus is a cluster of cell bodies in the CNS, and a ganglion is one in the PNS."],"lt":"t-neuronal-integration","lid":"c6","comp":179,"competencyId":"w8-tissue-collections"},{"id":"L02767","dok":1,"q":"What is a neuronal pool?","a":"A functional group of interconnected neurons in the CNS that process information together.","options":["A cluster of sensory cell bodies outside the cord","A fluid-filled cavity inside the brain, lined by glia","A group of linked CNS neurons that work together","A bundle of myelinated axons in a peripheral nerve"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Neurons rarely act alone. A neuronal pool is a functional group of interconnected neurons in the CNS that processes information together, and the way the pool is wired, such as convergence or divergence, shapes how a signal is handled. The term describes how neurons work as a team rather than naming a single anatomical part.","notes":["A cluster of sensory cell bodies outside the cord is a posterior root ganglion. This would be right for a question about where sensory neuron cell bodies sit.","A fluid-filled cavity lined by ependymal cells is a ventricle, which holds cerebrospinal fluid. Pick this for a question about the ventricles.","Correct. A neuronal pool is a functional group of linked neurons in the CNS.","A bundle of axons in the PNS is a nerve, a structural term rather than a working group. Choose this for a question asking what a nerve is."],"lt":"t-neuronal-integration","lid":"c10","comp":183,"competencyId":"w8-neuronal-pools"},{"id":"L02771","dok":2,"q":"The corpus callosum is a broad band of myelinated axons linking the two cerebral hemispheres. Which tissue type is it?","a":"White matter.","options":["A nucleus","A ganglion","Gray matter","White matter"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"White matter is made of myelinated axons running together in bundles, and the lipid-rich myelin gives it its white color. The corpus callosum is a large band of white matter that carries axons between the right and left cerebral hemispheres. In the cerebrum, white matter lies beneath the gray cortex.","notes":["A nucleus is a cluster of neuron cell bodies inside the CNS, which would be gray, not a band of axons. It would be right for a question about a group of cell bodies deep in the brain.","A ganglion is a cluster of neuron cell bodies outside the CNS. Choose it for a question about cell bodies grouped beside the spinal cord.","Gray matter is made of cell bodies, dendrites, and unmyelinated axons, such as the cerebral cortex. Pick it for a question about the outer layer of the cerebrum.","Correct. A band of myelinated axons is white matter."],"lt":"t-neuronal-integration","lid":"c14","comp":180,"competencyId":"w8-gray-white-matter"},{"id":"L02777","dok":1,"q":"Many neurons feed their signals onto a single target neuron. What is this circuit pattern called?","a":"Convergence.","options":["Divergence","Serial processing","Convergence","Parallel processing"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Convergence comes from a word meaning to come together. In a converging circuit, signals from many neurons funnel onto one target neuron, which then combines them into a single response. Its mirror image is divergence, in which one neuron spreads its signal to many.","notes":["Divergence is the opposite pattern, in which one neuron spreads its signal out to many. Pick it when a single neuron sends to many targets.","Serial processing is a signal passing along a single straight chain of neurons, as in a reflex arc. Choose it for a one-after-another chain.","Correct. Many inputs coming together onto one neuron is convergence.","Parallel processing is a signal sent along several pathways at the same time. It would be right for a question about one signal traveling several routes at once."],"lt":"t-neuronal-integration","lid":"c20","comp":183,"competencyId":"w8-neuronal-pools"},{"id":"L02778","dok":1,"q":"One neuron spreads its signal outward to many other neurons. What is this circuit pattern called?","a":"Divergence.","options":["Serial processing","Convergence","Integration","Divergence"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Divergence comes from a word meaning to branch apart. In a diverging circuit, one neuron passes its signal to many others, so a single input can affect many targets. Its mirror image is convergence, in which many neurons feed onto one.","notes":["Serial processing passes a signal along a single straight chain of neurons, one after another. Choose it for a reflex arc.","Convergence is the reverse, with many neurons feeding onto one. Pick it when many inputs funnel onto a single neuron.","Integration is the combining of incoming signals so that a neuron produces a single response, not the spreading of a signal. It would be right for a question about what a neuron does with many inputs.","Correct. One neuron sending to many is divergence."],"lt":"t-neuronal-integration","lid":"c21","comp":183,"competencyId":"w8-neuronal-pools"},{"id":"L02779","dok":1,"q":"A signal passes along a single straight chain of neurons, one after another, as in a reflex arc. What is this circuit pattern called?","a":"Serial processing.","options":["Convergence","Divergence","Parallel processing","Serial processing"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"In serial processing, a signal passes from one neuron to the next along a single chain, like links in a line. A reflex arc is the classic example: receptor, sensory neuron, integration center, motor neuron, and effector in a fixed sequence. Parallel processing, by contrast, sends the signal along several pathways at once.","notes":["Convergence is many neurons feeding onto one. It would be right for many inputs funneling onto a single neuron.","Divergence is one neuron spreading its signal to many. Choose it for a single neuron branching to many targets.","Parallel processing sends a signal along several pathways at the same time. Pick it when the signal splits into separate routes at once.","Correct. A single chain of neurons in a row is serial processing."],"lt":"t-neuronal-integration","lid":"c22","comp":183,"competencyId":"w8-neuronal-pools"},{"id":"L02782","dok":2,"q":"An axon ends on the axon of another neuron. What type of synapse is this?","a":"An axoaxonic synapse.","options":["An axosomatic synapse","A neuromuscular junction","An axoaxonic synapse","An axodendritic synapse"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Synapses between neurons are named for the parts that meet, starting with the axon of the sending neuron. When the receiving part is another axon, the synapse is axoaxonic. The other two neuron-to-neuron types are axodendritic, ending on a dendrite, and axosomatic, ending on a cell body.","notes":["An axosomatic synapse is an axon ending on a cell body (soma). Choose it when the receiving part is the cell body.","A neuromuscular junction is the synapse between a motor neuron and a skeletal muscle fiber, not between two neurons. It would be right for a question about where a nerve meets muscle.","Correct. An axon ending on another axon forms an axoaxonic synapse.","An axodendritic synapse is an axon ending on a dendrite, the most common type. Pick it when the receiving part is a dendrite."],"lt":"t-neuronal-integration","lid":"c25","comp":175,"competencyId":"w8-synapse-parts"},{"id":"L02783","dok":1,"q":"What are the chemical messengers that carry a signal across the synaptic cleft called?","a":"Neurotransmitters.","options":["Synaptic vesicles","Hormones","Neurofibrils","Neurotransmitters"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Neurotransmitters are the chemical messengers of a chemical synapse. They are stored in synaptic vesicles, released into the synaptic cleft when a signal arrives, and diffuse across the gap to reach the receiving cell. Their action is local to the synapse, unlike hormones, which travel in the blood.","notes":["Synaptic vesicles are the sacs that store neurotransmitter, not the messengers themselves. Choose them for a question about where neurotransmitter is held before release.","Hormones are chemical messengers too, but they are released by endocrine glands into the blood and travel throughout the body. Pick hormones for a question about endocrine signaling.","Neurofibrils are cytoskeletal fibers that give the neuron its shape and support. They would be right for a question about the internal support of a neuron.","Correct. Neurotransmitters carry the signal across the cleft."],"lt":"t-neuronal-integration","lid":"c26","comp":175,"competencyId":"w8-synapse-parts"},{"id":"L02789","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares convergence and divergence?","a":"Convergence funnels many neurons onto one; divergence spreads one neuron onto many.","options":["Both describe a pathway crossing over to the opposite side of the brain and cord","Both describe a signal passed along one straight chain of neurons","Convergence spreads one neuron to many; divergence funnels many onto one","Convergence funnels many neurons onto one; divergence spreads one to many"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Convergence and divergence are mirror-image circuit patterns in a neuronal pool. In convergence, signals from many neurons funnel onto one target neuron, and in divergence, one neuron spreads its signal out to many. The word roots help: converge means to come together, and diverge means to branch apart.","notes":["Crossing to the opposite side is decussation, which concerns pathways rather than circuit wiring. Choose this for a question about why one side of the brain serves the other side of the body.","A single straight chain of neurons is serial processing, a different pattern. Pick this description for a question about serial processing in a reflex arc.","This swaps the two definitions. Converge means to come together, so convergence is the pattern in which many inputs meet on one neuron.","Correct. The two patterns are mirror images of each other."],"lt":"t-neuronal-integration","lid":"c32","comp":183,"competencyId":"w8-neuronal-pools"},{"id":"L02790","dok":3,"q":"A single sensory neuron entering the spinal cord branches and passes its signal to many interneurons at several levels of the cord. Which circuit pattern is this?","a":"Divergence.","options":["Decussation","Serial processing","Convergence","Divergence"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"In a diverging circuit, one presynaptic neuron branches and synapses on many postsynaptic neurons. Here a single sensory neuron branches inside the cord and reaches many interneurons at different levels, so one input reaches many targets. Convergence is the reverse, with many neurons feeding onto one.","notes":["Decussation is a pathway crossing to the opposite side of the CNS, not a branching pattern. It would be right for a question about why the left brain serves the right body.","Serial processing is a single straight chain of neurons, one after another, with no branching. Choose it for a simple chain like the reflex arc.","Convergence is the reverse, with many neurons feeding onto one. Pick it if many sensory neurons all fed onto a single interneuron.","Correct. One neuron passing its signal to many neurons is divergence."],"lt":"t-neuronal-integration","lid":"c33","comp":183,"competencyId":"w8-neuronal-pools"},{"id":"L02791","dok":2,"q":"A signal is sent along several separate pathways at the same time instead of down one chain. Which pattern is this?","a":"Parallel processing.","options":["Serial processing","Decussation","Parallel processing","Convergence"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"In parallel processing, a signal travels along several pathways at the same time, so different parts of the CNS can handle the same information at once. Serial processing, by contrast, uses a single chain, as in a reflex arc.","notes":["Serial processing is the opposite arrangement, a single chain of neurons one after another. Pick it for a reflex arc.","Decussation is the crossing of a pathway to the opposite side of the CNS. It would be right for a question about why each side of the brain serves the opposite side of the body.","Correct. Sending a signal along several pathways at once is parallel processing.","Convergence is many neurons feeding onto one, a funneling pattern rather than several routes at once. Choose it when many inputs meet on a single neuron."],"lt":"t-neuronal-integration","lid":"c34","comp":183,"competencyId":"w8-neuronal-pools"},{"id":"L02792","dok":2,"q":"The cortex of the cerebellum is made mostly of neuron cell bodies and dendrites. Which tissue type is it?","a":"Gray matter.","options":["Gray matter","White matter","A peripheral nerve","A ganglion"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Gray matter is made of neuron cell bodies, dendrites, and unmyelinated axons. In the brain, including the cerebellum, gray matter forms the outer cortex and white matter lies beneath it. The spinal cord is arranged the other way around, with gray matter inside.","notes":["Correct. Cell bodies and dendrites make gray matter.","White matter is made of myelinated axons running in bundles, and it lies beneath the cortex. Pick it for a question about the tissue under the cerebellar cortex.","A peripheral nerve is a bundle of axons outside the CNS, not part of the cerebellum. Choose it for a question about bundles of axons in the PNS.","A ganglion is a cluster of cell bodies outside the CNS, while the cerebellum is part of the brain. It would be right for a question about cell bodies grouped beside the spinal cord."],"lt":"t-neuronal-integration","lid":"c35","comp":180,"competencyId":"w8-gray-white-matter"},{"id":"L02795","dok":2,"q":"At a synapse, which structure is the receiving membrane that the signal reaches after crossing the gap?","a":"The postsynaptic membrane.","options":["The postsynaptic membrane","The presynaptic terminal","The synaptic vesicle","The synaptic cleft"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"A synapse has a sending side, a gap, and a receiving side. Neurotransmitter is released from the presynaptic terminal, crosses the synaptic cleft, and reaches the postsynaptic membrane, the membrane of the receiving cell. Post means after, so the postsynaptic membrane is the structure after the gap.","notes":["Correct. The postsynaptic membrane is on the receiving side of the cleft.","The presynaptic terminal is on the sending side, before the cleft. Pick it for a question about the structure that releases neurotransmitter.","Synaptic vesicles are sacs inside the presynaptic terminal that store neurotransmitter. It would be right for a question about where neurotransmitter is held before release.","The synaptic cleft is the gap itself, between the two cells. Choose it for a question about the space that neurotransmitter crosses."],"lt":"t-neuronal-integration","lid":"c38","comp":175,"competencyId":"w8-synapse-parts"},{"id":"L02799","dok":2,"q":"Inside the CNS, which pair of terms names a bundle of axons and a cluster of cell bodies?","a":"Tract for the axon bundle, nucleus for the cell-body cluster.","options":["Tract for the axon bundle, nucleus for the cell-body cluster","Tract for the axon bundle, ganglion for the cell-body cluster","Nerve for the axon bundle, nucleus for the cell-body cluster","Nerve for the axon bundle, ganglion for the cell-body cluster"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The same structures take different names inside and outside the CNS. Inside the brain and spinal cord, a bundle of axons is a tract and a cluster of cell bodies is a nucleus. Outside the CNS, they are a nerve and a ganglion.","notes":["Correct. Tract and nucleus are the CNS terms.","Tract is right, but a ganglion lies outside the CNS. This mixes one CNS term with one PNS term.","Nucleus is right, but a nerve lies outside the CNS. This mixes one PNS term with one CNS term.","Nerve and ganglion are the matching terms for the PNS. Pick this pair when both structures lie outside the brain and spinal cord."],"lt":"t-neuronal-integration","lid":"c42","comp":179,"competencyId":"w8-tissue-collections"},{"id":"L02803","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares where gray and white matter sit in the brain and in the spinal cord?","a":"The brain has gray matter outside and white inside; the cord is the reverse.","options":["Both have white matter on the outside and gray matter on the inside","The brain has gray outside and white inside; the cord is the reverse","Both have gray matter on the outside and white matter on the inside","The brain has white outside and gray inside; the cord is the reverse"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Gray matter (cell bodies, dendrites, unmyelinated axons) and white matter (myelinated axons) swap positions between the two CNS organs. In the brain, gray matter forms the outer cortex with white matter beneath it. In the spinal cord, gray matter forms the butterfly-shaped core and white matter surrounds it.","notes":["This is true for the cord but not the brain. Students choose it when they apply the cord pattern to the whole CNS.","Correct. The two organs have opposite arrangements.","This is true for the brain but not the cord. Students choose it when they apply the brain pattern to the whole CNS.","This swaps the two organs. The brain has an outer cortex of gray matter, and the cord has its gray matter in the inner core."],"lt":"t-neuronal-integration","lid":"c1-x2","comp":180,"competencyId":"w8-gray-white-matter"},{"id":"L02804","dok":2,"q":"Why does white matter look white?","a":"Its axons are wrapped in fatty myelin.","options":["It is packed with neuron cell bodies","Its axons are wrapped in fatty myelin","It is filled with cerebrospinal fluid","It contains no blood vessels at all"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"White matter is made of myelinated axons, and myelin is a multilayered covering rich in lipid. That fatty material is what makes the tissue look white. Gray matter has few myelinated axons and many cell bodies, so it looks gray instead.","notes":["Neuron cell bodies are concentrated in gray matter, and they give it the gray look. Pick this for a question about what makes gray matter gray.","Correct. The lipid in myelin gives white matter its color.","Cerebrospinal fluid fills the ventricles and central canal, not the white matter. Choose it for a question about the fluid-filled spaces of the CNS.","White matter does have blood vessels, and having none would not make tissue white. This choice confuses color with blood supply."],"lt":"t-neuronal-integration","lid":"c2-x2","comp":180,"competencyId":"w8-gray-white-matter"},{"id":"L02826","dok":1,"q":"What is the combining of all the incoming signals so that a neuron produces a single response called?","a":"Integration.","options":["Decussation","Divergence","Integration","Serial processing"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"A neuron can receive signals from many other neurons at once. Integration is the combining of all those incoming signals so that the neuron produces a single response, such as firing or not firing. The same idea, on a larger scale, is the integrative function of the CNS, which analyzes input and decides on a response.","notes":["Decussation is the crossing of a pathway to the opposite side of the CNS. Pick it for a question about why each side of the brain serves the opposite side of the body.","Divergence is a circuit pattern in which one neuron spreads its signal to many. Choose it for a question about one neuron reaching many targets.","Correct. Integration is the combining of incoming signals into one response.","Serial processing is a signal passing along a single straight chain of neurons. It would be right for a question about the chain of a reflex arc."],"lt":"t-neuronal-integration","lid":"c25-x2","comp":183,"competencyId":"w8-neuronal-pools"},{"id":"L02828","dok":3,"q":"A brain MRI shows damage in the band of myelinated axons just beneath the cerebral cortex. Which tissue is damaged?","a":"White matter.","options":["Gray matter","A ganglion","White matter","The pia mater"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"In the brain, gray matter forms the outer cortex and white matter lies beneath it. White matter is made of myelinated axons running in bundles, and the myelin gives it its color. Damage to the band of myelinated axons under the cortex is therefore damage to white matter, the kind of change a radiologist looks for on a scan.","notes":["Gray matter is the cortex itself, made of cell bodies, dendrites, and unmyelinated axons. Pick it if the damage were in the outer layer of the cerebrum.","A ganglion is a cluster of cell bodies outside the CNS, not inside the brain. Choose it for a question about cell bodies grouped beside the spinal cord.","Correct. Myelinated axons beneath the cortex form the white matter.","The pia mater is the thin innermost membrane on the surface of the brain, outside the cortex rather than beneath it. It would be right for a question about the membrane that clings to the brain."],"lt":"t-neuronal-integration","lid":"c27-x2","comp":180,"competencyId":"w8-gray-white-matter"},{"id":"L02829","dok":2,"q":"A signal needs to reach several brain regions at the same time so they can work on it at once. Which processing pattern does this?","a":"Parallel processing.","options":["Convergence","Serial processing","Parallel processing","Decussation"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"In parallel processing, a signal travels along several pathways at the same time. That lets several brain regions receive and work on the same information at once instead of one after another. Serial processing, by contrast, passes the signal down a single chain, as in a reflex arc.","notes":["Convergence funnels many neurons onto one, the opposite of spreading a signal out. Choose it when many inputs meet on a single neuron.","Serial processing passes the signal along one chain, one neuron after another, so regions would receive it in turn rather than at once. Pick it for a reflex arc.","Correct. Parallel processing sends a signal along several pathways at the same time.","Decussation is a pathway crossing to the opposite side of the CNS. It would be right for a question about why each side of the brain serves the opposite side of the body."],"lt":"t-neuronal-integration","lid":"c28-x2","comp":183,"competencyId":"w8-neuronal-pools"},{"id":"L02831","dok":2,"q":"In a neuronal pool, many sensory neurons all feed their signals onto a single output neuron. Which circuit pattern is this?","a":"Convergence.","options":["Serial processing","Decussation","Divergence","Convergence"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"In a converging circuit, signals from many neurons come together onto one target neuron. That single neuron then combines, or integrates, all the input into one response. Convergence lets the CNS combine information from many sources before acting.","notes":["Serial processing is a single straight chain of neurons with no funneling. Choose it for the simple chain of a reflex arc.","Decussation is a pathway crossing to the opposite side of the CNS, not a wiring pattern within a pool. It would be right for a question about why one side of the brain serves the other side of the body.","Divergence is the reverse, one neuron spreading its signal to many. Pick it if a single sensory neuron sent its signal to many output neurons.","Correct. Many neurons feeding onto one is convergence."],"lt":"t-neuronal-integration","lid":"c30-x2","comp":183,"competencyId":"w8-neuronal-pools"},{"id":"L02832","dok":2,"q":"A ball flies toward you. Your eyes detect it, your brain judges its path and decides to duck, and your muscles move you. Which function is the judging and deciding step, and where does it happen?","a":"The integrative function, in the CNS.","options":["Sensory, in the receptors","Integrative, in the PNS","Integrative, in the CNS","Motor, in the muscles"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The nervous system handles every task in three steps. In the sensory function, receptors detect a change, in the integrative function, the CNS analyzes the input and decides on a response, and in the motor function, commands go out to the effectors. Judging the path of the ball and deciding to duck is the integrative step, which takes place in the CNS.","notes":["The sensory function is the first step, when the eyes detect the ball. Pick it for the detecting step, not the deciding step.","Integrative is the right function, but it happens in the brain and spinal cord, not in the PNS. The PNS only carries signals to and from the CNS.","Correct. The CNS analyzes the input and decides on the response.","The motor function is the last step, when commands reach the muscles and you duck. Choose it for the responding step."],"lt":"t-neuronal-integration","lid":"d3-t-neuronal-integration-1","comp":173,"competencyId":"w8-ns-organization"},{"id":"L02838","dok":2,"q":"Signals cross a chemical synapse in only one direction, from the sending cell to the receiving cell. Which structural fact explains this?","a":"Only the sending terminal holds synaptic vesicles of neurotransmitter.","options":["Only the sending terminal holds vesicles of neurotransmitter","Myelin covers the receiving side and blocks any return signal","The receiving cell is always a muscle fiber that cannot signal","Only the receiving cell has a membrane facing the cleft"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"At a chemical synapse, only the presynaptic terminal contains synaptic vesicles filled with neurotransmitter. When a signal arrives, those vesicles release the neurotransmitter, which crosses the cleft to the postsynaptic membrane. The receiving side has no vesicles to send a message back, so the signal can only travel from the sending cell to the receiving cell.","notes":["Correct. Only the presynaptic side can release neurotransmitter.","Myelin wraps axons along their length, not the receiving side of a synapse. Pick myelin for a question about what speeds conduction along an axon.","The receiving cell is often another neuron or a gland cell, not always a muscle fiber, and many receiving neurons go on to signal other cells. The one-way flow comes from where the vesicles are, not from the type of receiving cell.","Both cells have a membrane facing the cleft, the presynaptic membrane on one side and the postsynaptic membrane on the other. The difference lies in what each side holds, not in whether it has a membrane."],"lt":"t-neuronal-integration","lid":"d3x-t-neuronal-integration-2","comp":175,"competencyId":"w8-synapse-parts"},{"id":"L02839","dok":1,"q":"Which swellings at the tips of the axon terminals hold the synaptic vesicles?","a":"The synaptic end bulbs.","options":["The synaptic end bulbs","The axon hillocks","The Nissl bodies","The nodes of Ranvier"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Each axon terminal ends in a small swelling called a synaptic end bulb, which holds the signal-sending machinery of the neuron. Inside the end bulbs are the synaptic vesicles, tiny sacs that store neurotransmitter. When a signal arrives, the vesicles release their neurotransmitter into the synaptic cleft.","notes":["Correct. Synaptic end bulbs are the swellings that hold the vesicles.","The axon hillock is the cone-shaped region where the axon starts, at the opposite end from the terminals. Choose it for a question about where the axon leaves the cell body.","Nissl bodies are clusters of rough ER and ribosomes in the cell body that make proteins. They would be right for a question about protein synthesis in a neuron.","Nodes of Ranvier are bare gaps in the myelin along the length of the axon, not swellings at its tips. Pick them for a question about where the impulse jumps."],"lt":"t-neuronal-integration","lid":"d3x-t-neuronal-integration-3","comp":175,"competencyId":"w8-synapse-parts"},{"id":"L02840","dok":1,"q":"The central nervous system consists of which two structures?","a":"The brain and the spinal cord.","options":["The brain and the cranial nerves","The brain and the spinal cord","The cerebrum and the cerebellum","The spinal cord and the spinal nerves"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The central nervous system (CNS) is made of the brain and the spinal cord, the two organs that integrate information and send out commands. The brain sits in the cranial cavity and the cord runs down the vertebral canal. Everything outside them, including the cranial nerves, spinal nerves, and ganglia, belongs to the peripheral nervous system (PNS), which carries signals to and from the CNS.","notes":["The brain belongs to the CNS, but the cranial nerves are part of the PNS because they leave the brain and travel out to the head, neck, and viscera. Students pick this when they assume any nerve attached to the brain counts as central.","Correct. The brain and the spinal cord together make up the CNS.","The cerebrum and cerebellum are two regions of the brain, so this pair leaves out the diencephalon, the brainstem, and the whole spinal cord. Pick them if the question asked for the largest and second largest parts of the brain.","The spinal cord belongs to the CNS, but the spinal nerves branch off it and belong to the PNS. A spinal nerve would be the answer if the question asked which structure carries mixed sensory and motor axons out through an intervertebral foramen."],"lt":"t-cns-brain-spinal-cord","lid":"c1","comp":173,"competencyId":"w8-ns-organization"},{"id":"N-m5-nervous-tissue-1","dok":2,"q":"In the central nervous system, a cluster of neuron cell bodies is called what?","a":"A nucleus.","options":["A tract","A nucleus","A nerve","A ganglion"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The nervous system uses different names for the same kind of structure depending on whether it is in the CNS or the PNS. A cluster of cell bodies is a nucleus in the CNS and a ganglion in the PNS. A bundle of axons is a tract in the CNS and a nerve in the PNS.","notes":["A tract is a bundle of axons in the CNS, such as an ascending tract in the spinal cord. It would be the answer if the question asked about a bundle of axons rather than cell bodies.","Correct. A cluster of cell bodies in the CNS is a nucleus.","A nerve is a bundle of axons in the PNS, such as a spinal nerve. Pick it if the question asked what a bundle of axons is called outside the CNS.","A ganglion is a cluster of cell bodies in the peripheral nervous system, such as the dorsal root ganglion. Pick it if the question asked about the PNS."],"comp":179,"competencyId":"w8-tissue-collections"},{"id":"L02977","dok":1,"q":"What is a cluster of neuron cell bodies located in the peripheral nervous system called?","a":"A ganglion.","options":["A fascicle","A plexus","A ganglion","A nucleus"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"A ganglion is a cluster of neuron cell bodies that lies outside the central nervous system. Examples include the dorsal root ganglia beside the spinal cord and the autonomic ganglia. The matching cluster inside the brain or spinal cord has a different name, a nucleus, so the location decides which term to use.","notes":["A fascicle is a bundle of axons inside a nerve, wrapped by perineurium. It is made of nerve fibers, not cell bodies.","A plexus is a network where nerves join and regroup, such as the brachial plexus. Pick it for a branching network of nerves rather than a cluster of cell bodies.","Correct. A cluster of cell bodies outside the CNS is a ganglion.","A nucleus is a cluster of neuron cell bodies inside the central nervous system, such as a cranial nerve nucleus in the brainstem. Pick it if the cluster were in the brain or spinal cord."],"lt":"t-pns","lid":"c8","comp":179,"competencyId":"w8-tissue-collections"},{"id":"L03015","dok":1,"q":"Which structures make up the peripheral nervous system?","a":"The nerves and ganglia outside the brain and spinal cord.","options":["The nerves and ganglia outside the brain and spinal cord","The brain and spinal cord with their coverings","The brainstem, cerebellum, and cranial nerve nuclei","The tracts and nuclei found inside the brain and spinal cord"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The peripheral nervous system (PNS) includes all neural structures outside the brain and spinal cord: the cranial and spinal nerves, the ganglia, the sensory receptors, and the motor endings. Its job is to carry sensory input from receptors into the central nervous system (CNS) and carry motor commands back out to muscles and glands. The brain and spinal cord themselves make up the CNS.","notes":["Correct. Everything neural outside the brain and spinal cord belongs to the PNS.","The brain and spinal cord, with their meninges, make up the central nervous system. Pick this for the CNS.","The brainstem and cerebellum are parts of the brain, so they belong to the CNS. The cranial nerve nuclei sit inside the brainstem too, even though the nerves that leave them are peripheral.","Tracts and nuclei are the CNS names for bundles of axons and clusters of cell bodies. Their PNS counterparts are nerves and ganglia, so pick tracts and nuclei for the inside of the brain and cord."],"lt":"t-pns","lid":"c1-x2","comp":173,"competencyId":"w8-ns-organization"},{"id":"L03028","dok":2,"q":"A cordlike organ of the peripheral nervous system bundles many axons together inside connective tissue wrappings. What is it called?","a":"A nerve.","options":["A nerve","A nucleus","A tract","A ganglion"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"A nerve is a cordlike organ of the peripheral nervous system made of many axons bundled together and wrapped in three layers of connective tissue. Some of its axons are myelinated and some are not. The CNS counterpart, a bundle of axons inside the brain or spinal cord, is called a tract.","notes":["Correct. A wrapped bundle of peripheral axons is a nerve.","A nucleus is a cluster of neuron cell bodies inside the CNS. Pick it for a group of cell bodies in the brainstem or cord.","A tract is a bundle of axons inside the brain or spinal cord, and it lacks the connective tissue wrappings of a nerve. Pick it for a bundle of axons in the CNS.","A ganglion is a cluster of neuron cell bodies outside the CNS, not a bundle of axons. Pick it for a swelling such as the dorsal root ganglion."],"lt":"t-pns","lid":"c16-x2","comp":179,"competencyId":"w8-tissue-collections"},{"id":"L03049","dok":3,"q":"The dorsal root ganglion and the posterior gray horn of the cord both contain clusters of neuron cell bodies. Why is only the dorsal root ganglion called a ganglion?","a":"It lies outside the CNS, and clusters inside the CNS are called nuclei.","options":["It holds myelinated axons, and nuclei hold only unmyelinated axons","It holds sensory neurons, and only sensory clusters are called ganglia","It is larger, and only clusters above a certain size are called ganglia","It lies outside the CNS, and clusters inside the CNS are called nuclei"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The name for a cluster of neuron cell bodies depends on where it is. Inside the brain or spinal cord a cluster is a nucleus, and outside the CNS it is a ganglion. The dorsal root ganglion sits on the dorsal root just outside the cord, so it is a ganglion even though it lies right next to the CNS.","notes":["Myelinated versus unmyelinated axons is the difference between white and gray matter, not between ganglia and nuclei. Pick it for a question about white matter.","Autonomic ganglia hold motor neurons, so ganglia are not limited to sensory neurons. The type of neuron does not decide the name.","Size has nothing to do with the name. A tiny cluster outside the CNS is still a ganglion, and a large one inside the brain is still a nucleus.","Correct. Location decides the name: ganglion outside the CNS, nucleus inside it."],"lt":"t-pns","lid":"d3x-t-pns-2","comp":179,"competencyId":"w8-tissue-collections"},{"id":"L04499","dok":1,"q":"How are gray matter and white matter arranged in the brain?","a":"Gray matter is on the outside and white matter is on the inside.","options":["Gray matter outside, white matter inside","White matter outside, gray matter inside","Gray matter only in the brainstem and cerebellum","Gray and white matter evenly mixed throughout"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Gray matter is made of neuron cell bodies, dendrites, and unmyelinated fibers, while white matter is made of myelinated axons, and the fatty myelin is what makes it look white. In the brain, the gray matter forms an outer layer (the cortex), and the white matter lies beneath it, carrying signals between areas. The spinal cord has the reverse arrangement, so it helps to name the organ before naming the layer.","notes":["Correct. The cerebral cortex is an outer layer of gray matter over a core of white matter.","This is the arrangement in the spinal cord, where a butterfly of gray matter sits at the core and white matter wraps around it. Pick it if the question asked about a cross section of the spinal cord.","Gray matter is not limited to the brainstem and cerebellum. The cerebrum has a whole outer layer of gray matter, the cerebral cortex, along with deep gray masses such as the basal nuclei. This choice comes from forgetting that the cortex itself is gray matter.","The two are not randomly mixed. Gray matter collects where cell bodies gather (the cortex and deep nuclei), and white matter forms organized bundles of axons, so each sits in predictable places."],"lt":"t-w7-brain-organization","lid":"w7-brain-organization-r2","comp":180,"competencyId":"w8-gray-white-matter"},{"id":"L04588","dok":1,"q":"What are the two main divisions of the nervous system?","a":"The central nervous system (CNS) and the peripheral nervous system (PNS).","options":["The sensory and motor divisions","The sympathetic and parasympathetic divisions","The central and peripheral nervous systems","The somatic and autonomic nervous systems"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The nervous system is first split by location. The central nervous system (CNS) is the brain and spinal cord, which process information and issue commands. The peripheral nervous system (PNS) is every neural structure outside them, including the 12 pairs of cranial nerves, the 31 pairs of spinal nerves, the ganglia, and the sensory receptors. Every other split you learn in this unit is a subdivision inside one of these two.","notes":["Sensory and motor describe the direction signals travel within the PNS, input toward the CNS and output away from it. Choose this pair if the question asked how the PNS is divided by the direction its neurons carry signals.","The sympathetic and parasympathetic divisions are the two halves of the autonomic nervous system, two levels further down. They would be the answer for a question about fight or flight versus rest and digest.","Correct. The CNS and PNS are the top-level split, based on whether a structure lies inside or outside the brain and spinal cord.","The somatic and autonomic systems are subdivisions of the motor side of the PNS, sorted by the effector they control. Pick them if the question asked how the PNS motor division splits into voluntary and involuntary parts."],"lt":"t-w8-ns-organization","lid":"w8-ns-organization-r1","comp":173,"competencyId":"w8-ns-organization"},{"id":"L04589","dok":1,"q":"Which organs make up the central nervous system?","a":"The brain and the spinal cord.","options":["The brain, cord, and all ganglia","The spinal cord and spinal nerves","The brain and cranial nerves","The brain and spinal cord"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The CNS is made of just two organs: the brain in the cranial cavity and the spinal cord in the vertebral canal. Together they receive sensory input, process it, and send out commands. Anything neural that lies outside these two organs, such as nerves, ganglia, and sensory receptors, belongs to the peripheral nervous system.","notes":["Ganglia are clusters of neuron cell bodies located outside the CNS, so they are peripheral by definition. A cluster of cell bodies inside the CNS is called a nucleus instead.","Spinal nerves branch off the cord and exit the vertebral canal, so they belong to the PNS even though the cord itself is CNS. Students pick this when they assume anything named spinal is central.","Cranial nerves attach to the brain, but they leave it to reach the head and body, so they are part of the PNS. This pairing comes from assuming that anything attached to the brain must be central.","Correct. The brain and spinal cord are the only two organs of the CNS."],"lt":"t-w8-ns-organization","lid":"w8-ns-organization-r2","comp":173,"competencyId":"w8-ns-organization"},{"id":"L04590","dok":1,"q":"What are the three functions of the nervous system, in the order they happen during a response?","a":"Sensory function, integrative function, and motor function.","options":["Sensory, motor, and integrative","Sensory, protective, and secretory","Sensory, integrative, and motor","Integrative, sensory, and motor"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The nervous system handles every task in the same three steps. Sensory function comes first: receptors detect a change inside or outside the body and send that input toward the CNS. Integrative function is the CNS analyzing the input, storing it, and deciding on a response. Motor function comes last, when the CNS sends commands out to the effectors, the muscles and glands that carry out the response.","notes":["These are the right three functions, but motor output cannot happen before the CNS has processed the input and decided on a response. Students pick it when they remember the names but not the sequence.","Protection and secretion are not among the three nervous system functions. Glands do secrete, but they are effectors that receive motor commands, and the CNS is protected by bone, meninges, and cerebrospinal fluid rather than by its own function.","Correct. Input comes in, the CNS processes it, and a response goes out.","These are the right three functions, but integration cannot come first because the CNS has nothing to analyze until sensory input arrives. This order mixes up the sequence."],"lt":"t-w8-ns-organization","lid":"w8-ns-organization-r3","comp":173,"competencyId":"w8-ns-organization"},{"id":"L04593","dok":1,"q":"Which list names the three basic parts of every neuron?","a":"The cell body (soma), the dendrites, and the axon.","options":["Axon hillock, axon, and synapse","Cell body, dendrites, and axon","Cell body, axon, and Schwann cell","Dendrites, axon, and myelin sheath"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Every neuron has a cell body, also called the soma, which holds the nucleus and is the metabolic center of the cell. Branched dendrites receive incoming signals, and a single axon carries the impulse away toward other cells. Features such as myelin are added by other cells, and a synapse forms where a neuron meets its target, so neither is one of the three basic parts.","notes":["The axon hillock is only the cone-shaped region where the cell body joins the axon, and a synapse is a junction between two cells rather than part of one neuron. This list mixes a small region and a junction in with one true part.","Correct. The cell body, dendrites, and axon are the three parts every neuron has.","A Schwann cell is a PNS glial cell that wraps an axon in myelin, not a part of the neuron itself. It would be the answer if the question asked which cell myelinates a peripheral axon.","The myelin sheath is not part of every neuron. It is a fatty wrapping laid around some axons by glial cells, so pick it if the question asked what insulates an axon and speeds the impulse."],"lt":"t-w8-neuron-parts","lid":"w8-neuron-parts-r1","comp":174,"competencyId":"w8-neuron-parts"},{"id":"L04594","dok":1,"q":"What is the main job of the dendrites of a neuron?","a":"They receive incoming signals.","options":["They receive incoming signals","They carry the impulse to other cells","They insulate the axon","They make proteins for the neuron"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Dendrites are the short, branched, tree-like processes of a neuron. Their many branches give the neuron a large surface for receiving signals from other cells, and they pass that incoming information toward the cell body. A neuron usually has many dendrites but only one axon.","notes":["Correct. Dendrites are the receiving branches of the neuron.","Carrying the impulse away toward other cells is the job of the axon, the single long process of the neuron. Pick this if the question asked what the axon does.","Insulating the axon is the job of the myelin sheath, made by Schwann cells in the PNS and oligodendrocytes in the CNS. Choose this if the question asked what myelin does.","Protein synthesis is centered in the cell body, at the Nissl bodies. Some Nissl bodies reach into the dendrites, but the main job of the dendrites is receiving signals."],"lt":"t-w8-neuron-parts","lid":"w8-neuron-parts-r2","comp":174,"competencyId":"w8-neuron-parts"},{"id":"L04595","dok":1,"q":"What is the job of the axon of a neuron?","a":"It carries the nerve impulse away toward other cells.","options":["It holds the nucleus and makes the cell's proteins","It receives incoming signals from other neurons","It carries the impulse away to other cells","It speeds the impulse by insulating the axon"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The axon is the single long process of a neuron, and it conducts the nerve impulse away from the cell body. At its far end it branches into axon terminals, which contact the next neuron, a muscle cell, or a gland. Because the axon depends on the cell body for its proteins, it cannot survive if it is cut off from the cell body.","notes":["Holding the nucleus and making proteins describes the cell body, the metabolic center of the neuron. It would be the answer for a question about the soma.","Receiving incoming signals is the job of the dendrites, the branched processes of the neuron. Pick this if the question asked what dendrites do.","Correct. The axon is the outgoing process that carries the impulse away.","Insulating the axon and speeding the impulse is the job of the myelin sheath, a wrapping laid around the axon by glial cells. Choose this if the question asked what myelin does."],"lt":"t-w8-neuron-parts","lid":"w8-neuron-parts-r3","comp":174,"competencyId":"w8-neuron-parts"},{"id":"L04596","dok":2,"q":"What is the axon hillock?","a":"The cone-shaped region where the cell body joins the axon.","options":["The gaps in the myelin sheath spaced along the axon","The swelling on a spinal root that holds sensory cell bodies","The fine branching ends of the axon that contact the target cell","The cone-shaped region joining the cell body to the axon"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The axon hillock is the cone-shaped part of the cell body that narrows down into the axon. It marks where the cell body ends and the axon begins, so it is the landmark for finding the start of the axon on a neuron model or slide. A hillock is a small hill, which describes its tapered, mound-like shape.","notes":["The gaps in the myelin sheath are the nodes of Ranvier. They would be the answer if the question asked where the impulse jumps along a myelinated axon.","A swelling on a spinal root that holds sensory cell bodies is the posterior (dorsal) root ganglion. Choose it for a question about where sensory neuron cell bodies sit outside the cord.","The fine branches at the far end of the axon are the axon terminals, which contact the target cell. Pick them if the question asked where the axon meets the next cell.","Correct. The axon hillock is where the cell body tapers into the axon."],"lt":"t-w8-neuron-parts","lid":"w8-neuron-parts-r4","comp":174,"competencyId":"w8-neuron-parts"},{"id":"L04597","dok":3,"q":"An injury cuts through an axon partway along its length. Why does the part of the axon beyond the cut die?","a":"It is cut off from the cell body, which supplies its proteins.","options":["It loses the cell body that supplies its proteins","It loses the synapse that makes its proteins","It loses the myelin sheath that supplies its nutrients","It loses the dendrites that supply its energy"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The cell body is the neuron's metabolic center. Its Nissl bodies, clusters of rough ER and ribosomes, make the proteins the whole neuron needs, and microtubules carry those materials down the axon. Once part of the axon is separated from the cell body, it no longer receives these supplies and it degenerates. This is why the location of a nerve injury, near or far from the cell body, helps predict whether the neuron survives.","notes":["Correct. The axon depends on the cell body for its proteins, so the separated piece dies.","A synapse is a junction where the axon passes its signal to another cell, and it does not make proteins. The synapse would be the answer if the question asked where neurotransmitter is released.","The myelin sheath insulates the axon and speeds the impulse, but it is not the axon's supply line. Pick myelin if the question asked what is lost in multiple sclerosis.","Dendrites receive incoming signals; they do not supply the axon with energy or materials. This choice comes from thinking of the dendrites as the neuron's control center instead of the cell body."],"lt":"t-w8-neuron-parts","lid":"w8-neuron-parts-r5","comp":178,"competencyId":"w8-myelination"},{"id":"L04598","dok":1,"q":"Which list gives the three structural classes of neurons, named for their shape?","a":"Multipolar, bipolar, and unipolar neurons.","options":["Sensory, motor, and interneurons","Pyramidal, Purkinje, and bipolar","Multipolar, bipolar, and unipolar","Astrocytes, microglia, and Schwann cells"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Structural classification counts the processes leaving the cell body. A multipolar neuron has many dendrites and one axon, a bipolar neuron has one dendrite and one axon, and a unipolar neuron has a single fused process. The names come from the number of poles, or processes, the cell body has.","notes":["Sensory, motor, and interneurons are the functional classes, sorted by the direction a neuron carries its signal. Pick this list if the question asked how neurons are classified by function.","Pyramidal and Purkinje cells are named examples of multipolar neurons, found in the cerebral cortex and the cerebellum. They would be the answer if the question asked for distinctive multipolar neurons, not the three shape classes.","Correct. Multipolar, bipolar, and unipolar are the three shape classes.","Astrocytes, microglia, and Schwann cells are neuroglia, the support cells of nervous tissue, not neurons. Choose this list for a question about glial cells."],"lt":"t-w8-neuron-classification","lid":"w8-neuron-classification-r1","comp":176,"competencyId":"w8-neuron-classification"},{"id":"L04599","dok":1,"q":"Which structural type of neuron is the most common, found throughout the brain and spinal cord and as the motor neurons?","a":"The multipolar neuron.","options":["Unipolar neuron","Bipolar neuron","Sensory (afferent) neuron","Multipolar neuron"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Multipolar neurons have many dendrites and one axon, and they are the most common type in the body. Most neurons of the brain and spinal cord are multipolar, including the interneurons that link sensory and motor neurons, and every motor neuron that carries commands out to muscles and glands is multipolar. The pyramidal cells of the cerebral cortex and the Purkinje cells of the cerebellum are well-known examples.","notes":["Unipolar neurons have a single fused process and are the typical shape of a sensory neuron. They would be the answer if the question asked for the shape of the neurons that carry input toward the CNS.","Bipolar neurons have one dendrite and one axon and are rare, found only in the retina, the inner ear, and the olfactory area. Pick bipolar if the question asked which neurons are found in the retina.","Sensory (afferent) is a functional class, not a structural shape, and sensory neurons carry input toward the CNS rather than serving as motor neurons. Choose it if the question asked how a neuron carrying input from the skin is classified by function.","Correct. Multipolar neurons are the most common type and include all motor neurons."],"lt":"t-w8-neuron-classification","lid":"w8-neuron-classification-r2","comp":176,"competencyId":"w8-neuron-classification"},{"id":"L04600","dok":1,"q":"Which structural shape is typical of a sensory neuron?","a":"Unipolar.","options":["Multipolar","Unipolar","Bipolar","Pyramidal"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Most sensory (afferent) neurons are unipolar: a single fused process leaves the cell body and then splits, with one branch running out to the receptor and the other running into the CNS. The cell bodies of these neurons sit in ganglia outside the CNS, such as the posterior root ganglia of the spinal nerves.","notes":["Multipolar neurons have many dendrites and one axon. They are the shape of motor neurons and interneurons, so pick multipolar if the question asked about a neuron that carries commands to a muscle.","Correct. Unipolar is the typical shape of a sensory neuron.","Bipolar neurons are sensory too, but only in a few special sense organs: the retina, the inner ear, and the olfactory area. Pick bipolar if the question asked about the neurons of the retina, not the typical sensory neuron.","Pyramidal cells are a distinctive multipolar neuron of the cerebral cortex, not a sensory shape. They would be the answer for a question about named neurons of the cerebral cortex."],"lt":"t-w8-neuron-classification","lid":"w8-neuron-classification-r3","comp":176,"competencyId":"w8-neuron-classification"},{"id":"L04601","dok":2,"q":"Which list gives the three functional classes of neurons?","a":"Sensory (afferent) neurons, motor (efferent) neurons, and interneurons.","options":["Somatic, autonomic, and enteric","Sensory, integrative, and motor","Sensory, motor, and interneurons","Multipolar, bipolar, and unipolar"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Functional classification sorts neurons by the direction they carry a signal relative to the CNS. Sensory (afferent) neurons carry input toward the CNS, motor (efferent) neurons carry commands away from it to muscles and glands, and interneurons, also called association neurons, sit within the CNS and connect sensory neurons to motor neurons.","notes":["Somatic, autonomic, and enteric are divisions of the motor side of the nervous system, not classes of individual neurons. They would be the answer for a question about the motor divisions of the PNS.","Sensory, integrative, and motor are the three functions of the nervous system, not the names of neuron classes. The middle class of neuron is called the interneuron, even though integration is the work it does.","Correct. Sensory, motor, and interneurons are the three functional classes.","Multipolar, bipolar, and unipolar are the structural classes, sorted by shape. Pick this list if the question asked how neurons are classified by the number of processes."],"lt":"t-w8-neuron-classification","lid":"w8-neuron-classification-r4","comp":176,"competencyId":"w8-neuron-classification"},{"id":"L04602","dok":3,"q":"A neuron carries input from a skin receptor toward the spinal cord, and a single process leaves its cell body. How is it classified by structure and by function?","a":"Structurally unipolar; functionally sensory (afferent).","options":["Structurally bipolar; functionally sensory","Structurally unipolar; functionally sensory","Structurally multipolar; functionally interneuron","Structurally unipolar; functionally motor"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Neurons are classified two ways. By structure, a neuron with a single process leaving its cell body is unipolar. By function, a neuron that carries input from a receptor toward the CNS is sensory, or afferent. This pairing is the usual one, because most sensory neurons are unipolar.","notes":["Bipolar neurons have two processes, one dendrite and one axon, and are found only in the retina, inner ear, and olfactory area. This would fit a neuron in the retina, not one with a single process carrying touch from the skin.","Correct. A single process makes it unipolar, and carrying input toward the CNS makes it sensory.","Multipolar interneurons lie entirely within the CNS and link sensory to motor neurons. This would be the answer for a neuron inside the spinal cord gray matter that connects the incoming and outgoing neurons of a reflex.","The shape is right, but a motor (efferent) neuron carries commands away from the CNS, and motor neurons are multipolar. Pick motor if the neuron carried a command from the cord out to a muscle."],"lt":"t-w8-neuron-classification","lid":"w8-neuron-classification-r5","comp":176,"competencyId":"w8-neuron-classification"},{"id":"L04603","dok":1,"q":"Which statement correctly describes neuroglia compared with neurons?","a":"They are the support cells of nervous tissue, and unlike neurons they can still divide.","options":["They are larger than neurons and far fewer in number","They are support cells that, like neurons, cannot divide","They support neurons and, unlike neurons, can still divide","They are signaling cells that carry impulses like neurons do"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Neuroglia, or glial cells, are the support cells of nervous tissue. They nourish, support, and protect neurons and make up about half the volume of the CNS. They are smaller than neurons and very numerous, and unlike neurons they keep the ability to divide. That ability is why most tumors of nervous tissue arise from glia rather than from neurons.","notes":["Glia are smaller than neurons, not larger, and they are very numerous, making up about half the volume of the CNS. This statement gets the size backward.","Glia are support cells, but they can still divide. Only neurons have lost that ability, which is the main contrast between the two cell types.","Correct. Glia are support cells, and they can still divide while neurons cannot.","Carrying nerve impulses is the job of neurons, the excitable signaling cells. Glia support and protect neurons rather than sending the signals themselves."],"lt":"t-w8-neuroglia","lid":"w8-neuroglia-r1","comp":177,"competencyId":"w8-neuroglia"},{"id":"L04604","dok":1,"q":"Which cells form the myelin sheath in the CNS, and which form it in the PNS?","a":"Oligodendrocytes in the CNS; Schwann cells in the PNS.","options":["Ependymal cells in the CNS; Schwann cells in the PNS","Oligodendrocytes in the CNS; Schwann cells in the PNS","Astrocytes in the CNS; satellite cells in the PNS","Schwann cells in the CNS; oligodendrocytes in the PNS"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Myelin is made by a different glial cell on each side of the nervous system. In the CNS, an oligodendrocyte sends out a few long processes and myelinates a segment of each of several axons. In the PNS, a Schwann cell wraps one segment of one axon and leaves an outer layer called the neurolemma, which helps a damaged peripheral axon regrow.","notes":["Ependymal cells line the ventricles and central canal and help make cerebrospinal fluid; they do not make myelin. Pick ependymal cells for a question about the lining of the ventricles.","Correct. Oligodendrocytes myelinate CNS axons and Schwann cells myelinate PNS axons.","Astrocytes support CNS neurons and help form the blood-brain barrier, and satellite cells surround neuron cell bodies in PNS ganglia. This pair would be the answer if the question asked which glia support neurons without making myelin.","This swaps the two cells. Schwann cells work only in the PNS and oligodendrocytes only in the CNS, so the locations are reversed."],"lt":"t-w8-neuroglia","lid":"w8-neuroglia-r2","comp":178,"competencyId":"w8-myelination"},{"id":"L04605","dok":1,"q":"Which glial cells are the small phagocytes of the CNS that remove debris and microbes?","a":"Microglia.","options":["Satellite cells","Ependymal cells","Microglia","Astrocytes"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Microglia are small phagocytic cells of the CNS, meaning they engulf and digest material. They clear away debris from dead and damaged cells and engulf microbes. They are the CNS's own immune defense.","notes":["Satellite cells are PNS glia that surround and support neuron cell bodies inside ganglia. Choose them if the question asked which glia sit around the cell bodies in a posterior root ganglion.","Ependymal cells line the ventricles of the brain and the central canal of the cord and help produce and circulate cerebrospinal fluid. They would be the answer for a question about the lining of the ventricles.","Correct. Microglia are the phagocytes of the CNS.","Astrocytes are star-shaped glia that support neurons, help form the blood-brain barrier, guide neuron growth, and keep the chemical environment stable. Pick astrocytes for a question about the blood-brain barrier."],"lt":"t-w8-neuroglia","lid":"w8-neuroglia-r3","comp":177,"competencyId":"w8-neuroglia"},{"id":"L04606","dok":2,"q":"Which description matches the astrocytes?","a":"They give structural support, help form the blood-brain barrier, guide neuron growth, and maintain the chemical environment.","options":["Engulf debris and microbes, acting as the small phagocytes of the CNS","Line the ventricles and central canal and help produce cerebrospinal fluid","Support neurons, help form the blood-brain barrier, guide growth","Wrap myelin around segments of several CNS axons at the same time"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Astrocytes are star-shaped glial cells and the most numerous glia of the CNS. They hold neurons in place, help form the blood-brain barrier, guide growing neurons in the embryo, and keep the chemical environment around neurons stable. Their name comes from astro, meaning star, for their many radiating processes.","notes":["Engulfing debris and microbes is the job of the microglia, the phagocytes of the CNS. Choose this if the question asked which glia clean up after an injury or infection.","Lining the ventricles and central canal and helping make cerebrospinal fluid is the job of the ependymal cells. This would be the answer for a question about the cells that line the brain's cavities.","Correct. Support, the blood-brain barrier, and guiding neuron growth are astrocyte jobs.","Wrapping myelin around several CNS axons at once is the job of the oligodendrocytes. Pick this if the question asked which cell myelinates axons in the brain and cord."],"lt":"t-w8-neuroglia","lid":"w8-neuroglia-r4","comp":177,"competencyId":"w8-neuroglia"},{"id":"L04607","dok":3,"q":"Tumors of nervous tissue almost never arise from neurons. Which cells do they usually come from, and why?","a":"Glial cells, because they can still divide while neurons cannot.","options":["Glial cells, because they can still divide","Schwann cells, because they myelinate brain axons","Glial cells, because they are larger than neurons","Glial cells, because they lack a nucleus"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"A tumor is a mass of cells dividing out of control, so it can only arise from cells that still divide. Neurons have lost that ability, but neuroglia keep it throughout life. That is why tumors of nervous tissue usually come from glial cells. A tumor of glial cells is called a glioma, and it is a common type of brain tumor.","notes":["Correct. Glia keep the ability to divide, so they are the usual source of these tumors.","Schwann cells are PNS glia; the cells that myelinate axons in the brain are oligodendrocytes. Pick Schwann cells if the question asked which glia myelinate a peripheral nerve in the arm.","The cell type is right, but the reason is wrong. Glia are actually smaller than neurons, and size has nothing to do with forming a tumor; the ability to divide is what matters.","Glial cells do have a nucleus, and a cell without one could not divide at all. This reason confuses glia with mature red blood cells, which are the cells that lack a nucleus."],"lt":"t-w8-neuroglia","lid":"w8-neuroglia-r5","comp":178,"competencyId":"w8-myelination"},{"id":"L04608","dok":1,"q":"Inside the CNS, what is a cluster of neuron cell bodies called, and what is a bundle of axons called?","a":"A nucleus and a tract.","options":["A ganglion and a tract","A ganglion and a nerve","A nucleus and a tract","A nucleus and a nerve"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The same two kinds of structure get different names depending on whether they are inside or outside the CNS. Inside the brain and spinal cord, a cluster of neuron cell bodies is a nucleus and a bundle of axons is a tract. Outside the CNS, the matching terms are ganglion and nerve. Note that this kind of nucleus is a group of cells, not the nucleus inside a single cell.","notes":["A tract is correct for a CNS bundle of axons, but a ganglion is a cluster of cell bodies outside the CNS. This mixes one CNS term with one PNS term.","A ganglion and a nerve are the PNS terms for a cluster of cell bodies and a bundle of axons. Pick this pair if the question asked about structures outside the brain and spinal cord.","Correct. Nucleus and tract are the CNS terms.","A nucleus is correct for a CNS cluster of cell bodies, but a nerve is a bundle of axons in the PNS. This mixes one CNS term with one PNS term."],"lt":"t-w8-tissue-collections","lid":"w8-tissue-collections-r1","comp":179,"competencyId":"w8-tissue-collections"},{"id":"L04609","dok":1,"q":"In the PNS, what is a cluster of neuron cell bodies called, and what is a bundle of axons called?","a":"A ganglion and a nerve.","options":["A nucleus and a tract","A nucleus and a nerve","A ganglion and a tract","A ganglion and a nerve"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Outside the brain and spinal cord, a cluster of neuron cell bodies is called a ganglion, and a bundle of axons wrapped in connective tissue is called a nerve. A posterior root ganglion and the sciatic nerve are examples. Inside the CNS, the same two structures are called a nucleus and a tract.","notes":["A nucleus and a tract are the CNS terms for a cluster of cell bodies and a bundle of axons. Pick this pair if the question asked about structures inside the brain or spinal cord.","A nerve is correct for a PNS bundle of axons, but a nucleus is a cluster of cell bodies inside the CNS. This mixes one CNS term with one PNS term.","A ganglion is correct for a PNS cluster of cell bodies, but a tract is a bundle of axons inside the CNS. This mixes one PNS term with one CNS term.","Correct. Ganglion and nerve are the PNS terms."],"lt":"t-w8-tissue-collections","lid":"w8-tissue-collections-r2","comp":179,"competencyId":"w8-tissue-collections"},{"id":"L04610","dok":1,"q":"What is white matter made of?","a":"Myelinated axons.","options":["Unmyelinated axons and dendrites","Neuron cell bodies","Myelinated axons","Neuroglia and blood vessels"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"White matter is nervous tissue made of myelinated axons. Myelin is a fatty wrapping of lipid and protein, and that fat gives these regions their pale, glossy look. White matter is where signals travel over distance, and in the CNS its axons run in bundles called tracts.","notes":["Unmyelinated axons and dendrites lack the fatty myelin wrapping, so they are found in gray matter, not white. They would be part of the answer for a question about gray matter.","Neuron cell bodies are the main part of gray matter, the tissue where signals are processed. Pick cell bodies if the question asked what gray matter is made of.","Correct. Myelinated axons make up white matter.","Neuroglia and blood vessels are present throughout nervous tissue, but they are not what defines white matter. This choice comes from forgetting that the white color comes from myelin."],"lt":"t-w8-tissue-collections","lid":"w8-tissue-collections-r3","comp":180,"competencyId":"w8-gray-white-matter"},{"id":"L04611","dok":2,"q":"Which description fits gray matter?","a":"Neuron cell bodies, dendrites, unmyelinated axons, and neuroglia.","options":["Cell bodies, dendrites, and unmyelinated axons","Bundles of myelinated axons wrapped by Schwann cells","Connective tissue that wraps fascicles of nerve axons","Myelinated axons running in long bundles called tracts"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Gray matter is made of neuron cell bodies, dendrites, unmyelinated axons, and neuroglia. Because it lacks the fatty myelin that makes white matter pale, it looks gray. Gray matter is where synapses happen and information is processed, such as the cerebral cortex and the inner core of the spinal cord.","notes":["Correct. Cell bodies, dendrites, and unmyelinated axons make up gray matter, along with neuroglia.","Myelinated axons wrapped by Schwann cells are found in peripheral nerves, not in gray matter. This would fit a question about the axons inside a PNS nerve.","Connective tissue around fascicles of axons is the perineurium of a peripheral nerve. Choose this if the question asked which nerve wrapping surrounds each fascicle.","Myelinated axons running in tracts make up white matter, the tissue that carries signals over distance. Pick this if the question asked what white matter is made of."],"lt":"t-w8-tissue-collections","lid":"w8-tissue-collections-r4","comp":180,"competencyId":"w8-gray-white-matter"},{"id":"L04612","dok":2,"q":"On a spinal cord model, a swelling on the posterior root just outside the cord holds the cell bodies of sensory neurons. Which general term fits this structure?","a":"A ganglion.","options":["A ganglion","A nucleus","A nerve","A tract"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Two questions sort these four terms: is the structure a cluster of cell bodies or a bundle of axons, and is it inside the CNS or outside it? This swelling holds cell bodies and sits outside the cord, so it is a ganglion, the posterior (dorsal) root ganglion. A cluster of cell bodies inside the CNS would be a nucleus.","notes":["Correct. A cluster of cell bodies outside the CNS is a ganglion.","A nucleus is also a cluster of cell bodies, but it lies inside the brain or spinal cord. Pick nucleus if the cell bodies were within the CNS, such as the motor nuclei in the anterior horn.","A nerve is a bundle of axons in the PNS, such as the spinal nerve formed just beyond this swelling. Choose it for a bundle of axons outside the CNS.","A tract is a bundle of axons inside the CNS, such as the ascending and descending tracts in the white matter of the cord. It would be the answer for a bundle of axons, not a cluster of cell bodies."],"lt":"t-w8-tissue-collections","lid":"w8-tissue-collections-r5","comp":179,"competencyId":"w8-tissue-collections"},{"id":"L04614","dok":1,"q":"In the cerebrum, where is the gray matter of the cerebral cortex located relative to the white matter?","a":"Gray matter is on the outside; white matter is on the inside.","options":["Inside, surrounded by the white matter","Only in front of the white matter","Outside, covering the white matter","Mixed evenly through the white matter"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The cerebral cortex is a thin outer layer of gray matter, rich in neuron cell bodies, that covers the cerebrum. Beneath it lies the white matter, made of myelinated axons that link the cortex to other regions. This arrangement is the reverse of the spinal cord, so always name the organ before naming where each layer sits.","notes":["Gray matter inside with white matter around it is the arrangement of the spinal cord. Pick this if the question asked about a cord cross-section.","The cortex covers the whole surface of the cerebrum, not just the front. This mix-up may come from the frontal lobe, which is only one region of the cortex.","Correct. The gray cortex covers the white matter of the cerebrum.","The cortex is a distinct outer layer, not gray matter scattered evenly through the white. This choice comes from not separating the cortex from the white matter beneath it."],"lt":"t-w8-gray-white-matter","lid":"w8-gray-white-matter-r2","comp":180,"competencyId":"w8-gray-white-matter"},{"id":"L04616","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares the arrangement of gray and white matter in the brain and in the spinal cord?","a":"In the brain gray matter is outside and white inside; in the cord the arrangement is reversed.","options":["Brain: gray outside, white inside. Cord: white outside, gray inside","Brain: white outside, gray inside. Cord: gray outside, white inside","In both the brain and the spinal cord, gray sits outside and white inside","In both the brain and the spinal cord, white sits outside and gray inside"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Gray matter is where the cell bodies are, and white matter is where the myelinated axons run. In the cerebrum, the gray cortex lies on the outside over the white matter. In the spinal cord the pattern flips: a butterfly-shaped gray core is surrounded by white columns of tracts. Because the two swap positions, name the organ first, then the layer.","notes":["Correct. The brain has gray outside, and the cord has gray inside.","This statement reverses both organs. The cerebral cortex is gray and on the outside of the brain, and the cord's gray matter is the inner butterfly.","This is true of the brain only. The cord reverses the pattern, with gray matter in the core and white matter outside.","This is true of the spinal cord only. The cerebrum has its gray cortex on the outside."],"lt":"t-w8-gray-white-matter","lid":"w8-gray-white-matter-r4","comp":180,"competencyId":"w8-gray-white-matter"},{"id":"L04661","dok":1,"q":"What is a synapse?","a":"The site where a neuron communicates with another neuron, a muscle cell, or a gland.","options":["A gap in the myelin sheath where the impulse jumps ahead","The connective tissue wrapping around an axon","The site where a neuron communicates with another cell","The cone-shaped region of the cell body where the axon begins"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"A synapse is the junction where a neuron passes its signal to another cell: another neuron, a muscle cell, or a gland. At a chemical synapse the two cells do not touch. The sending cell releases neurotransmitter, which crosses a narrow gap to reach the receiving cell. Synapses are named for what the axon ends on, such as axodendritic (onto a dendrite) or axosomatic (onto a cell body).","notes":["A gap in the myelin sheath is a node of Ranvier. Pick it if the question asked where the impulse jumps along a myelinated axon.","The connective tissue wrapping around an individual axon in a nerve is the endoneurium. Choose it if the question asked for the innermost nerve wrapping.","Correct. A synapse is where a neuron passes its signal to another cell.","The cone-shaped region where the axon begins is the axon hillock. It would be the answer for a question about where the cell body joins the axon."],"lt":"t-w8-synapse-parts","lid":"w8-synapse-parts-r1","comp":175,"competencyId":"w8-synapse-parts"},{"id":"L04662","dok":1,"q":"What is the synaptic cleft?","a":"The narrow gap between the two cells that the neurotransmitter diffuses across.","options":["The sac that stores neurotransmitter in the end bulb","The narrow gap neurotransmitter crosses between cells","The bare gap between two segments of myelin along an axon","The swelling at the tip of an axon terminal that sends the signal"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"At a chemical synapse, the sending and receiving cells do not touch. The synaptic cleft is the narrow gap between them. Neurotransmitter released from the sending cell diffuses across the cleft to reach the receiving cell, which is how the signal crosses from one cell to the next.","notes":["The sacs that store neurotransmitter are the synaptic vesicles, found inside the end bulbs. Pick them if the question asked where neurotransmitter is stored.","Correct. The synaptic cleft is the gap the neurotransmitter crosses.","The bare gap between segments of myelin is a node of Ranvier. That is a gap along a single axon, not a gap between two cells.","The swelling at the tip of an axon terminal is a synaptic end bulb. Choose it if the question asked which structure holds the vesicles and sends the signal."],"lt":"t-w8-synapse-parts","lid":"w8-synapse-parts-r2","comp":175,"competencyId":"w8-synapse-parts"},{"id":"L04663","dok":2,"q":"What do synaptic vesicles store, and where are they found?","a":"Neurotransmitter, stored in the synaptic end bulbs of the axon terminal.","options":["Myelin, inside the Schwann cells wrapped around the axon","Cerebrospinal fluid, inside the ependymal cells of the ventricles","Neurotransmitter, inside the dendrites of the receiving neuron","Neurotransmitter, in the synaptic end bulbs of the axon"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Synaptic vesicles are tiny sacs inside the synaptic end bulbs, the swellings at the tips of the axon terminals. They store neurotransmitter, the chemical messenger. When an impulse reaches the end bulb, the vesicles release their neurotransmitter into the synaptic cleft, and it crosses to the next cell. The vesicles are on the sending side of the synapse.","notes":["Myelin is a wrapping of lipid and protein laid down around the axon by Schwann cells, not stored in vesicles. Pick Schwann cells if the question asked which cells myelinate PNS axons.","Cerebrospinal fluid is produced with the help of ependymal cells and the choroid plexus, but it fills the ventricles rather than being stored in vesicles. Choose ependymal cells for a question about the lining of the ventricles.","The contents are right, but the location is wrong. Dendrites are on the receiving side of the synapse, while vesicles sit in the sending axon's end bulbs.","Correct. Synaptic vesicles store neurotransmitter in the end bulbs."],"lt":"t-w8-synapse-parts","lid":"w8-synapse-parts-r3","comp":175,"competencyId":"w8-synapse-parts"},{"id":"L04664","dok":1,"q":"What is the myelin sheath, and what does it do?","a":"A fatty wrapping around an axon that insulates it and speeds the nerve impulse.","options":["A connective tissue layer that bundles axons into a fascicle","A fluid-filled sac that stores neurotransmitter at the terminal","A fatty wrapping that insulates the axon and speeds the impulse","A protein coat that slows the impulse to protect the axon"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The myelin sheath is a multilayered covering of lipid and protein wrapped around an axon by glial cells: Schwann cells in the PNS and oligodendrocytes in the CNS. It insulates the axon and makes the impulse travel much faster, because the impulse jumps from one gap in the myelin to the next. In multiple sclerosis, CNS myelin is stripped away and signals slow or fail.","notes":["The connective tissue layer around each fascicle is the perineurium. Pick it if the question asked which wrapping bundles axons into fascicles.","A sac that stores neurotransmitter is a synaptic vesicle. It would be the answer if the question asked where neurotransmitter is kept before release.","Correct. Myelin is a fatty wrapping that insulates the axon and speeds the impulse.","Myelin speeds the impulse rather than slowing it, and it is mostly lipid, not a simple protein coat. Students pick this when they think of myelin only as protection."],"lt":"t-w8-myelination","lid":"w8-myelination-r1","comp":178,"competencyId":"w8-myelination"},{"id":"L04665","dok":1,"q":"What are the nodes of Ranvier?","a":"The gaps in the myelin sheath spaced along the axon.","options":["Clusters of rough ER in the cell body","Swellings at the end of the axon terminals","Knots of interneurons in the gray matter","Gaps in the myelin sheath along the axon"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The myelin sheath is not continuous. It is laid down in segments, and the short bare gaps between segments are the nodes of Ranvier. On a myelinated axon the impulse jumps from node to node, called saltatory conduction, which is much faster than traveling along an unmyelinated axon.","notes":["Clusters of rough ER and ribosomes in the cell body are the Nissl bodies, where proteins are made. Pick them if the question asked about protein synthesis in a neuron.","The swellings at the ends of the axon terminals are the synaptic end bulbs. They would be the answer if the question asked where synaptic vesicles are found.","A cluster of neuron cell bodies inside the CNS is a nucleus, not a node. Choose nucleus if the question asked about a group of cell bodies in the brain or cord.","Correct. The nodes of Ranvier are the gaps between segments of myelin."],"lt":"t-w8-myelination","lid":"w8-myelination-r2","comp":178,"competencyId":"w8-myelination"},{"id":"L04666","dok":3,"q":"Why can a cut nerve in the arm often regrow, while a cut tract in the spinal cord usually cannot?","a":"PNS Schwann cells leave a neurolemma that guides regrowth; CNS oligodendrocytes do not.","options":["Oligodendrocytes leave a neurolemma that guides regrowth; Schwann cells do not","Schwann cells leave a neurolemma that guides regrowth; oligodendrocytes do not","Neurons in the PNS can divide to replace the cut cells; CNS neurons cannot","PNS axons lack myelin, so they regrow faster than myelinated CNS axons"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"In the PNS, each Schwann cell wraps one segment of one axon and leaves an outer layer called the neurolemma. After a cut, the neurolemma forms a regeneration tube that guides the regrowing axon back toward its target. In the CNS, oligodendrocytes leave no neurolemma, they release substances that block regrowth, and astrocytes fill the area with scar tissue, so regrowth is very limited.","notes":["This swaps the two cells. The neurolemma belongs to Schwann cells in the PNS, and oligodendrocytes in the CNS do not have one.","Correct. The Schwann cell's neurolemma guides PNS axons as they regrow.","Neurons cannot divide in either the PNS or the CNS. A peripheral nerve recovers when the surviving neuron regrows its axon, not when new neurons are made.","Many PNS axons are myelinated, and the arm's nerves contain plenty of them. The difference in regrowth comes from the neurolemma, not from whether the axon has myelin."],"lt":"t-w8-myelination","lid":"w8-myelination-r3","comp":178,"competencyId":"w8-myelination"},{"id":"L04674","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares convergence and divergence in a neuronal pool?","a":"In convergence many neurons feed onto one; in divergence one neuron spreads its signal to many.","options":["Convergence: one straight chain. Divergence: several paths at the same time","Convergence: many neurons feed onto one. Divergence: one neuron spreads to many","Convergence: one neuron spreads to many. Divergence: many neurons feed onto one","Convergence: pathways cross sides. Divergence: pathways stay on one side"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"A neuronal pool is a group of interconnected neurons in the CNS that process information together, and the wiring pattern shapes how a signal is handled. In convergence, many neurons send their signals onto a single neuron, so one cell can combine input from many sources. In divergence, one neuron sends its signal out to many neurons, so a single input can spread widely. Converge means come together, and diverge means spread apart.","notes":["A single straight chain is serial processing, and several paths at once is parallel processing. Pick these terms if the question asked about serial versus parallel processing.","Correct. Convergence is many onto one, and divergence is one onto many.","This swaps the two terms. Converge means come together, so convergence is many onto one, and divergence is one out to many.","Crossing to the opposite side is called decussation, which is why each half of the brain serves the opposite half of the body. This would be the answer for a question about why a stroke affects the opposite side."],"lt":"t-w8-neuronal-pools","lid":"w8-neuronal-pools-r1","comp":183,"competencyId":"w8-neuronal-pools"},{"id":"N-a17d-173-1","dok":3,"q":"An injury cuts only the motor fibers that run to the heart, smooth muscle, and glands, while skeletal muscle still works. Which motor division is damaged, and is it part of the CNS or the PNS?","a":"The autonomic nervous system, part of the PNS.","options":["The somatic nervous system, part of the PNS","The enteric nervous system, part of the CNS","The autonomic nervous system, part of the PNS","The autonomic nervous system, part of the CNS"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The motor side of the PNS is split by the effector it controls. The somatic nervous system runs skeletal muscle, while the autonomic nervous system runs smooth muscle, cardiac muscle, and glands. Both are motor divisions of the peripheral nervous system, which carries commands away from the CNS.","notes":["The somatic system controls skeletal muscle, which still works here. It is the autonomic system that runs the heart, smooth muscle, and glands.","The enteric system is a network in the wall of the digestive tract, and it sits outside the brain and cord. It is not the division for the heart and all glands.","Correct. Motor fibers to cardiac muscle, smooth muscle, and glands belong to the autonomic nervous system, a motor division of the PNS.","The division is right, but the CNS is only the brain and spinal cord. The autonomic motor division is part of the PNS."],"comp":173,"competencyId":"w8-ns-organization"},{"id":"N-a17d-173-2","dok":3,"q":"A network of neurons in the wall of a section of intestine keeps the gut's smooth muscle moving even after its links to the brain and spinal cord are cut. Which division is this, and which division normally connects to it and adjusts it?","a":"The enteric nervous system, adjusted by the autonomic nervous system.","options":["The autonomic nervous system, adjusted by the enteric nervous system","The enteric nervous system, adjusted by the autonomic nervous system","The enteric nervous system, adjusted by the somatic nervous system","The somatic nervous system, adjusted by the autonomic nervous system"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The enteric nervous system is a network of roughly 100 million neurons in the wall of the digestive tract. It controls the gut's smooth muscle and glands and can run on its own. The autonomic nervous system connects to it and adjusts it, which is why some textbooks count it as part of the ANS.","notes":["This reverses the relationship. The network in the gut wall is the enteric system, and the ANS adjusts it.","Correct. The enteric nervous system runs the gut wall on its own, and the autonomic nervous system connects to it and adjusts it.","The division is right, but the somatic system controls skeletal muscle. The ANS is the one that connects to the enteric system.","The somatic system sends voluntary commands to skeletal muscle. It does not run the smooth muscle in the gut wall."],"comp":173,"competencyId":"w8-ns-organization"},{"id":"N-a17d-174-1","dok":3,"q":"On a neuron slide, one process leaves the cell body from a pale cone with no dark granules, while the others leave from cytoplasm dotted with dark granules. Which process is the axon, and how do you know?","a":"The one from the pale cone, because the axon hillock lacks Nissl bodies.","options":["The one from the pale cone, because the axon hillock lacks Nissl bodies","One from the dotted cytoplasm, because Nissl bodies mark the axon","The one from the pale cone, because the hillock is packed with Nissl bodies","The most branched one, because the axon is the most branched process"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Nissl bodies are clusters of rough ER and ribosomes that stain dark, and they appear in the cell body and the dendrites. The axon hillock, where the cell body narrows into the axon, has none, so it stains paler. That pale cone is how you find the axon on a slide and tell it apart from the dendrites.","notes":["Correct. The axon hillock has no Nissl bodies, so the pale cone marks where the axon begins.","Nissl bodies appear in the cell body and dendrites, not at the start of the axon. A process leaving from dotted cytoplasm is a dendrite.","The process is right, but the reason is backward. The hillock is pale because it has no Nissl bodies.","Dendrites are the short, heavily branched processes. The axon is a single long process that branches mainly at its far end, into axon terminals."],"comp":174,"competencyId":"w8-neuron-parts"},{"id":"N-a17d-174-2","dok":3,"q":"A neuron's axon is cut just past the axon hillock. Signals arriving at the dendrites can still reach which part, and where will impulses no longer arrive?","a":"They still reach the cell body, but impulses no longer reach the axon terminals.","options":["They reach neither the cell body nor the axon terminals","They still reach the axon terminals, but not the cell body","They still reach the cell body, and the dendrites carry them on to the terminals","They still reach the cell body, but impulses no longer reach the axon terminals"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"A signal travels through a neuron in one direction. Dendrites receive it and carry it toward the cell body, the impulse starts at the trigger zone where the hillock meets the axon, and the axon carries it away to the axon terminals. A cut just past the hillock leaves the dendrites and cell body connected but separates them from the far end of the axon and its terminals.","notes":["The dendrites and cell body are on the same side of the cut, so signals still reach the cell body.","Dendrites carry signals toward the cell body, which is still connected to them. The cut is between the cell body and the terminals, so the terminals are what lose the signal.","Dendrites carry signals toward the cell body, not out to the axon terminals. Only the axon carries the impulse away to the terminals.","Correct. Dendrites still deliver signals to the cell body, but the cut axon cannot carry impulses out to the terminals."],"comp":174,"competencyId":"w8-neuron-parts"},{"id":"N-a17d-175-1","dok":3,"q":"On a model, an axon from neuron A ends on the long single process of neuron B, close to where that process branches into B's own terminals. What type of synapse is this, and which neuron is postsynaptic?","a":"Axoaxonic, with neuron B postsynaptic.","options":["Axodendritic, with neuron B postsynaptic","Axoaxonic, with neuron A postsynaptic","Axoaxonic, with neuron B postsynaptic","Axosomatic, with neuron A postsynaptic"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"A neuron's long single process that branches into terminals at its far end is its axon. Synapse names start with axo for the sending axon and end with the part it lands on, so an axon ending on another axon is axoaxonic. The cell that receives the signal is the postsynaptic cell, which here is neuron B.","notes":["Neuron B is postsynaptic, but its long single process that ends in terminals is an axon, not a dendrite. Dendrites are short and heavily branched.","The type is right, but neuron A is the one sending the signal, so it is presynaptic. The receiving neuron is B.","Correct. The long single process that ends in terminals is B's axon, so this is an axoaxonic synapse with B receiving.","An axosomatic synapse ends on a cell body, not on the long process. Neuron A is also the sender, not the receiver."],"comp":175,"competencyId":"w8-synapse-parts"},{"id":"N-a17d-175-2","dok":3,"q":"An electron micrograph shows two cells that do not touch, separated by a gap of about 30 nanometers, with tiny sacs on one side only. What kind of synapse is this, and how does the signal cross the gap?","a":"A chemical synapse, with neurotransmitter diffusing across the cleft.","options":["A chemical synapse, with neurotransmitter diffusing across the cleft","An electrical synapse, with gap junctions joining the cells","An electrical synapse, with neurotransmitter diffusing across the cleft","A chemical synapse, with ions passing through gap junctions"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"At a chemical synapse the two cells do not touch. They are separated by the synaptic cleft, only about 20 to 50 nanometers wide, and synaptic vesicles on the presynaptic side store neurotransmitter. The neurotransmitter is released and diffuses across the cleft to receptors on the postsynaptic membrane.","notes":["Correct. A gap of about 20 to 50 nanometers with vesicles on one side is a chemical synapse, and neurotransmitter diffuses across the cleft.","An electrical synapse joins two cells directly through gap junctions, so the cells are connected. Here the cells do not touch and vesicles are present.","Neurotransmitter crossing a cleft is how a chemical synapse works. An electrical synapse has no cleft to cross, because gap junctions connect the cells.","The synapse type is right, but gap junctions belong to electrical synapses. At a chemical synapse the signal crosses as neurotransmitter."],"comp":175,"competencyId":"w8-synapse-parts"},{"id":"N-a17d-179-1","dok":3,"q":"A slide taken from a swelling on a peripheral nerve shows neuron cell bodies, each surrounded by small satellite cells. What is this structure, and what would the same kind of cluster be called inside the brainstem?","a":"A ganglion, which inside the CNS would be called a nucleus.","options":["A nucleus, which inside the CNS would be called a ganglion","A ganglion, which inside the CNS would be called a tract","A tract, which inside the CNS would be called a nerve","A ganglion, which inside the CNS would be called a nucleus"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"First decide whether you are looking at cell bodies or axons, then whether you are inside the CNS or outside it. A cluster of neuron cell bodies outside the CNS, with satellite cells around them, is a ganglion. The same kind of cluster inside the CNS, such as a cranial nerve nucleus in the brainstem, is called a nucleus.","notes":["This reverses the rule. A nucleus is the CNS cluster of cell bodies, and a ganglion is the PNS cluster.","Ganglion is right, but a tract is a bundle of axons in the CNS. The CNS cluster of cell bodies is a nucleus.","Tracts and nerves are bundles of axons, not clusters of cell bodies. A nerve is also the PNS term, not the CNS term.","Correct. A cluster of cell bodies in the PNS is a ganglion, and the same kind of cluster inside the CNS is a nucleus."],"comp":179,"competencyId":"w8-tissue-collections"},{"id":"N-a17d-179-2","dok":3,"q":"The basal ganglia sit deep in the cerebrum. A student says their name proves they are part of the PNS. Why is the student wrong, and which term fits them better?","a":"They sit in the CNS, so basal nuclei is the better term.","options":["They sit in the CNS, so nerves is the better term","They sit in the CNS, so basal nuclei is the better term","They are bundles of axons, so tracts is the better term","They are in the PNS, so basal ganglia is correct"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"By the naming rule, a cluster of neuron cell bodies inside the CNS is a nucleus, and one in the PNS is a ganglion. The basal ganglia are clusters of cell bodies deep in the cerebrum, which is part of the CNS. Basal ganglia is an older name that breaks the rule, so many texts use basal nuclei instead.","notes":["They are in the CNS, but a nerve is a bundle of axons in the PNS. A cluster of cell bodies in the CNS is a nucleus.","Correct. Cell body clusters inside the CNS are nuclei, so basal nuclei fits, and basal ganglia is an older name that breaks the rule.","The basal nuclei are masses of gray matter, clusters of cell bodies, not bundles of axons. Tracts make up the white matter.","The cerebrum is part of the brain, which is CNS. The name ganglia here is an older exception, not proof of a PNS location."],"comp":179,"competencyId":"w8-tissue-collections"}]},{"id":"m5-cerebrum","title":"Cerebrum: lobes, cortex areas and deep structures","summary":"brain regions, gyri, sulci, fissures, lobes, functional cortical areas, white matter tracts, basal nuclei, limbic system","mw":"2026-11-30","tr":"2026-12-01","cards":[{"id":"L02841","dok":1,"q":"What are the four major regions of the brain?","a":"The cerebrum, diencephalon, brainstem, and cerebellum.","options":["The midbrain, pons, medulla oblongata, and hypothalamus","The frontal, parietal, temporal, and occipital lobes","The cortex, ventricles, gyri, and sulci","The cerebrum, diencephalon, brainstem, and cerebellum"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The brain is divided into four major regions. The cerebrum is the large upper region for conscious thought and voluntary movement, the diencephalon sits deep beneath it, the brainstem is the stalk that connects to the spinal cord, and the cerebellum sits behind the brainstem and coordinates movement. Every other named brain part, such as a lobe, the thalamus, or the pons, is a subdivision of one of these four.","notes":["This list mixes regions. The midbrain, pons, and medulla are the three parts of the brainstem, and the hypothalamus belongs to the diencephalon. The midbrain, pons, and medulla alone would answer a question about the regions of the brainstem.","These are four of the five lobes of the cerebrum, so they divide only one region of the brain. Pick this list if the question asked for the surface lobes of a cerebral hemisphere that are named for the skull bones above them.","These are features of the brain rather than regions. The cortex is the outer gray matter, gyri and sulci are its ridges and grooves, and the ventricles are fluid-filled chambers inside the brain.","Correct. The cerebrum, diencephalon, brainstem, and cerebellum are the four major regions."],"lt":"t-cns-brain-spinal-cord","lid":"c2","comp":151,"competencyId":"w7-brain-organization"},{"id":"L02842","dok":1,"q":"Which is the largest part of the brain?","a":"The cerebrum.","options":["The cerebellum","The diencephalon","The cerebrum","The brainstem"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The cerebrum is the largest part of the brain and is the seat of conscious thought, sensation, and voluntary movement. It is made of the right and left cerebral hemispheres, joined by the corpus callosum. Its surface is folded into gyri and sulci so that more cortex fits inside the skull.","notes":["The cerebellum is the second largest part of the brain, sitting behind the brainstem below the occipital lobes. It would be the answer if the question asked which region coordinates movement, posture, and balance.","The diencephalon is a small region tucked deep between the cerebrum and the brainstem. Pick it if the question asked which region holds the thalamus, hypothalamus, and epithalamus.","Correct. The cerebrum is the largest part of the brain.","The brainstem is the narrow stalk that connects the higher brain to the spinal cord. It would be the answer if the question asked which region is made of the midbrain, pons, and medulla oblongata."],"lt":"t-cns-brain-spinal-cord","lid":"c3","comp":151,"competencyId":"w7-brain-organization"},{"id":"L02843","dok":1,"q":"What is the cerebral cortex?","a":"The thin outer layer of gray matter on the cerebrum.","options":["The broad band of white matter joining the hemispheres","The fluid-filled chambers inside the cerebrum","The thin outer layer of gray matter on the cerebrum","The deep masses of gray matter within the cerebrum"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The cerebral cortex is the outer layer of gray matter that covers the cerebrum, only about 2 to 4 mm thick. Gray matter is made of neuron cell bodies, dendrites, and unmyelinated fibers, so the cortex is where most of the cerebrum's neurons actually sit. Its folds, the gyri and sulci, greatly increase the amount of cortex that fits in the skull.","notes":["This describes the corpus callosum, a band of white matter (myelinated axons) that connects the right and left hemispheres. Pick it if the question asked which structure lets the two hemispheres communicate.","This describes the ventricles, the fluid-filled chambers where cerebrospinal fluid is made. Pick it if the question asked where the choroid plexus is found.","Correct. The cortex is the thin outer gray matter layer of the cerebrum.","This describes the basal nuclei (basal ganglia), clusters of gray matter buried within the cerebral white matter that help regulate movement. They are deep, not on the surface."],"lt":"t-cns-brain-spinal-cord","lid":"c4","comp":151,"competencyId":"w7-brain-organization"},{"id":"L02844","dok":1,"q":"The raised folds on the surface of the cerebral cortex are called what?","a":"Gyri.","options":["Gyri","Sulci","Fissures","Ventricles"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The cerebral cortex is folded into raised ridges called gyri (singular gyrus), with grooves between them. Folding lets a large sheet of cortex fit inside the skull. Named gyri, such as the precentral gyrus, serve as landmarks for functional areas.","notes":["Correct. Gyri are the raised folds of the cortex.","Sulci are the shallow grooves that lie between the gyri, the dips rather than the ridges. Pick sulci if the question asked about the grooves, such as the central sulcus between the frontal and parietal lobes.","Fissures are the deepest grooves on the brain, which divide major regions, such as the longitudinal fissure between the hemispheres. They would be the answer for a question about the deepest grooves.","Ventricles are fluid-filled chambers deep inside the brain, not surface features. Pick them if the question asked where cerebrospinal fluid is produced."],"lt":"t-cns-brain-spinal-cord","lid":"c5","comp":153,"competencyId":"w7-cerebrum-surface"},{"id":"L02845","dok":1,"q":"What is the corpus callosum?","a":"The white matter band joining the two cerebral hemispheres.","options":["The white matter band joining the two hemispheres","The tapered inferior end of the spinal cord","The sensory relay station deep in the diencephalon","The outer layer of gray matter on the cerebrum"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The corpus callosum is a broad band of white matter, meaning bundles of myelinated axons, that connects the right and left cerebral hemispheres. It is the largest set of commissural fibers, the class of fibers that link matching areas in the two hemispheres. It lies at the bottom of the longitudinal fissure.","notes":["Correct. The corpus callosum is the white matter band that joins the hemispheres.","This describes the conus medullaris, the cone-shaped end of the spinal cord near the L1 to L2 disc. It would be the answer for a question about where the cord ends.","This describes the thalamus, the paired gray matter masses of the diencephalon that relay sensory signals to the cortex.","This describes the cerebral cortex, which is gray matter on the surface of the cerebrum. Pick it if the question asked which layer holds most of the cerebrum's neuron cell bodies."],"lt":"t-cns-brain-spinal-cord","lid":"c6","comp":155,"competencyId":"w7-white-matter-tracts"},{"id":"L02854","dok":2,"q":"The precentral gyrus lies at the rear of which lobe of the cerebrum?","a":"The frontal lobe.","options":["The parietal lobe","The occipital lobe","The temporal lobe","The frontal lobe"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The precentral gyrus is the ridge just in front of the central sulcus, which makes it the most posterior gyrus of the frontal lobe. It holds the primary motor cortex, which initiates voluntary movement. The name tells you its position: pre means in front of, and central refers to the central sulcus.","notes":["The parietal lobe lies just behind the central sulcus, and its front edge is the postcentral gyrus, home of the primary somatosensory cortex. Pick the parietal lobe if the question asked about the postcentral gyrus.","The occipital lobe is at the back of the cerebrum and processes vision. It would be the answer if the question asked where the primary visual cortex is found.","The temporal lobe lies on the lower side of the cerebrum, below the lateral sulcus. Pick it if the question asked where the primary auditory cortex is located.","Correct. The precentral gyrus forms the back edge of the frontal lobe."],"lt":"t-cns-brain-spinal-cord","lid":"c15","comp":153,"competencyId":"w7-cerebrum-surface"},{"id":"L02855","dok":2,"q":"Which lobe of the cerebrum sits at the very back and is dedicated to processing vision?","a":"The occipital lobe.","options":["The parietal lobe","The temporal lobe","The frontal lobe","The occipital lobe"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The occipital lobe forms the back of each cerebral hemisphere, beneath the occipital bone, and handles visual processing. The primary visual cortex lies at the calcarine sulcus on its medial surface. Damage to this lobe causes loss of vision even when the eyes themselves are healthy.","notes":["The parietal lobe lies behind the central sulcus and handles touch and body sensation. Pick it if the question asked where the postcentral gyrus is found.","The temporal lobe lies on the lower side of the cerebrum and handles hearing and memory. It would be the answer for a question about the primary auditory cortex.","The frontal lobe is at the front of the cerebrum and handles voluntary movement, planning, and speech production. Pick it if the question asked where the primary motor cortex or Broca area sits.","Correct. The occipital lobe is at the back and processes vision."],"lt":"t-cns-brain-spinal-cord","lid":"c16","comp":153,"competencyId":"w7-cerebrum-surface"},{"id":"L02856","dok":2,"q":"Which cerebral lobe lies on the lower side of the cerebrum and is associated with hearing and memory?","a":"The temporal lobe.","options":["The insula","The parietal lobe","The temporal lobe","The occipital lobe"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The temporal lobe sits on the lower side of each hemisphere, below the lateral sulcus and beneath the temporal bone. It holds the primary auditory cortex, much of the language comprehension area (Wernicke area), and structures used for memory. Its position near the ear helps students remember its role in hearing.","notes":["The insula is buried deep within the lateral sulcus rather than lying on the lower outer surface, and it handles taste and visceral sensation. Pick it if the question asked which lobe cannot be seen from the outside.","The parietal lobe lies behind the central sulcus, above the temporal lobe, and handles touch and spatial orientation. Pick it if the question asked which lobe holds the primary somatosensory cortex.","Correct. The temporal lobe handles hearing and memory.","The occipital lobe is at the back of the cerebrum and processes vision. It would be the answer for a question about the primary visual cortex."],"lt":"t-cns-brain-spinal-cord","lid":"c17","comp":153,"competencyId":"w7-cerebrum-surface"},{"id":"L02857","dok":2,"q":"Which cerebral lobe is buried deep within the lateral sulcus rather than lying on the outer surface?","a":"The insula.","options":["The temporal lobe","The insula","The parietal lobe","The frontal lobe"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Each cerebral hemisphere has five lobes. Four of them lie on the surface, but the fifth, the insula, is hidden deep within the lateral sulcus and covered by the frontal, parietal, and temporal lobes. The insula is involved in taste, visceral sensation, and emotion.","notes":["The temporal lobe forms the lower side of the cerebrum and is visible from outside, below the lateral sulcus. It would be the answer if the question asked which lobe handles hearing.","Correct. The insula is the lobe hidden in the lateral sulcus.","The parietal lobe lies on the surface behind the central sulcus. Pick it if the question asked which lobe handles touch and body sensation.","The frontal lobe forms the front of the cerebrum and is visible on the surface. It would be the answer for a question about the lobe holding the primary motor cortex."],"lt":"t-cns-brain-spinal-cord","lid":"c18","comp":153,"competencyId":"w7-cerebrum-surface"},{"id":"L02864","dok":2,"q":"The basal nuclei (basal ganglia) are deep masses of which type of matter, and within which brain region?","a":"Gray matter, deep within the cerebrum.","options":["Gray matter, deep within the cerebrum","White matter, deep within the cerebrum","White matter, within the brainstem","Gray matter, within the cerebellum"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The basal nuclei, also called the basal ganglia or cerebral nuclei, are clusters of neuron cell bodies buried within the white matter of the cerebrum, which makes them gray matter. They include the caudate nucleus, putamen, and globus pallidus. They help start and regulate movement and suppress unwanted movement.","notes":["Correct. The basal nuclei are gray matter deep in the cerebrum.","The region is right, but the basal nuclei are clusters of cell bodies, which makes them gray matter. The white matter around them is made of myelinated axons, such as the internal capsule, which runs beside the basal nuclei.","This misses on both counts. The brainstem does contain white matter tracts, but the basal nuclei are gray matter and lie in the cerebrum. Pick white matter in the brainstem if the question asked where the descending motor tracts pass through on their way to the cord.","The cerebellum does have deep gray matter nuclei, such as the dentate nucleus, but those are the deep cerebellar nuclei, not the basal nuclei. Pick this if the question asked about nuclei buried in the arbor vitae."],"lt":"t-cns-brain-spinal-cord","lid":"c25","comp":156,"competencyId":"w7-basal-ganglia"},{"id":"L02865","dok":2,"q":"The longitudinal fissure is best classified as which kind of surface feature?","a":"A fissure, the deepest type of groove.","options":["A fissure, the deepest type of groove","A gyrus, a raised fold of cortex","A sulcus, a shallow groove","A ventricle, a fluid-filled chamber"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The cerebral surface has ridges (gyri), shallow grooves (sulci), and the deepest grooves (fissures), which divide major regions of the brain. The longitudinal fissure is a fissure that separates the right and left cerebral hemispheres. The falx cerebri, a fold of dura mater, dips down into it.","notes":["Correct. The longitudinal fissure is one of the deepest grooves.","A gyrus is a raised ridge of cortex, such as the precentral gyrus, not a groove. Pick a gyrus if the question asked about the folds between the grooves.","A sulcus is a shallow groove between gyri, such as the central sulcus. It would be the answer for a groove that separates lobes rather than whole hemispheres.","A ventricle is a fluid-filled chamber inside the brain, not a surface feature. Pick it if the question asked about spaces that hold cerebrospinal fluid."],"lt":"t-cns-brain-spinal-cord","lid":"c26","comp":153,"competencyId":"w7-cerebrum-surface"},{"id":"L02867","dok":2,"q":"Four of the five cerebral lobes (frontal, parietal, temporal, and occipital) are named for what?","a":"The skull bone lying over each one","options":["The skull bone lying over each one","The cranial nerve passing through each one","The artery that supplies each one","The ventricle each one surrounds"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The frontal, parietal, temporal, and occipital lobes each lie under the cranial bone of the same name, so the bone tells you the lobe. The fifth lobe, the insula, is the exception, because it is hidden deep in the lateral sulcus and no bone lies directly over it. Using the skull as a map is a quick way to locate each lobe on a model.","notes":["Correct. Each surface lobe takes the name of the cranial bone above it.","Cranial nerves are numbered in the order they leave the brain, from front to back, and are named for their job or their path. No cerebral lobe is named after a cranial nerve, so this choice mixes up brain naming with nerve naming. Pick cranial nerves for a question about the twelve pairs of nerves attached to the brain.","The lobes are not named for their arteries. The cerebral arteries are named for their position (anterior, middle, and posterior), and each one supplies more than one lobe. For example, the posterior cerebral artery supplies the occipital lobe and the underside of the temporal lobe.","The lateral ventricles do extend into several lobes, but the lobes are not named for them. Students sometimes connect the two because the ventricle horns reach into the frontal, occipital, and temporal lobes."],"lt":"t-cns-brain-spinal-cord","lid":"c28","comp":153,"competencyId":"w7-cerebrum-surface"},{"id":"L02873","dok":2,"q":"Which deep groove separates the cerebrum from the cerebellum?","a":"The transverse fissure.","options":["The central sulcus","The lateral sulcus","The transverse fissure","The longitudinal fissure"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Fissures are the deepest grooves on the brain and divide major regions. The transverse fissure runs horizontally between the underside of the occipital lobes and the top of the cerebellum, separating the cerebrum from the cerebellum. The tentorium cerebelli, a fold of dura mater, extends into this fissure.","notes":["The central sulcus is a groove that separates the frontal lobe from the parietal lobe, with the precentral gyrus in front and the postcentral gyrus behind. It would be the answer for a question about the boundary between motor and somatosensory cortex.","The lateral sulcus separates the temporal lobe from the frontal and parietal lobes, and the insula lies hidden in it. Pick it if the question asked where the insula is found.","Correct. The transverse fissure separates the cerebrum from the cerebellum.","The longitudinal fissure runs front to back down the midline and separates the right and left cerebral hemispheres. Pick it if the question asked what divides the two hemispheres."],"lt":"t-cns-brain-spinal-cord","lid":"c34","comp":153,"competencyId":"w7-cerebrum-surface"},{"id":"L02875","dok":2,"q":"The corpus callosum is the largest example of which class of cerebral white matter fibers?","a":"Commissural fibers.","options":["Projection fibers","Ascending tract fibers","Commissural fibers","Association fibers"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Cerebral white matter is sorted into three classes of fibers by where they begin and end. Commissural fibers connect matching areas of the two hemispheres, and the corpus callosum is the largest commissure. The word commissure means a joining across the midline.","notes":["Projection fibers connect the cortex with lower brain regions and the spinal cord, and the internal capsule is the major example. They would be the answer if the question asked about fibers running up and down rather than across.","Ascending tracts carry sensory signals up the spinal cord and brainstem toward the brain. They are not one of the three classes of cerebral fibers, so a student who confuses cord tracts with cerebral fibers picks this.","Correct. The corpus callosum is made of commissural fibers.","Association fibers connect gyri within the same hemisphere, such as the arcuate fasciculus linking Broca and Wernicke areas. Pick this class if the question asked about fibers that stay on one side."],"lt":"t-cns-brain-spinal-cord","lid":"c36","comp":155,"competencyId":"w7-white-matter-tracts"},{"id":"L02877","dok":2,"q":"Which cerebral lobe lies just behind the central sulcus and processes touch and other body sensations?","a":"The parietal lobe.","options":["The frontal lobe","The occipital lobe","The parietal lobe","The temporal lobe"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The parietal lobe lies behind the central sulcus, under the parietal bone. Its front edge is the postcentral gyrus, which holds the primary somatosensory cortex, the area that receives conscious touch, pressure, and body position. The parietal lobe also contributes to spatial orientation.","notes":["The frontal lobe lies in front of the central sulcus, not behind it, and holds the primary motor cortex. Pick it if the question asked which lobe initiates voluntary movement.","The occipital lobe is at the back of the cerebrum and processes vision. It would be the answer if the question asked where the primary visual cortex sits.","Correct. The parietal lobe processes body sensation.","The temporal lobe is on the lower side of the cerebrum, below the lateral sulcus, and handles hearing and memory. Pick it for a question about the primary auditory cortex."],"lt":"t-cns-brain-spinal-cord","lid":"c38","comp":153,"competencyId":"w7-cerebrum-surface"},{"id":"L02881","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares gray matter and white matter in the cerebrum?","a":"Gray matter is cell bodies on the outside; white matter is myelinated axons inside.","options":["Gray matter is cell bodies on the outside; white matter is myelinated axons inside","Gray matter and white matter are both cell bodies, differing only in color","Gray matter is cell bodies on the inside; white matter is myelinated axons outside","Gray matter is myelinated axons on the outside; white matter is cell bodies inside"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Gray matter is made of neuron cell bodies, dendrites, and unmyelinated fibers, while white matter is bundles of myelinated axons whose fatty myelin gives them a pale color. In the cerebrum the gray matter forms the outer cortex and the white matter lies beneath it, with islands of deep gray matter (the basal nuclei) buried inside. The spinal cord has the reverse arrangement.","notes":["Correct. In the cerebrum, gray matter is the outer cortex of cell bodies and white matter is the inner myelinated axons.","White matter is axons, not cell bodies, and the color difference comes from myelin. A student who thinks the two differ only in shade picks this.","This is the layout of the spinal cord, where gray matter forms the inner butterfly and white matter lies outside. Pick it if the question asked about a cross-section of the cord.","This swaps the makeup of the two tissues. Myelin is what makes white matter white, so myelinated axons cannot be gray matter."],"lt":"t-cns-brain-spinal-cord","lid":"c42","comp":151,"competencyId":"w7-brain-organization"},{"id":"L02885","dok":2,"q":"Moving down the central axis of the brain from the cerebrum to the spinal cord, which sequence of regions is correct?","a":"Cerebrum, diencephalon, brainstem, spinal cord.","options":["Cerebrum, brainstem, diencephalon, spinal cord","Cerebrum, diencephalon, brainstem, spinal cord","Cerebrum, cerebellum, brainstem, spinal cord","Cerebellum, diencephalon, brainstem, spinal cord"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The cerebrum sits on top, and the diencephalon lies deep beneath it in the center of the brain. Below the diencephalon, the brainstem runs downward as the midbrain, pons, and medulla, and the medulla continues as the spinal cord. The cerebellum is off this central axis, attached to the back of the brainstem.","notes":["This places the brainstem above the diencephalon. The diencephalon connects the cerebrum to the brainstem, so it comes before the brainstem when moving down.","Correct. Cerebrum, diencephalon, brainstem, then spinal cord.","The cerebellum is attached to the back of the brainstem, not stacked between the cerebrum and brainstem. A student who orders the regions by how they look from the side, rather than along the central axis, picks this.","This starts at the cerebellum, which sits behind the brainstem rather than on top of the central axis. Pick the cerebellum if the question asked which region lies behind the brainstem."],"lt":"t-cns-brain-spinal-cord","lid":"c46","comp":151,"competencyId":"w7-brain-organization"},{"id":"L02889","dok":2,"q":"Moving inward from the surface of a cerebral hemisphere, which sequence of tissues is correct?","a":"Cerebral cortex, cerebral white matter, basal nuclei.","options":["Cerebral cortex, cerebral white matter, basal nuclei","Basal nuclei, cerebral white matter, cerebral cortex","Cerebral cortex, basal nuclei, cerebral white matter","Cerebral white matter, cerebral cortex, basal nuclei"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The cerebral cortex is the thin layer of gray matter on the surface of the cerebrum. Beneath it lies the cerebral white matter, the myelinated axons that connect cortical areas with each other and with lower regions. The basal nuclei are masses of gray matter buried deep within that white matter, so they are the deepest of the three.","notes":["Correct. Cortex, then white matter, then the basal nuclei.","This is the correct sequence read from deep to superficial. Pick it only if the question asked for the order moving outward from the center.","The basal nuclei are buried within the white matter, not sandwiched between the cortex and the white matter. Pick this only if the basal nuclei lay directly under the cortex, which they do not.","White matter is not on the surface of the cerebrum; it lies beneath the cortex. This order matches the spinal cord, where white matter is outside, which is a common mix-up."],"lt":"t-cns-brain-spinal-cord","lid":"c50","comp":151,"competencyId":"w7-brain-organization"},{"id":"N-m5-cerebrum-1","dok":1,"q":"Which groove separates the frontal lobe from the parietal lobe?","a":"The central sulcus.","options":["The central sulcus","The lateral sulcus","The parieto-occipital sulcus","The longitudinal fissure"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The central sulcus runs across the top of each hemisphere and separates the frontal lobe in front from the parietal lobe behind. The precentral gyrus, with the primary motor cortex, lies just in front of it, and the postcentral gyrus, with the primary somatosensory cortex, lies just behind it. That makes it a key landmark between movement and sensation areas.","notes":["Correct. The central sulcus separates the frontal and parietal lobes.","The lateral sulcus separates the temporal lobe from the frontal and parietal lobes, and the insula lies hidden within it. Pick it if the question asked what sets off the temporal lobe.","The parieto-occipital sulcus separates the parietal lobe from the occipital lobe. It would be the answer if the question asked about the boundary in front of the occipital lobe.","The longitudinal fissure separates the right and left cerebral hemispheres, not two lobes of the same hemisphere. Pick it if the question asked what divides the cerebrum into halves."],"comp":153,"competencyId":"w7-cerebrum-surface"},{"id":"L03135","dok":1,"q":"Four lobes of each cerebral hemisphere are named for the cranial bones above them. Which fifth lobe lies hidden deep within the lateral sulcus?","a":"The insula.","options":["The cingulate gyrus","The insula","The hippocampus","The temporal lobe"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The frontal, parietal, temporal, and occipital lobes each take their name from the skull bone that covers them. The insula is the fifth lobe, and it is folded out of sight deep in the lateral sulcus, so you only see it when the temporal lobe is pulled away. The insula handles taste, visceral sensation, and emotion.","notes":["The cingulate gyrus is a limbic gyrus that arches above the corpus callosum on the medial surface of each hemisphere. It would be the answer for a question about the limbic gyrus involved in expressing emotion.","Correct. The insula is the hidden fifth lobe, buried deep in the lateral sulcus.","The hippocampus is a limbic structure tucked into the medial temporal lobe, not a lobe of its own. Pick it if the question asks which structure is essential for forming new long-term memories.","The temporal lobe forms the lower border of the lateral sulcus, but it is one of the four bone-named lobes and sits on the surface. Choose it for a question about hearing, language comprehension, or memory."],"lt":"t-cns-brain","lid":"c3","comp":153,"competencyId":"w7-cerebrum-surface"},{"id":"L03136","dok":1,"q":"Which cerebral lobe contains the primary visual cortex?","a":"The occipital lobe.","options":["The temporal lobe","The frontal lobe","The parietal lobe","The occipital lobe"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The occipital lobe forms the back of each cerebral hemisphere, and its primary visual cortex sits along the calcarine sulcus. This area receives the raw visual signals relayed from the eyes, and the visual association area around it turns those signals into recognized faces and objects. Damage here causes loss of vision even when the eyes themselves are healthy.","notes":["The temporal lobe on the lower side of the cerebrum handles hearing, language comprehension, and memory. It would be the answer for the primary auditory cortex.","The frontal lobe at the front of the cerebrum handles voluntary movement, speech production at Broca's area, judgment, and personality. Pick it for a question about the primary motor cortex or the prefrontal cortex.","The parietal lobe sits behind the central sulcus and receives touch, pressure, and body position at the postcentral gyrus. Choose it for a question about the primary somatosensory cortex.","Correct. The primary visual cortex lies in the occipital lobe at the back of the cerebrum."],"lt":"t-cns-brain","lid":"c4","comp":154,"competencyId":"w7-cortex-functional-areas"},{"id":"L03137","dok":1,"q":"The primary motor cortex lies along the precentral gyrus. Which cerebral lobe is this gyrus part of?","a":"The frontal lobe.","options":["The frontal lobe","The occipital lobe","The temporal lobe","The parietal lobe"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The precentral gyrus is the ridge just in front of the central sulcus, and everything in front of that sulcus belongs to the frontal lobe. The primary motor cortex on this gyrus sends the commands that start voluntary movement. The name helps you place it, since pre means before, so the precentral gyrus sits before (anterior to) the central sulcus.","notes":["Correct. The precentral gyrus is the most posterior ridge of the frontal lobe and holds the primary motor cortex.","The occipital lobe at the back of the cerebrum processes vision. It would be the answer for a question about the primary visual cortex.","The temporal lobe lies below the lateral sulcus and handles hearing, language comprehension, and memory. Choose it for a question about the primary auditory cortex.","The parietal lobe begins just behind the central sulcus, and its first ridge is the postcentral gyrus. Pick it if the question asks where the primary somatosensory cortex lies."],"lt":"t-cns-brain","lid":"c5","comp":154,"competencyId":"w7-cortex-functional-areas"},{"id":"L03138","dok":1,"q":"What are the raised ridges of the folded cerebral cortex called?","a":"Gyri.","options":["Sulci","Folia","Gyri","Fissures"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The cerebral cortex is folded so that more of it fits inside the skull. Each raised ridge of the fold is a gyrus (plural gyri), and the grooves between them are sulci. Named gyri, such as the precentral and postcentral gyri, mark the locations of specific functional areas.","notes":["Sulci are the shallow grooves that run between the gyri, the low points of the folds rather than the ridges. Pick sulci if the question asks for the grooves, as in the central sulcus.","Folia are the fine, leaf-like folds on the surface of the cerebellum, not the cerebrum. Choose folia if the question asks about the surface folds of the cerebellum.","Correct. The raised ridges of the cerebral cortex are gyri.","Fissures are the deepest grooves, which divide major regions of the brain, such as the longitudinal fissure between the hemispheres. They would be the answer for a question about the deepest grooves."],"lt":"t-cns-brain","lid":"c6","comp":153,"competencyId":"w7-cerebrum-surface"},{"id":"L03139","dok":1,"q":"Which deep groove separates the left and right cerebral hemispheres?","a":"The longitudinal fissure.","options":["The transverse fissure","The longitudinal fissure","The central sulcus","The lateral sulcus"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The longitudinal fissure runs front to back along the midline and splits the cerebrum into right and left hemispheres. A fold of dura called the falx cerebri dips down into it. At the bottom of the fissure, the corpus callosum bridges the two hemispheres so they can still share information.","notes":["The transverse fissure is the horizontal groove that separates the cerebrum above from the cerebellum below. It would be the answer for a question about the boundary between those two regions.","Correct. The longitudinal fissure divides the cerebrum into left and right hemispheres.","The central sulcus runs across the top of each hemisphere and separates the frontal lobe from the parietal lobe. Pick it if the question asks what divides the primary motor cortex from the primary somatosensory cortex.","The lateral sulcus is the deep groove on the side of each hemisphere that separates the temporal lobe from the frontal and parietal lobes. Choose it for a question about where the insula is hidden."],"lt":"t-cns-brain","lid":"c7","comp":153,"competencyId":"w7-cerebrum-surface"},{"id":"L03140","dok":1,"q":"Which commissural tract is the largest connection between the two cerebral hemispheres?","a":"The corpus callosum.","options":["The corpus callosum","The arcuate fasciculus","The anterior commissure","The internal capsule"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Commissural fibers connect matching areas of the two hemispheres, and the corpus callosum is by far the largest bundle of them. It forms a thick arch of white matter at the bottom of the longitudinal fissure. Through it, each hemisphere can share what it is sensing and doing with the other side.","notes":["Correct. The corpus callosum is the largest commissural tract joining the two hemispheres.","The arcuate fasciculus is an association tract that stays within one hemisphere and links Broca's area to Wernicke's area. Pick it if the question asks which tract connects the two language areas.","The anterior commissure does connect the two hemispheres, but it is a much smaller commissure than the corpus callosum. Choose it only if the question asks for a smaller commissural tract other than the corpus callosum.","The internal capsule is a projection tract that carries signals between the cortex and lower regions such as the brainstem and spinal cord. It would be the answer for the major projection pathway beside the basal ganglia."],"lt":"t-cns-brain","lid":"c8","comp":155,"competencyId":"w7-white-matter-tracts"},{"id":"L03144","dok":1,"q":"Which limbic structure is essential for forming new long-term memories?","a":"The hippocampus","options":["The cingulate gyrus","The amygdala","The pineal gland","The hippocampus"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The hippocampus is a curved limbic structure in the medial temporal lobe. It is needed to turn new experiences into long-term memories. Memories already stored elsewhere in the cortex can still be recalled after hippocampal damage, but new ones are not laid down properly.","notes":["The cingulate gyrus is the limbic gyrus that arches above the corpus callosum and is involved in expressing emotion. It would be the answer for a question about that arching limbic gyrus.","The amygdala is a limbic structure near the hippocampus that handles fear and aggression and links emotion to memory. Pick it if the question asks which structure is tied to fear.","The pineal gland is the endocrine gland of the epithalamus that secretes melatonin, and it is not part of the limbic system. Choose it for a question about melatonin or sleep-wake rhythms.","Correct. The hippocampus is essential for forming new long-term memories."],"lt":"t-cns-brain","lid":"c12","comp":159,"competencyId":"w7-limbic-system"},{"id":"L03146","dok":1,"q":"From which embryonic structure does the entire central nervous system develop?","a":"The neural tube.","options":["The notochord","The somites","The neural tube","The neural crest"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Early in development, a strip of ectoderm along the back rolls up into a hollow neural tube. The front end of the tube swells into the brain vesicles that become the adult brain, and the rest becomes the spinal cord. The hollow center of the tube stays as the ventricles and the central canal.","notes":["The notochord is a rod of tissue beneath the neural tube that signals it to form but does not become nervous tissue. Its remnant survives as the nucleus pulposus of the intervertebral discs, so pick it for that question.","Somites are blocks of tissue beside the neural tube that form the vertebrae and skeletal muscles of the trunk. They would be the answer for a question about where the vertebrae come from.","Correct. The brain and spinal cord both develop from the neural tube.","The neural crest is a band of cells that breaks away from the edges of the neural tube and forms much of the peripheral nervous system, such as sensory ganglia. Choose it for a question about where PNS ganglia come from."],"lt":"t-cns-brain","lid":"c14","comp":152},{"id":"L03147","dok":1,"q":"Which limbic structure handles fear and aggression?","a":"The amygdala.","options":["The amygdala","The pineal gland","The hippocampus","The cingulate gyrus"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The amygdala is an almond-shaped limbic structure in the medial temporal lobe. It processes fear and aggression and links emotional responses to memories, which is why a frightening event can be remembered so vividly. It works alongside the hippocampus as part of the limbic system.","notes":["Correct. The amygdala is the limbic structure for fear and aggression.","The pineal gland is the endocrine gland of the epithalamus and secretes melatonin. It is not part of the limbic system, so choose it for a question about melatonin or sleep cycles.","The hippocampus sits right next to the amygdala in the medial temporal lobe, but its job is forming new long-term memories. Pick it if the question asks about memory formation.","The cingulate gyrus is the limbic gyrus arching above the corpus callosum, involved in expressing emotion. It would be the answer for a question about that arching gyrus."],"lt":"t-cns-brain","lid":"c15","comp":159,"competencyId":"w7-limbic-system"},{"id":"L03148","dok":2,"q":"A stroke destroys the postcentral gyrus on one side, and the patient can no longer feel touch on the opposite side of the body. Which lobe was struck?","a":"The parietal lobe.","options":["The temporal lobe","The frontal lobe","The parietal lobe","The occipital lobe"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The postcentral gyrus is the first ridge behind the central sulcus, and it belongs to the parietal lobe. It holds the primary somatosensory cortex, which receives conscious touch, pressure, and body position. Because the sensory pathways cross to the opposite side, damage on one side causes loss of feeling on the other side of the body.","notes":["The temporal lobe below the lateral sulcus handles hearing, language comprehension, and memory. Choose it if the stroke affected hearing or understanding of speech.","The frontal lobe lies in front of the central sulcus and holds the precentral gyrus with the primary motor cortex. Pick it if the stroke caused weakness of voluntary movement rather than loss of touch.","Correct. The postcentral gyrus and its primary somatosensory cortex are in the parietal lobe.","The occipital lobe at the back of the cerebrum holds the primary visual cortex. It would be the answer if the stroke caused loss of vision."],"lt":"t-cns-brain","lid":"c16","comp":154,"competencyId":"w7-cortex-functional-areas"},{"id":"L03149","dok":2,"q":"Damage to Broca's area leaves comprehension intact but blocks fluent speech. In which lobe is Broca's area?","a":"The frontal lobe.","options":["The temporal lobe","The occipital lobe","The frontal lobe","The parietal lobe"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Broca's area sits in the inferior frontal gyrus, usually on the left side. It controls the motor production of speech, planning the movements of the lips, tongue, and larynx needed to form words. That is why a person with damage here understands language but struggles to speak fluently.","notes":["The temporal lobe holds the primary auditory cortex and much of Wernicke's area. Pick it if the damage left speech fluent but blocked understanding of language.","The occipital lobe processes vision. Choose it for a question about loss of sight, not speech.","Correct. Broca's area lies in the inferior frontal gyrus of the frontal lobe.","The parietal lobe holds the primary somatosensory cortex and borders Wernicke's area where it meets the temporal lobe. Damage there would cause loss of touch on the opposite side, not a speech production problem."],"lt":"t-cns-brain","lid":"c17","comp":154,"competencyId":"w7-cortex-functional-areas"},{"id":"L03150","dok":2,"q":"Which class of cerebral white matter fibers connects matching areas of the two cerebral hemispheres?","a":"Commissural fibers.","options":["Commissural fibers","Association fibers","Reticular fibers","Projection fibers"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Cerebral white matter is sorted into three classes by where each tract starts and ends. Commissural fibers cross the midline and join matching gyri of the two hemispheres; the corpus callosum is the largest example. Association fibers stay within one hemisphere, and projection fibers run between the cortex and lower regions.","notes":["Correct. Commissural fibers, such as the corpus callosum, connect the two hemispheres.","Association fibers link gyri within the same hemisphere, such as the arcuate fasciculus joining Broca's and Wernicke's areas. Pick them if the question asks about connections on one side only.","Reticular fibers are thin collagen fibers of connective tissue that form supportive networks in organs like the spleen and lymph nodes. They are not a class of white matter tract, so choose them only for a connective tissue question.","Projection fibers connect the cortex with lower brain regions and the spinal cord, as in the internal capsule. They would be the answer for tracts carrying signals up from or down to the body."],"lt":"t-cns-brain","lid":"c18","comp":155,"competencyId":"w7-white-matter-tracts"},{"id":"L03151","dok":2,"q":"The cortex is mapped into motor, sensory, and association areas. Which description fits Wernicke's area?","a":"An association area that interprets language.","options":["A limbic area that sets emotional tone","An association area that interprets language","A primary sensory area that receives raw sound","A motor area that produces speech"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Association areas take input that sensory areas have already received and interpret it using memory and reasoning. Wernicke's area, at the junction of the temporal and parietal lobes and usually on the left, makes sense of spoken and written words. It does not receive raw sound itself or move any muscles.","notes":["The limbic system, including the amygdala and cingulate gyrus, sets emotional states. Choose this for a question about emotion, not language comprehension.","Correct. Wernicke's area is an association area for comprehension of spoken and written language.","This describes the primary auditory cortex in the superior temporal gyrus, which receives sound signals before they are interpreted. Pick it if the question asks which area first receives auditory input.","This describes Broca's area in the inferior frontal gyrus, which controls the motor production of speech. It would be the answer for a question about fluent speech output."],"lt":"t-cns-brain","lid":"c19","comp":154,"competencyId":"w7-cortex-functional-areas"},{"id":"L03152","dok":2,"q":"The secondary brain vesicle called the telencephalon matures into which adult structure?","a":"The cerebrum.","options":["The midbrain","The thalamus","The medulla oblongata","The cerebrum"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The five secondary brain vesicles line up from front to back in the same order as the adult brain. The telencephalon, the most anterior vesicle, becomes the cerebrum and the lateral ventricles inside it. The diencephalon, mesencephalon, metencephalon, and myelencephalon form the regions behind it.","notes":["The midbrain develops from the mesencephalon, the vesicle that stays undivided. Pick it if the question names the mesencephalon.","The thalamus develops from the diencephalon vesicle, along with the hypothalamus and epithalamus. Choose it if the question names the diencephalon.","The medulla oblongata develops from the myelencephalon, the most posterior vesicle. It would be the answer for a question about the myelencephalon.","Correct. The telencephalon becomes the cerebrum and the lateral ventricles."],"lt":"t-cns-brain","lid":"c20","comp":152},{"id":"L03155","dok":1,"q":"Which core component of the basal ganglia is the long, curved nucleus?","a":"The caudate nucleus.","options":["The putamen","The caudate nucleus","The subthalamic nucleus","The globus pallidus"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The caudate nucleus is a long, C-shaped nucleus that curves along the wall of the lateral ventricle, with a large head in front and a thin tail trailing behind. Its name comes from the Latin for tail. It is one of the core basal ganglia that help start and regulate movement.","notes":["The putamen is a core basal ganglia nucleus that lies lateral to the globus pallidus. Pick it if the question asks which nucleus sits on the outer side of the globus pallidus.","Correct. The caudate nucleus is the long, curved basal ganglia nucleus.","The subthalamic nucleus is a small diencephalic nucleus grouped with the basal ganglia by function, not a long curved nucleus. Choose it if the question asks which diencephalic nucleus works with the basal ganglia.","The globus pallidus is a core basal ganglia nucleus that lies medial to the putamen. It would be the answer for the nucleus on the inner side of the putamen."],"lt":"t-cns-brain","lid":"c23","comp":156,"competencyId":"w7-basal-ganglia"},{"id":"L03156","dok":2,"q":"Which sulcus separates the primary motor cortex in front from the primary somatosensory cortex behind?","a":"The central sulcus.","options":["The parieto-occipital sulcus","The lateral sulcus","The central sulcus","The calcarine sulcus"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The central sulcus runs across the top of each hemisphere and marks the border between the frontal and parietal lobes. The precentral gyrus just in front of it holds the primary motor cortex, and the postcentral gyrus just behind it holds the primary somatosensory cortex. Finding this sulcus is the quickest way to place both strips.","notes":["The parieto-occipital sulcus separates the parietal lobe from the occipital lobe on the medial surface. It would be the answer for the boundary in front of the occipital lobe.","The lateral sulcus separates the temporal lobe from the frontal and parietal lobes and hides the insula. Pick it if the question asks which groove borders the temporal lobe.","Correct. The central sulcus divides the primary motor cortex from the primary somatosensory cortex.","The calcarine sulcus lies in the occipital lobe, and the primary visual cortex runs along it. Choose it for a question about where the visual cortex sits."],"lt":"t-cns-brain","lid":"c24","comp":153,"competencyId":"w7-cerebrum-surface"},{"id":"L03158","dok":3,"q":"A patient suddenly cannot understand spoken or written language but still speaks fluently, although the words make little sense. Damage to which area best explains this?","a":"Wernicke's area.","options":["Wernicke's area","Broca's area","The primary auditory cortex","The primary motor cortex"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Wernicke's area, at the junction of the temporal and parietal lobes and usually on the left, interprets the meaning of both spoken and written words. When it is damaged, a person can still produce fluent speech but cannot understand language, so what they say often makes little sense. Because written words are affected too, the problem is with interpreting language rather than with hearing.","notes":["Correct. Wernicke's area handles comprehension of spoken and written language.","Broca's area in the inferior frontal gyrus controls the motor production of speech. Damage there blocks fluent speech while comprehension stays intact, which is the opposite of this patient.","The primary auditory cortex in the superior temporal gyrus receives sound. Its loss would affect hearing, but it would not explain trouble understanding written words. Choose it for a question about where sound is first received.","The primary motor cortex starts voluntary movement on the opposite side of the body. Pick it for a stroke that causes one-sided weakness."],"lt":"t-cns-brain","lid":"c26","comp":154,"competencyId":"w7-cortex-functional-areas"},{"id":"L03159","dok":3,"q":"A small stroke in one narrow band of white matter weakens an entire side of the body. Which structure funnels nearly all traffic between the cortex and the body into that band?","a":"The internal capsule.","options":["The internal capsule","The anterior commissure","The corpus callosum","The arcuate fasciculus"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The internal capsule is the major projection pathway, a compact band of white matter beside the basal ganglia. Nearly all the fibers running between the cortex and the brainstem and spinal cord squeeze through it. Because so much wiring is packed into so little space, a tiny lesion there can weaken a whole side of the body.","notes":["Correct. The internal capsule carries nearly all the projection fibers between the cortex and the body.","The anterior commissure is a small commissural tract joining the two hemispheres. Choose it only for a question about a commissure other than the corpus callosum.","The corpus callosum is commissural white matter that connects the two hemispheres to each other, not the cortex to the body. Pick it if the question asks about communication between the left and right cerebrum.","The arcuate fasciculus is an association tract within one hemisphere linking Broca's and Wernicke's areas. It would be the answer for a language problem caused by disconnecting those two areas."],"lt":"t-cns-brain","lid":"c27","comp":155,"competencyId":"w7-white-matter-tracts"},{"id":"L03161","dok":3,"q":"After a brain injury, a patient remembers childhood events clearly but cannot form any new long-term memories. Which limbic structure was most likely damaged?","a":"The hippocampus.","options":["The cingulate gyrus","The hippocampus","The pineal gland","The amygdala"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Old memories are stored across the cerebral cortex, but forming new long-term memories depends on the hippocampus in the medial temporal lobe. Damage to the hippocampus therefore spares the past while blocking new learning. This pattern of keeping old memories but losing new ones points straight to the hippocampus.","notes":["The cingulate gyrus arches above the corpus callosum and is involved in expressing emotion. It would be the answer for a question about the limbic gyrus that wraps the corpus callosum.","Correct. The hippocampus is needed to form new long-term memories.","The pineal gland is the endocrine gland of the epithalamus and secretes melatonin. It is not a limbic memory structure, so choose it for a question about sleep and wake rhythms.","The amygdala handles fear and aggression and links emotion to memory. Damage there blunts fear responses, so pick it if the patient no longer showed normal fear."],"lt":"t-cns-brain","lid":"c29","comp":159,"competencyId":"w7-limbic-system"},{"id":"L03163","dok":3,"q":"The cerebral cortex is folded into gyri and sulci instead of being smooth. What is the main advantage of this folding?","a":"It packs more cortical surface area, and more neurons, into the limited space of the skull.","options":["It fits more cortex, and so more neurons, inside the skull","It lets the two hemispheres exchange signals across the midline","It creates the spaces where cerebrospinal fluid is produced","It thickens the white matter so signals travel faster between lobes"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The cortex is a thin sheet of gray matter only 2 to 4 mm thick, and the amount of processing it can do depends on its surface area. The skull is a fixed size, so folding the sheet into ridges and grooves fits far more of it inside, much like crumpling a sheet of paper to fit a small box. More cortex means more neuron cell bodies for higher functions.","notes":["Correct. Folding increases cortical surface area so more neurons fit inside the skull.","Communication between the hemispheres runs through commissural fibers such as the corpus callosum. That would be the answer for a question about how the two halves share information.","Cerebrospinal fluid is made by the choroid plexus inside the ventricles, not in the grooves of the cortex. Choose the choroid plexus for a question about where CSF is produced.","Folding changes the surface of the cortex, not the thickness of the white matter beneath it. Speed of signals depends on myelinated axons, so pick this idea only for a question about why white matter conducts quickly."],"lt":"t-cns-brain","lid":"d3-t-cns-brain-1","comp":153,"competencyId":"w7-cerebrum-surface"},{"id":"L03165","dok":3,"q":"Because cortical functions are localized, a clinician can predict deficits from the site of damage. A small stroke destroys part of the left precentral gyrus. Which problem is most likely?","a":"Weakness of voluntary movement on the right side of the body.","options":["Weakness of voluntary movement on the left side","Loss of touch and pressure sensation on the right side","Loss of vision in both eyes","Weakness of voluntary movement on the right side"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The precentral gyrus of the frontal lobe holds the primary motor cortex, which starts voluntary movement. Most motor fibers from it cross to the opposite side at the decussation of the pyramids in the medulla, so the left motor cortex controls the right side of the body. Damage on the left therefore weakens movement on the right.","notes":["This answer forgets that the motor pathways cross in the medulla. Left-sided weakness would follow a stroke in the right precentral gyrus.","Loss of touch on the right would come from damage to the left postcentral gyrus, the primary somatosensory cortex in the parietal lobe. Pick this if the stroke hit the ridge just behind the central sulcus.","Vision is processed in the occipital lobe at the back of the cerebrum. Choose this for damage to the primary visual cortex, not the precentral gyrus.","Correct. The left primary motor cortex controls voluntary movement on the right side of the body."],"lt":"t-cns-brain","lid":"d3-t-cns-brain-3","comp":154,"competencyId":"w7-cortex-functional-areas"},{"id":"L03168","dok":3,"q":"In rare surgeries, the corpus callosum is cut to stop severe seizures from spreading. What connection does this surgery remove?","a":"The main route for signals between the left and right hemispheres.","options":["The main route for motor commands from cortex to spinal cord","The main link between the cerebrum and the cerebellum","The main route for signals between the two hemispheres","The links between gyri within the same hemisphere"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The corpus callosum is the largest commissural tract, a thick arch of white matter that joins matching areas of the two hemispheres. Cutting it means the two halves can no longer readily share information, which is why it can stop seizure activity from spreading from one side to the other. The rest of the wiring inside each hemisphere and down to the body stays intact.","notes":["Motor commands from the cortex to the spinal cord travel in projection fibers through the internal capsule. Pick this for a question about the internal capsule.","The cerebrum and cerebellum communicate through the pontine nuclei and the cerebellar peduncles. Choose this for a question about the middle cerebellar peduncle.","Correct. The corpus callosum is the main commissural route between the two hemispheres.","Connections within one hemisphere are association fibers, such as the arcuate fasciculus. They would be the answer for a question about linking gyri on the same side."],"lt":"t-cns-brain","lid":"d3-t-cns-brain-6","comp":155,"competencyId":"w7-white-matter-tracts"},{"id":"L03170","dok":2,"q":"In most people, Broca's area and Wernicke's area are concentrated on one side of the cerebrum. Which side is it?","a":"The left hemisphere","options":["Both hemispheres equally","The cerebellar hemispheres","The right hemisphere","The left hemisphere"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Language is a lateralized function, meaning it is concentrated in one hemisphere. In most people, Broca's area in the inferior frontal gyrus and Wernicke's area at the temporal and parietal junction both sit in the left hemisphere. A bundle of association fibers in that same hemisphere, the arcuate fasciculus, links the two areas.","notes":["Language is not split evenly between the two sides. An equal split is a common misconception, since the language areas are usually concentrated on the left.","The cerebellar hemispheres coordinate movement, posture, and balance and do not hold Broca's or Wernicke's area. Choose them for a question about coordination.","The right hemisphere holds the language areas in only a minority of people. This answer reverses the usual pattern, so pick it only if a question describes one of those exceptions.","Correct. In most people the language areas sit in the left hemisphere."],"lt":"t-cns-brain","lid":"d3x-t-cns-brain-1","comp":154,"competencyId":"w7-cortex-functional-areas"},{"id":"L03171","dok":1,"q":"Which deep masses of gray matter, including the putamen and globus pallidus, help start and regulate voluntary movement?","a":"The basal nuclei (basal ganglia).","options":["The basal nuclei","The cranial nerve nuclei","The thalamic nuclei","The deep cerebellar nuclei"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The basal nuclei, also called the basal ganglia or cerebral nuclei, are gray matter masses buried deep in the cerebral white matter. Their core parts are the caudate nucleus, putamen, and globus pallidus. They help start movement, suppress unwanted movement, and adjust muscle tone, as in the automatic arm swing of walking.","notes":["Correct. The putamen and globus pallidus are core parts of the basal nuclei.","Cranial nerve nuclei are clusters of cell bodies, mostly in the brainstem, where cranial nerves start or end. They are not in the cerebrum, so they would be the answer for a question about where CN III through X and XII have their nuclei.","The thalamic nuclei are in the diencephalon and relay sensory and motor signals to the cortex. Pick them for a question about the main relay station to the cortex.","The deep cerebellar nuclei, such as the dentate nucleus, are buried in the white matter of the cerebellum, not the cerebrum. Choose them for a question about the gray matter nuclei inside the cerebellum."],"lt":"t-cns-brain","lid":"w7g-br-1","comp":156,"competencyId":"w7-basal-ganglia"},{"id":"L03172","dok":1,"q":"The putamen and the globus pallidus together form which lens-shaped structure of the basal nuclei?","a":"The lentiform nucleus.","options":["The caudate nucleus","The subthalamic nucleus","The lentiform nucleus","The red nucleus"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Lentiform means lens-shaped. The putamen lies on the lateral side and the globus pallidus on the medial side, and together they form this lens-shaped mass lateral to the internal capsule. The caudate nucleus is the separate, long curved nucleus of the basal nuclei.","notes":["The caudate nucleus is the long, C-shaped basal nucleus that curves along the lateral ventricle. Pick it if the question asks for the curved nucleus with a head and tail.","The subthalamic nucleus is a small diencephalic nucleus grouped with the basal nuclei by function. Choose it if the question asks for the diencephalic nucleus that works with the basal nuclei.","Correct. The putamen and globus pallidus together make up the lentiform nucleus.","The red nucleus is a motor nucleus in the midbrain, separate from the basal nuclei. It would be the answer for a question about motor nuclei of the midbrain other than the substantia nigra."],"lt":"t-cns-brain","lid":"w7g-br-2","comp":156,"competencyId":"w7-basal-ganglia"},{"id":"L03173","dok":2,"q":"Parkinson disease, with tremor, rigidity, and slow movement, results from loss in the basal nuclei circuit, including the substantia nigra. What does this reveal about the normal job of the basal nuclei?","a":"They help start and smooth voluntary movement and set muscle tone.","options":["Receiving and processing raw visual signals","Setting the basic rhythm of breathing","Producing cerebrospinal fluid for the ventricles","Starting and smoothing voluntary movement"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The signs of Parkinson disease are trouble starting movement, unwanted tremor, and stiff muscles. Each sign is the reverse of a normal basal nuclei job: initiating movement, suppressing unwanted movement, and adjusting muscle tone. The substantia nigra of the midbrain works with the basal nuclei, so its loss disrupts the whole circuit.","notes":["Raw visual signals are received by the primary visual cortex in the occipital lobe. Choose this for a question about loss of vision after occipital damage.","The basic rhythm of breathing is set by the respiratory center of the medulla oblongata. It would be the answer for a question about that medullary center.","Cerebrospinal fluid is produced by the choroid plexus in the ventricles. Loss of CSF production would not cause tremor, so pick this for a question about the choroid plexus.","Correct. The basal nuclei normally help start and smooth movement and set muscle tone."],"lt":"t-cns-brain","lid":"w7g-br-3","comp":156,"competencyId":"w7-basal-ganglia"},{"id":"L03174","dok":1,"q":"Which cortical area, just anterior to the primary motor cortex, plans learned, patterned movements?","a":"The premotor cortex.","options":["The primary somatosensory cortex","The primary motor cortex","The prefrontal cortex","The premotor cortex"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The premotor cortex sits in the frontal lobe just in front of the precentral gyrus. It plans learned, patterned movements, such as typing or playing an instrument, and then passes the plan to the primary motor cortex, which sends the actual command. Its name places it, since it lies before the primary motor cortex.","notes":["The primary somatosensory cortex lies on the postcentral gyrus of the parietal lobe and receives touch and body position. Choose it for a question about conscious touch.","The primary motor cortex on the precentral gyrus sends the command that starts voluntary movement. Pick it if the question asks which area actually initiates the movement.","The prefrontal cortex forms the very front of the frontal lobe and handles judgment, planning, and personality. It would be the answer for a question about executive function.","Correct. The premotor cortex lies anterior to the primary motor cortex and plans patterned movements."],"lt":"t-cns-brain","lid":"w7g-br-4","comp":154,"competencyId":"w7-cortex-functional-areas"},{"id":"L03175","dok":1,"q":"Which area on the medial surface of the frontal lobe coordinates complex movements that involve both sides of the body?","a":"The supplementary motor area.","options":["Broca's area","The supplementary motor area","The frontal eye field","The primary gustatory cortex"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The supplementary motor area lies on the medial surface of the frontal lobe, facing the longitudinal fissure. It coordinates complex movements that use both sides of the body together, such as using two hands at once. It is one of several frontal motor areas that work ahead of the primary motor cortex.","notes":["Broca's area lies in the inferior frontal gyrus, usually on the left, and controls the motor production of speech. Choose it for a question about fluent speech.","Correct. The supplementary motor area on the medial frontal lobe coordinates complex movements on both sides.","The frontal eye field sits in the middle frontal gyrus and controls voluntary eye movement. Pick it for a question about moving the eyes on purpose.","The primary gustatory cortex lies in the insula and the inferior postcentral gyrus and receives taste. It would be the answer for a question about taste."],"lt":"t-cns-brain","lid":"w7g-br-5","comp":154,"competencyId":"w7-cortex-functional-areas"},{"id":"L03176","dok":1,"q":"Which region at the front of the frontal lobe governs judgment, planning, and personality?","a":"The prefrontal cortex.","options":["The prefrontal cortex","The primary motor cortex","The premotor cortex","The visual association area"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The prefrontal cortex forms the most anterior part of the frontal lobe. It handles executive function: judgment, reasoning, planning ahead, and personality. Damage here can leave movement and sensation normal while changing a person's behavior and decision making.","notes":["Correct. The prefrontal cortex at the front of the frontal lobe handles executive function.","The primary motor cortex on the precentral gyrus starts voluntary movement. Pick it for a question about the motor strip just in front of the central sulcus.","The premotor cortex sits just in front of the primary motor cortex and plans learned, patterned movements. It would be the answer for a question about planning skilled movements.","The visual association area surrounds the primary visual cortex in the occipital lobe and recognizes faces and objects. Choose it for a question about recognizing what you see."],"lt":"t-cns-brain","lid":"w7g-br-6","comp":154,"competencyId":"w7-cortex-functional-areas"},{"id":"L03177","dok":1,"q":"The primary gustatory cortex, which receives taste, lies mainly in which region?","a":"The insula.","options":["The insula","The medial temporal lobe at the uncus","The prefrontal cortex","The occipital lobe"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The primary gustatory cortex lies mainly in the insula, the lobe hidden deep in the lateral sulcus, and extends onto the inferior end of the postcentral gyrus. It receives taste signals from the tongue. The insula also handles visceral sensation and emotion.","notes":["Correct. Taste is received mainly in the insula.","The medial temporal lobe at the uncus holds the primary olfactory cortex, which receives smell. Choose it for a question about smell, a sense often confused with taste.","The prefrontal cortex handles judgment, planning, and personality. It would be the answer for a question about executive function, not a sense.","The occipital lobe holds the primary visual cortex at the calcarine sulcus. Pick it for a question about where vision is received."],"lt":"t-cns-brain","lid":"w7g-br-7","comp":154,"competencyId":"w7-cortex-functional-areas"},{"id":"L04498","dok":1,"q":"Which list names the four major parts of the brain?","a":"The cerebrum, diencephalon, brainstem, and cerebellum.","options":["Cerebrum, diencephalon, brainstem, and cerebellum","Forebrain, midbrain, hindbrain, and spinal cord","Cerebrum, thalamus, pons, and spinal cord","Frontal, parietal, temporal, and occipital lobes"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The brain is divided into four major parts. The cerebrum is the large, folded top part that handles conscious thought, sensation, and voluntary movement. The diencephalon sits deep between the cerebrum and the brainstem, the brainstem (midbrain, pons, and medulla) is the stalk that connects to the spinal cord, and the cerebellum sits behind the brainstem and coordinates movement.","notes":["Correct. These four parts make up the whole brain, from the cerebrum on top to the cerebellum at the back.","Forebrain, midbrain, and hindbrain are names for the three primary brain vesicles of the embryo, and the spinal cord is not part of the brain. This list would partly fit a question on brain development, not one on the four parts of the adult brain.","The thalamus is only one part of the diencephalon and the pons is only one part of the brainstem, and the spinal cord is not part of the brain at all. This list mixes levels, naming pieces of the major parts instead of the parts themselves.","These are lobes of a single cerebral hemisphere, so all four belong to the cerebrum alone. Pick this list if the question asked for four of the five lobes of the cerebrum (the fifth is the insula)."],"lt":"t-w7-brain-organization","lid":"w7-brain-organization-r1","comp":151,"competencyId":"w7-brain-organization"},{"id":"L04500","dok":1,"q":"Which description fits the cerebrum?","a":"The largest brain part, the seat of conscious thought, sensation, and voluntary movement.","options":["The largest brain part, handling conscious thought and voluntary movement","The second largest brain part, coordinating posture, balance, and movement","The deep region around the third ventricle that relays sensory signals","The stalk joining the brain to the spinal cord, holding the vital centers"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The cerebrum is the largest part of the brain, made of right and left hemispheres with a folded outer cortex of gray matter. It is the seat of conscious thought, sensation, memory, and voluntary movement. Its lobes take on different jobs, such as movement in the frontal lobe and vision in the occipital lobe.","notes":["Correct. The cerebrum is the largest part of the brain and handles conscious functions.","This describes the cerebellum, the second largest brain part, which sits behind the brainstem below the occipital lobes. Pick it if the question asked which part keeps movements smooth and maintains balance.","This describes the diencephalon, whose thalamus relays sensory signals to the cortex and which surrounds the third ventricle. It would be the answer for the deep region between the cerebrum and the brainstem.","This describes the brainstem (midbrain, pons, and medulla). It would be correct if the question asked which part connects to the spinal cord and contains the heart rate and breathing centers."],"lt":"t-w7-brain-organization","lid":"w7-brain-organization-r3","comp":151,"competencyId":"w7-brain-organization"},{"id":"L04501","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares the cerebrum and the cerebellum?","a":"The cerebrum starts conscious, voluntary movement; the cerebellum coordinates movement, posture, and balance.","options":["The cerebellum starts voluntary movement; the cerebrum coordinates it and keeps balance","The cerebrum starts voluntary movement; the cerebellum coordinates it and keeps balance","Both are mainly sensory relay stations that pass signals on to the brainstem","Both mainly regulate heart rate and breathing, with the cerebellum as the backup"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The cerebrum is the seat of conscious thought, sensation, and voluntary movement, and its primary motor cortex issues the commands for voluntary movement. The cerebellum, behind the brainstem, does not start movements; it smooths and coordinates them and keeps posture and balance. Damage to the cerebellum makes movements clumsy and unsteady rather than impossible.","notes":["This reverses the two roles. Commands for voluntary movement start in the primary motor cortex of the cerebrum, and the cerebellum refines them. The mix-up happens because both are involved in movement.","Correct. The cerebrum issues the commands, and the cerebellum makes the resulting movement smooth and balanced.","Sensory relay to the cortex is the job of the thalamus in the diencephalon. Neither the cerebrum nor the cerebellum is mainly a relay station; this would fit a question about the thalamus.","Heart rate and breathing are set by vital centers in the medulla oblongata of the brainstem, not by the cerebrum or cerebellum. Pick the medulla if a question asks where these vital centers sit."],"lt":"t-w7-brain-organization","lid":"w7-brain-organization-r4","comp":151,"competencyId":"w7-brain-organization"},{"id":"L04502","dok":2,"q":"The cerebral cortex is a thin layer of gray matter on the surface of the brain. Why is that surface folded into gyri and sulci?","a":"Folding packs more cortex into the limited space of the skull.","options":["Folding anchors the brain to the dura so that it does not shift","Folding packs more cortex into the limited space of the skull","Folding separates the gray matter from the white matter beneath","Folding creates channels that carry CSF into the ventricles"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The cortex is where neuron cell bodies sit, so more cortex means more neurons for processing. The skull has a fixed size, and folding the cortex into ridges (gyri) and grooves (sulci) greatly increases its surface area without needing a bigger skull. Much of the cortex is actually hidden inside the sulci.","notes":["The brain is held in place by the dural reflections, such as the falx cerebri, and cushioned by CSF, not by its folds. Pick the dural reflections if a question asks what keeps the brain from shifting in the skull.","Correct. Folding increases the surface area of the cortex within a skull of fixed size.","Gray and white matter are separated simply by where cell bodies and myelinated axons sit, cortex outside and white matter inside. Folding does not create that boundary; even a smooth brain would have both layers.","CSF is made inside the ventricles by the choroid plexus and flows out to the subarachnoid space, not in through the sulci. This idea confuses the grooves of the surface with the ventricles, which are internal chambers."],"lt":"t-w7-brain-organization","lid":"w7-brain-organization-r5","comp":153,"competencyId":"w7-cerebrum-surface"},{"id":"L04503","dok":1,"q":"Which list names the five lobes of a cerebral hemisphere?","a":"Frontal, parietal, temporal, occipital, and insula.","options":["Frontal, parietal, temporal, occipital, and insula","Frontal, parietal, sphenoid, occipital, and insula","Frontal, parietal, temporal, occipital, and cerebellar","Frontal, parietal, temporal, occipital, and thalamus"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Each cerebral hemisphere has five lobes. Four of them, the frontal, parietal, temporal, and occipital lobes, are named for the cranial bones that lie over them. The fifth, the insula, is hidden deep within the lateral sulcus and can only be seen by pulling the temporal lobe away.","notes":["Correct. Four lobes are named for the overlying bones, and the insula lies hidden in the lateral sulcus.","The sphenoid is a cranial bone at the base of the skull, and no cerebral lobe is named after it. The mix-up comes from knowing that four lobes take their names from the bones above them.","The cerebellum is a separate brain part behind the brainstem, not a lobe of the cerebrum. It has its own lobes (anterior, posterior, and flocculonodular), which would be the answer to a question about cerebellar lobes.","The thalamus is part of the diencephalon, a deep region below the cerebrum, so it is not a cerebral lobe. Pick it if the question asked for the main sensory relay station to the cortex."],"lt":"t-w7-cerebrum-surface","lid":"w7-cerebrum-surface-r1","comp":153,"competencyId":"w7-cerebrum-surface"},{"id":"L04504","dok":1,"q":"What does the central sulcus separate?","a":"The frontal lobe from the parietal lobe.","options":["The right hemisphere from the left hemisphere","The temporal lobe from the frontal and parietal lobes","The frontal lobe from the parietal lobe","The cerebrum from the cerebellum"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The central sulcus is the groove that runs down the side of each hemisphere, dividing the frontal lobe in front from the parietal lobe behind. The gyrus just in front of it, the precentral gyrus, is the primary motor cortex, and the gyrus just behind it, the postcentral gyrus, is the primary somatosensory cortex. This makes it a key landmark for finding the motor and sensory areas.","notes":["The right and left hemispheres are separated by the longitudinal fissure, the deep midline groove. Pick that fissure if the question asked what divides the two halves of the cerebrum.","The lateral sulcus separates the temporal lobe below from the frontal and parietal lobes above. It would be the answer if the question named the groove along the side of the brain that hides the insula.","Correct. The central sulcus is the boundary between the frontal and parietal lobes.","The cerebrum is separated from the cerebellum by the transverse fissure. Pick that fissure if the question asked which groove lies between the occipital lobes and the cerebellum."],"lt":"t-w7-cerebrum-surface","lid":"w7-cerebrum-surface-r2","comp":153,"competencyId":"w7-cerebrum-surface"},{"id":"L04505","dok":1,"q":"Which structure joins the right and left cerebral hemispheres?","a":"The corpus callosum.","options":["Arbor vitae","Corpus callosum","Falx cerebri","Internal capsule"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The corpus callosum is a thick band of white matter at the floor of the longitudinal fissure. It is made of commissural fibers, axons that cross from one hemisphere to the matching areas of the other, so it lets the two halves share information. It is the largest commissure in the brain.","notes":["The arbor vitae is the branching white matter inside the cerebellum. Pick it if the question asked about the tree-like pattern seen when the cerebellum is cut in half.","Correct. The corpus callosum is the large band of commissural fibers connecting the hemispheres.","The falx cerebri is a fold of dura mater that dips into the longitudinal fissure and separates the hemispheres rather than joining them. It would be correct for a question about the dural fold between the two cerebral hemispheres.","The internal capsule is a band of projection fibers that connects the cortex with lower brain regions and the spinal cord, running beside the basal nuclei. It would be the answer for the main pathway between the cortex and the body, not between the hemispheres."],"lt":"t-w7-cerebrum-surface","lid":"w7-cerebrum-surface-r3","comp":155,"competencyId":"w7-white-matter-tracts"},{"id":"L04506","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares gyri, sulci, and fissures?","a":"Gyri are raised ridges, sulci are shallow grooves between them, and fissures are the deepest grooves.","options":["Gyri are ridges, fissures are shallow grooves, and sulci are the deepest grooves","Gyri are ridges, sulci are deep grooves, and fissures are cavities holding CSF","Gyri are ridges, sulci are shallow grooves, and fissures are the deepest grooves","Sulci are ridges, gyri are shallow grooves, and fissures are the deepest grooves"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The folded cortex has three kinds of surface features. Gyri (singular gyrus) are the raised ridges, sulci (singular sulcus) are the shallow grooves between them, and fissures are the deepest grooves, which divide major regions. For example, the longitudinal fissure separates the two hemispheres, and the central sulcus separates the frontal and parietal lobes.","notes":["This swaps sulci and fissures by depth. Fissures are the deepest grooves, such as the longitudinal fissure between the hemispheres, while sulci are the shallower grooves between gyri.","Fissures are grooves on the surface, not fluid-filled cavities. The CSF-filled cavities inside the brain are the ventricles, which would be the answer for internal chambers that hold cerebrospinal fluid.","Correct. Ridges, shallow grooves, and deep grooves, in that order.","This swaps gyri and sulci. A gyrus is a raised ridge, such as the precentral gyrus, and a sulcus is a groove, such as the central sulcus. Students often reverse them because both words are new."],"lt":"t-w7-cerebrum-surface","lid":"w7-cerebrum-surface-r4","comp":153,"competencyId":"w7-cerebrum-surface"},{"id":"L04507","dok":3,"q":"After a stroke, a patient's eyes and optic nerves are healthy, but the patient can no longer see. Which lobe was most likely damaged?","a":"The occipital lobe.","options":["Frontal lobe","Temporal lobe","Occipital lobe","Parietal lobe"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The occipital lobe at the back of the cerebrum holds the primary visual cortex, which receives raw visual input, and the visual association area around it, which recognizes faces and objects. If the eyes and optic nerves are intact but the occipital cortex is destroyed, the signals arrive with nowhere to be processed, so vision is lost. Because each lobe has its own job, the deficit points to the location of the injury.","notes":["The frontal lobe controls voluntary movement, speech production at Broca's area, judgment, and personality. It would be the answer for a patient who loses movement or fluent speech, not vision.","The temporal lobe handles hearing, language comprehension at Wernicke's area, and memory. Damage there would be the answer for a patient who loses hearing or can no longer understand spoken words.","Correct. The visual cortex is in the occipital lobe, so damage there takes vision even when the eyes work.","The parietal lobe receives touch, pressure, and body position at the postcentral gyrus and handles spatial orientation. Pick it if the patient lost sensation on one side of the body."],"lt":"t-w7-cerebrum-surface","lid":"w7-cerebrum-surface-r5","comp":154,"competencyId":"w7-cortex-functional-areas"},{"id":"L04508","dok":1,"q":"Where is the primary motor cortex?","a":"The precentral gyrus of the frontal lobe.","options":["Precentral gyrus of the frontal lobe","Superior gyrus of the temporal lobe","Postcentral gyrus of the parietal lobe","Calcarine sulcus of the occipital lobe"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The primary motor cortex lies in the precentral gyrus, the ridge just in front of the central sulcus in the frontal lobe. Its neurons send the commands that start voluntary movement, and their axons descend through the internal capsule and the brainstem to the spinal cord. The word precentral simply means in front of the central sulcus.","notes":["Correct. The precentral gyrus, just in front of the central sulcus, is the primary motor cortex.","The superior temporal gyrus holds the primary auditory cortex, which receives sound input. It would be the answer for where hearing first reaches the cortex.","The postcentral gyrus, just behind the central sulcus, is the primary somatosensory cortex that receives touch, pressure, and body position. Pick it if the question asked where conscious touch is received.","The calcarine sulcus of the occipital lobe is the site of the primary visual cortex. Choose it if the question asked where raw visual input arrives."],"lt":"t-w7-cortex-functional-areas","lid":"w7-cortex-functional-areas-r1","comp":154,"competencyId":"w7-cortex-functional-areas"},{"id":"L04509","dok":1,"q":"Where is the primary somatosensory cortex?","a":"The postcentral gyrus of the parietal lobe.","options":["Precentral gyrus of the frontal lobe","Medial surface of the temporal lobe","Inferior gyrus of the frontal lobe","Postcentral gyrus of the parietal lobe"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The primary somatosensory cortex lies in the postcentral gyrus, the ridge just behind the central sulcus in the parietal lobe. It receives conscious touch, pressure, and proprioception (the sense of body position). The somatosensory association area behind it interprets texture, shape, and spatial orientation.","notes":["The precentral gyrus, just in front of the central sulcus, is the primary motor cortex, which starts voluntary movement. It would be the answer for where movement commands begin, the mirror image of this question.","The medial temporal lobe, at the uncus, holds the primary olfactory cortex, which receives smell. Choose it for a question about where smell input reaches the cortex.","The inferior frontal gyrus, usually on the left, holds Broca's area, which controls the motor production of speech. Pick it if the question asked where speech production is controlled.","Correct. The postcentral gyrus, just behind the central sulcus, receives conscious body sensation."],"lt":"t-w7-cortex-functional-areas","lid":"w7-cortex-functional-areas-r2","comp":154,"competencyId":"w7-cortex-functional-areas"},{"id":"L04510","dok":1,"q":"What does Broca's area do, and where is it?","a":"It controls the motor production of speech, in the inferior frontal gyrus, usually on the left.","options":["Understands language; temporal and parietal junction, usually the left","Produces speech; inferior frontal gyrus, usually the left","Controls voluntary eye movement; middle frontal gyrus","Receives sound input; superior temporal gyrus of each side"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Broca's area sits in the inferior frontal gyrus, usually in the left hemisphere, near the motor areas that control the lips, tongue, and larynx. It controls the motor production of speech, planning the movements needed to say words. Damage there leaves a person able to understand language but struggling to speak fluently.","notes":["This describes Wernicke's area, which lets us comprehend spoken and written language. Pick it if the question asked which area is damaged when a patient speaks fluently but cannot understand words.","Correct. Broca's area in the inferior frontal gyrus controls speech production.","This describes the frontal eye field, which controls voluntary eye movement. It would be the answer for a question about directing the gaze on purpose.","This describes the primary auditory cortex, which receives sound input. Choose it if the question asked where hearing first reaches the cortex."],"lt":"t-w7-cortex-functional-areas","lid":"w7-cortex-functional-areas-r3","comp":154,"competencyId":"w7-cortex-functional-areas"},{"id":"L04511","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares Broca's area and Wernicke's area?","a":"Broca's area produces speech; Wernicke's area comprehends spoken and written language.","options":["Broca's area receives touch; Wernicke's area controls eye movement","Both receive raw sound input from the ears in the temporal lobe","Broca's area produces speech; Wernicke's area comprehends language","Wernicke's area produces speech; Broca's area comprehends language"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Both are language areas, usually in the left hemisphere, but they do different jobs. Broca's area in the inferior frontal gyrus controls the motor production of speech, while Wernicke's area at the junction of the temporal and parietal lobes handles comprehension of spoken and written language. A bundle of association fibers, the arcuate fasciculus, links the two.","notes":["Touch is received in the postcentral gyrus (primary somatosensory cortex), and voluntary eye movement is controlled by the frontal eye field. This pairing has nothing to do with the language areas.","Raw sound input arrives at the primary auditory cortex in the superior temporal gyrus. Neither Broca's nor Wernicke's area is the first stop for sound; pick the primary auditory cortex for that question.","Correct. Broca's area handles speaking, and Wernicke's area handles understanding.","This swaps the two jobs. Broca's area is in the frontal lobe with the other motor areas, so it handles the output (speaking), and Wernicke's area sits near the hearing cortex, so it handles understanding."],"lt":"t-w7-cortex-functional-areas","lid":"w7-cortex-functional-areas-r4","comp":154,"competencyId":"w7-cortex-functional-areas"},{"id":"L04512","dok":3,"q":"A patient understands everything said to her but speaks slowly in broken, effortful phrases. Which area was most likely damaged?","a":"Broca's area.","options":["Prefrontal cortex","Primary auditory cortex","Wernicke's area","Broca's area"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Broca's area, in the inferior frontal gyrus usually on the left, controls the motor production of speech. When it is damaged, comprehension stays intact because Wernicke's area is untouched, but the person struggles to form words and speaks in slow, effortful fragments. This pattern is often called expressive aphasia (aphasia means a language disorder).","notes":["The prefrontal cortex handles judgment, planning, and personality. It would be the answer for a patient whose behavior or decision making changed, not one who understands speech but cannot produce it.","Damage to the primary auditory cortex would interfere with receiving sound in the first place, so the patient would have trouble hearing rather than speaking. Choose it for a patient who cannot perceive sounds.","Damage to Wernicke's area causes the opposite pattern: speech stays fluent but makes little sense, and the person cannot understand what is said. Pick it if the patient spoke easily but could not follow instructions.","Correct. Intact understanding with effortful speech points to Broca's area."],"lt":"t-w7-cortex-functional-areas","lid":"w7-cortex-functional-areas-r5","comp":154,"competencyId":"w7-cortex-functional-areas"},{"id":"L04562","dok":1,"q":"Which list names the three classes of cerebral white matter fibers?","a":"Association, commissural, and projection fibers.","options":["Association, commissural, and projection","Afferent, efferent, and autonomic","Sensory, motor, and mixed","Ascending, descending, and transverse"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Cerebral white matter is sorted by where its fibers begin and end. Association fibers connect gyri within the same hemisphere, commissural fibers cross between the two hemispheres, and projection fibers connect the cortex with lower brain regions and the spinal cord. The arcuate fasciculus, the corpus callosum, and the internal capsule are one example of each.","notes":["Correct. These three classes describe where cerebral white matter fibers run.","Afferent and efferent describe signals traveling toward or away from the CNS, and autonomic is a division of the motor system. These terms classify peripheral pathways, not cerebral white matter.","Sensory, motor, and mixed is how the cranial nerves are classified by the signals they carry. Pick it for a question about classifying CN I through XII.","Ascending and descending describe the direction of tracts, and both kinds are projection fibers. There is no transverse class; this choice uses direction words instead of the three named classes."],"lt":"t-w7-white-matter-tracts","lid":"w7-white-matter-tracts-r1","comp":155,"competencyId":"w7-white-matter-tracts"},{"id":"L04563","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares association fibers and commissural fibers?","a":"Association fibers connect gyri within one hemisphere; commissural fibers connect matching gyri between the hemispheres.","options":["Commissural fibers stay in one hemisphere; association fibers cross between them","Both link the cortex to the cord, but only commissural fibers are myelinated","Association fibers stay in one hemisphere; commissural fibers cross between them","Association fibers link cortex to cord; commissural fibers link cortex to thalamus"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Association fibers connect gyri within the same hemisphere, such as the arcuate fasciculus linking Broca's and Wernicke's areas. Commissural fibers connect matching gyri in the two hemispheres, and the corpus callosum is the largest example. A third class, projection fibers, connects the cortex with lower brain regions and the spinal cord.","notes":["This reverses the two classes. A commissure is a crossing connection, so commissural fibers such as the corpus callosum cross between hemispheres, while association fibers stay on one side.","All three classes of cerebral white matter are myelinated axons, which is why the tissue looks white. This choice also misplaces both classes, since neither runs to the spinal cord.","Correct. Association fibers stay on one side, and commissural fibers cross to the other.","Connections between the cortex and lower regions such as the thalamus and spinal cord are made by projection fibers, like those in the internal capsule. Pick projection fibers for that question."],"lt":"t-w7-white-matter-tracts","lid":"w7-white-matter-tracts-r2","comp":155,"competencyId":"w7-white-matter-tracts"},{"id":"L04564","dok":2,"q":"What is the internal capsule?","a":"The main band of projection fibers between the cortex and lower brain regions and the spinal cord.","options":["A band of association fibers linking the two language areas","A band of commissural fibers joining the two hemispheres","A band of projection fibers between the cortex and lower regions","A fold of dura mater separating the cerebrum from the cerebellum"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The internal capsule is the major band of projection fibers, carrying signals up from the thalamus to the cortex and down from the cortex to the brainstem and spinal cord. It passes beside the basal nuclei and the thalamus, funneling nearly all traffic between the cortex and the body into a narrow strip. That is why a small stroke there can weaken a whole side of the body.","notes":["This describes the arcuate fasciculus, an association tract within one hemisphere. It would be the answer for the tract linking Broca's and Wernicke's areas.","This describes commissural fibers such as the corpus callosum. Pick it if the question asked which structure joins the right and left hemispheres.","Correct. The internal capsule is the main projection pathway in and out of the cortex.","This describes the tentorium cerebelli, a dural reflection. Choose it if the question asked which fold of dura lies between the cerebrum and the cerebellum."],"lt":"t-w7-white-matter-tracts","lid":"w7-white-matter-tracts-r3","comp":155,"competencyId":"w7-white-matter-tracts"},{"id":"L04565","dok":1,"q":"What kind of movement do the basal nuclei (basal ganglia) mainly help regulate?","a":"Semi-voluntary movement, such as the arm swing of walking.","options":["The rhythm of the heartbeat and breathing","Semi-voluntary movement, like walking arm swing","Smooth muscle contraction in the wall of the gut","Reflex withdrawal of a limb from a painful stimulus"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The basal nuclei, also called the cerebral nuclei, are deep masses of gray matter inside the cerebral white matter. They help start and regulate movement, suppress unwanted movement, and set muscle tone, and they handle semi-voluntary movements such as the natural arm swing that comes with walking. Parkinson disease shows what happens when this control breaks down.","notes":["The rhythm of breathing is set by the respiratory center of the medulla, and the cardiac center there adjusts the heart rate. These are brainstem vital centers, not jobs of the basal nuclei.","Correct. The basal nuclei regulate semi-voluntary movement and muscle tone.","Smooth muscle in the gut is controlled by the autonomic nervous system and the enteric plexuses of the digestive wall. Choose those for a question about involuntary movement of the digestive tract.","A withdrawal reflex is handled at the spinal cord by a reflex arc, before the brain is involved. Pick the spinal cord for a question about the quick pull-away from a hot surface."],"lt":"t-w7-basal-ganglia","lid":"w7-basal-ganglia-r1","comp":156,"competencyId":"w7-basal-ganglia"},{"id":"L04566","dok":1,"q":"Which list names the core nuclei of the basal nuclei (basal ganglia)?","a":"The caudate nucleus, putamen, and globus pallidus.","options":["Caudate nucleus, putamen, and globus pallidus","Thalamus, hypothalamus, and epithalamus","Dentate nucleus, vermis, and arbor vitae","Amygdala, hippocampus, and the cingulate gyrus"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The core of the basal nuclei is three deep gray matter masses in each cerebral hemisphere. The caudate nucleus is long and curved, arching beside the lateral ventricle; the putamen lies laterally; and the globus pallidus lies medial to the putamen. The substantia nigra of the midbrain and the subthalamic nucleus are grouped with them by function.","notes":["Correct. The caudate nucleus, putamen, and globus pallidus are the core basal nuclei.","These are the three parts of the diencephalon. Pick them if the question asked what surrounds the third ventricle.","These are structures of the cerebellum: a deep cerebellar nucleus, the midline band, and the branching white matter. They would be the answer for parts of the cerebellum, not the cerebrum.","These are parts of the limbic system, which sets emotional state and supports memory. Choose them for a question about the brain's emotion and memory structures."],"lt":"t-w7-basal-ganglia","lid":"w7-basal-ganglia-r2","comp":156,"competencyId":"w7-basal-ganglia"},{"id":"L04567","dok":2,"q":"Which midbrain nucleus works with the basal nuclei, and its loss causes which disease?","a":"The substantia nigra; its loss causes Parkinson disease.","options":["Superior colliculus, Parkinson disease","Inferior colliculus, Alzheimer disease","Substantia nigra, multiple sclerosis","Substantia nigra, Parkinson disease"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The substantia nigra is a darkly pigmented nucleus in the midbrain that helps regulate movement by working with the basal nuclei, so it is grouped with them by function. When its neurons are lost, the basal nuclei lose an important input, and movement becomes slow and stiff, often with a resting tremor. This loss is the basis of Parkinson disease.","notes":["The superior colliculi are midbrain centers for visual reflexes, like turning the eyes toward a sudden movement. They would be the answer for a question about visual reflexes, not Parkinson disease.","The inferior colliculi are midbrain centers for auditory reflexes, and Alzheimer disease mainly affects the cortex and memory structures such as the hippocampus. Choose the inferior colliculi for a question about turning toward a sound.","The substantia nigra is right, but multiple sclerosis is a disease that destroys the myelin around axons in the CNS. Pick multiple sclerosis for a question about loss of myelin in white matter.","Correct. Loss of the substantia nigra underlies Parkinson disease."],"lt":"t-w7-basal-ganglia","lid":"w7-basal-ganglia-r3","comp":156,"competencyId":"w7-basal-ganglia"},{"id":"L04585","dok":1,"q":"Which pairing correctly matches two limbic system structures with their roles?","a":"The amygdala handles fear and aggression; the hippocampus forms new long-term memories.","options":["Amygdala: fear and aggression; hippocampus: forming new memories","Amygdala: body temperature; hippocampus: hunger and thirst","Amygdala: visual reflexes; hippocampus: auditory reflexes","Amygdala: forming new memories; hippocampus: fear and aggression"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The limbic system is a functional system, not one structure, spanning the cerebrum and diencephalon; it sets emotional state and supports memory. The amygdala handles fear and aggression and links emotion to memory, while the hippocampus is essential for forming new long-term memories. The cingulate gyrus, arching over the corpus callosum, is involved in expressing emotion.","notes":["Correct. The amygdala is for fear, and the hippocampus is for forming new memories.","Body temperature, hunger, and thirst are controlled by the hypothalamus. Pick the hypothalamus for a question about these homeostatic drives.","Visual and auditory reflexes are handled by the superior and inferior colliculi of the midbrain. Choose the colliculi for a question about turning the eyes or head toward a sight or sound.","This reverses the two roles. A person with hippocampus damage cannot form new long-term memories, while amygdala damage blunts fear responses."],"lt":"t-w7-limbic-system","lid":"w7-limbic-system-r1","comp":159,"competencyId":"w7-limbic-system"},{"id":"N-a17-152-1","dok":1,"q":"Which ventricle forms from the cavity of the diencephalon?","a":"The third ventricle.","options":["The lateral ventricles","The cerebral aqueduct","The fourth ventricle","The third ventricle"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Each secondary vesicle keeps part of the neural tube cavity as it grows into adult structures. The diencephalon becomes the thalamus, hypothalamus, and epithalamus, and its cavity becomes the third ventricle.","notes":["The lateral ventricles are the cavities of the telencephalon, which becomes the cerebrum.","The cerebral aqueduct is the cavity of the mesencephalon, which becomes the midbrain.","The fourth ventricle comes from the metencephalon and myelencephalon, which become the pons, cerebellum, and medulla.","Correct. The cavity of the diencephalon becomes the third ventricle."],"comp":152},{"id":"N-a17-152-2","dok":1,"q":"Which primary brain vesicle divides into the metencephalon and the myelencephalon?","a":"Rhombencephalon.","options":["Rhombencephalon","Prosencephalon","Mesencephalon","Telencephalon"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"By about the fifth week, the rhombencephalon (hindbrain) divides into the metencephalon and the myelencephalon. The prosencephalon divides into the telencephalon and the diencephalon, and the mesencephalon does not divide.","notes":["Correct. The rhombencephalon (hindbrain) divides into the metencephalon and the myelencephalon.","The prosencephalon (forebrain) divides into the telencephalon and the diencephalon.","The mesencephalon (midbrain) is the one primary vesicle that does not divide.","The telencephalon is a secondary vesicle, not a primary one. It comes from the prosencephalon and becomes the cerebrum."],"comp":152},{"id":"N-a17-152-3","dok":2,"q":"An imaging study shows a blockage in the cavity that runs through the adult midbrain. Which cavity is it, and which vesicle did it come from?","a":"Cerebral aqueduct, from the mesencephalon.","options":["Third ventricle, from the diencephalon","Fourth ventricle, from the metencephalon","Cerebral aqueduct, from the rhombencephalon","Cerebral aqueduct, from the mesencephalon"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The midbrain develops from the mesencephalon, the one primary vesicle that does not divide. Its part of the neural tube cavity becomes the cerebral aqueduct, which runs through the adult midbrain and opens into the fourth ventricle below.","notes":["The third ventricle is the cavity of the diencephalon, which becomes the thalamus, hypothalamus, and epithalamus, not the midbrain.","The upper fourth ventricle comes from the metencephalon, which becomes the pons and cerebellum, below the midbrain.","The cavity is the cerebral aqueduct, but it comes from the mesencephalon. The rhombencephalon gives rise to the pons, cerebellum, and medulla.","Correct. The cavity of the midbrain is the cerebral aqueduct, and it comes from the mesencephalon."],"comp":152},{"id":"N-a17-152-4","dok":3,"q":"A lesion affects the cerebellum and the upper part of the fourth ventricle. Which secondary vesicle gave rise to both?","a":"Metencephalon.","options":["Myelencephalon","Mesencephalon","Diencephalon","Metencephalon"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The metencephalon becomes the pons and cerebellum, and its part of the tube cavity becomes the upper part of the fourth ventricle. The myelencephalon below it becomes the medulla oblongata and the lower part of the fourth ventricle. Only the metencephalon matches both the structure and the cavity in this lesion.","notes":["The myelencephalon becomes the medulla oblongata and the lower part of the fourth ventricle, not the cerebellum.","The mesencephalon becomes the midbrain, and its cavity is the cerebral aqueduct, which opens into the fourth ventricle from above.","The diencephalon becomes the thalamus, hypothalamus, and epithalamus, and its cavity is the third ventricle.","Correct. The metencephalon becomes the pons and cerebellum and the upper part of the fourth ventricle."],"comp":152},{"id":"N-a17-152-5","dok":3,"q":"In the adult brain, the diencephalon lies hidden deep inside. Which developmental change explains this?","a":"The cerebral hemispheres grow backward over the diencephalon and midbrain.","options":["The cerebral hemispheres grow backward over the diencephalon and midbrain","The diencephalon shrinks and sinks down into the top of the brainstem","The cerebellum grows forward and covers the diencephalon from behind","The diencephalon fuses with the medulla and moves down below the pons"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The telencephalon grows far more than the other secondary vesicles. As the cerebral hemispheres expand, they grow backward over the diencephalon and midbrain. That growth is why the diencephalon ends up hidden deep in the adult brain.","notes":["Correct. The cerebral hemispheres expand backward over the diencephalon and midbrain and hide it.","The diencephalon does not shrink into the brainstem. It becomes the thalamus, hypothalamus, and epithalamus and is covered by the growing cerebral hemispheres.","The cerebellum comes from the metencephalon and lies over the fourth ventricle. The structure that grows over the diencephalon is the cerebrum.","The diencephalon does not fuse with the medulla. The medulla comes from the myelencephalon, at the back end of the brain."],"comp":152},{"id":"N-a17-159-1","dok":1,"q":"On a midsagittal section, which gyrus is the first one above the corpus callosum?","a":"Cingulate gyrus.","options":["Parahippocampal gyrus","Fornix","Uncus","Cingulate gyrus"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The cingulate gyrus arches just above the corpus callosum on the medial surface. The sulcus of the corpus callosum lies below it, and the cingulate sulcus lies above it. It is part of the limbic ring.","notes":["The parahippocampal gyrus is the continuation of the cingulate gyrus on the inferior and medial surface of the temporal lobe, not above the corpus callosum.","The fornix is an arch of white matter that runs under the corpus callosum, along the lower edge of the septum pellucidum. It is not a gyrus.","The uncus is the hooked front end of the parahippocampal gyrus, down in the temporal lobe, not above the corpus callosum.","Correct. The cingulate gyrus is the first gyrus above the corpus callosum on a midsagittal section."],"comp":159,"competencyId":"w7-limbic-system"},{"id":"N-a17-159-2","dok":2,"q":"In lab, a brain is dissected to open the inferior horn of the lateral ventricle. Which limbic structure is the curved ridge in its floor?","a":"Hippocampus.","options":["Amygdala","Hippocampus","Cingulate gyrus","Fornix"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The hippocampus is a curved ridge of cortex rolled into the floor of the inferior horn of the lateral ventricle, also called the temporal horn. It lies deep to the parahippocampal gyrus in the medial temporal lobe, and its name means seahorse, after its shape.","notes":["The amygdala is an almond-shaped nucleus deep to the uncus, just in front of the hippocampus, not the ridge in the ventricle floor.","Correct. The hippocampus is the curved, seahorse-shaped ridge in the floor of the inferior horn.","The cingulate gyrus arches above the corpus callosum on the medial surface, far from the inferior horn.","The fornix is the arch of white matter that carries hippocampal output forward under the corpus callosum. It leaves the hippocampus but is not the ridge itself."],"comp":159,"competencyId":"w7-limbic-system"},{"id":"N-a17-159-3","dok":2,"q":"A small lesion lies deep to the uncus, just in front of the hippocampus. Which limbic structure is it most likely in?","a":"Amygdala.","options":["Amygdala","Hippocampus","Cingulate gyrus","Mammillary body"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The amygdala is an almond-shaped nucleus deep in the front of the medial temporal lobe. It lies deep to the uncus, the hooked front end of the parahippocampal gyrus, and just in front of the hippocampus. It is involved in fear and links emotion to memory.","notes":["Correct. The amygdala sits deep to the uncus, just in front of the hippocampus.","The hippocampus lies just behind this spot, in the floor of the inferior horn of the lateral ventricle.","The cingulate gyrus is on the medial surface above the corpus callosum, not in the temporal lobe.","The mammillary bodies belong to the hypothalamus, where the fornix ends. They are not in the medial temporal lobe."],"comp":159,"competencyId":"w7-limbic-system"},{"id":"N-a17-159-4","dok":3,"q":"Why are the cingulate gyrus, hippocampus, and amygdala grouped under the name limbic system?","a":"They form a ring around the diencephalon and the top of the brainstem.","options":["They form a ring around the diencephalon and the top of the brainstem","They all sit together deep inside the temporal lobe of each hemisphere","They are all white matter tracts that link the two cerebral hemispheres","They all lie on the lateral surface of each cerebral hemisphere"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Limbic comes from limbus, meaning border or ring. On the medial side of each hemisphere, these structures form a ring around the diencephalon and the top of the brainstem. The cingulate gyrus arches over the corpus callosum and continues into the temporal lobe as the parahippocampal gyrus, completing the C-shaped ring.","notes":["Correct. Limbic means border or ring, and these structures ring the diencephalon and the top of the brainstem.","The hippocampus and amygdala are in the medial temporal lobe, but the cingulate gyrus arches over the corpus callosum, so they are not all in the temporal lobe.","The cingulate gyrus and hippocampus are cortex and the amygdala is a nucleus, so they are gray matter, not white matter tracts.","The limbic structures are on the medial side of each hemisphere, not on the lateral surface."],"comp":159,"competencyId":"w7-limbic-system"},{"id":"N-a17d-151-1","dok":3,"q":"On a coronal brain slice, a thin darker rim traces every fold, pale tissue lies beneath it, and darker patches sit buried inside the pale tissue. What are the buried darker patches?","a":"Basal nuclei, islands of gray matter.","options":["Tracts, bundles of myelinated axons","Cerebral cortex, the folded gray matter","Folia, the folds of the cerebellum","Basal nuclei, islands of gray matter"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"In the cerebrum, gray matter forms a thin outer cortex that follows every gyrus and sulcus, and pale white matter forms the deep core. Islands of gray matter also sit deep inside the white matter. These include the basal nuclei and the thalamus, and on a slice they appear as darker patches buried in the pale tissue.","notes":["Tracts are the bundles of myelinated axons that make up the pale white matter itself. Myelin is fatty and looks pale, not dark.","The cerebral cortex is the thin darker rim on the outside that traces each fold. The patches here are buried deep, not on the surface.","Folia are the fine leaf-like folds of the cerebellar surface. They are not found buried in the white matter of a cerebral slice.","Correct. Dark patches buried deep in the cerebral white matter are deep nuclei, such as the basal nuclei."],"comp":151,"competencyId":"w7-brain-organization"},{"id":"N-a17d-151-2","dok":3,"q":"A slice of CNS tissue shows pale white matter around the outside and gray matter in the center. Which part of the CNS could it come from?","a":"The brainstem.","options":["The cerebrum","The cerebral cortex","The brainstem","The cerebellum"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The cerebrum and cerebellum share one plan, with gray cortex on the outside and white matter inside. The brainstem and spinal cord are arranged the other way around, with most of their white matter on the outside. A slice with white matter outside and gray matter in the center fits the brainstem.","notes":["The cerebrum has a thin outer cortex of gray matter with white matter beneath it. That is the opposite of this slice.","The cerebral cortex is itself the outer layer of gray matter, only about 2 to 4 mm thick. A slice of it would not show white matter wrapped around gray matter.","Correct. The brainstem and spinal cord have most of their white matter on the outside, the reverse of the cerebrum.","The cerebellum follows the same plan as the cerebrum, gray cortex on the outside and white matter inside. Its white matter forms the arbor vitae within."],"comp":151,"competencyId":"w7-brain-organization"},{"id":"N-a17d-151-3","dok":3,"q":"On a midsagittal section, one division sits behind the brainstem and below the occipital lobes, with a thin gray rim and white matter inside. Which division is it, and which cranial fossa holds it?","a":"The cerebellum, in the posterior cranial fossa.","options":["The cerebellum, in the middle cranial fossa","The cerebellum, in the posterior cranial fossa","The cerebrum, in the anterior cranial fossa","The diencephalon, in the posterior cranial fossa"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The four major divisions are the cerebrum, diencephalon, brainstem, and cerebellum. The cerebellum sits behind the brainstem and below the occipital lobes, in the posterior cranial fossa. Like the cerebrum, it has gray cortex on the outside and white matter inside.","notes":["The division is right, but the cerebellum sits in the posterior cranial fossa, at the back and bottom of the skull.","Correct. The cerebellum lies behind the brainstem and below the occipital lobes, in the posterior cranial fossa.","The cerebrum is the largest division and fills the upper cranial cavity, with the occipital lobes above the structure described here. It is not behind the brainstem.","The diencephalon sits deep at the core of the brain, between the hemispheres and above the brainstem. It is not behind the brainstem."],"comp":151,"competencyId":"w7-brain-organization"},{"id":"N-a17d-152-1","dok":1,"q":"What are the three primary brain vesicles?","a":"Prosencephalon, mesencephalon, and rhombencephalon.","options":["Prosencephalon, mesencephalon, and rhombencephalon","Telencephalon, diencephalon, and mesencephalon","Cerebrum, midbrain, and medulla oblongata","Metencephalon, myelencephalon, and mesencephalon"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"By about the fourth week, the cranial end of the neural tube swells into three primary brain vesicles. They are the prosencephalon (forebrain), the mesencephalon (midbrain), and the rhombencephalon (hindbrain). By about the fifth week two of them split, giving the five secondary vesicles.","notes":["Correct. The three primary vesicles are the prosencephalon (forebrain), mesencephalon (midbrain), and rhombencephalon (hindbrain).","The telencephalon and diencephalon are secondary vesicles that split from the prosencephalon. Only the mesencephalon here is a primary vesicle.","These are adult structures, not embryonic vesicles. The primary vesicles are the early swellings that later develop into them.","The metencephalon and myelencephalon are secondary vesicles that split from the rhombencephalon. This list mixes secondary vesicles with one primary vesicle."],"comp":152},{"id":"N-a17d-152-2","dok":2,"q":"Which secondary brain vesicle develops into the medulla oblongata, and which cavity does it keep in the adult?","a":"Myelencephalon, the lower part of the fourth ventricle.","options":["Mesencephalon, the cerebral aqueduct","Myelencephalon, the third ventricle","Myelencephalon, the lower part of the fourth ventricle","Metencephalon, the upper part of the fourth ventricle"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The rhombencephalon splits into the metencephalon and the myelencephalon. The myelencephalon becomes the medulla oblongata, the part next to the spinal cord, and myel means marrow. Its cavity becomes the lower part of the fourth ventricle, which continues into the central canal of the spinal cord.","notes":["The mesencephalon does not divide and becomes the midbrain, with the cerebral aqueduct as its cavity. The medulla lies lower.","The vesicle is right, but the third ventricle is the cavity of the diencephalon. The myelencephalon keeps the lower part of the fourth ventricle.","Correct. The myelencephalon becomes the medulla, and its cavity is the lower fourth ventricle, which continues into the central canal.","The metencephalon becomes the pons and cerebellum, and its cavity is the upper part of the fourth ventricle. The medulla comes from the vesicle behind it."],"comp":152},{"id":"N-a17d-153-1","dok":3,"q":"On the side of a brain specimen, you find the deepest groove first. Then you find a groove that runs without a break from the top of the hemisphere almost down to that deep groove. Which gyrus lies just behind this second groove, and which lobe is it in?","a":"The postcentral gyrus, in the parietal lobe.","options":["The postcentral gyrus, in the frontal lobe","The insula, in the lateral sulcus","The precentral gyrus, in the frontal lobe","The postcentral gyrus, in the parietal lobe"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The deepest groove on the side is the lateral sulcus. The central sulcus usually runs without a break from the top of the hemisphere almost down to it, with a long gyrus on each side. Post means after, so the postcentral gyrus is just behind the central sulcus, at the front of the parietal lobe.","notes":["The gyrus name is right, but the central sulcus separates the frontal lobe in front from the parietal lobe behind. The postcentral gyrus is in the parietal lobe.","The insula is the fifth lobe, hidden deep in the lateral sulcus. It is not a gyrus behind the central sulcus on the surface.","The precentral gyrus is the one just in front of the central sulcus, at the back of the frontal lobe. The question asks for the gyrus behind it.","Correct. The unbroken groove is the central sulcus, and the gyrus just behind it is the postcentral gyrus of the parietal lobe."],"comp":153,"competencyId":"w7-cerebrum-surface"},{"id":"N-a17d-153-2","dok":3,"q":"To see the insula on a brain specimen, which lobe do you gently pull away, and from which lobes?","a":"The temporal lobe, away from frontal and parietal.","options":["The frontal lobe, away from the parietal lobe","The temporal lobe, away from frontal and parietal","One hemisphere, away from the other","The occipital lobe, away from the cerebellum"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The insula is the fifth lobe, hidden deep inside the lateral sulcus. The lateral sulcus separates the temporal lobe below from the frontal and parietal lobes above, and those three lobes cover the insula. Gently pulling the temporal lobe away from the frontal and parietal lobes opens the sulcus so you can look in.","notes":["The frontal and parietal lobes are separated by the central sulcus, which does not hide a lobe. The insula is under the lateral sulcus.","Correct. The insula lies deep in the lateral sulcus, so you pull the temporal lobe down away from the frontal and parietal lobes above.","Pulling the hemispheres apart opens the longitudinal fissure, which shows the corpus callosum at its floor. The insula is on the side of each hemisphere.","The occipital lobe and cerebellum are separated by the transverse fissure at the back of the brain. Opening that fissure does not reach the insula."],"comp":153,"competencyId":"w7-cerebrum-surface"},{"id":"N-a17d-155-1","dok":3,"q":"On a horizontal section, a white band bent into a V points toward the midline. The caudate nucleus and thalamus lie on its inner side, and a lens-shaped gray mass lies on its outer side. What is the band, and which class of fibers runs in it?","a":"The internal capsule, made of projection fibers.","options":["The internal capsule, made of projection fibers","The internal capsule, made of association fibers","The corpus callosum, made of commissural fibers","The arcuate fasciculus, made of projection fibers"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Projection fibers connect the cortex with the lower brain and spinal cord. Below the cortex they fan out as the corona radiata, then gather into one compact band, the internal capsule. On a horizontal section it is a V-shaped white band with the caudate nucleus and thalamus on its medial side and the lentiform nucleus on its lateral side.","notes":["Correct. The V-shaped band between the caudate and thalamus inside and the lentiform nucleus outside is the internal capsule, a band of projection fibers.","The band is right, but association fibers stay within one hemisphere, linking gyri and lobes. The internal capsule carries projection fibers to and from the brainstem and cord.","The corpus callosum is the largest commissural bundle, the arched band above the diencephalon on a midsagittal section. It crosses the midline instead of running between the thalamus and lentiform nucleus.","The arcuate fasciculus is a long association tract that links Broca's area and Wernicke's area. It is not the V-shaped band beside the thalamus."],"comp":155,"competencyId":"w7-white-matter-tracts"},{"id":"N-a17d-156-1","dok":3,"q":"On a coronal section, a lens-shaped gray mass lies lateral to the internal capsule, with a darker outer part and a paler inner part. Which part touches the internal capsule, and what is it called?","a":"The paler inner part, the globus pallidus.","options":["The darker outer part, the putamen","The darker outer part, the globus pallidus","The paler inner part, the globus pallidus","The paler inner part, the caudate nucleus"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The lens-shaped mass lateral to the internal capsule is the lentiform nucleus, made of the putamen and the globus pallidus. The putamen is the outer, darker part. The globus pallidus is the inner, paler part, and it sits between the putamen and the internal capsule. Pallidus means pale.","notes":["The putamen is the darker part, but it is the outer, lateral part of the lentiform nucleus. The globus pallidus lies between it and the internal capsule.","The globus pallidus is the paler part, not the darker one, and it is the inner part. The darker outer part is the putamen.","Correct. The globus pallidus is the paler, medial part of the lentiform nucleus, lying between the putamen and the internal capsule.","The caudate nucleus is not part of the lentiform nucleus. It lies on the medial side of the internal capsule, bulging into the lateral ventricle."],"comp":156,"competencyId":"w7-basal-ganglia"},{"id":"N-a17d-156-2","dok":3,"q":"On a coronal section, a gray mass bulges into the lateral wall of the anterior horn of the lateral ventricle. Which nucleus is this, and which nucleus does it pair with to form the striatum?","a":"The head of the caudate nucleus, paired with the putamen.","options":["The head of the caudate nucleus, paired with the globus pallidus","The thalamus, paired with the hypothalamus","The putamen, paired with the globus pallidus","The head of the caudate nucleus, paired with the putamen"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The caudate nucleus is long and curved, and its large head bulges into the lateral wall of the anterior horn of the lateral ventricle. The caudate nucleus and the putamen together are called the striatum. Bands of gray matter cross the internal capsule between them and give the area its striped look.","notes":["The nucleus is right, but the globus pallidus pairs with the putamen to form the lentiform nucleus. The striatum is the caudate with the putamen.","The thalamus and hypothalamus are parts of the diencephalon around the third ventricle. The mass in the wall of the anterior horn is the caudate head.","The putamen and globus pallidus form the lentiform nucleus, which lies lateral to the internal capsule. Neither bulges into the lateral ventricle.","Correct. The head of the caudate bulges into the anterior horn, and the caudate with the putamen forms the striatum."],"comp":156,"competencyId":"w7-basal-ganglia"},{"id":"N-a17d-159-1","dok":2,"q":"Which sequence traces the limbic ring from above the corpus callosum down into the temporal lobe?","a":"Cingulate gyrus, parahippocampal gyrus, uncus.","options":["Cingulate gyrus, uncus, parahippocampal gyrus","Parahippocampal gyrus, cingulate gyrus, uncus","Uncus, cingulate gyrus, parahippocampal gyrus","Cingulate gyrus, parahippocampal gyrus, uncus"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The cingulate gyrus arches just above the corpus callosum on the medial surface. It curves around the back of the corpus callosum and continues down into the temporal lobe as the parahippocampal gyrus. The front end of the parahippocampal gyrus hooks back on itself to form the uncus, completing the C-shaped ring.","notes":["The uncus is the hooked front end of the parahippocampal gyrus, so it comes last, at the end of that gyrus.","The parahippocampal gyrus is in the temporal lobe, so it comes after the cingulate gyrus, not before it.","The uncus is in the temporal lobe at the end of the ring, not above the corpus callosum where the ring starts.","Correct. The cingulate gyrus curves behind the corpus callosum into the parahippocampal gyrus, whose front end hooks back as the uncus."],"comp":159,"competencyId":"w7-limbic-system"},{"id":"N-a17d-159-2","dok":3,"q":"In a dissection, you follow the fornix backward from the mammillary bodies, under the corpus callosum and along the lower edge of the septum pellucidum. Which structure will it lead you to, and where does that structure sit?","a":"The hippocampus, in the floor of the inferior horn.","options":["The cingulate gyrus, above the corpus callosum","The amygdala, deep to the uncus in the temporal lobe","The hippocampus, in the floor of the inferior horn","The hippocampus, in the roof of the third ventricle"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The fornix is an arching band of white matter that carries the output of the hippocampus. It runs forward under the corpus callosum along the lower edge of the septum pellucidum and ends at the mammillary bodies. Traced backward, it leads to the hippocampus, a curved ridge rolled into the floor of the inferior horn of the lateral ventricle in the medial temporal lobe.","notes":["The cingulate gyrus arches above the corpus callosum on the medial surface. The fornix runs below the corpus callosum and leads to the hippocampus.","The amygdala is an almond-shaped nucleus in front of the hippocampus, deep to the uncus. The fornix carries the output of the hippocampus, not the amygdala.","Correct. The fornix carries hippocampal output to the mammillary bodies, so following it back leads to the hippocampus in the floor of the inferior horn.","The structure is right, but the hippocampus sits in the floor of the inferior horn of the lateral ventricle, in the temporal lobe. The back of the roof of the third ventricle is formed by the epithalamus."],"comp":159,"competencyId":"w7-limbic-system"}]},{"id":"m5-diencephalon-brainstem","title":"Diencephalon, brainstem and cerebellum","summary":"thalamus, hypothalamus, epithalamus, midbrain, pons, medulla, reticular formation, cerebellum parts","mw":"2026-11-30","tr":"2026-12-01","cards":[{"id":"L02846","dok":1,"q":"Which structure of the diencephalon serves as the relay station that sorts incoming sensory information and forwards it to the cortex?","a":"The thalamus.","options":["The hypothalamus","The thalamus","The epithalamus","The pineal gland"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The thalamus is a pair of oval gray matter masses that makes up about 80 percent of the diencephalon. Its nuclei receive incoming sensory signals and relay each type on to the correct area of the cerebral cortex. That is why it is called the main sensory relay station of the brain.","notes":["The hypothalamus sits below the thalamus and is the master control center for homeostasis, regulating body temperature, hunger, thirst, and the pituitary gland. Pick it for a question about homeostasis rather than sensory relay.","Correct. The thalamus relays sensory information to the cortex.","The epithalamus is the small posterior part of the diencephalon. It would be the answer if the question asked which part of the diencephalon contains the pineal gland.","The pineal gland is the endocrine gland of the epithalamus that secretes melatonin. Pick it if the question asked which brain structure produces melatonin."],"lt":"t-cns-brain-spinal-cord","lid":"c7","comp":157,"competencyId":"w7-diencephalon"},{"id":"L02847","dok":1,"q":"Which part of the diencephalon contains the pineal gland?","a":"The epithalamus.","options":["The epithalamus","The third ventricle","The hypothalamus","The thalamus"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The diencephalon has three parts: the thalamus, the hypothalamus, and the epithalamus. The epithalamus is the small posterior part, and it contains the pineal gland, an endocrine gland that secretes melatonin. The prefix epi means upon, a reminder that it sits on the back of the thalamus.","notes":["Correct. The pineal gland is part of the epithalamus.","The third ventricle is the fluid-filled cavity enclosed by the diencephalon, not one of its tissue parts. Pick it if the question asked which ventricle lies in the diencephalon.","The hypothalamus lies below the thalamus and controls homeostasis and the pituitary gland. It would be the answer if the question asked which part of the diencephalon connects to the pituitary.","The thalamus is the largest part of the diencephalon and relays sensory information to the cortex. Pick it if the question asked which structure is the main sensory relay station."],"lt":"t-cns-brain-spinal-cord","lid":"c8","comp":157,"competencyId":"w7-diencephalon"},{"id":"L02848","dok":1,"q":"What are the three regions of the brainstem?","a":"The midbrain, pons, and medulla oblongata.","options":["The thalamus, hypothalamus, and epithalamus","The cerebrum, diencephalon, and cerebellum","The midbrain, pons, and medulla oblongata","The frontal, parietal, and occipital lobes"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The brainstem is the stalk that connects the cerebrum, diencephalon, and cerebellum to the spinal cord. From top to bottom it is made of the midbrain, the pons, and the medulla oblongata. Every ascending and descending tract passes through it, and it holds the nuclei of most cranial nerves.","notes":["These are the three parts of the diencephalon, the region just above the brainstem. They would be the answer if the question asked about the parts of the diencephalon.","These are three of the four major brain regions, each separate from the brainstem. Pick them if the question asked which regions sit above or behind the brainstem.","Correct. The midbrain, pons, and medulla oblongata make up the brainstem.","These are lobes of the cerebrum, the outer part of the brain. Pick a list of lobes if the question asked about subdivisions of a cerebral hemisphere."],"lt":"t-cns-brain-spinal-cord","lid":"c9","comp":161,"competencyId":"w7-brainstem-regions"},{"id":"L02849","dok":1,"q":"Which region of the brainstem is the most inferior, joining the spinal cord?","a":"The medulla oblongata.","options":["The diencephalon","The pons","The medulla oblongata","The midbrain"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The medulla oblongata is the lowest region of the brainstem and merges with the spinal cord at the foramen magnum. It contains the pyramids and the vital centers that control heart rate, breathing rhythm, and blood vessel diameter. Because of those centers, injury to the medulla is quickly life-threatening.","notes":["The diencephalon sits above the brainstem and is a separate major brain region. Pick it if the question asked which region holds the thalamus and hypothalamus.","The pons is the rounded middle region of the brainstem, a bridge to the cerebellum. It would be the answer if the question asked which region lies between the midbrain and the medulla.","Correct. The medulla oblongata is the lowest brainstem region and is continuous with the cord.","The midbrain is the most superior region of the brainstem, surrounding the cerebral aqueduct. Pick it if the question asked which brainstem region is highest or which one holds the colliculi."],"lt":"t-cns-brain-spinal-cord","lid":"c10","comp":161,"competencyId":"w7-brainstem-regions"},{"id":"L02850","dok":1,"q":"Which brain structure sits behind the brainstem and coordinates movement, posture, and balance?","a":"The cerebellum.","options":["The cerebellum","The thalamus","The pons","The cerebrum"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The cerebellum is the second largest part of the brain. It sits in the posterior cranial fossa, behind the brainstem and below the occipital lobes, and is attached to the brainstem by three pairs of cerebellar peduncles. It coordinates movement, posture, and balance so that motion is smooth and accurate.","notes":["Correct. The cerebellum sits behind the brainstem and coordinates movement.","The thalamus is in the diencephalon, deep in the center of the brain. Pick it if the question asked which structure relays sensory information to the cortex.","The pons is part of the brainstem itself, in front of the cerebellum, and it relays motor information to the cerebellum through the middle cerebellar peduncles. Pick it for a question about the bridge between the cerebellum and the rest of the brainstem.","The cerebrum is the largest part of the brain and sits above the other regions. It would be the answer if the question asked which region initiates voluntary movement and conscious thought."],"lt":"t-cns-brain-spinal-cord","lid":"c11","comp":158,"competencyId":"w7-cerebellum"},{"id":"L02858","dok":2,"q":"The thalamus and hypothalamus are both structures of which major brain region?","a":"The diencephalon.","options":["The diencephalon","The cerebrum","The cerebellum","The brainstem"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The diencephalon is the deep central region between the cerebrum and the brainstem. It has three parts: the thalamus, the hypothalamus, and the epithalamus, and it surrounds the third ventricle. The thalamus is its largest part, and the hypothalamus lies just below the thalamus.","notes":["Correct. The thalamus and hypothalamus are parts of the diencephalon.","The cerebrum is the large outer region with the cortex, cerebral white matter, and basal nuclei. Pick it if the question asked which region contains the lobes.","The cerebellum is a separate region behind the brainstem that coordinates movement. Pick it if the question asked which region contains the vermis and arbor vitae.","The brainstem is made of the midbrain, pons, and medulla, below the diencephalon. It would be the answer if the question asked which region holds the vital centers of the medulla."],"lt":"t-cns-brain-spinal-cord","lid":"c19","comp":157,"competencyId":"w7-diencephalon"},{"id":"L02859","dok":2,"q":"Where is the midbrain located within the brainstem, relative to the pons and medulla?","a":"It is the most superior part.","options":["It is the most superior part","It is the lowest part, joining the cord","It lies behind the pons and medulla","It is the middle, rounded part"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The midbrain, also called the mesencephalon, is the short, most superior region of the brainstem, just below the diencephalon. It surrounds the cerebral aqueduct and contains the colliculi, the cerebral peduncles, and the substantia nigra. Below it come the pons and then the medulla oblongata.","notes":["Correct. The midbrain is the highest region of the brainstem.","The lowest region, continuous with the spinal cord, is the medulla oblongata. This position would be the answer if the question asked about the medulla.","The structure lying behind the brainstem is the cerebellum, which is not part of the brainstem at all. Pick this position for a question about the cerebellum.","The middle, rounded region of the brainstem is the pons, whose name means bridge. Pick this position if the question asked where the pons sits."],"lt":"t-cns-brain-spinal-cord","lid":"c20","comp":161,"competencyId":"w7-brainstem-regions"},{"id":"L02860","dok":2,"q":"The pons is the middle region of the brainstem. Which two regions sit immediately above and below it?","a":"The midbrain above and the medulla oblongata below.","options":["The medulla above and the midbrain below","The cerebrum above and the spinal cord below","The midbrain above and the medulla below","The diencephalon above and the cerebellum below"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"From top to bottom, the brainstem runs midbrain, pons, medulla oblongata. The pons sits between the midbrain above and the medulla below, and it also forms a bridge to the cerebellum behind it. Its pontine nuclei relay motor information from the cortex to the cerebellum.","notes":["This reverses the brainstem order. The midbrain is the highest brainstem region and the medulla is the lowest, so a student who flips the sequence picks this.","The cerebrum and spinal cord are at the far ends of the central axis, separated from the pons by the diencephalon and midbrain above and the medulla below. This would only fit a question about the ends of the whole brain-to-cord axis.","Correct. The midbrain lies above the pons and the medulla lies below it.","The diencephalon sits above the midbrain, not directly on the pons, and the cerebellum lies behind the pons rather than below it. This pairing mixes up neighboring regions that are not part of the brainstem."],"lt":"t-cns-brain-spinal-cord","lid":"c21","comp":161,"competencyId":"w7-brainstem-regions"},{"id":"L02872","dok":2,"q":"Which brain region lies deep between the cerebrum and the brainstem and contains the main sensory relay station and the homeostasis control center?","a":"The diencephalon.","options":["The cerebellum","The corpus callosum","The diencephalon","The medulla oblongata"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The diencephalon is the deep central region that links the cerebral hemispheres to the brainstem. It contains the thalamus, which relays sensory signals to the cortex, the hypothalamus, which controls homeostasis, and the epithalamus with the pineal gland. It surrounds the third ventricle.","notes":["The cerebellum sits behind the brainstem, not between the cerebrum and brainstem, and coordinates movement. Pick it if the question asked which region holds the arbor vitae and vermis.","The corpus callosum is a band of white matter connecting the two cerebral hemispheres, not a brain region with relay or control centers. Pick it if the question asked what joins the right and left hemispheres.","Correct. The diencephalon holds the thalamus and hypothalamus.","The medulla oblongata is the lowest part of the brainstem and holds the vital cardiac, respiratory, and vasomotor centers. It would be the answer for a question about the centers that control heartbeat and breathing."],"lt":"t-cns-brain-spinal-cord","lid":"c33","comp":157,"competencyId":"w7-diencephalon"},{"id":"L02874","dok":2,"q":"Which small endocrine gland is housed within the epithalamus?","a":"The pineal gland.","options":["The hypothalamus","The pineal gland","The thalamus","The pituitary gland"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The epithalamus is the small posterior part of the diencephalon, and its best-known structure is the pineal gland. The pineal gland is an endocrine gland that secretes melatonin, which helps set sleep-wake rhythms. It sits in the midline at the back of the third ventricle.","notes":["The hypothalamus is a separate part of the diencephalon below the thalamus and is the homeostasis control center. Pick it for a question about the link between the nervous and endocrine systems.","Correct. The pineal gland is the endocrine gland of the epithalamus.","The thalamus is the large paired relay station of the diencephalon, not a gland. Pick it if the question asked which structure forms about 80 percent of the diencephalon.","The pituitary gland hangs below the hypothalamus, attached by a stalk, rather than sitting in the epithalamus. It would be the answer if the question asked which endocrine gland the hypothalamus controls directly."],"lt":"t-cns-brain-spinal-cord","lid":"c35","comp":157,"competencyId":"w7-diencephalon"},{"id":"L02879","dok":2,"q":"Which part of the diencephalon links the nervous system with the endocrine system and is the main control center for homeostasis?","a":"The hypothalamus.","options":["The thalamus","The epithalamus","The pineal gland","The hypothalamus"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The hypothalamus lies below the thalamus and is the master control center for homeostasis. It controls the autonomic nervous system, regulates body temperature, hunger, thirst, and sleep-wake rhythms, and directs endocrine function through the pituitary gland. That pituitary connection is why it is called the link between the nervous and endocrine systems.","notes":["The thalamus is the sensory relay station that forwards signals to the cortex. Pick it if the question asked which structure sorts incoming sensory information.","The epithalamus is the small posterior part of the diencephalon containing the pineal gland. It would be the answer if the question asked which part of the diencephalon houses the pineal gland.","The pineal gland is an endocrine gland that secretes melatonin, but it does not control the pituitary or overall homeostasis. Pick it for a question about the source of melatonin.","Correct. The hypothalamus is the homeostasis center and the nervous-endocrine link."],"lt":"t-cns-brain-spinal-cord","lid":"c40","comp":157,"competencyId":"w7-diencephalon"},{"id":"L02884","dok":2,"q":"Which list orders the three regions of the brainstem from superior to inferior?","a":"Midbrain, pons, medulla oblongata.","options":["Midbrain, medulla oblongata, pons","Medulla oblongata, pons, midbrain","Midbrain, pons, medulla oblongata","Pons, midbrain, medulla oblongata"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The brainstem runs from the midbrain at the top, just below the diencephalon, through the rounded pons in the middle, to the medulla oblongata at the bottom, which merges with the spinal cord at the foramen magnum. The cranial nerve nuclei follow the same order, with III and IV in the midbrain, V through VIII in the pons, and IX, X, and XII in the medulla.","notes":["This swaps the pons and medulla. The medulla is the lowest region and is continuous with the spinal cord, so it cannot sit above the pons.","This is the right sequence read in the wrong direction, from inferior to superior. Pick it only if the question asked for the order starting at the spinal cord.","Correct. Midbrain, pons, then medulla oblongata.","This puts the pons above the midbrain. The midbrain is the highest region, directly below the diencephalon, so the pons belongs second."],"lt":"t-cns-brain-spinal-cord","lid":"c45","comp":161,"competencyId":"w7-brainstem-regions"},{"id":"L02887","dok":2,"q":"The third ventricle is the midline cavity enclosed by which brain region?","a":"The diencephalon.","options":["The cerebellum","The midbrain","The cerebrum","The diencephalon"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The diencephalon surrounds the third ventricle. The right and left thalami form its upper side walls, and the hypothalamus forms its floor and lower walls, with the massa intermedia crossing the ventricle in most brains. In front the third ventricle opens into each lateral ventricle through an interventricular foramen, and behind it drains into the cerebral aqueduct.","notes":["The cerebellum lies over the roof of the fourth ventricle, not around the third. Pick it if the question asked which region lies behind the fourth ventricle.","The midbrain surrounds the cerebral aqueduct, the narrow channel that carries CSF from the third to the fourth ventricle. It would be the answer for a question about the aqueduct.","The cerebrum encloses the lateral ventricles, one in each hemisphere. Pick it if the question asked which region holds the lateral ventricles.","Correct. The diencephalon encloses the third ventricle."],"lt":"t-cns-brain-spinal-cord","lid":"c48","comp":157,"competencyId":"w7-diencephalon"},{"id":"L02890","dok":3,"q":"Match each condition to the structure it involves: Parkinson disease, hydrocephalus, and spinal cord injury. Which set is correct?","a":"Parkinson disease, substantia nigra; hydrocephalus, ventricles; spinal cord injury, cord tracts.","options":["Parkinson disease, ventricles; hydrocephalus, substantia nigra; spinal cord injury, cord tracts","Parkinson disease, substantia nigra; hydrocephalus, ventricles; spinal cord injury, cord tracts","Parkinson disease, cord tracts; hydrocephalus, ventricles; spinal cord injury, substantia nigra","Parkinson disease, substantia nigra; hydrocephalus, cord tracts; spinal cord injury, ventricles"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Parkinson disease comes from loss of the substantia nigra, a darkly pigmented nucleus in the midbrain that works with the basal nuclei to regulate movement. Hydrocephalus is a buildup of cerebrospinal fluid that enlarges the ventricles. A spinal cord injury interrupts the ascending and descending tracts, so function is lost below the injury.","notes":["This swaps Parkinson disease and hydrocephalus. The ventricles hold CSF and enlarge in hydrocephalus, while Parkinson disease involves a movement-regulating nucleus.","Correct. Substantia nigra, ventricles, and cord tracts, in that order.","This swaps Parkinson disease and spinal cord injury. The substantia nigra is in the midbrain, not the cord, and loss of cord tracts is the injury itself.","This swaps hydrocephalus and spinal cord injury. Hydrocephalus is a fluid problem in the ventricles, and spinal cord injury damages tracts, not ventricles."],"lt":"t-cns-brain-spinal-cord","lid":"c51","comp":156,"competencyId":"w7-basal-ganglia"},{"id":"N-m5-diencephalon-brainstem-1","dok":1,"q":"Which region of the brainstem surrounds the cerebral aqueduct?","a":"The midbrain.","options":["The pons","The midbrain","The medulla oblongata","The diencephalon"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The midbrain, or mesencephalon, is the short upper region of the brainstem, and the cerebral aqueduct runs through its center. The aqueduct carries CSF from the third ventricle down to the fourth. The roof of the midbrain behind the aqueduct is the tectum, with its four colliculi.","notes":["The pons lies in front of the fourth ventricle, below the aqueduct. Pick it if the question asked which brainstem region lies in front of the fourth ventricle.","Correct. The cerebral aqueduct runs through the midbrain.","The medulla oblongata is the lowest brainstem region, with the lower fourth ventricle behind it and the central canal continuing below. It would be the answer for the region continuous with the spinal cord.","The diencephalon surrounds the third ventricle, above the midbrain, and it is not part of the brainstem. Pick it if the question asked which region encloses the third ventricle."],"comp":162,"competencyId":"w7-midbrain"},{"id":"N-m5-diencephalon-brainstem-2","dok":2,"q":"The massa intermedia (interthalamic adhesion) is a bridge that joins which two structures?","a":"The right and left thalami.","options":["The right and left cerebellar hemispheres","The third and fourth ventricles","The right and left thalami","The right and left cerebral hemispheres"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The thalami are paired oval masses of gray matter that form the side walls of the third ventricle. In most brains they touch across the middle of the ventricle at a small bridge called the massa intermedia, or interthalamic adhesion. Inter means between, so the name says it lies between the two thalami.","notes":["The right and left cerebellar hemispheres are joined by the vermis, the narrow midline band of the cerebellum. It would be the answer for a question about the cerebellum's midline.","The third and fourth ventricles are connected by the cerebral aqueduct, a channel through the midbrain. Pick the aqueduct if the question asked how CSF passes between those ventricles.","Correct. The massa intermedia joins the two thalami.","The right and left cerebral hemispheres are joined by the corpus callosum, a large band of commissural fibers. Pick that structure if the question asked what connects the hemispheres."],"comp":157,"competencyId":"w7-diencephalon"},{"id":"L03134","dok":1,"q":"Which three structures make up the diencephalon?","a":"The thalamus, hypothalamus, and epithalamus.","options":["The midbrain, pons, and medulla oblongata","The thalamus, hypothalamus, and epithalamus","The cerebrum, cerebellum, and brainstem","The caudate nucleus, putamen, and globus pallidus"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The diencephalon is the deep brain region that sits between the cerebrum and the brainstem and surrounds the third ventricle. It has three parts: the thalamus, the large sensory relay that makes up about 80 percent of it, the hypothalamus below it, which is the control center for homeostasis, and the small epithalamus at the back, which holds the pineal gland.","notes":["These three regions make up the brainstem, which lies below the diencephalon and connects it to the spinal cord. Pick them if the question asks which structures form the brainstem.","Correct. The thalamus, hypothalamus, and epithalamus together form the diencephalon around the third ventricle.","These are three of the four major parts of the brain, and the diencephalon is the fourth. Choose this set if the question asks for the major brain regions other than the diencephalon.","These are basal ganglia, deep gray matter masses buried in the cerebral white matter that help regulate movement. They would be the answer for a question about the core basal ganglia components."],"lt":"t-cns-brain","lid":"c2","comp":157,"competencyId":"w7-diencephalon"},{"id":"L03141","dok":1,"q":"Which part of the diencephalon is the main sensory relay station to the cerebral cortex?","a":"The thalamus.","options":["The thalamus","The substantia nigra","The hypothalamus","The epithalamus"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The thalamus is a pair of oval gray matter masses that makes up about 80 percent of the diencephalon. Nearly all sensory signals heading for the cortex stop at a thalamic nucleus first, where they are sorted and passed on to the correct cortical area. That is why the thalamus is called the gateway or relay station to the cortex.","notes":["Correct. The thalamus relays sensory input to the cerebral cortex.","The substantia nigra is a darkly pigmented nucleus of the midbrain, not the diencephalon, and it helps regulate movement with the basal ganglia. Choose it for a question about Parkinson disease.","The hypothalamus lies below the thalamus and is the master control center for homeostasis, including body temperature, hunger, thirst, and the autonomic nervous system. Pick it for a question about homeostatic control.","The epithalamus is the small posterior part of the diencephalon, and it holds the pineal gland. It would be the answer for a question about where melatonin is made."],"lt":"t-cns-brain","lid":"c9","comp":157,"competencyId":"w7-diencephalon"},{"id":"L03142","dok":1,"q":"Which endocrine gland of the epithalamus secretes melatonin?","a":"The pineal gland.","options":["The pineal gland","The pituitary gland","The hypothalamus","The habenular nuclei"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The epithalamus is the small posterior roof of the diencephalon, and its endocrine gland is the pineal gland. The pineal gland secretes melatonin, a hormone tied to the sleep and wake cycle. It is a small midline structure, so it is easy to find on a sagittal section of the brain just above the midbrain.","notes":["Correct. The pineal gland is the endocrine gland of the epithalamus and secretes melatonin.","The pituitary gland hangs below the hypothalamus on a stalk and sits in the sella turcica of the sphenoid bone, not in the epithalamus. Pick it for a question about the gland controlled by the hypothalamus.","The hypothalamus is a separate part of the diencephalon below the thalamus that controls homeostasis and the pituitary. It would be the answer for a question about the control center for body temperature or thirst.","The habenular nuclei are also part of the epithalamus, but they are clusters of neurons, not a gland, and they do not secrete melatonin. Choose them if the question asks about the other named structure of the epithalamus."],"lt":"t-cns-brain","lid":"c10","comp":157,"competencyId":"w7-diencephalon"},{"id":"L03143","dok":1,"q":"What is the branching, tree-like pattern of white matter inside the cerebellum called?","a":"The arbor vitae.","options":["The internal capsule","The folia","The corpus callosum","The arbor vitae"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"When the cerebellum is cut in a sagittal section, its inner white matter branches out like the limbs of a tree under the outer gray cortex. This pattern is called the arbor vitae, which means tree of life. It carries signals into and out of the cerebellar cortex.","notes":["The internal capsule is a band of projection fibers in the cerebrum that carries traffic between the cortex and the brainstem and spinal cord. It would be the answer for the major projection pathway beside the basal ganglia.","The folia are the fine, leaf-like folds on the outer surface of the cerebellum, made of cerebellar cortex. Choose them if the question asks about the surface folds rather than the inner white matter.","The corpus callosum is the large band of commissural white matter joining the two cerebral hemispheres. Pick it for a question about the tract that connects the left and right cerebrum.","Correct. The arbor vitae is the tree-like white matter inside the cerebellum."],"lt":"t-cns-brain","lid":"c11","comp":158,"competencyId":"w7-cerebellum"},{"id":"L03154","dok":2,"q":"A patient can move every limb but cannot coordinate movement, keep posture, or balance well. Which brain region is most likely affected?","a":"The cerebellum.","options":["The thalamus","The cerebellum","The hypothalamus","The cerebrum"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The cerebellum sits behind the brainstem in the posterior cranial fossa and coordinates movement, posture, and balance. It compares the movement that was commanded with what the body is actually doing and smooths out the difference. When it is damaged, a person can still move, but movements become clumsy and balance is poor.","notes":["The thalamus is the main sensory relay of the diencephalon. It would be the answer for a question about sensory signals being sorted and passed on to the cortex.","Correct. The cerebellum coordinates movement, posture, and balance.","The hypothalamus controls homeostasis, such as body temperature, hunger, and thirst. Choose it for a problem with temperature regulation, not coordination.","The cerebrum, through the primary motor cortex, starts voluntary movement. Damage there causes weakness or paralysis, so pick it if the patient could not move a limb at all."],"lt":"t-cns-brain","lid":"c22","comp":158,"competencyId":"w7-cerebellum"},{"id":"L03162","dok":3,"q":"An imaging study finds a lesion in the adult structure that develops from the mesencephalon. Which region is this?","a":"The midbrain.","options":["The midbrain","The cerebrum","The pons","The medulla oblongata"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The mesencephalon is the one primary brain vesicle that does not divide again, and it matures into the midbrain and the cerebral aqueduct running through it. The midbrain is the most superior region of the brainstem. Its name and the vesicle's name match, since mesencephalon means middle brain.","notes":["Correct. The mesencephalon matures into the midbrain.","The cerebrum develops from the telencephalon, the most anterior secondary vesicle. Pick it if the lesion were in a structure from the telencephalon.","The pons develops from the metencephalon, along with the cerebellum. It would be the answer for a lesion in a metencephalon structure.","The medulla oblongata develops from the myelencephalon, the most posterior vesicle. Choose it for a lesion in a myelencephalon structure."],"lt":"t-cns-brain","lid":"c30","comp":152},{"id":"L03166","dok":3,"q":"Damage to the cerebellum causes clumsy, poorly coordinated movement rather than paralysis. Which statement explains this?","a":"The cerebellum smooths and coordinates movements that other regions start.","options":["The cerebellum smooths and coordinates movements other regions start","The cerebellum relays sensory signals on to the cerebral cortex","The cerebellum sends the commands that start voluntary movement","The cerebellum holds the motor neurons that connect to skeletal muscle"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Voluntary movement is started by the primary motor cortex, and the signals reach muscles through motor neurons in the brainstem and spinal cord. The cerebellum works alongside this pathway, comparing the intended movement with what the body is actually doing and correcting the difference. Without it, the person can still move, but movements become jerky and balance suffers.","notes":["Correct. The cerebellum fine-tunes movement rather than starting it, so its loss causes clumsiness, not paralysis.","Relaying sensory signals to the cortex is the job of the thalamus in the diencephalon. Choose it for a question about the main sensory relay station.","Starting voluntary movement is the job of the primary motor cortex in the precentral gyrus. Damage there causes weakness or paralysis, so pick this for a question about that cortex.","Motor neurons that drive skeletal muscle sit in the anterior gray horn of the spinal cord and in cranial nerve nuclei of the brainstem. Their loss would cause paralysis, so pick this idea for a question about the anterior horn."],"lt":"t-cns-brain","lid":"d3-t-cns-brain-4","comp":158,"competencyId":"w7-cerebellum"},{"id":"L03167","dok":3,"q":"A small injury to the cerebral cortex is rarely fatal, but an injury of similar size in the medulla oblongata often is. What explains the difference?","a":"The medulla holds the centers that run the heartbeat, breathing, and blood pressure.","options":["It holds the hippocampus, which stores every long-term memory","It holds the primary motor cortex that starts all voluntary movement","It holds the centers that run heartbeat, breathing, and blood pressure","It produces nearly all of the cerebrospinal fluid that cushions the brain"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The medulla oblongata contains the cardiac center, the respiratory center, and the vasomotor center. These centers adjust the heartbeat, set the basic rhythm of breathing, and control blood vessel diameter, so they keep the body alive moment to moment. Losing a patch of cortex can cause a serious deficit, but losing these centers can stop breathing and circulation.","notes":["The hippocampus sits in the medial temporal lobe, not the medulla, and it forms new long-term memories rather than storing every memory. Damage to it causes memory loss, which is not immediately fatal.","The primary motor cortex is in the precentral gyrus of the frontal lobe, not the medulla. Damage there causes weakness, so pick it for a question about one-sided paralysis after a cortical stroke.","Correct. The medulla's cardiac, respiratory, and vasomotor centers make injury there immediately life-threatening.","Cerebrospinal fluid is made by the choroid plexus in the ventricles, not by the medulla itself. Choose the choroid plexus for a question about where CSF is made."],"lt":"t-cns-brain","lid":"d3-t-cns-brain-5","comp":164,"competencyId":"w7-medulla"},{"id":"L03178","dok":1,"q":"Which three regions make up the brainstem?","a":"The midbrain, pons, and medulla oblongata.","options":["The caudate, putamen, and globus pallidus","The cerebrum, cerebellum, and diencephalon","The midbrain, pons, and medulla oblongata","The thalamus, hypothalamus, and epithalamus"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The brainstem is the stalk that connects the cerebrum, diencephalon, and cerebellum to the spinal cord. From top to bottom it is made of the midbrain, the pons, and the medulla oblongata. Every ascending and descending tract passes through it, and it holds the nuclei of cranial nerves III through X and XII.","notes":["These are core basal ganglia buried deep in the cerebrum. They would be the answer for a question about the deep gray matter masses that regulate movement.","These are the other three major parts of the brain. Choose them if the question asks which major regions are not part of the brainstem.","Correct. The midbrain, pons, and medulla oblongata form the brainstem.","These three parts form the diencephalon, which sits above the brainstem around the third ventricle. Pick them if the question asks about the diencephalon."],"lt":"t-cns-brainstem","lid":"c1","comp":161,"competencyId":"w7-brainstem-regions"},{"id":"L03179","dok":1,"q":"Which region is the most superior part of the brainstem?","a":"The midbrain.","options":["The cerebellum","The medulla oblongata","The pons","The midbrain"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The brainstem runs from the midbrain at the top, through the pons in the middle, to the medulla oblongata at the bottom. The midbrain, also called the mesencephalon, is short and sits just below the diencephalon. The cerebral aqueduct runs through it.","notes":["The cerebellum sits behind the brainstem and attaches to it by the cerebellar peduncles, but it is not part of the brainstem. Choose it for a question about coordination of movement.","The medulla oblongata is the most inferior region of the brainstem and merges into the spinal cord. It would be the answer for the lowest region.","The pons is the rounded middle region of the brainstem. Pick it if the question asks for the middle region.","Correct. The midbrain is the most superior region of the brainstem."],"lt":"t-cns-brainstem","lid":"c2","comp":161,"competencyId":"w7-brainstem-regions"},{"id":"L03180","dok":1,"q":"Which region is the most inferior part of the brainstem?","a":"The medulla oblongata.","options":["The pons","The midbrain","The medulla oblongata","The tectum"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The medulla oblongata is the lowest region of the brainstem. It is continuous with the spinal cord at the foramen magnum. It holds the pyramids and the vital centers for heart rate, breathing, and blood vessel diameter.","notes":["The pons is the middle region of the brainstem, between the midbrain and the medulla. It would be the answer for the middle region.","The midbrain is the most superior region of the brainstem, just below the diencephalon. Pick it if the question asks for the highest region.","Correct. The medulla oblongata is the most inferior region of the brainstem.","The tectum is the roof of the midbrain, made of the four colliculi, so it sits near the top of the brainstem. Choose it for a question about the colliculi."],"lt":"t-cns-brainstem","lid":"c3","comp":161,"competencyId":"w7-brainstem-regions"},{"id":"L03181","dok":1,"q":"What is the roof of the midbrain, made of four rounded bumps, called?","a":"The tectum.","options":["The pyramids","The vermis","The cerebral peduncles","The tectum"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Tectum means roof. It forms the posterior surface of the midbrain and is made of four rounded bumps called colliculi, an upper pair and a lower pair. The superior colliculi serve visual reflexes and the inferior colliculi serve auditory reflexes.","notes":["The pyramids are a pair of ridges on the front of the medulla formed by the descending corticospinal tracts. Pick them for a question about the front surface of the medulla.","The vermis is the narrow midline band connecting the two cerebellar hemispheres. It would be the answer for a question about the middle of the cerebellum.","The cerebral peduncles are the paired stalks on the front of the midbrain that carry descending motor tracts. Choose them if the question asks about the front of the midbrain rather than its roof.","Correct. The tectum is the roof of the midbrain, made of the four colliculi."],"lt":"t-cns-brainstem","lid":"c4","comp":162,"competencyId":"w7-midbrain"},{"id":"L03182","dok":1,"q":"The superior colliculi of the midbrain are centers for which kind of reflex?","a":"Visual reflexes.","options":["Visual reflexes","Withdrawal reflexes","Auditory reflexes","Respiratory reflexes"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The superior colliculi are the upper pair of bumps on the roof of the midbrain. They are reflex centers that turn the eyes and head toward something seen, such as a sudden movement. They are not where you become aware of the sight; that happens in the occipital cortex.","notes":["Correct. The superior colliculi are visual reflex centers.","Withdrawal reflexes, such as pulling a hand off a hot stove, are spinal reflexes handled in the gray matter of the spinal cord. It would be the answer for a question about the reflex arc.","Auditory reflexes, such as turning toward a sound, are handled by the inferior colliculi, the lower pair. Pick this if the question asks about the inferior colliculi.","The respiratory center that sets breathing rhythm is in the medulla oblongata. Choose this for a question about the medullary vital centers."],"lt":"t-cns-brainstem","lid":"c5","comp":162,"competencyId":"w7-midbrain"},{"id":"L03183","dok":1,"q":"The inferior colliculi of the midbrain are centers for which kind of reflex?","a":"Auditory reflexes.","options":["Cough reflexes","Auditory reflexes","Stretch reflexes","Visual reflexes"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The inferior colliculi are the lower pair of bumps on the tectum, the roof of the midbrain. They serve auditory reflexes, such as turning the head toward a sudden sound. A handy way to keep the pairs straight is upper for eyes, lower for ears.","notes":["The cough reflex is coordinated in the medulla oblongata, which also handles swallowing and vomiting. It would be the answer for a question about protective airway reflexes.","Correct. The inferior colliculi are auditory reflex centers.","Stretch reflexes, such as the knee-jerk, are spinal reflexes completed in the spinal cord. Choose this for a question about a reflex arc through the cord.","Visual reflexes are handled by the superior colliculi, the upper pair. Pick this if the question asks about the superior colliculi."],"lt":"t-cns-brainstem","lid":"c6","comp":162,"competencyId":"w7-midbrain"},{"id":"L03184","dok":1,"q":"Which brainstem region has a name that means bridge?","a":"The pons.","options":["The midbrain","The tectum","The pons","The medulla oblongata"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Pons is Latin for bridge. This rounded middle region of the brainstem bridges the medulla, the midbrain, and the cerebellum. Its pontine nuclei relay motor information from the cerebral cortex across to the cerebellum through the middle cerebellar peduncles.","notes":["The midbrain, or mesencephalon, means middle brain. Pick it for a question about the most superior region of the brainstem.","Tectum means roof, and it is the roof of the midbrain. Choose it for a question about the four colliculi.","Correct. Pons means bridge.","The medulla oblongata means oblong marrow, and it is the lowest brainstem region. It would be the answer for a question about the region continuous with the spinal cord."],"lt":"t-cns-brainstem","lid":"c7","comp":161,"competencyId":"w7-brainstem-regions"},{"id":"L03185","dok":1,"q":"What is the pair of ridges on the front of the medulla, formed by the descending corticospinal tracts, called?","a":"The pyramids.","options":["The pontine nuclei","The colliculi","The cerebral peduncles","The pyramids"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The pyramids are two raised ridges on the anterior surface of the medulla oblongata. They are formed by the corticospinal tracts, the motor fibers running from the cerebral cortex down to the spinal cord. At the lower end of the medulla, most of these fibers cross to the other side at the decussation of the pyramids.","notes":["The pontine nuclei are gray matter clusters in the pons that relay motor information to the cerebellum. Choose them for a question about that relay.","The colliculi are the four bumps on the roof of the midbrain that serve visual and auditory reflexes. Pick them for a question about the tectum.","The cerebral peduncles are stalks on the front of the midbrain that also carry descending motor tracts. They would be the answer if the question asked about the front of the midbrain instead of the medulla.","Correct. The pyramids are the corticospinal ridges on the front of the medulla."],"lt":"t-cns-brainstem","lid":"c8","comp":164,"competencyId":"w7-medulla"},{"id":"L03186","dok":1,"q":"In which brainstem region do most corticospinal fibers cross to the opposite side?","a":"The medulla oblongata.","options":["The midbrain","The medulla oblongata","The cerebellum","The pons"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The corticospinal tracts form the pyramids on the front of the medulla. Near the lower end of the medulla, most of these fibers cross the midline at the decussation of the pyramids. This crossing is why each cerebral hemisphere controls movement on the opposite side of the body.","notes":["The midbrain carries the corticospinal fibers in its cerebral peduncles, but they have not crossed yet. Pick the midbrain for a question about the cerebral peduncles.","Correct. Most corticospinal fibers cross at the decussation of the pyramids in the medulla.","The cerebellum is not part of the brainstem and does not carry the corticospinal tracts. It would be the answer for a question about coordinating movement.","The pons carries the corticospinal fibers downward but does not host the crossover. Choose it for a question about the pontine nuclei relaying motor signals to the cerebellum."],"lt":"t-cns-brainstem","lid":"c9","comp":164,"competencyId":"w7-medulla"},{"id":"L03187","dok":1,"q":"Which center in the medulla oblongata sets the basic rhythm of breathing?","a":"The respiratory center.","options":["The cardiac center","The respiratory center","The pontine nuclei","The vasomotor center"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The medulla oblongata holds three vital centers. The respiratory center sets the basic rhythm of breathing, so breathing continues automatically even during sleep. The cardiac and vasomotor centers sit nearby and handle the heart and blood vessels.","notes":["The cardiac center adjusts the rate and force of the heartbeat. Pick it for a question about medullary control of the heart.","Correct. The respiratory center of the medulla sets the basic breathing rhythm.","The pontine nuclei are gray matter clusters in the pons that relay motor information from the cortex to the cerebellum. They would be the answer for a question about that relay.","The vasomotor center adjusts the diameter of blood vessels and so blood pressure. Choose it for a question about blood vessel diameter."],"lt":"t-cns-brainstem","lid":"c10","comp":164,"competencyId":"w7-medulla"},{"id":"L03188","dok":1,"q":"Which center in the medulla oblongata adjusts the diameter of blood vessels?","a":"The vasomotor center.","options":["The pontine nuclei","The vasomotor center","The cardiac center","The respiratory center"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The vasomotor center of the medulla controls how wide or narrow blood vessels are. Narrowing vessels raises blood pressure and widening them lowers it, so this center helps regulate blood pressure. The word vasomotor literally means vessel mover.","notes":["The pontine nuclei are in the pons and relay motor signals to the cerebellum. Choose them for a question about cortex-to-cerebellum relay.","Correct. The vasomotor center adjusts blood vessel diameter.","The cardiac center adjusts the rate and force of the heartbeat. Pick it if the question asks about heart rate rather than vessel diameter.","The respiratory center sets the basic rhythm of breathing. It would be the answer for a question about breathing rhythm."],"lt":"t-cns-brainstem","lid":"c11","comp":164,"competencyId":"w7-medulla"},{"id":"L03189","dok":1,"q":"What is the network of gray matter neurons spread through the core of the entire brainstem called?","a":"The reticular formation.","options":["The reticular formation","The tectum","The substantia nigra","The pontine nuclei"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The reticular formation is not a single nucleus. It is a net of gray matter cell bodies woven through the white matter of the midbrain, pons, and medulla. Its ascending part, the reticular activating system, keeps the cortex awake, and its descending part helps set resting muscle tone.","notes":["Correct. The reticular formation is the network of neurons running through the core of the brainstem.","The tectum is the roof of the midbrain, made of the four colliculi. Pick it for a question about the visual and auditory reflex centers.","The substantia nigra is a single, darkly pigmented nucleus of the midbrain that helps regulate movement. Choose it for a question about Parkinson disease.","The pontine nuclei are clusters of gray matter limited to the pons that relay motor signals to the cerebellum. They would be the answer for that relay."],"lt":"t-cns-brainstem","lid":"c12","comp":166,"competencyId":"w7-reticular-formation"},{"id":"L03190","dok":1,"q":"Which darkly pigmented midbrain nucleus helps regulate movement along with the basal ganglia?","a":"The substantia nigra.","options":["The superior colliculi","The pontine nuclei","The substantia nigra","The cardiac center"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Substantia nigra means black substance, named for the dark pigment in its neurons. This midbrain nucleus is grouped with the basal ganglia by function and helps start and smooth movement. Its loss is the basis of Parkinson disease.","notes":["The superior colliculi are bumps on the roof of the midbrain that serve visual reflexes. Choose them for a question about turning the eyes toward a sight.","The pontine nuclei lie in the pons and relay motor information from the cortex to the cerebellum. Pick them for a question about that relay.","Correct. The substantia nigra is the darkly pigmented midbrain nucleus that works with the basal ganglia.","The cardiac center is in the medulla oblongata and adjusts the heartbeat. It would be the answer for a question about medullary control of heart rate."],"lt":"t-cns-brainstem","lid":"c13","comp":162,"competencyId":"w7-midbrain"},{"id":"L03191","dok":1,"q":"What is the ascending part of the reticular formation that keeps the cerebral cortex awake and alert called?","a":"The reticular activating system.","options":["The superior colliculi","The reticular activating system","The substantia nigra","The vasomotor center"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The reticular activating system, or RAS, is the ascending part of the reticular formation. It sends a constant stream of signals up to the cortex that keeps it awake, alert, and attentive, and it filters out unimportant stimuli. When the RAS is turned down, the result is sleep, and severe damage to it can cause coma.","notes":["The superior colliculi are visual reflex centers on the roof of the midbrain. They would be the answer for a question about turning toward a sight.","Correct. The reticular activating system is the ascending arousal system of the reticular formation.","The substantia nigra is a pigmented midbrain nucleus that helps regulate movement. Choose it for a question about Parkinson disease.","The vasomotor center is a medullary center that adjusts blood vessel diameter. Pick it for a question about blood pressure control."],"lt":"t-cns-brainstem","lid":"c14","comp":166,"competencyId":"w7-reticular-formation"},{"id":"L03193","dok":2,"q":"The brainstem holds the nuclei for which cranial nerves?","a":"Cranial nerves III through X and XII.","options":["Cranial nerves III through X and XII","All twelve cranial nerves","Cranial nerves I through VI","Cranial nerves IX, X, and XII only"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Cranial nerves I and II connect to the forebrain, so they have no brainstem nucleus. The midbrain holds the nuclei of III and IV, the pons holds V through VIII, and the medulla holds IX, X, and XII, with the VIII nuclei sitting at the junction of the pons and medulla. The nucleus of XI sits just below the brainstem, in the upper cervical spinal cord.","notes":["Correct. The nuclei of III through X and XII sit in the brainstem, and the XI nucleus sits just below it in the upper cervical cord.","This forgets that CN I and CN II connect to the forebrain, so they have no brainstem nucleus, and that the CN XI nucleus sits in the upper cervical cord.","This set includes CN I and II, which connect to the forebrain, and leaves out VII through X and XII, which do have brainstem nuclei.","These are the nerves with nuclei in the medulla, but the midbrain and pons also hold nuclei, for III through VIII. Pick this set if the question asked about the medulla alone."],"lt":"t-cns-brainstem","lid":"c16","comp":165,"competencyId":"w7-cranial-nerve-nuclei"},{"id":"L03194","dok":2,"q":"The nuclei for the oculomotor nerve (CN III) and the trochlear nerve (CN IV) lie in which brainstem region?","a":"The midbrain.","options":["The midbrain","The pons","The cerebellum","The medulla oblongata"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Cranial nerve nuclei are stacked in the brainstem in roughly the same order as the nerve numbers, from top to bottom. The midbrain, the highest region, holds the nuclei for CN III and CN IV. Below it, the pons holds V through VIII, and the medulla holds IX, X, and XII.","notes":["Correct. The oculomotor and trochlear nuclei sit in the midbrain.","The pons holds the nuclei for CN V, VI, VII, and VIII. Pick it if the question asks about the trigeminal, abducens, or facial nerve nuclei.","The cerebellum is not part of the brainstem and holds no cranial nerve nuclei. Choose it for a question about coordinating movement.","The medulla oblongata holds the nuclei for CN IX, X, and XII, lower in the brainstem than III and IV. It would be the answer for the glossopharyngeal, vagus, or hypoglossal nuclei."],"lt":"t-cns-brainstem","lid":"c17","comp":165,"competencyId":"w7-cranial-nerve-nuclei"},{"id":"L03195","dok":2,"q":"An injury to the medulla oblongata leaves a patient unable to regulate heart rate and breathing. Which centers were damaged?","a":"The cardiac and respiratory centers.","options":["The pontine nuclei","The cardiac and respiratory centers","The cerebral peduncles","The superior and inferior colliculi"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The medulla oblongata holds three vital centers. The cardiac center adjusts the rate and force of the heartbeat, and the respiratory center sets the basic rhythm of breathing. The vasomotor center beside them controls blood vessel diameter, which is why injury to the medulla is so dangerous.","notes":["The pontine nuclei lie in the pons and relay motor information from the cortex to the cerebellum. Choose them for a question about that relay.","Correct. The cardiac and respiratory centers of the medulla control the heartbeat and breathing.","The cerebral peduncles are stalks on the front of the midbrain that carry descending motor tracts. They would be the answer for a question about the front of the midbrain.","The colliculi are the visual and auditory reflex centers on the roof of the midbrain. Pick them for a question about turning toward a sight or sound."],"lt":"t-cns-brainstem","lid":"c18","comp":164,"competencyId":"w7-medulla"},{"id":"L03196","dok":2,"q":"Which structures relay motor information from the cerebral cortex to the cerebellum?","a":"The pontine nuclei.","options":["The substantia nigra","The colliculi","The pontine nuclei","The pyramids"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The pontine nuclei are clusters of gray matter in the pons. They receive motor information from the cerebral cortex and pass it to the cerebellum through the thick middle cerebellar peduncles. This lets the cerebellum compare what the cortex intends with what the body is doing.","notes":["The substantia nigra is a pigmented midbrain nucleus that works with the basal ganglia to regulate movement. It would be the answer for a question about Parkinson disease.","The colliculi are reflex centers on the roof of the midbrain for vision and hearing. Choose them for a question about the tectum.","Correct. The pontine nuclei relay motor information from the cortex to the cerebellum.","The pyramids are ridges on the front of the medulla formed by the corticospinal tracts heading to the spinal cord, not the cerebellum. Pick them for a question about the front of the medulla."],"lt":"t-cns-brainstem","lid":"c19","comp":163,"competencyId":"w7-pons"},{"id":"L03197","dok":2,"q":"A stroke in the right cerebral hemisphere weakens the left side of the body. Which brainstem feature explains this crossover?","a":"The decussation of the pyramids.","options":["The reticular activating system","The internal capsule","The corpus callosum","The decussation of the pyramids"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The corticospinal motor fibers run down from the cortex and form the pyramids on the front of the medulla. At the lower medulla, most of them cross to the opposite side at the decussation of the pyramids. By the time they reach the spinal cord they serve the opposite side of the body, so right-sided brain damage weakens the left side.","notes":["The reticular activating system is the ascending part of the reticular formation that keeps the cortex awake. Choose it for a question about consciousness or coma.","The internal capsule carries the motor fibers from the cortex toward the brainstem, but they are still on the same side there. It would be the answer for a small stroke that weakens a whole side of the body.","The corpus callosum crosses the midline, but it connects the two hemispheres to each other rather than carrying motor commands to the body. Pick it for a question about communication between hemispheres.","Correct. The decussation of the pyramids is where most motor fibers cross to the opposite side."],"lt":"t-cns-brainstem","lid":"c20","comp":164,"competencyId":"w7-medulla"},{"id":"L03198","dok":2,"q":"The nuclei for cranial nerves V, VI, VII, and VIII are found mainly in which brainstem region?","a":"The pons.","options":["The pons","The medulla oblongata","The midbrain","The cerebellum"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The pons holds the nuclei for the trigeminal (V), abducens (VI), facial (VII), and vestibulocochlear (VIII) nerves. The CN VIII nuclei sit at the junction of the pons and medulla, so VIII is listed with both regions. The order follows the numbers: III and IV above in the midbrain, V through VIII in the pons, and IX, X, and XII below in the medulla.","notes":["Correct. The pons holds the nuclei for CN V through VIII.","The medulla oblongata holds the nuclei for CN IX, X, and XII and shares CN VIII with the pons, so it does not hold V, VI, or VII. Choose it for the glossopharyngeal, vagus, or hypoglossal nerves.","The midbrain holds the nuclei for CN III and IV, the oculomotor and trochlear nerves. Pick it for those two nerves.","The cerebellum is behind the brainstem and holds no cranial nerve nuclei. It would be the answer for a question about coordination."],"lt":"t-cns-brainstem","lid":"c21","comp":165,"competencyId":"w7-cranial-nerve-nuclei"},{"id":"L03199","dok":2,"q":"Which paired stalks on the front of the midbrain carry descending motor tracts?","a":"The cerebral peduncles.","options":["The cerebral peduncles","The colliculi","The middle cerebellar peduncles","The pyramids"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The cerebral peduncles are two thick stalks on the anterior side of the midbrain. They carry the descending motor tracts from the cerebral cortex down toward the pons, medulla, and spinal cord. Lower down, many of these same fibers form the pyramids of the medulla.","notes":["Correct. The cerebral peduncles are the motor stalks on the front of the midbrain.","The colliculi are the four bumps on the back of the midbrain that serve visual and auditory reflexes. They would be the answer for a question about the tectum.","The middle cerebellar peduncles are thick stalks that connect the pons to the cerebellum and carry input from the pontine nuclei. Choose them for a question about how the pons talks to the cerebellum.","The pyramids also carry descending corticospinal fibers, but they are ridges on the front of the medulla, not the midbrain. Pick them for a question about the medulla."],"lt":"t-cns-brainstem","lid":"c22","comp":162,"competencyId":"w7-midbrain"},{"id":"L03200","dok":2,"q":"Which function is the reticular activating system responsible for?","a":"Maintaining consciousness and arousal.","options":["Maintaining consciousness and arousal","Coordinating balance and posture","Producing cerebrospinal fluid","Relaying sensory signals to the cortex"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The reticular activating system, or RAS, is the ascending part of the reticular formation in the brainstem. It keeps the cortex awake, holds attention, and filters out unimportant stimuli so the brain is not overloaded. When its activity drops, a person falls asleep, and severe damage to it can cause coma.","notes":["Correct. The RAS maintains consciousness and arousal.","Balance and posture are coordinated by the cerebellum. Pick this for a question about cerebellar function.","Cerebrospinal fluid is produced by the choroid plexus in the ventricles. Choose this for a question about the choroid plexus.","Relaying sensory signals to the cortex is the job of the thalamus. It would be the answer for a question about the main sensory relay station."],"lt":"t-cns-brainstem","lid":"c23","comp":166,"competencyId":"w7-reticular-formation"},{"id":"L03202","dok":2,"q":"Which brainstem region is continuous with the spinal cord at the foramen magnum?","a":"The medulla oblongata.","options":["The midbrain","The pons","The cerebellum","The medulla oblongata"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The medulla oblongata is the lowest region of the brainstem. At the level of the foramen magnum, the large opening in the occipital bone, it merges smoothly into the spinal cord. The fourth ventricle behind it continues downward as the central canal of the cord.","notes":["The midbrain is the most superior region of the brainstem, just below the diencephalon. Pick it for a question about the top of the brainstem.","The pons is the middle region of the brainstem, above the medulla. It would be the answer for the region that bridges to the cerebellum.","The cerebellum sits behind the brainstem in the posterior cranial fossa and does not continue into the spinal cord. Choose it for a question about coordination.","Correct. The medulla oblongata merges into the spinal cord at the foramen magnum."],"lt":"t-cns-brainstem","lid":"c25","comp":161,"competencyId":"w7-brainstem-regions"},{"id":"L03203","dok":3,"q":"A clinician finds the abducens nerve (CN VI) weak and looks for the lesion in the brainstem region that holds its nucleus. Which region is that?","a":"The pons.","options":["The medulla oblongata","The pons","The midbrain","The cerebellum"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Each cranial nerve has a fixed nucleus, so testing the nerves one at a time helps localize a brainstem problem. The abducens nucleus sits in the pons, along with the nuclei for CN V, VII, and VIII. A weak CN VI therefore points the clinician to the pons.","notes":["The medulla oblongata holds the nuclei for CN IX, X, and XII, not the abducens nucleus. It would be the answer if the weak nerve were the vagus or hypoglossal nerve.","Correct. The abducens nucleus lies in the pons.","The midbrain holds the nuclei for CN III and IV. Pick it if the weak nerve were the oculomotor or trochlear nerve.","The cerebellum is not part of the brainstem and has no cranial nerve nuclei. Choose it for a problem with coordination and balance."],"lt":"t-cns-brainstem","lid":"c26","comp":165,"competencyId":"w7-cranial-nerve-nuclei"},{"id":"L03204","dok":3,"q":"A patient loses a large area of cerebral cortex but stays awake. Later, a small brainstem injury drops the patient into a coma. Which system did the brainstem injury most likely damage?","a":"The reticular activating system.","options":["The basal ganglia","The vasomotor center","The reticular activating system","The cerebral peduncles"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Staying awake depends on the reticular activating system, the ascending part of the reticular formation in the brainstem. It sends a steady stream of arousing signals up to the cortex. A person can lose much of the cortex and remain awake, but damage to this small ascending network can withdraw that arousal and cause coma.","notes":["The basal ganglia are deep cerebral nuclei that help start and regulate movement. Damage causes movement disorders, so pick them for a question about Parkinson-like signs.","The vasomotor center in the medulla adjusts blood vessel diameter and blood pressure. Choose it for a question about blood pressure control.","Correct. Damage to the reticular activating system can cause coma.","The cerebral peduncles carry descending motor tracts on the front of the midbrain. Damage there causes weakness, so they would be the answer for a motor problem, not loss of consciousness."],"lt":"t-cns-brainstem","lid":"c27","comp":166,"competencyId":"w7-reticular-formation"},{"id":"L03205","dok":3,"q":"A patient develops tremor, rigid muscles, and slow movements. The cause is loss of neurons in a darkly pigmented nucleus of the midbrain. Which structure is this?","a":"The substantia nigra.","options":["The superior colliculi","The substantia nigra","The respiratory center","The pontine nuclei"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Tremor, rigidity, and slow movement are the classic signs of Parkinson disease. Its basis is the loss of neurons in the substantia nigra, a dark-pigmented midbrain nucleus that works with the basal ganglia to start and smooth movement. Without it, the basal ganglia circuit cannot regulate movement normally.","notes":["The superior colliculi are visual reflex centers on the roof of the midbrain. They would be the answer for a question about turning the eyes toward a flash of light.","Correct. Loss of the substantia nigra is the basis of Parkinson disease.","The respiratory center is in the medulla and sets the breathing rhythm. Choose it for a question about breathing control.","The pontine nuclei are in the pons and relay motor information to the cerebellum. Pick them for a question about the cortex-to-cerebellum relay."],"lt":"t-cns-brainstem","lid":"c28","comp":162,"competencyId":"w7-midbrain"},{"id":"L03206","dok":3,"q":"An injury to one small brainstem region is immediately life-threatening because that region holds the centers for the heartbeat and breathing. Which region is it?","a":"The medulla oblongata.","options":["The pons","The tectum","The medulla oblongata","The midbrain"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The cardiac, respiratory, and vasomotor centers all sit in the medulla oblongata. They adjust the rate and force of the heartbeat, set the basic rhythm of breathing, and adjust blood vessel diameter, all without conscious effort. Damage here can stop breathing and disrupt circulation, which is why a medullary injury is so often fatal.","notes":["The pons bridges to the cerebellum and holds the nuclei for CN V through VIII. It would be the answer for a weak abducens or facial nerve.","The tectum is the roof of the midbrain with the four colliculi. Choose it for a question about visual and auditory reflex centers.","Correct. The medulla oblongata holds the vital cardiac and respiratory centers.","The midbrain holds the colliculi, the cerebral peduncles, the substantia nigra, and the nuclei for CN III and IV. Pick it for a question about visual reflexes or Parkinson disease."],"lt":"t-cns-brainstem","lid":"c29","comp":164,"competencyId":"w7-medulla"},{"id":"L03207","dok":3,"q":"A sudden bright flash makes a person turn the head and eyes toward it before they even think about it. Which midbrain structure handles this reflex?","a":"The superior colliculi.","options":["The superior colliculi","The inferior colliculi","The substantia nigra","The cerebral peduncles"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The superior colliculi, the upper pair of bumps on the roof of the midbrain, are visual reflex centers. They turn the eyes and head toward something seen without any conscious decision. Becoming aware of what you are looking at happens later, in the visual cortex of the occipital lobe.","notes":["Correct. The superior colliculi mediate visual reflexes.","The inferior colliculi handle auditory reflexes, turning the head toward a sudden sound. Pick them if the trigger were a loud noise instead of a flash.","The substantia nigra is a pigmented midbrain nucleus that helps regulate movement with the basal ganglia. Choose it for a question about Parkinson disease.","The cerebral peduncles carry descending motor tracts on the front of the midbrain. They would be the answer for a question about the motor stalks of the midbrain."],"lt":"t-cns-brainstem","lid":"c30","comp":162,"competencyId":"w7-midbrain"},{"id":"L03208","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly describes the tracts that pass through the brainstem?","a":"Every ascending and descending tract between the brain and spinal cord passes through it.","options":["No long tracts pass through; it holds only cranial nerve nuclei","Only ascending sensory tracts pass through; motor tracts bypass it","Only descending motor tracts pass through; sensory tracts bypass it","All tracts between the brain and spinal cord pass through it"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The brainstem is the stalk that connects the cerebrum, diencephalon, and cerebellum to the spinal cord. Every ascending sensory tract and every descending motor tract traveling between the brain and the body passes through it. That is why even a small brainstem lesion can interrupt many functions at once.","notes":["The brainstem does hold cranial nerve nuclei, but long tracts also pass through it. This option forgets structures like the pyramids that are made of passing tracts.","Motor tracts, such as the corticospinal tracts forming the pyramids, run down through the brainstem. This option ignores the pyramids and cerebral peduncles.","Sensory tracts also pass through the brainstem on their way up to the thalamus and cortex. This option would only fit if sensation reached the brain by some other route, which it does not.","Correct. All the ascending and descending tracts between the brain and the cord run through the brainstem."],"lt":"t-cns-brainstem","lid":"d3-t-cns-brainstem-1","comp":161,"competencyId":"w7-brainstem-regions"},{"id":"L03209","dok":3,"q":"Breathing continues during deep sleep and under general anesthesia even though the cerebral cortex is quiet. Which region keeps the basic breathing rhythm going?","a":"The medulla oblongata.","options":["The thalamus","The medulla oblongata","The primary motor cortex","The cerebellum"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The respiratory center that sets the basic rhythm of breathing lies in the medulla oblongata, below the level of conscious control. Because it works automatically, breathing goes on whether or not the cortex is active. You can override it for a short time, as when holding your breath, but the medulla takes back control.","notes":["The thalamus relays sensory signals to the cortex. It would be the answer for a question about the main sensory relay station.","Correct. The respiratory center of the medulla sets the breathing rhythm automatically.","The primary motor cortex can drive voluntary breathing, such as taking a deliberate deep breath, but it is quiet in deep sleep. Pick it for a question about starting voluntary movement.","The cerebellum coordinates movement, posture, and balance. Choose it for a question about clumsy, uncoordinated movement."],"lt":"t-cns-brainstem","lid":"d3-t-cns-brainstem-2","comp":164,"competencyId":"w7-medulla"},{"id":"L03211","dok":3,"q":"Most corticospinal fibers cross in the medulla before they enter the spinal cord. An injury damages the descending motor tracts in the left half of the cervical spinal cord. Which side of the body below the injury becomes weak?","a":"The left side.","options":["The left side","Both sides equally","The right side","Neither side"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The crossing happens at the decussation of the pyramids in the lower medulla, above the spinal cord. By the time the motor fibers run down the left half of the cord, they have already crossed and serve the left side of the body. Damage to the left half of the cord therefore weakens the left side below the injury, while a lesion above the crossing, such as in the left cerebrum, weakens the right side.","notes":["Correct. The fibers have already crossed in the medulla, so a left-sided cord injury weakens the left side below it.","Damage to both sides of the cord would weaken both sides. This injury involves only the left half, so only one side is affected.","Right-sided weakness would follow damage on the left above the crossing point, such as a stroke in the left motor cortex or internal capsule. This answer applies the crossover rule to a level where the fibers have already crossed.","Descending motor tracts do run through the spinal cord, so damaging them causes weakness. This option would only fit an injury that spared every tract."],"lt":"t-cns-brainstem","lid":"d3-t-cns-brainstem-4","comp":164,"competencyId":"w7-medulla"},{"id":"L03212","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares the reticular formation and the reticular activating system (RAS)?","a":"The RAS is the ascending part of the reticular formation and keeps the cortex alert.","options":["The reticular formation is a small part of the RAS found only in the midbrain","The RAS and the reticular formation are names for the same single nucleus","The RAS is the descending part of the reticular formation and sets resting muscle tone","The RAS is the ascending part of the reticular formation and keeps the cortex alert"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The reticular formation is a network of gray matter neurons running through the core of the midbrain, pons, and medulla. Its ascending part is the RAS, which keeps the cortex awake and filters incoming stimuli. Its descending part helps set the muscle tone of resting skeletal muscle.","notes":["This reverses the relationship, since the RAS is part of the reticular formation, not the other way around. The reticular formation also runs through the whole brainstem, not just the midbrain.","The reticular formation is a spread-out network, not a single nucleus, and the RAS is only one part of it. A single named nucleus would be something like the substantia nigra.","Setting resting muscle tone is the job of the descending part of the reticular formation, not the RAS. Pick this description for a question about the descending component.","Correct. The RAS is the ascending, arousal part of the reticular formation."],"lt":"t-cns-brainstem","lid":"d3-t-cns-brainstem-5","comp":166,"competencyId":"w7-reticular-formation"},{"id":"L03214","dok":3,"q":"A small brainstem stroke causes weakness of the arm and leg on one side plus weakness of the tongue. Why can one small brainstem lesion cause both kinds of signs?","a":"Cranial nerve nuclei sit right beside the long motor tracts passing through the brainstem.","options":["The brainstem holds the primary motor cortex for both limbs and tongue","Cranial nerve nuclei sit beside the long tracts passing through it","The tongue is served by spinal nerves from the cervical enlargement","The brainstem holds the anterior gray horns for the limbs and tongue"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The brainstem is crowded. The descending motor tracts to the limbs run through it, and the cranial nerve nuclei sit right beside them. A lesion in the medulla can therefore damage both the corticospinal fibers headed for the limbs and the hypoglossal nucleus that serves the tongue.","notes":["The primary motor cortex lies on the precentral gyrus of the frontal lobe, not in the brainstem. Pick it for a question about where voluntary movement is started.","Correct. Cranial nerve nuclei lie next to the passing long tracts, so one lesion can hit both.","The tongue is served by a cranial nerve, the hypoglossal (CN XII), not by spinal nerves. The cervical enlargement gives rise to the nerves of the upper limbs.","The anterior gray horns that drive limb muscles are in the spinal cord, not the brainstem. Choose them for a question about where somatic motor neurons for the limbs sit."],"lt":"t-cns-brainstem","lid":"d3x-t-cns-brainstem-2","comp":161,"competencyId":"w7-brainstem-regions"},{"id":"L03215","dok":2,"q":"During exercise, the heart speeds up without any conscious decision. Which center in the medulla oblongata adjusts the rate and force of the heartbeat?","a":"The cardiac center.","options":["The cardiac center","The reticular activating system","The vasomotor center","The respiratory center"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The cardiac center of the medulla adjusts how fast and how forcefully the heart beats. It works automatically, so the heart rate rises to meet demand during exercise and falls at rest without any conscious effort. Its neighbors in the medulla handle breathing rhythm and blood vessel diameter.","notes":["Correct. The cardiac center adjusts the rate and force of the heartbeat.","The reticular activating system keeps the cortex awake and alert. It would be the answer for a question about arousal and consciousness.","The vasomotor center adjusts the diameter of blood vessels and so blood pressure. Pick it for a question about vessel diameter.","The respiratory center sets the basic rhythm of breathing. Choose it for a question about breathing rhythm."],"lt":"t-cns-brainstem","lid":"d3x-t-cns-brainstem-3","comp":164,"competencyId":"w7-medulla"},{"id":"L04513","dok":1,"q":"Which list names the three parts of the diencephalon?","a":"The thalamus, hypothalamus, and epithalamus.","options":["Thalamus, hypothalamus, and cerebellum","Caudate nucleus, putamen, and globus pallidus","Thalamus, hypothalamus, and epithalamus","Midbrain, pons, and medulla oblongata"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The diencephalon is the deep region between the cerebrum and the brainstem, and it surrounds the third ventricle. It has three parts: the thalamus, which forms most of it and relays sensory signals; the hypothalamus below, which controls homeostasis; and the small epithalamus at the back, which includes the pineal gland.","notes":["The thalamus and hypothalamus are correct, but the cerebellum is a separate brain part behind the brainstem. The third part of the diencephalon is the epithalamus.","These are the core nuclei of the basal nuclei (basal ganglia), deep gray matter inside the cerebrum. Choose them for a question about the deep nuclei that regulate semi-voluntary movement.","Correct. The thalamus, hypothalamus, and epithalamus make up the diencephalon.","These are the three regions of the brainstem, which lies below the diencephalon. Pick this list if the question asked for the brainstem regions from superior to inferior."],"lt":"t-w7-diencephalon","lid":"w7-diencephalon-r1","comp":157,"competencyId":"w7-diencephalon"},{"id":"L04514","dok":1,"q":"Which description fits the thalamus?","a":"Paired gray matter masses forming most of the diencephalon; the main sensory relay station to the cortex.","options":["A region below it that controls hunger, thirst, and temperature","Darkly pigmented midbrain nuclei that help regulate movement","A small posterior region that holds the pineal gland","Paired gray masses that relay sensory signals to the cortex"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The thalamus is a pair of oval gray matter masses that make up about 80 percent of the diencephalon, joined in the middle by the interthalamic adhesion (massa intermedia). It is the main sensory relay station to the cortex: sensory signals stop in a thalamic nucleus before being sent on to the correct cortical area. The two thalami form the side walls of the third ventricle.","notes":["This describes the hypothalamus, the master control center for homeostasis. Pick it if the question asked which structure regulates body temperature, hunger, and thirst.","This describes the substantia nigra in the midbrain, which works with the basal nuclei. Choose it for a question about the structure whose loss causes Parkinson disease.","This describes the epithalamus, the third part of the diencephalon. It would be the answer for the structure that includes the melatonin-secreting pineal gland.","Correct. The thalamus is the main sensory relay station of the diencephalon."],"lt":"t-w7-diencephalon","lid":"w7-diencephalon-r2","comp":157,"competencyId":"w7-diencephalon"},{"id":"L04515","dok":1,"q":"Which gland is part of the epithalamus?","a":"The pineal gland.","options":["Adrenal gland","Pineal gland","Pituitary gland","Thyroid gland"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The epithalamus is the small posterior part of the diencephalon, forming part of the roof of the third ventricle. Its main structure is the pineal gland, an endocrine gland that secretes melatonin, a hormone tied to sleep-wake rhythms. The epithalamus also contains the habenular nuclei.","notes":["The adrenal glands sit on top of the kidneys in the abdomen, far from the diencephalon. They would be the answer for the glands with a cortex and medulla above each kidney.","Correct. The pineal gland is the endocrine gland of the epithalamus.","The pituitary gland hangs below the hypothalamus on a stalk and sits in the sella turcica of the sphenoid. Pick it if the question asked which gland the hypothalamus controls directly.","The thyroid gland is in the anterior neck, wrapped around the trachea below the larynx, so it is not part of the brain. Choose it for a question about the butterfly-shaped gland of the neck."],"lt":"t-w7-diencephalon","lid":"w7-diencephalon-r3","comp":157,"competencyId":"w7-diencephalon"},{"id":"L04516","dok":2,"q":"Body temperature, hunger, thirst, and sleep-wake rhythms are all regulated by which structure?","a":"The hypothalamus.","options":["Thalamus","Hypothalamus","Medulla oblongata","Cerebellum"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The hypothalamus, just below the thalamus, is the master control center for homeostasis, keeping the internal environment steady. It controls the autonomic nervous system, controls endocrine function through the pituitary, and regulates body temperature, hunger and thirst, and sleep-wake rhythms. It is small, but it manages many of the body's basic survival drives.","notes":["The thalamus relays sensory signals to the cerebral cortex. It sits right above the hypothalamus, which makes the two easy to confuse, but it is the answer for sensory relay, not for drives like hunger and thirst.","Correct. The hypothalamus regulates these homeostatic drives.","The medulla oblongata holds the vital centers that adjust heart rate, set the breathing rhythm, and adjust blood vessel diameter, not the centers for hunger, thirst, or temperature. It would be the answer for a question about those vital centers.","The cerebellum coordinates movement, posture, and balance. Pick it if the question asked which part keeps a person steady while walking."],"lt":"t-w7-diencephalon","lid":"w7-diencephalon-r4","comp":157,"competencyId":"w7-diencephalon"},{"id":"L04517","dok":2,"q":"Which brain structure links the nervous system to the endocrine system, and how does it do it?","a":"The hypothalamus, which controls the pituitary gland.","options":["The thalamus, which relays signals to the cortex","The pineal gland, which controls the pituitary gland","The pons, which relays signals to the cerebellum","The hypothalamus, which controls the pituitary gland"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The hypothalamus is neural tissue, yet it controls the pituitary gland that hangs beneath it on a stalk, and the pituitary in turn directs many other endocrine glands. This makes the hypothalamus the bridge between the nervous system and the endocrine system. It also controls the autonomic nervous system, so it can adjust the body through both nerves and hormones.","notes":["The thalamus does relay signals to the cortex, but it has no control over the pituitary. Pick it for a question about where sensory signals stop before reaching the cortex.","The pineal gland is an endocrine gland of the epithalamus that secretes melatonin, but it does not control the pituitary. It would be the answer for the gland in the diencephalon tied to sleep-wake rhythms.","The pons does relay motor information from the cortex to the cerebellum through its pontine nuclei, but that is a movement pathway, not an endocrine link. Choose it for a question about the brainstem bridge to the cerebellum.","Correct. Through its control of the pituitary, the hypothalamus connects the nervous and endocrine systems."],"lt":"t-w7-diencephalon","lid":"w7-diencephalon-r5","comp":157,"competencyId":"w7-diencephalon"},{"id":"L04518","dok":1,"q":"What is the main job of the cerebellum?","a":"It coordinates movement, posture, and balance.","options":["Coordinating movement, posture, and balance","Setting the basic rhythm of breathing","Relaying sensory signals to the cerebral cortex","Producing cerebrospinal fluid in the ventricles"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The cerebellum sits in the posterior cranial fossa behind the brainstem and below the occipital lobes. It does not start movement; instead it coordinates movements started by the cerebrum so they are smooth and accurate, and it maintains posture and balance. Damage to it causes clumsy, unsteady movement and poor balance.","notes":["Correct. The cerebellum coordinates movement and keeps posture and balance.","The respiratory center that sets the basic rhythm of breathing is in the medulla oblongata. It would be the answer for a question about the brainstem vital centers.","Relaying sensory signals to the cortex is the job of the thalamus in the diencephalon. Pick the thalamus if the question asked for the main sensory relay station.","CSF is produced by the choroid plexus, capillary networks inside the ventricles. Choose the choroid plexus if the question asked where cerebrospinal fluid is made."],"lt":"t-w7-cerebellum","lid":"w7-cerebellum-r1","comp":158,"competencyId":"w7-cerebellum"},{"id":"L04519","dok":1,"q":"What is the vermis of the cerebellum?","a":"The narrow midline band connecting the two cerebellar hemispheres.","options":["The fine, leaf-like folds of the cerebellar surface","The stalks of white matter joining it to the brainstem","The narrow midline band joining the two hemispheres","The branching white matter inside the cerebellum"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The cerebellum has two lateral hemispheres joined in the middle by a narrow strip called the vermis (the name means worm, for its segmented look). Like the hemispheres, the vermis has a folded gray matter cortex over white matter. It is a midline landmark when the cerebellum is viewed from above or behind.","notes":["This describes the folia, the cerebellar version of the cerebral gyri. They would be the answer for the thin parallel ridges on the cerebellar surface.","This describes the cerebellar peduncles, three pairs of white matter stalks. Choose them for a question about how the cerebellum connects to the midbrain, pons, and medulla.","Correct. The vermis is the worm-like midline band between the cerebellar hemispheres.","This describes the arbor vitae, the tree-like white matter seen when the cerebellum is cut down the middle. Pick it if the question asked what that white matter pattern is called."],"lt":"t-w7-cerebellum","lid":"w7-cerebellum-r2","comp":158,"competencyId":"w7-cerebellum"},{"id":"L04520","dok":1,"q":"What is the arbor vitae?","a":"The branching, tree-like pattern of cerebellar white matter.","options":["The network of capillaries in the ventricles that makes CSF","The tree-like pattern of white matter in the cerebellum","The midline strip joining the two cerebellar hemispheres","The thin outer layer of gray matter on the cerebellum"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Arbor vitae means tree of life. When the cerebellum is cut along the midline, its inner white matter branches out into the folds like the limbs of a tree, with gray matter cortex covering each branch. This white matter carries signals into and out of the cerebellar cortex.","notes":["This is the choroid plexus, found in the ventricles. Choose it if the question asked which structure makes cerebrospinal fluid.","Correct. The arbor vitae is the branching white matter of the cerebellum.","This is the vermis, the worm-like midline band between the hemispheres. It would be the answer for the structure joining the left and right halves of the cerebellum.","This is the cerebellar cortex, the outer gray matter that covers the folia. Pick it if the question asked where the cell bodies of the cerebellum sit at the surface."],"lt":"t-w7-cerebellum","lid":"w7-cerebellum-r3","comp":158,"competencyId":"w7-cerebellum"},{"id":"L04521","dok":2,"q":"How many pairs of cerebellar peduncles connect the cerebellum to the brainstem, and what are they called?","a":"Three pairs: superior, middle, and inferior cerebellar peduncles.","options":["Two pairs: anterior and posterior","Three pairs: superior, middle, and inferior","Four pairs: one per brainstem region plus the cord","Three pairs: anterior, lateral, and posterior"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Three pairs of cerebellar peduncles, thick stalks of white matter, attach the cerebellum to each level of the brainstem. The superior peduncles connect to the midbrain, the middle peduncles to the pons, and the inferior peduncles to the medulla. Their names follow the brainstem regions from top to bottom.","notes":["There are three pairs, not two, because each of the three brainstem regions (midbrain, pons, and medulla) has its own connection. Anterior and posterior labels do not match how the peduncles are named.","Correct. Superior, middle, and inferior peduncles connect to the midbrain, pons, and medulla.","There is no fourth pair to the spinal cord. Signals from the cord reach the cerebellum by way of the inferior peduncle at the medulla, so three pairs cover all the connections.","The count is right, but the names are not. The peduncles are named by vertical position (superior, middle, inferior), matching the midbrain, pons, and medulla they attach to."],"lt":"t-w7-cerebellum","lid":"w7-cerebellum-r4","comp":158,"competencyId":"w7-cerebellum"},{"id":"L04522","dok":2,"q":"Which cerebellar peduncle carries mostly output from the cerebellum, and where does it connect?","a":"The superior cerebellar peduncle, connecting to the midbrain.","options":["Superior peduncle, connecting to the midbrain","Inferior peduncle, connecting to the spinal cord","Inferior peduncle, connecting to the medulla","Middle peduncle, connecting to the pons"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The superior cerebellar peduncles connect the cerebellum to the midbrain and carry mostly output, the signals the cerebellum sends out to adjust movement. The middle and inferior peduncles mainly bring information in. Remembering that most output leaves through the top pair makes the three easy to sort.","notes":["Correct. The superior peduncle connects to the midbrain and carries mostly output.","No peduncle attaches directly to the spinal cord. Signals from the cord enter through the inferior peduncle at the medulla, so this choice comes from mixing up where the inferior peduncle attaches.","The inferior peduncles do connect to the medulla, but they mostly carry input from the spinal cord and the vestibular (balance) system. They would be the answer for the peduncle that brings in body position and balance signals.","The middle peduncles connect to the pons, but they carry input, the signals from the pontine nuclei that relay motor plans from the cortex. Pick them if the question asked which peduncle brings cortical information into the cerebellum."],"lt":"t-w7-cerebellum","lid":"w7-cerebellum-r5","comp":158,"competencyId":"w7-cerebellum"},{"id":"L04528","dok":1,"q":"Which list gives the three regions of the brainstem in order from superior to inferior?","a":"Midbrain, pons, medulla oblongata.","options":["Midbrain, pons, medulla oblongata","Medulla oblongata, pons, midbrain","Pons, midbrain, medulla oblongata","Thalamus, midbrain, pons"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The brainstem has three regions stacked from top to bottom. The midbrain is the short, most superior region just below the diencephalon, the pons is the rounded middle region that bulges forward, and the medulla oblongata is the most inferior region, continuous with the spinal cord at the foramen magnum.","notes":["Correct. Midbrain on top, pons in the middle, and medulla at the bottom.","This is the correct list in the wrong direction, from inferior to superior. Pick this order if the question asked to list the regions starting from the spinal cord.","The pons sits below the midbrain, not above it. The midbrain is the top of the brainstem, directly beneath the diencephalon.","The thalamus is part of the diencephalon, above the brainstem, and the medulla is missing from this list. It mixes the diencephalon with the brainstem."],"lt":"t-w7-brainstem-regions","lid":"w7-brainstem-regions-r1","comp":161,"competencyId":"w7-brainstem-regions"},{"id":"L04529","dok":1,"q":"Which statement correctly describes where the cranial nerve nuclei sit?","a":"III through X and XII are in the brainstem; XI is in the upper cervical cord.","options":["VII through XII are in the brainstem; III through VI are in the diencephalon","All twelve are in the brainstem, from the midbrain down to the medulla","III through X and XII are in the brainstem; XI is in the upper cervical cord","I and II are in the brainstem; the other ten are in the spinal cord"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Cranial nerves I and II connect to the forebrain, so they have no brainstem nucleus. The nuclei of III through X and XII sit in the brainstem: III and IV in the midbrain, V through VIII in the pons, and IX, X, and XII in the medulla, with the VIII nuclei at the junction of the pons and medulla. The nucleus of XI sits just below the brainstem, in the upper cervical spinal cord.","notes":["The nuclei of III through VI sit in the midbrain and pons, not the diencephalon, and the XI nucleus sits in the upper cervical cord rather than the brainstem. This choice places the upper nerves one region too high.","CN I and II connect to the forebrain and have no brainstem nucleus, and the XI nucleus sits just below the brainstem in the upper cervical cord. So not all twelve sit in the brainstem.","Correct. The nuclei of III through X and XII are in the brainstem, and the XI nucleus is just below it in the upper cervical cord.","This turns the pattern upside down. CN I and II are the two nerves with no brainstem nucleus, and only the XI nucleus sits in the spinal cord."],"lt":"t-w7-brainstem-regions","lid":"w7-brainstem-regions-r2","comp":165,"competencyId":"w7-cranial-nerve-nuclei"},{"id":"L04530","dok":1,"q":"Which brainstem region is continuous with the spinal cord?","a":"The medulla oblongata.","options":["Midbrain","Medulla oblongata","Pons","Conus medullaris"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The medulla oblongata is the most inferior region of the brainstem. It narrows and merges into the spinal cord at the level of the foramen magnum, so there is no sharp boundary between the two. All the tracts running between the brain and the cord pass through it.","notes":["The midbrain is the most superior region, connecting with the diencephalon above. It would be the answer for the brainstem region continuous with the diencephalon.","Correct. The medulla merges into the spinal cord at the foramen magnum.","The pons is the middle region, above the medulla, and it bridges to the cerebellum. Pick it if the question asked which region holds the pontine nuclei.","The conus medullaris is the tapered lower end of the spinal cord near the L1 to L2 disc, not part of the brainstem. Its name sounds like medulla, which causes the mix-up; pick it for where the cord ends."],"lt":"t-w7-brainstem-regions","lid":"w7-brainstem-regions-r3","comp":161,"competencyId":"w7-brainstem-regions"},{"id":"L04531","dok":2,"q":"Which tracts pass through the brainstem on their way between the brain and the spinal cord?","a":"Every ascending sensory tract and every descending motor tract.","options":["All ascending sensory and descending motor tracts","Only the commissural tracts linking the two hemispheres","Only descending motor tracts, since sensory ones bypass it","Only ascending sensory tracts, since motor ones bypass it"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The brainstem is the only connection between the higher brain and the spinal cord, so every ascending sensory tract and every descending motor tract runs through it. For example, the descending corticospinal tracts form the pyramids on the front of the medulla. This is one reason a small brainstem injury can affect sensation and movement over large parts of the body.","notes":["Correct. All traffic between the brain and the cord passes through the brainstem.","Tracts linking the two hemispheres are commissural fibers, such as the corpus callosum, and they stay in the cerebrum. Pick them for a question about connecting the right and left hemispheres.","Sensory tracts cannot bypass the brainstem, because there is no other route from the cord to the thalamus and cortex. This choice gets the motor part right but leaves out half the traffic.","Motor tracts also run through the brainstem; the corticospinal fibers even form the pyramids of the medulla. This choice keeps the sensory part but leaves out the motor pathways."],"lt":"t-w7-brainstem-regions","lid":"w7-brainstem-regions-r4","comp":161,"competencyId":"w7-brainstem-regions"},{"id":"L04532","dok":3,"q":"A small injury to the medulla oblongata is immediately life-threatening, while a similar injury to one cerebral lobe usually is not. Why?","a":"The medulla holds the vital cardiac, respiratory, and vasomotor centers.","options":["It holds the heartbeat, breathing, and blood pressure centers","It produces most of the cerebrospinal fluid that cushions the brain","It is the only brain region with its own arterial blood supply","It contains the primary motor cortex that controls every muscle"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The medulla oblongata holds three vital centers: the cardiac center adjusts the rate and force of the heartbeat, the respiratory center sets the basic rhythm of breathing, and the vasomotor center adjusts blood vessel diameter and so blood pressure. Damage there can stop breathing and disrupt circulation within minutes. A cerebral lobe has important but non-vital jobs, so damage there causes a specific loss such as vision or speech rather than death.","notes":["Correct. The cardiac, respiratory, and vasomotor centers in the medulla keep the body alive.","CSF is produced by the choroid plexus in the ventricles, not by the medulla. Pick the choroid plexus for a question about where CSF is made.","Every brain region has an arterial supply; the medulla gets its blood from branches of the vertebral arteries, such as the posterior inferior cerebellar artery. This choice is a made-up idea, not a real feature of the medulla.","The primary motor cortex is in the precentral gyrus of the frontal lobe, not in the brainstem. The medulla carries motor tracts down to the cord but does not start voluntary movement."],"lt":"t-w7-brainstem-regions","lid":"w7-brainstem-regions-r5","comp":164,"competencyId":"w7-medulla"},{"id":"L04568","dok":1,"q":"Which statement correctly describes the superior and inferior colliculi?","a":"The superior colliculi are centers for visual reflexes; the inferior colliculi are centers for auditory reflexes.","options":["Superior: auditory reflexes; inferior: visual reflexes","Superior: motor tracts; inferior: sensory tracts","Superior: conscious vision; inferior: conscious hearing","Superior: visual reflexes; inferior: auditory reflexes"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The colliculi are four rounded bumps on the back of the midbrain that form its roof, the tectum. The upper pair, the superior colliculi, are reflex centers for vision, turning the eyes and head toward something seen. The lower pair, the inferior colliculi, are reflex centers for hearing, turning the head toward a sudden sound.","notes":["This reverses the pairs. The upper (superior) pair serves sight and the lower (inferior) pair serves sound, so the superior colliculi handle visual reflexes.","Descending motor tracts run in the cerebral peduncles on the front of the midbrain, not in the colliculi on its back. Pick the cerebral peduncles for a question about the motor stalks of the midbrain.","The colliculi handle reflexes, not conscious perception. Conscious vision is in the primary visual cortex of the occipital lobe, and conscious hearing is in the primary auditory cortex of the temporal lobe.","Correct. Upper pair for visual reflexes, lower pair for auditory reflexes."],"lt":"t-w7-midbrain","lid":"w7-midbrain-r1","comp":162,"competencyId":"w7-midbrain"},{"id":"L04569","dok":1,"q":"The cerebral aqueduct, which carries CSF from the third to the fourth ventricle, runs through which part of the brain?","a":"The midbrain.","options":["Midbrain","Diencephalon","Pons","Medulla oblongata"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The cerebral aqueduct is a narrow channel running through the midbrain, the most superior region of the brainstem. The midbrain develops from the mesencephalon, and the cavity of that vesicle becomes the aqueduct. CSF flows through it from the third ventricle in the diencephalon above to the fourth ventricle behind the pons and medulla below.","notes":["Correct. The cerebral aqueduct runs through the midbrain.","The diencephalon encloses the third ventricle, where the aqueduct begins. Choose it if the question asked which region surrounds the third ventricle.","The fourth ventricle, not the aqueduct, lies behind the pons. Pick the pons if the question asked which brainstem region sits in front of the fourth ventricle.","The medulla lies in front of the lower fourth ventricle and continues into the spinal cord, where the central canal runs. It would be the answer for the region continuous with the cord."],"lt":"t-w7-midbrain","lid":"w7-midbrain-r2","comp":162,"competencyId":"w7-midbrain"},{"id":"L04570","dok":2,"q":"Which cranial nerve nuclei are located in the midbrain?","a":"CN III (oculomotor) and CN IV (trochlear).","options":["CN V, VI, VII, and VIII","CN IX, X, XI, and XII","CN I and CN II","CN III and CN IV"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The midbrain holds the nuclei of the oculomotor nerve (CN III) and the trochlear nerve (CN IV), both of which move the eye. The pons holds V through VIII, and the medulla holds IX, X, and XII. Moving down the brainstem, the cranial nerve numbers rise, which helps place each nucleus.","notes":["CN V through VIII have their nuclei in the pons. Pick this set if the question asked which cranial nerves arise from the pons.","These nerves belong to the lowest part of the brainstem and below it. IX, X, and XII have their nuclei in the medulla, and the XI nucleus sits in the upper cervical cord, so none of them are in the midbrain.","The olfactory and optic nerves attach to the cerebrum and diencephalon, above the brainstem, so they have no brainstem nuclei.","Correct. The oculomotor and trochlear nuclei are in the midbrain."],"lt":"t-w7-midbrain","lid":"w7-midbrain-r3","comp":165,"competencyId":"w7-cranial-nerve-nuclei"},{"id":"L04571","dok":1,"q":"What does the name pons mean, and what does the pons connect?","a":"Bridge; it connects the medulla, the midbrain, and the cerebellum.","options":["Bridge; it joins medulla, midbrain, and cerebellum","Marrow; it continues down into the spinal cord","Little brain; it coordinates posture, balance, and movement","Middle brain; it surrounds the cerebral aqueduct"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Pons is Latin for bridge. The pons is the rounded middle region of the brainstem, and it bridges the medulla below, the midbrain above, and the cerebellum behind it through the thick middle cerebellar peduncles. Its pontine nuclei relay motor information from the cortex across that bridge to the cerebellum.","notes":["Correct. The pons is the bridge between the medulla, the midbrain, and the cerebellum.","Medulla means marrow or inner part, and the medulla oblongata is the region that continues into the spinal cord. Choose it for the most inferior region of the brainstem.","Little brain is the meaning of cerebellum, which coordinates movement, posture, and balance. Pick it if the question asked about the part behind the brainstem.","Middle brain describes the midbrain (mesencephalon), which surrounds the cerebral aqueduct. It would be the answer for the most superior region of the brainstem."],"lt":"t-w7-pons","lid":"w7-pons-r1","comp":161,"competencyId":"w7-brainstem-regions"},{"id":"L04572","dok":1,"q":"What do the pontine nuclei relay?","a":"Motor information from the cerebral cortex to the cerebellum.","options":["Motor information from the cortex to the cerebellum","Hormone signals from the hypothalamus to the pituitary","Visual reflex signals from the eyes to the neck","Sensory signals from the body to the cortex"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The pontine nuclei are clusters of gray matter in the pons. They receive motor information from the cerebral cortex about planned movements and relay it through the middle cerebellar peduncles into the cerebellum. This lets the cerebellum compare what the cortex intends with what the body is actually doing.","notes":["Correct. The pontine nuclei pass the cortex's motor plans on to the cerebellum.","The hypothalamus controls the pituitary gland below it, linking the nervous and endocrine systems. That would be the answer for a question about how the brain controls hormone release.","Visual reflexes, such as turning the eyes and head toward a sight, are handled by the superior colliculi of the midbrain. Pick them for a question about visual reflex centers.","Relaying sensory signals to the cortex is the main job of the thalamus. Choose the thalamus if the question asked for the sensory relay station of the diencephalon."],"lt":"t-w7-pons","lid":"w7-pons-r2","comp":163,"competencyId":"w7-pons"},{"id":"L04573","dok":2,"q":"Which cranial nerve nuclei are located in the pons?","a":"CN V, VI, VII, and VIII.","options":["CN V, VI, VII, and VIII","CN III, IV, V, and VI","CN IX, X, XI, and XII","CN III and CN IV"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The pons holds the nuclei of the trigeminal (V), abducens (VI), facial (VII), and vestibulocochlear (VIII) nerves. The midbrain above holds III and IV, and the medulla below holds IX, X, and XII. The vestibulocochlear nuclei sit where the pons meets the medulla, so VIII is listed with both regions.","notes":["Correct. The trigeminal, abducens, facial, and vestibulocochlear nuclei are in the pons.","This set mixes regions: III and IV belong to the midbrain, and it leaves out VII and VIII, which are in the pons. The pons group starts at V and runs through VIII.","These nerves belong below the pons. IX, X, and XII have their nuclei in the medulla, and the XI nucleus sits in the upper cervical cord. Choose this set for a question about the nerves that attach to the medulla.","CN III and IV have their nuclei in the midbrain, the region above the pons. Pick this set if the question asked about the midbrain."],"lt":"t-w7-pons","lid":"w7-pons-r3","comp":165,"competencyId":"w7-cranial-nerve-nuclei"},{"id":"L04574","dok":1,"q":"What forms the pyramids on the front of the medulla oblongata?","a":"The descending corticospinal motor tracts.","options":["Descending corticospinal motor tracts","Ascending sensory tracts to the thalamus","Fibers of the middle cerebellar peduncles","Nuclei of the cardiac and respiratory centers"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The pyramids are a pair of ridges on the anterior surface of the medulla. They are formed by the corticospinal tracts, the descending motor axons that run from the primary motor cortex down to the spinal cord. Near the bottom of the medulla most of these fibers cross to the opposite side at the decussation of the pyramids.","notes":["Correct. The corticospinal motor tracts form the pyramids.","Ascending sensory tracts do pass through the medulla on their way to the thalamus, but they do not form the pyramids. The pyramids are made by the descending motor tract.","The middle cerebellar peduncles are thick stalks connecting the pons to the cerebellum, at a higher level than the medulla. Choose them for a question about the bridge from the pons to the cerebellum.","The cardiac, respiratory, and vasomotor centers are networks of neurons deep in the medulla, not surface ridges. Pick them for a question about what makes a medullary injury life-threatening."],"lt":"t-w7-medulla","lid":"w7-medulla-r1","comp":164,"competencyId":"w7-medulla"},{"id":"L04575","dok":1,"q":"Which list names the three vital centers of the medulla oblongata?","a":"The cardiac, respiratory, and vasomotor centers.","options":["Cardiac, respiratory, and vasomotor","Speech, hearing, and vision","Hunger, thirst, and temperature","Visual, auditory, and vasomotor"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The medulla holds three vital centers. The cardiac center adjusts the rate and force of the heartbeat, the respiratory center sets the basic rhythm of breathing, and the vasomotor center adjusts blood vessel diameter, which controls blood pressure. Because they keep the body alive, damage to the medulla is immediately dangerous.","notes":["Correct. These three centers adjust the heartbeat, set the breathing rhythm, and adjust blood vessel diameter.","Speech, hearing, and vision are handled by areas of the cerebral cortex, such as Broca's area and the primary auditory and visual cortices. They are not vital centers of the brainstem.","Hunger, thirst, and body temperature are regulated by the hypothalamus. Pick it if the question asked which structure keeps these homeostatic drives in balance.","Visual and auditory reflex centers are the superior and inferior colliculi of the midbrain. This choice includes one real medullary center but mixes in two that belong to the midbrain."],"lt":"t-w7-medulla","lid":"w7-medulla-r2","comp":164,"competencyId":"w7-medulla"},{"id":"L04576","dok":2,"q":"What is the decussation of the pyramids, and what does it explain?","a":"The crossover of corticospinal fibers to the opposite side; it is why each hemisphere controls the opposite side of the body.","options":["A crossing of motor fibers, so each hemisphere runs the other side","A bridge of fibers joining the two hemispheres to share memory","A split of motor fibers into the cerebellum to coordinate movement","A crossing of optic fibers, so each eye sends signals to both sides"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"At the lower end of the medulla, most of the corticospinal motor fibers in the pyramids cross to the opposite side, a crossing called the decussation of the pyramids. Because of this, the left motor cortex controls muscles on the right side of the body, and the right controls the left. This is why a stroke in the right hemisphere weakens the left side of the body.","notes":["Correct. The motor fibers cross in the medulla, so control is contralateral (opposite side).","The bridge joining the two hemispheres is the corpus callosum, a band of commissural fibers in the cerebrum. It would be the answer for a question about how the hemispheres share information.","Motor information reaches the cerebellum through the pontine nuclei and the middle cerebellar peduncles, not by splitting off at the pyramids. Choose the pontine nuclei for that question.","A crossing of optic nerve fibers happens at the optic chiasm, in front of the pituitary, not in the medulla. Pick the optic chiasm for a question about where visual pathways cross."],"lt":"t-w7-medulla","lid":"w7-medulla-r3","comp":164,"competencyId":"w7-medulla"},{"id":"L04586","dok":1,"q":"What does the reticular activating system (RAS) do?","a":"It keeps the cortex awake, alert, and attentive; its shutdown causes sleep, and severe damage can cause coma.","options":["Keeps the cortex awake, alert, and attentive","Relays touch and pain signals to the cortex","Coordinates posture, balance, and movement","Forms new long-term memories from experience"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The reticular formation is a net of gray matter neurons woven through the core of the brainstem. Its ascending part, the reticular activating system, keeps the cerebral cortex awake, alert, and attentive and filters out unimportant stimuli so the brain is not overloaded. When the RAS shuts down the result is sleep, and severe damage to it can cause coma.","notes":["Correct. The RAS maintains consciousness and alertness.","Relaying touch and pain signals to the cortex is the job of the thalamus. Pick the thalamus for a question about the main sensory relay station.","Coordinating posture, balance, and movement is the job of the cerebellum. It would be the answer if the question asked which part keeps movement smooth and steady.","Forming new long-term memories is the job of the hippocampus in the limbic system. Choose it for a question about memory formation."],"lt":"t-w7-reticular-formation","lid":"w7-reticular-formation-r1","comp":166,"competencyId":"w7-reticular-formation"},{"id":"N-a17-163-1","dok":1,"q":"What is the basilar pons?","a":"The thick band of crosswise fibers on the front of the pons.","options":["The back part of the pons, which holds cranial nerve nuclei","The thick band of crosswise fibers on the front of the pons","The thick stalks that attach the pons to the cerebellum","The diamond-shaped space filled with fluid behind the pons"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The basilar pons is the front surface of the pons, a thick band of fibers that runs crosswise and curves back on each side toward the cerebellum. The pontine nuclei are scattered among these fibers. The back part of the pons is a different region, the tegmentum.","notes":["The back part of the pons, with cranial nerve nuclei and part of the reticular formation, is the tegmentum.","Correct. The basilar pons is the thick band of crosswise fibers on the front of the pons.","The thick stalks that attach the side of the pons to each cerebellar hemisphere are the middle cerebellar peduncles.","The diamond-shaped space filled with cerebrospinal fluid behind the pons is the fourth ventricle."],"comp":163,"competencyId":"w7-pons"},{"id":"N-a17-163-2","dok":1,"q":"Where are the pontine nuclei found?","a":"Scattered through the basilar pons.","options":["In the medulla, below the pons","In the tegmentum of the pons","In the cerebellar hemispheres","Scattered through the basilar pons"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The pontine nuclei are clusters of gray matter scattered through the basilar pons, the front part of the pons. They receive motor information from the cerebral cortex, and their axons cross to the opposite side and enter the cerebellum.","notes":["The pontine nuclei are part of the pons, as their name says. The medulla lies below the pons.","The tegmentum is the back part of the pons. It carries cranial nerve nuclei and part of the reticular formation, but the pontine nuclei sit in the front part.","The pontine nuclei send their axons into the cerebellum, but the nuclei themselves stay in the basilar pons.","Correct. The pontine nuclei are clusters of gray matter scattered through the basilar pons."],"comp":163,"competencyId":"w7-pons"},{"id":"N-a17-163-3","dok":2,"q":"On a midsagittal model, you find the fourth ventricle, with the cerebellum over its roof. What forms its floor?","a":"The back of the pons and upper medulla.","options":["The front of the pons, the basilar pons","The back of the pons and upper medulla","The corpus callosum and the fornix","The thalamus and the hypothalamus"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The fourth ventricle is a diamond-shaped space filled with cerebrospinal fluid, behind the pons and upper medulla. Its floor is the back of the pons and the upper medulla, and the cerebellum lies over its roof. The cerebral aqueduct opens into it from above.","notes":["The basilar pons is the front surface of the pons, facing away from the ventricle. The fourth ventricle lies behind the pons.","Correct. The floor of the fourth ventricle is the back of the pons and the upper medulla.","The corpus callosum and the fornix belong to the cerebrum, far above the brainstem. They do not border the fourth ventricle.","The thalamus and hypothalamus come from the diencephalon, whose cavity is the third ventricle, not the fourth."],"comp":163,"competencyId":"w7-pons"},{"id":"N-a17-163-4","dok":1,"q":"Which cranial nerve leaves the side of the pons?","a":"Trigeminal nerve (CN V).","options":["Trigeminal nerve (CN V)","Abducens nerve (CN VI)","Facial nerve (CN VII)","Vestibulocochlear nerve (CN VIII)"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The trigeminal nerve (CN V) leaves the side of the pons. The abducens (CN VI), facial (CN VII), and vestibulocochlear (CN VIII) nerves leave lower, at the pontomedullary junction where the pons meets the medulla.","notes":["Correct. The trigeminal nerve (CN V) leaves the side of the pons.","The abducens nerve (CN VI) leaves at the pontomedullary junction, not the side of the pons. Pick it for a question about that junction.","The facial nerve (CN VII) leaves at the pontomedullary junction, along with CN VI and CN VIII, not from the side of the pons.","The vestibulocochlear nerve (CN VIII) leaves at the pontomedullary junction, not the side of the pons."],"comp":163,"competencyId":"w7-pons"},{"id":"N-a17-163-5","dok":2,"q":"On a brainstem model, you follow the crosswise fibers of the basilar pons around its side. Where do they lead?","a":"Into the cerebellum, as the middle cerebellar peduncles.","options":["Up into the thalamus, through the cerebral aqueduct","Down into the medulla, through the fourth ventricle","Forward to the cerebral cortex, through the fornix","Into the cerebellum, as the middle cerebellar peduncles"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The crosswise fibers of the basilar pons carry the axons of the pontine nuclei. They curve back on each side and leave the pons as the middle cerebellar peduncles, the thick stalks that enter each cerebellar hemisphere. These are the largest of the three pairs of cerebellar peduncles.","notes":["The cerebral aqueduct is a fluid channel that opens into the fourth ventricle from above. It carries cerebrospinal fluid, not nerve fibers.","The fourth ventricle is a fluid-filled space behind the pons, not a pathway for fibers into the medulla.","The fornix is the white matter arch that carries hippocampal output to the mammillary bodies. It runs under the corpus callosum, far above the pons.","Correct. The crosswise fibers leave the pons as the middle cerebellar peduncles and enter the cerebellum."],"comp":163,"competencyId":"w7-pons"},{"id":"N-a17-163-6","dok":3,"q":"A stroke damages both middle cerebellar peduncles. Which flow of information is most directly cut off?","a":"Motor information from the cortex, relayed by the pontine nuclei to the cerebellum.","options":["Cerebrospinal fluid flowing from the cerebral aqueduct into the fourth ventricle","Motor information from the cortex, relayed by the pontine nuclei to the cerebellum","Ascending signals that pass through the thalamus to keep the cortex awake","Output from the hippocampus carried forward to the mammillary bodies"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The pontine nuclei receive motor information from the cerebral cortex, and their axons cross and leave the pons as the middle cerebellar peduncles. Damage to the peduncles cuts this relay. It is the route the cerebrum uses to keep the cerebellum informed about planned movements.","notes":["CSF moves from the cerebral aqueduct into the fourth ventricle, a fluid space behind the pons. The peduncles carry nerve fibers, not fluid.","Correct. The middle cerebellar peduncles carry the pontine relay of motor information from the cortex to the cerebellum.","Signals that rise through the thalamus to keep the cortex awake belong to the reticular activating system, not the middle cerebellar peduncles.","Hippocampal output travels in the fornix to the mammillary bodies, a limbic pathway that does not run through the peduncles."],"comp":163,"competencyId":"w7-pons"},{"id":"N-a17-166-1","dok":1,"q":"What does the descending component of the reticular formation do?","a":"Helps set resting muscle tone and posture.","options":["Keeps the cerebral cortex awake and alert","Filters out unimportant sensory input","Helps set resting muscle tone and posture","Relays motor information to the cerebellum"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The descending component of the reticular formation sends fibers down to the spinal cord. These fibers help set the resting tone of skeletal muscles and help keep posture. It also includes the medullary centers for heart rate, breathing, and blood vessel diameter.","notes":["Keeping the cortex awake and alert is the job of the ascending reticular activating system (RAS), which sends fibers up through the thalamus.","Filtering out unimportant sensory input is also a job of the ascending RAS, so the cortex is not flooded.","Correct. The descending component sends fibers to the spinal cord that help set resting muscle tone and posture.","Relaying motor information from the cortex to the cerebellum is the job of the pontine nuclei. The descending component of the reticular formation sends its fibers down to the spinal cord instead."],"comp":166,"competencyId":"w7-reticular-formation"},{"id":"N-a17-166-2","dok":2,"q":"On a cross section of the brainstem, where do you find the reticular formation?","a":"In the central core, behind the large descending motor tracts.","options":["In the central core, behind the large descending motor tracts","On the front surface, among the crosswise fibers of the pons","Over the roof of the fourth ventricle, under the cerebellum","On the outer surface, as a raised ridge of gray matter"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The reticular formation fills the central core of the brainstem, the tegmentum. It lies behind the large descending motor tracts and in front of the ventricle or aqueduct. It runs this way through the medulla, pons, and midbrain.","notes":["Correct. The reticular formation fills the central core, behind the descending motor tracts and in front of the ventricle or aqueduct.","The crosswise fibers on the front of the pons are the basilar pons, with the pontine nuclei. The reticular formation lies deeper, in the core.","The cerebellum lies over the roof of the fourth ventricle. The reticular formation lies in front of the ventricle, in the brainstem core.","The reticular formation has no surface landmark. It is a loose net of neurons in the core, so you find it by its position in a cross section."],"comp":166,"competencyId":"w7-reticular-formation"},{"id":"N-a17-166-3","dok":3,"q":"Why can't you point to the reticular formation on the surface of a brainstem model?","a":"It is a loose net of scattered neurons in the core, with no sharp borders.","options":["It is a thin layer of white matter wrapped around the outside of the brainstem","It lies in the cerebral cortex, above the brainstem, not in the brainstem","It is a loose net of scattered neurons in the core, with no sharp borders","It is one compact nucleus hidden deep inside the cerebellum"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The reticular formation is made of small clusters of neurons scattered among bundles of white matter. Because it is spread out like a net, it has no sharp borders and no surface landmark. It fills the central core of the brainstem, so you find it by its position in a cross section.","notes":["It is a net of gray matter, not white matter, and it runs through the core of the brainstem rather than around its outside.","The reticular formation runs through the brainstem, from the medulla to the midbrain. Its ascending fibers reach the cortex, but its neurons are in the brainstem.","Correct. It is a loose net of scattered neurons in the brainstem core, with no sharp borders or surface landmark.","It is not one compact nucleus, and it is not in the cerebellum. It is a loose net spread through the whole brainstem core."],"comp":166,"competencyId":"w7-reticular-formation"},{"id":"N-a17d-157-1","dok":3,"q":"On a midsagittal section, you see a small cut oval in the middle of the thalamus, crossing a narrow midline cavity. What is the oval, and which cavity does it cross?","a":"The massa intermedia, crossing the third ventricle.","options":["The pineal gland, crossing the cerebral aqueduct","The massa intermedia, crossing the third ventricle","A mammillary body, crossing the fourth ventricle","The massa intermedia, crossing the lateral ventricle"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The two thalami sit on either side of the third ventricle, the narrow midline cavity of the diencephalon. The massa intermedia (interthalamic adhesion) is a small bridge of gray matter that crosses the third ventricle and joins them. On a midsagittal section it appears as a small cut oval in the middle of the thalamus, though some people do not have one.","notes":["The pineal gland projects backward from the roof of the third ventricle, above the midbrain. It sits at the back, not in the middle of the thalamus.","Correct. The massa intermedia is a small bridge of gray matter that crosses the third ventricle and joins the two thalami.","The mammillary bodies are small round bumps on the underside of the hypothalamus. The fourth ventricle lies behind the pons, far from the thalamus.","The oval is right, but the lateral ventricles are in the cerebral hemispheres. The midline cavity between the thalami is the third ventricle."],"comp":157,"competencyId":"w7-diencephalon"},{"id":"N-a17d-157-2","dok":3,"q":"A tumor grows in the small pine-cone-shaped gland that projects backward from the roof of the third ventricle. Which part of the diencephalon holds this gland, and which part of the brainstem lies just below it?","a":"The epithalamus, with the midbrain just below.","options":["The epithalamus, with the midbrain just below","The thalamus, with the cerebellum just below","The hypothalamus, with the pons just below","The epithalamus, with the medulla just below"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The epithalamus is the small, posterior, upper part of the diencephalon and forms part of the roof of the third ventricle. Its main structure is the pineal gland, which projects backward from that roof. The gland sits above the midbrain, the top region of the brainstem.","notes":["Correct. The pineal gland belongs to the epithalamus and projects backward above the midbrain.","The thalamus forms the upper side walls of the third ventricle and holds the massa intermedia. The pineal gland belongs to the epithalamus, behind and above it.","The hypothalamus forms the floor and lower walls of the third ventricle, not its roof. The pons is also lower than the region just beneath the pineal gland.","The epithalamus is right, but the medulla is the lowest part of the brainstem. The midbrain lies right below the diencephalon."],"comp":157,"competencyId":"w7-diencephalon"},{"id":"N-a17d-157-3","dok":3,"q":"On the inferior surface of the brain, you start at the optic chiasm and move backward along the midline. Which two structures do you meet next, in order, and which part of the diencephalon do they belong to?","a":"The infundibulum, then the mammillary bodies, both of the hypothalamus.","options":["The pineal gland, then the mammillary bodies, both of the thalamus","The infundibulum, then the pineal gland, both of the epithalamus","The infundibulum, then the mammillary bodies, both of the hypothalamus","The mammillary bodies, then the infundibulum, both of the hypothalamus"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Three landmarks of the hypothalamus show on the inferior surface of the brain. The optic chiasm sits at the front. Behind it is the infundibulum, the stalk that connects the hypothalamus to the pituitary gland, and behind that are the two small round mammillary bodies.","notes":["The pineal gland is part of the epithalamus on the roof of the third ventricle, and the mammillary bodies are part of the hypothalamus. Neither belongs to the thalamus.","The infundibulum belongs to the hypothalamus, and the pineal gland sits on the upper back of the diencephalon. It does not show on the inferior surface.","Correct. Behind the optic chiasm come the infundibulum and then the mammillary bodies, landmarks of the hypothalamus.","These belong to the hypothalamus, but the order is reversed. The infundibulum comes right behind the chiasm, and the mammillary bodies are behind it."],"comp":157,"competencyId":"w7-diencephalon"},{"id":"N-a17d-158-1","dok":3,"q":"On a model, you follow the largest of the three thick white stalks from the cerebellum forward into the pons. Which peduncle is it, and where does most of its input come from?","a":"The middle cerebellar peduncle, carrying input from the pontine nuclei.","options":["The middle cerebellar peduncle, carrying output to the thalamus","The middle cerebellar peduncle, carrying input from the pontine nuclei","The superior cerebellar peduncle, carrying input from the pontine nuclei","The inferior cerebellar peduncle, carrying input from the spinal cord"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Each pair of cerebellar peduncles joins a different part of the brainstem: superior to the midbrain, middle to the pons, and inferior to the medulla. The middle cerebellar peduncle is the largest. It carries input from the pontine nuclei, which relay signals from the cerebral cortex into the cerebellum.","notes":["The peduncle is right, but it carries input to the cerebellum. Output toward the midbrain and thalamus travels in the superior peduncle.","Correct. The middle cerebellar peduncle is the largest and joins the pons, carrying input from the pontine nuclei.","The superior peduncle joins the midbrain and carries mostly output from the deep cerebellar nuclei. The stalk into the pons is the middle peduncle.","The inferior peduncle does carry input from the spinal cord, but it joins the medulla. The largest stalk into the pons is the middle peduncle."],"comp":158,"competencyId":"w7-cerebellum"},{"id":"N-a17d-158-2","dok":3,"q":"A midsagittal cut through the cerebellum shows a narrow midline strip with a branching white tree inside it and fine leaf-like folds along its edge. What is the strip, and what are the folds?","a":"The vermis, with folia along its edge.","options":["The vermis, with folia along its edge","The vermis, with gyri along its edge","The arbor vitae, with sulci along its edge","A cerebellar hemisphere, with folia along its edge"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The two cerebellar hemispheres are joined in the midline by a narrow strip called the vermis, so a midsagittal cut passes right through it. Inside, the white matter branches like a tree, the arbor vitae. The surface is folded into folia, thin parallel folds that are much finer than the gyri of the cerebrum.","notes":["Correct. The midline strip is the vermis, the white tree is the arbor vitae, and the fine folds are folia.","The strip is right, but gyri are the larger ridges of the cerebrum. The fine leaf-like folds of the cerebellum are called folia.","The arbor vitae is the branching white tree inside the strip, not the strip itself. The folds of the cerebellar surface are folia.","The folds are folia, but the hemispheres lie to each side. A cut down the midline passes through the vermis that joins them."],"comp":158,"competencyId":"w7-cerebellum"},{"id":"N-a17d-161-1","dok":3,"q":"On the front of a brain model, you find the groove where the rounded bulge of the brainstem meets the tapering lowest region. What is this groove, and which cranial nerves leave along it?","a":"The pontomedullary junction, where CN VI to VIII leave.","options":["The foramen magnum, where CN IX, X, and XII leave","The pontomedullary junction, where CN III and IV leave","The cerebral peduncle, where CN VI to VIII leave","The pontomedullary junction, where CN VI to VIII leave"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The rounded bulge is the pons, and the tapering lowest region is the medulla oblongata. The groove where they meet on the front surface is the pontomedullary junction. It is a useful landmark because three cranial nerves, VI, VII, and VIII, leave the brainstem along it.","notes":["The foramen magnum is the skull opening where the medulla becomes the spinal cord. It is a bony opening, not a groove on the brainstem.","The groove is right, but CN III and IV leave the midbrain, above the pons. CN VI, VII, and VIII leave at this junction.","The cerebral peduncles are the stalks on the front of the midbrain. They are above the pons, not at its lower border.","Correct. The groove between the pons and medulla is the pontomedullary junction, where CN VI, VII, and VIII leave."],"comp":161,"competencyId":"w7-brainstem-regions"},{"id":"N-a17d-161-2","dok":3,"q":"A lesion damages the lowest region of the brainstem, the part that becomes the spinal cord at the foramen magnum. Which region is it, and which cerebellar peduncles attach to it?","a":"The medulla oblongata, with the inferior cerebellar peduncles.","options":["The pons, with the middle cerebellar peduncles","The medulla oblongata, with the inferior cerebellar peduncles","The midbrain, with the superior cerebellar peduncles","The medulla oblongata, with the middle cerebellar peduncles"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The brainstem runs midbrain, pons, and medulla oblongata from top to bottom. The medulla is the lowest and becomes the spinal cord at the foramen magnum. The cerebellar peduncles attach in the same top-to-bottom order, so the inferior peduncles attach to the medulla.","notes":["The pons is the middle region, and the middle peduncles do attach to it. But the pons does not continue as the spinal cord.","Correct. The medulla is continuous with the spinal cord at the foramen magnum, and the inferior peduncles attach to it.","The midbrain is the highest region, just below the diencephalon, and the superior peduncles attach to it. It is the farthest region from the spinal cord.","The region is right, but the middle peduncles attach to the pons. The medulla gets the inferior peduncles."],"comp":161,"competencyId":"w7-brainstem-regions"},{"id":"N-a17d-162-1","dok":2,"q":"On a cross section of the midbrain, which structures lie on the tectum, behind the cerebral aqueduct?","a":"The superior and inferior colliculi.","options":["The substantia nigra and crus cerebri","The cerebral peduncles","The tegmentum and its cranial nerve nuclei","The superior and inferior colliculi"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The cerebral aqueduct is the small hole near the back of a midbrain cross section, and it orients the specimen. Everything behind it is the roof, the tectum, which carries the four rounded bumps called the corpora quadrigemina. These are the superior and inferior colliculi.","notes":["These lie toward the front of the midbrain, with the substantia nigra between the crus cerebri and the tegmentum. They are well in front of the aqueduct.","The cerebral peduncles form the front of the midbrain and carry descending motor tracts. They are in front of the aqueduct, not behind it.","The tegmentum is the core of the midbrain between the aqueduct and the crura. It lies in front of the aqueduct, not on the roof.","Correct. The tectum is the roof behind the aqueduct, and it carries the four colliculi."],"comp":162,"competencyId":"w7-midbrain"},{"id":"N-a17d-162-2","dok":2,"q":"Which list orders the parts of the midbrain from front to back on a cross section?","a":"Crus cerebri, substantia nigra, tegmentum, cerebral aqueduct, tectum.","options":["Crus cerebri, substantia nigra, tegmentum, cerebral aqueduct, tectum","Tectum, cerebral aqueduct, tegmentum, substantia nigra, crus cerebri","Substantia nigra, crus cerebri, cerebral aqueduct, tegmentum, tectum","Crus cerebri, tegmentum, substantia nigra, tectum, cerebral aqueduct"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The most anterior part of each cerebral peduncle is the crus cerebri. Behind it lies the dark substantia nigra, then the tegmentum, the core of the midbrain. The cerebral aqueduct sits near the back, and the tectum with its colliculi forms the roof behind it.","notes":["Correct. From the front, the crus cerebri comes first, then the substantia nigra, the tegmentum, the aqueduct, and the tectum at the back.","This list runs from back to front. The tectum is the roof at the back, and the crus cerebri is the most anterior part.","The crus cerebri is the most anterior part, in front of the substantia nigra. The tegmentum lies in front of the aqueduct, not behind it.","The substantia nigra lies between the crus cerebri and the tegmentum, and the tectum lies behind the aqueduct. Both are out of place here."],"comp":162,"competencyId":"w7-midbrain"},{"id":"N-a17d-162-3","dok":3,"q":"On a midbrain specimen, a cranial nerve leaves the front surface in the gap between the two thick stalks. Which nerve is it, and what are the stalks?","a":"The oculomotor nerve (CN III), between the cerebral peduncles.","options":["The oculomotor nerve (CN III), between the inferior colliculi","The trochlear nerve (CN IV), between the cerebral peduncles","The oculomotor nerve (CN III), between the cerebral peduncles","The trochlear nerve (CN IV), between the superior colliculi"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The front of the midbrain is formed by the two cerebral peduncles, thick stalks that carry descending motor tracts. Two cranial nerves leave the midbrain. The oculomotor nerve (CN III) leaves the front, in the gap between the peduncles, while the trochlear nerve (CN IV) leaves the back, just below the inferior colliculi.","notes":["The nerve is right, but the colliculi are on the tectum at the back. CN III leaves the front, between the cerebral peduncles.","The stalks are right, but the trochlear nerve leaves the back of the midbrain, just below the inferior colliculi. The nerve between the peduncles is CN III.","Correct. CN III leaves the front of the midbrain in the gap between the two cerebral peduncles.","The trochlear nerve does leave the back, but below the inferior colliculi, not between the superior colliculi. The question describes the front surface."],"comp":162,"competencyId":"w7-midbrain"},{"id":"N-a17d-163-1","dok":3,"q":"A midsagittal model shows a diamond-shaped space behind the pons with the cerebellum over its roof. If the narrow channel that opens into this space from above were blocked, which channel would it be, and which ventricle would no longer send fluid down?","a":"The cerebral aqueduct, from the third ventricle.","options":["The central canal, from the spinal cord","The cerebral aqueduct, from the third ventricle","The cerebral aqueduct, from a lateral ventricle","An interventricular foramen, from a lateral ventricle"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The diamond-shaped space behind the pons, with the cerebellum over its roof, is the fourth ventricle. The cerebral aqueduct opens into it from above. The aqueduct carries cerebrospinal fluid down through the midbrain from the third ventricle, so a blocked aqueduct would cut the fourth ventricle off from the third.","notes":["The central canal runs downward from the bottom of the fourth ventricle into the spinal cord. It is below the fourth ventricle, not a channel opening into it from above.","Correct. The space is the fourth ventricle, and the cerebral aqueduct opens into it from above, carrying CSF from the third ventricle.","The channel is right, but the aqueduct drains the third ventricle. The lateral ventricles empty into the third ventricle first.","The interventricular foramina join each lateral ventricle to the third ventricle. They do not open into the fourth ventricle."],"comp":163,"competencyId":"w7-pons"},{"id":"N-a17d-163-2","dok":3,"q":"A small lesion damages the back part of the pons, just in front of the fourth ventricle. Which part of the pons is this, and what does it hold?","a":"The tegmentum, which holds cranial nerve nuclei.","options":["The middle cerebellar peduncle, which holds the dentate nucleus","The tectum, which holds the colliculi","The basilar pons, which holds the pontine nuclei","The tegmentum, which holds cranial nerve nuclei"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The pons has a front part and a back part. The basilar pons in front is a thick band of crosswise fibers with the pontine nuclei scattered through it. The back part is the tegmentum, which carries cranial nerve nuclei and part of the reticular formation, and it forms part of the floor of the fourth ventricle.","notes":["The middle peduncles are white matter stalks on the sides of the pons, and the dentate nucleus is a deep nucleus inside the cerebellum. Neither is the back part of the pons.","The tectum is the roof of the midbrain, behind the cerebral aqueduct. It is not part of the pons.","The basilar pons and its pontine nuclei are on the front surface of the pons. A lesion just in front of the fourth ventricle is in the back part.","Correct. The back part of the pons is its tegmentum, which holds cranial nerve nuclei and part of the reticular formation."],"comp":163,"competencyId":"w7-pons"},{"id":"N-a17d-165-1","dok":1,"q":"Which two cranial nerves have no nucleus in the brainstem?","a":"CN I and CN II.","options":["CN I and CN II","CN XI and CN XII","CN V and CN VIII","CN III and CN IV"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Cranial nerves I (olfactory) and II (optic) connect to the forebrain, so they have no nucleus in the brainstem. The nuclei of III through X and XII are housed in the brainstem. The nucleus of XI sits just below it, in the upper cervical spinal cord.","notes":["Correct. The olfactory (I) and optic (II) nerves connect to the forebrain, so they have no brainstem nucleus.","The CN XII nucleus is in the medulla, so this pair does not fit. CN XI has its nucleus just below the brainstem, but the two nerves with no brainstem nucleus at all are the ones that connect to the forebrain.","CN V is assigned to the pons, and CN VIII has its nuclei at the junction of the pons and medulla. Both are in the brainstem.","The nuclei of CN III and IV are in the midbrain, the top region of the brainstem."],"comp":165,"competencyId":"w7-cranial-nerve-nuclei"},{"id":"N-a17d-165-2","dok":1,"q":"Where does the motor nucleus of the accessory nerve (CN XI) sit?","a":"In the upper cervical spinal cord.","options":["In the pons of the brainstem","In the upper cervical spinal cord","In the midbrain of the brainstem","In the medulla oblongata of the brainstem"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Most cranial nerve nuclei from III to XII sit in the brainstem, but the accessory nerve is the exception. Its motor nucleus is in the upper cervical spinal cord, just below the medulla. Its rootlets rise through the foramen magnum and then leave the skull with the vagus nerve, which is why it is grouped with the medulla in the 2-2-4-4 rule.","notes":["The pons holds the nuclei of CN V, VI, VII, and VIII. CN XI is much lower.","Correct. The CN XI motor nucleus sits in the upper cervical cord, just below the brainstem.","The midbrain holds the nuclei of CN III and IV, the highest of the brainstem nuclei. CN XI is at the opposite end.","CN XI is grouped with the medulla in the 2-2-4-4 rule, but its motor nucleus actually sits below the medulla in the upper cervical cord."],"comp":165,"competencyId":"w7-cranial-nerve-nuclei"},{"id":"N-a17d-165-3","dok":1,"q":"Which brainstem region houses the nuclei of cranial nerves IX, X, and XII?","a":"The medulla oblongata.","options":["The upper cervical spinal cord","The midbrain","The medulla oblongata","The pons"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The cranial nerve nuclei line up from top to bottom in the same order as the nerve numbers. The midbrain holds III and IV, and the pons holds V through VIII. The medulla, the lowest region, holds IX, X, and XII.","notes":["The upper cervical cord holds the motor nucleus of CN XI. The nuclei of IX, X, and XII are in the medulla above it.","The midbrain holds only III and IV, the first of the brainstem nuclei.","Correct. The medulla holds the nuclei of IX, X, and XII.","The pons holds the nuclei of V, VI, VII, and VIII, which come before IX in number and sit higher."],"comp":165,"competencyId":"w7-cranial-nerve-nuclei"},{"id":"N-a17d-165-4","dok":3,"q":"On a brainstem model, a thin cranial nerve leaves the back surface, just below the inferior colliculi. Which nerve is it, and which region holds its nucleus?","a":"The trochlear nerve (CN IV), with its nucleus in the midbrain.","options":["The oculomotor nerve (CN III), with its nucleus in the midbrain","The abducens nerve (CN VI), with its nucleus in the pons","The trochlear nerve (CN IV), with its nucleus in the pons","The trochlear nerve (CN IV), with its nucleus in the midbrain"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The inferior colliculi are bumps on the tectum, the roof of the midbrain. The trochlear nerve (CN IV) leaves the back of the midbrain just below them. A nerve usually leaves near the level of its nucleus, and the midbrain holds the nuclei of III and IV.","notes":["CN III's nucleus is in the midbrain, but CN III leaves the front of the midbrain between the cerebral peduncles, not the back.","CN VI leaves the front of the brainstem at the pontomedullary junction, near the midline. It does not leave the back below the colliculi.","The nerve is right, but its nucleus is in the midbrain, at the same level where it leaves. The pons holds V through VIII.","Correct. CN IV leaves the back of the midbrain just below the inferior colliculi, and its nucleus is in the midbrain."],"comp":165,"competencyId":"w7-cranial-nerve-nuclei"},{"id":"N-a17d-166-1","dok":3,"q":"A lesion cuts only the reticular formation fibers that run down to the spinal cord. Which component is cut, and what is most affected?","a":"The descending component, with resting muscle tone and posture affected.","options":["The descending component, with alertness and attention affected","The descending component, with resting muscle tone and posture affected","The ascending reticular activating system, with alertness affected","The ascending reticular activating system, with muscle tone and posture affected"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The reticular formation has two components that run in opposite directions. The ascending reticular activating system (RAS) sends fibers up through the thalamus to the cortex and keeps it awake and alert. The descending component sends fibers down to the spinal cord and helps set the resting tone of skeletal muscles and keep posture.","notes":["The component is right, but alertness and attention depend on the ascending RAS. The descending fibers set muscle tone and posture.","Correct. Fibers running down to the cord belong to the descending component, which helps set resting muscle tone and posture.","This pairing is true of the RAS, but the RAS runs up to the cortex. The question describes fibers that run down to the spinal cord.","The RAS sends fibers up through the thalamus to the cortex, not down to the cord. Muscle tone and posture belong to the descending component."],"comp":166,"competencyId":"w7-reticular-formation"}]},{"id":"m5-meninges-csf","title":"Meninges, ventricles, CSF and brain blood supply","summary":"dura, arachnoid, pia, dural folds and sinuses, spaces, ventricles, choroid plexus, CSF path, blood-brain barrier, cerebral arterial circle","mw":"2026-11-30","tr":"2026-12-01","cards":[{"id":"L02853","dok":1,"q":"What are the ventricles of the brain?","a":"The fluid-filled chambers within the brain.","options":["The fluid-filled chambers within the brain","The dural folds that partition the cranium","The raised folds of the cerebral cortex","The deep gray matter masses of the cerebrum"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The ventricles are connected, fluid-filled chambers deep within the brain, lined by ependymal cells. Cerebrospinal fluid is made in them by the choroid plexus. There are four: two lateral ventricles, the third ventricle, and the fourth ventricle.","notes":["Correct. The ventricles are the fluid-filled chambers inside the brain.","These are the dural reflections, such as the falx cerebri and tentorium cerebelli, which are folds of dura mater. Pick them if the question asked what separates the hemispheres or the cerebrum from the cerebellum.","These are the gyri, the ridges of the folded cortex. Pick gyri if the question asked about the raised folds on the surface of the cerebrum.","These are the basal nuclei (basal ganglia), gray matter masses buried in the cerebral white matter that help regulate movement. They would be the answer for a question about deep gray matter."],"lt":"t-cns-brain-spinal-cord","lid":"c14","comp":170,"competencyId":"w7-ventricles"},{"id":"L02863","dok":2,"q":"Which ventricle is the fluid-filled cavity located within the diencephalon?","a":"The third ventricle.","options":["The fourth ventricle","The third ventricle","A lateral ventricle","The central canal"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The third ventricle is the narrow, midline cavity enclosed by the diencephalon, with the thalamus forming its side walls. It receives CSF from the lateral ventricles through the interventricular foramina and sends it on through the cerebral aqueduct to the fourth ventricle. Like the other ventricles, it contains choroid plexus.","notes":["The fourth ventricle lies between the brainstem (pons and upper medulla) and the cerebellum. It would be the answer if the question asked which ventricle sits in front of the cerebellum.","Correct. The third ventricle lies within the diencephalon.","The lateral ventricles are paired, one in each cerebral hemisphere. Pick a lateral ventricle if the question asked which ventricle lies within the cerebrum.","The central canal is the CSF channel running the length of the spinal cord. Pick it if the question asked which space is continuous with the fourth ventricle inside the cord."],"lt":"t-cns-brain-spinal-cord","lid":"c24","comp":170,"competencyId":"w7-ventricles"},{"id":"L02892","dok":1,"q":"Which meningeal layer is the outermost of the three?","a":"The dura mater.","options":["The choroid plexus","The arachnoid mater","The dura mater","The pia mater"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The dura mater is the outermost meninx, a tough membrane of dense connective tissue. Around the brain it has two layers, an outer periosteal layer against the skull and an inner meningeal layer. Its name means tough mother, which fits its role as the strong outer covering.","notes":["The choroid plexus is a network of capillaries in the ventricles that produces CSF, not a meningeal layer. Pick it if the question asked where cerebrospinal fluid is made.","The arachnoid mater is the middle meninx, a delicate, lace-like membrane. Pick it if the question asked which layer forms the arachnoid granulations.","Correct. The dura mater is the outermost meninx.","The pia mater is the innermost meninx, a thin vascular membrane that clings to the surface of the brain and cord. It would be the answer if the question asked which layer follows every gyrus and sulcus."],"lt":"t-cns-meninges-csf","lid":"c1","comp":167,"competencyId":"w7-cranial-meninges"},{"id":"L02893","dok":1,"q":"Which meningeal layer lies directly against the surface of the brain and spinal cord?","a":"The pia mater.","options":["The ependymal lining","The pia mater","The dura mater","The arachnoid mater"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The pia mater is the innermost meninx, a thin membrane rich in blood vessels that clings directly to the brain and spinal cord and dips into every sulcus. Its name means tender mother. Around the cord it also forms the denticulate ligaments and the filum terminale, which anchor the cord in place.","notes":["The ependymal lining is the layer of glial cells that lines the inside of the ventricles and central canal, not the outer surface. Pick ependymal cells if the question asked what lines the fluid-filled cavities inside the CNS.","Correct. The pia mater clings directly to the brain and cord.","The dura mater is the tough outermost layer, lying against the skull or vertebral canal rather than the brain. Pick it if the question asked which layer forms the dural venous sinuses.","The arachnoid mater is the delicate middle layer, separated from the brain by the CSF-filled subarachnoid space. It would be the answer if the question asked which layer lies between the dura and pia."],"lt":"t-cns-meninges-csf","lid":"c2","comp":167,"competencyId":"w7-cranial-meninges"},{"id":"L02894","dok":1,"q":"How many meningeal membranes wrap the brain and spinal cord?","a":"Three.","options":["Three","Two","Four","Five"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Three connective tissue membranes, the meninges, wrap the brain and spinal cord. From outside in they are the dura mater, the arachnoid mater, and the pia mater. The same three layers continue from the cranium down through the vertebral canal.","notes":["Correct. There are three meninges.","Two is the number of layers in the cranial dura (periosteal and meningeal), not the number of meninges. Students who count the dura's layers instead of the membranes pick this.","Four would add a layer that does not exist. A student who counts the CSF-filled subarachnoid space or the periosteum as a membrane may land on four.","Five has no basis in the meninges. It can come from mixing the meningeal layers with the spaces between them."],"lt":"t-cns-meninges-csf","lid":"c3","comp":167,"competencyId":"w7-cranial-meninges"},{"id":"L02895","dok":1,"q":"What is the name of the middle meningeal layer, a delicate, web-like membrane?","a":"The arachnoid mater.","options":["The arachnoid mater","The dura mater","The dural sinus","The pia mater"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The arachnoid mater is the middle meninx, a delicate membrane whose fine, web-like strands cross the subarachnoid space below it. Its name comes from the Greek word for spider, because of that web-like look. It also forms the arachnoid granulations, where CSF is returned to the blood.","notes":["Correct. The arachnoid mater is the web-like middle layer.","The dura mater is the tough outermost layer, not the delicate middle one. Pick it if the question asked which meninx is built of dense connective tissue.","A dural sinus is a venous channel formed where the two layers of the cranial dura separate, not a meningeal layer. Pick it if the question asked where venous blood from the brain collects.","The pia mater is the thin innermost layer that clings to the brain surface. It would be the answer for a question about the meninx that follows every sulcus."],"lt":"t-cns-meninges-csf","lid":"c4","comp":167,"competencyId":"w7-cranial-meninges"},{"id":"L02896","dok":1,"q":"Which meningeal space contains cerebrospinal fluid around the brain and spinal cord?","a":"The subarachnoid space.","options":["The epidural space","The subdural space","The subarachnoid space","The dural venous sinuses"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The subarachnoid space lies between the arachnoid mater and the pia mater, and it is filled with cerebrospinal fluid that bathes the whole surface of the brain and cord. Below the end of the cord, this space widens into the lumbar cistern. That is why a lumbar puncture targets the subarachnoid space to sample CSF.","notes":["The epidural space lies between the spinal dura and the vertebral bone and holds fat and veins, not CSF. It would be the answer if the question asked where an epidural injection is placed.","The subdural space lies between the dura and the arachnoid and is only a potential space with a thin film of fluid. Pick it if the question asked where a subdural hematoma collects.","Correct. CSF around the brain and cord is in the subarachnoid space.","The dural venous sinuses carry venous blood, and they receive CSF only after it is reabsorbed at the arachnoid granulations. Pick them if the question asked where CSF ends up after reabsorption."],"lt":"t-cns-meninges-csf","lid":"c5","comp":168,"competencyId":"w7-spinal-meninges-spaces"},{"id":"L02897","dok":1,"q":"Where is the choroid plexus located?","a":"In the ventricles of the brain.","options":["Along the central canal of the cord","Within the dura mater","In the subarachnoid space","In the ventricles of the brain"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The choroid plexus is a network of capillaries wrapped in ependymal cells, found in the lateral, third, and fourth ventricles. It produces cerebrospinal fluid, which is why CSF is said to be made in the ventricles. From there the fluid circulates out into the subarachnoid space.","notes":["The central canal is lined by ependymal cells and holds CSF, but it does not contain choroid plexus. Pick it for a question about the CSF channel continuous with the fourth ventricle.","The dura mater contains the dural venous sinuses, which drain venous blood, not the choroid plexus. It would be the answer if the question asked where the superior sagittal sinus is found.","The subarachnoid space holds CSF after it leaves the ventricles, but no choroid plexus lies there. Pick it if the question asked where CSF bathes the outer surface of the brain.","Correct. The choroid plexus sits in the ventricles."],"lt":"t-cns-meninges-csf","lid":"c6","comp":171,"competencyId":"w7-csf-circulation"},{"id":"L02898","dok":1,"q":"How many lateral ventricles are there in the brain?","a":"Two, one in each cerebral hemisphere.","options":["Three","Four","Two","One"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The lateral ventricles are paired, with one inside each cerebral hemisphere. Each connects to the single third ventricle through an interventricular foramen. Together with the third and fourth ventricles, they make four ventricles in all.","notes":["Three is the number of ventricles a student gets by counting both lateral ventricles plus only one of the unpaired ventricles. The lateral ventricles alone number two.","Four is the total number of ventricles in the brain (two lateral, the third, and the fourth). Pick four if the question asked how many ventricles the brain has altogether.","Correct. There are two lateral ventricles.","One would describe the third or fourth ventricle, each of which is a single midline chamber. The lateral ventricles are paired because there are two hemispheres."],"lt":"t-cns-meninges-csf","lid":"c7","comp":170,"competencyId":"w7-ventricles"},{"id":"L02899","dok":1,"q":"Which structure runs the length of the spinal cord and is continuous above with the fourth ventricle?","a":"The central canal.","options":["The central canal","The subarachnoid space","The cerebral aqueduct","The interventricular foramen"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The central canal is a narrow channel in the center of the spinal cord, lined by ependymal cells and filled with cerebrospinal fluid. At its upper end it opens into the fourth ventricle. Some of the CSF in the system passes into it, but most leaves the fourth ventricle through the apertures into the subarachnoid space.","notes":["Correct. The central canal runs through the cord and joins the fourth ventricle.","The subarachnoid space surrounds the outside of the cord, between the arachnoid and pia, rather than running through its center. Pick it if the question asked where CSF bathes the surface of the cord.","The cerebral aqueduct is the channel through the midbrain that connects the third ventricle to the fourth. Pick it if the question asked what links those two ventricles.","The interventricular foramen connects a lateral ventricle to the third ventricle. It would be the answer for the first opening CSF passes through after leaving a lateral ventricle."],"lt":"t-cns-meninges-csf","lid":"c8","comp":170,"competencyId":"w7-ventricles"},{"id":"L02900","dok":1,"q":"Which space around the spinal cord is the site of an epidural injection?","a":"The epidural space.","options":["The subarachnoid space","The subdural space","The central canal","The epidural space"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The epidural space lies between the spinal dura mater and the bone of the vertebral canal, and it is filled with fat and veins. Anesthetic injected there bathes the spinal nerve roots without the needle entering the dura. The prefix epi means upon, so the space sits on the outside of the dura.","notes":["The subarachnoid space lies deeper, between the arachnoid and pia, and holds CSF. It would be the answer if the question asked where a lumbar puncture draws fluid.","The subdural space lies between the dura and the arachnoid and is only a potential space. Pick it if the question asked where a subdural hematoma collects.","The central canal is the CSF channel inside the spinal cord itself, which no injection targets. Pick it if the question asked which channel is continuous with the fourth ventricle.","Correct. An epidural injection goes into the epidural space."],"lt":"t-cns-meninges-csf","lid":"c9","comp":168,"competencyId":"w7-spinal-meninges-spaces"},{"id":"L02901","dok":1,"q":"What clear fluid fills the ventricles and the subarachnoid space?","a":"Cerebrospinal fluid.","options":["Cerebrospinal fluid","Venous blood","Synovial fluid","Lymph"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) is a clear, watery fluid that fills the ventricles, the central canal, and the subarachnoid space around the brain and cord. It floats the brain so it does not crush its own base, cushions the CNS against blows, and keeps a stable chemical environment around the neurons. It is made by the choroid plexus and reabsorbed at the arachnoid granulations.","notes":["Correct. Cerebrospinal fluid fills the ventricles and subarachnoid space.","Venous blood from the brain drains into the dural venous sinuses, not the ventricles or subarachnoid space. Pick it if the question asked what the superior sagittal sinus carries.","Synovial fluid lubricates the joint cavity of a synovial joint. Pick it if the question asked what fills the space inside a knee or shoulder joint.","Lymph is fluid carried in lymphatic vessels and filtered by lymph nodes. It would be the answer for a question about the fluid in the thoracic duct."],"lt":"t-cns-meninges-csf","lid":"c10","comp":171,"competencyId":"w7-csf-circulation"},{"id":"L02902","dok":1,"q":"CSF enters the cerebral aqueduct from which ventricle?","a":"The third ventricle.","options":["The central canal","The third ventricle","A lateral ventricle","The fourth ventricle"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The cerebral aqueduct is the narrow channel through the midbrain that carries CSF from the third ventricle down to the fourth ventricle. The third ventricle sits in the diencephalon, just above the midbrain. Because the aqueduct is so narrow, it is one of the points where a blockage can back up CSF.","notes":["The central canal is in the spinal cord and connects to the fourth ventricle, far below the aqueduct's starting point. Pick it for a question about the CSF channel inside the cord.","Correct. The third ventricle drains into the cerebral aqueduct.","A lateral ventricle sends its CSF into the third ventricle through an interventricular foramen, not directly into the aqueduct. Pick it if the question asked where CSF flow through the ventricles begins.","The fourth ventricle receives CSF from the aqueduct rather than sending fluid into it. It would be the answer if the question asked where the cerebral aqueduct empties."],"lt":"t-cns-meninges-csf","lid":"c11","comp":170,"competencyId":"w7-ventricles"},{"id":"L02903","dok":2,"q":"The cranial dura mater has two layers. Where they separate, they form channels that collect venous blood from the brain. What are these channels called?","a":"The dural venous sinuses.","options":["The interventricular foramina","The dural venous sinuses","The arachnoid granulations","The subdural spaces"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The two layers of the cranial dura, the periosteal and meningeal layers, are fused except where they part to form the dural venous sinuses. These channels collect venous blood from the brain and CSF returned by the arachnoid granulations. The main route runs from the superior sagittal sinus to the confluence of sinuses, through the transverse and sigmoid sinuses, and out the internal jugular veins.","notes":["The interventricular foramina are openings that connect each lateral ventricle to the third ventricle. They would be the answer for a question about CSF flow between ventricles.","Correct. The dural venous sinuses form between the two dural layers.","Arachnoid granulations are projections of the arachnoid mater that push into the dural sinuses and return CSF to the blood. Pick them if the question asked where CSF is reabsorbed.","The subdural space lies between the dura and the arachnoid, below the dura, rather than between the dura's own two layers. Pick it if the question asked where a subdural hematoma collects."],"lt":"t-cns-meninges-csf","lid":"c12","comp":167,"competencyId":"w7-cranial-meninges"},{"id":"L02904","dok":2,"q":"Cerebrospinal fluid is returned to the venous blood through projections of which meningeal layer?","a":"The arachnoid mater.","options":["The dura mater","The pia mater","The ependymal lining","The arachnoid mater"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The arachnoid mater sends small projections called arachnoid granulations up through the dura into the dural venous sinuses, especially the superior sagittal sinus. CSF in the subarachnoid space passes through these granulations into the venous blood. This completes the one-way loop of CSF, from production in the ventricles to reabsorption into the blood.","notes":["The dura mater forms the venous sinuses that receive the CSF, but the granulations that carry the fluid across belong to the arachnoid. Pick the dura if the question asked which layer forms the sinuses.","The pia mater clings to the brain surface and does not form granulations. It would be the answer if the question asked which layer forms the denticulate ligaments.","Ependymal cells line the ventricles and cover the choroid plexus, where CSF is produced, not where it is reabsorbed. Pick them for a question about what lines the ventricles.","Correct. Arachnoid granulations are projections of the arachnoid mater."],"lt":"t-cns-meninges-csf","lid":"c13","comp":171,"competencyId":"w7-csf-circulation"},{"id":"L02905","dok":2,"q":"The lateral ventricles connect to the third ventricle through which openings?","a":"The interventricular foramina (of Monro).","options":["The cerebral aqueduct","The interventricular foramina","The apertures of the fourth ventricle","The central canal"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Each lateral ventricle opens into the third ventricle through an interventricular foramen, also called a foramen of Monro. This is the first step in CSF flow after the fluid is made in the lateral ventricles. The name describes the location: inter means between, so these foramina lie between ventricles.","notes":["The cerebral aqueduct connects the third ventricle to the fourth, one step further along the pathway. Pick it if the question asked how CSF leaves the third ventricle.","Correct. The interventricular foramina link the lateral ventricles to the third ventricle.","The apertures (two lateral and one median) let CSF leave the fourth ventricle for the subarachnoid space, near the end of the ventricular route. Pick them if the question asked how CSF exits the ventricles.","The central canal runs down the spinal cord from the fourth ventricle. It would be the answer for a question about the channel inside the cord."],"lt":"t-cns-meninges-csf","lid":"c14","comp":170,"competencyId":"w7-ventricles"},{"id":"L02906","dok":2,"q":"The third and fourth ventricles are connected by which channel?","a":"The cerebral aqueduct.","options":["The central canal","The cerebral aqueduct","An arachnoid granulation","The interventricular foramina"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The cerebral aqueduct, also called the aqueduct of Sylvius, is a narrow channel running through the midbrain. It carries CSF from the third ventricle in the diencephalon down to the fourth ventricle behind the pons. Because it is so narrow, a blockage here backs CSF up into the third and lateral ventricles and causes hydrocephalus.","notes":["The central canal continues from the fourth ventricle down the spinal cord. It would be the answer for a question about the channel below the fourth ventricle.","Correct. The cerebral aqueduct links the third and fourth ventricles.","An arachnoid granulation is where CSF is reabsorbed into the venous blood, at the end of the circulation. Pick it if the question asked where CSF leaves the CNS.","The interventricular foramina connect the lateral ventricles to the third ventricle, one step earlier in the pathway. Pick them if the question asked how CSF reaches the third ventricle."],"lt":"t-cns-meninges-csf","lid":"c15","comp":170,"competencyId":"w7-ventricles"},{"id":"L02907","dok":2,"q":"Which ventricle lies between the brainstem and the cerebellum?","a":"The fourth ventricle.","options":["The central canal","A lateral ventricle","The third ventricle","The fourth ventricle"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The fourth ventricle is the CSF space that lies behind the pons and upper medulla and in front of the cerebellum. It receives fluid from the cerebral aqueduct and passes it to the subarachnoid space through its apertures and into the central canal of the cord. It contains choroid plexus like the other ventricles.","notes":["The central canal is the CSF channel inside the spinal cord, continuous with the fourth ventricle but not a brain ventricle itself. Pick it for a question about the fluid channel in the cord.","The lateral ventricles lie within the cerebral hemispheres, one on each side. Pick a lateral ventricle if the question asked which ventricle lies in the cerebrum.","The third ventricle lies in the diencephalon, above the brainstem. It would be the answer if the question asked which ventricle is enclosed by the thalami.","Correct. The fourth ventricle lies between the brainstem and cerebellum."],"lt":"t-cns-meninges-csf","lid":"c16","comp":170,"competencyId":"w7-ventricles"},{"id":"L02908","dok":2,"q":"A spinal tap, or lumbar puncture, draws cerebrospinal fluid from which space?","a":"The subarachnoid space.","options":["The subdural space","The subarachnoid space","A dural venous sinus","The epidural space"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"A lumbar puncture withdraws CSF from the subarachnoid space in the lower back, where it widens into the lumbar cistern below the end of the cord. The needle passes the epidural space, then the dura and arachnoid, before CSF appears. Because the cord ends near the L1 to L2 disc, a needle placed lower meets only the floating cauda equina.","notes":["The subdural space is only a potential space between the dura and arachnoid and holds no pool of CSF. Pick it if the question asked where a subdural hematoma forms.","Correct. A spinal tap samples CSF from the subarachnoid space.","A dural venous sinus carries venous blood inside the skull, not fluid in the lower back. Pick it if the question asked where CSF drains after reabsorption.","The epidural space holds fat and veins, not CSF, and the needle passes through it on the way in. It would be the answer for where an epidural anesthetic is placed."],"lt":"t-cns-meninges-csf","lid":"c17","comp":169,"competencyId":"w7-cord-termination-lp"},{"id":"L02909","dok":2,"q":"Which meningeal space is normally only a potential space, with a thin film of fluid between its two layers?","a":"The subdural space, between the dura and the arachnoid.","options":["The epidural space","The subarachnoid space","The subdural space","The lumbar cistern"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The subdural space lies between the dura mater and the arachnoid mater. Normally these two layers lie against each other with only a thin film of fluid between them, which is why it is called a potential space. It becomes a real space only when something, such as blood from a torn vein, pushes the layers apart.","notes":["The epidural space in the vertebral canal is a true space filled with fat and veins. Pick it if the question asked where an epidural injection goes.","The subarachnoid space is a real, open space filled with CSF between the arachnoid and pia. It would be the answer if the question asked which space holds CSF.","Correct. The subdural space is a potential space between the dura and arachnoid.","The lumbar cistern is the enlarged part of the subarachnoid space below the conus medullaris, a large pool of CSF. Pick it if the question asked where a spinal tap draws fluid."],"lt":"t-cns-meninges-csf","lid":"c18","comp":168,"competencyId":"w7-spinal-meninges-spaces"},{"id":"L02910","dok":2,"q":"From its Latin name, which meninx is the tough mother on the outside?","a":"The dura mater.","options":["The falx cerebri","The dura mater","The pia mater","The arachnoid mater"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Mater means mother, and the meninges are named from tough mother on the outside to tender mother on the inside. Dura means tough, so the dura mater is the tough outer layer. Pia means tender, so the pia mater is the delicate innermost layer, and the arachnoid, named for its spider-web look, lies between them.","notes":["The falx cerebri is a fold of the dura that dips into the longitudinal fissure, not a separate meningeal layer. Pick it if the question asked what separates the two cerebral hemispheres.","Correct. Dura mater means tough mother.","Pia means tender, so the pia mater is the tender mother on the inside, clinging to the brain. Pick it if the question asked which name means tender mother.","The arachnoid mater is named for its spider-web appearance, not for toughness. It would be the answer if the question asked which meninx is named after a spider."],"lt":"t-cns-meninges-csf","lid":"c19","comp":167,"competencyId":"w7-cranial-meninges"},{"id":"L02911","dok":2,"q":"Hydrocephalus is a buildup of cerebrospinal fluid that enlarges which structures?","a":"The ventricles.","options":["The subdural spaces","The arachnoid granulations","The dural sinuses","The ventricles"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"In hydrocephalus, CSF builds up because its flow or reabsorption is blocked, so the ventricles fill and enlarge. Because the skull cannot expand in an adult, intracranial pressure rises and the enlarging ventricles press on the brain. Narrow points such as the cerebral aqueduct are common places for a blockage.","notes":["The subdural space is a potential space that fills with blood, not CSF, in a subdural hematoma. It would be the answer for a bleed between the dura and arachnoid.","The arachnoid granulations are where CSF is reabsorbed. Poor reabsorption there can cause hydrocephalus, but the structures that swell with fluid are the ventricles.","The dural sinuses carry venous blood and receive reabsorbed CSF, but they are not what enlarges in hydrocephalus. Pick them if the question asked where CSF finally drains.","Correct. Hydrocephalus enlarges the ventricles."],"lt":"t-cns-meninges-csf","lid":"c20","comp":171,"competencyId":"w7-csf-circulation"},{"id":"L02912","dok":2,"q":"An epidural hematoma is bleeding located between which two structures?","a":"The dura mater and the skull.","options":["The dura mater and the arachnoid mater","The dura mater and the skull","The pia mater and the brain","The arachnoid mater and the pia mater"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Epi means upon, so an epidural hematoma is a collection of blood on the outside of the dura, between the dura mater and the skull. In the cranium the dura is normally attached to the bone, so the bleeding strips the dura away from the skull as it collects. Each named bleed is defined by which layers it lies between.","notes":["Bleeding between the dura and the arachnoid is a subdural hematoma. Pick this pair if the question asked about a subdural bleed.","Correct. An epidural hematoma lies between the dura and the skull.","The pia mater clings directly to the brain with no open space between them. A student who assumes every pair of neighboring layers has a space between them picks this.","The space between the arachnoid and pia is the subarachnoid space, and bleeding there is a subarachnoid hemorrhage, which mixes with the CSF. Pick this pair for a subarachnoid hemorrhage."],"lt":"t-cns-meninges-csf","lid":"c21","comp":168,"competencyId":"w7-spinal-meninges-spaces"},{"id":"L02913","dok":2,"q":"Within which structures is cerebrospinal fluid produced?","a":"The ventricles, by the choroid plexus.","options":["The ventricles","The subarachnoid space","The arachnoid granulations","The dural sinuses"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"CSF is produced by the choroid plexus, networks of capillaries wrapped in ependymal cells, which are found in the lateral, third, and fourth ventricles. The fluid then flows through the ventricles and out into the subarachnoid space. CSF is made inside the ventricles but reabsorbed outside them, at the arachnoid granulations.","notes":["Correct. CSF is made in the ventricles by the choroid plexus.","The subarachnoid space holds CSF after it leaves the fourth ventricle, but the fluid is not made there. It would be the answer if the question asked where CSF surrounds the brain.","The arachnoid granulations are the site of reabsorption, the opposite end of the loop from production. Pick them if the question asked where CSF returns to the blood.","The dural sinuses carry venous blood and receive CSF at the end of its circulation. Pick them if the question asked where reabsorbed CSF ends up."],"lt":"t-cns-meninges-csf","lid":"c22","comp":171,"competencyId":"w7-csf-circulation"},{"id":"L02914","dok":2,"q":"The cranial dura mater has two layers. What are they?","a":"An outer periosteal layer and an inner meningeal layer.","options":["An outer periosteal layer and an inner meningeal layer","An outer arachnoid layer and an inner pia layer","An outer meningeal layer and an inner periosteal layer","An outer fibrous layer and an inner synovial layer"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Around the brain, the dura mater has an outer periosteal layer attached to the inside of the skull and an inner meningeal layer facing the arachnoid. The two are fused except where they part to form the dural venous sinuses. The inner meningeal layer is also the one that folds inward to form the dural reflections, such as the falx cerebri.","notes":["Correct. The periosteal layer is outer and the meningeal layer is inner.","The arachnoid and pia are separate meninges, not layers of the dura. Pick them if the question asked which two meninges border the subarachnoid space.","This reverses the layers. The periosteal layer is the one attached to bone, so it must be on the outside against the skull.","Fibrous and synovial layers make up the articular capsule of a synovial joint. This would be the answer if the question asked about the layers of a joint capsule."],"lt":"t-cns-meninges-csf","lid":"c23","comp":167,"competencyId":"w7-cranial-meninges"},{"id":"L02916","dok":2,"q":"A ventricle is described as paired, with one chamber in each cerebral hemisphere. Which ventricle is it?","a":"The lateral ventricle.","options":["The fourth ventricle","The central canal","The third ventricle","The lateral ventricle"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The lateral ventricles are the only paired ventricles, one in each cerebral hemisphere, and they are the largest. They develop from the cavity of the telencephalon, the same embryonic region that forms the cerebrum. Each opens into the third ventricle through an interventricular foramen.","notes":["The fourth ventricle is a single chamber between the brainstem and the cerebellum. It would be the answer for a question about the ventricle drained by the apertures.","The central canal is a single, unpaired channel through the spinal cord. Pick it if the question asked which CSF space lies inside the cord.","The third ventricle is a single midline chamber in the diencephalon. Pick it if the question asked which ventricle the thalami surround.","Correct. The lateral ventricles are paired, one per hemisphere."],"lt":"t-cns-meninges-csf","lid":"c25","comp":170,"competencyId":"w7-ventricles"},{"id":"L02919","dok":3,"q":"After a head injury, blood collects in the potential space just beneath the dura, between the dura and the arachnoid. Which condition is this?","a":"A subdural hematoma.","options":["A subdural hematoma","An epidural hematoma","Hydrocephalus","A subarachnoid hemorrhage"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Sub means beneath, so a subdural hematoma is a collection of blood beneath the dura, in the subdural space between the dura and the arachnoid. That space is normally only a potential space, so the blood forces the two layers apart. Naming each bleed by its layers tells a clinician exactly where it is.","notes":["Correct. Blood between the dura and arachnoid is a subdural hematoma.","An epidural hematoma sits outside the dura, between the dura and the skull. Pick it if the bleed lay above the dura rather than beneath it.","Hydrocephalus is a buildup of CSF in the ventricles, not a bleed between meninges. It would be the answer if the question described enlarged ventricles from blocked CSF flow.","A subarachnoid hemorrhage is bleeding into the CSF-filled space between the arachnoid and pia, one layer deeper. Pick it if the blood mixed into the CSF."],"lt":"t-cns-meninges-csf","lid":"c28","comp":168,"competencyId":"w7-spinal-meninges-spaces"},{"id":"L02920","dok":2,"q":"Which structure is a network of capillaries inside the ventricles that produces cerebrospinal fluid?","a":"The choroid plexus.","options":["The cerebral aqueduct","An arachnoid granulation","The choroid plexus","A dural venous sinus"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The choroid plexus is a network of capillaries wrapped in ependymal cells. It is found in the lateral, third, and fourth ventricles, and it produces CSF from the blood plasma that passes through it. The fluid then flows through the ventricles and out to the subarachnoid space.","notes":["The cerebral aqueduct is a narrow channel that carries CSF from the third to the fourth ventricle; it moves fluid but does not make it. Pick it for a question about the link between those ventricles.","An arachnoid granulation reabsorbs CSF into venous blood at the end of the circulation, the opposite job. Pick it if the question asked where CSF leaves the CNS.","Correct. The choroid plexus produces CSF.","A dural venous sinus is a channel in the dura that collects venous blood. It would be the answer if the question asked where the arachnoid granulations empty CSF."],"lt":"t-cns-meninges-csf","lid":"c29","comp":171,"competencyId":"w7-csf-circulation"},{"id":"L02921","dok":2,"q":"Which space lies between the spinal dura mater and the surrounding bone of the vertebral canal?","a":"The epidural space.","options":["The subdural space","The epidural space","The subarachnoid space","The central canal"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The spinal dura is a single layer that is not attached to bone, so a true epidural space lies between it and the vertebrae. This space is filled with fat and veins. Inside the skull, by contrast, the periosteal dura is attached to the bone, so the cranial epidural space is only a potential space.","notes":["The subdural space lies just deep to the dura, between it and the arachnoid. Pick it if the question asked which space lies beneath the dura.","Correct. The epidural space lies between the spinal dura and the vertebral bone.","The subarachnoid space lies deeper still, between the arachnoid and pia, and holds CSF. It would be the answer for where a lumbar puncture draws fluid.","The central canal is a channel inside the spinal cord, not a space around it. Pick it if the question asked which channel continues from the fourth ventricle."],"lt":"t-cns-meninges-csf","lid":"c30","comp":168,"competencyId":"w7-spinal-meninges-spaces"},{"id":"L02922","dok":2,"q":"Which projections extend from the arachnoid mater into a dural sinus and return cerebrospinal fluid to the venous blood?","a":"The arachnoid granulations.","options":["The denticulate ligaments","The cerebral aqueduct","The choroid plexus","The arachnoid granulations"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Arachnoid granulations are small projections of the arachnoid mater that push through the dura into the dural venous sinuses, mainly the superior sagittal sinus. CSF in the subarachnoid space passes through them into the venous blood. This reabsorption balances production, keeping the volume of CSF steady.","notes":["The denticulate ligaments are tooth-like extensions of pia mater that anchor the spinal cord to the dura. Pick them if the question asked what holds the cord in place side to side.","The cerebral aqueduct is a channel between the third and fourth ventricles, not a projection into a sinus. It would be the answer for the link between those two ventricles.","The choroid plexus produces CSF in the ventricles rather than returning it to the blood. Pick it if the question asked where CSF is made.","Correct. Arachnoid granulations return CSF to the venous blood."],"lt":"t-cns-meninges-csf","lid":"c31","comp":171,"competencyId":"w7-csf-circulation"},{"id":"L02923","dok":3,"q":"A patient has fever, headache, and a stiff neck, and tests show inflammation of the membranes that wrap the brain and spinal cord, caused by an infection. Which disorder is this?","a":"Meningitis.","options":["Hydrocephalus","Meningitis","Encephalitis","Epidural hematoma"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Meningitis is inflammation of the meninges, the three connective tissue membranes around the brain and cord, usually caused by infection. The suffix itis means inflammation, and mening refers to the meninges. It is diagnosed by testing CSF drawn at a lumbar puncture, since the CSF flows between these membranes.","notes":["Hydrocephalus is a buildup of CSF that enlarges the ventricles. Pick it if the question described enlarged ventricles and raised pressure from blocked CSF flow.","Correct. Infection and inflammation of the meninges is meningitis.","Encephalitis is inflammation of the brain tissue itself rather than its membranes. Pick it if the question described inflammation inside the brain.","An epidural hematoma is a collection of blood between the dura and the skull, not an infection. It would be the answer for a bleed outside the dura after a head injury."],"lt":"t-cns-meninges-csf","lid":"c32","comp":168,"competencyId":"w7-spinal-meninges-spaces"},{"id":"L02924","dok":3,"q":"A ventricle receives CSF from the cerebral aqueduct and passes it on to both the central canal and the subarachnoid space. Which ventricle is it?","a":"The fourth ventricle.","options":["The central canal","The fourth ventricle","A lateral ventricle","The third ventricle"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The fourth ventricle sits between the brainstem and cerebellum, at the end of the ventricular route. CSF arrives from the cerebral aqueduct and leaves through three apertures (two lateral and one median) into the subarachnoid space, with some passing down into the central canal of the cord. It is the only ventricle that opens to the subarachnoid space.","notes":["The central canal receives some CSF from the fourth ventricle; it is not a ventricle that feeds the subarachnoid space. Pick it for a question about the channel through the spinal cord.","Correct. The fourth ventricle receives fluid from the aqueduct and opens to the central canal and subarachnoid space.","A lateral ventricle drains into the third ventricle through an interventricular foramen. It would be the answer if the question asked where CSF begins its route through the ventricles.","The third ventricle sends CSF into the cerebral aqueduct rather than receiving from it. Pick it if the question asked which ventricle drains into the aqueduct."],"lt":"t-cns-meninges-csf","lid":"c33","comp":170,"competencyId":"w7-ventricles"},{"id":"L02927","dok":2,"q":"Which list orders the three meninges from the skull inward toward the brain surface?","a":"Dura mater, arachnoid mater, pia mater.","options":["Pia mater, arachnoid mater, dura mater","Dura mater, pia mater, arachnoid mater","Arachnoid mater, dura mater, pia mater","Dura mater, arachnoid mater, pia mater"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"From the skull inward, the dura mater is the tough outer layer, the arachnoid mater is the delicate middle layer, and the pia mater is the thin inner layer clinging to the brain. The Latin names run from tough mother on the outside to tender mother within. The CSF-filled subarachnoid space lies between the arachnoid and the pia.","notes":["This is the correct sequence read from the brain outward. Pick it only if the question asked for the order starting at the brain surface.","This places the pia between the dura and arachnoid. The pia always lies deepest, directly on the brain, with the subarachnoid space separating it from the arachnoid.","This puts the arachnoid outside the dura. The dura is always the outermost layer, against the skull.","Correct. Dura, arachnoid, then pia."],"lt":"t-cns-meninges-csf","lid":"c36","comp":167,"competencyId":"w7-cranial-meninges"},{"id":"L02928","dok":2,"q":"Which list orders the ventricles in the direction cerebrospinal fluid flows through them?","a":"Lateral ventricles, third ventricle, fourth ventricle.","options":["Lateral ventricles, fourth ventricle, third ventricle","Fourth ventricle, third ventricle, lateral ventricles","Third ventricle, lateral ventricles, fourth ventricle","Lateral ventricles, third ventricle, fourth ventricle"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"CSF made in the lateral ventricles flows through the interventricular foramina into the third ventricle. From there it runs down the cerebral aqueduct into the fourth ventricle, which then opens into the subarachnoid space and the central canal. The numbering of the ventricles matches the direction of flow.","notes":["This skips past the third ventricle. The lateral ventricles connect only to the third ventricle, which then connects to the fourth.","This is the correct sequence read backward, against the direction of flow. Pick it only if the question asked for the order moving upstream.","This starts at the third ventricle. The lateral ventricles are upstream of the third, so fluid passes from lateral to third, not the reverse.","Correct. Lateral, third, then fourth."],"lt":"t-cns-meninges-csf","lid":"c37","comp":170,"competencyId":"w7-ventricles"},{"id":"L02929","dok":2,"q":"Which list traces the circulation of cerebrospinal fluid in order, from production to reabsorption?","a":"Choroid plexus, ventricles, subarachnoid space, arachnoid granulations.","options":["Arachnoid granulations, subarachnoid space, ventricles, choroid plexus","Choroid plexus, subarachnoid space, ventricles, arachnoid granulations","Choroid plexus, ventricles, subarachnoid space, arachnoid granulations","Ventricles, choroid plexus, arachnoid granulations, subarachnoid space"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The choroid plexus in the ventricles produces CSF, which flows through the ventricles and leaves the fourth ventricle through its apertures. It then bathes the brain and cord in the subarachnoid space and is reabsorbed at the arachnoid granulations into the venous blood of the superior sagittal sinus. The loop is one way, made inside the ventricles and reabsorbed outside them.","notes":["This is the loop read backward, starting at reabsorption. Pick it only if the question asked for the order from reabsorption back to production.","This sends CSF to the subarachnoid space before the ventricles. The choroid plexus sits inside the ventricles, so fluid fills the ventricles first and only then reaches the subarachnoid space.","Correct. Choroid plexus, ventricles, subarachnoid space, then arachnoid granulations.","This reverses the last two steps and lists the ventricles before the choroid plexus that fills them. CSF reaches the subarachnoid space before it can be reabsorbed at the granulations."],"lt":"t-cns-meninges-csf","lid":"c38","comp":171,"competencyId":"w7-csf-circulation"},{"id":"L02930","dok":2,"q":"Starting in a lateral ventricle, which list names, in order, the passages and chamber CSF moves through to reach the fourth ventricle?","a":"Interventricular foramen, third ventricle, cerebral aqueduct.","options":["Cerebral aqueduct, third ventricle, interventricular foramen","Interventricular foramen, cerebral aqueduct, third ventricle","Central canal, third ventricle, cerebral aqueduct","Interventricular foramen, third ventricle, cerebral aqueduct"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"CSF leaves each lateral ventricle through an interventricular foramen (of Monro) and enters the third ventricle in the diencephalon. It then flows down the cerebral aqueduct through the midbrain and empties into the fourth ventricle. Each narrow passage on this route is a place where a blockage can back fluid up.","notes":["This reverses the two passages. The aqueduct leads out of the third ventricle toward the fourth, so it comes last, not first.","This sends CSF into the aqueduct before the third ventricle. The aqueduct begins at the third ventricle, so fluid must pass through that ventricle first.","The central canal is in the spinal cord, below the fourth ventricle, so it cannot lie on the route from a lateral ventricle. Pick the central canal if the question asked where CSF goes after the fourth ventricle within the cord.","Correct. Interventricular foramen, third ventricle, then cerebral aqueduct."],"lt":"t-cns-meninges-csf","lid":"c39","comp":170,"competencyId":"w7-ventricles"},{"id":"L02931","dok":2,"q":"In the vertebral canal, which list orders the three meningeal spaces from the vertebral bone inward toward the spinal cord?","a":"Epidural space, subdural space, subarachnoid space.","options":["Epidural space, subdural space, subarachnoid space","Subdural space, epidural space, subarachnoid space","Subarachnoid space, subdural space, epidural space","Epidural space, subarachnoid space, subdural space"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Each space is named for its position relative to a membrane. The epidural space lies outside the dura, against the bone, and holds fat and veins. The subdural space lies beneath the dura, between it and the arachnoid, and the subarachnoid space lies beneath the arachnoid, between it and the pia, holding CSF.","notes":["Correct. Epidural, subdural, then subarachnoid.","This places the subdural space outside the epidural space. Epi means upon, so the epidural space is on the outside of the dura, next to bone.","This is the correct sequence read from the cord outward. Pick it only if the question asked for the order starting at the spinal cord.","This swaps the subdural and subarachnoid spaces. The subdural space lies just under the dura, so it comes before the space under the arachnoid."],"lt":"t-cns-meninges-csf","lid":"c40","comp":168,"competencyId":"w7-spinal-meninges-spaces"},{"id":"L02935","dok":1,"q":"Which dural reflection dips into the longitudinal fissure between the cerebral hemispheres?","a":"The falx cerebri.","options":["The falx cerebri","The falx cerebelli","The filum terminale","The tentorium cerebelli"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The dural reflections are inward folds of the meningeal dura that partition the cranial cavity. The falx cerebri is a sickle-shaped fold that hangs down into the longitudinal fissure and separates the right and left cerebral hemispheres. The superior sagittal sinus runs along its top edge.","notes":["Correct. The falx cerebri dips into the longitudinal fissure.","The falx cerebelli is a small fold that separates the right and left cerebellar hemispheres. Pick it if the question asked what divides the two halves of the cerebellum.","The filum terminale is a thread of pia mater, not dura, that anchors the lower end of the spinal cord to the coccyx. Pick it if the question asked what anchors the conus medullaris.","The tentorium cerebelli is a horizontal fold that separates the cerebrum above from the cerebellum below. It would be the answer for the fold lying in the transverse fissure."],"lt":"t-cns-meninges-csf","lid":"c4-x2","comp":167,"competencyId":"w7-cranial-meninges"},{"id":"L02936","dok":1,"q":"Which dural reflection separates the cerebrum above from the cerebellum below?","a":"The tentorium cerebelli.","options":["The falx cerebri","The falx cerebelli","The denticulate ligament","The tentorium cerebelli"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The tentorium cerebelli is a tent-like, roughly horizontal fold of meningeal dura that lies in the transverse fissure. It forms a roof over the cerebellum and supports the occipital lobes above it. Its name means tent of the cerebellum.","notes":["The falx cerebri is a vertical fold that separates the right and left cerebral hemispheres in the longitudinal fissure. Pick it if the question asked what divides the two cerebral hemispheres.","The falx cerebelli is a small vertical fold between the right and left cerebellar hemispheres. It would be the answer for a fold that splits the cerebellum side to side.","The denticulate ligaments are tooth-like extensions of pia mater that anchor the spinal cord to the dura, not dural folds in the skull. Pick them if the question asked what holds the cord in place.","Correct. The tentorium cerebelli separates the cerebrum from the cerebellum."],"lt":"t-cns-meninges-csf","lid":"c5-x2","comp":167,"competencyId":"w7-cranial-meninges"},{"id":"L02940","dok":1,"q":"How many ventricles does the brain have?","a":"Four.","options":["Five","Four","Three","Two"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The brain has four ventricles: the two lateral ventricles (one in each hemisphere), the third ventricle in the diencephalon, and the fourth ventricle between the brainstem and the cerebellum. They are connected in sequence and all contain choroid plexus. The central canal of the spinal cord is continuous with them but is not counted as a ventricle.","notes":["Five comes from adding the central canal of the spinal cord to the four brain ventricles. The central canal is continuous with the fourth ventricle but is a separate channel in the cord.","Correct. The brain has four ventricles.","Three comes from counting the lateral ventricles as one chamber, or from leaving out one of the unpaired ventricles. Both lateral ventricles count separately.","Two is the number of lateral ventricles alone. Pick two if the question asked how many lateral ventricles there are."],"lt":"t-cns-meninges-csf","lid":"c9-x2","comp":170,"competencyId":"w7-ventricles"},{"id":"L02941","dok":1,"q":"Where are the lateral ventricles located?","a":"One in each cerebral hemisphere.","options":["In the diencephalon","Between the brainstem and cerebellum","One in each cerebral hemisphere","Within the spinal cord"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The lateral ventricles are a pair of large, curved chambers, one inside each cerebral hemisphere. They hold choroid plexus and are where CSF circulation begins. Each opens into the third ventricle through an interventricular foramen.","notes":["The diencephalon encloses the third ventricle. Pick this location if the question asked where the third ventricle sits.","The fourth ventricle lies between the brainstem and the cerebellum. This location would be the answer for the fourth ventricle.","Correct. There is one lateral ventricle in each hemisphere.","The central canal runs within the spinal cord. Pick this location if the question asked about the CSF channel continuous with the fourth ventricle."],"lt":"t-cns-meninges-csf","lid":"c10-x2","comp":170,"competencyId":"w7-ventricles"},{"id":"L02942","dok":2,"q":"Which meningeal space is found in the vertebral canal but not normally inside the cranium?","a":"The epidural space.","options":["The epidural space","The lumbar cistern","The subarachnoid space","The subdural space"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"In the cranium, the outer periosteal layer of the dura is attached to the skull, so the epidural space there is only a potential space that opens if something, such as blood, separates the dura from the bone. In the vertebral canal, the spinal dura is a single layer that is not fused to bone, which leaves a true epidural space filled with fat and veins. This is why epidural injections are given in the back.","notes":["Correct. The epidural space is a real space in the vertebral canal but only a potential space in the cranium.","The lumbar cistern is also found only in the vertebral canal, but it is part of the subarachnoid space rather than a separate meningeal space. Pick it if the question asked where a spinal tap draws CSF.","The subarachnoid space surrounds both the brain and the cord and holds CSF at both sites. It would be the answer if the question asked which space is continuous from the brain down to the cord.","The subdural space, a potential space between the dura and arachnoid, exists both in the cranium and in the vertebral canal. Pick it if the question asked where a subdural hematoma forms."],"lt":"t-cns-meninges-csf","lid":"c11-x2","comp":168,"competencyId":"w7-spinal-meninges-spaces"},{"id":"L02944","dok":1,"q":"Which tooth-like extensions of pia mater anchor the spinal cord to the dura along its length?","a":"The denticulate ligaments.","options":["The denticulate ligaments","The dural reflections","The arachnoid granulations","The filum terminale"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The denticulate ligaments are extensions of pia mater that project from each side of the spinal cord and attach to the dura. They hold the cord in the middle of the vertebral canal so it does not shift side to side. Denticulate means tooth-like, which describes their pointed shape.","notes":["Correct. The denticulate ligaments anchor the cord to the dura along its sides.","The dural reflections are folds of dura inside the skull, such as the falx cerebri and tentorium cerebelli. Pick them if the question asked what partitions the cranial cavity.","The arachnoid granulations are projections of the arachnoid into the dural sinuses that reabsorb CSF. Pick them if the question asked where CSF returns to the blood.","The filum terminale is also made of pia mater, but it is a single thread that runs down from the conus medullaris to the coccyx. It would be the answer if the question asked what anchors the lower end of the cord."],"lt":"t-cns-meninges-csf","lid":"c13-x2","comp":168,"competencyId":"w7-spinal-meninges-spaces"},{"id":"L02945","dok":1,"q":"Which glial cells line the ventricles and the central canal?","a":"Ependymal cells.","options":["Oligodendrocytes","Microglia","Astrocytes","Ependymal cells"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Ependymal cells are glial cells that form the lining of the ventricles and the central canal of the spinal cord. They also wrap the capillaries of the choroid plexus, where CSF is produced, and they help circulate the fluid. Their position on the walls of the fluid spaces is the clue to their identity.","notes":["Oligodendrocytes form the myelin sheaths around axons in the CNS. They would be the answer if the question asked which cells myelinate CNS axons.","Microglia are small phagocytic glia that clean up debris and microbes in the CNS. Pick them if the question asked which glia act as the immune defense of the brain.","Astrocytes are star-shaped glia whose end-feet wrap brain capillaries and help form the blood-brain barrier. Pick them if the question asked which glia contribute to that barrier.","Correct. Ependymal cells line the ventricles and central canal."],"lt":"t-cns-meninges-csf","lid":"c14-x2","comp":177,"competencyId":"w8-neuroglia"},{"id":"L02946","dok":1,"q":"What is the enlarged, CSF-filled part of the subarachnoid space below the conus medullaris called?","a":"The lumbar cistern.","options":["The central canal","The cisterna chyli","The lumbar cistern","The epidural space"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Because the spinal cord ends near the L1 to L2 disc while the subarachnoid space continues lower, a large pool of CSF lies below the conus medullaris. This pool is the lumbar cistern, and only the floating roots of the cauda equina lie within it. It is the target of a lumbar puncture.","notes":["The central canal is inside the spinal cord itself, so it ends with the cord rather than continuing below it. It would be the answer for the CSF channel continuous with the fourth ventricle.","The cisterna chyli is a lymphatic sac in the abdomen that collects lymph at the start of the thoracic duct. The similar name is the trap; pick it for a question about where the thoracic duct begins.","Correct. The lumbar cistern is the CSF pool below the conus medullaris.","The epidural space lies outside the dura and holds fat and veins rather than CSF. Pick it if the question asked where an epidural anesthetic is placed."],"lt":"t-cns-meninges-csf","lid":"c15-x2","comp":169,"competencyId":"w7-cord-termination-lp"},{"id":"L02948","dok":2,"q":"A bleed collects between the dura mater and the skull. Which type of bleed is this?","a":"An epidural hematoma.","options":["A dural sinus clot","A subdural hematoma","A subarachnoid hemorrhage","An epidural hematoma"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"A bleed between the dura and the skull lies on the outside of the dura, so it is called an epidural hematoma, from epi meaning upon. Because the cranial dura is normally attached to bone, the blood must strip the dura away from the skull as it collects. Each named bleed is identified by the layers on either side of it.","notes":["A clot inside a dural venous sinus blocks venous drainage within the dura rather than forming a pool of blood outside it. Pick it if the question described a blocked sinus such as the superior sagittal sinus.","A subdural hematoma lies beneath the dura, between the dura and the arachnoid. Pick it if the bleed were one layer deeper.","A subarachnoid hemorrhage is bleeding into the CSF-filled space between the arachnoid and pia. It would be the answer if the blood mixed with the CSF around the brain.","Correct. Blood between the dura and the skull is an epidural hematoma."],"lt":"t-cns-meninges-csf","lid":"c17-x2","comp":168,"competencyId":"w7-spinal-meninges-spaces"},{"id":"L02950","dok":2,"q":"Which meningeal space lies between the arachnoid mater and the pia mater?","a":"The subarachnoid space.","options":["The central canal","The subdural space","The subarachnoid space","The epidural space"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The subarachnoid space lies beneath the arachnoid mater and above the pia mater, which is how it gets its name. It is a real space, crossed by the arachnoid's web-like strands and filled with CSF. Below the end of the spinal cord it widens into the lumbar cistern.","notes":["The central canal is a channel inside the spinal cord, deep to all the meninges. Pick it if the question asked which CSF space lies within the cord itself.","The subdural space lies between the dura and the arachnoid, one layer more superficial. It would be the answer if the question asked which space lies just beneath the dura.","Correct. The subarachnoid space lies between the arachnoid and pia.","The epidural space lies outside the dura, between the spinal dura and the vertebral bone. Pick it if the question asked which space lies outside all three meninges."],"lt":"t-cns-meninges-csf","lid":"c19-x2","comp":168,"competencyId":"w7-spinal-meninges-spaces"},{"id":"L02951","dok":2,"q":"Which structure separates the right and left cerebellar hemispheres?","a":"The falx cerebelli.","options":["The tentorium cerebelli","The falx cerebelli","The vermis","The falx cerebri"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The falx cerebelli is a small vertical fold of meningeal dura that projects between the right and left cerebellar hemispheres. It is the cerebellar counterpart of the falx cerebri, which separates the cerebral hemispheres. Falx means sickle, describing the curved shape of both folds.","notes":["The tentorium cerebelli separates the cerebrum above from the cerebellum below. It would be the answer for the horizontal fold over the cerebellum.","Correct. The falx cerebelli separates the cerebellar hemispheres.","The vermis is the narrow midline band of cerebellar tissue that connects the two cerebellar hemispheres; it joins them rather than separating them. Pick it if the question asked what links the cerebellar hemispheres.","The falx cerebri separates the right and left cerebral hemispheres, not the cerebellar ones. Pick it if the question asked what fills the longitudinal fissure."],"lt":"t-cns-meninges-csf","lid":"c20-x2","comp":167,"competencyId":"w7-cranial-meninges"},{"id":"L02953","dok":2,"q":"Through which openings does CSF leave the fourth ventricle to enter the subarachnoid space?","a":"The apertures of the fourth ventricle (two lateral and one median).","options":["The apertures of the fourth ventricle","The arachnoid granulations in the sinus","The interventricular foramina","The cerebral aqueduct"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The fourth ventricle has three apertures: two lateral apertures (of Luschka) and one median aperture (of Magendie). Through them, CSF leaves the ventricular system and enters the subarachnoid space around the brain and cord. These are the only openings from the ventricles to the outside of the brain.","notes":["Correct. CSF exits through the lateral and median apertures of the fourth ventricle.","The arachnoid granulations are where CSF leaves the subarachnoid space and returns to the venous blood, a later step. Pick them for a question about reabsorption.","The interventricular foramina connect the lateral ventricles to the third ventricle, at the start of the route. Pick them if the question asked how CSF reaches the third ventricle.","The cerebral aqueduct brings CSF into the fourth ventricle from the third, rather than letting it out. It would be the answer if the question asked how CSF reaches the fourth ventricle."],"lt":"t-cns-meninges-csf","lid":"c22-x2","comp":170,"competencyId":"w7-ventricles"},{"id":"L02954","dok":2,"q":"How does the spinal dura mater differ from the cranial dura mater?","a":"The spinal dura is a single layer that is not fused to bone.","options":["It is a single layer not fused to bone","It has two layers fused to the vertebrae","It is the innermost layer, on the cord itself","It forms dural venous sinuses along the cord"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The cranial dura has two layers, an outer periosteal layer attached to the skull and an inner meningeal layer. The spinal dura is a single tough layer that is not attached to the vertebrae. That gap between the spinal dura and bone is the epidural space, a real space in the vertebral canal but only a potential space in the cranium.","notes":["Correct. The spinal dura is a single layer that is not fused to bone.","This describes the cranial dura, which has two layers with the periosteal layer attached to the skull. Pick it if the question asked about the dura around the brain.","The dura is the outermost meninx at every level. The layer on the surface of the cord is the pia mater, so this is a mix-up between dura and pia.","The dural venous sinuses form only in the cranium, where the two dural layers separate. The single-layered spinal dura has no second layer to split."],"lt":"t-cns-meninges-csf","lid":"c23-x2","comp":168,"competencyId":"w7-spinal-meninges-spaces"},{"id":"L02955","dok":2,"q":"Which three components build the blood-brain barrier?","a":"Capillary endothelial cells, a basement membrane, and astrocyte end-feet.","options":["Endothelium, basement membrane, astrocyte end-feet","The dura, the arachnoid, and the pia mater","Ependymal cells, the choroid plexus, and the pia mater","Microglia, oligodendrocytes, and Schwann cell sheaths"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The blood-brain barrier is the seal that controls what passes from the blood into brain tissue. Its first layer is the endothelial cells lining brain capillaries, joined by tight junctions and lacking the pores of ordinary capillaries. These cells sit on a basement membrane and are wrapped by the end-feet of astrocytes, which help control the barrier.","notes":["Correct. Endothelium, basement membrane, and astrocyte end-feet form the blood-brain barrier.","These are the three meninges, the membranes around the brain and cord. They would be the answer if the question asked which connective tissue layers wrap the CNS.","These are structures that make and line the CSF spaces, not the barrier around brain capillaries. Pick them if the question asked what produces CSF.","Microglia clean up debris, oligodendrocytes myelinate CNS axons, and Schwann cells myelinate PNS axons; none of them forms the barrier. Pick oligodendrocytes and Schwann cells for a question about which cells make myelin."],"lt":"t-cns-meninges-csf","lid":"c24-x2","comp":172,"competencyId":"w7-bbb"},{"id":"L02956","dok":2,"q":"A buildup of CSF that enlarges the ventricles and raises intracranial pressure is called what?","a":"Hydrocephalus.","options":["Hydrocephalus","Meningitis","A subdural hematoma","An epidural hematoma"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Hydrocephalus means water on the brain. It occurs when CSF is made faster than it can flow out or be reabsorbed, so fluid builds up and the ventricles enlarge. Because the adult skull cannot expand, the extra fluid raises intracranial pressure and compresses the brain.","notes":["Correct. A CSF buildup that enlarges the ventricles is hydrocephalus.","Meningitis is inflammation of the meninges, usually from infection, not a buildup of CSF. Pick it if the question described an infection diagnosed by testing CSF.","A subdural hematoma is a collection of blood between the dura and the arachnoid. Pick it if the question described a bleed just beneath the dura.","An epidural hematoma is a collection of blood between the dura and the skull. It would be the answer for a bleed outside the dura."],"lt":"t-cns-meninges-csf","lid":"c25-x2","comp":171,"competencyId":"w7-csf-circulation"},{"id":"L02957","dok":3,"q":"A patient needs CSF drawn for testing. The needle is placed in the midline between the L3 and L4 spinous processes. Which CSF-filled space is the target?","a":"The lumbar cistern.","options":["The subdural space","The epidural space","The lumbar cistern","The central canal"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The spinal cord ends at the conus medullaris near the L1 to L2 disc, but the subarachnoid space continues lower as the lumbar cistern, a pool of CSF holding only the floating cauda equina. A needle between L3 and L4 reaches this pool safely below the end of the cord. This mismatch between cord and column is the whole basis of the lumbar puncture.","notes":["The subdural space is only a potential space between the dura and arachnoid with no pool of CSF. It would be the answer if the question asked where a subdural hematoma collects.","The epidural space is passed on the way in, but it holds fat and veins rather than CSF. Pick it if the question asked where an epidural anesthetic is delivered.","Correct. A lumbar puncture targets the lumbar cistern.","The central canal is inside the spinal cord, which ends near L1 to L2, so it is not present at L3 to L4. Puncturing the cord is exactly what the low needle position avoids."],"lt":"t-cns-meninges-csf","lid":"c26-x2","comp":169,"competencyId":"w7-cord-termination-lp"},{"id":"L02958","dok":2,"q":"A clinician suspects meningitis and orders a lumbar puncture. Which structure is inflamed in meningitis?","a":"The meninges.","options":["The epidural space","The ventricles","The choroid plexus","The meninges"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Meningitis is inflammation of the meninges, the three connective tissue membranes that wrap the brain and spinal cord, usually caused by infection. Because CSF flows in the subarachnoid space between two of these membranes, signs of the infection show up in the CSF. That is why a lumbar puncture is used to diagnose it.","notes":["The epidural space is a space filled with fat and veins outside the spinal dura, not a membrane, so it is not what meningitis inflames. It would be the answer for where an epidural injection is placed.","The ventricles are fluid-filled chambers inside the brain, not the membranes around it. They are the structures that enlarge in hydrocephalus.","The choroid plexus is the capillary tuft inside the ventricles that makes CSF, not a covering membrane. Pick it if the question asked where CSF is made.","Correct. Meningitis is inflammation of the meninges."],"lt":"t-cns-meninges-csf","lid":"c27-x2","comp":168,"competencyId":"w7-spinal-meninges-spaces"},{"id":"L02959","dok":3,"q":"During a lumbar puncture, after crossing the epidural space, the needle pierces the tough outer meningeal membrane. Which membrane is this?","a":"The dura mater.","options":["The ligamentum flavum","The pia mater","The arachnoid mater","The dura mater"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"A lumbar puncture needle passes through skin, fat, three ligaments, and the epidural space before meeting the meninges. The first membrane it pierces is the dura mater, the tough outer layer. It then passes the delicate arachnoid mater and enters the subarachnoid space, where CSF is withdrawn.","notes":["The ligamentum flavum is the tough elastic ligament crossed before the epidural space, not a meningeal membrane. Pick it if the question asked which ligament a clinician feels give way just before the epidural space.","The pia mater clings to the surface of the cord and nerve roots, deep to the CSF, so the needle does not need to reach it. Pick it if the question asked which layer lies directly on the cord.","The arachnoid mater is pierced right after the dura, just before the needle reaches CSF, and it is delicate rather than tough. It would be the answer for the last membrane crossed before CSF appears.","Correct. The needle pierces the dura mater after the epidural space."],"lt":"t-cns-meninges-csf","lid":"c28-x2","comp":169,"competencyId":"w7-cord-termination-lp"},{"id":"L02961","dok":2,"q":"At the arachnoid granulations, CSF is reabsorbed mainly into the venous blood of which dural sinus?","a":"The superior sagittal sinus.","options":["The confluence of sinuses","The superior sagittal sinus","The internal jugular vein","The sigmoid sinus"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The superior sagittal sinus runs along the top edge of the falx cerebri, in the midline at the top of the skull. Arachnoid granulations push up into this sinus, and CSF passes through them into its venous blood, completing the CSF loop. The blood then drains back toward the heart through the other sinuses and the internal jugular veins.","notes":["The confluence of sinuses is the meeting point of the major sinuses at the internal occipital protuberance, downstream of the superior sagittal sinus. It would be the answer if the question asked where the superior sagittal sinus empties.","Correct. CSF is reabsorbed mainly into the superior sagittal sinus.","The internal jugular vein is not a dural sinus; it carries blood out of the skull after the sinuses drain into it. Pick it if the question asked which vessel takes venous blood from the brain back toward the heart.","The sigmoid sinuses are S-shaped sinuses that carry blood down toward the jugular foramen, near the end of the drainage route. Pick them if the question asked which sinuses lead into the internal jugular veins."],"lt":"t-cns-meninges-csf","lid":"c30-x2","comp":171,"competencyId":"w7-csf-circulation"},{"id":"L02962","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares the three meninges?","a":"The dura is tough and outermost, the arachnoid is lace-like and middle, and the pia is thin and clings to the brain.","options":["Dura is lace-like and middle; arachnoid is tough and outer; pia is thin and on the brain","Dura is thin and on the brain; arachnoid is tough and outer; pia is lace-like and middle","Dura is tough and outer; pia is lace-like and middle; arachnoid is thin and on the brain","Dura is tough and outer; arachnoid is lace-like and middle; pia is thin and on the brain"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The meninges protect the CNS in layers. The dura mater is a tough outer membrane of dense connective tissue, the arachnoid mater is a delicate, lace-like middle membrane, and the pia mater is a thin, vascular inner membrane that clings to the surface of the brain and cord. The CSF-filled subarachnoid space between the arachnoid and pia adds a cushion of fluid.","notes":["This swaps the dura and arachnoid. The dura is the tough outermost layer, and the arachnoid is the delicate middle layer.","This swaps every description. The dura is the tough outer layer, not the thin inner one, and the pia is the thin layer on the brain.","This swaps the arachnoid and pia. The pia is the layer that clings to the brain, and the arachnoid is the lace-like layer in the middle.","Correct. Dura tough and outer, arachnoid lace-like and middle, pia thin and on the brain."],"lt":"t-cns-meninges-csf","lid":"d3-t-cns-meninges-csf-1","comp":167,"competencyId":"w7-cranial-meninges"},{"id":"L02963","dok":2,"q":"Cerebrospinal fluid is made and reabsorbed continuously. Besides cushioning and floating the brain, which job does this circulating fluid do for the neurons it bathes?","a":"It carries nutrients to them and removes their wastes.","options":["It carries nutrients to them and removes their wastes","It insulates their axons so signals travel faster","It collects venous blood and drains it toward the heart","It screens what passes from the blood into brain tissue"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Besides buoyancy and protection, CSF provides chemical stability. As it circulates, it carries nutrients, removes wastes, and keeps a steady environment around the neurons. Constant production at the choroid plexus and reabsorption at the arachnoid granulations keep the fluid fresh.","notes":["Correct. CSF carries nutrients and removes wastes, keeping the neurons' surroundings stable.","Insulating axons is the job of myelin, made by oligodendrocytes in the CNS and Schwann cells in the PNS. Pick this if the question asked what myelin does.","Collecting venous blood is the job of the dural venous sinuses. This would be the answer if the question asked what the superior sagittal sinus does.","Screening what enters brain tissue from the blood is the job of the blood-brain barrier, built from capillary endothelium, basement membrane, and astrocyte end-feet. Pick this for a question about the BBB."],"lt":"t-cns-meninges-csf","lid":"d3-t-cns-meninges-csf-2","comp":171,"competencyId":"w7-csf-circulation"},{"id":"L02964","dok":2,"q":"Which property of cerebrospinal fluid keeps the brain from crushing its own lower structures under its own weight?","a":"Buoyancy, which floats the brain and lightens its load.","options":["Chemical stability, which keeps the neurons' fluid steady","Waste removal, which clears metabolic products from tissue","Cushioning, which absorbs the force of sudden blows","Buoyancy, which floats the brain and lightens its load"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Because the brain floats in CSF, the fluid supports most of its weight, so its effective weight is far lower than its weight in air. Without that support, the brain would press down on its own base. Buoyancy is one of three roles of CSF, along with protection against blows and chemical stability.","notes":["Chemical stability is a real role of CSF, keeping the environment around neurons steady, but it does not support weight. It would be the answer for a question about the fluid's chemical job.","Waste removal is part of the chemical role of CSF and has nothing to do with supporting the brain's weight. Pick it if the question asked why CSF must circulate and be replaced.","Cushioning is a real role of CSF, but it protects against jolts and blows to the head, not against the brain's constant weight. Pick it if the question asked how CSF protects the brain in a fall.","Correct. Buoyancy floats the brain and keeps it from crushing its base."],"lt":"t-cns-meninges-csf","lid":"d3-t-cns-meninges-csf-3","comp":171,"competencyId":"w7-csf-circulation"},{"id":"L02966","dok":3,"q":"CSF drawn from the lower back can reveal an infection or bleed around the brain. Which anatomical fact explains this?","a":"The subarachnoid space is continuous around the brain and cord.","options":["The central canal carries blood down from the brain to the cord","The subarachnoid space is continuous around the brain and cord","The lumbar cistern drains directly into the dural sinuses","The epidural space holds CSF that drains from the brain"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The subarachnoid space is one continuous space that surrounds the whole brain and spinal cord, and the lumbar cistern is simply its lowest part. The same CSF circulates through all of it, so blood or infection near the brain shows up in fluid sampled from the lumbar cistern. This is why a lumbar puncture is used to diagnose meningitis and subarachnoid hemorrhage.","notes":["The central canal carries CSF, not blood, and it runs inside the cord, which ends above the lumbar puncture site. Pick the central canal for a question about the CSF channel continuous with the fourth ventricle.","Correct. The subarachnoid space is continuous around the brain and cord.","CSF drains into the dural sinuses at the arachnoid granulations, mainly into the superior sagittal sinus at the top of the skull, not from the lumbar cistern. Pick the granulations if the question asked where CSF is reabsorbed.","The epidural space holds fat and veins, not CSF, and it lies outside the dura. It would be the answer if the question asked where an epidural anesthetic is placed."],"lt":"t-cns-meninges-csf","lid":"d3-t-cns-meninges-csf-5","comp":169,"competencyId":"w7-cord-termination-lp"},{"id":"L02967","dok":2,"q":"The dura mater is the toughest of the meninges. Which tissue type is it built from?","a":"Dense connective tissue.","options":["Simple squamous epithelium","Hyaline cartilage","Areolar connective tissue","Dense connective tissue"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The dura mater is a tough membrane of dense connective tissue, packed with collagen fibers that resist stretching and tearing. Its strength suits its position as the outermost protective layer against the skull and vertebral canal. The thinner arachnoid and pia are more delicate connective tissue layers.","notes":["Simple squamous epithelium is a single layer of flat cells, such as the lining of blood vessels and alveoli. Pick it if the question asked what tissue lines the capillaries of the blood-brain barrier.","Hyaline cartilage is a firm, glassy tissue found on joint surfaces and in the costal cartilages. It would be the answer for a question about the tissue covering the ends of long bones.","Areolar connective tissue is loose tissue with widely spaced fibers that fills spaces under epithelia. It would be the answer if the question asked what tissue packs loosely around organs and vessels.","Correct. The dura is built from dense connective tissue."],"lt":"t-cns-meninges-csf","lid":"d3x-t-cns-meninges-csf-1","comp":167,"competencyId":"w7-cranial-meninges"},{"id":"L02968","dok":3,"q":"A child's scan shows enlarged lateral ventricles and an enlarged third ventricle, but a normal-sized fourth ventricle. Where is the CSF most likely blocked?","a":"At the cerebral aqueduct.","options":["At one interventricular foramen","At the arachnoid granulations","At the cerebral aqueduct","At the apertures of the fourth ventricle"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"CSF backs up behind a blockage, so every chamber upstream enlarges and every chamber downstream stays normal. The lateral and third ventricles are upstream of the cerebral aqueduct, while the fourth ventricle lies downstream of it. The aqueduct is a narrow channel through the midbrain, which makes it a common site of blockage.","notes":["A blocked interventricular foramen would trap fluid in only one lateral ventricle, leaving the third ventricle normal. Pick it if the scan showed just one enlarged lateral ventricle.","Poor reabsorption at the arachnoid granulations would back fluid up through the whole system, including the fourth ventricle. Pick it if every ventricle and the subarachnoid space were enlarged.","Correct. A blocked cerebral aqueduct enlarges the lateral and third ventricles but spares the fourth.","Blocked apertures would trap CSF inside all the ventricles, so the fourth ventricle would enlarge too. This would be the answer if all four ventricles were enlarged."],"lt":"t-cns-meninges-csf","lid":"d3x-t-cns-meninges-csf-2","comp":171,"competencyId":"w7-csf-circulation"},{"id":"L02969","dok":3,"q":"If CSF production continued normally but reabsorption at the arachnoid granulations were blocked, which condition would result?","a":"Hydrocephalus.","options":["Subarachnoid hemorrhage","Hydrocephalus","Subdural hematoma","Meningitis"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"CSF volume stays steady only when reabsorption keeps pace with production. If the arachnoid granulations cannot return fluid to the venous blood, CSF accumulates, the ventricles enlarge, and pressure inside the rigid skull rises. That buildup of CSF is hydrocephalus.","notes":["A subarachnoid hemorrhage is bleeding into the CSF-filled space around the brain. Pick it if the question described blood mixing into the CSF.","Correct. Blocked reabsorption causes CSF to build up, which is hydrocephalus.","A subdural hematoma is bleeding between the dura and arachnoid, usually from trauma, not a buildup of CSF. Pick it if the question described blood collecting beneath the dura.","Meningitis is inflammation of the meninges, usually from infection. It would be the answer if the question described an infection found by testing CSF."],"lt":"t-cns-meninges-csf","lid":"d3x-t-cns-meninges-csf-3","comp":171,"competencyId":"w7-csf-circulation"},{"id":"N-m5-meninges-csf-1","dok":1,"q":"Which thread of pia mater extends from the conus medullaris and anchors the lower end of the spinal cord to the coccyx?","a":"The filum terminale.","options":["The filum terminale","The cauda equina","The ligamentum flavum","The denticulate ligament"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The filum terminale is a slender thread of pia mater that continues down from the conus medullaris, through the lumbar cistern, and anchors on the coccyx. It holds the lower end of the cord in place. Its name means terminal thread, because it begins where the cord ends.","notes":["Correct. The filum terminale anchors the cord to the coccyx.","The cauda equina is a bundle of many nerve roots below the conus, not a single anchoring thread of pia. It would be the answer if the question asked what nerve roots hang in the lumbar cistern.","The ligamentum flavum is an elastic ligament joining the laminae of adjacent vertebrae, outside the dura. Pick it if the question asked which ligament a lumbar puncture needle passes just before the epidural space.","The denticulate ligaments are also extensions of pia mater, but they project from the sides of the cord along its length and attach to the dura. Pick them if the question asked what anchors the cord side to side."],"comp":168,"competencyId":"w7-spinal-meninges-spaces"},{"id":"N-m5-meninges-csf-2","dok":2,"q":"Venous blood from the brain flows through the dural sinuses and leaves the skull. The sigmoid sinuses drain directly into which vessels?","a":"The internal jugular veins.","options":["The internal jugular veins","The transverse sinuses","The external jugular veins","The vertebral arteries"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The main drainage route runs from the superior sagittal sinus to the confluence of sinuses, then out through the paired transverse sinuses to the S-shaped sigmoid sinuses. The sigmoid sinuses curve down to the jugular foramen and become the internal jugular veins. These veins carry the blood out of the skull and back toward the heart.","notes":["Correct. The sigmoid sinuses drain into the internal jugular veins.","The transverse sinuses come before the sigmoid sinuses, carrying blood from the confluence out to them. They would be the answer if the question asked what drains into the sigmoid sinuses.","The external jugular veins drain the scalp and face on the outside of the neck, not the dural sinuses. Pick them if the question asked which superficial neck vein drains the face.","The vertebral arteries bring blood up to the brain rather than draining it, and they unite to form the basilar artery. Pick them if the question asked which arteries supply the posterior circulation."],"comp":167,"competencyId":"w7-cranial-meninges"},{"id":"N-m5-meninges-csf-3","dok":2,"q":"During a lumbar puncture, which tough elastic ligament joining the laminae of adjacent vertebrae does the needle pass just before entering the epidural space?","a":"The ligamentum flavum.","options":["The interspinous ligament","The ligamentum flavum","The denticulate ligament","The supraspinous ligament"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"From superficial to deep, a lumbar puncture needle crosses the skin, subcutaneous fat, the supraspinous ligament, the interspinous ligament, and then the ligamentum flavum before entering the epidural space. The ligamentum flavum is tough and elastic, and clinicians feel it give way as a landmark. Flavum means yellow, from the color of its elastic fibers.","notes":["The interspinous ligament lies between adjacent spinous processes and is crossed second, before the ligamentum flavum. It would be the answer for the ligament between the supraspinous ligament and the ligamentum flavum.","Correct. The ligamentum flavum is the last ligament before the epidural space.","The denticulate ligaments are extensions of pia mater inside the dura that anchor the cord, so the needle does not pass them on the way to the epidural space. Pick them if the question asked what holds the cord to the dura.","The supraspinous ligament runs along the tips of the spinous processes and is the first ligament the needle meets. Pick it if the question asked which ligament lies most superficially."],"comp":169,"competencyId":"w7-cord-termination-lp"},{"id":"N-m5-meninges-csf-4","dok":1,"q":"What fills the epidural space of the vertebral canal?","a":"Fat and veins","options":["A thin film of fluid only","Arachnoid strands and CSF","Fat and veins","Cerebrospinal fluid"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The epidural space lies between the spinal dura and the vertebral bone, and it is filled with fat and a network of veins. The fat cushions the dural sac inside the bony canal. This is the space where an epidural anesthetic is injected, outside the dura, so the needle does not enter the CSF-filled subarachnoid space.","notes":["A thin film of fluid describes the subdural space, which is only a potential space between the dura and arachnoid. It would be the answer for the subdural space.","Web-like arachnoid strands crossing a CSF-filled space describe the subarachnoid space. Pick this if the question asked about the space beneath the arachnoid.","Correct. The epidural space holds fat and veins.","CSF fills the subarachnoid space, which lies inside the dura and arachnoid. Pick CSF if the question asked what fills the subarachnoid space or lumbar cistern."],"comp":168,"competencyId":"w7-spinal-meninges-spaces"},{"id":"N-m5-meninges-csf-5","dok":2,"q":"A patient has bleeding directly into the CSF-filled space between the arachnoid and the pia. What is this called?","a":"A subarachnoid hemorrhage.","options":["A subdural hematoma","Hydrocephalus","A subarachnoid hemorrhage","An epidural hematoma"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Bleeding into the subarachnoid space is a subarachnoid hemorrhage. Because that space holds CSF, the blood mixes into the fluid instead of forming a separate pool, and it can be detected in CSF drawn at a lumbar puncture. The bleed is named for the space it fills, beneath the arachnoid.","notes":["A subdural hematoma is bleeding between the dura and arachnoid, one space more superficial. It would be the answer if the blood collected just beneath the dura.","Hydrocephalus is a buildup of CSF in the ventricles, not bleeding. Pick it if the question described enlarged ventricles from blocked CSF flow.","Correct. Bleeding into the subarachnoid space is a subarachnoid hemorrhage.","An epidural hematoma is bleeding between the dura and the skull, outside all the meninges. Pick it if the bleed lay above the dura."],"comp":168,"competencyId":"w7-spinal-meninges-spaces"},{"id":"N-m5-meninges-csf-6","dok":2,"q":"How do the endothelial cells of brain capillaries differ from those of ordinary capillaries, helping form the blood-brain barrier?","a":"They are joined by tight junctions and lack pores.","options":["They are full of pores that let solutes pass freely","They are lined on the inside by ependymal cells","They are joined by tight junctions and lack pores","They are wrapped in myelin from oligodendrocytes"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"In most capillaries, small gaps or pores let substances leak from the blood into the tissue. The endothelial cells of brain capillaries are sealed together by tight junctions and lack those pores, so substances must pass through the cells themselves. A basement membrane and astrocyte end-feet surround these capillaries to complete the barrier.","notes":["Pores that let solutes pass freely describe leaky capillaries elsewhere in the body, such as in the kidney. This is the opposite of what the blood-brain barrier needs.","Ependymal cells line the ventricles and the central canal, not the inside of blood vessels. Pick them if the question asked what lines the CSF-filled cavities.","Correct. Tight junctions and the absence of pores seal brain capillaries.","Myelin wraps axons, not capillaries; oligodendrocytes make it in the CNS. Pick myelin if the question asked what speeds signals along CNS axons."],"comp":172,"competencyId":"w7-bbb"},{"id":"N-m5-meninges-csf-7","dok":2,"q":"Where in the brain is the choroid plexus found?","a":"In the lateral, third, and fourth ventricles.","options":["In the fourth ventricle and central canal","In the lateral ventricles only","In the lateral, third, and fourth ventricles","In the subarachnoid space and lumbar cistern"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The choroid plexus, the capillary network that produces CSF, is found in all of the brain's ventricles: the lateral ventricles, the third ventricle, and the fourth ventricle. As CSF flows from one ventricle to the next, each one adds more fluid. CSF is then reabsorbed outside the ventricles, at the arachnoid granulations.","notes":["The fourth ventricle has choroid plexus, but the central canal of the cord does not. This option misses the lateral and third ventricles and adds a channel that only carries CSF.","The lateral ventricles do hold choroid plexus and are where CSF production starts, but the third and fourth ventricles have it too. A student who thinks CSF comes from one place picks this.","Correct. Choroid plexus is in the lateral, third, and fourth ventricles.","The subarachnoid space and lumbar cistern hold CSF after it leaves the ventricles, but no choroid plexus lies there. Pick them if the question asked where CSF surrounds the brain and cord."],"comp":171,"competencyId":"w7-csf-circulation"},{"id":"L03145","dok":1,"q":"The ring of arteries at the base of the brain that joins the anterior and posterior circulations is commonly called what?","a":"The Circle of Willis.","options":["The basilar artery","The confluence of sinuses","The Circle of Willis","The choroid plexus"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The cerebral arterial circle, usually called the Circle of Willis, is a ring of connected arteries under the brain. It links the internal carotid supply in front with the vertebral and basilar supply behind through the communicating arteries. Because it is a ring, blood can sometimes take another route if one vessel narrows slowly.","notes":["The basilar artery is a single vessel formed by the two vertebral arteries, running up the front of the pons. It feeds the back of the ring, so it would be the answer for the artery that supplies the brainstem.","The confluence of sinuses is where the major dural venous sinuses meet at the internal occipital protuberance. It drains venous blood, so pick it for a question about where the superior sagittal and transverse sinuses join.","Correct. The cerebral arterial circle at the base of the brain is commonly called the Circle of Willis.","The choroid plexus is a network of capillaries inside the ventricles that produces cerebrospinal fluid. Choose it if the question asks where CSF is made."],"lt":"t-cns-brain","lid":"c13","comp":160,"competencyId":"w7-circle-of-willis"},{"id":"L03153","dok":2,"q":"Which cerebral artery supplies the lateral surface of the cerebrum, including much of the motor and sensory cortex for the body?","a":"The middle cerebral artery.","options":["The anterior cerebral artery","The middle cerebral artery","The posterior cerebral artery","The basilar artery"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The internal carotid artery divides into the anterior and middle cerebral arteries. The middle cerebral artery is the larger branch, and it runs out through the lateral sulcus to supply the lateral surface of the cerebrum. That territory includes most of the primary motor and somatosensory cortex for the body, so a blockage here often causes weakness and numbness on the opposite side.","notes":["The anterior cerebral artery supplies the medial surface of the frontal and parietal lobes. Pick it for a question about blood supply to the inner surface of the hemisphere along the longitudinal fissure.","Correct. The middle cerebral artery feeds the lateral surface of the cerebrum.","The posterior cerebral artery supplies the occipital lobe and the inferior surface of the temporal lobe. It would be the answer for a stroke that causes loss of vision.","The basilar artery is formed by the two vertebral arteries and supplies the brainstem. Choose it for a question about the posterior circulation to the pons."],"lt":"t-cns-brain","lid":"c21","comp":160,"competencyId":"w7-circle-of-willis"},{"id":"L03157","dok":2,"q":"Which two arteries unite to form the basilar artery?","a":"The vertebral arteries.","options":["The posterior communicating arteries","The vertebral arteries","The posterior cerebral arteries","The internal carotid arteries"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The two vertebral arteries travel up through the transverse foramina of the cervical vertebrae, enter the skull through the foramen magnum, and join on the front of the brainstem to form the single basilar artery. The basilar artery then runs up the pons and supplies the brainstem and cerebellum. This vertebral and basilar system is the posterior circulation of the brain.","notes":["The posterior communicating arteries join each internal carotid to a posterior cerebral artery, linking the front and back of the Circle of Willis. Choose them for a question about that link.","Correct. The two vertebral arteries unite to form the basilar artery.","The posterior cerebral arteries are the branches at the top end of the basilar artery, not the vessels that form it. They would be the answer for the arteries supplying the occipital lobe.","The internal carotid arteries form the anterior circulation and divide into the anterior and middle cerebral arteries. Pick them if the question asks which vessels feed most of the cerebrum."],"lt":"t-cns-brain","lid":"c25","comp":160,"competencyId":"w7-circle-of-willis"},{"id":"L03160","dok":3,"q":"A slowly narrowing internal carotid artery causes few symptoms because blood reaches its territory through other vessels. Which structure makes this rerouting possible?","a":"The Circle of Willis.","options":["The middle cerebral artery","The Circle of Willis","The anterior spinal artery","The superior sagittal sinus"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The Circle of Willis is an anastomosis, a ring of connected arteries at the base of the brain that joins the anterior and posterior circulations through the communicating arteries. If one internal carotid artery narrows, blood can cross the ring from the other internal carotid or from the vertebral and basilar system. That alternate route keeps blood flowing to the territory of the narrowed artery.","notes":["The middle cerebral artery is a single branch of the internal carotid that feeds the lateral cerebrum. It is a common site of blockage, not a ring that reroutes blood, so choose it for the artery supplying the motor and sensory cortex.","Correct. The Circle of Willis lets blood cross from one circulation to another when a vessel narrows.","The anterior spinal artery is a single midline vessel formed from both vertebral arteries that runs down the front of the spinal cord. It would be the answer for a question about blood supply to the anterior cord.","The superior sagittal sinus is a dural venous sinus that drains blood and CSF away from the brain. It carries venous blood out, so pick it for a question about where arachnoid granulations return CSF."],"lt":"t-cns-brain","lid":"c28","comp":160,"competencyId":"w7-circle-of-willis"},{"id":"L03164","dok":3,"q":"The brain floats in cerebrospinal fluid inside the skull. How does this fluid protect the brain?","a":"It buoys the brain and cushions it against blows.","options":["It insulates the axons so that signals travel faster","It filters harmful substances out of the blood","It buoys the brain and cushions it against blows","It anchors the brain firmly against the skull"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Cerebrospinal fluid fills the subarachnoid space around the brain and spinal cord. Floating in it greatly reduces the brain's effective weight, so the soft tissue does not crush its own base. The fluid also acts as a liquid cushion that spreads out the force of a jolt or blow before the brain strikes bone.","notes":["Insulating axons to speed signals is the job of the myelin sheath, made by oligodendrocytes in the CNS. Pick this idea for a question about why white matter conducts quickly.","Screening what passes from the blood into brain tissue is the job of the blood-brain barrier, built from tight capillary walls and astrocyte end-feet. Choose it for a question about that barrier.","Correct. CSF provides buoyancy and a shock-absorbing cushion around the brain.","CSF lets the brain float rather than pressing it against bone. Anchoring is done by structures such as the dural folds around the brain and the denticulate ligaments along the spinal cord, so pick those for a question about holding the CNS in place."],"lt":"t-cns-brain","lid":"d3-t-cns-brain-2","comp":171,"competencyId":"w7-csf-circulation"},{"id":"L03169","dok":2,"q":"Which statement best describes the main job of the blood-brain barrier?","a":"It limits what passes from the blood into brain tissue.","options":["It returns cerebrospinal fluid to the venous blood","It limits what passes from blood into brain tissue","It cushions the brain against sudden blows to the head","It produces cerebrospinal fluid from blood plasma"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Neurons need a stable chemical environment, but the chemistry of the blood changes constantly. The blood-brain barrier is built from capillary endothelial cells sealed by tight junctions, a basement membrane, and the end-feet of astrocytes wrapped around the capillary. Together they let needed substances through while keeping out many toxins, drugs, and pathogens.","notes":["Returning CSF to the blood happens at the arachnoid granulations, which drain into the superior sagittal sinus. Choose them for a question about where CSF is reabsorbed.","Correct. The blood-brain barrier controls what passes from the blood into the brain tissue.","Cushioning the brain is the job of the cerebrospinal fluid itself. It would be the answer for a question about buoyancy and shock absorption.","Producing CSF is the job of the choroid plexus, capillary networks wrapped in ependymal cells inside the ventricles. Pick it for a question about where CSF is made."],"lt":"t-cns-brain","lid":"d3-t-cns-brain-7","comp":172,"competencyId":"w7-bbb"},{"id":"L03192","dok":1,"q":"Which narrow channel runs through the midbrain carrying cerebrospinal fluid?","a":"The cerebral aqueduct.","options":["The central canal","The cerebral aqueduct","The interventricular foramen","The fourth ventricle"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The cerebral aqueduct, also called the aqueduct of Sylvius, is a narrow channel through the midbrain. It carries cerebrospinal fluid from the third ventricle in the diencephalon down to the fourth ventricle behind the pons. Because it is so narrow, a blockage here is a classic cause of hydrocephalus.","notes":["The central canal is the small CSF channel running down the center of the spinal cord, continuous with the fourth ventricle. Pick it for a question about the spinal cord.","Correct. The cerebral aqueduct runs through the midbrain.","The interventricular foramina connect each lateral ventricle to the third ventricle. They would be the answer for the openings between the lateral and third ventricles.","The fourth ventricle is a CSF space between the brainstem and the cerebellum, below the aqueduct. Choose it for the cavity behind the pons and upper medulla."],"lt":"t-cns-brainstem","lid":"c15","comp":170,"competencyId":"w7-ventricles"},{"id":"L03201","dok":2,"q":"The cerebral aqueduct connects which two CSF spaces?","a":"The third and fourth ventricles.","options":["The fourth ventricle and central canal","The fourth ventricle and subarachnoid space","The third and fourth ventricles","The lateral and third ventricles"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Cerebrospinal fluid flows from the lateral ventricles to the third ventricle, then down the cerebral aqueduct through the midbrain to the fourth ventricle. The aqueduct is the narrow link between the third ventricle in the diencephalon and the fourth ventricle behind the pons. Each connection along this route has its own name.","notes":["The fourth ventricle continues directly down into the central canal of the spinal cord. Choose this pair for a question about what is continuous with the central canal.","The fourth ventricle opens into the subarachnoid space through its apertures, the two lateral apertures and the median aperture. It would be the answer for a question about those openings.","Correct. The cerebral aqueduct carries CSF from the third to the fourth ventricle.","The lateral ventricles connect to the third ventricle through the interventricular foramina. Pick this pair for a question about those foramina."],"lt":"t-cns-brainstem","lid":"c24","comp":170,"competencyId":"w7-ventricles"},{"id":"L04523","dok":1,"q":"What is the cerebral arterial circle (Circle of Willis)?","a":"The ring of arteries at the base of the brain joining the anterior and posterior circulations.","options":["A ring of arteries around the spinal cord that feeds its gray matter","A loop of capillaries in the ventricles that produces cerebrospinal fluid","A ring of venous sinuses that drains blood from the brain to the jugular veins","An arterial ring at the brain's base joining the anterior and posterior supply"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The brain is fed by two arterial systems: the anterior circulation from the internal carotid arteries and the posterior circulation from the vertebral and basilar arteries. At the base of the brain these join in a ring, the cerebral arterial circle, through the anterior and posterior communicating arteries. Because it is an anastomosis (a connection giving blood an alternate route), it can let blood reach an area by another path if one vessel narrows.","notes":["The spinal cord is supplied by the anterior and posterior spinal arteries running along its length, not by a ring. The Circle of Willis is at the base of the brain, around the pituitary stalk.","This describes the choroid plexus. It would be the answer if the question asked where cerebrospinal fluid is produced.","This describes the dural venous sinuses, such as the superior sagittal, transverse, and sigmoid sinuses, which drain venous blood to the internal jugular veins. Pick them for a question about how blood leaves the brain.","Correct. The Circle of Willis joins the carotid and vertebral-basilar supplies in a ring."],"lt":"t-w7-circle-of-willis","lid":"w7-circle-of-willis-r1","comp":160,"competencyId":"w7-circle-of-willis"},{"id":"L04524","dok":1,"q":"Which arteries supply the anterior circulation of the brain, and which supply the posterior circulation?","a":"The internal carotid arteries feed the anterior circulation; the vertebral and basilar arteries feed the posterior.","options":["Vertebral and basilar arteries feed the anterior; internal carotids the posterior","Internal carotids feed the anterior; vertebral and basilar arteries the posterior","Internal carotids feed both, while the basilar artery drains blood to the heart","External carotids feed the anterior; subclavian arteries feed the posterior"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The anterior circulation comes from the internal carotid arteries, which enter the skull and divide into the anterior and middle cerebral arteries, feeding most of the cerebrum. The posterior circulation comes from the two vertebral arteries, which enter through the foramen magnum and join to form the basilar artery, feeding the brainstem, cerebellum, and occipital lobe. The Circle of Willis joins the two systems.","notes":["This reverses the two systems. The vertebral arteries climb through the neck vertebrae and enter at the back of the skull, so they feed the posterior brain, while the internal carotids reach the front.","Correct. Carotids supply the front of the brain, and the vertebral and basilar arteries supply the back.","The basilar artery is an artery that carries blood to the brainstem and posterior brain, not a vein. Venous blood leaves the brain through the dural venous sinuses and internal jugular veins.","The external carotid arteries supply the face and scalp, outside the skull, not the brain. The subclavian arteries give rise to the vertebral arteries but do not enter the skull themselves. Pick the external carotid for a question about blood to the face."],"lt":"t-w7-circle-of-willis","lid":"w7-circle-of-willis-r2","comp":160,"competencyId":"w7-circle-of-willis"},{"id":"L04525","dok":1,"q":"What forms the basilar artery?","a":"Union of the two vertebral arteries","options":["Branching of the middle cerebral artery","Union of the two vertebral arteries","Union of the two internal carotid arteries","Union of the two anterior cerebral arteries"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The paired vertebral arteries rise through the cervical vertebrae, enter the skull through the foramen magnum, and unite on the front of the brainstem to form the single basilar artery. The basilar artery runs up the front of the pons, supplies the brainstem and cerebellum, and then divides into the two posterior cerebral arteries.","notes":["The middle cerebral artery is the largest branch of the internal carotid and supplies the lateral surface of the cerebrum. It belongs to the anterior circulation and does not give rise to the basilar artery. Choose it for a question about blood to the lateral cortex.","Correct. The two vertebral arteries join to form the basilar artery.","The internal carotid arteries never join each other. Each divides into an anterior and a middle cerebral artery. Pick them for a question about the main feed of the anterior circulation.","The two anterior cerebral arteries are joined by the short anterior communicating artery, not fused into one vessel. They would be the answer for the arteries supplying the medial surface of the frontal and parietal lobes."],"lt":"t-w7-circle-of-willis","lid":"w7-circle-of-willis-r3","comp":160,"competencyId":"w7-circle-of-willis"},{"id":"L04526","dok":2,"q":"Which artery supplies most of the lateral surface of the cerebrum?","a":"The middle cerebral artery.","options":["Middle cerebral artery","Basilar artery","Posterior cerebral artery","Anterior cerebral artery"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The middle cerebral artery is the largest branch of the internal carotid artery. It runs out through the lateral sulcus and fans across the lateral surface of the cerebrum, supplying large parts of the frontal, parietal, and temporal lobes, including much of the motor and sensory cortex. This is why a stroke in this artery often weakens the opposite side of the body.","notes":["Correct. The middle cerebral artery fans across the lateral surface of the cerebrum.","The basilar artery runs up the front of the pons and supplies the brainstem and cerebellum. Choose it for a question about the artery formed by the two vertebral arteries.","The posterior cerebral artery supplies the occipital lobe and the inferior surface of the temporal lobe. It would be the answer for blood supply to the visual cortex.","The anterior cerebral artery supplies the medial surface of the frontal and parietal lobes, along the longitudinal fissure. Pick it if the question asked about the inner surfaces of the hemispheres that face each other."],"lt":"t-w7-circle-of-willis","lid":"w7-circle-of-willis-r4","comp":160,"competencyId":"w7-circle-of-willis"},{"id":"L04527","dok":3,"q":"An internal carotid artery narrows slowly over many years, yet the patient never has a stroke. What best explains how the brain kept its blood supply?","a":"Blood crossed through the communicating arteries of the Circle of Willis from the other sources.","options":["Blood crossed the circle through its communicating arteries","Cerebrospinal fluid delivered extra oxygen to the brain tissue","The dural venous sinuses reversed flow and carried arterial blood","The blood-brain barrier loosened so tissue could take more blood"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The Circle of Willis joins the anterior circulation (internal carotids) and the posterior circulation (vertebral and basilar arteries) through the anterior and posterior communicating arteries. When one internal carotid narrows, blood can cross the ring through these communicating arteries, from the other internal carotid or from the posterior system. Because the ring gives blood an alternate route, the brain tissue beyond the narrowed artery keeps its supply.","notes":["Correct. The ring lets blood reach the starved territory from the other arteries feeding it.","CSF cushions and floats the brain and carries some nutrients, but it cannot replace arterial blood flow. This choice confuses the fluid around the brain with the blood supply.","The dural venous sinuses carry venous blood away from the brain toward the internal jugular veins, and they do not turn into an arterial supply. Pick them for a question about where blood and returned CSF drain.","The blood-brain barrier controls what passes out of the capillaries into brain tissue; it does not change how much blood arrives. This choice confuses the barrier with the arteries that deliver the blood."],"lt":"t-w7-circle-of-willis","lid":"w7-circle-of-willis-r5","comp":160,"competencyId":"w7-circle-of-willis"},{"id":"L04533","dok":1,"q":"Which list gives the three meninges in order from outermost to innermost?","a":"Dura mater, arachnoid mater, pia mater.","options":["Dura mater, arachnoid mater, pia mater","Arachnoid mater, dura mater, pia mater","Dura mater, pia mater, arachnoid mater","Pia mater, arachnoid mater, dura mater"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The meninges are three connective tissue membranes that wrap the brain and spinal cord. The dura mater (tough mother) is the outermost layer, the arachnoid mater is the delicate, web-like middle layer, and the pia mater (tender mother) is the thin innermost layer clinging to the brain. The names run from tough on the outside to delicate on the inside.","notes":["Correct. Dura outside, arachnoid in the middle, and pia on the brain.","The arachnoid is the middle layer, not the outermost. The tough dura lies against the skull, and the arachnoid sits just beneath it.","The pia mater is the innermost layer and clings directly to the brain, so it cannot sit between the dura and arachnoid. The CSF-filled subarachnoid space lies between the arachnoid and the pia.","This is the right order read from the inside out. Pick it if the question asked for the layers starting from the brain surface."],"lt":"t-w7-cranial-meninges","lid":"w7-cranial-meninges-r1","comp":167,"competencyId":"w7-cranial-meninges"},{"id":"L04534","dok":1,"q":"Which meninx is the tough outermost layer, and how many layers does it have in the cranium?","a":"The dura mater, with two layers in the cranium: periosteal and meningeal.","options":["Dura mater, with two layers","Dura mater, with a single layer","Pia mater, with three layers","Arachnoid mater, with two layers"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The dura mater is the tough outermost meninx. In the cranium it has two layers: an outer periosteal layer against the skull and an inner meningeal layer. The two are fused except where they separate to form the dural venous sinuses, which collect venous blood from the brain.","notes":["Correct. The cranial dura has a periosteal layer and a meningeal layer.","The dura is correct, but a single layer describes the spinal dura, not the cranial dura. Choose this if the question asked about the dura around the spinal cord.","The pia mater is the thin innermost layer that clings to the brain, and it is not divided into layers. Pick the pia for a question about the vessel-rich membrane on the brain surface.","The arachnoid mater is the delicate middle layer, not the tough outer one, and it is a single web-like membrane. Pick it if the question asked which layer has web-like strands spanning the CSF space."],"lt":"t-w7-cranial-meninges","lid":"w7-cranial-meninges-r2","comp":167,"competencyId":"w7-cranial-meninges"},{"id":"L04535","dok":1,"q":"Which meningeal layer clings directly to the surface of the brain and is rich in blood vessels?","a":"The pia mater.","options":["Meningeal dura","Periosteal dura","Arachnoid mater","Pia mater"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The pia mater is the thin, delicate innermost meninx. It follows the brain closely, dipping into every sulcus, and it is rich in small blood vessels that supply the surface of the brain. Around the spinal cord it also forms the denticulate ligaments and the filum terminale.","notes":["The meningeal dura is the inner layer of the cranial dura, still far from the brain surface with the arachnoid and the subarachnoid space between them. It is the layer that folds inward to form the dural reflections, such as the falx cerebri.","The periosteal dura is the outer layer of the cranial dura, attached to the inside of the skull, the farthest layer from the brain. Choose it if the question asked which layer is attached to bone.","The arachnoid mater is the middle layer and bridges over the sulci rather than dipping into them, with the CSF-filled subarachnoid space beneath it. Pick it if the question asked which layer sits directly above the CSF.","Correct. The pia mater is the vessel-rich layer on the brain surface."],"lt":"t-w7-cranial-meninges","lid":"w7-cranial-meninges-r3","comp":167,"competencyId":"w7-cranial-meninges"},{"id":"L04536","dok":2,"q":"Which structure is a dural reflection, an inward fold of the meningeal dura?","a":"The falx cerebri (others are the tentorium cerebelli and falx cerebelli).","options":["Falx cerebri","Denticulate ligament","Arachnoid granulation","Choroid plexus"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Dural reflections are places where the inner meningeal layer of the dura folds inward to partition the cranial cavity and hold the brain in place. The falx cerebri dips into the longitudinal fissure between the cerebral hemispheres. The other reflections are the tentorium cerebelli, between the cerebrum and cerebellum, and the falx cerebelli, between the cerebellar hemispheres.","notes":["Correct. The falx cerebri is the dural fold between the cerebral hemispheres.","Denticulate ligaments are tooth-like extensions of pia mater that anchor the spinal cord to the dura. They would be the answer for what holds the cord in place along its sides.","Arachnoid granulations are outpockets of the arachnoid mater that push into the dural venous sinuses. Pick them for a question about where CSF returns to the blood.","The choroid plexus is a capillary network inside the ventricles that produces CSF. Choose it if the question asked where cerebrospinal fluid is made."],"lt":"t-w7-cranial-meninges","lid":"w7-cranial-meninges-r4","comp":167,"competencyId":"w7-cranial-meninges"},{"id":"L04537","dok":2,"q":"Why is the epidural space only a potential space inside the cranium, with no real gap between the dura and the bone?","a":"The periosteal dura is attached directly to the skull.","options":["The pia mater fills that space with its blood vessels","CSF pressure pushes the dura away from the skull","The arachnoid mater is fused to the inner skull surface","The periosteal dura is attached directly to the skull"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"In the cranium, the outer periosteal layer of the dura is attached to the inner surface of the skull, so normally there is no open gap between them. The cranial epidural space is only a potential space that opens if something, such as blood, separates the dura from the bone, as in an epidural hematoma. In the vertebral canal, the spinal dura is not attached to bone, which leaves a real epidural space filled with fat and veins.","notes":["The pia mater is the innermost layer on the brain surface, far from the skull. Its vessels supply the brain, and it would be the answer for the layer that clings to the gyri.","CSF fills the subarachnoid space, beneath the arachnoid, so it does not press the dura away from the skull. If it did, a real space would open there, which is the opposite of what is true in the cranium.","The arachnoid mater is the middle layer and lies beneath the dura, never touching the skull. This choice mixes up which meninx is against the bone.","Correct. With the periosteal dura attached to the skull, the epidural space stays only a potential space."],"lt":"t-w7-cranial-meninges","lid":"w7-cranial-meninges-r5","comp":168,"competencyId":"w7-spinal-meninges-spaces"},{"id":"L04540","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares the subdural space and the subarachnoid space?","a":"The subdural space is a potential space with a thin film of fluid; the subarachnoid space holds cerebrospinal fluid.","options":["Subdural is a potential space; subarachnoid holds cerebrospinal fluid","Subdural holds fat and veins; subarachnoid holds blood from the sinuses","Subdural holds cerebrospinal fluid; subarachnoid is a potential space","Both hold cerebrospinal fluid, but only the subdural surrounds the cord"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The subdural space lies between the dura and the arachnoid. Normally it is only a potential space, holding a thin film of fluid, and it opens up only when something like blood separates the layers, as in a subdural hematoma. The subarachnoid space lies between the arachnoid and the pia, and it is a real space filled with cerebrospinal fluid around the entire brain and cord.","notes":["Correct. Only the subarachnoid space is a true, fluid-filled space.","Fat and veins fill the epidural space, outside the dura in the vertebral canal, and neither space holds sinus blood under normal conditions. Pick the epidural space for a question about the site of an epidural injection.","This reverses the two spaces. CSF sits beneath the arachnoid (subarachnoid), which is why a lumbar puncture must pass through both the dura and the arachnoid to reach fluid.","Only the subarachnoid space holds CSF, and both spaces surround the brain as well as the spinal cord. This choice mixes up the potential subdural space with the fluid-filled one."],"lt":"t-w7-spinal-meninges-spaces","lid":"w7-spinal-meninges-spaces-r4","comp":168,"competencyId":"w7-spinal-meninges-spaces"},{"id":"L04542","dok":1,"q":"Which list correctly names the four ventricles of the brain?","a":"The two lateral ventricles, the third ventricle, and the fourth ventricle.","options":["Two lateral ventricles, the third ventricle, and the fourth ventricle","First, second, third, and fourth ventricles, all along the midline","One lateral ventricle, the third, the fourth, and the central canal","Two lateral ventricles, the cerebral aqueduct, and the fourth ventricle"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The brain has four ventricles, connected chambers filled with cerebrospinal fluid and lined by ependymal cells. There is one lateral ventricle in each cerebral hemisphere, a single third ventricle in the diencephalon, and a single fourth ventricle between the brainstem and the cerebellum. The two lateral ventricles are sometimes called the first and second ventricles, which is how the count reaches four.","notes":["Correct. Two lateral ventricles plus the third and fourth make four.","Only the third and fourth ventricles are in the midline. The lateral ventricles (sometimes numbered first and second) sit one in each cerebral hemisphere, so this choice misplaces them.","The central canal runs down the spinal cord and is continuous with the fourth ventricle, but it is not a ventricle, and there are two lateral ventricles, not one. Choose the central canal for a question about the CSF channel within the cord.","The cerebral aqueduct is the narrow channel through the midbrain that connects the third and fourth ventricles, not a ventricle itself. Pick it if the question asked what links those two ventricles."],"lt":"t-w7-ventricles","lid":"w7-ventricles-r1","comp":170,"competencyId":"w7-ventricles"},{"id":"L04543","dok":1,"q":"Where is the third ventricle?","a":"The midline cavity enclosed by the diencephalon.","options":["Behind the pons, in front of the cerebellum","One inside each cerebral hemisphere","Midline, enclosed by the diencephalon","Running down through the midbrain"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The third ventricle is a narrow, midline chamber surrounded by the diencephalon, with the thalami forming its side walls and the hypothalamus forming its floor. It receives CSF from the two lateral ventricles through the interventricular foramina and passes it down the cerebral aqueduct to the fourth ventricle. It has its own choroid plexus that adds more CSF.","notes":["This describes the fourth ventricle, between the brainstem and the cerebellum. It would be the answer for the ventricle that opens into the subarachnoid space through its apertures.","This describes the lateral ventricles, one in each hemisphere. Pick them if the question asked which ventricles lie inside the cerebrum.","Correct. The third ventricle is the midline space within the diencephalon.","This describes the cerebral aqueduct, the narrow channel through the midbrain. Choose it if the question asked what connects the third ventricle to the fourth."],"lt":"t-w7-ventricles","lid":"w7-ventricles-r2","comp":170,"competencyId":"w7-ventricles"},{"id":"L04544","dok":1,"q":"Where is the fourth ventricle?","a":"Between the brainstem and the cerebellum, behind the pons and upper medulla.","options":["Inside each of the cerebral hemispheres","Between the brainstem and the cerebellum","Down the center of the spinal cord","Within the diencephalon, along the midline"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The fourth ventricle lies behind the pons and upper medulla and in front of the cerebellum. It receives CSF from the cerebral aqueduct above and has its own choroid plexus. CSF leaves it through two lateral apertures and one median aperture into the subarachnoid space, and some continues down into the central canal of the cord.","notes":["This describes the lateral ventricles, the largest ventricles, one in each hemisphere. They would be the answer for where CSF begins its usual pathway.","Correct. The fourth ventricle lies behind the pons and medulla, in front of the cerebellum.","This describes the central canal, which continues below the fourth ventricle. Choose it for a question about the CSF channel inside the spinal cord.","This describes the third ventricle, enclosed by the thalami and hypothalamus. Pick it if the question asked which ventricle is surrounded by the diencephalon."],"lt":"t-w7-ventricles","lid":"w7-ventricles-r3","comp":170,"competencyId":"w7-ventricles"},{"id":"L04545","dok":2,"q":"Which channel connects the third ventricle to the fourth ventricle?","a":"The cerebral aqueduct, running through the midbrain.","options":["Central canal","Interventricular foramen","Cerebral aqueduct","Median aperture"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The cerebral aqueduct (aqueduct of Sylvius) is a narrow channel that runs through the midbrain, carrying CSF from the third ventricle down to the fourth ventricle. It is one of the narrowest points in the CSF pathway, so a blockage there backs up fluid in the third and lateral ventricles, one cause of hydrocephalus (a buildup of CSF that enlarges the ventricles).","notes":["The central canal continues down from the fourth ventricle through the spinal cord, one step past this connection. It would be the answer for the CSF channel inside the cord.","The interventricular foramina connect each lateral ventricle to the third ventricle, one step earlier in the pathway. Pick them if the question asked how CSF leaves the lateral ventricles.","Correct. The cerebral aqueduct links the third and fourth ventricles.","The median aperture is one of the three openings that let CSF leave the fourth ventricle into the subarachnoid space. Choose the apertures for the step after the fourth ventricle."],"lt":"t-w7-ventricles","lid":"w7-ventricles-r4","comp":170,"competencyId":"w7-ventricles"},{"id":"L04546","dok":2,"q":"Which ventricles sit within the cerebral hemispheres, and what connects each one to the third ventricle?","a":"The lateral ventricles, each connected to the third ventricle by an interventricular foramen.","options":["Lateral ventricles, by the interventricular foramina","Lateral ventricles, by the cerebral aqueduct","Lateral ventricles, by the arachnoid granulations","Fourth ventricle, by its lateral apertures"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"There is one lateral ventricle inside each cerebral hemisphere, and these are the largest ventricles. Each opens into the midline third ventricle through a small opening called an interventricular foramen (foramen of Monro). CSF made by the choroid plexus in the lateral ventricles flows through these foramina as the first step of its circulation.","notes":["Correct. Each lateral ventricle drains into the third ventricle through an interventricular foramen.","The cerebral aqueduct connects the third ventricle to the fourth ventricle, one step later. Pick it if the question asked about the channel through the midbrain.","Arachnoid granulations return CSF to the venous blood of the dural sinuses at the end of the pathway. They do not connect ventricles; pick them for a question about CSF reabsorption.","The fourth ventricle lies between the brainstem and cerebellum, not in the hemispheres, and its lateral apertures open into the subarachnoid space. Choose this pairing for how CSF leaves the ventricles."],"lt":"t-w7-ventricles","lid":"w7-ventricles-r5","comp":170,"competencyId":"w7-ventricles"},{"id":"L04547","dok":1,"q":"What is cerebrospinal fluid (CSF)?","a":"The clear fluid that surrounds, cushions, and floats the central nervous system.","options":["The clear fluid that cushions and floats the CNS","The thin fluid film between the dura and the arachnoid","The venous blood that fills the dural venous sinuses","The fluid inside neurons that carries nutrients down axons"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Cerebrospinal fluid is a clear, watery fluid made by the choroid plexus in the ventricles. It fills the ventricles and central canal and surrounds the brain and cord in the subarachnoid space. It floats the brain so it does not crush its own base, cushions the CNS against blows, and keeps a stable chemical environment around the neurons.","notes":["Correct. CSF is the clear fluid that bathes and protects the brain and cord.","The thin fluid film between the dura and arachnoid belongs to the subdural space, which is only a potential space. Pick it if the question asked what the subdural space normally contains.","The dural venous sinuses hold venous blood. CSF does drain into these sinuses at the arachnoid granulations, which is the source of this mix-up, but CSF itself is a separate clear fluid made in the ventricles.","The fluid inside a neuron is its cytoplasm, which in the axon is called axoplasm. CSF lies outside neurons, around the whole brain and cord."],"lt":"t-w7-csf-circulation","lid":"w7-csf-circulation-r1","comp":171,"competencyId":"w7-csf-circulation"},{"id":"L04548","dok":1,"q":"Where is cerebrospinal fluid produced?","a":"By the choroid plexus in the ventricles.","options":["Dura mater lining the skull","Choroid plexus in the ventricles","Pia mater on the brain surface","Arachnoid granulations in the sinuses"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"CSF is made by the choroid plexus, networks of capillaries wrapped in ependymal cells that hang into the lateral, third, and fourth ventricles. Fluid from the capillaries is processed by the ependymal cells and released into the ventricles. From there it flows through the ventricles and out into the subarachnoid space.","notes":["The dura mater is the tough outer meninx and does not secrete fluid. Choose it for a question about the layer that forms the dural reflections and sinuses.","Correct. The choroid plexus in the ventricles makes CSF.","The pia mater is the vessel-rich innermost meninx on the brain surface, but it does not produce CSF. It would be the answer for the membrane that clings to the gyri.","Arachnoid granulations are where CSF is reabsorbed into the venous blood, at the opposite end of the pathway. Pick them if the question asked where CSF leaves the CNS."],"lt":"t-w7-csf-circulation","lid":"w7-csf-circulation-r2","comp":171,"competencyId":"w7-csf-circulation"},{"id":"L04549","dok":1,"q":"In which space is CSF found around the outside of the brain and spinal cord?","a":"The subarachnoid space.","options":["Central canal","Subdural space","Epidural space","Subarachnoid space"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Around the outside of the brain and spinal cord, CSF fills the subarachnoid space, which lies between the arachnoid mater and the pia mater. Web-like strands of the arachnoid cross this space, and the fluid bathes the entire surface of the CNS. Below the end of the cord this space enlarges into the lumbar cistern, where CSF is sampled in a lumbar puncture.","notes":["The central canal does hold CSF, but it runs inside the spinal cord, not around the outside. Choose it if the question asked about the channel continuous with the fourth ventricle.","The subdural space, between the dura and arachnoid, is only a potential space with a thin film of fluid. Pick it if the question asked where a subdural hematoma collects.","The epidural space lies outside the spinal dura and holds fat and veins, not CSF. It would be the answer for where an epidural injection is placed.","Correct. CSF bathes the brain and cord in the subarachnoid space."],"lt":"t-w7-csf-circulation","lid":"w7-csf-circulation-r3","comp":168,"competencyId":"w7-spinal-meninges-spaces"},{"id":"L04550","dok":2,"q":"How is cerebrospinal fluid returned to the blood?","a":"Through the arachnoid granulations into the dural venous sinuses, such as the superior sagittal sinus.","options":["Through arachnoid granulations into the venous sinuses","Through the central canal into the veins of the spinal cord","Through the epidural space into the vertebral arteries","Through the choroid plexus back into the brain capillaries"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Arachnoid granulations are small outpockets of the arachnoid mater that push through the dura into the dural venous sinuses, especially the superior sagittal sinus. CSF in the subarachnoid space passes through them into the venous blood, which drains to the internal jugular veins. This balances production in the ventricles and keeps CSF volume steady.","notes":["Correct. Arachnoid granulations return CSF to the venous sinuses.","The central canal is a CSF channel inside the spinal cord that is continuous with the fourth ventricle. It holds a little CSF but is not the route back to the blood.","The epidural space is outside the dura and holds fat and veins, and fluid does not drain into arteries, which carry blood away from the heart toward tissues. This choice mixes up arteries with the venous sinuses.","The choroid plexus is where CSF is produced, at the start of the pathway, not where it is reabsorbed. Pick it if the question asked where CSF is made."],"lt":"t-w7-csf-circulation","lid":"w7-csf-circulation-r4","comp":171,"competencyId":"w7-csf-circulation"},{"id":"L04551","dok":3,"q":"CSF leaves the lateral ventricles. Which sequence correctly traces its path from there until it is reabsorbed into the venous blood?","a":"Third ventricle, cerebral aqueduct, fourth ventricle, subarachnoid space, arachnoid granulations, dural venous sinus.","options":["Cerebral aqueduct, third ventricle, fourth ventricle, subarachnoid space, arachnoid granulations","Third ventricle, fourth ventricle, cerebral aqueduct, subarachnoid space, arachnoid granulations","Third ventricle, cerebral aqueduct, fourth ventricle, subarachnoid space, arachnoid granulations","Third ventricle, cerebral aqueduct, fourth ventricle, arachnoid granulations, subarachnoid space"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"CSF made in the lateral ventricles passes through the interventricular foramina into the third ventricle, then down the cerebral aqueduct through the midbrain into the fourth ventricle. It exits the fourth ventricle through its apertures into the subarachnoid space, where it bathes the brain and cord. Finally it passes through the arachnoid granulations into the venous blood of the superior sagittal sinus.","notes":["The lateral ventricles empty into the third ventricle through the interventricular foramina, not into the aqueduct. The aqueduct begins at the bottom of the third ventricle, so it comes one step later.","The cerebral aqueduct lies between the third and fourth ventricles, so it cannot come after the fourth ventricle. This order skips the only channel that links the two midline ventricles.","Correct. This is the one-way loop from the ventricles to the venous blood.","CSF must circulate through the subarachnoid space before it can reach the arachnoid granulations, which project from that space into the sinuses. Reversing the last two steps puts reabsorption before the fluid gets there."],"lt":"t-w7-csf-circulation","lid":"w7-csf-circulation-r5","comp":171,"competencyId":"w7-csf-circulation"},{"id":"L04587","dok":1,"q":"Which structures form the blood-brain barrier?","a":"Capillary endothelial cells joined by tight junctions, their basement membrane, and astrocyte end-feet.","options":["Microglia that wrap each capillary and digest anything that leaks out","Tight-junction capillary walls, basement membrane, and astrocyte feet","The three meninges: dura, arachnoid, and pia mater around the brain","Choroid plexus capillaries and the ependymal cells lining the ventricles"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The blood-brain barrier is a selective seal between the blood and the brain tissue. It is built from three components: capillary endothelial cells joined by tight junctions and lacking the pores of ordinary capillaries, the basement membrane around them, and the end-feet of astrocytes that wrap the capillary. Together they control what passes from the blood into the brain, protecting neurons from changes in blood chemistry.","notes":["Microglia are the immune cells of the CNS that engulf debris and microbes, but they are not part of the barrier. Astrocytes, not microglia, send end-feet around the capillaries.","Correct. Sealed capillary walls, their basement membrane, and astrocyte end-feet form the barrier.","The meninges protect the brain as wrapping membranes, but they do not screen what leaves the capillaries inside brain tissue. Pick them for a question about the three connective tissue layers around the CNS.","The choroid plexus and its ependymal cells make CSF inside the ventricles; they are not the seal around the capillaries in brain tissue. Choose them for a question about where CSF is made."],"lt":"t-w7-bbb","lid":"w7-bbb-r1","comp":172,"competencyId":"w7-bbb"},{"id":"N-a17-172-1","dok":1,"q":"What are the astrocyte end-feet of the blood-brain barrier?","a":"Flattened ends of astrocyte processes that wrap the capillary.","options":["A thin layer of extracellular material around the endothelium","Endothelial cells joined by tight junctions inside the capillary","Flattened ends of astrocyte processes that wrap the capillary","Pores in the capillary wall that let glucose into the brain"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Astrocytes are star-shaped glial cells. Their processes end in flattened feet that wrap around the outside of the brain capillaries, covering the basement membrane. The end-feet support the endothelial cells and help keep the tight junctions in place.","notes":["A thin layer of extracellular material around the endothelium is the basement membrane, the second component of the barrier.","Endothelial cells joined by tight junctions form the capillary lining, the first and most important component of the barrier.","Correct. Astrocyte end-feet are the flattened ends of astrocyte processes that wrap the outside of the capillary.","Brain capillaries lack the pores found in many capillaries elsewhere. That lack of pores is part of what makes the barrier tight."],"comp":172,"competencyId":"w7-bbb"},{"id":"N-a17-172-2","dok":2,"q":"Brain capillaries have tight junctions and no pores. How must most substances cross from the blood into brain tissue?","a":"By passing through the endothelial cells themselves.","options":["By slipping through the gaps between the endothelial cells","By passing through the endothelial cells themselves","Through pores in the capillary wall","They cannot cross at all, so the brain takes nothing from the blood"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The endothelial cells lining brain capillaries are joined by tight junctions that seal the gaps between them, and these capillaries lack the pores and wide clefts found in many capillaries elsewhere. With no way around the cells, most substances can cross only by passing through the endothelial cells themselves. That is why the capillary endothelium is the most important part of the barrier.","notes":["Tight junctions seal the gaps between neighboring endothelial cells in brain capillaries, so this route is closed. Many capillaries elsewhere in the body have wide clefts between cells that substances can slip through.","Correct. With the gaps sealed and no pores, most substances can cross only by passing through the endothelial cells.","Brain capillaries lack the pores found in many capillaries elsewhere in the body, so there are no pores to pass through.","The barrier still lets in what neurons need, such as glucose and oxygen, so substances do cross. It is selective, not a solid wall."],"comp":172,"competencyId":"w7-bbb"},{"id":"N-a17-172-3","dok":2,"q":"Moving from the blood outward into brain tissue, in which order do you cross the parts of the blood-brain barrier?","a":"Endothelium, basement membrane, astrocyte end-feet.","options":["Basement membrane, endothelium, astrocyte end-feet","Astrocyte end-feet, basement membrane, endothelium","Endothelium, astrocyte end-feet, basement membrane","Endothelium, basement membrane, astrocyte end-feet"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The capillary endothelium lines the vessel, so it is the first layer the blood touches. The basement membrane surrounds the endothelium, and the astrocyte end-feet wrap the outside of the capillary, covering the basement membrane.","notes":["The basement membrane surrounds the endothelium, so it comes after the endothelium, not before.","This is the order from brain tissue inward to the blood. Moving from the blood outward, the endothelium comes first.","The end-feet cover the basement membrane from outside, so the basement membrane comes before the end-feet.","Correct. From the blood outward, the order is endothelium, basement membrane, then astrocyte end-feet."],"comp":172,"competencyId":"w7-bbb"},{"id":"N-a17-172-4","dok":3,"q":"A few small areas around the ventricles, such as parts of the hypothalamus, have no blood-brain barrier. Why does the brain leave these spots open?","a":"So the brain can sample the blood directly in those spots.","options":["So glucose and oxygen can get in, since the barrier blocks them","So the brain can sample the blood directly in those spots","So the drugs and germs the barrier keeps out can reach neurons","Because astrocyte end-feet are found only in the brainstem"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The blood-brain barrier is missing in a few small areas around the ventricles, such as parts of the hypothalamus, where the brain needs to sample the blood directly. Everywhere else the barrier keeps out many drugs and germs while still letting in what neurons need, such as glucose and oxygen.","notes":["The barrier already lets in what neurons need, such as glucose and oxygen, so it does not need gaps for them.","Correct. These open spots let the brain sample the blood directly.","Keeping out many drugs and germs is the barrier's job. The open spots exist so the brain can sample the blood, not to let harmful substances in.","Astrocyte end-feet wrap brain capillaries as part of the barrier. The open spots are around the ventricles, such as parts of the hypothalamus, which is not in the brainstem."],"comp":172,"competencyId":"w7-bbb"},{"id":"N-a17d-160-1","dok":3,"q":"A blockage sits in the artery that runs into the longitudinal fissure and arches over the corpus callosum. Which artery is it, and which part of the body would be most affected on the opposite side?","a":"The anterior cerebral artery, with the opposite leg most affected.","options":["The anterior cerebral artery, with the opposite leg most affected","The middle cerebral artery, with the opposite face and arm most affected","The anterior cerebral artery, with the opposite face and arm most affected","The posterior cerebral artery, with the opposite leg most affected"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The anterior cerebral artery runs forward and medially into the longitudinal fissure and arches over the corpus callosum. It supplies the medial surface of the frontal and parietal lobes, including the leg and foot areas of the motor and somatosensory cortex. A stroke in this artery affects the opposite leg most.","notes":["Correct. The anterior cerebral artery supplies the medial surface, including the leg and foot areas, so the opposite leg is hit hardest.","This pairing is true of the middle cerebral artery, but that artery runs laterally into the lateral sulcus. The artery over the corpus callosum is the anterior cerebral.","The artery is right, but the face and arm areas are on the lateral surface, supplied by the middle cerebral artery. The anterior cerebral supplies the leg area.","The posterior cerebral artery curves around the midbrain to supply the occipital lobe and visual cortex. It does not arch over the corpus callosum."],"comp":160,"competencyId":"w7-circle-of-willis"},{"id":"N-a17d-167-1","dok":3,"q":"A clot blocks the right transverse sinus. Blood coming back along the superior sagittal sinus reaches which meeting point first, and which sinus on the right side loses its inflow?","a":"The confluence of sinuses, and the right sigmoid sinus.","options":["The straight sinus, and the right sigmoid sinus","The confluence of sinuses, and the straight sinus","The confluence of sinuses, and the right sigmoid sinus","The jugular foramen, and the inferior sagittal sinus"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Venous blood runs from the superior sagittal sinus to the confluence of sinuses at the back of the skull. From there the paired transverse sinuses run laterally, each curving down as an S-shaped sigmoid sinus to the jugular foramen. A clot in the right transverse sinus sits between the confluence and the right sigmoid sinus, so the right sigmoid sinus loses its inflow.","notes":["The straight sinus runs where the falx cerebri meets the tentorium and also drains into the confluence. The superior sagittal sinus empties into the confluence, not the straight sinus.","The confluence is right, but the straight sinus drains into the confluence. It is upstream of the transverse sinus, not fed by it.","Correct. The superior sagittal sinus empties into the confluence, and the right sigmoid sinus is fed by the blocked right transverse sinus.","The jugular foramen is where the sigmoid sinus leaves the skull, at the far end of the route. The inferior sagittal sinus runs in the lower edge of the falx cerebri and drains into the straight sinus."],"comp":167,"competencyId":"w7-cranial-meninges"},{"id":"N-a17d-167-2","dok":3,"q":"A sheet of dura lies in the transverse fissure like a tent over the cerebellum. Which fold is it, and which sinuses run along its back edge?","a":"The tentorium cerebelli, with the transverse sinuses.","options":["The tentorium cerebelli, with the transverse sinuses","The tentorium cerebelli, with the superior sagittal sinus","The falx cerebelli, with the transverse sinuses","The falx cerebri, with the superior sagittal sinus"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Three dural reflections divide the cranial cavity. The tentorium cerebelli lies in the transverse fissure, like a tent over the cerebellum, and separates it from the occipital lobes above. The paired transverse sinuses run laterally from the confluence of sinuses along its back edge.","notes":["Correct. The tentorium cerebelli roofs the cerebellum in the transverse fissure, and the transverse sinuses run along its back edge.","The fold is right, but the superior sagittal sinus runs along the top edge of the falx cerebri, in the midline. The transverse sinuses run along the tentorium.","The falx cerebelli is a small midline fold below the tentorium that separates the two cerebellar hemispheres. It is not the tent-like sheet roofing the cerebellum.","The falx cerebri drops into the longitudinal fissure between the cerebral hemispheres, and the superior sagittal sinus runs along its top edge. It does not lie over the cerebellum."],"comp":167,"competencyId":"w7-cranial-meninges"},{"id":"N-a17d-167-3","dok":3,"q":"In a dissection, you lift the arachnoid off the brain and find a very thin, vessel-rich membrane that follows the brain into every sulcus. What is this membrane, and what space did you open by lifting the arachnoid?","a":"The pia mater, and the subarachnoid space.","options":["The arachnoid strands, and the epidural space","The pia mater, and the subarachnoid space","The pia mater, and the subdural space","The meningeal dura, and the subarachnoid space"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The three meninges from outside in are the dura, the arachnoid, and the pia. The arachnoid is thin and delicate and does not dip into the grooves, while the pia is very thin, rich in small blood vessels, and follows every gyrus and sulcus. Lifting the arachnoid opens the space between the two, which is the subarachnoid space, filled with CSF and crossed by fine arachnoid strands.","notes":["The arachnoid strands are fine threads crossing to the pia, not a membrane. The epidural space lies outside the dura, against the bone, and is only a potential space in the skull.","Correct. The thin membrane in every sulcus is the pia mater, and the CSF-filled space between it and the arachnoid is the subarachnoid space.","The membrane is right, but the subdural space lies between the dura and the arachnoid, above the layer you lifted. The space under the arachnoid is subarachnoid.","The meningeal dura is the inner layer of the tough dura mater, outside the arachnoid. It does not follow the brain into the sulci."],"comp":167,"competencyId":"w7-cranial-meninges"},{"id":"N-a17d-168-1","dok":3,"q":"On a model of the lower spinal canal, you follow the filum terminale down from the tip of the spinal cord. Which space does it pass through, what covering does it pick up, and where does it anchor?","a":"Through the lumbar cistern, picking up dura, to anchor on the coccyx.","options":["Through the epidural space, picking up arachnoid, to anchor on the sacrum","Through the central canal, picking up pia, to anchor on the coccyx","Through the lumbar cistern, picking up dura, to anchor on the coccyx","Through the lumbar cistern, picking up arachnoid, to anchor at L2"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The filum terminale is a thin strand of pia mater that continues down from the bottom tip of the spinal cord. It passes through the lumbar cistern, the wide subarachnoid space below the cord. At the end of the dural sac it picks up a covering of dura, and then it anchors to the coccyx.","notes":["The filum starts inside the subarachnoid space, not the epidural space. It picks up dura, not arachnoid, and anchors on the coccyx.","The central canal runs inside the spinal cord. The filum is already made of pia, and it runs outside the cord through the lumbar cistern.","Correct. The filum terminale passes through the lumbar cistern, picks up a covering of dura at the end of the dural sac, and anchors to the coccyx.","The lumbar cistern is right, but the covering it picks up is dura, at the end of the dural sac near S2. The cord itself ends near L1 to L2, and the filum goes on to the coccyx."],"comp":168,"competencyId":"w7-spinal-meninges-spaces"},{"id":"N-a17d-169-1","dok":1,"q":"In an adult, at about what level does the spinal cord end?","a":"At the disc between L1 and L2.","options":["At the disc between L4 and L5","At about the level of S2","At the tip of the coccyx","At the disc between L1 and L2"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The spinal cord is shorter than the vertebral column. In an adult it ends at about the level of the disc between L1 and L2, as the conus medullaris. The dural sac and subarachnoid space continue lower, to about S2.","notes":["L4 to L5 is one of the lumbar puncture sites, chosen because it is well below the end of the cord.","S2 is where the dural sac and subarachnoid space end. The cord itself stops much higher.","The coccyx is where the filum terminale anchors. The filum is a strand of pia, not the cord itself.","Correct. The adult spinal cord ends at about the level of the L1 to L2 disc."],"comp":169,"competencyId":"w7-cord-termination-lp"},{"id":"N-a17d-169-2","dok":1,"q":"What is the tapered, cone-shaped lower end of the spinal cord called?","a":"The conus medullaris.","options":["The conus medullaris","The lumbar cistern","The filum terminale","The cauda equina"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The spinal cord does not end abruptly. It tapers to a cone-shaped tip called the conus medullaris, at about the L1 to L2 disc in an adult. Below it lie the roots of the cauda equina and the filum terminale.","notes":["Correct. The conus medullaris is the cone-shaped end of the cord, near the L1 to L2 disc in adults.","The lumbar cistern is the enlarged subarachnoid space below the cord, filled with CSF. It is a space, not part of the cord.","The filum terminale is the thin strand of pia that continues down from the tip of the cord to the coccyx.","The cauda equina is the bundle of long lumbar, sacral, and coccygeal nerve roots below the end of the cord. It is roots, not the end of the cord itself."],"comp":169,"competencyId":"w7-cord-termination-lp"},{"id":"N-a17d-169-3","dok":1,"q":"What is the bundle of long lumbar, sacral, and coccygeal nerve roots below the end of the spinal cord called?","a":"The cauda equina.","options":["The filum terminale","The denticulate ligaments","The conus medullaris","The cauda equina"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The lumbar, sacral, and coccygeal nerve roots leave the cord above its end. They must travel down the vertebral canal before reaching their exit openings. This bundle of long roots below the conus medullaris is the cauda equina, which means horse's tail.","notes":["The filum terminale is a single strand of pia that anchors the cord to the coccyx. It runs among the roots but is not made of nerve roots.","The denticulate ligaments are tooth-shaped flaps of pia along the sides of the cord that attach to the dura and keep it centered.","The conus medullaris is the cone-shaped end of the cord itself. The roots hang below it.","Correct. The cauda equina, meaning horse's tail, is the bundle of long roots below the conus medullaris."],"comp":169,"competencyId":"w7-cord-termination-lp"},{"id":"N-a17d-170-1","dok":3,"q":"A tumor blocks the cerebral aqueduct. Which ventricles would fill with trapped cerebrospinal fluid and enlarge?","a":"Both lateral ventricles and the third.","options":["Only the fourth ventricle below it","Both lateral ventricles and the third","Only the two lateral ventricles","All four ventricles of the brain"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"CSF moves from each lateral ventricle through an interventricular foramen into the third ventricle. The third ventricle drains down through the cerebral aqueduct into the fourth ventricle. A block in the aqueduct traps the fluid in every space above it, the third and both lateral ventricles, while the fourth ventricle lies below the block.","notes":["The fourth ventricle lies below the aqueduct. Fluid backs up above a blockage, not below it.","Correct. The lateral ventricles drain into the third, which drains through the aqueduct, so everything above the block fills up.","The third ventricle sits between the lateral ventricles and the aqueduct, so it fills too. Fluid from the lateral ventricles passes through it on the way to the aqueduct.","The fourth ventricle is downstream of the aqueduct, so it would not fill with trapped fluid. Only the spaces above the block back up."],"comp":170,"competencyId":"w7-ventricles"},{"id":"N-a17d-170-2","dok":3,"q":"On a midsagittal section, a thin sheet hangs below the corpus callosum, and a small hole sits at the front of the third ventricle. What does the sheet separate, and what does the hole connect?","a":"The lateral ventricles; a lateral ventricle to the third ventricle.","options":["The lateral ventricles; a lateral ventricle to the third ventricle","The third and fourth ventricles; the third ventricle to the aqueduct","The two thalami; the third ventricle to the central canal","The lateral ventricles; the third ventricle to the subarachnoid space"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The thin sheet below the corpus callosum is the septum pellucidum, which separates the two lateral ventricles in the midline. Each lateral ventricle opens into the third ventricle through a small hole, the interventricular foramen (foramen of Monro). There is one foramen on each side, at the front of the third ventricle.","notes":["Correct. The septum pellucidum separates the two lateral ventricles, and each interventricular foramen joins a lateral ventricle to the third ventricle.","The third and fourth ventricles are joined by the cerebral aqueduct through the midbrain, at the back of the third ventricle. No sheet separates them.","The two thalami are separated by the third ventricle itself. The central canal continues down only from the fourth ventricle.","The sheet is right, but only the apertures of the fourth ventricle open into the subarachnoid space. The hole at the front of the third ventricle is an interventricular foramen."],"comp":170,"competencyId":"w7-ventricles"},{"id":"N-a17d-172-1","dok":1,"q":"Which type of junction seals the gaps between the endothelial cells of brain capillaries?","a":"Tight junctions.","options":["Desmosomes","Fenestrations","Tight junctions","Gap junctions"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The capillary endothelium is the first and most important part of the blood-brain barrier. Its cells are joined by tight junctions, which seal the gaps between neighboring cells. With no pores and no wide clefts, most substances can only cross by passing through the endothelial cells themselves.","notes":["Desmosomes strongly bind neighboring cells together so they do not separate under tension. They do not seal the passageway between cells the way tight junctions do.","Fenestrations are pores in a capillary wall, like those in the glomerulus. Brain capillaries lack them, which is part of what makes the barrier.","Correct. Tight junctions seal the gaps between brain capillary endothelial cells, the actual seal of the barrier.","Gap junctions are channels that connect two cells directly, as at an electrical synapse. They do not seal the spaces between cells."],"comp":172,"competencyId":"w7-bbb"},{"id":"N-a17d-172-2","dok":1,"q":"What is the thin layer of extracellular material that surrounds the endothelium of a brain capillary in the blood-brain barrier?","a":"The basement membrane.","options":["The basement membrane","The astrocyte end-feet","The myelin sheath","The pia mater"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The blood-brain barrier has three components. The capillary endothelium is joined by tight junctions, and around it lies the basement membrane, a thin layer of extracellular material. Astrocyte end-feet wrap the outside of the capillary, covering the basement membrane.","notes":["Correct. The basement membrane is the thin layer of extracellular material around the endothelium.","Astrocyte end-feet are the flattened ends of glial cell processes, not extracellular material. They cover the basement membrane from outside.","Myelin is the fatty wrapping around many axons. It is not part of the capillary wall.","The pia mater is the innermost meninx on the brain surface. It covers the brain, not each capillary."],"comp":172,"competencyId":"w7-bbb"},{"id":"N-a17d-172-3","dok":3,"q":"An electron micrograph of a brain capillary shows flattened cell processes covering the outside of the capillary wall. Which cells do these processes come from, and which layer lies just beneath them?","a":"Astrocytes, with the basement membrane just beneath.","options":["Astrocytes, with the pia mater just beneath","Oligodendrocytes, with the basement membrane just beneath","Ependymal cells, with the endothelium just beneath","Astrocytes, with the basement membrane just beneath"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"From the blood outward, the blood-brain barrier is the capillary endothelium, the basement membrane, and the astrocyte end-feet. Astrocytes are star-shaped glial cells whose processes end in flattened feet that wrap the outside of the capillary. Those feet cover the basement membrane, the thin layer of extracellular material around the endothelium.","notes":["The cells are right, but the pia mater is the innermost meninx on the brain surface, not part of each capillary wall. The end-feet cover the basement membrane.","Oligodendrocytes make myelin in the CNS and do not wrap capillaries. The flattened feet on a brain capillary belong to astrocytes, the star-shaped glial cells.","Ependymal cells line the ventricles, not the outside of capillaries. The end-feet also sit on the basement membrane, which lies between them and the endothelium.","Correct. Astrocyte end-feet wrap the outside of the capillary and cover the basement membrane that surrounds the endothelium."],"comp":172,"competencyId":"w7-bbb"},{"id":"N-a17d-172-4","dok":3,"q":"A drug passes easily out of capillaries in many tissues but cannot cross from the blood into brain tissue. Which feature of the brain capillary wall best explains this, and which cells help keep that feature in place?","a":"Tight junctions with no pores, kept in place by astrocyte end-feet.","options":["Tight junctions with no pores, kept in place by oligodendrocytes","Tight junctions with no pores, kept in place by astrocyte end-feet","A thick basement membrane, made by ependymal cells","Pores in the endothelium, kept open by astrocyte end-feet"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Brain capillaries differ from many others in the body. Their endothelial cells are sealed by tight junctions and lack pores and wide clefts, so substances cannot slip between the cells. Astrocyte end-feet wrap the capillary, support the endothelial cells, and help keep the tight junctions in place.","notes":["The feature is right, but oligodendrocytes make myelin in the CNS. The glial cells that wrap capillaries and support the tight junctions are astrocytes.","Correct. Tight junctions and the lack of pores seal brain capillaries, and astrocyte end-feet help keep the tight junctions in place.","The basement membrane is part of the barrier, but the tight junctions in the endothelium are the actual seal. Ependymal cells line the ventricles, not the capillaries.","Brain capillaries lack pores. Pores would make substances cross more easily, not less."],"comp":172,"competencyId":"w7-bbb"}]},{"id":"m5-spinal-cord","title":"The spinal cord","summary":"external anatomy, enlargements, conus medullaris, cauda equina, spinal meninges and spaces, lumbar puncture, internal gray and white matter, roots, dorsal root ganglion, reflex arc","mw":"2026-12-02","tr":"2026-12-03","cards":[{"id":"L02707","dok":3,"q":"In a withdrawal reflex, a neuron inside the gray matter of the spinal cord links the incoming sensory neuron to the outgoing motor neuron. By function, which type of neuron is it?","a":"An interneuron.","options":["An interneuron","A motor neuron","A bipolar neuron","A sensory neuron"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Interneurons, also called association neurons, lie entirely within the brain and spinal cord and connect sensory neurons to motor neurons. In a withdrawal reflex the sensory neuron enters the posterior horn of the cord, where it synapses with interneurons that then pass the signal to motor neurons. Because the pathway has interneurons, the withdrawal reflex is a polysynaptic reflex.","notes":["Correct. Interneurons lie within the CNS and connect sensory neurons to motor neurons.","The motor neuron carries the command out of the cord to the effector muscle. Choose it for a question about the neuron that leaves through the anterior root.","Bipolar is a structural class (one dendrite and one axon) found in the retina, inner ear, and olfactory area, not a functional type. It would be right for a question about neurons in a special sense organ.","The sensory neuron is the one bringing the signal in from the receptor, so it is the neuron being linked, not the link. Pick it for a question about the neuron that carries the signal into the cord."],"lt":"t-nervous-tissue","lid":"c32","comp":176,"competencyId":"w8-neuron-classification"},{"id":"L02733","dok":1,"q":"Where is the gray matter located in the spinal cord?","a":"In the inner core.","options":["In the posterior roots","In the outer layer","In the spinal nerves","In the inner core"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"In a cross-section of the spinal cord, the gray matter forms a butterfly-shaped core of neuron cell bodies, dendrites, and unmyelinated axons. It is surrounded by white matter, made of myelinated axons arranged in tracts. This is the reverse of the brain, where the gray matter forms the outer cortex.","notes":["The posterior roots are bundles of sensory axons outside the cord, with cell bodies in the posterior root ganglion. Choose them for a question about where sensory axons enter the cord.","The outer layer of the cord is white matter, made of myelinated axons in tracts. Pick the outer layer for a question about where white matter sits in the spinal cord, or where gray matter sits in the brain.","Spinal nerves are PNS bundles of axons formed where the roots join, outside the cord. They would be right for a question about the mixed nerves leaving the vertebral canal.","Correct. Gray matter forms the butterfly-shaped core of the spinal cord."],"lt":"t-nervous-tissue","lid":"c13-x2","comp":180,"competencyId":"w8-gray-white-matter"},{"id":"L02755","dok":3,"q":"In a patellar reflex, the sensory neuron carrying the signal from the thigh muscle to the spinal cord is cut. What happens when the tendon below the kneecap is tapped?","a":"No reflex occurs, because the signal never reaches the spinal cord.","options":["The leg kicks harder, because the cord gets no braking signal","The reflex still occurs, because the brain sends the command","No reflex occurs, because the signal never reaches the cord","The reflex still occurs, because the motor neuron is intact"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"A reflex arc is a chain of five components: receptor, sensory neuron, integration center, motor neuron, and effector. In the patellar reflex, the sensory neuron carries the signal from the stretched thigh muscle into the spinal cord. If that neuron is cut, the signal never reaches the cord, so the motor neuron is never activated and the leg does not kick, which is why a missing reflex points to a break somewhere along the arc.","notes":["A cut sensory neuron removes the input entirely; it does not remove a brake. Students choose this when they confuse a lost input with a lost inhibition.","A reflex runs through the spinal cord without waiting for the brain, and the brain also depends on sensory input to know the tendon was tapped. Without the sensory neuron, no signal enters the arc.","Correct. Every link in the arc is needed, and the sensory link is broken.","The motor neuron can only fire once the signal reaches it through the cord. With the sensory neuron cut, the motor neuron never receives the input it needs."],"lt":"t-nervous-tissue","lid":"d3-t-nervous-tissue-6","comp":181,"competencyId":"w8-reflex-arc"},{"id":"L02764","dok":1,"q":"Which component comes first in a reflex arc?","a":"The receptor.","options":["The sensory neuron","The receptor","The integration center","The effector"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Every reflex arc has the same five components in order: receptor, sensory neuron, integration center, motor neuron, and effector. The receptor comes first because nothing can happen until a stimulus is detected. It then triggers a signal in the sensory neuron, which carries it into the CNS.","notes":["The sensory neuron is second; it carries the signal from the receptor to the CNS. Pick it for a question about which component brings the signal into the cord.","Correct. The receptor detects the stimulus and starts the reflex.","The integration center is third, the synapses in the CNS where the signal is processed. Choose it for a question about the middle of the arc.","The effector is the last component, the muscle or gland that carries out the response. It would be right for a question about the final component."],"lt":"t-neuronal-integration","lid":"c7","comp":181,"competencyId":"w8-reflex-arc"},{"id":"L02765","dok":1,"q":"Which component of a reflex arc is the muscle or gland that carries out the response?","a":"The effector.","options":["The motor neuron","The receptor","The integration center","The effector"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The effector is the fifth and final component of a reflex arc. It is a muscle or gland that carries out the response once the motor neuron delivers the command. In the patellar reflex, for example, the effector is the thigh muscle that contracts to kick the leg.","notes":["The motor neuron carries the command to the effector, but it is a neuron, not the muscle or gland itself. It would be right for a question about the component just before the effector.","The receptor detects the stimulus at the start of the arc rather than carrying out the response. Pick it for a question about the first component.","The integration center is the synapses in the CNS where the signal is processed. Choose it for a question about where the decision is made.","Correct. The effector is the muscle or gland that responds."],"lt":"t-neuronal-integration","lid":"c8","comp":181,"competencyId":"w8-reflex-arc"},{"id":"L02766","dok":1,"q":"Which type of reflex has just one synapse in the integration center?","a":"A monosynaptic reflex.","options":["A polysynaptic reflex","A monosynaptic reflex","A learned reflex","An autonomic reflex"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Reflexes can be classified by how many synapses lie in the integration center. A monosynaptic reflex has just one: the sensory neuron synapses directly on the motor neuron, with no interneuron in between. The stretch reflex is the standard example, and having only one synapse makes it very fast.","notes":["A polysynaptic reflex has two or more synapses, with interneurons between the sensory and motor neurons. Pick it for a question about the withdrawal reflex.","Correct. Mono means one, so a monosynaptic reflex has a single synapse.","Learned reflexes are named for being built through practice, not for their synapse count. It would be right for a question about catching a suddenly dropped object.","Autonomic reflexes are named for their effector (smooth muscle, cardiac muscle, or a gland), not for their number of synapses. Choose autonomic for a question about the pupillary light reflex."],"lt":"t-neuronal-integration","lid":"c9","comp":182,"competencyId":"w8-reflex-types"},{"id":"L02768","dok":1,"q":"What is decussation?","a":"The crossing of a pathway to the opposite side of the CNS.","options":["Wrapping of an axon in myelin by glial cells","Branching of one neuron's signal onto many neurons","Detection of a stimulus by a sensory receptor","Crossing of a pathway to the other side of the CNS"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Decussation is the crossing of a pathway from one side of the CNS to the other. Because most sensory and motor pathways decussate, each half of the brain senses and controls the opposite half of the body. This is why a stroke on the left side of the brain causes weakness on the right side of the body.","notes":["Wrapping an axon in myelin is myelination, done by Schwann cells and oligodendrocytes. Choose this for a question about how axons become insulated.","One neuron spreading its signal to many is divergence, a circuit pattern. Pick this description for a question about divergence.","Detecting a stimulus is the job of a receptor, not a crossing of pathways. It would be the answer for the first component of a reflex arc.","Correct. Decussation is the crossing over of a pathway."],"lt":"t-neuronal-integration","lid":"c11","comp":183,"competencyId":"w8-neuronal-pools"},{"id":"L02769","dok":1,"q":"Ascending pathways of the spinal cord carry what kind of information, and in which direction?","a":"Sensory information upward to the brain.","options":["Motor commands downward to the body","Motor commands upward to the brain","Sensory information downward to the body","Sensory information upward to the brain"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The white matter of the spinal cord holds long tracts that carry information between the brain and the body. Ascending pathways carry sensory information up the cord to the brain. Descending pathways carry motor commands down from the brain to the body.","notes":["Motor commands travel down in the descending pathways. Pick this for a question about what descending tracts carry.","Motor commands start in the brain and travel down, so they do not ascend. This choice gets both the type and the direction wrong.","Sensory information flows toward the brain, not away from it. Students choose this when they get the type of information right but the direction backward.","Correct. Ascending tracts carry sensory input up to the brain."],"lt":"t-neuronal-integration","lid":"c12","comp":183,"competencyId":"w8-neuronal-pools"},{"id":"L02770","dok":2,"q":"The outer region of the spinal cord, where the ascending and descending tracts run, is made of which tissue?","a":"White matter.","options":["Dura mater","Gray matter","White matter","Pia mater"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"In the spinal cord, white matter forms the outer region and surrounds a core of gray matter. The white matter is made of myelinated axons grouped into tracts, the ascending tracts carrying sensory information up and the descending tracts carrying motor commands down. This arrangement is the reverse of the brain, where gray matter is on the outside.","notes":["The dura mater is the tough outer meninx surrounding the cord within the vertebral canal, not the tissue of the cord itself. It would be right for a question about the outermost covering of the cord.","Gray matter forms the inner butterfly-shaped core of the cord, where the cell bodies are. Pick gray matter for a question about the core of the cord or the outer cortex of the brain.","Correct. The outer region of the cord is white matter made of myelinated tracts.","The pia mater is the thin innermost meninx that clings to the surface of the cord, covering it from outside. Choose it for a question about the membrane directly on the cord surface."],"lt":"t-neuronal-integration","lid":"c13","comp":180,"competencyId":"w8-gray-white-matter"},{"id":"L02773","dok":2,"q":"In a reflex arc, which component lies directly between the sensory neuron and the motor neuron?","a":"The integration center.","options":["The receptor","The effector","The integration center","The posterior root ganglion"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The five components of a reflex arc are, in order, receptor, sensory neuron, integration center, motor neuron, and effector. The integration center is the one or more synapses in the CNS where the incoming signal is processed and passed to the motor neuron. In a spinal reflex it lies in the gray matter of the spinal cord.","notes":["The receptor comes before the sensory neuron, at the very start of the arc. Pick it for a question about what detects the stimulus.","The effector comes after the motor neuron, at the very end of the arc. Choose it for a question about the muscle or gland that responds.","Correct. The integration center sits between the sensory and motor neurons.","The posterior root ganglion holds the cell body of the sensory neuron, so it is part of the sensory neuron rather than a separate step between the two neurons. It would be right for a question about where sensory cell bodies sit."],"lt":"t-neuronal-integration","lid":"c16","comp":181,"competencyId":"w8-reflex-arc"},{"id":"L02774","dok":2,"q":"The patellar reflex acts on skeletal muscle. By the effector-based classification, which type of reflex is it?","a":"A somatic reflex.","options":["An autonomic reflex","A somatic reflex","A learned reflex","A polysynaptic reflex"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Reflexes can be named for their effector. A somatic reflex acts on skeletal muscle, and an autonomic reflex acts on smooth muscle, cardiac muscle, or a gland. In the patellar (knee-jerk) reflex, the effector is the skeletal muscle on the front of the thigh, so it is a somatic reflex.","notes":["An autonomic reflex acts on smooth muscle, cardiac muscle, or a gland. Pick it for a question about the pupillary light reflex.","Correct. A reflex whose effector is skeletal muscle is somatic.","Learned describes a reflex built by practice, which is a classification by origin, not by effector. It would be right for a question about catching a suddenly dropped object.","Polysynaptic describes a reflex with two or more synapses, which is a classification by synapse count, not by effector. Choose it for a question about the withdrawal reflex."],"lt":"t-neuronal-integration","lid":"c17","comp":182,"competencyId":"w8-reflex-types"},{"id":"L02775","dok":2,"q":"The pupillary light reflex changes the size of the pupil using smooth muscle in the iris. By the effector-based classification, which type of reflex is it?","a":"An autonomic reflex.","options":["A learned reflex","A somatic reflex","A monosynaptic reflex","An autonomic reflex"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"When reflexes are classified by effector, a somatic reflex acts on skeletal muscle and an autonomic reflex acts on smooth muscle, cardiac muscle, or a gland. The muscle of the iris that changes the size of the pupil is smooth muscle. That makes the pupillary light reflex an autonomic reflex.","notes":["Learned describes a reflex built through practice, a classification by origin. It would be right for a question about catching a suddenly dropped object.","A somatic reflex acts on skeletal muscle, such as the patellar reflex. Pick somatic when the effector is a skeletal muscle.","Monosynaptic describes a reflex with a single synapse, a classification by synapse count rather than by effector. Choose it for a question about the stretch reflex.","Correct. A reflex whose effector is smooth muscle is autonomic."],"lt":"t-neuronal-integration","lid":"c18","comp":182,"competencyId":"w8-reflex-types"},{"id":"L02776","dok":2,"q":"A reflex pathway includes interneurons and has three synapses in the integration center. By synapse count, which type of reflex is it?","a":"A polysynaptic reflex.","options":["An innate reflex","A polysynaptic reflex","A somatic reflex","A monosynaptic reflex"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"When reflexes are classified by synapse count, a monosynaptic reflex has one synapse and a polysynaptic reflex has two or more. Extra synapses mean there are interneurons between the sensory and motor neurons. The withdrawal reflex, which pulls a hand from a hot surface, is the standard polysynaptic example.","notes":["Innate describes a reflex present from birth, a classification by origin. It would be right for a question about the blink reflex.","Correct. Poly means many, and two or more synapses makes a reflex polysynaptic.","Somatic describes a reflex whose effector is skeletal muscle, which is a classification by effector, not by synapse count. Choose it for a question about the patellar reflex.","A monosynaptic reflex has only one synapse and no interneuron. Pick it for a question about the stretch reflex."],"lt":"t-neuronal-integration","lid":"c19","comp":182,"competencyId":"w8-reflex-types"},{"id":"L02781","dok":2,"q":"Descending pathways of the spinal cord carry what kind of information, and in which direction?","a":"Motor commands down from the brain to the body.","options":["Motor commands down from the brain","Motor commands up to the brain","Sensory input down from the brain","Sensory input carried up to the brain"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The white matter of the spinal cord holds two sets of long tracts. Descending pathways carry motor commands from the brain down to the body, and ascending pathways carry sensory information up to the brain. A spinal cord injury interrupts both, which is why both sensation and movement are lost below the level of the injury.","notes":["Correct. Descending tracts carry motor commands down from the brain.","Motor commands start in the brain and travel outward, so they do not travel up to it. This choice reverses the direction of motor signals.","Sensory input flows toward the brain, never down from it. This choice gets the direction right for a descending tract but the type of information wrong.","Sensory input travels up the cord in the ascending pathways. Pick this for a question about ascending tracts."],"lt":"t-neuronal-integration","lid":"c24","comp":183,"competencyId":"w8-neuronal-pools"},{"id":"L02784","dok":2,"q":"The white matter of the spinal cord is divided into anterior, posterior, and lateral columns. What do these columns contain?","a":"Tracts, bundles of axons.","options":["Nuclei, clusters of cell bodies in the CNS","Ganglia, clusters of cell bodies in the PNS","Tracts, bundles of axons within the CNS","Nerves, bundles of axons in the PNS"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The white matter of the spinal cord is sorted into anterior, posterior, and lateral columns, and each column holds tracts. A tract is a bundle of axons within the CNS that share a common origin and destination. Ascending tracts carry sensory information up to the brain, and descending tracts carry motor commands down.","notes":["Nuclei are clusters of cell bodies, found in the gray matter of the cord, such as the motor nuclei of the anterior horn. Pick nuclei for a question about the gray horns.","Ganglia lie outside the CNS, such as the posterior root ganglion beside the cord. Choose ganglia for a question about where sensory cell bodies sit.","Correct. Each white column holds tracts, which are bundles of axons in the CNS.","Nerves are bundles of axons, but they lie outside the CNS, such as the spinal nerves leaving the vertebral canal. It would be right for a question about bundles of axons outside the cord."],"lt":"t-neuronal-integration","lid":"c27","comp":186,"competencyId":"w8-cord-internal"},{"id":"L02786","dok":2,"q":"The blink reflex is present at birth and does not have to be practiced. Based on whether it is inborn, which class of reflex is it?","a":"An innate reflex.","options":["An innate reflex","A learned reflex","A polysynaptic reflex","An autonomic reflex"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Reflexes can be classified by whether they are inborn or practiced. An innate reflex is present from birth and is not learned, and the blink reflex is the standard example. A learned reflex, by contrast, is built through practice and repetition.","notes":["Correct. A reflex present from birth is innate.","A learned reflex is built through practice and repetition, such as catching a suddenly dropped object. Pick learned when the reflex had to be acquired.","Polysynaptic describes a reflex with two or more synapses, a classification by synapse count. Choose it for a question about the withdrawal reflex.","Autonomic describes a reflex acting on smooth muscle, cardiac muscle, or a gland, a classification by effector. It would be right for a question about the pupillary light reflex."],"lt":"t-neuronal-integration","lid":"c29","comp":182,"competencyId":"w8-reflex-types"},{"id":"L02787","dok":2,"q":"Catching a suddenly dropped object is a reflex built through practice and repetition. Based on whether it is inborn or practiced, which class is it?","a":"A learned reflex.","options":["A monosynaptic reflex","An innate reflex","A learned reflex","An autonomic reflex"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"When reflexes are sorted by origin, an innate reflex is present from birth and a learned reflex is built through practice and repetition. Catching a dropped object becomes fast and automatic only after many repetitions, so it is a learned reflex. The same reflex can also be described by other systems, such as by its effector, but the question asks about origin.","notes":["Monosynaptic describes a reflex with only one synapse, a classification by synapse count. Choose it for a question about the stretch reflex.","An innate reflex is present from birth and not learned, such as the blink reflex. Pick innate when the reflex did not have to be practiced.","Correct. A reflex built through practice is learned.","An autonomic reflex acts on smooth muscle, cardiac muscle, or a gland, while catching an object uses skeletal muscle. It would be right for a question about the pupillary light reflex."],"lt":"t-neuronal-integration","lid":"c30","comp":182,"competencyId":"w8-reflex-types"},{"id":"L02788","dok":1,"q":"Which reflex is the standard example of a monosynaptic reflex?","a":"The stretch reflex.","options":["The stretch reflex","The withdrawal reflex","The blink reflex","The pupillary light reflex"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"A monosynaptic reflex has exactly one synapse in the integration center, where the sensory neuron synapses directly on the motor neuron with no interneuron. The stretch reflex, in which a stretched muscle contracts in response, is the standard example. The patellar reflex is one familiar stretch reflex.","notes":["Correct. The stretch reflex has a single synapse between the sensory and motor neurons.","The withdrawal reflex uses interneurons and has several synapses, so it is the standard polysynaptic example. Pick it for a question about a polysynaptic reflex.","The blink reflex is the standard example of an innate reflex, one present from birth. It would be right for a question about innate reflexes.","The pupillary light reflex is the standard example of an autonomic reflex, since its effector is smooth muscle in the iris. Choose it for a question about autonomic reflexes."],"lt":"t-neuronal-integration","lid":"c31","comp":182,"competencyId":"w8-reflex-types"},{"id":"L02793","dok":2,"q":"In the patellar reflex, where is the integration center located?","a":"In the spinal cord.","options":["In the posterior root ganglion","In the thigh muscle","In the cerebral cortex","In the spinal cord"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The integration center of a reflex arc is the synapse or synapses in the CNS where the incoming signal is processed. The patellar reflex is a spinal reflex, so its integration center is in the gray matter of the spinal cord, where the sensory neuron synapses with the motor neuron. Because the signal does not have to travel to the brain, the response is very fast.","notes":["The posterior root ganglion holds the cell bodies of the sensory neurons, but no processing synapse occurs there. Choose it for a question about where sensory cell bodies sit.","The thigh muscle is both where the stretch is detected and the effector that contracts, not the integration center. It would be right for a question about the effector.","The cerebral cortex handles conscious sensation and voluntary movement, but a spinal reflex does not need it. Pick the cortex for a question about where a voluntary decision is made.","Correct. The patellar reflex is a spinal reflex, processed in the gray matter of the cord."],"lt":"t-neuronal-integration","lid":"c36","comp":181,"competencyId":"w8-reflex-arc"},{"id":"L02794","dok":2,"q":"Tapping the patellar tendon stretches the thigh muscle. Which reflex arc component detects this stretch?","a":"The receptor.","options":["The integration center","The motor neuron","The receptor","The effector"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The receptor is the first component of a reflex arc; it detects the stimulus and triggers a signal in the sensory neuron. In the patellar reflex, the stimulus is the stretch of the thigh muscle caused by tapping its tendon, and receptors in that muscle detect it. The same muscle then serves as the effector when it contracts.","notes":["The integration center is the synapse in the spinal cord where the signal is processed, not where the stretch is detected. Choose it for a question about where the signal is processed.","The motor neuron carries the command from the cord back to the muscle. It would be right for a question about the component just before the effector.","Correct. The receptor detects the stretch and starts the signal.","The effector is the muscle that contracts at the end of the arc. The thigh muscle is the effector in this reflex, but the structure that senses the stretch is the receptor within it."],"lt":"t-neuronal-integration","lid":"c37","comp":181,"competencyId":"w8-reflex-arc"},{"id":"L02796","dok":2,"q":"A spinal pathway crosses from one side of the CNS to the other, so the right side of the brain serves the left side of the body. Which term names this crossing?","a":"Decussation.","options":["Decussation","Integration","Divergence","Convergence"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Decussation is the crossing of a pathway from one side of the CNS to the other. Because most sensory and motor pathways decussate, each half of the brain senses and controls the opposite half of the body. This crossing explains why damage to one side of the brain affects the opposite side of the body.","notes":["Correct. Decussation is the crossing of a pathway to the opposite side.","Integration is the combining of incoming signals so a neuron produces a single response. It would be right for a question about what happens when many signals reach one neuron.","Divergence is a circuit pattern in which one neuron spreads its signal to many. Choose it for a question about one neuron reaching many targets.","Convergence is a circuit pattern in which many neurons feed onto one. Pick it for a question about many inputs meeting on a single neuron."],"lt":"t-neuronal-integration","lid":"c39","comp":183,"competencyId":"w8-neuronal-pools"},{"id":"L02797","dok":3,"q":"Which sequence places the five components of a reflex arc in order from stimulus to response?","a":"Receptor, sensory neuron, integration center, motor neuron, effector.","options":["Sensory neuron, receptor, integration center, effector, motor neuron","Receptor, motor neuron, integration center, sensory neuron, effector","Receptor, sensory neuron, motor neuron, integration center, effector","Receptor, sensory neuron, integration center, motor neuron, effector"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Every reflex arc runs the same five components in order. The receptor detects the stimulus, the sensory neuron carries the signal to the CNS, the integration center processes it, the motor neuron carries the command out, and the effector, a muscle or gland, carries out the response. The order follows the three functions of the nervous system: sensory input, integration, and motor output.","notes":["This puts the sensory neuron before the receptor and the effector before the motor neuron. Nothing can travel until the receptor detects the stimulus, and the effector can only act after the motor neuron delivers the command.","This swaps the sensory and motor neurons. The sensory neuron must carry the signal into the CNS before the motor neuron can carry a command out.","This skips straight from the sensory neuron to the motor neuron, then processes the signal afterward. Processing in the CNS has to happen before a command goes out.","Correct. The signal is detected, carried in, processed, carried out, and acted on."],"lt":"t-neuronal-integration","lid":"c40","comp":181,"competencyId":"w8-reflex-arc"},{"id":"L02798","dok":3,"q":"In a withdrawal reflex, which sequence traces the signal from the structure that detects the stimulus to the synapses where it is processed?","a":"Receptor, sensory neuron, integration center.","options":["Receptor, sensory neuron, integration center","Effector, motor neuron, integration center","Receptor, integration center, sensory neuron","Sensory neuron, receptor, integration center"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The first half of a reflex arc brings information in. The receptor in the skin detects the painful stimulus, the sensory neuron carries the signal into the spinal cord, and the integration center, here a set of synapses with interneurons in the cord, processes it. The second half then sends the command out through the motor neuron to the effector.","notes":["Correct. These are the first three components of the arc, in order.","These are the last three components run backward. This sequence would fit a question that asked for the output half of the arc in reverse.","This processes the signal before it has been carried into the CNS. The sensory neuron is the link that brings the signal to the integration center.","This puts the sensory neuron before the receptor, but the sensory neuron has nothing to carry until the receptor detects the stimulus."],"lt":"t-neuronal-integration","lid":"c41","comp":181,"competencyId":"w8-reflex-arc"},{"id":"L02800","dok":3,"q":"In a withdrawal reflex, the signal has just been processed in the spinal cord. Which sequence completes the arc from that point?","a":"Integration center, motor neuron, effector.","options":["Integration center, sensory neuron, effector","Integration center, motor neuron, receptor","Integration center, motor neuron, effector","Integration center, effector, motor neuron"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The second half of a reflex arc sends the response out. From the integration center in the spinal cord, a motor neuron carries the command out through the anterior root to the effector. In a withdrawal reflex, the effector is the set of muscles that pull the limb away from the painful stimulus.","notes":["The sensory neuron brings the signal into the cord earlier in the arc. Commands leave the cord on a motor neuron.","The receptor is the first component of the arc, where the stimulus was detected, not the last. The command goes to the effector, the muscle that pulls the hand away.","Correct. The command leaves the cord on a motor neuron and reaches the muscle.","This has the muscle respond before the motor neuron has delivered the command. The motor neuron must come before the effector."],"lt":"t-neuronal-integration","lid":"c43","comp":181,"competencyId":"w8-reflex-arc"},{"id":"L02801","dok":3,"q":"Classify three reflexes: the patellar reflex by its effector, the stretch reflex by its number of synapses, and the blink reflex by whether it is inborn. Which set of labels is correct?","a":"Patellar is somatic, stretch is monosynaptic, blink is innate.","options":["Patellar is somatic, stretch is polysynaptic, blink is innate","Patellar is somatic, stretch is monosynaptic, blink is learned","Patellar is somatic, stretch is monosynaptic, blink is innate","Patellar is autonomic, stretch is monosynaptic, blink is innate"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Reflexes are classified in three separate ways. By effector, the patellar reflex is somatic because it acts on skeletal muscle. By synapse count, the stretch reflex is monosynaptic, and by origin, the blink reflex is innate because it is present from birth.","notes":["The stretch reflex has a single synapse, so it is monosynaptic. Polysynaptic would fit the withdrawal reflex, which uses interneurons.","The first two labels are right, but the blink reflex is present at birth, so it is innate. Learned would fit catching a suddenly dropped object.","Correct. Each reflex is labeled by the right basis.","The patellar reflex acts on skeletal muscle in the thigh, so it is somatic. Autonomic would fit the pupillary light reflex, which acts on smooth muscle."],"lt":"t-neuronal-integration","lid":"c44","comp":182,"competencyId":"w8-reflex-types"},{"id":"L02802","dok":2,"q":"Which order runs from the smallest structure to the largest: a single axon, a tract, and a white column of the spinal cord?","a":"Axon, tract, white column.","options":["White column, tract, axon","Axon, white column, tract","Tract, axon, white column","Axon, tract, white column"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The white matter of the spinal cord is organized in levels. A single axon is the smallest unit, many axons with a common origin and destination form a tract, and several tracts together make up a white column. The cord has anterior, posterior, and lateral white columns.","notes":["This is the correct order run from largest to smallest. It would fit a question that asked for the order from the largest structure down.","This puts the column inside the tract, but it is the other way around: each white column holds several tracts.","This places the tract below a single axon, but a tract is a bundle of many axons, so it must be larger.","Correct. Axons group into tracts, and tracts group into columns."],"lt":"t-neuronal-integration","lid":"c45","comp":186,"competencyId":"w8-cord-internal"},{"id":"L02805","dok":2,"q":"The anterior horn of the spinal cord holds clusters of motor neuron cell bodies. Because these clusters lie inside the CNS, what are they called?","a":"Nuclei.","options":["Ganglia","Nuclei","Tracts","Nerves"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The gray matter of the spinal cord is shaped into horns, and the anterior horn holds the somatic motor nuclei that drive skeletal muscle. A cluster of neuron cell bodies inside the CNS is called a nucleus, so these clusters are nuclei. The axons of these motor neurons leave the cord through the anterior root.","notes":["Ganglia are clusters of cell bodies outside the CNS, such as the posterior root ganglion. Pick ganglia when the cluster lies in the PNS.","Correct. Clusters of cell bodies inside the CNS are nuclei.","Tracts are bundles of axons in the CNS, found in the white columns rather than the gray horns. Choose tracts for a question about the white matter of the cord.","Nerves are bundles of axons outside the CNS. They would be right for a question about the spinal nerves leaving the vertebral canal."],"lt":"t-neuronal-integration","lid":"c4-x2","comp":179,"competencyId":"w8-tissue-collections"},{"id":"L02806","dok":1,"q":"In a reflex arc, which component carries the command from the CNS out to the effector?","a":"The motor neuron.","options":["The sensory neuron","The integration center","The motor neuron","The receptor"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The motor neuron is the fourth component of a reflex arc. It carries the command from the integration center in the CNS out to the effector, the muscle or gland that responds. Motor neurons are multipolar, and in a spinal reflex their cell bodies sit in the anterior horn of the cord.","notes":["The sensory neuron carries the signal in the other direction, from the receptor into the CNS. Pick it for a question about the component that brings the signal in.","The integration center is where the signal is processed inside the CNS, before anything is sent out. Choose it for a question about where the signal is processed.","Correct. The motor neuron carries the command out to the muscle or gland.","The receptor is at the start of the arc, where the stimulus is detected. It would be right for a question about the first component."],"lt":"t-neuronal-integration","lid":"c5-x2","comp":181,"competencyId":"w8-reflex-arc"},{"id":"L02807","dok":2,"q":"In a withdrawal reflex, arm muscles contract to pull the hand off a hot stove. Which reflex arc component are these muscles?","a":"The effector.","options":["The receptor","The integration center","The motor neuron","The effector"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The effector is the final component of a reflex arc, the muscle or gland that carries out the response. In a withdrawal reflex, the effectors are the skeletal muscles that pull the limb away from the painful stimulus. They act once the motor neurons deliver the command from the spinal cord.","notes":["The receptor is in the skin of the hand, where the heat is detected, not in the muscles that move the arm. Pick it for a question about what senses the heat.","The integration center is the set of synapses in the spinal cord where the signal is processed. Choose it for a question about where the decision to withdraw is made.","The motor neuron carries the command to the muscles, but it is a neuron, not the muscle itself. It would be right for a question about the component just before the effector.","Correct. The muscles that carry out the response are the effector."],"lt":"t-neuronal-integration","lid":"c6-x2","comp":181,"competencyId":"w8-reflex-arc"},{"id":"L02808","dok":1,"q":"In a reflex arc, which component carries the signal from the receptor to the CNS?","a":"The sensory neuron.","options":["The motor neuron","The integration center","The sensory neuron","The effector"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The sensory neuron is the second component of a reflex arc. It carries the signal from the receptor into the CNS, entering the spinal cord through the posterior root, with its cell body in the posterior root ganglion. There it reaches the integration center.","notes":["The motor neuron carries the command the other way, out of the CNS to the effector. Pick it for a question about the component that carries the response out.","The integration center is where the signal is processed inside the CNS, after the sensory neuron delivers it. It would be right for a question about the middle of the arc.","Correct. The sensory neuron carries the signal into the CNS.","The effector is the muscle or gland that responds at the end of the arc. Choose it for a question about the final component."],"lt":"t-neuronal-integration","lid":"c7-x2","comp":181,"competencyId":"w8-reflex-arc"},{"id":"L02809","dok":1,"q":"A reflex whose effector is skeletal muscle is which type?","a":"A somatic reflex.","options":["A monosynaptic reflex","A somatic reflex","A learned reflex","An autonomic reflex"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"When reflexes are classified by their effector, those that act on skeletal muscle are somatic reflexes, matching the somatic nervous system that controls skeletal muscle. Those that act on smooth muscle, cardiac muscle, or glands are autonomic reflexes. The patellar reflex is a common somatic example.","notes":["Monosynaptic describes a reflex with one synapse, a classification by synapse count rather than effector. Choose it for a question about the stretch reflex.","Correct. Reflexes acting on skeletal muscle are somatic.","Learned describes a reflex built by practice, a classification by origin. It would be right for a question about catching a suddenly dropped object.","An autonomic reflex acts on smooth muscle, cardiac muscle, or a gland, not skeletal muscle. Pick it for a reflex such as the pupillary light reflex."],"lt":"t-neuronal-integration","lid":"c8-x2","comp":182,"competencyId":"w8-reflex-types"},{"id":"L02816","dok":1,"q":"Which tracts carry sensory information up the spinal cord to the brain?","a":"Ascending tracts.","options":["Anterior roots","Ascending tracts","Descending tracts","Commissural fibers"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The white matter of the spinal cord contains ascending and descending tracts. Ascending tracts carry sensory information up the cord to the brain. Descending tracts carry motor commands in the opposite direction.","notes":["The anterior roots carry motor axons out of the cord to muscles and glands; they are not tracts within the cord. They would be right for a question about where motor axons leave the cord.","Correct. Ascending tracts carry sensory input up to the brain.","Descending tracts carry motor commands down from the brain to the body. Pick them for a question about motor pathways.","Commissural fibers, such as the corpus callosum, connect the right and left cerebral hemispheres rather than running up the cord. Choose them for a question about links between the hemispheres."],"lt":"t-neuronal-integration","lid":"c15-x2","comp":183,"competencyId":"w8-neuronal-pools"},{"id":"L02817","dok":2,"q":"A clinician taps just below the kneecap and watches the leg kick. Which reflex is being tested?","a":"The patellar reflex.","options":["The pupillary light reflex","The withdrawal reflex","The blink reflex","The patellar reflex"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The patellar, or knee-jerk, reflex is triggered by tapping the patellar tendon below the kneecap, which stretches the muscle on the front of the thigh and makes it contract. This one test checks the whole reflex arc: the receptor, the sensory and motor neurons, and the spinal cord between them. A missing reflex points to a break somewhere along that pathway.","notes":["The pupillary light reflex changes the size of the pupil in response to light. It would be right for a question about shining a light in the eye.","The withdrawal reflex pulls a limb away from a painful stimulus, such as a hot surface. Pick it for a question about pulling the hand off a stove.","The blink reflex closes the eyelids when something approaches or touches the eye. Choose it for a question about protecting the eye.","Correct. Tapping the patellar tendon tests the patellar (knee-jerk) reflex."],"lt":"t-neuronal-integration","lid":"c16-x2","comp":181,"competencyId":"w8-reflex-arc"},{"id":"L02818","dok":2,"q":"Which structure is present in a polysynaptic reflex but absent from a monosynaptic reflex?","a":"An interneuron.","options":["A sensory neuron","A receptor","An interneuron","A motor neuron"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"A monosynaptic reflex has one synapse, where the sensory neuron connects directly to the motor neuron. A polysynaptic reflex has two or more synapses because one or more interneurons sit between the sensory and motor neurons. Interneurons are what add the extra synapses, as in the withdrawal reflex.","notes":["Every reflex arc has a sensory neuron, so it cannot be what separates the two types. Pick it for a question about the component that carries the signal into the CNS.","Every reflex arc begins with a receptor that detects the stimulus. It would be right for a question about the first component of the arc.","Correct. Interneurons add the extra synapses in a polysynaptic reflex.","Every reflex arc has a motor neuron to carry the command out. Choose it for a question about the component that reaches the effector."],"lt":"t-neuronal-integration","lid":"c17-x2","comp":182,"competencyId":"w8-reflex-types"},{"id":"L02821","dok":2,"q":"A stroke on the left side of the brain causes weakness on the right side of the body. Which feature of the pathways explains this?","a":"Decussation of pathways.","options":["Convergence of neuronal pools","Saltatory conduction","Decussation of pathways","Serial processing"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Most sensory and motor pathways decussate, meaning they cross to the opposite side of the CNS along their route. As a result, the right half of the brain controls and senses the left half of the body, and the left half of the brain serves the right. A stroke that damages the left side of the brain therefore weakens the right side of the body.","notes":["Convergence is many neurons feeding onto one, a wiring pattern that does not explain a left and right switch. Pick it for a question about many inputs meeting on one neuron.","Saltatory conduction is the jumping of the impulse between nodes of Ranvier on a myelinated axon. It would be right for a question about why myelinated axons conduct fast.","Correct. Most pathways cross to the opposite side, so each half of the brain controls the opposite half of the body.","Serial processing is a signal passing along a single chain of neurons. Choose it for a question about the step-by-step chain of a reflex arc."],"lt":"t-neuronal-integration","lid":"c20-x2","comp":183,"competencyId":"w8-neuronal-pools"},{"id":"L02822","dok":1,"q":"In any reflex arc, the integration center lies in which part of the nervous system?","a":"The central nervous system.","options":["The posterior root ganglion","The peripheral nervous system","The effector organ","The central nervous system"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The integration center is the third component of a reflex arc, made of one or more synapses where the incoming signal is processed. These synapses always lie in the CNS, in the spinal cord for spinal reflexes or in the brainstem for cranial reflexes. The other components connect the CNS to the receptor and effector out in the body.","notes":["The posterior root ganglion holds the cell bodies of sensory neurons, but no processing synapses. Choose it for a question about where sensory cell bodies sit.","The PNS holds the receptor, the sensory and motor axons, and the effector side of the arc, but not the integration center. Pick the PNS for a question about where the nerves of the arc run.","The effector is the muscle or gland that responds at the end of the arc. It would be right for a question about which component carries out the response.","Correct. The integration center is always made of synapses in the brain or spinal cord."],"lt":"t-neuronal-integration","lid":"c21-x2","comp":181,"competencyId":"w8-reflex-arc"},{"id":"L02824","dok":1,"q":"Which class of tracts carries motor commands from the brain down to the body?","a":"Descending tracts.","options":["Descending tracts","Commissural fibers","Posterior roots","Ascending tracts"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Descending tracts run in the white matter of the spinal cord and carry motor commands from the brain down toward the body. They reach motor neurons in the cord, which then carry the commands out to the muscles. Ascending tracts carry sensory information the other way, up to the brain.","notes":["Correct. Descending tracts carry motor commands down the cord.","Commissural fibers, such as the corpus callosum, connect the two cerebral hemispheres. They would be right for a question about how the hemispheres communicate.","Posterior roots carry sensory axons into the cord from the body; they are roots, not tracts. Choose them for a question about where sensory axons enter the cord.","Ascending tracts carry sensory information up to the brain, the opposite direction. Pick them for a question about sensory pathways."],"lt":"t-neuronal-integration","lid":"c23-x2","comp":183,"competencyId":"w8-neuronal-pools"},{"id":"L02827","dok":3,"q":"A patient has no patellar reflex on one side. Along which structure could the break lie?","a":"Anywhere along the reflex arc.","options":["The cerebral cortex","The corpus callosum","The reflex arc","The cerebellum"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The patellar reflex depends on every link of its reflex arc: the receptor in the thigh muscle, the sensory neuron, the integration center in the spinal cord, the motor neuron, and the thigh muscle as the effector. Tapping the tendon tests all of these at once. If the reflex is missing, the break could be at any point along that arc.","notes":["The cerebral cortex handles conscious sensation and voluntary movement, but the patellar reflex runs through the spinal cord without it. Pick the cortex for a question about a voluntary movement.","The corpus callosum is the band of white matter connecting the two cerebral hemispheres, and it plays no part in a spinal reflex. Choose it for a question about communication between the hemispheres.","Correct. A missing reflex points to a break somewhere along the arc.","The cerebellum coordinates movement, but the patellar reflex can occur without it. It would be right for a question about smooth, coordinated movement."],"lt":"t-neuronal-integration","lid":"c26-x2","comp":181,"competencyId":"w8-reflex-arc"},{"id":"L02830","dok":3,"q":"A withdrawal reflex pulls the hand off a hot surface using skeletal muscle, with interneurons in the spinal cord. Which description fits this reflex?","a":"Polysynaptic and somatic.","options":["Monosynaptic and autonomic","Polysynaptic and autonomic","Monosynaptic and somatic","Polysynaptic and somatic"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Reflexes can be described by synapse count and by effector at the same time. The withdrawal reflex has interneurons between the sensory and motor neurons, so it has two or more synapses and is polysynaptic. Its effector is skeletal muscle in the arm, so it is also a somatic reflex.","notes":["Both labels are wrong here: interneurons make the reflex polysynaptic, and skeletal muscle makes it somatic. This choice reverses both classifications.","Polysynaptic is right, but the effector is skeletal muscle, so the reflex is somatic. Autonomic would fit a reflex acting on smooth muscle, such as the pupillary light reflex.","Somatic is right, but interneurons add synapses, so the reflex is polysynaptic. Monosynaptic and somatic would describe the stretch reflex.","Correct. Interneurons make it polysynaptic, and skeletal muscle makes it somatic."],"lt":"t-neuronal-integration","lid":"c29-x2","comp":182,"competencyId":"w8-reflex-types"},{"id":"L02833","dok":3,"q":"A spinal cord injury in the mid-back cuts through the tracts of the white matter. Why can the person no longer move the legs voluntarily?","a":"The descending tracts can no longer carry motor commands below the injury.","options":["The brain can no longer produce any motor commands at all","Descending tracts cannot carry commands past the injury","Sensory receptors in the legs stop detecting all stimuli","Ascending tracts can no longer carry commands down to the legs"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The white matter of the spinal cord holds ascending tracts, which carry sensory information up, and descending tracts, which carry motor commands down. A cord injury interrupts both, so function is lost below the level of the injury while everything above it is spared. Voluntary leg movement is lost because the descending tracts can no longer deliver the brain's commands to the motor neurons below the injury.","notes":["The brain above the injury is undamaged and can still issue commands; they simply cannot get past the injured cord. Function above the injury is spared.","Correct. Motor commands from the brain cannot pass the damaged tracts.","The receptors in the legs still work, but their signals cannot travel up past the injury. Even working receptors would not restore voluntary movement, which depends on descending commands.","Ascending tracts carry sensory information up to the brain, not commands down. Their loss explains the loss of feeling below the injury, not the loss of movement."],"lt":"t-neuronal-integration","lid":"d3-t-neuronal-integration-2","comp":183,"competencyId":"w8-neuronal-pools"},{"id":"L02835","dok":3,"q":"A goalkeeper who has trained for years blocks a sudden shot before having time to think about it. Based on whether it is inborn or practiced, which class of reflex fits this response?","a":"A learned reflex.","options":["An autonomic reflex","A learned reflex","A monosynaptic reflex","An innate reflex"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"When reflexes are classified by origin, innate reflexes are present from birth and learned reflexes are built through practice and repetition. The goalkeeper's save is fast and automatic only because of years of training, so it is a learned reflex. Catching a suddenly dropped object is another common example.","notes":["An autonomic reflex acts on smooth muscle, cardiac muscle, or a gland, while a save uses skeletal muscle. Choose autonomic for a question about the pupillary light reflex.","Correct. A fast, automatic response built through years of practice is learned.","A monosynaptic reflex has only one synapse, like the stretch reflex, and it is classified by synapse count rather than by origin. It would be right for a question about the stretch reflex.","An innate reflex is present from birth without practice, such as the blink reflex. Pick innate when no training was needed."],"lt":"t-neuronal-integration","lid":"d3-t-neuronal-integration-4","comp":182,"competencyId":"w8-reflex-types"},{"id":"L02851","dok":1,"q":"In a cross-section of the spinal cord, which region is butterfly-shaped and holds the neuron cell bodies?","a":"The gray matter.","options":["The white matter","The gray matter","The anterior white commissure","The posterior median sulcus"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The inner, butterfly-shaped core of the spinal cord is gray matter, made of neuron cell bodies, dendrites, and unmyelinated fibers. Its projections are called horns. This arrangement is the reverse of the cerebrum, where the gray matter lies on the outside as the cortex.","notes":["White matter is the outer region of the cord, made of myelinated axons sorted into columns. Pick it if the question asked which region carries the ascending and descending tracts.","Correct. The butterfly-shaped core is gray matter.","The anterior white commissure is a thin bridge of white matter that crosses between the two sides of the cord. It would be the answer if the question asked which white matter structure connects the right and left halves.","The posterior median sulcus is the shallow groove down the back of the cord. Pick it if the question asked which groove, together with the anterior median fissure, divides the cord into right and left halves."],"lt":"t-cns-brain-spinal-cord","lid":"c12","comp":180,"competencyId":"w8-gray-white-matter"},{"id":"L02852","dok":1,"q":"What is the conus medullaris?","a":"The tapered inferior end of the spinal cord.","options":["The tapered inferior end of the spinal cord","The fluid-filled channel at the center of the cord","The sensory root entering the back of the cord","The bundle of nerve roots below the cord"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The conus medullaris is the cone-shaped, tapered end of the spinal cord. In the adult it lies at about the level of the disc between L1 and L2, well above the bottom of the vertebral column. Below it, only nerve roots and the filum terminale continue down the vertebral canal.","notes":["Correct. The conus medullaris is the tapered end of the cord.","This describes the central canal, the small CSF channel continuous with the fourth ventricle. It would be the answer for a question about the fluid channel inside the cord.","This describes the posterior (dorsal) root, the sensory root of a spinal nerve. Pick it if the question asked which root carries sensory axons into the cord.","This describes the cauda equina, the hair-like bundle of nerve roots that descends past the conus medullaris. Pick it if the question asked what hangs in the vertebral canal below the end of the cord."],"lt":"t-cns-brain-spinal-cord","lid":"c13","comp":184,"competencyId":"w8-cord-external"},{"id":"L02861","dok":2,"q":"What does the posterior (dorsal) root of a spinal nerve carry?","a":"Sensory axons from the periphery into the cord.","options":["Motor axons from the cord out to muscles","Ascending tracts running up the cord to the brain","Both sensory and motor axons mixed together","Sensory axons from the periphery into the cord"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Each spinal nerve connects to the cord by two roots. The posterior (dorsal) root is the sensory root, carrying sensory axons from receptors in the body into the back of the cord, and it bears a swelling, the dorsal root ganglion, that holds the sensory neuron cell bodies. Posterior is sensory in, anterior is motor out.","notes":["Motor axons leaving the cord travel in the anterior (ventral) root. Pick this if the question asked about the ventral root.","Ascending tracts are bundles of axons in the white matter columns inside the cord, carrying sensory signals up to the brain. Pick them if the question asked what runs within the white matter of the cord.","Mixed sensory and motor axons are found in the spinal nerve itself, formed where the posterior and anterior roots join. This would be the answer for a question about the spinal nerve.","Correct. The posterior root carries sensory axons into the cord."],"lt":"t-cns-brain-spinal-cord","lid":"c22","comp":185,"competencyId":"w8-cord-roots"},{"id":"L02862","dok":2,"q":"In a cross-section of the spinal cord, the white matter is divided into what structural units?","a":"Columns.","options":["Columns","Ganglia","Horns","Gyri"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The white matter of the spinal cord surrounds the gray matter and is divided into anterior, posterior, and lateral white columns on each side. Each column holds tracts, which are bundles of axons that share a common origin and destination. Ascending tracts carry sensory signals up and descending tracts carry motor signals down.","notes":["Correct. Spinal white matter is divided into columns.","Ganglia are clusters of neuron cell bodies in the peripheral nervous system, such as the dorsal root ganglion. Pick a ganglion if the question asked where sensory cell bodies sit outside the cord.","Horns are the projections of the cord's gray matter (posterior, lateral, and anterior horns). Pick horns if the question asked how the gray matter is divided.","Gyri are the raised folds of the cerebral cortex, not features of the spinal cord. They would be the answer for a question about the surface of the cerebrum."],"lt":"t-cns-brain-spinal-cord","lid":"c23","comp":186,"competencyId":"w8-cord-internal"},{"id":"L02866","dok":2,"q":"Where is the cauda equina located relative to the conus medullaris?","a":"Below it, within the vertebral canal.","options":["Above it, in the cervical region of the cord","Lateral to it, outside the vertebral column","Inside it, as the central canal","Below it, within the vertebral canal"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The spinal cord ends at the conus medullaris near the L1 to L2 disc, but the nerve roots for the lumbar, sacral, and coccygeal spinal nerves must still travel down to their exit points. These roots form the cauda equina, a hair-like bundle that hangs in the vertebral canal below the conus. Its name means horse's tail.","notes":["The cervical region of the cord is far above the conus and holds the cervical enlargement, where upper limb nerves arise. Pick the cervical region if the question asked about the cervical enlargement.","Once spinal nerves leave the vertebral canal through the intervertebral foramina, they lie outside the vertebral column. The cauda equina is still inside the canal, so this describes spinal nerves after they exit.","The central canal is the CSF channel that runs through the center of the cord. It would be the answer if the question asked about the fluid-filled channel inside the cord, not the bundle of roots below it.","Correct. The cauda equina hangs below the conus medullaris inside the vertebral canal."],"lt":"t-cns-brain-spinal-cord","lid":"c27","comp":184,"competencyId":"w8-cord-external"},{"id":"L02868","dok":2,"q":"What does the anterior (ventral) root of a spinal nerve carry?","a":"Motor axons from the cord out to muscles.","options":["Both sensory and motor axons mixed together","Sensory axons from the periphery into the cord","Motor axons from the cord out to muscles","The cell bodies of sensory neurons"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The anterior (ventral) root is the motor root. It carries axons from motor neurons whose cell bodies sit in the anterior and lateral horns of the gray matter, out of the front of the cord toward skeletal muscles, smooth muscle, and glands. It joins the posterior root to form the mixed spinal nerve.","notes":["Sensory and motor axons travel together in the spinal nerve, formed where the two roots join. This would be the answer if the question asked about the spinal nerve.","Sensory axons entering the cord travel in the posterior (dorsal) root. Pick this if the question asked about the dorsal root.","Correct. The anterior root carries motor axons out of the cord.","Sensory neuron cell bodies sit in the dorsal root ganglion on the posterior root. Pick this if the question asked what the dorsal root ganglion holds; only the posterior root has a ganglion."],"lt":"t-cns-brain-spinal-cord","lid":"c29","comp":185,"competencyId":"w8-cord-roots"},{"id":"L02871","dok":3,"q":"On a cross-section of the spinal cord, a student points to the gray matter that holds the cell bodies of somatic motor neurons driving skeletal muscle. Which part of the gray matter is this?","a":"The anterior (ventral) horn.","options":["The posterior (dorsal) horn","The lateral horn","The anterior (ventral) horn","The gray commissure"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The gray matter of the cord is shaped into horns. The anterior (ventral) horn holds somatic motor nuclei, the cell bodies of motor neurons whose axons leave through the anterior root and make skeletal muscle contract. Damage to these cell bodies causes weakness or paralysis of the muscles served by that segment.","notes":["The posterior horn holds interneurons and receives the incoming axons of sensory neurons. Pick it if the question asked where sensory input first enters the gray matter.","The lateral horn holds autonomic motor nuclei, the neurons that serve smooth muscle, cardiac muscle, and glands, and it is present only in the T1 to L2 segments. Pick it for a question about autonomic motor neurons.","Correct. The anterior horn holds somatic motor neuron cell bodies.","The gray commissure is the bridge of gray matter that connects the two sides of the cord and surrounds the central canal. It would be the answer if the question asked what crosses the midline around the central canal."],"lt":"t-cns-brain-spinal-cord","lid":"c32","comp":186,"competencyId":"w8-cord-internal"},{"id":"L02876","dok":2,"q":"Which spinal cord structure is the small fluid-filled channel running through the center of the cord?","a":"The central canal.","options":["The central canal","The conus medullaris","The cauda equina","The dorsal root"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The central canal is a narrow channel in the middle of the spinal cord's gray matter, surrounded by the gray commissure. It holds cerebrospinal fluid and is continuous above with the fourth ventricle of the brain. Like the ventricles, it is lined by ependymal cells.","notes":["Correct. The central canal is the CSF channel at the center of the cord.","The conus medullaris is the tapered lower end of the spinal cord near the L1 to L2 disc. It would be the answer if the question asked where the cord ends.","The cauda equina is the bundle of nerve roots in the vertebral canal below the end of the cord. Pick it if the question asked what hangs below the conus medullaris.","The dorsal root is the sensory root that enters the back of the cord. Pick it if the question asked which root carries sensory axons in."],"lt":"t-cns-brain-spinal-cord","lid":"c37","comp":186,"competencyId":"w8-cord-internal"},{"id":"L02878","dok":2,"q":"Which region of a spinal cord cross-section is the outer layer of myelinated axons organized into columns?","a":"The white matter.","options":["The gray commissure","The gray matter","The white matter","The posterior horn"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"White matter is made of myelinated axons, and the fatty myelin makes it look pale. In the spinal cord it forms the outer region, divided into anterior, posterior, and lateral columns that contain the ascending and descending tracts. This is the reverse of the cerebrum, where white matter lies inside and gray matter forms the outer cortex.","notes":["The gray commissure is the bar of gray matter that crosses the midline around the central canal. Pick it if the question asked what connects the two halves of the gray butterfly.","The gray matter is the inner butterfly-shaped core of the cord, made of neuron cell bodies. Pick it if the question asked which region is divided into horns.","Correct. The outer layer of myelinated axons is the white matter.","The posterior horn is one projection of the gray matter, where sensory axons enter and interneurons sit. It would be the answer for a question about where sensory input first reaches the gray matter."],"lt":"t-cns-brain-spinal-cord","lid":"c39","comp":180,"competencyId":"w8-gray-white-matter"},{"id":"L02880","dok":3,"q":"After a fall, a patient has damage to the spinal cord that interrupts its ascending and descending tracts, and function is lost below the level of the damage. Which condition is this?","a":"Spinal cord injury.","options":["Spinal cord injury","Meningitis","Hydrocephalus","Stroke"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The ascending and descending tracts in the cord's white matter carry every signal between the brain and the body below. A spinal cord injury interrupts these tracts, so sensation and movement are lost below the level of the injury, while everything above it is spared. The higher the injury, the more of the body is affected.","notes":["Correct. Damage to the cord's tracts with loss of function below the injury is a spinal cord injury.","Meningitis is inflammation of the meninges, usually from infection, and is diagnosed by testing CSF. Pick it if the question described an infection of the membranes around the brain and cord.","Hydrocephalus is a buildup of cerebrospinal fluid that enlarges the ventricles and raises pressure inside the skull. It would be the answer for a question about blocked CSF flow.","A stroke is a loss of blood flow to part of the brain, and the deficit depends on which brain area is affected. Pick stroke if the question described sudden weakness on one side of the body after a blocked cerebral artery."],"lt":"t-cns-brain-spinal-cord","lid":"c41","comp":184,"competencyId":"w8-cord-external"},{"id":"L02882","dok":2,"q":"Which structure is the bundle of nerve roots that hangs in the vertebral canal below the point where the spinal cord ends?","a":"The cauda equina.","options":["The lateral horn","The filum terminale","The conus medullaris","The cauda equina"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The spinal cord stops growing in length in early childhood while the vertebral column keeps growing, so the adult cord ends near the L1 to L2 disc. The roots of the lower spinal nerves continue down the canal to reach their exit points, forming a hair-like bundle called the cauda equina. These roots float in the CSF of the lumbar cistern.","notes":["The lateral horn is part of the gray matter inside the cord, present only in the T1 to L2 segments, holding autonomic motor neurons. It would be the answer for a question about autonomic cell bodies in the cord.","The filum terminale is a single thread of pia mater, not a bundle of nerve roots, that runs from the conus to anchor the cord on the coccyx. Pick it if the question asked what anchors the lower end of the cord.","The conus medullaris is the tapered end of the cord itself, the point where the cauda equina begins. Pick it if the question asked where the cord ends.","Correct. The cauda equina is the bundle of roots below the end of the cord."],"lt":"t-cns-brain-spinal-cord","lid":"c43","comp":184,"competencyId":"w8-cord-external"},{"id":"L02883","dok":2,"q":"Which part of the spinal cord gray matter lies toward the back of the cord and receives the incoming sensory axons?","a":"The posterior (dorsal) horn.","options":["The posterior white column","The lateral horn","The posterior (dorsal) horn","The anterior (ventral) horn"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The posterior (dorsal) horn is the gray matter projection toward the back of the cord. Sensory axons arrive through the posterior root and enter this horn, where many of them connect with interneurons. The sensory cell bodies themselves are not in the horn; they sit outside the cord in the dorsal root ganglion.","notes":["The posterior white column is white matter made of tracts, many of them ascending sensory tracts, but it is not part of the gray matter. Pick it if the question asked where sensory tracts travel up toward the brain.","The lateral horn holds autonomic motor neurons and is found only in the T1 to L2 segments. It would be the answer for a question about autonomic output from the cord.","Correct. The posterior horn receives incoming sensory axons.","The anterior horn is toward the front of the cord and holds the cell bodies of somatic motor neurons. Pick it if the question asked where skeletal muscle motor neurons begin."],"lt":"t-cns-brain-spinal-cord","lid":"c44","comp":186,"competencyId":"w8-cord-internal"},{"id":"L02888","dok":2,"q":"A sensory signal from the skin reaches the spinal cord. Which root does it enter by, and which horn of gray matter does it reach first?","a":"The posterior (dorsal) root, then the posterior (dorsal) horn.","options":["The ventral root, then the ventral horn","The dorsal root, then the dorsal horn","The ventral root, then the dorsal horn","The dorsal root, then the ventral horn"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Sensory axons travel in the spinal nerve, split off into the posterior (dorsal) root, and pass the dorsal root ganglion where their cell bodies sit. They enter the back of the cord and reach the posterior (dorsal) horn of the gray matter. Posterior is sensory in and anterior is motor out, so a sensory signal stays on the posterior side at entry.","notes":["This is the motor side of the cord, used by signals leaving it. Pick the ventral horn and ventral root if the question traced a motor command from the cord out to a skeletal muscle.","Correct. Sensory signals enter by the dorsal root and reach the dorsal horn.","Sensory axons do not enter through the ventral root, which carries only motor axons out. This pairing mixes the motor root with the sensory horn.","The dorsal root is right, but the ventral horn holds motor neuron cell bodies. A sensory signal can reach the ventral horn later in a reflex, after passing through the dorsal horn first."],"lt":"t-cns-brain-spinal-cord","lid":"c49","comp":185,"competencyId":"w8-cord-roots"},{"id":"L02891","dok":2,"q":"Which list orders these spinal cord features from most superior to most inferior: the junction with the medulla, the cervical enlargement, the conus medullaris, and the cauda equina?","a":"Junction with the medulla, cervical enlargement, conus medullaris, cauda equina.","options":["Cervical enlargement, junction with the medulla, conus medullaris, cauda equina","Cauda equina, conus medullaris, cervical enlargement, junction with the medulla","Junction with the medulla, cervical enlargement, conus medullaris, cauda equina","Junction with the medulla, cervical enlargement, cauda equina, conus medullaris"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The spinal cord begins where it is continuous with the medulla oblongata at the foramen magnum. It widens at the cervical enlargement, where the nerves of the upper limbs arise, and later tapers to the conus medullaris near the L1 to L2 disc. The cauda equina, a bundle of nerve roots, hangs below the conus in the lower vertebral canal.","notes":["This places the cervical enlargement above the medulla junction. The cord starts at the medulla, and the cervical enlargement lies below that, in the neck.","This is the correct sequence read from bottom to top. Pick it only if the question asked for the order from inferior to superior.","Correct. Medulla junction, cervical enlargement, conus medullaris, then cauda equina.","This places the cauda equina above the conus medullaris. The cauda equina is the bundle of roots that continues below the end of the cord, so it must come last."],"lt":"t-cns-brain-spinal-cord","lid":"c52","comp":184,"competencyId":"w8-cord-external"},{"id":"N-m5-spinal-cord-1","dok":1,"q":"In an adult, at about what level does the spinal cord end at the conus medullaris?","a":"At the disc between L1 and L2.","options":["At the disc between L1 and L2","At the tip of the coccyx","At the disc between L4 and L5","At the foramen magnum"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The spinal cord stops lengthening at about 4 to 5 years of age while the vertebral column keeps growing, so in the adult the cord ends high in the lumbar region. Its tapered end, the conus medullaris, lies at about the disc between L1 and L2. Below that point only the cauda equina and filum terminale continue.","notes":["Correct. The conus medullaris lies near the L1 to L2 disc.","The tip of the coccyx is where the filum terminale anchors, not where the cord itself ends. Pick it if the question asked where the filum terminale attaches.","The L4 to L5 level is below the end of the cord, one of the safe sites for a lumbar puncture needle. It would be the answer if the question asked where a spinal tap is performed.","The foramen magnum is where the spinal cord begins, continuous with the medulla oblongata. Pick it if the question asked where the cord starts."],"comp":184,"competencyId":"w8-cord-external"},{"id":"N-m5-spinal-cord-2","dok":1,"q":"Which widening of the spinal cord is where the nerves of the upper limbs arise?","a":"The cervical enlargement.","options":["The conus medullaris","The cauda equina","The lumbar enlargement","The cervical enlargement"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The cord is wider in two regions because more neurons are needed to serve the limbs. The cervical enlargement, from about C4 to T1, is where the nerves of the upper limbs arise, and these nerves form the brachial plexus. The lumbar enlargement lower down serves the lower limbs.","notes":["The conus medullaris is the tapered end of the cord, a narrowing rather than a widening. It would be the answer if the question asked where the cord ends.","The cauda equina is the bundle of nerve roots below the end of the cord, not a widening of the cord itself. Pick it if the question asked what hangs in the lumbar cistern.","The lumbar enlargement is the lower widening of the cord, where the nerves of the lower limbs arise. Pick it if the question asked about the legs.","Correct. The cervical enlargement gives rise to the upper limb nerves."],"comp":184,"competencyId":"w8-cord-external"},{"id":"N-m5-spinal-cord-3","dok":2,"q":"Which swelling on the posterior root of a spinal nerve holds the cell bodies of sensory neurons?","a":"The dorsal root ganglion.","options":["The anterior horn","The lateral horn","The posterior white column","The dorsal root ganglion"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The dorsal (posterior) root ganglion is a swelling on the posterior root, just outside the cord, that holds the cell bodies of the sensory neurons for that segment. Only the posterior root has a ganglion. Because it lies outside the CNS, it is called a ganglion rather than a nucleus.","notes":["The anterior horn holds the cell bodies of somatic motor neurons inside the gray matter of the cord. Pick it if the question asked where skeletal muscle motor neurons begin.","The lateral horn holds autonomic motor neuron cell bodies and is found only in the T1 to L2 segments. It would be the answer for a question about autonomic output from the cord.","The posterior white column is white matter made of axon tracts, not cell bodies. Pick it if the question asked where ascending sensory tracts run in the cord.","Correct. The dorsal root ganglion holds sensory neuron cell bodies."],"comp":185,"competencyId":"w8-cord-roots"},{"id":"L02981","dok":1,"q":"Which part of a spinal nerve attaches it to the back of the spinal cord and carries sensory axons into the cord?","a":"The dorsal root.","options":["The ventral root","The dorsal root","The ventral ramus","The dorsal ramus"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The dorsal (posterior) root connects the back of the spinal cord to the spinal nerve and carries sensory axons into the cord. Along this root sits the dorsal root ganglion, which holds the cell bodies of those sensory neurons. Just past the cord, the dorsal root joins the ventral root to form one mixed spinal nerve.","notes":["The ventral root attaches to the front of the cord and carries motor axons out. Pick it if the question asked about the motor root.","Correct. The dorsal root brings sensory axons into the back of the cord.","The ventral ramus is a branch that forms after the spinal nerve leaves the vertebral column. It supplies the limbs and the front and sides of the trunk, so pick it for the branch that builds the plexuses.","The dorsal ramus is a branch past the intervertebral foramen that supplies the deep back muscles and the skin of the back. It is a branch of the finished spinal nerve, not one of its attachments to the cord."],"lt":"t-pns","lid":"c12","comp":185,"competencyId":"w8-cord-roots"},{"id":"L02990","dok":2,"q":"What is the cluster of sensory neuron cell bodies sitting on the dorsal root of a spinal nerve called?","a":"The dorsal root ganglion.","options":["The posterior gray horn","A sympathetic trunk ganglion","The dorsal root ganglion","The ventral root"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The dorsal root ganglion is a swelling on the dorsal (posterior) root, just outside the spinal cord, that holds the cell bodies of the sensory neurons for that spinal segment. Their axons carry signals from receptors in the body through the dorsal root and into the cord. Because the cluster lies outside the CNS, it is called a ganglion rather than a nucleus.","notes":["The posterior gray horn lies inside the cord and holds interneurons that receive the incoming sensory axons. Pick it for where sensory axons end inside the cord, not where their cell bodies sit.","A sympathetic trunk ganglion also lies beside the vertebral column, but it holds autonomic motor neurons, not sensory neurons. Pick it for the chain of ganglia linked to the spinal nerves by the rami communicantes.","Correct. The dorsal root ganglion holds the sensory cell bodies for its segment.","The ventral root carries motor axons out of the front of the cord and has no ganglion on it. Pick it for the motor root."],"lt":"t-pns","lid":"c21","comp":185,"competencyId":"w8-cord-roots"},{"id":"L02995","dok":2,"q":"Which root attaches a spinal nerve to the front of the spinal cord and carries motor axons out of the cord?","a":"The ventral root.","options":["The dorsal root","The ventral ramus","The ventral root","The dorsal root ganglion"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The ventral (anterior) root attaches the spinal nerve to the front of the spinal cord and carries motor axons out of the cord. Most of these axons come from motor neuron cell bodies in the ventral gray horn. Just past the cord, the ventral root joins the dorsal root to form a mixed spinal nerve.","notes":["The dorsal root attaches to the back of the cord and carries sensory axons in. Pick it for the sensory root.","The ventral ramus is a branch of the spinal nerve beyond the intervertebral foramen, not an attachment to the cord. Pick it for the branch that supplies the limbs and forms the plexuses.","Correct. The ventral root carries motor axons out of the front of the cord.","The dorsal root ganglion is the swelling on the dorsal root that holds sensory cell bodies. Pick it for where sensory neuron cell bodies sit."],"lt":"t-pns","lid":"c26","comp":185,"competencyId":"w8-cord-roots"},{"id":"L03000","dok":2,"q":"A dorsal root ganglion contains the cell bodies of which neurons?","a":"Sensory neurons.","options":["Sympathetic postganglionic neurons","Somatic motor neurons","Sensory neurons","Interneurons"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"A dorsal root ganglion holds the cell bodies of the sensory neurons for one spinal segment. Each of these neurons sends one branch out toward receptors in the body and another through the dorsal root into the spinal cord. Because the cluster lies outside the CNS, it is called a ganglion rather than a nucleus.","notes":["Sympathetic postganglionic cell bodies sit in the sympathetic trunk ganglia or the prevertebral ganglia. These also lie near the vertebral column, which makes them tempting, but they are motor ganglia of the autonomic system.","Somatic motor neuron cell bodies sit in the ventral (anterior) gray horn inside the cord, and their axons leave through the ventral root. Pick them for the ventral horn.","Correct. The dorsal root ganglion holds sensory neuron cell bodies.","Interneurons sit entirely inside the CNS, for example in the posterior gray horn where sensory axons arrive. Pick them for the neurons that connect sensory input to motor output inside the cord."],"lt":"t-pns","lid":"c31","comp":185,"competencyId":"w8-cord-roots"},{"id":"L03046","dok":3,"q":"An injury cuts the ventral roots of several spinal nerves. The muscles they served become paralyzed and slowly waste away, but skin sensation in the area stays normal. Which statement best explains this pattern?","a":"The ventral roots carry motor axons, while sensory axons enter through the dorsal roots.","options":["The ventral roots carry only autonomic axons to the limb muscles","The ventral roots hold the cell bodies of the sensory neurons for the skin","The ventral roots carry motor axons, while sensory axons enter through the dorsal roots","The ventral roots carry sensory axons, while motor axons leave through the dorsal roots"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The ventral root carries the motor axons that leave the cord for skeletal muscle. When it is cut, the muscles lose their nerve supply, become paralyzed, and slowly shrink, a change called atrophy. Sensory axons enter through the separate dorsal root, so sensation from the skin is spared.","notes":["Skeletal muscles are controlled by somatic motor axons, not autonomic axons. Autonomic axons serve cardiac muscle, smooth muscle, and glands.","Sensory cell bodies sit in the dorsal root ganglion on the dorsal root, not on the ventral root. Pick the dorsal root ganglion for where sensory cell bodies are.","Correct. Cutting the motor roots paralyzes the muscles while leaving the sensory roots intact.","This reverses the two roots. The ventral root is motor and the dorsal root is sensory, which is the reason cutting the ventral roots spares skin sensation."],"lt":"t-pns","lid":"d3-t-pns-4","comp":185,"competencyId":"w8-cord-roots"},{"id":"L03216","dok":1,"q":"Where does the spinal cord lie?","a":"Within the vertebral canal.","options":["Within the thoracic cavity","Within the abdominopelvic cavity","Within the cranial cavity","Within the vertebral canal"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The spinal cord runs down the vertebral canal, the tunnel formed by the stacked vertebral foramina. It is continuous with the medulla oblongata above and ends near the L1 to L2 disc. The cranial cavity and vertebral canal together make up the dorsal body cavity.","notes":["The thoracic cavity is part of the ventral body cavity and holds the heart and lungs. Choose it for a question about those organs.","The abdominopelvic cavity is the lower part of the ventral body cavity and holds the digestive, urinary, and reproductive organs. It would be the answer for organs like the stomach or bladder.","The cranial cavity holds the brain, the other half of the dorsal body cavity. Pick it for a question about where the brain sits.","Correct. The spinal cord lies within the vertebral canal."],"lt":"t-cns-spinal-cord","lid":"c1","comp":184,"competencyId":"w8-cord-external"},{"id":"L03217","dok":1,"q":"What is the tapered, cone-shaped end of the spinal cord called?","a":"The conus medullaris.","options":["The lumbar enlargement","The cauda equina","The conus medullaris","The filum terminale"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The conus medullaris is the cone-shaped tip where the spinal cord ends, at about the disc between L1 and L2 in adults. Below it, the canal holds the cauda equina and the thin filum terminale rather than cord tissue. Its name means cone of the cord.","notes":["The lumbar enlargement is a widening of the cord behind the lower thoracic vertebrae (about T9 to T12) that gives rise to the nerves of the lower limbs. It would be the answer for a question about the wide part just above the end.","The cauda equina is the bundle of nerve roots that hangs below the conus medullaris. Pick it for a question about the horse-tail of roots in the lower canal.","Correct. The conus medullaris is the tapered end of the spinal cord.","The filum terminale is a thread of pia mater that extends from the conus medullaris down to the coccyx. Choose it for a question about what anchors the cord below."],"lt":"t-cns-spinal-cord","lid":"c2","comp":184,"competencyId":"w8-cord-external"},{"id":"L03218","dok":1,"q":"What is the bundle of nerve roots that descends past the end of the spinal cord called?","a":"The cauda equina.","options":["The conus medullaris","The cauda equina","The denticulate ligaments","The filum terminale"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The spinal cord ends near L1 to L2, but the nerve roots for the lumbar, sacral, and coccygeal spinal nerves must still travel down to their exit points. These long roots hang together in the lower vertebral canal and look like a horse's tail, which is what cauda equina means. They float in CSF in the lumbar cistern.","notes":["The conus medullaris is the tapered end of the cord itself, just above the cauda equina. Pick it for a question about where the cord ends.","Correct. The cauda equina is the bundle of roots below the end of the cord.","The denticulate ligaments are tooth-like extensions of pia mater that anchor the cord to the dura along its length. They would be the answer for a question about side-to-side anchoring.","The filum terminale is a single thread of pia mater, not a bundle of nerve roots, running from the conus medullaris to the coccyx. Choose it for a question about anchoring the cord."],"lt":"t-cns-spinal-cord","lid":"c3","comp":184,"competencyId":"w8-cord-external"},{"id":"L03219","dok":1,"q":"Which part of a spinal nerve's connection to the cord carries only sensory axons into the cord?","a":"The posterior (dorsal) root.","options":["The anterior (ventral) ramus","The anterior (ventral) root","The posterior (dorsal) root","The posterior (dorsal) ramus"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Each spinal nerve connects to the cord by two roots. The posterior root is the sensory root; it carries sensory axons from receptors into the cord and has a ganglion holding the sensory cell bodies. A simple rule is posterior is sensory in, anterior is motor out.","notes":["The anterior ramus is a mixed branch of the spinal nerve that serves the limbs and the front and sides of the trunk and forms the plexuses. It would be the answer for a question about what forms the brachial plexus.","The anterior root is the motor root and carries motor axons out of the cord. Pick it for a question about the motor root.","Correct. The posterior root is the sensory root.","The posterior ramus is a branch of the spinal nerve after the roots have joined, so it carries both sensory and motor axons to the back. Choose it for a question about the branch serving the deep back muscles and skin."],"lt":"t-cns-spinal-cord","lid":"c4","comp":185,"competencyId":"w8-cord-roots"},{"id":"L03220","dok":1,"q":"Which root of a spinal nerve carries motor axons out of the spinal cord?","a":"The anterior (ventral) root.","options":["The anterior (ventral) root","The dorsal root ganglion","The posterior (dorsal) root","The gray commissure"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The anterior root is the motor root. Its axons start from motor neuron cell bodies in the anterior and lateral gray horns and leave the cord to reach muscles and glands. It joins the posterior root just beyond the ganglion to form the mixed spinal nerve.","notes":["Correct. The anterior root carries motor axons out of the cord.","The dorsal root ganglion is the swelling on the posterior root that holds sensory cell bodies. Choose it for a question about where sensory neuron cell bodies sit.","The posterior root is the sensory root, carrying sensory axons into the cord. Pick it for a question about the sensory root.","The gray commissure is the bridge of gray matter around the central canal that connects the two sides of the cord. It would be the answer for a question about that internal bridge."],"lt":"t-cns-spinal-cord","lid":"c5","comp":185,"competencyId":"w8-cord-roots"},{"id":"L03221","dok":1,"q":"What does the posterior (dorsal) root ganglion contain?","a":"The cell bodies of sensory neurons.","options":["Cell bodies of somatic motor neurons","Cell bodies of autonomic motor neurons","Interneurons that link sensory to motor","Cell bodies of sensory neurons"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"A ganglion is a cluster of neuron cell bodies in the peripheral nervous system. The posterior root ganglion is the swelling on the posterior root that holds the cell bodies of the sensory neurons for that spinal segment. Only the posterior root has a ganglion, so if a question puts cell bodies on a root, it is the posterior one.","notes":["Somatic motor neuron cell bodies sit inside the cord in the anterior gray horn. Pick them for a question about the anterior horn.","Autonomic motor neuron cell bodies inside the cord sit in the lateral gray horn of the T1 to L2 segments. Choose them for a question about the lateral horn.","Interneurons sit in the gray matter of the cord, especially the posterior horn. They would be the answer for a question about what the posterior horn holds besides incoming sensory axons.","Correct. The posterior root ganglion holds sensory neuron cell bodies."],"lt":"t-cns-spinal-cord","lid":"c6","comp":185,"competencyId":"w8-cord-roots"},{"id":"L03222","dok":1,"q":"In a cross-section of the spinal cord, where is the gray matter?","a":"In the inner, butterfly-shaped core.","options":["Only within the dorsal root ganglia","In an outer layer around the white matter","Only in a thin ring around the central canal","In the inner, butterfly-shaped core"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"In the spinal cord, gray matter forms an H or butterfly shape in the center, and white matter wraps around it on the outside. This is the reverse of the cerebrum, where gray matter forms the outer cortex. The wings of the butterfly are the gray horns, and the central canal sits in the middle of the crossbar.","notes":["The dorsal root ganglia hold sensory cell bodies, but they sit outside the cord on the posterior roots. Choose them for a question about where sensory cell bodies are found.","An outer layer of gray matter is the pattern of the cerebral cortex, not the spinal cord. Pick it for a question about where gray matter lies in the cerebrum.","The gray commissure does surround the central canal, but the gray matter also spreads out into the anterior, posterior, and lateral horns. This option describes only the crossbar.","Correct. Spinal cord gray matter forms the inner, butterfly-shaped core."],"lt":"t-cns-spinal-cord","lid":"c7","comp":186,"competencyId":"w8-cord-internal"},{"id":"L03223","dok":1,"q":"Which gray horn contains autonomic motor nuclei and is present only in the T1 to L2 segments?","a":"The lateral horn.","options":["The anterior horn","The gray commissure","The lateral horn","The posterior horn"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The lateral horn is a small side projection of gray matter between the anterior and posterior horns. It holds the cell bodies of autonomic motor neurons that serve organs, smooth muscle, and glands. It appears only in the T1 to L2 segments, which is where the sympathetic outflow leaves the cord.","notes":["The anterior horn holds somatic motor nuclei that drive skeletal muscle, and it is present at every level. Pick it for a question about motor neurons to skeletal muscle.","The gray commissure is the bridge of gray matter around the central canal, not a horn. It would be the answer for a question about what connects the two sides of the gray matter.","Correct. The lateral horn holds autonomic motor nuclei in the T1 to L2 segments.","The posterior horn holds interneurons and the incoming axons of sensory neurons, and it is present at every level. Choose it for a question about sensory input to the cord."],"lt":"t-cns-spinal-cord","lid":"c8","comp":186,"competencyId":"w8-cord-internal"},{"id":"L03224","dok":1,"q":"Which gray horn of the spinal cord holds the somatic motor nuclei that drive skeletal muscle?","a":"The anterior (ventral) horn.","options":["The posterior (dorsal) horn","The anterior (ventral) horn","The lateral horn","The gray commissure"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The anterior horn is the front wing of the gray matter butterfly. It holds the cell bodies of somatic motor neurons, whose axons leave through the anterior root to make skeletal muscles contract. It is present at every level of the cord and is especially large in the cervical and lumbar enlargements, where the limb muscles are served.","notes":["The posterior horn holds interneurons and receives the incoming axons of sensory neurons. Pick it for a question about sensory input to the gray matter.","Correct. The anterior horn holds somatic motor nuclei for skeletal muscle.","The lateral horn holds autonomic motor nuclei that serve organs, smooth muscle, and glands, and it is found only in the T1 to L2 segments. Choose it for a question about autonomic outflow.","The gray commissure is the bridge of gray matter around the central canal joining the two sides. It would be the answer for a question about that bridge."],"lt":"t-cns-spinal-cord","lid":"c9","comp":186,"competencyId":"w8-cord-internal"},{"id":"L03225","dok":1,"q":"What is the small cerebrospinal fluid channel at the center of the spinal cord called?","a":"The central canal.","options":["The anterior median fissure","The cerebral aqueduct","The fourth ventricle","The central canal"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The central canal is a tiny channel running the length of the spinal cord in the middle of the gray commissure. It is lined by ependymal cells and holds cerebrospinal fluid. At its upper end it is continuous with the fourth ventricle.","notes":["The anterior median fissure is a deep groove on the outside front of the cord, not a fluid channel. It would be the answer for a question about the groove down the front of the cord.","The cerebral aqueduct is the narrow CSF channel through the midbrain that joins the third and fourth ventricles. Pick it for a question about the midbrain.","The fourth ventricle is the CSF space between the brainstem and cerebellum, and the central canal continues down from it. Choose it for the cavity behind the pons.","Correct. The central canal is the CSF channel at the center of the cord."],"lt":"t-cns-spinal-cord","lid":"c10","comp":186,"competencyId":"w8-cord-internal"},{"id":"L03226","dok":1,"q":"What are the sensory tracts that conduct impulses up the spinal cord toward the brain called?","a":"Ascending tracts.","options":["Association tracts","Ascending tracts","Descending tracts","Commissural tracts"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Tracts in the spinal cord white matter are named by the direction they carry signals. Ascending tracts carry sensory impulses up toward the brain, such as the spinothalamic tract, which carries pain and temperature. Descending tracts carry motor commands down from the brain.","notes":["Association tracts link gyri within one cerebral hemisphere, such as the arcuate fasciculus. They would be the answer for a question about connections on one side of the cerebrum.","Correct. Ascending tracts carry sensory impulses up toward the brain.","Descending tracts are motor tracts that carry impulses down from the brain to the cord. Pick them for a question about motor commands.","Commissural tracts connect the two cerebral hemispheres, as the corpus callosum does. Choose them for a question about cerebral white matter between hemispheres."],"lt":"t-cns-spinal-cord","lid":"c11","comp":183,"competencyId":"w8-neuronal-pools"},{"id":"L03227","dok":1,"q":"Which two enlargements widen the spinal cord?","a":"The cervical and lumbar enlargements.","options":["The cervical and thoracic enlargements","The thoracic and sacral enlargements","The dorsal and ventral enlargements","The cervical and lumbar enlargements"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The cord widens where it must serve the limbs, because many more neurons are needed there. The cervical enlargement, from C4 to T1, gives rise to the nerves of the upper limbs, and the lumbar enlargement, behind the lower thoracic vertebrae (about T9 to T12), gives rise to the nerves of the lower limbs. The thoracic cord between them is the narrowest part.","notes":["This pairs the correct cervical enlargement with the thoracic region, which is actually the narrowest part of the cord. Only the regions serving limbs are enlarged.","The thoracic region is the narrowest part of the cord, not an enlargement. This option misses both limb regions.","Dorsal and ventral describe the back and front of the cord, such as the dorsal and ventral roots, not enlargements. Pick those terms for a question about the roots.","Correct. The cervical and lumbar enlargements serve the upper and lower limbs."],"lt":"t-cns-spinal-cord","lid":"c12","comp":184,"competencyId":"w8-cord-external"},{"id":"L03228","dok":1,"q":"What is the thread of pia mater that anchors the lower end of the spinal cord to the coccyx called?","a":"The filum terminale.","options":["The denticulate ligament","The filum terminale","The cauda equina","The conus medullaris"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The filum terminale is a thin thread of pia mater that continues down from the conus medullaris. It runs through the lumbar cistern among the roots of the cauda equina and attaches to the coccyx. Along the way it picks up a covering of dura at the end of the dural sac.","notes":["The denticulate ligaments are tooth-like extensions of pia mater that anchor the cord to the dura along its sides. They would be the answer for side-to-side anchoring along the length of the cord.","Correct. The filum terminale anchors the cord to the coccyx.","The cauda equina is the bundle of nerve roots below the end of the cord, not an anchoring thread. Pick it for a question about the horse-tail of roots.","The conus medullaris is the tapered end of the cord itself, where the filum terminale begins. Choose it for a question about where the cord ends."],"lt":"t-cns-spinal-cord","lid":"c13","comp":184,"competencyId":"w8-cord-external"},{"id":"L03229","dok":1,"q":"What is the deep groove running down the front of the spinal cord called?","a":"The anterior median fissure.","options":["The central canal","The anterior median fissure","The posterior median sulcus","The longitudinal fissure"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Two grooves run the full length of the cord and divide it into right and left halves. The anterior median fissure is the deep groove down the front, and the posterior median sulcus is the shallow groove down the back. Remember that fissures are deeper than sulci, here as in the brain.","notes":["The central canal is the CSF channel inside the cord, not a surface groove. Choose it for a question about the channel in the gray commissure.","Correct. The anterior median fissure is the deep groove down the front of the cord.","The posterior median sulcus is the shallow groove down the back of the cord. Pick it for the posterior groove.","The longitudinal fissure is in the brain and separates the two cerebral hemispheres. It would be the answer for the groove between the hemispheres."],"lt":"t-cns-spinal-cord","lid":"c14","comp":184,"competencyId":"w8-cord-external"},{"id":"L03230","dok":1,"q":"In the central nervous system, what is a bundle of axons that shares a common origin and destination called?","a":"A tract.","options":["A ganglion","A nerve","A tract","A nucleus"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Clusters of cell bodies and bundles of axons get different names depending on whether they are in the CNS or the PNS. In the CNS, a bundle of axons is a tract and a cluster of cell bodies is a nucleus. In the PNS, a bundle of axons is a nerve and a cluster of cell bodies is a ganglion.","notes":["A ganglion is a cluster of neuron cell bodies in the PNS, such as the dorsal root ganglion. It would be the answer for cell body clusters outside the CNS.","A nerve is a bundle of axons in the peripheral nervous system. Pick it if the question asks about axon bundles outside the brain and cord.","Correct. A CNS bundle of axons is a tract.","A nucleus is a cluster of neuron cell bodies in the CNS, not a bundle of axons. Choose it for a question about gray matter clusters such as cranial nerve nuclei."],"lt":"t-cns-spinal-cord","lid":"c15","comp":179,"competencyId":"w8-tissue-collections"},{"id":"L03232","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares the arrangement of gray and white matter in the spinal cord and the cerebrum?","a":"The cord has gray matter inside and white matter outside, the reverse of the cerebrum.","options":["Cord: gray outside, white inside, the reverse of the cerebrum","Both have gray matter outside and white matter inside","Cord: gray inside, white outside, the reverse of the cerebrum","Both have gray matter inside and white matter outside"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"In the spinal cord, gray matter forms the butterfly-shaped core and white matter tracts surround it. In the cerebrum, gray matter forms the outer cortex and white matter fills the inside. Both contain gray and white matter; only the arrangement is reversed.","notes":["This flips the cord's pattern. Gray matter outside and white inside is the arrangement of the cerebrum, not the cord.","This describes only the cerebrum, with its outer cortex. Pick it for a question about the cerebrum alone.","Correct. The cord has gray inside and white outside, while the cerebrum has gray outside and white inside.","This describes only the spinal cord. The cerebrum differs because its gray matter forms the outer cortex."],"lt":"t-cns-spinal-cord","lid":"c17","comp":180,"competencyId":"w8-gray-white-matter"},{"id":"L03233","dok":2,"q":"Tiny bundles of axons attach in a row along the back or the front of the cord and merge to form each root. What are these small bundles called?","a":"Rootlets.","options":["Tracts","Rami","Rootlets","Horns"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Each root does not leave the cord as one cable. Instead, a row of small rootlets fans out from the cord surface and gathers into a single posterior or anterior root. The two roots then join to form the spinal nerve.","notes":["Tracts are bundles of axons inside the white matter of the CNS that run up or down the cord. Pick them for a question about ascending or descending pathways.","Rami are the branches a spinal nerve splits into after it leaves the intervertebral foramen. They would be the answer for a question about the anterior and posterior branches of a spinal nerve.","Correct. Rootlets merge to form each root.","Horns are projections of the gray matter inside the cord, such as the anterior and posterior horns. Choose them for a question about the butterfly-shaped gray matter."],"lt":"t-cns-spinal-cord","lid":"c18","comp":185,"competencyId":"w8-cord-roots"},{"id":"L03234","dok":2,"q":"At which vertebral level does the adult spinal cord typically end?","a":"At about the L1 to L2 level.","options":["At the L1 to L2 level","At the T6 level","At the L4 to L5 level","At the S2 level"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The adult spinal cord ends at the conus medullaris near the disc between L1 and L2. The vertebral canal continues much lower, so below this point the canal holds only the cauda equina and CSF in the lumbar cistern. This is why a lumbar puncture is done lower, at L3 to L4 or L4 to L5.","notes":["Correct. The adult cord typically ends near the L1 to L2 level.","T6 is in the middle of the thoracic region, well above the end of the cord. The cord continues through the rest of the thoracic segments and the lumbar enlargement before ending.","L4 to L5 is below the end of the cord and is a common site for a lumbar puncture. Pick it for a question about where the needle is placed for a spinal tap.","S2 is roughly where the dural sac and subarachnoid space end, well below the end of the cord. Choose it for a question about where the lumbar cistern stops, not the cord."],"lt":"t-cns-spinal-cord","lid":"c19","comp":184,"competencyId":"w8-cord-external"},{"id":"L03235","dok":2,"q":"Which spinal cord segment has the largest diameter and a large amount of white matter in an oval shape?","a":"The cervical segment.","options":["The lumbar segment","The sacral segment","The thoracic segment","The cervical segment"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"White matter is thickest at the top of the cord because almost every tract is still running there, carrying signals for the whole body below. The cervical segments also serve the upper limbs, so they are wide and oval. White matter thins as you move down the cord, since fewer tracts are still traveling.","notes":["Lumbar segments are almost circular with less white matter than the cervical segments. Choose lumbar for a nearly round section with large anterior horns.","Sacral segments are relatively small, with more gray matter and little white matter. They would be the answer for a small section that is mostly gray.","Thoracic segments have a small diameter with little gray matter and a small lateral horn. Pick thoracic for a cross-section that is small and shows a lateral horn.","Correct. Cervical segments are the largest, oval, and rich in white matter."],"lt":"t-cns-spinal-cord","lid":"c20","comp":186,"competencyId":"w8-cord-internal"},{"id":"L03236","dok":2,"q":"Which gray horn would you expect to see in a thoracic segment but not in a cervical segment?","a":"The lateral horn.","options":["The anterior horn","The lateral horn","The gray commissure","The posterior horn"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The lateral horn holds autonomic motor nuclei and is present only in the T1 to L2 segments. A cervical section therefore shows anterior and posterior horns but no lateral horn. Spotting that small side projection is one way to tell a thoracic section from a cervical one.","notes":["The anterior horn holds somatic motor nuclei and is present at every level of the cord. It would not help tell a thoracic section from a cervical one.","Correct. The lateral horn appears in the T1 to L2 segments but not in cervical ones.","The gray commissure is the bridge of gray matter around the central canal, present at all levels, and it is not a horn. Choose it for a question about the connection between the two sides.","The posterior horn receives sensory input and is present at every level. Pick it for a question about where sensory axons enter the gray matter."],"lt":"t-cns-spinal-cord","lid":"c21","comp":186,"competencyId":"w8-cord-internal"},{"id":"L03237","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares ascending and descending tracts of the spinal cord?","a":"Ascending tracts are sensory and run up; descending tracts are motor and run down.","options":["Ascending are sensory and run down; descending are motor and run up","Ascending are motor and run up; descending are sensory and run down","Ascending are sensory and run up; descending are motor and run down","Both carry sensory and motor signals in either direction"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The names tell the direction. Ascending tracts carry sensory signals up toward the brain, such as the spinothalamic tract. Descending tracts carry motor commands down from the brain, such as the corticospinal tract. Each tract carries signals in one direction only.","notes":["This keeps the correct signal type but reverses the direction. Ascending means going up, so these tracts run toward the brain, not away from it.","This swaps the type of signal. Sensory information travels up to the brain and motor commands travel down, so ascending tracts cannot be motor.","Correct. Ascending tracts are sensory and run up, and descending tracts are motor and run down.","Each tract carries one kind of signal in one direction. A mixed, two-way bundle better describes a spinal nerve, which carries sensory and motor axons together."],"lt":"t-cns-spinal-cord","lid":"c22","comp":183,"competencyId":"w8-neuronal-pools"},{"id":"L03239","dok":2,"q":"The cervical enlargement of the spinal cord gives rise to the nerves of which body region?","a":"The upper limbs.","options":["The lower limbs","The upper limbs","The pelvic organs","The thoracic wall"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The cervical enlargement runs from about C4 to T1. The arms need many motor and sensory neurons for their detailed movement and sensation, so this part of the cord holds extra neurons and is wider. Its nerves form the brachial plexus, which supplies the upper limbs.","notes":["The lower limbs are served by the lumbar enlargement, behind the lower thoracic vertebrae (about T9 to T12). Pick it if the question names the lumbar enlargement.","Correct. The cervical enlargement gives rise to the nerves of the upper limbs.","The pelvic organs get their nerves from the lower part of the cord, far below the cervical enlargement in the neck. The cervical enlargement serves the upper limbs only.","The thoracic wall is served by spinal nerves from the thoracic segments, which form the narrowest part of the cord. Choose it for a question about the intercostal region."],"lt":"t-cns-spinal-cord","lid":"c24","comp":184,"competencyId":"w8-cord-external"},{"id":"L03240","dok":2,"q":"Which bridge of gray matter surrounds the central canal and connects the two sides of the cord?","a":"The gray commissure.","options":["The lateral horn","The gray commissure","The anterior white commissure","The posterior median sulcus"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The gray commissure is the crossbar of the gray matter butterfly. It connects the right and left halves of the gray matter, and the central canal runs through its middle. A commissure is any bridge that connects two sides.","notes":["The lateral horn is a side projection of gray matter in the T1 to L2 segments, holding autonomic motor nuclei. Choose it for a question about autonomic outflow.","Correct. The gray commissure surrounds the central canal and joins the two sides.","The anterior white commissure is also a bridge between the two sides, but it is made of white matter and lies in front of the gray commissure. Pick it if the question asks for the white matter bridge.","The posterior median sulcus is the shallow groove down the back surface of the cord. It would be the answer for the groove on the posterior side."],"lt":"t-cns-spinal-cord","lid":"c25","comp":186,"competencyId":"w8-cord-internal"},{"id":"L03241","dok":3,"q":"A clinician performs a lumbar puncture between the L3 and L4 spines. Why is this site safe for the spinal cord?","a":"The cord ends near L1 to L2, above the needle site.","options":["The dura ends at L2, so no membranes are pierced","The denticulate ligaments pull the cord aside there","The central canal widens into a large reservoir there","The cord ends near L1 to L2, above the needle site"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The spinal cord ends at the conus medullaris near the L1 to L2 disc, but the subarachnoid space continues lower as the lumbar cistern. A needle placed between L3 and L4 enters this pool of CSF where only the loose roots of the cauda equina float, and they tend to drift aside. The cord itself is safely above the needle.","notes":["The dura continues below L2, and a lumbar puncture needle does pass through it and the arachnoid to reach CSF. The dural sac ends around S2.","The denticulate ligaments anchor the cord to the dura along its sides, but they end with the cord. Pick them for a question about side-to-side anchoring.","The central canal is a tiny channel inside the cord, and it does not form a reservoir below. The CSF pool used for a tap is the lumbar cistern, part of the subarachnoid space.","Correct. The cord ends near L1 to L2, so a needle lower down meets only the cauda equina and CSF."],"lt":"t-cns-spinal-cord","lid":"c26","comp":169,"competencyId":"w7-cord-termination-lp"},{"id":"L03242","dok":3,"q":"In shingles, a virus reactivates and causes a rash along the exact strip of skin served by one spinal segment. Where did the virus lie dormant?","a":"In the posterior (dorsal) root ganglion.","options":["In the posterior root ganglion","In the anterior gray horn","In the lateral gray horn","In the posterior gray horn of the cord"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The varicella zoster virus, which causes chickenpox, hides for decades in sensory neuron cell bodies. For a spinal segment, those cell bodies sit in the posterior root ganglion. When the virus reactivates, it travels down that ganglion's sensory axons, so the rash follows the one strip of skin, or dermatome, that the ganglion serves.","notes":["Correct. The virus hides in the sensory cell bodies of the posterior root ganglion.","The anterior gray horn holds somatic motor neuron cell bodies inside the cord. Pick it for a question about motor neurons to skeletal muscle.","The lateral gray horn holds autonomic motor neurons in the T1 to L2 segments. It would be the answer for a question about autonomic outflow.","The posterior gray horn receives incoming sensory axons and holds interneurons, but the sensory cell bodies themselves sit outside the cord in the ganglion. Choose it for a question about where sensory axons synapse."],"lt":"t-cns-spinal-cord","lid":"c27","comp":185,"competencyId":"w8-cord-roots"},{"id":"L03243","dok":3,"q":"After a spinal cord injury, a patient loses sensation and movement everywhere below the injury but keeps normal function above it. Damage to which structures explains this pattern?","a":"The ascending and descending tracts.","options":["The ascending and descending tracts","The dorsal root ganglia below the injury","The roots of the spinal nerves at that level","The denticulate ligaments"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The ascending and descending tracts in the white matter carry every signal between the brain and the body below. When they are cut at one level, nothing below that level can send sensation up or receive motor commands from above. Everything served by segments above the injury still connects to the brain, so it keeps working.","notes":["Correct. Cutting the ascending and descending tracts disconnects the body below the injury from the brain.","The dorsal root ganglia below the injury are undamaged and still hold their sensory cell bodies. The problem is that their signals cannot travel past the injury to the brain.","Damage to the roots at one level affects only the strip of skin and the muscles served by that one segment. Pick this for a narrow, band-like loss, not loss of everything below.","The denticulate ligaments anchor the cord to the dura and carry no signals. Choose them for a question about how the cord is held in place."],"lt":"t-cns-spinal-cord","lid":"c28","comp":183,"competencyId":"w8-neuronal-pools"},{"id":"L03244","dok":3,"q":"A spinal cord cross-section is relatively small and is mostly gray matter, with only a thin rim of white matter. Which segment is it most likely from?","a":"A sacral segment.","options":["A thoracic segment","A lumbar segment","A cervical segment","A sacral segment"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"White matter thins as you go down the cord because fewer tracts are still running at each lower level. By the sacral segments, little white matter remains, so a sacral section is relatively small and has more gray matter than white matter.","notes":["A thoracic segment has a small diameter with little gray matter and a small lateral horn. Choose it for a small section with thin gray horns and a lateral horn.","A lumbar segment is almost circular, with less white matter than cervical but still a sizable white rim. It would be the answer for a nearly round section with large anterior horns.","A cervical segment is the largest and oval, with a large amount of white matter. Pick it for a big section that is mostly white.","Correct. Sacral segments are small, with more gray matter and little white matter."],"lt":"t-cns-spinal-cord","lid":"c29","comp":186,"competencyId":"w8-cord-internal"},{"id":"L03245","dok":3,"q":"A spinal cord cross-section is almost circular and fairly large, with less white matter than a cervical section. Which segment is it most likely from?","a":"A lumbar segment.","options":["A thoracic segment","A lumbar segment","A cervical segment","A coccygeal segment"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Lumbar segments sit in the lumbar enlargement, so they are fairly large with big anterior horns for the lower limbs. They are almost circular in outline and carry less white matter than cervical segments, because fewer tracts are still running that low. Shape and the amount of white matter are the main clues for identifying a segment.","notes":["A thoracic segment has a small diameter with little gray matter and a lateral horn. It would be the answer for a small section showing a lateral horn.","Correct. Lumbar segments are nearly circular with less white matter than cervical ones.","A cervical segment is large and oval with abundant white matter. Pick it for the widest, mostly white section.","The coccygeal segment is the smallest and looks like the lower sacral segments. Choose it for a tiny section at the very end of the cord."],"lt":"t-cns-spinal-cord","lid":"c30","comp":186,"competencyId":"w8-cord-internal"},{"id":"L03246","dok":3,"q":"You pull your hand off a hot stove before you even feel the pain. Why is this withdrawal reflex faster than a conscious response?","a":"The reflex arc is completed within the spinal cord, so the signal does not travel to the brain and back first.","options":["The cerebellum sends the command because it is closer to the limb","The signal skips the sensory neuron and goes straight to the muscle","The arc is completed in the spinal cord without a trip to the brain","The dorsal root ganglion sends the motor command to the muscle"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"In a reflex arc, a sensory neuron carries the signal into the spinal cord, gray matter there connects it to a motor neuron, and the motor neuron carries the command out to the muscle. The whole loop runs through the cord, so it is much shorter than a path up to the brain and back. The brain is informed afterward, which is why you feel the pain a moment later.","notes":["The cerebellum coordinates movement but is not part of a spinal withdrawal reflex. Choose it for a question about smoothing and coordinating movement.","Every reflex arc starts with a sensory neuron that detects the stimulus. Without it, the cord would never learn that the stove is hot.","Correct. The spinal cord completes the reflex arc, so the response does not wait for the brain.","The dorsal root ganglion holds sensory cell bodies and does not send motor commands. Motor commands leave through motor neurons in the anterior horn and the anterior root."],"lt":"t-cns-spinal-cord","lid":"d3-t-cns-spinal-cord-1","comp":181,"competencyId":"w8-reflex-arc"},{"id":"L03247","dok":3,"q":"White matter is made mostly of myelinated axons. In the spinal cord, white matter surrounds the central gray matter. What does this outer white matter do?","a":"It forms tracts that carry signals up and down between the brain and body.","options":["It produces cerebrospinal fluid for the central canal","It is where reflex connections between neurons are made","It forms tracts that carry signals up and down the cord","It holds the motor neuron cell bodies that drive muscles"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Myelinated axons are built for carrying signals quickly over long distances. In the cord, these axons are bundled into ascending and descending tracts in the outer white columns, forming highways between the brain and body. The central gray matter, with its cell bodies, handles local processing such as reflex connections.","notes":["CSF is made by the choroid plexus in the brain's ventricles. It would be the answer for a question about where cerebrospinal fluid is produced.","Reflex connections are made in the gray matter, where sensory neurons, interneurons, and motor neurons meet. Choose gray matter for a question about where reflexes are integrated.","Correct. The outer white matter forms ascending and descending tracts.","Motor neuron cell bodies sit in the anterior gray horn, part of the central gray matter. Pick that for a question about where somatic motor neurons are."],"lt":"t-cns-spinal-cord","lid":"d3-t-cns-spinal-cord-2","comp":180,"competencyId":"w8-gray-white-matter"},{"id":"L03248","dok":3,"q":"A surgeon cuts only the posterior roots of one spinal segment. What is the most likely result in the area served by that segment?","a":"Loss of sensation, with voluntary movement preserved.","options":["Loss of sensation, with movement preserved","Loss of movement, with sensation preserved","No change, because the anterior root takes over","Loss of both sensation and movement"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The posterior root is the sensory root, carrying sensory axons into the cord. Cutting it stops sensation from that segment from reaching the cord and brain. The anterior, motor root is untouched, so motor commands still reach the muscles and voluntary movement remains.","notes":["Correct. Cutting the posterior roots removes sensation but spares the motor pathway.","Loss of movement with sensation spared would follow cutting the anterior roots, which carry the motor axons. Pick this if the question named the anterior roots.","The anterior root carries only motor axons and cannot take over sensory work. Each root has its own direction of traffic, so losing one removes that function.","Loss of both would follow cutting the spinal nerve itself, after the two roots have joined. Choose this for damage to the mixed spinal nerve."],"lt":"t-cns-spinal-cord","lid":"d3-t-cns-spinal-cord-3","comp":185,"competencyId":"w8-cord-roots"},{"id":"L03249","dok":3,"q":"A spinal cord injury in the neck paralyzes far more of the body than a similar injury in the lower back. Why does the level of injury matter so much?","a":"Signals for every body region below the injury must pass that point, so a higher injury cuts off more of the body.","options":["The cervical cord is not protected by any vertebrae","Lower injuries also damage the brainstem above them","Signals for all regions below must pass the injury site","The cervical cord holds the only motor neurons for the body"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The tracts in the cord carry signals between the brain and every region below each level. An injury in the neck blocks the signals for the arms, trunk, and legs, while an injury in the lower back blocks only the regions below it. The higher the injury, the more of the body is disconnected from the brain.","notes":["The cervical cord is enclosed by the cervical vertebrae like the rest of the cord. Protection is not what differs between the levels.","An injury in the lower back damages the cord at that level, not the brainstem far above it. This reverses the direction of the effect.","Correct. Every signal to and from regions below the injury must pass through it, so higher injuries cut off more.","Motor neurons for each region sit in the anterior horns at many levels along the whole cord, not only in the neck. The lumbar enlargement, for example, holds the motor neurons for the legs."],"lt":"t-cns-spinal-cord","lid":"d3-t-cns-spinal-cord-4","comp":184,"competencyId":"w8-cord-external"},{"id":"L03250","dok":2,"q":"Which list gives the protective coverings of the spinal cord in order, from outside to inside?","a":"Vertebra, epidural space, dura mater, arachnoid mater, pia mater.","options":["Vertebra, epidural space, pia mater, arachnoid mater, dura mater","Vertebra, dura mater, epidural space, pia mater, arachnoid mater","Vertebra, epidural space, dura mater, arachnoid mater, pia mater","Vertebra, subdural space, arachnoid mater, dura mater, pia mater"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The cord is protected by bone, then the fat-filled epidural space, then three meninges. The tough dura mater is outermost, the lacy arachnoid mater is in the middle, and the thin pia mater clings to the cord itself. Remembering that the names run from tough mother to tender mother keeps the order straight.","notes":["This reverses the three meninges. The pia mater clings to the cord surface, so it is the innermost layer, not the one next to the epidural space.","The epidural space lies between the bone and the dura, so it comes before the dura, not after it. The arachnoid is also always outside the pia.","Correct. Bone, epidural space, dura, arachnoid, and pia run from outside to inside.","The subdural space lies between the dura and the arachnoid, not between the bone and the dura. This list also puts the arachnoid outside the dura."],"lt":"t-cns-spinal-cord","lid":"d3-t-cns-spinal-cord-5","comp":168,"competencyId":"w7-spinal-meninges-spaces"},{"id":"L03251","dok":2,"q":"In a lumbar puncture, the needle collects cerebrospinal fluid from which space?","a":"The subarachnoid space (the lumbar cistern).","options":["The epidural space","The subdural space","The subarachnoid space","The central canal"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Cerebrospinal fluid around the cord fills the subarachnoid space, between the arachnoid mater and the pia mater. Below the end of the cord this space widens into the lumbar cistern. The needle passes through the skin, ligaments, epidural space, dura, and arachnoid before CSF appears.","notes":["The epidural space lies outside the dura and holds fat and veins, not CSF. Pick it for a question about where an epidural injection is given.","The subdural space between the dura and arachnoid is only a potential space with a thin film of fluid. Choose it for a question about where a subdural hematoma forms.","Correct. CSF is drawn from the subarachnoid space in the lumbar cistern.","The central canal is a tiny channel inside the cord, and the cord ends above the needle site. It would be the answer for the CSF channel at the center of the cord."],"lt":"t-cns-spinal-cord","lid":"d3-t-cns-spinal-cord-6","comp":169,"competencyId":"w7-cord-termination-lp"},{"id":"L03252","dok":3,"q":"In a newborn the spinal cord reaches lower in the vertebral canal than in an adult. Why does the adult cord end well above the bottom of the vertebral column?","a":"The cord stops lengthening in early childhood while the vertebral column keeps growing.","options":["The lower end of the cord turns into the filum terminale with age","The column keeps growing after the cord stops lengthening","The cauda equina pulls the cord upward during growth","The cord shrinks after birth as unused neurons die off"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The spinal cord stops lengthening at about 4 to 5 years of age, but the vertebral column keeps growing through childhood and adolescence. As the column outgrows the cord, the end of the cord ends up higher in the canal, near L1 to L2. The nerve roots below stretch down to reach their own exit points, forming the cauda equina.","notes":["The filum terminale is a thread of pia mater that extends below the cord, not converted cord tissue. Choose it for a question about what anchors the cord to the coccyx.","Correct. The vertebral column outgrows the spinal cord.","The cauda equina is a result of the mismatch in growth, not its cause. Its long roots form because they must reach exit points far below the end of the cord.","The cord does not shrink. It simply stops growing in length while the bony column keeps getting longer."],"lt":"t-cns-spinal-cord","lid":"d3x-t-cns-spinal-cord-1","comp":184,"competencyId":"w8-cord-external"},{"id":"L03253","dok":2,"q":"The lumbar enlargement is wider than the thoracic cord just above it. What explains the extra width?","a":"It holds extra neurons for the nerves of the lower limbs.","options":["It holds extra neurons for the lower limb nerves","It stores CSF for the lumbar cistern below it","It holds the dorsal root ganglia for the whole body","It holds the cardiac and respiratory centers"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The legs need many motor and sensory neurons for movement and sensation. The lumbar enlargement, behind the lower thoracic vertebrae (about T9 to T12), holds those extra neurons, mostly in larger gray horns, and gives rise to the nerves of the lower limbs. The cervical enlargement does the same job for the upper limbs.","notes":["Correct. The lumbar enlargement holds the extra neurons that serve the lower limbs.","CSF fills the subarachnoid space around the cord and is made by the choroid plexus in the ventricles, not stored inside the cord. Choose the lumbar cistern for a question about the CSF pool below the cord.","Dorsal root ganglia sit outside the cord on each posterior root, one pair per segment. They would be the answer for a question about where sensory cell bodies are found.","The cardiac and respiratory centers are in the medulla oblongata of the brainstem. Pick them for a question about the vital centers."],"lt":"t-cns-spinal-cord","lid":"d3x-t-cns-spinal-cord-2","comp":184,"competencyId":"w8-cord-external"},{"id":"L04538","dok":1,"q":"How does the spinal dura mater differ from the cranial dura mater?","a":"The spinal dura is a single layer, not fused to bone, unlike the two-layered cranial dura.","options":["The spinal dura lies inside the arachnoid, on the cord","The spinal dura is one layer, not fused to bone","The spinal dura has two layers fused to the vertebrae","The spinal dura forms the dural venous sinuses"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The cranial dura has two layers, an outer periosteal layer attached to the skull and an inner meningeal layer. Around the spinal cord, the dura is a single tough layer, and it is not fused to the vertebrae. The gap between the spinal dura and the bone is the epidural space, which is why epidural injections are given in the vertebral canal.","notes":["The dura is always the outermost meninx, both in the skull and around the cord. The layer on the cord is the pia mater, which would be the answer for the innermost membrane.","Correct. The spinal dura is a single layer separated from bone by the epidural space.","This describes the cranial dura, where the outer layer is attached to the skull. Pick it if the question asked about the dura inside the cranium.","The dural venous sinuses form where the two layers of the cranial dura separate, so they are a cranial feature. Choose them for a question about how venous blood drains from the brain."],"lt":"t-w7-spinal-meninges-spaces","lid":"w7-spinal-meninges-spaces-r1","comp":168,"competencyId":"w7-spinal-meninges-spaces"},{"id":"L04539","dok":1,"q":"What is the epidural space, and what fills it?","a":"The space between the spinal dura and the vertebral bone, filled with fat and veins.","options":["Between arachnoid and pia; filled with cerebrospinal fluid","Between dura and arachnoid; a potential space with a fluid film","Inside the central canal of the cord; filled with CSF","Between spinal dura and vertebrae; filled with fat and veins"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The epidural space lies between the spinal dura mater and the bone of the vertebral canal, outside the dura (epi means upon). It is filled with fat and veins. It is a real space along the spinal cord, but inside the cranium it is only a potential space, and it is where anesthetic is placed in an epidural injection.","notes":["This describes the subarachnoid space, where CSF circulates around the brain and cord. It would be the answer for the space a lumbar puncture reaches.","This describes the subdural space, a potential space only, with a thin film of fluid. Pick it if the question asked where a subdural hematoma forms.","The central canal is the narrow channel running down the center of the spinal cord gray matter, continuous with the fourth ventricle. Choose it for a question about the CSF channel inside the cord itself.","Correct. The epidural space lies outside the spinal dura and holds fat and veins."],"lt":"t-w7-spinal-meninges-spaces","lid":"w7-spinal-meninges-spaces-r2","comp":168,"competencyId":"w7-spinal-meninges-spaces"},{"id":"L04541","dok":2,"q":"What do the denticulate ligaments and the filum terminale have in common?","a":"Both are extensions of pia mater that anchor the spinal cord.","options":["Both are bundles of nerve roots that hang below the conus","Both are bands of arachnoid that return CSF to the blood","Both are extensions of pia mater that anchor the spinal cord","Both are folds of dura mater that divide the cranial cavity"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The denticulate ligaments and the filum terminale are both made of pia mater, the innermost meninx, and both hold the spinal cord in place. The denticulate ligaments are tooth-like extensions along each side of the cord that anchor it to the dura. The filum terminale is a thread of pia that runs from the conus medullaris down to anchor on the coccyx.","notes":["The bundle of nerve roots below the conus medullaris is the cauda equina. It would be the answer for the horse-tail of roots in the lower vertebral canal, which the filum terminale runs through.","The structures that return CSF to the blood are the arachnoid granulations, which project into the dural venous sinuses. Choose them if the question asked how CSF is reabsorbed.","Correct. Both are pia mater, one anchoring the sides of the cord and one anchoring its end.","Folds of dura that divide the cranial cavity are the dural reflections, such as the falx cerebri and tentorium cerebelli. Pick them for a question about partitions inside the skull."],"lt":"t-w7-spinal-meninges-spaces","lid":"w7-spinal-meninges-spaces-r5","comp":168,"competencyId":"w7-spinal-meninges-spaces"},{"id":"L04577","dok":2,"q":"Why is a lumbar puncture done between the L3 and L4 or the L4 and L5 vertebrae?","a":"The cord ends near the L1 to L2 disc, so the needle enters the CSF-filled subarachnoid space below it without hitting the cord.","options":["The epidural space holds the most CSF in the lower lumbar region","The cord ends near L1 to L2, so the needle reaches CSF below it","The subarachnoid space ends at L2, so CSF pools only above it","The cord is thickest there, so the needle can find it easily"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The spinal cord is shorter than the vertebral column and ends at the conus medullaris near the L1 to L2 disc in an adult. The subarachnoid space continues below it as the lumbar cistern, a pool of CSF holding only the floating nerve roots of the cauda equina. A needle placed between L3 and L4 or L4 and L5 reaches this CSF without risk of hitting the cord, and the roots drift aside.","notes":["The epidural space holds fat and veins, not CSF; it is the site of an epidural injection. The CSF drawn in a lumbar puncture comes from the subarachnoid space, deeper, past the dura and arachnoid.","Correct. Below the end of the cord, the lumbar cistern holds CSF and only floating nerve roots.","It is the cord, not the subarachnoid space, that ends near L1 to L2. The subarachnoid space continues lower as the lumbar cistern, which is exactly why the tap works.","The goal of a lumbar puncture is to avoid the cord, not to find it, and the cord does not even reach this level. The needle is placed where there is CSF and no cord at all."],"lt":"t-w7-cord-termination-lp","lid":"w7-cord-termination-lp-r2","comp":169,"competencyId":"w7-cord-termination-lp"},{"id":"L04578","dok":2,"q":"What is the cauda equina?","a":"The bundle of nerve roots descending below the conus medullaris.","options":["The thread of pia mater anchoring the cord to the coccyx","The bundle of nerve roots below the conus medullaris","The enlarged CSF-filled space below the end of the cord","The tapered, cone-shaped lower end of the spinal cord"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Because the spinal cord ends near the L1 to L2 disc, the lumbar, sacral, and coccygeal nerve roots must travel down the vertebral canal to reach their exit points. This bundle of roots hanging below the conus medullaris looks like a horse's tail, which is what cauda equina means. The roots float in the CSF of the lumbar cistern.","notes":["This describes the filum terminale, which runs down among the roots of the cauda equina. It would be the answer for the pial thread that anchors the cord.","Correct. The cauda equina is the horse-tail of nerve roots below the end of the cord.","This describes the lumbar cistern, the enlarged subarachnoid space where a lumbar puncture draws CSF. The cauda equina floats inside it, which is why the two are easy to confuse.","This describes the conus medullaris, the tapered end of the cord. Pick it if the question asked where the spinal cord itself stops."],"lt":"t-w7-cord-termination-lp","lid":"w7-cord-termination-lp-r3","comp":184,"competencyId":"w8-cord-external"},{"id":"L04613","dok":1,"q":"In a cross-section of the spinal cord, where are the gray matter and white matter located?","a":"Gray matter is on the inside; white matter is on the outside.","options":["White inside, gray outside","Gray and white evenly mixed","Gray inside, white outside","Gray in front, white in back"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"In the spinal cord, the gray matter forms an inner core of neuron cell bodies shaped like a butterfly, and the white matter surrounds it on the outside. The outer white matter is made of myelinated axons organized into columns that hold the ascending and descending tracts. This is the reverse of the cerebrum, where the gray cortex is on the outside.","notes":["This is the arrangement in the cerebrum, where the gray cortex covers the white matter. Pick it if the question asked about the brain rather than the spinal cord.","Gray and white matter form separate regions in the cord, a gray core and a white outer layer, not an even mix. Gray nuclei scattered among white tracts is the pattern of the brainstem.","Correct. The cord has a gray core wrapped in white matter.","Gray matter does have anterior and posterior horns, but white matter surrounds it on all sides, not just at the back. This mix-up confuses the horns of the gray matter with the location of the white matter."],"lt":"t-w8-gray-white-matter","lid":"w8-gray-white-matter-r1","comp":180,"competencyId":"w8-gray-white-matter"},{"id":"L04615","dok":1,"q":"What shape does the gray matter form in a cross-section of the spinal cord?","a":"A butterfly (or H) shape.","options":["A crescent along the back","A butterfly or H shape","A thin outer ring","A solid circle"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"In cross-section, the gray matter of the cord looks like a butterfly or the letter H. Its projections are the horns: the posterior horns point toward the back, the anterior horns point toward the front, and small lateral horns appear in the T1 to L2 segments. A bridge called the gray commissure crosses the middle and surrounds the central canal.","notes":["The gray matter reaches both front and back, so it is not a crescent on one side. This mix-up may come from remembering only the posterior horns.","Correct. The cord's gray matter forms a butterfly or H shape.","A thin outer ring describes the cerebral cortex covering the brain, not the cord's gray matter. Pick it if the question asked about gray matter in the cerebrum.","The gray matter is not a solid circle; its horns project toward the front, back, and sides, with white matter filling in between. This choice ignores the horns."],"lt":"t-w8-gray-white-matter","lid":"w8-gray-white-matter-r3","comp":186,"competencyId":"w8-cord-internal"},{"id":"L04617","dok":3,"q":"The spinal cord relays signals between the brain and the body and also serves as a reflex center. Which statement explains how its gray and white matter divide these two jobs?","a":"The central gray processes reflexes, while the outer white tracts carry signals up and down.","options":["Outer white holds the motor neuron cell bodies; central gray holds only sensory axons","Central gray carries signals to and from the brain; outer white integrates reflexes","Central gray makes cerebrospinal fluid; outer white tracts absorb it back into the blood","Central gray integrates reflexes; outer white tracts carry signals to and from the brain"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The gray matter in the center of the cord holds cell bodies, interneurons, and synapses, so it is where signals are integrated, meaning combined and processed into a response, as in a spinal reflex. The white matter around it is made of myelinated axons organized into ascending tracts that carry sensory signals up to the brain and descending tracts that carry motor commands down. Processing happens in the gray core and long-distance conduction happens in the white shell.","notes":["Motor neuron cell bodies sit in the anterior horn of the gray matter, not in the white matter. This mix-up confuses where cell bodies live with where axons run.","This reverses the two tissues. Gray matter holds the synapses where processing happens, while the white matter tracts carry signals over distance.","Cerebrospinal fluid is made mainly by the choroid plexuses in the ventricles of the brain, not by the cord's gray matter, and the white matter does not absorb it. Pick the choroid plexus for a question about where cerebrospinal fluid is produced.","Correct. Gray matter processes, and white matter tracts conduct up and down the cord."],"lt":"t-w8-gray-white-matter","lid":"w8-gray-white-matter-r5","comp":180,"competencyId":"w8-gray-white-matter"},{"id":"L04618","dok":1,"q":"Which list gives the five components of a reflex arc in order?","a":"Receptor, sensory neuron, integration center, motor neuron, effector.","options":["Receptor, sensory neuron, integration center, motor neuron, effector","Effector, motor neuron, integration center, sensory neuron, receptor","Receptor, integration center, sensory neuron, motor neuron, effector","Receptor, motor neuron, integration center, sensory neuron, effector"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"A reflex arc is a short, fixed pathway, which is why reflexes are fast. The receptor detects the stimulus, and the sensory neuron carries the signal to the CNS. The integration center, one or more synapses in the CNS, processes the signal. The motor neuron then carries the command out to the effector, the muscle or gland that carries out the response.","notes":["Correct. Signal in, processing in the CNS, command out to the effector.","This is the whole arc written backward. A reflex starts with a stimulus at the receptor and ends with the effector's response, not the other way around.","The integration center sits in the CNS, so the signal needs the sensory neuron to get there first. This order puts processing before the signal has even reached the cord.","This order swaps the two neurons. The sensory neuron must come first to carry the signal in from the receptor, and the motor neuron comes after integration to carry the command out."],"lt":"t-w8-reflex-arc","lid":"w8-reflex-arc-r1","comp":181,"competencyId":"w8-reflex-arc"},{"id":"L04619","dok":1,"q":"What is a reflex?","a":"A fast, automatic, predictable response to a stimulus.","options":["A conscious decision made in the cerebral cortex","A signal that travels only along sensory neurons","A slow, voluntary response that must be learned by practice","A fast, automatic, predictable response to a stimulus"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"A reflex is a fast, automatic, predictable response to a stimulus. It is fast because it runs along a short, fixed pathway, the reflex arc, and it is predictable because the same stimulus produces the same response each time. Pulling your hand away from a hot stove and the knee-jerk at the doctor's office are familiar examples.","notes":["A conscious decision in the cerebral cortex is a voluntary action, which takes longer than a reflex. Many reflexes are completed in the spinal cord before the brain is even aware of the stimulus.","A reflex uses both a sensory neuron to carry the signal in and a motor neuron to carry the command out. A signal that only travels inward would produce a sensation, not a response.","Reflexes are fast and automatic rather than slow and voluntary. Some reflexes are learned through repetition, such as catching a dropped object, but even those run automatically once learned.","Correct. Fast, automatic, and predictable are the defining features of a reflex."],"lt":"t-w8-reflex-arc","lid":"w8-reflex-arc-r2","comp":181,"competencyId":"w8-reflex-arc"},{"id":"L04620","dok":1,"q":"In a reflex arc, what is the effector?","a":"The muscle or gland that carries out the response.","options":["The muscle or gland that responds","The neuron that carries the command out of the CNS","The CNS synapse where the signal is processed","The receptor that first detects the stimulus"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The effector is the last link of the reflex arc: the muscle or gland that carries out the response. In the knee-jerk reflex the effector is the thigh muscle that kicks the leg forward. In an autonomic reflex the effector is smooth muscle, cardiac muscle, or a gland, such as the smooth muscle of the iris in the pupillary light reflex.","notes":["Correct. The effector is the muscle or gland that produces the response.","The neuron that carries the command out of the CNS is the motor neuron, the link just before the effector. Choose it if the question asked which neuron delivers the command.","The synapses where the signal is processed in the CNS form the integration center. This would be the answer if the question asked where the reflex is processed.","The receptor is the first link of the arc, the structure that detects the stimulus. Pick it if the question asked which component starts the reflex."],"lt":"t-w8-reflex-arc","lid":"w8-reflex-arc-r3","comp":181,"competencyId":"w8-reflex-arc"},{"id":"L04621","dok":2,"q":"Why can a spinal reflex such as the knee-jerk happen without any input from the brain?","a":"Its integration center is in the spinal cord, so the arc is completed there.","options":["Its effector is a gland that acts on its own","Its receptors send signals straight to the muscle","Its integration center is in the spinal cord","Its sensory neuron is also its motor neuron"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"In a spinal reflex, the sensory neuron enters the cord and synapses in the gray matter, either directly on a motor neuron or through interneurons. The cord itself is the integration center, so the command goes back out without waiting for the brain. The brain may become aware of the event afterward through ascending tracts, but it is not needed to produce the response.","notes":["The effector of the knee-jerk is skeletal muscle, not a gland, and no effector acts without a command from a motor neuron. A gland would be the effector in an autonomic reflex such as salivation.","Receptors cannot signal a muscle directly. The signal must travel along a sensory neuron to the CNS and back out along a motor neuron, even in the fastest reflex.","Correct. The cord's gray matter processes the signal, so the brain is not needed.","The sensory and motor neurons are separate cells. The sensory neuron carries the signal into the cord, and a different neuron, the motor neuron, carries the command out to the muscle."],"lt":"t-w8-reflex-arc","lid":"w8-reflex-arc-r4","comp":181,"competencyId":"w8-reflex-arc"},{"id":"L04622","dok":3,"q":"A clinician taps just below a patient's kneecap and the leg kicks. What does this single test check, and what would a missing response suggest?","a":"It checks the whole reflex arc; a missing reflex points to a break somewhere along that pathway.","options":["The whole reflex arc; a break somewhere along that pathway","Only the brain's motor cortex; a stroke on the same side","Only the thigh muscle; weakness in that muscle alone","Only the receptor; loss of skin sensation over the knee"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The patellar, or knee-jerk, reflex is a somatic stretch reflex. Tapping the tendon below the kneecap stretches the thigh muscle, receptors in the muscle send signals along sensory neurons into the cord, and motor neurons carry the command back to make the muscle contract. Because the test depends on every link (receptor, sensory neuron, spinal cord, motor neuron, and effector), a missing reflex means the pathway is broken somewhere along the way.","notes":["Correct. The test runs the entire arc, so a missing response points to a break anywhere along it.","The knee-jerk is completed in the spinal cord and does not need the motor cortex. A stroke is tested by voluntary movement and strength, and it would affect the opposite side of the body, not the same side.","The muscle is only the effector, the last link of the arc. A weak muscle could reduce the kick, but a missing reflex could just as easily come from a damaged nerve or cord segment.","The receptors for this reflex sense muscle stretch, not touch on the skin, and they are only the first link. Skin sensation would be tested by touching the skin, not by tapping the tendon."],"lt":"t-w8-reflex-arc","lid":"w8-reflex-arc-r5","comp":181,"competencyId":"w8-reflex-arc"},{"id":"L04623","dok":1,"q":"Where does the spinal cord begin and end in an adult?","a":"It runs from the medulla oblongata down the vertebral canal and ends at the conus medullaris near the L1 to L2 disc.","options":["From the medulla oblongata to the L1 to L2 disc","From the pons to the second sacral vertebra","From the medulla oblongata to the L4 to L5 disc","From the medulla oblongata to the tip of the coccyx"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The spinal cord is continuous with the medulla oblongata at the foramen magnum and runs down the vertebral canal. In an adult it is about 42 to 45 cm (16 to 18 inches) long and ends at the conus medullaris, a tapered tip near the disc between L1 and L2. Below that point the canal holds only the cauda equina, the bundle of nerve roots that continues downward.","notes":["Correct. The cord starts at the medulla and ends near the L1 to L2 disc.","The pons sits above the medulla in the brainstem, so the cord does not begin there, and the cord ends far above the sacrum. This choice puts both ends of the cord in the wrong place.","The L4 to L5 level is well below the end of the cord. It is a common site for a lumbar puncture needle, because only the cauda equina lies there and the cord is safely out of reach.","The coccyx is where the filum terminale, a thread of pia mater, anchors the cord. Pick the coccyx if the question asked where the filum terminale attaches, not where the cord ends."],"lt":"t-w8-cord-external","lid":"w8-cord-external-r1","comp":184,"competencyId":"w8-cord-external"},{"id":"L04624","dok":1,"q":"Which two regions of the spinal cord are visibly enlarged?","a":"The cervical enlargement and the lumbar enlargement.","options":["Cervical and lumbar enlargements","Thoracic and sacral enlargements","Lumbar and coccygeal enlargements","Cervical and thoracic enlargements"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The cord widens in two places where the nerves to the limbs arise. The cervical enlargement, from C4 to T1, holds the extra neurons that serve the upper limbs. The lumbar enlargement, behind the lower thoracic vertebrae (about T9 to T12), holds the extra neurons that serve the lower limbs. More limb muscle and skin to serve means more neurons, and that extra gray matter makes the cord wider.","notes":["Correct. The cervical and lumbar enlargements serve the upper and lower limbs.","Neither region is enlarged. The thoracic cord serves the trunk rather than a limb, so it is narrow, and the sacral segments are relatively small. Pick thoracic if the question asked which region of the cord is narrow between the two enlargements.","The lumbar enlargement is real, but the coccygeal segment at the very end of the cord is tiny, not enlarged. This choice gets one of the two right.","The cervical enlargement is real, but the thoracic cord is narrow, not an enlargement. This choice gets one of the two right."],"lt":"t-w8-cord-external","lid":"w8-cord-external-r2","comp":184,"competencyId":"w8-cord-external"},{"id":"L04625","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly describes the anterior median fissure and the posterior median sulcus?","a":"They are the deep front groove and the shallow back groove that divide the cord into right and left halves.","options":["Shallow front groove and deep back groove; they split the cord into right and left","Deep front groove and shallow back groove; they split gray matter from white matter","Deep front groove and shallow back groove; they split the cord into right and left","Two gaps between vertebrae where the spinal nerves leave the vertebral canal"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Two grooves run the full length of the spinal cord. The anterior median fissure is the deep groove down the front, and the posterior median sulcus is the shallow groove down the back. Together they divide the cord into right and left halves. A fissure is a deep cleft and a sulcus is a shallower groove, which helps you remember which is which.","notes":["This reverses the depths. The anterior groove is the deep fissure, and the posterior groove is the shallower sulcus.","The grooves are described correctly, but they divide the cord into right and left halves, not gray from white. Gray matter is the inner butterfly and white matter surrounds it, with no groove between them.","Correct. The deep fissure is in front and the shallow sulcus is in back, and together they split the cord into halves.","The gaps between vertebrae where spinal nerves exit are the intervertebral foramina. Pick these if the question asked where a spinal nerve leaves the vertebral canal."],"lt":"t-w8-cord-external","lid":"w8-cord-external-r4","comp":184,"competencyId":"w8-cord-external"},{"id":"L04626","dok":3,"q":"Why does the adult spinal cord end near L1 to L2 even though the vertebral column runs much lower?","a":"The cord stops lengthening around age 4 to 5, while the vertebral column keeps growing.","options":["The cord stops growing at about age 4 or 5, but the column keeps growing","The lower part of the cord breaks down into the cauda equina during puberty","The column stops growing in childhood while the cord keeps shrinking","The filum terminale pulls the cord upward a little more each year"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The spinal cord stops lengthening at about 4 to 5 years of age, while the vertebral column keeps growing into adulthood. As a result, the end of the cord sits higher and higher relative to the vertebrae, finishing near the L1 to L2 disc. The nerve roots for the lower segments must run down the canal to reach their exits, forming the cauda equina. This is why a lumbar puncture needle can be placed below L2 without hitting the cord.","notes":["Correct. The cord stops lengthening in early childhood while the column keeps growing.","The cauda equina is the bundle of nerve roots below the conus medullaris, not a broken-down piece of cord. It forms because those roots must travel down to reach their lower exit points.","This reverses the growth story. It is the column that keeps growing and the cord that stops, and the cord does not shrink.","The filum terminale is a thread of pia mater that anchors the cord to the coccyx; it does not pull the cord upward. Pick it if the question asked what anchors the lower end of the cord."],"lt":"t-w8-cord-external","lid":"w8-cord-external-r5","comp":184,"competencyId":"w8-cord-external"},{"id":"L04627","dok":1,"q":"What are the two roots that connect each spinal nerve to the spinal cord?","a":"The posterior (dorsal) sensory root and the anterior (ventral) motor root.","options":["The posterior sensory root and the anterior motor root","The posterior ramus and the anterior ramus","The posterior motor root and the anterior sensory root","The medial sensory root and the lateral motor root"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Each spinal nerve connects to the cord by two roots. The posterior (dorsal) root is the sensory root, bringing sensory axons into the cord, and it carries a swelling called the posterior root ganglion. The anterior (ventral) root is the motor root, carrying motor axons out of the cord. The two roots join just past the cord to form one mixed spinal nerve.","notes":["Correct. The posterior root is sensory and the anterior root is motor.","The posterior and anterior rami are branches of the spinal nerve after it leaves the intervertebral foramen, not roots attached to the cord. Pick the rami if the question asked how a spinal nerve divides after it exits.","This swaps the roles. Posterior is sensory in and anterior is motor out, and only the posterior root has a ganglion.","Spinal nerve roots attach to the front and back of the cord, not its medial and lateral sides. This choice gets the functions right but uses the wrong directional terms."],"lt":"t-w8-cord-roots","lid":"w8-cord-roots-r1","comp":185,"competencyId":"w8-cord-roots"},{"id":"L04631","dok":3,"q":"Shingles causes a painful rash along one strip of skin on one side of the body. Where does the virus hide, and why does the rash follow a single strip?","a":"In a posterior root ganglion, which holds the sensory cell bodies for one segment, so the rash follows that segment's skin.","options":["Posterior root ganglion; it holds one segment's sensory cell bodies","Anterior horn; it holds motor cell bodies for one segment","Posterior root ganglion; it holds motor cell bodies for one limb","Sympathetic trunk ganglia; they hold sensory cell bodies for the trunk"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The chickenpox virus can hide for decades in the posterior root ganglia. Each ganglion holds the sensory neuron cell bodies for one spinal segment, and their axons serve one strip of skin, called a dermatome. When the virus reactivates as shingles, it travels out along those sensory axons, so the rash and pain follow that one strip on one side of the body.","notes":["Correct. The virus hides in a posterior root ganglion, which serves one segment's strip of skin.","The anterior horn holds somatic motor neuron cell bodies inside the cord, and it does not serve skin. Pick the anterior horn if the question asked where the cell bodies that drive skeletal muscle sit.","The location is right, but the posterior root ganglion holds sensory cell bodies, not motor ones, and it serves one segment rather than a whole limb.","The sympathetic trunk ganglia hold autonomic motor cell bodies, not the sensory cell bodies that serve the skin. They would be the answer for a question about the chain of ganglia beside the vertebral column."],"lt":"t-w8-cord-roots","lid":"w8-cord-roots-r5","comp":185,"competencyId":"w8-cord-roots"},{"id":"L04632","dok":1,"q":"Which list names the three gray horns of the spinal cord?","a":"The posterior (dorsal), lateral, and anterior (ventral) horns.","options":["Medial, intermediate, and lateral horns","Superior, middle, and inferior horns","Cervical, thoracic, and lumbar horns","Posterior, lateral, and anterior horns"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The butterfly of gray matter projects into horns. The posterior (dorsal) horn holds interneurons and the incoming sensory axons, the anterior (ventral) horn holds the somatic motor nuclei that drive skeletal muscle, and the small lateral horn holds autonomic motor nuclei. The lateral horn appears only in the thoracic and upper lumbar segments.","notes":["The horns are named posterior, lateral, and anterior. Medial and intermediate are not names of gray horns, so this choice only gets the lateral horn right.","Superior and inferior describe positions along the length of the cord, but the horns are seen in cross-section, pointing front, back, and sideways. This choice uses directional terms for the wrong plane.","Cervical, thoracic, and lumbar are regions of the cord, not horns. Pick these if the question asked how the cord is divided along its length.","Correct. The posterior, lateral, and anterior horns make up the gray matter."],"lt":"t-w8-cord-internal","lid":"w8-cord-internal-r1","comp":186,"competencyId":"w8-cord-internal"},{"id":"L04633","dok":1,"q":"What does the anterior (ventral) gray horn of the spinal cord contain?","a":"Somatic motor nuclei that drive skeletal muscle.","options":["Autonomic motor nuclei for smooth muscle and glands","Somatic motor nuclei for skeletal muscle","Interneurons and incoming sensory axons","Ascending tracts that carry sensation upward"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The anterior horn holds the somatic motor nuclei, clusters of motor neuron cell bodies whose axons leave through the anterior root to drive skeletal muscle contraction. Remember the pattern of the cord: posterior is sensory in, anterior is motor out. Damage to the anterior horn cells causes weakness of the muscles that segment controls.","notes":["Autonomic motor nuclei sit in the lateral horn, present in the thoracic and upper lumbar segments. This would be the answer for a question about the lateral horn.","Correct. The anterior horn holds the somatic motor nuclei.","Interneurons and incoming sensory axons are found in the posterior (dorsal) horn. Pick this if the question asked what the posterior horn contains.","Ascending tracts are bundles of myelinated axons in the white matter, not part of the gray horns. Choose this for a question about the white columns of the cord."],"lt":"t-w8-cord-internal","lid":"w8-cord-internal-r2","comp":186,"competencyId":"w8-cord-internal"},{"id":"L04634","dok":1,"q":"In which segments of the spinal cord is a lateral gray horn present?","a":"Only in the thoracic and upper lumbar segments.","options":["Sacral and coccygeal segments only","Thoracic and upper lumbar segments","Every segment of the cord","Cervical segments only"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The lateral horn holds autonomic motor nuclei, specifically the cell bodies of sympathetic preganglionic neurons. The sympathetic division leaves the cord from T1 to L2, so the lateral horn appears only in the thoracic and upper lumbar segments. Its presence is one way to recognize a thoracic section of the cord.","notes":["The sacral segments do carry parasympathetic outflow from S2 to S4, but they do not form a lateral horn, and the coccygeal segment is the smallest part of the cord. This mix-up comes from linking any autonomic outflow with a lateral horn.","Correct. The lateral horn appears only where the sympathetic division leaves the cord, from T1 to L2.","Most cord segments have only posterior and anterior horns. The lateral horn is limited to the region where sympathetic fibers leave the cord.","The cervical segments have large anterior horns for the upper limbs but no lateral horn. This choice may come from linking the cervical enlargement with extra gray matter."],"lt":"t-w8-cord-internal","lid":"w8-cord-internal-r3","comp":186,"competencyId":"w8-cord-internal"},{"id":"L04635","dok":2,"q":"What is the central canal of the spinal cord, and what is it continuous with?","a":"The small cerebrospinal fluid channel at the center of the cord, continuous with the fourth ventricle.","options":["A CSF channel in the gray commissure, continuous with the third ventricle","A CSF channel in the gray commissure, continuous with the fourth ventricle","A blood channel in the white matter, continuous with the vertebral arteries","A CSF channel in the white matter, continuous with the lateral ventricles"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The central canal is a tiny channel of cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) running down the center of the cord, surrounded by the gray commissure, the bar of gray matter that joins the two sides. It is lined by ependymal cells. At its upper end it opens into the fourth ventricle of the brainstem, the ventricle closest to the cord.","notes":["The third ventricle sits in the diencephalon, higher up, and connects to the fourth ventricle through the cerebral aqueduct. The canal joins the fourth ventricle, the one directly above the cord.","Correct. The central canal is a CSF channel continuous with the fourth ventricle.","The central canal carries CSF, not blood, and it sits in the gray commissure at the center of the cord. Pick the vertebral arteries for a question about the vessels that join to form the basilar artery.","The central canal sits in the gray commissure, not the white matter, and the lateral ventricles are deep within the cerebral hemispheres, far from the cord. Pick the lateral ventricles if the question asked which ventricles lie in the cerebrum."],"lt":"t-w8-cord-internal","lid":"w8-cord-internal-r4","comp":186,"competencyId":"w8-cord-internal"},{"id":"L04636","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares the ascending and descending tracts of the spinal cord?","a":"Ascending (sensory) tracts carry signals up to the brain; descending (motor) tracts carry commands down from the brain.","options":["Ascending tracts carry sensory signals up; descending tracts carry motor commands down","Ascending tracts carry motor commands up; descending tracts carry sensory signals down","Ascending tracts leave the cord as nerves; descending tracts stay within the cord","Ascending tracts run in the gray horns; descending tracts run in the white columns"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Tracts are bundles of axons in the white columns of the cord that share a common origin and destination. Ascending tracts are sensory and carry impulses up toward the brain. Descending tracts are motor and carry commands down from the brain to motor neurons in the cord. A spinal cord injury cuts both kinds, which is why sensation and movement are lost below the level of injury.","notes":["Correct. Ascending tracts are sensory and go up; descending tracts are motor and go down.","This swaps the roles. Sensation travels up to the brain, and motor commands come down from it.","Tracts are CNS structures by definition, so neither kind leaves the cord as a nerve. Axons that leave the cord do so through the anterior roots, which are part of the PNS.","Both kinds of tracts run in the white columns. The gray horns hold cell bodies and interneurons, not long tracts."],"lt":"t-w8-cord-internal","lid":"w8-cord-internal-r5","comp":183,"competencyId":"w8-neuronal-pools"},{"id":"L04673","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares a somatic reflex and an autonomic reflex?","a":"A somatic reflex acts on skeletal muscle (like the knee-jerk); an autonomic reflex acts on smooth muscle, cardiac muscle, or a gland (like the pupillary light reflex).","options":["Somatic: skeletal muscle effector. Autonomic: smooth or cardiac muscle, or a gland","Somatic reflexes are all learned; autonomic reflexes are all present from birth","Somatic reflexes have one synapse; autonomic reflexes have two or more synapses","Somatic: smooth muscle or gland effector. Autonomic: skeletal muscle effector"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Somatic and autonomic reflexes are named for their effector. A somatic reflex acts on skeletal muscle, as in the patellar (knee-jerk) reflex. An autonomic reflex acts on smooth muscle, cardiac muscle, or a gland, as in the pupillary light reflex, where the pupil narrows in bright light. Other reflex names are based on different features, such as the number of synapses or whether the reflex is inborn.","notes":["Correct. Somatic reflexes act on skeletal muscle, and autonomic reflexes act on smooth muscle, cardiac muscle, or glands.","Learned versus innate is a separate way of naming reflexes. The knee-jerk is a somatic reflex present from birth, and catching a dropped object is a learned somatic reflex, so somatic reflexes are not all learned.","One synapse versus several is the monosynaptic versus polysynaptic split, which cuts across both groups. The withdrawal reflex, for example, is somatic and polysynaptic.","This swaps the effectors. Skeletal muscle belongs to the somatic system, while smooth muscle and glands belong to the autonomic system."],"lt":"t-w8-reflex-types","lid":"w8-reflex-types-r1","comp":182,"competencyId":"w8-reflex-types"},{"id":"N-a17d-182-1","dok":1,"q":"What is another name for an autonomic reflex?","a":"A visceral reflex.","options":["A somatic reflex","A stretch reflex","A visceral reflex","An acquired reflex"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Reflexes sorted by their effector are either somatic or autonomic. A somatic reflex acts on skeletal muscle, while an autonomic reflex, also called a visceral reflex, acts on smooth muscle, cardiac muscle, or a gland. The pupillary light reflex, which constricts the pupil using the smooth muscle of the iris, is the course example.","notes":["A somatic reflex is the other half of the pair, with skeletal muscle as its effector, as in the patellar reflex.","A stretch reflex, such as the patellar reflex, is the course example of a monosynaptic reflex. It acts on skeletal muscle, so it is somatic.","Correct. An autonomic reflex is also called a visceral reflex, because its effector is smooth muscle, cardiac muscle, or a gland.","Acquired reflex is another name for a learned reflex, one built through practice. That name sorts reflexes by whether they are inborn, not by effector."],"comp":182,"competencyId":"w8-reflex-types"}]},{"id":"m5-cranial-nerves","title":"The cranial nerves","summary":"names, numbers, sensory, motor or mixed, where each exits and what region it serves","mw":"2026-12-02","tr":"2026-12-03","cards":[{"id":"L02970","dok":1,"q":"How many pairs of cranial nerves arise from the brain?","a":"Twelve pairs.","options":["Eight pairs","Thirty-one pairs","Twelve pairs","Ten pairs"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Twelve pairs of cranial nerves arise from the brain and brainstem. They are numbered with Roman numerals I to XII, from front to back, by where they attach. Even though they connect to the brain, they belong to the peripheral nervous system because they run out to the head, neck, and trunk.","notes":["Eight is the number of cervical spinal nerve pairs. Pick it if the question asked how many spinal nerves arise in the neck region.","Thirty-one is the number of spinal nerve pairs, which arise from the spinal cord rather than the brain. Pick it if the question asked how many spinal nerves there are.","Correct. The cranial nerves are numbered I through XII, so there are twelve pairs.","Ten counts only cranial nerves III through XII, the nerves below the forebrain. It leaves out the olfactory and optic nerves, which attach above the brainstem, so it falls short of the total."],"lt":"t-pns","lid":"c1","comp":190,"competencyId":"w8-cranial-nerves"},{"id":"L02974","dok":1,"q":"Cranial nerve I is which nerve?","a":"The olfactory nerve.","options":["The oculomotor nerve","The trigeminal nerve","The olfactory nerve","The optic nerve"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Cranial nerve I is the olfactory nerve, the nerve of smell. Its fibers begin in the olfactory epithelium high in the nasal cavity and pass up through the cribriform plate of the ethmoid bone to the olfactory bulb. It is a sensory nerve and one of only two cranial nerves that attach above the brainstem.","notes":["The oculomotor nerve is cranial nerve III and moves most of the eye muscles, raises the eyelid, and constricts the pupil. Its name starts like olfactory, which is why students mix them up.","The trigeminal nerve is cranial nerve V, the largest cranial nerve, carrying facial sensation and serving the chewing muscles. Pick it for the fifth cranial nerve.","Correct. The olfactory nerve is cranial nerve I and carries smell.","The optic nerve is cranial nerve II and carries vision from the retina. Pick it for the second cranial nerve."],"lt":"t-pns","lid":"c5","comp":190,"competencyId":"w8-cranial-nerves"},{"id":"L02975","dok":1,"q":"Cranial nerve II is which nerve?","a":"The optic nerve.","options":["The abducens nerve","The oculomotor nerve","The optic nerve","The olfactory nerve"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Cranial nerve II is the optic nerve, which carries visual information from the retina to the brain. It leaves the orbit through the optic canal of the sphenoid bone, and the two optic nerves meet at the optic chiasm. Like the olfactory nerve, it is purely sensory and attaches above the brainstem.","notes":["The abducens nerve is cranial nerve VI, a motor nerve that turns the eye outward. It works on the eye but carries no vision, so it is not cranial nerve II.","The oculomotor nerve is cranial nerve III. It moves the eye rather than seeing with it, so pick it for most eye movements, lifting the eyelid, and constricting the pupil.","Correct. The optic nerve is cranial nerve II and carries vision.","The olfactory nerve is cranial nerve I, the nerve of smell. Pick it for the first cranial nerve."],"lt":"t-pns","lid":"c6","comp":190,"competencyId":"w8-cranial-nerves"},{"id":"L02976","dok":1,"q":"What is the number of the vagus nerve among the cranial nerves?","a":"Cranial nerve X.","options":["Cranial nerve XI","Cranial nerve X","Cranial nerve VIII","Cranial nerve IX"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The vagus nerve is cranial nerve X. Its name means wanderer, because it is the only cranial nerve that travels well past the head and neck into the chest and abdomen. It is a mixed nerve that leaves the skull through the jugular foramen and carries most of the parasympathetic supply to the thoracic and abdominal organs.","notes":["Cranial nerve XI is the accessory nerve, a motor nerve to the sternocleidomastoid and trapezius. It also leaves through the jugular foramen, right beside the vagus.","Correct. The vagus nerve is cranial nerve X.","Cranial nerve VIII is the vestibulocochlear nerve, the sensory nerve for hearing and balance. Pick it for the nerve that enters through the internal acoustic meatus.","Cranial nerve IX is the glossopharyngeal nerve, which carries taste from the back of the tongue and helps with swallowing. It shares the jugular foramen with the vagus, which makes the two easy to confuse."],"lt":"t-pns","lid":"c7","comp":190,"competencyId":"w8-cranial-nerves"},{"id":"L02982","dok":1,"q":"Cranial nerve VIII, the vestibulocochlear nerve, is associated with which special senses?","a":"Hearing and balance.","options":["Hearing and balance","Smell and taste","Vision and eye movement","Facial sensation and chewing"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The vestibulocochlear nerve is a sensory nerve with two parts named for where they start. The cochlear part carries hearing from the cochlea, and the vestibular part carries balance information from the vestibule and semicircular canals of the inner ear. It enters the skull through the internal acoustic meatus alongside the facial nerve.","notes":["Correct. The cochlear part serves hearing and the vestibular part serves balance.","Smell is carried by the olfactory nerve (I), and taste by the facial and glossopharyngeal nerves (VII and IX). No single nerve does both.","Vision is carried by the optic nerve (II), and eye movement by the oculomotor, trochlear, and abducens nerves (III, IV, VI). Pick this pair for those nerves.","Facial sensation and chewing belong to the trigeminal nerve (V). Pick this pair if the question asked about cranial nerve V."],"lt":"t-pns","lid":"c13","comp":190,"competencyId":"w8-cranial-nerves"},{"id":"L02989","dok":2,"q":"Which cranial nerve carries most of the parasympathetic supply to the organs of the chest and abdomen?","a":"The vagus nerve.","options":["The facial nerve","The trigeminal nerve","The vagus nerve","The accessory nerve"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The vagus nerve, cranial nerve X, carries most of the parasympathetic supply to the organs of the chest and abdomen. It leaves the skull through the jugular foramen and travels down the neck into the thorax and abdomen, reaching the heart, the lungs, and much of the digestive tract. This long reach is why its name means wanderer.","notes":["The facial nerve (VII) does carry parasympathetic fibers, but only to glands of the head such as the tear glands and some salivary glands. Pick it for tear or saliva production.","The trigeminal nerve (V) carries sensation from the face and serves the chewing muscles. It is not one of the four cranial nerves that carry parasympathetic fibers (III, VII, IX, and X).","Correct. The vagus nerve carries most of the parasympathetic supply to the thoracic and abdominal organs.","The accessory nerve (XI) is a motor nerve to the sternocleidomastoid and trapezius. It leaves through the jugular foramen right beside the vagus, which is why students confuse them."],"lt":"t-pns","lid":"c20","comp":190,"competencyId":"w8-cranial-nerves"},{"id":"L02991","dok":2,"q":"Cranial nerve V, the trigeminal nerve, has which two main functions?","a":"Facial sensation and chewing.","options":["Tongue movement and swallowing","Hearing and balance","Smell and vision","Facial sensation and chewing"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The trigeminal nerve is a mixed nerve and the largest cranial nerve. Its sensory fibers carry touch, pain, and temperature from the face through three branches (ophthalmic, maxillary, and mandibular), and its motor fibers travel with the mandibular branch to the muscles of chewing. The name comes from a Latin word for triplets, a reminder of those three branches.","notes":["Tongue movement belongs to the hypoglossal nerve (XII), and swallowing to the glossopharyngeal and vagus nerves (IX and X). Pick this pair for nerves of the lower brainstem.","Hearing and balance belong to the vestibulocochlear nerve (VIII). Pick this pair for that nerve.","Smell and vision belong to the olfactory (I) and optic (II) nerves, the two purely sensory nerves at the front of the list.","Correct. The trigeminal nerve carries facial sensation and serves the chewing muscles."],"lt":"t-pns","lid":"c22","comp":190,"competencyId":"w8-cranial-nerves"},{"id":"L02992","dok":2,"q":"Which cranial nerve moves the tongue?","a":"The hypoglossal nerve.","options":["The facial nerve","The hypoglossal nerve","The accessory nerve","The glossopharyngeal nerve"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The hypoglossal nerve, cranial nerve XII, is a motor nerve that moves the tongue. Its name means under the tongue, which describes its path, and it leaves the skull through the hypoglossal canal of the occipital bone. It is the last of the twelve cranial nerves.","notes":["The facial nerve (VII) controls the muscles of facial expression and carries taste from the front of the tongue. Pick it for taste on the front of the tongue, not tongue movement.","Correct. The hypoglossal nerve moves the tongue.","The accessory nerve (XI) is a motor nerve to the sternocleidomastoid and trapezius. Pick it for turning the head and shrugging the shoulders.","The glossopharyngeal nerve (IX) carries taste and sensation from the back of the tongue and helps with swallowing, but it does not move the tongue. Its glosso- root (tongue) is what makes it tempting."],"lt":"t-pns","lid":"c23","comp":190,"competencyId":"w8-cranial-nerves"},{"id":"L02994","dok":2,"q":"Which motor cranial nerve leaves the skull through the jugular foramen and serves muscles of the neck and shoulder?","a":"The accessory nerve.","options":["The vagus nerve","The accessory nerve","The hypoglossal nerve","The glossopharyngeal nerve"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The accessory nerve, cranial nerve XI, is a motor nerve to two large muscles of the neck and shoulder, the sternocleidomastoid and the trapezius. Most of its fibers start in the upper cervical spinal cord, rise into the skull through the foramen magnum, and then leave through the jugular foramen. Weakness turning the head or shrugging the shoulders points to this nerve.","notes":["The vagus nerve (X) also exits the jugular foramen, but it is a mixed nerve serving the organs of the chest and abdomen, the voice, and swallowing. Pick it for parasympathetic supply to the viscera.","Correct. The accessory nerve is a motor nerve that exits the jugular foramen and serves neck and shoulder muscles.","The hypoglossal nerve (XII) is a motor nerve too, but it leaves through the hypoglossal canal and moves the tongue. Pick it for tongue movement.","The glossopharyngeal nerve (IX) shares the jugular foramen, but it is mixed and serves taste on the back of the tongue and swallowing. Pick it for the throat and posterior tongue."],"lt":"t-pns","lid":"c25","comp":190,"competencyId":"w8-cranial-nerves"},{"id":"L02998","dok":2,"q":"Through which opening does the hypoglossal nerve leave the skull?","a":"The hypoglossal canal.","options":["The jugular foramen","The stylomastoid foramen","The foramen ovale","The hypoglossal canal"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The hypoglossal nerve, cranial nerve XII, leaves the skull through the hypoglossal canal, a small tunnel in the occipital bone just above each occipital condyle. From there it runs forward beneath the tongue to reach the tongue muscles. The nerve and the canal share the same name, which makes this pairing easy to remember.","notes":["The jugular foramen carries cranial nerves IX, X, and XI. It sits right beside the hypoglossal canal, so pick it for the glossopharyngeal, vagus, or accessory nerve.","The stylomastoid foramen is where the facial nerve (VII) exits to reach the muscles of facial expression. Pick it for the facial nerve's final exit from the skull.","The foramen ovale in the sphenoid bone carries the mandibular branch of the trigeminal nerve (V3). Pick it for the nerve branch to the lower jaw and chewing muscles.","Correct. The hypoglossal nerve exits through the hypoglossal canal."],"lt":"t-pns","lid":"c29","comp":190,"competencyId":"w8-cranial-nerves"},{"id":"L03004","dok":2,"q":"Which cranial nerve controls the muscles of facial expression and carries taste from the front of the tongue?","a":"The facial nerve.","options":["The vagus nerve","The glossopharyngeal nerve","The facial nerve","The trigeminal nerve"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The facial nerve, cranial nerve VII, is a mixed nerve. Its motor fibers control the muscles of facial expression, its sensory fibers carry taste from the front of the tongue, and its parasympathetic fibers drive tear and saliva production. It enters the internal acoustic meatus and exits to the face through the stylomastoid foramen.","notes":["The vagus nerve (X) serves the organs of the chest and abdomen, the voice, and swallowing. Pick it for the parasympathetic supply to the heart and gut.","The glossopharyngeal nerve (IX) carries taste from the back of the tongue and helps with swallowing. Pick it for the posterior tongue and throat.","Correct. The facial nerve moves the face and carries taste from the front of the tongue.","The trigeminal nerve (V) carries sensation from the face and serves the chewing muscles. Students confuse feeling the face with moving the face; pick the trigeminal nerve for facial touch and pain."],"lt":"t-pns","lid":"c35","comp":190,"competencyId":"w8-cranial-nerves"},{"id":"L03008","dok":2,"q":"Which cranial nerve is classified as a mixed nerve?","a":"The trigeminal nerve.","options":["The trigeminal nerve","The vestibulocochlear nerve","The hypoglossal nerve","The optic nerve"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The trigeminal nerve (V) is a mixed nerve, carrying sensory axons from the face and motor axons to the chewing muscles. Only four cranial nerves are mixed: V, VII, IX, and X. The rest carry only sensory signals (I, II, and VIII) or mainly motor signals (III, IV, VI, XI, and XII).","notes":["Correct. The trigeminal nerve carries both sensory and motor axons.","The vestibulocochlear nerve (VIII) has two parts, which makes it look complex, but both parts are sensory (hearing and balance). Pick it as a sensory cranial nerve.","The hypoglossal nerve (XII) is a motor nerve that moves the tongue. Pick it as an example of a motor cranial nerve.","The optic nerve (II) is purely sensory and carries vision. Pick it as an example of a sensory cranial nerve."],"lt":"t-pns","lid":"c39","comp":190,"competencyId":"w8-cranial-nerves"},{"id":"L03014","dok":3,"q":"Which choice correctly lists cranial nerves I, II, and VIII in numerical order with the sense each one carries?","a":"Olfactory for smell, optic for vision, vestibulocochlear for hearing and balance.","options":["Optic for smell, olfactory for vision, vestibulocochlear for hearing and balance","Olfactory for smell, optic for vision, vestibulocochlear for taste and balance","Olfactory for smell, optic for vision, vestibulocochlear for hearing and balance","Olfactory for smell, oculomotor for vision, vestibulocochlear for hearing and balance"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Cranial nerve I, the olfactory nerve, carries smell; cranial nerve II, the optic nerve, carries vision; and cranial nerve VIII, the vestibulocochlear nerve, carries hearing and balance. These three are the purely sensory cranial nerves. Each name hints at its sense, since olfaction means smell and optic refers to sight.","notes":["This swaps the olfactory and optic nerves, giving each the other's sense and number. The mix-up happens because both names start with O.","Taste is not carried by cranial nerve VIII; it travels in the facial and glossopharyngeal nerves (VII and IX). The cochlear part of VIII carries hearing.","Correct. I is olfactory for smell, II is optic for vision, and VIII is vestibulocochlear for hearing and balance.","The oculomotor nerve is cranial nerve III and moves the eye; it does not carry vision. Pick oculomotor for eye movement and pupil constriction."],"lt":"t-pns","lid":"c45","comp":190,"competencyId":"w8-cranial-nerves"},{"id":"L03058","dok":1,"q":"Through which opening does the vagus nerve leave the skull?","a":"The jugular foramen.","options":["The hypoglossal canal","The jugular foramen","The foramen magnum","The internal acoustic meatus"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The vagus nerve, cranial nerve X, leaves the skull through the jugular foramen, the opening between the temporal and occipital bones where the internal jugular vein also begins. It shares this opening with the glossopharyngeal (IX) and accessory (XI) nerves. Because the three run together, a tumor or fracture at the jugular foramen can affect all three at once.","notes":["The hypoglossal canal carries the hypoglossal nerve (XII) to the tongue. Pick it for cranial nerve XII.","Correct. The vagus nerve exits through the jugular foramen.","The foramen magnum is where the brainstem joins the spinal cord. The spinal part of the accessory nerve climbs up through it, but no cranial nerve leaves the skull this way.","The internal acoustic meatus carries the facial (VII) and vestibulocochlear (VIII) nerves. Pick it for those two nerves."],"lt":"t-ans","lid":"c8","comp":190,"competencyId":"w8-cranial-nerves"},{"id":"L03116","dok":2,"q":"Which cranial nerve carries the parasympathetic fibers that constrict the pupil?","a":"The oculomotor nerve (III).","options":["The trochlear nerve (IV)","The oculomotor nerve (III)","The abducens nerve (VI)","The ophthalmic branch of the trigeminal (V1)"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The oculomotor nerve (III) carries parasympathetic fibers that relay in the ciliary ganglion and then reach the muscle that constricts the pupil. It is one of the four cranial nerves with a parasympathetic part (III, VII, IX, and X). The same nerve also moves most of the eye muscles and raises the eyelid.","notes":["The trochlear nerve (IV) is a motor nerve to the superior oblique muscle and carries no parasympathetic fibers. Pick it for that one eye muscle.","Correct. The oculomotor nerve carries the parasympathetic fibers that constrict the pupil.","The abducens nerve (VI) is a motor nerve to the lateral rectus, which turns the eye outward. Pick it for looking to the side.","The ophthalmic branch (V1) of the trigeminal nerve carries sensation from the surface of the eye, the forehead, and the nose. Pick it for touch and pain from the cornea."],"lt":"t-ans","lid":"c21-x2","comp":190,"competencyId":"w8-cranial-nerves"},{"id":"L03213","dok":2,"q":"The cranial nerves are numbered with Roman numerals I to XII. What does the numbering follow?","a":"The order in which they attach to the brain, from anterior to posterior.","options":["The order of their skull openings, lateral to medial","Where they attach, from anterior to posterior","Their class, with sensory nerves numbered first","Their size, from largest to smallest"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The cranial nerves are numbered by the order in which they attach to the brain, from front to back. The olfactory nerve (I) attaches farthest forward, under the frontal lobe, and the higher numbers attach farther back. So CN I and II, which attach above the brainstem, come first, and III through XII follow in order down the brainstem, with most of XI coming from the upper cervical spinal cord.","notes":["Skull openings do not set the numbers, and several nerves share one opening, such as IX, X, and XI in the jugular foramen. Pick the openings for a question about where a nerve exits the skull.","Correct. The numbers follow the attachment points from anterior to posterior.","The classes are mixed throughout the list. Sensory nerves are I, II, and VIII, so sensory nerves are not grouped first.","Size does not set the order. The largest nerve is the trigeminal (V) and the smallest is the trochlear (IV), which sit side by side in the numbering."],"lt":"t-cns-brainstem","lid":"d3x-t-cns-brainstem-1","comp":190,"competencyId":"w8-cranial-nerves"},{"id":"L03254","dok":1,"q":"Which cranial nerve carries the sense of smell?","a":"The olfactory nerve (CN I).","options":["The optic nerve (CN II)","The trigeminal nerve (CN V)","The facial nerve (CN VII)","The olfactory nerve (CN I)"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The olfactory nerve, CN I, is a purely sensory nerve that carries smell. Its fibers start in the olfactory epithelium at the roof of the nasal cavity, pass up through the cribriform plate of the ethmoid bone, and reach the olfactory bulb. From there the olfactory tract carries the signals back toward the cortex.","notes":["The optic nerve, CN II, is the other purely sensory nerve at the front of the list, and it carries vision. Pick it for a question about sight.","The trigeminal nerve, CN V, carries general sensation such as touch and pain from the face, including the inside of the nose, but not smell itself. It would be the answer for a question about facial sensation.","The facial nerve, CN VII, carries taste from the front of the tongue, a sense students often mix up with smell. Choose it for a question about taste or facial expression.","Correct. CN I, the olfactory nerve, carries smell."],"lt":"t-cranial-nerves","lid":"c2","comp":190,"competencyId":"w8-cranial-nerves"},{"id":"L03255","dok":1,"q":"Which cranial nerve carries vision?","a":"The optic nerve (CN II).","options":["The optic nerve (CN II)","The trochlear nerve (CN IV)","The olfactory nerve (CN I)","The oculomotor nerve (CN III)"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The optic nerve, CN II, is purely sensory and carries visual signals from the retina. It leaves the orbit through the optic canal of the sphenoid bone, and the two optic nerves meet at the optic chiasm. Several other cranial nerves move the eye, but only CN II carries what the eye sees.","notes":["Correct. CN II, the optic nerve, carries vision.","The trochlear nerve, CN IV, moves the eye but does not carry vision, and it is the smallest cranial nerve. It would be the answer for a question about the smallest cranial nerve.","The olfactory nerve, CN I, carries smell. Pick it for a question about the sense of smell.","The oculomotor nerve, CN III, moves most of the eye, raises the eyelid, and constricts the pupil, but carries no visual information. Choose it for a question about eye movement or a drooping eyelid."],"lt":"t-cranial-nerves","lid":"c3","comp":190,"competencyId":"w8-cranial-nerves"},{"id":"L03256","dok":1,"q":"Which is the largest cranial nerve?","a":"The trigeminal nerve (CN V).","options":["The vagus nerve (CN X)","The trigeminal nerve (CN V)","The optic nerve (CN II)","The facial nerve (CN VII)"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The trigeminal nerve, CN V, is the largest cranial nerve. It is mixed, carrying sensation from the face and motor fibers to the muscles of chewing. Its name means triplets, because it splits into three branches, V1, V2, and V3, and each leaves the skull through its own opening.","notes":["The vagus nerve, CN X, travels the farthest, reaching the thoracic and abdominal organs, which makes students guess it is the largest. Pick it for a question about the cranial nerve with the longest reach.","Correct. CN V, the trigeminal nerve, is the largest cranial nerve.","The optic nerve, CN II, is a thick sensory nerve for vision, but it is not the largest. Pick it for a question about vision.","The facial nerve, CN VII, is a mixed nerve for facial expression and taste, but it is not the largest. Choose it for a question about facial expression."],"lt":"t-cranial-nerves","lid":"c4","comp":190,"competencyId":"w8-cranial-nerves"},{"id":"L03257","dok":1,"q":"Which is the smallest cranial nerve?","a":"The trochlear nerve (CN IV).","options":["The hypoglossal nerve (CN XII)","The accessory nerve (CN XI)","The trochlear nerve (CN IV)","The abducens nerve (CN VI)"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The trochlear nerve, CN IV, is the smallest cranial nerve. It is purely motor, arises from a nucleus in the midbrain, and passes through the superior orbital fissure to an eye muscle. Its name comes from trochlea, meaning pulley, after the pulley-like loop its muscle's tendon runs through.","notes":["The hypoglossal nerve, CN XII, is the motor nerve of the tongue and exits through the hypoglossal canal. It would be the answer for a question about tongue movement.","The accessory nerve, CN XI, is a motor nerve to neck and shoulder muscles that exits through the jugular foramen. It is not the smallest, so choose it for a question about the purely motor nerve whose nucleus sits in the upper cervical spinal cord.","Correct. CN IV, the trochlear nerve, is the smallest cranial nerve.","The abducens nerve, CN VI, is also a small motor nerve to an eye muscle, but its nucleus is in the pons and it is not the smallest. Pick it for a question about the motor nerve from the pons that passes through the superior orbital fissure."],"lt":"t-cranial-nerves","lid":"c5","comp":190,"competencyId":"w8-cranial-nerves"},{"id":"L03258","dok":1,"q":"Which cranial nerve carries hearing and balance?","a":"The vestibulocochlear nerve (CN VIII)","options":["The trigeminal nerve (CN V)","The facial nerve (CN VII)","The vestibulocochlear nerve (CN VIII)","The glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX)"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The vestibulocochlear nerve, CN VIII, is purely sensory. Its cochlear part carries hearing from the cochlea, and its vestibular part carries balance information from the vestibule and semicircular canals. It enters the skull through the internal acoustic meatus alongside the facial nerve.","notes":["The trigeminal nerve, CN V, carries sensation from the face and controls the muscles of chewing. It would be the answer for a question about facial sensation.","The facial nerve, CN VII, shares the internal acoustic meatus with CN VIII, but it controls facial expression and carries taste from the front of the tongue. Pick it for a question about facial expression.","Correct. CN VIII, the vestibulocochlear nerve, carries hearing and balance.","The glossopharyngeal nerve, CN IX, carries taste from the back of the tongue and helps with swallowing. Choose it for a question about taste from the back of the tongue."],"lt":"t-cranial-nerves","lid":"c6","comp":190,"competencyId":"w8-cranial-nerves"},{"id":"L03259","dok":1,"q":"Which cranial nerve controls movement of the tongue?","a":"The hypoglossal nerve (CN XII).","options":["The facial nerve (CN VII)","The hypoglossal nerve (CN XII)","The accessory nerve (CN XI)","The glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX)"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The hypoglossal nerve, CN XII, is a purely motor nerve that moves the tongue. Its name means under the tongue, which is where it runs. It arises from the hypoglossal nucleus in the medulla and leaves the skull through the hypoglossal canal.","notes":["The facial nerve, CN VII, carries taste from the front of the tongue and controls facial expression. It would be the answer for a question about Bell's palsy.","Correct. CN XII, the hypoglossal nerve, moves the tongue.","The accessory nerve, CN XI, is purely motor too, but it moves neck and shoulder muscles. Pick it for a question about shrugging the shoulders or turning the head.","The glossopharyngeal nerve, CN IX, also has glosso in its name, but it carries taste from the back of the tongue and helps with swallowing rather than moving the tongue. Choose it for a question about taste from the back of the tongue."],"lt":"t-cranial-nerves","lid":"c7","comp":190,"competencyId":"w8-cranial-nerves"},{"id":"L03260","dok":1,"q":"Which cranial nerve provides autonomic control of the thoracic and abdominal organs?","a":"The vagus nerve (CN X).","options":["The accessory nerve (CN XI)","The trigeminal nerve (CN V)","The glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX)","The vagus nerve (CN X)"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The vagus nerve, CN X, is a mixed nerve whose name means wanderer. After leaving the skull through the jugular foramen, it travels down the neck into the thorax and abdomen, carrying parasympathetic fibers to the heart, lungs, and much of the digestive tract. It also serves the voice and swallowing.","notes":["The accessory nerve, CN XI, exits beside the vagus through the jugular foramen but is purely motor to neck and shoulder muscles. Pick it for a question about those muscles.","The trigeminal nerve, CN V, carries facial sensation and controls chewing, and it stays in the head. It would be the answer for a question about facial sensation.","The glossopharyngeal nerve, CN IX, also uses the jugular foramen and carries some parasympathetic fibers, but only to the parotid gland in the head. Choose it for a question about the parotid gland or taste from the back of the tongue.","Correct. CN X, the vagus nerve, provides autonomic control of the thoracic and abdominal organs."],"lt":"t-cranial-nerves","lid":"c8","comp":190,"competencyId":"w8-cranial-nerves"},{"id":"L03261","dok":1,"q":"Which three cranial nerves are purely sensory?","a":"CN I, CN II, and CN VIII.","options":["CN X, CN XI, and CN XII","CN I, CN II, and CN VIII","CN V, CN VII, and CN IX","CN III, CN IV, and CN VI"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The purely sensory cranial nerves carry special senses only: the olfactory nerve (I) for smell, the optic nerve (II) for vision, and the vestibulocochlear nerve (VIII) for hearing and balance. In the memory aid Some Say Marry Money But My Brother Says Big Brains Matter Most, the S letters fall on I, II, and VIII.","notes":["CN X is mixed, while CN XI and XII are purely motor. This set would only fit a question about the last three cranial nerves.","Correct. CN I, II, and VIII are the purely sensory cranial nerves.","CN V, VII, and IX are mixed nerves, carrying both sensory and motor fibers. Choose them, along with CN X, for a question about the mixed cranial nerves.","CN III, IV, and VI are the three motor nerves that move the eye. Pick them for a question about the nerves to the eye muscles."],"lt":"t-cranial-nerves","lid":"c9","comp":190,"competencyId":"w8-cranial-nerves"},{"id":"L03262","dok":1,"q":"Which four cranial nerves are mixed, carrying both sensory and motor signals?","a":"CN V, CN VII, CN IX, and CN X.","options":["CN I, CN II, CN III, and CN IV","CN VIII, CN X, CN XI, and CN XII","CN V, CN VII, CN IX, and CN X","CN III, CN IV, CN VI, and CN XII"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The mixed cranial nerves are the trigeminal (V), facial (VII), glossopharyngeal (IX), and vagus (X). Each serves a region where it must both sense and move something, such as the face or the throat. In the memory aid Some Say Marry Money But My Brother Says Big Brains Matter Most, the B letters fall on V, VII, IX, and X.","notes":["CN I and II are purely sensory, and CN III and IV are motor. This set is simply the first four nerves in order.","CN VIII is sensory and CN XI and XII are motor, so only CN X in this set is mixed. Choose this group only for a question about the last nerves on the list.","Correct. CN V, VII, IX, and X are the mixed cranial nerves.","CN III, IV, VI, and XII are all motor nerves. Pick them, along with CN XI, for a question about the motor cranial nerves."],"lt":"t-cranial-nerves","lid":"c10","comp":190,"competencyId":"w8-cranial-nerves"},{"id":"L03263","dok":1,"q":"Through which skull opening does the olfactory nerve (CN I) pass?","a":"The cribriform plate of the ethmoid bone.","options":["The superior orbital fissure","The jugular foramen","The cribriform plate","The optic canal"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The cribriform plate is the sieve-like horizontal part of the ethmoid bone in the roof of the nasal cavity. Many small olfactory fibers pass up through its tiny holes, the olfactory foramina, to reach the olfactory bulb lying on top of it. A fracture here can tear these fibers and cause loss of smell.","notes":["The superior orbital fissure carries CN III, IV, VI, and the V1 branch of CN V into the orbit. It would be the answer for the nerves that move the eye.","The jugular foramen carries CN IX, X, and XI. Choose it for a question about the glossopharyngeal, vagus, or accessory nerve.","Correct. CN I passes through the cribriform plate of the ethmoid bone.","The optic canal of the sphenoid bone carries the optic nerve, CN II. Pick it for a question about the path of the nerve for vision."],"lt":"t-cranial-nerves","lid":"c11","comp":190,"competencyId":"w8-cranial-nerves"},{"id":"L03264","dok":1,"q":"Through which skull opening does the optic nerve (CN II) pass?","a":"The optic canal of the sphenoid bone.","options":["The optic canal","The foramen ovale","The hypoglossal canal","The cribriform plate"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The optic canal is a short tunnel in the lesser wing of the sphenoid bone at the back of the orbit. The optic nerve passes through it from the eye to reach the optic chiasm inside the skull. Its name matches the nerve, which makes it one of the easier foramina to remember.","notes":["Correct. CN II passes through the optic canal of the sphenoid bone.","The foramen ovale of the sphenoid bone carries the V3 branch of the trigeminal nerve. Choose it for a question about the mandibular branch.","The hypoglossal canal carries the hypoglossal nerve, CN XII. It would be the answer for the nerve of the tongue.","The cribriform plate of the ethmoid bone carries the olfactory nerve, CN I. Pick it for a question about smell."],"lt":"t-cranial-nerves","lid":"c12","comp":190,"competencyId":"w8-cranial-nerves"},{"id":"L03265","dok":2,"q":"Which purely motor cranial nerve passes through the superior orbital fissure and has its nucleus in the pons?","a":"The abducens nerve (CN VI).","options":["The trigeminal nerve (CN V)","The oculomotor nerve (CN III)","The abducens nerve (CN VI)","The trochlear nerve (CN IV)"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Four nerves pass through the superior orbital fissure: CN III, CN IV, the V1 branch of CN V, and CN VI. Of these, the oculomotor and trochlear nuclei are in the midbrain, while the trigeminal and abducens nuclei are in the pons. The trigeminal nerve is mixed, so the only purely motor nerve that fits every clue is the abducens.","notes":["The trigeminal nerve has nuclei in the pons and its V1 branch uses the superior orbital fissure, but it is mixed, not purely motor. It would be the answer for a question about facial sensation.","The oculomotor nerve is purely motor and uses the superior orbital fissure, but its nucleus is in the midbrain. Pick it for a question about the nerve that raises the eyelid and constricts the pupil.","Correct. The abducens nerve is purely motor, uses the superior orbital fissure, and arises from the pons.","The trochlear nerve is purely motor and uses the superior orbital fissure, but its nucleus is also in the midbrain. Choose it for a question about the smallest cranial nerve."],"lt":"t-cranial-nerves","lid":"c13","comp":190,"competencyId":"w8-cranial-nerves"},{"id":"L03266","dok":1,"q":"Which cranial nerve controls facial expression and carries taste from the front of the tongue?","a":"The facial nerve (CN VII).","options":["The hypoglossal nerve (CN XII)","The trigeminal nerve (CN V)","The facial nerve (CN VII)","The glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX)"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The facial nerve, CN VII, is a mixed nerve. Its motor fibers control facial expression, its sensory fibers carry taste from the front of the tongue, and its parasympathetic fibers drive tear and saliva production. It enters the internal acoustic meatus and leaves the skull at the stylomastoid foramen.","notes":["The hypoglossal nerve, CN XII, moves the tongue but carries no taste. It would be the answer for a question about tongue movement.","The trigeminal nerve, CN V, carries touch and pain from the face and controls chewing, but not facial expression or taste. Pick it for a question about facial sensation.","Correct. CN VII controls facial expression and carries taste from the front of the tongue.","The glossopharyngeal nerve, CN IX, carries taste from the back of the tongue rather than the front. Choose it for a question about taste from the back of the tongue."],"lt":"t-cranial-nerves","lid":"c14","comp":190,"competencyId":"w8-cranial-nerves"},{"id":"L03267","dok":2,"q":"Which cranial nerve is purely motor, exits through the jugular foramen, and has its nucleus in the upper cervical spinal cord?","a":"The accessory nerve (CN XI).","options":["The vagus nerve (CN X)","The glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX)","The hypoglossal nerve (CN XII)","The accessory nerve (CN XI)"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The accessory nerve, CN XI, is unusual because its motor nucleus sits in the upper cervical spinal cord, just below the brainstem. Its rootlets rise through the foramen magnum into the skull and then leave through the jugular foramen with CN IX and CN X. It is purely motor and moves muscles of the neck and shoulder.","notes":["The vagus nerve also exits through the jugular foramen, but it is mixed and its nuclei sit in the medulla. Pick it for a question about autonomic control of the thoracic and abdominal organs.","The glossopharyngeal nerve also exits through the jugular foramen, but it is mixed and its nuclei are in the medulla. Choose it for a question about taste from the back of the tongue.","The hypoglossal nerve is purely motor with a medullary nucleus, but it exits through the hypoglossal canal, not the jugular foramen. It would be the answer for a question about the nerve of the tongue.","Correct. CN XI is purely motor, uses the jugular foramen, and has its nucleus in the upper cervical cord."],"lt":"t-cranial-nerves","lid":"c15","comp":190,"competencyId":"w8-cranial-nerves"},{"id":"L03268","dok":2,"q":"Besides CN IX and CN X, which other cranial nerve passes through the jugular foramen?","a":"The accessory nerve (CN XI).","options":["The accessory nerve (CN XI)","The trigeminal nerve (CN V)","The abducens nerve (CN VI)","The hypoglossal nerve (CN XII)"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The jugular foramen lies between the temporal and occipital bones, and the internal jugular vein drains out through it. Three cranial nerves run beside the vein: the glossopharyngeal (IX), the vagus (X), and the accessory (XI). Learning the opening gives you all three nerves at once.","notes":["Correct. CN XI joins CN IX and CN X in the jugular foramen.","The trigeminal nerve's three branches leave through the superior orbital fissure, the foramen rotundum, and the foramen ovale. Pick it for a question about those three openings.","The abducens nerve, CN VI, passes through the superior orbital fissure into the orbit. It would be the answer for a nerve that uses that fissure.","The hypoglossal nerve, CN XII, leaves through its own hypoglossal canal, just next to the jugular foramen. Choose it for a question about the hypoglossal canal."],"lt":"t-cranial-nerves","lid":"c16","comp":190,"competencyId":"w8-cranial-nerves"},{"id":"L03269","dok":2,"q":"Which cranial nerve passes through the hypoglossal canal of the occipital bone?","a":"The hypoglossal nerve (CN XII).","options":["The accessory nerve (CN XI)","The hypoglossal nerve (CN XII)","The glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX)","The vagus nerve (CN X)"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The hypoglossal canal is a small tunnel in the occipital bone just above the occipital condyle. It carries the hypoglossal nerve, CN XII, which shares its name. The nerve then runs below the tongue to move it.","notes":["The accessory nerve, CN XI, leaves through the jugular foramen, not the hypoglossal canal. Choose it for the purely motor nerve that exits beside the vagus.","Correct. CN XII passes through the hypoglossal canal.","The glossopharyngeal nerve, CN IX, leaves through the jugular foramen, not the hypoglossal canal, even though it also has glosso in its name. It would be the answer for taste from the back of the tongue.","The vagus nerve, CN X, leaves through the jugular foramen, not the hypoglossal canal. Pick it for a question about the nerve to the thoracic and abdominal organs."],"lt":"t-cranial-nerves","lid":"c17","comp":190,"competencyId":"w8-cranial-nerves"},{"id":"L03270","dok":2,"q":"How is the facial nerve (CN VII) classified?","a":"Mixed.","options":["Mixed","Special sensory only","Motor only","Sensory only"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The facial nerve carries motor fibers that control facial expression and sensory fibers that carry taste from the front of the tongue. It also carries parasympathetic fibers to the tear and salivary glands. Because it carries both sensory and motor signals, it is classified as mixed.","notes":["Correct. CN VII is mixed, carrying both sensory and motor fibers.","Taste is a special sense, but CN VII also carries motor fibers, so it cannot be special sensory only. Choose special sensory only for nerves like the olfactory or optic nerve.","Purely motor nerves, such as CN III, IV, VI, XI, and XII, carry no sensory fibers. CN VII cannot be motor only because it carries taste.","Purely sensory nerves, such as CN I, II, and VIII, carry no motor fibers. CN VII cannot be sensory only because it moves the muscles of facial expression."],"lt":"t-cranial-nerves","lid":"c18","comp":190,"competencyId":"w8-cranial-nerves"},{"id":"L03271","dok":2,"q":"A patient cannot raise one eyelid, cannot move that eye in most directions, and has a pupil on that side that will not constrict. Which cranial nerve is affected?","a":"The oculomotor nerve (CN III).","options":["The trochlear nerve (CN IV)","The oculomotor nerve (CN III)","The abducens nerve (CN VI)","The optic nerve (CN II)"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The oculomotor nerve, CN III, controls most eye movements, raises the upper eyelid, and constricts the pupil through its parasympathetic fibers. Losing all three functions on one side points to CN III. Its nuclei are in the midbrain, and it reaches the orbit through the superior orbital fissure.","notes":["The trochlear nerve, CN IV, controls only one eye movement and has nothing to do with the eyelid or pupil. Choose it for a question about the smallest cranial nerve.","Correct. CN III controls most eye movements, the eyelid, and pupil constriction.","The abducens nerve, CN VI, controls only one eye movement and does not raise the eyelid or constrict the pupil. It would be the answer for the purely motor nerve from the pons that uses the superior orbital fissure.","The optic nerve, CN II, carries vision but moves nothing. Pick it for a patient who has lost sight in one eye."],"lt":"t-cranial-nerves","lid":"c19","comp":190,"competencyId":"w8-cranial-nerves"},{"id":"L03272","dok":2,"q":"Which cranial nerves attach above the brainstem rather than to it?","a":"The olfactory (I) and optic (II) nerves.","options":["The olfactory (I) and optic (II) nerves","The vagus (X) and accessory (XI) nerves","The facial (VII) and vestibulocochlear (VIII) nerves","The oculomotor (III) and trochlear (IV) nerves"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The olfactory nerve attaches to the cerebrum through the olfactory bulb, and the optic nerve attaches to the diencephalon through the optic chiasm and tracts. Cranial nerves III through XII attach to the brainstem, except that most of CN XI comes from the upper cervical spinal cord. So CN I and II are the only two that attach above the brainstem.","notes":["Correct. CN I and CN II attach to the cerebrum and diencephalon, above the brainstem.","The vagus nerve attaches to the medulla, and the accessory nerve comes mostly from the upper cervical spinal cord, so both attach at or below the bottom of the brainstem. Choose them for a question about nerves that exit through the jugular foramen.","The facial and vestibulocochlear nerves attach near the junction of the pons and medulla. They would be the answer for the nerves that enter the internal acoustic meatus.","The oculomotor and trochlear nerves attach to the midbrain, the top of the brainstem. Pick them for a question about the cranial nerves with midbrain nuclei."],"lt":"t-cranial-nerves","lid":"c20","comp":190,"competencyId":"w8-cranial-nerves"},{"id":"L03273","dok":2,"q":"The dorsal motor nucleus and the nucleus ambiguus of the vagus nerve (CN X) lie in which brain region?","a":"The medulla oblongata.","options":["The cerebellum","The midbrain","The pons","The medulla oblongata"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The vagus nerve attaches to the side of the medulla oblongata, in the groove behind the olive, and its nuclei, including the dorsal motor nucleus and the nucleus ambiguus, sit there. The medulla holds the nuclei of IX, X, and XII. This fits the general rule that cranial nerve nuclei run in number order from the midbrain down.","notes":["The cerebellum holds no cranial nerve nuclei. It would be the answer for a question about coordination and balance.","The midbrain holds the nuclei for CN III and IV. Pick it for a question about the oculomotor or trochlear nucleus.","The pons holds the nuclei for CN V through VIII. Choose it for a question about the trigeminal, abducens, or facial nucleus.","Correct. The vagus nuclei lie in the medulla oblongata."],"lt":"t-cranial-nerves","lid":"c21","comp":165,"competencyId":"w7-cranial-nerve-nuclei"},{"id":"L03274","dok":2,"q":"Which cranial nerve carries taste from the back of the tongue, helps with swallowing, and serves the parotid gland?","a":"The glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX).","options":["The hypoglossal nerve (CN XII)","The vagus nerve (CN X)","The facial nerve (CN VII)","The glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX)"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The glossopharyngeal nerve, CN IX, is named for the tongue (glosso) and the pharynx. It is mixed: it carries taste from the back of the tongue, helps with swallowing, and sends parasympathetic fibers to the parotid salivary gland. It leaves the skull through the jugular foramen.","notes":["The hypoglossal nerve, CN XII, moves the tongue but carries no taste. It would be the answer for a question about tongue movement.","The vagus nerve, CN X, also helps with swallowing and voice, but it serves the thoracic and abdominal organs rather than the parotid gland. Choose it for a question about autonomic control of the viscera.","The facial nerve, CN VII, carries taste from the front of the tongue, not the back. Pick it for a question about taste from the front of the tongue.","Correct. CN IX carries taste from the back of the tongue, helps with swallowing, and serves the parotid gland."],"lt":"t-cranial-nerves","lid":"c22","comp":190,"competencyId":"w8-cranial-nerves"},{"id":"L03275","dok":2,"q":"The V2 (maxillary) branch of the trigeminal nerve passes through which skull opening?","a":"The foramen rotundum.","options":["The superior orbital fissure","The foramen ovale","The optic canal","The foramen rotundum"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Each of the three trigeminal branches has its own exit from the skull. V1 leaves through the superior orbital fissure, V2 through the foramen rotundum, and V3 through the foramen ovale, all in the sphenoid bone. A handy order to remember is Standing Room Only: superior orbital fissure, rotundum, ovale.","notes":["The superior orbital fissure carries V1, the ophthalmic branch, along with CN III, IV, and VI. Choose it if the question names V1.","The foramen ovale carries V3, the mandibular branch. Pick it if the question names V3.","The optic canal carries the optic nerve, CN II, not any trigeminal branch. It would be the answer for the nerve of vision.","Correct. V2 passes through the foramen rotundum."],"lt":"t-cranial-nerves","lid":"c23","comp":190,"competencyId":"w8-cranial-nerves"},{"id":"L03276","dok":2,"q":"How is the trigeminal nerve (CN V) classified?","a":"Mixed.","options":["Special sensory only","Motor only","Mixed","Sensory only"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The trigeminal nerve carries general sensation, such as touch and pain, from the face, and it also carries motor fibers to the muscles of chewing. Because it carries both sensory and motor signals, it is classified as mixed. Most of its fibers are sensory, which is why students sometimes call it sensory only.","notes":["Special senses are smell, vision, taste, hearing, and balance. CN V carries general sensation like touch and pain, not a special sense, so this label fits nerves like CN I or CN II instead.","CN V does have motor fibers for chewing, but it also carries facial sensation, so it cannot be motor only. Choose motor only for nerves like CN III or CN XII.","Correct. CN V is mixed, carrying facial sensation and motor fibers for chewing.","CN V carries a great deal of facial sensation, but it also moves the muscles of chewing, so it cannot be sensory only. Pick sensory only for CN I, II, or VIII."],"lt":"t-cranial-nerves","lid":"c24","comp":190,"competencyId":"w8-cranial-nerves"},{"id":"L03277","dok":2,"q":"The cochlear and vestibular nuclei connected to CN VIII sit at the junction of which two regions?","a":"The pons and the medulla.","options":["The midbrain and the pons","The medulla and the spinal cord","The pons and the medulla","The cerebrum and the diencephalon"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The vestibulocochlear nerve attaches at the pontomedullary junction, where the pons meets the medulla. Its cochlear and vestibular nuclei lie right at that border. This is why CN VIII is listed among the nerves of the pons and also among the nerves of the medulla.","notes":["The midbrain holds the nuclei for CN III and IV, above the pons. Pick this pair for a question about the oculomotor and trochlear nuclei.","The CN VIII nuclei do not sit at the junction of the medulla and spinal cord. The upper cervical cord just below that junction holds the accessory (CN XI) nucleus instead.","Correct. The CN VIII nuclei sit at the junction of the pons and medulla.","The cerebrum and diencephalon receive CN I and CN II, the two nerves that attach above the brainstem. Choose them for a question about smell and vision."],"lt":"t-cranial-nerves","lid":"c25","comp":165,"competencyId":"w7-cranial-nerve-nuclei"},{"id":"L03278","dok":3,"q":"A tumor at the jugular foramen causes trouble swallowing, a hoarse voice, and weakness shrugging one shoulder. Which set of cranial nerves is compressed?","a":"CN IX, CN X, and CN XI.","options":["CN VII, CN VIII, and CN XII","CN IX, CN X, and CN XI","CN III, CN IV, and CN VI","CN V, CN VII, and CN VIII"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The jugular foramen carries the glossopharyngeal (IX), vagus (X), and accessory (XI) nerves side by side. A mass there can press on all three at once. The glossopharyngeal and vagus nerves serve swallowing and the voice, and the accessory nerve serves the shoulder, producing this recognizable cluster of signs.","notes":["CN VII and VIII pass through the internal acoustic meatus, and CN XII passes through the hypoglossal canal. This set mixes nerves from two different openings.","Correct. CN IX, X, and XI pass through the jugular foramen together.","CN III, IV, and VI move the eye and pass through the superior orbital fissure. Pick them for a mass at that fissure causing double vision.","CN V, VII, and VIII serve facial sensation, facial expression, and hearing. Choose them for a mass near the pons that affects the face and ear."],"lt":"t-cranial-nerves","lid":"c26","comp":190,"competencyId":"w8-cranial-nerves"},{"id":"L03279","dok":3,"q":"A patient wakes up with one side of the face drooping and cannot close that eye or smile on that side. Which cranial nerve is involved in this Bell's palsy?","a":"The facial nerve (CN VII).","options":["The accessory nerve (CN XI)","The facial nerve (CN VII)","The trigeminal nerve (CN V)","The oculomotor nerve (CN III)"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Bell's palsy is a sudden weakness of the facial nerve, CN VII, which controls facial expression. Each facial nerve serves only its own side, so the affected side droops while the other side works normally. Taste from the front of the tongue can also be affected because CN VII carries it.","notes":["The accessory nerve, CN XI, moves the neck and shoulder. Choose it for a patient who cannot shrug one shoulder.","Correct. Bell's palsy is a weakness of CN VII.","The trigeminal nerve, CN V, carries facial sensation and controls chewing. Its classic disorder is trigeminal neuralgia, a stabbing facial pain, so pick it for that problem.","The oculomotor nerve, CN III, raises the eyelid and moves most of the eye but does not close the eye or move the face. It would be the answer for a drooping eyelid with a pupil that will not constrict."],"lt":"t-cranial-nerves","lid":"c27","comp":190,"competencyId":"w8-cranial-nerves"},{"id":"L03280","dok":3,"q":"A clinician finds that a patient's tongue is weak on one side and looks for the lesion at the hypoglossal (CN XII) nucleus. Which brain region holds that nucleus?","a":"The medulla oblongata.","options":["The upper cervical cord","The midbrain","The medulla oblongata","The pons"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The hypoglossal nucleus lies in the medulla oblongata, near the midline. Testing each cranial nerve helps localize brainstem lesions because each nucleus has a fixed home. A weak tongue from a CN XII problem points the clinician to the medulla.","notes":["The upper cervical cord holds the accessory (CN XI) nucleus, not the hypoglossal nucleus. It would be the answer for a question about where the CN XI nucleus sits.","The midbrain holds the nuclei for CN III and IV. Pick it for a patient with a drooping eyelid and a pupil that will not constrict.","Correct. The hypoglossal nucleus is in the medulla oblongata.","The pons holds the nuclei for CN V through VIII. Choose it for a weak abducens or facial nerve."],"lt":"t-cranial-nerves","lid":"c28","comp":165,"competencyId":"w7-cranial-nerve-nuclei"},{"id":"L03281","dok":3,"q":"A patient has brief, stabbing pain along the cheek and jaw on one side of the face. Which cranial nerve is the source of this trigeminal neuralgia?","a":"The trigeminal nerve (CN V).","options":["The glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX)","The vestibulocochlear nerve (CN VIII)","The trigeminal nerve (CN V)","The facial nerve (CN VII)"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Trigeminal neuralgia is a stabbing facial pain that follows the branches of CN V, the trigeminal nerve, which carries touch and pain from the face. The cheek is served by V2 and the jaw by V3, so pain along these areas matches the nerve's branches. The anatomy of the nerve predicts exactly where the pain appears.","notes":["The glossopharyngeal nerve, CN IX, serves the throat and the back of the tongue. Choose it for a question about taste from the back of the tongue or swallowing.","The vestibulocochlear nerve, CN VIII, carries hearing and balance, not facial sensation. It would be the answer for hearing loss or dizziness.","Correct. Trigeminal neuralgia comes from CN V, the nerve of facial sensation.","The facial nerve, CN VII, controls facial movement rather than facial pain. Pick it for a drooping face, as in Bell's palsy."],"lt":"t-cranial-nerves","lid":"c29","comp":190,"competencyId":"w8-cranial-nerves"},{"id":"L03282","dok":3,"q":"A patient's eye movement problem is traced to a nucleus in the midbrain. The nerve involved is purely motor, but the eyelid and pupil are normal. Which nerve is most likely affected?","a":"The trochlear nerve (CN IV).","options":["The oculomotor nerve (CN III)","The trochlear nerve (CN IV)","The optic nerve (CN II)","The abducens nerve (CN VI)"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Two cranial nerves have their motor nuclei in the midbrain, the oculomotor (III) and the trochlear (IV), and both move the eye. The oculomotor nerve also raises the eyelid and constricts the pupil, so a normal eyelid and pupil point away from it. That leaves the trochlear nerve, the smallest cranial nerve, which moves the eye through a single muscle.","notes":["The oculomotor nerve does have its nucleus in the midbrain, but damage would also cause a drooping eyelid and a pupil that will not constrict. Pick it when those signs are present.","Correct. The trochlear nerve is the purely motor eye nerve with a midbrain nucleus that leaves the eyelid and pupil alone.","The optic nerve carries vision and is sensory, not motor, and it attaches to the diencephalon rather than the midbrain. It would be the answer for loss of sight.","The abducens nerve is purely motor and moves the eye, but its nucleus is in the pons, not the midbrain. Choose it for a lesion localized to the pons."],"lt":"t-cranial-nerves","lid":"c30","comp":190,"competencyId":"w8-cranial-nerves"},{"id":"L03283","dok":1,"q":"The twelve pairs of cranial nerves belong to which division of the nervous system?","a":"The peripheral nervous system.","options":["The central nervous system","The peripheral nervous system","The enteric nervous system","Only the autonomic division of the PNS"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The central nervous system is the brain and spinal cord. The cranial nerves arise from the brain and brainstem but travel outside them to reach their targets, so they are part of the peripheral nervous system, just like the spinal nerves. Some cranial nerves carry autonomic fibers, but most also carry somatic sensory or motor fibers.","notes":["The central nervous system is the brain and spinal cord themselves. The cranial nerves connect to it but lie outside it, so pick the CNS for a question about the brain or cord.","Correct. The cranial nerves are part of the peripheral nervous system.","The enteric nervous system is the network of neurons in the wall of the digestive tract. Choose it for a question about the nerve plexuses within the gut wall.","Only CN III, VII, IX, and X carry autonomic fibers, and even they carry other types too. Most cranial nerves are sensory or somatic motor, so the autonomic division alone is too narrow."],"lt":"t-cranial-nerves","lid":"d3-t-cranial-nerves-1","comp":190,"competencyId":"w8-cranial-nerves"},{"id":"L03284","dok":2,"q":"The vagus nerve (CN X) reaches far beyond the head to the heart, lungs, and digestive tract. What type of fibers does it carry to these organs?","a":"Parasympathetic (visceral motor) fibers.","options":["Sympathetic fibers from the thoracic cord","Parasympathetic (visceral motor) fibers","Somatic motor fibers to trunk muscles","Special sensory fibers for hearing"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The vagus nerve carries parasympathetic, or rest-and-digest, fibers to the heart, lungs, and much of the digestive tract. These visceral motor fibers slow the heart and promote digestion. A single cranial nerve reaching so many organs is why the vagus is called the wanderer.","notes":["Sympathetic fibers leave the thoracic and lumbar spinal cord from the lateral horns, not from a cranial nerve. Pick this for a question about the sympathetic division.","Correct. The vagus carries parasympathetic fibers to the thoracic and abdominal organs.","Skeletal muscles of the trunk are served by spinal nerves, not the vagus. Choose spinal nerves for a question about trunk muscles.","Hearing is carried by the vestibulocochlear nerve, CN VIII. It would be the answer for a question about the sense of hearing."],"lt":"t-cranial-nerves","lid":"d3-t-cranial-nerves-2","comp":190,"competencyId":"w8-cranial-nerves"},{"id":"L03285","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares the optic nerve (CN II) and the facial nerve (CN VII)?","a":"CN II is purely sensory, while CN VII is mixed.","options":["CN II is purely sensory, while CN VII is mixed","Both are purely motor nerves","CN II is mixed, while CN VII is purely sensory","CN II is purely motor, while CN VII is purely sensory"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Each cranial nerve carries the kinds of fibers its job needs. The optic nerve only carries vision into the brain, so it is purely sensory. The facial nerve both moves the muscles of facial expression and carries taste, so it is mixed.","notes":["Correct. CN II is purely sensory and CN VII is mixed.","Neither nerve is purely motor. The optic nerve carries vision, and the facial nerve carries taste as well as motor fibers.","This reverses the two nerves. The optic nerve carries only vision, and the facial nerve has motor fibers for expression, so neither label fits here.","The optic nerve moves nothing, so it cannot be motor, and the facial nerve has motor fibers, so it cannot be purely sensory."],"lt":"t-cranial-nerves","lid":"d3-t-cranial-nerves-3","comp":190,"competencyId":"w8-cranial-nerves"},{"id":"L03286","dok":3,"q":"A fracture runs through the superior orbital fissure. Which group of cranial nerves is most at risk?","a":"CN III, IV, V1, and VI.","options":["CN IX, X, and XI","CN III, IV, V1, and VI","CN II, III, IV, and VI","CN VII and VIII only"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The superior orbital fissure is the slit between the greater and lesser wings of the sphenoid at the back of the orbit. The nerves that move the eye, CN III, IV, and VI, pass through it along with V1, the ophthalmic branch of the trigeminal nerve. Damage here can cause eye movement problems and numbness of the forehead together.","notes":["CN IX, X, and XI pass through the jugular foramen. They would be the answer for a lesion causing swallowing, voice, and shoulder problems.","Correct. CN III, IV, V1, and VI pass through the superior orbital fissure.","CN III, IV, and VI do use the superior orbital fissure, but CN II, the optic nerve, passes through the separate optic canal. Pick a group with CN II for a fracture involving the optic canal.","CN VII and VIII pass through the internal acoustic meatus in the temporal bone. Choose them for a fracture affecting facial movement and hearing."],"lt":"t-cranial-nerves","lid":"d3-t-cranial-nerves-4","comp":190,"competencyId":"w8-cranial-nerves"},{"id":"L03287","dok":2,"q":"Visual signals leave the retina in the optic nerve, pass through the optic chiasm, and travel in the optic tract to which relay nucleus?","a":"The lateral geniculate nucleus of the thalamus.","options":["The lateral geniculate nucleus","The Edinger-Westphal nucleus","The vestibular nuclei of the brainstem","The olfactory bulb"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The optic nerve carries visual signals from the retina back to the optic chiasm, where fibers from the two eyes partly cross. The pathway then continues as the optic tract to the lateral geniculate nucleus, a relay nucleus of the thalamus. From there the signals are passed on to the primary visual cortex in the occipital lobe.","notes":["Correct. The optic tract carries visual signals to the lateral geniculate nucleus of the thalamus.","The Edinger-Westphal nucleus in the midbrain belongs to CN III and sends the signals that constrict the pupil. It would be the answer for a question about the parasympathetic nucleus of the oculomotor nerve.","The vestibular nuclei at the pons and medulla receive balance signals from CN VIII. Choose them for a question about the pathway for balance.","The olfactory bulb receives smell signals from the olfactory epithelium and sends them along the olfactory tract. Pick it for the pathway of CN I."],"lt":"t-cranial-nerves","lid":"d3-t-cranial-nerves-5","comp":190,"competencyId":"w8-cranial-nerves"},{"id":"L03288","dok":2,"q":"Taste from the front of the tongue and taste from the back of the tongue are carried by which two cranial nerves?","a":"The facial (VII) and glossopharyngeal (IX) nerves.","options":["Facial (VII) and glossopharyngeal (IX)","Trigeminal (V) and hypoglossal (XII)","Olfactory (I) and vestibulocochlear (VIII)","Hypoglossal (XII) and accessory (XI)"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Taste is a special sense carried by mixed cranial nerves. The facial nerve, CN VII, carries taste from the front of the tongue, and the glossopharyngeal nerve, CN IX, carries taste from the back. Both send these signals to nuclei in the brainstem.","notes":["Correct. CN VII carries taste from the front of the tongue and CN IX from the back.","The trigeminal nerve carries general touch and pain from the front of the tongue, not taste, and the hypoglossal nerve moves the tongue. Pick this pair for a question about touch and movement of the tongue.","The olfactory nerve carries smell and the vestibulocochlear nerve carries hearing and balance. Choose this pair for a question about two purely sensory cranial nerves.","Both of these nerves are purely motor, moving the tongue and the neck and shoulder muscles. They would be the answer for a question about motor nerves near the jugular foramen and hypoglossal canal."],"lt":"t-cranial-nerves","lid":"d3x-t-cranial-nerves-1","comp":190,"competencyId":"w8-cranial-nerves"},{"id":"L03289","dok":2,"q":"The facial nerve (CN VII) enters the temporal bone through the internal acoustic meatus. Through which opening does it leave the skull to reach the face?","a":"The stylomastoid foramen.","options":["The jugular foramen","The stylomastoid foramen","The hypoglossal canal","The foramen ovale of the sphenoid"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The facial nerve travels through a canal in the temporal bone after entering the internal acoustic meatus with CN VIII. It exits the skull at the stylomastoid foramen, between the styloid and mastoid processes. From there its branches fan out across the face to the muscles of facial expression.","notes":["The jugular foramen carries CN IX, X, and XI. Pick it for a question about the glossopharyngeal, vagus, or accessory nerve.","Correct. CN VII leaves the skull through the stylomastoid foramen.","The hypoglossal canal carries CN XII to the tongue. It would be the answer for the nerve of tongue movement.","The foramen ovale carries V3, the mandibular branch of the trigeminal nerve. Choose it for a question about the nerve to the muscles of chewing."],"lt":"t-cranial-nerves","lid":"d3x-t-cranial-nerves-2","comp":190,"competencyId":"w8-cranial-nerves"},{"id":"L03290","dok":2,"q":"Which four cranial nerves carry parasympathetic (visceral motor) fibers?","a":"CN III, VII, IX, and X.","options":["CN I, II, and VIII","CN IV, VI, XI, and XII","CN III, VII, IX, and X","CN V, VII, IX, and X"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Parasympathetic fibers travel in four cranial nerves. CN III constricts the pupil, CN VII drives tear and saliva production, CN IX serves the parotid gland, and CN X serves the thoracic and abdominal organs. These nerves already reach the glands and organs involved, so they carry the signals that regulate them.","notes":["These are the purely sensory cranial nerves for smell, vision, hearing, and balance. They would be the answer for a question about the sensory nerves.","These are purely motor nerves to skeletal muscles of the eye, neck, shoulder, and tongue. Choose them for a question about somatic motor cranial nerves.","Correct. CN III, VII, IX, and X carry parasympathetic fibers.","This is close to the list of mixed nerves, but CN V is not parasympathetic, and CN III, which is, is missing. Pick V, VII, IX, and X for a question about the mixed cranial nerves."],"lt":"t-cranial-nerves","lid":"d3x-t-cranial-nerves-3","comp":190,"competencyId":"w8-cranial-nerves"},{"id":"L04556","dok":3,"q":"A patient wakes up unable to close one eye, raise that eyebrow, or smile on the same side of the face. Which cranial nerve is most likely affected?","a":"The facial nerve (CN VII), as in Bell palsy.","options":["Hypoglossal nerve (CN XII)","Oculomotor nerve (CN III)","Facial nerve (CN VII)","Trigeminal nerve (CN V)"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The facial nerve (CN VII) is a mixed cranial nerve whose motor job is facial expression; it leaves the skull through the stylomastoid foramen and fans out across the face. When it fails on one side, as in Bell palsy, expression on that whole side goes slack, so the eye will not close, the forehead will not wrinkle, and the mouth droops. Because it also carries taste from the front of the tongue and drives tears and saliva, those can be affected too.","notes":["The hypoglossal nerve controls movement of the tongue. It would be the answer for a patient whose tongue drifts to one side when stuck out.","The oculomotor nerve controls most eye movements, raises the upper eyelid, and constricts the pupil. Damage would cause a drooping eyelid and trouble moving the eye, not loss of the smile.","Correct. Loss of expression on one whole side of the face points to the facial nerve.","The trigeminal nerve carries sensation from the face and controls the muscles of chewing. Pick it if the patient lost feeling in the face or had trouble clenching the jaw while facial expression stayed normal."],"lt":"t-w7-lab-facial-expression","lid":"w7-lab-facial-expression-r5","comp":190,"competencyId":"w8-cranial-nerves"},{"id":"L04557","dok":1,"q":"Which cranial nerve carries sensation from the face and also controls the muscles of chewing?","a":"The trigeminal nerve (CN V).","options":["Facial nerve (CN VII)","Trigeminal nerve (CN V)","Vagus nerve (CN X)","Glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX)"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The trigeminal nerve (CN V) is the largest cranial nerve and is mixed, carrying both sensory and motor fibers. Its three branches, ophthalmic (V1), maxillary (V2), and mandibular (V3), carry sensation from the face, and the mandibular branch also carries the motor fibers for chewing. Its nuclei are in the pons.","notes":["The facial nerve controls facial expression and carries taste from the front of the tongue, but it is not the main sensory nerve of the face. Pick it if the question asked which nerve is involved in Bell palsy.","Correct. The trigeminal nerve is the sensory nerve of the face and the motor nerve for chewing.","The vagus nerve gives autonomic control of the thoracic and abdominal organs and serves the voice and swallowing. Choose it for the cranial nerve that travels down into the trunk.","The glossopharyngeal nerve carries taste from the back of the tongue, helps with swallowing, and drives saliva from the parotid gland. It would be the answer for a question about the back of the tongue and throat."],"lt":"t-w7-lab-mastication","lid":"w7-lab-mastication-r1","comp":190,"competencyId":"w8-cranial-nerves"},{"id":"L04583","dok":2,"q":"Which three cranial nerves control the movements of the eyeball?","a":"The oculomotor (III), trochlear (IV), and abducens (VI) nerves.","options":["Oculomotor (III), trigeminal (V), and abducens (VI)","Trigeminal (V), abducens (VI), and facial (VII)","Optic (II), oculomotor (III), and trochlear (IV)","Oculomotor (III), trochlear (IV), and abducens (VI)"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Three motor cranial nerves move the eyeball, and all three pass through the superior orbital fissure. The oculomotor nerve (CN III) handles most eye movements and also raises the eyelid and constricts the pupil, the trochlear nerve (CN IV) is the smallest cranial nerve, and the abducens nerve (CN VI) turns the eye outward. The optic nerve carries vision but does not move the eye.","notes":["The trigeminal nerve is the odd one out: its ophthalmic branch carries sensation from the eye region but does not move the eye. The trochlear nerve belongs in its place.","The trigeminal nerve carries facial sensation and controls chewing, and the facial nerve controls facial expression. Only the abducens in this set moves the eye.","The optic nerve carries vision from the retina, so it is purely sensory and moves nothing. Swap it for the abducens nerve and the set would be right.","Correct. CN III, IV, and VI are the three eye-movement nerves."],"lt":"t-w7-lab-extraocular","lid":"w7-lab-extraocular-r2","comp":190,"competencyId":"w8-cranial-nerves"},{"id":"L04647","dok":1,"q":"How many pairs of cranial nerves are there, and where do they arise?","a":"Twelve pairs, arising from the brain.","options":["12 pairs, from the brain","12 pairs, from the spinal cord","24 pairs, from the brain","31 pairs, from the spinal cord"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"There are 12 pairs of cranial nerves, numbered I to XII from front to back by where they attach to the brain. The olfactory (I) and optic (II) nerves attach to the cerebrum and diencephalon, and nerves III through XII attach to the brainstem, except that most of XI comes from the upper cervical spinal cord. Like the spinal nerves, they are part of the peripheral nervous system.","notes":["Correct. There are 12 pairs of cranial nerves, and they arise from the brain.","The count is right, but cranial nerves attach to the brain, not the spinal cord. That is what makes them cranial rather than spinal.","Twenty-four is the number of individual cranial nerves, counting left and right separately. Cranial nerves are counted in pairs, so the answer is 12 pairs.","There are 31 pairs of spinal nerves, and they arise from the spinal cord. Pick this if the question asked about spinal nerves."],"lt":"t-w8-cranial-nerves","lid":"w8-cranial-nerves-r1","comp":190,"competencyId":"w8-cranial-nerves"},{"id":"L04648","dok":1,"q":"Which cranial nerve is the vagus, and what makes its reach unusual?","a":"CN X, the vagus, which reaches beyond the head and neck into the thorax and abdomen.","options":["CN X; it reaches beyond the head into the thorax and abdomen","CN X; it carries only hearing and balance from the ear","CN IX; it reaches beyond the head into the thorax and abdomen","CN XI; its roots arise partly from the upper spinal cord"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The vagus is cranial nerve X, a mixed nerve that leaves the skull through the jugular foramen. Its name means wanderer, because it travels far beyond the head and neck, down into the thorax and abdomen. It carries most of the parasympathetic supply to the chest and abdominal organs, which is why one nerve can slow the heart and drive digestion, and it also serves voice and swallowing.","notes":["Correct. The vagus is CN X, and it wanders into the thorax and abdomen.","Hearing and balance are carried by CN VIII, the vestibulocochlear nerve. Pick CN VIII if the question asked which nerve serves the inner ear.","CN IX is the glossopharyngeal nerve, which serves taste from the back of the tongue, swallowing, and saliva from the parotid gland. It shares the jugular foramen with the vagus but stays in the head and neck.","CN XI is the accessory nerve, whose nucleus sits in the upper cervical spinal cord. It is not the vagus, so pick it only if the question asked which cranial nerve has roots in the cord."],"lt":"t-w8-cranial-nerves","lid":"w8-cranial-nerves-r2","comp":190,"competencyId":"w8-cranial-nerves"},{"id":"L04649","dok":1,"q":"Which cranial nerves carry vision and smell?","a":"The optic nerve (CN II) carries vision; the olfactory nerve (CN I) carries smell.","options":["Optic (II) for vision; olfactory (I) for smell","Olfactory (I) for vision; optic (II) for smell","Oculomotor (III) for vision; olfactory (I) for smell","Optic (II) for vision; trigeminal (V) for smell"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The first two cranial nerves are both purely sensory and both serve special senses. The olfactory nerve (CN I) carries smell from the nasal cavity through the cribriform plate of the ethmoid bone. The optic nerve (CN II) carries vision from the retina through the optic canal. These two are the only cranial nerves that attach above the brainstem.","notes":["Correct. The optic nerve carries vision and the olfactory nerve carries smell.","This swaps the two nerves. Olfactory refers to smell (as in the olfactory bulb), and optic refers to the eye and vision.","The oculomotor nerve (CN III) moves most of the eye muscles, raises the eyelid, and constricts the pupil, but it does not carry vision. Pick it if the question asked which nerve controls most eye movements.","The trigeminal nerve (CN V) carries general sensation from the face, such as touch and pain, but not the special sense of smell. It would be the answer for a question about facial sensation."],"lt":"t-w8-cranial-nerves","lid":"w8-cranial-nerves-r3","comp":190,"competencyId":"w8-cranial-nerves"},{"id":"L04650","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly matches two mixed cranial nerves to their main jobs?","a":"The facial nerve (CN VII) controls facial expression; the trigeminal nerve (CN V) carries face sensation and controls chewing.","options":["Vagus (X): facial expression; trigeminal (V): face sensation and chewing","Trigeminal (V): facial expression; facial (VII): face sensation and chewing","Facial (VII): facial expression; glossopharyngeal (IX): chewing","Facial (VII): facial expression; trigeminal (V): face sensation and chewing"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Both nerves are mixed and both serve the face, so they are easy to confuse. The facial nerve (CN VII) controls facial expression and also carries taste from the front of the tongue and drives tear and saliva production. The trigeminal nerve (CN V), the largest cranial nerve, has three branches that carry sensation from the face, and it also controls chewing. A drooping face points to CN VII, as in Bell's palsy, while stabbing facial pain points to CN V, as in trigeminal neuralgia.","notes":["The vagus (CN X) serves the thoracic and abdominal organs, voice, and swallowing, not facial expression. It would be the answer for a question about the nerve that reaches into the abdomen.","This swaps the two nerves. The facial nerve moves the face for expression, while the trigeminal nerve feels the face and moves the jaw for chewing.","The glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX) serves taste from the back of the tongue, swallowing, and saliva from the parotid gland, not chewing. Pick it if the question asked which nerve carries taste from the back of the tongue.","Correct. CN VII runs facial expression, and CN V carries face sensation and controls chewing."],"lt":"t-w8-cranial-nerves","lid":"w8-cranial-nerves-r4","comp":190,"competencyId":"w8-cranial-nerves"},{"id":"L04651","dok":3,"q":"A patient cannot shrug one shoulder or turn the head against resistance. Which cranial nerve is most likely damaged?","a":"The accessory nerve (CN XI).","options":["Facial nerve (VII)","Vagus nerve (X)","Accessory nerve (XI)","Hypoglossal nerve (XII)"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The accessory nerve (CN XI) is a motor nerve to the muscles that turn the head and shrug the shoulders. Its nucleus sits in the upper cervical spinal cord, its rootlets rise into the skull through the foramen magnum, and it leaves through the jugular foramen. Weak shrugging and weak head turning on one side are the classic signs of damage to this nerve.","notes":["The facial nerve (CN VII) controls facial expression. Choose it if one side of the face drooped, as in Bell's palsy.","The vagus nerve (CN X) serves voice, swallowing, and the thoracic and abdominal organs. It would be the answer for hoarseness or trouble swallowing, though it shares the jugular foramen with CN XI.","Correct. Weak shrugging and head turning point to the accessory nerve.","The hypoglossal nerve (CN XII) moves the tongue. Pick it if the patient's tongue deviated to one side when stuck out."],"lt":"t-w8-cranial-nerves","lid":"w8-cranial-nerves-r5","comp":190,"competencyId":"w8-cranial-nerves"}]},{"id":"m5-spinal-nerves","title":"Spinal nerves and nerve plexuses","summary":"nerve wrappings, spinal nerve formation, rami, cervical, brachial, lumbar and sacral plexuses, dermatomes, sensory receptors","mw":"2026-12-02","tr":"2026-12-03","cards":[{"id":"L02780","dok":1,"q":"A nociceptor is a sensory receptor named for detecting which kind of stimulus?","a":"Pain, from potentially damaging stimuli.","options":["Chemicals","Light","Temperature","Pain"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Sensory receptors are named for the kind of stimulus they detect. Nociceptors respond to potentially damaging stimuli, such as extreme heat, crushing, or cutting, and produce the sensation of pain. The other stimulus types are mechanical force (mechanoreceptors), temperature (thermoreceptors), light (photoreceptors), and chemicals (chemoreceptors).","notes":["Chemicals in solution, such as smell and taste molecules, are detected by chemoreceptors. It would be right for a question about chemoreceptors.","Light is detected by photoreceptors in the retina. Pick light for a question about photoreceptors.","Changes in temperature are detected by thermoreceptors. Choose temperature for a question about thermoreceptors.","Correct. Nociceptors respond to potentially damaging stimuli and produce the sensation of pain."],"lt":"t-neuronal-integration","lid":"c23","comp":189,"competencyId":"w8-sensory-receptors"},{"id":"L02814","dok":1,"q":"Sensory receptors can be named by location. Which term names receptors that detect stimuli inside the body, in the organs and blood vessels?","a":"Interoceptors.","options":["Exteroceptors","Photoreceptors","Proprioceptors","Interoceptors"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Receptors can be classified by where they sit. Interoceptors, also called visceroceptors, detect stimuli inside the body, in the organs and blood vessels. Exteroceptors detect stimuli at or near the surface, and proprioceptors detect body position and movement in the muscles, tendons, and joints.","notes":["Exteroceptors detect stimuli at or near the body surface, such as touch, temperature, and the special senses. Pick them for a question about receptors in the skin.","Photoreceptors are named for their stimulus, light, not their location; they are in the retina. They would be right for a question about which receptors detect light.","Proprioceptors detect the position and movement of the body and sit in muscles, tendons, and joints. Choose them for a question about sensing where a limb is.","Correct. Interoceptors (visceroceptors) sense conditions inside the body."],"lt":"t-neuronal-integration","lid":"c13-x2","comp":189,"competencyId":"w8-sensory-receptors"},{"id":"L02815","dok":1,"q":"Which receptors in muscles, tendons, and joints detect the position and movement of the body?","a":"Proprioceptors.","options":["Exteroceptors","Nociceptors","Interoceptors","Proprioceptors"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Proprioceptors are receptors located in muscles, tendons, and joints that detect the position and movement of the body. They let you know where a limb is without looking at it. The receptor that senses stretch in the patellar reflex is one example of a proprioceptor in muscle.","notes":["Exteroceptors detect stimuli at or near the body surface, such as touch and temperature on the skin. Pick them for a question about receptors near the surface.","Nociceptors respond to potentially damaging stimuli and produce pain; they are named for their stimulus, not their location. They would be right for a question about pain receptors.","Interoceptors detect stimuli inside the organs and blood vessels. Choose them for a question about sensing conditions in the viscera.","Correct. Proprioceptors sense body position and movement."],"lt":"t-neuronal-integration","lid":"c14-x2","comp":189,"competencyId":"w8-sensory-receptors"},{"id":"L02825","dok":1,"q":"A receptor in the skin detects warmth. Which type of receptor is responding?","a":"A thermoreceptor.","options":["A mechanoreceptor","A thermoreceptor","A chemoreceptor","A photoreceptor"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Sensory receptors are named for the kind of stimulus they detect. Thermo means heat, so thermoreceptors respond to changes in temperature, including warmth and cold on the skin. If the heat became strong enough to damage tissue, nociceptors would also respond and produce pain.","notes":["Mechanoreceptors respond to mechanical force such as touch, pressure, vibration, and stretch. Pick it for a question about feeling pressure on the skin.","Correct. Thermoreceptors respond to changes in temperature.","Chemoreceptors respond to chemicals in solution, such as smell and taste molecules. Choose it for a question about taste or smell.","Photoreceptors respond to light and are found in the retina. It would be right for a question about vision."],"lt":"t-neuronal-integration","lid":"c24-x2","comp":189,"competencyId":"w8-sensory-receptors"},{"id":"L02834","dok":1,"q":"Which receptors in the retina of the eye respond to light?","a":"Photoreceptors.","options":["Mechanoreceptors","Photoreceptors","Thermoreceptors","Chemoreceptors"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Sensory receptors are named for the kind of stimulus they detect. Photo means light, so photoreceptors respond to light, and they are found in the retina of the eye. The other stimulus types are mechanical force, temperature, chemicals, and potentially damaging stimuli that cause pain.","notes":["Mechanoreceptors respond to mechanical force such as touch, pressure, vibration, and stretch. Choose them for a question about touch.","Correct. Photo means light, and photoreceptors respond to it.","Thermoreceptors respond to changes in temperature. They would be right for a question about sensing warmth or cold.","Chemoreceptors respond to chemicals in solution, such as smell and taste molecules. Pick them for a question about taste or smell."],"lt":"t-neuronal-integration","lid":"d3-t-neuronal-integration-3","comp":189,"competencyId":"w8-sensory-receptors"},{"id":"L02836","dok":2,"q":"After bumping an elbow, a person rubs the spot. Which receptors detect the touch and pressure of the rubbing?","a":"Mechanoreceptors.","options":["Mechanoreceptors","Thermoreceptors","Chemoreceptors","Nociceptors"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Mechanoreceptors respond to mechanical force such as touch, pressure, vibration, and stretch. Rubbing the skin presses on and moves it, so mechanoreceptors in the skin detect the rubbing. The pain from the bump itself came from nociceptors, which respond to potentially damaging stimuli.","notes":["Correct. Mechanoreceptors respond to touch and pressure.","Thermoreceptors respond to changes in temperature. Choose them for a question about sensing warmth or cold on the skin.","Chemoreceptors respond to chemicals in solution, such as taste and smell molecules. They would be right for a question about taste.","Nociceptors respond to potentially damaging stimuli and produce pain, so they are the receptors that signaled the bump itself. Pick them for a question about what detected the injury."],"lt":"t-neuronal-integration","lid":"d3-t-neuronal-integration-5","comp":189,"competencyId":"w8-sensory-receptors"},{"id":"L02837","dok":1,"q":"Which receptors respond to the chemicals dissolved in food that produce the sense of taste?","a":"Chemoreceptors.","options":["Photoreceptors","Mechanoreceptors","Nociceptors","Chemoreceptors"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Chemoreceptors respond to chemicals in solution. Taste and smell both depend on chemoreceptors, which detect molecules dissolved in saliva or in the moist lining of the nose. They are one of the five receptor types named for their stimulus.","notes":["Photoreceptors respond to light and are found in the retina. Choose them for a question about vision.","Mechanoreceptors respond to mechanical force such as touch and pressure, like the feel of food in the mouth. Pick them for a question about texture or pressure.","Nociceptors respond to potentially damaging stimuli and produce pain. They would be right for a question about pain from a burn.","Correct. Chemoreceptors respond to chemicals in solution, including taste molecules."],"lt":"t-neuronal-integration","lid":"d3x-t-neuronal-integration-1","comp":189,"competencyId":"w8-sensory-receptors"},{"id":"L02971","dok":1,"q":"How many pairs of spinal nerves arise from the spinal cord?","a":"Thirty-one pairs.","options":["Twelve pairs","Thirty-three pairs","Twenty-four pairs","Thirty-one pairs"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Thirty-one pairs of spinal nerves arise from the spinal cord: 8 cervical, 12 thoracic, 5 lumbar, 5 sacral, and 1 coccygeal. Each pair is named for the region of the vertebral column where it leaves and is numbered within that region. Every spinal nerve is a mixed nerve, carrying both sensory and motor axons.","notes":["Twelve is the number of cranial nerve pairs, and also the number of thoracic spinal nerve pairs. Pick it if the question asked about nerves arising from the brain.","Thirty-three is the usual count of vertebrae before the sacral and coccygeal bones fuse. It comes from mixing up the bones of the column with the nerves that pass between them.","Twenty-four is the number of movable vertebrae (7 cervical, 12 thoracic, 5 lumbar). Students who assume one nerve per vertebra pick it, but the nerve count does not match the vertebra count.","Correct. Adding 8 cervical, 12 thoracic, 5 lumbar, 5 sacral, and 1 coccygeal gives 31 pairs."],"lt":"t-pns","lid":"c2","comp":188,"competencyId":"w8-spinal-nerves-rami"},{"id":"L02972","dok":1,"q":"Which connective tissue wrapping surrounds the entire nerve?","a":"The epineurium.","options":["The periosteum","The endoneurium","The epineurium","The perineurium"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The epineurium is the outermost connective tissue wrapping of a nerve, a tough sheath that encloses the whole nerve. The prefix epi- means upon or over, which matches its place on the outside. Inside it, the axons are grouped into fascicles wrapped by perineurium, and each single axon is wrapped by endoneurium.","notes":["The periosteum is the connective tissue membrane covering the outer surface of a bone. Its peri- prefix makes it look like a nerve wrapping, but it would be the answer only for a question about bone.","The endoneurium is the innermost wrapping, around each individual axon. Pick it if the question asked about the layer around one nerve fiber.","Correct. The epineurium is the outer sheath around the entire nerve.","The perineurium is the middle wrapping, around each fascicle (bundle of axons). Pick it if the question asked about the layer around one bundle inside the nerve."],"lt":"t-pns","lid":"c3","comp":187,"competencyId":"w8-nerve-structure"},{"id":"L02973","dok":1,"q":"Which connective tissue wrapping surrounds each individual axon within a nerve?","a":"The endoneurium.","options":["The epineurium","The perineurium","The endoneurium","The endomysium"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The endoneurium is the innermost wrapping, a delicate layer of connective tissue around each individual axon in a nerve. Endo- means within, so it is the wrapping deepest inside the nerve. Groups of these wrapped axons form fascicles bound by perineurium, and the whole nerve is enclosed by epineurium.","notes":["The epineurium is the outermost layer around the entire nerve. Pick it for the tough outer sheath.","The perineurium surrounds a fascicle, a whole bundle of axons, not one axon. Pick it for the middle layer of the nerve.","Correct. The endoneurium wraps each single axon.","The endomysium is the connective tissue around each individual skeletal muscle fiber. It is the muscle version of the same idea, so pick it if the question asked about the wrapping of one muscle cell."],"lt":"t-pns","lid":"c4","comp":187,"competencyId":"w8-nerve-structure"},{"id":"L02978","dok":1,"q":"What is the name for a nerve that carries both sensory and motor axons?","a":"A mixed nerve.","options":["A motor nerve","A sensory nerve","A mixed nerve","A nerve tract"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"A mixed nerve carries both sensory axons, which bring signals in toward the central nervous system, and motor axons, which carry commands out to muscles and glands. All 31 pairs of spinal nerves are mixed, and four cranial nerves (V, VII, IX, and X) are mixed as well. Nerves are classed as sensory, motor, or mixed by the kinds of axons they carry.","notes":["A motor nerve carries mainly motor signals out to muscles, as cranial nerves III, IV, VI, XI, and XII do. Pick it for a nerve such as the hypoglossal nerve.","A sensory nerve carries only sensory signals into the brain, as cranial nerves I, II, and VIII do. Pick it for a nerve such as the olfactory nerve.","Correct. A nerve carrying both kinds of axons is a mixed nerve.","A tract is a bundle of axons inside the brain or spinal cord, not a peripheral nerve. Pick it for a pathway in the white matter of the cord."],"lt":"t-pns","lid":"c9","comp":190,"competencyId":"w8-cranial-nerves"},{"id":"L02979","dok":1,"q":"What term names the area of skin supplied by a single spinal nerve?","a":"A dermatome.","options":["A ramus","A myotome","A plexus","A dermatome"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"A dermatome is the strip of skin whose sensation is carried by the sensory axons of a single spinal nerve. Derma- means skin, and the strips line up in bands down the body in the same order as the spinal nerves. Clinicians use dermatome maps to work out which spinal nerve is involved from where a patient feels numbness or pain.","notes":["A ramus is a branch of a spinal nerve just past the intervertebral foramen. Pick it for the branches that split off a spinal nerve.","A myotome is the group of muscles supplied by a single spinal nerve. Pick it if the question were about muscles rather than skin.","A plexus is a network formed by the anterior rami of several spinal nerves, such as the brachial plexus. Pick it for a network of nerves, not an area of skin.","Correct. The patch of skin served by one spinal nerve is a dermatome."],"lt":"t-pns","lid":"c10","comp":188,"competencyId":"w8-spinal-nerves-rami"},{"id":"L02980","dok":1,"q":"How many pairs of cervical spinal nerves are there?","a":"Eight pairs.","options":["Eight pairs","Seven pairs","Twelve pairs","Five pairs"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"There are eight pairs of cervical spinal nerves, even though there are only seven cervical vertebrae. The first cervical nerve exits above the atlas, between the skull and C1, so the cervical nerves exit above their matching vertebrae until the eighth, which exits below C7. From the thoracic region down, each nerve exits below the vertebra of the same number.","notes":["Correct. There are eight pairs of cervical nerves.","Seven is the number of cervical vertebrae, not cervical nerves. This is the most common mix-up, because students expect one nerve per vertebra.","Twelve is the number of thoracic spinal nerve pairs, matching the twelve thoracic vertebrae. Pick it if the question asked about the thoracic region.","Five is the number of lumbar pairs and also of sacral pairs. Pick it if the question asked about either of those regions."],"lt":"t-pns","lid":"c11","comp":188,"competencyId":"w8-spinal-nerves-rami"},{"id":"L02983","dok":1,"q":"Which connective tissue wrapping surrounds a fascicle, a bundle of axons within a nerve?","a":"The perineurium.","options":["The endoneurium","The perineurium","The perimysium","The epineurium"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The perineurium is the middle wrapping of a nerve, a layer of connective tissue around each fascicle. Peri- means around, and this layer surrounds a bundle of axons rather than a single axon or the whole nerve. It sits between the endoneurium inside and the epineurium outside.","notes":["The endoneurium wraps each single axon, the smallest level. Pick it if the question asked about one nerve fiber.","Correct. The perineurium wraps each fascicle.","The perimysium wraps a fascicle of skeletal muscle fibers, not nerve fibers. The two terms share a prefix and the same idea, so pick perimysium only for a question about muscle.","The epineurium wraps the entire nerve. Pick it for the tough outer sheath around all the fascicles together."],"lt":"t-pns","lid":"c14","comp":187,"competencyId":"w8-nerve-structure"},{"id":"L02984","dok":2,"q":"The cervical plexus is formed mainly from the anterior rami of which spinal nerves?","a":"C1 to C4.","options":["L1 to L4","C5 to T1","L4 to S4","C1 to C4"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The cervical plexus is built from the anterior rami of C1 to C4, with a small contribution from C5. It lies deep in the neck and serves the skin and muscles of the neck and upper shoulders. Its best-known branch is the phrenic nerve, which travels down to the diaphragm.","notes":["L1 to L4 form the lumbar plexus in the lower back. Pick it for the plexus serving the lower abdominal wall and part of the lower limb.","C5 to T1 form the brachial plexus, just below the cervical plexus. Pick it for the plexus that serves the shoulder and the whole upper limb.","L4 to S4 form the sacral plexus. Pick it for the plexus serving the buttock, perineum, and most of the lower limb.","Correct. The cervical plexus comes mainly from C1 to C4."],"lt":"t-pns","lid":"c15","comp":191,"competencyId":"w8-nerve-plexuses"},{"id":"L02985","dok":2,"q":"The brachial plexus is formed from the anterior rami of which spinal nerves?","a":"C5 to T1.","options":["C5 to T1","L4 to S4","L1 to L4","C1 to C4"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The brachial plexus forms from the anterior rami of C5, C6, C7, C8, and T1. These rami merge and sort in the lower neck and axilla (armpit) into the nerves of the upper limb. Because the arm sits at the level of the lower neck and upper chest, its nerves come from that part of the cord.","notes":["Correct. The brachial plexus comes from C5 to T1.","L4 to S4 form the sacral plexus, which serves most of the lower limb. Students mixing up the upper and lower limb plexuses pick it.","L1 to L4 form the lumbar plexus, which serves the lower abdominal wall and the front and inner thigh. Pick it for that region.","C1 to C4 form the cervical plexus, which serves the neck and gives off the phrenic nerve. Pick it for the plexus just above the brachial plexus."],"lt":"t-pns","lid":"c16","comp":191,"competencyId":"w8-nerve-plexuses"},{"id":"L02986","dok":2,"q":"The phrenic nerve, which supplies the diaphragm, arises from which plexus?","a":"The cervical plexus.","options":["The sacral plexus","The cervical plexus","The brachial plexus","The lumbar plexus"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The phrenic nerve arises from the cervical plexus, mainly from C3, C4, and C5, and runs down through the thorax to the diaphragm. It is the sole motor supply to the diaphragm, the main muscle of breathing. The teaching phrase C3, 4, 5 keep the diaphragm alive is a reminder that a high neck injury can stop breathing.","notes":["The sacral plexus (L4 to S4) gives rise to the sciatic nerve. Pick it for a nerve running down the back of the thigh.","Correct. The phrenic nerve leaves the cervical plexus and descends to the diaphragm.","The brachial plexus (C5 to T1) gives rise to the nerves of the upper limb, such as the median, ulnar, and radial nerves. Pick it for a nerve running down the arm.","The lumbar plexus (L1 to L4) gives rise to nerves such as the femoral nerve to the front of the thigh. Pick it for a nerve of the lower abdominal wall or anterior thigh."],"lt":"t-pns","lid":"c17","comp":191,"competencyId":"w8-nerve-plexuses"},{"id":"L02987","dok":2,"q":"The sciatic nerve, the largest nerve in the body, arises from which plexus?","a":"The sacral plexus.","options":["The lumbar plexus","The cervical plexus","The sacral plexus","The brachial plexus"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The sciatic nerve is the largest branch of the sacral plexus, which forms from the anterior rami of L4 to S4. It leaves the pelvis deep to the gluteal muscles and runs down the back of the thigh, where it divides into the tibial and common fibular nerves. The sacral plexus serves the buttock, the perineum, and most of the lower limb.","notes":["The lumbar plexus sits just above the sacral plexus and also serves the lower limb, which makes it a tempting choice. Pick it for the femoral or obturator nerve to the front and inner thigh.","The cervical plexus serves the neck and gives off the phrenic nerve. Pick it for the nerve to the diaphragm.","Correct. The sciatic nerve comes from the sacral plexus.","The brachial plexus serves the upper limb. Pick it for nerves such as the median or ulnar nerve."],"lt":"t-pns","lid":"c18","comp":191,"competencyId":"w8-nerve-plexuses"},{"id":"L02988","dok":2,"q":"Just after a spinal nerve leaves the vertebral column through the intervertebral foramen, it splits into branches. What are these branches called?","a":"Rami.","options":["Rami","Roots","Fascicles","Ganglia"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Just past the intervertebral foramen, each spinal nerve divides into branches called rami (singular ramus). The posterior ramus supplies the deep back muscles and the skin of the back, and the anterior ramus supplies the limbs and the front and sides of the trunk. Smaller branches include the meningeal branch and the rami communicantes, which link to the sympathetic trunk.","notes":["Correct. A spinal nerve splits into rami just past the intervertebral foramen.","The roots (dorsal and ventral) attach the spinal nerve to the cord on the inside, before the nerve forms. Pick roots for the attachments to the cord, not the branches outside the column.","Fascicles are bundles of axons inside a nerve, wrapped by perineurium. Pick them for the internal bundles of a nerve, not its branches.","Ganglia are clusters of neuron cell bodies outside the CNS, such as the dorsal root ganglion on the dorsal root. Pick them for a swelling of cell bodies, not a branch."],"lt":"t-pns","lid":"c19","comp":188,"competencyId":"w8-spinal-nerves-rami"},{"id":"L02993","dok":2,"q":"Spinal nerves regroup into networks called plexuses. Which part of the spinal nerves forms these plexuses?","a":"The ventral rami.","options":["The ventral roots","The dorsal roots","The ventral rami","The dorsal rami"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The ventral (anterior) rami of spinal nerves join and regroup into plexuses before their axons travel on to the body. This regrouping lets each region receive axons from several spinal nerves, so damage to one spinal nerve rarely erases all function in an area. The ventral rami of T2 to T12 are the exception; they run straight to the thoracic wall as the intercostal nerves.","notes":["The ventral roots are motor roots that leave the cord. They join the dorsal roots to make the spinal nerve, which only later splits into rami, so the roots cannot form plexuses.","The dorsal roots are sensory roots that enter the cord inside the vertebral canal, before the spinal nerve even forms. Pick them for the sensory attachment to the cord.","Correct. The ventral rami form the plexuses.","The dorsal rami supply the deep back muscles and the skin of the back, and they do not form plexuses. Pick them for the branch that serves the back of the trunk."],"lt":"t-pns","lid":"c24","comp":188,"competencyId":"w8-spinal-nerves-rami"},{"id":"L02996","dok":2,"q":"Which nerve plexus serves the shoulder and the entire upper limb?","a":"The brachial plexus.","options":["The cervical plexus","The sacral plexus","The lumbar plexus","The brachial plexus"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The brachial plexus serves the shoulder and the entire upper limb. It forms from the anterior rami of C5 to T1 in the lower neck and passes through the axilla, giving rise to the nerves of the arm, forearm, and hand. Brachial means arm, which ties the name to its territory.","notes":["The cervical plexus serves the neck and upper shoulders and gives off the phrenic nerve. Pick it for the neck or the diaphragm, not the whole limb.","The sacral plexus serves the buttock, the back of the thigh, and the leg and foot. It is the lower limb counterpart of the brachial plexus.","The lumbar plexus serves the lower abdominal wall and the front and inner thigh. Pick it for those regions of the lower body.","Correct. The brachial plexus serves the shoulder and the whole upper limb."],"lt":"t-pns","lid":"c27","comp":191,"competencyId":"w8-nerve-plexuses"},{"id":"L02997","dok":2,"q":"What does the epineurium surround?","a":"The entire nerve.","options":["A single axon","A single fascicle","A muscle fascicle","The entire nerve"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The epineurium surrounds the entire nerve, enclosing all of its fascicles along with the small blood vessels that supply the nerve. It is the outermost and toughest of the three nerve wrappings. Together with the perineurium, it holds a cut nerve's shape so a surgeon can line up the fascicles during repair.","notes":["A single axon is wrapped by the endoneurium, the innermost layer. Pick it if the question asked about the endoneurium.","A single fascicle is wrapped by the perineurium, the middle layer. Pick it if the question asked about the perineurium.","A fascicle of muscle fibers is wrapped by the perimysium, which belongs to a muscle, not a nerve. Students mix up the nerve and muscle wrappings because both use the same prefixes.","Correct. The epineurium is the outer sheath around the entire nerve."],"lt":"t-pns","lid":"c28","comp":187,"competencyId":"w8-nerve-structure"},{"id":"L02999","dok":2,"q":"A plexus forms from spinal nerves L1 to L4 and serves the lower abdominal wall and the front of the thigh. Which plexus is it?","a":"The lumbar plexus.","options":["The brachial plexus","The cervical plexus","The sacral plexus","The lumbar plexus"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The lumbar plexus forms from the anterior rami of L1 to L4 in the lower back. It serves the anterolateral abdominal wall, the external genitals, and part of the lower limb, mainly the front and inner thigh. Damage here causes weakness and sensory loss of the lower abdominal wall and the front and inner surfaces of the thigh.","notes":["The brachial plexus forms from C5 to T1 and serves the shoulder and the whole upper limb. Pick it for the arm, not the thigh.","The cervical plexus forms from C1 to C4 and serves the neck and upper shoulders. Pick it for the plexus that gives off the phrenic nerve.","The sacral plexus forms from L4 to S4 and serves the buttock, the back of the thigh, and the leg and foot. It overlaps the lumbar plexus at L4, which is why the two are easy to confuse.","Correct. L1 to L4 build the lumbar plexus."],"lt":"t-pns","lid":"c30","comp":191,"competencyId":"w8-nerve-plexuses"},{"id":"L03001","dok":2,"q":"A receptor is a bare dendrite tip with no connective tissue covering, and it detects pain and temperature. What is it?","a":"A free nerve ending.","options":["A lamellar (Pacinian) corpuscle","A tactile (Meissner) corpuscle","A free nerve ending","A muscle spindle"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"A free nerve ending is the simplest sensory receptor, a bare dendrite tip with no capsule around it. Free nerve endings are found in the epithelium and connective tissue throughout the body. They detect pain, temperature, and some touch.","notes":["A lamellar (Pacinian) corpuscle is an encapsulated receptor with a layered capsule in the deep dermis and hypodermis. Pick it for deep pressure and vibration.","A tactile (Meissner) corpuscle is an encapsulated receptor in the dermal papillae of hairless skin. Pick it for fine, discriminative touch.","Correct. A bare dendrite tip that senses pain and temperature is a free nerve ending.","A muscle spindle is a stretch receptor inside skeletal muscle. Pick it for detecting how far a muscle is stretched."],"lt":"t-pns","lid":"c32","comp":189,"competencyId":"w8-sensory-receptors"},{"id":"L03002","dok":2,"q":"The endoneurium of a nerve matches which wrapping in a skeletal muscle?","a":"The endomysium.","options":["The epimysium","The endomysium","The perimysium","The sarcolemma"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The endoneurium wraps each single axon in a nerve, and the endomysium wraps each single muscle fiber in a skeletal muscle. Nerves and muscles are organized the same way, with one wrapping around each fiber, another around each fascicle, and an outer one around the whole organ. The shared prefixes line them up: endo- for one fiber, peri- for a fascicle, and epi- for the whole organ.","notes":["The epimysium wraps the whole muscle, so it matches the epineurium of a nerve. Pick it if the question started from the epineurium.","Correct. Both the endoneurium and the endomysium wrap a single fiber.","The perimysium wraps a fascicle of muscle fibers, so it matches the perineurium of a nerve. Pick it if the question started from the perineurium.","The sarcolemma is the plasma membrane of a muscle fiber, part of the cell itself rather than a connective tissue wrapping. Pick it for the cell membrane of a muscle fiber."],"lt":"t-pns","lid":"c33","comp":187,"competencyId":"w8-nerve-structure"},{"id":"L03003","dok":2,"q":"A sensory nerve ending is wrapped in a connective tissue capsule and detects touch and pressure. What is its structural class?","a":"An encapsulated receptor.","options":["A nociceptor","A free nerve ending","An interoceptor","An encapsulated receptor"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Encapsulated receptors are nerve endings wrapped in a connective tissue capsule. Most of them are mechanoreceptors for touch, pressure, vibration, or stretch, such as the tactile (Meissner) corpuscles and lamellar (Pacinian) corpuscles. Structure is one of three ways receptors are classified, along with stimulus type and location.","notes":["A nociceptor is classified by stimulus type; it responds to potentially damaging stimuli and produces pain. Pick it for pain receptors, most of which are free nerve endings.","A free nerve ending is the other structural class, a bare dendrite tip with no capsule. Pick it for receptors of pain and temperature.","An interoceptor is classified by location, not structure; it detects stimuli inside the body, in the organs and blood vessels. Pick it for a receptor in the wall of an organ.","Correct. A capsule of connective tissue makes it an encapsulated receptor."],"lt":"t-pns","lid":"c34","comp":189,"competencyId":"w8-sensory-receptors"},{"id":"L03005","dok":3,"q":"An injury to the cervical plexus on one side also damages the phrenic nerve. Which structure is most directly weakened?","a":"The diaphragm on that side.","options":["The muscles of the upper limb","The muscles of the lower abdominal wall","The diaphragm on that side","The tongue on that side"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The phrenic nerve leaves the cervical plexus, which forms mainly from C1 to C4, and it is the sole motor supply to the diaphragm. If it is damaged on one side, the diaphragm on that side weakens and breathing is affected. Other cervical plexus damage shows up as weakness or numbness of the neck and shoulder.","notes":["Weakness across the shoulder and whole upper limb points to the brachial plexus (C5 to T1). Pick it for an injury in the lower neck or axilla.","Weakness of the lower abdominal wall points to the lumbar plexus (L1 to L4). Pick it for an injury in the lower back.","Correct. The phrenic nerve is the motor supply to the diaphragm.","The tongue is moved by the hypoglossal nerve, a cranial nerve, not by a spinal nerve plexus. Pick it for damage to cranial nerve XII."],"lt":"t-pns","lid":"c36","comp":191,"competencyId":"w8-nerve-plexuses"},{"id":"L03007","dok":3,"q":"A virus that has been dormant in one dorsal root ganglion reactivates and causes a painful rash in a band of skin along one dermatome. Which disorder is it?","a":"Shingles.","options":["Carpal tunnel syndrome","Peripheral neuropathy","Bell palsy","Shingles"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Shingles is caused by the varicella zoster virus, the chickenpox virus, which can hide in a dorsal root ganglion for decades. When it reactivates, it travels along the sensory axons of that one spinal nerve, so the rash follows the dermatome that ganglion serves. This is why a shingles rash usually forms a band on one side of the body.","notes":["Carpal tunnel syndrome is compression of the median nerve at the wrist, causing numbness in the hand. Pick it for a wrist problem with no rash.","Peripheral neuropathy is damage to many peripheral nerves, often causing numbness in both hands and feet, as in long-term diabetes. Pick it for widespread, symmetrical numbness rather than one band.","Bell palsy is a sudden weakness of the facial nerve (VII) that droops one side of the face, with no dermatome rash. Pick it for facial paralysis.","Correct. A rash following one dermatome from a reactivated virus is shingles."],"lt":"t-pns","lid":"c38","comp":185,"competencyId":"w8-cord-roots"},{"id":"L03009","dok":2,"q":"Which choice lists the three connective tissue wrappings of a nerve from the smallest scale to the largest?","a":"Endoneurium, then perineurium, then epineurium.","options":["Endoneurium, perineurium, epineurium","Perineurium, endoneurium, epineurium","Epineurium, perineurium, endoneurium","Endoneurium, epineurium, perineurium"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"From the smallest scale to the largest, the wrappings are the endoneurium around each axon, the perineurium around each fascicle, and the epineurium around the whole nerve. The prefixes give the order: endo- means within, peri- means around, and epi- means upon or over. Each wrapping is named for the level it surrounds.","notes":["Correct. Axon, then fascicle, then whole nerve: endoneurium, perineurium, epineurium.","This starts with the fascicle layer and puts the axon layer second. It treats the perineurium as the innermost layer, which it is not.","This is the correct order run backward, from largest to smallest. Pick it if the question asked for the order from the outside in.","This puts the epineurium in the middle. It comes from mixing up epi- (outermost) with peri- (around a fascicle)."],"lt":"t-pns","lid":"c40","comp":187,"competencyId":"w8-nerve-structure"},{"id":"L03010","dok":2,"q":"Which choice lists the spinal nerve regions in order from the neck down toward the tailbone?","a":"Cervical, thoracic, lumbar, sacral, coccygeal.","options":["Cervical, thoracic, sacral, lumbar, coccygeal","Cervical, lumbar, thoracic, sacral, coccygeal","Thoracic, cervical, lumbar, sacral, coccygeal","Cervical, thoracic, lumbar, sacral, coccygeal"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The spinal nerves are named for the region of the vertebral column where they leave, so they follow the same order as the column: cervical (8 pairs), thoracic (12), lumbar (5), sacral (5), and coccygeal (1). The neck comes first, then the chest, the lower back, the sacrum, and finally the tailbone. Knowing the order of the vertebral regions gives you the order of the nerve regions.","notes":["This puts the sacral region above the lumbar region. The sacrum sits below the lumbar vertebrae and joins the hip bones.","This puts the lumbar region above the thoracic region. The lumbar vertebrae are in the lower back, below the rib-bearing thoracic vertebrae.","This puts the thoracic (chest) region above the cervical (neck) region. The neck is the top of the column, so cervical always comes first.","Correct. Neck, chest, lower back, sacrum, tailbone."],"lt":"t-pns","lid":"c41","comp":188,"competencyId":"w8-spinal-nerves-rami"},{"id":"L03011","dok":3,"q":"Which choice correctly names the plexus formed from C5 to T1, one nerve that comes from it, and the region that nerve serves?","a":"Brachial plexus, median nerve, upper limb.","options":["Sacral plexus, median nerve, upper limb","Cervical plexus, phrenic nerve, upper limb","Brachial plexus, median nerve, upper limb","Brachial plexus, sciatic nerve, lower limb"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The anterior rami of C5 to T1 form the brachial plexus, which serves the shoulder and the entire upper limb. One of its major nerves is the median nerve, which runs down the front of the arm and forearm and passes through the carpal tunnel into the hand. Every part of the correct answer belongs to the upper limb, which is how the pieces fit together.","notes":["The sacral plexus forms from L4 to S4 and serves the lower limb, so it cannot give rise to the median nerve. Pick the sacral plexus for the sciatic nerve.","The phrenic nerve does come from the cervical plexus, but it serves the diaphragm, not the upper limb, and the cervical plexus forms from C1 to C4. Pick this plexus and nerve for breathing.","Correct. C5 to T1 form the brachial plexus, which gives off the median nerve to the upper limb.","The sciatic nerve belongs to the sacral plexus and serves the lower limb. Only the plexus name is right here, which is a common half-remembered pairing."],"lt":"t-pns","lid":"c42","comp":191,"competencyId":"w8-nerve-plexuses"},{"id":"L03012","dok":3,"q":"Which choice correctly names the plexus formed from L4 to S4, its largest nerve, and the region that nerve serves?","a":"Sacral plexus, sciatic nerve, back of the thigh and leg.","options":["Sacral plexus, sciatic nerve, back of thigh and leg","Lumbar plexus, sciatic nerve, back of thigh and leg","Sacral plexus, phrenic nerve, diaphragm","Sacral plexus, median nerve, hand and forearm"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The anterior rami of L4 to S4 form the sacral plexus, which serves the buttock, the perineum, and most of the lower limb. Its largest branch is the sciatic nerve, which runs down the back of the thigh and divides to supply the leg and foot. Damage to the sacral plexus causes weakness and sensory loss in the buttock, the back of the thigh, and the leg and foot.","notes":["Correct. L4 to S4 form the sacral plexus, and its sciatic nerve runs down the back of the thigh into the leg.","The lumbar plexus forms from L1 to L4, and its main nerves (femoral and obturator) serve the front and inner thigh. The sciatic nerve does not come from it.","The phrenic nerve comes from the cervical plexus and serves the diaphragm. Only the plexus name is right here; pick the phrenic nerve for breathing.","The median nerve comes from the brachial plexus and serves the forearm and hand. Pick it for the upper limb, not the sacral plexus."],"lt":"t-pns","lid":"c43","comp":191,"competencyId":"w8-nerve-plexuses"},{"id":"L03013","dok":2,"q":"Which choice traces the parts of a spinal nerve in order, from its attachments to the cord out to its branches beyond the vertebral column?","a":"Dorsal and ventral roots, then the spinal nerve, then the rami.","options":["Dorsal and ventral roots, then the spinal nerve, then the rami","Rami, then the spinal nerve, then the dorsal and ventral roots","Dorsal and ventral roots, then the rami, then the spinal nerve","The spinal nerve, then the dorsal and ventral roots, then the rami"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"A spinal nerve starts as two roots attached to the cord: the dorsal root, which is sensory, and the ventral root, which is motor. The two roots join to form the spinal nerve itself, which passes out through the intervertebral foramen. Just outside the foramen, the spinal nerve splits into its rami.","notes":["Correct. Roots join to form the spinal nerve, which then splits into rami.","This is the correct sequence run backward, from the branches in toward the cord. Pick it if the question asked you to trace from the body inward.","This puts the rami before the spinal nerve. The rami are branches of the finished spinal nerve, so they cannot come before it.","This puts the spinal nerve before the roots, but the nerve is formed by the roots joining, so the roots must come first."],"lt":"t-pns","lid":"c44","comp":188,"competencyId":"w8-spinal-nerves-rami"},{"id":"L03017","dok":2,"q":"A cross section of a nerve shows several round bundles inside the thick outer layer, each outlined by its own thin ring of connective tissue. What are the bundles and their ring called?","a":"Fascicles wrapped by perineurium.","options":["Fascicles wrapped by endoneurium","Fascicles wrapped by perineurium","Axons wrapped by epineurium","Ganglia wrapped by perineurium"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"On a nerve cross section, the thick outer ring is the epineurium. The round bundles inside it are fascicles, and each one is outlined by a thin ring of perineurium. The tiny circles packed inside a fascicle are single axons, each surrounded by endoneurium.","notes":["The endoneurium is the thin wrapping around each single axon inside a fascicle. Pick it for the delicate layer between individual fibers.","Correct. The round bundles are fascicles, and the ring around each is the perineurium.","Single axons are the tiny circles inside each bundle, not the bundles themselves, and the epineurium surrounds the whole nerve, not each bundle. Pick the epineurium for the thick outer ring of the section.","Ganglia are clusters of neuron cell bodies, not bundles inside a nerve. Pick a ganglion for a swelling such as the dorsal root ganglion."],"lt":"t-pns","lid":"c3-x2","comp":187,"competencyId":"w8-nerve-structure"},{"id":"L03019","dok":1,"q":"What is a bundle of axons grouped together within a nerve called?","a":"A fascicle.","options":["A ramus","A fascicle","A tract","A ganglion"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"A fascicle is a bundle of axons grouped together inside a nerve and wrapped by perineurium. A nerve is built from many fascicles held together by the epineurium. The same word is used in muscle, where a fascicle is a bundle of muscle fibers.","notes":["A ramus is a branch of a spinal nerve just past the intervertebral foramen. Pick it for a branch, not a bundle within the nerve.","Correct. A bundle of axons inside a nerve is a fascicle.","A tract is a bundle of axons inside the brain or spinal cord that share an origin and destination. Pick it for a bundle in the white matter of the CNS.","A ganglion is a cluster of neuron cell bodies outside the CNS. Pick it for a swelling of cell bodies, such as the dorsal root ganglion."],"lt":"t-pns","lid":"c5-x2","comp":187,"competencyId":"w8-nerve-structure"},{"id":"L03020","dok":1,"q":"How many pairs of thoracic spinal nerves are there?","a":"Twelve pairs.","options":["Five pairs","Seven pairs","Eight pairs","Twelve pairs"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"There are twelve pairs of thoracic spinal nerves, one exiting below each of the twelve thoracic vertebrae. The thoracic region has more pairs than any other region. Most of their anterior rami run between the ribs as intercostal nerves instead of joining a plexus.","notes":["Five is the number of lumbar pairs and also of sacral pairs. Pick it for either of those regions.","Seven is the number of cervical vertebrae. It comes from mixing up vertebrae and nerves, and it does not match any thoracic count.","Eight is the number of cervical nerve pairs. Pick it if the question asked about the neck region.","Correct. There are twelve pairs of thoracic nerves."],"lt":"t-pns","lid":"c8-x2","comp":188,"competencyId":"w8-spinal-nerves-rami"},{"id":"L03021","dok":1,"q":"Which kinds of axons does every spinal nerve carry?","a":"Both sensory and motor axons.","options":["Only sensory axons, entering the cord","Only autonomic axons to the organs","Sensory and motor axons","Only motor axons, leaving the cord"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Every spinal nerve is a mixed nerve, carrying both sensory and motor axons. The sensory axons arrive through the dorsal root and the motor axons leave through the ventral root, and the two roots join to form one spinal nerve. From that point on, the nerve and its main branches carry both kinds of axons.","notes":["Only sensory axons describes the dorsal root, before it joins the ventral root. Pick it for the dorsal root, not the spinal nerve.","Autonomic axons do travel in spinal nerves, but alongside sensory and somatic motor axons, never alone. Pick autonomic fibers for the rami communicantes that link to the sympathetic trunk.","Correct. All 31 pairs of spinal nerves are mixed.","Only motor axons describes the ventral root, before it joins the dorsal root. Pick it for the ventral root, not the spinal nerve."],"lt":"t-pns","lid":"c9-x2","comp":188,"competencyId":"w8-spinal-nerves-rami"},{"id":"L03022","dok":1,"q":"Which branch of a spinal nerve supplies the deep muscles and skin of the back of the trunk?","a":"The posterior (dorsal) ramus.","options":["The meningeal branch","The rami communicantes","The posterior ramus","The anterior ramus"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The posterior (dorsal) ramus supplies the deep muscles of the back and the skin over the posterior trunk. It is the smaller of the two main rami. Because its territory is the back, a problem in a posterior ramus stays in the back and does not reach the limbs.","notes":["The meningeal branch re-enters the vertebral canal to supply the vertebrae, ligaments, blood vessels of the cord, and meninges. Pick it for structures inside the vertebral canal.","The rami communicantes are small branches that carry autonomic fibers to and from the sympathetic trunk. Pick them for the link between a spinal nerve and the sympathetic chain.","Correct. The posterior ramus serves the deep back muscles and the skin of the back.","The anterior ramus supplies the limbs and the front and sides of the trunk, and it forms the plexuses. Pick it for the limbs."],"lt":"t-pns","lid":"c10-x2","comp":188,"competencyId":"w8-spinal-nerves-rami"},{"id":"L03023","dok":2,"q":"The anterior rami of which spinal nerves do not join a plexus but run straight to the thoracic wall?","a":"T2 to T12.","options":["T2 to T12","C1 to C4","L1 to L4","C5 to T1"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The anterior rami of T2 to T12 do not form a plexus. Instead, they run between the ribs as the intercostal nerves (T12, below the last rib, is called the subcostal nerve). They supply the intercostal muscles, the muscles of the abdominal wall, and the skin of the chest and abdomen. T1 joins the brachial plexus, and every other region also sends its anterior rami into a plexus.","notes":["Correct. T2 to T12 run straight to the thoracic wall as intercostal nerves.","C1 to C4 form the cervical plexus, which serves the neck and gives off the phrenic nerve. Pick it for the neck.","L1 to L4 form the lumbar plexus, which serves the lower abdominal wall and the front and inner thigh. Pick it for that plexus.","C5 to T1 form the brachial plexus. T1 joins this plexus, which is why the intercostal list starts at T2."],"lt":"t-pns","lid":"c11-x2","comp":188,"competencyId":"w8-spinal-nerves-rami"},{"id":"L03024","dok":1,"q":"Which receptors respond to mechanical force such as touch, pressure, and stretch?","a":"Mechanoreceptors.","options":["Chemoreceptors","Mechanoreceptors","Thermoreceptors","Photoreceptors"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Mechanoreceptors respond to mechanical force such as touch, pressure, vibration, and stretch. They include the tactile corpuscles of the skin, the lamellar corpuscles that sense vibration, and the muscle spindles that sense stretch. Classifying by stimulus type asks what kind of change the receptor answers to.","notes":["Chemoreceptors respond to chemicals dissolved in solution, such as smell and taste molecules. Pick them for taste buds or the olfactory epithelium.","Correct. Touch, pressure, and stretch are mechanical forces, sensed by mechanoreceptors.","Thermoreceptors respond to changes in temperature. Pick them for sensing heat or cold.","Photoreceptors respond to light and are found in the retina of the eye. Pick them for vision."],"lt":"t-pns","lid":"c12-x2","comp":189,"competencyId":"w8-sensory-receptors"},{"id":"L03025","dok":1,"q":"Which receptors detect the position and movement of the body from within the muscles, tendons, and joints?","a":"Proprioceptors.","options":["Exteroceptors","Proprioceptors","Interoceptors","Nociceptors"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Proprioceptors detect the position and movement of the body. They sit in the skeletal muscles, tendons, and joint capsules, and include muscle spindles, tendon organs, and joint kinesthetic receptors. Proprio- means one's own, so these receptors report on the body's own position.","notes":["Exteroceptors detect stimuli at or near the body surface, such as touch, temperature, and the special senses. Pick them for receptors in the skin.","Correct. Receptors in muscles, tendons, and joints that sense body position are proprioceptors.","Interoceptors (also called visceroceptors) detect stimuli inside the body, in the organs and blood vessels. Pick them for receptors in the wall of the stomach or an artery.","Nociceptors are classified by stimulus, not location; they respond to potentially damaging stimuli and produce pain. Pick them for pain receptors."],"lt":"t-pns","lid":"c13-x2","comp":189,"competencyId":"w8-sensory-receptors"},{"id":"L03026","dok":1,"q":"Where in the body are free nerve endings found?","a":"In most body tissues.","options":["Only within skeletal muscles","Only in the retina","In most body tissues","Only in the dermal papillae of hairless skin"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Free nerve endings are bare dendrite tips found in the epithelium and connective tissue throughout the body. Because they are nearly everywhere, almost any tissue can signal pain and temperature. They are the simplest receptors, with no connective tissue capsule around them.","notes":["Muscle spindles sit within skeletal muscles and detect stretch. Pick this location for muscle spindles, not free nerve endings.","The retina holds photoreceptors, the receptors for light. Pick the retina if the question asked where photoreceptors are found.","Correct. Free nerve endings are spread through most tissues of the body.","The dermal papillae of hairless skin hold tactile (Meissner) corpuscles, the receptors for fine touch. Pick this location for those corpuscles."],"lt":"t-pns","lid":"c14-x2","comp":189,"competencyId":"w8-sensory-receptors"},{"id":"L03027","dok":1,"q":"Which receptor in the deep dermis and hypodermis detects deep pressure and vibration?","a":"Lamellar (Pacinian) corpuscles.","options":["Tactile (Merkel) discs","Bulbous (Ruffini) corpuscles","Lamellar (Pacinian) corpuscles","Free nerve endings"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Lamellar (Pacinian) corpuscles are large encapsulated receptors in the deep dermis and hypodermis. Each one has a nerve ending surrounded by many layers of connective tissue, like the layers of an onion, which is where the name lamellar comes from. They detect deep pressure and vibration.","notes":["Tactile (Merkel) discs sit in the deepest layer of the epidermis and detect light touch, not deep pressure. Pick them for light touch near the surface.","Bulbous (Ruffini) corpuscles also sit in the deep dermis, but they detect steady deep pressure and stretching of the skin rather than vibration. Pick them for pressure that lasts.","Correct. Lamellar corpuscles in the deep dermis and hypodermis sense deep pressure and vibration.","Free nerve endings are bare dendrite tips that detect pain, temperature, and some touch, not deep pressure and vibration. Pick them for pain or temperature."],"lt":"t-pns","lid":"c15-x2","comp":189,"competencyId":"w8-sensory-receptors"},{"id":"L03029","dok":2,"q":"A receptor detects chemicals dissolved in solution, such as taste molecules. Which type of receptor is it?","a":"A chemoreceptor.","options":["A photoreceptor","A mechanoreceptor","A chemoreceptor","A thermoreceptor"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Chemoreceptors respond to chemicals in solution. Taste receptors in the taste buds and smell receptors in the olfactory epithelium are both chemoreceptors, because each detects molecules dissolved in saliva or mucus. Other chemoreceptors inside the body monitor the chemistry of the blood.","notes":["A photoreceptor responds to light and is found in the retina. Pick it for vision.","A mechanoreceptor responds to mechanical force such as touch, pressure, vibration, and stretch. Pick it for receptors such as tactile corpuscles or muscle spindles.","Correct. A receptor for dissolved chemicals is a chemoreceptor.","A thermoreceptor responds to changes in temperature. Pick it for sensing heat or cold."],"lt":"t-pns","lid":"c17-x2","comp":189,"competencyId":"w8-sensory-receptors"},{"id":"L03030","dok":2,"q":"A receptor in the deep dermis and the joint capsules senses continuous, steady pressure. Which receptor is it?","a":"Bulbous (Ruffini) corpuscles.","options":["Free nerve endings","Bulbous (Ruffini) corpuscles","Muscle spindles","Tactile (Meissner) corpuscles"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Bulbous (Ruffini) corpuscles are encapsulated receptors in the deep dermis, the hypodermis, and the joint capsules. They respond to steady deep pressure and to stretching of the skin. Lamellar corpuscles sit in the same deep layers but sense vibration and deep pressure, so the kind of stimulus is what tells the two apart.","notes":["Free nerve endings are bare dendrite tips that detect pain, temperature, and some touch, not steady pressure. Pick them for pain.","Correct. Bulbous corpuscles in the deep dermis and joint capsules sense steady pressure.","Muscle spindles lie within skeletal muscles and detect stretch. Pick them for how far a muscle is stretched.","Tactile (Meissner) corpuscles sit in the dermal papillae of hairless skin, close to the surface, and detect fine, discriminative touch. Pick them for the fingertips and lips."],"lt":"t-pns","lid":"c18-x2","comp":189,"competencyId":"w8-sensory-receptors"},{"id":"L03031","dok":2,"q":"Which receptor, found within skeletal muscles, detects the stretch of a muscle?","a":"Muscle spindles.","options":["Tendon organs","Lamellar corpuscles","Joint kinesthetic receptors","Muscle spindles"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Muscle spindles are proprioceptors that lie within skeletal muscles, among the ordinary muscle fibers. They detect the stretch of a muscle, telling the CNS how much the muscle is being lengthened. They are the receptors that start the knee-jerk stretch reflex.","notes":["Tendon organs sit within tendons and detect the tension a contracting muscle places on its tendon. Pick them for pull on the tendon rather than stretch of the muscle.","Lamellar corpuscles sit in the deep dermis and hypodermis and detect deep pressure and vibration. Pick them for vibration.","Joint kinesthetic receptors sit in the capsules of synovial joints and detect joint position and movement. Pick them for the angle of a joint.","Correct. Muscle spindles inside skeletal muscle detect stretch."],"lt":"t-pns","lid":"c19-x2","comp":189,"competencyId":"w8-sensory-receptors"},{"id":"L03032","dok":2,"q":"Where are the stimuli that an exteroceptor detects?","a":"At or near the body surface.","options":["Inside the organs and blood vessels","In the muscles, tendons, and joints","At or near the body surface","Only in the retina of the eye"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Exteroceptors detect stimuli at or near the body surface. They include the receptors for touch, pressure, pain, and temperature in the skin, along with most receptors of the special senses. Extero- means outside, so these receptors report on the world outside the body.","notes":["Stimuli inside the organs and blood vessels are detected by interoceptors, also called visceroceptors. Pick this location for interoceptors.","Stimuli in the muscles, tendons, and joints are detected by proprioceptors, which report body position and movement. Pick this location for proprioceptors.","Correct. Exteroceptors respond to stimuli at or near the surface of the body.","The retina holds photoreceptors, which are named for their stimulus (light). They do count as exteroceptors, but this answer is far too narrow, since exteroceptors are spread across the whole body surface."],"lt":"t-pns","lid":"c20-x2","comp":189,"competencyId":"w8-sensory-receptors"},{"id":"L03033","dok":2,"q":"Which small branches of a spinal nerve carry autonomic fibers to and from the sympathetic trunk?","a":"The rami communicantes.","options":["The rami communicantes","The dorsal roots","The meningeal branches","The posterior rami"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The rami communicantes are small branches that link a spinal nerve to the sympathetic trunk ganglion beside it. The white ramus carries sympathetic axons from the spinal nerve into the ganglion, and the gray ramus carries them back out to the spinal nerve. Communicantes means communicating, which describes their role as links.","notes":["Correct. The rami communicantes link the spinal nerve to the sympathetic trunk.","The dorsal roots are sensory roots attached to the back of the cord, inside the vertebral canal. They connect the nerve to the cord, so they are not branches of the spinal nerve and do not reach the sympathetic trunk.","The meningeal branches re-enter the vertebral canal to supply the vertebrae, ligaments, and meninges. Pick them for structures inside the canal.","The posterior rami supply the deep back muscles and the skin of the back. Pick them for the back of the trunk."],"lt":"t-pns","lid":"c21-x2","comp":188,"competencyId":"w8-spinal-nerves-rami"},{"id":"L03034","dok":2,"q":"Where does a spinal nerve split into its rami?","a":"Just past the intervertebral foramen.","options":["Just past the intervertebral foramen","Within the dorsal root ganglion","Inside the vertebral canal, right beside the cord","At the conus medullaris, where the cord ends"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"A spinal nerve forms where the dorsal and ventral roots join, then passes out of the vertebral canal through the intervertebral foramen. Just past that opening, it splits into its rami. So the roots lie inside the vertebral canal, while the rami lie outside the vertebral column.","notes":["Correct. The spinal nerve splits into rami just outside the intervertebral foramen.","The dorsal root ganglion sits on the dorsal root before the spinal nerve has even formed. Pick it for where the sensory cell bodies are.","Inside the vertebral canal beside the cord is where the dorsal and ventral roots run and join. Pick it for where the roots are, not the rami.","The conus medullaris is the tapered end of the spinal cord near the L1 to L2 disc, with the cauda equina below it. Pick it for where the cord ends."],"lt":"t-pns","lid":"c22-x2","comp":188,"competencyId":"w8-spinal-nerves-rami"},{"id":"L03035","dok":2,"q":"Which of these sensory receptors is an encapsulated receptor?","a":"A lamellar (Pacinian) corpuscle.","options":["A hair follicle receptor","A free nerve ending in the epidermis","A lamellar (Pacinian) corpuscle","A tactile (Merkel) disc"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"A lamellar (Pacinian) corpuscle is an encapsulated receptor: its nerve ending is wrapped in many layers of connective tissue. Other encapsulated receptors include the tactile (Meissner) corpuscles, bulbous (Ruffini) corpuscles, muscle spindles, and tendon organs. Free nerve endings, Merkel discs, and hair follicle receptors are nonencapsulated, meaning their endings have no capsule.","notes":["A hair follicle receptor is a nonencapsulated ending wrapped around a hair follicle that senses hair movement. Pick it for a receptor triggered by bending a hair.","A free nerve ending is the classic nonencapsulated receptor, a bare dendrite tip. Pick it for a receptor with no capsule that senses pain and temperature.","Correct. The lamellar corpuscle has a layered connective tissue capsule.","A tactile (Merkel) disc is a nonencapsulated ending in the deepest layer of the epidermis that senses light touch. Its name sounds like the encapsulated tactile (Meissner) corpuscle, which is the usual mix-up."],"lt":"t-pns","lid":"c23-x2","comp":189,"competencyId":"w8-sensory-receptors"},{"id":"L03036","dok":2,"q":"Which branch of a spinal nerve re-enters the vertebral canal to supply the vertebrae and meninges?","a":"The meningeal branch.","options":["The posterior ramus","The meningeal branch","The anterior ramus","The rami communicantes"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The meningeal branch is a small branch that turns back through the intervertebral foramen into the vertebral canal. It supplies the vertebrae, the ligaments, the blood vessels of the spinal cord, and the meninges. Its name tells you its main target, the membranes around the cord.","notes":["The posterior ramus supplies the deep back muscles and the skin of the back. Pick it for the back of the trunk.","Correct. The meningeal branch returns into the vertebral canal.","The anterior ramus supplies the limbs and the front and sides of the trunk, and it forms the plexuses. Pick it for the limbs.","The rami communicantes carry autonomic fibers to and from the sympathetic trunk. Pick them for the link to the sympathetic chain."],"lt":"t-pns","lid":"c24-x2","comp":188,"competencyId":"w8-spinal-nerves-rami"},{"id":"L03037","dok":2,"q":"Which receptor detects the tension a muscle places on its tendon?","a":"Tendon organs.","options":["Tendon organs","Muscle spindles","Hair follicle receptors","Joint kinesthetic receptors"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Tendon organs are proprioceptors within tendons, near the point where the muscle joins the tendon. They detect the tension a contracting muscle places on its tendon. Muscle spindles report stretch from inside the muscle, while tendon organs report pull from inside the tendon.","notes":["Correct. Tendon organs sense the pull of a muscle on its tendon.","Muscle spindles lie within the muscle itself and detect stretch, not tendon tension. Pick them for how far a muscle is lengthened.","Hair follicle receptors wrap around hair follicles in the skin and detect movement of the hair. Pick them for a light touch that bends a hair.","Joint kinesthetic receptors sit in synovial joint capsules and detect joint position and movement. Pick them for the angle of a joint."],"lt":"t-pns","lid":"c25-x2","comp":189,"competencyId":"w8-sensory-receptors"},{"id":"L03038","dok":3,"q":"A surgeon repairs a cut nerve by lining up its fascicles. Which connective tissue wrappings hold the nerve's shape so the fascicles can be matched?","a":"The epineurium and perineurium.","options":["The myelin sheath and neurilemma","The dura mater and arachnoid","The epineurium and perineurium","The endomysium and perimysium"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The epineurium wraps the whole nerve and the perineurium wraps each fascicle, and these two layers hold a cut nerve's shape. Because each fascicle stays outlined by its perineurium, a surgeon can line up the cut ends and stitch these layers together. This connective tissue framework is part of why peripheral nerves can be repaired.","notes":["The myelin sheath and neurilemma are Schwann cell coverings around individual axons, not connective tissue wrappings. Pick them for the insulating layer around a single axon.","The dura mater and arachnoid are meninges that cover the brain and spinal cord. Pick them for membranes of the CNS, not a peripheral nerve.","Correct. The epineurium and perineurium hold the nerve and its fascicles in shape.","The endomysium and perimysium are the wrappings of a skeletal muscle, around each fiber and each fascicle of muscle. Pick them for repairing a muscle, not a nerve."],"lt":"t-pns","lid":"c26-x2","comp":187,"competencyId":"w8-nerve-structure"},{"id":"L03039","dok":3,"q":"The fingertips sense fine detail far better than the skin of the back. Which difference best explains this?","a":"The fingertips have far more tactile corpuscles and Merkel discs.","options":["Larger dorsal root ganglia for the hand","Thicker myelin on their sensory axons","More tactile corpuscles and Merkel discs","No free nerve endings to compete for signals"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The fingertips are crowded with tactile (Meissner) corpuscles and tactile (Merkel) discs, the receptors for fine, discriminative touch. The more of these receptors packed into an area, the smaller the details that skin can tell apart. The skin of the back has far fewer of them, so it senses touch only crudely.","notes":["A ganglion holds cell bodies, and its size does not set how much detail the skin can feel. The number of receptors in the skin is the deciding factor.","Myelin affects how fast a signal travels, not how finely the skin can tell two points apart. Students reach for myelin whenever a nerve works better, but receptor density is what matters here.","Correct. A dense supply of fine-touch receptors gives the fingertips their sharp sense of detail.","The fingertips do have free nerve endings, which is why they feel pain and temperature. Receptors do not compete with one another, so this explanation is a misconception."],"lt":"t-pns","lid":"c27-x2","comp":189,"competencyId":"w8-sensory-receptors"},{"id":"L03040","dok":3,"q":"A nerve injury weakens the limbs but spares the muscles and skin of the back. Which branches of the spinal nerves were most likely involved?","a":"The anterior rami.","options":["The rami communicantes","The posterior rami","The anterior rami","The meningeal branches"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The anterior rami supply the limbs and the front and sides of the trunk, and they form the plexuses that send nerves into the limbs. An injury to the anterior rami or their plexuses affects the limbs, while the posterior rami, which serve the back, keep working. That is why the back is spared.","notes":["The rami communicantes carry autonomic fibers to and from the sympathetic trunk. Pick them for the link to the sympathetic chain, not limb weakness.","The posterior rami serve the deep back muscles and skin of the back. Damage to them would affect the back, which is exactly what was spared here.","Correct. The anterior rami supply the limbs, so their injury shows up in the limbs.","The meningeal branches supply the vertebrae, ligaments, and meninges inside the vertebral canal. They do not reach the limbs."],"lt":"t-pns","lid":"c28-x2","comp":188,"competencyId":"w8-spinal-nerves-rami"},{"id":"L03041","dok":3,"q":"A patch of skin can still feel pain and temperature but cannot sense fine touch. Which receptors are still working there?","a":"Free nerve endings.","options":["Tactile (Meissner) corpuscles","Free nerve endings","Lamellar corpuscles","Muscle spindles"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Free nerve endings sense pain, temperature, and some touch, so if pain and temperature remain, the free nerve endings are intact. Fine, discriminative touch depends on the tactile (Meissner) corpuscles and the Merkel discs, so those must be the receptors not working. Matching each remaining sensation to its receptor shows which receptors survived.","notes":["Tactile (Meissner) corpuscles carry fine, discriminative touch, which is the sensation that was lost. Pick them for fine touch.","Correct. Pain and temperature come from free nerve endings.","Lamellar corpuscles sense deep pressure and vibration in the deep dermis and hypodermis. The scenario does not test those sensations, so nothing points to them.","Muscle spindles lie within skeletal muscle and sense stretch, not skin sensation. Pick them for proprioception."],"lt":"t-pns","lid":"c29-x2","comp":189,"competencyId":"w8-sensory-receptors"},{"id":"L03042","dok":2,"q":"Which region of the spinal cord gives rise to the most pairs of spinal nerves?","a":"The thoracic region, with 12 pairs.","options":["The sacral region, with 5 pairs","The lumbar region, with 5 pairs","The thoracic region, with 12 pairs","The cervical region, with 8 pairs"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The thoracic region has the most spinal nerve pairs, twelve, one for each thoracic vertebra. The cervical region has eight, the lumbar and sacral regions have five each, and the coccygeal region has one. Together they add up to 31 pairs.","notes":["The sacral region also has 5 pairs. Pick it if the question asked about the nerves arising near the sacrum.","The lumbar region has 5 pairs. Pick it if the question asked about the lower back.","Correct. Twelve thoracic pairs is the largest count of any region.","The cervical region has 8 pairs, more than its 7 vertebrae, which makes it seem large. It still has fewer than the thoracic region."],"lt":"t-pns","lid":"c30-x2","comp":188,"competencyId":"w8-spinal-nerves-rami"},{"id":"L03043","dok":3,"q":"A cut peripheral nerve is more likely to regrow successfully when its connective tissue wrappings are lined up again. Which statement best explains why?","a":"The wrappings give regrowing axons a path back toward their targets.","options":["The wrappings fill the gap with connective tissue that conducts signals","The wrappings give regrowing axons a path back toward their targets","The wrappings hold neuron cell bodies that can build brand-new axons","The wrappings are made of myelin, which speeds up the regrowing fibers"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"A cut peripheral axon can regrow from the part still attached to its cell body, and the neurolemma of its Schwann cells helps it do so. The epineurium and perineurium hold the cut nerve's shape, so a surgeon can stitch the ends back in line. Lined-up fascicles give the regrowing axons a path into the matching part of the far side of the nerve and on toward their targets.","notes":["Connective tissue does not carry nerve signals; only axons do. Scar tissue in the gap actually blocks regrowth, which is why surgeons try to line up the ends closely.","Correct. Aligned wrappings act as guides that lead regrowing axons toward their targets.","Neuron cell bodies sit in ganglia or in the CNS, not in the connective tissue wrappings. This choice confuses a nerve, which is a bundle of axons, with a ganglion.","The wrappings are connective tissue, not myelin. Myelin comes from Schwann cells around individual axons, so pick myelin for the insulating layer, not the nerve wrappings."],"lt":"t-pns","lid":"d3-t-pns-1","comp":187,"competencyId":"w8-nerve-structure"},{"id":"L03044","dok":3,"q":"Every spinal nerve carries sensory and motor axons together. Which statement best explains why this arrangement works well?","a":"One nerve can serve a region for both sensation and movement.","options":["One nerve can serve a region for both sensation and movement","Sensory axons act as insulation for the motor axons beside them","Each axon in a nerve carries signals in both directions","Mixing the axons lets sensory signals pass directly into muscles"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"A mixed nerve carries sensory axons running toward the CNS and motor axons running away from it, side by side within the same connective tissue wrappings. Because one nerve does both jobs, a single route carries sensation from a region and commands back to the muscles of that same region. Each axon still carries its signals in one direction only.","notes":["Correct. Bundling both kinds of axons lets one nerve serve a region for sensation and movement.","Insulation around axons comes from myelin made by Schwann cells, and protection comes from the connective tissue wrappings. Neighboring axons do not insulate each other.","Each axon is either sensory or motor and carries signals one way. The nerve as a whole carries traffic both ways only because it holds both kinds of axons.","Sensory axons end in the CNS, not on muscles. A sensory signal must reach the cord before any motor command goes back out to a muscle."],"lt":"t-pns","lid":"d3-t-pns-2","comp":188,"competencyId":"w8-spinal-nerves-rami"},{"id":"L03045","dok":3,"q":"The sciatic nerve runs a long way down the back of the thigh and is stretched and pressed as the limb moves. Which feature of the nerve protects its axons from this wear?","a":"Its connective tissue wrappings, especially the tough epineurium.","options":["The dura mater, which extends down the limb around the nerve","Its myelin sheaths, which form the outer covering of the nerve","A layer of periosteum that wraps the nerve along the femur","Its connective tissue wrappings, especially the epineurium"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"A nerve is protected by its three connective tissue wrappings, and the outer epineurium is the toughest of them. These layers cushion the delicate axons and let the nerve bend and stretch a little as the limb moves and bears weight. The same wrappings also give a nerve some ability to heal after injury.","notes":["The dura mater is the tough outer meninx around the brain and spinal cord. It does not run down the limb around the sciatic nerve, so it cannot protect the nerve in the thigh.","Myelin sheaths wrap individual axons and insulate them; they do not form the outer covering of a whole nerve. Pick myelin for the insulating layer around one axon.","Periosteum is the membrane covering the surface of a bone such as the femur. It does not wrap nerves.","Correct. The connective tissue wrappings, led by the epineurium, protect the nerve."],"lt":"t-pns","lid":"d3-t-pns-3","comp":187,"competencyId":"w8-nerve-structure"},{"id":"L03047","dok":2,"q":"Which receptors sit in the dermal papillae of hairless skin, such as the fingertips, and detect fine, discriminative touch?","a":"Tactile (Meissner) corpuscles.","options":["Bulbous (Ruffini) corpuscles","Tactile (Meissner) corpuscles","Hair follicle receptors","Lamellar (Pacinian) corpuscles"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Tactile (Meissner) corpuscles are small encapsulated receptors in the dermal papillae of hairless skin. The dermal papillae are the bumps of dermis that push up into the epidermis, which puts these receptors close to the surface. They detect fine, discriminative touch and are packed most densely in the fingertips and lips.","notes":["Bulbous (Ruffini) corpuscles sit in the deep dermis and joint capsules and detect continuous, steady pressure. Pick them for pressure that lasts.","Correct. Tactile (Meissner) corpuscles in the dermal papillae sense fine touch.","Hair follicle receptors wrap around hair follicles, so they cannot be found in hairless skin. Pick them for a touch that bends a hair.","Lamellar (Pacinian) corpuscles sit deep, in the deep dermis and hypodermis, and detect deep pressure and vibration. Pick them for vibration."],"lt":"t-pns","lid":"d3-t-pns-5","comp":189,"competencyId":"w8-sensory-receptors"},{"id":"L03048","dok":2,"q":"A peripheral nerve is often compared to a cable of many wires. Which description of a nerve's structure is accurate?","a":"Many axons grouped into fascicles, all enclosed by an outer epineurium.","options":["Many neuron cell bodies packed together and wrapped in perineurium","Many axons grouped into fascicles, all enclosed by an outer epineurium","Many axons running loosely side by side with no connective tissue around them","One very large axon wrapped in several layers of epineurium and perineurium"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"A nerve is a bundle of many axons, much like a cable of many wires. The axons are grouped into fascicles, each wrapped by perineurium, and the whole nerve is enclosed by the epineurium, with endoneurium around each single axon. This lets one protected route carry signals for many different destinations.","notes":["A cluster of neuron cell bodies outside the CNS is a ganglion, not a nerve. Pick this description for a ganglion such as the dorsal root ganglion.","Correct. A nerve is many axons in fascicles, enclosed by epineurium.","Axons running side by side with no connective tissue wrappings describes a tract in the CNS. Peripheral nerves always have connective tissue wrappings.","A nerve holds many axons, not one, and the perineurium wraps fascicles inside the nerve. One axon wrapped by its own layer describes an axon in its endoneurium."],"lt":"t-pns","lid":"d3x-t-pns-1","comp":187,"competencyId":"w8-nerve-structure"},{"id":"L03050","dok":3,"q":"Pressure on a peripheral nerve, as at the wrist in carpal tunnel syndrome, causes numbness and tingling. Where are these symptoms mainly felt?","a":"In the skin that nerve serves, beyond the point of pressure.","options":["In the matching area of skin on the opposite side of the body","In the skin that nerve serves, beyond the point of pressure","In every dermatome below the level of the compressed nerve","Only at the exact spot where the nerve is being squeezed"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"A peripheral nerve carries sensation from the area of skin it serves, so pressure on the nerve disrupts the signals from that whole territory. The numbness and tingling are felt in the skin the nerve supplies beyond the point of pressure, not only at the pressure spot. In carpal tunnel syndrome, the compressed median nerve produces symptoms mainly on the thumb side of the hand.","notes":["Crossing to the opposite side happens in pathways inside the CNS, not in a peripheral nerve. A squeezed peripheral nerve affects the same side of the body.","Correct. Symptoms appear in the area the compressed nerve serves.","Loss in every dermatome below a certain level describes a spinal cord injury, which interrupts the tracts. Pick it for cord damage, not one compressed peripheral nerve.","The brain reads signals from a nerve as coming from where its endings are, not from where it is squeezed. That is why symptoms spread into the nerve's territory."],"lt":"t-pns","lid":"d3x-t-pns-3","comp":191,"competencyId":"w8-nerve-plexuses"},{"id":"L03231","dok":2,"q":"A spinal nerve is formed where which two structures join?","a":"The posterior and anterior roots.","options":["The anterior and posterior rami","The posterior and anterior roots","Two anterior roots from neighboring segments","The posterior root and the filum terminale"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The posterior root brings sensory axons and the anterior root carries motor axons. Just beyond the dorsal root ganglion, the two roots join to form the spinal nerve, which is therefore a mixed nerve carrying both. The spinal nerve then exits through the intervertebral foramen and soon splits into rami.","notes":["The rami are the branches the spinal nerve splits into after it forms, so this reverses the order. Pick the rami for a question about what the spinal nerve divides into.","Correct. The posterior and anterior roots join to form the spinal nerve.","Each spinal nerve gets one posterior and one anterior root from its own segment, not two anterior roots. Joining two motor roots would leave the nerve with no sensory axons.","The filum terminale is a thread of pia mater that anchors the cord to the coccyx and carries no axons. Choose it for a question about anchoring the end of the cord."],"lt":"t-cns-spinal-cord","lid":"c16","comp":185,"competencyId":"w8-cord-roots"},{"id":"L03238","dok":2,"q":"Through which opening does a spinal nerve leave the vertebral canal?","a":"The intervertebral foramen.","options":["The intervertebral foramen","The vertebral foramen","The foramen magnum","The transverse foramen"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The intervertebral foramen is the gap between the notches of two neighboring vertebrae. Each spinal nerve forms near this opening and passes out through it to reach the body. That is why a herniated disc or bone spur that narrows the foramen can pinch a spinal nerve.","notes":["Correct. Spinal nerves exit through the intervertebral foramina.","The vertebral foramen is the large central hole in each vertebra; stacked together, they form the vertebral canal that holds the cord. Pick it for a question about where the spinal cord runs.","The foramen magnum is the large opening in the occipital bone where the medulla becomes the spinal cord. It would be the answer for where the brainstem meets the cord.","The transverse foramina are holes in the transverse processes of cervical vertebrae that carry the vertebral arteries. Choose them for a question about the vertebral artery's path."],"lt":"t-cns-spinal-cord","lid":"c23","comp":185,"competencyId":"w8-cord-roots"},{"id":"L03291","dok":1,"q":"What is a nerve plexus?","a":"A network where the anterior rami of several spinal nerves join and regroup.","options":["A bundle of axons traveling together inside the spinal cord","A cluster of neuron cell bodies lying outside the CNS","A network where the anterior rami of several spinal nerves regroup","A cluster of neuron cell bodies inside the brain or spinal cord"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"A nerve plexus is a network where the anterior rami of neighboring spinal nerves weave together and then sort their axons into the named nerves that leave it. Because of this regrouping, each nerve leaving a plexus carries axons from more than one spinal nerve. Plexuses lie in the PNS (peripheral nervous system, the nerves outside the brain and spinal cord), close to the limbs and regions they serve.","notes":["A bundle of axons inside the CNS is a tract. That would be the answer for a pathway of white matter running up or down the spinal cord.","A cluster of cell bodies outside the CNS is a ganglion, such as the dorsal root ganglion. Pick ganglion if the question asks for a PNS collection of cell bodies.","Correct. A plexus is where anterior rami mix and regroup before their axons travel on to the body.","A cluster of cell bodies inside the CNS is a nucleus. Pick nucleus if a question asks for a group of neuron cell bodies within the brain or spinal cord."],"lt":"t-nerve-plexuses","lid":"c1","comp":191,"competencyId":"w8-nerve-plexuses"},{"id":"L03292","dok":1,"q":"Which branches of the spinal nerves join to form the nerve plexuses?","a":"The anterior (ventral) rami.","options":["The dorsal roots","The anterior rami","The posterior rami","The ventral roots"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Each spinal nerve splits into rami (branches) soon after it leaves the intervertebral foramen. The anterior rami are the larger branches that supply the limbs and the front and sides of the trunk, and these are the branches that weave together into the cervical, brachial, lumbar, sacral, and coccygeal plexuses. The posterior rami stay segmental and never form plexuses.","notes":["The dorsal roots carry sensory axons into the spinal cord before the spinal nerve forms. They would be the answer for the root that holds the dorsal root ganglion, not for plexus formation.","Correct. The anterior rami of neighboring spinal nerves join and regroup to form the plexuses.","The posterior rami are the smaller branches that run straight back to the deep back muscles and the skin of the back. They stay separate and do not form plexuses, so pick them only for a question about supply to the back.","The ventral roots carry motor axons out of the spinal cord and join the dorsal roots to form the spinal nerve. Pick them for the root that carries motor axons, but plexuses form only after the spinal nerve has divided into rami."],"lt":"t-nerve-plexuses","lid":"c2","comp":188,"competencyId":"w8-spinal-nerves-rami"},{"id":"L03293","dok":1,"q":"In which region of the spine do the anterior rami NOT form a plexus?","a":"The thoracic region.","options":["The thoracic region","The cervical region","The sacral region","The lumbar region"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"In the thoracic region, the anterior rami of T2 to T12 do not weave into a plexus. Instead they run between the ribs as the intercostal nerves, supplying the intercostal muscles, the abdominal wall muscles, and the skin of the chest and abdomen in orderly bands. Every other region of the spine sends its anterior rami into a plexus.","notes":["Correct. The thoracic anterior rami run separately as intercostal nerves instead of forming a plexus.","The cervical anterior rami form the cervical plexus (C1 to C4) and help form the brachial plexus. Pick the cervical region if the question asks where the plexus that gives off the phrenic nerve arises.","The sacral anterior rami help form the sacral plexus (L4 to S4). Pick the sacral region for the plexus that serves the buttock and most of the lower limb.","The lumbar anterior rami form the lumbar plexus (L1 to L4). Pick this region for the plexus serving the anterolateral abdominal wall and front of the thigh."],"lt":"t-nerve-plexuses","lid":"c3","comp":191,"competencyId":"w8-nerve-plexuses"},{"id":"L03294","dok":1,"q":"Which plexus is formed mainly by the anterior rami of C1 to C4?","a":"The cervical plexus.","options":["The cervical plexus","The sacral plexus","The brachial plexus","The lumbar plexus"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The cervical plexus forms from the anterior rami of C1 to C4, with a small contribution from C5. It lies deep in the neck and supplies the skin and muscles of the neck and upper shoulders. It also gives rise to the phrenic nerve, which travels down to the diaphragm.","notes":["Correct. C1 to C4 build the cervical plexus, which serves the neck.","The sacral plexus forms from L4 to L5 and S1 to S4. Pick it for the buttock, perineum, and most of the lower limb.","The brachial plexus forms from C5 to C8 and T1, just below the cervical plexus. Pick it for the plexus that serves the shoulder and entire upper limb.","The lumbar plexus forms from L1 to L4 in the lower back. Pick it for the anterolateral abdominal wall and the front and inner thigh."],"lt":"t-nerve-plexuses","lid":"c4","comp":191,"competencyId":"w8-nerve-plexuses"},{"id":"L03295","dok":1,"q":"Which plexus is formed by the anterior rami of C5 to C8 and T1?","a":"The brachial plexus.","options":["The cervical plexus","The coccygeal plexus","The brachial plexus","The lumbar plexus"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The brachial plexus forms from the anterior rami of C5, C6, C7, C8, and T1. These spinal levels sit right where the upper limb joins the trunk, and the plexus serves the shoulder and the entire upper limb. Its name comes from brachium, the Latin word for arm.","notes":["The cervical plexus forms from C1 to C4, the levels just above the brachial plexus. Pick it for the neck, the upper shoulders, and the phrenic nerve.","The coccygeal plexus forms from S4, S5, and the coccygeal nerve (Co1) at the very bottom of the cord. Pick it for the small patch of skin over the coccyx.","Correct. C5 to T1 build the brachial plexus, the nerve network for the upper limb.","The lumbar plexus forms from L1 to L4. Pick it for the lower abdominal wall and the front of the thigh."],"lt":"t-nerve-plexuses","lid":"c5","comp":191,"competencyId":"w8-nerve-plexuses"},{"id":"L03296","dok":1,"q":"Which plexus is formed by the anterior rami of L1 to L4?","a":"The lumbar plexus.","options":["The cervical plexus","The brachial plexus","The sacral plexus","The lumbar plexus"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The lumbar plexus forms from the anterior rami of L1 to L4 in the posterior abdominal wall. It serves the anterolateral abdominal wall, the external genitals, and part of the lower limb, mainly the front and inner thigh. The plexuses are named for the spinal region that builds them, so lumbar levels form the lumbar plexus.","notes":["The cervical plexus forms from C1 to C4. Pick it for the neck and the phrenic nerve.","The brachial plexus forms from C5 to T1. Pick it for the shoulder and upper limb.","The sacral plexus forms from L4 to L5 and S1 to S4, overlapping the lumbar plexus at L4. Pick it when the levels run down into the sacral nerves.","Correct. L1 to L4 build the lumbar plexus."],"lt":"t-nerve-plexuses","lid":"c6","comp":191,"competencyId":"w8-nerve-plexuses"},{"id":"L03297","dok":1,"q":"Which plexus serves the shoulder and the entire upper limb?","a":"The brachial plexus.","options":["The cervical plexus","The brachial plexus","The sacral plexus","The lumbar plexus"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The brachial plexus, built from C5 to T1, is the nerve network for the shoulder and the whole upper limb. The named nerves of the arm, forearm, and hand all leave this one plexus. That is why an injury to the brachial plexus can affect movement and sensation across the entire upper limb on that side.","notes":["The cervical plexus (C1 to C4) serves the skin and muscles of the neck and the upper shoulders, not the rest of the limb. Pick it for the neck or for the phrenic nerve.","Correct. The brachial plexus serves the shoulder and the entire upper limb.","The sacral plexus (L4 to S4) serves the buttock, perineum, and most of the lower limb. Pick it for the back of the thigh, leg, and foot.","The lumbar plexus (L1 to L4) serves the lower abdominal wall and part of the lower limb. Pick it for the front and inner thigh."],"lt":"t-nerve-plexuses","lid":"c7","comp":191,"competencyId":"w8-nerve-plexuses"},{"id":"L03298","dok":1,"q":"Which nerve leaves the cervical plexus and descends through the thorax to reach the diaphragm?","a":"The phrenic nerve.","options":["The vagus nerve","The axillary nerve","The intercostal nerve","The phrenic nerve"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The phrenic nerve arises from the cervical plexus, from C3, C4, and C5, and runs down through the thorax beside the heart to the diaphragm. It is the only motor supply to the diaphragm, the main muscle of breathing. That is why an injury to the cord at or above C3 to C5 can stop breathing.","notes":["The vagus nerve is cranial nerve X. It leaves the brainstem, not a spinal plexus, and runs down the neck to the thoracic and abdominal organs. Pick it for a question about the cranial nerve that wanders to the viscera.","The axillary nerve is a terminal branch of the brachial plexus that wraps around the neck of the humerus. Pick it for a nerve found near the shoulder joint.","Intercostal nerves are the anterior rami of T2 to T12 running between the ribs. They are the answer for the thoracic wall, and they do not come from any plexus.","Correct. The phrenic nerve leaves the cervical plexus and runs to the diaphragm."],"lt":"t-nerve-plexuses","lid":"c8","comp":191,"competencyId":"w8-nerve-plexuses"},{"id":"L03299","dok":1,"q":"Which plexus serves the buttocks, the perineum, and most of the lower limb?","a":"The sacral plexus.","options":["The coccygeal plexus","The brachial plexus","The sacral plexus","The lumbar plexus"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The sacral plexus forms from L4 to L5 and S1 to S4 on the posterior wall of the pelvis. It serves the buttocks, the perineum (the region between the thighs that holds the external genitals and anus), and most of the lower limb. Its largest branch, the sciatic nerve, runs down the back of the thigh.","notes":["The coccygeal plexus serves only a small patch of skin over the coccyx. Pick it for that tiny area, not for the whole buttock and limb.","The brachial plexus (C5 to T1) serves the shoulder and upper limb. Pick it for any region of the arm.","Correct. The sacral plexus serves the buttock, perineum, and most of the lower limb.","The lumbar plexus (L1 to L4) also reaches the lower limb, but only part of it, mainly the front and inner thigh, along with the lower abdominal wall. Pick it for those regions."],"lt":"t-nerve-plexuses","lid":"c9","comp":191,"competencyId":"w8-nerve-plexuses"},{"id":"L03300","dok":1,"q":"The anterior rami of T2 to T12 do not form a plexus. What nerves do they become?","a":"The intercostal nerves.","options":["The phrenic nerves","The intercostal nerves","The femoral nerves","The radial nerves"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The anterior rami of T2 to T12 skip the plexus stage and run one by one in the spaces between the ribs. These are the intercostal nerves (inter means between, costa means rib). Each one supplies a band of the thoracic wall muscles and skin, which is why the chest and abdomen show a very orderly, striped pattern of sensory supply.","notes":["The phrenic nerves come from the cervical plexus (C3 to C5) and run to the diaphragm. Pick them for the nerve that powers breathing, not for nerves between the ribs.","Correct. The thoracic anterior rami run between the ribs as the intercostal nerves.","The femoral nerve is a branch of the lumbar plexus that enters the front of the thigh. Pick it for a nerve passing down the anterior thigh.","The radial nerve is a terminal branch of the brachial plexus that runs along the back of the arm. Pick it for a nerve on the posterior arm and forearm."],"lt":"t-nerve-plexuses","lid":"c10","comp":188,"competencyId":"w8-spinal-nerves-rami"},{"id":"L03301","dok":1,"q":"Which plexus serves only a small area of skin over the coccyx?","a":"The coccygeal plexus.","options":["The sacral plexus","The coccygeal plexus","The cervical plexus","The lumbar plexus"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The coccygeal plexus forms from the anterior rami of S4, S5, and the coccygeal nerve (Co1) at the very bottom of the spinal column. It supplies only a small patch of skin over the coccyx (tailbone). Its name matches the bone it lies beside.","notes":["The sacral plexus sits just above the coccygeal plexus but serves a far larger area, including the buttock, perineum, and most of the lower limb. Pick it for those regions.","Correct. The coccygeal plexus supplies only the skin over the coccyx.","The cervical plexus is at the opposite end of the spine and serves the neck and upper shoulders. Pick it for numbness of the neck, not the skin over the tailbone.","The lumbar plexus serves the lower abdominal wall and the front and inner thigh. Pick it for a deficit in those areas."],"lt":"t-nerve-plexuses","lid":"c11","comp":191,"competencyId":"w8-nerve-plexuses"},{"id":"L03302","dok":1,"q":"Which plexus is formed by the anterior rami of L4 to L5 and S1 to S4?","a":"The sacral plexus.","options":["The brachial plexus","The lumbar plexus","The sacral plexus","The coccygeal plexus"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The sacral plexus forms from the anterior rami of L4, L5, and S1 to S4, so it draws on two lumbar levels as well as four sacral ones. It lies on the posterior wall of the pelvis. It serves the buttock, the perineum, and most of the lower limb.","notes":["The brachial plexus forms from C5 to T1. Pick it for the plexus serving the upper limb.","The lumbar plexus forms from L1 to L4. It shares L4 with the sacral plexus, which is why students mix them up, but it does not include any sacral nerves.","Correct. L4 to S4 build the sacral plexus.","The coccygeal plexus forms from S4, S5, and the coccygeal nerve (Co1). Pick it when the levels sit at the very bottom of the cord."],"lt":"t-nerve-plexuses","lid":"c12","comp":191,"competencyId":"w8-nerve-plexuses"},{"id":"L03303","dok":1,"q":"Which plexus serves the skin and muscles of the neck and upper shoulders?","a":"The cervical plexus.","options":["The coccygeal plexus","The lumbar plexus","The brachial plexus","The cervical plexus"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The cervical plexus, built from C1 to C4 deep in the neck, supplies the skin and muscles of the neck and the upper part of the shoulders. It also sends the phrenic nerve down to the diaphragm. The plexuses run head to tail in the same order as the regions they serve, so the highest plexus serves the neck.","notes":["The coccygeal plexus serves a small patch of skin over the coccyx. Pick it for numbness over the tailbone.","The lumbar plexus serves the lower abdominal wall and front of the thigh. Pick it for those lower regions.","The brachial plexus serves the shoulder and the entire upper limb. It sits just below the cervical plexus, so pick it when the deficit extends down the arm.","Correct. The cervical plexus serves the neck and upper shoulders."],"lt":"t-nerve-plexuses","lid":"c13","comp":191,"competencyId":"w8-nerve-plexuses"},{"id":"L03304","dok":2,"q":"Why do the anterior rami form a plexus instead of each spinal nerve running straight to its target?","a":"Regrouping lets each body region receive axons from several spinal nerves.","options":["Regrouping turns sensory axons into motor axons","Regrouping speeds up conduction along the axons","Regrouping lets each region receive axons from several spinal nerves","Regrouping adds new neuron cell bodies to the nerve"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"In a plexus, axons from several spinal nerves mix and then sort into the named nerves that leave it. As a result, each body region receives axons from more than one spinal nerve. This works as a safety feature, because damage to one spinal nerve weakens an area without erasing all of its function.","notes":["An axon keeps its function no matter where it travels, so a sensory axon stays sensory. Mixed nerves simply carry both kinds side by side.","Conduction speed depends on axon diameter and myelin, not on whether axons pass through a plexus. Pick myelination if a question asks what speeds up a nerve signal.","Correct. Regrouping gives each region input from several spinal nerves, which builds in overlap.","A plexus is made of axons, not cell bodies. A collection of cell bodies outside the CNS is a ganglion, which is the answer for a question about where sensory cell bodies sit."],"lt":"t-nerve-plexuses","lid":"c14","comp":191,"competencyId":"w8-nerve-plexuses"},{"id":"L03305","dok":1,"q":"How are the principal nerve plexuses named?","a":"For the body region where they sit or that they serve.","options":["For the body region where they sit or that they serve","For the cranial nerve they connect to","For the number of spinal nerves they contain","For the bone they pass through"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Each plexus is named for the body region where it sits or that it serves: cervical for the neck, brachial for the arm, lumbar for the lower back, sacral for the sacrum, and coccygeal for the coccyx. The names run head to tail in the same order as those regions, so a plexus name tells you roughly where it is and what it serves. The name is not an exact list of the spinal nerves that build it; the brachial plexus, for example, is built from C5 to T1.","notes":["Correct. Plexus names describe the body region where they sit or the region they serve.","Plexuses are built from spinal nerves, not cranial nerves, and they do not connect to any cranial nerve by name. Cranial nerves are named and numbered on their own, from the olfactory nerve (I) to the hypoglossal nerve (XII).","No plexus is named for a count of its spinal nerves. The names are regional, and the count of contributing levels varies from plexus to plexus.","No plexus passes through a bone, and the names are not taken from openings. The sacral and coccygeal names point to the regions around those bones, not to a passage the nerves run through."],"lt":"t-nerve-plexuses","lid":"c15","comp":191,"competencyId":"w8-nerve-plexuses"},{"id":"L03306","dok":2,"q":"A patient injures the brachial plexus. Which body region loses movement and sensation?","a":"The shoulder and upper limb on that side.","options":["The neck and diaphragm","The thoracic wall","The front of the thigh","The shoulder and upper limb"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The brachial plexus is the nerve network for the shoulder and the whole upper limb. Every named nerve of the arm, forearm, and hand leaves this plexus, so damage to it causes weakness and sensory loss across the shoulder and upper limb on the injured side. The location of a plexus injury predicts the region affected.","notes":["The neck and diaphragm are served by the cervical plexus and its phrenic nerve. Pick that region for a cervical plexus injury.","The thoracic wall is served by the intercostal nerves, which do not form a plexus. Pick it for damage to the T2 to T12 anterior rami.","The front of the thigh is served by the lumbar plexus. Pick it for a lumbar plexus injury.","Correct. The brachial plexus serves the shoulder and the entire upper limb."],"lt":"t-nerve-plexuses","lid":"c16","comp":191,"competencyId":"w8-nerve-plexuses"},{"id":"L03307","dok":2,"q":"Damage to which plexus could weaken the diaphragm and affect breathing?","a":"The cervical plexus.","options":["The sacral plexus","The lumbar plexus","The brachial plexus","The cervical plexus"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The phrenic nerve, the only motor supply to the diaphragm, leaves the cervical plexus from C3 to C5. If the cervical plexus is damaged and the phrenic nerve is involved, the diaphragm on that side weakens and breathing is affected. The teaching phrase is C3, 4, 5 keep the diaphragm alive.","notes":["The sacral plexus serves the buttock and most of the lower limb. Pick it for weakness of the back of the thigh, leg, and foot.","The lumbar plexus serves the lower abdominal wall and front of the thigh. Students sometimes pick it because the diaphragm separates the thorax from the abdomen, but its nerve supply comes from the neck.","The brachial plexus (C5 to T1) sits just below the cervical plexus and serves the upper limb. Its injury causes arm weakness, not diaphragm weakness.","Correct. The phrenic nerve to the diaphragm arises from the cervical plexus."],"lt":"t-nerve-plexuses","lid":"c17","comp":191,"competencyId":"w8-nerve-plexuses"},{"id":"L03308","dok":2,"q":"A plexus injury causes weakness and sensory loss in the front and inner thigh and the lower abdominal wall. Which plexus was damaged?","a":"The lumbar plexus.","options":["The lumbar plexus","The sacral plexus","The brachial plexus","The coccygeal plexus"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The lumbar plexus (L1 to L4) serves the anterolateral abdominal wall, the external genitals, and the front and inner surfaces of the thigh. A deficit in exactly those areas points to the lumbar plexus. The neighboring sacral plexus covers the back of the thigh and the leg instead.","notes":["Correct. The lumbar plexus serves the lower abdominal wall and the front and inner thigh.","The sacral plexus serves the buttock, the back of the thigh, and the leg and foot. Pick it when the deficit is on the posterior thigh and below the knee.","The brachial plexus serves the shoulder and upper limb. Pick it for weakness of the arm and hand.","The coccygeal plexus serves only the skin over the coccyx. Pick it for a small numb patch over the tailbone."],"lt":"t-nerve-plexuses","lid":"c18","comp":191,"competencyId":"w8-nerve-plexuses"},{"id":"L03309","dok":2,"q":"A patient has weakness and sensory loss of the buttock, the back of the thigh, and the leg and foot. Which plexus is involved?","a":"The sacral plexus.","options":["The lumbar plexus","The cervical plexus","The coccygeal plexus","The sacral plexus"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The sacral plexus (L4 to S4) serves the buttock, the perineum, the back of the thigh, and most of the leg and foot. Its largest branch, the sciatic nerve, runs down the back of the thigh. A deficit in that pattern points to the sacral plexus.","notes":["The lumbar plexus serves the lower abdominal wall and the front and inner thigh. Pick it when the deficit is on the anterior thigh rather than the back of the limb.","The cervical plexus serves the neck and upper shoulders. Pick it for neck weakness or diaphragm problems.","The coccygeal plexus serves only the skin over the coccyx. It sits next to the sacral plexus, but its territory is tiny.","Correct. The sacral plexus serves the buttock, back of the thigh, leg, and foot."],"lt":"t-nerve-plexuses","lid":"c19","comp":191,"competencyId":"w8-nerve-plexuses"},{"id":"L03310","dok":2,"q":"Which pairing correctly gives the plexus the phrenic nerve leaves and the structure it reaches?","a":"From the cervical plexus to the diaphragm.","options":["From the brachial plexus to the diaphragm","From the cervical plexus to the diaphragm","From the cervical plexus to the thoracic wall","From the brachial plexus to the thoracic wall"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The phrenic nerve leaves the cervical plexus, drawing on C3, C4, and C5, and descends through the thorax to the diaphragm. Because C5 also feeds the brachial plexus, students sometimes place the phrenic nerve there, but it is a cervical plexus branch. The thoracic wall is served by the intercostal nerves instead.","notes":["The destination is right, but the brachial plexus serves the upper limb. The phrenic nerve shares the C5 level with the brachial plexus, which is the source of this mix-up.","Correct. The phrenic nerve runs from the cervical plexus to the diaphragm.","The plexus is right, but the thoracic wall is served by the intercostal nerves (T2 to T12). Pick the thoracic wall for a question about the nerves running between the ribs.","Neither half fits. The phrenic nerve leaves the cervical plexus, not the brachial plexus, and the thoracic wall is served by the intercostal nerves, which come from no plexus."],"lt":"t-nerve-plexuses","lid":"c20","comp":191,"competencyId":"w8-nerve-plexuses"},{"id":"L03311","dok":2,"q":"Why does damage to a single spinal nerve root usually weaken, but rarely fully paralyze, a limb region?","a":"Each region draws axons from several spinal nerves through the plexus.","options":["Each spinal nerve regenerates within a few hours","The thoracic intercostal nerves take over the limb","The dorsal root ganglion stores spare axons","Each region draws axons from several spinal nerves"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"A plexus mixes axons from several spinal nerves before they leave as named nerves, so each limb region receives fibers from more than one spinal level. If one root is damaged, the fibers from the other levels still reach the region. The area is weakened, but the overlap keeps it from being completely paralyzed.","notes":["Damaged peripheral axons can regrow, but slowly, over weeks to months, and not within hours. This choice confuses slow regeneration with the immediate protection that overlap gives.","The intercostal nerves stay in the thoracic wall and do not reroute to the limbs. Pick them for the nerves running between the ribs.","The dorsal root ganglion holds the cell bodies of sensory neurons, not spare axons. Pick it for a question about where sensory cell bodies sit outside the spinal cord.","Correct. The overlap built by the plexus keeps some function when one root is lost."],"lt":"t-nerve-plexuses","lid":"c21","comp":191,"competencyId":"w8-nerve-plexuses"},{"id":"L03312","dok":2,"q":"An injury to a plexus itself, rather than to a single root, can take out a whole region at once. Why?","a":"The injury sits downstream of the regrouping, where the region's nerves are gathered together.","options":["A plexus carries only sensory axons","A plexus lies inside the spinal cord","A plexus has no connective tissue covering","The injury sits where the region's nerves are gathered together"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Root injuries hit only one spinal level, and the overlap from other levels covers the gap. A plexus, however, is where all the axons headed for one region are gathered and sorted. Damage at that point catches fibers from every contributing level at once, so it can wipe out movement and sensation across the whole region.","notes":["Plexuses carry both sensory and motor axons, which is why a plexus injury causes both weakness and numbness. Pick sensory only for a dorsal root.","Plexuses are part of the PNS and lie outside the spinal cord, in the neck, axilla, and posterior abdominal and pelvic walls. A structure inside the cord would be a tract or a nucleus.","Nerves and plexuses are wrapped in connective tissue (endoneurium, perineurium, and epineurium). Pick those wrappings for a question about the layers around a nerve.","Correct. A plexus collects the axons for a whole region, so damage there affects them all."],"lt":"t-nerve-plexuses","lid":"c22","comp":191,"competencyId":"w8-nerve-plexuses"},{"id":"L03313","dok":2,"q":"Which plexus serves the anterolateral abdominal wall and the external genitals along with part of the lower limb?","a":"The lumbar plexus.","options":["The cervical plexus","The lumbar plexus","The brachial plexus","The sacral plexus"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The lumbar plexus forms from L1 to L4 in the posterior abdominal wall. It supplies the anterolateral abdominal wall (the front and sides of the belly), the external genitals, and part of the lower limb, mainly the front and inner thigh. Its location just behind the abdomen explains why its branches reach the abdominal wall.","notes":["The cervical plexus serves the neck and upper shoulders. Pick it for the neck or the phrenic nerve.","Correct. The lumbar plexus serves the anterolateral abdominal wall, external genitals, and part of the lower limb.","The brachial plexus serves the shoulder and upper limb. Pick it for any region of the arm or hand.","The sacral plexus serves the perineum, buttock, and most of the lower limb. Pick it for the back of the thigh and the leg, not the abdominal wall."],"lt":"t-nerve-plexuses","lid":"c23","comp":191,"competencyId":"w8-nerve-plexuses"},{"id":"L03314","dok":2,"q":"The teaching phrase C3, 4, 5 keep the diaphragm alive refers to which nerve and plexus?","a":"The phrenic nerve from the cervical plexus.","options":["The radial nerve from the brachial plexus","The femoral nerve from the lumbar plexus","The intercostal nerves from the thoracic rami","The phrenic nerve from the cervical plexus"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The phrase reminds you that the phrenic nerve, which supplies the diaphragm, carries fibers from C3, C4, and C5 and arises from the cervical plexus. Because the diaphragm is the main muscle of breathing, a high cervical injury above these levels can stop breathing. The phrase is a way to remember both the levels and the stakes.","notes":["The radial nerve is a brachial plexus branch running along the back of the arm and forearm. Pick it for a nerve on the posterior upper limb.","The femoral nerve is a lumbar plexus branch that enters the front of the thigh. Pick it for a nerve of the anterior thigh.","The intercostal nerves come from T2 to T12 and serve the thoracic wall between the ribs. Pick them for the chest wall, not the diaphragm levels in this phrase.","Correct. The phrenic nerve from the cervical plexus carries C3 to C5 fibers to the diaphragm."],"lt":"t-nerve-plexuses","lid":"c24","comp":191,"competencyId":"w8-nerve-plexuses"},{"id":"L03315","dok":2,"q":"Which plexus, if injured, produces numbness limited to a small patch of skin over the coccyx?","a":"The coccygeal plexus.","options":["The lumbar plexus","The cervical plexus","The sacral plexus","The coccygeal plexus"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The coccygeal plexus forms from S4, S5, and the coccygeal nerve (Co1), and its only territory is a small area of skin over the coccyx. Damage to it therefore produces a small numb patch over the tailbone and nothing more. The size of the deficit matches the size of the territory.","notes":["The lumbar plexus serves the lower abdominal wall and front of the thigh. Pick it for numbness on the anterior thigh.","The cervical plexus serves the neck and upper shoulders. Pick it for numbness of the neck.","The sacral plexus lies just above it and serves the buttock, perineum, and most of the lower limb. Its injury causes a much larger deficit down the back of the leg.","Correct. The coccygeal plexus serves only the skin over the coccyx."],"lt":"t-nerve-plexuses","lid":"c25","comp":191,"competencyId":"w8-nerve-plexuses"},{"id":"L03316","dok":3,"q":"A patient with a spinal cord injury high in the neck cannot breathe without a ventilator. Which nerve and plexus best explain this?","a":"The phrenic nerve from the cervical plexus.","options":["The vagus nerve from the brainstem","The axillary nerve from the brachial plexus","The phrenic nerve from the cervical plexus","The intercostal nerves from the thoracic rami"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The diaphragm is the main muscle of breathing, and its only motor supply is the phrenic nerve, which arises from the cervical plexus at C3 to C5. A cord injury above these levels cuts the diaphragm off from the brain, so the patient cannot breathe without support. That is the meaning behind C3, 4, 5 keep the diaphragm alive.","notes":["The vagus nerve is a cranial nerve that leaves the brainstem and supplies the heart, lungs, and gut. It does not power the diaphragm, so pick it for a question about cranial nerve X and the viscera.","The axillary nerve leaves the brachial plexus and wraps around the neck of the humerus. Its loss affects the shoulder, not breathing.","Correct. A high cervical injury cuts off the phrenic nerve, which powers the diaphragm.","The intercostal nerves serve the muscles between the ribs, which help breathing but cannot replace the diaphragm. They also arise below the neck, from T2 to T12."],"lt":"t-nerve-plexuses","lid":"c26","comp":191,"competencyId":"w8-nerve-plexuses"},{"id":"L03317","dok":3,"q":"After a motorcycle crash that forced the head away from the shoulder, a rider loses movement and sensation across the whole upper limb. Which structure was most likely stretched or torn?","a":"The brachial plexus.","options":["The lumbar plexus","The brachial plexus","The sacral plexus","The cervical plexus"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The brachial plexus runs from the lower neck (C5 to T1) across the shoulder region into the arm. Forcing the head and shoulder apart stretches this network, and because all the upper limb nerves pass through it, the whole limb can lose movement and sensation. A deficit covering the entire upper limb points to the plexus rather than a single named nerve.","notes":["The lumbar plexus lies in the posterior abdominal wall and serves the abdominal wall and front of the thigh. Pick it for a lower limb deficit on the anterior thigh.","Correct. A whole upper limb deficit after a neck and shoulder stretch points to the brachial plexus.","The sacral plexus lies in the pelvis and serves the buttock and most of the lower limb. Pick it for a deficit in the back of the leg.","The cervical plexus sits higher in the neck and serves the neck and upper shoulders. Pick it for neck weakness or diaphragm problems."],"lt":"t-nerve-plexuses","lid":"c27","comp":191,"competencyId":"w8-nerve-plexuses"},{"id":"L03318","dok":3,"q":"A pelvic fracture damages the sacral plexus. Which pattern of deficits would you expect?","a":"Weakness and sensory loss of the buttock, back of the thigh, and the leg and foot.","options":["Weakness of the front and inner thigh and lower abdominal wall","Numbness limited to a small patch of skin over the coccyx","Weakness and numbness across the shoulder and whole upper limb","Weakness of the buttock, back of the thigh, and the leg and foot"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The sacral plexus lies on the posterior wall of the pelvis, so a pelvic fracture can injure it directly. It serves the buttock, the perineum, the back of the thigh, and most of the leg and foot. Damage therefore causes weakness and sensory loss in that territory.","notes":["This is the lumbar plexus pattern. Pick it for an injury to the L1 to L4 network in the posterior abdominal wall.","This is the coccygeal plexus pattern. Pick it for an injury to the smallest plexus at the bottom of the spine.","This is the brachial plexus pattern. Pick it for an injury in the lower neck and axilla.","Correct. The sacral plexus serves the buttock, back of the thigh, leg, and foot."],"lt":"t-nerve-plexuses","lid":"c28","comp":191,"competencyId":"w8-nerve-plexuses"},{"id":"L03319","dok":3,"q":"A herniated disc presses on a single cervical spinal nerve root. The patient has some arm weakness but can still move every part of the arm. Which feature of the nervous system explains the preserved movement?","a":"The overlap created by the brachial plexus.","options":["The myelin sheath around each axon","The crossing of tracts in the brainstem","The dorsal root ganglion beside the cord","The overlap created by the brachial plexus"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The brachial plexus mixes axons from C5 to T1, so each part of the arm receives fibers from more than one spinal level. When one root is compressed, the fibers from the other levels still arrive, which leaves the arm weaker but still able to move. This overlap is the built-in safety feature of a plexus.","notes":["Myelin insulates axons and speeds signals, but it cannot replace fibers that are compressed at the root. Pick myelin for a question about conduction speed or the job of Schwann cells.","Crossing of tracts (decussation) explains why each side of the brain controls the opposite side of the body. It has nothing to do with spared function after a root injury.","The dorsal root ganglion holds sensory neuron cell bodies on the dorsal root. Pick it for where sensory cell bodies sit, not for why movement is preserved.","Correct. Fibers from other spinal levels still reach the arm through the plexus."],"lt":"t-nerve-plexuses","lid":"c29","comp":191,"competencyId":"w8-nerve-plexuses"},{"id":"L03320","dok":3,"q":"A clinician must predict the deficit from an injury to the plexus formed from C5 to T1. Which region will be affected?","a":"The shoulder and upper limb.","options":["The neck and diaphragm","The skin over the coccyx","The buttock and lower limb","The shoulder and upper limb"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The plexus formed from C5 to T1 is the brachial plexus. It serves the shoulder and the entire upper limb, so its injury causes weakness and sensory loss across that whole limb on the injured side. To answer this kind of question, first name the plexus from its levels, then recall its territory.","notes":["The neck and diaphragm are served by the cervical plexus (C1 to C4), which sits just above C5. Pick this region for an injury at those higher levels.","The skin over the coccyx is served by the coccygeal plexus at the bottom of the spine. Pick it for an S4 to coccygeal level injury.","The buttock and most of the lower limb are served by the sacral plexus (L4 to S4). Pick this region for an injury in the pelvis.","Correct. C5 to T1 is the brachial plexus, which serves the shoulder and upper limb."],"lt":"t-nerve-plexuses","lid":"c30","comp":191,"competencyId":"w8-nerve-plexuses"},{"id":"L03321","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly describes the named nerves that leave a plexus?","a":"Each carries axons from more than one spinal level.","options":["Each carries axons from more than one spinal level","Each carries only sensory axons to the skin","Each carries axons from exactly one spinal level","Each returns to the spinal cord at a lower level"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Inside a plexus, axons from several anterior rami mix and are sorted into new bundles. Each named nerve that leaves the plexus, such as the radial or femoral nerve, therefore carries axons from more than one spinal level. This is why one muscle can receive input from more than one level of the spinal cord.","notes":["Correct. Plexus branches carry axons pooled from several spinal levels.","The main plexus branches, such as the radial, median, and femoral nerves, carry motor axons to muscles as well as sensory axons, so they are not all sensory. The root that carries only sensory axons is the posterior root, before the spinal nerve forms.","One level per nerve describes the intercostal nerves of the thorax, which skip the plexus stage. Pick this for a T2 to T12 anterior ramus, not a plexus branch.","Peripheral nerves travel out to their targets and do not loop back into the cord at a lower level. This choice describes a pathway that does not exist."],"lt":"t-nerve-plexuses","lid":"d3-t-nerve-plexuses-1","comp":191,"competencyId":"w8-nerve-plexuses"},{"id":"L03323","dok":2,"q":"The nerves to the shoulder, forearm, and hand all leave one network. Where do the axons in these nerves come from?","a":"From several spinal nerves, C5 to T1, mixed in the brachial plexus.","options":["From the C7 spinal nerve alone","From C5 to T1, mixed in the brachial plexus","From C1 to C4 through the cervical plexus","From the thoracic intercostal nerves"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"All of the nerves of the upper limb leave the brachial plexus, which gathers the anterior rami of C5, C6, C7, C8, and T1. Inside the plexus these axons are sorted so that each named nerve heads to its own part of the limb while still carrying fibers from several levels. One organized network supplies the whole limb.","notes":["C7 is only one of the five levels feeding the brachial plexus. Choosing a single level ignores the mixing that happens in the plexus.","Correct. The upper limb nerves carry axons from C5 to T1 sorted in the brachial plexus.","The cervical plexus (C1 to C4) serves the neck and gives off the phrenic nerve. Pick it for the neck, not the forearm and hand.","The intercostal nerves run between the ribs and serve the thoracic wall. Pick them for the chest wall, not the upper limb."],"lt":"t-nerve-plexuses","lid":"d3-t-nerve-plexuses-3","comp":191,"competencyId":"w8-nerve-plexuses"},{"id":"L03324","dok":3,"q":"A deep wound in the armpit affects movement and sensation across much of the arm at once. Why can one small injury have such widespread effects?","a":"Many of the arm's nerves pass through the brachial plexus there, so damage disrupts several at once.","options":["The armpit holds the spinal cord segments for the arm","Many arm nerves pass together through the brachial plexus there","Injury in the armpit spreads upward into the cervical plexus","The arm has only one nerve, so any injury affects all of it"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The brachial plexus extends from the lower neck into the axilla (armpit), and the named nerves of the arm are packed close together as they pass through it. A single wound in that area can therefore damage several of these nerves at the same time. That is why the deficit spreads across much of the limb instead of staying in one spot.","notes":["Spinal cord segments sit inside the vertebral canal, not in the armpit. Pick the cervical enlargement of the cord for the cord region that serves the upper limbs.","Correct. Many arm nerves run together through the plexus in the armpit, so one wound can hit several.","Nerve damage does not travel up into a different plexus. The cervical plexus would be the answer for a neck or diaphragm deficit.","The upper limb has several named nerves, including the musculocutaneous, axillary, radial, median, and ulnar nerves. This choice misses that the brachial plexus divides into many nerves."],"lt":"t-nerve-plexuses","lid":"d3-t-nerve-plexuses-4","comp":191,"competencyId":"w8-nerve-plexuses"},{"id":"L03325","dok":2,"q":"The nerves to the lower limb come from plexuses separate from those serving the upper limb. Which plexuses supply the lower limb?","a":"The lumbar and sacral plexuses.","options":["The lumbar and sacral plexuses","The brachial and lumbar plexuses","The cervical and coccygeal plexuses","The brachial and cervical plexuses"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Each limb is supplied by the plexuses formed at the spinal levels where that limb joins the trunk. The lower limb draws on the lumbar plexus (L1 to L4), which serves part of the limb such as the front and inner thigh, and the sacral plexus (L4 to S4), which serves most of the rest. The upper limb draws on the brachial plexus instead.","notes":["Correct. The lumbar and sacral plexuses together supply the lower limb.","The lumbar plexus is right, but the brachial plexus serves the upper limb. This mix-up comes from pairing one upper limb plexus with one lower limb plexus.","The cervical plexus serves the neck and the coccygeal plexus serves only skin over the coccyx. Neither reaches the lower limb.","The brachial plexus serves the upper limb and the cervical plexus serves the neck. Pick this pair for nerves of the arm and neck."],"lt":"t-nerve-plexuses","lid":"d3-t-nerve-plexuses-5","comp":191,"competencyId":"w8-nerve-plexuses"},{"id":"L03326","dok":2,"q":"The sciatic nerve runs down the back of the thigh and carries fibers from several spinal levels. Which plexus does it leave?","a":"The sacral plexus.","options":["The lumbar plexus","The coccygeal plexus","The brachial plexus","The sacral plexus"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The sciatic nerve is the largest branch of the sacral plexus and the largest nerve in the body. It leaves the pelvis deep to the gluteal muscles and runs down the back of the thigh. Because it carries fibers from several lumbar and sacral levels, damage to any one of those levels weakens it only partly.","notes":["The lumbar plexus sends its main branches to the front and inner thigh, such as the femoral and obturator nerves. Pick it for a nerve on the anterior or medial thigh.","The coccygeal plexus serves only skin over the coccyx and gives off no large limb nerve. It sits next to the sacral plexus, which leads to the mix-up.","The brachial plexus serves the upper limb. Pick it for any nerve of the arm, such as the radial or median nerve.","Correct. The sciatic nerve is the largest branch of the sacral plexus."],"lt":"t-nerve-plexuses","lid":"d3x-t-nerve-plexuses-1","comp":191,"competencyId":"w8-nerve-plexuses"},{"id":"L03327","dok":2,"q":"The brachial plexus lies in the lower neck and armpit, and the lumbar and sacral plexuses lie in the posterior abdominal wall and pelvis. What do these locations have in common?","a":"Each sits close to where the limb it serves joins the trunk.","options":["Each sits at the far end of the limb it serves","Each sits close to where its limb joins the trunk","Each lies inside the vertebral canal","Each lies between a pair of ribs"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Each limb plexus forms near the root of its limb, at the spinal levels where that limb attaches to the trunk. This lets the anterior rami regroup right before they fan out as named nerves into the nearby limb. The brachial plexus sits at the root of the upper limb, and the lumbar and sacral plexuses sit at the root of the lower limb.","notes":["Plexuses lie at the root of a limb, not at its far end. The named nerves leaving the plexus are what travel down to the hand or foot.","Correct. Each plexus sits near the root of the limb it supplies.","The vertebral canal holds the spinal cord and nerve roots. Plexuses form after the spinal nerves have left the canal, so pick the vertebral canal for where the cord and roots lie.","The spaces between the ribs hold the intercostal nerves, which do not form a plexus. Pick this location for T2 to T12 anterior rami."],"lt":"t-nerve-plexuses","lid":"d3x-t-nerve-plexuses-2","comp":191,"competencyId":"w8-nerve-plexuses"},{"id":"L03328","dok":3,"q":"A tumor on the posterior wall of the pelvis presses on a plexus, causing tingling and weakness in the buttock, the back of the thigh, and the leg. Which plexus is compressed?","a":"The sacral plexus.","options":["The sacral plexus","The brachial plexus","The coccygeal plexus","The lumbar plexus"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The sacral plexus lies on the posterior pelvic wall, right where this tumor sits. Many of the lower limb nerves, including the sciatic nerve, pass through it together, so pressure there causes symptoms spread across the buttock, the back of the thigh, and the leg. Matching both the location and the territory points to the sacral plexus.","notes":["Correct. The sacral plexus sits on the posterior pelvic wall and serves the buttock, back of the thigh, and leg.","The brachial plexus lies in the lower neck and armpit and serves the upper limb. Pick it for tingling down the arm.","The coccygeal plexus is at the bottom of the pelvis too, but it serves only skin over the coccyx. Its compression would not cause weakness down the leg.","The lumbar plexus lies higher, in the posterior abdominal wall, and serves the front and inner thigh. Pick it for symptoms on the anterior thigh and lower abdominal wall."],"lt":"t-nerve-plexuses","lid":"d3x-t-nerve-plexuses-3","comp":191,"competencyId":"w8-nerve-plexuses"},{"id":"L04630","dok":2,"q":"How is a spinal nerve formed, and what kind of axons does it carry?","a":"The posterior and anterior roots join to form it, so it carries both sensory and motor axons.","options":["Posterior and anterior roots join; it carries sensory and motor axons","Rootlets from the brainstem join; it carries only sensory axons","Posterior and anterior rami join; it carries only motor axons","Anterior rami from several levels join; it carries only motor axons"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Rootlets along the cord gather into two roots on each side. The posterior root brings sensory axons and the anterior root carries motor axons, and the two roots join to form the spinal nerve. Because it holds axons from both roots, every spinal nerve is a mixed nerve, carrying sensory and motor axons together.","notes":["Correct. The two roots join, so every spinal nerve is mixed.","Rootlets attach along the spinal cord, not the brainstem, and spinal nerves are mixed, not purely sensory. Nerves that arise from the brainstem are cranial nerves.","The rami are branches that split off after the spinal nerve has formed, not the parts that build it. Every spinal nerve and both of its rami carry sensory as well as motor axons.","Anterior rami from several levels weaving together form a nerve plexus, not a single spinal nerve. Pick this if the question asked how a plexus such as the brachial plexus is built."],"lt":"t-w8-cord-roots","lid":"w8-cord-roots-r4","comp":185,"competencyId":"w8-cord-roots"},{"id":"L04637","dok":1,"q":"What is a nerve?","a":"A cordlike PNS organ, a bundle of peripheral axons wrapped in connective tissue.","options":["A single long process carrying an impulse away from a cell body","A bundle of axons that runs within the brain or spinal cord","A cluster of neuron cell bodies lying outside the CNS","A PNS bundle of axons wrapped in connective tissue"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"A nerve is a cordlike organ of the peripheral nervous system: many axons, some myelinated and some not, bundled together and wrapped in three layers of connective tissue. The axons are grouped into fascicles, and the connective tissue protects them and holds the nerve together. A nerve can carry sensory axons, motor axons, or both.","notes":["A single long process carrying an impulse away from a cell body is one axon, one nerve fiber. A nerve contains many axons bundled together.","A bundle of axons within the brain or spinal cord is a tract. Pick tract if the question asked about a bundle of axons in the CNS.","A cluster of neuron cell bodies outside the CNS is a ganglion. This would be the answer for a question about the posterior root ganglion.","Correct. A nerve is a bundle of PNS axons wrapped in connective tissue."],"lt":"t-w8-nerve-structure","lid":"w8-nerve-structure-r1","comp":179,"competencyId":"w8-tissue-collections"},{"id":"L04638","dok":1,"q":"Which list gives the connective tissue wrappings of a nerve from innermost to outermost?","a":"Endoneurium (each axon), perineurium (each fascicle), epineurium (whole nerve).","options":["Endoneurium, perineurium, epineurium","Perineurium, endoneurium, epineurium","Epineurium, perineurium, endoneurium","Endomysium, perimysium, epimysium"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The three nerve wrappings nest from the inside out, and each prefix tells you the level. Endo means within, so the endoneurium wraps each individual axon. Peri means around, so the perineurium wraps each fascicle, a bundle of axons. Epi means upon, so the epineurium wraps the entire nerve.","notes":["Correct. Endoneurium, then perineurium, then epineurium.","The perineurium wraps a whole fascicle, so it cannot be inside the endoneurium, which wraps a single axon. This order mixes up the two inner layers.","These are the right three layers in reverse order, from outermost to innermost. Students pick it when they mix up the meanings of endo and epi.","Endomysium, perimysium, and epimysium are the matching wrappings of a skeletal muscle, not a nerve. Pick this list if the question asked about muscle wrappings."],"lt":"t-w8-nerve-structure","lid":"w8-nerve-structure-r2","comp":187,"competencyId":"w8-nerve-structure"},{"id":"L04639","dok":1,"q":"In a peripheral nerve, what is a fascicle?","a":"A bundle of axons grouped together.","options":["The outer wrapping of the whole nerve","A bundle of axons grouped together","A single axon with its myelin","A cluster of neuron cell bodies"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"A fascicle is a bundle of axons grouped together within a nerve, wrapped by the perineurium. A nerve contains many fascicles, all held together by the outer epineurium. Skeletal muscle uses the same word for a bundle of muscle fibers, so the term always means a bundle.","notes":["The outer wrapping of the whole nerve is the epineurium. This would be the answer if the question asked for the outermost nerve layer.","Correct. A fascicle is a bundle of axons inside a nerve.","A single axon with its myelin is one nerve fiber, wrapped by the endoneurium. Pick this if the question asked what the endoneurium surrounds.","A cluster of neuron cell bodies outside the CNS is a ganglion, not a fascicle. Fascicles hold axons, not cell bodies."],"lt":"t-w8-nerve-structure","lid":"w8-nerve-structure-r3","comp":187,"competencyId":"w8-nerve-structure"},{"id":"L04640","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares the endoneurium and the epineurium?","a":"The endoneurium wraps each axon; the epineurium wraps the entire nerve.","options":["Endoneurium wraps each fascicle; epineurium wraps each axon","Endoneurium wraps the whole nerve; epineurium wraps each axon","Endoneurium wraps each muscle fiber; epineurium wraps each muscle","Endoneurium wraps each axon; epineurium wraps the whole nerve"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The endoneurium is the innermost connective tissue wrapping, around each individual axon. The epineurium is the outermost wrapping, around the entire nerve. Between them, the perineurium wraps each fascicle. The prefixes help: endo means within, and epi means upon.","notes":["The layer around each fascicle is the perineurium, and the epineurium is outermost, not around single axons. This choice scrambles all three levels.","This reverses the two layers. Endo means within, so the endoneurium is the innermost wrapping, and epi means upon, so the epineurium is the outermost.","Wrappings around muscle fibers and whole muscles are the endomysium and epimysium. This choice confuses the nerve terms with their muscle counterparts.","Correct. Endoneurium is around each axon, and epineurium is around the whole nerve."],"lt":"t-w8-nerve-structure","lid":"w8-nerve-structure-r4","comp":187,"competencyId":"w8-nerve-structure"},{"id":"L04641","dok":2,"q":"Nerve wrappings follow the same pattern as muscle wrappings. Which nerve layer matches the perimysium, the layer around each muscle fascicle?","a":"The perineurium, which wraps each nerve fascicle.","options":["Perineurium","Neurolemma","Endoneurium","Epineurium"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Both muscles and nerves are bundles of long structures wrapped at three levels. In muscle, the endomysium wraps each fiber, the perimysium wraps each fascicle, and the epimysium wraps the whole muscle. In a nerve, the endoneurium wraps each axon, the perineurium wraps each fascicle, and the epineurium wraps the whole nerve. The perineurium matches the perimysium because both surround a fascicle.","notes":["Correct. The perineurium and perimysium both wrap a fascicle.","The neurolemma is the outer layer of a Schwann cell around a PNS axon, not a connective tissue wrapping. Choose it if the question asked which structure guides a cut peripheral axon as it regrows.","The endoneurium wraps each single axon, so it matches the endomysium around each muscle fiber. Pick it if the question asked for the counterpart of the endomysium.","The epineurium wraps the whole nerve, so it matches the epimysium around the whole muscle. It would be the answer for the counterpart of the epimysium."],"lt":"t-w8-nerve-structure","lid":"w8-nerve-structure-r5","comp":187,"competencyId":"w8-nerve-structure"},{"id":"L04642","dok":1,"q":"How many pairs of spinal nerves are there, and what kind of nerve is each one?","a":"Thirty-one pairs, all mixed nerves carrying sensory and motor axons.","options":["12 pairs, all mixed nerves","31 pairs, all motor nerves","31 pairs, all mixed nerves","33 pairs, all mixed nerves"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"There are 31 pairs of spinal nerves, one pair for each segment of the spinal cord. Every one is a mixed nerve, because it forms where a sensory posterior root and a motor anterior root join. The 12 pairs of cranial nerves are counted separately, and some of them are purely sensory or mainly motor.","notes":["There are 12 pairs of cranial nerves, not spinal nerves. Pick 12 pairs if the question asked how many nerves arise from the brain.","The count is right, but spinal nerves are not purely motor. Each one carries sensory axons from the posterior root along with motor axons from the anterior root.","Correct. There are 31 pairs, and every one is mixed.","The count of 33 matches the usual number of vertebrae, not spinal nerves. The nerve count differs because there are 8 cervical nerves for only 7 cervical vertebrae, and just 1 coccygeal nerve for about 4 small coccygeal vertebrae."],"lt":"t-w8-spinal-nerves-rami","lid":"w8-spinal-nerves-rami-r1","comp":188,"competencyId":"w8-spinal-nerves-rami"},{"id":"L04643","dok":1,"q":"How are the 31 pairs of spinal nerves divided by region?","a":"8 cervical, 12 thoracic, 5 lumbar, 5 sacral, and 1 coccygeal.","options":["7 cervical, 12 thoracic, 5 lumbar, 5 sacral, 1 coccygeal","8 cervical, 12 thoracic, 5 lumbar, 1 sacral, 1 coccygeal","8 cervical, 12 thoracic, 5 lumbar, 5 sacral, 4 coccygeal","8 cervical, 12 thoracic, 5 lumbar, 5 sacral, 1 coccygeal"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The spinal nerves are named for the region of the vertebral column where they leave: 8 cervical, 12 thoracic, 5 lumbar, 5 sacral, and 1 coccygeal pair, for a total of 31. The counts mostly match the vertebrae, with one well-known exception: there are 8 cervical nerves but only 7 cervical vertebrae, because the first cervical nerve leaves between the skull and C1.","notes":["Seven is the number of cervical vertebrae, not cervical nerves. There are 8 cervical nerve pairs because the first one leaves between the skull and C1.","The sacrum is one fused bone in the adult, but it still passes 5 pairs of sacral nerves. This mix-up comes from counting the fused bone instead of its segments.","The coccyx is made of about four small fused vertebrae, but there is only one pair of coccygeal nerves. This choice counts the bones instead of the nerves.","Correct. Eight cervical, 12 thoracic, 5 lumbar, 5 sacral, and 1 coccygeal make 31 pairs."],"lt":"t-w8-spinal-nerves-rami","lid":"w8-spinal-nerves-rami-r2","comp":188,"competencyId":"w8-spinal-nerves-rami"},{"id":"L04644","dok":1,"q":"What does the posterior (dorsal) ramus of a spinal nerve supply?","a":"The deep muscles and skin of the posterior trunk.","options":["Deep muscles and skin of the posterior trunk","Muscles and skin of the limbs and the front of the trunk","The vertebrae, spinal ligaments, and meninges","The ganglia of the sympathetic trunk"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Just past the intervertebral foramen, each spinal nerve splits into rami, or branches. The posterior ramus turns backward to supply the deep muscles of the back and the skin over the posterior surface of the trunk. Because of this, a posterior ramus problem stays in the back, while limb problems point to the anterior rami or the plexuses they form.","notes":["Correct. The posterior ramus serves the deep back muscles and the skin of the back.","The limbs and the front and sides of the trunk are supplied by the anterior (ventral) ramus. Pick this if the question asked what the anterior ramus supplies.","The vertebrae, ligaments, and meninges are supplied by the meningeal branch, which re-enters the vertebral canal. This would be the answer for a question about the meningeal branch.","The ganglia of the sympathetic trunk are reached by the rami communicantes, the small branches that link the spinal nerve to the trunk, not by the posterior ramus. Choose them for a question about how autonomic fibers reach the sympathetic trunk."],"lt":"t-w8-spinal-nerves-rami","lid":"w8-spinal-nerves-rami-r3","comp":188,"competencyId":"w8-spinal-nerves-rami"},{"id":"L04645","dok":2,"q":"What does the anterior (ventral) ramus of a spinal nerve supply, and what do the anterior rami form?","a":"The limbs and the lateral and anterior trunk; the anterior rami form the nerve plexuses.","options":["Limbs and the anterior and lateral trunk; they form the plexuses","The meninges and vertebrae; they form the sympathetic trunk","Limbs and the anterior and lateral trunk; they form the cauda equina","Deep back muscles and posterior skin; they form the plexuses"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The anterior ramus is the larger branch of a spinal nerve. It supplies the muscles and skin of the limbs and of the front and sides of the trunk. In most regions the anterior rami of neighboring nerves weave together into networks called plexuses (cervical, brachial, lumbar, sacral, and coccygeal) before sending nerves to the body. The anterior rami of T2 to T12 are the exception and run as intercostal nerves.","notes":["Correct. The anterior rami supply the limbs and front and sides of the trunk and form the plexuses.","The meninges and vertebrae are supplied by the meningeal branch, and the sympathetic trunk is a chain of autonomic ganglia. This choice confuses the anterior ramus with two other structures.","The territory is right, but the cauda equina is the bundle of nerve roots inside the vertebral canal below the end of the cord. It forms before the nerves exit, so the rami cannot form it.","The deep back muscles and posterior skin are supplied by the posterior (dorsal) ramus, and the posterior rami do not form plexuses. Pick this territory if the question asked about the posterior ramus."],"lt":"t-w8-spinal-nerves-rami","lid":"w8-spinal-nerves-rami-r4","comp":188,"competencyId":"w8-spinal-nerves-rami"},{"id":"L04652","dok":1,"q":"Which list gives the four large nerve plexuses that serve the neck and limbs, in order from superior to inferior?","a":"The cervical, brachial, lumbar, and sacral plexuses.","options":["Cervical, brachial, lumbar, sacral","Brachial, cervical, sacral, lumbar","Cervical, brachial, thoracic, lumbar","Cervical, thoracic, lumbar, sacral"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The plexuses run head to tail in the same order as the cord regions that build them. The cervical plexus (C1 to C4) serves the neck and shoulders, the brachial plexus (C5 to T1) serves the upper limb, the lumbar plexus (L1 to L4) serves the abdominal wall and part of the thigh, and the sacral plexus (L4 to S4) serves the buttock and most of the lower limb. A tiny coccygeal plexus below them serves only the skin over the coccyx.","notes":["Correct. Cervical, brachial, lumbar, and sacral run from top to bottom.","These are the right four plexuses in the wrong order. The cervical plexus forms above the brachial plexus, and the lumbar plexus forms above the sacral plexus.","There is no thoracic plexus. The thoracic anterior rami run straight to the chest wall as intercostal nerves instead of forming a network.","This list leaves out the brachial plexus and adds a thoracic plexus that does not exist. The thoracic anterior rami are the one group that does not form a plexus."],"lt":"t-w8-nerve-plexuses","lid":"w8-nerve-plexuses-r1","comp":191,"competencyId":"w8-nerve-plexuses"},{"id":"L04653","dok":1,"q":"Which plexus gives rise to the phrenic nerve, the nerve that runs down through the thorax to the diaphragm?","a":"The cervical plexus.","options":["Lumbar plexus","Cervical plexus","Brachial plexus","Sacral plexus"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The phrenic nerve leaves the cervical plexus in the neck and travels down through the thorax to the diaphragm. The cervical plexus is formed by the anterior rami of C1 to C4, and the phrenic nerve's fibers come mainly from C3 to C5. The teaching phrase 'C3, 4, 5 keep the diaphragm alive' is a reminder that a high cervical injury can stop breathing.","notes":["The lumbar plexus forms from L1 to L4 and serves the lower abdominal wall and part of the thigh, well below the diaphragm. It would be the answer for a nerve running into the front of the thigh.","Correct. The phrenic nerve comes from the cervical plexus.","The brachial plexus forms from C5 to T1 and serves the shoulder and upper limb. Pick it if the question asked which plexus gives rise to the nerves of the arm.","The sacral plexus forms from L4 to S4 and gives rise to the sciatic nerve. Choose it if the question asked about the nerve running down the back of the thigh."],"lt":"t-w8-nerve-plexuses","lid":"w8-nerve-plexuses-r2","comp":191,"competencyId":"w8-nerve-plexuses"},{"id":"L04654","dok":1,"q":"Which plexus serves the upper limb, and which spinal nerves form it?","a":"The brachial plexus, formed from C5 to T1.","options":["Cervical plexus, C1 to C4","Brachial plexus, C5 to T1","Lumbar plexus, L1 to L4","Brachial plexus, C1 to C4"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The brachial plexus is formed by the anterior rami of C5, C6, C7, C8, and T1, and it serves the shoulder and the entire upper limb. Brachial means arm. These segments match the cervical enlargement of the cord, where the extra neurons for the upper limb sit. Damage to this plexus causes weakness and sensory loss across the shoulder and the whole limb on that side.","notes":["The cervical plexus forms from C1 to C4 and serves the neck and upper shoulders, and it gives off the phrenic nerve. Pick it if the question asked which plexus serves the neck.","Correct. The brachial plexus forms from C5 to T1 and serves the upper limb.","The lumbar plexus forms from L1 to L4 and serves the lower abdominal wall and part of the lower limb. It would be the answer for a question about the front of the thigh.","The plexus name is right, but C1 to C4 are the levels of the cervical plexus. The brachial plexus begins just below it, at C5."],"lt":"t-w8-nerve-plexuses","lid":"w8-nerve-plexuses-r3","comp":191,"competencyId":"w8-nerve-plexuses"},{"id":"L04655","dok":2,"q":"Which plexus gives rise to the sciatic nerve?","a":"The sacral plexus.","options":["Brachial plexus","Lumbar plexus","Coccygeal plexus","Sacral plexus"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The sciatic nerve, the largest nerve in the body, comes from the sacral plexus. This plexus is formed by the anterior rami of L4 to S4 and serves the buttock, the perineum, and most of the lower limb. The sciatic nerve leaves the pelvis and runs down the back of the thigh, which is why sacral plexus damage affects the back of the thigh, the leg, and the foot.","notes":["The brachial plexus (C5 to T1) gives rise to the nerves of the upper limb, not the lower limb. It would be the answer for a nerve in the arm.","The lumbar plexus (L1 to L4) gives rise to the femoral and obturator nerves, which run into the front and inner thigh. Pick it if the question asked which plexus gives rise to the femoral nerve.","The coccygeal plexus is tiny and serves only a small patch of skin over the coccyx. Choose it if the question asked which plexus serves the skin over the tailbone.","Correct. The sciatic nerve comes from the sacral plexus."],"lt":"t-w8-nerve-plexuses","lid":"w8-nerve-plexuses-r4","comp":191,"competencyId":"w8-nerve-plexuses"},{"id":"L04656","dok":2,"q":"Most anterior rami weave together into plexuses. Which group of anterior rami is the exception and runs straight to its targets instead?","a":"The anterior rami of T2 to T12, which run as the intercostal nerves.","options":["Anterior rami of L1 to L4","Anterior rami of T2 to T12","Anterior rami of C1 to C4","Anterior rami of L4 to S4"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The anterior rami of T2 to T12 do not form a plexus. They run between the ribs as the intercostal nerves (T12, below the last rib, is the subcostal nerve) and supply the intercostal muscles, the abdominal wall muscles, and the skin of the chest and abdomen. T1 is not part of this group because it joins the brachial plexus.","notes":["The anterior rami of L1 to L4 form the lumbar plexus, serving the lower abdominal wall and part of the thigh. They would be the answer for the rami that build the lumbar plexus.","Correct. The anterior rami of T2 to T12 run as intercostal nerves without forming a plexus.","The anterior rami of C1 to C4 form the cervical plexus, which serves the neck and gives off the phrenic nerve. Pick them if the question asked which rami build the cervical plexus.","The anterior rami of L4 to S4 form the sacral plexus, which gives rise to the sciatic nerve. Choose them if the question asked which rami build the sacral plexus."],"lt":"t-w8-nerve-plexuses","lid":"w8-nerve-plexuses-r5","comp":191,"competencyId":"w8-nerve-plexuses"},{"id":"L04667","dok":1,"q":"Which list names sensory receptor types classified by the kind of stimulus they detect?","a":"Mechanoreceptors, thermoreceptors, photoreceptors, chemoreceptors, and nociceptors.","options":["Free nerve endings and encapsulated nerve endings","Multipolar, bipolar, and unipolar sensory neurons","Exteroceptors, interoceptors, and proprioceptors","Thermoreceptors, photoreceptors, nociceptors"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Receptors are classified three ways: by stimulus, by location, and by structure. By stimulus, mechanoreceptors respond to touch, pressure, vibration, and stretch; thermoreceptors to temperature; photoreceptors to light; chemoreceptors to chemicals such as taste and smell molecules; and nociceptors to potentially damaging stimuli, producing pain. Each name tells you what the receptor responds to.","notes":["Free and encapsulated nerve endings are classified by structure, whether the ending is bare or wrapped in a connective tissue capsule. This would be the answer for a question about receptor structure.","Multipolar, bipolar, and unipolar are the structural classes of neurons, not receptor types. Choose this list for a question about neuron shapes.","Exteroceptors, interoceptors, and proprioceptors are classified by location: the body surface, the internal organs, and the muscles, tendons, and joints. Pick this list if the question asked how receptors are classified by where they sit.","Correct. These receptors are named for the stimulus they detect."],"lt":"t-w8-sensory-receptors","lid":"w8-sensory-receptors-r1","comp":189,"competencyId":"w8-sensory-receptors"},{"id":"L04668","dok":1,"q":"What do nociceptors detect?","a":"Pain, from potentially damaging stimuli.","options":["Pain from potentially damaging stimuli","Stretch of a skeletal muscle","Changes in temperature","Chemicals such as taste and smell molecules"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Nociceptors respond to stimuli that could damage tissue, such as extreme heat, crushing pressure, or harmful chemicals, and they produce the sensation of pain. Many are free nerve endings, bare dendrite tips found in most body tissues. The root noci comes from the Latin word for harm.","notes":["Correct. Nociceptors detect potentially damaging stimuli and produce pain.","The stretch of a skeletal muscle is detected by muscle spindles, a type of proprioceptor. Choose them if the question asked which receptor senses muscle stretch.","Changes in temperature are detected by thermoreceptors. Pick them if the question asked which receptors sense warm and cold.","Chemicals such as taste and smell molecules are detected by chemoreceptors. They would be the answer for a question about the receptors of taste and smell."],"lt":"t-w8-sensory-receptors","lid":"w8-sensory-receptors-r2","comp":189,"competencyId":"w8-sensory-receptors"},{"id":"L04669","dok":2,"q":"What is the general job of a sensory receptor?","a":"To detect a stimulus and trigger a signal in a sensory neuron.","options":["Release neurotransmitter onto the next neuron at a synapse","Detect a stimulus and trigger a signal in a sensory neuron","Carry a motor command from the CNS to a muscle or gland","Process incoming signals and decide on a response"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"A sensory receptor is the first link in any sensory pathway or reflex arc. It monitors changes inside or outside the body, and when it detects a stimulus it triggers a signal in a sensory neuron, which carries the signal toward the CNS. Without receptors, the nervous system would have no input to process.","notes":["Releasing neurotransmitter onto the next cell is the job of the synaptic end bulbs of an axon. Choose it if the question asked how a signal crosses a synapse.","Correct. A receptor detects a stimulus and starts a signal in a sensory neuron.","Carrying a motor command from the CNS to a muscle or gland is the job of a motor neuron. Pick it if the question asked what an efferent neuron does.","Processing signals and deciding on a response is the integrative function of the CNS, carried out at the integration center. It would be the answer for a question about the integration center of a reflex arc."],"lt":"t-w8-sensory-receptors","lid":"w8-sensory-receptors-r3","comp":189,"competencyId":"w8-sensory-receptors"},{"id":"N-a17d-188-1","dok":3,"q":"On a model, you trace a small branch from a sympathetic trunk ganglion back into the L4 spinal nerve, and it looks gray. Which ramus is it, and could you also find a white ramus on the L4 nerve?","a":"A gray ramus communicans, and no, white rami are found only from T1 to L2.","options":["A white ramus communicans, and yes, white rami are on every spinal nerve","A gray ramus communicans, and no, white rami are found only from T1 to L2","A white ramus communicans, and no, white rami are only on cervical nerves","A gray ramus communicans, and yes, white rami are on every spinal nerve"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Rami communicantes link a spinal nerve to a sympathetic trunk ganglion. The gray ramus carries unmyelinated axons from the ganglion back to the spinal nerve and is found on every spinal nerve. The white ramus carries myelinated axons into the ganglion and is found only on the T1 to L2 nerves, because only those cord segments have lateral horns.","notes":["A branch that looks gray and runs from the ganglion back to the nerve is a gray ramus. Gray rami, not white rami, are the ones on every spinal nerve.","Correct. A gray ramus runs from the ganglion back to the spinal nerve, and white rami are found only on T1 to L2, so L4 has none.","The branch is a gray ramus, and white rami are on the T1 to L2 nerves, not the cervical nerves.","The ramus is right, but white rami are found only from T1 to L2. L4 is below that range."],"comp":188,"competencyId":"w8-spinal-nerves-rami"},{"id":"N-a17d-189-1","dok":3,"q":"A skin slide shows a receptor deep in the hypodermis that looks like a cut onion, with many layers of capsule around one dendrite. Which receptor is it, what does it detect, and what is its structural class?","a":"A lamellar corpuscle; deep pressure; encapsulated.","options":["A tactile corpuscle; light touch; encapsulated","A tactile disc; deep pressure; nonencapsulated","A lamellar corpuscle; deep pressure; encapsulated","A lamellar corpuscle; light touch; nonencapsulated"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Lamellar means layered, and on a slide a lamellar (Pacinian) corpuscle looks like a cut onion, with many capsule layers around one dendrite. It sits deep, in the deep dermis and hypodermis, and detects deep pressure and vibration. Because its nerve ending is wrapped in a connective tissue capsule, it is an encapsulated receptor.","notes":["A tactile (Meissner) corpuscle is small and sits up in a dermal papilla near the surface, where it senses light touch. The deep, onion-like receptor is lamellar.","Tactile (Merkel) discs are bare endings in the stratum basale of the epidermis that sense light touch. They have no layered capsule and are not deep.","Correct. The deep, onion-like receptor is a lamellar (Pacinian) corpuscle, an encapsulated receptor for deep pressure and vibration.","The receptor is right, but it detects deep pressure and vibration, and its layered capsule makes it encapsulated."],"comp":189,"competencyId":"w8-sensory-receptors"}]},{"id":"m5-ans","title":"The autonomic nervous system","summary":"somatic vs autonomic, two-neuron pathway, sympathetic and parasympathetic origins, ganglia, sympathetic trunk, adrenal medulla, autonomic plexuses","mw":"2026-12-07","tr":"2026-12-08","cards":[{"id":"L02714","dok":1,"q":"The autonomic nervous system has two divisions. Which one prepares the body for fight or flight, such as during exercise or an emergency?","a":"The sympathetic division.","options":["The parasympathetic division","The somatic division","The sympathetic division","The enteric division"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The autonomic nervous system, the involuntary motor branch of the PNS, has two divisions that usually act in opposite directions. The sympathetic division is the fight-or-flight division, supporting exercise and emergencies. The parasympathetic division is the rest-and-digest division, supporting routine activities.","notes":["The parasympathetic division is the rest-and-digest division that supports routine activities such as digestion. Pick it for a question about the body at rest.","The somatic nervous system is the voluntary motor branch to skeletal muscle and is not part of the autonomic system. Choose it for a question about voluntary movement.","Correct. The sympathetic division is the fight-or-flight division.","The enteric nervous system is the network of neurons in the wall of the GI tract that can run the gut on its own. It would be right for a question about the nervous system of the gut."],"lt":"t-nervous-tissue","lid":"c39","comp":193,"competencyId":"w8-ans-ganglia-divisions"},{"id":"L03052","dok":1,"q":"What is another name for the autonomic nervous system?","a":"The visceral motor system.","options":["The central nervous system","The sensory nervous system","The visceral motor system","The somatic motor system"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The autonomic nervous system is also called the visceral motor system because it sends motor commands to the viscera, the internal organs. Its effectors are cardiac muscle, smooth muscle, and glands, and it works without conscious control. The somatic motor system, by contrast, controls skeletal muscle voluntarily.","notes":["The central nervous system is the brain and spinal cord. The ANS is a motor division of the peripheral nervous system, with only its first neurons starting in the CNS.","The sensory (afferent) division carries input toward the CNS. The ANS does have some sensory neurons from the organs, but it is defined by its motor output to cardiac muscle, smooth muscle, and glands, so sensory nervous system is not another name for it.","Correct. The ANS is the visceral motor system.","The somatic motor system is the other motor division; it controls skeletal muscle voluntarily. Pick it for the system that moves your arm when you choose to."],"lt":"t-ans","lid":"c2","comp":192,"competencyId":"w8-ans-pathway"},{"id":"L03053","dok":1,"q":"Which three tissue types are the effectors of the autonomic nervous system?","a":"Cardiac muscle, smooth muscle, and glands.","options":["Skeletal muscle, cardiac muscle, and glands","Smooth muscle, skeletal muscle, and bone","Cardiac muscle, smooth muscle, and glands","Glands, skeletal muscle, and cartilage"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The effectors of the autonomic nervous system are cardiac muscle, smooth muscle, and glands. These tissues run the internal organs: the heartbeat, the walls of blood vessels and hollow organs, and the secretions of glands. Skeletal muscle is the one muscle type the ANS does not control, because it belongs to the voluntary somatic system.","notes":["Skeletal muscle is controlled by the somatic motor system, not the ANS. Swapping it in for smooth muscle is the most common slip.","Skeletal muscle belongs to the somatic system, and bone is not an effector of any motor system. Pick skeletal muscle only for somatic control.","Correct. Cardiac muscle, smooth muscle, and glands are the autonomic effectors.","Skeletal muscle is somatic, and cartilage is a connective tissue that receives no motor commands. Only glands in this list are autonomic effectors."],"lt":"t-ans","lid":"c3","comp":192,"competencyId":"w8-ans-pathway"},{"id":"L03054","dok":1,"q":"From which parts of the central nervous system does the sympathetic division arise?","a":"The thoracic and upper lumbar cord, T1 to L2","options":["The brainstem nuclei and the sacral cord, S2 to S4","The cervical cord, C1 to C8 only","The lower lumbar and coccygeal cord","The thoracic and upper lumbar cord, T1 to L2"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The sympathetic division arises from the thoracic and upper lumbar segments of the spinal cord, T1 to L2. Its preganglionic cell bodies sit in the lateral gray horns of those segments, which is why it is also called the thoracolumbar division. The parasympathetic division leaves from the two ends instead, the brainstem and S2 to S4.","notes":["The brainstem and S2 to S4 are the origins of the parasympathetic division, the craniosacral outflow. Pick them for the parasympathetic division.","No autonomic fibers leave the cervical cord. The head and neck do receive sympathetic fibers, but they climb up the sympathetic trunk from the thoracic segments.","No autonomic fibers leave the cord from the lower lumbar or coccygeal segments. The lower lumbar segments sit in the gap between the sympathetic outflow, which ends at L2, and the parasympathetic outflow, which starts at S2, and the coccygeal segment lies below S4, past the end of the parasympathetic outflow.","Correct. The sympathetic division leaves the cord from T1 to L2."],"lt":"t-ans","lid":"c4","comp":193,"competencyId":"w8-ans-ganglia-divisions"},{"id":"L03055","dok":1,"q":"What does the term thoracolumbar outflow describe?","a":"Sympathetic fibers leaving the cord from T1 to L2.","options":["Sensory fibers entering the cord through the dorsal roots","Parasympathetic fibers leaving the brainstem and S2 to S4","Somatic motor fibers leaving the thoracic and lumbar ventral horns","Sympathetic fibers leaving the cord from T1 to L2"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Thoracolumbar outflow describes the sympathetic preganglionic fibers leaving the spinal cord from segments T1 to L2. The name joins the two regions involved, thoracic and lumbar. It is the reason the sympathetic division is also called the thoracolumbar division.","notes":["Sensory fibers entering through the dorsal roots are input, not outflow. Outflow always means motor fibers leaving the CNS.","Parasympathetic fibers leaving the brainstem and S2 to S4 make up the craniosacral outflow. Pick it for the parasympathetic division.","Somatic motor fibers leave the ventral horn at every level of the cord to reach skeletal muscle. They are not autonomic, so they are not part of the thoracolumbar outflow.","Correct. Thoracolumbar outflow is the sympathetic exit from T1 to L2."],"lt":"t-ans","lid":"c5","comp":193,"competencyId":"w8-ans-ganglia-divisions"},{"id":"L03056","dok":1,"q":"Where in the spinal cord do the cell bodies of sympathetic preganglionic neurons sit?","a":"In the lateral gray horn.","options":["In the ventral gray horn","In the lateral gray horn","In the dorsal gray horn","In the dorsal root ganglion"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Sympathetic preganglionic cell bodies sit in the lateral gray horn, a small projection of gray matter between the dorsal and ventral horns. The lateral horn is found in the thoracic and upper lumbar segments, matching the T1 to L2 origin of the sympathetic division. The axons of these neurons leave the cord through the ventral root.","notes":["The ventral gray horn holds somatic motor neurons that drive skeletal muscle. Pick it for the cell bodies of motor neurons to skeletal muscle.","Correct. The lateral horn holds the sympathetic preganglionic cell bodies.","The dorsal gray horn holds interneurons and receives incoming sensory axons. Pick it for where sensory input arrives in the cord.","The dorsal root ganglion holds sensory neuron cell bodies and lies outside the cord. Pick it for sensory cell bodies."],"lt":"t-ans","lid":"c6","comp":193,"competencyId":"w8-ans-ganglia-divisions"},{"id":"L03057","dok":1,"q":"What is the sympathetic trunk also called?","a":"The sympathetic chain.","options":["The sympathetic chain","The thoracic splanchnic nerve","The cauda equina","The celiac plexus"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The sympathetic trunk is also called the sympathetic chain because its ganglia are linked one after another like beads on a string. A trunk runs on each side of the vertebral column from the base of the skull to the coccyx. The ganglia along it are the sympathetic trunk ganglia, also called paravertebral ganglia.","notes":["Correct. The sympathetic trunk is the sympathetic chain.","The thoracic splanchnic nerves branch off the sympathetic trunk and carry fibers forward to the prevertebral ganglia in the abdomen. They leave the chain rather than forming it.","The cauda equina is the bundle of spinal nerve roots below the end of the spinal cord; its name means horse's tail. Pick it for the roots in the lower vertebral canal.","The celiac plexus is the large autonomic network around the celiac trunk in the abdomen, also called the solar plexus. Pick it for the plexus behind the stomach."],"lt":"t-ans","lid":"c7","comp":193,"competencyId":"w8-ans-ganglia-divisions"},{"id":"L03059","dok":1,"q":"From which spinal cord segments do the pelvic splanchnic nerves arise?","a":"The sacral segments S2 to S4.","options":["The lumbar segments L3 to L5","The cervical segments C1 to C8","The sacral segments S2 to S4","The thoracic segments T1 to T12"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The pelvic splanchnic nerves arise from the S2 to S4 segments of the spinal cord and carry the sacral part of the parasympathetic outflow. They supply the pelvic organs, such as the urinary bladder, the lower large intestine, and the reproductive organs. Together with the four parasympathetic cranial nerves, they make up the craniosacral outflow.","notes":["L3 to L5 fall in the gap between the sympathetic outflow, which ends at L2, and the parasympathetic outflow, which starts at S2. No autonomic fibers leave there.","The cervical segments send out no autonomic fibers. Pick them for the spinal nerves that form the cervical plexus and most of the brachial plexus.","Correct. The pelvic splanchnic nerves come from S2 to S4.","The thoracic segments are part of the sympathetic outflow, and they give rise to the thoracic splanchnic nerves, which are sympathetic. Pick them for those nerves."],"lt":"t-ans","lid":"c9","comp":193,"competencyId":"w8-ans-ganglia-divisions"},{"id":"L03060","dok":1,"q":"What is the celiac plexus also called?","a":"The solar plexus.","options":["The pulmonary plexus","The hypogastric plexus","The solar plexus","The cardiac plexus"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The celiac plexus is also called the solar plexus. It is the largest autonomic plexus and surrounds the celiac trunk, the first large unpaired branch of the abdominal aorta, behind the stomach. The nickname comes from the way its fibers radiate outward like rays of the sun.","notes":["The pulmonary plexus lies at the root of each lung and supplies the bronchial tree. Pick it for the lungs.","The hypogastric plexus lies low in the abdominopelvic cavity and supplies the pelvic organs. Pick it for the bladder and rectum.","Correct. The celiac plexus is the solar plexus.","The cardiac plexus supplies the heart and lies at the base of the heart in the thorax. Pick it for the heart."],"lt":"t-ans","lid":"c10","comp":193,"competencyId":"w8-ans-ganglia-divisions"},{"id":"L03062","dok":1,"q":"Where does the cell body of an autonomic preganglionic neuron sit?","a":"In the brain or spinal cord (the CNS).","options":["In a dorsal root ganglion","In an autonomic ganglion","In the brain or spinal cord","In the wall of the effector organ"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The cell body of an autonomic preganglionic neuron sits in the central nervous system, either in a brainstem nucleus or in the lateral gray matter of the spinal cord. Its thin, lightly myelinated axon then runs out to an autonomic ganglion, where it synapses with the second neuron. The first neuron of the pathway always starts in the CNS.","notes":["A dorsal root ganglion holds sensory neuron cell bodies, not autonomic motor neurons. Pick it for sensory cell bodies.","An autonomic ganglion holds the cell body of the second neuron, the postganglionic neuron. Pick it for the postganglionic cell body.","Correct. The preganglionic cell body sits in the CNS.","The wall of the effector organ is where parasympathetic terminal (intramural) ganglia sit, so it holds postganglionic cell bodies, not preganglionic ones."],"lt":"t-ans","lid":"c12","comp":192,"competencyId":"w8-ans-pathway"},{"id":"L03063","dok":2,"q":"An autonomic preganglionic axon runs from the CNS to which structure?","a":"To an autonomic ganglion.","options":["To the dorsal gray horn","Directly to the effector organ","To a skeletal muscle","To an autonomic ganglion"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The preganglionic axon leaves the CNS and runs to an autonomic ganglion, where it synapses with a postganglionic neuron. The postganglionic axon then carries the signal the rest of the way to the effector. This synapse in a ganglion is the one feature the somatic motor pathway does not have.","notes":["The dorsal gray horn is inside the cord and receives incoming sensory axons. A preganglionic axon is leaving the CNS, not entering the dorsal horn.","Running straight from the CNS to the target is the somatic pattern, with one neuron and no ganglion. The autonomic pathway always adds a second neuron.","Skeletal muscle is the effector of the somatic system. Autonomic axons serve cardiac muscle, smooth muscle, and glands.","Correct. The preganglionic axon ends in an autonomic ganglion."],"lt":"t-ans","lid":"c13","comp":192,"competencyId":"w8-ans-pathway"},{"id":"L03064","dok":2,"q":"Where does the cell body of an autonomic postganglionic neuron sit?","a":"In the autonomic ganglion.","options":["In the dorsal root ganglion","In the brainstem","In the autonomic ganglion","In the lateral horn of the spinal cord"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The postganglionic neuron is the second neuron of the autonomic pathway. Its cell body sits in the autonomic ganglion, where it receives the synapse from the preganglionic axon, and its unmyelinated axon runs on to the effector. The word postganglionic means after the ganglion, describing the axon that leaves it.","notes":["The dorsal root ganglion holds sensory neuron cell bodies, not autonomic motor neurons. Pick it for sensory cell bodies.","The brainstem holds the preganglionic cell bodies of the cranial parasympathetic outflow. Pick it for the first neuron of a parasympathetic pathway in a cranial nerve.","Correct. The postganglionic cell body sits in the autonomic ganglion.","The lateral horn holds sympathetic preganglionic cell bodies, the first neuron of the pathway. Pick it for the sympathetic preganglionic neuron."],"lt":"t-ans","lid":"c14","comp":192,"competencyId":"w8-ans-pathway"},{"id":"L03065","dok":2,"q":"A motor pathway reaches a skeletal muscle through a single neuron with no ganglion along the way. Which system is this?","a":"The somatic motor system.","options":["The sympathetic division","The parasympathetic division","The somatic motor system","The autonomic nervous system"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The somatic motor system uses one motor neuron that runs from the CNS straight to a skeletal muscle, with no ganglion in between. Its cell body sits in the ventral gray horn or a brainstem motor nucleus. The autonomic system, by contrast, always uses two neurons with a synapse in a ganglion.","notes":["The sympathetic division uses two neurons, with a ganglion close to the spinal cord. It does not drive skeletal muscle contraction.","The parasympathetic division uses two neurons, with a ganglion near or in the target organ. Its effectors are cardiac muscle, smooth muscle, and glands, not skeletal muscle.","Correct. One neuron straight to skeletal muscle is the somatic pattern.","The autonomic nervous system always uses a two-neuron chain with a ganglion. Pick it for any pathway with a preganglionic and postganglionic neuron."],"lt":"t-ans","lid":"c15","comp":192,"competencyId":"w8-ans-pathway"},{"id":"L03066","dok":2,"q":"A preganglionic fiber synapses in a ganglion of the sympathetic trunk. Which division does it belong to?","a":"The sympathetic division.","options":["The somatic motor system","The sensory division","The parasympathetic division","The sympathetic division"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The sympathetic trunk ganglia (paravertebral ganglia) belong to the sympathetic division. Many sympathetic preganglionic axons leave the cord in the T1 to L2 spinal nerves, enter the trunk through a white ramus communicans, and synapse in a trunk ganglion close to the vertebral column. Parasympathetic fibers do not synapse in the sympathetic trunk; they synapse in terminal ganglia near or in the organ.","notes":["The somatic motor system uses one neuron and no ganglion, so it has no preganglionic fibers at all.","The sensory division carries input toward the CNS and has no preganglionic fibers. Its cell bodies sit in sensory ganglia such as the dorsal root ganglion.","Parasympathetic preganglionic fibers synapse in terminal ganglia near or within the target organ. Pick this division for a ganglion in the wall of an organ.","Correct. A synapse in the sympathetic trunk marks the sympathetic division."],"lt":"t-ans","lid":"c16","comp":193,"competencyId":"w8-ans-ganglia-divisions"},{"id":"L03067","dok":2,"q":"Cranial nerves III, VII, IX, and X carry fibers of which autonomic division?","a":"The parasympathetic division.","options":["The somatic motor system only","The enteric nervous system","The sympathetic division","The parasympathetic division"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Cranial nerves III, VII, IX, and X carry the cranial part of the parasympathetic division. The oculomotor nerve (III) constricts the pupil, the facial (VII) and glossopharyngeal (IX) nerves drive tear and saliva production, and the vagus (X) serves the thoracic and abdominal organs. The rest of the parasympathetic outflow leaves from the sacral cord, S2 to S4.","notes":["Several of these nerves do carry somatic motor fibers too, but not only those. The autonomic fibers they carry are parasympathetic.","The enteric nervous system is the network of plexuses inside the wall of the gut. It does not travel in cranial nerves, although the vagus does connect to it.","The sympathetic division leaves only from T1 to L2. Sympathetic fibers reach the head by climbing the sympathetic trunk, not by traveling in these cranial nerves.","Correct. These four cranial nerves carry parasympathetic fibers."],"lt":"t-ans","lid":"c17","comp":193,"competencyId":"w8-ans-ganglia-divisions"},{"id":"L03068","dok":2,"q":"The adrenal medulla works like a modified ganglion of which autonomic division?","a":"The sympathetic division.","options":["The sympathetic division","The parasympathetic division","The enteric nervous system","The somatic motor system"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The adrenal medulla, the inner part of the adrenal gland, works like a modified sympathetic ganglion. Sympathetic preganglionic fibers reach it directly, and its cells, which are related to postganglionic neurons, release epinephrine and norepinephrine into the blood instead of onto a nearby organ. This is how a fight-or-flight response spreads through the bloodstream as well as through nerves.","notes":["Correct. The adrenal medulla is a modified sympathetic ganglion.","The parasympathetic division does not supply the adrenal medulla. Pick it for terminal ganglia near or within organs.","The enteric nervous system lies in the wall of the gut. It does not include the adrenal gland.","The somatic motor system drives skeletal muscle with a single neuron and has no ganglia, so it has nothing to modify into a gland."],"lt":"t-ans","lid":"c18","comp":131,"competencyId":"w6-adrenal"},{"id":"L03069","dok":2,"q":"Where are the prevertebral ganglia located?","a":"In front of the vertebral column, near large abdominal arteries.","options":["In front of the vertebral column, near large abdominal arteries","Near or within the walls of the target organs they supply","Inside the lateral gray horns of the thoracic spinal cord","Alongside the vertebral column, linked together in a vertical chain"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Prevertebral (collateral) ganglia lie anterior to the vertebral column, clustered around the large arteries that branch off the abdominal aorta. They include the celiac, superior mesenteric, inferior mesenteric, aorticorenal, and renal ganglia, each named for the artery it sits beside. Like the trunk ganglia, they belong to the sympathetic division, but they sit farther forward.","notes":["Correct. Prevertebral ganglia sit in front of the vertebral column by the abdominal arteries.","Near or within the target organ describes the parasympathetic terminal ganglia. Pick it for terminal or intramural ganglia.","The lateral gray horns hold the sympathetic preganglionic cell bodies, which are inside the CNS. A ganglion by definition lies outside the CNS.","A vertical chain alongside the vertebral column describes the sympathetic trunk (paravertebral) ganglia. Pick it for paravertebral ganglia."],"lt":"t-ans","lid":"c19","comp":193,"competencyId":"w8-ans-ganglia-divisions"},{"id":"L03070","dok":2,"q":"Intramural ganglia belong to which broader group of autonomic ganglia?","a":"Terminal ganglia.","options":["Terminal ganglia","Dorsal root ganglia","Prevertebral ganglia","Paravertebral ganglia"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Intramural ganglia are terminal ganglia that sit within the wall of the organ they supply; intramural means within the wall. Terminal ganglia are the parasympathetic ganglia, located near or within the target organ. Because the ganglion is so close to the organ, the parasympathetic postganglionic axon is very short.","notes":["Correct. Intramural ganglia are a kind of terminal ganglion.","Dorsal root ganglia hold sensory neuron cell bodies and are not autonomic ganglia. Pick them for sensory cell bodies.","Prevertebral ganglia are sympathetic ganglia in front of the vertebral column near the abdominal arteries. Pick them for the celiac or mesenteric ganglia.","Paravertebral ganglia form the sympathetic trunk beside the vertebral column, far from the organs. Pick them for the chain ganglia."],"lt":"t-ans","lid":"c20","comp":193,"competencyId":"w8-ans-ganglia-divisions"},{"id":"L03071","dok":2,"q":"Splanchnic nerves carry autonomic fibers toward which destination?","a":"The visceral organs.","options":["The cerebral cortex","The skin of the back","The visceral organs","The skeletal muscles"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Splanchnic means relating to the viscera, and splanchnic nerves carry autonomic fibers toward the internal organs of the abdomen and pelvis. The thoracic splanchnic nerves carry sympathetic fibers from the trunk to the prevertebral ganglia, and the pelvic splanchnic nerves carry parasympathetic fibers from S2 to S4 to the pelvic organs. In both cases the target is the viscera, not skeletal muscle or skin.","notes":["The cerebral cortex is part of the CNS and is not a target of any peripheral nerve. Ascending tracts, not splanchnic nerves, carry signals there.","The skin of the back is supplied by the posterior rami of the spinal nerves. Pick it for the posterior rami.","Correct. Splanchnic nerves head for the visceral organs.","Skeletal muscles are reached by somatic motor axons in the spinal and cranial nerves. Pick them for the somatic system."],"lt":"t-ans","lid":"c21","comp":193,"competencyId":"w8-ans-ganglia-divisions"},{"id":"L03072","dok":2,"q":"Which description fits an autonomic plexus?","a":"A network of autonomic axons and ganglia around the organs.","options":["A cluster of sensory neuron cell bodies just beside the cord","A network of spinal nerve anterior rami serving a limb","A single large nerve that runs to one organ","A network of autonomic axons and ganglia around the organs"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"An autonomic plexus is a network of autonomic axons and ganglia in the thorax, abdomen, or pelvis. Sympathetic and parasympathetic fibers often travel together through these networks before spreading out to the organs. The cardiac, pulmonary, celiac, mesenteric, hypogastric, and renal plexuses are examples.","notes":["A cluster of sensory cell bodies beside the cord is a dorsal root ganglion. Pick it for where sensory neuron cell bodies sit.","A network of anterior rami serving a limb is a somatic nerve plexus, such as the brachial or sacral plexus. Pick it for the spinal nerve plexuses.","A single nerve to one organ is not a network. A plexus is defined by many fibers joining and branching.","Correct. An autonomic plexus is a network of autonomic axons and ganglia."],"lt":"t-ans","lid":"c22","comp":193,"competencyId":"w8-ans-ganglia-divisions"},{"id":"L03073","dok":2,"q":"Where is the pulmonary plexus located?","a":"At the root of each lung.","options":["At the base of the heart","Around the esophagus in the thorax","Deep in the pelvis","At the root of each lung"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The pulmonary plexus lies at the root of each lung, where the bronchi and pulmonary vessels enter at the hilum. Its fibers follow the bronchi into the lung to supply the bronchial tree. It receives fibers from both the vagus nerve and the sympathetic trunk.","notes":["The base of the heart is where the cardiac plexus lies. Pick it for the plexus that supplies the heart.","The esophageal plexus wraps around the esophagus in the thorax, formed mainly by the vagus nerves. Pick it for the esophagus.","The hypogastric plexus lies deep in the pelvis and supplies the pelvic organs. Pick it for the bladder and rectum.","Correct. The pulmonary plexus sits at the root of each lung."],"lt":"t-ans","lid":"c23","comp":193,"competencyId":"w8-ans-ganglia-divisions"},{"id":"L03074","dok":2,"q":"The hypogastric plexus supplies autonomic fibers to which organs?","a":"The organs of the pelvis.","options":["The organs of the head","The organs of the pelvis","The organs of the chest","The organs of the upper abdomen"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The hypogastric plexus supplies autonomic fibers to the pelvic organs, such as the urinary bladder, the rectum, and the reproductive organs. Hypogastric means below the stomach, which describes its low position in the abdominopelvic cavity. It receives parasympathetic fibers from the pelvic splanchnic nerves (S2 to S4) as well as sympathetic fibers.","notes":["The organs of the head receive parasympathetic fibers through cranial nerves and the terminal ganglia of the head, such as the ciliary and otic ganglia. The hypogastric plexus sits low in the pelvis, far from the head.","Correct. The hypogastric plexus supplies the pelvic organs.","The organs of the chest are supplied by the cardiac, pulmonary, and esophageal plexuses. Pick the chest for those plexuses.","The upper abdominal organs are supplied mainly by the celiac plexus. Pick the upper abdomen for the celiac (solar) plexus."],"lt":"t-ans","lid":"c24","comp":193,"competencyId":"w8-ans-ganglia-divisions"},{"id":"L03075","dok":2,"q":"The cardiac plexus delivers autonomic fibers to which organ?","a":"The heart.","options":["The stomach","The heart","The lungs","The esophagus"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The cardiac plexus is a network of autonomic fibers at the base of the heart that supplies the heart. Sympathetic fibers in this plexus speed the heart, and parasympathetic fibers from the vagus nerve slow it. The plexus lies in the thorax close to the pulmonary plexus.","notes":["The stomach is supplied mainly through the celiac plexus in the abdomen. Pick it for the celiac (solar) plexus.","Correct. Cardiac means heart, and the cardiac plexus supplies the heart.","The lungs are supplied by the pulmonary plexus at the root of each lung. Pick the lungs for that plexus.","The esophagus is supplied by the esophageal plexus, formed mainly by the vagus nerves in the thorax. Pick it for that plexus."],"lt":"t-ans","lid":"c25","comp":193,"competencyId":"w8-ans-ganglia-divisions"},{"id":"L03076","dok":2,"q":"The esophageal plexus is found in which body cavity?","a":"The thorax.","options":["The cranial cavity","The abdomen","The pelvis","The thorax"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The esophageal plexus surrounds the esophagus in the thorax, in the posterior mediastinum. It forms mainly from the two vagus nerves, which branch and weave around the esophagus as they travel down. Just above the diaphragm, the fibers regroup into vagal trunks that pass into the abdomen with the esophagus.","notes":["The cranial cavity holds the brain, not any autonomic trunk plexus. Pick it for the cranial nerve nuclei, not a plexus.","The abdomen holds the celiac, mesenteric, and renal plexuses. The esophagus passes through the diaphragm only at its very end, so its plexus sits above.","The pelvis holds the hypogastric plexus, which supplies the pelvic organs. Pick it for that plexus.","Correct. The esophageal plexus lies in the thorax."],"lt":"t-ans","lid":"c26","comp":193,"competencyId":"w8-ans-ganglia-divisions"},{"id":"L03077","dok":2,"q":"A ganglion lies in a vertical chain alongside the vertebral column. Which type of ganglion is it?","a":"A paravertebral ganglion.","options":["A prevertebral ganglion","A terminal ganglion","An intramural ganglion","A paravertebral ganglion"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Paravertebral ganglia, also called sympathetic trunk ganglia, form a vertical chain on each side of the vertebral column. Para- means beside, which matches their position next to the vertebrae. They belong to the sympathetic division and run from the base of the skull to the coccyx.","notes":["A prevertebral ganglion sits in front of the vertebral column near the large abdominal arteries, not in a chain beside it. Pre- means in front, para- means beside.","A terminal ganglion is a parasympathetic ganglion near or within the target organ. Pick it for a ganglion close to the organ.","An intramural ganglion is a terminal ganglion inside the wall of an organ. Pick it for a ganglion in the wall of the bladder or gut.","Correct. The chain beside the vertebral column is made of paravertebral ganglia."],"lt":"t-ans","lid":"c27","comp":193,"competencyId":"w8-ans-ganglia-divisions"},{"id":"L03078","dok":2,"q":"An autonomic division has short preganglionic axons and long postganglionic axons. Which division is it?","a":"The sympathetic division.","options":["The parasympathetic division","The sympathetic division","The somatic motor system","The enteric nervous system"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"In the sympathetic division the ganglia sit close to the spinal cord, in the sympathetic trunk or the prevertebral ganglia. That makes the preganglionic axon short and the postganglionic axon long, since the second neuron must travel all the way out to the organ. The parasympathetic division is the reverse, with a long preganglionic axon and a short postganglionic one.","notes":["The parasympathetic division has long preganglionic and short postganglionic axons, because its ganglia sit at the organ. Pick it for the reverse pattern.","Correct. Ganglia near the cord give the sympathetic division short preganglionic and long postganglionic axons.","The somatic motor system uses a single neuron with no ganglion, so it has no preganglionic or postganglionic axons.","The enteric nervous system is the network inside the gut wall. It is not described by preganglionic and postganglionic lengths."],"lt":"t-ans","lid":"c28","comp":193,"competencyId":"w8-ans-ganglia-divisions"},{"id":"L03079","dok":2,"q":"An autonomic division has its ganglia near or within the walls of the target organs. Which division is it?","a":"The parasympathetic division.","options":["The visceral sensory system","The parasympathetic division","The sympathetic division","The somatic motor system"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The parasympathetic division has its ganglia, the terminal ganglia, near or within the walls of the target organs. Its preganglionic axons therefore run a long way from the brainstem or sacral cord, and its postganglionic axons are short. Terminal ganglia inside the organ wall are also called intramural ganglia.","notes":["The visceral sensory system carries input from the organs to the CNS, with its cell bodies in sensory ganglia. It is not a motor division with ganglia at the organs.","Correct. Terminal ganglia at the organ mark the parasympathetic division.","The sympathetic division has its ganglia close to the spinal cord, in the sympathetic trunk or the prevertebral ganglia. Pick it for ganglia near the vertebral column.","The somatic motor system has no ganglia at all; one neuron runs straight to skeletal muscle."],"lt":"t-ans","lid":"c29","comp":193,"competencyId":"w8-ans-ganglia-divisions"},{"id":"L03080","dok":2,"q":"Which of these is a prevertebral ganglion?","a":"The celiac ganglion.","options":["The otic ganglion","The celiac ganglion","The ciliary ganglion","The dorsal root ganglion"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The celiac ganglion is a prevertebral (collateral) ganglion, one of the sympathetic ganglia that sit in front of the vertebral column around the large abdominal arteries. It lies beside the celiac trunk. The other prevertebral ganglia are the superior mesenteric, inferior mesenteric, aorticorenal, and renal ganglia.","notes":["The otic ganglion is a parasympathetic terminal ganglion of the head that serves the parotid salivary gland. Pick it as one of the four terminal ganglia of the head.","Correct. The celiac ganglion is a prevertebral ganglion.","The ciliary ganglion is a parasympathetic terminal ganglion in the orbit, serving the eye with fibers from the oculomotor nerve. Its name looks like celiac, which is the usual mix-up.","The dorsal root ganglion holds sensory neuron cell bodies and is not an autonomic ganglion. Pick it for sensory cell bodies."],"lt":"t-ans","lid":"c30","comp":193,"competencyId":"w8-ans-ganglia-divisions"},{"id":"L03081","dok":3,"q":"During surgery high in the chest, a structure is cut that runs as a chain of ganglia beside the vertebral column. Which structure was cut?","a":"The sympathetic trunk.","options":["The vagus nerve","The sympathetic trunk","The celiac plexus","The pelvic splanchnic nerves"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The sympathetic trunk is a chain of ganglia that runs on each side of the vertebral column from the base of the skull to the coccyx. In the thorax it lies along the heads of the ribs, beside the vertebral bodies. Cutting it interrupts the sympathetic fibers traveling through that part of the chain.","notes":["The vagus nerve does run through the thorax, but it travels beside the esophagus and is a single nerve, not a chain of ganglia.","Correct. A chain of ganglia beside the vertebral column is the sympathetic trunk.","The celiac plexus lies in the abdomen around the celiac trunk, below the diaphragm. Pick it for the network behind the stomach.","The pelvic splanchnic nerves arise from S2 to S4 and lie in the pelvis. Pick them for parasympathetic supply to the pelvic organs."],"lt":"t-ans","lid":"c31","comp":193,"competencyId":"w8-ans-ganglia-divisions"},{"id":"L03082","dok":2,"q":"Which of these ganglia of the head is a parasympathetic terminal ganglion?","a":"The ciliary ganglion.","options":["The ciliary ganglion","The celiac ganglion","The superior cervical ganglion","The trigeminal ganglion"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The ciliary ganglion is one of the four named parasympathetic terminal ganglia of the head, along with the pterygopalatine, submandibular, and otic ganglia. It sits in the orbit behind the eye and relays parasympathetic fibers from the oculomotor nerve (III) to the muscles that constrict the pupil. As a terminal ganglion, it lies close to the organ it serves.","notes":["Correct. The ciliary ganglion is a parasympathetic terminal ganglion of the head.","The celiac ganglion is a sympathetic prevertebral ganglion in the abdomen, beside the celiac trunk. Its name looks like ciliary, but it is not in the head at all.","The superior cervical ganglion is the top ganglion of the sympathetic trunk, in the neck. It sends sympathetic fibers to the head, so pick it for the sympathetic supply to the head.","The trigeminal ganglion holds the sensory cell bodies of the trigeminal nerve (V). It is a sensory ganglion, not an autonomic one."],"lt":"t-ans","lid":"c32","comp":193,"competencyId":"w8-ans-ganglia-divisions"},{"id":"L03083","dok":2,"q":"An autonomic division leaves the CNS only from the brainstem and the sacral cord. Which division is it?","a":"The parasympathetic division.","options":["The parasympathetic division","The enteric nervous system","The sympathetic division","The somatic motor system"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The parasympathetic division leaves the CNS from the brainstem, through cranial nerves III, VII, IX, and X, and from the sacral cord, S2 to S4. These two ends of the CNS give it the name craniosacral division. The sympathetic division fills the middle instead, leaving from T1 to L2.","notes":["Correct. Brainstem plus sacral cord is the craniosacral, or parasympathetic, outflow.","The enteric nervous system lies in the wall of the gut and is largely independent of the CNS. It does not leave the CNS at all.","The sympathetic division leaves from the middle of the cord, T1 to L2, the thoracolumbar outflow. Pick it for the thoracic and upper lumbar segments.","The somatic motor system sends motor neurons from the brainstem motor nuclei and the anterior horns along the whole cord to skeletal muscle. It is not limited to the two ends of the CNS and is not autonomic."],"lt":"t-ans","lid":"c33","comp":193,"competencyId":"w8-ans-ganglia-divisions"},{"id":"L03084","dok":2,"q":"The vagus nerve carries parasympathetic fibers to the chest and most of the abdomen. Which nerves carry parasympathetic fibers to the pelvic organs?","a":"The pelvic splanchnic nerves.","options":["The pelvic splanchnic nerves","The sympathetic trunks","The thoracic splanchnic nerves","The glossopharyngeal nerves"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The pelvic splanchnic nerves arise from S2 to S4 and carry the sacral parasympathetic fibers to the pelvic organs, such as the bladder, the rectum and lower colon, and the reproductive organs. They pass through the hypogastric plexus on the way. The vagus nerve covers the chest and most of the abdomen, and the pelvic splanchnic nerves take over below that.","notes":["Correct. The pelvic splanchnic nerves carry the sacral parasympathetic outflow to the pelvis.","The sympathetic trunks are chains of sympathetic ganglia. They carry no parasympathetic fibers.","The thoracic splanchnic nerves carry sympathetic fibers from the thoracic sympathetic trunk to the prevertebral ganglia. They share the word splanchnic, but they belong to the other division.","The glossopharyngeal nerves (IX) carry parasympathetic fibers, but only to the parotid salivary gland in the head. Pick them for saliva from the parotid gland."],"lt":"t-ans","lid":"c34","comp":193,"competencyId":"w8-ans-ganglia-divisions"},{"id":"L03085","dok":2,"q":"What does the celiac plexus supply?","a":"Most of the upper abdominal organs.","options":["The urinary bladder and the rectum","Most of the upper abdominal organs","The heart and the great vessels","The bronchial tree of both lungs"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The celiac (solar) plexus is the largest autonomic plexus. It surrounds the celiac trunk, and its fibers spread out to supply most of the upper abdominal organs. The intestines are supplied mainly by the superior and inferior mesenteric plexuses, and the pelvic organs by the hypogastric plexus.","notes":["The bladder and rectum are pelvic organs supplied by the hypogastric plexus. Pick them for the hypogastric plexus.","Correct. The celiac plexus supplies most of the upper abdominal organs.","The heart and great vessels are supplied by the cardiac plexus at the base of the heart. Pick them for the cardiac plexus.","The bronchial tree is supplied by the pulmonary plexus at the root of each lung. Pick it for the pulmonary plexus."],"lt":"t-ans","lid":"c35","comp":193,"competencyId":"w8-ans-ganglia-divisions"},{"id":"L03086","dok":2,"q":"An autonomic neuron has its cell body inside an autonomic ganglion and sends its axon out to a gland. Which neuron is this?","a":"A postganglionic neuron.","options":["A somatic motor neuron","A preganglionic neuron","A postganglionic neuron","A sensory neuron"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"A postganglionic neuron is the second neuron of the autonomic pathway. Its cell body sits in the autonomic ganglion, and its unmyelinated axon runs from the ganglion to the effector, here a gland. The name means after the ganglion, which describes the path of its axon.","notes":["A somatic motor neuron runs from the CNS straight to skeletal muscle, with no ganglion. Pick it for voluntary muscle control.","A preganglionic neuron has its cell body in the brain or spinal cord and sends its axon to the ganglion. Pick it for the first neuron of the pathway.","Correct. A cell body in the ganglion with an axon to the effector is a postganglionic neuron.","A sensory neuron carries input toward the CNS, and its cell body sits in a sensory ganglion such as the dorsal root ganglion. It does not send commands to a gland."],"lt":"t-ans","lid":"c36","comp":192,"competencyId":"w8-ans-pathway"},{"id":"L03087","dok":2,"q":"Which autonomic plexus supplies the kidneys and ureters?","a":"The renal plexus.","options":["The hypogastric plexus","The cardiac plexus","The renal plexus","The pulmonary plexus"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The renal plexus supplies the kidneys and ureters. It forms around the renal arteries, which branch from the abdominal aorta, and its fibers follow those arteries into each kidney. Many autonomic plexuses are named for the organ or vessel they wrap around, and renal means kidney.","notes":["The hypogastric plexus supplies the pelvic organs, including the urinary bladder. Because the bladder continues the urinary pathway, students sometimes extend this plexus up to the kidneys.","The cardiac plexus supplies the heart. Pick it for the plexus at the base of the heart.","Correct. Renal means kidney, and the renal plexus supplies the kidneys and ureters.","The pulmonary plexus supplies the bronchial tree at the root of each lung. Pick it for the lungs."],"lt":"t-ans","lid":"c37","comp":193,"competencyId":"w8-ans-ganglia-divisions"},{"id":"L03088","dok":3,"q":"In an autonomic pathway to the stomach, the second neuron sits in the stomach wall and its axon travels only a few millimeters. Which division does this describe?","a":"The parasympathetic division.","options":["The parasympathetic division","The somatic motor system","The sympathetic division","The visceral sensory system"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"A second neuron sitting in the wall of the organ means the ganglion is an intramural (terminal) ganglion, and terminal ganglia belong to the parasympathetic division. Because the ganglion is at the organ, the postganglionic axon is very short, while the preganglionic axon, here traveling in the vagus nerve, is long. Sympathetic pathways show the opposite pattern.","notes":["Correct. A ganglion in the organ wall with a short postganglionic axon is parasympathetic.","The somatic motor system has only one neuron and no ganglion, so it has no second neuron at all.","The sympathetic division has its ganglia near the spinal cord, so its postganglionic axons are long. Pick it for a second neuron in a chain or prevertebral ganglion.","The visceral sensory system carries input from the organs toward the CNS. It is not a two-neuron motor pathway ending at the organ."],"lt":"t-ans","lid":"c38","comp":193,"competencyId":"w8-ans-ganglia-divisions"},{"id":"L03089","dok":3,"q":"A preganglionic cell body sits in the lateral gray horn of a thoracic spinal segment. To which division does this neuron belong?","a":"The sympathetic division.","options":["The sympathetic division","The parasympathetic division","The enteric nervous system","The somatic motor system"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The lateral gray horn of segments T1 to L2 holds the preganglionic cell bodies of the sympathetic division. A thoracic segment falls within that range, so this neuron is sympathetic. Its axon will leave through the ventral root and head for the sympathetic trunk.","notes":["Correct. A preganglionic cell body in a thoracic lateral horn is sympathetic.","Parasympathetic preganglionic cell bodies sit in brainstem nuclei and in segments S2 to S4. A thoracic segment is outside the parasympathetic outflow.","The enteric nervous system has its neurons in the gut wall, not in the spinal cord. Pick it for the myenteric or submucosal plexus.","Somatic motor neurons sit in the ventral gray horn and have no preganglionic stage. Pick them for a cell body in the ventral horn."],"lt":"t-ans","lid":"c39","comp":193,"competencyId":"w8-ans-ganglia-divisions"},{"id":"L03090","dok":2,"q":"Which choice lists, in order, the structures an autonomic motor command passes through from start to target?","a":"CNS, preganglionic neuron, ganglion, postganglionic neuron, effector.","options":["CNS, preganglionic neuron, postganglionic neuron, ganglion, effector","Ganglion, preganglionic neuron, CNS, postganglionic neuron, effector","CNS, preganglionic neuron, ganglion, postganglionic neuron, effector","CNS, postganglionic neuron, ganglion, preganglionic neuron, effector"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Every autonomic command starts with a preganglionic neuron whose cell body is in the CNS. Its axon runs to an autonomic ganglion and synapses with a postganglionic neuron, whose axon carries the signal the rest of the way to the effector (cardiac muscle, smooth muscle, or a gland). The ganglion always sits between the two neurons.","notes":["This puts both neurons before the ganglion. The ganglion is where the two neurons meet, so it must sit between them.","This starts at the ganglion instead of the CNS. Every autonomic command begins in the brain or spinal cord.","Correct. CNS, preganglionic neuron, ganglion, postganglionic neuron, effector.","This swaps the two neurons. Pre- means before the ganglion and post- means after it, so the preganglionic neuron must come first."],"lt":"t-ans","lid":"c40","comp":192,"competencyId":"w8-ans-pathway"},{"id":"L03091","dok":3,"q":"Which combination correctly pairs the sympathetic division with its outflow and a type of ganglion it uses?","a":"Sympathetic division, thoracolumbar outflow, paravertebral (chain) ganglia.","options":["Sympathetic division, craniosacral outflow, paravertebral (chain) ganglia","Sympathetic division, craniosacral outflow, terminal ganglia","Sympathetic division, thoracolumbar outflow, terminal ganglia","Sympathetic division, thoracolumbar outflow, paravertebral (chain) ganglia"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The sympathetic division leaves the cord from T1 to L2, the thoracolumbar outflow, and many of its fibers synapse in the paravertebral (chain) ganglia of the sympathetic trunk beside the vertebral column. Its other ganglia, the prevertebral ganglia, also sit near the vertebral column. Craniosacral outflow and terminal ganglia both belong to the parasympathetic division.","notes":["The ganglia are right, but craniosacral outflow (brainstem and S2 to S4) is the parasympathetic origin.","Craniosacral outflow and terminal ganglia are both parasympathetic features. This choice describes the parasympathetic division with the wrong name attached.","The outflow is right, but terminal ganglia near or in the organ belong to the parasympathetic division.","Correct. Thoracolumbar outflow and chain ganglia both belong to the sympathetic division."],"lt":"t-ans","lid":"c41","comp":193,"competencyId":"w8-ans-ganglia-divisions"},{"id":"L03092","dok":3,"q":"Which combination correctly pairs the parasympathetic division with its outflow and the length of its preganglionic axon?","a":"Parasympathetic division, craniosacral outflow, long preganglionic axon.","options":["Parasympathetic division, thoracolumbar outflow, short preganglionic axon","Parasympathetic division, thoracolumbar outflow, long preganglionic axon","Parasympathetic division, craniosacral outflow, short preganglionic axon","Parasympathetic division, craniosacral outflow, long preganglionic axon"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The parasympathetic division leaves the CNS from the brainstem and S2 to S4, the craniosacral outflow. Its ganglia sit near or within the target organs, so its preganglionic axons must travel a long way and its postganglionic axons are short. The sympathetic division is the reverse on both points.","notes":["Thoracolumbar outflow and a short preganglionic axon are both sympathetic features. Pick this combination for the sympathetic division.","The axon length is right, but thoracolumbar outflow (T1 to L2) is the sympathetic origin.","The outflow is right, but a short preganglionic axon belongs to the sympathetic division, whose ganglia sit near the cord.","Correct. Craniosacral outflow with a long preganglionic axon is the parasympathetic pattern."],"lt":"t-ans","lid":"c42","comp":193,"competencyId":"w8-ans-ganglia-divisions"},{"id":"L03093","dok":3,"q":"Which statement correctly compares the two autonomic divisions on ganglion location and postganglionic axon length?","a":"Sympathetic ganglia lie near the cord with long postganglionic axons; parasympathetic ganglia lie near the organ with short ones.","options":["Sympathetic ganglia lie near the organ with long postganglionic axons; parasympathetic ganglia lie near the cord with short ones","Sympathetic ganglia lie near the organ with short postganglionic axons; parasympathetic ganglia lie near the cord with long ones","Sympathetic ganglia lie near the cord with short postganglionic axons; parasympathetic ganglia lie near the organ with long ones","Sympathetic ganglia lie near the cord with long postganglionic axons; parasympathetic ganglia lie near the organ with short ones"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The sympathetic ganglia sit close to the spinal cord, so the postganglionic axon has to run a long way out to the organ. The parasympathetic ganglia sit near or within the organ, so the postganglionic axon is short. Where the ganglion sits decides how long the axon leaving it must be.","notes":["This puts the sympathetic ganglia at the organ and the parasympathetic ganglia near the cord, which is backward, and the lengths do not fit those locations either.","This swaps the two divisions completely. Sympathetic ganglia are near the cord, and parasympathetic ganglia are at the organ.","The ganglion locations are right, but the axon lengths are reversed. A ganglion near the cord must send a long axon to reach a distant organ.","Correct. Ganglia near the cord give long postganglionic axons; ganglia at the organ give short ones."],"lt":"t-ans","lid":"c43","comp":193,"competencyId":"w8-ans-ganglia-divisions"},{"id":"L03094","dok":3,"q":"A sympathetic command travels from the spinal cord to a distant organ. Which list names, in order, the structures it passes through?","a":"Lateral horn, preganglionic axon, sympathetic chain ganglion, postganglionic axon, effector.","options":["Sympathetic chain ganglion, lateral horn, preganglionic axon, postganglionic axon, effector","Lateral horn, preganglionic axon, sympathetic chain ganglion, postganglionic axon, effector","Lateral horn, postganglionic axon, sympathetic chain ganglion, preganglionic axon, effector","Dorsal horn, preganglionic axon, terminal ganglion, postganglionic axon, effector"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"A sympathetic command starts at a preganglionic cell body in the lateral gray horn of a segment between T1 and L2. The preganglionic axon leaves through the ventral root and synapses in a sympathetic chain ganglion, and the postganglionic axon then runs out to the effector. Preganglionic always comes before the ganglion and postganglionic after it.","notes":["This starts at the chain ganglion instead of the cord. Every autonomic command begins with a cell body in the CNS, here the lateral horn.","Correct. Lateral horn, preganglionic axon, chain ganglion, postganglionic axon, effector.","This swaps the two axons. The preganglionic axon must reach the ganglion before the postganglionic axon can leave it.","The dorsal horn receives sensory input and holds no autonomic motor neurons, and terminal ganglia are parasympathetic. This list mixes in two wrong structures."],"lt":"t-ans","lid":"c44","comp":193,"competencyId":"w8-ans-ganglia-divisions"},{"id":"L03095","dok":3,"q":"Which set of structures all belong to the parasympathetic division?","a":"Cranial nerve X, the pelvic splanchnic nerves, and terminal ganglia.","options":["Cranial nerve X, the pelvic splanchnic nerves, and terminal ganglia","The sympathetic trunk, the thoracic splanchnic nerves, and prevertebral ganglia","Cranial nerve X, the sympathetic trunk, and paravertebral ganglia","The pelvic splanchnic nerves, the lateral horn, and chain ganglia"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The vagus nerve (cranial nerve X) carries the cranial parasympathetic outflow to the chest and abdomen, the pelvic splanchnic nerves carry the sacral outflow from S2 to S4, and terminal ganglia are where parasympathetic fibers synapse near or in the organ. All three belong to the craniosacral, or parasympathetic, division. The sympathetic trunk, the lateral horn, and the chain and prevertebral ganglia all belong to the sympathetic division.","notes":["Correct. The vagus nerve, pelvic splanchnic nerves, and terminal ganglia are all parasympathetic.","Every item here is sympathetic: the trunk, the thoracic splanchnic nerves, and the prevertebral ganglia. Pick this set for the sympathetic division.","The vagus nerve is parasympathetic, but the sympathetic trunk and its paravertebral ganglia are sympathetic. Only one item fits.","The pelvic splanchnic nerves are parasympathetic, but the lateral horn and chain ganglia are sympathetic. Only one item fits."],"lt":"t-ans","lid":"c45","comp":193,"competencyId":"w8-ans-ganglia-divisions"},{"id":"L03096","dok":1,"q":"Which of these effectors is controlled by the somatic motor system rather than the autonomic nervous system?","a":"Skeletal muscle of the arm.","options":["The salivary glands","Cardiac muscle of the heart","Skeletal muscle of the arm","Smooth muscle of the bronchioles"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Skeletal muscle, such as the muscles of the arm, is controlled by the somatic motor system, normally under voluntary control. The autonomic nervous system controls the other three kinds of effector: cardiac muscle, smooth muscle, and glands. Sorting by tissue type is the quickest way to tell which motor system is in charge.","notes":["Glands, including the salivary glands, are autonomic effectors. Pick them for autonomic control of secretion.","Cardiac muscle is an autonomic effector; the ANS speeds or slows the heart. Pick it for autonomic control.","Correct. Skeletal muscle is the effector of the somatic motor system.","Smooth muscle in the walls of the bronchioles is an autonomic effector; the sympathetic division opens the airways. Pick it for autonomic control."],"lt":"t-ans","lid":"c1-x2","comp":192,"competencyId":"w8-ans-pathway"},{"id":"L03097","dok":1,"q":"Which two divisions make up the autonomic nervous system?","a":"The sympathetic and parasympathetic divisions.","options":["The somatic and enteric motor divisions","The sympathetic and somatic motor divisions","The sympathetic and parasympathetic divisions","The afferent (sensory) and efferent (motor) divisions"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The autonomic nervous system has two divisions, the sympathetic and the parasympathetic. They usually act in opposite directions on the same organ: the sympathetic division prepares the body for action (fight or flight), and the parasympathetic division conserves energy (rest and digest). The enteric nervous system in the gut wall works alongside them but is described separately.","notes":["The somatic system is a separate motor system for skeletal muscle, not part of the ANS. The enteric system is described separately as well.","The sympathetic division is right, but the somatic motor system controls skeletal muscle and is not autonomic.","Correct. The ANS has a sympathetic and a parasympathetic division.","Afferent and efferent divide the whole peripheral nervous system into sensory and motor parts. The ANS sits entirely on the efferent (motor) side."],"lt":"t-ans","lid":"c2-x2","comp":193,"competencyId":"w8-ans-ganglia-divisions"},{"id":"L03098","dok":1,"q":"How many motor neurons does the autonomic pathway use between the CNS and the effector?","a":"Two, with a synapse in a ganglion.","options":["Two, with a synapse in a ganglion","One, running straight to the effector","Three, with two ganglia","None, since it relies on sensory neurons only"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The autonomic motor pathway always uses two neurons in a row. The preganglionic neuron runs from the CNS to an autonomic ganglion and synapses there, and the postganglionic neuron carries the signal on to the effector. The somatic pathway, by comparison, uses only one motor neuron.","notes":["Correct. Two neurons, with a synapse in a ganglion.","One neuron running straight to the effector is the somatic pattern for skeletal muscle. Pick it for the somatic motor system.","No autonomic pathway uses three motor neurons. Students sometimes count the ganglion as an extra neuron, but it is simply where the two neurons meet.","The ANS is a motor system that sends commands out to effectors. Sensory neurons carry input in the other direction."],"lt":"t-ans","lid":"c3-x2","comp":192,"competencyId":"w8-ans-pathway"},{"id":"L03099","dok":1,"q":"What is the first neuron of the autonomic two-neuron pathway called?","a":"The preganglionic neuron.","options":["The interneuron","The preganglionic neuron","The sensory neuron","The postganglionic neuron"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The first neuron of the autonomic pathway is the preganglionic neuron. Its cell body sits in the brain or spinal cord, and its thin, lightly myelinated axon runs out to an autonomic ganglion. Pre- means before, so this is the neuron before the ganglion.","notes":["An interneuron lies entirely within the CNS and links other neurons. It is not one of the two motor neurons of the autonomic chain.","Correct. The preganglionic neuron comes first.","A sensory neuron carries input toward the CNS. The autonomic two-neuron pathway is motor, carrying commands outward.","The postganglionic neuron is the second neuron, with its cell body in the ganglion. Pick it for the neuron after the ganglion."],"lt":"t-ans","lid":"c4-x2","comp":192,"competencyId":"w8-ans-pathway"},{"id":"L03100","dok":2,"q":"Which of these axons is typically unmyelinated?","a":"The autonomic postganglionic axon.","options":["The preganglionic axon","A somatic motor axon to skeletal muscle","The postganglionic axon","A sensory axon from a lamellar corpuscle"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"In the autonomic pathway, the preganglionic axon is lightly myelinated and the postganglionic axon is unmyelinated. Myelin is the fatty wrapping around an axon, and postganglionic axons lack it. The synapse in the ganglion marks the switch from the lightly myelinated first axon to the unmyelinated second one.","notes":["The preganglionic axon is lightly myelinated, so it does have some myelin. Pick it if the question asked which autonomic axon has myelin.","Somatic motor axons to skeletal muscle are myelinated, which helps them carry fast commands. Pick them for a myelinated motor axon.","Correct. Autonomic postganglionic axons are unmyelinated.","Sensory axons from lamellar corpuscles are myelinated. Pick them for a myelinated sensory axon."],"lt":"t-ans","lid":"c5-x2","comp":192,"competencyId":"w8-ans-pathway"},{"id":"L03101","dok":1,"q":"By what other name is the sympathetic division known?","a":"The thoracolumbar division.","options":["The craniosacral division","The enteric division","The somatic division","The thoracolumbar division"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The sympathetic division is also called the thoracolumbar division because its preganglionic neurons leave the spinal cord from the thoracic and upper lumbar segments, T1 to L2. The name is a map of where the division begins. Its partner, the parasympathetic division, is the craniosacral division.","notes":["The craniosacral division is the parasympathetic division, which leaves from the brainstem and S2 to S4. Pick it for the parasympathetic division.","The enteric nervous system is the network in the gut wall, not another name for the sympathetic division.","The somatic division controls skeletal muscle and is not autonomic. Pick it for voluntary motor control.","Correct. The sympathetic division is the thoracolumbar division."],"lt":"t-ans","lid":"c6-x2","comp":193,"competencyId":"w8-ans-ganglia-divisions"},{"id":"L03102","dok":1,"q":"By what other name is the parasympathetic division known?","a":"The craniosacral division.","options":["The craniosacral division","The thoracolumbar division","The enteric division","The somatic division"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The parasympathetic division is also called the craniosacral division because its preganglionic neurons leave from the brainstem (through cranial nerves III, VII, IX, and X) and from the sacral cord, S2 to S4. Cranio- refers to the head and sacral to the lowest part of the cord. Its partner, the sympathetic division, is the thoracolumbar division.","notes":["Correct. The parasympathetic division is the craniosacral division.","The thoracolumbar division is the sympathetic division, which leaves from T1 to L2. Pick it for the sympathetic division.","The enteric nervous system is the network in the gut wall. It is not another name for the parasympathetic division, though the vagus nerve connects to it.","The somatic division controls skeletal muscle and is not autonomic. Pick it for voluntary motor control."],"lt":"t-ans","lid":"c7-x2","comp":193,"competencyId":"w8-ans-ganglia-divisions"},{"id":"L03103","dok":2,"q":"A cross section of the thoracic spinal cord shows a small lateral gray horn on each side. Which cell bodies does the lateral horn hold?","a":"Sympathetic preganglionic neurons.","options":["Sympathetic postganglionic neurons","Sympathetic preganglionic neurons","Somatic motor neurons","Sensory neurons"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The lateral gray horn holds the cell bodies of sympathetic preganglionic neurons. It is found in the thoracic and upper lumbar segments, T1 to L2, which is exactly where the sympathetic division leaves the cord. The axons of these neurons exit through the ventral root and head for the sympathetic trunk.","notes":["Sympathetic postganglionic cell bodies sit in the sympathetic trunk and prevertebral ganglia, outside the CNS. Pick them for a sympathetic ganglion.","Correct. The lateral horn holds sympathetic preganglionic cell bodies.","Somatic motor neurons sit in the ventral gray horn and drive skeletal muscle. Pick them for the ventral horn.","Sensory neuron cell bodies sit outside the cord in the dorsal root ganglia. Pick them for the dorsal root ganglion."],"lt":"t-ans","lid":"c8-x2","comp":193,"competencyId":"w8-ans-ganglia-divisions"},{"id":"L03104","dok":1,"q":"From which structures does the parasympathetic division arise?","a":"Four cranial nerves (III, VII, IX, X) and the S2 to S4 segments.","options":["Cranial nerves III, VII, IX, X and cord segments S2 to S4","The lateral gray horns of the spinal segments from T1 to L2","The dorsal root ganglia of the sacral spinal nerves","The lateral gray horns found in every segment of the spinal cord"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The parasympathetic division arises from the nuclei of four cranial nerves in the brainstem (III, VII, IX, and X) and from the S2 to S4 segments of the spinal cord. These two ends of the CNS give it the name craniosacral division. The middle of the cord, T1 to L2, belongs to the sympathetic division instead.","notes":["Correct. Four cranial nerves plus S2 to S4 make up the craniosacral outflow.","The lateral gray horns of T1 to L2 are the origin of the sympathetic division. Pick them for the sympathetic division.","The dorsal root ganglia hold sensory neuron cell bodies, not autonomic motor neurons. Pick them for sensory cell bodies.","The lateral horn is not found in every segment; it appears in the thoracic and upper lumbar cord. Neither division arises from every segment."],"lt":"t-ans","lid":"c9-x2","comp":193,"competencyId":"w8-ans-ganglia-divisions"},{"id":"L03105","dok":1,"q":"Which phrase describes the overall role of the sympathetic division?","a":"Fight or flight.","options":["Producing cerebrospinal fluid","Fight or flight","Forming new memories","Rest and digest"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The sympathetic division is the fight-or-flight division. It takes over during exercise, excitement, emergency, and embarrassment, preparing the body for action by speeding the heart, opening the airways, dilating the pupils, and slowing digestion. Its effects spread through many organs at once.","notes":["Cerebrospinal fluid is produced by the choroid plexuses in the ventricles of the brain. Pick it for the choroid plexus, not the ANS.","Correct. The sympathetic division prepares the body for fight or flight.","Forming new memories is a job of brain structures in the limbic system, such as the hippocampus. It is not an autonomic role.","Rest and digest is the role of the parasympathetic division, which conserves energy and runs digestion. Pick it for the parasympathetic division."],"lt":"t-ans","lid":"c10-x2","comp":193,"competencyId":"w8-ans-ganglia-divisions"},{"id":"L03106","dok":1,"q":"Which phrase describes the overall role of the parasympathetic division?","a":"Rest and digest.","options":["Sensory relay to the cortex","Rest and digest","Voluntary movement","Fight or flight"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The parasympathetic division is the rest-and-digest division. It conserves energy and runs routine housekeeping such as digestion, defecation, and urination, while slowing the heart and constricting the pupils. It usually pulls each organ in the opposite direction from the sympathetic division.","notes":["Relaying sensory signals to the cortex is a job of the thalamus in the diencephalon. It is not an autonomic role.","Correct. The parasympathetic division handles rest and digest.","Voluntary movement is controlled by the somatic motor system acting on skeletal muscle. Pick it for the somatic system.","Fight or flight is the role of the sympathetic division, which prepares the body for action. Pick it for the sympathetic division."],"lt":"t-ans","lid":"c11-x2","comp":193,"competencyId":"w8-ans-ganglia-divisions"},{"id":"L03107","dok":1,"q":"What are the parasympathetic ganglia, located near or within the target organ, called?","a":"Terminal ganglia.","options":["Dorsal root ganglia","Prevertebral ganglia","Terminal ganglia","Sympathetic trunk ganglia"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The parasympathetic ganglia are called terminal ganglia because they sit at the end of the pathway, near or within the wall of the target organ. Those inside the organ wall are also called intramural ganglia. The four named terminal ganglia of the head are the ciliary, pterygopalatine, submandibular, and otic ganglia.","notes":["Dorsal root ganglia hold sensory neuron cell bodies and are not autonomic. Pick them for sensory cell bodies.","Prevertebral ganglia are sympathetic ganglia in front of the vertebral column near the abdominal arteries. Pick them for the celiac or mesenteric ganglia.","Correct. Parasympathetic ganglia at the organ are terminal ganglia.","Sympathetic trunk ganglia form the chain beside the vertebral column. They are sympathetic, far from the organs."],"lt":"t-ans","lid":"c12-x2","comp":193,"competencyId":"w8-ans-ganglia-divisions"},{"id":"L03108","dok":2,"q":"How far does each sympathetic trunk extend along the vertebral column?","a":"From the base of the skull to the coccyx.","options":["Only along the cervical vertebrae","Only from T1 to L2, where the outflow leaves","From the base of the skull to the coccyx","From the brainstem to the S2 to S4 segments"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Each sympathetic trunk runs the whole length of the vertebral column, from the base of the skull to the coccyx. Preganglionic fibers enter it only from T1 to L2, but they can travel up or down the chain before synapsing, which is how sympathetic fibers reach the head and the lower limbs. That is why the trunk is much longer than the region of sympathetic outflow.","notes":["The trunk does have ganglia in the neck, but it continues down the thoracic, lumbar, and sacral regions as well.","T1 to L2 is where sympathetic fibers enter the trunk, not the full length of the trunk. The chain continues above and below so it can serve the whole body.","Correct. The trunk runs from the base of the skull to the coccyx.","The brainstem and S2 to S4 are the origins of the parasympathetic division. They describe the craniosacral outflow, not the length of a sympathetic structure."],"lt":"t-ans","lid":"c13-x2","comp":193,"competencyId":"w8-ans-ganglia-divisions"},{"id":"L03109","dok":1,"q":"Which enteric plexus lies between the longitudinal and circular muscle layers of the gut wall?","a":"The myenteric plexus.","options":["The myenteric plexus","The celiac plexus","The submucosal plexus","The cardiac plexus"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The myenteric plexus lies in the muscularis externa, between the circular and longitudinal layers of smooth muscle in the gut wall. My- refers to muscle and enteric refers to the intestine, so the name describes a nerve network among the gut muscles. It mainly controls the movement of the gut.","notes":["Correct. The myenteric plexus sits between the two muscle layers.","The celiac plexus surrounds the celiac trunk outside the gut and supplies most of the upper abdominal organs. It is not one of the enteric plexuses within the gut wall.","The submucosal plexus lies in the submucosa, closer to the lumen than the muscle layers, and mainly controls the secretions of the gut. Pick it for gut secretion.","The cardiac plexus is an autonomic plexus at the base of the heart. It is not in the gut wall."],"lt":"t-ans","lid":"c14-x2","comp":110,"competencyId":"w5-enteric-plexuses"},{"id":"L03110","dok":1,"q":"Which is the largest autonomic plexus, surrounding the celiac trunk?","a":"The celiac (solar) plexus.","options":["The hypogastric plexus","The celiac plexus","The pulmonary plexus","The cardiac plexus"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The celiac plexus, also called the solar plexus, is the largest autonomic plexus. It surrounds the celiac trunk, the first large unpaired branch of the abdominal aorta, and supplies most of the upper abdominal organs. The celiac ganglia sit inside it, and many sympathetic fibers synapse there.","notes":["The hypogastric plexus lies low in the abdominopelvic cavity and supplies the pelvic organs. Pick it for the bladder and rectum.","Correct. The celiac plexus is the largest and wraps the celiac trunk.","The pulmonary plexus lies at the root of each lung and supplies the bronchial tree. Pick it for the lungs.","The cardiac plexus lies at the base of the heart and supplies the heart. Pick it for the heart."],"lt":"t-ans","lid":"c15-x2","comp":193,"competencyId":"w8-ans-ganglia-divisions"},{"id":"L03111","dok":2,"q":"A patient's pupils dilate, heart speeds up, and digestion slows. Which division is dominant?","a":"The sympathetic division.","options":["The somatic motor system","The sympathetic division","The enteric nervous system","The parasympathetic division"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Dilated pupils, a faster heart, and slowed digestion are classic effects of the sympathetic division. Together they prepare the body for action, the fight-or-flight response. The parasympathetic division would produce the opposite pattern: constricted pupils, a slower heart, and active digestion.","notes":["The somatic motor system controls skeletal muscle, not the pupils, heart, or gut. Pick it for voluntary movement.","Correct. These are fight-or-flight effects of the sympathetic division.","The enteric nervous system runs the gut wall, and here digestion is slowing, not being driven. It also has no control over the pupils or heart.","The parasympathetic division constricts the pupils, slows the heart, and stimulates digestion, the opposite of this pattern. Pick it for a person resting after a meal."],"lt":"t-ans","lid":"c16-x2","comp":193,"competencyId":"w8-ans-ganglia-divisions"},{"id":"L03112","dok":2,"q":"How does the somatic motor pathway differ from the autonomic motor pathway in the number of motor neurons?","a":"Somatic uses one neuron; autonomic uses two.","options":["Somatic uses one neuron; autonomic uses two","Both use a single neuron from the CNS to the effector","Both use two neurons with a ganglion in between","Somatic uses two neurons; autonomic uses one"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The somatic motor pathway uses one neuron that runs from the CNS straight to skeletal muscle. The autonomic pathway uses two, a preganglionic neuron from the CNS and a postganglionic neuron from a ganglion to the effector. The synapse in the ganglion is the feature the somatic system lacks.","notes":["Correct. One neuron for somatic, two for autonomic.","A single neuron describes only the somatic system. The autonomic system always adds a second neuron after the ganglion.","Two neurons with a ganglion describes only the autonomic system. The somatic system has no ganglion on its motor path.","This reverses the two systems. The autonomic system is the one with a ganglion, so it is the one with two neurons."],"lt":"t-ans","lid":"c17-x2","comp":192,"competencyId":"w8-ans-pathway"},{"id":"L03113","dok":2,"q":"Somatic motor neurons always excite their effector. What effect can autonomic motor neurons have on their effectors?","a":"Either excitation or inhibition.","options":["Always excitation","It has no direct effect on the effector","Always inhibition","Either excitation or inhibition"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Somatic motor neurons always excite skeletal muscle, causing it to contract. Autonomic motor neurons can either excite or inhibit their effectors, depending on the division and the organ. For example, the sympathetic division speeds the heart but slows digestion, and the parasympathetic division does the reverse.","notes":["Always excitation is true of the somatic system acting on skeletal muscle. Pick it for the somatic system.","Autonomic motor neurons act directly on cardiac muscle, smooth muscle, and glands. That is the whole purpose of the visceral motor system.","If autonomic neurons only inhibited, nothing could speed the heart or stimulate digestion. Each division excites some organs and inhibits others.","Correct. Autonomic neurons can excite or inhibit."],"lt":"t-ans","lid":"c18-x2","comp":192,"competencyId":"w8-ans-pathway"},{"id":"L03114","dok":2,"q":"In the somatic motor system, where does the cell body of the single motor neuron to a limb muscle sit?","a":"In the ventral gray horn of the spinal cord.","options":["In the dorsal root ganglion","In the ventral gray horn","In the lateral gray horn","In an autonomic ganglion"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The somatic motor neuron to a limb muscle has its cell body in the ventral (anterior) gray horn of the spinal cord. Its axon leaves through the ventral root and runs, without any ganglion, all the way to the skeletal muscle. This single-neuron path is the main structural difference from the two-neuron autonomic pathway.","notes":["The dorsal root ganglion holds sensory neuron cell bodies. Pick it for sensory neurons, not motor neurons.","Correct. Somatic motor neurons sit in the ventral gray horn.","The lateral gray horn holds sympathetic preganglionic cell bodies. Pick it for the first neuron of a sympathetic pathway.","An autonomic ganglion holds postganglionic cell bodies. The somatic pathway has no ganglion at all."],"lt":"t-ans","lid":"c19-x2","comp":192,"competencyId":"w8-ans-pathway"},{"id":"L03115","dok":2,"q":"Where are the ganglia of the sympathetic division located?","a":"Close to the spinal cord.","options":["Inside the brainstem","Inside the dorsal root ganglion","Close to the spinal cord","Close to or within the effector organ"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Sympathetic ganglia sit close to the spinal cord, either in the sympathetic trunk beside the vertebral column or in the prevertebral ganglia just in front of it. This placement gives the sympathetic division short preganglionic axons and long postganglionic axons. Parasympathetic ganglia, by contrast, sit close to or within the effector organ.","notes":["The brainstem is part of the CNS, and ganglia by definition lie outside the CNS. The brainstem holds the parasympathetic preganglionic nuclei instead.","The dorsal root ganglion is a sensory ganglion on the dorsal root. Autonomic ganglia are separate structures.","Correct. Sympathetic ganglia lie close to the spinal cord.","Close to or within the effector organ describes the parasympathetic terminal ganglia. Pick it for the parasympathetic division."],"lt":"t-ans","lid":"c20-x2","comp":193,"competencyId":"w8-ans-ganglia-divisions"},{"id":"L03117","dok":2,"q":"Which group of sympathetic ganglia lies anterior to the vertebral column, near the large abdominal arteries?","a":"The prevertebral (collateral) ganglia.","options":["The prevertebral ganglia","The terminal ganglia","The dorsal root ganglia","The sympathetic trunk ganglia"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The prevertebral ganglia, also called collateral ganglia, are sympathetic ganglia that lie anterior to the vertebral column near the large abdominal arteries. They include the celiac, superior mesenteric, inferior mesenteric, aorticorenal, and renal ganglia. Pre- means in front of, which describes their position relative to the vertebrae.","notes":["Correct. The prevertebral ganglia sit in front of the vertebral column by the abdominal arteries.","Terminal ganglia are parasympathetic and sit near or within the target organ. Pick them for a ganglion at the organ.","Dorsal root ganglia hold sensory cell bodies on the dorsal roots. Pick them for sensory neurons.","The sympathetic trunk ganglia lie beside the vertebral column in a chain, not in front of it. Pick them for the paravertebral chain."],"lt":"t-ans","lid":"c22-x2","comp":193,"competencyId":"w8-ans-ganglia-divisions"},{"id":"L03118","dok":2,"q":"Which enteric plexus controls the secretions of the gut?","a":"The submucosal plexus.","options":["The hypogastric plexus","The submucosal plexus","The cardiac plexus","The myenteric plexus"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The submucosal plexus lies in the submucosa, between the mucosa and the muscularis externa. Its place next to the mucosa, where the glands are, fits its job of mainly controlling the secretions of the gut. The myenteric plexus, farther out between the muscle layers, mainly controls movement instead.","notes":["The hypogastric plexus is an autonomic plexus that supplies the pelvic organs. It sits outside the gut wall.","Correct. The submucosal plexus controls gut secretions.","The cardiac plexus supplies the heart and is not in the gut wall. Pick it for the heart.","The myenteric plexus lies between the longitudinal and circular muscle layers and controls gut movement. Pick it for movement of the gut."],"lt":"t-ans","lid":"c23-x2","comp":193,"competencyId":"w8-ans-ganglia-divisions"},{"id":"L03119","dok":3,"q":"Which list traces, in order, a parasympathetic command from the brainstem to the heart?","a":"Brainstem nucleus, vagus nerve, terminal ganglion near the heart, short postganglionic axon, heart.","options":["Brainstem nucleus, vagus nerve, terminal ganglion near the heart, short postganglionic axon, heart","Brainstem nucleus, vagus nerve, sympathetic chain ganglion, long postganglionic axon, heart","Lateral horn of T1, preganglionic axon, terminal ganglion near the heart, short postganglionic axon, heart","Brainstem nucleus, phrenic nerve, terminal ganglion near the heart, short postganglionic axon, heart"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The parasympathetic supply to the heart starts in a brainstem nucleus of the vagus nerve (CN X). The long preganglionic axon travels in the vagus nerve, through the cardiac plexus, to a terminal ganglion near or in the heart, and a short postganglionic axon carries the signal the rest of the way. This follows the parasympathetic pattern of a long first axon and a short second one.","notes":["Correct. Brainstem, vagus nerve, terminal ganglion, short postganglionic axon, heart.","Parasympathetic fibers do not synapse in the sympathetic chain, and a long postganglionic axon is a sympathetic feature. This list mixes the two divisions.","The lateral horn of T1 is a sympathetic origin, not a parasympathetic one. Parasympathetic fibers to the heart start in the brainstem.","The phrenic nerve comes from the cervical plexus and is the motor supply to the diaphragm. It carries no parasympathetic fibers to the heart."],"lt":"t-ans","lid":"c24-x2","comp":193,"competencyId":"w8-ans-ganglia-divisions"},{"id":"L03120","dok":2,"q":"Which organs do the superior and inferior mesenteric plexuses supply?","a":"The small and large intestine.","options":["The urinary bladder and uterus","The small and large intestine","The kidneys and the ureters","The heart and the bronchial tree"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The superior and inferior mesenteric plexuses supply the small and large intestine. They surround the superior and inferior mesenteric arteries, which carry blood to the intestines, and their fibers follow those arteries. They sit just below the celiac plexus along the abdominal aorta.","notes":["The urinary bladder and uterus are pelvic organs supplied by the hypogastric plexus. Pick them for the hypogastric plexus.","Correct. The mesenteric plexuses supply the intestines.","The kidneys and ureters are supplied by the renal plexus around the renal arteries. Pick them for the renal plexus.","The heart and bronchial tree are supplied by the cardiac and pulmonary plexuses in the thorax. Pick them for those plexuses."],"lt":"t-ans","lid":"c25-x2","comp":193,"competencyId":"w8-ans-ganglia-divisions"},{"id":"L03121","dok":3,"q":"A person's heart rate rises at the start of exercise, even before the sympathetic division speeds it up. Which change best explains the early rise?","a":"Parasympathetic input is withdrawn.","options":["The enteric system stimulates the heart","The sympathetic trunk is cut on both sides","Parasympathetic input is withdrawn","The somatic system contracts the heart"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"At rest, parasympathetic input from the vagus nerve holds the heart at a slower rate. Because the two divisions pull in opposite directions, easing off this parasympathetic brake lets the heart rate rise even before the sympathetic division begins to speed it up. This shows how the body can adjust an organ by withdrawing one division, not only by pushing with the other.","notes":["The enteric nervous system lives in the wall of the gut and does not supply the heart. Pick it for gut movement and secretion.","Cutting the sympathetic trunk would remove the input that speeds the heart, so it could not explain a rise. Pick it for a loss of sympathetic effects.","Correct. Withdrawing the parasympathetic brake lets the heart rate climb.","The somatic system controls skeletal muscle only; cardiac muscle is an autonomic effector. Pick the somatic system for the leg muscles used in exercise."],"lt":"t-ans","lid":"c26-x2","comp":193,"competencyId":"w8-ans-ganglia-divisions"},{"id":"L03122","dok":3,"q":"A single cranial nerve slows the heart, constricts the airways, and drives digestion all at once. Which nerve, through its long reach, does this?","a":"The vagus nerve (CN X).","options":["The accessory nerve (CN XI)","The phrenic nerve","The vagus nerve (CN X)","The trigeminal nerve (CN V)"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The vagus nerve, cranial nerve X, carries most of the parasympathetic supply to the organs of the chest and abdomen. It leaves the skull through the jugular foramen and travels down through the neck and thorax into the abdomen, branching to the heart, lungs, and digestive tract. Its long reach is why one nerve can slow the heart, constrict the airways, and drive digestion at the same time.","notes":["The accessory nerve (XI) leaves the skull beside the vagus but is a motor nerve to the sternocleidomastoid and trapezius. Pick it for turning the head and shrugging.","The phrenic nerve is a branch of the cervical plexus, not a cranial nerve, and it is the motor supply to the diaphragm. It does not reach the heart or the gut.","Correct. The vagus nerve reaches the heart, lungs, and digestive tract.","The trigeminal nerve (V) carries facial sensation and serves the chewing muscles. It carries no parasympathetic fibers to the organs."],"lt":"t-ans","lid":"c27-x2","comp":193,"competencyId":"w8-ans-ganglia-divisions"},{"id":"L03123","dok":3,"q":"A section of bowel keeps moving food along even after its connection to the CNS is cut. Which system explains this independence?","a":"The enteric nervous system.","options":["The enteric nervous system","The cervical plexus","The sympathetic division","The somatic nervous system"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The enteric nervous system is the network of neurons in the wall of the digestive tract, built around the myenteric and submucosal plexuses. It is independent enough to be called the brain of the gut: smooth muscle keeps contracting in rhythm and glands keep secreting even when the link to the CNS is cut. That is why a denervated bowel can still move food along.","notes":["Correct. The enteric nervous system can run the gut on its own.","The cervical plexus serves the neck and diaphragm and has no role in the bowel. Pick it for the phrenic nerve.","The sympathetic division reaches the gut from the CNS through the trunk and splanchnic nerves. Cutting the CNS connection removes it, and it slows digestion rather than driving it.","The somatic nervous system controls skeletal muscle through nerves from the CNS. Cutting the link to the CNS would stop it, and the gut wall is mostly smooth muscle anyway."],"lt":"t-ans","lid":"c28-x2","comp":193,"competencyId":"w8-ans-ganglia-divisions"},{"id":"L03124","dok":3,"q":"A cluster of neuron cell bodies sits within the wall of the urinary bladder, where parasympathetic fibers synapse. Which type of ganglion is it?","a":"A terminal ganglion.","options":["A dorsal root ganglion","A sympathetic trunk ganglion","A prevertebral ganglion","A terminal ganglion"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"A ganglion in the wall of an organ where parasympathetic fibers synapse is a terminal ganglion, specifically an intramural one. The parasympathetic fibers to the bladder arrive in the pelvic splanchnic nerves from S2 to S4 and synapse right at the organ. Short postganglionic axons then reach the smooth muscle of the bladder wall.","notes":["A dorsal root ganglion holds sensory cell bodies on the dorsal root, not autonomic synapses. Pick it for sensory neurons.","A sympathetic trunk ganglion lies beside the vertebral column, far from the bladder. Pick it for the paravertebral chain.","A prevertebral ganglion is a sympathetic ganglion in front of the vertebral column near the abdominal arteries. Pick it for the celiac or mesenteric ganglia.","Correct. A parasympathetic ganglion in the organ wall is a terminal (intramural) ganglion."],"lt":"t-ans","lid":"c29-x2","comp":193,"competencyId":"w8-ans-ganglia-divisions"},{"id":"L03125","dok":3,"q":"After a large meal, a resting person has constricted pupils, a slow heart rate, and active digestion. Which division is dominant?","a":"The parasympathetic division.","options":["The sympathetic division","The somatic motor system","The parasympathetic division","The enteric nervous system"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Constricted pupils, a slower heart, and stimulated digestion are effects of the parasympathetic division, the rest-and-digest division. After a meal at rest, the body conserves energy and focuses on housekeeping such as digestion. The sympathetic division would produce the opposite pattern.","notes":["The sympathetic division would dilate the pupils, speed the heart, and slow digestion. Pick it for a person under stress.","The somatic motor system controls skeletal muscle and has no say over the pupils, heart, or gut. Pick it for voluntary movement.","Correct. These are rest-and-digest effects of the parasympathetic division.","The enteric nervous system helps run digestion inside the gut wall, but it cannot constrict the pupils or slow the heart. Only one of the three signs fits it."],"lt":"t-ans","lid":"c30-x2","comp":193,"competencyId":"w8-ans-ganglia-divisions"},{"id":"L03126","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly describes how the autonomic nervous system controls the organs?","a":"It works involuntarily, adjusting the organs without conscious effort.","options":["It works only during sleep and shuts off when we are awake","It works voluntarily, so we decide how fast the heart beats","It works by sending signals straight from the brain to skeletal muscle","It works involuntarily, adjusting the organs without conscious effort"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The autonomic nervous system is the involuntary motor division. It adjusts cardiac muscle, smooth muscle, and glands without conscious effort or awareness, so vital work such as the heartbeat and digestion continues while we sleep or focus on other things. Emotion and pain can influence it, but most of its activity cannot be consciously changed.","notes":["The ANS works around the clock, awake or asleep. The heart and gut never stop needing control.","Voluntary control describes the somatic system acting on skeletal muscle. We cannot simply decide how fast the heart beats.","Signals straight to skeletal muscle with one neuron describe the somatic motor system. The ANS uses two neurons and targets cardiac muscle, smooth muscle, and glands.","Correct. The ANS controls the organs involuntarily."],"lt":"t-ans","lid":"d3-t-ans-1","comp":192,"competencyId":"w8-ans-pathway"},{"id":"L03128","dok":3,"q":"After a stressful event ends, which part of the nervous system brings the heart rate back down and restarts digestion?","a":"The parasympathetic division.","options":["The parasympathetic division","The sympathetic division","The adrenal medulla","The somatic motor system"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The parasympathetic division restores resting conditions after activity. It slows the heart, constricts the pupils, and stimulates digestion, conserving energy for routine housekeeping. Because it pulls the organs in the opposite direction from the sympathetic division, it gives the body a way back from fight or flight.","notes":["Correct. The parasympathetic division restores rest and digest.","The sympathetic division is what sped the heart and slowed digestion in the first place. Pick it for the stress response itself.","The adrenal medulla is a modified sympathetic ganglion whose hormones extend the fight-or-flight response. It prolongs the stress effects rather than ending them.","The somatic motor system controls skeletal muscle and has no control over the heart rate or the gut."],"lt":"t-ans","lid":"d3-t-ans-3","comp":193,"competencyId":"w8-ans-ganglia-divisions"},{"id":"L03129","dok":3,"q":"Many organs receive both sympathetic and parasympathetic fibers that push them in opposite directions. What does this dual supply allow?","a":"Two-way adjustment, speeding up or slowing down the organ as needed.","options":["Two-way adjustment, speeding up or slowing down the organ as needed","Twice the force in one direction, since both divisions excite the organ","A backup, since the second division takes over only if the first is cut","Voluntary control, since one division is always under conscious command"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"With two divisions pulling in opposite directions, an organ can be turned up or down. The sympathetic division speeds the heart while the parasympathetic division slows it, and the balance between them sets the rate. The body can also adjust an organ by easing off one division rather than only by pushing with the other.","notes":["Correct. Opposing inputs let the body adjust an organ in either direction.","The two divisions usually have opposite effects on the same organ, not the same effect. Treating them as partners pushing one way is a common misconception.","Both divisions are active at the same time, and their balance sets the organ's activity. Neither one is a spare waiting for the other to fail.","Both divisions are involuntary. Neither is under conscious command; voluntary control belongs to the somatic system."],"lt":"t-ans","lid":"d3-t-ans-4","comp":193,"competencyId":"w8-ans-ganglia-divisions"},{"id":"L03132","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares where the two autonomic divisions leave the central nervous system?","a":"Sympathetic from the middle of the cord; parasympathetic from the two ends of the CNS.","options":["Sympathetic from the middle of the cord; parasympathetic from the two ends","Sympathetic from the two ends; parasympathetic from the middle of the cord","Both divisions leave only through cranial nerves of the brainstem","Both divisions leave from every segment of the cord, from C1 to S5"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The sympathetic division leaves from the middle of the spinal cord, T1 to L2, which is why it is called thoracolumbar. The parasympathetic division leaves from the two ends of the CNS, the brainstem (through cranial nerves III, VII, IX, and X) and the sacral cord at S2 to S4, which is why it is called craniosacral. The two other names are a map of where each division begins.","notes":["Correct. Sympathetic from T1 to L2; parasympathetic from the brainstem and S2 to S4.","This swaps the two divisions. The middle of the cord (T1 to L2) is sympathetic, and the ends are parasympathetic.","Only part of the parasympathetic division leaves through cranial nerves. The sympathetic division uses spinal nerves T1 to L2, and the sacral parasympathetic fibers leave from S2 to S4.","Neither division leaves from every segment. The cervical, lower lumbar, and coccygeal segments send out no autonomic fibers."],"lt":"t-ans","lid":"d3x-t-ans-1","comp":193,"competencyId":"w8-ans-ganglia-divisions"},{"id":"L04591","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares the somatic and autonomic nervous systems by what each one controls?","a":"The somatic system controls skeletal muscle; the autonomic system controls smooth muscle, cardiac muscle, and glands.","options":["Somatic runs smooth and cardiac muscle; autonomic runs skeletal muscle and glands","Somatic runs skeletal muscle; autonomic runs smooth muscle, cardiac muscle, and glands","Somatic carries only sensory input; autonomic carries only motor output to organs","Somatic runs skeletal muscle and glands; autonomic runs only cardiac and smooth muscle"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The somatic and autonomic systems are both motor divisions of the PNS, and they are told apart by the effector they control. The somatic system is voluntary and sends commands to skeletal muscle. The autonomic system is involuntary and sends commands to the visceral effectors: smooth muscle, cardiac muscle, and glands. The autonomic system also uses two motor neurons in a row with a ganglion between them, while the somatic system reaches its muscle with one.","notes":["This statement swaps the two systems. Skeletal muscle is the voluntary muscle the somatic system controls, while smooth and cardiac muscle are involuntary and belong to the autonomic system.","Correct. Skeletal muscle is the somatic effector, and smooth muscle, cardiac muscle, and glands are the autonomic effectors.","Both systems are motor systems that carry commands out from the CNS, so neither is purely sensory. This mix-up comes from confusing the sensory and motor sides of the PNS with the somatic and autonomic split.","Glands are autonomic effectors, not somatic ones. The somatic system controls only skeletal muscle, and the autonomic system controls glands along with cardiac and smooth muscle."],"lt":"t-w8-ns-organization","lid":"w8-ns-organization-r4","comp":192,"competencyId":"w8-ans-pathway"},{"id":"L04592","dok":3,"q":"A fire alarm startles a student. Her heart races, her pupils dilate, and her airways open. Which division of the nervous system is driving these changes?","a":"The sympathetic division.","options":["The parasympathetic division","The sympathetic division","The somatic nervous system","The enteric nervous system"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"A racing heart, dilated pupils, and open airways are fight or flight effects, the job of the sympathetic division of the autonomic nervous system. It takes over during exercise, excitement, and emergencies. Its preganglionic fibers leave the spinal cord from T1 to L2, which is why it is also called the thoracolumbar division.","notes":["The parasympathetic division is the rest and digest division, and it does the opposite here: it constricts the pupils, slows the heart, and narrows the airways. Pick it if the student were relaxing after a meal.","Correct. These are classic fight or flight effects of the sympathetic division.","The somatic nervous system controls skeletal muscle, which we move by choice. It would be the answer if the question asked what lets the student run for the exit, but heart rate, pupil size, and airway diameter are involuntary.","The enteric nervous system is the network of neurons in the wall of the GI tract that runs gut movement and secretion. It fits a question about the brain of the gut, not heart rate or pupils."],"lt":"t-w8-ns-organization","lid":"w8-ns-organization-r5","comp":193,"competencyId":"w8-ans-ganglia-divisions"},{"id":"L04657","dok":1,"q":"How many motor neurons are in an autonomic motor pathway, and where do they meet?","a":"Two neurons, a preganglionic and a postganglionic, which synapse in an autonomic ganglion.","options":["Two neurons, which synapse in an autonomic ganglion","Three neurons, which synapse in two separate ganglia","Two neurons, which synapse in the posterior root ganglion","One neuron, running straight from the CNS to the organ"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Every autonomic motor pathway uses two neurons in a row. The preganglionic neuron has its cell body in the brain or spinal cord and sends its axon out to an autonomic ganglion. There it synapses with the postganglionic neuron, whose cell body sits in the ganglion and whose axon runs on to the effector. The somatic system, by contrast, reaches skeletal muscle with a single motor neuron.","notes":["Correct. Two neurons meet at a synapse in an autonomic ganglion.","An autonomic motor pathway has exactly two motor neurons and one ganglion between them. This choice adds an extra neuron and an extra ganglion that the pathway does not have.","The posterior root ganglion holds sensory neuron cell bodies, and no synapse happens there. Autonomic neurons synapse in autonomic ganglia, such as the sympathetic trunk ganglia or the terminal ganglia near organs.","One motor neuron running straight from the CNS to its target describes the somatic system, which controls skeletal muscle. Pick this if the question asked about a somatic motor pathway."],"lt":"t-w8-ans-pathway","lid":"w8-ans-pathway-r2","comp":192,"competencyId":"w8-ans-pathway"},{"id":"L04658","dok":1,"q":"What are the two divisions of the autonomic nervous system?","a":"The sympathetic and parasympathetic divisions.","options":["Sympathetic and parasympathetic","Central and peripheral nervous systems","Somatic and enteric nervous systems","Afferent and efferent divisions"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The autonomic nervous system has two divisions that usually act in opposite directions on the same organ. The sympathetic division prepares the body for action (fight or flight), and the parasympathetic division conserves energy and runs routine housekeeping (rest and digest). Because they pull against each other, the body can fine-tune an organ by easing off one division as well as by pushing the other.","notes":["Correct. The sympathetic and parasympathetic divisions make up the ANS.","The central and peripheral nervous systems are the two main divisions of the whole nervous system. Pick them if the question asked about the top-level split into brain and cord versus everything else.","The somatic system is the voluntary motor division that runs skeletal muscle, and the enteric system is the network in the gut wall. Both sit beside the ANS rather than inside it.","Afferent and efferent describe direction, toward or away from the CNS. They would be the answer if the question asked how the PNS is split into sensory and motor sides."],"lt":"t-w8-ans-pathway","lid":"w8-ans-pathway-r3","comp":193,"competencyId":"w8-ans-ganglia-divisions"},{"id":"L04659","dok":2,"q":"Which statement correctly compares the general roles of the sympathetic and parasympathetic divisions?","a":"The sympathetic division prepares for fight or flight; the parasympathetic division supports rest and digest.","options":["Sympathetic prepares for fight or flight; parasympathetic supports rest and digest","Sympathetic supports rest and digest; parasympathetic prepares for fight or flight","Both prepare the body for action, but sympathetic acts only on the heart","Sympathetic controls skeletal muscle; parasympathetic controls smooth muscle and glands"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The sympathetic division takes over during exercise, excitement, and emergencies: it dilates the pupils, speeds the heart, opens the airways, and slows digestion. The parasympathetic division conserves energy and runs routine housekeeping such as digestion, defecation, and urination: it constricts the pupils, slows the heart, and stimulates digestion. The two usually act on the same organs in opposite directions.","notes":["Correct. Sympathetic is fight or flight, and parasympathetic is rest and digest.","This swaps the two divisions. The sympathetic division is fight or flight, and the parasympathetic division is rest and digest.","Only the sympathetic division prepares the body for action, and it acts on many organs, not just the heart. The parasympathetic division does the opposite, conserving energy.","Neither autonomic division controls skeletal muscle; that is the job of the somatic system. Both autonomic divisions act on smooth muscle, cardiac muscle, and glands."],"lt":"t-w8-ans-pathway","lid":"w8-ans-pathway-r4","comp":193,"competencyId":"w8-ans-ganglia-divisions"},{"id":"L04660","dok":2,"q":"Which sequence correctly traces an autonomic motor command from the CNS to the organ it controls?","a":"Preganglionic neuron, autonomic ganglion, postganglionic neuron, effector.","options":["Preganglionic neuron, posterior root ganglion, motor neuron, effector","Postganglionic neuron, autonomic ganglion, preganglionic neuron, effector","Preganglionic neuron, autonomic ganglion, postganglionic neuron, effector","Anterior horn motor neuron, straight to the skeletal muscle effector"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The preganglionic neuron has its cell body in the brain or spinal cord, and its lightly myelinated axon runs out to an autonomic ganglion. In the ganglion it synapses with the postganglionic neuron, whose unmyelinated axon carries the signal the rest of the way. The command ends at the effector tissue: cardiac muscle, smooth muscle, or a gland. Pre means before and post means after, so the names tell you which side of the ganglion each neuron is on.","notes":["The posterior root ganglion holds sensory cell bodies and is not part of an autonomic motor pathway. The autonomic synapse happens in an autonomic ganglion.","This swaps the two neurons. The preganglionic neuron comes first, before the ganglion, and the postganglionic neuron comes after it.","Correct. Preganglionic neuron, ganglion, postganglionic neuron, then the effector.","A single anterior horn motor neuron running straight to skeletal muscle is the somatic motor pathway. Pick this if the question asked how a command reaches a skeletal muscle."],"lt":"t-w8-ans-pathway","lid":"w8-ans-pathway-r5","comp":192,"competencyId":"w8-ans-pathway"},{"id":"L04670","dok":1,"q":"Where are the ganglia of the parasympathetic division located?","a":"Near or within the wall of the target organ (terminal ganglia).","options":["Near or within the wall of the target organ","Anterior to the vertebral column near the aorta","In a chain beside the vertebral column","On the posterior roots just outside the cord"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Parasympathetic ganglia are called terminal ganglia because they sit at the end of the line, near or within the wall of the organ they control. As a result, the parasympathetic preganglionic axon is long and the postganglionic axon is short. Four named terminal ganglia are found in the head: the ciliary, pterygopalatine, submandibular, and otic ganglia.","notes":["Correct. Parasympathetic ganglia sit near or within the target organ.","Ganglia in front of the vertebral column near the large abdominal arteries are the sympathetic prevertebral ganglia, such as the celiac ganglion. They would be the answer for a question about collateral ganglia.","The chain of ganglia beside the vertebral column is the sympathetic trunk. Pick it if the question asked where sympathetic trunk (paravertebral) ganglia sit.","The swellings on the posterior roots are the posterior root ganglia, which hold sensory cell bodies and are not autonomic. Choose them for a question about where sensory neuron cell bodies sit."],"lt":"t-w8-ans-ganglia-divisions","lid":"w8-ans-ganglia-divisions-r1","comp":193,"competencyId":"w8-ans-ganglia-divisions"},{"id":"L04671","dok":2,"q":"Where do the preganglionic neurons of each autonomic division arise in the CNS?","a":"Sympathetic from the T1 to L2 cord segments; parasympathetic from the brainstem and the S2 to S4 segments.","options":["Sympathetic: C1 to C4; parasympathetic: L1 to L4 and sacrum","Both divisions: the lateral horns from T1 to L2 only","Sympathetic: brainstem and S2 to S4; parasympathetic: T1 to L2","Sympathetic: T1 to L2; parasympathetic: brainstem and S2 to S4"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The two divisions are first told apart by where their fibers leave the CNS. Sympathetic preganglionic cell bodies sit in the lateral gray horns of the T1 to L2 cord segments, which is why it is called the thoracolumbar division. Parasympathetic preganglionic cell bodies sit in the nuclei of four cranial nerves (III, VII, IX, and X) in the brainstem and in the S2 to S4 segments, which is why it is called the craniosacral division.","notes":["C1 to C4 and L1 to L4 are the levels that form the cervical and lumbar nerve plexuses, not autonomic origins. This mix-up confuses plexus levels with autonomic outflow.","Only the sympathetic division arises from the lateral horns of T1 to L2. The parasympathetic division arises from the brainstem and the sacral cord, at the two ends of the CNS.","This swaps the two divisions. The thoracolumbar (T1 to L2) origin belongs to the sympathetic division, and the craniosacral origin belongs to the parasympathetic division.","Correct. Sympathetic is thoracolumbar, and parasympathetic is craniosacral."],"lt":"t-w8-ans-ganglia-divisions","lid":"w8-ans-ganglia-divisions-r2","comp":193,"competencyId":"w8-ans-ganglia-divisions"},{"id":"L04672","dok":2,"q":"A chain of autonomic ganglia runs down each side of the vertebral column from the base of the skull to the coccyx. Which division do these ganglia belong to?","a":"The sympathetic division (the sympathetic trunk ganglia).","options":["Somatic nervous system","Enteric nervous system","Sympathetic division","Parasympathetic division"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"This chain is the sympathetic trunk, and its ganglia are also called paravertebral ganglia, meaning beside the vertebrae. Sympathetic ganglia sit close to the spinal cord, either in this chain or in the prevertebral ganglia just in front of the vertebral column. Parasympathetic ganglia, by contrast, sit near or within the target organ.","notes":["The somatic nervous system has no ganglia in its motor pathway, because a single motor neuron runs straight from the cord to skeletal muscle. This choice confuses autonomic ganglia with the somatic pathway.","The enteric nervous system is made of networks within the wall of the GI tract, such as the myenteric and submucosal plexuses. It would be the answer for a question about the neurons in the gut wall.","Correct. The chain beside the vertebral column is the sympathetic trunk.","Parasympathetic ganglia are terminal ganglia located near or within the wall of the target organ, not in a chain beside the spine. Pick parasympathetic if the ganglion sat in the wall of the bladder or near the eye."],"lt":"t-w8-ans-ganglia-divisions","lid":"w8-ans-ganglia-divisions-r3","comp":193,"competencyId":"w8-ans-ganglia-divisions"},{"id":"N-a17d-192-1","dok":3,"q":"A tumor destroys neuron cell bodies in the lateral gray matter of the T5 segment of the spinal cord. Which autonomic neuron is lost, and where would its axon have ended?","a":"The preganglionic neuron, whose axon ended in a ganglion.","options":["The preganglionic neuron, whose axon ended in a ganglion","The preganglionic neuron, whose axon ended on the effector","A somatic motor neuron, whose axon ended on skeletal muscle","The postganglionic neuron, whose axon ended on the effector"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The autonomic system uses two motor neurons in a row. The preganglionic neuron has its cell body in the CNS, in a brainstem nucleus or the lateral gray matter of the spinal cord, and its axon ends in an autonomic ganglion. There it synapses with the postganglionic neuron, whose axon carries the signal to the effector.","notes":["Correct. Preganglionic cell bodies sit in the lateral gray matter, and their axons end in an autonomic ganglion.","The neuron is right, but a preganglionic axon ends in an autonomic ganglion. The second neuron carries the signal on to the effector.","Somatic motor cell bodies sit in the anterior horn of the cord, not the lateral gray matter. The lateral gray matter holds autonomic preganglionic neurons.","Postganglionic cell bodies sit in an autonomic ganglion outside the CNS, not in the lateral gray matter of the cord."],"comp":192,"competencyId":"w8-ans-pathway"},{"id":"N-a17d-192-2","dok":3,"q":"You are told that a ganglion contains synapses between neurons. Is it an autonomic ganglion or a posterior root ganglion, and whose cell bodies does it hold?","a":"An autonomic ganglion, holding postganglionic motor cell bodies.","options":["A posterior root ganglion, holding postganglionic cell bodies","An autonomic ganglion, holding preganglionic cell bodies","A posterior root ganglion, holding sensory cell bodies","An autonomic ganglion, holding postganglionic motor cell bodies"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Two kinds of ganglia are easy to mix up. A posterior root ganglion holds sensory cell bodies and has no synapses. An autonomic ganglion holds motor cell bodies, the postganglionic neurons, and it is where the preganglionic axon synapses with them.","notes":["Postganglionic cell bodies sit in autonomic ganglia. A posterior root ganglion holds sensory cell bodies and has no synapses.","The ganglion type is right, but preganglionic cell bodies are in the CNS. Only their axons reach the ganglion.","A posterior root ganglion does hold sensory cell bodies, but it has no synapses. A ganglion with synapses is autonomic.","Correct. Synapses happen in autonomic ganglia, where preganglionic axons meet the cell bodies of postganglionic motor neurons."],"comp":192,"competencyId":"w8-ans-pathway"},{"id":"N-a17d-192-3","dok":3,"q":"An unmyelinated axon is traced to the smooth muscle of a blood vessel, and its cell body sits in a ganglion. If you follow the pathway back one neuron, what do you reach, and where is that neuron's cell body?","a":"A preganglionic axon, from a cell body in the brainstem or cord.","options":["A second postganglionic neuron, from a cell body in another ganglion","A preganglionic axon, from a cell body in the brainstem or cord","A sensory neuron, from a cell body in a posterior root ganglion","A myelinated somatic axon, from a cell body in the anterior horn"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"An unmyelinated axon from a ganglion cell body to smooth muscle is a postganglionic axon. It received its signal in the ganglion from a preganglionic axon, which is thin and lightly myelinated. The preganglionic cell body sits in the CNS, in a brainstem nucleus or the lateral gray matter of the spinal cord.","notes":["The autonomic pathway uses exactly two motor neurons. The neuron before the postganglionic one is preganglionic, with its cell body in the CNS.","Correct. The unmyelinated axon is postganglionic, so one step back is the lightly myelinated preganglionic neuron whose cell body is in the CNS.","Posterior root ganglia hold sensory cell bodies and have no synapses. The motor command reaching this ganglion comes from a preganglionic neuron.","Somatic motor neurons run straight to skeletal muscle with no ganglion. They do not feed an autonomic ganglion."],"comp":192,"competencyId":"w8-ans-pathway"}]}]},{"id":"mx","n":6,"title":"More practice: topics outside this term's schedule","exam":"","extra":true,"topics":[{"id":"mx-head-neck-muscles","title":"Muscles of the head and neck","summary":"facial expression, mastication, neck, extraocular muscles","mw":"","tr":"","cards":[{"id":"L04552","dok":1,"q":"How do most muscles of facial expression differ from typical skeletal muscles of the limbs?","a":"They insert into the skin (or other facial muscles) instead of onto a bone.","options":["They contract without any nerve signal reaching them","They insert into the skin instead of onto a bone","They lack an origin and attach only at an insertion","They are made of smooth muscle, not skeletal muscle"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Most muscles of facial expression arise from bones of the skull but insert into the skin of the face or into other facial muscles rather than onto a bone. When they contract, they pull on the skin, which is how they produce smiles, frowns, and raised eyebrows. Limb muscles, in contrast, usually cross a joint and insert on a bone to move it.","notes":["Like all skeletal muscle, facial muscles contract only when a motor nerve signals them, which is why nerve damage can paralyze one side of the face. This choice confuses skeletal muscle with cardiac muscle, which has its own pacemaker.","Correct. Pulling on skin rather than bone is what lets these muscles change facial expression.","Every skeletal muscle has an origin and an insertion; for most facial muscles the origin is on a skull bone. This choice comes from noticing the unusual insertion and assuming the origin is missing too.","Facial muscles are skeletal muscle under voluntary control, with striated fibers like any limb muscle. Smooth muscle is found in the walls of hollow organs and vessels, which would be the answer for involuntary muscle in the digestive tract."],"lt":"t-w7-lab-facial-expression","lid":"w7-lab-facial-expression-r1","comp":93,"competencyId":"w7-lab-facial-expression"},{"id":"L04553","dok":1,"q":"Which muscle closes the eyelids, as in blinking?","a":"The orbicularis oculi.","options":["Orbicularis oculi","Levator palpebrae superioris","Frontalis","Orbicularis oris"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The orbicularis oculi is a ring of muscle around the eye. Its fascicles are arranged in a circle, so contracting it squeezes the eyelids closed like a sphincter, for blinking or squinting tightly. Its name tells you this: orbicularis means small circle, and oculi means of the eye.","notes":["Correct. The orbicularis oculi is the circular muscle that closes the eyelids.","The levator palpebrae superioris raises the upper eyelid, the opposite of blinking. It would be the answer for which muscle opens the eye.","The frontalis raises the eyebrows and wrinkles the forehead. Choose it if the question asked which muscle produces a look of surprise.","The orbicularis oris is the matching ring of muscle around the mouth, which closes and purses the lips. Pick it if the question asked which muscle you use to whistle or kiss."],"lt":"t-w7-lab-facial-expression","lid":"w7-lab-facial-expression-r2","comp":93,"competencyId":"w7-lab-facial-expression"},{"id":"L04554","dok":1,"q":"Which muscle closes and purses the lips?","a":"The orbicularis oris.","options":["Buccinator","Orbicularis oculi","Orbicularis oris","Zygomaticus major"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The orbicularis oris encircles the mouth, with fascicles arranged in rings that act like a sphincter. Contracting it closes the lips and pushes them forward, as when whistling or kissing. Its name means small circle of the mouth.","notes":["The buccinator is the cheek muscle that presses the cheek against the teeth, keeping food between the teeth while chewing and helping blow out air. It would be the answer for the trumpeter's muscle.","The orbicularis oculi is the ring of muscle around the eye that closes the eyelids. Pick it if the question asked about blinking or squinting.","Correct. The orbicularis oris is the circular muscle around the mouth.","The zygomaticus major pulls the corner of the mouth up and back to smile. Choose it for a question about which muscle raises the corners of the mouth."],"lt":"t-w7-lab-facial-expression","lid":"w7-lab-facial-expression-r3","comp":93,"competencyId":"w7-lab-facial-expression"},{"id":"L04555","dok":1,"q":"Which muscle raises the eyebrows and wrinkles the forehead?","a":"The frontal belly of the occipitofrontalis (frontalis).","options":["Orbicularis oculi","Occipital belly of the occipitofrontalis","Procerus","Frontal belly of the occipitofrontalis"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The occipitofrontalis has two bellies joined by a flat sheet of tendon over the top of the skull, the galea aponeurotica (epicranial aponeurosis). The frontal belly, over the forehead, inserts into the skin of the eyebrows; when it contracts it raises the eyebrows and wrinkles the forehead. The occipital belly at the back anchors the aponeurosis and pulls the scalp backward.","notes":["The orbicularis oculi closes the eyelids. Pick it if the question asked which muscle produces a blink or a squint.","The occipital belly lies over the back of the skull and pulls the scalp backward. It would be the answer for the part of the occipitofrontalis attached to the occipital bone.","The procerus is a small muscle over the bridge of the nose that pulls the skin between the eyebrows downward, wrinkling the bridge of the nose as in a frown. It works against the frontal belly, not with it.","Correct. The frontal belly lifts the eyebrows and wrinkles the forehead."],"lt":"t-w7-lab-facial-expression","lid":"w7-lab-facial-expression-r4","comp":93,"competencyId":"w7-lab-facial-expression"},{"id":"L04558","dok":1,"q":"Which chewing muscle elevates the mandible and can be felt bulging at the angle of the jaw when you clench your teeth?","a":"The masseter.","options":["Lateral pterygoid","Temporalis","Buccinator","Masseter"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The masseter runs from the zygomatic arch down to the angle and lateral surface of the ramus of the mandible. It is the prime mover for closing the jaw and one of the strongest muscles for its size. Because it lies just under the skin at the back of the cheek, you can feel it harden at the angle of the jaw when you clench your teeth.","notes":["The lateral pterygoid is a deep chewing muscle that pulls the mandible forward and moves it side to side, and it cannot be felt from the surface. Choose it for a question about protracting the jaw.","The temporalis also elevates the mandible, but it is a fan-shaped muscle over the side of the skull that you feel at the temple, not at the angle of the jaw. Pick it if the question described the muscle at the temple.","The buccinator is the thin cheek muscle that presses the cheek against the teeth and helps blow out air. It is not one of the chewing muscles that close the jaw.","Correct. The masseter closes the jaw and bulges at its angle when you clench."],"lt":"t-w7-lab-mastication","lid":"w7-lab-mastication-r2","comp":94,"competencyId":"w7-lab-mastication"},{"id":"L04559","dok":1,"q":"Which fan-shaped muscle over the temporal bone elevates and retracts the mandible?","a":"The temporalis.","options":["Masseter","Zygomaticus major","Temporalis","Medial pterygoid"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The temporalis has a broad, fan-shaped origin on the temporal fossa on the side of the skull, and its fascicles converge onto the coronoid process of the mandible. It elevates the mandible to close the jaw, and its posterior fibers retract it (pull it backward). Its name comes from its location over the temporal bone.","notes":["The masseter also elevates the mandible, but it runs from the zygomatic arch to the angle of the jaw and is not fan-shaped. Pick it for the muscle that bulges at the jaw angle when clenching.","The zygomaticus major is a muscle of facial expression that pulls the corner of the mouth up to smile; it does not move the mandible. It would be the answer for the smiling muscle.","Correct. The temporalis fans over the temple and closes and retracts the jaw.","The medial pterygoid lies deep, on the inner side of the ramus, and helps elevate the mandible and move it side to side. It is not over the temporal bone, so choose it for a question about the deep grinding muscles."],"lt":"t-w7-lab-mastication","lid":"w7-lab-mastication-r3","comp":94,"competencyId":"w7-lab-mastication"},{"id":"L04560","dok":1,"q":"Which deep muscles of mastication move the mandible from side to side for grinding food?","a":"The medial and lateral pterygoids.","options":["Buccinator and orbicularis oris","Sternohyoid and omohyoid","Medial and lateral pterygoids","Masseter and temporalis"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The medial and lateral pterygoids lie deep to the ramus of the mandible and arise mainly from the sphenoid bone. When the pterygoids on one side contract, they pull the mandible toward the opposite side, and alternating sides produces the side-to-side grinding motion of chewing. The lateral pterygoids also pull the jaw forward.","notes":["These are muscles of facial expression around the mouth; the buccinator keeps food between the teeth and the orbicularis oris closes the lips. They do not move the mandible.","The sternohyoid and omohyoid are infrahyoid neck muscles that pull the hyoid bone down. Choose them for a question about the strap muscles in front of the neck.","Correct. The pterygoids produce the side-to-side grinding movement of the jaw.","The masseter and temporalis are the superficial chewing muscles, and their main job is elevating the mandible to close the jaw. Pick them for a question about which muscles harden when you clench your teeth."],"lt":"t-w7-lab-mastication","lid":"w7-lab-mastication-r4","comp":94,"competencyId":"w7-lab-mastication"},{"id":"L04561","dok":2,"q":"When you clench your teeth, you can feel one muscle harden over the back of your cheek and another over your temple. Which two muscles are these?","a":"The masseter and the temporalis.","options":["Buccinator and frontal belly","Medial and lateral pterygoids","Masseter and temporalis","Masseter and sternocleidomastoid"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The masseter and the temporalis are the two superficial muscles of mastication, and both elevate the mandible to close the jaw. The masseter lies over the back of the cheek near the angle of the jaw, and the temporalis fans over the side of the skull at the temple. Because both are close to the surface, clenching makes both easy to feel.","notes":["The buccinator is the thin cheek muscle, and the frontal belly of the occipitofrontalis raises the eyebrows. Neither closes the jaw, so neither hardens strongly when you clench.","The pterygoids are deep chewing muscles hidden behind the ramus of the mandible, so they cannot be felt through the skin. Pick them for a question about grinding the jaw from side to side.","Correct. The masseter and temporalis both close the jaw and lie just under the skin.","The masseter is right, but the sternocleidomastoid is a neck muscle running from the sternum and clavicle to the mastoid process. It flexes the neck and turns the head, so it is not the muscle felt at the temple."],"lt":"t-w7-lab-mastication","lid":"w7-lab-mastication-r5","comp":94,"competencyId":"w7-lab-mastication"},{"id":"L04579","dok":1,"q":"Which muscle runs from the sternum and clavicle to the mastoid process, flexing the neck and turning the head?","a":"The sternocleidomastoid.","options":["Platysma","Sternohyoid","Trapezius","Sternocleidomastoid"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The sternocleidomastoid is named for its attachments: the sternum, the clavicle (cleido), and the mastoid process of the temporal bone. When both sides contract together, they flex the neck, bringing the chin toward the chest. When one side contracts alone, it turns the face toward the opposite side.","notes":["The platysma is a thin sheet of muscle in the superficial fascia of the anterior neck, extending from the upper chest to the mandible. It tenses the skin of the neck rather than turning the head; choose it for the thin muscle that stands out as cords when you grimace.","The sternohyoid runs from the sternum up to the hyoid bone and pulls the hyoid down during swallowing and speech. It shares a sternal origin, which causes the mix-up, but it does not reach the mastoid process.","The trapezius is the large diamond-shaped muscle of the upper back and back of the neck, running from the occipital bone and spinous processes to the clavicle and scapula. Pick it for a question about elevating or retracting the scapula.","Correct. The name of the sternocleidomastoid lists its attachments."],"lt":"t-w7-lab-neck","lid":"w7-lab-neck-r1","comp":95,"competencyId":"w7-lab-neck"},{"id":"L04581","dok":2,"q":"When the right sternocleidomastoid contracts alone, which way does the face turn?","a":"Toward the opposite side (the left).","options":["Down toward the chest","Up toward the ceiling","Toward the left side","Toward the right side"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The sternocleidomastoid runs from the sternum and clavicle in front up to the mastoid process behind the ear. When the right muscle shortens, it pulls the right mastoid process down and forward, which rotates the head so the face turns toward the left, the opposite side. You can see and feel the right muscle stand out when you turn your face to the left.","notes":["Bringing the chin down toward the chest is flexion of the neck, which happens when both sternocleidomastoids contract together. Pick this if the question asked what the two muscles do at the same time.","Tilting the head back to look up is extension, done mainly by muscles at the back of the neck. A single sternocleidomastoid contracting alone rotates the head rather than tipping it back.","Correct. One sternocleidomastoid turns the face toward the opposite side.","This is the side of the muscle that contracted, not the direction the face turns. Because the muscle attaches behind the ear, pulling that point forward swings the chin to the opposite side."],"lt":"t-w7-lab-neck","lid":"w7-lab-neck-r3","comp":95,"competencyId":"w7-lab-neck"},{"id":"L04582","dok":1,"q":"How many extraocular muscles move each eyeball?","a":"Six: four recti and two obliques.","options":["Seven: four recti, two obliques, and a levator","Eight: four recti and four obliques","Four: the four recti only","Six: four recti and two obliques"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Six extraocular muscles attach to each eyeball and move it: the superior, inferior, medial, and lateral recti, and the superior and inferior obliques. The recti pull the eye up, down, in, and out, while the obliques help with rotation and diagonal movements. The levator palpebrae superioris sits in the orbit too, but it raises the upper eyelid rather than moving the eyeball.","notes":["Seven counts the levator palpebrae superioris, which lies in the orbit with the eye muscles but inserts into the upper eyelid. Pick seven only if the question asked for all the muscles in the orbit listed on the lab structure list.","There are only two obliques, superior and inferior, not four. This choice assumes the obliques come in the same four-way set as the recti.","The four recti do move the eye, but this leaves out the two obliques. Without the superior and inferior obliques, the eye could not make its rotating and diagonal movements.","Correct. Four recti and two obliques move each eye."],"lt":"t-w7-lab-extraocular","lid":"w7-lab-extraocular-r1","comp":96,"competencyId":"w7-lab-extraocular"},{"id":"L04584","dok":1,"q":"Which extraocular muscle turns the eye laterally, away from the nose?","a":"The lateral rectus.","options":["Inferior rectus","Superior rectus","Medial rectus","Lateral rectus"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The lateral rectus attaches to the lateral side of the eyeball, so when it contracts it pulls the front of the eye outward, away from the nose. This movement is called abduction of the eye. Its partner on the opposite side of the eyeball, the medial rectus, pulls the eye toward the nose.","notes":["The inferior rectus attaches to the bottom of the eyeball and mainly depresses the eye, turning the gaze downward. Choose it for looking down.","The superior rectus attaches to the top of the eyeball and mainly elevates the eye, turning the gaze upward. It would be the answer for looking up.","The medial rectus attaches to the side of the eye nearest the nose and turns the eye medially, the opposite movement. Pick it if the question asked which muscle turns the eye toward the nose.","Correct. The lateral rectus abducts the eye, turning it outward."],"lt":"t-w7-lab-extraocular","lid":"w7-lab-extraocular-r3","comp":96,"competencyId":"w7-lab-extraocular"}]},{"id":"mx-abdominal-wall","title":"Abdominal wall muscles","summary":"rectus abdominis, external and internal obliques, transversus abdominis, linea alba, rectus sheath","mw":"","tr":"","cards":[{"id":"L04389","dok":1,"q":"Which list names the four paired muscles of the anterolateral abdominal wall?","a":"Rectus abdominis, external oblique, internal oblique, and transversus abdominis.","options":["Rectus abdominis, external oblique, internal oblique, quadratus lumborum","Rectus abdominis, external oblique, internal oblique, transversus abdominis","Rectus femoris, external oblique, internal oblique, transversus abdominis","Rectus abdominis, external oblique, serratus anterior, transversus abdominis"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The anterolateral abdominal wall has four paired muscles. The rectus abdominis runs vertically on each side of the midline, and three flat muscles layer the sides: the external oblique on the surface, the internal oblique beneath it, and the transversus abdominis deepest. Together they support and protect the abdominal organs and move the trunk.","notes":["The quadratus lumborum lies in the posterior abdominal wall, between the iliac crest and the last rib. It would be the answer for a muscle of the back wall of the abdomen.","Correct. These are the four paired muscles of the front and sides of the abdomen.","The rectus femoris is a quadriceps muscle on the front of the thigh. Students pick it because its name looks like rectus abdominis, but it belongs to the thigh.","The serratus anterior is a chest wall muscle on the side of the ribs that holds the scapula against the thorax. It fits a question about the thorax and shoulder, not the abdominal wall."],"lt":"t-w5-lab-abdominal-wall","lid":"w5-lab-abdominal-wall-r1","comp":90,"competencyId":"w5-lab-abdominal-wall"},{"id":"L04390","dok":1,"q":"Which abdominal wall muscle has fibers that run vertically from the pubis up to the lower ribs and sternum?","a":"The rectus abdominis.","options":["Transversus abdominis","Rectus abdominis","External oblique","Internal oblique"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Rectus means straight, and the rectus abdominis runs straight up the front of the abdomen on each side of the midline, from the pubis to the xiphoid process and the lower costal cartilages. Because its fibers run vertically, its main action is to flex the trunk, pulling the rib cage toward the pelvis as in a sit-up.","notes":["The transversus abdominis is the deepest flat muscle, and its fibers run horizontally across the abdomen like a belt. It is the answer for the muscle whose fibers run transversely.","Correct. The rectus abdominis is the vertical, straight muscle of the abdomen.","The external oblique is the most superficial flat muscle on the side of the abdomen, and its fibers run downward and toward the midline. Pick it for the outer diagonal layer.","The internal oblique lies just deep to the external oblique, and its fibers run upward and toward the midline, roughly at right angles to the external oblique. It is the answer for the middle diagonal layer."],"lt":"t-w5-lab-abdominal-wall","lid":"w5-lab-abdominal-wall-r2","comp":90,"competencyId":"w5-lab-abdominal-wall"},{"id":"L04391","dok":1,"q":"The linea alba is a fibrous band running down the midline of the abdomen. What forms it?","a":"The aponeuroses of the abdominal muscles of the right and left sides meeting at the midline.","options":["Costal cartilages of the lower ribs fused together","The falciform ligament fused to the parietal peritoneum","Tendinous intersections crossing the rectus abdominis","Aponeuroses of both sides meeting at the midline"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"An aponeurosis is a flat, sheet-like tendon. The three flat abdominal muscles on each side end in aponeuroses that wrap the rectus abdominis and then meet their partners from the other side in the midline. Where they interweave from the xiphoid process to the pubic symphysis, they form a tough white line, the linea alba (white line).","notes":["The costal cartilages connect the ribs to the sternum and sit above the abdomen. They are the answer for the cartilage that forms the edges of the rib cage, not a midline band of the abdominal wall.","The falciform ligament is a fold of peritoneum inside the abdomen that attaches the liver to the anterior wall. It lies deep to the abdominal muscles, so it is the answer for a liver question, not the linea alba.","Tendinous intersections are the horizontal fibrous bands that cross the rectus abdominis and create the separate bulges of a six-pack. They run across the muscle rather than down the midline.","Correct. The linea alba is where the aponeuroses of both sides meet in the midline."],"lt":"t-w5-lab-abdominal-wall","lid":"w5-lab-abdominal-wall-r3","comp":90,"competencyId":"w5-lab-abdominal-wall"},{"id":"L04392","dok":2,"q":"Which sequence lists the three flat abdominal muscles from superficial to deep?","a":"External oblique, internal oblique, transversus abdominis.","options":["External oblique, internal oblique, transversus abdominis","Transversus abdominis, internal oblique, external oblique","External oblique, transversus abdominis, internal oblique","Internal oblique, external oblique, transversus abdominis"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The external oblique is the outermost flat muscle, with fibers running down and toward the midline. Beneath it the internal oblique runs roughly at right angles to it, and deepest of all the transversus abdominis runs horizontally. Because the fibers of the three layers run in different directions, like the layers of plywood, together they make a strong wall from three thin sheets.","notes":["Correct. External oblique, then internal oblique, then transversus abdominis.","This is the correct sequence read from deep to superficial. Pick it only if a question asks you to start next to the peritoneum and work outward.","This places the transversus abdominis in the middle, but it is the deepest of the three, lying just outside the peritoneum. The internal oblique is the middle layer.","This puts the internal oblique on the surface, but external means outer, so the external oblique must come first. The names tell you the order of the two oblique muscles."],"lt":"t-w5-lab-abdominal-wall","lid":"w5-lab-abdominal-wall-r4","comp":90,"competencyId":"w5-lab-abdominal-wall"},{"id":"L04393","dok":2,"q":"A fit athlete has a visible six-pack, the rectus abdominis crossed by bands. Which vertical band separates the left half of the six-pack from the right?","a":"The linea alba.","options":["Tendinous intersections","Inguinal ligament","Linea semilunaris","Linea alba"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The two rectus abdominis muscles lie side by side, one on each side of the midline. Between them runs the linea alba, the white line formed where the aponeuroses of the left and right abdominal muscles meet. On a lean athlete it shows as the vertical groove down the center of the six-pack.","notes":["Tendinous intersections are the horizontal fibrous bands across each rectus abdominis that divide it into the separate blocks of the six-pack. They are the answer for the horizontal lines, not the vertical one.","The inguinal ligament is the lower border of the external oblique aponeurosis, running from the anterior superior iliac spine to the pubic tubercle. It marks the crease between the abdomen and thigh.","The linea semilunaris is a curved line along the lateral edge of each rectus abdominis, where it meets the flat muscles. It marks the outer border of the six-pack rather than the midline.","Correct. The linea alba is the vertical midline band between the two rectus muscles."],"lt":"t-w5-lab-abdominal-wall","lid":"w5-lab-abdominal-wall-r5","comp":90,"competencyId":"w5-lab-abdominal-wall"}]}]}]};

